, but his new, aptly named disc, Never Gonna Come Back Down, is a stone\'s throwfrom perfect, giving Paul Van Dyk and the Sasha/Digweedtag team a serious run for the money. This time, Mr. T ismatched with Belgian-based Hooverphonic, whose lush, beat-heavy new CD The Magnificent Tree is set to take over where Portishead left off. Expect big gooshey gobsof love raining down all over the place and phat beats clobbering your soothed and seething eardrums. Really, now, what more could you ask for? -- Marc Savlov \r\n\r\nrobotec9: do you have the Magnificent Tree?\r\ndutzdutz: yah - i got it from juan today\r\nrobotec9: dude ...\r\ndutzdutz: want it?\r\nrobotec9: yea\r\ndutzdutz: yah - hold on\r\ndutzdutz: its movin... 2000kb/s\r\ndutzdutz: fuck dude 5000\r\ndutzdutz: that took all of like 15 secs\r\nrobotec9: what is the address again?\r\ndutzdutz: ftp.ibooks.com\r\ndutzdutz: chrisl - desire\r\ndutzdutz: you getting?\r\nrobotec9: yea\r\ndutzdutz: they are everything that I wish the cockteu twins would have been... they sound like them, but they back the shit up with fresh beats.\r\nrobotec9: do you know about the conversion of illustrator fonts from mac format to pc?\r\ndutzdutz: nope - email chepo\r\nrobotec9: cpena@ibooks?\r\ndutzdutz: chepo@ibooks.com\r\ndutzdutz: either\r\ndutzdutz: cpenis@icock.com\r\nrobotec9: do you hate him now?\r\nrobotec9: having a spat?\r\ndutzdutz: nah - i just dont ever talk to him\r\nrobotec9: man, it is impossible to work with mac people\r\nrobotec9: i am going to have to get a fucking mac\r\nrobotec9: goddamnit\r\ndutzdutz: yes -\r\ndutzdutz: u will hate it too\r\nrobotec9: bitches need to grow up\r\ndutzdutz: yup\r\nrobotec9: macs are for children\r\nrobotec9: in grade school and shit\r\ndutzdutz: ;-)\r\nrobotec9: and snobs\r\nrobotec9: with too much money\r\ndutzdutz: fags\r\nrobotec9: and fags\r\nrobotec9: and fashion hookers\r\nrobotec9: and primadonnas\r\nrobotec9: esp. prima donnas\r\ndutzdutz: and fags\r\ndutzdutz: its 70 fucking degrees here\r\ndutzdutz: freezed my ass this mornin\r\nrobotec9: dude, this is funny\r\nrobotec9: \r\nchrissnafu: what are you doing for lunch?\r\nrobotec9: jerking off\r\nrobotec9: uh\r\nchrissnafu: ok, sounds good.  mind if I tag along?\r\nrobotec9: oops, wrong window :-)\r\ndutzdutz: you can tag along on my nuts\r\nrobotec9: ha\r\nrobotec9: dude, i wish we lived in the same city\r\nrobotec9: and could do lunch\r\nrobotec9:  that would be cool\r\ndutzdutz: yah - i need to get the fuck outa here, i should go work for some publisher in boston\r\nrobotec9: totally\r\nrobotec9: except i herd boston sucks\r\nrobotec9: probably doesn\'t tho\r\ndutzdutz: man, I went to see jesse\'s play on saty, and guess who was there\r\nrobotec9: whO?\r\ndutzdutz: minutz\r\nrobotec9: jesse gonna poke that nappy dugout?\r\ndutzdutz: everywhere i look her dumb ass is around\r\nrobotec9: no shit\r\nrobotec9: what a dumnass\r\ndutzdutz: i dont think so - she was there with kristen whom i threw some bmg to.\r\nrobotec9: haha\r\nrobotec9: i\'d put it kristen\'s as\r\nrobotec9: sss\r\ndutzdutz: she has no sex appeal tho\r\ndutzdutz: well she does she just cant use it\r\nrobotec9: no, she is about as quick as a road kill\r\ndutzdutz: she doesnt know how\r\ndutzdutz: she needs a cucumber '),(4,'Worked out with J from work. Feels good to be a little pumped. Got Phorum working on Texticle. No big deal. It was pretty easy. All you have to do is set the file ownership positions and have the database, make the install script executable and let her rip. \r\n\r\nI went to the Dodger\'s game with Arnold and an old friend of his family. I had an all beef hot dog. I protested that we should get Dodger Dogs. Majority ruled thought. I wanted to keep solidarity so I got a beefer. Later I snuck up to the concession stands and got a Dodger Dog and ate it real fast then went back down to my seat. I was embarrassed to be eating another hot dog so I didn\'t tell Arnold. \r\n\r\nThe Dodger\'s won with a tie breaking pitch homer in the bottom of the ninth to win. It was a clean ending to a long game. \r\n\r\nErnie and I talked about friend slash roommate situations and some general experiences we had. I talked about what it was like to live with b.m.g. We listened to Arnold\'s friend talk about being stationed at an Air Force Base just north of San Francisco during the latter part of the Viet Nam War. He told us about how he worked three days on and three days off whereas most service men and women had to be lucky or related to the President to get a single three-day weekend. He told us about his trips to SF on a Triumph 500 that he shipped with him from Hawaii, his homeland. \r\n\r\nThen we went to The Hill. The Hill is one of Los Angeles\' greatest tourist attractions. It is the steepest uphill followed by immediate steeper downhill in all of California. It is like driving up the side of a mountain and over the edge of a cliff. We drove it in Arnold\'s friend\'s rental caddie. Arnold told of a similar caddie, also a rental, that met its death on a similar hill only a few miles away. \r\n\r\nWhen we got back to Arnold\'s, Cha-cha was waiting. T'),(5,'Worked out with J from work. Feels good to be a little pumped. Got Phorum working on Texticle. No big deal. It was pretty easy. All you have to do is set the file ownership positions and have the database, make the install script executable and kick ass. \r\n\r\nI went to the Dodger\'s game with Arnold and an old friend of his family. I had an all beef hot dog. I protested that we should get Dodger Dogs. Majority ruled thought. I wanted to keep solidarity so I got a beefer. Later I snuck up to the concession stands and got a Dodger Dog and ate it real fast then went back down to my seat. I was embarrassed to be eating another hot dog so I didn\'t tell Arnold. \r\n\r\nThe Dodger\'s won with a tie breaking pitch homer in the bottom of the ninth to win. It was a clean ending to a long game. \r\n\r\nErnie and I talked about friend slash roommate situations and some general experiences we had. I talked about what it was like to live with b.m.g. We listened to Arnold\'s friend talk about being stationed at an Air Force Base just north of San Francisco during the latter part of the Viet Nam War. He told us about how he worked three days on and three days off whereas most service men and women had to be lucky or related to the President to get a single three-day weekend. He told us about his trips to SF on a Triumph 500 that he shipped with him from Hawaii, his homeland. \r\n\r\nThen we went to The Hill. The Hill is one of Los Angeles\' greatest tourist attractions. It is the steepest uphill followed by immediate steeper downhill in all of California. It is like driving up the side of a mountain and over the edge of a cliff. We drove it in Arnold\'s friend\'s rental caddie. Arnold told of a similar caddie, also a rental, that met its death on a similar hill only a few miles away. \r\n\r\nWhen we got back to Arnold\'s, Cha-cha was waitin'),(6,'SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  FafOO  farfi  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU  SNAFU   '),(7,'Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar PLEASE!  Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar Nar Nar Nar Far Nar '),(8,'yo\r\nrobotec9: whoa\r\nrobotec9: that was wierd\r\nMissRockieH: huh?\r\nrobotec9: i was just sitting here thinking, \"wow, this is really cool.\"\r\nrobotec9: \"i should show it to raquel\"\r\nrobotec9: then like 2 sec later you im\'d me\r\nMissRockieH: well....show me\r\nrobotec9: mmk\r\nrobotec9: ok, come here'),(9,'<P>It is Monday and I feel awful.  Drinking is bad.  I don\'t like it.  \r\nI am switching to orange juice.  If I can\'t get fucked up on orange \r\njuice then I will just have to live sober!  Jesse, can I get an amen?!  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I swore up and down that I do not need to talk about my girlfriends on \r\nthis site.  It is rude and a violation of their privacy.  However, I am not \r\nactually \'going out\' with the ultra-fine snowboarding, rock climbing, \r\nfilm making, advertising woman known henceforth as Somy.  We just went out on a date is all.  \r\nI think my friends are entitled to know what happened on my date.  \r\nIt is protocol to spill that kind of information.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n6:30, I drive down her street and she is waiting by the curb in the most \r\nfly black outfit with hyper black and gold sunglasses on.  I almost \r\nswerve into oncoming traffic.  I am struck with my own inadequacy like a \r\nlightning bolt from the finger of God.  \r\n</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nGod thundered, <FONT COLOR=\"WHITE\" BGCOLOR=\"BLACK\"><B>\"WHO ARE YOU TO DATE THIS BEAUTIFUL WOMAN!\"</B></FONT>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nIt was not a question, but a declarative statement.  In my experience, God does not question.  \r\nI think it is for effect.  Nothing is more intimidating than a thunderous \r\nrhetorical non-question.  That is usually his modus operandi when dealing with me, anyway.  \r\nI mumbled something about not being worthy, tuned it out, and \r\ncracked the passenger side door for her.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nWe engaged in some small talk and then headed out for Los Feliz to <a href=\"http://losangeles.citysearch.com/profile?fid=2&id=52316\" targ'),(10,'<P>It is Monday and I feel awful.  Drinking is bad.  I don\'t like it.  \r\nI am switching to orange juice.  If I can\'t get fucked up on orange \r\njuice then I will just have to live sober!  Jesse, can I get an amen?!  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I swore up and down that I do not need to talk about my girlfriends on \r\nthis site.  It is rude and a violation of their privacy.  However, I am not \r\nactually \'going out\' with the ultra-fine snowboarding, rock climbing, \r\nfilm making, advertising woman known henceforth as Somy.  We just went out on a date is all.  \r\nI think my friends are entitled to know what happened on my date.  \r\nIt is protocol to spill that kind of information.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n6:30, I drive down her street and she is waiting by the curb in the most \r\nfly black outfit with hyper black and gold sunglasses on.  I almost \r\nswerve into oncoming traffic.  I am struck with my own inadequacy like a \r\nlightning bolt from the finger of God.  \r\n</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nGod thundered, <FONT COLOR=\"WHITE\" BGCOLOR=\"BLACK\"><B>\"WHO ARE YOU TO DATE THIS BEAUTIFUL WOMAN!\"</B></FONT>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nIt was not a question, but a declarative statement.  In my experience, God does not question.  \r\nI think it is for effect.  Nothing is more intimidating than a thunderous \r\nrhetorical non-question.  That is usually his modus operandi when dealing with me, anyway.  \r\nI mumbled something about not being worthy, tuned it out, and \r\ncracked the passenger side door for her.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nWe engaged in some small talk and then headed out for Los Feliz to <a href=\"http://losangeles.citysearch.com/profile?fid=2&id=52316\" targ'),(11,'<P>It is Monday and I feel awful.  Drinking is bad.  I don\'t like it.  \r\nI am switching to orange juice.  If I can\'t get fucked up on orange \r\njuice then I will just have to live sober!  Jesse, can I get an amen?!  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I swore up and down that I do not need to talk about my girlfriends on \r\nthis site.  It is rude and a violation of their privacy.  However, I am not \r\nactually \'going out\' with the ultra-fine snowboarding, rock climbing, \r\nfilm making, advertising woman known henceforth as Somy.  We just went out on a date is all.  \r\nI think my friends are entitled to know what happened on my date.  \r\nIt is protocol to spill that kind of information.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n6:30, I drive down her street and she is waiting by the curb in the most \r\nfly black outfit with hyper black and gold sunglasses on.  I almost \r\nswerve into oncoming traffic.  I am struck with my own inadequacy like a \r\nlightning bolt from the finger of God.  \r\n</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nGod thundered, <FONT COLOR=\"WHITE\" BGCOLOR=\"BLACK\"><B>\"WHO ARE YOU TO DATE THIS BEAUTIFUL WOMAN!\"</B></FONT>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nIt was not a question, but a declarative statement.  In my experience, God does not question.  \r\nI think it is for effect.  Nothing is more intimidating than a thunderous \r\nrhetorical non-question.  That is usually his modus operandi when dealing with me, anyway.  \r\nI mumbled something about not being worthy, tuned it out, and \r\ncracked the passenger side door for her.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nWe engaged in some small talk and then headed out for Los Feliz to <a href=\"http://losangeles.citysearch.com/profile?fid=2&id=52316\" target=\'new\'>Trattoria Farfalla Italiana</a>.  More small talk ensued.  We talked about what she did that day: label VHS tapes.  This led to the question, of what?  Of a documentary she produced for her brother about people going home from work in New York City, she is mailing the tapes out to film festivals.  During this short drive she is giving me directions, pointing like a jackhammer:\r\n</P>\r\n<pre>\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\nPoint--->\r\n</pre>\r\n<P>\r\nWhen she wanted me to turn the car onto a street.  It freaked me out at first, and then I thought it was really funny.  \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nDinner was good.  I had some spinach noodles and a merlot.  She had tuna filet and a white wine.  Her meal was ten times better than mine.  I didn\'t want to spend 25 dollars on a tuna steak.  \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nThe only staggering the fdate took happened when she mentioned she wanted me to do a website for her film.  I scooted my chair back when she said that.  I don\'t particularly enjoy doing websites.  A website is a vehicle for something interesting.  Creating interesting things is fun.  Building websites is not. Is that why I am here?  Because she wants a website?  No, she does want a website, but she made it clear that was not on the top of her agenda.  It is probably fourth or fifth behind long barefoot walks on the beach at sunset and kicking bass.  So all right, I let her off the hook for a couple more bites of her tuna filet.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nWe talked about good movies.  That seemed to be the subject to choose given she knows nothing about the Internet and generally does not appreciate it.  She had seen about every movie that hit the theaters or not.  She had not seen the X-men.  I had seen it 4 times.  She had no idea who the X-men were until she was introduced to the movie\'s producer at a party recently.  She did not grow up in the States and has only been in here 2 years.  I don\'t think I am going to take her to see it.  She probably won\'t like it.  Too pop for her refined film judgment.  I didn\'t even try to give an argument to see it:\r\n</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<a href=\"aim:goim?screenname=robotec9\">robotec9</a>: what did you like about X-men: the Movie?\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"aim:goim?screenname=fwmurnau316\">fwmurnau316</a>: well, I guess if you needed a strong opinion I would say that the movie would have been a lot better had the 45 minutes of character development that  the studio made Singer cut really hurt the depth and complexity of the interaction between the X-Men as a team. Also the subplot w/ Sen. Kelly was kind of half-baked.\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"aim:goim?screenname=fwmurnau316\">fwmurnau316</a>: I thought Hugh Jackman\'s performance as Wolverine was pretty tight. They should do like a Weapon X spinoff. I thought the relationship between Dr. Xavier & Magneto was well executed by Stewart & McKellen (although Ian McKellen looked hella stoopid in that outfit=-O). \r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"aim:goim?screenname=robotec9\">robotec9</a>: cool, thanks.\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"aim:goim?screenname=fwmurnau316\">fwmurnau316</a>: sure thing\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nShe gave me a math problem as a game that I still have not figured out.  Not that I am going to figure it out.  I can\'t find it right now.  I\'ll post it later.  In return, I gave her an encrypted message that was her name based on my name for her to decrypt.  JCZQLBGJCRB. Good luck.  I let her have the answer.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nWe finished eating and she tossed me a wad of cash to cover her half and went to the restroom.  \r\nI asked her later if guys usually pay for the dates in Korea.  She said yes, but she pays for herself.\r\nCan I possibly impart to the reader the importance of this in a relationship with me?\r\nI do not enjoy paying for girls.  I generally have never had to with the exception of one girl.  Somy got major points for this.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter we left the restaurant, I suggested we go to a bar and she suggested the <a href=\"http://losangeles.citysearch.com/profile?fid=2&id=68670\" target=\'new\'>Red Lion</a>, a labyrinth of a medieval bar in Silver Lake.  On the way over I asked her what kind of music she liked.  She said heavy metal.  She is a metal babe.  I thought that was significant.  Metallica (early stuff), Rage against the machine, Korn, like that.  Her favorite radio station is K-Rock.  Her favorite bar: Space Bar.  At the Red Lion, she grabbed a booth and ordered a dark Beck\'s.  \r\n</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<A Href=\"Aim:Goim?Screenname=Robotec9\">robotec9</A>: I got a Spaten.\r\n<BR>\r\n<A Href=\"Aim:Goim?Screenname=Dutzdutz\">dutzdutz</A>: you pussy!\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nHer Beck\'s was no good so she stole my Spaten and I drank the Beck\'s.  I thought this was a remarkably bold gesture for her to steal my beer.  It was charming and I was glad to let her have it.  We talked about films some more and her hobbies: snowboarding, rock climbing.  She made me feel like a boring person.  This is amazing, because I am not boring, not at all.  \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nThere was a baby in the bar screaming.  Somy asked if I liked babies.  Now this is a loaded question usually.  It did not feel particularly heavy coming from her.  I answered that I don\'t mind them.  She asked me something vague and smacking of long term commitment.  I answered that it is not something I think about.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nWe got a little close toward the end of the evening, but generally kept our distance.  I didn\'t feel like moving in on her.  I think because she made me feel boring.  At no time did I feel like I was macking at all.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nOn the way home, I yawned and she tried to stick her finger in my mouth.  That was when I knew she was most likely cool, because I decided I would not date a girl that could not make me laugh and I almost blew snot when she did that.  It was really funny. If she is capable of that kind of spontaneity on the first date, then there is likely more where that came from.  I want to see what kind of tricks she can pull out of her hat.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI did not attempt or feel like it was appropriate to kiss her goodnight.  There was no parking on her street.  That was an easy out.  I just dropped her off where I found her.  She got out and I drove off.  On the way home I cranked the jungle sky comp in the CD player.  She had said she wanted to go to a rave.  Hmmm\r\n</P>'),(12,'      <p>monday, september 23</p>\r\n      <p>It has been a while since I have written anything. I \r\n        have had much going on lately. I haven\'t told many people what I have \r\n        been up to. I guess that not telling is safety for me, that way the next \r\n        time I talk to them I won\'t run out of things to say.</p>\r\n      <p class=\"journal\">I cought myself with a loss of words on Saturday. I had \r\n        talked about going to see my friend, Jesse, perform in \"midsummer \r\n        night dream,\" and my friends, Audra, Juan, and Jenn, agreed that \r\n        they would go. I was glad that I found people that wanted to go, becuase \r\n        there are so many things that I would like to do but usually get put aside \r\n        until I find someone who also would like to do those things. So, anyway, \r\n        we go - and I am loving it, as they *sigh* are hating every minute of. \r\n        At the intermission, my friend Jesse runs over to see us, we said hi, \r\n        and we all told him how wonderful it was - i truely liked it; i laughed \r\n        much - My former-girl-friend, that i never talk to, pops up to toss some \r\n        dough in jesse\'s donation bucket. Here i am not knowing what to say to \r\n        her (what can you say) and she starts some idle chit-chat with the others. \r\n        I then, thinking quickly - \"get her out of here\", turn to audra \r\n        and start talking about a little kids shoes with lights in it... I think \r\n        i distracted the \'X\' without being rude. To not be rude when it comes \r\n        easy is a hard thing to do. I am constantly testing myself with that one. \r\n        I did say hi to her, that was about it. It actualy makes me sad that I \r\n        can\'t say more to her, or her me. </p>\r\n      <p>So then, there was the wonderful time that I had on Sunday, \r\n        spending quality time with myself. Avoiding the phone at all costs, and \r\n        truely enjoying the wonderful weather. I started to read a bit, I am going \r\n        through k-dog\'s books by Alan Watts, trying to find something that I am \r\n        interested in reading. No luck, i think I fell asleep listening to the \r\n        cocteau twins. I made a salad, and i made pasta. I think i worked a bit. \r\n        I talked to nearly everyone i know. I was in a wonderful mood, i talked \r\n        to my ficus tree while I ate dinner. My ficus tree is looking really healthy, \r\n        I am sure it is because all the love that k-dog gives him during the day. \r\n        Plants are truely extraordinary. </p>\r\n      <p>Today i paid for my Object-Oriented Java class. I asked \r\n        the student working in the Bursar\'s office when 12 week classes start. \r\n        She told me that they started today. So, I go back to work, and I work, \r\n        and I look up the roomnumber for my class and the time. It was 6:00pm \r\n        and the class had started at 5:55, i hop in the car and race to school. \r\n        I run up the stairs, check the clock (6:15pm), open the door. The prof. \r\n        and a dozen students are staring at me as I head to the closest seat. \r\n        The prof. asks \"what class are you in?\" I reply with a stuter, \r\n        \"ahhh. programing.\" \"computer programing.\" Students \r\n        laugh, and the prof asks me, \"what kind of \'computer programing\' \r\n        do you want to learn?\" I am about ready to punch his face in when \r\n        I programaticaly reply that I am to be in the Java-Object-Oriented class. \r\n        He then, tells me that i am three weeks early. I excuse myself, and smirk \r\n        \"you can never be to early, huh!\" I left the room feeling good \r\n        that I didn\'t have to sit in that damn room for 3 1/2 hours... I went \r\n        home and fell asleep till 12;34am, it is now 4:43am...</p>'),(13,'bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar bar nar nar '),(14,'Iâ€™d like to think that Iâ€™m not a greedy person. Or high maintenance. But there are certain things I need. Certain things that would be good. Things that would make my life happier. Things that would make the world a better place. I have often been afraid to ask for what I need â€“ and what I want. But dammit, thatâ€™s over. Iâ€™ll tell you what I want. What I really, really want. \r\nI want to be referred to, from this very moment on, as Mr. Sparkly Prettypants, MD, DDS, PhD. \r\n = from somewhere out there in the wild web'),(16,'bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   bla bla bla, I need to keep the monkeys out!!!   '),(17,'Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG!  Gimme a UHG! 