ll her\r\n\r\nshe can stay with\r\n\r\nherb and gina.\r\n\r\nand then sheÂ¹ll take off\r\n\r\nfor home.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni can hear the phone\r\n\r\ncalls now\r\n\r\ntransatlantic negotiations\r\n\r\nÂ³iÂ¹m a florida boyÂ²\r\n\r\niÂ¹ll say\r\n\r\nÂ³i like it here.\r\n\r\njust for once in my life,\r\n\r\niÂ¹d like to see\r\n\r\nwhat itÂ¹s like\r\n\r\nto have a tan.\r\n\r\nbut, yeah, i guess\r\n\r\nyouÂ¹re right:\r\n\r\nit might be a nice\r\n\r\nchange of pace.Â²\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni can see her\r\n\r\nlike iÂ¹ve seen her:\r\n\r\ncopenhagen summer,\r\n\r\ncontemplating the void\r\n\r\nin the christiana,\r\n\r\nsitting in the window,\r\n\r\nplaying backgammon,\r\n\r\noccasionally\r\n\r\nletting me win.)</PRE>'),(555,'<p>today on the way home from 24th street, i decided it would be a good idea to walk behind church street along the train tracks.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe idea was to avoid hiking up sanchez, a steep uphill walk to get home. the tracks are flat (for the sake of the trains) so it\'s a good shortcut: a couple blocks of flat, a block of gradual incline, and finally a set of stairs.\r\n</p><p>\r\nanyone who has ever visited or lived in san francisco is familiar with this game. a \"shortcut\" can often take you blocks out of your way, but it\'s so worth it to find that magic staircase. hill avoidance is everything.\r\n</p><p>\r\nusually, i will only walk the tracks late at night, after the muni metro has stopped running. sometimes i imagine what i would do if two trains came at me from opposite directions at the same time.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni watched the next train disappear down the tracks. okay - it\'s probably safe now, what is the probability that another train is following? pretty low. so i followed down my shortcut.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen i turned around, i was wrong! here comes another one. (remember the new york maxim: they always come in 3\'s.) no problem, i switched tracks.\r\n</p><p>\r\njust as i switched, another train turned the corner, coming at me, head-on.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>fuck.</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy eyes popped open, 20 times their usual size, wide enough to swallow stars. \"i am going to die today,\" i thought. there was nowhere to go, neither train was prepared to stop in time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy eyes darted quickly: a fence.\r\n</p><p> \r\n<b>UP. UP. UP. UP. UP.</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\ninstinctually, i scrambled up the fence, getting out of the way of the trains.\r\n</p><p>\r\nboth trains screeched on their brakes, stopping well past where i was walking. it is certain that i would have been run over. physics was not on my side today.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni heaved deep breaths in and out, straddling the top of the fence. then i had a look at where i was sitting: the entire fence was lined with sharp spikey things, probably there to stop people from climbing over (\"thanks!\").\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe muni driver motioned to me that it was safe to come down. but simply jumping back over the fence would have meant further injury from the spikey things. and the way i was straddling, well. i really want to have kids someday. preserving that function was to be a challenge.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsomehow i figured it out, i don\'t remember what i did. i think i flipped my legs over and jumped off. (despite getting stabbed in several places, i am happy to report that reproduction continues to be an option.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nall i remember is landing badly. i felt my back say <b>CRRRRRUNCH</b>, and the wind was knocked out of me. fetal position, on the ground, next to the train.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni\'ve used the phrase \"adding insult to injury\" before, but this is what it\'s like:\r\n</p><p>\r\ni looked up at the commuters in the train, and they were just staring at me. no looks of concern, not saying anything, nothing. expressionless faces, with glazed eyes, showing no emotion beyond morbid curiosity.\r\n</p><p>\r\neven though i feel pretty shell-shocked, and various muscles and vertebrae are spasming and doing that thing, the memory i can\'t shake away is the feeling of the eyes, watching me like i was television.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalmost getting killed by a train was stupid, fucking stupid, i should know better. and while back injuries suck, my body will heal, it always does.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut the people. goddammit. i can\'t forget that.\r\n</p>'),(556,'i get this spam a few times a week.  today it came from Edetect@PAXEmail.com.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin just four minutes, i can know the truth about anyone.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni find that hard to imagine.  i don\'t even know the truth about ME and i\'ve been looking for it very carefully for 47 1/4 years.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsometimes i wish i could be a secret squirrel and find out some stuff about some people who have pissed me off over the years.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\'ve done some search downs a few times, i must admit.  the only answers i ever found actually held more questions.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni finally learned that people who are truly evil web sneaks build a fake persona from before they ever log on the first time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey almost never have the name they tell you they have, or live where they say they do.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmostly their families and jobs and histories are a bunch of crap they made up to tell people like me who are about to get really badly screwed in the ass by them in some way.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe truth is that people like me will always be screwed by the evil ones...<br><br>\r\n\r\n...because we just don\'t think that way.  we just can\'t see it coming because we never in a million years would have thought it up ourselves, not even with the aid of sci-fi and fantasy conspiracy novels.<br><br>\r\n\r\none thing is good, though, the innocence required to be the screwee rather than the screwer is it\'s own reward.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni still am willing to believe in the kindness of strangers.  i\'m only disappointed about one time in one thousand, which are pretty fucking good odds, don\'t you think?<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(557,'<p>\r\na girl i was smitten with refuses to give up certain things while im perfectly willing to give up pot smoking and cigarettes. i find life increasingly difficult. im up for the challenge, but god damn new shit keep popping up just as soon as i think i have some things covered. i feel like ranting on my page and just yelling at people but i dont even have the energy to update my page anymore. Iv been spending a lot of time with certain people and while most of the time spent has been wonderful, i find the tiny negative parts to be god damn aweful.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(558,'<p>\r\nwhat happens when you feel completely disconnected from your partner?  what happens when you feel like you live on different planets?  what happens when you don\'t think you know your partner and that your partner has no idea who you are?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni love her dearly, but she doesn\'t know what i\'m about.  that\'s partly my fault as i haven\'t been very telling and partly the fault of our 2100 mile long distance relationship.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso what happens?  do people part ways when this happen?  is it possible to get to know someone after you already think you know someone?\r\n</p>\r\nwanting to be alone...'),(559,'<p>\r\nwhat happens when you feel completely disconnected from your partner?  what happens when you feel like you don\'t know your partner and that your partner doesn\'t know you?  what happens when you don\'t have the energy or desire to explain every facet of yourself?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni love her dearly, but i can\'t even talk to her.  she has this idea of who i am and it doesn\'t fit into who i am at all.  i\'m changing.  how do i change with her?  how do you get to know someone that you already think you know?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso what happens?  do people stay together and work through this distance?  or is the distance a signal to say bye bye?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwanting to be alone...\r\n</p>'),(560,'<P>I love from the bottom of my...</P>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/stadium_rockstar/heart.jpg\">'),(562,'<p>One of my favorite things about reading books is the few times where I am reading a book and see that I have very few pages left to read in it. Rather than counting the pages, I find myself wishing that the book went on and on and on.</p>\r\n<p>I guess that those are the authors who I term as \'good\' authors.</p>\r\n<p>There are some books that I wish would end and end up counting the pages left. Some of those authors are good too, but their material doesnt seem to catch my interest at that moment.</p>\r\n<p>The current book that I am reading that I wish would not end is Willa Cather\'s \"My Antonia.\" I\'d tell anyone who hasnt read it to do so.</p>\r\n'),(563,'<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/563_1.jpg\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/563_1.jpg\' width=400></A>\r\n<p>\r\nwell since mom and grandma are going to england for easter, the whole family will be at the k\'n\'j manor house hopefully talking about my up coming twenty forth birthday, which if i might add, is a very important day for me. it marks the age of transition for mr david a matte. an age where baggage gets left behind and new baggage is aquired. Im painting a new canvas on life and for this i am very grateful. It will be a merry day, full of bliss and happiness. It is for this reason, I feel this year, would be THE year to celebrate. Showering me with gifts and money i will emerge a new man, ready to take on the world, repaying past generocities.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI think the best way to \"regulate\" and make most \"efficient\" the gift aquiring process would be for me to type out a master list of \"ideas\", from which all of you, around the fire, while Christian plays with a phone that rings and drives kevin crazy, can take responsibility for different \"ideas\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlets, hope april 28th is as fantastic as imagined!\r\n</p>\r\n--<br>\r\n<br>\r\nchia@chiamattt.com<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhomer is not a communist.<br>\r\nhe may be a liar, a pig,<br>\r\nan idiot, a communist,<br>\r\nbut he is not a porn star.<br>\r\n-grandpa simpson\r\n<BR>\r\n\r\n'),(564,'<P>\r\nI had an interview with a recruiter the other day.  he looked at my <a href=\"../../resume/index.txt\">resume</a> and worked his way up from oldest job experience pointing out that at those jobs i was not working as a programmer or any kind of technical position.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhe said that he felt there was a type of person that i fit the bill for.  a type of person that he like to call a \'doer\'.  what is a doer?  well, it is someone who gets told what to do and does it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhe said that most of the positions he is hiring for are senior positions.  seniors you see do not actually do anything.  like a senior programmer.  a senior system administrator.  these people are not doers.\r\n</P><P>\r\nbasically, there are doers and there are thinkers.  i am a doer, not a thinker.  this is what he would have me believe.\r\n</P><P>\r\ndeep breaths.  okay.  there are a certain type of people that jump to conclusions about others and then assume a high attitude about their opinion of that other person vocally.  the term i like to use for this type of person is \'fuck head\'.  you sir are a fuck head.  i really wanted to call you back and tell you that i don\'t want to ever hear from you.  please do not bother trying to find a job to submit my resume to.  i do not want you to have any money on my account.  you do not desrve to make money off me.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni decided not to call him back because i need a job really bad.  i will swallow my pride and try to think of all the positive reasons i could be a \'doer\'.  i admit i am not a technical person.  you \'fuck head\'.  you asshole.  you prick. you slave seller.  i thought you were a professional.  how do you make a living?  leave me alone.</P>'),(565,'<P>\r\ni had indian food tonight, sag paneer, from the BOMBAY Indian Restaurant, \"Best Indian Food in the Castro\".  i am breathing fire out my ass.  it is really starting to trouble  me.  at first i found it unsettling, but it has been 45 minutes and the problem is getting worse.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni lit some satya sai baba nag champa agarbatti to cover the odor.  that is beginning to not work either.  i am going to have to crack a window.\r\n</P><P>\r\ndischarged gas is displacing the oxygen.  ironically, i want to breath less with the smell, but find myself gasping for more.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni think peter is trying to be nice because he hasn\'t said anything.  or maybe he is a contributor.  he ate the food too.\r\n</P><P>\r\noh man, there went some more.  this is insane.  how can an entire meal be converted from solid form to gas state with such efficiency?  this is unbelievable.  perhaps i should go to St. John\'s hospital down the street.  somebody call the EMT.  oh wait (lifts cheek) maybe call the fired department instead!\r\n</P><P>\r\ndo i get in last rites before i die?  they better not try to incerate my corpse at the morgue. . .\r\n</P>'),(566,'<p>\r\nE:\\Coldplay - Yellow2.mp3\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had a good bad night. its ending on an utterly bad note and it just makes me mad that i feel like that mono foam crack filler stuff. its totally bullshit. i think i hate everthing right now, but i know that isnt true. god i love so many things that just dont show any sort of connection. and if there is a connection its just something  trivial and short term. im hurt and i have good reason to be, and i just feel like shit and i want to yell but no one is around to hear, and to be perfectly honest, yelling is so grade five. im going to bed. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(567,'<p>so early this morning the fickle sydney weather was saying \'stay in bed, stay in bed\'. instead i got up, downed a few cups of coffee, then walked.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and walked, and walked, and walked. fifteen kilometres (or 9.321 miles, but that sounds really pissy).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i noticed things in this city that i hadn\'t seen before, or had previously been walking too fast to stand a chance. it was exciting! </p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the process, a friend and i managed to raise a few hundred dollars for <a href=\"http://www.caa.org.au\" target=\"window\">charity</a>. so not only was it a fun day, but we can walk around with that warm, fuzzy, \'i did something that might make a difference\' feeling for a little while. woo!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, note for o and starlajo: walked past badde manors and thought of you two. and eating placenta. miss you both.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(568,'<p>so i was <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/constellation/2\">at my parents\' house</a> all weekend. and when i came home, after nuzzling my cat for awhile, i decided to clean up the apartment and take out the trash and such so the place would look nice when my roommate came home later tonight.</p>\r\n<p>now, john and i live in a cute little courtyard complex that happens to be right behind a carwash.<BR>\r\nthere are two dumpsters nearby: one at the carwash and one that is less easily accessible for us, but is the \"official\" dumpster for our complex, which is across the complex from our apartment and just past the driveway.<BR>\r\ni always dump the trash in the carwash\'s dumpster when i take it out.</p>\r\n<p>so i went and did that today. carried two heaping bags of trash to that dumpster, threw them in, and was walking back to my complex when i heard behind me, \"oh no, you don\'t do that.\"<BR>\r\ni turned around and it was a man in a jumpsuit carrying my two bags of trash.<BR>\r\n\"i don\'t know where your dumpster is, but you can\'t throw your trash in ours.\"</p>\r\n<p>i was ready to apologize but then, when he was 5 feet away from me, rather than handing the bags to me, he threw them both in the muddy water next to where i stood, glared at me, turned, and walked away.<BR>\r\nso i had to pick up the now muddy plastic bags and, holding them away from my body so they wouldn\'t make my clothes muddy, which made them feel twice as heavy, i lugged them back THROUGH the complex and across the street.</p>\r\n<p>so fucking much for southern fucking hospifuckingtality.</p>'),(569,'<p>\r\nphat pants are cool. lets be honest. i dont want to hear any of those lame arguments. people tell me its a waste of material as they read the daily paper and sip on coffee from a styrofoam cup sitting on ivory lawn chairs in their panda slippers. phat pants are cool.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI work at the local independent grocery store. these are my pants, and that is my nice ass.\r\n</p>\r\n<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/569_1.jpg\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/569_1.jpg\' width=400></a>'),(570,NULL),(571,'<p>i am getting ready to leave.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>this is a good thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my home will be the world. my house, my truck. my dog, my shadow.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can only take five books. the closet in my new dwelling with wheels is small.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i cant decide which ones to take.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(572,'<p>tomorrow - the 27th of March 2001 - a group of citizens gets together to make a decision on a certain space that will affekt a particular future  -  that future is mine and that space is a small theater in Mama Praha.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>\r\nfollowing the death of my dotcom last month - clarity descended and reminded me of my cirkus dreams - i want to do plays ; create events ; empower the kiddos ; make our own world -->and so i began a search for a space - the finite within the infinite - a space to make and be made by.\r\ni committed to using my rent and food money to fund it - for we have to make choices - and time is limited (for no human survives this brief earth moment)- and money moreso -\r\nand and and\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nluck smiled on my quest and i found the unfindable - an old empty 2 level theatre built into the groundfloor of an apartment block - vacant, owned by the government - and \"zoned\" for only arts purposes.  on march ninth we met the deadline for a proposal to use the space .  (i  believe they go up on the block once or twice per year- so this was our fleeting window of opprotunity) - \r\nwe will turn it into a community arts center - our primary fokus being creating theater for children - small and large   :))\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/572_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>\r\ntomorrow they decide.  if we get it - the rent will be supersupercheapcheap (a communist policy holdover whereby the government actually works and devotes energies to supporting the arts)- and we\'ll be eligible for many grants/citymonies - which means we will be financially free to create.  and i may finally get a chance to align my dreams with how to feed this skinny body- and work for no one but me- and those i want to share with-  </p>\r\n<p>\r\nmay the cirkus live.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsend good thoughts.\r\nwork magicks \r\nplease universe HELP ME OUT HERE \r\nour cause is noble\r\nwe fight for the forces of good\r\nhelp us with this one thing\r\nwe want to be free\r\nwe are slowly dying in front of advertisements and cathode ray tubes\r\n\r\nour cause is noble \r\nand our direction true.\r\nhelp us .\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(desire is dangerous to everlasting peace but fuck i am not at peace - i need this - i need something- and will find it this way or another  - but i feeeel my time is short - i need this NOW- and this road seems the shortest </p>\r\n\r\nuvidime.  (we will see)\r\n\r\ni hope to invite you all to prague for a visit to our space - to come play with us.  spring/summer is a delicious time here and the theater lies between the two nicest parks.  a stroll in the park and a play...?\r\n\r\nwish us well. \r\nj.s.'),(573,'<p>i am foraying into the non-internet world, and i am discovering that no one there actually gets paid anything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>people work, and go home. lint and dusty change gets deposited in their bank accounts every two weeks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what they get paid per hour doesn\'t cover the cost of dinner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\'t have a point. i guess i will just keep on keepin\' on, and see what turns up. </p>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/573_1.jpg\'>\r\n</center>'),(574,'<p>\r\nhadalongtalkwithstintonightaboutusandthingsarebasicallyhalteddontpassgoawfuckthi<br>ssuckstheroyalpissfuckwannagetviolentgotdrunkandhighinsteadandallforwhatcuzshe<br>stilllovessomeoneelseandthatsomeoneelseusedtotbemybestfriendandnowadaysijust<br>cantstandhimandivegrowntolikehermorethenshelikesmeandperhapsitsallforthebestb<br>utgodfuckingdamnit.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nback to square one.\r\n</p>'),(576,'<p>so i\'m thinking about publishing my postponed folder to the web.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"postponed\" is my haunted house, the dirty laundry. all the words i couldn\'t commit to in the moment. email that should wait overnight before i send, but never <a href=\"http://sent.diaryland.com/\">sent</a>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere\'s a quick list of everything:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>love letters\r\n<li>breakup letters\r\n<li>resignation letters\r\n<li>empassioned proposals, hair-brained schemes, and business ideas\r\n<li>angry words to my mom, for meddling (bless her heart anyway)\r\n<li>rambling opinionated nonsense to mailing lists\r\n<li>various other incriminating evidence, proving conclusively that nothing is ever perfect.\r\n</ul>\r\n</p><p>\r\nsomeday i will purge all the sludge, and invite everyone to go look at what i would have said, had there been more chutzpah (or less self-control) to go around.\r\n</p><p>\r\nuntil then:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\nNAME\r\n      ~/mail/postponed - postponed mail folder\r\n\r\nSYNOPSIS\r\n       /usr/local/bin/mutt -f =postponed\r\n\r\nDESCRIPTION\r\n       Where email goes to die.\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n</p>'),(577,'<p>\r\nlooking at my clock through a glass of water with millions of small bubbles attatched to the sides i decided to get up and start the day. once up the day starts like most lighting a cigarette taking a piss checking to see if the door was still locked kinda routine. taking a drag i feel as though i have recently lost more than i have gained. not that what i want in return is more. the word investment has come to mean the quest for profit and most forget that the investment of time and effort can do things that profit has no effect on. this past few months has seen me devote a lot more time and effort to various things, and for what? if I were a dungeons and dragons game id have gained a few more experience points and perhaps confidence. nevertheless, while i dont run around with a two handed sword and a magical ring, I do feel that life as of late has resembled a dice game.\r\n</p> '),(578,'<PRE>April almost here\r\na Calstate day\r\nin Isreali spiked\r\nfuck-off\r\nDesert storming\r\ntennis shoes.\r\nFrustration nears\r\nthe screaming zone.\r\nOpen mouth\r\nrock-star wide\r\nand rip tendons\r\nwith the sound.\r\nWhere is the information\r\nI need to succeed?\r\nWhat am I doing here?\r\nDoes anyone else \r\nbelieve\r\nthat blondes can \r\nprogram?\r\n</PRE>\r\n'),(579,'<p>\r\nI was walking to get cigarettes and go get a hair cut. I walked down bank street with my hands tucked into my sleeves as it was still cold. the cold wind made enjoying the sun a little harder and flakes of snow darted around my face. I crossed slater and was turning to go into the tobacco shop when infront of me i see an elderly woman fall face first into the interloc stone sidewalk. her face slid a few inches plowing right into the ground, peices of skin catching on the grooves of the walkway.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI moved towards her and asked if she was ok. blood started pouring out from above her eye and a steady stream began to come out of her nose and the sides of her mouth. I helped her up and went into a book shop to get her some napkins. while on my mission, others began to crowd around her, one calling an ambulance. I got her some water from the cigar shop. Her mouth was full of blood and it was horrible to look at. The ambulance came, i gave my report and got a pack of cigarettes and a haircut. \r\n</p>'),(580,'<P>I am incongruent\r\n<P>with nothing to cradle\r\n<P>my grown level of dissatisfaction.\r\n<P>a perpetual cloudy day\r\n<P>on my shoulder \r\n<P>for a week:\r\n<P>being inside a song,\r\n<P>driving past a street,\r\n<P>being in a room\r\n<P>that led me here, \r\n<P>stimulated the rain.\r\n<P>I know you sometimes\r\n<P>can read me like a book,\r\n<P>but I miss being bookmarked.'),(581,'<PRE>\r\nhot honey\r\nsticky sweet vibes\r\npresence of mind\r\nslowly seeping\r\ninfiltrating\r\novertaking\r\ncosmic surroundings\r\nsnow crash\r\nemotional whiplash\r\nfrozen dreams\r\nwinter frequencies\r\nlost track\r\nno glancing back\r\nreverse\r\nrewind\r\nno concept of time \r\n</PRE>'),(582,'<p>hot honey<br>\r\nsticky vibes<br>\r\ndark streets<br>\r\nwet and sweet<br>\r\nmocha with cream<br>\r\nscalding heat<br>\r\ncatnip<br>\r\nslow tease<br>\r\nsalt lick<br>\r\nraw skin<br>\r\nsugar frosting<br>\r\nfingertips</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>hungry soul<br>\r\nwanting more . . .</p>\r\n\r\n'),(583,'<P>Went to a funeral today - friend at church - 48 year old woman - cancer.  Alot of folks talked about the contributions she made to their lives.  She loved kids - adopted 2 of her own but cared for everyones - she was there for dozens of families over the years - all the kids at church knew her and loved her.  She was devoted to them and committed to improving the lives of all those around her.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>So - on to my funeral - \"well he spent alot of time on the computer - yelled at the kids alot - worked alot - took care of his family OK\"</P> \r\n\r\n<P>Seems pretty lame, self-centered, and pointless.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>It would seem that the truly meaningful endeavors are those directed at improving the lives and existence of others.  From my perspective that matters more than anything else.</P>  \r\n\r\n<P>But you know - that can take alot of energy, and can be mighty inconvenient.  And often it\'s not fun or gratifying - so screw it - I\'ll just retire to my keyboard where it\'s safe, I only do what I want, and I don\'t have to interact with all of those inconvenient needy others.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Everyday I face the same decisions and make the same wrong choices that I know add up to a convenient empty life.</P>'),(584,'<p>\r\nthere is this thing, i think many of us do it.  we\r\nhear about a good thing that is going to happen to us and get very excited so want to tell all our friends and family.  we tell this thing and their\r\nexcitement for us makes it all become more real.  then, of course the thing never happens.  we regret telling everyone because something that never was but seemed so tangible is gone and missing.  we think that if we hadn\'t said anything at all maybe it would have actually been realized.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/584_1.gif\" align=\"left\" hspace=\"10\" vspace=\"5\">\r\n</p> \r\n<p>\r\nthere is a flip side of this that i\'m hoping to touch upon.  if i talk about something that is unlikely to happen, will it become even more unlikely?  if you say \"i bet i see a bald eagle attack a businessman on market street today,\" chances are it\'s really not going to happen.  the thing is this.  i\'m going to get on an airplane tomorrow.  for the past few weeks i\'ve been having a lot of nightmares about plane crashes.  i\'m not afraid of flying, i never have been.  i\'m not afraid of death.  in fact, dying in a plane crash, while instantaneously scary, wouldn\'t be such a bad way to die.  i hear the families of plane crash victims get a lot of money.  but i don\'t want to die tomorrow.  there are some things i want to do first.  there are a lot of projects i\'d like to finish.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis message really only exists to add to the improbability of anything catostrophic happening tomorrow.  thanks for listening.\r\n</P>'),(585,'<p>\r\ni woke in a cold sweat, dreary, confused, dismayed. there was no dream. no recollection. my cells disposed of the information imposed on them almost as rapidly as it was written. so i laid in bed, uncomfortable from sweat, trying to get warm and dry. trying to slow my heart to a slow, methodic beat. slow beats are sleep beats.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next day the sun was no where in sight. hiding behind a veil of clouds that dissipated the light. east sky? west? overhead? there was only gray. the gray of imposing rain. but\'s not just the sky that screams of rain. it\'s not as though the sky is looking at my, screaming, \"hey you numb fuck. it\'s raining today and i\'m going to rain all over you and your shitty little parade.\" when the rain is coming you can feel it. skin tells you so. the air around you. it\'s heavy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nin my car i drove. there was no where i was going. no where in particular. just out, away. into the gorge. flat freeway, high speed limit, columbia on eye level. sasha was pouring from my speakers but the mood was wrong. the dream had made the mood wrong.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat dream?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe dream last night\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\'t dream\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nokay\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nswitched the cd and vulcan blasted out of the speakers. blasted and pumped and made my heart feel light and my brain tight. fast driving. falls to the right. tourists, hikers, fat people that look from the parking lot then walk over and to buy leftover meat stuffed in intestines. a glimpse. gone.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntrees and road and train tracks and train tunnels that reminded me of the talisman and my fear of the dark. the music pumped and thumped and made my skin crawl and my brain burn.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"you can talk me into fucking you but i don\'t think you\'d survive\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"SO FUCK ME\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nroad running by in more of a liquid form than solid. don\'t dip your hand in that water. and the rain. sheets of rain. oregon sheets. had to slow the car down. but only enough. only enough to let the tires feel the road. like the feel after love. hot sweaty love. connected eyes love. connected minds love. the soft touches that barely touch. that give shivers and goose flesh.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand the dream. a fester in the back of my brain. down some dark hallway, behind some black door, hidden in some black box with a padlock and chains.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut i will show you some of it. because he doesn\'t know. he only knows about me when he wakes up and i push a dream at him to see and remember. and then he disregards me as nothing. but i am something. just another piece of him in this jumble of electricity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhere, look. see, the hurt on his face. the blood on his cheek. and the rain. the rain and the wet and the cold. and i know he will end up here. and he will think that he has been there once, maybe in another life or another dimension. and he, which really is me, will start to hate the rain, and this dream is to prepare him. me. but i won\'t look. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncause that\'s what dreams are. they are what we want to do and what we think we are going to do. \r\n</p>'),(586,'<p style=\"font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; color: #990000\" font-size: 10pt>two daydreams:</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; color: #990000\" font-size: 10pt>1.  move to san francisco, live in xep and eric\'s basement, practise acrobatics and contortion every day, get coffee with yeti, lay around with le, gossip with s5, meet aus10, frolic with sine and have all the time in the world to lie around and gaze at my housemates a lot.  visit austin (and joanna!) often.</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; color: #990000\" font-size: 10pt>2.  move all the nice americans to australia and set up a kind of commune/utopian society.  go to circus oz lots.  get to live in <a href=\"http://nirvana.kilda.st target=\"_blank\">nirvana</a> with ix, and keep my job.</p>\r\n<p style=\"font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; color: #990000\" font-size: 10pt>dream dream dream.</p>\r\n\r\n<center><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/586_1.jpg\'></center>'),(587,'no'),(3278,'<P><B>Chapter 16</B><BR><BR><BR>I\\\'m dreaming that I\\\'m carrying a foot-wide magnifying glass around a mall/university/parking garage/bazaar when the alarm goes off.  It\\\'s six o\\\'clock.  I get dressed and go downstairs.  It\\\'s the middle of February, and the Christmas tree is still up.  What the hell, they\\\'ve let it go this long, might as well leave it up \\\'til December.</P><P>I have some orange juice and toast over the sink and look out the window at the forest behind the house.  You can see further back into the trees in the winter:  no leaves, just black and grey trunks that get thinner as you look deeper among them.  There are two horses on the other side of the fence, fifty feet out.  They belong to Dr. House.  My niece named the chestnut one \\\"Cookie\\\" when she was a year old because it would eat \\\'Nilla Wafers out of her hand.  We have no idea what its real name is, since we never see Dr. House.</P><P>I get a coat from the hall closet and step outside.  It\\\'s not that cold.  The driveway cuts through an acre-wide strip of woods at the eastern edge of the property then joins a dirt road.  The sun\\\'s barely up; the sky\\\'s still blue-black to the west.  I walk down the road past the Oxendines\\\' trailer, then the Smalleys\\\'.  The Oxendines are part Seminole.  Dad calls them \\\"red trash.\\\"</P><P>At the corner, I turn onto the paved road and head west.  There\\\'s Mrs. Millican\\\'s house on the right, Dr. House\\\'s past that, the Dales\\\' across the road.  Where the road curves by the dairy, blackberry vines grow along the fence.  They bear in May, at about the same time that red and blue and yellow flowers bloom in the ditches.</P><P>I cross the highway.  A convenience store stands at the intersection.  It\\\'s where I used to buy Heineken in big green bottles, liters, maybe, usually from a clerk named Margaret who had hair dyed red and went to school with my mother.</P><P>Further down the road, dogs bark at me from yards on both sides.  Luckily, they\\\'re behind fences.  I take a left on Howell by my grandma\\\'s apartment complex, where the sidewalk starts.  The apartments are also home to a family from India that owns the convenience store across the street.</P><P>I dart over to the store.  They also carry the big green bottles of Heineken.  In addition, they have the tangy fruit-flavored Mexican sodas called Jarritos that I enjoy now that I\\\'ve quit drinking.  I check out all the chilled beverages, pondering whether to get a Coke or some juice or milk.  Three cases down from the sodas, there\\\'s some Mexican beer in squat brown bottles, two for three dollars.  I could take a couple behind the store, open them with the attachment on my Swiss Army knife, drink them in the company of the kids skipping school back there, smoking joints and cigarrettes, looking out for cops.  Nah.  Better stick with the Jarritos.  I buy a tamarind one.</P><P>Past the store, there\\\'s the high school I graduated from.  The top of the sun\\\'s just peeking over the rim of the stadium.  I check my watch:  twenty after seven.</P><P>The sidewalk mounts a hill toward town.  I pass houses and a travel agency and come to a stop at First Baptist Church.</P><P>I\\\'m fifteen minutes early for work.</P><P>Mom came home on Thursday and said she\\\'d spoken to the pastor about me, and he was willing to try me out as a janitor for ten dollars an hour.  Being that all the other jobs I\\\'d been applying for paid six an hour tops, that sounded pretty good.  I walked uptown Friday to the church office and asked to speak to him.  He took me into his library.  On a stand in the corner, there was a hammered dulcimer that he had made.  He played me a little tune on it then sat us down in upholstered leather chairs.</P><P>I say, \\\"Mom says you have a job for me that pays ten dollars an hour.\\\"  I don\\\'t piss around.  Get right to it, that\\\'s my motto.</P><P>He laughs.  \\\"No, I think she must have misunderstood me.  It pays five seventy-five.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I wonder where she got ten from?  I thought that sounded high.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Moms are funny like that sometimes,\\\" he says.</P><P>\\\"Well, shit,\\\" I think.  \\\"Maybe that was just a trick to lure me in here.  So what do I wanna do?  Wash dishes or wash cars or be a janitor?  How hard could it be?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well as long as I\\\'m here,\\\"  I say.  I make a kind of underhanded waving gesture.  \\\"What would the job entail?\\\"</P><P>He explains how they have an old, but clean, set of buildings.  He shows me a job description that lays it all out:  mopping, scrubbing, the whole bit.  Plus, the clincher:  other duties as assigned.  Which means that your poor ass was subject to doing anything they could think up.</P><P>\\\"It\\\'s a right-to-work position,\\\" he says.  \\\"You can quit at any time, and we can fire you at any time, without notice or reason.  And that\\\'s just what we\\\'ll do if you come to work under the influence or if you\\\'re caught with drugs.\\\"</P><P>As he talked, I began to notice how much he looked like a monkey.  Something about the ears and the shape of his face.  A regular Curious George.  I couldn\\\'t stop staring at him once I saw it.</P><P>I told him I\\\'d take the job.  He brought in Freda Blackwelder, who would be my immediate supervisor.  She was short, in her sixties, moderately fat, with wiry grey and brown hair and knockers like a pair of bowling pins sticking out from her chest, long and conical.  Her beady eyes looked out from behind thick glasses.  Her nose was long and sharp.  All in all, she reminded me of a mole or similar night-dwelling creature.</P><P>\\\"Freda\\\'s the boss!\\\" my mother had warned me.</P><P>\\\"I\\\'ve known Matt since he was this big.\\\"  She held her hands about a foot apart.  And she had.  Freda and my parents had been members of the church for thirty or forty years.  The pastor had been there only ten.</P><P>They\\\'d told me to be at work at eight Monday morning, and here I was, fifteen minutes early, full of tamarind-flavored Jarritos, the bottle still in my hand.  I walked up the driveway past the sanctuary, through the parking lot, around the fellowship hall, and saw activity over at the office complex.  Two men were standing in front of open double-doors off to the left.  They appeared to be drinking coffee.  I drew close to them.</P><P>One was older, in his sixties or seventies, my height, strong-looking, slightly paunchy, with a full head of white hair.</P><P>\\\"You Matt?\\\" he asked.  He stuck his hand out.  \\\"Frank Cona.\\\"</P><P>I said, \\\"How ya doin\\\'?\\\"  He had a really firm grip.</P><P>I checked out his companion:  tall, gangly, late thirties, long hair and not too many teeth in his head.</P><P>I offered my hand.  \\\"Matt McCord.\\\"</P><P>He grasped it lightly and let it go in a loose sliding motion.  My father would not have approved.  Looking at a spot about six inches above my head, he said, \\\"Bill.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Ya wanna cuppa coffee?\\\" Frank asked.  \\\"There\\\'s plenty.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I wouldn\\\'t mind a cup.\\\"</P><P>He went inside past the push mowers and rakes and power tools and odds and ends.  I saw he had a little kitchen set up back in there.</P><P>\\\"Cream and sugar?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Just cream.\\\"</P><P>He brought the coffee out in a mug that said \\\"I\\\'d rather be forty than pregnant.\\\"  A cat padded across the other side of the parking lot and disappeared around a corner.</P><P>\\\"There goes that kitty,\\\" Bill said.  He had a sing-song, halting kind of voice that made him sound like a halfwit.  \\\"I wonder if that\\\'s the one that had all those kittens.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Not very likely,\\\" Frank said.  \\\"That one\\\'s a boy kitty.\\\"</P><P>Bill:  How d\\\'ya tell the difference?</P><P>Frank:  Well, you count the balls, and if there\\\'s two of \\\'em, then it\\\'s a male.</P><P>Bill:  No, I mean how d\\\'you tell all the way from over here?</P><P>Frank:  I got a helluva pair of eyes.</P><P>It was then that I noticed that one of Frank\\\'s eyes was brown and the other blue.  \\\"Hey,\\\" I said.  \\\"Your eyes are two different colors.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah,\\\" he winked.  \\\"It drives the women crazy.\\\"</P><P>A Dodge K-Car ten years old or more appeared around the curve by the sanctuary and came bearing down on us across the parking lot.</P><P>\\\"Uh-oh,\\\" Frank said.  \\\"Here she comes.  The Cruise Director.  Business is gonna pick up now.\\\"</P><P>She threw it into park and sprung out.</P><P>Freda:  It\\\'s five after.  What\\\'re ya\\\'ll doin\\\' just standin\\\' around?</P><P>Frank:  I got five of.  Your watch\\\'s fast.  Besides, if it\\\'s five after, that means you\\\'re late, lady.</P><P>She considered this, muttering something under her breath.  She made her way back into the maintenance room, toward the little kitchen.</P><P>Freda:  Who made the coffee this morning?</P><P>In the wintertime, you start in the Fellowship Hall in the morning because there\\\'s no heat in the Memorial Building.  You have to wait for it to warm up.  In the summer, you start in the Memorial Building while it\\\'s cool, because it doesn\\\'t have any air conditioning, either.</P><P>On Mondays, you stack up the chairs that were lined up in the Fellowship Hall for the old farts\\\' assembly Sunday morning.  Then you sweep the hall.  You do not play the pianos.  You do not eat the candy that is placed in the music room to soothe the choir\\\'s strained throats, though Bill will show you how to sneak pieces of it into your pockets when Freda is at the other end of the building.  You do not touch the brass fixtures in the sanctuary with your fingers, as you will be cleaning the brass on Tuesday and do not want to get it any dirtier than necessary.  You will use your shirttail to manipulate brass doorknobs, etc.  You will vacuum the sanctuary.  Then comes a blessed ten-minute break.  Glory and Hallelujah.  You clean the bathrooms in the Memorial Building and finish up in the office.</P><P>You and Bill get off at one.  The two of you walk down the driveway to the sidewalk.</P><P>You say, \\\"Nice meetin\\\' ya, workin\\\' with ya today.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, it was.  Whatcha gonna do now?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Goin\\\' up to the library.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh, yeah?  I\\\'m goin\\\' home \\\'n rollin\\\' up a big fatty.  Ya feel me?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I do.  I\\\'d go with ya if I was still smokin\\\'.\\\"</P><P>We head in opposite directions.  Further up Howell, past the convalescent home and a lawyer\\\'s office, I run into Bert in the library.  He used to be a ball player, big, getting a little fat. He\\\'s got a head as round as a crab cake. Says he\\\'s in there to look up something about his car, being the big car man that he is.</P><P>Him and the rest of the guys have shunned me like the plague ever since one night at the Hilltop when I let something slip. That was the night I got 86\\\'d, but not for what I let slip. Well, pulling down my pants and shaking my wiener all around could be tangentially related to that certain something. They might be distant cousins.</P><P>\\\"How long you been back in town?\\\" he wants to know.</P><P>\\\"Year and a half.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Really? Haven\\\'t seen you around.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I been around.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Not where I been.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.\\\"</P><P>And that\\\'s our conversation, mostly. He used to be my best friend.</P><P>I check out <U>My Antonia</U>, <U>To the Lighthouse</U>, and a book of Raymond Carver stories. I go outside and am waylayed by Bud, the crazy guy I always talk to. He spends a lot of time in the park. He\\\'s named all the squirrels after Greek philosophers or mythological figures. Sophocles, etc. He says he\\\'s homeless, but I always see him over by the grocery stores on the other side of town. I think he lives somewhere over that way. He used to be a physicist, and he\\\'s always trying to tell me about how we live in seven dimensions.</P><P>He points to the bank across the street. \\\"Jimmy Kimbrough\\\'s in there stealin\\\' money every day.\\\" Kimbrough\\\'s the bank president. \\\"Jimmy Kimbrough and I graduated in the same year, 1957. I left Brooksville and ended up at Cape Canaveral. But if I didn\\\'t have any morals, I could have ended up a millionaire, just like him.\\\"</P><P>I point past the bank to the courthouse. \\\"What about them in there?\\\" Local politics really agitate him. He should get wound up now and go off on one of his rants.</P><P>He surprises me, though. \\\"Yeah, them, too. Stealin\\\' money,\\\" is all he says. He\\\'s quiet, seems resigned to it all.</P><P>Sometimes I worry that I\\\'m going to end up like him, the town eccentric. Sometimes I feel well on my way.</P><P>When I get home, the power\\\'s out. Mom and Dad are sitting in the Florida room at the back of the house where there are wall-to-wall windows. Plenty of ambient light.</P><P>Me: How long\\\'s it been off?</P><P>Dad: Couple of hours. Power company says it won\\\'t be back on before nine o\\\'clock.</P><P>Me: Well, we should go out to eat somewhere then, shouldn\\\'t we?</P><P>A pause.</P><P>Mom: Pretty much have to, I guess. Call your grandma and see if she wants to go.</P><P>We\\\'re sitting in Anna Bananas, the four of us, west of town in Spring Hill. We\\\'ve ordered. Waiting for our food. I\\\'m going to have butterfly shrimp. \\\"Sexual Healing\\\" starts to ease out of the speakers overhead.</P><P>Grandma: Where\\\'s the bathroom?</P><P>The other three of us point toward the back. We\\\'ve been there before. She excuses herself and gets up.</P><P>Dad: You know who I saw the other day?</P><P>We drive back into town on Highway 50. Everything downtown\\\'s closed. The sidewalks roll up at five o\\\'clock. We turn onto Main Street, pass the courthouse and the bank. Main becomes Howell by the library. Downhill, pass the nursing home, the church, the high school. Let Grandma out at her apartment. Continue on past the dairy to our little dirt road, Idaline Lane. When we get home, the house is lit up like a Christmas tree with all the lights that were left on when the power went out.</P><BR><BR><BR><B>Chapter 17</B><BR><BR><BR><I><P>They met on a morning when Matt was taking a notebook to his safety deposit box.  In spiral-bound notebooks, he was writing a novel about a writer, also named Matt McCord, who was writing a novel about another writer, identically named.  He had a hard time keeping track of it sometimes.  When he filled a notebook, he trusted it to the bank against threat of theft, fire, or nuclear war.</P><P>The man who didn\\\'t sleep was waiting to put his box back in its slot and to be let out of the safety deposit cage.  He was holding a sheaf of tens and thinking of expenses like cat food and light bulbs, newspapers and cigarrettes.  Matt came out from one of the privacy cubicles, his box jammed so full of notebooks that it couldn\\\'t close.</P><P>\\\"Whatcha got in there?\\\" the little man asked.</P><P>\\\"Notebooks.  What\\\'s in your?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Money.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You don\\\'t keep your money in the bank?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Nah.  I got my own bank.  Right here.\\\"  He patted the box and winked.</P><P>They looked around at the walls and ceiling and each other and waited.</P><P>\\\"Where\\\'s that damn teller?\\\" went the little old man.  He banged on the door.</P><P>They waited some more.</P><P>\\\"What\\\'s with all the notebooks?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'m a writer.  Well, actually, I\\\'m a substitute teacher.  But I want to be a writer.  So I might as well say that\\\'s what I really am.\\\"  He bit his lip.  \\\"I gotta get a bigger box.\\\"</P><P>\\\"They might give you credit for whatever time you got left on this one.  Y\\\'think?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'ve only got three weeks left on it.\\\"</P><P>The teller came.</P><P>Matt said, \\\"I need to see about getting a new box.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Just a second.\\\"  She motioned for the little man to come along.  After they finished, they came back down the narrow hallway.</P><P>\\\"Listen:  It was nice to meet you,\\\" Matt said.</P><P>\\\"We didn\\\'t meet.\\\"  He stuck out his hand.  \\\"Carl Venslauskas.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Matt McCord.\\\"  They shook.</P><P>The old man said, \\\"Whaddaya say I buy you lunch?  Ya feel like a sandwich?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Only if we go dutch.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Let me take care of this first.\\\"</P><P>At the Main Street Eatery, they got to know each other.</P><P>Matt said, \\\"Tell me some of your favorite things.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.  My favorite things.  The song \\\'Happy Birthday.\\\'  In fact, the very idea that we celebrate birthdays.  But, really, that part of the song where they sing \\\'happy birthday, dear so-and-so.\\\'  Calling somebody \\\'dear\\\' like that, like, \\\'We think the world of you, we love you so much.\\\'  What else?  How when you look at a little kid you don\\\'t know, and they\\\'re crying, they\\\'ll quit crying.  The way a cat stares at something that isn\\\'t there, like it sees a ghost or something.  <U>Cannery Row</U>, by John Steinbeck.  Such a lyrical, beautiful work.  Um, when you\\\'re sitting in a bar, and there\\\'s a buncha old men in there drinking and smoking cigarrettes, and, every so often, you can smell the smell of death wafting off of them.  The idea that everything was created by somebody.  I mean, somebody wrote \\\'Happy Birthday,\\\' somebody wrote all those old children\\\'s songs like \\\'Bingo\\\' and \\\'Farmer in the Dell.\\\'\\\"</P><P>\\\"That\\\'s a very sad song, by the way,\\\" Matt said.</P><P>\\\"Sad?  Why do you say that?\\\"</P><P>\\\"It\\\'s a dairy-o and nobody took a cow.  They all starved to death.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  You\\\'re one of the world\\\'s great wits.  I can see that for myself now.  Seriously, though, somebody made up all the games we play, somebody had to design payphones and public restrooms ----\\\"</P><P>\\\"Somebody writes all the books we read.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yes.  You\\\'re right.  And all the creating is hard work.  Or some say it\\\'s play.  I don\\\'t know.  I like that game show \\\'Pyramid.\\\'  The first cigarrette of the evening.  Laughter.  And my favorite thing of all is in that Louis Armstrong song where he goes, \\\'I see friends shaking hands, saying, \\\"How do you do?\\\"  They\\\'re really saying, \\\"I love you.\\\"\\\'\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yes.  That <U>is</U> nice.  I\\\'ve got one question:  You said \\\'the first cigarrette of the evening.\\\'  Most people would say \\\'the first cigarrette of the <U>morning</U>\\\'.  Do you work nights or something?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, that\\\'s a long story.  I don\\\'t want to get into it just yet.  Now why don\\\'t you tell me <U>your</U> favorite things?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t think I could begin to do that.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, why don\\\'t you <U>try</U>?\\\"</P></I>\r\n  '),(588,'\r\n\r\n'),(3141,'<p>Dim light squeezes between the wash of clouds and sweeps under the thin curtains. It causes the dust to dance in swirls under the threadbare sofa. He peeks at the sky between the sheaths of gauzy green fabric that hang limply from a sagging wooden dowel. He is careful not to look down. He purses his lips into a scowl. It looks like rain is coming. He hooks the handle of his umbrella with a finger as he opens the door and steps out onto the landing.</p><p>\r\n\r\nThe elevator is an ancient contraption of sliding doors that open onto a wire cage. It has been broken for weeks but he flattens his thumb on the button just in case. Nothing. He hugs the wall to his right as he spirals down the staircase. He is careful not to look over the bare wooden banister into the hollow of the stairwell. Though he lives on the seventh floor he is afraid of heights. The compulsion to grab the banister with his left hand, push up, and vault over into the empty air grips him with panic with every trip up or down. He closes his eyes and feels his way with his shoulder.</p><p>\r\n\r\nIn the lobby, all traces of fear vanish by the time he pulls his key ring out of his right front pocket. The keys dangle on the end of a chain that swings from his hidden wallet. He lifts a tiny key to a mailbox that says â€œHemmerschmidt, Santiagoâ€ and turns it delicately to the right. No mail today. He swings the box closed and drops the keys back into their warm pouch.</p><p>\r\n\r\nHe exits past an old man who spends his days wilting on a weathered vinyl chair in the lobby. The old man raises his eyebrows in a sneer every time he passes. He knows that the old man believes that he is Hemmerschmidt, but that he also thinks Santiago is his small wiry and possibly Puerto Rican gay lover. He finds this too amusing to correct. He will never tell the old man that his full name is Santiago Hemmerschmidt. The old man will never find out that Santiago is the grandson of an Argentine cabaret star and a former S.S. officer. And Santiago will never learn that his grandfather gave the order that sent the old manâ€™s mother to her death during the war. </p>\r\n'),(589,'</HEAD><BODY  BGCOLOR=\"#000000\"><P><font color=\"238e23\"><h2>like it as knot, tying up mine time</font></h><P>'),(590,'<p>she said to me:</p>\r\n<blockquote>\"sweetheart, i want you to stand back and look at how you connect to people like dots.  when you can see that, you will understand: <b>the world is your ambiguous pronoun.</b>\"</blockquote>\r\n<p>i hung up the phone and planted all the memories as flowers in a garden that blooms only for me.</p>\r\n<p>i unzipped my skin, stepped out, and walked away.</p>\r\n'),(591,'<p>so does anyone have a (nonsexual) part of their body that they rub or touch for comfort?</p>\r\n<p>elly and i have talked about this some, i know, because hers is located very close to mine.</p>\r\n<p>mine is this little, maybe 4\" or 5\" circumference, patch of skin right above my hip and below my waistline, on the edge of my belly. i tend to rub it in counter clock wise circles when i\'m walking around my apartment in the morning, pre-shower. and sometimes when i\'m lying in bed thinking.</p>\r\n<p>(i also: suck my thumb, rub my head in clockwise circles with the tips of my fingers, and endlessly play games with my fingers. but none of these brings the same sort of comfort as rubbing my side.)</p>'),(592,'<P>Swinney is playing tour guide to my visiting sister, since I\'m (sadly) too busy.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Here we see them abusing network resources at the Metreon!  Wheeee!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Swinney is a swell guy.</P>'),(593,'i saw this commercial for tuna fish in a pouch and it made me so happy.  i hate draining tuna in a can because i never get it right and i always cut my finger.<br><br>\r\none of the best things to eat is tuna casserole, because it is so pot luck supper in a suburbian neighborhood and you should always have white bread with butter and a glass of milk with it, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhere is the famous recipe: <br>\r\none pouch or can of tuna<br>\r\ntwo cans of leseur tiny peas<br>\r\n1 small box of pasta, the best kind is shells or bowties or spirals and the kind that has green and orange and yellow pasta is good, too.<br>\r\n2 cans of cream of chicken soup or cream of celery if chicken doesn\'t thrill you or 1 can of cream of mushroom and one can of cream of celery is good, too.<br>\r\nsome finely chopped onion, depending on how much you like onion.  i use some i grate over the top of the mixing bowl until it looks like enough.<br>\r\nalso good to add is some cheese, swiss or mozarella gives the best flavor and texture.<br>\r\nboil up the pasta, drain it and dump it in a really big bowl.<br>\r\ndrain 1 can of peas completely, and the other one only a little and dump them in the bowl.<br>put your drained tuna in.  if you really like tuna you can double it.<br>\r\ntoss in the onion bits.<br>\r\nnow dump in both cans of soup.<br>\r\nstir it all up and bake at 350 in a GLASS pan for about an hour.<br>\r\nit makes enough to feed lots of hungry people and is very good the second day, too.<br>\r\nthis middle america recipe is brought to you by a real mom of le.<br>\r\n'),(594,'<P>yesterday was stranger than usual. in my head at least.  for some reason i seemed to have access to a fun ride in my head, and was treated to countless subtle but powerful mini hallucinations. maybe they were just flashbacks? like the doctors like to call em.  maybe im just a little more fucked in the head than you all. maybe im perfectly fine. or maybe im just perfect.  yeah. i think thats it. i must be perfect if youre all reading this now.  either way, it was a fun filled day in my amusement park mind yesterday.   i saw a man on the freewaste driving his rugged toyota (or something similar in gas mileage capabilities) and i saw him as a road warrior delivery unit. traveling the great expanse of concrete and spilt gasoline.  he was out of the future. at least to me he was.  i saw a group of men doing construction on a small street above, with a large mechanical tower for doing something to a city, and i felt them, and their position on planet earth and it felt like i should be watching Goonies.  i dunno.   I saw a chinese restaurant at night and it looked like the asian men inside were doing a syncronized dance with their mops and cleaning tools.  and i laughed. and i like to laugh when i see things like that cos they make me feel like a true body inhabiting a true world full of fun sights and sounds.   you should look around more often. u might see something worthwhile too.  </P>\r\n<CENTER> <img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/594_1.jpg\'></center>\r\n-TUBE\r\n\r\n'),(595,'<p>\r\nme i love life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have a head ache. pressure from within and out. I sit and ponder what to do. Nothing seems fun anymore. things are different in so many small ways and what to do with so much extra time scares me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme i love life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsluggish at work today i avoided doing anything in the freezers. my mind was coated in a substance preventing me from making any real decisions.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme i love life.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(596,'<P>\r\ni need the following things in my room:\r\n</P>\r\n<P>a bed.  it does not have to be a big bed, even a twin would be nice.  i feel so space conscious.  i like my room with very little.  kstar likes my room to have stuff in it.  but i think she stopped bugging me about it and accepted my sparseness.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>a dresser.  i don\'t like keeping my clothes in baskets on the floor.  the floor is dirty and has cat hair and the baskets are ugly.  so i want to compartmentalize my socks and undies.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>a lazyboy.  admittedly, i am lazy.  i like those large chairs with the foot rest that pops out.  call me oldfashioned.  let me recline socially into a comfortable position and drift off with my laptop.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>an alter.  i want there to be a place with special things on it.  i like those.  i have seen them other places and in other people\'s houses.  i want one in my room.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>that is all.</P>'),(597,'<p>o my gloria steinem was on the tele today with her hubbie</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we love gloria.. do you love gloria?</p>\r\n\r\n'),(598,'<p>sweet jesus...someone needs to slap me upside the head.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>its midnight and i am baking cakes<br>\r\nand chopping veggies<br>\r\nand making tabouli<br>\r\n...and spinach dip too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why do i feel the need to try so hard to make things so enjoyable!?!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my roommate and I are having a party tomorrow. its sort of my going away/moving out party. im not even all that excited about it. i dont do goodbyes very well...especially not in group situations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>okay...really NOT at all!<br> arrrrrrrrrrrrrrggggggggggg</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<p>and on top of all this im realizing as much as i think i\'ve accepted my family and that they aren\'t going to change.....and think i can deal with who they are, and love who they are...i get kicked in the ass.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>despite invitations, and despite the fact that i use to live 2000 miles away...and now only live 200 miles away. not one of them has come to visit me, or expressed any interest in seeing where i lived the past 10 months.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and now i\'m leaving again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck<br>\r\nall i ever seem to be good at is pushing people away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and yea, im feeling sorry for myself.<br>\r\n[so shoot me]</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>[please]</p>'),(599,'<p>I like walking at night. I like the empty streets and the dark houses, tonight I walked past them and to the abandoned train tracks. The puddles were deep. Dogs sometimes roam the tracks, so I brought a little knife. It\'s a useless thing, I bring it to make myself feel better, so I\'ll have something to do -- even if it does no good.</p>\r\n<p>I remember I had the same knife with me when I was mugged a while ago. The guy looked at me like, \"Uhh, and what are you going to do with <i>that</i>?\" I would have laughed when dropping the thing, you know, if I weren\'t being mugged.</p>\r\n<p>But the tracks were nice. There were strange birds making noises and it smelled good. Then I heard one of the dogs up ahead and, being alone, decided to turn back.</p>\r\n<p>When I got home my cat was waiting for me to let him inside. Perhaps he found a dog too?</p>\r\n '),(600,'<p>it went kind of like this:</p>\r\n<p>[weeb] CHAYA<br>[=Beep!=] weeb sent you a beep.<br>[chaya] WEEB<br>[weeb] will you help me permdread my hair?<br>[chaya] yes ok!<br>[chaya] next week?<br>[chaya] or right now?<br>[weeb] tonight?<br>[chaya] okay!</p>\r\n<p>and then there was assorted adventure and weeb\'s hair is too short to fit in the damn curlers, so i put it all in little tiny buns all over her head and took pictures.</p>\r\n<p>i hope she won\'t hate me if it ends up horribly horribly wrong. HAH.</p>'),(601,'<p>it went kind of like this:</p>\r\n<p>[weeb] CHAYA<br>[=Beep!=] weeb sent you a beep.<br>[chaya] WEEB<br>[weeb] will you help me permdread my hair?<br>[chaya] yes ok!<br>[chaya] next week?<br>[chaya] or right now?<br>[weeb] tonight?<br>[chaya] okay!</p>\r\n<p>and then there was assorted adventure and weeb\'s hair is too short to fit in the damn curlers, so i put it all in little tiny buns all over her head and took pictures.</p>\r\n<p>i hope she won\'t hate me if it ends up horribly horribly wrong. HAH.</p>\r\n<img src=\"http://hoohaw.org/weeb1.jpg\" alt=\"hi mom\"><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://hoohaw.org/weeb2.jpg\" alt=\"giggle\"><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://hoohaw.org/weeb3.jpg\" alt=\"so tiny\"><br>'),(602,'<P>Saturday morning - full of promise  - gorgeous day - Spring is finally here.  How do I want to use this blessing of day?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Get the car fixed,   clean up the back yard, go to my daughter\'s volleyball game, clean the garage, install a basketball net in the back (it\'s been in the box in the garage for a month), pay taxes, move the tortoises outside, go into work and fix a database problem I have, pay some bills, watch Venus Williams and Jennifer Capriati play tennis, do the laundry.  I\'m so industrious in my own mind, it makes me tired just to think about it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Maybe I\'ll just sit down at the computer for a \"little\" while, play a lttle, write a little, rest up from all my planning, and hopefully get it in gear by noon.</P>  '),(603,'</p><p>\r\nlast night, <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com\">joanna</a> and i were having an email chat about astrology, and i was complaining about how awful <a href=\"http://members.aol.com/laehar/zodiac/aries.html\">this rundown</a> of moon-in-aries qualities are. we back-and-forthed for a while (as we do) about our various astrologically-affected qualities. and then i sent her one last email asking her a hard question that could have maybe been seen as confrontational (\"do i really *brag* though?\" i asked. \"is it bragging? am i really out to impress, or do i just tell things?\"). and then, i went to bed. and then, i didn\'t get a response.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso i got worried.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>maybe joanna hates me</i> i thought. <i>maybe i pissed her off by accusing her of falsely labelling me as a braggart</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni fretted.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen i went and checked her site today, to see if maybe she had a huge new piece of writing up about how awful i am and how she is so confused about how much i can suck.\r\n</p><p>\r\ninstead, i found a little note on the front page:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<tt>\r\n(elly, remember. fort worth this weekend. not replying to your emails doesn\'t mean i hate you. i\'m just not getting them. kiss kiss.)\r\n</tt>\r\n</p><p>\r\njo knows me.'),(604,'I\'m still confused on how to use this so I\'m fearful of writing entries... ahh!'),(605,'<p>\r\ntoday was a beautiful day.  woke up late.. ate late.. drank coffee.. played frisbee in the park with darwin.. went record shopping.. listened to new records.. ate a cadbury egg.. only smoked one cigarette all day.. watched some tv.. and now, here i am about to go to sleep and my kitty is showing me how much she loves me by curling up on my lap.</p>\r\n<p>i have one question though.. this is completely unrelated.  what is the deal with sexual fetishes involving martha stuart?  i just don\'t get it.\r\n</p>'),(606,'  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >This week i am missing two U2 concerts. but it\'s ok because \r\n                    PJ Harvey got sick and is not opening for them on these dates, \r\n                    ans seeing her was half of my interest in going.<br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    New Comic book Reading Habbits: <a href=\"http://www.fortunecity.com/tatooine/spielberg/97/images/Auth1.jpg\">The \r\n                    Authority</a>, <a href=\"http://www.lucifermorningstar.com/\">Lucifer</a>, \r\n                    Zero Girl, The Spectre.<br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    E-mails sent last month: 521+<br>\r\n                  </p>\r\n                  <p>[ The March Soundtrack Was ] <br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    God Was Drunk When He Made ( Jim White)<br>\r\n                    Around My Smile (Hope Sandoval & The Warm Inventions)<br>\r\n                    You\'re The One (Paul Simmon)<br>\r\n                    I Remember You ( Bjork)<br>\r\n                    Sunshine and Chocolate (Semisonic)<br>\r\n                    Living In Your Letters ( Dashboard Confesional)<br>\r\n                    Do you wanna hit it - (the Donnas )<br>\r\n                    History ( joseph arthur)<br>\r\n                    Music for Egon Schiele (The Rachels)<br>\r\n                    Came So Far For Beauty ( Leonard Cohen)<br>\r\n                    Piano Black ( Cowboy Bebop Soundtrack)</p>\r\n                  <p>Let me share some <b>[ Audio ] </b></p>\r\n                  <p>If you want to hear the Radiohead song \"Packt Like Sardines \r\n                    In A Crushd Tin Box\" go <a href=\"http://home.worldonline.nl/%7Ea3aan/atease-packtlikesardinesinacrushdtinbox.mp3\">here</a>.</p>\r\n                  <p>If you want to hear the R.E.M song \"Imitation of Life\" \r\n                    go <a href=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/01-r.e.m.-imitation_of_life.mp3\">here</a>.<br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    [Aquisitions] <br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    DVD (it\'s an addiction): Seven Samurai, Keeping The Faith, \r\n                    Sliding Doors, Cruel Intentions, Who Framed Roger Rabbit, \r\n                    Rearview Window,Small Time Crooks, Almost Famous, Wonderboys, \r\n                    Absolutely Fabulous Boxed Set, Vertigo, Gundam Wing: Endless \r\n                    Waltz, Mary Poppins, Batman Beyond: Return of the Joker, Cowboy \r\n                    Bebop Volumes 3 and 4, Dancer In The Dark, Romeo + Juliet, \r\n                    Dr strangelove, Jeff Buckley: Live Chicago, The fifth Element, \r\n                    Rear Window.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/606_1.jpg\'>'),(607,'<P>Has Organazine ruined my generation\'s best writers?  Through no fault of their own.  If a nineteen-year-old James Joyce could have instantly published his most lucid thoughts only moments after he had them, wouldn\'t he likely have done so?  Particularly if dozens of nineteen-year-old females would have heralded the accomplishment with e-proposals and jpegs of their breasts?  And they would have still been profound in their own poorly punctuated, drunkenly misspelled, and semi-complete way.  But how likely is it that he would ever have completed Ulysses?  Isn\'t this novel, to an extent, the product of Joyce\'s pre-internet necessity?  What if the only way you could get your own weblog highlights before empathic eyes was to craft them into a complete, cohesive, and clever novel? I\'ll bet that jamesjoyce.diaryland.com would certainly have had its moments, but would it necessarily have had more than elly.org?  Was Fitzgerald really *that* much wittier than Ben Brown and would he have labored his way through Gatsby if he were an \"Internet Rockstar?\"  Not criticizing, mind you, just wondering.</P>'),(608,'no'),(609,'<PRE>\r\ntoday time moves forward\r\noutside the wind blows hard\r\nfrom the west over earth\r\npushing time ahead an hour\r\ninside the blues plays easy\r\nlightning hopkins is blind\r\nhis guitar sees just fine\r\nand the cat yoko warms my legs\r\noutside elly hails a taxi\r\nshe will be here shortly\r\ninside peter lays on his bed\r\nstaring at a his laptop\r\ni bop my legs to the guitar\r\nyoko bops her head to my legs\r\ntoday is a sunday\r\n</PRE>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.blueflamecafe.com/Lightnin_Hopkins.html\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/609_1.jpg\' align=left alt=\"if you ever head out to west texas, do stop in abilene, some of the sweetest women i ever seen. -- lightnin\' never did see this picture of himself.  \"></a>\r\n<a href=\"http://elly.org\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/609_2.jpg\' alt=\"yoko is a lap warmer.\"></a>\r\n<P>maybe i will go to the dog eared bookstore for my earthy down home texas book about being old and needing change.</P>\r\n<P>i sent out a resume for a job in austin, texas.  what can i say?  a recruiter emailed me wanting me to apply for it.</P>\r\n<P>i have settled into being unemployed.  i am somewhat used to it now.  i have a little belly.  i still do not like spending my time wisely.  i do not like saying i am going to be somewhere.</P>\r\n<P>i say i am going to be somewhere and before you know it i am hurrying to get there from  some place else i said i was going to be.</P>\r\n<P>i\'ll pass on that.  i much prefer going slow.  that will all change when i get a job.  then i will become a person with somewhere to be, but until then.  i am lightnin\' nowhere swinney.</P>\r\n'),(610,'<P>twice, i was asked by s5 about why i wanted to move to san francisco.  once, before i moved here, on chat, and again, after i got here, in person.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>a reasonable question.  part of the answer lies in that i had simply grown bored in greensboro, nc, where i used to live.  the reasons for this boredom are varied but unimportant.  so, i was already moving, the question was just where.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'m not a total risk taker type.  or maybe i am sometimes, but i don\'t think i would move to a city that i had never visited, soley on secondhand knowledge.  so there were a handful of cities i had visited enough to consider moving there: dc, atlanta, raleigh, sf, maybe a few others</P>\r\n\r\n<P>my first visit to sf was in high school.  i came to livermore, to use the cray supercomputer at livermore lab as part of one of those silly smart kids programs.  we visited sf as one of the things thet they made us do to keep us busy enough so we wouldn\'t cause any trouble.  at the time i wasn\'t terribly impressed with sf, but i did pick up a small rock from the beach somewhere near the golden gate bridge.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then, later, several visits to sf after college: road trip, skiing, visiting (a few friends had moved to sf, or nearby), etc.  then, there was burning man 99.  bm99 caused more friends to move out to sf.  more visits.  then, bm2k.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>after all this visiting, and meandering with folks in sf, i came to realize a bunch of different things.  (pardon me, i have this thing about lists.  it\'s probably because i had my first computer when i was in third grade.) anyway, so the things, in no particular order:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>a handful of good friends of mine now appear to be living in sf.  other friends are theorizing about moving to sf.  people in sf just seem to be more open to ideas in general.  sf is closer to burning man than the east coast.  i seem to sorta know a handful of really cool people that i met through friends at burning man.  i am talking to those folks on icb all the time, getting further neat impressions of the city.  you don\'t need a car in sf.  the women in sf appear to be more interesting (everyone is, really).  you can go dancing every night if you so choose, in sf.  it\'s hilly in the city, and that\'s neat too.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and on and on.  i\'m sure that some of those are just romanticized observations that got amplified as some mystical, internal, non-logical part of my brain finally decided to just do it and move to sf.  i guess it also helped that some neat places to live became available.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>anyway, i\'m here now.  so, hi.  oh, and the rock i picked up when i was in high school, i brought it back with me.</P>\r\n'),(611,'<p>so jason was sitting on my couch and i had my head in his lap and we were watching tv.</p>\r\n<p>\"you could just. watch a lot of tv,\" he suggested.<BR>\r\n\"there\'s a modest mouse song that says \'<a href=\"http://www.crystal-night.com/~bwillen/MMLyricsMA.htm#ADI\">i\'m watching tv, i guess that\'s a solution.\'\"</a><BR>\r\n\"and center of the universe\'s \'<a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/builttospillpage/kilaslyrics.html\">just keep watching the tv</a>\'.\"<BR>\r\n\"yeah.\"<BR>\r\n\"indie rockers know everything.\"<BR>\r\n\"yeah.\"</p>'),(612,'<pre><tt>\r\n. chalk in the erasers<br>\r\n. \"kick me\" signs<br>\r\n. banana in the tailpipe<br>\r\n. whoopie cushions<br>\r\n. pulling the fire alarm<br>\r\n. turning all the desks around<br>\r\n. hot pepper flavored gumballs<br>\r\n. double-stick tape<br>\r\n</tt></pre>'),(613,'<P>Today, in the city of Auckland, it is raining. Heavily.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Like a good young thing, I finished my essays and trudged off to university on the bus to hand them in and attend an English lecture.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>After the lecture I bumped into boy-who-is-taking-the-same-papers-as-me and we talked about our essays and did a \"I\'ll let you read mine if I can read yours.\" It was good. And reassuring. He is older than most of the first-years too, so we talked about what we did with our years off and about how me n\' my boy got together and what we wanted to be doing when we were in highschool and how that has changed. He\'d been living all over the place and he told me about New York and made me want to go there.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Anyway, after all that - he offered me a RIDE HOME! My jeans were already soaking and my shoes were wet-through, so I was ever so grateful.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I got home with minimal rain-damage done and thanked him profusely. I ran inside to cuddle my lovely, warm, non-rained-on boyfriend and tell him how good it is to meet nice people</P>\r\n\r\n<P>It is hard living in a new city where you don\'t know anyone. Meeting people like this makes me smile.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Hoorah for nice people.</P>'),(614,'<p>Walking home tonight, wind blowing you around. Blowing your clothing in a rippling, billowing, flapping frenzy.</p>\r\n<p>You realize you know this way so well that your feet and legs do the things that they need to do in order to keep you upright and walking. They know this way almost by heart.</p>\r\n<p>Watch out for the trains, cars, drunks, and the hole in the street that you used to trip on.</p>\r\n<p>You look up and realize you dont remember the last 2 blocks you walked.</p>\r\n<p>Save for the new graffiti, the man who has taken residence in the newly painted and never used bus shelter, the new motorcycle parked on the sidewalk, the absence of scaffholding that has taken up a place on the sidewalk and your life for 4 months, the old white metal filing cabinet with sagging drawers that someone has left out for the world to take. You begin to realize that you have started to memorize the patterns of the mailmen and garbage men who must work along your route. You know which days are garbage days, where the mailman should be at what time of each day. You take note of the scraps of the new ATT/RCN cable and broadband internet network in big coils on the sidewalk, stopping briefly to examine the cable scraps. You notice someone has kicked over the pile of dirt that was between two telephone poles.</p>\r\n<p>There are strains of country-blues music in your head. You hum softly to yourself.</p>\r\n<p>Your walking is zenlike, calming. Your body moves with the ebb and flow of the city, and of the wind. You rotate your hips and push one leg forward, bringing it down heel first, while you tranfer your momentum and energy from one leg to the other, coming up on to the ball of your foot, rotating your hips, swinging the other leg forward, pushing with your toes and calves. Every part of your body is moving, looking, thinking, nothing is out of it\'s place. Nothing moves without affecting every other part. Your strides lengthen, and you realize that you are breathing hard. You push harder. You realize that walking like this can reflect any of your moods. When you are calm, your walking is more fluid, taking the paths that do not interrupt your flow of movement. When you are upset, you feel the force of each footfall shoot up your legs, into your pelvis and up your spine, released through your shoulderblades and arms. Tonight, your walking is a calm, rolling gait that has a long stride and an easy and putty-like loosening of the ankles and knees for quick direction change.</P>\r\n<p>You are wondering how you can express the things your head contains, you\'re trying to figure out how you can share the excitement with someone of the way these things feel.</p>\r\n<p><br><br>-yeti</p>'),(615,'<pre><tt>\r\ni saw him sitting on a bench\r\nfull of scraggly\r\n\r\ni was between here and there\r\nhe was homeless\r\nreading a book\r\n\r\neverything in a backpack\r\nno attachments\r\nno entanglements\r\n\r\nfree to wander between here and there\r\nand read a book on a bench\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n'),(616,'<P>Here I sit biting back against the waves of hysterical laughter that threaten to have me spew coffee all over my new shirt.I am reading all about P.J O\'Rourkes adventures in warzone tourism,he is a mad bastard. The faces around me all carry the same helpless and hopeless visage that my own must display after almost 24hrs nonstop traveling.</P>\r\n<P> I feel that the heat from my coffee cup is my only tenuous connection with ,its hot it tastes good and cost me 4 american dollars...I have no choice but to love it at that price.I can feel the jet lag like a monkey wearing bovver boots creeping up my back,my vision seems blurry as well, perhaps the dry air on the plane has permanently dehydrated my eyeballs.As the minute tick by it seems that the arrival of my last flight to Glasgow has been delayed for yet another hour,every minute is an eternity at this pointI have lost my sense of time here ,how long have I sat here at gate #7?  was there a reality called Austin?  or was it all some hallucination that my mind conjured up to pass the time.I only hope that Aaron can hold out as long as It takes.</P> '),(617,'<p>\r\ni have been atrocious to be around the last two days. just ask jeff. thank god my period finally started today.\r\n</p><p>\r\nfirst, i whined about the deskspace that he lovingly cleared for me in the spare room. it\'s not big enough! i don\'t have enough room! then i cried thinking about all the things i am going to have to throw out in order to move into his snug 2 bedroom apartment. i was inconsolate, even though he pointed out that i didn\'t need to do any of that, we could work it out once my stuff was here. jeff tells me reasonable things, and i became more unreasonable.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy little dinky turntable that i got at salvation army for 10 bucks! my records... my DESK. all the orderly piles that salve my sanity. my washcloth. i felt like i was losing my identity, no longer would i be able to come into the front door, put my mail down in the mail pile on my desk, sort it into trash and a follow-up pile, and then plop into my orange armchair and read a mystery novel. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni was an emotional mess, mentally paralyzed at the thought of moving 4 blocks down the street. endless crying and halfhearted efforts at explaining why i was mewling like a wet cat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut listen. it wasn\'t the \"stuff\". it was the way i had arranged everything in the space customized Just The Way I Liked It. i couldn\'t articulate this, though. adding to the confusion.</p><p>\r\nthankfully, the earth has made another revolution. today is like that last scene in heathers, when winona ryder comes out, a demon singed by her fate. okay. i will go get some breakfast. really, i am much better now, but if you see jeff today, give him a big hug for me, and be extra nice to him, he\'s earned it.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni was joking around yesterday about how people bond in times of stress. so every month, we go through a small war together, and emerge, shellshocked and numb, but clinging to each other as we survey the ravaged wasteland of what is left. ah, love.\r\n</p>'),(618,'<p>gone since thursday, back on sunday... I went to Scottsdale, AZ to visit Dad, step-mom, and 3yr old adopted brother that i have never seen. Lots and lots of tv, meat, alcohol, and boredom.</p>\r\n<p>saturday i went out with my old marine buddy, dan. It was his 30th birthday. Some dance club called buzzz. i sat in the corner, while dan and his friend grabbed ass (literaly, but not eachothers). the strange thing, the women liked it, thats how you pick up girls?... i was frightened, as none of my friends act like that, nor do they go places like that... dan is my friend, yes, but from another time in my life. (yes, in thailand) ...</p>\r\n<p>when my step-mom first saw me, with my beard, she told me i was unatractive. that is my relationship with her. i know it sounds trite to say that you dont get along with your step-parent, but it is true... my step-dad, on the other hand, fucking rules. </p>\r\n<p>the little brother stuff was a bit too much for me to fathom at this point in the game. he is three, annoying, bratty, and spoiled by the step-mom... what can i say? i would have loved to of grown up with my grandparents! My dad is 52, step-mom is 40-ish...</p>\r\n<p>did I tell you about the turtles? Yes, fucking turtles, big huge ones roaming around the house. it was too much for me...</p>\r\n<p>honestly, it was really good seeing my dad, I love him dearly... he is the only reason why I will go back there.</p>\r\n<p>as for the culture? here yah go, right out of a restroom outside of scottsdale...</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/618_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>i missed my family of friends here in austin, sf, mb, where ever else...</p>\r\n'),(619,'<p>\r\nlanded in sf on a beautiful day. i got excited before i even made it into the city, as far back as LA. jiggling in my seat and chanting \"i am from california i live in california i am from california\" over and over in my head. pressing my face against the window. \r\n</p><p>\r\nritualistic cab ride from airport. beauty and chaos of city rushing in at all sides.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncracked out, shower, sleepy urgent sex. sushi and beautiful clouds.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncoffee and bagels with weeb and swinney. new beginning in my old home: art classes and grown up errands. clean new kitchen, soup plans!\r\n</p><p>\r\nhome home home. future laid out. \r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nabout au i miss: seitan sandwiches, sleeping with tuggy, climbing in bed with o and xep, revolver thai food. trips to comic book shop. comfy house and cheap food. sitting on the kitchen counter crosslegged eating soy ice cream.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(620,'i never talk about this part of my life with anyone except my sister.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\'t think i\'ve ever really told these stories to my husband, and certainly not on purpose to my kids.  i\'m not sure why, but probably because they are so personal, i never had words for them.<br><br>\r\nnow, decades later, i find that they may have some value for those who wonder about what it\'s really like to be homeless.<br><br>\r\nthe first time i found myself without a roof over my head to call my own i must have been about five years old.  my mother had just given birth to my baby brother, johnny, and since she was separated from my father she was in a bad way emotionally.  she actually gave my brother to my aunt for several months while she decided what to do, and i moved in with my next door neighbor, debbie gaventa.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\'t know how long i was there.  i remember wondering if my family was dead and hearing the gaventa mother talking about me to other people and being afraid to eat anything she cooked.  i still don\'t like to eat at other people\'s houses.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthat period was the precursor of many more prolonged instances of being without a roof of my own, some of the stories are worse than others, all of them are a direct result of my parents getting divorced and using me as a pawn in their battle for one upmanship.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou have to understand that my natural father, who i shall call carl in all these stories, was an unstable alcholic who was also one of the most manipulative human beings i have ever known, and in spite of all that, i loved him so much it hurt, which is why he was able to hurt me so many times until i got old enough and strong enough to stop him.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i was nine years old, in the 4th grade, my mother, unable to support the four of us without the child support my father was supposed to pay, decided that she would put us in a children\'s home while she sorted things out.<br><br>\r\nthis was her solution.  she thought anything was better than letting us live with my father, who, in spite of his craziness, did love us very much.<br><br>\r\nshe sent my baby brother to my aunt jackie, and mikie (age 11), inky (age 6), and i went to live in the Florida Methodist Children\'s Home in Enterprise, Florida.<br><br>\r\nyou have to understand that there was no reason for this to happen.  my paternal grandfather was a millionaire.   he could have taken us if he had wanted to, but there were issues that i don\'t understand, so off we went.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i left for the children\'s home, i had only heard the word fuck one time.  my friend diane sullivan told me she thought it meant taking your clothes off. <br><br>\r\n\r\n i was only there a few days before i learned exactly what it did mean.  this is because the other children in the home were there at the order of the courts, not because somebody elected for them to go there.<br><br>\r\n\r\n  the girls in my dorm had all been sexually abused or beaten, or had parents in jail, or all of the above. <br><br>\r\n   it was a rough introduction to the real world.  i had grown up with nannies and maids and always been perfectly protected from any suggestions of ugliness.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy roommate was 8 years old and she had some sort of std.  she would go into the closet at night and put on some mysterious cream to make the sores go away.  she was much shorter than me and probably only weighed 50 pounds.  it was years before i realized what the significance of her cream was.... i was a total innocent.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe day i arrived the housemother took all my clothes away, she said they were too fancy.  she then gave me some dresses that had been donated by the church and wrote my initials in everything, dresses, underwear and socks, \"<strong>AG</strong>\" in big black indelible magic marker.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey took all my toys away.  all of them.  even my books.  i don\'t know what happened to them.  i suspect that the toys and clothes were sold at a church sale, or maybe given to someone in the church who could appreciate girls clothing that had been purchased at the poshest kids specialty shops in tampa, florida.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni lived in a dormitory called west hardin.  it was joined by a connecting door to a dorm called east hardin where my sister was placed.  i was not allowed to speak to her or see her.  we had \"boundaries\" and could not pass them without permission.<br><br>  if we did, we would be beaten by the housemother.  she didn\'t spank, she punched us in the stomach.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy mother, unknown to me, was hospitalized with a nervous breakdown the day we went to the home.  i didn\'t see her for several months, i don\'t remember how long.  she was allowed to visit us on sundays from 2pm to 4pm and when she came, she looked like a stranger, and the only one of us she was really worried about was my baby sister, inky.  by the time she showed up, inky was so damaged it couldn\'t be fixed.  the girls in inky\'s dorm were wards of the court, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni quickly learned to hide.  i stayed to myself as much as possible and found secret places to go within boundaries that insured i would not have to deal with the other girls.  because of this, they made me their victim.  they stole gifts that my family sent me, stole money from me, hit me, taunted me, etc.<br><br>\r\nthe worst thing they did was when they would steal something of mine, then go to the housemother in a group and claim that i had stolen it from them.  it didn\'t matter what i said, the housemother was not going to believe me.  i was branded a thief, which is something that was so traumatic, i just have no words for it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe all ate in a big dining room and i was always a picky eater.  the one food i have never been able to abide was any sort of eggs that are not scrambled very well.  every morning we would have fried eggs and the housemother would hold my mouth open and shove the eggs down my throat and then i would puke them up on the floor, and then after breakfast i would have to clean up the puke before i could go to school.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen we got to school, we were the children\'s home kids. we had to wear the same dress for two days in a row because of laundry rules, no matter how dirty it got, and the other kids knew this, so they threw things on us that stained.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere are so many things.  i was there from march 27th 1963 until august 4th 1964. shit happened.  some of it is too hard to put into words.  most of it i have mercifully blocked from my memory.  when we got out of the home, i had no memory of my mother prior to the visitations on sundays.  it was 30 years before any of it began to come back.  i was very, very angry with her for doing that to us.  now i know that she did what she had the tools to do.  when she got better tools, she did a better job, but she and i were never close until about 5 years ago when i decided that the anger was hurting me, not her.  she had rewritten the history of that time into a form she could deal with.  i made a decision to forgive her and i did.  i am glad i did, because as an adult, she is a very good mother to me.  maybe god knew i would survive and needed a mother more now than i did then.  i don\'t know.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis is very long.  part two is about when i lived in a car for months with my five brothers and sisters.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni will write it tomorrow, maybe, i find this harder to do than i thought it would be.<br><br>\r\n'),(621,'i remembered some more things that were important about the children\'s home and i thought i would write them down while i was still feeling slightly brave.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere were some good things.  the methodist church people wanted to make this a nice place for kids, so they built things like a swimming pool and an outdoor skating rink, and they bought two limousines and a bus to tote the kids around in.  the limos were really long station wagons with 5 seats in them so lots of kids could ride.  and we had bicycles to share among the dorms, including bicycles built for two.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe had activity schedules for every dorm.  each dorm got a chance to go swimming or skating or ride bicycles or go to the craft barn (a real barn) and make ceramics and stuff at set times on particular days.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe were expected to do all the housework ourselves, including kitchen work and clean up.  they had an industrial kitchen and each of us had kitchen duty that might be setting the tables for the little girls, peeling potatoes for the older kids, mopping the floors, running the industrial dishwasher, waxing the stairs, cleaning the bathrooms, etc.<br><br>\r\n\r\ntheir were weird things.  every night before we went to bed we had to put our dirty underwear outside our bedroom doors on the floor and one girl would come by and count to make sure everyone changed their drawers.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere were some things that give me joy now.  we used to sing a lot.  all sorts of songs.  we would sing while we worked, or sing while we did our homework, or in the bus on the way to church.  i still sing to myself when i am working and i still sing when i am in the car on a trip.  my kids never minded i couldn\'t carry a tune.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe memorized bible verses for \"tickets,\" just like in tom sawyer.  if you saved enough tickets you got a bible.  i still have my bible.  my favorite verse was always...<br><br>\r\n\r\n\"<a href=\"http://bible.gospelcom.net/cgi-bin/bg?passage=JOHN+14&language=english&version=NKJV&showfn=off\">in my fathers house are many mansions.  i go to prepare a place for you...</a>\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nand <a href=\"http://bible.gospelcom.net/cgi-bin/bg?passage=Matthew+5:1-12\">the beatitudes</a>.  blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth...<br><br>\r\n\r\nand there were scary things.  like miss dena, who was a substitute housemother, and she was a bull dyke who was evil and hated children and she would beat us about the head and shoulders  with the side of her arm for the slightest infraction and none of the adults believed us because they didn\'t want to.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe had to go to a counselor every week and his name was mr. wilson and he was ineffectual and totally non-intuitive.  he never believed anything i said and put words into my mouth that were not there.  he believed that i was a thief which i never forgave him for and i always said that someday i would go to that children\'s home and look at my files and set the record straight.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut i never have.  i started to one time, but i couldn\'t do it.  my sister says that this year she is going to make me do it and we will go together and put the beast to rest at last.  maybe.  i dunno.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe had volunteer counselors in the summer.   one was named dotty argo and she was miss st. petersburg and had red hair and she was the kindest person and i loved her.  now i think back i realize that the summers, when the counselors were there, were the best, because they were too young to think evil thoughts about us.  they wanted us to be all that was bright and beautiful as they were.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere was a boy there named daryl ullery and he had a long scar on his chest from open heart surgery, and the year after we left the home we got a letter saying daryl had died from tuberculosis and we needed to get checked for it.  the medical care was so poor, i am not surprised that this could happen, but how could nobody have noticed a child had tb so bad that he would die before they took him to the doctor?<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe children sexually abused each other.  this is something i have a hard time talking about because i never realized what it was until i was in my late teens.  those of us who would not participate in were shunned.  i didn\'t understand what they were doing, not for years later.  now i do and i wonder how much farther they went when the \"babies\" like me were not around.  we had so little supervision, it\'s hard to say.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere was this one girl, she was so beautiful, and she had lived in the home all of her life just about, as had her sisters and brothers.  as each of them turned 18 they had to leave and go elsewhere.  the people at the home put them in some sort of ywca type place in sanford, florida, and there they were, with no skills and no college to look forward to and no support system.  the boys joined the military.  now i look back i wonder how it happens that a child is suddenly tossed out alone like that, and i realize it happens every day to kids in foster care.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere should be scholarships for kids in situations like this.  not necessarily college scholarships, but scholarships for the time when they are still in school, like in junior high, that would provide for them the lessons that other kids get as a matter of fact -- piano and art and horseback riding and camp and all the places that kids go and make contacts that see them through their adulthood and provide them lifelong friends.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere are no personally owned bicycles or computers or musical instruments or clothes that are not used or bought out of season in discount stores sales racks or dogs or cars or driving lessons for kids in childrens homes.  there are no letters to santa that are answered and believed in.  birthdays and dreams and futures aren\'t tolerated.  only the stark reality of a life that will be filled with waitress jobs and day labor and endless struggle to catch up to the lower middle class.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am lucky i survived.  i am not sure how i did.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i grew up and married i told my husband that i would never tell my kids i had been in a childrens home until they were 18 and old enough to understand.  everyone thought this was because i was ashamed of being there.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\'m not ashamed, i never was ashamed. maybe i was ashamed on behalf of my mother, though.<br><br>\r\n\r\n i didn\'t want to tell them because i wanted them to never know that their grandmother was so stupid.  i knew that they would not understand how a mother could put her child in such a situation, especially since i could not understand it myself.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni wanted my kids to have a grandmother who worshipped them like the grandmothers in stories, but that didn\'t exactly happen.  she loves them, that is true, but she doesn\'t have the courage that was needed to be a really good grandmother.<br><br>\r\n someday i hope to be a grandmother and when that happens i intend to be the very best grandmother in the whole world and i sure hope god lets me live that long.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhenever i related any experiences that happened at the childrens home they were good ones and i referred to that time as when i was away at a methodist boarding school.<br><br>\r\n\r\n  now i realize that my mother did this because she really was so innocent and protected herself, she just had no idea what she was sending us to.  she must have thought that the childrens home would be like a long sunday school camp and we would be loved and protected and she really believed that was better than us living with carl.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think that at some point she realized what she had done, but then she had this compulsion, as she still does, to find a way to justify and rationalize it to herself so she could live with it, so she rewrote the history of what happened and says that we had to go there because she had a nervous breakdown.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe truth is that she started planning for us to go there nearly a year before we actually left.  mr. wilson the counselor came to our house and told us  about how they had horses to ride and it would be so much fun.  i remember that because i always wanted to ride a horse.  and when  we got there, the horses had been sold the year before we arrived.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think that my mother will never forgive herself for doing this to us, but she also doesn\'t really want to know the truth of how it was because if she knew it would make her crazy for sure.  i will never tell her.  never.  she wouldn\'t have done it if she had really understood.<br><br>\r\n\r\nof course so many times the anger of divorce causes parents to do terrible things to children in a battle for love and this was just the first one for me.<br><br>'),(622,'<p>we pull into a wendy\'s. i get peeved for a second (i am a vegetarian) but realize that i really did feel like some heavy french fries and vegetables coated in dressing. the car stops, and as i reach into my bag to pull out a 10 dollar bill, you tell me that there is a vegetarian restaurant up the block, and to come back when i am done. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni slam the door to your car when i get out, and stomp up noisy austin streets, and am so upset that i don\'t even notice when i walk into the wrong restaurant. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen i come back, you are happily chatting on your cell phone. how was veggie heaven, you ask. it was great, i say. i light a cigarrete and turn away. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(623,'<P>Listening to Mojave 3 but feeling strangely energetic. Second wind has hit the fan and im hyper off kix and doritos. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI have a stalker ladies and gentlemen. Well, not a stalker, but an admiror it seems. He keeps staring at me which has lead for me to christen him the cleverly obvious nickname of Staring Boy. Staring boy isnt half bad looking but hes short and blasts the new daft punk cd from his room. red flags pop up and hes not for me.\r\n</P><P>\r\nso, i talk to this kid, and he walks away faster than one can say \"you suck A1 sauce, bitch\". granted, he was probably drunk and a-social on both occations, but they were parties, and even though i did mock trial for 2 years i dont feel like cross examining _my_ stalker, thank you very much.\r\n</P><P>\r\nso, im thinkning of confronting him next time his midget ass sashes in front of me on my way to class... which may probably be tomorrow. yeah.\r\n</P><P>\r\ncambio y fuera.\r\n</P>'),(624,'<p>\r\nstaring at my camera bag i was going up an elavator very tired, very tired. the winter garden theatre in toronto was built in 1913 and has a lot of charm and character. Its shape, smell, and brick suited the cold post winter rain. the people who worked there were gruff but very nice. they answered questions i had about the \"pipes\". I laughed when one of the crew members told me we were in the \"hemp room\".i made up for the laugh by telling him i knew it was because the ropes were made of hemp. we talked of the union and how it worked. it made me happy. he was proud of his work and proud of \"his\" union. it was pure. while the utopia of my morning was all fine and dandy i knew the foot was off the gas when i was introduced to the sound tech. the negative, un compromisable old wreched sound tech. the man who had not one solution to his many many problems. \"these mic\'s are really good for film, but not for live, i cant get the gain up.\" In a pleasant and mild mannered voice i returned something to the effect of:</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nwell if you had told us we needed to rent an equilizer you should have. we called and asked if there was anything else we needed to bring. Im sure renting an equilizer for 600 wouldnt be a problem seeing as how we have over one hundred and fifty thousand rented right now. im sure a man of your seniourity can be of some help in this situation. the micron mics are fine. they are the best. we need to get this thing solved now.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<P>I continued by telling him of our situation and how much we needed him to start thinking.</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nwe have eight people coming. three people have cancelled. eight of canada\'s most popular stars coming in. the twelve hundred people who are already let down due to the fact that three people cancelled out on them are going to want to know why a man who is making six thousand dollars for six hours work cant get 8 of the best mics working.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>he grumbled and went on break.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na good part of the afternoon was downtime. I didnt actually know what i was what the hell was going on. i felt like a some sort of groupie who infiltrated the green room. I picked up a program. i figured id need to know that i was there for.</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<i><b>An Evening With Funny Canadians</i></b>\r\n<br>\r\na scholarship benefit for prospective <a href=\"http://comedy.humberc.on.ca/\">Humber School of Comedy</a> students.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>My mom is the assistant director of the Humber Comedy School and the <a href=\"http://www.humberc.on.ca/~writers\">Humber School for Writers</a>. she has worked for humber for 27 years and im very proud of her. she loves her job. :)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n...continued from program.</P>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nFeaturing: mike myers, mark breslin(owner of yuk yuks), robin duke(sctv, snl), dave foley(kith, newsradio), eugene levy(cancelled), kevin mcdonald(kith), debra mcgrath(cancelled), mary walsh(cancelled), colin mochrie(drew cary, whose line), and hosted by ralph benmergui(big cbc(canadian broadcasting corporation) dude)).   \r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>there were these two women who were working for the program on a student work/study thing. angela was 18 fresh out of highschool loud annoying weird gay phat panted danced infront of the cannon 8mm and on stage singing and dancing i dont hate her she just shouldnt have been backstage. laura was 22 and pretty hot. she was mature and mild mannered. i think we flirted but word hasnt come back on that. im in ottawa. mild crush. she has an incredibly hot ass. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(*self inflicted autosave in notepad. i started this onehourandfortyeight minutes ago. ive taken breaks and naps and picture breaks. brb actually.)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had thought people would show up in exact opposite order as they appeared on the program. me and the stage door dude laughed about that over cancer. it didnt happen but it was sorta close in a sorta close as you can get in an extremely positive kinda close i lost track. so people show up and dave foley and mike myers are late. they show up in a white suv. bigup i say! the rest showed up in streches. the fan would mob those. the white suv might just be one of the many drops we got(later). i scored a walkie talkie off the front door guy and used it as much as possible. I love them. I just love them. hahaha. im a nerd. fuck off. I hung downstairs and basically informed the whole network of talkies of who was arriving with who and who was getting passes. I had a pocket full of them. I scored them early, taking responsibility. har.\r\n</P><blockquote>\r\non a side note. i would like <a href=\"http://elly.org\">le</a> to send me my horochartstargalaxymoonjupiterthingy for april02, thanks!\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\ni stood outside in the cold and basically chatted with laura and the door man. fans would try and suck up to me and some <a href=\"http://www.nationalpost.com\">national post</a> reporter got a few leaks of fiction from me. it was hillarious. mike and dave finally showed up late kinda drunk i think with a few extra friends. i got the friends in first seeing as how mike and dave were handling the mob in fine form. I didnt even notice kevin mcdonald and his very hot gf breigh anne(sp?) over in the corner handling the suprisinglytallweirdlooking fools. I grabbed his gf and got her with mike and daves friends. hooked the driver of the suv up with parking and got(in order of appearance haha) kevin, dave, mike,  all the friends, relatives, gf\'s/wife\'s, and laura into the elevator.\r\n</p> <blockquote>\r\nigaveoutpassesandgotnamesandshitforsecurityhookedshituponthe\r\ntalkieitwassofunlaurawastryingtogetmikeonthe8andhehidbehindwife\r\nsayingnoarchivingnoarchivingshebackeddowndistilldownthinkshe \r\nshouldhavewefinallyreachedthesixthfloor\r\n</blockquote\r\n</P><p>\r\neveryone shmoozed while i took pics, my shutter speed was fucking up because i hate flash and light was weird. my mom purchased 400 instead of the 800 i had asked for. a minor annoyance i didnt really care about. i got the artists out of small talk conversations and got them to take pics with other artists who i was inadveredly(sp?) getting out of other small talk conversations and off to where the light was good. it worked a lot.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey got ready to enter the stage like a heard of sheep. you could tell they were all nervous because it wasnt scripted. It was strange to me how so many of them were nervous, yet known for their improv. i entered the house for the first time and smiled when i saw the full room. the place was standing room only. the wintergarden theatre was beautifully decorated and had many remnents of its past magnificantly restored and there for everyone to see. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe show was great. it was funny and got very political. they talked about canadian culture and identity. they dissed americans and the crowd laughed and it was all just fun and games. most of the artists left right after the show and things wound down. the room emptied and the room felt so much colder. a clean draft whirling around the dead air. it had been a good night. i took part in co-ordinating something pretty important and big.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(625,'<p>cause i really like kissin. and i like to make my kisses drip like molasses. and its kinda hard to do that to myself.</p>'),(626,'<p>So, I\'m an austinite. (I meet austin-the-person once at MoJos, over coffee with Lynn.)  I\'m a stone\'s throw from getting my BSc chem (5 hours, so close yet such a huge mountain of work between here and point B).  I like austin-the-city, but Texas is starting to get to me.  I was born in Houston, grew up in a little shithole called Nacogdoches in the buttass-end of east texas.</p>\r\n<p>So I\'m wondering, for those of you that have lived in Austin, what cities would be cool to move too?  I\'ve heard SF is really expensive to live in, but I don\'t know any real data.  Somehow I suspect that melbourne is a bit of a leap, but what the hell, I\'m thinking about London anyway.  A chemical industry isn\'t neccessary, I\'m more into computers now (and I have some experience to back me up in that arena, about 18 months wortj of paid exp.)</p>\r\n<p>There\'s just too many bad memories here for me.  I need to get out.</p>\r\n<p>(This is my first journal entry btw so please forgive me any screw ups.)</p>'),(627,'<p>\r\nGreetings neighbors from the swinneyhood -\r\ni would like to thank y\'all for sending postive energies and kind thoughts across the sea to help us win the bid on that theater.  Our proposal was a tremendous success. Maybe too successful.  i asked you for magic assistance in the hope that i could increase the chances of tipping the scales in our favor. It seems we flipped them all the way over. They loved our proposal. The combined power of the document and the postive wishes worked.  Worked very well. Created a bit of a stir in the office. So much so in fakt that they have decided to keep the space for themselves.\r\nit\'s been vacant for 14 months but now......\r\nWell....It turns out the head of prague 7 office dabbles in the theatrical arts. The Major, as he is called, unabashedly told us that we won the bid\r\nand beat out all the other competion but that in the end he has decided that they will not offer the space at all. He and a friend will rent it from the city themselves..and open a community theater.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHe fingered our proposal and smiled.\r\n</p>\r\nmr. smith\r\n<p>\r\nplaying with magik is a tricky endeavour.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(628,'<p>\r\nthis is a list of things i have to do today. they are in no particular order and if i wasn\'t so lazy, i would put them through my randomizer to make them even more random than the randomness that my brain outputs. the list:\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>drive to gresham(45min)</li>\r\n<li>return flash5 to compusa</li>\r\n<li>drive to aloha(45min)</li>\r\n<li>wish that joins where possible where they are not</li>\r\n<li>retrieve libranet cd from dad</li>\r\n<li>let dogs out to pee and poop</li>\r\n<li>drive to jantzen beach(40min)</li>\r\n<li>move domian names to register.com</li>\r\n<li>stare at old broken modem on desk</li>\r\n<li>burn copy of titanic soundtrack for girlfriends student teaching</li>\r\n<li>change hosting companies</li>\r\n<li>see if purchases over internet have arrived at parents</li>\r\n<li>drive to gresham(40min)</li>\r\n<li>eat broccoli with rice cheese</li>\r\n<li>smoke cigarette(repeated roughly eight times)</li>\r\n<li>install libranet at home</li>\r\n<li>contemplate my new project</li>\r\n<li>think about a way to get money</li>\r\n<li>sleep</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nand that is about it. oh, and by the way, fuck compusa.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbunch of fuckers.\r\n</p>'),(629,'the next summer after we left the childrens home, my mother agreed to let carl take us to michigan for three weeks.  we were supposed to be back before school started.  i am not sure why she changed her mind about us spending time with our father, but i suspect it was because she had spent almost a year learning what the childrens home had done to us.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen carl arrived with his new wife and daughter, we were very surprised that they also had a new son, who was only a few weeks old.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey had a new station wagon and the baby was in a bassinette against one door, the back end was full of boxes.  they were also hauling a uhaul trailer.<br><br>\r\n\r\nour half-sister tina was 3 and she sat up front, the four of us, mikie 13, me 11, inky 8, and johnny 6, shared the back seat with the bassinette.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbefore we got out of town good, carl told us we were not going to michigan, we were going to tennessee  he did not have a job, and little money. <br><br>\r\n\r\nbefore we left florida, we had stopped in a dozen or so gas stations.  carl would go to the back of the uhaul and carry something in to the station we could not see, then he would come back to the car and take a drink of liquor out of the half-pint of corby\'s he kept on the seat.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe finally realized he was selling stuff to the gas station guys.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe ended up in morristown, tn, sharing an apartment in the projects with the sister of carl\'s stepmother and their three kids.  it was the summer of paul revere and the raiders.  they had a teevee.  every day we went out to the hills behind the projects with bologna sandwiches and picked blackberries.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin the evenings the adults would drink moonshine and dance with friends and we would dance, too and sing all the old songs on the back porch.... <br><br>\r\n\r\n&quot;chicken crowin\' on sourwood mountain, hey de ing day, diddle all the day...<br>\r\nso many pretty girls i can\'t count them, hey de ing day, diddle all the day...&quot;<br><br>\r\n\r\nafter a time carl rented a farmhouse in whitesburg,tn. from a woman named arterburn.  he told her he had two kids and hid us.  it was a game.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe were hungry most of the time.  we ate pancakes and biscuits and cane syrup a lot.<br><br>\r\n\r\ncarl would send mikie into the corn fields at night to steal corn, and he sent us into mrs. arterburn\'s root cellar to steal jars of canned vegetables.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsue would go to the grocery store and write hot checks and they would go to rufus moyers who lived in a schoolbus in the middle of the junkyard and buy moonshine and then all the adults and cousins would come over and start drinking and dancing on friday night and party until sunday and we kids would run wild through the hills and catch fireflies and laugh at the drunks.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfinally the partying got out of hand, i guess, and carl decided it was time to move on.  here is where i get confused.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think carl took us back to our mother at the end of the summer, because i remember starting the 7th grade.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut when early spring came, we went with carl again. this time we were supposed to live with him and go to school.  i think that my mother had a boyfriend she wanted to marry.  i am not sure.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis time we lived in the car for six weeks.  it was cold, really cold.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe slept in the car, but not at rest stops.  carl was afraid the police would bother him in rest stops, so he would find dirt roads off the main highway and we would sleep there.<br><br>\r\n\r\none night we got the trailer stuck in a muddy cornfield because it thawed overnight.  the mud was deep and like ice.  carl made us get out of the car and help push and pull the trailer.  the mud was up to our shins and we only had tennis shoes to wear.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe pulled on that trailer for hours and cried while we did it and then carl said we had to use the vector effect to get it out and showed us how to push the hitch to make that work.  we had to do it ourselves because the trailer was so overdue by this time it counted as stolen and if the police came they would arrest him, so we worked on it until the early hours of the morning and we finally got it out.<br><br>\r\n\r\ncarl found a roadside motel with little cottages that he had heard about from a trucker and there was an unlocked door and we snuck inside and all took a hot bath.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe next day he found a bunch of gas stations and sold all of our toys and books for food money.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe day after that he took us to the projects and opened up the back of the uhaul and sold our clothes and everything else he could for a nickel and a dime and a quarter and a dollar.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin the mornings sue, our stepmother, would get milk out of the cooler and pour cereal in a plastic bowl and that was breakfast.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat lunchtime she would get bread and bologna out of the cooler and that was what we had for supper, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit took some time to get to illinois, and we ended up in a farmhouse where sue\'s brother billy lived with his wife nedra and her two kids, ouida and gibbs.  they lived upstairs, we lived downstairs.  carl got a job in rockford.  there wasn\'t enough to eat.  billy and nedra both worked, and when they got paid they took food and toys upstairs but didn\'t share with us. <br><br> \r\n\r\none time all we had to eat for two days was a box of saltine crackers and some peanut butter.  carl decided to leave after a few months but he didn\'t tell billy and nedra, he just waited until they left for work and packed the car.  i think he took some stuff from there to sell, because we had to get in the car and wait while he loaded the uhaul and he stopped at a lot of gas stations as he moved north.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe ended up in monroe, wisconsin where he rented a house with a hot check on the main highway that was 100 years old.  we didn\'t have any furniture.  he got some furniture blankets from somewhere, i guess he stole them, and we made pallets on the floor.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe made us go to school.  i had three dresses and no coat, only a white summer weight wool blazer.  we had no refrigerator, we put the cold stuff in a cooler outside the back door in the snow.<br><br>\r\n\r\nafter a while he couldn\'t pay the rent and got worried about the people in town getting too curious about us, so we packed up in the middle of the night and left again.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe made us call our mother and tell her we lived in a nice house with nice furniture and we loved school.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni remember thinking that i would never be able to have any friends because i had this list of lies i had to tell when people asked me things like where was i from and could i come over and visit them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe went to lodi, wisconsin and he rented a fancy house with another hot check and it was empty, too, and we told people the moving company was on strike.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni was 12 then.  it was a hard way to live.  carl and sue started fighting a lot and i never saw him hit her, but i think i knew that he did.<br><br>\r\n\r\npretty soon he snuck us out of there in the middle of the night, too. we went to rome, wisconsin and lived in a trailer for a week until he thought the landlady was being too suspicious, and we left there in the middle of the night and he stole everything in the trailer that would fit in the uhaul and sold it on our way to stevens point, wisconsin.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe lived there for a couple weeks and then he must have decided it was too hard, and he took us back to our mother and left us alone for a while.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe hard thing to explain is that during all this time he manipulated us constantly in psychological ways so that we never thought he was evil, what we thought was that the world had done him wrong and his father, our grandfather especially, who was a millionaire and refused to give him any money.<br><br>\r\n\r\nall this time our mother refused to say anything really bad about him -- she told me years later that she thought it would be worse for us to grow up hating him because hate will eat you away into an evil person yourself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe said the reason our grandfather wouldn\'t give him any money was because carl had embezzled so much money from his business over the years he felt like it was a lost cause.<br><br>'),(630,'the hardest thing to explain about the rather sordid tale of my life is that i actually lived a double life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy mother was from an old florida family.  my aunt was, in fact, the mayor of pompano beach, fl for many years.  my maternal grandfather was a respected city father.<br><br>\r\n\r\ncarl\'s father was the son of a doctor.  he owned the largest wholesale fishing tackle business in the southeast for many years, and also owned a boat company that built single hull wooden boats, it was famous.<br><br>\r\n\r\non the one hand i was a child of privilege.  my grandparents dressed us in clothing from the posh children\'s shops and bought our toys from the best stores.  we had upscale pediatricians and dentists and a maid and a nanny and we took lessons of all sorts like wealthy kids do.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy mother was very proper in an old south way and we had myriad rules of etiquette to follow, which we did, without question.  always wear white gloves to church and when going downtown, carry a nice hanky at all times, say yes maam and no maam and don\'t say bad words and be nice to poor people and bigotry is not acceptable and sunday school always and thou shalt not steal or lie or talk back to your elders.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand then when we were with carl he <em>said</em> the same rules, but there were special ones for him because he had been <em>wronged</em> by his family and society for no good reason.  and you have to know that carl is/was a genius.  he had helped design the motorola cross something teevee antenna and he was a teevee repairman because electronics came to him like breath, but he was mostly a con artist and charming scoundrel some of the time and an alcoholic whiner some of the time, and in the middle of all that he could make you laugh and love him and forgive him for anything, but you had to follow the special rules about why it was okay to lie and cheat and steal and his whole thing was that it was okay to steal from rich people but not from poor people.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe had this litany about how you have to have money to make money and if you don\'t have any you will always be poor and since his father refused to give him what he was due, that was why he was forced to be a fly-by-night rip off artist.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand when he was charming you could forgive him anything.  he was, like all alcholics, always sorry for the paths of destruction he left in his wake, and you wanted to forgive him if you could, and until i was married and had a child of my own, i always did forgive him.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen we were with mother we were tiny little debutantes and young gentlemen and when we were with carl we were malnourished criminals in training.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe were taught never to trust any adult who asked us a question because all of them were probably out to take us away from him and put us in a childrens home.  we didn\'t need to hear more than that.  nothing was as bad as being in a childrens home.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand this first summer in the car as a nomad was not the last, not by a long shot. there were years more to go.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni lost count of all the places we lived but i remember some of the times when he made us do the worst things or know about the worst things. <br><br>\r\n\r\n like when we lived at big fish lake, mn in a cottage owned by felix and irene who owned a granite quarry in st.  cloud.  they were truly nice people and they invited us for dinner and wanted to befriend us and carl ripped them off and left in the middle of the night after he wrote bad checks to all their friends in town and i was 14 then and just beginning to hate him because he had begun to be sorry, i think, for the way he was, and he hated himself and didn\'t know how to change.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand when he pushed me into a goodwill box in the middle of the night to steal stuff and then the police came and he left me there for hours until it was safe to come back and he wouldn\'t let me out until i pushed all the stuff out first.  and then we went to the projects and sold the clothes out of the back of the car and all i could think about was the poor people who needed those clothes for free and the people with no hands who worked at the goodwill and needed those jobs and it didn\'t really matter to him because i was to do what he said to do.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand yet, in the middle of all this trauma he would do something wonderful and then i would forget the bad stuff.  like when he took my cousin evonda to the store and bought her the very first store bought clothes she ever had because she was such a pitiful thing and he said that vonda needed a flower for her soul more than we needed dinner and vonda was 13, too, so i knew he was right.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis double life went on until i was 15 and then i just couldn\'t do it anymore.  years of half a year with mother and regular rules and half a year with carl and total madness.  and then i met a boy and fell in love.  and i realized that someday i would not be a child unable to make my own decisions and i decided that now was as good a time as any and i got back to my mothers house that summer and left with carl again because i wanted to see the boy, and then in the fall i went back to my mother\'s until february and then i packed my bags and became an emancipated minor.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni worked two jobs and went to school and stayed on the honor roll and lived with a succession of friends for about a year.  then i got cancer.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni moved back home and got the cancer taken out and then i left again as soon as i was well, and lived with my friends again.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i was 18 and 4 months old, i met mark.  that was when my life really began.  he had lived through childhood trauma of his own, not nearly as severe as mine, of course, since his parents were still married and loved each other and him, but he had moved away from home at 15, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe found something in each other that filled the missing parts and we knew immediately that god meant us to be together and so, when we were both not quite 19 years old, we got married.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand nothing in my life has ever been more important than doing whatever it took to stay in love with mark and he with me and if i get run over by a truck tomorrow it will be okay because i know that god has given me a gift that few people get -- unconditional love from my husband.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<strong><em>the thing that i find the most interesting about writing these stories is that there have been very few comments when you consider the volatile content.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnobody really has any words to say when confronted by a truly sordid reality.<br><br></em></strong>\r\n\r\nthis is how it should be.  no child should live this sort of life.   too many do.  children who learn to lie as easily as they play and fool all the adults around them in order to protect what family they have.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy sister and i sometimes wonder how we survived.  she is more bitter than i am.  she hates carl and i think she should feel great pity for him, which i do.  my sister never had children.  she was married once for 10 years to a stupid man who was more her brother than her husband and then she couldn\'t abide that.  she will never really repair the damage, she wasn\'t old enough to know how to build a strong enough shield when it began.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni was lucky, because god and mark saved me from bitterness and a lifetime of anger and repeating the sins of the fathers -- because i had children.  children, if you value them correctly, have a way of giving you a perspective on the world that can save you from yourself if you let it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey don\'t have to be your own children.  children can be saved from despair and desperation by strangers who commit random acts of kindness.  they can be saved by teachers who recognize a need for applause at small victories.  they can be saved by aunts and uncles and next door neighbors who stop and listen when they don\'t really want to waste the time to do so.  really listen.  and answer.  and look for a need that they can fill,. if it\'s a book or a trip to the movies or a walk through the park or whatever it might be. <br><br>\r\n\r\n children need dreams to get them to their futures.  if you don\'t allow them to dream, they will die on the vine, just as surely as if they were watermelon vines in a drought.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni had books and songs and god to get me through until it was time to meet mark. <br><br> some kids don\'t have anything, and you could be the one to provide it. <br><br> if you make the time.  if you take the chance.<br><br>\r\n'),(631,'<pre>\r\nwhat is on my desk:\r\n\r\n(1) laptop computer\r\n\r\n(1) cell phone\r\n\r\n(1) dirty plate\r\n\r\n(1) bottle whiskey\r\n\r\n(1) book (Duane\'s Depressed by Larry McMurtry)\r\n\r\n(1) picture of kstar\r\n\r\nfuck this:\r\n\r\ni once saw a life in the contents of my pocket\r\nnow all i see is old stuff that i forgot i put there\r\nfound a carmel sucker in the back pocket of my jeans\r\ni found a business card in there too from the job before last\r\nthere were black bars over all the information but my name\r\ni think i thought that was funny when i did it\r\nsome reciept carbons were wadded up and crushed together\r\nmy wallet is filled with those shits\r\n\r\nfuck this:\r\n\r\nthere is an exchange in going back on your progress\r\npeople can kick at you and say that you are a failure\r\nthey can say i gave up and i hear it before it is spoken\r\nso i do not want to go back to accept the words\r\njust let them bounce around in those heads\r\nand i will bounce around this room\r\n\r\nfuck this:\r\n\r\ndepression is a national phenomenon that is economic and mental\r\nthere is no euphoria in a figure eight and no breaking it\r\nlook around at the hills stacked with buildings and imagine digging them up\r\nsometimes it takes thousands of years to build hills\r\nmillions of lives stack up on them for protection \r\nthey form commerce centers and then governments over as far as the eye can see\r\ni imagine digging san francisco up\r\nsending the artifacts to a museum in the rockies\r\na great flood is not so hard to imagine\r\n\r\nsometimes all it takes to get me thinking is a little profanity and frustration\r\n</PRE>'),(632,'time to vote'),(633,'a long time ago i noticed that there were clues to most websites designed by boys.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey almost always have dark backgrounds.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif they are corporate sites, they almost always have a particular look and feel to them that is unique to boys.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand they often require netscape in order to get the full effects.<br><br>\r\n\r\nboys like netscape and unix and girls don\'t much care as long as it is easy to maintain and easy to navigate and isn\'t black. <br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(634,'<FONT FACE=\"verdana,arial,helvetica\">\r\n<LI>I didn\'t fall on my ass<BR>\r\n<LI>I ate cheesecake and drank coffee with my friend Amanda who gives excellent hugs<BR>\r\n<LI>I mourned the loss of my favourite (and only) pair of pants/trousers/jeans/whatever you wanna call \'em.<BR>\r\n<LI>I bought Tim O\'Brien\'s <U>The Things They Carried</U> with my trusty credit card. It is for the 20th century literature paper I am taking.<BR>\r\n<LI>I went to the course advisor thingy place because they sent me a letter asking to see me and when I got there they told me that it was okay, really, they were just wanting to know what I was planning to do. I didn\'t know, couldn\'t tell them. So I left.<BR>\r\n<LI>I read by myself for an hour or so in the quad, while people danced and threw easter eggs at each other. One nearly hit me on the head.<BR>\r\n<LI>I went to an English tutorial and lecture.<BR>\r\n<LI>I said hi to someone who I was in a group of plays with in \'98 and she didn\'t recognise me so I had to explain who I was and then I felt stupid. She\'s the one that used to be \"the butter girl,\" not me. So there.<BR>\r\n<LI>Today has been a little bit weird for reasons I can\'t quite put my finger on.<BR>\r\n</FONT>'),(635,'<p>hi! i fell on my ass yesterday and it hurt. s5 said he\'d like to see the bruise.\r\n</p><p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/635_1.jpg\' alt=\"starlajo\'s ass\" height=\"240\" width=\"320\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p>\r\n'),(636,'one of the best things about me is that i fell in love several times before i met mark and in all but one of those relationships, i remained true friends with the old boyfriends.<br><br>\r\n\r\none of the things that is most interesting about this is that one of mark\'s few faults is that he is very jealous of me.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmandy always had friends that she loved and loved her, and there was rarely much drama involved.<br><br>\r\n\r\nelly fell in love a lot more often and there was usually drama involved.<br><br>\r\n\r\none thing i think is interesting is that the important boys and girls in my kids lives also became important to me, and to their father, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsometimes it was like gaining an extra child, sometimes it was like gaining a new distant relative, and sometimes i wanted to slap the kid in the face and tell them to leave and never come back.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think the test is whether or not i believe the friend truly loves my child.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen they are just using them for the moment i feel it in my bones and my protective instincts rear up in such fierce ways my kids freak out and often behave rebelliously by insisting that the friend is a true one and then spend more time than they would have anyway with the evil one.<br><br>\r\n\r\nelly has had more unsuitable friends than mandy over the years, but mandy has had more long term damage from unsuitable friends, if that makes any sense.<br><br>\r\n\r\none time elly had a boy that i adopted as my own and i loved him very much and then he left and she was heartbroken and so was i and i still am because i know he needed as much love as he could find and someday i hope to see him again but i don\'t think that will happen.<br><br>\r\n\r\nelly\'s best boys have remained her friends forever and this gives me great joy.<br><br>\r\n\r\npeter is a boy that i hope will be a keeper as i have said many times and i wish to hug him and bake him cookies and give him much motherly advice and remind him how wonderful he is. peter is my best boy these days.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni love ix very much, he is also the best of boys and i want him to always be happy and for someone to fall in love/lust with him and then they will marry and have fat babies and i will send presents and insist they bring the child to me in the summer times.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsteve is a boy that falls into your heart very easily because he has great passions and loves elly muchly and i watch his progress into his future with great interest and concern and wish to coddle him some and feed him comfort foods he probably would not appreciate since he is a vegan boy.<br><br>\r\n\r\naustin is now on my list of boys and this is nice for me to have a new boy to watch carefully and hope for good things coming his way.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni love all the boys who have pure love for elly and mandy of course, and i love the girls, too, but in different ways.  i think this is because i never had a boy of my own except mark and he does not count since he is a husband and not a child.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni want to have a grandson some day who will be my very best of boys and i will spoil him rotten and steal him from his parents every chance i get and make them live no farther than five miles away even if i have to sell my beloved and house and go where he is.<br><br>\r\n\r\nas time marches on, i am beginning to suspect that i may not have any grandchildren after all because it is very inconvenient to have children these days, most of my friends get grandchildren by accidental conception.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni wouldn\'t want my girls to have babies by accident if it made them unhappy.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni believe this ramble of babies and boys may be related to hormone deficiencies caused by my recent hysterectomy, so don\'t pay too much attention.'),(637,'<p>\r\ni was going to write something meaningful and interesting. about being in hibernation all winter and not living. instead, i have decided to make a list of things i hate and do that first idea later. for some reason, i really like lists.\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>the smell of bacon, chicken, beef, turkey, things cut off or made from animals</li>\r\n<li>all flavors of windows</li>\r\n<li>mean people really piss me off</li>\r\n<li>old, fat, drunk, asshole hicks</li>\r\n<li>the fact i can\'t do a join in a delete statement. i really feel i should be allowed to do that.</li>\r\n<li>people that offer me a bite of steak, burger, turkey, or any other meat lying around</li>\r\n<li>going to pick up bread sticks from american dream, to find out the stoners gave them to someone else</li>\r\n<li>i never win the eight spot on keno</li>\r\n<li>\"just eat around the meat\"</li>\r\n<li>people that cut me off on the freeway</li>\r\n<li>animal testing<li>\r\n<li>nerve gas, guns, bombs, torture, spies, and basically all things government</li>\r\n<li>oh, and pollution. i really hate that</li>\r\n<li>needles</li>\r\n<li>george bush sr. & jr.</li>\r\n<li>the fact that drugs don\'t make for a healthy body</li>\r\n<li>and that cigarettes cause cancer</li>\r\n<li>i can\'t have a six pack and big arms from typing all day</li>\r\n<li>this whole economy thing</li>\r\n<li>phish\'s hiatus</li>\r\n<li>compusa</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m sure there is more things that i hate. but it\'s still early and i am not awake yet.\r\n</p>'),(638,'<P>if i am going to be here for an extended period of time (something that i am grappling with) then i need to get some house shoes to keep my toes warm.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>if i have shoes on my feet are uncomfortable.  if i have just socks on, my feet are cold.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am telling myself that if i don\'t watch my email build up and just read my book or go for a walk to lunch or something, that when i get back i will have job email.  it isn\'t working very well.  but i have something like faith that this method will work, a watched pot does not boil as fast as an ignored one. </P>\r\n<P>insert guilt here because i keep leaving the water boiling for coffee or oatmeal and forgetting, leaving it for yeti to find later.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>also not that arnold never does that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i think for years to come i whenever i leave the pot on i will think of arnold not ever doing that.  he was so emphatically against people who would be so careless.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i sent email to <a href=\"http://www.eff.org\">electronic frontier foundation</a> today with the subject header \"layed off programmers seeks volunteer work\".  i do not think mr. stanton m* will reply.  and i will walk my happy ass down there and present my self to the receptionist for assignment.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i was thinking that if i got involved with eff like i got involved with <a href=\"http://www.cmpbs.org\">max\'s pot</a>, i.e. living in the closet getting paid money and becoming dedicated, then feeling really abused, i will be disappointed.  there are some factors that are different now from then.  1) i am not on drugs all the time.  2) they are not necessarily disfunctional at eff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><TT>.  the keyboard on my laptop is not recognising the 8/i/k/, key column.</TT></P>\r\n\r\n<P>if i want to move back to austin, all those friends are ready to have me back.  i am happy to know that.  but then i thought:  if i leave here so soon, all the friends that i have here will never reach that lifetime of welcomeness status.  i will lose touch with them maybe.  oh, i am wrong about that.  but you know what i mean.  i want to put down firmer roots in the city.  i want to know the joys of san francisco like elly talks about.  i want sf to be my nurturing female figure.  i want sf to be a home town.  i want to say i am from austin and then say i am from sf in the same breath.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>maybe if lots of kiddies go to melbs, i will go to melbs too.  i like the idea of going to a continent that is so similar (sounding) to texas.  i would maybe like it more than california.  i don\'t think i will ever get used to parking tickets for parking in my driveway (2).  i want to live out my days somewhere that the law allows for shooting the meter maid for setting foot on the drive way.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i need a haircut and i need to go present myself to employers.  i need a job like i need house shoes.</P>'),(639,'<P>\r\nso, say you\'re me. and you had things that were very urgent that needed to be mailed right this minute. so, your printer isn\'t functional and you go to the computer lab and try to print the urgent documents, but you can\'t because the local queue on the computer is messed up and you re-set the printer and delete the local queues. and you sit in front of the printer and wait, and wait, and wait. the printer has decided to print all of the old jobs that never got printed. so you say: \"fuck it\", and you go and eat lunch in the dining hall with your old roomate and her new friends. \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, you go home, and realize that while you\'re at the post office, you may as well send other stuff out too. so you put things in padded envelopes, and write checks, and realize that you have to be at work in 5 minutes. but, you remember the urgent documents, and you send an e-mail to your co-workers about the fact that you\'re going to be late. \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, you go to the computer lab nearest your dorm, and you get onto a PC, only to find out that it won\'t open microsoft word. so, then you go onto an iMac, and print out the urgent documents. you run to the post office, and there is a line. of course there is a line. why wouldn\'t there be a line? \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, you walk out of the post office with a feeling of accomplishment -- finally something has gotten done. and you waltz into work a whole forty minutes late, and your co-worker doesn\'t even bat an eye. you sit at your desk and help person after person after person, and you listen to them, and patiently answer their questions; the same questions you have answered millions of times, but you smile every time and make \"funny\" comments every time, and you even stay an extra hour and a half, and clock your co-worker out early. \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, you wake up the next morning, and you check e-mail before you go to class, to find that your supervisor has checked your timecard and is demanding to know why you were so late, and that next time, you should clear it with her. and so you write back during the break in your writing workshop that you didn\'t know it would take you so long (you didn\'t), and that you were taking care of urgent, personal business, and that you apologize, and that you managed to get in before three. </P><P>\r\n\r\nso, she writes back telling you that she has the impression that you could be late for any reason, and that you should take those things seriously. and you write back, and say that you were quite worried that you were so late, and that once again, you didn\'t think it would take so long, and that it was indeed quite urgent. \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, she writes back saying that you need to word your e-mails differently, and that you need to clear up personal emergencies with her, and to please go see her in the next two days. \r\n</P><P>\r\nso, you go from cheerily getting ready to have coffee with one of your closest friends, to becoming so frustrated you could cry in about five minutes. and you decide, that perhaps it\'s better to be a librarian next semester than to spend another year telling people how to change their e-mail passwords. \r\n</P><P>\r\njust a thought. \r\n\r\n</P>\r\n'),(640,'<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">Ahahahahhahhahahahhaahahahhaha!</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/640_1.jpg\' alt=\"austin: i am dealing with amateurs here, cut me some slack.\">\r\n\r\n<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">my evil plan is working!</p>\r\n<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">later on in THIS VERY MONTH, myself and my roommate brian will be graced with.......(drumroll please).....</p>\r\n<p style=\"font: 20pt, verdana; color: #f00\">THE INTERNETS OWN CHAYA!</p>\r\n<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">as you can probably tell, i\'m rather excited.</p>\r\n<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">soon, my pretties.  soon you will all live in my town and i will get to have your sweet selves all to myself.</p>\r\n<p style=\"font: 10pt, verdana; color: #900\">*cackle*</p>'),(645,'<p>I was just reading over the replies to my earlier query about moving, and noticed Olivia\'s rent (480/wk).  I swear the following is the first thing that ran through my head (and through the circuits of my trusty TI-85 calc as I used its mathematical talents to support mine):</p>\r\n<blockquote>Hmm.  There are 7*24*60 = 10080 minutes in a week.  If the rent is 480 a week, that\'s 4.762 cents a minute (rounding a bit).  Yowza.  Of course that\'s in Australian cents so I don\'t know what that equates to in American cents.  Either way it\'s cheaper than making a long-distance call that lasts for a month (unless you have dirt cheap LD).</blockquote>\r\n<p>Not that I\'m advocating paying your landlord in cents or by the minute, unless you really want to hack him/her/it off.  ;-)</p>\r\n<p>So, yeah, can you tell I\'m a nerd?</p>\r\n<p>(The other hint is that I did this as a diversion from this annoying, sucky quantum mechanics homework I\'m grinding through at 4am.  Huckel can approximately blow me.)</p>'),(646,NULL),(647,'<p>anyone want to meet for a beer?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i will be there on the 20th i think. lake havasu, grand canyon, four corners, abiqui, santa fe, roswell, carlsbad, marfa, and san antonio...are prior to that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yowza! i leave this saturday.</p>'),(648,'<p>\r\ninsomnia. quiet rustling, trying hard not to wake up your partner sleeping head next to you, little snortling sounds you don\'t usually hear.\r\n</p><p>\r\nangry mental one sided arguments with art professor who doesn\'t like your work, doesn\'t see your point- doesn\'t like anything you like. why does a painting need to cover the white canvas entirely? tell me that,or at least acknowledge that a good painting can possibly happen without covering all the white up, you closed minded archaic piece of withered history. ask me again what that paint is doing (points to favorite piece) again. ask it again and i will say, ramming my pen through your horehead, that it is there because it\'s a painting, and paintings need paint.\r\n</p><p>\r\non second thought, just squint and look at my art and look like you\'re watching me kill your cat in front of you. oh, i wish. i so wish.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni also wish that all teachers were good at teaching. at encouraging and giving good feedback on work that they obviously don\'t like, and doing it gracefully. i\'m pretty sure that in a few days, i will be fine about this, dismissing her opinion enough so i can go back to painting. but until then, i shall hate her and myself for taking her opinions to heart and letting her negativity affect my work. or maybe tomorrow will be the most productive day yet.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hope the latter.</p>\r\n'),(649,'<p>\r\ninsomnia. quiet rustling, trying hard not to wake up your partner sleeping head next to you, little snortling sounds you don\'t usually hear.\r\n</p><p>\r\nangry mental one sided arguments with art professor who doesn\'t like your work, doesn\'t see your point- doesn\'t like anything you like. why does a painting need to cover the white canvas entirely? tell me that,or at least acknowledge that a good painting can possibly happen without covering all the white up, you closed minded archaic piece of withered history. ask me again what that paint is doing (points to favorite piece) again. ask it again and i will say, ramming my pen through your forehead, that it is there because it\'s a painting, and paintings need paint.\r\n</p><p>\r\non second thought, just squint and look at my art and look like you\'re watching me kill your cat in front of you. oh, i wish. i so wish.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni also wish that all teachers were good at teaching. at encouraging and giving good feedback on work that they obviously don\'t like, and doing it gracefully. i\'m pretty sure that in a few days, i will be fine about this, dismissing her opinion enough so i can go back to painting. but until then, i shall hate her and myself for taking her opinions to heart and letting her negativity affect my work. or maybe tomorrow will be the most productive day yet.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hope the latter.</p>\r\n'),(650,'<p>\r\nso, i listed all the things i hate. and it just seems natural to turn around and list the things i love. so, here in no particular order but the one my brain puts them into, things that i love:\r\n</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>my girlfriend</li>\r\n<li>my family</li>\r\n<li>emacs</li>\r\n<li>rain storms on hot summer days</li>\r\n<li>the beach</li>\r\n<li>php</li>\r\n<li>august heat</li>\r\n<li>swimming in the columbia</li>\r\n<li>beer. and really, with all these micro brews, how can you not?</li>\r\n<li>road trips</li>\r\n<li>hot, sweaty, slippery love</li>\r\n<li><a href=\"http://www.oregoncountryfair.org\">ocf</a></li>\r\n<li>hot tubs in the snow</li>\r\n<li>tofurkey. (i love that shit)</li>\r\n<li>biking</li>\r\n<li>endorphines</li>\r\n<li>my mom and dad</li>\r\n<li>blue sky</li>\r\n<li>big bushy trees blowing in warm afternoon wind</li>\r\n<li>kindness in people</li>\r\n<li>the feel of the forest</li>\r\n<li>hiking</li>\r\n<li>tall grass in wind looking like water</li>\r\n<li>touch</li>\r\n<li>the smell of people you love</li>\r\n<li>life</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are more. days more. the things i love about winter but that aren\'t so strong because i want summer so badly. and this and that and so much more. love is so good. love makes me happy to be human.\r\n</p>'),(651,NULL),(652,'<p>bills are paid, rent is paid, and i have a couple hundred bucks till the 15th.</p>\r\n<p>I called the texas workforce commission today. I am officialy on the dol, er however you say that. While talking to them, I found that a publisher here in austin is looking for people with my abilities. I sent a resume. I hope I get a job there. Wish for me, please!</p>\r\n<p>a friend of mine was complaining about his under the table job that only pays 300 a week. They offered him 500 a week, but he woul have to go into work on site from 10-6. Their other offer was 250 a week plus having the employer\'s extra fine & beautiful assistant come to my friends apartment to clean... I would of taken it if it included laundry and tacos. He declined the $500, and the $250+ maid and is working from home, listening to stern and watching dirty videos. Thats the FUCKING LIFE!</p>\r\n<p>So I am trying to decide what to do with the rest of my day. I could go to a temp agency and apply, i could go ride my bike till my legs give out, i could stay here all day smoking. Nothing really appeals to me...</p>\r\n<p>I dont know who s5 is really, but I wish he would give another astrological forcast for this month. Everything seems to be calming down, a couple of weeks ago was hellish... now things seem to be falling into place, atleast for some.</p><p>You know, come to think of it, I don\'t know many of you. Something that I realized yesterday after looking at the long list of authors here on swinney.org... I think It would be interesting to have some sort of bio description of the authors... something small at first, like occupation, residence, eating habits (vegan, vegi, pseudo-vegi, meat eating) or something along those lines... maybe austin will do all of this for us by tomorrow? You can do all of that right Austin?</p><p>ok, i better get ready for my bike ride. Thanks for the eyes.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/652_1.jpg\' >\r\n'),(653,'<p><a href=\"http://groups.yahoo.com/group/swinneydotaustin\">swinney dot austin</a> email list for authors that live in austin.</p>\r\n<p>huzzah.</p> '),(654,'<p align=\"justify\">sometimes it\'s hard for me to believe i have a head. i look down at my body and it\'s just a pair of typing arms.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ni bruised myself in yoga yesterday, staying in shoulder stand, balanced up on a vertebrae for three minutes (not long). the vertebrae complains now.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ni seek comfort but feel awkward. i want to press my body against a lover, but my lover is a nervous wreck because he\'s a pile earth sign and muladhara chakra and all his comforts are in disarray: job, home, money. i want to collapse into something or someone, a person, a bed. instead i pace when comfort offers itself, pace, jiggle my leg, refuse to make eye contact, forget what to say.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nleaving the house continues to go poorly. eating is also a trial. sleep is ok. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ngoal: spend quiet nonthreatening time with people who i know actually like me. people i can trust with this mushy center of mine. (i: softshelled crab, honorary moon in cancer, stay home stay home oversensitive stay home)\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nanother goal: keep going to yoga.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(655,'<p>have you noticed that there has been a high percentage of swinney entries that consist of lists lately?  perhaps it\'s just me, but i find this fascinating in some way that i can\'t really put my finger on.</p>\r\n<p>on to the list:<ul>\r\n<li>two hippies smoking a joint arguing over the purchase of a sheet of acid.\r\n<li>one gay boy hitting on another gay boy in the castro.  conversation involved photons, photography, and astrology, and ended with \"i have a website, it\'s at members dot aol dot com.\"\r\n<li>a shopping cart full of things pushed 10 feet or so down the street in the wind.\r\n<li>four tiny dogs tied to each other going for a walk.\r\n<li>two dykes walking towards harvest market, one was concerned about the safety of her bicycle and refused to go in.  \"get me something with protein.\"\r\n<li>a very nice-smelling and gregarious homeless man who pontificated on the appropriateness of the color coordination between my hair and my frilly crinoline.\r\n<li>a built guy wearing leather climbing into a red el camino that was painted with the name \"someone\'s iron works.\"  i don\'t remember what the someone\'s name was.\r\n<li>a comfortingly mismatched <a href=\"http://elly.org\">elly</a>.  with stripey socks.  quiet appreciation is hard to convey over text.  (thanks jo)\r\n</ul></p>\r\n'),(656,'whenever i suggest red underpants to people i care about they seem to think i must be joking.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis is not true.  i learned the mysteries of them at my granny june\'s knee.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe believed in their power.  she always said you should buy the most decadent underpants you could afford because they will make you feel special and she was right.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut RED underpants are more than special.  they have power.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni know from extensive testing that this is so.  if you wear red drawers to a job interview or to sell something if you are a seller or to buy something if you are a buyer or if you are frightened or whatever, the knowledge that you have them on creates a special aura that bends the will of others to your bidding.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou do not believe me.  i suggest you go to your nearest purveyor of clothing for the nether regions and buy the most outrageously expensive and delightful red underpants you can find, and then TEST them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou will find out that they work.  job hunters should pay special attention.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni forgot to wear mine when i went to the liver doctor and you <a href=\"http://auntie.diaryland.com/81.html\">see what happened</a>.  tomorrow i am going to the mall where i will purchase many new styles of red drawers so i can get down to the business of a liver miracle.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(657,'<h1>Five More Things I am Good At</h1>\r\n<p>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Reading.\r\n<li>Synthesizing information and experience.\r\n<li>Skiing.\r\n<li>Remembering things and quoting them.\r\n<li>Playing Pinball.\r\n</ol>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat are <b><u>you</u></b> good at?</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn other news, just finished \"<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0345347862/qid%3D986550158/107-4354818-8185347\">The Sheep Look Up</a> by John Brunner (1982, 461 pages) and I recommend it to all readers. John Brunner sure does know how to write. Nearly 500 pages and more compelling and disturbing than <a  href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0553573861/qid=986550456/sr=1-1/ref=sc_b_2/107-4354818-8185347\">Mr. Excessively Verbose</a>.<p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n'),(658,'<P>Last year, as an  assignment in class, I made a CD ROM.  Not just any CD ROM, oh no.  It was a cd rom about my house, and the people that live in it, and the things we do.  I put a lot of heart and soul in it, and I\'m  quite proud of the end result.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Now I finally have access to a cd burner, I want to share the love, so what I am proposing is this.  You send me something, and I will send you a copy of \"The Garland Gang\".  It\'s  interactive.  It\'s colourful.  It\'s fun.  I made a better selling page about it <A HREF=\"http://hubris.co.nz/samples\">here</A> .  I think this is a good idea because it means that I get a wider audience for what I did, you get some fun, and I get whatever it is that you send me in exchange.   </P>\r\n<P><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/658_1.gif\'></P>\r\n<P> So yes, there is my little advertising pitch.  Thank you.  And <A HREF=\"http://hubris.co.nz/samples\">enjoy</A>.  </P>'),(659,'<p><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">My friend told me last night she\'s \r\n  bipolar and needs to start on lithium immediately. I don\'t know if I agree with \r\n  lithium, or drugs in general to \'cure\' mood swings or different levels of emotional \r\n  highs and lows. If your kid is hyper, put him/her on Ritalin. If your kid is \r\n  depressed, Prozac. Paxil. Xanax. Zoloft. I know people have different opinions, \r\n  like you should be happy for your friend to be able to exist through one day \r\n  without crazy thoughts in her head. And I think, no, the crazy thoughts are \r\n  half the fun of living. And she writes them all down, she\'s a genius, she\'s \r\n  creative and artistic and energetic. Instead of the \'wonderful\' or \'horrible\' \r\n  reply to how are you today, I will get an answer like okay, so-so, things are \r\n  fine. This will make her numb to the world, numb to her own feelings inside. \r\n  And they argue, but she\'s not healthy; she might be numb for a while but she\'s \r\n  oversensitive now, she\'s taking in too much of it all. She needs a rest.</font> \r\n</p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">No, no, no. These crazy thoughts \r\n  are who she is, who I am, who we all are. I would be jane or mary or sue if \r\n  I didn\'t think like a madwoman. Hopefully, me being a madwoman is half the reason \r\n  you like me. For who I am. Not for the more productive person of society I could \r\n  become if only I added a little lithium to my diet. But what does this all mean? \r\n  Why do people drink or smoke pot and cigarettes anyway? What are people trying \r\n  to reach out to or run away from? That which is in our own heads? It\'s natural, \r\n  It\'s normal. Confront it. Embrace it. Accept it. We are supposed to be insane. \r\n  It\'s an insane world. What is truly sane? Accepting a wife and two kids and \r\n  3.5 hours of bad TV every night. Mowing the lawn on Saturday mornings. Church \r\n  on Sundays. Working at a respectable job 9 - 5 Monday - Friday. Having an affair \r\n  with your neighbor? Divorcing your spouse after 12 years and three kids. Mid-life \r\n  crisis. Cancer. Depression. Weddings, funerals, nursing homes, death. What the \r\n  hell is it all about??!!</font> </p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">Every day, wake up. Wake up every \r\n  day and look around and love every fucking minute of it. Love every breath you \r\n  breathe in and every insane thought in your brain and every encounter you make \r\n  and every challenge you are faced with and every night you lie down in your \r\n  bed to sleep and every person you love. It would be nice to be loved right now. \r\n  I know nothing\'s forever, but just for today, it would be nice to know that \r\n  I was loved.</font> </p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">\"Given pain and nothingness, I would \r\n  always choose pain.\" --William Faulkner</font> </p>\r\n'),(660,'<Do people on this list prefer cats, to, say...dogs?>'),(661,'<P>looking for intelligent life.  Lonely soul needs food for thought and would appreciate comments, what have you. Love taste of irony. Witty and ascerbic when I can get it. Cynics aren\'t bad but a steady diet of it can get you bipolar quickly.Humor? Love some! Absurdity? oh, please!Melodrama and existential angst? What kind of a kinky feedee do you take me for? Mellow drama, no that\'s another kettle of verbiage. I encourage spontaneous segues and subtle but progressive digression! HEADS UP!</P>'),(662,'<p>\r\nIm currently thinking outside of reality. im in love? infatuated? obsessed? smitten? for a girl and she is away and i realize that for the past few months she has been my life. i think outside of reality because outside of reality is myway or the highway. the pun isnt intended but isnt inappropriate either. for the most part these past two days have been very lonely. literally alone for a lot more time i find that i have absolutely nothing to do outside of eat, irc, and play video games. the high from calling in sick for work is gone and nothing can replace the thought of her being around me. \r\n</p>'),(663,'<P>yeti and swinney were hungry for some dinner.  they looked up and down the kitchen and found a the following major items:</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n. potatoes\r\n. asparagus\r\n. tortillas\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>they quickly determined that with the spice curry (one jar of paste) they could have curry potatoes.  they had some rice too that they could use.</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>say there, i see in the final product here that you have some coconut in there...</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>swinney:  we needed something more.  something to make this meal more exotic.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>swinney remembered a can of coconut milk in the cubbard.  he grabed it.</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>d, d, did you know WHAT to do with the coconut milk?!?</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>swinney:  well bob, i didn\'t at first.  i had to get on the innernet and search google for some answers.</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>well it looks like you figured it out, heh.</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>swinney:  yea yeti was insturminal, thanks bob.</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>lets move over here and talk to yeti</PRE>\r\n\r\n<PRE>yeti, swinney said you were really instrumental</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>yeti:  well, i started with the potatoes, naturally, cause i am an idaho boy.  boy-howdy *spits* you know us idaho potato boys, we go strait for them taters every time.  i saw those suckers up in that mesh rack thing and i just went strait fer em.  i had them all peeled and cut up just as faster than you could say iowa corn husker.  an you know i can cook them things . . .</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>that is just great, sorry to cut you off yeti...  well the meal \r\nis done and cooked and i must say, if those other contestants think \r\nthey are going to beat this crew at the Iron Vegan on sunday \r\n(swinney grabs the mic)</PRE> \r\n\r\n<P>swinney (getting violent): that\'s right!  we made garlic nan out of tortillas!  we are ready for you people *growles*</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>wehehell, i guess swinney is excited ...\r\n(hit over the head with folding chair by yeti)</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>yeti: FUCK EAW BOB!  gawddamn right (in overalls) we are ready for you...\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>cut the camera, cut to commercial **(*&*(^%$%(</PRE>'),(664,'Yesterday I was wearing a cardigan that I own, and I haven\'t worn it for a long long time.   \r\n\r\nI haven\'t worn it for a while, I\'m kind of scared to.  If I hadn\'t been wearing it one night, if I\'d been wearing something stronger like jeans, I might not be here right now, and that scares me completely.  Have you ever  been in a place where you would have hung yourself if only you hadn\'t realised that your cardigan was frail and would have ripped and it never would have worked?  \r\n\r\nAnd then I come to Swinney, and lately there has been so much amazing writing here, so much strength.  People that have lived through  unbelievable things and have survived, and they seem like amazing wonderful people.  And I wish that I was like them and I wish that before I fall asleep every night I didn\'t think about  things that I nearly did, or should have done. \r\n\r\nI guess this feeling passes eventually, and I guess you are all  inspiring, so thank you.  '),(665,'filler filler filler.\r\n\r\nthis is article 665.\r\n\r\nblah blah blah blah.\r\n\r\ni want to hit the sack and no one has posted 665. so here it is!'),(666,'<h2>\"The Number of the Beast\"</h2>\r\n<h3>lyrics by: <a href=\"http://www.ironmaiden.co.uk/ironmaiden/discography/notb/index.shtml\">Iron Maiden</a></h3>\r\n\r\n<pre><tt>\r\n\r\nI left alone my mind was blank\r\nI needed time to think\r\nto get the memories from my mind\r\n\r\nWhat did I see can I believe that what I saw\r\nthat night was real and not just fantasy\r\n\r\nJust what I saw in my old dreams were they\r\nreflections of my warped mind staring back at me\r\n\r\nCos in my dreams it\'s always there\r\nthe evil face that twists my mind\r\nand brings me to despair\r\n\r\nThe night was black was no use holding back\r\nCos I just had to see was someone watching me\r\nIn the mist dark figures move and twist\r\nwas all this for real or some kind of hell\r\n<font color = \"#FF0000\">666</font> the Number of the Beast\r\nHell and fire was spawned to be released\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/666_2.gif\'> \r\n<pre><tt>\r\nTorches blazed and sacred chants were praised\r\nas they start to cry hands held to the sky\r\nIn the night the fires burning bright\r\nthe ritual has begun Satan\'s work is done\r\n\r\nSacrifice is going on tonight\r\n<a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=666.txt\"><font color=\"#FF0000\">666 the Number of the Beast</font></a>\r\nThis can\'t go on I must inform the law\r\nCan this still be real or some crazy dream\r\nbut I feel drawn towards the evil chanting hordes\r\nthey seem to mesmerise me...can\'t avoid their eyes\r\n<font color = \"#FF0000\">666</font> the Number of the Beast\r\n<font color = \"#FF0000\">666</font> the one for you and me\r\n\r\nI\'m coming back I will return\r\nAnd I\'ll possess your body and I\'ll make you burn\r\nI have the fire I have the force\r\nI have the power to make my evil take its course\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/666_1.jpg\'>\r\n'),(667,'<p>so, i am a part of a dining co-op. we pay 1000 dollars less than the dining hall kids to get better food, a sense of community, and complete control over just about everything. my co-op is part of a larger co-op organization that is actually a multi-million dollar corporation and we invest in low-income housing and help other co-ops out. there is one adult in the entire corporation -- she manages our finances, but other than that, it\'s mostly run by a group of student volunteers.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>as you can see (i hope) it\'s pretty fucking amazing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there is some co-op rivalry (but only between co-ops that have large housing components and in turn have an ever bigger sense of community), and sometimes we steal each other\'s stuff. but, it\'s all in good fun. once someone stole all the silverware of other people\'s co-op. we stole the tire swing from a rival co-op. yesterday, our cups and our porch swing got stolen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so today, i checked my e-mail and there was a message about an emergency clean up commando from 1-3. i thought it was perhaps a surprise inspectio from the county or the college, but it turns out that some people came into our kitchen last night and set off an emergency process that should smother a grease fire, but instead just got white powder on everything. all of the food we had out in the kitchen (lots of bread and granola) is going to get thrown out, and everything that needs to be visiously scrubbed over and over.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'d like to think that this wasn\'t part of the pranks, but it upsets me that one of our fellow co-opers would do something that was obviously malisious. it\'s kind of cute when you take all of our cups, or steal our bunny, the whatthefuck, but you could\'ve at least let us eat lunch and make our special meal dinner tonight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>stupid fucks.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(668,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/668_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>\r\nI compare my life as of late to being on a really long flight. i sit in my chair and wait for something to happen. waiting like i do for the end or something. knowing that in a few very long hours something new will happen. the long hours go by even slower when you have a watch or a taskbar. they idle while i wait. like the passengers. each one sits idle. a few might chit chat periodically. idle chit chat. cliched conversation programed over time no one notices. they show movies and provide magazines to promote an individualistic flight and as i sit and think about the whole concept of in-flight individualism, my own individualism hits me in the face. anti-social tendencies, spawn hate for the other. the other being them. them being the ones i hate because they do not act anti-social. where is this flight going? will i ever decide enough is enough and strap on a parachute and get the fuck outta dodge. i have a feeling the pilot has always been a figment of my immagination. playing god isnt fun. being god isnt desired. possible reasons for him being in cahoots to kill me is that i have been very critical. needlesstosay planes fly themselves these days. he\'s only there for insurance and a reassuring word everysooften the weather, the time, altitude blah blah blah. maybe he actually is god no dont be crazy like that. Doodling on the flight to godknowswhere i notice routine even in doodling. if a doodle becomes repeated it becomes an attempt at art. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlike a motherfuckingrepeateddoodle i make a lot of attempts in life. bad art makes for a bad life. yet art is so arbitrary. neat to me and yet godknowswhat to the rest. the same is to be said of my opinions, emotions, feelings, ideas, and morals. four hundred threedeeboxes later scribble scribble. still not there. i know the flight isnt even half over. the meals are nowheretobesmell\'t.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe doesnt like me like that. is she just saying it or is it all fact. i really really just dont know i have confused dreams and unrational thoughts about someone who isnt even on the flight anymore. she left when i was asleep. just after the drinks. when the cabin lights go out and the crew hands out blankets and pillows, something in the drinks made us all sleepy. not her tho. in cahoots with the airwarmafia she had her own water. i slept. she left. WHEN THE FUCK IS THE PLANE GONNA LAND AND LET ME OFF TO DO WHAT I WANT IN LIFE?\r\n</p>  \r\n'),(669,'<P align=\"center\">mm, the tabula rasa</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/669_1.jpg\' width=400 alt=\"does this mean i am a registered user?\">\r\n\r\n<!-- the keyboard on s5\'s mac sucks motherfucking ass. -- austin -->'),(670,'<p>i just made popcorn, the old fashioned way!  that is, without a microwave and without an expensive specialized add-on popcorn device.  incidentally, i don\'t have a microwave because i hear tell they are like little radiators of bad feng-shui, although i hesitate now because of their energy efficience.</p>\r\n<p>anyway, if you find yourself needing to do this at some point yourself, it\'s easy.  all you need is one of those bags of naked popping corns from the store (they\'re very cheap, US$2 or so), some vegetable oil, a pot with a lid, and (optionally) some salt and some butter.  i was hesitant because i feared my small camping-gear type cooking pot would be insufficiently heavy, but it worked just fine.</p>\r\n<p>you heat the oil in the pan until it\'s hot enough to readily pop one kernel that you throw in as a sacrafice to the mild and benevolent popcorn goddess.  then you dump however much you want in, turn the heat down a bit, throw the lid on loosely, and begin agitating the entire assembly.  you want to keep the lid on just enough to let the steam out, but not so much that the pent-up chaos of the popping corn is unleashed.  when the ensuing popping fury begins to relent, take it off the heat and add small bits of butter if you\'re so unveganly inclined, salt, and agitate more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>i managed to pull it off even this first time with only a tiny bit of burnage.  i vaguely recall this process from my childhood pre-microwave days, it\'s nice to see that it still works.  also, popcorn seems like it might be the kind of thing that\'s vaguely good for your intestines and assorted internal processes, but i might be wrong.\r\n</p>'),(671,'mark had to go on a business trip that required he have a 4 wheel drive vehicle and he left this morning before the sun came up and i tried not to cry.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis damned hormone weirdness since my hysterectomy make me weepy.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey also give me irrational fears. i am terrified mark will flip over in his top heavy montero and i tried to sleep but i had nightmares that he had an accident and i woke up sweating, which also might be hormones, since i have the air conditioner on so low the light bill is going to be outrageous.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni want mark to come home right now.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe is very handsome.<br><br>\r\n'),(672,'<P>I was thinking about different springtime holidays and observances and wondering how the dates of Passover and Easter are determined. People were discussing this at the Seder my girlfriend and I attended last night (not really a Seder, I guess, in the strictest sense, in that it\'s cobbled together from different traditions), and somebody said Passover begins on the night of the first full moon after the equinox, while Easter is the first Sunday </P>\r\n\r\n<P>If this is so, why isn\'t today Easter? Who makes the call on this? Calendar manufacturers? Some ecumenical council? Maybe the exact time of the full moon is taken into consideration, but is Greenwich Mean Time used, or what?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Stuff like this intrigues me. How the world works. How different cultures relate to each other and have things in common.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(673,'i\'m scanning photos.\r\nat work.\r\non a sunday afternoon.\r\ni\'m yawning. \r\nand sucking down a cup of coffee.\r\nlast night i went to trax.\r\nand entered a time warp, i think.\r\nsnuck in whiskey cokes.\r\nsame people from years ago.\r\nsome new ones.\r\nfalling over.\r\npushing each other around on the dance floor.\r\ni found my old tshirt with a stencil on it.\r\nit says fuk shit up!\r\nmy back hurts. breathing is irritating a little.\r\n<img border=0 src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/photologs/bedhead.jpg\">\r\n'),(674,'<PRE>\r\ni\'m scanning photos.\r\nat work.\r\non a sunday afternoon.\r\ni\'m yawning. \r\nand sucking down a cup of coffee.\r\nlast night i went to trax.\r\nand entered a time warp, i think.\r\nsnuck in whiskey cokes.\r\nsame people from years ago.\r\nsome new ones.\r\nfalling over.\r\npushing each other around on the dance floor.\r\ni found my old tshirt with a stencil on it.\r\nit says fuk shit up!\r\nmy back hurts. breathing is irritating a little.</PRE>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/674_1.jpg\'>'),(675,'<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/675_1.jpg\'>\r\n</p><p>\r\nsmall shaky sigh.\r\n</p>'),(676,'<PRE>\r\ni think 6\r\ni think 7\r\ni think 8\r\nthis goes in a blender\r\n ... spice?....\r\njust random ...   dont ask so many questions!  hey xep ...\r\nshould be cut as small as possible\r\n			                     would you like it DICED?!\r\n\r\ncompost?  yea there is  a bag outside\r\n                         i feel a little decomposy\r\ni think about three cloves of garlic\r\n     boo her!    ... is that what you were reading.\r\nrash of\r\nelly:      i was sort of overwhelmed by the whole garlic situation\r\nqq: hows that look xep?\r\nweeb:  ok, all those old fashioned cook books, they make you cook\r\n things you don\'t eat.  but the new ones, they have good stuff.\r\n\r\ns5: at this time we regret to inform you that we don\'t have ....\r\n\r\nelly: it is in the dishwasher or below the spoons\r\n\r\nweeb: have we chased out austin because we are doing girl stuff?  \r\n\r\naustin:  i\'m pretty sure we could make vegan haggis with what i pulled out of the garbage disposal\r\n\r\ns5: i \'m running interfereance\r\n\r\nweeb: we should just put the food in steve\'s butt\r\n\r\nqq:  cat!  hi cat!\r\n\r\nmika: mew mew\r\n\r\nweeb: wow, is this  a time machine?@  oh my god you have two \r\ndishwashers\r\n\r\nqq: is there land salt?\r\n\r\nweeb: skillet?\r\n\r\ns5: i must warn you about that skillet, it is old and has acquired syphalis\r\n\r\nqq:  .... it boils again\r\n\r\n[blender noise]\r\n\r\ns5:  i got this blender because it has on and off.\r\n\r\n[blender noise]\r\n\r\nqq: CAT!\r\n\r\nxep:  oooo, oils\r\n\r\nmmmm good [clapping]\r\n\r\nqq:  brown noodle proton water!\r\n\r\nweeb:  it\'s nutty\r\n\r\nqq: very spicy. garlic has been a reoccuring s\r\n\r\nweeb:  THAT\"S FAKE CHEESE?!\r\n\r\nqq: it doesnt  actually taste as bad as i thought it would be.\r\n\r\nweeb:  can i stir your thingers?\r\n\r\n[electronic music]\r\n\r\nweeb: shit man.  [whispers]\r\n\r\nqq: what kind of peices to we need?\r\n\r\nxep: do we need the blender?\r\n\r\nweeb: maybe later, actually sure why not?\r\n\r\ns5: [in shoes]\r\n\r\nweeb:  Wheat grass juicer?!  wheat grass juicer?!  oh it\'s so cute!\r\n\r\nweeb:   just put em in  a bowl, we can put them in a pot\r\n\r\nqq:  HERES CHOPPED SPINACHE!\r\n\r\n[commercial break]\r\n\r\nxep:  eric, put the spinache in the blender\r\n\r\nqq:  i don\'t think that is a good idea.  are you sure you want me to try that?\r\n\r\nxep:  yes\r\n\r\nweeb:  be sure you set it for \"pulse\"\r\n\r\n[blender noise]\r\n\r\nqq:  ok, it does nothing\r\n\r\naustin:  i think you need one of those cuisinart things\r\n\r\n[doorbell]\r\n\r\nweeb:  is that jeffie?\r\n\r\n[in walks yeti and s5]\r\n\r\nxep:  hey steve, maybe you should get that worm farm.  it was on\r\n craigs list\r\n\r\nqq:  i love worms, i love to play with them!\r\n\r\n[doorbell]\r\n\r\ns5: just leave the gate open\r\n\r\n[enter jeffie]\r\n\r\n[yeti enters, gives austin the goat horns]\r\n\r\n[jeff walks into kitchen, weeb gives him big smooches]\r\n\r\ns5:  jeffrey,  this is louise\r\n\r\nyeti:  good eveing sir, typing madly?\r\n\r\naustin:  i am scripting this eveings ambient conversation\r\n\r\nyeti:  word, [smiles big]  i\'ll leave you to that\r\n\r\ns5:  look at this spoon!\r\n\r\nqq:  it is a sea horse!  is it alive?!\r\n\r\nqq:  what is this?  it is seeds, tiny seeds, what kind of seeds are they?\r\n\r\nweeb:  little mustards.\r\n\r\nqq:  mustards grow out of them?\r\n\r\nweeb: okay but it will  be very mustardy\r\n\r\njeff:  ...radio shack cables...\r\n\r\ns5: ....they don\'t have to ....\r\n\r\nanon:  what is a matter jeff?  why?\r\n\r\nqq:  abah abah abah\r\n\r\nqq:  aha, you found the fun device!\r\n\r\njeff:  my new laptop has a light that at the top of the screen that \r\nshines down on the keyboard\r\n\r\n[a round of \"cools\"]\r\n\r\njeff to weeb:  hey, is that our spatula?\r\n\r\nweeb:  yesss, it is.\r\n\r\n[beat music]\r\n\r\njeff:  what, this one?\r\n\r\nxep:  this is nutritional yeast\r\n\r\njeff:  wow, dishwaher\r\n\r\nweeb:  dude, it is two!\r\n\r\njeff: cool\r\n\r\nweeb:  it dis-appEAred!  ohohoh, nice!\r\n\r\njeff: wow, got a lot of food in there\r\n[interlude]\r\n\r\nweeb:  oh shit, i forgot to bring garlic\r\n\r\nqq: hahaha, \"oh no, theres not enough garlic!\"\r\n\r\nweeb: it\'s a cake lollypop\r\n\r\n</PRE>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_1.jpg\' alt=\"weeb\"><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_2.jpg\' alt=\"weeb, xep\"><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_3.jpg\'><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_4.jpg\' alt=\"weeb and jeff\"><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_5.jpg\' alt=\"s5 and weeb again.  always with weeb....  what a camera hog.\"><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/676_6.jpg\' alt=\"weeb\'s product\"><BR>'),(677,'<p>I like graph paper.  Sometimes I change my mind about which kind I like best (5 to the inch, 10 to the inch, sometimes 4 to the inch; blue on white or dark green on light green (engineer\'s paper)).</p>\r\n<p>Graph paper is the place where things get prototyped.  It\'s the place where things get listed and enumerated.  A napkin is the place for a glimmer, but if you\'re serious it seems to end up on graph paper.</p>\r\n<p>Graph paper is perfect for making diagrams, and also having notes about the diagramms.  It fits text and pictures equally well.  (I\'m doodling on some UML as I speak...)</p>\r\n<p>Some people look at tools and feel the urge to build something (that happens to me also).  But for me it\'s graph paper that whispers the seductive creative urge that all builders know: \"come on, think of something to make, you know you want to\".</p>'),(678,'steveline sostilio was my friend. <br><br>\r\n\r\nwe were 12 years old and her mother was french and her father was in the air force and she looked like audrey hepburn with strawberry blonde hair and freckles.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe attacked life with gusto, and she was often inappropriate in the way she did so and sometimes cruel without realizing it and she had anger and lust and under it all she was the most fragile person in the world.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe ran down the halls of junior high screaming \"batman\" which was the hot teevee show that year and she brought whiskey to school in a perfume bottle so we could all taste it and then something happened that scared me...<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe started writing a porno novel in her notebook. it was very explicit. she and zoila cantos wrote it like a round robin, zoila would write a chapter and the stevvie would write a chapter and they would pass around the sheets of notebook paper so the rest of us could read it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand then joyce said to me that stevvie knew too much about what happened between grown ups and those were words that i didn\'t want to hear because i had been thinking them to myself and wondering what i could do and i knew there wasn\'t much.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\none of the summers i was gone with carl stevvie disappeared.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni asked everyone where she was and i went by her house in case maybe she moved and i saw her mother in the house but she wouldn\'t answer the door and i began to cry because i knew in my heart that stevvie was dead.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand i was right. marilyn finally told me that stevvie had killed herself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand i told miss babcock one day in the taco belle and she said she had been afraid that would happen<br><br> and she cried and i cried and i told her that somebody should have saved stevvie<br><br> and i wished that i had known how to do it and miss babcock said that only stevvie could have saved herself,<br><br> it wasn\'t on me to do it, and all it would have taken was for her to have told someone the truth one time and i knew this was true but we did not say anything else.<br><br>\r\n\r\nstevvie died the summer i was fifteen years old. that was almost 33 years ago.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe went to heaven or hell or purgatory and she is free now, but when she died she sentenced me and miss babcock and everyone who knew her to a lifetime of regrets and pain.<br><br>\r\n\r\nevery day of my life, every single day, on my wedding day, on the day my kids were born, on days when i moved or started a new job or went shopping and elly bought me red shoes...<br><br>\r\n\r\non days when mark kissed me senseless and on days when elly made me so proud or so scared or so sad and on days when mandy delighted me or pissed me off or graduated from college....<br><br>\r\n\r\non days that little children snuggled me and told me they loved me, on days when it rained or when the sun shown brightly...<br><br>\r\n\r\non all those days, every one, i thought of steveline sostilio and how she would never have days like that.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni thought of things i should have said or questions i could have asked or adults i might have gone to with my secret fears and on those days for a moment, just for a moment, i feel like a murderer.<br><br>\r\n\r\njust for a moment i know that in NOT acting on my fears i killed my friend who should have lived to grow up and fall in love and have children and get angry and see movies and dance until she was sweaty and.... all those things that you have done or will do and i have done or will do...<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut it ain\'t gonna happen.  stevvie is dead.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand this, my dear friend, is what suicide does.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand as many people have said or sung -- \"i\'d trade all my tomorrows for a single yesterday.\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nstevvie would have loved being an sf kid and she would have had blue hair and many tattoos and piercings even if she were old and she would have laughed and loved and ridden motorcycles with xep and eric and chatted up the boys in the castro and purchased strap on equipment for the parade and i am so sorry she wasn\'t born later when i was older and had some power to save her.\r\n'),(679,'<P>hi. i\'ve come here to read. so now i will write some too...hi..</P>\r\n<P>this weekend was amazing. the \'change\' happened here in the lovely chicagoland area. Spring has sprung. people were everywhere, jamming the streets.</P>\r\n<P>I heard 3 different people yelp out gleefully, \"I have spring fever!!\" i walked barefoot to the beach near my house. I got a *sunburn*!</P>\r\n<p>there were dogs at the dog beach leaping between the waves, digging holes and running full speed the length of the sandy beach.</P> \r\n<P>we got ice cream cones and grinned madly at everyone walking by who approved of our choice.</P>\r\n<p>margaritas were drunk in late afternoon luxury. barbecues could be smelled well into the evening.</P>\r\n<P>there is decadence here when the season changes. people here fall in love with the sun and the its warm embrace. after a winter here everyone forgets what its like in the summer. it feels like a suprise gift ever year-like no one saw it coming.\r\ni am happy.</P>'),(680,' <p>I\'ve been turning myself on to dried seaweed lately and I think it\'s time to take the final step, a diet consisting mainly of:</p>\r\n<p> Seaweed / Deer, boar and musk ox pemmican / Nuts / Berries / Dried fish / Lots of water</p>\r\n<p> I found a place where I can order some of this. Someone was telling me that the human body can digest pemmican more efficiently than almost any other food, that very little is wasted. I wonder if it\'s because the meat isn\'t cooked, only dried? And then mixed with animal fat? Or maybe because the animals are free-range? Probably a combination. I don\'t know. I also like the idea of eating things that don\'t go bad and are easy to carry around. In Newark this is especially important.</p>'),(681,'<P>*watched my mom and my niece down on the terrace sort through mom\'s earrings on a white cloth spread over the black tiles. from a distance, it looked like they were on a picnic or shooting marbles.</P><P>*laid on my bed and worked on a paper to present to The Society: \"recursively modifying ponzi schemes with clock algebra to facilitate pocket economies and asset equalization.\" (like mick jones, i\'m a communist who believes everyone should drive a cadillac, not work in factories. except i don\'t own a car or want to. maybe an electric one.) noticed for the first time that the crazy quilt my mother made years ago has unicorns in it: navy, lavender, carmine.</P><P>*in town, shooting exteriors, watched roger pitch a fit and refuse to use the storyboards we had all agreed on last night. took a vote, decided to let him wing it. he\'s got a shitload of optical glass in his room, and if tomorrow\'s rushes are fucked up, i\'m going to throw all of it off the balcony.  see how that grabs him.  i haven\'t been in a good fight in a while. feel crazy and strong lately. they can take the glass out of my share. it might be worth it. after that, pissed, walked back home. passed a dead cardinal and half an orange by the side of the road. i thought: the world is an altar and. i. do. not. exist.</P><P>*tried to work on the score, but needed to hear something from my reed man, and he was hungover. put on some latin jazz taped off of WMNF. anna says we should see if we can start a program on there called \"Radio Free Brooksville.\" have it on around 3 AM.</P><P>*helen called. told her if we can get a pickup deal, most of us have decided to buy mom and dad\'s house just for the security of it, use some of the money to start some new projects, and front off the rest to The Society.</P><P>*then janice called. wanted to formally thank anna on behalf of The Society for the bringing the <I>langpa</I> up here on the last leg of its trek from brazil (the <I>langpa</I> is like a beacon or modem; it\'s an object we use in divination to help tune into frequencies you can\'t pick up on the fm band.), not to mention bringing in all that good hash in the same consignment. (which reminds me - a word of advice: don\'t ever get your grandma stoned, no matter how much she thinks she wants to. don\'t get <I>my</I> grandma stoned, anyway. yours might be alright. mine was all like, \"mattie, i want to get high as a little birdie.\" then she couldn\'t handle her buzz.)</P><P>*went down at six and turned on the tv. my niece is staying here while mom\'s on spring break. four years old. she comes up and tells me, \"grownups can do anything they want to, but all they want to do is sit down and watch the news.\" so i got up and tossed the beach ball around with her outside then played hide and seek \'til she got hungry. (all i got out of the news anyway was bush, bush, bush. tired of it. janice says if people get fed up enough with him, they might be motivated to get off their ass and do something, like amend the constitution and call an interim election, kind of like they do in israel. but revolutionaries are prone to saying things like that.)</P><P>*tonight, called belosz and asked about using technicolor for the last half hour.  told me it was a triple-dye process, and i thought of all the eggs in the fridge downstairs cooling in their shells.</P><P>*talked to helen again about burning the facade down friday morning, shooting it day for night, legal ramifications. assured me a fire truck would be on hand, no liability issues. hope we don\'t start a forest fire.</P><P>*watched mom give paige a bath. they set out some plastic boats on the floor of the tub - red, yellow, blue, green - and when they opened up the taps, the water raised them  all.</P><P><B>*disclaimer*</B> this is fiction (as always). of course, even if it wasn\'t, it would still be prudent of me to claim it was anyway, no?</P> '),(682,'<p>I know what you mean about graph paper.  It has sometimes been the underpinning for my graphic excursions into the Beyond Within.  Its a certain structure that has a minimal presence, and it also offers a comforting support structure for new ideas to vine, twine and finally effloresce on..much better than napkins (I prefer paper Chinese placemats with horoscopes if I must)..I like graph paper as a journal....I also have a fondness for old bound ledgers as journals.  I also relate to the mechanical pencils (make all my points fine!) and of course the pilot pens are fun but have proved not to be a stable art medium over time. You are different than I in that you are a rational thinker, analytical--and I am oceanic, submerged up to my third eye in metaphor and still psychedelic flora and fauna and shamanic tidbits. I miss you alot still.  You were wonderful to be with-still are. Yo mama.</p>'),(683,'<P>I still like to color. Give me sheets of \"vanilla\" paper and a big box of colors. Or a roll of butcher paper so I can make a long fantasy scroll...which could include images, words, wild and swirly passages of color vortexes (is that vortii?)...I always liked \"Periwinkle\" as it was the color of twilight which is also the color of a type of sadness that is also beautiful, like how rain can be beautiful. Twilight is periwinkle in its opening passages before the deep night of the ocean floods in and the stars begin to shine. Periwinkle also reminds me of the part of me that will always be alone, always the mermaid sitting out on the far rock with the waves coming in around her.  Anais Nin said woman is a mermaid with her tail dipped into the unconscious. I want to color pictures of mermaids, and the moon, and the stars.  What do you want to color that you love or have forgotten but would like to remember?</P>'),(684,NULL),(685,'<a herf=\"http://www.gorillaz.com\">GORILLAZ</a>'),(686,'<P>i had a job interview yesterday.  it was very late.  i don\'t have much to say about it but i was just discussing this aspect with peter.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"on a scale of 1 to 10 where 1 is know nothing and 10 is kernel hacker, what kind of programmer are you?\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i never like that question.  but i said 3.5.  i think though if we are talking about the entire pool of programmers in existence, then i would be like a 1.5.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>there are people out there that really do know everthing, but as matsukaze says, there are things you don\'t know you don\'t know.  i have lots of those things, but there is no way for me to know really.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>do me this favor at your next job interview.  when the interviewer asks you, \"on a scale of 1 to 10...\"  please answer, \"are you testing my modesty?  i am a 10 at all times.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>thank you.</P>'),(687,'<PRE>\r\n<austin>\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i am hoping i get that job in san jose.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i want to disappear.  startup jobs do that.  i want to be gone from here and not see anyone that i see usually for a few weeks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there has been too much needing things to do around here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hi there, my name is austin.  want to do something fun?  i\'ve very little in my pocket.  i am bankrupt with life experience.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am done sharing, now when i want to do something it is disappear.  the only people to hang out with here seem to be other guys girlfriends while the guys are at work.  there is something i do not like about that. i know their boyfriends are not comfortable with it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i do not even want to go there.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i didn\'t get that job.  texas.</P>\r\n\r\n<code>\r\n</austin>\r\n</code>\r\n\r\n<code>\r\n<elly>\r\n</code>\r\n\r\n<font color=\"000099\">\r\n<p>i am trying not to add up the layoffs. i don\'t know why i care, i have savings.</p>\r\n<p>in my first class he other night, people said things like \"i am a sculptor\" or \"i am a graduate student at CCAC\" and i said \"i have found myself unemployed with no plans. i\'m just doing stuff until a plan comes to mind.\" i felt pathetic. </p>\r\n<p>in fact i have been feeling pathetic a lot lately.</p>\r\n<p>swinney, you think it\'s bad hanging out with the girlfriends. what about hanging out with the new girlfriends of ex boyfriends. or maybe hanging out with the ex girlfriends of new boyfriends. all of whom don\'t really like you much. i hate the concept of \'mutual friends\'. it is the worst thing about sf.</p>\r\n<p>i haven\'t seen my softskinned boyfriend anyway. i don\'t know what to say to him. he doesn\'t know what to say to me. i feel like an intruder everywhere. i feel paranoid, i feel like no one knows me and i don\'t know them. i feel threatened, i feel as if i\'ve lost or upset everyone.</p>\r\n<p>i think i would feel this way in texas too.</p>\r\n<p>i feel abandoned a lot. i wish i wanted to be un-abandoned, to press my body against someone, but i don\'t.</p>\r\n<p>i want the doubt to go away. people bring doubt with them, they bring themselves in awkward groups to say awkward things to people they don\'t care for. they barrel in, they knock things down, in a search for fuck, a search for food. i want no part of it.</p>\r\n<p>i want to disappear.</p>\r\n</font>\r\n<code></elly></code>'),(688,NULL),(689,'<p> normalization. i am wanting to normalize. not that i am abnormal. just a regular guy living in america trying to be noticed by people or to be completely anonymous, not sure which (depends on the day.) I am not profoundly against anything, not profoundly for anything. Education? little. I have learned much, mostly through mistakes, suffering and hard times. Have i got i rough, no, not by any means. I cant say that i have an opinion on graph paper, other than it reminds me of math, yet sometimes a home made paper star wars game i used to play with my brother during free time at home. i donâ€™t have a vast collection of 45\'s, though i do have some and a nice turntable to boot. yes, i do have 30+ gigs of music, and i listen to about 10gigs. Currently i have 4.5 computers in my room, but only two monitors (one on the floor not working). Besides my computer fetish, or passion, i am not into much else. I dabble here and there with photography, religion, i did yoga a few times but that was a couple of months ago. i have a mountain bike, in fact two, but one is broken. i eat healthy for the most part, but i do eat meat sometimes. I drink alcohol moderately and i smoke nearly every day. current goals include finishing school and being a part of my family. i have friends, and i love them. i do things with my friends, but usually it includes drinking or watching movies. i dont watch many movies. i know very little about tv and movies, i can\'t remember sitcoms as a kid, and i have little tv/movie trivia to share when my friends talk about, \"hey remember that show \"------?\" i think that some of this makes me a boring person, and i also think that it makes me somewhat normal in an abnormal way... creative, not very. on a scale between one and ten, i am probably an average 3 or 4. my friends are very creative, and i like to be around creative people. i love my family, and i dream about having one, someday. i would like to have a nice home that i am proud of, i would like to be free of addictions, i would like to have a healthy diet, i would like to be more creative. i would like to say that I understand philosophy, but i don\'t all that much. I would like to say that i know Perl or java, though i do probably know more than most about some of that. i have done things that people have said that i could never do, like graduate high school. (thanks Mrs. Lousainne for the support in 3rd grade, i did graduate, bitch). I attend college, and i make a\'s when i apply myself. i am bored in school, save algebra and science. i haven\'t read the greats, and i probably wouldn\'t know where to start... nor could i tell you who they are. i am just another guy, living and breathing.</p><p>i am struggling in a number of personal issues, and I am working on them currently. i am changing everyday, and i am becoming a better person. I am a real person. I am alive. I have dreams, i have emotions, i have concerns.</p><p>sometimes, i don\'t want to talk.</p>'),(690,'<p style=\"color: #990000; font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; font-size: 10pt;\">hi!  i went to a nice goth club tonight called revelations, on the corner of little flinders and elizabeth.  it was big, filled with beautifully dressed goths, hot and loud.  i loved it.</p>\r\n<img src=\"http://saturn5.com/olivia/images/people/olivia/olivia_gothclub.jpg\" border=\"1\" alt=\"weekend goth\"><br>\r\n<p style=\"color: #990000; font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; font-size: 10pt;\">so, i fully recommend dressing in black, donning your angst and gyrating on the floor for hours to pounding whining music.  also, the toilets are a fun place to hang out, touch up your makeup, meet nice boys who take photos of you, and gossip with cute chicks in corsets.</p>\r\n<p style=\"color: #990000; font-family: verdana, arial, helvetica; font-size: 10pt;\">that is all.</p>'),(691,'<PRE>\r\ni start trying again today.\r\n\r\ni start looking for jobs and shaving.  \r\ni will go to the gym and run and shower.\r\nso much for this business of coffee and naps and chat.\r\nsomeone please turn the depressing music off.  \r\nit is depressing me again,\r\ndamn this place that i am in.\r\n\r\n\r\nit could be worse.\r\noutside is a beautiful shiny day.\r\nsomewhere out there someone will give me money.\r\ni have never understood that,\r\nbut they will, have in the past.\r\ni am a beggar with lots of style.\r\nif you pay me, \r\ni will beg from someone or something else\r\npay me and i will beg computers.\r\n\r\nhi dad, i\'m back. yep, i failed.\r\nmind if i take your apples and sell them?\r\ni need money to buy my girlfriend out of hawk.\r\nthanks,\r\n         i will never forget this.\r\ni will get used to it.\r\n\r\nno really, i am not there yet.  \r\ni think i still have a few hundred dollars.\r\nall is not lost.\r\n</PRE>\r\n'),(692,'<P>ok. here it is. more than two months later. my severance has run out. i am eeking out an existance on $350/week. thank god i am married and have a wife that makes a good living. although i think she is getting resentful of the fact that i \"get\" to stay at home while she has to go to work.\r\n<p>ha. little does she know that this \"job\" of staying at home and looking for a new job sucks. i am reminded daily that i don\'t have the right skills or attitude or look or whatever. and that is really motivating to go out there and try it again.</P>\r\n<p>i went on an interview last thursday. i got a call on wednesday from this guy who was incredibly vague about a job; it sounded a bit like a recruiter (i have been getting a million calls a day from headhunters) and i was a bit skeptical about meeting with him. (for those of you who don\'t know, some recruiters-aka headhunters-want to interview you in their office for some reason. they rarely do anything for me... i don\'t think i have been on a single interview based on a recommendation from one of those recruiters. so, when they call, i immeadiately get all frustrated. if they want to spread my resume around, that\'s one thing. but if they want to take up all my time and make me drive all over town just to see them and then have them not do anything for me... yeah. anyway...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so, i decided that i wasn\'t going to dress up for this interview. if it was just going to be another headhunter, i wasn\'t going to put too much effort into it. i really didn\'t care. blue jeans, a beige button down shirt, open at the chest to reveal my white t-shirt, and blue converse sneakers. not exactly the type of apparel you use to impress people.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>guess what? it wasn\'t a headhunter. it was a real job interview. i did my absolute best to be answer all of his questions and show how talented and intelligent i was. i was hoping that if i could dazzle him with my wit, he wouldn\'t notice that i couldn\'t dress myself for an interview. while the interview was in progress, i felt my plan was working very well. then, as we were walking out of his office, he glanced down at the ground and did a double take.... he probably notice my beautiful blue converse sneakers... doh!</P>'),(693,'<p>that good friday and friday the 13th will cancel one another out.</p>\r\n<p>what are everyone\'s plans for this weekend?<BR>\r\ni\'m seeing over the rhine tomorrow night and guided by voices w/spoon saturday night and then celebrating zombie jesus day by lying around on my living room floor listening to music and petting my kitty.</p>'),(694,'<p>so. i hate my job. i hate it even more because i had The Meeting with my boss and according to her, my clever litle e-mails weren\'t \"proffesional\" enough for her taste. these were e-mails sent to my fellow co-workers, not to the grownups we work with, mind you. \r\n</p><p>\r\nit only made sense that two days later, i interviewed for a job where i would essentially be running my co-op organization with another person and getting about 1000 dollars a semester for it. it only made more sense that 45 minutes after the interview, i would get an e-mail offering me the position. what does this mean? \r\n</p><p>\r\ni can quit my damn fucking job!\r\n</p><p>\r\nand i get to do the following -- <br>\r\n++to be in any damn co-op i want to live AND eat in. <br>\r\n++to carry a pager! <br>\r\n++POWER. <br>\r\n++to work with awesome, awesome people.<br>\r\n++to be involved with something that i love. <br> \r\n</p><p>\r\nyep. sounds pretty damn good to me. </p>'),(695,'<pre>i\'ve been wondering where we\'ve gone<br>\r\n    when all i can think\r\n       is\r\n    i don\'t want this\r\n    and it\'s such a sad thing\r\n    it\'s such such a hard and miserable truth\r\n     that I use all of my energy to bury it deep\r\n        beneath the hope that it will\r\n                just go away\r\n            when i know well that it\r\n                 never will\r\n             that it\'s a forever decision\r\n              carelessly acquiesced\r\n               in a forever instant\r\n                that i can never take back\r\n                 and all i wawnt to do is\r\n                  cry and cry and cry\r\n                   because i cannot do this\r\n                    thing\r\n                     and i cannot not do this\r\n                      thing</pre>'),(696,'<pre>and i have known friends\r\nand my own mind\r\n      to stand on desktops protesting\r\n\r\n     \"I AM INNOCENT\r\n         OF THESE THINGS\"\r\n\r\n  as if acquittal from outside\r\n      the heart\r\n  could make such a statement\r\n      true</pre>'),(697,'<P>Last night I had this dream and in this dream I grew a penis. I am sure most of you girls have at one time or another dreamed this same dream. I remember my penis fondly. It was a two-fister, meaning I could hold it in two fists, one on top of the other, with the head sticking out of the top. I remember trying to keep it in my pants, but it was huge and hard and stuck out over the top of my waistband.</P>\r\n<P>At one point when I was grappling my new cock I felt a strong tingly sensation like when someone nibbles on my clit. It was rad. I immediately went around searching for my female friends. I wanted to fuck them all.</P>\r\n<P>I came upon my best pal, Laura B (no pun intended). I was giving her a convincing argument why she needed to immediately ride my cock and was about to agree to it when, of course, I woke up. Anyway, the dream was great and I am going to try again in my sleep to grow a penis and maybe get the chance to fuck my friends.</P>\r\n<P>THE END</P>\r\n'),(698,'<p>\r\nfor all you poor bachelor/ettes who want a change of pace in your kitchens:\r\n</p>\r\n<pre>\r\n\r\nspice    vitC+fiber  carbs      tasty/texture\r\n  A          B        C            D\r\n------    ------    ------      ------\r\ngarlic\r\nginger   tomato(s)\r\noliveoil broccoli\r\n         any green\r\n         leafy veg  avocado\r\n                    brown rice\r\n                    canned beans nutri yeast\r\n                                 bragg\'s\r\n                                 shredded carrot\r\n                                 kernel corn \r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n<p>\r\ncook all/any A with any B food, add C and D and eat!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is vitamin C. there is fiber. there is GARLIC.\r\ni survived many a period of unemployment with many permutations of this food system. buy organic! enjoy! </p>\r\n<p>\r\n   -- chef de gastronomie gigantique weebolique\r\n</p>'),(699,'<p>So I\'m making this backup of my home directory (linux).  It\'s an about 920 meg tar file (bz2).  I\'m using smbclient to push it over to christy\'s windows machine (easier than getting up to set up an ftp server over there; if you haven\'t seen smbclient it\'s pretty rockin\', sort of like FTP for the SMB protocol).  The lights on my hub for those two ports are blinking so fast they look solid.  :-) I don\'t know why that makes me happy, but it does.</p>\r\n<p>The thing is I know this behavior isn\'t unique to me.  I\'ve spoken to network admin friends before and they\'ve said that they too feel happy when they see lots of lights blinking.  (Well, preferrably green lights.)</p>\r\n<p>Some sort of primal fire/light thing?</p>\r\n\r\n'),(700,'<PRE>\r\ni\'m waiting on adamajah to get here\r\n\r\nhe is one of my very best friends\r\n\r\nsits and drinks jameson from a shot measure\r\n\r\ntalks to a <a href=\"http://steve.org\">nihilist</A>\r\n\r\nconsiders categories\r\n\r\nlistens to NoFX again and again\r\n\r\nwatches le tongue yeti\'s belly button\r\n\r\nscratches head\r\n\r\ntaps foot in time with the music\r\n\r\nwash rinse and repeat\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/700_1.jpg\" alt=\"the californial highway system\">'),(701,'<P><a href=\"http://www.gorillaz.com\">GO NOW</a>'),(702,'<P><a href=\"http://grillaz.com\">GO NOW</a>'),(703,'<P>I\'ve been getting a bit of a reputation amongst my peers as being somewhat grandmotherly. As in: \"Oh, that Jo, she just stays at home over the weekend and plays house... not as in playing house music, but as in playing house as in what grown-ups do.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>In an attempt to fool people, (into thinking that I am, in fact: energetic, lively, music-loving and a smiley, happy young thing) my partner in crime and I left the house AFTER midnight (never heard of... or not in months...) and made our way down to a stinking hole of humid heat known as The Box.</P>  \r\n\r\n<P>A friend-of-a-friend (Hi Sawks!) was having their vinyl release party that they had told me about weeks ago. Unfortunately I only heard about 10 minutes of them as I was drawn into the more bearable temperatured Cause Celebre. There was a man there. He was playing songs. Older songs that made me dance and not stop. Until I got tired. This was about 3:45am and so we bundled ourselves off and went home. We saw Myke on the street. He looked different without spikey hair. He had a Transformers t-shirt on. I liked it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I don\'t think I\'m so much of a boring stay-at-home as everyone thinks I am. I just need a little motivation to leave the house.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>A guy shook my hand and said happy easter and some pretty women smiled at me and I smiled back.</P>'),(704,'<p>well i did it. i got in my truck and left. six days and 750 miles gone by. how much more to go before i find myself? or just lose it maybe. we are disoriented...the dog and i. she wont eat really, i cant sleep. and neither one of us know what day it is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so far:<br>\r\nlistened to the ocean waves match the beat of my heart. <br>\r\nfound an old flame. found warm spots in each others arms.<br>\r\nfelt the buzzing pain of the tattoo gun.<br>\r\nwatched the moon rise over the desert dunes.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there is much more to come</p>\r\n'),(705,'<p><a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=700.txt\">who am i?</a></P>\r\n\r\n<P>hello, my name is adam.  sometimes i am called adamajah.  sometimes i am called dza.  i am a friend of austin\'s from our college/co-op days in texas.  long live the House of Commons (HOC!).  long live Mrs. Johnson\'s Doughnuts.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>austin, you<br>\r\n(tx)conducted house meetings as \'old man swinney\'<br>\r\n(tx)corrupted my vegetarian diet as \'bubba\'<br>\r\n(la)lived with me in my parents\' house as \'austin\'<br>\r\n(sf)and now introduce me to your irc/icb friends as \'robotec9\'<br></P>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'m glad i\'m visiting sf.  Peter, s5, sine, le, nice to meet you (other than in #annoying).  you guys rule :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>last night: s5\'s leftover chinese, sine\'s kettle one, whispering in the hot tub, kitty cats.</P> \r\n\r\n<p>tonight: dancing, drinking, doughnuts, ?.</p>'),(706,'test'),(707,'<P>How do I connect with the rest of the swinney community via IRC?  Is there a particular server I need to get on or is there just a #swinney on dal/under/whatever-net?  :-)  (whatever it is I hope it doesn\'t insist on identd, I\'m behind a NATing firewall and I haven\'t figured out how to get identd to play nice with that (the fw is running openBSD).)</p>\r\n<p>Thanks!</p>\r\n'),(708,'<P>well, i guess this means i\'m 31.  at one time, that would have seemed ancient to me. most days, i feel like i\'m about three years old. i feel a lot younger now than when i was in my early to mid twenties. no self-esteem then. couldn\'t keep a job. substance abuse. on the street. in jail. in the hospital. alienated from all family and friends. days without speaking to another human. tried to suicide twice.</P><P>things got better. i\'m glad i stuck around. back then, if i had met the me i am now, i never would have believed life could turn out this way. hope is a good thing. maybe the best of things.</P><P>jesus, it\'s hot in here. i think my parents gave up the air conditioner for lent or something. couldn\'t be because they\'re cheap. no way.</P><P>so how does my chart look for this next year? i done gave ya all my astromological information and ain\'t heard a damn thing. let me know....</P><P>well, i\'m going to bed. i want to take anna out on the casino boat tonight and drink complimentary gin and play blackjack. i feel lucky. </P>'),(709,'<P>\r\nI suppose I could sit here and bitchenwhine about how I can\'t seem to find the right pile of job stuff, and thus unable to find jobs/get interviews.  Or how I\'m too much of a non-aggressive social type for my own good to actually go out to bother meeting anyone.  Or, I could be happy about having gotten $1000 back from doing my taxes, thus extending the period of time for not caring about my lack of job.  And reaffirm that the one thing that always always makes me happy is dancing, even if it\'s in a room full of people I\'m too busy dancing to bother talking to.\r\n</P><P>\r\nPerhaps, another time.  Misery and happiness are irrelevant.  jD recently sent out a query to some list or another asking about the story behind your geek name, and I was going to talk about that.  But...   [Note: This is what happens when you leave swinney.org on your screen for too long.]\r\n</P><P>\r\nWait, no, I change my mind again.  I AM going to talk about dancing.  And happiness.  Because, the one thing I know more than any other truth in life is the simple statement: Dancing Makes Me Happy.  Period.  The End.  Thank You.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOther things may make me happy or not from time to time, but dancing is the one thing that seems to get me happy every time.  I\'m sure this has a lot to do with endorphines and exercise and bloodflow and not being a lazy fatass sitting in front of the computer.  I\'m sure.  But I think there is more to it than that.  I\'m just not sure what.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThere must be more to elaborate here, but I can\'t seem to find it.  Perhaps details of the specific dancing might help?  \r\n</P><P>\r\nClub Galia 2565 Mission St\r\n<BR>Standard issue music, some old stuff and some new stuff.  Who cares, it makes me dance.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHere\'s this other thing: People always seem to compliment me on my dancing.  (I guess it might contribute to the happiness factor?)  I never know what to say in response.   I just, like, dance.  I\'ve been dancing for so long I don\'t even know how long anymore.  It\'s all the music\'s fault, I have nothing to do with it, I promise.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nSometimes I wonder if might be asexual.  It seems talking to women is too much trouble, even if they just complimented me on my dancing.  I\'d really rather just dance.  Is it possible that I learned to dance in the first place to avoid people?  But I like to look at people and smile when I\'m dancing.  I just hate talking to them.  I\'m sure this is all way broken, and will probably result in my continuing to be single.  But, it\'s okay.  Dancing makes me happy.  Women, and people in general, they\'re tricky and don\'t always result in happiness.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEnough babbling for now before I wander across too many topics again.</P>'),(712,'When I get to the hospital, she is sitting up, wearing pig tails.  Pig tails in her hair.  She says this is because she hasn\'t been able to wash it in a couple of days, and it feels greasy and ick.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nShe is going to die.  Maybe not this time, or maybe not the next time in hospital, but sometime, and probably sooner than later.  She is five years past her use-by date, statistically speaking.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI am so terrified.  I don\'t know what to say, how to act, how to feel, what to do.  We always knew this was coming, SHE has always known that she will probably die young. I can\'t even comprehend what that must be like.  I wonder what kind of person she might have grown up to be if she knew that she was going to get to grow up.  Every time she gets sick now, she gets sicker.   <BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<I>This is what I am thinking:\r\n<LI>This is my fault for not getting up in the middle of the night when I was twelve and I heard the glass break.  If I\'d helped her then, maybe her liver wouldn\'t have collapsed, maybe she\'d still be better.   \r\n<LI>This is my fault for \'boasting\' to my friends when I was younger that my sister had an expiry date stamped on her by modern medicine, or bragging about how many operations she has had\r\n<LI>This is my fault for getting bored when I visit her in the hospital\r\n<LI>this is my fault for wondering what I would wear to her funeral, and what I would say in her eulogy\r\n<LI>This is my fault for being a drama queen\r\n<LI>This is my fault for writing about it on Swinney Org.  \r\n</I>\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis is what I know:\r\n<LI>This is not my fault\r\n<LI>I am scared.  \r\n<LI>I love her.  I will always love her\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nAnd I don\'t understand why it is her that is sick and not me.  We have the same genes.  We have the same hopes and dreams and ambitions.  I don\'t understand why she probably will never get to fulfill them. '),(713,'<p>I\'ve been living on the Internet since before it was really the Internet. I started out on BBSes in 1992 or so. When I got my first hacked university account in 1994, I connected to my brother\'s Upenn shell and read USENET. I gophered around all night long. I posted to rec.games.pinball from my brothers account.</P>\r\n<p>Later, I got my own account with access to news. I posted to alt.rhode-island and alt.fan.vonnegut and rec.games.pinball and said a whole lot of stuff to a lot of people. The most offensive stuff, I guess, was on alt.rhode-island.</p>\r\n<p>In late 1995, I started trading 0day warez on the first generation of private FTP sites dedicated to that purpose. The BBS was still the king in those days, and warez ftpds were primitive. Nowadays, they have <a href=\"http://www.glftpd.org/\">glftpd</a> which does basically anything any siteop would ever want. Back then, however, it was very basic and there were very few people couriering.</p>\r\n<p>The reason why this is titled \"lost and found\" is that these one of these parts of me is lost, and one is found.</p>\r\n<p>The one that is <b>lost</b> is not really lost, as my USENET postings (and the postings of people like <a href=\"http://www.cca.org\">Dave Fischer</a>) are not really gone. They were archived by DejaNews, later known as Deja, and now know as Google. In case you\'re unaware, Google bought <a href=\"http://www.deja.com\">Deja</a>. Anyway, Dejanews started out selling archives of USENET in monthly increments on CDROM. This was when USENET could fit in 650 megs and when CDROMs and burners were very expensive. When the website went up, they had a full archive that went back <u>years</u> and my life is in it. Later, they started retiring that info, so I can only assume it is archived somewhere, and now is an asset of <a href=\"http://www.google.com\">Google</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The one that is <b>found</b> is actually found. When I was trading warez for <a href=\"http://www.zdnet.com/zdnn/stories/news/0,4586,2433357,00.html?chkpt=zdnntop\">Pirates With Attitude (PWA)</a> and <a href=\"http://www.laric.com/razor/\">Razor 1911</a> and X-Force and RTS, some scene guys were writing a magazine called \"<a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/mags-inq.html\">Inquisition</a>\". I decided to write for them, and wrote a number of articles addressing the technical and human considerations of the newborn Internet piracy scene.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/files/magazines/inq-06.txt\">\"Net Ethics\"</a></p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/files/magazines/inq-07.txt\">\"An Interview with one of the Founders of Razor 1911\"</a></p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/files/magazines/inq-10.txt\">\"Group Loyalty and Pride\"</a></p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/files/magazines/inq-11.txt\">Autonomy & Austhetics [sic] for the Internet Scene</a><p>\r\n<p>These articles were written by me when I was 16 and 17 years old. They are a part of me, and until <a href=\"http://www.defacto2.net/scene-archive/\">the scene archive</a> goes down, that part of me will be readable forever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The patron saint of Lost Items is <a href=\"http://www.monksofadoration.org/patrons.html#Anthony\">Saint Anthony of Padua</a>. I mention this because it is Easter Weekend and I have recently found a piece of myself. Perhaps Anthony can convince Google to give me another part of my life back. And the data that was on all my hard-disks that have crashed over the years. And all of the things that I\'ve forgotten that really happened to me and that I don\'t have the magic thought that makes me remember them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n</p><p>\r\nPS - The links in this article link to <a href=\"http://www.textfiles.com\">interesting pages</a>, you should click on them!'),(714,'<p>Problems with Content Scrambling System on you DVD player in your linux box? Well then you need DeCSS, but where do you get it? Is it legal? Should it be? Or is it only legal to watch dvd\'s on macintosh and window boxes? </p>\r\n<p>well, check this song out, it happens to be the code for DeCSS. =)</p>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.lepard.org/decss/decss%20-%20descramble%20-%20joseph%20wecker.mp3\">download</a>\r\n\r\n<p>or, if you wish to cut and paste open this gif in your favorite text editor and look at the source... and laugh, as long as people band together we can beat the corporation.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/714_1.gif\'>\r\n<p>\r\nenjoy your dvd\'s.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(715,'<p>yesterday ended up being this really awesome magical day for me (and for others too it seems), and at the end of it I felt like, even tho I\'m 30 years old now, I\'m even less jaded about life than I\'ve ever been.  this feeling, this \"less jaded\" feeling (at a time when it seems like most folks just get more & more jaded as they get older), makes me really feel like I must be doing an ok job at living my life the right way, most of the time. :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yesterday, I feel like I saw a million different beautiful crazy fragile things in a million different beautiful crazy people, who cause me such a huge amount of pain somehow by the fact that they never get to see it in themselves, & cry because they\'re lonely, or because they feel that they failed at whatever it is someone else told them they\'re supposed to be or do or say, or because somebody else hurt them so badly  because that somebody else chose to live *their* own life out of fear they dont even know exists.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yesterday, all I could do was sit & cry as I thought about all that, & about how amazing it really all is & we are, how fragile & transparent. yesterday, I\'d swear that different timelines other than our own touched down into ours, co-mingling in such a way as to let many of us see those different streams. just tiny inexplicable bits, those intersections;  many of us had experiences we couldn\'t explain, saw things in ways we\'d never thought about before, opened our eyes & saw for the first time, re-discovered things we\'d long forgotten about & saw that those things were just as beautiful & painful as we\'d remembered, & we remembered why it was we loved & hated them in the first place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>something about you looked especially beautiful yesterday too; all three of us noticed & commented on it.  and the wind seemed to whip its way through you, us, everybody & everything; somehow that seemed to be the catalyst, the carrier of all this strangeness, this weird energy that pervaded everything with an almost sexual electricity, knocking over trees & blowing around weird bits of this & that, causing random chaos. and yet somehow the wind managed to hold it all together, like stitches in a crazy quilt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when I went to shower this morning, I found the bathtub covered in a whole sheet of little twigs & leafy bits, that had blown in through a tiny crack where the window didn\'t quite close all the way.  In spite of the fact that it was easily explainable, it still seemed spooky somehow, as though it were further evidence of that odd co-mingling of normally completely disparate worlds, one tiny bit of proof of the peculiar conjunction of time steams.  The wind was weird & warm, like it is during a huge fire or during that questionable phenomenon known as \"earthquake weather\", and puts everybody just a little more on edge than usual, a little more hyperaware.  It sure as hell made me hyperaware, almost made me feel as though I were tripping, even tho I wasn\'t;  probably just because it was accompanied by a sense of vision, of seeing things I dont normally see, sight normally only obtainable by chemical means, perhaps.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A wind like that is magical to me somehow, it blows through & affects everybody around, fills everybody with itself, with its energy, and opens everybodys eyes. just like tripping, it scares some people.  and others, they roll with it and look around them, checking shit out.  I feel awfully blessed to be one of the latter, because every time that happens, by whatever means, I once again feel reconfirmed in my life & how I live it, in the possibilities, in the accuracy of my view when I can suddenly see all the fragile complicated interconnections between everybody & everything as though somebody finally turned on that light in my head I didnt know was there,and in my belief that the only job we\'re pretty much responsible for as humans on this earth is to figure out how to make sure our lives really mean something to ourselves before we die, even if we only figure this out in the last 5 minutes of our lifetimes.   I believe in my own vision of death, as an explosive fiery grande finale to a life I tried to make as beautiful & meaningful as I could, even if I failed at times along the way & fell prey to all those things I worked so hard to figure out, even if I still lost my temper from time to time & screamed at the traffic stopped on the bridge or hurt somebody I loved in spite of the fact that I loved them (or perhaps because).  Hell, even if it takes me more than a lifetime just to figure out how to forgive myself for simply being born me (cuz by god that surely is harder than anything else I\'ve had to deal with to date, including wordless abandonment by once-treasured friends; or black eyes distributed by angry jealous boyfriends; or trips to the emergency room with my family after my father hurt my stepmother; or even while I sat & helplessly watched, crying my eyes out as a beloved friend let somebody else\'s knife-sharp fear-based actions carve a canyon through all the amazing accomplishments & thoughts & beauty that otherwise comprised her).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>All these things have somehow done nothing more than to make me see how amazing & wonderful it all is, make me appreciate life on this earth, appreciate my friends & family & work, & think how if given the chance, I wouldn\'t change a single bit of it somehow.  and all I could do yesterday evening was sit there in my friend\'s living room, crying and crying about all of it, every damn bit, all the shit and all the cool awesome stuff, and even at the very fact that I could even perceive this (oh marvel of marvels).  it hurts to see all of it at once, or even just a fraction, which is all I\'m sure I\'m seeing even then.  and I think thats why I cried; it didnt make me sad, just the opposite.  it made me elated!  excited about life, all the stuff I just described.  made everything that much more meaningful to me. Abso-fucking-lutely beautiful</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Did you ever see that movie American Beauty?  there was a quote from it I like, \"sometimes there\'s just so much beauty in the world that you almost can\'t stand it.\" This sunday made me feel that way too;  I ended the day exhausted & drained, and in love with everybody, passing out on my sofa in front of the tv.  I woke up at about 5:30 am finally, and felt quite peaceful; I felt like the majority of Whatever It Was that infused Sunday with such power & clarity for just about everybody had mostly gone in the night, leaving Monday pretty much more of a normal day again.  I can hear Patsy Cline singing off in the background through the doorway into Unit 3, people continue plugging away at all the usual things we plug away at every other day, yet another lovely kozmo order has made its way into the Unit 4 fridge.  But I can still feel a hint of It in there somewhere, like a tiny hidden trickle left behind from yesterday\'s torrent. And it doesn\'t matter if the trickle really is there or if I\'m just imagining it, because either way it really has changed me just a little, mere existence feels different. perhaps the trickle now exists *because* I\'ve been changed.  any way I look at it, it\'s all I can ask for. :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n- remember how to be silly<br>\r\n- remember when to stop<br>\r\n- learn that the only way you\'ll really be in control is when you can finally *choose* to let go of control<br>\r\n- laugh when you fall on your ass<br>\r\n- cry without shame<br>\r\n- never change a thing no matter how horrendous because it really all means something very important, even the little things<br>\r\n- remember to try & look at all those things you can\'t see, because they\'re often far more important than those you can see<br>\r\n- and again, remember to be silly!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>much love to you always,<br>\r\n-shawna</p>'),(716,'<P>hey, it\'s me again. try not to wet your pants. got in around 3:30 this morning. loaded. forgot about my sister and niece being here: there were little half-chewed carrots all over the floor by the front door. i stepped on one in the dark and nearly busted my ass. we investigated: a trail of them led to an easter basket in the living room. i guess my sister did that. she\'s so good to paige.</P><P>roger was watching \"8 1/2\" in the basement. he was like, \"my god, you two are fucked up.\" and i told him, \"yeah. maybe. but not crap-on-the-floor fucked up.\" so i had to tell them about christmas, \'89, when i snuck out to a party at my girlfriend\'s on xmas eve and woke up on the couch downstairs the next morning to find that i\'d shit on the linoleum in the kitchen. everybody else woke up while i was staggering around trying to clean it up. i tried to blame it on the cat. no dice. they don\'t make cat assholes in that gauge. would\'ve had to been a tiger. shoo, that was a miserable day. i had tried to forget about that.</P><P>so we smoked some with roger and went to bed. i woke up at 5:30 needing water. decided to stay up. still very woozy. i just know paige is gonna come alive at the crack of dawn and come down here and ask me if i saw any bunnies prowling around.</P><P>never listened to \"ok computer\" through headphones before. wow. so multi-layered. like \"dark side of the moon\" or something. \"interstella burst - i am back to save the universe.\" love that line.</P><P>went out on the gambling boat last night.  we played spades on the shuttle on the way out with dom and angie, this old couple we see out there every time we go. they must be regulars. from brooklyn. you can tell angie used to be nine kinds of fine. she\'s in her early seventies and still working it. not doing a bad job, either.</P><P>tried to play poker for awhile, but had a little buzz going, hard to front, anna was getting antsy. switched to blackjack. easier to play half-drunk. won a little, lost some back.</P><P>went out on the bow and smoked one of the hash joints we\'d made up before with cigarrettes (taking the tobacco out and stuffing the paper with weed and hash. we think we\'re slick.). looked back toward the glass door leading into the casino and saw jeff, the poker manager, standing there clocking us. just kind of played it off and kept on tokin\'. he walked off after a second.</P><P>went back in to the blackjack table. i think the dealer knew. he was fucking with us a little bit. couple of russians at the table. there were probly around twenty of \'em on the boat. i think they\'re trying to take over the concession from the italians or greeks or whoever\'s running it now. the boat\'s where i first ran into herb again. went to high school with him and knew he was working narcotics undercover in brooksville but hadn\'t seen him lately. saw him on there one day and kind of thought it was strange. clocked him for fbi. then got a brainstorm and called him up. laid out the story and told him he\'d be a natural actor, and he and gene had so much in common anyway. both black, law enforcement, undercover. i still see him out on the boat sometimes. to this day, he won\'t admit that\'s what he\'s doing out there, but he tells me to act like i don\'t know him, so you have to figure that\'s what\'s going on.</P><P>those russians are funny. there used to be this old fat guy at the five dollar table all the time. you\'d go, \"pana say russki?\", and he\'d act like he didn\'t know what you were talking about. you\'d ask him where he was from, and he\'d say, \"chicago.\" \"before that?\" no comment.</P><P>we won a couple hundred and decided to quit while ahead. went out to smoke again, and bald-head jeff comes high-stepping out and puts the kibosh on it. he\'s like, \"it was pretty obvious. first you\'re holding a cigarrette, then you give it to her. then she gives it back. everybody inside could see you.\" like he expected us to hang our heads in shame. so we went upstairs to the lounge. one of the russian guys was drinking at a table by himself. social butterfly that she is, anna bops right over there and starts chatting him up. now either she speaks some half-assed russian or she\'s out of practice, \'cause she kept saying a lot of the same words over and over, like i do when i\'m trying to get over in spanish. she kept throwing in english words every other sentence, too. the guy\'s eyes would get big every time she dropped in the english, like he was having a hard time switching back and forth. i think he was pretty lit by the time we rolled up. he was younger than the rest of the ones i see on there - around 45, short grey hair, built, unfortunately humorous fanny pack. he asks us to sit down. anna must\'ve said something about my birthday, \'cause the drinks start coming. he won\'t let me buy anything. point of pride. i\'m his guest. he had all these comp cards the casino gives for free drinks and food. musta had fifty of \'em. he spoke pretty good english. told me in his country, where he\'s from in russia (i think he was chechen), if someone is your guest, that\'s like the highest obligation you have. even if your sworn enemy, somebody \"you hate so bad in your bones you want to kill so bad,\" is being pursued by somebody else, and he makes it to your house, you have to protect him with your life. (how\'s that for a run-on sentence? man, i\'m still drunk.) you don\'t find many societies with that strong a sense of asylum. he asks what i\'m having. i ask him how you say \"beer.\" him and anna simultaneously: \"piva.\" (well, shit, there\'s no way anna\'s not gonna know how to say that. that was probly the first russian she learned, too.) so i\'m calling \"piva, dave\" to the bartender every ten minutes. no sense passing up a good thing like that.</P><P>after a while, i get a flash in the brainpan and ask him if he gets high. anna throws in a little translation, and hell, yeah, you know he\'s up for it. so we troop back downstairs with this guy like we\'re hot shit, through the casino, past jeff gawking by the poker table. out to the bow and fire one up. pass it around with big overwrought flourishes and a big wink for jeff peeking out the glass door. wormy little bastard.</P><P>back upstairs. i think the smoke got to him, \'cause he and she start havin\' one of those stoned conversations. even in russian, i can detect this. you know, one of those exchanges baked people have with their eyelids at half-mast, where one person talks for a long time and the other one zones out. first one of \'em will talk about something like eggs, giving a real lecture on \'em, and if the other person says anything at all, it\'ll be something just to keep the conversation going like, \"oh yeah, eggs are good for you.\" then it\'ll be the other person\'s turn, and they\'ll talk about nuclear arms or something for the next ten minutes while it\'s the first guy\'s turn to zone out. during anna\'s turn (she musta been talking about her work, \'cause she kept throwing \"alloy\" in there. i don\'t think that poor bastard had any clue what an alloy was.), i kept noticing him staring at her chest. eventually, he looked over and clocked that i had busted him and just gave me a big wink. i winked back. it felt like we were a pair of alpha male silverback gorillas or something. good times.</P><P>after the casino closed and we started heading back to port, everybody came up to the lounge from downstairs. on any given night, 95% of these people have dropped a load and aren\'t in a mood for any bullshit, so they have karaoke and dancing. anna asked the dj to play \"right back where we started from\" by i don\'t know who. (she became a scientologist, i think.) it was that song in \"slap shot.\" in the movie about four times. they sure got their money\'s worth outta that fucker. one of my faves. after that, anna asks the guy if he wants to dance. he tells her he\'s tired, maybe some other time. i think he got too high. couldn\'t hold his smoke.</P><P>after we docked, went to the seaside inn and drank there \'til close. shitty jukebox, but managed to find some doors and stones and bo diddley and aretha.\r\nnot too bad. getting old beats the alternative.</P> '),(717,'<p>\r\nchumsley the frog likes cake. his mouth was designed for cake shoveling, with self cleaning corners. they were webbed, just like the rest of chumsley.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"i wonder what would happen if i ate cake all day?\" warbled ol\' chumsley. so waddled over to his pantry and opened it up.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe ate some carrot cake. blueberry cake. vanilla strawberry, vanilla, strawberry, apple-pineapple, cherry tomato, raspberry-pina-colada, chinese egg custard, key lime, okra, orange-melon-grape cola, anise poppyseed, dandelion greens cake, coca cola cake, legos, apple marmalade, chocolate-broccoli, lemon ginger cake. with purple and pink and yellow and green and red and orange and rainbow icing. cake, austin\'s red underwear cake, yeti\'s flat panel monitor cake, elly\'s scarf cake, xep\'s trapeze bed cake, weeb\'s hair cake, cake in the shape of jeff\'s head, yoko hairball cake, and cake made from small european pillows. and small europeans.\r\n</p><p>\r\n \r\n</p><p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">then chumsley exploded.</font>\r\n</p><p>\r\n \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe end.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/717_2.jpg\' height=\"200\" alt=\'food balls\'><br><font size=\"1\">chumsley liked cakes.</font>\r\n</p><p>\r\nPart I of the Chumsley A. Higginsbottoms Adventures\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>next:</i> Chumsley likes to fart!</p>'),(718,'<p>\r\ni just woke up from this dream, so here it is:\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the dream, we were all on a game show, and i was the host. imagine me as somewhere between bob barker and wink martindale.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe show is called \"WHAT\'S MY JOB, ANYWAY??\". the contestants answer quiz show questions, and whoever wins with the most points at the end, gets a job! 2nd and 3rd place winners (ie. the losers) get nothing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbefore each show is aired, the contestants fill out questionaires so they can be matched up with a job that fits their chosen career path. and the employers aren\'t allowed to interview anyone first. whoever wins on the show, gets the job, no questions asked. (in exchange, the company gets free or reduced price product placement opportunities.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nto add to the humiliation, the show starts off with don pardo giving a little blurb about each of the contestants (\"jane robertson - an <b>EX</b>-accountant from sioux city, iowa! <b>welcome</b> jane!\"), while displaying a graph of how much is left in their bank accounts, and how many more months of unemployment they can afford to sustain.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso what do you think? real life TV at its best. i\'m going to shop the idea around to the networks.\r\n</p>'),(719,'<p>\r\nso im biking down o\'connor on my nishiki peice of nishiti. its only got two gears. i ripped some stuff that was hanging off and making noise and ever since its had two gears. the brakes sorta work. there is a loud screech before any gradual stop, and i sorta just take streets with stop signs rather then stop lights. the gears i have dont really help. walking would probably be less effort, but the distance covered would be much less in teh same ammount of time. anyway, i was riding down the street playing with the other gear shifter. the one that did nothing. not even a wire came out of where one is suppost to. with my right pant leg tucked into my sock, i peddle slowly. i can remember as a kid standing up on my pedals to increase speed. the nishiki has a mechanism of sorts that prohibits this strategy. if one were to attempt this manouver, the added pressure on the none existent gear-infastructure causes severe skipping and chaos. my bike is crap. ive gone to look at other bikes. they cost lots. i dont understand. what i also dont understand is why there are no \"simple\" bikes anymore. i dont need a combat ready bike. i have had 7 bikes stolen from me in my life. the concept of having something worth 900 times the value of the lock its \"protected\" by makes me mad. i want a bike worth less than the lock. i want the lock to be worth more. so that if i return to a bike without a lock i am not as mad as returning to a lock without a bike. you cant ride a lock home. well you might be able to if you were really short and small and there had been an ice storm and it was downhill all the way home, but where the fuck does that happen, and if i lived somewhere like that why the fuck would i be riding a bike? i got to where i wanted to be. chilled for a bit. smoked. smoked. smoked. came back home. email me for christ sake someone. chiam@gee.kz </P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/719_1.jpg\'>'),(720,'<p>\r\nim really just listening to music. im really just sitting here in my white lawn chair. im really just here thinking about how i could not get two other boxes to work. im really did just get off the phone with stinder. her voice is nice. she is nice. im really just trying to email and irc. im really just trying to be social. im trying not to say things i mean. im really trying to be supportive. im really just bored. </P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/720_1.jpg\' width=\'400\' alt=\'looks cold and scary\'>'),(721,'Ok, here goes with True Confessions.  Maybe there are others out there like me, maybe not.  Anyway, for many years, I have had a secret chocolate wabbit fetish at Easter.  This is selfish and gluttonous but nonetheless, I have to do it.  I go out and purchase a hollow milk chocolate bunny. I am alone. I open the package. I grab ahold of the rabbit and bite its head off. Savor.  Then I slowly eat the rest of it and throw away the package so as to destroy the evidence. That\'s what I associate Easter with--along with the sight and smell of lilies, and the true pagan meaning of the season.'),(722,'<p><b>six hours of hair pulling, some dollars, and two advils later:</b></p><p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/722_1.jpg\' height=\'200\' width=\"270\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br></p>\r\n<p><pre>\r\n\"you look like a mermaid\"\r\n\r\n\"it\'s... so many.. colors...\"\r\n\r\n\"you look like george clinton\"\r\n\r\n\"it looks like complicated fishing lures\"\r\n\r\n\"it\'s stunning\"\r\n\r\n\"well, we\'ll always be able to spot you in a crowd.\"</pre>\r\n</p>'),(723,'<p>For your viewing pleasure!</p>\r\n<img src=\\\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/723_1.gif\\\'>'),(724,'<p>\r\nwhat do you suppose would happen if a new language were developed and brought into existence (or a dead language revived, or a foreign language adopted) by a mass underground group of people?\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe new language would exist primarily for deviants and gypsies, artists and revolutionary thinkers. obscure enough such that it would be incomprehensible to a mainstream english speaker, yet expressive enough to encourage its teaching and use by generations of future families.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni ask this because i was in dolores park today, at an outdoor dance event. and one of the songs had lyrics that were probably too explicit or offensive to have been blasted at random pedestrians and nearby houses.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni wondered what would have happened had there been police nearby. would they have shut down the sound system? whenever you broadcast a message, there seems to be some implicit standard of overriding censorship.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut what if the offensive words were in chinese? maybe the police wouldn\'t notice, unless they were chinese. or if offended chinese passerbys were to report the indecencies to english speaking police.\r\n</p><p>\r\nor what if there were another language entirely, understood only between the listeners and performers of the above-mentioned smut. would the police, then, have any justification for censoring the sound system? in both cases, the ideas in the song are still offensive, and still amplified loudly.\r\n</p><p>\r\nif no one who is easily offended could understand or perceive an offensive message, was the message even offensive? if so, how? and if not, does it become no longer necessary to censor the producer of that message? in some ways, this is the same question as \"if a tree falls in the woods ...\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nat the core of our generation\'s (and i define this as the 1960s to the present) struggle to be granted cognitive liberty, has been the debate over how ideas are spread. you hear lawmakers throw around the phrase \"protecting the children\", which is a codeword for censorship: the process of preventing a community\'s legislated moral fiber from being infected by outside ideas.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhat is the ultimate goal of censorship? to eliminate deviant ideas, or to prevent them from tearing down structures? if dangerous or offensive ideas were invisible to the general public, would there still be a push to eliminate them?\r\n</p><p>\r\nto some extent, we\'re trying to solve these problems with technology; encryption and moderated email lists are examples. but what if we went a step further and encrypted verbal communication? ie. develop and spread the use of a new language, spoken only by those who wanted to/needed to know.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwould the users of such a language automatically be persecuted as purveyors of contraband? how would a revolution spread from its founders to the masses? would a new spoken language create an idea divide? a secret society? a class struggle?\r\n</p><p>\r\nto what extent would a separation of ideas undermine the vision of visionaries, and to what extent would it protect them?\r\n</p><p>\r\nif you have any thoughts on this, i\'d like to know. or <a href=\"mailto:steve@saturn5.com\">feel free</a> to drop me a line.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(725,'<P>i went to this place, <a href=\"http://planet-mag.com\">planet magazine</A> today looking for work.  it was a long shot.  i saw the listing on craig\'s list.  the location for the company on their website was a few blocks away.  i went down there and it was a gated shitty residential door.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni know what those two guys are up to at planet magazine.  i called their number, they are two guys.  like if me and peter were selling subscriptions for a monthly magazine that has not published yet, like Cocksmoker Magazine or something.\r\n</P><P>\r\noh well, i\'ll keep looking of course i will keep looking.  it is not like we live in a socialist state here.  i must find a job.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni am going to start moving away from computer job searching.  i am going to look for more editorial on computers stuff and also start making special appearances like i tried to today with Planet magazine.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nthere is some health medical journals that needs some one to log articles in the database and verify layout.  it lasts until 12/01.  the facility is in SOMA, which is on the sea side of the twin peaks that separate me from the sea.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni think i will also investigate contract possiblities through my one connection to that field.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>also i will do my taxes soon.</P>'),(726,'<P>why do you think that is?</P><P>could it be:</P><P><B>you don\'t know me very well?</B>  i gather that most of you know each other one way or another. you may see me as not much more than a name and a hotmail account. so i thought i should take some time and write a post to tell you my story and what\'s going on these days. which brings up the next point...</P><P><B>you consider me a liar, or, at the very least, an unreliable narrator?</B>  everything i put in my posts is true. i kind of hemmed and hawed around over that one thing i mentioned earlier, but entries like these probably couldn\'t be considered probable cause. it would be hard to use them to prove anything.</P><P><B>we don\'t have much in common?</B>  true, i\'m not into the computer programming stuff much. not the nuts and bolts aspects of it anyway. i do have a good grasp of the larger issues involved, though, and am, in fact, working with some people on various applications and products. i also like higher math, literature, comparative religious studies, all kinds of music, yoga, qigong, progressive politics, art, fucking, the kabala, dirty jokes, photography, running, intelligent agents, jumping up and down, macrobiotics, weightlifting, balance, and the synthesis of so many things that is called filmmaking. surely, somewhere in there, we can find common ground.</P><P><B>jealousy?</B>  there\'s really nothing about me that\'s enviable. if there is, it\'s nothing that you can\'t bring to fruition in your own life. my parents are wealthy, yes, but i\'m not. and that\'s a relatively recent development on their part. i grew up not in abject poverty, but there were lots of tough times. they\'re letting me stay here more out of pity than anything else, i think. seven years ago, i was alternating between bouts of homelessness, jail, and state-imposed hospitalization before i got stabilized on the right regimen of medication, diet, exercise, and alternative health practices. these days, i blow a little weed once or twice a week, but do other things to compensate for it. i maintain a daily two drink maximum except on special occaisons.</P><P>     i worked manual labor for four years to be able to finance what little of my film i was able to complete on my own. it took another year to find and convince a group of craftspeople to work on the project for a percentage of its profits.  i\'d also like to think they agreed to do it out of love for themselves, me, the group, and the film, above all. i wrote the most commercial script i possibly could without betraying my own personal convictions. anyone who might think i\'m selling out can just kiss my ass. i\'ve paid my dues, and nobody else has walked in my shoes. i started off using my parent\'s house as a location while maintaining my own apartment and found that production costs were eating into the budget i\'d alloted myself to live on. my parents saw the quality of what i was producing and are letting me stay here for the duration of the shoot. hopefully, we can sell the picture to a distributor soon.</P><P>     i met my girlfriend while attending night school at a university in tampa. she became a citizen in november.</P><P><B>you consider me to be \"outside the ordinary?\"</B>  i hold views, practice a blend of religions, and belong to an organization which some may consider bizarre. the society of which i am a member is dedicated to actualizing human potential and protecting the diversity of life on earth. we believe that human labor, in its current form, is a futile, spirit-crushing enterprise that serves chiefly as a means of social control and exploitation. among other things, we advocate a reordering of the global economic, political, and spiritual hegemonies that hold humanity in check. we practice a syncretization of eastern, afro-caribbean, native american, middle eastern, and earth-centered religions and use technology and science to further our efforts.</P><P><B>you just don\'t think i\'m worth the effort?</B>    get to know me. i\'m friendly, smart, compassionate. i\'ve been through a lot, and it\'s made me a stronger person. i think this is a wonderful, uplifting community, and i\'d like to be a part of it. some side of me seeks the validation of others, i guess. and that isn\'t a bad thing, cause we weren\'t put here to be alone. take care.</P>'),(727,'<P>I would, and I have thought about it before. I am not in search of it. If a friend were to ask for one I might say yes. Maybe not a friend, not for the first time, maybe a stranger would be best. Not in a bathroom at a bar, nor at an airport bathroom. I might do it in a movie theater, but I think that many people have gotten caught doing it there. I dont think i am gay, I don\'t want to be gay; I am afraid that it would be a hard life. Sometimes I masturbate thinking about what it would be like to hold a large penis in my hand and take it into my mouth. It seems to me that it is a dominat role for the cocksucker, I mean to have this man at the mercy of your crushing teeth. I wonder what sperm tastes like, for I have never tasted my own. I have thought about it, but could never actually do it. i like watching the porno\'s where a man is just about to come and decides to pull out and put it in her mouth. I could do that, if I were in the heat of the moment. I would like to think that being sprayed in the face would be interesting. Though I am not sure that I could do it to another. I have thought about doing such things to women I have been with, but I never do because I am afraid of how she would think of me after doing so. Dirty sex is a turn on, is it not? Is anal fun? I sometimes think it would, only I am too shy to ask, either way. I like to think about what it would feel like to take on a man, only I am afraid it would hurt. I wouldn\'t mind exploring, only sometimes I think I am the only one that thinks this way. Am I? What do girls think of this? What do boys think of this? Are you all repulsed? Are you curious? I saw an add in a paper, \"bi-curious?\", and it made me think of all this. Am I filthy because of these thoughts? Is it because of porno-american-culture that I think of this?  Should I be ashamed of what I have said? Are you all mad at me for posting this? AM I GAY? Or, are we all a little bit gay?</P>'),(728,'<p>\r\nso a few years ago i read this book called the story of b. it\'s by this guy daniel quinn. in fact, i read all his books. but it\'s the story of b that is sticking out right now.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou see, this is nature. everything, in fact, is nature. the street, cars, buildings, us. everything. to get in touch with nature by camping is really an oxymoron. we are in nature right now. and this is where i live. in nature.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand really, when i look outside, i don\'t mind nature. not that much. not the hulks of metal racing by, or the clouds of steam rising from camas. i don\'t really mind the fact that i drive for an hour and a half each day. i mean really, those fucks that drive half that in their suvs are worse for the planet than me. and i\'ll throw my butt out the window, cause it will break down. it\'s all natural. how long from now, i have no idea.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand i remember when i was in high school, and i read ishmael. i was sitting downtown in the middle of this nature that we built around ourselves, and this businessman walked by, stopped, told me that the book i was reading would change my life. i scoffed. i fucking dumbass corporate stiff telling me this. what could he possibly know about changing the world?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut when i think about it, i\'m now where he was then. i know that this planet we live on can\'t sustain us much longer. and famine and starvation will start increasing. and greed will rule, and our world we start to be run more by corporations than people. i see this.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut really what is there to do? revert to being a hunter gatherer and live in the woods? this thought really isn\'t all that bad and is really appealing, but where? that\'s the problem. we have been overtaken by one culture. one culture and one way of life. and that is sad.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut really, i am not sad with nature. i have food. i have a roof over my head. i have money, though it is living from paycheck to paycheck. i have family, and a girl i love.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe world is fucked, and i want to change it. but it\'s hard to change when people don\'t want it. or rather, they are taught that this is the way we are supposed to live and this way is going to work. cause, i want to change the world. i want to make this a place that i would want to have children in and leave to them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut, i have to survive too. and i have at least enjoy some of this life. it\'s the only one i get.\r\n</p>'),(729,'<P>i  do not know why i am so enamoured of this NoFX album,\"two heebs and\r\na bean.\"  i am one of those who just need to listen to theme music for\r\nlife.  i dont need albums that go nicely together.  i need albums that\r\nare relavant.  they are sound related to my situation.  this two heebs\r\nand a been album shows despiration.  the songs i go back to are the\r\nskin head song, \"bob,\" and the censorship song, \"please play this song\r\non the radio.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\ni think the song, \"please play this song on the radio,\" mirrors how i\r\nfeel about my resume, both the process of writing and the product.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni tried to share my love for this album with s5 but he has never heard\r\nNoFX.  i can only listen to the album when i am alone because my\r\nroomate yeti (it is his album) can\'t stand my taste in album order.\r\nyeti is a dj type.  his albums must fit the light mood of the moment\r\nin the progression of the day nestled between the previous album and\r\nthe next.  i like to listent to the same album on repeat while i work.\r\nthis would drive the yeti to obsessively insult my taste.\r\n</P><P>\r\nin other news, i am going to go to the medical journal place in a\r\ncouple hours.  it is 9:45 am.  i have eaten oatmeal and am about to\r\nmake coffee.  then i will print out resumes.\r\n</P><P>\r\noh, yeti just walked in.  wierd.  i wasn\'t expecting him till later.\r\noh well.  NoFX time is over.  bye.</P>'),(730,'<p>I have a friend.  She curses a lot.  And I mean a lot.  She\'s quite good at it.</p>\r\n<p>So she has this talent of making up curse phrases on the spot.  Like combo, triple-decker curses with fries on the side.  I think this is an unsung artform.  Because if nothing else it nails the argument that people who curse have poor vocabularies.</p>\r\n<p>So the other day the phrase \"cock-slapping buttmaster\" came up.  It\'s a funny image to me (of all the things you\'d do to a cock, would slapping it be in the top 5?).  It reminded me of the recent cock-mania on swinney.org.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve been thinking that perhaps English cursing is a closed grammar.  Like there is some set of all possible curse phrases of a given length or less generatable by a closed set of curse words.  And that it would be fun to write a program to generate that set...  I imagine my friend would have said most of them at some point.</p>'),(731,'<p>\r\ni told this joke to my girlfriend, and she thought, and still thinks, it is a classic. now it is an inside joke between us and i laugh everytime i hear it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthough, you will probably think it, and i are stupid. but please, just pretend to love me and read my joke. you can even fake laugh if you would like.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[joke]\r\n<br>\r\ntwo pigs are walking through the desert. one pig turns to the other and says, \"i\'m so hot, i\'m bacon.\"\r\n<br>\r\n[/joke]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nheh\r\n</p>'),(732,'<p>\r\nits seems that lots of people all over the place, all around me are having trouble with things. yeah well who isnt eh? i am. god is. africa is. asia is. all the continents are. everyone has some sort of problem and yet when problems occur nothing else changes. nothing changes when you have a problem. you still have friends, family and good times every so often. i do. you do. we all do. even people in concentration camps joke around. what i dont understand is how people can seem to ignore everything but their problems. possibly turning their problems into bigger ones. harping and pining over the same things over and over again isnt timely and isnt productive and isnt worth the time spent. i sure as hell know this. so take my word for it. while i do rant on here and blah blah blah all the time, im sick and tired of making an effort in life and having that effort put down because of lack of consideration from people around me and abroad. i like to talk to people and i like to interact and get advice from people that are mature and nice. i try. i tried. i will now stop. like the highschool days i will neglect to call anyone, i will neglect to email anyone, and i will neglect to do almost anything interactive due to the fact that no one wants or seems to want to interact these days. earthlimbo and fuck it all. thats what i want to say on this tuesday afternoon, after seeing that almost 90 emails have not been returned to almost 20 different people. phone calls dont get replied to and nor do icq and other msg\'s. i feel invisible to my so called friends and i think im just gonna dump em all and find new ones. if you cant find time for friends who the fuck can you find time for? yes. thats is the plan. </P>'),(733,'<P>Studies of an Invented Artist\r\n<P>[1] \r\n<P>The period stands against me. Iâ€™m the first <P>letter after it.\r\n<P>Idle reader, forget the source of this\r\n<P>predetermined transcription\r\n<P>and become part of the masses.\r\n<P>Learn to decipher the state of the artist.\r\n<P>Who may be caught in conception that lacks the <P>the contents of everywhere.\r\n<P>I maintain a prayer for \r\n<P>her animal cage indexed with flowers.\r\n<P>I tried sending love on a audio tape\r\n<P>she never got around to side b\r\n<P>[2]\r\n<P>This line is for your need of an island \r\n<P>and your modern execution of distance.\r\n<P>	This line is for the way you look in the morning,\r\n<P>	as brilliant as in the back of a crashed car at midnight.\r\n<P>This line, that lacks pain killers, is for the injection\r\n<P>of reverse repulsion I suffer at your invasion of my oxygen. 	\r\n<P>	This line is for the way your frame moves,\r\n	Without making my purpose come true.\r\n<P>This line is for the sweat\r\nand the ancient postponement of closure. \r\n<P>[3] \r\n<P>He would rather be commissioned \r\n<P>to paint a painting titled â€˜The Reasons For Colorsâ€™\r\n<P>than enter a bathroom with a \r\n<P>angel that spits soap.\r\n'),(734,'<BR>\r\n<img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/Serial/panel2.gif\" width=\"275\" height=\"216\"><br>\r\n                  <br>\r\n                  </font><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" color=\"#333333\" size=\"3\">The \r\n                  bar . . . is an exercise in solitude. Above all else, it must \r\n                  be quiet, dark, very comfortable-and, contrary to modern mores, \r\n                  no music of any kind, no matter how faint. In sum, there should \r\n                  be no more than a dozen tables, and a clientele that doesn\'t \r\n                  like to talk.</font><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" color=#333333 \r\n            size=1><br>\r\n                  <b>Luis BuÃ±uel</b><br>\r\n                  <br>\r\n                  <font size=\"3\">A right rule for a club would be, Admit no man \r\n                  whose presence excludes any one topic. It requires people who \r\n                  are not surprised and shocked, who do and let do, and let be, \r\n                  who sink trifles, and know solid values, and who take a great \r\n                  deal for granted.</font><br>\r\n                  <b>Ralph Waldo Emerson</b></font>'),(735,'<p>ten days of travelling and doing whatever the hell i want to do. it feels good. i fall asleep with a smile...regardless if it means sleeping in my truck. i write, write, write...and think, think, think. my writing doesnt keep up with my thinking. i need a tape recorder. or maybe i should just quit thinking.</p>'),(736,'<P>I have to take 3 pills every day. One in the morning and two at night, just so I can function enough to get out and see my therapist and go for long walks so that I don\'t sink back into my depression. Sometimes I don\'t want to take my medication, because it has all sorts of disturbing side effects, the worst being the jaw grinding, tension headaches, and ringing in the ears. My brain battles with itself, and the symptoms manifest themselves cranially.</P>\r\n<P>So, this past weekend has been full of tearful, resentful conversations with my burnt-out boyfriend. I get so overwhelmed at trying to explain to him how subtle things impact me tremendously sometimes. He shrugs and is too tired to be capable of compassion. I fix tea for both of us, and we decide to hang on to each other for the rough ride. I realize we are battling each other during times when we should be standing tall, together.</P>\r\n<P>It all came to a head last night, I was trying to get my photos ready and sent over to the magazine, and I just collapsed from emotional exhaustion. These times for me can be extremely aggravating, because I feel as if I am relapsing, backsliding, losing ground, going back to how I was after I got assaulted and had my life threatened with a gun back in September. I spent that whole month in bed, the only thing I can really remember doing is getting Fonem hosted.</P>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.saturn5.com/sine/electrofy/pics/miscart/lilshelly.jpg\">\r\n<P>In bed, all day. My body aches, but I can\'t get up to eat or bathe. My brain is a puddle of swill, unable to make coherent thoughts. I live just barely out of consciousness. I have to force myself to get up to drink water, maybe eat a banana, before I collapse with exhaustion again. I can\'t even get out to go see my therapist, so we have our hour long session over the phone.</P>\r\n<P>Lately we have been dealing with how my alcoholic and abusive father has thoroughly sabotaged my self-worth. For the first time in my life, I am realizing that many of the struggles I experience within myself aren\'t really my fault. The self-blame and self-hatred has less of a sting, because I am able to rationally explain to myself the reasons and causes of my deepest thoughts. But sometimes even this is difficult, because the crazy-making mixed messages my drunk father gave me when I was very small tell me that I am not worth of self-compassion. My guts wrench and writhe with contempt whenever my therapist speaks of \"loving yourself, self-care, self-love, self-compassion\". It makes me angry to think that I have been robbed of the ability to feel compassion for myself on a very deep level. Obviously I have enough to get by and survive, but this is truly ingrained and it has been difficult realizing that it isn\'t my fault. All this time I have felt the chaos around me was my responsibility alone to deal with. I wasn\'t deserving of help, since the mess was all mine.</P>\r\n<P>I am just beginning to understand how my dad\'s drinking affected my family. I grew up thinking this was normal, right? My mom put on such a good face to the world but I always knew something was pretty fucked up. It made me angry, but I didn\'t know why at the time. How could she not see how fucked up we are? I guess this is what people mean when they talk about enabling.</P>\r\n<P>The cruelty my father inflicted on us, seemingly me in particular, continues to this day. My therapist has explained to me that much of the time, the more unusual and gifted children receive the brunt of the abuse. Alcoholic parents punish their children by shaming them as well as punishing them. Normal parents punish, but the child always knows that it is loved. One time, when me and my family were out on the boat in the Gulf of Mexico with a few other families in boats, looking for scallops, I was hanging over the side of the boat looking at a pod of dolphins that were accompanying us. This wasn\'t that unusual, to see dolphin cavorting with boats. I was maybe 7 or 8 years old, wearing a life jacket. I leaned over a little too far or we hit a wave or something. I fell in. I swam as fast as I could away from the boat, as not to be caught in the propellor. My father steered the boat towards me, picked me up by my ankles, causing my head to go underwater. He pulled me out, held me up in the air for everyone to see, and stood there and spanked me so hard I had welts. That is shame.</P>\r\n<P>I don\'t want you to feel sorry for me, because those of you who know me know that I will come out of the ass end of this abuse totally better, more amazing, more creative than I have ever been. Sometimes, I just need to put out the garbage that has been sitting inside for so long.</P>'),(737,'<p>\r\n<LI>One measure Peach Schnapps\r\n<LI>One measure Bacardi Lemon\r\n<LI>One measure Coconut Cream\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nPour into a shaker with ice, and shake.  Serve in a martini glass.  It\'s really nice.  \r\n\r\n</p>'),(738,'<p>\r\n\"i once fucked a polyamorist astrophysicist and his body builder mistress on a murphy bed in a studio apartment in seattle, washington. he was married to a woman who was out of town, and she - the mistress - once told me she thought art was pointless. she brute forced me to orgasm with a glitter dildo, lube, and her fist.\" \r\n</p>\r\n<br><br><font size=\"-1\"><i>(first written over at <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com\">jo</a>\'s site.)</font></i></p>'),(739,'<p>\r\ni am continually amazed by the web connecting people. i don\'t mean this internet thing. what i am talking about is the actual knowing of a person. and how knowing one person connects us to all the people they know and connects that person to all the people we know.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nkind of like that whole kevin bacon program that i can\'t remember the name of or where it is residing these days.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand it amazes me to no end that even though i have only a couple friends, i am connected to so many people. and the span is mind boggling. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand from person to person, followed long enough, you might just find me connected to you.\r\n</p>'),(740,'<script>alert(\"The answer to your question, cornholio, is no.\\n  but you are welcome to do the development for \\nthat if you want.  I, austin, do not feel like it.\\nbut i welcome you to submit a patch for that \\nproblem.\");</script>\r\n\r\n<P>the answer to your quesion:</P>'),(741,'<p>\r\nconspiracy on my mind. inner circle meetings on phrantik. #r the co-ordinating core. #a intelligence. i do not know why. many jumped on the bandwagon. there were many things taken out of context in this thought. many things on my part and theirs. it ended with me finding out. i sit here now knowing its all untrue. i hate paranoia. it hasnt hit like this in a long time. not since i saw old men laughing at my door. the times people broke in and were going to tie me up and beat me to death. it makes me sad to think it takes a lot out of someone to interact with me. \r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/741_1.jpg\'>'),(742,'<p>Here\'s a spur-of-the-moment-list-of irritating things that are everyday but still really annoying.  Post yer own:</p>\r\n<ul><li>Lab mice (computer mice) that are all assmonkeyed tracking-wise so it\'s almost impossible to use them.</li>\r\n<li>Leaving a console thinking, \"Oh, I\'ll be back right away to write that paper ref down.  I remember the search words even if somebody nabs the terminal.\" And then forgetting the keywords.\r\n</li>\r\n<li>Lab keyboards where you have to have Herculean fingers to exert enough force to get the banged-out keys to realize you\'re touching them.</li>\r\n<li>Crapintoshes everywhere becuase of the outright bribery of higher educational institutions by Apple.  Apple == Arrogance Produces Profit losing Enterprises</p>\r\n<li>Getting insta-carpal-tunnel by touching one of those damned newfangled iMac hockey-puck mic or minisculeKeys(TM) keyboards.</li>\r\n<li>Anything having to do with the DMV.  Feh.</li>\r\n<li>Wanting to fry something in olive oil becuase it\'ll taste good but knowing you\'ll burn yourself and have a mess to clean because it\'s so poppy.</li>\r\n</ul>'),(743,'<p>\r\ni can remember on my dads farm when he would gather up all the leaves into a big pile and set them a blaze. they would go up almost instantly in one big blast of heat and flame. tonnes of black bits would fall to the ground and there would be this fantastic smell in the air. i dont quite know how to describe the smell. for some reason the word \"clean\" and \"pure\" comes to mind. like burning compost. there is a smell of renewal in the air onces its totally burned and put back into the atmosphere and environment. sometimes when i go for drives into the country with my mom we smell burning leaves and we try to follow the smell. its just such a fresh smell. sorta like freshly cut grass.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(744,'<p>\r\nwoke up around 3:30. irritated about missing the day. i don\'t want to sleep all day. it\'s cloudy out, raining. i said to swinney on chat \r\n</p><p><tt>[<br>\r\nwake up the first thing i do is open the laptop next to my head. it feels depraved to me to do this, it feels like a spiral down into the hell of chat and amotivation. like i may as well wake up and shoot heroin<br>\r\n]</tt></p><p>\r\n\"there\'s no way i\'m getting out of bed.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nand i paged my lover who i alternately feel is a healthy escape or a broken escape, a dependence, i don\'t know, i paged him with \"save me.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut he\'s soft, i can\'t keep from touching him. sometimes i feel upset because i know other people do not experience him as calming. and i can\'t explain it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand so i press myself against him and become a stupid mushy pile of a girl \"oh\" i say, \"oh i feel sick,\" and he will pet me. now he promises to bring me coffee, now he promises to wash my hair. \"i will even wash your back\" he says.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand so for the moment i am a female of the tribe, bloody down there and full of hormone, feeling primal and amazon maybe because of this big head of swamp hair i have now. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni had to step over a mountain of dirty clothes and work my way through an obstacle course of empty and half empty packing boxes to make it to the bathroom today, to wash my face and pee, to come back in the room and find some dirty hole filled clothes to put on top of each other on top of my dirty hole filled body. \r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday is the opposite of yesterday: a motorcycle ride through town, the smell of grass cuttings on dolores street and spring oh spring. \r\n</p>'),(745,'<p><font face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\"><br>\r\n  We are tired and ready<br>\r\n  to sing <a href=\"http://www.orbital.fr/briac/vu/files/vu&nico.lyr.html#1\">\"Sunday \r\n  Morning\"</a><br>\r\n  We are in rapture and strawberries (between them lies a lifetime) <br>\r\n  What\'s left is an \r\n  emergency abstract connection that no longer enchants or entertains <br>\r\n  Radio \r\n  reforms me - subversive lines for your test of taste<br>\r\n  An empty look and a sex starved room on the edge of her world.<br>\r\n  I\'m not going to be recollecting your dream.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  She says he can sum up my humanity: My humanity is horizontal.<br>\r\n  She says that her kiss is the monopoly of my dolphin skin <br>\r\n  She Says there are no surprises I can prove.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  Her sluggish inspiration lags<br>\r\n  with socialist matadors<br>\r\n  and emotional terrorists. <br>\r\n  I remix her mind to cohere with yours. <br>\r\n  In \r\n  this road to a devastated state <br>\r\n  I take the time to<br>\r\n  thank her for saving my life<br>\r\n  without wearing makeup.<br>\r\n  It \r\n  can\'t last,<br>\r\n  her and her Versace blanket,<br>\r\n  while i\'m stuck in the same familiar pain.<br>\r\n  Next time, wear less clothes.<br>\r\n  Can you take me to a 5\' 9 blonde<br>\r\n  dillusion, who shares my <br>\r\n  constitution to this city?<br>\r\n  Until i\'m sane and girl conquest # 1 <br>\r\n  is older I bid you<br>\r\n  forgivenss for something<br>\r\n  written under the influence of a Blazin Blue Fruit Roll Up.</font></p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\">$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$<br>\r\n  A work of art that contains theories is <br>\r\n  like an object on which the price tag has been left.<br>\r\n  M. P.<br>\r\n  </font><font face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\">$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$<br>\r\n  If a little dreaming is dangerous, the cure for it is not to dream less<br>\r\n  but to dream more, to dream all the time.<br>\r\n  M.l P.</font> </p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/745_1.gif\'>'),(746,'<p>Again, for your viewing pleasure:</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/746_1.gif\'>'),(747,'today on a mailing list someone asked if anyone had known anyone who died.  i wrote this.\r\n<p>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\nNo stories but:\r\n\r\n<li>8 years old: my grandmother who had lived with us my entire life\r\n<li>11 years old: jr. life guard instructor with sudden cerebral hemmhorage\r\n<li>14 years old: my father in car accident\r\n<li>15 years old: a close friend\'s father via cancer\r\n<li>15 years old: my best friend\'s father via cancer\r\n<li>17 years old: my brother\'s close friend committed suicide\r\n<li>17 years old: my sister\'s best friend via heroin od\r\n<li>18 years old: my brother\'s best friend committed suicide (after e-trip went bad)\r\n<li>18 years old: my other grandmother\r\n<li>19 years old: another jr. life gaurd coach.  committed suicide in jail.     \r\n<br><br>\r\nthere are lots of other people who i knew but not well.  both eye doctors  i\'ve ever had died.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni was camping once and a man had a heart attack.  i stood and watched people attempt to save him and fail.\r\n<br><br>\r\na girl on my swim team died while scuba diving.\r\n<br><br>\r\na kid in jr. high was hit by a truck and killed in front of the school.\r\n<br><br>\r\nall those people and i\'ve only ever been to three funerals.\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n<p>\r\nok, the thing that scares me about that post is i wrote it with no emotion.  i wasn\'t intending to, really, but by no means did i want to devote any energy to posting to that list which is mostly people saying intentionally schlocky things.  after i hit send my heart started to race and fears welled up inside me.  is it horrible to even have the ability write that without the emotion?\r\ni feel programmed to worry about this.   '),(748,'<B>Vegan Laksa</B>\r\n<LI>Get yourself a big wok\r\n<LI>Stirfry some onions\r\n<LI>Add a couple of teaspoons of green curry paste\r\n<LI>Toss in other assorted veges, like bok choi, brocoli, cauliflower, spinach and capsicums\r\n<LI>Pour in a can of coconut cream\r\n<LI>add the rind and juice of a couple of limes and some fresh coriander\r\n<LI>Bring to the boil and simmer briefly\r\n<LI>Cook some noodles (the kind I use are called Ezy Noodles, I guess they\'re sort of Ramen noodles)\r\n<LI>Add the cooked noodles to the  bubbling broth\r\n<LI>Stir well and serve, garnished with more coriander and lime rind\r\n<LI>Enjoy!'),(749,'<B>Vegan Laksa</B>\r\n<LI>Get yourself a big wok\r\n<LI>Stirfry some onions\r\n<LI>Add a couple of teaspoons of green curry paste\r\n<LI>Toss in other assorted veges, like bok choi, brocoli, cauliflower, spinach and capsicums\r\n<LI>Pour in a can of coconut cream\r\n<LI>add the rind and juice of a couple of limes and some fresh coriander\r\n<LI>Bring to the boil and simmer briefly\r\n<LI>Cook some noodles (the kind I use are called Ezy Noodles, I guess they\'re sort of Ramen noodles)\r\n<LI>Add the cooked noodles to the  bubbling broth\r\n<LI>Stir well and serve, garnished with more coriander and lime rind\r\n<LI>Enjoy!'),(750,'<p>\r\nso me corey and ummmmm derek were coming out of the blockbuster on a friday night. we had rented a doc called \"grass\", and chicken run. We also purchased a bag of \"munchies\". it was blatantly telling the person at the cash we were about to get shitfacedstonedoutofourskullsandwatch movies. as we were walking back there we see this guy standing right upto a garbage can, ass towards the street, urinating. It didnt really take me by suprise or discust me. I just felt like it was innapropriate. like there were pubs all around and eateries. if he really needed to go, he could have put a little effort into the task. corey started walking really fast. \"gettin away\". i dissed him for changing his lifestyle because of that. he claimed it wasnt changing his lifestyle, he was just repulsed by it. i did not understand. its just pee(nis). a guy will watch a porn with a woman being rammed in the cornhole, but runs away from a man releiving himself in a garbage can. infact, just two weeks ago when i was downtown with rob, we were parked on a side street and i was pretty buzzed up i had to pee. i saw the opportunity to pee behind a hedge and took it. it was inapropriate i know, but whatever. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nback to the dude we saw on bank. the funny part about this whole story is that when he knew we knew what he was doing he said in a low kinda saucy voice \"how ya doing boys?\". I think we all said something, except corey. so anyways, that was a friday night adventure on bank street. \r\n</p>'),(751,'<P>sittin in the coal mine<BR><BR>\r\nworkin downtown<BR><BR>\r\nworking in a coal mine of the mind<BR><BR>\r\nand swinney.org is here to provide me with thoughts about peeing on ATMs, antidepressants, bisexuality, and death.<BR><BR>\r\nIt\'s great!<BR><BR>\r\nMy most recent tragedy is a loss of feeling for my lover.  I moved out and now I feel...numb.<BR><BR>\r\nI want to love him, so I try, but there is an insulation all around me (asbestos of the soul) that prevents my heart from being open. It\'s very typical and very ironic that as soon as I realize that I feel this way--or rather, DON\'T feel--he is all concilatory and loving and wants to rub glitter all over my body.  It\'s maddening.<BR><BR>\r\nThen again, it\'s nice to be the cold and calculating one for once.  I didn\'t know it would feel so...empty.  I thought that once I had mastered indifference, I would be the winner.  But I want my bleeding heart back!  Oh well, soon enough I\'m sure it will start throbbing and squirming around in there again.  Right now it\'s traumatized into a sort of Greek serenity, polished and hard with lots of windy sky.  <BR><BR>\r\nIs this how men feel all the time?  I sure don\'t feel like myself.  I\'m thinking more clearly and without any emotional attachment to things.  Maybe I\'m just growing up. '),(754,'MUNI AVENGER STORY #3\r\n<p>\r\ntoday, i was unlucky enough to be traveling on muni today at the hour between 2:30-3:30pm. usually these hours are avoided due to the high concentration of squealing, baying and grunting young adults, fighting through their \'growing pain years\' and trying to goad a bus full of tired 40 somethings into fileting their guts just to get some peace and quiet.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe offenders: two youngish kids screaming \'Yo Momma\' jokes at each other, alternating between only offensive to shrill shrieking. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe victims: countless tired older men and women, just wanting to get home. one father and a 5 year old impressionable boy, who was paying attention despite the efforts of his father at drowning them out.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe action: after some particularly lame Yo Momma entries (\'yo momma is so fat.. she\'s the same width as her height heh heh heh\'). i prepared myself to join in with some pro-momma remarks. just to point out that these parasites were being stupid, loud, sexist, and they should be kissing the ground that their long suffering mommas walked on. </p><p>\r\n\"yo momma is so good, she works her ass off to feed your ungrateful asses\"<br>\r\n\"yo momma is so nice, she buys you your favorite cereal without you asking. and, your momma is so hard working, she washes your skid-marked underwear every week without any thanks from you.\"<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"you talking to us?\"<br>\r\n\"i was just trying to point out how mean those jokes are. i know you\'re bored, but can\'t you be quiet? we don\'t want to listen to you.\"<br>\r\n\"shit, we\'ll talk all we want, when we want.\"<br>\r\n\"so? so can i.\"<br>\r\nthere was some silence then some defensive remarks coupled with some attacks on my momma. after some more grumblings, they went on playing the game, but it was pretty half hearted after that. i got off the bus soon after. i\'m sure if there were more of them, older and more sophisticated, i would have been laughed off the bus. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni wish those kids the best. hope they thought about what they were saying, in a deeper context, but it probably won\'t happen for another ten years or so, at least.\r\n</p>'),(755,'<p>\r\nottawa is a pretty rich town. ottawa and her suburbs add upto just under a million. the city is for the most part government. union town. for this reason ottawa gets a good number of homeless drifters. the people on the street are somewhat conservative, but there is a much more small town mentality here. people will give a guy on the street change. there are some pros, some regulars, and some new faces. tonight on the way home; crossing sommerset on bank, a thirtyish dude came upto me pretty drunk asking for a cigarette. i dont give money out, but i do give cigarettes out. i said sure and handed him one. he appologized to me for being so fucked up and thanked me for the cigarette from what seemed like the bottom of his heart. there was so much in his eyes. sadness. trembling in his apology. it struck me as being odd. as though i was his AA sponsor. i smiled and said it was ok and told him to \"be cool brothah, you have a good night\" starting to walk home once more.\r\n</p>'),(756,'<P>i went to capoeira with darwin tonight.  it was ten times more fun than any group activity i have ever done.  what can i say about it to you?  \r\n</P><P>\r\nit was very physical. it was like:\r\n</P><P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n!weeb> *crawls around on my hands kicking in the air wildly*\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nplaying like a kid kind of only the dispatched instructor kept telling me to keep my elbow up and in front of my face so that i didnt get softened up by the competition.  and he kept telling me to cross  like and X and to LISTEN to him.  you are not listening, he would say.  </P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nascii capoeira moves by footstep\r\n\r\n      _\'_   ^ \r\n|\\ /|     |   |\r\n| V |\r\n\r\nrinse and repeat\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\ni crawled around and fell over and panted and licked sweat from my upper lip.  a couple of times i farted.  it was intense.\r\n</P><P>\r\nafter the practice was the games.  you have to maintain the circle.  you have to clap.  you have to sing.  you do not have to get in the ring the first month.  then you have to get in the ring.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nif you do not sing, or clap, the master will go home.  he does not watch show offs.  he does not eat dinner with people who do not help cook and clean.  he sometimes leaves his front door open so his friends can come in.  but sometimes total strangers will come in the house and steal stuff.  that is the analogy for the play.  the ring is the house and you have to listen for him opening the door, \"HAY!\", or \"PLINK!\", on the wire thingy.  so do not leave you house open, but do jump in the ring when the door is opened with a hay or a plink.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand you must love your fellows.  must be cool.  do not try to show off.  play with them.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand if you smoke weed, or drink whiskey, or have a girlfriend or a boyfriend, it is okay with him.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut do not try to view the site on linux with netscape because it is not compliant: <a href=\"http://www.omulu.org/\">omulo.org</a>\r\n\r\n</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/756_1.jpg\' alt=\"i am darwin the badass, look: i have a bulge in my pants!\">'),(757,'<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">\"although \r\n  we\'ve forgotten how to swim...<br>\r\n  I think, perhaps,<br>\r\n  since I saw you lying there<br>\r\n  naked on the beach<br>\r\n  maybe be could be friends.\"<br>\r\n  j. ellis</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">++++++++++++++++++<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  Speed through the divorce of skin and ambition.<br>\r\n  I am killing time by sleeping on the floor<br>\r\n  irked with the same positions of your body, I proposed to<br>\r\n  land in your vicinity<br>\r\n  lost like a tourist with a jellyfish shirt,<br>\r\n  imploring for directions to the<br>\r\n  soloist bed you keep. Don\'t break the lenguage<br>\r\n  guarded by the searchlight that is your smile.</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">Don\'t \r\n  consider your body<br>\r\n  a good meter for my love.<br>\r\n  Too many sounds. </font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">Man \r\n  made me like this, God approved<br>\r\n  Expecting that i would meet you.<br>\r\n  </font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">++++++++++++++++++++</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">\"The \r\n  night is eternally in the sewer, in exile with an<br>\r\n  optical waterfall. Lovely body, oh, lovely body like a<br>\r\n  white paper flag. I\'ve surrendered. God does not take<br>\r\n  captive. God does not take captive. Except for me.\"<br>\r\n  n.c.</font><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/757_1.gif\' border=\"0\" alt=\"the act of not believing.\">'),(758,'<PRE>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni ran out of gas outside\r\nstill like nature\r\nrain falls on my gears\r\nrusting, vines encrouch\r\ni will never move again.\r\n</PRE>'),(759,'<p>i only wanted to write<br>\r\nthe things you make me feel<br>\r\nand bury my SELF<br>\r\nmy emptiness<br>\r\nwith you<br>\r\nand maybe it was a certain sadness i sought<br>\r\na dull ache that squeezes out lines of beauty<br>\r\nlike orange pulp<br>\r\nbut i don\'t know sadness now<br>\r\nand i only know this day\'s taste<br>\r\nand now this day\'s taste<br>\r\nand so onward<br>\r\nand onward<br>\r\nand i start to get teary-eyed<br>\r\nand lumpy-throated<br>\r\nwhen i realize it\'s not me driving this machine<br>\r\nbut a mass of prejudice and lies<br>\r\nan infestation of decay<br>\r\na prison body of destruction<br>\r\nand not the deep admiration<br>\r\nthe love i feel for you<br>\r\nand i am ashamed to be weak<br>\r\nand imperfect<br>\r\nwhen you are so deserving<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\nand for tonight at least<br>\r\nthere is no resolve to this chord<br>\r\nsummed up in a skipping diminished seventh<br>\r\nhalf a rim shot and a drum roll that never stops<br>\r\nwe could wait for the curtain<br>\r\nthen we\'d know for certain<br>\r\nbut it\'s getting late<br>\r\nand there\'s useless drivel to worry about<br>\r\nand fuck me<br>\r\nplease<br>\r\njust fuck me</p>'),(760,'<p>Just for you!</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/760_1.gif\'>'),(761,'<p align=\"left\"><br>\r\n  <img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/logo.jpg\" width=\"436\" height=\"118\"> \r\n</p>\r\n<div align=\"left\"></div>\r\n<table width=\"44%\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"10\">\r\n  <tr> \r\n    <td width=\"37%\"><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/cowboy.jpg\" width=\"192\" height=\"120\"></td>\r\n    <td width=\"63%\"><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"3\" color=\"#333333\"><a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/welcometothebebop/main.html\">Cowboy \r\n      Bebop </a></font></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr> \r\n    <td width=\"37%\"><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/ghost.jpg\" width=\"108\" height=\"166\"></td>\r\n    <td width=\"63%\"><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" color=#333333 \r\n            size=1><font size=\"3\"><a href=\"http://fantagraphics.com/artist/clowes/ghostfilm.html\">Ghost \r\n      World</a></font></font></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr> \r\n    <td width=\"37%\"><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/radio.jpg\" width=\"113\" height=\"168\"></td>\r\n    <td width=\"63%\"><a href=\"http://www.greenplastic.com\">Radiohead</a></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr> \r\n    <td width=\"37%\"><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/josie.jpg\" width=\"130\" height=\"152\"></td>\r\n    <td width=\"63%\"><a href=\"http://www.josiecentral.com/#\">Josie </a>(don\'t get \r\n      jealous <a href=\"http://www.maximonline.com/girlfriend_of_day/girl_577.html\">Dominique</a>)</td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>'),(762,'<p>I need some imput from all dream couch analysts: I dreamed I was climbing up a mountain slope in the jungle somewhere...my mother was with me and we were having a difficult time struggling up the slopes...finally, gulping for breath, heaved ourselves up to the edge of the mountain, and looking down, saw that we had climbed the volcano (had not seen any smoke or lava overflows)..kept looking down into the core and saw a vision of a heart beating, as though I were watching open heart surgery.  Mother said that it was going to blow soon and she turned into a black bird and flew away, leaving me there. The dream ends then and I awake, sweaty and panicking.  My mother died some years ago.</p>'),(763,'<pre><tt>\r\nshe arrives in slanting light\r\nthe bells of evening announce Her\r\nas outside\r\nthe sky wears a veil of stars\r\nHer trees are black lace\r\npressed close to Her sunset heart\r\nShe speaks words that sink\r\ninto our bones\r\nshe knows the oldest tongues\r\nand is fluent in the rustle \r\nof leaves and fall grasses\r\nShe sings in a lullabye wind\r\na twilight song low and sweet\r\nto cool fevered dreams\r\nShe strings the songs\r\nof the owl and the wolf together\r\nand wears the necklace at full moon\r\nShe is the wild woman everywhere\r\nspring blossoming\r\nShe is all light at the core\r\nand shines out through the cracks\r\nin our laughter\r\nShe says:\r\nshow me something I ain\'t seen\r\nshow me something I ain\'t done\r\ntell me something I don\'t know\r\ntell me a riddle\r\nthat I can\'t answer\r\n</tt></pre>'),(764,'<pre><tt>\r\nas a kid\r\ni used to get high\r\nby spinning\r\ntwirling in place\r\nclothes swirling around me\r\nmy  mind\r\nthe pole star\r\nand the rest of the world\r\nin time/lapse\r\nslow motion\r\n</tt></pre>'),(765,'<P>This naked and clumsy girl threw herself in my bed. I gave her some sheets of paper to read.   She lacks grace and her bed manners are amusing to me. She says:\r\n<P>-I want to talk for hours and unravel your unspoken background. \r\n<P>-There\'s nothing to be discovered. I\'m just a lonely person.\r\n<P>-Lonely!! What are you talking about? You got drunk with your friends the other night.\r\n<P>-\"Loneliness is not always a lack of companions. Sometimes It is merely a lack Of someone who can read my soul.\" \r\n<P>This room can\'t escape the silence. I let her run her hands all over me. She crawls into my lap. After unspecified kisses I whisper a secret: \r\n<P>-What a poor custodian I was of those two great loves I once had\"'),(766,'<p>\r\nmy legs hurt from all the running, climbing, and avoiding. the police all dolled up in their green riot gear, the rcmp in their dark blue. behind jersey barriers with a nine foot fence erected, they stood in a line. sheilds and bats at the ready. every so often a dude with a 37mm hand held launcher would either run up and shoot tear gas at the feet of people. aswell as shooting at the people close to the fence, the police shot pop flies, the gas creating an arch, like the exaust of a plane in flight. they were using a few different CS gas devices. hand held grenade style, 12 GA shotgun, 37mm launcher. there were a few different sizes shapes and methods for each type of can. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe whole city smelled of tear gas. at once point i was trapped in a corridor of death and got blasted in the back with a water cannon, protecting my camera by folding myself over it. as soon as the water had bee shut off, a cs gas cannister blew up right beside me covering me in white dust. immediately i began to go blind and my nose began to run, snot running down my face. i ran to a safe distance, sat down and took off my sweater and pants and shook them out. covering myself in vinager and other mixtures people would kindly spray on me i felt better after about 15 minutes. i was wet and sand and dirt had washed up into my cloths. i went back. continued to take pics. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>the water cannon</b><br>\r\nthe water cannon, from what i can gather isnt even meant to hurt you. its meant to wash all the cs gas up off the ground and into liquid form, so that it penetrates cloths and send skin hurling into itcy madness. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni shot nine rolls of film. i think i shot more, but in all the running a few rolls were lost. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor the most part people were not violent. by the time we left tho, madness had started. frustrated, and out of water, people began to drink beer. a few hours before we left i was at an intersection where about 500 protesters were hurling rocks at police blocking a road. the water cannon shifted from spot to spot, however gas was being launched all over. from afar you could see huge clouds of white smoke rising through all the buildings. protesters were hitting whatever they could to make noise and drum up support. everytime a cop was hit with a rock cheers would spread. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe saw a lot of black bloc (anarchists) running around. being french, they were automatically crazy. these were the dudes id would have liked to follow. they were crazy. i saw a few filling up red gas cans at a petrol station. and a few were playing with butterfly knives.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nall in all it was a very intense time for me. i got up close and took pics. i was nervous but that didnt hold me back from taking pics and being where the action is. i hope my pics turn out. there were a lot of really good signs, nice people from all over the world, and for the most part the majority of hte demonstrators were kinds and non-violent. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe only draw back to the ftaa rally was the infighting that occured between all the groups there to protest. it made me mad and frustrated and many of the people there did not know what was happening and where. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas tokens of the mission i have two empty cs gas cannisters and a rubber bullet. \r\n</p>'),(767,'there are many kinds of zits but the worst ones are the ones that really hurt a lot but you can\'t squish them because they never get to the squishing point, they are just sort of hard round places on your face and then you mash them until you make the skin raw and all that happens is they hurt even worse but now you have a big ol\' sore on your face.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe best zits are somebody else\'s blackheads you can squish and they squeak when you squish them and you laugh and tell them to hold still because if they don\'t let you squish them they will get a boil and their face will rot off.<br><br>'),(768,'<p>\r\nhaving just gotten back from the ftaa rally im finding that walking down the street isnt quite the same. i take notice even more what is going on around me and im always expecting some sort of explosion. i think about things like mail boxes being around and that its not safe and that garbage cans, which were all gone in quebec city, were such easy targets for bombs. i look at walls and wish there was graffiti there and i see cop cars and want to spit on them. i expect gas at any moment and seeing crowds makes me nervous. i want to be there again. i want to be there for more. i want whats next so bad. im randy for the moment. like a travel bug, im experiencing withdrawl from one of the most intense adrenalin rushes of my life. something that proved to me i was capable of doing something id dreamed of doing all my life. i climbed a mountain on the weekend and im still sliding down. i hope i land softly. </p>'),(769,'<p>Do you ever get the feeling that the artist of a comic or an author or something was standing right next to you when something happened, and put it in their work?  This has happened to me several, nay, dozens of times (try working for a startup, and read Dibert).  But I think this instance is particularly strong, becuase the interchange in <a href=\"http://www.pvponline.com/archive.php3?archive=20010422\">this PvP Online comic</a> has happened almost <em>exactly</em> that way about a billion times between me and my beloved.  I had to smile.</p>'),(770,'<p>rainy, early.  the thunder and lightning--really, the best thing about the day--has stopped, though.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no lover to page, bother, surprise...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"and you were that interstate that i would roll my tanks down...\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think i\'ll have my mid-twenties coupland-esque crisis early.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>albany and boston are less than three weeks away, though.  we\'ll see.</p>'),(771,' <PRE>\r\n                         To The Thought That One \r\n                         Could Ever Leave- \r\n  HOME \r\n  there \r\n  on a field of condoms and crack vials \r\n  victims again of their conspired denials \r\n  where their nights are dark \r\n  and their days darker \r\n  where some choose to conceive \r\n  knowing \r\n  where encoded bruises \r\n  are left behind \r\n  a quick-fix of love or potions \r\n  to relieve discomforts \r\n  of hidden emotions \r\n  treated once again \r\n  with such violent elegance \r\n  reduced to a role \r\n  a lifeless soul \r\n  with so little time \r\n  to savor and suffer \r\n  they manage to pass \r\n  the desperate hours \r\n  with naked expression \r\n  with shattered hearts \r\n  it was left behind \r\n  their hysterical society \r\n  where they were different \r\n  at least to them \r\n  please not speak of virtue \r\n  as too little would be certain \r\n  and please not speak of verdicts \r\n  that may return to you \r\n  the time goes on \r\n  for robert and brigham and evelyn and roger \r\n  for alfred and his brother \r\n  for billy and his mother \r\n  for all once were \r\n  so they still are \r\n  a part \r\n  of \r\n  home \r\n  V O I C E L E S S   W I S H E S \r\n  there \r\n  behind a poorly hidden inviting smile \r\n  with an indented midsection and a biscuit butt-- \r\n  early on the rise and calves--slickedy smooth \r\n  still awkward in beauty though complete in composition \r\n  unable to hide the tremoring fingers \r\n  and screaming anticipation \r\n  surges of instinct and stampeding excitement \r\n  dashed the barriers of consciousness that had \r\n  rendered unattended our desperate affections \r\n  left incapable of pretending not to know \r\n  sacredest territories were suddenly exposed \r\n  anonymous desires and unclaimed wishes \r\n  met for nourishment and hugs & kisses \r\n  a relaxed entrance lined with welcome and \r\n  thought filled motions in united isolation \r\n  in space rendered undisguised and obvious \r\n  with truth and fidelity and unquestioned sincerity \r\n  unwithheld sounds of inner excitement \r\n  through unchartered passages and internal delightment \r\n  we quietly laid in our sacred cuddle \r\n  binded by evidence and mingled perspiration \r\n  in what was left of our mutual explanation \r\n  rich with account and certain in spirit \r\n  the rare spare moments in life when we live it \r\n  THE BUFFALO ROADHOUSE   (1981) \r\n  i can take it \r\n  the west side highway again \r\n  tough getting over \r\n  the loss of some friends \r\n  at happ\'ning joints \r\n  we sat for hours \r\n  swappin pasts and dreams \r\n  fast food and sours \r\n  and as we grew \r\n  one thing we knew \r\n  from what we\'d been through \r\n  and all the care \r\n  that in our future \r\n  we\'d each be there \r\n  he moved his lips \r\n  he raised his head \r\n  the n.y. times said \r\n  150 might surely be dead \r\n  and then the wonder \r\n  paralyzing wonder \r\n  the mother and father \r\n  of lightning and thunder \r\n  in its speed it surely came \r\n  sparing none with its blame \r\n  shadowy cremations \r\n  revealed the shame \r\n  i thought i failed \r\n  somewhere in our talks \r\n  cause i got precautious \r\n  early in my walk \r\n  with amended realities \r\n  i look ahead these days \r\n  thanking the powers \r\n  i could change my ways \r\n  i recall the bounce \r\n  of that highway beneath \r\n  of all it had meant \r\n  and all it could teach \r\n  TATTOOS DON\'T BLEED ON DEAD SKIN \r\n  there \r\n  we were taken long before we were ready \r\n  through barely lit rooms of horror and pain \r\n  where air is shared with unwelcoming scents \r\n  bouquets of antiseptic and cancer and the plague \r\n  surrounded by shelves up high in rows \r\n  candy jars filled with formaldehyde and hearts \r\n  tendered most naively then very sharply broken \r\n  not once or twice but over and over and over again \r\n  and not out of necessity no not out of need \r\n  broken for one reason: because they could be \r\n  my eyes they burned at every turn \r\n  i saw an archway short and narrow \r\n  and hit both sides fighting to get through \r\n  to the room of remains all open for view \r\n  in their clothes saturated not only with them \r\n  but with flies and worms and slugs and bugs \r\n  and empty boxes for dahmer and manson and his gang \r\n  in this room they kept the murdered saints \r\n  with a running nose i tried not to breathe \r\n  the lights went out so i had to feel \r\n  ahead down below to a faint flickering glow \r\n  to a forsakened place where angels no longer go \r\n  met by escorts we were silently led \r\n  through a corridor like life \r\n  where souls on fire are just passed by \r\n  a telling quiet the only warning \r\n  before sounds of air rushing thru their flames \r\n  staggering stuns strike long after the pain \r\n  not once or twice but over and over and over again \r\n  i begged the escorts to help me get back \r\n  i wanna go back to nana and the pact \r\n  if not for long for just a little while \r\n  back to the late late show \r\n  the million dollar movie \r\n  the overbearing seventies \r\n  the harrad experiment and the stepford wives \r\n  billy jack and the pam grier flicks \r\n  a time when hate seemed to decline \r\n  any time in my life that was pre aids \r\n  not hard to see this was not gonna be \r\n  heard by my ears eyes and soul: depend on us not \r\n  we speak only the language of convenience \r\n  no longer led we were now being pushed \r\n  into a room of memories and primitive senses \r\n  the short lived days of incomplete duets \r\n  times of promise and times of ignorance \r\n  with vanishing images and voices unseen \r\n  terrorizing fear recreates the view \r\n  up on the wall presented cinematically \r\n  were replays of harm and base humiliation \r\n  imposed degradation and personal moments of shame \r\n  times we wished God was not watching \r\n  uncomfortable times when life painfully describes \r\n  for us  who   we   were    then       then \r\n  i was tapped on the shoulder by the Saviour in me \r\n  saying wake up wake up do this not for Thee \r\n  or was it a necessary or unnecessary torment \r\n  for seasons of fallacy and spells of fraud \r\n  or for when and why we chose not to see \r\n  how the feelings of one affects us all \r\n  and the immeasurable value of every soul \r\n----- \r\nThanks for your time.  I wrote this work 8 years \r\nago. These are four of my first five poems, \r\npresented above in the order they were written. \r\nThe material is copyrighted.  You have my \r\npermission to print, copy, download, save, \r\nand email to a friend for a small \r\ndonation.  I recommend fifty cents per poem. \r\nIt\'s up to you.  I trust you. \r\n   J.C.Y.H. #211 \r\n   1614-O Union Valley Rd. \r\n   #211 \r\n   West Milford, New Jersey O748O \r\nIf you might be interested, the manuscript of \r\nmy first play, COMPANY, will soon be available online. \r\nIt is not available to minors.  Drop me an email at \r\nreply@justclickyourheels.com \r\n-------- \r\nThank you again, and be well.\r\n</pre>'),(772,'<P>I am so happy to hear that someone went into battle, as it were, and came back unharmed to tell the story.  </P><P>\r\nThere is a very frightening dynamic emerging right now as our government begins to unmask its ugly self and show itself as the corporate tyranny it really is.  Bush isn\'t interested in, nor is he smart enough to uphold, appearances.  This is a good thing for those of us who have always known that \"free trade\" meanscorporate and rule exploitation of people and the environment; it means that Joe Blow may finally get the picture and say to himself, \"Wow, the government is in bed with a bunch of greedy corporations.  They\'re no longer even attempting to disguise this as democracy.  My rights as a citizen are being violated.\"  When this happens, the \"leftists\"--students, intellectuals, activists, and other people who occupy their time with all of the noxious activities of Big Government--may finally be able to pull some of the apathetic wool back from the fat middle classes of America.  And this is what we have to hope for.  Once the middle class begins to grumble, it\'s just a matter of time before the corporations will at least make an effort to look better.  As well as covering their tarry tracks, this may mean that they actually clean up their acts.  The more transparency we demand, the more they will be held accountable for what they do in the Holy Name of Profit.  Going to the rally in Quebec and taking pictures in spite of risked and actual bodily harm is, in my opinion, a heroic act.  Thank you.</P><P>\r\nMy next question for any and all of you is:  Who do we write to in order to voice our opinions?  It sounds like Congress has been circumvented on this \"Treaty,\" and I\'ll bet that some of them are pissed about it, and feel stupid. Who do you guys think are the main players (state and federal)as far as stopping this horrible so-called \"Agreement?\" I shudder to think that the document could be signed, sealed, and delivered without ever having to pass through Congress (and thereby ostensibly through our approval as well).  I wrote my letter about it and then realized I didn\'t know who to send it to.  Any suggestions would be greatly appreciated.</P><P>\r\nMy other thought on this issue is that it is an interesting manifestation of the collective Shadow, both in terms of the \"evil corporations,\" from my viewpoint, and the \"dark and maniacal anarchists\" from the bureaucrats\' view.  I hope that we can integrate these two warring factions and make some sensible legislature.  I trust that integration is inevitable.  I just hope we don\'t all end up in a bubble on Mars before we learn how to do it.</P>\r\n<P>POWER TO THE PEOPLE</P>\r\n'),(773,'the saddest story ever told.'),(774,'<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">Poetry \r\n  is no substitute for survival.</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">Will \r\n  you burst under pressure my dear?<br>\r\n  Is the sign \"Handle with Care\" on my chest <br>\r\n  making you think twice?<br>\r\n  Will you deliver me from nature?<br>\r\n  The lightning, the roses<br>\r\n  and the majority of green.<br>\r\n  Bombard me with the listings of my pressured collection:<br>\r\n  a pretty girl, her dress, and<br>\r\n  the clothes I swim through to care for you.</font></p>\r\n<font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I \r\ntry composing her image:<br>\r\nher skin, colorless.<br>\r\nher eyes, universal.<br>\r\nShe came to me with the smell of silence<br>\r\nand the taste of tranquillity.<br>\r\n</font> \r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I \r\n  </font><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"> \r\n  left her world because I didn\'t belong there. <br>\r\n  I drained my ideas. I stopped tasting water.<br>\r\n  A girl named me again and again.<br>\r\n  She forced me to remember fiction.<br>\r\n  I stray, glancing at the video girl for cosmic reasons. <br>\r\n  </font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I \r\n  can\'t die from lack of attention? <br>\r\n  Damn it, she\'s part of my master tape which can\'t be erased.<br>\r\n  </font></p>\r\n<p> <font color=\"#006699\" face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><i>\"And \r\n  when he dreams he does not want to write, he does not have the power to dream \r\n  he wants to write; and when he dreams he wants to write, he does not have the \r\n  power to dream he does not want to write\"</i></font><br><BR>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/774_1.jpg\'>'),(775,'<p>I\'ve resorted to eating in the break room w/ other people I think I\'m supposed to be working w/. And my lunches haven\'t been too tasty lately. But I guess this is my problem. Tomorrow I\'m going to make a sandwich. Probably a fake ham and cheese on a pita w/ sprouts and honey mustard.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\'t go for walks anymore. Well, that\'s a lie. I walked around the block today and then I went to check on my car, which was covered in elephant shit. And that was pretty much the highlight of my day. Wait, that\'s a lie--I saw a blonde Armenian. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Later, I drew something on my shoe. </p>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://home.earthlink.net/~jfilan/shoes/shoes.html\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/775_1.gif\'></A>\r\n'),(776,'<pre>\r\nit is our job to watch over the city\r\nwe hear everything.\r\n\r\na fog horn, a boat in the bay.\r\ngunshots.\r\n\r\nthe j church line, rattling down the tracks.\r\n\r\nsounds make their way up through the cold air:\r\n\r\nwhite noise of \r\nthousands of cpu fans\r\nwashing machines\r\ncar engines\r\nhumming street lights\r\npeople breathing in their sleep\r\n\r\nfrom our perch we are audio experts\r\ndocumenting the noises of a living city\r\non the insides of our heads,\r\nto each other out loud\r\n\r\n\"did you hear that?\" we will say, at 3am, after a striking noise\r\n\"what was that?\" we will say, decide, and file it away\r\n\r\nwe are the masters of\r\naural worlds that drift in through the open window\r\n\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(777,'<p>i work in the mailroom at an austin community college campus.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>professors tend to treat it as a lounge (neglecting to notice that they\'re in my way the few times i\'m up working); they grade papers, eat, chat, stand around so i have to make quick turns and find new, interesting routes around them to do my job in our small office.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sometimes, late in the day when most of the classes are in session and it\'s quiet in the mailroom with only a few instructors hanging about, i\'ll listen to death cab for cutie\'s \"the army corps of architects\" or some other song that makes me get that tingly feeling good music does to people, and i wonder if they feel it, too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i wonder if they\'re numb to it; they\'ve grown out of their passionate stages...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i think, \"god, i never want to become that way.  i never want to lose the ability or defect or whatever-it-is that allows me to feel extreme emotions.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sort of like rilke said, i\'m afraid if my demons leave, my angels may take flight as well.</p>'),(778,'<p>i live in sparks, which is next to reno.  i take a bus to work every day, and sometimes weird stuff happens, or doesn\'t happen, which is even weirder.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>last week it rained a bit, and when the sky in the valley is overcast and grey people here are like people everywhere else - they stay inside unless they have somewhere to be.  i had to be at work, plus i don\'t mind the rain so much, so i walked to my bus stop (which is right next to the sparks police station - the big one, not a substation).   usually i am there by myself, but that day the barking man was there, too.  he\'s also on the 18 every day, but he usually gets on at the Vista stop - not mine, which is on Lillard.  he was bobbing and weaving, which he does, and had a yellow raincoat on over his green army surplus coat.  it wasn\'t zipped, so he was wet anyway, but i don\'t think that was the point of the raincoat anyway.  i think he digs on the color and the material.  he looked at me, and woofed a little. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>the barking man (i think his name is duane - one of the bus drivers calls him that anyway) never talks, except to say \"thankyou\" - he does bark, though.  mostly at people, but sometimes at the bus or at the bus stop.  he is staccatto and loud.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he wasn\'t either that day last week, though.  he was subdued, and kept looking up at the clouds and wincing.  it was like he didn\'t want to disturb them, or maybe he\'d already pissed them off and didn\'t want to make matters worse.  He kept eyeing my bag, which made me kind of nervous.  I gave him a mushed hostess fruit pie i\'d found at work the day before, which he started to eat.  He said \"thankyou.\"   then the 18 came.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we got on, and he sat next to the old guy that whistles all the time.  this is  usually pretty entertaining.  the whistling guy and the barking man don\'t get along:  barking man barks, freaks whistling guy out who then whistles louder, then barking man barks louder, and this goes on until the bus driver makes the barking man move since he\'s the crazy one.  that day neither one whistled or barked.  every one of the regular riders looked around at each other - waiting, then wondering what was wrong.  the whistling guy and the barking man just stared out of the bus windows over the freeway overpass at the clouds and mountains. they were both still on the bus when i got off;  neither made a sound the whole ride.</p>\r\n'),(779,'<P>just thinkin...if I can give whatever it is I think that I lack or need, then </P><P>where did the problem go?\r\nWhether you are an atheist or some other category, what does that have to do with the capacity to be kind, to be tender?  Is it what you think about you that\'s important or whether or not you are really being helpful to someone else?\r\nIf you live your life like you was driving a car, are you looking ahead and checking out the scenery and paying attention to the road--or are you always checking the rear view mirror to check out how ya doing? What ya look like?\r\nI like what the Dalai Lama said: My religion is kindness.  Ya don\'t have to get any fancier than that or twist yourself in metaphysical pretzels for it to work.</P>'),(780,'<P> I think that too many of us wander around with nasty voices in our heads.  Disconnet those old tapes and trash em. Throw out oughta, coulda,shoulda,whydidnya.</P> <P>toldjadinti,whatsamattawithya,whyugotta,notgonnamakit...what i dont understand it why we put this stuff in there ourselves or we marry that ugly flat voice of accusation that has privately been vomiting bile into our minds and hearts all our lives.  Is that why men marry bitches and women marry sons-of-bitches? That voice can also be the sound of culture drone that likes to drown out individual dreams. Never ever marry someone whose voice externalizes the enemy inside.  At the first signs of contempt, I take my exit cues because life has shown me that it all goes downhill from there.</P><P>\r\nI want someone to tell me...WHY do some men choose bitches to fall in love with?</P>'),(781,'<P>I have always enjoyed hallucinating.  Whether as a kid, pressing my eyes and seeing phosphenes, or thinking I could see the molecules vibrating in the air, sort of a constant shimmer going on continously...to more complex eyelid movies...seeing fractals before fractals were called fractals...seeing colors emanate from and around people, much like the swirls of colors in bubbles...seeing the fingerprints of the wind is fascinating, not to mention spontaneous faces appearing in wood grained doors and panelling..and that spot on the sidewalk...and that bowl of cumulo-popcorn...and the ripples in the deep blue tissues of the fabric of our minds.</P>'),(782,' <p>Guess its my turn to rant tonight. I am a big woman and I am tired of getting snide looks by all these little whippets of women on the streets and in stores..what in the hell is this obsession with anorexia!  In a sense, it doesn\'t matter to me because I am what I am..a big, bold, bright and colorful woman.  But its these ever present gnats that swarm you that annoy me.  The gnats of \"fashion\", that buzz in your face constantly.There are days when it takes energy to ignore something that can be so insidious.\r\nOnce I decided that I am whom to please, my life got immeasurably better. I also think that one way to neutralize us is to keep us glued to trivialities and banalities. enough!</p>'),(783,'<font face=\"verdana\" color=\"blue\">Feeling pain in new and weird places. Its definitely not the kind of pain that rips you apart as you try to sleep, tossing and turning, weeping away the night. Its not a desperate pain or a longing pain. Its the kind of pain that comes when you rise up into crane 100 times in a night and when you sit in a horse stance for 1 solid minute, legs quivering, heart racing. I can do it, I can do it! It\'s a good pain! Good and productive and satisfying. </FONT>'),(784,'<p>\r\ni started spinning with rage after i smoked and drank. i was talking to her and i felt like hitting something hard. like unloading a nine into a barrel full of gasoline. i just fel angry. i felt angry at the selfishness that i was hearing and all the dead end answers and pokes to try and divert attention towards some kind of false problem of whose mistake was not hers. i lay down and got up and talked on the phone twice and twice i yelled and twice i said things i might not have if under different substances. liquer brings out the rage. brings out the darkness. it lets loose the vortex of anxiety and frustration and converts it into vulgar speech and raised voices. i dont regret anything i said. i still feel as though i ws composed and on topic. i feel as though im still ontop and without fault. ifeel as though ihave been used and as though fault has attempted to be turned towards me. i have lost a lot of respect for her tonight. weather or not that respect re-appears tomorrow is yet to be seen. ill wake up groggy and will hopefully have forgotten this episode. however as it stands, i remember all this bullshit and let the stuff memories are made up slide. relentless is life and as life moves forward i feel relentless pain and sorrow. pain runs deep and as i try and crawl out of this trench, i feel hands pulling me back down. hands that belong to bodies that want to merely use me. keep me around for a rainy day. her voice used to make me laugh. tonight it was wicked and selfish. when things least expect, i expect the least when. '),(785,'<p>\r\ni love that they call me weebi-chan at country station sushi. i\'ve been working there for the last two days, and i am liking it very much. weebi because my nickname was weeb, and chan means little one, i think. little in age or stature? hmm.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit was a little slow tonight, and i was standing around bored, when one of the sushi chefs- Toshi, asked me what i was drinking-- \"tea?\" i answered, confused- he firmly advocated that i should be drinking hot sake instead. he poured me some. in a big glass. a few minutes later, Hiroko came in with a six pack of heineken for us, and then i had a beer on top of the sake. tosh then broke out the fake asahi logo tattoos, and applied one to myself and maki-san (fellow waitress in training). they looked super cool, dude.\r\n</p><p>\r\nha! oh yeah! also, i discovered Hoichi-san, the grand patriarch of the country station family, asleep on the floor in the back room, tonight. head back, topknot slipped to the side. so cute.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou know who you are. come get some sushi soon.\r\n</p>'),(786,'<p>\r\nwith no further adieu, Leonard Cohen lyrics:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<pre><tt>There is a war between the ones who say there is a war\r\nand the ones who say there isn\'t.</pre></tt>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI have nothing to say when presented with an empty text box, no words to weave my little thoughts around.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nevery conversation I have is all about me right now.  maybe that wouldn\'t be true if I could figure out how to put words in empty boxes.  I would like that.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\non chat, I said this about something I said about something I said about swinney.org:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<pre><tt><font color=\"#996699\">03:33</font> &lt;jim&gt; I jump into calling things things all the time!</pre></tt>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI said it to <a href=\"http://steve.org\">s5</a> and <a href=\"http://elly.org\">le</a>.  they are frequently treated to such pearls of wisdom.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI really like it when the station announcers for KQED, San Francisco\'s NPR affiliate, make mistakes.  the frequency of KQED is 88.5, and this station announcer just came on to do a station identification, and started to say \"ninety\" when he got to the frequency.  I recognize his voice; it was the same station announcer who is on almost every night, and he\'s been there for years and years and years.    he sounded strange and confused when he corrected himself.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m not sure what his name is.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\n'),(787,'<P>it is no secret, everyone knows that these three nationalities, US, AU, NZ, have many similiar issues.  all three were colonized by English speakers from Brittain.  NZ can correct me there because i dont know much about that nation. but let me continue.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>we read together.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>we sleep together.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>we think things are cool together.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>all that is common knowlege.  i used to think that did not necessarily make us brothers and sisters.  but i always had a hunch we were.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>we all have the undying contempt from snotty Brits.  i worked with snotty Brittish and believe me, they do look down upon us.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i think it is funny they look down on us considering their culture is entirely more rotten than our own.  they have bad teeth and they put sugar on top of their fried eggs.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>but that is not where i got my proof that US, AU, and NZ are in the same boat.  where is my proof that we suffer the same colonial insecurity?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>listen up big moma....</P>\r\n\r\n<P>a certain multi national female named weight loss company that has made it\'s niche in the, \"do i look fat?\" department has offices in guess where?</P>\r\n\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>United States</LI>\r\n<LI>Australia</LI>\r\n<LI>New Zealand</LI>\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\n<P>I was not surprised to find this out.  it is 6:13 am in san francisco.  the sun is coming out.  i thought i would take this quiet opportunity to share my proof that US, AU, and NZ are all just a bunch of porky the pig watching colonials.</P>\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<a href=\"http://biz.yahoo.com/p/j/jc.html\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/787_1.gif\' alt=\'ebity ebity ima lil fatty\'></A></CENTER>'),(788,'<p>\r\nHello,\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am a third year artschool student from Australia (Hello! My name is Estelle) and for my Art Thoery unit I am studying self representation. I am interested in looking at web based journals, and why people write them.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI have just been looking at your web journal and I found the inisght into your life quite fascinating.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI have a set of questions that I am asking people I come accross who keep online journals, I hope you can spend some time answering them. \r\n</p><p>\r\nYou can email me at estelle@astro-girl.com with any questions or concerns.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThankyou and good luck!\r\n</p><p>\r\n </p><p>\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe Questions -\r\n</p><p>\r\n1. Why do you keep an online journal?\r\n</p><p>\r\n2. Do you have any issues/concerns with public/private space (sharing your thoughts with the world)\r\n</p><p>\r\n3. How truthful is your journal? (Please specify by percentage)\r\n</p><p>\r\n4. Is it important to yourself to be truthful?\r\n</p><p>\r\n5. Can you monitor the number of visitors, and if so, how many hits per day?\r\n</p><p>\r\n6. Do you see online journals becoming important documents of the web, reflecting internet \'culture\'?\r\n</p><p>\r\n7. Is your online journal a truthful representation of yourself?\r\n</p><p>\r\n</p><p> \r\n</p><p>\r\nTHANKYOU :)\r\n</p><p>\r\nEstelle (aka astrogirl)\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n '),(789,'<p>a lot of my friends are thinking about money.<BR>\r\nor, rather, the lack of it.</p>\r\n<p>a lot of my friends are wondering what they want to be doing in 6 months, in 5 years.</p>\r\n<p>most of my friends are unemployed, and looking.<BR>\r\nthose of us with jobs are crippled with guilt when we have gripes about our jobs because at least we have jobs.</p>\r\n<p>my friends who are students are ready to be out of school.<BR>\r\nmy friends who are out of school crave the security of being a student.<BR>\r\nall of us still think in terms of semesters rather than seasons, years.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>i was listening to modest mouse today and this song seemed to sum it up better than i have:</p>\r\n<pre>\r\n<b><a href=\"http://www.crystal-night.com/~bwillen/MMLyrics7.htm#WHE\">Whenever You Breathe Out, I Breathe In (Positive Negative)</a></b>\r\n \r\nHey, haven\'t seen you around in a while [x4]\r\nI didn\'t go to work for a month\r\nI didn\'t leave my bed for eight days straight\r\nI haven\'t hung out with anyone\r\n\'Cause if I did, I\'d have nothing to say\r\nI didn\'t feel angry or depressed\r\nI didn\'t feel anything at all\r\nI didn\'t want to go to bed\r\nAnd I didn\'t want to stay up late\r\nWhen youre living your life, well, that\'s the price you pay\r\nWhenever I breath out, you\'re breathing it in\r\nWhenever I speak out, you\'re speaking out\r\nI didn\'t go to work for a month\r\nI didn\'t leave my bed for eight days straight\r\nI haven\'t hung out with anyone\r\n\'Cause if I did, I\'d have nothing to say\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n'),(790,'<p>\r\nsitting in the classic white lawn chair; my left hand twirling some hair in what could quite possibly be subconscious sexual frustration, i ctrl a d pine i ent r s. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe phone might ring and i might pick it up. depending on the mail or the day at work. depending on the jar and boris. depending on a lot of things. i can predict phone calls and i guess thats why i dont like to pick it up sometimes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsome days start with silence. however when i notice there isnt any music on, i will immediately get a playlist going. these days ive been listening to a lot of electro, breakz, ukhardhouse(nunrg), indie rock, and suprisingly, jungle. ive almost stopped listening to the whole hardcore genre, tho i do listen to a lot of the classics off and on.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na lot of the time spend in the white chair is of confused stares and expectation of activity on the screen. alting between putty and netscape not much changes. sometimes ill bring up icq to see if anyone interesting is on and ill usually msg a few people something or other.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni dont surf. i dont look at the internet much anymore. i check the pages linked from my own and only around 1/3 of all links sent to me. i just find the net to be, for the most part, boring. there are a lot of interesting sites on the internet, and there is a lot of amazing shit to be found. ive just lost the urge to explore. when i need something i can usually find it pretty fast. I dont go off topic and wander. not like when i write. har har. \"i\" probably account for 2/3 of my hits. i re-read and change tiny little things constantly. things people probably shouldnt notice. spelling mistakes i make. grammar. stupid sentences that just dont belong. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen i nap, tidy, nap, tidy, check mail, and nap. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nboris keeps me company.\r\n</p>'),(791,'<P>\"Our deepest fear is not that we are inadequate.  Our deepest fear is that we are powerful beyond measure.  It is our light, not our darkness, that frightens us.  We ask ourselves, who am I to be brilliant, gorgeous, talented and fabulous?  Actually who are you not to be?  You are a child of God.  Your playing small doesn\'t serve the world.  There\'s nothing enlightened about shrinking so that other people won\'t feel insecure around you.  We are born to manifest the glory of God within us.  It\'s in everyone.  And as we let our own light shine, we unconsciously give other people permission to do the same.  As we are liberated from our own fear, our presence automatically liberates others.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>                       --Nelson Mandela   </P>'),(792,'<p>\r\nwell i got two of my colour rolls back. one of the rolls had a serious light leak due to getting knocked, or something. so only four of the shots from that roll came out. oh well. i got 8 more black and whites to come. i hope light leak doesnt occur again. ok so here are a few of the pics. the link to more of them is ^chiamattt. ok take it ease. \r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_1.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_2.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_3.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_4.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_5.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/792_6.jpg\'>'),(793,'<P>It would be rather interesting to know where others are located, just as a matter of curiousity more than anything else. No conclusions or generalizations. anyone???</P>'),(794,'<P>Has anyone ever read the sequel to Alice in Wonderland---it\'s called \"Through the Looking Glass\"...and in it there is a scene where Alice is seated under a tree and the portly Red Queen runs in a circle around the tree, running faster and faster...and faster.  Finally, Alice asks her why she is doing that.  The Red Queen replys, between huffs and puffs, \"I have to run faster and faster these days, my dear, just to remain where I am\"...Why does this remind me of our lives today?</P>'),(795,'I dont know about the Red Queen parable but I do believe its the Mad Hatters Tea Party.\r\nViable suggestions:\r\n1. Is it finally time to set the dial for the heart of the sun?\r\n2. Can we groove with small furry species at last?\r\n3. Please be careful with that ax, and hand over the dwarves and no one will get hurt.\r\n4.  Is is the old same place or the same old place?\r\n5. Are we waiting for the electrician,or someone like him/her?\r\n6.  Are you an anvil or a hammer?\r\n7.  We no longer have a natural sense of time or of the natural progression of seasons.  We are out of sync with the old and not yet in sync with the new.  But should we be?\r\nThere\'s more but that\'s enough for now.\r\n		'),(796,'<p>Why do i have such a difficult time going to sleep? Its like my brain wont shut off, just keeps burning on and on.  I get so few hours of sleep.  My eyes feel dry and I doubt my sanity...I could sleep for days, weeks if left alone.  what does it mean when the thing  you want most in life is to be left alone to float in the blissful sea of unconsciousness.  What if that means more to you than love or friendship or food or anything. what if you dont want to wake up at all? what if waking up is your worst nightmare? THis has been going on for years now.</p>'),(797,'<P>\r\nI\'m at a computer in the library of a college owned by Disney.  The tuition is 20 thou a year.  Sam goes here, and he may or may not be getting 20-thou-a-year\'s worth of an education.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI look around me, at this computer in this library at this college, and I see dreadlocks and dark plastic glasses frames and everything a little ratty and weird.  I look at them and think, they can afford to go here.  I\'m sure most of them have extremely rich parents...I\'m sure they can afford to brush their hair and wear nice clothes.  It\'s nice to be comfortable, that\'s true, but are these kiddies simply trying to hide, disguise, deny?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIf I had rich parents who were willing to pay Disney 20 thou a year so I could get an education, I don\'t know if I\'d be supposed to be here or not.\r\n</P>'),(798,'<p>thursday.  at my desk at work.  there is a piece of paper next to me, on it is scrawled:</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1 Drain-o<br>\r\n2 toothpaste<br>\r\n3 laundry<br>\r\n4 Juan<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>i\'ve taken care of number four, but i look at the first three and my heart jumps and they remind me why my bathtub is still clogged, my teeth are unbrushed, and i\'m not wearing any underwear.  things are ending.  tuesday night, sitting at the house of pies, i learned that my exgirlfriend and friend of three and a half years has fallen in love and is moving to new york.  she finished my chocolate cream pie because i lost my appetite.  aw heck, she moved from texas to be with me.  it didn\'t work out afterall.  i guess that\'s just the way it goes.</p>\r\n<p>it feels kind of nice to let little things slide for a change.  i\'m not on top of things.  i\'m down but i can feel a spark inside very clearly now.  i\'ve been here before many times, and this next reawakening is a bit overdue.  i shall paint again.  i shall make music again.  i shall have a website again.  i shall rent movies by myself and go running at dusk.  by myself.</p>\r\n<p>Â¤ freedom Â¤</p>  '),(799,'<p align=\"center\"><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">the house sleeps and \r\n  i am awake <br>\r\n  watching movies all night on ifc as i used to<br>\r\n  the last one was a fairy tale romance with a happily-ever-after ending <br>\r\n  i step over one roommate as he is passed out peacefully on the floor <br>\r\n  another lies deep in slumber as she is about to depart for jazzfest in new orleans \r\n  the next morning <br>\r\n  and the other, well, he is about to embark on the journey of his life <br>\r\n  and he, as well, is sleeping blissfully in his room</font> </p>\r\n<p align=\"center\"><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">i sit out on the front \r\n  porch with my pack of american spirits, medium<br>\r\n  and i wonder about who i am and where i am and what i am doing and<br>\r\n  </font><font face=\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\">who i will be when i awake \r\n  in the morning, that is, if i ever fall asleep tonight <br>\r\n  i am having lunch with my father tomorrow <br>\r\n  he has to check into md anderson in houston for a few days <br>\r\n  it\'s the one-year anniversary since he had his bladder removed<br>\r\n  cancer. smoking. <br>\r\n  as i choke down another cigarette <br>\r\n  and press my luck <br>\r\n  no whammies <br>\r\n  big bucks<br>\r\n  Stop. </font></p>'),(800,'<p>i flew through Texas and missed all of you Austin....too tired. feeling rushed. so i hid out in San Antone with an old friend. listening to the rain. walking the missions. then went North on 281. a nice green and flowered drive right now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the New Mexico desert was good to me. but after five days of camping, and hiking, and being alone.....i felt a bit faded.</p>'),(801,'<P>class followed by class followed by the usual rice and tofu lunch with heavy, heated discussions on consensus process and what i think is that when you come into a group situation, you\'re going to have to learn to compromise. and that your needs aren\'t always going to be met. consensus isn\'t a unanimous greement; you may not agree with something, but you can live with it and it\'s better off for the group. \r\n</P><P>\r\npeople are so selfish, the more selfish than others should not be members of co-operatives. \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut i\'m not thinking about methods of facilitating discussions anymore, my thoughts lie in my e-mail inbox and what is currently living there. maybe things are better left unsaid, but it\'s hard to find closure and it\'s hard to forgive when you don\'t know the motivations behind certain actions. \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut i\'m going to let it go. there is homework to be done and people to have fun with and tofu to be eaten. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni\'m not entirely happy with this swinney, but fuck it. here goes. \r\n</P>\r\n'),(802,'<p>What happened to all the characters?  The list was getting long and interesting...what was the criteria used for elimination? I see that things have been redesigned somewhat, and I think they are changes for the better. Makes things clearer for those of us who are not geeks, or are evolving at a much slower rate. The vibe feels different now; flat coke as opposed to bubbly but I\'m sure time will tell the story.</p>'),(803,'time to go to the distant Isles\r\ngather in all those who see\r\nwith the acrostic eye\r\nsynaptical misfired misfits\r\ngleaming midst the last\r\ntwilight gloaming\r\nyou can see them if you look\r\nhard at the still lives\r\nall around you\r\nthey have the golden edges\r\nsilhouettes against an October \r\nvelvet coated and headed\r\nfor the Fall\r\ntrying to get back\r\nto the Garden\r\nstars bursting into\r\ndust at your feet\r\nangels burning\r\ndown the house'),(804,'then as They said:\r\nyou would have gone\r\na long time ago\r\nrunning out to where the road\r\ncarries big dreams &\r\nheavy nightmares \r\nsticking out your thumb\r\nalong with your life\r\ntake a ride\r\njust to see \r\nwhere in hell\r\nyour life went\r\nwhen you were looking\r\naway'),(805,'there will come that time\r\nwhen i will have to go alone \r\ninto the forest of no words\r\nwhere I will be devoured\r\ni leave what I draw what i paint & say,\r\nall that i have made flesh\r\nas evidence for you\r\nscattered pages of remembrance\r\nmaps, a light,\r\na door  to enter\r\nand a mirror to find\r\nyourself'),(806,'getting to yes\r\nwas along hard road\r\ni travelled on\r\nnot knowing the destination\r\nbut knowing i had to go\r\npicking out the path\r\nthat ran through the sharp stones of no\r\nthat persisted despite dead ends\r\ndetours and denials\r\ngetting to yes\r\ntook suffering\r\nthrough heat and pressure\r\nit took patience\r\nit took faith in knowing\r\nthat the journey is necessary\r\nand that every step is part of the way\r\ngetting to yes\r\nmeans that i know now\r\nthat the road i took\r\nto find myself\r\nturned into the road\r\nthat led to you'),(807,'<p>\r\ni have swintry block. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/807_1.jpg\'>'),(808,'<p> I hear the mob has started running an oil-shale processing plant off the coast of Nova Scotia. The idea is that crude prices are going to go blow up in the next 15 years and that by mining shale and refining fuel oil and chemical products from it, a market can be formed to compete with established petrochemical companies. Some people tried this in the 70\'s but it was too costly. Enter the mob. Tax evasion, cheap labor, zero regulations, inflated prices, faux distributors, etc. will make it a highly profitable venture. </p>\r\n<p> What I want to know is: how are they hiding this floating factory from the Canadian coast guard?</p>\r\n<p> My friend said they\'re paying off people in Quebec to overlook the thing. I\'m guessing that it\'s just because the station must be in international waters or something, but Lyd seems to think that the scope and audacity of the project is such that no one notices it because no one <i>thinks</i> to notice it.</p>\r\n<p> Or maybe they\'re just saying it\'s an olive-oil importing center? Somehow I don\'t think the usual SOP will work this time.</p>\r\n<p>Long live Canada.</p>\r\n'),(809,'<p>I am so busy.  Wrapping up a semester.  Moving.  Getting a new job.  Like a Mozart concerto, it must be fast and timed just right.  I am reminded of the cool poem somebody posted here a while back where they had a line like \"the scream builds inside of me/I can feel the tension building and my muscles screaming to let it out\" or something.  That\'s about how I feel.</p>\r\n<p>I realized recently that I don\'t care about anything really but being able to be peaceful, to have the kind of life where at least part of the day is spent with a book (that has nothing to do with school or work), some music, and a beer or cup of coffee.  It\'s like I\'ve been busy for so long, on the run, that I don\'t even know how to be calm anymore, which is frightening.</p>\r\n<p>If any of you have read _the power of one_ by bryce courtenay, you have some idea of the things going on in my head.</p>\r\n<p>And damn it all, somewhere in there I need to set down and bash out a preview comments/stories thing.  :-)  I live in ph34r of not closing a tag or something and assing up an entire page.</p>'),(810,'<p>in the vein of darwin\'s \"things i\'m good at\" lists, except some of the things i like about myself are quirky and useless. nonetheless:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>my hair is almost always very soft. perfect for rubbing my own head thoughtfully.</li>\r\n<li>i sing along with the music in my car, even when alone -- in fact, <b>especially</b> when alone -- unselfconsciously. occasionally this results in someone in a car next to me or on the street grinning at me. especially if i\'m at at stoplight and rocking out.</li>\r\n<li>i give freely and generously without keeping mental tabs of who owes me what</li>\r\n<li>i tell my friends and loved ones how i much i like them, regularly.</li>\r\n<li>i have a fondness for ugly things. especially ugly shirts. i wear some really ugly shirts.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>what do you like about yourself?</p>'),(811,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/811_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" color=\"#3300FF\">(Mental \r\n  notes, or not)<br>\r\n  Restart<br>\r\n  Reconfigure<br>\r\n  Reinterpret<br>\r\n  Evolve<br>\r\n  (My personality, myself)</font></p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<P>White Queen is cool, but she is not my muse and I need one.'),(812,'six years ago i had 40 web clients.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthen i got sick, and now i have two and i am shedding them both except for semi-annual site redesigns.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow i am actually getting well as i can get with a broken liver.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark is happy because he likes me to not have to do anything that takes my attention away from him unless it is a fun thing that doesn\'t involve work..<br><br>\r\n\r\nexcept now i have to ask for money if i want something, because i don\'t have any of my own and that sucks and i don\'t want to be unemployed anymore because i find i like to have money to spend that i don\'t have to explain.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\'t want to be unemployed anymore and i don\'t know how not to be.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(813,'<p><font face=\"Arial\" size=2>\r\nIn the interim, we should all go downstairs for some milk and perhaps cookies. This is just a suggestion, by no means an order or a directive. If, in fact, you take it as such, I demand that you stop this abuse at once. After all, the sun is shining, spring is here.. let\'s all stuff our faces with creamy delights and roast our bloated carcasses on the beach.\r\n</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial\" size=2>\r\nThat IS what this is all about, right? All this money, all this fame, all these dot coms and weblogs.. it\'s all about ME! Well, you. THIS is about me, about you. About the hedonistic circle jerk that has snowballed from a few VCs throwing cash at a bunch of nipple-pierced and hair-dyed west coast nerds into a self-styled \"new media\" clique starring YOU.\r\n</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial\" size=2>\r\nQuite frankly, it sucks. I don\'t want to hear about my friends lives in such explicit detail, and you, you\'re a total stranger. There\'s a reason that vanity (well, pride) was in god\'s top 7 sin countdown- because you\'re BORING. Just because it happened to you doesn\'t make it interesting. As if the web wasn\'t already crammed full of useless crap. It used to be that diaries were kept hidden, locked, private. Let\'s keep it that way.\r\n</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Arial\" size=2>\r\nWebcams are cool, of course, provided they feature boobies.\r\n</font></p>'),(814,'<p>Big Brother Bush flying over \r\nAustin in Helicopter One... I managed to get a shot at the man himself... I am pretty sure I got him, US Marshall\'s were at my house within 25 minutes...</p><p>Luckily for me, they were looking for my Landlord...</p>\r\n\r\nLove,<br>\r\nbmg\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/814_1.jpg\'>\r\n'),(815,'<P>i\'m really insane. real gone. i can go through the motions of my daily life, and if i never told anybody, they\'d never know. but things rage back there behind my eyes. i do bandas and mudras and take herbs and stand on my head, and things are copacetic. but there\'s a little glimmer of the thanatos urge or something still working it\'s magic someplace inside. i want to disappear. i want to get lost.</P><P>well, it\'s a wrap. principal photography completed. and i owe the lab <I>only</I> $70,000. have no idea where that\'s coming from. they retain possession of the negative until it\'s paid, though. i\'m going to have to go back to work until we can find completion funding. got my mcse last year and got a call from a staffing agency this week that a company in boston needs 40 people for a windows 2000 rollout. will last 3 months at least. anybody know where there are cheap but safe accomodations in the boston area?</P><P>i don\'t really know if i want to do that, though. got certified in 3 weeks and promptly forgot most of what i learned. used to be in times of stress like these, i would disappear into the chiaroscuro of mexico for months at a time. i could go down there and learn sorcery. change myself into a big black bird whenever i wanted to then have little feathers come away on the toilet paper whenever i wipe my ass for weeks afterward.</P><P>saying bye to all the people i\'ve worked with these past months was the hardest. my assistant wanted to know why i didn\'t give him anything for secretary\'s day. i was like, \"eddie, we\'re fucking. i think that invalidates the whole boss/secretary relationship.\" i mean, if we\'re busy tonguing each other\'s wondrous things, that kind of makes it a collaboration, not a hierarchy. (i think \"wondrous\" is the word or the day, no?)</P><P>they say if you lose your mind, you haven\'t lost much. that it\'s an artificial construct. sure helps make it easier to get along, though.</P> '),(816,'<p>happy something? be it job hunting, eyores birthday... your choice bubba.</p>\r\n<p>enjoy.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/816_1.jpg\'>'),(817,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/817_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>ive been thinking i look different. something in the eyes. a certain facial expression. maybe 3500 miles and 20 days added to your life will do that. or maybe thats just someone elses face staring back at me in the mirror.</P>\r\n'),(818,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthere is a girl, she wears glasses-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis girl, wearing glasses, and she smiles behind them to me in a way that (sometimes) gives me hope in a way that (sometimes) makes me think that I am getting to her in some way- this girl, wether wearing glasses or not, walks in a way that is different from other girls that I have seen walking and who wear glasses or contacts or nothing-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis girl, wearing glasses, and when she walks!- gawd-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwhen she walks,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nhmm</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwhen she walks, moving her hips up and down and I can\'t help but watch and stare- her pants barely hanging on to that gradual outward curve near her stomach, her stomach barely visible in that small space between where her shirt and pants fail to meet-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand when she walks I watch, and when she walks-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ngawd,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmoving her hips up and down like pistons in some sort of lustful machinery, her hips and ass semi circular motioning to my eye-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand when she wears glasses, she can\'t help looking anything other than cute- and when she smiles sometimes, in that way that sometimes,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I can\'t help but, sometimes,</p>'),(819,'<p>\r\nI was repulsed by this. It angered me. i avoid tv. i avoid it because it takes away a lot. it serves a purpose in that it acts as something to do once and a while. like drinking, drugs, water. too much of anything kills. abc sucks.\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/819_1.jpg\'>'),(820,'<p><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/remembering.jpg\"> </p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000066\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">I had one \r\n  male friend in elementary. He punched me and kissed two of the girls I had crushes \r\n  on.</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000066\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Nature demanded \r\n  girl friends from then on.</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000066\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">In seventh \r\n  grade one of my girl friends punched me. Black eye and all. She didn\'t mean \r\n  it.</font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000066\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">One competitive \r\n  male friend and 12 years later I came to find my hommies below. </font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000066\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/I_See_A_Darkness.mp3\">This \r\n  song</a> reminds me of them.</font></p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/austinandchris.jpg\"></p>'),(821,'<p>\r\now my head hurts.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyesterday afternoon chaya and i went to the local to read and throw back a couple pints. chaya left after a couple hours but i powered through until i finished wb\'s novel. when i got home, she had all but finished the vb in the fridge. we knocked out the last couple and then did jager shots while the girls dolled up. around 9 we rang the bottle shop and had them deliver a couple more slabs.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter a while, the girls and i jumped in a cab and met some friends at blue velvet, a tiny goth bar in fitzroy. then we all went downtown to this big goth club in an old jail. i had never actually been to a goth club before. i had to s/brown pants/black pants/. there were many beautiful boys and girls in makeup and powder and lace and velvet and what not. i saw a lot of people making out. i drank another half barrel of vb and smoked a couple cigarettes. altho it was so smokey in there we each probably got a good pack\'s worth secondhand. i walked around a lot and watched people dance, cos girls are even sexier when they\'re moving their body parts in circles. occasionally i walked back to the table and chatted with my friends. i think i agreed to go on a road trip to a cemetery or something.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe didn\'t leave the club until sunrise. we all felt like geriatrics. my heels were rubbed raw from my brand new hot n hunky fluevog boots. when we got home, i ate a jabba mug of ten dollah cheerios and  each of us collapsed into our bed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshout outs to adam, kim, thomas, the tall guy in the red shirt who looked like the dude from type o negative. extra special thanks to chaya and o for dragging me out of the house on a friday night. i had a great time. we were sexy bitches and everybody else was crap.\r\n</p>'),(822,'<pre>\r\nToo much time behind screens.\r\nIt\'s not entirely sad -- sterilization.\r\nIt\'s competent rot and lively distraction.\r\n\r\nFresh air is my vivid rerun, daydream;\r\nbefore I began anonymously.\r\n\r\nIn the end, loss of feeling: is it good? \r\nWatching my numb blazing Magnesium hand, bloodless: is it good?\r\nLike freedom, or great gray clouds, like nothing you nor I know.\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n'),(823,'<p>Staring into space: .. The good things we say shine brightly enough... Clarity is a winner... It\'s best to not matter.Twinge, crinkle:.. why do I suck?.. it smells like feet.. my ass itches\r\nedit, out of focus:.. they can\'t catch you.. get out of here.. empty heh:.. fuckin wierd.. whatever.. later<br><br></p>\r\n<tt>\r\n(TRANSCRIPT)<br>\r\nThe discrepancies have been noted. You shall be notified of the results. What has been set in motion remains so. What is above and beyond will remain so.\r\nThat what is will always be not that what it was.\r\n<br><br>\r\nAll bets are off. You\'re on your own.<br>\r\n<-----><br>\r\n</tt><p>\r\nPeace.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(824,'<pre>Sun Cancer,  \r\nMoon Aquarius,  \r\nMercury Gemini,  \r\nVenus Taurus, \r\nMars Sagittarius retrograde,  \r\nJupiter Virgo,  \r\nSaturn Taurus,  \r\nUranus Libra,  \r\nNeptune Scorpio retrograde,  \r\nPluto Virgo,  \r\nTrue Node Pisces retrograde</pre>\r\n\r\n\r\n<pre>Ascendant Taurus,  \r\n2nd House Gemini,  \r\n3rd House Cancer,  \r\nImum Coeli Leo,  \r\n5th House Virgo,  \r\n6th House Libra, \r\nDescendant Scorpio,  \r\n8th House Sagittarius,  \r\n9th House Capricorn, \r\nMedium Coeli Aquarius,  \r\n11th House Pisces,  \r\n12th House Aries</pre>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/824_1.gif\'>'),(825,'<p>so it\'s my mom\'s birthday and all of her friends are in town and so am i.<BR>\r\nand they\'re playing dominoes. and i\'m going around giving massages. and using what i know of astrology and chakras and reiki to do what i can.<BR> and force-feeding them water because of all the toxins that i\'m releasing from their bloodstreams.<BR>\r\nand i have middle-aged yuppies telling me that i\'m a godsend.<BR>\r\nmy father\'s accountant is telling me that i have healing powers.</p>\r\n<p>what a fantastic feeling!<BR>\r\nto know the body so well that i sense all of their stress points!<BR>\r\nthat i can push my thumbs and strong fingers into the right points and offer immediate relief.<BR>\r\ni am blessed. i seek to help people by sharing my gift.<BR>\r\nit feels good.</p>'),(826,NULL),(827,NULL),(828,'<pre><tt>\r\nflow\r\nstaccato\r\nlyrical\r\nchaos\r\nstillness\r\n</tt></pre>'),(829,'<p>\r\nmom called. dad didn\'t. brother didnt. stin showed up. happy. played baseball. nice. biked home. against the wind. things started going downhill. went to duke. fun. drink. eyes. smiles. drink. drink. ugh. no more. more comes. ugh. no more. more comes. everyone leaves. go home. sad. depressed. alone. sorry. selfish. lonely. everything all at once but without the one thing. oblivous. most are. the layers. the handshakes. the small talk. bring up things about them. they are amused. their ego is boosted. they dont think twice. it works. they dont suspect im having the worst fucking most fucking frustrating night in a long time. they dont see it. well they dont need to. why should they care? they got their own probs. alone across borders. its all the same. frustration. cant sleep. </P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/829_1.jpg\'>'),(830,'<P>i have been missing lately.  i take a step back and snap shoot my view that i am so familiar with.  empty water bottles.  near empty whiskey bottle.  a few revolving dirty dishes.  a pile of books and papers.  not enough leg room.  a chair that wont scoot out smoothly.  these are my things.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>this morning i woke up with the birds and the bees song in my head.  yesterday it was the Kenny Rogers song, \"the gambler.\"  i wonder what it will be tomorrow.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i washed some clothes this morning.  on the way from the laundrymat to the donut shop, i passed and stopped to talk to william.  i had been thinking about william in the past week often.  i have no idea why.  maybe i think of everyone all the time, rapidly, and that is the explanation for the seeming coincidence that william, a roomate of mine from my sophmore year in college, lives up the street from me on church and 26th.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>william is a tennis instructer like he was back when i first met him.  he would go on drug binges and wear really tight dresses and go dancing.  he looked really fine in those dresses.  he put most girls to shame.  he put most frat boys to shame too.  we lived in a large house in the area of austin called west campus--a large greek community.  w. hated the greeks.  one night he left the house with a can of blue spray paint and walked up the street tagging  cars with \"cock sucker\" on the back bumpers.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hi william.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>those were the days.  i gave him a wrong phone number so it is up to me to call him.  i will call him because i was thinking about him so much the past week.  it must mean i would like to see him as opposed to the former seven-eleven coworker that i ran into on lower haight and never called.  william is neat.  i will invite him to dinner with his girlfriend.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i finished my book tonight.  it was pretty long but an easy read.  i could have gotten it at the grocery store.  it was not literature.  it was television in the print form.  verbose and easily digestable.  what will i read next?  i am thinking of borrowing weeb\'s snow crash book.  she thinks i should read it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i should mention that i was out of sorts today and very cranky.  i went to the beach, but only after i chewed on yeti\'s leg like a dog for nearly half an hour.  elly\'s leg too.  by the time i was done, elly said she was cranky from talking to me and intended to push me down the three flights of steps from the front door gate to the street at her house.  \"next time i see you i will push you down the steps she said.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>after the beach i went to the movie akira with weeb and gage.  i felt like going to the movie but not really hanging out.  i pulled it off though.  i dont think i came across as a mad dog to them.  there were a couple times i felt like chewing a leg.  but i didn\'t.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i was going to stop this journal entry but i changed my mind.  i  will keep going.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i helped leonard set up a website <a href=\"http://showerhead.org\">showerhead.org</a> for his zine publishing company.  i am glad for that and so is he.  i have wanted to help him with that for some time.  i just got around to it when i was at his website on earthlink when it became obvious that such a cool website deserves a location of it\'s own well off the domain of money sucking cult enterprises.  not that hungry.com isnt a cult enterprise.  or swinney.org for that matter.  but have i ever asked for money?  no.  but if you want to become a better swinney, you should code for the site.  anyway.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>are you done reading this yet?  you should be.  watching all the credits for akira scroll by, all those japanese names, sent me back to highschool and i wondered what happened to takeshi nanasawa.  i am going to try to find him next week.  i will call that christian family that he stayed with and sponsored him when he came to the US.  they lent us a ford fuego once.  i accidently urinated all over the passenger seat on a road trip once.  i was trying to pee in a gatorade bottle but i had a water dog that caused logistical difficulties resulting in the catastraphy and a bout of hysterical laughter.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am done now.  i could keep going, but no one reads long posts.  i dont anyay.  if you are only going to read four lines, <B>read this</B>:</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>\r\nGod called me to the beach\r\nto give me some waves of wisdom\r\n\"life\'s ups and downs,\" he said,\r\n\"sometimes you have to get your feet wet.\"\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(831,'six a.m.<br>\r\ni can\'t sleep<br>\r\ni can\'t eat<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m in love<br>\r\nwith child<br>\r\nand he has gone<br>\r\n<br>\r\nnow<br>\r\n<br>\r\nall i want to do is<br>\r\ndrink<br>\r\nbut i am not afforded<br>\r\nthat luxury<br>\r\n<br>\r\nso while he probably lies<br>\r\npassed out<br>\r\ndrunk<br>\r\nin his tiny efficiency<br>\r\napartment<br>\r\ni sit up<br>\r\n(in this place that<br>\r\nseems to big now)<br>\r\nworry filling my womb<br>\r\n<br>\r\nnot crying<br>\r\nanymore<br>\r\nand trying to lay blame<br>\r\nat someone\'s feet<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhat didn\'t i give?<br>\r\nwhat didn\'t i take?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhe is inside me<br>\r\nalmost breathing<br>\r\n<br>\r\nnow<br>\r\n<br>\r\noh baby,<br>\r\nhow i love you.\r\n'),(832,'<p>Two Tuesdays ago, I had this interview.  I keep thinking it was last week, but no...it really was a whole almost 12 someodd days ago.  The rate at which time seems to be elapsing out from under me in this part of my life is downright disturbing.  The total amount of meaningful work I accomplished last week was almost exactly nil and even my quite unremarkable daily existence was unusually so.  I am not good at waiting for things.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBut this week before the last one, it was good.  The interview was supposed to be the day after my birthday, Monday the 16th, in the afternoon.  In Santa Barbara, which I knew very little about other than a vague and readily confirmed suspicion that it was not really anywhere near where I live.  Six hours away, straight down the 101, sayeth the internet.  I had a fun birthday and should\'ve been plenty tired, but I\'ve been having occasional bouts of insomnia and since anxiety tends to kick it in, I slept for maybe an hour Sunday night.  But I was determined to be a trooper, so I woke up, showered, finished packing, threw my crap in the car, and headed out around 6.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can\'t stand the 880.  I think it\'s the most bleak of all the highways in the bay area and of the half dozen times I\'ve travelled it, some horrible absurd traffic disaster has always occurred, such as a semi wedging itself longways across the center divide.  My plan was to deal with the bridge mess and trade off a relatively small wait there for the aesthetically superior and relatively less snarl-prone lanes of the 280.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI made it as far as the West Portal area, in the city.  While waiting to get on the bridge I realized the folly of thinking I could interview on six hours of driving after, essentially, zero hours of sleep.  Inertia got me as far as West Portal.  I found an almost too cute cafe to nest in and had a nice breakfast, a crepe and something caffeinated.  I signed and obtained stamps for my taxes (another source of much anxiety) and handed them off to the mercy of the postal service.  I called a couple of times and finally got through to my prospective employers, and they said this was all fine, they\'d reschedule me for sometime on Tuesday and they\'d let me know exactly when soon.  I dithered a bit in the city then drove home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEarly that afternoon they called with a suprise: my interview had been successfully rescheduled, but for 10am.  I needed to sleep and still wasn\'t tired, but I tried, napped a bit that afternoon, woke up, ate a bit, then fell back asleep after much tossing and turning around 10pm with the alarm set for 2.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I awoke, it was as if the authorities had come in to my brain without a warrant but determined to find something.  I felt like my mental furniture had all been kicked over and torn up.  I laid in bed almost crying; I was just miserable.  But again, I managed to drag myself to the shower and soon I was on the road.  No 280 crap this time: at 3am the 880 was wide open and soon I was headed south into the uncertain dawn.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDriving at that hour with good music isn\'t such a bad thing, even on an interrupted REM cycle.  The 101 that direction bears almost no resemblance to the equivalent dreadfully boring looking chunk of the 5 (I\'m ok with slower/prettier over faster/more boring).  As the sun rose, the territory went from pretty with mountains in the distance to stunning with mountains close on one side and beach towns on the other.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>I arrived early, so I drove around a bit, then found the campus and dorked around it a bit (the interview was with a research group at UCSB).  I obtained an iced coffee beverage that, had it been just a bit stronger, would\'ve required federal regulation.  I told myself I was good enough and smart enough and that doggone it, someone wants to work with me.  And despite a wave of immeadiate post-interview despair, I think I did ok.  Later that day, sitting in traffic on the 405, I decided that while they might\'ve interviewed a better match for the position and for that reason I might not get the job, at least it wasn\'t because I blew the interview.  Somehow at the time this seemed comforting, even while it raised the possibility that I might\'ve blown my first career.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>If you\'re familiar with the built environment of the greater Los Angeles region, you\'re now wondering how I ended up eating fumes on the hot tarmac of the 405, crawling across the western edge of the LA basin.  Did the fog of sleep deprivation cause me to turn the wrong direction out of Santa Barbara?  And what was this mysterious position like, and since it\'s been almost two weeks now, did I end up getting it?  All will be revealed in part two, coming to a link near here when I get around to writing it.\r\n</p>'),(833,'<p>austin i think wanted to know where i was now. specifically im back in a place where is spent ten years of my life. a pitstop on this trip. i guess it was always a pitstop. right now im staying on Nebraska Street. there are lots of friends on Nebraska....good friends. friends that like me just the way i am. they like the me, me...not the Oklahoma me [quiet, insipid, clingy, needing a mancrutch]. that person is not who i want to be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>two blocks away is Dakota Street. that is where you use to be able to find me. i have a friend there...but i dont think he is a very healthy friend to have. [he actually likes the Oklahaom me]. he makes you laugh. but all the time you\'re laughing your insides are crying. six years of clinging i do not need.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are a lot of other people in between Nebraska street and Dakota street. i might call some of them my friends. but i guess they are more just people that think they know me because they think they know him [local rockstar. wandering fool. alot think he walks on water]. they all want to know when i will walk those two blocks to confront Dakota street. or if Dakota street will see me crawling back on my knees.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>fuck them i say.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am not your soap opera to tune into just because there is nothing better to do in this cowtown.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck them and their wannabe cornbread dreams.</p>'),(834,'<p>Shiate... what can I say more about this boy? He is famous for his software, music, mind and friendship...</p>\r\n<p>Luna invites me over to his home for some movies, chow, conversation, mp3\'s and apps. We hang for a while and he then tells me that he is letting me babysit his studio speakers that are too loud for his apt. </p>\r\n<p>\"This shit is too much man! You can\'t give me this!\" I say.</p>\r\n<p>But the fact is that the speakers are awesome, even though the do downgrade my \"chepo\" brand receiver. No worry, it still sounds good. I could thank him for them, but that is not enough, not for this...</p>\r\n<p>Upon waking up this morning, at 6 when k-dawg does her routine, I thought about how much fun I have had with my friends in the past few months... reminds me of summer break in high school, cept this break seems to be lasting for a long time... <p>It is almost party time in Austin. May is the month of bbq\'s and late night booty shakin, Robotec9 usually has summer flings beginning in may.  My birthday is coming up (may 10) and it is almost for sure that I will be hosting a party that weekend... I hope you all can make it? Wear a dress, I want everyone to wear a dress for my birthday... I hope I can get my uncle LD (liquid dangerous) to come, maybe he will wear a dress? I won\'t tell mom! It would be cool if my brother Robotec9 could come down; but hopefully he is jobbing by then. I would like to see my favorite swinney.org authors at my party... Maybe they could just send me pictures of themselves, and I could place their faces on my monitor for the birthday party for all to see? Wearing a dress, or not... I don\'t care. Thats what I want for my birthday... pictures of swinney.org\'ers.</p><p>I will ask again, next week.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/834_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>I have been working, moderately. I work about 20 hrs a week, and it is just enough to keep my hands from being idle. Idle hands is what a convicted killer told me was the cause of his crimes; rehabilitated and god loving man... i see that he is pure and honest, more honest than some non-convicted killers. That is a shame. So, i have been forcing myself to do things like, pruning, mowing, cleaning, shaving, reading, jogging, Smiling, laughing, crying (damn bjork in dancer in the dark), and yes a little drinking. My habits are not routine, but there is usually a constant amount of smoking, sleeping, eating, and drinking. Sometimes it is nice to just sit in my room and look at pictures, or daydream... sometimes without music, but most of the time with.</p><p>Today, not different than most days, I am wondering back and forth from the living room to my bedroom... the living room has music waving through my new speakers, while the bedroom has my apple connected to the internet... I go outside for fresh air, and then smoke a cigarette, thinking about a time i was smoking with jebro while watching a formation of ants march on into the casa. I think it is time i did a little house cleansing, i wonder if k-dawg gets upset with me when i don\'t clean... she busts me at times doing spot cleaning... which is close to cleaning, but not the same. Its been a while since I have gone grocery shopping, today will probably be the day... i owe a few dinners to k-dawg - who by the way makes the best dinner\'s ... I also owe my girlfriend, zb, many meals, and time alone... This week will be fruitful in terms of getting things done.  </p><p>Damn, I am sorry i rambled, I forget no one reads more than 300 words per article... '),(835,'<p>\r\nthere was once a thriving economy on the west coast. there were young hip kids just in or out of their college ages and they were jumpin. they were eating lavishly. they could afford to try new lifestyle choices. they talked about how fair competition was \"good\" and let the stock option game build its own hierarchy. the car you drove. the area you lived. the type of chair you sat in at work. people merged into little circles that formed on the edges of the industry that they worked in. dot com\'s hosted dot org\'s and chat idled in the background.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbust\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncompanies are sweeping employees off their properties as if they were german shopkeepers sweeping currency out the door just after world war one. starbucks started selling more regular columbians and domestic beer was once again ok to drink on a friday night. where did it go? thats what I think people are asking. where did they go? a watershed has occured and #a has been hit hard. \r\n</p>  '),(836,'<p>It\'s now been almost 5 months of exile from IRC, this time not by choice -- I\'m a compertur returd and can\'t get around the uh, firewall. The device acting as the firewall is a blue and wholly anticlimatic object about the size of a breadbox. I\'ve never seen a breadbox. The solution, lies in this \'port forwarding\' poppycock. All in good time.</p>\r\n<p>Summer looms.</p>\r\n<p>What is summer without IRC? They\'ve gone together since I was 13. Like toads inna hole. Like pippins offa tree. Like crimson an clover. over and over.</p>'),(837,'today i had lunch with my mother and we were having a lovely day and then...<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe spilled a glass of water on the tile floor and then she slid in it and went down and oh god her knee was so broken the kneecap was gone and she and i were all alone and she was screaming and screaming and i had to go in the other room to call 911 and i couldn\'t help her except to keep her calm and alert because i knew they would not give her drugs if she passed out and she screamed for 15 minutes or more and it was truly terrible and she was so frightened and i was so helpless and she is my mother and now she has to have surgery on wednesday and she is so scared...<br><br>\r\n\r\nthank god to whoever invented morphine.'),(838,'<p>\r\nwell im dead broke. loan money gone. loan payment due. rent money gone. rent money due. i have a rental car and 124USD. colleen. stinder. road trip. 5 or 6 days of no ottawa. no erb. no irc. no swintries. no roommate. no smoking whenever the fuck i feel like it. got into a huge fight with mom on the phone. yelled at her for a good five minutes. felt good. im just sick and tired of being called irresponsible when there are such loftier examples in my family to look at. so im hoping we stay at flea bags and eat fruit and veggies in the grocery store. that would be fun. \r\n</p> '),(839,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/839_1.jpg\'>'),(840,'<PRE>\r\ni ran around the block today \r\nlike a rabit-hamster on a wheel\r\npound pound pound\r\nthumps the legs \r\ni saw a chevelle in two tone\r\ngreen and gray\r\ni dreamed it was mine\r\nthat would make my day \r\nthere is nothing like an ugly powerful car\r\nit is better than bad sex\r\ni had bad sex once because of a motorcycle\r\nshe was nibbling on my ear\r\nwhispering, \"sexy motorcycle boy.\"\r\nthat night was the first and only time \r\n  she rode on my motorcyle\r\ni sold it soon after\r\nbought a honda civic\r\nthat was mistake number two.\r\n</PRE>'),(841,'The MIS director of ibomb called me yesterday and asked me if I would host ibomb. Apparently, the site had to be up so that they could fulfill some obligations, and not be sued. And my place is increasingly becoming the choice for low cost colocation.\r\n\r\n<p>So i drove over to the now empty headquarters, and picked up a dell optiplex. This was all that remained of ibomb. The site that once ran on two $1.5M IBM S-80s was now contained inside a fragile old pentium box.\r\n\r\n<p>I carried the box upstairs, and plugged it into the ethernet and KVM box. I was impressed at how much the code had been cleaned up. I want to look at the code more before I pull the plug.\r\n\r\n<p>Surprised at how easy it would be, I pushed the launch button, then went to check my email.\r\nThe grey box asked no more of me.\r\n\r\n<p>This is weird, but the grey box sorta reminds me of Cinnamon, who was Mike Dringenburg\'s model for \'Death\' in Sandman. Cinnamon was dying. She had hiv and/or hep, and was still a junkie. She\'d pass every day, and we\'d buy her coffee or lunch, or pick up her cab fair. We all knew she was heading outta town, but were happy to put her up as long as would stay.'),(842,'<p>\r\nlala.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m at home, sitting in my green chair.  well, it isn\'t my green chair.. but it is mine until i get out of it.  funny how that works.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nat my feet, there is a box of captian crunch.  it tore up my mouth. *ouch*\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy new labret is irritating the hell out of me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nquitting smoking is really hard.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy kitty kitty likes to lick my fingers and look at me wildly with her funny kitty eyes.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m going out tonight to play after i start working on oof\'s birthday present.  it usually takes me a month to make or get his present every year.  sometimes, i collect things all year long to give to him.  he always seems pleased.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbrian and i have known each other for three years now.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndarwin and i have almost been together a year and his birthday is coming up soon too.  i don\'t know what to make him for his birthday.. but it will come to me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy feet hurt\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m hungry\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ngive me a random and fun idea of something to do.');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (843,'<PRE>\r\nCollection of pad thai recipes by cs.unca.edu/~stigle\r\n<P>\r\nDate:  August 5, 1994\r\n<p>\r\nChanges made in this version: \r\nAdded notes about alternatives to fish sauce\r\nAdded 7 new recipes (at end)\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nHere is a collection of pad thai recipes from various sources.\r\nI start off with some general notes and observations, based on\r\na study of the individual recipes and my own experience.\r\n<p>\r\nThis collection is maintained by Sue Stigleman.  It is posted\r\nperiodically to rec.food.recipes.  It is also available by\r\nanonymous ftp from ftp.cs.unca.edu and via the World Wide\r\nWeb at http://www.cs.unca.edu/~stigle/padthai.html.\r\n<p>\r\nSend comments, corrections, or additions to stigle@cs.unca.edu.\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nNotes:\r\n<p>\r\nRice Noodles:  you can use fresh or dried, in widths from 1/8 to 1/2\r\ninch wide.  I\'ve never tried fresh.  The dried ones have to be\r\nsoaked in water to soften them. The recipes call for soaking \r\nin cold water, lukewarm water, hot water, and boiling water for\r\nanywhere from 7 minutes to 2 hours.  I put mine into warm tap\r\nwater and let them soak while I\'m preparing everything else.\r\nJust before I start cooking, I dump them into a colander to \r\ndrain.  One recipe suggests cellophane noodles as an alternative\r\nto rice noodles -- I\'ve never tried that variation.\r\n<p>\r\nMeat or No Meat:  The most common meat called for is shrimp, with \r\nchicken and/or pork use in addition to or in place of the shrimp.\r\nSome recipes add bean curd; some substitute it for the meat.\r\nJeff Smith\'s recipe uses deep fried bean curd.  My own variation\r\nis to substitute various veggies (asparagus, red bell pepper,\r\nbroccoli, snow peas, or whatever else looks good.)  As Nancie\r\nMcDermott says, \"Thai cooks blithely tinker with the classic\r\nformula to create signature variations, and you can, too.\"\r\n<p>\r\nOil and Seasonings:  Cooking pad thai starts with vegetable or\r\npeanut oil.  Most versions add garlic, and sometimes shallots,\r\nshrimp paste (be prepared for the smell!), onions, fresh red\r\nchilies, and/or preserved sweet white radish.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Sauce:  What makes pad thai, in addition to the rice noodles,\r\nis the sauce.  The general mix of flavors is sweet, salty, sour,\r\nand hot.  Typical ingredients are:\r\n<p>\r\n	fish sauce (sometimes soy sauce is used in addition, or in\r\n		place of for pure vegetarian versions)\r\n<p>\r\n	sugar (sometimes palm sugar is suggested)\r\n<p>\r\n	vinegar (various kinds specified; tamarind sauce or lime\r\n		juice are sometimes used instead)\r\n<p>\r\n	\"red stuff\" -- may be paprika, tomato paste, catsup,\r\n		chili powder, hot chili sauce, chili paste with\r\n		garlic, tomato sauce, or cayenne pepper, depending\r\n		on the recipe.\r\n<p>\r\nOther possible additions: salt, black pepper, chicken stock, dried\r\nshrimp powder.  One recipe calls for boiling the sauce before using.\r\n<p>\r\nNotes on substitutes for fish sauce:\r\n<p>From Sundays at Moosewood:\r\nPrepare a strong vegetable stock, strong on the onions and black\r\npeppercorns, and use 2 tbsp of this (with an extra 1/2 to 1 tsp salt)\r\nto substitute fo 2 tbsp of fish sauce in recipes.\r\n<p>\r\nFrom Jonathan Kandell: \r\nfor 1/4 C fish sauce, use:\r\n1/4 C soy sauce plus a little water\r\nwith 1T miso with 2T fermented black beans crushed, marinated a while.\r\nexperiment with the proportions.\r\n<p>\r\nEggs:  anywhere from 0-6.  Some recipes call for beating the\r\neggs before adding; others suggested breaking the yolk after\r\nadding the egg to the pan.  Various techniques are suggested\r\nfor manipulating the egg while cooking.  One recipe calls\r\nfor cooking the egg before starting the pad thai, cutting\r\nit into strips, and then adding the egg strips back at the\r\nend of cooking.  I haven\'t tried this myself but have had\r\nit in restaurants.\r\n<p>\r\nBean Sprouts and Scallions:  These are usually added last in\r\ncooking, or added to the finished dish without cooking.\r\n<p>\r\nGarnishes:  various things can be added to finished dish as\r\nan edible garnish:\r\n	lime or lemon wedges\r\n	ground roasted chilies\r\n	ground roasted peanuts\r\n	dried red chili flakes\r\n	fresh coriander leaves\r\n	cucumber slices\r\n	dried shrimps\r\n	basil leaves\r\n	fried basil leaves\r\n	cherry tomatoes\r\n	mint sprigs\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nExperiment, and enjoy!\r\n--------------------------------------------------\r\n<p>\r\nThai Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom Thai Home-Cooking\r\n\r\n1/2 lb dried rice noodles 1/8 inch wide\r\nwarm water\r\n\r\n1/2 lb shrimp, chicken, pork, or a combination\r\n\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup + 2 tablespoons granulated sugar\r\n1/4 cup + 2 tablespoons white vinegar\r\n1 teaspoon paprika or 1 tablespoon tomato paste \r\n  or 1 tablespoon catsup (all are optional)\r\n\r\n4 green onions\r\n\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil\r\n1 teaspoon (2 cloves) finely chopped garlic\r\n\r\n2 eggs\r\n\r\n3/4 lb bean sprouts\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons ground roasted chilies\r\n3/4 cup ground roasted unsalted peanuts\r\n\r\nlime wedges\r\n\r\n1.  Soak the noodles for 20-25 minutes in enough warm water \r\nto cover them.  They should be flexible and soft, but not \r\nso soft that they can be mashed easily with the fingers.  \r\nLater cooking in liquid will soften them more.  Drain \r\nthem thoroughly in a colander while preparing the other \r\ningredients.  Traditionally they are left in full-length \r\nstrands, but you may cut them into 8-inch lengths if you \r\nfind it easier to stir-fry them that way.\r\n\r\n2.  Peel and devein the shrimp, leaving the tails intact, \r\nor slice the chicken or pork across the grain into strips \r\nnot more than 1/8 inch thick and 1 to 2 inches long.\r\n\r\n3.  Mix the fish sauce, sugar, vinegar, and optional paprika, \r\ntomato paste, or catsup in a bowl, and stir until the sugar \r\nis dissolved.  Set the mixture aside.  Slice the green onions, \r\nboth white and green parts, diagonally into pieces 1 1/2 inches \r\nlong and 1/4 inch thick.  Set aside.\r\n\r\n4.  Heat a wok, add the oil, and swirl it over the surface of \r\nthe pan.  Add the garlic and stir-fry until light golden.  \r\nAdd the meat and stir-fry until the pink color disappears \r\ncompletely.  If you are using shrimp, stir-fry until they turn \r\npink.  Add the noodles and toss lightly to coat them with oil \r\nand to distribute the meat and garlic.\r\n\r\n5.  Add the liquid from Step 3 and bring it to a boil rapidly, \r\ngently folding the noodles without breaking them.  Reduce the \r\nheat to medium and boil the mixture, folding frequently, until \r\nthe noodles have absorbed the liquid.\r\n\r\n6.  Using a wok scoop or a stiff spatula, lift the noodles \r\ngently from one side of the wok.  Pour a little oil along \r\nthe side of the wok, then break 1 egg and slip it into the oil.  \r\nBreak the yolk, and cover the egg with the noodles immediately.  \r\nRepeat this on the opposite side of the wok with the remaining \r\negg.  Allow the eggs to cook undisturbed, over moderate heat, \r\nuntil they are set and almost dry.  Additional oil may be added \r\nif the eggs or the noodles begin to stick to the wok.\r\n\r\n7.  When the eggs are set and almost dry, fold them gently but \r\nrapidly into the noodles.  Try not to break the noodles, which \r\nwill be soft and fragile at this point.  An effective way is to \r\ninsert the scoop under the eggs, lift it through, and fold the \r\nmixture over.  Continue the lifting and folding motion until \r\nthe eggs are broken up and well distributed.\r\n\r\n8.  Add the bean sprouts and sliced green onions and toss the \r\nentire mixture quickly and gently, still avoiding breaking the \r\nnoodles.  Cook for about 2 minutes, or until the bean sprouts \r\nand green onions are crisp-tender.\r\n\r\n9.  Place the mixture on a large warm platter.  Sprinkle the \r\nground chilies and peanuts over the top and squeeze lime juice \r\nover that, or serve these garnishes separately, for each diner \r\nto add according to taste.\r\n\r\nVariation:  Omit the shrimp, pork, or chicken from the list of \r\ningredients, and ignore any instructions for them.  Substitute \r\n1/2 pound tofu and 1/4 pound dried shrimp.  Put the tofu on a \r\ntriple layer of paper towels, cover it with another triple layer, \r\nput a plate on top of that, and put a 2-pound weight, e.g. a can \r\nof tomatoes, on top of the plate.  Let stand for 20 to 30 minutes \r\nto press out the excess water.  Put the dried shrimp in a sieve, \r\nrinse them quickly under hot running water, and set them aside \r\nto drain.  After the tofu has been pressed, slice it into 1/4-inch \r\ncubes.  Add the tofu and shrimp in Step 5 of the instructions and \r\nproceed with the main recipe.  Note: in Thailand, dried shrimp \r\nare available in a smaller size than is generally available in \r\nthe United States.  If you would like to simulate that, chop \r\nthe dried shrimp very coarsely after they have been rinsed.\r\n\r\nYield:  6-8 servings\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom The Southeast Asia Cookbook, by Ruth Law\r\n\r\n1/2 lb fresh rice noodles, cut into 1/2-inch slices, \r\n  or flat dried rice noodles\r\n\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n\r\n1/3 cup oil\r\n1 tablespoon minced garlic\r\n4 tablespoons minced shallots\r\n\r\n2 teaspoons shrimp paste (kapee)\r\n1 tablespoon chopped dried shrimp (optional)\r\n\r\n10 medium shrimp, shelled, deveined (or substitute pork)\r\n\r\n3 tablespoons fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n1 tablespoon rice vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons light brown sugar\r\n2 tablespoons tomato ketchup\r\n1/2 to 1 teaspoon chili powder (optional)\r\n\r\n2 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n\r\nGarnishes:\r\n1/3 cup coarsely ground unsalted peanuts\r\n1/2 teaspoon dried red chili flakes (optional)\r\n2 green onions, finely sliced\r\n2 tablespoons chopped coriander leaves\r\n2 limes, cut into wedges\r\n1 small cucumber, sliced.\r\n\r\nIf using dried rice noodles, soak in hot water for 20 minutes\r\nbefore cooking.  Drain.  In 4 quarts boiling water, cook fresh \r\nrice noodles 2 to 3 minutes or until just tender to the bite, al \r\ndente.  Drain.  Rinse.  Drain for 30 minutes or until dry.  In\r\nboiling water, blanch the bean sprouts for 30 seconds.  Refresh under\r\ncold water.  Drain.\r\n\r\nHeat oil.  Fry garlic and shallots until golden.  Add the shrimp\r\npaste and dried shrimp.  Stir.  Add the shrimp and stir-fry\r\nuntil done.  Add the fish sauce, vinegar, sugar, ketchup, and chili\r\npowder.  Stir until sugar dissolves.  Add the beaten eggs and let\r\nthem set slightly.  Then stir to scramble.  Add the noodles and\r\ntoss for about 2 minutes.  Place the Pad Thai on a platter.  Sprinkle\r\nthe noodles with peanuts, chili flakes, green onions, and coriander.\r\nArrange lime wedges around the edge of the platter.  Serve\r\nwith a side dish of fresh bean sprouts and cucumbers.\r\n\r\nYield:  3-4 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Rice Noodles \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n4 oz dried rice noodles, medium size\r\n1/4 cup vegetable oil\r\n2 cloves finely minced garlic\r\n3 tablespoons chopped raw shrimp\r\n3 tablespoons lean ground pork\r\n1 teaspoon sugar\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n1 teaspoon soy sauce\r\n2 teaspoons hot chili sauce\r\n2 beaten eggs\r\n2 tablespoon tamarind sauce\r\n1/4 cup bean sprouts\r\n1 sliced scallion\r\n1 tablespoon salty preserved radish\r\n2 tablespoon coarsely ground salted or unsalted peanuts\r\n2 tablespoon sliced green onions\r\n2 wedges lemon\r\n1 cucumber\r\n\r\n1.  Soak noodles in hot water for 20 minutes, or until soft.  Drain.\r\n\r\n2.  Heat oil in a wok or large skillet and saute garlic until \r\ngolden.  Add the shrimp and ground pork and stir-fry until \r\nlightly browned.  Add the sugar, fish sauce, soy sauce, and \r\nchili sauce and stir-fry until the sugar dissolves.  Pour \r\nin the beaten eggs, let them set slightly, then stir to scramble.\r\n\r\n3.  Add the noodles and stir-fry about 2 minutes.  Push noodles \r\nto side of wok or skillet.  Add the tamarind sauce and cook \r\nfor 1 minute.  Add the bean sprouts, scallions, radish, and \r\nstir-fry until bean sprouts are slightly cooked, about 1 minute.  \r\nStir noodles down into tamarind mixture and stir-fry until well-mixed.\r\n\r\n4.  Pile noodles on a serving dish and sprinkle with peanuts and \r\nscallions.  Seed cucumber and slice lengthwise into 4 pieces.  \r\nPlace lemon and cucumber wedges on side of plate.\r\n\r\nServe 2\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nStir-Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n1 package (16 oz) flat rice stick noodles\r\n4 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 cloves garlic, crushed\r\n1 yellow onion, chopped\r\n1 1/2 pounds pork loin, cut into julienne strips\r\n1/2 cup dried shrimp, soaked in 1/2 cup of warm water for 5 minutes\r\n2 fresh red chilies, cut into julienne strips\r\n1 teaspoon white sugar\r\n1/8 cup fish sauce\r\n1 teaspoon salt\r\n2 tablespoons lime juice\r\n3 tablespoons ketchup\r\n1 teaspoon ground black pepper\r\n1 pound fresh bean sprouts, washed, drained\r\n4 tablespoons green onion, chopped\r\n4 tablespoons fresh coriander, chopped\r\n4 tablespoons roasted peanuts, crushed\r\n\r\nIn a pot of boiling water, drop in the flat rice stick noodles\r\nfor about 1 minute, stirring constantly to prevent noodles \r\nfrom sticking together.  Remove from heat and drain in a colander.  \r\nRinse with cold water.  Set aside.  Heat the oil in a large wok \r\nand stir-fry the garlic and onion until golden brown.  Add the \r\npork, shrimp in water, and chilies.  Stir-fry for 5 minutes and \r\nadd the sugar, fish sauce, salt, lime juice, ketchup, black pepper, \r\nnoodles, and bean sprouts.  Stir well for 2 minutes.  Transfer \r\nto a large serving platter and garnish with green onion, fresh \r\ncoriander, and peanuts.\r\n\r\nServes 6-8\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nKwitiaow Pad Thai \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n1/2 cup peanut or corn oil\r\n1 oz raw prawns, shelled\r\n4 oz firm bean curd (tofu), diced\r\n3 tablespoons preserved sweet white radish, chopped\r\n3 tablespoons sliced shallots\r\n4 eggs\r\n11 oz rice or cellophane noodles (sen kel or woon sen), \r\n  soaked in cold water for 7-10 minutes, if dried\r\n1/4 cup chicken stock\r\n3 tablespoons dried shrimps, chopped\r\n1/3 cup unsalted peanuts, chopped\r\n4 spring onions, sliced\r\n15 oz bean sprouts\r\n\r\nsauce:\r\n1 cup water\r\n1/2 cup tamarind juice\r\n1/3 cup palm sugar\r\n1 tablespoon white soya sauce\r\n\r\n\r\nMix all the sauce ingredients together in a pan and boil \r\nuntil reduced to about 2/3 cup. Set aside to cool.\r\n\r\nHeat the oil in a wok or pan until very hot, then add \r\nthe prawns and bean curd and stir-fry lightly for 1 minute.  \r\nAdd the preserved radish and shallot, fry for 1 minute, and \r\nbreak in the eggs.  Stir-fry for a minute, then add the noodles \r\nand chicken stock.  When the noodles are soft (about 2 minutes), \r\nadd the dried shrimps, peanuts, spring onions and bean sprouts.  \r\nAdd the sauce, fry for a couple of minutes and serve.\r\n\r\nServe accompanied by chopped peanuts, chopped dry chillies, \r\nsugar, lime wedges, spring onions, and fresh bean sprouts, \r\nall in small containers.\r\n\r\nServes 6\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles  \r\n\r\nFrom James McNair\'s Pasta Cookbook\r\n\r\nAsian rice noodles, cut about 1/8 inch wide, \r\n  or 1 pound fresh or dried flat rice noodles\r\n3/4 cup fish sauce or 6 tablespoons soy sauce\r\n4 teaspoons rice wine vinegar or distilled white vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons sugar\r\n4 teaspoons high-quality paprika, or 1/4 cup catsup or tomato paste\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil, or more if needed\r\n8 oz boneless pork or boned and skinned chicken, cut into very small pieces\r\n2 tablespoons minced or pressed garlic\r\n2 teaspoons ground dried red hot chili, \r\n  or 1 tablespoon minced fresh hot chile\r\n4 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n8 oz medium-sized shrimp, shelled and deveined, tails left intact\r\n10 oz fresh bean sprouts\r\n3 green onions, thinly sliced\r\n1/2 cup chopped unsalted dry-roasted peanuts\r\n1/4 cup chopped fresh cilantro (coriander)\r\nfinely minced dried shrimp for garnish\r\nfresh cilantro sprigs for garnish\r\nlemon or lime wedges for garnish\r\n\r\nIn a bowl, cover the noodles in lukewarm water and let stand \r\nto soften, about 15 minutes for fresh noodles or about \r\n30 minutes for dry noodles.  Drain and cut into 6-inch \r\nlengths, if desired.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a small bowl, combine the fish sauce, vinegar, sugar, \r\nand paprika, catsup, or tomato paste.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat a wok or saute pan over high heat.  Add the oil and \r\nswirl to coat the pan.  Add the pork or chicken, garlic, \r\nand chili and stir-fry for 1 minute.  Stir in the drained \r\nnoodles and the reserved fish sauce mixture and stir-fry \r\nabout 30 seconds.  Push the noodles to one side, pour in \r\nabout a tablespoon more oil, if necessary, and add the eggs; \r\ncook just until slightly set, then break them up.  Add the \r\nshrimp and stir-fry just until they turn pink.  Add most \r\nof the bean sprouts, the green onion, and 1/4 cup of the \r\npeanuts and stir-fry until the sprouts and onions are \r\ncrisp-tender, 1 to 2 minutes.  Remove from the heat and \r\ntransfer to a serving plate.\r\n\r\nSprinkle with the chopped cilantro, remaining 1/4 cup peanuts, \r\nand dried shrimp.  Garnish with the remaining bean sprouts, \r\ncilantro sprigs, and lemon or lime wedges, and serve immediately.  \r\nDiners squeeze lemon or lime juice to taste.\r\n\r\nServes 8 as a pasta course, or 4 as a main course\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai\r\n\r\nfrom Frugal Gourmet on Our Immigrant Ancestors\r\n\r\n1/2 pound thai flat rice noodles (bahn pho) or rice sticks\r\n\r\noil for deep-frying\r\n1/2 pound fresh firm bean curd, cut into tiny cubes\r\n\r\n1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n1/2 tablespoon garlic, chopped\r\n1/2 cup very thinly sliced skinless and boneless chicken breast\r\n\r\n1/4 pound shrimp, peeled and cut in half the long way\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon dried shrimp powder\r\n1/4 teaspoon freshly ground black pepper\r\n3 tablespoons finely chopped dry-roasted salted peanuts\r\n2 tablespoons lime juice, freshly squeezed\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n6 tablespoons Thai fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup tamarind sauce\r\n2 teaspoons red chili paste with garlic\r\n2 cups fresh bean sprouts\r\n\r\n2 limes, quartered\r\n1/3 cup fresh coriander leaves\r\n3 chopped scallions\r\n4 tablespoons finely chopped dry-roasted peanuts\r\n\r\nSoak the noodles in ample warm water until supple, \r\nabout 15 minutes, drain and set aside.  Place the \r\nnoodles in boiling water and cook just until the \r\nwater returns to the boil.  Drain again.  \r\n\r\nHeat the oil for deep-frying to 375 degrees and \r\ndeep-fry the cut bean curd.  Be sure the pat the \r\nbean curd dry on a paper towel first so that it\r\nwill not spatter fat on you.  Drain the bean curd\r\nand set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat a large wok and add the peanut oil, garlic, and \r\nchicken.  Chow for a few minues and then add the \r\nshrimp, drained noodles, beaten eggs, and \r\ndeep-fried bean curd.  Toss well and chow for 3 to 4 \r\nminutes over medium-high heat.  Add the remaining\r\ningredients, except the garnishes, and chow or stir for\r\na few more minutes until the noodles are hot and \r\ntender.\r\n\r\nPlace on a serving platter with the garnishes, which are\r\nan integral part of the dish.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy Pad Thai \r\n\r\nFrom: Asian Pasta, by Linda Burum\r\n\r\n3 1/2 tablespoon distilled white vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons water\r\n2 1/2 tablespoons fish sauce\r\n3 tablespoons tomato paste\r\n2 1/2 tablespoons sugar\r\n1/2 tablespoon dried shrimp, pounded to a powder\r\n9 oz flat rice sticks, 1/8 inch wide\r\nvegetable oil\r\n1/3 cup fresh thai or purple or sweet basil leaves\r\n2 red Serrano chili peppers, seeded and very finely minced\r\n4 cloves garlic, minced\r\n1 1/2 large boned chicken breast halves, \r\n  cut crosswise into 3/8 inch thick strips,\r\n  or 1 pound lean pork, cut into thin slices 3/8 inch by 2 inches\r\n8 oz small, cooked shelled shrimp\r\n2 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n2 cups fresh bean sprouts, beans removed\r\n1/4 cup roasted, unsalted peanuts, coarsely ground\r\ncherry tomatoes, halved\r\nlime wedges\r\nmint sprigs or sliced green onions\r\n\r\nCombine the vinegar, water, fish sauce, tomato paste, sugar, \r\nand dried shrimp in a small bowl; mix until well blended and \r\nreserve.  In a large pot, soak the noodles in enough water to \r\ncover.  In a small skillet or pot, heat vegetable oil 3/4 to \r\n1 inch deep to 350 degrees, or until a dried rice noodle puffs \r\ninstantly when dropped into the oil.  Deep-fry the basil leaves \r\na few at a time, turning them once or twice until they are crisp, \r\nor 40 seconds to a minute; drain on paper toweling.\r\n\r\nBring the noodles to a boil and cook them 2 minutes, or until \r\nthey are almost tender.  Drain and rinse them well, then spread \r\nthem on paper toweling to dry slightly.  Head a wok or large \r\nskillet and add about 2 1/2 tablespoons vegetable oil.  Fry \r\nthe Serrano peppers about 30 seconds, then add the garlic and \r\nstir-fry until it is soft.  Add the chicken or pork and stir-fry \r\nuntil the chicken is almost opaque throughout or the pork is \r\nbrowned.  Stir in the shrimp and the sauce and mix completely.  \r\nMake a well in the center of the mixture and pour in the eggs.  \r\nWhen they are almost set, scramble them evenly.  Add half the \r\nnoodles, throughly incorporating them into the mixture; stir \r\nin the remaining noodles and half the bean sprouts.  Cook \r\njust until the bean sprouts are nearly wilted.\r\n\r\nHeap the meat and noodles onto a platter.  Cover one half of \r\nthem with ground peanuts and the other half with uncooked \r\nbean sprouts.  Ring the noodles with lime wedges, cherry \r\ntomatoes, and mint sprigs and garnish the top with the fried basil.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom \"The Original Thai Cookbook,\" by Jennifer Brennan:\r\n\r\n(posted to rec.food.cooking earlier)\r\n  \r\n  1/2     cup          vegetable oil\r\n  6       cloves       garlic, finely chopped\r\n  1       cup small    cooked shrimp\r\n  1       Tablespoon   granulated sugar\r\n  3       Tablespoons  fish sauce (Nam Pla)\r\n  1 1/2   Tablespoons  tomato ketchup\r\n  2                    eggs, beaten\r\n  3/4     pound        (approximately 3 hanks) rice vermicelli\r\n                       (Sen Mee), soaked in hot water for 15 minutes\r\n                       and drained.\r\n  1       cup          bean sprouts\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Garnish:\r\n  \r\n  1      Tablespoon    dried shrimp powder\r\n  2      Tablespoons   peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n  1/2    teaspoon      dried red chilli [sic] flakes\r\n  2                    green onions, finely chopped\r\n  2      Tablespoons   coriander leaves, chopped\r\n  2                    limes, sliced into 1/8\" circles\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Heat the oil in a wok and fry the garlic until golden.\r\nQuickly add the shrimp and stir-fry until heated through.\r\nAdd the sugar, fish sauce and ketchup and stir until sugar\r\ndissolves.  Add the beaten eggs, letting them set slightly,\r\nthen stir to scramble.  Add the noodles and toss and stir\r\nfor about 2 minutes.  Reserving about 4 Tablespoons of the\r\nbean sprouts, add the remainder to the wok.  Stir over heat\r\nuntil the bean sprouts are barely cooked.  Turn the Pud Thai\r\nonto a platter, placing the reserved, raw bean sprouts on one side.\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Presentation:\r\n  \r\nSprinkle the noodles with the garnish ingredients in the\r\nfollowing order: shrimp powder, peanuts, chilli flakes, green\r\nonions, coriander leaves.  Ring the platter with the lime slices\r\nand serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI (Serves 2)\r\n===================\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n1/4-1/3 pound flat rice stick noodles (banh pho), 1/4 \" wide\r\n      1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n    1/4 pound pork, cut into matchstick strips\r\n            6 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n        1 tsp crushed garlic\r\n            1 egg\r\n       2 Tbsp water\r\n       2 Tbsp rice vinegar\r\n       1 Tbsp fish sauce\r\n       1 Tbsp sugar\r\n      1/4 cup chopped toasted peanuts\r\n      1/4 tsp ground dry shrimp\r\n              freshly ground white pepper\r\n      1/4 tsp Asian chili powder (to taste)\r\n        1 cup bean sprouts, washed and drained\r\n      1/4 cup scallions, cut 3/4\" long\r\n              fresh coriander\r\n              wedges of fresh lime\r\n\r\nSoak noodles in warm water for 60 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nPrepare all other ingredients and arrange near the wok.  You will need\r\nto work fast.\r\n\r\nIn the wok, fry the pork in the peanut oil at medium heat.  When half \r\ncooked, add the shrimps and garlic and stir.  Cook until shrimp and \r\npork are done.\r\n\r\nBeat the egg and add it to the mixture.  Cook, stirring, for about \r\nhalf a minute.\r\n\r\nTurn the heat to high.  Add the drained noodles to shrimp mixture.\r\nAdd water, vinegar, fish sauce, sugar, shrimp powder, and most of \r\nthe peanuts.  Sprinkle in white pepper and chili powder.  Toss to \r\ncombine.  Let it cook on one side (Don\'t stir; keep checking the \r\nunderside as if it were a big pancake).  Flip it over as best you can\r\nand repeat until nearly cooked, about 5 to 10 minutes.\r\n\r\nAdd most of the sprouts and scallions.  Stir and cook for another \r\nminute.\r\n\r\nTurn onto a plate.  Top with the rest of the sprouts, scallions, and\r\npeanuts.  Garnish with coriander and serve with a wedge of fresh lemon.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai with Shrimp\r\n--------------------\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n\r\n8 oz.		small size rice noodles\r\n3 T		tomato sauce or tamarind paste\r\n2 T		veg. oil\r\n1 T		pickled radish\r\n3 T		sugar\r\n1/3 c		water or chicken stock\r\n1 		egg\r\n3 T		fish sauce\r\n1/2 lb		shrimp, cleaned and shelled\r\n1 handful	bean sprouts (fresh); chopped once or twice\r\n2 oz		green onions, cut into 1/2 inch pieces\r\n2 T		finely chopped peanuts\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Soak the rice noodles in cold tap water about 20 minutes, until \r\n   they are \"springy\".  Then drain in a colander until needed.\r\n\r\n2. If using dried tamarind, soak the tamarind in hot water for awhile,\r\n   then mash with a fork to soften.  Force as much of the mixture as \r\n   you can through a seive to remove bits of bark, etc.\r\n\r\n3. Heat oil in wok, and add the tamarind/tomato sauce, picked radish,\r\n   and sugar.  Mix well and let heat up.\r\n\r\n4. Add the noodles, small portions at a time, and add water/stock.\r\n   Mix well until all the noodles are coated with the mixture.  Add more\r\n   liquid if necessary -- it will cook out.  Don\'t be easy on the \r\n   noodles -- chop them with the spatula or spoon some to separate them.\r\n   It may help to \"toss\" the noodles like a salad, to get them coated.\r\n\r\n5. Beat the egg and mix with the noodles.  Add the fish sauce and shrimp.\r\n   Mix everything thoroughly.  The noodles will tend to \"clump\", so stir\r\n   or \"toss\" like a salad to get everything mixed, and to ensure that the\r\n   egg and shrimp cook thoroughly.  It will help to cover the wok with\r\n   a lid for a minute or so, then toss the mixture, then cover again.\r\n   You\'ll know it\'s done when the shrimp are completely pink.  There may\r\n   be a little browning of the noodles; stirring will keep them from\r\n   burning.\r\n\r\n6. Add the bean sprouts, green onions, and chopped peanuts.  Mix well,\r\n   then turn off the heat and let stand a minute or so.  Serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\nThis recipe is my absolute favorite.  It tastes just\r\nlike the pad thai served at Thai Palace in San Francisco on\r\nNoe and 15th.\r\n\r\nServes ten as a side dish.  Serves six as an only dish.\r\n\r\n1    16-ounce package chantaboon rice sticks, medium thread\r\n     (I think the spelling of \"chantaboon\" can also be found\r\n      as \"jantaboon\" or something like that.)\r\n1    tablespoon vegetable oil\r\n6    eggs beaten\r\n\r\n1/4  cup vegetable oil\r\n8    garlic gloves\r\n1    pound pork, beef or chicken, sliced thin, bite sized,\r\n     or shrimp, shelled and deveined.\r\n     (I used tofu when I made this. If you do the\r\n     the same, press the tofu between three sheets\r\n     of towel paper on the top and the bottom. Put a        \r\n     plate on top and a two pound weight on top of the\r\n     plate. Wait 20 minutes.)\r\n\r\n1/4  cup white vinegar\r\n\r\n1/4  cup sugar\r\n  1  cup sliced salted radish (chai Po)\r\n     (These are packaged in a plastic see through flat\r\n     container and slightly orange in color)\r\n1/4  cup fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n  1  cup coarse ground roasted peanuts\r\n  2  tablespoons chile powder or paprika\r\n     (I used paprika)\r\n\r\n  2  cups bean sprouts\r\n     (These I served on the side cold)\r\n  1  cup sliced green onion\r\n  1  cup sliced cilantro\r\n  1  lime\r\n\r\nSoak rice sticks in lukewarm water for 1 hour, drain and set\r\naside.  Set wok over high heat, for 1 minute.  Heat wok with\r\n1 tablespoon of oil until sizzling hot and coat sides of wok\r\nevenly.  Add eggs and fry, until eggs set, turn over and\r\nfry, until light brown on both sides.  Remove from wok and\r\nslice thin, bite size.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat 1/4 cup of oil in wok until sizzling hot.  Add garlic\r\nand cook until fragrant.  add meat, stir and cook, until\r\nmeat or tofu is done, about 1 to 2 minutes.  Add rice sticks\r\nand vinegar, cook until rice sticks soften.  Add eggs, and\r\nthe next 5 ingredients, stir to blend.  Remove to serving\r\nplate.  Serve bean sprouts  cold on the side. Garnish  with\r\ngreen onion and cilantro.\r\n\r\nServe with slices of fresh lime. Squeeze lime on pad thai.\r\n\r\nEnjoy!\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nStir-fried Rice Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom:  Cooking with Bon Appetit:  Oriental Favorites\r\n\r\n8 oz (1/8 inch wide) rice noodles\r\n1 whole chicken breast, boned, skinned\r\n8 medium-size shrimp, shelled, deveined\r\n1/2 cup water\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce\r\n3 tablespoons sugar\r\n1 tablespoon lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon paprika\r\n1/8 teaspoon red (cayenne) pepper\r\n1/2 pound bean sprouts\r\n3 green onions, white part only, cut into 1 inch shreds\r\n3 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 large garlic cloves, finely chopped\r\n1 egg\r\n4 tablespoons finely crushed roasted peanuts\r\n\r\nPlace rice noodles in a large bowl.  Cover with water; soak \r\n45 minutes.  Cut chicken into 1 1/2\" by 1/3\" strips.  Cut \r\nshrimp in half lengthwise; set aside.  Combine water, fish \r\nsauce, sugar, lime juice, paprika, and red pepper in a small \r\nbowl; set aside.  Reserve 1/4 of bean sprouts for topping; \r\ncombine remaining bean sprouts and green onions.  Drain noodles.  \r\nHeat a wok over medium-high heat.  Add oil and heat.  Add \r\ngarlic; fry until garlic starts to brown.  Increase heat.  \r\nAdd chicken; stir-fry until almost cooked, about 2 minutes.  \r\nPush chicken to one side.  Break egg into wok. Stir quickly \r\nto break up yolk and scramble egg.  When egg is set, mix \r\nwith chicken.  Add drained noodles, shrimp, fish-sauce mixture \r\nand 3 tablespoons peanuts.  Cook and stir over high heat \r\n2 to 3 minutes or until noodles are soft and most of liquid \r\nis absorbed.  Add green-onion mixture; cook, stirring, 1 \r\nmore minute.  Spoon onto a heated platter.  Sprinkle with \r\nreserved bean sprouts, then with remaining peanuts.\r\n\r\nServes 6\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai (Sauteed Rice Noodles) \r\n\r\nfrom rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n1 16 oz package rice noodles\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil\r\n5 or 6 garlic cloves, finely chopped\r\n1 pound medium shrimp, shelled and deveined\r\n2 firm-style bean curd squares, cut into 1/2 inch cubes\r\n1/4 cup coarsely chopped pickled turnips\r\n1/2 cup white vinegar\r\n1/5 cup fish sauce\r\n1 tablespoon paprika\r\n1/4 cup sugar\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n1/4 pound mung bean sprouts\r\n3 scallions, cut into 1/2 inch pieces\r\n1/2 cup ground unsalted peanuts\r\n1 fresh red chili pepper, seeded and coarsely chopped\r\n1 lemon, cut into wedges\r\ncoriander leaves for garnish\r\n1/4 cup crushed red pepper (optional)\r\n\r\nIn a large bowl, soak the rice noodles in 10 to 12 cups of \r\ncold water for 2 hours.  Drain and cover with a damp towel \r\nto retain moisture.\r\n\r\nIn a wok or large frying pan, heat the oil and stir-fry the \r\ngarlic until it is light brown.  Add the shrimp, bean curd \r\nand pickled turnips; stir in the vinegar, fish sauce, paprika \r\nand sugar.  When thoroughly mixed, fold in the noodles.  \r\nWhen the noodles are completely coated, spread them out to \r\nthe sides of the wok or frying pan, leaving a space in the middle.  \r\nAdd the beaten eggs.  As the eggs cook, fold the noodles over them \r\nand stir to combine all of the ingredients evenly.  Stir in half \r\nof the bean sprouts, then add the scallions, ground peanuts and \r\nchopped chili pepper. Toss several times to mix well.\r\n\r\nServe on a large platter with lemon wedges.  Top with the \r\nremaining bean sprouts and garnish with coriander leaves. \r\nServe the crushed red pepper on the side, for those who \r\nlike it extra-spicy.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPaht Thai  \r\n\r\nFrom Real Thai, by Nancie McDermott; Chronicle Books;\r\nISBN 0-8118-0017-2\r\n\r\nPaht Thai is a noodle dish almost everyone seems to like.  \r\nA tangle of slender rice noodles is sauteed with garlic, \r\nshallots, and an orchestra of sweet, sour, and salty \r\ningredients that play a piquant symphony of Thai flavors.  \r\nA handful of fresh bean sprouts provides a cooling contrast \r\nto the hot, seasoned noodles, and circles of lime invite you \r\nto bring sourness to center stage as you begin to eat.\r\n\r\nTraditional ingredients are salty dried shrimp; crispy pieces \r\nof fried, pressed bean curd; sweet-sour nuggets of pickled \r\nwhite radish; chopped peanuts; flat, green garlic chives; \r\nand a balanced chorus -- sweet, sour, salty, hot -- of palm \r\nsugar, tamarind, vinegar, lime, brown bean sauce, and crushed \r\ndried red chilies.\r\n\r\nThai cooks blithely tinker with the classic formula to create \r\nsignature versions, and you can, too.  Siriluk Williams, owner \r\nof Sukothai Restaurant in Ft. Lauderdale, Florida, gave me her \r\nrecipe for home-style paht Thai.  I love its accessible ingredients, \r\nsimple steps, and delicious results.\r\n\r\n1/4 pound dried rice stick noodles\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n1 tablespoon coarsely chopped garlic\r\n8 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n\r\n1 egg, lightly beaten\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n2 teaspoons sugar\r\n2 tablespoons coarsely chopped, dry-roasted peanuts\r\n\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n4 slender green onions, sliced in 1 inch lengths\r\n1 lime, quartered lengthwise\r\n\r\nSoak rice noodles in warm water to cover for 15 to 20 minutes.  \r\nMeanwhile, prepare all the remaining ingredients and place them \r\nnext to the stove, along with a small serving platter.  When \r\nthe noodles are very limp and white, drain and measure out \r\n2 1/2 cups.  Set these by the stove as well.\r\n\r\nHeat a wok or large, deep skillet over medium-high heat.  Add \r\n1 tablespoon of the oil and swirl to coat the surface.  When \r\nthe oil is very hot, drop a piece of the garlic into the pan.  \r\nIf it sizzles immediately, the oil is ready.  Add the garlic \r\nand toss until golden, about 30 seconds.  Add the shrimp and \r\ntoss until they turn pink and are opaque, no more than 1 minute.  \r\nRemove from the pan and set aside.\r\n\r\nAdd the egg to the pan and tilt the pan to spread it into a thin \r\nsheet.  As soon as it begins to set and is opaque, scramble it \r\nto break it into small lumps.  Remove from the pan and set aside \r\nwith the shrimp.\r\n\r\nAdd the remaining 1 tablespoon oil, heat for 30 seconds, and \r\nadd the softened noodles.  Using a spatula, spread and pull the \r\nnoodles into a thin layer covering the surface of the pan. Then \r\nscrape them into a clump again and gently turn them over.  Hook \r\nloops of noodles with the edge of the spatula and pull them up \r\nthe sides, spreading them out into a layer again.  Repeat this \r\nprocess several times as the stiff, white noodles soften and curl \r\ninto ivory ringlets.  Add the fish sauce and turn the noodles so \r\nthey are evenly seasoned.  Add the sugar and peanuts, turning the \r\nnoodles a few more times.\r\n\r\nReserving a small handful for garnish, add the bean sprouts, along \r\nwith the green onions and shrimp-egg mixture.  Cook for 1 minute, \r\nturning often.  Transfer the noodles to the serving platter and \r\nsqueeze the juice of 2 lime wedges over the top.  Garnish with \r\nremaining bean sprouts and lime wedges and serve at once.\r\n\r\nServes 1 as a main course, 2 as an appetizer.  \r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nfrom The Book of Thai Cooking, by Hilaire Walden; HPBooks,\r\nISBN 1-55788-038-7\r\n\r\n3 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 garlic cloves, finely crushed\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n3 to 4 tablespoons lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon crushed palm sugar\r\n\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n\r\n12 ounces rice vermicelli, soaked in water 20 minutes, drained\r\n4 ounces peeled shrimp\r\n4 ounces bean sprouts\r\n4 green onions, sliced\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons dried shrimp, ground, to garnish\r\nFinely chopped roasted peanuts, cilantro leaves and lime slices to garnish\r\n\r\nHeat oil in a wok, add garlic and cook, stirring occasionally, \r\nuntil golden.  Stir in fish sauce, lime juice and sugar until \r\nsugar has dissolved.  Quickly stir in eggs and cook for a few \r\nseconds.  Stir in noodles to coat with garlic and egg, then \r\nadd shrimps, 3/4 of the bean sprouts and half of the green onions.\r\n\r\nWhen noodles are tender, transfer contents of wok to a warmed \r\nserving dish.  Garnish with remaining bean sprouts and green \r\nonions, dried shrimp, peanuts, cilantro leaves and lime slices.\r\n\r\nMakes 4 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI (Serves 2)\r\n===================\r\n\r\n(posted on rec.food.cooking)\r\n\r\n1/4-1/3 pound flat rice stick noodles (banh pho), 1/4 \" wide\r\n      1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n    1/4 pound pork, cut into matchstick strips\r\n            6 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n        1 tsp crushed garlic\r\n            1 egg\r\n       2 Tbsp water\r\n       2 Tbsp rice vinegar\r\n       1 Tbsp fish sauce\r\n       1 Tbsp sugar\r\n      1/4 cup chopped toasted peanuts\r\n      1/4 tsp ground dry shrimp\r\n              freshly ground white pepper\r\n      1/4 tsp Asian chili powder (to taste)\r\n        1 cup bean sprouts, washed and drained\r\n      1/4 cup scallions, cut 3/4\" long\r\n              fresh coriander\r\n              wedges of fresh lime\r\n\r\nSoak noodles in warm water for 60 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nPrepare all other ingredients and arrange near the wok.  You will need\r\nto work fast.\r\n\r\nIn the wok, fry the pork in the peanut oil at medium heat.  When half \r\ncooked, add the shrimps and garlic and stir.  Cook until shrimp and \r\npork are done.\r\n\r\nBeat the egg and add it to the mixture.  Cook, stirring, for about \r\nhalf a minute.\r\n\r\nTurn the heat to high.  Add the drained noodles to shrimp mixture.\r\nAdd water, vinegar, fish sauce, sugar, shrimp powder, and most of \r\nthe peanuts.  Sprinkle in white pepper and chili powder.  Toss to \r\ncombine.  Let it cook on one side (Don\'t stir; keep checking the \r\nunderside as if it were a big pancake).  Flip it over as best you can\r\nand repeat until nearly cooked, about 5 to 10 minutes.\r\n\r\nAdd most of the sprouts and scallions.  Stir and cook for another \r\nminute.\r\n\r\nTurn onto a plate.  Top with the rest of the sprouts, scallions, and\r\npeanuts.  Garnish with coriander and serve with a wedge of fresh lemon.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai\r\n\r\n(posted on rec.food.cooking)\r\n\r\n1 Pkg (10-16 oz. (.3-.5 kg) rice stick noodles\r\n2Tbl oil (30 ml)\r\n3 or more cloves garlic, crushed or minced\r\n8oz. (250g) Shrimp, peeled (optional)\r\n8oz. (250g) Chicken, Pork or more Shrimp, cut into dice or matchsticks\r\n(optional)\r\n2 or 3 eggs\r\n1 Cup (250ml) bean sprouts\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) chopped/ground peanuts\r\n1 red chile, finely chopped, or 1 tsp (5ml) red pepper flakes (optional)\r\n1 Cup (250ml) sliced cabbage\r\nCilantro leaves\r\nLime wedges\r\n\r\nSauce\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) Thai Fish Sauce\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) White Vinegar\r\n2 Tbl (30 ml) white sugar\r\n3 Tbl (90 ml) Paprika\r\n\r\nSoak the rice noodles in cold water at least two hours before cooking. \r\nDrain\r\n\r\nIn a large wok, heat oil and stir-fry garlic for 30 seconds.  Add shrimp\r\nand other meat if used.\r\n\r\nAdd the noodles, and stir-fry until al dente.  Add sauce ingredients,\r\ncook to allow most of this to be absorbed (2 minutes or so).\r\n\r\nSpread the noodles, etc. out to the sides, and add eggs.  Some will crack\r\nthe eggs directly into the wok, others will pre-scramble.  If cracked\r\ninto the pan, start stirring them up when partially cooked, so you get\r\n\'streaky\' yellow and white eggs.  As they cook, fold the noodle mixture\r\nback in.\r\n\r\nAdd 1/2 the bean sprouts, peanuts, red pepper, folded into the mixture.\r\n\r\nServe hot, garnished with the rest of the bean sprouts, chopped cabbage,\r\ncilantro.  Serve with lime wedges to be squeezed into the noodles.\r\n\r\nIn my house, we have doctored this with thai hot chile sauce, sweet and\r\nsour sauce, or  sate-style peanut sauce.  It takes to any of these very\r\nwell, depending on your tastes.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nSpicy Thai Noodles (from Good Housekeeping (I think))\r\n(Not very authentic)\r\n\r\n1 16-ounce package Oriental noodles or linguine\r\n1/4 cup soy sauce\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n2 tablespoons chili sauce\r\n3 tablespoons salad oil\r\n1 small red pepper, thinly sliced\r\n1 tablespoon grated, peeled gingerroot\r\n1 garlic clove, minced\r\n1 bunch green onions, chopped\r\n2 large eggs, lightly beaten\r\n1/2 pound bean sprouts\r\n1 small tomato, cut into think wedges\r\n1/4 cup salted peanuts, coarsely chopped\r\n1 tablespoon chopped fresh cilantro or parsley (optional)\r\n1/4 teaspoon crushed red pepper\r\nlime wedges for garnish\r\n\r\n1.  Prepare noodles as label directs; drain and keep warm.\r\n2.  Meanwhile, in cup, stir soy sauce, sugar, chili sauce,\r\nand 1/2 cup water; set aside.  In 12-inch skillet over \r\nmedium-high heat, in hot salad oil, cook red pepper, ginger, \r\ngarlic, and all but 1/4 cup chopped green onions until tender \r\nand lightly browned, about 5 minutes.  Push vegetables to one \r\nside of skillet.\r\n3.  To same skillet over medium-high heat, add eggs, stirring \r\nuntil set but still moist.  Stir in bean sprouts and soy-sauce \r\nmixture; over high heat, heat to boiling.  Add noodles and tomato \r\nwedges, tossing to coat with sauce; heat through. \r\n4.  Spoon noodle mixture onto large warm platter.  Sprinkle \r\nwith chopped peanuts, chopped cilantro, crushed red pepper, and \r\nremaining chopped green onions.  Garnish with lime wedges.  \r\nMakes 5 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai  (posted on net)\r\n\r\nnoodles - vermicelli or rice noodles ~6 ounces\r\n2 Tablespoon peanut butter\r\n5 Tablespoon soy sauce or tamari\r\n1 Tablespoon brown sugar\r\n2 scrambled eggs\r\n6 diced scallions\r\n5 cloves pressed garlic\r\npeanuts\r\n1/3 cup vinegar\r\nquartered lime\r\n\r\nCook, rinse, and refrigerate the noodles ahead of time.\r\nIn a bowl, mix the peanut butter, soy sauce, and sugar.\r\n\r\nIn a wok, saute the scallionsa nd garlic.  You can add bean sprouts\r\nat this point too.  After a few minutes, add the noodles, and\r\nstir-fry them for about 5 minutes.  Then add the stuff in the bowl\r\nand the vinegar.  Cook this for a couple more minutes.  Last,\r\nadd the eggs and peanuts, and heat until hot.  Serve with the\r\nlime wedges on the side.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: valerie@jarthur.Claremont.EDU (Valerie Okinaka)\r\nSubject: Re: Pad Thai\r\n\r\nHere is a recipe which appeared in the LA times a while back:\r\n\r\nAtlas Bar and Grill Pad Thai\r\n1 (7-ounce) package dry Thai flat ribbon noodles\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n2 tablespoons minced garlic\r\n2 tablespoons peanut oil\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n1/2 teaspoon cayenne pepper or to taste\r\n1/4 cup Thai fish or shrimp sauce\r\n1/2 cup catsup\r\n2 tablespoons to 1/4 cup ground dried shrimp  (I suggest going easy on this)\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n2 tablespoons chopped roasted peanuts\r\nPad Thai Marinated Shrimp (See below)\r\nChopped green onions, lime, sunflower sprouts and carrot for garnish.\r\n\r\n  Soak noodles in water to cover 15 minutes.  Soak sprouts in cold water to\r\ncover.\r\n  Brown garlic lightly in oil.  Stir in sugar, cayenne, fish sauce, catsup and\r\ndried shrimp.  Add eggs and noodles.  Fry until noodles turn orange and are\r\ntender.\r\n  Add bean sprouts and cook 1 minute.  Top with peanuts, grilled Pad Thai\r\nMarinated Shrimp and garnish with green onions, lime, and sunflower sprouts\r\nand carrots as desired.  Makes 4 servings.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai Marinated Shrimp\r\n1/2 pound cleaned and butterflied shrimp\r\n2 tablespoons fish or shrimp sauce\r\n1/2 cup lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon minced garlic\r\n1/2 teaspoon cayenne pepper\r\n1/4 cup catsup\r\n\r\n  Place shrimp in shallow container.  Mix fish sauce, lime juice, garlic,\r\ncayenne to taste and catsup.  Pour over shrimp.  Marinate 2 hours.  Skewer on\r\nsoaked bamboo skewers and grill when ready to serve.\r\n\r\nBTW, I know that using catsup in Pad Thai is not very authentic but it does\r\nmake a reasonable substitude for Pad Thai sauce and is very easy.  Authentic\r\nPad Thai sauce uses tamarind and other ingredients.  A good authentic recipe\r\ncan be found in \"Thai Cooking from the Siam Cuisine Restaurant.\"  The latest\r\nfrugal gourmet book (Immigrant Ancestors) also has a Pad Thai recipe that I\r\nhaven\'t tried yet.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: horton@reed.uucp (Nike Horton)\r\nSubject: Cleartext copy of \"Pad-thai (noodles with peanut sauce) \"\r\n\r\nPAD-THAI(M)              USENET Cookbook              PAD-THAI(M)\r\n\r\nPAD-THAI\r\n\r\n     PAD-THAI - Thai noodles with peanuts and seafood\r\n\r\n     This recipe is originally from William Crawford and Kamolmal\r\n     Pootararka\'s Thai Home-Cooking: From Kamolmal\'s Kitchen.\r\n     This is a very popular Thai one-dish meal.  In Thai homes it\r\n     is normally prepared with dried shrimp and tofu; restaurants\r\n     often use shredded pork or chicken.\r\n\r\nINGREDIENTS (4 large servings)\r\n     1/2 lb    cellophane noodles\r\n     1/2 lb    shrimp\r\n     1/4 cup   fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n     1/4 cup   white vinegar\r\n     1/4 cup   sugar\r\n     1 tsp     paprika\r\n     1 bunch   scallions\r\n     1/2 cup   vegetable oil\r\n     2         cloves garlic, crushed\r\n     2         eggs\r\n     3/4 lb    bean sprouts\r\n     2 Tbsp    red chilis, crushed\r\n     3/4 cup   peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n     2         limes\r\n\r\nPROCEDURE\r\n          (1)  Soak noodles in warm water until soft, about 10\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (2)  Prepare shrimp (or shredded meat).\r\n\r\n          (3)  Mix together fish sauce, vinegar, sugar and\r\n               paprika.\r\n\r\n          (4)  Chop scallions into small bite-size pieces.\r\n\r\n          (5)  In wok or large skillet, stir-fry garlic in oil\r\n               for one minute. Add shrimp and cook until pink.\r\n\r\n          (6)  Add noodles and sauce from step 3.  Cook for 2\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (7)  Make space in the mixture to break the eggs.  Let\r\n               them set for 30 seconds then stir them into the\r\n               mixture.  Cook for 2 more minutes.\r\n\r\n          (8)  Add bean sprouts and scallions.  Cook for 2 more\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (9)  Serve hot topped with chopped chilis, peanuts and\r\n               lime juice.\r\n\r\nNOTES\r\n     I often add squid or chicken with the shrimp.\r\n\r\nRATING\r\n     Difficulty: easy to moderate.  Time: 30 minutes preparation,\r\n     15 minutes cooking.  Precision: approximate measurement OK.\r\n\r\nCONTRIBUTOR\r\n     Nicholas Horton\r\n     Reed College, Portland, Oregon, USA\r\n     tektronix!reed!horton\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: mmr@cs.rit.edu (Margaret M Reek)\r\nSubject: Pad Thai\r\nThai restaurant and catering business.\r\n\r\n1/4 lb. boneless chicken breast, sliced\r\n1/4 lb. rice noodles (1/4 inch wide)\r\n1/4 lb. bean sprouts\r\n3-4 Tbs oil\r\n1 Tbs Nam Pla (fish sauce)\r\n3 Tbs sugar\r\n1 Tbs vinegar\r\n3 Tbs ground peanuts (dry roasted, unsalted)\r\n1 egg\r\n2 scallions\r\n1 Tbs paprika (for color)\r\n1 Tbs pickled daikon radish\r\n[ ground chilis - optional (depending on your opinion of hot stuff)]\r\n\r\nSoak the rice noodles for 30 minutes in warm water; you might like to\r\nbreak them somewhat first so they are easier to eat.\r\nHeat a wok (or whatever you\'ve got) for several minutes, until it is\r\nreally hot.  Add oil and chicken, stiring until the chicken in tender\r\n(only a couple minutes for raw chicken).  Add egg, stir and add\r\nnoodles, fish sauce, sugar, vinegar, paprika, and radish.  Mix well\r\nand add bean sprouts and onion.  Cook long enough to heat through.\r\n\r\nIn theory this serves 1; my experience shows it serves 2 unless you\r\nhave a totally gargantuan appetite (my husband and I are not exactly\r\ntotal wimps in this area).  Don\'t do more than double the amount in\r\none pan, or it just won\'t turn out right.  The secret to Thai cooking\r\nis fast cooking, and you can\'t do it with volumes of food.\r\n\r\nAnother tip is to make sure EVERYTHING is ready to go once you turn\r\non the burner.  This dish goes very fast when cooking, and you can\'t\r\nbe fiddling around chopping or measuring once you\'ve started.  I put\r\ntogether in small bowls the things that get dumped in together, so I\r\ndon\'t have to think much at all when doing the cooking.  Thai cooking\r\nis something I have yet to feed to guest, because I haven\'t bothered\r\nto figure out how to get the timing right for more than 2 people (but\r\nwith several dishes per person).\r\n\r\nThe Nam Pla and pickled radish should be available at a variety of\r\nOriental stores, not just those catering to the Thai market.  The Nam\r\nPla comes in a huge bottle, but fear not, you\'ll use it a lot in Thai\r\ncooking.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai-pseudo-vegetarian style\r\njonathan kandell\r\ntucson arizona\r\njkandell@ccit.arizona.edu\r\n \r\n1/4 cup        thai fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup + 2 T  white vinegar\r\n2-4 T          sugar\r\n1 t            paprika\r\n8 oz           thai rice noodles about 1/4\" thick\r\n8 oz           tofu\r\n1-2 T          dried shrimp (optional)\r\n3 T            oil\r\n2-3 cloves     garlic\r\n2              eggs\r\n3/4 lb         bean sprouts\r\n3              green onions, sliced on the diagonal,   \r\n               including white part\r\n3/4 C          ground peanuts\r\n1+ T           roasted red chili peppers.  [Take some dried reds\r\n               and cook them in an ungreased wok over low\r\n               stirring constantly until they start to brown. \r\n               Grind in a coffee grinder or spice mill.]\r\n \r\nCombine first four ingredients and let sit until sugar dissolves.\r\nSoak rice noodles/sticks in warm water until they are soft but\r\ndon\'t disintegrate when pressed, about 40 minutes. Drain.\r\nDrain tofu by wrapping it in a clean towel and pressing with a\r\nlarge can of tomatoes, about 30 minutes.  Cut into 1/4 inch\r\ncubes.  Rinse dried shrimp in water and drain.\r\n \r\nPut oil in wok, heat to medium, put in crushed garlic. Swirl\r\naround for about a minute.  Add noodles and mix around for a\r\nminute or two.  \r\n \r\nAdd the tofu and shrimp then immediately add the liquid. Keep\r\nstirring until the liquid has all been absorbed into the noodles,\r\nabout a couple of minutes.\r\n \r\nLower heat to low or medium-low.  Push some of the stuff aside at\r\none end of wok, and break an egg in.  Immediately cover with\r\nnoodles.  Do the same at the other end of wok.  Let the eggs cook\r\n*undisturbed* until the yolks are practically cooked, about three\r\nminutes. (*Carefully* peak if unsure.)  Slip a slotted spoon\r\nunder each egg in turn, and bring upward, through the noodles,\r\nshaking as you go. The idea is to break up the cooked egg into\r\nthe rest of the mixture into tiny bits.\r\n \r\nMix in sprouts and green onions. Let cook for another two\r\nminutes.  Turn off heat.  Add crushed peanuts and enough roasted\r\nred chilies to your desired hotness.\r\n \r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai \r\n(Vegetarian Times, June 1993)\r\n\r\nNoodles and Sauce:\r\n8 to 10 ounces dried flat-sided rice noodles\r\n4 tablespoons distilled white vinegar\r\n3 tablespoons tomato paste\r\n3 tablespoons water\r\n2 tablespoons sugar\r\n1 to 2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n2 cloves garlic, minced\r\n1 fresh green chili, seeded and minced\r\n2 eggs (optional)\r\n\r\nVegetables:\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n1/3 cup chopped unsalted dry-roasted peanuts (optional)\r\nLime wedges and sliced green onions for garnish\r\n\r\nBring 2 quarts water to boil and remove from heat.  Soak\r\nnoodles in water for 20 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a bowl, mix together vinegar, tomato paste, water and \r\nsugar.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a frying pan, heat oil and stir-fry garlic and chili on\r\nmedium heat for 3 minutes.  Stir in tomato-vinegar mixture\r\nand make a well in the center of pan.  Crack in eggs, if desired, \r\nand cook until almost set, about 2 minutes; then stir quickly \r\ninto sauce.  Let sauce mxture continue to simmer on low heat \r\nuntil very thick, about 4 to 5 minutes.  Toss noodles into\r\nsauce and mix well.  Remove from heat.\r\n\r\nPlace noodles and sauce on one end of a large serving plate.\r\nOn the other end, place a pile of bean sprouts and peanuts \r\nif desired.  Garnish plate with lime wedges and green onions.\r\nLet diners help themselves to noodles, bean sprouts\r\nand peanuts, and squeeze lime juice over their portions.\r\nServes 4.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai with Vegetables\r\n(Bon Appetit, February 1991)\r\n\r\n6 ounces rice sticks or rice vermicelli\r\n2 1/2 cups fresh bean sprouts\r\n1/2 cup dry roasted peanuts\r\n2 stalks fresh lemongrass or 1 Tbsp grated lemon peel\r\n7 whole fresh cilantro sprigs\r\n4 large garlic cloves\r\n1 jalapeno chili, stemmed\r\n1 medium carrot, peeled, cut into 1-inch pieces\r\n2 eggs\r\n1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n1 large zucchini, diced\r\n4 large green onions, thinly sliced\r\n1/2 tsp salt\r\n2 Tbsp plus 1 tsp sugar\r\n2 Tbsp plus 1 tsp fresh lemon juice\r\n2 Tbsp catsup\r\n1 1/2 Tbsp Thai fish sauce (nam pla)\r\nmore cilantro sprigs for garnish\r\nlemon wedges for garnish (optional)\r\n\r\nSoak rice sticks (breaking up noodles into shorter pieces) \r\nin hot water to cover until soft, about 30 minutes. \r\nDrain thoroughly.\r\n              \r\nIn food processor fitted with steel knife, coarsely chop peanuts.\r\nPut in bowl for later.\r\nTrim root end and outer leaves of lemongrass stalks, leaving\r\nabout 2 1/2 inches of small white core.  In food processor,\r\nmince cilantro sprigs, lemongrass, garlic, and jalapeno.\r\nAdd carrot pieces and process until carrots are pea size, using\r\non/off turns.  Transfer carrot mixture to bowl.  Beat eggs in processor.\r\n\r\nHeat oil in wok or heavy large skillet over high heat.  Add\r\ncarrot mixture, diced zucchini, half of green onions, and salt.\r\nStir-fry just to heat through, about 1 minute.  Add sugar, lemon\r\njuice, catsup, and fish sauce and toss to blend.  Add beaten eggs\r\nand let set partially, about 30 seconds.  Scramble eggs lightly.\r\nAdd noodles and bean sprouts and stir-fry until eggs are cooked\r\nand the mixture is well mixed and heated through.  Mix in chopped\r\npeanuts and remaining green onions.  Adjust seasonings.  Transfer\r\npad Thai to warm platter. Garnish with fresh cilantro and lemon\r\nwedges, and serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: markv@masig.fsu.edu (Mark Verschell)\r\n\r\nI\'ll try to recall my recipe from memory. It should be Vegan with the exception\r\nof the eggs, which could easily be left out. I think they add alot, but I\r\nhaven\'t tried any substitutes.\r\n\r\nPhad Thai - Vegan {opt Ovo-Lacto} \r\n---------------------------------\r\n8 oz. Firm Tofu (firmer the better)\r\n10 oz. rice sticks (Thai or chinese rice noodles ~1/8\" wide)\r\n1/4 c. Soy Sauce, light sodium works best \r\n1/4 c. white vinegar\r\n1/4 c. less 1 T. sugar\r\n1 T. paprika (for color, optional)\r\n1/4 c. vegetable oil {plus a little extra if you use eggs}\r\n2 cloves garlic, minced\r\napprox 2 c. chopped, assorted vegetables (broccoli, carrots, etc...)\r\n{2 eggs}\r\n8 oz bean sprouts\r\nground roasted unsalted peanuts and ground red pepper (for garnish)\r\nlimes cut into wedges (for garnish)\r\n\r\nPlace 3 folded paper towels on a plate, then the tofu on the towels, 3 more\r\nfolded paper towels on top of the tofu, another plate on top of that and then a\r\nheavy object on top of that. The idea is to remove any excess water from the\r\ntofu.\r\n\r\nIn lukewarm water, let the rice sticks soak for 20 minutes while you prepare the\r\nrest of the ingredients, then drain thoroughly.\r\n\r\nCut the tofu into pieces ~ 1/2\"x1\"x1/4\"\r\n\r\nMix the Soy Sauce, vinegar, sugar and paprika together. Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a pre-heated wok, add the oil. When the oil is heated add the garlic. Do not\r\nburn the garlic! When the garlic just starts to turn light brown, add the tofu.\r\nI personally like to stir fry my tofu until it turns light brown all over for\r\nextra texture, if you like you can cook the tofu until it just starts to turn\r\nbrown for a less chewy texture. \r\n\r\nAdd the chopped vegetables, stirring constantly. Cook for a couple of minutes\r\nuntil the vegetables are heated but have not lost crispiness.\r\n\r\nAdd the drained rice sticks and carefully mix them to coat with oil. Cook for\r\nabout 1 minute.\r\n\r\nAdd the Soy Sauce mixture. Bring to boil, reduce heat to medium and\r\ncontinue gently mixing until all liquid is absorbed.\r\n\r\n{On each side of the wok, carefully lift up the phad thai, add ~ 1 t. oil and\r\nthen 1 of the eggs. Break the yolk, and cover the egg with the phad thai. Cook\r\nuntil eggs have set.}\r\n\r\nAdd the bean sprouts and continue cooking while mixing them in for about 2 minutes.\r\n\r\nServe the phad thai immediately on a pre-heated platter, garnished with the\r\npeanuts and red pepper, and with lime weges for all the diners. Or, do as I do,\r\njust place a bowl of peanuts and a bowl of red peppers to allow for individual\r\ntastes.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai (from Thai Vegetarian Cooking)\r\n \r\n3 Tbsp oil\r\n1 clove garlic (minced)\r\n1/2 cup tofu (cubed)\r\n1 egg\r\n4oz Sen Lek noodles (flat rice noodles)\r\n1 Tbsp perserved turnip\r\n2 scallions (minced)\r\n2 Tbsp roast peanuts (chopped)\r\n3 oz (mung) bean sprouts\r\n1/2 tsp chili powder\r\n1 tsp sugar\r\n2 Tbsp light soy sauce\r\n1 Tbsp lemon juice\r\n \r\nStir fry items in order of list, but save 1/2 peanuts and beansprouts\r\nfor the table.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad thai\r\nFrom: rickert@cco.caltech.edu (Keith Warren Rickert)\r\n\r\n6 garlic cloves, chopped\r\n1 lb meat (shrimp, chicken, pork, beef...in usefully small bits)\r\n6 eggs.\r\n1 cup chopped green onion\r\n1 lb rice noodles\r\n1/4 cup white vinegar\r\n1 cup chopped cilantro\r\n1 cup ground roasted peanuts. (No shells of course)\r\n1/4 cup sugar\r\n2 cups beansprouts\r\n2 tblspoons chile powder, \r\n2 teaspoons chile paste.\r\nvegetable oil.\r\n8 oz. sliced salted turnip/radish.\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce.\r\n\r\nOk, make sure you have just about all of this ready before you start.\r\nFirst of all, soak the noodles in lukewarm water for 40 mins to an hour.\r\n(Good time to get some of the other ingredients ready)\r\nWhen finished soaking, just pour off the water.\r\nSoak the radish in water for a few minutes, and pour off the water\r\nand squeeze dry. repeat 2-3 times or so.\r\nHeat up your wok with a bit of vegetable oil.\r\nMix the eggs as in for scrambled eggs, and toss them in the wok.\r\nCook until they are fairly \'hard\', moving them around as need be\r\n(but as little as possible). Remove them from the wok and \r\nchop into slices, and set them aside.\r\n\r\nHeat up some more oil (maybe 1/4 cup or a bit less) and add the garlic.\r\nWhen it is getting fragrant, before it starts to turn brown, add\r\nthe meat. \r\nWhen the meat is about halfway done, add the \r\nradish. When the meat is done, add the noodles and the vinegar\r\nand cook until the noodles are soft. From here on out you have\r\nto toss it a _lot_. Tongs might be useful. Dont step away for more\r\nthan about 20-30 seconds, cause you dont want it to burn.\r\nOnce the noodles start to get soft, add the eggs, the peanuts, the fish\r\nsauce, the sugar, the chile powder, and the chile paste. Continue\r\nstirring and tossing, until the mixture is all evenly coated.\r\nThis usually takes about 5 mins or so, maybe a bit longer.\r\nRemove this mixture from the wok and to another container.\r\nStirfry the beansprouts in the wok for maybe\r\n1 minute, maybe 1.5, then add beansprouts, green onions, and\r\ncilantro to the dish, and toss. Keep some unchopped cilantro\r\nand green onions for garnishes if you want (you can take bits of\r\ngreen onion, maybe 2 inches long, and cut one end (1-1.25 inches) into very \r\nthin strips while leaving the other end intact to get a very nice \r\ngarnish).\r\n Enjoy....\r\n\r\nOne or 2 random comments:\r\nIf you use shrimp, you\'d want to add them at the same\r\ntime as the radish, as they cook quickly, and get tough\r\nif overcooked.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom _A_Touch_of_Thailand_ by Duangduen Buranasombati \r\n\r\nPAD THAI (Thai Fried Noodles)\r\n\r\n225 grams (1/2 lb) Thai rice noodles\r\n12 medium size shrimp, shelled and cleaned\r\n1 medium onion, diced\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) preserved radish, sliced (should be at Asian market)\r\n1 tsp garlic, minced\r\n1 tbsp sugar\r\n1 tbsp nam pla (fish sauce)\r\n2 tbsp ketchup\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) vegetable oil\r\n200 grams (1 cup) bean sprouts\r\n1 tbsp tamarind soaked in 3 tbsp hot water (tamarind usually comes\r\n	in a package without seeds.  Add water and squeeze the pulp\r\n	of the fruit several times to obtain the sour liquid.  Sub-\r\n	stitute the juice of 1 lime if tamarind is not available.  You\r\n	should find tamarind in a Thai, Indian, Spanish, or Asian market)\r\njuice of 1 lemon\r\n2 eggs, slightly beaten\r\n\r\nSoak the noodles in cold water to cover for about 15 minutes.  Drain. \r\nPut noodles in a bowl and pour hot water over them.  Let stand for about \r\n10 minutes.  Drain and rinse with cold water; set aside. Put oil in a wok or\r\nlarge skillet over medium heat.  Add garlic and onion.  Stir-fry\r\nuntil onion turns translucent.  Add shrimp and stir-fry for about 2-3\r\nminutes, or until shrimp turns pink.  Add ketchup, sugar, nam pla,\r\npreserved radish, tamarind juice, and lemon juice.  Stir well.  Pour\r\nin the beaten eggs and let them slightly set for 3 minutes before mixing\r\nwith remaining ingredients.  Add noodles, bean sprouts, and peanuts.\r\nMix well.  Spoon noodles onto serving platter and garnish with 1 cup\r\nbean sprouts, lemon wedges, cilantro, and peanuts.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom Vegetarian Gourmet\r\n\r\nServes 4\r\n\r\n3 cups water\r\n1 7oz package thin rice noodles\r\n2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n1 clove garlic, minced\r\n1 teaspoon minced fresh gingerroot\r\n2 cups shredded cabbage, bok choy or chinese cabbage\r\n1 cup mung bean sprouts\r\n2 scallions, finely chopped\r\n2 teaspoons low-sodium soy sauce or tamari\r\nPinch of black pepper\r\n15 sweet basil leaves\r\n1/4 cup roasted peanuts, chopped\r\n1 lime, cut into wedges\r\n\r\n1.  Bring water to a boil, add rice noodles and cook until\r\nsoft, about 4 to 5 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n2.  In a wok or large skillet, heat oil for one minute\r\nover medium-high heat.  Add garlic and ginger, and stir-fry for\r\none minute.\r\n3.  add cabbage and stir-fry until tender-crisp, about 2 to 4 minutes.\r\n4.  Add rice noodles, bean sprouts, soy sauce and black pepper.\r\nStir-fry until heated through, about 1 to 2 minutes.  Mix in\r\nbasil leaves.\r\n5.  Garnish with peanuts and lime wedges.  Serve hot.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPaad Thai\r\nFrom unidentified magazine\r\n\r\n1/2 pound dried flat rice noodles, 1/4 to 1/2 inch wide\r\n3 tablespoons fish sauce\r\n2 tablespoons rice vinegar\r\n1 1/2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n6 green onions, coarsely chopped\r\n3 cloves garlic, finely chopped\r\n16 large shrimp, shelled and deveined\r\n2 large eggs, beaten\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n1 tablespoon fresh lime juice\r\n1/3 cup roasted unsalted peanuts, finely chopped\r\nFresh sprigs coriander (optional)\r\nLime wedges (optional)\r\n\r\n1.  In large bowl, soak noodles in warm water to cover\r\n30 to 35 mintues or until softened.  (Do not soak longer as \r\nnoodles will become mushy.)  Drain noodles well in colander \r\nand set aside.  In small bowl, combine fish sauce, vinegar, \r\nsugar, and paprika; set aside.\r\n2.  In large skillet or wok, heat oil over medium heat.  Add\r\nonions and garlic and stir-fry 1 minute; add shrimp and cook,\r\nstirring, just until onions and garlic are browned and shrimp\r\nare pink and cooked through -- 3 to 4 minutes.  With slotted\r\nspoon remove shrimp mixture to colander with noodles.\r\n3.  Add eggs to skillet and cook until almost set, breaking them\r\ninto small pieces as they cook.  Reduce heat to low and add\r\nnoodles with shrimp mixture, tossing to blend.  Stir in fish\r\nsauce mixture and heat 3 to 4 minutes or until most of liquid\r\nevaporates.  Add bean sprouts and lime juice, tossing gently\r\nuntil completely combined; transfer Paad Thai to serving platter\r\nor divide among individual serving plates.  Garnish with\r\nchopped peanus and, if desired, coriander sprigs and lime\r\nwedges.  Serve immediately.\r\n\r\nCollection of pad thai recipes\r\n<P>\r\nDate:  August 5, 1994\r\n<p>\r\nChanges made in this version: \r\nAdded notes about alternatives to fish sauce\r\nAdded 7 new recipes (at end)\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nHere is a collection of pad thai recipes from various sources.\r\nI start off with some general notes and observations, based on\r\na study of the individual recipes and my own experience.\r\n<p>\r\nThis collection is maintained by Sue Stigleman.  It is posted\r\nperiodically to rec.food.recipes.  It is also available by\r\nanonymous ftp from ftp.cs.unca.edu and via the World Wide\r\nWeb at http://www.cs.unca.edu/~stigle/padthai.html.\r\n<p>\r\nSend comments, corrections, or additions to stigle@cs.unca.edu.\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nNotes:\r\n<p>\r\nRice Noodles:  you can use fresh or dried, in widths from 1/8 to 1/2\r\ninch wide.  I\'ve never tried fresh.  The dried ones have to be\r\nsoaked in water to soften them. The recipes call for soaking \r\nin cold water, lukewarm water, hot water, and boiling water for\r\nanywhere from 7 minutes to 2 hours.  I put mine into warm tap\r\nwater and let them soak while I\'m preparing everything else.\r\nJust before I start cooking, I dump them into a colander to \r\ndrain.  One recipe suggests cellophane noodles as an alternative\r\nto rice noodles -- I\'ve never tried that variation.\r\n<p>\r\nMeat or No Meat:  The most common meat called for is shrimp, with \r\nchicken and/or pork use in addition to or in place of the shrimp.\r\nSome recipes add bean curd; some substitute it for the meat.\r\nJeff Smith\'s recipe uses deep fried bean curd.  My own variation\r\nis to substitute various veggies (asparagus, red bell pepper,\r\nbroccoli, snow peas, or whatever else looks good.)  As Nancie\r\nMcDermott says, \"Thai cooks blithely tinker with the classic\r\nformula to create signature variations, and you can, too.\"\r\n<p>\r\nOil and Seasonings:  Cooking pad thai starts with vegetable or\r\npeanut oil.  Most versions add garlic, and sometimes shallots,\r\nshrimp paste (be prepared for the smell!), onions, fresh red\r\nchilies, and/or preserved sweet white radish.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Sauce:  What makes pad thai, in addition to the rice noodles,\r\nis the sauce.  The general mix of flavors is sweet, salty, sour,\r\nand hot.  Typical ingredients are:\r\n<p>\r\n	fish sauce (sometimes soy sauce is used in addition, or in\r\n		place of for pure vegetarian versions)\r\n<p>\r\n	sugar (sometimes palm sugar is suggested)\r\n<p>\r\n	vinegar (various kinds specified; tamarind sauce or lime\r\n		juice are sometimes used instead)\r\n<p>\r\n	\"red stuff\" -- may be paprika, tomato paste, catsup,\r\n		chili powder, hot chili sauce, chili paste with\r\n		garlic, tomato sauce, or cayenne pepper, depending\r\n		on the recipe.\r\n<p>\r\nOther possible additions: salt, black pepper, chicken stock, dried\r\nshrimp powder.  One recipe calls for boiling the sauce before using.\r\n<p>\r\nNotes on substitutes for fish sauce:\r\n<p>From Sundays at Moosewood:\r\nPrepare a strong vegetable stock, strong on the onions and black\r\npeppercorns, and use 2 tbsp of this (with an extra 1/2 to 1 tsp salt)\r\nto substitute fo 2 tbsp of fish sauce in recipes.\r\n<p>\r\nFrom Jonathan Kandell: \r\nfor 1/4 C fish sauce, use:\r\n1/4 C soy sauce plus a little water\r\nwith 1T miso with 2T fermented black beans crushed, marinated a while.\r\nexperiment with the proportions.\r\n<p>\r\nEggs:  anywhere from 0-6.  Some recipes call for beating the\r\neggs before adding; others suggested breaking the yolk after\r\nadding the egg to the pan.  Various techniques are suggested\r\nfor manipulating the egg while cooking.  One recipe calls\r\nfor cooking the egg before starting the pad thai, cutting\r\nit into strips, and then adding the egg strips back at the\r\nend of cooking.  I haven\'t tried this myself but have had\r\nit in restaurants.\r\n<p>\r\nBean Sprouts and Scallions:  These are usually added last in\r\ncooking, or added to the finished dish without cooking.\r\n<p>\r\nGarnishes:  various things can be added to finished dish as\r\nan edible garnish:\r\n	lime or lemon wedges\r\n	ground roasted chilies\r\n	ground roasted peanuts\r\n	dried red chili flakes\r\n	fresh coriander leaves\r\n	cucumber slices\r\n	dried shrimps\r\n	basil leaves\r\n	fried basil leaves\r\n	cherry tomatoes\r\n	mint sprigs\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nExperiment, and enjoy!\r\n--------------------------------------------------\r\n<p>\r\nThai Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom Thai Home-Cooking\r\n\r\n1/2 lb dried rice noodles 1/8 inch wide\r\nwarm water\r\n\r\n1/2 lb shrimp, chicken, pork, or a combination\r\n\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup + 2 tablespoons granulated sugar\r\n1/4 cup + 2 tablespoons white vinegar\r\n1 teaspoon paprika or 1 tablespoon tomato paste \r\n  or 1 tablespoon catsup (all are optional)\r\n\r\n4 green onions\r\n\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil\r\n1 teaspoon (2 cloves) finely chopped garlic\r\n\r\n2 eggs\r\n\r\n3/4 lb bean sprouts\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons ground roasted chilies\r\n3/4 cup ground roasted unsalted peanuts\r\n\r\nlime wedges\r\n\r\n1.  Soak the noodles for 20-25 minutes in enough warm water \r\nto cover them.  They should be flexible and soft, but not \r\nso soft that they can be mashed easily with the fingers.  \r\nLater cooking in liquid will soften them more.  Drain \r\nthem thoroughly in a colander while preparing the other \r\ningredients.  Traditionally they are left in full-length \r\nstrands, but you may cut them into 8-inch lengths if you \r\nfind it easier to stir-fry them that way.\r\n\r\n2.  Peel and devein the shrimp, leaving the tails intact, \r\nor slice the chicken or pork across the grain into strips \r\nnot more than 1/8 inch thick and 1 to 2 inches long.\r\n\r\n3.  Mix the fish sauce, sugar, vinegar, and optional paprika, \r\ntomato paste, or catsup in a bowl, and stir until the sugar \r\nis dissolved.  Set the mixture aside.  Slice the green onions, \r\nboth white and green parts, diagonally into pieces 1 1/2 inches \r\nlong and 1/4 inch thick.  Set aside.\r\n\r\n4.  Heat a wok, add the oil, and swirl it over the surface of \r\nthe pan.  Add the garlic and stir-fry until light golden.  \r\nAdd the meat and stir-fry until the pink color disappears \r\ncompletely.  If you are using shrimp, stir-fry until they turn \r\npink.  Add the noodles and toss lightly to coat them with oil \r\nand to distribute the meat and garlic.\r\n\r\n5.  Add the liquid from Step 3 and bring it to a boil rapidly, \r\ngently folding the noodles without breaking them.  Reduce the \r\nheat to medium and boil the mixture, folding frequently, until \r\nthe noodles have absorbed the liquid.\r\n\r\n6.  Using a wok scoop or a stiff spatula, lift the noodles \r\ngently from one side of the wok.  Pour a little oil along \r\nthe side of the wok, then break 1 egg and slip it into the oil.  \r\nBreak the yolk, and cover the egg with the noodles immediately.  \r\nRepeat this on the opposite side of the wok with the remaining \r\negg.  Allow the eggs to cook undisturbed, over moderate heat, \r\nuntil they are set and almost dry.  Additional oil may be added \r\nif the eggs or the noodles begin to stick to the wok.\r\n\r\n7.  When the eggs are set and almost dry, fold them gently but \r\nrapidly into the noodles.  Try not to break the noodles, which \r\nwill be soft and fragile at this point.  An effective way is to \r\ninsert the scoop under the eggs, lift it through, and fold the \r\nmixture over.  Continue the lifting and folding motion until \r\nthe eggs are broken up and well distributed.\r\n\r\n8.  Add the bean sprouts and sliced green onions and toss the \r\nentire mixture quickly and gently, still avoiding breaking the \r\nnoodles.  Cook for about 2 minutes, or until the bean sprouts \r\nand green onions are crisp-tender.\r\n\r\n9.  Place the mixture on a large warm platter.  Sprinkle the \r\nground chilies and peanuts over the top and squeeze lime juice \r\nover that, or serve these garnishes separately, for each diner \r\nto add according to taste.\r\n\r\nVariation:  Omit the shrimp, pork, or chicken from the list of \r\ningredients, and ignore any instructions for them.  Substitute \r\n1/2 pound tofu and 1/4 pound dried shrimp.  Put the tofu on a \r\ntriple layer of paper towels, cover it with another triple layer, \r\nput a plate on top of that, and put a 2-pound weight, e.g. a can \r\nof tomatoes, on top of the plate.  Let stand for 20 to 30 minutes \r\nto press out the excess water.  Put the dried shrimp in a sieve, \r\nrinse them quickly under hot running water, and set them aside \r\nto drain.  After the tofu has been pressed, slice it into 1/4-inch \r\ncubes.  Add the tofu and shrimp in Step 5 of the instructions and \r\nproceed with the main recipe.  Note: in Thailand, dried shrimp \r\nare available in a smaller size than is generally available in \r\nthe United States.  If you would like to simulate that, chop \r\nthe dried shrimp very coarsely after they have been rinsed.\r\n\r\nYield:  6-8 servings\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom The Southeast Asia Cookbook, by Ruth Law\r\n\r\n1/2 lb fresh rice noodles, cut into 1/2-inch slices, \r\n  or flat dried rice noodles\r\n\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n\r\n1/3 cup oil\r\n1 tablespoon minced garlic\r\n4 tablespoons minced shallots\r\n\r\n2 teaspoons shrimp paste (kapee)\r\n1 tablespoon chopped dried shrimp (optional)\r\n\r\n10 medium shrimp, shelled, deveined (or substitute pork)\r\n\r\n3 tablespoons fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n1 tablespoon rice vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons light brown sugar\r\n2 tablespoons tomato ketchup\r\n1/2 to 1 teaspoon chili powder (optional)\r\n\r\n2 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n\r\nGarnishes:\r\n1/3 cup coarsely ground unsalted peanuts\r\n1/2 teaspoon dried red chili flakes (optional)\r\n2 green onions, finely sliced\r\n2 tablespoons chopped coriander leaves\r\n2 limes, cut into wedges\r\n1 small cucumber, sliced.\r\n\r\nIf using dried rice noodles, soak in hot water for 20 minutes\r\nbefore cooking.  Drain.  In 4 quarts boiling water, cook fresh \r\nrice noodles 2 to 3 minutes or until just tender to the bite, al \r\ndente.  Drain.  Rinse.  Drain for 30 minutes or until dry.  In\r\nboiling water, blanch the bean sprouts for 30 seconds.  Refresh under\r\ncold water.  Drain.\r\n\r\nHeat oil.  Fry garlic and shallots until golden.  Add the shrimp\r\npaste and dried shrimp.  Stir.  Add the shrimp and stir-fry\r\nuntil done.  Add the fish sauce, vinegar, sugar, ketchup, and chili\r\npowder.  Stir until sugar dissolves.  Add the beaten eggs and let\r\nthem set slightly.  Then stir to scramble.  Add the noodles and\r\ntoss for about 2 minutes.  Place the Pad Thai on a platter.  Sprinkle\r\nthe noodles with peanuts, chili flakes, green onions, and coriander.\r\nArrange lime wedges around the edge of the platter.  Serve\r\nwith a side dish of fresh bean sprouts and cucumbers.\r\n\r\nYield:  3-4 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Rice Noodles \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n4 oz dried rice noodles, medium size\r\n1/4 cup vegetable oil\r\n2 cloves finely minced garlic\r\n3 tablespoons chopped raw shrimp\r\n3 tablespoons lean ground pork\r\n1 teaspoon sugar\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n1 teaspoon soy sauce\r\n2 teaspoons hot chili sauce\r\n2 beaten eggs\r\n2 tablespoon tamarind sauce\r\n1/4 cup bean sprouts\r\n1 sliced scallion\r\n1 tablespoon salty preserved radish\r\n2 tablespoon coarsely ground salted or unsalted peanuts\r\n2 tablespoon sliced green onions\r\n2 wedges lemon\r\n1 cucumber\r\n\r\n1.  Soak noodles in hot water for 20 minutes, or until soft.  Drain.\r\n\r\n2.  Heat oil in a wok or large skillet and saute garlic until \r\ngolden.  Add the shrimp and ground pork and stir-fry until \r\nlightly browned.  Add the sugar, fish sauce, soy sauce, and \r\nchili sauce and stir-fry until the sugar dissolves.  Pour \r\nin the beaten eggs, let them set slightly, then stir to scramble.\r\n\r\n3.  Add the noodles and stir-fry about 2 minutes.  Push noodles \r\nto side of wok or skillet.  Add the tamarind sauce and cook \r\nfor 1 minute.  Add the bean sprouts, scallions, radish, and \r\nstir-fry until bean sprouts are slightly cooked, about 1 minute.  \r\nStir noodles down into tamarind mixture and stir-fry until well-mixed.\r\n\r\n4.  Pile noodles on a serving dish and sprinkle with peanuts and \r\nscallions.  Seed cucumber and slice lengthwise into 4 pieces.  \r\nPlace lemon and cucumber wedges on side of plate.\r\n\r\nServe 2\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nStir-Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n1 package (16 oz) flat rice stick noodles\r\n4 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 cloves garlic, crushed\r\n1 yellow onion, chopped\r\n1 1/2 pounds pork loin, cut into julienne strips\r\n1/2 cup dried shrimp, soaked in 1/2 cup of warm water for 5 minutes\r\n2 fresh red chilies, cut into julienne strips\r\n1 teaspoon white sugar\r\n1/8 cup fish sauce\r\n1 teaspoon salt\r\n2 tablespoons lime juice\r\n3 tablespoons ketchup\r\n1 teaspoon ground black pepper\r\n1 pound fresh bean sprouts, washed, drained\r\n4 tablespoons green onion, chopped\r\n4 tablespoons fresh coriander, chopped\r\n4 tablespoons roasted peanuts, crushed\r\n\r\nIn a pot of boiling water, drop in the flat rice stick noodles\r\nfor about 1 minute, stirring constantly to prevent noodles \r\nfrom sticking together.  Remove from heat and drain in a colander.  \r\nRinse with cold water.  Set aside.  Heat the oil in a large wok \r\nand stir-fry the garlic and onion until golden brown.  Add the \r\npork, shrimp in water, and chilies.  Stir-fry for 5 minutes and \r\nadd the sugar, fish sauce, salt, lime juice, ketchup, black pepper, \r\nnoodles, and bean sprouts.  Stir well for 2 minutes.  Transfer \r\nto a large serving platter and garnish with green onion, fresh \r\ncoriander, and peanuts.\r\n\r\nServes 6-8\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nKwitiaow Pad Thai \r\n\r\nSource:  unknown\r\n\r\n1/2 cup peanut or corn oil\r\n1 oz raw prawns, shelled\r\n4 oz firm bean curd (tofu), diced\r\n3 tablespoons preserved sweet white radish, chopped\r\n3 tablespoons sliced shallots\r\n4 eggs\r\n11 oz rice or cellophane noodles (sen kel or woon sen), \r\n  soaked in cold water for 7-10 minutes, if dried\r\n1/4 cup chicken stock\r\n3 tablespoons dried shrimps, chopped\r\n1/3 cup unsalted peanuts, chopped\r\n4 spring onions, sliced\r\n15 oz bean sprouts\r\n\r\nsauce:\r\n1 cup water\r\n1/2 cup tamarind juice\r\n1/3 cup palm sugar\r\n1 tablespoon white soya sauce\r\n\r\n\r\nMix all the sauce ingredients together in a pan and boil \r\nuntil reduced to about 2/3 cup. Set aside to cool.\r\n\r\nHeat the oil in a wok or pan until very hot, then add \r\nthe prawns and bean curd and stir-fry lightly for 1 minute.  \r\nAdd the preserved radish and shallot, fry for 1 minute, and \r\nbreak in the eggs.  Stir-fry for a minute, then add the noodles \r\nand chicken stock.  When the noodles are soft (about 2 minutes), \r\nadd the dried shrimps, peanuts, spring onions and bean sprouts.  \r\nAdd the sauce, fry for a couple of minutes and serve.\r\n\r\nServe accompanied by chopped peanuts, chopped dry chillies, \r\nsugar, lime wedges, spring onions, and fresh bean sprouts, \r\nall in small containers.\r\n\r\nServes 6\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles  \r\n\r\nFrom James McNair\'s Pasta Cookbook\r\n\r\nAsian rice noodles, cut about 1/8 inch wide, \r\n  or 1 pound fresh or dried flat rice noodles\r\n3/4 cup fish sauce or 6 tablespoons soy sauce\r\n4 teaspoons rice wine vinegar or distilled white vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons sugar\r\n4 teaspoons high-quality paprika, or 1/4 cup catsup or tomato paste\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil, or more if needed\r\n8 oz boneless pork or boned and skinned chicken, cut into very small pieces\r\n2 tablespoons minced or pressed garlic\r\n2 teaspoons ground dried red hot chili, \r\n  or 1 tablespoon minced fresh hot chile\r\n4 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n8 oz medium-sized shrimp, shelled and deveined, tails left intact\r\n10 oz fresh bean sprouts\r\n3 green onions, thinly sliced\r\n1/2 cup chopped unsalted dry-roasted peanuts\r\n1/4 cup chopped fresh cilantro (coriander)\r\nfinely minced dried shrimp for garnish\r\nfresh cilantro sprigs for garnish\r\nlemon or lime wedges for garnish\r\n\r\nIn a bowl, cover the noodles in lukewarm water and let stand \r\nto soften, about 15 minutes for fresh noodles or about \r\n30 minutes for dry noodles.  Drain and cut into 6-inch \r\nlengths, if desired.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a small bowl, combine the fish sauce, vinegar, sugar, \r\nand paprika, catsup, or tomato paste.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat a wok or saute pan over high heat.  Add the oil and \r\nswirl to coat the pan.  Add the pork or chicken, garlic, \r\nand chili and stir-fry for 1 minute.  Stir in the drained \r\nnoodles and the reserved fish sauce mixture and stir-fry \r\nabout 30 seconds.  Push the noodles to one side, pour in \r\nabout a tablespoon more oil, if necessary, and add the eggs; \r\ncook just until slightly set, then break them up.  Add the \r\nshrimp and stir-fry just until they turn pink.  Add most \r\nof the bean sprouts, the green onion, and 1/4 cup of the \r\npeanuts and stir-fry until the sprouts and onions are \r\ncrisp-tender, 1 to 2 minutes.  Remove from the heat and \r\ntransfer to a serving plate.\r\n\r\nSprinkle with the chopped cilantro, remaining 1/4 cup peanuts, \r\nand dried shrimp.  Garnish with the remaining bean sprouts, \r\ncilantro sprigs, and lemon or lime wedges, and serve immediately.  \r\nDiners squeeze lemon or lime juice to taste.\r\n\r\nServes 8 as a pasta course, or 4 as a main course\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai\r\n\r\nfrom Frugal Gourmet on Our Immigrant Ancestors\r\n\r\n1/2 pound thai flat rice noodles (bahn pho) or rice sticks\r\n\r\noil for deep-frying\r\n1/2 pound fresh firm bean curd, cut into tiny cubes\r\n\r\n1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n1/2 tablespoon garlic, chopped\r\n1/2 cup very thinly sliced skinless and boneless chicken breast\r\n\r\n1/4 pound shrimp, peeled and cut in half the long way\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon dried shrimp powder\r\n1/4 teaspoon freshly ground black pepper\r\n3 tablespoons finely chopped dry-roasted salted peanuts\r\n2 tablespoons lime juice, freshly squeezed\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n6 tablespoons Thai fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup tamarind sauce\r\n2 teaspoons red chili paste with garlic\r\n2 cups fresh bean sprouts\r\n\r\n2 limes, quartered\r\n1/3 cup fresh coriander leaves\r\n3 chopped scallions\r\n4 tablespoons finely chopped dry-roasted peanuts\r\n\r\nSoak the noodles in ample warm water until supple, \r\nabout 15 minutes, drain and set aside.  Place the \r\nnoodles in boiling water and cook just until the \r\nwater returns to the boil.  Drain again.  \r\n\r\nHeat the oil for deep-frying to 375 degrees and \r\ndeep-fry the cut bean curd.  Be sure the pat the \r\nbean curd dry on a paper towel first so that it\r\nwill not spatter fat on you.  Drain the bean curd\r\nand set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat a large wok and add the peanut oil, garlic, and \r\nchicken.  Chow for a few minues and then add the \r\nshrimp, drained noodles, beaten eggs, and \r\ndeep-fried bean curd.  Toss well and chow for 3 to 4 \r\nminutes over medium-high heat.  Add the remaining\r\ningredients, except the garnishes, and chow or stir for\r\na few more minutes until the noodles are hot and \r\ntender.\r\n\r\nPlace on a serving platter with the garnishes, which are\r\nan integral part of the dish.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy Pad Thai \r\n\r\nFrom: Asian Pasta, by Linda Burum\r\n\r\n3 1/2 tablespoon distilled white vinegar\r\n2 tablespoons water\r\n2 1/2 tablespoons fish sauce\r\n3 tablespoons tomato paste\r\n2 1/2 tablespoons sugar\r\n1/2 tablespoon dried shrimp, pounded to a powder\r\n9 oz flat rice sticks, 1/8 inch wide\r\nvegetable oil\r\n1/3 cup fresh thai or purple or sweet basil leaves\r\n2 red Serrano chili peppers, seeded and very finely minced\r\n4 cloves garlic, minced\r\n1 1/2 large boned chicken breast halves, \r\n  cut crosswise into 3/8 inch thick strips,\r\n  or 1 pound lean pork, cut into thin slices 3/8 inch by 2 inches\r\n8 oz small, cooked shelled shrimp\r\n2 eggs, lightly beaten\r\n2 cups fresh bean sprouts, beans removed\r\n1/4 cup roasted, unsalted peanuts, coarsely ground\r\ncherry tomatoes, halved\r\nlime wedges\r\nmint sprigs or sliced green onions\r\n\r\nCombine the vinegar, water, fish sauce, tomato paste, sugar, \r\nand dried shrimp in a small bowl; mix until well blended and \r\nreserve.  In a large pot, soak the noodles in enough water to \r\ncover.  In a small skillet or pot, heat vegetable oil 3/4 to \r\n1 inch deep to 350 degrees, or until a dried rice noodle puffs \r\ninstantly when dropped into the oil.  Deep-fry the basil leaves \r\na few at a time, turning them once or twice until they are crisp, \r\nor 40 seconds to a minute; drain on paper toweling.\r\n\r\nBring the noodles to a boil and cook them 2 minutes, or until \r\nthey are almost tender.  Drain and rinse them well, then spread \r\nthem on paper toweling to dry slightly.  Head a wok or large \r\nskillet and add about 2 1/2 tablespoons vegetable oil.  Fry \r\nthe Serrano peppers about 30 seconds, then add the garlic and \r\nstir-fry until it is soft.  Add the chicken or pork and stir-fry \r\nuntil the chicken is almost opaque throughout or the pork is \r\nbrowned.  Stir in the shrimp and the sauce and mix completely.  \r\nMake a well in the center of the mixture and pour in the eggs.  \r\nWhen they are almost set, scramble them evenly.  Add half the \r\nnoodles, throughly incorporating them into the mixture; stir \r\nin the remaining noodles and half the bean sprouts.  Cook \r\njust until the bean sprouts are nearly wilted.\r\n\r\nHeap the meat and noodles onto a platter.  Cover one half of \r\nthem with ground peanuts and the other half with uncooked \r\nbean sprouts.  Ring the noodles with lime wedges, cherry \r\ntomatoes, and mint sprigs and garnish the top with the fried basil.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom \"The Original Thai Cookbook,\" by Jennifer Brennan:\r\n\r\n(posted to rec.food.cooking earlier)\r\n  \r\n  1/2     cup          vegetable oil\r\n  6       cloves       garlic, finely chopped\r\n  1       cup small    cooked shrimp\r\n  1       Tablespoon   granulated sugar\r\n  3       Tablespoons  fish sauce (Nam Pla)\r\n  1 1/2   Tablespoons  tomato ketchup\r\n  2                    eggs, beaten\r\n  3/4     pound        (approximately 3 hanks) rice vermicelli\r\n                       (Sen Mee), soaked in hot water for 15 minutes\r\n                       and drained.\r\n  1       cup          bean sprouts\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Garnish:\r\n  \r\n  1      Tablespoon    dried shrimp powder\r\n  2      Tablespoons   peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n  1/2    teaspoon      dried red chilli [sic] flakes\r\n  2                    green onions, finely chopped\r\n  2      Tablespoons   coriander leaves, chopped\r\n  2                    limes, sliced into 1/8\" circles\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Heat the oil in a wok and fry the garlic until golden.\r\nQuickly add the shrimp and stir-fry until heated through.\r\nAdd the sugar, fish sauce and ketchup and stir until sugar\r\ndissolves.  Add the beaten eggs, letting them set slightly,\r\nthen stir to scramble.  Add the noodles and toss and stir\r\nfor about 2 minutes.  Reserving about 4 Tablespoons of the\r\nbean sprouts, add the remainder to the wok.  Stir over heat\r\nuntil the bean sprouts are barely cooked.  Turn the Pud Thai\r\nonto a platter, placing the reserved, raw bean sprouts on one side.\r\n  \r\n  \r\n     Presentation:\r\n  \r\nSprinkle the noodles with the garnish ingredients in the\r\nfollowing order: shrimp powder, peanuts, chilli flakes, green\r\nonions, coriander leaves.  Ring the platter with the lime slices\r\nand serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI (Serves 2)\r\n===================\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n1/4-1/3 pound flat rice stick noodles (banh pho), 1/4 \" wide\r\n      1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n    1/4 pound pork, cut into matchstick strips\r\n            6 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n        1 tsp crushed garlic\r\n            1 egg\r\n       2 Tbsp water\r\n       2 Tbsp rice vinegar\r\n       1 Tbsp fish sauce\r\n       1 Tbsp sugar\r\n      1/4 cup chopped toasted peanuts\r\n      1/4 tsp ground dry shrimp\r\n              freshly ground white pepper\r\n      1/4 tsp Asian chili powder (to taste)\r\n        1 cup bean sprouts, washed and drained\r\n      1/4 cup scallions, cut 3/4\" long\r\n              fresh coriander\r\n              wedges of fresh lime\r\n\r\nSoak noodles in warm water for 60 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nPrepare all other ingredients and arrange near the wok.  You will need\r\nto work fast.\r\n\r\nIn the wok, fry the pork in the peanut oil at medium heat.  When half \r\ncooked, add the shrimps and garlic and stir.  Cook until shrimp and \r\npork are done.\r\n\r\nBeat the egg and add it to the mixture.  Cook, stirring, for about \r\nhalf a minute.\r\n\r\nTurn the heat to high.  Add the drained noodles to shrimp mixture.\r\nAdd water, vinegar, fish sauce, sugar, shrimp powder, and most of \r\nthe peanuts.  Sprinkle in white pepper and chili powder.  Toss to \r\ncombine.  Let it cook on one side (Don\'t stir; keep checking the \r\nunderside as if it were a big pancake).  Flip it over as best you can\r\nand repeat until nearly cooked, about 5 to 10 minutes.\r\n\r\nAdd most of the sprouts and scallions.  Stir and cook for another \r\nminute.\r\n\r\nTurn onto a plate.  Top with the rest of the sprouts, scallions, and\r\npeanuts.  Garnish with coriander and serve with a wedge of fresh lemon.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai with Shrimp\r\n--------------------\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n\r\n8 oz.		small size rice noodles\r\n3 T		tomato sauce or tamarind paste\r\n2 T		veg. oil\r\n1 T		pickled radish\r\n3 T		sugar\r\n1/3 c		water or chicken stock\r\n1 		egg\r\n3 T		fish sauce\r\n1/2 lb		shrimp, cleaned and shelled\r\n1 handful	bean sprouts (fresh); chopped once or twice\r\n2 oz		green onions, cut into 1/2 inch pieces\r\n2 T		finely chopped peanuts\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Soak the rice noodles in cold tap water about 20 minutes, until \r\n   they are \"springy\".  Then drain in a colander until needed.\r\n\r\n2. If using dried tamarind, soak the tamarind in hot water for awhile,\r\n   then mash with a fork to soften.  Force as much of the mixture as \r\n   you can through a seive to remove bits of bark, etc.\r\n\r\n3. Heat oil in wok, and add the tamarind/tomato sauce, picked radish,\r\n   and sugar.  Mix well and let heat up.\r\n\r\n4. Add the noodles, small portions at a time, and add water/stock.\r\n   Mix well until all the noodles are coated with the mixture.  Add more\r\n   liquid if necessary -- it will cook out.  Don\'t be easy on the \r\n   noodles -- chop them with the spatula or spoon some to separate them.\r\n   It may help to \"toss\" the noodles like a salad, to get them coated.\r\n\r\n5. Beat the egg and mix with the noodles.  Add the fish sauce and shrimp.\r\n   Mix everything thoroughly.  The noodles will tend to \"clump\", so stir\r\n   or \"toss\" like a salad to get everything mixed, and to ensure that the\r\n   egg and shrimp cook thoroughly.  It will help to cover the wok with\r\n   a lid for a minute or so, then toss the mixture, then cover again.\r\n   You\'ll know it\'s done when the shrimp are completely pink.  There may\r\n   be a little browning of the noodles; stirring will keep them from\r\n   burning.\r\n\r\n6. Add the bean sprouts, green onions, and chopped peanuts.  Mix well,\r\n   then turn off the heat and let stand a minute or so.  Serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI\r\n\r\nposted on rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\nThis recipe is my absolute favorite.  It tastes just\r\nlike the pad thai served at Thai Palace in San Francisco on\r\nNoe and 15th.\r\n\r\nServes ten as a side dish.  Serves six as an only dish.\r\n\r\n1    16-ounce package chantaboon rice sticks, medium thread\r\n     (I think the spelling of \"chantaboon\" can also be found\r\n      as \"jantaboon\" or something like that.)\r\n1    tablespoon vegetable oil\r\n6    eggs beaten\r\n\r\n1/4  cup vegetable oil\r\n8    garlic gloves\r\n1    pound pork, beef or chicken, sliced thin, bite sized,\r\n     or shrimp, shelled and deveined.\r\n     (I used tofu when I made this. If you do the\r\n     the same, press the tofu between three sheets\r\n     of towel paper on the top and the bottom. Put a        \r\n     plate on top and a two pound weight on top of the\r\n     plate. Wait 20 minutes.)\r\n\r\n1/4  cup white vinegar\r\n\r\n1/4  cup sugar\r\n  1  cup sliced salted radish (chai Po)\r\n     (These are packaged in a plastic see through flat\r\n     container and slightly orange in color)\r\n1/4  cup fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n  1  cup coarse ground roasted peanuts\r\n  2  tablespoons chile powder or paprika\r\n     (I used paprika)\r\n\r\n  2  cups bean sprouts\r\n     (These I served on the side cold)\r\n  1  cup sliced green onion\r\n  1  cup sliced cilantro\r\n  1  lime\r\n\r\nSoak rice sticks in lukewarm water for 1 hour, drain and set\r\naside.  Set wok over high heat, for 1 minute.  Heat wok with\r\n1 tablespoon of oil until sizzling hot and coat sides of wok\r\nevenly.  Add eggs and fry, until eggs set, turn over and\r\nfry, until light brown on both sides.  Remove from wok and\r\nslice thin, bite size.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nHeat 1/4 cup of oil in wok until sizzling hot.  Add garlic\r\nand cook until fragrant.  add meat, stir and cook, until\r\nmeat or tofu is done, about 1 to 2 minutes.  Add rice sticks\r\nand vinegar, cook until rice sticks soften.  Add eggs, and\r\nthe next 5 ingredients, stir to blend.  Remove to serving\r\nplate.  Serve bean sprouts  cold on the side. Garnish  with\r\ngreen onion and cilantro.\r\n\r\nServe with slices of fresh lime. Squeeze lime on pad thai.\r\n\r\nEnjoy!\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nStir-fried Rice Noodles \r\n\r\nFrom:  Cooking with Bon Appetit:  Oriental Favorites\r\n\r\n8 oz (1/8 inch wide) rice noodles\r\n1 whole chicken breast, boned, skinned\r\n8 medium-size shrimp, shelled, deveined\r\n1/2 cup water\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce\r\n3 tablespoons sugar\r\n1 tablespoon lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon paprika\r\n1/8 teaspoon red (cayenne) pepper\r\n1/2 pound bean sprouts\r\n3 green onions, white part only, cut into 1 inch shreds\r\n3 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 large garlic cloves, finely chopped\r\n1 egg\r\n4 tablespoons finely crushed roasted peanuts\r\n\r\nPlace rice noodles in a large bowl.  Cover with water; soak \r\n45 minutes.  Cut chicken into 1 1/2\" by 1/3\" strips.  Cut \r\nshrimp in half lengthwise; set aside.  Combine water, fish \r\nsauce, sugar, lime juice, paprika, and red pepper in a small \r\nbowl; set aside.  Reserve 1/4 of bean sprouts for topping; \r\ncombine remaining bean sprouts and green onions.  Drain noodles.  \r\nHeat a wok over medium-high heat.  Add oil and heat.  Add \r\ngarlic; fry until garlic starts to brown.  Increase heat.  \r\nAdd chicken; stir-fry until almost cooked, about 2 minutes.  \r\nPush chicken to one side.  Break egg into wok. Stir quickly \r\nto break up yolk and scramble egg.  When egg is set, mix \r\nwith chicken.  Add drained noodles, shrimp, fish-sauce mixture \r\nand 3 tablespoons peanuts.  Cook and stir over high heat \r\n2 to 3 minutes or until noodles are soft and most of liquid \r\nis absorbed.  Add green-onion mixture; cook, stirring, 1 \r\nmore minute.  Spoon onto a heated platter.  Sprinkle with \r\nreserved bean sprouts, then with remaining peanuts.\r\n\r\nServes 6\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai (Sauteed Rice Noodles) \r\n\r\nfrom rec.food.cooking\r\n\r\n1 16 oz package rice noodles\r\n1/2 cup vegetable oil\r\n5 or 6 garlic cloves, finely chopped\r\n1 pound medium shrimp, shelled and deveined\r\n2 firm-style bean curd squares, cut into 1/2 inch cubes\r\n1/4 cup coarsely chopped pickled turnips\r\n1/2 cup white vinegar\r\n1/5 cup fish sauce\r\n1 tablespoon paprika\r\n1/4 cup sugar\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n1/4 pound mung bean sprouts\r\n3 scallions, cut into 1/2 inch pieces\r\n1/2 cup ground unsalted peanuts\r\n1 fresh red chili pepper, seeded and coarsely chopped\r\n1 lemon, cut into wedges\r\ncoriander leaves for garnish\r\n1/4 cup crushed red pepper (optional)\r\n\r\nIn a large bowl, soak the rice noodles in 10 to 12 cups of \r\ncold water for 2 hours.  Drain and cover with a damp towel \r\nto retain moisture.\r\n\r\nIn a wok or large frying pan, heat the oil and stir-fry the \r\ngarlic until it is light brown.  Add the shrimp, bean curd \r\nand pickled turnips; stir in the vinegar, fish sauce, paprika \r\nand sugar.  When thoroughly mixed, fold in the noodles.  \r\nWhen the noodles are completely coated, spread them out to \r\nthe sides of the wok or frying pan, leaving a space in the middle.  \r\nAdd the beaten eggs.  As the eggs cook, fold the noodles over them \r\nand stir to combine all of the ingredients evenly.  Stir in half \r\nof the bean sprouts, then add the scallions, ground peanuts and \r\nchopped chili pepper. Toss several times to mix well.\r\n\r\nServe on a large platter with lemon wedges.  Top with the \r\nremaining bean sprouts and garnish with coriander leaves. \r\nServe the crushed red pepper on the side, for those who \r\nlike it extra-spicy.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPaht Thai  \r\n\r\nFrom Real Thai, by Nancie McDermott; Chronicle Books;\r\nISBN 0-8118-0017-2\r\n\r\nPaht Thai is a noodle dish almost everyone seems to like.  \r\nA tangle of slender rice noodles is sauteed with garlic, \r\nshallots, and an orchestra of sweet, sour, and salty \r\ningredients that play a piquant symphony of Thai flavors.  \r\nA handful of fresh bean sprouts provides a cooling contrast \r\nto the hot, seasoned noodles, and circles of lime invite you \r\nto bring sourness to center stage as you begin to eat.\r\n\r\nTraditional ingredients are salty dried shrimp; crispy pieces \r\nof fried, pressed bean curd; sweet-sour nuggets of pickled \r\nwhite radish; chopped peanuts; flat, green garlic chives; \r\nand a balanced chorus -- sweet, sour, salty, hot -- of palm \r\nsugar, tamarind, vinegar, lime, brown bean sauce, and crushed \r\ndried red chilies.\r\n\r\nThai cooks blithely tinker with the classic formula to create \r\nsignature versions, and you can, too.  Siriluk Williams, owner \r\nof Sukothai Restaurant in Ft. Lauderdale, Florida, gave me her \r\nrecipe for home-style paht Thai.  I love its accessible ingredients, \r\nsimple steps, and delicious results.\r\n\r\n1/4 pound dried rice stick noodles\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n1 tablespoon coarsely chopped garlic\r\n8 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n\r\n1 egg, lightly beaten\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n2 teaspoons sugar\r\n2 tablespoons coarsely chopped, dry-roasted peanuts\r\n\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n4 slender green onions, sliced in 1 inch lengths\r\n1 lime, quartered lengthwise\r\n\r\nSoak rice noodles in warm water to cover for 15 to 20 minutes.  \r\nMeanwhile, prepare all the remaining ingredients and place them \r\nnext to the stove, along with a small serving platter.  When \r\nthe noodles are very limp and white, drain and measure out \r\n2 1/2 cups.  Set these by the stove as well.\r\n\r\nHeat a wok or large, deep skillet over medium-high heat.  Add \r\n1 tablespoon of the oil and swirl to coat the surface.  When \r\nthe oil is very hot, drop a piece of the garlic into the pan.  \r\nIf it sizzles immediately, the oil is ready.  Add the garlic \r\nand toss until golden, about 30 seconds.  Add the shrimp and \r\ntoss until they turn pink and are opaque, no more than 1 minute.  \r\nRemove from the pan and set aside.\r\n\r\nAdd the egg to the pan and tilt the pan to spread it into a thin \r\nsheet.  As soon as it begins to set and is opaque, scramble it \r\nto break it into small lumps.  Remove from the pan and set aside \r\nwith the shrimp.\r\n\r\nAdd the remaining 1 tablespoon oil, heat for 30 seconds, and \r\nadd the softened noodles.  Using a spatula, spread and pull the \r\nnoodles into a thin layer covering the surface of the pan. Then \r\nscrape them into a clump again and gently turn them over.  Hook \r\nloops of noodles with the edge of the spatula and pull them up \r\nthe sides, spreading them out into a layer again.  Repeat this \r\nprocess several times as the stiff, white noodles soften and curl \r\ninto ivory ringlets.  Add the fish sauce and turn the noodles so \r\nthey are evenly seasoned.  Add the sugar and peanuts, turning the \r\nnoodles a few more times.\r\n\r\nReserving a small handful for garnish, add the bean sprouts, along \r\nwith the green onions and shrimp-egg mixture.  Cook for 1 minute, \r\nturning often.  Transfer the noodles to the serving platter and \r\nsqueeze the juice of 2 lime wedges over the top.  Garnish with \r\nremaining bean sprouts and lime wedges and serve at once.\r\n\r\nServes 1 as a main course, 2 as an appetizer.  \r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThai Fried Noodles \r\n\r\nfrom The Book of Thai Cooking, by Hilaire Walden; HPBooks,\r\nISBN 1-55788-038-7\r\n\r\n3 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n4 garlic cloves, finely crushed\r\n\r\n1 tablespoon fish sauce\r\n3 to 4 tablespoons lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon crushed palm sugar\r\n\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n\r\n12 ounces rice vermicelli, soaked in water 20 minutes, drained\r\n4 ounces peeled shrimp\r\n4 ounces bean sprouts\r\n4 green onions, sliced\r\n\r\n2 tablespoons dried shrimp, ground, to garnish\r\nFinely chopped roasted peanuts, cilantro leaves and lime slices to garnish\r\n\r\nHeat oil in a wok, add garlic and cook, stirring occasionally, \r\nuntil golden.  Stir in fish sauce, lime juice and sugar until \r\nsugar has dissolved.  Quickly stir in eggs and cook for a few \r\nseconds.  Stir in noodles to coat with garlic and egg, then \r\nadd shrimps, 3/4 of the bean sprouts and half of the green onions.\r\n\r\nWhen noodles are tender, transfer contents of wok to a warmed \r\nserving dish.  Garnish with remaining bean sprouts and green \r\nonions, dried shrimp, peanuts, cilantro leaves and lime slices.\r\n\r\nMakes 4 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPAD THAI (Serves 2)\r\n===================\r\n\r\n(posted on rec.food.cooking)\r\n\r\n1/4-1/3 pound flat rice stick noodles (banh pho), 1/4 \" wide\r\n      1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n    1/4 pound pork, cut into matchstick strips\r\n            6 shrimps, peeled and deveined\r\n        1 tsp crushed garlic\r\n            1 egg\r\n       2 Tbsp water\r\n       2 Tbsp rice vinegar\r\n       1 Tbsp fish sauce\r\n       1 Tbsp sugar\r\n      1/4 cup chopped toasted peanuts\r\n      1/4 tsp ground dry shrimp\r\n              freshly ground white pepper\r\n      1/4 tsp Asian chili powder (to taste)\r\n        1 cup bean sprouts, washed and drained\r\n      1/4 cup scallions, cut 3/4\" long\r\n              fresh coriander\r\n              wedges of fresh lime\r\n\r\nSoak noodles in warm water for 60 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nPrepare all other ingredients and arrange near the wok.  You will need\r\nto work fast.\r\n\r\nIn the wok, fry the pork in the peanut oil at medium heat.  When half \r\ncooked, add the shrimps and garlic and stir.  Cook until shrimp and \r\npork are done.\r\n\r\nBeat the egg and add it to the mixture.  Cook, stirring, for about \r\nhalf a minute.\r\n\r\nTurn the heat to high.  Add the drained noodles to shrimp mixture.\r\nAdd water, vinegar, fish sauce, sugar, shrimp powder, and most of \r\nthe peanuts.  Sprinkle in white pepper and chili powder.  Toss to \r\ncombine.  Let it cook on one side (Don\'t stir; keep checking the \r\nunderside as if it were a big pancake).  Flip it over as best you can\r\nand repeat until nearly cooked, about 5 to 10 minutes.\r\n\r\nAdd most of the sprouts and scallions.  Stir and cook for another \r\nminute.\r\n\r\nTurn onto a plate.  Top with the rest of the sprouts, scallions, and\r\npeanuts.  Garnish with coriander and serve with a wedge of fresh lemon.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai\r\n\r\n(posted on rec.food.cooking)\r\n\r\n1 Pkg (10-16 oz. (.3-.5 kg) rice stick noodles\r\n2Tbl oil (30 ml)\r\n3 or more cloves garlic, crushed or minced\r\n8oz. (250g) Shrimp, peeled (optional)\r\n8oz. (250g) Chicken, Pork or more Shrimp, cut into dice or matchsticks\r\n(optional)\r\n2 or 3 eggs\r\n1 Cup (250ml) bean sprouts\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) chopped/ground peanuts\r\n1 red chile, finely chopped, or 1 tsp (5ml) red pepper flakes (optional)\r\n1 Cup (250ml) sliced cabbage\r\nCilantro leaves\r\nLime wedges\r\n\r\nSauce\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) Thai Fish Sauce\r\n1/4 Cup (60ml) White Vinegar\r\n2 Tbl (30 ml) white sugar\r\n3 Tbl (90 ml) Paprika\r\n\r\nSoak the rice noodles in cold water at least two hours before cooking. \r\nDrain\r\n\r\nIn a large wok, heat oil and stir-fry garlic for 30 seconds.  Add shrimp\r\nand other meat if used.\r\n\r\nAdd the noodles, and stir-fry until al dente.  Add sauce ingredients,\r\ncook to allow most of this to be absorbed (2 minutes or so).\r\n\r\nSpread the noodles, etc. out to the sides, and add eggs.  Some will crack\r\nthe eggs directly into the wok, others will pre-scramble.  If cracked\r\ninto the pan, start stirring them up when partially cooked, so you get\r\n\'streaky\' yellow and white eggs.  As they cook, fold the noodle mixture\r\nback in.\r\n\r\nAdd 1/2 the bean sprouts, peanuts, red pepper, folded into the mixture.\r\n\r\nServe hot, garnished with the rest of the bean sprouts, chopped cabbage,\r\ncilantro.  Serve with lime wedges to be squeezed into the noodles.\r\n\r\nIn my house, we have doctored this with thai hot chile sauce, sweet and\r\nsour sauce, or  sate-style peanut sauce.  It takes to any of these very\r\nwell, depending on your tastes.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nSpicy Thai Noodles (from Good Housekeeping (I think))\r\n(Not very authentic)\r\n\r\n1 16-ounce package Oriental noodles or linguine\r\n1/4 cup soy sauce\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n2 tablespoons chili sauce\r\n3 tablespoons salad oil\r\n1 small red pepper, thinly sliced\r\n1 tablespoon grated, peeled gingerroot\r\n1 garlic clove, minced\r\n1 bunch green onions, chopped\r\n2 large eggs, lightly beaten\r\n1/2 pound bean sprouts\r\n1 small tomato, cut into think wedges\r\n1/4 cup salted peanuts, coarsely chopped\r\n1 tablespoon chopped fresh cilantro or parsley (optional)\r\n1/4 teaspoon crushed red pepper\r\nlime wedges for garnish\r\n\r\n1.  Prepare noodles as label directs; drain and keep warm.\r\n2.  Meanwhile, in cup, stir soy sauce, sugar, chili sauce,\r\nand 1/2 cup water; set aside.  In 12-inch skillet over \r\nmedium-high heat, in hot salad oil, cook red pepper, ginger, \r\ngarlic, and all but 1/4 cup chopped green onions until tender \r\nand lightly browned, about 5 minutes.  Push vegetables to one \r\nside of skillet.\r\n3.  To same skillet over medium-high heat, add eggs, stirring \r\nuntil set but still moist.  Stir in bean sprouts and soy-sauce \r\nmixture; over high heat, heat to boiling.  Add noodles and tomato \r\nwedges, tossing to coat with sauce; heat through. \r\n4.  Spoon noodle mixture onto large warm platter.  Sprinkle \r\nwith chopped peanuts, chopped cilantro, crushed red pepper, and \r\nremaining chopped green onions.  Garnish with lime wedges.  \r\nMakes 5 servings.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai  (posted on net)\r\n\r\nnoodles - vermicelli or rice noodles ~6 ounces\r\n2 Tablespoon peanut butter\r\n5 Tablespoon soy sauce or tamari\r\n1 Tablespoon brown sugar\r\n2 scrambled eggs\r\n6 diced scallions\r\n5 cloves pressed garlic\r\npeanuts\r\n1/3 cup vinegar\r\nquartered lime\r\n\r\nCook, rinse, and refrigerate the noodles ahead of time.\r\nIn a bowl, mix the peanut butter, soy sauce, and sugar.\r\n\r\nIn a wok, saute the scallionsa nd garlic.  You can add bean sprouts\r\nat this point too.  After a few minutes, add the noodles, and\r\nstir-fry them for about 5 minutes.  Then add the stuff in the bowl\r\nand the vinegar.  Cook this for a couple more minutes.  Last,\r\nadd the eggs and peanuts, and heat until hot.  Serve with the\r\nlime wedges on the side.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: valerie@jarthur.Claremont.EDU (Valerie Okinaka)\r\nSubject: Re: Pad Thai\r\n\r\nHere is a recipe which appeared in the LA times a while back:\r\n\r\nAtlas Bar and Grill Pad Thai\r\n1 (7-ounce) package dry Thai flat ribbon noodles\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n2 tablespoons minced garlic\r\n2 tablespoons peanut oil\r\n1 tablespoon sugar\r\n1/2 teaspoon cayenne pepper or to taste\r\n1/4 cup Thai fish or shrimp sauce\r\n1/2 cup catsup\r\n2 tablespoons to 1/4 cup ground dried shrimp  (I suggest going easy on this)\r\n2 eggs, beaten\r\n1 cup bean sprouts\r\n2 tablespoons chopped roasted peanuts\r\nPad Thai Marinated Shrimp (See below)\r\nChopped green onions, lime, sunflower sprouts and carrot for garnish.\r\n\r\n  Soak noodles in water to cover 15 minutes.  Soak sprouts in cold water to\r\ncover.\r\n  Brown garlic lightly in oil.  Stir in sugar, cayenne, fish sauce, catsup and\r\ndried shrimp.  Add eggs and noodles.  Fry until noodles turn orange and are\r\ntender.\r\n  Add bean sprouts and cook 1 minute.  Top with peanuts, grilled Pad Thai\r\nMarinated Shrimp and garnish with green onions, lime, and sunflower sprouts\r\nand carrots as desired.  Makes 4 servings.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai Marinated Shrimp\r\n1/2 pound cleaned and butterflied shrimp\r\n2 tablespoons fish or shrimp sauce\r\n1/2 cup lime juice\r\n1 teaspoon minced garlic\r\n1/2 teaspoon cayenne pepper\r\n1/4 cup catsup\r\n\r\n  Place shrimp in shallow container.  Mix fish sauce, lime juice, garlic,\r\ncayenne to taste and catsup.  Pour over shrimp.  Marinate 2 hours.  Skewer on\r\nsoaked bamboo skewers and grill when ready to serve.\r\n\r\nBTW, I know that using catsup in Pad Thai is not very authentic but it does\r\nmake a reasonable substitude for Pad Thai sauce and is very easy.  Authentic\r\nPad Thai sauce uses tamarind and other ingredients.  A good authentic recipe\r\ncan be found in \"Thai Cooking from the Siam Cuisine Restaurant.\"  The latest\r\nfrugal gourmet book (Immigrant Ancestors) also has a Pad Thai recipe that I\r\nhaven\'t tried yet.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: horton@reed.uucp (Nike Horton)\r\nSubject: Cleartext copy of \"Pad-thai (noodles with peanut sauce) \"\r\n\r\nPAD-THAI(M)              USENET Cookbook              PAD-THAI(M)\r\n\r\nPAD-THAI\r\n\r\n     PAD-THAI - Thai noodles with peanuts and seafood\r\n\r\n     This recipe is originally from William Crawford and Kamolmal\r\n     Pootararka\'s Thai Home-Cooking: From Kamolmal\'s Kitchen.\r\n     This is a very popular Thai one-dish meal.  In Thai homes it\r\n     is normally prepared with dried shrimp and tofu; restaurants\r\n     often use shredded pork or chicken.\r\n\r\nINGREDIENTS (4 large servings)\r\n     1/2 lb    cellophane noodles\r\n     1/2 lb    shrimp\r\n     1/4 cup   fish sauce (nam pla)\r\n     1/4 cup   white vinegar\r\n     1/4 cup   sugar\r\n     1 tsp     paprika\r\n     1 bunch   scallions\r\n     1/2 cup   vegetable oil\r\n     2         cloves garlic, crushed\r\n     2         eggs\r\n     3/4 lb    bean sprouts\r\n     2 Tbsp    red chilis, crushed\r\n     3/4 cup   peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n     2         limes\r\n\r\nPROCEDURE\r\n          (1)  Soak noodles in warm water until soft, about 10\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (2)  Prepare shrimp (or shredded meat).\r\n\r\n          (3)  Mix together fish sauce, vinegar, sugar and\r\n               paprika.\r\n\r\n          (4)  Chop scallions into small bite-size pieces.\r\n\r\n          (5)  In wok or large skillet, stir-fry garlic in oil\r\n               for one minute. Add shrimp and cook until pink.\r\n\r\n          (6)  Add noodles and sauce from step 3.  Cook for 2\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (7)  Make space in the mixture to break the eggs.  Let\r\n               them set for 30 seconds then stir them into the\r\n               mixture.  Cook for 2 more minutes.\r\n\r\n          (8)  Add bean sprouts and scallions.  Cook for 2 more\r\n               minutes.\r\n\r\n          (9)  Serve hot topped with chopped chilis, peanuts and\r\n               lime juice.\r\n\r\nNOTES\r\n     I often add squid or chicken with the shrimp.\r\n\r\nRATING\r\n     Difficulty: easy to moderate.  Time: 30 minutes preparation,\r\n     15 minutes cooking.  Precision: approximate measurement OK.\r\n\r\nCONTRIBUTOR\r\n     Nicholas Horton\r\n     Reed College, Portland, Oregon, USA\r\n     tektronix!reed!horton\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: mmr@cs.rit.edu (Margaret M Reek)\r\nSubject: Pad Thai\r\nThai restaurant and catering business.\r\n\r\n1/4 lb. boneless chicken breast, sliced\r\n1/4 lb. rice noodles (1/4 inch wide)\r\n1/4 lb. bean sprouts\r\n3-4 Tbs oil\r\n1 Tbs Nam Pla (fish sauce)\r\n3 Tbs sugar\r\n1 Tbs vinegar\r\n3 Tbs ground peanuts (dry roasted, unsalted)\r\n1 egg\r\n2 scallions\r\n1 Tbs paprika (for color)\r\n1 Tbs pickled daikon radish\r\n[ ground chilis - optional (depending on your opinion of hot stuff)]\r\n\r\nSoak the rice noodles for 30 minutes in warm water; you might like to\r\nbreak them somewhat first so they are easier to eat.\r\nHeat a wok (or whatever you\'ve got) for several minutes, until it is\r\nreally hot.  Add oil and chicken, stiring until the chicken in tender\r\n(only a couple minutes for raw chicken).  Add egg, stir and add\r\nnoodles, fish sauce, sugar, vinegar, paprika, and radish.  Mix well\r\nand add bean sprouts and onion.  Cook long enough to heat through.\r\n\r\nIn theory this serves 1; my experience shows it serves 2 unless you\r\nhave a totally gargantuan appetite (my husband and I are not exactly\r\ntotal wimps in this area).  Don\'t do more than double the amount in\r\none pan, or it just won\'t turn out right.  The secret to Thai cooking\r\nis fast cooking, and you can\'t do it with volumes of food.\r\n\r\nAnother tip is to make sure EVERYTHING is ready to go once you turn\r\non the burner.  This dish goes very fast when cooking, and you can\'t\r\nbe fiddling around chopping or measuring once you\'ve started.  I put\r\ntogether in small bowls the things that get dumped in together, so I\r\ndon\'t have to think much at all when doing the cooking.  Thai cooking\r\nis something I have yet to feed to guest, because I haven\'t bothered\r\nto figure out how to get the timing right for more than 2 people (but\r\nwith several dishes per person).\r\n\r\nThe Nam Pla and pickled radish should be available at a variety of\r\nOriental stores, not just those catering to the Thai market.  The Nam\r\nPla comes in a huge bottle, but fear not, you\'ll use it a lot in Thai\r\ncooking.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai-pseudo-vegetarian style\r\njonathan kandell\r\ntucson arizona\r\njkandell@ccit.arizona.edu\r\n \r\n1/4 cup        thai fish sauce\r\n1/4 cup + 2 T  white vinegar\r\n2-4 T          sugar\r\n1 t            paprika\r\n8 oz           thai rice noodles about 1/4\" thick\r\n8 oz           tofu\r\n1-2 T          dried shrimp (optional)\r\n3 T            oil\r\n2-3 cloves     garlic\r\n2              eggs\r\n3/4 lb         bean sprouts\r\n3              green onions, sliced on the diagonal,   \r\n               including white part\r\n3/4 C          ground peanuts\r\n1+ T           roasted red chili peppers.  [Take some dried reds\r\n               and cook them in an ungreased wok over low\r\n               stirring constantly until they start to brown. \r\n               Grind in a coffee grinder or spice mill.]\r\n \r\nCombine first four ingredients and let sit until sugar dissolves.\r\nSoak rice noodles/sticks in warm water until they are soft but\r\ndon\'t disintegrate when pressed, about 40 minutes. Drain.\r\nDrain tofu by wrapping it in a clean towel and pressing with a\r\nlarge can of tomatoes, about 30 minutes.  Cut into 1/4 inch\r\ncubes.  Rinse dried shrimp in water and drain.\r\n \r\nPut oil in wok, heat to medium, put in crushed garlic. Swirl\r\naround for about a minute.  Add noodles and mix around for a\r\nminute or two.  \r\n \r\nAdd the tofu and shrimp then immediately add the liquid. Keep\r\nstirring until the liquid has all been absorbed into the noodles,\r\nabout a couple of minutes.\r\n \r\nLower heat to low or medium-low.  Push some of the stuff aside at\r\none end of wok, and break an egg in.  Immediately cover with\r\nnoodles.  Do the same at the other end of wok.  Let the eggs cook\r\n*undisturbed* until the yolks are practically cooked, about three\r\nminutes. (*Carefully* peak if unsure.)  Slip a slotted spoon\r\nunder each egg in turn, and bring upward, through the noodles,\r\nshaking as you go. The idea is to break up the cooked egg into\r\nthe rest of the mixture into tiny bits.\r\n \r\nMix in sprouts and green onions. Let cook for another two\r\nminutes.  Turn off heat.  Add crushed peanuts and enough roasted\r\nred chilies to your desired hotness.\r\n \r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai \r\n(Vegetarian Times, June 1993)\r\n\r\nNoodles and Sauce:\r\n8 to 10 ounces dried flat-sided rice noodles\r\n4 tablespoons distilled white vinegar\r\n3 tablespoons tomato paste\r\n3 tablespoons water\r\n2 tablespoons sugar\r\n1 to 2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n2 cloves garlic, minced\r\n1 fresh green chili, seeded and minced\r\n2 eggs (optional)\r\n\r\nVegetables:\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n1/3 cup chopped unsalted dry-roasted peanuts (optional)\r\nLime wedges and sliced green onions for garnish\r\n\r\nBring 2 quarts water to boil and remove from heat.  Soak\r\nnoodles in water for 20 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a bowl, mix together vinegar, tomato paste, water and \r\nsugar.  Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a frying pan, heat oil and stir-fry garlic and chili on\r\nmedium heat for 3 minutes.  Stir in tomato-vinegar mixture\r\nand make a well in the center of pan.  Crack in eggs, if desired, \r\nand cook until almost set, about 2 minutes; then stir quickly \r\ninto sauce.  Let sauce mxture continue to simmer on low heat \r\nuntil very thick, about 4 to 5 minutes.  Toss noodles into\r\nsauce and mix well.  Remove from heat.\r\n\r\nPlace noodles and sauce on one end of a large serving plate.\r\nOn the other end, place a pile of bean sprouts and peanuts \r\nif desired.  Garnish plate with lime wedges and green onions.\r\nLet diners help themselves to noodles, bean sprouts\r\nand peanuts, and squeeze lime juice over their portions.\r\nServes 4.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai with Vegetables\r\n(Bon Appetit, February 1991)\r\n\r\n6 ounces rice sticks or rice vermicelli\r\n2 1/2 cups fresh bean sprouts\r\n1/2 cup dry roasted peanuts\r\n2 stalks fresh lemongrass or 1 Tbsp grated lemon peel\r\n7 whole fresh cilantro sprigs\r\n4 large garlic cloves\r\n1 jalapeno chili, stemmed\r\n1 medium carrot, peeled, cut into 1-inch pieces\r\n2 eggs\r\n1/4 cup peanut oil\r\n1 large zucchini, diced\r\n4 large green onions, thinly sliced\r\n1/2 tsp salt\r\n2 Tbsp plus 1 tsp sugar\r\n2 Tbsp plus 1 tsp fresh lemon juice\r\n2 Tbsp catsup\r\n1 1/2 Tbsp Thai fish sauce (nam pla)\r\nmore cilantro sprigs for garnish\r\nlemon wedges for garnish (optional)\r\n\r\nSoak rice sticks (breaking up noodles into shorter pieces) \r\nin hot water to cover until soft, about 30 minutes. \r\nDrain thoroughly.\r\n              \r\nIn food processor fitted with steel knife, coarsely chop peanuts.\r\nPut in bowl for later.\r\nTrim root end and outer leaves of lemongrass stalks, leaving\r\nabout 2 1/2 inches of small white core.  In food processor,\r\nmince cilantro sprigs, lemongrass, garlic, and jalapeno.\r\nAdd carrot pieces and process until carrots are pea size, using\r\non/off turns.  Transfer carrot mixture to bowl.  Beat eggs in processor.\r\n\r\nHeat oil in wok or heavy large skillet over high heat.  Add\r\ncarrot mixture, diced zucchini, half of green onions, and salt.\r\nStir-fry just to heat through, about 1 minute.  Add sugar, lemon\r\njuice, catsup, and fish sauce and toss to blend.  Add beaten eggs\r\nand let set partially, about 30 seconds.  Scramble eggs lightly.\r\nAdd noodles and bean sprouts and stir-fry until eggs are cooked\r\nand the mixture is well mixed and heated through.  Mix in chopped\r\npeanuts and remaining green onions.  Adjust seasonings.  Transfer\r\npad Thai to warm platter. Garnish with fresh cilantro and lemon\r\nwedges, and serve.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom: markv@masig.fsu.edu (Mark Verschell)\r\n\r\nI\'ll try to recall my recipe from memory. It should be Vegan with the exception\r\nof the eggs, which could easily be left out. I think they add alot, but I\r\nhaven\'t tried any substitutes.\r\n\r\nPhad Thai - Vegan {opt Ovo-Lacto} \r\n---------------------------------\r\n8 oz. Firm Tofu (firmer the better)\r\n10 oz. rice sticks (Thai or chinese rice noodles ~1/8\" wide)\r\n1/4 c. Soy Sauce, light sodium works best \r\n1/4 c. white vinegar\r\n1/4 c. less 1 T. sugar\r\n1 T. paprika (for color, optional)\r\n1/4 c. vegetable oil {plus a little extra if you use eggs}\r\n2 cloves garlic, minced\r\napprox 2 c. chopped, assorted vegetables (broccoli, carrots, etc...)\r\n{2 eggs}\r\n8 oz bean sprouts\r\nground roasted unsalted peanuts and ground red pepper (for garnish)\r\nlimes cut into wedges (for garnish)\r\n\r\nPlace 3 folded paper towels on a plate, then the tofu on the towels, 3 more\r\nfolded paper towels on top of the tofu, another plate on top of that and then a\r\nheavy object on top of that. The idea is to remove any excess water from the\r\ntofu.\r\n\r\nIn lukewarm water, let the rice sticks soak for 20 minutes while you prepare the\r\nrest of the ingredients, then drain thoroughly.\r\n\r\nCut the tofu into pieces ~ 1/2\"x1\"x1/4\"\r\n\r\nMix the Soy Sauce, vinegar, sugar and paprika together. Set aside.\r\n\r\nIn a pre-heated wok, add the oil. When the oil is heated add the garlic. Do not\r\nburn the garlic! When the garlic just starts to turn light brown, add the tofu.\r\nI personally like to stir fry my tofu until it turns light brown all over for\r\nextra texture, if you like you can cook the tofu until it just starts to turn\r\nbrown for a less chewy texture. \r\n\r\nAdd the chopped vegetables, stirring constantly. Cook for a couple of minutes\r\nuntil the vegetables are heated but have not lost crispiness.\r\n\r\nAdd the drained rice sticks and carefully mix them to coat with oil. Cook for\r\nabout 1 minute.\r\n\r\nAdd the Soy Sauce mixture. Bring to boil, reduce heat to medium and\r\ncontinue gently mixing until all liquid is absorbed.\r\n\r\n{On each side of the wok, carefully lift up the phad thai, add ~ 1 t. oil and\r\nthen 1 of the eggs. Break the yolk, and cover the egg with the phad thai. Cook\r\nuntil eggs have set.}\r\n\r\nAdd the bean sprouts and continue cooking while mixing them in for about 2 minutes.\r\n\r\nServe the phad thai immediately on a pre-heated platter, garnished with the\r\npeanuts and red pepper, and with lime weges for all the diners. Or, do as I do,\r\njust place a bowl of peanuts and a bowl of red peppers to allow for individual\r\ntastes.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad Thai (from Thai Vegetarian Cooking)\r\n \r\n3 Tbsp oil\r\n1 clove garlic (minced)\r\n1/2 cup tofu (cubed)\r\n1 egg\r\n4oz Sen Lek noodles (flat rice noodles)\r\n1 Tbsp perserved turnip\r\n2 scallions (minced)\r\n2 Tbsp roast peanuts (chopped)\r\n3 oz (mung) bean sprouts\r\n1/2 tsp chili powder\r\n1 tsp sugar\r\n2 Tbsp light soy sauce\r\n1 Tbsp lemon juice\r\n \r\nStir fry items in order of list, but save 1/2 peanuts and beansprouts\r\nfor the table.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPad thai\r\nFrom: rickert@cco.caltech.edu (Keith Warren Rickert)\r\n\r\n6 garlic cloves, chopped\r\n1 lb meat (shrimp, chicken, pork, beef...in usefully small bits)\r\n6 eggs.\r\n1 cup chopped green onion\r\n1 lb rice noodles\r\n1/4 cup white vinegar\r\n1 cup chopped cilantro\r\n1 cup ground roasted peanuts. (No shells of course)\r\n1/4 cup sugar\r\n2 cups beansprouts\r\n2 tblspoons chile powder, \r\n2 teaspoons chile paste.\r\nvegetable oil.\r\n8 oz. sliced salted turnip/radish.\r\n1/4 cup fish sauce.\r\n\r\nOk, make sure you have just about all of this ready before you start.\r\nFirst of all, soak the noodles in lukewarm water for 40 mins to an hour.\r\n(Good time to get some of the other ingredients ready)\r\nWhen finished soaking, just pour off the water.\r\nSoak the radish in water for a few minutes, and pour off the water\r\nand squeeze dry. repeat 2-3 times or so.\r\nHeat up your wok with a bit of vegetable oil.\r\nMix the eggs as in for scrambled eggs, and toss them in the wok.\r\nCook until they are fairly \'hard\', moving them around as need be\r\n(but as little as possible). Remove them from the wok and \r\nchop into slices, and set them aside.\r\n\r\nHeat up some more oil (maybe 1/4 cup or a bit less) and add the garlic.\r\nWhen it is getting fragrant, before it starts to turn brown, add\r\nthe meat. \r\nWhen the meat is about halfway done, add the \r\nradish. When the meat is done, add the noodles and the vinegar\r\nand cook until the noodles are soft. From here on out you have\r\nto toss it a _lot_. Tongs might be useful. Dont step away for more\r\nthan about 20-30 seconds, cause you dont want it to burn.\r\nOnce the noodles start to get soft, add the eggs, the peanuts, the fish\r\nsauce, the sugar, the chile powder, and the chile paste. Continue\r\nstirring and tossing, until the mixture is all evenly coated.\r\nThis usually takes about 5 mins or so, maybe a bit longer.\r\nRemove this mixture from the wok and to another container.\r\nStirfry the beansprouts in the wok for maybe\r\n1 minute, maybe 1.5, then add beansprouts, green onions, and\r\ncilantro to the dish, and toss. Keep some unchopped cilantro\r\nand green onions for garnishes if you want (you can take bits of\r\ngreen onion, maybe 2 inches long, and cut one end (1-1.25 inches) into very \r\nthin strips while leaving the other end intact to get a very nice \r\ngarnish).\r\n Enjoy....\r\n\r\nOne or 2 random comments:\r\nIf you use shrimp, you\'d want to add them at the same\r\ntime as the radish, as they cook quickly, and get tough\r\nif overcooked.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom _A_Touch_of_Thailand_ by Duangduen Buranasombati \r\n\r\nPAD THAI (Thai Fried Noodles)\r\n\r\n225 grams (1/2 lb) Thai rice noodles\r\n12 medium size shrimp, shelled and cleaned\r\n1 medium onion, diced\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) preserved radish, sliced (should be at Asian market)\r\n1 tsp garlic, minced\r\n1 tbsp sugar\r\n1 tbsp nam pla (fish sauce)\r\n2 tbsp ketchup\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) peanuts, coarsely ground\r\n100 grams (1/2 cup) vegetable oil\r\n200 grams (1 cup) bean sprouts\r\n1 tbsp tamarind soaked in 3 tbsp hot water (tamarind usually comes\r\n	in a package without seeds.  Add water and squeeze the pulp\r\n	of the fruit several times to obtain the sour liquid.  Sub-\r\n	stitute the juice of 1 lime if tamarind is not available.  You\r\n	should find tamarind in a Thai, Indian, Spanish, or Asian market)\r\njuice of 1 lemon\r\n2 eggs, slightly beaten\r\n\r\nSoak the noodles in cold water to cover for about 15 minutes.  Drain. \r\nPut noodles in a bowl and pour hot water over them.  Let stand for about \r\n10 minutes.  Drain and rinse with cold water; set aside. Put oil in a wok or\r\nlarge skillet over medium heat.  Add garlic and onion.  Stir-fry\r\nuntil onion turns translucent.  Add shrimp and stir-fry for about 2-3\r\nminutes, or until shrimp turns pink.  Add ketchup, sugar, nam pla,\r\npreserved radish, tamarind juice, and lemon juice.  Stir well.  Pour\r\nin the beaten eggs and let them slightly set for 3 minutes before mixing\r\nwith remaining ingredients.  Add noodles, bean sprouts, and peanuts.\r\nMix well.  Spoon noodles onto serving platter and garnish with 1 cup\r\nbean sprouts, lemon wedges, cilantro, and peanuts.\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFrom Vegetarian Gourmet\r\n\r\nServes 4\r\n\r\n3 cups water\r\n1 7oz package thin rice noodles\r\n2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n1 clove garlic, minced\r\n1 teaspoon minced fresh gingerroot\r\n2 cups shredded cabbage, bok choy or chinese cabbage\r\n1 cup mung bean sprouts\r\n2 scallions, finely chopped\r\n2 teaspoons low-sodium soy sauce or tamari\r\nPinch of black pepper\r\n15 sweet basil leaves\r\n1/4 cup roasted peanuts, chopped\r\n1 lime, cut into wedges\r\n\r\n1.  Bring water to a boil, add rice noodles and cook until\r\nsoft, about 4 to 5 minutes.  Drain and set aside.\r\n2.  In a wok or large skillet, heat oil for one minute\r\nover medium-high heat.  Add garlic and ginger, and stir-fry for\r\none minute.\r\n3.  add cabbage and stir-fry until tender-crisp, about 2 to 4 minutes.\r\n4.  Add rice noodles, bean sprouts, soy sauce and black pepper.\r\nStir-fry until heated through, about 1 to 2 minutes.  Mix in\r\nbasil leaves.\r\n5.  Garnish with peanuts and lime wedges.  Serve hot.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nPaad Thai\r\nFrom unidentified magazine\r\n\r\n1/2 pound dried flat rice noodles, 1/4 to 1/2 inch wide\r\n3 tablespoons fish sauce\r\n2 tablespoons rice vinegar\r\n1 1/2 tablespoons vegetable oil\r\n6 green onions, coarsely chopped\r\n3 cloves garlic, finely chopped\r\n16 large shrimp, shelled and deveined\r\n2 large eggs, beaten\r\n1 cup fresh bean sprouts\r\n1 tablespoon fresh lime juice\r\n1/3 cup roasted unsalted peanuts, finely chopped\r\nFresh sprigs coriander (optional)\r\nLime wedges (optional)\r\n\r\n1.  In large bowl, soak noodles in warm water to cover\r\n30 to 35 mintues or until softened.  (Do not soak longer as \r\nnoodles will become mushy.)  Drain noodles well in colander \r\nand set aside.  In small bowl, combine fish sauce, vinegar, \r\nsugar, and paprika; set aside.\r\n2.  In large skillet or wok, heat oil over medium heat.  Add\r\nonions and garlic and stir-fry 1 minute; add shrimp and cook,\r\nstirring, just until onions and garlic are browned and shrimp\r\nare pink and cooked through -- 3 to 4 minutes.  With slotted\r\nspoon remove shrimp mixture to colander with noodles.\r\n3.  Add eggs to skillet and cook until almost set, breaking them\r\ninto small pieces as they cook.  Reduce heat to low and add\r\nnoodles with shrimp mixture, tossing to blend.  Stir in fish\r\nsauce mixture and heat 3 to 4 minutes or until most of liquid\r\nevaporates.  Add bean sprouts and lime juice, tossing gently\r\nuntil completely combined; transfer Paad Thai to serving platter\r\nor divide among individual serving plates.  Garnish with\r\nchopped peanus and, if desired, coriander sprigs and lime\r\nwedges.  Serve immediately.\r\n\r\n</PRE>'),(844,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/844_1.jpg\' >\r\n\r\n<p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\">It\'s a Bird, It\'s a Plane, No, it\'s SUPERMAN on DVD. Hooray \r\n                    for this day. It\'s very funny to see the computers used in \r\n                    Superman III, which I remember seeing in the theater when \r\n                    I was kid. the original SUPERMAN is the first movie my memory \r\n                    remembers recording. </p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >[April \r\n                    sounds sent to friends, sort of the set list you wanted austin, \r\n                    just not in order and not all the songs]</p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >Ocean \r\n                    Spray - Manic Street Preachers<br>\r\n                    Marrow - Ani DiFranco<br>\r\n                    Salt in the wound - Beck<br>\r\n                    You Must Go On - Bernard Butler<br>\r\n                    Bocuma - Board of Canada<br>\r\n                    Help is Around The Corner - Coldplay<br>\r\n                    As Close As This - PJ Harvey<br>\r\n                    A Cheery Wave From Stranded Youngsters - Mogwai<br>\r\n                    Downtown Train - Tom Waits<br>\r\n                    Track 10 - Wings of Desire Soundtrack<br>\r\n                    Just A Friend - Biz Markie<br>\r\n                  </p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >I \r\n                    don\'t like the new R.E.M album that much. Automatic For the \r\n                    People was important to me in high school and I love MONSTER \r\n                    but have yet to meet anyone who likes it as much as me. I \r\n                    saw them twice on that tour. with Radiohead. before I owned \r\n                    the Bends, so I had no idea what ecstasy my ears were in for \r\n                    the 2nd nite, b/c the 1st nite at Reunion Arena in Dallas \r\n                    I almost went deaf b/c of radiohead and my high up 50 speakers \r\n                    in my face seat.</p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >DVD \r\n                    Acquisitions: Dazed and confused, gone with the wind, Rebel \r\n                    without a cause, Manhattan Murder Mystery, Mighty Aphrodite, \r\n                    Guinevere & the Tenchi Muyo boxed set. oh and Superman \r\n                    1 - 3. ( Part 4 was a joke, but it was the last movie i saw \r\n                    in my hometown before i came to Texas so i might get it in \r\n                    the future)<br>\r\n                    <br>\r\n                    Sent E-Mails: 357 from home.<br>\r\n                  </p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" >A \r\n                    poet is an unhappy<br>\r\n                    being whose heart is torn by secret sufferings, but whose \r\n                    lips are so<br>\r\n                    strangely formed that when the sighs and the cries escape \r\n                    them, they sound<br>\r\n                    like beautiful music.<br>\r\n                    --Kierkegaard</p>\r\n                  <p style=\"FONT-SIZE: 8pt; FONT-FAMILY: lucida sans unicode\" > </p>'),(845,'<p><i>continued from\r\n<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=832.txt\">part one</a>...</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAfter the interview, I crawled south to go see my pal Robin, who lives in\r\n<a href=\"http://www.delmar.ca.us/\">Del Mar</a>, just north of San Diego.  (which is why I was on the 405 headed south, as described in part one)  Her house is about a block from the cliffs that lead down to the beach.  It\'s one of those paradisical, naturally fecund places in California where stuff just grows everywhere.  Neat double decker commuter trains cruise the tracks above the cliffs.  The beach is nice and wide and pretty.  There are lots of surfers.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I got there we had a really long great conversation, she told me about the black art/scient of protein research in great detail.  Despite my lack of context for understanding it, it\'s completely fascinating stuff; incredibly detailed cellular mechanisms of life.  The next day she had to go work so I woke up and read and wrote a thank you email and ate fish tacos and did nothing and went running.  Based on my highly nonscientific sample of one run along the beach, exactly 100% of surfers are hotties.  That night we went to a bar and heard some punkesque folk rock band that kicked ass.  Her friends are nice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She took the next day off and we rented a little sailboat and discovered that I still mostly remember how.  That ruled, then we got yummy dinner, and then ended up at a coffee joint listening to some singer-songwriter guy who reminded me of seeing shows in Charlottesville.  That ruled too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I split the next morning for the slog up the 5.  The 5 <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=467.txt\">isn\'t really so bad</a>, sometimes, and this time it wasn\'t.  I stopped halfway, near the turnoff to Tehachapi, to call these <a href=\"http://www.wind.enron.com/\">other people I\'d been talking to about a job.</a>  They still had no fu, so I grabbed some in\'n\'out and kept driving.  The area around the grapevine was just beautiful - isolated rain showers poked with rays of sunlight off in the hills.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I got home, and began the wait.  They had a slightly hard time contacting my references but it finally worked out.  And on Monday of this week, 13 days after the interview, they offered me the job.  I gave Xerox Parc one more chance, didn\'t hear from them, and officially accept the offer this morning.  The feeling of an almost 3 month job search ending was really peculiar, one of those lots and lots of emotions all at once situations.  Mostly I\'m relieved but I\'m apprehensive about moving and less than thrilled to be leaving this area.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The job is with the <a href=\"http://www.alexandria.ucsb.edu/\">Digital Alexandria Project</a> down there.  They have some back end stuff that needs doing, but mostly what I\'d like to do is think about front end stuff for them, 2D or possibly 3D.  I\'m also hoping to find them some specific clients and maybe set up a nice feedback loop.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Four years ago, I left a really sweet academic job at another <a href=\"http://www.iath.virginia.edu/\">\r\nplace that was doing super interesting research.</a>  Some of the reasons I left were smart (I could get more money elsewhere, I needed to leave Charlottesville) and some were dumb (I thought industry might be a better channel for getting my work out than academia).  I like academia.  I like conferences.  I like learning stuff.  The people are pretty consistently top-notch.  So while I was a bit hesitant, I think it\'s ok.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Plus, it <i>is</i> paradise, at least sort of.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Meanwhile, there\'s still a bit more waiting.  I was hoping to go look for places to live tomorrow, but the bureaucracy down there still has to chew on all this a bit more.  I was planning on going down this week to fill out paperwork and find somewhere to live.  Now it\'s looking more like early next week, which is fine; I have a lot of people I want to hang out with before I split.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On Sunday I wound up shaking it at this sweet little rooftop deck party in SOMA.  Cute, wacky crowd, good mix of people I knew, people I didn\'t, and people I hadn\'t seen in too long.  Amazing music.  Watching the sun drop towards the horizon over the city I was struck by a profound sense of having been <i>affected</i> both by this place and moreso by the people I\'ve met here.  I\'m glad I\'ll only be six hours away by road - and 15ns by internet.</p>'),(846,'just about all packed up'),(847,'<PRE>\r\nthere is an army of ants marching in by brain\r\ndevouring thoughts and tearing them apart and carrying them away\r\nback to their mound deep in my chest\r\nwhere mother eats and kicks them out\r\nmore ants more ants, they look for places with heat\r\nair conditioners, water heaters, my hollow robot\'s heart\r\none day i saw one crawl across my eyeball\r\nmy wires tensed with fear\r\nants are no good to a robot\r\nthey mean the end is near.\r\n</PRE>'),(848,'test'),(849,'<p>\r\nwhen i was young we had a cabin on the beach. my grandfather built the house with one of my uncles and dad. it was beach front. made of brick with a tar roof. it was a real beach cabin. not these houses but a cabin. built by hand. the downstairs was all garage and used to house the dory. there was a creepy walk-in cooler that my sister locked me into once. i screamed and pounded on the door until my parents heard me. my sister got sent to her room. i laughed at her and went outside cause when i was a kid, i was a brat.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have more memories of time spent in that cabin than i do of any other part of my life. we spent christmas and easter there. it was were i found out santa was my parents cause i saw the presents in the back of the car with the \'from santa\' tags on them. we had the fourth of july on the beach shooting illegal fireworks and not being worried of the police because there was only one cop, and he was one of my parents friends. bonfires and marshmellows. burnt hotdogs and dead lighters thrown into the fire just to make things exciting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntwo summers i spent at the cabin with my mom. my sister and dad would drive back to portland for the week so my dad could work and my sister could see her friends. i played in the ocean and laid in the sun and blew up army men with m-80s. i walked to this burger place called \'fat freddy\'s fast food\' and ate greasy cheese burgers that i loved then but make me sick now. i climbed sand dunes. i hiked all over the place and made forts in the woods behind the dunes. and my heart ached when i saw the sign that the woods were being torn down. to make room for a much needed golf course.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand those are some of the best times of my life. nothing to do but explore and have fun and watch movies and play board games and cook food with my mom and read and do nothing that meant anything besides happiness. and when i am tired and run down and sad i can see myself in the cabin again. with the dark blue mat carpet and horrid charcoal red couches. i can see myself looking out the windows like i used to just staring into the ocean. staring at the curve of the world. seeing the motions and hearing the movement and feeling the beat of the waves deep in my chest.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand i just wish that i could have met my grandfather once, and thank him for that wonderful cabin he built.\r\n</p>'),(850,'<p>Hi.<br>\r\nI have a problem. I have little to no motivation and my concentration is shot. I have a lot of work to do in the next week for university and I can\'t even begin it. I can\'t even read the play for one of the essays because as soon as I start my mind is off flying elsewhere - Usually being pathetic and feeling sorry for my priviledged ass.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI must have some slight little amount of motivation in there somewhere because I\'m prepared to do something to change all of this.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI\'m also not a happy girl, for no particular reason. I just seem to get all sad and mopey, but when I evaluate my life I have no reason for it! I\'m warm, clothed, fed, loved by my family, I have a few friends up here, I have a loving, wonderful boyfriend and I guess I\'m not as stupid as I think I am.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHow do you gain motivation? <br>\r\nDoes anyone have any good ideas on how I can improve my concentration?\r\n</p>'),(851,'<p>reckless abandon<br><br>\r\n\r\nedges \r\nblurred<br><br>\r\n\r\ni lust for you\r\nand those patent leather day-glo words<br><br>\r\n\r\ntattered shirt<br>\r\nyou * give * me * good<br><br>\r\n\r\ngravy</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/851_1.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n'),(852,'<p>(Lately) Life has provided me with nothing<br>\r\n  and I have provided nothing to life<br>\r\n  not a woman to shape me<br>\r\n  not a girl to be shaped by me.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  strangest of all was an intense dream <br>\r\n  of a dark haired girl loving me.<br>\r\n  I\'m hoping my life is not a crusade<br>\r\n  to find the girl in my dream, <br>\r\n  not of my dreams.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  and this whole decent<br>\r\n  was started by a blue shirt<br>\r\n  with three buttons missing<br>\r\n  otherwise known<br>\r\n  as the sex thorn shirt.<br>\r\n  retaliation, since i ripped her shirt <br>\r\n  as well. all woth it<br>\r\n  for a blonde ambition<br>\r\n  and a prefect figure. </p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://a1388.g.akamai.net/7/1388/691/00e1940afba3ba/www.salon.com/sex/feature/2001/05/03/stuart/story.jpg\"><br>\r\n  <br>'),(853,'<PRE>\r\nI am inspired to reply to s5.  The 5 Rhythms.  \r\nI am Staccato. \r\nI am rarely still.\r\nWriting is my purest form of stillness and lyricality. \r\nI need more flow, to smoothly glide to and from the more heightened moments of\r\n  staccato action.\r\nChaos I dread, but live in it.  It surrounds me like a wet blanket and I am\r\n  learning to fling it with my energy, not crumple under its sticky weight.\r\nFLow\r\nStaccato\r\nChaos\r\nLyrical\r\nStillness\r\ncan you find yoursend in these rhythms of motion?\r\n</PRE>'),(854,'<p>I was digging around on a <a href=\"http://www.jenerator.org/\">website</a> that belongs to a friend of mine, and found <a href=\"http://www.jenerator.org/images/jh1.gif\">this</a>.  Bummer, Noah.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It made me think of a party called Tron that I went to in PHX.  There was a noodle that had scaled one of the bassbins during a hardcore set (can\'t remember the DJ) and was sitting there on top of the \'bin crosslegged, pounding a Prairie Dawn doll in time to some throbbing Nasenbluten. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jim, reach out from the great beyond - tell Legal they\'re about to cost your kids some money when they alienate a big chunk of their audience here.</p>'),(855,'<p>i was asleep this afternoon, and i had this strange experience while sleeping... i was sound asleep, but i was dreaming that someone was in my room (jill i think) and i was trying to wake up and couldnt. every muscle in my body was trying to move, to wake up, to do something!!! it was scary to tell you the truth, i was frightened that i would never wake up. as my nap went on, i could hear the trash collectors in my front yard; as i had all the windows and doors open. i dreamt that they were in my house, going through all my things... again i tried to wake myself up, but couldnt... i remember trying to groan to let them know i was there, i couldn\'t even groan... i was frozen! has this ever happened to anyone before? shit! scary!!! i didnt get much sleep last night, which could explain it but still - it was freaky!</p> <p>i found out today that i will have a summer job. that is what i have been waiting for! THANK YOU! THANK YOU!!! maybe i will be able to sleep at night!!!</p>'),(856,'<p>these flowers make me happy, even when it rains and i feel bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/856_1.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/856_2.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/856_3.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/856_4.jpg\'>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/856_5.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n<p>and i had one more, that i forgot to upload - for symmetr reasons - i dont want to bother posting again - so just imagine a 6th picture that is really beautiful...</p>'),(857,'<!--\r\n<P>there was a stinking dead seal on the shore. it\'s face had caved in and been picked away by birds.  same with the fins.  gross:</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/857_1.jpg\' alt=\'a dead seal\'><BR>\r\n-->\r\n<P>ert was standing on a sand ledge admiring the sun over the water:</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/857_2.jpg\' alt=\'see ert look at sunset too close to the edge\'><BR>\r\n<!--\r\n<P>then the sand gave and ert went down:</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/857_3.jpg\' alt=\'see ert just fell down the sand ledge because she was standing too close\'><BR>\r\n<P>it was funny:</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/857_4.jpg\' alt=\'see ert try to get up from the bottom\'><BR>\r\n-->\r\n<P>she climbed out just fine.</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/857_5.jpg\' alt=\'see ert climbing up\'>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>it was good to get out of the city and see some of God.  i associate the ocean with God.  it is so comprehensive and out of control with such destructive force and healing ability.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>'),(858,NULL),(859,'<P>i am a lazy bad party person.</P>\r\n<P>simon is coming over.  i have never met simon in person.  i am afeared of meeting internet people inperson for the first time.</P>\r\n<P>i took a nap starting at 1030 because i was really tired and just wanted to sleep.  then simon called, she is coming over with a friend i think.  i got up and went to get coffee and money from the atm.  two things i do not want to be:</P>\r\n<LI>sleepy\r\n<LI>broke\r\n<P>i will walk around tonight like godzilla, half thinking, green, scaley, stumbling, breathing coffee breath all over everyone.  destroying whole blocks of down town in this manner.</P>\r\n<P>weeb is not trying to help.  she is talking about falling asleep in her comfy bed in her new pajamas and counting sheep.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am a shepherd at heart.</P>\r\n<P>but tonight i will be counting shot glasses and bottles of beer on the wall.  and at some point, simon and i will become drinking friends.  and i will fall under the table.</P>\r\n<P>i hope she is driving the el camino.</P>'),(860,'<P>bah, sometimes reading this site is so depressing.  there\'s a lot of downness in the world in general.  and then people getting laid off doesn\'t help, either.  but, at the same time, it is comforting in a weird way... that people are having the same thoughts together.  moving back \'home\' would be especially weird for me, since i JUST MOVED HERE.  also, i have this weird problem.  i\'m bursty, i do things in bursts.  and get mad/dumb/down/depressed/etc when i\'m in a period of not doing anything at the moment.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>but no no, there is enough doom and gloom here, so i shall relay instead, of good things.  amazingly, i have gotten some job interviews.  i do not know where they will go, but nonetheless interviews are good.  today, after an interview, i went and met jenifer in the park.  we talked of swimming in the pond, jobstuff, pregnant stuff, stevestuff briefly (he called).  jenifer espoused her love for the encryptObot, though I suspect it was a secret plot to try to obtain the <encrypted> list. :)  we walked up the panhandle, talked about our meeting at burning man with me inside the goat and her head up its ass, heehee.  she bought me icecream! yay icecream!  and then into the little italian store in cole valley.  so many names and words for little tiny places.  goodbye, reminded her to call steve, and i walked back home.  OW.  i am so out of shape.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so though, i am in this non-burst period.  people are good in non-burst periods.  yay people.  perhaps i should go dance,  but, well, hmm.  err, hi.  BURSTY, i tell you, that is the way i am.  help!  i\'m either doing stuff at 100mph, or dead in the water.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>also, interestingly, while searching for the famous quote from \"American Beauty\" (all for a silly *encrypted* joke), i stumbled across some eastern philosophy of some sort which was expressing an idea:  <B>in</B>action is the path to personal change.  seems odd now, but the way it was expressed at the time seems to make some sense.  something about leaving the world alone (inaction) so that it does not in turn impose its artificial constraints back and you can find your true self etc.  but i probably have it all wrong, but it\'s interesting to think about nonetheless.  odd, in relation the 0mph periods in my bursty nature. too.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hmm.  it appears that i stayed in tonight.  silly me.  oh well, helping friends on chat with silly script stuff is good in some way, i suppose.  if i can\'t manage to do my own stuff, at least do other\'s stuff, right? </P>\r\n\r\n<P>oh, and, the last bit of goodness: \"Swinney.org Black Submit Form\"</P>\r\n'),(861,'<p>how long does it take for the redness of a labret to go away?  what sort of jewlery should i get?  right now, i have a huge flatback.  with time, i imagine that it is going to destroy my teeth.</p>'),(862,'<MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#949edb\" WIDTH=\"139\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#0aed43\" WIDTH=\"156\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7d7cad\" WIDTH=\"103\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#81c8c8\" WIDTH=\"25\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#16694a\" WIDTH=\"20\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#803755\" WIDTH=\"32\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#3fdac1\" WIDTH=\"25\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=right SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#a2e08f\" WIDTH=\"17\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#e3e937\" WIDTH=\"130\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#520406\" WIDTH=\"138\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7ed64b\" WIDTH=\"73\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#caa686\" WIDTH=\"159\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#88de52\" WIDTH=\"48\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#e21d44\" WIDTH=\"18\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#b97875\" WIDTH=\"108\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#ccf8ef\" WIDTH=\"62\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#33f0b5\" WIDTH=\"40\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#a84d9f\" WIDTH=\"169\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#8462c3\" WIDTH=\"117\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#fe64a1\" WIDTH=\"130\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#9214c7\" WIDTH=\"129\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=right SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#13c12c\" WIDTH=\"119\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#5e31f7\" WIDTH=\"83\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#218de7\" WIDTH=\"32\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#b0e51b\" WIDTH=\"179\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#6c6663\" WIDTH=\"100\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#99d8eb\" WIDTH=\"64\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#3b845f\" WIDTH=\"181\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#db094e\" WIDTH=\"159\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#b04b00\" WIDTH=\"122\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#330878\" WIDTH=\"63\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7c2fda\" WIDTH=\"4\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7d91ea\" WIDTH=\"27\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7702e2\" WIDTH=\"51\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#bc8851\" WIDTH=\"110\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=right SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#19a13c\" WIDTH=\"23\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#015443\" WIDTH=\"1\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#cffdec\" WIDTH=\"80\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#3399e3\" WIDTH=\"129\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#31845f\" WIDTH=\"124\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#b3555a\" WIDTH=\"34\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#1cefe3\" WIDTH=\"9\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#e6583a\" WIDTH=\"21\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#a86eee\" WIDTH=\"37\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#9b20ba\" WIDTH=\"89\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#fd2f51\" WIDTH=\"116\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#5318da\" WIDTH=\"132\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#0b4b48\" WIDTH=\"7\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#652c5b\" WIDTH=\"16\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=right SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#241265\" WIDTH=\"33\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#07c8a4\" WIDTH=\"133\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#6fb561\" WIDTH=\"72\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#38367f\" WIDTH=\"25\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#3f51cd\" WIDTH=\"22\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#8051b6\" WIDTH=\"90\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#4e5a9e\" WIDTH=\"137\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#9da794\" WIDTH=\"160\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#d0669e\" WIDTH=\"48\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#09f246\" WIDTH=\"147\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#6c59b9\" WIDTH=\"48\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#5df115\" WIDTH=\"178\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#13ed30\" WIDTH=\"57\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#9ea689\" WIDTH=\"192\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=right SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#b5a1a1\" WIDTH=\"67\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#5eef67\" WIDTH=\"20\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#da02fe\" WIDTH=\"49\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#ca838e\" WIDTH=\"137\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#195455\" WIDTH=\"149\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#8948b9\" WIDTH=\"88\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#6fa386\" WIDTH=\"152\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee><BR><MARQUEE BGCOLOR=BLACK behavior=alternate DIRECTION=left SCROLLAMOUNT=30 SCROLLDELAY=1 width=100><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=1000>\r\n\r\n       <TR><TD BGCOLOR=\"#89151e\" WIDTH=\"192\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#7326cb\" WIDTH=\"17\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#a3c8ea\" WIDTH=\"120\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#73102b\" WIDTH=\"99\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#24c3a1\" WIDTH=\"142\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#64bac9\" WIDTH=\"33\"><br></TD> <TD BGCOLOR=\"#33c85f\" WIDTH=\"63\"><br></TD>\r\n</table>\r\n</marquee>\r\n<TABLE width=400 border=0><TR><TD>\r\n<p>Last night I was drinking beer and thinking a lot about escapism. No, not the James Brown song. A major problem I\'ve been coming to terms with is my tendency to run into behaviors that supply me with immediate distraction or gratification. Something bothers me, I immediately look to solve it with something else. There\'s plenty of examples, from video games to smoking dope to trading mp3s &c &c.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The thing that I realized last night is that while using these things to escape depression, I am tricking myself into thinking depression is what I\'m escaping from. Depression is what I\'m escaping <u><b>to</b></u>. It\'s one of the ways that I cope with things that are very hard or disturbing to me. I lapse into a depressive state, allowing myself to focus my resources on changing the depressive state, which isn\'t the same thing as changing what caused the depression. I then use the secondary escape mechanisms to cope with the depression.</p>\r\n<p>I am going to focus my energies on being able to be sad without being depressed. Being sad is perfectly legitimate and not an unproductive place to be. In fact, many people feel that sadness is more conducive to the creative process than happiness. Depression, however, is a full time occupation and a dead end. It\'s like that blanket that you have had for years that never quite keeps you warm enough.</p>\r\n<p>For example, I was sad, and found out about IE\'s \"marquee\" tag, and modified some old code and made that thing up there. That thing is fucking beautiful and it makes me less sad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n<p>\"major more soul than james \'escapism\' / de la soul is here to stay like <b>racism</b>\"</TR></TD></TABLE>'),(863,'<FONT FACE=\"Verdana\" SIZE=\"2\">\r\n<P>\r\nso i got nicely wrapped up in a nice independent film (as actresses are wont to do) and today a makeup artist came to make me look beaten up.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nsometimes i meet people i really wish i was more like.  she has pink and maroon hair and a crazy londoner-type accent.  her jeep has bright seat coverings.  she walked around barefoot and gave me gashes, bruises, cuts, a black eye, and a big fat lip.  and i\'ve got it on good authority that it all looked a little too real.  topped it off with fake blood running down my thighs.  sam filmed it all as the director took polaroids.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ni\'m a young call girl, new to the business, who gets a little roughed up by a client.  it\'s more difficult to do than it seems, if i really want to do it right.  and if the plot was just a little bit different i\'d feel bad about playing a victim, but i should feel bad about not feeling bad now.  my big question is: does being a prostitute make it okay for you to get beaten?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nyou\'d think i wouldn\'t think such things, but i do.  i make a person out of this faceless call girl, and if the whole film wasn\'t this incredibly dark dark comedy, i\'d feel downright bad about the whole thing...\r\n</P>\r\n</FONT>'),(864,'<p>\r\nthank you everyone for your help with the piercing.  i\'m going to follow all of the advice, mainly starting with the seasalt compress tonight.  the redness has gone down a bit, but yesterday it was quite bad.  i\'ve been using biotene most of the day and that helps.\r\n</P><P>\r\nright now, i\'m trying my hardest not to smoke.  i don\'t know if anyone out there is trying to quit, but i have some good advice to give.\r\n</P><P>\r\nif you want to quit smoking, just quit.  don\'t use any sort of over the counter remedies.  don\'t use the patch, chew gum, or take pills.  just quit.  find some nice stuff to keep in your mouth and chew on and get an oral piercing.  (they help greatly)\r\n</P><P>\r\ni cut down my smoking over the past two months and finally said \"this is it, i\'ve had it, i\'m sick and tired of smoking\".  after getting my labret pierced.. i\'ve had a drive and determination that i\'ve never had before and i\'m extremely proud of myself.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhowever, i couldn\'t have done it if i didn\'t conquer that initial fear of quitting as well as letting the drug use me and control me.\r\n</P><P>\r\ncigarettes are pure evil.  nothing should control your mind and actions like that.  i refuse to be a slave to phillip morris.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nif you smoke.  please .. *please* quit now.  if not now.. please quit soon.\r\n</P><P>\r\nsigh, the birds are chirping.. i better go to bed.\r\n</P>'),(865,'<P>i was just talking to kdog on the phone.  her dad is on hard times, big surprise.  he has it worse than anyone i know though.  he has had five traffic tickets in the last month.  one of them was for honking at a police officer.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>last week, he hit a donkey on the highway.  there was a horse there too, but he missed the horse.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'m bored.  having just got off the phone with kdog, it is now 6:22 a.m.  being awake this early is boring.  when i first got here, i used to go bicycling when i woke up this early.  that sounds nice actually.  i want to take myself up on that.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>i should eat some musli first and maybe take a shower.  i dont like moving in the morning before i shower.  typing on the computer is not considered moving.  leaving the house definately is.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>kdog heard a rumor that i am coming to austin.  maybe i am.  i figure it would cost me somewhere in the nieghborhood of 400 dollars in gas.  that is a large figure.  really i cant afford it.  i would be busted by the time i got back.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>damn, i need to find some work.  it is true.  i need to slow the burn.</P>\r\n<P>6:28 a.m.</p>'),(866,'<p>\r\nwe were twenty minutes out of ottz and i got a 295$ speeding ticket. that was at 4:40am tuesday. we arrived in new bedford. spent the night. went to hyannisport and spent a couple of days there. the beach. main st. the motor inn. three of us for the price of two. woke up every morning around six am and went to dunkin donuts and got a large coffee and usa today. read sip wait. woke the girls up at nine and the day usually started with a few showers and some grumbling giggling and suntan lotion applications. the trip was fun. it was really fun. there was some squabbling but nothing major. well only once. one big fight. yelling. i smoked eighteen basic ultra lites on the bench outside the room. imported molson canadian. we drove back through north conway n.h and we lived free. i am now beyond broke but the trip was good for me. i let go of some things and i feel good about it. my hair is blonde. \r\n</p>'),(867,'<p>The dream: I\'m in a classroom of some kind, it\'s crowded. I\'m sitting next to a woman I know well, I\'m guessing Jelu. As she\'s talking to me, it becomes clear that the world is somehow split into two opposing factions trying to destroy eachother. I am representing one, Jelu the other.</p>\r\n<p>Apparently my faction exists in secrecy. She is accusing us of launching missiles at innocent people -- estimates between 10-20 million. I say that she doesn\'t understand.</p>\r\n<p>There is only <i>one</i> weapon, I tell her.</p>\r\n<p>She asks me where it is.</p>\r\n<p>Even though she is the enemy, I\'m very aware of the fact that I love her and that I must tell her everything.</p>\r\n<p>Somehow, deep in the ocean, my government has found a way to <i>separate</i> part of the world from itself. A giant spherical submarine, easily the size of a large city, is down in the murk but at the same time <i>not</i> down there. It exists in space only metaphorically -- the space it truly takes up is somehow carved out of the universe, making it undetectable. From within this virtual space, we have made a weapon, a single weapon that can touch the entire world.</p>\r\n<p>I tell her this, all of this, because I love her. I don\'t remember what her reaction is, but I\'m pretty certain that she will use the information against me. I don\'t care. She will survive and that\'s all that matters.</p>\r\n<p>That\'s the dream.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(868,'<p>\r\nshe makes me so happy and sad all at once these days. I think about both sides yet rest on the negative. the money and job factor doesn\'t help but for now ill let them slide. she thinks the whole \"i want a gf\" factor is silly, yet i dont think she understands that humans, like dogs, are social creatures. creatures that like to nuzzle and pet each other; maybe sniff a crotch here and there. I sit in bed cross legged and i wish for things that appear in my mind. in my mind they are graceful and smooth like new roads in spring, yet when i open my eyes all i see is road work and detours. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na friend said in an email that i seemed better and i laughed at that. it was an uneasy laugh. a laugh to shrug the truth off. i quit smoking pot and now the flood gates of emotion have opened up and i dont quite know how to channel the increasingly strong currents. I am strong and dont think twice about quitting. Its something i should have done a long time ago. I did it for her in the beginning but now i know it was the right choice for ME to make for MYSELF.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"maybe you aren\'t working hard enough\" was suggested by her. a cliche. a cliche on life and why life aint working out. perfection is arbitrary and yet all i think about is how perfect she is. how i like to listen to her talk and how i like the facial expressions she makes. how she sticks her tongue out when she laughs and buries her face into her arms when she is embarassed. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe\'s happy being single and im happy for her. i really really am. she\'s had a lot happen to her in the past year and its good to know she is happy. i really do mean that and yet i find myself trying to defend that happiness i feel towards her. i try and legitimize everything as though it were some kind of national policy, buried deep within government papers. sifting through all the words and clauses i find myself lost in many ratifications and ammendmants. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is a cloud over me, dinosaur green. all the colours of the rainbow mixed into one. like in kindergarden. dinosaur green. \r\n</p> '),(869,'<p>I just spent a depressing day with my sad boyfrind, on the occasion of his birthday.<br>\r\nHe frowned a lot and moped a lot and generally felt miserable all day.\r\nIs this the karma i deserve for inflicting my depression on him a year ago?  I don\'t think it has anything to do with it.<br>\r\nRather, this is the very reason we are together, indeed that any couple is together for a long time...you can console each other through the rough spots.  And I suppose his spots are getting rough like the ocean in winter.  Mine, when visible, were drab like dried snot on a baby--easily wiped off, but quickly returned.</p><p>\r\n\r\nI am not as good a consoler as I\'d like.  I give up easily and mope myself when the first attempts fail or are rebuffed. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI made a cake and it broke when I took it out of the pan, only to find it still runny inside and kinda brown on the bottom.  I wasn\'t exactly pleased, and released a loud expletive when it happened.  His nerves were shot, and he let me know that my perceived tragedy over his birthday cake was nowhere near his personal hell.<br>\r\nIt made me feel worse, so I responded by taking a big bite of the unfrosted cake.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThat was all I needed.  Once the cake was completed, rebaked and cooling, we ate it with glee.  Well, with a light heart and sticky fingers, which is all you can ask for on a birthday where money is MIA, and sadness abounds.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMy gift to him? (besides the sticky banana cake?) Happy morning sex, a backrub, and my support.  Is that good enough?  I think so. \r\n</p><p>\r\nNext year I\'m buying him a pony.</p>'),(870,'<P>i have a degenerative sleeping disorder.  it is ten thirty one at night and i am ready to hit the hay.  i am so tired, that right now i am trying really hard to not rhyme my sentences.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni have three job prospects as i see it.  one is with a contact who is trying to create a job which he feels would make his own job easier, and place me in it.  on job as a nebulous expanding tech galaxy astronomer ensuring the planets collide for this company.  and then the most favored independent work from home and get payed way more job.  one should pan out.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy mom read this stuff.  she said, \"some of it i dont need to know.\"  true enough.  hi mom.  i am okay.  i am not doing drugs.  i have that sleep problem though.</P>\r\n<!--\r\ni have a new friend to trade sexy emails with.\r\n-->\r\n<P>\r\nreading snow crash is fun.  weeb made me read it.  darwin thinks it sucks dick.  xep likes it very much.  i wish there was an area, a mailing list or something that i could read darwin flaming the books that i read.  and then weeb getting angry and testy and flaming darwin back twice as bad.  and xep adding a well placed half paragraph to the thread.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><a href=\"http://armyofle.net\">armyofle.net</a> is up thanks to the innate server prowess of the yeti.   if you want a no cost unreliable shell on a linux box with no tech support, just let me know, <a href=\"mailto:austin@swinney.org\">austin@swinney.org</a>.  it is good for bitchx on irc and email.  other stuff too, due to its unregulated nature.  hell, you could take over the world maybe.</P>\r\n'),(871,'<p>\r\ni am lost.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou aren\'t.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nremember that time we laughed?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(872,'I am currently planning my 21st birthday party.  I will be having a cocktail evening, and as such, I\'m trying to figure out just what I should  be serving. What are your suggestions?'),(873,'<p>\r\nfirst, what is depression supposedly? two things according to psychology as it is taught today\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1 - chemicals in the brain affect mood, and some people have a naturally low level of these chemicals, and can benefit from drugs that improve the production of them... They help improve emotions in general this way. It helps get rid of all negative emotions in general. this means anti-depressants can actually be used to help get rid of any negative emotions, whether they are repessed or not, although it does the best job on sadness, and depression... emotions that do not also involve other chemicals in the brain... for example anger includes many other chemicals, which is why one is inclined to react rather than hide... anti-depressents don\'t do a good job of stopping anger, but they do repress it some too... anti-depressants effect all emotions, whether from just a natural imbalance or not.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n2 - depression not created by a natural chemical imbalance is repressed emotions. depression acts essentially as a negative emotion around whatever other emotions it is hiding. this can be easier and not as bitingly painful as the other emotions would be. depression can mask negative AND positive emotions. therefore taking anti-depressants does not strictly get rid of depression. anti-depressants can in a sense repress depression, sadness, anger, longing, lonliness, love, and any other emotion that is either negative, or that might get wrapped inside of depression by someone trying to not feel biting hurt. Love can hurt when you are far from the one you love...\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/873_1.jpg\'>\r\n<P>\r\nI have called myself \'depressed\' many times in the past...  But more often than not, I knew what emotions were effecting me...  I took anti-depressants once...  And I was a damn happy little shit...  But I also didn\'t CARE...  I wasn\'t concerned...  And that ain\'t me...  I\'ve seen it in others, also, when they are on depressants...  I did some things while I was on anti-depressants I never would\'ve done normally...  And honestly, I don\'t regret that at all...  I have no judgement here...  I think they can be useful...  hell, sometimes I have been tempted to take them again...  But there\'s a lot of things in this life that can give you a natural effect similar to what anti-depressants give you...  Talk to someone wonderful...  make some art... look at the skyline... listen to some music...  realize how long you\'ve wandered through the strange big world, and how many amazing things you\'ve already seen, and therefore how much more you\'re obviously going to see...  lol... when you think about it it can be a bit overwhelming, and maybe make you wanna shut off again...  But ain\'t it beautiful?  And dammit, even when it\'s bad... it\'s beautiful...  revel in the horror...\r\n</p>'),(874,'<P>Is anyone aware of an existing project to archive old web publications?  I haven\'t been able to locate one.  I (rather surprisingly) had two back to back requests for content from my ancient, ancient webzine and I had to reply that said webzine had vanished in a mid-twenties mood swing.  I wonder how many of my favorite sites have gone the same way.  Remember all those innovative sites that had mile-long URLs with pages.prodigy.com or some Midwestern college.edu/~something?  Did anyone keep those?  By the time the mainstream libraries appreciate the historical literary significance of the Crash Site or VasEzine, will they still be on a functioning hard drive somewhere?  Tips appreciated.</P>'),(875,'<P>This morning, as I watched the bmg sweep the hardwoods of our shared central Austin duplex, I caught him whistling the Marine Hymn (\"...we will fight Our Country\'s battles on the air, land and the sea.\") I was about to tease him about it when he proceeded to chase a pillbug and a dead worm with the broom and dustpan. (What, with the rain and all we get lots of visitors from another species.) In all the excitement! the subject changed.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Later, as I was getting ready for work I had that damn hymn stuck in my head.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks Mr. Marine!\" I hollered at him from my room.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks for what?\" he hollered back.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks for getting the Marine song stuck in my head.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>The bmg appears at my door, looking confused.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"When you were sweeping...?\" I prompted.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Nothing.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"While you were sweeping you were whistling the Marine song...?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"I was?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Mmm hmm, I was about to say something to you about it and then the subject changed.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>The bmg: surprise, confusion.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"You don\'t remember?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"No.\"  He thinks for a moment.  \"I was humming?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Whistling.\"  I watch the cogs and wheels spin in his head.  \"You don\'t remember?\"  Dazed, he shakes his head.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>After silently pondering the wonders of the human mind for a moment we both begin to wander off to whatever we had been doing before.  The bmg stops:</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"I used to LOVE sweeping duty in the Marines...in bootcamp,\" he recalls with rapture and relish.  He mimes sweeping motions and makes with his mouth the soothing \"tchhh, tchhh\" sound of a broom sliding across the earth.  \"Two hours of peace.  And nobody would fuck with you.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Then, with the quiet, serious tone of a True Believer he says: \"It was better than church.\"</P>'),(876,'<P>This morning, as I watched the bmg sweep the hardwoods of our shared central Austin duplex, I caught him whistling the Marine Hymn (\"...we will fight Our Country\'s battles on the air, land and the sea.\") I was about to tease him about it when he proceeded to chase a pillbug and a dead worm with the broom and dustpan. (What, with the rain and all we get lots of visitors from another species.) In all the excitement! the subject changed.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Later, as I was getting ready for work I had that damn hymn stuck in my head.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks Mr. Marine!\" I hollered at him from my room.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks for what?\" he hollered back.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Thanks for getting the Marine song stuck in my head.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>The bmg appears at my door, looking confused.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"When you were sweeping...?\" I prompted.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Nothing.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"While you were sweeping you were whistling the Marine song...?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"I was?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Mmm hmm, I was about to say something to you about it and then the subject changed.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>The bmg: surprise, confusion.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"You don\'t remember?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"No.\"  He thinks for a moment.  \"I was humming?\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"Whistling.\"  I watch the cogs and wheels spin in his head.  \"You don\'t remember?\"  Dazed, he shakes his head.</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>After silently pondering the wonders of the human mind for a moment we both begin to wander off to whatever we had been doing before.  The bmg stops:</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>\"I used to LOVE sweeping duty in the Marines...in bootcamp,\" he recalls with rapture and relish.  He mimes sweeping motions and makes with his mouth the soothing \"tchhh, tchhh\" sound of a broom sliding across the earth.  \"Two hours of peace.  And nobody would fuck with you.\"</P>\r\n<P></P>\r\n<P>Then, with the quiet, serious tone of a True Believer he says: \"It was better than church.\"</P>'),(877,'<p>\r\nThe little silicon-based life-forms need love too...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt my first SF start-up company, where I learned I could act like me, look how I wanted, and be a total nut, I called myself \'the keeper of the silicon-based life-forms\'...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey are little children...  My server was a bad little girl last weekend...  Tender has been living up in SF for about a year and a half, with no problems...  I had to bring her home to give her a spanking for breaking, and then give her lots of TLC to show her that she was still loved, and wanted, but now she knows she still has a job to do, and she will go back up to SF soon...  probably tommorow...  Perhaps I will give her a reward soon, and let her live in a co-lo facility, where she can have more friends other than just rapture.xtreme.com...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere were some people in our company, in the Hong Kong office, who didn\'t like us white guys in the US telling them what to do...  they would refer to us as \'gewlios\' which I believe should really be phoenetically \'gwailos\'...  The thought we were telling them what to do, but we\'re there to give them knowledge, and they don\'t get it...  Wuite a few of us are people who were building the foundation of what is currently called the \'Internet\' as far back as 12-16 years ago...  But apparently many of them still knew that we were clueless...  This was very funny though...  There is a site called IceRed, where many things about Hong Kong and Singapore are discussed...  Some of these fellows don\'t like my brother...  He\'s based out of the Hong Kong office...  Here\'s what they had to say:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"IntheKnow Posted - 15 February 2001\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\nI agree that iAsiaWorks are a total wasted of space.... Randy Bias is a total prick... typical Silicon twat!!!!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is typical grammar, you have to get used to interpreting what they are getting at...  I think this is hilarious...  My brau, is a lousy Silicon Valley Penis, and Pussy!  He can do himself!  What luck!  ;)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe ignored the posting...  But I later decided to respond after it was announced we were going to do layoffs, cause I didn\'t give a fuck...  My oh so witty reply:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"Rusty Bias Posted - 3 April 2001\r\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\nIf Randy gets to be the Silicon Twat, can I be the breast implants, PLEASE??!?!?!?!?!?!? \"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDoes anyone think that there might be people out there who get labia implants?!?!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nroflmao\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis IceRed page, for reference, to back up my little amusing tale, is:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhttp://www.icered.com/work/IT/topic.asp?Topic_ID=5322\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/877_1.jpg\'>'),(878,'Today, love is a crime and my punishment for having hope is a face full of lies and a lonely heart. \r\n\r\n\"So here we are, stuck in hell; same old game we know it well.\" ~D.H.\r\n\r\nThey say that when you have nothing else, you still have hope.\r\n\r\n'),(879,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/879_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>My good friend past away this past saturday.</p><p>\r\ni am sad, just sad. i am not angry, i am not crying - i am just sad. a bit of pain, thinking that i have turned down his offers to go out drinking with him. wishing that i would have stopped what i thought was more important and just spent a little more time with him.</p>'),(880,'<p>last night i heard oliver stone say of charlie sheen, \"he became more insensate as his focus became more materialistic.\"  it\'s not a spectacular insight, by any means, but i love the way it\'s worded.  i like words.</p>\r\n<p>i don\'t eat as much this week as i did last week...and this is a good thing.  in some ways i am more healthy than i was when the year started.  in many ways i am less at ease.  <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=875.txt\">kdog\'s</a> kind of peace is the kind i seek.  it\'s a peace that doesn\'t really go anywhere, that stays with you in every action and movement.  it is like a deity, were it not for the fact that no idol can ever accurately represent it, not can any concept ever correctly frame it.  it exists outside of definition.</p>\r\n<p>when the philosophers speak of non-duality, my head always tilts to one side, like a dog\'s does sometimes.  to exist in a state where the apprehension of a perceiver and an object of perception is transcended, or overcome, or lost, or whatever, is what it means to be god.  to fully understand only the present instant.  to perceive only what is, and not what is conjured, imagined, or conceded:</p>\r\n<p><i>this is the peace i long for</i></p>'),(881,'<p> right now, i have a few cool projects that i\'m working on and they all make me wish that i had 8 more hours in my day.  so, i\'m determined to be able to start working for myself or for a company that i help start.<br>\r\ni\'m starting to learn perl (slowly) and i\'m working on a few geek projects that keep me quite happy, but my social life is killing them dead on. <br>\r\nhow do you maintain a social life and manage all of your projects outside of work at once?  do i go out less?  do i try to combine group activities?  do i geek out with groups of people?  how does this work?<br>\r\nhelp :)\r\n</P>'),(882,'<P>heated weights and repair signs for rent. a city made of old photographs \r\nand drunken slurs. broken and beige with a smell of burnt hair and tree \r\nsap.</P><P>\r\ni want to be lost there, tucked away inside a manila folder sitting \r\nagainst a painted curb. inside, sealed and tight. suffocating to the \r\nsounds of my breathing breathe, bouncing off the paper and back into my \r\nface. tickling my eyelashes. claustrophobic and snug like. i want \r\nsomeone to send my manila folder life to Manila, Phillipines. where my \r\npackage will hopefully be found and opened by anxious and starving small \r\nfillipino boys. longing for a good thumb wrestling match.</P>\r\n<P>\r\nTUBE\r\n</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/882_1.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n'),(883,'<html>\r\n<head>\r\n<title>Untitled Document</title>\r\n<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Type\" content=\"text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\">\r\n<style type=\"text/css\">\r\n<!--\r\nbody {  font-family: Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; line-height: 23.5px; text-align: justify; font-size: 10px}\r\np {  font-family: Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; line-height: 23.5px; text-align: justify; font-size: 10px}\r\n-->\r\n</style>\r\n<script>\r\n\r\n// =======================================\r\n// set the following variables\r\n// =======================================\r\n\r\n// Set slideShowSpeed (milliseconds)\r\nvar slideShowSpeed = 5000\r\n\r\n// Duration of crossfade (seconds)\r\nvar crossFadeDuration = 5\r\n\r\n// Specify the image files\r\nvar Pic = new Array() // don\'t touch this\r\n// to add more images, just continue\r\n// the pattern, adding to the array below\r\n\r\nPic[0] = \'http://www.achtungbaby.org/1.jpg\'\r\nPic[1] = \'http://www.achtungbaby.org/2.jpg\'\r\nPic[2] = \'http://www.achtungbaby.org/3.jpg\'\r\n\r\n\r\n// =======================================\r\n// do not edit anything below this line\r\n// =======================================\r\n\r\nvar t\r\nvar j = 0\r\nvar p = Pic.length\r\n\r\nvar preLoad = new Array()\r\nfor (i = 0; i < p; i++){\r\n   preLoad[i] = new Image()\r\n   preLoad[i].src = Pic[i]\r\n}\r\n\r\nfunction runSlideShow(){\r\n   if (document.all){\r\n      document.images.SlideShow.style.filter=\"blendTrans(duration=2)\"\r\n      document.images.SlideShow.style.filter=\"blendTrans(duration=crossFadeDuration)\"\r\n      document.images.SlideShow.filters.blendTrans.Apply()      \r\n   }\r\n   document.images.SlideShow.src = preLoad[j].src\r\n   if (document.all){\r\n      document.images.SlideShow.filters.blendTrans.Play()\r\n   }\r\n   j = j + 1\r\n   if (j > (p-1)) j=0\r\n   t = setTimeout(\'runSlideShow()\', slideShowSpeed)\r\n}\r\n</script>\r\n\r\n</head>\r\n\r\n\r\n<body onload=\"runSlideShow()\">  \r\n<div align=\"center\">\r\n  <table width=\"400\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" mm:layoutgroup=\"true\" height=\"500\">\r\n    <tr> \r\n      <td width=\"2\" height=\"12\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"199\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"134\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"1\"></td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n    <tr> \r\n      <td height=\"437\" width=\"2\"> \r\n        <p>Â </p>\r\n      </td>\r\n      <td width=\"199\"> \r\n        <p>I know every crease in her voice.<br>\r\n          I can hear her now saying<br>\r\n          \"Is this fucking charming<br>\r\n          or a charming fuck ?\"<br>\r\n        </p>\r\n        <p>============== </p>\r\n        <p>Or maybe Iâ€™ll just take the shortcut<br>\r\n          between the spirit and the flesh,<br>\r\n          and punch someone in the face,<br>\r\n          because human beings havenâ€™t come that far </p>\r\n        <p>=============</p>\r\n        <p>and in this way, what we are practicing <br>\r\n          is suffering,<br>\r\n          which everybody practices,<br>\r\n          but strangely few of us<br>\r\n          grow graceful in.</p>\r\n        <p>last two bits by T. Hoagland</p>\r\n        </td>\r\n      <td width=\"134\"> \r\n        <table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\r\n          <tr> \r\n            <td id=\"VU\" height=150 width=150> <img src=\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/1.jpg\" name=\'SlideShow\' ></td>\r\n          </tr>\r\n        </table>\r\n      </td>\r\n      <td valign=\"top\" width=\"1\" bordercolor=\"#000000\">Â  </td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n    <tr> \r\n      <td height=\"19\" width=\"2\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"199\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"134\"></td>\r\n      <td width=\"1\"></td>\r\n    </tr>\r\n  </table>\r\n</div>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(884,'<p>\r\ni tried to sleep but my tossing and turning would not let me lay still. sitting up crosslegged i ponder things and then laying back down i ponder more. i tried to cry. i think i need to. i think i need to just let something out. something big. something big just waiting for the right moment to jump out and scare the fuck out of me. like when i was a kid. covers on my feet. the covers protected me from that man who was going to grab my feet if they became uncovered. i crave things i dont want and cant have. two things. one i dont want because of the other and the other i cant have because of lots of reasons. i love the laugh. i love the smile i love the way it all fits together. im a man of my word and my word will prevail. its up to them now. its up to the ones ive given good reason to make up minds and for choices i wish i didnt have to make i know that answers wont come without some sort of confusion frustration and benign delight. i have never let my shadow answer and my point of view is clouded by uncertain thoughts and uncertain futures. the only thing that is certain is that i do not want past mistake made again and i tremble thinking that is impossible. \r\n</p>'),(885,'<p>So I was chatting on icb.  Then stuff happened.  I forgot about swinney.org and icb, for about two weeks.  So austin sends me this letter to kill my icb process because I\'m about to break the record idle time.  I forget about it for another few days. Then I log in an nuke it from (well not orbit but tcsh anyway).</p>\r\n<p>So did I win the most-idle title?</p>\r\n<p>:-)</p>\r\n<P>($stuff = \"Got a job.  Planned, conducted, and wrote report on large chem experiment in four days (I now know mo\' about olive oil than any 300 people you could pick off the street).  Got an apt.  Job and apt. are in a city 2 & 1/2 hours away from Austin.  Drive back and forth to that city 4 times in about 12 days, listening to crappy radio b/c the tape deck has been busted since 1993.  Read Read Read.  Study Study Study.  Highlight.  Freak out about school, then unfreak becuase I realize that I don\'t care about it.  Drink beer. Eat cheap mexican food.  Eat good mexican food when IRS check comes in.  Etc. Etc. Contemplate filling out CS final in crayon just to piss the stupid prof off one last time before I go.\")</p>'),(886,'<P>Well, Orion\'s off to Houston. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>...and after two solid months of looking for work, I may be as well. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ve lived in Aus 17 years now, and it\'s my home. Since I have no family to speak of, it\'s more than my home -- but there simply doesn\'t seem to be any work here -- unless your a Linux Kernel hacker with over 10yr exp (at least Linus knows he\'s got a job if Transmeta tanks).</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Every day, recruiters call me, saying \"Are you sure you don\'t want to take something in Houston or Dallas?\" I\'m no longer sure.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>All the local business owners tell me that their business is down. People aren\'t spending, not shopping. I get the sense that if I leave for a year or two, a lot of what I like may no longer be here when I get back.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Swinney always used to joke and tell me I\'m famous. It\'s just that I\'ve lived downtown here for so long, that I\'ve gotten to know everyone. I go to the Sonic and people in the drive-thru are honking their horns and waving. That shit really makes it hard to have a bad day.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>Fuck.</P>'),(887,'Well, Orion\'s off to Houston. \r\n\r\n<p>...and after two solid months of looking for work, I may be as well. \r\n\r\n<p>I\'ve lived in Aus 17 years now, and it\'s my home. Since I have no family to speak of, it\'s more than my home -- but there simply doesn\'t seem to be any work here -- unless your a Linux Kernel hacker with over 10yr exp (at least Linus knows he\'s got a job if Transmeta tanks).\r\n\r\n<p>Every day, recruiters call me, saying \"Are you sure you don\'t want to take something in Houston or Dallas?\" I\'m no longer sure.\r\n\r\n<p>All the local business owners tell me that their business is down. People aren\'t spending, not shopping. I get the sense that if I leave for a year or two, a lot of what I like may no longer be here when I get back.\r\n\r\n<p>Swinney always used to joke and tell me I\'m famous. It\'s just that I\'ve lived downtown here for so long, that I\'ve gotten to know everyone. I go to the Sonic and people in the drive-thru are honking their horns and waving. That shit really makes it hard to have a bad day.\r\n\r\n<p>Fuck.'),(888,'<p>\r\ni walked down bank street looking for drink. i found myself at the duke. i knew id end up there. i had left the apartment with that destination in mind. the five minute walk seems much longer than it actually is. the familiar shops hold no secrets and even if they did, being hidden for five years makes those secrets hard to find. I didnt expect the crowd to be so big. it was almost packed. the raptors and leafs game were on and so i guess all those starving students without cable were there to take the bread and circus\'s in with some good ol drink and cheer. the leafs are losing. the raptors are within 2 points. i dont care. at the end of the day they still goto bed and wake up no matter if they win or lose. winning and losing is a part of life and in sports its just a statistic. like african famine victims in the mass media. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsaw my table and walked to the bar. the one who is hot took my order. a pint of carlsburg and two shots of yager. i sat down. soon after the drink came. downed the shots in almost as many seconds as it takes to think of your own name. gulped half the pint. ordered another shot.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe third shot took a little longer than expected to show up. my impatience showing through the constant movement of my legs. up. down. up. down. as rapid as the micromachine man talked. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere was a table of good looking women infront of me. they looked shallow and conceited in their dress and movements. i took notice of their hair and eyes and lips but stopped at that. that table isnt for me i say to myself as though i was thinking about going upto them and saying something. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni down the third shot and finish my beer. drop twenty two(cdn) and get the hell out of dodge. six dollar tip for the hottie who never asks questions. i drink alone and too many it seems wrong, but to me it seems normal. whats the difference? i sleep alone. i shower alone. i laugh alone. i play alone. i am alone. why is drinking alone a sin? it is because drinking is social phenominons(sp). fuck social constructs and fuck what people think about me and the way i act and what i do and what i have done. brisk exit. i dont want to see or hear what people say. their conversations seem so futile and simple. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni ponder the possibilities of impossibilities and in the end i lose. the impossibilities are deep within and the possibilities are far from real. real in the sense of being feasable. whats feasable these days?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy heart goes out to empty ears and to empty emotions. do they understand? do they concur? do i live in reality? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif i were pez(if comments begin on this subject i will vomit) my stem would be gray and the head would be of something lifeless. something that lies still and does not utter a word. something simple and uncompromisable. pez sucks. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe days grow longer and yet sleep is in my mind almost all day. however when nightfall comes there is no chance.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>i foget the feeling of the old routine but god damn i crave it. i crave the scent and i crave the undecribable feeling i get when boris talks to me. boris is dead. i killed him. fuck boris. why did/does boris have such a hold on me? why does she have such a hold on me? an involuntary hold on me for i know if she could she would let go. she has. i havnt. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere seems to be something about the whole thing that puzzles me. i fear the unexpected and i hope for the unrealistic. i hope i hope i choke up with tears that refuse to come out. the bags under my eyes are full of tears and for whatever reason they refuse to leave. they refuse to leave me like a subdermal zit refuses to pop.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy mind is a flutter of activity. seconds of the day turn into hours and hours turn into weeks. i speak the same thing every time i write a swintry, yet everytime i read over what iv written the meaning is different. do i make sense to people? do i fucking make sense?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni should have had one more shot, but to be honest i really didnt feel like waking up tomorrow in a pool of my own drewl trying to remember if i vomitted or not. last time that happened i found my t-shirt covered in it and the bathroom smelt horrid. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nno matter if i drink or if i dont i wake up with a splitting head-ache and a feeling of total defeat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni wake up slow and sulky. the shower helps and the playlist that replaces the other one opens my eyes. classical at night. punk in the morning. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce on a canoe trip at camp we went through a petrafied forest. the stumps were erie and cold. i felt at home. paddle on camper. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(889,'<p>Yesterday I played at a club in Oakland. A <a href=http://www.animevigilante.net>friend</a> of mine described me as \"bitter\".</p>\r\n<p>I almost argued but I think she\'s right. I think when people get hurt they look for reasons. When they can\'t find reasons, they hold the hurt inside of them and become bitter. The problem with this is that I want <b>good</b> reasons and I only find <b>real</b> reasons.</p>\r\n<p>I am a bitter person.</p>\r\n<p>Bob Dylan says :</p>\r\n<p>\"Well, I\'ve never gotten used to it /<br>\r\nI just learned to turn it off /<br>\r\nEither I\'m too sensitive /<br>\r\nOr else I\'m getting soft.\"</p>\r\n<p>So I was thinking about this bitterness and I searched the InterWeb. I found the <a href=http://www.angelfire.com/me2/bitter4u/ask.html>\"Ask BitterGurl\"</a> page, which appears to have lots of 14 year old visitors. I think the bitterness is both a protection mechanism and a very real result of hurt overload. I\'m going to be thinking a bit about how the bitterness serves me and how I can be realistic without being bitter.</p>\r\n<p>I am learning from <a href=http://www.omulu.org>Capoeira</a> that there are different ways of being strong. There are different kinds of pain. The kind of pain when your callouses are peeling off and every step hurts is one. The kind of pain when you are actually injured is another. The callous pain is a part of playing Capoeira and it is unavoidable and unresolvable. What I\'m trying to say is that if something hurts but you know you\'re not injured, does it really hurt?</p>\r\n<p>Should my <a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=Weltschmerz\">weltschmerz</a> add to the weight of my bitterness? What does it accomplish for me? Do I really need bitterness to protect myself? I think I will concentrate on using my good judgement, and breathe the bitterness away.</p>\r\n<p>Either that, or I\'ll ask BitterGurl what she thinks.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin \r\n'),(890,'<p>\r\nSitting at my computer, chatting away with two old friends online, I realized that I\'m not what I had hoped to be.\r\nCurrently I make my wages as an electrician, and before that I worked the midnight shift at a convenience store.  At various times I\'ve\r\nworked as a bodyguard, investigator, information broker, factory worker, janitor, and even had a stint in retail management.  I threw away\r\ntwo chances at college, both were excellent schools.  And if i\'d meant good i\'d have said good.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn between splurges of typing to my friends and reading their replies I worked on a curriculum for the self-defense school i\'m hoping to\r\nstart.  My roomates would periodically come in and bother me about one thing or another.  And the whole damn time, i couldn\'t shake the\r\nfeeling that i\'ve become something like one of Robert Heinlein\'s male protagonists.  I\'m the self-made man, the tough guy.  Everyone\r\ncomes to me for advice or aid.  Friends in need are always succored.  And so on.  Somewhere not all that long ago, call it 12 years ago,\r\ni began to alter myself.  I made terrible changes to my fundamental self.  From a shy little kid i turned aggresive.  I drank and did drugs\r\ngot in fights.  Started to hang out with older kids.  Got into punk and then the skinhead movement.  Nah, i wasn\'t racist, but who cares;\r\nmost skins i\'ve known are scum no matter what their professed beliefs.  I became a hard man.  But my fantasies always revolved around science,\r\nhacking code, being a geek.  While some in my life today look at me in envy...seeing a dangerous scarred up fighter, I spend my idle time\r\nwishing i\'d kept going as a geek.  I love geeks.  My best friends are pretty geeky.  And I wish i could be like that again.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI might be a different person if i hadn\'t, for instance, joined the militia.  The militia was good for me, cleaned me up, helped me find\r\nmyself.  But who was i?  I was someone who knew how to destroy.  I could create nothing.  I still roleplayed, I was active in the SCA, I\r\nnever stopped reading like a man possesed.  But much of my time went towards becoming a better \"soldier\", tougher, more dangerous.  What a\r\nwaste.  Now I spend my time alone for the most part.  Mocked by my friends for believing in the Constitution.  Laughed at for being such\r\nan honor bound anachronism.  For my part, i can\'t stand most humans.  My love life, when it exists at all, is a complete disaster.  I\'ll\r\nlikely never have the family i always dreamt of.  And i make a shit wage for working in a place where i\'m called \'nigger-jew\'.  Gods above,\r\nif you value your sanity, never work in the construction industry.  Anyway.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nToday i have nothing i really want.  I hate my job, i hate the city i live in, and i suppose i hate myself.  I often wonder if it\'s too late\r\nto be a geek.  There was once a time in life when i could explain code far better than i could teach someone to shoot a pistol or kill\r\nwith one blow.  Soon i\'ll be moving to a dangerous area of the city, and my roomate, Skip, wants me to teach him to use his new pistol.  \r\nAnd the shotgun.  Should i feel pride in being able to upgrade a person\'s chances of survival in dangerous situations?  or only this sense\r\nof utter failure for knowing little more than how to kill?  it\'s a question i need to come to terms with soon i think.\r\n<p>'),(891,'<p>\r\nGoing through trash left over from a relationship which ended a few months ago, I discovered something which i\'d written.  One night, this girl i was dating, as we walked in the rain, asked me to write her something.  \"what?\" i asked.  \"anything.\"  Anything.  Well okay.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt some point i always look at these women i meet.  No matter whether we become romanticaly involved or not.  I look at them and think:  \"wow, i really despise this creature.\"  Always this seems to happen, even when everything seems to be going well.  And they look at all my poetry and my essays and my fiction...and want something just for them.  So trying to ignore the voice in my head telling me how much i really don\'t like them, even though i do, i write.  Let me be clear about something, i should not be allowed to write anything for a girlfriend...as you\'ll see...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider if you wish, that i am not the man you assume.  that what i feel is nothing like what i say.  that my words are no more than my heart...empty and fleeting.  i chose my path so very young, but it might very well not be the path you think.  i am a lie.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neverything you think about me is wrong.  every story you hear is not the whole.  every smile is false and every tear contrived.  beliefs are just memes, and morals a description of fears.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider that we are not friends.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat i lost the ability to discern between lust and love so long ago i had yet to learn the meanings of either.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider that you can trust me no more than you can trust anyone.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat truth to me is malleable.  hatred is love.  and love impossible.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndeath has been a companion to me.  striking down those around me while i watch from the wings.  unable perhaps unwilling to stay the scythe\'s swing.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider well that in your knowledge of me you are mistaken.  that you cannot ever know me because to know me would be too much.  understanding can be only achieved in hating me. hatred brings the clarity needed to view hypocrisy as it is.  morals as they are.  beliefs as a motive force in the life of a creature so hateful of himself that only through love of others can he stand another waking moment.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto sleep...can you believe that is what i wish for?  a good night, not fitful and filled with fragmented memories of pain and spite and fear playing across my mind\'s eye until i can take no more and awake sweating in the dark.  alone.  screaming silently in the darkness of my room.  knowing that a simple touch could calm my aching brain and set the pressure of my veins aright.  a touch that will not come.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider that i had everything i wanted.  and lost it. i had that fucking touch.  the one thing which could always bring calm where only storm had been before.  which could bring a sincere smile to lips so often set in a frown.  eyes frosted over in joy thought impossible.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwatching myself see a smile so wonderful to behold...watching a love i never truly lived until it was gone.  hoping for another chance.  this time i\'d notice my feelings, enjoy them for their reality.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni cannot help what i am.  i fought hard to be something other than i was and i\'m so very weary of fighting.  rather death than one more conflict.  i wish to never raise my hand in anger, nor my voice.  to lie amongst the worms, feeding lives worth more than mine.  any who look upon me and think i need to continue must not love me very well.  i\'m done.  worn down by my own thoughts.  no longer able to take any pride even in the struggle,  or the morals, or the \'cause\'.  mocked by even my closest fellows for being this fucking thing.  a laughingstock for simple minds, and for complex ones.  a simple man i am, turned from the face of beauty by stolen happiness and dashed hopes.  reaching in desperation for that last chance at sanity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconsider that i can even feel my sanity slipping from my grasp.  that you are accellerating this process.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni would rather die than let you have the victory.  you and i are at war.  i can\'t win and i can\'t fight to a draw, but i can get out.  i will.  when dignity is gone, and hope, and pride.  what choice do i have?  silent as always i see.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith you it\'s always the same eh?  if i speak a simple but powerful truth you have nothing to offer.  no words, nothing.  yet your touch could render this whole page false.  how amusing then that you will be my destruction.  how very ironic.\r\n</p>'),(892,'<p>being a geek right now means either being unemployed in an area with too many other geeks or being overworked and underpaid in an under-served area.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have some silly work ethic which means i dedicate much more of my time than is healthy to working. i do it after hours at home, and on weekends as well. i take my laptop on vacation and refuse to go anywhere without my phone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on the other hand, i\'m trying to teach myself perl because i think it will be fun. hobby and work blend together so often that most of the time i don\'t know which i am doing. i learn by doing, so to learn perl i build tools for work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at work, we are the basement dwellers. we have no windows. we distract ourselves with dry ice and soda bottles. at the \"other\" (read: <i>corporate hq</i>) building, blue cross occupies floors 1-5. i have travelled on the elevator to our floor, the 6th floor, quite often over the past year. i had never seen anyone get off at floor three. all day long, people travel from 2->4 and 4-> 2, but nobody ever goes to 3. recently, i started asking these floors 2&4 people what is hidden there. i make reference to the conspiracy, the martians, the illegal testing, whatever. last week finally i saw someone push \'3\' and finally got to ask exactly what they did. surprise, surprise, they said, \"we run the computers.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>anyway, being one of the two old friends incitor referred to <a href=http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=890.txt>here</a>, i felt some need to reply. not that i have much to say, but there you go.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you want to be a geek? use vi and read _learning perl_. why be so negative about something that earns me (read:  <i>college dropout</i>) a decent living? i\'d rather be biking or hiking or kayaking or doing anything else\r\noutside. the only problem is i can\'t get paid to do those things. i just don\'t like being in the basement anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the news today: \"a controversial new study finds that some gays can go straight if they really want to, challenging previous views of homosexuality.\"</p>'),(893,'<p>siblings of same\r\nlost game</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hopscotch \r\ntoday</p>\r\n \r\n</p>old-school-lame-brain \r\nnever knowing\r\nwhat \r\nto say</p>\r\n\r\n<p>desperate claim, all shame\r\ngrasping \r\nneon fame</p>\r\n\r\n<p>always numb to the pain</p>\r\n\r\n<p>stained ideals \r\nmorphing \r\nsacred profanity \r\nspinning wheels</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a loveless bane \r\n10 o\'clock train</p>\r\n\r\n<p>always late\r\nerased tastes \r\nviolent \r\nplain vanilla</p>\r\n\r\n<p> . . . drowning ophelia</p>\r\n\r\n<p>pillows of flame \r\nutterly insane \r\nperforated membrane</p>\r\n\r\n<p>coveted \r\nhighspeed \r\nrabid</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just a prefix \r\namputated forgiveness</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a \r\nwelcomed\r\nabyss</p> \r\n'),(894,'<p><br>\r\n  <font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-1\">Only when you drink from the river of silence  <br>\r\nshall you indeed sing.<br>\r\nAnd when you have reached the mountain top, <br>\r\nthen you shall begin to climb. <br>\r\nAnd when the earth shall claim your limbs, <br>\r\nthen shall you truly dance.<br></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-1\">--Kahlil Gibran \r\n  <br>\r\n  The Prophet </font></p>\r\n'),(895,'<P>so our lease ends 3 wks before we can get into our new apt and there\'s no where to go cept the dorms.\r\nbegging gets us no where with the owners to extend our lease. they have hearts of stone cold molded over rocks.\r\ndorms to live sleep and shit in a tiny cubicle which you overpayed for. hell no\r\nim gonna se how bein homeless is like. i hear they make good money downtown.</P>'),(896,'i am a mom.  i am elly and mandy\'s mom.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni would like to get mother\'s day cards and presents, but if they don\'t come, i will not weep or anything, i am not much of a date person.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsome people get really out of whack if they don\'t get a date/event related gift on the exact day it should happen.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni have a more liberal view.  anything i buy or receive within a month or so of mom\'s day or my birthday or whatever is assigned the title of the event.  i have been buying myself mother\'s day presents since mid april and will continue on until mid june, and that\'s no bad thing.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(897,'<p>A partially-enclosed plaza of polished or enameled cement. Chairs, benches and tables of solid smooth concrete rise seamlessly from the plaza itself, it is all a single object.</p>\r\n<p>A girl I know is there, her name is Elizabeth. I haven\'t seen her in two years.</p>\r\n<p>She says something like: How are you? I\'m a nurse now.</p>\r\n<p>And I say something like: You always looked like one.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(898,'<a href=\"http://www.cm.utexas.edu/CH354L/Bersuker/final-su.pdf\">Prof. Ivan Bersuker\'s Physical Chemistry II final exam prep questions</a>'),(899,'<p>\r\nSo i woke up this morning, feeling good, still tired of course.  I spoke late into the night with good friends, the best of friends.  I vented myself of worries and anger and fear.  Slept well, no dreams, which is a good sign for me.  Grabbed breakfast, turned on the news, tied my boots and all too soon headed out the door.  On the way my roomate and I discussed politics, our usual morning fare.  I still felt good.  Tired but good.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI load up the work van while my crew leader bullshits with the other crew leaders.  I am not bitter, today, that he does this while i work.  His son, the other guy on the crew shows up and helps me.  I am not annoyed when he asks me if i\'ve done my job correctly.  I smile and eat a few peanut m&ms, a rare treat as i am on a high protein body building diet.  We sit and smoke for a few minutes, Duke and I, while Joe, his son chews Copenhagen.  We wait for word from the Boss as to whether i will be needed by another crew today.  Although i hope not, today, i don\'t stress about it.  I feel good, even though i\'m tired and at work.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nOn the way to the job site i sit and read _Children_of_Dune_, while Duke drives and Joe reads the paper.   My ass goes numb, but i ignore it and read, stopping now and again to take a drink of water.  As i read, i try to integrate the novel with my combat skills.  I firmly believe that anything can be applied to combat.  However, my thoughts keep moving to memories of Darwin and Dawn, my two best friends.  But today it doesn\'t sadden me that we cannot be together.  It\'s a pleasant drive.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWe work.  Lunch passes quickly and we head back into the house.  I\'m so tired now that if i don\'t stay busy i begin to drift off.  I fall asleep on my ladder at one point.  Duke kicks the ladder out from under me; \"nigger-jew!  wake the fuck up!\"  I fall to the ground, it hurts, but not terribly.  My face begins to burn, but i know myself well enough to know i\'m not flushed, i\'ve gone stark white.  I get up, my hand near my knife, \"Duke i warned you once before what would happen if you struck me.  Now apologize here and now or i\'ll have you begging for the chance!\"  That\'s when Joe steps forward.  We exchange words while the plumbers look on in bemusement.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter several minutes of argument, Joe swings for my face.  I move to the side a bit and help his arm along.  I shift my body and push him hard away from me at which point he throws his screwdriver at me.  In a fury I hurl my claw-hammer at him.  It misses, and flies into the brand new 480 volt panel we just finished putting up before lunch.  In horror i watch as it flies into the bus bars, and then my hammer, the panel, and all those expensive GE breakers disappear in a flash and a KABOOM.  I feel weak, and Joe\'s face is long with fear.  Duke looks as if he\'s going to have a coronary any second.  Then i realize how much trouble we\'re all in.  I begin to laugh.  I laugh so hard i can\'t stand it.  And Joe laughs.  And Duke laughs.  The plumbers watch us in awe, thinking no doubt that these electricians must be insane.  We\'re laughing, while a now smoldering ruin of a $3000 panel faces us.  Within minutes we calm down.  We all shake hands and go have a smoke.  When we come back in, Duke, using my phone calls the boss to tell him about our little accident.  Result?  I get a new claw hammer, and tomorrow we\'ll have to do it all over again.  The anger has been forgotten.  My life has become yet more surreal.  And i\'m now more tired than ever.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWriting this now, i try to think of a good reason for what happened.  If the hammer had hit Joe it would have split his skull.  And i was ready to draw my knife and put it in Duke if he came closer to me.  Being tired when you are a violent person can be pretty dangerous i guess.  What a day.  You know?  I\'m still in a good mood!  \r\n</p>'),(900,'<p>\r\ni really miss phish. i miss summer tour. driving all night through the desert. all the stars over head. dust and sand racing by. smoking and drinking coca-cola and watching for cops but still driving way too fast. and being in a town or city for a day, or perhaps only part of day. trying to get a quick orientation to get things you need. supplies. food. sleep.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand last year i didn\'t go. and this year there is no tour. and a show that i should have been at last year just started to play while i am sitting here working. and it made me sad because of all the year, tour was the best. running to as many shows as can be afforded.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand i think another reason it made me sad is that i know i am changing again. and i always feel myself the strongest when i listen to these recordings.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndon\'t get me wrong. i do love life and change and all that, i just feel that one life here is just too short. too short by a long shot.\r\n</p>'),(901,'<pre>\r\nwe were lying on the grass beneath the stars\r\nhaving made love earlier now no longer breathless\r\nshe moved her head on my chest, trying perhaps to look at my face\r\nher arm brushed along my thigh, a lazy fingertipped touch\r\na short sound came from her throat, half a sigh and half a query\r\n\"lover,\" she said to me then, \"what is it you see?\"\r\n\r\n\r\ni thought of women raped in public while no one helped\r\ni witness chilren sold to pedophiles in Thailand\r\nrode along with the shooters on a drivebye\r\nwatched the brightest of our youth go to waste\r\nclapped and hooted with the masses as a lonely man leapt from the tenth floor\r\n\r\n\r\ni turned\r\nso that my eyes could meet hers\r\nthere on the grass\r\nbeneath the stars\r\ni moved me hand to her head\r\n\"i see death and hatred, war,\" i said\r\n\"i see fire.\"\r\n\r\n\r\nher lips were seventeen seconds from a scream\r\ni felt her body stiffen with my words\r\neyes searched mine, confused\r\nbehind them i saw the pain of the world\r\nshe gasped-\r\nmy woman melted back into me\r\nour hearts slowed to the beat of one\r\nher whisper blew into my ear\r\n -like a dream\r\n\"i see it too\"\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(902,'<P>\r\nI have just finished reading the first half of a book called Polyamory: the New Love Without Limits. (by Deborah Anapol, MD)  It made me cringe to read the section on jealousy. Not till this relationship have I felt jealousy, and I have to say loudly that it sux.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI realize that, since we are trying to be polyamorous, there are clear reasons for jealousy, but I?d be a healthier person if I could get past it.  Not push away from it, but work past it.  I need to reach those feelings to see where they are hidden.  It?s like poking a stick at an unknown beast.  Sometimes the beast purrs, sometimes it roars, and sometimes you lose the stick.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI have lost the stick to my own roaring beast on this issue, and I am not too eager to get close to it again.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBad metaphors aside, anyone have any experience with this?  Not to start a long dialogue about the merits of poly v. mono love.  To each their own, and their friend, and their friend?s own.  If they?re lucky.  heehee\r\n</P><P>\r\nI can be very strong and ready for the smack of his roaming eyes, but some people who T is attracted to make me very uneasy.  I feel like I am not nearly as cool as the other person, imagining the two of them flying away on the wings of a cloud, having magickal babies and eating mangoes in rosewater baths together.  It always goes away, but after some ridiculous number of hours spent squirming and sweating about what might (and never does) happen.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWould I be better off if he went off with people I thought were geeks, ugly or stupid?  Of course.  I?d feel very comfortable that there was no chance in hell he?d run off with them.  I do feel better when the people turn out to be monogamous and not into our sick little game.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe best scenario I can imagine:  T and I meet a couple who are both cool and cute, and both into both of us.  We pair off in the four ways 4 people can, then choose to stay with our primary partner.  Except for birthdays and holidays, when we all get together for romps.\r\n</P><P>\r\nDo I need counselling for this?  Maybe, but that is what YOU are all for!!!\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(903,'<tt>::sighs::</tt>\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhere to begin..pardon my sudden appearance here. i dont mean to intrude. \r\n</p><p>\r\nim caught in between a series of mindgames and im not prepared to lose any time soon.\r\n</p><p>\r\nall of my friends seem to be falling apart in one way or another and i cant seem to help. i can\'t find any words of reassurance. (please dont mine my spelling mistakes..)\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hate listening to myself make up this lies to make everyone happy. and i hate living this fake life.\r\n</p><p> \r\ni hate it when the spring is turning into the wayyyyy too hot summer.\r\ni dont want a job..i want him.. and yet i feel like im constantly settling one someone new. i feel like there are better out there..im just not able to let myself at em.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsorry about this\r\n</p>'),(904,'At work we have a big restaurant grade coffee maker.  Being used to a normal household 10-12 cup coffee maker, I usually don\'t put enough grounds into the filter when I make coffee at work.  Everybody complains, myself included.\r\n<P>\r\nI\'m trying to improve.  Really I am.\r\n<P>\r\nThe 45 year old hippie burnout complains loudest of all.  He pours my coffee out, and makes more.\r\n<P>\r\nBut he uses well over a half pound of coffee grounds every time he makes coffee.  That is a large amount.\r\n<P>\r\nBut by this time people in the office are tired of other people arguing about coffee, and nobody complains.  They just grumble to themselves.  So he thinks people are happy with his coffee, and resists my efforts to search for a happy medium.\r\n<P>\r\nThe filter basket on this coffee maker is, again, quite large.  Despite this, he manages to use enough coffee grounds that the grounds have no choice but to overflow the filter, and get into the pot.  He also doesn\'t wash the pot between runs, which is bad enough anyway, but especially when you are accumulating coffee grounds in the bottom all day.\r\n<P>\r\nOh, and when he pours in the water, one third of it doesn\'t make it out of the basket.  It takes a lot of coffee grounds to absorb that amount of water.\r\n<P>\r\nI want to kill him.\r\n<P>\r\nI want a drinkable cup of coffee.\r\n<P>\r\nI want to elect someone at the office as the official coffee maker, and his/her job is to make sure as many people are as happy with the coffee as possible.  If that means buying multiple coffee makers, so be it.  But he/she would make that decision.\r\n<P>\r\nI want to not be the only person who turns off the burner when the pot is at or near empty.  The pot boiled dry six times last week.  That makes an awful mess.\r\n<P>\r\nBut most of all I just want to kill the hippie.'),(905,'<p>I just got a new car: 2001 VW Jetta 1.8 turbo Wolfsburg Edition. I just wanted to tell <i>someone</i>, and since I already told the two friends and two family members I have, I figured.. eh.. '),(906,'<!-- nevermind. -le -->\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am oversensitive tonight. and defensive. i did not want to speak, much less argue, but managed to flounder around a pointless argument, expressing myself poorly and feeling like i was drowing in my own inarticulate behavior.\r\n</p><p>\r\nswinney was quiet. sometimes i sit next to him and i can\'t tell if he knows all. that is the thing with people who have the willpower to shut up, everyone thinks they know something they\'re not saying.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni haven\'t seen peter in a while. when i see him, he falls asleep soon thereafter. in some ways this is perfect. i do not have to have a relationship but i get to avoid all other irritating overtures at new relationships because i\'m in one.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni realize that\'s a really shitty sentiment. but it\'s like - i don\'t want to date anyone but peter. but, dating is really hard for me. beginning a relationship is the worst, most stressful thing, but maintaining it is really bad too. so, i have this mostly absent boyfriend, so i like.. don\'t have to start a relationship, and i don\'t have to really have my current one because my boyfriend works 14 hour days. \r\n</p><p>\r\nbest of all worlds: no pressure to look for a lover, i have one on weekends. no pressure to spend time with my lover. floating le.\r\n </p><p>\r\nexcept when i need attention, it is sort of sad. i go to bed alone.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am really on edge. it is like all hairs on my body are standing up and my shoulders are in my ears. i want to sleep but that seems like the wrong thing, too calm a pursuit for such a mood. </p>\r\n<p>ok.</p>\r\n'),(907,'<pre><tt>\r\nHave unsuccessfully tried to reach you by phone. Tonight I\r\ngo to my acting class so I\'ll try about 10:30pm when I get\r\nhome. Had a dream about you and there were wolves in the\r\ndream - is there someone in your life who is dishonest or\r\nsomeone you should watch out for? hmmmmm......Mom\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n<p>\r\ni called her today and she told me more about the dream. i\'m in a room, a familiar place; wolves are lurking, reeking of deceit.\r\n</p><p>\r\nher dreams of me are infrequent but always premonitions of what is to come. her last dream, she dreamt of impending hardship and struggle, days before critical path\'s stock tanked. before then, she dreamt i was trapped in a box, threatened with harm, days before i was robbed at an ATM.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the past i\'ve dismissed her. but i\'m not sure what to make of this one. i feel on edge, waiting for the terrible thing to happen.\r\n</p>'),(908,'<P>it all started when cupcake replied to my email.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>my day is just been too much energy for me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>really it is just the cupcake thing.  she is -- like -- such a star, and i am -- like -- such a fan.  need i say more?  anyway, cupcake has a movie and she will screen it for us probably.  where is it described?  jim knows.  please post.  anyway it is a move about people with the carry fettish.  they get off carrying people that are passed out.</P>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<TT>\r\n<a href=\"http://sugarandspice.com\">cupcake sez</a>:\r\n\r\nthe carry fetish is pretty bizarre. i am still trying to understand it. some\r\npeople think it is a form of necrophilia, but i am not sold on that yet.\r\nit\'s all about carrying an unconscious woman/person. what i find fascinating\r\nis that some of them are into the cradle carry and some are into the\r\nover-the-shoulder carry. that is why i don\'t buy the necrophilia thing - the\r\ncarrying type is too specific to be into just a dead body.\r\n</TT>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>sounds good to me.  she is really a great email exchanger.  she is coming to visit us.  will you be there?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i feel like i have more than payed my debt to society today.  i even got this <a href=\"comments.phtml\">neat comment view thing working</a>.  so tell me if you do not like it and i will modify it to your liking.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>on that note, i am going to get beer now partly because i have accomplished so much today and feel overwhelmed by the energy that it took, and partly because i wish had bought a plane ticket to austin, tx, for <a href=\"http://www.lepard.org\">bmg\'s birthday</A>.  all my old pals will be there.  \"pals\" incidentally is a <a href=\"userpages.phtml?username=simon_f_k\">*simon*</a> word.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>bmg: le and yeti say hi!</P>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/908_1.jpg\' align=\"center\">'),(909,'<p>\r\ni waited for an imaginary phone call today. i waited for a knock on the door. i waited for a smile and while i played out scenario after scenario in my mind i was not suprised at waking up to nothing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlistening to weird german electro on ckcu fm; staring off and on at the empty bowl on my desk. it had special k and strawberries in it. it was good. it satisfied my need for substance. substance is something iv had a hard time getting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hope she had a good night.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(910,'<PRE>\r\nhe is the deer hunter\r\n\r\noff camera, he is human\r\n\r\non camera, he is a god \r\n\r\nsitting in the wilds of his back yard\r\n\r\nnature happens in stock footage\r\n\r\nall those old tapes\r\n\r\nof animals dying   \r\n\r\nhe is killing those now\r\n\r\non the television set\r\n\r\nsunday afternoon\r\n\r\nafter church\r\n\r\nthe children worship him \r\n\r\nthe men think he gets layed         \r\n\r\nthey do not know  \r\n\r\nwhen he fires his gun\r\n\r\nblanks explode   \r\n\r\nand thirteen years ago\r\n\r\nin a quiet field in west texas\r\n\r\na deer gets layed\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--\r\ni am the deer hunter\r\noff camera i am human\r\non camera i am a god\r\nsitting in the wilds of my back yard\r\nnature happens in stock footage\r\nall those old tapes\r\nof animals dying\r\ni am killing those now\r\non your television set\r\nsunday afternoon\r\nafter church\r\nthe children worship me  \r\nthe men think i get layed         \r\nthey do not know  \r\nwhen i fire my gun\r\nblanks explode\r\nand thirteen years ago\r\nin a quiet field in west texas\r\na deer dies \r\n-->\r\n'),(911,'okay, so at some point icb kickball needs to happen.  i think s5 originally floated the idea.\r\n\r\nthat is all.\r\n\r\ninspired by people playing real life tekken ball (well more or less) at ambient groove.\r\n'),(912,'<p>\r\ni\'m a little scared. i\'m scared because i might have a job soon, and i will be working with some workoholics in the south bay.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis will mean possibly two things:\r\n</p><p>\r\n-i will start to wear jean shorts with black shoes with white socks and work 80 hour weeks. i will only be able to say things like \"user and object model\" while guffawing and pushing my glasses up my nose.\r\n</p><p>\r\n-linen pantsuits.\r\n</p><p>\r\nor! even worse, i will dress up vampy and go out drinking all the time, convinced only with a good dose of oblivion, that my life is worth living because i WORK HARD and PLAY HARD. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwhimper. please to kill me now.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(913,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/913_1.jpg\' width=400>\r\n<p>\r\nI come up against argument after argument. socialism is scary to many because they fear it will take away from them what they love. what do people love? cars? private property? having more then the person next to them. isnt life about working hard and getting what you deserve through respect and admiration? che guevara might not have been a great person, but he professed great things. ghandi too: he hit his wife on occasion. marx the same. is there anyone in this world who is not at fault for something? while che might have professed violent uprising, what social movement hasnt had violence. trade unions all over the world had to fight for their rights. women had to fight for the vote, so did minorities. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen atrocoties happen over seas we are flooded with names and demons. saddam was a bad man because he gassed the kurds(so did winston churchill) and killed his own brother. quadaffi is a bad man because he supports international terrorism. milosovich is a war criminal because he was the leader of a nation guilty of \"ethnic cleansing\". madeleine albright was responsible for the genocide in rwanda. her bottom line was that the shipment of equipment cost too much. yet when the KLA(a recognized terrorist group and leading heroin trafficing group) wants independence they are labelled \"ethnic albanians\" and millions upon millions is spent trying to \"stop the slaughter\". there isnt a general in KFOR that thinks the mission was succesful...and they are now having more problems with the KLA then they were with the serbs. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy is it when soldiers shot at and killed demonstrators at kent university, the president wasnt demonized? why is it when jimmy carter flew latin american generals out of central america in red cross planes he was not charged under the geneva convention. reagan was aquitted on all charges in the iran contra scandel and bush sr has had nothing to say about gulf war syndrome.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you goto libya the people are happy. they have the lowest infant mortality rate in africa and one of the highest literacy rates. college is free and people are given work. libya takes in refugees and helps them through various social programs. reagan assasinated his children in an attempted assasination and the headlines spoke of nothing. we have this tendency to never look at hte point of view of other nations. canadians are the same. we ride in the coat tails of uncle sam and have nothing to say in return. about hte most challenging thing canada has ever done in teh face of america is continue trade with cuba. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni ask this. why do americans feel guiltless in what their government does and how does american society find itself so content to just let the statistics on prison populations and poverty rates sore. i suggest people read up on why the roman empire failed and i hope to \"god\" it scares you. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(914,'<P>kind of weird, <a href=\"http://links.net\">i have heard of this guy justin</a>.  i saw a trailer for a movie (five years ago?) about some guy that became obsessed with people who make journals and so he made a documentary.  all i remember was that justin got carpal tunnel by the end of the movie.</P>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\"is it sex? is it happy? is it pop culture?\"<BR> -- justin</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<P>unknown.  but you know, he is a pretty nice guy.  and there is only a one letter difference in our names. (a|j)ustin.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there are pics of <a href=\"http://harlem.org\">wayne bremser</a> everywhere.  i feel like i was with wayne all over the world, these pics are from everywhere.  wayne is a generous guy, he gave me cake for yeti.  wayne\'s website harlem.org (ibid.) was featured in my jazz appreciation class at university of texas.</P>\r\n'),(915,'<p>remember to call your mom and when you are done, write to me and tell me what she said.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni found a weird book on the ground that tells me that i\'ll win a trip to hawaii or inherit 7,000.00 to buy my own mobile home.. if i send money for this company to pray for me.  if they pray for me.. god will be my financial advisor.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhoa.'),(916,'<p>today, in just a few moments, i\'ll be leaving for work.  that\'ll make seven days in a row.  which sucks, but could be worse.  in fact, i know just how much worse.  try the next 20 days.  meanwhile somehow i gotta find a new place to live.  i never believe in the whole socialist wage slave idea.  but here i am, and my boss is telling me that if i wish to continue working at all, i will suborn all other desires and problems.  maybe at the end of it all i\'ll be homeless again.  wouldn\'t that be ironic, working so desperately to make a wage, only to lose a roof in order to obtain it. </p>'),(917,'<P>the road goes on. mind is already gone. i find myself feeling comfortable, tired...typing and writing and stretching in this less than posh, but nicer than a dumpster cabin. mountains surrounding me and my one bedroom suite next to the creek. colorado. callers wanting to know if im ok and not too gloomy from last weeks ex siting. they dont realize im already a million miles away. who? what? i have no idea what they are talking about. finit. my eyes go purple supernova again. and my heart knows i need noone to make that happen. it just is.\r\n</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/917_1.jpg\'>'),(918,'<P align=\"justify\"><tt>\r\nso.Â  as i tried to explain <A HREF=\"http://www.distantvoices.net/alita/penmanship/01/05_13.html\">here</A>, sam and i, while on a little hike in a park, came across a hanged body.Â  the funny part is that i wasn\'t spooked in the way you think you\'d be spooked if you came across a hanged body.Â  you\'d think that you\'d either be shocked or nauseated by the sight of a hanged body, or feel very sad and mournful over the person that used to be in that body, or afraid because you live less than ten blocks from that particular park or angry because...i don\'t know exactly because, but you\'d think you\'d be angry over that.\r\n</tt></P>\r\n<P align=\"justify\"><tt>\r\nat the very least, you\'d think it would change your perception of the world and life and all that, having seen the aftermath of a somewhat violent death.Â  but if it has changed my perception, i\'m so far very slow in recognizing it.\r\n</tt></P>\r\n'),(919,'<p>quivery; tensed and relaxed intermittently in each second, and my brain is the same.</p>\r\n<p>i can pause to feel textures, to place some oven chips in the furnace and even to ponder the lack of a partner.</p>\r\n<p>where\'s your partner dude?<br>\r\ndude, where\'s my partner?</p>\r\n<p>all my life i had a partner!  a mother, a best friend, a lover.</p>\r\n<p>my hands are friction burned and torn.</p>\r\n<p>i read about hanging bodies and i thought i should write a song about it.</p>\r\n<p>here is some of something i want to become a song:</p>\r\n<p>perpetual today to permanent tonight<br>\r\nsand and the waves in a torrential fight<br>\r\ndawn is defeated by diminutive daylight<br>\r\nnature wars for our delight<br>\r\n</p>\r\n'),(920,'<p>\r\nmy job at hartmans independently owned grocery store.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits the best job iv ever had. its so rewarding and nice to help out customers...especially the ones who have language problems. i feel as though im the grocery store angel....there to save all customers from confusion and frustration. i make their shopping experience better.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor instance, yesterday a man came upto me. using limited sign language i knew he needed peewee eggs. I told him nicely that we didn\'t have any peewee eggs. he didn\'t beleive. he pointed to the back room as though there might be some magically appearing peewee eggs. i told him again that there were none. i explained to him that i was the keeper of the egg cooler and that i knew we hadn\'t had any peewee eggs in about a month. he pointed again. at this point i turned and made a grimace and then turned back and told him we didnt have any \"fucking peewee eggs\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni then asked my manager if i could go have a smoke outside to get a little unstressed. he said yes. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(921,'<p>\r\nLast night, just before i meant to go to bed, my roomate Arianna comes streaming into the computer room bawling her eyes out.  Nearly knocking my box over, she managed to climb into my lap, spilling my lovely Guinness.  But i had no time to mourn for lost stout...she blubbered away for maybe ten minutes before i got her to speak in a manner i could understand.  The jist was that another of her friends had fallen in love with her.  And now she felt bad because she\'d hurt him, and she was going to lose his friendship.  Now Ari is the worst sort of flirt i\'ve ever known, and i\'ve watched many times as a man has gotten the wrong idea.  She\'s never been willing to listen to me when i say that she\'s the cause of her own problems in this.  My bedtime is 10pm most nights, I didn\'t even make it out of the \'puter room till 1am.  I think maybe i\'ve finally gotten through to her.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen i awoke at 5 my eyes were burning and i wasn\'t so sure i could stand.  But i made it all the way to work, cursing Ari and her silly ways the entire time.  Before she\'d disturbed me i had been working on documenting the self-defense curriculum my business partner and i have been working on.  Trying to find an insurance policy that\'ll cover us is a real bitch.  Anyway, all day at work all i could think about was this damn curriculum.  My crew leader got pissed and stuck me in the filthy attic all day.  There, in the dark, surrounded by squirrel corpses and fiberglass i could sweat and think on my own.  It wasn\'t much fun.  On the way home I feel asleep in the car and dreamt of this bloody curriculum...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen i awoke the car was parked and my roomate Matt was looking at me oddly.  He later told me i\'d been mumbling about self defense in my sleep.  I got out of the car and felt a fearsome need for a cigarette.  Bending over to retrieve my lighter from floor of the car i smacked my head into the car door so hard it gashed my forehead.  I screamed obscenities until i got my cigarette lit.  Then smoked in silence as we made our way to the apartment.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHere i was, last night, the master of self defense...I felt so aware all day, aware of potential threats and such.  And then a simple car door fucked my sorry ass up.  Wonderful!\r\n</p>'),(922,'<p>My new place of work is across the street from Cheng\'s Cafe. I could tender several complaints about the job -- the smell, the hours -- but these things are irrelevant.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I operate and manage aquatic tanks. These are just large tanks of water in the basement of the building. Within the tanks are smaller tanks, also filled with water, made of different materials, some are connected to the rest of the fluid system and others are closed, and within these tanks are other tanks which are smaller still. Some contain circuitry and electrical devices. Precise monitoring is involved, however I do not know by whom or to what end.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If the purpose behind the system is worthy of any mention at all, I will only say that the reason for the tanks is itself an absurdity.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is no toilet in the place. The basement isn\'t a proper workarea and we (including my boss) have to go to Cheng\'s to use the watercloset. Cheng, if this is really the owner\'s name, does not seem to mind so long as we go there for lunch. It\'s a dingy place, never very crowded and it seems to invite the patronage of several unlikely characters from the area. My boss claims to have once heard a fight going on in the kitchen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I usually get pu-erh tea and a bowl of noodles. My hands are always soggy from the gloves. I want to get paid.</p>\r\n'),(923,'<P>This morning whilst doing my daily news-catchup, I was reading Stuff, a Wellington based news site for The Evening Post and The Dominion. I clicked on a story and began reading... Body found in the Rimutakas. Half-way through the article the murder victim was named. Shock.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>It\'s not that I knew her all that well, but she was a drinking buddy. Someone I\'d bump into on the street, someone to go for drinks with. Someone I\'d sit in the cafe with and drink endless cups of tea or coffee and babble with. Someone seen at parties.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>She had an incredible voice and I loved hearing her sing.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I\'ve been trying to get in contact with my friend in Wellington who knew her well. I want to check that she is okay. I\'m worried.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>People you kind-of know don\'t get murdered, especially not in little old New Zealand. It is too sickening.</P>\r\n'),(924,'<p>\r\nDrew is my oldest Cyber Pen-Pal that I still haven\'t met...  We met online about 4 years ago.\r\nI tended to really only interact with people I\r\nknew on the Net for a long time...  Or most of\r\nthe people I met on the Net, I also met in RL\r\npretty quickly, because they knew people I knew.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDrew is going into officer training for the Marines, and may make a life of it...  What a trip...  Well, here I will immortalize a piece of her pre-warmachine days...  I am not sure what of\r\nthe many things she has told me about her life really inspired this poetry...  But I like it better without being sure of the story behind it...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/877_1.jpg\">\r\n<br>\r\n-\r\n<hr>\r\n-\r\n<p>\r\nJust in case you don\'t quite care to recollect my words, \r\nThey mean the same forever now, that I\'m fucked up and vain\r\nSee there\'s this seckness that I have that kills me everyway\r\nNever love, only lust, the fellings lie, I never trust.\r\nIt seems I toy with naive minds then bash their broken heads,\r\nThis illness makes me crave the hate and love the violence.\r\n</p>\r\n \r\n<p>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\nExtreme Measures\r\nThere\'s blazing circles around my eyes burning deeper the more I spin\r\nThere\'s too many ways but I may choose only one\r\nseeking, searching through this haze\r\nWhat the hell is this fucking craze\r\nI\'m lost I tell ya the lights are dimming\r\nthe world\'s closing down but i\'m still living\r\nlosing myself in all extreme measures\r\nthe fear that creeps is all to clear\r\ni cannot see what\'s all too near\r\nthe paths you glimpse, you must pick one now\r\nnone will cross but some lead down\r\ntorn; you\'re frantic, racing death\r\nwhat if i\'m wrong, i want a new chance!\r\n\"too late you\'re fucked, you writhing mortal!!!\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\n<pre><tt>\r\nPAIN\r\nTorn apart\r\nlove and fear\r\nfeelings too deep\r\ni\'ve already been here\r\ni have to leave\r\ni cannot deal\r\nwith things you say\r\nor pain i feel\r\nmy heart screams\r\nin anguished cries\r\ni cannot break\r\nso i cage it inside\r\ni cannot tell \r\nfor the emotions i fear\r\nwould take my life\r\nno matter how dear\r\n</tt></pre></p>\r\n<p>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\nSmoke still has my mind.\r\n remembering the night that i\'ve just left behind\r\nso much done, many dutchmasters split.\r\nRunning from the fuzz and all other shit\r\nlounging on cushions with my head in a daze\r\neveryting went so fast inside a red smoke-filled haze\r\nno laughing no cough, i think it\'s the couch that i fell off\r\nmy arms change, I see them go green\r\nthe stranges thing i\'ve ever seen\r\nisn\'t it fun when the brain has no cells?\r\neverything pretty and tinkling bells\r\n</tt></pre>\r\n</p>'),(925,'<p>\r\nHmmm. Mother\'s day has passed.  I didn\'t call.  I didn\'t write.  I didn\'t sent FTP flowers.  I didn\'t send an e-card.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI didn\'t intend to.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI\'ve been giving mom the cold shoulder for about 9 months now, after a conversation last summer that left me thinking it just wasn\'t worth the effort.  I almost completely ignored Christmas, but gave in 2 weeks late and sent a lukewarm letter and an impersonal gift.  She returned lukewarm email and impersonal gift. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI feel guilt once in a while, and think about calling, but what to say?  \r\n</p><p>\r\n\"Hi Ma.  Just thought you might be sitting around in your living room, crocheting, and wondering if life passed you by.  It didn\'t.  YOu passed it up.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo, I don\'t call.  It seems more adult, and less cruel to NOT vocalize those sentiments.  I secretly hope she knows exactly why I am not calling her, but I know she is wondering why I have forsaken her...\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo, I wonder, would the effort that I could put out to befriend her really be for her, anyway?  Or would I be doing it to relieve my own guilt?\r\n</p><p>\r\nIf I mentioned WHY I had been avoiding her unwarranted opinions, she\'d be hurt, possibly more than the silence confuses her.  I\'ve never told her the things that upset me about her, because I feel that if she was faced with them she\'d just die inside, knowing that she truly had failed herself.  I do not begrudge how she raised me, I pity her for inflicting herself with suffering of her own making.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAm I the one to teach her?  If not I, then who?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(926,'<p>\r\nI\'ve always said that a man must rest well with the truths he finds within his heart.  We can lie about many things through the day, but alone in the dark we must face ourselves and accept what we find.  It\'s been a rare night when i could not bear the speech of my heart.  No matter how bad life has been, or the terrible things i\'ve done.  Recently however i cannot sleep and cannot look into myself without cringing.  I\'ve never been a very attractive man, but i\'ve learned to work with that limitation; i\'ve accepted it.  But those things which, for certain women, might have made up for my appearance have become increasingly hard to bring out.  Courtesy which once came with ease and with a smile has become just a habit.  I\'ve grown to dislike eye contact.  I no longer wish to look upon beauty, or exchange pleasantries.  The few women in my life have for the most part learned to accept me as a distant person.  Someone to be greeted, perhaps hugged, and then left alone lest i become agitated.  I am the friend you come to in distress, in emergencies, but avoid the rest of the time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nJust a few moments ago a woman i knew a few years back said hello to me.  I turned and looked at her, said \"you\'re lucky i don\'t cut your throat.\"  And went into my apartment.  Now that\'s extreme i admit, but to be honest, i despise her.  She chose sex and drugs over her little girl, when my girlfriend and i were trying to help her regain custody.  Then she ripped my girlfriend off for around two large.  So i don\'t like her.  What bothered me was that it was a knee jerk statement.  I wonder if i\'d have said the same to one of my friends.  I know that every time i beckon my roomate Ari over, she looks afraid.  Her friend Dana seems to, all at once; pity me, revile me, fear me, and genuinely like me.  Soon Ari and Dana will be here, smiling and laughing.  Already i am annoyed.  Have i truly sunk so low that i can no longer seperate the public, hard self from the private self?  Once, not so long ago, in private i could smile and cuddle and laugh.  I wonder now where that has gone and why?  The truly confusing and disturbing bit is that my behavior has become inconsistent with my thoughts.  Why, feeling such love for these people, do i cut myself off from them?  I do not remember choosing such a behavior.  I would not consider it an option under normal circumstances. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSure, i\'ve had some terrible experiences with love, friends, relatives, lovers.  But much of it is in the past.  The future might be bleak, but i always thought so, what is different now?  As i regain a closeness with myself, i become cut off from others?  I\'ve written off more people this year than in any five years of my life.  Have my standards, once so realistic (or at least pragmatic), become too harsh.  Bitter and twisted after years of mistakes and heartache, lost friends and wasted opportunities, can i have really brought myself so close to that place i sought years ago.  That place where i am apart from those around me, where my feelings for them don\'t get it the way of my inhumanity?  How can i be this thing when i stopped trying for it a decade or more ago?  Is there any validity to my belief that while i once was not a good man, i am now?  The fear i can engender in people is quite something.  It\'s no problem when i dislike a person, there are times when i want them to fear me.  But years ago, the woman i\'d later ask to marry me, after having made love for the first time, told me it was not what she\'d expected.  Confused i asked her what she\'d expected.  Pain.  She\'d thought i\'d hurt her.  I now know the reasons she thought such things, but i\'ll never stop be astonished at it.  Every time i am introduced to someone, i am terse in my speach and edgy in body language.  Often my hand moves ever so slightly closer to one of my weapons.  Thinking now, i wonder how many times i\'ve ruined a chance at a relationship when the girl i\'m introduced to flinches away from my bridled but all to obvious agression.  How often do my friends have to apologize to their friends for trying to set them up with me.  Maybe i\'ll ask. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt is apparent to me, and i think to all decent humans that refraining from outright violence is not enough.  Violent behavior is so much more than the physical attack.  Why am i commiting acts of violence for reasons other than defense of myself, my circle, or the innocent?  Or am i defending myself against something i cannot see, something i fear without that fear intruding upon my thoughts.  I have no trouble admitting fear, fear is that friend which keeps me from the life i once led.  When i don\'t fear i am empty and dangerous.  Why should i not admit a fear to myself?  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWow.  I can\'t even finish this fucking thing, i can feel each question slip away from me as i try to type it.  Maybe it doesn\'t matter.  I might get better tomorrow.  Or i\'ll sink so deep in this that i\'ll finish life as the creature i was years ago.  Violent in thought and deed,  finding pleasure in the pain of others.  Who knows.  I need a Guinness...\r\n</p>'),(927,'<P>i was having a conversation with s5 on our walk yesterday from the mission area about books.  i finished reading snowcrash the other day and have  been trying to recover any interest in reading another book.  i pick books up to read but they are less entertaining and i lose interest.  i want to continue to read snow crash.  i like the exciting nature of the book.  there is action and the characters are hot and smart and dangerous.  i want to keep reading about the fifteen year old hot skater chick and the half black half chinese swordfighting hacker.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>instead i have this book about cowboys, all the pretty horses, and it does not rock my ass at all.  i think my new test when picking out a book is \"would i pay to see the move?\"  i say this as i trot along saddle sore from a book that shuns quotation marks making it even more of a pain in the ass than just riding for several days on a stinking mule.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i think i will go to the grocery store for some john grisham.</P>\r\n'),(928,'<p>\r\nwhoa today all this responsibility was laid down on ol chia. i got to carry the covetted keys to the loading bay doors. i know i know. big deal eh? ohhhhh not at hartmans my friends. outside the loading bay doors is a favourite spot for homeless junkies to shoot their shit up. needles all over the place. the doors are important because they need to be kept locked at all times. tonight i was given the keys and told to receive the three skids of product from national grocers (parent company of loblaws). it was great. i got to use all the jiggers and move shit all pro like. yeah it was fantastic. i even got to sign the shipping slip. of course i put my webpage address...i mean who wouldnt right? \r\n</p>'),(929,'<PRE>\r\n<*le*> when i was in savannah, there were a lot of legendary women in\r\n<*le*> my social group. they had all had emotional hysterics relating\r\n<*le*> to various men in the group. one by one, each of them was\r\n<*le*> labeled psycho. they had a\r\n<*le*> ring leader named jen brady\r\n<*le*> jen brady once fucked the bassist from skinny puppy\r\n<*le*> in the morning when they woke up, he came stumbling out of\r\n<*le*> jen\'s room, where her crazy bostonian thug roomate (buck) was\r\n<*le*> making pancakes.\r\n<*le*> buck offered pancakes to the bassist from skinny puppy\r\n<*le*> he said no\r\n<*le*> buck said EAT MY FUCKING PANCAKES\r\n<*le*> I DON\'T CARE WHO YOU ARE, EAT MY PANCAKES\r\n<*le*> i think buck was drunk or something.\r\n<*le*> this is one of the legends i was told when i hung out with the\r\n<*le*> art kids in savannah\r\n<*le*> told over and over at coffee shops</PRE>'),(930,'i meandered into this site and thought it pretty cool; just thought i would share....\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.explodingdog.com/\" target=\"_blank\">exlpodingdog.com</a>\r\n\r\n<p>puppy *'),(931,'<p>morning at Falls Creek hiking. afternoon in Utah reading petroglyphs. nightfall at Flaming Gorge, Wyoming. the ground moves beneath me. history passes by. the world moves me...but not anything like the awe i feel when i wake to see <a href=\"images/931_1.jpg\">you smiling at me</a>.</p>\r\n<a href=\"images/931_1.jpg\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/931_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'></a>\r\n<a href=\"images/931_2.jpg\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/931_2.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'></a>\r\n<a href=\"images/931_3.jpg\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/931_3.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'></a>\r\n<a href=\"images/931_4.jpg\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/931_4.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'bottom\'></A>\r\n<br>'),(932,'<p>test lala och bloo aye foomar I say!</p>'),(933,'<p>Austin --> Albany --> Austin</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Spent last weekend in Albany.  A trip bookended with my trudging through Newark Int\'l, a new addition to my \"worst place on earth\" list.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Up and down and back again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jillian and I went from not talking to each other for over a month to spending every second with each other laughing, joking, being intimate while I was in Albany... and now the big split has happened again--1800+ miles.  Distance needs to be made obsolete.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ll readily admit that I feel something for her, and it doesn\'t just vanish when she\'s out of sight.  Not quite.  She\'s, honestly, the first girl with which I\'ve ever felt comfortable.  No worries, insecurities when we\'re together.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\'t know what\'s going to happen tomorrow, though.  No expectations.  There are no plans for either of us to make the move.  As much as I\'d like to report otherwise, it isn\'t so.  At this point it probably wouldn\'t be realistic anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Either way, I want to see her again soon.  Me there or her here.  The city doesn\'t matter to me, I only want to see her.</p>'),(934,'<p>hot, hot, hot. Damn, it is hot!</p><p>I am running around today like a madman. Which is fine, but it seems as though i am going to forget everything before I actualy get out of town. So, I am going to New Mexico for a little vacation, to see my mother and sisters. I am also going to see an old friend of mine that I haven\'t seen for some 7-8 years. I am excited about the trip. I am made a bunch of cd\'s for the trip...orbital, tricky, all compliments of dj_luna and his fine music finding... he had gorillaz weeks before it was out in the UK.... hot stuff that man.... HOT!!!!</p>\r\n<p>As soon as I get back, I am gonna hit the ol\'e books... I enrolled in summer school. This will be the first time I have been in school full time since last spring... I am ready to get back and get it over with... well, I must run... peeps are waiting for me... Talk to you all soon.</p>'),(935,'<p>\r\nthe other day, i wrote a post about a book that i found on the ground and post was rejected due to lack of content.  so, i\'m going to try to post again with more content.  however, i\'m increasingly suprised by what people consider content and what people consider crap.  personally i think that you should screen postings to filter out flames, advertisement, spam, and material that could be deemed harmful to a person or others.  \r\n<br><BR>\r\ni don\'t feel that i did any of the above with my post and yes, i\'ve taken it personally.  perhaps it wasn\'t anything i *should* take personally, however i\'ve noticed MANY other posts that in my SUBJECTIVE opinion lack \"content\".  so, i know that saying what i think is probably not well received within this community and seems to cause uproar whenever anybody does.  it surely won\'t make me popular will it?\r\n<br><BR>\r\nhave i reached the mystical cutoff point to justify this post?  have i made it to the final cut?  did i make the team coach?  am i allowed to have my opinion amongst all these free thinkers?  probably not.  this post might not even make it.\r\n<br><BR>\r\nperhaps it is just the month of may, and i\'m feeling like a taurus.  perhaps you can blame it on the stars.  who knows.\r\n<br><BR>\r\nanyhow, my point is.. that if you want filter content.. perhaps you should post that on your site, otherwise people will continue to contribute what they feel is appropriate.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhere is my original post:\r\n<br><BR>\r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>remember to call your mom and when you are done, write to me and tell\r\nme what she said.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni found a weird book on the ground that tells me that i\'ll win a trip to hawaii or inherit 7,000.00 to buy my own mobile home.. if i send money for this company to pray for me.  if they pray for me.. god will be my financial advisor.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is all i wanted to say in my post.  nothing more nothing less.\r\n<br><BR>\r\ni found a book on the ground.. and it puzzled me.  i didn\'t understand how someone could print a book with a multimarketing scam involving jesus christ.  i also wanted to simply remind people to call their mother.  \r\n<br><BR>\r\ndid i dissapoint you?  did i bring a bad taste to your mouth?  \r\n<br><BR>\r\nanyhow, i\'m not trying to start a flame war here, i just want to voice my opinion.  i\'m full of those.  thanks for the rant.                                                                        \r\n</P>\r\n'),(936,'i\'ve noticed several messages mentioning famous people who may or may not be famous everywhere, but they are here.<br><br>\r\n\r\njustin, scooter, wayne, etc.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin grease rizzo sings about sandra dee, and many people knew exactly who sandra dee was, but many of the kids who saw grease really did not have a clue.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyesterday someone told me they thought bob hope was a \"doofus\" (sic)  i love bob hope movies.  they are silly, but so full of hope. my favorite is the damon runyon take-off -- the lemon drop kid.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe french love jerry lewis, and i did too, when i was a kid.  when i got older i realized that most of his movies included tediously long slapstick bits that irritate and bore me to the extreme.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsomeday one of the swinneys will be amazingly famous outside the sf/austin/techie/web community.  this is a sure bet.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen that happens, will we all look back and remember them as they bumbled through here questioning themselves, their jobs, their cities, and the world?<br><br>\r\n\r\nor will we make sure to tell people at parties that we knew them \"when they were nobody\" ??<br><br>\r\n'),(937,'<P>tuesday morning i passed by a bunch of kids doin yoga on the lawn. they never do that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>today i read the paper and it was woody harrelson leading them in a yoga class. i didnt know he was that gung ho.</P>'),(938,'<pre>\r\na moment without\r\n     is a beautiful thing\r\n   wander past the steps\r\n       beyond the sweet residue\r\n         beyond the photos in mind\r\n      tagged with captions\r\n        and layered with feel good crust\r\n  still\r\n     today is today\r\n         like\r\n        now is now\r\n   when i said anything you want\r\n   i meant\r\n   as long as i don\'t go crazy like i could\r\n   like you knew that i would\r\n   like i am\r\n   goddam\r\n   crazy like i am\r\n\r\na moment without\r\n     is a beautiful thing\r\n   and i\'m set to move on again\r\n  but this love child\r\n         and this place\r\n          and your perfect face\r\n            stays my pace\r\n no sleep in this boiler room\r\n  working shit jobs for peanut trix\r\n and each eyes the other\r\n   waiting for a sign\r\n  each eyes the other till we\'re blind\r\nin pornographic light the scene unfolds\r\n blot out the ghosts and old molds\r\nno sleep till white sands\r\n       goddam\r\n  crazy like i am\r\n\r\n i\'ve had enough drama\r\n    for ten million hours of prime time\r\n  in the last month\r\n  it\'s all the choices you make\r\n  each one takes its toll\r\n  makes the new scene unfold\r\n  and an unborn life form\r\n  that baby in you\r\n  keeps everything here\r\n  keeps out love for the fear\r\n  and the old mold my father\r\n  is not me\r\n  i am crazy like my father\r\n  but not quite the same man he\'s\r\n     not crazy like i am\r\n</pre>\r\n<p>relive your memories<br>\r\nthere isn\'t anything left<br>\r\nto cherish<br>\r\nwhen you ax your tracks<br>\r\nrelive your prayers<br>\r\nsay yes to all dares<br>\r\nfrom devils and squares<br>\r\nfind the middle way<br>\r\neveryday</p>'),(939,'<P>\r\nMy partner and I enjoy dabbling with experimental and \'legal\' drugs. (Before I go any further, know that I am almost 30, he is 27 and we both are into this, not for recreational purposes, but for consciousness expansion). We\'ve both had good, and many, interactions with mushrooms, acid, ecstacy, and other lesser known psychotrophics. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nRecently, he\'s begun to read up on the effects of ingesting Morning Glory seeds (the flower). Apparently, if prepared properly, the ensuing trip can be extremely mind blowing. Extreme expansion of consciousness , empathy, inducing of great levels of communication with loved ones. But if prepared incorrectly, it can be a very bad experience. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nI was wondering if anyone has experimented themselves with MG, or if anyone knows anyone who has. I feel that I am experienced enough with psychotrophics to be able to handle anything that comes, but I am also responsible enough to do all my research until I ingest anything \'unknown\'. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Any help/advice would be appreciated. Respond here or at given email.\r\n</P>'),(940,'i came home from a long day about 20 minutes ago and noticed a red flag hanging from the balcony of my neighbor\'s house.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni saw a \"k\" out of the corner of my eye and thought it was some radio station promotion to win a contest or something.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni suddenly realized there was more than on \"k\" on the flag and i made mandy back up the car so i could put on my glasses and see if it said what i was afraid it said.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit did.  it is a huge red flag, 3 ft x 5 ft or so, and it has a funny looking white celtic cross on it and big black KKK.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe people who live there are old.  in their late seventies..<br><br>\r\n\r\ni knew they were uneducated, and i knew they were bigots, since they use the \"n\" word .<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey are the only caucasion people i have heard use that word since i can\'t remember when....<br><br>\r\n\r\nflorida, is, of course, the deep south.  these people and their families have lived in this rural area since the mid 1800s or so, and i suppose that at one time being a member of the klan was something everyone did around here.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut i don\'t think so.  i am not stupid, and i am not so naive that i believe all people are good, but i never thought i\'d KNOW a member of the klan, much less live next door to one.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe thing is, until about 4:30 pm today, i liked the guy.  he\'s old, and he\'s a bigot, but there are a lot of old bigots around, all colors, all religions and races in all the places in the world.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmandy and i are both in shock.  we don\'t know what to do, and it seems like we should DO SOMETHNG, but what?.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni know the wife got robbed by a black guy in a brutal incident about 6 years ago.  but lots of people get robbed and the don\'t have kkk flags hanging from their houses and they know that there are bad people in every color.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni can\'t figure out WHY he would put up that flag. i keep thinking something must have happened to cause him to do so, something bad, something that made him dig out that flag and stick it on the house, and i try to find an excuse for an old, stupid man who is a bigot to do that, but i can\'t think of anything that would make a rational person OWN a fucking kkk flag, much less display it on the front of their house.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand it\'s all about me now.  i am afraid the news media will hear about it and come around with cameras and trucks and reporters because i know the postman saw it and he is black.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am afraid the postman and the ups think that other people on this street agree with that old bigot.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni love my house and i love my street and my neighborhood and my town, but i don\'t want to live next door to the klan and i am frightened and shaky and i feel guilty now because i just wish he would get some old people\'s disease and fucking die and then someone rational would buy the house, and i want it to happen before i go to bed tonight.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow that i\'m writing it, i have decided what i will do.  i will wait until tomorrow morning, and if it is still there, i will go knock on the door and ask him to please take it down.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni believe totally in the rights of people to believe what they wish, even when the things they wish are things i despise and fear, and i know that anything i can say to him is not going to make him a non-bigot, or resign from the klan, but the flag offends me dreadfully and maybe he will take it down if i ask nice and suggest he doesn\'t want the newspaper out here.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(941,'<p>\r\ni think that when a community begins to consciously think of itself as a community, it\'s only a matter of time until the community sinks under the weight of its own self-importance and pomposity.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\none of the things i always liked about swinney was that nobody ever posted saying \"wow i really like our community a whole lot, aren\'t we great? *group hug*\".\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni think the swinney editors can rest easy that there won\'t be twenty \"i\'m taking a dump\" posts per day. cos the kind of people that are amused by such goofs already have an outlet. it\'s called pals.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ngood luck swinney!\r\n</p>\r\n'),(942,'<P>there are many uses for my nokia 6100 that make it a good phone.  the least of which is calling my mom and dad on the weekends.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>some qualities and facts about my nokia 6100 series:\r\n<UL type=\"square\">\r\n<LI>makes phone calls\r\n<LI><a href=\"../music/\">it is a go anywhere cordless microphone\r\n<LI>outs to a high tech mixer</A>\r\n<LI>opens beer bottles\r\n<LI>the antenna nub is good for picking my nose and scratching the inside of my ear\r\n<LI>used to belong to yeti and was given to him by critical path\r\n<LI>fits in the side pocket of my carhart jeans\r\n<LI><a href=\"http://elly.org\">le</a> has one\r\n<LI><a href=\"http://steve.org\">s5</A> has one\r\n<li><a href=\"http://durak.org\">sean</A> has one too\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\n<P>i used to think i didnt need a cell phone.  i still dont, i could just walk around with $00.35 at all times.  no one ever calls me.  and what is more, i dont want any phone calls.  i am not going to post my number here so that RFB (random <*ENCRYPTED*> bag) can dial me.  no way.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i just wanted to share this.  mainly because i used it to open my beer and felt that that alone deserved recognition and praise.</P>'),(943,'<p>\r\nyou think i give a shit what nationality someone is? you think i give a shit to hear shit like \"if we werent friends we\'d go out\"? i dont think so. if we werent friends youd look at me as a pot bellied freak, just like you looked at jeremy like a four eyes loser. you take in and love the attention you get and for what? everything that you say you dont like in men....yet you eat it up. you eat it up because it feeds your ego. fuck you. fuck you. i want to be so violent right now. i want to bash people in the head with rocks and throw things. you make me mad. im pissed and im so utterly mad that i cant even express it in words. yeah no email for you. no fucking satisfying anything within your ego. nothing. im sick of being honest while you play men like they are toys. fucking grow up. fucking stop trying to divert me from you. fuck you!!!!! yes thats what i feel like saying to you. my shrink said today that i should say to you \"i have a problem and i need help\" yet i dont think you are even capable of helping anyone but yourself. i hated tonight. yeah you laughed at four eyes while i had to listen to him blather on about nothing and then have one of hte staff come upto me and say \"if you dont mind me saying, your friend is cute.\" i ended that conversation with \"they are both cute\". he answered with \"no doubt\", but i knew he was just saying it. fake like every one of the men she shoddied. fuck em. fucking losers. im the one who is honest and caring....yet she looks at them. with lustful eyes and then turns to me and says sorry like she\'d never do it again and then is upto the same thing the next time i see her trotting away to speak to \"yeah but he\'s indian.\"....FUCK YOU YOU FUCK. IM SO FUCKING MAD. you dont even understand. im glad she\'s leaving tomorrow. i dont even want to  talk to her. im so pissed. im so pissed that she didnt leave with us. im so pissed that that\'s one of her pet peeves yet she does it. fucking hipocrit. fuck you. fuck everyone. im too demanding...fuck you. im just sure of a good  thing and im usually right yet im given cliche exuses and stupid exuses. fuck everything. im so sick and tired of being dealt the short end of every stick. what am i? am i the fucking loser everyone picks last in sports? what the fuck else am i supposed to do. yeah i know a lot of you are saying \"oh there is the chia tape back on re-run\" .... yeah well fuck you too! im trying and i try. i seek help from pro\'s and i get help from pro\'s...yet none of my friends seem to give two fucks. they look at it as me complaining...and yet i look at it as me just being honest at 2am when i havve to work at 9am. should i call in sick? no way. why should i even give her the freedom to think i was too drunk and just over reacting. tonight some  fuck had the balls to sit beside me and hit on her. one of the waiters had the guts to say to me \'if you dont mind your friend is really cute\'. i replied with \"yeah both of them are\"...and he replied so fucking fakely \"yeah yo uare right\"....yet the only thing he had on his mind was her. one of them. well fuck everyone. fuck the people who think im just being repetative...and fuck the freinds who said shit like \"i just dont wanna see you get hurt\". fuck everyone. im so fucking sick of opinions. never once have i have some kind of justification for any of the opinions and never once have i had any sort of explanation. im always selfish and im always demanding. im just so sick of it. people just dont understand the shit ive had to go through and yet they all tell me they understand. well fuck you all. how the fuck can you tell me you understand when all of you are not virgins and all of you have been in relationships. \"we\'d go out if we we\'rent friend.\". WHAT THE FUCK DOES THAT MEAN? lets face it. i might have a pretty face, but my body is less than desireable. so what the fuck does she say that for? and then i have to accept appologies that i know are half asses and yet the shit continiues. i feel like bashing peoples heads in with rocks and bottles yet i dont. maybe i should. fuck all those losers. and then i think im a loser. and for what reason? because im in love with a girl who does not give two shits about me. im clear. im calm. im collected. its the truth. im just sick of it all and im sick of ottawa. im sick of working hard for things that never go myway. jobs. girls. you name it. everything i get my hopes up for never come true and  for what? nothing. their lives are perfect and ther is always  something there to keep them alive...yet for me...nothing keeps me alive. i live life for other people and for what i give them, i receive nothing in return. i had two really good and deep sessions with my shrink and no one seems to give two shits. it was her last day here for a bunch of days and yet she spent them looking at waiters and talking to losers who were trying to pick up a \"17\"year old hwo was 24. what the fuck do i live for? what the fuck am i here for? my doc said i had to get ahold of my positive aggresstion and he read the letter i had sent her and said that the letter was an example of how i had harnessed my positive aggression and the next time i see him im going ot be like \'where the fuck did this get me? what did it do to improve my life?\" if i imagine myself dying or beinbg in a coma from being hit by a car or fighting in a revolutionary war....how does that hurt me as much as what is happening. what hteh fuck? people just expect me to just sit back and relax. WELL FUCK YOU ALL. '),(944,'<p>\r\ni got up today all sluggish and rank feeling. i showered and attempted to get on with my day. i got to work a few minutes early and started a few minutes early. i was there for about thirty minutes and i just broke down in the dairy cooler. after wiping my face i went to speak to the manager. i could hardly get a word in before i started crying again. he didnt ask any questions and he let me come home. i was releived at that. i thought id be given a hard time. im just tired. im very tired. \r\n</p>'),(945,'<p align=\'justify\'>\r\nthis is a perfect moment:</p>\r\n<p align=\'justify\'>\r\nsitting on the train, stuck between daly city and colma, waiting- she is sitting next to me and i am listening to a tape and looking out the window that she is looking out- we are sitting, looking, and i am listening- listening, sitting, looking- listening and finally that stevie wonder white label remix comes on-</p>\r\n<p align=\'justify\'>\r\nlistening and stevie sings if you really love me and i smile- she is sitting, looking, and i turn to her and give her the headphones and say, listen to this, this is good- and she listens-</p>\r\n<p align=\'justify\'>\r\nand we are sitting, looking- she is listening and i am smiling- and she nods her head to the song and she smiles too- we are sitting and looking and the sun is shining in and she is listening-</p>\r\n<p align=\'justify\'>\r\nsitting, listening, and she (without looking) grabs the chain that holds my wallet to my pants and she holds it in her hand.</P>'),(946,'i had an interview this morning downtown. what a morning it was... my dr. pepper explodes all over me as i drive down mopac. shit. fuck damn. then i get downtown and realize i forgot to bring change. no, no... scratch that... i forgot to bring ANY money. and i forgot my atm card. stupid parking meters. it\'s okay though... steph works downtown. i\'ll pop my head in and borrow some change from her. shit. damn. fuck. she\'s not at work yet. won\'t be in until 9. my interview is at 9, and i\'m parked 4 blocks away. shit.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nso i walk back outside, and decide to read the meter... not being very educated on the whole parking meter thing, i wanted to know what the worst case scenario was for me parking without feeding the meter. nothing. no consequences listed. damn. ahh... i see a police officer parked close by, i\'ll ask her. i motion to the officer to roll her window down, and ask what would happen if i left my car there without paying the meter. she asks if i have any tickets already. no. she says they\'ll ticket me. whew! i say my thank yous and go on to explain that being the dumbass that i am, i left home without any money and without my atm card. i explained that i was worried i would get towed, but that i could deal with just getting a ticket.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe asks how long i am planning on being down there. i don\'t know, i say, i have an interview. she begins digging around in her purse and finally produces a dollar in quarters. i thank her profusely, walk to the meter, and stuff it. 1:20. yes, yes. as i\'m walking past the officer, she calls to me...<br> \r\n<br>\r\n\"Good luck on your interview.\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsome people kick so much ass.'),(947,'these things don\'t exist.  except in my mind.\r\nbut they could exist in the real world.  either, because they are makeable, or i am simply not aware of their existence. they are all certainly possible.  they would exist, except, i lack two resources: time, and money. \r\n<P>\r\n1. i want a laptop to have a pull out mouse that attaches to the laptop (with springy retractable cord, or maybe wireless mouse).  also, optical.\r\n<BR>\r\n1a. okay, maybe home wireless mouse magically works with laptop if you ask.  that\'d be nice.\r\n<BR>\r\n1b. also, shawna wants wireless optical mice.  \r\n<P>\r\nthere are lots of things like this.   i think everyone should make this kind of list.  then companies would know what people really want, instead of trying to guess and do dumb marketing to make you want to buy it.\r\n<P>\r\nsome other ideas, equally unlikely, and less generally applicable.l\r\n<P>\r\nuse spare wires in wire from telephone pole to house for alternate functions:\r\n<P>\r\npower led\'s on pole (or other lowpower stuff)\r\nlow voltage control\r\nweather sensors\r\n<P>\r\n'),(948,'<P>i heard bout the anime club durin open house and decided to check it out.  its held in the huge lecture hall on campus which can seat up to 200 peeps.  i walked in and saw 30 of the most hardcore anime kids around.  they were trading anime tapes like mad, furiously typing on their lap tops for some reason, and letting their glasses gleam under the flourescent lights.  i admit, i was scared.  then i whipped out my glasses and i\'ve been goin every week since.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnext week is their tenth anniversary. im bringin my bf. fun stuff</P>'),(949,'<P>Diaries.  For some reason, I feel there\'s a \"sissy\" connotation that accompanies that word; however, I\'ve done it for years: kept diaries, journals, things of the written persuasion.  As I write them, I\'m never quite sure why I\'m doing it; why I\'m spending the time and exerting the enery and enduring the carpal stress.  In fact, I never understand why I do it until I stumble upon an old journal unexpectedly, just as I have tonight.</P><P>After reading it through, I realize that I may still be a little girl.  I\'m afraid that I\'ve spent too much time investing my beliefs in the ideal that I\'m a Grown-Up, with adult emotions and demeanor.  But as I review the poorly structured, puerile, and shallow ramblings of two and three years ago, a recurring notion crosses my mind: I have no clue.  About anything.  Anything at all.</P><P>My journals are filled with run-downs of events at late-night Denny\'s: who spilled what, who\'s working late, who sat on whose lap, who the unfamiliar face at the counter is.  Sometimes, if I were engaged in trying to bring an illogical impulse to fruition (which was often the case), such as packing up all my belongings and moving to Philaldephia, I would have specified \"Project\" pages, where I outlined my objectives, my plans of action, my goals.  ...All of which were completely irrational and virtually impossible considering I was 18 or 19 years old, working in a drugstore and living with Mom.</P><P>What have I learned so far in this life?  What do these journals tell me about myself?  What can I say has shaped me into who I am today?  I don\'t know.  I\'m left clueless.  Each entry is comprised of a new love-interest, a new friend, or a new dessert menu item.  I talk about all sorts of useless things in these entries, frequently referring either to how \"happy\" or \"pissed off\" I am.  I am Generic to a T.  Of course, my words do make me laugh... but they don\'t teach me anything.  Nothing I can work with... and that\'s what troubles me.  I read pages upon pages or entries, and I don\'t learn a thing about myself.  Rather, I\'m reminded of silly and inconsequential events.  Nothing more.  If anything, I feel as though I \"dumb down\" just to understand the points I was trying to convey.</P><P>Why do we write...? For other people?  For ourselves?  To clear our heads, our hearts?  Is it selfish, or self-less?  Am I the only one with this problem?</P><P>Why do I want to learn now, and how is this any different than before?</P>'),(950,'<p>\r\ni didn\'t expect him to call, twice, with excuses i didn\'t believe, and\r\ni had no choice but to believe them, twice. how can i trust my own\r\nmemory, with its inventive nature and deceptive habit of rewriting\r\nbiography into fiction.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut it was too unlikely and too well-rehearsed; concequently i ignored\r\nhis appeal, speaking instead with a callous tone that asserted the\r\ntransparency of his cue card.\r\n</p><p>\r\ndisbelief is astonishment turned filter, not coloring but inverting\r\nevery other frame 30 times each second, turning your head just as \r\nquickly to see what, you hope, is upright.     \r\n</p>'),(951,'A fear that I have is being old and alone.<BR>\r\nActually I don\'t really fear it, but I just think it would be terrible.<BR>\r\nHave you ever seen old people in low-rent apartments, living in dreary rooms alone with a funny smell? Or mumbling and incoherent in some \"old-person\'s home\" where it smells like piss?<BR><BR>\r\nAnyway this isn\'t a real fear, but I can see the beginnings of it within myself. The phases in your life are seen just for what they are: periods that come and go that won\'t ever be revisited, filled with people that have faded away, interests that have been forgotten, dreams abondoned, ideals corroded away. Meanwhile your body is very gradually falling apart.<BR><BR>\r\nNostalgia and a perpetual adolescent feeling are two big themes of mine.\r\nI notice a drying up within, a lack of vividness and drama that I used to have. It doesn\'t really bother me. It\'s just part of getting older. I\'m ambivalent. I feel kind of worried or nostalgic about it, but then that seems like a whiner kind of attitude and so I just totally accept it. Then I start to feel numb and grizzled, and proud of that in a strange way. It\'s kind of a proud bitterness that says \"fuck you, fuck this\". It gives me this kind of anger and energy. It\'s kind of like the anger I used to have when I was adolescent age, only now more focused and patient.<BR>\r\nThere was a 20yr old guy at my work who had a lot of attitude, and was a really arrogant know-it-all, in that low-key, geek programmer kind of way.<BR>\r\nAfter a lot of condescending and pretentious disrespect from him I really started to have this anger. I kept thinking to myself \"you\'re going to get squashed like a grape you punk-ass.\"<BR>\r\nAnd so after patiently putting up with this, I saw his little project get canned, followed by him not coming back to work anymore after being so thoroughly pissed/dissed himself. Part of me felt like a WWF wrestler inside inside arms over my head \"Yes...\". I did not feel bad. Some people need to learn humility.<BR>\r\nI do at least acknowledge that makes me something of an asshole myself. I would not try to presume anything else. It just sucks when you\'re pugnacious and defensive though when people give you shit.<BR><BR>\r\nLife, reality, has that great quality about it of destruction and impermanence. Forget the old-age is a drag angle from before. Embrace your inevitable destruction. Enjoy it. Let go of it.<BR>\r\nWe all adore our bullshit that says that we\'re so special or great or whatever, what we want, what we\'re going to do, what we think, what we feel, who we are, blah, blah, blah... ego. Who cares.<BR>The fire of life gradually burns it all up. Flesh and bones, ideas, feelings... all of it going away, gone. You\'re on the temporary ride here. Get used to it.<BR>\r\nIt\'s great. Everything is equalized eventually. It\'s just a matter of time. I can\'t really do it actually, but it\'s best to just let it burn. Give it up and let the manure of your existence burn in the pure clean fire of reality.<BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(952,'<p>\r\nLast night my business partner and i, with a few friends along, suited up and fought on and off for around two hours in the park two blocks over from my apartment.  We\'d been trying to find time for this for a while, it was exciting when he rang the doorbell carrying two enormous gear bags.  I\'d been in a terrible mood all day at work, and I was missing the soccer match, so i really needed to do something fun.  My roomate Ari and some of her friends came along, talking and laughing, sometimes asking us a question.  We stopped in at the coffee shop for bottled water; there our little troupe picked up several others.  With a shit-eating grin on my face, i led the procession to the 12th street park.  There Steve and I each took a red man suit and donned it.  The park was filled with teenagers and a group of men playing basketball.  It was a beautiful night, and i was trembling with excitement, it\'s been so long since i\'ve been able to really fight someone.  After demonstrating some of the movements we were likely to use, we squared off and went at it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSteve and I fought and in between fighting, drank water and giggled.  People would ask questions about our class, and \"would i be able to do that?!\"  When we stopped for the night i was smiling so much that my head was aching.  I was completely drained.  Last night i thought my city was beautiful, my friends were perfect, my life was full of hope.  Last night i was able to be the warrior again, if only for a few hours.  I can\'t describe how good it felt, but i think everyone has something in their life which brings this feeling.  Our friends were excited about us starting the classes, and kept asking how long.  Strangers in the park had stopped to watch and they too had questions.  We adjourned to the coffee shop for more water and laid it all out for everyone who was interested.  It\'s been a long time since i\'d felt so unagressive, fuck, i felt downright friendly.  My head was still aching from smiling more than i\'m used to.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith luck, i\'ll soon be doing this kind of thing almost every night.  I wonder how that will effect my moods.  Is this what i\'ve been missing for so long.  There was a time when i could do it or something similar almost every day.  Swordfighting one night, militia training the next, a punch up the next.  But then i was younger and not as tested as i am now.  I was not happy then, nor am i now.  But i almost look forward to work right now, and i really do look forward to the future.  I can\'t wait to see what happens when i leave my apartment today.  The only thing really missing last night and today, are my two most valued friends; Darwin and Dawn, whom i miss terribly and despair of ever seeing again.  Oh, and fighting gets me terribly horny and i\'m single.  Ah well.  Gotta take the best i can and no complaints.\r\n</p>'),(953,'<P>It\'s after 4 am in Atlanta, GA, on an early summer night, wet and buzzing with insects. We are crammed into my little red Escort, and I am trying desperately to steer it deep into the southwest end of Atlanta. Bankhead highway slips away behind us, and I bounce my shoulders up and down in time with the bassline pumping from my shitty soundsystem. Bankhead bounce baby. We goin\' undahcovah tonight. There are fewer streetlights down here. An underpass. Tall grass.</P>\r\n<P>My best friend Tom is squirming around in the passenger seat, alternately screaming out the window and attempting to tweak my factory stereo. He throws on a tape emblazoned with the words \"DJ TWONZ : DON\'T FUCK WITH DETROIT.\" The speaker on the driver\'s side had blown out from such activity long before, and now it sadly farted along to the sheer terror of hard, fast techno beats. In the back seat, my roommate Jeff was pushing on Tom\'s seat with his knees, trying to shove Tom\'s 6\' 4\" frame an inch forward. Tom stops screaming \"YOU CAN\'T FIGHT THE MOONLIGHT\" out the window long enough to notice. He turns around in his seat and starts yelling a string of expletives at Jeff, who yells back. At this point Tom unbuckles his seatbelt in order to climb into the back to strangle Jeff. The car veers down a pitch black road, and I begin screaming with them. At them. SIT THE FUCK DOWN AND SHUT THE FUCK UP RIGHT NOW YOU STUPID FUCKS FOR FUCKS SAKE!! FUCK YOU! WE ARE GOING TO GO RAVE OKAY SO JUST SHUT THE FUCK UP!! CAN\'T YOU STAY CALM LONG ENOUGH TO GO FROM ONE DANCEFLOOR TO THE NEXT! SHUT UP YOU STUPID CUNT AND DRIVE! I\'LL KILL YOU! YOU HONKY JIZZMEISTER AAAAAAAAAAAGH!</P>\r\n<P>We get to this....place. I have been here before. It\'s completely illegal. There are underage children here, dancing all night and taking drugs. The space is inside an immense Uhaul warehouse strip, and standing next to the strip is probably the coolest structure in Atlanta, a 4 story bright orange tower. It looks much like a lighthouse, and someone even lives in it. In the back, ironically, is an old school bus and a steep grassy embankment. People sit on the hill and snort meth, or smoke weed laced with meth, or shoot things up. There is a small herd of goats that runs around, confused by all the ravers.</P>\r\n<P>I pull the decibel-splitting escort up to a chain-link gated entrance leading into the parking lot. There are rows and rows of cars here, many of them obviously belonging to affluent north Atlanta parents. A lookout is posted at the gate, and he looks at us and laughs. He opens the gate and calls after the Escort, \"Have a good time, kids.\"</P>\r\nAs we walk towards the warehouse, kids are partying inbetween the rows of cars, eyeing us cautiously. Tom is pretty much running to the door, trying to get inside to dance and talk shit. I hear something clatter on the gound. I look down. A hypodermic.</P>\r\n<P>Inside, I find my friends in the drum n\' bass room, which is walled off from the main room by a curtain of hefty bags and a PA stack. Haze has a mag light clenched in his teeth, trying to wire the soundsystem together. It fried ass right before we got there. He spends an hour trying to get it going, and finally, terse, crisp breakbeats fragment the muffled 4/4 beats thumping under our feet.</P>\r\n<P>I look up, through a dirty greenish skylight, and notice it is a lighter rectangle than the dark room I am in. The dawn.</P> '),(954,'<p>\r\nIt wasn\'t until around 4:30pm that i finally left my house today.  I was tired from the fight the night before, and although very hungry, just couldn\'t get my sorry ass in gear.  once i left i almost immediately wished i\'d stayed home, the coffee shop was barren of friendly faces; mind, i don\'t have that many friends here so i wasn\'t all too suprised.  So i sit down with a coffee and a water and do some more research for our class.  Maybe a half hour goes by when two older guys i know, fellow recovering addicts, show up.  One of them has brought his 1 year old daughter.  They sit down at my table and i just brighten when i see my buddie\'s daughter.  This beautiful, smiling creature.  All coos and raspberries.  And fearsome big blue eyes, taking everything in.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni played with her, watching how quickly she learned the games i made up.  clucking to myself in between discussing business matters with my friends.  I held her for a while, letting her sharp nails (needed trimmed!) rip my arms up a bit.  She liked chewing on my eye glasses.  I thought about when my sara was pregnant, and my hopes for our child.  And the miscarriage.  I didn\'t feel sad, i felt wonderful.  When i gave her back, the three of us were all grinning like idiots.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe were alternating between playing hide and seek with her, and chatting about various things, just enjoying the afternoon, when a punk girl walked by us.  I looked her over, her face was so sad, i\'m not used to seeing such sadness except in the mirror.  My glance went over her arms, and stopped at her wrist, it was bandaged.  It was obviously very recent as the bandage was being soaked through with blood.  I caught the attention of my friends and pointed to my wrist, drew my fingernail back from my inside palm to my elbow.  Pointed at her.  They nodded and looked sad.  So two of us got up and asked her to join us.  She had a sketch pad, and showing was a drawing of a woman\'s face, torn and bloody.  It wasn\'t grotesque in any way.  I thought it quite wonderful actually, but so very sad.  She sat, and we asked her how long it had been.  \"Got out this morning.\" she said, she seemed uncomfortable, unable to look us in the eyes.  but the baby, she knew, she knew just what to do.  she pointed to this girl, Natalie, and smiled so big, drool spilling out of her little mouth.  and Natalie stares emptily at the baby, then her face twitched, slowly becoming a grudging smile.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy buddy asked her to draw his daughter, and she did a wonderful job, catching those big eyes so full of joy.  and that kick ass baby smile.  while she drew we talked to her, just being nice, letting her know she\'s not alone.  that where ever she\'s been, we\'ve been some place similar.  that she needn\'t feel hopeless and useless.  i bought her a coffee and we continued to talk.  sometimes she reminded me of a deer caught in headlights.  when she left, her face wasn\'t so gray.  and the drawing showing on her notebook\'s face was of a little baby girl and our phone numbers in case she needed to talk to someone.  it was a good afternoon.\r\n</p>'),(955,'<p>I am a sad artifact<br>\r\n  maybe an architect of the failed formation of words.<br>\r\n  with a body on autopilot and one track mind.<br>\r\n  a ticking clock waiting for certain words to disappear<br>\r\n  from my language.<br>\r\n  Then I will have expired<br>\r\n  like the 90\'s <br>\r\n  in which I was never bored.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  I stare as fast as I a breathe<br>\r\n  at my failure<br>\r\n  and the seven shades of despair<br>\r\n  that stained me over the years.<br>\r\n  What comes after<br>\r\n  the incredible death of talent,<br>\r\n  the inability to survey a kingdom body,<br>\r\n  and the sickness of solitary games?<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  I trade three stars out of my universe<br>\r\n  for every motion you make<br>\r\n  away from me.<br>\r\n  I trade my oxygen<br>\r\n  for that strawberry perfume<br>\r\n  in her skin, as an homage to a year gone by.<br>\r\n  this is me giving up. <br>'),(956,'<P>trying to see if i get past the censors...\r\n<br><BR>\r\nsee,,, i was ignorant...i had no idea that people moderated the postings...since the recent dialogues regarding the editing of the site i have watched the site in a different manner...meaning now i expect more...because if there is a team of people deciding what gets passes and what does not- i assume the content will somehow be enhanced ...but maybe not...now i wonder what the hell possessed the passing of a certain article over another and what delcicious morsels were vetoed by the powers that be...an interesting thing to think about...especially since i dont know all the editors..nor their particular tastes and predisposition towards certain forms of writing./can i apply for an editor position?....//i always thought i could write simply: \"i stepped on a shoe\" and it would go live (even though i wrote no such postings). But... the fakt that i could if i wanted to gave me a certain potential energy that now is lost.  Such is the way of attachment to how we want things to be and the streamlinedness of efficiency and dedicated purpose... :)  ah ...the life ---- just when you think you have a handle on it -    it slips thru your grasp.   i love it....for as long as it lasts......i wish you all well........</P>'),(957,'<p>\r\nholy shit im drunnnk. i wanted to ask to the bartender out but that just seems so cliche\'d i didnt. you know....the whole asking a bartender out is just cliche...sorta like askiing a flight attendant to help you join the mile high club. just ludicrous. oh man so anyway...i have pretty much come to the realization that im not going to get anywhere with  stinder and that she has her own problems to fix and that by me trying to be with her i just make new problems for her. it sucks. i dont think any of you will even understand how much this sucks for me. i really dont. i think everyone who reads this looks at it from a 1+1=2 point of view and while thats just really selfish i just feel that everyone who was apart of this whole thing just dint give it a chance....im frustrated. im drunk. im reaading a book called \"sick puppy\" by carl hiaasen and its really funny and takes my mind off of things. i went to the bathroom a few minutes ago and the season finale of simpsons was funny and so was the finale of malcomn in the middle. why does life not work out for so many and for those it does it seems like they were given shit on a silver platter? im frustrated with the world and also my personal ideology. im changing at a rapid pace and my personal beleifs have trouble keeping up with the whole mess and i am unable to legitamize the change. i have no point of view for the first time in my life and im  scared. i dont know what tbglk;sfe fuck is goiig on and it scares me more then capitalism. im just so unsure of everything aaround me and when i see certain people it throws me deeper into the hole of everything. i just wish i could take the blue pills with pms on them and not worry. i wish i could smoke a doobie without feeling guilty with myself. what the fuck has happened to me? i just  dont know. its like im slanting towards the right and forgetting everythiiiiing i held so dear on the left. my hair is constantly wet from stress and i dont dream anything familiar. i dream dreams that are confused and end up in me crying. i just dont know what the fuck is going on in my life...and that scares me. what hte fuck. what ethe fucking fuck. what      the fucking fuckity fuck fuck fuck? ugh. none of you will understand...and if you do...you will not admit to it. its shameful....its pussy...its panzy...its just me being so blind to the fact that what i want is uncontrollable and unacheivable. fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fukc ufkuckufkdjsl;kfjsdk;fjsdk;fjas;dkfj;. '),(958,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/958_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>11:11pm</P>\r\n<p>a not so hot boring monday night.</P>\r\n<p>Dan Clowes sums it all up for me.</P>\r\n'),(959,'<p>\r\nI have this pen at this job, it is covered in gooey plastic stuff that makes it feel better to grip.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was in a meeting at this place I used to work called Organic, in a meeting about template structures and I started pushing and pulling the gooey stuff off.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo weeks later I was laid off and put all my stuff in boxes. When I got my new job downtown I took some stuff in here, including that pen. Now I look at it and remember the meeting, what the light was like in the room, thinking, \"I wonder how much longer I will be here.\" Now I am a brightly lit quiet office and the Pacific Stock Exchange is under my feet many floors down, sirens wail and protesters hoot....and my pen is still with me. How did it all happen so fast?</P>\r\n'),(960,'<P>it is tuesday afternoon.  today i do nothing.  i went out drinking last night and had a below the shelf shot of poison that nailed my coffin shut. always pay the extra dollar.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i never do these journal things where the writer drops names and details.  maybe here and there i swallow the lump in my throat and divulge, but on the whole, as s5 says, \"you keep things at an arms length and never get to close.\"  i want to get close now.  i want to tell you exactly what i did last night and how i felt.  instead, i wrote it in my paperback.  the cheap grocery store novel lives on, on my night stand.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am still looking for a job.  one company calls me every now and again to ask if i am still interested.  it is a surreal feeling.  i want to cut them off and ask them if they are still interested?  it has been so long.  i am going to apply to assist a welder in southbay to  prepare for his art creations.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i miss the chaos of a creators work space.  i got to thinking about it on end when i had a conversation with <a href=\"http://www.eggthings.com\">egg</a> about crafting.  she made doors for her warehouse in oakland.  i saw a picture in smithsonian magazine of alexander calder\'s studio with tools everywhere, files layed out and crossed, boxes of wire and sheets of metal everywhere, and calder sitting on a stool the artist among it all.  he knew where everything was exactly.  it looks cramped.  it looks chaotic.  it is not though, only the environment of creation.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the picture took me back to <a href=\"http://www.cmpbs.org\">the center</a> where i worked for over a year.  one day i spend yours in the shed heated to oven temperatures griding 3/4 inch bolts down from an inch in length to half an inch.  the threading on the bolts had to be ground just right, then polished or they were useless.  i joked that i was working at the franklin mint.  it took days to do them all.  repetitive.  and i liked it.  huge fans blew the hot air all around and a little radio blared KOOP punk rock in between the screaming of the grinder.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>nostalgia wants that life back with it\'s good people, hot weather, and cold mexican beer.  i think my life will go back there because so many people want to feel that.  they want to work with their hands for next to nothing.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>that place had an economy all its own.  parts for whatever creation was on the mind were in abundance.  there was a shack filled with motors.  a welding shed.  a field of castaside machinery.  a room for solidifying earth mixtures.  i could go on.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the only thing that place lacked was sanity.  the heat would drive people out of their minds.  there was always stress to get projects done on time, and plenty of projects that did not have a timeline, but had been going on so long that they were considered the most important item to work on.  and there were several huge projects that never did get finished because there were fundamental flaws in their design that with the limitations of the rough building materials, could not be ground or bend to work properly.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there is nothing quite like being forced to work on something that you know will not work, just to see how well it does not work.  and then there was making things look good that did not work.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>talking about this helps i suppose.  i will just put it back in the drawer of my memory with all the files, between the drawer with all the planes and the drawer with all the shears.  i made icons once so volunteers could find tools.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(961,'<p>\r\nThe phone rang the other night, and I jumped at the disturbance in the air it caused.  That disturbance: definitely a rarity in my house, as I dissolve into the couch while Drew Carey spews shtick on TV.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHello, yes, oh, hi, how the hell have you been, haven\'t seen you in years. Moved back to NY? Hey, that\'s great. Yeah. Mm-hmmm. Coffee? Sure. Tonight? Yeah, I can be there in like twenty minutes, yeah, no problem.  See ya.\r\n</p><p>\r\nTwenty seconds later, I\'m overwhelmed by confusion. Why am I going out? I don\'t even want to go out. I\'d rather stay in with Drew Carey\'s BCGs and be \"one\" with the couch. Where are my shoes.. should I fix my hair? Dammit, where\'s that freaking lighter, much less my cigarettes, oh - found my sandals.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI crawl out of the house, and I feel the suction pull me back as I shut the door behind me. My car sits frozen in the driveway from lack of use, and the door cries out in arthritic pain as I swing it open and climb into my seat. I\'m suddenly invisible in the dark, quietly watching the trees in the yard as they signal their approval of my departure. I want to cry. Cleveland Rocks.\r\n</p>'),(962,'<p>\r\nupstairs vs downstairs: hurried typing in separate rooms, fingers\r\nracing against the half-life of an inspired moment. last night was the\r\nbirthday of gemini, and how better to celebrate, than to manipulate\r\nwords about manipulation.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe twins of the zodiac: opposite but equal, never caring to merge or\r\ndissolve the detached space between them. simply carrying forward with\r\nduplicate (but conflicted) passions.\r\n</p>'),(963,'<P>     The churling dreams I was having subsided and that left me wide and clearly awake at 5:15AM.</P>\r\n<P>     I got up to start my day, which is always easier on my days off from work.  Most days my day starts with meditation.</P>\r\n<P>     In the seven seconds it takes to go from my bed to the bathroom sink I remembered the indulgence of the night before:  sugar.  A big ol\' gluttonous sugar Affair.  I laughed to recall how, when I used to drink, I\'d wake up just as early but less clearly after bingeing.  Which is not to say I\'m anymore content about sugar binges than I am about booze binges.  Alcohol is easier for me to ignore entirely and sugar, as a wise woman once pointed out, is my Dark Teacher.</P>\r\n<P>     Oh well.  Last night is over. Start again.</P>\r\n<P>\r\n<P>My meditation.</P>\r\n<P>\r\n<P>     In February I took a 10 day Vipassana Meditation Course in Kaufman, TX (<a href=\"http://www.siri.dhamma.org\">http://www.siri.dhamma.org</a>).  Kaufman is about 45 minutes SE of Dallas.  Being located in Kaufman is the Southwest Vipassana Meditation Center\'s greatest inconvenience as well as its greatest asset.  It takes a fair effort to get there but once you are there there\'s not much there there, which is just the thing for meditation.</P>\r\n<P>     The course changed me, changed my life.  I\'ve never done anything so worthwhile.</P>\r\n<P>     I now maintain my meditation practice on a daily basis.  This all occurs to me today because in a few hours I leave for Kaufman to volunteer, or serve, for the next course.  There are at last report 50 people signed up for this course and, as of a week and a half ago, no one signed up to staff the Center, feed, and support the meditators.  The course is hard work.  It\'s very structured and precise, and one needs lots of support in completing it.  I received so much benefit from my 10 day course and the strangers that served it that it truly is for me a pleasure and privilege to do the same.  I get to recharge my own batteries there too.</P>\r\n<P>     The other thought that occurs to me about my practice this morning is how easy it was for me to rise from my bed in the dark at 5:15AM.  In the almost three months since the course the battles have varied in their frequency and intensity when it has come to actually sitting down to meditate twice a day.</P>\r\n<P>     Initially my inspiration came from the fear of breaking my stride.  I had established my practice in the 10 days and now was afraid I\'d lose it by flaking out or quitting.  But while I\'ve quit lots of things in my life (and derided myself for it occasionally), in hindsight it\'s all been for the best.  I know a lot more as a result of that process about what I do and do not want my life to be.</P>\r\n<P>     Then there is obligation.  On the rougher, tougher days - the days where maybe I wasn\'t so nice to myself or I was less aware than I would prefer to be - obligation is the thing that got me to sit down on my mat.  Once I sit down and start I\'m usually set.  But obliged to whom?  Obliged, I think, to the calmer, more centered, more aware version of myself with whom I made the deepest contact yet at the Center.  I remember that she\'s still in me and that all things considered meditating is really what I\'d rather be doing.  More than sleeping later, or going out, or just futzing (sp?) around the house.  I still have plenty of time for all of those things.</P>\r\n<P>     Today, however, it was neither fear nor obligation that got me to my mat.  Today it was just what I did because it is just what I do.  And that is one of the greatest benefits of this Meditation so far.</P>            '),(964,'\r\n<p>\r\nJust as Thomas is walking through the park lamenting the bad weather, the melody he is listening to through his two can-like headphones clouds his vision. A D, an E minor, and a C# are getting harder and noisier after each repetition; chords are coming down like bricks, a guitar soars placidly with arpeggios, a keyboard is overflowing with blue electric rivers.  Thomas feels a waterfall of ice-cold water going down his body, but when he looks at himself he sees and smells nothing.  Later, eight lasers in green and orange color invade the audiovisual fantasy with violin melodies, cutting through the landscape.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThomas feels cramps in his stomach.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHis vision is getting heavy and he is now down on his knees; a drum comes in, and it is a hail storm.  Thomas is hurt with a hundred aching lumps.  Then he feels another flow of ice cold water washing his body but then this furnace-like heat comes like a wave that melts his hair.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"Is it coming?\" thinks Thomas.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe orchestra is going around a sequence of three notes played by an electric bass; it sounds like a horror movie.  The sound that Thomas fears so much is coming slowly in the background; now he feels two huge hands trying to crush him from the sides.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThomas agonizes.  Nothing is coming out. The sound continues to gain independence from the mix of harmony and cacophony and it is all just a a matter of seconds...\r\n</p><p>\r\nSuddenly, Thomas gives in to the pressure; the huge hands win over his body\'s resistance and they press on his lungs and his stomach, and from his mouth comes the mind-blowing scream of an eagle on fire that evokes an accordion playing a chord in G minor.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIn a screen, a black and white movie in projected, in which we see a couple dancing an aggressive tango.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe music continues; the drums get a bit sleepy for a while, but they come back thundering in distempo and the accordion burns the sky and the stars and everything goes dark as the night in the desert...\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"hey man, are you ok?\" \r\n</p><p>\r\nThomas wakes up... He is face down on the floor, his headphones on, his tango CD playing at full volume, and on his face and clothing are a hotdog and a pizza in the form of vomit that also color the lawn of Central Park.  Thomas gets up; he cleans some of the vomit, thanks the American youth that has helped him, and heads towards the subway station to catch the train to his dorm room at NYU.</p>'),(965,'Just as Thomas is walking through the park lamenting the bad weather, the melody he is listening to through his two can-like headphones clouds his vision. A D, an E minor, and a C# are getting harder and noisier after each repetition; chords are coming down like bricks, a guitar soars placidly with arpeggios, a keyboard is overflowing with blue electric rivers.  Thomas feels a waterfall of ice-cold water going down his body, but when he looks at himself he sees and smells nothing.  Later, eight lasers in green and orange color invade the audiovisual fantasy with violin melodies, cutting through the landscape.\r\n<p>\r\nThomas feels cramps in his stomach.\r\n<p>\r\nHis vision is getting heavy and he is now down on his knees; a drum comes in, and it is a hail storm.  Thomas is hurt with a hundred aching lumps.  Then he feels another flow of ice cold water washing his body but then this furnace-like heat comes like a wave that melts his hair.\r\n<p>\r\n\"Is it coming?\" thinks Thomas.\r\n<p>\r\nThe orchestra is going around a sequence of three notes played by an electric bass; it sounds like a horror movie.  The sound that Thomas fears so much is coming slowly in the background; now he feels two huge hands trying to crush him from the sides.\r\n</br>\r\nThomas agonizes.  Nothing is coming out. The sound continues to gain independence from the mix of harmony and cacophony and it is all just a a matter of seconds...\r\n<p>\r\nSuddenly, Thomas gives in to the pressure; the huge hands win over his body\'s resistance and they press on his lungs and his stomach, and from his mouth comes the mind-blowing scream of an eagle on fire that evokes an accordion playing a chord in G minor.\r\n<p>\r\nIn a screen, a black and white movie in projected, in which we see a couple dancing an aggressive tango.\r\n<p>\r\nThe music continues; the drums get a bit sleepy for a while, but they come back thundering in distempo and the accordion burns the sky and the stars and everything goes dark as the night in the desert...\r\n<p>\r\n\"hey man, are you ok?\" \r\n<p>\r\nThomas wakes up... He is face down on the floor, his headphones on, his tango CD playing at full volume, and on his face and clothing are a hotdog and a pizza in the form of vomit that also color the lawn of Central Park.  Thomas gets up; he cleans some of the vomit, thanks the American youth that has helped him, and heads towards the subway station to catch the train to his dorm room at NYU.'),(966,'<p>\r\nsitting in the duke i had a couple of pints of the carlsburg years and a bacon cheese chicken burger. I can just see some of you cringe thinking inside that im the equivelant of a baby killer. I read my book. people watched. at the time not many people were there so i got more reading then watching done. better that way and im releived.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlots of old men were there at about 2:30 when the valencia milan game was on. football makes me listen. the crowds chant in moans and waves of emotion can be heard over the commentators thick scotish and welsh accents. i sat there trying to concentrate on the pages, the penalty kick getting my attention over the potential rape of a literary character.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIve been in a weird good mood. a good mood which makes me uneasy. good moods for me often result in some sort of crisis or letdown. Ive received email suprisingly from some people i thought didnt like to email and ive been holding things back from people. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhite silence is better then dark thoughts i always convince myself of that fact. i listen and look at the people im around and for every good story there is a bad one. for every bad story there is one worse and it is because of this rational that i think life is beginning to look up. the sunrise has take a long time but may, just maybe i am beginning to understand the world around me. \r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/966_1.jpg\' width=\"500\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p>\r\n'),(967,'<P>\r\nsam drove me over the bridge yesterday morning. they are always laying wagers on whether i will do what i say ill do. we were supposed to go record shopping last night. Monday night I drank and sam predicted that i would be hungover and yell at everyone and say i as too tired to go record shopping. it\\\'s a dare. it\\\'s a passive aggressive strategy. \r\n</P><p>\r\nI tried to lecture him on his sugar intake. this is why you look like an old migrant farm worker all day long, I say. your goddamn droppy eyes and grumpy disposition, with only spurts of cheer created by songs about charm bracelets by the All Girl Summer Fun Band, your old man perversions awakened by morning sugar and girl singers who suck helium. THIS. wielding the Lucky Charms box that shared the passenger seat with me. \r\n</P><p>\r\n\\\"gotta have one vice.\\\" the economy of a junkie\\\'s words. \r\n</P><p>\r\n\\\"You eat too much easy food! Today we will go to lunch and you will eat a salad, with egg and chicken in it. protein, goddamnit. iron. potassium.\\\" I read aloud the nutritional value on the side of the Lucky Charms. \\\"pure glucose. you are like Little Krissy, Pecker\\\'s sister.\\\" \r\n</P><p>\r\nI opened the box and picked out a marshmallow for myself. \\\"what is this?\\\" an orange blob. I consulted the pictures on the box. There were alien objects on display.\r\n</P><p>\r\n\\\"a pot of gold.\\\"\r\n</P><p>\r\n\\\"WHAT IS THIS!!\\\" I wouldnt listen. \\\"THIs- This- Revolt against Abstraction???! this. this. why did they have to change the charm collection? why a pot of gold? ever since they started with the purple horseshoes when i was a kid i should have known it wouldnt stop there. how possible is it to make a quarter inch piece of marshmallow into a pot of gold? what was wrong with orange stars? why did it have to morph into SHOOTING stars, detailed by creating a white background? why not a fifth of fucking jamesons for cryin nothings ever good enough. everythingsorangethisweekthats funnywhat did we need new ones formutherfuckin cockeaters\\\" in caffeine tears, coffee wearing off, beginning to remember Monday night, I pulled a few more marshmallows out of the box and chewed them on my way into work.  \r\n</P><p>\r\nI found myself aggravated and edgy all day, like a sugar junkie with no time to grab a candy bar. probably just hung over. People were coming at me with whines and roars and others werent answering my beeps and whistles. \r\n</P><p>\r\nSam just loves the All Girl Summer Fun Band. charm bracelet, charm bracelet, 1,2,3. thats how I remember it at least. I like that song because it reminds me of 3rd grade, when I had the plastic chain I could load up at claire\\\'s gift and jewelry store in Pelham, NH with jingly yellow clocks and pink telephones, red dildos, orange hats, green clovers. any object was possible, at the height of the craze. they probably even made a pot of gold at some point.\r\n</P><p>\r\nWhen someone is new, when someone is new and his service to you is a world full of promise, you can fill all sorts of hollow time with secret jokes and fantastic conversations, the cacophony of mixed metaphors and fabricated cosmic signs, the person doesnt even have to be involved at all. you can collect people in this way, never having to get to know them but approximating their value by how much they shine in the store window. the newest charm is always the favorite.\r\n</P><p>\r\nWe did go record shopping, after I made everyone\\\'s life hell with my yelling about how every whiny little bitch in this small and sorry town is out to predict my fucking behavoir. I tried to apologize for being a haggard bitch.\r\n</P><p>\r\n\\\"it\\\'s those lucky charms,\\\" sam said. \\\"you blew your energy on them this morning.\\\" Sam is a Gemini. \r\n</P><p>\r\nMy aggravation was mollified by the soothing task of spending money. And then re-ignited by the perplexing task of making choices between charms, each new vinyl display dangling and beckoning me in disorganized seduction.\r\n</P><p>\r\nsometimes people dangle their shiny spells in front of me and there isnt a whole helluva lot i can do about it. when you are on a rocket ship experiencing intergalactic turbulence all you can do is try to keep from choking on your puke and stay strapped in. but I have never been on a rocket ship before. Im lying about the spell. I can un-zombie myself, turn my obsession to homemade curry and canopy sculptures, but it\\\'s easier to eat sugar, it\\\'s easier to turn it on someone else.\r\n</P>'),(968,'<p>\r\ndone fiddling with my web page for the night, unable to concentrate on learning CGI scripts anymore, i head out in search of coffee.  I bring only a notebook and my current research book, the Lucifer Principle, which i highly recommend by the way.  it\'s a nice evening, a cool breaze is actually blowing.  i walk past the tattoo parlor, where after work, i got in a shouting match with the owner over an argument i had with one of his employees.  i cross the street and enter the hive.  joe hands over the coffee and i sit by some old guy who does odd things to photographs and trades them for cigarettes and coffee.  i read.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni keep getting the feeling that i should go home.  that something bad is going to happen.  i ignore this and read, stopping to take a note here and there.  maybe twenty minutes go by like this.  then mark sits down and we discuss work.  i still keep wondering what\'s going to happen.  i finish my coffee and switch to water.  then dana walks in...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na fellow soccer fan, although she actually plays regularly as well; dana is the second girl i\'ve asked out here who turned out to be a lesbian.  like the last one, we ended up becoming great friends.  and like the last one, she one day, stopped hanging out with me.  she found some broad, and chased her for months.  finally i got fed up and stopped even trying to talk to her.  when i first met the girl dana wanted so badly, i spoke to her for five minutes.  then i took dana aside and said: \"she\'s bad news, a damned whore and no mistake.\"  cruel?  maybe.  but i\'ve gotten good at judging whether or not people are worth letting into my life.  i do indeed make mistakes, but rarely.  anyway, dana walks in and with a huge smile yells: \"dave!  you were right.  you are always right about people.  from now on i\'ll listen!  i\'m so fucking stupid.\"  she moves to hug me and i pull back.  \"dana,\" i say, \"it\'s too late for that.  i don\'t need to be told when i\'m right.  it doesn\'t matter.  i\'m only right about bad things.  and i don\'t give a toss about you anymore.  you ditched me, and that\'s not friendship.  go away.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso she goes off in a huff.  no doubt bitching to my friends about me.  ten minutes later i leave.  feeling a sudden urge to try something with my website.  not five minutes later crystal calls.  my roomate has had his nose broken.  dana, mad about what i said to her, picked a fight with some goth chick.  my roomate, matt, tried to hold her back and she smashed her head into his nose.  off to the hospital he goes.  wonderful.  on the one hand i\'m proud of him doing the right thing.  to the left however, he didn\'t remember anything i\'ve been teaching him, which saddens me.  and i don\'t want him to be hurt.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy judgements are so often correct.  and it pains me, and lessens me.  my ex fiance (or wife, depending on which laws you look at) had to almost rape me before i realized she was interested in me.  i never predict the good.  i never know the good; not in people, places, or anything.  but well do i know the bad.  where it takes me years to decide someone it truly worthwhile to me, it takes only minutes to decide they are not my kind of people.  and i\'m so often correct.  and i hate it.  being \'right\' isn\'t fun for me.  i don\'t want to be right about these things.  i want people to be more than i expect.  i want to find that, yes, i really am nothing but an asshole.  that i\'ve rejected all these people solely because i\'m a creep, and not because i somehow knew they were no good.  \r\n</p>'),(969,'<p>\r\ni unfolded and stepped into a pair of pants from the suitcase, and\r\nnoticed for the first time the unfamiliar smells collecting in the room.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni only spent half my time in this bedroom. but the other half was not\r\nspent with her. we were apart, and meant to be apart for quite some\r\ntime. i relished this thought, because it allowed both my freedom and\r\nan extended courtship.\r\n</p><p>\r\njust outside the closet is the half-filled suitcase i refill before\r\nleaving again. when i\'m away, the suitcase is miniature home (closet,\r\npantry, medicine cabinet, library); the suitcase is my dollhouse. it\r\nstays by my side, the sole consistency amidst distant couches, hotels\r\nand lovers.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni come home and drop the suitcase back in its usual spot, just outside\r\nthe closet, and instantly i forget to unpack. i continue living out of\r\nthe suitcase, until it is half-filled and useless. (the remaining half\r\nare objects less desirable than the stationary objects of home.) by\r\nthis time, i\'m ready to leave again.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni loved her because she was indifferent to my comings and goings, never\r\npresent to notice the curious fluctuation of the suitcase.\r\n</p><p>\r\none night we looked into each other\'s eyes, looking into a reflection\r\nof our own notions of love. we fell asleep beside each other, but we\r\nmet only in dreams. i whispered to her as we slept, and by morning\r\ni would wake up to find her no longer there, never there in the first\r\nplace. the eyes were my own, reflecting against the window opposite\r\nthe bed.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntwo halves form completion. home, travel. objects, air. me, her.\r\nthe stationary and reliable, the distant and fluid.\r\n</p>'),(970,'<TT>\r\n<P>\r\nso how many things cause more dread than vacationing at your in-laws?  the term \"vacationing\" is in itself a joke.  i have been trying *so hard* to be the non-insulting angel wife person, but i\'m starting to crack.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nfirst off, his family is strange in its own right (and i\'m sure nobody will deny that) and i myself am a little on the wacky side when it comes to human interaction, but we\'re who we are in totally different ways.  second off, i was raised in the middle of the south, in humidity and mosquitos and a big wide river and nice drawls and days that hold you close and carry you on.  sam was raised here, very northeast, in a world of pine trees and cold rain and wilderness-covered mountains less than an hour\'s drive away.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nthe worst part is that his family is a very indoors family - y\'know, they just like to stay inside all day.  and not to say that i don\'t have my indoor tendencies, but not when it isn\'t my house.  so i\'m going nuts here, in a land that doesn\'t in any way feel like home, with people i don\'t in any way feel comfortable around.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ni refuse to even update my site while i\'m here, because if i say what i\'m really thinking some member of the family will read it and confront me in a very bully-like way.  i hate repressing my own thoughts to keep the peace, and i know that a \"i\'m not happy here\" post wouldn\'t be a huge revelation to anyone...but that\'s just how thing are here.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nbeam me up, please.\r\n</P>\r\n</TT>'),(971,'<p>\r\nrecently, i ventured to my local dump to help my dad unload some old worthless crap. this was the first time in ages i had been to the dump and to my delight the smell of rot and decay i remember from my childhood only lingered lightly in the air. hardly noticable. the sights, however, were much worse. the reason, i am sure, is that i am older now and can understand what i see. and it\'s disgusting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso, we go to a pit and throw all the shit in the back of the truck into this pit. in the pit is a big cat driving back and forth mashing all the trash into the ground and pushing dirt over it. old lamps and styrofoam and plastic boxes and computer monitors. and what\'s left is this pit that is flat on the bottom with pieces of trash sticking up every which way.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen i started to look around and realize that all these mounds around me were the same thing. once pits that grew into small hills full of trash. and it\'s full of trash that i am partly responisble for. it is not like i really think about my trash any other time. seeing this dump filled me with such revulsion that i couldn\'t stand to look out my window. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsad.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnot just an, \'oh i\'m sad\' either. it made me sad to look at. a sad that hurt deep.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow i know that this whole society thing won\'t last all that much longer. seeing as how we are pushing the breaking point. what i wonder is after the point breaks, and the survivors start new, is what they will think of us. of me. and my wastefulness that is second nature.\r\n</p>'),(972,'<P>The tiniest things can give you pleasure when you haven\'t treated yourself for a while.  Today was a good day.  Is still a good day.  I got a paycheck for the first time in weeks, and got to leave a job I wasn\'t enjoying.  The sun is warm, and I walked around in it, feeling the sweat build up under my hoodie, and revelled in the feeling of heat.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI skipped away from the office, paycheck in my greedy little fist, grin beaming right round to the back of my head.  I wondered how I could treat myself.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI only needed a small pleasure, wondrous only to me.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI decided on my special treat:  I would buy a large foamy and sweet soy chai from the charming streetside coffee stall I walked by everyday on the way to and from the job I just quit.  I often walked by quickly, refusing to look over, trying to block out the great jazz careening out of it, whistling at me to come over and chat with the friendly and cute staff behind the glass.  I was not spending money on frivolities like large soy chais then, and it hurt to deny myself the small pleasure.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThis time I stopped.  I ordered my chai treat and paid the $3 for it.  What fun.  I waited, enjoying the funky world beat music and smelling the wafts of coffees brewing, steaming, brewing, steaming, as I waited for my yummy thing to arrive in my mouth.  Others stood around and waited, ordered, chatted, loitered. \r\n</P><P>\r\nUp comes a couple of guys, mid-twenties, kinda messy-looking.  \"Hey Buddy?\"  They ask, surprisingly congenially, for the money for a coffee, to a guy working out his sugar and cream recipe.  They got the common blow off, but rather than mumbling and toddling away, they stood around, quietly, like they might order something from the window.  They didn\'t.\r\n</P><P>\r\nInstead, they watched the barristas carefully, as one of them deftly reached in front of me and behind another man, into the basket of freshly baked goodies, and nabbed one.  They casually walked away.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNow, I was really pleased.  I rarely get to see such brazen shoplifting in action, which was a real charmer in itself.  But I absolutely fell in love with the group of bystanders.  No one made any move to stop them, and after they walked off, there wasn\'t even a hint of agitation or guilty-by-association   Everyone just kept on with their particular keepin\' on, sipping, brewing, chatting, waiting, like a theft had not happened under our noses.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI want to say to those people who I shared in the benevolent gifl of averting our eyes, \"Thank you.  That was beautiful.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nI got two treats for the price of one, and was able to partipate in group-mind charity.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nTomorrow I am getting my haircut.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nLife is sweet.  </P>'),(973,'<P>this is the first entry i ever wrote, for anything, ever </P>\r\n\r\n<P>eight years against weblogs, sullied with this whatever</P>\r\n\r\ni\'m bright and young and gifted<BR>\r\nin my autobiography<BR>\r\ni figure, who would know<BR>\r\nbetter than me?<BR>\r\ni\'m certainly the former<BR>\r\nbut i\'m not so much the latter<BR>\r\nno one\'s going to read it<BR>\r\nso i\'m sure it doesn\'t matter<BR>\r\n\r\n<P>SUBJECT TO VETO</P>\r\n<P>SUBJECT TO VETO</P>\r\n<P>SUBJECT TO VETO</P>\r\n\r\n<p>lunch was really gross, i had to spit things out into my napkin</P>\r\n\r\n<p>life is just like that, spitting gross things into napkins</P>\r\n\r\n<p>seriously, just like that, i know stuff about things, i\'m telling you about it</P>\r\n\r\n<p>and sometimes one runs out of au jus</P>\r\n<p>and the waitress, oh, the waitress, she won\'t bring you any more</P>\r\n<p> no she won\'t</P>\r\n\r\n'),(974,'<p><b>Face by <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0375504214/qid=990750459/sr=1-1/ref=sc_b_1/107-8089282-7665318\">Dan \r\n  Rhodes</a></b></p>\r\n<p>My girlfriend is so pretty that I can\'t get over it. Every week I celebrate \r\n  the alignment of her features by parading a giant photograph of her lovely face \r\n  around the town center. I\'ve written the words \"pretty face\" on the \r\n  picture\'s border, and drawn an arrow to direct people\'s attention toward it. \r\n  It\'s not bragging, because it\'s her that\'s the pretty one, not me. I\'m going \r\n  to parade every week for as long as she lets me be her boyfriend, and probably \r\n  even longer. Nothing\'s going to put me off, not even the shouts of \"Had \r\n  her\" or \"Been there.\"</p>\r\n<p>More<b> <a href=\"http://www.randomhouse.com/boldtype/0900/rhodes/excerpt.html\">here.</a></b></p>'),(975,'<p>\r\ni like swinney.org, i enjoy reading the things people here have to say.  it\'s nice to see a tiny glimpse of lives i have no part in.  but most, i greatly value the responses i get from you all.  i\'ve most certainly not been let down.  there are some wonderful eloquent writers here, and to have them attack me so scathingly is important to me.  i don\'t want a pat on the back for everything i say.  but i need criticism, else i have no way to improve myself, eh?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou will however, note that i wrote above \"tiny glimpse of lives\".  short of two people, i know no one at swinney.  so i cannot make much of a judgement about anyone here.  nor can any make a serious judgement of /my/ character.  here i do not shy away from the word \'judgement\', nor should i.  i am not ashamed to make judgements.  this is how i interact with reality.  i know full well that absolute judgement of a human is not possible.  after all, i know plenty of people who i think should die, yet they are well loved by many.  those who think i am but an asshole should consider well that i too am loved by many.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that any person who criticizes another for being judgemental is making a serious error.  life is often little more than a series of judgement calls.  which job is better, where shall we eat, which movie do i want to see, which band is superior...  this applies to people as well, and everyone knows it, whether they choose to acknowledge it or not.  when i judge a person, i am deciding whether that person fits into my life in a way that i can tolerate.  this is based on many things.  i\'ve lived a life many people wouldn\'t understand.  i don\'t think anyone really wants to hear about it, so i will avoid it.  it should suffice to say that violence and serious betrayal has figured heavily in my life to date.  i\'ve had friends turn on me, and attack me.  this is not a simple case of a friend talking shit behind my back.  i have a history with heroin, and this colors my outlook on those who use drugs.  i look for people with character, those who struggle to maintain their own personal set of morals.  who like to do interesting things, who can teach me something new, or smack me around until i relent and go do something fun.  i look for people who i can relate to.  i don\'t ask that they believe as i do.  i ask for honesty and forthrightness.  i ask for bilateral friendship.  i ask that they fight for me as i fight for them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTruth is, i ignore most of my former friends.  even the ones who\'ve stolen from me.  i don\'t hound them, threaten them, or attack them.  i simply let them alone.  but i\'ve never been one to shy away from expressing my exact feelings to or about someone.  if they approach me, i leave no doubt where i stand.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that people need to have the courage to move their arguments beyond the bandying about of axioms and famous quotes.  i collect quotes actually, i realize their power, but just because a few words sound true does not make them so.  likewise, while it shall always be acceptable to attack the beliefs of others, we must always attack our own beliefs as well.  in a short time, swinney has become an excellent place for me to do so.  here i get unexpected responses to my posts.  these in turn make me think about myself, they help me see myself through the eyes of others.  this is a good thing.  we all need to understand, that in the end, we can only make our judgements on the basis of our experiences.  this results in mistakes.  i have, and will continue to make mistakes in my judgement of humans.  when i realize these errors, i admit them, to myself and publicly.  the life i\'ve lived is not the life most people live here in America.  i made poor decisions and ended up with violence and drugs being a huge part of my life.  escaping this life has led me to always err on the side of caution.  i\'m not in this to be popular, or even to have fun, i chose to continue my life because i feel i need to be something more than i am.  i hope i shall always feel this way.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am the most loving and loyal friend you can have.  i am a dangerous and loyal enemy.  and i am a cold neutral.  if you aren\'t in my circle, you are not relevant to my life.  i see you only as a potential.  sometimes i come out of my shell and initiate contact with someone.  more often they disrupt my life, throw everything out of whack, and become well loved by me.  very often i sense that they are no good /for me/ so i send them away.  i like having friends.  i like being challenged at all levels of my life.  when i love a human, i hold little back.  this leaves me uncomfortably vunerable; i refuse to be so open with someone who seems to take advantage of me.  if this seems cruel, perhaps it is.  but i too deserve a measure of consideration, just like everyone.  i am able to enforce this sometimes by ending friendships which have become unilateral.  \r\n</p>'),(976,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/976_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>\r\nim bored. im bored a lot. i think about smoking the cheeba a lot these days but dont for the sole reason that i think life is beginning to turn. i feel that direction is only so far away now and while i smoked the green i felt it was no where. the lights turn red in my dreams and yellow in real life. slow down my head says while my legs want to speed up. deserve deserve deserve no i dont know what i deserve. nothing perhaps. i drink a bit more then i used to but i dont get drunk alone. i just end up drunk alone. i go out and try and talk. i go out and i try and look around. i go out and i try to make eye contact. contact until contact is made. its a start. i dont smile. well i do. but only to a few. its hard to smile. i feel as though smiles are meant for people. they are meant to display something and something more then cheap thrills. cheap thrills are what make people untrustworthy. cheap thrills are what makes life so boring and cheap thrills are what make us take so much for granted. i take a lot for granted but not my friends. the ones i have left. the ones i let go might not know it but i have. they complain. they are negative. 100% of the time i cant handle. i cant handle the problems when i have so many of my own to resolve. i feel selfish these days and i feel less and less socialist on an everyday basis. i feel socialist on a social level but not on the independent level and its making me kinda nervous. im lost in thought a lot of the day and confused when i dream. buckets of meat. i dont know where to put htem. i cry at the bar and i lose things easily. dreams are revealing ive learned but to what end? i try to hold back. i  think im doing pretty good. i dont mention things that make me happy and i dont mention things i think are cute. to people. to people who know i would. but its to those people i just dont know if they know. i dont know if they know how much im trying. my brother. my mom. my dad. her. life is utterly confusing and im having a hard time keeping straight. not hard in the way that im itching to go out and get high or drunk. itching in the way that id like at least one aspect of my life to go through the closure phase. id like at least one of the pop up cartoons to take shape. i dont want to cry becuase of being let down and i dont feel like being taken advantage of anymore. im just plain sick and tired of trying so hard while those around me sit idle. life will change soon. im sure of it. im optimistic. things will turn around. u-turn. retracing he years is hard. reliving the unlived youth is hard. opening up to people is hard. i dont know who wants me to open up and who doesnt. its the ones who do i want to hang on to yet i have trouble judging who does and who doesnt. life is confusing the shit out of me. social activity comes in spirts and then ends as abruptly as it starts. but you\'ve heard my repeating ass. im on repeat like history. like mistakes ive made so has society...and its because of this i dont quite understand why i get so much flack. death isnt near, but its coming. i shall try to make the best of things. i hope you all understand, but im sure im being as ambiguous as the bible right now.\r\n</p>'),(977,'<p>I am on the last leg of my five month vacation. I decided to make a journey out to New Mexico to visit old friends but more importantly my Mother whom I rarely get to see. I have been here for a week now and I have spent very little time with my Mom. I am kinda sad about that. Saturday I have the option to go to my Brother\'s surprise belated birthday party hosted by his fiance\' and friends. I want to be there, but it will be the only day that I will have to spend alone with my Mom. Recent events, death of a friend, vanishing of a friend, getting layed off of my cushy job, blossuming romantic relationship have all lead me to this point of self evaluation and self gratification. I feel as though I am the most selfish person in this world! I am all about myself and what I want to do, see and hear. I have a newfound lust for my life, my family and my relationships. Yet, I feel lost, direction-less. I feel as though I am in need of purification, cleansing. I want to give my soul an enema.</p><p>Tonight I went to dinner with my Mom and Step-dad. During dinner I started to have a rush of lethargy, discomfort, and grumpiness. I have not been treating myself very well this past week. Though I don\'t consider myself a vegatarian, I rarely eat meat, I have been eating meat every day this week. Having Vegi/Vegan friends, it is easy not to even think about eating meat. I can tell that the meat is making me feel bad. I prefer not to think of myself as anything, whether it be vegan, vegitarian, cool, geekish, quick, intelligent... I simply want to \"be.\" Does that make any sense? I am often offended, or shall I say put off, when my friends start to clasify others. This is a big turn off to me. I pick my friends carefully. My friends are everything, they are of every classification... or so I like to think...</p><p> but back to my discomfort... So at dinner I started being short with my mother and step-dad, immediately making them uncomfortable, defensive. I proceeded to tell them that I could not eat this way anymore, as I chomp down another bite of the wonderful stuffed sophapia with beans, chease, and ground beef. I have been drinking coffee and liquir all week, smoking as if camel cigarrettes are free (which they are at my friends bar.) Last night was the first night that I had to pay for a drink, I drank less last night than I have all week. I did get a free shot and a cocktail at my friends bar later. I woke up this morning wanting to get out of bed, but simply couldn\'t. My body is mad at me. I feel like crap. I haven\'t exercised in a week, I haven\'t had any fresh vegi\'s in a week. I replace herbal tea with coffee. I feel angry, ready to fight, my temper (what little I have) is flowing through me. I am ready to snap, ready to be fussy and mean... This simply is not me. All of this I barked at them. When we got home, i tried taking a nap but it was one of those hot naps... where you sweat and roll around for two hours... ugh... I FEEL LIKE SHIT!!!</p><p>I am ready to love things again, to enjoy my mornings, enjoy evenings, enjoy my afternoon naps, enjoy my tea breaks, enjoy a long bike ride, enjoy a conversation with my lovely friends.</p><p> I want my friend jesse to knoe I love him, and that no matter how hard things get that friends will allways be a phonecall, email, post away. I want to tell him that he doesn\'t have to run. I want to tell him that life will continue. Mainly, I miss his him.</p><p>I feel so much better, sharing all this with you all... I appologized to my Mom and Step-dad earlier. They smiled, and told me that it was ok. LOVE YOUR MOM!</p><p>Goodnight.</p>  <img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/977_1.jpg\'>'),(978,'<p align=\"justify\">it\'s 2am. <a href=\"http://steve.org\">steve</a> and i are having a quiet night in the living room, clacking clicking type typing away on our respective laptops, ostensibly \"working.\" <a href=\"http://hungry.com/~yeti\">peter</a> is upstairs asleep, no doubt dreaming of his future as one of those Bike People who ride mountain bikes around and wear clothing from REI.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nif i walk into the kitchen, there is carnage as far as the eye can see. chunks of uncooked squash languish in a bowl, and a mutilated finger of ginger rests on the cutting board, forgotten. bread wrappers, empty hummus container, two chocolate bar wrappers, empty can of cashews. an enormous soup pot is on the stove, its sides are coated in butternut squash soup, but it is empty. we have devoured everything vegan in sight, we are so fat with life.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nrecently i have been researching diet sites on the internet, because i got a job helping a webhouse in san diego re-do a diet site. i have been scouring these sites, reviewing their features, and trying not to think too hard about the societal issues behind dieting, the need to diet, the desire to diet, etc. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut it\'s hard. i was once a chubby girl. i am 5\'9\" and i weighed almost 200 pounds at my heaviest. i was not fat. but i was big enough that shopping was a chore. i never felt the need to diet. i didn\'t care. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nthen, i went vegetarian. then a few months later, i went vegan. i did yoga. i am not sure why i did those things. i think sometimes i just do things because i know they are what needs to happen. it\'s instinctual. my spirituality is often instinctual. we can talk about that another time.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nso, i went vegan and i lost fifty pounds. and i didn\'t even mean to. i did not do these things to lose weight. i did them because they felt good.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nabout 8 months after going vegan, i was my perfect body weight. i had extra skin hanging off my body. i would slather myself with cocoa butter every night trying to get the three inches (i\'m not joking) of excess skin on my tits to shrink back up (it did, but it took a year).\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nit was amazing, my body\'s way of thanking me! i was comfortable everywhere, things fit me, i sat differently in chairs and felt less awkward. in some ways, it was frustrating. i got all new clothes after the first 20 pounds then i lost another 20 and had to do it all over again. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ni would find my bones. i had never really noticed them before. once, sitting in bed with jeff, i found my shoulder blade. i reached an arm around my chest, grabbed at it: \"what is this!\" i was so excited. \"what is this thing sticking out! what the fuck!\" he snorted and whispered in my ear, stage whisper: IT\'S YOUR SHOULDERBLADE.\r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nso i look at these diet sites, with their daily food intake journals and java chat rooms, and i worry about the housewives who are writing down everything they eat. i remember that part of losing weight for me was just being happy. being mindful, fulfilling my own needs, eating only what i truly needed and what i wanted, what i could spiritually fathom. what my body was asking me for. it was like i finally knew myself, all of myself, body included. i wish these women knew themselves too! \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ni know this vegan evangelism will probably piss someone off. i want you to know i\'m not preaching. this is not about preaching. i don\'t care what you eat. i just want you to be happy. i want you to have thought about it. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\nsometimes it\'s good to remind yourself who you are, and what you identify with. i am doing that tonight. i have been callous recently, i have forgotten my yoga practice, my meditation, my spirituality. i have forgotten why i try not to eat the products of suffering lives. i have been gossiping, speculating, spouting disinterested negativity about the people in my life. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">i am relearning my gentleness. i want to tap into the part of myself that i found in 1999, a person that couldn\'t help but page friends with joy at the beauty of the sky. i want to appreciate every moment and every bite of food and every fat vegan dinner with loved ones. i want to touch austin\'s shoulder when he is cranky. i want to remember to make coffee for steve if i wake up before him, and i want to bring chaya soup when she is sick. \r\n</p><p align=\"justify\">\r\ndo you understand? we are fat, fat vegans.\r\n</p><p><br><br><br><br>\r\n------------------: (<tt>before and after</tt>)\r\n</p><p>\r\n<table><tr><td nowrap><p>\r\nBEFORE:<br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/979_4.jpg\" height=\"160\" width=\"200\" border=\"1\"><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/979_1.jpg\" height=\"160\" width=\"200\" border=\"1\"> \r\n</p><p>\r\nAFTER:<br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/979_2.jpg\" height=\"160\" width=\"200\" border=\"1\"><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/979_3.jpg\" height=\"160\" width=\"200\" border=\"1\"> \r\n</td></tr></table>\r\n\r\n'),(979,NULL),(980,'yesterday, while commuting down 101, a 16-wheeler maybe 100 yards ahead of me had a tire blow out<br>\r\nluckily 15 other tires continued to support the weight, but the explosion sent dust and debris showering out into the lane, pelting the small commuter cars alongside it<P>\r\nonce, on a similar commute southbound, i hit a chunk of steel-belted radial and it trashed my right front brake drum and some of the suspension (i wasn\'t able to dodge around it in time; the cars ahead of me ran over it as well)<p>\r\nthis time, however, I had a good 5 seconds to bridge three lanes of traffic and get out of its path, swerving left, right, and left again,<br>\r\ngiving the skills i acquired from ThunderForceII and AstroWarrior a real-world application (dodgeball at 75mph? good stuff)<P>\r\nthe point of all this, i guess, is that i\'d have otherwise suspected that such an incident would produce at least an adrenaline rush, if not an outright life-flashing-before-ones-eyes.. but my reaction was entirely objective \"oh, there\'s some junk, better change lanes\" , no fight-or-flight or anything. how detached have i become from my own survival instinct? or is this just minor compared to previous incidents? maybe i\'m a veteran now. <p>\r\n\r\nbrings to mind the time a dumptruck was headed at me, the wrong way down a deserted one-way street at 3am (\"oh, there\'s a dump truck, better change lanes\") .. didn\'t even register til i\'d made it home. autopilot, i guess. <p>\r\n\r\nonce upon a time, i drove a good two miles down a one-way street, not realizing it til i passed the cop shop and got pulled over.. again it was 3am, and i\'d been the only car on the road; the policemen were amused enough that i was let go with no citation. \r\n'),(981,'<p> I\'ve been debilitated by an unknown lethargic force. :(</p>\r\n<p> All I can do these days is drink different types of tea and stare at clouds, the ceiling, leaves. I\'ve been obsessing over smells, identifying smells, floral smells. Already equivocal attempts at socializing have fallen to the wayside in favor of sitting in bed and listening to Messiaen. The phone rings, I stare at it. There is no energy. It is not unpleasant. It\'s just unproductive.</p>\r\n<p> IRC working again. Is this a symptom or a cause?</p>\r\n'),(982,'<TABLE border=0><TR><TD>\r\n<P><B>cons</B></P>\r\n<OL>\r\n<LI>house is mess.\r\n<LI>have no pot, only shake.\r\n<LI>ex girlfriend makes me crazy\r\n<LI>bills to pay\r\n<LI>plants are dying\r\n</OL>\r\n</TD><TD>\r\n<P><B>pros</B></P>\r\n<OL>\r\n<LI>woob\r\n<LI>magic glasses\r\n<LI>bob and jim\r\n<LI>brooklyn\r\n<LI>things are gonna change, I can feel it\r\n</OL>\r\n</TD></TR></TABLE>\r\n<P><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/982_1.jpg\' alt=\"LEATHER or PLEATHER?\"></P>\r\n'),(983,'<p>\r\nweather you are dead or alive your body always has a shadow.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni contemplate death almost everyday. i have day dreams of being hit by cars and going into a nice coma where i am able to sleep. sleep while always under the watchful eye of the services of which my hard earned taxes pay for. i dream of getting hit by cars, jumping off bridges and such. why? well its because im tired of living in the circles, squares, and triangles of life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe only thing that holds me back is my mother and the fact that while im able too i should use my limbs. i should use my thumb. the thumb that makes us \"better\" then every other living creature on this earth. we made tools first and for that reason we rule.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe human race lives using big stick diplomacy on a scale of which connects every human on this earth. perhaps the only thing that the majority can agree on is the fact that we, as humans, can kill, eat, plow, erect, disolve, and torture anything that lays in our paths. we are borg.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni sat at the duke and read countless socialist speeches made by soviets, british labour party members and dissenters. i read their wisdom and relished in their insight. the drink within me made me slow but i pressed on. after i was done my drink and food and my eyes became tired from the damp lighting that makes the duke the comfortable place it is, i paid my bill. upon leaving two friends walked in and i was suprised. i didnt want them to seem me drunk, depressed, slouched. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni composed myself as though i was walking into work still buzzed from a session. one asked if i had been drinking and i responded with \"oh i had a couple of beers with dinner\". what i failed to release was the fact that i had accompanied the couple of beers with a couple more and a shot of jager. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went to the senate tonight and sat outside and drew. i drew lines and circles and shades in my black book filled with almost all white pages. on the way back i talked to myself inside my head and came up with a speech of which has long since been forgotten. circles with squares and triangles inside them...and inside those triangles tears. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am fed up to a certain extent and i am willing to give up. i am willing to give up for complacency and ignorance yet ignorance is far from acheivable at this stage in my life. i hate sports and i cant stand reading tabloids and entertainment news is tolerable until salaries are mentioned. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni email. i email. no response. i talk. i hint. no response. i had so much to say on that walk home. so much to record for history my inner thoughts. so much to devulge and make public. and yet when i sit down at this console and type i am overcome by depression and foggy thoughts. my mind is a flutter of activity and for what? the activity from which i write falls on deaf ears and yet while there might be some listeners out there, the same cliche answers echo from their throats. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni walked home and didnt say much. she didnt say much. there is an awkward silence between us. its awkward to me and yet i think its on purpose from her. my life sucks and there is nothing i  can do to make it better. i have tried the routes so prophesized by so many and i have toiled with what i think are positive routes and honest answers. honesty is the best policy is a farce and all those who beleive in it are in denial. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto what end is this life im living going to take me. how much sorrow must i endure. how much is the sorrow of my life man-made and to what extent are the people around me taking me for granted? i am sick and tired of being the lamb of god in the face of such oppostion. i am sick and tired of complacency and status quo. i am sick and tired of myself, and more importantly i am sick and tired of the sun, the grass, and life. i am tired of this charade we call living. i am tired of being within the minority and being ridiculed and tested by the majority. i am tired. i am tired of only being consulted when things look TOO gray...or when problems for the axis are too great. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat 11:11 every night i make the same wish and everyday that wish does not come true. i fear next week and i fear next year. what i see as the future is all too dismal. what i see for myself is more and more let downs. more and more sagging hopes and shrinking dreams. you all look at me as some sort of  complainer. some sort of person who gives up in the face of challenge. i know. you are too afraid to say it. but i know. i can tell by the way you reply. i can tell by the way you stand away from me in the elevator. i can tell by the way you small talk me and make jokes about my mom. i can tell by the way you dont reply to me. i can tell when you only reply when you feel guilty for feeling guilty and try to put the blame on my actions. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat do i want from myself and what do you want from me? i have tried. i have tried and i will continue to try...and perhaps the only thing what i will acheive is the end. the end is near. i can feal it. \r\n</p>'),(984,'The Fear of Shadows\r\n<p>\r\nthe street was free of cars.  my bicycle was going downhill at top speed.  this was at dusk and the trees created huge, dark cloths of nothing that covered the sidewalk.  i was thinking about an album by pink floyd called \"the final cut\" which has as a recurrent theme the falklands war, which was between britain and argentina.\r\n<p>\r\ni remembered that someone told me that british soldiers that were parachuted into the island were given a mixture of drugs that gave them the same effects of PCP; there are tales of british soldiers capturing argentine prisoners, mutilating them, raping them and killing them.  i thought about the gross insanity of this; i though about seeing images of young, white, rich argentinians rooting for the war effort in downtown buenos aires, while the grim reality was that argentinian military police \'recruited\' most of the army reserve by kidnapping the poor, mestizo youth of the northern parts of the country, around corrientes, misiones, arroyos y esteros, and salta, among other provinces.\r\n<p>\r\nsuddenly a man, driving a red scooter motorcycle appeared from one of the almost black sidewalks and stationed himself in the middle of the road to light a cigarrette.  i was at about ten metres from him, and going at approximately 30 kilometers an hour.  i tried to a)brake, which i rapidly acknowledged would not work because i was already about to crash on him, so i tried tob b) dodge him.  i failed to save myself, in a stupid and spectacular way... my bike crashed onto his scooter, the man fell down on his side, but i glided 2 metres, and then I landed on the pavement, scraping 5 red, bleeding spots on my arms, and one unreachable scar on my back.  i still have a scar on my left arm that reminds me of the crash.  today, when i go out at dusk, i go walking, in mostly white clothing, and i probably drink a cup of coffee before heading outside.  i avoid streets with lots of trees.'),(985,'no'),(986,'Remote swinney.org #1<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwell, as best i can tell, my car has been stolen- the natural tendency is to bitchenwhinenmoan. it certainly heightens my predicament: if i cannot find a job, i won\'t be able to drive me and my stuff back to the east coast. everything is a little harder, because i am constrained by public transport. but, really i think i choose to look at it instead as the city demanding that i stay, but only on her terms. so here i sit, on muni, in sf on sf\'s terms, trying to overcome motion sickness while writing for swinney.org on my palm.  i am on my way to weeb\'s to look for my car some more.  like we\'ll find it.  nearly here.  must stop writinq before i vomit.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n----<br>\r\n<br>\r\nof course, much has changed since that was written.  i found my car, i haven\'t written any more remote journaling on my palm pilot, and i may even almost have a BORING ASS job.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n-joe'),(987,'<P>It happened last September. As I sit here, realizing that writing about it brings up icy waves of anxiety, I know it is something I must do. I feel as if I am throwing the last shovelful of earth onto the grave of the entire incident. I will be grateful to know that I can visit the grave after this if I feel I need to, and that the grave will always be there, but that what it contains is long dead and buried.</P>\r\n<P>It was a great night. I had overcome a horrendous summer and met someone I adored. My new relationship gave me strength and made me glow. It was my friend Andy\'s birthday, so we went down to the Top to celebrate. It was a Tuesday night, and I was nearly always there for the fantastic drum n\' bass. We were drinking. A lot. People were buying me drinks. Many friends were there, and everyone was happy. I was overjoyed and ready for new life.</P>\r\n<P>Laura B was there hanging out, and my new friend Ben, who was a little creepy but seemed harmless. Laura and I decided we respected each other a lot and wanted to become better pals. We decided to go to her place at closing time to smoke bong hits and talk shit. Ben asked to come along. I said, slurring, \"Sure dude. The more the merrier.\" It\'s not like I would be alone with the guy, anyway.</P>\r\n<P>We got back to Laura\'s and sat at her kitchen table, talking about hiphop mostly and how jungle could use more MC\'s in SF and Wu Tang Clan. Laura brought out a nice Wu Tang backstage laminate. Ben turned it over, admiring it. We smoked more weed and talked into the wee hours.</P>\r\n<P>So it comes time where the booze and weed catches up to me and I decide to pass out on the futon. Ben is sleepy too but has to get up early to go to Oakland to work. He pulls out a little travel alarm and sets it. He falls asleep in the chair and Laura B says goodnight to go take a shower. All is well and we sleep.</P>\r\n<P>I slowly emerge from consciousness a few hours later, still drunk. I realize that something is touching my leg, something is prying at my clothing. Without waking up, I realize it is Ben. I brush him off jokingly, as if it were no big deal. \"I have a boyfriend, dude.\" I am still barely conscious when suddenly, he flips me over onto my back and holds me down by my throat. Shit.</P>\r\n<P>Prickling adrenaline rush, my heart starts racing and suddenly I am totally awake and sober. Fear throbs in my brain. He is over me, trying to remove my pants. I begin to protest, trying to shove him off. He becomes more agitated, and covers my mouth with his hand, holding me down. I try to scream. He gets into my face and spits at me, \"If you don\'t shut the fuck up I have a .35 in my backpack and I\'ll fucking use it so SHUT THE FUCK UP. DO YOU UNDERSTAND?\". I nod my head furiously and he begins to take my pants down. It\'s too much for me to bear. I decide that this will not happen to me. I have a man I adore and love, and I have to protect my body for HIM, and for ME. His hand over my mouth, I begin gasping for breath, panicking. This is not a possibility for me. This is not going to be my reality.</P>\r\n<P>With a great struggle I pull his hand off my mouth and gasp, \"I\'m going to be sick. I\'m going to throw up.\" Astonishingly, he lets me up. I run into the bathroom, everything moving 500 mph. The possibilities for the outcome of this race through my head. He shoots me and Laura dead. He rapes us both. Anything could happen. I collect my thoughts, wash my face, peek in at Laura sleeping soundly. Do I run in and whisper to her to call the cops? What if he catches us? We are dead. I decide to walk back into the other room and face him.</P>\r\n<P>He begins apologizing profusely, trying to pull me down onto the floor again. I tell him I am going home. I manage to slide on my shoes quickly. He informs me that he is coming with me. I don\'t even give him a chance. I bolt out the door. I am stuck for a moment, frozen and in shock. What about Laura? I can\'t leave her alone with him!?</P>\r\n<P>Suddenly my body tells me it is time to run. I bolt down the hallway, not looking back, trying to take the most complicated route out of the building. I reach 16th Street, the morning fog slapping me in the face. My heart is pounding so fast it may explode, and I am hyperventilating. I am in a blind panic. I run down 16th street to my friend\'s apartment. He is away for the week and I have a key. I run upstairs and call Laura.</P>\r\n<P>I am crying, screaming, \"Laura call the cops! That fucker tried to rape me! Make sure he is gone! Oh god!\" She puts down the phone and checks her apartment. She shuts the front door that was left standing wide open. She gets back on the phone with me. \"Oh my god, my laptop is gone. It was right here next to my fucking bed!!\" She goes to the kitchen. \"My wallet, my credit cards, my weed, gone. Even my fucking spare change! My cellphone!\" She calls the cops. I call Kris and he comes panicking, holding onto me as tightly as possible, crying with me. The cops arrive and we give them a report. I find that my pants are streaked with semen. I give them to the cops as evidince. They take the laminate for fingerprints.</P>\r\n<P>After a week of initial shock, I spiral into horrible symptoms of post traumatic stress disorder. I can\'t have sex, can\'t be touched without thinking about him assaulting me. I can\'t sleep. His words echo in my head over and over again, like a razor-edged tape loop. I can\'t leave my house. The world closes in on me. Finally, after a month, I decide that if I don\'t get some help that I will not be able to recover from this. I do get help. I am glad to say, after a long battle of nearly nine months, I am well and good and happy and at peace. My relationship even survived, which is saying a great deal about the character of my boyfriend, Kris.</P>\r\n<P>Laura had her laptop replaced, her phone replaced, her credit cards halted. But what we can never replace is a sense of innocence, a sense of safety and goodwill, a sense of human decency that we may have expected from people before. Our friendship is strong and true.</P>\r\n<P>As I toss this last shovelful of dirt onto this grave, I wonder where Ben is now. He is not in jail, not awaiting trial for the crimes he commited against us. Almost certainly, he is out there earning the trust of other good people like me and Laura, and raping them of the their sense of peace.</P>'),(988,'<p>\r\nyesterday i was walking down bank towards a look-out point behind the canadian senate. it looks over the ottawa river and on small islands in the centre gulls chat amongst themselves. its a nice sound they make from a distance. its soothing. on my way past the senate i saw a green range rover parked infront. the driver was probably my age and in the back were a couple of ladies, also around my age. i walked past and didnt pay much attention to them. i had more on my mind.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlast night i dreamed i stole the green range rover. i remember that it drove like a pig and that i was nervous being on the road with it. driving down some nameless street i saw a dirt road that down into a valley. i decided to take the dirt road and do some acrobatics with the range rover. however once in the valley it was apparent that something else was happening. the dirt road emptied into what seemed like a large parking lot. filled with minivans and families, i parked the rover in the middle of the lot. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nexiting and entering a tunnel i walked towards what seemed like a carnival. there were boths selling wares and rides and such. i entered what seemed to be the main building. walking up a flight of stairs which ended at a counter. I forget what i purchased but the lady behind the counter started a conversation. she tried to tell me that it wasnt good that i was buying what i was buying if i was on \"you know what\". I looked at her and said \"paxil?\". she whispered a yes to me and i shrugged it off and paid for my purchase. heading back down the stairs she followed, trying to change my mind. I dont know what she said but i know i was annoyed with her and all the people around me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit took me a long time to find the rover. i entered different lots from strage hallways and tunnels and finally found the one i was looking for; spending another long while trying to find the right automobile. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni woke up to a phone call.\r\n</p>'),(989,'<P>i have a tough decision to make and i feel it is being made for me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>look here austin, you have to find work.  you found it.  welcome to santa cruz.  hippie land. college students.  welcome with open arms.  the donut shop has video games.  i dont know why you need to be talked into coming down here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>well, video games in my donut shop are charming.  i did not play galaxia the first time around and i dont think i will be playing it with my donuts.  although i might play spy hunter.  i loved that game as a kid.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>great, we have spy hunter here.  we have every game you have ever played in Earth\'s largest video game plex.  new, old, you name it.  we have the first street fighter.  we have the high end driving games with rattling steering wheels.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i dont mean to cut you off but you are going the wrong direction with this.  i know how to drive now.  if i want to play spy hunter with rattling steering wheel i will burn the spy hunter midi theme to cd and play it in my car.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>did i mention we have some of the best roads for motorcycling in all of california?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i think i heard that somewhere, but i dont have a motorcycle.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>well, there you go, we can take care of that too!  santa cruz has many fine motorcycles to choose from just you wait.  and xep\'s mom lives here and qq -- all he does is ride a loop from sf to santa cruz, so you know you will be seeing them.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>yes but they are the only friends that i will see.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>well santa cruz is conventiently located near sf so you can make that drive whenever you want to see your old friends from sf.  and there is also the internet, dont forget about that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>yes, but what if i want to go have coffee or lunch or dinner with them?  i cant.  that is the whole reason i moved here was to have friends to do that kind of stuff with.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>can i remind you that you did not really move there?  you have been on vacation there freeloading and having a gay old time but the party is over.  it is time to get serious, now you have not have had a single job interview that went well since you know what.  get with the program.  santa cruz has the job you need.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>man, why do you have to be such a dick sometimes?  you are so insensitive.  i am trying here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>you are not trying, you are thinking only of the negatives.  think of the positives.  there are lots of down to earth people in santa cruz, have you forgotten what is like to just hang out at coffee shops and read and ride your bike around in a place with green and trees and young college babes?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>now you are talking...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>university of california santa cruz is here.  university towns are great and liberal, you remember how much you liked austin?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>sure, but i dont find that kind of dialogue fun anymore.  i am not a dawning young intellect.  i have passed the age where statistically people stop learning new vocabulary.  i am getting conservative and liking it.  pass on my helping of whiney liberalism</P>\r\n\r\n<P>hey now, no reason to go below the belt.  you are the whiner here.  be a man.  you have a career to build and this company is great.  the benifits make other people\'s jaws drop.  you have better benifits than old man swinney.  he said it himself.  do you think that is easy to come by.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>five and a half weeks of paid vacation plus holidays is awesome. you are right there.  alright, i\'m done whining.  i am sure it will be great.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and you can always go visit sf...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>alright, alright!  stop talking to me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>sorry.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>yea.</P>'),(990,'<p>\r\nwe stand watch in the twilight.  looking at the hills, and hoping not to see the signs on the horizon.  trying not to think of home.  listening to the cries from our friends who have lost a limb to the musket ball.  dreading the ripping sound of the german reaper.  our faces smeared with blood and streaked with dirt, we grin.  terror and bloodlust, weary of the hate felt for our fellows across the battle fields we\'ve seen.  always wondering if the officer will give the dreaded word.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo those we fight for care about us?  will they despise us for doing these things demanded of us?  will this be the bomb that breaks the back of tyranny?  will that be the shell that slays my last friend, tears his body to shreds - depositing his torso on my lap.  is this water safe to drink we wonder, and drink it no matter what we decide.  where is the sweetness in dying here, lungs boiling over from the mustard oil?  can we bury the dead before disease takes too strong a hold?  was that plane one of ours?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe lost nine out of ten on the cliffs.  tomorrow we shall lose 9 out of ten once more.  to take a piece of ground now worthless.  which in years to come will kill children as they play.  our friends have gotten letters from wives and girlfriends, saying goodbye.  we dread the next mail call now.  we save the lives of those who despise us, and we\'d do it again.  we are told that this time, war will bring peace.  we fight for freedom.  they do too.  we fight for oil for equality and for tyrants.  we are daily expected to do the impossible for the ungrateful.  we crave to see our children again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncontemplating the creed of the mercenary.  or the crack of a rifle in the distance.  grimly we maintain the weapons of war.  we have done these things for so long, will we ever be able to stop?  watching blood pool in the craters.  as the enemy collects pieces of their fallen.  maybe it will be one of us later.  here in the hell we\'ve created we find love.  we would rather have not.  our stares grow long so quickly now.  ashen faces turned from the sun which shows too much.  the mud cannot bother us now.  this trench has yet to fall in.  we are so tired.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin a year will we stand again upon these honorless ramparts.  fighting once more for honor we can no longer see.  will any understand what we have seen?  this time when we fire, it will be over their heads.  but death will come anyway.  we don\'t fear death now.  to stand and do what is expected, that is all we ask of ourselves.  today.  the next.  every moment is spent this way.  watching.  keeping our swords ready.  boxes of pikemen to fend off the charge.  grenades and mortars and rifles nearbye.  hoping for the end.  to go home.  to parades, cheers, and medals - perhaps.  revilement, fear, and disgust - maybe.  to understanding, to be human once again.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(991,'<p>\r\nCutting my hair makes me feel like iâ€™m about to do something amazing at any moment. i tried to give myself a mohawk but i couldnâ€™t see the back so it got all uneven and shaggy. itâ€™s grown out already and you canâ€™t even tell. i remember a long time ago, my friend mary lashes used to come over to my apartment after someone broke her heart and weâ€™d sit on the fire escape and give each other haircuts. that was when i lived across the street from a big abandoned armory. i knew a girl who had broken into it and just walked around. she told me it was pretty much just full of dead pigeons.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hate pigeons. theyâ€™re everywhere. theyâ€™re all over my neighborhood now, but itâ€™s nothing like that old armory neighborhood. those pigeons were grotesque oil slick amputee pigeons. they walked on no legs. they pecked with no beaks. their feathers were slicked back in tough biker pompadours with gasoline pomade. there was a rumor going around that they ate the puke of crackheads and would fly straight into cars and buildings and even bicycle wheels. can you imagine? crack-addicted, suicidal biker pigeons.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncompared to them, the pigeons in my current neighborhood are crocheting grandmas. they sit on the stoop and chat. they never try to kill you. i think i live in a nice neighborhood. but i have to tell everyone who comes to visit to go past all the nice houses to the boarded-up building on the corner. then i get embarrassed because people get excited and assume iâ€™m squatting it. no, i have to say, i pay rent. the creeps who own the building just never fix it. phyllis and christine, the polish ladies who sell stuff at a table on the corner, told me that they think the building is going to be condemned.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsometimes i think iâ€™m really exciting. other times, i bore myself to death. those are the times when i get impressed by everyone i talk to, jealous of their abilities or their fearlessness. i met someone who walked across ohio. he said it was nice but it took forever. carolyn, the same girl who broke into the armory and saw the pigeons, spent the night at alcatraz, the old creepy prison island. she said it was just cold and very quiet. emma told me yesterday that she found a flattened and dried dead rat on the side of the road and she brought it to kinkoâ€™s and attempted to photocopy it. the people at kinkoâ€™s kicked her out and she left it in the copier for them. i met a boy named freddie who went to jail wearing his girlfriendâ€™s red lace panties under his jeans. he said the cops were remarkably nice about it when they searched him.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshaolinâ€™s friend slept with johnny thunders.\r\n</p><p>\r\nandy got his picture taken with larry flynt and ron jeremy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nevery morning when i get up to go to school,  i think about what else i could do instead. itâ€™s kind of like an inner battle for me: go to school, do what you are supposed to or run away and do something youâ€™ve always wanted to do. the other day i wondered if iâ€™d have the guts to steal a car and drive it to miami. i couldnâ€™t do it so i went to my studio class instead.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis is nothing new. iâ€™ve been having this problem for as long as i can remember. in junior high school, we used to sneak out at night and â€œborrowâ€ some kidâ€™s momâ€™s car when she was out of town and drive it around. then in high school we would cut class for anything. sometimes weâ€™d go buy drugs. sometimes weâ€™d just go to zoeâ€™s dadâ€™s house to watch oprah.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnow that i live here, i have to be extra inventive. i mean, cutting classes right and left when iâ€™m going to school voluntarily and not because some creep is making me isnâ€™t as much fun. i end up thinking i missed out on learning something in the studio or, wow, i wonder how much that one class i just missed costs. i still have my moments. last week i almost got on the greyhound and went to north carolina. i called the 800 numberâ€”i know it by heart. i even went down to port authority and hung around the gate, drinking soda and reading a newspaper. if i ate hot dogs and port authority wasnâ€™t so annoying, iâ€™d make it my new office and go write there every day for an hour. thereâ€™s something comforting to me about a place where people are constantly getting out of town. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni like to sit there and read the newspaper. but the foodâ€™s horrible and sort of expensive so i still go to the taco counter for sopes con frijoles and mexican coke and get my work done.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso far, all my offices have been downtown or in brooklyn but lately iâ€™ve been getting into the idea of going up to midtown every day to write. maybe itâ€™s because i want to feel more business-like. or maybe itâ€™s because i like to make things hard for myself. i think next month, iâ€™ll scout out a nice busy lunch counter or a loud noodle shop as my place of business. i was even thinking of having business cards that said something like, Simon F.K., writer, my number, and the particular restaurant, bus station, mini mart, or roller rink i choose to call my own for that particular month. that way people will stop bitching that it is so hard to find me when they need me. \r\n</p>'),(992,'<p>i am i guess what you\'d call home. the last short and slow leg of the race. i wanted it bad. but now that im here i feel like shit. all i want is dark deep sleep. crawling under the covers for days. my arms ring with electric shooting pains. i want the needles like a junkie. but my cuppung savior is another five hours away and i just cant bring myself to face the beast again so soon. sleep is what im looking for. but all i can find are haunting dreams that leave me dripping angry sweat. i thought id let it go. . . so why is he still there. and why do i always end up on the bottom? there is a message here but i just aint gettin it.</p>'),(993,'<p>\r\nwhile im asleep my brain has been upto a lot. I have been having the most confusing, exciting, and intense dreams lately. last night was one of the most intense in a long time.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nit was weird. i was with my friends stinder and colleen and we were at colleens place. a one bedroom loft nothing like the place she has now. we were watching videos of them having sex with people i didnt know. it was weird. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni find a phone number and decide to call after a talking somethingorother tells me too. the woman on the other end had such a nice voice. i remember her voice so vividly yet i dont quite know what she was saying. id call every night and she\'d give me wisdom and talk to me about all my problems. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen things started getting really weird. i started meeting with people from some unknown organization and id get advice and information from them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere was one part of the dream i remember well. me and sean\'s apartment was a very spacious upper crust apartment in a condo unit. i invited my neighbour in: a large succesful black woman. we chatted a bit and while she was there i can remember running for the phone to pick it up and speak with the man from the unknown organization. i hung up and hte lady was gone and sean was questioning me on why i invited her in. i explained that it was because i was trying to be a good neighbour or something. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe last part of the dream i can remember is being in a car with the lady from canada trust...who in the dream was not at canada trust. she was part of the unknown organization and there was a man tailing us. i had a gun and we both began to shoot innocent people on the side of hte road. we just shot so many people. it was so bizarre. then i woke up. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni thought about this dream all day at work and at times it made me kinda scared and depressed. it was the sort of dream that left a lot of unanswered questions and it was also the type of dream i fear most. it was just me in the dream. no one seemed to be on my side and even the friends i think i still have seemed to be trying to push me away. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ninsight would be nice.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(994,'<p>\r\n<b>five activities often happening where steve and elly live on the hill</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li> depraved amounts of eating, sometimes in groups \r\n<li> dawn discussions of consciousness, religion, drugs, the future, art\r\n<li> chasing the cat out of the crawl space\r\n<li> sitting in the hot tub\r\n<li> discussing astrology\r\n</ol>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>five thoughts had while riding bitch on one of xep and eric\'s motorcycles</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li> i love you/life/sky/city\r\n<li> how can i distill what i want to express into a small enough sentence that it can be uttered at the next stop light and understood (often \"peem\" or \"mew\" seems to suffice).\r\n<li> yipe yipe yipe\r\n<li> we are so cool\r\n<li> vivid fear of auto related death\r\n</ol>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>five things i can see out my bedroom window</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li> the sky\r\n<li> an extremely fictitious looking magical wonderful towering tree\r\n<li> the swing, gently swaying in the breeze\r\n<li> gemini/virgo gardeners moving things around for inexplicable reasons\r\n<li> yappy punt dog owned by angry neighbors\r\n</ol>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>five things i do to groom myself on a semi-daily basis</b>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li> pluck eyebrows\r\n<li> apply moisturizing sunscreen to face to avoid premature UV RAY related aging\r\n<li> apply peach scented lotion (\"you smell like a scented \'my little pony\'\") to body in hopes of making boys want to eat me\r\n<li> put tea tree oil or tom\'s of maine deodorant in armpits, depending on desired hippie level \r\n<li> brush teeth, preferably while standing in shower\r\n</ol>\r\n</p>'),(995,'<p>I was invited to a gnostic mass today. It was free so I decided to go.</p>\r\n<p>It was okay. There were strange chantings and there was a nude priestess. The people were older and more serious than I though they\'d be, which was refreshing.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m not really into this sort of thing, but any church that considers Nietzsche a saint will get me to attend one of their masses. It\'s interesting to see how people use symbols and ritual to influence their lives -- it\'s at the core of all religions, but few do it as openly and with as much purpose as this one.</p>\r\n<p>All in all it was an unpleasant experience. I don\'t like NYC, I don\'t really like cities in general. I used too, but it\'s just an overload of information. I\'m obsessed with detail. I\'m lost in it. A city wreaks havoc on my nerves. There was also physical contact involved in the mass, and this is something that also tends to turn my stomach.</p>\r\n<p>The rose: After the mass, the priestess was handing out roses to everyone. I took one, I wanted to smell it, but later on the street I didn\'t want it anymore. I decided to give it to a pretty girl or just a girl in general. I did so.</p>\r\n<p>I handed it out to her, saying: Here! This is for you!</p>\r\n<p>But she mumbled something like, \"No thanks, I don\'t want any.\" Like I was someone selling them on the street. All I wanted was to give someone a rose.</p>\r\n<p>Maybe that\'s another reaosn why I don\'t like cities.</p>\r\n'),(996,'<p>there are some things that invade my senses that bring me peace and happiness and make me feel like joy is not far away:</p>\r\n<p>watching the winter waves crash onto a dark shore, bright electric blue flashes in the surf as some kind of phosphorescent sea creature performs a function of life which enchants me so utterly that i am unable to tear myself away from the beach despite my hands begining to go numb in wind...</p>\r\n<p>closing my eyes and feeling the promise of kisses to come on my lips...</p>\r\n<p>the way the gold satin sheets keep the warmth of my tiny fan heater when thrust under the covers, so that slipping into bed is like immersing myself in a pool of warm, melted chocolate...</p>\r\n<p>unlocking the door to a house called nirvana each and every evening.  having an entire room in nirvana for me only, filled with the things i like, such as quilts and wooly things and books and music and alf, too.</p>\r\n<p></p>'),(997,'<p>\r\n01/01/1995\r\n</p><p>\r\nRevolution.  For a revolution to take place three types of people are required.  In addition to these three types, one more thing must be present; group cohesion.  But first the three personality types.  The first and rarest is a true genius.  True genius views life in a radically different way than other people.  Sadly though, without the other types to listen and promote their ideas, the true genius is seen as nothing more than a babbling lunatic.  The next rarest type is the transmuter.  Though highly intelligent, the transmuter does not possess the \"spark\" that the true genius has.  However, this type is well conditioned in the norms of society, and while they do not like them, they go along with them.  They see the need for the genius and the change they can bring.  Without the company of the other two, the transmuter will live life as an unsatisfied thinker.  The third and most common type is the promoter.  The promoter is very gregarious and gets along with almost anyone.  Like the transmuter, the promoter sees a need for change, but is not sure how it should take place.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo how does it work?  The transmuter deciphers the message of the genius and packages it for the promoter to deliver to society in a digestible format...Simple huh?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(998,'<p>So yeah, the <a href=http://saturn5.com/darwin/02-negativland--cityman-i.mp3>city</a>, man it\'s..</p>\r\n<p>Earlier today I was in Pancho Villa. The security guy was in the back, and this guy comes in asking people for change. white male, 5\'7\" or 5\'8\", 130 pounds, scruffy beard and hair, chapped lips and beady eyes.</p>\r\n<p>Now, I\'ve seen this guy before, on two seperate occasions. The first, I saw him flagging down cars and he needed 10 cents for some reason. I gave him a dollar. Then, I saw him yelling at some people in the Safeway Castro parking lot, top of his lungs, slamming their door shut on them. I helped him with a few cents, and then he proceeded to get upset at me because I wouldn\'t give him five dollars. He stalked away, and I told him something about being rude to people who are willing to help you.</p>\r\n<p>So, he comes into Pancho Villa, and there is no way in he\'s going to get another cent from me. The security guy comes out of the back, and I mention to him that the guy is sparechanging people. Not only do I not like this particular guy\'s attitude, but I don\'t feel it\'s appropriate to ask people for money while they\'re trying to eat. It\'s doubly inappropriate, I think, to come inside of places of business in order to hassle people. I understand that people are needy, but there\'s a such thing as common decency.</p>\r\n<p>So, the guy notices that I pointed him out to the security guy, and he starts yelling at me. He walks over the few feet and pushes me. A split second later, the security guy grabs him from behind and pushes him out the door.</p>\r\n<p>Now, I think the guy is a basehead or crazy, so he probably won\'t recognize me the next time he sees me. There\'s always the chance, though, and then I guess I\'ll see how good my reactions really are. I didn\'t assess him properly as a threat, as he got to me and pushed me. I didn\'t fall over or anything, and if he had been punching at me or trying to grab me, I doubt a 130lb crackhead is going to be too fast or too strong for me. Ideally, though, he shouldn\'t have even been able to reach me. I think if we were on the street, I would have been more prepared, but you don\'t expect people to attack you in a taqueria.</p>\r\n<p>So anyway, I was thinking about this experience. I\'ve always viewed physical conflict as a last resort, or something that happens after an argument or escalation. Something that happens on the streets or in a bar. In some cases, I guess, people are just apeshit.</p>\r\n<p>I think I\'ll be better prepared next time.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t like the mindset that tells me that there <u>will</u> be a next time. It seems like people are getting desperate out there. In the last two days, I have seen three seperate homeless people eat food that others discarded as garbage. In Kate\'s Kitchen yesterday, The Crepevine today, outside of Pancho Villa\'s today. On every corner there seems to be 2 or 3 homeless people, and they seem more desperate than usual. </p>\r\n<p>Could the collapse of the dotconomy be drying up the spare change in SF? I don\'t really understand the sheer amount of homeless people in San Francisco, and I wonder if anyone is collecting information from or about them. Where are they all from? How\'d the end up on the streets? Are there more or less of them now than there were a year ago? two years ago? How many of them die without ever getting back into \"normal\" life? How many of them actually get off the streets? <a href=http://www.elly.org>le</a> gave me these links to the <a href=http://www.sfo.com/~coh/basics.html>San Francisco Coalition on Homelessness</a> (which has lots of info) and a really interesting <a href=http://www.thematrix.com/~sherrod/diary.html>diary</a> of a homeless girl junkie in San Francisco. </p>\r\n<p>Last month, a man named Dennis Hillard was killed at the corner of 9th and Irving, across the street from a Jamba Juice. <a href=\"http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?file=/chronicle/archive/2001/04/29/MN162181.DTL\">This article</a> details how he lay shot dead on the sidewalk from ~10pm to 12:36am. Lots of people walked by him, but it took almost two hours for one to notice that he was dead. I met Dennis a few times, we talked about Rutland, Vermont. He was a nice guy, polite, he shook my hand. There was a memorial on the corner he lived on, but I think the flowers all died and someone cleaned it up.</p>\r\n<p>Maybe most of the people on the streets are drug addicted and/or crazy. I\'ve started questioning whether giving spare change to 12 different people a day is providing any benefit to anyone. I\'ve often seen the same person in the same place every day for extended periods of time. If they can earn a certain amount in a typical day with typical patter, is it impossible for them to work out a budget that allows them to accrue some sort of savings? Basic camping supplies? A safe and remote place to live and the bus fare to get there?</p>\r\n<p>I\'m quite sure it\'s difficult, but I can\'t understand where the line is between a person who can make a plan to get off the streets and one who is stuck there forever. How heavily does the lack of interim support weigh in? If there were enough halfway houses and other services, how many of these people would become Productive Members of Society? How many of them <u>want</u> to be?</p>\r\n<p>Maybe some of them will be happy again sometime. Hope so. Maybe there\'s something that I can do to help.</p>\r\n<p>-darwin<br>\r\n.. <a href=\"http://www.saturn5.com/darwin/02-negativland--cityman-i.mp3\">killing me</a>.'),(999,'<p>Darwin\'s latest got me thinking about things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A few years ago i was an english major at U of AZ in Tucson.  The arts district was about 4 blocks west of the U, and is teeming with the homeless from Fall to Spring (beats freezing to death back east, I guess).   The last year I was there the City Council passed what was supposed to be an anti \"aggressive panhandling\" law.  There\'d been some problems with some of the homeless getting together en masse ( like 15-20) and accosting yuppies.   Naturally, there was a public outcry - bear in mind that this is the city that would go out of it\'s way to bulldoze shantytowns (even ones that weren\'t near their precious new subdivisions).  The whole thing snowballed pretty quickly, and I remember hearing about TPD beat cops herding the homeless off the main streets and breaking up groups of more than 5.  Total overreaction, yeah, but at the same time I can understand to a degree.  I think that pretty much everyone that rides public transport or spends much time downtown in any mid-size or larger city has at least one \"man, there was this freaked-out homeless guy\" story. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'d just be nice if we could find a balance.  I\'ll give you some change or buy you a burrito - now you hold up your end of it and don\'t freak me out too much.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Anyone here ever run into the \"support the united negro hamburger fund\" guy down in Berkeley?  He was always cool with me, whether I had change or not.</p> '),(1000,'<PRE>\r\naustin:  \"hi, i would like to accept the offer\"\r\nnewjob:  \"oh really?  great, congratulations, i mean thanks!\"\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i am going to santa cruz.  i have never felt apprehension before a job before.  but i am feeling it now.  i feel like it is the first day of highschool.  it is not the same situation at all, except that i just dont want to go.</P>\r\n<P>sure it will be great, there is coffee there.  the people are great, i\'ll bet.  i will be fine there.  i will be depressed for a couple weeks.  then i will meet some cool people and sell my car for a motorbike and live the sunny life until the rainy season comes in the winter.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'ll go riding around drunk one night and wrap myself around a pine tree.</P>\r\n<P>scratch that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i will become the qbert champion in 2003.  people will worship me.  the qbert king.  see you at the donut shop.</P>'),(1001,'<p>\r\nyou cant be a patriot<br>\r\nor love america<br>\r\nJews<br>\r\nthey aren\'t<br>\r\nAmericans<br>\r\nthey wish to<br>\r\nsubjugate us<br>\r\nusing the gangs and<br>\r\nforeign powers<br>\r\nto usurp control<br>\r\nto take away our liberty<br>\r\nJews should all be shot<br>\r\nwith no trial<br>\r\nnow that is Liberty.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\n---\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing jew-color sucks...\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1002,'<p>\r\nwell im saying goodbye to ottz. its been good while it lasted, but its time to leave. i can\'t afford to live here anymore and i cant afford to wait any longer for possible employers to call me back. im also not happy with the way things have worked out here concerning some people i know. ive received a few nasty emails from friends and im just sick of getting down over something that wont ever change. its gotten to the point where i just want to wake up and not be afraid of seeing them in the elevator, at work, or on the street. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nim actually excited about moving back to toronto. Ill have a room there(i didnt have a wall before) and ill have more freedom. both my grandma and mom know i smoke cigarettes and i wont be within walking distance of a bar. ill have cable and ill get high speed edition and things will pretty much go back to normal for me i hope. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni want to get a mon-fri|9-5 and save up for a car...i want a passat. i want to hang out with my friend rob and have the conversations with him i love to have. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nill have a dark room to set up with two really good enlargers, and possible a print dryer. ill build the tables myself and put my handy hands back to work doing something other then wacking off and typing. i want to pick up a hammer and some nails, maybe screw a few things. har har. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nim excited about something for once that shouldnt really go wrong. this pleases me. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1003,'<pre>\r\n\r\ncranefly, i say, you are out of place here\r\n\r\ni am leaning against my plush couch\r\nmy body is curled and wide like bold typeface\r\ncompared to the crane fly that hovers around my ceiling: \r\nit is a distilled asterisk of nature\r\n\r\nalone in the house, i take to speaking to my longlegged frenetic \r\nintruders as they hover and bounce just above the floor\r\n\r\nlong speeches to the craneflies. lectures and words of affection. \r\n\r\nyou are a worthless dragonfly wannabe, i tell it.\r\nyou are a gangly teenager of an insect, i tell it.\r\nyou need to learn to fly straight, i lecture\r\nas the crane fly hurtles into the wall, the floor, the couch.\r\n\r\nnothing but a winged daddy long legs! i mutter, leaning to examine \r\na stoic wall-perched crane fly.\r\n\r\nyou don\'t belong in my world of boxes, lamps, audio gear, \r\nfour walls and roof.\r\n\r\nskinny crane fly. git! go be beautiful somewhere else.\r\n\r\nno time for your antics here.\r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1003_1.jpg\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1004,'<p>\r\nin moab right now, staying at the landmark motel. girl at check-in counter was super cute: short hair and sparkles. i look at her again and forget i\'m in utah.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlast night we slept in las vegas. silly last minute decision, but i did it for my canadians. and anyway the luxor is more comfortable than best western.\r\n</p><p>\r\nspaces between rest stops: listening to silence, the wind, passengers struggling with sleep, <a href=\"http://www.extropy.org/faq/eprime.html\">unyielding pendantry</a> (\"what are you eating?\" \"i\'m not eating, i\'m theo\"), laughter.\r\n</p><p>\r\nradar detector continues to be silent.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe desert treats my body well. sinuses clear, pollen drains. i feel the dry heat; i never need to sleep again. the desert is alive.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe revise the four food groups: petroleum, synchronicity, potato chips, and things that offend people at utah truck stops (example: popsicles).\r\n</p><p>\r\nrewinding. i strike up conversation with cool hippies in arizona. (ex?) mormons. we don\'t tell them <a href=\"http://www.drugpolicy.org/conference/\">where we\'re going</a>. (you never know who you\'re talking to.) so we keep the conversation light: vehicles, off-roading, desert hiking. he tells me he\'s friends with the guy who owns the bar and the gas station but we should buy our gas up the road; it\'s 40 cents cheaper.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncan\'t imagine living in a town where everyone knows everyone else\'s business.\r\n</p><p>\r\njim says utah is the \"show-me state\".\r\n</p><p>\r\ntomorrow is dead horse point. everything in utah is beautiful. thank you, glaciers.\r\n</p>'),(1005,'<p>auntie said the infps aren\'t writing. okay so i want to tell you about the people that i see over and over again in austin.</p>\r\n<p>these are people that spend a lot of time in establishments at the north end of the drag, like showdown, and on up to spider house and i luv video and toy joy. <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/movetoaustin/\">observe</a>. i\'m talking about #20 up to #9 on the map. but also in and around hyde park, especially at the fresh plus.</p>\r\n<p>they are:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><b>cardigan guy/foosball shark</b>. he\'s an older man, foreign, short, and he\'s at showdown <b>every night</b> wearing a grey cardigan sweater. he rarely talks. he waits until college students start playing foosball and then he joins them. then he slaughters them. none of us have ever seen betting take place nor money exchanged so it could be that he does this <i>just to humiliate college students</i>.<BR>\r\ni saw him last night at spider house. he walked in and sat at a table across the walkway from me and berated the man at whose table he was sitting for calling him, after which the man said very plaintively, repeatedly, \"i\'m truly very <b>sorry</b>.\" new hypothesis: cardigan guy is <b>crazy</b>, not just eccentric.</li>\r\n<li><b><a href=\"http://www.goldenhandproductions.com/\">levi glassrock</a></b>, one of austin\'s more famous dragrats.<BR>\r\ni <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/crushes/3/\">wrote about</a> a night when he sat down with sara and i at spider house, drunk, and after telling us about his father who\'d killed himself that day, asked us to buy his prints and music. we did.<BR>\r\ni have seen him multiple times since that night. he always reintroduces himself and he always tries to get me to buy his stuff. he has an energy comparable to <a href=\"http://www.links.net\">justin</a>\'s.</li>\r\n<li><b>the cute couple</b> actually lives in my apartment complex. he looks like an unkempt <a href=\"http://www.bryanboyer.com\">bryan boyer</a> and she is small, wiry, like a gypsy, and they walk everywhere together; their body language is so comfortable that i question if they are even a couple. i don\'t know if i\'m picking up a <i>lack</i> of sexual chemistry or if i\'m just stunned by how their mutual movements complement one another so well that it seems like they are one, large, jangly, chattering being.<BR>\r\ni dig them. i am too shy to initiate conversation.</li>\r\n<li>the others, including <b>red-headed indie rock backpack austin java company boy</b>, <b>chain wallet boy</b> -- who actually works someplace that i frequent but i can never remember where because he\'s only waited on me once but for some reason i\'m convinced that we had a conversation about the magnetic fields, and <b>unboyfriendable boy</b>, who is at spider house <i>every single time</i> that i am there, even when i was going several times a week, studying.</li></ul>\r\n<p>so this raises the question: am i really seeing the same hundred, hundred-fifty, people over and over again but these are the faces that i recognize?</p>'),(1006,'<P>\r\nI.<br>\r\neve is like the enamel on a carousel that glows at the beginning of spring. crisp\'d polisht salty crunchy cyclones spun by hands of honey and soot.<br>\r\nash.<br>\r\ndweller of the forest fire.</P><p>\r\nII.<br>\r\nI really honestly dont know why, but eve makes me think about the beginning of the world. when you were trapped in a closet by your own design, having hid there in a game of Hide and Go Seek. it is nighttime and all the other kids have given up and gone away, there is only you in a dark tight space. noone will find you for days. you can hear your own heart, and something is being born.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1007,'<P>\r\n<*egg*> i love you egg<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> really? you dont know what that means to me<br>\r\n<*egg*> yes i do, because im you<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> im the very person who is eating peanut butter chocolate pie<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> right now<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> hung over on a tuesday<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> a working stiff<br>\r\n<*egg*> forget it egg<br>\r\n<*egg*> youre not as bored as you think you are<br>\r\n<*egg*> only boring people really get bored<br>\r\n<*egg*> people like you eat pie and make web pages<br>\r\n<*egg*> write love poems<br>\r\n<*egg*> get gaga<br>\r\n<*egg*> stare at pictures of boys with curly hair<br>\r\n[=You have new mail=]<br>\r\n<*egg*> wonder if anyone is really your friend<br>\r\n[=You have new mail=]<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i just did 5 pushups<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> it really exhausted me<br>\r\n<*egg*> aw<br>\r\n<*egg*> youre so out of shape for such a young shapely girl<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> what a thing to say to yourself!<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> youve internalized the male gaze, egg<br>\r\n<*egg*> amy<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> what<br>\r\n<*egg*> youre talking to yourself again<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> so<br>\r\n<*egg*> what if someone were to see this<br>\r\n<*egg*> theyd lock you up<br>\r\n<*egg*> theyd take you out back<br>\r\n[=You have new mail=]<br>\r\n<*egg*> you need at least someone to pretend theyre listening while<br>\r\n<*egg*> you talk to yourself and pretend youre talking to him/her<br>\r\n<*egg*> preferably \"her\"<br>\r\n<*egg*> you really have a lot of problems relating to people<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i know<br>\r\n<*egg*> you dont give them what you want<br>\r\n<*egg*> and i mean, what they want<br>\r\n<*egg*> and you dont have the slightest idea what you want from them<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i know<br>\r\n<*egg*> how come if you know all this you dont do something about it<br>\r\n<*egg*> you need to be more solutions oriented<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i guess<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> but i dont feel like improving myself<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i just want to sleep<br>\r\n<*egg*> thats just right now, because youre tired<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> but im always tired<br>\r\n<*egg*> thats because you dont get enough iron<br>\r\n<*egg*> you have too much sex late at night<br>\r\n<*egg*> whutsup, theres so much shit you cant own up to<br>\r\n<*egg*> dont get freaky about all this hsit<br>\r\n--> you dont want to drag yourself through another series of<br>\r\n--> interpersonal dealings that make you wish your insides would<br>\r\n--> rot out<br>\r\noops<br>\r\n--> oops<br>\r\n<*egg*> that was meant for you<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i figured<br>\r\n[=You have new mail=]<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> you know, i really feel like i can talk to you<br>\r\n<*egg*> well, thats nice<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> no, im serious<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i really dont get to be this frank with anyone else<br>\r\n<*egg*> yeah<br>\r\n<*egg*> did you really think that youd be able to?<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i dont know<br>\r\n<*egg*> other people have mystery<br>\r\n<*egg*> youre strange to yourself sometimes though<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> thats true<br>\r\n<*egg*> but is it enoguh<br>\r\n<*egg*> with the proper equipment, i dont see why you couldnt just<br>\r\n<*egg*> love yourself forever<br>\r\n<*egg*> everyone thinks your pie is quite a phenomenon<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> well, i have to say, it really actually is a phenomenon<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i think ill just leave it out for the world to see<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> even U2 sounds good to me right now<br>\r\n<*egg*> even U2 sounds good to me right now<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i feel like crying<br>\r\n<*egg*> i know you do hon<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i want luv<br>\r\n<*egg*> yeah but you dont wanna do any of the work it takes to hold<br>\r\n<*egg*> onto it<br>\r\n<*egg*> you just want it when you want it<br>\r\n<*egg*> drive thru luv<br>\r\n<*egg*> you can get it from me<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> ok<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> i love you egg<br>\r\n<*egg*> i love you egg<br>\r\n-{to egg}-> really? you dont know what that means to me<br>\r\n<*egg*> really? you dont know what that means to me<br></P>'),(1008,'<p>\r\nin an hour i have to go with my mother and sister (she\'s actually sixteen, no matter that i consider her my baby sister still) to meet her thirty year old fiance.  my step-father refuses to even meet this guy.  my mother vacillates between wanting me to intimidate him and wanting me to provide her some moral support.  for myself, i dunno what to do.  my sister has an opportunity to go to CMU in the fall in their electrical engineering program.  i hate to see her waste the chance.  so of course, my knee jerk reaction is just to waste this guy.  not a true option anyway you look at it.  i guess i want to know what would make a 30 year old Navy veteran want to marry my sixteen year old little sister.  i want to see if he really holds any love for her or if something sick is going on.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nno matter what i find, my sister is going to do what she wants.  she\'s much like me at that age, only without the violence.  she refuses to listen to anyone who tells her that a situation is strange.  i think it\'s possible there could be real love here...but it seems so unlikely.  what game is this guy playing...and how can i stop it before my sister gets messed up?  i suppose i can\'t do a thing except meet this guy and memorize his face.  i know i don\'t want to ride in the car with him, i wonder if i should frisk him before he gets in.  should i be friendly...distant...confrontational?  what the hell is the right way to handle this.  i\'m tired and cranky as it is, having carried 50lb light fixtures up three flights of stairs all day, and now i\'m going to be up 4-5 hours past bedtime.  it\'s going to be awful hard to be objective i think.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand scenario-planning the possibilities tonight holds.  dear gods!  the worst cases are not pretty.  thankfully they are just worst cases, and not likely to happen.  i want everything to turn out good.  i want this guy to truly love my sister and not be some sick fuckin freak.  i want to like him.  going into this remembering the things i\'ve seen makes it hard to believe anything good.  and it\'s giving me the worst headache.  damn.  i\'ll see in only 45 minutes.\r\n</p>'),(1009,'	<P>When the boy was really young, like three or four, his family lived on a street that had a lot of kids.  Most of them were all older than him, but they still included him and let him play with them.  He would like to think they liked him; they would all play at the boy\'s house sometimes, running around, screaming and yelling like kids do.  His best friend was Colby.  He lived on the same side of the street, but several houses down.  He and his sister, who was cross-eyed, were in the boy\'s back yard when he broke his arm.  There was a Mexican family who lived next door; they had a daughter, not much older than the boy.  She tried to get him to eat mud pies, but he would have none of that delicacy.  They briefly had a chicken, as a pet or something.  Briefly, because the dog belonging to the boy\'s mother, a chihuahua named Shelley (who hated the boy), killed it.  How that chicken allowed that little dog to catch it is still a mystery.  David Dawkins lived on the other side of the boy\'s family.  He was in high school and his mother was the boy\'s babysitter; she and her husband (they were older, in their fifties at least) slept in twin beds.  There was a girl across the street, who was in fifth grade or therabouts, maybe named Monica.  She sticks out because of this one day she was wearing a halter top and had to keep pulling it up; it was a little small for her growing, pubescent body.  A couple doors down from Monica lived another friend, Jennifer, and her family.  Her dad was a fireman, and her older brother broke both of his arms skateboarding once.  Jennifer was the boy\'s introduction to female anatomy; they played \"doctor\" a couple of times at Mrs. Dawkin\'s house.</P>\r\n	<P>Inside the boy\'s home, his parents? marriage was falling apart.  He played in his room a lot.  His red ViewMaster was one of his favorite toys.  There was also this thing that was a kind of personal movie projector.  A plastic box with a Disney filmstrip was inserted and then, by turning this handle, the pictures moved.  The boy\'s head would fill with nervous anxiety when his parents were fighting, his three year-old mind unable to comprehend the situation unfolding downstairs.  The dinner table is where it seemed all the drama took place.  Doors slamming, he and his mother alone, his father saying they weren\'t a family, not because he felt that way, so much as to spite his wife.  When they finally got divorced and the boy and his mother moved to a new neighborhood, he didn\'t really go out and try to make any new friends, even though there were plenty of kids on the street.  He\'d sit in his room and watch television or entertain himself, quietly.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n	<P>The boy\'s mother was very concerned with his well-being, like a good mother would be.  And because of that, he was pretty sheltered from what was out there, unaware of popular culture really, until he got to the sixth grade.  But even then, he was one naÃ¯ve loser.  He was in private schools until the ninth grade, sheltered from the so-called dregs of society by uniforms and Religion class.  The only year he really ever played any sports was eighth grade when he played basketball (the team had about 20 players; his position was bench warmer #7) and ran track.  He was the anchor leg of a pathetic 4x400 relay team so bad that he was once cheered as he completed a race more than one full lap behind everyone else; this was not his fault as he actually made up a little time. </P>\r\n	<P>Because of this pathetic display of athletic ability, he didn\'t feel bad when he had to miss a meet one weekend for a trip to Oklahoma.  He and his mother were going with a friend of hers from work to visit some of her family, whom they had also known for several years: two cousins, who were sisters, and one\'s husband who was chief of a small Native American tribe.  To the best of the boy\'s knowledge, there wasn?t much Indian heritage practiced or passed on by the man; he could be wrong, though, being as he was young when he knew them.  The man was a big guy, over six feet tall and more than two hundred pounds.  He always knew plenty of jokes and had fun games to play; he was Santa Claus without the beard.  They had stayed with the man and his wife a couple of times previously, either at their lake house, or at their home in Tulsa.  Wherever they stayed, it always worked out so that the boy had to sleep in the man\'s bed.  Maybe they were being good hosts, the wife taking the sleeper sofa, the boy\'s mother and her friend in the guest room, and the man and the boy in he and his wife\'s bed; it wouldn\'t have surprised the boy if the man stayed in his bed because of his weight on those early trips.  But by this time, he had become diabetic and lost a leg from the knee down because of it; now, it was no doubt easier and more comfortable for him to be in his own bed.  Whatever the reason, the boy never thought to question it, even on this trip when he was thirteen.</P>\r\n	<P>He woke up the next morning to find the man already awake.  They talked about driving out to the lake house that afternoon so the boy could ride the man\'s four-wheeler, and then he began to tickle the boy.  This wasn\'t a surprise and wouldn\'t have been to anyone else in the house had they walked in.  He was renowned for it, being a former Scout leader, taking boys from church up to the lake for fishing, epic shouts of laughter from his room signalling his wife to start breakfast.  The tickling went on for a little while, until the boy was laughing so hard he couldn\'t breathe.  The man stopped harassing him and started to massage his own thigh, and the stump below it, first with his hand, and then with an electric massager.  It was black and silver, plastic and metal, with an elastic loop on the bottom.  The man would put his hand through the loop and the motor above would vibrate, letting his own hand gently rub the needed area.  He worked it over his thigh, moved on to his knee, and then his inch-long nub of a leg.  Every morning he did this, so the muscles in that leg would still get something of a workout.  </P>\r\n\r\n	<P>\"Here, see how it feels.\"</P>\r\n\r\n	<P>The man slipped it under the boy\'s shirt, onto his back, but it was too tight with the machine on his hand, so he pulled the boy\'s shirt off.  Rubbing his back, he felt the boy\'s ribs and spine vibrate under the pressure.  The man turned him over and started to work his arms, his chest, his stomach.  With a smooth, natural motion, he undid the boy?s shorts and lifted him up to move them down, before the boy could try and stop him, not that he could speak.  His jaws were unable to work, and while his mind was recording everything that was happening, it could not comprehend that same information, or find a way to stop it.  He knew there were perverts in the world.  He had seen the very special two-part \"Diff\'rent Strokes\" and knew about all the missing kids.  His mother had taken him to be fingerprinted years earlier just in case anything should ever happen to him.  This wasn?t supposed to be happening to him.  He was too smart for this.  He was too old for this.  There wasn\'t anyone they knew who would do these things.</P><P>  \r\nIt didn\'t take much for the man to make the boy hard.  He asked how long that had been happening.  A year or so, he wondered.  The boy half-nodded as the hand went up and down.  How long had the hair been there, about the same, he guessed.  The boy half-nodded again as the hand stopped and squeezed, sending pulsing waves throughout his body.  His breathing became deeper as the man moved his hand and squeezed at the same time.  It didn\'t take much for him to make the boy finish.  The man felt it building and moved his hand so as not to get anything on his machine.  He asked how long that had been happening.  The boy was finally able to whisper an answer, about a year.  He went to the bathroom connected to the man\'s room, where he cleaned himself off and got dressed.  The boy said he was going to take a shower and the man said to use his, but the boy told him all his stuff was already in the other one.</P>\r\n	<P>The boy spent the rest of the day, by himself, trying to interact with other people as little as possible.  He knew he should say something, but the thought of it made him sick, so he stayed quiet.  He told everyone he had a headache so they wouldn\'t have to go to the lake house, the man and the boy.  He took a nap for hours.  He tried to switch to another bedroom or the couch, but no one would hear of it.  After supper, the boy went to bed early and pretended to be asleep when the man came in.  He tried to start tickling the boy again the next morning, but the boy jumped out of the bed, saying no thanks.  And then, being as he was already out of bed, he went and got dressed.</P>\r\n	<P>That afternoon on the plane ride home, he pretended to sleep against his mother\'s shoulder, playing over and over in his head what he should say to her.  But again, he couldn\'t speak.  When he got back to school on Monday, it turned out hardly anyone had shown up for the track meet.  The coaches were really angry and embarrassed at the poor turnout so they made everyone run sprints for the whole hour.  All the boy knew was that they wouldn\'t be making him run if they knew what had happened, but there wasn\'t any way he was going to say anything.  So, like that morning two days before with the man and his machine, like when he didn?t want any friends in the new neighborhood, like when his parents had fought, like always, he stayed quiet.</P>\r\n\r\n	<P>Unlike plenty of other kids, the boy was lucky and what happened didn\'t scar him for life.  Within a few weeks, he hardly thought about it at all.  He never had to go back there and see the man again.  Things went on, the boy grew up, had a girlfriend, and graduated from high school.  One day, his mom called him at college to say that  complications brought on by the diabetes had killed the man.  The boy tried to act sorrowful, but it was far from easy and he wasn\'t even convincing himself, so he gave up.  His mother didn\'t notice.  Before long, he again faded from the boy\'s thoughts.  Every now and then he thinks about it, but never for very long.  After all, it\'s just not that important.<P>\r\n'),(1010,'<pre>\r\nOkay.\r\n\r\nI said something quiet and restrained.\r\nusing \r\nthe stretch of words\r\nto illustrate my point.\r\n\r\n:Your breath on the pillow:\r\n\r\n:I was touching your arm:\r\n\r\nThen:\r\nup in smoke: open\r\nmy eyes: \r\n\r\nyou were gone.\r\n\r\nThe sheet flapped in the wind,\r\ncracking like a \r\nwhip.\r\n</pre>'),(1011,'<p>earlier tonight i wanted to eat the world, i was so in need of touching or life or god or beauty or intensity that my teeth and mouth ached, like i needed to eat, suck, or be punched in the face. so i invited chaya over and we got drunk.\r\n<p>\r\ni am writing this at 11:00am, after sleeping zero (<tt>0</tt>) hours, and drinking a lot of whiskey. i don\'t have much to say, except to say that 8 hours ago i needed some trouble, and since then i\'ve been drinking hard and feeling like breathing fire. \r\n</p><p>\r\ndarwin is here, at 11am, and we are listening to the I-F track PORTRAIT OF A DEAD GIRL really fucking loud, all bass, talking about that moment in a track where as DJ or dancer all you can do is scream FUUUUUUCK or SHIIIIIIT.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis is a frivolous post and it has been a frivolous night and morning, multiple hot tub trips, getting breakfast at boogaloos (mimosas!), keeping the drunkenness at a controllable level via carefully spaced out sips of knob creek. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwe stumbled around san francisco, we stumbled so much, we were so loud, annoying, full of ourselves (aries sun aries moon) we are young and drunk sometimes and it\'s all good. more and more i just love chaya, i love being with her, i love egging her on and trusting her to egg me on.\r\n</p><p>\r\nspeaking of egging. i love egg and i am so glad she has re-entered my life lately. \r\n</p><p>\r\nalrighty. back to talking loud and being debaucherous and pretending like it\'s still last night.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1012,'<P>\r\ni am going to wear a belted sweater dress<br>\r\nand noone can stop me</P><p>\r\n\r\nthat is the most unthinkable thing i can think to do</P><p>\r\n\r\nin times of false rebellion, you have to take what you can get.</P><p>\r\n\r\nim a girl, and i like to eat berries and let them drip all over my clothes and down my shirt.</P><p>\r\n\r\nand i do think its sexy when girls keep money in their cleavage. and so does the older lady at the gas station when you go to pay for your gum.</p><p>\r\n\r\nyesterday i jogged all the way to the oakland rose garden</P><p> \r\n\r\nI wore my Mills two toned mesh tank top, and a very robust sports bra.<br>\r\ngo cyclones!</P><p>\r\n\r\nand through its undulating curves and knits and all the way back to the bend where i live\r\n</P><p>\r\n\r\nbelted into the silver round turn of the freeway\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhere arwas hair was gushing volcanic from the humidity and the red shampoo that leaves color deposits</P>\r\n<pre>\r\n-{to le}-> i wore a sundress today and im freezing<br>\r\n<*le*> that is san francisco\'s dirty trick<br>\r\n-{to le}-> so im wearing a flashdance over the shoulder cut collar<br>\r\n-{to le}-> sweatshirt<br>\r\n<*le*> wow<br>\r\n<*le*> stop flirting with me</PRE>\r\n</P><p>\r\narwa has the best tattoo, of a goat jumping for moon. her name means \"mythical mountain goat.\" and she is a capricorn. arwa talks like an ordinary girl, but there is something about the way she moves her hands, she has grace, mythical grace and serious dreamy space.\r\n</P><p>\r\nle made a shirt out of striped tights. she and I should use each other as a fashion accessory, me in my belted sweater dress, and she with her head and neck busted through the crotch like a newborn babe.\r\n</P><p>\r\ni slept in my bra again. it takes too much energy when youre drifting to the dreammachine to lift a finger. \r\n</P><p>\r\nthis boy I like was trying to make me write stuff down, because he likes the way i use words. I quoted eartha kitt that only good girls have time for diaries. and only good girls have time not to sleep in their bras.\r\n</P><p>\r\ni got words. i got words in my hips. i got words between my tits.\r\n</P><p>\r\njoanne said she is making an action figure doll of moi that winds up and squeaks \"i slept in my bra again!\". joanne is the goddess of toy warehouses. black market toy warehouses. \r\n</P><p>\r\nOur Lady of Black Market Toy Warehouses, in a blue hoodie and baseball cap. full of grace.\r\n</P><p>\r\nhave I mentioned that I will wear a belted sweater dress?\r\n</P><p>\r\npeople will be upset.\r\n</P><p>\r\npeople who believe in the healing power of fashion will think that i have spit on their church.\r\n</P><p>\r\nbut I will wake up before anyone, or never go to sleep<br>\r\nand chisel to the door of that church<br>\r\nmy proclamation\r\n</P><p>\r\n\"Fuck you, I am wearing a belted sweater dress!\"<br>\r\nand noone will stop me.</P>'),(1013,'<P>I have a pretty cool apartment. It\'s fairly large for SF, it\'s light and airy, and there are sublimely comfortable spaces within. I spend many hours here, sometimes not leaving for a day or two. I sit on the floor, drawing feverishly so I can make money for neccessities. Stella the cat helps out by sitting in the middle of my artwork.</P>\r\n<P>I\'ve always had this fear of abandonment. Lately I\'ve been feeling like my intense personality keeps people I like from spending time with me. I want company so badly that people are not inclined to give it to me. Why? I like people. I want to be around them more and more now. I want to run through the park and fly kites and throw parties and be surrounded by you.</P>\r\n<P>Sometimes I feel I am not smart enough, witty enough, restrained enough, chatty enough, vegan enough. I want to know what your lives are like. I want to have deep political conversations. I want you to realize that sometimes I make mistakes, sometimes I push a little too hard, and that can make you think of me in an unflattering light. I understand that. Sometimes I push to see how far you will stand with me. It is something I don\'t mean to do. You leave, you avoid, you fear, you are put off by me. Please, don\'t. I may be eccentric and even a little neurotic, but I have a huge loving heart that wants to make tea for you and hear about your dreams.</P>\r\n<P>I feel left out of life. Or is it that I am leaving myself out?</P>\r\n<P>Pathetic.</P>'),(1014,'<p>My doctor told me to try taking some amino acid compound to help balance that shit out in my body, and my brain.</p>\r\n<p>It was to make me feel better, or happier, or something. I dont really remember what. Maybe it was to help me remember more things.</p>\r\n<p>Anyway. That\'s not the point. The point is that this stuff is mixed with water and looks like the urine of a dehydrated person. It smells a little like sweat, and a little like vomit, and a little like burning axle grease, and a little like tears and like dry skin.<br><br>\r\nIt used to make me gag.<br><br>\r\nNow it makes me think I am drinking little bits of myself synthesized by some crazy scientist in oregon, and then it makes me gag.</p>\r\n<p>I still dont know if it is doing me any good, or if I\'m wasting my time and I should go back to living my life without the vomit-sweat-dry-skin-dehydrated-person-urine-tasty-twice-a-day-whether-I-need-it-or-not-beverage.</p>\r\n<p>What I really should do is read about the ones that I am taking while riding the train to my job. But feminism and oracle databases and my paltry stash of CDs have kept me more interested than amino acids have.</p>\r\n<p>Part of the problem is that I dont know where to start. What research is good in this field? How does it really affect my body and brain? If you have any suggestions, dear swinney.org reader, please inform me.</p>\r\n<p>I quit smoking.</p>\r\n<p>While my cravings and mood are fairly manageable in real life, (with a rare exception) my dreams are another story. I\'ve been dreaming more vividly and well, about cigarettes. I\'ve been dreaming of smoking accidentally and then FEELING GUITY for it.</p>\r\n<p>I was meant to be catholic.</p>\r\n<p>It is my brother Joshua\'s birthday. Happy birthday Josh. I still feel like you\'re 10, and I\'m 12. We\'ve both grown up now. We are adults now. We are fucking up now. You\'re about the best brother anyone could ever ask for. You\'ll be about the best boy a girl will ever ask for some day. You will never read this site, though, or see this picture of yourself from the time we ate mushrooms in the park.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1015_1.jpg\'>\r\n'),(1015,'whee\r\n'),(1016,'<p>i\'ve found the problem</p>\r\n<p> i want to be that charmer too</p>\r\n<p>i wanna sucker in every soul around me</p>\r\n<p>every living sorry soul that\'s asking for suckerin</p>\r\n<p>i fantasize about being charming</p>\r\n<p>but that day has come where i realize that i\'m as charming as a four week old panty wedged up an elephant\'s ass</p>\r\n<p> it\'s always the charmers who are heros, </p>\r\n<p> and who doesn\'t want to be a hero or a heroine</p>\r\n<p>i sure as hell do</p>\r\n<p>but i\'m not that charmer</p>\r\n<p> so my hero days are over</p>\r\n<p>and my fantasies dead</p>\r\n<p>i\'m back to the blue ribbon beer</p>\r\n<p>hostess twinkies and smores</p>\r\n<p>i\'m not a charmer nor am i charming</p>\r\n<p>i am not you, cause i smell like poo</p>\r\n'),(1017,'<P>I\'m younger. And my mother drags me to Barnes and Nobles. But all the windows \r\nat B&N have been painted over with some Orange Cream paint. We walk in and \r\nB&N is just HUGE, and there is a guy by a little stand, with two girls next \r\nto him, We Need reservations for B&N, it\'s just like a restaurant. \"There\'s \r\nan opening by Children\'s Books\" he says. We opt out and my mother drags me \r\nto a mall that is adjacent to this B&N. We go into a tight, small clothing \r\nshop. My mother goes wild for some Jeans designed by <a href=\"http://bmwfilms.com/site_layout/film_synopsis.asp?FilmID=3\">Wong \r\nKar-Wai</a>. So I ask the check out girl if they are really designed by him. Well, \r\nthis cute behind the counter tiny Asian chick gives me attitude, and there\'s another \r\ncheck out girl backing her up and I get into a fight with them. Last thing I remember \r\nI was choking her and she was laughing at me. Strange. </P>\r\n\r\n'),(1018,'<p>Upon return from my vacation in New Mexico I found out that once again I have a tent dweller in my back yard. This happened once before while Austin and I were roommates at this house... it bugged the shit out of us because we were never told. The first squater was quiet and rarely bothered us. Besides the fact that he did bring a rotting cow corpse with him (seriously). </p><p>This time is different, it is my upstairs neighbor\'s transient brother. K-dogg and I were never given the option to say, \"yes, it is fine with us to have our privacy invaded,\" or \"no, I don\'t feel like keeping a tent in my back yard for people to live.\" I asked him how long he was going to be living right outside my widow, and he said until he gets his sea leggs back... sounds like a while.</p><p>For clarification, I have no problem with things as such, but this guy lacks a little sense and judgement (he interupted my nap to show me a catapillar he had found in the back yard). Yesturday morning I was watching a movie and was startled by this man\'s voice at my door... he had been looking in for sometime I take it, and he proceeds to talk about a strange looking dump truck that passed by... he tells me that he is going to investigate this... and then starts ranting about the weather and more. I am watching a movie, though I rarely get uneasy about interuptions of any sort - this made me uneasy because of the lack of content this gentleman provided me in  his conversation... as it was apparent i was into my \"Charlies Angels\" movie. Later in the day, he appears again at my screen door as I am resting peacefuly on my couch having a conversation with k-dogg. Intrusive, and uninviting... ugh.</p><p> I feel as though I should be more warm with the  guy, but I have to admit that I do carry a prejudice about a few of the homeless that roam my neighborhood, brother of a friend or not... What steps shall I take with this? It seems obvious that I should speak with my neighbors about their lack of neighborly respect for our harmony. The other problem is that the neighbor does not live here! He lives with his girlfriend, and is only seen about once a month if that. So k-dogg, derek (the other neighbor, who seems miffed as well), and my self are left with his needy brother, two broken down cars, a rotting piano in our backyard, and lack of respect for us.</p> <p>I guess this is more of a statement rather than a question, but has anybody have any suggestions? Am I upset because I lack compassion? Should I work on this, or is my complaint valid? I am hoping that K-dogg will unleash her dogg teeth on my neighbor next time she see\'s him... mainly cause I don\'t want to do it my self... WWSD (what would swinney do?)... my best bet is that he would bite all of them with words my feeble little mind can\'t comprehend... </p>'),(1019,'<p>i\'m tired of college. i\'m tired of the scantrons, the number two pencils, the administration charging miscellaneous fees for god knows what. i\'m tired of having my parents hoping that i pull up my grades to be an academic hero at graduation.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni have a year left if i carry a lot of units - and i dont care - i\'ll fuckin do it.  i\'ll pass my classes and i\'ll be outta here.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut my best friend tells me that if i rush into the real world i\'ll regret it. that i have the rest of my life to work.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni dunno\r\n</p>'),(1020,'<p>i found the fun in funeral</p>\r\n<p>i got up and put on a suit, one of them slick cut suits that say \"i\'m slick\"</p>\r\n<p>slipped on my black ann taylor pumps</p>\r\n<p>and put on my shades</p>\r\n<p>off i go to a funeral service where i realize everyone there looked like they were from some mafia</p>\r\n<p>the big sunglasses</p>\r\n<p>big suits</p>\r\n<p>the godfather is there and he rips a big one in the middle of the service</p>\r\n<p>you know you\'re rich and powerful when you can rip gas out loud anywhere and anytime</p>\r\n<p>someone\'s son...his wife...she looks like a stewardess...we find out she was a news anchor women back in good ol ko-re-a.</p>\r\n<p>no one else is ripping gas but the godfather</p>\r\n<p>a sign of deep respect</p>\r\n<p>we take a couple of deep bows and pay our respects</p>\r\n<p>pour a glass of johnny red and somberly empty it onto the grave</p>\r\n<p>i know he was rolling in his grave, \"somebody, anybody, just drink that willya!  quit wasting it. do you know how much a bottle of that stuff is?\"</p>\r\n<p>of course after they pour half a gallon of various scotch onto the gravesite, the rotound captains of the families have to light a cig for poor dead uncle</p>\r\n<p>that\'s right, just place the lit cig right on top of the puddle of alcohol</p>\r\n<p>the ceremony doesn\'t end there</p>\r\n<p>it never ends</p>\r\n<p>it\'ll go on, another\'ll keel over from too much liquor and smoking</p>\r\n<p>and the party\'ll continue one</p>\r\n<p>and the rotound captains will drink again, and the women will cry...</p>'),(1021,'<p>\r\nI was just coming back from the bar with my old friend Brian when i heard glass shattering.  Suddenly alert for trouble, i began scanning my surroundings.  Then i saw them.  Two cops chasing some guy in the rain.  When they caught up with him, he began to fight.  Hard.  They pepper gassed him, to no effect.  Then one of them kicked his knee out.  On the ground now, he continued to fight.  The other cop drew his baton and beat this guy over and over.  Finally he stopped fighting and allowed himself to be handcuffed.  The cops did everything right this time.  They didn\'t hit him in the head, instead aiming pain-compliance blows to his hips and arms.  I know he\'d hurt them both at least a bit, one of them is going to have a huge shiner come morning.  But they did the job right, didn\'t let temper enter into it.  For once, the cops in my neighborhood proved to be worthwhile.  \r\n</p>'),(1022,'<p>planning social outings sometimes seems so impossibly stressful.  who wants to do this, who wants to do that, trying to do something with everyone because you want to hang out together.  so you try to find something that is interesting.  and at the right time.  and then make sure everyone can get there.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand sometimes we try to do to much.   or sometimes there is traffic.  and sometimes people flake out.  and sometimes I flake out.    and sometimes we get all bent out of shape.  or overly apologetic for our own flakiness.\r\n</p><p>\r\nif i don\'t see you today, i\'m sure i will see you again soon.  sometimes after a bout of social planning i feel all clingy, like a smothering all-encompassing romantic attachment.  because i DO love my friends that much sometimes.  but really, you will see your friends again soon, there is no need for the clingy.  there is no need for the profuse apologies.  just relax.  your friends still love you even if you think you suck.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso this is the deal.  i like to hang out with my friends.  i\'m usually pretty easy to please most of the time.  i try to get people to hang out.  sometimes it will fail and there is only the future.  it\'s okay if you flake out sometimes, after all, i might flake out next time.  but, if we actually get together, and hang out, it is super splendid.\r\n</p>'),(1023,'<p>i get a lot of email from young girls who read my <a href=\"http://auntie.diaryland.com\">diaryland</a> entries about mothering le and mandy. <br><br>\r\n\r\none of the emails i received this week was from a 15 year old girl who had just begun reading kerouac recently.  she identifies with my stories of le and has been reading both of our journals...<br><br>\r\n\r\na long time ago - ten years this month, as a matter of fact - le met a guy on prodigy who \"turned her on\" to kerouac and henry rollins and thinking outside herself. <br><br>\r\n\r\nthat was not a bad thing.  the bad thing was that the person she met represented himself in the beginning as a 17 year old boy, and he turned out to be a mentally ill 28 year old man. <br><br>\r\n\r\nhe urged le to run away from home and join him, i can\'t remember where, new mexico or somewhere. <br><br>\r\n\r\nit was a difficult time for us.  mostly because the ideas about life he imparted to her were not totally reprehensible.  the literature he shared with her was enlightening in many respects, and i will agree that it was information that didn\'t harm her, in fact, it probably helped her tremendously in some ways.  i don\'t know how she thinks back on that period, \"she was so much older then, she\'s younger than that now...\" . <br><br>\r\n\r\nthe predator who found her is probably still alive. he is probably still trolling about online, looking for young girls in transition who are too smart to buy into YM magazine, who don\'t giggle, who think britney spears is a fucking troll. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni have asked this girl WHO suggested she read kerouac, and i will not be surprised if it turns out to who i am terribly afraid it is. <br><br>\r\n\r\nhe\'s suggesting the dalai lama these days... <br><br>\r\n</p>'),(1024,'<p>\r\nim feeling very lazy. i have thought about going out for a walk but its raining. its glum outside. people are sleeping and the apartment is empty. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel all this bent up energy inside. im anxious about going home yet im unable to concentrate on packing. ive done a few boxes. ill clean out the storage room today. im leaving my room to do last. its an easy pack in here. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was out on my balcony this morning watching the drops of water hit the railing. i looked out at the city and thought about how leaving is hard and easy all at once. i could zoom out and look at the whole city. a city like most. apartment buildings, shops and scrapers. when i zoom in i notice all the things that make the city what it is. the people, the stores, the road signs, and my liberty. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim willing to lose some liberty by going home. im willing to because i want to save up some money to travel further then ottawa. moving to toronto will give me the modivation to get the fuck out again. friends are moving all over the country/world and well ive talked of doing the same thing for long that i need a bit of a push. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni want to get a paying job and apply like a madman for jobs in england, dubai(u.a.e), and nyc. the job in england im looking at most seriously right now is with janes publications. in dubai im gonna look for anything interesting, and in nyc its the assosiated press. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor now tho i feel really alone in all of my plans. im confused a bit about leaving ottawa, but deep down inside i think its the right thing to do. there are more reasons to go back home then to stay here. its too easy to let depression take hold here. its too easy to walk down to the pub or call on some gee\'z. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hope i dont regret this. \r\n</p>\r\n '),(1025,'<p>I need to get a haircut. Plain and simple. No, not the cut, just the need to get one. No doubt about it. Yes, the need and the cut is what I need bad. But I\'m no sadist, if that\'s what you\'re thinking. Or am I into cutting myself. That kind of stuff is for elitists. And I\'m not. I just need a haircut. But that\'s probably not going to happen today.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had a list of stuff I was planning to do today:\r\n<br><br>\r\nget a silk screening kit<br>\r\nride my bike 24 miles to my gf\'s house (24.2  miles)<br>\r\nget a ride back<br>\r\ncut my hair<br>\r\nwork on something for work<br>\r\nwash my car<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>What I actually did today:\r\n<br><br>\r\nwent to the art store but they have new hour (none on sunday)<br>\r\nlooked at my bike <br>\r\ntook a shower (no hot water)<br>\r\nate lunch and fell asleep on the floor<br>\r\nwoke up to the dog sitting on my hand<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I feel kind of bad because I told the gf that I had a lot of things to do today and she was like, \"Okay. I\'ll just see you another day.\" But I haven\'t done jack shit... shit. Sorry about my language but I just spilled my beverage. Great, that reminds me--I was supposed to do laundry today, too. That\'s not going to happen either.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I\'m going to cut my nails though and watch the Bucks and 76ers duke it out. Both teams should feel very lucky not to have such a failure like me on either of their teams. </p>'),(1026,'<p>nothing comes from nothing comes to nothing.  grey blobby clay. waiting. waiting. waiting. every day means something new to be molded. i try hard. sculpting. painting. firing myself. some bits i scrape away. dead parts. very very tasty. i gave some to a guy. beautiful he said. then kept licking. wanting more. scavenger sharks circling. red meat. they dont get no flavor. recreating the pieces. shaping. deep places and serious burn. sometimes im cracked. a big mistake they say. magical providence is my quiet reply.</p>'),(1027,'<p>after being in hong kong for a week, i\'ve noticed some distinct differences between the american culture and the culture here in hong kong.  although hong kong isn\'t the best representation of the rest of asia (being that it was once a brittish colony), it is still interesting to see how different we really are in some aspects.<br><br>\r\nthis weekend, we went to a part of town called \"lan kwai fong\" which is a hip place for expats and gweilo to hang out.<br><br>\r\nwhile in other parts of hong kong that appeared to be more asian in nature, i saw one dispute between what looked like a customer and a shop owner.  they were calm and talking to each other in the presence of a police officer.  they looked almost ashamed to be having a dispute.  however, when i went to the part of town that was mostly brits, american folk, and people australia.. i saw two brawls and one verbal spat that looked like it was about to head out of control.  this really suprised me and i filed it away for later use.  people in general here seem to be extremely respectful and reserved.  for the most part, everyone tries to make an effort to be \"polite\", which to americans .. might come off as rude.<br><br>\r\nanother observation is the video game arcades.  i went to the namco arcade yesterday and i was amazed at how peaceful all the games were.  they had games based around typing skill, photography, music (guitar, turntables, drums, beatboxing) and dance.  very few games had anything to do with violence.  also, because of the nature of the games, there was a better representation of females in the arcade.  the games seemed to be fun for both sexes as well as teaching them skills to retain information as well as coordination.  to be honest, i think we have much to learn from these other cultures that we forget exist in our day to day muggle lives.  i\'ll write more observations later.'),(1028,'<p>I\'m busting out with bubble gum<br>\r\nlike the ocean<br>\r\nits flourescent tendrils catching fleshy tide after tide<br>\r\nlike when the image of your face<br>\r\nwashes over my legs, between the dark<br>\r\nhot salty dankness of midnight at<br>\r\nRalph\'s on the leather couch,<br> \r\non the phone like I\'m 16.<br>\r\non the phone giggling, while jellyfish<br>\r\nsqueeze through the wall<br>\r\nwhen i can see through dawn at<br>\r\nAuto Row in Oakland<br>\r\nin windows moving across the ceiling, when waves of <br>sun and saliva flash between the blankets<br>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou\'ve drooled on me now,<br>\r\ntelling me I taste like my father\'s basement, <br>\r\ntelling me it\'s not over,<br>\r\nit will always be turning on itself,<br>\r\nlike that translucent fish that catches itself for food.</p>\r\n'),(1029,'<P>Pick me<br>\r\nbecause my dick is thick<br>\r\nthough my blue swimsuit<br>\r\nas you spot me fickle<br>\r\nin my eyes<br>\r\nsultry lean against<br>\r\nthe trees<p>\r\nSize me<br>\r\nup with my pressed chest<br>\r\nglowing like brass<br>\r\nmy chiseled gaze in the sun<br>\r\nas you pass<br>\r\nalone.<p>\r\nHow bout me<br>\r\nwith my fine ass<br>\r\nmincing past you<br>\r\nin a yellow Speedo<br>\r\nas the sun<br>\r\ngoes down<br>\r\non two guys at once.<p>\r\nOver here<br>\r\nyou\'ll get lucky<br>\r\nmy cock cutting an<br>\r\narc inside my lycra life<br>\r\nbeside my<br>\r\nwell-healed house.<p>\r\nFuck me<br>\r\nbecause my pink nipples<br>\r\nspring back to the finger<br>\r\nand you\'re free<br>\r\nto do what you want to do.<br>\r\nLet\'s do E<br>\r\nand I\'ll lose you<br>\r\nin the undulating<br>\r\nmass.<p>\r\nChoose me<br>\r\ncause there ain\'t<br>\r\nno one else like me<br>\r\nand my abs are<br>\r\nway harder<br>\r\nthan they look.<p>'),(1030,'<p>i\'m at a loss for words, it\'s just a tangle mess inside,</P>\r\n<p>every noun, pronoun, adverb, verb, philosophy, theory, fact, opinion, thought, resounds</p>\r\n<p>clear in the head</p>\r\n<p>it\'s dying to shout, tho it\'s just never had a voice<p/>\r\n<p>oh i know...you...someone...looks at it and can\'t help but to think</p>\r\n<p>it\'s pathetic; a louse; no use, except for maybe to fetch the drinks</p>\r\n<p>it sits on the couch, and slurps pepsi unconspicuously</p>\r\n<p>it grows gray hairs, and wrinkles, probably faster than the average fellow.  it watches the tube with a notepad in it\'s hand.  it eats fast food for dinner every night.  but it sees through the thick and never mentions a word cause the thick always clears when given time.</p>\r\n<p>and he\'ll see some things, new elements he missed.  and he\'ll want to yell, but the fog rolls in and it never left.</p>\r\n'),(1031,'<p>a lot of words begin with l. i will list a few briefly.\r\n<p>\r\nle.<br>\r\nlady.<br>\r\nlassie.<br>\r\nlather.<br>\r\nlavendar.<br>\r\n(like the soap in your shower.)<br>\r\nlanguid.<br>\r\nlandmine.<br>\r\nlover.<br>\r\nlore.<br>\r\nlucky.<br>\r\nlime.<br>\r\nlick.<br>\r\nlashes.<br>\r\nlure.<br>\r\nloopy.<br>\r\nliturgy.<br>\r\nlay.<br>\r\nlonging.<br>\r\nreally, you shuold say that word this way:\r\n<p>\r\nlawwwwwwwwwnging.\r\n<p>\r\n(robotec9 (1:21 AM):	so you love le like a possession?<br>\r\negglit (1:21 AM):	no<br>\r\negglit (1:21 AM):	i love le like sunsets<br>\r\nrobotec9 (1:21 AM):	like a design?<br>\r\negglit (1:21 AM):	i love le like art<br>\r\nrobotec9 (1:21 AM):	ok<br>\r\negglit (1:22 AM):	when im with le i feel like she gives me her whole heart, like she wants to show you that she is alive and she is not afraid, and she wants to know how you are alive and she wants to put her hands on your chest and feel you breathing)\r\n<p>\r\na friend and I<br>\r\nonce invented a word<br>\r\nto describe the concentration of energy at the horizon<br>\r\nthat word began with l too<br>\r\n\"lumina.\"\r\n<p>\r\n(coming to california<br>\r\nmakes it so<br>\r\nyou can watch the sunset<br>\r\nover the water<br>\r\nat the magic hour<br>\r\nwhen skin turns blue,<br>\r\nand those goddesses hushes you <br>\r\nwith their sky gels, and love songs)<br>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is the best way to understand about le love:\r\n<p>\r\nmake any of these words out loud<br>\r\nand the moment where you open your mouth and\r\n<p>\r\nyour tongue<br>\r\nlicks the roof of your mouth\r\n<p>\r\nis where le \r\n<p>\r\nlives.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1032,'<PRE>\r\nThere is a cup of coffee \r\non the desk next to me.\r\n\r\nIt is another\r\nmemorized day.\r\n\r\nMy fingers move\r\nas if independent.\r\n\r\nThe computer is impassive,\r\nBut the cursor still moves.\r\n\r\nThis poem feels more important\r\nthan any paycheck ever has.\r\n\r\nI love my job.\r\nDon\'t get me wrong.\r\nIt\'s the best fucking\r\njob I\'ve ever had.\r\n\r\nSing to the Lord\r\na new song.\r\n</PRE>'),(1033,'<p><i><b>Vegetarian \"Poultry\" - Tofu </i><</b></p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>77.  Velvet tofu</b><i> \"chicken\" </i>...............7.95</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>78.  Gluten fried</b><i> \"chicken\" </i><b>with lemongrass, green pepper and onion</b>...............8.25</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>79.  Sliced tofu</b><i> \"chicken\" </i><b>with snow peas and mushrooms</b>...............8.25</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>80.  Sliced tofu</b><i> \"chicken\" </i><b>with onion</b>...............8.25</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>81.  Ginger gluten</b><i> \"chicken\" </i><b> and Tofu-Ki</b>...............8.25</p>\r\n'),(1034,'<P>who is this guy swinney? should i invite him to live with me? could i stand it? could he stand me? would he be nice to spaz? does age matter? do beliefs matter? is change good? can we even find a place to share in this renters hellhole of a place? does he make good coffee? would he enjoy eating dinner with me? does silent intensity bother him? does he need a home, like i need a home?</P>'),(1035,'<p>saturday, i sent an email to the austin chapter of swinney posters asking if anyone wanted to meet for beer, and bmg replied, so i met him at showdown at 9:30.<BR>\r\ni was at my friend chris\'s apartment for his birthday when i said, \"i have to leave; i\'m meeting total strangers at showdown.\"<BR>\r\n\"uh. good luck with that!\"</p>\r\n<p>bmg was wearing a white t-shirt on which he had hand-written with a big fat black marker, \"b M g.\" when i walked in, he and matt were at the bar getting a pitcher and he spun around and said, \"are you?\" and i nodded and he pointed at his shirt.<BR>\r\nhe won <b>big points</b> right away.</p>\r\n<p>my friend melody ended up joining the three of us and after beers, we went to <a href=\"http://www.drafthouse.com\">alamo draft</a> to see <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0091860\">river\'s edge</a>, meeting dj luna and mscreant (and little dahlia) there.<BR>\r\nthen a car ride where tipsy matt kept melody and i laughing all the way home.</p>\r\n<p>hooray for new friends. we have plans to see mouse on mars and tortoise when they come to town in two weeks. mogwai, too!</p>\r\n<p>then, last night, i was finishing sewing my cargo rave skirt (pockets pockets and some more pockets) when there was a knock on my door.<BR>\r\nit was <a href=\"http://cromulent.org\">nancy</a>, who i met during <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/sxsw/2/\">south by southwest</a>, who just moved to austin for the summer and had walked to my apartment because she has no phone, no internet, and is massively stressed out.</p>\r\n<p>i suggested spider house. so we went and while i drank iced coffee, she ate two enormous gooey brownies and told me about her problems finding a summer job.</P>\r\n<p>and while she was inside getting the second brownie, i thought, \"<a href=\"http://mr.unpopular.com/~szajic\">url</a>s, <a href=\"http://mr.unpopular.com/~gac\">url</a>s, <a href=\"http://www.glassamp.org\">url</a>s, <a href=\"http://elly.org\">url</a>s. i give a lot of my words to people that i can\'t touch.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.links.net/vita/speak/vonnegut/\">can you get this from a computer</a>?\"</p>\r\n<p>i tried to imagine my life without these people.<BR>\r\ni would not be half of the person that i am without them! which is not to say that i would not be as rich and multi-faceted of a person, but i would not be <b>this joanna</b>, i\'d be someone else entirely.</p>\r\n<p>can you get this from a computer?</p>'),(1036,'<p>Grayson Angel graduated with a BA in English from a California state college and promptly moved away.  He wears a fedora. \r\n\r\n<p>Undecided on an ultimate destination, he drove across the country in a Piece Arrow with a suitcase full of brown, three piece suits, a handgun and fourteen bottles of Old Granddad.   He didn?t stay long in any city, stopping only long enough to give out dollar cigars to the prostitutes.  \r\n\r\n<p>Something, he?s not sure what brought him to Boston, perhaps a propensity for the fire-and-brimstone sermons of Puritan preacher, Jonathan Edwards (1703-1758).  Since settling in Beantown, he?s worked in a copy shop and a coffee shop.  He\'s been a gravedigger, a pruner of trees and a drinker of prune juice.  He\'s also been an office manager of a architecture firm and a legal assistant at an insurance firm.  He?s been unemployed, down-on-his luck and has gambled in the streets for pocket change.  He?s drunk cheap beer at cheaper bars and eaten bad food using greasy spoons.\r\n\r\n<p>Somewhere along the line he learned how to play the ukulele and he now sings and plays on the platforms of Boston?s subway stations which produces enough income to subsist on a diet of saltine crackers and American cheese, both of which he shares with the rats in his coldwater flat.  The one window in his apartment is cracked, which lets in a bitter chill in the winter.  \r\n\r\n<p>Grayson  writes when he finds the time, penning stories that have no plot or character and are vague and symbolic at best.  While they have not yet achieved much acceptance in the literary community, Grayson continues to try.  He also writes poetry, most of which is bad and dispensable, but he will not be dissuaded by critics, assuming that his poetry prompts his fiction which in turn prompts more poetry.\r\n\r\n<p>Today he is sick to his stomach, having consumed to much Metamucil and vegetarian hot dogs.  He likes sauerkraut but doesn?t have any in the medicine cabinet.   \r\n\r\n<p>He sometimes wishes that a nice young woman would come up to his room and dust. \r\n'),(1037,'<p>You can\'t help automatically blaming a young author who does tacky, desperate publicity things and when interviews come out more like \'SWM iso vapid sitcom actress,\' but maybe sometimes it isn\'t that they are machinating publicity whores, maybe it\'s the opposite, maybe they haven\'t really given their *public image* a moment\'s thought or even really acknowledged it\'s separate existence and maybe the professionally-dressed people with vacuumed BMWs and kryptonite business cards bought them an expensive lunch and said they were going to tour some teen magazine and maybe have their pictures taken and the whole bit about the sheer, glittery shirt didn\'t come up until after they had a taken a dozen other pictures and anyway it just seemed like a funny, kitschy thing to do, more fun than you probably had today and maybe they can\'t be bothered to strategize about their fucking indie cred every time an intriguingly novel suggestion comes up over a forty dollar pitcher of margaritas, you know?</P>\r\n<P>Anyway, I am trying to think of a book that is a significantly bigger celebrity than its author.  The best I could do so far is *Lord of the Flies.*</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1038,'We lounge round the breakfast table at night<br>\r\nsmoking and sipping our zinfandels<br>\r\nquoting from movies and lyrics we like<br>\r\nzingers by bitches and infidels.<br>\r\nI think of cummings\' Cambridge ladies<br>\r\nbut our souls are unfurnished and damp.<br>\r\nSit up and lay down, make small stabs,<br>\r\ngive in to pleasure, immediate and cramped<br>\r\nas we are by defining our place and running<br>\r\nthe race, with the great lazy human condition<br>\r\nrestless and wack as it is, waiting to make us<br>\r\naware and unknown, make us struggle and moan<br>\r\nfor those brief tingling bursts from the gut<br>\r\nthe saying yes the knowing nod-<br>\r\nbut where to start when we\'ve gotta<br>\r\nmiddle manage and get to class at the gym,<br>\r\nThursday TV and here we sit seraphim<br>\r\nlaughing at the coming crash.<br>\r\nMaybe jobless, credit rating in the trash<br>\r\nwe\'d make more art and host salons<br>\r\nstart a movement and try our luck<br>\r\nBut what the fuck with the local news<br>\r\nthe talking heads and all the yammerin<br>\r\n(at least our parents have their liberal views)<br>\r\nbut we believe in Kerouac and Pollock (both dead)<br>\r\nwe believe in jazz singers and hip-hop and punk<br>\r\nthe tenorman who squeals and the beatboy<br>\r\nwho rises up and weaves great mad loops of <br>\r\nwomp womp bomp rig-dig-dig-diggity bomp-<br>\r\nAnd will we have to stop the quiet<br>\r\nborn and cleansed over all these years<br>\r\nby college grads and suburban fears<br>\r\nthe commuters with their coffee mugs<br>\r\ntheir footwear and software and new age beers.<br>\r\nIs it a lull that can be broken or will we<br>\r\njust cause small rumblings in cities<br>\r\nthat get reported in Newsweek and Time?<br>\r\nWill the soon be as bland as now<br>\r\nand how soon is now, how now is now?<br>\r\nI don\'t wanna know I don\'t wanna know<br>\r\nbut will we get to make some noise<br>\r\none day one day, or will we keep on biding time<br>\r\nshoveling shit and making these rhymes<br>\r\n\"synching up\" and \"looping in\" while the<br>\r\nemail pours in and out, so on and so forth<br>\r\ninkless words born on wires like smack<br>\r\ncoarsing through corporate capillaries<br>\r\nthe effort of the group the only effort raised<br>\r\nbut here we all are with hands, amazed,<br>\r\nwith novels to write and poems to read<br>\r\nand brand new grooves that\'ll blow our minds<br>\r\nI don\'t wanna know what\'s true in the end<br>\r\nI don\'t wanna know where we\'re gonna go<br>\r\nbut let\'s trumpet the holy-roller war cry over the sea<br>\r\nwe\'re people with bloodlust and rage aren\'t we? <br>\r\nwe\'re savages who fight when we\'re wronged<br>\r\nwe\'re beasts who gnaw on bones and suckle our young-<br>\r\nLet\'s all have sex when the moment\'s right<br>\r\nand let\'s sit back and banter some night<br>\r\nbut not for too long before we get down <br>\r\nfall on the floor get up and jump around<br>\r\nbecause there\'s new moons to lick<br>\r\nand asses to kick and dumb fucking<br>\r\nideas to blow apart and dance upon like <br>\r\nthe bone true tribe we are, young and sharp,<br>\r\nfull of the might our mothers knew we had.<br>\r\nI don\'t hate the ladies who wanna get engaged<br>\r\nI don\'t hate the fellas who\'ve never known rage<br>\r\nbut we need more rat bastards and we need more cuntz.<br>\r\nWe need more scandals and rituals and ballsout bloodstains.<br>\r\nGet me a gun, a lead pipe and some warpaint,<br>\r\ngimme your wallet, your life, and all your sorry debt<br>\r\nbring me a truckload of loose women, or better yet<br>\r\na gaggle of fags (with nothing in their pretty heads <br>\r\nbut E and K and hard cocks and stock trades)<br>\r\nand I\'ll show you a new universe of noise<br>\r\na big goddamn mess of faces and lines that\'ll<br> \r\nmake children cry and fools lay down and die<br>\r\nand make the deities weep like dogs<br>\r\nand become the true great American thing<br>\r\nthe whatzit we\'ve all been waiting for, folks,<br>\r\nyes give me what I want, give me days and love<br>\r\nand some money to buy food and wine<br>\r\nand I\'ll show you the answer my friends<br>\r\nthe life of the leaves, pie in the sky<br>\r\nmost all-knowingest, breakbeat, tangible,<br>\r\nlaughable, transcendent, sign-o-the-times cultural event<br>\r\never to touch the souls of ten million.<br>\r\nThat\'ll be the day, o brother, that\'ll be the day.<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1039,'<p>\r\nive been listening to the same 3 smiths songs lets say for the past week. on repeat. not the whole week. sometimes ill throw on a happyhardcore set, something ive made, electro, punk, and my nightime sleepysleepytimeohsochillaxin6000 mp3 playlists. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nD:\\m p 3\\g u i t a r\\The Smiths - Please, Please, Please, Let Me Get What I Want.mp3\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\ni like this song. i guess its got the same lines i use a lot when im talking to myself. \r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nD:\\m p 3\\g u i t a r\\The Smiths - Asleep.MP3\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nits about suicide i guess, but i dont think of it as that. i too dont want to wake up on my own anymore. indeed if i had a girl who got me to sleep by singing a sweet little song id smile with my eyes closed.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nD:\\m p 3\\g u i t a r\\The Smiths - back to the old house (acoustic).mp3\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nwhen my uncle lived at home he burned the place down. this song just reminds me of the fire and what it put my mom and grandmother through. to be honest my uncle came out ontop. he didnt plan any of it, but im sure he thinks it was a blessing. It might have been. the house is brand spanking new with a lot of the sentimental value type shit remaining. the uncle is kicked out and im going home to something sorta new.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni sit here with almost nothing to say. i have things on my mind im sure none of you would care to read about. i went to the duke today and the bartender who just got engaged to be married(in october) was there and she was lookin really good. she had on this blue top and a really nice long jean skirt with some of those new fancy sketchers on. she looked good. cloths are fantastic when you got style. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe past week has been dreamless. well nothing amazing. i can remember one night i had a dream i was a spitfire in wwII. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni sat down and had a beer and a bacon cheese chicken burger. its my fave sandwich they have there. the only other thing i really like on their menu is their chicken nachos. they are good.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe duke is downstairs of the lockmaster pub which inturn is downstairs from the somerset hotel. the whole kit and kaboodle is owned by the same dude/company and well the duke is their sorta ritzy type place. the lockmaster is dirty and has loud obnox people all the time sawdust on the floor type place. the somerset hotel is basically a prost hangout and band hotel. id pay to stay there but not to sleep....if ya know what i mean. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe duke of somerset was some rich dude back in th e days of all that jive. they named a bar after him because his picture is pretty cool looking. for the most part the duke is a british pub. darts tournaments, soccer/football on the tv, and any game where a british dude is playing. its a chill place. dimly lit, it has really nice and comfy chairs you can slouch in. i go there pretty much every day to have something to eat and drink. i havnt really cooked a meal in my kitchen since january. i like to say my kitchen is getting remodled but that isnt the case. im just lazy and eating is a good excuse the leave the house and be social. when i go wandering i feel as though everyone else knows im wandering. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni guess ill miss going to the duke. there are some fine looking ladies who attend and work there. however there are fine looking ladies all over the world. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1040,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1040_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>\r\nall this offtopic shit in the comment windows is buggin me out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlet me tell you why.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncomments are meant to comment on the above article. they are not meant to catch up on old times and plan for new ones. we have email, icb, irc, icq, aol messanger, msn messanger, and a whole lot of other ways to communicate to people on the one and one.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni really dont mean to be rude, but like it just seems a little weird for me to go to the comments portion of a page only to read comments that have nothing to do with the article. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have joined countless socialist and left wing discussion groups and i had to leave all of them because nothing talked about had anything to do with the discussion list. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just dont want to see swinney org get bogged down with information that no one really cares about or understands. secondly, if you want to get in contact with the author, then write them an email or drop them a line if you know them personally. why do the rest of us have to see it?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow ill be all you offtopic comment monkies out there think im a real bitch. I might be, but im positive im not the only bitch out there. \r\n</p>'),(1041,'<p>i\'m really shocked at the state of homeless people in this country.  on the first day, i saw a man on the street corner wrapped in robes and he appeared to have leprosy.  his flesh was falling OFF.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe next person i saw was a girl that was obviously a burn victim.. after her, a man who beat his head on the ground while laying there with no legs.  later down the street, a man with no arms.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy heart went out to them.  i wanted to scoop them all up and give them a good home.  it was obvious that serious medical conditions while you are poor here go untreated and you are left in the streets to die.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni want to take pictures of these people and bring them back home to show to homeless people in america.  i want to show them how lucky they are and that they are far better off and can do much more for themselves.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhomeless people here are treated like an apparition.  people walk by them as if they might be able to walk through them.  millions of people and all i see in their buckets .. equals one american dollar.  i give them my food and i do what i can,  but i wish there was something else that i could do to help.  </P>'),(1042,'<p>INCOMING MESSAGE FROM THE FRONTIER:</p>\r\n<p>About 6,000 cattle, the lifeline of Mongolia\'s nomadic herders, have been killed. It seemed like it was coming from a few houses down, so I wasn\'t concerned. Plus, it did smell pretty good.<p>\r\n<p>Sometimes he or she comes from an abusive household. The potentially deadly fires these young people set may be symbolic and even self-destructive.</p>\r\n<p>First get your child out of danger and away from the source of the burn. If his clothing is on fire, smother the flames with a towel, blanket, or whatever covering is available. Then remove any clothing that may be covering the burned skin, unless it\'s stuck to the skin.</p>\r\n<p>Probe the wound to see if you can figure out what caused it. Remove live insects with tweezers. Drain the baby\'s fluid by holding him in a sitting position. Give abdominal thrusts. Hot gases can come under the shelter and right up into your face. I can remember when they took that stocking mask off, it took some of the skin right off his face.</p>\r\n<p>There may be hope. The first human voices were transmitted from Little America on February 1, 1934 and later a weekly broadcast was carried over the Columbia Broadcasting System in the United States. Additionally, this expedition marked the first time that cosmic ray and meteor observations were taken. Although the First Expedition was the beginning of the mechanical age in Antarctica, the Second Expedition took mechanical and electrical resources to a new level. Motor-driven generating plants provided Little America with electrical power, thereby allowing use of electrical power tools used in construction and maintenance of mechanical devices used at Little America as well as exercises in the field.</p>\r\n<p>END TRANSMISSION</p>\r\n'),(1043,'<p>\r\nim so drunk.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went out today and got a six pack and a plastic bottle of mike hard lemonade. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni drank the mikes. i drank three beers and got restless. i went to he duke and started reading fear and loathing in las vegas and had a chicken nachos, three shots of yager and a couple of pints. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhool showed up.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nhe brought me some green goodness and we chatted and then i came home and talked on the phone with an irc friend who called me. it kinda got my mind off of the shitty things i was thinking about before i got on the phone with her. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen i hung up, talked to roommate for a bit and then told him i was going to bed. trying to jerk off while on paxil, erb, and alcohol is hard so i gave up after about 30 minutes and the phone rang. my heart jumped and i faked going out to get some water only to hear that it was who i had hoped it was on the phone. im so pathetic. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntalked to the phone person for a bit and phone person didnt wanna go with me to get some smokes so i just tried to talk and phone person was tired so i just left it at that. phone person didnt goto shanes going away party so phone person wont goto mine. it hurts. i need smokes. i need them a lot right now. maybe ill make the journey alone.  yes im going to do that now. brb to finish this. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe only reason i know its been over 15 minutes since i last wrote was because my screen was off when i came back. no phone messages. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni decided to go on the mission to get smokes because once i decided i needed a cigarette the nervousness of going outside and getting violently mugged went away and i felt like i could do it. on the way to the 24hour store i bumped into an old guy from work. paul. we chatted for a couple of minutes and then motioned as though i had someplace to go. he understood and said goodnight and we were off. it was good to interact with someone at that time of night. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nentered the 24hour store and right away there were two people flirting with each other with the fruit this place sold. i was discusted and depressed and decided to get an orange juice before looking to see if any of the porn mags were worth their weight. none of  them were so i got cigs and some orange juice and left. the two flirts still going at it with some grapefruit. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the way home a woman in a taurus(my sign) drove by with a back right(my right) tire blown out. the flat noise of the tire annoyed me and i shot her a glance of death. she looked at me, but only to register my presence. i was too far for my death stare. she had no clue. the only thing she must have known was that she had a flat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the duration of the walk home i had hoped paul was standing where id left him. he wasnt of course and on the duration after that point i looked up to see if phone person\'s light was still on. it was. still up. on the phone. not lonely. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim drinking my orange juice, beck\'s, and having a cig. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncould life get any better?\r\n</p>'),(1044,'So today while I was at work, sitting in my office writing reworking and rewriting scripts so as best to appease the store owners and convince them that we\'re not really wasting their time by trying to keep their staff from getting Occupational Overuse Syndrome when one of my closest friends emailed me to tell me that she\'d have enough money for her ticket overseas saved by August.  Which is only two months away. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nI think I\'m pretty much the only person I know who doesn\'t want to go on an OE, partly because I was a diplomat brat and traveled a lot as a child.  But I couldn\'t stomach the thought of packing my life up into a backpack and setting off for an indefinate amount of time. I don\'t want to stay in hostels or sleep on floors, or work in a pub earning 2 pounds an hour.  I am not that brave.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThis doesn\'t mean that I don\'t want to travel, because I do.  I just have set destinations and would only go for limited amounts of time.  I want to go to \r\n<LI>1.  San Fran, Seattle (what can I say, I am a child of the Grunge generation), Las Vegas and New York\r\n<LI>2.  Back to Europe, specifically to Bonn and Amsterdam\r\n<LI>3.  Iceland.  I\'ve just always wanted to go there.  \r\n<LI>4.  Back to Tokyo, but only if I knew that I had a whole lot of spending money.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nI think one thing that most Swinnites have in common is that you\'ve all traveled lots, or at least want to.  What am I missing on my list? '),(1045,'<p>Grayson Angel graduated with a BA in English from a California college and promptly moved away from the state.  He wears a fedora. Undecided on an ultimate destination he drove across the country in a Piece Arrow with a suitcase full of three piece suits, a handgun and fourteen bottle of Old Granddad.   He didnâ€™t stay long in any city, stopping only long enough to give out dollar cigars to the prostitutes.  \r\n\r\n<p>Something, heâ€™s not sure what, brought him to Boston.  Perhaps a propensity for the fire-and-brimstone sermons of Puritan preacher, Jonathan Edwards (1703-1758).  Since settling in Beantown, heâ€™s worked for an attendant in a copy shop, a short order cook, a gravedigger, a pruner of trees and an eater of prunes.  Heâ€™s been an office manager at a architecture firm and a legal assistant for an insurance firm.  Heâ€™s been unemployed, down-on-his luck and has gambled in the streets for pocket change.  Heâ€™s drunk cheap beer at cheaper bars and eaten bad food with greasy spoons.  Heâ€™s played cards outside during blizzards wearing a only a Hawaiian shirt with parrots on it.\r\n\r\n<p>Somewhere along the line he learned how to play the ukulele and now sings and plays Hoagie Carmichael tunes on the platforms of Bostonâ€™s subway stations which produces enough money to subsist on a diet of saltine crackers and American cheese, both of which he shares with the rats in his coldwater flat.  The one window in his apartment is cracked which lets in a bitter chill in the winter.  His furniture is made from toilet paper rolls, Popsicle sticks and duct tape.  He does not have a girlfriend and has never been married.\r\n\r\n<p>Grayson also writes, when he finds the time, penning stories that have no plot and very little character and are vague and symbolic at best.  While they have not yet achieved much acceptance in the literary community, Grayson continues to try.  He also writes poetry, most of which is bad and dispensable, but he will not be dissuaded by critics, assuming that his poetry prompts his fiction which in turn prompts more poetry.\r\n\r\n<p>Today he is sick to his stomach, having consumed too much Metamucil and vegetarian hot dogs.  He likes sauerkraut but doesnâ€™t have any on hand.   He sometimes wishes that a nice young woman would come up to his room and dust. \r\n'),(1046,'<p>it\'s 6 fuckin 30 a.m.\r\nthe sun\'s out and shining up my tired ass\r\ni go online to read the news\r\nyou know\r\nget a jump on the fresh new, bright fuckin virgin day,</p>\r\n<p>oh but wait, \r\nwhat\'s this?\r\nswinney.ORG...online journal you say?</p>\r\n<p>oh oh oh, it\'s so hip and cool, and who\'s this swinney fellow?</p>\r\n<p>jee wiz, he seems soooo cool and</p>\r\n<p>mellow</p>\r\n<p>can i touch you, are you golden like that sun shining up my sagging tired ass?</p>\r\n<p>what\'s that guy?\r\nwho the fuck am i?</p>\r\n<p>i am lulu</p>\r\n<p>you\'re worst dreams come true</p>\r\n<p>who?</p>\r\n<p>lulu</p>'),(1047,' <P> I think i\'m in need of a midget. i\'m not sure why but i do have the desire to have one around the house having one at work also would be alot of fun. <P>'),(1048,'<p>I\'m sitting here in my office, peacefully contemplating mountains of java middleware code, and there\'s a slight quacking outside.  I shake my head a little bit - I had a nice slice of turkey for lunch and I\'m wondering if I\'ve become somehow differently attuned to tryptophan.  But my coworker hears it too, so I stand up and peer down at the ground outside my window where there is, quite literally, a mother mallard duck being followed by nine little tiny baby ducks.  I nearly pass out from the raw cuteness of it.  Stabilizing myself, we walk outside and around the corner just as the lot of them are walking that direction.  They walk towards and then calmly past us, slowly into the courtyard.  Cute barefoot girls walking to class stop and watch, too.  They slowly pace the courtyard (the ducks, not the cute girls) and we go back inside.  There\'s water not too far (although certainly far enough that seeing ducks seems odd) in pretty much any direction they walk from here, so I think they\'ll be ok.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nApparently mama ducks\r\n<a href=\"http://animaldiversity.ummz.umich.edu/accounts/anas/a._platyrhynchos$narrative.html\">\r\nquack</a> when they want to bring other ducks closer to them.  I know this feeling.  Quack, quack.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m distinctly aware of being at a different latitude here, somehow.  Looking at the nice National Geographic map on the wall outside my office, the Tropic of Cancer (where the sun would be right overhead during the summer, if I\'m remembering all this correctly) lays far to the south.  It\'s at 23 degrees N or so, through what\'s left of California and all the way down at the tip of the Baja peninsula.  Santa Barbara is at about 34 degrees N, San Francisco is only about 38. (I was guessing, but topozone.com says I\'m about right on all of these)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo the sun should be easily 10 someodd degrees off the zenith, but for some reason it doesn\'t feel like it.  On the other hand, from steering small boats at night by compass heading and trying to get a feel for what small increments of being off course felt like, ten degrees is just one thirty-sixth of the arc.  It doesn\'t feel like much.  It only matters if you\'re moving, if you\'re multiplying that part of the arc out by miles of distance.</p>\r\n<p>Which I suppose I am, both metaphorically and literally.  The sun beats down here but the sea breeze keeps it cool and the combination seems to make everyone smiley and mostly mellow, even when the DNS servers have crashed for no reason.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\'s more of course, but work beckons.\r\n</p>'),(1049,'<p>\r\nHello. My name is Simon Forbes Keough. I am from San Francisco. sometimes I live other places. I take pictures of stuff<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1049_1.jpg\'>\r\n<br>\r\nand also, things.<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1049_2.jpg\'>\r\n<br>\r\nI write about this or that as well and sometimes, <a href=\"http://www.zingmagazine.com/zing11/reviews/guests.html\">people</a> print them. Yay, the fun never stops, let me tell you.\r\n</p>'),(1050,'i painted the bathroom today.  it\'s now offensively bright and cheery.  it will either bring joy to your heart or incite violence.  both are probably good things to feel while in\r\nthe bathroom.  it was a dismally drab grey and white.  landlord non-color.  one of those things where in the attempt to offend no one they offend everyone.  like elevator music.  <br><br>\r\nwhile painting i stood on a rickety step ladder.  i\'ve known this ladder for a long time.  it\'s splattered with all manner of paint and goo.  on the side of it are two stripes, one purple and one green.  these are the stripes i left when i painted my bedroom 10 years ago.  somehow the ladder got from santa cruz to san francisco.  i\'m not sure how.  seeing those stripes reminded me of the happiness that bedroom brought me.  every wall was a different color.  red.  yellow.  blue.  green. purple with a huge green spiral.  i didn\'t ask my mom if i could paint the room but i don\'t remember her ever saying anything about it.  everyone seemed to agree that it was the right thing to do.  the room was like a jungle of the bizzare.  you had to push aside magnetic tape that hung from the ceiling to get through.  anything that could be stuck to the wall would be.  feathers were painted into the red wall.  the green wall was covered almost entirely in plastic grapes.  the yellow wall had half-painted egg crate foam covering a portion of it.  i considered it a work-in-progress installation at all times.  it was home.  so many things happened in that room.  so much growth, emotion, terror, joy, creation, love, hate, everything.<br><br>\r\nwhen i went away to college i dismantled the room.  it was done.  five years in the making and it was done. the walls of that room are white now (a shocking discovery when i went home over the first winter break).  many of the items that lived there are still in boxes that travel around with me.  i take them out every once in a while and wonder why they still exist.  some of them are gone, intentionally  thrown away.  the plastic grapes were all left in a bag outside my apartment on church and market.  it took about 10 minutes for them to be snatched up.  perhaps they are hanging on someone\'s wall now. <br><br>\r\ni keep thinking about the purge -- a final farewell to items of the past.  something keeps stopping me though.  i wish i had control over it but some deep seated part of me still wants to be in that room.'),(1051,'<p> I guess this doesn\'t relate to the blurb.<br> well, kinda<br> I broke up with my girl. It wasnt working, and now I feel like the arsehole ex boyfriend who\'s dumped the girl right in the middle of everything.<br> But, then this is my life. I should live it how I want to .<br> right?</p>\r\n<p> I dont mean to sound angsty. im just sick of everything blowing up in my face.<br> i\'d like for things to work out my way for a change.<br> I remember Olivia! oxy.gen.nz/olivia used to be the coolest. I really miss that journal. <br> checking it every day used to be a ritual. <br> there was a time when she even used to live sorta close to me, same country type thing. and that was pretty cool.<br> Its amazing. there\'s so many awesome, talented people.<br> Im in awe of how incredible everyone is. <br> i feel like crying and smiling and laughing about everything and nothing all at once.<br> </p>\r\n<p> This isn\'t my normal type of journal entry. its not even structured anymore, its just rambling bollocks.<br> I guess its important to get it out, or something. <br> I never meant to hurt her, and I know that this has hurt her more than anything i\'ve ever done before, and that makes me cry.<br> i couldn\'t keep on going the way I was. i needed to tell her, and let her know what i was thinking.<br> Maybe it was for the best, that things went this way, it wasn\'t meant to be.<br> </p>\r\n<p> She told me that i should be happy. she was upset because i wasn\'t happy, and i knew that she wouldn\'t make me happy, only I can make me happy.<br> she told me to get back on medication, to make myself happy, but im more than that now.<br> I dont need to take a pill to make myself feel better. <br> Im going to be okay, this is just another stupid phase thats going on.<br> </p>\r\n<p> I guess it was always going to happen. I just sped things along.<br> We\'re both better off this way.<br> I guess.<br> </p>\r\n<p> <br> </p>\r\n<p> sometimes i just go sit in the park, on my own and look at the sky and the clouds, and i feel so content with the world and everything in my life.<br> I have these recuring moments of COMPLETE clarity.<br> are they the times when i\'m actually happy, or is that just when im manic-happy?<br> </p>\r\n<p> i think its going to be okay</p>'),(1052,'-{to herf}-> i quit smoking today<br>\r\n<*herf*> oh my! yeah you go!<br>\r\n-{to herf}-> i go to hell<br>\r\n<*herf*> cool, i\'ll meet ya there<br>\r\n-{to herf}-> soon<br>\r\n<*herf*> not that soon, you quit smoking, remember? :)<br>\r\n<p>\r\nhere is a rhyme that i tried to start last week on day one:\r\n<p>\r\nI never came up with a plan. and here I am.<br> \r\nI wish I could get a tan.<br>\r\nit was the \"fun now, pay later\" plan. <br>\r\nI thought of acupuncture, or Xioban.<br>\r\n<p>\r\num, thats as far as it went.\r\n<p>\r\nI need some beef jerky [- what a beefy hip!]. I need a lollipop. i ate a meatball sandwich. that was the wrong thing to do. I remmebered when I quit smokin g sophomore year at Mills and ellen and I made a video called \"lollipop\" together in which we filmed each other merely putting things into our mouths. candy, toys, strips of RAM, boogers, big floppy donkey dicks, you name it, we shoved it in there. \r\n<p>\r\nIs it really that my mother didn\'t breast feed me? Breast-feeding is back in, and I wonder what the socio-political implications are. I sort of miss the \'70s. Depravity was a character asset. I was not old enough to take advantage of that.\r\n<p>\r\nQuitting smoking is like leaving a lover who you know is bad for you, but who you really like to put your mouth on. Shrinks say that when ending a relationship you have to suffer the Five Stages of Grief associated with coming to terms with death. Denial, Anger, Bargaining, Depression, Acceptance. I think I am in Bargaining right now.\r\n<p>\r\nThis is a guide for bright and creative young individuals who are too spiritually advanced to use drugs, gum, the patch, or hypnosis to quit smoking and transcend the nipple once and for all. I have done a few of these things, and so far I have not smoked in 7 days.\r\n<p>\r\n-<b>listening to either grotesquely morose or maliciously mean-spirited music</b> will satisfy the forked tongue of a booby-starved oral fixator. My two recommendations are <b>Cat Power/Moon Pix</b> for the former, and the local <b>Roofies/Blame it On the Roofies</b> for the latter. sample this morsel from their song \"love darts\":\r\n<p>\r\nId like to take your penis<br>\r\nand dip it in a vat of acid<br>\r\nit would shrivel up and fall off<br>\r\nand youd be left with NOTHING, except your useless testacles!\r\n<p>\r\nit\'s so satisfying, righteous indignation.\r\n<p>\r\n-if yr one of those political types who eats piles of tofu and marches on washington, then <b>supposedly reading a lot of adbusters or renting the major motion picutre, \"the insider\" feeds your delusion that you are an individual who can exert personal will over the Patriarchy/Big Brother/Man/etc. and you will be motivated to keep from sucking those Big Brother Dick sticks.</b> I watched \"the insider\" and it was really boring. I prefer movies about hermaphrodites having an affair, or about a boy with no nose, something with a little pizzazz, yknow?\r\n<p>\r\n-<b>engage in an affair</b> with somoene who is an unrealistic choice for a real relationship, ie who is emotionally unavailable/retarded, someone who will move far away, whose astro chart doesnt match yours, whose sexual orientation swings away from your type, who is on the rebound, or all of the above. you can participate in high drama and irrationality to an infinite degree and in the end, always blame it on the fact that you quit smoking. you can get away with infidelity, fickleness, bitchery, indecisiveness, you name it. but the person should have big lips.\r\n<p>\r\n-dare people to <b>do stupid shit</b> with you. some ideas are: go roof-hopping, shoplift alcohol, find out the numbers of convicted sex offenders and prank call them, ride a motorcycle,  take a pile of drugs whose names you dont know, let some guy from sales at your company snort coke off your tits, go walking around by yourself in barren city areas yelling about the work of art in the mechanical age of reproduction, fall in love.\r\n<p>\r\n-<b>just yell out arbitrarily and wave your arms in the air and hit yourself in the head</b>. it feels good. noone\'s going to fuck with you.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n-write a \"Dear John, I\'m Over You\" letter to cigarrettes. like so:\r\n<p>\r\ndear smoky treats,\r\n<p>\r\nI get along without you very well, of course I do.\r\n<p>\r\nI never loved you. I never needed you. I dont need anyone. Im a one man band. every man for himself.\r\n<p>\r\nIve got friends. ive got lovers. people like me, they think im cool. they wanna kiss me. they wanna touch my girl parts. I dont need you!\r\n<p>\r\nIm an artist, a free-thinker, an individual, a free-wheeler. I warned you that some day it would all come to an end. goodbye.\r\n<p>\r\negg.'),(1053,'<p>\r\ni remember sly smiles and red fogged evenings on the couch.  that rarest of touches able to pull me even from the simpsons or a firearm.  laughing children and running roughshod over the rules of slapjack.  Hi-C and dirty dishes while being groped.  arm in arm in the night, each with a pistol and a silly grin.  sweat and blood, scent of cordite and exhuastion; shower first or after?  after...always after she said.  arguing across the room and sitting together so tight, fitting good.  slow pulse cuddling while rugrats plays and the rugrats play.  knowing my woman can beat even me four out of ten falls, and fearing anyway.  head in her lap, her against a gravestone and laughing about our love.  \"you don\'t know how to subtract!?\"  being called a puddle of skinhead \'cause no force can tear me away from her.  shooting practice side by side, and red knives against our throats.  her first real strike, and painfully, her first real strike against me.  getting her to read Friday.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nback to back against the world, not much mattered then.  just that stupid love and damn the consequences.  double whopper night and art class, \"draw this picture from memory.\"  being called pumpkin and hating it, but not wanting her to stop.  melty guts and burning arms, praying for her to show up, her showing up and missing her before parting.  The exploited and crass painting the air with scathing sounds, while we lay entwined.  twenty first kisses and a lifetime of bitching.  ripped sweaters and collapsible batons and those damn corduroys.  arguing in the shower and having to shower again when we get into a spitting match, Tom watching us naked, rolling around, bugs out and goes to bed.  \"i think we hurt him.\"  kissing children goodnight and meeting in the hall, slow plodding hugs, and carrying her down the stairs.  exploring the soft skin scars her body had to offer, letting her in turn explore the calloused wasted body of scars etched into me.  probing weaknesses with a tender word and a feather stroke of the hand.  tapping her pulse out on her hip as she slept.  bleary eyed goodmornings and struggles to stay in bed.  dancing around the fire, her in a skirt and i in a kilt; dancing slowly in the dark to unchained melody.  watching her cigarette flare in the dark, feeling happy when it briefly illuminated her face.  watching her chest rise and fall, breathing in sleep, by the moonlight which made it past the curtains.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfeeling the life we\'d created grow in her belly, and knowing we\'d do alright.  agreeing on names easily, and arguing rules that were never needed.  saving her life one night, only to lose our child.  starting smoking again while she lay in some hospital bed, my friends crowded around me but not feeling.  crying slowly for so long...  and her tears against my shoulder; \"was it kaine or rebekah?  we\'ll never know.\"  helpless, being able to say nothing, do nothing.  just mourn, letting her crying become ours.  knowing the next day our real family would be there for us.  prayers said for us by members of faiths we did not believe in.  firing out rifles for hours but not enjoying it, going back and not making love as we had before.  just sitting on the couch while clannad played their bittersweet celtic sounds.  the fights that came later, and finally the recovery; trying again.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe struggle, me for gentle lovemaking, and her for forceful.  finding that middle ground and laughing hard enough to stop it all anyway.  the struggle for survival, sometimes to find shelter or food; knowing by her pages that she was crying for me.  666.  i\'m sad and scared you will die.  happier times, 666666666:  i\'m happy and can\'t wait to see you.  her watching as i read for hours.  nights spend under garbage in a dumpster, but happy knowing she\'d be with me come morning.  days away from her, spent with my last two friends; dawn teaching me to drive.  being so scared i\'d wreck her car, and then almost wrecking it.  eating pasta with rob, and remembering what true friendship is like.  geek talk and jungle music.  wishing she could be there, but knowing it would all work out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nleaving the land of my birth, and kissing her goodbye.  soon enough we\'ll be together sara, i believed.  losing her because i\'d learned silence too early and never told her...  and the struggle i still fight, to forget.\r\n</p>'),(1054,'<p>\r\nwhat happens when someone you thought was a friend reveals that they are at heart, too shallow and afraid to take responsibilty for their actions? when they don\'t realize that they\'re even worse than an enemy, they are a false friend. someone who shirks all responsibility for their side of the relationship, because after all, they expect you to be pissed off when you realize that they don\'t give two shits about you. understandably.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwell, if you\'re intelligent, you let the relationship die. because someone who tries to convince you that the only reason they were friends with you in the first place was because of your own needs and weaknesses, doesn\'t deserve your friendship. someone who absolves themselves of all responsibility because they are afraid of rejection, will make sure that they get that rejection because it supports their theory that it\'s the other person\'s fault. it\'s so sad. and senseless.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhat happened, weeb? that made you think this way? well, a new friend (not close, but one that had possibilities) just stopped calling, /m\'ing or talking to me. and then when i tried to initiate contact, they accused me of being needy and demanding. wow. this shocked me, especially since they had been the one initiating get-togethers, leaving voicemails, and seemed to enjoy my company. but i guess i learned my lesson. beware the people who instantly want to become best friends with you. who have a history of saying dissociative things like, \"i never feel responsible for any of my relationships.\" because they\'re cowards, and cowards don\'t have friends. they have victims. i guess s/he\'s good at making new friends because he has to be.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwatch out. you could be next. or has it happened to you already?</p>'),(1055,'<p>i drove over the bay bridge tonight at sunset and felt the tires rumble over the last stretch between memories and home. an unfamiliar tan glances back in the rear view mirror. unfinished construction languishes over the skyline, and i smile to myself. 3165.1 miles and nothing has changed a bit.</p>\r\n<p>i woke up this morning at 4:30am, still sleep deprived, next to two of my closest friends, on desert ground below snow capped peaks. the air was electric. and nothing could stop them, not even the sunrise. 104 moons; the extended family expands and swirls, spinning around in hips-to-neck orbit.</p>\r\n<p>now i am searching for clumps of algae in unexpected places. i am home but still so far away.</p>\r\n<p>all the lines are blurred now.</p>'),(1056,'here i sit, once again on the sf muni, this time by choice. I\'m headed to the first day of paid employment that i\'ve had in over 9 months. Yay employment. as a consolation to anyone who is unemployed, the job will most likely be boring as hell. on the flipside, it is in sf (very convenient muniwise- the 6 bus stops within 2 blocks of my house&job), and they are paying me more $ than i\'ve ever made before.\r\n<P>\r\nSo i notice people have been filling up the bus from the front. I\'m the freak who got on all the way back here, i figured since i was riding all the way to the terminus that the back made sense (and the bus was empty when i got on, so people-phobia can be discounted.) and wow, apparently all the missed connections got on at the same stop (really i was starting to wonder).  So many people on the bus now, i must only imagine at their wonderfulness, for surely they must be wonderful people. many seem employed as they call places on their cellphones to say theyll be late.  Oops apparently the bus just became unplugged! Quickly fixed though.\r\n<P>\r\nWell i still seem to have this motion sickness as i write these observations.  But it must be overcome. I\'m sick of not being able to read and write on moving vehicles.\r\n<P>\r\n----\r\n<P>\r\nafternoon:\r\n<P>\r\nwell work didn\'t last all day, they don\'t have my laptop yet. Still i paid a third of my rent today (dependinq on how bad the taxes are). I tried to call weeb to see if she wanted to hang out, but alas, her cell is off.  Tried sending an e-mail from my phone.  I guess i need e-mail to icb gateway.<BR>\r\nAlong with 80000 other things i should do.\r\n<P>\r\n\"hey that\'s illegal motherfucker!\" yells a passenger to someone on the street.  Of course, i was too busy writing to actually see the infraction in question.\r\n<P>\r\neww. Gross. A couple, smelling of cigarette smoke just sat in front of me. But the she may be a fellow journaller as she has a steno pad, and a pen and is writing... They find a pair of old bell bottoms someone left on the bus, claim them, and get off.\r\n<P>\r\nyay sunny day! Yay muni!  Yay swinney.org!  Up the hill up the hill...\r\n<P>\r\nsoon i will have a laptop from work, which will open up many more commute time activity possibilities.<P>\r\n-----------<BR>\r\n(i think the slowness of the palm really affects my true stream of consciousness.  we\'ll see with the laptop.)'),(1057,'<p>\r\nThis last weekend was the first in approximately four years that I didn\'t spend some significant amount of time interacting in person with some kind of a loved one, or a good friend, or someone who turned into a good friend.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe not the first in that whole time, but one of a really awfully small number.  Less than half a dozen, probably.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m wondering if the past four relatively happy but so ephemeral seeming years are going to look like an abberation when I look back later.  They were definitely an abberation compared to the first 23 or so.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nOK no more analysis beyond that, I\'m just in a crappy mood.  Upheavels on top of upheavels.\r\n</p>'),(1058,'<p>i want to go to los angeles...go to the beaches and jack off with a sandy sea cucumber</p>\r\n<p>i don\'t care if you don\'t want to hear that cause i can write anything i want on this oh so cooooll website that mr. coolio himself, swinney shmwinney, set up....ooooooh you\'re so godly. i and the rest of the pathetic world of literatis and \"contemporary artists\" worship you like a gold plated indie rock star.</p>\r\n<p>oooohhh do i upset you, quinn? jo? fred? george? steve? ooooohh do i rub you the wrong way?  that\'s ok, cause you know...you can write about it right here on the glorious SWINNEY.ORG!</p>\r\n<p>you can even say \"retard\"</p>\r\n<p>oooohh i know what you\'re thinking. you\'re thinking i\'m just a poor pathetic, ignorant, jerk off with nothing better to do than to write crap on some website that glorifies shit for poetry.</p>\r\n<p>maybe you should write about it</p>\r\n<p>get it off your chest.  i did, and boy does it feel remarkable.  just watch; example:  \"OOOOHHHH, you\'re sooo special, look at you, you\'re in with the hip crowd, ooooohhh, you\'re soooo awesome and radical cause you listen to indie punk, oh god, you go shopping for clothes at thrift stores and wear vintage clothing, wow, you must be REAL hip.\"</p>\r\n<p>everybody now......</p>\r\n '),(1059,'<p>In was early morning and the heat was already unbearable.  the sun was comming up over the distant hills tht lay across the horizon lik pale, limp bodies.  He sat in the lounge chair on the balcony looking out over the vast expanse of desert smoking cigarettes  and drinking from a tumbler of whisky.  Lorna was still in bed.  He hadn\'t even gone to sleep that night.  He was tired but not sleepy.  There was something pure about his wakefulness, something pure and perfect as if the whiskey had burned away all his faults.  <i>Let the people sleep</i>,  he thought, pulling at the whiskey.  <i>Let the people sleep while I rise above them</i>.  He flicked the cigarette and the ashes floated out over the desert.  If he  was perfectly still and silent for he could hear Lorna\'s ragged breathing in the room behind him.</p>'),(1060,'<P>So, I just took my lunch and walked down to the edge (it probably used to be more of a \"bank\", but is definitely an edge now, what with the encroaching cement buttresses and such) of the East River.  For the first time, I noticed that it has no \"river smell\". If you\'ve grown up near any body of water, or spent alot of time on a river or ocean or shore, you know what it is I\'m talking about... \"river smell\"- it isn\'t bad, just river-y. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI suppose I noticed the same thing, several years ago, while boating on the Harlem River, and attributed this to a sealing oil-slick licking the surface.  I find it strange that, with three rivers and a major harbor in such close proximity, there\'s never a river/ocean/moisture/water smell in New York City.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOdd.</P>'),(1061,'me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me me '),(1062,'<PRE>\r\nI am mopping twice.\r\nNot ev\'ryone takes the time.\r\nSunny day, no job.\r\n</PRE>'),(1063,'<pre>\r\ni am in love with lulu\r\nher sharp tongue\r\nher wry wry \r\n\r\nwry\r\n\r\n... wit?\r\n\r\ni can only hope to be with her\r\n\r\noh lulu, i will say, as i take her hand in mine\r\nyou are a goddess\r\na zen witness\r\nyour observations make me squirm with desire\r\n\r\n\"are you a haiku\" lulu asks of us\r\nguru on the mountain lulu,\r\ni will suckle at your teat of cynical truth\r\n\r\ntake me\r\n</pre> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1064,'<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1064_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'><a href=\"http://www.muzak.com\">Muzak</a> is Everywhere; even their web-page says so.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Where to begin? You\'ve all heard Muzak before; in the shopping malls, when put on hold, perhaps even enrapturing the occasional cliche elevator. When I was much younger, a family member actually convinced me that there was a giant faceless machine that \"muzaked\" all the popular tunes; they\'d be played into it, the output being only a cloying mist of the original, set alight with flutes, mellow saxophone solos, soaring synth-strings and various atmospheric oohs and aahs.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Muzak has now undoubtedly gone ultra-hi-fi, and I was delighted to discover their very chic web-presence. Quite a far cry from their boxy, window-less headquarters, tangled with wires, antennae, and various broadcasting minutia. I used to ride past it daily in my childhood hometown commute, its facade marked only by non-descript lowercase maroon letters spelling out their name, their logo, all in a style that could only now be described as vintage-y retro.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I\'m now excitedly listening to Muzak, streaming gloriously yet non-obtrusively through the web. The \"Urban Beat\" program looks pretty enticing, but for the moment, I\'m regaling in \"Popular Contemporary Instrumentals\", the classic heart-and-soul of Muzak. Ahh, the People\'s Music.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1065,'<P>Why can\'t I come over?</P><BR><BR><BR><P>\r\nSlight headache. A few tears, some sake, desert mushroom hangover?</P>\r\n<P>I want to sleep in the cleft of your chin. I want to touch it, put my mouth to it, like labia. You seem to water my soul with serenity, soothing the searing pain in my guts. Old, dry, burning, expiring pain. Like the desert. It yields to your cooling touch, as the hot dust yields to a chill, crisp moon. The brightness of the moon snaps like the white insides of a fresh apple.</P>\r\n<P>I wander through rocks, scrub, cold scraping powdered old denim. I stamp my feet around rattlesnake holes, watching quartz flash like milk in the moonlight. I wander through, around, with hard beats bouncing off the canyon. I circle and watch. I hear you following me.</P>\r\n<P>I reach out to you, fifty thousand miles per hour, all my molecules on a collision course with uncertainty. They separate and breathe and pulse, wanting to crash into you, finding themselves to be not solid enough for crashing. Everything rushes in, the earth spins around me, the stars, the turntables, the feet on the earth. Everything stands still and moves blindingly fast at once. I breathe in sacred wind, the very breath of creation blowing unseen through the desert. Yh- inhale. Wh- exhale. The name of god.</P>\r\n<P>Naked, head wrapped in orange Chinese silk. I wear a coat of freckles through the marsh. Green spiraling shoots grow from the mud. I am followed through the salt marsh by redwinged blackbirds, swifts, and a beautiful screeching waterbird in a panic. I wants me to get out of its neighborhood. I try to step softly, crunching on the hard crystalline salted earth. Clink, my foot hits a pile of discarded beer bottles. Deep tire tracks scar the virgin marsh. A dead soda can floats in a bubbling spring. Assholes.</P>\r\n<P>I can\'t keep my eyes away. Something inside me gives way, is curious, fears nothing. I am free within my skin, unashamed. You are there, breathing on my neck, close enough to send warm electric current through my body. There is a lightness within that used to be coated in tar, black. Was it you throwing back the shutters? Was it the screaming insistent beauty all around me? Somewhere along the way, the lightness has won out. As I tiptoe over rocks, I hold onto you and you hold back, grounding me solidly in a new and real bliss.</P>'),(1066,'This movie, <a href=http://www.otnemem.com/>Memento</a>, is a mind fuck...\r\n<p>\r\nCan\'t believe I\'ve never heard of this movie before...  I just watched it.\r\nThis movie like totally goes along with some of the psychology shit I <a href=http://tender.lumplings.com/crap/ramblings/053001.html>talked</a>\r\nwith Laura about recently... \r\n<p>\r\nLenny\'s main things are that his last memory is his wife dying.\r\nAnd his only reason to life is to find her killer.\r\nAccording to some psychology theories, this is part of the <a href=http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/hstein/adler.htm>Adler</a> stuff\r\nI was talking with Laura about, everyone has a goal in life and usually it\'s related to the first thing they remember in their life\r\nand if the first thing you remember changes, then your goal in life changes...\r\nsupposedly...\r\n<p>\r\nThis movie is an interesting twist...\r\nwhere his goal is based on the last thing he remembered...\r\n<p>\r\nI had just been chatting with Cyan and telling her about when I had brain damage before watching the movie...\r\nIt was so trippy...\r\n<p>\r\nHim not being able to hold time reminded me of how much Slaughterhouse Five messed with my mind...\r\n<p>\r\nI love a good Mind Fuck...\r\n<p>\r\nI love mementos now, too...  I can handle them...\r\n<p>\r\nIt used to be that after a relationship, after someone was gone from my life, and I felt ripped into\r\na million pieces of useless oblivion, and that I was doomed to never get anything I wanted from this\r\nlife, I would dump all my mementos...  I burned journals, and pictures, and crud...\r\n<p>\r\nNow, I hold onto that stuff...  Perhaps it was the influence of going out with so many Virgos...\r\n<p>\r\nIt also made me think of Xep\'s potential purge...\r\n<p>\r\nI also thought about all the little pictures, and notes, and emails, and crap that comprise my little computer\r\nonline world...  a lot of us have that crud...  things to remind us who we are...  things we relate to our\r\nidentity...  we relate our identity to ourselves...  I am john doe, I am a plumber, I am a good soccer player,\r\nI eat cheese for breakfast, this is my ritual, this is my scene...  this is me...  We relate the loss of those\r\nthings to the loss of life... it puts us in survival mode...  I\'m talking like <a href=http://www.gerrold.com/>Solomon Short</a>...\r\n<p>\r\nYou are not what you own, or what you do, or who you fuck, or where you go, or how you talk, or where you live,\r\nor what you see...\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ve binged and purged many a time...  I have regretted it since then...  It felt important for my survival\r\nthen, however...  I would\'ve gone insane without letting go of the past...  I related the change to my survival,\r\nand thought there was no reason to live with my identity being so changed (from having been moved, or someone leaving my life)...  \r\nBut now I know it doesn\'t matter,\r\nso now I often regret not having the mementos...  I could have them without it making me want to die anymore...\r\nI\'ve tried to write a lot about past memories on my website, so I can have a place to go look and remember...\r\n<p>\r\nIt all adds to who we are now, but it is not who we are, and not who we will be...  but it\'s nice to remember\r\nhow you got here, and the bits that have added onto you...\r\n<p>\r\nPart of me is very jealous of Lenny...  But I get jealous of everyone and everything...  I can only ever\r\nsee reflections of myself, and see things that I can relate to, in this world...  I can never actually\r\nexperience what the other people in this world I know have experienced...  And that is easy to be jealous\r\nof...  I want to see the world through your eyes...  I\'ll show you mine, if you\'ll show me yours.\r\n<p>\r\nOh yes, when I logged onto my server today I got a fortune that made me think of Incitor:\r\n<p>\r\n\"I\'m prepared for all emergencies but totally unprepared for everyday life.\"\r\n<p>\r\nHope that doesn\'t offend you, Incitor...  just for some reason I thought of you instantly...\r\n<p>\r\nI love your writing, although it\'s twisted differently than mine is, I appreciate most things, as long as\r\nthey\'re at least a little twisted...   yours counts as very twisted...  that is a good thing...  ;)\r\n<center>\r\n<a href=http://tender.lumplings.com>\r\n<img border=0 src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/877_1.jpg\">\r\n</a>\r\n</center>'),(1067,'<p>i know that many of you think im\' dum and a werthliss peice of shit; and that i may be a waist of space.  i may not have a reel job or even a job for that matter and i may be a lay about who smokes to much ganja and drinks to much miller hi life, but i dont\' think i\'m disintelligent or a boar. maybe some of you guys are more smarter than me and understand the world a little beter than me; but that\'s the reeson why i wanted to be so much a part of your community.  i know my writing isen\'t awesome and deep like all you good folk\'s, you know; talking about life and the importent stuff in it like life and good stuff and bad stuff and happy things and sad things. i guess maybe i should reed some more books like you guys do and not be on chat all day talking shit to people.  well, anyways, i suppose this goes out to people like quinn-quinn, dude, i\'ve reely learned a lot from you and you\'ve opened my eyes. </p>\r\n<p>with love,<br>\r\nlulu</p>'),(1068,'FOOLED YOU!BWAHAHAHAHA! you thought i was gonna leave. you fuckin yahoos. i\'d rather waste my time ripping you mother fuckers a new asshole than go get fucked by uncle sam for twelve hours.  you pathetic yocals, yeah you...you on the palm pilot, why don\'t you take a break from playing dope wars and go get your double nonfat latte from some no name generic coffee shop down the street.  and you, yeah you on the cellie, why don\'t you get off the fuckin phone and get a tan. i can see you\'re veins from here you gray, pasty, sorry son of a motherless goat.  you\'re all sons of motherless goats and dim witted bimbos who have nothing better to do than to wallow in a pit of stale conversations about the effects of facism on the survivor show. go put on your black leather coats and slap those black plastic rimmed glasses on and go out for a joy ride in your volvos and jettas and paint the streets of your very coooooool city, san fransisco.....  '),(1069,'no'),(1070,'le said on june 9th, <a href=\"http://www.elly.org/passion/01/06/\">if i bought you a plastic parrot with a light inside it, would that be something you would like?</a><br><br>\r\n\r\na plastic parrot with a light inside conjures up images in my mind of a party on the beach at trader somebody\'s beach bar and the parrots are all faded and cracked by the sun and sand and wind and the smell of wet sand and sweaty bodies and tanning lotion and beer and cheap pizza is all around, all whipped together with salty spray of the ocean and we dance and laugh and drink and dance some more and then we head out under the boardwalk and do it with our clothes on because nice girls don\'t take them off and we suck on each other\'s necks until the proof of our passion is branded for all to see.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand one time i was young and careless and beau and bob and danny and i would spend all of our days and nights near the water dancing and fishing and laughing and oh!<br><br>\r\n\r\nyes, i know how you feel, you don\'t have to find words.  those who have lived know the words you mean.  those who pretend to live will always be confused.<br><br>\r\n'),(1071,'<p>\"I bet you didn\'t expect a call from an Igor, now did you, hmm, or something, yes, yes.\" I love it when Igor calls. He\'s the silliest excuse for a person I\'ve ever met in my life. Something straight out of a Mercer Mayer children\'s book. Except not so hairy and hamster-like. I suppose.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIgor lets me talk. He loves to lend an ear. At 6\'8\", 38 years old and bonafide Dutch, he\'s got some big ears to lend. I met him two years ago when he worked at a headshop downtown. He had long straggly hair and a skin condition, and loved to read my Tarot. He\'d always let me ramble, and he\'d even pretend to make sense of it all.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHe calls me tonight for the first time in a year and a half. He\'s now the systems engineer at the pseudo-Ivy League up in Rensselaer, and wants to suggest a studio assistant position to me, because he knows it\'d be perfect, or something, yes. He says it just like that. He\'s just an Igor, trying to help. Yes.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI drive to the ghetto to see his new house. His hair is cut short now, and he almost looks professional. Big ears under less hair. He tells me he\'s buying a VW Golf next week. I sit, light a cigarette, and being talking about circles.\r\n</p><p>\r\nCircles that prevent me from moving forward or backward. My biggest fear being that I don\'t know how to break out. Circles containing people and lovers from years past, who reappear, systematically and sequentially, one by one, out of the blue and so forth until I\'ve made my trek around the circle again. Sometimes I let new people in; and the cycle begins again, only this time a little bit larger. The circle includes my home, my family, my friends, my jobs. Occasionally, like a valence electron, I drop off some stragglers and move to the next energy level - this is when I\'ll allow the maximum volume of new people into my life. But for the most part, my life is in constant revolution around some hidden unspeakable nucleus that I can\'t destroy.  Igor is another notch in the circle, and as I mumble on, I watch him grow tired. One more voyage completed.</p>'),(1072,'<pre>\r\ninfatuation.\r\nit shouldn\'t really matter\r\nwell, obviously.\r\n\r\n\r\nit dosen\'t matter\r\nthere shouldn\'t be surprises\r\nthere\'s no suprises\r\n</pre>'),(1073,'<p>My friend and I accidently rammed a house. He was turning around in their parking lot and the front of the car rammed into the porch. My friend has no depth perception. It\'s a medical thing. He freaked and left before anyone came.</p>\r\n<p>He was really upset.</p>\r\n<p>But I was like: Dood. You rammed a HOUSE.</p>\r\n<p>Dream: I had a dream that I was in some kind of underground classroom. Myself and several others were being held captive by secretive creatures that live underground. They wear dark cloaks and their heads look like horse-skulls.</p>\r\n<p>I am trying to prove something to them.</p>\r\n<p>I think I am trying to betray the rest of my group and cut some sort of deal with them.</p>\r\n<p>I once had a dream about aliens invading, and this is what I did too. I betrayed humanity in exchange for securing passage off Earth.</p>\r\n<p>All in a day\'s work.</p>\r\n'),(1074,'preacher curl, tricep press<br>\r\nlove won\'t matter any less<br>\r\nincline fly, upright row<br>\r\ngive me abs and veins that show<br>\r\nfront lunge, door squat, lateral raise<br>\r\nI\'ve been waiting days and days<br>\r\npump me up, spirit me through<br>\r\nthere\'s nothing I want more than you<br>\r\ndecline press, hypertrophy<br>\r\nat home undressed, just look at me<br>\r\ntorso twist, hammer curl<br>\r\nbring some mercy to my world<br>\r\nno ripped mass, I\'ve plateaued<br>\r\nI never said that I was owed<br>\r\ndeadlift, leg lift, feel the burn<br>\r\nmy heart still has a lot to learn<br>\r\n'),(1075,'Subject : heartbreak hill<br>\r\n----- Message Text -----<br>\r\n<p>\r\ndearest mala kallisti:\r\n<p>\r\nmy legs are sore. jogged down and up the hill at Joanne\'s, in a fit of\r\nanxiety Monday morning, and it seemed sppropriate that I survey the hills\r\nwhere they filmed MASH on memorial day. downhill with sunny golden view first, sweating, sticky uphill last. fun now, pay later.\r\n<p>\r\nlindsey tried to get me to join cross country in like 10th grade. she took\r\nme on exactly ONE single trial run through the bird sanctuary up and down\r\nwhat those freaks called Heartbreak Hill, and you can imagine how many\r\ncigarrettes I smoked after that. \r\n<p>\r\nso i quit smoking today, broke up with ben two days ago. ive been eating\r\nmy hand all morning, and I have a new friend who makes me think about you.\r\nhe wanted to know how to make a mobius strip, and when he figured out he\r\ndidnt know what the big deal was.\r\n<p>\r\nI was trying to explain that when you run your finger against the surface, your finger could touch that surface for eternity. as if your legs could slide over the surface of a sphere and return to the same spot again and again, without jumping or lifting. a moonwalk. \r\n<p>\r\nlindsey kept telling me, \"dont break, when youre going downhill, dont break.\" my instinct was to grip the side of the earth with my shins, because gravity was hurtling my body towards the unknown. california is such a hillfest. I cant run anywhere near my house without hitting a hill after five minutes. so every time i want to bound through the roses I have to remember this lesson in efficient energy espenditure. dont break. let gravity propel you forward, let your muscles rest in inertia.\r\n<p>\r\nit so happens that my runs begin with uphill, as in the run that began at joanne\'s house. labor always comes last somehow. that could be the secret to hell. especially because the side of the road that takes you up the hill to joanne;s house puts you in direct confrontation with the motor garden machinery of mid-morning Republican lawns. you cant deny that roses smell sweetly, but these gardens are manicured like Porn Pussy. the compulsive attempt to make eveyrthing, including nature, look like its made out of fuckin toothpaste, minty white and flesh-free. this fuckin reminded me of something I had to read in art history class about figure painting, and about the shit i get into with these goddamn geek boys.\r\n<p>\r\nin 16th century florence the practice of paintinig, the pleasure of color as it expressed the sculptural qualities of light was continually held in check by the need for monochromatic forms to dictate the body of the composition. in other words, line and shade definitions of a nude figure was always more important than expressing life through the flush of pink flesh shadow that reminded you how a lover\'s lips looked at dusk while you were drifting to sleep. one mathetmatic, one poetic- form to color, structure to decoration. I never could seperate them. \r\n<p>\r\nshit was a catholic institution, after all.\r\n<p>\r\nthe expression of color was equated with the expression of pleasure, of blood rushing through the skin during sticky summer sweaty sex, of the inside of the mouth when it waters for fruit, of the blue chords that hold breasts in place under skin. in terms of the figure, color was an expression, in its barest identity, of blood. \r\n<p>\r\nI have trouble remembering to keep my blood flowing within the confines of line definition. I want all of me to seep through the membranes and merge with you and you and him and her. YOU. ME. NOW. yes. it is frustrating because i find myself getting excited from one minute to the next by the closest thing that keeps me warm. I talk and talk about one boy and then without much hiatus transition smoothly into heavy enthusiasm for another, all the while Jay shaking his head, imploring me, \"dont go from one to the next, WOMAN.\" but i Like him, hes good to go, why fight it? dont break. dont break. let yourself coast the hill, gravity will keep you on earth.\r\n<p>\r\n[and blood yeah, and color yeah, is the shape of \"woman\" in the humanist imagination. the flesh, woman, muse, bearer of emotion and breath.]\r\n<p>\r\nI worry sometimes that because of my earthless origin I will continually be drawn to the ones who can place all manner of experience on a sharp blue grid, play their love games inside the movie \"tron\" and move on when the quarters run out. that I need that groundedness to complete me. but that Ill never be satisfied.\r\n<p>\r\nand meanwhile i run inside that grid because i want to survive. becayse those are the rules. that grid dictates that there is one way to move forward and one way to win. and i know how to win. dont look back, and dont languor in your own body.\r\n<p>\r\n<a name=\"apple\">but then hunger creeps in and takes me over. and forget egg, forget you, lusty Applicious, there is only a Roman feast that wants to make itself through my body to its dessert and digestion. witness: a Roman banquet progresses <i>ab ovo usque mala</i>- \"from eggs to apples\" - beginning with the symbol of creation and ending with symbol of completion.\r\n<p>\r\nyou and i stay away from each other because we fear the pleasure of color. its all late nights of red wine and not enough clear planning or executed projects. downhill in the sun without breaking first, heating up the brimstone of labor in debt to the pleasure of color only as an afterthuoght, the way a hangover is an afterthought.\r\n<p>\r\nfuckin long email eh. I prefer the mobius strip, the way it connects creation to completion- when you run on it, there is no rose garden that you reach for, and no point of accomplishment. your legs just meet the earth without a rest, dessert and dinner intextricably intertwined, math incapable of wrestling free from art, body without boundary.\r\n<p>\r\nI remember going to see Richie Hotten at the 101 Minna with s5 and ellen, Labor Day Weekend a few years ago. the place was packed as you might guess, and the whole room was a sweathouse. I wanted too fuckin bad to DANCE though, that sounds was so right. so for one night only i allied myself with the long blond hippies, let me overheated body move in circles, let those circles move, and let the sweat seep into the carpeting of the gallery. it felt at first more of a workout than a pleasure, every cell of me crying in exasperated hot sobs, but every endorphin in my brain available like candy. I remember distinctly thinking, to the one I loved, \"aww baby, labor is HEAT.\"\r\n<p>\r\nLabor can be a kind of pleasure, my proteinous Apple, my wise and lusty temptress. Labor. can be an aggressive sort of leisure.\r\n<p>\r\ndont forget about me. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1076,'<p>I surrender my faded voucher, a stub stamped in violet, 6:50 am. The clerk reluctantly torquing the key, hefting the wheel, growling the engine up the ramp to where I stand, I see the car, covered with dust, appear; tire and brake-shoe dust, city dust, headlights dimming as he accellerates, cement squeaky under the matte tire-treads. We swap places, and from behind the wheel I see handprints on the outside of my windshield. I\'m waddling through the delivery trucks, double-parkers on the cross-streets, service vans and carpools, piloting my rusty sedan towards 5th Avenue, southbound. The spedometer needle waffles as I slowly accellerate to speed, and its a surprisingly smooth ride, nay a few pot-holes, bumps, pits and pedestrians; scattered gravel, barricades saying Police Line, do not cross. I dodge a biker, a courier, horn feebly balking like an aged sheep, a city bus, and scores of taxi-cabs, glaring by in yellow as if an electrical sunset had choked the congested intersections and crosswalks. Horns. An acapella aurora of sorts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Pedestrians step from the curb, inch into the roads edge where people curb their pets, further into the face of traffic, holding a hand aloft, gazing uptown. Every gridded block, every corner, they\'re waving to me, anticipating, holding their hands high as I pass, hundreds of hopeful, beackoning people looking at the cool blue of my vehicle as I swerve by. They see me pass. They hear the music slip out of my radio, they hear my clutch whir and my hand crank another gear around, they see my tires slip over the man-hole covers, and they hold their hands high, higher towards me.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey\'re hailing cabs. </p>\r\n'),(1077,'I just about had a heart attack, trying to figure out who it was that I dreamt about last night. <P>I dreamt that I was on Haight Street in some weird little market. It was very hot and stuffy. There were people in a cult, walking around, all wearing brown cord\'s and chanting things about Jesus. Then I saw a girl meditating, naked. I picked up a piece of pottery and it crumbled into pierces in my hand. Someone touched me on the shoulder and I looked back. It was Xep, trying to help me fix the broken pottery.<P>Why was I dreaming about you Xep? I don\'t even know you.. <P>Weirdness...'),(1078,'I just about had a heart attack, trying to figure out who it was that I dreamt about last night. <P>I dreamt that I was on Haight Street in some weird little market. It was very hot and stuffy. There were people in a cult, walking around, all wearing brown cord\'s and chanting things about Jesus. Then I saw a girl meditating, naked. I picked up a piece of pottery and it crumbled into pierces in my hand. Someone touched me on the shoulder and I looked back. It was Xep, trying to help me fix the broken pottery.<P>Why was I dreaming about you Xep? I don\'t even know you.. <P>Weirdness...'),(1079,'<p>i am not a complex person, although i play with complex things like computers. i cannot claim that in know much about astrology, Buddhism, health, science, math, or even computers. When I read le\'s <a href=\'http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1069.txt#3830\' target=new>comment</a> about mercury retrograde, I immediately agreed. Thinking, \"yah man, it\'s fucking hot and therefore people are getting cranky.\" No pun intended there, in fact i am not even sure if i got the jist of that either... i often hear things or see things in my own way, and continue to think of things this way... for instance, to me the word \"awry\" is pronounced a-rye - does it really matter? i often say, \"something has gone a-rye!\" and then i get strange looks...\"oh, you mean AWRY.\" they say... \"well, ya, so it is.\"   but if  there is one thing that I know, it is this... you can put New Mexican Hatch Green Chile on anything, and it will be good. </p><p>last night I learned the correct way to pronounce \"Goethe\". I am learning everyday. </p>\r\n<img src=\'http://www.renweb.net/scouts/program/scoutsow/images/vinegar.gif\'>'),(1080,'<P>walking down the street thinking about his list of things that he had to discuss with the woman, the list that grows longer and longer every day.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>he is a progressive technocrat.  he reads science fiction.  he considers the possiblities.  his mind drifts to thx1138.  he thinks about the possible reality of jerking off into a stainless steel plate as a form of procreation.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>life -- can be subjugated.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>all these passions like the one that he will never be copulated long enough to know for instance i am sick of your nagging me doing cliche things like swirling around in his brain.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>she thinks he is dangerous with all his tension and unresolved anger.  but then, she is the one that pealed the paint off the side of his porsche like the skin off <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1075.txt#apple\">a shiny red apple</a> while backing it out of the garage this morning.</P>'),(1081,'<p>\r\nsitting on the couch staring at the sexy exterior of my bitchy lover. he is blinking at me dimly, harboring all kinds of resentment and inner turmoil. his 15 inch screen is alluring but he\'s got a screw loose. g4 powerbook, in a mean mood. restart restart restart. broken macos.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere are a lot of things i have wanted to say in the past few days. i am smiling back a million incorrect observations all day. i want to make untrue confessions. i want spin shit out of control, tell someone i don\'t like that i am in love with them, scream out car windows, get myself beat up using my cellphone in the inner mission.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsome feelings are so fragile and sacred that even talking about them alters them. there are events like this too. if i tell you, i\'ll jinx it. i don\'t want your opinions touching mine so i\'m just not saying anything.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni went out back and the prayer flags had wrapped themselves around the rope they hang from. i went to yoga but due to clock skew i was late and they wouldn\'t let me in. i stubbed my toe against her bed and now it\'s big and purple. i went to the circus but i couldn\'t deal with the emotions so i had to leave. i went to a restaurant just for the jasmine rice but they were out.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni turned on my tibook and it suddenly knows nothing about the airport card.\r\n</p><p>\r\npeople want to argue with me. people want to fuck me, someone wants to stare at me with watery eyes. i am not up for these challenges. i am NOT a badass. next time you decide to speak to me, interact with me, whatever your deal is, whether you\'re god, human, machine, try to remember. i am not a badass. be gentle.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1082,'<p>empty containers<br>\r\narranged carefully on shelves<br>\r\nplaced in empty rooms<br>\r\n</p>'),(1083,'<p>empty containers<br>\r\narranged carefully on shelves<br>\r\nplaced in empty rooms<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.tzolkin.com/dreamcal.htm\">\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1083_1.gif\' border=\"0\"></a></p>'),(1084,'<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><b><font size=\"4\">\"What\'s \r\n  the worse way to die?\" </font></b>asked the video store clerk.<br>\r\n  Being that I was at the video store, <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0101829\">\"Z \r\n  e n t r o p a\"</a> came to mind so I said <font color=\"#003399\"><b>\"Drowning\"</b></font><b>.</b></font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">A small conversation \r\n  ensued between him and the other video clerk and I moved along thinking: Is \r\n  this person weird or do i have a certain look that exclaims \"hey, ask me \r\n  anything, I\'ll answer it!\" [ ? ] </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I had to go H O \r\n  M E this weekend. Within an hour of being there a great crisis had occured, \r\n  my dad happened to go outside only to find his car full of smoke, a scary thing. \r\n  He had left a lit ciggarette inside and it had fallen on the floor and started \r\n  a fire inside. Who knows how long before a big B O O M would have come about. \r\n  </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"4\"><b>\"You are \r\n  born <font color=\"#003399\">alone</font>, and you will die <font color=\"#003399\">alone</font>\"</b></font><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"> \r\n  my mother has always told me. Am I always think, wait a minute, I wasn\'t alone \r\n  when I was born, you were there damn it!. I know what she means though. </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">My friend Brooke \r\n  gets to read poetry outloud at the <a href=\"http://images.amazon.com/images/P/0688171095.01.LZZZZZZZ.jpg\">KGB \r\n  Bar</a> in NYC for a class this summer. I thinks that\'s cool. </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">My Mix CD Soundtrack \r\n  of the moment is called \"Sad Panda\" (title coined by <a href=\"http://www.interiority.net/starcandy/words.html\">bridgie</a>) \r\n  </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://www.astheyare.com/lyrics/lyrics.html\">\"L \r\n  a s t T r a i n \"Â </a> - Travis | \" D e u c e \" - Cardigans \r\n  | \" A s C l o s e A s T h i s \"- PJ Harvey | <a href=\"http://uk.sonymusic.co.uk/manics/music/lyric.php?title=So%2BWhy%2BSo%2BSad&type=LP\">\" \r\n  W h y S o S a d \"</a> - Manic Street Preachers</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://members.tripod.com/%7Epink_glove/lyrics/dont_you_want_me_anymore.html\">\" \r\n  D o n \' t Y o u W a n t M e A n y m o r e ? \"</a> - PULP | \" W i t \r\n  h o u t Y o u I \' m N o t h i n g \" - Placebo | </font><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://www.muse-base.com/mb/?m=Feeling%20Good\">\" \r\n  F e e l i n g G o o d \"</a> - Muse</font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/post/pandatwo.jpg\"></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">\" While you \r\n  make pretty speeches, <br>\r\n  I\'m being cut to shreds. <br>\r\n  You feed me to the lions, <br>\r\n  a delicate balance </font> </p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">When this just \r\n  feels like spinning plates. <br>\r\n  I\'m living in cloud cuckoo land. <br>\r\n  And this just feels like spinning plates <br>\r\n  Our bodies floating down the muddy river. \" t .yorke</font></p>'),(1085,'<p>\r\nmatt and i are moving to our new apartment, we\'ve already begun to pack our shit.  how the hell did we accumulate so much stuff?!  Five couches?!?!  what the hell was i thinking?  three of them are in the goddamned workout room upstairs, unused.  the remains of close to 15 computers ranging from Indigo junk to a DECstation 1 to 386sx boxes to a pentium III system scrapped just months ago.  why?  ehh, who cares anyway.  i look forward to getting out of here, leaving behind another unsuccesful chapter of my life.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\npacking up the shit in my room, i stumble across the few remaining letters from sara, the only ones which somehow escaped the fire.  like a moron, i read them, which does no good at all.  moving on, i found the collected remnants of every relationship i\'ve had since her.  oh me oh my, what fun.  i\'m torn between wondering what demon possessed me to make me go out with these women, and trying to decide if i still want to keep these little reminders.  little notes left on my pillow: \"you\'re so wonderful...~\"  breakup notes found in my mailbox: \"you don\'t have sex with me enough!\" i grin.  or grimace.  both.  i remember nicole punching me out in the hive, hanging a shiner to on me opposite the one i\'d gotten the night before.  next box...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nah, a picture of...Kelly, that was her name.  my big chance to play the \'other man\'.  that was fun.  honesty...yeah right.  darwin\'s good bye note to me...smile...he made me bawl in front of hundreds of commuters waiting for the train to boston.  grr.  big tough skinhead crying his bloody eyes out over two friends and one future wife.  \"postcards should mean \'i will see you before you get this.\'\"  anyone who reads this will never question why i love darwin so.  i tuck it away where i can find it again in a year.  Ishmael...given me by Merewyn.  my attempt at dating a socialist, tree hugging, maniac.  i remember our second date: \"well dave, in truth, i hope to someday create a virus that will kill everyone on earth while leaving the rest of nature unharmed.  !bang.  i giggle thinking of it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni find old paperwork from the militia...reading i find some of it is marked by sara.  stuff like: \"how cumm my gun is smaller?\" \"my pumpkin is gonna get thrashed by a chick from exeter.\"  it\'s been so long since i\'d looked at those papers last.  i break out my lighter, strike it, toss the papers in a box, put the lighter away.  then do it all over again to light my cigarette.  my old doc martens, originally to be saved for my kid.  toss \'em.  no kids in my future anymore.  a gaelic-english dictionary, hrmph, so that\'s where it\'s been!  then...what the fuck?!  a giant pile of guinness cans under some clothes i kept forgetting to throw away, from my last big bender.  musta been there over a year.  i hope no one sees this.  papers everywhere.  old poems i\'d forgot; notebooks of crap and the occasional gem.  my notebook from when i was reading the Koran.  memories begin to strike my crown faster and faster until the pain becomes pressure.  i start to shake my legs, play with a tongue stud i took out four months ago, clench and unclech my fists.  angry music from when i was younger tickles my eardrums.  my body seems on the verge of an adrenaline dump, then backs into sanity again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhmmm.  old copies of 4-4-2 magazine from england.  Manchester United fucking sucks, up CITY!  matt leans in, \"what the hell are you doing?!  holy fuck dave, what are all those guinness cans doing there?!\"  shaddup.  i find $20, and a zillion pennies.  police reports from the night i had to take out a 15 year old kid who attacked me, or from the night i got robbed at knife point.  pieces of an old collapsible baton.  rattan shavings from my SCA heavy list days.  a carton of dorals that must be three years old if it\'s a day.  beer labels...condoms that are probably dust now.  carnegie library books overdue since i first moved here five years ago.  a pair of brass knuckles that aren\'t even mine!  whose i wonder.  ahh, musta been the punker girl...Tara.  beauty to my beast.  how often i\'ve been embarassed that these fine looking ladies have had to walk in public with me.  poor creatures.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAmi: \"david, why won\'t you hold my hand while we walk?\"  has my usual answer to that question ever been accepted i wonder.  a picture of Ethan, Ami\'s little boy, who liked to eat my eyeglasses and Irish Republican Army pins.  my IRA world tour shirt.  a shotgun shell missing its primer.  hehe.  i know where that went!  three pounds of ammonium nitrate, pure, but now useless due to moisture.  dud blasting caps.  more papers.  lost love, shitty sex, nasty stains, angry glares, scarred people...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni sit at my computer and type, trying to remember how all of this makes me feel.  every words i type is two words lost.  i do not like moving.  it does my head in.  i am shamed, sometimes.  proud, sure.  sad, always.  i feel lost in my room.  my computer becomes a stranger to me.  grasping at straws i ask questions of people who are dead to me.  some are just dead.  those who\'ve gone before:  sean, brett, cmdr robert, his wife and children.  that guy who OD\'d in front of me when i was 14.  every scar on my body begins to clamor, to tell the story of its birth.  i now lose three words.  sirens outside wail and i blink back into the world long enough to take a drink.  i know i should not post this.  i killed a friendship last night.  i feel freer now, but miss the person i once loved.  i\'ll go clear off my bed, grab the gin, for a while i\'ll forget.  sleep without dreaming.  break a promise.  \r\n</p>'),(1086,'a list of posts i started writing today, but cancelled at the last minute:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>something about mercury retrograde<br>\r\n<li>something about some stuff that happened during my roadtrip<br>\r\n<li>something about a dream i had this morning when i woke up but couldn\'t breathe<br>\r\n<li>something about a dream i had two nights ago where peter was flying an airplane<br>\r\n<li>something about mayan astrology<br>\r\n<li>something about <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=438.txt\">living with elly</a><br>\r\n<li>something about how it\'s relationship carnage season again<br>\r\n<li>something about russian grammar<br>\r\n<li>something about amends with ex-girlfriends<br>\r\n<li>something about san francisco, wind, and unrest<br>\r\n<li>something about posts i started writing today, but cancelled at the last minute<br>\r\n</ul>\r\n<br>\r\ninstead i stared at the chat window and walked around, trying to break in my new shoes.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<form action=\"\">\r\n<input type=\"submit\" value=\"CANCEL\">\r\n</form>\r\n'),(1087,'<p>i wish i had a scar for every pain i ever suffered.</p>\r\n<p>my flesh should be pink, ridged and tough, changing colour with the temperature, pearly and shiny.</p>\r\n<p>i want a clean cut for every time my soul has cried out for respite.  every honest explosion of self.  every time i\'ve shattered at the bottom of the well for lack of sunlight.</p>\r\n<p>these scars would prove i feel.  they\'d warn people to stay away, to speak in low whispers and to treat me like i might break if their scorn penetrates my scar tissue.</p>\r\n<p>i want to wear the scars to show from where i came.</p>'),(1088,'I love that this website is living and breathing.  While you\'re viewing one page, another changes. Maybe Swinney is like the ocean or like the clouds.  There\'s so much dead content on the Internet, that\'s why I think this site is so special, because it\'s lit by the lifeforce of magic people.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n\r\n(and because I can write little poetic rants that sounded better in my head here without too much judgement)  '),(1089,'i woke up to enough work to keep me busy for a month.  if i hadn\'t played hooky yesterday, i would have one day less to do.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\'t know what got into me.  i usually wake up on mondays and just slog away at the updates one by one, down the email list, pushing things from preorder1.asp to instock.asp mindlessly, punching in the sold.gif images endlessly.  pretty soon i have breakfast, then i come back and do it some more. and then i have lunch and do it some more.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut yesterday i didn\'t want to.  i did everything else.<br><br>\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li>cleaned out my inbox\r\n	<li>searched for western art prints\r\n	<li>browsed the message boards at classmates and alumni\r\n	<li>got lots of spiffy new sim stuff for my game\r\n	<li>killed all my sims and started fresh\r\n	<li>read two really bad books\r\n	<li>made strawberry shortcake\r\n	<li>perused the dirty binaries newsgroups\r\n	<li>called many people who were not home\r\n	<li>called some other people who were\r\n	<li>shopped ebay for nothing in particular\r\n	<li>felt guilty for not working and did a bunch of submit-it for about an hour.\r\n	<li>wrote four diary entries i decided to delete\r\n	<li>took a nap\r\n	<li>pondered the death penalty and came to no firm conclusion\r\n	<li>ate things that are bad for me\r\n</ol>\r\n'),(1090,'i woke up to enough work to keep me busy for a month.  if i hadn\'t played hooky yesterday, i would have one day less to do.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\'t know what got into me.  i usually wake up on mondays and just slog away at the updates one by one, down the email list, pushing things from preorder1.asp to instock.asp mindlessly, punching in the sold.gif images endlessly.  pretty soon i have breakfast, then i come back and do it some more. and then i have lunch and do it some more.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut yesterday i didn\'t want to.  i did everything else.<br><br>\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li>cleaned out my inbox\r\n	<li>searched for western art prints\r\n	<li>browsed the message boards at classmates and alumni\r\n	<li>got lots of spiffy new sim stuff for my game\r\n	<li>killed all my sims and started fresh\r\n	<li>read two really bad books\r\n	<li>made strawberry shortcake\r\n	<li>perused the dirty binaries newsgroups\r\n	<li>called many people who were not home\r\n	<li>called some other people who were\r\n	<li>shopped ebay for nothing in particular\r\n	<li>felt guilty for not working and did a bunch of submit-it for about an hour.\r\n	<li>wrote four diary entries i decided to delete\r\n	<li>took a nap\r\n	<li>pondered the death penalty and came to no firm conclusion\r\n	<li>ate things that are bad for me\r\n</ol><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.marlbe.com/preorder.gif\" width=\"30\" height=\"10\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\"> \r\n<img src=\"http://www.marlbe.com/instock.gif\" width=\"30\" height=\"10\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\"> <img src=\"http://www.marlbe.com/sold.GIF\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\">\r\n'),(1091,'<p>Look around this city: between the downtown office towers, behind that new parking garage next door, under the mouldering FDR on-ramps, in the alleys, the run-down plazas, the forgotten storefronts - the invisible places. This unseen city doesn\'t make the nightly news, there are no gala grand openings here, no celebrity ribbon-cuttings; it exists in people\'s blind spots.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Nevertheless, it is alive with a variety unknown in the gleaming steel world of squeaky-clean urban planners and monolithic smiling corporations. Living a twilight existence, the citizens of this substrata move among the beautiful and powerful unseen, unheard, ghosts in this urban machine.</p>\r\n'),(1092,'<PRE>\r\nConstruction worker\r\nSit in a box smoking pot\r\nGo hammer some wood\r\n\r\nSwing that wrecking ball\r\nHold on to shattering glass\r\nSlide back down the crane\r\n\r\nCat calls whistling by\r\nHey sexy lady come walk near me\r\nShow me your panties\r\n\r\n</PRE>'),(1093,'i\'ve finally figured out what i want to do with my life.  it came to me while i was looking at an <a href=http://www.hrw.org/reports/2001/prison/>article</a> that voiced concern over prison rape in the US.  this absolutely appalled me.  for the first time in a long time, i was motivated to dig into a site a bit further.  i read, and i read some more until i figured out that my future involves working for a .org<br><br>\r\nwhat drains me with the jobs i\'ve been working, is that i no longer believe in them after working there for some time.  i\'m putting my heart into another person\'s bank balance.  i\'ve watch CEO\'s play the \"game\", and i think i\'m done.<br><br>\r\nafter searching for .org jobs, i was really suprised to find that there are hundreds of them. i\'m thinking that it might be because they are half my salary.  however, i think i\'m ready to take that plunge.  what i make shouldn\'t be as important as my happiness, right?<br><br>\r\ndoes anyone else have any good contact information for human rights groups?  has anyone here held a .org job?  was there just as much of a political nightmare?  please, share!\r\n'),(1094,NULL),(1095,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1095_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>i am alone.</p>'),(1096,'<p>Riverbeds of voice who\'s dark flames flow upward into time, flame-shaped throats form the syllables that sculpt the body\'s caves and secret passageways, the stream of night carves dawn\'s shore.  The riverbed is the river\'s mirrored secret, a veined and creviced womb of breath.  We shape our death as the throat shapes a song, molding the shimmering flow of our own holocaust, inscribing the air with letters, searing imprints of passion, a calligraphy of syllabled silences that trace the path of our first cry\'s arc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ressurrection, the keenest point of light inside your eye\'s deepening inward flight.  I saw you flare, brilliant behind the night-wraped sillhouette.  You haloed it as you danced, your passion spirited into stacatto sound as you shone, blinding, a wake into the matrix of your own last breath.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You dance and fly in rhythm with your intimate enemy\'s image.  Your darkest charged impulse clings to the caves and hollows of your beating heart.  You are sistered to your river\'s birth to the sea and we are entwined and twinned on a chord of black-light sound.</p>'),(1097,'I feel like I\'ve already asked this before but, my cat has really bad dandruff and I don\'t know how to get rid of it. Any suggestions?'),(1098,'<p>Do they have Taco Cabanas out in .ca.us or .au?\r\nIf not, let me forewarn you in case they expand.</p>\r\n<p>They\'re a fast foodish texmex chain, distinguishable by their bright pink stucco decor.  And they\'re usually open 24 hours, near enough.</p>\r\n<p>They have decent/good salsa, and fresh, vaguely yummy tortillas.  A fist full of tortillas costs about three bucks American.  Not a bad late-night meal.</p>\r\n<p>But unless you like seeing your dinner more than once, <em>don\'t eat anythomg else on the menu</em>.  Every time I\'ve crossed the tortilla boundry, I\'ve puked my guts out the next morning and had the shits for the next day or two.  The meat they use is Naaaaas-Taaaaay, and I discovered last night that even their chee(z|se) quesadillas aren\'t safe.</p>\r\n<p>I think the pink stucco is a subliminal reminder to buy some pepto bismol on the way home...</p>\r\n<p>Yours in gastrointestinal distress,<br>\r\nMike</p>'),(1099,'<p>so two nights ago I was trying to talk to two boys about my fuckin love life. </p>\r\n<p>it was not long before I threw a stuffed lamb at one of their shiteating grins and screeched hoarsely for them to both GET OOUUUUUUT of my room, let me for once be alone in my own goddamn room.</p>\r\n<p>and then maybe a joke about junkie whores or chat hos, and then maybe acquiescence. they did try, but they couldn\'t help themselves, I was too ridiculous to take seriously. how many times have I said these things about my love life? who gives a shit? why do I go around doing the same stupid shit over and over again? what difference does it make to them? let\'s have a drink.</p>\r\n<p>there comes a time when one has digested boys. when one has learned each boy\'s two wishes in life, or each boy\'s 10 big ideas, and learned the subtle way those simplified constructions of identity play themselves predictably out in every slight gesture or system of behavoir, and learns that she will be bored with men until she dies.</p>\r\n<p>ok. but then there comes a time when boys climb your fire escape without much provocation, agree to a shot of whiskey, yell excitedly about music they like, admire your paintings, and remind you of the brief moments of joy possible from childhood.</p>\r\n<p>It happened to me when Liam and and his friend showed up for a break in between welding their vintage moped to the skeleton of a small tank. we sat by my wall and talked about the vertical beginnings of a fort i am slowly making. the focal point being a post-cave painting that one referred to as \"molecular structure\" and the other as \"bike parts\". somehow Liam betrayed that he knew a whole lot about tape as a way of building, and we drew up plans to teach a seminar in adhesives. The class was quickly cancelled when I remembered <a href=\"http://www.thistothat.com\">thistothat</a>.  do you know how to glue leather to metal?</p> \r\n<p>do you remember building forts in the living room? with blankets and books and random coat hangers and maybe soccer balls taped together?</p>\r\n<p>I made one a few years ago in my friend eve\'s living room, anchoring a series of tall and narrow blue blankets from her ceiling fan, and padding hte floor underneath it with sofa pillows. we would only need to grow a harem to be satisfied forever. </p>\r\n<p>my lovah had passed out in the next room; i crept in and asked for his pants. they were knickers, really. he was limp and slumbering enough for me to tug those knickers off his legs without waiting for an answer, and as I rushed from the room heard him muttering, \"yeah pants baby. all pants all the time.\"</p>\r\n<p>I used his pants as a flag for our empire. a patriotic velcro khaki number. it\'s always an honor to build the kind of trust that warrants you another person\'s drawers without much struggle or written permission. it\'s a familial, implicit consent.</p>\r\n<p>What I acn dig about boys is that they know how to play. You won\'t waste a lot of time verbally exhausting the language of emotion if you are busy taking things apart and setting things on fire just to watch it burn. That is what I have to learn from these boys, as long as they don\'t start to think of me as a girl.</p>'),(1100,'<p>\r\nso i got home last wednesday. the big move wasnt so bad because a friend of mine came along for the ride. for his help i dropped off a bed in trenton at his rents place and then we scooted on down to toronto on the 401 listening to country and western music. it was horrible. the music. so horrible. so we reverted to talking about stuff. i cant quite retell the talks we had. they were on many topics involving many people, places, and theories. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni got home. got settled. got unpacked. got supplies. got a cat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhis name is maxy. he is a orange cat with white splotches. he is longer then most cats ive seen. long and thing. cute. cute. cute. he is so cute, i think as a kitten the other cats used to make fun of his cuteness from which a nervous and paranoid complex surfaced. he hides under my futon for most of the day and then when things settle down he comes up and head buts me in the face for attention. he sleeps on my bed all night long and prrrz all the time. its nice. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe wont go upstairs and im not sure if he\'s eating. i went out last night and got him some wet food to mix with the dry to make things a bit more scrumptious but alas when i bring him to his bowl he scrammbles for the dark cool confines of my futon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim glad to be home. i dont yet regret it. i have a cable modem now so ill be able to occupy my time with napigator and directconnect. i have a tv with cable so i can flick for three hours and then shut it off. tv is boring. i dont know why it lasted. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1101,'<p>\r\n<b>#1:</b> my job is eating me alive<br>\r\ni was thinking back to all my other publishing jobs while i was driving here today<br>\r\ni was thinking about paste-up and fist-fights in the mailroom and asshole editors and smelly art directors obsessed with rotting fruit and a managing editor with tequila in the fridge of her office.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>#2:</b> i heard the best joke at la rondalla last night and i keep saying it to myself over and over and laughing.<br>\r\nso this pirate walks into a bar and he has a steering wheel in front of his pants. and the bartender says hey how come you have a steering wheel in front of your pants. and the pirate says (in pirate accent, please):<br>\r\narrr, it\'s drivin me nuts!</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>#3:</b> lulu<br>\r\negg: oh my god i think im in love with lulu<br>\r\nme: really?<br>\r\negg: she left a voice mail that is just a roofies song<br>\r\nme: there is something about lulu that makes me want to curl up on her back and take a nap<br>\r\ni don\'t know what it is</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>#4:</b> song lyrics<br>\r\negg: i lay out on the bathroom floor, everyone i know is a bore<br>\r\nsimon: \"NO i don\'t want to piss in a cup, i want to FUK SHIT UP\"</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1102,'<p>i\'m going to run away, and i want you to come with me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'m going to run away from fear. i\'m going to run away from regret. i\'m going to run away from denial. i\'m going to run away from self-doubt. i\'m going to run away from dishonesty. i\'m going to run away from insincerity. i\'m going to run away from anxiety. i\'m going to run away from distrust. i\'m going to run away from inconsistency. i\'m going to run away from selfishness. i\'m going to run away from impotence. i\'m going to run away from the cold. i\'m going to run away from expectation. i\'m going to run away from uncertainty. i\'m going to run away from depression. i\'m going to run away from the questions. i\'m going to run away from calculation. i\'m going to run away from rejection. i\'m going to run away from pessimism. i\'m going to run away from negativity. i\'m going to run away from distraction. i\'m going to run away from insensitivity. i\'m going to run away from bitterness. i\'m going to run away from tension. i\'m going to run away from loneliness. i\'m going to run away frustration. i\'m going to run away from discontent. i\'m going to run away from self-obsession. i\'m going to run away from impatience. i\'m going to run away from faithlessness. i\'m going to run away from immorality. i\'m going to run away from dissatisfaction. i\'m going to run away from impatience. i\'m going to run away from embarrassment. i\'m going to run away from hopelessness. i\'m going to run away from dejection. i\'m going to run away from cruelty. i\'m going to run away from devastation. i\'m going to run away from hurt. i\'m going to run away from emptiness. i\'m going to run away from anger. i\'m going to run away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'ll bring my heart with me. i\'ll wear it on my shirtsleeve. you will see me there with a smile curving the corners of my mouth, and a twinkle in my eye.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'m running.</p>'),(1103,'<p>today, <a href=\"http://redemption.co.nz/badamerican.txt\" target=\"_blank\">this link</a> cropped up in chat.</p>\r\n<p>most of the statements made in that document make me feel physically sick.  it seemed to read as a statement of endorsing and practising uncompassionate behaviour.  but everyone else in the channel on the whole couldn\'t disagree with most of the statements.</p>\r\n<p>\"don\'t you think it\'s rude if someone in a shop can\'t communicate with you enough so you can get your business done?\"  no.  i don\'t find it rude.  it may inconvenience me, it may frustrate me, but i don\'t think it\'s rude to speak another language in a country that predominantly speaks english.  is it rude to be a mute shopkeeper?</p>\r\n<p>i mean, it seems like a ridiculous generalisation to say that we don\'t need more laws, and the laws in place just need to be enforced.  what about the laws that are stupid and make no sense and are outdated?  if you couldn\'t make new laws then there would be no protection for stuff like \"cybercrime\" which is a relatively new concept.  right?  or am i just too fucking idealistic?</p>\r\n<p>mostly i tend towards the buddhist concept of compassion in my thinking on grand scales.  i can\'t hate without a earth-shattering reason. i don\'t even know if i could hate then.</p>\r\n<p>i guess this is a pretty pointless post.  i wanted to vent my frustration at intolerant and uncompassionate viewpoints.  whenever i get in conversations like this, i just want to sob my fucking heart out at how fucked up we all are.  it\'s so fucking tragic.</p>\r\n<p> that\'s all.</p>'),(1104,'<p>Tonight I went to go see \"Moulin Rouge\" with my friend <a href=\'http://mscreant.com\' target=new>Ms. Creant</a>. It was a good movie. Sorry if you didn\'t like it.</p>\r\n<p>Driving home, I am thinking about what I am going to post this evening. I was thinking about writing a love poem, or maybe a story about the meaning of life... Instead I come home to hear k-dogg tell of her conversation with a friend who just happens to be calling alot lately... But, I will let K-dogg tell you all about that... as it is quite an ammusing yet sad story.</p>\r\n<p>I go back to my hot and humid room to prepare myself for dream time, when I hear a clickin noise coming from my week old re-conditioned pc; that\'s right the one that holds a prize amount of mp3\'s. I get right down to the box to investigate, is it a wire hiting the fan? Is it a floppy drive on the blitz? No, I know this sound all to well. Hard drives are quite cheap these days, and yes there are many peer2peer networks lurking around to replenish my stash, but damn... this is the third one in like a year! It was 40gigs. It had 39 gigs containing only mp3\'s. I am sad.</p><p>Why does this upset me so? Hmmm, were to start? Two years ago, I remember my friend austin teasing me about my pc that allways seemed to be crashing. Being that I was the house computer tech, I took this personal... it iratated me so. Anyway, I got back to it, and in a few weeks I upgraded everything to a nice level of efficiency and speed. I had the dream box! A few weeks go by and I am wanting more. Damn, well thats how it goes.</p><p>This year I have bought the following:</p>\r\n<li>router X2 = $100ea\r\n<li>MotherBoard X2 = $160ea\r\n<li>PowerSupply X3 = $35\r\n<li>Hardrive X2 = $200\r\n<li>Monitor X2 = $500\r\n<p>\r\nAnd, My burner quit working. I have had a really hard time with this, and I am trying to be mature about all this... trying to say, \"Oh now, it is just stuff, Christopher... and you will get more, not to mention you have three other computers lying around waiting for you to hook up and check your email with...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It is time to retire, time to make a sculpture out of all this shit. Hey egg, how do you glue <a href=\'http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1099.txt\'>silicon to wood</a>?</p> \r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1104_1.jpg\' >'),(1105,'<P>i spoke to my first boyfriend, my first love on the phone tonight.  this was the first time we have spoken at length in a year and a half.  we went out off and on for nearly two years after high school graduation.  it\'s *grimace* my longest relationship.  he wants to get back together after 11 years.  he said so on my answering machine on monday night.  he loves me.  he wants to begin our future together.</P>\r\n<P>he\'s almost completely delusional.  lots of people probably say that about their exes, but in this case i mean it in the most clinical way possible.  i won\'t go into detail.  that\'s not why i\'m writing.</P>\r\n<P>i, along with several mutual friends and family, have watched his decline with bated breath for around three years now.  his \"crazy talk\" used to just rise up unexpectedly in conversations.  then it became more frequent.  now it\'s all you hear when you talk to him.  it frightens and saddens me.  i cannot do anything for him except to perhaps talk to his family about checking him in for serious treatment.  i know they are at their wits\' end with him and must have considered it by now.</P>\r\n<P>he\'s not dangerous.  he means well.  but he\'s not functioning either.  no job.  no money.  no transport. no footing in reality.  hell, that describes most of my friends, but with my ex - it\'s serious.  his parent have tried to throw him out of their house (finally) and he won\'t leave.  i think his actions this week have really been a cry for help.</P>\r\n<P>but i didn\'t write to vent.</P>\r\n<P>12:30AM. disturbed after the phone call ended i made a run to the grocery store, the H.E.B., for comfort food and this bubbled up: we\'re all crazy.  we\'re all sick.  we\'ve all lost our footing in reality.  we\'re all delusional.  we\'ve all made last ditch attempts to connect.  we\'ve all thrown ourselves at people or things.  and we\'ve all lost ourselves.  If not completely, if not for all time then at least somewhat and for some time.  those thoughts percolated through the parking lot, down the aisles, in the checkout, and back out again.  heartsong, compassion shone at the H.E.B. tonight.</P>   '),(1106,'<p>There are alot of people out there who are so completely convinced of their downtroddenness that they can\'t help letting their vitriol seeth, oozing onto anyone who happens into their general vicinity.  This also goes for \"superiority\".  It just pours right out of some people like sweat.  I\'ve especially noticed this watching people on the subway and on street corners.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I could elaborate, but this single thought leaves me with more than enough to contemplate for the moment.  I do wonder, though- why do so many people seem compelled to give strangers a taste of their pain, those ghosts walking the streets in search of attention, like martyrs?  What possesses one to shout their gripes at the world, gripes we all share at one time or another? Why do shiny-suited Wall Streeters guffaw, showing off their Star Tacs, testing the rings at full volume on the bus? Why does that guy keep driving around the middle-income block, showing off his leased Lexus, music turned up, sneakily looking around to see who\'s watching?  Why do I sometimes find myself being berated for being anything from self-proud, hard working, confident, status-quo, to a person following his goals?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It gets exhausting.  Everyone in NYC should be required to receive weekly reality checks.  Meanwhile, someone please tell me it isn\'t like this everywhere...</p>    \r\n'),(1107,'<p>i have dandruff.<br>\r\ni have lots of dandruff.<br>\r\nmy head itches and smells like musty rat\'s ass.<br>\r\npeople give me head and shoulders for my birthday<br>\r\npeople give me dandruff shampoo everyday<br>\r\nbut i don\'t use it<br>\r\ncause i miss winter<br>\r\nand when i look over my shoulder to say hello<br>\r\ni see precious white flakes and reminds me of snow</p>\r\n<p>the end</p>\r\n'),(1108,'<p>\r\nmy friend seth told me about the celibate glow. it is this aura of contentedness that women find irresistable that men get when they dont jerk off and spew for a long time - anywhere from a few months to longer. he says he has a friend who NEVER EJACULATES. this friend is able to have orgasms without ejaculating. and this friend gets anyone he wants, anytime, because he has the celibate glow.</p> \r\n<p>\r\ni chatted with s5 a few nights ago about masturbating [i\'ll let him testify if he wants to share anything]. when i was a teen, eve used to make fun of me for saying that when i masturbate i see \"colors and  pictures and stuff.\" rather than fantasies about, i dunno, Todd Lubin the football star giving it to me standing doggie style in the jock strap laundry room with my face pressed against the door to keep it locked. for example. Ive found that as Ive gotten older ive had to concentrate on past experiences or pretend someone else is touching me in order to get off faster. why is that? Ive been trying to recapture the glory days of youth by letting my mind tool through abstractions while i lightly rub myself and let it happen, just me here now.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy old roommate kim used to go on and on about how monks dont masturbate; instead when they get steel porksword they use the energy to meditate. Arousal gives them extra fuel for enlightenment. Ive thought of experimenting with celibacy and art, like writing a short script or building a miniature empire out of hot glue instead of polishing my pearl, and seeing how my productivity is affected.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nandy warhol said that he couldnt enjoy sex because he was too busy thinking all the time. that to really be a good lover you have to be able to empty your mind and fill it up with the sex. this is the spiritual approach to fucking, akin to the monk\'s meditation. it manifests the same energy in different ways.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<egg> girls dont lose chi masturbating like boys do<br>\r\n<pie> they don\'t use so many kleenex, either</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm not sure if the celibate glow is supposed to happen to women - we dont exactly let go of a wad of reproductive worms when we come. In fact, I think that masturbating serves to feed my desire rather than placate it, at times. and while having sex, sometimes coming once makes it easier to come again soon after.</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have to say, im really fascinated with my own sexuality right now. I used to have very rigid requirements about fucking- how much, with whom, etc. right now Im not even sure ill ever have sex again. and that turns me on. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ncan girls get the celibate glow? there is such a strange double standard about heterosexuality, that assumes men will always be ready and women will always need to be seduced. so why would a woman need to have the celibate glow if men are good to go? can girls get the celibate glow? i want to know.</p>'),(1109,'<p>\r\ni\'m a little out of practice when it comes to the english language these days.  either i\'m saying much shit to people when i get drunk or i just sit silent and make pictures with my sweet unemployed time.  (it\'s a vacation, get over it, spend your savings, have fun, draw things at least an hour a day -- the hour of power -- you owe it to yourself). </p>\r\n<p>\r\nso with that in mind, here i go. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i think i have a point here) </p>\r\n<p>\r\ntomorrow morning, way too early, going to go driving.  driving up north from san francisco.  over the bridge, 101, past the redwoods, through that tree up there, and on and up to the southern coast of oregon. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy friend, he was my roomie in college (lots of stories around that but not for here) he has a cabin up north on the coast.  he gets his running water from a creek up the hill, 200 acres of solitude, he\'s got an outhouse, a couple guns, and eats a lot of cajun for some reason.  two cats, a computer run off of solar energy, and a neighbor over the hill with a phd in psychology who is fun to talk to. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m taking my friend ken with me for this trip.  ken is from philly, a recent transplant to the west coast.  ken has never slept outside before.  he has never gone camping.  i am proud to introduce him to this even though it will eventually turn into a redneck drunkfest.  cheap beer, campsite on the river, much shit talked. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nreed, the friend from college grew up on the oregon coast, which is rather remote compared to most californian coastal villages.  he had an outhouse his entire life, his first day at public school he wipped it out (age seven i think) and took a leak on the playground because when he was a kid you took a crap in the outhouse, took a leak on the tree. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe\'s good.  i met him in las vegas once.  he spent two months driving around the west in his pickup and i flew to vegas to meet him, return trip spent camping one night in death valley.  another time he drove the ten hours down from the northern coast to visit with me in san francisco.  he wears plaid and a baseball cap, sometimes cowboy boots.  he didn\'t go to college, works mostly manual labor.  very intelligent person. </p>  \r\n<p>\r\nwe took some ecstasy and went down to the top (i think) and hung out a bit, drinking water, talking, him in his cowboy boots, me just sitting.  we had a very good time, a very productive experience. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe gets it you know. </p> \r\n<p>\r\nthis weekend i will be introducing my friend to camping, my friend ken.  slowly for sure, this will be some type of car camping and i think in gold beach there will be some sort of jet boat race.  there will be much beer, there will be some smoke, there will be a campfire.  it will be a nice introduction to sleeping outside for him, next i\'ll take him seven days on the pacific crest trail if he digs the trees this time. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere will be rednecks up there.  they work as auto mechanics, ranchers, and tree cutters, they are uneducated, they could care less about art or drug laws or the prison system.  they make cracks about faggots sometimes which makes me squirm but i just watch and take it all in.  they are decent people.  they live in working class small lumber towns and they drink a lot.  they follow the law, they may not like gay people but you know, it\'s the backwoods.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i\'m not defending that, i\'m getting to the point). </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni appreciate my friend reed from college because he was comfortable down here, on some rave drug, in a dance club type of situation in his cowboy boots, etc \r\n<p>\r\n(he even talked up some city girls) </p>\r\n<p>\r\nand at the same time he is comfortable around the rednecks up there.  comfortable in both situations, comfortable talking satre over sake down here, comfortable hunting and fishing and living in a cabin up there.  he is good. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am wondering right now if i am apathetic.  that is, i like to watch.  i want to go to the south and see some bad shit, i am going on a three week solo backpacking trip up in eastern idaho and western montana in july.  i was in oklahoma city on acid.  i\'ve hung out with a speed freak at a pizza restaurant in some backwoods town in oregon listening to him spout about killing niggers and spics.  good and bad.  i am wondering if it is apathetic not to say anything, just to sit around and notice things and observe with a keen eye and collect material and keep a record. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m wondering if that\'s bad, being a watcher. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i\'ll post my montana/idaho trip diary up here if i think it\'s worth it.  three weeks probably not talking to another soul, 15 miles a day).\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1110,'<p>\r\ni\'m a little out of practice when it comes to the english language these days.  either i\'m saying much shit to people when i get drunk or i just sit silent and make pictures with my sweet unemployed time.  (it\'s a vacation, get over it, spend your savings, have fun, draw things at least an hour a day -- the hour of power -- you owe it to yourself). </p>\r\n<p>\r\nso with that in mind, here i go. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i think i have a point here) </p>\r\n<p>\r\ntomorrow morning, way too early, going to go driving.  driving up north from san francisco.  over the bridge, 101, past the redwoods, through that tree up there, and on and up to the southern coast of oregon. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy friend, he was my roomie in college (lots of stories around that but not for here) he has a cabin up north on the coast.  he gets his running water from a creek up the hill, 200 acres of solitude, he\'s got an outhouse, a couple guns, and eats a lot of cajun for some reason.  two cats, a computer run off of solar energy, and a neighbor over the hill with a phd in psychology who is fun to talk to. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m taking my friend ken with me for this trip.  ken is from philly, a recent transplant to the west coast.  ken has never slept outside before.  he has never gone camping.  i am proud to introduce him to this even though it will eventually turn into a redneck drunkfest.  cheap beer, campsite on the river, much shit talked. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nreed, the friend from college grew up on the oregon coast, which is rather remote compared to most californian coastal villages.  he had an outhouse his entire life, his first day at public school he wipped it out (age seven i think) and took a leak on the playground because when he was a kid you took a crap in the outhouse, took a leak on the tree. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe\'s good.  i met him in las vegas once.  he spent two months driving around the west in his pickup and i flew to vegas to meet him, return trip spent camping one night in death valley.  another time he drove the ten hours down from the northern coast to visit with me in san francisco.  he wears plaid and a baseball cap, sometimes cowboy boots.  he didn\'t go to college, works mostly manual labor.  very intelligent person. </p>  \r\n<p>\r\nwe took some ecstasy and went down to the top (i think) and hung out a bit, drinking water, talking, him in his cowboy boots, me just sitting.  we had a very good time, a very productive experience. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe gets it you know. </p> \r\n<p>\r\nthis weekend i will be introducing my friend to camping, my friend ken.  slowly for sure, this will be some type of car camping and i think in gold beach there will be some sort of jet boat race.  there will be much beer, there will be some smoke, there will be a campfire.  it will be a nice introduction to sleeping outside for him, next i\'ll take him seven days on the pacific crest trail if he digs the trees this time. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere will be rednecks up there.  they work as auto mechanics, ranchers, and tree cutters, they are uneducated, they could care less about art or drug laws or the prison system.  they make cracks about faggots sometimes which makes me squirm but i just watch and take it all in.  they are decent people.  they live in working class small lumber towns and they drink a lot.  they follow the law, they may not like gay people but you know, it\'s the backwoods.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i\'m not defending that, i\'m getting to the point). </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni appreciate my friend reed from college because he was comfortable down here, on some rave drug, in a dance club type of situation in his cowboy boots, etc \r\n<p>\r\n(he even talked up some city girls) </p>\r\n<p>\r\nand at the same time he is comfortable around the rednecks up there.  comfortable in both situations, comfortable talking satre over sake down here, comfortable hunting and fishing and living in a cabin up there.  he is good. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am wondering right now if i am apathetic.  that is, i like to watch.  i want to go to the south and see some bad shit, i am going on a three week solo backpacking trip up in eastern idaho and western montana in july.  i was in oklahoma city on acid.  i\'ve hung out with a speed freak at a pizza restaurant in some backwoods town in oregon listening to him spout about killing niggers and spics.  good and bad.  i am wondering if it is apathetic not to say anything, just to sit around and notice things and observe with a keen eye and collect material and keep a record. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m wondering if that\'s bad, being a watcher. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i\'ll post my montana/idaho trip diary up here if i think it\'s worth it.  three weeks probably not talking to another soul, 15 miles a day).\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1111,'<p>It starts like most of my dreams. I\'m in a small town, deserted and late at night, I have the ability to fly and hover as I move. I leap through people\'s yards, my face brushes past flowers, the night is foggy and sweet.</p>\r\n<p>I come to the center of town, white christmaslights are strung up around the shops, the air glows like warm milk, dark shapes are moving, it\'s hard to see. Horses. They fill the street, a herd of wild horses, black horses. A few people have come to watch, confused.</p>\r\n<p>I move to the horses. I want to go with them, become them. I realize this may confuse the people more, but I don\'t want to be one of the people. The horses are bolting and leaping. I move closer...</p>\r\n<p>The phone rings, bringing me out. Bastards.</p>\r\n'),(1112,'<p>\"In England\"... a man from the corner sits, and I wait to board the train.  I\'d sit too if there were chairs enough.  The red 3 train slides right up to the aging platform, glowing like an infernal summer cyclops.  Odd.  An odd number, three.  A trinity of sorts; local, express, cross-town.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The platform creaks, a thousand rose-head nails and milled rivets loosed under the dingy and faded yellow strip.  I think it was orange once, too, but years fade the color as do the troddings of a millennium\'s feet, gum, and cigarette ashes.  No smoking on the platform, I think, but the man settles into his metal chair, his graying tattered papers stacked, a bridge across his knobby old knees.  He tosses the spent match to the ground.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Above me, a flake of debris snows through the grate, onto the track, just moments before the train stampedes in on squealing steel feet.  Its cyclops eye twinkling, sparks dancing, and the bit of paper is whisked away amid the rising swirls of heat, back from where it came.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Violence is punished!\", his lips admonish, below a crooked left-set nose holding up crackling rigid frames of glasses, askew.  His slack jaw dangles and glazed eyes dart about the unlistening crowd for someone who might forfeit an ear.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\'s a pay telephone to the left, which sits, unringing.  I\'m sure it doesn\'t even have a number.  After all, who would call a train platform?  I\'d imagine its coin slot is obscured with the same mineral scale that\'s overtaken most of its support, the platform support, the roof irons.  Brown rivers of rust, dripping.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe dried mustard.  From here, it looks like some abandoned luncheon meal.  Maybe that\'s what attracts the flies.</p>\r\n'),(1113,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1113_1.gif\' width=400>\r\n<P>digging around on a *.com mp3 server, i have found iron maiden songs.  they are talking of two minutes to midnight, the hands that glitter in doom, to kill the unborn in the womb.  this is iron maiden.  guitar solos and some specs of dirt. who can imagine these guys in the studio?  working out their deadly guitar lyrics.\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>[10:33] <*adam*> iron maiden makes perfect sense</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\nbody rags and children cut in two... the killing spree of demon seeds, the glamour of killing and bla.  dont pray for the soul anymore.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni dont understand where they are going with all this.  lots of lyrics about the comming attack.  death.  guns.  running.  messiah.  the eighties.  Aces High, The Number Of The Beast.  i guess this is not worse than obsessing over technology.  the evil face the twists my mind and brings me to dispair.  we all have one.\r\n</P><P>\r\ncan i get a religious obsession for my grounding here?  religious obsession and knowledge of empending terminator like technodestruction (building bigger guns) to bring civilization back into the dark ages of petrifying evil.</P>\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe number of the beast 666 the one for you and me.  i\'m coming back, i will return, i have the fire, i have the force, i have the power to make my evil take it\'s course!\r\n</P><P>\r\nand the tune does not change.  reganomaniac production of war machinery and impending heat death as the world is reduced to it\'s prelife state. the destitution of those suburbs that are no longer on the outskirts of town but breaking down between the metropolitan centers and the polished track homes of the 90\'s, all fresh and treeless.\r\n</P><P>\r\niron maiden lock us up again.  show us your ways and our death.  iron maiden, unite us and our death with religion again and purge this godless land.  take up that rain check of death and make due.  it is the last hour and we are all slaves to the power of death!\r\n</P>'),(1114,'<p>\r\nhe is like a land mine a weapon that waits until the enemy shows up then punishes him unlike a land mine he is selective he floats through society observing behaviors and threats when violence looms over the heads of the innocent only then does he take action if you are in need \r\nand he sees even if you are not his he will defend you this is called duty a warrior is not entirely of his society to defend it he must stand half outside of it he must countenance actions most cannot where others would fold he must move a warrior is both proud and ashamed of what he is he searches too often in vain for a gentler soul which can accept his duality to be loved for <i>who</i> he is a warrior is a foundation ugly and often ignored needed for reasons most do not fully understand only when he is gone is he missed, and then not by many\r\nthose who are protected by him often resent and fear him a warrior is often a lonely creature an abandoned creation of society worst of all, he knows this aware of his position he stays on anyway knowing his duty is vital and right knowing he is not a mere soldier a warrior can question he can refuse but never because he is tired or hurt or sorry he hears the call to duty always.\r\n</p>'),(1115,'<p>\r\nThis apple sits and makes me think of other fruits sitting, glistening and waiting, fair juicy pulps before me, but saved.  I behold these fruits, other fragrant pods and spheres, a citrus beauty, crescent fruits picked from luscious trees sweet on my lips, a daily dose of health and you polish my teeth with your pulp as I bite into you, my teeth sinking deep into your core, a seed falls\r\nand a twisted stem drops to the floor.  The browning core sits atop the heap, reminding me\r\nof other fruits and other days, for health, the apple of my eye.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1115_1.jpg\' height=\'150\'>\r\n</p>'),(1116,'<p>\r\ni was stalking my housemate steve tonight, by searching for his name on google. i will note that there are seven pages of google results for the name \"steve simitzis\" and only 3 for the name \"elly millican.\"  webcam tits only get you so far.\r\n</p><p>\r\ninsight into who s5 was before he came to san francisco and got liberated:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.ele.uri.edu/Courses/ele447/spring.97/old.projects/96/simitzis/project.html\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1116_1.gif\' border=\"0\"></a>\r\n<br> \r\na telling class picture from electrical engineering days at university of rhode island. \r\n'),(1117,'<P>i\'m surveying my room and i see this:\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>snow flakes\r\n<LI>koko crunch\r\n<LI>honey stars\r\n<LI>bonaqua\r\n<LI>overseas staff\r\n<LI>judy and mary\r\n<LI>learning perl\r\n<LI>smoking kills\r\n<LI>nesvita\r\n<LI>choco muc\r\n<LI>unbreakable\r\n<LI>traffic\r\n<LI>on demand\r\n<LI>convenient\r\n<LI>do not disturb\r\n<LI>$115\r\n<LI>intertouch\r\n<LI>clean!\r\n<LI>maximum RPM\r\n<LI>kodak\r\n</UL>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m amazed how much branding creeps into our every day lives.  not all of the above is a product, but it is interesting how much we\'ve been conditioned to accept brand recognition and blatent advertisement at every turn.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbeing in such a commercially assaultive city has made me notice this more and more.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nkind of like the kodak sign at fisherman\'s wharf. \"this scenic view brought to you by kodak\"  every picture a tourist takes in front of the bay gets branded.  how sick is that?  i wonder what else is out there that i\'ve failed to notice.  hrm.\r\n'),(1118,'<P>this girl i know is an academic, and i try to be there for her.  she likes to take photographs with her polaroid.  they are fuzzy and abstract, some portraiture.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am sitting here with a technicolor bandana on, a elron neopets tee shirt, camo shorts, and a sequined bra.  and where is she with her camera? </P>\r\n<blockquote>out of film</blockquote>\r\n<p>she said, frantically working to find some.  she does not have film and does not have a digital camera.  why?   she is not a photojournalist who would always have film.  she is an academic who does not adopt new technology and thus has no digital camera.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>you will never see this outfit that i have on.  it will be taken off soon and lost for ever.  such is life.  water flows in the stream.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>mang.</P>'),(1119,'<p>he wasnt really asking to clip my fingernails. he had just complained about them more than once in the past week. \"you really need to clip your fingernails.\" he is cleaner than most other boys; we argue about whether he stinks like a man. </p>\r\n<p>\"am I not musky enough for you? would you like me to wear old spice?\"</p>\r\n<p>so i let him clip them. you want it done, you do it.</p>\r\n<p>\"wait, are you sure you dont want a certain shape?\" chicken shit. backing out. </p>\r\n<p>I always had long fingernails without trying. I was the envy of every girl in 5th grade. \"your fingernails are not your most beautiful point\", he dipped his head over my hands and squinted his eyes looking like a pink baby piggy.</p>\r\n<p>\"fuck you, my fingernails are part of my character. they are my weapons. dont insult me like that.\" i open things with my nails. i scrape things with my nails. when i draw or paint, i use my nails to make lines.</p>\r\n<p>my fingernails were my weapons. boys played \"chase\"; they ran around trying to catch you. what they would do once they caught you was a mystery. theyd just sort of gleefully hold onto you, victorious, until some other entertainment passed through recess.</P>\r\n<p> I used my fingernails to claw them off me. they learned early on not to try to \"catch\" me.</p>\r\n<p> my fingernails are my weapons, and I let him clip them. it was not even his idea. I have been playing three chords on the guitar, so I figure if I clip one left hand why not the other? </P>\r\n<p>\"I like when you grab onto me and scratch me, but these hurt.\" if they are my weapons, why did I let him clip my fingernails? my claws. out of mercy?</p>\r\n<p>I found myself insisting that he be the one to do it. Backseat driving my hygeine would not do. If he would alter my body, his hand would participate.  I wanted to see how he might change my way of touching. his hand molding mine, mine making other worlds. I wanted to give something up to him.</p>\r\n<p>he wants to sap my virility.</p>\r\n<p>I complain that it\'s kind of a commitment.</p>\r\n<p>\"just for a few weeks, \" he assures me.</p>\r\n'),(1120,'<p>I know introductions aren\'t exactly necessary here, but I feel compelled to give one. here goes:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so itâ€™s just under two weeks ago. Iâ€™m down at CafÃ© Abir working on the last third of a latte, which I ordered an hour earlier against my better judgment considering itâ€™s now 10 pm and I really ought to try to go to bed before 3am tonight. my friend Johnâ€™s there, reading aloud the piece Iâ€™d just finished after a brief struggle for inspiration: a short essay and poem that Iâ€™ll read at my younger sisterâ€™s wedding the following Saturday. heâ€™s hamming it up, reciting the whole thing in this overdramatic growly deathmetal voice, but pausing every now and then to say stuff like â€œhey, thatâ€™s nice.â€ Iâ€™m so happyâ€”itâ€™s been years since I wrote anything for a public reading that wasnâ€™t in some way work relatedâ€”and itâ€™s looking like this will go over well.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and then out of nowhere thereâ€™s this new guy at the table, young, handsome, kind of bouncy. heâ€™s talking about this thing and thatâ€”something about how he worked with the creator of Radiskull and Devil Doll, I think. perhaps it was Johnâ€™s performanceâ€”he was unwittingly doing a fair impression of everybodyâ€™s favorite Flash-animated burning skull. but John decides itâ€™s time for a smoke, so he heads out to his usual post at the sidewalk chairs to roll up a smoke, and by this time, my visitor is already on a new tangentâ€”the new Cerebus compilation I happened to have on my table.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so this guy and I start up a dialogue and at first Iâ€™m wary. usually when someone shows up out of nowhere at a cafÃ© to chat you up nonstop about whatever itâ€™s generally a good indicator that you have a nutjob on your hands. after a few minutes, though, I relax: he makes sense. and weâ€™re making connectionsâ€”after touching on the ongoing misogyny in Cerebus Vol.14 (this time Dave Simâ€™s disparaging Hemmingwayâ€™s wife for no good reason), we talk about the homeless situation in SF (worse or better over the years?), the mania for online gaming in Korea (as the phenomenon of hacker media stars there), his work as an aspiring musician/dj, the fact both our girlfriends are in executive management positions (his partner is in Hong Kong) and so on. At some point the cafÃ© closed and we had to go out and stand on the corner under the streetlight, shivering in the cold, still talking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and thenâ€”perhaps around the time Iâ€™m telling him about my student days as a unix geek at UCSC in the late â€˜80â€™s, doing ethnographies of f-net chatters and soforthâ€”he asks me a stunning question: â€œDo you know anyone involved with, say, Organic Online or Critical Path?â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>!!!  where did <i>that</i> come from? I think to myself. itâ€™s one of those rare Philadelpha soul-song lightning strokes of intuition/connection, surprising and strange. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I know that an old college friend of mineâ€”Rocky Mullen (aka Caliban) has been a prominent member of the Organic tribe for some time now, though weâ€™ve hardly spoken at all since graduation.  And a close friend of John (now sitting a few chairs away, still smoking)â€”a guy named Langâ€”worked, perhaps still works, at Critical Path.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but the person who put Critical Path on the map for me was (of course, who else?) elly. I guess I stumbled upon her site way back in â€™97 or so. probably from some sort of â€œcool webcamsâ€ link or another, back when cams were cool & novel & and the big whoop-de-doo. but over time, whatâ€™s brought me back to ellyâ€™s site is her writing. itâ€™s interesting, compelling, lyric, etc. and so on. an interesting read, though sometimes months go by between visits. and lots of drama, too. very few dull moments, and even those elly finds a way to spice up. itâ€™s really the only online diary Iâ€™ve ever bothered to read with any regularity.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so I mention this elly connection to my new friend and he does this kind of spin kick thing and weâ€™re talking about all of that for awhile. and thatâ€™s when I learn Iâ€™m talking to Darwin. maybe some of you already guessed this.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and Darwin & I keep talking for awhile longer and he invites me to write here, in swinney. which is funny because I checked this spot out a few months ago, and saw the invitation to post, but it seemed like an intimate placeâ€”perhaps too intimate to just barge into when nobody knows who you are. and I still feel that wayâ€”thereâ€™s a tight network of close friends here, it seemsâ€”but Iâ€™ve got an invitation, so Iâ€™ll go for it. so: hey, hello, hi.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>after a short while it got colder and Darwin was hungry and my girlfriend was doubtless wondering why I was out so late, so he and I split up. a few days later, he sent me an email: â€œI look forward to your writing.â€ and so here it isâ€”the start anyway. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>More soon, hopefully not so meandering.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>thanks, Moe</p>\r\n\r\n<p>btw, my handle was one of my first account names at UCSC all those years ago. thereâ€™s already a Matt and a Chiamattt hereâ€”we donâ€™t need another Matt to confuse everyone even more.</p>\r\n'),(1121,'<p>\r\ni keep having these two dreams.  every night it\'s one or the other, unless i don\'t seem to dream at all.  in the first one, which i\'ve been having on and off since childhood, i find myself in those pajamas with the little footies.  you know the kind.  as i\'ve grown older, so my dream self has, but always those cool ass blue pajamas with the footies.  anyway, i find myself with a female, always older than me, even if she isn\'t.  and she\'s leading me by the hand into a grocery store.  but the only thing they sell are legos.  i mean, there are legos everywhere.  every shelf has lego sets of all kinds.  some that don\'t even exist.  and she tells me she\'ll buy me one of each lego set.  there\'s like millions, but i get one of each.  and she pats me on the head and we leave.  then i wake up and look around my room for the legos.  of course they aren\'t there...which ALWAYS bums me out.  i\'ve been having that one since i can remember.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe other dream is far less pleasant,and has only recently been showing up.  i find myself in the desert or the Rif, in a Foreign Legionaire uniform from the \'50s.  i\'ve got the black kepi of a NCO, and the insignia on my uniform tell me that i\'m a <i>caporal chef</i> in the 2e REP, the legion parachute regiment.  i don\'t have the correct rifle for the time period of my uniform, instead having the Steyr AUG, which is the piece of shit they issue now.  my boots are worn almost through, and i don\'t look like me, but i\'m me anyway.  i\'ll be either looking across the sand or at the Rif mountains, and then i\'ll turn to my left and see my old friend Sean.  Sean was actually in the legion, the 2e REP section 4, the sabateurs actually.  he died around 4 years ago in a training accident.  so i look over at him and he\'s dressed the same as i.  we grimace and step forward together.  always i see the nipple beneath his foot just before it happens.  but i can\'t speak no matter how i try.  screaming silently i watch as his foot comes down, so slowly it\'s painful.  his foot finally comes down and there is an explosion.  not like in the movies, with the flames and all.  just a bang and dust, the pressure wave knocks me down, and i can see his body fall.  the leg is torn off and the stump blackened.  he\'s dead, somehow i know it in the dream without checking, but his eyes are open and staring at me.  i feel as if he\'s accusing me.  then i wake up.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe first one is funny and doesn\'t much bother me.  but the second recurring dream has been driving me to drink, as when i drink i don\'t dream.  or at least don\'t remember dreaming.  it hurts because Sean, before he left for the legion taught me so much about...the thing i know.  he was a great guy, and never fucked me off just \'cause i was a youngster when we knew each other.  to me he was very much the romantic image...i\'d wanted to join the legion when i\'d known him.  when he left, i\'d hear from him now and again, and always i\'d want to know more.  i first learned the story of Camerone in one of his letters.  he\'d made use of the <i>anonymat</i> and had changed his name but the mail came with only his number and the barracks address in Corsica.  i couldn\'t wait for him to finish his time in the Legion and come visit.  all the stories he\'d have.  when i received word that he\'d died in a training accident i was furious.  i knew that he\'d gotten a promotion and was teaching certain demolitions courses.  dangerous work.  i don\'t know why it\'s taken so long for me to feel anything more than anger and sadness.  but now i think about him so much.  i dunno.\r\n</p>'),(1122,'<P>\r\nStay Liminal<br>\r\nWalk the Line<br>\r\nThe shaman creeps over the edge of this world and stays there, straddling the border between worlds.<br>\r\nWe all can become shamans, but only some of us will survive the experience.<br>\r\nWe can walk over the edge in a number of ways: meditation, self-flagellation, sexual negation (getting that celibate glow,) drugs, etc.  \r\nI prefer drugs.  <br>\r\nHow do you get there?<br>\r\n</P> <P>\r\nMark Pesce (author of The Playful World) said that we have two possible roads to transhuman existence, and both are madness if you look down them from our human point of view.\r\nThe first: continue standing on the borders, as shamans staring into the light that blinds.  \r\nThe second: allow technology to create a planet of carefully carved calm and radiant minds.  Gods by design.\r\nIs there another way?\r\n</P> <P>\r\nThe \"God Gene\" has been discovered.  It is a neurotransmitter or region of the brain that creates feelings of the divine when excited, causing people to experience God.  What if there was a drug that would induce that very feeling, stimulate those neurons? would you take it?  \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1123,'<p>shaving all my hair off<br>\r\nshaving all my dogs hair off<br>\r\ntrading in the evil suv for a car<br>\r\ngiving away everything i own to start over<br>\r\ngoing back to living on the road<br>\r\ngoing back to school<br>\r\nhaving insane sex with some strange man<br>\r\ngetting pregnant<br>\r\nhaving insane sex with some strange woman<br>\r\nnever getting pregnant<br>\r\ngetting another dog<br>\r\nor maybe a cat<br>\r\nhaving insane sex with some strange couple<br>\r\nrewriting my resume<br>\r\nasking my doctor for some meds<br>\r\nfinding someone to fall in love with<br>\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1124,'test'),(1125,'<P>hi</P>\r\n\r\n<P>html should be stripped out</P>'),(1126,'<P>mc killa</P>'),(1127,'<p>\r\nid like to be able to sit in the corner. the shade just covering my eyes. to eat in silence and to think for a change. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni found myself putting a cigarette out on my friends arm the other night at a rock n roll club called the bovine. then it was my turn. it was carnal and primitive. i tell everyone someone walked into me with a cig at a club.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlife is different here in toronto. im being pushed and voluntarilly thinking thoughts id never have thought about in ottawa. i think about money and sucess and all those things that make people think people are ambitious. ive never been ambitious. ive thought about it and planned it in my head but ive never gotten around to do anything about it. not that i have now. I just feel this \"need\".</p>\r\n<p>\r\nid like to say some things but im afraid they might come out as complaints or sound as though im feeling sorry for myself. what id like to say would come out in totally cliche ways and would be so unclear and uncertain of reflecting the honest to goodness truth about myself. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel as though im fake. i feel as though i have been living a lie and that i have tricked myself and others into beleiving things about myself. i act as though and am not as though.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni skim the surface of a lot of things and in so doing that i am often regarded as being knowledgable about things. there isnt any one thing i feel i know a lot about. i feel as though i know a little about a lot of things. not enough to win at jeopardy, but enough to play at home. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went to school for five years and never REALLY researched a topic. i found the answers to questions, essays, and discussions at the last possible minute and made it out to look like i did a lot of work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsince i have been home i have been asked questions by people who think i have answers and i find myself skirting the issues and going off on rants that dont really have anything to do with the topic at hand. it then struck me that thats what i have been doing my whole life. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went outside earlier for a cigarette and a spider hung infront of my face and scared the shit out of me. i went out again a few hours later and another one lowered itself right beside me and scared the shit out of me. two earwigs were on my smoking chair and a daddy long legs crossed my path a few times. i hate bugs. i hate bugs so much. \r\n</p>'),(1128,'I\'ve been doing some late spring-cleaning.  At some point, I found a stack of read books, and decided to take them to the local coffee joint for recirculation.  Among them was was <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0767902890/o/qid=992865125/sr=2-1/104-9292592-8109517\">The Things They Carried</a>.  Emblaisoned on the back cover was:<p>\r\n<center>\r\n<i>\"They carried all the emotional baggage of men who might die. Grief, terror, love, longing--these were intangibles, but the intangibles had their own mass and specific gravity, they had tangible weight. They carried shameful memories. They carried the common secret of cowardice.... Men killed, and died, because they were embarrassed not to.\"</i>\r\n</center>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWe all carry many of the same intangibles; its the tangibles though, that are unique and interesting.  I managed to learn a little about myself this weekend by taking inventory.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nContents of my wallet as of 6/18/01:<br>\r\n1 unlimited ride metrocard<br>\r\n1 old college id<br>\r\n1 NYFD chemical certification liscense<br>\r\n1 empty pre-paid calling card<br>\r\n1 old id from a different college<br>\r\n1 discover card<br>\r\n1 chase mastercard<br>\r\n1 drivers lic. for a state i left 6 years ago<br>\r\n1 Duane Reede discount card<br>\r\n2 NJ transit tickets (to rahway)<br>\r\n2 expired metrocards<br>\r\n2 blockbuster cards<br>\r\n1 gnc gold card<br>\r\n1 chase atm card<br>\r\n1 picture of significant other<br>\r\n1 expired nj driver\'s license<br>\r\n1 dental insurance card<br>\r\n1 ppo insurance card<br>\r\n1 ticket to my own college graduation<br>\r\n9 dollars<br>\r\n1 clothing store receipt<br>\r\n1 taco bell gift-certificate<br>\r\n1 blank bank counter-check<br>\r\n1 locke of passed dog\'s hair<br>\r\n1 fortune reading \"you will cross a large body of water today\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nThe contents of my backpack are even more intersting and insightful- I won\'t diugress here, but I can\'t believe what I haul back and forth each day, for no reason.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1128_1.jpg\' align=\"left\">\r\n\r\n'),(1129,'<font face=verdana size=2>Today I feel very perky<Br>Because I<BR>Drank coffee.<BR>Is it better to be happy<Br>But only from<BR>False stimulant<BR>Than to be healthy<BR>And unhappy?<BR>I feel poked<BR>And proded<BR>And pulled.<BR>Its a Cancer\'s way to help help help but to not be<BR>Helped.<BR>To be motherly<BR>But never mothered.<BR>I need to be taken care of<BR>Not by the person who<BR>Smothers<Br>And Judges.<BR>Someone with no history.'),(1130,'<p> I can tip over and you can pour me out.  swinney.org is awesome. I\'ve never been to a place like this.  For the past few months I\'ve read archives and watched people post occasionally.  This is great.  I\'m happy to be part of this community.  This has inspired me to make a project similar to this one that doesn\'t clash with the same idea.</P>\r\n<P>Where does one get good tea in this city?  Where does one buy good vegan food here?  I\'ve found two or so places and have searched online BUT no luck really other than Rainbow and Herbivore.  Are there any cool hang outs?</P>'),(1131,'<p> I can tip over and you can pour me out.  swinney.org is awesome. I\'ve never been to a place like this.  For the past few months I\'ve read archives and watched people post occasionally.  This is great.  I\'m happy to be part of this community.  This has inspired me to make a project similar to this one that doesn\'t clash with the same idea.</P>\r\n<P>Where does one get good tea in this city?  Where does one buy good vegan food here?  I\'ve found two or so places and have searched online BUT no luck really other than Rainbow and Herbivore.  Are there any cool hang outs?</P>'),(1132,'<p> I can tip over and you can pour me out.  swinney.org is awesome. I\'ve never been to a place like this.  For the past few months I\'ve read archives and watched people post occasionally.  This is great.  I\'m happy to be part of this community.  This has inspired me to make a project similar to this one that doesn\'t clash with the same idea.</P>\r\n<P>Where does one get good tea in this city?  Where does one buy good vegan food here?  I\'ve found two or so places and have searched online BUT no luck really other than Rainbow and Herbivore.  Are there any cool hang outs?</P>'),(1133,'<p> I can tip over and you can pour me out.  swinney.org is awesome. I\'ve never been to a place like this.  For the past few months I\'ve read archives and watched people post occasionally.  This is great.  I\'m happy to be part of this community.  This has inspired me to make a project similar to this one that doesn\'t clash with the same idea.</P>\r\n<P>Where does one get good tea in this city?  Where does one buy good vegan food here?  I\'ve found two or so places and have searched online BUT no luck really other than Rainbow and Herbivore.  Are there any cool hang outs?</P>'),(1134,'<p> I can tip over and you can pour me out.  swinney.org is awesome. I\'ve never been to a place like this.  For the past few months I\'ve read archives and watched people post occasionally.  This is great.  I\'m happy to be part of this community.  This has inspired me to make a project similar to this one that doesn\'t clash with the same idea.</P>\r\n<P>Where does one get good tea in this city?  Where does one buy good vegan food here?  I\'ve found two or so places and have searched online BUT no luck really other than Rainbow and Herbivore.  Are there any cool hang outs?</P>'),(1135,'<p>\r\nmy stomach<br>\r\nfeels butterflies<br>\r\nin it.<br>\r\nis it her?<br>\r\nis it \'the biz\'?<br>\r\na side note<br>\r\nis that i don\'t have<br>\r\nher<br>\r\ni mean, i don\'t<br>\r\nhave headphones<br>\r\ntoo many post-its.<br>\r\ni drink green tea.<br>\r\nit\'s the antithesis<br>\r\nof coffee</p>\r\n\r\n<p>never before has a woman affected organs other than my brain, my heart, and my dick.  this girl, she\'s like too much coffee in my tummy.  <a href=\"http://www.coffeescience.org/heart.html\">she</a> comes around not very much, like a treat, she gives herself to me.  as soon as the caffeine is gone, another dose.  she is so yummy, but can i keep living like this with <i>coffea arabica</i> acids gnawing at my stomach lining constantly?  it is proven that coffee prevents cirrhosis of the liver.  she drinks 3 cups every morning with her bagel, the new yorker.  she takes \'liver pills\'.</p>\r\n<p>i think her liver must be in fine condition.  it\'s mine i\'m worried about.</p>  \r\n'),(1136,'<p>\r\nThat look darwin gets when he\'s thinking <i>really</i> hard about something.  not his pensive look, the OTHER one.  when dawn blinks because i said or did something obnoxious and/or unexpected.  hugging my friends.  helping people in need.  putting new racing stripes on Maeve, my computer...red makes it go faster!  watching or playing soccer.  coming home from work and looking at my roomate, and then we both fall down and holler about needing new jobs.  using my skills in a positive manner.  pork pie hats.  watching as my younger brothers and sisters develop into people.  making peace with my father after 24 years of hell.  looking back on where i\'ve been and realizing i\'ve actually survived and grown.  those  nights with close friends, where you complain nonstop about being bored, only to later realize it was a magical time.  watching the lightning over the bay back home, then moshing in the rain at three in the morning.  role playing games.  sitting down with a new book and then forgetting to save some for later.  food that makes me sweat.  knowing i\'ll never have to undergo survival training again.  remembering those nights with sara, coming home reeking of sweat and gunpowder, bruised and bloody, and deciding to make love rather than shower right away.  punching my little slice of a steel I beam.  making my gas machine soup in 10 gallon lots.  swordfish and scallops.  those wife swappers over in New Hampshire who i met while i was Rob\'s bodyguard.  cleaning firearms while watching the simpsons.  just watching the simpsons.  trying to reverse engineer the rules of cricket while drunk, with several good friends.  coaching those little kids who show up at the field and asked me to teach them soccer.  good beer and bombay sapphire gin.  those BIG bottles of manichevitz in between learning the turkey basting dance.  being so in love with someone that i forget who i am and how i normally behave.  Kenya AA.  chai tea over ice with skim milk.  the way i can\'t walk down a single flight of stairs after our friday leg workout.  the terrible burn in my chest after our wednsday chest and arms workout.  western style sword fighting or pole arm fighting.  wargaming.  doing dished when i feel depressed.  my picture of me and dawn at darwin\'s old place.  not feeling empty.  little kids and babies, even when they are loud or smelly or not smiling and shit.  female punk vocalists...hell female punks.  reading the dictionary with some friends.  wooden matches.  big trees that haven\'t been raped by psycho enviromentalists.  those ducks and their chicks in my boss\'s pond.  getting really bruised while teaching self defense or practicing with my business partner.  a new pair of diagonal cutters.  computer games that can engage me for more than a few hours.  old celtic music.  dancing around big fires with a spear and half naked women covered with woad.  learning latin and gaelic.  pretending i\'m five again and exploring the city, sliding down big boulders, and watching the sun set while sitting by the train tracks.  pinball.  my shin guards.  bagpipes.  my favorite pub before i turned into an italian bistro and filled up with college brats.  listening to the music i liked before i got into punk.  making faces at babies.  the way good friends can sit in silence and feel comfortable with it.  trying new food.  peltier coolers.  the learning cathedral.  bob dobbs.  smoking a pipe.  spearmint...for some reason.  garlic laden meals.  guinness stew and shepards pie.  wow, put that way i guess i\'m happier than i give myself credit for.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1137,'<p>\r\nso i work at a college. what i do isnt really all that important for this story. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin alan guttmans office is a tv/vcr thing borrowed from the media thing on campus. they dont know we have it and its just this big joke in the office. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntoday mark went upto media studies to check up on something and he brought back a picture of the tv/vcr with a big \"HAVE YOU SEEN ME\" on it. he took it and we stuck it to the stand of the tv/vcr thing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow thats fucking hillarious.\r\n</p>'),(1138,'During a conversation about my parents and divorce:\r\n<p>\r\n[1 : dawn] so it goes, you know\r\n<br>\r\n[1 : dawn] divorce is certainly not (even close to) the worst thing in the world\r\n<br>\r\n[1 : d] no, but for kids it\'s a bad time.  it\'s like being a two dimensional being, and seeing a three dimensional creature.  you can\'t turly understand what\'s happening.  and you know it\'s happening to you but not really.\r\n<p>\r\nSo, there you go. Divorce. Yippie. '),(1139,'<p>Due to dissatisfying workplace conditions - ie, hating the nature of my responsibilities,  consistently wanting to commit violent acts on those around me, fantasizing about the public suicide i could punish them with, etc., I began today to brainstorm ideas for a new career.</p>\r\n<p>I didnt get much farther than the idea of having a peep show at home. Ive been to exactly one peep show, where you pay $5 to look at this girl\'s tits through a booth. I figured considering my difficult location and amateur qualifications that I would charge that for maybe, a titty petting booth.  I ran the idea by my roommates over pizza.</p>\r\n<p><b>jay</b>: what, youre gonna run it out the house?<br>\r\n<b>arwa</b>: hey, yeah, it\'s a \"live/work\" space.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>what, youre gonna have em line out the door, down the block...<br>\r\n<b>arwa</b>: ...do the saw mill guys get a discount?[sniffs nasal Vicks vapo rub] <br>\r\n<b>jay</b>:...and what, they\'re gonna wait outside the place on a leather couch, come in when their numbers are called, give you a five and then what squeeze your tits? do you realize you\'ll have to get 20 guys to squeeze your tits to make $100? YOUR TITS WILL GET DIRTY.</p>\r\n<p> Im not sure what the going rate is these days. But this conversation prompted me to write out some notes on my possible dream career.<p>\r\n<p>words + music<br>\r\nmusic + pictures<br>\r\npictures + words</p>\r\n<p> sounds like a peep show.</p>'),(1140,'<p>the other day I picked up a cheap copy of Sarah Vowel\'s book <a href=\'http://http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0684867974/ref=ase_thisamericanlife/104-1707066-7423919\'> Take the Cannoli</a> from the bookstore remainder table and pretty much immediately flipped to her essay \"Thanks for the Memorex.\" It\'s about the joy, rapture, despair of giving and receiving mix tapes. You may have heard it; it was broadcast on the radio program \"This American Life\" a few years ago and gets rebroadcast suprisingly regularly. </p>\r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<i>\"A homemade tape is a work of friendship, an act of love.\"</i>\r\n</center>\r\n\r\n<p>the essay reminds me of my recent archeological experience in the storage space under our apt. we have this towering stack of old boxes down there that I finally went through a few weeks ago. in the course of sorting out old clothes, books, appliances and papers to be either recycled or given to the mighty Salvation Army, I found my old tape collection, which these days generally stays out of sight, out of mind. among them were most of my mix tape masters. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I say \"most\" because sadly it appears that a few tapes have been lost. only the forlorn boxes remain, with lists of songs that i\'ll never hear in that order again. I haven\'t the heart to go dredge up all the source material for them and redo the tape. the tapes are now like records of another time and place--it just seems wrong to try to \"recreate\" them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I almost never made tapes without some listener in mind--a girlfriend, my favorite cafe barrista, my boss, even sometimes my dad. But more often than not, I\'d make the tape but would somehow never manage to send a dub to the music\'s intended target. I\'m not sure why that was.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>my earliest tape:</b> was made at age 16 for a girl I loved who never loved me back. I can\'t recall much about it except a pretty Depeche Mode song. I was so confused back then. I didn\'t make another mix for several years.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>themes:</b> sad drunken nighttime songs (tom waits, etc). lo-fi \"alternative\" pop (titled \"Twilight on Valencia Street\"). industrial/punk/free-jazz/Japanoise barrages. two mixes of dub (creaky old school dub vs. electronica-infused new school). unsuccesful trip-hop/trance comps. very successful Indian-influenced club music comps(titled \"Hypnogogic Ragas\"). ungodly mixes of film soundtracks and funk and african jazz. and so on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>tape i miss the most:</b> would date circa 1992. disappeared from my haight&ashbury apartment. it was a wonderful, incredible, shot-straight-to-the-heart eligiac tape. all i can recall about it now is that it had the best sequence of Pavement-->This Mortal Coil-->Big Star. i think either a roommate or a friend\'s girlfriend stole it, but never found any proof. i loved it so.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>tape that made someone else cry:</b> \"Famous American Pop Hits, Vol.1,\" which I made for my friend Chip just before he shipped off to teach English in Poland. he later told me the Bjork song \"Come To Me\" made him weep on the train to Warsaw. his polish friends loved the tape and made many copies. for all i know dubs of it continue to circulate there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now of course I\'ve caught up with the rest of the world, and make mix CDs. easier to duplicate, and the mix process is so much more efficient. but it also seems to lack a certain requisite level of effort, of invested time, and I just don\'t feel about them the same way. takes getting used to, i guess.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>what mix tape stories do you have?</p>\r\n'),(1141,'<p>\r\ni was laying flat on my back in my fort-bed \r\n</p><p>\r\n(the bed is in the middle of the room surrounded by furniture. i was painting the walls completely unrestful colors. i love sleeping amidst the chaos of stacked up furniture and fireball walls)\r\n</p><p>\r\nnaked, covered with a thin cotton blanket \r\n</p><p>\r\n(the kind of  blanket that oof complained about once, i think he said, <i>what is this, a blanket or a doily</i> but i could be remembering that wrong but it wouldn\'t matter because oof\'s memory is shite too. i could make up lies and he would have to agree with them.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe tibook, my only true love, was sleeping next to me, his little LED heart blinking dimly. outside, a truck was backing up. two trucks were backing up. <b>BEEP BEEP BEEP BEEP.</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni heard steve a room away, typing. i love steve.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe trucks kept backing up out there.  silence disturbed.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(i was going to start reading \"song of the lark\" tonight but i opened the book up and couldn\'t look at the words. what are these glyphs. i am too tired to make pictures out of words.)\r\n</p><p>\r\ni became annoyed at the beeping trucks. <b>i don\'t care to know when you put it in reverse!</b> i convulsed like a fish and yelled into the darkness of my room, but i was really yelling to steve, i yelled:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<I>WHAT THE FUCK?!?</i>\r\n</p><!-- <p>\r\nand this week, i know, this week has been a week of hell, no, it\'s easy to say hell. it\'s been joyous and intense and soaring i don\'t know. i\'ve been crying twice a day because that\'s what happens when you love someone you think they have to go away, but then i was in his bed again and... does he really have to go? i crouched over him, his dick in me naked, sobbing, some part of my cheek pressed to some part of his, crying: <i>i don\'t want you to go away</i>. is this codependency? is it? what is this? why do i feel this way? nevermind don\'t answer that. i\'ve become convinced today that no one can know but me. it\'s too ethereal.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut that process was only running real low level, the main thing was, the trucks, the beeping. so i yelled. remember? i yelled:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>WHAT THE FUCK!?!?</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\ninto the darkness but at steve. \r\n</p>--><p>\r\nin the other room, he chortled. i heard him pushing little muttley bursts of air out his nose. i could see, in my head, the vision of him i saw a few moments before - not a visage, an imagining, but i actually looked at him on the way back from the bathroom - lying in his purple bed naked or maybe wearing  boxer briefs, typing. \r\n</p><p>\r\nas he snortled a room over i felt some kind of intense childlike joy, we were siblings, or we were kids at a slumber party, i made him laugh i made him laugh at bedtime!\r\n</p><p>\r\npause, stare up at the ceiling, an aftershock fish convulse, and then steve responds:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>they\'re landing</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\noutside, the sounds through the window:\r\n</p><p>\r\nBEEP BEEP BEEP WHIIIRRRR BEEP BEEP BEEP *engine*\r\n</p><p>\r\nthey\'re landing out there.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni grabbed my titanium lover, clutched at his rectangle of possibility: to tell you.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<!-- whoops, the words started coming out the wrong direction, coming OUT, i couldn\'t look at words only a moment before on a page in a book and now they\'re coming OUT? why can\'t i contain a goddamn thing.\r\n</p><p>-->\r\n'),(1142,'<p>\r\ncan\'t sleep<br'),(1143,'<p>\r\ncan\'t sleep. toss, turn. adjust blanket. cough. pollen. cough.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<blockquote><i>\r\ntomorrow you will call me, from across the room, on the phone, and i  won\'t notice. instead i\'ll turn over and cover my head with a pillow and curse and blame.</i>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni stare at the ceiling and nothing changes. the ceiling is white, white, white, flat and white. i imagine bumps and ripples in the white but the white is still white. textureless, static white.\r\n</p><p>\r\n... waiting ...\r\n</p><p>\r\n<blockquote><i>\r\ntomorrow i will pick you up late, as i always do, no surprises. you\'ll feel stressed and so will i. but everything\'s okay.</i>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p><p>\r\nevery part feels wrong, is wrong. itchy or dry or thirsty: lotion will help. water will help. blanket off. turn down music. turn up music. different position. sleep on stomach. sleep on back. sleep on stomach. sleep on side. curl up in ball ... yes, this.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<blockquote><i>\r\ntomorrow you will tell me things; a realization, an update, a forgotten truth, a longing. i\'m not with you but i\'ll say the right thing regardless. you won\'t notice.</i>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p><p>\r\neyes spring open. the ceiling. the book casually dropped by the bed. the laptop. the lotion bottle. the book. the cat. the laptop.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwaiting.\r\n</p>'),(1144,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1144_1.jpg\'>\r\nI count the drops of water from an<br>\r\nover head pipe, passed through<br>\r\nJune air into a crackling bucket.<br>\r\nTenatively, my feet pad over tiles, broken, ingrown<br>\r\nwith hardy springtime moss.<br>\r\nI stand, a pediment of marble before me<br>\r\non which laps the waves of evaporating puddles,<br>\r\nstolen away in a single breath,<br>\r\nand a trickle of water touches my fingertip.<br>\r\nEffluence from a stony facade condenses from<br>\r\nthe arcane reactions of weather, or perhaps<br>\r\nanonymous tears,<br>\r\nshed by some faceless monolith for me.<br>'),(1145,'<p>Jeremy came at me with words and I had to hit him in the face.   The blow broke his nose and blood ran down over his lips and chin.  He smiled at me and said it didn\'t hurt so I jabbed at his mouth which broke a couple of teeth.  He still has those broken teeth (no money for dental work), they\'re jagged and make for an  irregular grin that works on a certain type of woman (luckily, the kind of women that Jeremy likes.  Somehow we\'re still friends.  When we\'re drunk enough, I\'ll buy him a Guiness and a Bushmill\'s as an apology for breaking his teeth and we drink \'em down in memory of the fight.  We don\'t even remember, neither one of us, what the fight was about  Of course, it isn\'t long until we\'re fighting again, smashing chairs and tables, thowing beer bottles at each other.  I don\'t think there\'s any bar in Cleveland that we haven\'t been thrown out of and we\'ve had to drink in progressively seedier bars as time has progressed.  The police are sometimes involved.  I guess that some people are scared by our antics, but the fights aren\'t real anymore.  They\'re a shadow of that one big fight.  More like playing really; nobody really gets hurt and nothing more than a couple of bumps and bruises come out of it.  It\'s too bad that I can\'t remember what that fight was about.  I\'m sure it was something dumb though, some little thing that beer and whiskey blurred from minor slight into gigantic relevance. But then it could have been a woman, and you know how that goes don\'t you?  Women change everything.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1146,'<pre>\r\n\r\nThese are only words and phrases\r\ntaken  out of a box\r\nand nailed to a wall.\r\n\r\nMy thumb is bleeding.  \r\n\r\nMistakes happen.  \r\n\r\nI am Trying to create a lanugage outside myself, rather than the usual.\r\n\r\nWords are like bridges, toungless.\r\n\r\nI put on my oldest shirt.  The one with\r\ncharacter rips\r\naround the collar from where it\'s been pulled over my big head\r\ncountless times.\r\n\r\nAt the party\r\nSomebody said that \r\nyou should sew up the holes for me\r\n\r\nand we\r\nlaughed\r\nsilently, shaking out heads,\r\ndisagreeing.\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(1147,'i have diabetes.  diabetes sucks.  you gotta stick your finger with a sharp object and drip your blood on a little computer chip blood sucker thing and stick it in a glucose meter to see if you are passing or failing, and you have to do it all the damned time and sometimes you can\'t get any blood out so you have to drink a bunch of water and massage your hands so you can have the pleasure of stabbing them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nso, i have been failing the tests.  my dr. gave me some new meds.  and now i have to check my sugar every hour for three days.  and i am STILL failing the damned tests.  and now dad tells me he dropped the fucking glucose meter and it is prolly broken and that is why my sugar says 182 when i am feeling faint with hunger.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am not amused.  my fingers are full of holes and i am really, really not amused.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow there goes the clock and i gotta do it again.  i believe i will resign.'),(1148,'<p>Once upon a time, I bent my finger back and broke it  just to see what it felt like.  It hurt.  It hurt like a mother fucker.  At the time I didn\'t have the wherewithal to go to the doctor so it healed badly and, now, my little finger points out from my hand like a strange tree branch, pointing in a funny, errant direction.  When I drink a pint of beer, I look like a dandy sipping from a bone china tea cup.  The kind of bars in which I drink donâ€™t look friendly on this seeming affectation and I end up with my fists raised in the back alleys, or fighting in the gutters.  Cleveland is a hard city, a hard poor, mean city and the men here are bitter and old at twenty-four years old.  They canâ€™t fight though.  None of them.  I canâ€™t tell you how many times Iâ€™ve mopped the dirty alley floors with a mans beard and I canâ€™t tell you how many times Iâ€™ve been jumped by gangs of guys seeking revenge.  Iâ€™ve been clubbed with baseball bats, hit with boards and one time I was stabbed in the gut.  Itâ€™s true that a gut wound hurts more than hell itself, but you donâ€™t lose blood as quickly as you think you would.  I donâ€™t know.  \r\n\r\n<p>I drink a lot.  Beer and whiskey. Thereâ€™s not to much to do in this city, its burned out and broken.  Thereâ€™s more unemployment here than in any other American city.  I donâ€™t know why I stay here.  My friends are here, I guess:  Jeremy, Baz and Fred.  We go to the bars and drink until we canâ€™t see and then staggering home, falling from parking meter to parking meter, we sing songs to the big Midwestern moon.  If you havenâ€™t seen the moon from Ohio, you havenâ€™t really seen the moon, my friends.  Itâ€™s bigger out here.  I donâ€™t know why.  Iâ€™ve been to California and the moon seems diminished somehow.  Maybe itâ€™s Californiaâ€™s proximity to the ocean, the celestial body overwhelmed by such a big body of water, but the moon gets lost out there.  The California moon actually seems smaller.  I could of course be prejudiced, or maybe Iâ€™m just a starr-eyed, romantic drunk, but I love my Ohio moon.\r\n\r\n<p>I donâ€™t expect any of you to really understand.  Reading your journals on line here, your all considerably younger than me and pretty idealistic.  I donâ€™t think youâ€™ve been unemployed or tasted the bitter pill of unemployment and real, hard, depression.  You the kind of optimism of people who have never known what its like to be hungry or depressed, to have lost a friend to a drunken bastard with a handgun.  Itâ€™s nice to abstain from meat when you have the luxury, but when you havenâ€™t had food in three or four days, a hamburger is the best goddamn thing youâ€™ve ever tasted.\r\n\r\n<p>I suppose Iâ€™m not that much older than you, really.   Maybe by six or seven years.   And, in comparison to some of the people around me Iâ€™m lucky to be sitting at this computer and working for a company on the East Coast.  Iâ€™m lucky that I went to college.  So I donâ€™t have much to complain about.  I choose to live here in Cleveland when I could as easily be in Baltimore, but I donâ€™t want that.  I love this place.  I guess the reason I feel so reactionary and violent is that you are all so fucking innocent.  What do you know if you havenâ€™t gone through hard times, if you havenâ€™t had your head smashed into a brick wall a number of times.  I mean that both figuratively and literally.  A cracked cranium teaches you a fine lesson, if you goddamn survive it. \r\n\r\n<p>Kids, youâ€™d be scared to live in my world.  I sit behind a computer like most of you probably do, but thatâ€™s were my white collar ends.  Iâ€™m blue collar to the core, babies, and with that comes a lot of shit and problems that youâ€™ve never had to deal with.  In Cleveland you really canâ€™t escape it.  And why would you want to. Weâ€™ve got that big, beautiful moon shining down on us once a month.  Iâ€™m sorry, not even the Golden Gate bridge can beat that.  \r\n'),(1149,'<P>I have a lot of nervous energy, like I\'ve had 10 pots of coffee today, in just a few short hours. I\'m clacking on my Mac keyboard, wanting le to get her webcam back up so I can see random shots of SF that I miss because I\'m here and not there. Why am I here? I don\'t know- Id rather be there. My feet are bouncing up and down and my hands are shaking. al;ksjdf;as</P>'),(1150,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1150_1.jpg\'>\r\n<BR>\r\n<br>\r\n\"...Move to Brooklyn and you\'ll practically live on the train...\" but I pack my life into boxes, neatly, and the shade flaps as a breeze leaks over my beaded brow, evaporating the sweat of this painful time of growth.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nShouldering my load, I trod the cobbles, purging my empathies in measured breaths.  A wailing siren, a diesel grunt sings out; a sorrowful sigh amidst a punchy, nauseating anguish, and I hesitate too long before raising my hand to block the noise from my ringing ear.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWho can silence the voice of the city?  Its tongues call to me in veiled timbres, preaching the blackness of the pavement, harbingers of a melting sun they are, gilding rhymes from the click-clack of the passing subway train.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI enter the station, a shower of blue-white sparks arcing over my head as the noon sun half-cascades onto me, its heat irradiating, hot solar fire.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\"That which burns the body ignites the soul.\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWith another step, I\'m south-bound, and the clouds slip away, bringing shady solace to someone.  Anyone.  Anyone but me.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n'),(1151,'<P><TT>\r\nI donâ€™t even know where I was last night.  Jeremy and I were over at his place drinking cheap Scorsbey scotch.   We killed that bottle then, with nothing else in the apartment, we hit the streets.  It was cloudy last night, but dark and humid.  Pools of light had gathered at the foot of the lampposts.  Jeremy was singing something.  I didnâ€™t recognize the tune so I joined in with my own.  \r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nWe found our way to Debâ€™s Bar.  Deb was there, tending bar as usual.  She yelled at us soon as we came in.  â€œShut the door!â€  And we shut it. \r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nDebâ€™s Bar is on the edge of chaos at all times.  The men that drink there drink hard and deep.  They are disturbed men; some are unemployed, others collect disability and social security.  Most are burned out and shattered by something, bad family lives and marriages show like roadmaps on their faces.  Some of them have been driven mad by drink.  They are quick to anger; bump into one of them and you may start a fight.  But Deb rules the place with an iron hand.  At the first sign of trouble she has you shuttled  out of the bar.  You could be getting into somebodyâ€™s face, bumping chests and the next thing you know youâ€™re blinking in the moonlit street.  The drunks are on Debâ€™s side and they help out.  She pours their drinks strong and they like it that way.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nGay Frank was their last night.  Heâ€™s an ugly man that looks like a ferret.  His thin, ruined teeth are crooked and yellow.  A little John Waters mustache on his lip looks more like  fungus.  We sat next to Frank and he grinned at Jeremy.  Weâ€™ve always thought he has a little thing for Jeremy, but since Frank doesnâ€™t really do more than a bit of innocent flirting Jeremy just plays along.  Frank may be gay but that doesnâ€™t make him any less dangerous.  Once, Jeremy and I saw him slash a guy named Jerzy in the arm with a long, curved knife that he carries in his boot.  The knife was immaculately clean, stainless steel and it glinted even in the low barroom light.  It wasnâ€™t a bad cut, but it required 23 stitches and left a nasty scar.  All Jerzy did was call Frank a fag.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nAnyway, Frank bought Jeremy and me a beer.  I donâ€™t know where he got the money and didnâ€™t want to know.  It was cheap beer.  It tasted like the kind of stuff at the bottom of a Coors can after it sat in the sun for eight hours.  But it was beer.  Frank was saying something about his favorite movie, Mel Brooksâ€™ â€œThe Producers.â€  Frank said that heâ€™d heard it was on Broadway and it was his newest dream to go to NYC and see it.  Neither me or Jeremy had seen the flick and we told Frank as much.  \r\n</TT></P><P><TT>â€œYou should come over to my apartment,â€ Frank said.  â€œWe can watch it on video.â€\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nIt didnâ€™t seem like that great of an idea, what with Frankâ€™s crush on Jeremy and all.   But Frank said he had Scotch.  He had good stuff, Dewars or Chivas Regal.  He didnâ€™t remember because he had swiped it from a liquor store a couple of days ago and was saving it for a special occasion.  That convinced us.  We had a few more beers, drank some of Debâ€™s heavy-handed pours of Scorsbey and wended our way over to Frankâ€™s place.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nFrank lived in a shithole one-bed.  A crappy flowered sofa that looked like it had been there since Nixon was president was pushed against one wall and an old black-and-white with a tinfoil antenna sat under a cracked window.  Frank found the bottle and a couple of coffee cups. He poured the whiskey into the cups and handed to them.  â€œSit down.  Sit down.â€  He said.  â€œIâ€™ll put the movie on.â€  He moved like a hummingbird, obviously excited to have guests.  Probably more excited because Jeremy was there.  In a way it was sad.  Poor Frank wasnâ€™t going to get any action, even though he probably thought it was a sure bet.\r\n\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nâ€œThe Producersâ€ was the funniest fucking movie I have ever seen.  We all laughed and cackled.  I donâ€™t usually like movies, but that fucker cracked me up.  We watched that movie and when it was over we just sat there drinking scotch.  When we killed that bottle Frank produced another and we dug into that.  At some point Frank fell asleep.  It was getting light outside.   Jeremy went through Frankâ€™s wallet and found twenty bucks.  I told him that he should leave it, that Frank needed the money more than he did, but Jeremy is a bastard when heâ€™s drunk.  He pocked the money and grinned at me with that fucked-up mug of his.  \r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nâ€œHe aint never gonna know he had it, so it doesnâ€™t really matter does it?â€  Jeremy said, turning towards the door.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nWhen Jeremy left, I looked back at Gay Frank who was probably saving that $20 for his breakfast.  Frank had slumped forward and was drooling on his chest.  I took a $20 and a $5 out of my pocket and put it on the table under the remainder of the scotch.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nOutside Jeremy was gone.   The sun was bleaching the sky.   I lit up a cigarette and took a deep, good drag.  Then I came home and wrote this.\r\n</TT></P>'),(1152,'Each hour, fresh pain.<br>\r\nBoxing class has left me sore.<br>\r\nJab, punch, hook. Who\'s bad.'),(1153,'<p>I did the wallet.  Taking its inventory was a relieving exercise in efficiency- it let me get rid of alot of the <i>stuff</i> in it, some bad memories; to savor some good ones, to enjoy some thoughs, and to do a much needed cleaning-out. So, now for the bag.  My apologies in advance to all of you who find this sort of thing tedious- I understand, but this sort of exercise makes it easy for me to clean out...<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1153_1.jpg\' border=\"1\" width=\"350\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>Contents of my bag</b> as of 6/20/01:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n1 Work ID badge<br>\r\n1 radiation exposure badge<br>\r\n1 cd walkman (delerium\'s \"silence\" inside)<br>\r\n2 sharpie markers<br>\r\n1 pay-stub<br>\r\n1 toothbrush<br>\r\n1 journal<br>\r\n1 keychain with random keys<br>\r\n1 check book<br>\r\n1 NYC dog lisence (i found it on the sidewalk)<br>\r\n1 crumpled pile of receipts<br>\r\n1 flashing reflector clip-on<br>\r\n1 green plastic toy army-man (bazooka man)<br>\r\n1 orange-colored journal<br>\r\n1 datebook<br>\r\n1 handfull of change (72 cents)<br>\r\n3 barettes (?)<br>\r\n2 pkgs certs<br>\r\n1 pkg altoids<br>\r\n1 tin with lighter and rolling papers<br>\r\n1 headphones<br>\r\n1 Lomo<br>\r\n2 rolls film (b&w; slide)<br>\r\n3 cd-r\'s<br>\r\n2 midi cables<br>\r\n1 umbrella<br>\r\n1 scrawled-on pad of paper<br>\r\n2 diskettes<br>\r\n1 rose-head nail<br>\r\n1 cell phone<br>\r\n1 stack of photos<br>\r\n1 bottle rubber cement<br>\r\n3 club invites<br>\r\n1 stanton cartridge without stylus<br>\r\n3 aaa batteries<br>\r\n1 pc. driftwood<br>\r\n1 deoderant<br>\r\n1 superglue<br>\r\n2 ball-point pens<br>\r\n1 book (Jude the Obscure)<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'ve been trashing alot of this stuff as I\'ve catalogued it here, basically my motive for doing it to begin with.  Thanks for indulging me, you all.<br><br>\r\n'),(1154,'<P>Today was long and now i contain no soul. This dusk is protest against control.\r\n\r\n<P>Sleeping is just the desire not to walk anymore. A pause in a search.\r\n\r\n<P>You awake to slap in the face another day without a female orgasm within reach. You know that poetry will not get you into the correct position to hunt for orgams. \r\n\r\n<P>Not today.\r\n\r\n<P>I was born to ignore your post.\r\n\r\n<P>You seems to feel for a moment that you are an edifice of creativity. This is your mind, where sometimes human is spoken. \r\n\r\n<P>Sometimes you come to this place, were rule number one is Ignore The Audience. You are self important. You matter. You will be heard.\r\n\r\n<P>Be obnoxious. It will get you noticed. Be weirder, become more readable. Mundane? Make it bold.\r\n\r\n<P>In the end you wake up a human. Orginality is running low.'),(1155,'<p>I wake sometimes 6:30am but usually 7:00am.  I leave the house an hour before my class, and I stop for decaf coffee and sometimes a bagel. I usually ride my bike to school, but this morning my legs wouldnâ€™t pedal me. I took my car. It takes longer to drive than it does to ride my bike to school.</p><p> I am walking lightly up to the steps of the school. Realizing that I am early, as usual. I smile at the pretty, young girls in their fancy clothes. I smile at the pretty boys talking on the cell phones. I say hello to an elderly woman walking her Chihuahua. She smiles back. I enter class, tired but awake. Lecture starts and my mind goes numb. I always day dream in my c++ class instead of listening to the lecture. I doodle in my notebook, doing these looping exercises that my father taught me to improve my handwriting when I was a child. I listen to the chatter in the halls on break, talk to my classmates about the dryness of the course we are taking. </p><p>Lab time consists of me checking my empty email box, and checking for posts from my friends on swinneydotorg. I log out, and get back into my steamy black car, melting in the seat. I drive toward home, still daydreaming. Daydreaming about what my next 26 years will bring me. Rent a movie, delicatessen, and return home.</p><p> It is only 11am. Maybe a nap, maybe some food, maybe check the email again. I watch the movie, take a nap, wake up for a while, and take another nap. Today is my day off. Wait, every day has been day off. My job, school. </p><p>Later in the day, I drift back to early morning thoughts about a dream I had, and thoughts that I had while sipping on coffee, massaging my soar legs. I am sitting, thinking, remembering; this is what we humans do. Now, it is night time, roommates home, ice-cream and beer (not a good combination). Tonight is a night for ice cream, but it is also a night for watching a movie with my friends next door. Tomorrow wonâ€™t vary much from today, nor Friday. </p>'),(1156,'<p>i was wrong.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>your freckles and moles don\'t draw a map lacking in highways and lines.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>looking closely under these dim hotel lights, i can see the sun setting over a city.  your hair flat against the pillow, arms spread wide welcoming me, i can see its expanse... i can see its alleys.</p><p>\r\n\r\nfourth street must be your liver.  secreting the bile onto the streets to search for itself.  a dollop of creme and a bit more sugar in the coffee.  hours spent peeling the paint from a table.  the urge to create overridden by the ease of stagnation.</p><p>\r\n\r\njust south your river flows warm and silent.  what has been lost to its dark depths?  what has been caused to arise?</p><p>\r\n\r\nunder soft halos of eveningtide lights, i can drive around your city marveling at the minute differences of your epiderm.  smoothness here, rough edges there.  a bus stop to nowhere.</p><p>\r\n\r\ngliding along, i run my tongue up your drag past the crest of the tower... in the distance the capitol shines through the trees.</p><p>\r\n\r\nhere in the heart i can travel through your arteries to separate sections of the city and further.  </p><p>\r\n\r\ncontinuing northwest i curve into your arm, into that freeway vein flowing endlessly into the darker and darker night... past the bottom of your palm outside the limits.  </p><p>\r\n\r\nyour fingers barely touching seward junction.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1157,'<P><B>SWINNEY, AUSTIN : 14 JUNE 2001</B></P>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_1.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_2.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_3.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p>'),(1158,'<P>charles bronson kicks ass.  he is a big man.  he is bigger than darwin.  bigger than incitor.  bigger than griece.  bronson is THE MAN.  he never looses his temper.  ever.  in this movie, mr majestyc, he clenched his fists when the mob bozos shot up his mellons.  but that was it.  the rest of the time, it was just business -- the business of revenge.  bronson is at play in the fields of the lord with a shotgun.  he doesnt give hoot about pistols.  give the man a shotgun and he will use it on humans, even if it means he has to shoot \'em twice.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>rowen is sniffing at the window.  he wants to come in and snarfle me.  rowin is my mang\'s best friend.  my main mang.  big rowen.  as far as dogs go, he is a goof ass.  but as far as humans go, so am i.  we get allong well.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i interviewed at a co-op today.  the rent there is an absurdly low 360 a month.  the place has co-op couches.  the nasty kind.  you dont want to be naked humping with your girl on co-op couches.  no you dont.  but for this price, you can go rent a motel room and although the quality and sanitation wont be good, twill be a step up.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the people at the co-op seemed cool.  i completely hid from them the fact that i know what computers do, or anything about the internet.  i told them i write pamplets for planned parenthood.  and that i am austin from austin.  i introduce myself to so many people these days that i started some time ago honing a myth for myself.  a collection of sketchy facts that have some degree of truth, but that the details have been lost in time.  the object of this myth is to endeer myself with others; with holding the complexities of my life.  i did not in this case think it in my best interest that they think i one of those overpaid internet geeks.  really i am not overpaid anyway.  but i dont want to be associated with what ever economic factors led to the insane cost of living in the bay area.  frankly i never participated in any of that.  i just got here.  and for geeking, i am no good at that either.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am going to see this imax movie about the sun this weekend with some girl.  i cant wait.  i love the imax.  easing back into the reclined seats and fastening my eyes open with clamps like in a clockwork orange, there is no better mang.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i want rowen to sleep at the foot of my bed.  me and rowen.  a couple of goof asses.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1159,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1159_1.jpg\' width=\"300\" border=\"1\">'),(1160,'<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_1.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_2.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1160_3.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p>'),(1161,'<p>i\'m here in a mall, and in the visited links your website appears to have been browsed here before!!  swinney is going around the world.</p>'),(1162,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1162_1.jpg\'><BR>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nI like little things<br>\r\nand search for little ways<br>\r\nto compartmentalize them,<br>\r\nto store them away<br>\r\nfor a time when my mind can relax,<br>\r\nto curl about the seemingly insignificant,<br>\r\nand with joy, place myself among little things<br>\r\nneatly compartmentalized<br>\r\nand saved for another day<br>\r\nin sturdy boxes<br>\r\n<br>\r\n'),(1163,'What a a busy night. Rage, violence, blood-letting, alienation from all people loved, dying house plants...  Woke up refreshed and wanting to hold and stroke all my kids -- to nuzzle, groom, nibble at. I have experienced a far greater range of emotions in dreams than while awake. Xep -- do you cry in your dreams? '),(1164,'What a busy night. Rage, howling, blood-letting, insanity, alienation from all things loved, dying house plants. Woke up feeling refreshed and ready to go kick butt at a union meeting. Xep, do you cry in your dreams?'),(1165,'What a busy night. Rage, grief, alienation from all things loved, insanity and dying house plants. Woke up this morning feeling refreshed and wanting to hug, nibble at and groom all my children. Xep, do you cry in your dreams?'),(1166,'<p>I was sitting here at work minding my own business. and this girl I work with walked by. and she is wearing a tight white tank top. and she has enormous tits. they tremble just slightly while she is walking.</p>\r\n<p>I did not turn my head to see the other parts.</p>\r\n<p>I am tingling.</p>\r\n<p>yesterday le came to visit me. she had been in the heat. she smelled salty and her cheeks were shiny. that made me want an iced coffee drink, and to touch myself.</p> \r\n<p>instead of bangs I wear a sun hat to keep the elements (read, \"boy\" \"strangers with candy\" etc) from getting involved with me. I wear a stringy tank top with jeans every day. that is my summertime outfit.</p>\r\n<p>also, I bring my shiny blue bathing suit to work with me every day, in case i get laid off. I will call homies and we will go directly to the water. do not pass go. do not collect $200.</p> \r\n<p>I want to share cold drinks on my roof, and jump up and down in water, and run my hand over some boy\'s sticky waist.</p>\r\n<p> I have enormous tits. they shake slightly when im walking. like jello on springs.</p>\r\n<p> let\'s go to the beach. let\'s sit on the porch and drink sangria. let\'s peel off clothes and lay in the river. let\'s make near-pornographic but artistically valid visual media and tack it up all over the city, project it onto the bushes, broadcast in the park.</p>\r\n'),(1167,'<pre>terrific is derivative of terror<br>\r\n  and in the end\r\n  silently wasting\r\n  like blistered skin\r\n i cease to fight the demon that i am\r\n  what used to be light and sound\r\n  became articles of neglect\r\n  became sinks full of crusted dishes\r\n  became entropy and disregard\r\n here, on this armageddon precipice\r\n   village armies of doubt\r\n  and negation threw down\r\n   their arms\r\n\r\n and the life in this body\r\n  i have spent\r\n  seeking that conversation\r\n  from one with eyes more\r\n   black contusions\r\n  than transluscent\r\n  windows of direct knowledge\r\n  every day spent chasing the piper\r\n    spent tracing the steps of terrific\r\n      failures\r\n    spent excavating the ruins\r\n      of the equally hapless\r\n   in search of the quadriatic equation\r\n    that explains how energy\r\n   of essentially identical\r\n   quantum components\r\n    is good food \r\n     on one side of my bowels\r\n    and shit \r\n     on the other\r\n\r\n o tiny spider\r\n you traverse my notebook at\r\n   your peril\r\n  and i can\'t be held responsible\r\n   should you refuse this offer of escape\r\n    by my pen,\r\n   if when i close these leaves\r\n    however unwittingly\r\n     i crush and kill you\r\n\r\ni know of you, death\r\ni have experienced you more times than\r\n   16 infinities\r\nyou alone keep your word\r\n neither protesting nor gloating\r\n but certain, secret and seminal\r\n the reward for all things composite\r\nyou humbly plod along\r\n   conquering ceaselessly\r\n\r\n it\'s going to be all right\r\n it\'s going to be all right\r\n it\'s going to be all right\r\n it\'s going to be all right\r\n it\'s going to be all right\r\nhe mutters the phrase noiselessly\r\neach utterance, a derivative of terror\r\n and a lie\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1168,'lying back on the couch trying to read the technical service manual for my new motorcycle.  i need to know the fork size.  right.  fork size.  ok.  forks... frame.. ok.. forks... and the music is playing... and it\'s a little overhwhelming.  it\'s taking me away.  it\'s creating the lump in my throat that is so familiar but so misleading.  it\'s the crying that only comes from happiness and being so insanely overhwelmed by beauty that you feel like you\'re going to collapse... you\'re going to implode into nothingness because you\'re so small compared to the forces of the universe. it\'s more than a little overwhelming now.  it\'s taken over completely.  i am powerless.\r\n<br><br>\r\nand the only response i have is to walk over to the stereo and hit the back button because i have to hear that song again.  because i want to feel overwhelmed again.  but it\'s not the same the second time.  it\'s a power that has to take you by surprise.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nif you ask for it you can\'t have it. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1168_1.jpg\'>'),(1169,'<p>sitting on the stoop. the sweat from my ice tea drips down. a pattern of summer life. you run your hand across the back of my neck. hot sweet stickiness. june bugs humming. we watch the fireflies dance. and talk of who we saw today. billy threehats rides his bike by. down on the corner. all the locals are there. i rise in the heat. like fresh bread. sultry with melted butter. i want you to eat me. taste me. smell me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but its enough to just sit here. on the stoop. listening. watching the world go by.</p>'),(1170,'<P>\r\nDesmond loves Amanda.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI would like to say more than this, but how am I to describe the look that is now in Desmondâ€™s eyes as he watches Amanda now from the bushes across the street from her house.  Dreamy, glassy-eyed?  I could say these things, but they border on clichÃ©.  It is not fit or proper to discuss Desmond\'s deep emotions in this way, the nuance of his love can not be described in terms of clichÃ©s.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAmanda\'s house is yellow with painted white eves.  There always seems to be a lawn sprinkler going out front, drenching the green, green grass.  The house is surrounded by a white picket fence.   If you closed your eyes and thought of a perfect suburban house, you\'d see Amanda\'s house.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n(This is the kind of story that does no favors for the writer.  How do I avoid clichÃ© when things are so standard-issue.  It\'s as if I was assembling a jigsaw puzzle or placing Colorforms on a predetermined scene.)\r\n</P><P>\r\nLast night Desmond baked a turkey.  It was on sale at the market for only twenty dollars so Desmond took it home an cooked it up.  He had to call his mother and get a recipe, but when he set to work he seemed to have natural affinity for preparing the bird.  He was an instinctive chef.   \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhile the bird was cooking, he set in on his first bottle of red wine, to Desmond wine is the most romantic of intoxicating liquors.  He raised his glass and pictured Amanda, her long sculpted neck and the scope of her shoulders.  He finished the first bottle and uncorked a second, drinking this time directly from the bottle.\r\n</P><P>\r\nDesmond did not sleep until after he took the bird from the oven and he set it out to cool.  And then he only slept for a few hours, dreaming dreams of the big Thanksgiving meals he ate with the family he left behind so many years ago.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen he finally woke-up, Desmond wrapped the still-warm bird in tin foil and stuck it in his backpack.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe early morning light was just slipping over the neighborhood.  As Desmond walked the familiar path to Amanda\'s, he felt as if they were the only two people in the world.  This thing he was doing was natural and perfect.  It would, no doubt, bring them inevitably together.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen he came to Amanda\'s yellow and white house, he slipped up to the porch, took the turkey out of his bag and laid it on her porch.  Then he stole across to the bushes and hid himself .  \r\n</P><P>\r\nDesmond is in the bushes now.  It is where we first met him, back at the beginning of this story.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe sun in now higher in the sky.  People have backed their cars out of driveways on their way to work.  It is summer, so some of the neighborhood kids have been by, playing in that obsessive way that children play in the summer, with a kind of reckless abandon, running this way and that because at their age, summer feels magical and endless.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Do you see what I mean?  Do you see how this story dwells on clichÃ©â€™s and doesn\'t let go?  I have tried to steer it away from these things because you have read about them before.  But here they come again.  How do you avoid it?  ClichÃ©s are clichÃ©s because there is truth in them.  ---Ah, this is bad.  I need a drink about now, a real one.  Whiskey, a writerâ€™s drink.)\r\n</P><P>\r\nDown the street a little boy has brought out a plastic bat and a wiffle ball.  He sets the ball on a stand and whacks it hard.  From his vantage in the bushes, Desmond can hear the collision of plastic-on-plastic.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThen Amanda, suddenly, opens the door.  She sees the wrapped bird lying on the porch and perhaps smells it.  Her head tilts slightly back, her perfect neck stretches, her golden hair sweeps behind her head, the morning sun catches her golden hair and it shines, shines, shines.  Desmond is enthralled, motionless.  She is perfect, absolute; the world around her has made space for her beauty; she is carved into the air itself.  Amanda looks up and down the block with perfect, questioning eyes.   \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe little boy has set up his ball again.  He takes a swing and the plastic again connects. His little body spins around with the force of his swing. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe ball catches a current and glides into the air, rising higher and higher, reaching heights heretofore unknown.\r\n</P></FONT>'),(1171,'<PRE>\r\nIn that bar I tired to hard to be somebody else and ended up\r\nin the back alley with my fist curled in front of me, screaming \r\nprofanities  FUCK YOU MOTHERFUCKER, the beer and whiskey \r\nin my gut someplace, \r\ngiving me anotherworld of courage.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI ducked the first punch and followed-up into his gut and as he\r\n came forward I brought my knee into connection with his nose.  \r\n\r\nI felt it GIVE WAY on impact.\r\nHe was thrown backwards to the dirty asphalt, \r\nhis hands clutched at that fountain of red.  \r\nThere were cheers from those who had gathered to watch.\r\n\r\nWe left him there \r\nbecause nobody likes a loser.\r\n\r\nBack at the bar, a drink was in front of me.  \r\nI drank it off and there was another.  \r\n\r\nI was a hero.  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAs far as I knew, he was still back there, bleeding.  \r\nA good blow to the nose could drive the bone back up into the brain.  \r\n\r\nChrist, he could be dead \r\n\r\nWhen I was in school people like him used to beat the shit out of me on the playground.  \r\nI\'d find myself thrown in garbage cans; \r\nmy face shoved into toilet bowls and flushed.\r\n\r\nI was to weak and scared to do anything about it so it went on.  \r\nRationally, I knew that these guys were bullies.  If I stood up to them, they\'d go away.  \r\nBut I was scared.  \r\nYou can\'t doubt the power of fear in a kids psyche.  \r\nIt paralyzed me.\r\n\r\nSo I\'d find myself head first in a pile of shit.\r\nI\'d get up, wipe it off and say nothing.\r\n\r\nIn the barroom Iâ€™m a fucking hero.\r\nMan you can really duke it, someone said.  \r\nI had a few more free drinks and then went out into the night.\r\n\r\nThe stars up there in the sky were exploding with ancient light.  \r\nI lit up a cigarette and took a long, cocky drag.  \r\n\r\nAll that light, the light of the centuries, \r\nrained down on my shoulders.\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1172,'<P> Exactly what is going on here?  Okay, I was told to write here by a certain Matt Bless, lately of <i>Fight</i> magazine, but I have arrived to a certain musical blackness, inkier than the insides of my soul, blanker than the blankness of my gestures.  Exactly what is going on here?</P>\r\n<br>\r\n<P>To this end I dedicate this galimatias, this nothing, this monument.  Thank you Matt.</P>\r\n<br>\r\nendscript'),(1173,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni cut this out and stuck it in my wallet and forgot about it- and it has been sitting there since april.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni thought it was kinda neet, and maybe a little telling.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1173_1.jpg\'>\r\n</p>'),(1174,'Well, today, I muse, what is one supposed to do on the bus ride to\r\nwork?  I suppose, I could look around the bus for ideas.  A balding\r\nguy sits reading a book.  Most everyone else just sits staring ahead.\r\nPerhaps they don\'t have to ride all the way to the end of the bus.\r\nAnother passenger sits and listens to music on headphones.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nStaring ahead on the bus, just seems, so blah and an unproductive\r\nwaste of time.  It is neat to actually actively <B>look</B> around. I\r\nsuppose I could read, but I get motion sickness pretty badly.  (I was\r\nfighting it with the palm pilot too.  With the laptop I seem to not be\r\ngetting it because I can look away from the screen as I type.)  As for\r\nmusic, as soon as I get some MP3s on the laptop, I can music on the\r\nway to work if I choose to be that isolated.<P>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nBut then I wouldn\'t get to hear the amazing quietness when the bus\r\ncompletely stops.  Yay electric bus, despite being old and crotchety\r\nand occasionally getting unplugged and etc.  Or the sounds of the city\r\nthat tell me I\'m getting close to downtown.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nOr, there is the socially questionable activity of talking on one\'s\r\ncellphone.  This just happened to me, as my friend Lisa called... I\r\ncouldn\'t NOT answer, she\'s getting ready to go to France for the\r\nsummer, and we\'d been playing phone tag (with her being busy packing,\r\nand dealing with the timezone difference and my whack-ass sleep\r\nschedules.)  <P>\r\n\r\nAs hinted at above, work finally got me my laptop, and I\'m going to\r\nwork, so I have to take the laptop with me anyway, so the dorkfish in\r\nme says that the only reasonable thing to do is sit on the bus and\r\nwrite for swinney.org.  I suppose if I were a productive little worker\r\nbee I could do work on the bus in the morning.  Perhaps I will do work\r\non the way home.  <P>\r\n\r\nAlso, yay job.  I don\'t have any money yet, and I don\'t know how much\r\nI will get paid on the 31st (silly taxes)... And god, the job is so\r\nboring.  Reviewing other people\'s code for security holes.  The\r\ncompany is called AsTech.  As I\'ve told many people, it should\r\nprobably be called AssTech.  I shall continue looking for a better\r\njob.  And in the meantime they did give me this laptop and pay me\r\nludicrous money to look for security holes in other people\'s code.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nThe bus ride to work takes me around my neighborhood a little bit, by\r\nucsf, into cole valley, along haight, down page... \r\n<P>\r\n\r\nOh, look, it\'s the \'download deli\'.  Soon I will have money to buy\r\ntoys, and I will buy meself a wireless card.  Then I will see how many\r\nwireless networks I can USE on the way to work.  That will be fun.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\n<BR>\r\n\r\nA lot of random folks lately have been asking me what have I been up\r\nto.  The main thing is hanging out with ivan and weeb and gage a lot.\r\nI like them, they are fun people, and don\'t seem to mind my hanging\r\naround all the time... And going to parties whenever they pop up and\r\nlook interesting.  Last party I went to was Rhythm Society, it\'s a\r\nrave in a church.  Neat.  This is why I moved to SF: To see all the\r\nvariations of 10,000 kids packed into a dingy warehouse shaking their\r\nbutts to some music.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nAlso, I finally got my room arranged.  This is the third time I\'ve\r\nrearranged it since I moved here.  But finally, everything is\r\nsensible.  The computers get the smallest reasonable amount of glare.\r\nThe bed is under the heating vent (though I can hear noise from\r\nupstairs) in case it is cold.  I can look out the deck from bed.\r\nThere is a couch, it\'s sorta across from the bookshelf.  The CD\'s are\r\nnext to the stereo which is next to the computer.  There is a random\r\nwork area next to the computer.  It all makes sense now.  Yay.  <P>\r\n\r\nOkay, time to get off the bus....\r\n<BR>\r\n\r\nAfternoon.  Or should I say evening.  Rowdy bus passengers today.  The\r\nbus had to stop and the cops came on and everything.  I suppose I\'m\r\nlearning the random muni event generator.  I like random generators.\r\nThere was a program for the Vic20 that you could load off of tape and\r\nit would generate random sentences.  You could modify the DATA\r\nstatments to add your own words (at least until you filled up the\r\nmeasley 3K of BASIC program memory that you get).  Anyway, so far,\r\nrandom muni event generator:\r\n\r\n<UL>\r\n  <LI>rowdy passengers</LI>\r\n  <LI>rowdy passengers, bus needs to stop for cops</LI>\r\n  <LI>bus becomes unplugged</LI>\r\n  <LI>random stranger bugs you about laptop</LI>\r\n  <LI>random stranger is obnoxious to you</LI>\r\n  <LI>you have a missed connection</LI>\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\nOkay, now roll 1d6 to determine what happened to YOU on muni today.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nGah gah motion sickness.  It\'s so bizzare.  What is it about motion\r\nsickness?  How does it work.  I must know.  Perhaps I will look it up\r\nonce I get home.  I will get over motion sickness.  It is possible to\r\ntrain. It must be.  It might have something to do with being a control\r\nfreak.\r\n<P>\r\nOkay home now.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1175,'<P><TT>\r\nEverything is something of an exercise.  \r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nMorning was hot and humid.  Gary woke up with a headache.  The knuckles of his right hand were raw, as if he?d dragged them across a brick wall.  He went to the bathroom, washed them in the sink and doused the with rubbing alcohol. He winced.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nGary lived a strange double life.  This is true.  He wrote a column for a popular west coast newspaper, a technology column that covered the industry from a wry, insiders perspective.  It was a geek column, read by thousands of industry insiders.  Dotcom millionaires, manufacturers of semiconductors, wireless telecom moguls.  The job paid.  It paid well.  This is what he had gone to school for, to be a writer, but it wasn?t nearly as romantic as all that.  He was a hack, churning our regurgitated press releases for a buck.  He didn?t write for himself much anymore, just a poem here and a story there.  But he never really got down to business, put pen to paper and made the ART happen.  It was beyond him.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nAs a result, his frustration manifested itself in drinking.  Not much at first, but the drinking soon had a hold of him.  He let it creep in, a parasitic flower spreading through his chest, he let it take him.  Gary didn?t like sports bars or pick-up joints.  He didn?t like slick places.  Gary was a writer and he instinctively sought out honesty.  Jocks with baseball caps are anything but honest.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nGranted, in Cleveland it was easy to find a down-and-out bar, a place where the burnouts and dregs clung to their whiskey like it was a life raft or a handgun.  So Gary found himself in these places at night, rubbing shoulders with bums and drunks.  He began to dress like them, to smell like them.  And the more he drunk, he began to act like them.  This didn?t happen all of a sudden.  It was a process.  And Gary proudly strode into this secret life.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nBut as in any case, drinking adds variables to a situation.  Alcohol adds fuel to deep-seated resentments and hidden angers.  Burnouts and lost men harbor more frustration than you can imagine.  Gary found himself witness to violence and fights; he saw men get stabbed and bleed onto pool tables; he saw a man rip a barstool from its mooring and throw it through a window; he saw a man smash a jukebox with his bare hands.  And Gary loved every minute of it.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nHe was smarter than the other barflies.  He had a strange detachment.  Gary was able to watch things transgress with a writerly eye. He pretended he was Philip Marlowe, Ernest Hemingway, Charles Bukowski.  He began to create a persona around himself and as that personality became apparent to the men around him, it drew him into opposition with them.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nGary lost his first fight.  He woke up in an alley and had to find his way home.  But he began to enjoy the fights.  This was something different.  In High School he?d been the thin, nerdy weakling, who backed down rather than stand up for himself.  But now he was a crusader, if he felt slighted, he would tackle a man, bring him down with fists and kicks.  He dragged men into alleys and found himself dragged into alleys.  But somewhere in the process he learned how to fight.  He learned how to take punches and deliver them.  He learned how to move his feet.  He became a fighter.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nThis is what he is.  He is not proud of it.  But, for now it is interesting.   Sorry you have to be subject to it as he seeks his voice.  He has never been this before.\r\n</TT></P>'),(1176,'<p>\r\nBlech.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI make a lot of mistakes.  A lot. I\'m all about mistakes, mis-managing time, learning things for certain, over and over again and nonetheless letting them go and subjecting myself to relearning them.   Arrrggghhhh me matey. . .\r\n</p><p>\r\nI was doing ok.  I was doing my job, yes? But now I\'m not.  Now I just want to flee or hide or close my eyes and lump together like dough.  I\'m just craving some hotness in my life.  Serious hotness.  Angelina Jolie smoldering pillow lipped hotness.  I don\'t just mean in the libido-quenching sense.  I just wish things were not progressing in the slow sleepy way that they are.  I want fast furious carnal intellectual connected limbic SOMETHING and a lot of it.  I want it to sink right down into my skin before I get all embalmed like a corpse.   \r\n</p>'),(1177,'On Friday night, I went out drinking with my flatmate because I wanted to bond with her, and because I had a whole lot of pain in my arms from Occupational Overuse Syndrome, and I figured at least alcohol would make them go away. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nWalking home that evening, I was hit by a car.  I can tell you from the pain that it clipped the left side of me.  I can\'t tell you any more than that about the accident, because I don\'t remember.  Maybe I stepped in front of it.  Maybe it didn\'t stop at a pedestrian crossing when it should have.  It is probably my own fault, but I know that they didn\'t stop to see if I was okay.  The people I was with didn\'t see it happen either, they just turned around to see me sitting on the ground.  Dave told me to stand up, I was just a drunken slapper, or words to that effect.  I asked him to give me a moment please because I\'d just been hit by a car.  He still tried to make me get up, so eventually, I got up, and got in a taxi and went home. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThen I fully realised what had happened, so I called my friends and they took me to the hospital (\"take me private! i have health insurance, dammit!\").  At the hospital, the doctor disapproved of me a lot because I was drunk, and gave me two panadol.  The much nicer nurse put an icepack on my foot, and eventually told my friend where some blankets were, and he gave them a paperbag for me to breathe into if I kept hyperventilating me, and an hour later or so he came back to bandage me up.  They use a funny metal frame to stretch bandages over.  It was very interesting.  I got to sit in a wheelchair and everything.  They gave me a sheet of information about possible head concussions, but my flatmates didn\'t bother waking me up twice in the night like they should have.  Nevermind.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nNow my foot is purple, as are parts of my arm.  I\'m also a little freaked out by thinking about what might have happened if I\'d been standing a foot more to the left.  However, it has definately been an interesting experience, and strangely enough, my OOS doesn\'t hurt so much.  And now I should go back to bed, where I have been all weekend (i am so bored).  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nWhat did <B>you</B> do on friday night?'),(1178,'<p>All over bathrooms in austin. Inside unsold copies of Cryptonomicon. Placed in windshields of cars all over 6th street. That\'s where I\'ve seen the bellow flyers:  </p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1178_1.jpg\'><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1178_2.jpg\'><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1178_3.jpg\'>'),(1179,'Hello World.'),(1180,'test'),(1181,'test'),(1182,'test'),(1183,'test'),(1184,'test'),(1185,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1185_1.jpg\'>'),(1186,'<P><TT>hi there kids,\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nthere have been a few chages with the sites code.  i am displaying this in true type font to illustrate the techical nature. mountain lion with me.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nswinney.org was previously partially database driven.  now it is completely database driven.  this has been done for a few reasons.  let me ennumerate for you now.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\n<OL>\r\n<LI><TT> a partially database driven site is rooted in the server it is on.  the articles if you look in the URL have a NUMBER.txt at the end.  those used to be text files, now the are in the database.  whoopty fucking shit right?  well, now you can take swinney wherever you want, utilizing the central database.  all  this so that people like darwin and especially <a href=\"http://oxy.gen.nz\">oliviar</a> can \"skin\" the site how ever they please.  for that matter, so can i.</TT>\r\n</OL>\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nthat is really all i had to say about that.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nthanks to <a href=\"http://maz.org/\">ix</a> for lending me his perl script, and ix and s5 installing mysql on atari, also php.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\nwhat else, i\'m sure you have complaints.  let fly.  i have a thick skin.  c\'mon big guy.  mister e-pinion, show me what you got.\r\n</TT></P><P><TT>\r\np.s., in the next version of swinney we should find full support for emacs key combinations in all the text boxes so that you may submit comments with the keystroke, CTR-x CTR-s.  ok.  bye.\r\n</TT></P>'),(1187,''),(1188,'<P>\"I\'m glad you are back.\"</p>\r\n<P>It\'s that winter that comes in the middle of summer here in San Francsico. Gusting winds carrying freezing fog over the hill, into the sunny Misson, blowing down Haight Street to our block. It seeps through clothing and settles in your bones. The warmth of occasional sun is negated by the wind and fog. You just have to wait it out for a couple of weeks.</p>\r\n<P>He beckoned to me once, come along with me, no words. We seemed not to need them. For weeks his eyes lived smiling in my mind, hands, belly, black hair rich as coffee. I imagined him engulfed by the beauty of Europe, engulfed by the beauty of the women, I could picture in my mind his widening curious eyes taking it all in. I missed him, but an invisible thread was stitched into our psyches. It connected us, but never ever bound us together. People like us need to be allowed to play, create, experience, be intense without bounds.</p>\r\n<P>He crept through a curtained doorway, dark and milky, into the night air. I heard him ascend the wooden stairs above me. I stepped slowly through, following him, and found a chair just outside and sat. He came back down the stairs and passed by me, not seeing me, and I reached out to him. His heart was broken, all I wanted to do was give him some peace. He stood next to me for several minutes, turning my hand in his, leaning on me slightly. He put his hand over my heart, and I felt a surge of energy that startled me. \"What, how are you doing that?\" He wanted to know what I felt. I couldn\'t describe it to him, but somehow I knew I didn\'t need to. I pulled him onto my lap, putting my arms around his, and laid my head into his heartbeat.</p>\r\n<P>We stayed that way for many moments, warm and dark, and generated heat between our bodies. I grounded him, his infalliable stratospheric energy surging through his heart into my ear down my spine through my feet to the ground. It pulsed, rang, and illuminated my insides. I drew him in, and he took me as well, but we will never capture each other.</p>\r\n'),(1189,'<p>\r\nbumping up against new people that are old this weekend, wanting to touch and open them up and see what makes them purr. want to be able to look them in the eye. opening chasm. wanting another body to inhabit. wanting another laugh to come out of my mouth. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni want to look down and see someone else\'s feet. i get impatient easily, lately. it\'s time to molt.\r\n</p><p>\r\na good description of my mood would be \'malaised.\'\r\n</p><p>\r\ni want push/pull, action/reaction. someone should kick my asssss<br>\r\n#<br>\r\n$<br>\r\n5<br>\r\n&<br></p>\r\n<p>\r\nread me something naughty. slap me around. slit me up and suck me dry. i am a dry vessel about to crack. </p>\r\n\r\n'),(1190,'<pre>\r\nYou\'re somewhere crushing roses\r\nunderneath a naked heel.\r\nAnd the roses aren\'t complaining,\r\nit\'s the best they\'ll ever feel.\r\n</pre>'),(1191,'<PRE>\r\nblack eyeliner smudged around her eyes\r\ndyed black hair \r\nsuspenders- suspending.\r\nthin and smiling and young.\r\nshe is so cute and i can\'t get her out of my head.\r\n</PRE>'),(1192,'<P>\r\nSomebody on this site questioned one of my poems.  This poem  is truer than a police siren at 3am.  The questioner asked how I knew that I was smarter than the other barflies and burnouts that I hang out with.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nThis is easy.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nYou don?t go into these bars and sit around silently, sipping spritzers and Mai Tais will chatting with a select group of friends and excluding those on the outside of your circle.  No.  Those are trendy bars, social excursions.  In comparison to the kind of bars I?m talking about, these trendy bars are like fucking church socials.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nYou have to understand, in my bars you end up talking to people, lonely drunks are forever striking up conversations and starting shit.  Some things are interesting, some things turn violent.  Every day is not a bar fight.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI once met a man who cleaned stables at a racetrack down in Georgia.  How he got to Cleveland he wouldn?t say.  The man called himself ?Pa.?  He was a very thin, angular black man with the DT shakes and a ragged scar that traveled from his ear to his shoulder..  His voice was reedy and broken and I really had to lean in to hear him.  Pa told me that he had $4,000 in mayonnaise jars buried in one of those stables.  He?d won the money playing the ponies.  But since his life was mixed up loan sharks, bookies and boozers, he didn?t trust keeping the money in his house.  Apparently was in deep to a big time loan shark.  So, after winning big, he  went out at night and buried it one night in the stable of a horse named Corrupted Element.  He didn?t need the money right away so he figured he?d go back and get it when the time was right and then he?d beat it out of town.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI asked Pa why he didn?t just take the money and get the hell out of there then and there.\r\n</P><P>\r\nPa said that he was young then.  Impossibly young.  He may have shoveled shit for a living, but it made him strong and sculpted.  He had a string of women that wanted him and he knew how to play them.  One of these women was special.  Her name was Louinda.  Pa described Louinda as so black that lights shone under her skin.  She glowed.  He could leave anything behind, all the other women could fall into a pit for all he cared, but Louinda he couldn?t leave.  It was she who kept him tied to his life. \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen Pa went back to his apartment, he found it ravaged.  The bed was torn up, drawers were pulled from the dresser and emptied, even the wall paper was torn from the walls.  There were two guys there.  One of them grabbed Pa from behind.  They worked him over good, demanding that he pay-up the money he owed, because they heard he?d won big at the race track.  Pa denied it.  Said he didn?t have the money and he wasn?t paying them shit.  They told him to get out of town.  If they saw him again they?d fucking kill him.\r\n</P><P>\r\nPa knew that these guys didn?t fool around.  He knew he had no time to get the money he?d buried, so he beat it out of town on a ten spot that he kept in his shoe.  He figured he?d wait a few months and slip back into town for his money.  But somehow he just kept going north.  Months turned into years.  Dead-end job led to dead-end job.  He never got back to get the money.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nBut it haunted him, daily. Whenever he was hungry or had to beg for change, he thought of that $4,000.  But more than that, Pa said he thinks of Louinda.  He misses her and wonders whatever happened t her.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n****\r\n</P><P>\r\nI don\'t know what kind of lives you lead and I don\'t care.  But I know that Pa is a better man than all of us.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1193,'<p>I still have my smile, only I can feel that it is forced. The muscles in my jaw are tight, and my teeth are quenched; hardly a smile. I am not sure what is going on this morning, though I am sure that am being tested by some force. \r\n</p><p>\r\nLast night I ended my day at 9:30pm, I put myself to sleep. I slept well, and I woke when the birds woke. This is first morning that I can remember that I have actually woken up without the aid of an alarm for some months now. I am against the alarm, but for some reason I have had to use it to wake me in the mornings. So, like a good little disciplined boy I woke before the sun and proceeded to start my day. I said good morning to my roommate, and then I took a refreshing shower. My morning was going good.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI was up early enough to cook my breakfast, which is always a nice thing to do in the morning. I decided to heat up a tortilla in the oven. I turned the oven on, and because our oven is very old you have to light it manually. This is a scary procedure. Before I lit it, I asked Kyrsten to back away as to save her from possible 3rd degree burns. I lit it, and KABOOOM. Eye lashes, eyebrows, bangs and arm hair burned again. After that explosion, I nearly blew myself. I maintained control. I took a break, and decided that I needed to relax a bit and wait till Kyrsten was done in our little kitchen before I continued. She wasn\'t in my way, but I needed the space. Eventually I had my delightful breakfast.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI decided to drive over to quacks for some coffee. Everything was fine there. I was in a good mood once again.  I decided to drive to school, thinking that I needed the comfort after my breakfast ordeal, but then I decided to return home and ride my bike to school. Riding my bike gives me such a great feeling and I seem to be ready to take on anything after the traffic dodging ride to school. Riding through Hyde Park I watched the little kids play on the playground near the Baptist church. The children\'s laughter and play made me smile. A football cam flying over the fence and a little boy ran up to the fence and asked me if I would please throw his football back to him. I picked up the ball and tossed it back to him with pleasure, he smiled and said thank you as if he really meant it. I rode away feeling like the biggest person in the world, smiling and happy. \r\n</p><p>\r\nHalf way to school I blew my back tire. Tire and tube were both torn. I calmly walked the bike to the nearest place to lock it up. I walked to kyrsten\'s work, which was just down the street and on the way to my school. She was there, smiling while watering plants. I smiled back and asked if I could borrow her truck to get to school on time. I told her of my day after the blow out in the kitchen, she remarked that I had some strange juju going on. I agreed whole heartedly. I showed my smile, and it felt genuine; I wasn\'t going to allow a little tire to ruin my good mood! \r\n</p><p>\r\nI got to school on time, even had enough change for the meter. I saw my class mate, westley, on the way in to the building. He asked how I thought I did on my test. I told him that I thought it was a little rough. We had minutes till class started, and he explained that he needed to put change in his meter, but had no change only dollars. I offered him two hours worth of change, and he accepted with hesitation. I insisted, he smiled, and I smiled having done something for someone else. I sat in my desk and waited to get the test back. First he handed back homework, 100 %, as I usually get on homework assignments. Exam, 72; right here my jaw tightens, my teeth chatter and I can feel my self heat up. I calmly sit, and try to regain my composure, but I can\'t. I am beat, my tires are torn, and my hair looks funny all singed. I leave class and I go home, deflated. \r\n</p><p>'),(1194,'Y\'all sittin\' in da dark 2?  lemme tell you kidbot, ain\'t nuthin\' like da heart o\' darkness i sits in, yo!  consider this bleeding dark.  consider this fuckless, luckless, thankless night.  shit howdy, am i bored.  i\'d be jillin\' myself now if i weren\'t jacked out of my head.<br><br>\r\n\r\n!- shit I\'m an idiot>\r\n\r\nPlug me in Batboy'),(1195,'what the hell?  i don\'t have any writing on this site anymore.  everything is gone from articles to comments.      what the hellllllllll?'),(1196,'<p>I carry your letter in my pocket\r\nwrapped in a yahoo map of my house.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dont have a lot of dresses with pockets. this one is pink gingham with a blue stripe across the top zips up the front like a housecoat. like the raspberry mumu you wear to be decent when you run to the bathroom. the pocket is a utility pocket, meant for house-moms to carry sewing needles and scissors and meat cleavers and hot glue guns. when it comes to moms, we should replace the word \"utility\" with \"love\".</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou have written me nice emails. but I cant hold them in my hand. on the toilet, or on the train. thye dont have your scribbles.</p>\r\n<p>\r\njay sat next to me this morning reading his brochure on \"the clay studio.\" he pressed on the crease with his fingers and talked about the vessels he would make for us in his ceramics class.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI loved sitting next to him silently, while he made plans for breakfast plates and I tried to memorize how your hand makes the words \"egg\" and \"bed\".</p>\r\n'),(1197,'i\'m not a techie wiz mom.  i do straight html.  i build shipping carts for small, web challenged retailers.  my cartserver of choice is <a href=\"http://www.cartserver.com/americart\">americart</a><br><br>\r\n\r\nfor several years i have been trying to get my clients to build \"real\" databases of their inventories so that i can use the search functions on their sites.<br><br>\r\n\r\nto do this, as you geeky types know, you must name your images the same name as the stock number.  it is the only way to make it work.  AND, you must be consistent in naming product, assigning stock numbers, etc.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsmall biz is often resistant to change.  and when you do finally get them to change things, they balk at the cost of doing it.  one of my clients built the database just fine, but i have to go in change the image names by hand.  1897 images, as a matter of fact.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat a minimum of 30 seconds per image, that\'s approximately 16 hours of tedious, blinding, obnoxious scut work.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am reluctant to send a bill for 16 hours of name changing.  especially since i am the one who insisted on the changes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbecause....  the reason i wanted the changes is because i intend to fucking retire from building fucking shopping carts for small biz, i don\'t fucking care how much money they pay me, and it\'s no small number, let me tell you.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat the same time, i don\'t want to work for free.  and i am in a predicament of my own making, and i bet i am not the only web person who ever had this trouble before, am i?<br><br>\r\n\r\ni just want to go back to bed now, please.\r\n\r\n'),(1198,'i am more pissed than a lesbian in a men\'s steam room.  WHERE ARE MY ARTICLES?'),(1199,'i will not remain silent?  why have my articles been erased.  who has arbitrarily decided to silence me?  i open my name and there are NO ARTICLES.  my coments have been erased from others\' articles.  how and why?  am i the only one?  why this injustice?  who took me down without warning?  who is so afraid of my words that they must be eliminated?  you are equivelent to book burners!!!  you censors!  j\'accuse!  because I am different than you, you remove me!  i will not stand for it.  why do you decide to do this now?  '),(1200,'okay, okay.  I have a hard time being sincere.  I can\'t look at anything without the desire to fuck with it.  i\'m pretty relentless when it comes to antagonism.  i was just begining to get to more real posts and then you pull the rug out from underneath me.  \r\n\r\nyeah, yeah, i posted under the name \"quinn\" and the name \"griece.\"  it\'s pretty obvious if you look at the writing tones.  i also posted under \"grayson.\"  each one is invested with a different personality.  i just wanted to see who could provoke the biggest response.\r\n\r\ni am not interested by grayson\'s writing.  he got boring quickly because nobody cared about his poetry.  quinn provoked some fights, but didn\'t really have a personality of his own.  griece, who is just an alter ego of mine, came off full-blown and realized.  so he lied some. \r\nwho on this site hasn\'t?\r\n\r\ni understand now that someone is deliberately silencing me and that someone will probably read this before editing me out of your community.  so here I rant, to my audience of one.\r\n\r\nmaybe i will be back in a month to see if you\'ll let me post under a different name.  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1201,'Let it be, if it must be.  One tries to ignore depression, but it surfaces like the submerged body of a dead sister, acrawl with leaves and infant maggots, floating across the swimming pool of one\'s consciousness.  One tries not to dramatise oneself, trying to live the Dhammapada: <i>all is fire, everything is on fire</i>; trying to reason that nothing is important, not even <i>this</i>; trying to convince oneself that <i>there is no I</i>, and who gives a fuck if there is an I anyway? and did I finish George Bataille\'s <i>L\'histoire d\'un oeil?</i> and I must concentrate on what I\'m doing, if I\'ll sever the vein completely and enough to stop thinking.  Stop thinking of the I and let it be, if it must be.<br><br>Oh, this is fucking lunacy.  How do you people deal with despair?  Do you dismiss it as illusory, as a momentary surrender, as a wrong genuflection of the planets?  Can one surrender?  If so,<br><br><br><i>tell me how</i>'),(1202,'<p>Sometimes I wish I grew up in a workingclass Brit household, maybe in the 70\'s, something out of a punkrock video, you know? Or like an episode of that show \"The Young Ones\", although I guess that was more like an early-80\'s college flat, but anyway, we\'d live in one of those rowhouses and me mum wut string our knickers onna line inna backyard you know, an every night there\'d be sum shoutin to the effect of \"oy, fok yew mum! ican smok in the house if i wanna\" and she\'d be \"yew jes wait till yer da comes home johnny ee\'s gonna knock tha living piss outta ye\" and there would be factories in the background and all kinds of snotnosed kids running through the streets and screaming babies and the sun would never shine and everything wud smell like petrol and I\'m going to go lie down now bye :o</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1203,'<pre>\r\nthe tabletop now runs wild\r\nwith tangelos. they spring\r\nfrom their bowls into pools of\r\nsomeone else\'s milk, rolling\r\nrecklessly over the edge in a \r\nhot orange flash of rebellion and\r\nwanderlust, a nascent sense of \r\nadventure finally unbound.\r\nyou chase after them as they\r\nexplode across the kitchen floor,\r\ncurses and laughter sputtering\r\nfrom your lips, but in an inward\r\nplace you harbor an admiration\r\nof their spirit, their mission to \r\nroll forward until stopped by\r\nlaws of physics or our gentle \r\nhands. it is a display of motion\r\nthat recalls motion: the sundazed\r\nsimplicity of tumbling down grassy hillsides,\r\nover beds, across the lawn,\r\noff the diving board, into mother\'s arms\r\n\r\nfollow the tangelo, follow it\r\nback to ordinary days warped into \r\nmythologies by the weight of time,\r\nto summer afternoons at the beach\r\nor within softly murmuring fields \r\nof tall grass, to blue skies \r\nthat do not remember rain.\r\nfollow it back to the time\r\nwhen you were unpeeled.\r\n\r\n(9/14/93)\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>i decided to bring this poem out of retirement when a friend asked for a copy. as it turns out, she sees it on our fridge every time she visits and has secretly admired it. made my day! </p> \r\n\r\n<p>it was meant to be part of a series of \"kitchen poems,\" originally written to appease (somehow) a set of bitchy roommates who were disappointed in my lack of cooking skills and disinterest in gaining same. the gambit didn\'t work and i never wrote more. perhaps i\'ll try a few now that i have a bit of free time....</p>'),(1204,'<P><B>I MADE ART.</B></P>'),(1205,'<P><B>I MADE ART</B></P>'),(1206,'<P>I MADE ART</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1206_1.jpg\">'),(1207,'<p>what i have missed<br>\r\nwhat i have failed to understand<br>\r\nis that the reflection<br>\r\nof me<br>\r\nis everywhere<br>\r\nwhich<br>\r\nrendered in a sort of negation<br>\r\namounts to nothing<br>\r\nwhich is not<br>\r\nmy reflection<br>\r\ni am public narcissist<br>\r\nnumber one<br>\r\nwhen it comes to my world<br>\r\nwhich is only not less<br>\r\nthan everything<br>\r\nmeaning<br>\r\nthe I AM i call me<br>\r\nis not my body<br>\r\nonly<br>\r\nbut every part of all that is<br>\r\nlike john donne said<br><br>\r\nthe bell that tolls<br>\r\nit tolls for me<br>\r\nso, the shriek that keens<br>\r\nit keens for me<br>\r\nand the ants on the counter<br>\r\nand the cicada in the tree<br>\r\nand the deer on the street<br>\r\nand my sister and her husband<br>\r\nand the form i call ENEMY<br>\r\nand all things that be<br>\r\nexist because of that me<br>\r\nbecause of that i am<br>\r\nbecause of that energy<br>\r\nwhich is free of possession<br>\r\nor encumberance<br>\r\nwhich is and has been<br>\r\nfor 19,000 times 19,000<br>\r\ntimes 19,000 infinities of<br>\r\neternities of timeless quantities<br>\r\nof superconductivity<br>\r\nand which will be and must be<br>\r\nbecause this IS keeps IS-ing<br>\r\nand energy can never be<br>\r\nbroken or lost or severed<br>\r\nbut is the cause of causes<br>\r\nof causing<br><br>\r\nand when the sage said<br>\r\ngod is the creator of your wisdom<br>\r\nhe only meant<br>\r\nyour IS, your I AM<br>\r\nis the creator of every part of your being<br>\r\nand the only responsibility you<br>\r\nhave<br>\r\nEVER and for all of EVER<br>\r\nis to yourself<br>\r\nwhich is only not less<br>\r\nthan everything</p>\r\n<p>behold the kingdom of god<br>\r\nis with men<br>\r\nbehold the kingdom of god is,<br>\r\nis the is<br>\r\nis is<br>\r\nis</p>'),(1208,'<br>\r\nI tread disillusionment like water<br>\r\nand see its feeble textures melt<br>\r\nthrough the grate and drip towards the river.<br>\r\nAnd vapid angel-breath sentiments<br>\r\nweld me to the June sky.<br>\r\nOrange belched sparks fleck sunset.<br>\r\nInfinity compressed into a season, I wisen.<br>\r\nRipely, I seek forgotten falls,<br>\r\nfor I\'ve never seen this color orange.<br>\r\nit\'s never licked my fingers and<br>\r\ntugged a shading hand away from my covered eyes.<br>\r\nMy pupils burn, but i savor its glow in the west.<br>\r\nI\'ve felt the seasons change, tingling,<br>\r\nyet never touched them.<br>\r\ni\'ve smelled their fresh baked, thawed and seared days<br>\r\nwith rising suns and desert moons<br>\r\nbut never rolled them on my tongue<br>\r\nlapping their twilight tingle,<br>\r\nnever urged to dangle my feet in their cool pools<br>\r\nthat lap lazily at my threshold.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1209,'<p><font size=\"-1\"><i>egg<br>please accept this humble aknowledgement of all that the world you and i create together means to me and the rest of humanity. this book may signal the end of the beginning and the beginning of the new. apple.</i></font></p>\r\n<p>the gift was a book titled \"The Televisionary Oracle\" and written by my favorite astrology columnist, Rob Brezsny.</p>\r\n<p>on the cover is a contemporary illustration of <a href=\"http://www.links.net/spirit/kali/\">Kali</a> who some say is the goddess of the internet, standing near a tower of televisions which each hold a new age-cum-mtv image of blue babies or ufo lotus flowers, shit like that.</p>\r\n<p>when the ups guy arrived I was just writing a short story about a sushi date that ends up with a lot of hackneyed astrology talk. Hackneyed astrology talk probably makes up about 25% of all my conversations, as sushi probably makes up 5% of my diet. That was one distinct difference I noticed about moving to California. People really do talk about astrology here. and people eat a lot of sushi. </p>\r\n<p> I read my <a href=\"http://www.freewillastrology.com/pages/pisces.shtml\">free will astrology</a> horoscope every week in the sfweekly, and I know my basic chart pretty well. I like the language of charts. I am a pisces but my moon is in aquarius, which saves me from being a little whiny bitch building of a human.</p> \r\n<p>the thing about eve sending me this book is that while she worked as a tarot card reader (on her Kabbalah deck) for a time and has mystical tendencies, she never ever bought astrology all those high school years that I was dressing up her birthday crowns with Athenian adornments. it wasn\'t until she had a full reading from an astrologer friend of the family that she broke down and cried and started sending me these wacky books.</p>\r\n<p>I have no argument over the Newtonian validity of astrology, nor do I care to enter that conversation. Nor do I refute the idea that it is merely the power of suggestion that moves people and events. </p>\r\n<p>what compels me about rob brezsny is his ability to weave magic into the fiber of morning coffee and traffic jams. the ordinary is transformed by hackneyed astrology talk in 60 seconds or less. eve and I have pillow talks about the language of chance and how it molds our world; umberto ecco fills in some gaps. astrology provides this endless assortment of archetypes and relationships to combine and break apart: a working mythical infrastructure, with no certain answers nor any concrete predictions.</p>\r\n<p>and what compels me here today, <a href=\"http://www.cs.usyd.edu.au/~matty/Shakespeare/texts/comedies/midsummersnightsdream.html#xref015\">gentles</a>, is the synchronicitous joy of receiving such a gift when chance -- the meat of magic, for me-- brings unexpected presents to direct me now that I\'m looking towards a summer of  alchemical experiments and economic uncertainty.  my pals at the bend say it best when they exchange this dialogue:</p>\r\n<p>jay: the world is full of promise today.<br>\r\njoanne: mmmm. promises. <br>\r\njay: the world is full of PROMISE. not promises.<br>\r\njoanne: oh. I heard \"promises\".<br>\r\njay: promises.</p>'),(1210,'<P>How do you change a network interface card? My neighbor will pay me $20 if I can do it for her, and that will be $20 I don\'t have now. It\'s a Windows 98 machine, and she uses the Roadrunner thing from Time Warner. Do I have to configure the IP address and subnet mask, stuff like that?</P><P>Any advice would be greatly appreciated. Thanks.</P>'),(1211,'<p>Overanalysis breeds suspicion, doubt and unecessarily negative views regarding the situations you find yourself thrown into.</p>\r\n<p>On occasion, it is simply a more efficient processing of energy to simply let yourself be comfortable with things the way they are and not attempt such difficult changes.</p>\r\n<p>The more you think about how horrible it would be to drown in a pool is the greater probability of you never so much as sticking your legs in that pool. Focusing on the negative aspects of actions and dwelling on them, forever doomed to repeat the same mistakes.</p>\r\n<p>On some twisted Moebius Strip of fear, I ride the loops, never once allowing myself the simple pleasure of stopping and thinking about what else there is for me.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m simply being too reasonable and it is driving me crazy.</p>'),(1212,'<p>\r\ni have a confession to make. it\'s super cheesy, but here goes. i think you\'ll understand.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhenever i go to indie shows, i watch myself on stage instead of the band. then i pick whoever in the band i want to be and watch the audience. tonight i was neko case.\r\n</p><p>\r\nonce i feel that familiar buzzing in my right ear, it all comes rushing back. just like it was 6 years ago all over again. and every drop of sweat rises up to the stage; the ceiling drips, like a filthy raincloud. the monitors are never loud enough. everything is deafening but nothing is ever loud enough.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthat one creep gets close enough to the stage to yell something shitty. everyone else is rocking out. she\'s singing along. he\'s convincing his girlfriend to stay another song. he\'s getting more drinks. she\'s playing air tambourine against her thigh. the t-shirts are selling. after the show, everyone wants to know the band. but the band wants to slump on the couch. cables wrapped up, the night is over.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni will always remember how hot the stage is. it\'s so hot you crawl out of your skin. but you forget about the spotlights peeling off your face. in fact you forget everything, even your bladder. even though it\'s pressed up against the guitar. nothing matters but the stage and the crowd.\r\n</p><p>\r\nas soon as elly leaves the house, i play rush real loud and screech like geddy lee. me and geddy. we\'re two of a kind. bass players, big noses. i even have a juno-60.\r\n</p>'),(1213,'<P>he said we would want smoothies.</P>\r\n<P>i scoffed.</P>\r\n<P>but the seed had been planted.</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>that was thursday. he and i became best friends on Thursday.</P>\r\n<P>summer solstice. drinks on the roof. watch the sunset.</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>instead I walked up on the roof to see her pulling his pants off.</P>\r\n<P>i had a view of the sun hanging low over marin, </P>\r\n<P>minutes before it was obscured by hills.</P>\r\n<P>and his tighty whities</P>\r\n<P>&nbsp;</P>\r\n<P>on friday she said she wanted a smoothie.</P>\r\n'),(1214,'<p>Okay, okay...\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m going to tell a different version of the truth here, so if you haven\'t dismissed me already, here it is.\r\n\r\n<p>griece is, as you might have guessed, a fiction.  I can\'t quite help what I say as him because his voice isn\'t quite determined.  He got a little out of hand, what with the cursing and all, and therefore the god of this site stepped in and reprimanded him.  \r\n\r\n<p>Now I had intended griece to be beligerent and angry, reactionary and close-to-the-bone.  He was that, but he was also thoughtless and stupid.  He made bad choices and was one-dimensional.  I couldn\'t get him past that.  But griece didn\'t mind.  He could hide behind his digital appelation and continue shelling out barroom stories and antagonisms.  \r\n\r\n<p>god told me that griece was causing angry e-mails to be written and this pleased and pleases him.  But because the his balloon has been somewhat punctured, he can\'t go on being the same.  He is forced to reinvent himself.\r\n\r\n<p>So, you can say good-bye to that barroom griece, he never really existed anyway.  I will coninue to post under his name, though I think his experiences are going to run closer to the man behind the digital curtain.  Hell... I\'ll even include an e-mail address for hate mail.  '),(1215,'<p><b>(written on 05/04/2001)</b></p>\r\n<p>It\'s hot out today. The Weather Network <a href=\"http://www.theweathernetwork.com/cities/can/toronto_on.htm\" target=\"weather_network\">tells me</a> that is\' currently 27, 29 with the humidex. As much as I enjoy the warm weather, I also like cold. I love water.  Being around, in and covered with water.  However, I also like cold and snow and gazing out my windows on a snowy night.</p>\r\n<p>I enjoy warm weather as much as I dislike it. I want to go outside and play frisbee or go jump in a lake about as much as I just want to sit around reading and listening to music.</p>\r\n<p>And yet, I can\'t help but think that all this is brought on by listening to the promo I just acquired of Bj&ouml;rk\'s new album <a href=\"http://www.bjork.com/specials/vespertine\" target=\"vespertine\">Vespertine</a> (which is due for release in August). As much as some of the songs are <b>very</b> summer-oriented. I think the album, as a whole, deserves a cold morning, shut in because there is five feet of snow blocking your driveway and you\'d rather call in sick to work than shovel it all.</p>\r\n<p>I think it\'s time to get some air conditioning.</p>'),(1216,'<p>\r\nlight on three sides\r\n<br>his new place has light on three sides\r\n<br>his new place has light\r\n<br>his new place\r\n</p><p>\r\nnew.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"new\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nnot old, not ours, nothing to do with me, didn\'t see it, don\'t know about it, don\'t know the address, haven\'t been there, didn\'t help find it, didn\'t help pay for it, won\'t help decorate it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe says, halfhearted, he says to me: <i>i\'d like you to see it.</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni say, halfhearted, i say to him: <i>yeah that would be nice i\'d like to see it.</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>have you seen his new place?</i> they ask.<br>\r\nhot question. question of the week.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(vague, nonchalant, i answer)\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>no, no, not yet</i> and look around, away, appearing disinterested and unmoved by my lack of involvement in this new place of his.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>it\'s really nice<br>\r\nit\'s got light on three sides</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\n(nod, well rehearsed friendly smile, nod, respond)\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>oh cool!</i> <br>\r\n<i> that sounds nice</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nyep, new place. don\'t have keys. my stuff isn\'t there. can\'t come over at 3am crying and climb into bed without alerting the new roomates to my pathetic drama. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnew place with lots of light so much better than the old place we lived together that dark place where everything went wrong which now holds the detritus of  loss and depression. \r\n</p><p>\r\nobsessive and overwhelming upset leap of logic:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\n<i>he\'s better without me</i><br>\r\n(light on three sides)<br>\r\n<i>he\'s better without me</i><br>\r\n(light on three sides)<br>\r\n<i>he\'s better without me</i>\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\ngreat, i don\'t matter.<br>\r\nall that love did no good and i don\'t matter.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlove doesn\'t conquer much actually. in case you were wondering.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>things love doesn\'t appear to conquer:</b><br>\r\n<font size=\"-2\">a list, by le</font>\r\n<br>\r\n<br> depression\r\n<br> poverty\r\n<br> codependence\r\n<br> misunderstandings\r\n<br> time\r\n<br> loss\r\n<br> definitely not loss\r\n\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1217,'<P>\r\nIt\'s easier to be seem witty and intelligent when there a fewer people in the room.  When there are more people, I feel a bit lackluster and I gravitate towards whatever alcoholic beverages may be on hand.  I like art openings and parties because the booze is free, but if I\'m at a bar I won\'t hesitate to pay for a drink.  I will usually offer to get drinks for a large group of people and in that way I can both have a bit of time to regroup, and have that crucial drink that will enable me to better converse, and maybe even tell a joke, usually some sort of circumstantial pun or relevant comment about recent events in the news.  I like jokes about George W. Bush, though Iâ€™m not adverse to mocking the democrats, generally all politicians are fair game.  And Brooke Shields, nothing beats a good Brooke Shields joke.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBut I guess humor is also a defense mechanism, much like this â€œsocial drinkingâ€ as we who are alcoholically inclined like to call it.  Humor also is a ruse that blocks true conversation.  If I say, â€œa string walks into a bar,â€  and as is probably expected, youâ€™ve already heard that joke, I can segue into jokes about peanuts and/or Polish people, blondes and glasses of water.  I have elaborate jokes to tell about just about anything.  I go to the bookstores and memorize seven or eight before any social event.  I used to buy the books, but friends began to find my joke books scattered around my apartment.  And, as you well know, a single guy spending his nights at home, drinking iced coffee and memorizing jokes renders a rather pathetic picture.  One night I gathered up all my books, tied a rope them and donated them to the Goodwill.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nNow its bookstores.  Although it may BE as pathetic, it doesnâ€™t SEEM as pathetic to be standing in my local Waldenbooks, giggling to myself. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI have friends who are the picture of social success.  The communicate with aplomb, segueing from joke to conversation that actually has CONTENT.  They know how to talk about things, exploring tangents and darting linguistically about a multitude of topics while still sticking to an overriding theme.  I just get too nervous to accomplish this.  Once my repertoire of humor is exhausted, I just drink, nod, listen and laugh whenever itâ€™s appropriate.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSo the problem here is that I end up drunker than anyone else.  I drink faster, make more trips to the bar and end up rocking on my feet, staring dumbly into the faces of my friends, a goofy grin smeared across my face.  I get to the point where even if I had something to say, I wouldnâ€™t be able to say it because Iâ€™m toooo DAMN drunnnk toooo unnerstaand Engleesh.  My eyes glaze over and my body feels all numb and tingly.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nBut hereâ€™s what Iâ€™ve noticed.  When you donâ€™t say anything, nobody really CARES.  They just plow onward, making their own observations and jokes and witty comments.  Art openings are notorious for this.  At most parties, like rock shows, you canâ€™t hear anybody anyway so it doesnâ€™t matter.  Bars are congregations of egos.  Iâ€™ve learned that all my stress is totally needless, because people like it when I donâ€™t talk, they have the perfect audience.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nStill, I memorize jokes.  Itâ€™s important to me to at least feel as if Iâ€™ve participated in a conversation.  I guess this is my ego acting up.  When I donâ€™t talk I feel as if the person is secretly wondering why I donâ€™t say anything.  â€œIs he stuck up?â€™  They might be thinking.  â€œDoes he even care enough to share his own opinion.â€  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI do have opinions and ideas, I have answers and retorts to questions.  I only think of them two or three hours too late.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1218,'<p>\r\nI had this idea a few weeks ago that I would write about bars in oakland, after we visited Baggy\'s by the Lake, near the Parkway. last night was the next immediate time that I went to a bar, for Yuri\'s birthday. and it marked the four week anniversary of my not smoking, and of being crowned the Lord\'s Ass.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ll tell you why the Ruby Room is possibly the worst place on earth to drink if you have recently quit smoking and still consider it your one true pleasure on earth. the Ruby Room is dark. the Ruby Room is red. the dj spins punk music on Tuesdays. girls in studded belts shoot pool and dance to the song \"Cherry Bomb.\" the bartender wants to light your cigarrette. the door guy wants to buy you a drink. the smoke visually filters the red-lit faux brick in perfect trashy glamour. in short, it is a place where a scene from \"desperately seeking susan\" (complete with Richard Hell cameo) could have been filmed.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand I was wearing my decadent unemployment dress. it is sheer and hot pink with ruffled sleeves, clothed buttons. like a pirate shirt that was turned into a tight fitting dress. the sort of thing you wear if you have nothing better to do all day than lounge around the house, draw self-portraits, get fucked and smoke cigarrettes.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI drank bourbon and ginger despite the fact that i had violent stomach troubles all day. I was feeling powerful and mean, so I started taking pictures of people. Jay was on a blind date with an Aquarius who looked like River Phoenix. I mercilessly snapped photos of the event. There was a girl sitting next to him, with no affiliation, who turned to me and said, <b>\"oh my god I love your hair.\"</b></p>\r\n<p>\r\nhubba hubba. there is no closer way to my heart than through hair. this is one of the reasons I\'ve felt I might be more suited to date women. the idea of hanging around the house all day, doing each other\'s hair, spanking each other and shooting self-indulgent movies in different outfits, fulfills me utterly. I dont need grad school, I dont need the whitney biennial, just give me a girl with a good bob and big lips who is willing to pucker up and pose. we chatted about her hair, how she cut it herself (marvelously) into this choppy bob with straight short betty page bangs, how she loved everything about mine, <i>what is your ethnicity? you are so stunningly beautiful. i just think asian women are soooo beuatiful.</i> she had large dark eyes creamy french vanilla skin and round red lips. she asked my roommate how he could stand to keep his hands off me. \"im gay,\" he responded dryly.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe came on so strong I was imagining myself starring in one of the episodes of drunken dyke bathroom sex that I so frequently heard about from <a href=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/\">simon</a>. But only 10 minutes after I had taken a seat next to her, she launched into a long description of her recent breakup from the dude who worked the door. Thirty minutes after that, I found myself looking around the room and offering half-hearted encouragements and advice on her love predicament.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe didnt wanna make out with me! She wanted to talk. That\'s what girls want to do. talk. Girls just wanna talk. as my thighs loosened and my sexual acquisition bubble deflated, I vaguely heard my favorite Peechees song:</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni fell in love ohoho with a moscow woman<br>\r\nyou dont impress me<br>\r\nyou dont astound me<br>\r\nyou dont arouse me<br>\r\nyr just around me!! oh oh.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the end, I found a good reason to have spent so much time on her. She is a keyboard player. She would go to a pawn shop with me and tell me which cheap Casio is better than the other. she wrote her number on a napkin with the added note, \"call me you sexy bitch\".</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy do women confuse me so? They have been taught since birth how to flirt with everyone so that when they are merely being friendly, it comes off like sex. How could I misread the signal, being a self-righteous tease myself? Was she trying to make lip-ring-boy at the door jealous? Why am I so consistently a colossal fool? a few eyelashes and puckery exclamations and I think ill be getting fingered in the bathroom. I suddenly felt bad for straight men all over the country.</p>\r\n<p>in the end, I just wanted to go home and check my email. and in the end, there is only one person that I really want to make out with. Oakland girls at Oakland bars appeal to your vanity, and in the end, they really do just like your hair.</p>'),(1219,'<p>So, what the fuck!  Does this mercury retrograde thing have to do with entropy or mechanical, physical, logical things just not connecting or plain breaking in the worst ways at the worst times?   Yesterday left me feeling like Murphy threw up on my lap.</p>\r\n<p>Wake up.  Yay, I woke up on time.  Happy noshing on cold biscuits for breakfast.  Traipse out to car.  <em>And of course the motherfucker won\'t start.</em>  Battery ran down.  But how?  Didn\'t leave any lights on.  Oh, no worry, I have jumper cables.  <em>Oops, left those in austin.</em>  Fuck.  Call work, surly intern to the rescue in a noseless red S-10.  (Well, maybe he\'s not surly but he sure is quiet.)</p>\r\n<p>Get to work.  Coding goes ok.  Get <a href=\"http://interchange.redhat.com\">interchange</a> sort of working.  ph34r the c0w.  Hack hack hack.  Code code code.  The phone, it rings.  <em>Why isn\'t this working?!?!?</em>  (important client)  Um, ok.  Hmm.  Works here.  Dig dig dig.  Oh, you forgot to pull over the files I told you to that fix the bugs you thought you were seeing.  Simple directions.  Unbidden thought of movie quote I know not whence \"ENGLISH, MOTHERFUCKER.  DO YOU SPEAK IT?\".  Sigh.  Play ping pong.  Sweaty but better now.  Hey, sonic for lunch sounds good.  Argh!  They canceled the peanut butter milk shakes!</p>\r\n<p>So I go home and read after work.  Talk to fiancee (the part of me that I didn\'t know how much it would hurt, ache to miss until it wasn\'t there).   Happy happy joy joy.  All is right with the world.</p>\r\n<p>Taking one of my hearing aids out (the right one, I\'m right ear dominant, strongly so; and the one that isn\'t fubar electronically from sweat), the fucker snaps along a join.  One half holds speaker, one half holds battery and amp.  Joined by a thin wire.  Utterly, goddamn fucking fuckity fuck broken.  And me hundreds of miles from the closest audiologist or hearing aid repair place I know.  No idea where to go, no idea who is on Blue Cross Blue Shield, barely enough money to buy a pot to piss in much less a window to throw it out of. Fuckshitgoddamnfuck! Choke panic back, real hard.  OK, well, I\'ve had worse moments.  Not many.  But enough.</p>\r\n<p>Car not starting: 8:45 cst.  hearing aid going *snap*: 11:40 cst.  What the fuck was it about 6/26/01?</p>\r\n<p>But I lucked today becuase the yahoo-maps-closest hearing aid place fixed my aid for free (the right one, only mechanically broken), and quoted me a really good price to fix the other one elctronically.  Yay, in a few weeks I can hear in stereo again!  And ork didn\'t fire me (!PHB must\'ve read the email I sent him last night explaining what was up before I hit the fireingly late time zone).  wheee!</p>\r\n<p>So pleeeez tell me that this retrograde fuckwit planet is done jerking itself off all over the ass end of my universe.  Or else I\'m going to stay home from work tomorrow and pack up to move to Idaho to become a pig farmer.  Oink!</p>'),(1220,'<P>rowan is big and stinky.  the more he gets petted the more intrusive he gets.  he loves his food too.  he pants and grrs and nozzles his way under the arm.</P><P>\r\n\r\ntry typing and petting rowan at the same time.  typing must happen one handed.\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nrobotec9:	rowan is stinkums\r\nrobotec9:	he smells like rotten fried chicken\r\nrowan:  	jingle jingle, snarf. \r\nle elly:	that is a specific smell\r\nrobotec9:	rowan is greasy\r\nrowan:  	*snarfle*\r\nrobotec9:	the more u pet him the more intrusive he gets\r\nle elly:	like a lot of people i know\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1221,'<P>i met this great girl in oakland last weekend.  she is really cool.  she promises to string little white lights all over my naked body.  she wants to put me in a pink tutu and fuck me with the skirt up over my head.  she wants me to keep her under my desk.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni want a house in the wooded interlands of the santa cruz, san jose puchase.  with no one around, we could make all the noise we want.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthis is what i was dreaming today while sitting in my meeting at a so-cal university.  i was dreaming of cabins in the woods with this strange girl.  alternately i would flip screens on my laptop from boring demo pages and read the messages she sent me.  i would giggle.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni dreamed she was under the conference table.  trying to get at me.  she sent me a message, \"i am under the table, pinching your inner thigh, little pinches.\"  and i am talking about dynamic websites in the year three thousand four.  porny.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy day was set like this.  when i got on the plan this morning the stewardess doing the obligatory spoken word on airplane safety said, \"...fasten your seatbelt securely over your lips -- i mean hips...\"  a chortle rippled throughout the plane.  this is my life.\r\n</P><P>\r\non the plane ride home, my boss sitting next to me asked me some question or it just came into coversation that i met this girl in oakland and it had me going up there on occasion during the week.  traffic is a wasteland between santa cruz and oakland in the post work rush time.  it takes hours to drive the eighty something miles.  i tell this to him by way of wishing i could work remotely from oakland with this girl under my desk at all times pinching my inner thighs, *pinch*.  he does not go for it.  he counters with a story of delerious drives from san diego to orange county to meet up with the woman who is now his wife.\r\n</P><P>\r\nlife rolls on.  i have a tooth ache, two actually.  i am considering the door knob, string tied to tooth mechanism for removal.  dentists are a joke.  good night.</P>'),(1222,'<p>\r\nA few weeks ago k-dogg and I went to spider house to study. While there we happened upon a sign saying something to the effect:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"It has been brought to our attention that women sitting alone are being hit on by men while trying to study here at Spider house. Spider house is not a dating service, therefore please leave women alone.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nI have got to tell you, that this place is notorious for being the place to get some tail, split or not! I laugh to myself as I see the owner of Spider house read this sign. Instantly, he rips it off the counter and throws it away. Good for him, he knows what goes on around there...</p>Surely if it is a problem, wouldnâ€™t a person peeved about go else where to study? Or is it a thang that I am not aware of? Is it not flattering to get hit on? Maybe I am wrong. I enjoy going to spider house to see girls and boys, boys and boys, girls and girls mackenâ€™ on eachother. Seeing things work out like this makes me smileâ€¦</p>\r\n'),(1223,'Way back in 1991 there was a flame war that was so big, so hot, so burning, it spawned flash fires across all of the online services then available, from The Well, to CI$, to Prodigy, to AOL, local bbs systems, you name it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt began on GENIE, which was the homestead for members of the SFWA, among other laudable and notable personages.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFOLK stood for \"Friends of Linda Kaplan,\"  someone most of you have never heard of, and may never hear of again.  She was a MAC-o-phile and psycotherapist, an intelligent woman in a cyberland where most women were similarly blessed, since back then, only men and boys used modems.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe Folk Wars were notable for two reasons.  The first reason is big, all about privacy and copyright and intellectual property and one of the first disputes on who had permission to read your email, and under what circumstances, that i just don\'t have time to capsulize it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe Second was a little treatise written by Stephen Kahn (you may know him, another macophile and prof at some university in portland).<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe called it \"The Energy Monsters.\"  It outlined a group of people who came online and sucked the life out of forums for expression. Their methods were sometimes subtle, sometimes blatant.  But the upshot of it all was that some people seem to enjoy finding new ways to make others feel insignificant, and those people, those energy monsters, are so talented at what they do, sometimes nobody realizes what they are about, even when their sucking of the energy and light have been so successful that the forum has disappeared entirely.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFamous sysops of famous online bbs systems have been felled by energy monsters.  Entire online services have been sucked dry and sent to the big bit bucket in the sky by these creatures.  More than one innocent forum member has been reduced to tears of frustration by them, and they prosper.<br><br>\r\n\r\nEven here, even on swinney.  They are among us.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLucky for us, the \"Great Kahn\" had a sure-fire solution to the problem.  It\'s ever so easy, and you can do it, you can make them go away!<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo simple!  DNR   Do Not Respond.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen they come at your posts, your soul spilling, your confidences to your pals, pretend that they didn\'t.  When they come at your friends, pretend you didn\'t see it.  Ignore them.  It is the only way to make them go away, and IT WORKS!!<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1224,'<P>I got on the A train this morning and a woman tried to make room for me on the loosely packed packed bench lining the side of the car.  She apologized when she noticed that no one else was making any kind of effort thus nullifying her attempt.  I found the incident so compelling and rare for New York City.</p><P>I was back home in Idaho for Christmas and I found myself recoiling in alarm whenever someone made a random friendly gesture or nicety on the street.  After living in NYC for over two years now, my immediate reaction to someone who is friendly to me for no apparent reason is something like: \"What scam is this guy running\" or \"Fuck, another bum begging for money.\"  Of course, back in Idaho, people are just friendly and I readjusted myself within a couple days, but in NYC these \'survival skills\' feel necessary because most of the time someone saying \'hi\' to you the street really is just trying to get money from you... or get in your pants.</p><P>Thinking about culture shocks, I remember one day trying to carry a large, heavy box onto the subway and falling down the stairs, twisting my ankle in the process.  The expected helping hands that would have been abundant in the town I grew up in were conspicuously absent.  In fact, I was completely ignored, people actually stepping over and around me without even looking down.  Maybe I\'m just an easy person to ignore?</p><P>I\'ve wandered from my initial thought.  Living in an environment surrounded by so many people compressed together so compactly who have trained themselves to turn a cold shoulder and ignore the fact that so many live in such close proximity can create strange reactions.  A 35 yr. old woman tries to make space for me to sit then looks up at me and smiles while apologizing when she fails.  She goes back to reading her magazine, shutting herself off from the crowded world around.  I notice a diamond ring on the ring finger of her left hand.  I notice this because I feel somehow attracted to her, like some strange connection has sparked between us.  Her friendly overture had taken me off guard.  Her smile had drawn me towards her and made me smile back.  Something that would be an expected everyday occurance in the place where I grew up has become major event in this cold world where I now find myself.</p><P>Then again, maybe I just need to get laid.'),(1225,'When i was in elementary school, we used to always have \'hot dog day\', \'movie day\', \'pancake day\' etc where we\'d all meet in the gym and eat hot dogs or pancakes and maybe watch old yeller or the jungle book. we always had big christmas parties and big halloween parties. on rembrance day all those in girl guides and boy scouts would wear their uniforms and we\'d have a big ceremony. my elementary school had a lot of traditions, it seems. anywho, on rembrance day (i think you guys call it vetrans day, november 11th) i was wearing my girl guide (scout) outfit and i had to go to the bathroom. i went to school in a sandstone building built sometime in the 1910\'s (19-teens?) and it was a massive building with secret hallways and a haunted attic. i had to go down 3 flights of stairs to find a bathroom in working order. by the time i got there, i had peed my \'pants\'. let me tell ya, peeing your \'pants\' while wearing a skirt is pretty lame. its funny now, sure, but when you\'re 9, wetting your pants is bad enough. do you need to be wearing a skirt where there is little to know absorbant material? thats right.. i made a puddle.<P>Today we\'re having a hot dog day at my college. I\'m not wearing a skirt today. whooo.'),(1226,'<p>There is an archetype that was never real to me, never made sense, and now she is <b> HERE</b> looming over me like a big black lumpy howling pile of flaming bloody refuse.  she stalks past me, unseeing, her feet shake the ground, and the skulls strung on her breast rattle, and boiling tears fall from under her eyelids as she stomps and tears the flesh of her babies.</p>\r\n<p>Kali is cutting a swath through my psyche, spawning and swallowing life in one flash of her sharp glistening bloodstained teeth.<br>\r\nFor weeks I was praying for her arrival, I was fervently willing the gore to flow in the streets like a flood.  I was turned toward the dark but I didn\'t see it coming, and as I finally caught a glimpse it plowed me over like a tiny straw figure, smashed me in its greasy black tide.  I felt its weight and let it send me under for a good long while.</p>\r\n<p>So I\'m paying homage now, and Kali doesn\'t smile at me.  The Dark Mother has initiated me into her ranks, and I live inside her belly and she in mine.  I am a wounded, snarling mother, burying afterbirth in moonlight, scrabbling, bleeding into the dirt and sobbing and gibbering at the moon.</p>\r\n'),(1227,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1227_1.jpg\">'),(1228,'I went to a concert last night. Ezra drove. I can\'t drive stick, so he always gets stuck doing it. We were going to see a well-known band, a great band really that anyone who\'s in their twenties or thirties and living in this time and place, having had a decent education and adequate exposure to the media, has likely heard of and listened to. Ezra and I were tired. We\'d been out late the night before on another disorganized trip to a bar, each one in our group separately drinking and chatting with strangers, and nobody got to sleep before 3. And after suffering through work, there we were in traffic. <br><br>This band came out of a college town, had a huge hit a long time ago, and ever since have been churning out new sounds, gambling with their marketability in order to be artists and constantly change. They\'re reluctantly famous, and have managed to gain fame by making strange new music. Critics rave about them, and people have listened and felt they were being indoctrinated into a fresh world of sound and gone out and bought the records. As musicians, they\'ve made not-for-the-masses music, and somehow the masses have noticed. They went on stage before maybe 25,000 people, and there wasn\'t much bullshitting with the audience by the lead singer. No real fishing for applause.  At one point he introduced a song by saying, \"This is for all of you who are stuck in traffic every morning, and then on Saturdays and Sundays too.\"  <br><br>\r\nEzra and I were stuck in traffic forever, just trying to exit the freeway to get to the amphitheatre. I got high in the car, and let my eyes gaze along at all our fellow concert-goers. Oddly they were most often found in groups of three, driving Jettas. I was cynical about most of them, not looking forward to finding myself just one of many, another nameless wonky twentysomething with rectangular-frame glasses. We wound our way through the prescribed course, our cars like lemmings. Ezra joked we were probably all being led off a cliff.  Along the way Ezra gestured to some guys in a Jetta, gave them the hang loose sign, and they, probably high as me, hooted and yelled back something to the effect of \"Yeah dude!\" and turned their music up--some hip-hop Ezra recognized. Down an endless drive we stopped and started, past the empty parking lots of various office parks, until the pavement ended, and we drove down into a parking lot lined with dirt and grass.  Some guys, probably no more than 18, were standing around a car having a tailgate party.  Ezra and I were anxious to claim a good spot on the lawn, because we only had lawn seats, and I found myself wondering at these kids who didn\'t seem to be in any hurry to get to the show, hanging out and laughing in the evening sun.  Or maybe this was just an amateur business venture.  They looked at Ezra and I, surveyed us and nodded, gestured as if to say, \"thanks for coming\" or \"great to see you here\" or \"ain\'t it cool to be here tonight, alive and young, going to see this concert and the weather\'s fine.\" They were drunk and happy as hell, and I just sort of smiled sideways and looked on.  One pretty kid with short blond curls looked me in the eye as we passed, came up to the car and said, \"You wanna buy a beer, dude? Only a dollar.\"  I smirked, a little high and paranoid, not sure what to say or whether this was really happening. My hands moved slowly to my wallet and I got out a dollar. Handed to him and he handed me the beer inside the car, below the window as if hiding it from someone, or maybe just because I was quick on the uptake in reaching for it.  I kept thinking how strange it was that all these people we passed were looking me in the eye, and smiling, acknowledging me like this was Woodstock and we were all here for peace and solidarity. Another young kid, this one cockier than the first, came past the window and said, \"Did you just buy a beer from that guy?\" <br>\r\n\"Yeah,\" I said, taking my second sip.<br>\r\n\"<i>That</I> guy? That shit\'s laced, dude. That\'s pure GHB.\"<br>\r\nMy heart stopped. Or it felt like it did. I had that awful sinking feeling that I\'d done something dumb again, that this time I\'d pay for it, that I should never be so trusting or thoughtless. GHB killed people didn\'t it? Oh, god. I was tripping already. Something was overcoming me, in my blood, the ecstasy had so quickly the last time I took it.<br>\r\nEzra laughed, clearly in on the joke, and said, \"Yah. Check for holes.\"<br>\r\nThis didn\'t seem funny. The kid must have seen the worried look wash over my face because he stopped laughing a bit, and said, \"I\'m just fuckin with ya, man. It\'s cool.\"<br>\r\nI wasn\'t sure what to trust. High and paranoid now, and feeling like I might still be in danger of ODing on some drug I\'d never meant to take, I tried to breathe easy and calm myself down, recognize I was, in fact, on drugs (marija-ho-ho, as we\'d called in high school) and that this scare thing had happened before. I needed to chill out.<br>\r\n\"You don\'t think this is really laced, right?\" I said to Ezra, as he drove up an incline and into the wide field that was the main parking lot. <br>\r\n\"No, dude. You\'re high and aparanoid. And why would he have a reason to lace cans of Coors with GHB? If those kids had GHB they wouldn\'ta been givin it away for a dollar.\"<br>\r\n\"Ok. Thanks,\" I said. It\'s good to have a friend who can swiftly talk you down.<br><br>We made it through the obstacles and found ourselves aÂ squareÂ of lawn. We listened intently, talking only a little here and there,Â sometimes standing up to see the stage. I stood there on the blanket with Ezra, surveying the vast mob trapped between walls, still a little stoned, and I felt old. I used to be a teenager who looked people in the eye and liked being in throngs cause it was all new to me. I feel like an old person who can\'t stand crowds or inconvenience. I\'m less idealistic, and though idealistic song lyrics might move me, I\'m always reminded of\r\ncompromise these days. Take love, for example. I always thought it was\r\nsomething unquestionable and pure when you found it. I understood it\r\ndidn\'t always work out, but I believed that meant it wasn\'t \"true love,\"\r\nthat it must have been sort of a delusion from the start. Now I know\r\nbetter. I also believed that if you knew what your passion was, it was\r\njust a matter of sticking to it, and everything would work out, but it\'s\r\nhard to think about passion when you just go to work every day, and drink\r\nwith your friends to wipe away the machine-like patterns of motion you\r\nfall into on weekdays. I just don\'t want to end up so consumed by the\r\nmovements of money (gone as soon as its made, like my mom keeps saying) that I can\'t enjoy the present.<br><br>\r\nWe got sort of beaten down by the crowd, and we weren\'t really close enough to the stage or the speakers to have the music wash over us like it should, when you\'re there seeing it live. We observed from a distance, and watched the projected images of hands on guitars and the singer pinched face as he sang.<br><br>\r\nWhile the band was playing their encores I found myself staring at the stars, picking out a bright yellow one that was probably Venus, and thinking that even though I was just one of many--my individuality and my personal love for the music hopelessly dwarfed in this mob--it was good to be reminded of something that was so deeply important when I was 18: that we\'re all growing up together, a part of some whole, sharing common loves and common pains, and with enough lightness and art maybe we can keep each other from getting lost, from feeling too alone in the numbness and compromise which are inevitable, cause time doesn\'t stop, and neither do bills or heartache.'),(1238,'<P>at 6:30 this morning i was going north on scott street. the wind was in my hair; helmetless because there are so few cars at that hour... and because i like to. it was almost warm enough, not quite, skin alert as the breeze moves right through my clothes. at 6:30 air is misty and light feels new.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>thoughts flit in, out, trees pass overhead. a word enters my mind: inchoate.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>couldn\'t remember what it meant, didn\'t know where it came from, but i could feel the word in my mouth: in / co / ate. i knew how to spell it, could feel in my mouth the contrast between pronunciation and spelling, the h dragging at the back of my tongue, thickening the c.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>inchoate.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i taught a yoga class. got on my bike, taught another. got on my bike, came home, ate a salad, climbed back into bed. when i woke it was still there, inchoate, so i asked qbot to look it up in merriam-webster, and the bot said</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\n&lt;*qbot*&gt; : being only partly in existence or operation; especially: <br>\r\n&lt;*qbot*&gt; .  imperfectly formed or formulated : FORMLESS &lt;misty, <br>\r\n&lt;*qbot*&gt; .  inchoate suspicions that all is not well with the <br>\r\n&lt;*qbot*&gt; .  nation -- J. M. Perry&gt;</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i wanted to tell all of this to someone. thought of you. and here we are.</P>\r\n'),(1230,'over the years i have done things that embarrassed my children.  interestingly enough, the things that embarrass mandy do not embarrass elly, and vice versa.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhere are some of my favorites<br><br>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>danced and sang right said fred \"too sexy\" in payless shoe store while shopping.\r\n<li>lined up all the chairs and played choo-choo train with my sister with accompanying sound effects.\r\n<li>made references to sex in public places\r\n<li>had serious contraception talks with boys in living room\r\n<li>made random comments of no social or political importance at great length in chat rooms\r\n<li>told childhood stories to friends of children\r\n<li>stared down soldiers in theater who tried to hit on daughter, pointed out that they were underage, and therefore, \"jailbait\"\r\n<li>struck up countless conversations with strangers in checkout lines, libraries, other waiting places\r\n<li>burped loudly in public and then said \"wow! that was the best one yet!\"\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\nsomeday they will have children they can embarrass.  i am willing to bet that they will outdo me by many miles, lol!<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1229,'<p>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.christiemclelland.com\">christie</a> lives in the lower haight above a store called life. she has a wooden sundeck and all over the place there are pictures of herself with her friends, everyone smiling.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni visited her this morning and she worked her fingers into me, under my wings. she pulled fingertips down forearms.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe showed me stacks of books, all meticulously bookmarked with post it notes. <i>this is the information i want people to have</i> she said and gestured. \r\n</p><p>\r\nunderneath her apartment, two floors below, there\'s a store called LIFE. they burn incense in the doorway, and play music for passersby. \r\n</p><p>\r\nas i wandered hazy-headed down the stairs onto the street, one of the guys who works at LIFE was sweeping off the sidewalk. he had beautiful long dreadlocks. <i>your locks are beautiful</i>  i said, and we stopped and talked about the nature of dreadlocks, and i pointed at my own messy head of unwashed rampant hopeful dreads. we grinned and nodded and squinted at each other. we shook hands.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni wandered off then, down haight, smiling at everyone.  i called chaya to lure her to sushi, no answer. i said sexual things on her voicemail about sushi, about my willing waiting tongue. i called xep and said: <i>i am having lacivious impure thoughts involving my mouth and sushi</i>. she said <i>um</i> and <i>er</i> and <i>presidio</i>.\r\n</p>\r\n<br><br><tt>(tangent: san francisco words and places that i love, that live in me, for sound or memory:\r\npresidio, arguello, sanchez, divisadero. divisadero. divisadero. valencia. the haight. sausalito. when people say \"the 280\" when people say \"east bay?\" or \"south bay?\")\r\n</tt><br><br>\r\n<p>\r\noh smiles, face all big and open. worrying that i might fall out of my body and not get back in. oh sun. someone sleeping in the grass in duboce park. putting my shoulders back and breathing into my heart. oh sun. on this day i am allowing myself all possible feelings of joy and unabashed expression thereof. \r\n</p><p>\r\nalso i thought about tuggy coming here and that was so great. i was talking to peter on the phone at the time (oh peter, good peter. he says \"it\'s alright\" and it is, when he says it, it is), and i just hooted, i yelled at the phone and the street OH TUGGY IS COMING HERE THANK GOD. also i drank orange juice. fuckin a. i looked at trees and i used communication devices and i have had my favorite pants on and favorite shoes on and off and on and off. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnow steve is upstairs meowing down at me. now it\'s time to go out there again. rad! hooray.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni know it seems cheesy but really: hooray! \r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>robotec9:</b></font> eat more candy\r\n</p>'),(1231,'<P> I opened the door to find her sleeping in a fort made of sheets and tacks, nails and chairs moved up against walls. Going to eat at Denny\'s, we get ready, talking as girls do, a practice of show and tell always and never and she and I agree that whenever one is suicidal and tries to call everyone and all, they are on their way out or busy...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I am surprised we are all still here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Wonder how long he and I will stay together, if marriage is an option and also maybe...maybe how long the preservatives on optimism may last me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> This girl in Israel. She made me wonder if women give better head than men...and I doubt any man could make me scream as loud as I do towards my mother.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> When she pisses me off of course.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1232,'<p>i need the persistent splendor<br>\r\ni need a night where i crawl to my steps<br>\r\nenervated<br>\r\nsoaked<br>\r\nspent<br>\r\nears ringing<br>\r\nlegs like jelly<br>\r\ni need to dance all the poison out of my mind<br>\r\ni need to watch my limbs move in strobe-light<br>\r\ni need to breathe artificial fog<br>\r\ni need to take the drugs i took 6 years ago<br>\r\nagain<br>\r\nfor the first time<br>\r\ni need the persistent splendor<br>\r\ni need a machine gun snare<br>\r\nbattering a crescendo<br>\r\nlike a frightened would-be bungy jumper<br>\r\nover the rail<br>\r\nmy eyes closed<br>\r\nand my lungs open<br>\r\nand my mind reeling cleansing trance<br>\r\nmy jaws tightening<br>\r\nand my belly burning<br>\r\nand my skin melting into chrome puddles<br>\r\nthat spatter walls<br>\r\nwhen pixel fairies stomp them<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni want to be so drunk with kinetic necessity<br>\r\nthat i puke grace</p>'),(1233,'<p>i was on the phone with my friend patrick tonight and he was telling me about his new apartment in chico and how there\'s no tv, computer, furniture -- \"the closest things i have to technology are my clock radio and my lamp.\"</p>\r\n<p>still i knew all the patrick things that were laying around his new space without needing to see it: books about religion (primarily judaism), nag champa boxes, cigarettes, empty cd cases (most of the cases scratched or cracked from years of sliding around in the trunk of his car), the blue and green tiled coasters, pot paraphrenalia.</p>\r\n<p>it made me think of what joanna things would be, and i was conscious of this as i walked around my apartment tonight. i think they are:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>candles</li>\r\n<li>hanging plants</li>\r\n<li>big empty mugs</li>\r\n<li>sewing pins</li>\r\n<li>socks, shoes, flip-flops</li>\r\n<li>music magazines</li>\r\n<li>postcards</li>\r\n<li>vials of essential oil</li>\r\n<li>bobby pins and tiny hair clips</li>\r\n</ul>'),(1234,'<p>I am obsessed with my space today. I spent the first third of my day in class, listening carefully. The second third I spent doing homework I didn\'t complete yesterday. The last third, which in another hour I will have completed, has been spent engrossed in thought. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI spent a little time yacking back and forth to friends over the internet, and the phone. I even watched a movie, nurse betty. The rest of the time I spent thinking about how much I didn\'t feel like socializing or talking. I watched two stick bugs make love on my living room ceiling. I sat under the a/c, and I turned the radio off. I listened to hisses and groans of the a/c, and the cicadas doing their mating calls back and forth to each other. I spent a good half hour looking at the fresh <a href=\'http://www.lepard.org/pot/pot01.jpg\'>marijuana</a> plant that my upstairs duplex-mate planted; which is groing nicely from an outsider\'s perspective. I hope it works out for him; he definitely smokes enough that he should grow it. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI decided that I was going to go get my third dose of ice-cream for the week at the local H.E.B. (grocery store). On my way to the ice-cream isle, I saw what I thought was an old friend from my days as a marine. I stopped the guy and said, \"hey, [marine rank] Ford! It\'s me, [marine rank] Lepard!\" His expression was all too familiar, but not in the way that I knew of him. It wasn\'t him, and I smiled pleasantly, excused myself and vacated the area. I went back to a thought of Ford while I was in, and tried to determine if he had lived in Austin before. He hadn\'t, as I realized that Ford is now on 8th and I duty at Camp David. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMoving on, I grabbed my things and strolled through the produce isle admiring the pretty colors and energy that the fruits and veggies emitted. I thought back to the <a href=\'http://www.lepard.org/pot/pot01.jpg\'>marijuana</a> plant, and imagined what I would be like without current restrictions of <a href=\'http://www.lepard.org/pot/pot01.jpg\'>marijuana</a> drug use. Would you be able to purchase it in the veggie isle? Or, would it be strategically placed in the brownie and cake isle? It would probably be behind glass with other smoke products huh. What if they had an entire isle dedicated to inebriation type products. Well I guess that\'s wine and beer isles huh? \r\n</p><p>\r\nI am standing in line when I realize these lines are really long, and that my hands are freezing. Wearing cargo pants, I slip the cold bottle into my cargo pocket. There are 8 people in front of me, and I am not impatient. I wait, and I wait. The redhead lady behind me is furious that only 4 out of the 5 emergency, 10 item or less lines are open. She hisses and groans to a checker walking by her. I think back to my a/c when I hear her talk. I look back and smile. I think she though I was looking at her tits, because she covered them up as soon as I smiled. So my first reaction is to see what she is covering, not being a perve just watching her arms cross her chest. I catch a glimpse of her nipples ripping through her shirt. I giggle, not intentionally but because if she hadn\'t of covered herself I would have never noticed. I really didn\'t have to share that... maybe i shouldn\'t have. C\'mon, they are just nipples, I wasn\'t drooling! [Geez!]\r\n</p><p>\r\nTurned forward, not wanting to turn around after that, I am approached by a young man saying to follow him. I do, and he pulls behind a register, and I think to myself what a cool guy, thanks dude. I place my items on the counter, and pull out my end of the bargain. Get my receipt, grab my plastic bags [damn it, I didn\'t ask for a bags!], and proceed toward the plastic bag recycling bin. I get there, place the empty bags [that\'s right 2 bags] in the container proudly. Just then my arm is grabbed and yanked from behind. A patrol man, a manager, and the checker are in my direct line of site. I smile and the manager says to me, \"I saw you place something in your pockets. Would you mind emptying your pockets?\" I comply, as instructed. The patrol man asks if he can frisk me. \"Sure!\" I say. He does, and then asks me what I was doing there. I explained to him that I came to buy some ice-cream. The puzzled checker and manager look to each other, and then point out the obvious to me. I am holding to pints of Newcastle Brown ale and large Hass avocado, but no ice-cream. It was a shock to me too! I almost said, \"Hey, how did I get this?\" Then I realized that wouldn\'t help current matters. The patrol man had the nerve to ask if i was stoned. I told him I didn\'t have to answer that. [security guard\'s can\'t do that shit, plus I wasn\'t stoned. Did make me think about that plant on my front porch and how hard it would be for me to explain to authorities that it is not mine. And, because my whole ice-cream to beer story was so foggy...]\r\n</p><p>\r\nWell, things smoothed out nicely after that and I proceeded to tell them just about everything that I have told you, save some major detail. They apologized for questioning my integrity, honesty, morals and all that but asked me to not place items in my pockets next time. As it most definitely is suspicious, even if you pull them out and pay for them I guess. Then the manager explained to me that I had to place my beer bottles in a bag while transporting them to my car. I explained that I had pockets, and did not care to have a bag. He obliged, maybe cause he felt guilty. I smiled at them, and walked away. As I am walking out the door, I notice that I managed to be walking out at the same time as the hurried redheaded, nipple woman. She looks at me, I look at her. I smile, she scowls. \r\n</p><p>	\r\nI am home now. All is safe in my little world. I had a good 3rd of a day. Smiling, drinking a beer, being me.\r\n</p>'),(1235,'A feather falls crisply to the bricky ground<br>\r\nfrom its canvas of summer wind<br>\r\nhovering at the side of a faceless facade,<br>\r\nhigher now, to the tower\'s top.<br>\r\nThis plateau, crow\'s nest of some urban craft,<br>\r\nspires reaching up towards heaven,<br> \r\nand wires cascading down like unkempt hair<br>\r\nor tendrils, transmitting from this<br>\r\nstarlight static height,<br>\r\nwhere rain lingers weightless, and heavy winds<br>\r\nblow upward while the sun bakes this<br> \r\npanoramic window of crackled tar.<br>\r\nThose hazy humid eves and days brought<br> \r\nmirages of summer seas and rivers,<br> \r\nblanketing sweltering bridges and boroughs<br> \r\nwith mist and the oasis-like haze of heat,<br> \r\nhave become now a tangible testament to the<br> tenative, forged by tick-tocks of passing time,<br>\r\nchiming anticipation from the awaited.<br>\r\nCelebrate with me, with joy,<br> \r\nour sunburned palms turned towards heaven.<br>'),(1236,'<p>Last night.  Late.  Walking home from the subway station.  I went one extra stop so that I could take a longer walk home through more attractive surroundings.  Upon leaving the park pathway, I saw a piece of VHS tape wrapped and tied to a post preventing people from driving dump trucks along the path I was treading upon.  It was waving about like a tube worm and shimmering in the moonlight.  One of those moments where you pause to consider the mystical qualities of something that for all outward appearances, seems to be trash.</p>\r\n<p>This long stroll affords me a nice shortcut via the very small parking lot of the IGA behind my building.  Last night, I noticed a single car parked in front of the pop machine in this parking lot.  This, in itself, is not so terribly odd, however, the passenger door was open.  That was odd, but as I got closer, I noticed a leg begin to poke from the confines of the car.  Realisation and a slight chuckle as I noticed the couple inside notice me noticing them.</p>\r\n<p>This is not the first time I\'ve witness people in that exact same spot doing <i>stuff</i> in a car.  The last time, I was walking with my friend Ali, around the same time of night.  We have just recently arrived on the train, upon which we were drinking airplane bottles of whiskey mixed in cans of cola (nasty, \"I feel like a teenager again\" stuff).  We needed to visit Pasqualie\'s 24 Hour Supermarket to purchase coke to mix with some rye in order to keep drinking.  On our way back, we saw a couple making out in their car.  Of course, some jokes were made on our part, such as Ali\'s: \"When it\'s over, I\'ll wash your back with a can of your favourite flavour.\"</p>\r\n<p>I can\'t quite put my finger on it, but it does make me wish I owned a car so I could see what all the fuss is about.  Maybe this pop machine is the pure mechanical embodiment of Cupid, spreading throughout the world (or the parking lot) it\'s message of love and happiness (or refreshment and carbonation) to one and all (or people in need of a place to fuck).</p>'),(1237,'<P>Here\'s a pic of my friend Jeremy.  As you can see it\'s not a completely ruined mouth, but a slightly damaged mouth.  So I pushed some limits of the truth while retaining some parts of it.\r\n</p>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1237_1.jpg\">'),(1239,'what is going on?<br>\r\nboss calls me up to his office<br>\r\nas always, I think I\'m about to get fired<br>\r\nhe hands me $1000<br>\r\nwhat the hell???<br>\r\n'),(1240,'<p>so I was at Joseph\'s postage-stamp of a studio, over that famous sixth st. vietnamese restauraunt. we\'re talking traffic noises, vicious cycles, Andy Warhol\'s ghost. clicks and cuts. this is the first time I\'ve spent any amount of time with him in about five years. feels good, to hear his cheery voice, his \"hold that thought\" chirp when he leaps up to make adjustments to the coffee maker. such strong coffee.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the old rhythms of rediscovered friends. like coming home, if you\'re lucky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and Joseph\'s got many things new around the house, most notably all these stenciled phrases, quotes, words. on posterboard, picture frames, up walls, down the legs of chairs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he pulls out a multimedia thing he did on plexiglass; two juxtaposed poems, printed between a set of angel\'s wings fabricated from circuitry. Unfortunately it\'s decomposing, wires and paint flaking off. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>but I love the central quote on this erstwhile masterpiece of his. it sends chills up my spine. I say, let\'s cut another stencil for this and put it up all over SF. on cracked sidewalks, in the corners of fraying billboards, on the walls of blind alleys:</p>\r\n\r\n<center><i>She said: What is history?\r\nAnd he said: History is an angel\r\nblowing backwards into the future\r\nHe said: History is a pile of debris\r\nAnd the angel wants to go back and fix things\r\nTo repair the things that have been broken\r\nBut there is a storm blowing from Paradise\r\nAnd the storm keeps blowing the angel\r\nbackwards into the future.\r\nAnd this storm, this storm\r\nis called\r\nProgress.</i></center>\r\n\r\n<p>(from \"The Dream Before\" by Laurie Anderson)</p>\r\n '),(1241,'<p>the piano was just<br>\r\noutside the foyer<br>\r\nthe tiny linoleum square<br>\r\nin the sun state cookie<br>\r\ncutter structure<br>\r\nof edward scissorhand<br>\r\ninfamy<br>\r\nand inside my mind<br>\r\ni hear the melodies<br>\r\ncanter like self-propelled<br>\r\ndominos in an<br>\r\nunending cadence<br>\r\nthe misery and lies<br>\r\nturned to crystalline tone<br><br>\r\nand in this creature habitat<br>\r\nmade dull and fenceless<br>\r\ni see your roof, dotted<br>\r\nwith calico latter-day cats<br>\r\nand so many cigarette butts<br>\r\ni sat before the holy<br>\r\npercussion altar<br>\r\nand made myself a<br>\r\nrock star, a<br>\r\nwarrior poet<br>\r\nsomething better than<br>\r\ni was<br>\r\nwith the way my fingers<br>\r\ndanced<br>\r\nmy aching center gasping and praying<br>\r\ntriads<br>\r\ntrills<br>\r\narpeggios<br><br>\r\ni wrote the soundtrack<br>\r\nfor a lifetime, this time<br>\r\na minor key<br>\r\npiano melody<br>\r\na sanctuary<br>\r\nimmediate<br>\r\nemotional<br>\r\nveil-renting<br>\r\napocalyptic in its melancholy<br>\r\nand stupefying to dumbness<br>\r\nwith its witchcraft<br>\r\nand all the sound produced<br>\r\nand traduced<br>\r\nreverberating forever in your<br>\r\nrotting walls<br>\r\nforever the comforter<br>\r\ni pull to my chin<br>\r\nwhen the stark landscape relief<br>\r\nwasted gift, sets in<br><br>\r\nin that fading wooden resonance cavity<br>\r\nin that crumbling cinder-block homestead<br>\r\nin that fucking nowhere town de florida<br>\r\nis entwined<br>\r\nsanity<br>\r\nhope<br>\r\nwretched belief<br>\r\nprecious sadness</p>'),(1242,'<p>\r\nend of workshop on a friday night. zoli\'s place by seven. pub dinner oh so credit card good and some low\'z to keep my wistle wet. two hours later the mission began. dj shimon at turbo; conclusion to plan.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nloft off spadina. office bedrooms. glassed in studio. full of records, decks, g4, gear, red couch you sink into. punks, rockers, ravers. unnamed girl so hot....so hot. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nthe old man delivers some good shit. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsketchy driv mission to get the AO.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nawkward alone time. i stay. many leave. two stay. i feel like im interupting some kind of session between a man and a woman. the troops return. all good. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncrush crush, slice slice. the new ten dollar bill hid its new design. we should have used a twenty and put the new ten on display.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nturbo: dj shimon.<br>\r\nthe system was too loud even with ear plugs. his mixing was crap and we only stayed for what seemed like much more then forty five minutes. i was zip zip zap zap booooooooooooyaking for a long time. we left and it was good. the air outside was hot and sticky, but the open air and the sounds far away from us were much better then the sounds overpowering inside. mission 3; get home(zoli\'z)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe long was was long and walkative. 45 degree jay walking all the way. get back, roll a five and sit down at the glass table. nothing on tv. nothing on tv. nothing on the fucking tv. conversation is cracked out and late at nightish. sleep onthe pull out bed with a duvet cover. sweet in and out sleep. too lazy to walk to the fridge to get water. in and out. in and out. up. clean up. breakfast. oh so good. \r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nCHAPTER TWO\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\ngot to gords at aboot four. rented gauntlet for ps2(fucking ubergoodgame) and sat and skinned up to the sound of the archer and the warrior. random people i dont know show up and say there is a party on gords porch, organized in haste by his sister oj. boooyaka i think inside as gord talks of boycotting the shindig. we play along. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngirls galore! like im talking GIRLS GALORE. hey hey we\'re the monkey\'s and girls say we monkey around(but they laugh and smile). thebeer and food is flowing. homemade sangria is good when you get a chunk of mango and kiwi stuck in your teeth. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nall night i talked to people who had a lot to say and seemed very interested in what i had to say. i met a lot of really cool fun people this weekend and i finally think there is a new crop of people on the scene. not just yet, but slowly but surely people are remembering my name(i forget most. im awful that way). canada day weekend was ottawaless and im glad it was in so many ways. im glad i spend the long weekend at \"home\" in toronto. i havnt been home for two nights and couch crashing is just so good on a long weekend. my hair is bedhead factor 12 right now and i dontknow how old my cloths are. there is still a plan for today. updated soon. '),(1243,''),(1244,'<p>sweet jesus.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is this what 32 is?<br>\r\nbirthday today.<br>\r\ni remember the days when i thought that was so old. forver in the future. and now that im here im thinking i\'m just a kid still. so much more still to learn. to see. to be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>not enough time. that is my biggest fear. that i will run out of time. when i hit 30...i started feeling mortality. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>now i am just<br>\r\n\r\n 32<br>\r\n\r\na babe in the woods<br>\r\n\r\n...and...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>self-involved. over-organized. giving. untaking. constantly craving good comments, but always expecting bad ones. i strive for perfection. i\'m good. i\'m bad. i\'m ugly. and sometimes i even think i\'m awesome. i want someone who can take what i can give, and give back as much as i need. now that i can ask for it. a cancer to the 10th degree. i am the cosmos. i think that one day, i will be so happy i\'ll just implode. i\'ve come close. and even made it a few times, in my dreams. i overcommit. i indulge. i fantasize. i daydream. i never cease to keep surprising myself with the things i do. i am erotic. i dream of sex. and kinkiness. i make my own porn. i think that one day, i will get to be all of these things. i always want more.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am haunted by waters.<br>\r\ni am yearning for a roadtrip.</p>'),(1245,'<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">â€œ I guess \r\n  I have a very loose interpretation of â€˜work,â€™Â Â because I \r\n  think that just being alive is so much work at something you donâ€™t always \r\n  want to do. â€ -Andy WarholÂ Â </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I am without propulsion. \r\n  Lingering around my bed with constant dual desires. Stay In Bed. Get Out of \r\n  Bed. Read, don\'t read. Write, don\'t write. Each day, wanting to be the first \r\n  day I do something new. Instead, I let everything go. My apartment is a mess. \r\n  An elongated sleep pattern has been my companion for a few weeks now. A lot \r\n  of writing happens as I wait to fall asleep. I need a little recorder so I can \r\n  speak into it, I\'ve lost some good materials. Memory has been the first thing \r\n  to go. I keep losing my ATM card. When I was 18 I thought my only salvation \r\n  would be to write a novel. Something frantic, fragile, violent. I survived without \r\n  doing so. </font><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">I \r\n  miss <a href=\"http://members.aol.com/zooberries/anna/sunshine.htm\">Sunshine</a>. \r\n  I miss my mentor. I miss Eon. Dear Eon, forever sixteen in my mind. I miss Indignation, \r\n  Incandesce, Indecency, Incorrigible texts. Some goals arrive in this haze. I \r\n  want to go to Spain. It shall happen. Painting is in my horizon. Something seems \r\n  to dim from day to day but I can not identify it. </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wired/archive/5.01/ffsupertoys.html\">Super-Toys \r\n  Last All Summer Long</a></font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.smcm.edu/academics/aldiv/art/webcourses/arth100/smallworld/Margin/dustheadsB.gif\"></p>'),(1246,'<p>\r\ni love my new town, columbia, south carolina. i am a page at the state house, a job that has become sort of famous around here because of a memo...do a search. anyway, i moved from florence, which really was everything you could want in a town. a town that your worst enemy would be forced to live in for all eternity. i mean, columbia is a typical southern city i guess, there\'s tensions etc., and frat boys, and frat ho\'s, and it\'s not the most bohemian place to live, but it\'s a city. a cit-t-t-t-t- ayyy! \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe have a restaurant called \"The Lizard\'s Thicket\". It has a big board at the end of the dining room with the meats and side items listed (oh macaroni and cheese, oh baby) and you get a meat and three sides like mashed potatoes and gravy and string beans and sliced peaches. All for five dollars. now i know what you\'re gonna say. you\'re gonna say, \"But Tyler, do you get sweet tea with that? Included in the price? Such a thing cannot exist!!\" Well, guess what! Tea included! I\'m not making this up!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hope i\'m doing the \"p\'s\" right.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\'s kind of a pain in the ass.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwanna know a secret? i tell everyone that i moved here so i would have a better chance of finding like-minded musicians to play with, and because usc is better than francis marion u., but really i moved because i couldn\'t find a girlfriend that i liked there. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\never heard the lyrics to the Star Trek theme song? download the Tenacious D version.\r\n</p>'),(1247,'<br>\r\nAnd in a dream we\'re connected, you, sustainer,<br>\r\nHowler, like dogs stuck with needles,<br> \r\nentrenched beside me.<br>\r\nIn a twilight aftershock, some dreaded nightmare foe rises,<br>\r\nthrough sundown-frosted roof-top windows, impeding,<br>\r\nand my slumber bleeds into awakeness<br>\r\nthis dream continuous with my first waking breath.<br>\r\nHazily, a single word of prayer begs the forgotten<br>\r\ngrip to ease and this riveting unknown evil to flee,<br>\r\nmy eyes hover there and then descend<br>\r\nfrom that frosted skyline view.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1248,'<p>the summer of my discontent</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'m quite volatile these days, i\'m told i have a foul attitude and that my anger is misdirected\r\nwhy is this?  how come i am to blame for all that has come down on me and mine?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if karma is real then i was hitler with a leather dress and bloody knuckles</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'m angry at:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><pre>\r\n- i have no money\r\n- i\'m here rather than there\r\n- i\'m married but no longer in love\r\n- my mothers significant other is quickly dying of cancer\r\n- my father is a fucking baby and i\'m the designated sitter\r\n- my speakers cannot seem to play my music as loud as i want them to\r\n- i seemed to have lost my creativity and artistic fervor\r\n- i\'m jealous of everybody\r\n- i have few friends here \r\n- i don\'t sleep anymore\r\n- my nightmares are getting worse not better\r\n- i can\'t afford sessions because i don\'t have insurance\r\n- i\'m losing weight\r\n- i can\'t do drugs anymore and all my escape routes are blocked\r\n- suddenly i don\'t warrant a phone call anymore from prospective employers\r\n- i rub my eyes a lot and the pressure i apply to my forhead doesn\'t seem to ease the headaches\r\n- i\'m out of kona and hazelnut and i don\'t have the cash for more right now\r\n- i\'m analog and not digital\r\n- my cell phone bill is due\r\n- i\'m warm when i want to be cold\r\n- sex isn\'t a release anymore\r\n- i\'m cold when i don\'t want to be\r\n- i spew venomous things at people i used to care about\r\n- i can\'t finish anything anymore...\r\n- i\'m about to lose my house\r\n- i\'m fighting all the time, it\'s all i know\r\n</pre>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n- and i\'m angry at the posts that will follow this with \'you think you\'re the only one with problems\' and \'awww, you poor fellow\' in the body of the text</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hello you, tell me again how god will only give what he thinks we can handle... hey you, i\'m through with you</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i hope this wasn\'t a mistake</p>\r\n\r\n<p>*trouble</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1249,'<P>Have you ever played <i>The Pain Game</i>?  This is played with a very fresh ex.  There\'s usually some emotional pain floating around, and one person decides they want some pay back.  They strike.  Naturally, the other person strikes back.  Typically, this very quickly escalates to the point where one person realizes that they are not capable of hurting the other as much as they\'ve been damaged and therefore completely cut off all communication.  I\'ve played both ends of this in the past and lost a damn good friend once because of it.  So it goes....</p>\r\n<P>I spent two years living, sharing my life, with EM.  I broke up with her last December, and thus commenced <I>The Pain Game</i>.  It was a stunning and decisive victory for EM.  After a few cursory rounds, she delivered the knock-out punch in the form of a long email describing her perfect life without me including how much better her new boyfriend is in bed and all around.  This is while I\'m still looking for a new place to live and staying with my sister.</p><P>As I limped away to lick my wounds, I put a block sender on her email address and made sure that she wouldn\'t be able to find out my new address or phone number.  It had gotten to the point where just getting an email from her saying, \'how are you?\' would tear a new hole in my heart.  Life went on... I resumed my life as a dancer-in-training which I had abandoned for her.  I briefly dated a fashion model looking woman which restored my shattered self-confidence.  I finally made some NYC friends which I was unable to do before because I was spending all of my time with her and her friends.  I have a well paying job that is tolerable, and I share a nice apartment (complete with jaccuzzi tub ;->) with two lovely people.  Over the course of a few months I had managed to carve out a pretty decent NYC existence for a lanky geek with few social skills, and I was pleased.</p>\r\n<P>So, this should be old news and not worth writing up here, except for the day that I was cleaning up my room and found some old things of hers.  I had gotten to the point where I could actually smile when I thought of her, but I didn\'t have any desire to keep her stuff, so I packaged it up with a letter describing why I had been ignoring her and that I never wanted to hear from her again (perhaps not in the sweetest terms), and I figured that would be the end of it.  A lot of angry words had been exchanged, mostly from her.</p>\r\n<P>Anyways, I found a dreaded letter in my mailbox yesterday.  She had mailed it to my dad who had forwarded it to me.  Hello classic fight or flight response: tunnel vision, increased respiration, heart beat....  I spent a long time staring at it trying to decide what I should do.  Read it.  Burn it.  Read it.  Burn it.  Read it.  Fine, I\'ll read the goddamn thing!  The curiosity just got the better of me... I should have burned it.</p>\r\n<P>I get really pissed off at my own frailty.  I actually thought that I\'d be able to handle it, but this palpable pain came forth and attacked me.  I can see it and hold it in my hands and scrutinize it rationally, but that doesn\'t make it hurt any less.  It\'s like there are two parts of me: one that only knows pain and bitterness, and the other which is just an observerer and can tell me exactly whats happening but can\'t do anything about it.  Unggg..... I feel like shit and couldn\'t sleep last night.</p>\r\n<P>Damn, this is long and whiney.  Whatever... catharsis.  I should feel better after I\'ve sweated 2 liters of water in dance class.</p>'),(1250,'<PRE>\r\nSun licks my forehead.\r\nI can\'t believe it\'s July.\r\nWhere has my life gone.\r\n</PRE>'),(1251,'<p>\r\nlooks like i\'m getting the p\'s right after all.\r\nam i a programmer now?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni took classes in Basic when i was in the fifth grade, i was pretty good at it too. i wrote this program where the computer would ask you a question and, depending on then answer you gave it, it would do different things. i was inordinately proud of it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut i got bored really quickly\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ntonight i\'m supposed to go to this guy\'s house to jam. his name is \"Juice\", which may or may not be a bad sign. you never can tell with musicians. he wants to play covers as an acoustic duo, songs like \"You\'re So Vain\", and maybe if we really get out there \"Friend of the Devil\". Beggars can\'t be choosers. Maybe i can slip in something a little more controversial like \"Sample in a Jar\" or \"Gold Soundz\". That\'s not what I want to do with music but it does get you out there where people can see that you play, and hook you up with other people, and increase your chances of getting a band together.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni like to write too. i can\'t seem to get past the first page or two of the Great American Novel(tm). My hard drive is filled with many chapter ones. no, i do not write chapter one at the top when i start. right now i\'m hoping that eventually some of these random chapters will start to coalesce into a larger story, and i will finish a book and move to jamaica. another idea was to compile them all into a book, UNconnected, and call the book, you guessed it, \"Chapter One\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni can\'t stand pretentiousness, it to me is the deadliest of sins. destroyer of joy. karmic car-wreck. i\'m being pretentious though, so i guess the best i can hope for is unconcious pretension. i\'m still being pretentious though, aren\'t i? and now i\'m whining. i\'m whining, aren\'t i?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nslash-p\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1252,'<p>\r\nmy heart races, and my stomache clenches <br> i gather my supplies, and ready myself<br>the first day of school school,<br> and i\'m bloody nervous.<br>\r\nwill i find the class? <br>\r\nwill they like me?<br>\r\ni hope i don\'t fall off my chair.<br>\r\nor trip and fall infront of everyone. <br>\r\ni don\'t think i\'m cut out for this, but i want to make a career of school. </p>'),(1253,'<P> Faces looking downwards, our arms sewn together and your hand , like a root pushes towards mine, barely touching the fingers. \r\nFor moments at a time I can hear things clearly and then a buzz of clearly nothing. Still, it is the sound one hears when you keep your head underwater. There is nothing but at the same time there is still some sound. This buzzing you cannot escape. And I couldn\'t escape the questions, the penetratiing stare.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I had promised him that we would go gaming as well as camping with a few friends of his this past weekend. When the day to go had arrived my decision was changed and I had stead my stubborn mind to not go. He had arrived with his friends girlfriend and that on top of everything else killed the moment.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I am sinking into the carpet. Melting into its shade and becoming unnoticeable. No one can see me. I see their soles...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> We game. I am surrounded by a bunch of Metallica people. The type of people that would kick my ass in highschool. Of course, Smiths people dress so much better. Nonetheless I was having a horrible time. Everything was tangent upon tangent and a slow trickle of time fell and fell and left me slowly. I wanted to leave. Hitchhike home. Anything. It\'s the day when you\'re angry at the world and loved ones, favorite books and food are included.</P>\r\n'),(1254,'<p>Tomorrow night I board a plane that will deposit me in the middle of the desert.  I\'ve been to Vegas before, and partied pretty sincerely.  This time I\'d like to go deeper into the seedy glamourous myth and twist it around me like a boa and make it my own.</p>\r\n<p>  My dad has this big vendetta against Vegas because he blames his broken home (and subsequent inability to cope with his life) on his father\'s gambling habit.  Actually I think in his mind the real culprit is Frank Sinatra, who made getting soused, smoking cigars, whoring, and gambling look really swank in the fifties.  Frank Sinatra and Sammy Davis Jr. are real villains to my father.  For some reason this makes Vegas all the more appealing to me, as though I am now obliged to enjoy it for the both of us.  Also I want to emulate Sharon Stone in Casino.  Must bring rolled up $100 bills to tip the drivers and snort cocaine.  Must wear diamonds. Must date DeNiro. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>Any suggestions from anyone knowing good ways to mine the magic of Vegas are greatly appreciated.</p>'),(1255,'I am going to sleep soon. It is 1:50 am here. I have to fight my natural sleep pattern, which is to stay awake as long as possible, and then sleep as long as possible. I didn\'t realize that\'s what I was doing until just now.\r\n<p>\r\nI am clinging. First to being awake, then to being asleep. I mean, I hate going to sleep, then I hate getting up. What\'s the operative word here, kids? Hate. I should gently let go of being awake, and then, gently, wake up again. I should just allow myself to be happy and feel pleasant. I will pour myself into the bed like warm pancake batter into the pan, and fry myself. With sausage.  '),(1256,'<p>\r\nI am going to sleep soon. It is 1:50 am here. I have to fight my natural sleep pattern, which is to stay awake as long as possible, and then sleep as long as possible. I didn\'t realize that\'s what I was doing until just now.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am clinging. First to being awake, then to being asleep. I mean, I hate going to sleep, then I hate getting up. What\'s the operative word here, kids? Hate. I should gently let go of being awake, and then, gently, wake up again. I should just allow myself to be happy and feel pleasant. I will pour myself into the bed like warm pancake batter into the pan, and fry myself. With sausage.  \r\n</p>'),(1257,'<p>So they say that moving away won\'t get rid of your problems. You can take the horse out of the country, but you can\'t take the country out of the horse. Or something like that, I never was very good at colloquialisms, cliches, sayings.</p><p>\r\nI\'ve stayed here. Stayed in this town, in this city, in this state. I\'ve gone on extended vacations and moved to different dwellings, but I\'ve stayed Here. Here, because I knew that my problems wouldn\'t leave me if I left town. But I\'m ready. I\'m leaving. I\'ve picked myself up, I\'ve gotten prepared. I\'ve made plans.</p><p>\r\nI\'ve changed.</p><p>\r\nAnd while playing the waiting game, I\'ve come back to what I knew oh-so-many years ago. I moved home again to save money, to condense my belongings, to prepare.</p><p>\r\nAnd now all I can do is revert back to that fateful night. I was sixteen years old, the world didn\'t seem big enough for me, the only comfort I found was in the cold wall and the carpeted floor. I hudled myself up in that corner and I cried until my eyes ran dry. I cried right up until I moved out of this room, out of this house, out of this.</p><p>\r\nAnd they say that moving doesn\'t take away your problems. But what happens when they go away? Does coming home make them come back?</p><p>\r\nI just found myself laying in the silent dark. On my back. I was waiting, waiting, waiting. Two more weeks. Two more weeks I kept telling myself, two more weeks. And then I got suddenly all too aware of the pillow laying next to me.</p><p>\r\n<i>If I just put this pillow over my face I won\'t have to hear anything. I\'ll make all the light go away. Everything will go away, until morning. I won\'t have to listen to this pain inside my chest. I won\'t have to breathe. I won\'t have to wait. Two more weeks.</i></p><p>\r\nIt\'s been five years since I crumpled down on the floor and cried until my eyes ran dry. Five years since I thought that maybe it would be okay if I just stopped breathing for a couple of minutes. Five years since I was an angsty teenager. </p><p>\r\nMaybe it <i>is</i> this room. This house. This town. This city. This state. Maybe. Hopefully.</p><p>\r\nThey say you can\'t make your problems go away by moving away. The only problem I have, though, is waiting until I move away.</p>'),(1258,'<PRE>\r\nI have no crushes\r\nbut for you\r\nmy desire is twice as tall as god\r\nthat\'s how hard I think about you.\r\nI also dream\r\nof icons for my dreams, icons for your thoughts.\r\nPreciosa, visit me soon. \r\n</PRE>'),(1259,'<br>\r\nLike stalagmites thrust upward, they bear their water-towers towards heaven\'s threshold, obese cylinder bodies crowned with dingy parapets, wrapped around.<Br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe tenements bear their resevoirs up, to both rain and sun and the ceaseless doldrum clouds of summer, wrapping their eaves, etching their masonry with a morning\'s dew and silent breaths of condensation.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTheir stares, ancient.  Their battered surfaces rendered clean by generations of eagerly brushing hands, but grown dingy; for some angles defy any human\'s gaze, only sought by finches, pigeons, and vermin for nests and roosts and places to hide above the ethereal heat.'),(1260,'tell me 5 uselss things about you?\r\n\r\n1 - my mother can\'t walk\r\n\r\n2 - i can\'t fly\r\n\r\n3 - i like cereal\r\n\r\n4 - the sexiest day was when her and i locked ourselves in a closet and touched each other for the first time. \r\n\r\n\r\n5 -  once i went down on her and she tasted like strawberry. she was so nice to me.'),(1261,'<P>\r\n1 - my mother can\'t walk\r\n</p><P>\r\n2 - i can\'t fly\r\n</p><P>\r\n3 - i like cereal\r\n</p><P>\r\n4 - the sexiest day was when her and i locked ourselves in a closet and touched each other for the first time. \r\n</p><P>\r\n5 -  once i went down on her and she tasted like strawberry. she was so nice to me.\r\n</p>'),(1267,'<P> I hate having cold sores. It isn\'t even a dislike but much more like a Hitler hatred that accompanies the small blisters on my bottom lip. They are just annoying.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> And after the tingling has gone away and  you find yourself \"cold sored\" one goes on a shopping spree. Well, at least I do. I head for Rite Aid and find the \"cold sore\" medication aisle. I buy as much as the twenty dollars left in my checkings account will allow me and then procede on towards application.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> That is why I own so many lip products.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Then the question of whether of how you came to be blistered becomes something to think about since yoiu will be spending the next week and a half with a this _thing_ on your lip. And it\'s tingling.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> You know maybe it\'s because I drink loads and loads of caffeine. Or maybe because I live off of my roommates food as well as chips and salsa. Maybe all is a cause of a low immune system...and the cold sore is just the result. Then again I had alot of sex recently. And I just started bleeding...so maybe all these things factor in.</P?\r\n\r\n<P> It doesn\'t take away from the fact that I do not like being in the state I am in. Why me? Why not the hootchie that never sleeps and fucks alot?\r\nBut it is too late to ask questions like that. I as a student of philosophy should know these things are irrelevant. But oh well. I can\'t make-out....kissing wise and all. And that sucks. It itches and I can\'t scratch it. The painful part hasn\'t come yet but I have to go since it\'s time for another application of what I wish will be the next miracle cold sore medication. And it\'s tingling.</P>'),(1262,'July 3, 1998. It was an overcast and rainy day; somewhat cool for Texas in July. I am from New England and sometimes I get nostolgic for wearing heavy clothes, so I made it a point to wear a blue hooded sweatshirt and jeans, even though it was probably 75+ degrees (F). 75 feels cool after a few days of 100 degree weather...\r\n\r\n<p>I was finishing up my last few semesters of college and signed up for summer classes and what I hoped would be relatively easy courses. I didn\'t have a job, since I was studying so much. I had signed up for summer classes in lieu of a much more prestigious endeavor; I was supposed to be in Belize, photographing some archeologists at a dig on some ancient Mayan site. It was going to be my senior thesis and pave the way for my doing other photo ethnographies. I should have been returning from my adventures in Belieze by July 3. However, things didn\'t work out for me; I was unable to embark on the journey and I lost out on all sorts of scholarships, etc. because I just couldn\'t get everything together in time. Perhaps I\'ll make it to Belize some other time...\r\n\r\n<p>Through several complicated moves, the previous three months or so had proven to be pretty rough; I was homeless for the month of April (well, I was able to stay with <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org\" target=\"_blank\">Austin</a>, but only because I knew one of his other roommates.... this is also one of the places I should have met <a href=\"http://www.lepard.org\" target=\"_blank\">Chris</a>, but I didn\'t for another year or so). By the beginning of May, I had moved out of Austin\'s place and moved into a cockroach infested/air condition deprived Co-op (the <a href=\"http://www.602elmwood.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Sandia Co-op</a>). I was stone-cold broke at this point and needed to find some work.\r\n\r\n<p>I finally found a job, as an extra on a movie set (the movie was <A HREF=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0792162617/puppyvaugn\">Varsity Blues</a>\r\n); It was one of the hottest Mays on record; the tempurature was regularly over 100 degrees and I had no air conditioning. I was working from 6 PM to 6 AM. I had classes from 10 AM to 2 PM; I slept until 5:00 or so and then went off to work. I was hot, exhausted, and virtually brain dead; my studies suffered.\r\n\r\n<p>Then came July. I had finished the first summer session in unspectacular fashion. I had a short break from classes; essentially just the July 4 weekend, classes resumed July 6. The job had ended, so I could go back to some sort of regular sleeping pattern. But, on this rainy July 3, my friend Christine called me up to go <a href=\"http://www.thamusemeant.com\" target=\"_blank\">see a band</a>. I had never heard of them before, it was raining, and I was tired. I really wasn\'t in the mood to go. But I also had nothing better to do; I acquiesced.\r\n\r\n<p>By the time we got there, the band had already started. It was still raining out and the club had the air conditioner on; the cold air on my wet skin made me glad I had my sweatshirt on. We found a couple of seats on the side of the small cafe/bar and started to watch the band. Soon, Christine got up to go to the bathroom; then again to smoke a cigarette outside; once again she got up to get another beer. It was during one of Christine\'s absences that I noticed someone sitting at the table next to me. She was very beautiful and it was hard for me not to stare at her. She was with several friends, so I figured that I would only be able to appreciate her from afar.\r\n\r\n<p>The band decided to take a break, and so did Christine; I guess another cigarette had to be smoked, or she was in need of bladder relief. Either way, I was, again, left alone at the table. One of the beautiful girl\'s friends must have notice this (and/or my staring at the beautiful girl) and invited me over to their table. We introduced ourselves and almost started a conversation, but the band started up again... it\'s hard to talk over a good band.\r\n\r\n<p>Christine eventually came back and I went and sat with her. The beautiful girl got up and went to the back of the club (for what, I wasn\'t sure), so I decided that I would follow... under the disguise of needing another beer. She, too, got another drink, although I think it may have been water. She started to sway to the music. I watched and sipped a Guiness. Eventually, she went back to her seat. So did I.\r\n\r\n<p>The band finished its set and Christine went up to the stage to say hello to the singer, whom she apparently knew. The club was relatively quiet now, as people were filing out to go home... I was still siting at my table, waiting for Christine. The beautiful girl and her friends were still sitting at their table talking about something; I overheard a little bit of it and it made me laugh. They soon included me in their conversation and we followed some strange, convoluted, inebriated storytelling; I can\'t even remember what was said. Soon, there were just four of us in the club, sitting around the table telling our stories: Christine, myself, the beautiful girl, and her friend Johnny. It was very late (sometime after 2 AM) and we all decided that we were hungry and decided to meet at a restaurant on Kerby Lane. Johnny had no idea where that was, so we did our best to give him directions...\r\n\r\n<p>Christine was riding with me and on the way to the restaurant, she decided that she didn\'t want to go. Since her place was on the way to the restaurant, I dropped her off. I eventually got to the restaurant; the only one there was the beautiful girl...\r\n\r\n<p>We were seated, saving a space for Johnny, and proceeded to order... she couldn\'t make up her mind what she wanted; I asked her if she was a Piscies (indecision being a trademark characteristic of Piscies... I should know. I am one). She said yes.\r\n\r\n<p>Our food came and we ate and made nervous conversation. Johnny never showed. It was still raining out and I walked her back to her car. She opened her car door and sat down in the drivers seat, her legs still outside the car.\r\n\r\n<p>\"If I gave you my number, do you think you will call me?\" I asked. She said yes. She reached into her car and pulled out a pencil and paper. It was still raining and it was hard to make my name and number clear. She gave me her number too.\r\n\r\n<p>She stood up, perhaps to shake my hand, maybe to give me a hug. I am usually to indecisive; choosing between two options is usually more problematic for me than taking the only option. So I chose one and stuck to it. I kissed her. She may have been shocked, but she kissed me back. It was still raining.\r\n\r\n<p>Three years later, this beautiful girl is now my wife...'),(1263,'nevermind. writer\'s block.'),(1264,'<p>\r\nyou came when i least expected you to<br>\r\nright after that whore of a man messed with my heart<br>\r\nyou came at me lustful and mysterious<br>\r\nyou finally told me you loved me<br>\r\nyou looked at other girls in front of me<br>\r\nare you sure you want a relationship?<br>\r\nwe still love each other<br>\r\nBut is it enough?<br>\r\n</p>'),(1265,'<p>I am kinda drunk, kinda stoned, pleasant. My best friend Barrett (two r\'s, two t\'s) has moved in; we will share my one-bedroom A-P-T until the first of August, at which point we will move across the hall into a two-bedrrom. How convenient is that? I mean DIRECTLY across the hall. I hate moving.\r\n<p>\r\nWe will have a super A-P-T, with washer and dryer connections and plenty of living space. Barrett also has an excellent stereo that will supplant my 1990 CD boombox that doesnt work unless you jiggle the volume control like a jillion times.\r\n<p>\r\nThere is a bar here, incidentally, called \"Gillian\'s\". It is lame. There are so many bars here, it is so nice compared to Florence. I have an idea in my head of the kind of bar that I would consider ideal. It involves plush leather couches, lava lamps, really good drinks that I have only read about in bartending books, jazz, brass rails, and excellent appetizers to munch on. There is a bar approximating these criteria in an area of town called \"The Vista\", which actually is a good example of gentrification, which you sf\'ers should be familiar with. The gentrification in Columbia, however, mostly involves businesses like restaurants and bars, though, not like sf with it\'s residential takeovers(mostly by gay male couples as I am told in geography class but of course I\'ve never seen it). anyway, there\'s this bar called the Blue Martini. It has leather couches, lava lamps, and a wide selection of tasty martini-style drinks, and even jazz 5 nights a week, but--often it is nearly deserted. Lame. I guess people here aren\'t hip to tha trip.\r\n<p>\r\nIt is nice having all these bars to choose from. It feels like you could try a different one every night. When I graduate and move to San Francisco, I hope some of you will show me a good time. I\'m dead serious, life is to short not to live out west at least once. I\'m on the first train as soon as I finish jumping through these stupid college hoops. \r\n<p>\r\nJames Tyler Lee, Jr. USC class of 2003 and 1/2.\r\n'),(1266,'<p>\r\nyo yo writing to you from the depths of the humber college dungeons. this place is a box, recycling air over and over again. I have done my snooping. i have found that there are no vents in this place. its a kleenex box and the door to get in is where you take the tissue from. alarms go off if you keep doors open too long and thugs come and \"teach\" you how to maintain the dry mouldy air inside and the fresh smoggy air outside.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just finished filling out some account business for the department i work in. i know that one day i will have to file these horrid pink forms but for now i just have to record them in a big red three ring binder. i can remember four years ago we did the big \"move\" in this place. from four ring to three ring. a revolution had occured it seemed. boxes lined the halls filled with what seemed like perfectly good binders. once opened though it was blatantly clear how backward this place had been for so long. like starting up a free box given to you as a gift only find out that the gift was a 386 paper weight. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have been in this building my whole life. i went to kindergarden here and came to work with my mom a lot when i was a kid. my old kindergarden is now the student centre and i can remember when whole buildings and hallways were not and when lockers lined hallways that look very wide and lonely now. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy career at humber might have started at a young age, but in that time i have stuffed acceptance letter, rejection letters, written an article for Humber Today, and cleaned the lockers. I\'ve worked for the Humber school for writers, the humber school for comedy, and the humber screenwriters workshop. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy contract job here now is a personal favour to me. budgets were looked at and money was shifted. a lot of trouble went into getting me here and im very grateful. the transition from ottawa to toronto would not have been so nice if i had been sitting at home with my grandma complaining and listening and being bored. Im forced to wake up at 7 and be at work for 830 and while some of the days are boring and uneventful, im not at home sitting in my room listening to music and downloading warez.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing in a nine to five type life right now is also helping me manage my time a little better. I am careful to plan things, making sure i have enough time to sleep. i really like it here at humber and i really like being back in toronto. things are coming together for me and im growing stronger everyday. the friends that mattered in ottawa have shown their true faces and in most cases im on better terms and communicate more, and with some the opposite. I have made more toronto friends and life here seems to be getting a bit more social and relaxed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nanother big thing that has happened to me since the move is my attitude. I have grown to become stronger both in how i stick up for myself and in how i think of myself. im much more confident and i have a much more \"f jackie\" mentality towards the world. Im not out to get anyone, but im not out to let anyone get the best of me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhere does this bring me? i smile for no reason on the street and i dont think about all the crap in life. i think about the good and i want the good and reject the negative. i filter things out and only keep those things that pass through easilly. I refuse to make holes bigger in the filter for anyone or anything. its time for chia...\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1268,'<p>\r\nIf I were still a little kid, I would be really pissed off right now. It\'s raining on the Fourth of July, and everybody knows what that means: no fireworks.\r\n<p>\r\nI used to LIVE for fireworks on the Fourth of July. We had cookouts, big parties where every grownup would know me but I wouldn\'t know them and that was strangely funny to them. We had hotdogs and hamburgers, and sweet tea, and fireflys, and that was all fine and dandy; I wanted fireworks. \r\n<p>\r\nMore specifically, I wanted to handle the fireworks, toss them around, and finally be allowed to light them, and run...more fun or excitement did not exist for young Tyler. My mother and I did not see eye to eye on the subject of fireworks, and the lighting thereof. She didn\'t even like letting me hold sparklers, the \"safe\" firework(they burn very very hot). My uncle, thank god, did his best to keep me from growing up a snake lighting, sparkler twirling little sissy, reassuring my mother that I would be fine while simultaneously keeping me from lighting the edge of the fireworks bag. Now HE was brave.\r\n<p>\r\nMy uncle Billy is the greatest person in the world. He has three incredible, I mean unbelievable and beautiful children, and he deserves them. I try to be just like him. I am almost as old now as he was then; I mean how many guys my age are really concerned with how they come across to a little kid, maybe 4 or five years old? I remember especially that he talked to me like I was an adult, like I thought on my own, which of course I did. When I am around children, I try to make contact, do you know what i mean? One person to another.\r\n<p>\r\nThis is really only very loosely connected to what I was saying, but....\r\n<p>\r\nI was just thinking about something I read in my buddhism book the other day, about beggars. A true beggar, according to the monk quoted in the book, will immediately fall to his knees before someone whom he percieves as being above his station; Conversely, when he is approached by someone beneath his station, he demands that he be prostrated to in the same manner. I can\'t stand people like that. I see it all the time though.\r\n<p>\r\nSince I started learning about eastern religious philosopy, I have noticed that my friend Randy embodies most of the negative charecteristics that the books warn you about. I first started to see these things in him a couple of years ago, and have slowly become disgusted with his behavior. It wasn\'t always this bad. In fact, when we were 18 or so, we had that \"on the same level\" feeling. I hated to see that go. Recently he went crazy, spouting all these ridiculous ideas, in a belligerent fashion, to anyone who would listen. I won\'t bore you with the details, but it didn\'t make sense and he started dismantling (\"customizing\") his Expedition, his TV, turntables, all his cool stuff, and his father had him committed. He was in the hospital, down ion Charleston, for about a week. They gave him medication, and he is calm again, but he still believes the crazy ideas. I\'m afraid that they don\'t make a pill for that. I have learned that some fates are worse than simple death.\r\n<p>\r\nthunder. rainy fourth.'),(1269,'i am flushed. he\'s making me want to squeeze. <p>myself and him and a lot more. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>we write a lot. provocative digital conversation.</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<p>but i am not sure how to bridge that gap. from knowing/sharing this way...to something more?</p>\r\n\r\ni want it slow. and i want it all. experience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he invited me to his house the other night. so i went. three hours of his voice. his eyes. his smell. i wanted to curl up next to him and just be. to see how close i could get without touching....freezing that moment of us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i do not even really know him.<br>\r\nso i said g\'night and went home.</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<p>i\'m going back.</p>\r\n'),(1270,'<P>just stare at her when you walk down the street together. when she looks back, laugh. when she asks why you\'re laughing, smile. when she starts yelling about how much of an idiot you\'re being, kiss her. hold her hand and skip. hold her hand and run. hold her hand, period. pick her flowers out of other people\'s planters and tell her you stole them. spray paint her name with a heart around it on an alley wall. carve her name into your arm. offer to dye her hair. tell her she looks pretty. let her pay for stuff if she offers. bring her homeless animals, one will do. introduce her to your friends as \"the rockinst girl i know.\" sit in the park and talk to her. go on dates to the library and playgrounds. tell her dirty jokes. go trash picking. talk about politics. write poems about her and put them in your zine. don\'t mention sex too much, if she wants it, she\'ll jump on you. go get tattoos together. just walk around. throw pebbles at her window at night, when she starts cursing at you, tell her you love her. take her to shows of bands she never heard. pull her into the middle of the crowd and dance with her, if she resists, force her, she\'ll love it. cook her dinner. do her homework. let her fall asleep in your arms. suck on her earlobes. call her back. sing to her, no matter how bad you are. make out in bathrooms. carve your names into a tree. give her a fake ring as an engagement present. get her mad, then kiss her. give her piggy-back rides. let her sing in your band. give her the space she needs. push her on swings. stay up with her all night when she\'s sick. let her win in wrestling matches sometimes. offer to cut her hair. pick her up from school. share clothes. Make out in photo booths. fall in love. </P>'),(1272,'<p>When I was a little kid, I would go camping with the Boy Scouts. We didn\'t have much in the way of organized activities on these trips, maybe an axe-handling class, naming trees and plants, looking for signs of wildlife. To me, and most of the other boys, going camping was a way to indulge the deepest desires of boyhood: to play with fire; to stay up all night, relatively unsupervised; and to carry weapons. We weren\'t any safer doing these things out in the woods, but the property damage numbers tended to be much lower. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMany harsh lessons were learned on these trips, and much wisdom gained. In this world, if it rains on your sleeping bag, your ass will be wet and cold. If you can\'t cook something over the fire, you\'re ass will be wet, and hungry, and cold. If you piss off everybody else, you will not have any fun. When someone hands you a sharp object, you should say \"thank you\", which is the signal that you have  got a firm grip and won\'t drop it. Don\'t say it unless you are serious, or somebody might get hurt. This may be extrapolated any number of ways. \r\n</p><p>\r\nLate at night, we would sit around the fire, poking it with sticks, lighting them and chasing each other around, talking about Air Wolf and the structure of the female anatomy, telling horrible and funny jokes, making plans for the next day...dam up ditch runoff, make pond (flooded half the park next big rain); dirt clod war; sneak out to interstate for firsthand look; hike to convenience store and try to buy cigars. As the council wound down, each boy, one by one, would get tired and crawl in his sleeping bag, and fall asleep. I was the last one, always. There would be a few minutes where I would be the only one sitting, in the darkness of warm embers, in the silence of sleeping baby boys, and hear the soft sounds of the country, deep Carolina swampland. Staring into the fire-pit, or into the darkness, I would know that I was alive, and that I was the only one still awake. </p>'),(1271,'<p>I didn\'t do anything for j4. I went to malls.</p>\r\n<p>It started with one. I forgot why I went. Then I decided: shit. If you\'re going to do something, might as well do it right. So I decided to go to every mall in central/coastal New Jersey.</p>\r\n<p>I didn\'t buy anything. I went into a store my friend used to work at even though I knew he didn\'t work there anymore. I asked the people at the counter if he was there. They said no and asked if I wanted to leave a message. He hasn\'t worked there in years, folks. I said no and left.</p>\r\n<p>I saw a girl I had a crush on in high school at one of the malls. She looked even better. She was withs someone I used to know. They pause. Oh craps. My cover is blown. They might say something. I didn\'t stick around to find out.</p>\r\n<p>I had other malls to go to.</p>\r\n<p>When I came home there were messages on my machine and computer. They spanned several hours and together they drew a sketchy picture of my poorly organized friends trying to decide whether or not to create a BBQ or attend one or whathaveyou. Friends quoting friends about what might be happening. After a while I can\'t understand what is going on.</p>\r\n<p>I call one of them. Friend: \"No decision has been reached.\" I make a statement. This is my statement to the friend and my only formal statement on the subject of j4 festivities this year:</p>\r\n<p>\"I have no interest in doing anything that goes beyond sharing a cup of after-dinner coffee with a small number of intimate friends.\"</p>\r\n<p>I hang up. I make some coffee myself and start drawing pictures. I draw a picture of a guy. His nose comes out weird. Oh well.</p>\r\n<p>That\'s my 4th of July.</p>'),(1273,'<p>There is a startling number of different brands of chocolate syrup one can make chocolate milk from, and Nathan wanted to scientifically show which brand is best (this is the same man that brought us the mac and cheese tasting).  To this end, me and a number of my peers were asked to participate in a double blind taste test and rate each unknown brand on a variety of different criteria.  These results will be tabulated and statistically analyzed whenever Nathan and his cohorts get around to it.</p>\r\n<P>I\'m telling ya\'.  Nine brands of chocalate milk, a 2 oz. sample of each to swirl around your mouth and examine for taste, texture, and color.  That\'s 18 oz. of chocomilk.  No one was feeling well afterwards.  And I\'ll tell ya\' another thing.  There\'s a big difference between different brands.  My personal favorite was #3 (which ended up being all natural), which everyone else disliked due to its \'sacchrinny\' taste.  Whatever....</p>\r\n<P>I guess there\'s worse ways to spend the fourth.</p>'),(1274,'<p>True goodbyes aren\'t said in cars.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>True goodbyes start with a coming together, a merging as much as possible of two beings into one.  They end with a slow separation; a dissolution of the bonds and a melting back into two separate persons.  Then comes the growing space and the longing glances aimed at burning a final image into your mind of the other to take with you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This doesn\'t happen when only your lips touch three or four times in a car where one then exits and the other drives off into Washington Street traffic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And now, back home in Austin, and you back in Albany, I can propose an answer to your question left at my door while I floated in limbo without you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What is the point of this?  I\'d like to skip to the end and find out, but that would be cheating even if it were possible.  While I can only barely imagine the end being a continuation of our separation, I also wonder if this isn\'t a mystery novel I\'m reading (especially with your previous mercurial behavior) that will end after a sudden twist and surprise me as to the outcome.  But I don\'t want to be surprised right now.  Certainly that would be fine then, after the fact, but now, right now, I want an \"answer I can hold onto,\" as Art of Fighting sings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But that doesn\'t seem forthcoming.  Your words and actions confuse me.  I can divide you into three different parts and find that sometimes the puzzle pieces don\'t match although they came from the same box.  You, in person; You, on the phone; You, in your writing.  I can only hope they all agree one day in my favor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So I hold onto what I have at hand.  Our memories of togetherness, pieces of the places that played as venues for our unfolding story:  a business card from the EconoLodge in Albany; receipts and bags from Brattle Books in Boston.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I purchase your favorite Lipton Unsweetened Iced Tea from the corner store as if it were my own idea, but knowing full well it\'s only a small piece of you, a reminder.  I\'ll hold my Air Force PC t-shirt under my nose until I\'ve breathed in all the scent you left behind after wearing it to bed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ll walk around in this gathering heat that only reminds me how truly far away from each other we are and hope, pray, wish, believe that we never have to say a true goodbye, if only you will it as much as I.</p>'),(1275,'So just now I was paying my credit cards.  Naturally at several points this entails listening to a few bars (or movements) worth of classical music whilst on hold.  It occured to me that this might be the most exposure some folks have to classical music, aside from maybe movie intros/outros/scores.  I\'m not a snob (about music at least, operating systems debatably ;^) ), but still I think it\'s a terrible thing to just miss out on.<br><br>\r\nMaybe in addition to the civic/economics class most (American) high school seniors sleep through, have a semester or year of cultural smorgasbord where kids are exposed to at least a sampling of classical music, classic movies, and cuisines outside of their own ordinary realm.  Eh, I can dream, right?  Maybe if people knew more of the world they wouldn\'t fear it so much, and maybe if they didn\'t fear it so much they wouldn\'t hate (it/things in it) so much.<br><br>\r\nHate makes me sad, so sad it feels like the sadness is soaking into my bones and oozing out of my skin.  Hate makes me angry, so angry I feel like flames are coming out of my soul and I see the whole world in flames.  smash rip tear and roar, burn, burn, burn and rend the forces of darkness and tyranny.  This is what I feel.\r\n<br><br>\r\nOk, so maybe that\'s a little disjointed.  Eh.  What do you expect out of someone who played with <a href=\"http://www.catseye.mb.ca/esoteric/befunge/\">Befunge</a> all day? :-)'),(1276,'moments of total oblivious happiness:\r\n<p>\r\nthe space between two beats of some really kick-ass dance music (contraction)\r\n<p>\r\non the beat (expansion)\r\n<p>\r\nwind in face\r\n<p>\r\nhugging mama--that extra moment on the end\r\n<p>\r\nsuccessful paper airplane\r\n<p>\r\nsuccessfully mixing two records\r\n<p>\r\nthat extra half-a-second when she looks at you, just oh-so-slightly too long and you know, you KNOW...\r\n'),(1277,'<P> Sometimes I wonder if I am straight or not. I am looking at this girl of Juliette Lewis beauty and wonder what I am doing with a man. Okay....okay....Juliette Lewis is a taste which I do not try to lay on anyone else. I just didg skinny chicks. Maybe because I have a pretty good self-esteem and so when I\'m skiny and I like the look then I like other skinny.....loosing my choo-choo thought there.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I have been with men all of my short 22 years of life. Yes, I like men. They are human beings and they are nice and smart and ....why treat them as if they were froma different planet, you know? But a woman would understand the crotch and appreciate it much more I think. A woman would know that sometimes red lipstick and a black slip is a fashion statement and not a hootchie statement. A woman would know that certain smells and tastes were NATURAL. And then again most importantly, she would understand clearly what it is like to be a woman. Period.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> But I love him. I think the manner in which he sometimes doesn\'t know is highly attractive. Because then I get to teach. It can be fun. And men are just so very fascinating to me. Women are as well. All the differences and details are what form this one singular person you love. But men have the ejaculation thing that I just cannot seem to get over. It\'s very interesting because you can feel the power of an orgasm coming on. At point A the thing is spongy...at point B it\'s muscles and sinew and it\'s pushing out 50% of someone ...maybe.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I am not downplaying a female orgasm....or their parts whatsoever. My friend Lily said to me once when I told her that I had a crush on a certain girl...\" You like her because you are comfortable in your relationship.\" I wonder. Could that be it? Am I so comfortable that I will start looking at the other gender? Or am I so open?</p>\r\n\r\n<P> It\'s just that sometimes a hint of cleavage and nice shoulders, a teasing smile is very tempting...coming from a girl. </p>'),(1278,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni\'ve met a girl that likes to read sports illustrated.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nshe tells me this as we play trivial pursuit and the question is to me and the answer is wade boggs and i get it right (and even though she doesn\'t say it, i think she is a little impressed).</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nit\'s after eight in the morning and i\'ve been up since before six, with just about four hours sleep- and really, i don\'t have to be anywhere, but i like the morning sleepily sometimes-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe morning, sometimes- sleepily- with coffee too strong and cigarettes and sportscenter on the television and maybe a little mcdonalds breakfast.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis girl- she reads sports illustrated. i like this girl.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis is why i like this girl:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwhen i first met her i was hanging out with my roommate who is friends with her roommate and the four of us were hanging out- in their apartment on haight we were hanging out and listening to the beatles and i say (to be cute) \'who\'s this?\' and they say the beatles and i say \'who? the beatles? i\'ve never heard of them, are they any good?\' and they laugh and i still play serious and it goes on like this for a while until we loose interest-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand then later happiness is a warm gun comes on and i say \'ooh, this is my favorite\' and she looks at me and says, i thought you didn\'t know who the beatles were- smirking-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand then later (weeks later), the four of us again, waiting for the muni to go back up to their apartment to play trivial pursuit- and we are talking about music again and i know her roommate loves paul simon and i joke about paul simon because (in my oppinion) he is not so good-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she turns to me, smirking again, and says, who\'s paul simon?.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni like her because she is cute and smirking and calls me on all the shit that no one calls me on.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she reads sports illustrated.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso- this morning, sleepily with coffee and sportscenter on, and, i don\'t know- to be cheesy me i think about the morning sleepily with coffee and sportscenter on and maybe her here too- about maybe, watching, waking up, her maybe nuzzled into my side and we\'re cuddling and watching sportscenter and maybe it goes on for a while-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand maybe we call in sick to work and watch the rerun and again and maybe the baseball tonight rerun too and maybe it goes on like that until early afternoon- in pajamas with cold coffee and hair all messy and no shower and the sun coming into the room muted through closed drapes-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmaybe.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni like her because she is quiet and a little demure and has a warm stare sometimes- and then all of a sudden she\'s surprised about something, exploding with a \'reeeeaaaallly?\' and brightly with a smile, and then aware of itself, her smile turns to that smirk and she becomes all bashful and turns to demure stare again-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sometimes, when the four of us are hanging out, our respective roommates will be talking and i\'ll be staring out a window, blanking, and then aware of myself i\'ll turn back to the room and find her staring at me and smiling.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni don\'t know- i have a crush on this girl. i hardly know this girl, but i know there is something there that i will like once i discover it.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nanyway-</p>'),(1279,'<P> I do not understand why men stay friends with women they like...you know it KILLS them, .they. know it kills them, and it shows. I really dont appreciate it when I see it in motion.Don\'t they know... She will never date you?</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I was in this position once with my ex-boyfriend before he became my ex. Nonetheless, it is slightly a position of power the woman holds over the man. And it\'s just not cool. First of all, as a chick you know they like you and it IS NOT a friendship... now, don\'t we all wish we could call it that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Truth is it isn\'t.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Like this girl I work with dated a guy and now they are best friends...supposedly. Now, she knows that he likes her. And obviously he still does. Thing is that I guess they like the pain. He likes watching her check out other men and mention how so amd so was way too touchy the other night at so and so\'s party but she loved every minute of it. Things like this are not healthy. It is much better to nip it in the bud.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P> She just asked me if I listened to country....\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"He is gay.\" She mentions as some guy I happen to know from the English dep. passes by. Just cause he isn\'t checking out your fat ass doesnt mean he is, I think. Too bad you can\'t always choose the people you work with.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Ugh. People don\'t allow yourselves to be treated like crap. Not necessarily like crap. But if all you get is \" Should I wear the black top or the white top?\" or \" Would he think I was a slut if we made out on the first date?\" then get over it and get yourself someone you can tap that hiney with.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Oh, and the cold sore is recovering...I think. Knock on wood. Since I\'ve been applying 5 different medicines on its nasty little blistering body...ugh!</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1280,'<P>I do pretty well when it comes to being drunk and doing things I shouldnt do...or so he said.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Relationships are like babies. You have to hold them up by the neck and give the right support or else they fall limp and who knows. All I know is that one has to be careful. You don\'t remain in love with someone without working at it. Why? Because the world is full of beautiful and interesting personalities. And sometimes we forget who we really want and we let go of the babies neck. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> It was a few months ago or maybe less. I remember M being late and I slightly worried over my situation. I was stuck at Rennaissance Faire without a ride home. My boyfriend nowhere in sight and so I saw this chap who had a familiar face...he had been at a few parties of my friends as well as a Kovenant concert in LA. So we struck up a conversation. We had more than a few similarities. He runs and so do I ( or so I did), makes art... and carries a dictionary in his purse. All this was refreshing. Odd. Interesting. A bit too interesting.We spoke, exchanged numbers so as to get together and go running and then I saw M waiting for me...my ride was there.And I left.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Now, it turns out nothing of much consequence occurred. It was more of how much this person inhabited my mind for a few weeks. That was the real danger. Because I am in love with someone and I didnt wish to let go of that. Still, my interest had lingered for a bit too long...M seemed different, things between us were strained, I began to start shit...I decided to take time off to truly test if this \'interest\' was a sign. And it wasn\'t.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> It wasn\'t because I didnt want this certain person. I think sometimes we meet people which represent certain things. And Darb was one of them. But its M that I really care for. Its this that I want to support. Plus, its the spring and people are just like that around that time of the year...or so I think.So, now I think I have become more aware of my psuedo-nympho-ness and it\'s helped.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Abu-dabi.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> What\'s affecting your behaviour now?</p>\r\n<P>You aint seen nothing yet.</p>\r\n<P>So do you always drop your pants in parking lots?</p>\r\n<P>Only when Im changing.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>You freak.\r\n'),(1281,'<P> I have a friend who doesnt ejaculate. he says it is negative training and so he wont do it. Why does he say this? And why do I love rhetorical questions? Anyhow...he says masturbating teaches boys to cum quickly and that is NOT a good thing unless you are Jen from Eugene who cums in less than 5 minutes.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Lucky whore.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> So he wont pet the monkey. he said he\'s had a few wet dreams here and there....it HAS to take more than just not doing it?</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Now on to my ejaculate. I think I need someone there...that and Im just too lazy...yeah, I get off somewhat but then all it is is fooling myself Im with someone or something...Im just not a boy.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Why do the work when someone will do it for you?</p>'),(1282,'<p>I keep hearing about sf kids on here and so my question is do I have what it takes to be an sf kid?  What does it take to be one? hmph</p>\r\n\r\n</p><p><center><img src=\"http://www.boltpages.com:8081/punk_velcro/photos/me/giddy.gif\"></center></p>'),(1283,''),(1284,'<P> It is a pitty..I am stuck at work. I listen to this girl and her aspirations of being on Mtv...and how they will soooo love her once she auditions. AMERICA! This is what sororities do to women.GAWD.........I need to leave.Why Mtv? Why not watch the Charlie Rose show once in a while you mindless idiot? Yeah...sometimes while visiting my parents I\'ll watch some Real World ..my sister and I making fun of the people on the show, cracking up in the unstoppable third grade way and it feels great until something better comes along. BUT, I dont want to be on the show. That and the Miss America pageant aren\'t my sole aspirations, you know? I want top make meaningless films and cover my walls in b/w photos I took,live in ripped stockings and slips with long, long hair until I get sick of it and chop it off in a very mess chaotic wannabe bob...I want to go to Spain and visit family, live in Tel Aviv\'s humidity for a few months, have multiple orgasms, learn how to cook and sew...( yeah , yeah , I am one of \'those\' women), ahhh, the thinngs one can do before they decide to audition for the umpteenth time to be on Mtv...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I want a Janes Addiction slash Red House Painters and some Betsy Johnson and some Billy Holiday Great Gatsby veggie kind of world.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Only ten more minutes to go....Thank GAWD. \r\nTake your Roxy shorts and go ....far.</p>  '),(1285,'<p>or how in this<br>\r\nheart-heavy fens<br>\r\nsoaked through marrow deep<br>\r\nwith the century\'s sweat<br>\r\nher new stink<br>\r\nlike crustacean-castle-crunch<br>\r\nin horizontal sheets<br>\r\nin flashes of brilliance<br>\r\ntoo luminous for flesh to hold<br>\r\nwith soggy socks<br>\r\nand soggy feet<br>\r\nand wrinkled fingers<br>\r\nand whetted appetite<br>\r\ni traverse this arid zone<br>\r\ni have scavenger nerves<br>\r\ni make sounds only i know<br>\r\ni wake apparitions who only notice me ever<br>\r\ni finger cave drawings with fecal paint<br>\r\ni am the great being i have always been<br>\r\nonly now<br>\r\ni have no weakness<br>\r\ni have no blind spots<br>\r\ni am incapable of misjudgement<br>\r\ni am implaccable, turgid, dense<br>\r\nwith clarity<br>\r\nand finally<br>\r\nfor the first time<br>\r\nwide awake<br>\r\ni\'m wide fucking awake<br>\r\ngoddam<br>\r\nhallelujah<br>\r\ni bathe in jacuzzi spas of sand<br>\r\ni squeeze all substantial matter<br>\r\ninto baseball-sized spheres<br>\r\nset them on fire<br>\r\nand worship the suns<br>\r\ni am the hedonist\'s hedonist<br>\r\ni am the god of abraham, issac, and israel<br>\r\ni am the god that licked elijah\'s<br>\r\naltar clean before the prophets of baal<br>\r\ni am before the before<br>\r\n the after there was a before</p>'),(1286,'<p>\r\n<li> round box full of coasters made of circuit boards (rarely used)\r\n<li> a cd with the word COASTER written on it in marker (used fairly often)\r\n<li> books: <br>\r\n  \"how to think like a cat\"<br> \r\n  \"earth science for christian schools\"<br>\r\n  \"the guide to getting it on\"<br>\r\n  \"alex grey: sacred mirrors\"<br>\r\n  \"the american ephemeris for the 21st century\"<br>\r\n  \"the intricate corruption and passionate mistakes of one girl in america\" <br>\r\n   \"the basic writings of C.G. Jung\"\r\n  \r\n<li> five (5) snowglobes: three from paris (from chaya), san diego (from oof), illinois (from xep)\r\n<li> vaporizer, brightly painted\r\n<li> two (2) incense burners\r\n<li> wind up pink fuzzy bunny\r\n<li> sparkly purple stuffed lizard\r\n<li> pair swiss army goggles (in case of sudden duststorm or need to fly a plane)\r\n<li> an old polaroid camera (loaner from egg)\r\n<li> a coffee mug, emblazoned with the logo \"electric minds\" (half full of old coffee)\r\n<li> four (4) remote controls\r\n<li> cellphone\r\n<li> gameboy\r\n<li> wind up silver angel (its wings move!)\r\n<li> unopened packet of gross looking orange stuff, labeled SUSHI GINGER\r\n<li> eyedrops\r\n<li> hot tub owners manual\r\n<li> decommissioned webcam with cord wrapped around it\r\n<li> rca cables\r\n<li> bottle opener\r\n<li> australian ten dollar bill\r\n<li> set of tarot-esque cards\r\n<li> phone jack splitter\r\n<li> two (2) brightly painted wooden cat figurines (one of them is holding some sort of stringed instrument!)\r\n<li> bus transfer from many months ago\r\n<li> various popsicle sticks\r\n<li> various loose change\r\n<li> packet of EMERGENC powdered vitamin c\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1287,'Those little moments right after you\'ve done something and you\r\nsuddenly can\'t recall why you did it.  Sometimes right in the middle of\r\ndoing it.<br><br>\r\nExample:  being tired.  wanting to listen to music and make a PB&J sandwich.  staring at CD in one hand and knife with peanut butter in the other, thinking \"what\'s wrong with this picture?\"\r\n(This happened to my friend John Hamulak)<br><br>\r\nExample: Someone gripes about the chronologically penultimate chapter of a novel coming first in a book review on slashdot.  3am.  post quasi-rant about the Homeric influence in Western literature...on slashdot.\r\nExample: accidentally making a file called \'*\' and wondering why it\'s taking so long to delete\r\n<br><br>\r\nI mention them because they\'re happening to me a lot lately.  Need more sleep I guess.'),(1288,'<p>\r\ni love yeti. he is tall with nice arms and wrists. he is very strong and can carry me around. he can carry most things around, and sometimes he does! he smells very good and sweats in his sleep. he is salty.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni like to think about the jobs yeti would be good at, because none of them are jobs i would be good at. here are some of the jobs i think yeti should have:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li> park ranger\r\n<li> forest fire fighter \r\n<li> roadie (\"where you need this amp?\")\r\n<li> construction worker (\"sheetrock today eh?\")\r\n<li> sysadmin (\"freebsd rocks your lame ass\")\r\n<li> barrista (t shirt: \"born to be wired\")\r\n<li> record store worker (\"oh yeah, that was soandso\'s side project in 92 when he was working with this band, and if you like that band you should check out suchandsuch, who are all on suchandsuch label\")\r\n</ul>\r\n</p><p>\r\nyeti plays hackeysack, hippie sticks, and rides his bike. yeti wakes up early and sits straight up. yeti walks long ways with a nice backpack. yeti loves his brother, yeti loves his mom. yeti wears his shoes out faster than anyone i know. yeti trips over stuff sometimes but also has moments of unbridled grace. yeti is kind, yeti is smart. yeti writes pages in secret places and yeti knows all about nails and hammers and levels. yeti is a builder, yeti has good ideas. yeti. love yeti. yeti has the serial cable you need and the desire to find it for you. yeti always answers his phone, and if he doesn\'t, he always calls you back.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni wish i were more like yeti.\r\n</p>'),(1289,'<p><font size=\"2\">Once I had a penpal named Jade. I lost touch with her rather \r\n  quickly, but I\'ll never forget the questions she sent me shortly after our correspondace \r\n  began...</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Are you religous? If mother \r\n  theresa was an agnostic and still did everything she has done where would she \r\n  go when she died?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Age mentally and physically.</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Do you believe in determinsim \r\n  or free will? Why?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Do you are with Heideggar \r\n  that we are all born into a facticle situation in which we must embrace our \r\n  situation as inconceivable that it be something else and move on from there--that \r\n  everything that has happened in your life<br>\r\n  to this point had to happen and it could be no other way and accept and take \r\n  full responsiblity for as actions in your life? Why?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Do you perceive yourself \r\n  more as the ubermensh(Nietz.) or the knight of faith(Kierkeggard) if those were \r\n  your only options? Why?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Do you think the good of \r\n  government should supercede the good of the republic? Why?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> What is the one thing you \r\n  want to accomplish in this life?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Do you think humanity will \r\n  survive another two thousand years? Why?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Why experience in your life \r\n  has had the singlemost impact on who you\'ve become?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> What is your philosophy on \r\n  life?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> What do you want to be remembered \r\n  for? </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> How do you perceive society \r\n  at large, women and their role in society, men and their role in society, and \r\n  minorities and their respective role in society?</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"> Based on the addictions of \r\n  most great artists, writers, poets, musicians etc., do you think it\'s possible \r\n  to become a great visionary of your art without external inducements? Why? (i.e. \r\n  Jimmy Hendrix, Edgar Allen Poe, Kurt<br>\r\n  Cobain, William Borroughs, Henry Miller etc.)</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">By the way yes I\'m bizzare, \r\n  but I\'ve met anyone that was artistically inclined that wasn\'t complex.</font></p>'),(1290,''),(1291,''),(1292,'<P> \" These two are from Turkey,\" he raises 2 pistachio nut shells and his eyes widen as he drops them into the bowl of empty shells.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Joy Division plays in the background as we both sit on suede green oversixed couch pillows. They used to be his grandmothers.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Heart and Soul\" is playing. Tom Collinses in hand I head out towards 1952 and pass old trainstations and the stains my grandmother would wear on old blouses. You pass by abandoned buildings five children each. The noise unbearable.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Heart and Soul...one will burn...\" Smokers in the next room. Somehow I dare not pick up what they offer. Yet, These walls prove unworthy filters for the smoke reaching my breath. Vicarioulsy, I am one again...like them. And vicariously through me they are not. </P>\r\n\r\n<P><I> \"\'Past is now part of the future ...present is well out of hand.\'\"</I></P>\r\n\r\n<P> Blonde dirty blondes preside inside all exteriors. But inside they are outcast redheads screaming to be let out. </P>'),(1293,'<p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>i don\'t squeeze the toothpaste from the bottom. i squeeze it up from the middle, and i never replace the cap. there are chunks of dried toothpaste all over the sink. i just don\'t care.<br>\r\n<li>i buy toilet paper in lots of 48. quilted northern. mmm.<br>\r\n<li>i read on the toilet. sometimes i flip through the 1998 ikea catalogue.<br>\r\n<li>i tweeze.<br>\r\n</ul>\r\nokay.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1294,'<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\">[Intrusions] \r\n  into the archeology of my past is unrecompensed by my heart, or something like \r\n  it.<br>\r\n  Complications [Evaporate] on days like this. <br>\r\n  Where are my [Instructions] to life ? Are they misplaced near my new coda: <br>\r\n  Be [Inspired], Be Creative, Admire Creativity.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  I am thinking of capricious girls, with their picture\'s inside my copy of Hemingway\'s \r\n  &quot; Men Without Women &quot;. <br>\r\n  Nude, a thousand kittens sliding down their backs, this was her dream, her [Invocation]. \r\n  </font><br>\r\n  <font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\">Their \r\n  [Explosion] in my skin a mark of ownership. I miss them. I don\'t know of they \r\n  miss me.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  Longing is aggravating.</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#006699\">&#147;..with \r\n  your fingers on your self destruct button like an orgasm just waiting for the \r\n  right body&#148; joann ellis, where ever you are, thank you for your words. \r\n  </font></p>'),(1295,'<P><I> \"It is different here. It is a way of life. We are ready to die if God wishes it. Just like you in LA with earthquakes...you do not know when one is going to hit? No one knows when they will be next. But if it is Gods will then I will follow. It is the way of life here in Israel.\"-woman at Ben-Gurion airport, Tel Aviv.<P> </I>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> I have just decided what I want to take pictures of for my portfolio...and it is a great weight off of my shoulders. I just read a terrif  article by Adrienne Rich where she addresses being a Jew and then not. See, I suppose it\'s because her father was one and her mother a \'gentile\' as she put it. I can relate being in the same situation and all and just having had returned from the Holy Land...Israel. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> It would seem from the outside that I learned nothing from this trip. It would seem that I blew some cash only to regret it later on. But I did have some memorable experiences. The skinny dipping in the Mediterranean with a bunch of girls from Oregon that I had never met before.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I realized, stepping onto Israeli soil for the first time ever that I am not much of what they would call a religious Jew. I have much similarities with the country in its beinf secular and all. I miss Tel Aviv the gay nightclub Ksenya and I went dancing at, and the beautiful gorgeous people which make you feel so small in relation to the world. SInce you meet them everywhere around this planet.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> THe whole deal with the Palestinians and the Jews as well as any non- Jew/ non-Israaeli living there bothers me. I see this country built on the past ancestry of another people. I can sympathize with these people who have had their land taken from them...because it said so in a book. But then again this turns circular as we quest to see whose book is correct. And that in my opinion will never happen.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I, as a Jew, should be happy with my acceptance by Israel. At least I felt very accepted. I just wish that the very people who work there, sleep there, eat there and love there would have the same privelage. It appears incorrect to see a country founded on the roots of curing injustice and seen as a safe haven for refugees, turn its back on the refugees knocking at its door because they only have room for \"enough\" and that is all.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I realized how hard it is to be homosexual in the US of A...at least I re-realized to a further extent thanks to a newly made great friend. And it\'s tiring the manner in which people take what they wish not holding both the bad and the good in both hands...rather running off with it in one. Whether they choose the good or the bad society has instilled in us since being toddlers at the playground...perhaps earlier than that...who knows.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I now know what I want to take pictures of. It\'s feminine and then not...as one could see these photos as taking pictures of objects created as social contraints. I have learned by staying in Israel for a bit how indefinately there will always be more than two sides to every story and how each side is quite valid indeed.</P>'),(1296,'<P> i dont like the word panties. its a pervy kind o\'word and it makes me think of child molestors and old men in their fifties who wear baseball caps and offer little children candy.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P> When this issue began...the entire pantie issue...was quite sometime ago. Another thing is I hate wearing pants...so I wear skirts and dresses and its <I>summer</I> si I cant do the stickings with dresses thing.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> how i do not like thst word panties...i mean what is so wrong with underwear...that and i dont understand women who have <B> \"period\"</B> underwear and <B> \"non-period\"</B>underwear...its gonna get juiced up no matter what. hey, the color of the juice, whether it be marroon or clear ( and whatever in between or off scale suits you) should be of no import.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> zat is zvy i prefer to go without <I>panties</I>. It is much easier on the laundry and fresh. Did i mention fresh? The only problem is one has to watch how one sits during this. i would like not to be handing out beaver shots all day and night long if preferrable. the way i look at it, is that i enjoy the freedome pants offer in that we can move around in any which manner without showing pussy. on the other hand skirts are just much more appealing. at least to me. so freeball it i do.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i have no skills when it comes to sitting. that and using the ATM machines at super markets. yeah, i.m lame-0. deal with it...or don\'t...just keep in mind the old men that coddle the word <I>panties</I> okay?...</P>'),(1297,'<PRE>\r\nsunday morning\r\n\r\ngloom and rolling traffic.\r\n\r\nwaiting for jay to wake up and cook scrambled \r\neggs with ingredients.\r\n\r\ni dug around for shaving cream.  i think i used \r\njays.  \r\n\r\nin the shower i shaved, cut my right earlobe.\r\n\r\nthere is a girl behind me laughing about\r\nphone messages.\r\n\r\nSun 10:07:09 AM\r\n\r\nthat crazy cat woman music is playing.\r\n\r\n\"wow,\" she said.  \"it is like the back of your head \r\nis talking.\"  \r\n\r\n\"i am staring at the back of you, listening to you \r\ntalk to me in the telephone about your \r\nchampagne colored honda.\"\r\n</PRE>'),(1298,'<p>The first two songs on <a href=\"http://www.matadorrecords.com/helium/\">Helium</a>\'s first album, \"Pirate Prude\" , are titled \"Baby Vampire Made Me\" and \"Wanna Be a Baby Vampire Too\". I listen to a Helium album every day. Currently I am convinced that Mary Timony and Helium are responsible for all my creative power.</p>\r\n<p><i>I\'ve got a baby vampire in me <br>I saw you put it in me like a seed.</i></p>\r\n<p>Last night I went to a birthday party and yelled in the <a href=\"http://www.jessfine.com\">guest of honor</a>\'s face about how I am \"against babies\" - but only real babies. Babies that have to be woken up for and sent to college. i had had some wine to drink.</p>\r\n<p> The imaginative/magical/metaphorical \"baby\" [egg.] turns me on; I like to call lovers \"baby\" and listen to songs with a healthy proliferation of the word \"baby\" in the lyrics. Haphazard use of the word baby in most figurative contexts gets me hot. I like to think of the ferocious baby in me, devouring my insides, ripping through my body with its own will for the sake of the Lord\'s creation. an immortal parasite without permission. That\'s what love is like.</p>\r\n<p><i>you\'ve got a skull; baby, you\'ve got a spine. After I\'m done with you I\'ll spit them out like rinds</i></p>\r\n<p>This morning my imaginary friend who I call Mr Penis made baby seal noises to endear himself to me. Baby seal is warm flesh. a mammal-fish.</p>\r\n<p>He asked me to give him hickies on his back. I wanted to be a carnviorous baby animal so I grazed his imaginary skin with my teeth and sucked down on his back with all my might. After three hickies I tasted metallic salt.</p>\r\n<p>Blood.</p>\r\n<p>Baby seal blood.Bloody welts had erupted all over his imaginary back. At first I was frightened but now I am proud. </p>\r\n<p>Once you have had mr penis baby seal blood, you can never go back. I will build a sticky invisible web next to my bedroom. Helpless baby animals will get caught. I will never be hungry.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1299,'Trey Anastasio is my hero, and Phish is my favorite band. Why does saying that make me feel like I should be ashamed? People ask me what kind of music I like, and this is how I usually respond: \r\n<p>me: \"Oh, I listen to some classical, and a lot of jazz, but my favorite is Phish.\"\r\n<p>you: \"So you\'re a Phish-Head!\"\r\n<p>\r\nI can\'t stand that. I listen to all kinds of music, I am in fact a real bona-fide musician, and yet I am instantly, neatly categorized before all of creation by my love for this one band. So now, I usually try to avoid talking about it to people who don\'t already know. I mean, if asked directly I\'ll give an honest answer, but I still feel a twinge of shame. Maybe it\'s because of the way people respond. Most people say, \"I don\'t like Phish\" or \"I haven\'t listened to a lot of their stuff\" or \"That one song on the radio is good.\" And I raise my hands to the sky and say...\r\n<p>\r\nWHY, God? Why don\'t people like this perfect, beautiful music? It is silly, and complicated, yet they play it as if it were the last song ever to be played before the end of human existance. From the first notes of a really smoking song, from a good show, I am transported to another dimension filled with happiness. I don\'t, in fact, understand how someone could possibly BE unhappy for long if they have some good Phish recordings laying around. It is one of the few things in life that does not suffer from the law of diminishing returns, but can actually get better with repeated listening. I love Phish.\r\n<p>\r\nPeople who do somehow stumble into getting the \'Phish thing\' often react in strange ways. Nothing in life, really, has prepared them to be taken this far with something, and it can blow the circuits. Some become rabidly defensive fanatics. I have seen people cry. I have cried, even just riding down the street. Some pick apart every little thing, trying to find the true source of the magic. Some, like me, know that it won\'t last forever and have made plans to carry the torch of what we have learned into our own music, and expand on it. \r\n<p>\r\nSo I get a little pissed when somebody says \"Oh, you\'re a Phish head!\" with that \"how cute\" expression on ther face, as if I just don\'t quite get it.'),(1300,'<FONT SIZE=\"+1\">\r\n<P>\r\nlately i have been spending upwards of five hundred miles a week in my car.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ndriving.\r\n</P><P>\r\ndriving is one long advertisement for people, their cars, and occasionally advertisements for services, billboards.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe music turns on, i stop thinking about my projects and dreams and start  wanting everything that surrounds me.  or noting everything around me and placing them on the shelf at a large superstore.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<TT>*blinking cursor*<blink>_</blink></TT>\r\n</P><P>\r\nparking lots full of cars and the people who bought them all waiting in line to purchase the products.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>double double cheese cheese burger burger please.</I>\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe heartbeat of america moves along <BR>\r\nbetween three and seven miles per hour.\r\n</P><P>\r\nany less than three miles per hour and everything stops.  zero to three miles per hour is not worth the effort.\r\n</P><P>\r\nin this time, stare. \r\n</P><P>\r\nover there the billboard of something that is beyond the scope of the this individual\'s decision making finger.\r\n</P><P>\r\nsomebody some where in this chain gang is looking at that billboard and knows the billboard is for them.  they are taking out a pen and paper, writing down the product with the intention of investigating it further.  thousands of dollars are spent on that one link in the chain.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthat link is the one hole needed for a single strand of verticle marketing.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe music is playing in that person\'s car. the sound of chain links chinking along.\r\n</P><P>\r\non the 880 north to oakland, at four miles per hour, i popped the face plate off my cd player and tossed it out on to the one hundred and twenty degree asphalt.</P></FONT>\r\n\r\n<A HREF=\"http://bcn.boulder.co.us/transportation/bbc/bbcc-main-factoids.html\"><IMG SRC=\"http://www.tidepool.com/alliance/apmlogosm.GIF\"></A>\r\n\r\n<P>and i drive on hating myself.</P>\r\n'),(1301,'<p>I posted about 3 days ago on how I wanted to be a SF kid and I asked people here what it took for me to be an SF kid besides moving to SF ofcourse.  I don\'t know what happen to the post but on the bottom it says 0 articals pending so I\'m supposing that my post got lost.  So.... what does it take to be an SF kid?  :('),(1302,''),(1303,'<p><img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/post/waragainstdusk.jpg\"></p>\r\n<p>I am sustained</p>\r\n<p>by ravens only and by the fancies</p>\r\n<p>of female benefactors.</p>\r\n<p><b>Thomas Merton </b></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/post/04panel3.gif\"></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/post/110500.jpg\"></p>'),(1304,'<p>\r\nshe asked me if i\'ve ever thought about running the car up against the dividing barrier, just to see the sparks fly out. yes i\'ve thought about it. when the trucks pass i imagine crossing over to the oncoming lane, headlights coming at me, no chance to turn away.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>momentum: velocity multiplied by mass.</i> when two objects in motion collide, momentum is conserved. a car driving 80 mph, colliding head-on into a truck at the same speed, will feel impact equivalent to hitting a brick wall at several hundred miles per hour.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyes i\'ve thought about it, just to know how it feels; how unlikely it all is, that i\'m here, now, experiencing the instant before - and then it stops, everything stops: the instant during, when the thrilling impossible is the unalterable reality.\r\n</p><p>\r\ndissolve into scraps and shards, and a black trail of tire tread left behind.\r\n</p><p>\r\nfuck yes.\r\n</p>'),(1305,'<P>when i was in college i lived in a housing co-op that was famous for its passion.\r\n</p><P>\r\ntoday i visited a housing co-op and applied to live there.\r\n</p><P>\r\nthe place had wonderful vibrance and filth and color.  everywhere was painted with pictures, from the walk way entrance under the vegetation trellises to the living room and out the back door.\r\n</p><P>\r\nthere were young people there, some very young, some my age, some older than me.  they talked out loud about revolutions.  it was charming.  cute.\r\n</p><P>\r\ni dont think about that stuff much anymore.  i just want people to be happy and i frame up my work in terms of making others happy.  they will revolt in their own ways as they see fit.  revolt from boredom.  revolt from mediocrity.  revolt from apathy.  \r\n</p><P>\r\nthey love in a way that is annoying in display of intolerance.  there would be people that could not tolerate me and my technology and my nice car.  when they buy watermelon at the grocery store, it comes from a \"luxury\" account for food.  watermelon is a luxury to these people.  all twenty three cents a pound of it,  seeds, rinds, and all.  \r\n</p><P>\r\nthey said rent was five hundred something, including food. doing the math, i determined that i could give them one of my pay checks and pay for four months rent and food all at once.  i could do that anywhere that rent was three hundred dollars, but here, when all bills and expenses are distilled into one single check, it is easy to frame up financially.  when all your bills are divided up into little bits here and there, money falls through the cracks and at the end of every pay period i have nothing.\r\n</p><P>\r\nthat is the beauty of the coop house life.  simplicity of resource allotment.  life becomes one single check a month.\r\n</p><P>\r\nanother beautiful aspect of the consensus building co-op environment is the waffling and mumbling that surrounds even the simplest decision.  it is baffling to watch.  it is fantastic.  sitting in an hour and a half meeting of waffling and mumbling about agenda items makes all my decisions in life seem trivial.  sure i will do this.  that is no problem.  witness the wheels of consensus voting and hear the voices in your head drown out.  life is actually much simpler than is thought, simply come to a consensus: yes or no.  there are no maybes.  in your head, your self, your one voice: yes or no.  a lesson easily forgotten.\r\n</p><P>\r\nthese people are great.\r\n</p><P>\r\nwaffle and mumble on my brothers and sisters.  \r\n</p><P>\r\neven if you don\'t vote me into your co-op.\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.porter.ucsc.edu/scshc/chavez.jpg\">'),(1306,'<P> i see the shape of his back in pale moonlight...i think the moon is near full tonight and even as we speak of the future of grandiose becomings i cannot but help notice the small , the peculiar, the shape of his nose...a light blue in comparison to the dark night we seem to swim by. everything is memory and i do not think that i will remember this tomorrow or the day after so it is best to have it somewhere...accessable.and so i write.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> salty skins we all are. in the day shiny faces and we rub off the dreams from our closed eyes wih loosly formed fists. i want to devour what he is, what he will become and i am sometimes frightened that this is all near imaginary. the traintracks to reality are two feet away and i, like a bad girl will never follow them. perhaps this is not the correct thing to do. it is an overwhelming thing to feel. a beautiful and great feeling.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> fingernails and shed hair are remains and reminders that they were and will be there soon. the things which you covet in one will be hated by another. i held on as tightly as i could in my last relationship...if one could call it that. loving...as i thought i did...with every pore, with every ounce of want i pushed and gave until i went dry. my mouth , my eyes...and there was nothing left. i do not wish it to be like that again. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> seeing a friend of mine yesterday whose boyfriend of many years passed away in a car accident, reminded me of the fleeting bugs we all are. she kisses my chek and holds on to me strong. yet, i have felt so far away from her for months that it pains. it seems some of us can loose our friends when we become involved with lovers. and the word \"involved\" is such a nonchalant word...lovers are much more than involved.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> she is seeing a new boy. he reminds me and M of he who died at such young an age. i wonder if it bothers her new love that she mentions his name so very often. i wonder if her new boyfriend feels number two. i desire him not to, knowing what it is like to feel number two. you are still wanted, but do not meet the mark. you are still desired but not coveted as much. i think...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> thinking of my ex and what went wrong so it will not happen again with my new relationship. something, i think many people do. yet, i  do think that there are certain fixed occurrances. it is a sickness this love thing. and i grasp on to it tightly. it can be a thing of near perfection...never reaching that height for it is the small details of this mundane living which make it so very unique. the way se walks. the maner in which he opens wide his mouth to laugh. his bottom lip plumper than the top. he manne rin which his head rests upon my shoulder crook and the manner in which i cannot think of anything being more comfortable. small waves of something start at the lungs and reach my throat...maybe it is all this music i listen to reminding me of a past that is no more as i was yesterday. a drowning thing love is. one immerses themselves into the other and the salt water leaves trails of salt...like snail trails glistening in the morning sun. i want to touch everyone. i need to find something to hold onto. sometimes the waves can carry one out to far. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> an overwhelming ocean of things i could say. i am not bitter towards any of them. I would love to shake hands with the past, although at times it turns its head and leaves me alone wih hand in mid-air. i am amazed and startled and confused all at once that one being could be so certain that it is me and no one else. i am surprised. i am content. like a small cat happy on the chest of its caretaker i will sleep next to him dreaming dreams that with them never carry endings; they do not know of the word. it does not exist to them. his smell, the laughter that comes like mist from another room that is yours forever, the look, the gaze , the smile are unbearable at times. our arms and legs make tangles of skin and bone and seperated we are two. how human, how wonderful, how good. the details. the runny noses, the comments off the wall that bounce from floor to ceiling, the laughter....ahh, the laughter that hopelessly continues and falls from my lips to my shoulders to the floor and back to the sky. i would love to bite him a million times, devour what is him, and keep him, as a memory that continues to be renewed. A memory that does not know an end.</p>'),(1307,'test'),(1308,'<P>I think I love to write. I\'ve thought about writing a screenplay, just for the fun of it. I\'ve created some poetry, but in my distant past. I\'ve wanted to write short fiction, because my attention span is too short to write a novel. Actually, I have written a screenplay; it\'s not very long; about 15 minutes or so, and I\'m not finished with it. It was good to try and work out dialoge and character development. I don\'t know if it is any good. And it\'s not really mine; it\'s based on someone else\'s short story.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>I have some friends of mine who write and they are good at it. They recently wrote a radio show called \"The Intergalatic Nemesis\" which they performed at the Paramount Theater in downtown Austin; it was also broadcast on our local public radio station. This inspired me to write more.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>The other day, I saw the movie \"State and Main.\" In some respects, it is very similar to \"The Player\" and maybe even \"Bowfinger.\" After watching this movie, I don\'t think I can be a professional writer; my ego is too fragile.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>I usually listen to NPR on my way to work; I don\'t do it out of pretentiousness, but because I really dislike the morning deejays in this city. There are several \"essays\" which are read during \"Morning Edition\" to people who are driving in their cars, just like me. Sometimes these essays are exceptional. (Well, I guess that\'s that the word exceptional means... if everything was \"exceptional,\" everything would then become average, right?) But most of the time, I am unimpressed. I could have written that piece. I could have read that to everyone... It\'s just that no one asked for my opinion on this topic... Then, I am inspired to write again. This time, no one will be spared from my opinion. I will write great prose and inspire debates and thought. I will impose myself on everyone, everywhere. I will be the most prolific writer of our age...</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Then, I think, why do I write? Is it to be noticed? Is it to be praised? I only want the praise, because my ego is too fragile accept anything less. I don\'t want to hear the bad things, unless they are packaged nicely to protect my ego...</P>\r\n\r\n<p>I work as an editor at a company that could very well be considered an Intergalactic Nemesis (with the initials I, B, and M). My job is to crush other people\'s egos. I recently got into a mini argument with a PhD about esoteric grammar rules. I crushed him. But I don\'t appreciate it when others do that to me.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>But I don\'t want to edit. I want to create. I don\'t want to nit pick and mold. I want to create. I don\'t want to be detail oriented. I want to create. I don\'t want to crush or diminish. I want to create....</P>'),(1309,'<p>\r\nso the comedy workshop started and its been laughs a plenty. the speakers making jokes. the students making jokes. all jokes and no time for the serious shit. its a bit weird because after a while you think they are just trying to hard to be funny, when its a workshop to help people become funnier. I guess things might change when the classes start happening and the students are with their respective and respected faculty members, but during the panels it seems as though everyone is \"on\" and that they have a show to do. people yell out one liners all the time and there is laughs and also awkward laughs coming from all sorts of direction. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is this thick layer of insecurity. perhaps a plague that occurs throughout the entire comedy industry ive found that most comics and comic writers are very insecure about their own lives and use comedy as a means of getting frustration, anger, and all sorts of other problems out and into the public without having to do it in a serious manner. this is perhaps the craft. its those that let too much out and that use the crowd as a shrinks office that dont seem to be that funny and those who know exactly what the audience wants to hear and wants to listen to that make it big.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non sunday chevy chase is coming to speak to the student at the final dinner. he has bailed on us the past two years and for the most inane reasons. he felt sick once, and his daughter had a bad dream the other. this year he promises to come and the forecast looks good and everything seems to be on track. there are bets going around that he wont make it and bets going around that he will. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe comedy workshop has some strange fellows. both faculty and students come from a wide geographic area, one student making her way her from china. ontop of all the weird people from weird places, you have a strange and often goofy formation of cliques and groups. people together that youd never have suspected. apples and oranges. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe good thing about the workshops from my perspective is that i dont have to pay for lunch or coffee or muffins or cookies or anything else that i need. I have tonnes of petty cash to throw around for faculty and there is always food and beverage around waiting for me to see it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are also hotties all over the place and well its always nice to look at hotties with a nice coffee and blueberry muffin in hand. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCOMEDY HOTTIES, whodathunk it. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1310,'sweep scrub grind groan grunt<br>\r\nyou will come home to green scents<br>\r\ndo you love me now?'),(1311,'<P> hey yo</p>\r\n<P> see, the keyboard messed up\r\npepsi one...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i found my other cd case</p>\r\n\r\n<P> the \"x\" key didnt seem to be working and nothing seemed to</p>\r\n<P> The keyboard was stuck</p>\r\n<P>are you still mad?</p>\r\n<P> i was thinking of breaking up last night i was so very mad</p>\r\n<P> yeah, well now you know</p>\r\n<P> birth is pure to me</p>\r\n<P> babies are cool. i dont know what else to say...small people</p>\r\n<P>so did you speak to jeremy?</p>\r\n<P> maybe i\'ll saty in israel for a month...</p>\r\n<P> might as well use my passport</p>\r\n<P> i felt like ian today while i was clocking in, weird.</p>\r\n<P> i felt like ian in my hips...like i was standing like him.</p>\r\n<P>yes</p>\r\n<P> okay we dont agree what more do you want must you be so scientific about everything</p>\r\n<P> i just saw two people kiss </p>\r\n<P> if if i had cancer iw wouldnt get chemotherapy</p>\r\n<P>did you read my email from a day a go?</p>\r\n<P> i feel distant from you</p>\r\n<P> not as much now, but it\'s still there.</p>\r\n<P> i have 10 minutes to speak to you left.</p>\r\n<P> where do you thnk jeremy went?</p>\r\n<P>passion isnt love</p>\r\n<P> i need to sniff my blue blanket and sleep on the couch or in my room or wherever it is cool</p>\r\n<P>yeah, i really did myself at k-mart</p>\r\n<P>i have some underwear of yours in the underwear thing</p>\r\n<P><B> You\'re driving me insane that\'s why we need to see eachother. We may bite our tongues a bit more.</b></p>\r\n<P><B>Please not now.</b></p>\r\n<P><B> Quit it.</b></p>\r\n\r\n<P> bye i hafta go</p>'),(1312,'<p><font face=\"Courier\">\r\n\"those things will kill you.\"<br>\r\n\"this river is gonna kill me!\"<p>\r\ni had just trekked 1/4 mile along the river searching for my friends and raft. after hitting a 3 foot drop in a banana like raft 2 of the crew decided to bail (or maybe they just fell\r\noff), leaving me and the guide no choice but to follow. we were only a\r\n1/3 of the way through the class 3 rapids at that point. after swallowing\r\nriver water, being dragged under the raft, bumping my knees and ass on\r\nunseen rocks, being hit by someone else\'s paddle and being choked my day-glo\r\norange life preserver i decided to ditch them and head for shore. i was\r\nbeat up and full of water, and all i wanted was a cigarette.<p>\r\npeople jump off cliffs, planes, bridges. they dive to 100 ft, risking the bends. race cars at extreme speeds with tires made to skim, not grip, the asphalt.<br>\r\nand it\'s cigarettes that will kill you.<p>\r\nwhat the fuck! i want my smokes, i need them. they reaffirm that i\'m still alive. they calm my racing heart before giving me that much desired nicotine rush. i don\'t care if they\r\nkill me. i saw my life, and it said, \"you need a smoke, goat! you get this\r\nbody to shore and you can smoke.\"<p>\r\nbut rivers and cigaretts don\'t really go, so i got back on that damn raft.'),(1313,'<xmp>\r\nThe toilet is on the blink!\r\nWhatever shall we do?\r\nForget the door, don\'t fix the sink,\r\nIf I can\'t crap, I\'ll sue!\r\n\r\nIs the chain around the flapper?\r\nShall we bend the arm?\r\nOh, this most malodorous crapper --\r\nSomeone sound the alarm!\r\n\r\nIt will not do\r\nTo whine and moan\r\nAnd wail against the world.\r\n\r\nI cannot poo,\r\nCan\'t use the throne,\r\nYou in that chair still curled.\r\n\r\nI beg of you\r\nGo fetch supplies.\r\n\r\nI have in me a bunch of doo.\r\nAny longer I\'ll draw flies.\r\n</xmp>'),(1314,'<P>hmm.  recently i appear to have tried too hard.  some interpersonal relationships (platonic) are just not meant to be.  this is definitely my personal fault, trying too hard sometimes instead of just letting things be. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>i think it is likely that we really just don\'t click.  this happens sometimes with people, and it must be accepted by all parties involved.  i cannot quite accept this yet i do not think.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and sometimes there are people you see fairly often in your life.  think like neighbor, a coworker, or a panhandler on the way to work or something like that.  someone that you sort of need to have some kind of relationship with in order to better function with the people who are around you.  and, well, it doesn\'t work.  for whatever reason.  probably because you tried too hard, exacerbating the non-workingness of the relationship to the point of assness and no turning back.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>sigh.</P>\r\n'),(1315,'<P> i have been listening to alot of Red House Painters lately....\"retrospective\" the most...I come home lay down, turn on the fan, and just think of the past and men, and food, what to do to my hair and all the shit i wanna buy at thrift stores.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it\'s alot of shit</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i love effeminate men. men that other men think are gay but aren\'t. a friend of mine described effeminate as the manner in which you hold a cigarettte. but it.s much more than that.\r\nit.s their belief thingy and the clothes they choose over others if any at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> my ex is pretty darn effeminate. he likes to cook 24/7.he wants to do it with martha stewart.if he is realy horny he will tell a man how nice he is. he just doesnt care which side he is on. i guess one could say androgynous...but why be PC? .two years ago. me and he and a few other friends worked as swim instructors for my university\'s summer swim program. I don\'t remember any of it...I guess i must have been too damn tired for three months. we were good.....we were damn good. (teachers)i work at the same place now ...with a two year gap...it is pretty depressing.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i have moved on. yet, it\'s pretty depressing to not have an effeminate swim instructor nearby.\r\nI guess what i mean is men who do not care whether they are MALE enough or whatever. (i can only write at night. It isnt sticky and i can walk around in my underwear safely...knowing that all company is asleep.)</p>\r\n\r\n<P> its the David Bowie thing I tell him. the eyeliner and \'sly\' cockiness..he.s already got the cocky part down all we need is the sly. Maybe it is the area i happen to reside in, but there arent many nice effeminate men. effeminate men are my achilles heel along with malnutrition. one effeminate man made me see how i unappreciatted my relationship.and another just the opposite. they listen when boyfriends and girlfriends will not and play with my hair because they like to.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i realise i dont know if im even spelling effeminate right...hmmmmm.oh well. if this doesnt get on ill just write some more about effeminate men vs steroid men. steroid men suck. <I>you\'re an american girl,red hair eues black, living on a freckle in the face of the world</i> mmm doggy\r\neffeminate men are nice.</p>'),(1316,'<p>i often sit and wonder...<br>\r\nuntil i get smothered by an extraordinarily large maxi pad<br>\r\nthen hog tied by a <I>Boy and His Dog </I><br>\r\nand forced to watch Howdy Doody with a oscar meyer weiner shoved up my ass.<br>\r\nbut it\'s ok cause i won a free trip to delaware.<br>\r\ni\'m on my way up the ladder and to the big times.</P>'),(1317,'<p>\r\nso ive been reading my horoscope everyday now. I read the Jonathan Cainer zodiac forecast. I dont know if its any better then the ones in the paper, but it is longer and has more to say. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"The object of the exercise is to make the rent / to make a name for yourself / to make those so called experts look stupid / to make you-know-who fall in love with you / to make you-know-who fall out of love with you / to make each moment as magical as possible by refusing to become bogged down by petty or selfish concerns. Fancy the last one? Then get this. It is totally incompatible with any of the other options. If you want to embrace the best today, stop clinging on so tightly to the rest.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits kind of intersting. it really is. over the past few weeks i have come to terms with a few major things. I have decided that i am no longer going to be the person who makes the effort to keep friendships alive. i am no longer going to worry about friendships because they ARE two way streets and for the most part im driving one way. there are a few exceptions and well im very happy about those exceptions. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\napart from those one way streets i have also decided that there are those i want to get to know and those i want to get to know less. ya know?'),(1318,'<p><b>lulu-\"</b> can i show you a method that might help you out a little and maybe even save some time...\"<br>\r\n<b> phillip (shaking head \"no\")-</b> \"no no no...no no no...this is the way i do it, i don\'t change how i do things.\"<br>\r\n<b>lulu-</b>\"um...i\'m sorry?  listen, pal i\'m just trying to show you how to do this shit efficiently, i\'d really like it if you\'d just get off your high horse and just listen for a second and try a new fuckin\' way.\"<br>\r\n<b>phillip-</b>\"well, i\'ve been working on these things for years and i know what i\'m doing...\"<br>\r\n<b>lulu (now sweating with neck vein pulsating rapidly)-</b>\"then why\'d you ask me for help?\"<br>\r\n<b>phillip-</b>\"oh i see, so i\'m not allowed to ask for help now?\"<br>\r\nlulu-\"alright, let\'s not get snippy now, phil\"<br>\r\n<b>phillip-</b>\"phillip\"<br>\r\n<b>lulu-</b>\"what.\"<br>\r\n<b>phillip-</b>\"it\'s phillip.\"<br>\r\n<p>(pause)</p>\r\n<p>lulu stares bewildered at the meak boy named phillip.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>lulu-</b>\"right, whatever, philly. let\'s just get started on this shit, alright.\"<br>\r\n(couple of hours pass, and all lulu wants is to be at home in bed, sleeping, but no. <br>\r\nenter philly)<br>\r\n<b>lulu (yelling very loudly)-</b>\"gahdammit! fuckin\'-A, why the fuck can\'t shit ever go fuckin\' right in this fuckin\' god awful place!!?\"<br>\r\n<b>philly (aka phillip)-</b>\"why the fuck do you say so fuckin much?\"<br>\r\n<b>lulu</b> (still yelling)-\"cause i went to a fuckin\' all womens fuckin college in fuckin Oakland!\"<br>\r\n<b>philly-</b>\"oh really, i went to bard.  i studied film as an undergraduate there cause you know they really have an excellent if not world class film department there and i learned how to develop film, and all i want to do is tell stories and make films, i mean that\'s all i want to do. and all this stuff, it\'s gonna go, cause you know, it\'s all gonna be marketable on the internet.....no really, it\'s going to be. and i made sooooooo many films while i was in school and i\'m soooooo awe-some. oh and have you ever been to this cool hip french restaurant called <I>part deaux</I>?  it\'s where all the hip people in l.a. hang out and eat twenty dollar burgers and drink thirty dollar martinis. you should go, you\'re hip aren\'t you?  oh and lemme tell you more about myself...i\'m stunningly handsome even tho i may have a big schnoz and look like a complete goober to you, but if you just look past that and notice my shweet leather jacket that i paid $500 for at this insane store on melrose called <I>Stunned but Fun</I>, it\'s where all the hipsters go to shop for their garb...that\'s cool talk for \"clothes\", right. anyway, back to me...did i tell i went to this really rich school called bard where all i did was make films cause i\'m an artist and i wear plastic black rimmed glasses and talk about fluxus and the importance of nice shoes.  oh and did i tell you i worked on a feature before i came here?<br>\r\n<b>lulu-</b>\"i thought you were unemployed before you came here?\"<br>\r\nphilly-\"yeah, but before that i was working on a feature, cutting it and shit, and before that i was working at disney, and oh boy, that disney place sure is a trippy place.  i\'m sure you don\'t know about this stuff, but lemme just tell you...blah blah blah... blah blahblah...\"<br>\r\n<b>lulu (in tears, partially cause phil has managed to make her lose her mind and partially cause she\'s realized that if the gods continue to do as they have been, she will be working with this prick everyday until her eyeballs fall out...she is in agony) -</b>\"a feature, huh?  well, woopty friggin doo for you phil. that\'s really impressive. soooooo...why is it that you don\'t know how to do something as simple as putting together a first assembly?\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nphilly opens his flap to say something - something sooper interesting lulu bets.</p><p>\r\n\r\n<b>lulu-</b>\"no don\'t.....don\'t say anything, i don\'t care if you\'ve made films, i don\'t care if you went to bard, i don\'t care if you\'ve fucked ten thousand women while vaccuming your bear rug. i\'m not here to be impressed by your fucking history.  alls i want from you, is to finish your work here, that\'s all. i just want you to do this shit and do it well. and you don\'t know the proccess here, so just listen to what i have to fuckin say and keep your fuckin trap shut cause i\'ve just about heard enough from you for the rest of my night...no...life. \r\n</p><p>\r\nphilly pulls out a semi automatic from behind him.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlulu throws a death tap at his chest.  philly clenches his heart as he gasps for air and stumbles to his knees and finally croaks a miserable death at lulu\'s dainty, adorable, little feet.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlulu, thinking the job is unfinished, picks up the semiautomatic and proceeds to pump a round into philly\'s limp body.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlulu takes philly\'s head and gives it to the security guard for his 33rd birthday.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlulu and security guard get married in santa cruz and have three kids.\r\n</p><p>\r\nkids play with philly\'s head in a jar.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1319,'I hate to fly. It scares me to no end. This is really frustrating because I love to travel. I would much rather drive to my destination, however I am well aware that this will not only slow me down and limit the amount of time I can spend at my destination, but that this also limits where my destinations can be. Most of my favorite trips were accomplished by traveling by plane....\r\n\r\n<p>I have flown at least 20 or 30 times in my lifetime. Nothing bad has ever happened to me to warrant such irrational fears. I recently learned that I have a greater chance of dying from an asteroid hitting the earth than I do of dying in a plane crash (this is because more people will die in the former). This doesn\'t make me feel any better.\r\n\r\n<p>I have a friend of mine (Chad) who has also flown about 20 or 30 times in his lifetime. He has had several horrible experiences. One time, his plane was preparing for takeoff, got to whatever the critical speed is to actually lift off of the ground, and then it had to slam on its brakes because they thought that a hatch was still open. Another time, they had to fly back to the airport they just left because the engine caught on fire. I don\'t know if you could get me on a plane if I had these experiences. Chad is going to be leaving for Germany in a few weeks...\r\n\r\n<p>Despite my better judgment, I will watch all those \"Word\'s Most Amazing Air Crashes\" and the like. I just find it too enthralling to turn away. I can\'t even think what it must be like to be in one of those planes that suddenly drops from the sky.... Although I guess it\'s all about what your perception of life and death... Which reminds me: There\'s a really great radio play on this topic at the SciFi.com\'s <a href=\"http://www.scifi.com/set/playhouse/flat/description.html\">Seeing Eye Theater</a>. If you have a spare 70 minutes, I would highly recommend that you give this a listen...'),(1320,'I had to go to the worst place in the world today: The <a href=\"http://www.scdps.org/dmv/\">South Carolina Department of Motor Vehicles</a>. It is a large concrete building, on the southwest side of Columbia, on Park Street. My roommate Barrett had to go to get a copy of his driving record for his new job with Miller Beer, and so I tagged along to show him where it was, borrow some money, get breakfast, etc. \r\n<p>\r\nAs soon as we walked in the door, a lady stuck her head out the window and said, \"Our computers are down statewide. We don\'t know how long it will be until they come back up.\" Oh God. \r\nWe decided to wait.\r\n<p>\r\nThe DMV is a great equalizer. Everybody has to go sometimes, no matter how rich or famous you might be, and wallow down in the mud with the masses. A truer portrait of America cannot be found--In fact, it would be a good experiment to interview everyone in the DMV at two pm for a week, and see how their income levels and the proportion of men/women/race compares to the state as a whole; I\'d be willing to bet that it\'s pretty close. \r\n<p>\r\nThe DMV ladies--awful. I don\'t know what they\'re doing up there behind the counter, with it\'s plastic divider, but whatever it is, they are doing it very, very slowly and with great concentration. It takes me about an hour to an hour and a half from the time I walk in to get served. Sometimes, the little counter stays on the same number for something like 20 minutes. When I finally do get up there, it only takes about three to handle my problem. But the real kicker is how they have nine windows to serve the public, but only three ladies working(yes it\'s always a lady). Why tease us like that? They could have saved some money on windows if they\'d been a little more realistic.'),(1321,'<br>\r\nTonight I said it, and the words slipped out,<br> inaudible, spoken only by my movements.<br>\r\nAnd there is nothing that I, so willingly, could<br> do that would make you happy.<br>\r\nand there is nothing that you\'d tolerate, even so, <br> if I were to try.<br>\r\nand there\'s nothing i could, in all my<br> deliberations, do to make you love yourself, feel<br> joy, or have a passion for anyting at all.<br>\r\nThe whispers of the passersby whirlpool around my<br> reddened ears, my pulsing neck; they say,<br> \"walk away. poof. the time is always now\".<br>\r\ntonight.<br>\r\nface to face, pen to paper, ear to phone, tears to<br> shirt-tail uttered, summer sky misted, bits blown<br> up from the street in thermals, debris<br> scratching your empty, hardened eyes.<br>\r\ntonight<br>\r\nI took your head in my hands and washed these bits<br> from your eye with love while you anguished over<br> your somethings, my nothings.\r\nso much time passes.<br>\r\nwhere did all the days go?<br>\r\nmaybe they were writing a story called \"love can<br> exist even with nothing else\".<br>'),(1322,'The last time someone was as bored as I am\r\nthey came up with sex.\r\nthere\'s no topping such achivements.\r\n\r\nin this anti-social sweat\r\nall i can do is dream every few hours\r\nlast one was about G-d taping\r\nthe first female orgasm ever ( it was a \r\nvery long fable )\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.gimptroll.com/calls\">Al Pacino</a> was making noise in my backround.\r\nWhen a home becomes a prision\r\nand meeting people is not easy\r\nwhat now? another Sinatra record?\r\nLess food intake. more amazement.\r\na nap at the museum. \r\nanarchy against self evaluation.\r\n'),(1323,'<P>Erin left her fingerless satiny velvety glove at my place.  I find it under the couch while I\'m watching tv.  I stare at it thinking about how she forgot about half her stuff here that night.  Pulling it on, the material stretches to encase my palm and wrist in a lounge chair green.  I press the back of my hand to my lips and inhale her smell.  I really like Erin\'s smell.  It\'s herby and little sharp and above all... pungent.  It pervades everything she\'s in contact with: her clothes, all the things in her purse, even me sometimes when we cuddle for long periods.  I once talked to her about it and found out the smell comes from some gourmet, all-natural deoderent she uses.  The way it intermingles with her own natural oder.  I like Erin\'s smell.</P>\r\n<P>The desire for some scotch and a cigarette inspire me to climb up to the roof.  Erin\'s glove still cradles my hand.  I stand midst the night air drinking... smoking... and occasionally sniffing Erin\'s smell.  I think about how life seems to move in circles and the fact that I\'m attracted to pungent women.  I think about Heather.</P><P>\r\nThe relationship I had with Heather is very similar to the one I have with Erin.  They both started out romantically and evolved into very satisfying friendships.  I fell in love with Heather and ended up just appreciating the chance to spend time with such an amazing character.  Erin and I had meaningless sex, her a rebound chick and me some sort of sexual experiment, and we discovered how eerily similar we are.  They both have their harem of whipped men standing ready to do their bidding.  Heather was emotionally intense.  Erin is just straight up gorgeous.  I think about my other friends in NYC and the many paralells with the people I spent my time with back in Idaho.</P><P>\r\nHeather wasn\'t as pungent as Erin is.  She had the same kind of smell though, a sort of herby, sharp smell.  Heather\'s came from the incense she would burn in her apartment, which was the only place where I could really smell it.  We used to sit there until dawn arguing about the usefulness of psychologists and the power of the subconscious, rubbing eachother\'s feet, and smoking cigarettes.</P><P>\r\nI drop my butt in a puddle and listen to the hiss as is goes out.  I wash down the last of the smoke taste with some scotch and stare at the sky hoping to see a couple stars.  All the while, I\'m thinking about living life in little circles and when I\'ll be able to get around to returning Erin\'s glove.</P>'),(1324,'<p>If I were to document last night in moving pictures the final product would include one minute of double channel video, looping in tandem. Projected on one wall, midnight strikes at the Albany Bowl. The lights dim to near blackout and the lanes become punctuated by blinking red lights. Destiny\'s Child blares on the PA. Snotty punk Amoeba records employees look about sheepishly. Three girls lift the t-shirt of a skinny white boy to reveal his undies and studded belt before he slides down the lane, his performance marred by the harassment. One of the girls periodically reaches into her mouth to pull out a jawbreaker and see what color it is. A mantra is vaguely audible underneath the screechy R&B: \"Im gonna kick your ass. no, AHM gonna kick YOUR ass. Im gonna kick your mutherfuckin ASS. Im gonna fuck you up the ass so hard youll shit through your mouth!\" Blue, purple, green balls, oversized jawbreakers roll, roll, roll one after the other in syncopated rhythm towards the horizon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On the other wall, Karon the bartender in her off-the-shoulder purple Flashdance sweater, Camel in hand, leads the 10 drunken 20somethings at the bar in a top of the lung singalong to Sinatra\'a \"New York, New York.\" At the center of the chaos is Simon, who happens to know every single last word to the song, in all black, his suit jacket lapel sealed with a fake black dahlia, swinging his bud back and forth [\"we have an elegant selection on tap. bud, bud, bud, or bud.\"]. And hanging on her shoulder, the lovely Miss Jessica of Missoula and Alphabet City, in a bright white off the shoulder Flashdance t-shirt, dangling white balls swinging from her earlobes, eyes crinkled from laughter. [side note: in a feature film, jessica would walk to the bathroom, open up a white ball earring, scoop a nug of Blow out of it with her fingernail and snort.] and me, forehead to the bar, tapped every 20 seconds by the Brit next door to ask if Im ok. I dart him an unimpressed glare. Loop. Tap. Glare. Tap. Glare. New York, Neeeeeeeewwwwwhoo Yoooooooork.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>The footage I would edit out, but which I secretly play back on my television at night from bed when everyone is asleep, shows the three of us -- Simon, Jessica, and I, with each of us ladies on an arm of Simon\'s like he\'s the Mac of Hotsy Totsy, the Lustre King of Albany Bowl -- introducing ourselves to Karon the bartender. Jessica chatters melodically in her frosty timbre about her first night at the fish grotto, her 30 percent tips and the beautiful coworker who looks like a Liquid Sky alien. Hearing a description of one lovely coworker, Simon believes he may have slept with her back in the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When Karon the bartender addresses me she shouts, \"This one here must be a WRITER. are you taking this all down, honey. Youre the quiet one who takes it all in.\"</p> \r\n\r\n<p>um. I was neither scribbling in a notepad nor holding a dictaphone to anyone\'s mouth. I was just sitting there. Laughing a lot. But like a Sophoclesian prophet Karon nailed what I was doing. In my drunken head, I was recording text. It was like being Outed. I have never self-identified as a Writer.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Nor am I quiet. I am a LOUD bitch. On the Richter scale of Loud, I would bust the Warren Freeway in half. Sometimes when I am in public, trying to have a poignant conversation with a friend, my suddenly flushed, silent companion\'s eyes dart around nervously because I have spoken so loudly about the petty details of my life that everyone else in the joint has stopped talking. I live in the bend of the 580; I have its noise to contend with. Girl\'s got to shout to get what she wants.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>But I enjoyed that role for an hour, laying my cheek on the cool pleather and memorizing Jessica\'s gestures, Simon\'s smell and my neighbor\'s British accent. I will keep my mouth shut, watch and listen. I want to be that girl in the secret movie more often.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Secret girl in the movie more often. <br>\r\nSecret movie in the girl more often.<br> \r\nOften More girl in the secret movie.</p> '),(1325,'<p>Efnet</p>\r\n<p><i>08/88 - 07/01</i></p>\r\n<p>R. I. P.</p>\r\n '),(1326,'I get along without him very well<br>\r\nof course I do<br>\r\nexcept when I watch a movie with Italians in it<br>\r\nand I\'m reminded of his giggle<br>\r\nand the way he would say, \"Not for nothin,\"<br>\r\nand \"You\'re giving me the agita.\"<br>\r\n(which I guess is more of a Jewish thing<br>\r\nbut he did grow up in Queens.)<br><br>\r\n\r\nI get along without him very well<br>\r\nindeed I do<br>\r\nexcept perhaps when I\'m folding shirts.<br>\r\nI fold them in threes now<br>\r\nlike he showed me, once from the bottom<br>\r\nand once from the top.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI sure do get along just fine these days<br>\r\na year has passed<br>\r\nand back then we were miserable<br>\r\nwent to the beach for the Fourth<br>\r\nand couldn\'t stand the plain awful<br>\r\npoignancy of it all, obviously<br>\r\nthe last time we would ever go to<br>\r\nthe beach like that together<br>\r\nwith friends, pretending like<br>\r\neverything was as it always was.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe\'s getting along just fine I bet<br>\r\nof course he does.<br>\r\nHe\'s in LA I hear, <br>\r\npursuing a plum dandy career<br>\r\nand shopping on Melrose I suppose.<br>\r\nI was always the stronger one,<br>\r\nbut maybe he\'s ok by now<br>\r\nand he could always bury his pains<br>\r\nbetter than I ever could,<br>\r\nnice and deep in the dirt.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\'ve forgotten him<br>\r\njust like I said I would.<br>\r\nExcept when I hear certain songs<br>\r\nor watch certain TV shows<br>\r\nor think of certain lines<br>\r\nor the wind blows a certain<br>\r\nfucking way and I\'m lost <br>\r\nwithout him at those moments<br>\r\nuntil I gather myself back <br>\r\ntogether again<br>\r\nand remind myself that I\'m<br> \r\nbetter off alone.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI get along without him very well<br>\r\nof course I do.<br>\r\nExcept perhaps at night, when<br>\r\nI wish I was confined to one half of the bed<br>\r\nhaving my sleep ruined<br>\r\nby his warm snores.<br>\r\nIf only we could have kept<br>\r\nthe comfort of each other<br>\r\nwithout the heaviness<br>\r\nof our wants and fears<br>\r\nbearing down like <br>\r\nsludge in a drainpipe <br>\r\nover our heads.<br><br>\r\n'),(1327,'<p>\r\ni often think that writing on this page gives other people too much of an insight into me. i often wish i could limit who reads this and the content level. i know that in the end id rather let those who i dont really want reading this to read it because i also want people i dont know to read it, but it annoys the hell out of me when they dont respond or communicate with me directly when something they dont agree with comes up.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nchildish is the world that comes to mind when i notice this happening. it bugs me because i have always beleived in the direct approach when something is on my mind. while i understand that everyone deals with things differently i still think its unhealthy to hide behind false identities yet still lash out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>the unknown</i> decides to rant on about me in the open for all to see yet they are not big enough to admit to it. they are afraid, and hide like little kids. if you want to say something to me, then tell me. the whole wide world does not need to know whats going on...and secondly, if you ARE going to hide...hide completely.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI SEE YOU.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1328,''),(1329,'<p align=\"center\">\r\nI</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco is a city that grabs you by the hand and pets your head and hair and says softly, hello.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco is everything that i thought it would be and nothing at all like what i was secretly hoping it would be.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nthere is a girl that i know that is very cute and smilely most of the time and she goes along with whatever and all that- and she is very nice and sweet and mostly non argumenative except for when she has to be- and this girl, cutely, talks about things that are important and not so (but, all the same, important to her) and hardly raises her voice and sometimes, sometimes she is apt to laugh whole heartedly at nothing in particular and not stop.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nthis girl, she is very cute, and i love her very much- she (and i moved out here to be with her and around her)- she is my friend and she is very much like this city.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nII</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco is a locked gate and a missed bus and a stong breeze that messes up my hair- it is a step out every twenty mintues to smoke- it is a town that i loved and now am indifferent and eventually will (maybe) grow to favor not-</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n(and how can i tell my mother this when she calls to see how i am doing?- she would not understand-).</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nIII</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco secretly is a bundled oportunity.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nIV</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco is exactly like where i was (and exactly like where you are right now) and san francsico is exactly like every where i will ever be and san francisco will never change anything that really mattters.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco reminds me not to get my hopes up.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nV</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\nsan francisco has a nice breeze and warm sun and is a nice back drop for things.</p>'),(1330,'<P> i am disappearing with some friends this weekend...</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>So last night I was at some sushi place...had too much to drink. Then i, a good female friend and my effeminate male friend decide to go to a club after a half night   of braiding hair. We get in the car chug over to a coffe shop since there are a few paintings done with him as the model. We look, chat then get back in the car and off to the club we go.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> It is a slow night at this club. No one is out on the dance floor. But it was the closest thing besides LA and nasty Orange County clubs. (only the gay clubs in Orange County are worth anything) So we start dancing ...we go to the cages, we dance and we leave are fears behind so to say, nudge nudge wink wink.</p> \r\n\r\n<P> we are offered spots on the bar. boys and girls rubbing my legs and my friends...our good androgynous friend was watching from afar. Probably getting off. He is male. We are given free drinks, money, and coupons for tatoo parlors and the like. We dance rubbing eachothers bodies with no stopping us. Hands over the shoulders and down, qround the hips. Tossing hair back for more effect. Then this small guy asked me to dance. He wasnt drunk. He said I was beautiful. So I shrug and say sure. He cant dance worth shit. I get back on the bar, only this time i almost tip over and they make me get off. Being a danger to customers and the like.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> it is getting late so i am off looking for Brad, whom I find...this is the femme femme boy who is escorting my friend and I for the night. \"I need water.\" He looks at me and points to the bar.I shake shake my head a viscous no. \"Skin water. I want to go skinny dipping so find me some water.\" The words are heavy and peanut buttery the fal from tongue. Okay, he says...we go look for our third partner in crime...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> on the way to the water i puke out the window. if i still liked brad that would not be a good thing, i think. But it doesnt matter. Because I dont. Because I am being good wih someone good so i suppposedly think. This is not questioning your fidelity my dear</p>\r\n\r\n<P> it is hard getting over chain linked fenced the size of Sir Galahad when you are drunk. Really hard. Brad gives me a boost and untangles my slip from some poking out wire. For Brad swimming in the skinny is nada new. he has posed  for art classes like me. we nudists out to convert my friend who had never shed her clothes to feel free.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> it is his highschools pool. a damn big fucking pool. Now in retrospect, the swimming, the jumping off highdive and the other littler one were just making it easier for me to puke when we go...true , true. We stop at his house to hose off the car and i slip inside. He offers me stuff to drink so I take some Rice Dream and then realise my body will not take nothing in so while he isnt looking i slowly throw it into their aluminum sink.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> already having thrown up in his bathroom twice i am now steady ENOUGH to wander into the living room. Indian crafts decorate the walls and shelves are full of videos. His mother is a lesbian. He lives with his mother and sister. He\'s a nudist. If only you were a girl, i think.\r\n(sigh)</p>\r\n\r\n<P> dropping me off i sleep. i think of death and meat hamburger jell-O demons eating my legs off. I wake, vomit. Sleep. Wake, vomit. In the morning it continues. I cannot lifeguard today. The alcohol and sushi are killing me. Tearing up my guts with newly cut nails and i feel the acids fill up the scars. I cannot lifeguard today.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i call <I>him</i> so that he knows i am on the brink of death. He...calling once while i was asleep and leaving one message of how he would be at a friends gaming.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> if he were sick i think Id make more of an effort to call. I dont think they know they are sometimes like booze to your insides. YOu cannot feel it going down but when it hits you are sure that it has arrived. CALL ME!!!</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i am disappearing with my frends this weekend...</p> '),(1331,'<P>Arbitrary</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Chandra Levy & El Nino<BR>\r\ngot together and decided<BR>\r\nto clone Jesus from cells<BR>\r\nin the shroud of Turin.</p>\r\n\r\n<P><em>We answer to a higher authority.</em></p>\r\n\r\n<P>A school in Jonesboro was <BR>\r\nterrorized by Ebola monkeys.<BR>\r\nBill Clinton sent his sympathies<BR>\r\nfrom the deck of the Titanic.</p>\r\n\r\n<P><em>Did somebody say McDonalds?</em></p>\r\n\r\n<P>Today on Jerry Springer:<BR>\r\nMilitant Ally McBeal fans<BR>\r\nand the Michael Jordan look alikes who<BR>\r\nwant to have their children.</p>\r\n\r\n<P><em>Just Do It!</em></p>\r\n\r\n<P>All the while, Bill Gates<BR>\r\nSits back singing:<BR>\r\nJesus loves them, this I know<BR>\r\nfor the Bible tells me so.<BR>\r\nLittle ones to Him belong.<BR>\r\nThey all are weak, but I am strong.</p>\r\n\r\n<P><em>Where do you want to go today?</em></p>'),(1332,'you took all my articles away again.  i didn\'t say fuck you to anybody.'),(1333,'<p>I thought my new roommate was the sort of earth sign person who I want around in order to tell me what time it is and ask why I have not picked up my dry cleaning yet.</p>\r\n<p>but no. She is just like me. I cant\'t believe it. She woke up babbling about the dream of a movie she had. I shepherded her through a brainstorming of images.</p>\r\n<p> She wanders jitterily through the bend, choosing one task and then picking another, tripping over herself, yelling at herself for being clumsy, her mind occupied with all manner of dreams and poetry while her legs forget where she was going.</p>\r\n<p>It is so unsettling to see another person who suffers the same ailments of distraction as you. I try best to keep sane persons on me so that I dont wake up in a pile of my own steaming shit whispering cat language.</p>\r\n<p>I just walked over to the stove because something smelled like it was burning. The front burner was on, and an old pan that I had cooked from was turning brown. I scolded myself for not shutting the burner off after cooking. DUMMY! DUMMY! DUM-wait. </p>\r\n<p>it wasnt me. it was her. She meant to boil water on a different burner and had turned the wrong one to \"high\" and gone to the bathroom, most likely to change her hair or pluck something and then in the middle of the task choosing to start a load of laundry or write a thought on the white board.</p>\r\n<p>\"OHHH IM such a dummy,\" she screeched to me and herself when she saw what she had done.</p>\r\n<p>\"I cant believe it. I thought you were all business.\"\r\n<p>\"no im very distracted, like you.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"we have trouble getting around. we are too busy always thinking about god.\" </p>\r\n<p>lord help this house.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1334,'<p><i>some choose a fool for bed.</i></p>\r\n<p><<b>*</b>><br>\r\n  i\'ve worked another 10 hours @ the pod, and my ass is tired from sitting on \r\n  &quot;it&quot; all day. i should bring a pillow or wear padded ass cheeks. but, \r\n  you know, i think &quot;it\'s&quot; going to be okay, so don\'t worry. i\'m going \r\n  to figure something out, like why i get doves and asses mixed-up when i go outside.</p>\r\n<p>i guess coming home w/ ass pain is normal for most people, even gay prostitutes, \r\n  except they probably come home complaining that their asses hurt for other reasons \r\n  that mister a. swinney can totally relate to. unless he\'s a giver. but i don\'t \r\n  think so, he used to take my lunch all the time.</p>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF0000\"><b>beanspasm:</b></font> ass cream <br>\r\n  <b><font color=\"#3333FF\">robotec9:</font></b> mmmmmmmm good </p>\r\n<p>it\'s &quot;ass&quot; if the phrase &quot;ass cream&quot; conjured up lovely \r\n  memories for him or remembrances of a lovely thursday night out on the docks \r\n  doing favours for sailors and scuba divers or when he put his hand on my shoulder \r\n  that one and only time in the restroom and said, &quot;i can shape the curse \r\n  that shapes your life.&quot;</p>\r\n<p>i don\'t know, i think swinneyâ„¢ girls have it easy. i just felt like saying \r\n  that.</p>'),(1335,'<p>I like this girl named Dru. She is a cool girl. She goes to the College of Charleston, a faux-hip school, where she studies religion, taking classes like \"German film noir and it\'s impact on feminism.\" She is short, reasonably attractive, and intelligent. She is my roomate Barrett\'s girlfriend\'s roomate. Everytime I go to Charleston, Barrett and his girlfriend want to go out, shopping, dining, whatever. Usually, Dru comes with us, but she\'s not my date, if you follow me. Catch my drift? Pickin\' up what I\'m puttin\' down? \r\n<p>\r\nOnce, a few months ago, the four of us went shopping in Mount Pleasant, right outside of Charleston. Barrett and I went in one store, the girls in another. They came out of Old Navy, giggling. Dru, bubbling over, told us that a guy had started a conversation with her, and then asked her out. Being the stoic that I am, I showed no emotion other than friendly interest. Barrett\'s girlfriend said \"He\'s really smart too; he\'s an English major and he\'s really into buddhism.\" Barrett just looked at me, and I looked at him. I\'m an English major and a buddhist, goddammit. The irony. I mean, that had to be God poking me in the ribs. Just a little joke from the big guy to me, man to man like. Turns out, the guy never called.\r\n<p>\r\nSo today, Barrett tells me that last weekend, at Dru and his girlfriend\'s apartment, he woke to find a strange boy out in the den, playing hackey-sack. Dru came out of her room, after the boy left, and said that she had met him in a bar the night before, and that they had had sex all night. He was a wonderful guy. He, however, never called again either. But that doesn\'t make me feel any better, because I\'m a buddhist, and I know that\'s not how karma is supposed to work. And I\'m an English major, so I\'ve heard it all before. But I would call. '),(1336,'<p>\r\nas i read every post,<br>\r\nsitting here every day.<br>\r\nnot thinking of the cost,<br>\r\nand only want to play.<p>\r\n\r\njay works on the haiku<br>\r\nthere\'s all this witticism.<br>\r\nwe all adore lulu,<br>\r\nand her antagonism.<p>\r\n\r\nsometimes it\'s fernando<br>\r\nwho gives us a comment.<br>\r\n(he\'d look cute with a bow,<br>\r\nthis is my one lament.)<p>\r\n\r\nwhat is this \'best friendly\'?<br>\r\npeople here seem to care.<br>\r\nsometimes i\'m just lonely,<br>\r\nthings are too much to bear.<p>\r\n\r\ngo to swinney dot org,<br>\r\nand unburden your soul.<br>\r\nbecause i\'m not a borg,<br>\r\nand i\'m not the mole.<p>\r\n\r\nplease don\'t mock my poem,<br>\r\ni  wrote it during school.<br>\r\nlearned about the phoneme,<br>\r\nand now i\'m not a fool.<p>\r\n\r\nnow i feel all better,<br>\r\nthat i\'ve said what i said.<br>\r\nit doesn\'t really matter,<br>\r\nso i\'m going to bed.</p>\r\n'),(1337,'<P>You\'re sitting at the counter, slightly unshaven, wearing a brown leather jacket, and you\'re so very Russell Crowe. You\'re drinking probably bourbon, and you stare incredulously at my frozen Charleston cocktail, and ask me what it is. There\'s a silver ring on your finger, so I worry for a minute, but when I look again, it\'s on your third, not your fourth. And that\'s okay. You\'re smoking malboro lights, a softpack thrown casually on the bar, but I know it\'s your form of self expression, and you\'re with a friend who is obviously uncomfortable to be with you, because you\'re sex and he\'s in your shadow. And so I stare at you, and I know that if you followed me to the bathroom, you\'d throw me against the wall, and we\'d fuck, but if you followed me out the front door of the bar, we\'d get half way home before I\'d have to turn around and say to you \"no, you\'re not the one,\" because what it boils down to is that this is just lust, and I don\'t even want to ask your name. </P>\r\n<!-- he is not the one I love, although maybe he could have been-->'),(1338,'<p>\r\nSomewhere around Historic Ranch \'F\' a cow had broken free of its confines and was busy chewing on its gigantic tongue inches from the side of the highway we\'d been driving on for hours and hours and hours of painful overfull bladders, starvation, road rage at the crystal lady in the white Volvo, extreme temperatures, blinding glare, white polo shirts, neon Nikes, and listening to OMD throughout.<br>\r\nJulian: Ok, cow, BE COOL.<br>\r\nMe: Oh my god, it\'s the size of a fucking Buick. I\'ve never seen a cow so big in my life.<br>\r\nJessica: *laugh* be cool cow.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe road to the Point Reyes Lighthouse is endless. Really. I mean I didn\'t actually SEE the lighthouse at all. How do I know that the .4 miles advertised by the Point Reyes National Seashore sign wasn\'t another sort of trick. Another stretch of unending, mind-numbing prairie after we had mounted what we hoped to be the final hill, the 37th \"final\" just-after-this-one-we\'ll-see-the-ocean-again hill.<br>\r\nMe: Laura Engles, what did we get ourselves into?\r\n</p><p>\r\nOnly .4 miles farther. .4 miles that we so adamantly refused to hike after 3 and a half hours of driving. We pulled into the parking lot, into a wall of swirling fog. No huge stoic lighthouse in sight. A lighthouse I <i>know</i> exists only because I can picture it in my mind from early childhood drives to the coast. How do I know I didn\'t make it up? Maybe I did. Maybe that .4 miles to go sign was actually code for YOU CAN WALK FOREVER AND WILL NEVER SEE IT BECAUSE IT\'S NOT THERE. We got out of the car.<br>\r\nJessica: You\'ve got to be fucking kidding me.<br>\r\nJulian: This is freezing.<br>\r\nMe: Sigh.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe picnic itself took place on a windswept crag of a cliff on a green western blanket spread over prickly bursts of dried grass and rocky dust piles. The picnic itself, the actual event, is not really important.<br>\r\nHere are a few details:<br>\r\nFour pepper goat cheese.<br>\r\nFrench bread.<br>\r\nWine.<br>\r\nAvocado.<br>\r\nRoasted red peppers.<br>\r\nStrawberries.<br>\r\nA toast to America.<br>\r\nScared children looking over the edge.<br>\r\nPhotographers milling about.<br>\r\nCircling birds.<br>\r\nCravings for cigarettes.<br>\r\nJulian: This isn\'t so bad.<br>\r\nMe: No, this is fun.<br>\r\nJessica: This is fun.<br>\r\nJulian: Yeah.<br>\r\nPhotographer: Isn\'t this great, honey.<br>\r\nGirl: No, it\'s scary.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI wanted to spend the 4th of July staring at my wallpaper. I got a chair ready and some Calistoga with lime and a bottle of Jack for the occasion. But I mistakenly answered the door. I had had a little bit to drink. I grabbed some goat cheese out of the fridge and a tie tack and my wingtips to dress up my crumpled outfit and left with J and J to see the lighthouse.<br>\r\nJulian: We\'ve gone all merchant ivory.<br>\r\nMe: Let me get my better suit.<br>\r\nJulian: There\'s no time! There\'s no time! To the lighthouse!\r\n</p><p>\r\nI sat in the backseat and stared out the window. I pretended I was skateboarding as fast as the car, alongside it, and jumping over cars as we passed them. Then I thought about what would happen if I opened the door. Or if I could will the car into the next lane or the guard rail. I weighed the options of taking the 1 or Sir Francis Drake. I knew the coast highway would be packed by the beach but I suggested it anyway because I didn\'t want to drive through Greenbrae, my least favorite town name in Marin County. Traffic stopped almost dead before Stinson Beach while we had to piss like we\'ve never had to piss before. The white Volvo crystal lady would let the people in front of her get what seemed like an enormous distance ahead before she\'d suddenly lurch forward and close the gap.<br> \r\nJessica: Look at the Volvo lady, you can see the instant she\'s going to make her move. I hate her.\r\n<br>\r\nMe: I\'m this close to dragging her from the car.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe highway goes straight through Stinson Beach and you have to drive slow and not hit people. Even when you do stop to let them walk across, they turn and look at you and put up THE HAND, the STOP-now-I\'m-walking hand which makes Jessica want to run them down. I looked at her from the back seat and I knew she was seeing the world through a yellow holding-in-the-pee haze and she just couldn\'t step on the accelerator fast enough as surfers 1, 2, and 3 crossed and gave us the hand. We pulled up at the Stinson Market to use the toilet and a crowd of recent San Andreas High graduates were chillin-chillin against a car by the espresso cart.<br>\r\nBro 1, whose shirt is open to reveal a six-pack and a superman tit tattoo: Hey man, what\'s up?<br>\r\nBro 2: Chillin<br>\r\nBro 1 (chugging the remainder of the flat beer in his Aquafina water bottle): Man, I\'ve been drinkin Hennessee since 8 in the morning, I\'m just <i>starting</i> to get my mellow perv on<br>\r\nBros 2-4: Yeah (Complicated hand shakes and slaps ensue.)\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1339,'<P> my mom did it. cut my hair. she used to do that for a living before she flew over to the states to come live with my dad. My father who is cheat-ing on her with asian phone sex. I guess no ones parents stay together for long.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I am thinking of leaving my apartment and taking only my slips and red lipstick, some bobby pins and moving to big sur. then moving up to alaska. I am sick of where i live now. The hum-drum, my roomie listening to minor threat in the kitchen is too much for me. I am picking up bobby pins off the floor and placing them in this jar just for them. Everything is look, pick-up, put away. My job. My family. My boyfriend.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i can see myself now....slowly selling bits and pieces of my furniture. Only taking my kriptonite stockings collection and some slips, some boots and my cameras. Once everything is sold. I leave. I grab my shit at night...which will most likely be early morning since they dont fall asleep early around here. and slowly slip out of the door. i will have a ride waiting for me with the car going in front of my three bedroom 1.5 bath apt. i get in and throw my bag in the back</P>\r\n\r\n<P> ready for a road trip i say nothing to no one. no one. no chains around the ankles in the form of frienships and lovers and parents. they wont have to pay tuition and he wont have to remember birthdays.I look in the mirror. I have a ffew stray hairs on my forehead...they are holding on for dear life. I am art again. my sleave off the shoulder. my hair bobbed and short my lips red and my deoderant sticking out of my purse which is torn from an adventurous night. free i am.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> free is being scared to me. not knowing what comes next. holding on to the leather seat. nails digging in. searching for people with the same crazy stare in their eyes. sauntering, i walk with my hips. My baby, my camera at my hip. I am a barefoot mother in the kitchen of the city and I am serving everyone breakfast.</P>'),(1340,'<p>Whoo, lord.  It is a slow day today.  <em>Really Slow.</em>  Like total_work = jack/squat.</p>\r\n<p>So here\'s the non-inclusive list:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li> Headphones.  Silver and black.  Cheap from wal-mart but if your hearing sucks they do alright.</li>\r\n<li>Great big box of business cards.  First job I\'ve ever had those...</li>\r\n<li>Shiny metallic rj11 patch cable.  Sort of the iMac of the phone cable world.  I like it; found it in my desk.</li>\r\n<li>Lot\'s of blue postits.</li>\r\n<li>2x25 Lb. barbells.</li>\r\n<li>1x15 Lb. barbell.</li>\r\n<li>Every issue of the Perl Journal.</li>\r\n<li>Two computers.  One win32, one linux.</li>\r\n<li>Four different operating systems.</li>\r\n<li>The lyrics to VnV Nation\'s _Dark Angel_.</li>\r\n<li>An issue of \"Chemical and Engineering News\", topic Computational Chemistry, open to an article talking about GRAPEs.</li>\r\n<li>my Big Ass coffee cup</li>\r\n<li>pencils, rubber bands, thumbtacks, tape, staples.  made into a catapult.</li>\r\n<li>A whiteboard with a beach scene drawn on it.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nOh, and a picture of my fiancee taped to my moniter so that it looks like she\'s smiling right at me.  morale++\r\n</p>'),(1341,'I\'ed rather die. If only I didn\'t feel responsible.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nObligations, procedures, methodical, preplanned. Thus is life. Thus you may survive.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMy world crashes, but I will not lose it. Two options left. 1) Fire first. Lay waste to everything, blow it out of the water. Take her out. 2) Meet her there. No leaving for the weekend. No dissapearing.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nPrehapse I can ignore the... flirting. Rubbing on legs, dancing on bars, taunting men. Nude with the enemy.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nPrehapse I cannot.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBut no matter what, I cannot ignore the churning in my gut, the raised skin on my back, and the pit and pressure in my chest. No penis or pussy should have her. Selfish? Brutish? Pigish? Fuckit. It is worth the labels to just wake up next to her.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMen don\'t visit when they are throwing up. Or so it would seem. I recall a similar event, filled with EMT quality care. Only the foregin rubbing on the legs and the dancing with other men and the skin for the water was not there.\r\n'),(1342,'<p>\r\nMy father has cable television installed the summer after fifth grade, some year in the early 1980â€™s. This changes everything. Itâ€™s the only time during school that I have a group of friends, as the kids who donâ€™t have cable end up at my house. They come inside damp from July humidity and the cool fabric of the sofa in our TV room absorbs the sweat from the back of their legs.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAt the beginning of the summer we democratically pick a channel and watch it without any surfing. Only HBO and MTV get everyoneâ€™s vote.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIf a movie is on HBO, itâ€™s deemed worthy of watching. We sit for four hours at a time, watching two or three bad movies. Even films with crying women in one hundred-year-old costumes warrant a viewing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSomebody calls out: New channel.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMike Lerner says: Wait, wait.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHe has never seen the movie, but has heard that thereâ€™s a possibility of tit.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo we wait, get bored, groan and he begs us: Wait, really, itâ€™s coming up. \r\n</p><p>\r\nLater in the summer my new friends become aggressive channel surfers, even though thereâ€™s no remote control for the cable box. The brown General Instruments switch box normally sits on the top of the television, but it has a long cable that allows one of them to sit on the floor with it and pop the row of buttons that looks like a childâ€™s toy piano.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSteve Linder is the kid who can push the buttons the fastest. At his highest speeds I become worried that he will break the box.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI warn him: Linder, donâ€™t break it or weâ€™ll hafta watch â€œPrice is Right.â€ \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe smiles and mentions the breasts of the girls in primary-colored dresses gesturing at the prizes. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSometimes Steve Linder accidentally presses down two buttons next to each other at once, something thatâ€™s not possible in the design of the box, but still happens. Instead of magic snow, the screen goes blue, then black with a slight vibration at the bottom. We go quiet for a second until he pops another button, a button as far away as possible from the two stuck buttons. \r\n</p><p>\r\nWhen the credits roll on a bad movie, we flip to MTV and take in a few videos. They donâ€™t really like any of the music on MTV, but they really like the videos. My new friends get excited when the Twisted Sister video comes on and even stand up and jump in the air at the appropriate parts, but none of them would ever buy a Twisted Sister album, Def Leppard or any other pop metal. \r\n</p><p>\r\nVideos like â€œLegsâ€ with the girls and their bluejay blue eye shadow, hair moussed and pushed up vertically on both sides, and the clothes, the folded over pink fabrics, elicit Steve Linder to stand up, strut, pinch his nipples and fake moan: \r\n<blockquote>Sheâ€™s totally hot <br>\r\nLook at her socks, <br>with those frills, dude.<br>\r\nLook near the bottom of the dress, <br>\r\nLegs yeah youâ€™re so hot baby!<br>\r\nYou know how to use them, hot bitches!<br>\r\nOh, dude. </blockquote>\r\n</p><p>\r\nJust as I get tired of my new social life, before it becomes unbearable, the summer ends. \r\n</p><p>\r\nBy the middle of sixth grade everyone has cable and I figure that my friends must be watching cable at their houses, because they sure arenâ€™t watching it at mine. \r\n</p>'),(1343,'<p>i\'m not use to this. hanging on, looking for a job. i use to create my own work. hustle my own schedule. wipe my own ass.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am tired of waiting and waiting...only to be told i am overqualified, or underqualified.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am tired of meeting people and having them ask me what i do and struggle to come up with something other than circus performer.</p>\r\n\r\ni started telling my friends that i\'m breaking into the porn industry. it was a joke.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><br>\r\nbut now i\'m seriously considering it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><br>\r\nsome of them have expressed interest in participating. they are backend people, i\'m into content. between the three of us a porn site is something we could pull off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but i swore after i sold the last business i wouldn\'t start another one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and yet, i\'m also fed up with the lack of interesting porn.<br>\r\ncan i do this? do i want to do this?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what would i tell my mother?<br>\r\n\"hi mom, i\'ve got a job finally. it has to do with fucking.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p><br>\r\ni was always the \'good girl\'. i am tired of being the good girl.<br>\r\ni really like that word.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>porn</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1344,'<p>saturday, summer, concrete and brick, snafu and me. it\'s a cruel summer. the sky is white and everything looks like cardboard. it is the perfect sort of day for him to be drawing me indoors in the cold cold bend. Ashley is playing records I have never heard before, and 580 swirls stereophonically between the windows.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have not art-modelled in 3 or 4 years. I sit. I stand. I make my muscles freeze. To keep the body still under command, to be an object, is a spiritual exercise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remembered and resumed the daydreams/thoughts I had then with the ease of reconnecting to icb through screen. Ideas for narrative movies about art models. The lyrics to \"<a href=\"http://www.bobdylan.com/songs/visions.html\">Visions of Johanna</a>\". Memories of Brooklyn, Kim, and Tim. <a href=\"http://www.creativetime.org/900_all_karen/bio_partners.html\">Karen Finley</a>\'s work on <a href=\"http://www.artnet.com/Magazine/news/robinson/robinson7-8-11.asp\">figure model</a> as performance art.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She once modelled for a series of drawing classes and collected all the drawings of herself. She also had friends describe her to police sketch artists and collected those drawings. The <a href=\"http://www.elly.org\">self-mythologization</a> of current female artists as a way of examining the cultural manifestation of gender politics fascinates me, perhaps evident in the way I like making up corny nicknames for myself and have a copy of the \"Womens Encyclopedia of Myths and Secrets\" floating around my desk with the rest of the plankton.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>egg .o0O(I wish elly were still making <a href=\"http://www.divinities.org\">divinities.org</a>)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Most artists like to talk to you when you are in a private session. snafu and I had a surface conversation about art, like dogs sniffing each other\'s butts. When I model for young artists we talk about the relevance of the figure in their art practice. Usually a young artist spits on the need for such things. the figure, pah! go back to 16th century Florence why dont you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>snafu is not one of those. even though he subscribes to the philosophy of ayn rand, modernism does not appear to excite him in art. when he heard my jabberjaw about abstraction, he muttered \"jackson pollock\" (who is considered The Patriarchy by hip post-chicks like me). i commented that pollock was mostly drunk, and probably had few well-formed verbal convictions about what he painted. this pleased snafu. but I didnt mean it to invalidate Pollock\'s work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>snafu is a rebel against the spirit of the times. this pleases me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he worked on my nose. I have an unusual face. When he tried to isolate the features, the forms were different from what he\'s used to. I hated my nose as a kid because I was surrounded by white people. I had a boyfriend who wrote me long letters about the qualities of my nose that could not be found in art or nature. I thought of eve, and how she is in love with a boy who likes bellys a lot. and I thought of how sad and desperate women are for feeling confirmed by this approval. and how boys can reveal a revolutionary nature by their tastes in women. and how fucking satisfying it is to be an object, and to be loved as an object. and then I thought of Karen Finley again. and I still like my nose, either way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>snafu</b>: I should make an armature of your nose.<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: you should make an enormous sculpture of my nose. a very detailed sculpture, enormous, with the freckles and hairs and things.<br>\r\n<b>snafu</b> the nose out of context from the face is a little weird<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>:YEUH!<br>\r\n<b>snafu</b> no.<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: Im into it....\"weird\". I guess you can tell.<br>\r\n<b>snafu</b> people get pissed off when i say this but<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: what? what? [poised for confrontation]<br>\r\n<b>snafu</b> I dont believe that art should be played around with.<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: [upraorious outburst of uncontrollable laughter]. BWAHEHAHSHSHHAaahaha<br>\r\n<b>snafu</b> I don\'t think it\'s a laughing matter.<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: that just makes me laugh more.<br> \r\n<b>snafu</b> fine.</p>\r\n'),(1345,'<p>I went to Charleston, and saw that girl. I have to admit, I was feeling a little weird, but things went pretty well. We all went to this new bar, where some old friends of mine happened to be playing, and my roomate and his girlfriend left early, leaving Dru and myself to walk home together. She was paying me some attention, and we had a good time, dancing hippie-style etc., but when we left, she stopped to talk to the bouncer for like five minutes, all giggly and shit. For the first time in my life, I feel like I handled the situation properly. I am so proud of myself. Really. When I saw that she meant to kind of ignore me, to talk to this other dude, instead of hanging around looking interested, I just sort of started moseying on down the street, all cool-like. I mean, we were leaving by mutual agreement, and then without a word she starts this long conversation with the chump working the door; I\'ve played this little game WAY too many times. I said to myself, \"Fuck it. I\'m out.\" I was rewarded a few moments later with the sound of running feet behind me, and a pounce onto my back. Oh, it was so very, very sweet, let me tell you. We went back to the apartment, and sat up drinking for a while. The conversation did NOT turn to boys that she liked, boys that she thinks are hot, or boys that she has recently fucked. I was trying to put out the \"that sort of thing will end our conversation quickly\" vibe. Whether that played a part in it or not I don\'t know, but either way, it was true and she didn\'t bring it up. We talked shit about her friend, who is hotter than her, but much less interesting/intelligent/cool. Also a good sign. Then, I pulled the candy! No, not really, but I felt some groundwork was laid down.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe most important thing, though, the really crucial element, the reason I feel this is worth your time and mine to write down, is the huge change in my attitude. I mean, I wasn\'t arrogant, but I held in my mind firmly what was acceptable and what was not, and acted accordingly, and I\'m really happy about that. Usually, I fall into the same old traps every time, being too nice, too friendly, etc. and not standing up for myself. I kind of think now that women can be emotional bullies like men can be physical ones, but since you can\'t really pin them down about it, they don\'t see it that way. In other words, it can be excused a thousand different ways, and it doesn\'t leave a visible mark. . I felt for the first time the very real battle that takes place, I saw where to apply pressure and where not to...always before I just knew that I was losing. Badly. \r\n</p><p>\r\nWhy this change in my approach? Who can say. Lately, I feel like God and I have negotiated a new deal somehow. I am listening more. I am playing the guitar better. I must be on the right track, moving here, deciding to be a teacher(at least for a while). I feel reborn, stronger. I hope I can keep it up.</p>'),(1346,'<p> Hi. Here we go with the writers block. Am i even supposed to be doing this? Good god, will it get through , and will people see this? In any case here goes:<P>\r\n\r\n<p> The art of writing is one that I am familiar with but not used to. It is one that I strive to master but in the end it masters me. Never satisfied and always the opposite of how the events turn out in my head, I seem to write more or less as part of my quest for verbal dominance. I may seem a bit obsessive-compulsive when it comes to the English language but, bear with me here and throughout. Seeing as I plan to make a living out verbal pursuits, I figure that in the very least I would like to know what I am doing and controll those abilities to their full extent. If those pursuits leave me emaciated and emotionally exasperated, then so be it. I will be more than happy with myself for becoming that way instead of nothing at all, or worse yet achieveing contentment. The writer\'s block still hasnt gone away yet. However, when the block hits you with full force, the best way to get your intelectual, imaginative, and verbal juices flowing is to just sit back and rant, rave, and simply improvise your thoughts. They always edit themselves on the way out, so what you think may sound good inside, but record differently outside.<p>\r\n\r\n<p>While pondering the nuisance of \"the Block,\" and also researching for tips on how to actually write, I thrust an exercise or rather self discipline upon myself: to write once a day every day. The length of my practice text did not have to make sense nor did it have to be of the same length. All I had to do was jot down a few ideas here and an occasional idea over there every day to make myself used to the practiced of writing. Pretty soon this became as common as a bodily function. I later resoved that as time passed I would teach myself a new aspect of writing. Journaling one day, plot sequencing another etc. As I made the one step forward, I was presented with the giant leap backwards: how do I find an individual voice?<p>\r\n\r\n<p>Times are getting desperate and I am on the threshold of giving up this decadently educational practice. How would I manage to find my literary voice. I am lightyears away from getting published and I  need to distinguish my words from those of the next man. But how? Then it all starts to make sense. Practicing was where I found my voice. Those words. Those thoughts. Those scribbles that I jotted down or typed on a worthless typewriter, were all what made my voice sound, or rather read, the way that they do. And then it dawns on me that writing is like a fingerprint, they are so very unique and the writer is like the finger that the print comes from. So whatever is said can only be expressed in unique manner by whoever is authoring the current work. Hell, I am starting to get used to this whole writing thing after all. Maybe I have nothing to worry about and was just ranting for raving\'s sake. Wait, this needs proofreading...<p>'),(1347,'<FONT SIZE=\"+3\" COLOR=\"#FF2020\">WARNING</FONT> sick cuddlyness ahead\r\n\r\n<P>oh, the distractions that you come across on your way home.  i was\r\non my way back from oakland, to pay the government lots of money for\r\ntowing my car away and whatnot.  silly me had accidentally spent all\r\nmy money on food because i was hungry.  oops, i forgot to save that\r\ndollar i would need to get on the bus.  though, i still have this bart\r\nticket with zillions of dollars of bartmoney that i bought before i\r\never lived here.  that ticket has saved my ass more than once already.\r\nprobably one of the better investments i ever made, really.  but alas\r\nbart only brings me into the city yet not all the way home, there was\r\nan empty wallet.  tried the bank machine, amazing i actually was able\r\nto get some money out of there.  but then, it is a $20.  where to get\r\nchange?  try inside the bank, but they just closed... walking down the\r\nstreet...  had just eaten, so i wasn\'t hungry... </P>\r\n\r\n<P>and then.  cute kittens.  like everyone else on the side of the\r\nroad, the spca is here in downtown, trying to sell CUTE CUDDLY\r\nKITTENS!  oh <I>meow meow mau mrow</I>!  they were in their little\r\ncages and they were so cute and everyone knows how cute kittens are\r\nand and i just needed change for my $20 but there were cute\r\nkittens.... and <I>meow meow</I> they said poor little kittens in\r\ntheir little cat cages.  certainly stopping to pet kittens was more\r\nimportant than change for the bus. oh how i want a cute cuddly kitten.\r\nsuch torture, for i cannot have cute kittens at my house; ert has rats\r\nand it just wouldn\'t work out.  but i had to hang out with the\r\ncats... such a nice charcoal gray cat with a little squeaky meow, and\r\nhe kept meowing at me. meowing at me, just me, no one else, of course.\r\n<I>meowsqueak!  meowsqueak!</I></P>\r\n\r\n<P>So you really should go get yourself a cute cuddly kitten either\r\nfrom someone\'s backyard in the city or from the spca!  I would take\r\nyou to the spca to get animals if my car worked!  Yes, I was coming\r\nhome from rescuing my car when I wanted to rescue the kittens.  So\r\neventually I found Radio Shack, that seemed as good a place as any to\r\nwaste a buck or two so I\'d have change for the bus. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>and now i\'m on the bus, distracting myself with the laptop.  Such a\r\npale distraction it is in comparison to the cute cuddly kittens in the\r\nspca cages. </P>\r\n\r\n<hr>\r\n\r\n<P>and now, even more car rescuing... so i got my paycheck today.  how\r\nmuch like a crack addict i feel, like a junkie, a money junkie, gotta\r\nhave money, gotta feed my money addiction so i can get my car out of\r\njail and get toys and pay the bills.  but the green does the job, i\r\nget my car out of jail... thanks to ann for the long ride over and\r\nback and waiting and stuff... such a scary weird place, full of burnt\r\nand wrecked and towed cars... and they have this weird forklift for\r\nmoving cars, it picks up two of the wheels off the ground.  anyone who\r\nhas been to the towyard before certainly knows this scary, scary\r\ndevice.  in any case my car is safe from that peril, though i did have\r\nto leave it in a scary part of oakland to be fixed... poor car... i\r\nreally don\'t think anyone would steal a car with the entire exhaust\r\nsystem bungee-corded to the roof rack, but you never know...  if it\r\ngets stolen between now and monday then i guess i really really don\'t\r\nneed a car.  and hopefully the cops would stop them, the car is LOUD.\r\ni mean, LOUD!  louder than a honda should ever be, ever.  but soon,\r\nwithin a week i would think i will have a fixed smogged car that will\r\nbe eligible for the next steps of getting registered, and completely\r\nlegal to drive in the meantime.  yay money.  or something.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and now, for something completely different...</P>'),(1348,'<FONT SIZE=\"+3\" COLOR=\"#FF2020\">WARNING</FONT> sick cuddlyness ahead\r\n\r\n<P>oh, the distractions that you come across on your way home.  i was\r\non my way back from oakland, to pay the government lots of money for\r\ntowing my car away and whatnot.  silly me had accidentally spent all\r\nmy money on food because i was hungry.  oops, i forgot to save that\r\ndollar i would need to get on the bus.  though, i still have this bart\r\nticket with zillions of dollars of bartmoney that i bought before i\r\never lived here.  that ticket has saved my ass more than once already.\r\nprobably one of the better investments i ever made, really.  but alas\r\nbart only brings me into the city yet not all the way home, there was\r\nan empty wallet.  tried the bank machine, amazing i actually was able\r\nto get some money out of there.  but then, it is a $20.  where to get\r\nchange?  try inside the bank, but they just closed... walking down the\r\nstreet...  had just eaten, so i wasn\'t hungry... </P>\r\n\r\n<P>and then.  cute kittens.  like everyone else on the side of the\r\nroad, the spca is here in downtown, trying to sell CUTE CUDDLY\r\nKITTENS!  oh <I>meow meow mau mrow</I>!  they were in their little\r\ncages and they were so cute and everyone knows how cute kittens are\r\nand and i just needed change for my $20 but there were cute\r\nkittens.... and <I>meow meow</I> they said poor little kittens in\r\ntheir little cat cages.  certainly stopping to pet kittens was more\r\nimportant than change for the bus. oh how i want a cute cuddly kitten.\r\nsuch torture, for i cannot have cute kittens at my house; ert has rats\r\nand it just wouldn\'t work out.  but i had to hang out with the\r\ncats... such a nice charcoal gray cat with a little squeaky meow, and\r\nhe kept meowing at me. meowing at me, just me, no one else, of course.\r\n<I>meowsqueak!  meowsqueak!</I></P>\r\n\r\n<P>So you really should go get yourself a cute cuddly kitten either\r\nfrom someone\'s backyard in the city or from the spca!  I would take\r\nyou to the spca to get animals if my car worked!  Yes, I was coming\r\nhome from rescuing my car when I wanted to rescue the kittens.  So\r\neventually I found Radio Shack, that seemed as good a place as any to\r\nwaste a buck or two so I\'d have change for the bus. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>and now i\'m on the bus, distracting myself with the laptop.  Such a\r\npale distraction it is in comparison to the cute cuddly kittens in the\r\nspca cages. </P>\r\n\r\n<hr>\r\n\r\n<P>and now, even more car rescuing... so i got my paycheck today.  how\r\nmuch like a crack addict i feel, like a junkie, a money junkie, gotta\r\nhave money, gotta feed my money addiction so i can get my car out of\r\njail and get toys and pay the bills.  but the green does the job, i\r\nget my car out of jail... thanks to ann for the long ride over and\r\nback and waiting and stuff... such a scary weird place, full of burnt\r\nand wrecked and towed cars... and they have this weird forklift for\r\nmoving cars, it picks up two of the wheels off the ground.  anyone who\r\nhas been to the towyard before certainly knows this scary, scary\r\ndevice.  in any case my car is safe from that peril, though i did have\r\nto leave it in a scary part of oakland to be fixed... poor car... i\r\nreally don\'t think anyone would steal a car with the entire exhaust\r\nsystem bungee-corded to the roof rack, but you never know...  if it\r\ngets stolen between now and monday then i guess i really really don\'t\r\nneed a car.  and hopefully the cops would stop them, the car is LOUD.\r\ni mean, LOUD!  louder than a honda should ever be, ever.  but soon,\r\nwithin a week i would think i will have a fixed smogged car that will\r\nbe eligible for the next steps of getting registered, and completely\r\nlegal to drive in the meantime.  yay money.  or something.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and now, for something completely different...</P>'),(1349,'<P>I forget. All the time. That not everyone has a secret history. I always assume everyone has one. They may go through the motions day in and day out and pretend the past is the past. But deep inside they are living with something they don\'t talk about all the time. It doesn\'t have to always be dark. Actually it would be more fun to hunt for the happiest person alive. Someone put them in a museum please. On nights, where things felt horrible I turned to music. Made it my religion. I\'ve yet to meet someone my age that doesn\'t goof on religion. I\'m one to talk, i\'m in love with an athiest (spelling?) \r\n\r\n<P>String theory made sense to me yesterday, God\'s mind is made of movements, musical, probably violins. \r\n\r\n<P>I dreamed I paid two million dollars to get launched into space, I was in orbit, it was a little cabin, i landed back in water, space food was fun to play with. It was the nicest dream ever.\r\n\r\n<P>Sunday morning will end. while i still wait for a girl to buy me a coloring book, those girls are the best. god bless them.\r\n'),(1350,'<P>today has been a slide downward from the morning.   sunday whether i like it or not is always my day of heavy understanding.  the day i remember everything i have to do.  it is a form of worship. things larger than me that control my life as i see it, i stand before them and prostrate my mind.  forgive me for the last year.  for the last five years.  for my entire life of procrastination, indulgement in instant gratification, spending of other peoples money, and sadness for it all.\r\n</p><P>\r\ni was walking valencia past zietgiest motorcycle bar.  black sabbath was playing.  my only want was to sit in there beer garden with a pitcher and listen to the juke box.  not go to yeti\'s and clean.  not go to santa cruz and interview for apartments.  not call my parents.\r\n</p><P>\r\nthe only thought that makes me happy is logging on to the bank website and direct paying my credit card company two thousand dollars to put in their ears and burn.\r\n</p><P>\r\ni am at elly\'s writing this on her laptop and crying inside becausee i am so happy to see her.  cool.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\"Fan\"</p>'),(1351,'<p>\r\nthe comedy workshop is a one week intensive course for people who want to become better at being funny. the workshop is not intended to make people funny, its intended to show them what its like on stage, and behind the scenes. the faculty help students cut things from their acts, and also teach them the tricks of the trade with regards to sketch and improv comedy. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nfor the past two years, chevy chase has been invited to the final dinner to receive an award: a statuette of the muse of comedy. for the past two years he has had to bail out due to two very different reasons. however this year he showed up and well he was really nice.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsince he\'s calmed down the whole acting thing he has been playing piano. apparently he plays four hours a day and has played carnegie hall twice. he was a very nice man and both my mom and maria thought he was dashing and got all hot and bothered. an electric keyboard was hooked up for him in my bosses office and he played some jazz for us and it was very nice. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyeah so that was the end of a very long week for me. it was fun fun fun. now for the writing workshop in one week. ohhhhh man its dry some o tha time. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1352,'<p>The smell of the room is at once unforgettable and undescribable. It hits you like a shot of whisky - leaving a warm sensation deep within your soul and then several minutes later reaffirming its existence with pulsations of contentment. The wave-like patterns of the wind-blown rain are still quite audible. Inside, which is not much bigger than the nutshell of a certain Danish prince, is somewhat wet, but there is still the feeling of freedom, of spaciousness that comes with the fresh April rains. Candles are the only source of light in the room and they carelessly throw their color. The walls dance about the room trying to catch the yellow, perhaps in an effort to cover their exposed flesh.\r\n\r\n<p>Dark yellow splotches are spontaneously cast about the wall as well, implying that the plaster flesh of the walls was not applied very well. This skin, most likely, was thrown on with a paper plate, or by some other mechanism that was surely not designed for constructing walls. The flesh of the wall is so thin in spots, the bones can be seen, and in other places it is so thick that folds of fat bulge forth. Intricate patterning, most likely derived by accident, could easily seduce one into staring at the textures all day and discover as many new patterns as one would dare to count. The design is broken on the far wall, where an exposed pipe runs straight down the middle and is sporadically conjoined with the rolls of fat on the wall. \r\n\r\n<p>The main fixtures of the room, two chairs and a table, are rather plain. However, they are so nondescript in the extreme, that they are most assuredly special. The furniture is of the usual sturdy variety, all made of wood, four legs, the chairs with sturdy backs, the table with a strong, solid top. They are unfinished, and so show off their unique patterns and when inspected up close, one can easily find the nicks and scars that are inevitable. The table is marbled with coffee stains that seem to grow out of the surface, or perhaps fade into it. Perhaps the stains are remnants of once vibrant, rebellious drops of coffee that were absorbed into the table, and now all that remains is a tribute in the form of a glorious brown splotch. Cigarettes, some partially decayed, others completely decomposed, are strewn about the table haphazardly - as are their ashed remains. Plates offer the resemblance of elevated plains in contrast to the clutter on the table. Scattered around these high plains, are two coffee mugs; in the plains lies some food.\r\n\r\n<p>The stubby, white ceramic coffee mugs and their mated saucers - one of which has been cracked under the weight of its relationship - contain just a bit of coffee at the bottom. On the outside of one mug sits a drop that has run down the side about half-way down and stopped, as though it had suddenly become aware of its situation and is trying in vain to be in control of its fate. Yet, it too will soon be sucked up into the atmosphere, or pulled to the ground by gravity, or in some way be forced to submit to the forces of nature. \r\n\r\n<p>There is some raspberry jam clinging to the side of a saucer - the saucer whose mug-mate harbors the daring, yet confused drip. This red welt on the saucer is brought out even more by the strong, deep green of the ceramic. (To view these colors together in the flickering yellow light and through absinthe eyes could remind one of The Night Cafe.) There is also some coffee which has gathered in the saucer as though it sought to escape from the coffee mug and then realized that it just ran into yet another obstacle; perhaps it should just be happy where it lay. Yet it is only one small step away of being freed from the ceramic prison. . . .\r\n\r\n<p>The ceramic plates - colored like ancient stationery - have concentric circles leading from the outer edge to the heart of the plate which would almost surely tempt an eight-year-old child to follow the trail with a finger until all of the food was swirled together. But there were never any children near these plates. Here we find eggs and toast with a side of crushed butts. It is difficult to tell if the food has been eaten, although it has been thoroughly disrupted. Slices of butter are cast among the food. The shape - a little yellow square made even more yellow and warm from the candlelight - is still recognizable, but a halo has developed making it much more difficult to define where the butter ends and where the rest of its world begins. \r\n\r\n<p>The toast and eggs, however, had left their pre-determined boundaries behind long ago and merged into one; the white viscous egg is peppered with toast fragments and one large chunk of bread had been guided straight into one of the domes, letting the once contained yellow molasses to ooze across the plate - held up here and there by sausage link levees. \r\n'),(1353,'<p>i want to go to tulum, mexico. and make sundials in mexico. and i want to drive to the lightnight fields. and white sands. and lay down in naked in the scorching sand and take pictures of you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to stare out the window and have you turn on music that i have not heard and babble in my ear about how you found it at that bar that time  when it was free before the models and the renegade fashion stylists\r\nstarted coming.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to lay on your collar bone and smell the nape of your neck, and i want you to tell me i smell like pez.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to make movies of girls freaking out and screaming and project them on a wall while other, live girls turn knobs and punch their powerbooks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i want to grow tomatoes. and chop them up and sautee them with fresh\r\nrosemary and garlic and orange zest and balsamic vinegar, and serve them\r\nwith spinach pasta and garlic bread and drink it with red wine.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and also, a picnic. at big maam, with smoked oysters. and maybe camping that night by the river. and go to the river at night and lay down in the water and look at the stars.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i will try not to want too much today. i will make difficult phone calls<br> and pay bills,<br>and take apart furniture<br> and send you a picture of me<br> and clean off my desk<br> and revise my writing<br> and breathe.</p>'),(1354,'<pre>\r\na cup//\r\na saucer and//\r\ntwo lemons and///\r\nsweet!//\r\nsour!//\r\ncold!///\r\nhot tea and steam in face like//\r\nwaves like//\r\nsmoke///\r\n(like)//\r\nsugar\r\n</pre>'),(1355,'<p>\r\ntoday i went over to 24th street to check my mailbox. i have a mailbox at mailboxes etc, so that creepy stalkers can have my address and mail me things. creepy stalkers like caitlin darfler in wayne, MI.\r\n</p><p>\r\na few weeks ago, when i was in the midst of thick and poignant depression regarding boys (something i\'m not sure i\'m over yet but is not as dire as it was then), i told caitlin on AIM that i am avoiding boys. i am getting them to leave me alone by not washing my hair or anything really. do not touch me. \r\n</p><p>\r\nshe told me when she was in that mode she wore a JESUS headband (it occurs to me now that the headband is good because not only does it mention jesus to wary onlookers, but it can be used to cover the third eye. two birds, one stone). then she said she would mail the JESUS headband to me so i could use it.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni never believe anyone when they say they\'re going to mail something, because i am so bad at mailing things.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut today i had a yellow slip in my mailbox, saying i had a package. i brought the slip up to the counter. <i>who could be sending me a package</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nreturn address: caitlin. wayne MI.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<font color=\"red\">WOW SHE REALLY SENT THE JESUS HEADBAND</font>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni stared at the return address and laughed a loud shriek-laugh and covered my face. the nice men of mailboxes etc were unperturbed.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere is the letter that was laying at the top of the package when i opened it:\r\n</p><br>\r\n<tt>\r\nTo ward off boys:\r\n<br>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n1 \"Jesus\" headband<br>\r\n1 vial of patchouli, to cover up body stink<br>\r\n4 Gaurdian pokemon<br>\r\na shitload of special gum, for when you don\'t brush your teeth<br>\r\n1 book on living with cats. (since you\'ll be spending less time with boys and more with your feline counterpart)<br>\r\n1 homemade toothbrush bracelet and fake pearls for dress up<br>\r\n1 80\'s rock mullet wig<br>\r\n1 old enquirer magazine (toilet reading)<br>\r\n1 squirtle ring<br>\r\n3 tapes (girly music)<br>\r\n1 dozen roses (all the romance you need)<br>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<br>\r\nIN CASE OF EMERGENCY -> 1 magnum condom<br>\r\n(never accept anything less than horse cock (aka donkey dick) during this time in your life.)\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>i think i will loan one of these pokemons to egg. she needs it. maybe she could tape it over her third eye. duct tape. \r\n</p><p>\r\ncaitlin rules. i still don\'t wash my hair much, but i\'m beginning to catch on that boys like stinky armpits. thank god for the jesus headband and patchouli. for serious defense. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1356,'<P>I\'m about to embark on some very grownup things.  First of all, I\'m getting married in about 7 or so months.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I\'ve been super-excited and nervously telling the people that I love and gauging reactions and things like that.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>When I get married, I will be 21. Traditionally, an adult.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>One of my favourite things is wide-eyed-child-like-wonder and I make sure I view the world through it regularly. I\'m not giving that up just because I\'m about to do grownup stuff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We\'ve been living together for over a year anyway, it won\'t change the way we feel about each other because our love just grows with time. The only thing that will really change will be my name. I like the thought of taking his name.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>People will still think I am too young to be doing this and I am naive and little and \"just jo\".  That is okay by me. I am quite young, I am still learning and I am just jo... but this is a decision I\'ve been making for a long time and I definitely think it is the right one. I am in love with the person I want to spend the rest of my life with. He gets me. I get him. *glee*</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1357,'<p>- <b>jj the fat kid</b> (fuck you all)<br>\r\n        - <b>whiz kid</b> (killed some trees)<br>\r\n        - <b>weirdo the wuss</b> (sensitive and strange)<br>\r\n        - <b>fuckface</b> (not what you think)<br>\r\n        - <b>brotha</b> (i\'m not even black)<br>\r\n        - <b>asshole</b> (by like 12 people)<br>\r\n        - <b>bitch tits</b> (had bigger tits than the girls in 3rd grade)<br>\r\n        - <b>squash</b> (went through a squash only eating stage)<br>\r\n        - <b>fag </b>(was putting my hands on my hips for a while)<br>\r\n        - <b>joma</b> (combo name)<br>\r\n        - <b>leonard</b> (fuck you, mmmk?)<br>\r\n        - <b>hey you</b> (some people think they\'re funny)<br>\r\n        - <b>cooter </b>(like the vagina)<br>\r\n        - <b>a.b. </b>(adult baby stage)<br>\r\n        - <b>conehead</b> (the doctors pulled so hard on my head)<br>\r\n        - <b>incredible sulk</b> (only when i\'m real mad)<br>\r\n        - <b>soup eater</b> (i had my teeth pulled out and sent to the lab for \r\n        3 months)<br>\r\n        - nipple pincher (how can you not? ripe)<br>\r\n        - <b>shoe guy the shy guy</b> (stylish but humble)<br>\r\n        - <b>male slut w/ stirrups</b> (i\'ll always remember those 24 hours (hey \r\n        ladies))<br>\r\n        - <b>tight trousers</b> (i was growing at an unbelievably fast rate)<br>\r\n        - <b>loser</b> (but i always win)<br>\r\n        - <i>generic names for sons named for father</i>: <b>chip, skip, sonny</b> \r\n        (i don\'t even know my dad\'s name or where he lives)<br>\r\n        - <b>the taco inspector</b> (i\'m a natural)<br>\r\n        - <b>jack</b> (off)<br>\r\n        - <b>the washer</b> (back scrubber)<br>\r\n        - <b>theitalianlastname.com</b> (only if you say it wrong)<br>\r\n        - <b>the r00ster</b> (must be the hair)<br>\r\n        - <b>crime dog</b> (was in jail for 3 hours (they had the wrong white guy))<br>\r\n        - <b>bill</b> <i>or</i> <b>wilhelm</b> (kraut)<br>\r\n        - <b>eyescoor all the time</b> (i really do)<br>\r\n        - <b>igor or yegor</b> (i was limping for a while)<br>\r\n        - <b>pink lipstick</b> (sometimes it just pops up)<br>\r\n        - <b>irritable alice</b> (don\'t fucking touch me)<br>\r\n        - <b>the fish</b> (i smell like one, too)<br>\r\n        - <b>choir boy</b> (i can do nothing wrong)<br>\r\n      </p>'),(1358,'<p>\r\ni am a pest.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni put people through some sort of trauma and i seem to be demanding. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy political views are looked down upon.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni demand, and dont expect, replies. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni try too hard.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim shy in real life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am a pest. \r\n</p>'),(1359,'<p>Monday night. I\'m trying to go to sleep. I feel fine. I hadn\'t been drinking or anything. Just a regular night at the Moll residence.</p>\r\n<p>Then I PUKE ALL OVER THE PLACE.</p>\r\n<p>For no reason. I couldn\'t believe it. I contemplated my vomit, thinking, Is this happening? I didn\'t feel sick, not at all. I was about to start laughing when I hurled again and it didn\'t feel very good. And again.</p>\r\n<p>So I cleaned it up, changed my shirt and went to bed. As I drifted off to sleep I kept picking food chunks out of my nose. I woke up today to find out that I didn\'t get another job.</p>\r\n<p>This is truly becoming the Lost Summer.</p>\r\n'),(1360,'<P> ive decided that i hate what i am doing. sitting across from my mom who is complaining that i never brush my hair. that the reason why i still teach swim lessons is my hair. i guess it all boils down to hair.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i order an omelette with swiss and spinach at COCOS.i remember ben and how we would rarely go there my soophmore year...it was DENNYS we would ride our bikes to.food is a few bucks more at COCOS and when youre a student it all adds up. i eat my toast and drink my coffee and fork my eggs and potatos into mouth methodically. i dont want to be late for a job i hate. i dont want to not have rent money and be forced to move from a city i cant stand.feeling like some orphan who never gets breakfast sitting here accross from my mother thinking this is college life, this is it. My mother sits accross from me. Her medium length nails nicely lacquered. Her lips good and rosy. She hold her coffee cup in a dainty I-AM-BEING-WATCHED fashion. She looks 35 but is 49. She\'s a hot looking woman. but i dont need to brush my hair. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> he thinks i am turning alcoholic. maybe. while i was in israel i drank alot. my problem is my throat starts turning numb after a bit and the clear fluid that is vodka just rolls on down. i have no end. maybe thats what i am. i need to go to frisco and visit friends.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> tonight i need to shower to get chlorine out of my hair, the kind of chlorine that will stay with me until late september and does not wash out.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> tonight i need to play some Quake I and then sleep for a bit for i want to stay up all night and make a movie on 4th and Lime st.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> tonight i will not call my mother who asked me to for she saw me this morning, and i, if i die would like to be remembered as having had a dishevelled head as i did this morning eating breakfast.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> \"ooooh child things are gonna get easier......oooooh child things will be brighter....\" they used to write uplifting songs.\r\ni guess i must make the best of being a philsophy major and must focus on postmodernism and be the lone ranjer on campus since nobody else does this.\r\ni guess i must.</p>\r\n\r\n \r\n '),(1361,'<P> that is what relationships seem to say. stay riled in a certain space. dont stray to far from the dge or you may drown. or maybe it is the edges that cut us so we cant speak.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> he says he doesnt want me to see him anymore. he says he doesnt want me to talk of him which i can do since i cant go over every aspect of my day in one phonecall. in one email. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> i have tried. we have tried.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> at first i though how nice. i felt like a baby in the water. so smooth and slippery and nothing got him heated in that jeolous way and things were early i suppose and that is why.now things are oldER. there are a few days with more color in them than most. but still and i wonder what the hell? (with a compton accent)</p>\r\n\r\n<P> i want to choose who i am friends with.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> maybe he knows that with this person i will drown. and their wont be any for anyone. how can one tell like that???maybe i am a freak and making too much out of not speaking to one guy for the sake of my relationship but at the same time its crazy.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> freedom and who i associate with is who i am .\r\n</p>'),(1362,'Pain can be good thing - an excellent alarm clock.\r\nI experienced a little bit of personal pain and confusion today. Nothing major at all just disturbing in a minor way. The kind of pain that really gets to you but then goes away fairly quickly.<BR>\r\nBefore I go on, a side point on <I>politeness</i>.<BR>\r\nPoliteness has an element of distance. When you\'re polite it means that you are behaving in a ritualized, formalized manner. You\'re not revealing or giving yourself fully or truly. Politeness is an act of respect, accomodation and gratitude. When you\'re polite you are gracefully dancing with the other. Giving an acknowledgement of \"you matter\".<BR><BR>\r\nA guy who I used to work with mentioned by email that some people from our old work were going to be getting together. I decided to drop by.<BR>\r\nIt had been three and a half months since I had seen these people. I felt slightly nervous walking in. There they were -- faces I was familiar with, faces of the people I saw every day for years -- gabbing away and drinking margaritas. There wasn\'t much reaction from the group except for a couple. In fact they guy in the office next to mine barely gave me a shrug. There wasn\'t much curiousity from anyone to what I was doing. They\'re too involved talking about the same boring crap that I was glad to not be a part of anymore.<BR>\r\nTo make this long story short, afterwards while I was driving back home I had this feeling of alienation and worthlessness. I really didn\'t even know why. Well I knew the cause of course, but the intensity of it was confusing.<BR>\r\nI guess because for me it was a reassuring sight to be around people I used to spend time with. For some reason I thought it would fun. That I could talk to these people about my recent experiences. The harsh reality though is that most of these people considered nothing more than a piece of furniture in their daily crappy routine. On their internal radar it must have been something like, \"Oh there\'s that guy...whatever\". It wasn\'t even resentment or envy, just indifference.<BR>\r\nDriving home what was depressing was to look back in time and see how my social sphere has slowly shrunk and shriveled up to the point where I could somehow confuse <I>co-workers</i> with old friends. You gain one thing, you lose another. I was painfully aware of what I lost.<BR><BR>\r\nSo anyway, when I got home I threw a <A HREF=\"http://www.akirarabelais.com/i/i.html\">I-Ching hexagram</a> which I do daily.<BR>\r\nIt came up with <A HREF=\"http://www.akirarabelais.com/i/i.html#8\">8. \"Holding Together\"</a>, third line. (The I-ching is a chinese oracle thing and a long subject to explain, so I won\'t here.)<BR>\r\nHere\'s the text from that that is dead-on:<BR><BR>\r\n<TT>\r\nSix in the third place means:<BR>\r\nYou hold together with the wrong people.<BR><BR>\r\n We are often among people who do not belong to our own sphere. In that case we must beware of being drawn into false intimacy through force of habit. Needless to say, this would have evil consequences. Maintaining sociability without intimacy is the only right attitude toward people, because otherwise we should not be free to enter into relationship with people of our own kind later on.\r\n</tt><BR>\r\nSo going back on my side thing on politeness. I guess in a way what would have liked was one of two things: a friendly response or a polite response. I got neither -- more like a familiar disregard.<BR>\r\nSo anyway, I just wanted to share that with swinney.org since hopefully someone could relate to me and what I just wrote, rather than as a piece of furniture.<BR>\r\n\r\n'),(1363,'<P> I cant stand that other people know. So this is for the other people. ha, I guess you guys know. Im the bitch in the relationship. Im the one that sucks and says mean things and talks about reality and breaking up. I stir up the dish full of water and those floaty things and some water splashes out.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I cant stand long days of feeling alone and WHY do all those people think that being in a committed relationship means you will not feel ALONE because now I feel more alone than I ever have.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> why the hell did i come back? for what? I shouldve stayed in Tel Aviv and bummed the narrow streets become the only taxi driver that spoke english better than hebrew and was a woman...of course, i would have to learn how to drive and all first off...now one of my GoOD  good friends is off to law school and it seems she will be growing up and i feel so young and fucktarded at 22. off to the discoteque we go tonight...only not to worry it is THREE women tonight and no man to see me in my birthday suit as we randomly go skinny dipping at some local highschool.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> you make me feel a bit hidden.all of me is not out and free and sometimes i think i do things to go back to where i once was. you are at the beach now. maybe still on the way there. i dont think i miss you. i am mad and angry and disheveled inside over everything that i thought we would be already. it has been a year and i thought i would know your insides much more by now.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>yes i think one has to live with the person to know them, we are far away all this sand in the middle. angel and i shed some tears for you and ben  and chris.oh, how you men make us hurt. so so very bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> my neighbor chick is a MALENA. beautiful. long black hair and a good belly. tall and slim. tina and i were calling dibs. I think she got her.\r\noh, well...i thought i had you.</p>'),(1364,'<br>\r\n<tt>Ten Sordid Things on My Mind at the Moment:</tt>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n1. NT machine here at work that crashed and burned, defies fixing, wondering why HD sounds like a drum-machine, wondering why I didn\'t back up more often, contemplating stripping machine and beefing up others with stripped components.\r\n<br>\r\n2. I drank too much coffee this morning and am feeling the onset of caffeine-induced depression (like a bad trip, though, you can convince yourself that it is foreign-substance-induced, and will pass, not a permanent state of your psyche, thankfully).\r\n<br>\r\n3. Girlfriend-related disillusionment; four years of intense vibe, now I\'m at an arm\'s reach without any explanation.  Did I do/not do something?  Hello...?\r\n<br>\r\n4. Good mutual friend moving from East Coast to SF, and how this is effecting everyone\'s state-of-mind.  Recent breakup of two good friends, and how this is effecting things.\r\n<br>\r\n5. Mental state of Gf\'s sister/family, and how this is effecting Gf\'s state-of-mind.\r\n<br>\r\n6. Anxiety after reflecting on the statement \"I\'m in too deep; sometimes I think my family and friends like you better than I do\" and wondering, then, how deep is too deep after 4 years, and why wasn\'t this ever discussed before now?  Combined anxiety over the statement \"you\'re my soul-mate... I <i>know</i> I\'ll end up with you permanently, I just need some time now to do my thing\" and weighing how I feel about this... knowing the intention is right, but stomaching it is a bitch.\r\n<br>\r\n7. Realization that I haven\'t talked to any of my family in over two weeks, and at the same time, have indulged too deeply in psychotropics.\r\n<br>\r\n8. Happy that the new apartment I\'m moving into in a month is amazingly cool and better suited for me than anywhere I\'ve lived in a decade, and happy about good friend that fixed me up with this great living arrangement.\r\n<br>\r\n9. I need to get outside and run and sweat more, get more sunburned, get the summer-sun-bleached hair going again.  Too bad today is rainy.\r\n<br>\r\n10. I really have the craving for an orange soda.\r\n<br>'),(1365,'<br>\r\nTo wake,<br>\r\nto transduce rays of light into music.<br>\r\nPhotons sing out, \"a new month\"<br>\r\na new day<br>\r\na year, passing so quickly.<br>\r\nWind blows the sky clear, a giggle of<br>\r\nautumn, tempting<br>\r\nhumid faces with tepid winds,<br>\r\nand I long for September.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1366,'<P>I\'ve always been an intuitive person. Very intuitive. I\'m good at reading situations and people. I seem to have a knack for \'knowing\' what\'s going on. I don\'t know why this is; perhaps it\'s because I\'m a scorpio. Perhaps it\'s a gift, who knows. It\'s not always been a good thing for me; it\'s caused it\'s share of pain. </p>\r\n<P>I\'ve also always had very active dreams. My brain seems to really work my hearts biggest issues out when I dream. For instance, I\'ve had dear friends whom I haven\'t spoken to for years. A few of the relationships ended on a somewhat bad note. In most instances, I\'ve begun having dreams about someone. Very intense dreams, leaving me upon waking with a very deep sense of longing for the person and the sudden drive to find them and reconnect. Every time I\'ve done so, and every time I\'ve rekindled the friendship. And the dreams stop. This kind of thing happens in other situations. I\'ll have intense dreams about a person, that they\'re in a bad place. The dreams won\'t stop until I take some sort of action. The dreams are almost always intuitive. So I pay attention.</p>\r\n<P>Now there\'s a new one. My partner wants to move in together. We\'ve been talking about it for a month or so and have come to the decision to do it. Since that decision has been made, I\'ve been having dreams that he\'s either cheating on me (He\'s an admitted ex-cheater), breaking up with me in a very bad way or that we\'re having a huge fight. </p>\r\n<P>This is a situation so close to home. It\'s so damn intimate. I can\'t decide the course of action. Are the dreams intuitive that we\'re making a HUGE mistake? Am I making a mistake? Or is it the more simple definition that I\'m just scared, having lived with a partner who was really not the  one for me. Meanwhile, these dreams are so intense that they leave me drained and achy. I woke up this morning crying, after dreaming that he dumped me in a very mean way. I need them to stop. </p>\r\n<P>Fuck. I wish I could turn this shit off. That\'d make life so much easier.</p>\r\n'),(1367,'<P>I occasionally get cravings for a Big Mac which I normally ignore and get falafel or a chicken burrito instead, but today I ate a Big Mac.  My craving wasn\'t even that great, but McDonald\'s is giving away $1 million.... yeah, what can I say?</p><P>Did you ever hear the joke about the devout priest who prays to God and asks to win the lottery?  After weeks of rigorous prayer, God finally comes down and tells the guy to buy a freakin\' ticket.</p><P>The only way to guarantee failure is to not try.</p><P>So, every day I find myself thinking about buying a lottery ticket.  It\'s only $1, and it\'s a hope, a dream, a possibility, and I never get around to actually buying one.  That doesn\'t stop me from dreaming of a life of financial freedom, of not having to work and being able to do all the things I really want to do.  I moved to NYC to train to be a dancer, but working eight hours a day really cuts into my free time.  My days consist of working full time and trying to squeeze in 2-3 hours of classes.  That hardly leaves any time for mundane tasks like laundry, cleaning my room, showering much less the yoga I should be doing to increase strength and flexibility and a social life I\'m feeling hungry for.  There\'s so much I want to do and the liberation of those eight unpleasant hours of my day is so tantalizing, not to mention the phat pad I could afford with a million in the bank.</p><P>Anyways, I peeled back my game pieces and discovered that.... I didn\'t win.  Big surprise.  When it comes down to it, I\'m right where I should be in my life.  I\'m reaping what I\'ve sown and learning the lessons I need to learn.  The easy way out is appealing, but adversity builds strength.</p>'),(1368,''),(1369,'<p>\r\nIt is 2 pm on Wednesday, July 18th. The blues came in this morning. I told the press we needed an extra day with them so I am now faced with a relatively empty afternoon while the proofreaders meticulously examine every page, a task I maintain is ridiculous because I refuse to let them change typos in the body text anyway.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe Art Department is located in the center of the office and everyone else sits in individual small offices around the perimeter of it. I am sitting here looking at people\'s doors. I am remembering the nicknames I gave them all when I first started working here. Puffy 2 in the corner office (at an old job there were puffy and buffy in the circ dept so now my editor in chief gets stuck with Puffy 2), and Puffy 2\'s assistant, Cutey, is in the next office.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI have an ongoing stupid crush on Cutey that seems to flare up mostly when I haven\'t gotten laid in a while. It\'s a lot like the crush I had on Nicky before we started dating. A northern blonde, waspy, taller than me, drives a nice new car. A lot like Nicky, actually. It\'s this crush where you assume nothing will ever come of it at the beginning and that\'s kind of how you want it to stay. It\'s just something to keep your mind busy, something to think about when you\'re daydreaming, an excuse to act nervous around someone every day.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe other thing about Cutey is her adultness. She behaves like a grown-up. She looks like she buys her stylish pants at Macy\'s and pays her credit card bill on time. It\'s hot. When I was about 18 or so, my friend Brooks and I used to go down to the financial district from time to time and stand around on a street corner at lunch time and wait for a couple of business ladies to pick us up, take us to a hotel and fuck us, reapply their lipstick and be back to their desks by 1. Business ladies. Adults. I\'m telling you, they\'ve got something.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe boy who sits near me and keeps the photo archives organized and occasionally I give him projects doesn\'t have a nickname yet. He has little hushed phone conversations with his boyfriend all day and I always strain to hear. Sometimes I catch the sweet moments.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe other editors are Hairy Man, The Teeth, Curly, Too Cool and The Performance Artist. The Ad Traffic Person is Maroo. Li\'l Short Hairs is the head of the circ dept. and she has the office next to The Lady, who is HR. There are assorted others with no nicknames but I guess they\'re not important. Now that I think about it, I\'m going to start calling the DB Manager Muppet.\r\n</p><p>\r\nTo be continued....'),(1370,'<pre>\r\n<font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">my shroud.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">   it shall cover me</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">      shelter me.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">maybe comfort me.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">i will wrap myself</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">   hide myself</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">i will be safe</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">   and alone</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">and noone will know me.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">i will not see you</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">    be far from you</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">be next to you.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">i will be invisable</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">       and viable</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">   and hear every secret.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">cry to myself</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">and when uncovered will be as new.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Courier New,Courier\">alive and reborn. I.</font></pre>'),(1371,'<p>Im not too sure of how im feeling anymore, but its like a total drain.<br>I\'ve almost completely lost the feeling of happiness.<br>theres times when i can laugh about something and laugh with someone, but I\'ve totally lost that feeling of joy.<br> The one where you know its going to be okay, or you know its going to work out.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m tired, and lonely, and I feel really bored with everything. Like i\'ve totally lost all motivation.<br>I seriously can\'t be bothered doing anything.<br> Every day is the same, I turn up to my job, waste the day away, end up maybe doing a few things, just to go home, have an argument with someone i\'m supposed to be in love with, make up, argue, make up, and do it all again the next day</p>\r\n<p>I dont know if I want to do it anymore, and I dont know any way of getting this fixed.<br> Perhaps its just some kind of seasonal thing, or i\'m just unhappy with the relationship i\'m in, or i\'m thinking too much.</p>\r\n'),(1372,'<P> We went dancing. it was more like being dragged to get ones head chopped off but i reconciled with the much promised cup of coffee that perhaps awaited me that night. i wasnt in the mood for dancing. everything was sad that day. i had been ( and continue at this very second) to have problems with my so called so called loved one, partner, boyfriend, fiancee, lover, friend..whatever one wishes to name it. things have not been well. and so here i am at this club. trying to dance but i cant. i lok at others move their bodies and everything is SEX. ussually i know this, but this night something in the air made it so much more relevant and fresh in a sickening and quite disturbing fashion. so im dancing. or trying to.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> then i think of the way my friend drew dances to house. Its hilarious and terriffic because you know the boy has no concern whatsoever for what others may thing of the manner in which he moves. i.ve always admired that loads and loads in people. Yet, everyone is so self-conscious at clubs. well, of course it is a meat market. they butcher ya man. and i was in tune with everything much more since i hadnt had some vodka for dinner that night.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> so i try to move. so techno/house beats come on and i start to sweat. i am hating every moment of it. thinking everything with my him has been a futile domestic thing. something you wrap in foil and hand out at dinner parties. so false. very not the manner i pictured the entire thing. see, he wants me to quit being this guys friend...at one point this guy...hmm, Mr. X i guess liked me...(it was a drunken stupor of course) and I the same....for a week or something. So there is that \"threatening\" element. But I dont like X anymore. He\'s a mean boy. But quite eccentric and fun once in while...quite in the way a loud shrieky laugh seems to be.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i cant just not be friends with this guy. because some other guy tells me. so IF i dont like some chick...that is it. finnito...the final churn in the stomach? the last drink? and then all is gone. So i am being obstinate. so like a child. stomp my foot and not answer phones. Mr. X is not worth it. I know that. But it it something beyond this jeolousy trip now. it is something dark and feathery and you cant grasp it or we all die.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> We finally decide to screw the crappy club and go to a sex shop. my friend had never been to one so angel and i decide to take her. Looking at the bright advertising and plastic in a range of colors and textures is like being in that club. a way to get off. crissakes. i am sick of the whole deal so we go home.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> lying in bed i think i think. he is telling them things about me. and how they think im some sort of gypsy. to be careful...she does not like limitations...she does not do normal things...\r\ndo not love something if your going to keep it from being itself. and dont call be a goddamn gypsy...unless i use the term myself.</P>'),(1373,'<p>I\'m so tired. Another night of washing dishes. Hey--it\'s not as rewarding as you might think. I get to cook tomorrow though, yay! I like to pick the head chef\'s brain all night, asking why we serve this with that, how long to cook pork, what\'s in that sauce etc. I am not going to be a chef but I do want to be able to feed myself and my close ones real food. Good food. I like being a provider. Once school starts, I will be able to quit, and just go to school. That\'ll be the first time it\'s worked out that way ever. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI basically have two choices. 1) Make B\'s and C\'s like I\'ve been doing, and be an English teacher in public school or 2) Make A\'s and be a lawyer. After serious consideration, I feel like I can help people better as a lawyer. i want to help people who have been hurt and can\'t work get the money that they are entitled to from the big insurance companies. I know that sounds like a commercial, but it\'s true. They will get screwed financially, and STILL can\'t work, because big business wants to save another dollar. I worked for a lawyer while I was at FMU and he often reduced or waived his fee to needy people, so that they could have the extra money. I want to be like that.\r\n</p>'),(1374,'<TT>\r\n<P>\r\ntoday has been bad vibe day.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nyou know, i think i have a death wish.&nbsp; it\'s like picking at scars.&nbsp; a coupla of you know puce, right, and one of her friends\' name is anna, and i used to know her via an old bush (band) mailinglist that i\'m sure she vehemently denies ever having a part of.&nbsp; i know her, and her writing is good, but she is very annoyed by me.&nbsp; yet, without fail, once a year i go back to her site and e-mail her a very neutral message with snottish underscore, because she used to treat everyone on that list not in her personal clique as garbage not worth her time.&nbsp; and i always remembered that, and it still knaws at me so much that i have to flip my skirts at her, even if every year i\'m made the fool once again.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ndoes anyone else have tendencies like that?&nbsp; times where you have a compulsion to repeatedly do something even when you know the outcome will be worse for you?\r\n</P>\r\n</TT>'),(1375,'<P> we are okay or so it seems. he swallowed it all. i will not ask him for tastes...just keeping mum about the deal</P>\r\n\r\n<P>maybe it sounds like cheating took place but no. small amounts of trust leaked out of our red balloon. and we were left flat tired.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> my hair has been all chlorine these days. nothing but false blue hope is what i swim in.vulnerable like in fetal.taking and held him close.eyeing out the window for strange cars.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> and i wonder when and where and why. all have been my counterparts and the counterpain swishes either one way or the other. i suppose maybe two artists would drive eachother to the death. and i cannot do that. leaving clothes unwashed for months and hair in knots. because the loving gets too angry.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> and he is good for me. i say that and know by how he says things.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> for a few we laid.mouths resting firm.i almost tip over. so i cannot breathe. when you give me the reasons i think of how underneath the water\'s surface the light still reaches and looks like gold. i think of pale blues and how we once danced to Joy Division.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> a greater act. something you put down so we can make our next move. i ate all the pawns instead...if it had been up to me nothing would be resolved.</P>'),(1376,'<p>at the top of the peak above <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.ventephoto.com/popclif.jpg\">Tennesse Valley</a> Beach, harley remarked that the landscape was \"hard\". I knew what she meant. I said, \"yes, it is a brutal place.\" the sand was brown, the drops from cliff to sea were deadly. even hiking on the side of this steep hill could break my ass potentially. in Northern California there are always clouds waiting to disrupt your sense of youthful continuity. everything glowed with a rough and wild demeaner. it would not forgive us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>hey mister wont you come for me<br>\r\nim a psychosomatic sister runnin around without a leash</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We discussed hillary. the polaroids of her on <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.zlilo.com/~sammich/4-3/pages/amys_wall.html\">my wall</a>, with angular bangs, tattoos, and heavy eye-liner. Harley had never known her that way. I tried to assure her that the best hillary I knew had a \"hard\" look. harley had only known her as vaguely jocky and preppy, so-and-so\'s girlfriend, Frau-like, civilized and scrubbed. I liked her better the other way. brutal. delicate and cruel.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I collected stones, composing love letters in my head. </p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>you can take me home but i will never be your girl</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>a swarm of teenagers set up camp to the front-right of us. Some of them began fashioning spears out of long sticks. I chanted:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>kill the pig<br>\r\nspill her blood<br>\r\nbash her in<br>\r\nslit her throat</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sam riffed on the Lord of the Flies theme. When <a href=\"http://www.saturn5.com/~ben/\">benadam</a> began building stone-garden installations, Sam grumbled \"goddamn those Wiccans.\" I cried, \"kill the pig! spill her blood!\" as a testament to the possible bloodshed that could erupt as a result of all this primitive horseplay. we were on a beach where a short tv pilot about horny teens washed up on a deserted island could have been filmed. Sam remarked, \"we can break his glasses.\" not until we make a fire. son of man indeed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>harley talked of war incantations. she gets off on war. I remembered that her mother came from Tennessee; she was named after a family farm in the south that was named after an air base in England. I like events and places that share words but no other origin. \"Tennessee\" Valley Farm Harley Home. Harley had a lot to say about the \"really bloody battles\" that had erupted near her family farm in Tennessee; I couldnt help but concentrate on the way she relished saying \"REALLY BLOODY BATTLES\". she assured me, \"no, see, there are people there that shoot other people and themselves. it\'s like a Faulker novel.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>bmg had asked me the night before to help him with a paper he was writing about \"<a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.online-library.org/fictions/emily.html\">A Rose for Emily</a>\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>egglit (12:14 AM):	i love that story<br>\r\negglit (12:18 AM):	a rose for emily is about southern nostalgia<br>\r\niamtheBMG (12:18 AM):	failure to adapt to change<br>\r\niamtheBMG (12:18 AM):	and necrophelia<br>\r\niamtheBMG (12:18 AM):	yumm<br>\r\negglit (12:18 AM):	youve got the whole thing figured out</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the brutality of the stoney shore on my ticklish <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://winshop.com.au/annew/Pisces.html\">piscean</a> feet became the brutality of shade on our warmed shoulders. harley hates the clouds. she panicks quickly. I tried to summon her ability to \"cling to nothing\". harley\'s acceptance of ephemeral phenomena is slight. she wants all events and people under as much control as possible. I want to master myself. love gets in the way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hillary writes the best love poems. I memorized one, and it sits on an index card shrine on my wall:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>little puppy kat<br>\r\ngoes pitter-pat<br>\r\non my stomach at that<br>\r\nand on my back<br>\r\nasking me if i want a towel<br>\r\ntonight i own red pearls<br>\r\nand your growl,<br>\r\neven if it\'s only pretend.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i came home and listened to whitechocolatespacegg and talked to jay about the brutal transcience of love. liz phair songs are such our auto-fuckin-biographies it\'s laughable sometimes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>thats the whole thing about it<br>\r\n<a target=\"window\" href=\"http://imv.au.dk/publikationer/pov/Issue_04/section_1/artc5A.html#RTFToC3\">im so in love today</a></i></p>'),(1377,'<br>\r\nTo wake,<br>\r\nto transduce rays of light into music.<br>\r\nPhotons sing out, \"a new month\"<br>\r\na new day<br>\r\na year, passing so quickly.<br>\r\nWind blows the sky clear, a giggle of<br>\r\nautumn, tempting<br>\r\nhumid faces with tepid winds,<br>\r\nand I long for September.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n'),(1378,'<P>journal entry from last year:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>July 20, 2000</P>\r\n\r\n<P><i>I crave violence. I want to tear something, anything, to shreds. I want to squeeze until I feel the pulp of it between my fingers. It. Anything. Scream until my lungs ache and my head hurts. I want to kick the shit out of something and then fuck it to death. I want death and lust and blood. I want to cry and scream and break my own teeth out of my fucking head. I want to rip my own hair out by the roots and fling it at the wall. I want this feeling to fucking go away. I hate it and it\'s all that I\'ve felt for the past four days. I hate it. I hate him. Why is he such an ass? Why doesn\'t he give a shit about me, or them? Or her. Why did he pass down this feeling to me? This feeling that I\'m walking on the precipice. About to go over the edge. JUST levelheaded enough, and armed with the right amount of knowledge, to keep myself from falling accidentally. If I fall, it will be with full knowledge of what I\'ve done and then I\'m as bad as he is. Why did he give me the craving? The urge to kick the shit out of something and then the urge to fuck it to death. I fucking hate him. If I know how to hate, I should know how to love. But I seriously question that ability within myself. I don\'t know that I\'ve ever felt true love. That\'s a lie. I have. It\'s just always always hurt. I feel like I\'m losing my mind. What have I done, in choosing to go back? Am I going to self-destruct or am I going to handle it better once I\'ve gotten there? If this feeling, right now, doesn\'t go or gets worse once I get there, I am fucked big time. I am so scared. I am scared of what is going to happen. I am scared of what might happen to me. I am scared of what will happen to them. I am scared. Blunt. SCARED. I wish he would just fucking die already. If he\'s bent on it, please do it quickly. Save us the agony of watching you choose to die. That\'s why everyone is afraid. They don\'t want the pain of having to watch my father choose to kill himself. I have chosen to watch. With my Mom. Perhaps we are the only strong ones. Perhaps they are the weak ones. Perhaps I am living my life to the fullest; right here, right now, by making the decision to watch and maybe even help a little (nothing so overt as to be called \"helping\"), but maybe my life will be more the richer for being right there. Like choosing to watch your favorite pet be put to sleep. You can\'t just abandon the one that you love in the last moments of life. No matter how much of an asshole the pet may be, you have to be true to yourself. You\'d never forgive yourself if you didn\'t go back. You will do the right thing. You generally do. </P>\r\n<P>The craving to destroy is fading. Is it because of the release in writing, or because of the Valerian Root that I just took with my wine? Does it matter, as long as it\'s fading? Probably not.  </P></i>\r\n\r\n<P>I can\'t believe that this was only one year ago. So much has changed in such a seemingly short period of time. I re-read this and gasp; I feel the physical place where I was when I wrote it, but it seems like a hundred million miles ago. A hundred million miles and 25 good choices. </P>\r\n\r\n'),(1379,'<p>\"I don\'t know what I desire<br>\r\n  When Summer nights are dark and still,<br>\r\n  When the wind\'s many voiced quire\"<br>\r\n  Sleeps among the muffled branches.<br>\r\n  I long and know not what I will:<br>\r\n  And not a sound of life or laughter stances<br>\r\n  Time\'s black and silent flow.<br>\r\n  I do not know what I desire,<br>\r\n  I do not know.\"<br>\r\n  - Aldous Huxley<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  When she used to get drunk, I had her all to myself. To listen and make sing. \r\n  To stumble in dark rooms. To giggle and admire. She reminds me of summer <a href=\"http://books.guardian.co.uk/firstchapters\">reading</a>, \r\n  of <a href=\"http://www.interiority.net/starcandy/flow.html\">sci-fi</a> and of \r\n  long telephone calls. I\'m not <a href=\"http://www.muse-base.com/mb/\">falling \r\n  down</a> any more. She is proud of that. Summer smells of confusion around here. \r\n  That\'s <a href=\"http://members.tripod.com/RoadSide6/season.htm\">life</a> a month \r\n  before <a href=\"http://public.logica.com/%7Eperkinsa/hunter-mott/lyrics/atyd.html#AllTheYoungDudes\">twenty \r\n  five</a>. </p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1379_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'>'),(1380,'Y\'all:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhich is better - the <b>Chevy El Camino</b> or the <Ford Ranchero</b>?<br><br>\r\njust a thought.'),(1381,'<p>\r\ni am 21. soon i will be 22. i look at that and think that the number looks so small. if i live to be 90, i am just getting started. hell, if i live to 70, i still have 50 years to go. but i am feeling old. the feeling that time is racing by and i am running with it, sprinting with it, but can\'t slow to a walk. the days and weeks wisp away in front of me until months have passed leaving me confused if i am even in the right month. this movement, this pace of time is leaving me with a crisis about my life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe crisis basically settles around the fact that i don\'t want to look back on my life and feel like it was just a waste of time. if i lie on my death bed and can\'t look back at accomplishments i have made, fun i have had, i am going to be one pissed individual.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso this leaves me looking at myself. looking at the fact that i work all day. then go home and read books to pack my brain with things i want to know and things i think i need to know. this cycle continues day after day. while my roommates and friends drink and pop pills staying out at bars all night long, i am home reading, or sleeping.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto make myself feel better i tell myself that i will be more prepared to take care of myself, getting money together for a house and such. this i guess is true.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit is also true that if i get hit by a semi-truck on the way home from work tonight and die, i am not more prepared for anything.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat current standings i am very confused about life and where i stand with the world, the universe, and my soul.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\'s been along time since i have had one of these disorentations with the reality of my life. i need orientaion. i need travel. i need music. i need my perception to have altercations with itself. lots of things to need. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni thought trouble with life going by too fast came with 40. if it\'s like this now, i might end up crazy when 40 finds me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the upside of this depressing, self-pity diatribe, love does make things more interesting. \r\n</p>'),(1382,'<P>i am glistening this mornang.  early dew settled on my bed and i woke\r\nat three.  static electricity from my brain is making my 1/3 inch\r\nhairs stand.  my phone is blinking at me.  i don\'t know the access\r\ncode.  don\'t care.  i made coffix.  i missed my surfing event yesterday\r\nmorn because i could not wake up.  this morning as if a joke i woke\r\nup, yeah, early.  i ate a bowl of frosted mini wheatblix.  they are in\r\na large bag, generic because i am living that way.\r\n</p><P>\r\ndeath metal is happening to me right now.  i am listening to isis and\r\ngoing through a moment right now similar to the one yesterday in the\r\nshower when i realized i had slept through five thirty and missed my\r\nsurfing engagement.  only this time i am listening to heavy metal and\r\nrealizing slowly that i missed the isis concert in san francisco that\r\ni had writ on smtzs wall calendar.  bummer.  that sucks dix.\r\n</p><P>\r\nwhat have i been reading lately?  i keep picking up these art boox\r\nthat talk about hot rods and how they are everyman\'s art form.  it is\r\nembarrassing how much i want a motorcycle.  i am trying to be level\r\nheaded and responsible.  but i want to be and artrix with a muscle\r\nbike that goes around down punching it everywhere and being really\r\nloud making passerbys askew their faces and scratch their heads and\r\nsay aloud, \"huh, wow!\" like in that book.  to say, \"the incredible\r\ndecibel of that machine has made me reconsider what life means and\r\nwhat noise is.\"  keep away from me materialism books.  i am not buying\r\nanything today!  i am only reading.\r\n</p><P>\r\ntime to think about working.  i would rather stroll in the pines.  i\r\nwould rather fall asleep and wake up in the biggity biggity o bed.\r\nsnurzle and huggle, not work.  but i must work and get paid.  it is on\r\nmy list of things to do.</p>'),(1383,'<p>\r\nforlorn we sit and watch\r\nthe concrete go by, a\r\nshadowplay that in no\r\nuncertain terms\r\nwill end one day\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme? well, you then\r\nthere, mounted ready\r\nand willing. when left\r\nwith nothing but speed\r\nand distance we\r\nfragment and lead\r\nourselves astray.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhot blistery wind and\r\na soul chilled by yearning\r\ncombine to  fuel the fires\r\nof no regret as i climb\r\nhomesick and angelic\r\nracing sunsets on moonbeams.\r\n<p/>\r\n<p>\r\nlosing what\'s found by the lost.\r\n</p>'),(1384,'<P> we are making an alter to menstruation. \r\nmy friend and i - i think and she thinks it deserves a high regard amidst all other forms of worship and idolatry. it is a saint .</p>\r\n\r\n<P> it is to help unwanted preggers leave and go\r\nand never come back. our small token of affectionate gratitude will be conveyed by our humble creation of this altar.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> \"What for the nipples?\" i ask...regarding our lifesized trace of a female bod....</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Angel thinks ob tampons over gents condoms would do just fine.for the nipple that is. i agree. mesntruation is a goddamn important occurance. a celebration it should seem if you are among those experiencing some form of _beyond_ genital stimulation.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> <I> anti-tall</i> as well. see we look at it in this way when it comes to steering our focus at the ideas of height. for chrissakes, it helps things if you are a woman.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> \"short\" women are considered <B> cute</b> whereas tall not. i dont know, they have longer legs okay. and angel and i , having both occupied ourselves in the business of running, tend to agree that LOnger LEgs help indeed. its all about the five foot 4 and the five foot 3. indeed. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> so next to a halo of unwrapped yellow regular tampons will be a face. representative of all the female <I>sistahs</i> out there who have had no where to turn to, once the 72 hour time frame collapsed upon their fertil uteruses. time for a change ...a new era.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>fuck the white man.</p>\r\n<P> oops. i meant fuck the man.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> since i am being <I> fucked</i> by one as well as fucking back, i suppose i have earned enough moan points to deserve me the right to say that. so once again...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> fuck the white man.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> for you and for me are these <B> fucks</b>just mentioned i tell angel. yeah...we will have a saint, a goddess, our queen that is not one of our gay friends...but a queen who doesnt dance for she cringes in a shared pain of much needed uterine contractions...to push...PUSH...shove em out...go RAId...wait, nevermind. Yeah.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> so help us. Women of menstruating age and men who act with menstruating talent to bring our ovarian talisman to life...a name...mayhaps she be nameless... You know a communal type o\' thing. A\'la MARX.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Angel: arms crossed. her man ..oh, i mean her \"man\" ...oh, i meant her \"dick\"...should bow down to our mistress of the BLOOD and help us cringe towards(* by cringe i mean in a good way) a day when the underwear are bloody, when the sheet is wet in a warm non\'semeny, non\'sex juice type o\' way...a day when we feel that buttermilk of crimson begin its churning and we KNOW...it will be a goddamn <B><I> RED</b></i> tide. and for chrissakes we dont even care if its an unplanned unprepared type of day...LET IT RUN...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> LET IT RUN DOWN our legs.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> and i will not wipe away...</p>\r\n<P> i will wear my red badge of courage with Goddamn fallopian courage.</p>'),(1385,'<font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">yesterday i had my livescan. modern-day\r\nfingerprinting.. or finger scanning. i hope they don\'t find out i\'m a \"damned\r\na-rab terrorist\". the day before a friend spooked me by asking if i would\r\nhave to do a urine analysis. i got worried that they\'d find out about the\r\ndrug trafficking i do to support my terrorist activities...</font>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">people always scoff and call me paranoid\r\nwhen i tell them that the c.i.a is onto me. it\'s no joke.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when the world trade center in new\r\nyork was bombed, my parent\'s phone went dead. well first we received a\r\nphone call asking if this was the toulan residence, when i answered in\r\nthe affirmative the line went dead. in a building of 146 apartments the\r\nphone company could not tell us why our line was suddenly not working.\r\nalso my uncle\'s line was cut.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">right after the oklahoma city bombing,\r\nthere was speculation that it was done by some arabs. our phone went dead.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">and when the u.s. embassies in somalia\r\nwere bombed our phone went dead.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">is this a coincidence? no. it can\'t\r\nbe. we\'re a-rab terrorists and they are on to us.</font>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">now my parents live in cairo, egypt.\r\nmy mother is convinced the c.i.a. has moved into their building. a bunch\r\nof americans sporting american diplomatic licenses (only cars tied to embassies\r\ncan have diplomatic license plates in egypt) have taken up residence in\r\nthe building. they have caged in their balcony , my father says that this\r\nis to prevent people from scaling the building and breaking in that way.\r\nwhat gets my mother is that they generate about 5-8 bags full of shredded\r\npaper a week. she says that it\'s some nefarious american agency keeping\r\ntabs.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">am i being paranoid? or am i being\r\nlied to?</font>\r\n'),(1386,'<p>\r\n<font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">yesterday i had my livescan. modern-day\r\nfingerprinting.. or finger scanning. i hope they don\'t find out i\'m a \"damned\r\na-rab terrorist\". the day before a friend spooked me by asking if i would\r\nhave to do a urine analysis. i got worried that they\'d find out about the\r\ndrug trafficking i do to support my terrorist activities...</font>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">people always scoff and call me paranoid\r\nwhen i tell them that the c.i.a is onto me. it\'s no joke.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when the world trade center in new\r\nyork was bombed, my parent\'s phone went dead. well first we received a\r\nphone call asking if this was the toulan residence, when i answered in\r\nthe affirmative the line went dead. in a building of 146 apartments the\r\nphone company could not tell us why our line was suddenly not working.\r\nalso my uncle\'s line was cut.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">right after the oklahoma city bombing,\r\nthere was speculation that it was done by some arabs. our phone went dead.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">and when the u.s. embassies in somalia\r\nwere bombed our phone went dead.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">is this a coincidence? no. it can\'t\r\nbe. we\'re a-rab terrorists and they are on to us.</font>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">now my parents live in cairo, egypt.\r\nmy mother is convinced the c.i.a. has moved into their building. a bunch\r\nof americans sporting american diplomatic licenses (only cars tied to embassies\r\ncan have diplomatic license plates in egypt) have taken up residence in\r\nthe building. they have caged in their balcony , my father says that this\r\nis to prevent people from scaling the building and breaking in that way.\r\nwhat gets my mother is that they generate about 5-8 bags full of shredded\r\npaper a week. she says that it\'s some nefarious american agency keeping\r\ntabs.</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">am i being paranoid? or am i being\r\nlied to?</font>\r\n'),(1387,'<p>when your former lover\r\n<br>finds another, who then finds another\r\n<br>who happens to be  your friends ex-lover\'s ex-lover\r\n<br>who just happens to live with your former\r\n<br>and  yet you still find yourself with no other</p>\r\n<p>you then meet another<br>\r\n who just happens to carry the name of another\r\n<br>lover\r\n<br>who just happens to be with your former friend\'s lover\r\n<br>and then your brother is married to someone elses other\r\n<br>of course your mother, married to another\r\n<br>your father living with his lover\r\n<br>still you find  yourself with out a lover\r\n<br>then you find out your first lover, has another,<br>\r\nbut still wanting to be your significant other\r\n</p>\r\n<p>friends, i tell you this must end\r\n<br>maybe if i move i will find another\r\n<br>who will be my lover\r\n<br>and look for none other.</p> \r\n'),(1388,'In a relationship, theres a person who does all the \"yes-ing\" and a person who does all the \"no-ing\".  A Yes Man and a No Man.  This is how the gap broadens as neither can say the other\'s consequent answer.  Do not omit an answer, especially a sequestered Cancer\'s.<br><br>\r\nA film advancer, transistor, receptor.  Persuades in a rampant climbing throng of wise emperors, vectors and lipids.  Voices of Olympus sing a \"D\" for diesel, forming a head for the dinosaur from whence this hydrocarbon was birthed.<br><br>\r\nRepeat after me, \"send a S.A.S.E.\" to McPhee for release to the folks with the keys to release all the peace to Police.<br><br>\r\nDraw this answer out on yourself.  Etch this into flesh, retch this human bless.  Fetch it from your wrists and drizzle it, crime running rampant on a slicing fingertip.  Wipe my mantel with a crude rip of a gangly fandango-ly strip of the tip of my lip.  And I\'m the link that holds this yes-man/no-ma\'am chain together, yessiree, and without me, no one could cut, and the birds could nest in the go-arounds in the cog-wheels.  Microcomputers switched on for me micromelodies, in the breeze. <br><br>\r\nOrion said \"Wish, or I\'ll miss this brief skiff, so amiss with the kiss of your wish!\".  So, I wished for a moist bowel. Then he kissed my Miss and shouted \"Ole!\", and then \"Who goes there?!\".'),(1389,'<P> I am in a cyber cafe. Have been playing quake for more than 5 hours and am now taking a break. There are 20 consoles and each is occupied by a boy ranging in age from 13-21. So, that make me the only female here. I dont ussually play \"games\"...don\'t consier myself a \"gamer\" since i only do it in the company of friends and whatnot.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i wonder why there arent more girls in this highly male subcurrent. Yeah, alot of my friends \"used\" to play when they were younger but now dont. I know ...I know. There is a stigma against the guys that do. Stereotypically, its the male sitting at home, network set up, playing against friends, that will do this for hours and hours...breaks for urination only when entirely necessary...yeah.okay.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> My roomies ex was/is a gamer. she hated it. Well, i dont know. personally, i hate counterstrike. Ill play it sometimes but rarely. Its all about the Quake series. And i also hate console games...I think thats what you call them...nintento-like stuff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> so why dont more girls play? I dont know. Maybe they have better things in their hands than a mouse and some keys.Its nice once in a while to take a day aside and just laze around and play, cuss, play...win..etc. Then again in my personal opinion you have to balance it out with some damn good pizza, and/or skinny dipping in the Pacific or Med. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> and thats the only way ill do it.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1390,'<p>\r\nyo italy is wack. its so wack. i love it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe situation there is great for morale. its getting to the point now that information is spreading to the masses even if only in limited   \r\nforms. there is a lot of press now regarding the different groups. since seattle there has been to a certain degree an understanding by the media\r\nthat there are a multiple number of interests being  represented in the democratic protests happening at for example seattle, quebec city, and genoa. world leaders are being pressed more and more for their views on the demonstrations and the media is investigating with zeal at the degree\'s of force being used to \"quell\" the activities of those who\r\nsupport violence as well as those who stand pacifist but vigilant in their efforts to resist \"globalization\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEven if the media still portrays those in the cause as violent \"anarchists\", the public still see\'s how the establishment reacts and   \r\ntreats them. a media broadcast that starts off with footage and commentary on the civil unrest in israel then see\'s the civil unrest closer to home\r\nin seattle, quebec city, or genoa. they understand what watercanons and rubber bullets do and if they dont see a connection to the violence, then\r\nwe must work harder to make them understand.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat people tend to do a lot of the time is look at something happening in a less prosperous part of the world as being brutal and undemocratic.\r\ninfact, politicians love to use the various forms of the word democratic all the time, yet when people organize and demonstrate they use the same \r\nmethods used abroad and claim they are upholding democracy and public safety.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe claim to live in the most open and democratic systems being in apart of the \"west\" and \"western civilization\", yet we read about things being\r\npassed and agreements being signed without having the openness and democratic rights to be apart of those negotiations. it is often argued\r\nthat when one is elected to office it is the public who elected to choose that person as their voice. this is not true. we elect public officials to\r\nmaintain and \"run\" their respective constituency and to make information available to the public regarding whats going on. elected officials\r\nmaintain a working relationship with the public, and [should] react to public opinion.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is perhaps an understanding that no nation anywhere could possibly be one hundred percent democratic, yet when i hear about the ceo of a\r\ncorporation sitting at a table of elected officials regarding something that has everything to do with how me and coming generations are going to\r\nbe effected you\'d hope the voices of social groups, unions, and interest , and interest groups would be represented too.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto maintain the status quo democracy what was invision by the welfare state model, we muse make the voices of a wide spectrum heard and not just\r\nthose who represent a profit margin or \"opening\" markets.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npeople should take note, unite, and fight for what they want. you cannot hold the masses back with barbed wire, tear gas and water cannons. the\r\nestablishment should loosen the reigns and let some of the opinons being voiced on the street inside and at the negotiation tables, because if\r\nthey dont the violence will escalate and a new breed of former raver/goth/artist/hippy/yuppy/teckie organize the rest of the world will\r\nsee how \"wired\" and \"connected\" the establishment\r\nwishes we werent.</p>'),(1391,'<p>It all started at the grocery store. There was a very pretty girl working the cash register; she had long fingernails, but not super long, just sexy long. And long hair, blonde, a really light shade, almost platinum. Her lips were painted a purplish color, one of those new metallic shades.  Joshua thought that she was a really good-looking checkout girl.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAs the line in front of him slowly shortened, he began to feel a little nervous. It wasnâ€™t the first time. When confronted with an attractive member of the opposite sex, Joshua usually got a little nervous, especially in public, where his fumbling was on display. He studied the various tabloid magazines and newspapers arranged to catch his eye, with headlines like Congressman Sex Scandal with Alien Lover and Grandma Has Triplets at 80. He gave the contents of his shopping cart a quick once-over, calculating the cost of everything once again, making sure he had enough to cover everything. \r\n</p><p>\r\nLook her in the eye, he told himself as he placed his purchases on the countertop. He made eye contact, and said, â€œHello.â€</p><p>\r\n	<p>â€œHow are you?â€ she said, with a smile.</p>\r\n<p>Josh just looked at her, and tried to smile. Why didnâ€™t you answer her, asshole? She finished ringing up his groceries, and he fumbled the money out of his pocket and into her hand, but not without dropping it on the floor first. He looked up at her face again, relieved to see that she was smiling; he smiled back, but inside he was a whirlwind of emotion, shouting voices, discomfort. He felt like at any moment his body might simply stop working. He was acutely aware of everything, every signal his brain sent to his body, like he was glowing, like everyoneâ€™s eyes were focused on him, during his brief moment in the checkout spotlight. He picked up his bags, mumbled a thank you, and walked out of the store. \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe loaded his groceries into the trunk, and got behind the wheel. It was late. Joshua liked to buy groceries at night, when he had nothing else to do, and didnâ€™t have to hurry. He put the Beatlesâ€™ Revolver into his cheap CD player, and sat for a minute, singing along with John and Paul about the Taxman, yeah-ah the taxman, thinking about his abysmal performance in the checkout line. No one else seemed to have such a hard time dealing with little things like this; maybe it was the pot. But no, he didnâ€™t smoke that often, it wasnâ€™t such a big deal. He lit a cigarette with almost unconcious disgust, almost but not quite, still smoking, and drove out of the parking lot and onto State Street, towards home. \r\n</p><p>\r\nInstead of turning right on Addison, as he usually did, he made a left and drove slowly through the old downtown area. He liked being out on warm nights like tonight, liked driving between the old buildings, with the windows down, imagining that his was in a big city, doing important things. The night air was cool, which was especially refreshing since the temperature during the day had been in the 90â€™s the past few days. He drove with one arm hanging out of the window, letting it flap against the cool side of the car, feeling free.</p>\r\n'),(1392,'<p>it\'s all about the good,<br>\r\nthe bad,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...the ugly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am not totally human.<br>\r\na hotbox<br>\r\n induced vision.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just an embryo<br>\r\nof other peoples dreams.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>pushing buttons. walking the edge.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>everyday is like eating cotton candy<br>\r\nsucking on those red hots.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...damn that candy,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>always melting before im ready for it.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1393,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Danger Slut owned my intimacy. \r\n  I was endlessly annoyed when she <a href=\"http://lenny.dyadel.net/card.htm#deuce\" target=\"_blank\">left \r\n  me</a>.<br>\r\n  I don\'t agree with any of the garbage people say.<br>\r\n  - It\'s for the better.<br>\r\n  - Life goes on.<br>\r\n  Blah. Bah. Go screw a donkey with a nickle you wankers.<br>\r\n  She has forced me to go out and about, searching for that strange little girl.<br>\r\n  Chasing skirt. I am a <a href=\"http://www.fashion.tripnet.se/models.htm\" target=\"_blank\">human \r\n  boy</a> after all.<br>\r\n  I will recover from the shadow of her unintended curves. Don\'t call me crazy \r\n  for loving.</font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.animeondvd.com/images/contests/wolfwood.jpg\"> </p>'),(1394,'<P>goat (chi chi) drove egg and moi to hike in the wildwoods.  i\'m speeding through the details.  it was a usual day in bay area driving, though not too crowded.  we got to the muir woods parking lot and tried to park and gave up.  the minivan we were waiting on was too painfully family like with its mother holding on kid off the ground by an arm like a chicken by the leg or a lamb shank.  and changing dipers and spanking with the other hand.  the minivan for five minutes.  waiting.  dad was in the car already.  kids were hopping over seats.  mom was wiping asses in the back.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ngoat rolled on and we parked somewhere down the road where the getting was good and took the first trail out of the madness made by millions of tourists a year.  hi.  and we walked toward the muir beach area.  \r\n</P><P>\r\non the walk we speculated about post population bell shattering apocalyptic after math where we were walking down the same trail.  something like that.  egg kept ruining the fantasy with her, \"yeah running around in rags trying to start fires with sticks and drinking poisoned water.\"  i thought it would be fun anyway.  then we turned to settling planets and egg was, \"i\'m not having kids ever,\" while goat pointed out that, \"in order to be settling a planet you have to have kids.\"  and i threw in, \"you want to reinvent culture dont you\", yes, \"well you have to have kids to have culture.\"  but egg won in the end saying that as artist in residence of planet Planet, she would be the asexual wise person.  also that she would service the entire settlement\'s men.  at this point i added that it would probably be \'poly\' and servicing of men tired of their broken down wives from all the years of spitting out babies would not be necessesary.\r\n</P><P>\r\nit was a nice walk.  we found the beach.  we never found the woods though.  we went in every direction but the muir woods, looking for the muir woods.  eventually i spotted the muir woods from a hilltop while checking the echo times on \"EGG COME BACK THE WOODS ARE OVER THERE....egg come back the woods are over...come back the woods are over ...\" and we went back to the car and drove to the parking lot which by now was deserted and got out so that i could exclaim, \"what a cool thing,\" althought hugging of the trees or in any way \'violating the bark\' was strictly prohibitted and then we could leave for oakland and jay and the new roomate\'s two oven roasted chickens.\r\n</P><P>\r\nright this moment, i am waiting on dakari (sp.?) and egg to get back with the beer.  what i really want is a beer.  i\'m just counting minutes.</P>'),(1395,'<br>\r\nAll we see and our reflection, is from the past.<br>Past.  Measured in metrics, past. Present<br>Not a gift but a future.  Future perfect,<br>of things dreamed of.<br>Mechanical strokes keyed out, beats, nurturing.<br>Calling, call out.  Shout.  A breath oozing across a<br> rhythmic stretch of time.<br>Hit, slide, drift.  A syncopated bliss.<br> A sparkling, fading notion.  A click of personality.<br>A whistle from the past, sent to call,<br> to kick in, to prop up.<br>To change things... repetition, changing repetitions.<br>A fade, across thought assumptions, across ears, sliding.<br>A siren, calling through a jungle, warning, eliminating, hoping.<br>A siren.  A mechanical scream.  A hope.<br>A hope of machines.<br>A dream of technology.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1395_1.jpg\' width=\'296\' height=\'444\'>'),(1396,'<PRE>\r\nI just spent the last 30 minutes trying to work out a forward/preface/explanation as to what I am, or at least what I thought I was, hopeing to achieve with this post.  A pouring forth, maybe, and explanation of why I\'ve never kept a diary or journal or weblog or whatever this is, save for a few pseudonymous rabble-rouser posts. \r\n\r\n  Got a good 60 lines written, realized it was garbage, and nuked the whole thing.  I\'ve spent the last two hours (and, all told, the better part of the last two weeks) unable to sleep, somewhat tormented by the aimlessness (hopelessness?) of my current situation, and the violent change I\'m experiencing, within and without, as a result of it. \r\n\r\n  I haven\'t had more than a week off of work/school/etc since I was 15 (I\'ve _never_ taken a vacation), and ten years of postponed business (like, for instance, adolescence) has just caught up with me, and shook some stuff loose. So I\'m writing. Not gonna start from the beginning, not even gonna try and write anything significant. Just gonna commit to writing daily. \r\n\r\nI think.\r\n\r\nI\'ve been unemployed for just over a month now, and so far i haven\'t succumbed to the chronically-unemployed lifestyle. I have, so far: gotten dressed every day, left the house at least once each day, maintained a close shave (which I did not bother to do while employed), and my room is cleaner than it has been, I would say, in my entire life. \r\n\r\n  These may seem like small things, but just the same, I am sure there are plenty of you readers, currently unemployed, and thoroughly embarassed by this list. I haven\'t even spent so much as an afteroon playing videogames, much less spent a week in my drawers playing UT (y\'all know who you are). \r\n\r\nBut I digress; the point of the preceding paragraphs was to add weight to the idea of just how much time I\'ve had for introspection.. I\'m damned introverted as it is, but to have a whole month with no other obligations save to contemplate my navel..   well, here we are. \r\n\r\nAnyway, this is just the preface, the real entries will follow.'),(1397,'<pre>\r\n\r\nactually, sorry to be a tease, but I decided I\'m gonna go with a handwritten journal after all\r\n\r\ninsomnia fuels my madness\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(1398,'<p>Watch and smile, so reasonable, so complacent, aren\'t we wonderful, we\'ve come so far, isn\'t it all just so wonderful, aren\'t we so civilized, yes, let\'s celebrate, yes, I raise my hand, a toast to that impotent abortion we all know and love, yes that infinite vacuum of mediocrity and repetition, friends -- the human race. Do stand up. Take a bow. Drink of my vomit. Please. I <i>insist.</i></p> \r\n<p>Humanity left to do it\'s math homework 400 years ago and it hasn\'t really snapped out of it yet. The science fair is over, fucks. There\'s more to life than this. It\'s not like I think we were screwed from the beginning, we\'ve just either lost or have become detached from anything that would make me remotely proud to be a human being. We\'ve been so beautiful. The clamor and chaos of the city-state and the commune, the religious ecstasy of the orgy, the crude spirituality of cannibalism, the pinch of hunger, an awareness of survival, a close and sometimes miserable attachment to life itself, I just want something with a pulse.</p>\r\n<p>Sometimes I wish the Golden Horde hadn\'t turned back. If only they could have swept across Europe and euthanized this shit-stain of a civilization before it grew up and gave birth to the retarded child that is now Planet Earth. The riders of the Golden Horde were people I would be proud to call human beings -- their purity and wholeness never ceases to amaze me. There is no denying that they were fully aware of their own ugliness; they intentionally scarred their faces, soaked their clothes in horse piss and used rotting meat as a weapon during sieges. They were so consciously and absolutely vile. But there is something totally beautiful about them in how close they were to everything they did. The kind of understanding and intimacy they must have had with terror, pure human terror, was probably amazing in a way that I couldn\'t even begin to understand. As a species we have precious few of those close relationships left.</p>\r\n<p>Instead we have this sterile monster whacking off to its own technological achievements. It\'s impossible (not to mention stupid) to want to go backwards, but how can we value our own lives if we are so rarely put in a position to lose them? Is anyone else made spiritually nauseous by the very idea of an insurance company and what that says about our species? How do we value the lives of others if nothing they think or do, other than consuming, is responsible for their perpetuation? How do we cherish something that we take for granted in the abstract? Shouldn\'t life be earned? Shouldn\'t we be hungry? Shouldn\'t we be beautiful?</p>\r\n<p>There is a way to reconcile our instincts with civilization, I\'m sure, but I don\'t think it will ever happen. Expecting a new political movement from the vacuous depths of the human imagination is hard enough as it is -- but something even more radical than that? A whole new process of social and linguistic experimentation? A new way of being human? Forget about it. So I am lost. There is no beautiful language. There is no shining kingdom. The wrongness of everything we\'ve become is so overwhelming sometimes. But I smile anyway. Because I know someday all of this will end.</p>'),(1399,'<p>Sitting at work.  Slowly nursing bad coffee and muttering maledictions against MySQL.  Hmmm.  Eyes squint and reach coda of disgust about trigger-less databases used to store tree structures.</p>\r\n<p>*slurp*</p>\r\n<p>Oh well, at least I can read webcomix for half an hour to blow off steam and then go look for a health club over lunch.  :-)</p>\r\n<p>*slurp*</p>\r\n<p>Laughing with coworker that we make seven digits a year but only five of them are to the left of the decimal point.  Significant  figures, indeed. (well, not really)  wish I made enought to drop the bomb on my student loans in one swell foop.</p>\r\n<p>*slurp*</p>\r\n<p>hey, new mail!  crap, only spam.</p>\r\n<p>*slurp*</p>\r\n<p>ok, back to work.</p>'),(1400,'<P> swallowing it it feels inside a bit of </p>\r\n<P> something it will not fall down</p>\r\n<P> holds on with two fists, eyes open and well\r\nveined</p>\r\n\r\n<P>  i try to swallow this small petty irritant</p>\r\n\r\n<P> like the dawn it catches you in a hole and </p>\r\n\r\n<P> time passes. weeks.days.hours.melt into the hourglass. chunks of time.chunks of einstein\'s\r\ntheory.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> how can you swallow the green bitter green flavor swallow deep inside the throat without tasting?</p>\r\n\r\n<P> this jeolousy is precocious and\r\n like a child age five...knows, knows, knows. All these things which make our stomachs hurt.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> acid kisses burn.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> deep inside the hollow of your throat i will rest. knees up to my chest. forgetting all the rest. they did not need me. i did not see what is see in your eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> they are skies to me unbridled by the weather.</p>\r\n<P> holding oregon in my pocket. i hold you in the nape of my neck and in the shadow of my hip.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> and we dance until the day is taken once again.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> blue eyes are so painful at times. drowning in seas so familiar. how their gazes sting. brown is comfort, a knowing i sing.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> and your hair. nobody knows its feel as i. the stature of mother and lover falls as one into my lap.i\'ve memorized your face, and the air of your gaze. i know these things. they are like</p>\r\n\r\n<P> alarm clocks and pencils</p>\r\n\r\n<P> lunches and plastic</p>\r\n\r\n<P> penicillin and glasses.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> swallowing it feels like bit by bit...</p>'),(1401,'<br>\r\nThe wind blowing through the bathroom cooled to a chill; made me think of me and you on the roof-top on summer evenings.  Just now, I tried to visualize your name in my mind - then to immediately visualize you each time it popped up - a different picture each time... in each and every ensuing image, you were smiling.<br><br>\r\nMay I ask this of you: appreciate me.  Bliss me as you kiss me.  Envelop me, send me.  Open me.  Hold me.  Revive me.  Ressurrect me.  Shine on me from opals.  Ride me.  Sing to me.  Hide me.  Horrendous me.  Surrender me.<br><br>'),(1402,'<p>i knew i was crazy, but i asked before opening jeff\'s new designy zoom-zoomy magazine before he got home and unwrapped it himself.\r\n</p><p>  \r\nsomething about that fresh magazine goodness that is gone once someone else ruffles through it, you know?\r\n</p><p>\r\nkinda like that special feeling you get when you unwrap your burrito. this is my fucking burrito, and i am the first tongue and teeth you\'ve ever known, baby. mmm virgin tortilla skin. who\'s your daddy?!\r\n</p><p>\r\ntoo much packaging foreplay in this world. why does a magazine need to be shrinkwrapped? is it going to spoil in the noonday sun? \r\n</p><p>\r\noverheard this today, walking down mission street: \"I\'m smoking for two these days.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\ni kinda hope she was kidding, but i have a feeling she wasn\'t. \r\n</p><p>\r\nalso. it\'s amazing how badly one cigarette stub can smell. one! one little measly butt. but put it in an open trash can, and it blooms, a flower of putrification and doom. the smell is somewhere in the highest registers of rot. i can barely smell it, but that certain something in soggy cigarette butts is my kryptonite. \r\n</p><p>\r\nanyway. i think this is cute:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1402_1.jpg\'></p>\r\n\r\n'),(1403,'<PRE>\r\nALL THE NEWS THAT?S FIT TO PRINT.\r\n\r\n\r\nComa Falls Slowly\r\nWorld Sickens\r\nAfrican Babies Die of AIDS Just Like Homos\r\n\r\nDateline: January 8, 2001\r\neven when we don?t see the breathing \r\nsuck and gasp and stop\r\nclock time twists and burns\r\na slow motion cauterization\r\nflopping eyeless as toxic fish\r\n\r\nThere is no womb any longer\r\nJust a hole and a cunt.\r\n\r\nThe stoic unresolve to make it better reigns\r\nNo cautionary tales reside \r\nin the collective memory\r\nFor memory itself has been un-persistent\r\nand the collective turned to \r\nan army of java writers \r\nand garment factory workers\r\nUnited by the corporations we serve\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1405,'<P>\r\n[It seems to me that I can remember Christmas Eves back to the dawn of my life - or at least, to when my brain had developed enough memory cells to store such joyous times.  It seems to me that even after I learned of the Great Christmas Hoax, my memories were only magnified, only more vivid and warm.]\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI once wrote a story, called \'Controlled Bleeding\', in which the narrator\'s name was Eve, and through her voice I spoke of good times and bad, slowly allowing myself to finally capture how I really felt about the people and events I noted.  I found that the strangest things can often shape a life, such as a tattoo, or a haircut, or a word.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI have no issues with my past anymore.  I grew tired of permitting it to consume me and create little pockets of guilt that were carried around with me everywhere.  I think a Past is really only a vehicle to the Present and the Future.  Why should I be made to doubt my choices and be ashamed of my actions?  If, at the time, they felt right, then why should their consequences inhibit me now?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI took a trip to Austin, to reunite with an old semi-friend, only to find I couldn\'t stand what he\'d become.  I took a trip to Austin, to rekindle my motivation and inspiration.  I took a trip to Austin, to earn the right to say I\'d been to Texas, and no, it wasn\'t all cowboys and cacti.  In fact, what I found there was the last thing I expected: Love.\r\n<br><BR>\r\nOf course, Love isn\'t on the menu at Ruta Maya, or Spider House, or Magnolia\'s.  Love isn\'t in the air surrounding 4th Street\'s 24-hour parade of youth and beauty and naivete.  No, Love was wearing long sleeves as I sweated clear through my t-shirt, as we drove and listened and for once, I heard everything - said or unsaid. Love was waiting for me to make the first move, and of course, in my faux oblivion, I neglected to acknowledge its presence.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n[Last Christmas was the first I\'d spent apart from my family.  To this day, I sense their feeling of neglect.  Christmas was the one thing that was always done the same way.  Every year.  Well, that is, until my parents split up.]\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nNow my collective Past resurfaces in the strangest way, as if to say, Take heed, for if you\'re not careful, Love will be thrust into the depths of your past and you will undoubtedly do irreparable damage.  I laugh in my Past\'s face.  Oh no, I say.  Not this time.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nSoon I will roll over, mid-slumber, and feel your skin.  If you stir, you may accept my warmth and move closer.  If your dreams pull you deeper than from where I may retrieve you, I will rejoice in your security.  I laugh in my Past\'s face.  And what about this one?  I\'ll ask.  But what about this happiness?  Was this one ever figured in?  What about this?  It is unexpected, certainly.  But what about if I screw up?  What if...?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOh no, I say.  Not this time.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n[My father stood at the bottom of the stairway, while my sister and brother and I waited impatiently at the top.  \"Just a sec,\" he\'d say, as he tried desperately to get the video camera to work.  \"Just a sec, kids.\"  Finally, the churning of the reel.  The sound that reminds me of pink pajamas, happy times, chocolate Santas and the most pairs of socks I\'d ever seen.]</P>');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (1404,'<p>why do cats run up to you when you sit on the toilet? I had a cat in brooklyn who was prolifically vocal. ooh mang she was a talker. id try to take a dump and she\'d trot up and mew, slowly and sweetly. I would respond with a meow. it would go back and forth until she pounced me. the conversation would be subtitled like this:</p>\r\n<p><b>cat</b>: can i come up there?<br>\r\n<b>woman</b>:what would you like babycat?<br>\r\n<b>cat</b>: I want to come and sit on you.</br>\r\n<b>woman</b>:would you like to hop on me and and twist in two circles luxuriously on my lap and then plop down on your side with your paws limply hanging over me?</br>\r\n<b>cat</b>: yes, that sounds good can I?</br>\r\n<b>woman</b>:ok. [hop. plop.]</p>\r\n<p>love with fernando is not as tender. our subtitles would look more like this:<br><br>\r\n<b>f</b>: feed me again you negligent, inconsistent bitch!<br>\r\n<b>e</b> : yah right live off your fat you slovenly testacle-free malcontent.<br>\r\n<b>f</b>: if yr gonna be home all day yr gonna hear my testimony!<br>\r\n<b>e</b> : whatever, PUSSY.<br>\r\n<b>f</b>: oh you think i was kidding? ill show you kidding. [either plays hop-scotch on the head before dawn or shits in an unauthorized location]\r\n<p>noone really likes fernando. I claim to <a href=\"http://www.links.net/vita/web/suck/carl/fernando/whyamyloves.html\">love</a> him, but most of the day I respond to his whines with crabby shrieks and biting irony.</p>\r\n<p> Irony is like an emotional parachute, a Swedish fellow once told me. they have a lot of humanitarian shit figured out there, so I felt humbled by his international observation of my social behavoir.</p>\r\n<p>Im trying to work on this problem I have, that I have had since the days before fernando. I cant seem to feel anything without making sarcastic jokes. it is the Chicken Shit method of love.</p>\r\n<p>I am using fernando to experiment with emotional sincerity. He suffers from the same fears as me. If he lays on the bed, and I shift the covers, he panics: \"she doesnt want me. I will pretend I didnt want her.\" Also, he wiggles his ass for pets and scratches, but at the height of pleasure and comfort, he screeches and bites. </p>\r\n<p>Cats know that you are vulnerable and indisposed when you sit on the throne. \r\nWe think they are cold to us, but how else would they survive without stringing us along with their capricious love? We are so large and capable of crushing them. When we drop our drawers and sit on the toilet, they feel that we are accessible.</p>\r\n<p>When I take my seat, my live-in catlover doesnt jump hastily on the lap. He scampers up without eye contact and slinks by my legs, softly brushing my shin with cheek, side-belly, tail. I pretend not to see him, and nonchalantly tug the tip of the tail with my fingers. Unaknowledged tenderness is a step above irony. We are working on it.</p> '),(1406,'When Josh got home, he peeked in his room at the answering machine. No flashing lights. â€œGuess Iâ€™m not in demand todayâ€, he said, and turned out the light. He pulled the sliding keyboard holder on his computer desk out, activating the monitor. His mind kept returning to the grocery store, to the girl behind the counter, to his nervousness. It was getting worse. He could hardly stand to go anywhere, to see the questions in their eyes. Their eyesâ€¦jesus, werenâ€™t you supposed to outgrow this sort of thing? With a heavy sigh, he got up, and went to the window. He saw a couple walking down the sidewalk, hand in hand,  about his age. Thatâ€™s when it hit him, like a bolt of lightening. He put his shoes back on, and practically ran to the car. \r\n<p>\r\nThe whole way out to the mall, the image of his slavation, his armor, danced in his head. Why hadnâ€™t he seen it before? It was perfect. Fifteen minutes later, he arrived in the parking lot, and walked quickly through the door. Immediately, he was hit with a burst of sensation, and he felt his knees go weak. â€œI can do this,â€ he said to himself, and with his head down he made his way to the jewelry store.\r\n<p>\r\nThe first thing Kate noticed when she looked up was those eyesâ€”they were bright blue, but light blue, you know, baby blue. Whatever you wanted to call them, they were certainly bright. The eyes belonged to a man, reasonably attractive, dressed sharply in a button up white shirt that wasnâ€™t quite a business shirt, or what her mother would call â€œsunday-clothesâ€, but still very nice. He wore slacks, and a belt with a silver buckle. She tried immediately to look like she hadnâ€™t been looking at the belt buckle. <p>\r\n	â€œHello, what can I help you with?â€ she asked, in her usual way, though his smile made her slightly flustered. She was only 19.\r\n<P>	â€œIâ€™m looking for a wedding ring,â€ he said, looking everywhere but at her, â€œcheapest one youâ€™ve got.â€ \r\n<P>	â€œThe cheapest? Well, we have some very reasonably priced ones over hereâ€¦this oneâ€™s nice, only fifty dollarsâ€¦â€\r\n<p>	â€œIâ€™ll take it.â€\r\n<p>	â€œWhat size do you wear?â€\r\n<p>	â€œI donâ€™t know.â€ He fidgeted. Kate wondered for a second if he would try to rob her, then pushed the thought out of her mind. She sized his finger, rung up the appropriate ring, and handed him the receipt. \r\n<p>	â€œWould you like that gift-wrapped?â€\r\n<p>	â€œNo, thank you,â€ he said, â€œitâ€™s fine like it is.â€\r\n<p>\r\nHe turned and walked out of the jewelry store like a man with a purpose. He went into the bathroom, and tried on the ring, the ring that would save him, the magic ring. It fit perfectly. Josh took a deep breath, and walked out into the food court. He decided to try it out first at Taco Bell, because it had the prettiest girl working the counter.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n	\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1407,'<p>\r\ni started reading <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1583940006/\">the televisionary oracle</a>; 450+ pages of rob brezsny, rob brezsny, rob brezsny. rob brezsny turned up to 11 through a concert stadium system. all rob brezsney all the time. too much rob brezsny. but i can\'t put it down. reading rob brezsny makes me think of egg. maybe it\'s that they both remind me of a psychic garage sale; a dumpster-diving shaped fairytale of improbable bathroom encounters that eavesdrop on the life and times of joan of arc.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni want to write an ode to egg but i don\'t know what it would say. egg and i are opposites. my deal is that i need to build stuff out of chaos. i summarize things; i live for bullet points. egg just lives chaos. egg wears silk pants and a camisole top and a wool ski mask and a netted shawl just because they were sitting next to each other - and let me make this clear, \"sitting next to each other\" doesn\'t mean they were sitting next to each other on the floor or anywhere near the same place - but you know, they were just <i>sitting next to each other</i>. egg. egg is one of the only friends i trust to tear me to shreds while i file away loose cds that didn\'t end up back on the shelves; trying to look unperturbed, but really i know i\'m on the line. egg is a good sport. egg asks me for favors and i\'m happy to deliver.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntonight i ran into paul - excuse me - \"polaris\" - again while i was sitting on the same bench on the same street, eating soup again and reading rob brezsny and thinking of all the pisces i\'ve known. he was wearing the same pants. polaris is also a pisces. polaris had just left a wanky book signing at <a href=\"http://www.limn.com/\">limn gallery</a>. limn is the wankiest interior design shop in san francisco. everyone goes apeshit over limn but honestly i find their dentist-office-for-rich-people aesthetic to be unnerving. he flipped through the book with me while we had inspired conversation (rehashed but inspired) about the nature of modern artistic expression (\"something something exploring beauty in the grotesque\") and hardboiled eggs wrapped in condoms as models to fabricate lamps and people who shit on canvases. i love polaris, and i love that the only way i know to get in touch with him is to sit in one place and see who walks by. we first met five years ago at the bahia cabana. he followed me and my roommate home to our apartment in the alley on natoma street. he didn\'t stop talking the entire time but i liked everything he had to say.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen polaris left and ate greasy diner food without me, undoubtedly flipping through the signed wanky designer book again, this time with even more care than the last flip-through; this time i bet using a toothpick to keep greasy fingerprints off the pages to, you know, respect the sanctity of the stop-motion sneeze mucous flower vase. (i was impressed too actually.)\r\n</p><p>\r\ni ran into polaris exactly right there, two weeks ago. conversation picked up where it left off.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen unexpected friend number 2 showed up out of nowhere (by the way it\'s midnight, foggy, market street, the F train is near) after riding his new electric scooter on the sidewalk for the last four miles - and i swear they just show up out of nowhere as soon as the last one leaves - and described to me, in great detail, all about the little plastic box (he taps on the box affectionately) that boosts the speed up to 30mph. we will call this friend \'D\'. \'D\' was tripping, having consumed 2 hits of liquid acid before hitting the streets with his demonic red two-wheeled modified john deere. \'D\' had a mission: to unleash full terror upon passing newspaper stands and street bazaars. \'D\' and i chatted for a bit. i will never miss an opportunity to fuck with someone tripping.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni went by the late night diner on the way home, hoping to run into someone else. but you really can\'t force synchronicity.\r\n</p>'),(1408,'<p>i used to be young. now i\'m 62 and japanese food gives me strange dreams, \r\n        like last night\'s for instance. first, here\'s a little background to maybe \r\n        why the Japanese food is bringing out these things.</p>\r\n      <p>okay, it\'s been cold here. the a/c is on high and it\'s kicking ice cubes out \r\n        from the vents, so i bring a sweater/black FBI jacket to keep the chill \r\n        out from my nipples. why doesn\'t anyone else around here? it doesn\'t take much of a brain \r\n        to bring a jacket or a scarf or some mittens or a heat dish or something. \r\n        why do they have to borrow mine? why can\'t i say no? and why do they have \r\n        to be fat? i mean, fuck, my clothes are getting stretched out.</p>\r\n      <p>so in my dream i used my sweater to clean up the juice after i masturbated \r\n        and then brought it to work. the fat one asked for the sweater and i gladly \r\n        handed it over. she wore it and got a rash on her body in the shape of little sweaters.</p>\r\n      <p><i>guitar solo</i></p>\r\n      <p>i\'m not really 62 but in my dream i was. i was a dirty old man, getting \r\n        my sweaters dirty for the rest of the day. i had a whole box of sweaters \r\n        from the salvation army and i was handing them out and the fat girls were \r\n        putting them on and spraying them w/ their rite-aid scent. instantly, \r\n        they were breaking out in rashes. i was also wearing ski boots and goggles \r\n        and a goose-down vest. that\'s how cold it gets in here. </p>\r\n      <p>today, i didn\'t bring a sweater and it\'s pretty cold (think: you\'re the first one to take a dump on the white porcelain. no one has warmed it up for you. that gives me the chills just thinking about it). </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The girl behind me is \r\n        out sick today. maybe she has a rash, but she sleeps w/ a lot of guys, \r\n        so it\'s not my fault. oh, by the way, i\'m getting married next may. </p>'),(1409,'<p>i used to be young. now i\'m 62 and japanese food gives me strange dreams, \r\n        like last night\'s for instance. first, here\'s a little background to maybe \r\n        why the Japanese food is bringing out these things.</p>\r\n      <p>okay, it\'s been cold here. the a/c is on high and it\'s kicking ice cubes \r\n        out from the vents, so i bring a sweater/black FBI jacket to keep the \r\n        chill out from my nipples. why doesn\'t anyone else around here? it doesn\'t \r\n        take much of a brain to bring a jacket or a scarf or some mittens or a \r\n        heat dish or something. why do they have to borrow mine? why can\'t i say \r\n        no? and why do they have to be fat? i mean, fuck, my clothes are getting \r\n        stretched out.</p>\r\n      <p>so in my dream i used my sweater to clean up the juice after i masturbated \r\n        and then brought it to work. the fat one asked for the sweater and i gladly \r\n        handed it over. she wore it and got a rash on her body in the shape of \r\n        little sweaters.</p>\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><i>[guitar solo here]</i></p>\r\n      <p>i\'m not really 62 but in my dream i was. i was a dirty old man, getting \r\n        my sweaters dirty for the rest of the day. i had a whole box of sweaters \r\n        from the salvation army and i was handing them out and the fat girls were \r\n        putting them on and spraying them w/ their rite-aid scent. instantly, \r\n        they were breaking out in rashes. i was also wearing ski boots and goggles \r\n        and a goose-down vest. that\'s how cold it gets in here. and there was \r\n        a little boy named rash running around the office with his little brothers. \r\n        i don\'t know who these people are, but i found this picture on the ground \r\n        this morning. they were giving people rashes too.</p>\r\n      <p align=\"center\"><img src=\"http://www.cms.dmu.ac.uk/%7Emmc98sg/old/BRO%27S.JPG\" width=\"250\" height=\"188\"><br>\r\n        <b>from left to right: rash (age 8), sat (5), raj (3-4)</b></p>\r\n      <p>today, i didn\'t bring a sweater and it\'s pretty cold (think: you\'re the \r\n        first one to take a dump on the white porcelain. no one has warmed it \r\n        up for you. that gives me the chills just thinking about it). </p>\r\n      <p>The girl behind me is out sick today. maybe she has a rash, but she sleeps \r\n        w/ a lot of guys, so it\'s not my fault. oh, by the way, i\'m getting married \r\n        next may. </p>'),(1410,'<P>\r\nfrom email:\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\n\"i can understand why you wanted to contact her. but after being burned from her long ago in the past (remember i told you years ago that i emailed her telling her i like my site and she responded that at least she knows how to write and design a site? so completely insulted me). anyway, after that, i became fascinated with her in an appalled kindof way. like, how does anyone\r\nget through life being such an asshole to other people? but i just really let it go. let bygones be bygones.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nman, those internet vultures really get on my nerves. careful what you say when you speak you mind out here in the cyber world....geez.\"\r\n</p>'),(1411,'<p>they call me Tourettes Girl at \"work\". really, Im not THAT talented. I have a few tricks up my sleeve. they involve foreign languages. a little french from the continent, a little dirty Thai talk. yknow, like 2Live Crew. not.</p>\r\n<p>James my \"boss\" is talking about his new yellow girlfriend, and I am singing \"shes got bamboo fever, i got bamboo fever, he got bamboo fever, HES A PERV!\" Yolanda is playing with a slinky in Olivia\'s corner and \"bitching already\". I am distractedly chatting, confused and bewildered about this fresh hell before me and yelling out exotic swearwords. It is all the same fucking day, mang, and I havent been here in a month.</p>\r\n<p>one word that often pulsates through my brain is \"fucktong\". I have never shared this yet. \"fucktong\" is a rough phonetic translation of the Thai for \"pumpkin\". it sounds dirty to me.<i>fucktong!</i> the demons in my head yell to themselves and me. another word I like to yell is \"fuckter!\" that is roughly the french for \"mailman\", spelled correctly, \"facteur\".</p>\r\n<p>I am emailing with jory about scrabble. when you are drunk and/or punchy you can best play scrabble by inventing words and their definitions. some good ones that have come out of this are \"lampomo\", \"neuronathon\", and \"yabilgew\". </p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://memory.loc.gov/ammem/today/jul24.html\">happy pioneer day</a>. other employees of this company have today off. I have sounded my echo across this hollow shell of an office. </p><p>\"FUCKTONG\". <br><i>fucktong?fucktong?fucktong?</i></p>'),(1412,'<p>\r\nMe: I remember the day I moved to Pennsylvania. You won\'t even believe what I was wearing. I remember every detail of it. A red jumpsuit type thing but I cut the sleeves so they were a bit jagged and short to show off my little skater muscles. One earring. Maybe it was a gold dagger, I can\'t recall. An asymetrical california skater haircut with that bit in the front that just sort of sat there, except I think for that first day, I gelled it up and to the side. Most likely I used L\'oreal Studio. That stuff in the white tube with the primary color squares. Vision Street Wear shoes. A bunch of those little rubber bracelets, black and white only.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nJessica: After my parents\' got divorced, my mom sent my sister and I to Catholic School. My sister went first and loved it. She was the princess of the school. Then I went. I also remember exactly what I was rocking that first day. Kind of the girl version of your little 5th grade outfit. A red Esprit jumpsuit with a skirt, a white shirt underneath and I also had a weird asymetrical haircut with a big permed part that poofed out on the one side. These shoes, these patent leather black flats with these crazy color swirls and cut-outs. I mean, they were <blink>NEON</blink>. And a red hat that was tilted to the side with a weird brim, like Pretty in Pink style.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMe: After the first two months of school, Mr. Yokum called my mom in for a conference to inform her that he had \"never met anyone like me before\" and quite frankly didn\'t have the faintest idea how to deal with me. So I had to leave. They put me in a private school for the psychotic children of rich assholes and I had to take a bus almost an hour each way every day. One of my first days there, I met Jeremy Cramer when his mother dropped him off in her white Lexus with gold detailing. He kicked me in the stomach on the football field and called me a faggot. He was the head boy and Lori Goldstein was the head girl. I hesitate to call them \"most popular\" because I don\'t think anyone <i>really</i> liked either of them. Anyway, I\'ll spare you the gorey details.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nJessica: Yeah, they all hated me. I ate my lunch alone for the longest time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMe: No one liked me either. For a while, my mom was my only friend.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n....\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSome of my shining moments in junior high school:<br>\r\nRyan Hart got a scholarship to the private school and his asymetrical skater cut was even better than mine. He was a dream. Lori Goldstein liked him. And he was my friend. He lived in a tiny dirty white house with his step-sister, Kimber, a mythical prom queen from another school who everyone spoke about in hushed reverent tones. We skated in my driveway and talked about how cool we were that everyone wanted to beat us up. He moved that year and I never saw him again. We had the same black and white sweater and actually, I still have it.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIndia was the 8th grade blow-job queen. She hung out with the most popular girls in junior high but they all trashed her behind her back. I heard Lori whisper once that India had slept with the-9th-grade-hot-tuff-guy who she later got kicked out of school with. In that moment, I knew I loved her. The day she got kicked out of school, Mr. Meleske (Mr. Molest-Me) paraded her through the halls with everything from her locker piled haphazardly on top of her binder. Everyone stood in front of their lockers and stared. I looked at the floor. She stopped in front of me and smiled and handed me a grape Jolly Rancher. Mr. Molest-Me yelled at her to hurry up. I never saw her again either.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhen I finally made it to 8th grade, my scholarship ran out and I transferred to another Appalachian public school called Middle School West. At MSW, I learned how to sniff glue, steal parents\' cars when they went out of town, a boy named Shannon taught me the delicate ways of giving good hand jobs and a girl named Amy taught me the rewards of patience as it took me all year to get into her pants. I was still mostly afraid of people (thanks to the private school years) and didn\'t have too many friends. Amy\'s three brothers liked me though and they taught me complicated Zippo tricks, how to smoke pot, and how to break in to locked places.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThat same year, a poplular girl named Molly became disillusioned with the clique and started dating a big mohawk-having punk rocker named Sean. At lunch, the ladies would sit around and discuss Molly\'s fall in mostly make-up and outfit terms. I was lucky enough to be in history class on the Tuesday of Molly\'s moment of glory. She sat one behind and one over from me, in the back of the room. Wearing a black sweatshirt, black jeans and no make-up, the once pink and sparkly Molly Jenkins of the popular table lit a Marb Red with a match, stood up and turned to the entire class. She said, \"I just want to say, fuck you. Fuck you, Mr. Monroe. Fuck this junior high bullshit. I think you\'re all disgusting.\" With that she walked out. And really, as far as I remember it, I never saw her again either.\r\n</p>'),(1413,'<p>Mid-morning my supervisor\'s daughter, who\'s here today helping her mom out at \r\nwork, was on the phone with a boy.  He wanted to know how old I am, and, \r\nknowing how the jealous boy\'s mind works, I understood his reasons for asking her \r\nto ask my age.  Apparently he let his jealousy show through because, shortly \r\nthereafter, she said to him, \"Don\'t trip.  I\'ll cut you.\"  A bit later she said something \r\nsmart to me (don\'t you all remember how fun it was to be sarcastic and smart with \r\nyour parents\' coworkers when you were growing up?) and I said, \"Don\'t trip.  I\'ll\r\ncut you.\"  This got a hefty amount of laughs from her mom.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My dad never took me to work with him just to take me to work with him.  Maybe I \r\nspent an hour or two there before an appointment at the hospital, but I can\'t really \r\never remember going and sitting in his office for the eight or ten straight hours of a \r\nduty day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I hit middle school I would ride my bike around the flightline and across base \r\nto whatever orderly room or admin. office he was assigned to that year.  I even \r\nremember taking a girl, who I \"dated\" for a very short time in elementary or middle \r\nschool, over to The Triangle (the training area of the base, where all the NPS \r\n(non-prior service) airmen dorms were located) and picked up some money before \r\nheading over to the Mini-BX (Base Exchange) for ice cream.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That year, while my dad was assigned to what is now (the number designation has \r\nchanged since that time) the 332d Training Squadron, the \"Mad Dogs,\" I could see \r\nhim any time I wanted.  It was a fifteen minute ride around the runway to his office, in \r\nthe midst of The Triangle, where all my friends and I loved to hang out anyway--we \r\ncould play pool (sometimes even taking on the above-mentioned airmen who were \r\npassing through for training) or piano in the banquet hall at The V (Vandenberg Hall, \r\nthe recreation center), play video games or buy CDs at the Mini-BX (I still \r\nremember buying Green Day\'s <i>dookie</i> there).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Years later, when I went back as one of those NPS airmen myself, it was both a \r\nhomecoming and a dark moment.  I didn\'t want those carefree memories of my \r\nearlier youth tainted with the horrors and stresses of active-duty military training.  We \r\nstepped off the bus onto the drillpad/basketball court of the 332d Training Squadron \r\nacross the street from The V and all snapped to attention at the first sight of an MTL \r\n(Military Training Leader) only to be given rest, something sorely needed in the \r\nMississippi humidity.   I looked around me under the blazing summer sun:  off-base, \r\nacross the street and railroad tracks stood my old middle school; the Mini-BX stood down the street\r\nmuch the same as it had when I was there years before; in the\r\nother direction lay the golf course bordering the runway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You can never go home again, they say.  They\'re wrong.  You can go home again.  \r\nThe place will always embrace you.  Once you know its pieces, its secrets, its \r\nstreets, its desires, the place can\'t reject you.  The <i>place</i> can\'t reject you.  The people \r\nare a different matter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I didn\'t spend much time with old non-military brat friends who still lived in the local \r\ncommunity, and all the military brat friends had, of course, long ago moved on.  It\'s \r\nthe way those things work.  People change and grow apart, as the cliche goes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wasn\'t assigned to the 332d, though I had friends from basic who were.  My \r\ntraining field had recently been consolidated into the 338th MTF (Military Training \r\nFlight), the \"Dark Knights.\"  (The military really is, in some ways, like high school:  \r\nsquadrons have mascots and t-shirts, there are drill-downs that are a sort of \r\ncombination pep rally and football game, there are groups such as the band you can \r\njoin to pass your free time, and the similarities go on.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I haven\'t been back to Keesler AFB (Air Force Base) since that time.  I can say that \r\nmy memories weren\'t tainted, even though I had a rough time of it while there the \r\nsecond time.  If anything, the memories were dusted off by my walks and drives \r\nthrough old neighborhoods and the short visits with old friends and teachers, and \r\nthen added to by the experiences of training there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You can never lose a place or time.  You can always go back.</p>'),(1414,'<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">you know that time when you realize\r\nmortality?</font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when two of your mother\'s sisters\r\nhave breast cancer and are going through chemo..</font>\r\n<br><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">    this time\r\nyou only have to go through chemo for six months.</font></i>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when another aunt\'s mother has just\r\nbeen diagonosed with cancer of the pancreas, and it\'s all over.</font>\r\n<br><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">    you have one\r\nmonth to live.</font></i>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when a family friend discovers that\r\nher unborn child has been is dead for two weeks, and her 7 year old daughter\r\nhates god for taking away the baby brother they\'d been hoping for..</font>\r\n<br><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">    we\'ll let\r\nyour body expel the fetus.</font></i>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when your adoptive grandmother dies\r\nin a hospital from a misdiagnosed internal rupture.</font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><i>    she\'s old\r\nand making up pains. </i></font>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">when you\'re father has surgery and\r\nyou don\'t even know why.</font>\r\n<br><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">    don\'t tell\r\nher, don\'t want her to worry.</font></i>\r\n\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">i just want scream yell and kick.\r\nshake my hands in the air, and howl at the moon. run into my mother\'s arms\r\nand be comforted. curl up in a ball and hold it all in me. get on the floor\r\nand have a royal tantrum. to say FUCK YOU! FUCK YOU FOR TAKING THEM AWAY!</font>\r\n<p><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">fuck you</font></i>\r\n<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">i want to cry. cry like i never have\r\nbefore. big hot tears streaming down my face. i want to mourn. to feel\r\ntheir death their pain coursing through my body, leaving me limp and wet\r\nand tired. to take in their suffering, to know it, understand it. to accept\r\nit.</font>\r\n<br><i><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">good bye, i cared.</font></i>'),(1415,'<p>austin swinney is a glorious piece of shite!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he says let\'s go have beers...and then wants to know while you\'re at the bar if you can cover it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you tell him that you don\'t like to talk about art he will make sure to mention <a href=\"http://www.cordair.com/larsen/heroes.htm\">some painting</a> you like along with his own version of innocent commentary meant to make your blood boil.<a href=\"http://thumbtack.org\">[ask snafu]</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>he waits until the movie has started to decide he\'s going to run off to find an atm to pay you back for the beers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he waits until the movie has started and you\'re already late to decide that he HAS to go pee.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>after the movie he will do nothing but complain about how ill he feels because of the pizza he so desperately wanted five minutes before the movie started.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you are sick and puking the next week with flu he will call and ask if you\'d like some fried chicken with meatballs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><br>\r\n*sigh*<br>\r\ni love that guy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>[swinney dude...you are going to get the most wonderfully painful titty twister the next time i see you. your little whiteboy nipples are mine mister!]</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>~kat</i></p>'),(1416,'<I>Strikes crook of elbow with two fingers.</i><P>\r\nI don\'t own a computer, so the only internet access I have is at work, and sometimes I find myself jOn\'zin for a little swinney.org and having to wait until Monday.</p><P>So... I was looking at the \'list of frequent contributors,\' shall we say, and it seems that the magic number is six.  This being my sixth article submitted, it seems like a good time to talk about the Swinney experience.</p><P>\r\nSwinney is the first online community I\'ve found that keeps me coming back.  The banality and meaningless arguments and flames that occur in newsgroups is boring and the only way to make chat rooms interesting is to pretend to be a sixteen year old girl.  But Swinney... Swinney is something special.  It gives me a feeling of being back in college.  More than anything, I miss being surrounded by young, interested, intellectual people.  This place lets me almost pretend that my life isn\'t slipping into a rut, but maybe I should start from the beginning...</p>\r\n<P>Josh to Swat to Justin to Links to Amy to Swinney -- and now I\'m here.  At first, just looking around, a sort of peeping essemar being amused by the musings of others.  Then reading the rules and thinking that\'s cool.  It keeps people honest, so to speak, it keeps things interesting, but I remember the fear of rejection looming over me as I wrote my first piece.  Will it please?  Is it alright?  and it was fine.  No standing ovations.  No sniffs from arrogent noses held high in the air.  So, I\'m in the front door.  I\'m not just looking in through the windows.</p><P>I felt like the guy trying to slip into the circle of conversation that everyone nods to in recognition of his existence then returns their attention to people they find more interesting.  It was like being at a party where I don\'t know a single person.  I see the cliques, the groups of people that have known eachother forever, since before Swinney.org, and I feel a little left out.  I don\'t get to have a beer with BMG or snort coke off of egg\'s breasts or watch Incitor beat the shit out of someone in the park.  I don\'t actually know these people, but isn\'t that how life is?  Everyone we know was once a stranger, even our parents.</p><P>Now, I feel like the guy you see once in a while at the bar you hang out at.  You wave to him, call him by name, sit and chat with him for a while.  It\'s a comfortable relationship.  You wouldn\'t trust him alone in your house, but you trust that he\'ll save your seat for you.  Having grown up in a small town I\'m only now realizing that it takes time to get to know people, to make friends.</p><P>So... I like Swinney.org.  It makes me happy, and I really want to get to know some of you better.  I appreciate the chance to look into other people\'s lives and laugh and cry and learn from their experiences.  It makes me a better person</p><P>Oh yeah....</p><P>One little disclaimer: The caliber of writing that I come across here is really high, and the place seems to attract a lot of self-titled writers, people who love to write and do it well.  I studied physics in school and am currently an aspiring dancer.  I\'ve never written much in my life and sometimes feel a little intimidated here, but then I remember that no one will be harsh in their criticism, and the practice is good for me.</p><P>Now after that brief moment of low self-esteem, I\'d like spread wide my virtual arms and engage everyone in one enormous group hug.</p>'),(1417,'This is a strange time for me. I feel disconnected, signed off...like I have nothing to say. If you knew me you might think that was strange. I don\'t want to see my old friends. In a way, I somehow don\'t want to know them anymore. They are like dolls, like actors, one-dimensional. I feel like everyone I thought I knew, I really don\'t. Or maybe...there\'s nothing to know.\r\n<p>\r\nEverybody talks about changing, \"Oh, I\'m gonna change, you\'ll see.\" I think that this is the first time it has ever really happened to me. It\'s not like you might expect. My mind is filled with music, I\'m playing really well...nothing else seems real. My family, I don\'t recognize them. I don\'t recognize me. I like it, sort of. I have moved on. My motivations are all different. Somebody please tell me what\'s going on?\r\n<p>\r\nI want somebody to use me for a life preserver. I want to save someone from despair. I want to be somebody\'s beautiful dream. I want to be a real boy.'),(1418,'<P> well, the truth is i dont even know him...except for a few messages left on the answering machine and some detailed emails of him. still, he seems like a neat person. and when i go to new york, i will be sleeping on your couch.i wonder if you are a trendy twenty something dude or older. or if you are into saying you are a vegetarian but still eat fish.hmmm.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> this is a public reaching-out service to <B> YOU </B> , Essemar. Since it appears that you never call at the choice moments when my pressence is available. Or maybe...I.m never there. I want to move out. I live with 2 girls in a 3 bedroom loft and i think my rent will go up high high is i sneak my man to live in with me. SO we might as well live together, just us two. \r\nIm a swim teacher. So i guess im good with kids.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> trick with kids is to treat them as you would anybody else. they\'re little small and dreadfully precocious, at least most of them, and see their surroundings in very matter the fact ways.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> running. i guess one could call me a runner. I never ran in highscool though. my friend was \"herb\" in highschool...so I take a year off, say screw you to the people at Scripps ( claremont) and decide to vaga-bond-it everywhere for a year. During this year i realise that school is a very good invention. and i run for this school. i guess i have half of a knack at running. i can hang. but with one hand if you know what i mean.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Half huh? guys that are half asian and half white are hot. just talking from experience.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Viva le\' Brandon Lee!</P>'),(1419,'<P>It disturbs me how much people assume about the thoughts and feelings of people who post here.  I guess part of the problem is that internet-based communication is not holistic.  We can\'t see the face, we can\'t see the eyes, and in general, we\'re lacking all of the meta-communication that can serve to define context.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Many of Lou\'s postings are cutting, and she can be judgemental, but on the whole, there\'s no maliciousness in her (although she can be quite vindictive).  It\'s obvious that she\'s hurt some people, but it\'s disappointing that instead of confronting her on her shortcomings in sensitivity, they try to hurt her back instead, and judge her.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>I think it would be a great step forward if everyone could integrate Lou\'s fearless honesty with a bit more humility and a dose of tact.  Lou and I have always disagreed about tact.  She always thought it phony; I could never get her to see that tact didn\'t have to be phony.  I still can\'t get her to see that.  Oh well.</P>'),(1420,'<P>It disturbs me how much people assume about the thoughts and feelings of people who post here.  I guess part of the problem is that internet-based communication is not holistic.  We can\'t see the face, we can\'t see the eyes, and in general, we\'re lacking all of the meta-communication that can serve to define context.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Many of Lou\'s postings are cutting, and she can be judgemental, but on the whole, there\'s no maliciousness in her (although she can be quite vindictive).  It\'s obvious that she\'s hurt some people, but it\'s disappointing that instead of confronting her on her shortcomings in sensitivity, they try to hurt her back instead, and judge her.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>I think it would be a great step forward if everyone could integrate Lou\'s fearless honesty with a bit more humility and a dose of tact.  Lou and I have always disagreed about tact.  She always thought it phony; I could never get her to see that tact didn\'t have to be phony.  I still can\'t get her to see that.  Oh well.</P>'),(1421,'<P>trickle of non-sound from ears</P>\r\n<P>unfound and laying lap in legs</P>\r\n\r\n<P>we are the dregs the anticdotes the</P>\r\n\r\n       <p>psycho</P>\r\n         <p>mass murderers</P>\r\n           <P>collide</P>\r\n\r\n<P>down sheets</P>\r\n<P>into cellars</P>\r\n<P> outside window sills</P>\r\n\r\n<P>sit and wait we contemplate the now the then the this</P>\r\n\r\n<P>forgetting shoulder hill, shrill laughs, wet kiss</P>\r\n\r\n<P>understand in handshake mode their walk and talk</P>\r\n\r\n<P>feel inside not out with tight eyelids open\r\nand breathe and breath and breath \r\nwe say to doubt, \" Hop in.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>opening the door, setting down a place at our dinner table, serving it our dreams and fears.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>desolate squalor in a champagne glass.\r\nI pass. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>the cynics age. an aura of non-aura\'s. a smell where images are taken from a warehouse cobweb corner...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and nothing novice is prepared</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>looking through the eyes of a baby-boomer salesman</P>\r\n\r\n<P>looking through the heart of an unkept housewife</P>\r\n\r\n<P>looking through a vessel torn in the side.</P>'),(1422,'<br>\r\nRacing, careening towards the other,<br>\r\ndisoriented vehicles, collision-course set<br>\r\naspirating flaming fuels,<br>\r\ngrowling rpms, drinking tenor cries of gears,<br>\r\nbarrelling towards midnight moments.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nEngines roar, night air whistling, squealing<br>\r\npast rubber gasket intakes and chrome fixtures<br>\r\nwhich sparkle in the cricket-song moonlight.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI never knew I passed the point of no return.<br>\r\nYour oncoming beams blinding me<br>\r\nas the crash-course distance closed.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nA screaming collision, met head-on.<br>\r\nA rip of bent metal and shattering glass<br>\r\nand locked horns blaring<br>\r\ninto the twilight void<br>\r\n<br>\r\nQuenched.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1423,'<p>i could be inconsolable<br>\r\ni could be intransigent and frank<br>\r\ni could be the third incarnation of regret<br>\r\nand i could be a dilettante<br>\r\nand hey look at all the things that i know<br>\r\nand yes yes<br>\r\nvery impressed<br>\r\nwe\'re all very impressed<br>\r\nand why don\'t you try out for that game show, son<br>\r\nand why don\'t i parachute naked into a foreign country where i don\'t know the language<br>\r\ni have no currency<br>\r\ni am not current<br>\r\ni am carried away by this current<br>\r\nand into eddies and vortexes of my own imagination<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut not this time<br>\r\nthis time everyone gets out<br>\r\nthis time everyone\'s fat and content<br>\r\nthis time is shaking with involuntary orgasms in twenty second intervals<br>\r\nand when that great demon, death, debility, despair demands anxiety ransom<br>\r\nand though i\'ve paid before<br>\r\ni won\'t anymore<br>\r\nnot this time<br><br>\r\nand all the guests come and go<br>\r\nwildly drunk<br>\r\nand full of words<br>\r\nand i am only pleased<br>\r\nthe most extraordinary thing is<br>\r\nto be</p>'),(1424,'when i was 16 i met a boy named bobby palmer who made me burn.  touching his hand was never enough.<br><br>\r\nfor two years we joined together in the most exquisite physical joy.  we played.  we laughed.  we owned the world and all within it. he was a virgin, i was not.<br><br>\r\nnobody in the history of sex had ever had bigger and better sex than we had.<br><br>\r\nwhen i was 18 i realized that i wanted to marry and have fat babies and live happily ever after.  bobby wanted that too, but not just then, he wanted me, but not just then, but he didn\'t realize he felt that way.<br><br>\r\nwe got engaged and planned a wedding.  one night i found him in the arms of someone else.   she was everything i was not.<br><br>\r\nfor me it was an epiphany.  it wasn\'t that i couldn\'t forgive him, it wasn\'t that i REALLY cared he was fucking someone else.<br><br>\r\nit was that i knew in that moment that our places in time had not merged.  he was older than me, but he was also younger in time.<br><br>\r\ni ran.<br><br>\r\nthe next day i met mark.<br><br>\r\ni knew the moment i saw him that i would marry him and have fat babies with him.  i thought i knew something else - i thought i knew that in doing so, i was saying goodbye to passion, to wanting, to burning.<br><br>\r\nthe sex was great, but it wasn\'t explosive.  at least not at first...<br><br>\r\nas the years passed i began to think that passion was highly over-rated as a criteria for love.  here was this man who worshipped me, and i him, this man who was wonderful and maddening and irritating and the father of children we both adored and opinionated and grouchy and sometimes judgemental.  this man who thought he knew me.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am not sure when it happened.  one day i found myself wishing he would come home for lunch so we could knock off a quickie...  and then i began to urge the children into bed early so we could slip away.  we began meeting in the bathroom and locking the door, telling the children that we were taking a shower...<br><br>\r\n\r\ntwenty-nine years on august 20th, and the only thing i am sure of in my life is that when i was young i thought i chose stability over passion, only to find that i\'d actually chosen a lifetime of passion so big, so hot, so burning, that the fires can never go out.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhow lucky i am.<br><br>'),(1425,'Grey morning Oakland--<br>\r\nlightness won\'t come \'til later.<br>\r\nLove is lost on me.<p><p>'),(1426,'<img src=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/images/bensamme.jpg\" width=\"80\" height=\"387\" border=\"0\" alt=\"weirdie sans beardie\" align=\"right\"><p>this morning the first thing I looked at was <a href=\"http://www.saturn5.com/~ben/prod/slap.mov\">this movie</a>. I woke up groggy having gone to the store at 2 AM with Sam to get milk for jay. Strangely Sam is a motivator at that hour. After three Pepsis and Breakfast and Tiffany\'s, he\'s good to go. I don\'t like jay to wake up without his things.</p>\r\n<p>I am an aspiring matchmaker. whenever girlfriends or boyfriends complain of any ailment I say, \"you need to get laid. let me see who is on my rolodex.\" and nothing ever comes of it. nothing successful at least. I dont know why I am obsessed with delivering pootie tang.</p>\r\n<p>but I successfully matched up sam and ben. they dont fuck or anything. Im beginning to realize that one needs many different kinds of lovers.</p> \r\n<p>i took them to see deerhoof at the Stork Club. we had hours to kill ahead of time. I introduced them, sat them down in my living room and puttered around the house . they sat there and stared in silence. no eye contact. no small talk. like a grammy I nudged them \"cmon you guys TALK TALK TALK. CMON!!!\"</p>\r\n<p>then ben found a pc gamer magazine in sam\'s car on the way to burritos. and then the Amy Linton conversation. and now when I call Sam\'s cell phone he is often at Ben\'s, or with Ben. I think they love each other.</p>\r\n<p>Yesterday Sam came over with one agenda. Velcro. Can I have velcro? yeah just dont take too much its for my project!! Just some little strips. We chided him. what for velcro??? what you need velcro?? He disappeared and reappeared without answering my shrill questions.</p>\r\n<p>Later in his car I saw that on his dashboard he had velcroed the portable CD player. These delights happen every hour with Sam, and I try to write them all down.</p>\r\n<p>Sam\'s concise method of communication guides me through spiritual trials.</p>\r\n<p><b>egg</b>: tim volunteer-tutors poor kids in math, drives an ambulance, works at a cancer treatment lab and applies to medical school in his spare fuckin time, goddamnit! there is so much work to do in the world. why not me? why am I not healing people? why do I just sit here and diddle?<br>\r\n<b>sam</b>: because you are a lazy sack of shit.<br>\r\n<b>egg</b>: oh. right.</p>\r\n<p>I play with Sam n Ben because they are Willing. They want to go hear the loud music and play the pinball games at sleazy bowling alleys. They dont whine about wanting to leave early. They dont say theyre bored.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>egglit </b>(1:16 PM):	DUNKIN DONUTS<br>\r\n<b>benadamX</b> (1:16 PM):	right now?<br>\r\n<b>egglit</b> (1:20 PM):	no!<br>\r\n<b>egglit</b> (1:22 PM):	later.<br>\r\n<b>egglit</b> (1:22 PM):	someday<br>\r\n<b>benadamX </b>(1:22 PM):	tease</p>\r\n<p> I am inspired this morning by Ben\'s prolific videomaking, Sam DOING THE DISHES UNPROVOKED IN MY HOUSE, and the transformative possibilities of friendship. Too much premium is placed in this world on the Noah\'s ark mating scheme of social interaction. Bros before hos. hmm.  I mean, hos before bros. I mean. we can be bros AND hos, together, now, tonight, and later.</p>'),(1427,'anyway...'),(1428,'<P>ripped from the rib parents. no longer speaking parents. and when we fight, it is worse than a break-up with an ex-lover. these people are like lovers in a sense. and its evern worse when money is involved. then there is really nothing to do about things. You are screwed. because they will throw that wet towel of money they  spent on you in your face...over and over again.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>then having epilepsy. is like have a chain around the waist with a big ten ton pill attached to it. nowhere can i go if i do not have my meds. if i run low on meds and have no money for refill such is now...then it it time to make a few phonecalls.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i dont think i envy people whose relationship with parents is smooth and undeterred. I just cannot comprehend. the comparisons, the guilt trips, the absolute bullshit are all common in my home. yes, im 22. and have two more years of school to finish. and yes, i get that fact- that my \"little\" sister may finish before me, thrown in my face all the time.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>sick,sick. now i have work and i am not in the mood. I think i will call in sick. I know i will. I need to lay in bed, put on some self-pitying music and cry. We had our last fight today. my mum and i. i really think its over. we were both mad as she drove me here...thats another thing. i dont drive due to the seizures. so im dependent. bleh...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>our eyes were both red as we told eachother off. and i act as if i dont need her. but i know out of everyone she did love me first. that i loked to her for comfort. that i looked to her because i was in awe of the world and she held my hand and squeezed once in a while.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>now i will try and forget that. its time to bum 20 bucks for my med refill and go to work...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>or call in sick.</p>'),(1429,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1429_1.gif\' align=\'center\'>'),(1430,'<P>Essemar\'s entry has inspired me, so here goes my no. 2.  (There\'s plenty of boob action later on, if you want to scroll past the intro.)</p>\r\n<P><em>I only know a couple of you from a time long ago in a place far away (meaning I <B>really</b> don\'t know anyone here very well), so I\'ll give a breif intro that will help put some perspective on how I accidentally turned my toddler into a seemingly sex-crazed maniac.</em>\r\n\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\nI am a 25 year old \"pseudo\" web designer/graphic designer/bi-weekly columnist/substitute accounts receivable person/sometime sales chick/anything that doesn\'t invovle cleaning toilets (not that there\'s anything wrong with cleaning toilets, just that someone already has that job) employee at a little newspaper in a town that\'s really easy to miss.  I the single mom of a sometimes angelic two year old boy. <small><Font color=\"mahogany\"><em>Memo to self: look into that \"blind faith in modern methods of birth control\" thing.</em></font></small>  I love music and have my office plastered with poetry and the quotes that I have highlighted during my many readings of Naked Lunch. (Does that make me cool enough for Swinney?) I am an avid enjoyer of good food (as evidenced by my impressive physical presence)and a shameless over-user of parentheticals.  (Feel free to email with any other questions.)...Oh, yeah, and I had an 11 pound breast reduction a year and a half ago.</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>After my son was born, I swore that I wasn\'t going to be one of those parents whose obvious lack of child-rearing skills often lead to embarassing public situations.  I would never swear around my son (that never fucking works), and never allow his cherubic face to be bathed in the light of something as vile as a questionable television program (<B>that</b> lasted about 2 months).</p>\r\n<P>If you know anything about toddlers, you understand that they have an inborn need to climb, grasping at whatever will assist them in their ascent - shelves, chairs, and especially the extremities of an untwitting seated adult.</p>\r\n<P>My son\'s personal favorite climbing aids happen to be my breasts.  While they are certainly smaller following my \"little procedure,\" there\'s still plenty left for groping baby fingers.  After his first few attemts at conquering mommy\'s personal Mount Everests, I would, firmly but with motherly love in my eyes, tell him that he was hurting mommy\'s boo-boos (a reference to the surgery, not to my anatomy).  It only took a few warnings before he caught on...not to the fact that he shouldn\'t be climing my boobs, but that women have breasts.</p><p>\r\n\r\nEvery time a Pepsi commercial came on tv he would run to the screen, gleefully and repeatedly shouting, \"Brinney\'s Boo-boos!\"  Potentially embarassing...sure, but how often would he see a Pepsi commercial in a public place.  If anything ever did happen, I could always blame the behavior on his father.  Then we went from potentially embarrassing to absolutely mortifying.</p>\r\n<p>Two weeks ago, he was fastidiously working on removing all of the contents of my bookshelf, when he came across a manual for my breast pump.  The picture on the cover was of a woman nursing her child.  With great speed, and inquisitive wonder beyond his years, he held the book to my face and asked, \"Boo-boos Ba-Ba?\"  \"Yes, Conner,\" I replied impressed at his comprehension of the picture, \"when you were a tiny baby, mommy\'s boo-boos were your bottle.\"  He immediately ran to the pile of magazines on the floor, picked up one of daddy\'s \"scantilly clad ladies\" magazines, held it to his face, and began making incredibly loud slurping noises.</p>\r\n<p>Now, we can no longer walk past a magazine rack in a store, watch tv at a friends house, or even walk past a woman in a low cut shirt without those incredibly embarrassing sound effects.  I know he doesn\'t mean anything by it, but that doesn\'t stop the piercing stares of grocery store patrons.  I have admittedly lapsed occasionally in my quest to raise a wholesome child, but certainly nothing to deserve embarrassment of this magnitude.  I think it may be my cosmic penalty for boasting about my 15 minute, three push, labor and delivery.</p>\r\n<p>So, while you may not know me now, you can listen for the sounds of my slurping son on your next trip to the supermarket, book store, or strip bar.  I\'ll be the plump, harried-looking, semi-large breasted woman with a face as red as a beet.</p>'),(1431,'<p>A little baby<br> \r\nis growing in my wife\'s womb;<br> \r\nmy heart smiles proud.'),(1432,'a mslazarus told me: \"one time i dreamed i was a spokes person for pain, like it was some kind of \r\nproduct you could buy, and all i did was ger tortured and beat, emotionaly \r\nand physicaly, but i had all this money, and i was famouse, and i woke up \r\nnot knowong what it meant, maybe that some things just arent worth being \r\nnoticed\"\r\n\r\nwhile i told her that once i dreamed of a girl whose parents where killed in a plane crash. so she became this anti-flight crusador. all i could really visually remember was her \"Anti Flight\" shirt and how sad she was when she won and the whole world ceased flight.'),(1433,'<P>a mslazarus told me: \"one time i dreamed i was a spokes person for pain, like it was some kind of \r\nproduct you could buy, and all i did was ger tortured and beat, emotionaly \r\nand physicaly, but i had all this money, and i was famouse, and i woke up \r\nnot knowong what it meant, maybe that some things just arent worth being \r\nnoticed\"\r\n\r\n<P>while i told her that once i dreamed of a girl whose parents where killed in a plane crash. so she became this anti-flight crusador. all i could really visually remember was her \"Anti Flight\" shirt and how sad she was when she won and the whole world ceased flight.'),(1434,'<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#990000\">â€œ \r\n  I guess I have a very loose interpretation of â€˜work,â€™Â Â because \r\n  I think that just being alive is so much work at something you donâ€™t always \r\n  want to do. â€ Andy Warhol</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\">Our arguing abour \r\n  art has always been sexy. You are even kind to the blithering idiocy of people \r\n  who ignore books and buddha. I always admired your List for Love Making: cellos, \r\n  chinese food and chocolate. It stinks of desire and deviancy. It was eye contact \r\n  that did me in. Not the promise of feathers, five album long foreplay or the \r\n  study of god\'s gluttony. I never complain, insomnia is my friend, lunacy my \r\n  guide. I take long sighs and I pause often to soak in all the snogging we did \r\n  inside the manipulation of your mischief. Your movie would be called \"nudity\", \r\n  My play \"obscurity\". I\'m writing down your omnisexuality towards earth \r\n  since I will never see again. I can only be distracted by pop culture and strange \r\n  rumors of paradise. I can be completely floored by your screaming solipsism. \r\n  Suburbia contains us, like we are victims of some underachieving voodoo. It \r\n  can\'t be your warm skin that spells weirdness. I will come back to you.</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\" color=\"#990033\">â€œ \r\n  Sometimes people having breakdown problems can look very beautiful because they \r\n  have that fragile something to the way they move or walk. They put on a mood \r\n  that makes then more beautiful. â€ Andy Warhol</font></p>'),(1435,'<P> Im suicidal.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> just kidding, really...I am just out of toilet paper.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> This is a test..(long beep)..</P>\r\n\r\n<P> If anyone would like to fedEX me some tp, please do so at the following coordinates...\r\n(alcohol would be much apreciated as well.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> green, 1201 Blaine Apt 54, RIverside, CA 92507</P>\r\n\r\n<P> thanx its been a shit day.</P>\r\n'),(1436,'<p>dear dark,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am finding myself caught off-guard by you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are moments<br>\r\nwhen my normal capacity for functioning ceases to exist<br>\r\nwhile near you</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all things slow to stop motion. freeze frame.<br>\r\ni see you now, talking</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but also the you,<br>\r\nseconds ago....doing that thing you did.<br>\r\nan ordinary action, dissolving into fluid energy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>breath</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is beyond me.<br>\r\nas the details click over in my mind....one, two, three<br>\r\ni will not forget this.<br>\r\nthe you of a moment ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i ask myself, does this mean anything?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my imagination on overdrive.<br>\r\nit is all,<br>\r\nonly<br>\r\nin my head.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>revving</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you make me think strange thoughts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>--->>law of physics<<---</p>\r\n\r\n<p>such as the potential for one very average human<br>\r\nto change<br>\r\na simple kiss</p>\r\n\r\n<p>into,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>radioactive<br>\r\nhot<br>\r\ndripping<br>\r\nhoney</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is there a way?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think<br>\r\ni can show you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>sincerely,</i><br>\r\n~kat</p>'),(1437,'<P>A certain MsLazarus told me: \"One time I dreamed I was a spokes person for pain, like it was some kind of product you could buy, and all I did was get tortured and beat, emotionally and physically, but I had all this money, and I was famous, and I woke up not knowing what it meant, maybe that some things just aren?t worth being noticed\"\r\n\r\n<P>In return I told her that once I dreamed of a girl whose parents where killed in a plane crash. So she became this anti-flight crusader. All I could really visually remember was her \"Anti Flight\" shirt and how sad she was when she won and the whole world ceased flight.  \r\n'),(1438,'<center><pre>\r\nDigit  Occurrences  Proportion\r\n    0            8       2.34%\r\n    1           54      15.79%\r\n    2           73      21.34%\r\n    3           58      16.96%\r\n    4           48      14.04%\r\n    5           34       9.94%\r\n    6           17       4.97%\r\n    7           28       8.19%\r\n    8            6       1.75%\r\n    9           16       4.68%\r\nTotal          342     100.00%\r\n</pre>\r\n</center>\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1438_1.jpg\'>\r\n</center>'),(1439,'<br>\r\nA sound<br>\r\nlost in the roar of the applause<br>\r\nlost in the humming of machines<br>\r\ncarried away home on bird wings.<br>\r\nA ricochet, etched in the sky,<br>\r\nfloating clouds I watch<br>\r\nfrom rooftop perches and midtown parapets,<br>\r\nstolen out from work-day moments.<br>\r\nA chance to dream,<br>\r\nto dream, to seize<br>\r\nownership of what is mine<br>\r\n<br>'),(1440,'Tenderloin morning--<br>\r\ntrucks barrel past the window.<br>\r\nCoffee, I\'m reborn.<br>'),(1441,'<PRE>\r\ngears whirrr\r\n\r\ncranks turn\r\n\r\nfulcrums lever\r\n\r\ncables tighten\r\n\r\nslower every day\r\n\r\noil helps\r\n\r\na little\r\n\r\nbut things wear\r\n\r\nparts drop off\r\n\r\nuse is abuse\r\n\r\npaint builds up\r\n\r\nmultiple coats\r\n\r\nparts become obselete\r\n\r\njoints gum up\r\n\r\nnewer robots\r\n\r\ngo faster\r\n\r\nlonger\r\n\r\nt.f.w.o.\r\n</pre>'),(1442,'<p><font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	why are you always making <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1438.txt#6095\">unrealistic, high-fallutin and fantastical exclamations</a> to your man-mates?<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	\"run away with me, my prince!\"<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	pillow talk, baby<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	doesn\'t it feel cheap?<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	why would it<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	talk is cheap...<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	and?<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	oh, i don\'t know... it\'s romantic, but knowing you it feels like a sort of mockery of romance.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	im feeling romantic today<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	yah. talk is cheap.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	romance is cheap<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	NO IT\'S NOTT!!!<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	not real romance<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	what is real romance<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	not when you\'re not faking it and sarcastic all the time<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	im not sure where youre coming from here<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	REAL. REAL FEELING... HONEST TO GOD REAL EMOTION. GUT-WRENCHING EMOTION<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	is romance real emotion?<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	yes. i\'m saying real romance is when you\'re truly convinced. when you honestly feel something, at least most of the time, and it\'s no joke<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	like Milan Kundera... the chance aspect<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	wait. develop this.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	you mean, i shouldnt say, \"run away with me?\" on chat unless im ready to get on the boat tonight?<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	what does this have to do with milan kundera<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	be careful of metaphors, for they give birth to love?<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	yes, i\'m saying that when you say too many things that you don\'t totally mean, it cheapens the reality that may be there<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	unless you\'re both on the same rhetorical plane, i guess<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	it dilutes, perhaps, more than it cheapens<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	and it is sort of like \"an emotional parachute.\"  You don\'t have to be serious all the time, but I guess it disturbs me a little to hear you always undercut your feelings, and back away from them, and say \"this\'ll all be over soon\" when I know you\'re singin a very different, and very convincing tune in the presence of your lover.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	i dont sing no tune<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	sure you do<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	one of the reasons the menfolk like you so much is you make them feel loved<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	but the tune i sing is like \"you are so cute\" and \"this is so fun\"<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	but i genuinely feel the affection so?<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	one of the reasons i think he is my match is that he likes to make up fairy tales<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	the same \"rhetorical plane\" if you will<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	Yes. i understand that. <br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	it\'s just that idea of making someone feel special, loved, wanted... and then having it all just end up being a lot of hot air and performance art.<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	I know you love me. We go way back. I don\'t worry too much about it.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	fuck<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	youre blowing my mind right now<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	i guess i\'m just in a mood today to talk about what\'s what.<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	truth is a hard thing to come by in love. <br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	i think the thing is that i get carried away in the moment<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	and i get all excited and dripping between the legs<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	and then life goes on </ma joad><br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	you\'re Dean Moriarty<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	you inspire me. i get disenchanted when our spontaneous plans, like going to Mexico, can\'t be made real... when I think about how long the car trip will be<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	and youve trained me to get the milk in the fridge before dawn and wake up and make breakfast for My Man<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	i\'m always trying to achieve this balance between spontaneous joy and pragmatic satisfaction.<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	it\'s a hard balance to strike.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	but balance is the goal<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">jayper00</font>:	balance. the scales. Libra.<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">egglit</font>:	fuckin libra.</p>\r\n'),(1443,'<p>Today would have been a great day for stream-of-consciousness writing, but I can\'t quite type that fast.  You\'ll just have to take my word that I thought of these things in exactly this order.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had a dream last night that I was a jilted lesbian.  I kept trying to remember if there had been any signs leading to the breakup.  Then there was something about my gynecologist offering to do a quick procedure to make my toes straighter so that they would look good in sandals.  It involved stiches, but I <b>really</b> wanted pretty feet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I woke up, my toes felt a little funny, and I had the burning urge to write angsty lesbian prose/poetry, despite the fact that I am neither angsty nor a lesbian.  (I do however spend lots of time hanging out with the pot-smoking, Wiccan, bongo-playing lesbian couple that lives in the apartment downstairs.) Here\'s what I came up with (it\'s not a particularly good poem, but it leads me to another point, so follow along):<blockquote>\r\n\r\n<font color=\"ecru\">I climb back into bed with her after my shower, like I do every morning.  There\'s something almost sweet about the smell of her sleep sweat.  She instinctively wraps her arms around me.  She\'s not yet awake enough to remember that she doesn\'t love me any more.</font>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n(I warned you...it wasn\'t that great)  Then, when I was at work today, for whatever reason, I was thinking about my lesbian poem and poems in general when I remembered something from a college class I took many years ago.  William Carlos Williams once said that a poem is a machine made out of words.  I don\'t remember the context of the statement, but it kind of upsets me, so I modified my poem and dedicated it to WCW (as proof that you can throw a wrench into a poem without compromising its mechanics or its meaning).<blockquote>\r\n<font color=\"ecru\">I climb back into bed with her after my shower, like I do every morning.  There\'s something almost sweet about the smell of her sleep sweat.  She instinctively wraps her arms around me.  She\'s not yet awake enough to remember that <img src=\"http://www.infoblvd.net/tribadv/gutwrench.gif\" width=\"20\" height=\"21\"> she doesn\'t love me any more.</font></blockquote></p>\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe I overthink things a bit.</p>'),(1444,'<P>Why am i one? Or why even....bleh. In the car ,on the road , seat reclined and out of my mouth spill small treasons towards a triangle of his friends. I do not dig them. So, knowing I am being rude, I go on...knowing if he did the same with MY friends I would flip out and let no more be said.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"YOu know, I never say anything......just deal with it.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"I know, I know, \" I say hurriedly.\"i.m being a bitch okay...\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there are some people i love. And some i just can\'t stand. some i dont really care about. there are a few i will die for. there are those that slowly sneak and crawl silently into your heart. there are those that you pass by, just faces made of nothing, shadows bouncing off walls and we ignore what COULD be there. Or  maybe it ignores us.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I cant like everyone. and i wont. i wont deal with it because there is nothing to deal. one cant swallow something that tastes bad. why would one want to do that? ever. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Oh well. now i think we have hit another bump in relationship road. but hopefull, we will get up and over. riding things out is not healthy ive learned. being blind and holding the hands behind the back can only work for so long. until someone claps and we rock tight and wide awake. and realise things were never as they seem.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>never</P>'),(1445,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe are lying in bed (fully clothed) and she is worried that she might make it home late- she says she is supposed to call her parents- she says that her father is sick, that he has leukemia-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nI say that I am sorry- I say that my father died last January-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nShe says that she is sorry and asks how it happened and I say that he had a heartattack (and I leave out the messy stuff: the driveway, the cpr, the ever nagging feeling that it is my fault)- she says that although her father is sick, he is getting better, and that he will make a recovery-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nI\'m say good and I feel good for her.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nShe is laying on her side facing me lying on my stomach and her head is prompt up on her hand- I keep noticing how her lips come together when she talks and I keep noticing how her hair is exploding everywhere over her face and her hand holding up her head, and I\'m noticing all those almond streaks over the black, and I realize that I love her hair-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd I tell her, you know, I say, I love your hair, you have really nice hair-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd she says, thanks, but I have too much.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nThis is at about eight- in the morning, on a Sunday- I have been up since the morning previous, for the party and the preparing for the party-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nThe party, on a Saturday- it was my birthday, but not (underlined) a birthday party, just a party that happened to fall on my birthday- but she showed up early with her roommate who is my friend as well and they, along with my roommates, sang me happy birthday with a cake and candles- it was very sweet-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd during this party I was busy the whole time- spinning house records in our basement for people to dance to and talk over, and this was for around five hours- slowly getting drunk and not realizing it and missing out on the only thing that I really wanted to do, and that was talk to her.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd I tell her this, back in bed- in bed, after the hair comment, I say, you know, I was kinda disappointed, cause the only thing I wanted to do was talk to you, and I really didn\'t get a chance to, although you did give me a hug that one time, and I think that was the highlight of my night I think-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd she laughs.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd really, really, I know I\'m blowing it- I know I\'m giving away too much information too quickly and I\'m showing my hand too quickly and fuck!- fuck, why do I always have to be high when we\'re alone?.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nYeah, high- I was high- not as high as before, but- god.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd I tell her this, still in bed- I tell ask her (rhetorically), I tell her- why do I always have to be high when I\'m around you, I\'m always making an ass out of myself-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd she laughs- she says, the other day?, playing trivial pursuit?, you were on e?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd I laugh too and I say, haha, no- board games and ecstasy, not really a big thing- and we smile together (pause)- although, sometimes, sometimes I like to smoke a little crystal and stay up all night and play with jigsaw puzzles- and she laughs and I smile- smile, cause I made her laugh and smile, and smile cause she\'s beautiful when she smiles, and smile because I am high and smiley.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd earlier, before- after the records, and I am drunk and upstairs, and most people have left and we are saying our goodbyes to the few that remain and that are not staying the night, and she is there and I am drunk and, I don\'t know- I\'m having a hard time remembering this part,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nUmm, let\'s see:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nI remember smoking a cigarette and we are talking and we are outside and it is a little cold and we are alone and somehow the trivial pursuit comes up again and I say, I\'m not really big on trivial pursuit cause I\'m not that good at it and it makes me feel dumb cause I\'m not that good at it and it\'s one of those thinkie games and when you\'re not good at one of those thinkie game it means that you\'re stupid, right?,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nLike scrabble I say, scrabble is another one of those thinkie games- scrabble makes me feel like a four year old- but the other night, that was fun, I had fun the other night-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nAnd, hmm- sometime around there my roommate was on the phone trying to get pizza delivered to us and I was starting to sober up and everyone was pretty much laying around conking out and I\'m sobering up and eventually do and then it\'s then!- it\'s then that I decide is the perfect moment to take a pill and get real high-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nThis was about 4:00am-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n4:35am I am sunshine and explosions and I am running around the house trying to find ok computer and kind of blue on cds so I can listen to no surprises and blue in green because they are just oh so beautiful and,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she is laying on the floor with her head cradled in her hand, her hair sprawled out about tan shag all around her- and she is falling asleep and I lean down and say, are you sleepy?, you can sleep in my room- and she looks up, smiles and thinks about it for a second- and she smiles and she nods and says thanks and disappears-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand that\'s it for a while.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand that\'s it for a while- for a while after that it is me high and awake and everything ever so beautiful and up watching the sun almost up and a couple other people drinking their beers nursing their comedowns and me up and up and up-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand walking around maybe?- maybe walking around the streets of colma and talking with two of my roommates and some of their friends and some how ending up walking around the parking lot of a police station (and this probably isn\'t a good idea, let\'s leave) and walking somemore and talking about housemusic and san francisco and her-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand walking and the sun is coming up eventually and, well- she gave me a hug, what does that mean?, and she kept coming down to look through my record collection, what does that mean?, and, well, I don\'t know, I think she likes me,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she was drunk or high or something and she gave me a hug and asked me if I like the birthday card and I said yes and she smiled and looked happy and apologized for not being more creative and I said that\'s ok and she asked (seriously), hoping that I didn\'t think she was boring- she asked (and she was completely serious), you don\'t think I\'m boring, do you?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand ofcourse not!- I don\'t know, I don\'t think she\'s boring at all, not at all!- not at all!- if she\'s anything, she\'s the most exciting thing that has happened to me since I moved here, and, I don\'t know,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwhat does that mean?- do you think she likes me?- I mean she gave me a card for my birthday and later asked me if I thought that she was boring because of the card and she asked because (maybe?) she was seriously worried about whether or not I thought she was boring, you know?, I mean, I don\'t know, wouldn\'t you think that that would mean that she likes me?, I mean, atleast a little?, yeah?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'yeah, I guess- well- I don\'t know, I mean- I guess it could go either way, couldn\'t it?- I mean, she was drunk or whatever right?-\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nyeah, I guess- I don\'t know, I mean, being high, well, I mean- that doesn\'t create feelings, does it?- I mean, doesn\'t it just make one more apt to act on feelings that they already have?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'sure- I guess- I don\'t know-\'</p>'),(1446,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Danger Slut came without an \r\n  instruction book. <br>\r\n  There was no manual on how to keep things exciting with her.<br>\r\n  She expected me to be a damn psychic. <br>\r\n  Without Communication there was no saving me.<br>\r\n  We were not inside intensity. I did not admire her <a href=\"http://cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=2132701652/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/ArtistID=WAINWRIGHT*RUFUS/ITEMID=1379160\">Poses</a> \r\n  . <br>\r\n  I\'m <a href=\"http://www.crosswinds.net/~estellawarren/alone.jpg\">alone</a> \r\n  but I adore her still. Always looking for another chance at the <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B00005BCJO/qid=996231899/sr=1-4/ref=sc_d_4/104-5615694-4667928\">Big \r\n  O</a>. </font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1446_1.jpg\'></p>'),(1447,'<p>I must look like a crystal head, sitting here on the subway, madly bobbing my head to this Bukem tune, totally picking up the rhyme that MC Conrad is putting down.  Maybe if it weren\'t 6:30am and I didn\'t just get off a nine hour shift then this would not be the case.</p>\r\n<p>My \"work shirt,\" stained with grease, is a like a picture telling others that I either work a shitty job or live on the streets.  The Palm Pilot I write this on tells people that I am a geeky university student who couldn\'t get a computer job for the summer and is now either working a shitty job or living on the streets.  Or, at least, that\'s what I\'d like them to think.  I think I\'d like to know what they are thinking.  Sadly, my mind-reading skills aren\'t up to snuff.</p>'),(1448,'it\'s been said that until you can laugh at yourself you can\'t laugh at all.  gather round children i\'m going to tell you a story of how i laughed at myself last night amidst the most awful of circumstances.  <P>i will caution some of you more \'civilized\' folk here at swinney.org that this true and silly tale (borderline immature) of mine should not be taken as high art or serious and biting social commentary.  you guys need a reason to ignore simple people like myself, or at least i need to think that i know why you avoid simple people like myself, so after i relay this little experience i will work with the notion that \"they read my post, felt i was too banal for them and therefore i will be forever given the raised eyebrow of contempt\".  o joy, the knowledge of self.<P>now with sarcasm expelled i shall continue with my little theme.<P><B><I>how do you do, my name is shitbreak</I></B><P>yesterday my wife and i celebrated our 5th anniversary.  with possible divorce looming we decided that a simple dinner out would suffice.  as to avoid deeply pensive discussion i chose a bar known for it\'s live jazz, i figured that if i\'m going to be uncomfortable it might as well be set to good music.  we arrived to find the dining area almost empty but the bar was quite populated with the denizens of the old city.  \'miles runs the voodoo down\' played on the stereo, the place was filled with lovelies and i knew that the night would be fine.  we sat down, ordered drinks and talked randomly about recent events and other topics of a non-combative nature until our attractive and curvaceous waitress offered up the specials and we placed our order.  we continued to reside in our mutual silence while we drank (in the quiet hope that the booze would take us over like all good booze is supposed to) and listened to the jazz drift in and out of the other room.  our salads arrived and we ate, the vinegerette was incredibly sour.  the crew in the other room started to pick up steam and we listened, the gentleman playing bass was slamming a solo.  our waitress in the tight dress made sure we were never out of drink and i thanked her repeatedly for that.  our food arrived just as the crew in the next room was improvising and then suddenly careened into \'my funny valentine\' like a bad train wreck.  such a rampant lack of respect for this song made me angry.  in retrospect it was the defining moment, the beginning of the end to what <I><B>was</B></I> going to be an ok evening.  <P>the meal was outstanding, i had venison with bÃ©arnaise sauce, horseradish and dijon mustard mashed potatoes and steamed broccoli.  my wife had chicken with a cajun roux-creole sauce.  we ate wonderful food, drank excessively, enjoyed jazz often played twice as fast as it should have been and i did my best to control my wandering eyes and keep them away from our waitress\'s ample rear end.  we talked more about things of no particular importance, clanked our glasses together toasting unusual things and people alike and finished our meal.  we declined our waitress\'s offer of desert, ordered one more round of drinks and decided we would head home.<P>after paying our bill (and my fat tip to our waitress with the bedroom eyes) we spilled out into the street.  we walked among the fine folks in the old city enjoying the night air and free-form jazz floating about and found our car.  feeling the effects of house wine and bourbon i chose to take it easy, \"why don\'t we take the long way home?\"  i couldn\'t have made a more disastrous mistake.<P>my feeling was if i took the long way home i would avoid the interstate and more importantly the police i would surely encounter.  i also thought the \'back way\' would be quiet, i could drive a little slower, enjoy the night air, clear my head and listen to tom waits croon about how \'jesus is gonna be here soon\'.  my wife could sleep in the passenger seat as she often does and we would have an uneventful ride home to some playful drunken slumber.<P>i made it out of the city with ease.  i was relaxed, the streets were empty for the most part, i drove through campus and it too was quiet.  it was about 10 minutes into our ride that i knew something was wrong.<P>it first felt like i had been shot in the stomach and then shot in the ass simultaneously, one of those \'out of nowhere\' pains that curls your toes and makes you be as still as possible.  i quickly became aware of my breathing, it had changed from slow and drunken to quick and alert.  as all of you know, with great and sudden pain comes great and sudden clarity.  i layed as straight as i could, i adjusted the seat as low and as far back as possible as to accommodate my frame\'s new and very rigid state.  i clenched my ass and tightened my stomach as another pain hit me with such force that i felt for sure my toes would bust right through my semi-vintage green suede puma sneakers.  i breathed deep several times to get my bearings and rationalize what could be going wrong within me.  i felt sweat on my brow and i braced for what i knew would be another shot to the mid-section.  what came next was not a pain, at least not like the previous, this was more an explosion within my lower stomach.  in my belly i felt a mass expansion, a quick gaseous bloat, then a quiver and then an eruption that sent my ass a memo.  the memo read:<P><CENTER><B>prepare thyself you fool, for there is about to be a quick and great exodus that will leave you weak and shivering like a child</B></CENTER><P>my ass told me to pay attention to this particular memo by letting out a loud noise (the first of many) followed by a strong odor of importance.  when it comes to orifices sending memo\'s to one\'s self <B>i tend to take note</B> and so i slammed the accelerator to the floor and drove like the wind (albeit a slightly drunken, foul smelling wind).  my guts continued to make arrangements for the release by talking it up with my ass:<P><U><B>GUTS</B></U>: hey ass, i\'m sending you some more nastiness and filth, give me a yell when you have room for it.<P><U><B>ASS</B></U>: alrighty, i\'m doing the best i can but this fucker has been working out and he\'s got me on lockdown, how about giving him a little incentive to lighten up... i\'ve got a plan.  if you can grab his attention long enough for me to spit a little we can get rid of this garbage a whole lot quicker<P><U><B>GUTS</B></U>: no problem, i\'ll detonate another h-bomb right below his ribcage that will send him into shock and you can do your thing... on the count of 3 ok?<P><U><B>ASS</B></U>: ok, on the count of 3<P><U><B>GUTS</B></U>: 1, 2......<P>at this point i\'m praying to any deity that will listen to me in the hopes that i don\'t have one of those moments that will haunt me forever and cost a lot in dry cleaning and fabric stain remover.  the pain was incredible, i was unsure of what to do.  \"people go to hospitals for not letting it go\", i thought to myself.  then i thought \"i\'ll be god-damned if i\'m showing up in some hospital in this condition... i\'ll take my chances with death at home\", my legs got the jimmy-shakes at that point and it became hard to keep them still.  i was clearly driving as fast and as well as a drunken fool could drive but i wasn\'t sure if it was enough.  i was taking turns at speeds that would have proved all my physics teachers wrong, i was making plans of my escape from the car when i finally rolled into the driveway, i was measuring in my head the amount of steps it is to the front door, i was picturing the silver key in my hand entering the key hole and the reckless abandon i would show by stepping on the cat if he was in my way onwards to the bathroom.  there was cause now for great control and i was almost without.  my guts and ass were working together now to destroy me as i sped through our neighborhood.  my house, i saw my house... driveway, i saw my driveway.... i worried i would end up hitting my 73 VW bus, the one i haven\'t finished restoring yet, when i slammed the breaks and ran from the door into the house.  it was a chance i had to take and i layed into the breaks hard leaving marks 4feet in length up my driveway.  with ass clenched and ready to explode, with odor most foul practically dripping from my rear i sprinted from car to porch the keys met the lock my feet hit the foyer.  my subversive suburban ass was moving steadily towards the bathroom when another pain caught me from behind and, as if speaking through me, i let out a howl that scared the small animals dining on my dogs food outside.  i hit the bathroom, slammed into the wall to slow my motion and then fell on the porcelain receptacle that would be my friend for the duration of my discomfort.<P>from there on out it was pure body chemistry gone horribly wrong and the stuff of great teen comedy.  legs in air the initial blast lifted me up and slammed me down again and i grabbed for anything i could hold onto, subsequently breaking the TP rod and tearing pages of my worn o-reilly linux admin book.  my ass and guts had failed in their quest to ruin my clothes and car seat so they took vengeance on my person for the next 15-20 minutes.  it was painful, it was funny, it was all so disgusting, that waitress was so attractive.<P>i fully recovered almost 3 hours and 4 trips to the head later, i slept for a few hours and then was up.  my body was in complete harmony, my ass was apologetic and my guts... well, my guts didn\'t talk to me.<P>it\'s friday, laugh at my misfortune because i learned how to and because i shared it with you and i want you all to smile if only once today.  did i enlighten?  no.  did i provoke?  no.  did i steal a few minutes from your day?  i might have.  ok, now you have a reason to ignore the simple folks like me who enjoy all of your creativity, i gave you a foul and crude tale of temporary pain, relevant to nobody and with no major significance but i like you all and there is nothing you can do about it.'),(1449,'Her scent was awful, bending over me with those huge spectacles resting on the last rounded bump of her nose.  With each word she appeared to be osculating with some invisible object, stretching and contorting her lips to emphasize her question.  \"Did you lose your mommy?\"  No, I thought.  She\'s lost me.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe relationship I have with my mother is the coolest thing I\'ve ever known.  She\'s fun, she\'s beautiful, and we cackle and cuss about men and officework while we smoke cigarettes, one after another, in the garage.  We discuss sex, the opposite sex, oral sex, psychological sex.  We\'re both self-proclaimed nymphomaniacs.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt occurs to me sometimes that I feel as though I\'ve watched her grow up. With my father... well, us kids were always well aware that they weren\'t exactly functional.  Actually, when they sat us down for the big \'divorce discussion\', we were pretty much just relieved.  We\'d all just been waiting for them to realize what they had to do.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWhen I was littler, I used to lose my mother all the time in stores.  I was too curious, she used to say.  I started to lose her less and less as I grew up, and now I feel like I\'m finally starting to find her.'),(1450,'<p>\r\n@zoli\'s.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsess is best after two long days at work. five long weeks. wanting to do something considerably strained by the energy levels. low levels. already too much coffee. already too much sustinance. already out of ideas. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbehind me some nobody on the toronto jungle scene is spinning hard dnb. its the good shit you dont fuck with. it isnt anyone in the scene plays all that often. or when they do they play it out and make it boring and horrible and then have the gaul to spin the remix. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni got out of the scene here about four years ago. it got depressing. the music was going downhill and so were the antics of the dj\'s who live around here. they became so focused on one sound that they all began playing the same records. they\'d change it up a bit. kids around town would talk of a dj having \"krust\'s sound\". the dj\'s here copied to a certain degree a uk artists feel by playing all the tracks that a uk artist put out. it was strange. i dont know that it matters if they did it on purpose or not; they did it. \r\nthis guy behind me isnt one of those. its the shit you dont fuck with. \r\n</p>'),(1451,'<p>i was introduced to you in a crappy apartment in \'95<br>\r\nin the greystone on the corner of 91st street and broadway<br>\r\nin manhattan<br>\r\nthe city that knew you first<br>\r\nand i admired and slightly adored your beauty then<br>\r\nbut i was busy being 22 years old<br>\r\nand you know how those things go<br><br>\r\nand here in the swamp of now<br>\r\nof trying to say or do<br>\r\nsomething that matters<br>\r\ni am swept perchance<br>\r\ninto a swarming nest<br>\r\nof pure feeling<br>\r\nof the most perfect part<br>\r\nof my being<br>\r\nin your sweet honey tone<br>\r\nand i weep that you are gone<br>\r\nthat you\'re not still here<br>\r\nto evoke and amaze and liberate<br>but o for o<br>\r\nwhat legacy you left<br>\r\nwhat magic and complete<br>\r\nstuff is that bi-generational legacy<br>\r\n<br>\r\nfreeway like a river<br>\r\nswallowed your voice and your fingers<br>\r\non a night of who knows<br>\r\nwhat was going through your mind<br>\r\nwhen you made that leap<br>\r\nand it\'s so sad<br>\r\nlike few pains i have ever allowed<br>\r\nit\'s so sad<br>\r\nand the irony is you are<br>\r\nthat much more everything<br>\r\nthat you are gone<br>\r\nthat you are somewhere still<br>\r\nand you know that <br>\r\nthis night you are my<br>\r\nsole companion<br>\r\nlover, idol, and eternal<br>\r\nfriend<br>\r\n<br>\r\nand it\'s such a melancholy sweetness<br>\r\nto miss you like this</p>'),(1452,'<P>see that you are sleep in covered with blankets\r\nmumbles from mouth and I cracked out</P>\r\n\r\n<P>had too much caffeine at the Beach today\r\nwhere she and I spent the day looking for</P>\r\n\r\n<P>matching friendship rings</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>She is off to law school and I am off to two more years of UNDERGRAD studies</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I have a mouthful of words to spill out and show you, and hold inside a million moving pieces of the day I\'d like to share with you</P>\r\n\r\n<P>how they wish to be let forth.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>I want you as awake and jitter-handed as I.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I need to hug you and kiss you and make sure the red that bled into the white of your eyes was only due to sleep and not another</P>\r\n\r\n<P>unappetizing reason.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I hope you have good dreams and not the usual.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Where I leave you for someone and vice-versa. No,you will not find men hidden in my closet when you come home, mouth pretty and alone.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Yours to own.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Two thirty and I cannot close my eyes in truth.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Two thirty and I want to shove the sleep right off from you, or maybe a compromise...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>lend me your tired</P>\r\n\r\n<P>unwaking, yearning to be (a) sleep.</P>'),(1453,'<p><b>Whatâ€™s happening here:</b> <i>I know that in general folks like to use swinney.org for posting journal-like ruminations, abstract thoughts about daily events, poems about breaking up and getting together, so-called \\\"porny\\\" commentary, and so on, but I thought Iâ€™d take a slightly different tack for awhile. Once upon a time I wrote fiction. Then a few things came up and well, I never got back to it. So I thought Iâ€™d give it a stab again with a series of short prose experimentsâ€”exercises to get me back in shape, basically. Hereâ€™s the first. Bear with me if you can....</i></p><p>\r\n\r\nWeâ€™re all wired in here, hardwired that is, fixed into our seats by inertia, too-deep thoughts, the loss of love, lack of imagination, whatever. And outside the world outside spins and trembles and strobes and general chaos reigns. Weâ€™re calmly waiting for something to show itself, some direction to the night, but no conclusive signs have come in.</p><p>\r\n\r\nIâ€™m vibrating in my favorite corner, under the table lamp that reminds me of my grandmother, working on the second double depth charge of the evening. The first cup was the bestâ€”like sipping power right out of a nine-volt battery. Sometimes I need that first cup to get my drawing hand going, to unlock the bonds that often seem to hold my pen back. I can almost sit back and let the sketches flow out. Tonight my drawing hand and I have focused upon Lia: our pagan barista priestess, our exalted Patron Saint of Java Buzz. Weâ€™ve especially enjoyed that tattooâ€”you know the oneâ€”the serpent skeleton, in jade and aqua blue and black, that winds up her left arm, across her shoulders, and down her right. My drawing hand sketches each individual vertebra lovingly, caressingly. Itâ€™s a way to pass the evening while we wait, we suppose.</p><p>\r\n \r\nThere was an incident a bit earlier. It was just past the hour of the vampire girls, our dark flowers, who tend to drop by for a quick bit of tea and a smoke or two before hitting the clubs. Sultry black velvet dahlias, tall sprigs of night-blooming jasmine, even a few rare corpse blossoms: they make a pretty bouquet for their Friday midnight mass. Iâ€™d managed to catch a couple in the sketchbook before they ran off, and was still filling out the ragged edges when Lia dropped by to collect my first glass.  She paused for a quick glance. â€œOh, I didnâ€™t know you were into the goth babes,â€ she said.</p><p>\r\n\r\nBefore I could come up with a reply, a forty-ounce rocketed through the doorway, past my right ear (whoosh), to the floor about fifteen feet beyond my table, where it shattered in a spectacular explosion of glass and lager. Folks looked up, chess games came to an abrupt halt. Maybe a jaw or two dropped. This kind of thing generally happens in the bar next door.</p><p>\r\n\r\nâ€œAw, motherfuck, this again,â€ Lia muttered, and set my empty back down.</p><p>\r\n\r\nThus began another series of events, strangely typical in their atypicality (it is Friday night in the Lower Haight after all). The bottle was apparently intended for a trembling hysterical frat-boy type, who now cowered in the corner by the payphone, shouting something about gangstas across the street and calling a friend to bring him a gun. Lia handled everything effortlessly. She calmed the guy down, got some cops in, they took a statement, and then the kid fled to whatever Pac Heights cubbyhole heâ€™d escaped from. Nobody really knew or cared who the bottle thrower was; by the time the kid left Iâ€™m sure even the chessplayers wouldâ€™ve lobbed a glass or two at him if that wouldâ€™ve shut him up.</p><p>\r\n\r\nAnd now itâ€™s another hour before closing time. Iâ€™ve closed the book for now, dropped the pen. Liaâ€™s at the counter, cleaning up and talking motorcycles with Raechelle, a local patron that she seems toâ€™ve taken a fancy to. But Iâ€™m not really listening; Iâ€™m just watching Lia pull out a heavy jug of chai and pour a pint for Rachelle. As she pours, her biceps flex. And as her biceps flex, that voodoo snake seems to coil tight, as if preparing to strike. Raechelle doesnâ€™t seem to notice, sheâ€™s too busy chatting about her oh-so-powerful 750cc engine. But I do. And I think: now this nightâ€™s secret motives have become clear.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1453_1.jpg\\\"></p>'),(1454,'<p>I have to ask myself how many cones I can take out on the road to self-improvement.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Why do I hurt her?  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Why, thirty seconds later, does the guilt and horror hit me like a Texas heat wave?  Why not before I do it?  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is it some mislaid assumption of guaranteed affection?  Thirty seconds later I realize that it may really be a very shaky precipice I\'m treading on.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m working on it, though.  I\'ve told her this.  I suppose it might be more than some have done.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>For now, I\'ll sit here and fiend for her, and wish that apologies and explanations really could fix things.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wonder:  Can we get just a little more heat over here?  I\'m not feeling it yet, God.</p>'),(1455,'<p>i have been walking scattered paths of humanity. the best thing for foggy Sunday mornings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>flea market manic... bazarre of life<br>\r\nwhatever you want we got it!<br>\r\n[just a little bit bruised...if you dont mind]</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i took a new friend. i always seem to have new friends. he seemed preoccupied, not a lot of talking took place. even before the crush of broken mass merchandise, farmers finds, and lost art.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>breakfast was more like a test of patience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we sit, one across from the other. intimate eggs and toast. but he wanted to talk to the waitress \'friend\' more than me. i dont mind talking to others...i enjoy discovering what there may be to discover, but if you ask to be with me then at least be there some of the time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was more excited about my purchase of tattered prehistoric Chinese travel books in foreign languages.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i could live at the flea market.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh wait...i have lived at the flea market. dad took us here back in \'74.<br>\r\nand all the years in between.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we never went on family vacations....we just hit the drive-in. </p>'),(1456,'I had fun this weekend. I set out to see one show, in Atlanta GA, put on by my personal hero, Mr. Trey Anastasio. We decided---dude. Raleigh. We go. Tire failure! Stop. Spare. Spare low! Late at night! Ask directions. Store closed, faces at window, will they? Yes! $30! Too late for show. Call work. Invent vehicle malfunction, can\'t make it. Fuel pump. Happens all the time. Should we? Maryland. Never been there. Massive rain. Morale dropping, visibility zero. Press on. Sun! Warm! Trees! Brownies! Good show, tired, sleep, drive, home. Swinney.'),(1457,'<p>...As she gazed deviously into my eyes, her lips formed an inviting smile as she cooed softly while playfully grabbing me by the tie and pulling me closer toward her presence...</P>\r\n<p>Some of the many suddenly reoccuring memories that carry no negative connotation deserve to be cherished and shared, for they motivate us to create more the next day</P>'),(1458,'<P>Ever found yourself, in the middle of the woods, waiting, waiting, with this crrazy psycho girl with 4 personalities and two hippies?  We can only kick hackysac until dark.  The crrrazy psycho girl is now talking to herself.  We met her at the store actually, she was sucking on a vial of some kind of flower extract.  She swore it was not acid, but declined to share it with anyone.  Crrrazy!  I grew up on sesame street...\r\n</p><P>\r\nShe trips the fuck out all of the sudden and demands to get out of the car.  She jumps in a truck with these two redneck dudes and we get back on our way, only to encounter 4 cops at the store while stirring the sugar into my coffee.  The other hippie decdes to find a ride to arcada...   \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1459,'<P>I was day-dreaming yesterday about having a vigil in the woods, a sort of vision-quest.  My musings eventually developed into encountering a cougar or bear and being forced to kill it with my bare hands.  Rights of passage...</P><P>\r\nI had a really good friend who told me that one of the big problems with american culture is that we have no rights of passage.  We have no ceremony, no defining moment, that says, \'before you were a child, now you are an adult.\'  That\'s one of the reasons I was fantasizing about killing some dangerous creature.  I need a right or passage ceremony.  I\'m sure there\'s some \'Fight Club\' influence in there somewhere too.</P><P>\r\nI still feel like a child incapable of taking on the responsibilities of adulthood.  I remember being a little kid and seeing adults as all-knowing, all-powerful, all-benevolent beings (yeah, makes me think about the origins of gods), and now that I\'m supposed to be one of those, the reality kinda sucks.  I may be expecting too much from myself, but I feel like I keep lowering my expectations and continue to fail to reach them.  Sometimes, I feel like just giving up and accepting the fact that I\'m going to hate my job and my only respite will be the hypnogogic powers of television.  Then I realize that I\'d have to kill myself if my life degenerated to that level and I go buy a lottery ticket and sign up for another dance class.</P><P>\r\nI hate my job.  I want to be brilliant again and answering phones and making photocopies doesn\'t lend itself to expressions of brilliance.  Not doing those things doesn\'t lend itself to eating and sleeping under a roof.  I\'ve been trying to figure out a way to accomadate the various needs and desires in my life, but what it comes down to, is that I need to figure out a way to grow up.</P>'),(1460,'don\'t talk down to me. don\'t \r\nbe polite to me. don\'t \r\ntry to make me feel nice. \r\ndon\'t relax. i\'ll cut the \r\nsmile off your face. you \r\nthink i don\'t know what\'s \r\ngoing on. you think i\'m \r\nafraid to react. the joke\'s \r\non you. i\'m biding my time, \r\nlooking for the spot. you \r\nthink no one can reach you, \r\nno one can have what you \r\nhave. i\'ve been planning \r\nwhile you\'re playing. i\'ve \r\nbeen saving while you\'re \r\nspending. the game is \r\nalmost over so it\'s \r\ntime you acknowledge me. \r\ndo you want to fall not \r\neven knowing who took you? \r\n\r\n(from) inflammatory essays - jenny holzer'),(1461,'<p>everyone knows what Mark Twain said about summer in San Francisco. if you work with office people, you learn it twice a month at least. sitting by the water bubbler, \"Jeff\" or \"Rick\" will quip, \"you know what Mark Twain said...\"</p>\r\n<p>I had a sticky childhood in Massachusetts. Mosquitos throwing themselves passionately against screen doors, bloody scrapes from raspberry patch prickers, fireflies in jars, sticky hands steering bike handles to the ice cream stand, hot semen dripping down the thighs, ice cubes on the nipples before mom gets home. </p>\r\n<p>we have to fabricate summer here in the bay area by driving 2 hours east. chi chi said she wanted it to be so hot that she\'s driven to jump in the water. and she got it. and I got what i wanted. splash fights, bridge jump, farm fruit, acrobatics, hills like golden thighs, semen on the leg.</p>\r\n<p>it got hot enough that jay stopped for gatorade on the way home. austin wanted gatorade too. he said, \"I want to be water brothers.\"</p>\r\n<p>I paused to see if this was a kinky offering. Really it just meant we would drink out of the same bottle. he sipped. I sipped.</p>\r\n<p>him: \"it tastes like those icee popsicles.\"<br>\r\nme: \"it tastes like <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.showerhead.org/pepper/pepper_jack.html\">leonard\'s paintings</a>.\"</p>\r\n<p>no sunday family outing is complete in my home without a discussion of spooge. in the water I insisted that it could be used as an effective conditioner because of its many phospholipids. Chi chi objected that since it gets sticky and makes the hair stiff, it would not be as effective a hair product as horse conditioner.</p>\r\n<p> Later I was scribbling in my sketch book about the glow-in-the-dark plankton on the North Shore in MA, \"glow in the dark things the size of sperm.\" I asked, \"wait is sperm the same size as plankton?\" again, chi chi interjected. she argued that you can see plankton in the water, but you cant see sperm. I argued that semen had so many other contents and the color was so milky that you might not be able to see it wiggling around in there.</p>\r\n<p>by the time I had her riled up enough to exclaim, \"well PERSONALLy I think its OBVIOUS that plankton is larger than sperm,\" jay told us to \"quit yammerin, Im on the phone givin ashley the grocery list, goddamnit.\"</p>\r\n<p> I scribbled down some observations and curled up on uncle swinney\'s shoulder, dreamy on Dinah Washington\'s \"What a Difference a Day Makes\". I thought of what the scene looked like, the female entranced by slow jazzy strings, eyes closed, ecstatically hugging at the male\'s chest. the male staring out the window, trying not to shift too much,  thinking about motorcycles.</p>\r\n<p>a van sped by bearing the ad copy, \"we believe in magic.\"</p>\r\n<p>jay hung up and announced, \"ok you can start yelling about plankton again.\"</p>'),(1462,'<P>i sit here painting with a dead woman\'s brushes...painting skulls and shopping carts and angry words about pollution, society and consumerism and tomorrow I\'ll go to work and think a hundred more while I type and fax and put a thousand more imperatives to buy and consume and get your hair cut at Supercuts and get your coupon for a free Cheeseburger happy meal. 12 and under. Purchase necessary. While supplies last. Call 1-800 Supercuts for a store near you. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> And I strain the acrylic of this woman\'s brushes onto my canvas and wonder how it must feel to know you\'re dying, to feel the cancer in you, gnawing at your will, your cells, your soul. Thinking of the blood pumping out of your heart in vain, the nausea rising, and all the time the cells go on their sick mad race to multiply, beraying themselves, kiling yourself, with no recourse, no drug cocktail strong enough to halt the exponential excess of your own body.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI suppose the doctors lied, like the government, the churches, your neighbors, the crazy homeless man on your street corner, they all lie, I lie, and cheat, and sell haircuts on TV.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEveryone can\'t be an activist, they say, eveyone can\'t just help the poor because then everyone will be poor and who will help us then? We can\'t stop driving our cars, they say, because then the economy would suffer and the American automakers would have to build their plans in Guam or Indonesia and where would all the great American jobs go out into the great American night, blue-black and covered with the haze of a million factories and a billion leaf blowers and a billion more gray freeways streaming across the hundreds of flat acres of standing your ground and making a living and mowing the lawn? And the Tvs go on flickering and the moms go on driving and the kids keep on eating everything as seen on TV, and the cows keep on getting killed, and the trees keep on dying, and the woman on the frozen street still dies, and the factories go on being factories and the genetically engineered chickens keep laying eggs and the activists keep on protesting and the workers go on working, and I still sell haircuts on TV.</P>'),(1463,'<P>i think loneliness should be a punishment.\r\n<p>i think sometimes the west coast kids think they are cooler somehow.\r\n<p>i think lack of extraneous friend means you have actual taste.\r\n<p>i think I deserve it all. don\'t we all?\r\n<p>i am a prisoner and I don\'t like being told i can change things.\r\n<p>i am suspicious of positive thinking during moments like this.\r\n<p>i am against arrogant bastards.\r\n<p>i am against your brutal youth.\r\n<p>Sit next to me for a while, without me begging, I\'ll be your friend.\r\n<p>Keep talking.\r\n'),(1464,'My sleep schedule is all fucked up.  Ever since I\'ve been unemployed, I\'ve been going to bed at like 7AM and getting up at around 5PM.  \r\n\r\nToday, I decided to break that trend and force myself out of bed at 9:30 AM.\r\n\r\nWell, that isn\'t entirely true.. because I had other motivations such as a job interview and possibly a protest to go to.\r\n\r\nThe interview went well and we talked about the job over yummy dim sum.  I left with the feeling that the job was in the bag. \r\n\r\nI headed down to civic center to the \"BanAdobe\" rally, and didn\'t find anyone.. so I paced around.  Eventually, my phone rang and it was Darwin.  He and I debated a meeting spot for a few minutes and eventually gathered at 8th and market.\r\n\r\nSo, everything is going great until I see him trying to hand out flyers to people for the freedom of Dmitry Sklyarov.  If you haven\'t heard about this.. read <a href=http://www.planetebook.com/mainpage.asp?webpageid=165>here</a>  \r\n\r\nHe\'s asking people, \"Please take this if you care about freedom of speech\".\r\n\r\nThen he says, \"Are you concerned about free speech?\"\r\n\r\nEveryone just says, \"no thank you\".  If they are kind of enough to say that even.  Some people gave Darwin bitter stares and others just brushed him aside.  There were a few people that took the paper and didn\'t even seem to care about it.  They probably threw it away when we were out of their site.  \r\n\r\nHowever, why do people answer, \"no\", when you ask them if they care about their freedom of speech.  I understand they don\'t want the flyer, but answering \"NO\", seems just ludicrous to me.  \r\n\r\nAre we that jaded?'),(1465,'<pre>\r\nMy sleep schedule is all fucked up.  Ever since I\'ve been unemployed, I\'ve been going to bed at like 7AM and getting up at around 5PM.  \r\n\r\nToday, I decided to break that trend and force myself out of bed at 9:30 AM.\r\n\r\nWell, that isn\'t entirely true.. because I had other motivations such as a job interview and possibly a protest to go to.\r\n\r\nThe interview went well and we talked about the job over yummy dim sum.  I left with the feeling that the job was in the bag. \r\n\r\nI headed down to civic center to the \"BanAdobe\" rally, and didn\'t find anyone.. so I paced around.  Eventually, my phone rang and it was Darwin.  He and I debated a meeting spot for a few minutes and eventually gathered at 8th and market.\r\n\r\nSo, everything is going great until I see him trying to hand out flyers to people for the freedom of Dmitry Sklyarov.  If you haven\'t heard about this.. read <a href=http://www.planetebook.com/mainpage.asp?webpageid=165>here</a>  \r\n\r\nHe\'s asking people, \"Please take this if you care about freedom of speech\".\r\n\r\nThen he says, \"Are you concerned about free speech?\"\r\n\r\nEveryone just says, \"no thank you\".  If they are kind of enough to say that even.  Some people gave Darwin bitter stares and others just brushed him aside.  There were a few people that took the paper and didn\'t even seem to care about it.  They probably threw it away when we were out of their site.  \r\n\r\nHowever, why do people answer, \"no\", when you ask them if they care about their freedom of speech.  I understand they don\'t want the flyer, but answering \"NO\", seems just ludicrous to me.  \r\n\r\nAre we that jaded?\r\n</pre>'),(1466,'<pre>\r\nMy sleep schedule is all fucked up.  Ever since I\'ve\r\nbeen unemployed, I\'ve been going to bed at like 7AM\r\nand getting up at around 5PM.  \r\n\r\nToday, I decided to break that trend and force myself\r\nout of bed at 9:30 AM.\r\n\r\nWell, that isn\'t entirely true.. because I had other\r\nmotivations such as a job interview and possibly a \r\nprotest to go to.\r\n\r\nThe interview went well and we talked about the job \r\nover yummy dim sum.  I left with the feeling that \r\nthe job was in the bag. \r\n\r\nI headed down to civic center to the \"BanAdobe\" rally, \r\nand didn\'t find anyone.. so I paced around.  Eventually,\r\nmy phone rang and it was Darwin.  He and I debated a meeting \r\nspot for a few minutes and eventually gathered at 8th and \r\nmarket.\r\n\r\nSo, everything is going great until I see him trying to \r\nhand out flyers to people for the freedom of Dmitry Sklyarov.  \r\nIf you haven\'t heard about this.. read <a href=http://www.planetebook.com/mainpage.asp?webpageid=165>here</a> or <a href=http://www.boycottadobe.com/>here</a>\r\n\r\nHe\'s asking people, \"Please take this if you care about \r\nfreedom of speech\".\r\n\r\nThen he says, \"Are you concerned about free speech?\"\r\n\r\nEveryone just says, \"no thank you\".  If they are kind of enough\r\nto say that even.  Some people gave Darwin bitter stares and \r\nothers just brushed him aside.  There were a few people that took \r\nthe paper and didn\'t even seem to care about it.  They probably \r\nthrew it away when we were out of their site.  \r\n\r\nHowever, why do people answer, \"no\", when you ask them if they \r\ncare about their freedom of speech.  I understand they don\'t \r\nwant the flyer, but answering \"NO\", seems just ludicrous to me.  \r\n\r\nAre we that jaded?\r\n</pre>'),(1467,'everywhere i look there are web companies exploding, imploding, and disintegrating.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nlast year the big companies ate the little companies and this year the stock market is eating them all.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe only things that appear to do well online anymore are gambling and sex sites.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nany day now we\'ll find sex sites with spinners for people to win various types of virtual sex.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\neveryone i know in retail is suffering. sales are down 30% across the board.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni blame it all on alan greenspan and free trade with china.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni blame it on clinton and the selling of american technology.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni blame it on the spammers and crooks and hackers and virus buiders who scared the bejesus out of consumer america.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni blame myself for something, i am not sure what, but it\'s like when someone you love commits suicide, all you can think of is whether or not you could have done or said something, anything, to make it not happen.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ngoing to the mall is like watching a train wreck in slow motion.  sidewalk sales and stores that used to be so full you couldn\'t get down the aisles and now they have space, too much space.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat will we do?  how will we cope?  depression is around the corner and i don\'t know how.\r\n<br><br>\r\n'),(1468,'<P> I have walked into rooms and been met with cold stares.  I don\'t know why.  I try to remember what it was like to be a child.  When we are little, everyone loves us. We\'re so small; so cute; so delicate, fragile. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> I\'m confused. Maybe I\'ve walked into the wrong room.  The faces looked different when I was outside, looking in. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> I\'d like to turn down your loud music and let you hear this one, absolutely beautiful song. Libertango, Astor Piazzola. Or maybe Milonga Triste, Hugo Diaz. I\'d like to take your hand. I\'d like for you to dance with me.  Close your eyes and feel everything, through every step.  I\'d like to hear you breathe. </p>'),(1474,'<p>i am a beastie<br>\r\nrodential teeth and sour breath<br>\r\ni am a possum.</p>\r\n<p>have you ever seen<br>\r\ndog shit decompose outside?<br>\r\nthat\'s the sky today.</p>'),(1469,'<P>Tea-bagging\", she says, \" when he puts\r\nhis nuts on someones face or arm.\" interesting. i think.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\"you don\'t seem like a relationship kind of girl,\" he says. flailing arms and fingers about in free house rhythmn.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i guess i go to clubs too often. maybe i speak about sex too much with everyone. maybe i think.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\"everytime i hear about your relationship there is some drama goig on with some other guy or something.\" Drew plays with his bracelets.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>nah, i say. it just so happens...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\"shes never not been in a relationship,\" my roomie mentions.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>true. but thats not because i love men to death or need their company absolutely. because i dont. \r\nit just so happens i am always in one. they\'re just there. waiting for me.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\" maybe if you act single thats when you get action?\" i add.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\"but i act single...\"</p>\r\n\r\n<P>nevermind. soon i.ll be living with my man. co-ersing and what not. my mom brought groceries over without my permission and i told her i was throwing them out. of course i didnt. i.m too poor at this moment in life and if certain boys in New York dont stop rubbing it in i will be the deviant and decrepid starving girlliving on so and so\'s floor.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i guess i dont like being told that single is the way i act because i dont. if having fun is single that makes relationships look really bad. and the whole marriage thing. i suppose if ones open enough to want to talk about it and they feel ready. then its all fine.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>today there is the pair-phobia. everything in pairs is frightening. but i am sure you can do both...go clubbing and be in a relationship...the drama doesnt go away, single or not...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it.s waiting, right around the corner.</p>'),(1470,'<p>We, the young, don\'t think of death.  We may hide from it, we may ignore it, we may not have ever even come close to experiencing it in our lives, but we certainly don\'t want to think about it.  While we are children, our parents are immortals.  We don\'t even think of them as dying, they will always be there to protect us, to shield us from the cruel world.  If your parents had a brush with mortality when you were a child, it came as a frightening wakeup call.  The kind where you\'re not sure if it really happened or not and just wish to move on and hope to ignore it.  Like a waking dream that turns out to actually have happened.</p>\r\n<p>When I was a young child, my family and I used to go for bicycle rides to the cemetary every few weeks or so to water the flowers on various headstones from deceased members of our family.  These trips always used to terrify me.  I had this irrational fear of death as a child.  I was always attempting to figure out what happened after a person died.  Without the proper vocabulary to express myself, these thoughts remained internalized and snowballed into each other.  My family used to go to church every Sunday and I was subjected to the great excitement that was Sunday School.  I hated it, but it did beat sitting in a pew for an hour during a service.  I never bought into the whole religion thing because no-one sat me down and explained to me that these things they were telling me were supposed to be real.  I thought it was just some really boring storybook with no pictures and no movie that I could watch.  So I had no clear picture of an afterlife.  I don\'t think I do even now, however, I feel I have something that will do well during the interim.</p>\r\n<p>So, as a child, attempting to figure out what happens after a person dies, I could only think of a candle being snuffed out.  The sudden evaporation of a person\'s consciousness.  The fear this caused in me was heightened even more as a result of attempting to comprehend what nothingness would be like.  I recally trying to imagine what it would be like before The Big Bang (even as a young child, I believed that articular theory).  Can you comprehend nothingness even now?  Imagine the universe not existing... no planets, no stars, no chnks of matter, no atoms, nothing.  No blackness.... just the absense of existence.  Can you fathom this?  Now, imagine a small child grappling with these very same ideas.</p>\r\n<p>I was afraid of all those headstones, the only mark left from people who had lived entire lives.  I believed that they had just snapped from existance... never to return, never to continue to experience wonder and awe.  Just... gone.  I was afraid that it would happen to me some day.  I believed that life would be too short and that I would not be ready to die when it was my time.  I didn\'t want to rot in the ground along with the rest of the leftover shells.  Tiny reminders in the sometimes intricate, sometimes sentimental pieces of stone sitting in rows.  Remdinding me that everyone has to go sometime and that you can\'t continue on forever.</p>\r\n<p>If you were to ask me whether I still believe this today, I would tell you that I do not.  I decided long ago that life would be long and good and I would get what I could from it.  I no longer have an irrational fear of death but I do wish my parents to be immortal.</p>\r\n<p>Because I am young, I don\'t want to think of death.</p>'),(1471,'<p>\r\nstrange bed, restless sleep.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni spent the last four days in the forest, in british columbia, somewhere called \"mile 36\". mile 36 is a marker along one of the logging roads that cuts through the squamish nation native land reserve. about a quarter mile past the marker, we turned into a narrow path through the woods, leading to a clearing, a river, and a glacier. it\'s beautiful there. i took no pictures.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy feet started off wet, my neck started off sore. i slept more than i thought i would; i even slept through most of the pounding 5am sunrise techno. the desert keeps me awake and buzzing, the forest blankets me with sleep.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe people here are like elves. they live in trees and they have pointy ears. i\'m sure it\'s all the DMT.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmosquitos are much more aggressive than anywhere else. i am covered. they bit me in places i didn\'t know i had. can\'t stop scratching. nnnnnhh.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe are back in vancouver. my car was broken into. but i was prepared. i followed the local custom and emptied my valuables, left the windows rolled down, glove compartment empty and hanging open. they tossed around my maps and left the american coins in the ashtray.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis week, theo and i will wander around the seedy underbelly of vancouver. i said, show me anything here that might shock me. i want to see the brothels and the open air drug markets and all the sketch. so he\'s taking me on a tour. i would take pictures, but well.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(to \"prevent crime\", the city of vancouver has 24-hour surveillance cameras set up in the inner city. paddy wagons will show up out of nowhere and arrest anyone standing on street corner X who fits racial profile Y. the program has been such a stunning failure that the city has decided to expand surveillance and add more cameras.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nother plans for the week involve the full moon and chasing <a href=\"http://www.confluence.org/confluence.php?lat=50&lon=-124\">confluences</a>. then i will visit olympia and play guitars.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni feel really tired, but i\'m happy i\'m here. this trip has been rewarding on so many levels.\r\n</p><p>\r\npluto crosses neptune\'s orbit for 20 years during its cycle, leaving neptune as the furthest planet from the sun. this happened most recently between 1979-1999. two years later, they are still close, really close. but the two planets orbit in resonance; they never collide.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni would like to sleep in my bed.\r\n</p>'),(1472,'I dream of things <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0679744479/qid=996585763/sr=2-1/102-8767099-4749709\">written \r\non the body</a>. \r\n\r\n<P><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1472_1.jpg\' >\r\n<br>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.artandlies.com/mp3/The_Sorrow_of_Losing_the_Object_of_ones_Dependence.mp3\">The \r\n  Sorrow of Losing the Object of one\'s Dependence</a></p>'),(1473,'<P><I>Too many sad days<br>too many Tuesday mornings.<br>I thought of you today.<br>And I dreamed you were dressed in mourning.</i></p><P>\r\nI don\'t feel like writing today.  In fact, I don\'t feel like doing much of anything.  It\'s like I\'m being sucked into a general societal apathy.  No one seems to really care about anything but getting paid, getting laid, and not missing the newest reality show on NBC.  The societal group mind has sucked me in.  Even though I\'m a \"fringe person\" I find myself having trendy thoughts and doing trendy things without even realizing that they\'re trendy.  A friend of mine once said, \"This is how I\'ve been dressing for years.  Now, all of a sudden, I\'m stylish.\"\r\n</p><P>\r\nSo, what does this have to do with Green?  Nothing really.  At least not yet.  I was talking to her on the phone last night, and she thought it would be appropriate for me to write an article with her as the subject.  I disagreed, but after much persistence, I finally agreed to think about it.  So here I am making a sort of Essemar-Green clique on Swinney, even though I really don\'t feel like writing.</p><P>\r\nIt sometimes amazes me when I meet someone that I get along with, especially when it\'s someone I randomly called because she posted her phone number on the web and dared people to call her.  Green tolerates my obsessive personality and the fact no filter exists between my brain and my mouth well.  Probably because she\'s a psycho-hippy-bitch.  I think it\'s amazing that I can actually meet people to befriend, people who are on the my wavelength.  They seem so rare, but they also seem to seek me out.  Group minds...</p><P>\r\n[<i>raising glass</i>]So, here\'s to Green for giving me someone to talk to at midnight when I\'m feeling lonely.  Here\'s to Green for having sympathetic words when I rail about cunnilingus.  Here\'s to Green for being a psycho-hippy-bitch.</p>'),(1475,'<br>\r\nfind summer, closed doors<br>\r\nsee pictures of weddings now<br>\r\nand graduations<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhope, lying await<br>\r\nand i see it, transparent<br>\r\nfocuses this scene<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthank you, thank you, you<br>\r\ni wish you would change back now<br>\r\nto whom you were then<br>\r\n<br>\r\nor else smile when I<br>\r\ntry to meet you where you are<br>\r\nand be your me, loved<br>\r\n<br>\r\nnow and then cast on<br>\r\nme shadows of burnt colors<br>\r\nand i taste them all<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni live my life in<br>\r\nfleeting moments you spare me<br>\r\nto wait for then now<br>\r\n<br>\r\n'),(1476,'<P>When i was younger getting the runs would absolutely freak the crap out of me , no pun intended. then yesterday my mom brings me some food, which i didnt want since i want to be independent and all but after i leave a nasty message on her cell phone i end up having some of the pasta and one of the yogurt cups she left. so, i.m a hypocrite, the greater of the Unites States of A follows in my routine so what have I to worry about being a hypocrite. Then i get an awful case of runs, runs , runs...and the worst part was it was right after i had a very filling meal. so YOU know the entire meal is gone and in the flusher and I wasnt about to make some more pasta...Im not <I> that</I> domestic. by far.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>yesterday drew was telling me how i act SINGLE even though i.ve been in a relationship for over a year and we plan on eloping. there is this fear when it comes to people and pairs and committment. because yeah, if it doesnt work out you get all worked up, contemplate how they were down in the holiest of holies and know practically EVERYTHING about you, and yeah I bet it dont feel to hot, but I think being in love and hurting is ALL very VERY worth it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so then i make my roomate get me and ICEE. which is mean of me because i.m too occupied with the goddamn phone and all to put it down for a millisecond so i stay while she is buying me one with her dough which is absolutely terrific of her and all.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i love ICEE\'s. they are sooo cold and your lips get blue, that is if you get the better color of the two, and for the summer it.s the best.\" there is one around here i just forgot where exactly, \" are the kind of answers i give to people when i havent the slightest clue as to what i am conversing about.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so that ICEE, blue in its color , really helped my stomach out last night. and when i took esse to the bathroom with me he said that he hadnt heard anything \"really.\" see, i dont mind if he heard some poopees squirting here and there just goddamn admit it man. and the word \"really\" is a SPACE word...in the sense it leaves alot of SPACE for all kinds of stuff. and i know he heard something. it was like the freaking fourth of july in there. in the can i mean.</P>\r\n<P>okay, ESSEMAR....next time, we\'ll make sure you hear something, HA!</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Hippies are dirty. And im not that dirty, wait until winter when i go into hibernation .and i dont even change my bobby pins, then call me a hippy chick, eh.</P>'),(1477,'<p>\r\ni want to go to melbourne australia in december\r\nbecause it will be very hot there.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbefore, i was there, with some of you, and it was like this:\r\n</p><p>\r\nsleeping late into the day. waking up alone next to the back door with the house empty. every day the following conversation:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"you sleep all day?\"<br>\r\n\"yeah.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nand with eric saying sometimes, more than once or twice, in a few different ways:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"i wake up early and everyone else is asleep so i wait for everyone before i go get coffee or food but by the time everyone is awake i\'m grumpy\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe has big eyes and long fingers so instead of offering advice i stuttered and stared at the table sometimes. and sometimes i said, <i>yeah, you should get up and go get food leave us asleep when that happens</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\ntuggy was taking film classes and i walked to meet him there once, lost on the tiny campus of the victorian school of the arts. i found a dance class. i didn\'t see them, just heard them tap dancing on the other side of a locked door. a woman yelling instructions and then a great clattering of shoes on wooden floor. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe curtains in the lounge room were forever in flux. open, closed, open. i would sit in a chair there and read.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe dirt in melbourne is half sand half dirt. i have a forever memory of looking down at the edge of the sidewalk, where the dirt met the concrete, where the dirt was littered with red and brown leaves. where the sanddirt was indented with old raindrop craters.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwake up hot. sticky walking to that street where we spent our time. what is life when you\'re not with who you love, in a hot place, drinking coffee when you know it will dehydrate you. walking. with you and us? \r\n</p><p>\r\ni will go there again. that\'s a goal.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1478,'Green\'s neighbor is one of those waife thin, long black straight hair, hollywood loved bodies. Three girls, all envying the perfection. Tina even got dibs on her.<BR><BR>\r\nEeef. I\'ed rather just have my green desires.'),(1479,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Danger Slut was the one to \r\n  make me crawl out of <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0118163\">my world</a>.<br>\r\n  It\'s the things I have yet to learn that made me a man in her eyes.<br>\r\n  For some reason I listened to her:<br>\r\n  - At this time of the night Colors have no meaning. Let\'s go corrupt a <a href=\"http://www.backyardcircus.com/backyardcircus/book/HTMLpages/36-37.html\">painting</a>!<br>\r\n  All I do is follow. If she manifest the slightest interest in the future, <br>\r\n  it means that there is such a thing. There will be another <a href=\"http://www.davidusher.ca/pages/morning_orbit.asp\">morning</a>.</font></p>\r\n<p>Â <img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1479_1.jpg\' > </p>'),(1480,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1480_1.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n<p>pleasure point</p>\r\n<p>riding the rails...over the cliff.<br>\r\nwhat would it be to let go again. the closeness of it once frightened me.</p>\r\n<p>too many memories.</p>\r\n<p>scabs to pick. the wounds still there. forever itching.<br>\r\nit only happens in repetition. rewind.</p>\r\n<p>staring in the mirror. ghost of me.</p>\r\n<p><i>do they see me?</i></p>\r\n<p>my heart hollow. like the empty whiskey glass<br>\r\nechoing on the floor</p>\r\n<p>beneath the bed.</p>\r\n<p>bloody lip. swing shift. empty house. broken skin. nose bleed. bruised green.</p>\r\n<p>he hit me...like a freight train<br>\r\nover and over again.</p>\r\n<p>but i hid it well. in the back of my mind.</p>\r\n<p>a rotten thing. decayed song. bitter changeling</p>\r\n<p>this was all i knew. bad timing...abusive recollections.<br>\r\nhe was one in a million. one of the rest. a welcome home family extension.</p>\r\n<p>unseen feelings. beyond. beneath. unrequited forgotten dreams.</p>\r\n<p>welcome home.<br>\r\nalone. no one to hold me. brace me. remember the deaths.<br>\r\nlost innocence.</p>\r\n<p>and the fog still whispers...and the waves still roll in.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>tidalpull<br>\r\nbring me back home.</p>\r\n<p>roaming nightshade. shadowed. winged creature.</p>\r\n<p><i>do they see me?</i></p>\r\n'),(1481,'<P>videotaped a girl that loked like you. at this artist friend.s party.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>hair so straight yet underneath a wave lay like yours and her nose bronzed under sun, dark squinty eyes are like yours.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>she is smiling, laughing, eyes like the cracks of our parents bedroom underneath the door while they are sleeping, so dark.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>small, thin frame. shoulders round in her gap tank and i think you. how closer can two people be when it comes to genetics?</p>\r\n\r\n<P>besides lovers, you my sister have seen me grow and regress. through grades and schools and lovers and pairs of jeans i would hand to you...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>criticism pours from your pretty lip-glossed mouth all in good love. and you shake your curls at my second hand store negligee wardrobe</p>\r\n\r\n<P>at my dirty pinned choppy hair you shake those curls and frown...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>but in those squinty eyes of yours there is a pride when i make daniel and ashley laugh. those preppy highschool friends of yours</p>\r\n\r\n<P>that think i take acid like vitamins(probably)</p>\r\n\r\n<P>maybe because i am so not like you we fit.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it has been to long</p>\r\n'),(1482,'<P>PHB. Psycho Hippie Bitch is what I wrote on my jeans on the way to Knotts Berry farm as myself and three other boys drove to seek employment. I wrote PHB because that is what essemar calls me.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>Its fantastic...not in the good way, but in the awe striking manner the way we-and I mean \"we\" in the generalised form, tend to fool ourselves when it comes to certain people. There are those \"eccentrics\" ( or at least one thinks they are) that reel us in for the moment until we realise how very dull and \"REAL\" they are. </p>\r\n\r\n<P>So this is about one of those boys in the van, on the way to get jobs. and how i was his \"acquaintance\" for a while, even though my closest friends did tell me he sucked. I was all \"nah, he was just raised different.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<P>But here is where PHB comes in.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>I cannot stand misogynist men/women/pets. And there finaly struck something in me today. I finally realised that all this joking with me was not in brotherly jest...it was because in ALL situations I had been the only female present. </p>\r\n\r\n<P>So this is the TNT that blows the bridge up. Since burning it would be too much effort.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>\"You\'re not good at asking questions are you?\"</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Yep.</p>\r\n'),(1483,'<P>When I was living with my sister, my brother in-law would come home and ask, \"How was today?\"<br>I\'d reply, \"Same day.  How \'bout you?\"<br>\"Yeah, same day, just like every day,\" laugh.</p><P>When people ask me how I\'m doing, I tell them that I\'m still alive.  I don\'t want to tell them I\'m fine because that would be a lie, but I also realize they don\'t really want to know how I\'m doing; they don\'t want to listen to me bitch, so I state the obvious.  Yes, I am still blowing air between my teeth.</p><P>I\'ve got so much shit to do: supplies to order, monthly budgets to audit, stuff to file.  Those are the work things I\'m not doing.  The stuff someone gives me the money for that feeds me, houses me, pays for dance classes.  Then there\'s the emails I haven\'t written, appointments I haven\'t made, dishes unwashed, vacations unplanned.  (side note: I\'m going to Burning Man.  It\'ll be my first time, so to anyone out there who\'s going: email me, I\'m going to be totally lost.  But that\'s part of the fun, right?)  So, instead of tackling my intimidating to-do list, I\'m writing.  I feel like writing today when it\'s not forced.  When it\'s procrastination.</p><P>Today is the same day as all my other days.  Too much shit to do, looking for ways to procrastinate.  Heather would have something to say about karma at this point then she would point out the fact that I\'m a Taurus and somehow make that pertinent.  She can always make you stop, look at yourself, and say, \'huh.\'  You gotta love people who can do that, and Heather was an easy person to love.</p><P>I made a drunken declaration last Friday night that I would find a girlfriend, no matter what it takes.  So far, it hasn\'t worked.  I deal <I>very</i> poorly with rejection, so I tend to sit in my room waiting for my closet door to burst open and a beautiful woman to walk out ala <I>Weird Science</i>.  Then she would say, \"Hey rat bastard,\" and I\'d say, \"Hey bitch,\" and life would be perfect.</p><P>I\'ve always thought that life should be more like a movie, and there was a time when I was doing a fairly decent job of fulfilling that fantasy, but movies eventually end and life always goes on.  That era of my life was short and magical.</p><P>Hmmm.... I think I should go now.  I\'ve run out of cute things to say.</p>'),(1484,'<FONT SIZE=\"-1\"><PRE>\r\n._____LessThanNetZero Ghetto Internet Excess and Email______.\r\n|  Download Now           -->       http://www.swinney.org  |\r\n|  Request a CDROM           -->       request@swinney.org  |\r\n|___________________________________________________________|\r\n</PRE></FONT>\r\n\r\n<P>hi folks.  Hall wants a banner ad. I dont do banner ads.  would somebody please make banner ads?  you can post them in comments or just email them to <a href=\"mailto:banners@swinney.org\">banners@swinney.org</A>.</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nDate: Fri, 6 Jul 2001 07:59:13 -0700\r\nFrom: Justin Hall <justin@bud.com>\r\nTo: austin@swinney.org\r\nSubject: ad banner\r\n\r\nAustin -\r\n\r\nI\'d like to put some ad banners up on Links.net \r\nfor cool sites.  If you feel like it, maybe a \r\n468 x 60 gif animation or jpg ad banner for \r\nSwinney.org?\r\n\r\nJustin\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>note the format here is 468x60.  it can be animated or static. those of you who know hall\'s site <a href=\"http://links.net\">links.net</a>, it can be tits and ass or whatever.</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>\r\nDate: Wed, 1 Aug 2001 13:58:14 -0700\r\nFrom: Justin Hall <justin@bud.com>\r\nTo: Austin Swinney <austin@swinney.org>\r\nSubject: ain\'t too proud\r\n\r\nAustin -\r\n\r\nI ain\'t too proud to beg.  I\'d love to get an \r\nadvertisement for Swinney running on Links.net.  \r\nWhat can I do to aid the process?  I could maybe\r\nbring you back some sushi from Japan.\r\n\r\nJustin\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>really it can be anything:</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n[10:34] <*egg*> and humbert doesnt wanna do it?\r\n[10:34] <*egg*> he can just crop a victorias secret ad\r\n[10:34] <*egg*> and write \"uncle\r\n[10:34] <*egg*> swinney\" on the ass\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>so please, go for it.  sieze the opportunity.  raise your status.  LIVE!</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1485,'<P>So this summer I have been working as both a clerk and a lifeguard/swim teacher. Now, for the swim instructor I am getting paid very well, 10 bucks an hour. The clerical job is around 6\'ish. I was thinking around 800 bucks this month, nope.\r\nI made just ten dollars over what I have to pay in bills and rent. No accessories included. No grocery money, no nothing. It really makes me angry. I guess I should\'ve done the math a bit more but didnt.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>I got taxed ALOT. They practically raped me, the goddamn bastards with their goddamn taxes and all.I need a REAL job. After today screw University jobs that pay shit. It.s time to actually grow up and stop skimming the poverty line everytime a pay-check rolls in.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Then my office boss wants to know why I would be adding preference to pool job. Well, it pays much much more. Four dollars more to be exact. So now Im screwed when it comes to going to New York because a dumb roomate of mine didnt get the stupid April phone bill on time. So this month lucky me gets to pay TWO phone bills instead of one.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>ohhhh. At least I have money to do laundry.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>If any would like to contribute by means of used ill fitting clothing, some face soap, regular soap or bobby pins, lemme know.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>my addy is:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>1201 W. Blaine St. Apt #54</P>\r\n<P>Riverside, CA 92507</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P> peace out</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1486,'<P>She looked in the mirror and slowly tilted her head, first to the left then slowly to the right. Her thin bangs would slide obscenely from one side to the other so when she finally centered her head she had this bunch of dark hairs plastered on the left side of her forehead.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The apartment was stiffling and she could feel as the beads of sweat on her back, face, and chest would slowly seperate from the skin and begin their trail downward.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>A door slammed and a man\'s heavy and busy stroll resounded throughout the entire apartment. Keys were heard being dropped on the sofa and a coat was removed.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Are you ready or what?\"</P>\r\n<P>\"I\'ve been ready for ages now. Practically ages. I should shower again. I am soaked to the bone in sweat-\"</P>\r\n<P>\"No. No-no-no-no. We\'re leaving now.\"</P>\r\n<P>\"They\'re always late. It wouldnt hurt to follow in our friends footsteps once in a while.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The girl sat with strong resillience on the old floral sofa, slowly getting up halfway as if she had sat upon something. His lighter. She balanced it between both index fingers, until it fell with a slight noise on her lap. Arms fell exhausted on her sides then raised to fiddle with halfway mussed and halfway done hair. The lighter fell back into the crack in the sofa.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Well, if you\'re just sitting there, doing <I> absolutely</I> nothing, I suppose I could take a shower.\" His footseps led her closed eyes and going mind into the shower and were starled by the abrupt and violent closing of the door.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>She rested her head on the back of the sofa and halfway closed her eyes. Clumps of mascara added a charming reality to her face. She bit her bottom lip, sighed and pressed her hands to her head. She hadnt been able to sleep for days and the whites of her eyes were a lined red, kinda like a map, when her eyes were open wide enough for anyone to notice. All she was capable of was laying on the couch, hands behind her head.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Suddenly, her two maps opened and she put both stockinged feet on the floor. Getting up she walked in a gingerly manner,as if not wishing to wake anyone, although there was no one there besides him and her. Walking to the window she looked down and felt a small breeze tear the silk of her blouse from the stickiness of her skin.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Later that night, she opened her eyes. She could feel his chest taking in the air and expelling it. He lay on the complete opposite of the bed. She couldnt sleep. Everything had been attempted. Nothing was working. Slowly, she moved the sheets from herself and sat up. Her head turned and looked at his sleeping body and quickly she moved for the door. Her movements were paralyzed for a moment when a creak in the wooden floor popped out of nowhere.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Out in the living room she sat by the phone on the sofa. Her hair was down and in her white robe for an instant Camilla looked rather angelic. Although she felt quite the latter. The laquer on her nails had been the same as two months ago. With her left hand Camilla picked up the phone, and with her right hand she balanced the square silver lighter in between her thumb and index finger.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \" Hello, I do believe you\'ve left your lighter here again.\" And Camilla smiled. </P>\r\n\r\n'),(1487,'<P> I\'m sure that I\'ve spent my entire life trying to impress the hell out of my parents.  I can\'t remember it, but I\'m sure I wanted to roll over, crawl, walk, talk, and potty train, just to make them happy.  I wanted their constant approval. I needed it. We all needed it. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> They sent me off to school and wanted nothing less then the best.  Elementary school, junior high.  I never fit in anywhere.  They didn\'t see this.  They just wanted what they wanted (\"and I want it NOW\"). </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Why don\'t you wear skirts anymore. You always used to dress so nice.  Now all you ever wear to school are jeans and t-shirts.\"  Well, mom and dad, I used to get picked on because you laid my clothes out on my bed every morning and you made me look stupid.  I finally got smart enough to put back what you took out and pick out my own clothes.  It was just too late. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> My dad calls my junior year of high school \"the year debbie didn\'t care.\"  My dad is full of shit.  My dad has no idea.  My parents, amongst other things, had driven me to the edge.  It wasn\'t that I didn\'t care.  I was just so god damn depressed that I couldn\'t care. So, because of my lack of concern I brought home ....wait, hold onto your seats now... a couple of C\'s.  Holy terror.  Christ, the world is going to end.  I didn\'t graduate first in my class.  I was ONLY third. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I find that <almost> adult life, is much like what we grow up with.  We have climbed the ladder of our parents expectations (sometimes cutting off rungs while they aren\'t looking, to make things easier).  Now, we have the rest of the world to look after. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Now, it\'s corporate america.  I despise the corporate ladder and everything it stands for.  Unfortunately, on the road to greater things, I plan on using and abusing the strong standpoint of major corporations to the best of my ability.  Someone has to pay for the house I\'ll grow old in. Maybe when I\'m old I\'ll finally be the starving artist type writer that I\'ll strive to be.  In any case, I\'m not afraid to admit that I hate what I\'m doing to get there, along the way. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I had my first formal interview ever today. For ONCE in my life, I felt good as I left the house in the morning.  The assistant vice president asked me where I thought I saw myself in about three years.  I told her I saw myself in the same industry that I was in now, with a college degree.  I pointed out, however, that my degree would not be in business, because I hated the classes I that I would need to take and because I had no interest in a business degree to start with.  I told her I wanted my degree to be in something that I really, truely liked.  (After all, I\'ll be spending their money on it.)</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I don\'t know what it was, but I was offered the position on the spot. </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1488,'<tt>\r\ndriving along the long flat end of\r\nthe san mateo bridge tonight i was reminded of childhood fancy...\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nas i sped along, seemingly right on\r\nthe bay, a glimmer of light would catch my eye from the right. i tried concentrating on the road ahead, but the impulse was to strong. i turned\r\nmy head to glance at the bright banner of light shimmering across the water. incredibly bright i imagined a full moon imbuing the inky waters of the bay with it\'s sunshine.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthis made me remember childhood trips,\r\nlong endless drives across desert, mountains, or forest -what ever geographical\r\nlocation we were living in at the time. \r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen i was little i was convinced, <i>absolutely\r\nconvinced</i>, that the sun followed me. no matter where i\'d go, or how\r\nlong it took to get there, that sunny globe was my faithful follower.\r\n<br><br>\r\nat first i was frightened, \"oh my god the sun is after me!\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nthen i went through a brief period of worry, \"what happens to those other people when the sun decides to follow <i>me</i>?\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nand then i just thought it was cool,\r\n\"oh wow, everywhere i go, the sun is sure to follow\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nevery once in a while now, when it\'s\r\nan incredible clear sunny day, and perhaps i\'m driving up the 5 to oregon,\r\nthat sun remembers the child in me, and decides to follow me home.\r\n</tt>'),(1489,'Not a good day today.\r\n<BR>\r\nAt work I am when I receive a call from <FONT Color=008800>Green</font>. Of bicycles and bumps and scuffs and falls she tells me. <FONT Size=-1><I>Cool, battle damage.</i></font> Her voice is norm, her inflections are calm. But I detect a bit of distraction/distance in her. <FONT Size=-1><I>Must be me, since I\'m trying to look like I\'m still working and all. Yeah. I\'m the one that was distracted.</i></font>\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe conversation was short.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nHome I arrive to. Hours past my normal arrival time, having worked five hours of overtime for a small company that doesn\'t pay for overtime. (Eight hours a day, that\'s it, as far as their accounting goes. I\'ve put in as many as 32 hours straight, several times. None the less, 8 hours.) Big meetings will be had so things needed to get done. Besides, I\'m a computer programmer, what else is expected? Late hours to programmers is as common as crying is to babies.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nTechnically, as far as the modeus operandi goes, I should have gone to bed the moment I got home. You know, for a full 8 hours of rest. So I staied up. Received a call from my best friend in Fontana asking me to visit tomorrow. Okay. Then I sit down and start programming my code gift to Green. Nothing much, but it\'ll give her a place of her own on the www, an outlet for her art and something I can lend my programming passion to. Her art and my art working as one. Kinda cool.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nExcept then SHE called me, one hour after I got home.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<FONT Size=-1><I>Seems I did not take the hint.</i></font>\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n\"I want to take a break,\" the first words to come at me over the wire.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nConfused, I asked what she meant.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nShe was at the hospital, waiting for her turn on the x-ray machine. She tells me that she expected me to call her or something. Maybe come see her. But now it\'s all too late. This is the second time I\'ve ignored her.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOkay, some background now... Bear with me, I\'m trying.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nA few weeks ago, <FONT Color=008800>Green</font> her good girl friend and an aquintance guy friend of hers all were drinking, having a good time at her apt. After a while they went clubbing at the local meat market and drank some more. After some time of drinking, dancing on bars, and having a good time, she eventually ends up getting totally drunk. (skipped lots here, but you probably can figure out for yourself what happpened... Just ask <FONT Color=008800>Green</font>.) So she is totally wasted. Throws up constantly. The next morning she is still throwing up. She gives me a call and tells me that she feels sick, has been throwing up, and drank too much the night before. <FONT Size=-1><I>Hmm... Don\'t remember if she told me she was going to go to the doctor then...</i></font> She ends up going to the doctor for alcohol poisoning.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt so happended that I was totally depressed that day and the next day. I wanted to do nothing but sit at home and be depressed. I almost even cried for no reason several times during that time. I even took the next day of work off I was so depressed. So depressed infact, that I was unwilling/unable to drive out and see <FONT Color=008800>Green</font> and find out how she was. I called many many times, but got the machine many many times. I knew she wanted me to go see her, but I needed her to come see me much more.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe nearly broke up over this incident. She needed me, and I couldn\'t provide.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHere we are again. The differences:\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI><B>I didn\'t realize she actually needed me this time.</b> (Unlike last time). She sounded fine over the phone. I told her to go see the doctor, but that was really only echoing her mentioning that her mom wanted her to go to the doctor. Now, from my standing she didn\'t seem concerned about damage or anything. I (probably more subconsciensly than actualized) figured she was going to get antibiotics or something for the scrapes. (I had an incident once when I was a kid where I was riding double on a bike, I on the handle bars. We hit a bump, my foot got caught behind the fork and the spokes, and down I went, taking the full force of my buddy and myself on my face. After crying for 20 minutes I felt fine. Went to the doc, and all I got was antibiotics and a trip back to school to show my battle damage.) Does anyone see my POV? Or was I out of line?</li>\r\n<LI><B>She never said anything.</b> How am I supposed to know this was an important event to her if it\'s not conveyed? I love art, and I love the strange. She is both. But unless the author tells me, I can only come up with my own interpation. I need a nudge at these times.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<BR>\r\nSo now I\'m told that she needs a break.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\"A break?\" I say. \"There is no such thing as a break. You\'re either in a relationship or you\'re not.\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\"Then I guess we\'re not in a relationship.\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFuck me.\r\n<BR><BR>'),(1490,'<P>Remembering how when i was younger one would give their friend a small bracelet of some kind of threaded ring to express their love for that person. We seem to move from parents as friends, to girls our own age that know all of our secrets to men that seem to understand and sometimes don\'t.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>It\'s funny the manner in which things change with time. They are added freckles over the bridge of your nose, added lines at the ends of your eyes, they are those white hairs that poke from the top of ones head even though they may be twenty-two.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>My loves are speckled and varied. Some I would die, kill, suffer for. And it is not that they contain so much passion in the eye of a needle, but that they deserve it. Because you know. A reciprocal kind of loving.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Most loves of mine are like that. They remind me tepidly of being a messy and scrubby seven year old playing dodgeball and hopscotch, tetherball and getting pushed on the swings. It was loving someone with all your might one day and then having the absolute right to change your mind the next. And be so and so\'s best friend for a few days.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Well, now I.ve moved from girlfriends of my youth to these men that I love/loved. I dont think I can say loved.Because I still love Benjamin from 2 years ago. And he is off in Europe somewhere, It is the small, the undeclared, the unnoticed actions that speak our soul by means of hands, words, and actions.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Before Benjamin, it was Erick. My infatuation, My innocence kept. My best friend and confidant. How I dreamt that something would come of it. Only to later learn that we at night, shared in our dreams but made no move towards them. He is somewhere 60 or so miles away. His dreams clash with mine and it is difficult the air I breathe when on the phone with him.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Now, it\'s you. Michael. I do not expect you to be psychic. Just <B> listen </B>. Listen to how I do not and would not ever live with your friends and to ask me to is a quest in futility. Listen to my reaction last time I went to the doctor. I was crying into the goddamn reciever. Just listen. Just call. Just to come home and have my father tell me that you had called would have been enough. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Turning into my street I beg, I pray , I hope that you are there. That you said, screw Erika. I.m going there anyways because <I> I </I> want to. And it didnt happen. Sometimes, when I need you most it feels like I stand alone. And I.ve realized how we all do. It is this small facade of family and friends and lovers we hide under, when really it is just our naked selves against sun, moon, stars, rain...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I count on myself. I count on me. Because I know  that I will always be there.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Maybe we are still best friends, Angry at eachother because we thought things would be different. I do not know. Even if I am that.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1491,'<p>&lt;pie&gt; poem:<br>\r\n&lt;pie&gt; egg, egg, don\'t make me beg<br>\r\n&lt;pie&gt; come out with me and drink a keg<br>\r\n&lt;pie&gt; i promise not to hump your leg<br></p>\r\n<p>I just ran through the streets of San Francisco to get some energy off . I am <a href=\"http://www.heathen.net/random/eggsong.html\">insatiable</a> today. I want that thing with those people and this place all the time here, there, all at once. I cannot be expected to work today.</p>\r\n<p>leonard stepped out of elusive character two nights ago and got on icb, complaining of jitters from cream soda and begging the lord of group texas for pamphlets that would help him with his dick warts. I feel that he implanted the seed of a virus in me that night that I must shake me body ecstatically and yell at strangers to cure.</p>\r\n<p>last night said lord was trying to make allowances for my nervous behavoir when really all I did was ask him how his week was going. he: \"you are not relaxing because you anticipate the drive back, and you are nervous because you are out of your element.\" me: \"no, Im not relaxing because we are sitting here and im trying unsuccessfully to make small talk and Im nervous because I really drove here to fuck.\"</p>\r\n<p>I have trouble sitting still when morsels of thrusting white ass tease me tenderly from the butcher shop window, when the air is fresh with redwoods and stinky dogs and minty bubble gum and I want to gnash my teeth and talk in tongues and chew someone\'s neck or twist his limb like gumby.</p>\r\n<p>I can come on pretty strong, it\'s true that I was the girl in school who was always wanting to prod and thrust and chatter and kibbles n bits n bits n bits. I imagine if i had been in school with the lord of group texas that he would have pulled my pigtail to make me shutup and i would have thrown him down with my knee-to-nut talent.</p>\r\n<p>his new workplace makes a pamphlet on how to survive route 17, the long and winding road to santa cruz that has sent nervous lovers to their death. I had to be careful not to get too dreamy on the drive, to focus on hugging the curves and keeping my nose in lane. no poetry, no transgression. just drive, you silly bitch!</p>\r\n<p>something about the 17 is Massachusettsian. except for the redwoods. and the eucalyptus. and the cars and drivers. now i sound like one of those assholes who says \"hey! I have that bag, except it\'s leather, not plastic, and they\'re shoes.\"</p>\r\n<p>Nothing is possibly called by its right name today, and that is too bad. Im not the type to jump into a car on a treacherous journey for love. Im just too pragmatic for that sort of thing. But I have travelled a number of distances around this fine country to see beloveds, and while the white bunny I seek is a fine reward, the deadly curves on the way teach me new skills.</p>\r\n<p>Tom Robbins wrote \"spontaneous travel plans are dancing lessons from god.\" his corniness makes me want to puke, but it\'s true.</p>\r\n<p>eve: we have travelled countless distances.<br>\r\negg: yeah, I cant even count them all.<br>\r\neve: countless.</p>'),(1492,'<p>it has been a while since I have talked to yall, since i have read anything from yall, since i have felt like talking...</p><p>life has taken a turn for the better, and little for the worse. I had a two family members burried this past week. After all these funerals I have had a lust for living, living life today. I am not sure what it takes to start over, though I have started over several times the last few years! A couple of months ago, I decided that I was going to leave here to joing the <a href=\'http://www.ambafrance-us.org/america/embassy/legion/legion.htm\'> french foreign legion</a>, frill i was ready! I served for 4 years in the marines, though it sucked and war is stoopid and all that, i find comfort within the ranks of war fighting, egomaniacs. I finally put it to rest (the idea of going) this past weekend... I think there are better things i can do with life... i am sure that you all agree. This was my \"escape plan\" to erase my identity, my name; thinking that I could start again... still it gives me chills to think about... having been through the usmc, and knowing how easy it really is, it seemed the most fun thing to do. </p><p>It is time for something else, education. I am going to be a full time student in the fall. I think that i will find a new life there, embedding myself in books and that life... still it doesnt sounds as exciting as the FFL!!!</p>\r\n<p>so, as i return home last night from a bad week, my very great friend K-dogg is waiting for me at the gate, which to me is the greates honor that one can receive; having a friend waiting for you at the gate! Don\'t be lazy at the airport, park and go in - it makes all the difference!!!</p>'),(1493,'<P>I was going through my pregnancy journal today (my son is 2 now) and I realized that I never wrote about how I told my mother that I was pregnant (easily one of the most difficult things I\'ve ever had to do...not counting sex with the double leg amputee <em>(You\'ll never guess who was on top.)</em>)</P>\r\n<P>The seeds of my pregnancy story were actually laid many years ago.  I was 13 years old and had been living in Germany for 2 weeks when my grandfather died.  My family flew back to New York for the funeral.  While the circumstances were less than happy, there was one bright spot during the week we spent in the States...Anthony.  He was 16, resembled a New Kid on The Block, and actually payed attention to me. </P><P>We both spent a lot of time at my aunt\'s house that week. (My aunt is married to his uncle...no blood relation.)  He would sit on the porch swing and talk with me for hours, keeping my infatuated little teenage mind off of the situation at hand.  By the time we were ready to leave for the airport, I had developed quite the severe crush on this \"older-man.\"</P>\r\n<P>As my family\'s car pulled out of the driveway, and we waved goodbye to all of our relatives, my Mother started the following conversation:<br> \"So...how did Anthony treat you this week?\"<br>(dreamy teenage voice)\"Oh, Mom.  He was great.\"<br>\"Good, \'cause we gave him twenty bucks to be nice to you.\"</p>\r\n<p>I\'m sure I must have been fairly traumatized by that statement (as anyone in that situation would have been), because I didn\'t ever think about that afternoon until years later, when I decided to move to New York to be closer to my extended family.  As soon as I saw Anthony, the memories flooded back.  He wasn\'t 16 any more, he was usually drunk, and had developed quite the arrest record...but teenage hormones die hard.  We had a few \"flings.\"  Nothing but good, non-committed, no-strings-attached sex...and lots of it.</P>\r\n<P>Then...the birth control failed, and I found myself standing in front of my mom trying to come up with the words to explain my predicament.  I was 23, had a full time job, and was actually financially stable enough to have a kid on my own...so I\'m not sure what I was so worried about.  I finally just blurted it out. \"I\'m pregnant.\"  Then came the heart to heart conversations and bonding that I hope all mothers-to-be can have with their own mothers.</P>\r\n<P>To this day, however, I wonder how different things would have been if I had just told my mom, \"Well, you got your twenty bucks worth.\"</P>'),(1494,'<P>\r\nmore than anything, i like to hunt out bad things.  i like to notice that someone\'s doing something wrong, decide that it\'s wrong, and then tell them that they\'re doing something wrong.  regardless of who they are, or how many people \"back them up\", or who they\'ve shut up through intimidation or bribery.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni know i make mistakes, though, because i\'m human and a fanatic (as humans are wont to be).  so i think i should join an already-founded karma police and review cases with a team and decide with them in an entirely objective view about whether or not they\'re worth the battle.  you know, nobody \'fesses up to being bad, to doing the wrong thing, \'specially if they\'ve been wrong for so long that they\'ve made it their right.  sometimes, even, i turn the finger back around and question myself, on my views and actions.  and i\'m human every time.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nbut i don\'t think i\'ve been intentionally mean since i made michael j. cheat on the \"love of his life\" whom he actually didn\'t love at all.  and that, even, was a sick sort of battle to the truth, because he never would have believed me any other way.  if i would have just told him, he would have told me i didn\'t understand, that i was jealous because i was with him one summer ago, and a thousand other excuses.  but i offered myself and he fell for me in a moment and right when things could have gotten too hot i reminded him of what he thought a minute ago, of what he at that moment knew was false and couldn\'t think anymore.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nneil gaiman: \"innocence, once lost, can never be regained.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\npeople might think i\'m a sick, twisted person, but how many people are itching to say what they see as the truth, to offer it up to be accepted or denied?  and do they really mute their views in order to be polite...or is it because they don\'t have it inside them?  or maybe they just don\'t care enough...\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nhmm...i know at least one person who doesn\'t seem to care enough about anything.  least of all truth.  who needs truth in such an uncaring world.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\npoo.  anyway, i\'m lucy.  in dracula.  the original play, from the original book.  none of this keanu reeves nonsense, either.  hollywood certainly can bastardize things quickly.  no, the original play goes back to the original horror.  and if that simple tale was good enough to spawn all the hollywood junk, then isn\'t it just fine by itself?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1497,'<p>somebody show me some relief,<br>\r\nsomebody find me a gun,<br>\r\nsomebody show me where the door is,<br>\r\nsomebody teach me how to run.</p>\r\n<p>i wish i had a boy to play with.<br>\r\none who would do exactly as i said.<br>\r\nthat i could watch afterwards,<br>\r\nnaked skin curled round my pink comforter,<br>\r\nlegs tangled in my sheets,<br>\r\nsleeping with a soft smile on his lips.<br>\r\njust so i could wake him,<br>\r\nand make him lick my boots all over again.</p>\r\n<p>i am not this<br>\r\nmonday barf.<br>\r\nsearching for work,<br>\r\nlooking at homes,<br>\r\nhunting passion and flesh.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>or as jon spencer would say....\'god damn motherfuck\'</p>'),(1495,'<P>mountain echoes from one side to the next</p>\r\n<P>the sound of nothing is a buzzing in my ears</p>\r\n<P>sighing how the moon ignores the rest</p>\r\n<P>nights of heavy eyes and lit unrest</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>this heartbeat nothingness one hears</p>\r\n<P>this hiccup time that will have gone astray</p>\r\n<P>flutter silent, it amplifies the fears</p>\r\n<P>that one day maybe next to this today </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>this mellow feel becomes the say</p>\r\n<P>of ones own mind</p> \r\n<P>of how and when and what and whom</p>\r\n<P>are allowed into the narrow room</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>two months ago in June</p>\r\n<P>I dreamt of drowning.</p>\r\n<P>I should.ve dreamt of falling</p>\r\n<P>for the falling did come true.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1496,'<p>the boss reprimanded me for bringing dark humor into the workplace<br>\r\ni was and still am a smart ass to even my boss.<br>\r\nmy vocabulary is grossly limited, and now even smaller comparable to a dust mite.<br>\r\nthe only colors i recognize are the pink, blue, green, and yellow of generic highlighters and stickie notes.<br></p>\r\n<p> you may have noticed, by now, the banality in tone.<br>\r\nwell, you can suck it.<br>\r\nthe morale is low and all i have are nightmares of white faced, bloody toothed, clowns named seymour and roger and Muhammad.<br>\r\noh ho ho, i\'m running like a chihuahua; sprinting like a greyhound on speed<br>\r\ni\'m in full gear...it\'s all fungula...it\'s a race to the end on a runaway treadmill with no \"off\" button.<br>\r\nthe neuro synapses are overloaded and transforming into some kind of nebulous matter that stinks of rotten dairy products and dirty sheets.<br>\r\nit\'s a wonder i don\'t wonder why i wake up in a pool of my own three week old shit...*snif snif*<br>\r\nall i did was make a donation box with a funny note on it.<br>\r\ni even attached an addendum to make it clear that it was funny.<br>\r\njust for shits and giggles, don\'t you see?<br>\r\nchi chi can have the lousy sun.<br>\r\negg, keep shooting for the stars.<br>\r\nas for me, i\'m going to hellywood.<br>\r\ni know what\'s real, and lemme tell you something,<br>\r\nmountains and hills, sunshine and pork rine, <br>\r\nthat shit is as real as jellyfish spine.<br></p>\r\n<p>i can rhyme.<br>\r\nsomebody, quick, give me a dime<br></p>\r\n<p>you go on your lil rafting adventure, swiney.<br>\r\ngo piss in the clear, blue waters of god bless america!<br>\r\ngo pollute magnificent CA, USA with yo nasty, grubby, humanoid oils!<br>\r\ni like to wash down my granola with ice cold odwalla, man!<br>\r\ni say save the dolphins and the little people, man!<br>\r\ni say end globalization and feed the hungry in china, man!<br>\r\nc\'mon people! have some team spirit!<br>\r\nGO DOG GO!</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1498,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nit\'s nearly one and I wake up and I\'m sooo, I don\'t know- I\'m just sooo tempted to throw on an elliott smith cd or something, something by david gray or will oldham or something, you know?- cause everything is just, sooo,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nI don\'t know- everything is just sooo the same today as it was yesterday (and will probably be tomorrow), and I have no friends and I have no job and I\'m just pretty miserable all around these days and always tempted to wallow deep in it and enjoy it in some sort of twisted poetic irony- but,</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut I stop myself, you know?- I stop myself- I step outside and I light a cigarette and I sit down on the first available step and I sip my newly made cup of coffee and I stop myself, right there- I stop myself and I say enough and I go inside-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand, back inside, and rummaging through cds, and I find something- <a href=\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=610353343/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/artistid=DJ+DEEP/itemid=1344834\">respect!</a>-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I bought this cd a while back, when it came out, and I have listened to it on and off (and actually this was the soundtrack of my vegas to oakland southwest connection)- and I love the songs to death and, really- really the only thing that it is lacking in the mixing, but, you know- I can get over it-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso- I throw this cd on and after the initial rising action and another cigarette, I start to remember why I shouldn\'t wallow and why I should listen to house music all the time and why, even when I don\'t think I should be listening to house music, I should be-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nhouse music is your hopes and dreams and possibilities (free of fears) weaved into one gigantic blanket and thrown over you- it\'s like a new pair of glasses or a hug from your mother.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nyou know in good will hunting?, at the end?, where robin williams is saying over and over again \'it\'s not your fault\' and matt damon is baffled at first and then a little unnerved and then he starts crying and gets a little violent and then finally gives in and admits that, in fact, it\'s not his fault and he accepts everything and is finally, atleast for the time being, healed?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthat\'s kinda like house music.</p>'),(1499,'<pre>\r\nanna says we\'re not getting any younger.\r\nyeah, i say, and i want to have ten babies.\r\nhow about two? she says.\r\n\r\ni say, beer and pot.  i like it a lot.\r\nshe says, you need some boundaries in your life.\r\ni need more than that.\r\n\r\ni\'ll give you fifty years, anna says.\r\nand after that i\'m on my own?\r\nwe can renegotiate then, she says.\r\n\r\ni didn\'t know about love before.\r\ni guess i didn\'t know about friends, either.\r\nthese people, who are they?  i guess one is matt.\r\n\r\ndon\'t pull the long face now, she says.\r\ni guess she tickles me.\r\ni used to be alone.\r\n</pre>'),(1500,'<p>\r\ni was laughing to jeff about the number of muffins i had eaten before he got home-- like a momma cat eating her kittens, i eat the muffins that aren\'t fit to be eaten by others. the small, the hard, the wobbly. nothing sadder than a muffin that can\'t stand on its own two feet. by the time he got home, there were only 36 left. the muffins are a little soft in the middle and pale, but not too bad for a first try.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni tell him that we should not eat muffins for dinner. he agrees mmorphthggph with muffin in his mouth. \r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday a material purchase made me laugh like a chimp. \r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.weeb.com/tempura_afro300.jpg\" width=\"300\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am so fucking happy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyes, it indeed a little stuffed dog on a plate with tempura for hair. and fake soft sewn chopsticks. on a little soft stuff plate. is this not incredible? is this thing not completely wonderful? if i had enough money, i would buy one for all of you. all of you!</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1501,'<p>So, this is it, the magic sixth entry, embeding myself in swinney.org fame by allowing me entry to <b>the list</b>.  I\'ve been thinking about what I would like to write for this one and several ideas came and went.  I decided to take the cop-out avenue and simply write a little introduction for all of you people who don\'t know me (read: all of you people).</p>\r\n<p>Hello, my name is Justin.  <tt>*waves hand*</tt>  I am a perculiar brew of ticks and contradictions.</p>\r\n<p>Okay, that was fun.  With that out of the way. Err, wait.  No, that was terrible.  Let\'s have another go at that.</p>\r\n<p>None of you people know who I am, fair enough.  So, how did I find all of you?  Well, I\'ve been a fan of the <a href=\"http://www.chiamattt.com/\" target=\"new\">chiamattt.com</a> empire for several years now, ever since a friend of mine moved to big, bad Ottawa and met him in university.  This directly filtered back to me because a) cool guy, b) has cool website.  So, either directly through that or some other weird way, I came to know about <a href=\"http://www.elly.org/\" target=\"new\">elly</a>\'s site.  I\'ve sort of been an off-and-on casual browser of the latter.  But one day I followed a link to this site from chiamattt\'s domain.  I sort of became hooked.  Well, sort of, but I\'m sure you understand because you\'re reading this and probably contribute and well, yeah, you know where I\'m commin\' from.</p>\r\n<p>So, we know how I got here.  Why would I write here when I already have my <b>own</b> little online <a href=\"http://selectah.yi.org/fuzzy/\" target=\"new\">presence</a>?  Well, simply put: I felt like a new audience for some of my thoughts.  Plus, this is a great thing that has been created for people to share in and I wanted a little piece of the action.  I\'ve been wondering whether or not to take some kind of theme (maybe delved into more personal subjects here, things that my normal group of friends might not enjoy reading about) for my entires, but so far they have been sort of hodge-podge.  Just kludging random stories and ideas into this <i>thing</i> they call a web browser.</p>\r\n<p>What about me?  Well, what about me?  I\'m a university student (computer science [nerd]).  I currently reside in the *.to.on.ca and I wouldn\'t be lying to say that music is my life.  I like so many kinds of music that it\'s hard to keep track.  It\'s a horrible addiction that I have and wish never to shake.  I am currently building up my collection of every release from <a href=\"http://www.warprecords.com/\" target=\"new\">Warp Records</a>.  At my last count, I only need 29 more albums and a whole lot of singles/ep\'s.  A drastic contrast to this would be my two tickets to see the Dave Matthews Band play this Friday, here in Toronto.  If Phish were touring this summer (<tt>*grumble*</tt>), I\'d be seeing them sometime around now as well.  I am obsessed with Bj&ouml;rk and just discovered a supposed date for a show here on October 9th (<tt>*glee*</tt>).  I am going to see Plaid/Squarepusher/Mira Calix on the Aug. 22nd and can\'t hardly wait for that one.</p>\r\n<p>I currently work a shitty 9-5 (that\'s, uh, 9pm-5am) job in a shitty Chinese Restaurant downtown.  I have some other things lined up I\'m waiting to hear about and I have a business meeting on Tuesday afternoon (Tuesday directly maps to Sunday for me as my weekend is Monday and Tuesday) regarding me doing some database design work for a new web site cataloging nightlife activities to do in this <i>hooj see-tay</i>.</p>\r\n<p>So, once again, <tt>*hi*</tt> and I hope that you people enjoy my additions to this wonderful web site and I hope to continue catching the pixels you people are throwing out.</p>\r\n<p>Oh, one last bit, this is (and has been for several years now) my e-mail .signature file:</p>\r\n<pre>\r\n\"The reasonable man adapts himself to the world;\r\nthe unreasonable one persists in trying to adapt\r\nthe world to himself.  Therefore all progress\r\ndepends on the unreasonable man.\"\r\n                       -- George Bernard Shaw\r\n_____________________________________ |\r\n[ justin.filip :: jfilip@idirect.ca ] |\r\n[ http://selectah.yi.org/fuzzy/     ] |\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1502,'<P>eggless, butterless, millkless cake.<BR>\r\neggless eggnog.<BR>\r\nhow to make eggless easter eggs.<BR>\r\nEggless salad,<BR>\r\neggless cookie recipes<BR>\r\npad thai without egg<BR>\r\ntofu bendict with eggless hollandaise sauce<BR>\r\ntofu scramble<BR>\r\ntofu ranchero<BR>\r\neggless whipped cream<BR>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nalmost anything can be made without egg.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit does not taste the same though.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\neggless pad thai is actually pretty good,<BR>\r\nbut it is thai still.</P>'),(1503,'<P><a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=648.txt\">insomnia.</A>  thugh ree aye em.  this is far too late for me to be up.  i should have gone to bed when the feeling first hit me at about eleven.  now look at me stranded on a deserted loneliness dong crazy things to survive.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>everyone is in bed and i am going through the proverbial things and stuff of swinney.org looking for old insomnia posts.  a nice little encyclopedia of experience is what we have here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the one i was thinking of in particular was number six hundred and forty eight.  i am yawning.  this talk is helping.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>lo, i took the girl to the airport in my head today and said goodbye to her.  then i tried to buy a motorcycle and felt the depression of post commitment consumer realization even before i got to the salvage yard where the bike sat.  i was in the car listening to british reporters talk to american stock broker analysts about how they think the economy is \"now maybe possibly referable to as \'in a recession\' but ...\"  comitment was at an all time high today.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have no motorcycle to show for it, fat bellied, slope shouldered, hunched over at a desk at an early hour of the morning.  time is slipping away and time is coming to sleep.  bring on the sandman to sing me a tune about lovers with the same dreams.  ick.</P>'),(1504,'I have a category, titled <i>Chicken Scratch</i>, on my Palm where I keep all my (password protected) private thoughts.</p>\r\n<p>I think that under certain circumstances, I would be willing to let someone have the key to that particular lock.  It\'s similar to allowing someone into your mind.  This would also bring the same manner of self-conscious fear that allowing someone free-reign on your mind would bring.</p>\r\n<p>I feel an almost need to share these things with another/others.  It is my personal belief that everyone, whether conscious of it or not, is writing a story.  Everyday, just by living, people are writing pages in some great imaginary text.  The truly sad thing is that nearly everyone does not realise this and fewer still have the opportunity to tell thier tales.</p>\r\n<p>So, when I meet new people, people deemed interesting enough, I will ask the question: <b><i>\"So, what\'s your story?\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p>I love deep, involved, highly personal talks with people.  I enjoy having others trust me with their feelings, troubles and their thoughts.  I feel sometimes a need to live through other people.  Perhaps this could add to the explanation of why I feel that people usually let me down.  Or at least why I allow myself to be regularly let down.</p>'),(1505,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Danger Slut did not find it \r\n  <a href=\"http://www.indelibleinc.com/kubrick/films/strangelove/\">strange</a> \r\n  that we played solitaire in front of each other<br>\r\n  at the corner diner. We lingered like evil angels, proposing a <a href=\"http://images.amazon.com/images/P/B00005AUK9.01.LZZZZZZZ.jpg\">kiss</a> \r\n  to each other loudly. <br>\r\n  <a href=\"http://www.tonypierce.com/summer/buk/1.htm\">Strange men</a> ignored \r\n  us.<br>\r\n  She is a wonderland of emotion. An essential daily </font><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\"><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/books/first/a/amis-experience.html\">experience.</a><br>\r\n  I\'m sweating envy right now, <a href=\"http://www.queendom.com/tests/relationships/jealousy.html\">jealousy</a> \r\n  is choking me. I can not escape it.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1505_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' >\r\n<BR>'),(1506,'<P>(help)</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So, we\'ve all been through it.  We meet the guy (or girl), like him, maybe date him, have lots of fun, and then have our hearts broken.  It\'s a timeless tale, we know. It\'s inevitable. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Months and months later, say 7 or 8, you\'ve been in and out of yet another bad relationship, and suddenly you hear through the grapevine that guy #1 thinks enough time has passed.  He wants to be friends again. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You think, right.  We have mutual friends.  It won\'t be that bad to be at least somewhat social.  Then it all starts to spiral out of control. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You realize WHY you didn\'t speak for 8 months.  It was probably the same reason why he dumped you after not calling for a week: because he\'s another asshole. </P> \r\n\r\n<P> Regardless, you hang out.  You hook up.  He goes away for a couple of weeks, leaving you wondering just what the hell is going on.  He wants to hang out again, and hook up, and more... </P> \r\n\r\n<P> So you hang out again and the next thing you know he\'s telling you that he doesn\'t want a relationship but that he really likes you.  It\'s just that he doesn\'t know where he is going to be in six months and he really doesn\'t have the time...  You knew this was coming too.  Then he wants to hook up again.  This is bullshit. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> So, you start to avoid him because a) the above point, he\'s an ass and b) he\'s using you. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> A couple more months have passed.  You\'ve made the polite appearances at parties, trying not to be a bitch yourself.  You don\'t let him walk you out to your car... you\'re doing well. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> But now, he wants to hang out again, and he knows too much about what you do every day. The problem? You DO NOT LIKE HIM; but he wants to show up at the bowling alley Wednesday night.  It might be ok a) if you wanted to see him, b) if your OTHER ex who ABSOLUTELY hates him wasn\'t going to be there and, c) if your mother wasn\'t going to be there. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Maybe I should forget to show up. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> What\'s a girl to do?  ( -or- How do I get the point across?)</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1507,'<P>I always thought one could be friends with men...or anything having to do with the opposite sex. It turns out, in my humble opinion, that one cannot. I don\'t know, but I get perved out very easily by boys. And I happen to have many male friends.<I> They</I> just arent\'t perves. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>It all starts off with a seemingly safe friendship. You know, no hardcore attraction on your side of things and- hopefully- on their side of the fence, things are the same. But peole get <B>pervy</B> and I don\'t dig that.\r\n\r\nI think I\'m a narcissist in the sense that if I could I would date myself. But that doesn\'t mean that self-confidence is an indicator of whether or not one is deemed as \"hot\" by their peers. I don\'t think I.m \"hot\". Just normal. I.m a <I>normal</I> chick who thinks it.s great to be me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>See, it starts safe enough. You have conversations, you hang out grab some coffee, then it escalates to having lunch where you talk about the big things in your life  like SCHOOL, FAMILY, and your <FONT Color=FFFFFF>crack-head</FONT> of a BOYFRIEND...yet some people don\'t take the hint that all you are interested in is their simple and comfortable \"platonic\" company. Nothing more. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Those that don\'t grab at this concept I place into the \"Tina\" category. Tina is my roomate and Tina is her real name since I really feel no harm in writing down her name since there are thousands upon thousands of \'em out there. She works at a coffee shop/ diner thingy where she has befriended a rather attractive boy, her age, a veggie as she, and with GIRLFRIEND. She doesn\'t see this as a bad thing. She will go right for it and not care whether or not she is breaking up a good relationship. Sure, the guy would be at an equal fault as she for accepting, yet she is the one who planted the seed, she is the one who began this \"domino\" effect of sorts. A good friend of mine has a similar philosophy...he will continue to \"like\" someone whether or not they are with anyone else... which is fine in my eyes because the idea of taking \"action\" does not come into play.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Yeah, so there are these <B>pervy</B> moments I go through. Even with my own best friend and lover...sometimes I feel \"meaty\" as in \" a piece of meat\" and he has to remind me that no, it isn\'t like that all the time. And i love him for that . For admitting he can be pervy...and then not.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> <B>Pervy:</B> the situation bringing upon discomfort during which an acquaintance , simple as a platonic friendship discovers the hurdle made present when either of two people discussed decide that there is an attraction there on the \"pervy\" ones behalf, making the non-pervy person feel awkward...and gypped of a patonic friendship.</P>'),(1508,'<b>I.</b><br>\r\n<br>\r\n...So, I rub the sleep-encrusted detritus from the edge of my eye, gaze out at morning\'s refrain, and revel in the cooled dowry bestowed on us in a new day.  The sunshine of love\'s warmth glowing on your face from the bright, up-top Jersey shore sun.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd it feels good - that faint minutia of feelings that I collect: cleaning out my ears (even with a key when nothing else is handy), sneezing, having someone else wash my hair (ending with a towel-dry and a neck rub), those were the greatest pleasures this side of Nirvana.  But then there was sex.  The immediacy of this feeling marched right over my sublime likes; shook me up.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nLove, intimacy, connection, all martyred as one, and painted on my heart like the passover\'s marked lentil in those moments when the Death Angel approaches.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>II.</b><br>\r\n<br>I drew an octopus last night, even surprising myself with how well it turned out.  Its the design I said I\'d do for that tatoo you always wanted.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>III.</b><br>\r\n<br><i>note to self:</i> Don\'t dance while DJ-ing... it skips the records and breaks the vibe.<br>\r\n<br><b>IV.</b><br>\r\nThank you for sending me to entertain your family this whole weekend while you were visiting friends.  That had to be one of the most worthwhile things that anyone has entrusted me with in a while.  The vulnerability you unknowingly endured through the things they told me of your youth, your past, and what they whimsied about over pool-side dinners made me once again feel that bond we had.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd its there, still, and that makes me very happy, you know.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhat a good weekend.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1509,'<p>i start at least ten sentences a week, usually in an email, sometimes in a <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/postcards/\">postcard</a>, with, \"if you were here...\"</p>\r\n<p><i><a href=\"http://www.kpunk.com/mirah/\">if i could see you</a>, i\'d take off your clothes<BR>\r\nand we\'d lie in the garden and watch the weeds grow</i></p>\r\n<p>in austin, tx i am chainsmoking and drinking iced tea and listening to songs on repeat, mostly this one, yes, mostly \"million miles\" over and over again.</p>\r\n<p><i>i\'m far away and i\'m feeling alone</i></p>\r\n<p>tonight, at jason\'s house, after studying for the GRE at spider house for hours, i was flipping through his <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0670875279/\">relationships book</a>, reading about relationships between cancer IIs and cancer IIIs. i\'m a cancer III. cancer IIs in my life: <a href=\"http://www.wannabejournals.com/~anna\">anna</a>, <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/me/jesse.html\">jesse</a>, <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/me/patrick.html\">patrick</a>, <a href=\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/me/brantley.html\">brantley</a>. people who dropped into my lives and sat there until i paid attention.</p>\r\n<p>reading about myself, the book tells me this:</p>\r\n<p><tt>\"Part of the personal power of Cancer III\'s comes from their ability to control their own needs. In relationships, for example, they would sooner be alone than enter a dubious or unstable partnership.\"</tt></p>\r\n<p><i>the floods and fires might be coming through but<BR>\r\nyou would know just what to do, yeah<BR>\r\nyou would know just what to do</i></p>\r\n<p>one more cigarette. my hand stays on the phone.</p>'),(1510,'\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Last night, I had an unreal dream.  Any lack of continuity is due to the strange dream sequence:</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>I dreamed about an air-crash.  I was atop my apartment building, which was also the departure point for a large NYC-to-London airport, having the runway cross most of the midtown NYC skyline, my apartment roof being the runway\'s Point of No Return, the point at which the planes are committed to takeoff.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>I was there with my significant other.  As the \"airport\" seemed to operate in Greyhound-bus fashion, we were waiting on the tarmac to board the next jumbo-jet bound for London.  While waiting, we were observing the huge jets ahead of us laboriously climb into the air at bold 45 degree angles, engines at full thrust, the air smelling of jet fuel and kerosene, and the noise so loud we couldn\'t even converse.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>We were watching a <i>gigantic</i> six-engined airplane take off- and I mean was it ever huge.  It appeared to be a mile long, jetting off into the noon sun under full thrust, all six engines roaring in cacophony.  It had hundreds of windows and hundreds of passengers, all probably waving to their familes who had come to my rooftop to see them off.  I distinctly remember getting the impression that the plane was an <a href=\"http://www.aeroflot.com/\">Aeroflot</a> plane for some reason.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Anyway, as the plane climbed, we eagerly watched its ascent, knowing we\'d be on the next flight out, in a few minutes.  Suddenly, the rear starboard engine began to vibrate on the grand Aeroflot jet, and actually looked like it was melting and falling away.  The plane began to list and roll to the port side; it was going down for sure.  The plane continued to roll, its albatross wings almost perfectly perpendicular to the horizon, stalling without any lift.  We caught a far-away glimpse of the hundreds of bewildered faces in the windows, a gorgeous sunset gleam reflecting off their window\'s surfaces.  The plane suddenly dripped below the line of sight over the apartment bulldings, downed for sure in the New York Harbor.  A deafening blast sounded, and the sky filled with smoke from the crashed plane.</p>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<p>Everyone was speechless, having watched this enchanting machine take off so powerfully, and we were confused by its immediate disappearance.  Everyone waiting to board the next flight was speechless; we didn\'t know how to react- totally at a loss.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Everyone began boarding the next plane, completely silenced and hushed, but otherwise as if nothing had happened.  My girlfriend and I deliberated over whether we should boad or not, but suddenly found ourselves aboard before we had had any significant time to consider our options.  We began a sudden takeoff in a plane with a beautiful russet-colored suede interior, engines loud and powerful, well-tuned and solid-feeling.  We soared into the sunset over the NYC skyline...</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>And my alarm went off and I woke up from this most disturbing dream.  I\'ve never feared flying and have flown often.  I\'ve never been to London.  I\'ve never flown Aeroflot.  I\'ve never known anyone involved with an air catastrophe.  This dream has obvious surface connotations (loved ones, departures, loss, decisoins, etc), but I know there are some of you out there that are adept at this sort of thing and might see something I\'ve missed.  Any insights would be interesting, if not helpful.....</p> '),(1514,'<br>\r\n<p>The bus-boy came by to wipe my table off after eating- I keep brushing aside the remaining drops of disinfectant-water with my hand, so I can set my book down to write, and he comes back to wipe it again, watching me wiping, thinking the table might still be dirty.  We go through this process three times and he brings me an orange.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>As he\'s wiping the table again, my phone rings.  I answer.  A voice says \"Summer in the city is as perplexing as it is beautiful\".  \"As wonderful as it is humid and as lonely as it is lovely\", I reply.  Even with friends around, folks, lovers, it still irradiates an arcane sense of lonliness.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>So I awake from the strange dreams of my dizzy afternoon nap, put on a record or two and get dressed for the third time today- all in front of the fan- on high, that runs so loud that you can hardly hear the phone ring, not that anyone\'s calling.  I throw on the rotting sandals and trod up 96th street, already growing damp from the humidity, walking uphill past the grocery and drugstore, past the subway stop, downtown.  As I pass the benches in the median of Broadway, I have the urge to sit a while and talk to the neighborhood sages... if only it weren\'t so hot.  I make my way up, further now, to the Strand Diner, open 24 hours, although I strike in the midst of the dinner crowd.  Its not exactly hustling along in here, even so.  I\'ve dined on ice-cream waffles at 3am, staved off chemical twilight with coffee, drank Polish beer while eating eggplant.  Always the same number of people, working, eating, flirting and pouring demitasse cups of scalding espresso.  What a quaint thing to serve on such a hot day.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Me giving up, or simply me resigned to fate, I\'m not completely sure; maybe thats why I behave the way I do.  Maybe I\'ve tried so hard to diagnose my predicament that I\'ve overworked the actual causes and sordid motivations all along.  I can\'t resist a predicament, and sometimes I\'m happiest when I\'m sad.  But, I <i>hate</i> being lonely.  Alone is fine, but lonely on a day like today is terrible; people sweat best in pairs.  Maybe that\'s why I sleep so much, put on a little weight, and forget to shave.  Maybe thats why I spend the afternoon smoking up in Riverside Park, watching the sun set over Jersey\'s palisades, watching the garbage scowls and barges huff by, hauling, mighty diesel tugs pulling cement barges upriver, making that slight wake that I constantly hear from my apartment, the one that laps on the rocks, on the banks, on the sea-wall abuttments, the same sound that the passerby\'s dogs make when they drink from the river\'s edge or from the puddles spawned in the rubble-pocked pot holes on West End.</p><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Life is beautiful all around me, and I know this.  Thats the precise consolation that allows me to go on this way without loathing what I do or how I feel.  Things <i>are</i> coming together nicely, after all, and they have been for several years.  Ok, I think.  So I can aford to hesitate in my summertime stasis, at least until fall...</p>'),(1511,'<PRE>\r\nSpoons\r\n     where are you?\r\nI look in the drawer\r\n     you are not there.\r\nIn the dishwasher\r\n     you hide from me.\r\nSpoons, spoons, spoons\r\n     I need you.\r\n\r\nOnce I had more spoons\r\n     than I needed.\r\nThey were on the counter\r\n     in the dishwasher\r\ntalking sweet spoon language to me\r\n     I\'m a soup spoon\r\n     I\'m a serving spoon\r\n     I\'m the spoon that lies next to you at night\r\n\r\nBut now you are gone\r\n     and I look for you.\r\nWhere are you spoons?\r\n\r\n</PRE>'),(1512,'<P>\r\n<b>Age:</b> 25\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Weight:</b> 180 lbs, give or take\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Height:</b> Unimportant\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Hair:</b> wavy, brown, unruly at times\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Eyes:</b> green\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Waist:</b> 33 in.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Chest:</b> Never measured.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Location:</b> Unimportant\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Identifies as:</b> gay, with all that entails (I only swish on command, though, and though I might have a sibilant \'s\' or two in my speech, I don\'t know that I\'m terribly effeminate, and though I can lapse into being a show-tune listening, \'girlfriend!\' screaming, hard-drug doing, good-time, singalong, AbFab watching bitch, I\'m really a nice guy.)\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Relationship status:</b> Single. And I think I\'m happy about this, though I seem to be obsessed with the idea of looking for someone, passive though I may be about it. I read personal ads, I find new places to write personal ads, I go to bars now and then. I wish I didn\'t have to do any of this. I wish I were content to be alone and to have someone fall into my lap one day. But I can\'t seem to avoid this obsession. Maybe it\'s just that I\'m horny. Perhaps that\'s all. I\'ve always said masturbation is better than awkward sex with a stranger. Maybe I\'m just bored. It\'s a terrible thing to be in life: bored. But you know I\'m not desperate, and I have friends whom I love. I just don\'t want to get left in the dust, either, when they all run off with their significant others. I refuse to be the perpetually single and wittily self-deprecating supportive gay friend.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Tatoos:</b> None\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Piercings:</b> Unimportant\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Religion:</b> Unimportant -- well, I take that back. Belief is important, a sense of connectivity in the world. Spiritual feeling. Such wonky things exist, and god probably does too, in some form, some force or other, an ordering force. These things are beyond our weak understanding. We need to attach a face and a name and sing \"Praise Jesus\" on Sundays to perpetuate our belief, and there is nothing wrong with this need. I myself don\'t need to perform rituals really, but then, I get depressed sometimes like everybody else and think we\'re all just ants and everything is futile and dumb. But hey, it beats the hell out of church!\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Race:</b> Unimportant (but I have the luxury of being so blasÃ© because I\'m white).\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Body type:</b> Aw, shucks. I dunno. I\'m vaguely in shape, unhappy with my mid-section at the moment.  Let\'s call me \"Athletic\" but let\'s not get any unrealistic ideas.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>What I\'m looking for:</b> This is tough. I keep saying I don\'t want a relationship, because I got out of one a year ago, and it exhausted me, and I feel sort of sick of love. It nauseates me to think about taking responsibility for another person\'s feelings again, and to think about someone looking in my eyes and searching for \"love\" somehow. I hate the expectation of romance, the notion that someone\'s going home after a date and hoping I might be \"the one.\" Or, more depressingly, I hate the thought of dating, and always going home disappointed with the wasted effort, the steady stream of meetings and repetitive conversations (Oh, you\'re a <i>writer</i>... what do you <i>write</i>?) that never lead anywhere because you\'re just tossing out lures and never guaranteed a catch. I guess I\'m just looking for something that feels natural, not at all forced, and something that\'s right on many levels, passionate, but cerebral, and centering rather than destabilizing. But hopefully it won\'t take up too much time, because I have work to get done, and friends to hang out with, and I need my sleep, after all.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Favorite food:</b> burrito. \r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Favorite quote:</b> Christ, there are hundreds. Don\'t make me commit to just one.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>If you could be one place in the world right now, where would you be?:</b> at home mopping. Or in Venice, at the Biennale.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Favorite city:</b> New York\r\n</P><P>\r\n<b>Anything else you\'d like to add?:</b> I\'m a romantic who\'s lost the taste for romance. I\'ve heard people say that they\'re emotionally dead inside, and that\'s not me really. I think I\'m just being protective of myself right now. That and my needs are being satisfied by my friends. So, if you\'re interested in me, please know that I rarely answer the phone and unless you\'re especially funny and have a really hot body, I\'m probably only going to want to see you once or twice, and then I\'m not going to answer your phone calls, or your email, because it will pain me too much to tell you that I don\'t feel much of a connection, and it\'s not going anywhere, so you\'re basically just a drain on my psyche and a waste of my time.\r\n</P>'),(1513,'<P>It is a nice hot day in s. california. i ride my bike to the pool where i teach swim lessons and there is my boss, sitting at her desk shuffling a bunch of papers in her hand. She tells me very stoicly that I have to come in and lifeguard that Saturday. I remember telling her ...before i began working for this \"ex\" friend of mine, that i would not be available on the weekends. i guess it didnt sink in. so i have to work this saturday and head to N.hollywood a tad late.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it isnt just that. it is the fact that in order to run X-country i need to take a class this summer in order to be eligible. Yeah, well she told me that she would <I> see</i> if I could take any classes because we were booked with students and we needed every instructor out there.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>I understand that. In a sense I am \"flaking\". But you know what, SCHOOL and RUNNING have priority over guarding and teaching at some crap ass pool at some crap ass University. Yes, I made a committment to HER to work all summer, but now things have changed. And i always make room for change.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Now there is my roomie, soon to be EX-roomie, who goes by the same name as my boss at the pool. She lets guys go down on her, goes down on them, its called mutual masterbation city with her YET she loves judging our other roomate who has actual intercourse. As far as I am conserned, you do certain things that are sexual and what is the difference between recieving head from a guy than having his member inside you....hey, two fingers vs.penis...Its the same IDEA. Yet, because she still has her CHERRY intact she feels superior. I have to LIVE listening to her make chiding comments about the WHY behind me not brushing my hair. This is coming from someone who turned Veggie to impress a guy. YUCK </p>\r\n\r\n<P>She listens to Weezer not because she actually feels the music....I doubt she feels anything after she told me that my relatives(who DO NOT buy german cars due to us having had family die due to the Holocaust) should get over it and that she probably didnt understand because she had not experienced it.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>BITCH its called fucking have some empathy.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Poser ass whore ....ugh....*sigh* </p>'),(1515,'<p>Why are members of my peer group getting married and having babies? I am 25. Perhaps I shouldn\'t be shocked.</p>\r\n<p>but what about those nights spent taking bong hits and deconstructing the work of art in the age of mechanical reproduction? the cyborg as post-feminist/marxist media icon? <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.cohums.ohio-state.edu/english/people/Choi.192/courses/99win574/pub/BarbaraT/ong.html\">orality</a> as it empowers women on the internet? is there still time for discussion/debate/disagreement/denial on how to translate political self-empowerment into creative discourse? was it all pillow talk, girls? are we done with our own experience?</p>\r\n<p>or am i just too much of an old timer to understand that given the <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.artbyte.com/mag/mar_apr_01/letigre_content.shtml\">vast smorgasboard</a> of choices available to a woman today, she could still want to wipe shitty asses and build lasting resentments with someone she used to have sex with?</p>\r\n<p><a target=\"window\" href=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/shower.html\"><img src=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/sam.gif\" width=\"200\" height=\"220\" alt=\"I ran out of film so here is a crap sketch that betrays my feelings of tenderness toward the creature\" border=\"0\" align=\"left\"></a> excuse me, the heat  in Assachusetts is getting to me. My role is already written. I am \"auntie egg\": in thai, \"na kai\". The cool thing about my mother\'s side of the family is that all of our aunts have nicknames like internet people. I have an \"auntie red\", \"auntie black\", the abbrevation of what is roughly translated as \"auntie \'girl who costs a lot\'\" (after a popular play title in Thailand). My cousins call my mother \"Ba Eh\", because as a child she used to be addicted to playing the thai version of peekaboo, \"JAH EH\".</p>\r\n<p>But as enticing as the thought of squeezing out a litter of tiny robots is at this crossroads of my twenties, I just cant get into it. Perhaps I am too lazy/selfish/low-pain-threshold-having. I probably could use overpopulation as an excuse. Perhaps it is my bloated sense of entitlement to my own time as an \"artist\". the astrologer told me that Mars rules both artistic creativity AND childbirth. My mars is in gemini, which means Im a big bisexual indecisive wordy untrustworthy and distractible building. I keep wondering if chicks like Kim Gordon do most of the baby-work AND show up to record vocals while the husband takes bong hits AND shows up to record vocals. I know it\'s possible that two partners share equally in the domestic and child-rearing work, I\'ve just never seen it happen.</p>\r\n<p><a target=\"window\" href=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/barefoot.html\"><img src=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/clink.gif\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" alt=\"this is some drunken nonsensical shit talking\" border=\"0\" align=\"right\"></a>Some women feel guilty or apologetic about not having a maternal instinct. I do feel a maternal instinct, I just dont want to get my labia ripped in half. I do not know one female who has not had pregnancy dreams/nightmares, who has not considered the question of whether to birth or not to birth. After all, it\'s one of the things the Mutha built us to do.</p> \r\n<p>I imagine I may eat shit one day and all my blathering at family gatherings will come back to bite me in the stitched labia, that i will, as southern gentlemen tell me, get \"<a target=\"window\" href=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/shower.html\">swept off my feet</a>\" and set them into metal stirrups. heave ho. even the toughest girl I know, Miss Eve, is in love right now. She must be, cause she might consider revoking her long professed lack of vision for children.</p>\r\n<p>Our roles are switching. Our future projection used to be that I would be the loud sweaty Mama, she the cool, glamorous, idolized Aunt.</p>\r\n<p>so far my sister Erika has had one year old Sam (pictured above). My cousin Chom, who is nearly my age, has one in the oven that we think is a pussycat. As Na Kai, I make up humorous song lyrics to Beatles tunes, or sing ready-made cat power laments on the drudgery of adult cocktail party [baby shower] reality:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>they tell me what they do\r\nI dont know what theyre talking about.\r\nI did not start this conversation. I dont intend to keep talkin about it.\r\nthey tell me about it.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sam appreciates that. my singing keeps him from wailing in my ear. and he likes to have his back zubberted.</p>\r\n<p> I look at a picture of my cousin Chom and I when we wore pink tutus and posed with maracas for embarassing photos that would be displayed forever on top the piano, and I remember how clear our personalities were at that age. it makes me jittery to think of that ready-made person who is ready to rip out of her with devil laughter and make her own insistent dent in the earth.</p>\r\n<p>Now my fantasy of myself is Auntie Egg, driving up in a Cougar convertible in a red do-rag and big sunglasses, delivering books by bell hooks AND \"our bodies, ourselves\" on your 12th birthday, you unborn lovely.  I will show you your first porn, play you your first Patti Smith record, and send you a plane ticket to California or New York where we will attend avant-garde costume parties and you will experiment with bisexuality. I will visit by myself on hot summer days just to hand you exotic and original surprise works of art, and speed away into the sunset while you gape in glamorized awe.</p>\r\n<p>That is where the fantasy ends. I am not certain where I am speeding off to.</p>'),(1516,'<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">I woke up today midmorning. i\'ve come\r\nto the conclusion that i have bad sleeping habits, that i oversleep, so\r\ni\'ve been setting my alarm to annoy myself in the mornings in hopes of\r\nactually waking. so far it hasn\'t really worked. i wake up and i roll out\r\nof bed, turn on the computer, get the coffe going, put in my eyes, and\r\nsit down to work on my class journal. i look at the date, AUGUST 6TH! what?\r\ni thought the 6th was wednesday. damn, i get my bookbag, fish out my midterm\r\n(class generated, cause that\'s the kind of froofy-express-your-emotion-everyone-is-involved\r\nclass i\'m in) and start working on it. it\'s due tonight, i thought it was\r\ndue wednesday. i thought that later in the afternoon i would take a look\r\nat it, and actually start early (2days is early for me). but no. it\'s due\r\ntonight. no problem i think, only three questions, one page each. amazingly\r\nenough i\'m trying to find smaller fonts, eliminating my boarders, thining\r\nout the redundant stuff. one page each. the phone rings, it\'s momma of\r\nthe creature, confirming a shopping date later on the week. sure i say,\r\nwednesday is great. we talk about her move to eugene oregon in a week,\r\ni am very unsupportive. we talk about babies, and mom\'s groups, and getting\r\na message liscence up there. then i talk about my ex-room mate, who i think\r\nhates me, cause i snapped at her a few months ago. she never calls me anymore,\r\nand i can\'t say i\'m truly sorry. then we talk about unreasonable people.\r\nmomma of the creature notices the time, reconfirm wedensday, and hangs\r\nup. a nice break. i finish, i decide that i\'ll take a shower and then run\r\nto longs (god, i love longs.) suddenly like 5 aim windows pop up and all\r\nthese people are talking to me asking me stuff. ummm, i gotta go. <i>hey\r\nyou walk lola right? so you\'re over at the bend, fedex is coming to the\r\nbend with something for me, can you let them in?</i> uhhh. <i>hey! how\'s\r\nit going? have you gone to see lolita today?</i> uhhhh.Â  <i>yeah!\r\nvegas, we\'re going woohoo! why are you renting a car, and not driving yours?</i>\r\nummm. <i>so goat, when\'s the next time you\'re coming upto portland? </i>ahhhh.Â \r\n<i>yes, sure, gotta go. no not yet, but i\'ll try to stop in later, gotta\r\ngo bye. egg can\'t drive my car, this way we can share driving, gotta go,\r\nbye. i dunno, probably not before thanksgiving, gotta go, bye. jump</i>\r\nin the shower. squeeze squeeze squeeze VO5 shampoo bottle trying to get\r\nsome out. get a little try to lather my abundant hair. rinse. no repeat.\r\ngod love that mane and tail conditioner, it works wonders. soap up myself.\r\nrinse. jump out of the shower. get dressed. hungry. no food in the house.\r\nwho cares goat, you don\'t need food. 3.30pm head out to longs. damn pedestrians\r\non piedmont. get there, park. get out of car. hmm purse feels light, a\r\nsudden picture of wallet on couch. fuck! get back in car, take howe instead\r\nof piedmont. damn pedestrians. get home, run up, grab wallet, run down,\r\nget in car. drive up broadway to avoid pedestrians. damn fucking red light.\r\nhungry. HUNGRY! shit, i\'m hungry, hmmm. get to the parking lot. uh. fuck\r\nlongs, must go to safeway and get some sort of food. run in, grab pita\r\nbread, tortilla bread, cream cheese, and some 5min ravioli. get to the\r\ntill. shift change. wait a few minutes as he punches out and closes the\r\ntill, and then she punches in and opens the till. <i>safeway club card,\r\nma\'am?</i> umm, yeah. swipe.<i> please swipe club card again.</i> swipe.\r\n<i>please swipe club card again.</i> swipe. <i>please swipe club card again.</i>\r\nswipe, ugh. *beep* *beep* *beep* oh no. <i>thank you ms.goat, your pita\r\nwas free, would you like help out with that?</i> what do i look like? i\r\nbought 4 items, of course i don\'t need help with that. <i>no thank you.</i>\r\nwalk out, jump in car. hmm, which is the best way? what the fuck. finally\r\nget home. 4:15. boil water, make pasta, scarff it down. 4:40 damn. lola!\r\ndamn. 20min. no problem. run over to the bend, take lola out to the roof.\r\nshe wobbles. hmm. i hope she\'s ok. she pees then looks at me and just sits\r\nthere.<i> what? that\'s it? run around! shit! do something, this is it till\r\ndaddy comes home</i>. nothing. we go back downstairs. i refill water bowl.\r\nfernando hisses at lola, meows at me. <i>what? what! it\'s not nearly dinner\r\ntime. lola get away from fernando!</i> *meow* *meow* *meow* fernando braves\r\nthe presence of lola and comes to rub me. hmm. <i>did lola eat your food\r\nor something?</i> *meow* *hisss* oh, well. give fernando some kitty treats.\r\nnot enough, more meowling. shit. <i>ok you damn cat, here, a little bit\r\nof cat food but don\'t tell jay or egg, she complains you\'re fat.</i> ok,\r\nanimals set i head for the door. aha! 4:55. run back home get in car and\r\nhead for school. 5minutes to spare, hmm, i\'ll get there early and grab\r\na drink or something. no. 580 is horrible. no reason. 92 SUV tries to run\r\nme off the road. 101 ok. ralston, i exit. i\'m half a mile from school and\r\nit take 25 minutes. no reason. find a space, park and run into class. <i>please\r\nturn your midterms over, on the back i would like you to answer these 6\r\nquestions: what grade do you want? what grade do you deserve? what grade\r\ndo you think i\'ll give?....</i>goddamn psychiatrist for a teacher. um,\r\nyeah. i want an F i think i deserve a C and you\'ll give me an A. learn\r\nsome stuff. something that involved watching a clip from <i>ghandi</i>\r\nand <i>in search of bobby fischer</i>. hmm. class is over, i head to the\r\ncar. get in, turn on. <i>hey mickey</i> comes blaring at me from the radio.\r\ni drive home. a CHP pulls out in front of me. i slow down, i drive slower,\r\nbut not limit, just slower than usual the rest of the way. it take me longer\r\nthan usual to get home. i arrive. get to the computer. chat. eyes unfocused\r\ni switch to word. fuck! my journal.</font></p>'),(1517,'<p>betsy, hilly, mindy, jody, kitty, casey, jenny, then a jill but her nick name ended in y. there was a stephanie and a christie too. many times i wish olivia ended with a \"y\" or an \"ie.\" i promissed an \"le\" not to even think about it, and of course along the same lines of that, there is an amy that is by bestfriends girl. brandy is one of my best friends, so that won\'t work. can jesse be one? nah he likes girls. I had a friend jeff that i called jeffy all the time.  when kimberly leaned against me the other night, i thought i was in for it. i had a crush on a sally, but that never happened. there was one that ended in an \"a,\" but we don\'t talk anymore so that doesnt count anyway. I met a new \"y\" this past weekend. I am going to see her at a part\"y\" this weekend. i am excited, speechless, and nearly faint - part of me wishes she had a middle name that I could call her by just to break the cycle. </p><p>oh yah! I forgot about mommy and daddy (and larryD), of course I love them too.</p>'),(1518,'<p>i mixed a bunch of randomly read lines and thoughts of my own together and heres what i got:</p>\r\n<pre>\r\nwhat a difference 3 degrees makes\r\nafter the cake was eaten and\r\nwith warm desert air \r\nsmorgasbord, offering presentationsrannging from\r\ntouch and go freinds\r\nlengdary cost cutting ultraefficiency\r\nscensters and hip interior\r\nwhy tired?why wired?\r\nsneaking peaking leaking reeking\r\nthan normal by the end oh the month\r\nto chalk stripes and tapestry\r\ndemonsrtations are irresistable to the media\r\npeople change cause times and circumstances dont fit\r\nbut theres no guarantee that after\r\nvetoed any all-toille rooms\r\nthen reattaches them\r\nlegitamite agencies will not ask</pre>'),(1519,'<p>\r\nor maybe i\'m the hemoroid that keeps flaring up on you,<br>\r\ni can\'t exactly figure it out anymore.<br>\r\noh baby, you and me, doll, we\'ve come a long way...oh ho ho, baby...<br>\r\ni\'m usually wrong in all the scenarios we muster up in our conversations,<br>\r\ndon\'t you see?<br>\r\nit\'s clearly some kind of misnomer on my part, clearly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>EVERYONE...EVERYONE! GATHER AROUND, GATHER AROUND!\r\n</p><p>\r\nI JUST WANT YOU ALL TO KNOW THAT I HAPPEN TO ADORE THIS FANTABULOUS YOUNG LADY THAT YOU ALL KNOW AS EGG.  SHE\'S A PAIN IN MY ASSHOLE, BUT NEEDLESS TO SAY, I\'M A PAIN IN HERS AS WELL.  NEVERTHELESS, I\'D STILL DRINK CACTUS NEEDLES FOR HER AT ANY GIVEN MOMENT.  OK...YOU MAY DISPERSE.\r\n</p><p>\r\noh, but nubby...it\'s not a joke.<br>\r\nwhat\'s happened here?  is it my bad one liner jokes?<br>\r\nis it the cold, heartless, and insensitive things i say?<br>\r\n\"Dear John...\"<br>\r\noh nubby, dear, sweet, nubby...the trigger\'s so sensitive these days,<br>\r\nand i\'m not just talking about yours.<br>\r\nor maybe it isn\'t even your trigger,<br>\r\nmaybe i simply squeezed to hard.<br>\r\noh nub, do you remember those golden days of high flyin\' and parties every night.<br>\r\nthe boys, the coke, the weed, the money, the fun and free spirit.<br>\r\nah nubby, i\'m gettin\' all weepy and shit over here.<br>\r\nc\'mon squires...take for granted:<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\n        1.To consider as true, real, or forthcoming; anticipate correctly. <br>\r\ni am truly frustrated<br>\r\nand i can feel my hemoroids flaring up.<br>\r\nit\'s a welcoming sign just like my period.<br>\r\nof course the definition has a number 2. i won\'t bother to put up.</P>\r\n<p>can\'t we go back to the good ol days of plastic garbage bags and late night editorials?<br>\r\nhave i changed that much?  i mean i have gray hair now, and live in l.a.<br>\r\nand i may drink evian and have fake boobs and eyelashes, <br>\r\nand a chihuahua named willie, but i haven\'t change that much, have i?<br>\r\nnubby, i really would drink a pint of cactus needles for you,<br>\r\nand then i\'d wash it down with %150 proof moonshine.<br>\r\nbut that\'s not to say you\'re not a louse.<br>\r\ngod help me, but i\'ll love my hemoroid till the day i die.</p><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1520,'<br>\r\nFleeing the world in winded words<br>Hiding in shadows of unkempt silk<br>Drifting through spaces of warm breast milk<br>Consumed by the cold, bitter flame.<br>Drawing two long beaten swords<br>and burying metal deep into flesh<br>Fighting the whimsy of of contemptuous words<br>Fleeing the death to which it lures.<br>Trying to find a framework within<br>to discover the truth of their lies and sin<br>Eating away their very own souls<br>and ever onward their hunger grows.<br>The light pours through them leaving dark<br>until someone else strikes a spark<br>and shows them a place where his warmth glows<br>and ever onward their hunger grows<br>but the light pours through them and does not linger<br>so they slip back to blindness and groping trigger.<br>His tears fall like rain and threaten to drown him<br>He is consumed by cold, bitter flame<br>The veil has been dropped, his small light extinguished<br>Will his soul burn burn free again?'),(1521,'<p>I am your Medulla Oblongata. Without me you could not regulate your heart rate, blood pressure, or breathing.</p>\r\n<p>They seem to be watching you. The silver black boxes with beady eyes, recording your day to day monotomy in a stream of numbers. The cold cathode, light turns to current recorded to tape.</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1521_1.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p>\r\n<p>I alert you to their presence. You do not know. You only feel. Hairs on the back of your neck stand on end. You feel the burning of their glare.</p>\r\n<p>Who are they?</p>\r\n'),(1522,'<P>i like to sleep.  it\'s one of the only things i truly enjoy doing.  despite often having disturbing dreams, and my tendency to sprawl in one uncomfortable position and stay there all night (and wake up sore and stiff), i love to sleep.  it\'s rare that i can do one thing without doing something else at the same time--i read while i\'m walking, i play games and answer email while i work, i write letters to my friends during class, i crochet while i watch television.</p>\r\n\r\n<P><font size=1>granted, i listen to music while i sleep, but i always have music on anyway.</font></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i like to sleep. i love to sleep. and what that means is that i hate waking up, getting out of bed, washing my face and brushing my teeth and getting dressed, and then leaving the house for the harsh, cruel world.  i don\'t even like waking up on days when i have nothing to do. i don\'t like leaving my bed, leaving my soft quilts and fluffy pillows, opening my eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and so it comes down to hitting my snooze button six or seven times. sometimes instead of hitting the snooze button, i hit the \"alarm reset\" button, and the alarm doesn\'t go off more than once.  then i\'m jolted awake by the cat, or by a slamming door, or by my internal clock, and i realize that not only have i awoken too late to get to work by 9:30am (which is when my workday starts; let\'s hear it for the <a href=\"http://www.tor.com\">publishing industry</a>) but it\'s already 9:38am, so i am probably not even going to arrive before 10am.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it worries me that no matter what time i show up, nobody mentions it.  nobody says, \"why didn\'t you get here at 9:30?\" nobody says, \"you have only shown up before 10am twice in the past three weeks!\"  my boss thinks it\'s cute and funny, and laughed at me when i took summer classes and dragged my ass out of bed at six o\'clock every morning, and bit the head off anyone who spoke to me about sleeping. her boss, The Big Guy (aka: The Man) knows i get all my work done, so he doesn\'t care.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it\'s nice, but it\'s sort of creepy.  every other job i\'ve had, i\'ve had to punch the clock (albeit figuratively), so to have this freedom now is strange.  and knowing i am going to be late, i always stop to write email, to call someone, to clean the catbox, to listen to a song three times, to check everyone\'s website to see if anybody had anything to say, and sometimes to say something myself.</p>\r\n'),(1523,'<p>we go undercover.</p>\r\n<p>because there are too many thoughts for words. or times to share. and lost\r\nhopes...mostly none at all. we wondered if you cared.</p>\r\n<p>and i saw you with my mother. my mother in a wheelchair. </p>\r\n<p>the child cares for all. takes care of all. to the end. till death do us part. add another scar.</p>\r\n<p>this is lice in your hair. and blackened bruised eyes. this is oozing hatred. deathwishes. and wanting to die.</p>\r\n<p>i am not lost without you.</p>\r\n<p>because you have me tied up inside.</p>\r\n<p>i am just lost. always have been always will be.</p>\r\n<p>because you held my hand and made me cry. </p>\r\n<p>a savior is not what i need. nor what you long to be.</p>\r\n<p>time to let go. time to be free.</p>'),(1524,'<P>these days, i\'m taking the antidepressant paxil (10 mg - little orangey caps).  one of its interesting side effects (aside from the sexual dysfunction) is that it gives you very intense dreams - unlike anything you\'ve had before.  for your perusal:</P><P>it\'s sometime in the future.  i\'m living with my whole extended family in this modernist mansion - all white interior - on a beach somewhere.  i\'m out on the balcony and see that we\'re under attack by what seem to be heavy metal fans, real nasty-looking mothers.</P><P>i signal my buddy/valet/something or other (he\'s a middle-aged asian man: i don\'t want to think my subconscious is racist, but anyway) to activate the barricades and security measures, but it\'s too late.  they come pouring over the fence.  this gong rings - the doorbell.  my family and i go into the foyer/entrance hall.  there\'s a huge painting of andy warhol opposite the front door.  it looks like a self-portrait, square, about twenty-by-twenty.  it\'s the pic of him with his hair all spiked-looking.  his face and hair are blue, background black.</P><P>i look through the peephole.  it\'s george fut, my old neighbor from athens.  he was always coming over to \"borrow some cable\" \'cause the people i lived with had a satellite dish, and he didn\'t even have a tv.  i open up this massive three foot thick white door, and he shoots me in the stomach!  like wile e. coyote, i\'m okay again in three seconds.  we let him in anyway.  must protect ol\' george from the horde.</P><P>headbangers start busting through the windows.  i shoot them with an invisible (maybe imaginary) gun.  they fall, succumbing to bloodless, unseen wounds .  they are legion.  i don\'t know what happens to my family.  my buddy takes me back to one of the bedrooms.  suddenly, we\'re in my grandma\'s old house.  he pulls me into the closet and slides the door to.  it\'s a secret elevator.  we are whisked down into a bunker of some kind which looks suspiciously like my grandma\'s dining room.</P><P>i sit at the table with a shirtless mark wahlberg.  and i don\'t mean the present day one, the actor with a little age on him.  i\'m talking about marky mark of ten years ago, he of the fabulous boy-meat, always showcasing the body in the videos and underwear ads.  from where i\'m sitting, i can\'t tell what kind of panties he\'s wearing, if any.</P><P>a giant green glass egg cup rests on the table between us.  it holds a pastry of some sort, the size of a watermelon, sitting on one end in the cup.  it has a flaky golden crust.  i reach out and feel the top of it.  it\'s a little squishy, like half-full water balloon.  i comment to marky that it feels like a breast.  we dig into it with long-handled gold spoons.  it turns out to be an apple tart like they used to have at wendy\'s</P><P>after about two bites apiece, i get up on the table and kick the dessert off onto the floor then turn and grab marky under the arms and pull him up on the table.  ha ha, no undies.  we take turns fucking each other with this stainless steel or chrome dildo, then i toss it away into the pastry and go to town on him.  then i look up.  my buddy (the asian guy) and my grandma are standing over by the china cabinet watching us.  i wake up in mid-stroke.</P><P>what do you think it MEANS?  tell me quick.</P>  \r\n'),(1525,'<P> trickle down the side of buildings</P>\r\n\r\n<P> we living ,dying crying on our couches to a movie half-ass made</P>\r\n\r\n<P> because what it is;we could be</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I sleep in the warmth of where your body lay</P>\r\n<P> shadow outline your soul to mine</P>\r\n\r\n<P> how quickly this clock seems to make time go by</P>\r\n\r\n<P> shall we break it into a million filaments into the night sky. they call them stars</P>\r\n\r\n<P> reminders of our short time, short space, double-parked life of mine.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> my white raindrop cat is off to no good in the kitchen</P>\r\n\r\n<P> how the mornings vanish into afternoons</P>\r\n\r\n<P> not alone. but content. you asked how I was about everything last night</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"everything what?\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I am full, so full sometimes I feel an eruption coming on of uncontrolled laughing squeals..</P>\r\n\r\n<P> you delight and frighten me with your understanding</P>\r\n\r\n<P> and laying in bed this morning after the alarm had been pushed off to sleep, time and time again</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i think everything I love is in this room</P>\r\n\r\n<P> moving from shades of green and blue</P>'),(1526,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\" color=\"#0000CC\">Dear Jock \r\n  Strap (egg supporter):</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\" color=\"#0000CC\"> Go ahead \r\n  and watch me--with your wife! Hope you won\'t mind<br>\r\n  when I <a href=\"http://www.webone.com.au/%7Egreggles/wwwboard/messages/3726.html\" target=\"_blank\">back \r\n  her ass up</a> against the window seconds before I drill it. <br>\r\n  Oh, don\'t worry, I won\'t tell her you\'re \"watching.\" </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\" color=\"#0000CC\">Bull Woods, \r\n  Corporate Prick</font></p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.bsnn.net/who%20sucks/Chicken.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://www.bsnn.net/cgi-bin/chicken.jpg\" width=\"195\" height=\"274\" border=\"0\"></a></p>\r\n<p>\r\n<pre>\r\n\r\n----- Original Message ----- \r\nFrom: \"leonard\"\r\nTo: <a href=\"http://www.goldeneggs.com.au/home.html\" target=\"_blank\">Golden Eggs Farm</a>\r\nSent: Friday, August 03, 2001 3:57 PM\r\nSubject: scrambled\r\n\r\n<i>&gt; Mr. Woods,\r\n\r\n&gt; I know you are farming <a href=\"http://www.tao.ca/%7Eban/1199MSeggs.htm\" target=\"_blank\">GM</a> <a href=\"http://www.gaiabooks.co.uk/environment/dioxin.html\" target=\"_blank\">eggs</a> and I am not happy about it.\r\n&gt; If I were you, I would be careful and stop farming these\r\n&gt; products. However, if you don\'t stop farming these eggs, \r\n&gt; I will personally make sure you pay. \r\n&gt; \r\n&gt; I\'m watching you,\r\n&gt;\r\n&gt; An organic <a href=\"http://www.historicaltrekking.com/tips/messages/41.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">egg</a> supporter </i>'),(1527,'<P>Is right now. I wish I could change things and not had gotten all those F\'s so I could run X-Country and be eligible. But the big-man has won it seems, since I cannot run. Me, the slowest or third slowest on the team, yet I really love the sport.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>And I can\'t run on my own. I haven\'t the patience. I haven\'t the will-power. I will miss getting up at 6:50 in the a.m., half asleep and sore. Meeting at the brick wall we all would be at by 7. Listening to our fat coach gurgle off some command, and then go...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Nothing more beautiful than pushing yourself to the extreme and then having girls, understand what you are going through...going to the races...going to everything. </p>\r\n\r\n<P>I guess when you are a poor student that is what you get.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1528,'<P>falling face first and eyes open</p>\r\n\r\n<P>lids full</p> \r\n\r\n<P>water filled lids</p>\r\n\r\n<P>wet cheeks</p>\r\n\r\n<P>how can i ask without begging</p>\r\n\r\n<P>how can i be without feeling</p> \r\n\r\n<P>how can i want without screaming</p>\r\n\r\n<P>walking under tree</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i remember what a have been</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i.ve become</p>\r\n\r\n<P>falling face first</p>\r\n\r\n<P>lids full</p>\r\n\r\n<P>cheeks moist</p>\r\n\r\n<P>this chlorine won.t wash off</p>'),(1529,'10. The cheese lady at Piedmont Grocery who gives love advice, and knows about cheese.\r\n<p>\r\n9. <a href=\"http://www.senate.gov/~jeffords/524statement.html\">Senator Jim Jeffords, VT (I)</a>\r\n<p>\r\n8. This kid in Brooklyn, to whom I sold a couple of bookshelves when I was broke and trying to move to California, who didn\'t give me the money when we agreed to it (he already had the shelves in his apartment), because he said he was broke and stretching things just to pay rent, and I said \"OK, send it to me next month,\" and even though I gave up emailing him about it six months ago, he sent me the money (the week I got laid off), out of the blue.\r\n<p>\r\n7. The sometimes irritating habit of Californians to strike up conversations on the street, in line at the store, or wherever, just to say, \"Hi there, we\'re experiencing this together, and we don\'t have to be silent strangers.\"\r\n<p>\r\n6. swinney.org!!\r\n<p>\r\n5. The enduring popularity of sketch comedy.\r\n<p>\r\n4. The fact that some folks took the time to put <a href=\"http://www.bartleby.com\">all this stuff</a> on the internet.<p>\r\n3. Electronic music, and hip-hop, and any music that makes one feel like our time does have a unique music of its own (besides teen pop).<p>\r\n2. <a href=\"http://www.mcsweeneys.net/\">McSweeney\'s</a> (especially, but not only, the <a href=\"http://www.mcsweeneys.net/links/lists/\">lists</a>)<p>\r\n1. New books are getting written every day.<p>\r\n'),(1530,'<p>today we are with the flow. getting free lunches <br>\r\nand gratuitous lovin.</p>\r\n<p>visited my friend Dave at his work. he owns a music shop. an older,\r\nfortysomething guy...i knew him back when i was 18 and struggling to be an adult\r\nand living with the abusive exboyfriend.</p>\r\n<p>he sort of adopted me, although he\'s very honest about having pervy thoughts.\r\nwhich actually i like. i prefer to know up front what people are thinking, to\r\nhave it all out on the table...and Dave is just one of those kinds of people\r\nwho\'ll tell you anything.</p>\r\n<p>one of the true eccentrics that i know.</p>\r\n<p>mostly bizarre. always on the go. more than a bit scattered. and extremely\r\naggressive.<br>\r\nexuding genius.</p>\r\n<p>so i show up at his shop because it is hard to catch him on the phone, and he almost never calls back. he says i am his lucky charm. it is slow when i walk in and sitdown and 20 minutes later the place is hopping. it never fails.</p>\r\n<p>he tells me he adores me, and lets me rub his belly and call him Buddha.</p>\r\n<p>he is a huggy kind of guy. squeezing me. massaging me. i say, \'pet me Dave\' and he pets me. smoothing down my hair like Gramma use to do when i was a kid.</p>\r\n<p>he is answering phones, talking to customers, and writing out checks for the UPS guy...all inbetween grasping some part of me. telling me he wants to set me up with a friend. then telling me he won\'t do it. he want\'s me all to himself.</p>\r\n<p>dave: how\'s your love life<br>\r\nme: what love life<br>\r\ndave: don\'t talk me like that you naughty girl<br>\r\nme: i want a boyfriend Dave<br>\r\ndave: men are scum<br>\r\nme: i was thinking of dating, not sainthood<br>\r\ndave: and you\'re not seeing someone<br>\r\nme: women<br>\r\ndave: well you dress like a boy<br>\r\nme: i tried womens clothes but noone can get past the breasts<br>\r\ndave: yea tell me about it [drooling]<br>\r\nme: drama queen<br>\r\ndave: who cares what\'s on the outside as long as you love what\'s underneath</p>\r\n\r\n<p>thanks buddha, but i think i knew that.</p>'),(1531,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1531_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'center\'>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>I make you late for work</P>\r\n<p>The spider in the shower isn\'t really a problem. Is it?</P>\r\n<p>Just pick the little fella up and throw him out the window</P>\r\n<p>Black legs. Eight legs. Beady eyes</P>\r\n<p>Because of me you stand rigid. afraid to move forward</P>\r\n<p>Late for work again Jack?</P>'),(1532,'<font color=\"green\">Peter</font>:I had a bad dream last night, actually, that i accidentally opened the back of my camera while rewinding the roll, exposing the film... in my dream, i could see the actual pictures on the exposed film...<br> \r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1532_1.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p><br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\">Jamazon</font>:In a completely un-related dream last night, I dreamt i was in a plane crash. Details are hazy but i remember an old lady getting her face burned off. Bizzarely i was taking photos of this with my Smena 35.<br>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1532_3.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p><br>\r\n<font color=\"green\">Peter</font>:Oh my gosh. I had a dream about a plane crash two nights ago! Weird. I posted a write-up about it on <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1510.txt\">swinney</a>.<br> \r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1532_4.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p><br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">nannerbug</font>:My friend told me a different, less traffic, route from LAX to home. I tried this last night as I was dropping someone off. I was driving east and there were all these planes, one right behind the other, at least five, just <b>HOVERING</b>. I guess they were waiting to land, but I never realized that those planes got so damn close to one another. I almost crashed my car I was so mesmerized by the sight of it. I don\'t know how they don\'t crash into each other!<br> \r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1532_5.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p><br>\r\n<font color=\"green\">Peter</font>:I know this probably sounds weird, but since my dream, I\'ve been pretty terrified of seeing planes. admittedly, the image of planes hovering, mid-air, en masse, really freaks me out. I was a-ok with planes and flying until two nights ago. how strange is that? I think it\'ll pass, though.<br> \r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1532_6.jpg\' height=\'150\'></p><br>\r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>:I know exactly what you mean man. Something about gigantic tons of manmade steel flying. but I keep on telling myself, flying is still the safest way to travel. Comparing to traffic accidents and all.<br><br>\r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>: Now our British friends are making the A-340xx, which is a even bigger airplane with two decks, with casino, showers, gym, amusement park, parking garage, and a subway. imagine if only one of the engine went kaput.<br><br>\r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>:Check out this weird site called <a href=\"http://www.amigoingdown.com\">Am I Going Down?</a>. You enter the city you\'re from and to, airline, time, month. then the computer gives you an odd if you\'re going down.<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>:when you exit LAX, go down Century Blvd toward the 405 not the 105 (105 gets you on Sepulveda), hang a right on Aviation; there is a Circle K/76 station on the corner with a freight train overpass right at the intersection. Hang a right on to Aviation, go straight down toward the middle, that\'s where all the planes coming in to LAX have to cross, the road is at the end of the runway. On the left or face east, you\'ll see all the planes on their pattern approaching 26L, then on your right side or west, you\'ll see all the planes lining up coming toward you, then they\'ll make an U-ie to take off into the Santa Monica. Want a good sonic vibration, a good whiff of sea mist mixed with fueljet, and fantasize, that\'s the place to be.<br><br>\r\n<font color=\"red\">nannerbug</font>:That\'s close enough to Venice Beach to hit the two spots in the same day. Are we still up for Sunday? All two of us?<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>:You HAVE to be there at night though... in the day time it is really boring, because all the short hop airplanes, not the big jets, come down, no fun there.<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"yellow\">Triphop</font>:You want to be there at night where all the 747 takes off, and it is a lot more fun get streaks of lines in the dark than frozen airplane in the middle. One or two maybe... I once got all the schedule down for all the big planes taking off, Wednesday night, 10-11, aww, that\'s when all the loud planes go!<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"red\">nannerbug</font>:We can try it on Sunday night, right? I was thinking we could meet in Venice at a little before twilight. That way we get the day freaks vs. night freaks<br><br>\r\nelle: you\'re not alone, peter......<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"blue\">Jamazon</font>:I don\'t really remember my dreams very vividly. I just remember a big fireball and my with my Smena! All very weird.<br><br>\r\n<font color=\"green\">Peter</font>:Nah, don\'t sweat the flight- my pseudo-flight-fantasy-phobia most likely stemmed from sleep deprivation, too many drugs, and being a little depressed. that and my dream was so damn vivid that it scared the shit out of me. It certainly didn\'t help that a friend bought me a pictorial book yesterday called <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1840440260/qid=997271323/sr=1-5/ref=sc_b_5/107-9740599-7617309\">\"Air Disasters\"</a>, which gives a freakily fetishistic (ever read ballard\'s <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0374524122/qid=997277054/sr=1-15/ref=sc_b_15/107-9740599-7617309\">\"crash\"</a>?) look at the \"50 greatest aircrashes of all time\". But no, flying is SAFE and GOOD.<br><br> \r\n<font color=\"blue\">Jamazon</font>:I\'m taking a flight from Belfast to Liverpool on Saturday. I\'m scared now<br><br>\r\n<font color=\"green\">Peter</font>:hey, Belfast to Liverpool isn\'t too far, right? <br><br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1533,'<p>\r\nHow many people you don\'t know have tried to poison you? How often have you been hounded out of your home by local residents and had pictures of yourself put up on lamp posts? Seen letters encouraging others to \"flay you in hell\" and \"piss on your severed legs\"? No more, though. No more will we open up our front doors and see people whom once welcomed us chase us from our homes like piss ridden worry-dogs.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Sheltered Housing for Maniacs</b> is run by maniacs for maniacs. <b>Sheltered Housing for Maniacs</b> is new and secure housing built with finger-biting mental people in mind. But don\'t take our word for it, come along for the day and give our facilities a whirl! You could spend the morning talking Turkish to a spoon. Or while away the afternoon painting the wall brown and red with gravy and blood. You can chew your own ear off for all we care! We are cool about you being insane! At <b>Sheltered Housing for Maniacs</b> you might get eaten alive, but you\'ll never have to worry about getting stuff poked through your letter box ever again!\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nJust $30,000 will buy you a bedroom in our little block. Inside you\'ll find a perforated Bible with easy tear pages, some glue for the wall, a bag of nails and some bite-sized scalpel blades. Remember because we are maniacs like yourself we\'ll take payments in boogers, animal skins, or old pencils. We\'re so mad we\'ll probably just bury whatever you give us anyway. So why run from the mob when you can run amok, here at <b>Sheltered Housing for Maniacs</b> - The Happy Place...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor more information call Clive on 555-222-55189\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1535,'It wasn\'t like everyone else was in sweatshirts and flannel, you know.  And most mornings I have no desire to wear anything but pants.  Today was different.  I was feeling a little edgy.  A little invincible.\r\n\r\nI stepped out of the shower and just didn\'t feel like pulling on stiff hot jeans.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a long skirt.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a bra.  They\'re tiresome items.  Uncomfortable.  Inhibiting.\r\n\r\nI try desperately to step gracefully into my car wearing my little blue dress.\r\n\r\nI fail miserably, and the man driving by in the shiny BMW swerves slightly as he glances at me.\r\n\r\nI\'m running a little late this morning, and have to make it to work in under twenty minutes now, which is possible, but will require some aggressive driving.  I glance at my thighs, smooth fair skin emerging from the bunched blue dress that rests comfortably beneath the steering wheel.\r\n\r\nYellow light.  Have to make it.  Can\'t afford to stop here.  I throw my left foot down on the clutch, downshift to fourth gear and let the RPMs build until I catapult my car through the intersection.  My legs have come apart from each other, and the dress, in its stressed state, is stretched fully and retreating up my thighs.\r\n\r\nI fumble absentmindedly through my bag for my cigarettes, and victoriously feel my fingers grace the plastic.  I put one to my lips, tossing a sideways glance at the young man in the Trans Am next to me.  I\'m into reactions today.  I flex my right bicep and my tattoo slides slightly to the right.  He raises an eyebrow.  Green light.\r\n\r\nDown comes the left foot again, assaulting the clutch into first gear.  A woman in a minivan looks down from her high vantage point and I pull my dress as far north as possible without exposing myself.  She is horrified.  Yet she doesn\'t look away.'),(1534,'<p>\r\n<b>Woodcock:</b> Snoopy...You know a lot about motor racing...Who won the world \r\nchampionship in 1975? <br>\r\n<b>Snoopy: </b>Lauda<br>\r\n<b>Woodcock: </b>WHO WON THE WORLD CHAMPIONSHIP IN 1975?<br>\r\n<b>Snoopy:</b> Bloody Lauda I said!<br>\r\n<b>Woodcock: <font color=\"#FF0000\">WHO WON THE FUCKING WORLD CHAMPIONSHIP?!</font></b><br>\r\n<b>Snoopy: <font color=\"#FF0000\" size=\"+1\">FUCKING LAUDA!!!!!!!</font></b><br>\r\n<b>Woodcock:</b> !<br>\r\n<b>Snoopy:</b> .oO( Deaf Cunt! )<br>\r\n<b>Woodcock:</b> .oO( Wanker! ) <br>\r\n</p>'),(1536,'<p>It wasn\'t like everyone else was in sweatshirts and flannel, you know.  And most mornings I have no desire to wear anything but pants.  Today was different.  I was feeling a little edgy.  A little invincible.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI stepped out of the shower and just didn\'t feel like pulling on stiff hot jeans.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a long skirt.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a bra.  They\'re tiresome items.  Uncomfortable.  Inhibiting.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI try desperately to step gracefully into my car wearing my little blue dress.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI fail miserably, and the man driving by in the shiny BMW veers slightly as he glances at me.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI\'m running a little late this morning, and have to make it to work in under twenty minutes now, which is possible, but will require some aggressive driving.  I glance at my thighs, smooth fair skin emerging from the bunched blue dress that rests comfortably beneath the steering wheel.\r\n</p><p>\r\nYellow light.  Have to make it.  Can\'t afford to stop here.  I throw my left foot down on the clutch, downshift to fourth gear and let the RPMs build until I catapult my car through the intersection.  My legs have come apart from each other, and the dress, in its stressed state, is stretched fully and retreating up my thighs.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI fumble absentmindedly through my bag for my cigarettes, and victoriously feel my fingers grace the plastic.  I put one to my lips, tossing a sideways glance at the young man in the Trans Am next to me.  I\'m into reactions today.  I flex my right bicep and my tattoo slides slightly to the right.  He raises an eyebrow.  Green light.\r\n</p><p>\r\nDown comes the left foot again, assaulting the clutch into first gear.  A woman in a minivan looks down from her high vantage point and I pull my dress as far north as possible without exposing myself.  She is horrified.  Yet she doesn\'t look away.\r\n</p>'),(1537,'It wasn\'t like everyone else was in sweatshirts and flannel, you know.  And most mornings I have no desire to wear anything but pants.  Today was different.  I was feeling a little edgy.  A little invincible.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI stepped out of the shower and just didn\'t feel like pulling on stiff hot jeans.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a long skirt.  Didn\'t feel like wearing a bra.  They\'re tiresome items.  Uncomfortable.  Inhibiting.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI try desperately to step gracefully into my car wearing my little blue dress.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI fail miserably, and the man driving by in the shiny BMW veers slightly as he glances at me.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'m running a little late this morning, and have to make it to work in under twenty minutes now, which is possible, but will require some aggressive driving.  I glance at my thighs, smooth fair skin emerging from the bunched blue dress that rests comfortably beneath the steering wheel.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYellow light.  Have to make it.  Can\'t afford to stop here.  I throw my left foot down on the clutch, downshift to fourth gear and let the RPMs build until I catapult my car through the intersection.  My legs have come apart from each other, and the dress, in its stressed state, is stretched fully and retreating up my thighs.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI fumble absentmindedly through my bag for my cigarettes, and victoriously feel my fingers graze the plastic.  I put one to my lips, tossing a sideways glance at the young man in the Trans Am riding alongside me.  I\'m into reactions today.  I flex my right bicep and my tattoo slides slightly to the right.  He raises an eyebrow.  Red light.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe all wait, trapped in our boxes, yearning for interaction.  Mr. Trans Am is playing air guitar.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nA woman in a minivan looks down from her high vantage point and I pull my dress as far north as possible without exposing myself.  She is horrified.  Yet she doesn\'t look away.<br>  \r\n<br>\r\nGreen light.<br>'),(1538,'That last article I sent.. got screwed up a couple times. Can you delete it and let me send the correct one? My server kept timing out on the \"add photo\" page.\r\n\r\nThanks.'),(1539,'Time to face the demons.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'ve gone and done it.  I\'ve called up the person for whom I\'m harbouring all this unnecessary frustration and ill-will.  I\'ve called her and asked her to go hang out.  With me and the boy.<br>  \r\n<br>\r\nThe boy wants her.  He\'s not the only one, fortunately.  In fact, at this time, he may not be the freshest catch in the market.  Other boys, cuter ones even, are also hanging all over her.  Watching her as she dances and walks.  Eyeing her up and down in her bright pink and black that shines like glowsticks in the night.  She\'s nightshade.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd we\'re gonna go hang out, talk about the stuff she\'s going through, be girls together.  Maybe she\'ll come to yoga with me.  Maybe she\'ll get me spinning cds on her mixer.  That\'d be fun.  Maybe I\'ll even get good, and we could play together, all tag-team styles.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOr she\'ll sense my discomfort, play it up, and I\'ll leave feeling even more judgemental and incapable of true friendship. '),(1540,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1540_1.jpg\'><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'m the only one in here right now, and I\'ve managed to somehow get that Russian waitress who\'s a strange old flirt.  As soon as I cross that threshold, we go through our usual schemes- I groan when I see that I\'m alone here, and she dives to grab a menu and slowly guide me to a shady seat.  I have a craving for corn on the cob, and order some; she repeats my every item back to me in her heavily draped accent, not entirely devoid of emotion, and having that erotic raspiness that trills in the voice of women who smoke too much.  I shut my eyes and listen.<br>  \r\n<br>\r\nHer multi-syllabled renditions of my words sing out at me.  \"You\'ll yave the teee-aaa with lee-mo-ne, yessss\"... \"Ahh, and ze co-or-en cobs\".  My phone rings.  Damn.  \"You.  <i>Get</i> that\", she exhorts, commanding with a quirky middle-aged smile.  Stepping barely a foot away, she shifts her weight back and forth, hand on hip, every few seconds, pushing her bangs back atop her smooth forehead.  \"Blee-uuu cheeses on ze saaa-lad, yes. And you will have an or-an-geee\", she recites, her incisors delicately nibbling the eraser of the pencil she uses to scratch my order onto her pad.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nToday is so hot.  Just walking across my small room to turn the fan to high is an exercise in stamina.  Strangely enough, I\'ve always enjoyed the heat; but not this urban stink, humid clouds and steamy sunsets.  No, I\'ve mused a thousand times that I\'d be more content in a desert, I think, dry baking air lapping over me, dehydrating, my kidneys growing sluggish in the still air.  I squeeze the lemon into my tea- its juice runs brown, but it turns the tea golden; oxidation at work, and the tea tastes plastic.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe way the sedans and cabs sit at the traffic light outside reminds me of some movie set- lighting unreal, searing, layout almost over-wrought, streets too clean.  It all has an indoor look.  Lately, I\'ve been content to sit and watch traffic pass, to muse amidst the diesel bus fumes, to whistle the refrain of the bleating taxi horns, all this offered as some sordid urban prayer to the spirits of summer.  A delivery truck passes, slips away west, enveloping me in its warm slipstream- just rewards.  Oh, to be in the desert  at this exact moment.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nChildren play and wade in the hepatitis-laden water sitting stagnant in the gutter outside, splashing it upon the curb, onto the sidewalk.  A passing gypsy cab plays Barry White.  \"Deeper and deeper\" I hear it fade down Broadway, \"sweeter and sweeter\".<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd a woman stoops to pick up the discount blouse she knocked from the sidewalk-sale rack, cursing as it blows towards the gutter on the steamy breeze.  And a child dips his finger into the gutter water and in a moist, lopsided script, writes his innitials on the back of the delapidated news-stand.  And a man emerges from the door-less bodega, a burrito clutched in hand.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI stir another Sweet-n-Low into my tea and squeeze the softening lemon rind against the edge of the cup with my tarnished spoon.  The waitress is stacking boxes of Raisin Bran up behind the counter, looking over her shoulder and smiling at me.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1541,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nI am talking with corrie, and I say, corrie, this girl one time told me a story about her friend who was a guy, and this guy, he would go after the most unavailable girls he could find and he was always shot down- and this guy, he was pretty miserable because he was always alone because he would always go after girls that would never go for him-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand continuing, I say- so, one day, this guy, he realizes that he\'s gay, and that all this time he was going after the girls that would shoot him down because he (subconsciously) wanted to be shot down because he didn\'t really want to be with girls at all- he wanted to be with guys-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she says, are you coming on to me?, and laughs-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I say, coming on to you?, how would this be coming on to you?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she says, no, coming OUT- are you coming OUT to me-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I say oh- coming out- no, not really- but, I don\'t know, I\'ve been thinking- I don\'t know, I think maybe I might be in the same situation, you know?- cause, like, I don\'t know, I\'m that guy- I\'m that guy that is always going after the unavailable girls-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand this is where she interrupts and says, well, yeah- one would take your constant joking about how you\'re only really attracted to the girls that think they\'re too good for you, you know, one would take that as an good example of what you are saying-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I say, yeah- well, yeah, that\'s true- I don\'t know-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I say, yeah, well, it\'s like this: anytime I get close to maybe, possiblely, getting the girl into some sort of concrete relationship I always feel like, umm, I don\'t know- well, I guess it\'s kinda like when you\'re at a club and it\'s over and you\'ve just had the most amazing time in your whole life and now it\'s over and you\'re like, \'now what?,\' you know?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nlike, well, that was fucking amazing, but, well, no matter what I do now, it\'s not even gonna compare to that- like, you could go to an afterparty, sure, or maybe somebody\'s house, or you could just go home and go to sleep, but, you know- it just all seems too futile, cause, like, what\'s the point, you know?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand girls are kinda like that with me- cause, you know, what the hell do I do once I get what I want?- and really, what\'s more fun then the first falling part of love anyway- you know what I mean?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she does, and nods-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand I say, but, at the same, I really would love to be able to buy flowers sometime and not have to worry about whether or not I\'m buying flowers too early, you know?-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she does, and nods-</p>'),(1542,'<p>I made the mistake of telling heiney that I missed taking her for granted. On one of our drunken love chat sprees. she wasnt givin me any chat milk after that. I would have to find some other drunken teat to suckle text from.</p>\r\n<p>She used to drive me over the bridge in the morning. We had a ritual. a routine. At dawn we\'d suck down a red bull each and smack each other\'s hands off the stereo until she won with NPR. NPR was our destiny on the bridge. NPR was our destiny because we had to be informed on the school lunch for that day.</p>\r\n<p>I would force her to take the carpool lane right at the maze, the one that puts hair on your chest by forcing you to prove your manhood and cross 4 lanes of trucks and bitches. she is still trying to pluck all the hairs out. I think she has begun to find grays. right there above the nipple.</p>\r\n<p>My memory of the school lunch always plays itself back when we are approaching treasure island and everything looks rosey. greasey. apocalyptic. somehow it is the end of the world when i am alone with, trying to make something, get somewhere with heiney. I am exhausted, trying to make movies in my sleep, snapping at every word, laughing at all things inanimate and banal.</p>\r\n<p><i>and im tryin to make some boy<br>\r\nbaby baby baby come back</i></p>\r\n<p> That is how she works her sinister magic on me. Half asleep, desperate, drunken, or maddeningly inspired, I find heiney next to me. Looking sideways at me conspiratorily, subtley turning up the volume as the jolly man announces the feast. Invariably it is baked macaroni and cheese with green jello for dessert, but despite the variety of the solid diet available to children in the Bay Area, they always get one thing to drink: \"the ever present container of milk.\" i don\'t know if someone copies the same script every day and then someone else pastes just that day\'s unique item over it and they hand it to the guy right before he goes on, or what, but he without fail uses that exact phrase. the ever present container of milk. every goddamn day.</p> \r\n<p>We have such moments of implicit ritual and unspoken memory that the ache of her absence only makes itself known after the rhythm of my days takes on a humorless whimper. Heiney really punctuated my routine with the most insistently banal, self-consciously humorous repetition that these phrases and rituals became hilarious.</p>\r\n<p>I find myself tired, or over-excited, but with noone to laugh at me about it. i find myself getting laid, or getting paid, but with noone to create a new repeatable syntax of mockery to seal the experience. I find myself without heiney. and I get thirsty.</p>\r\n<p>I have been complaining about oakland this summer. how it is not a real summer. but the cold makes it pleasurable to cuggle and snoozle under blankets. the cold makes warm water at dawn a necessary ritual. </p>\r\n<p>Laying with Steve a year ago by my window, I absorbed his heartbeat through one ear and the rush of 580 through the other. he asked me if I had \"gotten used to\" the sound of the freeway yet. But I never had to get used to the sound of the freeway, which has its own difficult rhythm, like a rocky stream. I never had to reconcile my own noise with the outside. the patterns repeat themselves, and I transcend my own heartbeat.</p>'),(1543,'<p>8/9/01 1:36 am</p>\r\n<p>Chris is a retard.  She is always spewing out \"facts\" about things which are totally inaccurate.  Often I find myself questioning the validity of her information.  However, when she begins to lay down \"truths\" about subjects of which I am in the know, she might as well be speaking <a href=\"http://www.pseudodictionary.com/word.php?id=4196\" target=\"new\">Pirish</a> at me, because it would make about as much sense.  I hold my tongue, though, because I don\'t really feel like stirring the pot.  It\'s a general rule of thumb that you should be on good terms with a co-worker when 2/3 of your time at work is spent with that person and a cook who cannot understand much English beyond food orders.  I think it would be difficult to hold a conversation with someone when every fourth word is: \"chickenballs.\"</p>\r\n<p>I really hope that I am involved in this organisation long enough to be promoted ahead of her.  That would be delightful, says that mean bone(s) I harbour in my body.</p>\r\n<p>Some woman just told me that I suck because I didn\'t have any scissors to cut her little Freezie.  Well, sucks to her assmar.</p>\r\n<p>I think that I might finish making my blinds when I get home from work.  It is worth the effort so that I can completely block the sunlight and get a good day\'s sleep.</p>\r\n<p>At least Chris is blasting one of my Mark Farina CD inside the restaurant.  Maybe she isn\'t so bad after all...  no, wait, she is.</p>\r\n<p>A homeless guy just came and switched an unopened can of pop with me for a colder one.  When I told him to have a good night, he responded with: <b><i>\"Have a great night.\"</i></b>  <b>Positivity</b>.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s now 3:40am, it\'s nearly time to pack up and head on home.  Thus concludes my bored-at-work ramblings.</p>'),(1544,'<P>I just finished up a PowerPoint presentation in the hopes that my bosses will give me a pay raise.  I wanted to run it past you guys first to see if you think it\'ll fly.  Keep in mind that the individual slides have a tastefull background and each feature the following footnote: I <B>AM</B> worth it!</P><P>Here Goes:<BR><font color=004a4a>Slide One</font><BR><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>Felicity\'s not-so-feeble (and exceptionally justified) attempt at getting a pay raise</font></B></P><font color=004a4a><P>Slide Two</font><BR><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>Just the facts...</B></font><br><ul type=\"square\"><li>Since switching over to composing,  I have been working a minimum of 10 hours per week (off the clock and without complaint) to keep up with my work related responsibilities.  (I was here for over  12 hours this weekend to prepare for the I-86 meeting.)  These hours are above and beyond any paid overtime I have been receiving.<li>Overtime, as you know quite well, is an evil creature whose greedy little hands, in my case, snatch $XX.XX/hour from The Tribuneâ€™s proverbial pockets.</ul></p><font color=004a4a><P>Slide Three</font><BR><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>Some more facts</B></font> <font size=\"4\" color=520031>(and one opinion)...</font><br><ul type=\"square\"><li>The federal governmentâ€™s current income guidelines for Steuben County, NY show that my income falls directly between the $XX,XXX \"very low income\" and the $XX,XXX \"low income.\"<li>I perform job duties that include, but are not limited to, my original 40 hr/week Internet work, a large portion of the workload in composing, substituting for vacationing employees, writing two (2) columns every week, and allowing my infectious positive attitude to help create a happy, healthy work environment for my coworkers and myself.  I would like to think that this is work that merits pay that would at least disqualify me from most poverty level social services.</ul></p><font color=004a4a><P>Slide Four</font><BR><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>Action</B></font><br><ul type=\"square\"><li>Bi-Weekly Salary<li>I recommend a bi-weekly salary that would both allow me to work unlimited hours without burdening you with overtime pay, and that would increase my income to a point that will justly compensate me for the extra time I am willing to spend at work.</ul></p><font color=004a4a><P>Slide Five</font><BR><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>Benefits</B></font><br>The Tribune will benefit from this in several ways:<br><UL>-Saving money<li>The time I am currently \"donating\" would cost The tribune at least $X,XXX per year ($XXX.XX/week)</UL><UL>-Improved Job Performance<li>Without the constraints of a 40 hour work week and fear of overtime, I will be able to devote myself fully to all of my job responsibilities.</UL><UL>-Bribery<li>I would gladly buy a beer at the Christmas party for any person who had a hand in increasing my current pay.</UL></p><font color=004a4a><P>Slide Six</font><br><font size=\"5\" color=520031><B>In Closing</B></font><br>A salary for Felicity = Lots of good stuff for The Tribune<ul><li>Paying overtime is <B>BAD</B>.  Nobody likes paying overtime.<li>Employees with more than $25 expendable income each month are <b>GOOD</b>.  Everyone likes good employees.<li>(See the footnote)</ul></p><p align=\"center\">--------------------</p><p>That\'s it.  Let me know if you can suggest any changes.  Thanks in advance for your help!</p>'),(1545,'<p>\r\nOff to buy petrol. In my shiny little red Ford Fiesta. Me an Caroline. We live in Northern Ireland near the border with the Republic. She is Irish, I\'m English. I\'m from the city. The lights. The noise. The concrete. All i see here is grass.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd a cow.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI buy petrol across the border. In a town known as Clones. Away from the money grabbing hands of the British Government. Almsot half the price. A bird in the hand\'s worth two in the bush as they say</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI pull up. They fill up for you here. It makes me feel uncomfortable. I like to do it myself. Watching the numbers flick past. They stop at Â£12.50.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\"That\'s Â£10 sterling\" They know i\'m from the North they can see by my license plate.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAfter some gentle encouragement, the car starts. We\'re heading home. The streets here are narrow and the road markings vague. I come to the junction. Three roads. No lights. No markings. Three of us sit there. No one seems sure. I put my foot down and go.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI wanna get home quick now. I\'m hungry. I\'m doing 50 in a 30 but there\'s no police here. There never is. A small dog runs out into the road, closely followed by an old man. He\'s chasing it.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nInstinctively i press hard on the brakes as the tiny unkempt Yorkshire Terrier scuttles into my path. The brakes lock.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni skid\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI didn\'t hit the dog. Missed the lamp post too.\r\n</p>'),(1546,'<p><font size=\"5\">\r\n\"Worker bees can leave<br> \r\nEven drones can fly away<br> \r\nThe queen is their slave.\"</font></p>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1546_1.gif\'>'),(1547,'<p><font size=\"6\"> Broad minds discuss ideas.</font><br>\r\n<font size=\"4\">Average minds discuss events.</font><br>\r\n<font size=\"2\">Small minds discuss people. </font></p>'),(1548,'<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1548_1.jpg\'>\r\n<br><br>\r\nessemar checks out<br>\r\nthe ladies as i try to<br>\r\nbum his smokes for free<br>\r\n<br>\r\nyo waiter bring some<br>\r\nmore mayo for this burger<br>\r\nman, how you not fat?!<br>\r\n<br>\r\nchill in median<br>\r\ndodging traffic on Broadway<br>\r\nmuse about fat chicks<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlaid-back guys talking<br>\r\nlinux and ladies, sharing<br>\r\ntherapy for both<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndespite the damn heat<br>\r\nI manage to find a cool<br>\r\nguy in Manhattan<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1549,'<p>\r\nWay down deep in the middle of the Congo,<br> \r\nA hippo took an apricot, a guava and a mango.<br>\r\nHe stuck it with the others and he danced a dainty tango.<br>\r\nThe rhino said, \"I know, we\'ll call it <b>UmBoNgO!</b>\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<b>UmBoNgO</b>, <b>UmBoNgO</b>, they drink it in the Congo!\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThe python picked the passion-fruit,<br>\r\nThe marmoset the mandarine, the parrot painted packets<br>\r\nThat the whole caboodle landed in.<br> \r\nSo when it comes to sun and fun and goodness in the jungle,<br> \r\nThey all prefer the sunny, funny one they call <b>UmBoNgO!</b>\"\r\n</p>'),(1550,'<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1550_1.jpg\"><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1550_1.jpg\' width=150 alt=\"come have a drink at the bar with me honey\" align=\"left\"></a>\r\n\r\n<p>Music does that sometimes. Tunes follow you: they catch lifts in cars that drive by and they lean out of the window, they follow you into shops. They appear on your TV unexpectedly. They crop up in your friends\' conversations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Or they just embed themselves in your head so that you need to hear them every half an hour or thereabouts. So that you end up carrying albums around with you like Linus and his security blanket.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This is what I\'ve been doing with Cat Power\'s <b>The Covers Record</b>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>and you better come come, come come to me<br>\r\nbetter come, come come, come come to me<br>\r\nbetter run, run run, run run to me<br>\r\nbetter come...</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chan Marshall is keeping me sane and soothing my brain. But she\'s also haunting me.</p>'),(1551,'<p>\r\ni get razzed by friends who are still in the scene. i dont mind. i dont mind the fact that they make fun of everything else i do and refer to it as being too l33t and whathaveyou. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif i hear a good tune i tap my feet. if i hear a good set i bob my head. if i get a good tape i thank the person who gave it to me. i like music. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif i hear a good chewn i dont go out and buy it. if i see a good tape i dont drop any cash. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy spend money to hear music? I spend money where i think money is to be spent. paying over twenty to get into a show to hear one decent dj spin for one point five hours is not my idea of supporting an artist. that over twenty supports a lot. a lot of what i dont agree with. production companies are corporations. the production company became the name of the party; the \"name\" of the party used only to catalogue the event. if it has always been that way i did not notice it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i pay five dollars i feel good. i feel better. to me paying five dollars represents a lot of what i do like about music. to me paying five dollars usually means im going to hear dj\'s who are actually still trying to be good. no doubt there are a lot of five dollar things out there that suck ballz, but paying five dollars to hear shit is better then losing an additional twenty five at a party where the only dj you wanted to see did not show up. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni fail to come up with reasons for why i stopped partaking. i used to be \"so in\", taking pictures and just sorta showing up at all the key events and nights. perhaps i got a bit lazy. expecting too much from too many. it just became so much more clear how lucrative the scene had become and with that came a lot of bling bling. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"3\"><b>STOP THE BLING</b></font>'),(1552,'<TT>\r\nthe setting is a box with two desks facing opposing corners.  the box is opened up for the audience to view at not exactly right angles.  more like a box with two sides removed and the remaining two moved apart to reveal a better view of the inside yet still giving the audience a sense of confinement.  the roof is still attached.  work the set designing magic on this, things are what they seem.\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nthey have one fluorescent desk lamp each.  the desks are slightly different and not large, the two chairs are identical cheap office chairs, a third chair is different and not as functional as the two other office chairs and sits in the only remaining unoccupied corner facing the audience. (this is where the manager will sit), monitors are small to accentuate the \'room\' and there is no computer box, only the keyboard.\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann and austin are both wearing small cheap head phones.  two almost inaudible strands of music play from the background of either side of the stage.  intermittent clicking of the keyboards, a sharp plastic sound, as well.\r\n<BR><BR><BR>the play begins..<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ 8:30 a. m.  ann is on the stage seated as the dim desk lamp comes on, brown hair, brown vest, khaki pleated pants, and tasseled cowgirl boots, her desk is neat and tiddy. ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  s n i f f\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  s i g h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  h m m\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ 8:35 a. m.  austin walks on the stage, blond hair under orange cap, grey hooded sweatshirt, black wranglers with rain pail cuffs, blue and yellow tennis shoes. he speaks as if to someone hearing impaired. ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  \'good morning ann\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  h u h m r h a\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ austin turns on his desk lamp and takes a thermal mug off his desk and leaves immediately and comes back twenty seconds later with coffee, sits down. ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  h u h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  h h h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  t h u h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  u h h n n a\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  \'fuck\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  s n i f f\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  s i g h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  \'ha!\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  m m m\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  \'ok\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  s c r a t c h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  s c r a t c h\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  s n i f f\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\naustin:  \'ha!\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nann:  h u m m\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ this carries on for several minutes, then ann gets up to leave comes back and it carries on for minutes more.  then the manager comes in, a very tall man with a mustache goat-tee combination.   he walks directly to the only empty chair and sits erect with his feet planted on the ground, knees together, looking as though he is going to talk and only moans just as the words are appear to be coming out.  this carries on for a minute and a half, ann and austin continue to click and carry on as before. ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nmanager:  \'.. muh!\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ austin typing away ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ ann typing away ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nmanager:  \'.. muh!\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ austin typing away ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ ann typing away ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\nmanager: \'.. muh!\'\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ manager gets up and walks out ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ clicking grows louder, always above the opposing musics which alternate in style and grow more distorted pop/country and punk/electronic ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ really fucking loud bell goes off from back stage scaring the crap out of everyone, music stops ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ ann gets up to leave and says good bye to austin as though speaking to a hearing impaired person ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ austin mumbles a sound over his shoulder and the audience hears just the clicking from his keyboard without amplification ]\r\n<BR><BR><BR>\r\n[ ann leaves and the stage goes dark on austin ]\r\n</TT>\r\n'),(1553,'<P> I absolutely believe that autistic peole know something we don\'t. When they began laughing at inopportune moments, it is because there is somethings funny going on. There is a highlight to my day. When one of my students outsmarts the wit in me. When I see Janet laughing, she knows something I don\'t...and if she can laugh than maybe so can I.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> My normal everyday life. Go to work for nine hours. Come home, take the shit out of the litter and throw it out, then I go to my room, check messages, maybe eat, then sleep. Yesterday, I got home at six, fell asleep at seven and awoke the next morning at six. Time is just slipping away, I cannot even see it. It feels like I am a mechanical robot and everything I do, everyone I see ( during the week) is pre-ordained.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I am experienceing \"problems\" in the workplace. I guess I talk back. I ask questions. I threaten. I will not let some girl my age, tell me what is right and what is wrong in this place of business. Because there are none. I may just quit. I may just wake up....and decide to sleep in. How I pay the rent is up to me. I am not glad. I am bleh.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"We need to have a meeting.\" she says. ( the gall!)</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"OK.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P>...\"you\'re so cute when you\'re mad,\" I say.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"I\'m not mad.\"\r\n\r\n<P> \'Yeah right \', i think. So, if you\'re not mad then why act like a bitch. If you\'re not mad why make big things out of small nothings?</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I\'ve given up. I do not think I will ever be fine for long working a \"normal\" job. So maybe i will paint for some \"horror\" theme park and help then do scenery. The hours are horrible, the work is demanding...but I will perhaps meet people a bit less structured than the norm who are running away from the same thing I detest.</P>\r\n'),(1554,''),(1555,'<P>Thats what I just did. That and I.m about to shave my head. To shave or not to....I think maybe I will. I am tired of having this brown bobbed mop. So, I am currently jobless. It is better than being dead. Since I really honestly think this would have driven me towards suicide.\r\nYeah, I.m weak. Weak and weird. I can only work for a while until that job drives me psycho slash looony and its time to find another.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>My roommates will kill me.</P> \r\n\r\n\r\n<P>No money. I think I have 3 bucks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>No family support.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>They will assist in the suicide.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I.m twenty-two without a liscence.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>What will people think? oooH. It.s so nice. Now I can draw, all FUCKING DAY.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Yeah, IM A BUM.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Let\'s say it all together</P>\r\n\r\n<P>GREEN IS A BUM</P>\r\n\r\n<P>yay.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1556,'<pre>\r\nThe monster wants in, but I cant hear him knocking...\r\n\r\nI was walking up the street today and saw the most beautiful girl. Is she a princess, \r\nwhore, wiccan goddess, bitch...?  Oh, someone elses love slave...\r\n\r\nA watch pot never boils...\r\nWell, eventually...\r\n\r\nStrongbow easy baked jasper with India on my mind.\r\nCould ash direct a path to aloha?\r\nWhat is the reason, have you found the gate to the key?\r\nIn a sense I am talking about a metamorphosis.\r\nI am talking about going from caterpilar to butterfly.\r\nYou will never become a butterfly if you take away your caterpilarness.\r\nIts like the sunbeams shining through the trees in a deep forest...\r\nShedding life to the floor of the world.\r\n\r\nThe Monster wants in, but he cant see the door...\r\n\r\nNow she is thinking, it takes one to know one.\r\nIn a kinkos state of mind, her level of reality is the same meaningless blahhh!\r\nEgo trips her mind, an orange glow of awareness shines my face...\r\nHostility is an emotion of cowerdice.\r\nCan you understand this meaning?\r\n\r\nBelguim is a land of perfected Alchemy.\r\nPortugal is a land of unrecognized talents.\r\n\r\nThe monster wants in, and nobody cares!\r\nIt will come to pass...</pre>'),(1557,'<p>\r\n<font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\">do all people who work in non-profits\r\ntry to get their friends to come in and donate their time? and why the\r\nfuck is that? it\'s your job. you\'re the one who wanted to work a non-profit.\r\nwhy get me invoved? if i had the time and/or inclination i would volunteer.\r\nsomewhere. outside a fucking office. i get an average of 2 calls a week\r\nfrom my friend asking me for something or other involving her work. i\'ve\r\ntaken to diligently screening my phone calls.\r\n<br><b>*beep* </b>hi goat, it\'s me. now i realize you have school, and stuff and that you have\r\nthat project to do, but i was wondering if you could come in and give me\r\nan hour or so on the old manual typewriter typing out membership renewal\r\ncards..\r\n<br><b>*beep* </b>hey goat, how\'s your midterm going? So do you think you have any free time? I need someone to stuff some envelops for me, they have to go out ASAPâ€¦\r\n<br><b>*beep* </b>oh god goat, i\'m justâ€¦ i can\'t remember if i sent these membership renewal\r\ninformation, i need a better system, could you come in one day and help\r\nme? i need some one to cross check the info on the physical renewal cards,\r\nwith the information in the computer\r\n<br><b>*beep* </b>so goat, remember those pamphlets that they won\'t let me get rid ofâ€¦\r\n<br>and today, just now, i picked up\r\nthe phone without checking. oh woes me. seems like i\'m gonna be spending\r\ntomorrow stamping or something a bazillion letters or something. woowee.</font>'),(1558,'<p><b>what is bling</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nfor me bling represents those kids in their lowered and tricked out cars blasting \"chart topping\" hiphop \"joints\". it represents all those city living people driving SUV\'s. it represents the club kids, acting like club kids. it represents the ones who act like pigs. the greedy and the ones who show off. the ones who try too hard and the ones who dont try enough. bling is rampant. people living a lifestyle. a lifestyle with a marketing company acting as puppet master. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbling is hard to explain. to me its something so big it cant be put into words or catagorized. there is bling in every culture, it just takes on different shapes. i speak as a canadian. i am not american, but the influence is there at every corner. bling does not have its roots in america, its just going through a hyper-active phase of bling right now, and so there is considerable spill over into other nations; canada being the best example. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbling has been around a long time. it is also not the first time bling has risen. bling hit n.america in the early twenties. a primitive form of bling i dub \"pre-industrial bling\". however bling took a back seat for a number of years, due in part to the number of wars participated in by the united states. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next big rise in bling came during the eighties when people were listening to dead of alive and john couger melancamp. the cars. the style. the yup yup of the yuppies. this period benchmarked so much bling that it should now be thought of as the \"bling renaissance\", or \"bling reformation\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen the berlin wall fell and they world was so different and things became sorta serious for a while. bling took a back seat to the gulf war and ossama bin laden. the oldschool bling traditions were proving to be utterly obsolete. the godfathers of bling slowly passed on and to fill the vacuum: information age bling. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ninformation age bling came about when a very large influx of invisible money entered the economy. the trickle down effect gave undeveloped minds more money to spend and marketing was there to tell them what spend it on. bling is back. \r\n</p>\r\n<p><b>STOP THE BLING</b></p>\r\n'),(1559,'<p>\r\nhello and welcome! please find a seat along the edge. place all\r\ncalculators under your desk. number 2 pencils only. no talking,\r\nnon-sanctioned prayer, forgiveness, regrets, or gum chewing are\r\npermitted during exam time. show all your work.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou will be tested on your knowledge of spiritual growth, humility,\r\nand united states geography. this exam will be open book and open\r\nnotes. you may work in small groups to solve individual exam\r\nproblems. you have 90 minutes to complete this exam.\r\n</p><p>\r\nexam results will be tabulated, standardized, and posted publicly\r\noutside the dean\'s office by chinese new year, 2002.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsharpen your pencils.\r\n</p><p>\r\nset your stopwatch.\r\n</p><p>\r\nstrap it on.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<hr>\r\n</p><p>\r\n(1) create a table with three columns. in the first column, list seven\r\nof your false realities that were burned away during the first\r\nsemester. in the second column, describe how each has provided an\r\nopportunity for growth and renewal. in the third column, describe how\r\neach has come back to haunt you. list only your first impulses.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(2) use the word \"oracle\" in a sentence.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(3) you are lying supine on a cold steel operating table. your torso,\r\nsliced vertically from neck to lower abdomen, is folded open like a\r\nnewspaper and pinned to the operating table. a red lightbulb is\r\nplanted on your face, wired in series to a 9 volt DC battery and a\r\nshrill electronic buzzer. wrist and ankle restraints clasp closed\r\nsecurely. now visualize the goddess Kali, wielding each of the\r\nfollowing: scalpel, scissors, sponge, drill, wet/dry vac, curling\r\niron, needlenose pliers, runcible spoon. Kali floats over you,\r\nseductively waving and spiraling her instruments of psychic surgery;\r\nmoments of calm (really just habituation) punctuated by periodic\r\nblasts of buzzer and red nose lamp. in the space provided below -\r\nusing the english language - compose a haiku illustrating this\r\nsensation. do not refer to concrete events. points deducted for\r\nsanskrit and use of simile.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(4) draw a picture of your inner self, using crayons on graph\r\npaper. exaggerate your most beautiful features and your ugliest\r\nfeatures. with red pen, circle each feature that could be either.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(5) true or false: <i>i accept my own motivations.</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(6) locate a road map of the united states. using a black sharpie,\r\nnarrate the story of your darkest escapist fantasy and cover the road\r\nmap completely with words. staple the road map to this exam.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(7) name the person who betrayed your trust most during the first\r\nsemester. phrase your response in the form of a question.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(8) list all of your remaining attachments, in order by weight and texture.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(9) write two contradictory sentences about a loved one with whom you\r\nare in conflict.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(10) describe your ideal job or career. describe how you would fire\r\nyourself from this ideal job or career.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(11) in the space provided below, write the lyrics to your theme\r\nsong. choose one of the following twelve themes: the hero, the earth\r\nmother, the scholar, the nurturer, the rockstar, the servant, the\r\nlover, the keeper of mystery, the seeker, the provider, the visionary,\r\nthe dreamer.\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\n(12) list your two recurring defense mechanisms and recall a situation\r\nwhere you employed them. next to each defense mechanism, write its\r\nopposite. visualize the situation again, replacing your defense\r\nmechanisms with their opposites. now describe (a) how the situation\r\nwould be worse and (b) how the situation would be better. revise and\r\noptimize the opposites. solve for (b).\r\n</p>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>EXTRA CREDIT:</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nyour home with all your possessions burns to the ground. (if you\r\ncannot imagine this, project lingering memories of any time you\'ve\r\nspent in jail onto the gestalt.) - which is the false reality? (a) the\r\nhome and the possessions. (b) the notion of \"starting over\". (c) the\r\nfire. (d) the arsonist. (e) the attachment to suffering. (f) the\r\nattachment to non-attachment. (g) all or none of the above.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1560,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1560_1.jpg\'>\r\n<br>\r\nI got a postcard in the mail today, a descriptive statement that annotated the ethereal heatwave that envelops Texas during this time of year.  As much as I do swelter here, I have the river for respite, and for this I am thankful; the landlocked folks down South really do have it hard during August.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhen hunger hit (and it teases in spates during a heatwave), I walked towards the taco truck that lingers each day on Broadway cross-streets, wafting its food scents on the brownstones like a cullinary embrace.  There, I found its rusty, greasy hull sweating out the usual fare; enchiladas steaming, burritos dripping.<br>  \r\n<br>\r\nWhen I made my way to the front of the line, I requested tamales.  He asked if I \"wanted spicy\".  \"Yes\", I answered, \"make it hotter than Texas\".  I was unexpectedly greeted with a homesick smile and served a corn-husk filled with the most shattering sidewalk meal I\'ve ever eaten.  Exquisite.<br>'),(1561,'<P>Thanks, Gwayne. Quick roundup of today\'s gayness, starting with the roads. The M70, the A3, the B664 and the A48(M), they\'re all gay as of midnight tonight. The gay elements are potassium, zinc, hydrogen,copper and argon. Quick look at the world\'s walls - the Wailing Wall is gay, Hadrian\'s Wall is very gay, the Great Wall Of China, that\'s not gay, and the Old London Wall has also stopped being gay. Gay cars next, they\'re the same as last night - all Volkswagens registered between 1982 and 1985, they stay gay for another fortnight. And finally, the gay seas are the Caspian and the Mediterranean, so see you there.</P> '),(1562,'God damn it, i need something to do. I have a lot of time on my hands, too much time. I read books, but they don\'t seem to grab my mind. I listen to music, and it just doesn\'t grab me. Its as if nothing matters to me. Fuck, I can\'t explain it. This feeling of lack of inspiration, loss of motivation. The room gets clean, the room gets dirty. The floors need scrubbing, c\'mon I hate scrubbing the floors. I turned the tv on the other day, the screen hissed at me from lack of use, and I proceded to watch only commercials to point out to myself that advertising consists of lying to people by telling them that need something they don\'t really need nor would have wanted if they hadn\'t of seen the commercial. You know this, I am preaching to my very own choir. I need a revolution, I need a new desire. School is interesting enough to keep me motivated, but what happens when I finish that? Can you tell me? Can some one help me out here? I realize that I am reaching out for attention here, but who else to ask? Yes, really I am asking these questions to receive an answer. Sometimes I drool in my own boredom here. I admit this, I check swinney.org nearly every chance I get while I am at home awake. I am home a lot lately. I don\'t work, I don\'t play (often enough), and I definately don\'t study. I do, however, sleep much, daydream much, and eat things that I know hurt me. Also, I drink when I feel like this, which is reson enough for me to cry out. I have wonderful friends here, most of which I never see. I am restless, I am bored, I am sick of listening to sad songs. I need some inspiration. I have three more weeks of this till school starts. I have been doing this since Feburary. Geez, I fucking hate it when I am like this. Somebody slap me.'),(1563,'<P>fade clear into what is</P>\r\n<P>now is entry and his</P>\r\n\r\n<P>smell stays remains in my sheets</P>\r\n<P>in my blankets.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>comrades and we, speak of plans</P>\r\n\r\n<P>soon to be encrypted into sand</P>\r\n<P>made hard by imaginary rain</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i wish no one to claim this spot</P>\r\n\r\n<P>or that and tread it hard with feet</P>\r\n\r\n<P>having seen the globe and back</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>fade clear and into what is</P>\r\n<P>shiver, tears, sweat are this</P>\r\n\r\n<P>slices of being and pieces to miss</P>\r\n\r\n<P>when speaking of what is to be and what is</P>\r\n\r\n<P><I> cuando llorro sola</I></P>\r\n<P><I> el dia empiesa</I></P>\r\n<P><I>esa desagrada amarilla bola</I></P>\r\n<P><I>nos despierta e abriendo</I></P>\r\n<P><I> ojos cerrados ante la luz</I></P>\r\n\r\n<P>faded into jean back pocket</P> \r\n\r\n<P>is the chalky figure of my father\'s wallet</P>\r\n\r\n<P>trying with foot one in front the other</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and at times remembering not to bother</P>\r\n\r\n<P>with the small tangible everyday strains</P>\r\n\r\n<P>which make me stand out too much in a way</P>\r\n\r\n<P>you once said that you knew i was \"weird\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and my number one supporter</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Thank-you .</P>'),(1564,'<p> So I sit here pretending to work. That\'s why i\'m here. Typing this. If i type \r\n  i look busy. No one will question me. In fact no one is here. I\'m alone in the \r\n  office. It\'s 12:46, four hours and fourteen minutes to go. </p>\r\n<p> My significant other keeps texting me << BeEp bEeP >> </p>\r\n<p> <font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" color=\"white\">Hi baby im just textin \r\n  u2 see how ur wat time u gettin home sexy?</font> </p>\r\n<p> <font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" color=\"red\">im fine howru? i b home \r\n  at 5.30ish</font> </p>\r\n<p> <font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" color=\"white\">ok gorgeous wanna fuck?</font> \r\n</p>\r\n<p> <font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" color=\"red\">do i ever! cu l8r thn</font> \r\n</p>\r\n<p> She\'s always doing this. Sure she\'s horny when i\'m at work but by the time \r\n  i get home she\'s been diddling herself off all day long and she\'s tired.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m still here. Sitting alone in the office. Strangely i still pretend to work \r\n  even when i\'m alone. I don\'t know why.</p>\r\n<p>I get an email from a guy i used to work with back in England:</p>\r\n<p>----------------------------------------------------------------</p>\r\n<p>Only 1 fight at a time guys...</p>\r\n<p>-----Original Message-----<br>\r\n  From: Jamie Thompson [mailto:jaxxxx@texxxxweb.com]<br>\r\n  Sent: 10 August 2001 12:37<br>\r\n  To: joxx.bexxy@itrxx.co.uk<br>\r\n  Subject: RE: His name is Jxxxx Bxxxx...<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  When someone yells \"Stop\", goes limp, or taps out, the fight is over...</p>\r\n<p>----------------------------------------------------------------</p>\r\n<p>We like Fight Club. We carry on like this all day. It fills the monotomy. Email \r\n  tennis as it were. It\'s nearly 13:00 now. I need a wee. Why am i holding it? \r\n  Hang on...</p>\r\n<p>I\'m back. The toilet (restroom) here is haunted. It\'s an old building and there\'s \r\n  a definate presence in the toilet. It comes mid-flow. It\'s behind you but you \r\n  know you can\'t stop the flow. It\'s very dark. Very musky.</p>\r\n<p>Back to work then...</p>'),(1566,'<p><font face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\" size=\"2\">Danger Slut\'s eyes gave me \r\n  <a href=\"http://www.dttlyrics.com/albums/symbol.html#7\">seven</a> miracles a \r\n  second. <br>\r\n  I used to open my eyes in the middle of the night,<br>\r\n  an <a href=\"http://www.endless.org/%7Escotch/?view=6\">endless</a> victim of \r\n  her impossible waist<br>\r\n  searching for a <a href=\"http://www.linkworthy.com/\">link</a> to our madness. \r\n  <br>\r\n  Genuine amazement within me is what I miss.<br>\r\n  I never felt as loved as when she watched my sleep.</font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.tncuniverse.com/anime/JATGwolfwood2.jpg\"> </p>'),(1565,'<h2><b>The Headlines Tonight</b></h2>\r\n<p>Fist-headed man destroys church, car drives past window in town and Leicester \r\n  man wins right to eat sister.</p>\r\n<p><i>Coming up as stories in the programme</i> - peace talks collapse as Serbs \r\n  strap 400 monkeys to the back of Owen\'s Volvo- \"It does make it harder \r\n  to reverse\"</p>\r\n<p>Buckingham Palace has announced that over 40 members of royal staff have been \r\n  destroyed to save money. The 43 workers learned of their fate after church on \r\n  Sunday. They met the Queen and were told they would be slaughtered with scythes. \r\n  That evening, they were herded into a Sandringham attic and culled in less than \r\n  ten minutes. It\'s not known who conducted the killings, but Princess Margaret \r\n  is believed to have been involved. She was overheard yesterday saying \'You should \r\n  have seen the looks on their faces: I laughed and laughed and laughed\'.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Conditions on board the train stuck on the line for two days in Hampshire have \r\n  deteriorated dramatically. There are fears now for the lives and sanity of those \r\n  on board. Ted Maul has that story reporting.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>At first the concern was for the commuters, stuck on a train because a signal \r\n    got jammed by a dead bird. But when farmer Peter Yates took them sandwiches \r\n    this afternoon, the situation changed dramatically. Two commuters jumped him \r\n    like raptors and threw him aboard. A few minutes later he managed to get off \r\n    the train and tried to escape, but was lassoed by a man with a rope and pulped! \r\n    A little over an hour after that, a naked Mr Yates appeared briefly for the \r\n    last time, trying to haul himself out of a window. But it was a naff effort. \r\n    Most ominiously, this evening the commuters were seen struggling with a sack \r\n    containing an object that was obviously as heavy as a human body, and then \r\n    throwing some kind of weird ritual.</p>\r\n  <p>Many here fear the worst - that Mr Yates has been killed and sacrificed by \r\n    a group of ordinary men and women who less than two days ago were content \r\n    to sit opposite each other in silence, yet today beat their chests and yelled \r\n    like savages. Moments ago, we intercepted the sound of the guard trying to \r\n    radio for help.</p>\r\n  <p><i>GUARD: Get these people - there\'s a woman - yyyaaaaaaarrghhhh!</i></p>\r\n  <p>All afternoon, aggressive volleys of rubbish thwarted attempts to get the \r\n    train moving again. Barricaded into his engine, the driver has constantly \r\n    to defend himself with tools. In a rare lull - perhaps the commuters were \r\n    sleeping or praying to some new god - one of them left the train. Gerard Hawt, \r\n    a civil aviation engineer, had broken free from the train of hell, but it \r\n    had left its mark.</p>\r\n  <p>The man was in no state to give an interview, but it was possible to extract \r\n    the following information. The commuters are split into two factions, one \r\n    led by a woman believed to be an insurance broker from Staines - they wear \r\n    blue ink on their heads - and a smaller group who worship fire. They rallied \r\n    round a grease-monkey from Acton. With the signal here still jammed by the \r\n    dead bird, there\'s only one hope now - that a police marksman can shoot it \r\n    out, giving the driver a slim chance of making it to Liverpool Street before \r\n    he\'s torn to pieces.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>And just time for a quick look at tomorrow\'s, er... the Independent, \'Bank \r\n  of England recovers from swollen chairman unusualness\', the Telegraph go with \r\n  \'Simon Rattle lost in cress\', the Daily Mail pretty keen on \'Lassoed bat wins \r\n  Booker\', they devote four pages to that, the Daily Mirror rather upset, \'Fleetwood \r\n  Mac buried in dog avalanche\' and Today ride with \'Old woman killed by little \r\n  glass planet\', that\'s a story there by Andrew Penman. Due to a printing error \r\n  tomorrow\'s Guardian is full of water. That\'s it, that\'s The Day Today on the \r\n  day the world learned that Cliff Richard is pregnant. Good night.</p>'),(1567,'<p>\r\nAllegedly this is an ACTUAL radio conversation transcript between a US naval ship with Canadian authorities off the coast of Newfoundland in October, 1995. Don\'t know about you but makes me glad they safeguard the future of the free world. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Americans:</b> Please divert your course 15 degrees to the North to avoid a collision. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Canadians:</b> Recommend you divert YOUR course 15 degrees to the South to avoid a collision. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Americans:</b> This is the Captain of a US Navy ship. I say again, divert YOUR course. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Canadians:</b> No, I say again, you divert YOUR course. \r\n</p>\r\n<p> <b>Americans:</b> <font color=\"#FF0000\">THIS IS THE AIRCRAFT CARRIER USS LINCOLN, \r\n  THE SECOND LARGEST SHIP IN THE UNITED STATES ATLANTIC FLEET. WE ARE ACCOMPANIED \r\n  BY THREE DESTROYERS, THREE CRUISERS AND NUMEROUS SUPPORT VESSELS. I DEMAND THAT \r\n  YOU CHANGE YOUR COURSE 15 DEGREES NORTH, THAT\'S ONE FIVE DEGREES NORTH, OR COUNTER \r\n  MEASURES WILL BE UNDERTAKEN TO ENSURE THE SAFETY OF THIS SHIP.</font> </p>\r\n<p> <b>Canadians:</b> We are a lighthouse. Your call.</p>'),(1568,'</head>\r\n<body text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFCC\" link=\"#0000EE\" vlink=\"#551A8B\" alink=\"#FF0000\">\r\nBeing unemployed, I have too mush time on my hands.\r\n<br>Sleeping in is great, but not when you are <font color=\"#CC33CC\">alone</font>.\r\n<br>I sometimes find myself painting little cardboard squares by acid lake.\r\n<br>It was Janice\'s place...\r\n<p>It has been speculated that she was reincarnated as one of the ducks\r\nthere.\r\n<br>The duck is very mysterious.\r\n<br>He will not be in a picture.\r\n<br>Maybe I should paint him...\r\n<p>So, what am I to do now?\r\n<br>I havent worked in 2 months.\r\n<br>I could move in with some friends in the mountains.\r\n<br>The <font color=\"#3333FF\">rent</font> there is 1/7th of the <font color=\"#3333FF\">rent</font>\r\nhere.\r\n<p>I could grow <font color=\"#009900\">pot</font> in the basement and fall\r\nin <font color=\"#FF0000\">love</font> with a nice hippie girl.\r\n<br>We could have a child together and name her Zoe.\r\n<br>I could move to <font color=\"#FF6600\">Hawaii</font> and live out of\r\na backpack until everything settles.\r\n<br>1,2,3..<font color=\"#FF0000\">Redlight</font>!\r\n<p>Does anyone <font color=\"#CC66CC\">remember</font> how to play that game?\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1569,'<P> \"Why does this person keep flashing his high beams at me?\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"I don\'t know.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I check my speed... perfect 65.  The car flashes his lights at me again.  The person in front of me swerves.  I tap the brakes, more to avoid the person in front of me, but the person behind me is riding up my ass, too... </P>\r\n\r\n<P> He flashes his high beams again.  I maintain my speed.  He turns on his pretty red and blue lights for me. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Shit.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I pull over but I\'m too close to an entrance ramp.  He gets on his little loudspeaker and tells us to pull up past the exit, so I do. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> After blinding us with a spotlight, he gets out of the car and asks for my information.  I had it over. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Do you know why I pulled you over?\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"No.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"You\'re supposed to keep to the right.  There are signs every mile.  On this road you have to keep to the right.  Every time I flashed my lights you could\'ve moved over because there were no cars in the right lane. Then you had to try and be smart and hit your brakes.  Someday someone with road rage isn\'t going to take that very well. Look, there is a sign up there.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P> No point in explaining. I looked but I did not see a sign.  I didn\'t say this.  \"Ok.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"I\'m not going to give you a ticket.  I\'m just going to write you a warning.  I\'ll be right back.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> He walks away.  I turn to my friend. \"Do you think yelling over the loudspeaker thingy was a power trip for him?\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> She laughs and I motion for her to quiet down as the cop walks back to the car. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Here. It\'s just a warning. Pay attention.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> The cop didn\'t bother to turn his spotlight off when he got back to his car.  I couldn\'t see to pull back onto the road.  We almost got hit. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> We watched the rest of the way home.  There were no god damn signs. </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1570,'<p>this summer was better than i thought it would be. true i had to stay in the dorms for a couple weeks before my apt opened up but it was fuckin worth it.  i met some chill ass people. we partied. we still party.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut theres this nagging notion that something is wrong. and yea i got this killer germ from the dorms that made me sick for a couple weeks, which forced me to down antibiotics.  sometimes i still cough. maybe its a disease.  maybe im dying.  maybe i should quit drinking.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe other nite i had a dream my mom died. the day kept repeating itself. me, my dad, and my brother knew she would die the next day but she had no clue.  and each time the day repeated itself i tried more and more to show her how much i really loved her. no matter how we acted, no matter how happy she was, she still died the next day. i remember waking up in tears. sobbing. trying to go to sleep through my liquid sadness.\r\n</p><p>\r\ndays are so much more then they are cracked out to be. i try to make a point in lettin all my friends know i love em without them gettin freaked out. because maybe if they know i\'ll stop coughing</p>'),(1571,'<p>My sister says \'mang\'. I couldn\'t believe it. Today I was taking a shower and she offered me her towel. I paused; she assured me \"i ain\'t dirty, MANG\". I said, \"what did you say?\" she said, \"im not dirty.\" \"no, did you say, \'mang\'\" \"well, yeah, brian and i say it to each other all the time, just jokin around....\"</p>\r\n<p>if there is any way that I love to be with my family, it is sitting around a table or series of tables, shooting the shit, and eating. Those are the two everlasting gobstoppers of us all. Talking shit (=loudgratinglaughter+mockery+invention of funny nicknames/repetition of dorky things one person said+gossip+2bit philosophy+mysticalbullshitting), and eating food. eating food as an activity of great ritual and import is common to all Thai people; I always thought \"talking shit loudly and with vigor\" was as well, with only my mother\'s family and the outskirts as evidence to go on.</p>\r\n<p>According to the real Thais hanging around our great country, that is not so. Supposedly our loudness is not a cultural affliction, but perhaps a genetic one. My cousin Vasin was complaining that wherever he went in Boston, he saw Thai people who \'felt the need to talk loudly\' and it bothered him. it also bothered him when while watching the Princess of Thailand on our grandmother\'s funeral video, my sister needed to exclaim \"what a fatso!\" (in thai) and I, \"she\'s a dyke for sure\"...\"doesn\'t she have a royal trainer/nutritionist? oh yeah, she\'s getting all the royal bitches.\" supposedly, we would be arrested for talking in such a manner in the King\'s country.</p>\r\n<p>When I mused on the possible reasons for our collective deafening loudness, Na Chung (auntie girl who costs a lot) broke my train of thought with the fortune cookie, \"Loud people in the world are loud because they are happy!...People with self-confidence are loud....When families get together and they love each other, they are loud! they are just so happy!...Except for when you go to the state school for the deaf. but actually then they turn the music up, because they can\'t hear it. so it\'s still loud.\" Each of these statements belted out with deafening volume, the irony being that Na Chung is a teacher of the deaf.</p>\r\n<p>She teaches mentally retarded kids too. her secret is to place a shiny silver star on the middle of her forehead while speaking. The children are fixated by her shiny star and they sit and stare.</p>\r\n<p>\"Im going to write this down, this is good shit.\"<br>\r\n\"Oh, I have more good words for you.\"<br>\r\n\"what, like \'MANG\'?\"</p>\r\n<p>I instantly thought of a bet I made with Austin a few weeks ago. I bet that he could not avoid complaining for an entire day; he bet that i could not avoid raising my voice in conversation with loved ones. I lost. I wondered if my loud and overbearing manner would cost me any affection or sex.</p>\r\n<p>I absorb Na Chung\'s wisdom once or twice a year. The oldest fortune cookie I remember is from second grade; my cousin decided to be a dead bride for halloween. Na Chung sews everything. She sewed dresses and halloween costumes back then. Sitting in the kitchen, the bitch of the family because I was the baby, with milk in my hair because I dared chom to pour it, I asked na Chung one of my early famous irksome questions:\"But how could someone who was so happy and so in love, die?\" I guess I was thikning that a \"bride\" was a \"person in love\" back then.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1571_1.jpg\'\r\n width=\"250\" border=\"0\" alt=\"rain broke the heat today\" align=\"right\">She had too much love. Too much of anything is bad for you.\"<br>\r\n\"Even LUV?\"<br>\r\n\"yes.\"<br>\r\n\"even milk!\" when i was 7, milk was King.<br>\r\n\"anything.\"</p>\r\n<p>Even meat pies. Na Chung has lived in Natchitoches, LA for 20 years (that is another story). She makes a mean meat pie. I ate too many tonight. She adds Thai chili to the recipe. It is divine, I tell you. When I bite her meat pie, Im the sort who would die for love.</p>'),(1572,'<P>i was sitting this morning,<BR>\r\nmusing in my own tweed smoking jacket sort of way<BR>\r\nonly in a thai housewife\'s robe<BR>\r\nthinking about how two of egg\'s best friends have now said:<BR>\r\n\"come and take me austin, i am here for you, i wont put up a fight...\"<BR>\r\nwhen these song lyrics came to me</P><BR>\r\n\r\n<TT>\r\n<BR>id like to help the world to sing\r\n<BR>in perfect harmony\r\n<BR>id like to teach the world a thing\r\n<BR>about polyamory!\r\n<BR>it doesnt take a thing or two\r\n<BR>it takes three or four\r\n<BR>it doesnt take a couple of babes\r\n<BR> just a couple of co\'oke whores!\r\n</TT>\r\n<P>thank you thank you, really it was nothing.</P>\r\n<P>hm, it doesnt flow right either.  oh well.</P>\r\n<P>sue me.</P>\r\n'),(1573,'<P>Since I am currently without a job I think I will take a break from you people. Unless something incredibly interesting comes up. I am very elated at the moment. My love asleep on the couch and you feel as if nothing could top this moment...but I was at Denny\'s having a boca burger when Van Morrison\'s \"Moondance\" came on and made me think of my past relationship with Benjamin.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>I really thought it would work and now all I seek from him is friendship because he knows a great deal of all that I am.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>A good friend of thought I had copied her do when shaving my head...only mine was job-related stress induced. hee-hee ( sigh)</P>\r\n\r\n \r\n<P>I have a song stuck in my head and a yearning not for that individual, if this makes any sense, but for that moment in time when I had good grades, was in love with twigs, and ran track. Needed no job, no money, just my friends and some coffe/wine...Those were barefoot sneaking into pool days. Riding bikes at three am days only to not wake up to the smell of tofu over rice.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>Last night my friend came up with this theory that all our boyfriends were dating us because we looked like their exes...yeah, M\'s ex is dark haired, small, thinny, like me...still M explained it well. maybe it is because the first thing men see when looking at someone for the first time is not that funny thing they do with their coffee cup after it is empty...but what they look like overall...hmmm</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Oh well....he is asleep. Taking a nap before we head out to Venice Beach for an \"outing\" of sorts and I feel the need to join him.</P>'),(1574,'you know its been so long since you\'ve said anything I gotta wonder whats up....not sure just confused, can\'t think...And who said I could at least Im trying ...I just keep thinking you\'re leaving me...but thats it, its always about the end of the world ...I\'m confused....you assume too much...well I never said I was perfect...shit no and you think I expected that ...I dont have anyone to talk to who would know or understand...see thats what I mean the lets pretend its ok bullshit its such a struggle to deal with anything...whats to understand...our lives have been a fuckin cocoon like zombies we should have been doing heavy drugs at least then we could have enjoyed it... you make it sound so bad...well maybe it hasnt been but we sure complained a lot for it to be so good I cant even get away from this people talk but it always about you ...sure they love to tear you apart like meat ...well who really gives a fuck next month itll be something else...and youll be gone...yea guess ill be gone...youre so glad to leave just add me to the ex list...yea like you dont have your own list just add me to the dumbasses who dont understand list...I just hoped it would work itself out...you know you make a decision by not doing anything that was your choice cant you see it all staring you in the face....well I like doing my thing everyday am I supposed to give that up....I dont know you tell me youre the one who says there is noone to talk too but then its all jokes and posing...I want to make you laugh...been there done that did I ask for it its like you cant trust anybody I wonder why Im even here...so does this mean its over I mean are you telling me this just to tell me youre unhappy or are you leading into something....can you really sit there and just act like its happy camp everyday this has been going on for years and I just beat my head bloody trying to make it work...but I tried too...hhhhmmmm yea and are we any better off then we were before last year you know LAST YEAR you tried really hard then....Ive cried all I can cry....yea thats what I thought cause I broke your heart or at least thats what all the perfect fucking strangers tell meÂ guess I dont have one... you wont agree but I gave it all up for you....you gave it all up for me or you gave it all up to hide behind me?Â Â Â '),(1575,'<P><CITE>Session on the Creative Act, Convention of the American Federation of Arts, Houston, Texas, April 1957.</CITE></P>\r\n<P>Professor Seitz, Princeton University<BR>\r\nProfessor Arnheim, Sarah Lawrence College<BR>\r\nGregory Bateson, anthropologist<BR>\r\nMarcel Duchamp, mere artist<BR></P>\r\n<P>Let us consider two important factors, the two poles of the creation of art: the artist on the one hand, and on the other the spectator who later becomes the posterity.</P>\r\n<P>To all appearances, the artist acts like a mediumistic being who, from the labyrinth beyond time and space, seeks her way out to a clearing.</P>\r\n<P>If we give the attributes of a medium to the artist, we must then deny him the state of consciousness on the esthetic plane about what he is doing or why he is doing it. All his decisions in the artistic execution of the work rest with pure intuition and cannot be translated into a self-analysis, spoken or written, or even thought out.</P>\r\n<P>T.S. Eliot, in his essay on \'Tradition and Individual Talent\', writes: \'The more perfect the artist, the more completely separate in her will be the woman who suffers and the mind which creates; the more perfectly will the mind digest and transmute the passions which are its material.\'</P>\r\n<P>Millions of artists create; only a few thousands are discussed or accepted by the spectator and many less again are consecrated by posterity.</P>\r\n<P>In the last analysis, the artist may shout from all the rooftops that he is a genius; he will have to wait for the verdict of the spectator in order that his declaration take a social value and that, finally, posterity includes him in the primers of Art History.</P>\r\n<P>I know that this sentiment will not meet with the approval of many artists who refuse this mediumistic role and insist on the validity of their awareness in the creative act- yet, art history has consistently desided upon the virtues of a work of art through considerations completely divorced from the rationalized explanations of the artist.</P>\r\n<P>If the artist, as a human being, full of the best intentions toward herself and the whole world, plays no role at all in the judgement of her own work, how can one describe the phenomenon which prompts the spectator to react critically to the work of art? In other words, how does this reaction come about?</P>\r\n<P>This phenomenon is comparable to a transference from the artist to the spectator in the form of an estetic osmosis taking place through the inert matter, such as pigment, piano, or marble.</P>\r\n<P>But before we go further, I want to clarify our understanding of the word \'art\'- to be sure, without any attempt at a definition.</P>\r\n<P>What I have in mind is that art may be bad, good, or indifferent, but, whatever adjective is used, we must call it art, and bad art is still art in the same way that a bad emotion is still and emotion.</P>\r\n<P>Therefore, when I refer to \'art coefficient\', it will be understood that I refer not only to great art, but I am trying to describe the subjective mechanism which produces art in the raw state- a l\'etat brut- bad, good or indifferent.</P>\r\n<P>In the creative act, the artist goes from intention to realization through a chain of totally subjective reactions. His struggle toward the realization is a series of efforts, pains, satisfactions, refusals, decisions, which also cannot and must not be fully self-conscious, at least on the esthetic plane.</P>\r\n<P>The result of this struggle is a difference between the intention and its realization, a difference which the artist is not aware of.</P>\r\n<P>Consequently, in the chain of reactions accompanying the creative act, a link is missing. This gap, representing the inability of the artist to express fully his intenion, this difference between what he intended to realize and did realize, is the personal \'art coefficient\' contained in the work.</P>\r\n<P>In other words, the personal \'art coefficient\' is like an arithmetical relation between the unexpressed but intended and the unintentionally expressed.</P>\r\n<P>To avoid a misunderstanding, we must remember that his \'art coefficient\' is a personal expression of art <CITE>a l\'etat brut</CITE>, that is, still in a raw state, which must be \'refined\' as pure sugar from molasses by the spectator; the digit of this coefficient had no bearing whatsoever on his verdict. The creative act takes another aspect when the spectator experiences the phenomenon of transmutation: through the change from inert matter into a work of art, an actual transubstatntiation has taken place, and the role of  the spectator is to determine the weight of the work on the esthetic scale.</P>\r\n<P>All in all, the creative act is not performed by the artist alone; the spectator brings the work in contact with the external world by ceciphering and interpreting its inner qualifications and thus adds his contribution to the creative act. This becomes even more obvious when posterity gives its final verdict and sometimes rehabilitates forgotten artists.</P>'),(1576,'<P><CITE>Session on the Creative Act, Convention of the American Federation of Arts, Houston, Texas, April 1957.</CITE></P>\r\n<P>Professor Seitz, Princeton University<BR>\r\nProfessor Arnheim, Sarah Lawrence College<BR>\r\nGregory Bateson, anthropologist<BR>\r\nMarcel Duchamp, mere artist<BR></P>\r\n<P>Let us consider two important factors, the two poles of the creation of art: the artist on the one hand, and on the other the spectator who later becomes the posterity.</P>\r\n<P>To all appearances, the artist acts like a mediumistic being who, from the labyrinth beyond time and space, seeks her way out to a clearing.</P>\r\n<P>If we give the attributes of a medium to the artist, we must then deny him the state of consciousness on the esthetic plane about what he is doing or why he is doing it. All his decisions in the artistic execution of the work rest with pure intuition and cannot be translated into a self-analysis, spoken or written, or even thought out.</P>\r\n<P>T.S. Eliot, in his essay on \'Tradition and Individual Talent\', writes: \'The more perfect the artist, the more completely separate in her will be the woman who suffers and the mind which creates; the more perfectly will the mind digest and transmute the passions which are its material.\'</P>\r\n<P>Millions of artists create; only a few thousands are discussed or accepted by the spectator and many less again are consecrated by posterity.</P>\r\n<P>In the last analysis, the artist may shout from all the rooftops that he is a genius; he will have to wait for the verdict of the spectator in order that his declaration take a social value and that, finally, posterity includes him in the primers of Art History.</P>\r\n<P>I know that this sentiment will not meet with the approval of many artists who refuse this mediumistic role and insist on the validity of their awareness in the creative act- yet, art history has consistently desided upon the virtues of a work of art through considerations completely divorced from the rationalized explanations of the artist.</P>\r\n<P>If the artist, as a human being, full of the best intentions toward herself and the whole world, plays no role at all in the judgement of her own work, how can one describe the phenomenon which prompts the spectator to react critically to the work of art? In other words, how does this reaction come about?</P>\r\n<P>This phenomenon is comparable to a transference from the artist to the spectator in the form of an estetic osmosis taking place through the inert matter, such as pigment, piano, or marble.</P>\r\n<P>But before we go further, I want to clarify our understanding of the word \'art\'- to be sure, without any attempt at a definition.</P>\r\n<P>What I have in mind is that art may be bad, good, or indifferent, but, whatever adjective is used, we must call it art, and bad art is still art in the same way that a bad emotion is still and emotion.</P>\r\n<P>Therefore, when I refer to \'art coefficient\', it will be understood that I refer not only to great art, but I am trying to describe the subjective mechanism which produces art in the raw state- a l\'etat brut- bad, good or indifferent.</P>\r\n<P>In the creative act, the artist goes from intention to realization through a chain of totally subjective reactions. His struggle toward the realization is a series of efforts, pains, satisfactions, refusals, decisions, which also cannot and must not be fully self-conscious, at least on the esthetic plane.</P>\r\n<P>The result of this struggle is a difference between the intention and its realization, a difference which the artist is not aware of.</P>\r\n<P>Consequently, in the chain of reactions accompanying the creative act, a link is missing. This gap, representing the inability of the artist to express fully his intenion, this difference between what he intended to realize and did realize, is the personal \'art coefficient\' contained in the work.</P>\r\n<P>In other words, the personal \'art coefficient\' is like an arithmetical relation between the unexpressed but intended and the unintentionally expressed.</P>\r\n<P>To avoid a misunderstanding, we must remember that his \'art coefficient\' is a personal expression of art <CITE>a l\'etat brut</CITE>, that is, still in a raw state, which must be \'refined\' as pure sugar from molasses by the spectator; the digit of this coefficient had no bearing whatsoever on his verdict. The creative act takes another aspect when the spectator experiences the phenomenon of transmutation: through the change from inert matter into a work of art, an actual transubstatntiation has taken place, and the role of  the spectator is to determine the weight of the work on the esthetic scale.</P>\r\n<P>All in all, the creative act is not performed by the artist alone; the spectator brings the work in contact with the external world by ceciphering and interpreting its inner qualifications and thus adds his contribution to the creative act. This becomes even more obvious when posterity gives its final verdict and sometimes rehabilitates forgotten artists.</P>'),(1577,'<p><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"> <b>l</b>: <i>i wish i could just disappear into the earth</i>.\r\n<br><b>g</b>: <i>careful what you wish for</i>.\r\n<p><b>j</b>, <b>a</b>, <b>l</b> and i went to see the meteor shower tonight.\r\ngetting away from ambient light of the megalopolis we headed east past\r\nthe vast sprawl of burbs through livermore and toward some lake. there\r\nare a lot of fucking street lights out there. this was our big discovery.\r\nall we wanted was some darkness, to feel we were away from it all, while\r\nbeing 40min away from home. everywhere street lamps, its incredible really.\r\n<p><b>l</b>: <i>so is this really fair?</i>\r\n<br><b>g</b>: <i>hrmgh?</i>\r\n<br><b>l</b>: <i>isn\'t there some rule that you can\'t come to a meteor\r\nshower and make wishes? you can\'t make a wish for every shooting star you\r\nsee. you can\'t come with a list.</i>\r\n<br><b>g</b>: <i>hrmgh.</i>\r\n<br><b>l</b>: <i>it just doesn\'t seem right.</i>\r\n<p>lying on the hood of the car l must have seen about 20 of them. i lay\r\nthere having trouble focusing mesmerized by the milky way, i\'ve really\r\nbeen in the city too long. we lay there just the two of us, j and a over\r\nthe hill occasionally responding to the animal mating noises l was making.\r\nshe also kept pointing out all the shooting stars i misses. we lay there\r\ntill some car pulled over a little down the hill from us.\r\n<p><b>g</b>: <i>did those people just stop there? oh god.</i>\r\n<br><b>l</b>: <i>get in the car! get in the car, quick! get the blanket.</i>\r\n<br><b>g</b>: <i>fuck the blanket, just get in the car. what\'s going on?\r\ndid some one just get out of that car?</i>\r\n<br><b>l</b>: <i>lock the doors! lock the doors! what the hell? what\'s\r\ngoing on?</i>\r\n<p>paranoid about any car that would stop in the middle of nowhere near\r\nus<b> l </b>and i barricaded ourselves in the car. craning our heads to\r\nlook out the sun-roof we didn\'t dare come out. we sat there until<b> j\r\n</b>and <b>a</b> came back and we scampered off. i&nbsp; guess i forgot\r\nto make a wish list. i forgot to wish on any shooting stars. oh well, as\r\n<b>j</b> said, you gotta give up on shooting stars sometime.\r\n</font>'),(1578,'</head>\r\n<body>\r\n\r\n<center><font face=\"Lucida Blackletter\"><font size=+1>Gathering</font></font>\r\n<br>Â \r\n<br>Solar panels.\r\n<br>They store <font color=\"#3333FF\">energy</font> for the batteries.\r\n<br>The batteries power the vibrations of <font color=\"#009900\">life</font>.\r\n<p>The <font color=\"#FF0000\">burned</font> man will be enlightend...\r\n<p>Music plays in all sectors of <font color=\"#66FFFF\">transparency</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF99FF\">translucennt</font> energy envelops...\r\n<p>~\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF9900\"><font size=+1>The cosmis chair</font></font>\r\n<p>Think of an aotm,\r\n<br>spinning with electrons, neutrons, and quarks.\r\n<br>Now think of the <font color=\"#FFCC00\">Sun</font> spinning, with planets,\r\n<font color=\"#3333FF\">moons</font>, and orbitals.\r\n<br>The atoms are a part of something larger.\r\n<br><font color=\"#009900\">You see</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#009900\">where this story is going.</font>\r\n<br>Our universe is nothing more than sub atomic particles\r\n<br>making up a much larger <font color=\"#993366\">MASS</font>...\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF6600\">The cosmic chair</font><font color=\"#FF6600\"></font>\r\n<p><img SRC=\"http://saturn5.com/swinney/lsd.jpg\" height=138 width=150></center>\r\n\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1579,'<P>\r\nyesterday morning i went down to my car to head from oakland over to the city of san francisco and found that my car no longer contained my stereo or my laptop.  the previous night, under the 580 overpass my car was very neatly broken into and the stereo was very neatly pryed out of the console with some sort of screw driver, leaving only a few scratches on the dash.  there was no dirt smeared where the door might have been jimmied.  there was no trace of entry.  yes the doors were locked.  i didnt take the time to put my laptop in the trunk, or the face plate to my stereo in my bag, but i did take the time to check that all the doors were locked and seats were in the upright position.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni lost some things and stuff.  the reaction had was one of mixed suprise that the gamble was finally lost.  leaving my car under the overpass of the 580, i never had any illusions of safety.  my car was going to get broken into.  at the very least i would lose the musical device.  whatever will be will be.  \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthe thieves did not take the envelope with a thousand dollars in it that was in the glove compartment.  that money i was going to use to purchase a motorcycle, but then decided to save it for the recession, or purchase tickets to see the aurora in alaska this winter.  nor was my envelope of polaroids taken, pictures that egg gave me, a half dozen or so pictures of the los angeles sky line overlooking east hollywood and los feliz at night, and a few of her self portraits that  gave to me.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nsitting in traffic, considering my feelings, staring at the wires hanging from a gaping hole where i used to listen to NPR from and think how tired i was of hearing the same sound bites and commentary over and over again, i remembered my first truck which i paid 1200 for and worked perfectly for years, the only problems ever to arise being a rotted coolant hose, and rust in the radiator.  it was an 87 isuzu pup.  it had been totalled before i bought it in 98 and was very ugly and white.  there was no stereo, only wires hanging out.  this was the connection.  i thought, why did i pay fifteen thousand dollars for a car that would after two years and one night become the equivalent from my driver\'s perspective of a 1200 dollar isuzu pup?  raped dashboard with wires hanging down.  i felt violated as an owner.  my car will never have the security that i put in it.  it has been my home, the place i hang my hat (above the back passenger seat and the rear window) for the last year.  i spend hours upon hours, miles upon miles, with that car every week.  \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nhave you ever paniced because you felt your relasionship with your significant other was taking too much effort and would have to end if only from exhaustion and felt compelled to terminated the affair for those reasons?  that has been me and my car.  i was always scared of being let down by it.  having my feelings of security and purity of ownership lost all at once.  i tried to sell it this summer before it was vandalized, or damaged, or wrecked.  now in the last three weeks it has met not only with this recent robbery, but also with vandalization at the same scene.  the back corner panel of the passenger side was pushed in without leaving any trace of the action, any smear of dirt.  i thought it was for no reason, just a malicious act.  now i see that incident as a warning to me, that if i kept coming to oakland and sleeping with oakland\'s woman, and parking under oakland\'s 580 overpass, oakland would do something worse than just push in a panel on my car.  oakland would steal into my car during the night and remove my stereo and other obvious valuables and disappear into the night without leaving a trace.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nwhere did i fail?  \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ndid i not pour out a forty ounce beer onto oakland\'s curb?\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ndid i not burn a nickle bag of weed into oakland\'s air?\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\noakland, i get your message.  i understand now.  i fear you.  i respect you.  i will give you offerings and learn to accept my mortality and know that my lacadaisical materialism offends you.  oakland, you are richer than me, more powereful than me, my belongings are mine only because you let me keep them.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni know you know there was a spare key to the car in the compartment under the steering wheel, and you left it there.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthank you.\r\n</P>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>\r\n<a href=\"http://swinney.org/gal/austin/desert/\">pictures from the road trip from LA to Austin last year in my car.</A>\r\n<LI><a href=\"http://swinney.org/gal/austin/me-car500.jpg\">a picture the day i got my new car</a>, and <a href=\"http://swinney.org/gal/austin/mi-car500.jpg\">another</a>\r\n\r\n'),(1580,'<p><i>dear limey...</i></p>\r\n<p>i have no idea why i am writing you. it\'s been awhile hasn\'t it.<br>\r\nhmmm, you just popped into my head. this second. right now. so i am following spontaneity.</p>\r\n<p>i am fierce today. i am making plans. grad school? possibly. in a year or\r\ntwo. but maybe i wont need to go.</p>\r\n<p>i\'m submitting stuff for shows. <a href=\"http://www.sxsw.com/2001/interactive/art/index.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">sxsw</a>.\r\n<a href=\"http://www.sfmoma.org/espace/espace_overview.html\" target=\"_blank\">sfmoma</a>.\r\n<a href=\"http://www.siggraph.org/tas/\" target=\"_blank\">siggraph</a>. working on\r\ndigital storytelling, three-d environments, some web projects, interactive\r\ndocumentaries, a few graphic novels.</p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p>by the way....</p>\r\n<p>i think you are hot.</p>\r\n<p>would you ever let me paint you? <br>\r\n[i don\'t mean paint a picture of you...i mean paint ON you]</p>\r\n<p>take pictures of you? </p>\r\n<p>feed you fruit? lick melted chocolate off your skin?</p>\r\n<p>devour you?</p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p>yes...i am feeling fierce today.</p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p><i>~k</i></p>'),(1581,'<P>Hey \r\n\r\n<P>i could eat you </P>\r\n\r\n<P>bury my face in your arm and shiver</P>\r\n\r\n<P>harsh </P>\r\n\r\n<P>inside and feel you </P>\r\n\r\n<P>loving me as much</P> \r\n\r\n<P>and I can feel through</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the way we are</P> \r\n\r\n<P>like nipples  dancing</P> \r\n\r\n<P>through a silk shirt</P>\r\n\r\n<P>unhindered</P>\r\n\r\n<P>how i need you</P>\r\n\r\n<P> lemme take a bite and</P>\r\n\r\n<P> salt on tongue </P>\r\n\r\n<P> i think i know you from a different </P>\r\n\r\n<P>angle</P>\r\n\r\n<P>your own personal photographer</P>'),(1582,'<br>\r\nEver have one of those epiphanic moments where everything seems to orbit in your mind, just so, all momentum, energy and mass balancing out for a single glimmering instant?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nEver hyperalimented that with the most intense feelings of personal ecstasy, and unraveled within youself the most mindless moment of bliss, a moment where you actually felt like your truest self, boiled down to its essence?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nLately, I have not communicated well with my soulmate, myself not causing the minority of our shared obfuscation.  The frustration that gorges itself on failed attempts and kamikaze patch-jobs can be crippling at times, you know.  My problem was that I knew what I wanted to say, but couldn\'t force these dreamed-up cures into words, or at least logical, universal thought patterns.  All I spewed was dangling fragments of genuine effort, tinged with frustration and sadness, ulterior motives, selfishness.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThis weekend, after stomping these motives, emotions, hurts and worries into the floor, after sweating out what would have been saved away as this week\'s tears, my physical willingness met my mental logic halfway, and the cosmos provided the perfect setting, time and emotional state.  What transpired upon coming home was one of the most memorable moments of my life, the most exhilirating evening of re-togetherness, a touchstone moment of realignment, sour things became well again, and I feel that same bliss that i felt the first moment i knew i had been blessed with love for life.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYou, you reading this right now- thank you.  You, all of you who have ever read my blabbering words have been soundboards, testing grounds to help me find my words.  Many influences and empathies, not least among which have been people\'s comments have helped me find the true words I\'d have said without all the noise, and have, in some far-off way, helped create a necesary moment for me.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1583,'<p>\r\ncan somebody explain why people use <a href=\"/journals/article.phtml?id=1581.txt\">seemingly nonsensical spacing techniques</a> in their writing?\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni was going to ask \"why do people write poetry?\", but i realized that because it\'s a little more abstract, lyrical, it\'s supposed to be a more evocative and intense reading experience. and hey, that\'s totally reasonable.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>but</p>\r\n<p>i just don\'t get</p>\r\n<p>why</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<p>the</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<p>spacing?</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</blockquote>'),(1584,'<br>\r\nAs I saw the coked-up, loved-up folks come down Sunday morning, I felt like my world had just exponentially split, propigating upwards in multidimensional growth.  Fractal-like, multiplying like a bacterial culture of shared empathies and love, insight, and greater-than-ness. Saturday night, I felt the shooooom! and killed it.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI remember the guys from Ireland looking for ecstasy.  I remember petting the sweaty, bald-head of the bearded fellow, the dude who bribed the DJ to spin his track, the strangers cheering me, the pretty necklace the lady was wearing as she repeated, \"mental, mental, mental...\".<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI can feel this feeling, so thick- the open-ness of knowing that all is ok, and what isn\'t is at least neatly sorted away, finally broken down to its elements- a reverse exponential propigation of the bad things.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf I can fight my emotions and exercise my patience, sitting back and letting this roll, knowing that personal things will fare well as time passes.  I\'m posting this as a reminder to myself, because at some point, I\'ll have redeemed myself in the ultimate way, just by waiting.  Until then, I can come back and read this.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nDo I mind waiting another year for my lover to grow up?  Some guy in the Bible waited 7, I think, so maybe it\'d be worth it after all.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1585,'<P>there are a lot of people who have said to me, \"i think you are a better person than me, because you are willing to care and be good to someone even in the face of insult and injury.\" <!--  Date: Mon, 13 Aug 2001 12:20:00 -0700, From: anagnorisis, To: anna  -->  that\'s only the most recent time it\'s been said to me; so many other times has the equivalent been said that i cannot even count them on all of my fingers and all of my toes.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'m not sure how i feel about this. on the one hand, yes. it\'s true. when i give my friendship and my caring to someone, usually i will only take away the friendship. the caring never goes away.  i\'ve been told that\'s partially because i am a cancer. (i have also been told that this is because i am the second coming of christ/a prophetes, but that is another story all together.)  i am a mommy to the world, and i don\'t mind it.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>there are people i have not spoken to in many years. i stopped speaking to them because they hurt me beyond all forgiveness, beyond redemption.  it took me months before i didn\'t think about everything that happened every single day, and only just now am i forgetting that the events actually happened, thinking maybe it was all a bad, teen-angst-induced dream.  but should these unforgiveables show up on my doorstep tomorrow, looking for dry and clean clothing, a bed, and warm food, i would take them in and pet them and spoon soup into hungry mouths and give the shirt off my back.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that\'s not even what i think i would do, or what i would like to believe i would do. i <b>know</b> that is what i would do.  i\'ve done it before, more times than i would like to admit.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>sometimes i feel as though this is something to be ashamed of. i\'m a better person because i am compassionate? when did compassion become a requirement for being a better person?  you see compassion and love and caring--i see someone who cannot stand suffering in any form, who would <b>love</b> to be able to watch her worst enemy suffer in a ditch somewhere, but wouldn\'t be able to stand more than a few seconds of it. i would have to help.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'m jealous of people, a lot of the time, who can be malicious.  i\'m vindictive, definitely, but i am not malicious. it was described as the \"if-you-fuck-with-my-homies, you-fuck-with-me\" type of vindictiveness.  well, loyalty.  so if you\'re ever in my posse (we don\'t do homework, you see) then you are always in my posse forever, and if you ever need me, i\'m there. no questions asked.  and i am jealous of all of the people who don\'t feel that kind of bond with everyone else.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>because really what this means is that i waste my time and waste my time and waste my time hoping that i will make a difference, and in the end i am just tired and broke and hurt all over again when i give and give and give and get stepped on and laughed at--and then used again. <!-- i have just as many feelings as you and it hurts just as much when someone steps all over them. --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>and sometimes it\'s difficult for me to understand why it is so hard for people to take what i want to give them.  is generosity and caring so difficult to understand and accept?  even <U><I><B>i</b></i></u> am not that cynical and jaded.</p>'),(1586,'whenever i\'m in the bathroom, someone calls. usually long distance, and cannot be contacted again for days.</p><p>\r\n\r\ni am constantly hit on and asked out by various men when im in a relationship.</p><p>\r\n\r\nmy bladder always wants release when there is no bathroom within a 15 minute radius.</p><p>\r\n\r\nwhen i have lots of time on my hands i get less work done than when my schedule is fuckin full.</p><p>\r\n\r\ni get allergies whenever i go to my hometown but never when im in my college town</p><p>\r\n\r\nmy bf'),(1587,'Lately, I have been trying to make sense of my life. You know how you feel one way about something for a while, and then later you reevaluate how you feel about it and discover that things have changed 180 degrees? Before you may have said, \'this is very important to me\', and then a few years later you say, \'that was dumb, I don\'t care about that.\' Sort of like when you look at your handwriting from when you were ten, and recognize it as yours but you don\'t make T\'s like that anymore? \r\n<p>\r\nI feel like that about twice a week now.\r\n<p>\r\nI am drifting, I don\'t recognize myself. I\'m here, I\'m there, I\'m everywhere. My growth rate is exponential, like technology after the invention of the computer. I didn\'t know life could be like this. I hope it continues, but it is frightening. I have to fight the urge to try to create timelessness, like clinging to an old stuffed animal. It doesn\'t work. You aren\'t as scared if you don\'t cling to something.'),(1588,'</head>\r\n<body>\r\n\r\n<center><font color=\"#FF0000\">Strange Dream</font></center>\r\n<font color=\"#3333FF\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">I just took a nap because I had this small headache.</font><font color=\"#FF0000\"></font><font color=\"#FF0000\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF0000\">I had this dream that I was riding a four wheeler.&nbsp;\r\nI was sitting behind&nbsp; Mark.&nbsp; I remember holding on to his ear\r\nand pressing the gas.&nbsp; Somehow we end up at a beach.&nbsp; It is night\r\ntime.&nbsp; We and are both surprised that we made it all the way on a\r\nfour wheeler.&nbsp; I pick some pot off of a plant by a fence.&nbsp; I\r\nturn around and look at the rocky, barren beach.&nbsp; I contemplate going\r\neven further to my family beach house but decide it too far.&nbsp; I begin\r\nto head back...</font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF0000\">The next thing I know I am riding a unicycle down\r\na dorm hallway.&nbsp; I pass by a room and think I see dawn.&nbsp; I stop\r\nand ride into the room.&nbsp; It is not her.&nbsp; I keep moving.&nbsp;\r\nI ride into another room because I think I recognize someone else.&nbsp;\r\nI crash into their sega and spill soda all over the carpet.&nbsp; I start\r\nto clean it up and I notice Cabbage and Luther.&nbsp; They are happy to\r\nsee me.&nbsp; They ask me if I have any extra pot.&nbsp; I agree to give\r\nthem some of what I have, even though I have very little...</font>\r\n<center>\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">I wake up....</font></center>\r\n\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">I lie in bed for a moment and think about the\r\ndream.</font><font color=\"#3333FF\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">I have no idea where the dorm rooms were.&nbsp;\r\nThe girl, Dawn, I haven\'t seen since I was in High school.&nbsp; I never\r\nreally talked to or hung out with her.&nbsp; We were part of different\r\ncircles I guess.&nbsp; The two guys, Luther and Cabbage, I knew from college.&nbsp;\r\nWe were all good friends at first, until they turned my girlfriends into\r\ncoke whores.&nbsp; No hard feelings.&nbsp; The guy I was on the four wheeler\r\nwith, Mark, I knew when I was 10 or so.&nbsp; The beach we arrived does\r\nnot actually exist.</font><font color=\"#FF0000\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF0000\">But, in my mind I have memories of it outside\r\nof the dream.&nbsp; It is where you turn to go my families beach house.&nbsp;\r\nTo get there from home you must travel south until you hit the beach.&nbsp;\r\nThen take a road to the west for an hour or so and turn south again for\r\na few more hours.&nbsp;</font><font color=\"#3333FF\"></font><font color=\"#3333FF\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">Our real&nbsp; house is in a totally different\r\ngeography.&nbsp; I didn\'t dream about going to our real house.&nbsp; But,\r\nin my dream it was real.&nbsp; Its like I dreamed about a past life or\r\na past dream because I have these previous memories.</font><font color=\"#FF0000\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF0000\">I remember a little road that follows the cliff.&nbsp;\r\nIt has a couple of houseboats and sailboats tied up along the way.&nbsp;\r\nIt was close to our house, and I remember my father telling me not to go\r\nthere.&nbsp; I remember every time we went there more things had been built\r\nand more people had moved there.</font><font color=\"#FF0000\"></font>\r\n<center>\r\n<p><font color=\"#3333FF\">It is all getting fuzzy now...</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#3333FF\">My headache is gone :)</font><font color=\"#3333FF\"></font>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1588_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=center\'></center>\r\n\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1589,'Normally movies don\'t make me sad, because normally I watch funny movies (more than enough exists in the real world  to make me sad).  But every now and again I watch a good movie that happens to also be a sad movie.  Any nominations for those movies\Zthat really balled up and hit you in the gut?  Here\'s a trio from me:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>\r\n<a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0102443\">A Midnight Clear</a>\r\n</li>\r\n<li>\r\n<a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0083658\">Blade Runner</a>\r\n</li>\r\n<li>\r\n<a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0111579\">Burnt by the Sun</a> (russian w/ english subtitles)\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nso, swinneyites, what sad movies do you like?'),(1590,'<p>seems as though that is my theme. I don\'t wanna play. </p><p>So tonight, I get invited to hang out with a friend for a drink, and then get cornered into a hook-up situation. I am not comfortable with it from the start. I am in my house clothes, and we are going out on the town. Not being ready for a night out on the town, I am in a mood of discomfort already. To add to it, I am to meet up with a woman that has an interest in me. Thats not a big deal, but this girl walks in ready for the evening. Meaning, she has got on this fabulous short dress, beautiful hair and shoes. She looks great, good enough to eat. I am caught off gaurd, intentional foul on the part of my friend who has brought me there. I should have guessed it when Francisco told me to meet him at his hair studio, and when I get there he plops me in the chair for a neck trim that I was to meet this woman. </p><p>High finance I tell you, way too much upkeep for my income level. This girl has got the curves and the moves, she could have me at any minute.</p> Thinking back I wish I was forwarned that I was going to meet this woman. </p><p>Then as I am talking to her, I detect this level of shallowness that I despise, so now it makes things all okay.  It wasn\'t what she said but the manner in which she said it, far too comfident and too un-sympethetic. The conversation was about the blind school in which I worked at over the summer. She said something about havind to deal with head cases. I was defensive at that comment, and wished she had not made such a judgement. </p><p>I am not accustumed to playing the game, nor do I wanna play. I get a few moments alone with her, and immediately I am turned off. She is outgoing, but in the way that is not very flattering to her beauty. I turn away, uninterested. I am fully aware that this woman can devoure me at me at any given moment, but yet her ego was not appealing. For instance, the time tonight were she place her hand upon my elbow and began to caress it, along with a slight touch of my ass. Instantly turned off, back away woman - I don\'t play. Yet, there is that part of me that thinks, \"okay, I\'ll play your game.\" Still, I have concluded that I won\'t be seeing this woman again...</p><p>There was a time there where I flirted with the idea of playing the game with her, but then... I thought of lulu, and how she told me that she would put my balls in a bear trap... and for a moment there I pretended that i was talking to a beautiful woman, with much wisdom to pass on to my young soul.</p><p> Sorry honey, better luck next time.</p><p>But wait, why is it that I come home, pour myself a drink, and allow these thoughts to flow through my head... I think it is a protective mechanism. Maybe I should play???</p>'),(1591,'<p>let\'s start with number 6 shall we, cause if there\'s one thing i\'ve learned about humanity in the past few months it\'s that all rules, unwritten or written, are broken all the fucking time.  so let\'s proceed shall we...<br>\r\n6. (i figure i should just clump all these together into one) George dubya Bush, recession, conservative democrats, starbucks, drilling in alaska, and...George dubya Bush.  all reasons to tear my fingernails out with pliers and pour lemon juice on my hands.<br></p>\r\n<p>let\'s move onto numero tres.<br>\r\n3.  everyday a new reason arises for me to become more and more skeptical of what people say.  one minute they\'re praising you and complimenting your hard work, the next minute they\'re ratting on you to the boss because it\'s a rat race, folks...all a bad fucking joke.  it\'s every man for themselves,  don\'t expect a coworker, friend, or even family to take you into consideration for anything.  when it all comes down to it, friends, it\'s simply about getting what you want, isn\'t it?  c\'mon, don\'t kid yourselves, admit, that\'s really what it comes down to. <br>\r\n7. to compound the everday stress of graveyard shift work, pathetic office politics and bad attitudes, the one person that\'s supposed to be a constant and a pillar of strength in my life, gets into a drunk driving accident and calls me in the middle of the night to tell me that she has no clue as to where she is and that her new car is crushed.  the lady who has always told me to drive responsibly, the lady who has always lectured me on being a responsible person...and now i\'m stuck at work worrying about her, wondering if my brother will be able to find her somewhere in Topanga in a parking lot...let me interject with a \"what the fuck.\"<br></P>\r\ni don\'t really care to go on with #5, 10, 1, 2, 9, 8, or 4, cause like i said, rules are broken. \r\n\"that\'s life\" as i keep hearing.  \"yeah, people screw you over and you get shit on, things don\'t go in any coherent direction....but that\'s life\"<br>\r\nthe phrase has clearer meaning for me these days and i feel i can use it with confidence, and i recommend people get accustomed to it just the same.  cause really \"humanity\" is just a euphemism for a burning bag of shit on your front porch on a bright sunny day where blue birds with signs around their necks that say \"alms for the poor\" fly overhead.\r\nso, suck it you SWINNEY.ORG schmucks.'),(1592,'<p>I was just one stop from where I was going.  5pm rush hour press on the 9 train.  It\'s hot outside.  At least the subway cars are airconditioned.  I\'m looking around at the other people, thinking about their stories.  Everyone else is trying to ignore the copious mass of humanity invading their personal space, trying to create a sort of psychological bubble of personal space.  New York City can be the loneliest place on earth, surrounded by people pretending you don\'t exist.  Too much flesh in too small space.  She has her back to everyone, forehead pressed against plexi-glass, face buried in handkerchief buried in hands.  Her shoulders were shaking in rythmic fashion; her chest heaved.  She raises her head a moment and I see the quiet tears drip down her face.  I want to touch her shoulder, smile and ask.  I want to let her hold me, find some comfort in this sea of human indifference.  I used to cry on subways.  I used to wonder if people noticed or cared, if someone would touch my shoulder, smile and ask.  I think about this as I watch her.  I think, \'what would happen?\'  She\'s an attractive woman and I\'m a man.  Would I be seen as another sexual predator? or a relief from this city of anonymity?  but I have my own psychological bubble of personal space.  The train stops.  The doors open.  This is my stop.</P>'),(1593,'<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] All the sexy \r\n  people swim at night.</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] If my life \r\n  so far was a movie trailer what would it take to get people to engage and be \r\n  interested</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] Not seeing \r\n  a movie of <img src=\"http://images.amazon.com/images/P/0679748253.01.MZZZZZZZ.gif\" align=\"middle\"> \r\n  <br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  with Johnny Depp as the narrator, Sean Penn as F. and <a href=\"http://www.candy-fans.com/anwar/pictures/anwar012.jpg\">Gabrielle \r\n  Anwar </a>as Catherine.</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] <a href=\"http://achtungbaby.org/dvd/\">addiction</a> \r\n  as fun</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] I love you \r\n  <a href=\"http://www.xs4all.nl/%7Eatease/ateaseweb_com-likespinningplates.mp3\">Like \r\n  Spinning Plates</a>. </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"-5\">[] a messy office</font></p>\r\n<P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1593_1.jpg\' >\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1594,'<p>My last day of work today, perhaps for many months. Coworkers bought bagels and some pastries to celebrate. They\'re all out of a job in six weeks, too, but we might as well celebrate all we can. The first time in my life, since college I guess, that I\'ll be taking time off and have some money to spare and not be completely stressed out and facing starvation.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s been a strange summer. People shifting, perspectives shifting, cars disappearing. Layoffs. All kinds of layoffs. My first California summer and the mornings sure are gray. I know this isn\'t SoCal, but I didn\'t expect all these clouds. It\'s disheartening in the morning, makes me want to sleep till noon.</p>\r\n<p>Amy says the web is dead, but I doubt it. The Gold Rush is over, it\'s back to old banal reality in the business world, where the smart ones are thrifty. The mood of everyone is dark and pessimistic. How come we just flow with the whims of the economy, letting the zeitgeist pick us up and twirl us in the air and drop us back down again? Why should we listen to the droning politicians, and the wrist-slapping economists saying \"I told you so\" when really this year is no different than last year? If we lived in Montana, that is, this year would be no different than last year, except for the specifics of weather patterns perhaps, and the changing faces of livestock in the barn. The talking heads still sputter the same shit, and if you listen, you\'re no better than they are. You are being numbed, and brainwashed. Get off your ass and do what you gotta do. It\'s all the same fuckin year, man.</p>\r\n<p>So, like Bob Dylan, I\'m going back to New York City, I do believe I\'ve had enough. I\'m only going back for a week, gonna see a play and wander the streets (a friend says \"It\'s like Vietnam here, it\'s so fuckin hot...\"), gonna smoke in bars and see my homies, then travel north to the lush green hills of Vermont, and bemoan the woes of life with my ever-woeful parents. It\'s going to be nice to see the East Coast in August again, and then to return to California in September, when the mornings will be less gray. </p>\r\n<p>I don\'t know why I\'m optimistic. I have no particular reason to be. It\'s sort of a matter of survival, and I\'ve come to understand that it takes more imagination, more willfulness to be positive than to be defeated. This is not to say I couldn\'t get the piss pummeled out of me and lose all hope, but I think I\'d bounce back eventually. There\'s just nothing else to do, and moping doesn\'t make a lot of sense.</p>\r\n<p>Look out Manhattan, I\'m coming back to see your dirt and your crying masses and your \'tini-toting yuppies in their tailored clothes. Maybe I won\'t be homesick for you this time, because I\'ve fallen in love with California, too. But I\'m an open-minded kind of guy. You can be my East-Coast mistress.</p>'),(1595,'<p>a windless afternoon</p>\r\n<p>my daughter tried to cheat at the game of Life to get the biggest house</p>\r\n<p>my wife is out buying panty hose at the neighborhood drug store</p>\r\n<p>i have five minutes to read the dead black worms at Swinney.org</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>John calls. He\'s not sure if the chapter he told me to read is the one we\'re going to discuss tonight. (at a book discussion)</p>\r\n<p>I say \"Hey, I\'m not expecting anything to go the way I think it will.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"That\'s right,\" he says, \"We\'ll take it whichever way it comes.\"\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>My daughter is running around shooting little cartoon disks out of some kind of gun</p>\r\n<p>I am worried about her. Is she doing the kind of thing kids are supposed to do during summer vacation?</P>\r\n<p>I want her to have maximum fun during her free time. I want to have maximum fun. That\'s why I\'m here, right?</p>\r\n<p>So here I am trying to be honest and tell the truth and be raw and crucial.</p>\r\n<p> Meanwhile those closest to me go without the touch of my typing fingers</p>\r\n<p>But they don\'t like to have their buttons pushed</p>\r\n<p>I just want them, like me, to be free to spin out useless stories like disks from a toy gun</P>\r\n<p>I want them and you and me to be free to get the chapter wrong</p>\r\n<p>And I don\'t just want to read the wrong chapter. I want to write it.</p>\r\n<p>And I think I just did</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>My friend Steve once said that I needed to keep writing because it was \"like bathing\".</p>\r\n<p> Off the words go down the drain </p>\r\n<p> And he said \"Don\'t think about what you write anymore after you write it\".</p>\r\n<p>\"Just let it travel on out past the drain.\"\r\n<p>And so I won\'t. And so I will.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1596,'<br>\r\nI haven\'t been sleeping well lately.  I\'m not in my girlfriend\'s place anymore, and it could be due to that.  Again, it could be due to any of the deviances I\'ve been indulging in, things not a part of my usual repertoire.  I managed to find myself a small place in a nice doorman-brass-and-marble-foyer neighborhood 16 blocks away.  Less than a mile; far enough, close enough, and I get alot of night-time walking in.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTonight I was walking home.  West, walking three avenues over; south, 13 blocks.  It was drizzling as it has been all week, giving the city streets an eerie, glistening appeal, steamy and slick like fresh sex, rancid and black like love gone wrong; the composition of nightmares.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI watch the traffic lights play over the drooling pavement, the greens yellows and reds melting into  a dirty glow beneath my feet.  This is my home.  Control boxes click as I pass, turning yellow overhead beams to red, glowing down.  I see the red diffuse across the intersection on West End, like blood running through the gutters to the clogged rain drain and eventually into the Hudson River<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTreetop says \"I like it, you should write it\", so I sit here to do just that.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAs I said, my nights have been interesting- hours of black-out sleep interspersed with the waking jolt of a night terror, a subconscious belch bubbled up into a waking moment.  I dream of plane-crashes and floods, searing deserts that meld into steamy deserted jungles devoid of sustenance.  I dream of chemical burns and scorched lungs, threadbare arteries and falling.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSeven nights from the past two weeks I\'ve had an old dream reappear from my childhood.  Seven; a number impregnated with possibilities, luck, and fate.  Like an old friend, it slipped back into my sleepy nights, this odd recurring dream; playing itself out, identically, night after night after night, as if trying to tell me something, what it is, I can only guess.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe setting is a twilight moment in some forgotten depression-era dust-bowl town.  Irrevocable family-photo images echo my waking visions into my dream; family homesteads in Louisiana, Arkansas, Missouri, Tennessee, small towns, villes with names no one here would recognize.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'m somewhere in the plains-states in my dreamscape. All around me and far into the distance, the topsoil has eroded away from the farmlands leaving barren fields at the aorta of America\'s heartland, brown dirt billowing up in thermal tufts, summer mirage freshettes.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI ride into the scene in third person, pedaling an old rusty bicycle; you know, the old style ones with leather springy seats and pneumatic tires.  I glide into the main town square, passing a giant rusty water-tower on my left, looming as the day becomes night. I enter a dilapidated old general store and purchase their single remaining live plant- a cactus, the Crown-of-Thorns variety which the kindly old counterman tells me will bloom orange in August. I grin because I know at least this plant will grow, despite the drought, despite the empty water-towers and gritty, muddy wells and cisterns.<br> \r\n<br>\r\nThis morning, my good friend explained to me this dream is most likely an artifact from a previous life. Odd. You never know.  I hope someone here has insights.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n'),(1597,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1597_1.jpg\'>\r\n<br><br>\r\nI haven\'t been sleeping well lately.  I\'m not in my girlfriend\'s place anymore, and it could be due to that.  Again, it could be due to any of the deviances I\'ve been indulging in, things not a part of my usual repertoire.  I managed to find myself a small place in a nice doorman-brass-and-marble-foyer neighborhood 16 blocks away.  Less than a mile; far enough, close enough, and I get alot of night-time walking in.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTonight I was walking home.  West, walking three avenues over; south, 13 blocks.  It was drizzling as it has been all week, giving the city streets an eerie, glistening appeal, steamy and slick like fresh sex, rancid and black like love gone wrong; the composition of nightmares.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI watch the traffic lights play over the drooling pavement, the greens yellows and reds melting into  a dirty glow beneath my feet.  This is my home.  Control boxes click as I pass, turning yellow overhead beams to red, glowing down.  I see the red diffuse across the intersection on West End, like blood running through the gutters to the clogged rain drain and eventually into the Hudson River<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTreetop says \"I like it, you should write it\", so I sit here to do just that.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAs I said, my nights have been interesting- hours of black-out sleep interspersed with the waking jolt of a night terror, a subconscious belch bubbled up into a waking moment.  I dream of plane-crashes and floods, searing deserts that meld into steamy deserted jungles devoid of sustenance.  I dream of chemical burns and scorched lungs, threadbare arteries and falling.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSeven nights from the past two weeks I\'ve had an old dream reappear from my childhood.  Seven; a number impregnated with possibilities, luck, and fate.  Like an old friend, it slipped back into my sleepy nights, this odd recurring dream; playing itself out, identically, night after night after night, as if trying to tell me something, what it is, I can only guess.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe setting is a twilight moment in some forgotten depression-era dust-bowl town.  Irrevocable family-photo images echo my waking visions into my dream; family homesteads in Louisiana, Arkansas, Missouri, Tennessee, small towns, villes with names no one here would recognize.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'m somewhere in the plains-states in my dreamscape. All around me and far into the distance, the topsoil has eroded away from the farmlands leaving barren fields at the aorta of America\'s heartland, brown dirt billowing up in thermal tufts, summer mirage freshettes.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI ride into the scene in third person, pedaling an old rusty bicycle; you know, the old style ones with leather springy seats and pneumatic tires.  I glide into the main town square, passing a giant rusty water-tower on my left, looming as the day becomes night. I enter a dilapidated old general store and purchase their single remaining live plant- a cactus, the Crown-of-Thorns variety which the kindly old counterman tells me will bloom orange in August. I grin because I know at least this plant will grow, despite the drought, despite the empty water-towers and gritty, muddy wells and cisterns.<br> \r\n<br>\r\nThis morning, my good friend explained to me this dream is most likely an artifact from a previous life. Odd. You never know.  I hope someone here has insights.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n'),(1598,'<P> I am here, 12 o\'clock pacific time. Just got back from eating out with my significant other who has already fallen asleep. It sucks. I thought we would talk, laugh and be silly. I feel alone. I think I am getting bronchitus. This cough hasnt been getting better...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So I continue my babble. I have friends who think  they will <I> never </I> get laid again. I tell them to wait. They will. Especially when someone is twenty something...you know  they definately have time to get laid. I wonder what is going on at this time. Which people are out, what the music playing sounds like, who loves who and who hates who...It is the same feeling I had when I was a little girl who had a specified bed-time. I would wonder what was happening beyond my window. I would wonder why I am not out there participating.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I want to go out. But everyone else has jobs and responsibilities and everyone else is asleep. I should go for a run. But then my coughing may flare up some more and it is the middle of the night. I mean I am sorta <B> low </B> but I wouldnt wanna die either</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Yeah, so if there is one thing I can promise besides me not doing anything tonight, is that YOU will ALL get laid. The \"YOU\" know who you are...</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1599,'<p><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New, Courier, mono\">Danger slut was against practical \r\n  levels of unhappiness<br>\r\n  She wanted to live like dolphins<br>\r\n  While I was in the middle of <br>\r\n  an interrogation of my feelings. <br>\r\n  Months later I am still haunted<br>\r\n  by the improper history of our written pages. <br>\r\n  My dreams are reloading, trying to destroy words like soul mate.<br>\r\n  I recover this comfort, looking away from her hair, the only vortex my eyes \r\n  need.</font></p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1599_1.jpg\' >'),(1600,'<P>\r\nI\'ve been bored recently.  I\'ve been dreaming up ways to entertain myself.  I decided that the biggest hoot in the world would be to synchroniously switch the entire populations of San Francisco and New York City.  Just imagine.  Actually, it\'d be even better to switch only half of each. Techno-hippies in Times Square trying to find a tofu smoothie, Greasy Guidos clad in black wandering around the Mission District looking for a good steakhouse. Ayyy, are you with me?  Wall Street brokers snagging their Brooks Brothers suits as they traipse off the BART at 16th street for a power-lunch, pony-tailed holistic tie-dyed yoga reiki shamen getting off the 6 at 125th Street, asking a passerby \"Excuse me, man, is this Harlem?\". Shit!  Wouldn\'t that be a hoot?  Just picture those whiny Brooklynites complaining that they can\'t see the Golden Gate Bridge due to al the fog, wondering why they call it the Sunshine State.  Or, some stoner asking NYPD\'s finest how he can find his way to Manhattan. Ha, ha, ha.</P>   \r\n<p>\r\nI can just see the conversations, also:<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n\"Have a good one, dood!\"<BR>\r\n\"FUCK YOU!\"...<BR>\r\n\"Mang, you have some bad vibes in your karma\"<BR>\r\n\"FUCK YOU!\"...<BR>\r\n\"Um, where can i get a vegan burrito?!\"<BR>\r\n\"FUCK YOU!\"...<BR>\r\n\"Giuliani sucks, Mang.\"<BR>\r\n\"No kidding, Dude.  I agree\"<BR>\r\n'),(1601,'<P> I am washing the dishes when I have an epiphany of sorts. I, in the midst of suds and whatnot, realize I must call my childhood friend...one of the very few that I have. So I do it. And it depresses the hell out of me. I guess I just filter what comes to me, but not very well, emotionally that is. I can\'t take away...I always add on and take someone else\'s sadness and adopt it for a bit. It\'s horribly sadistic.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So, then my significant other and I go out to eat. I feel lame-0 all night. I want to talk and tell him volumes, but I can\'t. So I dont. Plus I have this hacking cough. It just wont go away. It shakes and tears apart my lung tissue. That and my right ear drum feels all jingly in a bad way. As if there are extra peices in there that shouldnt be there and werent there before.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Yeah, so I go down on my significant other. In the middle of the night, while he is asleep...of course he wakes up. Then we go back to sleep. Only <I> I </I> cannot go to sleep, I am coughing and weezing and it feels like everyone in the whole goddamn world is asleep so I go online and play Quake. Then I go back to bed.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So three hours later he attempts to seduce me. Right? Do we follow? Only he wants to jump straight to sex...and I personally don\'t want to because I feel like shit from coughing and all...Plus, it just seems very <I> guy-ish</I> and all...So I turn around, opposite him.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"What\'s wrong?\" he asks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Nothing. I didnt want to have sex...\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P> That second line came a few minutes later in all actuality. Soooo, then I decide I need to leave the room. I am terribly neurotic as well as everything else, and at this point I am feeling excruciatingly shitty and decrepit and I just want to crawl into a hole...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \"Go masterbate...\" he mutters.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Actually he didn\'t mutter it. He said it. So then that is the icing on the frosting of the icing on the cake so I get up and go to the bathroom. yeah, so I <I> cry </I> a bit for chrissakes  but oh well right... I go and sleep with my cat...my only goddamn friend in the whole nasty green and blue world it seems...Then he wakes up.<P>\r\n\r\n<P> Now I explained to him WHY I was so upset. And \"upset\" to me seems such a \"grown-up\" relationship word, so I explained to him why I was so PISSED. he didnt get it.So, in silence I go through my CD\'s and he reads and article in SPIN. For about 2 goddamn hours until he needs to go to work.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> He tells me to go to the doctor as he is out the door and I tell him I will <I> and </I> I\'ll go masterbate. That pisses him off. He leaves and I throw the remote at the wall....it is my roomates TV so thank GOD I didnt break the stupid thing. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> See, so I don\'t know WHY I\'m mad...I just am.i don\'t know WHY you didnt just stalk off at the first instance of me being psycho. I don\'t know how you can take my moronitude, I don\'t know why I am so depressed and fucking mad. But I am. So fuck you even though I love you. You piss the HELL out of me, even though I do love you...so fuck getting laid.</P>'),(1602,'most things are gray to me<br>\r\nvagueness undefined.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit has become my life.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nviews in black and white <br>\r\nhave no reverb. no vibration.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nif you could have one thing what would it be?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nto wave the magic wand and make everything perfect again?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshould life be that easy?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwould it be worth living if it was?<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n...<i>clarity</i>...if only for a moment now and then can be ecstasy.<br>'),(1603,'<p>She was on elderly lady glittering like a thousand jewels</p>\r\n<p>someone\'s wise guardian archangel</P>\r\n<p>and I saw her everywhere I looked when I tried to leave the seminar</P>\r\n<p>No longer interested in the casinos, I was on the road to higher things</p>\r\n<p>So I took in the sexless, shining eyes, her silver hair a sprinkler head spraying in the sunlight</p>\r\n<p>Innocently, Innocent She took my innocent hand</p>\r\n<p>and she told me, after closing her eyes to see Inner Truth</P>\r\n<p>\"You are deficient in Potassium and Magnesium\"</p>\r\n<p>So then after seeing her everywhere like she was burned onto my eyeballs (as I\'ve already described)</P>\r\n<p>I figure, this lady who shines must have all the answers WHERE.DO.I.SIGN.UP?</p>\r\n<p>Both of us back to our lands, me to California, she to Louisiana</P>\r\n<p>a few e-mails later and she wants two hundred and twenty dollars</p>\r\n<p>she is sending me a kit and she wants a lock of my hair to go to a lab in Phoenix Arizona where they will tell me what minerals I have going on enough of and which I don\'t have going on enough of  and besideswhich i get 30.PAGES.ABOUT.ME!</p>\r\n<p>(Yes, you read that right)</P>\r\n<p>so i am left to process. processing ... processing ... processing... and write...tap... tap...(bored girl... I hear my daughter slapping the walls in the hallway... why am I wrestling with this and not playing with her?)</P>\r\n<p>Do I blow my dough on Higher Health and Shine Like the Angels?</p>\r\n<p>Or do I hold onto my ongoing monotonously good health and shun the supplements that could make me God\'s Light Bulb like the Silver One who wants my HAIR?</P>\r\n<p>These are the tough problems of our times</P>\r\n<p>So I guess this one\'s for the time capsule.</P>\r\n<p>O Great and Many Great Grandchildren... Do you see how it was?</P>'),(1604,'<p>\r\nthey are sitting on <b>stromstad\'s</b> and <b>poang</b>, resting their feet on <b>pallbo</b>. they are measuring <b>vitabies</b> and <b>krokshult\'s</b>. they pick up <b>lacks</b> and put down <b>nartorp\'s</b>, wincing at the <b>heri</b>. drinking out of <b>gardera</b> and <b>svalka</b> they sit and laugh. they give the crook eye to <b>billy</b>, but look <b>anton</b> up and down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso many hotties in one place. using a <b>dilling</b> i cross paths with some of them more then once. sometimes ill crouch behind a <b>skugga</b> or <b>tranby</b>; using a <b>lysboj</b> as my questionable device.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni found one especially good looking lass lurking among the <b>aneboda</b> and <b>narvik</b>. she smelled good, but i could just not see her getting the <b>billeberga</b>, so i acted as shallow as i could inside and walked on. a fucking billeberga, who did she think she was?!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npicking up a skyar and a lack of my own i left this hottie paradise with fond memories. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nikea: swedish for chick magnet.\r\n</p> \r\n</p>\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\np.s there is a computer desk in the catalogue called <i>the JERKER desk</i>'),(1605,'</head>\r\n<body>\r\n\r\n<center><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Blue sharpie</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Tribal corn\r\nhusks</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">The dizziness\r\nslides to the side</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Someone asks\r\nfor a snakebite</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">New world\r\nbetween Spain and India</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">The cloud\r\ntranscends</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Gemitlichkeit</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">It\'s all you\r\nneed</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Its all in\r\nyour head</font></font>\r\n<br><font face=\"Times New Roman,Times\"><font color=\"#3333FF\">Back to India</font></font><font face=\"Century Gothic\"><font color=\"#FF6600\"><font size=+1></font></font></font>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1605_1.jpg\' height=150 width=200><font face=\"Century Gothic\"><font color=\"#FF6600\"><font size=+1></font></font></font>\r\n<p><b><font face=\"Century Gothic\"><font color=\"#FF6600\"><font size=+1>The\r\nTiger</font></font></font></b><font color=\"#FF6600\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF6600\">I am the delightful paradox.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">All the world is my stage.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">I seek the unattainable</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">and the untried.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">I dance to life\'s music</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">in happy abandon.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">Come with me on my carousel rides.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">See the myriad colors,</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">in the flickering lights.</font>\r\n<br><font color=\"#FF6600\">All hail the unparalleled performer.</font><font color=\"#FF6600\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#FF6600\">I am the tiger.</font><font color=\"#FF6600\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\"#000000\">(insert thought)</font></center>\r\n\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1606,'<p>8/15/01 10:14 pm</p>\r\n<p>There are three hearing impaired guys sitting on the front porch of Mr. Pong\'s.  After asking if his friend could smoke on the porch, the one wearing a hearing aid asked me if I worked for Mr. Pong\'s; a viable question considering I was setting up a hot dog cart in the alley beside the restaurant.</p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"How long have you worked here?\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"Three weeks.\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"Do you like this job?\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"No.\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"Why not?\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p><b><i>\"It\'s no good.\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p>Damn straight it\'s no good.  Visions of database design and dollar signs permeate my thoughts.  I dread the following two weeks.  The drudgery and dullness of this job will be especially difficult to take once I recieve half of the phat pay for this contract that I am going to be working on for the next little while.  After I talk with the owner, I shall go home after work and write a formal letter of resignation, a first for me. I\'ve never quit or been fired from a job before.  I have only left either when my time has run out or for extenuating circumstances; like moving ~300km away for school.</p>\r\n<p>I really can\'t wait to get out of this fucking alley.</p>'),(1607,'I was leisurely reading on this site today and came across the following comment:<BR><BR>\r\n<B>s5:</B> <I>i once masturbated at the ikea in orange county, california. i don\'t remember the circumstances.</I><BR><BR>\r\nThis admission struck me as odd.  Then, however, I got to thinking back, felt guilty for blushing at s5\'s candidness and thought I\'d share some of the more memorable moments of self-pleasure I\'ve experienced, location-wise.<BR><BR>\r\nIn the Sunday-school bathroom in church, growing up<BR>In the back-seat of my grandmother\'s 1974 Buick<BR>In the hammock in my back-yard<BR>on top of a giant boulder in Yellowstone<BR>in my high school bathroom<BR>at work<BR>in the local taco bell\'s bathroom<BR>in front of the tv<BR>in front of the computer<BR>in teh shower at college<br>in my significant other\'s bed while watching talk-shows<BR>in the public library bathroom<BR>at the doctor\'s office<BR>in my significant other\'s pool<BR>in my treehouse<BR><BR>just throught i\'d get that off my chest. i\'d like to see your personal feedback regarding this issue and see what the general norm is regarding this activity.'),(1608,'<P>This love has dried up.</P>\r\n<P>I am sorry to all of those from whom I have attempted to extract a certain measure of joy. You have distanced and criticized, gaped and condescended, held silent grudges. I apologize.</P>\r\n<P>Here, the line is cut free from the dock. I can float away, quietly dipping a healed hand into the waters, feeling. I can see where I am headed, but really, I don\'t want to see it so clearly. From here it looks sparse and bleak and empty. Perhaps I have to populate it with life.</P>\r\n<P>I cannot continue, my heart caged in a loveless struggle. Art seems to be the answer, but it never comes about. Life has gone to seed, and I am stunted. My flavors have mingled and dissolved in you, and I need them back. Thanks.</P>\r\n<P>The pain of realizing you can never know love with me, knowing every day is subtracting from my happiness rather than building on it, aches in my throat. When I try to speak, I can merely croak. My love for you is not great enough to warrant suffering. I long to be held. Why am I not?</P>\r\n<P>I look in your eyes for answers, and only find decimals and dollar signs. I\'m cold, empty, sodden, barren. This is not me. This must end.</P>\r\n'),(1609,'I\'m at work right now. I work in marketing. I work in a corner. I work for an established firm. I\'m not working for me, am I? Hence the need for independence-- the opportunity to live it up for myself and myself alone. Responsibility free. Well that\'s where I lead you into my plans for the latter year... I am attending a University in Sussex England for the year. Officially to study abroad, unofficial to study the broads. Disappointment in American women has certainly put me into tilt. Disappointment on what level? lack of what i need. intelligence/opinionated, playful, compassionate and understanding. What I\'m seeking is an independent girl who has got her wits about her, that can work with me while I work for me. I found one such girl in America however uncompassionate and her birthday was yesterday. We were supposed to go to Disneyland, however what happened was a tumult that ended up in our friendship shattering like glass. It feels soo awkward because for a friendship it is way too comparable to a relationship. Why is that bad? Because she broke my heart in a big way. She thinks she is the only one with feelings, well I have them too. Yeah so if I\'m ranting I know you guys out there that hear me all too well and you girls-- realize that us guys have feelings too. I dealt with it by realizing how it didnt hurt her, so i would not let it hurt me. However I am more in love with her than Ive ever been with anyone and I\'m about to let go to go to England. It is the most precious declaration I have-- I Love You. I am curious to learn what love means in Europe, of course some of u might know and see this idealism in me that begs to be shot at however hear me out when I say that you must take all I say with a grain of salt and perhaps some sugar. Hell, go ahead and break out the weed. \r\nBecause it\'s feeling like the longest summer ever. And not in that great way. Heartbreak and so I turn to my family. A difficulty in opening up to them why? because I dont want to show them anything less than what I wish to choreograph into excellence however I am unexcellent and we all have that issue. SO what is love? unconditional that is-- is it excellent? unexcellent? inherently perfect? inherently imperfect? because you\'ve got me there. \r\nTo be the perfect guy-- what is that? thats where I\'m getting into. Ive always tried to approximate what i think he ought to be. My best friend while we were attempting to form a relationship -- manages this way of attempting to turn me into what she considers the perfect guy, my parents always say i dont have enough common sense, the girls I date think they think im great but what does that mean in the grand scheme cuz they dont really know me? we\'re talking idealism and realism. and i have to use the word sex once because -- well, gotta love me.\r\nFuck this shit. Bring on the hoes? or not?\r\nCuz I dunno what hope there is. OR rather I dunno who I am or ought to be(lieve in)?'),(1610,'<p>\r\ntomorrow, my girlfriend and i will leave on an adventure. two days down I-5, driving through the heat and the flatness. two days of driving to get to the start of our adventure.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow, is the pre adventure. burning data discs full of mp3s. making shopping lists. find coolers and clothes and bags. making sure i get the oil changed. feeling bad cause i wanted to have my project at work done before i left, but instead, it will have to wait until i get back. half beautiful, half broken.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nan adventure that begins in la. or the out skirts of la. water and thrills and g-forces. up to the out skirts of san francisco. more water, mammals, wonder. up to the cresent city. redwoods - ocean - awe.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat a time when i am disappointed with getting older and growing up, remembering i am still young is the best remedy.\r\n</p>'),(1611,'<P>\r\nhm.  yeah, i feel better now.  dating nycag was an effort that i never fully made.  i did not deserve the benefits i got.  in america, you don\'t get benefits for working part time.  the background checks and references were just subterfuge; hiding behind protocol for a few months for a little bedspring action.  \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\na paper shredder is not necessary.  non-disclosure agreements were substituted with a hand shake.  i will assist her in mass-emailing her invites, and i will attend her show.  this will not be going through the motions, but how it should be.  how it was meant to be.   \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s nice to explore the possibilities of a relationship without all the usually ensuing drama and pathos.  it was like the AYSO after-game handshake.  \"Good game,\" \"Good game,\" \"Good game,\" \"Good game,\" on down the line...  and now off to orange wedges and Hi-C and some nintendo on a saturday afternoon.\r\n</P>  \r\n'),(1612,'<p>today</p>\r\n<p>so someone says \\\'did you know...she is dead\\\'</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>the news through the grapevine, i am just one of the unknown<br>\r\nhearing it for the first time, after the fact</p>\r\n<p>this can\\\'t really be happening i think</p>\r\n<p>horrible gossip</p>\r\n<p>she was there a year ago...sitting in my house<br>\r\nour house</p>\r\n<p>but i guess i left that when i walked away</p>\r\n<p>i couldn\\\'t talk to her after that<br>\r\nwe had never been close</p>\r\n<p>but at least</p>\r\n<p>she took me in<br>\r\nshe gave me what she could<br>\r\nfor the time i was there</p>\r\n<p>does it matter that he was the one that brought us together?<br>\r\nheld us close</p>\r\n<p>she gave me what she could</p>\r\n<p>i think we know more than we\\\'re able to voice<br>\r\nor grasp</p>\r\n<p>this last month</p>\r\n<p>i have felt so empty<br>\r\nmy petty struggles with life, so pointless<br>\r\nwhile she had to have known her last days<br>\r\nwere near</p>\r\n<p>and i knew nothing</p>\r\n<p>...lost in this song</p>\r\n<p><i>When the flood calls,<br>\r\nYou have no home, you have no walls<br>\r\nIn the thunder crash<br>\r\nYou\\\'re a thousand minds, within a flash<br>\r\nDon\\\'t be afraid to cry at what you see<br>\r\nThe actors gone, there\\\'s only you and me<br>\r\nAnd if we break before the dawn,&nbsp;<br>\r\nthey\\\'ll use up what we used to be.</i></p>\r\n<p>it amazes me listening to it....i cannot even breath</p>\r\n<p>and it spoke to me of letting go<br>\r\nor mortality, and endings</p>\r\n<p>and here is death on my doorstep</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>do you think she\\\'d forgive me?</p>\r\n<p>that i shut her out...that i did to her what i felt others were doing to me<br>\r\nthe thing i hate the most</p>\r\n<p>abandoned</p>\r\n<p>how stupid and insignificant<br>\r\nmy reasons</p>\r\n<p>avoiding her out of fear<br>\r\nand embarrassment</p>\r\n<p>not wanting to swallow whatever medicine she might have to serve<br>\r\nafraid</p>\r\n<p>i could have learned something<br>\r\ni could have said goodbye</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>life goes on</p>\r\n<p>i race down the freeway<br>\r\ndoing 80 or more<br>\r\nwindows open<br>\r\nmusic blaring</p>\r\n<p>hoping the roaring will push the thoughts from my head<br>\r\nthe angry voice in my heart</p>\r\n<p>but i can\\\'t ignore the gnawing<br>\r\nin the pit of my stomach<br>\r\nthe bottom of my soul...</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>pride is an evil bitch that will kick your ass every time<br>\r\nand leave you with nothing...but self loathing.</p>'),(1613,'<P>So there are like only two guys that I would ever be with I have decided. My ex, Benjamin and my current boyfriend, Michael. Why? Well, they know me and I don\\\'t think I am patient enough to ever give anyone else a chance.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Last night I went to the local pub/eatery with my friend Jeff. Also, a mutual friend of mine and Ben\\\'s. So, he calls Ben up on his cell phone after I beg him, and Ben shows up. The last time I saw this guy he was throwing my underwear at me while I was in a moving car and leaving his house for being a jerk-off. Also, he had screamed out to me one day as I was leaving Jeff\\\'s house, \\\" Be sure not get AIDS!\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Do we sense a bit of tension?...um, yeah. I tried to make it work and so did he and it just couldn\\\'t. His overbearing best friend Marlene hated me...but that is one of the many details. Sitting there accross from him, I realize how painful separations are. Because I still love him. Not for how we were, although there are many great memories attached to our togetherness...but also cuz it was just a great carefree moment in living of mine. He stared too much that night. He tried playing footsies. He tried getting \\\"fresh\\\" one might say. But Ben can\\\'t be labeled a perve. As one of my past loves...my first true relationship, he isn\\\'t a perve. He is just Ben.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I just hope he finds a woman and moves on. Then we can really begin to be friends.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Sitting there I thought was is so special about my relationship now, that will keep it from ending the way Ben and I did? And what was so horrible about Ben and I that we didn\\\'t work?</P> \r\n\r\n<P>A few pitchers later and half a pack of malboro lights later I open the door to my apartment. Jeff had forbidden Ben to come up after dropping me off. A demand stirring only from the lips of a good friend. I say bye...call me...knowing he won\\\'t and we hug, he kisses my cheek and I get out of the truck...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>So on the second floor of my complex, in apt number 54 I am alone. Tina is asleep. Jen gone and I worry over the recent quarrel my \\\"significant\\\" one and I just had. Will we be a casualty? Because honestly if this doesn\\\'t work it is hermitsville for me. I become too involved, too in love, too addicted...Opening the door to my bedroom is M asleep on my bed. He\\\'d come to see me. And I know.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Benjamin, I will never forget riding bikes at 3 am, making love in public between the dorms and the health center, throwing bikes into the pool, our dinner parties, your punk music...everything including the mole on the back of your <I> left </I> ear, everything I will love and cherish and hopefully we will be friends one day. With no tension. No nothing but care.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And M you kick ass for letting me blabbber on while you lie unconscious in bed about my ex. About our past, how I miss him and you aren\\\'t insecure and I admire the concrete knowing you have of us both and our hot slurpee summers, listening to talk radio in the car and you imitating Ross Perot and saying my German accent sucks in your crappy German accent, and complimenting my Jewish <I> muth-uh </I> accent.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>A toast to boys, past and present.</P>'),(1614,'man i cant even cook toast without burning it\r\n\r\nwe will see if i can do this deli thing\r\n\r\nif not its back to doing odd painting jobs\r\n\r\nme and my ephysema cough\r\n\r\nhacking over their deli shit\r\n\r\nwoah...i hope i dont suck at the food server \r\nthing'),(1615,'<p>Autumn is coming. Finally. My season.</p>\r\n<p>I was thinking about studying in South Africa this winter.</p>\r\n<p>The desire for travel fades. It was in me for so long, I have gone nowhere, but it seems like an increasingly empty distraction. One of those things people make a huge fuss about, you know, it\\\'s simply what all young people do if they can, I never bothered asking myself why -- various cliches would boil up in response if I had -- maybe all the shit I\\\'ve had thrown in my face has ruined it for me, all the hollow appeals to exoticism, the tourist\\\'s inane appreciation for sight and sound, why bother? Should I bother? Should I waste my time to become that?</p>\r\n<p>I think what I\\\'ve wanted all along is a change, a pretty radical change, in <i>lifestyle</i> and just assumed that it would also require a change of scenery, too. But romping around KwaZulu-Natal or Foreignlandia X won\\\'t do that if I\\\'m still connected with \\\'The System\\\', as it were, I mean, why bother? Should I bother?</p>\r\n<p>There is so much work to do here. An urgency in the research and the meetings. More groups, more meetings, more material. Maybe my desire to travel has decreased because I\\\'m actually more than vaguely interested in what I\\\'m working on. I feel like everything is coming together. There is some kind of freaky merging going on. The work isn\\\'t <i>just</i> interesting anymore; it\\\'s starting to completely merge with things I\\\'ve always kept relatively private. (I even had a dream about the chairman of my old department the other night). Everything I\\\'ve been working on in private, all of my drives to do anything at all, might be directly related with how some of the research is connected. But I\\\'m not sure yet.</p>\r\n<p>Do I put it on hold? Can I put it on hold? This might be my first and last chance to get out of here and run around some blistering-hot fly-infested wunderland. But should I bother? I just don\\\'t know.</p>\r\n<p>Oh well. I have a month and a half to decide.</p>'),(1616,'<p>Danger Slut\'s verdict: We \r\n  were Symbiotic. The end.<br>\r\n  Her autopsy of us did not reveal each other\'s name<br>\r\n  tattooed outside our hearts like I predicted.<br>\r\n  She grew tired because I would not give up the <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/SunsetStrip/Palms/3625/mirrormo.htm#theghost\">ghost</a> \r\n  <br>\r\n  of this relationship. At a moment\'s notice <br>\r\n  I could be possessed and taken <br>\r\n  back to us making love to <a href=\"http://www.massiveattack.com/\">massive attack</a>\'s \r\n  \"Angel\".<br>\r\n  God Bless being in love with an atheist.</font></p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://members.tripod.com/blackcatsmoon/images/picturesection/x006_small.gif\'>'),(1617,'<p>She called.  By that point in the week, I had kind of given up on her calling, but she called, and she told me to come.  \\\'Why?\\\' I asked.  \\\'I have to work.  I have to sleep.  I\\\'ll call you back.\\\' I said.</p><p>If he hadn\\\'t been sitting right next to me... if he hadn\\\'t been there at all, I would have been asleep by then, but he told me to go.  He said I should go.  My roommate said I should go, so I came.</p><p>Now, here I sit on the huge piece of cardboard we scored in the garbage.  It\\\'s more comfortable than raw cobblestones.  We were in the park, on the grass, laying on a blanket, smoking a cigarette.  We weren\\\'t there long though; the police told us to leave, so we gathered in a semi-orderly line and marched out en masse.  Exiled to the pavement for five hours.</p><p>\\\'Hey, where\\\'s the coke?\\\'  It gets passed around leisurely but unobtrusively.  (It\\\'s New York City, but there\\\'s still cops around and Guiliani is still mayor.)  The obvious topic for conversation sets the mood, the pace.  Something I have no experience with; I have little to contribute.  I want to contribute; I don\\\'t want to just observe, so I find someone else, someone who talks about Prague and Budapest, someone who offers me herbal tea, someone to speak German with, someone who thinks Roberto Benini is a total ass.  We talk about movies and other banalities.  It\\\'s light and unforced.  It\\\'s 2am just outside Central Park.</p><p>Her and her friend (his name is Jodi, \\\"like the actress\\\"), we play cards, a variation of a variation of poker, something Jodi learned growing up in Indonesia.  I\\\'m the big winner.  The someone else is the big loser.  <i>Lucky in cards, unlucky in love.  Unlucky in cards, lucky in love.</i>  She talks about Prince Charming.  She can see him walking down the street towards her.</p><p>I\\\'m no longer pissed about being here, letting myself do this.  I should be asleep, but I don\\\'t want to leave.  When am I next going to be invited to hang out with a posse of strangers in Central Park in the middle of the night?  I no longer think I made the wrong choice.  I remember that I\\\'m not supposed to be a responsible adult.  In the morning, I\\\'m going to wake up having had only a couple hours sleep and seriously consider calling in sick.  Then I\\\'ll remind myself that I\\\'m <b>young</b>, right?  I can deal with this.  I\\\'m just being a whiny pussy.  I\\\'ll go to work and drink crappy espresso just for the caffeine.</p><p>I have to pee.  It\\\'s 4am.  That\\\'s as good an excuse as any to convince myself to go home, get at least a little sleep.  I walk towards the subway and see a cab on my way there.</p>'),(1618,'<p>Evergreen, Everfolding</p>\r\n<p>like a season of glass</p>\r\n<p>when I look through at you</p>\r\n<p>the wind blows in my eyes</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>I want to blink</p>\r\n<p>I want to stare</p>\r\n<p>seeing you over there</p>\r\n<p>stretching the fragrant petals of your womanliness</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Catch a call from me</p>\r\n<p>Catch a whisper</p>\r\n<p>Open the doors for me</p>\r\n<p>emerald and jasper</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>You called me from the window</p>\r\n<p>Let me come up your hidden staircase</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ll bring you flowers</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Jest no more</p>\r\n<p>Hold my fingers, and take my hand</p>\r\n<p>while I stroke your hair</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Everdark, Neverflowing</p>'),(1619,'<p>Evergreen, Everfolding</p>\r\n<p>like a season of glass</p>\r\n<p>when I look through at you</p>\r\n<p>The wind blows in my eyes</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>I want to blink</p>\r\n<p>I want to stare</p>\r\n<p>Seeing you over there</p>\r\n<p>Stretching the fragrant petals of your womanliness</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Catch a call from me</p>\r\n<p>Catch my whispers</p>\r\n<p>Open the doors for me</p>\r\n<p>emerald and jasper</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>You called me from the window</p>\r\n<p>let me come up your hidden staircase</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ll bring you flowers</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Jest no more</p>\r\n<p>Hold my fingers, and take my hand</p>\r\n<p>Wonder at the starlight</p>\r\n<p>while I stroke your hair</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Everdark, Neverflowing</p>'),(1620,'<p>Evergreen, Everfolding</p>\r\n<p>like a season of glass</p>\r\n<p>when I look through at you</p>\r\n<p>The wind blows in my eyes</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>I want to blink</p>\r\n<p>I want to stare</p>\r\n<p>Seeing you over there</p>\r\n<p>Stretching the fragrant petals of your womanliness</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Catch a call from me</p>\r\n<p>Catch my whispers</p>\r\n<p>Open the doors for me</p>\r\n<p>emerald and jasper</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>You called me from the window</p>\r\n<p>let me come up your hidden staircase</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ll bring you flowers</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Jest no more</p>\r\n<p>Hold my fingers, and take my hand</p>\r\n<p>Wonder at the starlight</p>\r\n<p>while I stroke your hair</p>\r\n<p>***</p>\r\n<p>Everdark, Neverflowing</p>'),(1621,'yeti bought me a present and it came in the mail yesterday and i was very happy and delighted and surprised, and all the best things you should be when you get a present.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut more than that, i could visualize my dear boy shopping for the present, with worry and indecision and angst about whether i would like it.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni love my present.  it is perfect, and couldn\\\'t have been more perfect if i had been watching him a webcam in the store and giving him icb directions -- \\\"no not that one, the one on the left\\\"....<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe present is a pin from italy and i will wear it many hundreds of days in my life if i get that many more and when i wear it, every time, i will think of my yeti boy and the day we went on an adventure to oakland together and how lovely he is and look forward to the next time when i can see him again.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthank you dear boy, i will treasure it for always<br><br>.\r\n'),(1622,'<P>i used to have this friend. she was super nice and we got along really well.  sometimes i would get annoyed with her, and sometimes she would get annoyed with me.  we could go for weeks without speaking or emailing, but i read her online journal, and she read mine; we kept up on each other, and it was never really awkward when we\\\'d finally get together.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>one day we\\\'d made plans and she didn\\\'t show up.  i\\\'m pretty sure that only happened once or twice, but in my head it happened ten or fifteen.  it made me angry the first time, and annoyed the second. it wasn\\\'t like she didn\\\'t have a way to contact me--she had my phone number, she had my email address.  we didn\\\'t talk for a while, and neither of us really cared.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>but i got over it, although sometimes i still made fun of her for standing me up, pretended i cared more than i did.  i mean--who cares, right?  she promised not to do it again, she showed herself to be reliable, she regained my trust.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>she\\\'s two hours and 14 minutes late.  of course, she emailed me and told me she would show up around three-thirty, and that was the last i heard from her.  i\\\'m not really angry that she didn\\\'t show up--we were meeting at my house, so it\\\'s not like i\\\'m left standing in the rain somewhere--but i\\\'m angry that now i have to worry about her until she thinks to call or email and tell me that she forgot, tell me that she was sidetracked, tell me that the train from new jersey stalled in the middle of the tracks for six hours.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>but come on. what could the excuse be, that she couldn\\\'t call, couldn\\\'t email?  i\\\'m suspicious.</p>'),(1623,'<P> When we turn 18, we can buy cigarettes. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> When we turn 21, we can drink. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> When we turn 25, our insurance goes down. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> When we get old, it goes back up.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> We think we have control, but we don\\\'t. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Today was my last day with my first REAL job.  I have two days of \\\'independance.\\\'  I\\\'d like to think of it as that because on Sunday night I won\\\'t dread going to work.  I\\\'ll look forward to a new beginning, for whatever that\\\'s worth. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I went to my first legal happy hour and was carded.  A former co-worker bought me a drink.  I\\\'m part of the crowd.  I hate the crowd. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I stopped by the liquor store for my first bottle of cheap wine and (at high recommendation) a 6pack of mike\\\'s hard lemonade.  The guy looked at my license, asked me my birthday, demanded a second photo id, and then wanted to know what color my eyes were.  He finally let me buy my $4.99 bottle of wine. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I came home and decided that I was going to drink just a little bit.  There is a strange vapor when I open the bottle and the flavor just sort of explodes in my mouth.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I\\\'m not drunk, and I\\\'m not even near buzzed. </P> \r\n\r\n<P> I\\\'m free, and I\\\'m too damn tired to care. </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1624,'<br>\r\nI want to know what I want.  What I want right now is to not be sitting in Jersey chasing down something I can\\\'t remember, reminding some girl why we just don\\\'t click anymore; what a wasted night. I want to be walking the familiar streets of Manhattan, alone.  I\\\'m not so frightened of being alone anymore.  I want to take to the dew-condensed avenues, even though I was doing just that 6 hours ago, looking forward to the weekend change of pace in the suburbs, at the shore.<br><br> This morning, walking towards the West Side at 5:30am, sleepless, dreamless, nose burning, bladder full, eyes swollen, mind full of snippets of conversation, facial expressions, night time eyes, I decided I should trod the streets more often in this state, stone sober, at an hour when no one else is out except the drunks, addicts and garbage men.<br><br>\r\nI want to spend more time sitting on uptown rooftops looking at the city below me, walking at midnight though unfamiliar Harlem locales, drinking with friends and reminiscing on the moment, recreating the patchwork stories of my past for increasinglty familiar acquaintences.  I want to gaze out over Manhattan and redream the reasons why I ended up here, and smile, enjoying my circumstsances, reminding myself that most everything ends up having some purpose, sometimes even one for the better.<br><br>\r\nI want to spend more nights outside, I\\\'ve discovered.  I want to stay up all night sitting on the street corner talking, chain-smoking, smiling when someone brings a box to sit on, a crate, an old moldy persian rug, a six-pack, thinking, and most of all listening and observing.  I never knew I\\\'d  like that sort of thing so much.  I never saw myself as being a very good listener until recently.<br><br>\r\nI want to keep connecting with people and being thrilled by good humanity; the more I let down my guard, the more worth my life contains, and I love it.  I relish dissolving ulterior motives and undercurrent thoughts, staring out into the night while someone I\\\'ve only just met talks about things, real things, interesting things, silly things.<br><br>\r\nI do not want to be in New Jersey tonight.'),(1625,'<p>\r\nlet me speak to you as best i can in this situation. let me look you in the eye and tell you the truth about what is going on with us right now. let me be there to try to explain what it is i want to ask you. will you not do this for me? will you not turn the tv off and listen to me. press mute. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim in a catagory thats hard to get out of. a folder much thinner then the others. im in the bottom drawer of the filing cabinet. its got a mock wood finish ontop. mock enough that it needs to be dusted. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe lock had been drilled out, the keys lost many months prior. if one wasnt careful they might get cut. the bottom drawer had many folders in it. they bore the names of people. they were dated month-day-year. they were not cronological or alphabetical. in addition to not being in any clear order, not all folders rested in their hooked filing cabinet folders. some of them were lounging on the floor of the drawer. piled up like tires in a yard; haphazzard and careless. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni notice you paying more attention to the dj\\\'s battling in the gap commercial. i feel useless when you are around me. you dont know how to act around me. its not that i dont know how to act around you. i respect you. you dont respect me. i cant beleive he told you that i stole. i cant beleive you questioned me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbaskets piling up on my desk, i know im not going to be living this life for that much longer. not life as in death. life as in the way im living right now and have been living for the past couple of years. who am i talking to now? id rather not think about the exact individuals. id rather make up vast apolagies and answers. id rather speak to the masses than speak to the person. broadly speaking i dont like the person, i like the people. I dont like the kid, i like youth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am inundated by moshpits and titties wherever i go. it is unbeleivable how many times in a day i see titties and moshpits. it is really weird. when i speak of titties, they dont have to be uncovered. most of the tit i see is covered. contoured. then i see mosh pits. i see mosh pits. maybe not a bunch of hooligans bashing together. but i see complete individualistic chaos. i see it in the office. i see it trying to turn left at an intersection. i see it when im at a sports game. i see it when im at the mall. i dont see drones. i see people doing dramatically different things on a very large scale, or at completely different poles. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni once thought i was being chased by the cia. i was releived to find out it was only revenue canada. i was releived because money i dont have. they can garnish my wages. im unemployed. if the cia was chasing me, it would be because they wanted information. being unemployed doesnt help you when someone wants information. it just means that, that many less people will notice you being gone. but gone where? i shudder to think. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim a loser but i dont want to die. that fact alone to me proves to me as an utterly unique individual that music does not make people kill people. music just adds to a situation much bigger. all the biggest killers out there had the fewest of problems. the people with lots and lots of problems dont have the time to kill people. enough time maybe to commit suicide. power to the individual. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthank you for listening. i knew you were lying when you said the mute button didnt work.\r\n</p> '),(1626,'<P>she was a certain way today</p> \r\n<P>eyes shiny lips closed</p>\r\n\r\n<P>casually in all ten fingers</p>\r\n<P>laughter gay and</p>\r\n<P>small lips rosed</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i catch a slant of light my way and</p>\r\n<P>turn from the beam she seems</p>\r\n<P>different and changed and closed</p>\r\n\r\n<P>do not want to seem opposed</p>\r\n<P>to her new boyfriend, new clothes</p>\r\n<P>new happiness, new foes</p>\r\n\r\n<P>we, good friends and it seems our era goes</p>\r\n<P>having different lovers now.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>having differnet friends.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it is her friend.s (not.mine) going \\\"away\\\" parti</p>\r\n<P>see if i care and see if i go</p>\r\n\r\n<P>she once thought I used you </p>\r\n<P>wrong was she. sometimes one knows</p>\r\n<P>someone so deep, no asking is needed</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and this is for she</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1627,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1627_1.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n'),(1628,''),(1629,''),(1630,'<P> My cousin had gone to totalaccess.com and signed up to be a street teamster (something like that) for the xgames.  They mailed her a shitload of cd\\\'s, beef jerky, and postcards to hand out.  (Well, the postcards should have been handed out well before today because they had scratch offs for show on ABC tonight, so we left those home.)</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Anyway, she waited until last night to tell me her plans, and tonight we headed towards south street with a bookbag full of cd\\\'s...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> We each took a handful and tried to scope out people who looked like they cared about the xgames. (This was hard for me; I technically didn\\\'t care.)  Most people accepted the free gifts but it was interesting to watch how people moved their hands away when they saw us coming; and here we thought we were being selective. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> We were down to about 20 cd\\\'s when we remembered that we had to have a picture or two of Janet trying to unload this stuff.  She needs it to send in to try to win a prize of some sort.  This was tough. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"My face is copyrighted.\\\"  I told him to hold the cd in front of his face. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> After we finished with the cd\\\'s we headed back towards 8th.  Janet needed to sit so I went in and bought water and left her at a table on the sidewalk next to a guy trying to sell roses to the people passing by. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Some of these guys were real assholes about it.  \\\"she doesn\\\'t deserve it.\\\"  \\\"Let her buy it herself.\\\"  Most of these girls really did react as though they were shocked.  We had fun pointing out which guys aren\\\'t getting laid tonight. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> The man selling the roses watched a girl pass by and told me that he wanted to go out with her.  \\\"Go introduce me,\\\" he says. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"I don\\\'t even know your name. I can\\\'t introduce you to anyone.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Good point. Mark.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> We sang along. \\\"Girls just wanna have funnnn!\\\"  \\\"Walking on sunshine..ohhh ohhh.\\\" \\\"You may be right, I may be crazy...\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> He holds a rose out to the couple walking past. \\\"A rose for your rose?\\\"  </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"No, thanks.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I piped in, \\\"She\\\'s not worth a rose?\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> The guy turns around, \\\"I don\\\'t need a rose, she\\\'s a diamond.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Damn.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Wimp,\\\" says Mark. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Yea, but it was a good comeback.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Well, I put him on the spot once, but you put him on the spot a second time. He had to think of something good.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Ohhh. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"This\\\", he says, \\\"is observational research.  Some guys don\\\'t even know what they have standing next to them.  Some of those girls don\\\'t realize it either.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Rose for your rose?\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> The girl responded, \\\"No thanks.\\\" She was sorta bitchy about it. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"See? I dunno who\\\'s wearing the pants in that relationship.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Hah! Obviously not him. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> A little boy with his mom walked by. \\\"Hey, why don\\\'t you buy a rose for your mom?\\\" I asked.  He just looked at me.  Mark holds out a rose and tells him to just take it for her, free.  He says it was just something about knowing that boy didn\\\'t have the money but his hesitation was enough. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> His mom turned up her nose. She couldn\\\'t have cared less. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> It was interesting to watch reactions.  I bought a rose from Mark right before we left. \\\"Good luck.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> He thanked us for hanging around.. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I\\\'m not sure how to turn this into a story with a point. I\\\'m not saying you have to go out and spend money to keep your girl, it was just strange. Guys, at least once, do something for no reason if you haven\\\'t already. (Hell, if you\\\'re in Philly, see if Mark is still sitting outside the South Caffe.) Don\\\'t ask her if she wants the flower ; you\\\'ll embarass her. Just do it. The flower is not the point, the gesture is.  </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1631,'<p>For Funny<br>\r\nInteresting<br>\r\nSmart Conversation<br>\r\nemail me at<br>\r\nfistballer@aol.com</p>'),(1632,'trying to subit images of the sphnix'),(1633,'hi hi test images'),(1634,'<p>I\'m not sleeping well.  I arrived home from work this morning at 6:00am to find a drunken message left for me on ICQ from a friend.  This put me in a good mood after a rather unappealing night at work.</p> \r\n<p>When I attempted slumber, all I got was four hours of lying awake, writhing in anger at my mind for running overclocked processing on multiple scenarios.</p> \r\n<p><b>P implies Q.</b></p>\r\n<p>Eight hours and one hour nap later, it\'s time to go and do it all over again.</p> \r\n<p>Not eating well.<br>Not sleeping well.<br>Not thinking well.<br>I thought I was getting better.</p> \r\n<p>I thought wrong.  Now, it would seem, my body is wasting itself and my mind is killing me for an entirely different set of reasons.</p>'),(1635,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1635_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1635_2.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'><B>Bmg</B>: wake up fatass.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: wake the fuck up!<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: Leave me alone, I\'m hungover. I\'m depressed. I should sleep.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: As I expected.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: Get your lazy ass out of bed and go run, read a book afterwards.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: Oh, okay, shit.<BR>\r\n(I toss and turn for a while in bed)<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: But I have a headache, and I was up late watching a bad elron movie, drinking... <BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: sloth.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: You should be ashamed of yourself. Take a shower, you stink.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: it is you that stinks.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: you look ridiculous, lying there in bed, daydreaming about your miserable life.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: It is called suffering, you selfish bastard, leave me to my suffering. Let me sleep.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: you can sleep for the rest of your life. Go ahead, drink some more beer, dream about that girl you\'ll never have. Do nothing, be nothing.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: go bother someone else.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: what are you going to do with yourself?<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: oh, lemme guess... you are gonna sleep till noon, wake up, drink bad coffee, eat fat tacos, nap, pop some asprin, talk to k-dogg about how un-happy you are with you life, eat some more, nap again, shower, get a drink, daydream about romance, drink some more...you are so fucking predictable.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: right, that\'s what I am gonna do, fuck you. There is nothing in the world that interests me.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: get a haircut you dirty bastard. <BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: I got one yesterday, thank you for noticing.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: yah, it looks stupid on you. Get another haircut.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: I like it, I think I look cool.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: you look like a little boy.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: I am a little boy.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: go roll around in shit, you are fucking pathetic.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: look at you, what are you doing?<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: ME! What am I doing? I am trying to save your helpless life, I am your last hope. I am here to see to it that you live. I am here to wake your ass up. <BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: but life is so miserable right now.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: shut the fuck up.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: I won\'t.<BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: Why do you have to be so mean to me?<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: Cause I am you.<BR>\r\n<B>Bmg</B>: Now wake the fuck up, hit the track, do some sit-ups, and wash your filthy ass. <BR>\r\n<B>Me</B>: Owe, okay...</P>'),(1636,'<p>I dream with bloody eyes. I think it is because creativity is gone and I won\\\'t \r\n  feel it again until my daughter is born.<br>\r\n  This symphony of anxiety pushes me along. It is too late now for the <a href=\\\"http://www.inthe80s.com/movies/brightlightsbigcity.shtml\\\">Bright \r\n  Lights, Big City</a> life I wanted. <br>\r\n  I hate these dreams that feel like memories. I convince myself that it is a \r\n  medical condition.<br>\r\n  The unatural acts around me do not help at all. Every other night my friend \r\n  is prosecuted by dreams from various stages of my life. I\\\'m still having strange \r\n  dreams myself , days after being off the medicine. Last nite it was girl x taking \r\n  me to some house for a tom waits performance in someone living room. It was \r\n  dark in the house. I remember the living room vividly. There were some other \r\n  people there.<br>\r\n  A girl that looked like girl b from 1994. I saw tom waits and then passed out. \r\n  I woke up and everyone was stoned out of their minds.<br>\r\n  girl x had fed me some drusg and that\\\'s why I passed out. Totally missed the \r\n  gosh darn tom waits performance.<br>\r\n  I woke up and she was in daze. I wanted out of there. So I carried her out even \r\n  though she did not want to leave. <br>\r\n  Mornings make me want to hug my violin. I press against it and die constantly. god is in the house.\r\n</p>'),(1637,'\\\"Don\\\'t buy your plane ticket unless you\\\'ve talked to me first!\\\"\r\n\r\nHmmm, interesting. A glitch in the plans, I wonder? Cheaper fares? Different date? No... in a sentimentally-driven bravado, he\\\'s chosen to trek here alone, 1800 miles, alone, to be here a month earlier than planned.\r\n\r\nWow.\r\n\r\nBut what about...? I mean, don\\\'t we still have to...? Are you sure that...? But...?\r\n\r\nWell, no. It\\\'s fine. I guess it\\\'s... perfect.\r\n\r\nI  must say I love when the bigger hand of life smacks me hard across the face. \\\"Wake up, you stupid girl! Wake up and recognize that life does not revolve around you and your fickle ways!\\\"  I shake my eyes back into focus.  Slowly.  Now the back-handed bitch slap. \\\"Do you hear me, dumbass?!\\\"\r\n\r\nNever before has life\\\'s backhand stung so much. The intoxicating burn and scent of its dirty fingernails refuses to subside. The welt it\\\'s left will leave a permanent reminder, I\\\'m sure. It figures - just yesterday I threw out my last box of bandaids by saying yes. Weeks earlier, I disposed of the rest of the first aid kit as I realized I was abandoned in Yonkers, yet I kept on going. And months ago, I proclaimed immunity as we drove placidly with San Antonio at our tails.\r\n\r\nMy blood rushes to my face, painting my cheeks a splotchy pink. I brace myself for another swift blow, and it fails to deliver.\r\n\r\nHe\\\'ll be here in time for pumpkins and pound cake, Ben & Jerry and eggnog. He\\\'ll be here in time for thick oppressive blankets and the newest batch of snow angels.'),(1638,'<p>I parachute white sheets over hillaryâ€™s bed and listen to nostalgic <a href=http://www.whitestripes.com/about.html\\\">white boy blues</a>. This album suddenly makes me lay on my stomach. Thinking of Allen Ginsburg even though he was never my fave.</p>\r\n<p><I>oh, it want love, its been so long since I had someone to touch on the knee, man or woman I donâ€™t care what anymore</I></p>\r\n<p>him: oh oh, there he goes.<br>\r\nme: who, HIM? Jesus why do you like the soap opera types.<br>\r\nhim: why do you always like the pale dorks??<br>\r\nme: I like the characters. I cant get into silent mounds of man.</p>\r\n<p>  Laughter is more important than sex. Or at least essential to it. He says I am pathetic. Everytime we see a foxy straight boy and I manage to get a look he encourages me to jump it for his sake. I told him I would rather have \\\"cyber\\\" with my old mang than furtive vacation-sex with the Meditteranean hunk who is staring me down. He says I am pathetic.</p>\r\n<p><I>one more cup of coffee fore I goâ€¦ to the valley below</I></p>\r\n<p>We discussed the possibility of me getting it on with the panting 18 year olds from Thursday night.  Granted, two black eyes and a broken nose under sunglasses at night, all that nervous drunken energy make me feel like a vampire. Want to suck their youth and be excited again for the sidewalk slumber party. That was his attraction to them anyway. Their eagerness. I myself was running the slumber party. I donâ€™t need a 5 minute teen fuck to feel like Iâ€™m still alive.</p>\r\n<p>Synchronicity abounds, everywhere I turn.  We were waiting in line to see a play in which an old writer, sucked dry from the Sisyphean pursuit of his compulsive craft, falls in love with a fresh young thang in a flowing white skirt by the lake in summertime. It is a story as old as the freakin patriarchy. When I enter this plot playing itself out again and again, my identity splits between the old man and the sweet young girl. Trouncing around this city barely clothed, I am giggling Nina. Instructing young boys to bring me beer, interrogating them of their studies and their opinions on jazz music, I am Trigorin.</p>\r\n<p>Their  energy intrigued him; less than a decade younger, and they \\\"had something\\\", a sense of joy, or enthusiasm, that had died in him. In the cab back from the play he brought this up to Kim, our older friend who had once been our young theater teacher. \\\"they were just sooo CHARMING\\\" i shrieked.</p>\r\n<p>She chuckled, remarked with pointed smugness: \\\"yes, they ARE, aren\\\'t they.\\\"</p>\r\n<p> I realized that Kim was 25, and I, 18, when we met. She brought around her boyfriends, who joked about 18 yr old pussy, who took such interest in us for our sassy yet innocent passion. Who gave us books to read, music to listen to. Who lured us to move to New York at the age of 18 and live like them.</p>\r\n<p>From justinâ€™s web site to dinner with jays finance pals from columbia last night, everyone aged 25 seems to be beaten down from the mere whisper of work and survival. \\\"producing\\\". We are such boring old fucks that we are stimulated by 18 year old West side dipshits I would not have sneezed at when I was 18 (having fancied myself a bloody bohemian barmaid who would only get with boys who know Nietsche <I>and</I> Nirvana).  After spending my adolescence dressing like an old lady I put one toe into the youth cult and lick my lips at a bare juvenile chest.</p> \r\n<p>When I was younger I had brief incidences as Frannie Fuck a Lot, partially because I was trying to be like my dad. I wanted to slip encounters into my pocket; the boy I loved accused me of being \\\"acquisitive\\\". <I>mistaking sex for freedom</I>. I donâ€™t want to be like that archetypal man. There is nothing in it for me.</p>\r\n<p>I shopped in hillaryâ€™s closet this week for terry cloth treasures. Oh, <a href=http://www.aboutface.org/HTMLPages/PostRoad/Issue_1/Criticism/chute.html>rebel girl</a>, is that all there is?</p>\r\n<p><I>I think I wanna take you home I wanna try on your clothes, oouh.</I></p>\r\n<p>This city was made to seduce and ultimately dissatisfy. We chase drinks, culture, pretty trinkets, people, pretty people as trinkets. Everywhere is an opportunity to join, detach, attach, and re-attach with thrilled and coked up abandon. As soon as you get bored dancing with one boy you can move onto his brother.<p>\r\n<p>I have seen a lot here. I may have choked from so much beauty. A painter stopped me on the street and kissed my hand. Everywhere my eyes choose shiny things, and there is someone else I donâ€™t see, choosing me. I cultivate myself as a colorful piece of candy yet I donâ€™t want to be eaten. And I donâ€™t want to lay down for this culture of desire.</P>\r\n<p><I>and I will look you in the eye.</I></p>\r\n<p>I started writing here on a sugar high, stalking <a href=http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=967.txt>lucky charms</a>. Now I crave a tomato, something watery and sweet that will sustain me. Something I can grow in my own garden and share with friends. I want genuine warmth and careful luv. I want to come home.</p>\r\n'),(1639,'your hand over mine. passion fruit. eyes and skin. my face resting along your arm. i suck skin. the tiniest bit. between my lips. liquid heat. through walls and night. from the v of your collarbone. along the edge. strength and quivering flesh. i tilt your head...arched back...grinding hips. biting the exposed heartbeat. a straightline under your chin. your lips want me. i tease them. the tip of my tongue. you sweet thing. you are mine. drinking your scent. i travel down. tasting the curve of your face. the line of your jaw. an innocent earlobe...waiting for my corruption. moans and breath move into me. my fingers caressing the hotspot across the back of your neck. feeling life vibrations. soaking in your energy. i give it back with each kiss. inhale. exhale. like the tides we move together. a slow descent of your neck. biting. biting. biting. i can\\\'t get enough. luscious nipple. melted. you are mine. be careful you whisper. '),(1640,'<font face=\\\"Times New Roman,Times\\\">\r\ni was gonna pick egg up at the gate. like k-dog and bmg. i was supposed to meet her at some northwest arrival\r\ngate with a black slinky teddy on my head. welcome back to california eggy.\r\n<br>now i\\\'ve decided to go to london instead. spur of the moment kinda. very un-goat like. i have this free ticket, and i just found out i don\\\'t have class for another 2 weeks. so. like all my friends i\\\'m heading east.\r\n<br>6 days with my parents.\r\n<br>6 days to go to the tate, tate modern, the v&a, the british museum\r\n<br>6 days of immersion with a bunch of family. attendance at a family wedding, in which my cousin (the bride) is marrying a khawaja (outlander, non-arab).\r\n<br>6 days away from home. from my friends, my car, my computer, smoking, drinking.\r\n<br> 1.5 days to mentally prepare myself to get on a plane.\r\n<p>is being out of the country a good enough excuse not to pick egg at the gate?</font>'),(1641,'<p>i liked this site better when it was grey.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He\\\'s just sitting there<br>\r\nLooking angry, children gaze<br>\r\nat his hairy girth</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sitting, watching, growls<br>\r\nthey shouldn\\\'t bang on the glass<br>\r\nIt makes him nervous</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Every day\\\'s the same<br>\r\nHe keeps his cache hidden from view<br>\r\nSo they will not see</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Mussels, cockles, whelks<br>\r\nThey\\\'re his passion. They\\\'re his joy<br>\r\nHe\\\'s a Gorilla</p>'),(1642,'<p>Turbulence, the seatbelts lights are illuminated</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The captain. We\\\'ve got a jokey one here. \\\"Just as soon as we can find the users manual we\\\'ll begin our descent into Liverpool John Lennon\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The cotton wool pseudo land beneath us, babies screaming, the sickly smell of vomit hanging heavy in the stale cabin air</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A fat man taking up two seats with his wide derriere. How do the work the baggage weight allocation? Do fat people have to bring less baggage than skinny people? What if every single passenger was obese and brought his full 25kg of hold baggage?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Could the plane take off?</p>'),(1643,'<P>I need eye candy.  I need some exciting, shiny shimmering images to secure my point of view, to set me off visually, to amuse me.  I have a short attention span.  I used to browse the web in search of interesting visual treats, to little avail.  Now, the tedium of the web, ca. \\\'01 deflates the possibilities of my inquiring eye, ca. \\\'95.  Its all bust.</P>\r\n<P>So do me right. Set me up with something interesting, something worthwhile to set as my wallpaper, something to teethe my infant eyes upon.  Show me a life, a world I\\\'ve never seen, a documentaty on something sublime, human, technoid or otherwise.  Confuse me, challenge me, make me laugh.  Make for me something with visual impact that I\\\'ll never forget.  Please indulge me.</P>\r\n<P>My email is afernier@yahoo.com.  Make use of it liberally, please.</P>'),(1644,'\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1644_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>\r\nTrue. She can be fun<br>\r\nSometimes gets her boobs out and<br>\r\nRubs them in my face\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes she goes mad<br>\r\nStrangely this is once a month<br>\r\nI must run and hide\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Does my ass look big?\\\"<br>\r\nYes it does. But i\\\'ll say \\\"No\\\"<br>\r\nLies are thinly veiled\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"DoN\\\'t LoOk aT ThAt GiRl!\\\"<br>\r\nVeins in her head pulse with blood<br>\r\nGoing mad at me...\r\n</p>'),(1645,'<p>Sunday afternoon I left the city for Santa Cruz.  As I am\r\nGliding\r\ndown the highway I am thinking life is good even though I don\'t have a\r\njob.  The traffic begins to get a little tight so I ease off the\r\npedal\r\na little.  All of the sudden BRAKE lights, SMOKE!  An accident\r\nhas just happened.  I must be very close.  The cars slowly ease\r\nforward and I notice everyone wants into the right lane.  Just\r\naround\r\nthe corner and a few moments later I see a huge RV that was pulling a\r\nboat\r\nhas flipped over on its side.  The boat is laying upside down. \r\nWreckage is strewn all over the road.  Someone is climbing out of\r\na window of the RV.  The boy looks like he is ok.  I wonder how\r\nmany people were inside.  On the other side of the highway I see\r\nseveral\r\ncars that have pulled over.  Some woman is blabbering about how she\r\nhas never called 911 on her cell phone.   I get over in the\r\nright\r\nlane and weave my way through the glass and wreckage.\r\n<p>I am back on my way now.  I eventually find the downtown and park\r\nmy car on Pacific Ave.  I am several hours early so I decide to\r\nexplore\r\na little.  Its always nice to soak in the energy of a new\r\n\r\ntown. \r\nI walk past a few shops and into a grocery store.  Hmmm, no Red\r\nBull?&\r\nnbsp;\r\nI had spent the last few nights up pretty late, so I grabbed a ginseng\r\ndrink.  This will do the trick.  I walk back out to pacific and\r\ncontinue to explore.  The red lights meep at me as I cross the\r\nstreets.\r\n<p>Time for the show!   I stand in line outside of Palookaville\r\nsmiling.  I get inside and proceed upstairs to have a beer. \r\nI am recording tonight, so I scope out potential opportunities to set\r\nup.&\r\nnbsp;\r\nThe sound board would be perfect if the guy will let me patch in. \r\nHmmm, no.  I notice a few other tapers and I ask one of them if I\r\ncan clip my mic on his stand.  He said no problem.  Thank you\r\nsteve :)  The show begins.\r\n<p>*Due to incriminating factors I cannot publicize this portion. \r\nBut, for you information, during the show there was lots of booty shakin\r\nand big smiles.  The glitter girls were there.  We looked into\r\neach others eyes and saw ourselves.  Bubbles...\r\n<p>The next morning I shake the sleep from my eyes.  My head is a\r\nlittle hazy.  What time is it?  Oh, Im going back to\r\nsleep. \r\nI finally get up and out and grab some munchies.  I decide it is\r\ntime\r\nto head back to the city.  I stop by a lighthouse on the cliff and\r\ndaydream for a little while.  Ok, lets go.  I get on highway\r\n1 and head for the city.  I feel dehydrated.  After driving for\r\na little while I decide to stop at a store and get something to\r\ndrink. \r\nAhhh, much better.  I get back into my car and decide to roll a\r\nlittle\r\nherb into my cigarette.  You know, Dutch style.  Hehe. \r\nAnyway, as I look up after licking the paper I see a cop in the window\r\nin front of me pulling a doughnut off of the shelf.  Our eyes\r\nmeet. \r\nI try not to look at the big green bud sitting happily on my\r\ndashboard. \r\nI smile and nod my head.  He smiles back and walks away from the\r\nwindow.&\r\nnbsp;\r\nShew!  I put my car in reverse and get back on the road.  I\r\nlight\r\nup my Dutch splif and turn up the recording of the show and think again\r\nabout how life is good...\r\n<p>I knock on wood...\r\n<center><img SRC=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1645_1.gif\"></center>\r\n'),(1646,'<P>I haven\\\'t felt the urge to write lately. I got scads of money from my favorite uncle, Uncle Sam, for financial assistance. So far, the government has assisted me in buying a new computer, coffee table, and bookshelf, and there\\\'s plenty left. For the first time ever, I\\\'m rich. Rich, I tell you. But school starts Thursday, and I\\\'m feeling the old fear, only doubled because it\\\'s a new school, a big school, and it might be harder than the old school. So I\\\'m scared. Happy. Tense. Here we go again.</P>'),(1647,'</head>\r\n<body text=\\\"#000000\\\" bgcolor=\\\"#C0C0C0\\\" link=\\\"#0000EE\\\" vlink=\\\"#551A8B\\\" alink=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\r\n<font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">oh city of vibration the explanation is simple</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">she is near, her energy is coexisting now</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">i see her in my window pane</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">love and joy fills within</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">she is close</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">snuggle, smile, giggle, and be happy</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">its the positive way</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">she is beautiful</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">money glitters gold but the earth is real</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">she looks at me with eyes of innocence</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">you will see the reality that encompass us now</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">her eyes are wide open</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">it is a time of change</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">the pure of mind and heart will overcome all\r\nobstacles in our path</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">can she see me</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">ever expanding outward, reaching, wanting to\r\nknow the truth</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">she walks with her feet on the ground</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">feeling, loving, sharing</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">a taste of life</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#3333FF\\\">i whisper,Â Â  Yummmm...</font>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1648,'<p><b>As i sit here, half-assed, designing a website for a hotel, i wonder.</b></p>\r\n<p><b>What\\\'s the point?</b></p>\r\n<p><b>On the fireplace is a Sea Anenomie. I wonder what did he achieve during \r\n  his sub-aquatic lifetime beneath the waves.</b></p>\r\n<p><b>Did he have dreams? Aspirations?</b></p>\r\n\r\n<i>\r\n<p>Remove any tentacles adhering to the skin by rinsing with salt water.\r\nSoak a gauze pad or cloth in vinegar and place over the area for 15 minutes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dry affected area.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Be prepared to treat the victim for shock and transport to the nearest hospital.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If the affected area is the eyes, flush with at least one quart of fresh water. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>DO NOT USE VINEGAR on the eyes. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>DO NOT RUB THE AREA</p>\r\n</i>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Or did he just eat plankton</b></p>'),(1649,'the roaring waves engulfing<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndrowned<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhave you been there?<br>\r\nwhy why why<br>\r\nthis is not right...<br>\r\nthe little girl playing her alone games<br>\r\nghost of me<br>\r\nhaunted dreams<br><br>\r\nthe old man...locked outside. he wont let you in<br>\r\nand i take this from you<br>\r\ni claim my price<br><br>\r\ntorn past. jagged wreckage<br>\r\nthe lover...never to be. denying my faith<br><br>\r\ndenying me<br>\r\nchain-linked heart...screaming meanness<br>\r\nthere is no soul in life, he says<br>\r\nand walks away<br><br>\r\nstanding. waiting<br>\r\nthe waves come<br>\r\nto this place of ancient hope. slowly fading<br><br>\r\nnothing left to hold me<br>\r\nnoone left to say goodbye<br>\r\n'),(1650,'<p>The Ingredients Added to Tobacco in the Manufacture of Cigarettes by the Five Major American Cigarette Companies:</b><P>\r\nAcctanisole, Acetic Acid, Acetoin, Acetophenone, 6i- Acetoxydihydrotheaspirane, 2- Acetyl-3-Ethylpyrazine, 2-Acetyl- 5-Methylfuran, Acetylpyrazine, 2- Acetylpyridine, 3-Acetylpyridine, 2-Acetylthiszole, Aconitic Acid, Dl-Alanine, Alfalfa Extract, Allspice Extract, Oleoresin and Oil, Allyl Hexanoate, Allyl Ionone, Almond Bitter Oil, Ambergris Tincture, Ammonia, Ammonium Bicarbonate, Ammonium Hydroxide, Ammonium Phosphate Dibasic, Ammonium Sulfide, Amyl Alcohol, Amyl Butyrate, Amyl Formate, Amyl Octanoate, alpha- Amylcinnamaldebyde, Amyns Oil, Trans-Anethole, Angelica Root Extract, Oil and Seed Oil, Anise, Anise Star, Extract and Oils, Anisy Acctate, Anisyl Alcohol, Anisyl Formate, Anisyl Phenylacctate, Apple Juice Concentrate, Extract and Skins, Apricot Extract and Juice Concentrate, 1-Arginine, Asafetida Fluid Extract and Oil, Ascorbic Acid, 1-Asparagine Monohydrate, 1-Aspaartic Acid, Balsam Peru and Oil, Basil Oil, Bay Leaf Oil and Sweet Oil, Beeswax White, Beet Juice Concentrate, Benzaldehyde, Benzaldehyde Glyceryl Acetal, Benzoic Acid, Benzoin, Benzoin Resin, Benzophenone, Benzyl Alcohol, Benzyl Benzoate, Benzyl Butyrate, Benzyl Cinnamate, Benzyl Propionste, Benzyl Salicylate, Bergamot Oil, Bisabolene, Black Currant Buds Absolute, Borncol, Bornyl Acctate, Buchu Lea Oil, 1,3-Butanediol, 2,3-Butanedione, 1-Butanol, 2- Butanone, 4(2-Butenlidene)- 3,5,5-Trunethyl-2-Cyclohexen-1 Oue, Butter, Butter Esters, and Butter Oil, Butyl Acetate, Butyl Butyrate, Butyl Butyryl Lactate, Butyl Isovalcrate, Butyl Phenylacetate,Butyl Undocylenate, 3- Butylidenephthalide, Butyric Acid, Cadinene, Caffeine, Calcium Carbonate, Camphene, Cananga Oil, Capsicum Oleoresin, Caramel Color, Caraway Oil, Carbon Dioxide,Cardamom Oleoresin, Extract, Seed Oil and Powder, C