Tetramethylethyl-Cyclohexanone, 2,3,5,6-Tetramethylphyrazine, Thiamine Hydrochloride, Thiazole, 1-Thereonine, Thyme Oil, White and Red, TLhymol, Tobacco Extracts, Tochopherols (mixed), Tolu Balsam Glum and Extract, Tolualdehydes, para-Tolyl, 3- Methylbutyrate, para-Tolyl- kAcetaldehyde, pars-Tolyl Acetate, para-Tolyl Isobutyrate, para-Tolyl-Phenylacetate Triacetin, 2-Tridecanone, 2- Tridecenal, Triethyl Citrate, 3,5,5- Grimethyl-1-Hexanol, para, alpha, alpha Trimethylbenzyl Alcohol, 4- (2,6,6-Trimethylcyclohex-1-Enyl) But-2-En-4-One, 2,6,6- Trimethylcyclohex-2-Ene-1,4- Dione, 2,6,6-Trimethylcyclohexa- 1,3-Dienyl Methan, 4-(2,6,6- Trimethylcyclohexa-1, 3-Dienyl) But-2-En-4-One-2,2,6- Trimethylcyclohexanone, 2,3,5- Grimethylpyrazine, 1-Tyrosine, delta-Undercalactone, gamma- Undecalactone, Undecanal, 2- Undecanone, 10-Undecenal, Urea, Valencene, Valeraldehyde, Valerian Root Extract, Oil and Powder, Valeric Acid, gamma- Valerplactone, Valine, Vanilla Extract and Oleresin, Vanillin, Vertaraldehyde, Vetiver Oil, Vinegar, Violet Leaf Absolute, Walnut Hull Extract, Water, Wheat Extract and Flour, LWild CLherry Bark Extract, Wine and Wine Sherry, Xanthan Gum, 3,4- XLylenol, Yeast.</p>'),(1651,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1651_1.jpg\'><br>\r\n<br>\r\nSummer said you\\\'ve become a man.<br>\r\nHe told me of your investments<br>\r\nand of your donations, expecting no return<br>\r\nYet grasping each fleeting indulgence of time.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSummer said the warm air passed,<br>\r\nreading like a book of dreams<br>\r\nand your sore arm laughs,<br>\r\nfeet hanging off the bed,<br>\r\npicking her hairs from your mouth.<br>\r\nYou know.  You and he<br>\r\nshare the same knowing gaze.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSummer says maybe you\\\'ve fallen<br>\r\nbut are rational, quite the wise one<br>\r\nnot to be distracted, not playing your hand<br>\r\nHe says she\\\'s the same, so smart.<br>\r\nHe speaks of a yoke and a measure<br>\r\nand dreams the eons by which <br>\r\nGod measures time.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSummer told me much, but he\\\'s gone now,<br>\r\nand the girl of spring has bloomed<br> \r\nwilted and gone to seed<br>\r\nand you knew, for he gave you his wisdom<br>\r\nbefore he passed.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOne day she bounded through the door,<br>\r\nshutting it behind her as you worked<br>\r\nand summer headed south for the fall<br>\r\nleaving you with new doors<br>\r\nnew sketches<br>\r\nand dreams of unfinished paintings.<br>'),(1652,'I like ix. \r\n<p>\r\nIs it pronounced \\\"icks\\\", or is it just \\\"nine\\\"? \r\n<p>\r\nWhenever there is a really emotionally charged issue, ix goes straight to the point, the heart, the very <i>jugular</i> of the issue, with callous disregard for the feelings of the people involved. By god and Jiminy Cricket, we need <b>more</b> people like him in this world, where self-indulgence and ridiculous justifications are running rampant, choking the life out of all public debate, and most private conversations. Here in this community, ix is like the ego, no, he <i>is</i> the ego, the part going, \\\"man. that\\\'s a really, really shitty poem,\\\" or \\\"is this worth sharing?\\\" We should all ask ourselves, before submitting a post, \\\"What would ix have to say?\\\" Then submit it anyway, you rebellious bastard. '),(1653,'no'),(1654,'<br>\r\nAll bent out of shape<br>  \r\nAll bets are off<br>  \r\nAll dressed up and nowhere to go<br>  \r\nAll ears<br>  \r\nAll for one, and one for all<br>   \r\nAll hands on deck <br> \r\nAll hands to the pump<br>  \r\nAll heck (hell) breaks loose<br>  \r\nAll in a day\\\'s work<br>  \r\nAll in due time<br>  \r\nAll over the map<br>  \r\nAll paled in comparison<br>  \r\nAll talk and no action<br>  \r\nAll that glitters is not gold<br>   \r\nAll that jazz <br>  \r\nAll the bits and pieces<br>  \r\nAll thumbs<br>  \r\nAll work and no play, makes Jack a dull boy<br>  \r\nAll\\\'s fair in love and war<br>  \r\nAll\\\'s well that ends well  <br>\r\n<br>'),(1655,'<FONT SIZE=\"4\" FACE=\"arial, helvetica, sans-serif\">\r\nShe has got <a href=\"http://www.kellys.com/jokeb1.html\">blonde</a> hair<br>\r\nSome says she\'s an <a href=\"http://www.ampsoccer.org/wc98-pix.htm\">amputee</a><br>\r\nThis is just not true\r\n</FONT>'),(1656,'Baby, won\\\'t you come and let me kiss you<br>\r\nThen I\\\'m gonna touch you<br>\r\nOh baby let me show you<br>\r\nHow I\\\'m gonna freak you<br>\r\n\r\nBaby I\\\'ve been wanting you<br> \r\nAnd you\\\'re putting up a hell of a fight<br> \r\nGirl I can tell <br>\r\nYou want me as bad as I want you<br>\r\nSo we got to get this things started right<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSee I don\\\'t wanna beg<br>\r\nBut I will if I have to<br>\r\nYou been playin\\\' games<br>\r\nWith me for a long time<br>\r\nAnd you know very well<br>\r\nI can make you feel alright<br>\r\nSo stop frontin\\\' and let\\\'s get it on<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSee I don\\\'t wanna chase you baby<br> \r\nBut you got to understand<br> \r\nIf you let me have my way with you<br>\r\nI will never have to beg you again<br>\r\n<br>'),(1657,'<p><i>Let this be the eternal warning.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\\\'s the idea that the pay-off from all the pain will make it worthwhile.  That one day I\\\'ll awaken by her side and, even knowing she never felt the ache of aborted love times two, realize that, in the end, the patience and understanding and second and third chances produced a greater dividend.  It\\\'s a very Nietzschean way too look at it, I suppose.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\\\"I feel like I\\\'m going to spend the rest of my life making things up to you.\\\"</i>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t want that.  Well, if you insist.  When shall we begin?  When do the small infractions start really counting?  After the sacrifice? </p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>Small infractions.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you think you\\\'re worth this, you\\\'re wrong.  I\\\'m not singling you out.  No one is inherently worth another person\\\'s love.  There\\\'s giving and giving more involved.  There\\\'s respect and reciprocation.  There\\\'s concern.  There\\\'s work.  The work starts from the very beginning, or should.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s easy to give up.   It\\\'s easy to remain unconcerned.  It\\\'s easy to demand.  It\\\'s not so easy to demand the same of yourself, to care, to continue.</p>'),(1658,'FADE IN<br>\r\n<br>\r\n1ECU of manâ€™s face. Heâ€™s wrinkling his nose vigorously as if he has an itch. Finally he sneezes, and then picks his nose, furtively, with a side-glance to see if anyone notices.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n2Wide overhead shot. Bobâ€™s semi-balding head is sitting in a sea of cubicles. Right next-door, a woman sits applying lipstick. No one appeared to notice the nose-picking.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n3Low pan up underneath Bobâ€™s elbow as he lifts Red Herring coffee cup. Bob drinks coffee. Itâ€™s early. He shuffles papers. He scratches. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n4Cut back to Wide Overhead Shot. Bob stares at the computer. He crosses his legs. He looks over at lipstick woman. His head drifts sideways. He might be looking at her legs. He canâ€™t really see anything. She never looks over. He looks back at the screen.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n5Long take, still in overhead position. There are at least 30 cubes positioned identically. The various office workers do various things. Nothing too interesting.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n6The mail cart comes. Bob gets some envelopes. Invoices. He gets up and leaves the cube. The camera stays. He comes back with a shit-load of copies.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n7Itâ€™s lunch-time. Bob leaves, alone. Other people leave too. Bob comes back with a Styrofoam take-out of Chinese food. He shovels in rice with a plastic fork. Some of the other people come back, greasy and full. Smelling like cigarettes.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n8Bob processes some more invoices. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n9Cut to second camera (outdoor security b&w.)Bob takes a ciggy break.  He paces, coughs, itâ€™s breezy, the smoke swirls back into the elevator lobby. He drops it, stamps it out. Goes back up. The security guard leaves his kiosk, mini broom and dustpan, sweeps it up.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n10Back to overhead cam. Bob types. Entering invoices. Checking email. Sends a few forwards to his friends. Blows his nose again. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n11It must be 5:00PM. Bob puts on his coat. Shuts down. The lipstick lady is leaving too. She stuffs her purse with her left-over takeout container. They leave at the same time, exchanging pleasantries. Itâ€™s still silent. No music.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n12Back to overhead cam. Pointing down on Bobâ€™s semi-balding head. Nothing has changed except his shirt. Heâ€™s wearing a dark colored one today. He doesnâ€™t pick his nose, but he does get coffee. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n13He drinks coffee over his invoices. A little spills. Quick dab with the shirtsleeve. He types. He gets more coffee. He keeps on typing, entering data into the DOS platform billing application he has open, green letters on a black screen, blinking white cursors, red error messages. Long take.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n14Cut to camera in office kitchen, placed behind microwave. We see Bobâ€™s face peering into the microwave. Heâ€™s heating up his lunch. The Saran wrap has melted a little into the soup. He peels it off, cursing when he burns his finger on the liquid. He fishes out some plastic silverware from under the sink and puts change into the soda machine and punches in D1.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n15A Diet Coke in hand, he goes back to his cube. He puts his Tupperware down, and then goes to the bathroom. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n16Camera follows him to the door. A woman comes out of the opposing bathroom, he tries to sneak a look past the swinging door. Nothing but tiles.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n17He pees. He zips, goes back to his cube. Eats.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n18Overhead cam. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n19Bob is typing again. He checks his email. His buddy has written him, asking him to go the game tonight. Bob writes back, hell yeah! <br>\r\n<br>\r\n20A manager comes by. Bob quickly switches screens. Nods politely. The manager leaves. Bob emails his buddy back. They agree to meet at 5:45PM in front of the Willie Mays statue.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n21Itâ€™s 4:55PM. Bob is stealthily putting on his coat, ducking down below his cube. His lipstick co-worker looks over, sniffs and stares back at her screen. Heâ€™s nervous now, and sneaks to the elevator.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n22Overhead cam. Bob is sitting at his desk, coffee pinkly brown from artificial creamer sits next to his elbow. Heâ€™s wearing a checked shirt today. Little unobtrusive checks. Not like a tablecloth or anything. Very J Crew. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n23Bob is typing. He seems tired. Heâ€™s on his 3rd cup of coffee by lunchtime.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n24More security camera cigarette smoking. This time, a woman is there too. They donâ€™t talk. They look past each other and blow smoke out towards the planter full of ferns. She smokes hers to the filter, goes back inside. Bob lights another. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n25Cut to overhead cam, back inside. Back to Bobâ€™s bald spot. Bob types. Bob types. Bob writes notes on a little pad. He answers the phone. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n26He hangs up the phone. He picks up a stack of papers and leaves the cube. He comes back without the papers. He sits back down. He checks his email. Thereâ€™s some junk mail about being debt free for life and â€œsee Britney Spears get fucked!!!!â€ He deletes it.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n27Bob goes to the bathroom. He stays there a while. He comes back. No new messages. He sits and stares into space.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n28Itâ€™s 5:00PM. Bob leaves. He doesnâ€™t have an umbrella in his hand. The drizzle on the way to BART is slow but steady. (we see through the security camera Bob hesitating under the overhang, and then plunging into the stream of commuters, lost in a field of umbrellas).<br>\r\n<br>\r\n29Overhead cam. Bobâ€™s bald spot seems bigger. His belly is for sure. Thereâ€™s a birthday party today. His co-worker across the way. Sheâ€™s turning 50. Her face is sour. Everyone eats cake around their cubes and sheds sprinkles onto the carpet. Someone leaves their scraped frosting plate on Bobâ€™s desk. He dumps it in the trash.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n30Bob is typing. He types all day. (3 long takes.)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n31Overhead cam. Bobâ€™s bald spot is bigger. We (the viewer)think maybe time is passing, but weâ€™re not sure.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n32Bob must be almost 60. Itâ€™s hard to tell. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n33Bob shuts his computer off and goes home.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n34Bob wears a white shirt today. He absent-mindedly gets some grease on the front from the pizza heâ€™s eating for lunch.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n35Bob has a big stack of invoices. Someone brings him some cookies she baked at home. They chat for a while. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n36Bob doesnâ€™t finish his invoices before 5:00PM. He leaves them in a big untidy pile.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n37Bob is wearing a blue shirt. Heâ€™s not really doing anything right now. He stares. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n38Overhead cam. The cube next to him has a younger girl in it now. Sheâ€™s nervously friendly. She talks to Bob a lot. He doesnâ€™t seem too sure about how to respond.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n39One day, she invites him to lunch. They leave, the girl talking, flapping her hands.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n40They come back to their desks at the same time. Bob seems like he doesnâ€™t know what to say. He starts typing.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n41She looks over, starts to say something, doesnâ€™t. She types. They both type all day.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n42The mail guy is new too. He has a floppy mod haircut. It gets in his eyes. He doesnâ€™t talk to Bob. He just puts his envelopes down. He talks to the girl across the way. Bob watches.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n43Bob goes out for a smoke. The girl doesnâ€™t smoke.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n44Bob comes back in. He types.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n45A big packet gets delivered to Bobâ€™s desk. He starts reviewing it. We realize itâ€™s his 401K plan information and retirement information. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n46Bob is wearing a white shirt and vest. He seems excited. He isnâ€™t doing any work today. An older woman comes by and squeezes his shoulder. They both seem embarrassed. She leaves.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n47Bob goes to lunch. He gets Chinese. He is careful with the noodles. He doesnâ€™t seem to want to get anything on his shirt. The mail guy comes by with some boxes. He flips his long mod cut, and watches Bob put coffee cups, pencils, his plastic Simpsons figures signed baseballs and paperclip chains in the box. Bob carefully rolls up his Casablanca poster and puts a rubber band around it. Someone else comes by with a dolly for his files. He helps them load them up.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n48About 10 people come over with a cake. It says: â€œHappy Retirement Bob! Weâ€™ll Miss Youâ€ in curvy cursive sugar writing. Everyone has a piece. They chat for a while. Some of the men thump Bob on the back. Mostly everyone just shakes his hand. One of the women gives him a little peck. He likes it. He becomes effusive. Someone has a bottle of champagne. Paper cups appear. Everyone has some. Bob has two glasses. There seems to be some talk of going somewhere to get a few drinks after work. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n49People start to trickle back to their desks. Some take extra pieces of cake. The champagne is all gone.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n50Bob sits down in his chair. He is smiling. He stands up to swipe some frosting off the remainder of the cake sitting in the sticky cardboard box on his desk. <br>\r\n<br>\r\n51Bob falls to the floor.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n52Bob dies from a heart attack at age 61.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nCUT TO CREDITS<br>\r\n(theme from \\\"RockyII\\\" plays)<br>'),(1659,' I remember nothing with any great clarity.Is it that my mind is lazy ,incapable of reaching back past the day before yesterday? or worse is it that my past is so devoid of action that there\\\'s nothing worth remembering?\r\n  Most memories easily conjured ,are ones of iether great pain or great joy .At least they are the most clear in my mind..\r\n '),(1660,'<p>We were sitting on the L-shaped couch in my living room, both of us involved in individual activities.  You reading while I was flipping through television channels displaying nothing to me.  Bright light filtering through sheer curtains along the windows.  Both of us separate, yet together.</p>\r\n<p>We began discussing tattoos and we were jokingly arguing (in that way we do) regarding the merits of me designing my own tattoo for something which was an inside joke.  Somehow the Iron Maiden-ish graffiti on the wall was worked into the discussion.</p>\r\n<p>We half-heartedly continued to argue until I moved my left foot.  You reacted and I hadn\\\'t even realised.  A stern look and a correcting phrase: \\\"Put it back!\\\"  I hadn\\\'t even realised that our feet were pressed together, sole to sole.  So I moved my foot back into position, soles brushing together, the slightest friction.  Heat.  You smiled then.  That smile you do were the tip of your tongue just slightly juts out to touch your upper lip.</p>\r\n<p>You went back to reading and I continued stared at our feet, sensing the growing warmth there.  I wanted to go there, to feel that heat with my whole body.  I could feel the cold metal of your toe ring then, accenting the body heat.  Your right hand fell down onto a pillow.  My attention was diverted from our feet.  The chipped nail polish and inviting lines drawing me to it.  I let my own hand slip under yours, lifting it.  I began to play with your fingers.  Gently stroking them, staring.  I noticed you unable to contain your smile while reading and returned my attention wholly onto your hand.</p>\r\n<p>Without warning, you arose and left the room.  I didn\\\'t see where you went because I was still staring a the space where, just moments earlier, your hand was joined with mine.</p>\r\n<p>My foot felt the cool air, the room appeared darker now.  And then I woke up.</p>'),(1661,'<P>the sun burned through my jeans in the park today, making my knees itch.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nit annoyed me intensely, but I enjoyed that it changed my mood, and allowed the direction of my day to be altered.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand I lay there and I let the sun continue to drench me with its heat, and when I sat up to smoke a cigarette, I felt a bug crawl on my arm- so I smooshed it.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1662,'I had my tips in my small purse pocket.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n32 bucks and some change, including do\' from Israel<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMy military dependets ID, the only ID i have so show i can get alchohol.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThat and me passport, but who carries that around? ...except my uncle<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWith ma\' tips Tina and I head to the thrift store to buy...<br>\r\n<br>\r\ncool Wranglers, a slip skirt, 2 old lady shirts, velvet-ee pants and one long skirt later ...<br>\r\n<br>\r\n...it is time to pay and time to NOTICE  i have no wallet...<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\"Must have dropped the thing...\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\nNot to worry, not to fret... I looked everywhere, retracing steps, scents of steps, de-ja-vu nightmares and find jack shit because someone probably looked at my University ID and thought \"rich gurl\" ...she can afford missing 30 bucks....<br>\r\n<br>\r\nTruth is you mother fucker, wallet stealer,<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<B>I CAN\'T.</B><br>\r\n<br>\r\n...all the wallets I\'ve turned in, where is Karma when you need her?<br><br>'),(1664,'I do not rememeber what my first word was.<br>\r\nWhat\'s the most useless thing you own?Â <br>\r\n  - - - - - - - - [ I will stop my daily grind<br>\r\n  - - - - - - - - and play with crayons again ]<br>\r\n  oo Absolutely no disconnection.<br>\r\n  oo Part of no movement. Wave hello. Insert smile here.</p>'),(1663,'<p>It\\\'s Friday. Another week has passed.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m scanning photo\\\'s of a guesthouse in County Cavan, Ireland.</p>\r\n<p>Caroline is texting me again:</p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">Hi baby im lonely</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\" color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">tough shit im at work</font></p>\r\n<p>On the subject of my mobile i\\\'ve just changed the tarriff. The \r\n  tarriff i was on was costing Â£15 a month line rental before i made any \r\n  calls. I got me a new \"Pay as you go\" sim card. No i pay no line rental. \r\n  I have a little swipe card that i can top up my credit with and talk away.</p>\r\n<p>The surprising thing is that now, with the exact same phone, the exact same \r\n  phone company but a cheaper tarriff, i get better reception. When i was paying \r\n  them Â£15 a month just for the privelige of owning the phone they couldn\\\'t \r\n  give a crap. Now i\\\'m paying them bugger all they\\\'re only to happy to help me \r\n  and i get great reception where ever i go.</p>\r\n<p>Surely this is a backwards business strategy...</p>\r\n<p>Why are women so difficult? Especially when it comes to sex. It\\\'s a simple \r\n  matter. You want sex, or you don\\\'t. It\\\'s not a complex concept. Is it? I might \r\n  turn gay.</p>\r\n<p>So, i gave her my computer since i don\\\'t use it anymore. I\\\'m on the internet \r\n  all day at work so i don\\\'t have much use for a computer at all. She left it \r\n  switched on during a HUGE thunderstorm and now it\\\'s broken. I\\\'m not getting \r\n  it fixed. I don\\\'t need it. I\\\'m broke. A company in LA who i bought some domain \r\n  names from a couple of years ago have just rebilled my credit card to the tune \r\n  of over $300! Apparently there\\\'s not much i can do.</p>'),(1665,'>How are you?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRiding the rollercoaster as best i can. i am still mostly bitter. yea. i haven\\\'t used that word before but i think thats it. i feel like something inside of me has died. innocence? i feel betrayed.<br><br>\r\nI think what has hit me the hardest is that this is all one more death on a list of things i\\\'ve felt like i lost last year. not only did i have to proclaim the love of my life DOA, but then the best friend i thought i had also\r\ndisappeared. and now this...all related, all twisted together.<br><br>\r\nWhen i am doing things im okay. but i notice that most things just make me seethe inside. even people i love. this is NOT me! its kind of frightening.<br><br>\r\nMy friends say that i have been too nice. that i have not once told him straight up about the anger i have for things done, or not done in the past. i dont know. i have always felt that it is best to tell people what you think in the most mature way possible. anger doesnt seem to do anything but put people on the defensive. or confuse things even more.<br><br>\r\nAnd also how can i confront someone whose mother just died??? who am i to whine about not being told, when it\\\'s HIS mother?<br><br>\r\nIt doesnt seem to matter...<br><br>\r\nIt is not always anger that i feel.<br><br>\r\nIn the mornings it is sadness. mornings are the worst. maybe because i can\\\'t help but be open to everything in my sleep, and then after waking it takes some time to acclimate myself. that inbetween space where its all raw wound hurts the most. the space between waking and achieving numbness.<br><br>\r\nI came close to crying half a million times yesterday. especially the morning.<br>\r\nmorning...mourning<br><br>\r\nI ache.<br><br>\r\nI wonder if the anger is simply a mechanism to avoid the pain. once the crying starts there is not much else you can do, but at least the anger allows you to function [a lonesome angry bitch to be sure...but at least its\r\nsomething].<br><br>\r\nThis morning was bad.<br><br>\r\nAt one point i was saying over and over again in my head...please make the pain go away. please make it stop. i cant take it anymore.<br><br>\r\nI have not really cried at all. not really at all since things went bad between us. since the last real hardcore incident happened. that was awhile ago. months before i finally left. i asked him what he thought of getting married and he said, \"well thats really out of the blue!\" and turned his back on me and walked away.<br><br>\r\nSix years of living together and marriage is out of the blue???<br><br>\r\nI had to leave. even though he still kept saying that he loved me. even though i still kept saying i loved him.<br><br>\r\nPointless aching.<br><br>\r\nHe just walked away and never said anything else about it again.<br><br>\r\nI dont know why im writing this. i guess i just need to get it out. this stuff that keeps playing over and over in my head. a video tape stuck on rewind.<br><br>\r\nMy guts are wrenching.<br><br>\r\nIt doesnt matter...<br><br>\r\nThey are twisted and so many of my friends seem to think this is all no big deal. that its been a year, why should i care? that i left him, so why does it hurt?<br><br>\r\nSo i find myself telling everyone that i am fine.<br><br>\r\nBecause id rather not hear their analysis, or advice, or the indifference.<br><br>\r\nIt doesnt matter<br>\r\ni am fine.<br><br>\r\nDon\'t worry i am fine.<br>\r\nAm i trying to convince myself of that?<br>\r\ni dont know.<br><br>\r\nIt is easier to just say i dont know, i am fine.<br>'),(1666,'<p>I am waiting for a friend of mine to come over. i haven\\\'t seen her in a long time, last words spoken were on the bad side. we almost had a love affair, but at the last minute I  deflected the advance. Sometimes I think I did the right thing in that situation, other times I miss her company. I miss the company, I miss the talks. I miss her, but I am not sure I am ready to accept the advances again. There was a time when she was my best friend. We did lots together and enjoyed eachothers company. Then there came this point were we both realized that we had stronger feelings for eachother, I scrammed. Took off, never looked back. Now, I am having to deal with that. Infact, she\\\'ll be here in about 5 minutes... my mind is scrambling, I am not prepared. I have 3X5 cards that I usually use when I am to say something to someone, sounds strange? I write down little key words, and statements to help me during the conversation. I have done this all my life, especially when speaking to a person about courtship. This time, I am not prepared, a bit scared I will say something that comes from my heart and not my head... might be interesting... my heart is thumping, and I am sweating, right now.</p>'),(1667,'<p>\\\"Let\\\'s just ease off of your current meds, while I consult with my friend the psych nurse to see what route we should take with you,\\\"\r\nshe said with that pseudo-friendly-but-mostly-\"PLEEEEEEEZZZZZEEEEEEEEE-don\\\'t-sue-me-for-malpractice\" kind of smile on her face.  \\\"I guess you did mention that you had a tendency to get a little manic sometimes, so that particular antidepressant might not have been the best choice for you, but at least your boss didn\\\'t fire you, since we\r\n<b> should</b> try to look at things on the bright side, which can be a little tough to do after a sleepless 36 hours that included a \\\"little\\\" shopping excursion equal to 3 times your average bi-weekly paycheck, but lets not dwell on those little negative things...ok?\\\" (why is it that people feel the need to speak in unending sentences when they\\\'re trying to cover their asses?)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"So I should just take half a pill for the next couple of days, then nothing for a couple of days, then you\\\'ll call me with the new prescription...right?\\\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Absolutely.  We\\\'ll get you on the right track.  You have nothing to worry about.\\\" (except the whispers and stares of co-workers, the typical disappearance of your \\\"significant other\\\" during times of crisis, your mother conveniently offering to watch your kid for a little while (not because she thinks you\\\'re temporarily unfit, or anything),  and your mortal fear of becoming your \\\"post-breakdown\\\" father (the we\\\'ve-almost-got-the-meds-figured-out puppet of his former self that is dangled in front of you daily to taunt you with false hopes that you will once again be able to chat comfortably about\r\nTolkien and fossils and all the geeky things by which you two are genetically bound.)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Great.  I look forward to your call.\\\"</p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><br>\r\n<font face=\\\"Impact\\\" size=\\\"6\\\">Rapid Descent Into Instability</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\">Where is the logic in this?:</font></p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n  <p><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\">It takes 4-8 weeks for a particular psych drug\r\n  to become effective, but after two days of \"easing off\" I find\r\n  myself in some sort of rapid cycling, mixed state, bipolar purgatory,\r\n  painfully aware that the world is going on around me, but unable to get off\r\n  the sofa, until the burning urge strikes me to buy lots of soda and tacos, and\r\n  learn to play a few songs on the guitar, even though I can only play a G7\r\n  chord (for now), until 3AM when upon mastery of \"Red River Valley,\"\r\n  I realize I\\\'m probably doing it all wrong, anyways, and why the fuck did I\r\n  even buy a guitar if I knew I was never going to be able to teach the\r\n  requisite contortion to my fat little fingers, which I know will just get\r\n  fatter and<img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"file:///E:/body.gif\\\" align=\\\"right\\\" width=\\\"161\\\" height=\\\"333\\\"> fatter on whatever new (hopefully more carefully thought out)\r\n  medication I happen to be prescribed, but I should be used to being\r\n  worthless...i mean fat...because I have been for as long as I can remember,\r\n  which is why at 3:15AM I must \"photoshop\" my ridiculous \"food\r\n  review column\" mug shot onto skinny bodies as motivation for losing\r\n  weight, which should be fairly simple since the second job I had to take (see\r\n  above shopping excursion) is at the YMCA, and they have a great fitness\r\n  center...oh, yeah, and a pool, because swimming is supposed to be great\r\n  exercise for eating enthusiasts like me, and WOW!!!! I realize how lucky I am\r\n  for having a job where I can eat free food once a week and people stop me at\r\n  the grocery store to tell me how much they love my column and \"those\r\n  stuffed burgers at The Country Kitchen sound absolutely delicious and I can\\\'t\r\n  wait to try one,\" but they don\\\'t realize that I only review paid\r\n  advertisers so I can\\\'t ever say anything bad in my column, even if the stuffed\r\n  burger is disgusting, so I just say \"it was one of the most unique\r\n  burgers I\\\'ve ever eaten,\" and equally-fat-grocery-store-lady gets\r\n  suckered into wasting five bucks on a bland piece of crap when she could have\r\n  invested that cash in a few issues of a \"real\" newspaper and she\r\n  might actually learn something that doesn\\\'t involve the compulsion to stop a\r\n  complete stranger in the produce section to talk about crappy food...</font></p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\"><font size=\\\"1\\\">i do not hate\r\nmyself</font><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\"> <font size=\\\"2\\\">i do not hate myself </font></font><font size=\\\"3\\\" color=\\\"#00FF00\\\">i\r\ndo not hate myself</font> <font size=\\\"4\\\" color=\\\"#FFFF00\\\">i do not hate myself</font>\r\n<font size=\\\"5\\\" color=\\\"#FF00FF\\\">i do not hate myself</font> <font size=\\\"6\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">I\r\nDO NOT HATE MYSELF!</font></font></p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier\\\"><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"Honey?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Yes, dear?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"Why can\\\'t you just snap out of it?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Fuck you!\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"I mean, I get upset all the time, but then I just snap out of it.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Up the ass.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"You\\\'re starting to make <b><u> me</u></b> crazy.  I don\\\'t think I should come around for a few days...until you feel better, you know...so that you don\\\'t bring me down or anything.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"With a steam locomotive.\\\"</font></font></p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p>I\\\'m still waiting for her call.</p>'),(1669,'<P> There is something about you that attracts people to you.  I hate it and I\\\'m starting to hate you.  It\\\'s something that I just don\\\'t understand. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Allow me to express my individualized opinion.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> You are thoughtless.  You don\\\'t give a damn.  You think only of yourself and nothing else. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You are insensitive. You cause pain and you think nothing of it. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You are cold and mean spirited. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You are a liar and it\\\'s obscene. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You take some of the simplest things in life and make them so fucking difficult.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Forgive the list for being short.  I can\\\'t find my thesaurus.  You probably stole that, too. Bitch. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Oh yea, and I think you\\\'re a bitch.  The Merriam Webster Dictionary defines a bitch as a lewd or immoral woman; a malicious, spiteful, or domineering woman. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> You are quickly turning into someone I\\\'d like to avoid.  The sick part is that there are people like you everywhere. </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1668,'<p>\\\"Let\\\'s just ease off of your current meds, while I consult with my friend the psych nurse to see what route we should take with you,\\\"\r\nshe said with that pseudo-friendly-but-mostly-\"PLEEEEEEEZZZZZEEEEEEEEE-don\\\'t-sue-me-for-malpractice\" kind of smile on her face.  \\\"I guess you did mention that you had a tendency to get a little manic sometimes, so that particular antidepressant might not have been the best choice for you, but at least your boss didn\\\'t fire you, since we\r\n<b> should</b> try to look at things on the bright side, which can be a little tough to do after a sleepless 36 hours that included a \\\"little\\\" shopping excursion equal to 3 times your average bi-weekly paycheck, but lets not dwell on those little negative things...ok?\\\" (why is it that people feel the need to speak in unending sentences when they\\\'re trying to cover their asses?)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"So I should just take half a pill for the next couple of days, then nothing for a couple of days, then you\\\'ll call me with the new prescription...right?\\\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Absolutely.  We\\\'ll get you on the right track.  You have nothing to worry about.\\\" (except the whispers and stares of co-workers, the typical disappearance of your \\\"significant other\\\" during times of crisis, your mother conveniently offering to watch your kid for a little while (not because she thinks you\\\'re temporarily unfit, or anything),  and your mortal fear of becoming your \\\"post-breakdown\\\" father (the we\\\'ve-almost-got-the-meds-figured-out puppet of his former self that is dangled in front of you daily to taunt you with false hopes that you will once again be able to chat comfortably about\r\nTolkien and fossils and all the geeky things by which you two are genetically bound.)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Great.  I look forward to your call.\\\"</p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><br>\r\n<font face=\\\"Impact\\\" size=\\\"6\\\">Rapid Descent Into Instability</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\">Where is the logic in this?:</font></p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n  <p><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\">It takes 4-8 weeks for a particular psych drug\r\n  to become effective, but after two days of \"easing off\" I find\r\n  myself in some sort of rapid cycling, mixed state, bipolar purgatory,\r\n  painfully aware that the world is going on around me, but unable to get off\r\n  the sofa, until the burning urge strikes me to buy lots of soda and tacos, and\r\n  learn to play a few songs on the guitar, even though I can only play a G7\r\n  chord (for now), until 3AM when upon mastery of \"Red River Valley,\"\r\n  I realize I\\\'m probably doing it all wrong, anyways, and why the fuck did I\r\n  even buy a guitar if I knew I was never going to be able to teach the\r\n  requisite contortion to my fat little fingers, which I know will just get\r\n  fatter and<img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.infoblvd.net/flansbmp/body.gif\\\" align=\\\"right\\\" width=\\\"161\\\" height=\\\"333\\\"> fatter on whatever new (hopefully more carefully thought out)\r\n  medication I happen to be prescribed, but I should be used to being\r\n  worthless...i mean fat...because I have been for as long as I can remember,\r\n  which is why at 3:15AM I must \"photoshop\" my ridiculous \"food\r\n  review column\" mug shot onto skinny bodies as motivation for losing\r\n  weight, which should be fairly simple since the second job I had to take (see\r\n  above shopping excursion) is at the YMCA, and they have a great fitness\r\n  center...oh, yeah, and a pool, because swimming is supposed to be great\r\n  exercise for eating enthusiasts like me, and WOW!!!! I realize how lucky I am\r\n  for having a job where I can eat free food once a week and people stop me at\r\n  the grocery store to tell me how much they love my column and \"those\r\n  stuffed burgers at The Country Kitchen sound absolutely delicious and I can\\\'t\r\n  wait to try one,\" but they don\\\'t realize that I only review paid\r\n  advertisers so I can\\\'t ever say anything bad in my column, even if the stuffed\r\n  burger is disgusting, so I just say \"it was one of the most unique\r\n  burgers I\\\'ve ever eaten,\" and equally-fat-grocery-store-lady gets\r\n  suckered into wasting five bucks on a bland piece of crap when she could have\r\n  invested that cash in a few issues of a \"real\" newspaper and she\r\n  might actually learn something that doesn\\\'t involve the compulsion to stop a\r\n  complete stranger in the produce section to talk about crappy food...</font></p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><font face=\\\"Arial,Helvetica\\\"><font size=\\\"1\\\">i do not hate\r\nmyself</font><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\"> <font size=\\\"2\\\">i do not hate myself </font></font><font size=\\\"3\\\" color=\\\"#00FF00\\\">i\r\ndo not hate myself</font> <font size=\\\"4\\\" color=\\\"#FFFF00\\\">i do not hate myself</font>\r\n<font size=\\\"5\\\" color=\\\"#FF00FF\\\">i do not hate myself</font> <font size=\\\"6\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">I\r\nDO NOT HATE MYSELF!</font></font></p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier\\\"><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"Honey?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Yes, dear?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"Why can\\\'t you just snap out of it?\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Fuck you!\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"I mean, I get upset all the time, but then I just snap out of it.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"Up the ass.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">\\\"You\\\'re starting to make <b><u> me</u></b> crazy.  I don\\\'t think I should come around for a few days...until you feel better, you know...so that you don\\\'t bring me down or anything.\\\"</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\\\"With a steam locomotive.\\\"</font></font></p>\r\n<hr width=\\\"30%\\\" size=\\\"8\\\" color=\\\"#800080\\\">\r\n<p>I\\\'m still waiting for her call.</p>'),(1670,'<p>\r\nSome people are \\\"tits people,\\\" others are \\\"ass people.\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nMe - I\\\'m all about the stomach.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBeyonce is not only too bootylicious for me, but she is far too tummylicious for me too.\r\n</p><p>\r\nDoes anyone else have weird thoughts about blowing raspberries on that tummy of hers?\r\n</p><p>\r\nApparently, my boyfriend can tell when I am hungry because I walk up to him and expose my stomach. \r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1670_1.jpg\' >\r\n</p>'),(1671,'<P>hi,</P>\r\n<P>i am austin swinney and i am not going to burning man.</P>\r\n<P>while me and the rest of san francisco (yeti, ix, and wb) are here next week all by our lonesome, we will do the following things:</P>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>drink at bars\r\n<LI>chase skirt\r\n<LI>hop s5\\\'s fence and go hot tubbing\r\n<LI>chase skirt\r\n<LI>watch movies\r\n<LI>eat good food\r\n<LI>shower when ever we want, maybe even together\r\n<LI>chase skirt\r\n<LI>turn on the airconditioning when it gets hot\r\n<LI>cap fool ass suckas\r\n<LI>do nothing\r\n<LI>in air conditioning\r\n<LI>sleep on beds\r\n<LI>chase skirt\r\n<LI>yawn\r\n<LI>drink beer\r\n</UL>\r\n<P>...and other things like that.  we will have lots of fun in comfort and think of our burning friends who are high, crashed, sketched out, dirty, sore, happy, sexed, dazed, wandering, biking, dirty, hot, dirty, hot, building, burning in the desert.</P>'),(1672,'two worlds encompassed<br>\r\nin twenty four hours, my<br>\r\nhighest, my lowest<br>\r\n<br>\r\ntwo words finally<br> \r\nsaid, \\\"no passion\\\", its over,<br>\r\nsoulmates gone separate ways.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni have never felt<br> \r\nso disenchanted, so dead<br>\r\nand my face shows it.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni maintain the farce<br>\r\nof hope as its all i have<br>\r\nwhat i need is sleep<br>\r\n<br>\r\nor a chance to talk<br>\r\nand make sense of this earthquake<br>\r\nthen i\\\'ll understand.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n'),(1673,'<p>\r\n<em><small>apologies to those who are sick of burning man. this is the first topic i\\\'ve been moved to write on in ages.</small></em>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npeople often ask why i don\\\'t go to burning man. usually i answer \\\"it\\\'s not my scene\\\". that reply isn\\\'t very satisfactory to either party.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went in 1997 and, while i had a pretty nice time, it certainly didn\\\'t change my life. however, it did shock and confuse me. i remember looking out of the rv window, seeing naked people everywhere, and feeling an adrenalin rush and the beginnings of fear. it was certainly a challenge to my traditional values, such as they are, and a big yellow highlight marking over my self esteem issues. it also immediately inserted doubt in my mind about the fidelity of my lover. with all those cool, artsy naked people around, i was definitely one of the less fulfilling and attractive options.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat experience also, surprisingly, made me feel like an outsider. i often felt unwelcome at the camps of people who are my best friends back in the city. it seems as if the cliquishness of the city folk is exacerbated in the desert, altho i don\\\'t think that\\\'s the whole answer. i think perhaps people were offended that i didn\\\'t have a schtick, but just walked around observing others\\\' schticks. since that time i\\\'ve gathered that \\\"participation\\\" is expected and that spectators are not welcome. pardon me, i thought i was just going to hang out with a bunch of my friends in the desert.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsince then, when burning man time rolls around, i feel a mixture of elation and sadness. i look forward to the quietness of an emptied san francisco, but i miss my friends and know that what they experience on the playa is a very important part of their lives that i\\\'m unable to share with them. i recall the breathtaking sunsets; anonymous fireballs and evil carnival music; a ballista firing tiny macintosh computers into the sky. but then i imagine ex-lovers enjoying new partners. i remember the shockingly public flaunting of intimacy. i reckon some things are best left to memory, and some experiences should not be shared. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe most useful thing about burning man is that, even though i don\\\'t participate in the event itself, its influence is felt even here, in xep\\\'s basement, far from the playa. in trying to understand my need to avoid burning man, i find myself in a tag team steel cage match with my demons. a lot about me would need to change before i could be comfortable going back. it\\\'s hard to imagine burning man having a positive effect on my low self opinion. jealousy and egotism are inconsistent with the vibe. while lack of self control is a hallmark of the event, it\\\'s meant to be the exception, rather than the norm.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have no conclusions to share. my mission is not yet complete. the vc keep blowing up the bridge every night, and i have no idea who\\\'s in command.\r\n</p>'),(1674,'<p>I must confess, I dream in swinney.org.</p>\r\n<p>I thought that I was not the only one. But noone I have consulted has been able to corroborate. I dream in \\\"title/blurb\\\". &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;.  wax on/wax off. my subconscious is so impressionable. so pr-egg-nable. my rational shell is a weak fortress.</p>\r\n<p>the night before I was to aeroplane back to californy, I tossed and turned.  I kept half-dreaming that a man I knew posted a swinney titled \\\"californy rhymes with porny\\\". and in the dream, I thought  \\\"I know I\\\'m about to bust a stone cold rhyme!\\\".</p>\r\n<p> I was sitting with two lads this weekend who wanted to talk about why they were sexy. I volleyed between them. They are two exceptionally sexy young men, I have to say. </p>\r\n<p>One of them has a nickname that rhymes with the River Styx. That alone is sex-points. I find him to be rather forthright. Honesty is sexy. Earnestness. As Nick Cave said, in a world of men with big mouths and no guts, I get wet for the ones with that certain earnest quality. Well, Nick didn\\\'t say the second part.</p>\r\n<p>I am so pleased to be back in northy californy<br> where the grass is brown and the girls are porny. <br>580 is doing its business, the animals are keeping me sleepless, and the world is full of promise. s. It just makes me want to belt a nonsensical tune on the rooftops of oaktown.</p>\r\n<p>huevita rhymes with senorita. <br>\r\negg rhymes with peg-leg.<br>\r\nvaginismus rhymes with christmas<br>\r\ncaliforny rhymes with porny</p>\r\n<p>I just know I\\\'m about to bust a stone cold rhyme. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1675,'Well. Here I am in the big city, and school has started. I have lived in several places in my life, beginning in lovely Dillon SC, moving to Destin FL for a while, back to D-Town, then Destin again, then Norfolk during my time in the Navy. Norfolk is the largest city in which I have lived. After that, Myrtle Beach, then Florence (1st college attempt), then MB, back to Florence (2nd college attempt, successful), and now here I am in Columbia. It\\\'s not a big city, compared even to Charlotte or Savannah it\\\'s kind of small, but the school is what I want to talk about.\r\n<p>\r\nAt Francis Marion U, there were something like 2500 daytime students. You could be pretty sure of seeing the same people outside every day, and not of course just the ones in your class. This created a strange high-schoolish atmosphere, like you could feel people sizing you up, deciding where you fit in, what you were, <i>judging</i> you. Just like HS, by the way, the cool kids hung out by the drink machines. That really cracked me up. I wasn\\\'t your typical student either, I mean I look like I\\\'m 20 or so(24 actually), but I didn\\\'t go to frat parties, or the shitty dance club in town, or any of it. I tutored kids in the writing center, and played guitar in the jazz band. You know, I wasn\\\'t in a clique, and NOT being in some kind of clique was the exception. Here at USC, with it\\\'s 20K+ students, it isn\\\'t like that at all. I can feel this warm blanket of anonymity, people don\\\'t look you in the eye here like they did there, they don\\\'t care, they\\\'ll probably never see you again anyway. I love it. What does that say about me? I don\\\'t know, and I couldn\\\'t really give a fuck less. That\\\'s me right now. That\\\'s what makes me feel good.'),(1676,'<PRE>username, password, please.</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\ni type these titles in and i really don\\\'t have anything to say about them.  this time the cursor blinked for me to type my name, i put austin.  motor habit and i typed my password.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthere are usernames and passwords for everything i do these days.  i cant go putting in a new original password for every site i visit requesting one, so i usually use the same one.  usually <B>aswinney</B>, not because it is cool to me.  you know why, i cannot remember my username much less my password if it strays from aswinney.\r\n</P><P>\r\nover the years i have had these usernames:</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\naswinney\r\naustin_swinney\r\nrobotec9\r\npinkhoody\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i think that is it.  as for my passwords, i really only use one.  it is a last name stamped on the sidewalks around austin.  a side walk manufacturer.  for the sake of this essay, let us call it johnston.  if the site is of little security importance:</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nusername: aswinney\r\npassword: johnston\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>aswinney is for large sites where:</P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>username: austin</PRE>\r\n<P>is most likely taken.  i used to try to get austin, or swinney.  but then i realized that as i said earlier, it is best to stick to the one universal username that is most likely available:\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>aswinney</PRE>\r\n\r\n<P>the password, if i want a level of security then becomes some combination of johnston and numbers or punctuation.  for example:</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n%john@ston!\r\nor \r\nj0hn5ton&\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>something like that.  although there are a few people on this planet that know my keyword like the bmg, who know which street i use, the combination of numerics and such is enough to keep them guessing whether or not i chose a different street name, like Alexandria, the street i lived on in los angeles, or Dolores, the street i lived on in san francisco.  and with each try, the cracker will have to consider how long they are willing to exhaust key combinations on that one supposed word before they have wasted enough of their time.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthen there are other passwords that i might use just because they make no sense and i have them burned into my psyche, like my old university of texas computer username ifko337, to which the password was s8e5bt3a.  it took me a semester to be able to remember those, but now i will never forget them.</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nusername: aswinney\r\npassword: s8e5bt3a\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\nmy point is that although i may not remember what password i use, i dont make up new ones.  and if i do use and original, it is a variation on an old theme.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni have friends that are fairly predictable.  two girls only uses sex terms, or gender clarification, or anatomy of self. one friend uses an emotion much of the time.  i am not sure what it means to him.  i ponder  if it is meant to remind him what he wants out of life.  like for instance, that he seeks, or that he should seek it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand, there are always those friends who just pound the keyboard for a password like:\r\n</P><Pre>\r\npassword: jsh8ck3w*$h3w\r\n</Pre><P>\r\ni hate those passwords.  they always remind me of the biology teacher i had in college who refused to learn his students names claiming that he only had so much space in his brain for information storage and he did not want to waste it on fleeting faces <sub>flinging feces</sub>.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni became a programmer and system administrator recently and came with it was an introduction to encryption.  keeping peoples secrets is important to them.  to this end we have md5, blowfish, ssh1, ssh2, and hashing.  all different uses.\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nusername: austin\r\npassword: <? echo crypt(\\\"streetname\\\"); ?>\r\n</PRE><P>\r\nthis server that you are reading S.O from refuses non-encrypted connections.  you have to have and ssh2 encryption on all things sending and expect ssh2 on all things coming back once a connection is established.\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>hi, my name is <? echo crypt(\\\"austin\\\") ?>.</PRE>\r\n</P><P>\r\nit is hard business to learn.  my friend qq cannot learn it.  he said it is too hard.  he has a book on encryption.  i am sure it is the technical edition.  personnaly i was happy with reading arm chair and toilet time grade reading material on encryption.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy friend bmg\\\'s dad went on a business trip to europe and had to uninstall his version of netscape because it had 128 bit encryption.  the united states does not allow the export of thing that our surveylance cannot decrypt easily.  i dont remember if 128 bit encryption is can be unscrambled.  i bet it can with lots of computerized effort.  one of the hailing trumpets of quantum computers, computers that function in part in another demention, should they ever be realized, is that they will be able to slow or halt time and decrypt information that has been scatered evenly over the most vast landscapes of <? encrypt(\\\"landscape\\\"); ?>, or gibberish.\r\n</P><P>just think of converting your entire essense into text, encrypting it and then painting it in  twelve  point font over the face of the planet, several times over even, at some extrememly high rate of encryption and then a computer phasing out of our deminsion and returning with the original text after some undisclosed amount of work that took no time at all.  neat huh?\r\n</P><P>\r\nnow imagine this enourmous string of your essense is your password carried in a key.  from a resemblence stand point, it would not be much different from mine in that we both have our essense in text as our password and we might as well be dealing with johnston, or guerrero, or broadway, or some other street name.  they are all variations on the same theme, human suffering (<i>h</I>) and triumph (<i>t</i>), encrypted (<i>e</i>) to an insane degree and decryptable in time, <i>t</i>, and space, <i>s</i>, where  e(h+t)<sup>ts</sup>=h+t.  i think my point here is that the time it takes to break one of these passwords is relative.  you might think it is a daunting problem to figure out what my admin password is, but then how many street names are there in the city of austin, and how many have names printed on the concrete?  very few, many are repeated more than once.  and maybe you wont be done in this millenium, but you will have an enourmous amount of time to think about better ways to do it.  maybe you will figure out how to jump into another dimension and do your math.</P>\r\n<P>in any event, i hope you have found this helpful to you, and i think it is almost my bedtime.  also this is dedicated to the girl i know (you know who you are) that thinks that all things conceptual are better.  good night!</P>'),(1677,'Doll me up in my bad luck, I\\\'ll meet you there....\r\n\r\n<p>I really envy people who know who they are. Or, rather, people who act as though they know who they are. Do they really know? Or did they just figure out that the key to happiness is to find something you like, pursue it, and damn everything else, at least for a little while.\r\n\r\n<p>Baby steps. I need to keep telling myself that. Accomplish things in baby steps....\r\n\r\n<p>I live the cliche, \\\"the grass is always greener on the other side.\\\" Very little that I do satisfies me. At one point in my life, I wanted to be a musician. I pursued it with a veracity that I rarely show. Eventually, I was fed up with the day jobs I had to have in order to support myself, so I went back to school and got my bachelors degree so I could get better paying jobs. In the process, I lost my passion for playing music.... But now, I envy those who are pursuing music....\r\n\r\n<p>I was unemployed and had all day to do virutally whatever I wanted. I wanted nothing more than to be employed. Now that I am employed, all I want is to have free time to do virtually whatever I want....\r\n\r\n<p>Stay focused. I need to keep telling myself that. Stay focused to accomplish things....\r\n\r\n<p>Our society is such that one almost has to specialize in something to become adequately skilled (in relation to others). I was practicing between three to six hours a day, and I was an adequate musician. Hardly making a living, but I was half-way decent. However, if I were to dedicate any less time to that endeavor, I would gradually lose ground and become amateurish.\r\n\r\n<p>My latest endeavor has been computers (in general), which probably requires more time and dedication than music did. And, of course, I developed new desires while attending school. Things like photography, writing, drawing, etc. So, I really haven\\\'t put as much time into learning strong computing skills. I also haven\\\'t been able to dedicate the time necessary to be a good photographer or a writer or an artist or whatever. And, of course, if I were to try and make a profession out of any of the above list (with the possible exception of programming and its ilk), I would be dedicating a lot of time towards something that would barely be able to support me, which puts me back in the place I was before I went back to school...\r\n\r\n<p>Choose. The worst thing I can do is to not choose to do <i>something</i>. Choose it, do it, then just add it to my bag of tricks....\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t have a bag of tricks. I used to. Do you know what I wish someone would have shared with me 20 years ago? You can make a decent living from any one class that I took in high school (of course, with a college degree behind it). For example, right now, I am making a decent living as an editor. English class. All I do is correct grammar and punctuation. And I wasn\\\'t even a good English student. If I had known this back then, I could have focused on any one of my favorite classes and turned that into a college major and then a job. But no one told me that English class was actual job training.\r\n\r\n<p>Realize that nothing is forever and I have the power to change. I don\\\'t have to do what I am doing forever; I can do anything I want....\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t think I do well with choices. Perhaps it\\\'s something fundamental in my psyche. I think I may second guess myself too often. The more I learn, the more I realize that there are no fundamental truths; nothing is always correct; it\\\'s all a matter of perspective.\r\n\r\n<p>This is an enlightening thought, however it is also paralyzing. If everything I choose is not correct, why choose? Or, if everything I choose is correct, why choose?'),(1678,'<p><img src=\"http://achtungbaby.org/post/favegame.jpg\"> </p>\r\n<P><font color=\"#CC0033\">At 25</font>: Location, Austin. <br>\r\n  Book: Favorite Game. <br>\r\n  CD: .<img src=\"http://194.134.35.3/qqwe.macdonald/img/garbage.jpg\" align=\"absmiddle\"><br>\r\n  Tv Show: <a href=\"http://www.hbo.com/sixfeetunder/\">Six Feet Under. </a><br>\r\n  <br>\r\n<P>A fun little birthday for me to remember is my 18th. \r\n<P> Eon took me out for dinner. She got me \"The \r\n  Wall</a>\" CD as a gift. \r\n<P> She stayed with me until late, She had school the next day. \r\n<P> So I wrote a cheeky note for her teacher, who had been my teacher the year \r\n  before. \r\n<P>When I turned 21 I did not go out to drink. Rochelle Rochelle was the only \r\n  person to call me.\r\n<P> Two years ago, Austin suprised me by wanting to throw me a party. Him and \r\n  BMG were very exicted to do it. I had not had a party since I was 10 or something. \r\n  It was quite <a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=awesome\">awesome</a>. \r\n<P><font color=\"#CC0033\">\"I\'m still alive, I\'m still alive, I\'m still alive.\"</font>'),(1679,''),(1680,'<P>eventhough i\\\'m going deaf, i listen and listen and\r\nlisten to music.\r\n</P><P>\r\nit can be anything even the crap percy plays.\r\n\r\n</P><P>music was my mainstay for so many years, the\r\nhopelessly cheasy overwrought stuff of teen dreams was\r\nthe only way I let myself get worked up and feel\r\nanything i high school.\r\n\r\n</P><P>i would sit in my basement room with the walkman\r\nheadphones plastered to my teenage skull, thumping\r\nthumping-- and write bad poetry.\r\n\r\n</P><P>music was like the bridge, the first expressive thing\r\ni encountered on my way to expressing myself.\r\n\r\n</P><P>sure i got sick of the gimmicks, trying to stay\r\ncurrent and hip can be infinitely annoying.\r\n\r\n</P><P>and I stopped buying new music for many years during\r\nmy final years in college. i remember buying Marvin\r\nGaye\\\'s \\\"What\\\'s Going On\\\" and then nothing else for\r\nyears-- this after weekly trips to spend my teen and\r\ncollege liquid cash.</P><P>\r\n\r\nMarvin lasted me well for those many years.\r\n\r\n</P><P>Still music matters.\r\n\r\n</P><P>I\\\'m not complex, deep into it, jazzophile, able to\r\nrecite every recording charlie parker made with\r\ncharlie mingus, but I\\\'m still moved by a clever\r\nphrase, or deep beat, I\\\'m still human.</P>\r\n'),(1681,'<P>learning to walk forward in karate is like becoming a blind man.  the level of concentration it takes will caused you to forget where you were going.  neat huh?  you should try it.  walk forward making \\\'L\\\' movements with your legs.  L to the left.  L to the right. </P>\r\n\r\n<PRE>\r\n__| \r\n  __\r\n    |\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i was told a bunch of things.  i don\'t remember much.  i remember the movements better than the names.  the names were:</P>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>upper block\r\n<LI>lower block\r\n<LI>thrust kick\r\n<LI>snap kick\r\n<LI>round (\"the coffee table\") kick\r\n<LI>bow\r\n</UL>\r\n<P>i learned some other things too about stance.  the woman that showed me these things was cool.  i can\'t remember her name.  i\'ll add it later.  after class i asked her about the scheduling and if it was alright to miss a day and make it up at a different class time, like miss a monday 6pm and catch a tuesday 8pm?  \r\n</P><P>\r\nshe said, \"no problem.  that is one of the cool things about this dojo is that it is so flexible.  we call those socials.\"   \r\n</P><P>\r\n\"cool,\" i said, \"me an my social life, you know?\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\"yea,\" she said, \"or the waves were good.\"\r\n</P>\r\nthe waves were good, mister swinney.  yes i am sure they are.  santa cruze surfer excuses are the best.</P>\r\n<P>i realized the first day i went to orientation at the dojo that this karate thing was going to get in the way.  it is a huge commitment.  they want you to buzz off if you do not want to reach blackbelt.  that is four years usually.  at that rate i would be in santa cruz four years from now.  i highly doubt it.  me in one place for four years, heh.  we will see.  if the economy collapses completely and i still have a job in here while the dirty hungry masses are sleeping in my garage, then fine.</P>\r\n<P>as i was saying, the commitment is real.  already it is interfereing with my life.  this girl i know is having a red curry dinner party thursday night.  that is my karate night.  i am going to go to class tomorrow or friday to make up.  i would forget about the curry night except that i really have the hots for the cook and am hoping to get in good with her -- maybe even score.  i will put on the old swinney charm.  maybe i will bring her flowers.  i bought some <i>eaux de cologne</i> especially for the occasion.  esstraspecially, mang.  i am going to mack.  but anyway.</P>\r\n<P>i hope to keep at the karate as long as i am here in santa cruz and maybe keep it up if i go anywhere else.  i have a gym membership in glendale from the last time i tried to commit to something for the long term.  oh well.  life moves fast when you are never where you want to be.</P>'),(1682,'<p>\r\ni\\\'m sitting in the british airways IT lounge at heathrow. free use of copiers and computers. i guess this is what a free first class ticket will get you. <br>\r\ni\\\'m so excited. i\\\'m coming home. i\\\'ve been in london for one week. sharing a room with an aunt, spending every day with my parents. i just want to lock myself in my apt. sit at my computer an smoke all i want. be alone in my bedroom with my pocket-rocket.<br>\r\ni\\\'m gonna go find some food, and drink free booze. try not to think about all possibilities... <br>\r\noh, on the way here i saw <i>castaway</i>. i thought they didn\\\'t show movies like that on planes. <i> warning: this movie contains an air disaster. please refrain from watching if you suffer from nervousness</i>'),(1683,'<P>I had this \\\"person\\\" I work with come up to me a few months ago and tell me that I needed to read \\\"The Vagina Monologues\\\" by Eve Ensler. I admitted that I had heard of it but that from what I could tell from the reviews, I would have as much interest in it as I would in a manual on how to repair my transmission, as in, not much. I have never had a vagina, and do not plan on having one in the future, so as far as I was concerned, I would have a very hard time relating to the topic. (It is at times like this when I wish I had persued my dream of writing a book called \\\"The Nut Sack Papers\\\" or maybe, \\\"The Dickhead Dialogue,\\\" but alas, another dream gone unfulfilled.)</P> \r\n<P>Anyhow, this \\\"person\\\" is a screaming Liberal Whack-Job, and as such, of course considers herself very open-minded, \\\"tolerant\\\" (more on this later), and non-judgemental. I felt somewhat bad for dismissing her recommended reading, so the next day I suggested that I would read her suggestion if she would read whatever I suggested for her. Without so much as a heartbeat skip, her answer was a resounding, \\\"No.\\\" \r\nI said, \\\"And you call yourself \\\'open-minded\\\'.....?\\\" </P>\r\n<P>She knew she was trapped, and so she very reluctantly agreed to read my choice of book for her.</P> \r\n<P>For the next few days, I tried assembling an array of choices for her to read. The books on the \\\"short list\\\" of consideration were, \\\"Legislating Morality\\\", \\\"The Case for Christ\\\", \\\"The Myth of Separation\\\", maybe any one of the 4 Gospels, or maybe the first 20-30 of \\\"The Federalist Papers.\\\"</P>\r\n<P>Anyhow, push came to shove, and I asked for her copy of \\\"The Vagina Monologues\\\" so I could start reading it, and........\r\nshe backed out. She said she didn\\\'t want to go through with it afterall, that she thought I would try to \\\"convert\\\" her. </P>\r\n<P>I\\\'m sorry, but in the great theory that iron sharpens iron, and that a healthy and free exchange of ideas is essential to understanding, I thought this book exchange was an excellent idea to find some middle ground with an ideological nemesis. \\\"Convert her?,\\\" I thought. From what to what? </P>\r\n'),(1684,'<p>The animals have us on a schedule. One would think that a cat is low maintenance and wouldn\\\'t alter your  sleep schedule in any way, like a human baby does. But before 9, and sometimes before 8, it goes like this: </p>\r\n<p>one person has woken up to fernando\\\'s incessant prattling and pleading, which sounds like a dying baby cheetah wailing for its life, and feeds him a healthy portion. That person curses this animal and flops back onto the bed, the bed. An hour later, Ashley wakes up for work . Fernando plays him for a fool and puts on the wailing routine, and he pours a load into the purple cat bowl.</p>\r\n<p><b>me</b>: hey i already fed him! <br>\r\n<b>ashley</b>: oh well *yawn* it shuts him up.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b> (yelling from his bedroom, having been woken): noooo, he\\\'s sooo fat!<br>\r\n<b>me</b>: hes playin us all!<br>\r\n<b>ashley</b>: so what if he\\\'s fat, he\\\'s happy.<br>\r\n<b>me</b>: he\\\'ll get heart disease! YOULL KILL HIM!</p>\r\n<p>I already have trouble controlling my gentleman caller, who comes over on the weekends and fattens up my poor kitty for the slaughter. I think secretly he is an Indonesian cat poacher who is waiting to whip out his knife and fork and have at my juicy fat kitty! Hes got those slanty eyes. I dont trust him.</p>\r\n<p>And then there is Lola. Lola is Daddy and Uncle\\\'s Little Princess. She bats her eyelashes and giggles and rolls around and snuggles up to your chest, but all she is secretly thinking is, \\\"how can I fuck with the cat today.\\\" She drinks fernando\\\'s water and gobbles up his food, and steps back innocently with her head cocked to one side and murmurs, \\\"who. me? I just want to be friends. I <i>love</i> the cat animals.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>They square off all over the house, with me as the only referee.  lola lowers her head and crouches toward him, and he *hiss*es and walks backwards, \\\"halloween kitty\\\" mohawk in place. they stare. I scream.</p>\r\n<p> If I wasnt home, the boys would let her tear him to shreds. and then make a cheery fernando pie and drink it with some IPA and Harp.</p>\r\n<p>I dont know that fernando and lola will ever be friends. fernando is a cantankerous bastard, and lola will always be best loved. The only thing they have in common is that they are both named after pop songs. Fernando\\\'s is about war and Lola\\\'s is about love. Oh, the fictional perfection of my life.</p>'),(1685,'<P>I have two fears.\r\n\r\n<P>I fear Roaches. and I fear the Ten Commandments being real. \r\n<P>Sometimes I let myself believe that the commandments are real and Iâ€™m struck with real deep fear. \r\n<P>Sometimes I just let myself believe that Mel Brooks was right and Moses dropped one of the tablets with the other commandments. \r\n<P>Sometimes I think that if they\'re true, well then hell, some of the brothers Grimm fables are true as well. \r\n<P>Sometimes Kristianity and fear go hand in hand for me. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1686,'<p>Christmas hated Christmas, parents stressed with relationship dramatics. Dad going to buy a new color TV at Target to try to suck up to my mother.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Driving at night with my sister and father, the three magi, to my grandmother\\\'s house in Lodge Grass. We brought candy and a little decorated tree and a big bag of oranges.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>All the way my father telling a story on the way of two Indian guys who had to sleep naked together during a snowstorm to keep from getting frostbite. I forget the punchline-- something about \\\"I didn\\\'t know fullbloods were so hairy.\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<p>The stars shining over snowy lodgegrass, my uncle louis the cererbral palsy child in a manger.</P>\r\n'),(1687,'My friend and I went out to dinner at an Indian restaurant tonight.  (It\\\'s good for me to get out of the house occasionally, to remind myself that I\\\'m still living.)  When we ordered, I looked at the menu and went for the vindaloo because it said \\\"(very hot!)\\\" next to it on the menu.  I was hoping that the curry would be so hot that it would make my heart stop, that somewhere in the blistering heat I\\\'d get an electric shock to wake me up, or that somehow it\\\'d be a baptisim dish.  \r\nI wanted something drastic to come out of the curry, something to change my life.  Was that asking too much? \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBut it wasn\\\'t that hot.  It didn\\\'t even make me cough.  Apparently rebirth is off the menu in Indian restaurants, and I will have to look somewhere else.  '),(1688,'<P>Sometimes I wonder if I\\\'m the only god-forsaken individual in the Web industry who doesn\\\'t believe viral marketing is always a good thing.</P>\r\n<P>The concept of viral marketing buzzes around the fact that you can dream up a potent idea, tailored to drive sales of your product/service/whatever, and then set off to try to \\\"infect\\\" a bunch of people with it. Conceptually, they spread it to other interested folks, making everyone it touches feel the need to buy/use/develop/invest in the product.\r\n<P>This whole damned principle, in my opinion, is just another way of piggybacking on culture and yet again manipulating people into buying stuff they don\\\'t genuinely want or need.  What psychology!</P>\r\n<P>Where are the trenched, priceless moral values in all of this, I ask you, what is the common good? While corporations have us busy feeling terrifically good about everything from Tide laundry detergent to Pokemon characters, the real issues like poverty, violence and education get forced to the outskirts of colelctive consciousness, and earn an ever-reducing share of the public\\\'s malleable attention span.</P>\r\n<P>Don\\\'t get me wrong- I\\\'m not crying for hellish torment to all Capitalists.  There are some products and services out there that genuinely do enhance people\\\'s lives, in very real ways. Sadly, the majority of the products, however, do not, at least not in a lasting, long-term, redeeming way.\r\n<P>The most worrisome thing is that anyone with an eye for profit can (ab)use the technology of this \\\"ideavirus\\\", regardless of the result.  Talk about abuse of power! It\\\'s hardwired into us to absorb and transmit ideas, that\\\'s what we do as American human beings. So no matter how cynical we get, we\\\'re still susceptible. Even I got hooked on Big Brother, and it isn\\\'t always a terrible ride, believe you me.</P>\r\n<P>In our marketing saturated society where personal discretion becomes just another \\\"trade barrier,\\\" (to be broken down at all costs, of course) a dangerous scenario plays out. As harmonizing ideas like love, respect and virtue get crowded out by Deloitte, Coke and Nike... what\\\'s left to hold society together? Marketers need to realize that the system they\\\'re hacking has far more profound effects than what people are buying. It\\\'s the difference between civilization and chaos.</P>\r\n'),(1689,'<P>As I don my silver tube-top for the thirty third time, i wonder why i do this- is it to look pretty or to just catch the errant gaze of some old married man (thats all i seem to attract anyway)?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIs this why i don my silver lycra pants, also?<BR><BR>What is it about sex that so appealing? I like it as much as the next person, but for real.  Can I not create my own image without it being a feeding frenzy, a grab-n-go fest for the eyes and dreamy senses?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nall i want is to look pretty, and the prettier i look, the more i feel like shit. thanks mister businessman for ruining my day. thanks mister ego for spoiling the smile i woke up with. thanks fucking ex boyfriend for fucking me for two years and then taking off when i smiled and hinted that i was ready to take this to the next level.\r\n<BR><BR> \r\ni cant even wear this shit to work.\r\n</P>'),(1690,'<p>At night I am plagued with choices. Most of these choices involve one of the following.</p>\r\n<li>beer\r\n<li>coffee\r\n<p>If I go with beer, that means that I will probably smoke cigarrettes. If I drink coffee, I will probably smoke cigarrettes. If I were to eliminate both choices, I will probably just masturbate. Sometimes, I will masturbate after having a coffee. I will most likely masturbate after having a beer.</p><p> I guess this all depends on what I did the night before, though. If the night before I drank beer and smoked cigarrettes thus ending in a little rubby rub, then I am most likely to drink coffee the next night, smoke cigarrettes and give it a good wack. There are times where I just give myself some love without having any coffee or beer.</p>'),(1691,'<P>jerome wants to kick my ass.  I tell him to go beat off or something, but he turns around and tags me right in the ass. he and I have some kind of sordid relationship. all I want to do is kiss him.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"I am not gay I tell him, I just want to know how you kiss.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\njoanna tells me he is a real good kisser and I want to find out if it\\\'s true.  because, really, when it comes down to it, I want to be a good kisser too.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Fuck you,\\\" he says.\r\n</P>'),(1692,'<pre>[=Idle-Mod=] A piano suddenly falls on chiam, <br>dislodging moderatorship of adamajah.\r\n[=Timeout=] adam is now mod. \r\n<*adam>hello\r\n<*adam>is there anything out there?\r\n<*adam>a ping\r\n<*adam>a beep\r\n<*adam>a pulse?\r\n<*adam>we are coming together to form one giant \r\n<*adam>brain\r\n<*adam>a collective consciousness\r\n<*adam>log in\r\n<*adam>you are me\r\n<*adam>i am you\r\n<*adam>log out\r\n<*adam>log in\r\n<*adam>\\\"let\\\'s be together\\\"\r\n<*adam>i need this certain piece of information\r\n<*adam>let me log in\r\n<*adam>let me think\r\n<*adam>give me a second\r\n<*adam>...\r\n<*adam>got it\r\n<*adam>log out\r\n</pre>\r\n<p>i know that this is not an original thought by a long shot, but why do we all take part in it, but never talk about it?  conscious, but not self-conscious.</p>\r\n'),(1693,'<p>Yes, men are like this. \r\nIt\\\'s true. For better or worse, it is so. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Only when they lose interest in life do men lose\r\ninterest in such trouble. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>It really is our essence, our \\\'will to power\\\' what gets us through life.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Once a female friend and I sat outside a crowded downtown promenade in some unamed city. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>And for fun I would tell her every time there was a woman who passed, that I would not want-- If there were no consequences. And she would do the same for men she would want-- if there were no consequences.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>The ratio of my unwanted women to her wanted men was about equal.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>It is true, that men slow down. Perhaps this is an unexpected, an unwanted gift. Maybe then it\\\'s easier to get through life, to get more done but this route is never chosen.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>It is not so much that reason and higher faculties win, it\\\'s that the lower functions give up. We get old, and out of shape and tired and distracted and busy and all that-- all the while the body fades.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Arteries clog, flesh sags, lungs grow shallow and cloudy, eyes fade-- it\\\'s not pretty.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>But still somewhere inside the old man, a young man is still wanting to sample every sweet treat, dip his finger in every cake.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Many older men, still spiritually desire, though the flesh is unwilling-- men are hunters or collectors, or accumlators.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m sure i\\\'m no different.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>and yes it is annoying<br>\r\nbut it\\\'s about all we have.</p>\r\n'),(1694,'</head>\r\n<body text=\\\"#000000\\\" bgcolor=\\\"#FFFFCC\\\" link=\\\"#0000EE\\\" vlink=\\\"#551A8B\\\" alink=\\\"#FF0000\\\">\r\n&nbsp;\r\n<br>I have no <a href=\\\"http://www.cdc.net/~drjekyll/scooby/darkside/\\\">idea</a>.&nbsp;\r\nI haven\\\'t figured it out yet, I guess.&nbsp; Ok, so I am stuck at this\r\ndilemma.&nbsp; I want the world to be my playground.&nbsp; Can\\\'t it be?&nbsp;\r\nIm currently in San Francisco.&nbsp; I haven\\\'t worked in three months.&nbsp;\r\nI am not hurting.&nbsp; Its actually kinda nice.&nbsp; I like it here.&nbsp;\r\nBut, on the other side, I don\\\'t have a job and I feel like I could be hopping\r\nislands in another country.&nbsp; And that would mean giving up on what\r\nI have been working on for the last year.&nbsp; Well, not necsassarily,\r\nI can always come back.&nbsp; But in the meanwhile, there are job opportunities\r\nbecoming available.\r\n<center>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1694_1.jpg\' ></center>\r\n\r\n<p>I could take some <a href=\\\"http://www.worksucks.com/jackasscontest.htm\\\">crappy</a>\r\nadministrative job where I am doing the work of five people who just got\r\nlaid off.&nbsp; Yea right!&nbsp; I don\\\'t think so!&nbsp; Unless, you pay\r\nme for doing five peoples work...&nbsp; That will never happen.&nbsp; So\r\nby december I might don the backpack and head to Hawaii and learn the energy\r\nthere.&nbsp; Oregon is an option also.&nbsp; It has been perfect there,\r\nbut I feel that I must keep traveling.&nbsp; It is another good place to\r\ncome back to.\r\n<p>Wouldent it me nice to work for Let\\\'s Go or Lonely Planet?&nbsp; Imagine\r\ntraveling around and writing reviews for different countries.&nbsp; I recently\r\nheard from a friend of mine in the Czech Republic.&nbsp; It is beautiful\r\nthere.&nbsp; They are just past the brink of freedom.&nbsp; Hungary also.&nbsp;\r\nBoth countries are rather poor, but they are rich in culture and spirit.&nbsp;\r\nAnyway, my friend sent me a card from this little town called Cesky\r\nKrumlov.&nbsp; He took my advice and stopped my to see another friend\r\nof mine named Skippy who runs a hostel.&nbsp; Skippy is the bomb.&nbsp;\r\nShe plays guitar on her porch by the river.&nbsp; She remembers the <a href=\\\"http://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/english/world/europe/newsid_523000/523227.stm\\\">revolt</a>\r\nin Praha against communism.&nbsp; She told me a story...\r\n<p>\\\"When I was younger and a student, we had to take a test to determine\r\nwhether we went to uni or went to work in the factory.&nbsp; I scored well\r\non the test so I went to uni.&nbsp; While I was there I had a good friend\r\nwho was asked to be a spy for the United States.&nbsp; My friend asked\r\nhis parents what he should do.&nbsp; His mother was against it, but his\r\nfather said he should do what he feels is right.&nbsp; So he decided to\r\ndo it.&nbsp; But, his contacts were actually the secret police who had\r\nentrapped him.&nbsp; He lost his life...\\\"\r\n<p>This story amazed me.&nbsp; Their country has been out of communism\r\nfor only 11 years.&nbsp; If you go there, you can still feel the aftermath\r\nof suppression.&nbsp; You can also feel the <a href=\\\"http://sts9.fm/audio/7-7-01_1.ram\\\">energy</a>\r\nof rebirth and new hope.\r\n<p>So, what should I do?&nbsp; Should I stick around and see what happens\r\nhere?&nbsp; Or hould I journey to a new land and discover their cause for\r\nthe reason?\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1696,'<P>Gray rainy days. This afternoon I find solace in \\\"down by the seaside\\\", the 1994 version with piano. Almost eight minutes long.\r\n\r\n<P>I hate it when you are trying not to think of someone and an immobile object rushes them to your mind.\r\n\r\n<P>It was my bed.\r\n\r\n<P>I was reminded that I sleep alone now. I used to spoon her. I couldnâ€™t sleep that way. I could only sleep if our backs were pressed against each others. \r\n\r\n<p>Itâ€™s the lack of nudity that I miss. \r\n'),(1695,'<P>â€œAndrew Mehrtens loves it when Darryl Gibson comes inside of himâ€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(NZ rugby commentator) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThis is Gregoriava from Bulgaria....I saw her snatch this morning and \r\nit \r\nwas amazing.â€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Pat Glenn - Weightlifting commentator) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThis is really a lovely horse, I once rode her mother.â€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Ted Walsh - Horse Racing Commentator) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œHe\\\'s pulling him off! The Spanish manager is pulling his captain off!â€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Soccer commentator George Hamilton on Spain manager Luis Suarez\\\'s \r\nsubstitution of Butragueno during their world cup qualifier with Ireland \r\nin Seville, 1992) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThe black players at this club lend the side a lot of skill and flair, \r\nbut \r\nyou also need white players in there to balance things up and give the \r\nteam \r\nsome brains and some common sense.â€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Crystal Palace chairman Ron Noades, speaking in 1991) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œI\\\'ve never had major knee surgery on any other part of my bodyâ€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Winston Bennett) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThe lead car is absolutely unique, except for the one behind it which \r\nis \r\nidenticalâ€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Murray Walker) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œI owe a lot to my parents, especially my mother and fatherâ€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Greg Norman) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œSure there have been injuries and deaths in boxing - but none of them \r\nseriousâ€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(Alan Minter) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThe racecourse is as level as a billiard ball â€œ \r\n</P><P>\r\n(John Francombe) \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œIf history repeats itself, I should think we can expect the same thing \r\nagainâ€œ \r\n\r\n(Terry Venables) \r\n\r\nâ€œI would not say he (David Ginola) is the best left winger in the \r\nPremiership, but there are none better.â€œ \r\n\r\n(Ron Atkinson) \r\n\r\nâ€œHe dribbles a lot and the opposition don\\\'t like it - you can see it all \r\nover their faces.â€œ \r\n\r\n(Ron Atkinson) \r\n\r\nâ€œAh, isn\\\'t that nice, the wife of the Cambridge president is kissing the \r\ncox of the Oxford crew.â€œ \r\n\r\n(Harry Carpenter - BBC TV Boat Race 1977) \r\n\r\nâ€œMorcelli has four fastest 1500-metre times ever. And all those times \r\n>are \r\nat 1500 metres.â€œ \r\n\r\n(David Coleman) \r\n\r\nâ€œJulian Dicks is everywhere. It\\\'s like they\\\'ve got eleven Dicks on the \r\nfield.â€œ \r\n\r\n(Metro Radio) \r\n\r\nâ€œ ...and later we will have action from the men\\\'s cockless pairs...â€œ \r\n\r\n(Sue Barker) \r\n\r\nâ€œWell, either side could win it, or it could be a draw.â€œ \r\n\r\n(Ron Atkinson) \r\n\r\nâ€œStrangely, in slow motion replay, the ball seemed to hang in the air \r\n>for \r\neven longer.â€œ \r\n\r\n(David Acfield) \r\n\r\nâ€œWhat will you do when you leave football, Jack, will you stay in \r\nfootball?â€œ \r\n\r\n(Stuart Hall - Radio 5 live ) \r\n\r\nâ€œThere goes Juantorena down the back straight, opening his legs and \r\n>showing \r\nhis classâ€œ \r\n\r\n(David Coleman at The Montreal Olympics) \r\n\r\nâ€œOne of the reasons Arnie (Arnold Palmer) is playing so well is that, \r\nbefore each tee-shot, his wife takes out his balls and kisses \r\nthem...........Oh my God, what have I just said?â€œ \r\n\r\n(USTV commentator) '),(1697,'<P>I noticed right away the she was blind in one eye - the way her head was slightly cocked the whole time, and the way her hairstyle conveniently covered half of her face.\r\n</P><P>\r\nCreepy.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nI knew it was time. It had just become too intrusive, and the longer it got, the more foreign it became. Why didn\\\'t I just keep it short in the first place? Who knows. Maybe I just wanted \r\n\r\n</P><P>to\r\n\r\n</P><P>try.\r\n\r\n</P><P>Oh, no, but that can\\\'t be.  Blasted sideglances.  Creepy lady.  <shudder></P><P>\r\n\r\nTo make it worse, she had no idea what I was talking about, and in her victim-of-the-80s state, she tried desperately to accomodate my wishes.  Oh well.  I don\\\'t mind my ears too much.  Except now I gotta keep \\\'em clean.\r\n\r\n</P><P>It\\\'ll grow back. Everything always does.</P>'),(1698,'<P>thinking.  walking down haight after dinner at an ethiopian restaurant that steve \\\"desert rat\\\" five took me too, i observed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nit is cool, almost cold.  a summery san francisco fog and mist is in the air.  it is ten in the evening.  through three inches of exposed window, five feet away, an image of peering shaft of vision panoramically sweeps the inside of a volkswagon bus to reveal two figures huddled over a bong.  after a second it is gone and i am walking away curious if the couple is cozy in there shut off from the road and sidewalk by an inch of steel and minimal apolstery.</P><P>\r\nsecond observation, sine\\\'s ex boy friend kris standing in his second floor haight apartment watching television and picking his nose.  i see this through a large signature bay window with no dress on.  i stop to watch him, wondering how he is taking the single man life.  how is his friday night?  i consider going up to say hi.  i wonder if sine would be angry that i consorted with her ex boyfriend.  i thought he was an alright guy.  very nice.  i watch him for a few more minutes.  he is watching television.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni avoid a few couples and head back to ix\\\'s where he is not, then move on to  my car feeling cold and lonely in san francisco.  most everyone is gone for one reason or another.  burning men and bachlorettes.  i call the bed and head to oakland for the smell of brownies and egg\\\'s bed.   eggless egg bed.</P>'),(1699,'<P>I see how employers have to look at their employees...as nothings, as those replacable objects which fuel their labour. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>There is no longer a person attached to number so and so. But a talent, a trade.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I was just let go after a 2 week stay at my former job.</P>\r\n<P>\"Simple Simons\" Riverside, CA</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I put too much tuna on the tuna salad plate, I forgot the provologne in a sandwich and I dinged the side of a cake going out to a customer. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I know deep inside I was way to sensitive for the job and for Susie\'s neurotic outbursts.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>In the end in her office she gave me in cash what i would have recieved the last two days in check. She had my time card on her desk. My roommate works there, along  with every other indie kid in Riverside. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>And I like it...the bustle. The rush.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The last two days and those three mistakes were my suicide.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I recieved Kat\'s parcel yesterday after finally picking it up at my apt. manager\'s office.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"You\'re a sweet girl, I can tell from your face.I\'m sorry it didn\'t work out.\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Don\'t be a stranger...you know Tina ( my roomate) works here....ahhh, don\'t cry.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Those were the words from my former employer. How am I not to cry? I am now jobless and for the first time fired. I feel inept at everything.\r\nI could\'t make a fucking sandwhich.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Does she think of whether or not I will be capable of paying bills?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Does she think of how much I cried?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Does she think I am a person?</P>'),(1700,'<p>\r\njust got back from seeing <a href=\\\"http://www.imdb.com/Title?0184791\\\">o</a> with austin. never having read othello, i had the good fortune of seeing the film without any preconceived notions (other than the knowledge of how gruesome shakespeare\\\'s tragedies can be). what fun!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto enjoy the best parts of shakespeare stories - the villain\\\'s tangled machinations, the clever twists of fate - you have to suspend disbelief a bit. but i\\\'m more than willing to make that sacrifice when i can actually relate to the characters and setting. experiencing traditional shakespeare forces you to shoulder the burden of entering that far-off world and accepting it on its terms in order to have any chance of understanding the actual story. whereas contemporary adaptations allow you to relax and feel comfortable with the context of the story so you can pay attention to the actual ideas that are being presented.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\\\'t want movie-going and book-reading to be hard work. i just want to be engaged and have an emotional experience. i\\\'m not suggesting that i want to be spoon-fed pap or that i dislike entertainment that exercises the intellect. but energy is a scarce resource, and i don\\\'t want to be forced to devote an excess amount of it to what i consider the window dressing. altho there are definitely some times when that part is just as nice. to make a bad analogy, it\\\'s like perving a hot chick with a chastity belt. you\\\'re cool if all you want is to enjoy the view, but you are going to have a tough time using the drill.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nanyway, regarding this specific movie: i was particularly impressed with josh hartnett\\\'s performance. at one point, after he does something particularly despicable, he covers his face and his shoulders shake, but you can\\\'t tell whether he\\\'s laughing or crying. which is a fine physical illustration of the ambiguity surrounding his motivations. i also liked the way julia stiles reacts to mekhi phifer jumping her shit: \\\"if you think i\\\'m cheating on you, find some balls and say it!\\\" strap on! i often have a problem with people acting believably. most of the time actors rely on some schtick rather than thinking about what a  real person would do. or else they are just wooden. but these kids have apparently attended the claire danes chin wobbling class. bravo!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none final note: i had high hopes for the hate fuck scene but i was a bit disappointed. no tits. anybody got any julia stiles naked celeb urls?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1701,'<P>I feel shitty. There isn\\\'t anything really bothering me in particular, I just feel kind of depressed. Last night I went to see Toots and the Maytals down in Charleston. It was alright. My not-so-trusty sidekick David pussied out on me, so I had to go alone. I had already bought the tix on etix.com. So there I am, an hour and 20 min. south of Columbia, alone I might add, and I\\\'m surrounded by these children everywhere, college kids, and I\\\'m drinking, and it was just lame. \r\n<p>\r\nSo I leave at midnight, it\\\'s just too crowded and hot. I did get to touch Toots, I love that guy.\r\n<p>\r\nI had made plans beforehand to stay with my roomate\\\'s girlfriend, Nicki. She lives on John\\\'s Island, which is just outside Charleston. It was my first time at her new apartment. When I got there, she was out on the porch with her roomate Caroline.\r\n<p>\r\nNicki isn\\\'t really the brightest girl in the world, and usually her friends kind of suck, or are seriously dysfunctional in one or more areas. She is a <i>nice</i> girl, and this friend thing may or may not be due to her ability to judge character; you know how some girls, when they first get to college, sort of make all their friends for the next four years in like a week, and they don\\\'t really make new ones or get rid of the old ones while they are there. Even if they are in a sorority, like Caroline is, they stick with their buddies more often then not. Anyway, there we are.\r\n<p>\r\nCaroline is drunk, but more importantly, she is what I like to call <i>crazy</i> drunk, meaning 1)way too fucking drunk and 2)acting crazier than simply being drunk would normally entail. Like crazy rambling, etc. Nicki, god bless her, did not notice that this was really creepy and disturbing. So Caroline says weird depressed things, and I\\\'m smashed on red wine, and it just starts to become incredibly surreal, and I want to say something brilliant to save her from the misery of samsara or something, but nothing comes. Then of course there is the obligatory drama call from one of their friends, and the ensuing drama, and all that bullshit. So Tyler, you may be asking, what\\\'s the point of all this?\r\n<p>\r\nThe point is, I\\\'m tired of all these second-rate same-old-played-out-story \\\"friends\\\" of mine. And it\\\'s got me down. I want to be in a band, I want to DO something goddammit. I feel like that Who song, and so I will now quote some of it for you, my loving electronic friends.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>I\\\'ve had my share of living...had my share of dying...I\\\'ve had my share of laughing...had my share of crying...I\\\'ve taken all the high roads...I\\\'ve squandered and I\\\'ve saved..I\\\'ve had my share of childhood...I\\\'ve had my share of grades...</i>'),(1702,'<p>The answers that I recieve only lead to more questions.  Big, ugly, dirty questions that slushball into fearful paranoid thoughts and silent negativity in the wrong direction.  The questions I ask are smokey and essentially don\\\'t resolve into much, I suppose.  Maybe things are my fault.  I don\\\'t know why my life is so crazy out-of-control lately.  It seems I\\\'m just along for the ride with no control what-so-ever over what is happening or where I\\\'m going.  In all reality, I was more in control of my life during those years of early highschool that I don\\\'t really remember too well due to pharmaceutical reasons.</p>\r\n<p>I just don\\\'t know anymore.  I\\\'ll be glad once I am back into the school routine next week.  That will give me some grounding.  The new job I\\\'ll start next week will also be a positive boost to my spirits.  I just wish the last night of my <b>shitty</b> job could have gone smoother.</p>'),(1703,'<P>\r\n<FONT SIZE=\\\"+3\\\" FACE=\\\"Arial, Helvetica, Sans Serif\\\">\r\nRoll up, Roll up<BR>\r\nFor the greatest show in the world<BR>\r\nMarvel at the Spider Baby<BR>\r\nDon\\\'t miss the Bearded Lady<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nOur main attraction tonight<BR>\r\nIt has the body of a spider<BR>\r\nBut the mind of a baby<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nThey keep it in a pram.\r\n</FONT>\r\n</P>'),(1704,'i did not drive home. i floated home<br>\r\nmmm<br>\r\nburning eyes / burning heart<br>\r\nis what i have.<br>\r\nbut<br>\r\ncannot resist the email temptation...<br>\r\nlaughable to read.<br>\r\n?what?<br>\r\n34 responses to austin\\\'s post on \\\'the MAN\\\'<br>\r\nblah, blah, blah<br>\r\nyou can label anything if you desire it<br>\r\nbut what\\\'s the point?<br>\r\ncan we not just forget about all that crap for once and have some fun?<br>\r\nif you ask me for the truth...i will probably just laugh and show you what i have on under my tutu.<br>\r\nsilence is all we need to know.'),(1705,'<pre>\r\n\r\nThe journey begins with a question,\r\na question with no answer. \r\nIt gets interesting,\r\nwhen you become both question and answer.\r\n\r\nA flickering candle in darkness and wind,\r\nblown about in the gusts.\r\nIt should go out,\r\nbut stays alight regardless.\r\n\r\nFeel the stormy wind,\r\nhear the gale.\r\nCurious candle,\r\nit always seems to prevail.\r\n\r\nPeople can try to trap you,\r\nall things that they know.\r\nYou can\\\'t catch the mist,\r\nwhere did he go?\r\n\r\nSix ways to sunday,\r\nsay what you like.\r\nWrite a thesis,\r\ncandle still alight.\r\n\r\nIt can\\\'t think.\r\nIt can\\\'t explain.\r\nIt can\\\'t do things for you.\r\nIt\\\'s just a flame.\r\n\r\nThe sun just shines.\r\nThe stars just glow.\r\nPlanes fly, engines work, computers compute.\r\nThe candle flame just goes.\r\n\r\nShow me the truths,\r\nby your flickering candlelight.\r\nI\\\'ll wait patiently,\r\nin the dark night.\r\n\r\nFor the truths you light up,\r\nare candles themselves.\r\nUnder water, in front of giants,\r\nin the mirror, in my eyes.\r\n\r\nWhere there is light i can go,\r\nno fear, no fear allowed.\r\nWhere there is light i must go,\r\nnever too proud.\r\n\r\nI believe in you candle,\r\nwhere-ever i go.\r\nThings may not be right,\r\nbut at least i can grow.\r\n\r\nMK.\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(1706,'<PRE><p>put your ear\r\nto the bark\r\nof this lodgepole\r\nand listen</P>\r\n\r\n<p>hear the deep river\r\nrunning underground</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>walk downtown at night\r\nwhen the wind picks up\r\nand the leaves fall\r\nwinter is coming</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>hear the cries of the old woman\r\nwho didn\\\'t have enough \r\nfood for her children\r\nher tears fell\r\nbecoming the bitteroot</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>hear the ringing of ram horns\r\nagainst wood\r\nram tricked again by coyote</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>those horns remain\r\nin the medicine tree\r\nyou can go there today\r\noff highway 93 down by Darby</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>heart of the monster, idaho\r\ncataldo mission\r\nst ignatius\r\npierre desmet\r\nhead smashed in buffalo jump</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>four runners\r\nwere sent to saint louis\r\ntheir breath coming \r\nin ragged gasps\r\nas they left the rim of the valley</P></PRE>\r\n'),(1707,'The grass is always greener on the other side<BR>\r\nLook over the wall, you see what they hide<BR>\r\nThe lights, the sounds<BR>\r\nSpinning round and round and round...<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nYou wish you knew what they know you don\\\'t<BR>\r\nYou wish you did what they know you can\\\'t<BR>\r\nBut you don\\\'t<BR>\r\nYou can\\\'t<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nYou just sit<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nIt\\\'s steamed, not fried...<BR>\r\non the other side<BR>\r\nThey think you think they thought you lied<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nYou didn\\\'t<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nThat\\\'s the problem...'),(1708,'<P>I was sitting in my car the other day, outside Subway in fact, and it was a fairly wet day so the windscreen and windows were covered in little droplets of rain. I usually just look through the windows to whatever\\\'s on the outside, but this time I happened to focus on the water itself and noticed the hundreds of perfectly distorted, tiny pictures, one projected on the back of each drop. It was so amazing, and I got to thinking that the real beauty of anything is in the interpretation of truth. While the building and scenery outside the window was mundane and true, the twisted reflections offered up by the trickles of rain were infinitely more spectacular.</p>\r\n'),(1709,'<P>I was sitting in my car the other day, outside Subway in fact, and it was a fairly wet day so the windscreen and windows were covered in little droplets of rain. I usually just look through the windows to whatever\\\'s on the outside, but this time I happened to focus on the water itself and noticed the hundreds of perfectly distorted, tiny pictures, one projected on the back of each drop. It was so amazing, and I got to thinking that the real beauty of anything is in the interpretation of truth. While the building and scenery outside the window was mundane and true, the twisted reflections offered up by the trickles of rain were infinitely more spectacular.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1710,'<p>this week i met essemar. i also met several other people i had only known online or who i only see once a year due to geographic constraints. burning man is, in many ways, much like a convention or a conference. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni sliced off a chunk of my thumb while disassembling camp. not a very big chunk. but it reminded me of how much i enjoyed the raw manual labor of the week: collaborating with friends and using my hands to build a temporary home, complete with entertainment and unnecessary architecture.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni was happy to see weeb and gage there. qq\\\'s box made me smile a lot. i am still craving brunch at boogaloos. emotions were a mixed bag of oblivious, ecstatic, indifferent, exhausted, and blissed out.\r\n</p><p>\r\nanyway. that\\\'s the abridged scene report. there was lots of fire and one head-on collision. the weather was calm, dusty, no storms. attendance was 17,000 (down from last year).\r\n</p><p>\r\nsome readers of this site will judge me for attending, or otherwise find burning man discussion uninteresting or irritating. all i will say is, it really doesn\\\'t matter either way.\r\n</p><p>\r\nearlier i watched the sun set behind the mountains and soon, i will watch the sun rise over downtown san francisco. i am content.\r\n</p>'),(1711,'He reaches out to grab my hand, but I pull it back.  <BR>\r\n- <I>Donâ€™t,</I> I say, and I roll over and shut my eyes.  \r\nWhy is he still here?  And why is he snuggling up to me, throwing an arm over me possessively.  I try to wriggle away, but thereâ€™s a wall on that side of the bed.<BR>\r\nGo home, I think, youâ€™ve finished the task I brought you over to do. <BR><BR>\r\nI lie still until I hear him fall asleep and start snoring, and then I grab the rest of my clothes. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nSo hereâ€™s me, being forced out of my own bed again. Next time, I think I should plan better, so that I can escape easier.  I crawl into bed with my flatmate instead.  When she wakes up, we go out for breakfast, both of us blocking out the bright grey sky with sunglasses.  The cafÃ© is all hustle and bustle, so we have to speak up to hear each otherâ€™s post mortems of the night before.  The man sleeping in my bed is graded, dissected and measured over the coffee, and a greasy breakfast to combat the hangover surfacing.  He loses many points for trying to hold my hand. <BR><I>\r\n- He was there to shag me for godsâ€™ sake; if I wanted intimacy, Iâ€™d get a cat.</I><BR>\r\nMy flatmate laughs, but understands my sentiment.  We donâ€™t harbour any delusions that sleeping around is Girl Power, but we donâ€™t waste much time with regrets either. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe get home a couple of hours later, after trawling through local street markets.  I am pleased to find that my bed is empty, though Iâ€™m less impressed with the used condom on the floor.  Still, at least he didnâ€™t stick around, wanting breakfast or conversation or confrontation.  \r\n'),(1712,'<p>\r\nwoke up in the middle of night and played with the feather that lay beside me. i had forgotten or lost interest in it a long time ago. the feather is purple and is connected to a black rod of some sort or another. dave uses it to tease me. i know what he\\\'s doing, but running after a feather is a lot better than watching him play AO all day long. I see him in the garden from time to time. smoke comes out of his mouth so i keep a distance. when he comes inside i rub up against his leg and purr so that he knows im on the same team.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nduring the day i sleep. nothing much goes on in the house during the day. the old lady lies in bed and doesnt do much. i often think she\\\'s one of us, but her shape is more like daves, and she doesnt have a very nice coat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni like to sit on the big bed when dave isnt home. a lady is there too and dave comes to visit us a lot. when i hear him at the door i jump off the best and go wait for him. he always talks to me when he gets home. immitating me and telling me im cute.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis house was so scary when i first got here. it had been the third house in six months. at the first house the people were mean and yelled at me a lot. the second house had a lot of other cats and some of them were very anti social and violent. a lot of them teased me because i was fixed. i didnt like them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni slept for a good 9 days behind the furnace. it was warm back there and some of the spiders made for good conversation. dave hates spiders and bashes them with his fists and rolled up things. he talks to their dead bodies often. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni got tangled up in the curtains today. it doesnt happen a lot but the voices in my tail were taunting me so i tried to kill them and ended up winding myself up into the curtains. dave saved me and the voices went away for the time being. what scares me is that i know they will come back...i know. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nstretching, i look at the clock. what a bright object. i think ill go sniff it.\r\n</p> '),(1713,'<BR>\r\nGood morning to you<BR>\r\nI\\\'d like to say good morning<BR>\r\nI\\\'ll have some coffee<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nperhaps a danish<BR>\r\nand watch the sun rise just now<BR>\r\na gleaming new day<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nNo New York Times<BR>\r\nnor Wall Street Journal here mate<BR>\r\nperhaps Village Voice<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI hate reading in<BR>\r\nthe morning I prefer to<BR>\r\njust watch the sun rise<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nUp over buildings<BR>\r\nup over my sleepy head<BR>\r\nwhile I sip coffee<BR>\r\n<BR>'),(1714,'<p>\r\nCan i take your name<br>\r\nThankyou, what is your complaint?<br>\r\nGenital Herpes<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWill you take a seat<br>\r\nin waiting room number two<br>\r\nWait for Doctor Duane<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRead some magazines<br>\r\nGardener\\\'s World, TV Times<br>\r\nChild is sick on floor<br>\r\n<br>\r\nGenital Herpes?<br>\r\nYes Doctor Duane that is me<br>\r\nAh, please walk this way<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThis looks serious<br>\r\nTake these tablets twice a day<br>\r\nAnd topical cream<br>\r\n<br>\r\nNo more sex for you<br>\r\nKeep your legs closed if you want<br>\r\nTo be cured this time\r\n</p>'),(1715,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1715_1.jpg\' ><p>\r\nthis is dave. dave is so cool. i met dave about twnety four years ago at a hospital in toronto canada.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme and dave both grew up together. we spent almost every waking moment together. sometimes we\\\'d get into fights, but seeing as how we both can\\\'t hold a grudge we shake hands and change the subject.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen we learned how to ride a bike, he was the one that told me id be able to do it without the training wheels. he said that if i fell the world would not end and that id be closer to mother earth. I thought it a weird thing to say, but i took his word for it and by george he was right.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think id still have a lot of unanswered questions if it were not for dave\\\'s nit pick style thinking methods. rationalizing all the dumb things i have thought about doing, he has kept me out of jail.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou knew dave had something serious to say when he started out by saying \\\"you know, back in nam...\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndave is cool\r\n</p>'),(1716,'<p>So I was drinking my coffee and fiddling on the computer, and for one reason or another it became necessary to reboot, and as I often do before rebooting, I went through a mental list of the different applications running and whether or not I had to save what I was working on, and for a moment I genuinely, seriously, naturally considered <i>whether or not my cup of coffee would disappear during the reboot</i>.</p>\r\n<p>And then I remembered that, for better or worse, my coffee existed outside the confines and caprices of my operating system, and I thought: Well, that\\\'s convenient.</p>\r\n<p>So cheers to the real world, folks.</p>\r\n'),(1717,'<p><i>dear dark,</i></p>\r\n<p>i wanted to stay longer.</p>\r\n<p>i wanted to be your shadow.</p>\r\n<p>you were too tempting. too delicious. too beautiful.</p>\r\n<p>i thought it would be nice to see it all disappear. like a dream vision.</p>\r\n<p>slowly waking to see you looking back at me.</p>\r\n<p>but then i realized that was just as bad, or worse than leaving.</p>\r\n<p>i wanted to wander away with it all still there.</p>\r\n<p>knowing the mirage existed somehow.</p>\r\n<p>something to come back too.</p>\r\n<p>is there no way to make this real?</p>\r\n<p>and i am left wondering...</p>\r\n<p>if you feel what i feel.</p>\r\n<p>Â </p>\r\n<p>i hate goodbyes.</p>'),(1718,'<pre>\r\n\r\nI come with you,\r\nI listen and think.\r\nI\\\'ll do what you say,\r\nyou\\\'ve written in ink.\r\n\r\nBut the words do not live,\r\non their own without you.\r\nA building of a person,\r\ni wish i knew.\r\n\r\nWhy write like that,\r\nwith words that don\\\'t fly?\r\nI listened to your words,\r\nbecause I wanted to know why.\r\n\r\nWhere are your words now?\r\nyou no longer write.\r\nWhat has changed?\r\nAre you no longer right?\r\n\r\nThe paper is written,\r\nsealed and delivered.\r\nI watched it go too,\r\nlittle care if it mattered.\r\n\r\nBlank paper, \r\nno words,\r\nno ink,\r\nno writer.\r\n\r\nI\\\'ll send you a blank letter,\r\nwith words that don\\\'t live.\r\nThey\\\'re just words,\r\nlike the others you give.\r\n\r\nWords are gifts,\r\ncrystals of thought.\r\ni want them to live,\r\non their own and not taught.\r\n\r\nThey write themselves,\r\njust let them flow.\r\nSomehow it works,\r\nthey just seem to know.\r\n\r\nmk\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(1719,''),(1720,'<p>A mind can be a terrible thing to obey.</p>\r\n<p>My mind is like a hyperactive child sometimes.  At other times it is like a busted car engine, pistons mis-firing.  Yet still, at other times, it is precise and exacting, like a sniper taking aim.</p>\r\n<p>When my mind runs wild with ideas that are soley it\\\'s own creation and hold little or no grounding in reality, things can get ineresting.  Imagination turns on me and I begin to suspect friends of treasonous acts.</p>\r\n<p>My mind will create all manner of absurd situations that, when looped long enough, eventually become rather plausible.</p>\r\n<p>And for this reason, at least in my own personal experience, your mind can be your own worst enemy.</p>\r\n<p>Fortunately in situations like this, all it takes is a heart-felt phone call and plenty of laughter to give your mind the kicking it deserves.</p>\r\n'),(1721,'My flatmate received an invitation to a party tonight that said â€œSwanky Cocktail Party by the Beachâ€ so Iâ€™m going with her as â€œAnd Partnerâ€.  We start our evening off with cheapish bubbles as we frock up, planning a big night.  We paint each otherâ€™s nails, fasten necklaces and reassure each other hundreds of times that yes, that outfit is fantastic darling, but no, I do prefer those other shoes, and yes, the blue looks good too. <BR> \r\n- <I>You look fabulous.  Youâ€™re on fire tonight.  \r\n<BR>- Planning on making it two for two?</I> she asks.  \r\n<BR>-   <I> Iâ€™m on a roll</I>, I say, <I>why stop now?</I><BR>\r\nI suspect I have issues with things besides false intimacy, but who wants to waste time thinking about that when thereâ€™s drinking to be done?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe tell our taxi driver, when he asks, that we are assassins.  She stabs through the eye with her stilettos, while I can snap a manâ€™s neck with my thigh muscles.  He eyes us warily in the rear view mirror.<BR> \r\n- <I>Why arenâ€™t you married?  In my day, young ladies your age were much better behaved</I><BR>\r\nWe freeze for a moment, but then he switches talkback over to the dance station, and laughs.<BR>\r\n- <I>Gotcha,</I> he says, and when he drives off, he orders us to live dangerously. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe house looks new and expensive.  There are throngs of people all over its wrap-around decks, glowing under citronella candles and fairy lights.  The party host rushes up to us, all kiss kiss, and rushes off with my flatmate, pointing me in the direction of the bar.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n The back of my neck is prickling, and so I turn around.  There is a man in a blue shirt staring at me from the other side of the room.  He smiles when he realises that heâ€™s been caught watching.<BR>    \r\n- <I>Do I know you?</I> I mouth at him.<BR>\r\n He shakes his head, no, and so I beckon him over.  But as soon as I perch on the arm of a deep leather armchair, a girl I used to know in High School pounces on me. It takes me five minutes to extract myself from her babble about people that I donâ€™t like, but eventually I am able make an exit using the excuse of needing to freshen my drink.  The party is large enough that with any luck, I wonâ€™t have to talk to her again.  But the man in the blue shirt is gone. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAlcohol and heat are starting to get to me a little, so I slip back onto the deck.  Around the front door itâ€™s still crowded and noisy, so I place a hand on the railing to steady myself, and then follow it around the side of the house.  From the back porch, I can hear the ocean two blocks away, lapping against the sand, intermingled with the laughter and music from the house.  This is a good place to rest.  I curl up on a bench feeling peaceful.<BR> \r\n- <I>There you are.  </I><BR>\r\nA figure lurches out a side door, bottle clasped firmly in hand.  Heâ€™s wearing a suit, tie hanging loose around his neck.  Oh god, itâ€™s the guy from last night. <BR>\r\n- <I>So you thought youâ€™d just go off and leave me, did you?</I><BR> \r\nHeâ€™s drunk, drunker than last night even, and his words are slurred.  I get to my feet but he continues his rant. <BR> \r\n- <I>You little bitch.  Think youâ€™re too good for me, do you?</I><BR>\r\nYes I do.<BR>\r\n- <I>You knew what the deal was,</I> I say, <I>you picked me up in a bar.  Were you expecting marriage?</I><BR>\r\nHe is stumbling closer and closer.  I donâ€™t know what to do, and suddenly I feel really unsure of myself.  This wasn\\\'t the way things were supposed to happen. Standing between me and the house, he backs me against the deck railings.  <BR>\r\n- <I>Iâ€™ll tell you what the deal is<BR>\r\n- Donâ€™t touch me.  Youâ€™re pathetic.  Piss off.</I><BR>  \r\nIâ€™m angry now, with him and with myself, because 30 years of feminism aside, this is my fault.<BR> \r\n<I>- Not so in control now, are you?  Come on, you little tease. You were gagging for it last night.  Have another drink.</I> <BR>\r\nI stare at the bottle in his hands and start to feel sick.  But maybe itâ€™d be easier, if itâ€™ll make him go away, and stop pressing me into the railing, remove his hand from my breast and get his breath out of my face.  Maybe I owe him anyway.  I close my eyes.  <BR>\r\n- <I>There you are.</I><BR>\r\nI open them again.  The guy in the blue shirt steps out of the house, comes up to me and wraps his arms around my shoulders.  Mr Last Night is too confused to say anything.<BR>\r\n- <I>Iâ€™m sorry we fought, Darling,</I> he says to me, stepping into the role of boyfriend and protector.  <I>Letâ€™s never do it again.  So whoâ€™s your friend?</I><BR>\r\nHe introduces himself to Mr Last Night, and they shake hands. <BR>\r\n- <I>Oh yeah, your cabâ€™s here, Mate.  I donâ€™t think heâ€™ll wait long, youâ€™d better hurry.</I><BR>  \r\nHow do guys do that? <BR> \r\n- <I>Thanks,</I> I say, when Mr Last Night has staggered off, <I>I appreciate it.</I><BR>\r\n- <I>No worries.  It looked like another conversation you didnâ€™t want to be having.</I>  So heâ€™d noticed the old school friend too then. That wasnâ€™t quite in the same league, but still.  <I>Besides, I always fancied myself to be a knight, or a cowboy, or something like that.</I><BR> \r\n- <I>I never want to be a damsel in distress</I> I say.  <BR>\r\nHe laughs and vanishes back into the house. \r\n<BR>\r\n...'),(1722,'<p>i don\'t guess putting myself down helps any, but whatever it takes to get my ass going.</p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1722_1.jpg\'>\r\n<p>Things are good over here in my world. I have school to keep me busy, and educated. plenty of 19yr olds to chase, and some good friends to keep me rollin along. Yet, I am constantly battling depression. About a month ago, I took up jogging again - and drama and depression seemed to fade. Then last week we got some rain, and I couldn\'t get myself out of the house to run (it was raining hard). Yesterday I cougt myself bruding, again - and I says to meself \"you need to go run.\" Thats what I did, and I felt much better.</p><p> Later that night I was traveling south to my uncles house to fetch my luggage left in his SUV, and while driving down I-35 I began to cry, uncontrollably.  I was thinking about where my life is at, where is it going, who am I, all that. I walked into my uncles house, greeted him, and then proceeded to discuss family matters... We retreated to the top deck over looking downtown austin, and started a long conversation about my life, my uncles life, and how to cope. My uncle is getting ready to turn fifty in 16 months, and he explains to me that he still goes through the same things even at 48. Relieved, but not relieved. It felt so good to talk to someone about this, on top of exercising, on top of writing about it, which is exactly why i am sharing this now, cause it makes me feel better to share it.</p><p>no point to this, no real story... just felt like talking to open minds. Time for bed, now. Tomorrow there are things to be done in my life, but for now I am going to enjoy what I have right now. One foot at a time.</p><p>also, tomorrow, I am going to wear my favorite cowboy shirt, the white one with the blue flowers (it makes me feel sexy.) and <a href=\\\'http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=656\\\'>red panties</a>.</p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1722_2.jpg\'>'),(1723,'<p>She knows <br>\r\nI know she knows<br>\r\nBut<br>\r\nDoes she know that i know that she knows?<br>\r\n\r\nI know<br>\r\nShe knows i know<br>\r\nAnd<br>\r\nI know that she knows that i know?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo <br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf i know that she knows that i know, and she knows that i know that she knows...<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThen<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhy are we not speaking?</p>'),(1724,'The first rainfall of the summer was last night.  Keira walked at a moderate pace, with each step carefully measured out.  Her hiking boots pressed down in the mud encapsulating the sidewalk, leaving wet, brownish footprints in her wake.  The rain was falling at a slant now, and some of the droplets caught on her eyelashes, blurring her makeup across her washed out face.  She looked at the few lifeless trees lining the avenue, trapped under viscous looking blankets of wetness.<br><br>  \r\nKeira tried to swallow, but found her throat suddenly and ironically dry. She clenched her fists, which were inside her damp, fleece jacket pockets, but it happend anyway.  Her mouth quivered undetectably, and a solitary tear slid down her cheek.  The skin beneath the tear\\\'s trail became colder than the rest, and wetter still, especially with the biting wind and downpour whipping across it.  She was about to bring up a hand to wipe the single saline drop away, when a stream of collected raindrops streamed from where they had collected on her delicately plucked eyebrow, sweeping the tear down towards the end of her nose.<br><br>\r\nSometimes that\\\'s all it was- a single tear.  Keira didn\\\'t cry very much.  She didn\\\'t allow herself.  When it felt like tears were coming, she gritted her teeth and told herself to cease.  Sometimes, though, when she just couldn\\\'t take it any longer, she let herself go.  She would cry for five or ten or twenty minutes straight, and then clean herself up.  But there were also times like these, times when just one tear fell.  At these still moments, it seemed as if she was crying more than if she was no-holds-barred bawling.<br><br>\r\nKeira sighed. Instead of walking on the roadway, she stepped over the curb, the sidewalk, and paced towards the park. She made her way across the manicured lawn, luxuriating in the rare feel of grass beneath her light steps.  It was just a plain, grassy park, and it was quickly becoming mired in the downfall.  Keira looked up at the urban, night sky, which was shrouded in dark clouds of gloomy rain.  The stars looked like thousands of diamonds scattered all over a velvet blanket.  The puddles, glimmering below street-lights, stood out against the night and she stood very still, gazing at their silvery surfaces.<br><br>\r\nKeira narrowed her eyes, looking closely at the falling spheres of rain, trying to catch them in mid-fall.  They seemed to be making patterns, shapes even, in the air.  She blinked hard, but shapes were still emerging from the darkness.  A figure soaked with precipitation and shiny-wet leaned forward, and gently pressed his lips aginst hers, placing a hand in the middle of her back.  Keira, not exactly sure what to do, started to kiss back, when all of a sudden, the shape was gone.  She looked around, but the park was startlingly deserted.  She frowned, and turned downtown, again, the direction her trek had been going to begin with.<br><br>\r\nKeira continued her solitary march.  '),(1725,'A path of many years<br>\r\nChallenged by delight<br>\r\nTo eventually finish<br>\r\nThe polishing of life<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe each chose the journey<br>\r\nBeginning to end<br>\r\nScant promise, we hurry<br>\r\nTo mark the spot<br>\r\nOur End<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhen it is over <br>\r\nWill we know where we stand<br>\r\nSome happen chance planned retirement<br>\r\nAmid the endings of others<br>\r\nEach life a flicker of moments<br>\r\nThe swan dance of life<br>\r\nA performance for one another<br>\r\nReaching some peak of grandeur<br>\r\nBefore fading away<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIts not the beginning <br>\r\nNor even the middle<br>\r\nThat matters<br>\r\nTowards the end where everything happens<br>\r\nThat matters more than life<br>\r\nSome peak early, but OH so bright<br>\r\nWhile other hum in coherent light<br>\r\nSome decide when or how, the age and way<br>\r\nWorn bold and proud<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI thought 20th century Earth <br>\r\nWould make a good time<br>\r\nUsing a whole as matter of fact<br>\r\nDid make it sublime<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo living the puzzle made of questions<br>\r\nDeciphering the words of never time<br>\r\nFind the right words to end a rhyme<br>\r\nJust some of the quests to begin and end<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOf all the choices just one would due<br>\r\nAll the rest spend in holy bliss<br>\r\nLike all the rest, Each moment<br>\r\nTicked away in life size chunks<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThey\\\'ve all been done a gazillion times<br>\r\nThe end for me is home again<br>\r\nNo matter where I have been<br>\r\nIts a nice place plain and simple<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf you like grace, art, and the temple<br>\r\nLacking disgrace is no incentive<br>\r\nI\\\'d gladly show the way<br>\r\nIts about integrity<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI picked an old favorite<br>\r\nSome God and some glory<br>\r\nTurning the light on<br>\r\nMakes such a great story<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIt just seems you never peak<br>\r\nThat damn light is so bright<br>\r\nThe quest of course is to<br>\r\nfind our way back<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWith no door in sight<br>\r\nI just know its right around here<br>\r\nAnd it feels just to my right<br>\r\nI wonder if someone around me might open the door<br> a bit<br>\r\nThe key it seems lies inside<br>\r\nOf course the one I have doesn\\\'t fit<br>\r\n\r\n'),(1726,'I head back to the bar once my heart slows down, fear adrenalins pumping too late after I needed them, as usual.  I take my vodka straight and go and sit on an empty couch.  Moments later, my flatmate collapses down next to me.  We hug and toast each other.  <BR>\r\n<I>- Guess who just showed up here?</I>I say to her, staging a laugh<BR>\r\n-    <I>Who?  <BR>\r\n- Mr. Last Night.<BR>  \r\n- No!  How?  Why?  What\\\'d he want?  What\\\'d you say?<BR>\r\n- I dunno - maybe I mentioned this party to him last night.  He wanted another round.  I said no. </I><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s easy to gloss over the panic I\\\'d felt, rough hands pushing me into the hard wood of the railing, fierce eyes.<BR>  \r\n- <I>Why did you sleep with him anyway?  You deserve better.  You\\\'re so special. </I><BR>\r\nWe do this a lot, get very sentimental towards each other and gush about how much we love one another.<BR> \r\n-  <I>You deserve someone who\\\'ll treat you right.  Someone that you love. </I>She strokes my hair back from my face in a motherly way.<BR>\r\nI laugh<I><BR>\r\n- You old sentimental drunkard.  This is one posh party, Darling<BR>\r\n- You\\\'re changing the subject </I>she says, <I>what are you so afraid of?</I>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBut she knows, and so she changes the subject herself, suggesting we go dance. Amidst the throngs of people all moving in time, it\\\'s easy to forget the fear, and time speeds by.  But my body won\\\'t let me forget the amount of alcohol it\\\'s processing, so I go to find the bathroom.   \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s easy to find because of the queue of people outside it, trailing down the hallway. I lean my head against the wall, determined to stay awake, to stay upright.  Finally I hear the flush and fumble with the door lock that signifies my turn.  I slam the door behind me, twisting the key.  A moment to myself, finally.  The bathroom is pretty; claw-foot bath and wood panelled walls, and a soft pink marble floor.  Candles are lit all along the windowsills, and underneath the mirror.  I stare at my reflection for a while, smiling at myself, and then I start to pull faces and laugh.\r\n<BR><I>- You\\\'re a dork\r\n<BR>- No, you\\\'re a dork\r\n</I><BR>But then I remember the queue outside and I don\\\'t want them to hear me, so I flounce over to the toilet.  I piss in a long hard stream for what seems like hours.  My poor liver and kidneys, always working overtime with no extra pay.  I don\\\'t treat them very well.  To be perfectly honest, I don\\\'t treat anything very well.  I flush, then slump down, leaning my head on the cool porcelain toilet seat.  The marble underneath me looks so inviting that I sprawl out on the floor for a while and reflect on the events of the party so far.  The boy in the blue shirt must have absolutely no respect for me now.  I wonder why that bothers me so much.  I start to giggle thinking about how much worse his opinion of me would be if he came into the bathroom and found me like this.  Then again, he could always take advantage of the situation.  I briefly fantasise about being together on the floor, marble smooth beneath us, watching ourselves in the candlelit mirror, but reality soon kicks in when someone knocks on the door. <BR> \r\n- <I>Just a second.</I><BR>\r\nI scrounge through the cabinet looking for mouthwash, but find none. Someone\\\'s banging on the door.  Too long spent in self-analysis.  This is a party, not a vision quest.  I wash my hands, smear on some more lipstick, and I\\\'m back out into the party again.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI find Peppermint Schnapps at the bar, which is practically the same thing as mouthwash. <BR>\r\n<I>- You\\\'ve been throwing up, haven\\\'t you?</I><BR>\r\nThe bar man\\\'s obviously seen me swirling it around my mouth.<BR><I>  \r\n- Darling, it\\\'s second nature to girls my age. </I><BR>\r\nHe pours me a glass of water.  \r\n<BR><I>- Be a bit easier on yourself, okay? </I>he says.<BR> \r\nI\\\'m not sure if he\\\'s referring to the drinking or the puking, so I don\\\'t ask.  But I feel a little humbled by his sincerity, so I thank him and take my water outside.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n....\r\n'),(1727,'<p>\r\ncelery. the most underappreciated seasoning veggie in my life, has made a wonderous comeback. beating out dr. bronners, mustard seed, cumin seeds, and EVEN *gasp* SALT, it makes a breakaway in my carrot tofu soup and it\\\'s better than it\\\'s ever ever been. the crowd goes apeshit.\r\n</p><p>\r\noh celery. where have you been all my life? i caress you like a fair princess, i lay down in the mud so you can step on my head. i put little valentines in your mailbox and pretend to be happening by so i can see you open it. the air is full of love love love (when i think of love i think of a sidewalk stained with squashed plums)\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy stomach is warm and full like a kittens. i am lolling.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlife is so unbearably good when you can eat after being sick. i think my nose is starting to work again, rescueing my appetite from the scuttlebin.</p>\r\n'),(1728,'<p>\r\ncelery. the most underappreciated seasoning veggie in my life, has made a wonderous comeback. beating out dr. bronners, mustard seed, cumin seeds, and EVEN *gasp* SALT, it makes a breakaway in my carrot tofu soup and it\\\'s better than it\\\'s ever ever been. the crowd goes apeshit.\r\n</p><p>\r\noh celery. where have you been all my life? i caress you like a fair princess, i lay down in the mud so you can step on my head. i put little valentines in your mailbox and pretend to be happening by so i can see you open it. the air is full of love love love (when i think of love i think of a sidewalk stained with squashed plums)\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy stomach is warm and full like a kittens. i am lolling.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlife is so unbearably good when you can eat after being sick. i think my nose is starting to work again, rescueing my appetite from the scuttlebin. the unbearable noseness of being.</p>\r\n'),(1729,'<p>i am the wind<br>\r\nuntouchable<br>\r\ncaressing<br>\r\nyour shadow<br>\r\ni am not like others<br>\r\nyour deep breath<br>\r\nnever to be held<br>\r\nforever<br>\r\na flower blooming and gone<br>\r\nonly to be reborn again<br>\r\ntomorrow<br>\r\nnext year<br>\r\nyou see me in the mirror<br>\r\nin your eyes<br>\r\ni am not there<br>\r\njust a taste<br>\r\nof a days gone by<br>\r\nof love <br>\r\nwithheld<br>\r\ninside<br>\r\nto hold onto the dream<br>\r\nbetween waking and sleep<br>\r\ni am ever present<br>\r\nalways changing<br>\r\nethereal<br>\r\nunrelenting<br>\r\ni am beyond life<br>\r\na witness<br>\r\nto the pain<br>\r\nbittersweet<br>\r\nstained<br>\r\noblivion</p>'),(1730,'<P> After another long, confusing weekend, I have come to the conclusion that the things that I thought were going to change soon, will not.  That, however, is the story of my life. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Moments ago, I had a thought. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Around this time last year, I was introduced to a guy that I really liked.  I was remembering the first time that we had gone out alone.  He came to my apartment to pick me up, and we sat out on my balcony for a long time before we went anywhere.  Right before we left, he stopped and asked for something to write on.  He sat down and wrote what I\\\'m pretty sure was a poem, folded it up, put it in his pocket, and we were on our way. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Weeks later, he mentioned that the poem he had written was inspired by that night, or by me. He asked me if I wanted to see it sometime and I told him sure, but I never brought it up. I didn\\\'t want to be pushy because really, it wasn\\\'t going to kill me not to see it if he didn\\\'t want me to. I\\\'m just not like that. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> We broke up at the end of three months; after he becamse self-absorbed, stopped calling, stopped dropping by and just outright ignorned me for a full week. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Seven months later, I\\\'m not quite so bitter and I was never really mad. Just hurt. Here he is, wanting to be \\\'friends\\\' again. One of the few times we hung out (before I decided that he was really full of shit) he brought up the poem again. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"Did I ever show you the poem I wrote about you the first time I came over to your place?\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> \\\"No.\\\" </P>\r\n\r\n<P> And just like that, the conversation was over.  The phone rings, he gets involved in a computer game, he sees something interesting on TV... and it\\\'s all just as unimportant as everything was before. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Today, for a brief moment, I wondered what the hell that poem was about. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I was reading some personal ads, for fun; or honestly, the blurbs before the ads. Thirty characters worth of insight.  If you want more, you have to click, but I don\\\'t need to.  There are a lot of \\\"poets searching for inspiration\\\" out there lately.  Now I\\\'m curious as to what we find to be truely inspiring. Does anyone really know? </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1731,'this morning before i woke up i was dreaming about a hotel room, at south of the border i think but it wasnt a south of the border room interior, it was like a hampton inn nice clean modern bluegray room. so there i am in the room and there is a girl there, she looks like a couple of girls i know but was neither one exactly. she is folding sheets on one bed, and the other bed has the bedcovers turned down some and wrinkled. she says to go ahead and lay myself down and so i do, and even though i am on the other bed my head is somehow in her lap, and she is folding towels for us, and the room is for us, and the bed too.\r\n<p>\r\nthis makes me feel very warm and comfortable, like i am in a secret place, a special place, like under the covers with a flashlight, impenetrable, underwater underground. \r\n<p>\r\nthen i wake up.\r\n<p>\r\nafter i woke up i lay there for a few minutes, looking at the windows, which were holding back no sun at all. in the mornings it is as bright inside my room as it is outside, there are five windows in my room, and wood floors, and it feels and sounds like you are sleeping outside. i stretched my legs back over my head into the wall, and held them there for a few seconds, and let them fall back onto the bed. i thought about my dream, the ghost of which was still flying around in my head. i have my theories about what it means, but i am trying not to stick to just one.'),(1732,'The front deck has emptied, and so I find myself a seat on a bench.  For a party, I really seem to be spending a lot of time by myself. \r\n<BR>\r\n- <I>Hi.</I>  It\\\'s the blue shirt boy again.  I almost blush, thinking of my bathroom fantasies, but I\\\'ve been training myself not to show emotion for a long time. <BR> \r\n- <I>Hi again, are you stalking me?</I>  I tease. <BR>\r\n- <I>Kind of,</I> he says earnestly. <I>I feel very drawn to you.</I>  <BR> \r\n- <I>That\\\'s a good line,</I> I laugh.  <BR>\r\n<I>- You\\\'re not very good with compliments are you?</I><BR>  \r\n- <I>Sorry,</I> I say, <I>it\\\'s been a long night. Shall we start again?\r\n</I><BR>He looks straight into my eyes.\r\n<BR>-    <I>I\\\'d like that.</I><BR>  \r\nThere\\\'s tension and crackle between us.  Neither of us can move.  I can feel my skin puckering with goose bumps.  His eyes are soft, and suprisingly vulnerable.  <BR>\r\n- <I>Oh wow, sorry.</I> It\\\'s my flatmate. <I>Sorry to interrupt, but I need to borrow something</I>.<BR> \r\nThe moment is gone.  She reaches for my handbag, and I know she\\\'s going for the condoms in the inside pocket, so I take them out and hand them to her.  She kisses me on the cheek and runs off giggling.  He laughs too.<BR>\r\n- <I>Well that was one way to get rid of that tension. </I>\r\n<BR>- <I>Typical flatmate,</I> I say.<BR> \r\n<I>- Look, I don\\\'t want to sound like a real sleazebag or clichÃ© or anything like that, but do you want to... I dunno.. go for a walk on the beach or something?</I><BR>\r\nI smile shyly, tingling all over.<BR>  \r\n<I>- I\\\'d like that. </I> \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHe picks up a rug off the back of an empty sofa, and we walk out together.  There\\\'s something about this boy.  He seems so strong and in control.  I think I\\\'m drawn to him too, and I refuse to get scared by that feeling.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe don\\\'t speak for the two block journey to the beach, but once we\\\'re on the sand, we both relax as our shoes come off and we go wading.  He kicks water at me, so I squeal and sidestep, splashing him back.  Our laughter echoes around the beach, bouncing off the water and the dunes.<BR>\r\n<I>- I don\\\'t even know your name,</I> I say.<BR>\r\n<I>- I don\\\'t know yours.  You\\\'re a mystery woman.<BR>\r\n- I\\\'ve worked hard to become so, yes.  </I><BR>\r\nThat sobers our mood a little.  He spreads the rug out on the sand, and we both sit down a couple of feet apart.  This is weird; I don\\\'t feel at all in control.  If he\\\'s trying to seduce me, he\\\'s being very slow about it. I\\\'m not sure what\\\'s going on.  He seems almost too good to be true, so if the bubble is going to burst, it\\\'s better that it\\\'s sooner than later. <BR>\r\n- <I>You think I\\\'m trashy, don\\\'t you? </I> As soon as the words are out of my mouth, I regret them.<BR> \r\n- <I>What? Why?</I>  He looks genuinely puzzled. <BR> \r\n<I>- That guy out on the deck.</I><BR> \r\n<I>- I don\\\'t think you\\\'re trashy.<BR>  \r\n- But I slept with him last night, and didn\\\'t want to see him again.<BR>  \r\n- Personally, I wouldn\\\'t have wanted to see him the first time.<BR></I> \r\nHe\\\'s trying to make this into a joke, so I make a noise of frustration. <I>- I\\\'m just not a good person, I guess.\r\n<BR>- I don\\\'t believe you</I> he says.<BR>  \r\nAnd then there\\\'s silence, and we both stare at the ocean.  The moment is heavily pregnant, dilated and crowning.  I turn to him.<BR>  \r\n<I>- I just wanted to know what you thought of me.</I>  <BR>\r\nHe reaches out and touches my face, gently.  This is our first contact, and the warmth of his fingers travels through me, all over.  I stop breathing.  <BR>\r\n- <I>I think you\\\'re beautiful.  </I><BR>\r\nThere\\\'s a noise in my head, a rush of blood, a stirring of my senses, everything coming alive before I think of someone else who used to say that to me. <BR>\r\n- <I>Don\\\'t say that</I>, I tell him. <BR>\r\n<I>- Why not?</I>\r\n-<I> Say you think I\\\'m sexy. Say \\\"god you\\\'ve got nice tits\\\".  Tell me that you want to sleep with me, or that you want to tie me up and do hideous things to me, or anything at all, but don\\\'t tell me I\\\'m beautiful.  It\\\'s not fair to seduce me like that.  </I><BR>\r\n- <I>What?</I> He seems angry.<BR> \r\n- <I>Beauty suggests emotion.  Look, you\\\'ve got me.  We\\\'re here on the beach, we\\\'re drunk.  We can have sex, and it\\\'ll be great, but it\\\'s not fair for you to make me fall for you as well when that\\\'s not what you\\\'re after.</I>  \r\nI\\\'m near tears now.  Oh god, what am I doing? He stares at me for a while, and I can see he\\\'s straining to find the right words.  \r\n<BR><I>- You think I just want to shag you?</I><BR>\r\nHave I completely taken him the wrong way?  I want the earth to swallow me up. <BR>\r\n<I>- Umm. I... </I>I stammer.<BR> \r\n<I>- God, someone must have really hurt you once, didn\\\'t they? </I><BR>\r\nI nod. <BR>\r\n- <I>I\\\'m sorry</I>, he says, and takes my hand. <I> You\\\'re sexy.  You\\\'ve got lovely breasts.  And I very much would like to go to bed with you.  But I want more than that.</I><BR>  \r\nAnd then he tells me his name, and I tell him mine, and we sit in silence again, holding hands and drinking in the implications of this new level of intimacy.  \r\n'),(1733,'\\\"If you were a refugee, your life would be short, full of toil, and miserable\\\" my good friend said to me as he held open the brassy doors of my apartment with a flourish.  I grunted, sweatily, and moved my desk out; my bed and chairs, computers and stereo followed, clothes and a crate or two of records, onto which he later junked my admonishment and stacked two cpu\\\'s.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIts true- as most of us do, I own too much <i>stuff</i>.  And this is even after weeding-out what must have been hundreds of pounds of junk-n-tidbits before my move.  I live within the scattered shrapnel of my life, the collected minutia.  I\\\'ve lived the past 4.5 months in a tiny place, waiting for the lease to come available on a larger place I\\\'ve had my eye on for a year.  Finally it happened, but I had to face the inevitable scourge of moving, my nightmare, my enemy, and at a very short notice.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo, I packed all my stuff, painstakingly, in on and around my tiny, rusting car.  Surprisingly, it all fit in one load, and Toyotas aren\\\'t that big.  It dawned on me that maybe it wasn\\\'t so much that I had collected tons of junk but that I\\\'ve been living in such a tiny room for months.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Hey, I haven\\\'t driven stick in years\\\", said my friend as he helped me load my car, \\\"mind if I give it a shot on the way back?\\\".  I acquiesced, handed him the keys, and he sat there tweaking the dashboard knobs as I loaded the trunk, tied to the roof-rack, stuffed the back seat.  We listened to AM ragga while loading.  My hefting boxes was almost in tune with the flashing rhythm of the hazard-lights that he flicked on and off as police passed, allowing me to park in front of a hydrant.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe load was finally ready to go as the clock struck about 11pm.  He cranked the engine... nothing.  \\\"Click\\\".  The engine wouldn\\\'t start- too much light-flashing, knob twiddling and radio tuning.  \\\"Um, maybe I should go home\\\", he said.  Dead battery.  He walked.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nFine.  There I was, nearing midnight a block from the the West Side Highway, all my belongings in and on top of my car, my keys locked, as directed, inside my old, vacant apartment, my new apartment too far north to push the car to, even with 5 friends, much less one.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo, I reluctantly fish my phone from amidst the boxes and bags now lapping across the passenger seat and begin calling.  Jaime is sleeping off a hangover from the day before.  Saz is on her way to the club, and just got her nails done anyway.  Essemar is out getting high and probably stumbling blissfully into some sordid one night stand.  Vai is weekending in New Jersey with some college girls he met down the shore.  Damn you all.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe police cruiser passed me for the third time in ten minutes, stopping along side.  Giant flash-light in hand, the officer approached my car gesturing me to put the phone down.  \\\"You gonna have to move this car, mister\\\", he said as I smirked, trying to figure out how to explain this one. I chuckled at being called <i>mister</i> by a 50 year old NY cop.  Jaded, he frowned at me as I explained what was to him, a likely story, I\\\'m sure.  Soon, I found myself attempting to crank the engine to prove that my story was indeed sincere.<br> \r\n<br>\r\nThe officer, so surprised at my genuine predicament, offered me a ride to a nearby garage.  I accepted- after all, how often does a law-abiding citizen get to ride with the NYPD?  I sat in back, pretending I was a fugitive, a meth dealer, a crime overlord; a graffiti artist, a hustler, Jimmy Hoffa.  I almost asked him to run the sirens and lights, but that would have been a little odd.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe fortuitous man at the Downtown Exxon made $28 for 30 minutes of battery-charging after being reluctantly coerced to this alternative; he would have preferred to sell me his new $159 battery.  My whole car\\\'s barely worth that much. \\\"This is a <i>terrible</i> battery, my friend.  You car will surely not start this winter\\\".  Good.  Then this winter, I\\\'ll abandon it on the Harlem River drive and claim insurance on my smoke-chuffing vehicle.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo, a half-hour walk back from Exxon, several hours of moving, a police car ride, and an inventory of my abandoned car/stuff completed,  I managed to find myself amidst a big pile of my junk in the living room of my new apartment.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYep, I\\\'d never make it as a refugee, but somehow all this junk just seems to fit so nicely into my new place.  I think I might even have room to spare.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n '),(1734,'I am a New Zealander. NZ is not a part of Australia.  If you believe the word on the street, New Zealanders have a healthy dislike of/competitiveness towards Australians.  I hear it\\\'s kind of like the whole US/Canada thing.\r\n\r\nI don\\\'t really care.\r\nI\\\'m in love with Melbourne and Sydney.\r\n\r\nI watched two Australian movies this evening and lusted after Melborne in the first.  Funny green trams with crazy smelly people on them, St Kilda, Prahran... etcetc...\r\n\r\nThe second made me want to visit Sydney again. It\\\'s so busy and bigger than what I am used to. There\\\'s that street that I forget the name of - Josie Alibrandi runs down it in the movie (I think!?) - in Surry Hills.  All the houses are almost the same and so close together. Little wrought iron balconies.\r\n\r\nWhat I\\\'m wondering is whether my love of these places is just misdirected desire to run away from normal everyday stuff, or whether I truly love them and could one day relocate.\r\n\r\n'),(1735,'<p>Because I got lots of time before class. <br>Because the house is empty, and I can play it loud. <br>Because I am hyper at 8am. <br>Because most people don\'t like it when I play it around them. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSasha. Digweed. Nick Warren.<br> A little trance, mixed with some down tempo.<br>  Some coffee, an apple, and a banana-na-na. \r\n</p><p>dutz-dutz-dutz-dutz-dutz-dutz</p><p>\r\nIf I were a painter, an artist painter, I would be doing that right now. A big canvas, lots of cool colors that make me smile. A little red here, a little blue there. And people would look at it and go, \"hmmm its upside down.\" \r\n</p><p>\r\nAfter painting for a few hours, or minutes in my case (because i can hold onto thoughts for only minutes), I would do some more booty shakin. Maybe read a bit. A shower, and some prep time before my class. \r\n</p><p>\r\nGo to class, talk about literature with my ultra-conservative-christian-classmates and get all riled up, blood boiling and excited about something I read and liked. They of course hate anything that I like. Try and try again, I would try to show them the way of the world. I would read them this poem, slowly emphasizing each word, and they stare back at me with question marks in their eyes just as they did last week:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Without\" by Gary Snyder</p>\r\n<p>the silence<br>\r\nof nature<br>\r\nwithin</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe power within<br>\r\nthe power</p>\r\n<p>without</p>\r\n<p>the path is whatever passes - no<br>\r\nend in itself</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe end is,<br>\r\ngrace-ease-<br>\r\n<p>healing,<br>\r\nnot saving</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsinging<br>\r\nthe proof</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe proof of the power within</p>'),(1736,'<p>Don\\\'t take life too seriously. It\\\'s not permanent. You WILL die. On a long enough timeline the survival rate for everyone drops to zero. You might make it into your 70\\\'s or 80\\\'s. You might die tommorow. If you like someone, tell them so. If you don\\\'t, keep your mouth shut. Don\\\'t assume that that special person in you\\\'re life will still be there tommorow. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDo whatever makes you happy. Forget the other people. The small minded people. Does it matter what other people think? If stealing cars makes you happy then steal cars. Whatever you do, always be aware of the consquences of your actions. It\\\'s no ones fault but your own. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou are not special. You just are. You\\\'re life has no inherent meaning. You\\\'re life is what you do with it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEnjoy it.\r\n</p>'),(1737,'<b>Memory List:</b><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>Objects:</b>\r\nThe shiny red and black plaid lunch box I nervously clutched on my first day of school.\r\nThe starched white apron with the light brown appliquÃ©d puppy dogs on the bottom that I wore over my apricot cotton puffed sleeved Easter outfit in the third grade.<br>\r\nThe suffocating plastic Bugs Bunny Halloween mask with the thin elastic band which caused painful tangles in my hair.<br>\r\nThe bright yellow ceramic dish with the smiley faced sun i made in the first grade.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>People:</b><br>\r\nThe terrifying sound of coins jingling in Mr. Pottsâ€™ pockets as he ran down the hall in a fit of rage.<br>\r\nThe sweet sound of my motherâ€™s voice as she sang â€œGood Morning To Youâ€ as she came into my room each day.<br>\r\nMy little brother wearing his Roy Rogers red cowboy hat, worn cowboy boots and a leather holster with its shiny gun handles hanging out over his cloth diaper.<br>\r\nCoach McNeelâ€™s new blonde toupe riding high on his head looking as if he had just came from the beauty parlor.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>Places:</b><br>\r\nThe metal taste of the water as you dipped it out of the bucket at my Grandmotherâ€™s house.<br>\r\nThe smell of hot oil and the sizzle of catfish frying in black cast iron skillets at my Aunt Nadineâ€™s house.<br>\r\nThe chilled dampness of the Carlsbad Caverns.<br>\r\nThe sharp crunching sound of the seashells underfoot as you walk along the beaches of Captiva Island.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>Words:</b><br>\r\nIâ€™m so poor I canâ€™t pay attention, my father often joked.<br>\r\nYouâ€™re ugly and your momma dresses you funny.<br>\r\nDo you understand what Iâ€™m saying?<br>\r\nWhy canâ€™t I have it, itâ€™s my money.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>Music:</b><br>\r\nHis voice enveloped your soul as Andrea Bocelli sang to the performance of the Bellagio fountains.\r\nThe heart happy sounds of guitar played by Mark Anton fills you with joy.<br>\r\nThe blasting beat of the car driving by rattled the crystal in the antique china hutch.<br>\r\nThe sounds toe tapping sounds of swing music demands your immediate attention.<br>\r\nHouse music.<br>\r\n<br>'),(1738,'<PRE>\r\nanother small suffering body\r\nsweating in a metal bed\r\nanother swollen limb\r\nanother sweating forehead\r\nanother spasming muscle\r\n\r\nconnecting the generations\r\nin the IHS hospital\r\ncrow agency, montana\r\n\r\nthey will come at you \r\nwith morphine\r\nto wash everything away\r\nthey will offer you\r\npercocet\r\noranges\r\nblues\r\ndiladud\r\ndarvocet\r\nand olympia\r\n\r\nwhat will you say\r\nto the electric pain?\r\n</PRE>'),(1739,'<p>Dear Swinney.Org,</p>\r\n<p>Today I have spent trying to get out of the house to return a stolen car to my ex. You may know him from such web sites as myfirstname.org or allaboutme.net., where exciting chronicles of me doing damage to his property and person are detailed for your consumption. I stole his car, it\\\'s true, I have no excuse for it. I just needed to use a car to go to Costco and I didnt think he would be back for awhile.</p>\r\n<p>I didnt hotwire it, because I have a key. You see, when two people with no emotional or social boundaries live together, seperation is like pulling apart a piece of caramel. Little strands just keep hanging on.</p>\r\n<p>Having a key to another person\\\'s car or home is a tangible expression of intimacy. When I first moved out of his house, he would nag me for weeks to have a key to my place. This was his way of insuring that me moving out did not mean, \\\"so long sucker, get another girlfriend.\\\" I never got around to giving him a key, out of sheer laziness to make copies. In retrospect, perhaps I enjoyed witholding that token of connectedness.</p>\r\n<p>I got an email from him this morning reminding me to bring his fedex package when I drop off the car. Ah yes, the fedex package. I seem to remember nonchalantly signing off for it while he was in Europe.</p>\r\n<p>I can\\\'t find it anywhere. I am running throughout the house screeching and roaring like the incredible hulk. Where is it? Now I cant remember the details . Is it bigger than a baby\\\'s arm? Is it brown? When did I last see it?</p>\r\n<p>This house is a fucking vortex. Here, matter is created and destroyed. NASA has requested to conduct experiments on the anti-Conservation of Matter properties of my home. I found a brown package but it was for chi chi\\\'s Wine of the Month subscription. I sifted through the mail area and all I found was bucket loads of mail for PEOPLE WHO DO NOT LIVE HERE.</p>\r\n<p>And suddenly, as my wont dictates when it comes to Mr Bare My Bullocks Dot Com, I became angry. Did he ever ask me to receive a package for him? And if so, did I say \\\"SURE, ANYTIME\\\"?! And if so, why do I agree to such things? As is a woman\\\'s wont, I became angry with myself. Since I stole his car, I can\\\'t refuse to do a favor. There are no explicit agreements, just circles of obligation, webs of entanglement.</p>\r\n<p>Come on over and store your things. Oh sure, you can establish residency, right here in my living room. Come on over for a visit! Bring your girlfriend/lover/best friend/crew/band.</p>\r\n<p> I love people. Im a people person. I want to be close. That is one of my spiritual weaknesses. I want to be inside you. I want you to live all over me. When it\\\'s over and done, we wont remember whose laugh is whose, whose keys fit in which honda, whose rice maker cooked up our love, whose pet peed on our pants, whose jokes to take credit for, and oh my gawd whose STUFF IS THIS?</p>\r\n<p>fuck.</p>'),(1740,'<P>\r\nDear Friend-\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou taught me how to hold my coffee correctly.  Right hand holding mug, handle away from grip, cigarette between pointer and middle fingers. And although you like The Pallbearer and The Mexican, and you tend to ignore me in public, I am still writing you, because I like the way you write about my roommate. And, no, I haven\\\'t shown her any of your writing.  And, no, I am not from Wisconsin. And, no, I am not easy.   \r\n</P><P>\r\nSee, I told you I\\\'d write something. Satisfied?\r\n</P><P>\r\n  Sincerely, Me\r\n</P>'),(1741,'<P>Didn\\\'t you know that?  (I fib:  It\\\'s just Soup Night at Chez Matt.  [Make that Chez Matt\\\'s Mommy and Daddy, chiefly because it used to be Meatloat Night before I moved back in.])</P><P>Moorish Gazpacho (from Morocco).  First, in honor of Morocco, take your sweetie up to The Opium Chamber and burn some Ketama (or any of your finer blends from anywhere, really.  Nothing special about Morocco.)  (The Opium Chamber is our private club on top of the house.  It used to be a captain\\\'s walk.  I screened it in and put a Parent Alarm at the foot of the stairs.  No opium is consumed up there [that I know of]:  We Gen X thirtysomethings are just hip like that.)  Burn some more and have a Kirin or three.  Now you\\\'re ready for the kitchen.</P><P>Heat 2 T. olive oil in a large saucepan over medium low heat.  Saute 1/2 c. leeks or green onions, finely chopped, with 1 stalk celery (including leaves), 1 carrot, and 2 cloves of garlic, all finely chopped.  Saute until tender - about 12 minutes.  Mix in 1 c. each parsley and cilantro, minced, along with 1/2 t. fennel seed, slightly crushed, and 1/2 c. sugar.  Add 4 c. vegetable stock.</P><P>Heat 1/8 t. saffron until it\\\'s brittle, then take it upstairs and smoke it.  Or crush it into the soup.  Your choice.   Cook the soup at medium boil for 15 minutes.</P><P>Strain the solids from the soup and puree them with a 28-oz. can of tomatoes, peeled.  Add juice from can and the soup stock.  Stir in 2 T. white vinegar and salt to taste.  Refrigerate until cold or serve hot.  Garnish with flowers made from paper thin cucumber slices and Kalamata olives.</P><P>While waiting for the soup to get cold, slice the cukes and tell your sweetie a story.</P><P>\\\"Can I tell you a story, my sweetie?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Maybe.  We\\\'ll see how it goes.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Once there were some people who loved to get together and make soup.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Were they stoned?\\\"</P><P>\\\"History does not speak to this.  We do know, though, that they loved to eat them some soup.  Everybody helped out, took turns stirring \\\'til it was done.  Then they all sat down and had some.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, daddy?  What else?\\\"</P><P>\\\"One day, they heard about all these other soup groups in the next valley over.  They went on a fact-finding mission and discovered that some groups always stirred their soup to the right, some to the left.  \\\'Til then, they\\\'d never really thought about which way to stir the soup.  As long as it got cooked, nobody gave a shit.  They just knew they liked soup.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Doubts began to surface.  Some members of the group started a Right-Handed Stirring Party.  Others formed the Left-Handers.  But most of the people didn\\\'t join either one.  They just stood back and watched.\\\"</P><P>\\\"At the next soup-making, The Right-Handers started a kettle up and stirred it to the right.   The Left-Handers got out their ladle and stirred to the left.  To the left, to the right.  Soup got stirred like a motherfucker.  Stirred so fast it flew out of the pots and onto the walls.\\\"</P><P>\\\"People were hungry, but the kettles were dry.  The non-affiliated started home.  Behind them, the Right- and Left-Handers both urged them to join their respective parties.  \\\'Stay with us,\\\' they said.  \\\'You\\\'ll get some great soup.\\\'  But the hungry ones suspected there was more to soup-making than the direction of the stir.\\\"</P><P>She munches on a slice of pita bread.  \\\"I wish I had been there.  I would have kicked some ass.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I just know you would.\\\"</P><P>Mom comes in.  She goes, \\\"You two are just like my Aunt Lenore.  She had to dirty up every pot in the kitchen to cook anything.\\\"</P><P>\\\"We\\\'ll clean it up.\\\"</P><BR><BR><BR>(thanks to soupsongs.com and elmer proudly.)'),(1742,'<body>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1742_1.jpg\'> Â \r\n\r\n<p>Save our city!Â  It seems to be getting stale.Â  Businesses\r\nare hurting themselves by not letting in the fresh air...Â  I\\\'ve seen\r\nhow several corporations are run here.Â  No wonder we are in a recession.Â \r\nHere is an example from my first job in San Francisco...\r\n<p>\r\n<hr WIDTH=\\\"100%\\\">\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#000000\\\">Â Â Â  I worked at a large law firm\r\nfor 8 months.Â  I am sitting in an office with my manager and I tell\r\nhim I might have a few ideas that could help the efficiency of our department\r\nand to make everyone\\\'s job easier.Â  (I have a degree in business management.)Â \r\nHe says great, give me your ideas.Â  So a few days later sitting at\r\nmy desk surrounded by staff, and I stop my manager and sayÂ  \\\"You know\r\nhow I\\\'ve been working on this spreadsheet?Â  Well, I\\\'ve figured out\r\nan easier way to update it...\\\"Â  His face turns red and he asks me\r\nto come to his office.</font><font color=\\\"#000000\\\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#000000\\\">Â Â Â  In his office he says, \\\"You\r\nhave a better idea than I had, and I feel threatened by you.Â  I want\r\nyou to give me your ideas, but only when nobody else is around because\r\nI am going to take credit for them.Â  See?\\\"Â  I say, \\\"Ummmm sure...(Thinking:Â \r\nWhat a jerk!Â  I don\\\'t mind you using my ideas, as long as I get some\r\ncredit.)\\\"</font><font color=\\\"#000000\\\"></font>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#000000\\\">Â Â Â  A week or so later I call in\r\nsick for the first time in my 8 months of working there.Â  My head\r\nis pounding.Â  Over the phone my manager tells me I will need a doctors\r\nexcuse.Â  I tell him that\\\'s no problem.Â  (He doesn\\\'t want to do\r\nmy job while I\\\'m out)Â  Then he tells me that I should come in anyway\r\nand that I am jeopardizing my future by not coming in.Â  He asks me\r\nif I know what that means, and I tell him \\\"yes.\\\"Â  Then he says, \\\"No,\r\nI don\\\'t think you know what that means.\\\"Â  I say, \\\"Yes, I know what\r\nthat means.\\\"Â  He pauses for a second and then says,Â  \\\"Tell me\r\nwhat that means.\\\"Â Â  I answer by saying, \\\"It means your an asshole!\\\"\r\nand hang up the phone...</font><font color=\\\"#000000\\\"></font>\r\n<center>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#000000\\\">~They sent my personal items to me via bike messenger...Â \r\nHaha</font>\r\n<br><a href=\\\"http://w3.informatik.gu.se/~dixi/lyrics/stonefree.htm\\\">Stone\r\nfree</a>\r\n<br>\r\n<hr WIDTH=\\\"100%\\\"></center>\r\n\r\n<p>Life is just too short to put up with people like that.Â  Right\r\nnow that firm is having to lay off people because all of their clients\r\nhave gone bankrupt.Â  I still have friends who work there.Â  The\r\nsad part is the unproductive people, like my old manager, are still there.Â \r\nI imagine that a lot of the companies that went bankrupt had similar structural\r\nproblems.Â  I am disgusted at the corporate dryness of the businesses\r\nI have worked at.Â Â  I go to job interviews every week and I see\r\nthe same situations over and over.\r\n<p>At one interview they told me to cut my hair.Â  Don\\\'t they knows\r\nthat discrimination?Â  They could get sued if I wasn\\\'t such a nice\r\nperson.Â  Isn\\\'t this the city of diversity?Â  There is this HUGE\r\ndiversity flag that flies here.Â  Yes, I could cut my hair.Â  I\r\ncould dress like everyone else.Â  I could get a pair of those damn\r\nblack framed glasses that everyone wears.Â  But no, I am not a peg.Â \r\nI don\\\'t fit thier slot.Â  I am a person.Â  I have ideas...\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1743,'<P>This was taken by Mari while I was still studying in North Scotland. Maybe 6 years ago. I was cutting timber on the weekends to help pay the bills. It was hard work, but still to this day my favorite smell in the world is crushed foliage, cut timber and the diesel fumes from the forwarder.\r\n</P>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1743_1.jpg\'>'),(1744,'<P><a href=\"userpages.phtml?username=xepmom\">xepsmom</a> woke me up to tell me, \\\"austin, both world trade centers and the pentagon have been bombed.\\\"  dan told me, \\\"fucking planes crashed into them, man.\\\" Good Morning America.  i am watching cnn.  it appears that the center of new york city is one big column of smoke.  stringed instruments sigh morosely.  god was here.\r\n</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n[09:03] <*austin*> planes\r\n[09:04] <*jim*> buildings\r\n[09:11] <*egg*> my dad emailed me \\\"you ok? email me back!!\\\"\r\n[09:12] <*egg*> jaay: \\\"random planes are crashing\\\"\r\n9:31:46 iamthebmg: wathching people help people.\r\n9:32:09 iamthebmg: oh what a site.\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i was talking with ix about moving to australia last night.  that if i go there in december to visit, there is the possibility that i will just stay.</P>\r\n<P>driving in my car to work this morning, i wonder if i would go to work today if i had spent the morning in a burning smoking death trap. would i go hunt wallabee\\\'s with my friends and gather berries.</P>\r\n<P>pray to god, please dont hurt us anymore unless you want to.</P>\r\n<P><TT>so it is done with? are there any more planes in the air?</TT></P>\r\n<P>radar scours the american sky looking for blips to blap.</P>\r\n<P>GW predictions?</P>\r\n<P><*justin*> \\\"this is a military failure caused be a lack of resources and complacency\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P><*justin*> \\\"our #1 responsibility is not education or health care - it\\\'s safety and security</P>'),(1745,'In September 1996, while my ship was deployed in the Persian Gulf, we got orders to line up all our ships, north to south, and fire cruise missles at Iraq. There were CNN correspondants on board, walking around asking questions, getting in the way. At the time of the first launch, I was on the bridge manning the radar station, and I watched the missiles on my screen slowly approach the mainland, and disappear. On the surface radar, I could actually see the clouds of smoke and debris pretty clearly where the tomahawks had struck their targets. \r\n<p>\r\nThere was an air of celebration on board. We had actually <i>done</i> something, served our purpose as it were.\r\n<p>\r\nI, however, was not feeling so great. After the initial excitement, I started to feel that man, those guys in Iraq were kids just like I was at the time, just as scared, just as hopeful for a bright future. They had about as much to do with command-level decisions as I did, meaning absolutely nothing, and were just as innocent as I was of the whole thing. \r\n<p>\r\nThat\\\'s when I first decided that maybe the military wasn\\\'t for me. '),(1746,'<p>I don\\\'t want to drink the water.  We get no TV reception.  Got woken up by Amy, cause Lulu called, and she wasn\\\'t sure whether to believe her, it was early, but she couldn\\\'t go back to sleep.  We\\\'re in history.  Is this what it takes to feel alive?? Is this what we\\\'ve all needed? Maybe now we can get to work, I think. But god DAMN, is this what it takes? (Refer <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1038.txt\\\">here</a> for insight into my mindset a few months ago.)</p>\r\n<p>The neighbors upstairs are filling the tub and the washing machine. They\\\'re from Florida. This is what you do if a hurricane\\\'s coming.  But then I think, THE WATER.  What if they poison our water.  The Britta won\\\'t filter out Anthrax? Where do they get this Anthrax?  How did they hijack those planes?  What\\\'s going to happen next, or do they only have one attack like this in them per year.  This great big They.  Terrorists.  The inducers of terror. They got what they wanted. I\\\'m terrified, and so\\\'s the guy at Radio Shack. I went there to buy an antenna, because we\\\'ve been living quite happily without television until now, but now we need it.  As soon as I stopped invoking the name of God this morning, I started screaming for cable.  WHY DON\\\'T WE HAVE CABLE???!!! In times of crisis we\\\'ve learned.  CNN is our only savior.  When the election refused to end, that\\\'s what I dialed up on my little computer every hour at work. CNN. They must know something new.  It\\\'s been an hour already. There must be some more news by now. Give it to me dammit. I want to hear more about the carnage. I want to hear more from the people who saw it, the woman crying about seeing people jump from windows of the World Trade Center, unable to fathom going down with the tallest towers in New York. Panicked, like we humans do, they leapt.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t want to leave the house, but we did. We went to the store for some soup and bottled water. We\\\'re not sure why. Amy wanted chocolate. \\\"The world is ending and you want chocolate?\\\" Ashley says. He AIMs someone with a comic mantra from Lenny Bruce, during the Cuban Missile Crisis, as imagined by Don DeLillo: \\\"We\\\'re all gonna die!\\\"</p>\r\n<p>I will drink the water. I\\\'ll make ice cubes for a bourbon drink later.  It\\\'s still before noon, though, and for the same reason I\\\'m not filling the bathtub with water, I\\\'m not drinking before noon. And I\\\'m not smoking a cigarette yet either, because I might want to get some exercize later. We\\\'re civilized people.  The country\\\'s in crisis, a couple of my friends narrowly averted being drowned by rubble this very morning, and I just saw two of the tallest buildings in the world crumble in a cloud on the television, but I\\\'m making breakfast. We might roast a chicken later.  If the apocalypse comes and armed marauders storm Oakland, guns blastin, I hope to be face down in a pool of my own sick in an opium den somewhere. (Maybe opium will come back in vogue, we can grow some poppies on the roof.) I\\\'m a lover not a fighter. I can\\\'t stand this. But Amy and I were saying, no matter what peaceable beliefs we might have, no matter how we might despise shows of aggression, the dick-wagging military showin their might, we\\\'re glad they\\\'re there. In the end, we\\\'re just children who want to be protected. I want a house in the woods now with my loved ones and our own well water.  I want a garden. Maybe vegetarianism wouldn\\\'t be that bad, because I don\\\'t want to kill my own food.  I just want to be calm again and I want Clinton back. Didn\\\'t anyone who voted for that putz REALIZE WHAT THEY WERE IN FOR!!??? Press conferences, people!! Press conferences in a time of war in which he SAYS THE WRONG WORD!! Because the teleprompters are too slow!! Because he hasn\\\'t got a fucking clue what to do or say and shit is goin down. We have a puppet for a president at the worst possible time, but maybe it makes no difference. Maybe he\\\'s as good as any. He keeps an apparently confident face on while he\\\'s shitting in his pants.  Terrific. He\\\'ll be great. I can\\\'t wait. </p>\r\n<p>Okay, I realize I\\\'m just panicked. I\\\'m gettin it out on Swinney.org because what else can one do? We get on the phone, on AIM, on ICB, and we chat, because what else can we do. We\\\'re beings who can\\\'t stop communicating, can\\\'t stop watching the footage on the box. We say I love you and we hang up. I\\\'m making tuna casserole tonight. \\\"Good food for the apocolypse,\\\" Juni says. The neighbors from upstairs are knocking again. They really want us to join them, for beers. It\\\'s noon now.  Maybe we\\\'ll end up giving in.  We want to be numbed.  We want to be like the cat and the dog, Fernando and Lola, laying like they always do, vaguely sensing a disruption, but just nosing about innocently like it\\\'s any other Tuesday. I\\\'m not sure beer can remove one from time, but I wish it could.</p>'),(1747,'<p>\r\nLet me say first that I do not think the attacks in America were \\\"beautiful\\\" or \\\"amazing\\\". However I do not think the attacks on NYC and D.C are any different then things going on in the rest of the world today. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI wont go into details regarding the situation because I know that for some of you, friends and family might have been caught up in this act. This is tragic. What I will go into is the U.S government\\\'s response and rhetoric concerning these attacks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nColin Powel referred to the attacks as a cowardly act. He talked about how \\\"they\\\" can kill out citizens and bomb out buildings, but that these cowards cannot take away our democracy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is very interesting to me because the United States does not condemn, and veto\\\'s all United Nations resolutions regarding, the use of assassination and target bombing by the Israelis. They look away as hundreds of thousands of Hutus and Tutsis are slaughtered in Rwanda, claiming that the cost of shipping equipments and personal to that part of Africa is too costly. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf the act of using a passenger plane to attack economic and military targets is cowardly, why is it accepted that using cruise missiles to react to various situations is ok?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am not supporting the actions of those responsible for today\\\'s actions. If I had the power to stop such actions id have stopped them. However this is not the case. We can look at this situation from two angles. Those who let their hatred and emotions run wild, and those who might try and figure out why these attacks happened and what can be done to stop them from happening again. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n60% of  U.S arms sales end up in the middle east. Do any of the men and women who make these weapons feel any guilt when they hear about some tragedy happening over seas? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDo the pilots who fly \\\"authorized\\\" aircraft over Kosovo and Serbia feel any remorse when they see a building blow up? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDo you feel any remorse for those who die overseas at the hands of American foreign policy, or do you only feel remorse when the people who die carry the same passport as you. \r\n</p>'),(1748,'<P>Peter.. Essemar? </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Anyone else in NY(C)... Are you guys ok?</P>'),(1749,'<p>\r\nhere is something that not too many people know about me. i am patriotic. i am patriotic because i grew up military, and it\\\'s really hard not to be patriotic when you were raised surrounded by patriotism. civilians in america can blather on all they like about the politics of war, but soldiers have blind faith. they may be puppets of a disgusting bigger picture of republican dominance mongering, but they are, overall, good people who are trying to do good things. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni feel a sick understanding for what must be happening right now on military bases overseas like the ones i grew up on. i have a clear memory for what it was like to live on base as a child during the gulf war. there were constant terrorist threats, and i walked to school past soldiers with loaded m-16s, who guarded streetcorners behind stacks of sandbags. my mother and i would bring them food and water as they stood out all day in the weather. local turkish hoodlums would throw cherry bombs over the fence of the base just to see us all scramble. \r\n</p><p>\r\nright now, american families overseas are probably not allowed to leave base, or if they are, they are being required to get out of their cars and be searched on the way in. men in uniform are looking under the hood for car bombs, checking under the body of the car with long mirrors.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit\\\'s terrifying to be a child during a time like this. i *still* cringe at the sound of low flying planes, and when i was younger, the sound of a low flying plane would sometimes paralyze me with fear as i waited motionless for a bomb to fall on me. i can\\\'t imagine what palestinian children have been going through for years, living with this.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthough i can\\\'t ever justify war or violence, i am very proud of my father. he was in the army for 20 years. when i was a child, he would take me to the motor pool with him and let me climb around inside tanks and missile launchers. later in his career he was in charge of other soldiers and he was a gentle boss, letting them go home when they needed, and generally being fair and good. like many soldiers, i don\\\'t think he ever considered the ethics of being a soldier, the mass machine of government. he was just a man with a job, and he did his job well.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni remember when my father first became an officer. two days in as an officer, he was uncomfortable with being saluted. once, walking with him on a sunny day, we passed a bench where two enlisted soldiers sat. they scrambled to their feet to salute him and he waved hands: <i>good god, sit down, sit down, enjoy your day.</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nnow my dad is a logistics assistance representative for the army. he spends his time making phone calls and sending emails to make sure that the soldiers he works with have the right parts and people to get their job done. \r\n</p><p>\r\nunfortunately, right now, my father\\\'s work has taken him to a base on the iraqi border of kuwait. i can only imagine what must be going on there right now. i have heard from my mother that they are on heavy alert there, no phone calls are allowed in or out, and soldiers have been issued weapons. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni am worried about my dad. not right now, but in the next few months. he is scheduled to be there until january. if america goes to war with any country in the middle east, the bases in kuwait and in germany where i grew up will be clearing houses for weapons and soldiers. they will all be potential targets. there are lots of civilians there, good people like my father. i\\\'m disgusted at the concept of war, at the bloodlust i hear coming out of government mouths right now, because i know what kind of people will be caught up in the ground zero of it if we go to war. the kind of people i grew up around. \r\n</p>'),(1750,'<P>Yesterday was a day of surreal chaos.  It\\\'s funny the ways in which these things hit you.  I wasn\\\'t struck by the magnitude of what had happened until I walked out of the battlefield wasteland that used to be the financial district and saw a makeshift blood donation center in a park.  A few ambulances and gurneys.  People lined up.  Other people holding up handwritten signs for AB-, O+ and such.  Beyond that some construction workers gather supplies, presumably for the rescue efforts.  There were tables set up with fresh water and snacks for people who needed them.  I saw a table in front of a fire station with a phone sitting on it and sign saying, \\\"Need to make a phone call?\\\"  Finally I come to the police barricade set up to hold back the throngs of gawkers trying to get a closer look.  It was at this point that I started wandering the streets of NYC struck by the reality that 10,000 people probably just died, close enough for me to watch it happen.  Wandering the streets covered in dust, catching snatches of conversation about the fucking Arabs, noticing people looking at my dust-covered clothes.</p><p>At one point earlier in the day, I broke down crying thinking about my friend Jas.  Jas works in one of the builings directly adjacent the WTC, and the very real possiblity existed that he might be dead.  <i>Jas might be dead!</i>  The thought of that was more than I could bear.  (I just heard word from him about and hour ago.  Thank God.)</p><p>It was another banal day at work yesterday morning.  I came in about 8:35am, five minutes late again.  Had a voice mail waiting for me from my boss from last night.  Something that needed doing first thing in the morning.  She wouldn\\\'t be coming in until later.  Check my email.  More drama from an exgirlfriend I thought would be a good idea to get back in touch with.  Settle down to get started working.  Someone comes running down the hall blabbering something about the World Trade Center and an airplane.  Typical over-reaction, it\\\'s probably nothing.  Regardless, I wander over the window to see what\\\'s going on and see a gaping hole in the side of the building belching flames and smoke.  All I can think about is the movie <i>Brazil</i>.  Almost all of the debris fluttering around my office building is paper, files dislodged because of the explosion.  When I see the second plane buzz by my office, bank around, and smash into the other tower in a huge explosion, I feel like I\\\'m watching a movie.  Everything was in slow-motion, huge fireball, very spectacular.  Watching the events on TV, complete with comentary, then seeing the actual thing out the window somehow adds to the unreality of it all.</P><P>The other people in my office are milling about confused.  Should we stay and get back to work?  Should we go home?  I think most of the people feel the same way about it as I do, most of them prefering to watch the events on TV rather than out the window.  It feels so far away even though it\\\'s so close.  Sarah is fighting to hold back tears fucking terrified and babbling about how we need to get the hell out of here.  I put a hand on her shoulder and tell her to go home; it\\\'s ridiculous for us to stay here.  She stands transfixed by the news reports, hugging herself, holding back tears.</p><P>I go back to my desk wondering what to do.  It\\\'s announced that the office is closed and we should go home.  I call my dad to tell him I\\\'m okay.  I figure the worst is over.  The attack is done.  I\\\'m safe.  I\\\'ll wait until the chaos of people trying to get home dies down before I try to get out of there, so I talk to my dad a bit, respond to some emails from people asking if I\\\'m alright, try to get a hold of people who might be worried about me.</P><P>Eventually, I go downstairs and outside, start smoking a cigarette.  There\\\'s still a lot of people milling about in front of the building.  I try to call some friends on my cell phone.  <i>All circuits are busy at this time</i>.  I\\\'m pondering the mob in front of me when everyone starts running.  No one knows where to run to.  Everyone is just following everyone else.  I hear people talk about how the tower has collapsed as they scurry by, and I feel a twinge of real fear thinking that the skyscraper has actually fallen over.  It\\\'s tall enough that it would have fallen very close where I was.  I\\\'m not sure what to do seeing the futility of just blindly running.  When I see the cloud the dust billowing towards me, I hurriedly make my way back inside.</P><P>Back in my office on the 38th floor, it\\\'s dark as night outside.  The dust obscurs everything.  We are all asked to gather in the 37th floor conference center.  I talk to someone covered in debris.  He was caught in the dust cloud trying to walk home and forced to come back to the office.  Other people are relating their stories of coming out of the commuter train station in the basement of the WTC when the first plane hit.  Slow trudging up the escalator turned into frantic running as rescue worker yell at people to get the hell out of there.  Others are trying to figure out how to get home to New Jersey or upstate New York as much of the city\\\'s public transit and roads are shut down.</p><P>We all sit in a conference room drinking coffee and munching pastries.  CNN is continually blaring on the projector TV giving constant commentary on what\\\'s happening right outside.  I start crying thinking about Jas.  All I want is to be among friends, drinking a beer, ignoring the reality I\\\'m caught in, but I\\\'m stuck in this building until the smoke clears enough outside for me to be able to breathe.  I\\\'m reminded of Burning Man, and I wish that I had the mask and goggles that were a constant accessorie out in the desert.  I just want to get the fuck out of there.</P><P>People are lined up to use the phone.  <i>All circuits are busy at this time</i>.  When It\\\'s my turn, I pull out my palm pilot and try to call everyone I know.  Reaching people in Idaho, Oregon, California is no problem.  Reaching people in NYC is nearly impossible, but I manage to get through to a few.  Everyone is relieved to know I\\\'m still alive.</p><P>Milling about the office waiting for the dust to settle.  I manage to sneak onto a computer for a bit and reply to emails from people asking if I\\\'m okay.  Everyone is eerily calm.  Reality hasn\\\'t really sunk in.  People are more concerned with how they\\\'re going to get home.</P><P>By 2pm the air is fairly clear, even though smoke is still rising from where the WTC once stood.  I\\\'m one of the last people to leave the office.  Outside, I smoke a cigarette then tie a t-shirt from my gym bag around my face.  None of the buildings where I am are damaged, but everything is covered with dust and debris.  Very few people are out on the street.  The entire landscape is transformed.</P><P>Walking out of the the disaster area police usher me away from the location of primary destruction, but I manage to catch a glimpse of a pile of rubble obscurred by smoke and fire.  I\\\'m in a slight state of shock.  I\\\'m just walking without any definite destination in mind.</P><P>After the blood donation center, after the police barricade, back in the \\\"normal\\\" world, I find myself a couple blocks from Deborah\\\'s apartment.  It takes about five or six tries, but I finally get through to her on my cell and invite myself over.  Once there, she\\\'s throwing things into a bag on her way out of Manhattan.  She\\\'s spending the night at a friend\\\'s in Brooklyn.  He freaked her out with thoughts of continued terrorist attacks, anthrax, nerve gas, and insisted that she spend the night with him, away from the epicenter of these phantom attacks.  She\\\'s in a panic, the valium she took just muddying her mind and not calming her nerves.  I calm her down and walk her to one of the few subway lines that are running.  She promises to take me out for drinks tomorrow night.</P><P>I go to Edward\\\'s next, not wanting to take the long journey home.  He has spent all day watching the news, popping valium, and drinking beer.  I join him in the valium and beer and ask that  the TV be turned off.  I\\\'m sick of thinking about what\\\'s happened today.  We watch <i>Golden Girls</I> instead.</P><P>It\\\'s about 8pm and people are finally starting to be able to get through to me on my cell phone.  My roommate calls and is glad to know I\\\'m not hurt.  She tells me I\\\'ve got a ton of messages and my boss called to say there would be no work tomorrow.  <i>no shit</i>  A few other people call.  Erin is on her way to Canada with her boyfriend and Tae is spending the night in a hotel in Manhattan.  The city is still mostly closed off.</p><P>Edward lives on 10th street and the city has been closed south of 14th.  We go out looking for some food and McDonald\\\'s is about the only thing open.  There\\\'s line going out the door and down the block.  We manage to find a bougie little bistro selling over-priced hamburgers.  I enjoy my first meal since breakfast.</P><P>I spent last night on Edward\\\'s couch being too exhausted to want to try to get home.  I got up late this morning to coffee, more news of what\\\'s happening, and more emails from people wondering how I\\\'m doing.  Part of me is happy about not having to go to work today.  Edward\\\'s boyfriend had to go in.  He works in mid-town.  The reality of what happened still hasn\\\'t fully sunk in.  Stuff like this happens every day in Beirut, right?</p><P>It\\\'s strange not seeing the twin towers rising above the NYC skyline anymore.</P>'),(1751,'<P>i\\\'m running tonight. for a while there i was\r\nfeeling good, less like marlon brando in Apocalypse Now-- the big Brando. \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen I gained a bunch of weight I went, for a while, from the sexy brando of On the Waterfront to the big Brando. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m somewhere in the middle now, the Godfather maybe.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThat\\\'s how men should do it, talk about their weight, in terms of Brando or Elvis maybe.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOnce someone described me as \\\"middle Elvis.\\\"\r\n</P>'),(1752,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1752_1.jpg\'>\r\nThis is quite a recent photo of me. Mari and I went with Sari (her sister) and their father clay pigeon shooting on the weekend. Mari has also just bought a hunting bow, so I think this summer will be a bit perilous around our house. I\\\'m looking forward to going back to Canada so I can really develop my hunting and shooting activity. \r\n\r\n'),(1753,'<p>waking up to see the clouds upside down and the sun rising</p>\r\n<p>that silly mischievous look in my dog\\\'s golden eyes</p>\r\n<p>singing along to the <a href=\\\"http://www.badlivers.com/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">Bad Livers</a> while driving</p>\r\n<p>my friend Kim giving me the bird</p>\r\n<p>imagining horse galloping</p>\r\n<p>remembering baby lulu reaching out her tiny hand to hold mine</p>\r\n<p>rubbing buddah\\\'s belly</p>\r\n<p>seeing my coworkers <a href=\\\"http://www.oldwoodies.com/img/ads/50dodge.jpg\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">woody</a></p>\r\n<p>that email and picture <i>he</i> sent me last night</p>\r\n<p>overhearing someone say, \\\'tits...they\\\'re wonderful amazing things!\\\'</p>'),(1754,'<p>\r\n<br>I know that even though some bad things have happened, there is a good\r\nreason to look on the positive side of things.&nbsp; The events of this\r\nweek have brought a lot of people together.&nbsp; We are binded in our\r\ndesire for freedom and the preservation of freedom.&nbsp; I would die for\r\nour freedom.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>People in other countries have given us great sympathy and have pledged\r\nto stand by us in our quest for a safe and free world.&nbsp; This makes\r\nmy really happy because I have traveled though many countries and have\r\nobserved a great anti-American sentiment.&nbsp; I think that this will\r\nall change now.&nbsp; They look back and see what our country has done\r\nfor them in the past.&nbsp; In looking at the attacks on our country they\r\nalso see it as an attack on their freedom as well as the well being of\r\nthe world.\r\n<p>This evening I was sitting on the stoop of my house.&nbsp; People are\r\nwalking by going this way and that.&nbsp; Some I assume are going home\r\nfrom work.&nbsp; Others are going out for the evening, ready to escape\r\nfrom the hum-drum of daily life.&nbsp; So Im sitting there thinking about\r\nlife, the world, and how everyone\\\'s reality can totally change within a\r\nmatter of minutes.\r\n<p>Along came a friend of my who stops to chat for a second.&nbsp; Our\r\nconversation was brief, but terribly funny.&nbsp; She leaves me with a\r\nhuge smile on my face.&nbsp; I keep smiling.&nbsp; People keep passing\r\nby, and I smile at them.&nbsp; They smile back and keep smiling as they\r\nwalk away.&nbsp; They smile at other people who in turn, smile back.&nbsp;\r\nNow people are passing me, smiling.&nbsp; Some of them look over at me\r\nand smile at me.&nbsp; Im already smiling.&nbsp; Others smile at passers\r\nby and they begin smiling.\r\n<p>Normally when I see people pass by me, they have this gloomy look on\r\ntheir face and try to avoid contact.&nbsp; But today is different.&nbsp;\r\nThis made me really happy.&nbsp; Happy to be smiling, alive, and safe.&nbsp;\r\nWe have hope...\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1755,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1755_1.jpg\'>\r\nWay back, I had a succession of bikes. In a way they were all my favorites, but this was the most fun. There are so many stories and experiences associated to this bike, I can\\\'t even scratch the surface here, so I won\\\'t. I have a hankering to buy a new bike here in Finland, but I am sure that <b>redline fever</b> is bubbling away in me, and maybe its not such a good idea if I want to live to see my next birthday. \r\n\r\n'),(1756,'<P>as is life isn\\\'t interesting enough, we have an impending hurricane here in sunny fla.  i swear nobody noticed it coming til today, though. it\\\'s like somebody in a car accident with a massive head wound who also happens to have a broken toe. kind of immaterial at this point.  usually, during the day before landfall, there\\\'s nothing on tv but storm coverage.  with this other, it\\\'s kind of slipped under the radar.</P><P>fuck it.  unless it\\\'s category 3 or better, we don\\\'t give them much notice anyway.  you got to be tough to live in florida.  hell, i guess you got to be tough to live anywhere.  around here, they come to tell us natives to evacuate, and if we\\\'re drunk enough, we say, \\\"evacuate yourself, copper.  this is private property.  evacuate my colon.\\\"</P><P>by \\\"us\\\", i mean the members of the hurricane party, a singular institution here along the water.  we usually have it out at burt\\\'s, in spring lake.  his house is made out of limestone and coquina, a hundred years old, his great-grandpa built it.  practical pig, great-grandpa.  burt\\\'s one of my town friends, as opposed to my more recently-acquainted city friends, with their new-fangled ideas about sex and The Unknown.  burt used to play farm league ball for the expos, never made it to the bigtime, but he was our hero for a while there, our burt.  my town friends have no idea i\\\'m a little sexual libertine.  and don\\\'t you go telling them!  blow my cover here.  i don\\\'t care, really. everybody\\\'s gonna know soon enough, anyway.  i\\\'ll be coming out like those singing belly buttons.</P><P>actually, i think burt might switch hit a little his own damn self.  might\\\'ve picked up some naughty habits among those dirty canadians:)  i find myself doing this now:  looking over the pickings among my straight friends, seeing who might pull me aside sometime in the future:  \\\"why didn\\\'t you say something before? i wish i knew:  we could have burnt the house down a long time ago, baby.\\\"</P><P>yeah, he\\\'s a possibility.  don\\\'t tell me he didn\\\'t think about it all those times we were tag-teamin\\\' it with sammi.  anybody that will get naked in front of you and fuck a third party while you get naked and fuck said party has got to be at least considering fucking you, too.  you would think.  at least sucking you.</P><P>i tell you what else i\\\'m doing, too, lately, is checking out everybody past puberty up to and including the old folks. there is a boy i see in the library who looks to be about 15.  smoking hot.  he\\\'s got a nicer body than mine, bigger, better definition, medium tan, this glossy smooth skin that catches the light and glows, short blonde hair, a slight sprinkling of pimples across his upper cheeks that\\\'s cute, not at all a turn-off.  he\\\'s big for his age, and i know he\\\'s 15 (maybe 14) \\\'cause he waits for his mommy outside on the bench every afternoon.  i wish i was living in ancient greece.  you got a nasty mind, mccord.  damn those age of consent laws;)  they like to keep a man down, don\\\'t they?  but he ain\\\'t goin\\\' anywhere.  and i ain\\\'t goin\\\' anywhere.  i can wait 3 or 4 years.  i\\\'ll be making a wwII picture in brussels around that time: convince him he\\\'d be a great actor, and, there, in that pretty little city, personally welcome him to sodom, another sweet little town.  here\\\'s your gift basket, son, along with some coupons from the local business community.  what??  a man can dream, can\\\'t he?  tell you what:  i\\\'ll tell you my secrets, and you tell the world.</P><P>well, anyway, it\\\'s gonna be a good party.  anymore, none of us see each other much outside these get-togethers.  once a year, maybe.  live in the same town and we\\\'re like ghosts haunting one another.  but that\\\'s not why:  the party\\\'s gonna be good \\\'cause both burt and i are gonna be there, and that means but one thing:  fisticuffs.  between me \\\'n burt.  or me \\\'n burt vs. everybody else.  sure thing.  betcha.  somebody\\\'s checkin\\\' in to the graybar hotel tonight, probly all of us if that fuckin\\\' deputy comes out there when the wind picks up a little.  the law around here\\\'s got no respect for tradition.</P><BR><BR><P>you have been listening to:  hurricane party.  rock over london.  rock on chicago.</P><BR><BR><BR>*******<BR><BR><BR>(sung)Beyond these things/there\\\'s a band/playing every favorite song/you ever had./Beyond these things/there\\\'s a park/where you can play/way past dark.<BR>Chorus:  And I don\\\'t know it/but I can feel it/it\\\'s here but it\\\'s there and it\\\'s here and it\\\'s gone/And I\\\'ll never know it/but something in me is already here, already there.<BR><BR><P>i tell you what, i never thought i\\\'d say this, but i\\\'m starting to suspect i might be having a little newfound respect for mr. george w. bush.  he looks like he\\\'s having a rough go of things.  there might be more to him than we thought.</P><P>somebody else i\\\'ve got to give the propers to is dan rather.  EVERY fucking time i\\\'ve turned on cbs in the last 48 hours, his old basset hound face has been on there.  either he\\\'s some kind of sleep camel or he gots the good speed connections.  give it up, dan.  kick some down to the rest of us.</P><P>i\\\'m so tired of looking at the pictures.  yesterday i turned off the tv, fired up the radio, and it\\\'s the same shit:  everybody talking about it, dumbass shock jocks, public radio people, teeny-bopper djs.  i find one station with music on it, Lite Rock, and the first three songs out of the box, no lie, are:  \\\"American Woman\\\", \\\"Jet\\\", and \\\"No Reply at All.\\\"  i didn\\\'t know if that was pure coincidence or if they had some sadistic motherfucker down there doing the programming.  then i turn it on again today.  once more, the first thing i hear is \\\"American Woman\\\".  at this point, decided it was a message from god and listened to the lyrics.  they seem to point to a new epoch in human evolution.  \\\"it\\\'s the new Mother Nature takin\\\' over.\\\"  a more earth-centered, Matriarchal approach to things for a change.  like my niece\\\'s powerpuff girls soundtrack cd i was listening to, that frank black song on it that goes \\\"better pray for the girls, they\\\'re the only hope in this whole world.\\\"  good stuff for her to hear.  and that song by that guy who used to be in olivia tremor control, about how the monkey villain character is all bad, he can\\\'t play nice, but \\\"friends win in the end,\\\"  those superhero girls TEACH him to play nice and ultimately make friends with him.  unfortunately, they have to use violence to do it.  i wish we could just go over and talk to the \\\"terrorists\\\" as people.  i don\\\'t see where killing anybody else is going to solve anything.  i guess it would keep THOSE people from doing it again, but somebody would eventually get mad again about something or other.  it\\\'s a mess.  but love keeps this world together.</P><P>went to get ice cream at the jiffy store the night of the attack and hit a possum on croom road on the way back right in front of mrs. millican\\\'s.  swerved, but i guess when you haven\\\'t been driving for eight years, the skillz lack a little something.</P><P>yesterday, went to winn dixie, looked for a st. lazarus candle, all they had by the ethnic food section where they usually are were big red ones, so opted for an anonymous yellow candle.  took it out to the cemetary, put it in a jelly glass, lit in, put it on my grandfather\\\'s grave, sang \\\"happy birthday.\\\"  he would have been 80 yesterday if he was alive.  this is something i do that makes my life richer.  a week from now, i\\\'ll take a purple candle out there and light it.  he died eight days after he turned 65.  i lit it and the wind blew it out.  i relit it then thought, \\\"duh, i just sang \\\'happy birthday.\\\'\\\"  i figure the wind or the rain will put it out soon enough.  saw four cranes walking around out there, other than me, the only animals.  don\\\'t know what that portends.</P><P>i notice people have been friendlier than usual around here the last couple of days.  maybe something good will come out of this whole thing.</P><P>this week, i\\\'ve been reading \\\"light in august\\\" by william faulkner, eudora welty\\\'s collected stories (looking for possible festival projects), and the letters of george bernard shaw.  the best writers are mystics, and these folks definitely qualify, and they give me comfort.  i\\\'m trying to learn to be an artistic mystic myself, beginning to sense that you have to polish your link to the infinite yet also remain grounded so you can actually enjoy life and pass along secrets in monkeyspeak to all your fellow monkeys.  i also was deeply struck by a story by william saroyan, one that i first read last year that i read again yesterday.  saroyan was born and lived much of his life in fresno, growing up there in abject poverty.  the story is called \\\"resurrection of a life\\\", and, in it, he brings back from memory the little boy he once was, selling papers in the city, shouting out the headlines, prowling all the corners of the city, seeking the myriad flavors of life.  he tells about buying \\\"chicken bread\\\" from the bakery, bread that has fallen from the wrapping machine and can\\\'t be sold for human consumption, bread that the baker and he both know will be taken home for his family.  he tells of resting in the bosom of a white fig tree, wanting to be of the earth, the old world of nature, be in the world and yet eternal.  he tells about the hosannas with which his shouted wartime headline is greeted:  \\\"shouting that ten thousand huns had been destroyed.  Huns? he asked.  What do you mean, huns?  They are men, aren\\\'t they?\\\"  he mourns yet exults in the boy he was that is gone.</P><P>\\\"I was this boy who is now lost and buried in the succeeding forms of myself, and I am now of this last moment, of this small room, and the night hush, time going, time coming, and myself here, breathing, this last moment, inhale, exhale, the boy dead and alive.  All that I have learned is that we breathe, from moment to moment, now, always now, and then we remember, and we see the boy moving through a city that has become lost, among people who have become dead, alive among dead moments, crossing a street, the scene thus, or standing by the bread bin in the bakery, a sack of chicken bread please so that we can live and shout about it, and it begins nowhere and it ends nowhere, and all that I know is that we are somehow alive, all of us in the light, making shadows, the sun overhead, space all around us, inhaling, exhaling, the face and form of man everywhere, pleasure and pain, sanity and madness, over and over again, war and no war, peace and no peace, the earth solid and unaware of us, unaware of our cities, our dreams, unaware of this love I have for life, the love that was the boy\\\'s, unaware of all things, my going, my coming, the earth everlastingly itself, not of me, everlastingly precise, and the sea sullen with movement like my breathing, waves pounding the shore of myself, coming and going, and all that I know is that I am alive and glad to be, glad to be of this ugliness and this glory, somehow glad that I can remember, somehow remember the boy climbing the fig tree, unpraying but religious with joy, somehow of the earth, of the time of the earth, somehow everlastingly of life, nothingness, blessed or unblessed, somehow deathless like myself, timeless, glad, insanely glad to be here, and so it is true, there is no death, somehow there is no death, and can never be.\\\"</P>'),(1757,'<p>i was sending this as an email to our friend kat, then decided that i should send it to all of you.</p><p>earlier this summer I did a short session at the Texas School for the Blind. I was a \"residential instructor\" (babysitter) for young kids between the ages 6-9. I had boys of course, three of em; matt, michael, and forrest. I can\'t forget them wild lil boys. they were wonderful. So anyway, we did have 3 lil girls in the dorm too. One little girl, amee, ended up sticking to me like glue. She is a 6 year old orphan from Thailand, visually impaired slightly and has a brain tumor that will eventually cause complete blindness in the next few years if is not removed soon. They would remove it right away if they could, but this is her third brain tumor, the other two have already been removed. They are afraid that it would cause too much trauma to her brain to remove more, and so soon. They have to wait for her head to heal from the the last two... They don\'t expect her to live much longer than her eyes. Our job was to teach her how to mobilize herself once/if that does happen. One day, at a park she was very tired and in a cranky but touchy mood. She came over to me, sank her little head under my arm - forcing a hug, (strange hug rules at these schools). She began to talk about her \"bwain tuma\" as she would say, and told me that she had never had a boyfriend... then asked if i would be her boyfriend, of course i said yes! then, she asked if we could make animals out of the clouds - and thats what we did. My little girlfriend and i made clouds move into whatever we wanted to.  I nearly lost it, fighting to hold back the tears, staring into the blue sky - thinking that one day cancer would take her away from me. \"See that one Amee, that one is a rose - and I made it just for you.\"</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1757_1.jpg\'></p>\r\n<p>\r\nI want to make clouds again, with you. </p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1757_2.jpg\'>\r\n<p>I found some tranquility this morning...in the midst of all the fear.</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1757_3.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n'),(1758,'<p>(they got me working on a supersecret campaign-- part of the war effort)</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\\\"spend money for america!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"buy to win!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"we all win with consumer confidence!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"buy against terrorism!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"every nintendo game you buy, goes to fight dirty terrorists\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"attack them with cash!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"don\\\'t let them take your credit cards away!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"give me consumer confidence or give me death!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"we will fight at the malls, the shopping centers, online and at the grocery store-- and we will never give up, never give up, never give up spending!\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"every dollar spent goes to fight godlessness\\\"\r\n<br>\\\"buy a new nintendo for uncle sam\\\"\r\n\r\n'),(1759,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1759_1.jpg\'>\r\nThis is one of the views from the top of the Sugarloaf mountain in South Wales. Thats me with the white shirt, sitting next to Slim, a mad drummer I have been friends with for years. Carl was taking the photograph. We were a bit cruel to Slim that day. It was really hot, and he was wearing Jeans, so we persuaded him that his boxers looked enough like shorts. He was getting some funny looks, especially when he got a bit of wind!!!'),(1760,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1760_1.jpg\'><br>\r\nThere are alot of interesting things around me:<br>\r\n<br>\r\nNoise of Fighter Jets flying above-head<br>\r\nPotato gun on desk<br>\r\nConvoys of troops rumbling by on Broadway<br>\r\nLeft over Ginger-Ale<br>\r\nNY Times front pages of people leaping from WTC<br>\r\nStamp Collection<br>\r\nPile of rubble rescue-worker friend brought from disaster to \\\'remember\\\'<br>\r\nWhimsical wood-carving of frog<br>\r\nSmoke still wafting through midtown<br>\r\nBright Fauve-ish painting of flowers, unfinished<br>\r\nDirty work gloves<br>\r\nGrinning, dopey polaroids of last time i was rolling.<br>\r\nBad Dreams, insomnia<br>\r\nUp tempo records, airy trance<br>\r\nThe Unending TV News<br><br>\r\n'),(1761,'<p>Daft Punk\\\'s album <b>Discovery</b> had the single <i>One More Time</i> released as the first single.  It was and is a piece of trash.  <b>Homework</b> was genius and <i>One More Time</i> really let me down when I first heard it.  By the time I got a promo copy of <b>Discovery</b>, I was expecting the worst.  1/3 of the album is genius.  By far <i>Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger</i> is the best track on the entire album.  I don\\\'t know why 1/3 is eurodance, 1/3 is filler and the other is genius but I know that 2/3 isn\\\'t discouraging me from being eager for their next release.</p>\r\n<p>Just listening to <i>Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger</i> moments ago reminded me of walking around campus, melty snow and friends respecting my asking to be left alone to enjoy the track on someone\\\'s mini-disc unit.</p>\r\n<p>The album is worth bying for that track alone.</p>'),(1762,'<p>\r\n<b><font size=\\\"1\\\">Bombay, 3 December 1958.</font></b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDo please consider this. The very word <i>Moslem</i> prevents you from looking at the human being who represents that word. The nervous response and the psychological response to that word are very deep, and it evokes in you all kinds of ideas, beliefs, prejudices. But if one could think very deeply, it would become obvious that one must separate the word from the actual thing. A great deal of misunderstanding in our relationships lies in the wrong significance we give to words. Therefore it is very important that you and I , as two individuals, estabish right communication so that we understand each other on the same level at the same time. I do not know if you have noticed it, but when you love somebody communication between the two of you is immediate. Similarly, if we can establish such communion then I think we shall be able to explore this very complex problem. The great difficulty in establishing communication is the word, and you and I must pierce through the word and go beyond if we are to commune with each other, to share, partake in the problem that we are going to unroll, uncover, discuss.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n--krishnamurti (on freedom)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis paragraph has been in my head a lot these past few days. I thought i would share it with you because ive read in the paper that people are blaming the moslem community for the tuesday attacks. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni took a middle eastern politics course at carleton. my prof was quite the middle east expert. he was in his seventies, long since retired, volunteering for the job because no other prof at the school was qualified to teach middle eastern politics. he believed that because carleton had a large middle eastern community, comprising of egyptians, palastinians, israelis, iranians, that it was important that they offer a middle eastern course. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI did well in the course and learned a lot about what was going on in the region. However what was driven into me was how to use my language. lecture after lecture we discussed the language of the middle east. The use of the words \\\"arab\\\", \\\"jew\\\", \\\"moslem\\\"; even the term \\\"middle east\\\". I was exposed to orientalism(said) and in the end, i was no closer to any conclusions. perhaps the code would be easier to break if language didn\\\'t play as big a part in what was going on today in the middle east and now new york city and d.c. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nKrishnamurti was talking of the differences then, and now between the moslem and hindu communities of india, pakistan, and bangladesh. However i think what he has to say about that part of the world is relevent in almost every ethnic and tribal conflict.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe bibles tower of babel story talks of god scattering people all over the earth so they could not finish the tower. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe english language is massive. it is an extremly powerful engine through which emotions and ideas can be explained, exploited, and interpreted. \r\n</p>'),(1763,'<P>i am so angry. just because i dont want to live with his friends now. because it isnt my fault that i got fired or that i couldnt stsand my job and my sanity comes beforehand. i would rather live on my own...isnt it SANER that way...than to live with your lover.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so i am screwing everyone out of rent money. I have thought of you too much my dear. it is now time and moment for me. for thoughts of my graduation, of Iceland, of leaving and never leaving again.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>you make me so fucking mad.so i sit on the couch while you wash dishes something you NEVER do and I punch the air out of the bubbles that came with my roomies package.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Oh and PETER.yes I would appreciate if you could help me with a forum of my own. I dont think my better half is ever going to get it done.</P>'),(1764,'I readily associate smells.  And lately, I\\\'ve grown increasingly convinced that my olfactory sense is one of my best vehicles for recollection.\r\n<br><br>\r\nLately, I\\\'ve been taking stock of alot of the things in my life that I take for granted; its a good time for rememberance, particularly after this past week\\\'s immense hiccup of experiences.  The simultaneous events of late, the WTC incident, my moving to a new apartment, the end of a 4 year relationship with someone I loved- they\\\'ve all given me the most profound pause to stop and take a sort of metaphysical inventory of myself.  So, lately, my mind has been lingering over the minutia in my life, among other things.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThis morning, I was uncannily aware of the collective scents of my surroundings.  When my alarm clock went off, I was aware that my olfactory bulbs were firing salvos of memory, even at 5:50am, as the sweet Ikea-smell of new furniture in my apartment greeted me, groggily.  Catching the 1 train at 110th street, I was enveloped by that pungent stench I can only describe as Upper-West-Side-Subway-Stank; a smell I remember from catching that same train all through college, 6 years ago.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe bus smelled of hydraulic grease, my workplace like the brass-cleaner the doorman uses to buff the railings to a lustrious shine. The septic wafts of air emitted by the hospital were as familiar as an old friend.\r\n<br><br>\r\nOn my way in, I picked up a cup of coffee on the streetcorner, as I often do.  Immediately, I was grasped by that unique mixture of utility-grade coffee and street-vendor cups, still smelling of the stale doughnuts and bagels they sat all weekend with.  Its nice.  Memories of the past 500+ mornings of this same ritual came flooding in; entering work in the snow, in the 100 degree summer, in April\\\'s torrents, always clutching that same, generic-patterned paper cup.  Drinking my coffee by the East River, getting off the train from Brooklyn, getting off the train from the West Side, walking around my job\\\'s neighborhood on the East side, making the morning call to my significant other who has suddenly vanished from my life, not altogether terribly, all these things freely associated in my mind by smells, sights and thoughts subjugated to a lesser rank.\r\n<br><br>\r\nYes, smells awake some primordial sense of recognition in my mind, and cause me to remember things burried deep inside, things that I might never appreciate without a chance to collect and examine their scents. \r\n<br><br>\r\nAnd there are smells in my life that I long for and miss, terribly.  The smell of my childhood home, particularly the back yard.  The smell of dog at the end of my bed on waking up.  The lovely and immediate smell of in-love sex. The smell of ginger-bread cooking during holidays.  The smell of the Harlem River and carbon-fiber boats in my rowing gear.  The scent of antique engine parts and burnt oil from my friend\\\'s army-jeep.  The smell of my brother\\\'s cheap teenage cologne. The smell of the Jersey Shore. The smell of vegetables grilling.  The smell of autumn in Central Park, returning once again.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1765,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1765_1.jpg\'><br> This is me having the time of my life. What could be better than driving like a lunatic across the frozen sea? (near Mari\\\'s family summer cottage). I can tell you that it fair gets the adrenalin going when you accidentally drive over a fishing hole...Ye Ha!!\r\n\r\n'),(1766,'All images are<BR>\r\nconnected, like buildings on<BR>\r\ntoronto skyline.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nall words can dig in<BR>\r\nlike spider bites on my hand<BR>\r\ntoothsome surprises.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nall sounds resounding<BR>\r\nacross my tympanic ears<BR>\r\nyou know what i am<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nyou smell me, daybreak<BR>\r\ntaste me in the noon-time sun<BR>\r\ndrink me up at night.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI adore you my<BR>\r\nlittle play-thing man, and have<BR>\r\nyou now and again.<BR>\r\n\r\n<BR>\r\n'),(1767,'<P>i was sleeping at the los gatos club parking lot this morning.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i woke up today, before los gatos, in a favorite local hang out in\r\noakland next to a familiar girl and a cranky cat.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>it seems i could not sleep without dreaming out loud.  at 1:40 am i\r\nwoke up thinking i was having an allergic reaction to said cranky cat\r\nand was choking to death.  morbid, i attibute this to my girls roomate\r\nwho has been oh so morbid lately.  influenced dreams you know.  he\r\nwoke up the other day thinking terrorists had poisoned the water\r\nsupply.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then later at 5:00 am i woke up from a vivid dream i had been\r\nscouring lower marin looking for a 73 bmw r60 toaster top motorcycle\r\nlike dan\\\'s.  qq was there complaining about webmail.  which doesnt\r\nmake sense because ix is the one that complains about webmail.  but qq\r\nwas there and we were riding around alleys in his bronco.  he was\r\nmeeping and beeping.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>between the dreams i woke fitfully in a pool of my own sweat.  this\r\nnever happens anywhere but under the heavy comforter.  i am not a\r\n\\\"sweat sleeper\\\".  that thing is just too damn hot, even when the\r\nweather is cold.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the sweat and the dreams, i decided to rise and shower.  in the\r\nbathroom, i started the drier to warm my clothes.  showered.  wrote\r\ndown directions to the motorcycle in marin, if i left at 5:30 i might\r\nbe able to leave oakland, check it out, and be back through sf in time\r\nto beat traffic.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>instead i layed back down in bed and watched her face.  she is a\r\nsweetie.  i wanted to lay and watch the light move on her sleeping\r\nface, partly for me, partly for her.  she likes the light so much.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>elly says i am a wanter.  i want things, and that defines who i am.\r\nit is true.  i want, i don\\\'t get.  i think it is the natural\r\nevolution of my family.  my dad is a getter.  he wants something and\r\nhe gets it. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>example, \\\"Son, i have spent too much money on cars in my\r\nlife.\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am not the same way, i want something and i figure if i wait long\r\nenough, i will want something else.  keep doing that and i will never\r\nget anything.  that makes me a more responsible person financially and\r\nless of a consumer trawler weighted down with a huge net filled with\r\nplastic and metal junk.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>at 6:15 i left the bend in oakland.  hurtling down the 880 i\r\nremember meeting koz the mechanic and his 1970 bmw that he had rebuilt\r\nan unknown number of times but that he said was getting warn out from\r\nuse.  dan says bmw\\\'s run forever.  </p>\r\n\r\n<P> Dan says, \\\"they will idle in first gear all the way back to\r\ngermany.\\\" at about 3 or 4 miles per hour,  that would take around\r\ntwo years if you drove 8 hours a day and the ocean was dried up.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i used to like working on cars and motorcycles.  it is like\r\ninternet work or anything else that you can zone out concentrating.\r\ngrease gets up in your finger nails.  when done, it either works or\r\ndoesn\\\'t.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>in the mean time i started to fall asleep driving round about san\r\njose.  i pulled over in los gatos and stopped to sleep at the country\r\nclub.  that was the first parking lot off the highway.  perhaps that\r\nwas not such a hot idea.  rich people are such pricks when it comes to\r\nprotecting their plastic and metal.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i fell asleep with my hoodie pulled over my head to keep the rising\r\nsun out of my eyes.  i heard some time later a few beeps and jingles.\r\ni pulled up out of sleep and there was a tow truck getting ready to\r\nlift my car out of there with me in it.  talk about no class, don\\\'t\r\nlook at me.  he could have knocked first.  i checked him in reverse\r\nand then left the board.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>in the last leg of my trip driving on the 17, i was day\r\ndreaming in the misty dawn light about my huevita and i came within\r\ninches of laying a streak of champaign christina aguilera on the highway\r\ndivider, a little testament to our multi-cultural road ways.  i guess\r\nchristina got jealous that i was not paying enough attention to\r\nher.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the rest of the trip went fine and i got to work on time.  we are\r\nall supposed to be here at 8:00am.  as far as i can tell, only one\r\nperson cares about this and she is the boss.  she is consistently not\r\nhere at 8, those 10 or so times i have been here to confirm, nor is\r\nanyone else, but she is the only one that acts like she is here when\r\nshe sends out emails about company policy.  after i was hired, she\r\ngave me a paper to sign that said i was working for X number of\r\ndollars and that i would be here at 8 every day.  i separated in my\r\nmind what seemed to clearly be two documents.</P>\r\n'),(1768,'<p>bowl of cereal.</p>\r\n<p>dog hair on the floor [well, everywhere really].</p>\r\n<p>dreams of being lost...the truck blew up. it was night. i walk through empty scary parking lots. someone stops, not to help but to give me money....they think i am a homeless person. do i look that slobby i wonder.</p>\r\n<p>the dog wakes me a total of four times. i do not want to be awake. this is my day off. i want to sleep in. i wake...at 6am, at 7:30, at 9, then 10:15. something tells me that maybe i should just get up and start the day.</p>\r\n<p>cloudy light filters through fog into my room.</p>\r\n<p>thoughts of running away to Baja to live on the beach enter my mind. would i be there alone? does it matter? feelings for someone have invaded my head, my heart. i want to wake up to the ocean waves. and him curved against my back, an\r\narm wrapped protectively around me. where did this come from? what happens next?</p>\r\n<p>there is dog hair on the University of Oklahoma sweatshirt i am wearing.</p>\r\n<p>i am still sleepy. i lay my head on my arms at the edge of my desk. the table shakes. i watch my wiggly dashboard hula girl do her thing.</p>\r\n<p>i am thinking about listening to music...massive attack, johnny cash, fleetwood mac, odb, coltrane, son house. i cannot make up my mind. i listen to my gurgling stomach instead.</p>\r\n<p>cereal it says. must have cereal.</p>'),(1769,'<p>At a party on Saturday night, the sort of local band scene at a downstairs neighbor\\\'s garret-of-sorts.  A bunch of boys live there. \\\"Six of us pay rent,\\\" is how they put it.  And they charge admission, $1 for a can of beer, and have some guys wailing on guitars and saxophones, and one act where a girl sings along to eighties hits accompanied by an accordion while we all hoot and holler.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I walk in and am confronted by one of the most insanely theatrical people I think I\\\'ve ever known. Her name is Julia, and she\\\'s like the love child of Robin Williams and Phyllis Diller.  It\\\'s hard to know what to make of her. She says she\\\'s here recording her solo demo.  Says she\\\'s gonna be a rock star.  Micah says she will, too, because it\\\'s people like her who are just relentlessly kooky enough, always \\\"on,\\\" who have the drive to make it.  Yeah, I said, they either make it or their relentless looniness lands them in the loony bin or the gutter when success comes too slowly or at too high a price. Anyway, Julia was SO GLAD to see me, and she ALWAYS LOVED me in school and were all my loved ones safe? oh good and WE JUST HAVE TO TALK but apparently the cacaphony of instrument wailing was preventing her from being wildly expressive self so she said she\\\'d find me later, and I went back outside to smoke.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Smoking has taken on new meaning again.  I\\\'m destroying myself because the world wants to destroy itself. Yeah, that sounds like a good reason this week.  I\\\'ve been making excuses since I started. I started smoking because I wanted to kill myself.  Like any healthy teenager realizing they\\\'re gay in a cruel and intolerant world, I wanted nothing more than to waste away hours in the woods with my friends, who also wanted to kill themselves for various reasons, or simply to feel like they were doing their part in speeding along their deaths because adolescence was so fucking unbearable.  My parents never smoked. Well, my dad did, sometime in 50s, for ten years or so, during his first marriage, and he never talks about the marriage, and all he\\\'s said about the smoking was that he quit cold-turkey one day when he found himself searching the house, in ashtrays and under furniture, for butts. Throughout my childhood my father might as well have been a campaigner for the American Lung Association. O how he proselytized in restaurants if any breath of smoke drifted from the smoking section to the non-smoking section. He thought the smokers should all be stuck in a sealed, glass-walled room, suffering in a cloud of their evil, much as they are today in airport smoking lounges. So when I was 16 and stuck for a reason to keep on living, I started bumming smokes from friends.  Then I started getting a cough all winter long and then they tell me I have asthma and then I move to New York for college where everyone smokes and I befriend smokers (because often the ones who are nihilist and cynical enough to smoke are also the most amusing companions), and I\\\'m able to drink everywhere I go, which just begets more smoking, and by the end of six months there I was thoroughly disgusted with myself. I got a smoker for a roommate, cause all the older kids I knew said to sign up as a smoker even if you don\\\'t plan to smoke all the time, cause smokers are guaranteed to be cooler, and we were allowed to smoke in our rooms as long as all agreed. I told him it was fine as long as windows were open, but that early morning stench, before I awoke, as he lit up the first butt of the day, that really got to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So now I\\\'m smoking, continuing to smoke really, because there\\\'s a war on. It\\\'s part of the war effort, I say. I\\\'m contributing more tax dollars to the troubled US economy, paying them back for that stupid Rebate for America\\\'s Workers they just sent me, cause Old Glory needs all she can get now to fight back the evil A-rabs. (I know, not all A-rabs are evil, but the evil ones are comin for us. They think we\\\'re trying to destroy the Islamic world and spew our Anglo-Saxon Christian piss all over them.)  I\\\'m going outside to smoke at parties and I\\\'m not sure I can stop now because suddenly the helm has been stolen away from me again. Nothing seems in my control again, and I might as well be sixteen and alone and just glad to have a few understanding friends who think being gay is just fine by them.  Jerry Falwell thinks it\\\'s all my fault, and he can go fuck himself with the purple dildo he keeps under the mattress for all I care because I\\\'m hurrying to my doom. Yes I want to lead a long and productive life, and when I find love again I\\\'ll want to live long and everything will be filled with promise, but then I\\\'ll lose love again probably, because that seems to be how it goes, and at least cigarettes will be there again to keep me company. Christ, that is how it will go, huh.  I always said, back when I was nineteen and cocky, that alcoholism remained vaguely charming until you turned 25 or so, and then it starts to look pathetic. But here I am, 25, and the pathetic years await me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ashley\\\'s outside, on his tenth beer and starting to sway.  He\\\'s going back and forth, from inside to outside, looking for some girl with pigtails and she seems to have left. I leave him chatting with some guy about their dogs, and go back in for more beer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I return to find Julia over near the bathroom, laughing and wriggling and shaking her tits in my face. She introduces me to her friend Nathan, who I think is flirting with me but I really can\\\'t be sure, because he\\\'s drunk and also is, apparently, going to be a rock star. Julia can\\\'t stop with the jokes and the stories and the funny voices and shit. She\\\'s up on tip-toe and then she\\\'s down on the floor again, imitating a fish or a lizard or something.  I\\\'m stoned and drunk now and all I can do is smile and nod and mumble things. Apparently everything I say is absolutely hilarious, though, and she wants to get together FOR LUNCH because IT\\\'LL BE SO MUCH FUN.  I take off my pullover and she stars feeling my arm, tellin me I\\\'ve been working out and isn\\\'t it marvelous how you can not see someone for five years and be like WOW YOU\\\'RE FAT or WOW YOU\\\'VE BEEN WORKING OUT, HONEY!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the kitchen, on the counter facing the band is a half-eaten cake that bears the message, in yellow frosting, \\\"HAVE A SUPER WAR!!\\\"  I\\\'m sort of amused. The cynical Gen X piece of me is amused, but I think sometimes I should whip that piece of me into submission, shove it in a closet and say, \\\"The Gay Piece stood it in there long enough, now it\\\'s your turn!! Stay in there and shut up!\\\"  But I can\\\'t help it. I have an ironic sensibility like the rest of the assholes in my age group.  We like seeing messages of sheer, absurd wit on cakes just like we like hearing a girl singing Laura Branigan\\\'s \\\"Gloria\\\" with an accordion.  I hear snippets of conversation here and there. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"I don\\\'t think we should invade man. Just bomb the bastards.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\\\"The Islamic world could rally against us, and then we\\\'d be screwed. Pakistan\\\'s got nukes.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"The Afghans are all waiting to be bombed. People are fleeing the country like crazy. What good is that gonna do?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Is it politically correct to call the Pakistani\\\'s \\\'Paks\\\'?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I leave the party behind. I return upstairs to stuff my face and watch Ab Fab on tape.  I go to bed with a full stomach and have nightmares, weird, circular, feverish dreams about getting a car and running for the border and something about hot dogs.  I wake up wondering what\\\'s happened now. My hangover takes on an irrational, panicky tone.  I\\\'m worried about the water again.  I see an aerial shot of a crater full of rubble in an urban landscape and I immediately think HOLY SHIT WHAT CITY IS THAT IS THAT CHICAGO??? but I quickly realize that it\\\'s just new footage of the same old shit, \\\"Ground Zero\\\" which is what we are now supposed to call Lower Manhattan, where I used to go to Century 21 to buy discount designer underwear.  Where I went just 3 weeks ago, in fact, to buy underwear, and use an ATM in the mall underneath the towers whose shops are now filled with detritus and blood. Disembodied hands and feet.  I keep thinking about the real estate.  Poor bastard real estate speculators that have been trying to sell Lower Manhattan for years as The New SoHo.  Shit outta luck now.  But maybe that\\\'s the crazy thing about New York. The apartments will have a view of a pile of rubble for years to come, but the property values won\\\'t go down. They\\\'ll just plateau.  After the smoke clears, the selling points will include the new state-of-the-art entertainment complex and the World Trade Center Memorial Towers which will be built much taller than the originals, large phalluses like middle fingers pointed higher at the world. We\\\'re America and the tallest buildings in the world are now in New York again.  You did us a favor, you silly terrorists. You got those ugly, out-dated monstrosities out of the way so that we could rebuild! Bigger and better, that\\\'s America.  You won\\\'t be able to take these buildings down with a couple of flimsy 767s, no sir.  These are gonna be some missile-proof motherfucking towers, a mile high and painted with the stars and stripes, all bright and shiny.  We will not be terrorized, friends.  It\\\'s time for a change. New new new. Brand new fixtures and everything.</p>\r\n'),(1770,'<p>Goat paid me an Everything bagel with sundried tomato cream cheese to go romping through the bookstores with her. The magazine rack at cody\\\'s distracts me for hours; I catch up with the art world and the feminists. I flipped between Shirin Neshat, Laurie Andersen (\\\"her secret to success is never being afraid to fail\\\"), and Bjork, the mere image of whom always injects me with renewed purpose. Photographs of women fill my head. Women in makeup. Women in dog collars. Women on mirrors. Women selling themselves and each other.</p>\r\n<p>When I got home, jay pointed out a photo of a girl in our high school yearbook. Did you know her? Stephanie Sanders. yes. Well, she\\\'s dead now.</p>\r\n<p> If he hadnt pointed out the picture, I may not have remembered her by name. I remember her skin. It was perfectly smooth, and looked like it was wrapped tightly like cellophane against her bones, her tight pinched nose and smoothed over with careful love by a porcelain ceramicist. The glaze is a honey rose at the cheekbones and nose, perfect ivory for the rest. This sounds like a skin care ad, but that is really how her skin looked. She had marble blue eyes and curly dark hair. I don\\\'t remember anything else about her.</p>\r\n<p>I was angry this morning. He used to dress me up in words until the bodice got too tight to breathe. I, in turn, dressed him up in a red dress and took pictures of his dick. lo, it passeth. With the help of The Bangs and the new Blake Babies. </p>\r\n<p><i>You can say I\\\'m anti-everything. I just dont think the world owes him something.</i></p>\r\n<p>I am trying to keep my temper from exploding ham because I want to shoot this movie before rice-boy goes out of town. David Mamet says that no character opens his mouth without wanting something. Most importantly, I want to master my own anger so that I can direct him. Last time, we almost called off shooting for the day because I tried to strangle him. This time, the strangling is written into the script.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1770_1.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1770_2.jpg\' height=\'150\' align=\'left\'>\r\n<i> And every time I try to hide\r\nI get caught from way inside<br>\r\nand walk down the aisle like some stupid bride.</i></p>\r\n<p>I used to fantasize about revolutionizing the director-actress cliche. That whoever dates me should expect to be in my movies, or that whoever is in my movies should expect to date me. Like a Godard or Peter Bogdanovich. Hot young tarts for me to mold. yeah honey, bend over just a little more. But he was too much of a prima donna. please baby please baby please baby please. It made me never want to shit where I ate again.</p>\r\n<p><i>what do girls want?<br>\r\na heart to haunt<br>\r\na new repetition<br>\r\nto cut the superstition</i></p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m aware of the need for reverse subjugation. you, movie star. me, movie czar. To take hold of this medium that has wrapped us in birthday cakes and mud flaps is only one minor action, and I\\\'m not winning anything by simply pointing a camera. I have to ask the right questions. I have to dismantle the language of objects that I\\\'ve learned from the movies.</p>\r\n<p>When I put myself under the camera too, I am a surgeon on the slab. Everything is at risk - my vanity, my credibility, my art-ego. If I am willing, then noone else should be able to refuse. </p>\r\n<p><i>you cant make my ears bleed this time</i></p>\r\n<p>Ugh. there are other questions to answer. I\\\'ve already drawn up new scripts in my head to go with my new luv. You want to make art with me? gulp. wow, that\\\'s hot. But we were just having so much fun. Get ready for more sweat.</p>\r\n<p><i>you cant make my mouth move this time</i></p>'),(1771,'I took acid and went to see The Big Wu last night. I don\\\'t usually do a whole lot of drugs, but I had some left over from a concert I went to on my birthday, immediately before which I broke one of my cardinal rules: never buy hard drugs from a man with prison tattoos. What can i say? It seemed like the thing to do at the time. It\\\'s pretty good though. The band was also good. They played some of their own stuff, which really doesn\\\'t have a definable style; it goes from bluegrassy type stuff to more radio-friendly rock, bouncy pop songs, etc. \r\n<p>\r\nAfter the show, I made my ol\\\' buddy David drive me home, because I\\\'d been drinking, and we sat around discussing politics, food, and my new band. I am still trying to come up with a name. Maybe you can help! Actually calling it a band may not be entirely accurate, since it is just me and a drummer so far, and a bass player who hasn\\\'t quite made it out yet but is supposed to on Wednesday. I am so serious about doing this, and this is as close as I\\\'ve come in well over a year to putting all the pieces together. Oh God, please just let me have a band. I love to play, and being the lead guitar, everyone in the band is following you and trying to complement what you are saying. It is like being the captain of your own little spaceship, flying wherever you choose, wherever the moment takes you.\r\n<p>\r\nObviously, the drugs have not quite cleared my system completely.\r\n<p>\r\nOh yes God, also please let things be cool when I call Margaret up this week and ask her out to lunch. Really that\\\'s all for right now.\r\n<p>\r\nWait Latin is getting pretty hard too. But seriously Big Guy, I got it after that.\r\n<p>\r\nLast night I had a dream that I remembered in the morning. I was in an undefined, vaguely scientific looking place under the ocean, and Drew Barrymore was there. She liked me, I could tell, and I was suppoesed to go do something, and then I\\\'d come back and we would spend more time together. When I got back, she was shooting a water pistol at me, and making faces, and running away. Why you wanna do me like that, baby?\r\n<p>\r\nacid dreams. a-sssssss-id.'),(1772,'<i>While she was in NYC, I got a chance to play with Egg\\\'s Polaroid Sx-70, briefly.  Fortuitously, I picked one up for $10 in the Village the next weekend, thinking I\\\'d use it to document things that required a quick-and-easy picture, like self-potraits while tripping. As a picture is worth a thousand words, I\'ll leave it at that; the only text I\\\'ve posted here is whatever I scrawled on the back of each photo at the moment it was taken.  Cheesy? Yes. But, I thought they were interesting, and wanted to share a departure from some of the usual stoned-prose.</i>\r\n<hr>\r\n<br>\r\n<b>E:</b><br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_1.jpg\'><br>\r\nMdma what a beauty you are, your name is remembered. Open, chakras, emotions, feelings.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_2.jpg\'><br>\r\nA Peter, so at one with self. A peace, so aligned in concentric circles, slowly spinning like precision watch-works clocking the passing time.  Monotony stacatto like breathless taxi horns, the present is valuable but as sustaining as ocean salt.  But, today\\\'s heroic moments are the grains of sand on tomorrow\\\'s beaches, the hour-glass, the sailboat.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_3.jpg\'><br>\r\nIrony the Cat<br>\r\nsharpens his claws on my soul<br>\r\nand heals like white light<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_4.jpg\'><br>\r\nJust because you don\\\'t always like how things are going doesn\\\'t mean that they aren\\\'t going <i>okay</i>. Maybe they could be better, but they\\\'re not bad or messed up. Trust yourself, trust this final realization. Be calm.\r\n<br><br> \r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_5.jpg\'><br>\r\nThe insights when the clouds clear do ring true to an extent. See? You\\\'re just uncovering what was already known, there.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_6.jpg\'><br>\r\nA heart so special, so wonderful, blessed and lucky.  The rhythmic palpitations of heart; the rhythm of breath; the cycle of life; the rings of Saturn; beauty is all.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<hr>\r\n<b>The K-Hole:</b><br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_7.jpg\'><br>\r\nFree your tomorrows by liberating your todays. Ok. Then you die. The point is?\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_8.jpg\'><br>\r\nGod whispers reason to you in infinities as he drinks from your soul. He smiles, makes a slurping sound with the straw after he\'s finished you off.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_9.jpg\'><br>\r\nExploding rigid heart, your rigid, my rigid stalagmites of flesh, translating a testimony to death. Entomed in a womb-spire. I\'m gone. Hello!\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_10.jpg\'><br>\r\nGo on, take my flesh as lagniappe. Holidays are tests for requiems, staunch horns of death, tinkling, prickling suffocation.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_11.jpg\'><br>\r\nSpirits are the grand finale for paradigms; die, dying, dead. My childhood life was a tool allowed to rust. Each new day is bribed beauty. Each sunrise, crimson-neon death. Plasma, placentas, returning.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1772_12.jpg\'><br>\r\nNeonatal.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1773,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1773_1.jpg\r\n\'><br>This was back in Scotland about 5 or 6 years ago. In a day of drunken sledging we used a multitude of slippery items to satisfy our desire for speed. Great fun.\r\n'),(1774,'<p>I am living in separation, counting the hours I\'ve lived<br>\r\n  or they the hours since I died. This is day one. Again. Hopefully the last.<br>\r\n  And just last Sunday I found the last poem<br>\r\n  From the girl who went from being my mirror to being vicious<br>\r\n  the one that wins me over again. Letters always get burned, Poems are the first \r\n  to go.<br>\r\n  Now I\'m just <a href=\"http://greenplastic.com/lyrics/songs/truelovewaits.html\">waiting.</a> \r\n  Trying not to break from one second to the next.<br>\r\n  The pain from the tattoo on my chest has ceased. The tattoo says I\'m Not In \r\n  Love.<br>\r\n  The post script is to sing <a href=\"http://lenny.dyadel.net/lou2.htm#n5\">New \r\n  Sensations</a> </p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.thedent.com/jpgs/y2000/booknewage.jpg\"></p>'),(1775,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1775_1.jpg\'><br>\r\nThis is obviously the aerial fertiliser again (mostly N,P,K @650Kg/ha) in North Scotland. That helicopter is a single engine Llima, the only helicopter that has the ability to lift more than it\\\'s own weight (so the pilot told me) It was a bit ropey, to be honest. I bit like an aviation farm tractor. '),(1776,'<p>i don\\\'t know about you, but i prefer the old time ethnic stereotypes, friendly mexican mice that run fast and say things like \\\"arriba, arriba\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s just so hard to get used to the new ones.</P>\r\n'),(1777,'every wave is a spiral\r\nrubber ducky bobbing in a circle<br>\r\nno land as far as baby can see<br>\r\njust the sun careening<br>\r\nhaphazard on ocean skin.<br>\r\n<BR>\r\ni\\\'m always worried about sharks<br>\r\neven in the bathtub i get creeped out<br>\r\nsomething about the shapes and patterns on the wall<br>\r\nso i\\\'ll just find my way under<br>\r\nthis is my soul arrival.<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nlearn to breathe the water<br>\r\nand to bite into juicy white legs<br>\r\nlet the blood draw me in<br>  \r\ndrink it like a salty wine.<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nevery wave is a spiral.<br>\r\nevery shock feeds survival.<br>\r\nevery wave is a spiral.<br>'),(1778,'<PRE>\r\nPimpin\\\' myself, break\r\ndancin\\\' to the corporate \r\ncrowd, I salute, dead.\r\n\r\nOakiefenokie\r\ndrains like some ancient\r\nsinus, watershed.\r\n\r\na triple-barrel\r\nsalute to the vanquished mom\\\'s\r\nof tomorrow\\\'s now.\r\n\r\nHappiness is when\r\nopening the student loan\r\npayment, only the\r\n\r\nleast expensive is\r\ndue, immediately, and\r\nonly forty bucks\r\n\r\nis due, lucky you.\r\nnow go to bed and sleep, dream\r\nof sweet nothings now.\r\n</PRE>'),(1779,'<P>it is getting late, but before i go to bed i just want to say that i can smell big stinky rowan laying on the floor.  he is sighing often.  he groans.  i want to put a few wrestling moves on him.  pin him.  give him a huge hug.  he is big stinky rowan.  love him, dont try to change him.</P>\r\n<P>i went to karate tonight and did some stuff.  i am scared i will not be here much longer.  i am only scared for my karate lessons because i like them and there is such an emphasis on commitment.  i guess i can go somewhere else up in san francisco or oakland or wherever i end up.  maybe i will even get this pointed out to me by egg in a year when she makes me eat my words for saying i was not going to be down here very long.</P>\r\n<P>really i could stay here a while, but hate the drive from oakland.  it sucks ass.  the drive from sf is not so bad, there is never any traffic.</P>\r\n<PRE>\r\negglit:	hi\r\negglit:	its past your bedtime\r\nrobotec9:	it is\r\nrobotec9:	what time is it?\r\n11:35 PM \r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>i should go to bed.  i am up chatting.  not sure why.  i had energy after karate and going to sushi with kat.  it is nervous energy.  sex energy?  i\\\'m not sure.</P>\r\n<P>soon i have to consider if i want to quit my job or rent an efficiency in scotts valley.  it is a pretty big decision because most housing contracts last about a year and breaking them you loose your deposit.  maybe i can find a month to month.</P>\r\n<P>i should really go to bed.  i have to be at work at 8:45 for a group photo.  i think that is the most moronic thing i have heard of in my work career.  pictures at 8:45 am.</P>\r\n<P>the horror</P>\r\n'),(1780,'<p>I\\\'ve bumped into you too many times to not notice. Book in hand, flashing eyes. Mischievous smile. I was that geek girl sitting by herself, waiting for the right ship to stowaway on. You had rodeo written all over the way you walked, and hearing your voice was like coming home to an old friend.</p>\r\n<p>The air between us was electric with things unsaid.</p>\r\n<p> </p>\r\n<p>Are there words and worlds for us to find each other again?</p>\r\n'),(1781,'<p>\r\nafter cleaning the fingerprint smudges and dirt off the wall corners, door frames, outlets, and switches, i was looking for adventure. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntelling my grandma i was just going out to inspect the grounds i headed out. in my white t-shirt with \\\"cape cod\\\" written in blue across the chest, blue shorts, and messy hair i gave praise to jah in the driveway. there was a light rain, but it certainly wasnt enough for me to call off plans for high adventure. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni gave the patch of grass i was trying to turn green a look asking myself if it looked any greener. it might have. im not too sure. the front yard was all clear. nothing much happens on the front lawn. a few years ago i might have been able to say i saw some field mice, but these days i dont let the flower beds grow over. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npondering my situation for a few minutes more then intended i decided to go check the bird feeders out in the back. my grandma pointed out this morning that a blue jay was sitting on one of my feeders hogging all the food. the blue jay here is the equivelant of the cardinal in st louis. its toronto\\\'s \\\"bird\\\", so people use all sorts of zany methods to attract them(ok im not sure anyone cares, but i thought id spice it up a bit).\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthinking id take a look again i headed down the side of my house past the less than blooming plants. taking a few drags from a cig i turn the corner. the adventure begins. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni turn the corner and i see a black cat having a stand-off with my cat whose sitting in my moms window. his tail is all puffed up and he isnt meowing. my cat meows. when i picked him up at the humain society they told me he talked a lot. they were not shitting me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe black cat looks at me and backs up. I had sauntered around the corner at a good clip, abruptly stopping in my tracks. the black cat looked at me, i looked at my cat, my cat looked at the black cat, the black cat turned to my cat. you could feel the tension in the air. the birds had stopped feeding and were all fixed on the situation at hand.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni decided to go on the offensive. according to my grandmother the black cat hides in the bushes and kills birds feeding on the feeders i so generously provide. i do not want the neighbourhood cats thinking my backyard is a killing fields. black cat had to be made the precident for future bird attacks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni lunged forward and said in the best dirty hairy voice i could muster \\\"get the fuck outta here punk\\\" and i chased the cat three houses. during that run i had managed to pick up a few dirt balls. no direct hits, but im sure the thing was mad scared.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i returned to the backyard, my cat was meowing away. \\\"dont worry maxie, i got your back.\\\"\r\n</p>'),(1782,'<p>I\\\'m back to my normal life again.  Today\\\'s the first day back to work.  Back to the same day, but it isn\\\'t quite.....</P><P>I\\\'ve been spending today trying to ignore the acrid taste in my mouth from the remaining dust in the air, not thinking about the massive amounts of asbestos used in those buildings, playing up the fact that I\\\'ve got a cough and stuffed nose, so people aren\\\'t suspicious if I call in sick tomorrow.  Most of all I\\\'m avoiding people because I\\\'m <i>fucking sick to death of talking about what happened last week!!!</I>  For some reason people have been really pissing me off lately.  The fucking candle-light vigils.  The shows of concern.  The ubiquitious questions repeated again and again.  <i><b>Fuck off and leave me alone.</b></i></p><P>Deborah makes me happy though.  Well..... she makes me happy and frustrated.  I call.  She doesn\\\'t call back, so I call again.  She doesn\\\'t call back, so I call again, and she\\\'s home.  We talk for hours.  We laugh.  I smile and feel comfortable in my skin again.  <i>When can I see you?</i> I ask.  <i>I don\\\'t know,</I> she says.  Fuck...... I\\\'m not used to all this chasing bullshit.  Sometimes I wish I were a girl.  Most people just aren\\\'t worth all this effort.</P><P>The other thing that makes me happy is the package I recieved from Toshiba today.  My dad would congratulate me on my capitilistic sentiments.  The Satellite 3005-s303.  Dropped in an extra 256mb ram.  The first computer I\\\'ve ever owned.</p><P>My boss just told me to go home.  Go figure.  Even being told that pissed me off.</P>'),(1783,'<p>I\\\'m sitting here in my CPS530 lecture, attempting not to pass out, attempting to care about decipher my prof\\\'s thick accent in order to learn about Containers Canvasses and Layout Managers.</p>\r\n<p>I am not succeeding.</p>\r\n<p>I am having more fun noisily ripping up a copy of <i>Metro</i> with my feet.  I\\\'m laughing internally; waiting for Matt or Zoli to ask me what I\\\'m doing so that I may answer with: <b><i>\\\"Building a nest.\\\"</i></b></p>\r\n<p>I need more sleep.  I\\\'m not regretting the choice to sleep through my Moral Philosophy lecture this morning.  Those extra two hours of sleep were blissful.</p>\r\n<p>Okay, back to the nest-buildlng.</p>\r\n'),(1784,'\\\"Hey man, every Wednesday! I see you every Wednesday night\\\", said Eddie, although I\\\'d passed him on my way home every night for years. \\\"Can you help a brother out?\\\" he asked, as I walked right by, not looking- you can\\\'t look or concede a bit if you\\\'re a <i>true</i> New Yorker, you know.<br><br>\r\n\\\"Hey, how\\\'s about sharing a slice of that pizza with me?\\\" he asked, although I clearly carried none- never had, not in that part of town.<br><br>\r\n\\\"Humph\\\", I exhaled in a long sigh, and walked back to my apartment, carefully unlocking all three locks on my way in; locking them, one by one, just as carefully after I was safely tucked away inside.<br>'),(1785,'<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nwhat to do, what to do,</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nIâ€™m wondering how things got to be the way that they are- Iâ€™m wondering why things feel so dream like and not at all real most of the time-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nhave I told you that most of the time I donâ€™t even feel real?- that most of the time Iâ€™m caught in a perpetual out of body type of feeling?-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nyou know what I mean?, like most of the time I donâ€™t even feel like I have control over myself-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nI donâ€™t know man, itâ€™s weird-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nlike most of the time Iâ€™m kinda in the back seat watching, uninvolved- uninvolved and watching, thinking- saying to myself, and thinking, what the hell are you doing?- I donâ€™t know man, seriously-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nitâ€™s weird- and Iâ€™m trying- really Iâ€™m trying- Iâ€™m  trying not to be sad and get more involved and, I donâ€™t know, maybe, like, be good- to do good and try to be better, but, I donâ€™t know- itâ€™s hard-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand maybe if I werenâ€™t so bored (and, by the way, the movie home page is on the television, right now, as Iâ€™m typing,</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nthatâ€™s kinda funny)- so, yeah, bored- I donâ€™t know-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nsan francisco!, youâ€™re putting me to sleep!- never mind, san francisco, that itâ€™s two thirty in the morning and I probably should be asleep, but-</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ngawd.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nIâ€™m not even tired,</p>'),(1786,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1786_1.jpg\'><br>\r\nThis is Mari and I ascending Stac Polidh, a very impressive mountain on the West coast of Scotland, about 5 years ago. It was beautiful clear weather when we left Inverness, but obviously it was pissing down when we got out to the West. We went with 2 Finnish friends Eva and Marjo. Despite the rain it was a worthwhile trip! It was a pity when we got to the peak that it was impossible to see the view.\r\n\r\n'),(1787,'<p>The following comes at Peter\\\'s request.  It\\\'s also very short, again at Peter\\\'s request.</p><p>\r\nWhen I was going to school in Idaho, I had a friend, Cathy.  Every single time I called Cathy, her roommate, Tara, would answer the phone.  After a while, Tara and I started chatting more and more, and I eventually asked her out on a date.  She seemed cool, and she had told me that she was tall, blond, and a swimmer.</p><p>Since she had a car and I didn\\\'t, on the evening of our date, I was waiting for her in the lobby of my fraternity.  (Yes, I\\\'m a frat boy.)  Now, fraternities being what they are, a lot of different people come in and out of them.  When I saw a tall, blond girl walking towards our front door, I thought, \\\"Oh please God, don\\\'t let that be her.\\\"</p><p>Tara and I went out that night and had a mediocre time.  After that night, we never really talked again.</p>'),(1788,'I\\\'ve been romping around my flat.  I still feel somewhat breathless from my little romp.  I\\\'ve been feeling really stressed lately, and I have no idea why as I have basically nothing in my sordid life that should be causing me stress now. I\\\'ve been shakin my skinny white booty around the old place, listening to Basement Jaxx. Stressed. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI wish i had a watermelon that needed smashing, or some big beams to practice karate-chops on. You know, I think I do more when I\\\'m stressed, although thats notto say I accomplish anythign mroe than usual. I\\\'ve been running alot recently, playing the guitar, chain smoking, watching the weather change through my big picture window, trying to fix my mobile. Anything to disappate my mystery stress.  Maybe its the mystery itself that has me bothered.  I\\\'m to where I even wash the dishes everytime there\\\'s a dirty dish in the sink nowadays. Anything. Its amazing that I actually care. I don\\\'t even cook.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI want to go work in a zoo. What fun that could be. With all the monkeys and zebras and ocelots and everything else, and just think of the crazy jokes I could tell all the guys who used to tell me that thats where I belonged in the first place? I wanted to work in the zoo when i was about eight, but then I got bitten by a cat. Then I wanted to be a lawyer. Then my father got sued. Then I wanted to be a dentist. Then I got braces. Now I want to work in a zoo. I\\\'ll probably get eaten my an Emu.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nDammit, now i\\\'m stressed again. \r\n'),(1789,'I\\\'ve been romping around my flat. I still feel somewhat breathless from my little romp. I\\\'ve been feeling really stressed lately, and I have no idea why as I have basically nothing in my sordid life that should be causing me stress now. I\\\'ve been shakin my skinny white booty around the old place, listening to Basement Jaxx. Stressed. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI wish i had a watermelon that needed smashing, or some big beams to practice karate-chops on. You know, I think I do more when I\\\'m stressed, although thats not to say I accomplish anything more than usual. I\\\'ve been running alot recently, playing the guitar, chain smoking, watching the weather change through my big picture window, trying to fix my mobile. Anything to disappate my mystery stress. Maybe its the mystery itself that has me bothered. I\\\'m to where I even wash the dishes everytime there\\\'s a dirty dish in the sink nowadays. Anything. Its amazing that I actually care. I don\\\'t even cook. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI want to go work in a zoo. What fun that could be. With all the monkeys and zebras and ocelots and everything else, and just think of the crazy jokes I could tell all the guys who used to tell me that thats where I belonged in the first place? I wanted to work in the zoo when i was about eight, but then I got bitten by a cat. Then I wanted to be a lawyer. Then my father got sued. Then I wanted to be a dentist. Then I got braces. Now I want to work in a zoo. I\\\'ll probably get eaten my an Emu. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nDammit, now i\\\'m stressed again. \r\n<BR><BR>'),(1790,'<pre>\r\ni don\\\'t know what to write, but i want to write.  i can think of no witty things to say today, no funny stories to relate.  actually, i don\\\'t ever write witty things.   i usually write about relationships beginning or ending, or i make up silly urban-inspired poems for y\\\'all kids.  \r\n\r\ni\\\'ve known this shel silverstein poem by heart since forever:\r\n\r\nThe saddest thing that i ever did see\r\nwas a woodpecker peckin\\\' on a plastic tree\r\nhe looked at me and, \\\"Friend,\\\" said he,\r\n\\\"things just aren\\\'t as sweet as they used to be.\\\"\r\n\r\nto tell you the truth, i am bored.  but i\\\'m not writing just because i have nothing else to do.  \r\n\r\ni have something to say, but i\\\'m not sure yet what it is.  \r\n\r\n\r\ni wonder if i will look back and with no regrets.\r\n\r\ni wonder if i will ever understand.\r\n\r\ni wonder if i will meet her.\r\n\r\ni wonder if i am a hermit.\r\n\r\ni wonder if \r\n\r\ni wonder \r\n\r\ni wonder if riding a skateboard to yoga is poor bodily and mental preparation for the class.\r\n\r\n(bigger wheels on skateboards have an advantage.  they are faster, and are easier to ride over rough terrain with.  the extra weight is negligible.  old school soul skating is way cooler anyways.)\r\n\r\ni wonder why girls take pictures of their pussies and put them on the internet.\r\n</pre>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1790_1.gif\'>'),(1791,'book #2 entry #4 3/16/00 15:30 Thursday, Starbucks at the University of Buffalo\r\n\r\nI had sex with Jo.  I never wanted it.  I never fantasized about it, and in fact, I have always planned on avoiding it.  But unfortunately there were many factors working against me; 1.  I hadn\\\'t had sex in over a year -ouch!  2.  I was under the influence of alcohol -this has always made even the least attractive of woman seem desirable.  3.  We were in NYC on spring break -this contributed to a feeling of: what happens here is not what happens in Buffalo.  4.  We were sleeping in the same bed -from now on I\\\'ll avoid that -female flesh is female flesh and is simply too tempting (this is probably the real reason) and 5.  She wanted it -she just kept sending these really obvious signals that she was there for the taking.  But what it really comes down to is a lack of self control, a trait I?ve exhibited in the past and in many, many, different forms.  I thought that I had learned my lesson, and maybe now I have.  Since even though Jo wants a relationship, I was able to successfully convey that it was not to be.   And as usual, I was able to do so without making her feel too bad, and I wonder if she didn\\\'t agree with me deep down..................\r\n\r\nNow if only she\\\'d stop trying to hold my hand!  Oh, if you want details I can tell you, it was disgusting.  It started out innocently enough.  I though it no harm to hold her through the night.  It\\\'s been so long since I\\\'ve held someone, and I think it comforted her as well.  But then we found ourselves sweltering in a violent room temperature that must have far exceeded 130 degrees Fahrenheit.  This caused our sweat glands to kick in, and soon the scent of diluted uric acid wafted forth from in-between our tightly compacted bodies.  And of course this provided the perfect excuse for her to peal of her limp, sweat laden T-shirt.\r\n\r\nI was repulsed and intrigued at the same time.  My hand brushed against her tiny breasts, she murmured in pleasure and turned around to bestow a noxious kiss upon my own mucous laden lips.  Her breath was like that smell that drifts out of the high school science room during the frog and grasshopper dissections.  As disgusting as it was, and even as I was chocking back the bail in the back of my throat (as I do now in recreating this horrible affair), it brought back fond memories of my grandmother.  It was like kissing my grandmother.  \\\"Oh, my how you\\\'ve grown, come over here and give me a hug and a kiss.\\\"  And then my 86 year old grandmother is ramming her grandmotherly tong down my throat.\r\n\r\nI\\\'ve never woken from a dream screaming, but if I had been dreaming (I wish I was), I most surely would have employed that Hollywood clichÃ© at that time.\r\n\r\nInstead I grabbed her head and thrust my own tong into her mouth.  She didn\\\'t taste as bad as she smelled.  And for a few minutes our mouths made loud slurpy sounds as we explored each others oral cavities.  But then, desperately searching for an escape route, I held her by the back of her neck and fiercely clenched my teeth on her throat (leaving a sore spot she would complain about for days afterwards).  I continued on my journey downwards.  I found that she sported large fleshy nipples that sat on what were barely discernable as breasts -uuggh \\\"better not stop here, continue onward brave men\\\" shouted the fearless safari scout leader.  They trekked onward, their spirits undaunted, and forever hopeful.  Down the small mountain precipice in search of the treasure they marched, over a plain and into a jungle, where they were hopelessly lost in a thick jungle bush without shelter or sustenance.  \\\"What\\\'s that?  That there!  It is!  It\\\'s a cave!  It\\\'s THE cave!  Hold on brave lads the treasure will soon be ours!\\\"\r\n\r\nI thought I\\\'d try to give her a little oral pleasure, how noble I was, what a gentleman.  I was to persist in this even as my mouth was full of pubic hairs.  Pubic hairs that extraordinarily seemed to exceed 5\\\" in length!  Is that typical for Asian woman?!  I doubt it very much.  So considerate was I, and how did she repay me?\r\n\r\n\\\"Push onward men, it\\\'s starting to give way!\\\"  Sweat poured from their brows as they redoubled their efforts in an attempt to gain entry to the cave containing the sweet purpose of their noble campaign.  When suddenly the cave began hissing and a vent of hot noxious fumes gushed out of the entrance with such force that it knocked those who were in the front onto their backs.  It then spewed forth a large geyser of foul sputum.  So foul and vial it was that it made their eyes sting and noses run as tear gas would, and they had to struggle to maintain consciousness.  It was too much.  The thick globulous matter now dripping from the foliage around them, landing on them and sticking to their bodies like napalm, it was JUST TO MUCH!  Brave they might be, in the face of lions and gruesome deaths, but the rotting stench of Mother Nature, of womanhood, was simply too too much.  They scattered before it could blow again.\r\n\r\nShe groaned in pleasure.  Back again I went, past her stomach, past her meaty nipples, still spitting out stray pubes on the way.\r\n\r\nHere, it was mutually decided our underpants were to come off.  I helped her wriggle free of hers only getting stuck on some strange hillock on her behind butt (pun) once.  I then clumsily freed myself of my sweaty underwear.\r\n\r\nI tried to enter her, but found myself caught in the thickly interwoven mesh of (again, I am amazed to remember) her extremely long pubic hairs.  Ouch, how wiry and scratchy.  And even with her assistance I found that the hairs followed me inward?????????!!!!!  #@*&$^*$#*#!!!\r\n\r\nBut at last, after how long?  I was finally relieving some seriously pent up frustrations.  YEAH!! \r\n\r\n*NOTE TO SELF:  If you ever find yourself in this situation I highly recommend getting up to go to the bathroom where you should then take your head and lay it on the open toilet seat.  Then proceed by slamming the lid down as hard as possible until you loose consciousness.  Don\\\'t think, don\\\'t question, just do it.  You\\\'ll be saving yourself from a memory that may haunt you for a lifetime.\r\n'),(1792,'<PRE>\r\n<p>lately i\'ve taken to \r\ntaking kava kava\r\nfor recreation<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>to relax\r\nand chill out\r\nto get away from it all\r\n(which is the real goal\r\nof many things from pot,\r\nto booze, to S&M)<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>get away from it\r\nwithout really really losing it\r\nit\'s alright\r\nbut kava kava \r\ntastes crummy<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>but so does jack daniels<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>and it seems to be \'safe\'\r\nwithout the drawbacks\r\nliver damage\r\nhangovers<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>polynesians drink more\r\nwhen they have to wake\r\nup early the next day<p>\r\n\r\n<p>it doesn\'t make me fat\r\nor apathetic\r\nor pathetic<p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\'t imagine i will\r\nend up on the street\r\nin my hometown montana\r\nin some pukey green army coat\r\nand greasy hair\r\nwith red face and bulbous nose\r\nhitting up folks for change\r\n\'cousin, got a quarter\r\ni need to get another \r\ncup of kava kava tea\'<p>\r\n\r\n<p>no one wants to be straight all the time\r\nkava is my escape\r\nand if it is harmful\r\ni\'m on the cutting edge\r\nof kava abuse<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s not even against the law<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>oi my life, bike rides and runs\r\nand organic fruits\r\nand getting wild\r\nis a night with the sega\r\nand a cup of kava<p>\r\n\r\n<p>i might start abusing hot cocoa next<p>\r\n</PRE>'),(1793,'<p>i just bought this for austin...<br>\r\n<img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.lefteyelazy.com/images/photos/misc/bushmills.jpg\\\" width=\\\"250\\\" height=\\\"354\\\"></p>\r\n<p>...this is the lunch that treetop and i shared on tuesday. he drank RC Cola, i had ice tea. we both ate veggie burgers.<br>\r\n<img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.lefteyelazy.com/images/photos/misc/lunch.jpg\\\" width=\\\"250\\\" height=\\\"381\\\"></p>\r\n<p>i sent a pic to bmg:<br>\r\n<img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.lefteyelazy.com/images/photos/misc/double.jpg\\\" width=\\\"250\\\" height=\\\"283\\\"><br>\r\n[ix and wb have also been recipients of above photo. wb accused me of doing a \\\'napkin\\\' thing.]</p>\r\n<p> </p>\r\n<p>what is it with the swinney boys that makes my heart pitter-patter so?</p>'),(1794,'<p>The horizon is less cloudy these days.  New things are in motion and I\\\'m excited by things once again.  Not worrying, but looking forward to.  Not regretting, but feeling at ease with myself and my actions.</p>\r\n<p>For the first time in quite a while, I actually believe what people have been telling me for the longest time: things will work out in the end.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m glad.  I might be crabby and depressed a week from now, but for right now, everything is okay with the world.  I just wathced Dub-ya\\\'s speech on the big cathode ray tube in my deathroom and even that, or scalding my tongue on boiling peppermint tea, isn\\\'t enough to shake this feeling.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m feeling a positive longing for something other than just an out to a bad feeling.  I\\\'m feeling refreshed and focused.  I\\\'m feeling slighty uppity and that\\\'s okay.</p>\r\n<p>When you\\\'re hot, you\\\'re hot.</p>\r\n'),(1795,'Some wanker has been posting in my name on this and other sites. A fairly inert and sad person, probably.\r\n\r\nStill, if it gives them pleasure, why not. I guess they are so insecure with their own lives, they can only go up in the world by pretending to be me.\r\n\r\nReal Gavin Cruickshank'),(1796,'<p>\r\nsome times i just sit here, in my one bedroom apt. alone and quiet, listening to the music filtering down from my upstairs neighbor. and i love my solitude. i love that i\\\'m all alone.\r\n<br>other times i lay in bed awakened at 3 am to the sounds of my upstairs neighbors having sex. she\\\'s a moaner. and i just want to run out and find some one to giggle with.\r\n<p>right now i\\\'m sitting smoking my all natural cigarettes and drinking my red wine, and i\\\'m wondering wherei\\\'m going.\r\n<br>i think i know where i came from. i\\\'m pretty sure i know my life up until now. i dream of what ifs, but i know they\\\'re only that. i accept that i only cry when pacbell is screwing me and i can\\\'t get a straight answer. i accept that my life, a sheltered global upbringing has left me both naive and aware.\r\n<br>but what if my parents didn\\\'t raise two american children? would i now be married with a kid or two living the modest and sedate lifestyle of an arab woman?\r\n<br>what if my parents weren\\\'t so open and understanding? would i be some closed minded bigot, snarling at other people and their way of life?\r\n<br>what if i hadn\\\'t been failing french in my senior year of high school? would i even be here?\r\n<p>it doesn\\\'t really matter. i\\\'m me. i\\\'m here. but now that i\\\'m me, what am i gonna do with my self?\r\n<br>i like history. i like knowing all contributing factors, partly cause i don\\\'t want to repeat something that might not be good, and partly cause i can\\\'t form a decision with out knowing everything.\r\n<br>egg hates this, she\\\'s always accusing me of asking too many unimportant useless questions. but i want to know. i want to form the complete image in my head. i like to know what happened before, what is happening now and what is going to happen, so i can react.\r\n<p>here i sat. here i sit. and then?'),(1797,'HI TESTES'),(1798,'At university the other day, a girl walking in front of me had a patch safety-pinned to her backpack expressing anti-McDonalds sentiments... \r\n\r\nShe was also smoking a cigarette.\r\n\r\nI don\\\'t know much about this world, but surely tobacco companies have done a shitload more damage than McDonalds have...\r\n'),(1799,'<p>It is that time, again. You\\\'re here, I\\\'m here, hell, why not have a chat, right? We both picked this bar, so we\\\'ve got to have a little something in common. Maybe it\\\'s a fluke. Maybe it\\\'s the weather. Maybe nobody in this town is really that different, and sooner or later everybody ends up here.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Whatever the reason, here we are. Both sending out feelers, making stellar comments, ducking behind beverages or cohorts, regrouping, heading back into the fray. On a philosophical level, we both know what we want, right? And we\\\'re both willing to set that aside for a few months at least, right? That\\\'s good, then. Makes this easier, you know. Not so much pressure to be clever, or intelligent, or remotely interesting. I mean, after all, we\\\'re both just piles of meat flapping about for other piles of meat to inject/absorb portions of meat into/from, right?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ah, but it\\\'s just as well, your friends dragging you off. I\\\'ve got these numbers, this cryptic medium/message that gives me permission, gives me the RIGHT. Gives me the green light, the a-OK, the go-ahead, the cleared for landing on runway V, and I will call. Yes, I will call!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...in 4 days.</p>\r\n'),(1800,'<p>\r\nI\'m a black \'n white boy with a feather in my cap<br>\r\nSome might say i\'ve got a flair<br>\r\nSome might disagree with that\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m a Hyperbolic Paraboid with tungsten coated nerves<br>\r\nI\'m a dirty talking lyricist i\'ll take you round the curves\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m a doctor of surprise <br>\r\nWhen i poke you in the eyes<br>\r\nYou might not like my style<br>\r\nBut i smell just like french fries<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m a sinner i\'m a saint<br>\r\nI prefer matt to glossy paint<br>\r\nAnd a ladie\'s man i aint<br>\r\nCus i make the ladies faint\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSee my dog, see my bone<br>\r\nSee my Sony Cordless Phone<br>\r\nGotta chinese chick in Rome<br>\r\nWhen i call her she aint home\r\n</p>'),(1801,'I am not posting to this, or any other site so images from my site are being used without my permission.\r\n\r\nReal gavin'),(1802,'<P>Austin\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou have been censoring my comments. I am the real Gavin Cruickshank, and images have been posted on your site by someone pretending to be me. These images are my property, and I have not given concent for their use elsewhere.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI appreciate your cooperation in removing these images.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI am also interested to know who has been impersonating me, but of course it is your perogative not to do so, if thats what you want.\r\n</P>'),(1803,'<P>book #2 entry #4 3/16/00 15:30 Thursday, Starbucks at the University of Buffalo\r\n</P><P>\r\nOne Night in NYC\r\n</P><P>\r\nI had sex with Jo.  I never wanted it.  I never fantasized about it, and in fact, I have always planned on avoiding it.  But unfortunately there were many factors working against me; 1.  I hadn\\\'t had sex in over a year -ouch!  2.  I was under the influence of alcohol -this has always made even the least attractive of woman seem desirable.  3.  We were in NYC on spring break -this contributed to a feeling of: what happens here is not what happens in Buffalo.  4.  We were sleeping in the same bed -from now on I\\\'ll avoid that -female flesh is female flesh and is simply too tempting (this is probably the real reason) and 5.  She wanted it -she just kept sending these really obvious signals that she was there for the taking.  But what it really comes down to is a lack of self control, a trait I?ve exhibited in the past and in many, many, different forms.  I thought that I had learned my lesson, and maybe now I have.  Since even though Jo wants a relationship, I was able to successfully convey that it was not to be.   And as usual, I was able to do so without making her feel too bad, and I wonder if she didn\\\'t agree with me deep down..................</P><P>\r\n  Now if only she\\\'d stop trying to hold my hand!  Oh, if you want details I can tell you, it was disgusting.  It started out innocently enough.  I though it no harm to hold her through the night.  It\\\'s been so long since I\\\'ve held someone, and I think it comforted her as well.  But then we found ourselves sweltering in a violent room temperature that must have far exceeded 130 degrees Fahrenheit.  This caused our sweat glands to kick in, and soon the scent of diluted uric acid wafted forth from in-between out tightly compacted bodies.  And of course this provided the perfect excuse for her to peal of her limp, sweat laden T-shirt.                      \r\n</P><P>  I was repulsed and intrigued at the same time.  My hand brushed against her tiny breasts, she murmured in pleasure and turned around to bestow a noxious kiss upon my own mucous laden lips.  Her breath was like that smell that drifts out of the high school science room during the frog and grasshopper dissections.  As disgusting as it was, and even as I was chocking back the bail in the back of my throat (as I do now in recreating this horrible affair), it brought back fond memories of my grandmother.  It was like kissing my grandmother.  \\\"Oh, my how you\\\'ve grown, come over here and give me a hug and a kiss.\\\"  And then my 86 year old grandmother is ramming her grandmotherly tongue down my throat.</P><P>\r\n  I\\\'ve never woken from a dream screaming, but if I had been dreaming (I wish I was), I most surely would have employed that Hollywood clichÃ© at that time.</P><P>\r\n  Instead I grabbed her head and thrust my own tong into her mouth.  She didn\\\'t taste as bad as she smelled.  And for a few minutes our mouths made loud slurpy sounds as we explored each others oral cavities.  But then, desperately searching for an escape route, I held her by the back of her neck and fiercely clenched my teeth on her throat (leaving a sore spot she would complain about for days afterwards).  I continued on my journey downwards.  I found that she sported large fleshy nipples that sat on what were barely discernable as breasts -uuggh \\\"better not stop here, continue onward brave men\\\" shouted the fearless safari scout leader.  They trekked onward, their spirits undaunted, and forever hopeful.  Down the small mountain precipice in search of the treasure they marched, over a plain and into a jungle, where they were hopelessly lost in a thick jungle bush without shelter or sustenance.  \\\"What\\\'s that?  That there!  It is!  It\\\'s a cave!  It\\\'s THE cave!  Hold on brave lads the treasure will soon be ours!\\\"</P><P>\r\n  I thought I\\\'d try to give her a little oral pleasure, how noble I was, what a gentleman.  I was to persist in this even as my mouth was full of pubic hairs.  Pubic hairs that extraordinarily seemed to exceed 5\\\" in length!  Is that typical for Asian woman?!  I doubt it very much.  So considerate was I, and how did she repay me?</P><P>\r\n  \\\"Push onward men, it\\\'s starting to give way!\\\"  Sweat poured from their brows as they redoubled their efforts in an attempt to gain entry to the cave containing the sweet purpose of their noble campaign.  When suddenly the cave began hissing and a vent of hot noxious fumes gushed out of the entrance with such force that it knocked those who were in the front onto their backs.  It then spewed forth a large geyser of foul sputum.  So foul and vial it was that it made their eyes sting and noses run as tear gas would, and they had to struggle to maintain consciousness.  It was too much.  The thick globulous matter now dripping from the foliage around them, landing on them and sticking to their bodies like napalm, it was JUST TO MUCH!  Brave they might be, in the face of lions and gruesome deaths, but the rotting stench of Mother Nature, of womanhood, was simply too too much.  They scattered before it could blow again.</P><P>\r\n  She groaned in pleasure.  Back again I went, past her stomach, past her meaty nipples, still spitting out stray pubes on the way.</P><P>\r\n  Here, it was mutually decided our underpants were to come off.  I helped her wriggle free of hers only getting stuck on some strange hillock on her behind butt (pun) once.  I then clumsily freed myself of my sweaty underwear.\r\n  I tried to enter her, but found myself caught in the thickly interwoven mesh of (again, I am amazed to remember) her extremely long pubic hairs.  Ouch, how wiry and scratchy.  And even with her assistance I found that the hairs followed me inward?????????!!!!!  #@*&$^*$#*#!!!\r\n  But at last, after how long?  I was finally relieving some seriously pent up frustrations.  YEAH!! </P><P>\r\n\r\n*NOTE TO SELF:  If you ever find yourself in this situation I highly recommend getting up to go to the bathroom where you should then take your head and lay it on the open toilet seat.  Then proceed by slamming the lid down as hard as possible until you loose consciousness.  Don\\\'t think, don\\\'t question, just do it.  You\\\'ll be saving yourself from a memory that may haunt you for a lifetime.\r\n</P>'),(1804,'<p>so yeah this morning i hop down the apartment building stairs all happy and singing, bike helmet in hands like a basket containing keys and lights, and what do i see when i reach street level?</p>\r\n<p>not only is my lovely bike gone, but the stair railing where i locked it is also gone, there is a big hole in the stairs and chunks of rock scattered around, and the gate of our building has been torn open.</p>\r\n<p>guilt.  horrible guilt.  how could i have locked it up in such a stupid place?  bait for vicious thieves.  usually it is safe tucked away in the basement.  last night i had an unbelievably naive sense of security, thinking, oh, well, it won\\\'t <b>bother</b> anyone here for the night.  won\\\'t bother anyone.  ha.  won\\\'t bother the people who scope the streets looking for shiny prizes to take.</p>\r\n<p>i can just imagine the justifications in someone\\\'s head who does something like this.  they think if i can buy one bike, i can buy another.  well, i can certainly buy a junker!  and really, in the city, that\\\'s the most intelligent thing to do.</p>\r\n<p>worst part is, i had my bike stolen in june, too, and that bike i really loved.  something about it.  it just had character.  gary fisher, a little too big, but solid.  used.  someone took that off a parking meter while i was at the doctor\\\'s office.  total disbelief.</p>\r\n<p>in my work, i try to help others.  emergencies, crises, family hardships, these all touch me and move my soul to reach out and give.  so why am i confronted with violent thievery?  i guess this is a spiritual message i have to heed.  these things we buy, they can be stolen, lifted, bought again and sold, broken down and forgotten in a junk pile.  there\\\'s no soul there.  the soul was in my legs, in the freedom i felt zipping around the city, delivering songs and love to friends and strangers alike. gettin around.</p>\r\n<p>so i called the cops, trying to choke back tears (this is not a tragedy, i keep repeating to myself.  losing your <b>family</b> is a tragedy. losing your <b>home</b> is a tragedy.  even your job.  even your relationship. these things are really important.  that bike was just a thing. the cop was very understanding and reassuring, exclaiming over the broken steps, \\\"wow, this musta been a really nice bike.\\\" well, no, buddy.  people are just really, really desperate.</p>\r\n<p>he gave me a ride to work, which really rocked my world.  sometimes, especially in times of vunerability and woundedness, you really gotta love cops.  they\\\'re so down to earth. talking that funny walkie-talkie number talk into the black boxes on their chests.  jiving with the female ghost voices that live back at the ranch.</P>\r\n<p>onward.  to junky bikes and more motion.  to love for all of humanity, even the thieves, even the killers.  they too have their stories.  and i have my life still.</p>\r\n<p>anyone in sf gotta funky bike they wanna sell me real cheap?</p>\r\ne'),(1805,'<P>i am on the hunt for the perfect concert-going, show-attending shoes.</p>\r\n<p>i see between one and four shows a week, usually two, and this involves:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>parking downtown and walking to the venue</li>\r\n<li>standing around during the opening bands, 1-3 hours</li>\r\n<li>standing, sometimes very confined with bodies pressing up against you from every angle, during headlining act, sometimes <b>rocking out</b>, 1-2 hours</li>\r\n<li>walking back to your car, which is actually one of my favorite things about seeing shows. that elated, exhausted march back home.</li></ul>\r\n\r\n<p>all of this inflicts a tragedy upon my feet. i have really high arches; this was great during years of gymnastics when my little feet made my jumps springy and my toes curl. now it means that shoes that are both comfortable for a long wearing <b>and</b> fashionable are a hard combination to come by.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>typically i just buy <a href=\\\"http://www.delias.com\\\">delia\\\'s</a>, and they\\\'ve been <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/cam/boy_kicking/info.txt\\\">doing me right</a>. so far, my favorite shoes of theirs for long wear are the koala boots:</p>\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://image.delias.com/delias/current/prodimg/14266l.jpg\\\"></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have them in navy blue suede. almost every time i wear them, a stranger will say to me, \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.blairmag.com/blair3/clarks/clarks.html\\\">those boots look <i>really comfortable</a></i>.\\\"<BR>\r\nthey are. they\\\'re like wearing slippers out in public.<BR>\r\nbut even the koala boots, after 4 hours of standing, start to hurt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m trying to be practical about this! i said to myself, \\\"okay. professions where people are on their feet 8 hours at a time. nurses, chefs. nurses wear those <a href=\\\"http://www.international-best.com/101.htm\\\">really ugly white shoes</a>. i could get those.\\\"<BR>\r\ni could put gel insoles in them and paint them funky colors! they could be my ROCK SHOES!</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>they\\\'re really ugly.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i could use some advice here, denizens!<BR>\r\ni saw modest mouse and the donnas last night and i\\\'m seeing swearing at motorists tonight and wilco tomorrow night! <i>heeellllp.</i></p>'),(1806,'<p>8/20/01-BREA, CALIFORNIA-I was in California when it happened.   We had intended to fly home to Boston on the day of the attack, but our flights were cancelled and we could not get a flight home, nor were we inclined to fly home after watching the endless loops of the fiery disaster that the networks broadcast.  The week before weâ€™d taken an American Airlines flight from Boston to LAX.  The same flight that had been hijacked.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI broke down and cried when the buildings collapsed, burying my face in my wife Elaine\\\'s breast and sobbing .  Perhaps it was because I felt detached from it all, the enormity of the circumstances dwarfing my emotions, my life experience not preparing me for such a horrible human act.  Whoever flew those planes into the buildings had little regard for human life (despite the newspapers accounts of names and faces, we do not yet know for certain) and the truest horror of it is inconceivable to those of us who were mere witnesses.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMen who perpetrated other wartime horrors, platoon leaders in Vietnam who commanded their men to decimate and burn entire villages,  or the men who flew the planes that dropped Fat Man and Little Boy on Japan have told the stories of having to live with the horror of their indiscriminate mass murder.  But the men who attacked the Trade Center are dead now, free of guilt; their last emotions probably a mix of fear and fevered religious pride;  their leader(s) left to abstract human life and death like all warlords before them.  For the men who pull the strings, the body count is a point system; the team who racks up the most points wins.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWhen the first pictures of the events at the Trade Center came to us, Elaine was still asleep.  Later that day, we were to board a flight to Boston and because I don\\\'t like air travel I was up early with anticipation.  My first instinct was that it was an horrific accident, an amateur pilot\\\'s terrible miscalculation, a single engine Cessna involved in yet another tragedy.  The news coming through was still tentative, assuming an accident rather than intention; and, since Elaine is a even more skittish than I about flying, I thought not to wake her with such jarring news.  But as I watched, the second plane, a commercial jet, barreled into the building and exploded in a ball of orange and black flame.  It was clear that this was no accident.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nI went in to the bedroom and woke her.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"Elaine, Elaine.  Get up.  Something terrible is happening.  They\\\'re flying airplanes into the World Trade Center.  Two already.  I don\\\'t know what\\\'s happening.\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nElaine sat bolt upright in bed, confused and startled.  The sights on TV were terrible to see.  I imagine that it must be terrible to wake up to someone saying those words,  \\\"someone\\\'s flying airplanes into the World Trade Center.\\\"  What kind of images must flash in front of your eyes?\r\n</p><p>\r\nWe both went out and sat dumfounded in front of the TV, shocked and scared.  Terrified.  My parents sat there too, somehow watching the events unfold with a of cool, detached aspect.  I can understand their reaction somewhat.  The footage of broken and burning towers on television looked like a scene from a disaster movie.  I believe it actually takes a leap of the imagination to get to the realization that this was very real, that it was happening, people were dying, in New York, right now.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt sounds odd to say it takes imagination to break the images free from a televised context, but television has depicted fictions and faux-horror for so long.  Weâ€™ve seen the Titanic sink, the Towering Inferno burn, and weâ€™re so used to suspending our disbelief that itâ€™s not even a choice anymore.  Our brain-filter instinctively switches on when we turn on the TV: turn on, tune in, zone out.  To my parents, the crumbling World Trade Center towers, didnâ€™t have a context other than what theyâ€™d seen of them on television, two abstract buildings, tokens really.  My dad, retired now, with many about-the-house projects, actually got up to paint the back porch at some point.  My mom left for work. I couldn\\\'t even conceive of such trivialities, let alone act them out.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI remember that I got up and fried a couple of eggs for breakfast, toasted some bread and poured black coffee into two mugs.  I put the food on the kitchen table and Elaine and I sat down.  It felt terrible to be doing something so banal in the face of such dreadful events.  Eating was simple and mechanical,  we said nothing and our eyes did not leave the TV set.  But eating was also reassuring; as Raymond Carver said, eating was a â€œsmall good thingâ€ at  an intensely emotional time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWe tried to call some of our friends in The City, but we couldnâ€™t get a line through.  Elaine got her parents (who live in NJ) on the line and they talked quietly for a time.  I called my work and explained the obvious, that I wouldnâ€™t be coming to work because I was stranded in California.  I think I chuckled nervously while i explained my situation a couple of times, rephrasing it, feeling the words like wafers in my mouth.  Iâ€™m not sure why I chuckled. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAt some point during all of this, the planes came down on the Pentagon, and another in Pennsylvania; it seemed that planes were raining maliciously from the sky.  They said that there will still at least 50 planes in the air over The United States, unaccounted for.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWe could only take so much of it, so I told Elaine that Iâ€™d like to take a walk, to get away from the TV for a few minutes and just walk aimlessly through the streets.  We put on out clothes and stepped out into the unrelenting Brea, California sun.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy parents neighborhood is nestled in a sleepy block of similar houses with perfectly manicured uber-lawns that rival the golf course putting greens not two blocks away.  In the early September morning the thick, haze of the marine layer rolls in and brackets everything from the beach to the foothills with a thick layer of cool gray.  By late midmorning, the marine layer burns off, and the sun rolls angrily in a bleached blue sky, light glares off of white sidewalks and radiates from tar black streets.  There are sparse trees in my parents neighborhood and the dearth of shade does not lend itself to casual walking.  This is why everybody drives in Southern California, to travel in air-conditioned comfort is one\\\'s turtle shell; walking involves you too much in your surroundings and god-knows you donâ€™t want to be involved.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBy the time we stepped outside, the sun was high in the sky and the clouds had been banished until the next morning.  It was probably 85 degrees out, the sky was cloudless and bright and very little stirred up and down the block.  We just started walking, not really caring what direction we were walking in, but wanting to get the sense of motion going, to get away from the TV telling us what to think and try to think some of our own thoughts, let our minds begin to sort out all the raw information that weâ€™d been absorbing.  An older man with gray hair walked by with his short, scruffy-looking dog and he gave as a hearty â€œHello!â€ Neither one of us could even summon the energy to grunt. We just shuffled past, leaving with a rather puzzled and hurt expression on his face, his â€œHello!â€ sticking out between us like a stubbed toe.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œHe probably doesnâ€™t even understand what happened.â€  I said to Elaine.  I repressed the urge to turn and yell at him.  I didnâ€™t know what I wanted to say, something curt and incisive, designed to both hurt and instruct.  But I didnâ€™t yell anything.   We walked on.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nA few trucks and SUVs drove by (Southern Californians favor huge, gas-guzzling pick-ups and impractical SUVs).  We saw a few joggers.  It was hard to believe that someone could practice such a meaninglessness health-ritual when it felt as if the world was falling apart.  Why werenâ€™t they glued to their TV sets or walking around in a daze?  Why werenâ€™t we all walking around in a daze, falling to our knees and pounding those perfectly manicured lawns and railing at the passively blue sky?  \r\n</p><p>\r\nDamn you, damn you to hell for what youâ€™ve done!\r\n</p><p>\r\nBut who was there to yell at?\r\n</p><p>\r\nWhen we got back to the house my dad was deeply committed to his porch painting, listening to an obnoxious radio personality talk about bombing Arabs: â€œrag-headsâ€ he called them.  We turned on the TV nothing had changed, though every ten minutes or so they seemed to have new footage of the plane slamming into the trade center.  The interminably replayed the tape of the buildingsâ€™ collapse.\r\n</p><p>\r\nDamn WHO to WHERE for WHAT?\r\n</p><p>\r\nElaine and I watched the TV and felt far away from the East Coast.  We felt detached and somewhat broken, cut adrift.  For three days we could not stray far from the news.  Each morning we got up and stood in front of Tower Records for one of only two copies of the New York Times or the Wall Street Journal.  I read the stories and marveled at the talent of journalists who could process all this information and write it out so fast.  I knew then that sitting down to write would leave me sobbing piteously at my lack of ability to say how I felt about the thing.  I was living history and with all my pretentious attitudes toward writing, I couldnâ€™t do a thing with words.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWe ended up taking the train back from California.  I bought a notebook and a pen, but couldn\\\'t do any writing on the train.  We met people from New York, New Jersey, Boston, people trying to make it home in the face of cancelled flights and fear of air travel.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nNow I donâ€™t now how to conclude this.  There is no artful summation, no appropriate turn of the phrase.  No gimmick.  We are at war with an enemy we do not know, we may strike blindly, the battleships are moving into place and our military is poised.   Afghanistan, Iraq, Osama Bin Laden, Sadam Hussien, the Taliban, Islam are words on everybodyâ€™s lips.  We are moving into history.  We do not know what will happen and I\\\'m frightened.\r\n</p>'),(1807,'<p>On the subway, I listen to Mos Def and read Aristotle.</p>\r\n<p>I am absorbing greatness.</p>\r\n<p>In the search for a muse, I am trying to inundate myself with high-art from a variety of sources and disciplines.  So far I have been rather unsuccesful.</p>\r\n<p>I must say that I\\\'m really feelin\\\' this Aristotle, though.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m translating my emotions via the DJ Shadow sideproject, U.N.K.L.E.; currently seeping out of my stereo.  I am retardedly tired right now and wish for nothing more than to chill while waiting for my laundry to be done.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m feeling very Massive Attack fabric softener right now.</p>\r\n'),(1808,'<P>\r\nIn the last few days, The word \\\"evil\\\" has been used a lot.  Here is the Webster\\\'s definition for the word evil.  Its funny, maybe I am just a strange person.  I have oftened wondered about \\\"evil\\\".  What is \\\"evil\\\"?  \r\n</P><P>\r\n(beginning of definition)\r\n</P><P>\r\neÂ·vil (vl)\r\nadj. eÂ·vilÂ·er, eÂ·vilÂ·est \r\n</P><P>\r\nMorally bad or wrong; wicked: an evil tyrant. \r\n</P><P>\r\nCausing ruin, injury, or pain; harmful: the evil effects of a poor diet. \r\n</P><P>\r\nCharacterized by or indicating future misfortune; ominous: evil omens. \r\n</P><P>\r\nBad or blameworthy by report; infamous: an evil reputation. \r\n</P><P>\r\nCharacterized by anger or spite; malicious: an evil temper. \r\n</P><P>\r\nn. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe quality of being morally bad or wrong; wickedness. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThat which causes harm, misfortune, or destruction: a leader\\\'s power to do both good and evil. \r\n</P><P>\r\nAn evil force, power, or personification. \r\nSomething that is a cause or source of suffering, injury, or destruction: the social evils of poverty and injustice\r\n\r\n(end of definition)\r\n </P><P>\r\nStrange how the definition doesn\\\'t seem nearly as \\\"evil\\\" as the word itself.  It is almost as if the word has outgrown its origins.  As a noun, \\\"wickedness\\\" is introduced into the definition.  But wicked has a certain playfulness that we do not attach to sheer \\\"evil\\\".  It has taken on a life of its own overcoming its meager beginnings of \\\"bad or blameworthy\\\" and taking on the attributes of everything we percieve as dark and sinister.  A demon on the loose in a world otherwise populated by daisies.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBeing raised in a very biblical family in a very biblical region of our country, the first visions I had of evil all pertained to Satan and his minions.  After all, \\\"evil\\\" is the last four letters in \\\"devil\\\".  Plus, the visions of a character cloaked in red, eyes glowing, and a pitchfork at hand all seem pretty menacing when one is young.  However, as I aged and my views towards religion, the church, and the bible evolved, I started to reassess my views of the word \\\"evil\\\".  There are many crimes commited that gain the label \\\"evil\\\".   Usually these are serial killings or crimes against the innocent.  Strong evidence is building that these types of violent crimes are very much tied to genetic traits and/or chemical imbalances.  So, can \\\"evil\\\" simply be a chemical imbalance that could at some point in time be fixed with a pill.  Then there are the gross acts that one finds in history (Hitler and the Holocaust or Pol Pot and the Khymer Rouge in Cambodia).  Most of the \\\"evil\\\" crimes in the Holocaust were committed by very normal people.  Most of the Khymer Rouge\\\'s atrocities were commited by the very young.  Does this mean that \\\"evil\\\" resides within even the most innocent and can be called out by certain times or icons.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe latest atrocity in New York is very hard to fathom.  This is especially so for Westerners.  We have always tended to think of a certain level of fairness when it comes to war.  We have not always necessarily practiced it but at some level we like to think that in war we are honorable and give the enemy a fighting chance.  Once again, this is more theory than practice.  We (myself included) do not have a good understanding of the ideas and thoughts of other cultures and how they might differ from ours in terms of war.  What we do know is that for some reason, some probably otherwise normal men made the decision to forgo their lives, their families, and everything they know and strike a blow to the pride of the United States.  While their actions are certainly misplaced and hard to understand,  do they represent \\\"evil\\\"?  Does \\\"evil\\\" truly exist?  Maybe if we limit the word to it \\\"Webster\\\'s definition\\\" it does but once we let it of its box and allow it to play and grow into the demons that exist within the corners of our conscious, then maybe just maybe it loses its place and becomes just another overused word with very little meaning.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(1809,''),(1810,'grounded, spin slowed<br>\r\nand the torque i thought i had \r\nis gone<br>\r\nleft only with a solitary and impotent anger in my bones<br>\r\n<br>\r\noh, what\\\'s goin on with the intercom<br>\r\ni don\\\'t think that poor lady knows it\\\'s on<br>\r\nher flustered protests on the phone<br>\r\nand feedback in stereo<br>\r\nfeedback in stereo<br>\r\nfeedback<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsobered, brought down<br>\r\ni sure as hell don\\\'t run this ship<br>\r\nthe strength presumed to keep me safe<br>\r\nis flimsy as a hooker\\\'s slip<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'m hearing pain in waves pour in<br>\r\ni\\\'m hearing new delights<br>\r\ni\\\'m hearing bashing crashing boots<br>\r\ni\\\'m hearing sleepless nights<br>\r\nand feedback in stereo<br>\r\nfeedback in stereo<br>\r\nfeedback<br>\r\n'),(1811,'<P>When I conceived of writing this letter Tricky?s ?She makes Me Wanna Die? played in my mind.\r\n<P>In a hall. In a church. In my funeral.  Nineteen Ninety seven was a good year. \r\n\r\n<P>Much to my god damn antipathy you are in Houston now I suppose. \r\n\r\n<P>I?m here, through living inside a bootleg of your love.\r\n\r\n<P>I had a dream, in which I die and an angel says:\r\n\r\n<P>You were meant to be longing. \r\n<P>You were meant to experience that which killed you.\r\n<P>You were my pet. \r\n\r\n<P>It momentarily made me stop. \r\n\r\n<P>Are you the enemy of my creativity? \r\n\r\n<P>Can I push myself forwards? \r\n\r\n<P>I?ll never be free if I?m not free now.\r\n'),(1812,'Its been a long week. Not technically tough or <i>bad</i>, per se, but nevertheless long.  It creeped by like the earth\\\'s crust, obeying the laws of plate techtonics.<br><br>\r\nIts been a long week because I\\\'ve stressed every every minute of it; although i have nothing to stress about. About. He makes fun of me because I say <i>\\\"aboot\\\"</i>, and talk like the Canadian I am.  If I\\\'m gone, I say I was \\\"oot\\\". Its normal, here.  Undoubtedly it isn\\\'t for him, though- but I digress.<br><Br>\r\nI have nothing to stress about- no work, I dropped out of school, I moved from my own town.  Idle hands are the devil\\\'s workshop, they say, and boy, has Satan been giving me a manicure.<br><br>\r\n<b>A Bold List of Things I\\\'d like:</b><br>\r\n1. I\\\'d like for him to fall in love me for who I am, not because of my voice, hair, body, or sex. I doubt I have the brains anyway.<br>\r\n2. I\\\'d like to not wake up, day after day, and spend half the morning staring at the unused fork on the table.<br><br>\r\n3. I\\\'d like to not spend half the afternoon alternating between dancing, crying, sleeping and chain-smoking<br><Br>\r\n4. I\\\'d like to have a more regular and healthy eating schedule, like not forgetting to eat for days as I currently do. <br><br>\r\nNo one\\\'s ever taken time to get to know me.  No, not here, but like in real life.<br><br>I forget that like\\\'s processes have a logical process for a purpose, like, don\\\'t skip steps, obstinately.<br><br>\r\nBut, I bitch and I whine- thats really all this is, deep down I\\\'m <i>happy</i>.  This constant stress I feel is pointless.  Its the guilt-trip, the cosmic punishment I dole out on myself for my life- for flaking out, being a daddy\\\'s girl, failing school, mooching, being too pretty, moving out of, back to, out of, back to Toronto, leaving NY, leaving Detroit, leaving James, disowning my Mom... its all a pile of <i>shit</i> that I\\\'ve created for no reason.<br><br>\r\nI really need to get over myself, get on with my life, and start living again!<br>'),(1813,'he looked like an angel.<BR>a fat, hawaiian-shirted, 40ish angel.<BR>with a mustache and a big backpack.<BR>nobody to be denied.<BR><BR>picked him up at the mango street on-ramp.<BR>said he had some money stashed in pensacola. headed toward it.<BR>earned it bouncing at a bar on maui.<BR>saved $3k in 6 months.<BR>slept on a bald hill in an abandoned pineapple plantation.<BR>snuck into a water park at night to wash.<BR>said samoans and tongans were the worst drunks.<BR><BR>\\\"you\\\'ve got a lot of anger,\\\" he told me.<BR>\\\"paint your bedroom sky blue.\\\"<BR>\\\"or just quit playing poker drunk<BR>at the seminole casino,\\\" i thought.<BR><BR>the \\\"low fuel\\\" light came on a mile before my exit.<BR>he\\\'s been there before: couldn\\\'t get a ride one night.<BR>slept up behind the wafflehouse.<BR>\\\"at least you know the area,\\\" i say.<BR>exit 61 revisited.<BR><BR>inside the gas station,<BR>he borrows a scrap of paper<BR>from the attendant\\\'s pump log,<BR>gives me his e-mail: robertsway@hotmail.com.'),(1814,'<P> Today\\\'s Sunday!! Which makes tomorrow monday and yesterday saturday.. currently it is drizzling.. and there\\\'s some song playing on the radio about not being alone.  I am alone.  It\\\'s at times like these I pity my pitiful existence with mournful pity.  I\\\'ve just killed a spider, with a cd, it looked pretty malnourished, not surprising since I clean my room so thoroughly there isn\\\'t a crumb left to eat off these sickeningly shining wooden floors.  I\\\'m a murderer.</P>\r\n<P> I start uni for real tomorrow, my brain\\\'s been dying to be switched on all summer.. three whole months of absolute nightmarish boredom. I admit i cracked within 3 weeks and started o learn Italian, although I didn\\\'t go very far as the pack belongs to my sister and she takes it everywhere with her.. so far I know \\\'hello\\\', \\\'goodbye\\\', \\\'what\\\'s ur name\\\', \\\'my name is..\\\' and \\\'take the bus\\\' which I think would be a good guy deterrent. Talking of which I was whistled at by a group in uni hence i am peecheezed off, feministic instincts tell me to go pound em.. my brain mathematically calculates that since the group consists around 15 of them = impossible.. maybe next time.'),(1815,'<P> Today\\\'s Sunday!! Which makes tomorrow monday and yesterday saturday.. currently it is drizzling.. and there\\\'s some song playing on the radio about not being alone.  I am alone.  It\\\'s at times like these I pity my pitiful existence with mournful pity.  I\\\'ve just killed a spider, with a cd, it looked pretty malnourished, not surprising since I clean my room so thoroughly there isn\\\'t a crumb left to eat off these sickeningly shining wooden floors.  I\\\'m a murderer.</P>\r\n<P> I start uni for real tomorrow, my brain\\\'s been dying to be switched on all summer.. three whole months of absolute nightmarish boredom. I admit i cracked within 3 weeks and started o learn Italian, although I didn\\\'t go very far as the pack belongs to my sister and she takes it everywhere with her.. so far I know \\\'hello\\\', \\\'goodbye\\\', \\\'what\\\'s ur name\\\', \\\'my name is..\\\' and \\\'take the bus\\\' which I think would be a good guy deterrent. Talking of which I was whistled at by a group in uni hence i am peecheezed off, feministic instincts tell me to go pound em.. my brain mathematically calculates that since the group consists around 15 of them = impossible.. maybe next time.</P>'),(1816,'Where are my damn words?!'),(1817,'<P>damn chicken pie</P>'),(1818,'liar liar pants on fire'),(1819,'<P>\r\nrightio\r\n</P>'),(1820,'<p><pre>\r\nDate: Sun, 23 Sep 2001 18:32:58 -0700\r\nFrom: mike clark <*cynnxxx@magrathea.ca>\r\nTo: Justin Filip <*jfilipxxx@idirect.ca>\r\nSubject: Re: Speedy J - Pannik\r\n\r\n\r\ndid you just call me the internet movie database?  *checks site*  haha,\r\ninteresting ... i hadn\\\'t seen that before now.\r\n\r\ncosmic forces seem to be at work.\r\n\r\n----- Original Message -----\r\nFrom: \\\"Justin Filip\\\" <*jfilipxxx@idirect.ca>\r\nTo: \\\"mike clark\\\" <*cynnxxx@magrathea.ca>\r\nSent: Sunday, September 23, 2001 3:03 PM\r\nSubject: Re: Speedy J - Pannik\r\n\r\n\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> > written and directed by david lynch, stars dennis hopper (and features the\r\n> > guy w/ the handheld computer from quantum leap).  it\\\'s sort of a \\\"perfect\r\n> > little town has some very bad things happening just under the surface\\\"\r\n> > story.\r\n>\r\n> Uhhh, thanks IMDB.  ;)\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n> \\\"The reasonable man adapts himself to the world; the\r\n>  unreasonable one persists in trying to adapt the world\r\n>  to himself.  Therefore all progress depends on the\r\n>  unreasonable man.\\\"\r\n>                        -- George Bernard Shaw\r\n> _____________________________________ |\r\n> [ justin.filip :: jfilip@idirect.ca ] |\r\n> [ http://selectah.yi.org/fuzzy/     ] |\r\n>\r\n>\r\n>\r\n</pre></p>'),(1821,'<p>our Cocks have not been beaten at all this year, and the tension is building day by day; we are longing for that sweet release, that sweet smell of victory. our cocks will not be denied their highest pleasure.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfirst, our Cocks destroyed the broncos, but not without one hell of a fight. next, they tore an enormous hole in one set of bulldogs\\\' asses, flags flying high, true. most recently, our Cocks raped <i>another</i> set of bulldogs, that\\\'s 2 for 2 on bulldogs as it stands now. this saturday, I fully expect our Cocks to piss all over the pussies in tuscaloosa, and swamp the field with a crimson tide. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s gonna be messy, it\\\'s gonna be brutal--it\\\'s gonna be beautiful.\r\n</p>'),(1822,'<p>\r\n\r\n<img align=center  src=\\\"http://durak.org/sean/pix/2001-09-24/s.jpg\\\" width=618 height=150 alt=\\\"austin & entourage visit seans house in upper haight\\\"><br>\r\n\r\nhe demonstrates karate for them, takes them motorcycle shopping. he manicures them, tests surgical staple guns on them, gets them hopelessly lost in the simplest of tourist attractions. \r\n\r\n<br>what is the difference between a posse, a harem, an entourage?\r\n\r\n</p>'),(1823,'<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://durak.org/sean/pix/2001-09-24/\"><img align=center  src=\"http://durak.org/sean/pix/2001-09-24/s.jpg\" width=618 height=150 border=1 alt=\"austin & entourage visit seans house in upper haight\"></a><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nhe demonstrates karate for them, takes them motorcycle shopping. he manicures them, tests surgical staple guns on them, gets them hopelessly lost in the simplest of tourist attractions. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<br>what is the difference between a posse, a harem, an entourage?\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n</p>'),(1824,'<br><i>This is short and fragmented, but I wanted to share:</i><br><br>  \r\nI had an amazing dream last night. I dreamed that I had a single super-power that rendered my life completely different- it made any potential actions and schemes absolutely untouchable. I dreamed that I had the power to make <i>anyone</i> <b>vouch</b> for me for <i>anything</i>, any time.<br><br>\r\nJust imagine: being able, through thought, to motivate anyone to validate anything you\\\'ve done, to give unquestionable alibis to anything you could dream up, to stand behind anything you could ever set into action, whether they wanted to or not.<br><br>\r\nA strange dream, but endless possibilities...\r\n'),(1825,'<P>\r\nso everyone\\\'s still on the \\\"give blood\\\" and \\\"donate tons of money to the red cross\\\" trend, and though i have no problem with that, i *do* have a large problem with the fact that i\\\'ve watched the stock market sink ever since it opened back up last monday.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nwhat\\\'s the deal, stockies?  you care for your country, but you\\\'re still selfish poopheads when it comes to your portfolio?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ni don\\\'t know what other people believe is important, now, when we\\\'re getting over the aftermath, but i consider the united states\\\' economy to be very important.  so i think we should all chip in a few bucks and go show our support for the usa.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nand if you want to be selfish about it, you can just see your donation as a little bit of stock insurance.'),(1826,'<P>\r\nso everyone\\\'s still on the \\\"give blood\\\" and \\\"donate tons of money to the red cross\\\" trend, and though i have no problem with that, i *do* have a large problem with the fact that i\\\'ve watched the stock market sink ever since it opened back up last monday.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nwhat\\\'s the deal, stockies?  you care for your country, but you\\\'re still selfish poopheads when it comes to your portfolio?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ni don\\\'t know what other people believe is important, now, when we\\\'re getting over the aftermath, but i consider the united states\\\' economy to be very important.  so i think we should all chip in a few bucks and go show our support for the usa.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nand if you want to be selfish about it, you can just see your donation as a little bit of stock insurance.\r\n</P>'),(1827,'<p>Crossing Piedmont, Arwa wailed that it was raining. it is autumn, I reminded us. We missed the equinox. when? sometime this weekend. </p>\r\n<p>We have been asleep.</p>\r\n<p>Better to pay attention to the weather, to be present in your body, than to float through the day in a dream. I used to measure time by cigarrettes - \\\"I will leave after one cigarrette\\\" - in support of a Concrete Measurement of Time. I don\\\'t wear a watch. As far as Im concerned, today is the first day of autumn because today is the first day we smelled rain, and wore jackets to the store.</p>\r\n<p>We were walking to the store because I woke up and the first thing I saw was a bowl of chopped, uncooked mushrooms and fresh basil left over from when I cooked curry saturday night. \\\"A Taste of Thai\\\" coconut milk, which was all the store had available, BITES. It was so watery that I had to throw some of it out, so there wasnt enough sauce to fit all the veggies I had chopped.</p>\r\n<p>As in a midsummer night\\\'s dream, someone sprinkled my eyes with love and this day is marked by leftover mushrooms. I walked to the store and purchased english muffins (Ashley\\\'s favorite) and spinach. We ate sauteed spinach, garlic, and mushroom, poured it onto toasted muffins, sprinkled with chunks of Vermont Extra Sharp Cheddar, topped by poached eggs, and accompanied by a chicken apple sausage. Goat arranged the round muffins and sausage on her plate like balls and cock. We named the dish \\\"mama\\\'s little babies\\\" after the sleater-kinney song.</p>\r\n<p>Jay moved here because he wanted to have a home and a kitchen, because he wanted to grow a garden. I spotted a sprig of rosemary on the table and looked out the window at his rosemary and chive plants on the fire escape. I am grateful.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"You are a cooking building lately\\\". There is always food in this house, because we always cook too much, because you never know who might want to stop by for dinner. And you never want anyone to go hungry. Each ingredient left behind dictates the life of the next meal.</p>\r\n<p>Now there is half a bag of cleaned spinach on the table, and still more basil. Tomorrow I will make something with spinach. </p>\r\n<p>I am in love. I am in love with homemade apple pie, and baked tofu treats, and leftover red curry, and sticky rice,  and the hand that feeds me, and the mouth that bites me.</p>\r\n<p>Today is exquisite.</p>'),(1828,'Hi, I\\\'m Camilla Henrikke. Until August my name was Camilla, but I like changes that make me feel better. For some obscure reason my great grandmothers middle name makes me feel like that, Better.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI have a boyfriend who lives 6-7 hours away from me, all depending on the car or train. Travelling for that long, on my own, bores the living hell outta me. But now he\\\'s the one coming here. One more night alone and I\\\'ll be safe. I don\\\'t know what he\\\'s gonna do from 5 to 9 pm, when I\\\'m at school, but it\\\'ll be okay. It\\\'d be fun to have him sitting next to me in the classroom, but I just started at this school and I\\\'ve been the most noisy and annoying (and also the youngest) one there ever since I got there, so I don\\\'t really think it\\\'s such a good idea after all.\r\n<br><br>\r\nPssh, I need to go to bed before 5 AM tonight. I have turned the days upside down. This is what afternoon classes do to a person.\r\n<br><br>\r\nWhy am I even typing this? I should go to bed.'),(1829,'<P>i remember as a child watching my parents rake the yard.  my eyes, with happiness growing within, intently followed until each leaf had been carefully added to the ever growing pile.  the effort with which they sought out each stray blowing in the breeze only served to increased my glee. once the leaves were piled and before they were stuffed in the barrel to meet their fiery end, my parents would rest their weary chins upon the handle the rakes.  soft smiles slid across their faces.  they knew their work was not quite done.  slowly rising, as if something had caught my eye, sneakingly i would stare off into the distance; fast, turn, bolt, run, jump, fly, me, leaves, parent\\\'s laughter, sheer joy.</P><P>\r\n\r\nin looking back to youth, there is a comfort in the joy of little things and our parent\\\'s willingness to sacrifice to supply them.  </P>'),(1830,'<p>September 24th. Autumnal equinox. Day Zero: The Satritain New Year.</p>\r\n<p>I celebrated alone. I bought some smoked fish and boiled some potatos. I went for a hike in the hunting preserve and walked in the rain.</p>\r\n<p>James Pict writes in his <i>Travels</i>:</p>\r\n<p>\\\"The Satritain people have no word for \\\'trust\\\' in their language. The closest translation is <i>wa</i>ch<i>a\\\'ch</i> -- \\\'mutually understood predictability\\\' or \\\'harmony of personal initiatives\\\'. Similarly, they have no word for \\\'should\\\' or \\\'ought\\\' -- only \\\'it will be\\\'. One questions how such a rigorously unified people can have such a loose standard of loyalty and cooperation...\\\"</p>\r\n<p>Happy New Year.</p>'),(1831,'<p>\r\nI didn\\\'t get many Z\\\'s tonight so I\\\'m dead tired. But I have to be up now, so I suppose I\\\'ll survive. Can\\\'t say I\\\'m looking forward to school. School now means staring at a computer for 4 hours straight almost without breaks. I complain too much. I know. But I\\\'m sleepy. My Mickey and Minnie Mouse pillow is disturbing. Maybe that\\\'s what kept me awake. The scary smiles on their faces. I dunno I dunno I dunno.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m..what do you call it? Insecure? And freezing cold. Home alone as well, but that won\\\'t last long. I need food, money for the bus, and I also need to change.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyaWWWWWn.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIs it anywhere near normal to be tired this time of the day? It\\\'s soon 1 PM and I\\\'m about to pass out.\r\n</p>'),(1832,'<P>sweeping and raking the back yard was one of my chores growing up.  i always complained about it, but when it came right down to it, this was my favorite chore.  it sounds cheesy but i have always considered sweeping to be a meditative thing.  it\\\'s a simple, repetitive task; your work is cut out for you, and all that\\\'s left to do is to think.  thinking is definitely one of my favorite things to do.</p>\r\n<p>the only problem with -thinking- being your favorite pastime is that you don\\\'t get much done.  well, when i was sweeping, i was being productive, <i>and</i> i was able to let my mind wander, as i put order to chaos with the almighty broom.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nochre and burnt sienna<br>\r\ntabs<br>\r\nlike paper chads<br>\r\nlike<br>\r\nwhen you walk on chaotic leaves<br>\r\nand it feels like<br>\r\nair<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"Navy\\\">i emailed a friend yesterday because i miss her.  it\\\'s been two years since we were friends.  two years since we couldn\\\'t resist the temptation and took a bite from a big red apple.  she called me an hour later.  she is forgiving.  for two weeks the world has been a different place, and i have realized that life is even more precious than i thought and that good people that live in a state of wakefulness are hard to find.  this is why i emailed her.</font></p>\r\n<P>i will email you.</p> \r\n\r\n'),(1833,'<p>Only children and fools fall in love.  Everyone else is too afraid.  Emotional damage...... baggage.  I remember my first/last serious girlfriend.  I entered the relationship in a state of wide-eyed wonderment.  Beauty, Truth, Freedom, and above all... Love.  I was able to cherish someone without any feelings of insecurity or jealousy, just pure joie de vivre.</p><P>Now, I\\\'m trying to be like a child again.  I\\\'m feeling the stirrings of emotions that I thought had died within me, but with them are coming fears and insecurities... baggage.  She has some pretty enormous bags herself which add to my burden.  Part of me wants to run from her into a life of shallow one nights stands and empty kisses, avoid any possibility of reliving pain already experienced.  Of course, I\\\'d rather stay and curl up in her warmth.</p><p>I still believe the only chance two people can have at true love is if they are relationship \\\'virgins.\\\'  Two who don\\\'t have any preconceived notions or expectations to cloud the beauty of the present.</p><P>I hope one day I\\\'ll be proven wrong.</P>'),(1834,'Ok. So, I broke up with my lover of four years.  This is old news. I\\\'ve dealt with it in a classic manner; you know, the old give-and-take games, the old <i>\\\"what if?\\\"</i> scanearios.  The trusty favorites range from <i>It would just be so much easier if we were still togethter.  Sure we\\\'re not right for each other, but we still love each other.</i> to <i>I\\\'m glad- this was just a matter of time- now its finally over!  Life begins again! I don\\\'t regret the winderful time I did have wit her! Refreshing! Sad, but had to happen!</i>...\r\n<br><br>\r\nSomething still stings me, day by day, though.  Its not the potential profound loss that can crest a breakup\\\'s wake.  No, its not even the emotional vancancy that settles like a fog- these things do pass, and are certainly passing for me.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe major kicker for me are every day\\\'s <i>first few waking moments</i>. 6:00am, the alarm buzzes. I dart up, aware that she\\\'s not there; there\\\'s a hurt flaring.  6:01am, and how can I face this day and get out of this bed without my whole being collapsing into that gaping cavern that she, until recently, overfilled?  6:02am, head rested in hands, groggily... <i>this was a bad decision</i>.<br><br>\r\n6:05am comes and I\\\'m in the shower, soaping up and feeling good. I made the right decision. I look in the mirrir and smile; <i>Way to go Peter, you\\\'re finally reclaiming who you were before she claimed you.  You\\\'re getting back to those personality traits and quirks that attracted her to begin with... the ones she swallowed up when you jumped head-long into her</i>.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe breakup was easy. We just drifted apart over the course of this past year.  The aftermath was sour for a week, but has now passed. I\\\'m square with this; resigned that its a good thing, and I\\\'m all smiles.\r\n<br><br>\r\nBut the <i>real</i> bitch are those first waking moments. '),(1835,'<p>Hacking on code.  I\\\'m beginning to intensely dislike this bit I\\\'m working on, even though I\\\'m the one that came up with it.  I can see all the steps in my mind, logically progressing towards completion and perfect functionality.  But my fingers are rebelling and I don\\\'t want to actually <em>type</em> any of that crap.</p>\r\n<p>So <a href=\\\"http://www.winternet.com/~mikelr/flame39.html\\\">this picture</a> sums up perfectly to me how I feel at the moment.  Ungh!  Grrr!</p>\r\n<p>The prognosis is not bad, after some table tennis therapy. Jump!  Thwack!  Leap! ok, I feel better now.</p>\r\n'),(1836,''),(1838,'<p>last night, even though i was sniffling and weakened and ill, i went to the beach. i really just needed to go. i am frustrated at being sick. i am becoming one of those people who tries to go to work when they have a 104 fever.</p>\r\n<p>there was a a thunderstorm, and we sat around a fire on the beach watching lightning and roasting marshmallows. it was only sprinkling, and i turned away the flasks of whiskey and bottles of tequila offered to me. </p>\r\n<p>i was exhausted by the time the beach adventure was over, and i was home, around 10pm. i was so tired that i was nodding out, my eyes closing involuntarily and my head falling around on my shoulders. \r\n</p><p>\r\nas i was making somewhat joyous preparation for the long sleep i was about to have (my nose was still relativley clear then - i was going to have a healing nap, i was sure), i heard four sharp loud explosions outside. i immediately thought it was gunshots, and crouched down on the floor. but when i looked toward the window i saw that the transformer atop the powerline pole outside was arcing enormously, and throwing sparks, which looked, from where i was, like a fireball. finally, after a rain of golden orange sparks fell onto the sidewalk below, the transformer stopped exploding and  just sat there on fire, burning quietly against the dark sky.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni was kind of upset because the power line is like 20 feet from my window, and i started imagining scenarios wherein it would burst through the window in flames, etc. the police showed up because someone else had called in gunshots, and then the fire dept showed up because i had called in the fire. both of them left pretty quickly, saying they had called pacific gas and electric, who were supposed to come and take care of it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nafter they left, peter and i settled into bed to try and sleep, and the stupid thing started arcing again! SO LOUD! no sleep for sick people like me, not with a huge clapping arc happening just outside the window at random intervals. i was shaken up and grumpy, so we got our shit together and walked to peter\\\'s place.\r\n</p><p>\r\nat about 10 this morning, at peter\\\'s, after laying there half sleeping and restless, i just started crying. i was just.. so.. tired. and i hadn\\\'t been able to sleep. it was a bad trip that wouldn\\\'t end. i just wanted to be allowed to sleep. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni really hate being sick. i am really bad at it. is this what it feels like all the time when you are old? i always wonder that when i\\\'m really sick.  \r\n</p><p>\r\npeter fed me nyquil and i finally passed out. i am trying to think of something really nice to do for him because he stayed here and was late for work to take care of me this morning. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwoke up 8 hours later, still tripping on nyquil, and antsy. i have things to do. that is all i can think. this is a setback. i have errands, chores, tasks. you should see my task list dammit. my body is not letting me get things done.\r\n</p>'),(1837,'<p>\r\nwhere: safeway parking lot on pleasant valley rd. <br>\r\nwhen: noonish<br>\r\nwhy: egg was inside getting OJ<br>\r\nwho: me, and passersby<p>\r\n\r\nthe safeway parking lot is a mess. it\\\'s always crowded, cruising at 4mph up and down the isles hoping to see reverse lights. after one go at it, egg does a shoulder roll out the car, and lets me worry about finding a space. the gods are with me today for a change and i find a space, pull in, hide egg\\\'s unemployment check and get out.<br>\r\ni wait for egg on the bench outside. sitting there, with my big sunglasses feeling the warm rays fall around me, a cigarrette in my hand, i am content. <p>\r\n<i> you\\\'re on the job, aren\\\'t you? right now on the job.<br>\r\nhuh?<br>\r\nman, that\\\'s a great job, just sitting here.<br>\r\nwhaaa?<p></i>\r\ni just look at this man, i\\\'m a bit perplexed. on the job? no. he can\\\'t mean that. he gives me a smile and starts walking away to his car, turning back a few times to look at me. i ignore him. another man walks by gives me a look, and mumbles a <i>hey, how you doing?</i> i just smile and nod. quick look down, are my boobies showing? what is going on here?<br>\r\nthe first man drives by in his wrangler:<br>\r\n<i> when you change your mind about that job, you let me know, alright?</i><br>\r\ni just continue sitting there smoking my cigarette, ignoring anyone else. what the hell does that guy think i\\\'m doing? \\\"<i>on the job</i>\\\"??? the moment egg comes out i ask her if i look like a hooker, she reassures me that i look classy. nothing to worry about.<br>\r\n<i>do hookers troll in the day time?</i> i ask egg<br>\r\n<i>sure, how do you think those motels on macarthur make money?<br>\r\noh, well i thought at night<br>\r\nno dude, hourly, it\\\'s all about the hourly rate</i> responds the wise egg.<br>\r\nbut at a safeway parking lot?'),(1839,'i\\\'ve been reading a lot as of late.  the rise in blog popularity has left me with infinite sources of lives being lived and stories being told.  it\\\'s also shown me that i have a serious and potentially destructive problem...\r\n<br>\r\n<br><b>i think i\\\'m trying to live through other people.<br>\r\n</b><br>i had suspected this a while back but tried to assure myself that \\\'no, i\\\'m getting <b>my</b> shit together...\\\' but in retrospect it was nothing more than one of those fleeting statements made only to make me feel better at the time.  funny thing about that is i <b>never</b> make those statements because i can always see through them and see them for what they are.  they never make me feel better.   i guess that one slipped through my radar while i was momentarily \r\nweakened.  my anger towards myself is easy to understand, i\\\'m surrounded by those who try to live through me and i know how much i hate it.  i hate it for me because of the pressure i see being the object of peoples \r\nfascination (or lack thereof) and i hate it for those who do it because it illustrates how much in need of their own thoughts and experiences.  hate is such a strong word, maybe it\\\'s more just empathy for all parties involved.<br><br>i want to write so badly, \r\ni have nothing going right now that would be of any real interest to anybody but \r\nmy head is still swimming in thought  i want to write without restrictions and without worry of how i\\\'m perceived and then because i\\\'m paranoid i want to know that people understand what i\\\'m going through and can relate.<br><br>it\\\'s easy to walk in the shadows but it\\\'s so fucking hard for me to stand in the sun.'),(1840,'<P>courtney love. (in the words of nerf herder, even: <I>courtney love, don\\\'t throw it all away.</i>)  courtney love said that. granted, it was about gwen stefani, but still.  it applies to a lot of my life.  in fact, i say it a hell of a lot--to other people, and silently, to myself.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>the problem with this is that i am not sixteen. i\\\'m twenty-one. i just feel sixteen.  all of the time.  i have always felt sixteen. i probably always will. it\\\'s not just an age--it is a state of mind.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>(except, when i was chronologically sixteen, i felt like i was fifty.  the only time i didn\\\'t act sixteen in my whole life so far, i think, is when i <i>was</i> sixteen.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and this has been at the forefront of my mind because last night i was told that i was too young.  he said, \\\"if you were even only three years older...\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<P>this coming from a boy who, despite being chronologically twenty-seeven, acts like he\\\'s 12.  he\\\'s cheating on his girlfriend with me, sometimes. he spent 45 minutes last night listing all of the reasons why he couldn\\\'t have sex with me, and number one on his list was my age. not his girlfriend, or that he might be in love with her, but my age. what is wrong with this picture?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>still, though, even as i asked him, \\\"what is <I>with</i> this age thing?\\\" i was feeling like a fraud, because he thinks i\\\'m twenty-one and i\\\'m really sixteen.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and his answer was that even though he knows that i think i know everything and have no farther to go, really twenty-one is the beginning and not the end, and it\\\'s all about my state of mind and what i think i want from people, and how i don\\\'t have a focus in my life, and i sat there staring at him, dumbfounded, letting my cigarette burn down to the filtre, and not drinking my grapefruit juice-and-vodka.  because i <i>know</i> that i don\\\'t know anything, that i have so far yet to go before i am close to knowing much at all, and really the person he\\\'s describing is himself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so it shouldn\\\'t bother me as much as it does.  because i know that i\\\'m sixteen and he thinks that i am sixteen anyway, and really, he is the one with all the problems.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>(i guess that makes me the one who\\\'s full of shit.  maybe i should just pick my head up and walk away, walking the coolest walk that i know.  cause in a month or two... well.)</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i\\\'ve forgotten my point, which is all right, because i forgot my point last night, too.  what i ended up doing was going in to my boss\\\'s office this morning and spilling the whole story to her, right from the very beginning, ending with his list, ending with me being too young.  and she looked at me with this furrow between her brows and she said, \\\"i know that he\\\'s a couple of years older than you, but he\\\'s <i>so</i> much younger than you. i hope you told him that.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(i didn\\\'t. but i wanted to.)</p>'),(1841,'<p>A couple of weeks ago I dumped my boyfriend, because I had dinner with a complete stranger who said more nice things about me in 15 minutes than Anthony had said in the past 6 months...and, no, \"Sure you can meet my friends...when you lose a hundred pounds,\" doesn\'t count as nice.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve been spending a lot of time with the new guy, Joel, since the breakup - just hanging out and the occasional unabashed, marathon, not-committal sex. (Hang on...the last time I said that, I wound up pregnant...ooops!)</P>\r\n<p>We were hanging out a couple of nights ago talking about potential guests for his radio show and somehow wound up talking about the <a href=\"http://www.stopclownpornnow.org\">Stop Clown Porn Now web site</a>.  I said that to be contrary, I was going to start the National Association for the Advancement of Clown Porn and hope that no one got confused by the acronym.  Joel, who frequently jokes about his massive schnaz, less than adequate sexual abilities, and miniscule winkie (only the nose part is true), said something to the effect of \"Why isn\'t there an organization out there promoting the porn careers of Italian guys with huge noses and small dicks?\"</P>\r\n<p>So I was sitting here at work tonight, all by myself, way too late, and way too overworked to look at another page of the web site I was supposed to be updating, and I decided to give Joel what he wanted.</P>\r\n<p align=center><img src=\"http://www.infoblvd.net/flansbmp/italian.gif\"></p>\r\n<p>It was either that or read more Cheever (work was not an option).  When the first penis pump picture I found was of a \"Dr. Joel\'s\" pump, I figured that fate was telling me that Cheever could wait until I go back to the Y tomorrow.</p>\r\n<p>I have way too much time on my hands.</p>'),(1842,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1842_1.jpg\'><br><br>\r\n<tt>\r\n<*_p*> i\'ve never been anywhere around the appalaichans or the bible<br>\r\n<*_p*> belt<br>\r\n<*_p*> i wanna see that stuff soon<br>\r\n<*_p*> need to start driving in that direction isntead of all this<br>\r\n<*_p*> western crap<br>\r\n/m _p yeah, it\'s worth seeing<br>\r\n/m _p it\'s insane<br>\r\n<*_p*> i want to see \"mississippi hill country\"<br>\r\n/m _p mississippi is the scariest place i\'ve been in all my young life<br>\r\n<*_p*> real america<br>\r\n/m _p hanging out with drug dealers in south central and puerto rican\r\nbikers in\r\nbrooklyn have nothing on that place, believe me.<br>\r\n\r\n</p>'),(1843,'<p>I used to smoke Lucky Strike Filters, but when I saw the list of chemicals those cigarette makers put in their smokey-treats, I switched back to a more all-natural offering, either Drum or American Spirits.  Now, after having just smoked, I want a goddamn, fucking cigarette.  I want a Marlboro Red.  I want fiberglass shards to cut my lungs.  I want sulfur to make it burn faster.  I want 4,000+ chemicals to leave that processing plant taste in my mouth.  I want it.  I want it bad.</p><p>Obviously, all that shit is there to make us more addicted.  I\\\'m feeling it, and that\\\'s why I switched.  Someday, I hope to breathe without rasping and be able to walk up a flight of stairs without getting winded.  Unfortunatly, I possess the opposite of willpower.  The harder I try to do something, the more unlikely it is that it will happen.  Eventually, I\\\'m going to have to learn how to zen that shit.</p><p>I remember one of my first experiences in my dance studio in NYC:<br>\\\"Hey, can I get a cigarette off of you?\\\"<br>He pulls cigarette from pack holding it between us but not handing it over yet, \\\"Before I give this to you, let me say one thing: You\\\'re a dancer.  Someone who engages in a physically demanding endevour.  Cigarettes are bad for you.  Why the hell are you smoking?\\\"<Br>\\\"Just about every actor, singer, dancer I know smokes.  It\\\'s just a performer thing.\\\"<br>\\\"No, it\\\'s a depressive thing,\\\" then he hands me the smoke.</p><p>Green and I decided that smoking feels better when you have bronchitis.  It hurts more.  You can feel it killing you faster.</p>'),(1844,'<p>As I write this I am listening to the new LTJ Bukem release, Progression Sessions Vol. 6.  It\\\'s a live mix of Bukem and MC Conrad live in Boston.  Conrad is basically singing throughout the entire mix (taking emceeing to a whole other level) and this is one of the tightest Bukem mixes I\\\'ve ever heard.  This is a wicked, wicked set!</p>\r\n<p>Stereolab\\\'s new album, Sound-Dust, is quickly becoming my favourite disc by the British duo.  It\\\'s such a pretty (yes, pretty) album.  For people who know nothing of Stereolab, other than knowing Parsec and Les Yper-Sound from the two Volkswagen commercials they were featured in; this would be a great disc to pick up to get into them.  I think I\\\'m going to try to pick up some tickets for their Toronto date (November 2 @ The Phoenix) of their current world tour.  I missed them when they were here two years ago with Sonic Youth.  That would have been something to witness.</p>\r\n<p>Yesterday, while killing time before a lecture and chilling at the 333 Yonge HMV, I impulse-bought Roots Manuva\\\'s disc Run Come Save Me.  I got turned on to him via his collaborations with Herbalizer and his live track on Ninja Tune\\\'s Xen Cuts is just pure gold.  This disc is fucking awesome.  And when you have lyrics such as: \\\"Roots Manuva, kick you in the cock,\\\" you really cannot go wrong.  A review of the disc in last weeks\\\' issue of Now described him as the UK\\\'s Kardinal Offishall.</p>\r\n<p>And finally we come to the other great album I have been lovin\\\' off lately: Bj&ouml;rk\\\'s Vespertine.  Definately my favourite Bj&ouml;rk release to date; so much better than I expected it to be.  In eleven days I will be seeing her live in the Hummingbird Centre, which is home to the National Ballet and the Candian Opera Company, performing with a choir from Greenland, an Inuit throat-singer, a full orchestra and San Fransisco\\\'s duo Matmos.  It\\\'s going to be so freaking good.  Most of my friends will be there, so that will make it just that much more powerful.  We\\\'re all getting dressed up fancy-like and are going to act like the civilised twenty-somethings we are supposed to be.  Or something like that.</p>\r\n<p>Yes.  Now is a great time to be a music fan.  I know I\\\'m extremely pleased with the sounds being driven out of my speakers lately.</p>\r\n'),(1845,'<p>\r\nI\\\'m in class. IRC doesn\\\'t work for me, which means I\\\'m bored, cause I know the stuff we\\\'re going through. It\\\'s fun though. HTML! But I\\\'m waiting for my break. Or pretty much waiting for the clock to turn 9 so that I can leap into his car. We\\\'re eating with my parents tonight. Haha! They don\\\'t even hate him, and that\\\'s FUN. Everyone we don\\\'t know hate the fact that we\\\'re together though. That doesn\\\'t exactly make sense, does it?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTired tired tired. I have to DO stuff now. Amazing, isn\\\'t it?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI love wasting space. It\\\'s even more fun when people don\\\'t believe I\\\'m me. Hi.\r\n</p>'),(1846,'<P>Honest women are unerving. Like this girl on the phone telling me she needs dick, and acting like a maniac. It makes me imagine all my female friends in this \\\" i have not gotten laid\\\" in a month massive neurotic mood. Sucidal tendencies are invisible. Mix it with being honry and the taste of boredom slowly spreading all over my body. the desperation marks me for death. no sleep. no food. may the body suffer what the mind will not. speed me up to everlasting nudity. wrap me around your fingers. if you hug me, i will feel you up, and don\\\'t mind a bleeding heart.             </P>'),(1847,'<P>i have always wanted to live in a world where the great mysteries actually exist.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhere leprechauns\r\nhide in the forest hatching mischievous schemes to trick the duffus humans into falling into deep dark holes.  tickling them endlessly until they relent all of their worldy wealth to the little green ones.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhere shatter skirted lithe legged nymphs tempt young lads into woodsy perils.  \r\n</P><P>\r\na world where to prove ones valor you could traipse off into the hills and slaughter the furnace wielding dragons.  in so doing gain the admiration of the people and the love of the fairest princess of the land. \r\n</P><P>\r\nnow this would be a world of infinite adventure and marvel.  however, i have come to the sad conclusion that maybe these things only exist in the bits and bytes of computer games, the dreams of doe-eyed children, and the pages of endless books.  \r\n</P><P>i have only experienced one such mystery.  even though not as mighty or legendary as a dragon, to me it is still goes unexplained.  this is deja vu.  \r\n</P><P>\r\neach time the wave sweeps through me it rekindles the smallest of beliefs that maybe some of these other greater legends exist.  hidden away, experienced by a select few and that maybe just maybe some day i will be invited into the special circle that lives in this alter-world.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nnow i know someone out there can probably spout some scientific explanation for deja vu.  however, i am not listening.  my ears are closed.  my brain can not - will not process your explanations. \r\n</P><P>\r\n i see deja vu as my first test from the guardians of the secret world. if i pass, the invitation to partake will, carried by a raven to my window sill, arrive soon .\r\n</P>'),(1848,'<p>I was just trying to clean my room, and listen to the new Tori Amos jay brought home, which is a cover album of songs written by men.</p>\r\n<p>The second song,  a cover of eminem\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/lyricseminem/shadylp_lyrics/bonnie_clyde.html\\\">97 bonnie and clyde</a>, has sent adrenaline coarsing throught my body.</p>\r\n<p>I remembered driving through Sweden with Svante, Eminem on the boom box in the back seat of his Cadillac. At some point in the album is a vignette in which some dude is beating his wife or his girlfriend. I dont remember the details that well now. We had wailing and punching blasting out of the windows in Stockholm. I hollered, \\\"what the FUCK iS THIS????\\\" and he became embarassed and turned it down.</p>\r\n<p>I avoid listening to eminem. I have an immediately gut-wrenching physical reaction to his words, whether they are meant to represent his personal beliefs or not. Noone\\\'s glamorous rhetoric can usually convince me that he is not deeply misogynist. I have heard all the arguments, all the commentary, all the sound bytes, about his intention to \\\"stir controversy\\\" with his \\\"shocking\\\" lyrics.</p>\r\n<p>Every boy I have liked recently has an eminem album. Rather than interest me in eminem, it takes my respect down a small notch for any given boy, to find that cd in the case. I think even Sam has an Eminem album</p>\r\n<p>Hearing Tori cover these lyrics, knowing she is a victim of sexual abuse and if not self-defined as a \\\"feminist\\\" that she at least comments on gender as part of her work, my understanding of Eminem is open again for questions. </p>\r\n<p>Considering the <a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/lyricseminem/shadylp_lyrics/bonnie_clyde.html\\\">lyrics</a> of this song, should it matter if Eminem understands the very hard and real <a href=\"http://campus.murraystate.edu/services/women.center/domesticviolence.htm\">statistics</a> of domestic violence and the life and autonomy it takes from thousands of women every year? Is he actually <i>sympathetic</i> to this issue? Is it enough that he has written this provocative and disturbingly poetic song, creating an impulse in a hairy feminazi such as myself to ask questions about the relationship between popular media and social progress? Do I believe that Eminem romanticizes and even advocates beating your wife? Do I think he is just a cheap sensationalist? Does his personal political beliefs matter at all once the song is out there? Does his words have a negative impact on the fight against gender violence? Am I wrong to be angry? Will anyone carefully and consciously address these questions or just write me off as missing the point? If I am \\\"missing the point\\\", can someone describe \\\"it\\\" in detail to me?</p>'),(1849,'wheee'),(1850,'hi'),(1851,'Damn. Right now I\\\'m really stoned and I can\\\'t keep a certain song from looping inside my head.  The song? \\\"Back Dat Ass Up\\\". Shite.<br><br>\r\nmeanwhile, im posting here as somehow the mental processes of putting random thoughts to text sparks my mind and helps me get things in some mental order<br><br>\r\nthings like\\\" why do i smoke so much? (i croaked when i read essemar\\\'s recent post about ciggies), why can\\\'t i have a lasting relationship? why do i have no ambition? why am i so damned content?<br><br>\r\nwriting helps me sort these types of things out, so thanks for indulging me, here. you guys are great.\r\n<br><br>\r\nperhaps i just need to grow up some. i think most everyone does in one way or another. i need more experience. speaking of, i\\\'m travelling to new york next week. the idea of travel at least ought to spark me to be somwhat motivated.<br><Br>\r\ngoodnight, aly'),(1852,''),(1853,'<p>\r\nMost (rational) people realise that they aren\\\'t the centre of THE universe, but in a way, we\\\'re all the centre of our own personal universe. We experience THE universe from our own exclusive point of view. it\\\'s the only way we can, right? </p>\r\n<p>\r\nI know what an apple tastes like. You know what an apple tastes like. But do you taste an apple in the same way that i do? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI know what the colour blue looks like, and so do you. We could both pick a blue pen out of a pot of multi-coloured pens without a problem. But do we both see the colour <i>blue</i> in the same way? Maybe i see the colour <i>blue</i> the same way you see the colour <i>red</i>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe what i taste as an apple is what you taste as steak.\r\n</p>'),(1854,'<center><img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1854_1.jpg\' height=\'150\'><br> \r\n<br>Mmmmmmmmmmm. I may leave this space for a space.\r\n<br>A wildÂ  flowering humanity searches for growth within translucent\r\nforests.\r\n<br>On the mirror side, the line can be backwards.Â  Shining back to\r\nyou is the infinite realization of existence.\r\n<br>When we touch our souls and we see clarity in the mind of our eyes,Â \r\nwhere on the evolution of society dwells our line of life, our murmuringÂ \r\nheart beats to bring an air of sense of need of fresh fruit and no hallowed\r\npersonalities.\r\n<br>Whereas shining suns beam music of joy around languent aggression.\r\n<br>Always remember, there is an existence beyond all trite voids.\r\n<br>The fair of dreams will bring solitude only after embarking on incandescent\r\nvoyages of habitual kindness.\r\n<br>I say all with love and wishes to prosper all.\r\n<br>Too return to this space to you\r\n<br></center>\r\n\r\n</body>\r\n</html>'),(1855,'<P>I\\\'m flying today.<BR>\r\nNo one\\\'s trying to think about it.<BR>\r\nGET YOUR IDS OUT, JUST GET ON THE PLANE.<BR>\r\n(It\\\'s the quickest way we can all get back to normal.) \r\n</P><P>\r\nStay bewildered about the whys and wherefores, just know<BR> that the buildings are gone and there was fire and death and the proper emotion to feel is anger.<BR>\r\nI\\\'m flying today, like a lot of u.s.<BR>\r\nUS, Americans, flying<BR>\r\nWithout thinking, flying high<BR>\r\nTaking the high road,<BR>\r\nFar above the moral grounds<BR>\r\nFor living - or dying<BR>\r\nat a velocity of over 100mph<BR>\r\nstraight into the earth<BR>\r\n62 ft. deep<BR>\r\nWreckage, brown skin white skin<BR>\r\nWho cares?<BR>\r\nIf some die so that more live<BR>\r\nIt\\\'s utilitarian<BR>\r\nlike utility knives<BR>\r\nin the hands of scared, crazy martyrs dying for something that is so worth dying for, if only everyone would see, so worth dying you\\\'ll kill everyone to make your point to the empty burning skies.</P>\r\n<P>\r\nOnly 8 dollars an hour they pay them<BR>\r\nNo benefits to live<BR>\r\nNo benefits to die<BR>\r\nIf they find a bomb<BR>\r\nWe all die<BR>\r\nAirport rent-a-cops<BR>\r\nScared, tired<BR>\r\nBlamed if anything goes wrong<BR>\r\nSecurity in the hands of the insecure<BR>\r\nthe underpaid<BR>\r\nthe overworked<BR>\r\nwith no benefits<BR>\r\nto remove the schrapnel in their leg<BR>\r\nso don\\\'t you think they\\\'d run faster than you?</P>\r\n<P>\r\nLadies and gentlemen, cellular telephones must be turned off for the duration of the flight. (pause)<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nUNSPOKEN: unless, of course, um, the plane has been taken over by hijackers.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>. . . . . . . . static . . . . . . . . .</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and the safety video goes black<BR>\r\nno more smiling blondes in oxygen masks looking like a plane full of little piggies waiting in line at the<BR> slaughterhouse.<BR>\r\n\r\n. . . .SShhhhclick . . click <BR>\r\n(the TVs come to life)<BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"And once again, thank you for flying the friendly skies.\\\"<BR>'),(1856,'<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1856_1.jpg\'><p>\r\n[=Topic=] adam changed the topic to \\\"rekanize all area crew\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nyah yah yo<br>\r\nyou better rekanize my whole area crew<br>\r\nwe comin to you live from out the blue<br>\r\nrepresenting southern cali. and the bay area too<br>\r\ncommunicatin\\\' on the net via finger kung fu<br>\r\nwe be shellin\\\' into still.hungry and saturn5<br>\r\nif you want to message me, just type \\\"hi\\\"<br>\r\nand we can scheme on a plan to meet up at the hive<br>\r\njust jump in your dodge and cruise down the I-5<br>\r\nwe be bustin up wack servers like they was pin~atas<br>\r\npurifying yall bustas with some holy waters<br>\r\nshorting out circuit boards cuz you can\\\'t stop us<br>\r\nwe at broadband speeds so you can\\\'t cop us<br>\r\nrockin mad poetry on swinney.org<br>\r\nit\\\'s the place to be whether or not you\\\'re bored<br>\r\nit\\\'s where all the best logs be gettin stored<br>\r\nand all the hottest internet tail be gettin whored<br>\r\nyo i\\\'m gettin rude<br>\r\ni better step off the mic<br>\r\nbecause i\\\'m skinny and white<br>\r\nand i don\\\'t like to fight<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[=Topic=] adam changed the topic to \\\"sleep\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1857,'<b>Ode to a Kebab:</b> <i>a tribute in <a href=\\\"http://www.jamazon.co.uk/index.php?article=37\\\">text</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org\\\">photographs</a></i><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab1.jpg\\\">Aha! A freshly procured kebab platter \r\nfrom <i>Jerusalem Shaworma, NYC</i>.  While waiting for the kebab to fully cook, the man serving it gleefully \r\nshouted <i>\\\"On the HOUSE!\\\"</i> and handed me a hummus-dipped pita to munch on while I waited. Kebab people\r\nare <i>good people</i>.<br><br><i>Spinning around your juices drip<br>Spin cook spin cook spin<br>\r\nYou make me dribble on my lip<br>Saliva on my chin</i><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab2.jpg\\\">Kebab, I see you, peeking out from behind that soda! How succulent.  My mouth waters.<br><br>\r\n<i>Mahir asks \\\"Chilli sauce?\\\" of me<br>And weilds his giant knife<br>Towards you he strides galantly<br>\r\nAnd takes from you a slice<br></i><br><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab3.jpg\\\">Watch while I devour you, fair thing of cullinary beauty!<br><br>\r\n<i>Your chilli sauce and salad garnish<br>All these things we love<br>Your reputation can never tarnish<br>\r\nAlthough they push and shove</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab4.jpg\\\">Argh! Attack!<br><br><i>As i chew upon your meat<br>\r\nSo flavoursome and juicy<br>The chilli sauce upon my feet<br>Resembles <a href=\\\"http://www.buseyworld.com/gary.htm\\\">Gary Busey</a></i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab5.jpg\\\">Argh! I wash you down with soda!<br><br><i>Hey Mister, your so tasty\r\nWith your juicy juicy meat<br>Hey Mister, you\\\'re never pasty<br>You\\\'re a wholesome treat</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br> \r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab6.jpg\\\">Delightful!<br><br><i>Wrap him up. Wrap him well.<br>\r\nIn a Pita, he\\\'ll think it\\\'s swell.</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab7.jpg\\\">Mmmm.<br><br><i>Add some salad, add a chilli.<br>Wash your hands, Don\\\'t you touch your willy.<br>\r\nFinish off with chilli sauce.<br>Complement with Lager, of course</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br>\r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab8.jpg\\\">Kebab, I feast on you!<br><br><i>Your Turkish friends rally around<br>\r\nAttending to your needs<br>And when i give them my Â£2.00<br>You are my tasty feed</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br> \r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab9.jpg\\\">Finishing off the pepper garnish...<br><br><i>Although you cook while rotating<br>\r\nWith flies licking your shank<br>And fall off in your grease floatating<br>Money in the bank</i><br><br><br><br><br><br><br><br> \r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab10.jpg\\\">Finis!<br><br><i>Kebabs, as we all know, are the<br>staple of any well balanced diet.<br>The humble Doner Kebab, with<br>it\\\'s hot chilli sauce beautifully<br>offsetting the subtle flavour of<br>the lamb. Of course, there\\\'s<br>salad in there for the ladies too.</i><br><br><br><br><br> \r\n<img align=\\\"left\\\" src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/pics/kebab11.jpg\\\">A happy customer of <i>Jarusalem Shaworma</i>. <br><br><i>Repeat as needed...</i>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1858,'<b>Ode to a Kebab:</b> <i>a tribute in <a href=\\\"http://www.jamazon.co.uk/index.php?article=37\\\">text</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org\">photographs</a></i><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_1.jpg\'>\r\n<P>Aha! A freshly procured kebab platter \r\nfrom <i>Jerusalem Shaworma, NYC</i>.  While waiting for the kebab to fully cook, the man serving it gleefully \r\nshouted <i>\\\"On the HOUSE!\\\"</i> and handed me a hummus-dipped pita to munch on while I waited. Kebab people\r\nare <i>good people</i>.<br><br><i>Spinning around your juices drip<br>Spin cook spin cook spin<br>\r\nYou make me dribble on my lip<br>Saliva on my chin</i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_2.jpg\'>\r\n<P>\r\nKebab, I see you, peeking out from behind that soda! How succulent.  My mouth waters.<br><br>\r\n<i>Mahir asks \\\"Chilli sauce?\\\" of me<br>And wields his giant knife<br>Towards you he strides galantly<br>\r\nAnd takes from you a slice<br></i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_3.jpg\'>\r\n<P>\r\nWatch while I devour you, fair thing of cullinary beauty!<br><br>\r\n<i>Your chili sauce and salad garnish<br>All these things we love<br>Your reputation can never tarnish<br>\r\nAlthough they push and shove</i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_4.jpg\'><P>Argh! Attack!<br><br><i>As i chew upon your meat<br>\r\nSo flavoursome and juicy<br>The chili sauce upon my feet<br>Resembles <a href=\\\"http://www.buseyworld.com/gary.htm\\\">Gary Busey</a></i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_5.jpg\'><P>Argh! I wash you down with soda!<br><br><i>Hey Mister, your so tasty\r\nWith your juicy juicy meat<br>Hey Mister, you\\\'re never pasty<br>You\\\'re a wholesome treat</i><br><br> \r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_6.jpg\'><P>Delightful!<br><br><i>Wrap him up. Wrap him well.<br>\r\nIn a Pita, he\\\'ll think it\\\'s swell.</i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_7.jpg\'><P>Mmmm.<br><br><i>Add some salad, add a chili.<br>Wash your hands, Don\\\'t you touch your willy.<br>\r\nFinish off with chili sauce.<br>Complement with Lager, of course</i><br><br>\r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_8.jpg\'><P>Kebab, I feast on you!<br><br><i>Your Turkish friends rally around<br>\r\nAttending to your needs<br>And when i give them my Â£2.00<br>You are my tasty feed</i><br><br> \r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_9.jpg\'><P>Finishing off the pepper garnish...<br><br><i>Although you cook while rotating<br>\r\nWith flies licking your shank<br>And fall off in your grease floatating<br>Money in the bank</i><br><br> \r\n<img  src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_10.jpg\'><P>Finis!<br><br><i>Kebabs, as we all know, are the<br>staple of any well balanced diet.<br>The humble Doner Kebab, with<br>it\\\'s hot chilli sauce beautifully<br>offsetting the subtle flavour of<br>the lamb. Of course, there\\\'s<br>salad in there for the ladies too.</i><br><br>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1858_11.jpg\'><P>A happy customer of <i>Jarusalem Shaworma</i>. <br><br><i>Repeat as needed...</i>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1859,'Book #2 entry #18 B 9/6/00 3:04pm Wednesday, Spot on Elmwood\r\n\r\nCaffeine Hope\r\n\r\nThe coffee sits on the coffee table, in a glass.  \r\nIt?s iced coffee.  \r\nIt is, in fact, \r\nan iced Americano. \r\nThe glass is half full of coffee.  It is also half full of air.  \r\nThere is allot of cream in this coffee.  A filmy residue of coffee/cream clings to the inside of the glass.  \r\nIn the distance it?s another coffee, \r\non another coffee table, \r\nbut this one is in a cup, a coffee cup.  Steam drifts out of the cup in gentle wafts.  \r\nThere is no more to say about this coffee, it is to far away to study it in further detail.  The closer coffee is almost gone now.  \r\nThe glass is only about a sixth full now, \r\nnow one twelfth, \r\nnow none.  \r\nBut there is still ice in this glass and the residue that clings to the sides slowly slips to the bottom.  Perhaps it will mix with the ice as it melts to make more coffee like liquid worth drinking.  \r\nIt will not be enough to fill the glass, but perhaps it will be, \r\nenough.  \r\nThe farther cup is now gone, and along with it the coffee it held.  \r\nThere is nothing caffeinated within sight.  Yet I hold onto hope.  \r\n\r\nCory Hallett Vinyard\r\n'),(1860,'<p>Last night, I had one of those weird pre-sleep dreams.  Those dreams where you\\\'re still technically awake but are basically retarted and unable to do anything.</p>\r\n<p>It also wasn\\\'t so much a dream as it was a nightmare.  The setting was last year, at Jae\\\'s birthday party on the top floor of his condo building.  I was outside with everyone on the patio that leads to the roof, drink in hand, having a good time.  Mikey began getting all <i>I\\\'m Mikey and like to rough-house when I\\\'ve been drinking</i>.</p>\r\n<p>Somehow, this lead to his running at me and basically throwing me over the railing of the patio.</p>\r\n<p>Now, this is where things really got fukt.  You see, the dream kept replaying from me going over the edge.  In one variation, Mikey was trying to knock me off after I had grabbed hold of the ledge and Sara came and put out a cigarette on his kneck and then proceeded to kick him in the crotch while people helped me back over the railing.  I then proceeded to give Mikey an Angel Face-style beating, nearly killing him.</p>\r\n<p>After that, we go back to where I fall over the railing and I am hanging there for a while.  Mikey doesn\\\'t really do anything and everyone is kind of standing around in shock, staring at me in disbelief.  I eventually loose my grip and fall, screaming, the 20-plus stories to the area directy in front of the main entrance.</p>\r\n<p>Then more variations of those two themes were played out before I finally regained some semblance of consciousness and began digesting everything that had just happened to me.  My heart was racing and  I was thinking about how horrible it is to run through the same falling scenario multiple times, each time finding a new and horrible way for your body to impact with a concrete surface after an extended and uncontrolled fall (landing on your feet [or at least, with your legs extended downwards] provides for many disgusting possibilities).</p>\r\n<p>It was totally uncalled for.  I\\\'m abou to go to sleep now and am hoping for a little more sympathy from my brain tonight.</p>\r\n'),(1861,'our Cocks remain unbeaten! it looked like for a while there that they would get licked, but people are learning--you can\\\'t lick a Cock. in the end, our cocks found the hole they were looking for, rammed into it, and carried the ball into the zone.\r\n<br>\r\n'),(1862,'<P> I see many of my friends in the twenty-something but under twenty-five quandry devoted to love. It outsmarts them how one minute all we long for is to be held and needed, and then there is the whole carnal onset of things...</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I am sure everyone knows some of what this entry is gonna say. And some won\\\'t. It is a movement. A fluidity we want with the other. To be able to sqeeze through crevices and know/understand that individual. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> We want everything in reciprocal amounts.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Needing him to love me and jump fences as often as I do that for him.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I do not have the patience to fix broken things...like relationships. When the air has settled and reality hits is when I seem to leave.\r\nI am not comfortable with long conversations of how I feel and what does he think and Yes I am inconsiderate and yes I am hard bit if you don\\\'t like it then leave I always say...and they never leave.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> Movement of an arm, of a smile. How we all act as water. laughing one into the other. Hiding from identity as hard as we are caught looking for one and millions. Travel gives me fluidity. Like long black hair moving as tentacles beneath water, the blonde hairs on the back of his neck and the small paying lamp light accentuating them.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> I haven\\\'t been immersed, obsessed with something as driving as death, love, life or hate.To a waking, imbedded by fleeting thoughts..</p>\r\n\r\n<P> He says I need to work on my art. That I haven\\\'t drawn anything. That all I do is sleep.\r\nAll I do is talk on the phone. Four days ago travel was running from my problems. I will not settle though as many of my friends have for the same zip code. </p>\r\n\r\n<P> Being single allows me better work...that or the watery relationship I had with benjamin. Many well written passionate poems came from our fights. Love can be art. I cannot stand mundane love...when things become ritual is when I come up with excuses why we are not well mated. Why it would be easier to be without me and how I wish not to be blamed for making someones life hard-<I>er</i>.</p>'),(1863,'<p>I wanted very desperately for last night to be good...a little vacation for my soul, but it wound up being very much the opposite.  I wish I knew why I always allow myself to get hypersensitive about the stupidest things, and why nothing magnifies overblown emotions like a dark, empty apartment.  I had to sleep on the couch because, after trying for hours, the bed was just too barren and expansive.  I used to think I was afraid of the dark, always sleeping (never any more soundly) with at least one light on.  Really, I\\\'m just afraid of the emptiness, and the more I think about it, the more I can convince myself that I\\\'m never going to fill that space.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can say that I need to be alone and that only some good \\\"me\\\" time will allow me to feel good about myself again - but that doesn\\\'t necessarily mean that I <B>want</b> to be alone.  I can say that all I want is to have someone that will hold me and protect me from the emptiness while I sleep - but the truth is that I don\\\'t really want to trust anyone with that kind of task.  I have never met anyone who was willing to help me take on my fears that wasn\\\'t at some later time very eager to throw them back in my face.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The most ridiculous thing about all of this is that I continue to set myself up for that kind of soul-crushing dissapointment.  I know exactly why my relationships don\\\'t work.  I know exactly what I do to get myself into each and every one of those relationships.  I know that I have no idea how to change that. Or maybe I\\\'m just too scared or lazy or whatever to do anything differently.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s not all bad, but I sincerely hope that I deserve better.</p>'),(1864,'<LI>1 Job Application per day = 1 rejection per day\r\n<LI>last pay cheque on August 15 = not a whole lot of money left over now\r\n<LI>My self esteem - all the hits it has been taking = less self esteem\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nI know that I do not equal my job status, but it\\\'s hard to keep remembering that.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nMeh. '),(1865,'</head>\r\n<body>\r\n\r\n<center>joy\r\n<br>\r\n<TABLE BORDER=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" CELLSPACING=\"0\" HEIGHT=\"1000\" WIDTH=\"685\">\r\n<TR>\r\n<TD  BACKGROUND=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1866_1.jpg\" VALIGN=\"center\" align=\"center\"><B>\r\n<BR>today is my birthday :)\r\n<BR>i am 27\r\n<br>i hear snap n\\\' pops crackling outside\r\n<br>megan like to throw them out of her window\r\n<br>i am a space monkey\r\n<br>there is no denying it now\r\n<br>the pink elephant told me so\r\n<br>&\r\n<br>that I should start a micro brewery\r\n<br>I made a batch of blueberry beer once\r\n<br>using real blueberries\r\n<br>i didn\\\'t account for the berries having their own sugars\r\n<br>so it blew up and sprayed blueberries all over the ceiling\r\n<br>it still worked out ok though\r\n<br>a mighty tasty blueberry brew\r\n<br>13.5%\r\n<br>two was the recommended dosage\r\n<br>next time i will stick to the extract</center></B>\r\n</TD></TR></TABLE>\r\n</center>'),(1866,''),(1867,'<p>then we\\\'re going to move to Iceland.</p><p>We\\\'re going to each spend our days flirting with, making love to, all the beautiful Icelandic people.  Either that, or we\\\'ll just get married to eachother and live as hermits.  But somehow in our conversations, we do both while doing neither.</p><P>We\\\'ve already decided on names for our four children (2 boys, 2 girls): Table, Essemar, Blue, Inertia.  When we talk about how we\\\'ll feed and shelter them, the conversation usually ends with me beating sea otters to death against rocks while Icelandic fishermen stare at me and think, \\\"Fucked Up Americans.\\\"</p><p><i>So... Deborah hasn\\\'t called.  How are things with Mike?  Maybe we should wait until I get my PhD, or become a renowned dancer.  Then we could get married, but those Icelandic chicks are pretty hot, huh?  Yeah, we should go right now.  WE SHOULD GO RIGHT NOW!!</i></p><p>That was last night.  Now, I\\\'m at work.  It\\\'s like torture to allow people the freedom to dream so freely, then force them to wake up at 7am.  That was the very first thought in my head this morning.  <i>Waking up this early in the morning is torture.</i></p>'),(1868,'<p>this weekend was nice</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1868_1.jpg\'>\r\n\r\n<p>somebody\\\'s dog tried to rape me in Sausalito</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1868_2.jpg\'>\r\n<p>many gifts were given and enjoyed</p>\r\n<img src=\'http://swinney.org/journals/images/1868_3.jpg\'>\r\n<p>i am elusive</p>'),(1869,'<p>\r\nhiya!  I\'m Jim.  I redesigned swinney.org.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<b>new features</b><br>\r\nthere are also a bunch of new features in this version, which were cooked up by <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=austin\">austin</a> and <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=ix\">ix</a>.  these include a comments by username viewer and user accounts, which allow you to register your username, change your settings, and create a bozolist, which allows you to filter articles and comments written by a particular user from your swinney.org experience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<b>new design</b><br>\r\nI didn\'t want to deviate too much from the HTML 2.0 spirit of swinney.org, so I stuck with blue, purple and shades of grey.  the logotype is based on an <a href=\"http://www.emigre.com/\">Emigre</a> typeface called Sabbath Black, designed by Miles Newlyn.  the navigation type is in a <a href=\"http://www.fontfont.com\">FontShop</a> typeface called FF Info Text, designed by Erik Spiekermann.  I tried to keep the interface design as familiar as possible, limiting my changes to making terminology and link behavior more consistent and improving navigation.  I wanted to make the collaborative aspects of swinney.org more obvious, and make the distinction between entries and comments clearer while at the same time making it easier to get back and forth between them.  i also wanted to improve the default look of the entries and comments, while maitaining the design flexibility swinney.org writers have come to expect.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nlet me know what you think.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1870,'a dream of mine is to buy a sailboat for low $$ and \r\nfix it up, and then sail to the caribbean. not around \r\nthe world, just down that way.<br><br> \r\ni want to go to st john \r\nagain. <br><br>\r\nwe stopped there once when i was in the navy, \r\nand some friends and i rented a boat and drove around \r\nthe whole island, and went snorkeling. it was pretty \r\noutstanding. anyway it is like a 6-7 hr sail from FL \r\nto the bahamas, and another day or so after that to \r\nthe virgins. also i want to see hemingway country out \r\non bimini.<br><br> i want to be half-naked and sunburned and \r\nhungry for a while. all i need is a boat, good weather,\r\n ice, beer, vodka, tonic, limes, reefer, a woman, a \r\ncoleman stove, another woman, and some food and i\'m set. and maybe a \r\ngps and a weather radio. probably some flares too. i \r\nget so excited thinking about it. you can get a \r\nsailboat that will do it for $5k or less plus extra \r\ngear. also i would take a gun, just in case. its \r\nalways better to have a gun is one of my philosophies. \r\ni dont like violence but i REALLY dont like being hurt \r\nor robbed. what do you think?\r\n'),(1871,'This past week has been pretty exhausting for some reason; I don\'t know why. It\'s absoultely beautiful outside, but I\'ve been tired out for some reason. In fact, this is my very favourite time of year, as late summer fades into autumn; the air gets crisper, cooler, the angle of the sunlight streaming down becomes more acute, strange shadows abound. I do love this time of year- always have, always will. I delight in it, as a matter of fact. <br><br>\r\n\r\nBut for some reason, I haven\'t been enjoying the seasonal change as much as usual this year, or these past couple of weeks; I observed this last night when I felt troubled, upon gazing at a <i>very</i> full, beautiful moon. I could attribute it to so many reasons that I dont know where to begin- busy, not enough sleep, too much moving, too many activities, too tired, too lazy, too much TV, lonliness. I haven\'t been getting out for enough runs in the park, rows on the river, or urban adventures around NYC like I always did, not enough Lomo-accompanied jaunts around the City\'s gritty underside, not enough trips to the shore to watch for migrating whales, geese, porpoises. I definitely am not getting outside for walks around the city enough, but as thats something thats best enjoyed with a companion, perhaps thats why I haven\'t indulged so much this season; I\'ve been a loner lately, for no apparent reason. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe crux of all this drivel is this: I\'m the type of person that thinks that one should work to sustain their life. Not vice-versa; not living to sustain their work, job, occupation, etc. I\'m sure that sounds so trite; most all people have to work to support themselves, myself included. Its just that I wish I could sink my teeth into my job more without it consuming so much of me, the good sides of me; it taps into my non-work self a little too much sometimes. I wish I enjoyed my job, no, <i>relished</i> it enough to thrill in each morning that I got to wake up to commute to my office. I\'m not sure that I feel that way at the moment... thats not to say I don\'t like my job, of course, but it isn\'t the be-all-and-end-all of my personal existence, thats for sure. Its left me feeling somewhat disconnected lately.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMaybe things will change when I begin grad school this spring. Maybe I\'m just one of those poor souls that\'s a born student. We\'ll certainly see. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIf I\'m still whining about this then, someone please slap me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(1872,'<P>I found a single kitten, mewling for a mom in our backyard today.</P>\r\n<P>Anyone want to adopt?</P>\r\n<P>Probably less than two weeks old.  I\'m gonna take her to the animal control peoples to have her checked out soon.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><img src=\"http://www.jihad.to/colby/images/kitten/IveDiscoveredPants.jpg\" width=450></P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://www.jihad.to/colby/images/kitten/tinyDrink.jpg\" width=450></P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://www.jihad.to/colby/images/kitten/tinyDrinkie.jpg\" width=450></P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://www.jihad.to/colby/images/kitten/tinyOne.jpg\" width=450></P>\r\n<P><img src=\"http://www.jihad.to/colby/images/kitten/tinyTwo.jpg\" width=450></P>'),(1873,'<img src=\"\" border=\"0\" alt=\"sugar never tasted so good\">'),(1874,'this is a test. only a test. pls ignore.'),(1875,'http://jihad.to/colby/images/kitten'),(1876,'<p>trying to find a good house-mate is hard to do. I am renting a room, a nice room, for $360 + $150 down - sounds reasonable to me. Some fucker is climbing under my skin here. Read on:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dude:<br>\r\nIs it okay if i rent the room temporally without a lease?and your deposit is kind of too much for a rental room,don,t you think?let me know okay.i am interested to know how gracious you can be.let me know soon.</p><p>\r\nBMG:<br>\r\nwithout a lease is cool, but I canâ€™t waiver on the deposit. Unless my old roommate wants to see her money go bye-bye. She might not care.</p><p>\r\nDude:<br>\r\nwhose roommate and what money would go bye bye?you mean you are renting the place for someone else?let me know what you are talking about.</p><p>\r\nBMG:<br>\r\nsorry for the confusion. My former roommate just moved out, in the middle of the lease. she paid 150 down on the room, and in order for her to get it back the new renter will have to pay her. this will have to be up to her. If you are interested I think it could be worked out.</p><p>\r\nDude:<br>\r\nthe confussion is still in your words.is it not you who should know wether he or she gets it it back if she or he moved out in the middle of the lease?axcept of course if his or her moving out is justifiable.did you mistreat your roomate?becouse i have seen that people are more evil minded this days than ever.from my experiences,they pay evil for good.i hope you are not one of those,becouse they are hiden everywhere.wolves in sheep clothing.they call you friends but they are worst enemy.instruments of evil empires.believe me they are everywhere today,but the good ones are hard to find.please don,t be afraide to let me know the real truth alright</p><p>\r\nBMG:<br>\r\nheh. um, well I am overwhelmed. Here is the situation. I have been renting this duplex for 3 years now. My former roommate, friend was called to serve at a meditation center, therefore leaving her obligations as a house mate here. It is most unfortunate to see her go, alas she is where she needs to be. I have been left with the choice to take in another roommate or pay the entire rent. Our lease stated that we were to find a replacement if we were to break the lease. No penalties. The original deposit is still in the landlords hands. Originally 300, half mine half hers. I am renting the room under the same agreement - adding a name to the lease, which is on notebook paper and faxed. It is all negotiable, but still my decisions because I desire to live with good people - as do we all! If you are a nice person, and can pay half the rent + half the bills we can work something out on the deposit... point being, i love my former roommate enough to ensure that she gets her original deposit back... fair? So my friend, would you like to see the room - see if this is a place you would like to live? Feel free to give me a call - BMG 666-6666, and we can discuss this further - or not. I apologize if I came across rash, these email things create a mess sometimes!</p><p>\r\nDude:<br>\r\nIs she in sycic, vodoo,ocult,witchcraft,satanism?what kind of meditation center is that though?becouse i am concerned about where and who i share a house with.so that people woun,t be tricking me into another false faith again and then claim they are helping me;when actually,they are entrapping me into a webb of deception.and you know what?all these evil entities call themselves gods.To me,they are god of the flesh.My GOD is the most high,living GOD,of righteousness,of truth and holliness and he never chenges.so would you now tell me the truth.i am interested in truth always.but many people are afraid of the truth.that is why they lie,cheat,decieve,robb,steal,and kill for their father the devil.Like i always say to others;i was before i met them,and i will be always says the LORD who dwells in me.i will b waiting to hear you speak the truth friend.untill then,bye for now.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBMG:<br>\r\nDo you have a microwave?\r\n</p>'),(1877,'<p>Just saw <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0162346\" target=\"movvie\">Ghost World</a>.  Fantastic.</p>\r\n<p>Feeling kinda down for some people.</p>\r\n<p>Going to see BjÃ¶rk in six days.</p>\r\n<p>Had some things affected by circumstances outside my control.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m not going to try and fiqht what I can\'t even touch.</p>\r\n<p>Riding in the subway without music is an excercise in finding the <i>natural</i> rhythms in non-natural processes.</p>\r\n<p>Looking at my clothes & accessories right now and realising that I am currently grayscale.</p>\r\n<p>Fuck a RGB, give me eight bee pee pee.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s all cyclical.  It\'s all cyclical.</p>\r\n'),(1878,'<p>\r\nok i just got back from quebec. i was visiting my father who owns a something or other in knowlton.  its a cash cow and everyone knows it. knowlton is in the eastern townships. it is an area of quebec just east of montreal and west of sherbrooke. it was originally settled by loyalists during american independence. everyone speaks english, or franglais. it is a picturesque town. a kaybek tourist trap. i dont like it there. the trees are nice. the houses are nice, yet there is this attitude in town that makes me a little sad. the image of the town is all that matters, and i know that for most of the residents, the \"thrill\" of knowlton isnt what it is for those who travel there on the weekends to buy arts crafts and over priced coffee and clothing. in any event,,,i must get down to business.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy swinney? why? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntoronto is the home of the person with the second most posts on this site, yet you refuse to add it to the list of lovely cities. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n'),(1879,'<p>my texas hero comes in several colours, as seen below.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.showerhead.org/hero/11.jpg\" width=\"265\" height=\"213\"> \r\n  <img src=\"http://www.showerhead.org/hero/12b.jpg\" width=\"265\" height=\"213\"><br>\r\n  <img src=\"http://www.showerhead.org/hero/10.jpg\" width=\"265\" height=\"213\"> <img src=\"http://www.showerhead.org/hero/14.jpg\" width=\"265\" height=\"213\"> \r\n</p>\r\n<p>maybe one day we\'ll meet again when the <a href=\"http://www.showerhead.org/hero/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">fighting</a> \r\n  stops. maybe in two weeks we\'ll have lunch. maybe a bean pie.</p>'),(1880,'<p>Normally I don\'t do this, normally I don\'t annoy people with book recomendations (as I hate it when people suggest books for me to read) but Dave Eggers\' \"A Heartbreaking Work of Staggering Genius\"...  Yes.  Inspirational.  Heâ€™s me, he\'s my friends.  I feel like I could have written the same book had I lived the same life, many of my friends identify with him too.  It\'s one of those very few books I will read over and over again for some time to come, as if it were the bible, kind of like \"Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance\" -but actually much more so, as it has more real life application.  Mostly it\'s the thought process that I identify with.  It shows me that I am not alone in the way I think, and tells me that it\'s okay to do and say what I want to, what I need to do. I would recomend this book to just about anybody.\r\n\r\n</p> '),(1881,'<p>\r\nMy father died at age 40.<br>\r\nMy sister is age 40.<br>\r\nMy sister was just diagnosed with ovarian cancer.<br>\r\nI do not want my sister to die at age 40.<br>\r\n</p>'),(1882,'<p>\r\ni canoed down the humber river towards the QEW. the onramp was a bit tricky as the salmon farm sprung a leak. fish flipping and flopping on all sides. I wasn\'t taking on water, but dinner wouldn\'t be a problem. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\narrived at zoli\'s around seven. it isnt normally hard to find portage parking in toronto, but on the night of the toronto maple leafs home opener, things were bleak.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe esplanade is a pretty yuppy street. parking was ample, but it was only four blocks away from the expensive \"closer\" parking. zoli lives across the road from a strip of very yuppy pubs. wednesday isn\'t that busy of a night, but like i said, on the night of the toronto maple leafs\' home opener, a lot of very yuppy men and women were taking a lot of very yuppy men and women to dinner before the first drop of the puck. j-stroking my way up church i came face to face with a traffic jam. a large bus was blocking the whole right lane with its hazards on. thinking quickly i decided to paddle like hell up left lane into a group of oncoming taxi kayak. i dont normally fuck with these paddlers but i NEEDED parking. deaking(no pun intended to it being the home opener between the toronto maple leafs and the ottawa senators) the salmon and taxi kayak i was safely back in the right lane just infront of the bus. it was as if moses had parted the parking seas. infront of the bus were three glorious spots. parking and attaching the paddle clubâ„¢ i felt so triumphant. it was if i was general wolfe on the plains of abraham when he defeated the wounded french troops on that faitful day back whenever it happened. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith everything working out, i knew it would be a good night. and as far as i can tell, it will be. tah tah from the hockey night in canada capital of the world, toronto, ontario, canada. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1883,'newspapers thrown<br> \r\ncarelessly<br> \r\nthe man tired <br> \r\njust wants to get home <br> \r\na side of the building <br> \r\nthey are piled <br> \r\nno one notices <br> \r\nthe plastic wrapped bag <br> \r\nthis is our legacy <br> \r\nas the the world grinds down <br> \r\n<br> \r\na man <br> \r\nsells another man\'s <br> \r\nbones on e-bay <br> \r\nto his descendants <br> \r\nfor a \'fair price\' only <br> \r\n<br> \r\nanother doesn\'t speak <br> \r\nthe same language <br> \r\nover kitchen dishes <br> \r\nemptied trash <br> \r\n<br> \r\nlet go <br> \r\nlet go <br> \r\nthey tell me <br> \r\nand still i cannot <br> \r\nbut love this world <br> \r\nin these cigarette ends of days <br> \r\n<br> \r\npaycheck to paycheck <br> \r\nthe clock is ticking <br> \r\nhand to mouth <br> \r\nand hospital visit away <br> \r\nwhere will we live when <br> \r\nwe get old? <br> '),(1884,'<p>\r\nrentasloth is not a goth.<br>\r\nsimon wears a pinky ring witha diamon<br>\r\nlulu is the best, she aces the test<br>\r\ni think she\'s neat and doesn\'t smell like feet.<br>\r\nif she sucks a big fat donkey dick....her butt, austin swinney, surely will pick<br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1885,'<p>\r\ni went to lunch with my boss today. i call her bk. jessica calls her bk broiler and when i come over to her house after work, she says, drink it off, drink off that bk broiler bullshit. bk invited me out because her regular lunch pals had already left and she wanted to show off her new car. a 2002 bmw 325i. there was some woodgrain in that shit and 5 speeds and a perfect, sparse dash that did just about everything you could ever want it to do and more. she yelled at me over some folk/country singer she had blaring on her exquisite stereo system. i turned it down for a second to see what she was yelling about. she was yelling about how great and loud the stereo was.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe took me to this place called cookoo\'s for a salad. i was worried she might yell at me about work. earlier today she freaked because i told her we could probably put an extra full page ad right hand page far forward then found out later when our ad production person returned from vacation that it was impossible. but she didn\'t want to talk about work. she wanted to tell me stories. and make sure i was eating well. i guess that\'s nice of her. her mom-like behavior worries me a bit. i already have one mom who lives nearby.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe were talking about duchamp for some reason. she was eating potato salad. i stared at the mayonaisse dripping off of it and couldn\'t finish my baby spinach salad. she told me that she had her daughter during the second half of her senior year in college. she turned the baby in for her sculpture final. i thought about quitting today but this weird little lunch made me feel the tiniest bit better for some reason.\r\n</p>'),(1886,'<p>The grief in me is bigger than a breadbox<br>\r\nsmaller than the sky<br>\r\nmessier than the next room<br>\r\nas wide as it is high</p>\r\n\r\n<p>coarser than a curbstone<br>\r\nbroader than the bay<br>\r\nsmall enough to swallow back down<br>\r\nwith nothing more to say.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>A pall that arrived like winter<br>\r\nminus the wind and the rain<br>\r\nwith nothing to redeem it<br>\r\nbut a gray and restless pain.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>This is a polite, rhyming poem<br>\r\nbecause everything\'s coming apart--<br>\r\nfiles flying down Murray Street<br>\r\nby an engine bigger than a bagel cart.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Shutter me up and do me in<br>\r\nthere\'s nothing here to see.<br>\r\nPeople are falling from the sky<br>\r\nand onto my TV.</P>'),(1887,'<P>today i have been struck by the urge to call up all of my friends--who all (mostly) live far away from me--and say:</p>\r\n\r\n<P>please come drinking with me tonight.  we will get drunk on vodkas and frozen margaritas and gin and tonic with a lot of lime.  we will cavort to the bar sounds of matches striking matchbooks to light cigarettes, and jukeboxes changing records.  we will laugh and tell jokes that aren\'t funny and dance even though there isn\'t a dance floor.  we will all smoke too many cigarettes, rehydrate ourselves with tall, frosty glasses of ice water between drinks, and stumble home with our arms interlocked, singing silly songs.  when we are all tired and sleepy, but still quite drunk, we will watch <i>10 things i hate about you</i> and <i>reality bites</i> and <i>the breakfast club</i> and recite the lines along with the characters.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>then we will fall asleep, everyone on my bed, curled up like wolves. when we wake up, we\'ll take turns taking showers, someone will make coffee, someone else will make fresh-squeezed orange juice; we\'ll relax until night falls...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>...and then we will go do it all again.</p>'),(1888,'<P> he is a delinquent. an evil theif. stealing me away to thoughts of <I>betrayal...betrayal....betrayal...</I></P>\r\n\r\n<P> \'we are to young to think that\' i suppose i say to him , those restless nights of being dressed in skin not yours. of cold inside and in between where no one sees what is really in your mind.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> i want philosophical speeches made, and midnight adventures into aimless sidewalk sauntering. needing the breath of anticipation on my neck at ever step.i need those vodka nights, of painting on walls abstract images of the virgin mary, mother and child, giver and taker.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> after someone has intentionally hurt me there is no forgetting. forgiveness and forget are 2 distinct substances. i forgive everyone but never forget. the capacity to hurt is existant, has become a piece of all the pebbles that hold you together, has and is in your record. my trust leaves.a small needles sixed hole in the side of continental plate. slowly ocean water leaks in,out</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and then there is iceland.then there is that there are a million people out there and i have to meet them all. happiness is for the dull. manic joy is instant death. when we wake and see the trail behind us and choose to ignore it.</P>'),(1889,'<p>\r\nI am a bit confused by the copyright symbol at the bottom of the page.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndoes swinney.org make a profit? is it possible for someone to make a profit from swinney.org? is swinney.org registered with a copyrighting agency? is \"swinney loves you\" a registered slogan?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni love swinney.org and everything it stands for, but i just dont see why there is a copyright. at no time have i waved the ownership of my writing and given those rights to swinney.org.  at no time has swinney.org asked me for the rights to my work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nputting the copyright symbol seems to me a bit cliche. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif there is a reason, is it the ambition of swinney.org operatives to use legal action in the future, if and when one of its writers makes money from writing? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just dont understand the concept of copyrighting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nplease unconfuse me.\r\n</p>'),(1890,'<p>\r\n<b>schools attended k-12 (7)</b>\r\n<br>k-.5 dhahran academy\r\n<br>.5-2.5 hail girls academy\r\n<br>2.5-3 some school in riyadh\r\n<br>3 oregon episcopal school\r\n<br>4-5 champion elementary\r\n<br>6-9 parents cooperative school\r\n<br>10-12 the catlin gable school\r\n<p><b>countries visited (in my memory) (15)</b>\r\n<br>china, england, ireland, norway, sweden, denmark, egypt, holland, luxembourg,\r\nfrance, switzerland, scotland, canada, saudi arabia, united arab emirates\r\n<p><b>1st cousins on mother\'s side (23)</b>\r\n<br>houda, rida, abdullah, abdulrahman, ghazi, liela, adnan, ghazi, nadine,\r\nsummer, walied, saied, loujayne, imamah, hiba, ahmed, obied, ala\'a, soha,\r\nnoura, thorya, mahmoud, sara\r\n<p><b>states visited (10)</b>\r\n<br>washington, oregon, california, hawaii, arizona, new york, new hampshire,\r\nmassachusettes, vermont, pensilvania, district of columbia\r\n<p><b>things i do when i\'m bored (1)</b>\r\n<br>make lists'),(1891,'um. so not to sound arty and pretencious and calling out for attention, but i seem to have this problem where i just.. suck. i quit everything i start.. school, jobs (i\'ve had 3 jobs in the last month.. one which i still have, the other 2 which i\'m either quitting or have quit) im supposed to be going travelling but im quitting on that too. its not really travelling.. its more like 9 crusty punks in a van for 6 weeks. anywho. quitting quitting quitting. and i really wish i could just sit at home online and cuddle in my bed with my sick and dying dog and not have to think about life. why am i so fucking immature? someone please reach out and let me know!'),(1892,'<P>\r\naustin/swinnmeister:</P><P>\r\nyou went away to oakland</P><P>	\r\nyes, you went out of town,</P><P>\r\ni worked on the r75 you bought,</P><P>\r\nand swilled your last beers down!!!</P>\r\n'),(1893,'<P>i have this thing about mexican food that isn\'t really mexican food.  i mean, i get these intense cravings.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and i know it\'s thoroughly americanized--completely and totally inauthentic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>still, though, there is (almost) no meal more satisfying than an oversized flour tortilla filled with sour cream, black beans, and pico de gallo with lots of cilantro.  a nice frozen margarita with extra limes wouldn\'t be unwelcome as beverage.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>this could be every meal every day for at least a couple of months before i even <i>began</i> to tire of it.</p>\r\n\r\n<P> and the only reason i am even thinking these thoughts right now is because instead of going to <a href=\"http://www.menus.com/menus.com/modular_templ/menu_page.asp?menu_id=81098&rest_id=2127518&link_id=3001\">Burritoville</a> after work today like i wanted to, i pretended to be a grownup, and went to Gristedes and bought cat food and vegetables and an Amy\'s Organic burrito with beans and rice.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it. just. does. not. compare.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>(my cat--who has taken to digging through my laundry bag and dropping dirty socks into her water bowl when she is hungry--is finishing the last half of abovementioned burrito. she\'s enjoying it more than i ever will.)</p>'),(1894,'<P>My shadow is not dry yet.\r\n<P>If I stop stirring my feeling with a rusted nail\r\n<P>Tonight might pass faster and I could dream of\r\n<P>Bikinis and bullets. \r\n\r\n<P>At night I randomly open the dictionary. It is on a desk by my window. I move the curtain so that there is some room for the morningâ€™s sunlight to fall upon it. I wake up and look at the first word I see illuminated.\r\n\r\n<P>Today it was Hope.\r\n\r\n<P>I feel like I have access to the divine this way. \r\n'),(1895,''),(1898,'<P><span style=\"backgroung-color:pink\">oh simon oh simon, you excite my hymen<br>\r\nif you were a grapefruit, i\'d slice you open<br>\r\nand squeeze you over my chicken ramen.<br>\r\nif you were a donkey all dressed in maroon<br>\r\ni\'d ride you like a drunken whore and laugh like a baboon.<br>\r\noh simon oh simon, you\'re the fun at my park<br>\r\nand when you talk, you sound like a naughty lark<br>\r\nmost days i\'m no good to even an elephant<br>\r\nbut when i see you, <br>\r\ni can\'t help being nothing but a lousy sycophant.\r\n</SPAN></P>'),(1899,'mark has been in kuwait for 46 days.  only 44 more to go.  it has been much harder than i expected, this being alone without him, even with email.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy friend <a href=\"http://auntie.diaryland.com/twentythree.html\">jane</a> lives here in town and she takes me places i need to go because i don\'t drive, but a friend is not the same as a lover or a husband or a husband/lover.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni find myself unable to function on the most basic levels.  i don\'t know how to feed myself.  i don\'t get the bills paid on time. i forget to take the trashcan out to the street.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni stopped watching teevee because there is nobody in the other chair.  in fact, i stopped going into the living room at all.  nowadays i stay in the office in front of the machine more than ever before.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni pick up the newspaper every morning and throw it into the trash, plastic wrapper intact.  i don\'t want to know what is happening in the places near the iraqi border where mark is currently being miserable.<br><br>\r\n\r\ntoday i loaded the dishwasher and tossed the empty boxes that have been sitting by the door for three weeks.  i was strangely elated by my productivity... <br><br>\r\n\r\nbut i\'m not depressed, really, i am pretty happy on a general level, i am just lonely.  very lonely, and i want mark to come home and i don\'t want to wait 44 more days for that to happen, but i don\'t feel angry, or even resentful, just waiting, waiting, waiting and watching the calendar and maybe is should change the filter on the air conditioner since he told me to change it every two weeks and i haven\'t done it since he left....<br><br>'),(1900,'<P> there are 2 different types of women....Le Tigre slash Bikini Kill etc versus the velure covered lacey gals that enjoy the tunes of Hole. \r\nWell, that is how I see it. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> My friend cari is somewhere in between. She holds a neutral stance and is quite beautiful about it. its very melt banana.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> tall brown hair lanky small chested innocent seeming. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> kinda like leslie burke in that book bridge to terabithia.remember?</P>\r\n\r\n<P> why is it always us small scrawney chicks longing for meat? because we arent for the whole anorexic model look...hmmm.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1901,'Itâ€™s been raining all day long.  Itâ€™s been raining for 4 years.  Ok, so this is an equivocal moment (has there ever been another type of moment for me?) in my life and I sense a decision being made soon.  That\'s how I am, I am such an indecisive person that I can see decions coming from miles away.  What I do know is that it is finally time to do something \'real\' with my life.  The three things I have always wanted in my life simply put are:<br> \r\n 1. To travel and adventure and help the world<br>  \r\n 2. To create works of art, be it through whatever medium, writing, photography, video, etc. <br> \r\n 3. To love. <br> \r\n And in that order of importance.  I have never had a question that this is what I need to do to be satisfied and happy.  What I donâ€™t know is how to go about doing it.  I have found love, for the second time in my life, and for the second time I am probably going to let it go due to timing and circumstance. <p> \r\n\r\nMarisol tells me my dreams are childish and immature.  Susan says they\'re beautiful and that I should never compromise them.  Perhaps they are, but does it matter if I never follow through?  They\'re do-able aren\'t they?  Yes they are I think, some of them, enough of them to satisfy me. <p> \r\n \r\nSo I ask myself am I getting closer to doing any of this.  No.  From the movie \"American History X\" I picked up the question \'has anything I done made my life better?\'  And from a stale fortune cookie I ate last week I get the retort \'sometimes it is better simply to do than to ask what to do\' (or something like that).  So what have I learned?  Well, Iâ€™ve learned that true love is when you can meld your psyche so completely with someone that you can\'t tell where your mind begins and where her\'s ends.  Iâ€™ve learned that that isn\'t enough, not right now.  Iâ€™ve learned that Iâ€™ve had a pretty easy life with allot of opportunity that I haven\'t taken complete advantage of and with good people that care for me and that I can rely on to be there, always and without condition.  I have learned what spectacular sex is.  I have learned to carpe fucking diem!  I have leaned that 2+2 does not equal 4, not ever (but then Iâ€™ve always known that).  Iâ€™ve learned that 2+3 always equals 5, ALWAYS.  I have learned what it is and why it\'s important to be a MAN, not just for you yourself, but for others and the world.  I have learned that I am not one, but that realizing that is a step towards becoming one.  Most importantly I have learned that the 2 most important things, period, are living and giving and that everything else will follow if you master them. \r\n \r\n<p>but oh, SUSAN!\r\n'),(1902,'<p>\r\ni went to <a href=\"http://www.cellspace.org\">cellspace</a> to see the bindlestiff family cirkus. \r\n</p><p>\r\non the way there, peter and i stopped and gathered up leah, who has very nice eyes and is gentle and comforting. she wore a fuzzy sweater and a fuzzy scarf and i don\'t think i hugged her near enough. i offered her sips of my earl grey tea.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<tt>(i was never interested in tea until i learned about it at burning man. every morning i would wake up, walk a few feet through the duststorm to our camp, and sit there and squint and eat froot loop brand cereal as the dust blew on me. eric made me tea every single morning. i put my froot loop flavored soy milk in it.)</tt>\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe also stopped and rang sonic\'s bell and tried to get her to come along with us, but she only went as far as the corner store where she picked up some smokes. \r\n</p><p>\r\noutside, it was crisp in that subtle sf pseudo-autumn way. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe circus is hard to tell about. it was a circus, but not for kids. i can\'t explain to you what it was like, but i\'ll tell you, i moaned and groaned and squeaked and was very happy and pleased to be there. the show contained the following: sword swallowing, trapeze, magic and comedy, plate spinning, accordian playing, daredevil things, disturbing and bizarre things, and a marching band with mostly naked majorettes and dreadlocked trumpet players.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the audience, sf was sf. people i knew were there, like xep and rachel and my megaphone brother ross. there were also people that i don\'t know but wish i did. the plant trees guy was there, too, but i know as much about him as i need to. \r\n</p><p>\r\n<tt>\r\n(the plant trees guy is a tall leo named scott who gives out redwood saplings at sf events. he wears clothes that have the words PLANT NATIVE TREES stencil painted on them, and he gives out little flyers about planting trees. he also dumpster dives and gathers up his findings in his big white van, then redistributes  his booty to willing recepients. he\'s a fixture, and i like seeing him even though he can be kind of hard to deal with)</tt>\r\n</p><p>\r\nas we were milling about after the show, xep pointed something out to me. flea was in the audience! FLEA from the red hot chili peppers. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnow, i am the type of person who is generally happy to leave poor overstimulated famous people alone when i see them.\r\nbut, i think everyone has some band or movie star that they were really obsessed with at some point in their life, and red hot chili peppers was that for me. they were my favorite band when i was 14/15, and i had every album, posters, documentaries, taped MTV video music awards appearances, the rolling stone with them naked on the cover, everything. i was <i>really into them</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso,  when xep told me that flea was milling around and pointed him out, i figured i would not bother him with my fangirl stupidity. but, i stood there getting excited about him being there and decided i just needed to do it.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni went up to flea, apologized for bugging him, asked for a hug, received and returned a hug, thanked the man profusely and ran away again. i spent the next few minutes trying not to spaz out in a teenybopper way. \r\n</p><p>\r\nafter we left, leah and peter and i walked towards sparky\'s, talking about the performers we had crushes on in our youth. we talked about eddie vedder. i felt sort of old. flea looked sort of old. and jim called me today from nordstrom\'s to tell me that there was a muzak version of Under the Bridge playing over the speakers there. i guess it has been ten years since that song came out.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsparky\'s offered us its usual: diner food and wacky waitstaff. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnow it is late and i am home. i\'m glad i went out. i felt inspired by the circus and glad to see some people i knew. it was good. things are good. ok. nite.\r\n</p>'),(1896,'<p>It\'s been raining all day (as far as I can tell).  It has been raining since I woke up at 9:00am this morning.  Lightly and then more heavy.  It\'s coming down fairly consistently strong at the moment.</p>\r\n<p>After I woke up I worked on my computer graphics assignment for a bit and then, after a sufficient enough amount of work to hit a rather large brick wall, I decided that it was a good time to shower and head out the door.</p>\r\n<p>I walked through the light rain and had some geographical deja-vu when I walked through an intersection that reminded me of one not to far from my parents home.  That made me think of the majority of my friends either in or en route to where we\'re all from.  That, in turn, made me long for a visit to the home-front (no <b>Turkey Day</b> with the family for me this year.  That\'s a first).</p>\r\n<p>I went to the CIBC, deposited some money into Jesus\' account for his Bj&ouml;rk tickets that I sold and then proceeded to walk not to far down Eglinton St, to work, where I stopped in for a few large cups of coffee, a nice grilled mediterranian vegetable pita and a large cup of fresh-squeezed strawberry bannanna oragne juice.  I also took that opportunity to do some research from <u>Interactive Computer Graphics w/ OpenGL</u> and the <u>OpenGL SuperBible</u> for the assignment I was taking a break from.  A slight intermission to deposit a pay chque into my bank near work and I had killed roughly two hours.  Not bad, I got a lot done, including discovering some good routines which made me run home to try them out with the code in my assignment.</p>\r\n<p>After coding for the remainder of the afternoon and into the evening, I took leave of the assignment (leaving myself a large enough problem to deal with tomorrow) in order to accomplish food ingestion and laundry completion.</p>\r\n<p>Now fed and clean-clothed, I\'m just relaxing with some green tea before I go to sleep.  It\'s raining outside and I feel nice and warm.  My desktop and Aphex Twin\'s <a href=\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=1370958979/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/artistid=APHEX+TWIN/itemid=324367\" target=\"music\">Selected Ambient Works Volume II</a> perfectly suit my current mood.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m making my own comfort bubble.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1896_1.jpg\"></p>\r\n\r\n'),(1897,'<pre>\r\nthis strange plan is random at best\r\nthis strange how much more can i take\r\nthis strange change in atmosphere\r\nand in gravity too and its severity\r\nthis strange day is almost over\r\njust started to get sick of it\r\n\r\nand it\'s strange but nothing\'s all that strange\r\nyeah it\'s strange but what\'s so strange about that\r\nyeah it\'s strange but what isn\'t strange\r\nyeah it\'s strange but oh well\r\n\r\nthis strange plan is random at best\r\nthis strange how much more can i take\r\nthis strange sound you said i said\r\nyou\'re not listening, or i\'m not saying it right\r\nthis strange war of promises\r\nlet\'s call us a truce, we\'ll call it the truth\r\n\r\nand it\'s strange but nothing\'s all that strange\r\nyeah it\'s strange but what\'s so strange about that\r\nyeah it\'s strange but what isn\'t strange\r\nyeah it\'s strange but oh well\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n- <a href=\"http://www.builttospill.com/\">built to spill</a>\r\n</blockquote>'),(1903,'<P> It started with that half awake but not quite ready to open my eyes feeling.  I could feel the cool air around me in contrast to the blankets over me, and it was one of those perfect, very comfortable mornings.  Except, something has been missing for a very long time. </P>  \r\n\r\n<P> There was a time when I thought I had found a friend, companion, someone to talk to... and it was a good feeling; aside from the fact that I felt strangely attracted, which was nice, too.  It was different than any other attraction.  It left me on the edge. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> And then suddenly, we did not exist to each other.  Nothing bad ever happened, just nothing monumental either.  So we were just like friends who had lost touch, except every once in a while the thought crept back into my mind. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Then the other day we both happened to be online at the same time and it was like nothing changed.  The next night we hung out, and the night after that.  To me, everything feels the same.  Except I am unsure, because I\'ve never really felt like that before.  I\'ve never been THIS unsure about anything before.  I\'ve never been faced with someone as unreadable as I try to be.  I don\'t know what to do and I don\'t know what to say. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> So, this morning I woke up with the thought of this person, and I just don\'t know if I want the same disappearing act to take place again; but I don\'t know how to stop it... </P>'),(1904,'<P> i thought we would be longer together</P>\r\n\r\n<P> now, having to unmemorize all love</P>\r\n\r\n<P> it falls like i do, shards of cold beating rain</P>\r\n\r\n<P> we barely speak as he drives me home.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I will never wait for your phone call</P>\r\n\r\n<P> or wait for you at home</P>\r\n\r\n<P> This living is the in betweens of sleep</P>\r\n\r\n<P> The smell that lies inside the sheets</P>\r\n\r\n<P> reminds me of our happiness and what it used to be</P>\r\n\r\n<P> we both fell short, you leaving me...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> with no one there to catch me.</P>'),(1905,'<h3>Another Five Things I am Good At</h3>\r\n<p>\r\n<ol><li>Worrying.</li>\r\n<li>Writing music.</li>\r\n<li>Jumping up onto things.</li>\r\n<li>Coding sysadmin perl.</li>\r\n<li>Being myself, regardless of what people think.</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat are <b><u>you</u></b> good at?\r\n<p>\r\nIn other news, just finished \"<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0862418542/qid=1002534319/sr=2-1/ref=sr_8_7_1/102-9107817-4843336\">People Funny Boy</a>\" by David Katz. David Katz sure does know how to write. Lee Perry sure is interesting. 500 pages or so, and it\'s not overrated like <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/search-handle-url/index=books&field-author=Stephenson%2C%20Neal/102-9107817-4843336\">Neal Stephenson</a>.\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin'),(1906,'Its good to meet new people.  Sometimes when you reach out and reaquaint yourself with someone you thought you sort of knew, your social time swells, your weekend filled with sparkly glitter and vibe on some sort of twisted magic.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAlthough in a half-sober, only half-serious state, I promised <i>not</i> to babble about it here, I find myself doing just that. Some stories are just too good to not share, some moments just bring too much of a selfish smile when put into text, so here I find myself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI want to live in NYC.  I\'ve been whining that lament for months, and only yet have finally gotten aligned enough to actually feel out the place. Its sparkly, for sure. It sucks you in. I\'ve never experienced a place that had that effect on me.  Ok, maybe City Centre does, as I always associate the airport with leaving town on adventures, but straight away.<br><br>  \r\n\r\nI had to cut my visit short and come home, due to worries from family, worries that I couldn\'t catch a plane back due to the militarty activities of late.  If it were up to me, I\'d have just stayed south of my chilly hometown ville.  I regret not staying longer (I will return shortly), especially long enough to do nice things like step aside from my typical hedonism and see the city during the day, return calls of NYC pseudo-friends that I accidentally overlooked, figuring I\'d call later in the week (I\'ll return shortly, I said!). O poor me, procrastinator who always pays.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut I got to taste the Sparkly Glitter, the erotic trance of good drugs and new flesh, the grind of the house scene, doing sordid things in the DJ booth.  I also forged a deep bond with someone I just realized that I\'ve only heretofore known as deeply as a surface scratch.  I also realised that I have a special vacuum in my heart for individuals that spin records well, love well, and know a little about chemistry.  My needs are simple, and I\'m only now realising how to fill them again. What a weekend. How sparkly and glittery, how much I love being alive again.<br><br>\r\n'),(1907,'<p>The smell of this new girl lingers in my sheets.  It gives me wet, squishy thoughts.  I noticed this last night as I fell asleep, alone, without her body pressed against mine.</p><p> Lauren says I should keep her - <i>I like her, she\'s totally cool</i> - but I haven\'t decided what I\'m going to do with her... and as I speak those words, I realize that the decision has already been made, largely without my input.  My body and emotion know what they want and don\'t give a fuck what I think.  I like to pretend the question is still open though.  She looks at me with a purity and intensity of emotion that... would be a pity to waste.  She\'s only 19.  I don\'t want to be the one who jades her youthful innocence.  And of course there\'s the circumstances in which we met.</p><p>Friday night at a dance club, I watched Deborah grab a boy by the hand and drag him into the bathroom, into a stall with her.  I didn\'t stick around to watch what the too many feet were going to do.  Instead, I went to the bar and asked the bartender for a ridiculously strong drink.  She set a glass of Wild Turkey in front of me with an ice cube floating in it.</p><p>Drunk now, looking for Deborah, drowning myself in the music, lights, sweat, bodies.  Someone\'s hands are gliding down my chest.  <i>You\'re so beautiful </i>is murmured in my ear.  I can see the hunger in her eyes.  She\'s not the one; she isn\'t even attractive.  My mouth is on hers.  <i>Where is Deborah?</i>  I\'ll let this person take me home with her.  I want someone to say nice things to me, to wrap their arms around me.  I\'m willing to buy that with sex.  <i>Where the fuck is Deborah?</i>  I push away from her mumbling, \"I\'ll be right back.\"  Scouring the club for the one I want, and she\'s gone without a trace.  Back on the dance floor.  Tonight I\'m a slut, a dirty whore, an easy lay.  (How many girls did I make out with that night?)  I <i>need</i> to be with someone tonight.  <i>Wow, you\'re so cool.  I can\'t believe I met you,</i> she laughs throwing her head back with a wide, beautiful smile.  I can see the innocent joy in her eyes.  So... you\'re the one; damn, you\'re fucking hot.</p><p> We went to a diner after the club.  While she was in the bathroom, the waiter came up to me and said I was the luckiest guy in the place.  I was going home with a model.</p><p>Deborah is like cocaine.  I want her even though she\'s so bad for me, but I won\'t be calling her anymore.  I\'m not that much of an emotional masochist.  Besides, I suddenly find myself with someone new, a 5\'11\" beauty who looks at me with adoration in her eyes.  I feel a twinge of guilt though.  Am I just using her?  Like I said, I don\'t want to be the one who jades her youthful innocence.</p>'),(1908,'<p>\r\nsuprised, but not that surprised by the loud commotion, as a young child i awoke into the faint morning light.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSLAM!  door knob meets wall.  \"you can\'t do anything right.\" \"what do you expect me to do?\"  \"get a job like everyone else.\"  \"its not that simple.\"  \"nothing\'s that simple for you.\"  \"i can\'t take this anymore!\"\r\nFOOTSTEPS, door opens, door shuts, footsteps, garage door opens, garage door closes, gunshot BANG!.  \"oh shit, what has he done now!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni wrote this in response to an email i recieved concerning \"sheer joy\".  a previous post i had made.  the email was from a friend who said that she wished her childhood memories were as warm.  parents make many sacrifices to insure their children\'s happiness. often though,  they are incapable of the most important thing. simply getting along.   \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni would like to state that my father did not commit suicide.  i will never know why he went into the basement and fired a gun.\r\n</p>'),(1909,'<p>For the first time in my life, I have found a job that is perfect for me. ME! egg. Diviner of the impractical. Scorner of institution. An Artist. A person who makes a mockery out of the American way for a living. Somoene who would be the first to die if electricity and other modern accoutrements were cut off from her way of life due to natural disaster or warfare because she has no real survival skills.</p>\r\n<p>The only thing standing in my way is the application process. You see, if youve been on this planet for a while, you know that the only way to land a job that requires any sort of competition at all is to know someone who is proximate to that department and  have them accidentally delete your competition from consideration. I have searched under every barrel in my vineyard of aquaintances to find someone in or related to this institution, to no avail.</p>\r\n<p>I would resort to pushy behavoir if I could think of a viable pushy course of action. On the application page of their web site, they posted the text in bold, \"<b>NO PHONE CALLS, PLEASE</b>\". I took that message at face value, but after speaking to various men in my life was convinced that the message was only a \"filter for people who dont really want the job\" or \"If you think that note was addressed to you, IT IS. meaning, if you really want that job, CALL THEM. They are practically DARING you to call them.\"</p>\r\n<p>I have trouble resisting dares. Today I tried to call. There was only one number I could get a hold of, and the receptionist gently informed me that noone on her watch was taking any sort of calls from prospective applicants, and all I can really do is sit tight and wait for them to use my resume as toilet paper.</p>\r\n<p>I have such trouble with being required to muster this particular brand of assertiveness. You see, my natal chart locates me as Mars in Gemini, which the astrologer Coral Cadman calls \"a crippled masculinity.\" You see, Gemini is the only sign in the zodiac that is not assigned a gender. the twins. Equal grounding. </p>\r\n<p>Mars is the warrior, the ruler of aggression and the masculine pole of sexuality. Mars tells you how well you might do with throwing someone down on the bed and forcing their genitals to your will. Because I have mars in gemini, I am supposedly neither a champion of combat nor a ninny pillow-biter. I am both. A strategist. A rational voice. A thinker. A warrior when my analytical resources have concluded that it is time to act. </p>\r\n<p>When it comes to sex,  I would probably sum up my combative style to the song \"Should I stay or should I go?\" Consistent indecision, peppered by the will of the other. Does he want me to throw him down? It is the same with this job-stalking nonsense. If I am given a hint of encouragement, I\'ll babble in their ears. But without any invitation to charge, Ill sit here filing me nails until kingdom come.</p>\r\n<p>I want to rise to this occasion like the proverbial Phallus of power, hard, throbbing, and full of hot blood! I want to seize and topple any obstacle in my way with the force of a burning horn of steel skewering flora, fauna, and enemies in my path. Why couldnt I just be Mars in Aries for a day? I wish I were dead.</p>'),(1910,'<P>i hate being in trouble and the law has been on my back since i rode out of Texas two years ago.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>it all started in when i bought a spanking new honda civic in february of 2000 with money from my ibooks.com job and a loan from the honda finance corporation of irving texas.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the first thing i did was go on a road trip to montana with BMG.  outside san angelo, BMG\'s old home town where he used to get speeding tickets, i got my first speeding ticket ever, 83 in a 65.  right after the cop left, BMG said, \"i knew you were going to get a ticket there.\"  i inquired as to why he didnt say something and he shrugged.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then i got a ticket for doing 82 down the side of a mountain just over the Montana boarder into Wyoming and headed back to Texas.  that was a cheap shot, it was a good 14% incline.  of course i was speeding.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then leaving california for texas after diving out for an interview at neopets.com i made a right turn on red at a prohibitive intersection in some aweful place like Rancho Cucamunga.  i had gotten off the highway for an oil change and some lunch.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have had a number of parking tickets in Los Angeles and San Francisco numbering in the double digets.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i never paid any of the tickets.  then when my car was stolen a couple months back, i paid all the parking tickets and the California right on red  ticket in Cucamunga.  i did this out of fear that the police might arrest me on the spot for having the car to steal in the first place.  like, \"we been looking for you, Swinney!\"  and i wasn\'t wanting that to happen.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>oh yea, and my montana drivers license that i got four or five years ago expired two years ago.  also i have had spotty insurance coverage at best.  i hope my dad does not read that, he is an insurance buff.  but i was unemployed for about a year in the last two years, at different times.  i was eating beans out of the can.  i had no home.  i was not paying insurance.  do the math.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>here i am, stable and ready to enter society, do my taxes (have not done that in a while either) , have insurance, have a license in my home state, all that. but i am still a cheap man.  i am cheaper than cheap.  i eat tofu dogs at work every day and never go out with my co workers.  there are few cheaper.   so when i was signing up for auto insurance this guy in capitola near my home, i lied about my driving history.  i said i dont have anything on my record.  spotless.  crystal.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>his parting words to me were, \"well if we just have you come in to the office we can do that driving record check and have you off and rolling.\"\r\n\r\n<P>so i feel busted.  though i have not actually gone in yet and they might now actually find my driving record.  who knows.  i still feel busted.  i hate that.</P>'),(1911,'<p>\r\nspent the last two weeks running away from a two year old. im two years old too, but im so much more mature. i dont think im alone when i say that i dont like to be chased and yelled at by someone. i hid under daves futon for most of the day. id come out when dave was around; that usually meant the coast was clear. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ndaves brother didn\'t pay much attention to me when he was sitting at the desk. id brush up against his leg every so often, but i didn\'t get much attention. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe white box in the food room is a wonder i have not seen in my life. this box is full of food and all i can do is stare at it. im just not tall enough. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni didn\'t get to see them much while they were here. their child was scarier than the vacuum cleaner. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe voices in my tail were back with vengence while i was sniffing the milk crate in the closet. i spun around and around and then fell over from being dizzy. pondering my dillema on the window ledge i came up with an idea. perhaps the voices in my tail are poltergeist. i swear to god my tail knocked over the picture frame. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n'),(1912,'I\'ve recently moved, and I really like where I live.  Not recently have my living quarters taken on such an appeal to me for more than a couple of weeks.  Midtown, West Side, Brooklyn, none of these places <i>worked</i> for me; my Tennessee hometown certainly didn\'t.  No, but I finally found my little niche on the Upper West Side, my neighborhood, a viable substrate on which to survive.  Not to belabor the point, but I <i>really</i> like where I live; there\'s not alot I would change about it.<br><br>\r\nSo, you can imagine how disheartened I was to learn that I was living next door to a <a href=\"http://thebridge.citysearch.com/\">half-way house</a>, a secret that the landlord had conveniently only let spill after the lease was signed.  And its not just <i>any</i> half-way house, but the undisputed crown jewel of such establishments, treating the royal-flush of humanities\' sins: Narcotics Adddiction, AIDS/HIV Treatment, Eating Disorders, VD, and \'Sexual Disorders\'. How apropoe.<br><br>\r\n\"Good fences make good neighbors\", said Robert Frost.  In my case, its a railing.  The <i>only</i> thing that separates my world of domestic bliss from that rag tag realm of narcotic-laced, sexual-disordered addiction is a thin black ornamental metal railing.<br><br>\r\nThe Half-Way-ers have taken quite a liking to sitting on said raling between their \"Stay Clean!\" check-ins.  As this rail is situated on my building\'s property, directly in front of my sidewalk-level window, it seems that I have relatively permanent loiterers, realistically within arm\'s reach.  At first, it was amusing.  How often does one get to hear, from the comfort of their apartment, tales of extreme addiction and the crack wars of 1980\'s NYC, gambling addicitons, tall tales of sordid exploits and Studio 54-esque debaucherous trysts? For a while, it was quite amusing.<br><br>\r\nBut, the newness soon wore off, as these things often go.  As the stories began repeating themselves, the variations becoming more and more grandiose and less and less interesting, the loiterers became more of an annoyance than entertainment, much less their constant clouds of weed and cheap cigarettes.  With a full swing, the halfway hosue kicked into their autumn hours, and my loiterers began occupying my railing from 6am until past midnight, 7 days a week. Then, they spread to my stoop, where they would only move for you to pass after several requests, and even then, with great sluggishness, and the occasional mutter of \"fucker\" or \"asshole\".<br><br>\r\nAs if the ceaseless conversation wasn\'t enough,  frequent public urination, vomiting and spitting quickly began to be a major issue, and our Tennant\'s Council placed several calls to our encroaching neighbor\'s administration, to no avail.  As matters worsened, the police were asked to intervene.  Although they installed some fetching <i>No Loitering!</i> signs, we observed no other effect; the neighbors simply wouldn\'t give in, budge or aquiesce in any way.<br><br>\r\nAbout a week ago, neighborly relations at their breaking point, I observed one of the patrons and usual rail-sitters urinating on my slightly-opened window.  I stormed into the office, informing them of this blasphemy of neighborly tact.  I was told, \"If you don\'t <i>like</i> it, then <b>do</b> something about it yourself\".  Who <i>would</i> like this sort of thing? I am the type of man who takes a hint easily; this character trait is essential to live in this City.<br><br>\r\nAfter a quick stop at Academy Hardware, a flatmate and I found ourselves armed with two carbide-tipped drill bits, an arsenal of cold-forged steel machine-head bolts, and several tubes of high-carbon steel epoxy.  Went to work.  After securing our Super\'s approval, we peppered said railing with 20 1/4\" holes.  Into each, we jammed a 5/16\" bolt, feeling our machismo rise with each bore of the bit, each grinding, steel-biting crank of the socket wrench, each slather of that pricy but necessary epoxy.  In less than an hour, we had secured our stoop (and my window) from future squatters, urinators, spitters, and chain-smokers.<br><br>\r\nOr so we thought.<br><br>\r\nYesterday, I returned from work, tired, ready to relax.  I entered my room and heard words that would set <i>any</i> resident of 246 west 108th street on edge: \"Sheeeit, man, can\'t they feed us <i>anything</i> besides split-pea soup?!\"... the ever-present whine of a disgruntled half-way-er.  Frowning, I raised my blind to find the rear-ends of the usual suspects, perched high on the railing, sitting squarely on top of the almost 2 inch tall bolts, seemingly oblivious to the fact that they were lounging on steel, much less the fact that it was about 40 degrees outside.  I was baffled.  If the discomfort wasn\'t enough, wouldn\'t the plain fact as to why those bolts were there send a clear enough signal?<br><br>\r\nI phoned the administrator\'s office again, and was told, \"Yeah, we\'ll see what we can do.  By the way, nice handywork on the railing.  Our patrons seem to really like it.\"\r\n '),(1913,'<p>\r\nthis morning driving into work in a \r\nslightly roundabout way, i passed oakland\r\nhigh school, which is a few blocks from \r\nthe <a href=\"http://houseness.org\">unspeakable houseness of oakland</a>. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit always strikes me how mean and angry and \r\nscary the kids hanging out around that school\r\nlook. and i have noticed lately that teenagers \r\ni see in general appear so intent on looking badass...it\'s not something i have\r\nseen before, except in movies about street \r\nkids and gangbangers and so on...but these\r\nkids are attending a high school in my \r\nreasonably nice neighborhood...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey saunter purposely into the street against \r\nthe light and then bang on my car as i drive \r\nby (slowly so as not to hit them)...they shout \r\nobscenities and insults at me...they act\r\nin ways i could never have imagined acting\r\nas a teenager--in ways i never saw anyone\r\nact except on tv. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni wonder if this is a widespread phenomenon--\r\ndo kids who now attend the \r\n<a href=\"http://www.mpsaz.org/~dobson/\">big public high school</a> i went to in mesa, \r\narizona look like this too? or is it because oakland high school is more of an \'inner-city school\'? do i live in the inner-city? how \r\ncould i not notice that?</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is so much like one of those \"if it\'s too\r\nloud, you\'re too old\" situations. am i suddenly\r\nafraid of these teenagers because i don\'t \r\nunderstand them anymore? would i have understood\r\nthem when i was a teenager? is it irrational\r\nto assume that because they\'re scowling and dressed like gangbangers, they\'d have guns and \r\nbe violent for no reason? or am i missing some\r\ncultural/fashion phenomenon that says it\'s \r\neven cooler than ever to seem angry and mean?\r\nare they pretending to be this way because \r\nthat\'s what other teenagers want? are big \r\ncorporations marketing clothes/etc this way \r\nfor a reason? how did this happen? should i be \r\nthis concerned? what does this imply about \r\nthe future of these kids and the world in \r\ngeneral? perhaps i am overreacting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni guess the point is that i am always trying\r\nto determine if my perception of things is based\r\nmore on my personal lack of understanding of \r\nthe world and how it\'s changing, or on reality.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand i just don\'t have enough information. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\'t like this.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1914,'<p>\r\ni woke up this morning with really bad cramps. i was hungry, too. delirious with hunger. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni walk out the house -- managed to stop at the bank and deposit some checks before making it to the bagel hut. changed my mind about eating it there or to go four times. ended up ordering it to go and eating it there. blinded by noon sunshine, i waddle home and buy an onion on a whim.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit\'s like wanting to shit and pass painful gas at the same time. you bend over from the intensity. there\'s the low level gurglings in the abdomen. ominious. some wiffle farts that relieve the pressure. some queefs. hmm i think i want to lay down. no, i wanna get up, it\'s too hot/i\'m too thirsty/my ass hurts. i\'m sleepy. zzzzz.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntime for hot baths and girlie magazines. shouts out to all the uteruses out there and of course there is love for the boy parts too. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni think i am sharing too much. am i sharing too much? i think i need to buy www.bloodywedgie.com. okay, i am definitely sharing too much. \r\n</p><p>\r\nhi!</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1915,'<p>Tuesday: Woke up and declared myself incapable of anything worthwhile or productive, so Fern and I drove to Napa and pretended to be dillettantes for an afternoon.  Tasted some things, took a gondola ride up to the ridge at Sterling, and made some frivolous purchases. Left feeling generally happy, as we usually do--tipsy from wine and satisfied with existence. It also always makes us want to eat a good meal afterwards, so I bought some clams and shrimp and made a saffron and shellfish risotto, which we ate eagerly along with Ezra, and drank some Viana Sauvignon Blanc from the fridge, which went nicely. (Note: Make sure to use unsalted butter, and never add salt to this recipe, as it obscures the flavor and there is a saltiness inherent to the stock after the shellfish boils in it.) Following dinner, went to a bar for someone\'s birthday and had a martini, followed by a beer, Stella Artois. Went to bed and dreamt of Lola falling from the fire escape and dying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Wednesday: Paid bills, called Sprint and bitched at them for half an hour for trying to screw me; called Citibank and bitched at them for trying to screw me again, and proceeded to look idly for a job on the internet, even though I don\'t really care to go to work right now, given my mental state, and the fact that any activity besides little creative projects, cooking, and numbing myself seem particularly futile when death by aiborne bacteria could be right around the corner, the entire country shaken and fearful, everyone dealing in his or her own way.  Dragged myself to the gym, against my will, and did twenty minutes of cardio, ten minutes of abs, and spent the rest of the time in the steam room stretching.  Returned home where Ezra had found the recipe book and had begun cooking the lamb for the massman curry.  I completed the cooking, stewed the meat until tender, and added it to the coconut milk and curry-paste mixture, with potato, peanuts, and some roasted cardamom. We ate this while watching a movie, accompanied by a sweet Fetzer Gewurztraminer, purchased from a Webvan close-out sale at Grocery Outlet down the street. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Thursday: All attempts at productivity proved enervating and demoralizing, so I began plans for a coconut layer cake. Such a glorious dessert deserved a glorious meal, and so I researched a boeuf bourguignon recipe, which turned out fabulously, albeit four hours later.  With it we had a potato and cheese souffl&#233; and a 1998 Cline Zinfandel that was quite nice, really the best eight-dollar bottle of red out there I think, and available in many California supermarkets.  This was followed by watching a video and sipping two (maybe three) glasses of bourbon and water, after which I went to bed with a headache.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Friday: Shopping was done for a chicken pasta with sundried tomatoes, but proceeded out to the gym, where I did about twelve sets on chest, and upon arriving home, Ezra suggests we go out to eat, his treat, because I\'ve been so glum, and he has some money to spend.  We go to a French place up the street where I\'ve wanted to go for a long time, and this made me happy. Shared a bottle of 1999 Carignane with our Nieman Ranch steaks, a shoulder cut, apparently, called \"Flat-Iron,\" topped with a Dijon-anchovy butter that we both agreed was a bit overstated and unnecessary. The steaks were fantastically tender, though, and the dinner pleasant. Afterwards, we went to a bar, threw some darts and threw back a few pints of Hefeweizen.  I returned home to surf porn and make an attempt to read, which lasted about three minutes, before I \"passed out,\" as they say.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Saturday: Started the day with some breakfast burritos, containing eggs, kidney beans, jalapeno cheese dip, and salsa. These were eaten with much start-of-the-day relish, and as it was Saturday, there was much buzzing about the old homestead, with a new roommate moving in, a neighbor borrowing and lending tools and offering advice.  An Ativan was consumed around four o\'clock, as the combined impact of the news that day of terrorists sending messages such as \"You will be happy soon\" to one another, and the noise of the hammer drill were enough to send me into a panicked state. Went with Ezra to purchase a television, because our old one broke, and we had movies to watch. Dinner consisted of leftover lamb curry from Thursday, washed down with the cheap Gewurztraminer, followed by Bohemia beer, of which I think i had five or six, before moving on to a bong hit or three. Later in the night noticed, while on the toilet, that the thick-pile threads of the bathroom rug were swimming below me like kelp in the ocean.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sunday: Awoken by call from mom informing us of air raids on Afghanistan, after which I had an Ativan and some coffee, a cookie, and a bagel with a lovely spinach and water chestnut cream cheese spread.  Turned on the TV to watch the Pentagon briefing, and soon the power went out. We assumed terrorist counter-strikes, but the lights were still on up the street. Some friends came over for dinner, and I served a sundried-tomato and chicken pasta with garlic, capers, and almonds.  It turned out well, if a little on the dry side. (Note to self: try a penne or farfalle pasta next time, as these work better than spaghetti in catching small ingredient morsels and being tossed in oil... or perhaps rotini.)  This was served with a 2000 Viansa Prindello, a light, fruity blend I think of Merlot and Montepulciano, but cannot recall exactly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Monday: Made a Thai green curry with pork and discussed Anthrax over dinner.  Washed it down with a 2000 Trefethen Reisling which Louise and I agree is the best accompaniment we\'ve found so far for curry.  The Thai basil and fresh peas worked especially well with the pork, I found, and I managed not to overcook the eggplant this time.  I began dinner with an Ativan and a Manhattan, and finished off with a few bong hits and some more wine. Considered reading, or doing some writing on the computer, but all that managed to be accomplished was a long contemplative shit, followed by a curling up, fetally, on the living room couch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tuesday: Began with an inability to get out of bed, followed by a large mug of coffee, and later by a breakfast of scrambled eggs with cheddar cheese and sourdough toast.  Dinner is shaping up to be either a balsamic chicken breast and rice sort of number, or perhaps the news from the big screen by the stairmaster at the gym will once again deflate me and I\'ll just order Chinese.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Recipes:</b></p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.epicurious.com/run/recipe/view?id=102144\">boeuf bourguignon</a></p>\r\n<p>Find Saffron and Shellfish Risotto, and Coconut Layer Cake in <a href=\"http://www.marthastewart.com/channels/cooking/recipesearch/search.asp\">Martha\'s Recipe Finder.</a></p>\r\n<p>For Thai curries, I recommend <u><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0876543565/qid=1002663193/sr=2-1/ref=sr_8_7_1/002-4545083-2917618\">It Rains Fishes</a></u></p>'),(1916,'<p>when i worked at critical pants they would sometimes cater big meetings for the sales or marketing teams.  at the ends of the meetings, if there was food left, all us techies would scrounge around in the remains and see what we could find.  it was usually cold cuts and crusty mayo and other not so nice trimmings to put on sandwiches.  but it was always at the end of the day when you had forgotten to eat lunch so any food was welcome.  \r\n</p><p>\r\ni would make sandwiches on the little anise seeded sourdough baguettes.  the sandwiches would be made of tiny tiny dill pickles and mustard because those would be the only vegan things left.  they were absolutely digusting but i ate a countless number of them.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nthey were my desperation food.  what is yours?\r\n</p>'),(1917,'Grrrr.  Arrrgggg!  Iâ€™m so angsty, so frustrated, so angry.  I canâ€™t sleep for more than 6 hours every couple of days, Iâ€™m unshaven and unshowered.  I need focus.  I need hope.  I need energy.  I need to get the fuck out of this shitty ass town.  I feel like getting in a fight.  I wanna kick the shit out of some deserving asshole.  Bring it on!  Come on, come and get it!  Heeya!  Hwa!  Hu!  (Followed by a flurry of hands). <p><b> <font color=\"red\">\r\n\r\nNumber one short term goal:  get the mothaâ€™ fuckinâ€™ fuck outa fucksville.</b></font><p>\r\n  \r\nWhatâ€™s a day in the life of Hallett in the city of Buffalo like lately?  Pretty F-in dull.\r\n<p>Get up.  Check the various utilities to see which ones are still working.  Remember just how decedent it is to have hot water available at the turn of a spicket.  Experiment with the idea of taking a freezing cold shower versus heating up water in the microwave and taking a sponge bath.  Skip bathing.  Eat a potato.  Scrounge for change to feed my caffeine addiction.  Meet friends at local coffee shop.  Ride in my friendsâ€™ jeep to a close by mountain bike trail.  Pedal my ass off in the woods for about 5 hours reveling in the smell of the autumn leaves and sweat.  Sleep on the ride home.  Get home and eat another potato.  Talk to essemar on the phone while heâ€™s supposed to be working.  Kick my roommate of my computer, watch Farscape and check my email.  Dread expected phone calls from various ex-girlfriends (there are at least 3 that still call me on a regular basis â€“which is strange because Iâ€™ve only ever had 4).  Play Warlords 3 (a stupid computer game which I hate and donâ€™t know why I continue to play), and get my ass kicked by the computer.  Reflect on what a waste of time that game is and think about all the things I could be, and should be, doing.  Eat yet another potato.  Get online and browse Swinney.   Play more Warlords 3.  Mourn the death of my level 6 Paladin lovingly named â€˜The Clawâ€™.  Listen to the Peter Gunn theme song and concentrate on growing a third eyeball in the middle of my forehead.  Think about what I would get were I to get a tattoo.  Lie in bed trying to sleep.  Ask myself over and over again what it is that Iâ€™m not doing â€“and know that I know the answer and that the only problem is that Iâ€™m not doing it. </p> <i><b>Follow?</b></i>\r\n'),(1918,'<p>\r\nrecently i went into new college on valencia street, in search of an application. it was late in the day and i had not eaten. the minty green building reminded me of a type of mint and chocolate cookie i used to eat when i was a kid. the cookies were called \"grasshoppers\".  i have always admired the mint and brick-red facade of the new college buildings. there\'s one on each side of valencia. \r\n</p><p>\r\ninside, i stood and stared meekly at the racks of pamphlets and viewbooks, in search of an application. i was low blood sugar but not unhappy or annoyed, just a little slower than usual. \r\n</p><p>\r\nscanning the labeled piles of brochures left me no  closer to an application than i had been when i dragged myself out of peter\'s saltysour smelling sheets an hour before. now it was 4pm and i was standing near where there must be paperwork i needed, standing on those red and brown tiles that i usually associate with taquerias or wendy\'s.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni meant to wheel around with purpose and ask the scruffyfriendly buzzcut sleepy looking man behind the counter in the new college cafe. instead of wheeling around with purpose i sort of wobbled in an oval and ended up facing him, and facing a woman who was seated at one of the cheap white plastic tables sipping a drink and talking to the buzzcut man.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni opened my mouth, dazed, to ask the question. <i>i am looking for an application</i> i articulated. <i>are they over here</i> i gestured noncommitally at the rack of papers. the dark haired buzzcut man came from behind the counter and began to look at the rack of brochures with me. <i>i thought they were here</i> he said and poked a thick pink-brown finger into a pristine white envelope full of ink-smelling printed materials.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe rack was dark wood, the kind of cheap wood that is used to make these things, the kind that gets oily and browner after years of hands touching it. there were signs taped above the stacks of brochures, taped right to the wood. years of old yellowed tape bits from years of rearranging these signs were clinging to the rack, sticking out behind new signs, behind new clean cheaper tape.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe signs were made from white paper and had their labels written with black marker: <i>BA weekday</i> and <i>SCHOOL OF LAW</i> and <i>MA WOMEN\'S SPIRITUALITY</i>. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni examined this signage as the buzzcut thick comforting man poked his digit into the envelope. <i>i thought the applications were in the envelopes</i> he poked. he wore a blue and grey striped shirt, thin stripes, and an apron. overall he seemed about as worn in as the wood on the rack, but not as dirty as the wall behind the rack where smudges and nicks and streaks of fingers and furniture had been allowed to stay as long as they wanted.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe man disengaged his scuffed finger from the envelope and put the envelope back on the rack, right back where he found it, and i felt a strange longing to return the envelope to some virginal state by carefully creasing its tab back down to flat. the next person who would come to pick up a viewbook and a xeroxed copy of the catalog, all contained in this envelope, should have the honor of breaking into an untainted package. i blinked the desire away and the man returned to his station behind the counter.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe counter had many of those colorful bottles of torani italian soda flavoring, a row of them with sticky white pumps all facing the same direction and obscuring my view of the casual but serious and gentle buzzcut man as he fumbled behind the counter, as he spoke to me of what he could not find in his probing of that envelope that i had watched him probe.\r\n</p><p>\r\nfacing me, he raised his right arm and held it in a wobbly parallel to the top of  silver espresso machine below it. the thick finger came out again, this time to point me across the street to the official admissions office. as his mouth flopped up and down pink inside, and the woman at the white table combed her unremarkable brown hair with one delicate hand, combed it behind her ear, i understood that where he was directing me would probably be closed now, now at 4:30pm.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhile i engaged in taking verbal instruction from the man and in taking a mild stare from the mild woman at the table, something else happened.\r\n</p><p>\r\nout on the street cars went by, and somewhere in south bay, peter typed on a keyboard, and somewhere in peter\'s apartment, seth padded down the hallway barefoot. and in the new college lobby i was hungry, and a man came downstairs holding a cat.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hadn\'t noticed the staircase, really, because i had been trying to limit my already-weakened focus to the rack, the envelope, the man, the woman, the counter of torani. my brain had subtly noted the hall beyond the rack of brochures. in my memory i want the hall to be minty like the facade of the building but i know it wasn\'t, it was all white and scuffed like the wall i\'ve described before, and the lights were flourescent.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe staircase was a vertical rectangle of red carpet and sudden lack of light in the lefthand expanse of the hallway, and out of it burst the man with the cat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe man had a murky head of brown hair, and seemed oily and young. his hair was longest in the spot just in front of his ears, not quite sideburns, but tendrils in a chaotic spiral. a pile of c-shaped bangs kept his eyebrows and eyes a secret from me forever.\r\nhe wore denim pants that hung off his wiry frame and a thin white tshirt. as he walked briskly toward the door of the building, clutching the cat, his thin shirt moved gently closer to his chest and his pants rustled. he had no shoes on.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe man behind the counter continued to flop his lips around and hold the arm out, retract the arm, hold it out again.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe stopped to regard the man with the cat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe cat was yowling. it yowled more realistically than any cat i had ever heard yowl before. the man clutched it, a hand under it\'s belly and another hand grabbing the skin at it\'s neck. the black and white cat yowled, and didn\'t move much.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe man with the cat moved toward the door of the building, toward that opening that had a tree beyond and cars and a filthy mission sidewalk, to that area where the welcome mat is, where the minty facade of new college begins.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe held the cat out the door, horizontally relating the cats feet and his own arms to the sidewalk. he let go of the cat, who landed on all four feet, yowling, and unruffled. the man who had just let go of the cat put his arms on his hips and sternly finished the interaction: <i>AND STAY OUT!</i> he proceeded to wheel around, and he wheeled with resolve and completeness, much like i had wanted to wheel around before. as he padded past us, now catless in his white socks, white socks on that brown and red taqueria tile, he looked down (the only direction his hair enabled him to look) and muttered <i>fuckin cat</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe mild woman smiled.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni tried to explain how i felt about the cat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe man behind the counter continued to gesture and explain.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand the cat ran back into the building and up the stairs.\r\n</p>'),(1919,'<p>\r\nI\'m gonna stop this before I even get to start. I never enjoyed places where people treat me like a 5 year old kid with downs syndrom.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n;P\r\n</p>'),(1920,'<p><b>me</b> (9:59 AM):	do you have any interest or reason to drive me to mills<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:02 AM):	ill take that as a \"no\"<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:02 AM):	notice me<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:02 AM):	notice me<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:02 AM):	notice me<br>\r\n<b>her</b> (10:04 AM):	notice you notice you notice you<br>\r\n<b>her</b> (10:04 AM):	you you you<br>\r\n<b>her</b> (10:05 AM):	i\'m going back to the bathroom<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:06 AM):	oh<br>\r\n<b>me</b> (10:06 AM):	so thats no i guess?<br></p>\r\n<p> So I took the bus. Here is a list of oakland fashions that I was able to jot down on the short ride home.</p>\r\n<p><ol>\r\n<li><b>meh tell you ma storay mang</b>: skinny old black man, black and white plaid mail cap, cream-colored jacket with butterfly collar, big block black and white plaid button-down, tortoiseshell sunglasses.</li>\r\n<li><b> directly across from him</b> : skinny old white man, identical mail cap in earth-tone herringbone tweed, beige members-only jacket, white button down shirt, pearl pants, BRIGHT YELLOW SOCKS, beige hush puppy-esques.</li>\r\n<li><b>art school dropout</b>: or current student? young white female, short black Velma hair with severely angular short bangs dyed purple-red, high-necked (Members Only-esque) concept denim jacket, purple vintage dress with Mod white pattern serving as both figure and ground to the purple. chunky rubber-soled Mary Janes.</li>\r\n<li><b> sistah pagan</b> : has read every Alice Walker novel. 30something black woman, sashed top hat covering hair, loose fitting black off the shoulder knit shirt, a whole jungle of charms around the neck on black cord, including: the Pentagram, a symbol embraced by Wiccans, a pewter cauldron, and a large silver key to handcuffs with the curly three-ring head. </li>\r\n<li><b>the sky is falling</b> : old white woman with big prescription specs and sensibly cropped white-top. wearing a red shirt, red blazer, and red pants. carrying a red and white striped umbrella that reads \"McGill\" and a square basket bag. Upon leaving the bus she opens the umbrella and walks down the sunny oakland street with it over her head. a parasol, I guess.</li>\r\n<li><b>Nurse Plum</b>: young black woman, purple nursing scrubs, 3/4 length black cardigan, big silver hoops, nike pull-ons, purple butterfly hair clip holding back skinny corn rows.</li>\r\n<li><b>thats all folks</b> : black man with jheri curl under curduroy cap, combat boots, denim ensemble that includes a denim jacket with embroidered WB cartoon characters across the back and the infamous, \"that\'s all folks!\", walking with cane.</li>\r\n<li><b> bongo baby</b> black woman wearing large floppy beret, black gloves, a long black sweatshirt dress, and amber amulet. rolling small flight attendant suitcase down the street. pointy witch shoes.</li></ol></p>'),(1921,'<P> It all started when I saw a documentary on him once while doing nothing at my parents home. Here was this young, tall and skinny long haired freak at Swarthmore. So I checked out his web page.At first it was a random thing, done only in my spare time to calm down the stirrings of a bored out of my mind adolesence. Later, it became material for my more intimate moments with myself. ...I even tried emailing him once...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I saw myself as one of his future girlfriends. Following him onto Salon.com and keeping a correspondence with him there. I empathised with his break-up of Chendra...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> But Justin has immense dirty crotch and I would never in reality be able to sleep with him. \r\nThe \"Dirty Crotch\" phenomenon goes way back to and before \"little penis\" theorems.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I don\'t know why egg and he did not make it work. I see much of her genius stems from having shared a life with a prodigy...of course! Whose swintries wouldn\'t be great then!</P>\r\n\r\n<P> This is a thank-yu to Justin A. Hall ...he who has served as masterbation material many a night, morning, afternoon and whatnot. His poetry sucks but then his body is pretty cute. I am glad he is letting his hair grow out. My gonads thank him</P>\r\n'),(1922,'<a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1920.txt\">Egg\'s most recent</a> got me thinking.  Almost two years; every day, I ride the same bus across town, and every day the same people, looking as bland as they did the day before, accompany me.  Blame it on the sleepy early-morning twilight or the realization that no one has had their coffee yet.  Blame it on the fact that people just aren\'t that friendly in New York City, but none of us seem to ever notice each other, much less make eye contact or interact in even the most minute way.  Consequently, no one seems interesting enough to side-track even the most vague passing moment of attention.<br><br>\r\nThis morning, I resigned myself to look for <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/search.phtml?find=hats\">hats</a> on the bus. I rarely see people wearing hats here, the only exceptions being for the Easter Parade, yarmulkes, dressy church folk, uniforms and the occasional Giants or Knicks fan.  All I spotted was a construction worker sporting a new FDNY hat; solidarity in the aftermath.<br><br>\r\nIn my heightened state this morning, though, I took some time to notice, passively.  I surveyed my surroundings, and fought the urge to dig into my bag for my usual defenses agains the routine, my book, my cd player.  It seems that these are the toys that we commuters use most often to estrange ourselves from each other\'s notice.<br><br>\r\nAlthough I didn\'t see many hats, I did get a first look into the surface lives of these people, these working folks that I\'ve ridden with over 200 times...<br><br>\r\nThe <i>FDNY-Hat Ma</i>n caught my eye first, of course.  He gets off at Sotheby\'s every day, and gets back on in the afternoon, frowning, brushing the renovation dust from his dry-wall spackled Carhartt pants, picking at the \'Local 40\' sticker on his hard hat. He has a problem. Each day, I see three packs of Parliaments in his flannel pocket.  Each afternoon, he tosses the last empty box and butt to the ground as the bus approaches.  He wears his pants really low, probably because his gut has grown so large that he can\'t keep them up anymore.  His belt has the word \"Bobby\" tooled into it, in a contrasting, delicately intricate script.<br><br>\r\n<i>Lottery Lady</i> is his counterpart.  Her pauve-diamond name-necklace reads \"Capricorn\", and her firetruck-red dynasty-length tips scratch the surface of a quarter-inch stack of lottery cards; they\'re all <i>high cards</i>, the $5 games.  She takes her lottery seriously; touching her crucifix, she scratches and frowns, sequentially dropping each card to the bus floor and brushing the scratchings from her oversized woolen sweater.  She\'s the only one that\'s ever vocal.  She says good morning to \"Bobby\" sometimes, and their four-word chit-chats are the only words spoken in my 20 minute commute, his \"Hey there\" reply seeming to echo all the way across Central Park.  Well, that or someone shouting <i>\"Back Door, DAMMIT!\"</i> at the driver...<br><br> \r\nAs if <i>The Driver</i> would neglect the rear door; he seems the epitome of the MTA\'s finest.  Consequently, he\'s very proud of his \"MTA - 20 Years of Safe Driving\" medal. He often wears it on the <i>outside</i> of his winter coat.  I\'ve never seen such a genuine grin as the day I regarded his medal and nodded.  He let me ride for free that day.<br><br>\r\n<i>Captain Merengue</i> is the one with the scruffy facial hair, the dirty one who dresses in worn, all-leather every day, and often smells like yesterday\'s sushi.  I like to play a commuter-beat-matching game to amuse myself, where I try to swipe my Metrocard and get the meter to beep in rythm with the salsa y merengue blasting from his walkman for all to hear.  Yesterday he hooked my interest when he removed said machine from his grimy, greasy pocket to adjust some setting and I was surprised to see that it was a rather fancy, shining, anodized mini-disc player.  The element of surprise never fails to captivate, especially on the morning bus.<br><br>\r\nThere\'s a sprinkling of researchers, educators and scientists that are bound for the same wing of the hospital that I am, also.  Sometimes they see my hospital ID hanging from my bag and give me a knowing nod or zoned-out morning stare.  There\'s also the trusty assortment of pastel-scrub-clad, white shoed nurses who\'s rainbowy numbers never vary- they\'re bound for the clinical wing, and arrive like clockwork.  Occasionally, there are a number of hospital-bound orderlies, but their numbers dwindle, swell and ebb by the seasons, their cheap-cigarette pungency the only evidence remaining after they exit the bus.<br><br>   \r\n<i>The Executive</i> is always in either gray, black or taupe Armani.  If I had his wardrobe of delicate cashmere, I\'d splurge for a cab, but he seems content to sit next to me alternating his morning reading between the placid pink pages of <i>Financial Times</i>, les histoires dans <i>Le Parisien</i>, or a glossy <i>New Yorker</i>.  Hardly a day passes that I\'m not somewhat choked by the still-evaporating astringency of his cologne.  It smells of Versace.<br><br>\r\nThen, the rider that everyone loves to hate gets on.  <i>Crazy Wheelchair Man</i>.  Every Monday and Wednesday, he gets on at 5th Ave and 66th, and rides to Madison and 66th- one block.  I\'m amused by how the other riders generally sigh and frown when they see him flagging down the bus.  They do this because it takes longer for him to get on the bus itself than it does for the bus to carry him his one block commute.  Perhaps they frown because they worry he\'ll delay their commute,  although I\'m persuaded to think they\'re frowning because he has some rather eccentric ways.  Rare is the day he\'s not pulling a flourescent orange toy pistol, aiming at fellow passengers and slowly mouthing <i>\"POW! Die Bitch!\"</i> or something to that effect, and savoring each syllable coming from his toothless mouth.  He frequently carries a toy cell-phone, presses its buttons to make its lo-fi mary-had-a-little-lamb ringer sound, then picks it up and whispers vague but destabilizingly scatter-brained instructions into it, usually regarding <a href=\"http://www.webcom.com/~pinknoiz/coldwar/microwave.html\">microwaves</a> or something to those ends.  Now that I think about it, I haven\'t seen this fellow since September 11th.  God only knows his circumstances.<br><br>\r\nThe <i>Go Getter</i> is the obese but ever-smiling over-rouged porcine woman who presses in front of me each morning to get the first seat on the otherwise empty bus.  I usually get off the train, arriving at my 72nd and B\'way bus stop slightly early.  I bide my time with my cup of coffee, waiting.  It never fails to make me chuckle that the exact <i>moment</i> the bus turns in from West End Ave, she light-footedly darts from the shadows, strafes in front of me (often bumping- I have to be careful not to spill the coffee on her), and squeezes into the bus, a radiant \"I got her FIRST!\" smile cresting her face.  I\'ve begun to step aside lately; after all, what right have I to deprive her of such a simple pleasure?<br><br>\r\nI could go on and on.  There are about ten other regular, quirky riders, not least among which is myself. But, I\'ll leave it at this as I have work to do and you have other worthy entries to read.  But, if you get bored on your commute and the urge moves you, take some time to observe the people you ride the bus/train/whatever with.  I\'ve only recently opened my eyes to the infinite societies and sub-societies, social structures and tact systems that exist in my city, and they\'re one of the most intriguing things I\'ve ever observed.  Strange, bland, perverse and snooty at the same time, they radiate a matchless energy that never fails to captivate me.  Well, at least until my stop arrives.<br><br>\r\n \r\n'),(1923,'<p>THEORY:</p>\r\n<p>Two people, one rubber duck, a jug of Paul Newman\'s Virgin Lemonade, and a dog-eared copy of <i>Art & Physics</i>...can all easily fit into my tub for a leisurely bubblebath.<p>\r\n<p>*Please Note*<br>\r\nThis theory has not yet been tested and proven true.<p>\r\n<p><i>...but it seems to work out fine when i fantasize about it.</i></p>'),(1924,'<p>ok. so there\'s this guy.  last night we went to a bonfire (beach party consisting entirely of a gaggle of laid-off dotcommers [and me]: can you say SURREAL?) and drank two bottles of wine.  today i sent him this poem (by rumi):</p>\r\ndon\'t tell me i had enough<br>\r\ndon\'t stop me from having more<br>\r\nmy soul isn\'t yet satisfied<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlast night an intoxicated friend<br>\r\nhanded me his wine jar<br>\r\ni broke the jar in spite of my desire<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'m not enslaved<br>\r\nby my craving body<br>\r\ni\'ll not pollute this endless longing<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\'ve broken the barriers<br>\r\nof the past and the future<br>\r\nwithout being drunk<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlove\'s message came to me this morning<br>\r\nhiding itself as a healer<br>\r\ntaking my pulse and declaring i\'m weak<br>\r\n\"don\'t drink wine<br>\r\ngiven by anyone<br>\r\nbut your beloved\"<br>\r\nif i can only find i said<br>\r\nthe fountainhead named love<br>\r\nwhat use is any wine<br>\r\n\r\n<p>i found this poem sort of randomly today and felt soothed--it\'s good to know that someone else was as confused as i am at one time.  way back then in the 12th century. rumi knows what\'s up.</p>\r\n<p>see there would be no drama except that well i kinda have this other lover with whom i keep breaking up for reasons too numerous to even keep track of, or for no reason at all, depending on how you look at it.  i love him.  but somehow i find myself gettin all riled up over this other fellow, and well you get the picture.  anyway that wasn\'t what i originally intended to cite as the source of drama; that was just getting THAT off my chest.  the real source of the drama is the fact that New Guy and i are in a rock and roll band together, and the last i mean absolute last thing any band needs is for the singer and the guitarist to hop in bed together.</p>\r\n<p>so that\'s why i sent the poem.  i\'m not going to give in to the cravings.  the ridiculous, misplaced affection which makes me giddy and inarticulate and stupid. i told him i was trying to withdraw my projections and see him as a real person.  he told me he wanted to go to thailand with me. well!</p>\r\n<p>it feels so good to have a crush.</P>\r\n<p>dammit.</p>'),(1925,'<p>I thought about the way people own each other. He said I can\'t cut his hair. You know the story about that. warrior hair.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	no<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	not now<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	i\'m going to bed<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	what do you mean no<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	i cant watch it?<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	you watch things all the time while im bedding<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	well i wanna watch it<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	oh oh oh<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	i\'ve been waiting to watch it with yo<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	you<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	ok!<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	you<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	and only you</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I cut and colored her hair on September 9th, I called le \"baby wolf girl\" as she pawed and nip-pinched at her dreadlocks (if she had been flexible like a wolf she would be bending her mouth behind her head to gnaw for better dreadlocks). She was insulted, but it was the gravest of compliments. she has such dainty little paws. I yelled about Moonstruck; \"I seen the wolf in every person I ever met, and I see the wolf in you.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We have language that protects our property, and we have language that protects us from ownership. Secretly, everyone knows the contract, and here in the galaxy of poly-pan-plexi-post-post-, where we are all free and fluid, we only pretend that people are not property when it gets us what we want. Which is to own each other. Better not to call the bluff though. You could end up married, or in jail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I saved her hair. I\'ve saved a lot of hair. Carlos saw my heart-shaped box and commented, \"that is some voodoo shit.\" when a psychic wants to read you, she asks for something you carry on your body. a strand of hair holds everything. drugs, lovers, products, weather, landscape, habits.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the cat walks across my lap when i type late at night. he leaves his fur all over me, my bed, my dishware, and my friends. gnawing. slinking. he keeps his asshole clean and his fur shiny; ilana says this is a sign of happiness.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the other night, while we were rutting like wolfdogs, the monitor mysteriously turned on (it was the cat, reminding us that we belonged to him, stepping on the spacebar to wake the computer up). a cozy blue light filled the room, rising from the center of the earth until it cast a shadow on the wall of our bodies, still and moving, like when the projector makes pictures move so fast that your eye only sees one form. persistence of vision.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I actually find pieces of his hair stuck to me some mornings. I hold it next to my skin. He is there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>slinking across me in the morning, the cat prints shadows on the back of my eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>slinking across me in the morning. my fingers and my mouth move like a ripped film too. Sometimes I type something and erase the line and sit. then I begin to type it again, and I erase the word. how - delete delete delete. what - delete delete delete. pause. wait. think. no. don\'t. this is how I talk when I am trying to talk about how I feel. \"I cant.\"...\"I dont.\"...\"what\'s.\"...\"you\'re not?!\". broken beginnings of fragments of possible expressions. I open my mouth, and in my mind, delete-delete-delete. no sentence emerges. suppressing the wolf requires a diligent editor.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Suspending my conditioning to neo-liberal concepts of personal freedom and property, I seen the wolf in every single person I ever met. Sometimes I can\'t stay in the city, can\'t accept Las Vegas, can\'t be present on the freeway, because of the wet hunger dripping off each face, even just waiting in line for popcorn or listlessly commuting. Wrapped in this mortal coil of DNA, playa remnants and two wolf pinches of magic, this strand is you. the biological you, the romantic you, your physical history and your token of desire.</p>    '),(1926,'<p>The light fades earlier than i\'m used to<br>\r\nDarkness falls and my world becomes illuminated <br>\r\nIlluminated by the incandessence of weathered street lamps <br>\r\nFlooding light, sometimes flickering <br>\r\nAlways casting an eerie glow <br>\r\nThe lights of cars streak by me <br>\r\nThe blue neon signage proclaims \"Chinese Food\" as the streetlamps flicker <br>\r\nFlooding the street with a wash of intermittent orange light <br>\r\nThe glass-like surface of the wet road <br>\r\nPuddles of water reflect the shapes and colours toward my retina <br>\r\nBeads of water on parked cars scatter the light in a million directions <br>\r\nI\'m happy <br>\r\nThis is where i belong</p>\r\n'),(1927,'<p>I guess we\'ve got to expect a degree of nationalism during wartime, and war is sort of like a sporting event, you\'ve got to cheer for somebody.  I have a hard time cheering for anybody.  However, the events of Sept. 11 were indeed contemptible, despicable acts which demand a response.  While I don\'t think bombing the hell out of Afghanistan is either strategically (or politically) a good idea, some sort of armed response is pretty much unavoidable.  We must go after Bin Laden and al Quaeda, and in that pursuit lives will be sacrificed.  It is terrible.  It is horrible, but it is the cold hard fact.  \r\n\r\n<p>Unfortunately the United States decided to fight a unconventional war in a conventional manner.  We should be conducting specific, intelligence driven, covert actions to root out the terrorists, but we lash out in the only way we know how: with fighter jets and smart bombs, and eventually ground troops.  So now it\'s too late.  We can\'t go back and undo all the things that brought us to this juncture in the world history.  The things that motivated the terrorist attacks are so ingrained, I can\'t merely heap coals on Bush II, Clinton and Bush I.  That would be simplifying matters.  \r\n\r\n<p>If I have learned anything from my years as a left-leaning politico, I\'ve learned that my feelings, words, letters, etc. don\'t really make any kind of difference in the American political system.  My votes for Green Party candidates and American Socialists don\'t amount to a hill of beans because the United States doesn\'t operate from my personal moral or ethical base.  We\'re a nation of enterprising capitalists, industrious money-makers; it\'s the money that talks and initiates change.  I don\'t have any money.  \r\n\r\n<p>So what\'s my point?  It\'s that political idealism simply isn\'t realistic for me anymore.  I can feel and think and speak out things in anyway I want, but it\'s impossible for me to ballyhoo these ethics in the face of war.  War is simply unethical.  It\'s ugly.  As a famous general said, war is hell.  But it still happens.\r\n\r\n<p>And so I\'m confused.  I truly love my country.  I love our freedoms.  I love the very geography of our nation.  I am proud of being an American, of our acceptance of national diversity.   And I know something MUST happen because of September 11.  But what?  The terrorists have us by the collective balls, we don\'t respond and we look like wimps, we do respond and they can accuse us of persecuting Muslim\'s.  Either way we\'re screwed; we open ourselves to further attacks.  \r\n\r\n<p>What has happened has happened.  We\'re at war.  This is the first war in which every single American, at home or abroad, is at risk.  Every one of us, like it or not, is a soldier.  Each morning when I get on the subway, I think that there\'s a chance I may not return home.\r\n\r\n<p>Did you ever read, \"Man\'s Search for Meaning,\"  by Viktor Frankl? (http://www.geocities.com/~webwinds/frankl/frankl.htm)   He was a survivor of the concentration camps who came up with the idea that \"The Best of Us Will Not Return.\"  That meant that the people who maintained the most ethical standards, who wouldn\'t steal the shoes of the dead, or food from the already dying, those ethical people would DIE in the camps.  Those that broke the standards, who did anything necessary to survive would emerge from the camps.  They would LIVE.  The Best of us Will Not Return.  \r\n\r\n<p>What I\'m saying here is, despite my ethical base, I can neither condemn nor praise this war.  It doesn\'t seem like the best idea to me, but we\'re living in very strange and scary times.  If it wasn\'t this type of conflict it MUST be another.  Right now there is no such thing as peace.  It simply doesn\'t exist.  Frankl posited that, when it comes right downto it, everybody is responsible for only their own life.  I believe that we define our own unique ethical paradigms. So I have to redefine my ethical system and hope to muddle through.  As much as I hate it, I have to compromise... '),(1928,'1. i know this isn\'t as contemporary as it should/could be, but i have a \"hole\" song or two in my mp3 playlist and when you listen to courtney love\'s voice, you get a sense that this voice isn\'t the voice of contemporary rock, voice of a youthful generation, as much as it is as the voice of contemporary celebrity, all those celebrities you see in magazines but never hear speak. the jennifer love hewitts, the pam andersons, the males with brown hair and chins that i don\'t know the names of. dylan mcdonald fraiser mcdermott something something. the kid who was in pearl harbor (didn\'t see it) and ends up on the cover of vanity fair, clearly the voice of the pre-9/11 world.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n2. there are 3 or 4 songs i\'ve heard on the radio in the last couple of years that do this cute thing where if there is the word \"stop\" in the lyrics they suddenly stop the song for a second or two, maybe this is something a single engineer or producer thinks is clever, maybe this is supposed to be a jazz-like technique displaying the agility of the band (even though these are studio recordings and one can imagine a band having to practice it and doing a few false \"stop\"s to get it right). whatever the explanation, as somebody who hears the radio at the gym, i\'m going to publicly call for the end of this practice. '),(1929,'<p>I thought about the way people own each other. He said I can\'t cut his hair. You know the story about that. warrior hair.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/hand2.jpg\" width=\"161\" height\" 195\" border=\"0\" align=\"right\"><font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	no<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	not now<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	i\'m going to bed<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	what do you mean no<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	i cant watch it?<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	you watch things all the time while im bedding<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	well i wanna watch it<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	oh oh oh<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	i\'ve been waiting to watch it with yo<br>\r\n<font color=\"blue\"><b>j</b></font>:	you<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	ok!<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	you<br>\r\n<font color=\"red\"><b>e</b></font>:	and only you</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1929_1.jpg\" width=\"161\" border=\"0\" align=\"right\">When I cut and colored her hair on September 9th, I called le \"baby wolf girl\" as she pawed and nip-pinched at her dreadlocks (if she had been flexible like a wolf she would be bending her mouth behind her head to gnaw for better dreadlocks). She was insulted, but it was the gravest of compliments. she has such dainty little paws. I yelled about Moonstruck; \"I seen the wolf in every person I ever met, and I see the wolf in you.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1929_3.jpg\" width=\"200\" height=\"186\" border=\"0\" align=\"left\">We have language that protects our property, and we have language that protects us from ownership. Secretly, everyone knows the contract, and here in the galaxy of poly-pan-plexi-post-post-, where we are all free and fluid, we only pretend that people are not property when it gets us what we want. Which is to own each other. Better not to call the bluff though. You could end up married, or in jail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I saved her hair. I\'ve saved a lot of hair. Carlos saw my heart-shaped box and commented, \"that is some voodoo shit.\" when a psychic wants to read you, she asks for something you carry on your body. a strand of hair holds everything. drugs, lovers, products, weather, landscape, habits.<br clear=\"all\"></p>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1929_2.jpg\" width=\"200\" height=\"183\" border=\"0\" align=\"left\">the cat walks across my lap when i type late at night. he leaves his fur all over me, my bed, my dishware, and my friends. gnawing. slinking. he keeps his asshole clean and his fur shiny; ilana says this is a sign of happiness.<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>the other night, while we were rutting like wolfdogs, the monitor mysteriously turned on (it was the cat, reminding us that we belonged to him, stepping on the spacebar to wake the computer up). a cozy blue light filled the room, rising from the center of the earth until it cast a shadow on the wall of our bodies, still and moving, like when the projector makes pictures move so fast that your eye only sees one form. persistence of vision.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1929_4.jpg\" width=\"200\" height=\"189\" border=\"0\" align=\"left\">I actually find pieces of his hair stuck to me some mornings. I hold it next to my skin. He is there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>slinking across me in the morning, the cat prints shadows on the back of my eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>slinking across me in the morning. my fingers and my mouth move like a ripped film too. Sometimes I type something and erase the line and sit. then I begin to type it again, and I erase the word. how - delete delete delete. what - delete delete delete. pause. wait. think. no. don\'t. this is how I talk when I am trying to talk about how I feel. \"I cant.\"...\"I dont.\"...\"what\'s.\"...\"you\'re not?!\". broken beginnings of fragments of possible expressions. I open my mouth, and in my mind, delete-delete-delete. no sentence emerges. suppressing the wolf requires a diligent editor.</p> \r\n<br clear=\"all\">\r\n<p><img src=\"http://atari.saturn5.com/~egg/evehair.jpg\" width=\"200\" height=\"143\" border=\"0\" align=\"right\">Suspending my conditioning to neo-liberal concepts of personal freedom and property, I seen the wolf in every single person I ever met. Sometimes I can\'t stay in the city, can\'t accept Las Vegas, can\'t be present on the freeway, because of the wet hunger dripping off each face, even just waiting in line for popcorn or listlessly commuting. Wrapped in this mortal coil of DNA, playa remnants and two wolf pinches of magic, this strand is you. the biological you, the romantic you, your physical history and your token of desire.</p>                \r\n'),(1930,'I leave English class every Tuesday and Thursday in a sort of daze. We sit in the classroom, and the teacher teaches, and the students ask questions and make comments. Some of them, the commenters especially, annoy me. It is the state of being annoyed where actually you are just embarrassed for them, like you would be embarrassed for yourself if you had said what they said. I don\'t mind when somebody asks a question to clarify something, but when they begin with \"I feel..\", I just think to myself <i>right, here we go again.</i><br><br>\r\nIn this state of annoyance, which totals about 20 percent of the period, I like to scribble down story ideas and little nonsense drawings.\r\n<br><br>\r\nOf course there\'s that one girl, it\'s not always a girl but in this class it is, who has to show everyone how smart and well-read she is. She uses lots of big words and mentions Alan Ginsburg a lot.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI do it too but only when it\'s directly relevant. And I try to restrain myself too. I do read a lot though.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe most important ting I have learned in English class this semester is to reserve judgement on a piece of writing until we have discussed it in class. For example, we are reading Whitman right now, <i>Song of Myself</i>, and I just hated it so much when I read it at home, I could hardly get through it. Our professor pointed some things out to me that have changed my opinion. I still don\'t like it, but now I at least appreciate more or less what he was trying to say. After class, when I am walking home, my awareness of everything seems heightened, I am in a psuedo-hallucinogenic state. I walk down the tree-lined sidewalk, four blocks to my apartment, looking at the oversized sculptures on the lawn of the School of Art, thinking. <i>Those will kill the grass</i>. I cross Gervais Street, alone, me against all those cars, like a soldier in enemy territory. I pass people on the sidewalk, going to school I suppose, and say hello. I pick a leaf off of a bush as I pass by, and study the intricate webbing of the veins on its underside. I walk over a section of concrete paving, which says to me and to everyone, <i>John 1981</i>. The sun shines bright and warm on my face, and sometimes I think, <i>this is my life</i>.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI helped my teacher put the desks back in rows after class yesterday. She asked me if I was a practicing buddhist. I said that I guessed I was, as much as I was anything. <i>You know</i>, I said, gesturing vaguely, <i>walking home from school...after class.....</i> She nodded as if she understood completely, and said that she thought I should stick with English--nothing else will do that for you.'),(1931,'<p>i am writing this because i think everyone needs to sit back, smoke a bowl (if yer into that sorta thing), and watch the \"paranoid android\" video by radiohead.  i\'ve always liked radiohead and that song, but jesus christ, that video is the greatest thing to happen to me tonight...if not in the past month.  you all probably think i\'m an idiot, but it is 336am and this video has been the only thing that could put me in a good mood all day...it\'s been one of those.  i know this is alot of useless blabbing, but everyone (especially BMG) needs to download it right away.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\'ll put a smile on yer face.  :)<br>\r\n..................................................</p>'),(1932,'<p>\r\nhe went away crying and couldn\'t tell me \r\nwhy. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\'s hard for me not to assume it is because of me but i am pretty sure that if i am at all \r\ninvolved, it is extremely tangentially. i\'m very \r\nproud of that. it has taken me so very long to \r\nfight the brutal cycle of having such low \r\nself-esteem that i assume everyone is just pretending to like me, and having such a distorted ego that i think that the reason the \r\none i love is depressed is because of me and \r\nonly me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut, like a scientist, i turn over all the chained\r\ntogether pieces of fact in my head. these pieces \r\nof evidence tell me that i am not inventing his \r\naffection. i cling to these things, these memories\r\nof soft words, gentle motions; smooth, cool, and \r\ncomforting, like the flat agate stones i used to \r\ncarry in my pocket. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni use these facts to prevent obsessing. i touch \r\nthem, each in turn, and then move forward, away \r\nfrom him and the sadness caused by not being \r\nable to help. and when he is ready to be happier\r\nagain, he will come back. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1933,'<CENTER><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1933_1.jpg\"></CENTER>\r\n<P>How strange to pray for a blank slate. On my way out of a desert planet I did just that. \r\n<BR>\r\n<P>From one island to the next, a sudden prayer for an earthling lullaby.  A way out of my atomic little heart.\r\n<P> These send-ups of the spiritual life are aimed to end my previous motifs. Wake up in the morning; refuse to mimic anyone on earth. That propels me. \r\n'),(1934,'even though a girl blows you off, she still thinks youre cute and probably wants you. a girl probably means yes or at least maybe when she says um, no or looks the other way. i know that girl will appreciate me if i light her ciggie. maybe shell wanna come home with me.<BR><BR>These were the thoughts that I know went through the mind of the older french canadian man i passed on the street just this evening, i know it. i <i>know</i> it.<BR><BR>\r\neven though these things are not true, people still think them and things like them.\r\n<BR><BR><br>\r\n<i>l\'homme canadien francais vous meurent s\'il vous plait matrice et tout le vous meurt satisfait. je suis si triste en raison de vous. Les hommes de New York sont meilleurs... </i>'),(1935,'<p>woken up by sirens somewhere around 8 am.  that\'s pretty normal.\r\nit\'s a city... lots of sirens.  but today they didn\'t stop.  it sounded like every fire truck in the city was driving by.  then driving by again then maybe going around the block for one more pass.  the strange thing about this morning\'s sirens though were they didn\'t have the standard doppler shift passing.  the sirens stayed. they stayed because they\'d reached their destination.  i looked out the window of the front door and saw people running by.  oh god, i thought, this is what the media\'s been talking about.  something bad is going down.  i threw some clothes on and went outside.  donald, the very gay upstairs neighbor said \"the casa loma\'s burning!\"  it took me a minute to register what the casa loma was.  then i realized that the casa loma is yvonne\'s home and has been for the past seven years.  it\'s a residential hotel, or, to put it more bluntly, a crack house.  and yvonne is our neighbor who lives there.  she\'s a personality and a half, always there when you\'re late and need to be going, always talking your ear off when you don\'t have time to listen.  i like her a lot.  we exchange gifts on our birthdays.  she\'s a virgo, too.  she asks for money and if it\'s ever more than a dollar, she always pays it back. \r\nwe\'ve spent many evenings sitting on the steps of my apartment talking and she is a good part of what living in this house means to me.  she always knows what\'s going on.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nwhen i ran outside this morning to see what was going on there was yvonne, in tears, holding her hands on her head.  she was saying \"oh man oh man oh man\" and couldn\'t say anything else.  i\'d never seen her speechless before.  she paced up and down the sidewalk, amid the other residents in their bathrobes, and half dressed states.  some with their cats clutched tight to their chests.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe casa loma was deemed to be uninhabitable.  they can\'t move back in.  sitting on the steps this afternoon and she walked by, backpack on her shoulder.  she barely said hi... just nodded and said \"i\'ll see you round\" then went across the street and got on the bus.  that means a neighborhood with no yvonne.  she will be missed.  she will be missed.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/1935_1.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n'),(1936,'<P> There are always fatalist questions that are interesting to ask. \"What would you take from your room if you knew you could never go back to it?\", \" Which parent would you save in the situation where you can only save one?\" , very interesting to see who one would save.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Now in the case of twins things can seem to be rather different. Now you have this person who has been next to you since time immemorium and knows everything there is to know about you. Such is the case with my boyfriend and his brother. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Last night I asked Michael who he would save and he answered his brother if the case were saving either me or his brother. An answer which is absolutely understandable since I think now after thinking it over I would save my sister before Michael. M was adamant though, that his brother would save him before his girlfriend.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I knew...though that Robert would choose his girlfriend over his brother. So this afternoon we called them and sure enough I was right. Rob wants to \"extend \" his family and he can\'t do it with Michael...Julie has the fertility M lacks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Now I wonder certain things. How I don\'t give my family the importance they deserve. As I rub my victory in Michael\'s face I realize I am making him feel guilty for a natural disposition of his. He is more into his brother than his brother into him and here I am advertising that all love should come in equal amounts.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Unfortunately it doesn\'t. There are people who one would enjoy watching burn while there exist those whom we would trade places with in order to save their lives. They are all integral parts of ones life, yet loved my us in different ways. So M is number five on people I would save, with my parents, my sister and my cat taking the lead. At least he would save me before his sister...ummm.</P>'),(1937,'<P> I spent most of my day off earlier this week, stressing over wether or not to call the guy I thought I liked.  I did.  I hung up on his answering machine.  I left him a cute little email instead.  He did not respond. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Then, I had mustered up the courage to ask another person to go to a wedding with me, but before I had a chance to ask the question, he mentioned his really shitty work schedule.  So, I did not bother.  I\'d like to thank him for sparing me the anxiety attack I would have had while asking.  He doesn\'t know. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I tried to page him later, to talk, and in a moment of dyslexia, typed my own number wrong.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I thought that I would stay home today and be a good girl.  I thought I\'d stay for my brothers birthday dinner, even though we don\'t really get along anymore.  When he got here, I had nothing to say to him, and he had nothing to say to me. I didn\'t want to start another family feud though, so, I stayed; the model of support.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I didn\'t need to worry about starting a fight by leaving. When my grandmom finds out about the \'party that wasn\'t a party\' that she wasn\'t invited to, the shit will hit the fan. It wasn\'t a party. My mom accidentally told my aunt that he was coming over and she asked if she could too.  It\'s not going to seem like that, though...whew...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> ...and tomorrow is Monday... </P>\r\n\r\n'),(1938,''),(1939,'austin\'s farts are loud<br>\r\nthrusts ass into sky, smiles<br>\r\nwhy are men so proud?'),(1940,'I used to be a morning person. I thought I still was, but when I woke up this morning, ahead of schedule, I was in such a pleasant mood that it made my past \'morning person\' wakings seem inferior. This morning was delicious; a feeling I haven\'t felt for quite a while. I like mornings like these- they can inspire.<br><br>\r\nIn no rush, I put on my favourite shirt; I had been saving it for tomorrow\'s meeting, but forget it, I <i>like</i> it and wanted to wear it today. I stuff into my favourite shoes; true, they\'re a little small, but I <i>like</i> them alot; they\'re broken in just right. I sauntered off to the train.  Sure, the train stinks of stale Burger King and vomit, but there\'s something nice about riding on one of the new 2 train cars so early; the metal surfaces are still shiny with factory oil, and they reflect the passing kaliedoscope lights in an auora of morning colours.<br><br>\r\nThis morning was mine.<br><br>\r\nI made my way to the m72 bus, unusually excited to go to work. Perhaps my mood was due to a good <a href=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/hotelyorba.mp3\">song</a> or <a href=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/fellinlovewithagirl.mp3\">two</a>, or maybe I just woke up on <i>the right side of the bed</i>.  Regardless, I had a smile on my face, a cup of unusually good street-corner coffee in my hand, and felt like striking up a conversation.<br><br>\r\nEnter <i>Metal Head</i>.  This fellow gets on my bus every day at 72nd street and Amsterday Ave.  When I\'m bored, I play <i>Count Metal Head\'s Piercings</i>... he has many. We\'re talking visible double-digits.  Sometimes, I play <i>Name that Metal Head Tune</i>; this game should be easy as he savours his audio-death fugues at such a volume that everyone can hear. Unfortunately, I\'m not well versed enough in his genre to be entirely proficient, but I\'m getting better.  Some mornings, I watch as he gels back his dyed-black ponytail, untangling his obscenely large wallet chain.  Some mornings, he spits on his finger and uses it to rub scuffs from his oxblood Doc\'s.<br><br>\r\nBut this morning, something was different.  The Basic Black clad rokker that usually slinks on, finishing the last drag from his Benson & Hedges, was a no show. Instead, his shape-shifter zeitgeist entered and sat next to me on the over-crowded bus.<br><br>\r\nOxford cloth shirt. Natural brown hair, cropped to a sensible chic. Goatee shaved.  Horseshoes, barbells and loops conspicuously removed; fitted with spacers where appropriate. No walkman. Khaki dockers. Pennyloafers. No wallet chain, no spiked collar.<br><br>\r\nI looked at <i>Metal Head</i> and said, \"dude?\".<br><br>\r\n<i>Metal Head</i> replied: \"job interview\", as a large grin burst over his face.<br><br>\r\nAs any good New Yorker would do, <i>Metal Head</i> dug his walkman out of his bag in order to avoid further conversation, but stopped, muttering in frustration when he realized that he had no disc in it.<br><br>\r\nWith a promise to return my CD later in the week, I turned <i>Metal Head</i> on to what I was listening to.<br><br>\r\n\"Nobody lends no one fuckin shit without a gadam reason. If you\'re fuckin serious, you\'ve just made my fuckin morning!\" smiled <i>Metal Head</i> at me.  Ahh, true personalities run deep...<br><br>\r\n'),(1941,'<p>\r\nsweet fruit, you are my instant pick-me-up: narcotic bliss, wrapped discretely in nature\'s package. fruit, you put it out there and take it, from behind or whatever way i want you. you are my misplaced outlet; with each slurp, i sublimate.\r\n</p><p>\r\njust now, i consumed a plum, a pear, and a kiwi fruit. they were juicy and delicious. first, i started with the plum: i ate its flesh with a corncob typewriter technique, savoring the last bits before biting them off the pit. then, i cut the kiwi fruit in half and sucked the insides out - until the skin gave in, caved in, and split. the pear, i devoured, with neither grace nor form: abject terror and chaos, like an invasion of teeth armies, raining fire and fury on the defensiveless landscape of pear.\r\n</p><p>\r\noh fruit, you give so much; your musky market temptation and FDA-approved nutrition. you ask for nothing in return but to moisten my chin. even the coffee bean, with its jittery roast, leaves a joyless funk of dissatisfaction. fruit, nothing compares to you. if we all turned into fruit at once, i would let you eat me back.\r\n</p>'),(1942,'One thing about San Francisco weather is that it is never really warm. The exception is a few days that occur sometime from mid-September to mid-October. This once-a-year weekend of summer weather has just recently occurred.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWith the weather at its best, Heidi and I have been spending afternoons at Alamo Square. San Francisco has some of the nicest urban parks in the world and Alamo Square, with its views that are reproduced on post cards and TV shows, is one of the nicest parks in the city. Despite the beauty, trees which often make the area smell wonderful, it\'s often too windy and cold to relax in a t-shirt and take a nap. Naturally, when the temperature hits the 90 degree mark Alamo Square is a popular destination. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nLike walking around the city streets, waiting for a table at a restaurant, taking the bus downtown to the library, hanging out waiting for a movie, browsing at Green Apple books, spending a few hours at the park provides the sort of interaction with the general populace that offers a perspective on civilization. Manners, values, whimsies and concerns are all in plain view. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nEach of these afternoons we hauled our little camping chairs up to the park with a bottle of water, the sunblock and a backpack full of books. We sometimes sat in the shade, or later in the day with the sun behind us, sitting lower than the Twin Peaks radio tower, offering an orange glow to the famous row of Victorians with the pyramid peaking up behind, our shadows growing up to thirty feet long.\r\n<BR><BR>  \r\nWhile our experience of the park justified our high rent for those few days, our experience of the people did not. Make no mistake: whatever you have heard about California bay area people vs. east coast people is wrong. While these people may be well-traveled and the recipients of good education, their selfishness and self-centered-ness reduces them to savages, shitting on any possibility of high civilization here.<BR><BR> I say this as somebody who has lived in the neighborhood for over 6 years, often distracted at Alamo Square by the never-ending busloads of tourists from England and Japan. Well in the recent weeks the tourists have canceled, we saw no groups at the park in the past few days, and the people we were left with, the locals, were ghastly and awful. Come back Welsh grandmother, great aunt from Kyoto! Bring back your VHS-C cameras! Germans return in your tans and greens!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI\'ll offer detailed explanation, with the request that readers in other cities who recognize the same offenses in their own parks imagine Alamo Square saturated with these crimes against civilization. Imagine it so bad that going home and sitting in the dark seems like the only option. <BR><BR>\r\n<b>the music people:</b>This relatively small group of people, with their impotent little BBQ unit recently purchased at Walgreens, decides that everyone at the park should \"enjoy\" their drum and bass. Clearly these are savages who have trucked up the hill, not food and refreshing beverage, but large wood crates with their turntables, amplifiers and \"vinyl.\" Are they daring me to get up and say: If I wanted to listen to your music I would stay at home and wait by the tv for a Nissan ad? \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI hate drum and bass, this music with all beat and no rhythm, but even if they were playing Lee Morgan or Miles \"Sketches of Spain,\" something I love, it would be bad. Bring your boom box, play music for those sitting at your picnic table, in your area, but what on earth gives anyone the right to force your music on an entire public park. Does that one stupid girl wearing bright red corduroy pants standing next to your turntables flopping aimlessly in her bad skin justify this? \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<b>the frisbee people:</b> Certainly some parks and playgrounds are meant for frisbees, but many were not. If there is a large field full of people with blankets and lawn chairs, reading, relaxing, napping, it should be obvious that this is not a place for frisbee. The awful thing about frisbee is the people who play it are not usually the sports fuckos (more on them later). These people should know better. Like their awful surf music, Californians seem to love frisbee. <BR><BR>\r\nI witnessed two males playing frisbee who allowed their frisbee to hit three different people. Two of those people folded up their blankets and left, figuring the park was no longer open to people who just want to mind their own business. Of course these frisbee people continued to toss the disc of regret back and forth, guffawing each time it flew out of control. Smirking at each other when it hit some girl in the back of the head. After the first or second time, non-savages would have determined that it wasn\'t the place to play frisbee. <BR><BR> You could say to me, being one of the unhit spectators, that I should continue to read my book and mind my business. The problem is when you witness other readers, other lovers of the sun being hit, you can\'t help but have that twinge of paranoia, that crink in your shoulders that you are next. This feeling intensifies each time one of the savages tramples by at their highest possible running speed. You hear the ground behind you thud like an approaching cattle drive and suddenly there\'s the wind of the flapping meat poles carrying the brainless miracle across the field towards the plastic disc. Amazing that they can\'t see that people came to the park for relaxation, not to be dashed up against, at a distance of sometimes only inches.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<b>the sports people:</b> Have some relation to the frisbee people, but a little different. Two guys with a football is clearly a much better situation than a frisbee. They have some sense of control, some idea of the physics of the ball. People with baseballs are entirely different. It is ironic that baseball is one of the more civilized sports, but once you take amateurs out of a designated field with glove, ball and bat they turn into savages. <BR><BR>\r\nWeeks ago in Golden Gate park, once again, the field was full of people who are sitting enjoying the sun, reading, talking, picnicking and two dads and their kids decide that it\'s a good time to have batting practice. With a frisbee or football, you can imagine the savages fooling themselves into thinking that they some control or aim over where the object goes. It must be clear to anyone when you are hitting a baseball with a bat, you have no control. And the fathers are rooting the kids on to hit it harder and farther. <BR><BR>\r\nSimilar to the frisbee experience, their goal is not to want to have a good time playing their sport, but to see how large of a section of the field they can clear out. Also similar to frisbee, is that men seem to think holding the ball justifies the removal of their shirts. Watching these fathers and sons with the baseball, having the ball roll up to my feet more than once, when the kid huffed up to me to retrieve his ball (of course without saying sorry) I was not tempted to ask him to move their game to an actual baseball field, but rather to ask: Can you please ask your father to cover up his bitch tits?\r\n<BR><BR>Soccer is also high on the list of sports crimes at city parks. Similar to baseball, it is a sport whose proper place is a field. I don\'t belong sitting in the middle of a baseball diamond while the little league plays. You don\'t belong punting that soccer ball towards my blanket. Soccer balls seemed to be designed to inflict the most amount of pain to the heads of reclining women. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nlast but not least, <b> the fucking dog people:</b> Dog people are the worst examples of human beings. Dog people commit crimes, but don\'t seem to want to acknowledge that these are crimes. Even if they witness the crimes they seem to ignore them. There are so many levels of denial with dog people that a psychiatric fund needs to be established. They deny that their beast has come up to you eating your picnic, that it is standing only 3 inches away, they refuse to pull it back, they deny it is drooling on you. They deny that a beast drooling on your leg is gross. The most common denial you will hear from these savages is: \"Oh, (he/she/Rover) <i>never</i> does that!!!\" Violence against these people seems to be the only way to correct their problem. A swift shot to the nose and then: Oh, I never do that!<BR><BR>\r\nThe level of denial constantly forces the sane around the person to question whether they know and just do not care. The dog people act nice and smile. They pretend to be kind people. They will pay 10 grand to operate on the beast, to keep it alive. They want to think that they are doing good. Dog people seem to want to be good citizens. The problem is they are only nice in the context of their own self-centered world. They aren\'t helping anyone but themselves. At least with smokers, giving you cancer, making everything stink, they know what they are doing, they are helpless addicts and they can\'t stop, while there might not be a sense of remorse, there is always a twinge of regret in acknowledging the situation, there is some honesty that you will never find with the dog people.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nSimilar to the frisbee people, the issue of proximity violation is key. If you can\'t control where your beast is going to run, keep it on the chain. Don\'t act like it\'s an accident that he is fucking my leg, you let him go! <BR><BR> \r\n\r\nDog people seem to think this half hour or so in the park amounts to freedom for the beast. So what they do is allow the dog to wander around the park and follow it where ever it goes. They are hesitant to tug it back on the chain. We are sitting eating our picnic and this small genetically modified dog is standing right next to us. His owner is two feet away. Would this middle aged man think it is acceptable to stand right over us, in the middle of a park with other wide empty spaces, as we eat and have a conversation if he didn\'t have a dog with him? I\'d guess no. What has to happen is: admit that 85% of the beast\'s time is spent in your god awful apartment, there\'s no freedom by letting him wander up to us, yank the chain and back the fuck up.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nLike the frisbee people there is the proximity and speed issue. I\'m trying to relax, I don\'t need your beast blitzing by me. If I can smell it as it dashes by, it has invaded my personal space.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThen there is the dog owner screaming issue. I would probably think twice before screaming bloody murder in the midst of a quiet park (obviously if the music people are around this point is invalid). Dog owners obviously don\'t think like this. While I appreciate that your dog\'s \"cute\" name is your only creative outlet, it\'s a fact that unlike children, these beasts aren\'t really responding to a specific name, they can be retrained to a new name in hours. Stop yelling next to my head. <BR><BR>\r\nThere is the sex issue. There is a symbiotic relationship between the way dog owners seem to gather socially and the beasts\' sexual appetites. Herds of dog owners come to the park at the same time and they are all in denial about sex. You may have cut off it\'s balls, sewn up it\'s beast gully, but no matter how much you trained it, your beast likes to fuck other beasts. Once again, the park is for relaxation of humans, while you may enjoy bestial pleasures, I do not. It\'s really quite simple: I did not come to the park to watch animals fuck. <BR><BR>Related to sex is violence. The barking and growling that happens around the constant humping, the whines of tiny females skewered often becomes the howls of fighting. I could say I didn\'t come to the park to watch beasts maul each other, but actually, I have to admit to finding it amusing. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFinally there is the shit issue. Too much has been said and written about it. Maybe it\'s hard to make somebody who allows the windows of their car to become browned and tacky with dried dog slobber to understand: Parks aren\'t for dogs. Parks are for people. I should be able to sit on the ground. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAt the end of it all, despite these attacks on civilization at our city parks, I still go. I still have hope. I think the more of us quiet, peaceful people who go, the higher the chance the savages will some day think twice. We are here! Other people besides you exist in this world! We sit unmoved, close the grass, content and still, on those days where those of us with hearts must question: is there ever going to be another day as nice as this? \r\n\r\n'),(1943,'<p>Lately, I\'ve been pretty angry about how our country\'s handling things.  Ever since the last presidential \"election\", actually.  Never before have I seen the US\'s collective intelligence insulted so flagrantly and with such frequency.  In searching for flowery words and resplendent prose to express my feelings about the recent events in NYC and Washington, I\'ve realized, somewhat regretfully,  that most of the people I\'m about to complain about probably wouldn\'t be able to follow that kind of language anyhow.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I just can\'t believe that the people of this country are not enraged at the media, for example, that leaves out crucial details and strives not to inform, but to smooth over.  I can\'t imagine what kind of mind one must have to gleefully cheer on an administration that struts around like a peacock when it should be thinking critically.  In the end, it is far easier (and less terrifying) to sit in an armchair in front of the tube and veg out than to contemplate the consequences of our leaders\' actions, or to question thier motives.  I only wish I had the funds to run a national TV spot that just showed the following message in white text on a black background for 30 seconds:</P> \r\n\r\n<p>\"The president is going to get us all killed.\"</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1944,'<P>I ask myself how i got here or why i work here sometimes.  This office space in Scott\'s Valley, California, nested between the tech power house of San Jose and the sleepy surf town of Santa Cruz.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Simple reasons.  It is non-profit which is profitable.  People are not stressed out about making money and yet they make money so they are not stressed out about not having any.  There are a large number of expensive european and japanese sports cars outside.  People here are taken care of by somebody.</P>\r\n<P>Another reason, there are not very many losers around here.  Somehow, I am not a loser in the context of this job.  Maybe that is because I know more now than in the past.  There is less that I cannot do this year than last year.</P>\r\n<P>But the real reason I am here is to say this:  there is a jigsaw puzzle upstairs by the picture window and next to the coffee machine.  People walk by and put a piece in if they can find one.  It is always one of three puzzles that is out there.  They rotate.  It is a meditative venture only.  Today for the first time I stopped to put in a puzzle piece.</P>'),(1945,'<pre>\r\nBind me in your love.  \r\n\r\nMy hands and feet, eyes and ears are as pages for you.  \r\n\r\nMy spine should be gilt by the searing finger of God.\r\n\r\nTransform me from the manuscript \r\nof your holy Word \r\nto the editio princeps of divine publication.\r\n\r\nI am your\r\nyour ceaseless folio, \r\nyour enduring duodecimo, \r\nyour longstanding tome.  \r\n\r\nMake me a book, oh Lord, \r\nso that I may enter the mind of man.\r\n\r\nOh Lord, \r\nI detach my kneecaps with a lever\r\nand shave my head with an awl;\r\nI count my teeth in a jar \r\nand hold my eyeballs in a cup.\r\n\r\nAll that I am I sacrifice:\r\nI catalogue my bones, index my flesh. \r\nMy blood is the glowing ink of your hot letterpress.\r\nI am list and gazetteer, encyclopedia and lexicon, \r\nmanual and almanac.    \r\n\r\n Make me a book, oh Lord, \r\nand I will speak with more than flesh, \r\nwith words that transcend corporeal precincts.  \r\n\r\nOh Lord, \r\nI can no longer be a man, so I plead: \r\nmake me a book.\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1946,'<p>I used my handweights again for the first time in months today. I used to keep them at the desk at work for when I was bored. Prevents RSI, efficiently builds my rippling physique. I noticed them again because austin pinched my arm this weekend and told me I was getting soft.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I become indignant at the word, like Jennifer Jason Leigh\'s Hepburn caricature in \"The Hudsucker Proxy\". She befriends a simpleton CEO in order to write an expose on his foolhardiness, and finds herself warming up to his uber-tard wisdom - and even thinking he is sort of cute. Her wisecracking pal at the newspaper (played by Sam Remi) chides her for getting \"<i>SOFT</i>\". She decks him. At the end of the movie, she falls for the simpleton. of course.</p>\r\n\r\n<br><a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.wdog.com/rider/writings/real_kruger.htm\"><img src=\"http://www.wdog.com/rider/writings/hero.gif\" width=\"273\" height=\"200\" border=\"0\" alt=\"this one\'s for tuggy\" align=\"center\"></a><br><p> I have two Barbaras on my mind, Kruger and Ehrenreich. </p>\r\n\r\n<p><font size=\"-1\"><b>BRIAN LAMB</b>: Barbara Ehrenreich author of \"Fear of Falling: The Inner Life of the Middle Class.\" Why the title \"Fear of Falling?\" <br>\r\n<a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.booknotes.org/transcripts/10204.htm\">BARBARA EHRENREICH</a>: Well, it\'s a book about anxiety -- about the anxieties the secret self doubt and worries of the middle class. Actually it\'s about the professional middle class. The doctor or lawyer or social worker teacher, college professor stratum of people. And fear of falling refers first to the kind of obvious fear of just sliding down in terms of income down into a poorer class. But it also about something I don\'t think has been written much about. A more subtle psychological fear of going soft really. Of losing that will to succeed. That drive that it takes for professional achievement.</font></p>\r\n<p>I sat on his back while he read his java book. Whenever there is downtime on weekends I run in my mind through the backlogged list of things to do. I always think of Anna Murch first: \"Every week, write an artist\'s statement. Each week, do not look at the last.\" I lifted my weight off him and headed towards the computer. He murmured a baby animal noise. You want me to sit on you while you read? Incoherent vocal affirmation. OK. I sat back down on him. It gave me an excuse from the dreaded Statement Exercise. <i>soft</i>. my own wisecracking office chum chides me in my head.</p>\r\n<p>Last night Ray Wang mused, \"Who wants to be an artist after September 11?\" He was so Jay. \"In this world of suffering and golf pants, what do I possibly have to say?\". Except Ray Wang was my teacher, one of the people who is supposed to prop me up and convince me that I am a very special human being with a gift of intoxicating aesthetic wisdom to pour like a storm of wine over the bliss-thirsty universe.</p>\r\n<p>Juniper countered him with her usual pseudo-bohemian philosophy:\"I think people who think outside of the culture are important at a time like this!\" Her politics annoy me, because she doesnt read the news or history books and make informed decisions about \"rejecting the mainstream\". She pulls non-conformity from her gut, and feels that not voting makes her a rebel. Feels that not participating in certain rituals (and embracing other commonplace ones) somehow qualifies her as \"thinking outside the culture.\"</p>\r\n<p>I grew up in the suburbs, so I am always suspicious of people who think that they are somehow exempt from history and social conditioning. Even those who camped at Women\'s Land. <i>Especially</i> those who make artwork under the tiny umbrella of post-european visual culture.</p> \r\n<p>And me? Im just poo-pooing in the corner over here in my ruffled dresses, wondering how much cleavage I can get away with at the next daytime ball. All this talk of war is spoiling all the parties this year. Fiddle dee dee! Oh ashley, I love you!</p>\r\n<p>I am not the aspiring saint who will skip town to build shelter for brown people in order to assuage my middle class guilt, either. I have no answer for Ray Wang, and no statement that will let us all lay down again in our dream. I\'m just going to work on my muscles, and try to write the perfect love song. Someone will find a use for me.</p>'),(1947,'The human olfactory senses never cease to amaze me, to take me back to oasises of memory, to provide my brain with an unyielding <i>smell-track</i> to my life.<br><br>\r\nToday, for the first time, I took the emergency exit stairwell out of my office. A major renovation is underway in the building, and the elevators were subsequently out of service.  As I hefted the heavy, leadened steel fire-door and stepped onto the dusty, disused landing, I was greeted with a pungent, earthy smell, not pleasant, but not entirely devoid of a deep appeal. I shouldered my bag and slowly paced down the 16 flights, nostrils flared wide, nasal pasages drinking in this organo-corporate-faceless smell, trying to identify it.<br><br>\r\n<i>This smell is a picture in my past, somehow, and I can\'t place it.</i><br><br>\r\nI exited the emergency fire door onto the apron of the FDR Parkway, barely dodging a gypsy cab, a dairy truck, a city bus.  The smell, now absent from my nostrils, quickly replaced by diesel fumes, the East River stench, and the hospital dumpsters nearby, continued to linger in my brain.<br><br>\r\nAs if carried into conscious by the tepid afternoon breeze, neurons fired; the connection was ultimately made. Middle School. <i>Killebrew Gymnasium</i>. Wrestling practice. Sweat, dirty mats, stinky clothes bursting out of rusty lockers.<br><br>\r\nMy school required its students to participate in an <i>afternoon activity</i>.  As you moved up in grades, you had more choices- sports, yearbook staff, drama, music, art... but the middle-schoolers were resigned to a fate that smacked of the southern upringing that engulfed my school on every side; <i>\"we\'ll make men out of \'em yet, we\'ll make them play sports\"</i>.<br><br> \r\nAs middle-schoolers, we had very precious few options, and fewer choices at that. Fall was football or cross-country. Winter was wrestling or basketball. Spring was soccer, baseball, track or crew.  I went the route of football as I\'m not a runner by nature. I went the route of crew as I adore sleek crafts gracefully skimming inches above glassy waterways. I resolved to be a wrestler because it was the lesser of two evils: <i>I lack the co-ordination of a natural b-baller</i>.<br><br>\r\nSo middle-school wrestling practice for the 1990 season convened, and did so in the only gym that wasn\'t being used for upper-school atheletics or activities, or anything more pressing than containing i nits basement the maintainence department, and in its steamy bowels, 45 sweatily frustrated 13 year olds.  And Killebrew Gym was quite something; it defied comparison unless it was to something evil and unspeakable, perhaps akin to \"Grandpa\'s little disorder\" or \"why Mommy cries alot\". The gym was a holdout from my school\'s pre-reinvention and subsequent renovation; and on the furthest edge of campus lay this bastion of 1920\'s southern institutional brick architecture, bordered on the east by the housing projects, the phone company sub-station encroaching up to the west and south facades.<br><br>\r\nWe wrestled there, or patiently attempted to, every day amidst the cacophony.  Occasionally, a vigilant ear could discern strains of <i>2 Live Crew</i> drifting in from outside, but more often than not, we simply toiled to the hum of the phone trunks, switches clicking, transformers steaming and chuffing.<br><br>\r\nWith windows painted over and sealed shut, doorways and abandoned rooms caulked with a half-century of disused grime, Killebrew developed a certain savoury odour, again, not pleasant, but not so foul as to be offensive.  Skipper, my practice-partner for the winter of \'90 likened it to the fishery near his home on the Housatonic.  Big Will, our only heavyweight, was constantly musing that it hinted of his Grandmother\'s Mexican Casserole or his alcoholic father\'s farts.  Nick, our social-group\'s designated pervert, told us it smelled like <i>\'pussy\'</i>; none of us knew, of course, so we nodded solemnly and blushingly agreed with his esoteric insights.<br><br>\r\nSo today, I placed the smell, and rediscovered decade-old memories in the emergency stairwell of the hospital where I work.  Killebrew Gym was demolished to build a soccer field in \'91. My memory-lane stairway is being demolished next week to make way for a biomechanics research lab.  And the strangest thing is, perhaps no one but me will remember these dead, released, forgotten smells and their accompanying memories.<br><br> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1948,'<p>\r\ni think music can be dangerous. i used to listen to lots of music\r\nwith lyrics, back when i had my emotions under lock and key. \r\ni used to listen to love songs and broken heart songs and \r\nall those things. but now that i am more...me...less controlling\r\nof how i react to things, i am afraid of listening to music sometimes.\r\ni don\'t like how a track can bring me to tears on the freeway\r\nwhen i wasn\'t even thinking about how much i want want want what \r\ni can\'t have. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconversely, music can make me intensely, magnificently happy.\r\nso happy i have to stop and remember to breathe. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni treat music like a controlled substance. i cannot handle feeling\r\nso strongly too often, or for too long at a time. so i am careful\r\nwhen i listen to the tracks that i know knock me for a loop, one\r\nway or the other. i hide in my room, i pull over, i wait until\r\nno one is home. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut most of the time i listen to non-dangerous\r\nmusic. stuff that\'s nice and groovy and carries\r\nme along, stuff i can beatmatch and use as the \r\nsoundtrack to my life. silly stuff (they\r\nmight be giants, wagon christ, soul coughing), \r\ncontemplative and calming stuff (bola, tetsu inoue, vidna obmana), and so on. things that \r\ndo not tax my emotions. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n3 cups of coffee versus 2 hits of ecstasy. \r\n2 glasses of scotch versus 4 grams of psilocybin. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel foolish for being afraid. but i know\r\nmyself. and i cannot risk the consequences.\r\ni must use in moderation.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1949,'Thinking about Annie Dillard today, the role of the artist, the idea of getting soft, the idea of stepping up to the plate and doing my job, this job that I\'ve chosen or which chose me. Now I\'m going to quote her at length, because it seems prescient.</p>\r\n<p><i>There is no one but us. There is no one to send, not a clean hand, nor a pure heart on the face of the earth, nor in the earth, but only us, a generation comforting ourselves with the notion that we have come at an awkward time, that our innocent fathers are all dead--as if innocence had ever been--and our children busy and troubled, and we ourselves unfit, not yet ready, having each of us chosen wrongly, made a false start, failed, yielded to impulse and the tangled comfort of pleasures, and grown exhausted, unable to seek a thread, weak, and involved. But there is no one but us. There never has been. There have been generations which remembered, and generations which forgot; there has never been a generation of whole men and women who lived well for even one day. Yet some have imagined well, with honesty and art, the detail of such a life, and have described it with such grace, that we mistake vision for history, dream for description, and fancy that life has devolved. So. You learn this studying any history at all, especially the lives of artists and visionaries; you learn it from Emerson, who noticed that the meanness of our days is itself worth our thought.</p>\r\n<p>...What can any artist set on fire but his world?... His face is flame like a seraph\'s, lighting the kingdom of God for the people to see; his life goes up in the works; his feet are waxen and salt. He is holy and he is firm, spanning all the long gap with the length of his love, in flawed imitation of Christ on the cross stretched both ways unbroken and thorned. So must the work be also, in touch with, in touch with; spanning the gap, from here to eternity, home.</p></i>\r\n<div align=\"right\">-Annie Dillard, <i>Holy the Firm</i></div>\r\n<p>I\'m a heathen. I don\'t go to church or compare myself to nuns and martyrs like Annie does, but I am a believer. I can\'t deny, in all my Piscean longing, that there is an order, a purpose to our days, a movement from ignorance to something better. I\'ve found in my reading in the past few weeks, multiple times but most eloquently in Martha Gellhorn\'s <i>The Face of War</i>, that the subject of the perfectability of man is central to the philosophical discussion of war. We believe somehow, at this late date, that we\'re getting better at this living-together-on-earth thing. We want to think that progress has occurred, that we should be able to solve things without fighting now that it\'s the twenty-first century and we\'ve known Vietnam and Gandhi and we have a fresh generation of idealists.</p> <p>But if the next few years aren\'t very peaceful, despite anyone\'s hopes or efforts, then we in our (mostly) secular society are going to need artists to re-imagine the world for us, and to describe new ones. We will always need new ways of understanding light and space, of interpreting ideas like rage and sorrow. The art world is going to be flooded with a new energy, after we get over the initial shock. Because sure, it\'s hard to understand the importance of a silly sculpture when you\'ve been faced with calamity and devastation, but when the dust settles what do we have left but art? I don\'t go to church. I listen to music, I watch films, I read books. I go to museums and relax in the unreal world that gets created there, austere rooms with pictures on the walls, and great chaotic collages of objects. </p>\r\n<p>Others have passions that are rooted in deep, certain faith. They know a god and they know his name and they know what rules must be followed to remain in his favor. Fundamentalist Christians want damnation to all the heathens who were apparently named in the Old Testament, and fundamentalist Muslims want their political leaders to be ordained by disciples of Allah, and for their holy lands to be protected and their women to remain covered and subservient. These people have rewards ahead. Heaven, Paradise. They have meaning inherent in their days. Their children are safe and prayed for. There is a strict moral code that binds and comforts them. Anything that threatens this order must be rooted out or destroyed.</p>\r\n<p>Mine is a world of no such certainties. We live in a country full of churches, and Arab people are often astounded at this. The only America they\'ve known is the one I grew up in, the televised America of product placements and irony. In this America, the only salvation I\'ve ever known has been in art. The interpretations given of the material world by women and men who don\'t know any better but to put themselves on display, to pour their hearts onto pages or canvases in the hopes that someone might listen, look, and understand. My only moments of spiritual affirmation have been in the trance of reading, or writing. The religion of shared experience, coincidence, and image. </p>\r\n<p>Yesterday I was thinking alot about Colson Whitehead. <i>The Intuitionist</i> may be one of the last great novels of the 20th century. He imagines a film-noir world of elevator inspectors divided into two camps, the Empiricists who believe in strict rules of observation and science, and the Intuitionists who believe in their own innate ability to feel and sense a machine\'s diagnosis, and who, inexplicably, have a greater record of accuracy than their empirical foes. In a novel that some might see as a book about race, centering on a black woman struggling to save herself in a world of white men who want to destroy her, Whitehead creates a wholly original foil for the modern world. In cities that only grow upward, where elevators made this possible, the people struggle to rise as well, to progress, and still our natures divide us into two distinct communities--the empirical vs. the spiritual, science vs. belief, earth vs. sky. It\'s rare that a novelist can succeed in creating such a convincing alternate reality that speaks so eloquently to the problems in our own. All I can think is that I want to be like him. I want to have the clarity of mind to imagine my own worlds, and I want to have the perseverence to give that to other people someday. It\'s all I have to give.</p>\r\nIn a New Yorker piece about a firefighter who died in the World Trade Center, the writer noted that if you were an Irish boy growing up in Brooklyn in the 1940s, you only had three career choices: priest, policeman, or fireman. Perhaps having received the education I was lucky enough to have received, my choices are broader than most. I never considered the clergy. No one I ever met has considered the clergy. I\'ve constantly doubted whether it was important enough for me to be an artist to maybe not live that luxuriously, to never own extra houses or a boat or a nice new car. In the wake of great calamity, I\'ve sat around fairly paralyzed and wondering what the fuck good I could possibly be doing putting little thoughts into words. But then I think, I would be even more bereft if I was doing something else, if I wasn\'t putting the bulk of my industrious energies toward understanding things, interpreting them. Ever since reading Annie Dillard\'s <i>Holy the Firm</i> this has been clear: to be an artist is to be a nun, a priest. I am a missionary with no specific dogma, and you don\'t have to listen to me. And I\'ll only grow as soft as my lazy mind allows.</p>'),(1950,'<b>I.</b><br>\r\nYesterday, riding the subway home, I found myself sitting across from a girl who quite possibly had the prettiest face I\'ve ever seen; one of those rare humans, not so much on the sideline of <i>sexy</i> or <i>hot</i> as just the sum of fascinatingly pleasing to look at, which is even better, in my opinion. She didn\'t have the <i>\'look\'</i> that I typically get all fluttery-hearted for, yet for some reason, I couldnt take my eyes off of her face; it was that alluring. She looked sad and crestfallen, and perhaps it was her expressions that focused my glance in the first place, her demeanour, like potential tears, waiting.  I kept nervously anticipating her upward gaze, so that maybe I could make eye contact, eschange a smile, stave off the tears, but her visage remained locked towards the train-car floor.  She got off the train at my stop but walked the other direction, oblivious to the fact that I had been staring at her face for about 20 minutes.<br><br>\r\nLast night i had a dream that this Girl with the Beautiful Face was writing a note on my arm with my <a href=\"http://www.alescoweb.com/rhodamine6gcp.html\">rhodamine</a> pen, drawing some sort of important diagram or layout, interspersed between her flowing script. As quickly as I tried to read the note or decypher the diagram, it kept getting absorbed into my skin, smearing or getting covered by some item of my clothing. In that moment of my dream, it seemed imperative that I read the note, as if it contained some clue or cosmic tenet, but I couldnt. The Fates do this to you somtimes, you know.<br><br>\r\nI woke up, my alarm clock beeping, \"Thats life. Disappear Here. Beautiful. Face. Girl. Disappear Rhodamine. Ink.  Missed. Connection. On. The. Train, in dreams. Beep.<br><br><br>\r\n<b>II.</b><br>\r\nWe have a digital scale here at the hospital where I work. Its quite something. I weigh myself on it frequently, and marvel at its design; a simple rubber pad (like a mousepad) that you step onto.  Its even calibrated to be accurate to 1/10th of a pound.<br><br>\r\nOften are the days I weigh myself, and do most every time I pass it. It never sees use otherwise, and somtimes I find nothing sadder than expensive technology going to waste, growing obsolete while still brand-new.  Sometimes I even go drink some coffee, weigh myself again, go to the bathroom, weigh myself, etc... And I watch the fluctuations.  Theres even a special function to plot the changes. Standard deviation, average, statistics. Its amusing as all get out.<br><br> \r\nBut, I just noticed that in the past week or so, I\'ve lost almost 12 pounds; roughly more than 5% of my entire body weight. I havent even been trying. I havent been exercising more than usual, eating less, consuming any metabolic-altering substances, or doing <i>anything</i> differently.  One of my coworkers says i look tired lately, somewhat pale, and \"less strapping\".  And its true, I\'ve been getting less sleep, and do look somewhat tired.<br><br>\r\nSaid coworker thinks this is probably due to my not-so-recent breakup, and its ensuing anticlimax, lack of drama, slurped-to-the-bottom-of-the-cup relationship.<br><br>\r\nMy former-lover-still-soulmate has been coming around lately, visiting my flat alot, walking by, knocking on my window <i>\"just to say hi\"</i> and stuff. I dont know what to think; my heart and brain say two different things. My left and right brain battle it out while I try to sleep, alone.<br><br>\r\nSo, I\'m somewhat confused. Tired. Frustrated. Losing weight. Digital scale, look what youve done.<br><br>'),(1951,'<P>yeti are great people.  they listen to bluegrass and have large feet that they tap out the beat with.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the problem is that yeti are always disappearing, running off to the woods up north.  they have their reasons.  they are indeginous to the land and do not need any explanation, they come and go like the seasons.</P>\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>migrant.<BLOCKQUOTE>natural.</BLOCKQUOTE></BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>do not scare a yeti.  they fight back.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>do not take away their blue grass.  they become sad.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>do not obstruct their migrations, it is a waste of time.  </P>\r\n<P>they walk among us when they must.  they pass through our cities.  they go back into the forest.  the latin root of forest is <i>foris</i>, outside, from which we get forum.  latin <i>fores</i> is door.  for the yeti, the door is open one way or another.  on the other side are often other yetis.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the yeti is the wise folk of the woods.  they are born wise.  wise cracking.  wise knowing.  they are stubborn.  that is the way it is when growning up with more inate knowlege than the average littlefoot.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i try not to miss the yeti i know.  but it always happens that i need his wisdom and wise cracks and he is off in the woods somewhere.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am sure you understand.</P>'),(1952,'<p>The fall comes on. Makes me want an album with depth. I rush to look for an \r\n  album that will hug me. The new <a href=\"http://www.10newsongs.com/\">Leonard \r\n  cohen</a> kinda grabbed my ass, thinking I was easy. The new <a href=\"http://www.garbage.net/main.htm\">garbage</a> \r\n  licked me in places. I\'ve got a date with the new <a href=\"http://cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=500238122/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/ArtistID=KRAVITZ*LENNY/ITEMID=1431113\">Lenny</a> \r\n  K. album tonite. I wink at the new Lamb album, it winks back. To feel warm I \r\n  drive along to \"<a href=\"http://www.front.net/gtausch/news99.htm\">Mule \r\n  Variations</a>\" and \"<a href=\"http://kdlang.com/drag.html\">Drag</a>\". \r\n  Alone in my bed it\'s \"<a href=\"http://anidifranco.org/lyrics/songs.asp?display=album&title=Revelling%2FReckoning%3A%2BReckoning%2BDisc\">Reckoning</a>\" \r\n  who hugs me deeply.<br>\r\n</p>'),(1954,'<P>I\'m coming up on an age, that for me is very confusing. As my family likes to tease, the big \'30\'.</P> \r\n<P>I\'m not one of those who feels as if my life is going to end based upon my age. It\'s a number, that\'s all. One of many others that I\'ve been and none of them (aside from 16 and 21 for the obvious reasons) had any effect on me. </P>\r\n<P>The confusion comes mainly from this feeling that I\'m starting to get from people that they might be forming opinions of me, based on my age, and the fact that I\'m single and childless. I\'ve never given any thought at all to my relationship status, nor my reproductive habits; meaning that I could care less about either. If we get married, so be it. If we have a kid, great. But I look around me and see women who are younger than me who have kids. And then I have conversations with people who seem to pity me because I don\'t. Is there some kind of social timelimit that I was unaware of, that once you get to a certain age and haven\'t been married and pregnant, you\'re automatically an \'other\'? </P>\r\n<P>My god, the \"old maid\' syndrome is alive and well, and I\'ve become victim to it! Why is my worth as a female based upon the status of my womb? Used or unused... </P>'),(1953,'<pre>\r\n\r\nanthrax\r\nbombings\r\nterrorism\r\nwar\r\nanthrax\r\nbombings\r\nterrorism\r\nwar\r\n\r\ndeath\r\ndeath\r\ndeath\r\n\r\nwar\r\nterrorism\r\nbombings\r\nanthrax\r\nwar\r\nbombings\r\nterrorism\r\nanthrax\r\n\r\ndeath\r\ndeath \r\ndeath\r\n\r\nthis must stop\r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n'),(1955,'this morning my department held two \'emergency\' meetings: one to discuss the proper handling of incoming mail; the other to discuss our practice drill/evacuation that will be taking place tomorrow morning.\r\n\r\nit was made clear that the entire company would be evacuating by department. what the hell sense does that make? in a real emergency, everyone runs like chickens with their respective heads cut off -- how is leaving in groups of ten going to provide any sort of standard in evacuation abilities?\r\n\r\n**\r\n\r\nbeing suspicious is a strange sensation. i\'m pretty sure it contributes greatly to my fear of the dark. what is there to fear about the dark unless you\'re suspicious of something? i feel suspicious about most things, like my housemates purposely knocking the bathroom garbage can over in a sly attempt to get me to notice that it needs to be emptied. or my mother calling \'out of the blue\' just to \'see how i\'m doing\'. hmm.\r\n\r\nmy favorite uncle from when i was younger will be flying up from florida this weekend to visit my father. being that they are brothers, i guess that\'s not too suspicious...\r\n\r\nbut why now? why hasn\'t he visited for over seven years? why now? is something wrong? somebody\'s not telling me something. this is how suspicion is perpetuated.'),(1956,'<P>you exit the interstate, turn left into a grove, around the lake once, twice, third time\'s the CHARM. with your lady. she waves at a little boy standing in his yard beating hell out of a stump with what looks like a broken drumstick. second time around he doesn\'t wave back, just watches you. runs into the house crying on the last go-round.</P><P>\"he\'s going to go tell his mommy we\'re terrorists,\" she says.</P><P>\"let \'im tell away.\"</P><P>\'cause, as you head back into the tangelo trees on this dirt road, you\'re thinking you will not be found. \'cause it\'s 6:25, you were supposed to have been here at 6:00, everybody else who\'ll be at this little shindig is punctual as death, ergo either none of the other guests was required to perform the driving ritual (or the boy would have been non-plussed at you doing it) (and jan\'s just fucking with you) or you are going someplace not really on the property tax rolls. you don\'t know if jan\'s house would be there if you just lit out on a sunday afternoon, decided to take a little ride over there to shoot the shit with her. you would most likely get <B><I>lost</B></I> and <B><I>confused.</B></I></P><P>the sun\'s coming down. the house is made of white boards with green trim. you look at it and don\'t know how it contains all the rooms it does, but it does. two cars out front: janice\'s and sir\'s. he\'s out there on the front steps peeling something.</P><P>\"hey, bub,\" you say.</P><P>\"howdy.\"</P><P>\"what\'s REALLY goin\' on?\"</P><P>\"what\'s really goin\' on? wish i knew, jack.\"</P><P>he doesn\'t much look like the guy you met three years ago at the hub (corner of florida and zack, downtown tampa. home of the tall and strong cocktail). that guy said his name was \"willie\". played \"born in the u.s.a.\" five times on the jukebox, dancing around to it, plopping down beside you, saying that song was the story of his life, moaning about what the cia did to him in the \'nam. gave you his hotmail address, hooked you.</P><P>he goes by \"mansour\" now (probly then, too), or \"sir\", if he\'s your friend. bald on top, dreads around the fringe. wearing what look like new desert camos.</P><P>\"did you re-enlist?\"</P><P>\"yessir. just point me in the right direction.\"</P><P>\"what\'s that?\" anna says. \"a tomato?\"</P><P>\"naw. persimmon. hybrid variety. no seeds.\" he offers her a slice.</P><P>in the kitchen, jan\'s got on camoflauge, too. you see now that it\'s a joke. <B><I>joke\'s on you, whiteboy.</B></I></P><P>you look around. there are at least fifty people here. they must have taken the bus or something. none of them is wearing anything but their native garb or street clothes.</P><P>\"i didn\'t know it was a costume party.\"</P><P>\"it isn\'t. but\'choo did alright,\" she says, checking me out. \"then again, you could ride up buck nekkid, and YOU\'D still be wearin\' a costume. wouldn\'tcha?\"</P><P>you help a man make cornbread, mixing soy flour and cornmeal, water, olive oil. anna stands over by the oven adding cous-cous to chickpeas and carrots and peppers. you get hungry-hungry looking at the spinach salads with sesame seeds, the lentil and tomato soup, the racks of quail. you haven\'t eaten in three days and won\'t be eating any of this. the stereo on the shelf\'s playing dub remixes of the clash, joe strummer rapping, bless his heart: \"Luther King and Mahatma Gandhi / Went to the park to check on the game /</P><P>But they was murdered by the other team.\"</P><P>in the white-plastered great room, little tables are dressed for dinner. people circulate, drinking from jan\'s university of georgia bulldog tumblers. you look behind the bar: two cases of bottles with no labels (probly anejo rum). also orange and grapefruit juice, tonic. you aren\'t drinking tonight, just curious: possessed of a natural curiousity and a natural thirst, you are. and a natural something else, before long.</P><P>eight women and men wearing white robes smoke out of an airless bong on the porch. the men wear skullcaps, the women\'s heads are uncovered. you go over and open the french door a crack.</P><P>\"hey, save me a little for later on.\"</P><P>one of the women nods. anna slips out there with them. she looks back at me. \"ha, ha,\" she taunts. at dinner, you watch her get her eat on.</P><P>\"is that good?\"</P><P>\"mattie, you just don\'t know.\"</P><P>\"i hope it is, \'cause you got barbecue sauce from ear to ear.\"</P><P>you\'re seated across from a woman who you think is an emissary from the Hopi nation. she\'s talking to the man next to her in german. you sit there sipping water, catching the occaisonal phrase. he says something about \"im ddr ... geboren.\" funny, but he doesn\'t look like he was \"geboren\" in the ddr or anywhere else. he appears to have been forged in olympic fires. he is too perfect. beautiful to the point of absurdity, of ugliness. like somebody told him to dream himself a body and this is what he came up with. like his animal/god ratio is too skewed toward the latter. you look at him, and some hebrew letters flash into your mind. you don\'t know hebrew, but you know the letters spell \"mossad\". the moment you intuit this, the word \"stasi\" superimposes itself over the other. one word\'s in red, the other blue. where they overlap, the writing turns purple.</P><P>in the green room, you get dressed. one adult diaper, made of whatever material they make those old-fashioned baby diapers from, but thicker. white terrycloth jumpsuit, loosefitting, no shoes. there\'s a tv in the corner playing an episode of the ed sullivan show. looks like it\'s from the late sixties. intense color saturation that is oddly calming. muppets singing \"monomenot....doot doo di doo doo.\" the furry one winks at you. you wink at anna. she winks back. soon, she will hold your hand. you will hold hers. you will hold hands and dance. but first, the horse must relax, get his head into the right space. anna is not a horse, but she dances. there is another horse here, in another room. judy. you saw her before dinner, hanging around the piano by herself.</P><P>on ed sullivan, there are some jugglers, a comedian, neil diamond looking about twenty. you wonder what became of these people who didn\'t \"make it,\" the young girl singer in particular. does she have a tape of this show in her walk-up apartment in brooklyn? get drunk every once in awhile and watch it, maybe laugh, maybe cry?</P><P>you listen to some music after the tape runs out. happy songs, love stuff: \"boy in the bubble,\" \"the kids are alright\", \"hot fun in the summertime,\" \"both sides now,\" \"lovely day,\" \"another girl, another planet,\" \"all the way from memphis,\" \"the rover,\" \"sweet jane,\" \"rave on,\" \"beautiful day,\" \"natural woman,\" \"alex chilton,\" \"let\'s spend the night together,\" \"there is a light that never goes out,\" \"can\'t hardly wait\". the cd player clicks off. you pick up the phone.</P><P>sir pops his head in the door. \"you ready? we gonna fill y\'ass fulla candy \'n pretend you a pinata.\" you laugh. this is a little test to see if you\'re in a good mood. if not, you have to try again. you tried it once stoned, pissed off, scared to death. very nearly to death. a pleasant time was not had by anyone.</P><P>all the tables have been moved onto the porch. the very oldest women and men sit closest to the inside of the circle, tapping staves on the parquet floor in unison. the langpa oscillates quietly. conga players kick up a fuss with syncopated paradiddles, then everybody starts singing or chanting \"[REDACTED].\" in the corner, a guy who looks like kurtis blow is giving it hell.</P><P>first the whirling. then the rhythym changes, and all at once it feels like the top of your head is being unwound in a spiral, like an orange being peeled or a mummy unwrapped. you disappear.</P><P>the void is not the void that you can imagine. for instance, what does a blind man see? not blackness, not a night sky with no stars. the senses are gone. after a second, to avoid further dissolution, to hold you there at the edge of space, you hear al green\'s voice singing \"let\'s stay together.\" like a placeholder, like a patient on saline solution to keep the vein open, keep them hanging on. inside the song, you have no idea how long it takes to conclude, fade out.</P><P>hardness. laying on something hard. then, all of a sudden, cold and wet. you scream like a woman. open your eyes. sir stands over you with a garden hose. you look around: you\'re laying on a cistern, wrapped in a sheet. something stinks.</P><P>\"something stinks.\"</P><P>\"you stink. you the stinkin\' mothuhfucka. nothin\' but you.\"</P><P>\"i mess myself?\"</P><P>\"how you got so much shit in you, matt? just tell me. whisper it right here in my little ear.\"</P><P>\"oh, me.\"</P><P>\"what is it?\"</P><P>\"i got a number 99 excedrin headache.\"</P><P>\"just be glad you don\'t got a number one hundred headache.\"</P><P>\"what\'s that?\"</P><P>\"thass where you don\'t got a head anymore. there\'s some of \'em wanted to do that a long time ago.\"</P><P>\"who? terry and them? tell \'em anytime they think they\'re men enough.\"</P><P>he laughs.</P><P>\"so how\'d we do?\" you ask. \"anything good tonight?\"</P><P>\"ah, shit. i dunno. could go either way. got a few addresses in st. pete.\"</P><P>\"sendin\' some wetboys over there?\"</P><P>\"yeah. terry \'n a couple others. explain to them the error uh their ways.\"</P><P>\"then the knife?\"</P><P>\"well...  that\'s not my department.\"</P><P>in the kitchen, jan and sir, judy, anna and I sit around the table doing rum shooters, getting blunted, eating potato salad and grapes. everybody else is gone. judy\'s laughing. JO 101.5 on the radio. \"jammin\' hits of the seventies.\" you hear all the songs that have been sampled. \"the beat goes on\" comes on.</P><P>judy says, \"isn\'t that from a will smith song?\"</P><P>jan: \"no. other way around. that millenium song of his. he was trying to magick the world into not ending. if he knew it or not.\"</P><P>judy: \"kinda like the anti-\"1999\", huh?\"</P><P>when we get home, i\'m going to stand out by the road awhile, watch the cars go by. you can see the lights come over the hill then watch them recede down into the tunnel of trees, their taillights throwing a circle of light around the car onto the trees and road. it will be wednesday morning.</P><P>anna wants to drive home.</P><P>i say, \"i hope you know you don\'t have anything resembling a license.\"</P><P>\"put your drunk ass in this car before it gets beaten.\"</P><P>\"aw, you\'re gonna beat Car? c\'mon. don\'t beat Car.  i love Car.\"</P><P>i hold my head out the window like a dog, feel the air on my face, watch the interstate loveliness go by.</P><P>anna: \"you\'re funny. inside of you, i mean.\"</P><P>\"i don\'t know what you\'re talking about.\"</P><P>\"when you\'re under.\"</P><P>\"don\'t tell me. i don\'t want to know anything about it.\"</P><P>\"go to sleep.\"</P><P>\"ok, wake me up when we get there.\"</P>\r\n'),(1957,'<p>\r\nlast night i was sitting in math class and had to fart. not just one of those little bubbles, where you can squeeze your cheeks and it rolls back into your stomach. this was a big one and was rolling right on out whether i wanted it to or not. so i squeezed my cheeks, trying to keep it from squeaking or flapping on the way out. my teacher kept lecturing and writing theories on the board.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsweat from strain and tension began to break out on my arms. it started out and thank the stars it was silent. oh, thank anything and everything that may be looking down over me. it was silent and just flowing on out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter about half the air had evecuated my bowels, i got a smell of it. the only thing i can put it close to is the smell of my old roommate\'s cat\'s litter box. but that wasn\'t the worst of it. the guy sitting next to me, in direct line of my slightly tilted butt, was starting to smell the putridness and looking around. i tried to stop. i tried to plug my butt back up and i think that was the biggest mistake of this whole fiasco.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso i tried to stop and just as my sphincter was closing, something non gaseous slipped out. and not only was it just something, it was the beginning of everything that was behind that big air bubble. see, as i figure, i have this big bubble and behind the bubble is a lot of poop. i try to push the bubble back, it goes around the poop, finds there is no room, and pushes the poop out. or something like that.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso i ened up sitting in my chair filling my pants up with warm poop that smells a lot like a litter box. panic overtook me. the smell was horrible. i jumped up, grabbed all my stuff and ran. i put my sweatshirt down on my seat and drove the half hour home sitting in my own filth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni haven\'t been back to school yet. i don\'t think that i can go back. at least not there. everyone had to have known. i\'m sure i lost some on the floor on the way out. baggy pants with boxers. come on, there isn\'t a whole lot there to catch anything on the way down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsigh...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nokay, so this really didn\'t happen. but, when i am sitting in class, and my stomach gurgles with air just trying to get out, it does make me a bit nervous and then thoughts like this start bouncing around my head. maybe this will give you second thoughts about farting in public too.\r\n</p>'),(1958,'I was born in a small town, its four-digits of population quite quaint compared to these twenty-two square miles of structural steel, noise and filth. This city makes you pensive, thoughtful about your past, inquisitive about your future, enamored with your present. Here, I do alot of thinking.  I wake and ride the train, everyone quiet in their half-sleep while I ponder and I ruminate. I consider a variety of things.<br><br>\r\nIs it possible to be in love with fifty thousand buildings, ten million works of art, patches of wheat-grass in store fronts?  Can one read passion from the reflecting, refracting, gleaming street-side slabs of glass? Concrete, cars, stampeding trains, noisy piers; can one have an affair with such things?<br><br>\r\nI find myself seduced by five-and-a-half million people all at once, and if this city has a pulse, I\'m determined to synchronize my watch to its beat. Day by day by day, I\'m hopelessly struck by the massiveness of this place, its physical space, its spirits and its people.<br><br> \r\nThe city\'s buildings are so grand and loom as if from some maniacal architect\'s dream. They bear their sign towers, billboards and winking neon like spectacles on the faces of amiable strangers who pass on the streets.  Sometimes, they overwhelm me. Sometimes, they\'re unassuming, grinning at me with a smile as warm and bristly as a welcome mat; sometimes they\'re closed doors, turned up collars.  Sometimes they\'re cold.<br><br>\r\nThis is an interesting place to exist, as I\'m not sure how fully one can <i>thrive</i> here, as a blip among a countless sea of faces. Yet I take it all in stride; existing here is as good as thriving elsewhere. Anonymity is a mixed blessing, of sorts, freeing me to roam as I please. But, I am dumbstruck at how easily I could fade into the crowds, this town, and absolutely disappear.<br><br> \r\nAnd yet I try sometimes. This is the City of Show and Tell.'),(1959,'that was supposed to be my title of my last submitted piece. but, my thumbs were too quick and off it went. please mod this one. but if you can, please change my title. cause as it stands, the title of the first just doesn\'t have a damn thing to do with the body.'),(1960,'Tamarind Candy<br>\r\nKeo Me<br>\r\ning: tamarind, sugar, salt<br>\r\nthan pah me, duong, muoi<br>\r\npacked for dong buh<br>\r\nWing Ho IND, Part<br><br>\r\n\r\nProduct of Thailand/San Pham Thailande<br>\r\nNet WT/Trong Luong NET: 3.5 oz.<br>\r\n3.5<br><br>\r\n\r\nPHAYANAK BRAND<br>'),(1961,'<P>i named my motorcycle donkey today because it would not start.  the battery is dead.  fucker.  the key was in the ignition a prolonged period of time causing the battery to run down.  i pulled the battery out and put it in the garage with the charger plugged up to it over night.  the battery then went back in the bike.  and it is charged, but not enough.  the spark plugs clicked away but the pistons would not turn over.</P>\r\n<P>\"donkey.\" <BR>*cl-cl-cl-cl-cl-ick*</P>\r\n<P>\"start donkey.\"<BR> *cl-cl-cl-cl-cl-ick*</P>\r\n<P>\"DONKEY START! NOW!\"<BR>  *cl-cl-cl-cl-cl-ick*</P>\r\n<P><FONT SIZE=5>\"GODDAMIT DONKEY ! ! ! \"</FONT>  <BR>*cl-cl-cl-cl-cl-ick*</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then the neighbor came out and said can i help you? as neighbors ask when they think you are a burlar.</P>\r\n<P>I LIVE HERE! i shouted.  i was mad.  piss off.  then i calmed down.  i asked where dan and xepmom were.  they had not been seen for a day, but the danvan was always parked outside.  no dan though.  she said oregon.  oh.  well, call the cops back and tell them not to pick me up, ok?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>oh look, i have the key to dan\'s bike.  it is a little donkey.  it starts too.  all donkeys 1972 and previous use the same key.   i could go to kmart down the street and get a motorcycle jump cable.  then i remembered i only have one donkey key.  both donkeys must have keys to start the sick donkey.  this sucks donkey dicks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then i went back to work.  no donkey rides today.</P>'),(1962,'<p>It\'s a very nice day out today.  It smells like Autumn on the breeze and the breeze is plentiful today.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m sitting at a table, outside of work, enjoying the traffic, a nice pita and a large, black good morning mug of coffee.  It may or may not be afternoon, but I\'m trying not to worry myself with details.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m playfully shirking my responsibilities right now.  I should be at home working on any number of things on my scholastic to-do list but instead, I am taking a break and enjoying being outdoors for a while.</p>\r\n<p>Soon, it will be too climatically inconvenient to sit outside on a metal chair.  In fact, there will be no metal chairs to speak of.  They will all have gone into hibernation for the winter, to reappear, again, with nature in the spring.</p>\r\n<p>Last week I had the pleasure of meeting <a href=\"http://www.coupland.com/\" target=\"new\">Douglas Coupland</a> when he did a reading/signing at my school in support of his new novel <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1582341656/qid%3D1003533480/ref%3Dsr%5F11%5F0%5F1/107-6384642-8658943\" target=\"new\">All Families Are Psychotic</a>.  He was refreshingly pleasent, even though under the influence of weird sleeping pills and his new book was great.  Matt and I were joking around with him about mullet wigs and us being geniuses.  It was good times.  Oh, I also went and saw Bj&ouml;rk the day before that.  So last week officially was amazing.</p>\r\n<p>Right now I find my mind with trying to figure out how efficient a device my ass is for thermal transfer with this cold chair.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t think my ass would be a very good heat sink, but I hope never to find out.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s time to go home and optimize my assignment for higher efficiency while running in multi-threaded mode on SMP systems.</p>\r\n<p>Joy.</p>\r\n'),(1963,'<p>car stalled<br>\r\nfaded<br>\r\nlike the day<br>\r\nwords come<br>\r\nand words<br>\r\ngo</p>\r\n\r\n<p>memories between<br>\r\n\r\n<p>inside skin<br>\r\nblind heart<br>\r\nwolf eyes</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>hunting . . . the dream</p>'),(1964,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nexample number one:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni am standing at colma bar and i am smoking a cigarette. waiting waiting waiting. waiting and eventually i look at the ground. smoking a cigarette and looking at the ground, thinking. thinking, and my feet hurt, and thinking, maybe i should sit.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nclearing the ground with my foot, moving papers and things and i sit. thankfully today no chicken bones, yesterday there were chicken bones. but today, no chicken bones, just paper.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nanyway, so, sitting on the ground and i finish my cigarette and i am waiting.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwaiting (no smoking), and eventually this guy rolls up. this guy, he rolls up in a motorized wheelchair of thing. rolls up with a vroom of a lawnmower, and he is waiting too.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe are waiting together, him in his lawnmower with padded seat and me, on the ground. and the ground is hard!</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nconcrete and cold dewy at seven thirty. and hard. and my tailbone hurts.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nyeah, so. the tailbone and the hurting, and this guy with the padded lawnmower. and i\'m staring at him and i think, \'you know, that guy\'s lucky- cause anytime he wants to sit down, he can.\' this is what i say. to myself. exactly.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe thing that gets me is the word \'lucky.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nexample number two:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni am riding with dug and jen. jen is driving and we are going to jack in the box for dinner. driving and riding, and we are listening to a cd that jen has recently made. it is a cd filled with overplayed radio hits that everyone knows all the words to, and that everyone has heard a million times.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthey are good songs. good songs but overplayed songs. they are songs that jen likes and likes to sing along to.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ntrack seven is cake\'s version of i will survive and to pull jen\'s chain i say, \'wow jen, could you have picked a better know song than i will survive? maybe the cake theme song?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ndug turns around, contemplative. turns around and contemplates two seconds and decides i am an idiot. dug smiles after two seconds contemplative, and says (stone faced), \'you are an idiot.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni am an idiot.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut i love dug solely for knowing what i meant after only two seconds.</p>'),(1966,'<pre>\r\n\r\n                                                                   \r\n                                 ____                              \r\n                                :    :                             \r\n            ___                 :____:                             \r\n    ___ --- ~~ /---___         :  []:                             \r\n        ~ ~ /~~~/~~~/     ----,-------    I\'M GOING TO FUCK THIS\r\n  ,\' ~~ ~~ / ~~/~~~/ `,     ,\'  0 0 __    SULTRY LITTLE PUMPKIN \r\n -_~~    ,------,  / ~ _`    ;    _____  /                      \r\n;   - _ ,\'^^^^^^^ \"\"`,_-     `, `--\'; u                          \r\n; ~~   ,\'^^^^-----------   /   ,\'`,,,\'                             \r\n;~ ---, ^^^,`__----,  ..`,/  ,\'..,\'                                \r\n`,  ~ ,^^ <_\'__--__ `, .. `,/ .. `                                 \r\n `,---` ^<________--  `, .. ..  ,\'     ___ [] ___    MADRE DE DIOS \r\n  `,---` <__ -__ ___  ,\' .. . ,`     _/   )(/   _ /              \r\n   `, --` <__ __ _  ,\' ... _,`      /   /                        \r\n    `--,___<___   ,\'`-___,\'       ,\'   :   |        `,             \r\n            <___,\'(||)            :             :    :             \r\n              ||   ||             :    :   |         ;             \r\n            __||_ _||_            _            :   _/             \r\n           // ;;\\ ;;\\             _    |   /  _/               \r\n                                      __________/                 \r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(1965,'<P>\r\nthe bent yellows. the crisp dry oranges. reds popping in and out of view. the wind was not so bad the other day. the autumn garden season is dictated by wind most of the time. the wind can make a day too cold to work outside. the wind can make the task almost impossible. four bags i filled respectfully. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo any of you pet your cats while you are sitting on the john dropping the kids off at school? this morning was the first time i dabbled in the act. my cat broke the seal and entered the white tile room. he\'s been talking a lot. I think he wants me to let him outside. ive been reading a good book on freedom and on the whole, i dont think my cat is repressed. i think the outside is too dangerous for him. While i know that he should be able to experience life for himself, i know the harsh reality. ive seen the rotting meat on the side of the road. the splatter of skunk brain and racoon hide. to release my cat into that environment wouldnt, in my view, be responsible. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe trip to the home depot isnt complete without the credit card. this place is a stay home dad/gardeners heaven, which means that ill love it even more when im a stay home dad. ailes and ailes of fancy shmancy tools and \"revolutionary\" home and garden ideas. the possibilites are endless seeing as how most of the product they sell is configurable in some way. heh! sorta like windows 95! \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy mother has a picture of prince william in the laundry room. its from HELLO. that magazine that \"reports\" on all the blue blood activities. it even reports on down through the royal hierachy. earls and dukes. regents and lords. its crazy. there are around three copies(rotated monthly) of HELLO in the bathroom magazine basket. i had to smuggle some world press review up there the other day. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(1967,'<P> i draw lines in crayon<BR>\r\non the walls of the brick house<BR>\r\ni saw it in my dream<BR>\r\nbut it wasn\'t what it seemed<BR>\r\nthe glass walls were painted brown </P>\r\n\r\n<P> the beer was shit<BR>\r\nbut something about it was fantastic<BR>\r\norgasmic, fantastic, and strong<BR>\r\nmade me feel like i belonged </P>\r\n\r\n<P> but what about the bottle<BR>\r\nonce we\'re done with all the water<BR>\r\nwhen the band goes home<BR>\r\nwe\'re all alone<BR>\r\nand there\'s nothing left to dance to </P>'),(1968,'So I\'ve decided that I need to move to Italy. I\'m thinking I\'ll take some Italian courses and then just go there and maybe work and just fuck around and see these insane white washed buildings and cathedrals and nuns and just fucken italy man. Drink some red wine, have sex with dirty italian men, eat fresh pasta and warm fucken bread man. I have no idea where this is coming from but at least I have a goal in life now. What do you guys think?'),(1969,'TEST TESTTESTTES  TTESTT ESTTESTTE   TTESTTESTTE STTES TTETTEST TTESTTESTTES TTESTTESTTES TTE  TESTTESTTEST TESTTES TTETTESTT ESTTESTTESTTE STTES  TTETTESTTESTT ESTTESTTESTTE STTETTESTTEST TESTTESTTESTTE STTETTESTTEST TESTTEST TESTT ESTTE'),(1970,'asdfasdfasdfasdf'),(1971,'<p>monday morning, time to check voice mail from the weekend, hoping to hear about work. Instead, a mysterious message from a friend in other regions of this porny bear republic.</p>\r\n<p>\"I have one word for you. Are you ready? One word. girth. yes, girth. I\'m telling you. girth. Do you hear me? girth.\"</p>\r\n<p>I know what she means. I don\'t know if you know what she means, but you might be able to guess. When a person calls to leave a message containing only the word \"girth\", you must recall childhood memories of the first corn dog at a red sox game and how it was too wide to fit in your mouth, or perhaps your fat friend who couldn\'t fit through the underground sewage tunnels you used to sneak into. His flesh was just a bit too voluminous to squeeze through that body-size cylinder. Or maybe your first experience drilling into concrete, when you ground a hole too big with your oversized bit, crippling and shredding the porous masonry in your wake, leaving an opening too large to snugly hold the screw.</p>\r\n<p> Yes, girth. My boyfriend from high school got fat in college, and I fucked him again years later. I tried to explain that he was even sexier with the extra mass. I used the word \"girth\".</p>\r\n<p> Mounds of earth have girth to hold the evidence of our naughty consumption. When I wear a tight bodiced dress my hips dribble on the side with girth like ice cream melting off the sides of a cone. Venus at Willendorf\'s girth conceived the birth of our goddess-fearing ways.</p>\r\n<p>My sculpture teacher explained to me what a lingam is. You know what it is right. It\'s the opposite of yoni. She used to comment that my forms were a balance of lingam and yoni, the forward shooting rocket into a round and devouring black hole. Worshipful is the rocket with girth. Girth of the earth.</p>\r\n<p>We are living in thin times. Thin beauty, thin fashion, thin computer hard drives, thin bank accounts. But girth, my friends, makes the girl you want come back, again and again.</p>'),(1972,'<p>Grillo gets five calls a day from his 17yr old girlfriend that is 1200 miles away. Thatcher and I explain to him that this is ill. Problem is not that he talks to her, or that he is maintaining a long distance relationship, but that when it is time to drink the wine - its time to drink the wine. Two Benjamin?s down on a fine homemade meal, bottles of wine, and good music playing doesn\'t call for spending the rest of the evening on the phone. \r\n</p><p>\r\nEating cake, any type of cake, at a cafe alone is like admitting to the world that you are depressed and lonely. Do not eat cake alone, especially at a cafe. Going to movies alone is sometimes ok, as long as you are going to see something along the lines of Tigerland, or Apocalypse Now. If you go to see the latest John Cusak movie you might as well go to a cafe afterwards and eat cake alone.  Eating cake in a cafe is pathetic, you will get no sympathy there. Go to the bar, order whiskey; this is therapy.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nListening to music is considered to be therapeutic. If you are going to listen to the blues, you had better understand the blues. Don\'t just be blue, listen to what is being told - and by God, follow that man\'s advice. If I hear that someone hasn\'t followed these rules, I get angry; it is written man, it is in the songs! All you need to know about a woman is in the blues. Don\'t fuck this up.\r\n</p><p>\r\nTake a deep breath, live within these moments, exhale the poison and the little devils in your head. In the \"meantime\" of a relationship - point out your fellow brothers that have gone down the highway of love - acknowledge the road less traveled by you and your remaining brothers. \r\n\r\nThe Christmas Sweater shall be remembered. When a man succumbs to the Christmas Sweater, he is like a wounded soldier. We men must stick together at times such as these, explain to them what the sweater symbolizes. Christmas Sweaters worn by your fallen brothers can be a major topic of conversation; this conversation alone can last up to 2 bottles of  red. Whatever you do man, do not consult with the other sex about these things, they will only fill your head with words of hope, and they might try to take advantage of you during this time. Remember that you too can be vulnerable.  This can be very detracting. \r\n</p><p>\r\nOne last point to note, when you tell her that it is over, it is over - and you can?t go back on your word. Even the week after, no matter how good it was. I am reminded of the story of the guy somewhere in Columbia. He breaks up with his woman, and a few months later she comes to his door, dressed for the part, and proceeds to advance the man, sexually. He is fine with this, it was good, just as good as it always had been. The next morning she kisses him, and says that she must go. He is proud of his achievement, until the following month when she calls him on the phone. She asks how he liked his present, he is thinking about the sex and tells her that it was wonderful. Then she tells him that he needs to go to the doctor. His present, herpes for life.\r\n</p><p>If you are going to fight in the war, you had better be drafted. Enlisting in the war, you will just find yourself wearing a Christmas Sweater eatin cake at startbucks.</p><p>\r\n<i>\r\nDo not think about tomorrow, let tomorrow come and go.<br> Tonight, you got a nice warm box car, safe from all this wind and snow. </i><br>-- Woodie Guthrie.</p><p>\r\n'),(1973,'<P>it is eight thirty in the morning.  all last night i dreamed vividly.  having sex and going on vacation.  nothing unusual about the sex except that i think it involved my motorcycle as a prop and doggystyle.\r\n\r\n<P>then i dreamed about the new american aristocracy going on vacation and how the recommended items to bring were enunciated.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>two suit cases with specific items such as: (1) american flag made of nice material.  (1) pair white tennis shoes. (2) slacks.  (4) white underwear.  and so on.  it was like the list for summer camp.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then i woke up and did tai chi for the first time in a about a week.  i think the strain in my neck is back.  at one point i was watching the sky and trees move through the space between my fingers when i remembered the <i>archer pose</i>.  i had not done the archer outside of the one tai chi class that i went to.  i had forgotten about it.  then i remembered all of the sudden.  it is strange what hides in the brain.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>perhaps it was released bye one of the other stretches?  some one told me that stretching out the spine can release emotions and memories, sometimes moving one to tears.  i think today it released my archer.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>note: it is 15 miles to work from home.</P>'),(1974,'<p>I recently found out that, a few weeks ago, I spent the better part of a Saturday afternoon sitting a few feet away from a bomb in the Philadelphia bus station. According to the news, it had been put in a locker right next to where I was sitting the morning earlier, and would\'ve destroyed the entire station had it been detonated.</p>\r\n<p>I wish I had known. I was very, very bored.</p>\r\n<p>On my way to the bus station I stopped by a public square where some kind of black church/born-again thing was going on. I asked a lady what was going on. She told me about revelation and how we had to accept Jesus if we wanted to be saved. I shook her hand. I told her I might be back later.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m pretty glad I didn\'t get to meet \"The Jeeze\".</p>\r\n<p>I was hoping that discovering this would make current events, AMERICA ON ALERT ETC. more relevant to me, more understandable to me, but it hasn\'t. It still feels like a statistical improbability.</p>\r\n<p>I think I\'ve been really out of things in general.</p>\r\n'),(1975,'yes, all of your dreams are about to come true. i know, i know, it\'s what you\'ve \r\nall been waiting for your entire lives--the 2002 swinney.org calendar. but wait, \r\nthat\'s not all. i need your help. actually, i need anyone out there that wants \r\nto be a part of this monumental occurrence, not to mention future collector\'s \r\nitem (20 years from now you\'ll be able to sell it on eBay for big bucks and send \r\nyour kids to college or pay off your debts or have hookers for life), to follow \r\nthe following directions: \r\n<p> <b>what to submit: </b>\r\n<p> one black & white photo (jpeg or gif) around 720 x 504 pixels (10\" x 7\" print \r\n  size) of yourself or any adaptation of what you think \"self\" might \r\n  mean.\r\n<p> your name (on swinney.org or real name, whatever you prefer). this who you \r\n  are. who you be. what you are called.\r\n<p> your measurements--ankles, cranium, length of tongue, do you have a finger \r\n  toe? your biceps when flexed. whatever you want.\r\n<p> your location. this is important. you want to represent.\r\n<p>your birthday or any purposed holiday, besides october 2nd.\r\n<p>any advice you\'d like to give to new swinneys. \r\n<p> any ideas you have for this project would be much appreciated, as I am not \r\n  exactly sure what\'s going to happen. you can send your submissions to me at \r\n  my pod <a href=\"mailto:jfilan@earthlink.net \">e-mail address</a>. if you\'d like \r\n  to upload your photo and info somewhere, please send me the link. that would be great.\r\n<p>i\'d like to get this started in the next couple of weeks. swinney says i should \r\n  only accept the best submissions and if you have a problem w/ that, he says, \r\n  \"well, they should go to sears and get their portrait taken. this is a \r\n  high class operation. we don\'t fuck around.\" \r\n<p>by the way, i\'ll probably do a run of about 100-150. they will all be numbered \r\n  and signed by OMS.'),(1976,'<p>I really want to say something incredibly witty here, but I don\'t always have cute, interesting things to say.  I\'m not all that witty.  I don\'t always say just the right thing at just the right time.  Why can\'t life be more like a movie?  Where\'s my soundtrack?  Is that too much to ask?</p><p>It\'s hard to avoid the bleeding of unreality into reality.  TV... movies... someone once told me that your subconscious believes everything it sees/hears.  <i>Are we dissatisfied with our lives?</i>  Well, I think we all believe a lot of things that aren\'t true.  Not cerberally of course, we\'re all too smart for that, but viscerally.  In our gut, we feel like something just isn\'t quite right.  Check out this guy\'s <a href=\"http://www.vomitus.com/museum/rants/statment_0xxx02.html\">rant</a>.</p><p>Yes, I am yearning.  It\'s a very non-specific yearning, a quest for a grail that I have no knowledge or understanding of.  Most nights, I find myself sitting on my roof, smoking a cigarette, staring at the city, asking it to tell me what I want.  That\'s what I want, to know what I want, so I can start pursuing it.</p><p>I went to my first rich man\'s party and had my first ride in a limo a week ago.  They invited me along in order to have my girlfriend\'s body around to stare at and try to seduce.  So, there I was in a ridiculous Manhattan penthouse, coming down off my drugs, staring at all the pretentious people.  My girlfriend had a great time hanging out on the balcony having some guy stuff coke up her nose trying desperately get her naked while I sat on a massive leather couch listening to some guy go on and on <b>and on</b> about how Glenn Close just moved in next door to him.  <i>This is fun?</i>  Somehow, the movies seem to paint a much more glamorous picture of this kind of life.  The shallow hollowness... no... <i>utter vacuousness</i> of these people left me feeling defeated.  An entire culture predicated on sex and drugs, on feeling good <b>right now</b> without regard to any consequence.  What are these people going to be doing in ten years?  Still partying?  Living each day as if it is your last is a nice sentiment, but doing so would certainly cause imminent death.</p><p>As I have more experiences, I learn more of what I don\'t want.  I learn that my fantasies are just that; reality ruins dreams.</p>'),(1977,'i\'m going to write it down, not think it out.\r\n'),(1978,'i\'m going to write it out, not think it out. \r\nenjoy. '),(1979,'<p>\r\nearlier this week my DSL futzed. first there was a power outage (omigod! it\'s the terrorists! they\'ve taken away my tv and my internet! what am i gonna do!) but when the power finally came back on my DSL was fucked.<br>\r\nthe only thing that worked perfectly was aim. as for the web, the only thing i could access was swinney.org. for over two days: aim, no mail, no icb, and swinney.org. nothing else. i couldn\'t go to any other site. just here.<br>\r\nwhy? i resented swinney.org. why was it the only thing i could get to? it was tormenting me. haunting me.<br>\r\nwhere does this power come from?</P>'),(1980,'<p>Iâ€™m alienated and Iâ€™m mearly sad about it, instead of mad.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"The distinguishing features are a profound painful dejection, cessation of interest in all outside world, loss of capacity to love, inhibition of all activity and a lowering of the self-regarding feelings.\" s.f.\r\n</p>'),(1981,'<p>So, me and LinearB run this <a href=\"http://www.geekaustin.org\">site</a>.  It\'s for geeks (mostly) in austin.  We\'re having a little get together this saturday the 27th from 6pm to whenever at Mojo\'s on the drag.  I figured some of the austin-based swinneyites might want to come chill too (b33r!).  So consider y\'allself invited, ya hear?</p>'),(1982,'<p>I am now listening to the last track, <i>Sunrays</i>, off this amazing new <a href=\"http://www.warprecords.com/\" target=\"new\">Warp Records</a> release by The Other People Place titled <a href=\"http://www.warprecords.com/frameset.php?section=/mart/music/release.php?cat=WARP90\" target=\"new\">Lifestyles Of The Laptop Cafe</a>.  It\'s been a while since something of this nature was released on Warp and is a total audio complement to Black Dog Productions\' <a href=\"http://www.warprecords.com/frameset.php?section=/mart/music/release.php?cat=WARP8\" target=\"new\">Bytes</a>.  <i>Bytes</i> is one of most favourite albums (if not number one) and is hands down my favourite Warp release.  So when I hear something that sounds similar to said album, I can\'t help but have my heart skip a beat or two.</p>\r\n<p>This is a perfect soundtrack for the work I have to do right now.  It\'s a perfect soundtrack for pretty much anything.  Go buy it, or download it.  Or something, just get it.</p>\r\n<p>Oh, and <a href=\"http://www.mpi-agency.com/djpatife.htm\" target=\"new\">DJ Patife</a> is changing the drum\'n\'bass scene in amazing ways.  His Cool Steps Drum\'n\'Bass Grooves mix disc just might be the best d\'n\'b mix I\'ve ever heard.  Bigups ya Brasil stylez!</p>\r\n'),(1983,'<p>the fog hasn\'t made it in this far tonight<br>\r\nsometimes it does tho<br>\r\ni like that<br>\r\nbut i like the moon too</p>\r\n<p>today i drove home from work the long way<br>\r\n152 to Watsonville<br>\r\ni stopped on Mount Madonna<br>\r\ni watched crows glide<br>\r\nand curled up into the leopard stance<br>\r\non the edge of the cliff</p>\r\n<p>i didn\'t want to come home<br>\r\nbut i love it here<br>\r\nand i am alone</p>\r\n<p>its the ocean and the smell of green that brought me back<br>\r\nand the way to sun falls like rain</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p>i was born to it<br>\r\nLos Gatos</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p><i>~kat</i></p>\r\n<p>...i ate nothing but hot french bread with butter for dinner</p>'),(1984,'<p>i like myself the best when i have just gotten out of the shower, and I\'m in the middle of dressing. When I catch a glimpse of myself, I see how fragile I am, my ribs are showing, and I have messy hair. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>I have trouble defining my identity. I am always thinking up something that feels like it\'s true. It usually contradicts something I just said, or meant to say. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> I feel like I know myself when I am still a little wet, with little droplets dripping down my temples, or the inside of my thighs. This is when I feel like I could conquer the entire world, or at least my small portion of it. I am identified by the feeling running over me as if I were entirely made of sand, and every inch of my was taking on a new presence when wetted.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I feel safe here, because I can conceal my identity. It is raining today, and as I walk to work I will think of my new sense of security.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1985,'<p>cardboard universe<br>\r\nmiss alf beauty pageant<br>\r\nyou only like to do the dishes<br>\r\nuntil they fill the rack<br>\r\ntake off your glasses before nap</p>\r\n<p>it\'s saturday<br>\r\nI\'m full of bread products<br>\r\nand your cock.</p>\r\n<p>she\'s my private dancer<br>\r\nthe men are all the same<br>\r\nwe trade cliches about boys<br>\r\nbrainstorm costumes<br>\r\nand go back to our rooms.<br>\r\nyou wanna see me do the shimmy again?</p>\r\n<p>what\'s so funny<br>\r\nget your gutteral laff off my back<br>\r\nyou cant mock me, kiss up on my ear<br>\r\nand then write on my web site.</p>\r\n<p>my smug love say<br>\r\nyou can\'t get up in deez nutz<br>\r\ndeez nutz get up in you.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(1986,'<p>The car is wrecked, front end bashed in but radio still playing as I walk up.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI hear â€œCountry favorites,\" but you arenâ€™t in the car, where, did you already go to find a phone I wonder.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSnow drifts down, dusting the highway, me, my pizzaburger.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI go inside the Walmart parking lot, Dave is ringing the bell for the Salvation Army and I ask if heâ€™s seen you. He dressed in an elvesâ€™ cap and red velvet santa outfit. I think he should have pointy shoes, to pull off the look. I think of kids looking at his regular shoes, as he answers.\r\n</p><p>\r\nNo he hasnâ€™t seen you, but he just got on shift, and maybe you beat him inside.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe car is was still idling, when I walked up. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI wander inside, past the greeter and the automatic doors. I eye the womenâ€™s department, jewelry thinking you might be there. But first I check out by the bathroom, ask at the customer service counter. Judy Miller works there, and she says she hasnâ€™t seen you.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAfter looking around for a half an hour. I wander back out and to the car. The snow is melting as it heats the defrost warm windshield a few flakes at a time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere I see your tracks. Youâ€™re wearing your narrow nurses shoes, white with that funny wavy heel. I was fascinated with that heel, when I was a kid. I used to hide in your closet when you were at nursing class, smelling your clothes racing my hotwheels car on your old pair.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe waves I follow the waves, off to the side of the road. You must have stopped here, shifting, shuffling your feet.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThen you moved, first towards the store, my shoes are caught up with yours. Then abruptly you turn the other way. Towards the golf course, I follow the trail.\r\n</p><p>\r\nCrossing the snow, my shoes get filled with snow, I hate that feeling. I follow your path. As it heads across the table smooth greens that I know lie beneath.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI remember the time you bought me that golf set. I was young, maybe only 3. I was excited and you had Dad take me out here, to the golf course, so I could knock that little plastic ball around.\r\n</p><p>\r\nYou were always like that, thinking about me.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBut after daddy left, leaving first for his home in Ronan, then later afterâ€”well you just got quieter and quieter, your laughter just dried up.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI follow the path, by the dock, the sand, that is strangely exposed, must be the wind. To the thing ice near shore.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHere I can see you walked, towards the Missions, large white backbones of ice and snow, walking on water, made solid, a lake in winter. Here I do not follow.\r\n</p>'),(1987,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1987_1.jpg\">\r\n<p>\r\ni decided to get a car. i needed something manly. rugged and capable of crossing the biggest creeks and taking out small tree\'s.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(1988,'<p>I read usenet.  Sometimes you see the most insightful things there, and sometimes you have no idea who said them:\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\"We live in a narrow inteface between molten radioactive rock and hard vacuum, and yet we worry about safety.\"  ~unknown\r\n</blockquote>\r\nThat quote has been on my mind a lot since 9/11.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>Of course, speaking of usenet:\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\"Usenet is like a herd of performing elephants with diarrhea -- massive, difficult to redirect, \r\nawe-inspiring, entertaining, and a source of mind-boggling amounts of excrement when you least expect it.\"  ~Gene Spafford (1992)\r\n</blockquote>\r\nSo don\'t believe everything you get that has a \"news://\" protocol specifier. :^)</p> ');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (1989,'<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWhen did you first start hacking?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nI started hacking when I was about 15, which was about 6 years ago. I was really interested because I had always loved fucking with people. I was just interested. I come from the BBS days and I would always fight with people and their message boards and then pick fights with them. I would get their telephone numbers and turn their phones into payphones or shut their lines off and all that other fun stuff. Mostly run of the mill shit, like credit card fraud.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWould you hack into the phone company, trace their number and then put warrants out for their arrest by hacking into the FBI?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nNo, but sometimes we would hack renegade boards and change their nightly.bat. The batch file runs every night at a certain time and we would set it to dial 911 a few times. This got a few bbs\'s shut down because the people had fines and/or jail terms.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nCould you then, make a pay phone a regular phone, hook it up with some MCI nonsense and call overseas?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nOf course! With PBX?s you can call over seas, all you have to do is find yourself a PBX and guess the password. This isn?t very hard because most systems use the LogIn as the password. You wouldn?t believe how many stupid people are out there.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nwhatever the hell a PBX is, but let me ask you this, CAN YOU CHANGE TV SHOWS LIKE IN THE MOVIE HACKERS?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nPBX?s are private branch exchange which basically means; miniature telephone system used by businesses. Also the movie hackers is totally fake. Don?t believe a damn thing that movie tells you or displays. I watched 5 minutes of that shit and I was laughing my ass off.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nYou mean the computer screen doesn?t project the screen on to their faces when they are in Hack mode?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nNo, I have never seen a screen do that. I think is fake because, kids turning sprinkler systems on and off via computers? I mean that is totally fake!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nDo you agree with the idea that hacking is an underground thing? Or do you think its getting more mainstream?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nIts definitely more mainstream. I guess its because of the development of the Internet. There are more kiddies who wish to become \'hackers\' because of movies like hackers. I myself do not consider myself \'elite\' nor am I a hacker I?m just an enthusiast who is interested in what \'hackers\' do\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nDo you think that publications like 2600 are doing the profession any good or bad?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nWell 2600 was good, when it used to be super underground. I used to hang in those days and I am good friends with the people who put it together. Don?t get me wrong its still decent, however its not getting any better, only worse.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nDo you think you?d ever be able to hack the pentagon and launch nuclear weapons against Denmark? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nhah. hack the pentagon maybe, but launch weapons? no. I doubt they have those systems attached to the Internet, that would be purely stupid.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWhat was the easiest \"hack\"?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nTwo years ago we broke into NASA?s jet propulsion labs. It was as easy as pie. However as of late I have been a little rusty. I?m beginning to get back into it and should be back up to speed in no time. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nAs an enthusiast, what kind of things do you do to keep up with the times?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nI just get on IRC and talk to old friends/foes and they keep me updated. I?m a member of a few development groups and I read the newsgroups and text files I get in the mail.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWhat is a development group, how would someone get involved?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nWell there are groups, whether or not these groups do anything is up to them. They are usually a bunch of kids who hook up and plan something. In my day we would code a few useful things and a few harmful things, phreak a bit, and just cause shit on IRC. Most groups nowadays are just made up of \'script kiddies\' people who take other peoples work and use it to their benefit. They never try to create anything new because they really have no interest in understanding what the programs they use really do.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nAre there any HACKER FRATERNITIES?  ALPHA BETA FULL VERSION? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nheh, no but we used to smurf a lot. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nSmurf? I?m not familiar with your hacker jive, bro, cut me some slack.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\n\'smurf\' is a little program written in C that is used to send ICMP Broadcast messages, spoofed of course, to various places. This causes the packets to bounce back and wherever you make them bounce to it will cause a major flood to that IP. So what you do is you make the person you want to smurf the place they bounce to your target.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWhat?s the difference between hackers and crackers?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nThe mindset that exists in all people who are interested in hacking/phreaking/cracking is the same. They ask themselves; \'is there a way to get around this?\'. This is the question that most \'hacks\' ask themselves when they want to avoid something. This question is what makes you a hacker. Answering the question is what makes you a good hacker. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nSo if I figure out the password to a pornography site (ASIAN ASS OF COURSE), would I then be a hacker?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nNah. Just lucky. Especially if there are some fake ass Asian blondes on there.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nOK, if I wanted to be a wicked hacker, what advice would you give me, a budding young computer hacker, to become a wicked hacker? die my hair blonde and wear a leather jacket and get a see through laptop?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nImage is nothing, most hackers are slobs.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nAre there legal ways for hackers to test out their knowledge? Do companies invite hackers to try and hack into their systems for testing purposes, and award prizes?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nI believe there are \'events\' like this. However, I?ve never heard/seen an advertisement for one.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nCan anyone be a hacker?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nI honestly couldn?t\' say. I mean sure, anyone could be a hacker. Its a mindset, a way of thinking more then anything. A lot of the time they are thinking about hacking rather then actually doing the hack.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nThose rude boys who hacked into citibank and transferred funds and crazy stuff like that ... have you ever tried that? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nNope. Banks run on mainframes. Maybe connect to it w/ your 2400 baud modem and guess some logins or find someone that works at the bank and get them to give you the dialup number and a LogIn/pass. You could even go into the bank and look for one near the desk, sometimes they write the numbers on the terminals. There are all kinds of ways, its just making those ways work properly. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nHave you and your friends ever hacked into that secret Russian radar base from  goldeneye and used the weapon of destruction to hold a small nation for ransom?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nHave you ever tried to connect to a Russian site? Fucking slowest shit on earth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">Chiamattt.</font><br>\r\nWell its been real fun talking with you \'pSyCh0H4cK, and I thank you for this little talk. HACK HARD!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\"1\">\'pSyCh0H4cK.</font><br>\r\nNo.. don?t hack hard, h4ck th4 pl4n3t!\r\n</p>'),(1990,'<IMG SRC=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/1990_1.pjpeg\">\r\n<p align=\"center\">i am the world\'s girl<br>\r\nthe evil dawn<br>\r\na northern star</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">i belong to noone</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">not even<br>\r\nmyself</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">hideous</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">i am the beast<br>\r\nwaiting for beauty<br>\r\nto kiss<br>\r\nthe enchantment away</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">to that one<br>\r\ni would give everything</p>'),(1991,'<p>many nights are spent sitting in front of a glowing monitor, wishing i was in my nice warm bed.  what fun it is to be an insomniac.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>many strange people are up late at night, many strange conversations to be had.  much time is wasted doing nothing.  time which is not to be wasted during sunlit hours.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>life feels like it is stuck in a loop, the same events repeating over and over again.  it\'s like my own personal groundhog day, only i don\'t get to rob an armoured cars.  trying to break this loop is proving harder than was originally counted on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>love clouds the head a lot.  strange reactions are noticed when the mind is in this state.  unexplained things can happen.  insanity is close to this.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(1992,'<p><i>\"When we step out of the light of what we know into the dark of the unknown, we will either find solid ground under our feet... or we will fly.\"</i></p><p>\r\n\r\nIt\'s a nice sentiment meant to inspire, but it purposely omits the fact that most of us fall.</p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nFalling. Falling... falling........</i></p><p>\r\n\r\nWhere has my courage gone, my love?<br>\r\nWhere does it now reside?<br>\r\nHas it flown up to heaven on the wings of a dove?<br>Does it stay close to God\'s own side?</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was standing on the edge of my roof imagining taking a few steps back, running, and leaping into the night air.</p><p>\r\nThen...</p><P>falling.</p><p>Of course, this would be where the dream ends and the reality begins.  I can\'t fly; F=ma; concrete isn\'t very elastic.</p><p>\r\n\r\nand so I stay<br>\r\nand try to find a way<br>\r\nto break the bonds<br>\r\nthat hold me sway</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy dreams sometimes engulf me in their reality.  I closed my eyes and felt the cool breeze embrace me.</p><p>Then...</p><P>falling.</p><p>\r\n\r\n<i>\"The art of flying is nothing more than falling and missing the ground.\"</i></p><P>...and I missed.</p>'),(1993,'TestTestTestTestTestTest'),(1994,'<P>happy halloween.  the sugar candy has been disseminated.  the terrorists evil plan is at hand.  individually wrapped sugar and chocolate tainted with anthrax spores.  little kids everywhere dressed up in costumes ready to file out to their death.  who will save the world?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>captain penis, that\'s who!  flying around the neighborhoods in his cape and helmet to all the homes and gathering up all the tainted candy from the unsespecting mothers who are perhaps already infected from snacking on the treats in preparation, but we hope not.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>captain penis takes all the candy from all the homes and waves good-bye to the children just begining to start their rounds, promising the candy will be better next year!</P>\r\n<P>off he flies to afganistan, where he then spreads the candy far and wide!  children everywhere are happy to see captain penis and his treats of mini chocolate bars and candy corn that looks like rotten teeth.</P>\r\n<P>captain penis\' nemisis, Assman Bin Badman, sees the candy that he worked so hard to containate and becomes enraged.</P>\r\n<P>\"Captain Penis!\" he shouts, \"I\'ll have my revenge!  You will never know when it comes, but I will be seen again!\"</P>\r\n<P>and he then disappeared into the ground.</P>\r\n<P>Halloween was saved by captain penis!</P>'),(1995,'<p>Jay just left the house grumbling about \"trying to work out Amy\'s desires\" because I have been yowling like a cat in heat for my peanut butter cakey treat. It was called Funny Bones when I was a kid in the states, I mean on the east coast.</p>\r\n<p>we went over several scenarios on how we could make this \"treat\". He has concluded out of impatience that he is going to get hostess cup cakes and I am going to scoop out the cream and slide in peanut butter.</p>\r\n<p>I do have a lot of desires. Im bleeding. Im hungry every minute. this morning I ate peanut butter toast and juniper warned, \"careful. peanut butter is a super hot food. not good in the morning which is Yin time.\"  That reminded me that when austin first started following me around everywhere I complained that I had a yang overabundance, and that he was Mr Yangy Yangy. he said,\"if Im yangy, what are you?\" and I replied, \"yangmonger\". and then he got scared and ran away, and I havent heard from him since.</p>\r\n<p>Good thing it\'s halloween. Here\'s a list of movies you could watch with your spanky-spanky and some cakey-cakey. i chose them because they inspire good costumes. A couple of them are also scary.</p>\r\n<p><b>Blood of a Poet</b> Jean Cocteau classic about statues that talk and mirrors that you can swim in. hey, have some drugs with your surrealism, it cant hurt. I am dressing up like the statue tonight.</p>\r\n<p><b>Wings of Desire</b> Slow and cumbersome masterpiece starring an angel and a trapeze artist who dresses up like an angel. Youll need about 3 cakeys to get through the whole thing, but it\'s worth it to get to the Nick Cave scene.</p>\r\n<p><b>The Lost Highway</b> This was Jay\'s choice for the pee-your-pants scariest movie. Patricia Arquette imho is kittenish and desirable. Out of Lynch\'s oeuvre I would have chosen <b>Erasherhead</b>, or for costume ideas,  <b>Wild at Heart</b>.</p>\r\n<p><b>A Chinese Ghost Story</b> A historical costume kung fu love story drama. This is the movie I rented almost two weeks ago, but because my wanky-wanky doesnt like to watch movies, I havent had a chance to see it yet. supposedly the string-stunt style of \"Crouching Tiger\" was pilfered from this.</p>\r\n<p><b>Nang Nak</b> The 2000 version of a Thai classic story about a guy who goes away to war and comes back to a dead wife and baby but doesnt know theyre dead so he has ghost sex with his wife. OH MY GOD its scary.</p>\r\n<p><b>A Nightmare on Elm Street, I or III</b> My favorite horror movie is nightmare on elm street. Part three isn\'t that scary but it has a host of loveable characters, including one played by my aforementioned bride-to-be, Patricia Arquette.</p>\r\n<p><b>Night of the Demons</b> I\'ll bet you haven\'t seen this one! There is a lipstick and breast scene in this one you just can\'t miss, as well as a ghoulish \'80s dance scene to a Ministry song.</p>\r\n<p><b>Sleepaway Camp</b> I can\'t give away any information about this movie for fear of ruining its various moments of glory. SEE IT NOW.</p>\r\n<p><b>Evil Dead Series</b> Youve probably all already seen this. </p>\r\n<p><b>Once Bitten</b> a young Jim Carrey plays the last american teenage virgin being stalked by vampire Lauren Hutton, who needs 3 doses of a virgin\'s blood before midnight, Halloween. UNBEARABLY WONDERFUL \'80s dance scene.</p>\r\n<p><b><a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.trepan.com/\"><font color=\"black\">A Hole In the Head</font></a></b> You get to watch some people drill holes in their heads for spiritual and physiological reasons. I scream out loud when I watch this.</p>'),(1996,'<p>hello all </p>\r\n\r\n<p>new in town. wanted to share a halloween story from my girlfriend who recently moved.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Since moving to California to be with Joe, I have been blessed enough to revisit the routine of Halloween with children. In Baltimore, in a second floor apartment, across the street from a bustling university, I was only privvy to young Americans dressed in whatever they could find that passed as a costume, yelling in the streets and partying until the wee hours of the morning, and young foreign students trying to make sense of what they were seeing and hearing.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>A young Chinese couple next door, who had moved here so the husband could get his doctorate at Johns Hopkins, had asked me what was going on and what they should do. They looked scared and confused but willing to try and understand. I remember thinking to myself how brave they were and I had an urge to ask our other neighbors, who were drinking it up down the hall, to keep it down. I didn\'t though. I did invite the couple into our apartment for some tea but they refused, looking more scared. So I told them that this was Halloween. They asked for a deeper explanation but I found myself blocked. I was going to say \'a celebration of death\', but that seemed an awfully macabre thing to say to a couple who looked about ready to jump if I said \'boo\'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the end I told them that it was a day set aside to explore the darker side of life, the things that frighten us and keep us up at night, in order to put real fear in its place. By the looks on their faces this sounded worse to them than a celebration of death. They asked me why anyone would want to make a joke of things that scare us. Because in their eyes, to not take a threat seriously, was opening a door into a world where we took nothing seriously, and that led to a place far more frightening than our darkest nightmares.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I then tried to explain the many facets of Halloween to them, dredging up all of the religious and spiritual connotations I could think of in order to offset any panic they may be feeling. And when I reached in and told them about El Dia de los Muertos, they both smiled.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Now that makes sense,\" the husband had said. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>We chatted some more. In the dingy hallway of a 100 year old building we talked about the dead we knew, they about grandparents and friends, me about my grandparents and long gone relatives from Mexico. I felt we had honored the holiday and, in a way, eachother. In that moment, I had more in common with them than my roommates or the kids down the hall. But I was glad for it. It did make sense. It now had meaning.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They ended up moving the following month to another building that housed a few Chinese families they knew. One day in February, I had received a message from my roommate that they had stopped by to wish me a happy new year. He handed me a little red envelope that they left. Inside of it was a typed explanation of the Chinese calendar and what the significance of the year meant for the days to come. At the bottom was a handwritten sentence that read, \"Take this gift in honor of your ancestors. Your future is long and bright.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That, my friends, is what unites us. In acknowledging death we reaffirm our life and the lives of others. Think about the loved ones who have left us and celebrate their time here. Then look to the future and acknowledge the things to come, knowing that we all go there together.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Happy Halloween.\" </p>\r\n\r\n<p>be well,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Buck<br>'),(1997,'<p>What\'s a mostly Christian country, with solid, humorless, Calvinist roots, doing embracing a holiday like Halloween year after year. The strict Baptists don\'t, I imagine, or the Jehovah\'s Witnesses. Perhaps the die-hard fundamentalist Christians frown on it, but who\'s to tell they\'re kids they can\'t go out and collect bales of candy with their friend Little Krissy and eat themselves into a stupor? I recall hearing in my childhood about parents who let their kids go trick-or-treating but when they got home, they were only allowed to keep like one fuckin piece of the candy they had, and the parents hid the rest, or threw it out&#151;or, more likely, ate it themselves after the kiddies went  to sleep, smearing each other with the chocolate on their fingers and fucking like pagans by candlelight.</p>\r\n<p>My mom never liked Halloween. She went all out when I was a kid, because I was her hobby, her only child and pride and joy. I recall pictures of one clown outfit that she sewed, orange poofy pants green trim, and a rainbow afro wig. Thankfully I don\'t recall the impotent humiliation of walking around like that. My mom never dressed up, and generally shunned costume parties. There was a Great Gatsby party my parents went to every summer, and she got into that for some reason. A fantasy of living in that time, I suppose, of being able to wear beading and feathers without appearing tacky. She\'s long had some obsession with the romance of that book, Scott and Zelda, which runs mostly counter to her distaste for hedonism, her lack of a taste for hard liquor, and her generally ninny-ish nature. I don\'t really see her having been a flapper, probably more like a tee-totaller. But maybe I\'m wrong. We are all as much the disappointing product of our accumulated choices as we are a slave to the pretty dreams we had for our futures when we were young. She says she doesn\'t like dressing up, doesn\'t relate to people who want to dress up and act like someone else. Acting, the donning of a mask, is something innate to me that she has never been able to understand. She frowns on artifice.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve compared my mom to Martha Stewart&#151;as have others who have tasted her baked goods. Now, Martha <i>really</i> loves Halloween. There\'s a scary picture of her wearing blood-red contacts from last year\'s Halloween issue (I\'m not a die-hard fan, really, but I used to be in madame\'s employ), and she claims she strolled into Le Cirque one night with these on, and that the maitre d\', after the briefest startled glance, made no mention of her eyes at all. I find this funny. It\'s like, you\'re in the service industry, you run into someone like her, and it just seems completely plausible somehow that her eyes might just turn red all of a sudden and a serpent\'s tongue could come lashing out of her lips. Martha is a big fan of artifice, as anyone should know. But I digress... </p>\r\n<p>I guess the fact is, most people like the act of dress-up at one time or another, and when it all comes down to it, why have an oppressive religious doctrine rule your life if you can\'t revel in its opposite once or twice a year. The pagan trappings have come down through the ages and been commercialized. We have Santa, Christmas trees, yule logs, Mardi Gras, Easter bunnies who lay eggs, May Day, and Halloween. We have a schizophrenic culture and always have. The same Calvinists and Puritans who founded our little colonies made witches out of their widowed old women, suspected them of terrible, devilish acts, because they had no place for them the communal psyche. In a country in which 85% of the population identifies themselves as Christian, 37% also believe in astrology. Among Christians, 22% believe they have been reincarnated, despite the obvious theological contradiction therein. And I would imagine that the majority of these people at least buy trick-or-treat candy, or attend a costume party, or recognize Halloween when it arrives. Clearly we\'re a country that doesn\'t know what to think, and never really has. Given the freedom to believe what we want, and not to believe at all, most of us end up at loose ends. I, myself, sometimes say I\'m \"spiritual.\" But I don\'t know what the fuck I mean sometimes. I\'m too lazy to work it all out. I will, however, be donning white-out contacts tonight and pretending I\'m a blood sucker, because everyone knows vampires have the most sex.</p>'),(1998,'<p>hey all</p> \r\n<p>new in town, and thought i would share something that my girlfriend wrote that i love.</p>\r\n<p>it nicely represents where i proudly live.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s a local tradition (local being relative...it\'s celebrated all over California, Arizona, NM, Texas, and Mexico) made Holy by the following El Dia de los Santos on the 1st. Death and Sainthood paired. Ever wonder much about our shunted view of death? \'Our\' being the general American aversion to speaking of it, let alone accepting it. Perhaps more so in recent months for obvious reasons. Here\'s a different perspective brought to you by a language very close to home.</p>\r\n<p>In Espanol, the verb \'to be\' is represented by two verbs: estar and ser.</p> \r\n<p>Ser (Yo soy mexicana, I am Mexican) is a verb used to denote permanent status on things unchangeable, like your nationality, or your place of birth (Yo soy de Pasadena. I am of Pasadena.).</p>\r\n<p>Estar denotes the temporary, changeable things like emotions (Yo estoy triste. I am sad.), or your current location (Yo estoy en mi casa. I am in my house.).</p>\r\n<p>When one speaks of the dead in Spanish, the proper verb usage is not Ser, but Estar. Sheila esta muerto. Sheila is dead. But the verb usage suggests something bigger. She may be dead, but it is not permanent. Death does not destroy life. It displaces it. But for how long? Think about it and interpret how you see fit.</p>\r\n<p>happy halloween</p>\r\n<p>Buck</p>\r\n<p>np: Dimmer-I Believe You Are A Star</p>'),(1999,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmy friend tom is in town and that\'s pretty neat.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmy friend tom is in town and it\'s halloween and i\'m at work. right now, at work, and they\'re having some sort of party upstairs and i am downstairs and i am working. right now. i get off in fifteen minutes and then i will go upstairs and to the party and eat some free corporate lawyer type food.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ndug has just im\'d me and says there is no food left. he has a tendency to exaggerate, but, still, that\'s a little disheartening.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso. work and the party is over.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ntonight i am spinning records at a small little bar called pow!. that will be fun. and my friend tom, he\'s here too.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmaybe there\'s some cheese left. i never could turn down a good cheese plate. i like the cheese even better when it has been sitting around room temperature and gets kinda squishy.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nten minutes!</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni wanted to dress up like spike from cowboy bebop for halloween. my hair is almost exactly like his is except mine is bleached blonde and his is green. i wanted to dye my hair green for halloween and get a blue suit and look like spike, but- no money.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni said to dug that i would be neet to dress up like spike from cowboy bebop and just randomly run into someone dressed up like faye. then we would run off together and it would be true love.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nhe told me that i was retarded.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut, i\'m kinda still contemplating dying my hair green anyway. i think it\'d be fun. to piss off the squares and to open up the heart chakra (</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand to look like spike from cowboy bebop (but, shhh!, don\'t tell anybody, cause, you know, that\'s kinda lame)).</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nfive minutes!</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nanyway, this is kinda boring isn\'t it?.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nlet\'s see, good halloween story. hmm. oh!, here, listen:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni was ten and it was around nine thirty at night and i had already gone around trick or treating and i was home and answering the door. my friend eric panis was over and we had gone out for candy together and he was going to sleep over and we were going to watch scary movies all night until we fell asleep.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\neric was my best friend and we were hanging out and answering the door so my mother didn\'t have to anymore and because we wanted to catch a glimpse at the older kids and see how cool they were and see what we were going to become.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso, the door bell rings and eric and i answer and there are too girls standing there and they are a little older then us. they don\'t have on costumes and they have attitudes. the two girls, one heavy and one not so, and the heavy girl says give us some candy. and she has quite the attitude. i didn\'t like her very much and eric didn\'t like her either.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe give them candy and, as i am closing the door and they are walking to my neighbors, i scream out \'chow down wide load!!\' and eric and i laugh and i close the door. chow down wide load, i\'m not really sure where that came from, i think it was from a movie.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nnever the less, we were vindicated.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ngood story right?, no not really.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nit\'s six! i wonder what\'s up stairs.</p>'),(2001,'<p>hola,</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I have had this discussion many times with many people in regards to folk who might think they are creative. It varies each time, and will eventually appear more formally elsewhere, but I thought I would throw it to the swinneyfolk...</p> \r\n\r\n<p>People are artists because they can not help but be so. Jimi Hendrix was a guitarist. In the truest sense apparently. He carried a guitar with him at all times. He played while eating breakfast, he played in bed, he even used the facilities with 6 string in tow. Why? He had no choice. It was him and he was it. If an artist is lucky, they will be recognized in their lifetime for their work and perhaps they may break even at the end of the day, or make mas dosh.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>But that is not the point.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>For if other calls are answered, children, 9-5, partners, family, charity, then it will get in the way of art- which is the process of committed creation. Many love art, and wish to live in this process vicariously, or dip their foot into its waters from time to time. For them, they have a valuable hobby. An artist is really at work.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Hobby vs. work seems harsh, I know. but to paraphrase Alec Baldwin (who was the best Jack Ryan and I said so to his face once) on Inside the Actors Studio, do you want to act or do you want to be a movie star? You could act, really anywhere you want. There are tons of community theaters, there are many schools, etc. But if you want to work regularly in film, with a name above or below title then it means that much work, and many prices will be paid.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Some have no choice. Jimi had no choice. Old Vincent had no choice. Legend has it that Van Gogh sold only one painting during his entire lifetime- \"The Red Vineyard,\" but many believe this to be untrue. But even these people believe that he may have sold a few at only the sheer cost of materials. What is for certain is that he generated something like 2500 sketches and paintings in his lifetime for no monetary reward. Because he had no choice.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Besides an inability to do anything else but art, an artist has the habit of doing something else.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Not give a rat\'s ass what anyone else thinks.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Miles Davis and Picasso come to mind. They spent entire lifetimes of creating work, jettisoning past successes (and subsequent fan bases) for new points on the map that just as the public went to, they were on to other places. This is a roundabout way of saying that they didn\'t care what people thought of the work. And perhaps them for that matter. They had their own compass to steer by, and told folk to like it or not.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Now this is not all either/or. If anything it is axis stuff. You don\'t have to keep family at bay like Toni Morrison apparently had to create a novel, but you probably won\'t win a Nobel prize for literature (for sure a measure of commitment to the word in a lifetime) either.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I guess the moral of the story is, find out who and what you are, and be in service to whatever that might be.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Kisses,</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Joseph Buck</P>'),(2000,'<p>\r\nwhen i was twelve, i put on my dad\'s army jacket, stole his bbgun that looked like a real revolver, and put on his boots to go yowling around with the other kids. when i was 11, ib took a fake ring to school and told everyone it was my mom\'s engagement ring and showed it off. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni was a really show-off as a kid.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni just remember howling my head off, running after people with my gun.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthose were more innocent times.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere\'s nothing like being out on halloween night though, on a full moon. we should arrange a grown-up posse some year, and we will come home with all the loot. \r\n</p><p>\r\nfavorite candies: baby ruths, kit kats, whatchamacallits, snickers, those little crispy chocolate coins. i secretly loved candy corn, eating it color layer by layer. the best thing i got once was a beautiful candied apple, but my mom made me give it to her.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsigh.\r\n</p><p>\r\npoisoned candy is an urban legend, i think. it\'s been documented that the razor in the apple story is bunk. seems like you would be able to see a razor imbedded in your apple!! unless it got covered with candy coating, i guess.\r\n</p><p>\r\nup until i was 11, i was only allowed to trick or treat on my floor. i lived on the tenth floor of an old tenement building, with a gate you pulled shut to make the elevator go. i still have nightmares about that elevator, about going inside it and realizing that it had no floor, and i had to hold onto the little strip on the sides with all my might. oh yes. the trick or treating: i would politely knock on all the doors, holding my brother\'s hand, and get some candy. wten minutes later, there was enough candy to slightly weigh my optimistic pillowcase down. some lollypops. some mini hersheys. then it was time to happily plop down in the front of the tv for the halloween specials. so it was fun until i was 9 and realized i was being gyped and made my mom come with me to all the floors. oh my god, i could barely lift my bag when i was done, and me  and my brother wore shiteating grins until we got home, and Mother took most of it away. the savagery of adults.\r\n</p><p>\r\nanyways. it\'s a lovely night. i love all the smiles and nice vibes i get on halloween. kids really do deserve a night of uncontrolled candy gobblery.</p>'),(2002,'<p>These past few days have been disorienting.  The combination of a weekend with little sleep and lots of working as well as too much work and a nasty all-nighter on Tuesday night have left my mind-body connection out of phase.  I find myself being confused by things that shouldn\'t be confusing.  I\'m in no condition to be working on my computer graphics assignment.  It\'s being highly uncooperative and I\'m no mood to put up with it\'s shenanigans any more this evening.  There\'s that and the fact that I have to work a nine hour shift tomorrow morning pushing me towards attempting sleep.  It\'s going to be a difficult state to acheive as my sleeping schedule is totally messed up as a result of the past week.  At least I don\'t have any more midterms left.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m patiently awaiting the snow to begin falling as that is one of my favourite times of the year.  The first snowfall is great.  And it\'s not just due to the fact that the city hasn\'t had a chance to translate it into gray goo.</p>\r\n<p>Going out for drinks with friends was a lot of fun last night, even despite the recent unemployment status of 1/4 of the group and the continued unemployment of the other 1/4.  The highlight (or lowlight depending on your point of view) would definately have to be the point where I was dressed up in angel wings and a fluffy halo.  Fortunately there were no pictures of this, but it will always live on in the minds (and nightmares) of the participants.</p>\r\n<p>Halloween downtown let me down.  I didn\'t go out last year, but the year before that I remember being downtown and the sheer insanity that was being on the streets that night. I guess it\'s unbalanced to compare Halloween on a night when most people don\'t have to work in the morning compared to Halloween on a Wednesday night, though.</p>\r\n<p>I must wake up in seven hours for work and thusly will begin my weekend of working the whole time.  &lt;sarcasm&gt;I\'m <b>really</b> looking forward to that.&lt;/sarcasm&gt;.</p>\r\n<p>At least OpenGL assignments produce pretty bugs.</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2002_1.jpg\" height=150></p>'),(2003,'<p><font face=\"Arial\">http://www.everythinggoodwastaken.com is a Flash based site which is full of other crappy sites including: Ovulation Girl, Vinyl Dominion, My Resume, Anatomy of a Random Girl, The Vat, and the up and coming AntiPorn.\r\n<p>It\'s ever so poopy and oh so creamy. Um, was I supposed to write an article? I...uh...well, maybe next time. With love, Ursula.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2003_1.gif\">'),(2004,'I dunno who wants to take credit for this one, but it certainly takes the cake for best halloween horror story and deserves wider viewing than an \"i thought it\"...<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nmasturbation horrors  .o0O (I was a 16-year-old girl visiting my very old-school grandparents on my summer break. I had also recently discovered the joys of masturbation, and for some reason, I decided to see if I could fit my entire hand into my vagina. Well, I managed to fit it, but I couldn\'t get it back out. I had to call my grandmother for help. She couldn\'t get it out, either, so I wound up wrapped in a blanket, sitting in the emergency room. The nice doctor managed to lube me up and stretch me enough to finally get it out. --Handy Work in Canada) '),(2005,'<p>Deep inside the night, you sit watching freaks and banshees whirl around the street like discarded cellophane. They disappear into Edwardian crevices filled with dark pastel shadows. The fog floats densely, drifting the wrong way up the hill.</p>\r\n<p>I step out of the fog onto the stairs, and as I appear I hear you hold your breath. You cannot understand who you see in front of you, not knowing here before you stands your most delicate and tenuous lover. I whisper your name in your ear as we embrace, and you press your fingers through my coat and hold onto me.</p>\r\n<p>In the gentle tones of your room, we lounge on plush greyscale, stomachs pressed into the carpet. We tinker with sounds on the laptop, whispering words; \"loop it there\", \"reverse the snare.\" A fractured crunch of bassline and shattered sine wave percusses in the soft grey chamber. We giggle. I stretch over you and recline on your back, my ear to the barrel of your ribcage.</p>\r\n<p>You whisper to yourself and delight completely in the sounds you create. I lay upon you, hearing your gentle chuckles ricochet in your chest. I take a deep breath and let your intoxicating smell sink deep into my lungs. I wrap my arms around your chest and you hold onto me, and I am there for a moment perfectly contented. I think perhaps this is the only moment in my life I will feel exactly this way, with your creative soul rippling beneath me, your senses aroused.</p>\r\n<p>Everything possible has been said about the act of lovemaking. It is within the ephemera which true love is found. The moments before hands find their way under clothing, the seconds after the first kiss of the night, the minutes spent breathless between pleasures. This is where my love lives for you.</p>\r\n<p>We have but moments in which to delight for a lifetime.</p>'),(2006,'<p>\r\npicked up a cd called bollywood breaks. the cd is jam packed with groovin songs inspired by the indian movie scene. soarings strings and driftings flutes combined with funky drum breaks. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nneeding more i got the show on the road using winmx. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfound a dj shadow track called uptown bollywood nights. fucking ruff track folks. it feels like you are in a car chase. ive never been to the streets of bombay, but im sure the car chases they come up with are pretty gnarly. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nyeah check it out. \r\n</p>'),(2007,'A Head doctor of Deep Africa and Maker of Fine Dolls.  D.H.V., <i>Doctor of Hoodoo and Vexes</i>, from the Greater Metropolitan Yorba Linda Jesus Will Save Your Bod Home Study Bible School; and F.I.H.G.W.P., <i>Fellow of the Intergalactic Haitian Guerillas for World Peace</i>.<br><br>\r\n<b>Note:</b>Erisians of the Laughing Christ sect are of the silly contention that Dr. Mojo is an imposter and that PATAMUNZO LINGANANDA is the True Second Apostle.  Lord Omar claims that Dr. Mojo heaps hatred and curses upon Patamunzo, who sends only Love Vibrations in return.<br><br>\r\nBut we of the POEE sect know that Patamunzo is the Ream Imposter, and that those vibrations of his are actually an attempt to subvert Dr. Mojo\'s rightful apostilic authority by shaking him out of his wits.'),(2008,'<p>lately, because of my new haircut, i\'ve had a bad attitude at work. it\'s like, \r\n  especially when you shave your head, you either look like you\'ve got the \"sick\" \r\n  or you\'re pretty tough. i guess i look pretty tough or something. i\'ve already \r\n  told two people to go to hell, and if i did that with my other usual haircut \r\n  they would have laughed like it was a joke. but not lately. it\'s like they think \r\n  i\'m serious. chip came up me the day after i shaved it off and asked me, \"why\'d \r\n  you cut it off?\" oh, for your information, chip is balder than an eagle. \r\n  so i looked at him and said, \"i dunno, just needed a change.\" then \r\n  i did something i never do: <b>i thought out loud</b>. \"at least mine will \r\n  grow back,\" i said, getting back to pretending to work. everyone looked \r\n  at me like <i>wow, what a fucking bastard. </i>they were right.</p>\r\n<p>i sit next to the water cooler and before my cut i was the only one girls asked \r\n  to change the bottle because of my \"nice look\". now i look like a \r\n  fucking bastard that will cut your throat if you even ask me the time. one girl \r\n  was about to ask and i gave her a look and she had dry mouth the rest of the \r\n  day. </p>\r\n<p>and lately, when i use the bathroom at work, there\'s always someone there taking \r\n  a huge crap. i\'m tired of it--walking in there and ducking like some bombs went \r\n  off. so now, since i got this new cut, i flip the lights off when someone is \r\n  sitting down. they usually say, \"hey, someone\'s in here!\" and i walk \r\n  off thinking, <i>yeah, i know, you fucking pod.</i> i go back to desk and laugh \r\n  to myself. i told a co-worker about it and said, \"only a fucking bastard \r\n  would even think of doing something like that.\"</p>\r\n<p>i did a favor for a co-worker the other day and she said, \"you\'re the \r\n  coolest!\" and i replied, \"no, you got that wrong.\" and then she \r\n  said, \"ok, you\'re pretty ok.\" i shook my head and said, \"i\'m \r\n  a fucking bastard.\" i even say things at work out loud like, \"what \r\n  else don\'t we get here?\" and \"where are the papers for my 401k?\"</p>\r\n<p>anyhow, not to change the subject or anything, but i was in the shitter at \r\n  macy\'s and i saw this:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2008_1.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and then i remembered this:</p>\r\n<p><b><font color=\"#0000ff\">robotec9</font></b><font color=\"#0000ff\">:</font><font color=\"#000000\"> \r\n  i think one of your cronies is messaging me</font><br>\r\n  <b><font color=\"#0000ff\" size=3>robotec9</font></b><font color=\"#0000ff\">:</font><font color=\"#000000\"> \r\n  12:48:18 JazzyTori: Well... I got your screenname when I was in L.A.<br>\r\n  12:48:29 robotec9: from who?<br>\r\n  12:48:38 JazzyTori: A bathroom wall...</font> </p>'),(2011,'<p>\r\ni am an alex grey groupie. i promise i\'ll try not to make too many more mentions of him on this site, but here is something:\r\n</p><p>\r\nin 1989, alex grey did the painting below. it is called gaia, and is based on a vision he had during the birth of his daughter. the painting depicts the tree of gaia, and on either side, two different possible futures for planet earth. on the left side, joy and harmony. on the right, pestilence and destruction. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe right side of the painting shows two planes and the world trade center.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.alexgrey.com/a-gallery/8-24/gaia.jpg\">\r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nsome other things:\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis is the first time i\'ve noticed that there\'s a new selection of categories for articles. i am going to check \"spiritual\" from now on, like i used to always check \"art.\" i think the two are one and the same.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalso, i am trying to finish a novel for <a href=\"http://www.nanowrimo.com\">nanowrimo</a> and one of the suggested tips is to enforce some weird imposed structure for yourself. my imposed structure is that i\'ve chosen about thirty deities from all the world\'s religions (from a very helpful book, encyclopedia of the gods by one michael jordon, no relation to the basketball player) and each chapter will relate to one of these deities somehow. i am really pleased with this plan.\r\n</p><p>\r\n'),(2009,'<p>monster\'s inc. was totally cute.  plenty of pixar flexing its animation muscles.  \"see this?  we can animate hair!  check out our snow!! check out this action scene!!\"  the previews were pretty good too!  there was one for harry potter, another for the rerelease of ET, and episode II of star wars, but that one was just a little teaser and didn\'t leave me with any impressions.  didn\'t see any jar jar though.  good sign.</p>'),(2010,'<P> Now, I can assume the thoughts reverbing from the minds of those who commonly read Swinney. Oh, nooo....not her again. Not yet again another of her drab and misleading tales drenched in banality. No, not this time my friends.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> This is to a dear friend of mine who writes often on this site. Yes, one could email as a tool of communication but this appears so much at hand at the moment and so I decide upon using it.\r\nThere are many who love you. But do not say you would sleep with me but will not because our friendship is of more import. And that is not even why I write this...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> You live your life and strange enough it clouds who you are. The steps you do not see are misleading. Take a walk on the wild side. Tell your boss to fuck himself. Tell Debra you could refer her to a really great shrink. Call your father up and tell him that you love him. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Be active. I know it can be difficult at times. Without the hardships of love and life in general, things in all of their vagueness would be clear and defined...no questions asked.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Monetarily you have so much more than I. Us, we, of same age...Yet, live on the spur of waves, drink from the faucet, imagine your death and YOUR LIFE.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Most of all I write to tell everyone that winter is soon to be here and baths extinct. For who needs a wash when due to the chill of out-door weather, they have become unecessary. \"Febreeze\" it all I say. Cuts down on the laundry bill. That and I love Aphganistan. It is on my shirt. Fuck this impudent and greed soaked country where a suburban is the way and fashion rules. There exists ingenious in only moderate amounts. I see it in my friends. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I see ingenious in relevent minds. We open them up wide and laugh, we can see eachother\'s molars, swallowed up in a living I cherish yet despise.</P>'),(2012,''),(2013,'hi'),(2014,'hi'),(2015,'<p><font face=\"Arial\" size=\"2\">Â . . . the sharks are <b><font color=\"#800000\">forever</font></b> circling. and I don\'t understand it. the people you love the most hurt you the most. maybe they don\'t realize it, maybe its all some sort of horrible domino effect. they think I hurt them and it hurts me and i\'m hurting so they hurt more...and we\'re all stuck in a mire of <b><font color=\"#800000\">silent</font></b> agony. I feel so misunderstood. so many years and it\'s as if they still don\'t KNOW me. to say that I don\'t love? or that I don\'t love enough. how can you measure something like that? how can you hold it? I am who I am. I can\'t even talk anymore. or really say what I think and i\'m not even sure anymore what I DO think. or if I think at all. I second guess myself constantly. if I think that I love and then those that I love the most say I don\'t well then where does that leave me? my ground is gone I have nothing to stand on. do you understand how this tortures me? I am just me. a wanderer, a seeker <b><font color=\"#800000\"> full of passion</font></b>. a roaming <b><font color=\"#800000\"> soul feeding</font></b>. is that so horrible? so evil? must we all be the same? do we all have to squeeze into one box? it breaks my heart to know that ultimately I cannot give those that I love whatever it is that they need. it leaves me staring at a brick wall immobile. emotionless. <b><font color=\"#800000\">devoured</font></b>. staring at my own failed <b><font color=\"#800000\">reflection</font></b>. can\'t they see that I was made for one thing? to love. that is all I am that is all I want. to share love. there are NO expectations. no referees or written rules. it\'s right there in everything I see. and yet I know that I can only be me. that I am built to be a certain way to respond to certain things to create a unique path. is there no value to it because the path is one that others don\'t want? don\'t see? your caged creature. <b><font color=\"#800000\"> crying out</font></b>. suffocated. I am red when they want white. I try to be white for them but I only end up a dull pink. sorry, not good enough. and i know this. but i try again. and again. and again. thinking maybe for once i will get something right. it leaves me sleepless. wondering what i have missed. alone in the night. with bad dreams and restless wakenings. <b><font color=\"#800000\">never seen</font></b>. just minute moments of blissful escape in other places and other hearts far removed. it leaves me disoriented. with <b><font color=\"#800000\"> endless</font></b> questions. what happened to it all? I am just ME. not somebody else\'s daughter. not somebody else\'s sister. not somebody else\'s lover. not somebody else\'s friend. I can\'t be a dream, a fantasy, a savior, an answer, a <b><font color=\"#800000\">wanting</font></b>. and yet i can. I am all human. imperfect, unwhole, with sin. moving in a million different shades of gray. what I do is because I am driven to it. this is my life. my body tells me how to live it. that there are things to know and people to meet and love to be shared. my soul quests for things. it\'s thirsty. it wants to know, to touch, to smell, to taste. and so I feed it. my mind responds in its own way....where others might talk I need to listen. where others might scream I sit silent. where others might laugh I cry. THIS IS ME. it is all I have to give. my <font color=\"#800000\"><b>voiceless dream</b></font>.</font> i am sorry i\'m not what you wanted me to be. i am worth nothing. and i am worth everything.</p>'),(2016,'<p>\r\nI have been unemployed for a good time now. living off a line of credit that runs as far as the eye can see. \r\nI have been very lazy. I dont know where i want to go or what i want to do when i get there. I know i need something, but i just dont want to stoop down to jobs ive done in the past. I sort of want to move on with something \"good?\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhile the search for a job has been less than feverish, i have looked ad the job sites. what i have found in my journeys to the many sites out there is that there is so much redundancy. the \"job site\" scene is bloated and full of redundant information. There are perhaps sites that lack certain features and then there are sites that offer far too many. There are sites full of certain types of jobs and others lacking any structure what so ever. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe competitive goal to provide people with an efficient and convienient way to search for employment has failed.  The beurocracy of today\'s capitalist world, esspecially on the net, has in certain ways made the internet inefficient and useless. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith the events of 9/11 displaying how ineffective the layers and layers of \"protection\" were, we see agencies like the INS linking their systems with other agencies, creating a single database, making their system an effective, efficient, and streamlined. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition, we have been seeing a rise in unemployment. we hear politicians and big business talk of tax cuts and others talking about make work projects. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperhaps there is an almost simple and easy way to streamline and provide efficient and up to date job options and listings. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntake all the job sites. buy them. nationalise them. do what you need to do. when you need a road built you move the house in the way or pave over it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncreate a single database. nationwide. one site for all employers and job seekers to use. jobs.ca could be the main site. providing links to job sites for each province and territory. likewise, jobs.on.ca would provide job listings for ontario as would jobs.bc.ca would provide job listings for those looking for work in british columbia.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngovernment is the answer for services that provide for the \"whole\" population. competition isnt. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nps. there should be an \"unemployed\" catagory. i cant afford to be part of any of the other catagories. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2017,'<p>This page is unassailable.  Within its lines are no doubts or worries.  This page is steadfast.  As you read this page your future is mapped before you.  And, like this page (which is already partially behind you), your future cannot be altered. This page cannot be torn from the book in which it is printed.  You can try to rip this page from its faultless binding, but you will miserably fail.  This page cannot be burned.  It cannot be marred.  It is bulletproof.  This page is armored.  After you leave this page you will not be able to forget it.  It will return to you in snatches; its phrasings will drift into your mind on lazy summer days.  You cannot leave this page behind like that abandoned fall when the woman suddenly left and you shuffled aimlessly through the streets of Boston, kicking the dry leaves and looking at the blue, blue sky.  At the time you lived on a couch in a friend\'s apartment, drank cheap California wine and smoked expensive French cigarettes.  That pain dimmed.  This page will never so dim.  Men have fought wars over such pages, such imprints.  They have shed blood and died in foreign lands contending words such as these.  Less than twenty-six letter are arranged on this page, yet they form sentences more enduring than the stone gods of antiquity.  Do not doubt the omnipotence of this page.\r\n\r\n<p><i>The enduring passivity of metallurgy - Commentary\r\n\r\n<p>The woman in the forgoing poem, though she is never mentioned directly, is real.  The last time I saw her she me that she had gone back to her husband and was now quite clearly pregnant.  She was eating a bagel with cream cheese on the sidewalk patio of a bagel shop on Boylston Street, directly across from the Boston Public Library.  She had quit her teaching job because her husband worked for a successful Internet company and didn\'t want her to work.  This woman, whom I will call V.C., informed me that she was glad to be back wither her husband.  He was different now and she was happy now, happy at last.  Her interim of promiscuity, which encompassed our brief relationship, \"had been fun.\"  V.C. expressed that she wanted to hug me by way of friendly greeting.  I admit that at first I was hesitant to connect with her physically, remembering her tastes and textures, but after a few seconds of internal conflict I leaned in.</p> \r\n<p>When we hugged I felt the protuberance of her belly , the nascent life contained inside her womb.  For some reason V.C.\'s impending motherhood disgusted me.  I drew back from our embrace hardly able to disguise my revulsion.  A vile, swampy blackness was attempting to collect me, nipping at my heels from the fringes of the world.  I ignored this strange sensation.  I said: \"It was nice to see you again.\"   There was no mention of getting together for coffee, which might include the introduction of current lovers, as sometimes is the custom in situations such as this.</i></p>'),(2018,'<p>This page is unassailable.  Within its lines are no doubts or worries.  This page is steadfast.  As you read this page your future is mapped before you.  And, like this page (which is already partially behind you), your future cannot be altered. This page cannot be torn from the book in which it is printed.  You can try to rip this page from its faultless binding, but you will miserably fail.  This page cannot be burned.  It cannot be marred.  It is bulletproof.  This page is armored.  After you leave this page you will not be able to forget it.  It will return to you in snatches; its phrasings will drift into your mind on lazy summer days.  You cannot leave this page behind like that abandoned fall when the woman suddenly left and you shuffled aimlessly through the streets of Boston, kicking the dry leaves and looking at the blue, blue sky.  At the time you lived on a couch in a friend\'s apartment, drank cheap California wine and smoked expensive French cigarettes.  That pain dimmed.  This page will never so dim.  Men have fought wars over such pages, such imprints.  They have shed blood and died in foreign lands contending words such as these.  Less than twenty-six letter are arranged on this page, yet they form sentences more enduring than the stone gods of antiquity.  Do not doubt the omnipotence of this page.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>The enduring passivity of metallurgy - Commentary\r\n\r\n<p>The woman in the forgoing poem, though she is never mentioned directly, is real.  The last time I saw her she me that she had gone back to her husband and was now quite clearly pregnant.  She was eating a bagel with cream cheese on the sidewalk patio of a bagel shop on Boylston Street, directly across from the Boston Public Library.  She had quit her teaching job because her husband worked for a successful Internet company and didn\'t want her to work.  This woman, whom I will call V.C., informed me that she was glad to be back wither her husband.  He was different now and she was happy at last, happy at last.  Her interim of promiscuity, which encompassed our brief relationship, \"had been fun.\"  V.C. expressed that she wanted to hug me by way of friendly greeting.  I admit that at first I was hesitant to connect with her physically, remembering her tastes and textures, but after a few seconds of internal conflict I leaned in. \r\n\r\n<p>When we hugged, I felt the protuberance of her belly , the nascent life contained inside her womb.  For some reason V.C.\'s impending motherhood disgusted me.  I drew back from our embrace hardly able to disguise my revulsion.  A vile, swampy blackness was attempting to collect me, nipping at my heels from the fringes of the world.  I ignored this strange sensation.  I said: \"It was nice to see you again.\"   \r\n\r\n<p>There was no mention of getting together for coffee, which might include the introduction of current lovers, as sometimes is the custom in situations such as this.</i></p>\r\n'),(2019,'test!  yeah.'),(2020,''),(2021,'<P>for many months my friend worked at the local \"bug zoo\" as an entomological tour guide and by the end she made a wiggly gesture with her pointer and middle fingers when she referred to smelling. it reawakened my whole appreciation for feelers, and for waving body parts into the middle of sensory stimuli.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>this same friend initiated the everyday use of the official PADI scuba training gesture for \"i have to pee\" when she was trying to get out of the bug zoo and into the undersea gardens as an octopus and eel wrangler. you cross your fingers and point them down, so they look like two legs doing the pee pee dance. this spawned a similar gesture for \"do me,\" wherein the finger legs aren\'t so much crossed as they are coy, with one finger foot rubbing up and down the other finger leg, or maybe turning sort of restlessly back and forth with one finger foot on point.<P>\r\n\r\n<P>incidental knowledge accrued at random jobs fascinates me, but gestures and motor memory seem like the most invasive and bizarre acquisition. i have extensive knowledge of the dewey decimal code, orchestral score order, universal produce codes, whmis safety symbols, etc permanently lodged in my brain, but the only gesture i can think of is the one that lifeguards at my pool used when we had to go check the chlorine levels. it was like pouring two test tubes back and forth. to the untrained eye it looked like building with lego.</P>'),(2022,'<IMG SRC=\"http://www.beatthief.com/images/brianseparatedatbirth.jpg\">\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnow the thing about this show \'boston public,\' i\'ve seen it a few times and, while it gave one the impression that it had promise of being better than most shows, it seems to have slipped deep deep into TV land. <BR><BR>they have a kid whose mother locked him in the basement for weeks as a kid and now he\'s the smartest kid in the class and over summer break did the same to her! re re re! and then cut off her hand! and then brought the hand to biology class to cut up and study! <BR><BR>and guess what! they didn\'t kick him out of school when they learned where the hand came from! and then guess what! his mother came to the school with a metal hook hand and wants to be a teacher\'s assistant! (ok, ok, your brother has a metal hook hand and works in a school and you don\'t think this is funny...) and guess what! they are letting her work in the school! a woman who they _know_ locked her son in the basement! <BR><BR>now there\'s a high school! and brian moseley\'s long lost brother is the \"headmaster\"! (note: brian currently lives in australia and words like this have been popping up in his vocabulary)'),(2023,'<p>You\'re a million miles away yet I am looking at you sitting there across from me.</p>\r\n<p>I am attempting to pin-point the locations of events behind your eyes.</p>\r\n<p>It isn\'t safe to assume anything anymore.</p>\r\n<p>Often I wonder whether we will ever leave this place.</p>\r\n<p>Cursed with equilibrium.</p>\r\n<p>In the really large pool.</p>\r\n<p>Uterine amnesia giving way to Sepultura swimming with the stingrays.</p>\r\n<p>Closeness and distance symmetrically opposed and somehow perfectly balanced in a willful show of determination on your part.</p>\r\n<p>You make me envious and eager for the unknown later.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve got these inspirational photographs etched  onto my mind.</p>\r\n<p>Annotated with emotional footnotes.</p>\r\n<p>I can reference them on demand and right now I\'m feeling quite demanding.</p>\r\n<p>Physical models become updated with increasingly more accurate approximations.</p>\r\n<p>I search for these words but cannot find the syntax when I need them most.</p>\r\n<p>You cause transmission errors in my mind and create, for me, a loss for words.</p>\r\n<p>You make me stupid.</p>\r\n'),(2024,'<p>When I walk home I look down at the sidewalk. I stop thinking about what I did that day, I let my instincts take over to guide me home. When I do look up, I am jarred back into a reality I don\'t want to see, and so again, my eyes fall to the sidewalk, and I watch the ground pass beneath my feet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I hate politics, I was thinking today. I was thinking in narration of what I will write, and was writing it along with my steps. I hate it for these reasons. I hate it because it forces .2% to be worth $2000. I hate it because of the people.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> The rhythm as I walk counts out numbers and beats. I close my eyes and try to keep walking with them closed. I can never get past ten. I am too afraid that I will fall into that pit. I am thinking of all the numbers in my head, and of what I will write here about them. I am narrating my narration of what I am thinking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Little memories float in and out of my sequence between thinking and not thinking, between counting and not counting. I am jarred back into reality, and then I stop. I watch the car go by, thinking about how people take too much for granted. I continue to walk, I feel the connection of my feet hitting the pavement jarring my shoulders slightly, weighed down by the bag I carry. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Back to a shard of memory, and then analysis. Why do I constantly remind myself of these times? I will skip this analysis. I walk on, and I let my  senses tell me when to turn the last corner. I\'m taking a longer way home. I\'m wondering how long it will be before the things that feel so right in my head get translated, and the point is lost.</p>\r\n <p> I am resolved, because I know that eventually this will end. I am a nihilist. As I enter the last steps, I feel like the world I am a part of is crashing down. When I walk in the door, the tv is on. It\'s the Simpsons season premier.</p>'),(2025,'<p>My face is on fire again. Something happens to me that makes my eyes puffy and my skin flare up and a seething pain crawl under the surface of my face like deadly crabs circulating my jaw and taking tiny little bites ignited by a single spark each.</p>\r\n<p>I dont have health insurance, so I didnt go to a dermatologist. I got lots of free advice though. have i tried ice? chamomile? calendula? nettles? oatmeal? exorcism? cunilingus? Ashley\'s mother told me not to eat dairy or fat, which is like, my whole diet. She told me to buy every healing product from While Foods and rub it all over my naked body in an ecstatic \"om\" too. Francoise, my new best friend, told me she had the same \"thing\" a few weeks ago and that the dermatologist told her it was common \"in this area\".</p>\r\n<p>The last time I had this rash, which was exactly two weeks ago, I went to see The Breeders, and for the moment, the itches were relieved. I hopped in place at a packed Slim\'s to such classics as \"Head to Toe\".</p>\r\n<p><i>Your FACE. LOOKS GOOD. TO ME.<br>\r\nYOUR FACE LOOKS GOOD TO ME</i><br> rinse, repeat. I plotted out a performance in which just Kim and I populated the club, me dancing in place alone, her crooning and wailing, crooning and wailing. oh mama. audio catharsis heals.</p>\r\n<p>Jay is bitching in the kitchen again. it comforts me. He has been finding rough pen tattoos all over his hand inexplicably this evening because someone left a capless pen in the utensils bucket and the metal spatula got all gooey. I may have irritated my face by changing the kitty litter today, the dustbowls of kitty kansas circling my face.Our labor leaves marks.</p>\r\n<p>really, im flaring up on the surface because my body and self are irritated in every way. I am maladjusted, underexercised, overcrowded, brazen, bitchy, and generally, a bad person. If you could read all my bad thoughts right now youd know why my skin is reacting so badly to me. I am a bad, rotten, itchy egg. I\'m just waiting now for pills to knock me out, or for Jay\'s mint chocolate chip cookies to be done. Whichever happens first. '),(2026,'<p>\r\nthe following is an excerpt from an email: </p>\r\n<p>Further, while we are on the subject of  le onion, let me digress for\r\nmon entertainment, and ton edification, a most noble vegetable!  As\r\nnoble as Life itself!   <<  No wonder the Egyptian peasant, and peasants\r\nall around this world for that matter, love it.  The ancient Jewish\r\npeople refused to give it up for heavenly Manna.  All relative, my dear\r\nWatson.  >>   But to get back to the O,  it is like life, layer after\r\nlayer;   each an essence in itself, yet cannot individualize, without\r\nthat which is before , or that  which  is after;  it is a n IT , for the\r\ncomposite layer,  yet  always a layer is but a layer and can never be a\r\nform in itself.   also, it is pungent, alive and sweet;  and so is\r\nLife.   it brings tears to a housewife eyes during the peeling process,\r\nand so is dear LIFE as it unfolds!</p>'),(2027,'<P>We made vejigantes; Puerto Rican warrior masks used originally to scare off the enemy. Creations of paper mache and wire; masks with horns and gnashing teeth. Mine was bright green, with orange and red eyes. Scary pointed teeth. A cross between a sleestack and a vampire. I wore it with a huge black grim reaper-esque hooded cloak. We went to the Beacon Theater on Halloween, to see MMW. What a scene. Most revelers were in costume. In my oversized cloak, it was impossible to tell that I am female. Most girlies wore girlie costumes; sexy vampires or devils. Lots of glitter and giggles. OUr faces were shocking in this place, where everyone is chemically altered and content. I caught them off guard, when I lifted my head and the cloak hood slipped back. I wasn\'t a good vibe. I liked it. I took to standing in the lobby, during the break in music, leaning against the wall. I would pick someone, typically a young nymphette type, there to be seen more than to enjoy the environment, and stare. Long and penetrating. I was creepy. It made me laugh. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>It still does. </P>\r\n'),(2028,'<P>We made vejigantes; Puerto Rican warrior masks used originally to scare off the enemy. Creations of paper mache and wire; masks with horns and gnashing teeth. Mine was bright green, with orange and red eyes. Scary pointed teeth. A cross between a sleestack and a vampire. I wore it with a huge black grim reaper-esque hooded cloak. We went to the Beacon Theater on Halloween, to see MMW. What a scene. Most revelers were in costume. In my oversized cloak, it was impossible to tell that I am female. Most girlies wore girlie costumes; sexy vampires or devils. Lots of glitter and giggles. OUr faces were shocking in this place, where everyone is chemically altered and content. I caught them off guard, when I lifted my head and the cloak hood slipped back. I wasn\'t a good vibe. I liked it. I took to standing in the lobby, during the break in music, leaning against the wall. I would pick someone, typically a young nymphette type, there to be seen more than to enjoy the environment, and stare. Long and penetrating. I was creepy. It made me laugh. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>It still does. </P>\r\n'),(2029,'<p>Triple Americano:<br>\r\n<ul>\r\n   <li>3 espresso shots</li>\r\n   <li>run water throuqh the double until large cup full</li>\r\n</ul></p>\r\n<p>It smells like <i>Good Morning</i>.  It\'s good for when you have just slept through your Philosophy test and, as a result, will most likely fail the course.</p>\r\n<p>There was one thing positive about today, however, and that thing was picking up my opt-out cheque.  Every full-time student at my school is automatically covered under a health plan.  If you already have health coverage, you may opt-out of the plan and  receive a cheque for $207.00, provided that you can prove you are already covered.</p>\r\n<p>After discussinq Ethernet in our Data Communications lecture for two hours; Eli and I picked up our cheques and then made the journey to HMV with Zoli.  I did my part in the fight against terrorism by purchasing the <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0120202\" target=\"new\">State and Main</a> DVD as well as the new <a href=\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=1510404587/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/ArtistID=TRUBY*RAINER+TRIO/ITEMID=1400239\" target=\"new\">TrÃ¼by Trio DJ Kicks</a> disc.</p>\r\n<p>I also laughed while readinq the caption for the photograph of a 7.2 ton <i>Daisy Cutter</i> bomb on the front page of the National Post this morning.  They use conventional warheads and create a mushroom cloud.  The caption said they were used in Iraq and helped lower Iraqi troop morale.  Apparently the US is dropping these things on Afghanistan.</p>\r\n<p>Sucks to be them </p>'),(2030,'<p>\r\nwell. peter and i were supposed to go to australia today. a lot of people did a lot of things to enable this. oof loaned me his credit card to buy the tickets, since i don\'t have one, then he ran all over town for me yesterday because the airline needs to see the credit card holder in person to verify the ticket purchase. peter ran all over to get tools to take apart our bikes, so that we could ride them in australia. tuggy paypal\'d me half the money for the tickets. and xep rode me around on her bike last night to help me run various pre australia errands.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen, this morning, as i packed up my clothes and got my stuff together, i went to grab my passport  from the place i thought it was, and it wasn\'t there. and i don\'t know where it is. places where it is not:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<pre>\r\nunder the desk\r\non the desk\r\nin the orange crate\r\nin any of the file folders in either of my file boxes\r\nthe dresser drawers\r\nunder the dresser\r\non top of the dresser \r\nin any of the boxes on top of the dresser\r\nin the closet\r\nthe closet floor\r\nany of the boxes in the closet\r\non the counter\r\non the bookshelf\r\nbehind or under the bookshelf\r\nin any of my jacket pockets\r\nin any of the pockets of any of my bags\r\nin any of my record crates\r\nunderneath my turntables\r\nunder the mixer\r\nbehind the speakers and amp\r\nunder the amp\r\nin the cd crates\r\nin the cd books\r\nin the pile of clothes\r\nin the clothes hamper\r\nin the bottom of any of the crates of clothes\r\nin the bathroom\r\nin the bathroom cabinet\r\nin the fishing tackle box of hair gear in the bathroom\r\non top of the fridge\r\nbehind the fridge\r\n</pre>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni can go get a new passport tomorrow and wait 24 hours for it to be issued, and pay some money for it, and we could fly on friday. rebooking the flight will cost about 500 dollars each. i have the money, but i am very very angry at myself and feel like i shouldn\'t go because it\'s unrealistic for me to think that i will always have the money to cover for my own irresponsibility.\r\n</p><p>\r\nright now my life is based on me having the money to cover my ass in the face of a total inability to exist in regular society. i can\'t hold a fulltime job, last time i tried, i came home and cried every night and quit after three weeks. the last couple of times i have had work, it\'s been contract, and the last contract gig i had, i fucked up. i fucked up in such a way  that my partner and good friend lisa had to call me and be upset with me. i dropped the ball, and it was a really easy ball that i should have been able to hang onto. generally, with regular work, people have a hard time working with me, and i don\'t do well. \r\n</p><p>\r\neven when i have something on my schedule that i would LIKE to do, something other than work, i often can\'t live up to the challenge. just this weekend i missed the second day of the alex grey workshop that i was really excited about and enjoying, because i overlslept. i should not have overslept, because i went to be at 2am, which isn\'t that late, and 8 hours should be enough sleep for me to get up. but it\'s not.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni have very little responsibility, very few things i really need to take care of. and yet i can\'t pull it off. my taxes languish. i haven\'t invested the dwindling amount in my money market account, and i haven\'t been able to force myself to go out and get a job, any job, so i can pay my rent by working instead of from my savings. \r\n</p><p>\r\nlast time i travelled, to austin, i ended up paying an extra 300 dollars on my ticket due to indecision and flakiness.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen will i learn how to be on time for things, how to do what i say i\'m going to, and how to hold  a regular job so i can get by? \r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\'t know what to do. i guess i should go to a shrink and learn why i can\'t seem to sleep less than ten hours a night, why it takes me forever to fall asleep even when i\'m very tired, why my brain won\'t shut up and let me go to bed when i want to. i should learn why i always feel like i\'m in a bubble removed from other people, why when i talk i don\'t feel like it\'s me talking, and why i can\'t seem to understand the consequences of my actions.\r\n</p><p>\r\nto tell you something very honest:  i generally believe that this life and this plane is not where i was meant to be, and that\'s why even the most rudimentary aspects of existence are  a struggle for me. i often believe that i don\'t exist at all, and that everything around me is a pointless charade. \r\n</p><p>\r\nsometimes i feel like if i could distill my true desires from the desires imposed on me by society, then i would be truly free. working within the frame work of median normal humanity is a constant struggle for me, and always has been. i know that the line between getting by and being really truly insane is kind of thin for me, and sometimes i wonder if i would be better off just crossing it. instead of keeping up the fight.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmaybe it is time to find a shrink.\r\n</p><p>\r\nanyway.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere i sit. my flight leaves in one hour and i am packed. but i am not going anywhere. \r\n</p><p>\r\neverything they say about me is true.\r\n</p> \r\n<tt>\r\n[x] drama<br>\r\n[x] spiritual<br>\r\n[x] tragedy<br>\r\n</tt>'),(2031,'<p>the binge that won\'t unhinge.</p><p>\r\nthe guilt of drinking, you drag people into your shit. - look at me</p>\r\nhaving to stagger to class after smoking way too much, drinking way too much coffee, reading about gary snyder, and thinking about the subculture of the subcultures subculture... <br>big money grabber, not bass master general, i am talking about thacker, garrett. well he says, it takes all kinds - to drink cheap wine - you pray to your god, and i\'ll pray to mine.</p><p>\r\nthe beats, and snyder never mixed.</p><p>I was listening to money grabba talk about chasing women, drinking way too much, doin some intense nonsense. his life, my life, seeing the interconnected-ness of his life compared to mine, where the common ground stems... the man, he is 38, i 26. </p><p>he was telling me about a time when he decided to sleep in a crack hotel outside of new orleans, or maybe it was jacksonville... anyway, he decides that its better to be in the crack hotel than the car, with all the skeeters biting... writes a song, records it then and there, telling he wished I could hear the black women talking about fucking in the next room. - addiction, Curtis Mayfield. -- </p><p>an almanac of subculture is what we need.</p>I think about my X\'s and how they would all open the door for me, no matter what state I am in. save one, maybe two. Who knows, maybe none of em\' would. I care, though...</p><p>res ipsa loquitur. - the thing speaks for itself.</p><p>She gets a little hazy, when I think of her now, first love - reminding me of snyder\'s \"Four Poems for Robin.\"</p>\r\n<p>.... and, believe it or not, I am all to sober - right now...</p>\r\n<p>having not had, a good meal - save Sams on the east side of town. drinking nothing but port and coffee. smokin \"nigga weed\" as the black man explained to me... listening to son house, again. some more of that, and little more of this. I found out today, from a cousin, that I am 1/8 cherokee, or something like that - maybe more. Also, that my brother\'s wife is pregnant - he didnt tell me that - the cousin did. kinda sad, sad for him. Realizing that, that, that, I am not a part of this or that. Wondering if the EuroDollar is going to sink Amerika - and if the 15 gazillion dollars we spent on the euro was a prophecy of something bad to come. Why, why the world trade Center? I am not talkin Alex Jones here, I am talking about the 5% that can\'t afford to loose their place in Life - the boardgame. </p> <p>You know! The political economy of democratic transitions!</p>\r\n<p>I think it has something to do with the drinking<br>\r\nor the smoking,<br>\r\nor the drugs - <br>\r\nthat I have fallen, again.<br>\r\nI feel okay, I feel -<br>\r\nsorta, ALIVE<br>\r\nin the fog,<br>\r\nas if I can see - something,<br>\r\nthat I could not see before,<br>\r\nI am not sure where I am, nor<br>\r\nwhere I am going today, or -<br>\r\ntomorrow  - and tomorrow <br>\r\nI might open my eyes some more<br>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nGreen fields,<br>\r\nmake me smile<br>\r\nunder a cloudy sky<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nI think of you,<br>\r\namongst the grass<br>\r\nspring time<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\ncome Closer,<br>\r\nblue Light,<br>\r\na congress of lawnmowers<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nWinter Rain,<br>\r\nacross a blue Veil,<br>\r\nI look up!<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nFresh coffee -<br>\r\na Heavy Sky,<br>\r\n-Just for me</p>\r\n'),(2032,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2032_1.jpg\" height=\"150\" align=\"left\"><p>the binge that won\'t unhinge.</p><p>\r\nthe guilt of drinking, you drag people into your shit. - look at me</p>\r\nhaving to stagger to class after smoking way too much, drinking way too much coffee, reading about gary snyder, and thinking about the subculture of the subcultures subculture... <br>big money grabber, not bass master general, i am talking about gt. well he says, it takes all kinds - to drink cheap wine - you pray to your god, and i\'ll pray to mine.</p><p>\r\nthe beats, and snyder never mixed.</p><p>I was listening to money grabba talk about chasing women, drinking way too much, doin some intense nonsense. his life, my life, seeing the interconnected-ness of his life compared to mine, where the common ground stems... the man, he is 38, i 26. </p><p>he was telling me about a time when he decided to sleep in a crack hotel outside of new orleans, or maybe it was jacksonville... anyway, he decides that its better to be in the crack hotel than the car, with all the skeeters biting... writes a song, records it then and there, telling he wished I could hear the black women talking about fucking in the next room. - addiction, Curtis Mayfield. -- </p><p>an almanac of subculture is what we need.</p>I think about my X\'s and how they would all open the door for me, no matter what state I am in. save one, maybe two. Who knows, maybe none of em\' would. I care, though...</p><p>res ipsa loquitur. - the thing speaks for itself.</p><p>She gets a little hazy, when I think of her now, first love - reminding me of snyder\'s \"Four Poems for Robin.\"</p>\r\n<p>.... and, believe it or not, I am all to sober - right now...</p>\r\n<p>having not had, a good meal - save Sams on the east side of town. drinking nothing but port and coffee. smokin \"nigga weed\" as the black man explained to me... listening to son house, again. some more of that, and little more of this. I found out today, from a cousin, that I am 1/8 cherokee, or something like that - maybe more. Also, that my brother\'s wife is pregnant - he didnt tell me that - the cousin did. kinda sad, sad for him. Realizing that, that, that, I am not a part of this or that. Wondering if the EuroDollar is going to sink Amerika - and if the 15 gazillion dollars we spent on the euro was a prophecy of something bad to come. Why, why the world trade Center? I am not talkin Alex Jones here, I am talking about the 5% that can\'t afford to loose their place in Life - the boardgame. </p> <p>You know! The political economy of democratic transitions!</p>\r\n<p>I think it has something to do with the drinking<br>\r\nor the smoking,<br>\r\nor the drugs - <br>\r\nthat I have fallen, again.<br>\r\nI feel okay, I feel -<br>\r\nsorta, ALIVE<br>\r\nin the fog,<br>\r\nas if I can see - something,<br>\r\nthat I could not see before,<br>\r\nI am not sure where I am, nor<br>\r\nwhere I am going today, or -<br>\r\ntomorrow  - and tomorrow <br>\r\nI might open my eyes some more<br>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\nGreen fields,\r\nmake me smile\r\nunder a cloudy sky\r\n\r\nI think of you,\r\namongst the grass\r\nspring time\r\n\r\ncome Closer,\r\nblue Light,\r\na congress of lawnmowers\r\n\r\nWinter Rain,\r\nacross a blue Veil,\r\nI look up!\r\n\r\nFresh coffee -\r\na Heavy Sky,\r\n-Just for me</pre>\r\n'),(2033,'<P>the other day, i went into my boss\'s office and told her that i don\'t think i am working to my full capacity.  i don\'t feel like i am giving enough to the company.  i think i need more work to do.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>she told me that she didn\'t want to overwork me. she doesn\'t want me to burn out at a young age.  she thinks i could be the editor-in-chief if i just give myself time. instead of adding another editor to my roster, so that i am the assistant to more than her, why don\'t we find some \"special projects\" for me to do?  i could reorganize the filing system, or i could read more unsolicited manuscripts, or i could...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i sit at my desk and i work really hard.  i answer emails from authors and i keep spreadsheets that show what books are where in the production process, and i run numbers to make sure books are selling, and i write the copy that goes on the jacket flaps and the backs of mass-market paperbacks, and i keep files of the reviews and correspondence and contracts and royalty statements.  i do a hell of a lot.  i have these binders that sit on my bookshelf: i keep copies of all of the book orders for author\'s copies (required under contract!), and i keep copies of transmittal forms and check requests and contract requests and remainder orders.  i have a list of which copyeditors are okay to use for which authors, who is going to what convention when, which mechanicals we\'ve looked at and which we haven\'t . . .</P>\r\n\r\n<p>but i have this button that is clipped to my calendar, and it says:</p>\r\n\r\n<P><I>of course i don\'t look busy.  i did it right the first time.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<P>translated, it means that doing all of that takes about 1/6 of my day.  so i spend 5/6 taking two hour lunches and chatting with my friends on AIM and answering emails and calling people who live in texas and missouri, and making liner notes for mix cds.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and i <i>want to do more</i>.  i can\'t seem to get it into anyone\'s head that i could do at least three times the amount of work that i am doing now and still spend one-third of my day answering emails and goofing off.  i don\'t want to do more filing and i don\'t want to read more slush.  i want to do some real work that has real results.  i don\'t want to do make-work.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>yesterday i reorganized my filing system <i>for the third time</i> in two months.  i also reorganized all my spreadsheets.  i also reorganized my binders.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'m dying of boredom, and nobody cares.</p>'),(2034,'<p>I wrote this in Mexico, on the honeymoon, the Monday after, over a week ago now. Talk about feeling alive...I reread it and I relive it immediately. It\'s been a while since something has happened in my life (and not just out in the world) that makes me feel alive. It\'s that first instant when you jump in cold, spring-fed water and your head tingles, only it lasted for over a day. </P>\r\n<p>What a ride, man. What a fucking ride.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>We got married roughly 38 1/2 hours ago.  It was awesome.  And I donâ€™t mean that just in the sense that it was \"cool\" or \"great\" but also, that I am in awe of that moment.  It took less than 10 minutes and went by so very much faster.  The reception was a blur of a social occasion, but the wedding itself is burned into my mind.>/p>\r\n<p>Friday night at 12:30, Iâ€™m finally done for the day.  Iâ€™m at Robâ€™s (my best man) place with his girlfriend and him.  I wouldnâ€™t see Pam again until she walked down the aisle.  We get a little high and nerves and paranoia come into play.  I think Rob thought I was in a bad way, but I wasnâ€™t.  My voice was shaking just because I was emotional.  I was feeling very alive going through what is pretty much the only rite of passage still left for someone like me (prom? graduation? theyâ€™re jokes compared to this).  The planning and organization that we did were really stressful and tried our last nerve, both collectively and individually.  And now, here we were, the night before D-Day so to speak, and my heart was pounding in my chest.  It wasnâ€™t fear, though, just anticipation, of the unknown.  How would I handle it?  How would she handle it?  How much would we be affected by those around us?  Eventually, I was able to fall asleep and slept pretty well, all things considered.</P>\r\n<p>Of course, the next morning I couldnâ€™t eat much.  Half a plate of huevos a la Mexicana and one cup of coffee. (The night before, at the rehearsal dinner, I hadnâ€™t eaten much either, as the smallest hint of nervousness causes my stomach to close shop.) After breakfast, I had a couple of hours to myself, which I spent getting cleaned up, listening to music and reading the paper.  My heart was no longer racing.  The only physical sign of anxiety was sweaty palms.  And these stayed with me for most of the remaining time before the ceremonyâ€”while my cousin gave me my last haircut of bachelorhood, while Rob, Tim (groomsman) and myself drove out to the location, and while we got dressed.  They would disappear though, once I was dressed and started meeting and greeting.  All was well and good until our friend Ivy got there and I spoke with her.  She says how sheâ€™s nervous and is on the verge of tears.  I tell her I canâ€™t talk with her anymore if thatâ€™s the case, because sheâ€™ll make me start crying.  But with that, the nervousness returns the sweaty palms, this time to be joined by dry mouth.  I disappear upstairs to calm myself, pop an Altoid, and practice what I have to say a few last times.  I only get it right a couple of times.</p>\r\n<p>Downstairs, the girls keep us waiting a few minutes, until finally they are ready.  I give the DJ a nod and the opening notes of Elvis doing \"Itâ€™s Now or Never\" fill the courtyard.  I walk my mom to her seat and hear laughter as some of the crowd recognizes the song.  Mom doesnâ€™t start crying or anything, as I thought she would, but hoped she wouldnâ€™t.  I take my place beside Trevor, our friend who sent in his $25 to become a minister.  The first set of attendants, Roger and Lorey follow us, and then Tim and Christy (who is Timâ€™s lady).  Rob and Amy (Pamâ€™s sister and maid of honor) wait at the door and behind them, I can see the edges of Pamâ€™s dress, but not her.  Soon though, they clear my field of vision and I see her, but not very well.  Rob and Amy reach their spots and the music changes to Mazzy Starâ€™s \"Fade Into You.\"  A few people, and then everyone, rise to their feet as she and her dad walk down the aisle, out the back door of a Tuschan-style villa in the Texas Hill Country.  Itâ€™s an edited version of the song, but I couldnâ€™t tell you anything about it; I knew it was playing but I couldnâ€™t hear it.  The first (and still the best) word that comes to my mind is \"stunning.\"  I had an idea of how her dress looked but hadnâ€™t seen it, had no idea how good sheâ€™d look in it.  Her eyes are literally sparkling and I can tell sheâ€™s on as much of a natural high as I am.  Finally, she is again with me.  There are no butterflies, no heart palpitations, no sweaty palms.  Sheâ€™s beautiful and sheâ€™s mine.</P>\r\n<p>Trevor begins to speak the words we wrote, the words we didnâ€™t want to write initially, hoping he would do it, only to realize we would be able to say whatever we wanted, to tell people what we thought of marriage and how we were going to treat it.  As he goes on, Trevorâ€™s voice is steady, but his hands are shaking so much that I wonder how he can read the words.  I hear cameras clicking every now and then and see the photographer and videographer moving behind Trevor.  He gets to the lines about the beginnings of love and I catch Tim and Christyâ€™s eyes at the part about them; theyâ€™re smilingâ€”all big.</P>\r\n<p>In what seems to be no time at all, Trevor comes to the vows, the moment of truth.  Hereâ€™s where I donâ€™t know whatâ€™s going to happen, how my body and mind will handle the moment.  Being the quiet person that I am, I expect my voice to not be much louder than a choked whisper, barely audible to all but Trevor and Pam.  But from somewhere inside me, peace and strength issue forth, and my voice is clear, steady and loud enough for everyone to hear.  I know 150 of our closest friends and family are there, but we might as well have been surrounded by cardboard cutouts.  Itâ€™s just her and me.  For a secondâ€”a half of a secondâ€”I forget a line, but recover and nobody knows.  As she returns the vows to me, there is no tremble in her voice, no hesitancy in her delivery, and her words are also clear and loud enough to be heard by all.</P>\r\n<p>Continuing, Trevor flubs a line about the rings, but probably only the three of us know.  We slip our rings of fire onto their respective fingers.  Then the audience is asked for their blessing, and they respond with a rousing \"Amen!\" and a few whoops and yells.  After blessing us with a Buddhist proverb about love and compassion, Trevorâ€™s voice practically turns giddy as he says, \"By the power vested in me by the Universal Church of Life, I now pronounce you husband and wife.  You may kiss the bride.\"  I do just that and there is much cheering, though Iâ€™m still not sure if itâ€™s the people there with us or all in my head.  So much cheering that I think most people donâ€™t hear the five years of inevitability line, which probably would have also gotten a good laugh.  Dean Martin starts singing \"Ainâ€™t That a Kick in the Head\" as we walk up the aisle, while orchids, purple and white, are showered upon us.  At the steps I catch one, a good sign for that day if ever there was one.</P>\r\n'),(2035,'<p>\r\na canvas filled with black, represented by other colours. found history: lost life. the pitch black, represented by blue, had been engulfed on all sides by the blood red of black. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnever in my life had so many yellows and oranges shown up. how could they have known the outcome. the outcome of which remains top secret. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe rulers remain silent. they remain idle. they remain informed. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe painting, thought to have been destroyed by itself some years prior, had surfaced. information which should have remained behind closed doors was leaked to the public by oil and water. they knew the location of the jacket. the jacket used by the one with the brush. the one with the brush who filled the canvas with black, represented by other colours. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nword of the canvas destroying itself had spread wide and far. almost as wide and far as information about the frame. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe scripture, not ours, but theirs, depicts a man dressed in black, represented by other colours, holding a frame. through the frame sprays a bright light. so bright everything turns pitch black. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit was thought, that whomever held the frame held the power to lay waste to whole bodies of land and water. the four flags each took a side of the frame.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe pacifist flag encased their piece in a concrete slab which replaced the medium size lake that once acted as a destination for migrating heards.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe reactionary flag used their piece to project an image of greatness to the rest of the world. on display within a bulletproof mountain, it has been said that every forth grader within the flag had seen it.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe third flag ordered to have their piece fired into space, ensuring forever, that the frame would never be put back together. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nno one knows what the forth flag did with their piece. the wordless flag is an empire of silence. they know what to say when and hardly ever make a sound. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter old mountains gave way to new ones, the people had long since forgotten about the frame which once held the canvas covered in black, represented by other colours. they had forgotten about the one who held the brush. the only one who had actually died. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe brush laughed.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2036,'<p>one week and one day after being laid off.  if layoffs have stages, i have passed through numbness, panic, anger, depression, panic again, and more panic, and now i\'ve hit an interesting one for which i can\'t think of a catchy one-word description.</p>\r\n<p>i\'ll explain.</p>\r\n<p>the first time i ever got laid off, i was on a contract and it ended a month early.  big deal.  i was expecting it, and so it wasn\'t a real layoff.  i was prepared.  this time i am so far from prepared it\'s stupid, and i have a feeling i won\'t have any offers in two weeks like i did after that contract.  by all rights, i should be feeling totally lost and confused and adrift and (checks thesaurus) well, you get the idea.</p>\r\n<p>and i am, but it\'s starting to feel okay.  not just because i now get to sit around in my pajamas until 4 PM eating doritos and writing my novel.</p>\r\n<p>first you have to understand that when i moved out here, i was leaving a big corporation to come work for a dotcom, just like a lot of other suckers.  i probably would still be working for that big corporation if i hadn\'t quit, and i probably would have gotten some sweet promotion action, and i would have continued to come to work every day in khakis and polo shirts to talk about quality and count the hours till the time i could go home and put on green glitter nail polish and listen to momus without my headphones.  petty oppressions, petty rebellions.  but this isn\'t about evil big business versus good youth culture (they\'re both the same fucking thing, now more than ever).</p>\r\n<p>\'cause the dotcom was supposed to be about coming to work every day in a t-shirt and sneakers (and it was).  it was supposed to be about hanging out with a bunch of geeks and adjourning to the game room on a regular basis for geeky recreation (and it was).  it was, most importantly, supposed to be about working on a cool product that would give the average person (assuming he or she could afford semi-useful expensive gadgetry) more fun and ease in his or her life (and it was).</p>\r\n<p>and it was supposed to be about letting down the corporate facade and being real for a change. i\'m only realizing now that, somehow, it wasn\'t.</p>\r\n<p>it\'s not just that we knew the day was coming that we\'d all move across the bay and put on the khakis and find something positive to say about marketing and take away the technology from the average geek.  though we did.</p>\r\n<p>i\'m just guessing, but i think it has something to do with an attitude toward tech and toward the business from which it is inextricable that we all just sort of casually assumed and forgot to forget when the industry, like a five-tier wedding cake launched straight up into the air by a cake-sized catapult, began to decelerate to a stop and then come back down at a rate of 9.8 grams per meter squared.  that we can look this rate up (because we haven\'t taken physics since high school) by typing the words \"gravitational constant\" and hitting return is, especially to a librarian\'s son, psychotically cool.  when we go out and get jobs related to the phenomenon that provides this power we probably don\'t take enough cynicism along.</p>\r\n<p>well - to qualify, because i\'m starting to feel like a salon article here (old news and inaccurate to boot) - i definitely didn\'t.  and as i sit here typing this (fully dressed, those of you who are forming a mental picture will be relieved to learn) instead of searching the job boards yet again, it\'s all rushing back to my head like blood after a faint and i\'m starting to feel really perversely good about this.  i\'ve lost the paycheck that i and my girlfriend and my cats and my credit card companies need to put food in our bellies, but i\'ve regained some strange sense of perspective.</p>\r\n<p>suddenly it doesn\'t really matter to me where i live.  (cognitive dissonance between alleged coolness of san francisco versus high rent and vapid hipsters -> poof.)  suddenly it doesn\'t really matter to me how privileged my job is.  (cognitive dissonance between high salary and supposedly altruistic internet business versus trickle of layoffs and elitist gadget requirements -> poof.)  suddenly it doesn\'t really matter to me what i have to wear to work or how much like me my co-workers are.  (cognitive dissonance between casual atmosphere versus danger of getting fucked by management at any moment -> poof.)</p>\r\n<p>when and if i get a new job i may forget all of these lessons, though they seem intensely obvious to me now.  so i have to write them down just in case.</p>\r\n<p>first you have to understand that when i moved out here, i '),(2048,'<P>i wish i had images for this story.  i swear i will get a camera when i have paid off my credit cards, my car, and my student loans.  i SWEAR IT!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>at lunch today i made my usual tofu dogs on a wheat bun with Stubb\\\'s sauce and spicy mustard.  i put a dog and a half in the microwave because i was really hungry.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the dogs were touching.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have witnessed strange activity with all brands of the tofu dogs before.  tofu dogs in general do wierd things in the microwave.</P>\r\n<P>some boil up and turn into balloons.</P><P>  some get blisters. </P><P> some do nothing. </P><P> this brand i have at the moment i thought were the do nothing type.  but when i put two of them in the microwave, they welded together.  i dont just mean stuck together either.  all brands get sticky when microwaved.  these fused together with a hard black shell.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>they are the kind with the cartoon fat itallian tofu guy on the bag.</P>\r\n\r\n<B><TT>Thu May 16 15:23:38 PDT 2002</TT></B>\r\n<P>i felt the need to make an adendum to this piece because information was made available that sheds a new light on tofu dog cooking techniques.  Anna T. came back from her recent vacation and placed an ad for the Hot Diggity Dogger on my chair at work.  she clipped it from the  Skymall magazine.  i did a little investigation and came up with a picture fromthe website.  far be it from me to shamelessly promote consumerism and faceless corporations that create crap like a dr. seuss assembly line, but leu and behold:</P>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">\r\n'),(2049,'<p>\"Those fuckin cocksuckers downtown were tryin to screw me again,\" he said, his voice weak on the phone. He coughed, \"So I went online, took my timeâ€¦ went through the computer, and wouldn\'t you know it, I found the same goddamn snow tires, the nice ones, for three hundred bucks.\"<br>\r\n\"No kiddin,\" I said.<br>\r\n\"You know, you gotta use your instincts,\" he said. \"As human beings we all have instincts to sense when things are wrong, and maybe with evolution we\'re losing \'em but I\'ve still got mine.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He had been really sick. I was calling mostly to gauge how he sounded, after hearing from my mom, in her usual panicked tone, that he was really sick again.  The latest treatment had really done him in.  He said he\'d rather die than go on feeling this way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"They\'re gonna have to lower the treatment next time,\" he said. \"I can\'t keep feeling this way.\"<br>\r\n\"You should call Dr. Ely. Maybe go to Dartmouth-Hitchcock if you need to,\" I said. <br>\r\n\"I can barely make it Rutland. And your mother has to leave work. It\'s already enough trouble.\" <br>\r\n\"Well, get her on the phone, the doctor. Make sure this is how it\'s supposed to go. How\'s your white-blood-cell count?\" <br>\r\n\"I don\'t know.  The overall number wasn\'t good.  It\'s some overall count.  Not supposed to go below 1500, and mine was at 1090.  I won\'t let them do it again. I don\'t trust them. Those assholes in Rutland are stupid as hell.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My Dad was diagnosed about six years ago.  Lumps in his neck, swollen glands.  He knew there was something wrong. I seem to remember him being in bed a lot that Christmas, and he was waiting for his birthday, in January, when he would turn 65 and be covered under Medicare, before he went to the doctor.  It was lymphoma, which is a cancer of the blood, essentially.  Abnormal lymphocytes in the blood.  Lowered immune system.  Bad blood.  He had chemo then, and he was better for a while.  But he has other problems, and it\'s been getting worse, so he\'s in chemo again.  They said he wouldn\'t lose his hair this time.  Said it would be better, but they didn\'t really get into how sick he would feel.  Sicker than usual.  More immobile than usual.  But he\'s spent the last two years in and out of bed anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I try to sound positive. \"Well, get some rest.  It will get better.\" <br>\r\n\"Heh,\" he laughed and started to choke. \"Yah. Get some rest.  Everyone keeps sayin get some rest and that\'s all I do.  Get up for fifteen minutes and I\'m so wiped out I gotta lay down again.  All I do is watch TV.\" <br>\r\n\"You hear about the crash in Queens?\" <br>\r\n\"Yeah.  They don\'t know what\'s goin on down there.  Your friend lives near there.  What\'s her name? In Brooklyn.\" <br>\r\n\"Daphne. She doesn\'t live anywhere near Rockaway, though.\" <br>\r\n\"How was the wedding?  Did you guys have fun?\" <br>\r\n\"Yeah, it was a good time.  A lot of dancing.  Great food.\" <br>\r\n\"Tell me about it, what kind of food did they have?\" <br>\r\n\"Well, the dinner wasn\'t anything spectacular, but I guess at Jewish weddings the focus is often on the cocktail hour, because that\'s what everyone has first, and it\'s the only time anyone is really focused just on the food, because as soon as the dinner starts, there\'s dancing, through every course.\" <br>\r\n\"Yeah, yeah.\" <br>\r\n\"So, they had a sushi station, with ladies putting together plates for people, and they had a caviar and vodka station, because the groom\'s family was Russianâ€¦ and some other stuff.\" <br>\r\n\"Well that\'s great,\" he said.  \"You know, you\'re going to learn that life is made up of momentsâ€”just separate moments along the way when you\'re having fun and eating good foodâ€”and then the rest of the time is just waiting around, doing bullshit, waiting for the next good time.\" <br>\r\nI paused at this piece of wisdom.  Not very Zen of him, but I shouldn\'t be surprised.  It was smart in a way.  Life often feels that way. But it reminded me of a Sondheim lyric, from Into the Woods: \"Oh, if life were only moments, even now and then a bad one/ But if life were made of moments, then you\'d never know you had one.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"You know those movies you told us to rentâ€¦ god damn were they weird.\" <br>\r\n\"I thought you\'d like themâ€¦ I mean I know they\'re weird.\" <br>\r\n\"God damn.  That backwards movieâ€¦ your mother couldn\'t figure it out, but I did pretty soon. I said \'they\'re showing it all backwards!\'\"<br>\r\n\"I thought you\'d like Dead Man. The Indian named Nobody.\" <br>\r\n\"Yah,\" he said. \"It was so weird!  He rides the train to the end of the line and expects to have a job, and they\'re all laughing at himâ€¦ and he walks down the street and there\'s a horse takin a piss and some whore blowing some guy.  That\'s what the Old West was like, you know.  Most people don\'t know that.  Guys shoving women down on tables in the saloons and goin at it right there.\" <br>\r\n\"Yeah, I thought it was a pretty good picture.\"  I was laying on my bed staring at the brick wall.  My feet were cold.  He sounded tired and old, more tired than usual, but he still seemed to perk up on the phone, almost moreso than when I was home and talking to him throughout the day. </p>\r\n<p>\"I\'m glad you had fun at the funeral,\" he said. <br>\r\n\"Wedding.\" <br>\r\n\"Right,\" he chuckled. \"Heh. You can see where my mind\'s at. I swear I\'m losing it.  You know, I could barely sleep last week. I didn\'t sleep.  For seven days and nights, I didn\'t sleep, and I didn\'t want to eat, and that\'s really what did it to me.  I was so sick and I couldn\'t escape it you know? Couldn\'t even fall asleep.  Felt like I was going crazy.  Never been so sick in my life.\"</p>\r\n<p>It\'s an unfortunate fact that I\'ve had to ignore this phrase when he says it, because it seems impossible to me that he could be so sick as to have \"never been so sick\" in his life at least once a month.  But that is apparently how it feels, at least every couple of months, so much that he can\'t ever seem to remember the last time, quite how bad it was the last time that it all seems brand new again. A new character of pain altogether, each time.<br>\r\n\"Remember two summers ago, when I was curled in bed and couldn\'t move?  It was like that time. But worse.\" <br>\r\n\"Were you throwing up like that time?\" <br>\r\n\"No, no.  But at least I\'m coming out of the woods now, not going into the woods.\" <br>\r\nInto the Woods. They came to see that show when they put it on in high school, to inaugurate the new George Washington Hall, and the Tang Theatre.  Jack Lemmon came, too.  Now he\'s dead, poor fella.  For the production that had trees made out of burlap that sat in mounds on the stage, and at the close of the opening number, the trees suddenly grew out of the stage to full height, rising above the proscenium.  </p>\r\n<p>The best times they\'ve had in the last ten years, my parents, have involved visiting me at school, coming to see plays in high school, my graduations from high school and college.  The last good time we all had probably, outside of the house, was three and a half years ago when they came to New York.  I put a five-hundred-dollar dinner on a credit card.  We went to see Ragtime on Broadway and my dad loved it.  He loves big production numbers, fancy sets.  He was a child of the forties, grew up listening to big band music on the radio.  That was the soundtrack to his adolescence, as the war came to a close he was fifteen.  Staying out and stealing beer with friends probably, listening to Benny Goodman. Glen Miller. Xavier Cougat.  He took a girl out to the city once, drove down from Kingston and went to the movies in Manhattan.  And there was Xavier Cougat and a forty-piece orchestra performing on the stage between shows.  It must have been at Radio City, but he doesn\'t remember where.  Some movie palace.  He couldn\'t believe it.  The band from the radio and there they were just doing intermission entertainment.</p>\r\n<p>I was home a few weeks ago and my dad had shingles.  He started getting them after his first round of treatment. Turns out this was common, but it meant more antibiotics.  \r\nI have to laugh sometimes, or, if not laugh, just treat it all rather lightly.  Like it\'s expected for all this to happen. For it just to get worse and worse.  Because that\'s how life goes.  Sometimes it just gets worse and worse and maybe someday it will just be over, but in the meantime we have to be strong. Pragmatic.</p>\r\n<p>\"You know, I always have something positive to say,\" he said. \"I\'m always trying to think something positive, but this time I really didn\'t know what to think.  It just felt so awful.  It was the bottom.  I couldn\'t even think straight, and when I did all I could think about was feeling awful.\"</p>\r\n<p>Whenever I explain to anyone what it is like when I go home, or tell someone who doesn\'t know that my dad is sick, they of course offer their apologies.  \"I\'m sorry,\" they are compelled to say. Sorry for the ways of life. Sorry that illness exists. Sorry that fate has dropped such suffering in your lap. Sorry that this has to happen to you, to anyone.  </p>\r\n<p>I\'m not sure how I would be if I had to live with sickness everyday, like my mother does.  She leaps at every new complaint, endlessly repeats to me how worried she is about him.  But how worried can one be?  I\'m not especially worried.  I haven\'t been terrified by the thought of his death since the first time I found out he was sick. I was nineteen and it all seemed too sudden, too soon.  I had school to think about.  And there were his debts. Who would pay those?  Some still remain, but it hardly matters.  Someday they won\'t be there anymore. And neither will he.  And though I spend energy preparing for such a circumstance, I don\'t know what it will be like.  What our lives will be like without him.  The idea of death is not hard to accept, especially when someone\'s life becomes defined by discomfort, and illness.  What is harder to conceive of is the reality of someone\'s absence.  I\'ve barely begun to collect the details of his life, to stash them away.  That is my job as his son, to be the bearer of his specific experience, to record it and make meaning from it.  For a long time I\'ve wanted to hear him talk about his divorce, this ancient love of which he\'s never honestly spoken.  About his resentments, or his shame. Why his other kids don\'t really talk to him much.  What happened in this former life that is so awful to put into words?  Or is it just that he does not want to remember having been sad, or having loved the wrong person, or he does not want to remember his life being ugly once.  </p>\r\n<p>I may not ever know the details.  He doesn\'t want to talk about unhappy things these days.  Doesn\'t want to watch weird movies.  He is not dying any faster than he was before, at least I don\'t think, so perhaps there is plenty of time.  He said once he just wanted to live to see me do something with my life.  Produce something, I guess.  I\'d better get a move on.</p>\r\n'),(2045,'it\'s been a long summer/fall. <br><br>\r\n\r\ndad just got home from kuwait, in the nick of time.  they were saying they would shoot any americans leaving the gates and he had to turn the rental car around that he was driving to the airport and wait while soldiers with machine guns checked out the area and made sure it was safe for him to go... <br><br>\r\n\r\n31 hours later, there he was, looking somewhat worse for the wear.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nwe both have all of these words to say about what we felt when we were apart, but we\'ve been married long enough that we just keep hugging and saying \"i love you, i missed you, i am glad we are together again.\" <br><br>\r\n\r\nthe prolonged fear has changed both of us.  he in fear for his life on a daily basis in a real way with danger in his face constantly, and me, imagining how he must feel in a place i cannot picture in my mind\'s eye. <br><br>\r\n\r\nwe long for a return to normal life, but we each know that what was normal before he left is no longer a possiblity, not just for us, but for everyone. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni wish that i was a crier.  i have never been able to weep out my stress as most women can.  i just hold it all in and write it down to send to the nether regions of cyberspace and hope that someone, somewhere, sometime, will learn a lesson the easy way, through the experiences of a stranger. <br><br>\r\n\r\nand this is the lesson....<br><br>\r\n\r\nlife is too short to be separated from someone you love for any reason but dire necessity.<br><br>'),(2046,'<p>A few weeks ago, I cashed in on 37.5 accrued vaction hours and split for Portland.  This was against the grave advice of my supervisor, who reminded me that my next check would be short 2.5 hours, and that I may want to re-evaluate my priorities.  I told him that had already been done - that\'s why I was going.  No glass desk or dockets or server problems.  I had better things to do.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Portland is gorgeous and wonderful and hides itself in fog and trees.  Fantastic things happened, and I saw old friends, and dug through cut-out bins and ate bento etc.  I have plenty to say about Portland and why it rocks, and why I am moving there next Spring.  I don\'t have much to say about my day trip to the coast, though, because it all comes off as flaky and/or perfunctory, but I\'ll try anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tyler and I climbed through a cliff near Oceanside.  A 30\' long tunnel had been hacked through the cliff - the inside was painted with third eyes and weird illuminati dayglo krylon.  On the far side of the tunnel there was a short, deserted beach - whitecaps bashed themselves on sea rock towers.  There was no horizon;  mist obscured the sky and water.  The only sound was sneakers on sand and the pounding of water on basalt.  We walked down the beach and kept our mouths shut. We were there for an hour or so, then the tide started creeping up the beach and we left through the tunnel.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>It just really strikes me that watching entropy incarnate put something inspiring in me.  Weeks later it still makes me feel calm and awestruck, and I don\'t worry about losing it.  It\'s a good feeling;  it makes me trust the universe.</p> \r\n\r\n'),(2047,'<p>Sometimes it\'s easier to write than it is to read.  Caught up in vortices of your own thought.  I was thinking about subway moments the other day.  Someone was telling me how much they hate riding the subway.  They\'re just not openning their eyes to what\'s happening around them.  That and they only ride the trains at rush hour (to and from work) which does in fact suck.  I like riding the subway.  It\'s like a gathering place for New Yorkers.  One of the few places where people stop and hang out if not with eachother, at least near eachother.  You see interesting things in the tunnels under the city.  People in love, children scurying underfoot, friends, lovers, enemies.  I know a guy who originally met almost all of his friends on the subway.  That\'s where I met him.  I remember standing next to a woman one morning and noticing bite marks on her neck.  I\'ve been yelled at and called a fag, found companions in drunken debauchery, held my girlfriend\'s hair back as she vomited, smelled the most horrible stench anyone could ever experience, seen some impressive busking and begging.</p><p>My top two NYC subway moments:</p><p>I got on the A train after dance class one evening and sat accross from a woman with a sketch pad.  We smiled at eachother, and she proceeded to draw my picture.  I complied by sitting very still for her.  When I stood to get off the train, she murmured, \"Oh, is this your stop?\"  I thought about trying to get a phone number or something, but that would have somehow ruined the moment.  Meeting her outside of that context would have moved the dream-like nature full force into reality.  I prefer the untainted memory.</p><P>The other great moment was Subway Twister.  About 11pm one Friday night I was getting on the A train to meet an ex at some bar downtown to \"watch her sing karaoke\" (long story).  I got on the train and noticed a group of people playing Twister.  I sat down near them to watch and they invited me to join in.  That made living in NYC all worth it, right there.  Playing Twister, on the subway, on a Friday night.</p><p>I\'m friends with those people now: a clown, a fag, a woman pining for a boy in France, a med student.  I currently have a massive crush on one of those girls and knowing these people has definitely made my life better.  It\'s funny.  If I had never asked Erin out, if she hadn\'t begged me to come spend time with her that night, if I hadn\'t argued with her about it those extra fifteen minutes, if I had gotten on a different subway car....</p>'),(2040,''),(2041,'<p>dog hair. sushi. bills. bedtime. tweezing. writing. traffic. toys. acid bluegrass. laundry. driving. naptime. cleaning. doorbell. mom. cliffsitting. saltair. dirty socks. chocolate. stickers. flannel pjs. kata. red red wine. juju. baggy pants. cowboys. the redroom. dog slobber. lost keys. phone calls. pink walls. lemonade. secret notes. friends. cinnamon altoids. hardware. dirty slide. maurice sendak. porn. window watching. greasy prima pizza. nightmares. crayons. girlflesh. hot showers. slippers. solo sex. cgi. mineral water. midnight expeditions. naptime. snuggling. morning fog. laptop. binoculars. laughter. nightlights. milk n cookies. poprocks. rubber ducks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Adult/Cult/Horror</p>'),(2042,'<p>Interesting thought while walking to work today:  I\'d really like to rent a car and just take off somewhere for a few days.  Economicaly, I think this is feasible.  Depending on the events of this upcoming week, I will definately be considering this course of action for Wednesday night through to Friday.</p>\r\n<p>I have a fairly sound idea of where I\'d like to travel to.  If I do decide to go through with this plan, the only question will be whether to play the spontaneity card or not.</p>\r\n<p>Obviously, if I\'m taking a multi-day trip, I\'ve got to stay with someone.  As much as I can actually afford a motel or desire to sleep in a car, I don\'t think so.</p>\r\n<p>I have a friend who is in the city this weekend.  He is driving to my place to meet me after I am done work.  I am going to suggest that we either go dowtown for big city hijinks or that we drive somewhere outside of the city.  I just want to go on a little adventure is all.  I don\'t think that is too much to ask for.  Some interaction with nature would be good, as well.</p>\r\n<p>I feel an intense need to discover something.</p>'),(2043,'<p>\r\nthis morning i walked to cafe abir, which i recently found out opens at the ungodly hour of 5:30am. just in time for me to get dinner before going to bed. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe streets in the lower haight were wet from yesterday\'s rain, but drying in uneven patches. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy friend neck once said something about people trying to pour as much concrete into san francisco as possible. i thought about that this morning as i looked at the cold soggy concrete. little squares of plant life grow in random increments on the sidewalk. the city has graciously allowed the occasional tree by cutting out a square of concrete.\r\n</p><p>\r\njust outside my apartment on page street i passed one of these lucky trees, growing crooked and fraternizing with a morning glory vine. the vine wrapped it\'s way halfway up the tree. the tree grew out of the square of earth rationed for it, stuck out over the sidewalk, and offered just enough of an idea of lushness to keep passersby sane.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni was about to cross divis, standing a few feet off the curb (as i often do, stupidly, while deciding whether or not to cross the street), when i saw a car to my approaching to my left. i hopped back out of it\'s way, five succinct hops back toward the curb, cartoon style. \r\n</p><p>\r\nas the car passed me, it turned out to be full of quite a few things, including chaya, who was sitting on someone\'s lap. she squealed upon recognizing me and i had time to grin at her too, which is pretty good because usually when people i know pass in cars, they\'re long gone before i can figure out what happened.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni kept a half grin at the chaya sighting as i continued my journey down divisadero. my eyes followed the car she was in for a little while. i was wondering if they were planning to turn around and say hi, but when they didn\'t, i didn\'t feel jilted. \r\n</p><p>\r\nbehind me, the sun was rising and turning the underside of all the clouds orange and pink. in front of me, a huge rainbow appeared.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni walked in a daze, turning back and forth between sunrise and rainbow. sunrise, rainbow. i tried walking backwards, to see the sunrise, and forwards into the rainbow with lots of pauses to look back. \r\n</p><p>\r\naround me, the lower haight fought back with seediness. i passed a bike frame, stripped of its tires, seat, handlebars, pedals. it was still locked to a pole. i went to the atm and shared the awning with a homeless fellow who had no pants on. i passed two winos, and passed them again on the way back home. the second time i passed them, i watched one of the grab a sip from a can concealed in a paper bag, and i had to hold my breath because one of the poor guys smelled so much like urine. i also passed a tall black man who cooed \"good morning\" at me. i passed a scruffy  white guy who was smoking and looked sort of like nick cave. he said good morning, too. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni burst into abir, elated post rainbow, and described the rainbow to the foreign man behind the counter there. <i>oh nice,</i> he approved. <i>did you have a camera?</i> i had been wishing for one, i explained, but no, no, i didn\'t have one.\r\n</p><p>\r\nas the foreign man made my sandwich, i watched the back of his neck. he was bald and the back of his neck was very red. on the other side of the counter from where he was busy building me a sandwich (<i>you want baguette?</i> he had asked me <i>it\'s fresh!</i> he added and made the decision for me. he was writing it down before i even said yes. but i did say yes) two cops sat, having some frothy coffee drinks. one of them asked <i>do you know who paul mccartney is? you know, from the beatles?</i> as a precursor to some conversation he hoped to have with the foreign counter man about paul mccartney. the counter man was offended: <i>of course i know who paul mccartney is</i> and the cop, shamed: <i>well.. well i didn\'t know if you.. well you might not have.. uh</i> realizing his mistake.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe counter man asked me if i wanted sprouts. i said i didn\'t really feel excited about sprouts. he said <i>yeah, i was wondering..</i> as if he must have agreed with me about sprouts. too loudly for the hour of morning, i hollered my annoyance with sprouts: <i>they\'re just so ungraceful!</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni accepted my sandwich, enveloped in a bleached white paper bag. this type of bag seems very popular with san francisco establishments. it is exactly like a brown paper bag, only white. it\'s like the upscale version of the brown paper bag.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni cradled my white paper bag and my chamomile tea all the way home. the sky had become less about getting my attention and more about filling itself with grey clouds during the time that i was in the cafe.\r\n</p><p>\r\nat home, i carefully picked the onions off the sandwich. i ate it quickly and happily, dropping chunks of mustard laden hummus onto my ankles. it always happens when i eat sloppy things cross legged that i drop food right onto my ankles.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin a few hours, it will be time to fly to australia for reals. i have a new passport and i\'m mostly packed, with gifts for brian and dirty laundry that i hope to launder down under. the bikes are all tucked into boxes virgo style, thanks to peter. i\'m going to have a little nap now, and then head to the other hemisphere of planet earth.\r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nother news: applying to <a href=\"http://www.reed.edu\">reed</a> for fall 2002. very hopeful, as they have a cool religions program, are in a cool city (portland), and do scholar sharing with <a href=\"http://www.slc.edu\">sarah lawrence</a>. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnanowrimo goes poorly for me, mostly because i can\'t stand one of my main characters and don\'t give a shit what happens to him. \r\n</p><tt>\r\n<br>-->\r\n<br><br>\r\n[x] non-fiction<br>\r\n[x] spiritual<br>\r\n[x] travel\r\n</tt>'),(2044,'<p>So far this morning, since waking up, I have, in no particular order:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>read <a href=\"http://slashdot.org\">slashdot</a> and the \r\n<a href=\"http://dailynews.yahoo.com\">\"real\" news</a></li>\r\n<li>played a quick game of unreal tournament</li>\r\n<li>thought about how nice a fresh bagel with cream cheese would taste</li>\r\n<li>made that wonderful weekend decision to just lounge in a robe on the couch for a while reading a <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0201347741/qid=1005503805/sr=1-1/ref=sr_1_13_1/102-2823654-3926548\">book</a>, listening to some <a href=\"http://www.shoutcast.com/directory/?genre=Ambient\">music</a>, instead of leaping in the shower righ off</li>\r\n<li>discovered the wonders of <tt><a href=\"http://packetstorm.decepticons.org/programming-tutorials/Assembly/online-tutorial/\">debug</a></tt> and write a cheesy little 36 byte asm program that tells me \"Howdy!\" 16 times.</li>\r\n<li>thought lusty thoughts about my lover(s?)</li>\r\n<li>half-heartedly looked for warez, but didn\'t find anything exciting</li>\r\n<li>thought lusty thoughts about my acquaintances</li>\r\n<li>decided against taking a nap</li>\r\n<li>grumbled becuase it was sunday and not saturday</li>\r\n<li>tried to decide if I wanted ordinary breakfast food or <a href=\"http://yp.yahoo.com/py/ypMap.py?Pyt=Typ&tuid=7957207&ck=2435004580&tab=B2C&city=Houston&state=TX&zip=77042&country=us&slt=29.744699&sln=-95.559898&cs=5&stat=:pos:0:regular:regT:1:fbT:0\">persian</a>  (barg! barg! barg! barg! WE LOVE BARG!)</li>\r\n<li>scratched in a debauched-bachelor sort of way</li>\r\n<li>wondered where the heck all my disk space went!  yikes!</li>\r\n<li>downloaded and burned a copy of <a href=\"http://www.openbsd.org\">OpenBSD 3.0</a>, or at least, an unofficial x86-only iso thereof. (from this crazy hungarian hacking/security/warez/nrrd FTP <a href=\"ftp://ftp.zedz.net\">site</a>)</li>\r\n<li>fed the cats</li>\r\n<li>wondered what fit of drunkeness on friday night led me to put a bottle of goldschlager on top of a speaker instead of back in the freezer???</li>\r\n<li>squirted a cat with a water bottle when she was being naughty.</li>\r\n<li>squirted her <em>again</em> when she started doing the same thing a minute later.  what, cat, like i\'d just forget what you were doing a minute ago???</li>\r\n<li>laughed at how much my apartment has changed since I got a female roommate and (her words) \"the girl bomb went off\" (e.g. there\'s furniture now)</li>\r\n</ul>'),(2050,''),(2051,'<p>I am so stressed.  Got to fill the stores with DEEZ BEANZ before christmas. Everyone in retail knows when christmas begins. Next friday, that\'s when. The day after thanksgiving. If you are a thinking commie, that day is national \"buy nothing\" day. I tend not to buy anything that day; my tradition is to go t the movies. Maybe Harry Potter this year. So Im not exactly a commie, but I support the anti-consumer spirit, with my mouth if not my money.</p>\r\n<p>But EVERYBODY KNOWS when christmas begins- the commies, the hippies, the buy-nothings, know-nothings, the atheists and the new agers. Most of all, me, and my jsp-coding co-hort, Kevin Blectum, who masquerades as an obedient coder at walmart.com. <i>They</i> know when christmas begins. This year, they are afraid.</p>\r\n<p>One might be able to guess the holidays are coming by the number of reddi whip canisters there are in the fridge at the bend. Same brand, different sizes, all in the side door of the fridge. Jay and I, who do most of the eating in the house, speculated about where they might have come from. </p>\r\n<p>Our best guess is that when Eve and her boyfriend Jim, my loveable cross-country camper-driving pals, offered to unload their trash bag full of condiments on us when they went to Portland, some duplicate reddi whips were discovered. Perhaps one was to eat with cake, the other to eat with nipple on some boring night in Utah where there was no beer, only sin.</p>\r\n<p>Runner-up would be that lulu bought the multiple canisters just to be absurd. SHe loves rhubarb pie. In fact, I dont believe Ive seen her get so excited about anything. </p>\r\n<p>In ANY CASE, kevin and I are working on this web site and we are super stressed because we both have lethal \"CAN_DO\" attitudes and underestimated our time limits. She came bouncing off the walls about her car battery dying and traffic in the rain and everything else, and noticed the reddi whip canisters in the fridge, and joked that we shold suck them down.</p>\r\n<p> \"HA hA! LEts WHIPPIT!!\" I joined in on the jollitude. Somehow in my world, jokes become super fantastic ideas for reality. Within minutes of our laughter we were sucking down nitrous from the cans to alleviate our respective states of distress.</p>\r\n<p>Oh the holidays. High-pitched helicopter frequencies still whip-whip through my ears at press time. and the last memory of any other audio, the faint shriek of Kevin:</p>\r\n<p>\"OOH, I see the meaning of life!! OOH!\"</p>\r\n<p> Some who wish to conflate and relate the east and the west say that to reach the sought after Buddhist state of <i>satori</i> is to be with God before he said, \"Let there be light.\" Apparently Kevin was picking up some heavy vibes of enlightenment, for in her whipped up state of higher powers she chased the dog through the kitchen yelling, \"YOU! YOu are naked! Put some clothes on, I can see your nipples.\"</p>'),(2052,'<p>it\'s a frigid morning in Boston.  28*F.\r\n\r\n<p>Jaime complains that his middle finger hurts when its below freezing, a result of a taxi cab opening his door and his slamming into it.  He flipped over the handlebars, over the door, and broke his middle finger.  He complains about the ache, but  I tell him he\'s dam lucky.\r\n\r\n<p>Orlando has a thin fall coat he brought from California and though we tell him that it will get even colder than this,  he doesn\'t seem to believe us.  Already though, the other night when we left the bar Orlando broke into gales of hysterical laughter.  \'It can\'t possibly be this cold.\' He said as he laughed and cackled, bent over at the knees and choked he was laughing so hard.  \'It can\'t possibly be this fucking cold.\'  Orlando laughed so hard he fell onto the pavement.  Kiya said we should leave him there (We were drunk, remember).  But we collected him anyway and got him home, where we tucked him into bed as he giggled uncontrolably.\r\n\r\n<p>Vick didn\'t realize that clouds can freeze and was shocked the other morning to see them crystalizing and plummeting out of the sky.  Granted, it is an amazing sight, but after you\'ve lived her for awhile you get used to these kinds of things.  The clouds are not that heavy.  When they freeze they become heavier than air and fall out of the sky.  But when they hit the ground, or a car, or the rooftop, the vaporize.  Human beings don\'t fare so well when being struck by the frozen clouds.  Every year a several people end up hospitalized (or worse) because they weren\'t aware of a cloud coming down on their head.  \r\n\r\n<p>Gigs sleeps outside in a tree in the wintertime.  He\'s built himself a little shack in Boston Common and leers out of tree trees waving his bare arms at the squirrels.  Gigs eats pinenuts and chews Blackjack gum and walks the park naked at night.  He is a miracle.  Bostonians have come to accept him as a regular.\r\n\r\n<p>Betty and Isbel go away for the winter.  And we miss them.  They return in spring.\r\n\r\n<p>Myself?  I am a ghostly presence; a purple crayon; a little cave of deceit.  I am a eater of matchsticks and a writer of love letters to the editor.  I have no hands and my feet have rotted away.  My eyes are tunnels on the moon.  Please love me once and then leave.  I have nothing to give and nothing to relieve.'),(2053,'<UL TYPE=\"square\">\r\n<LI>If it protects you, it can\'t be bad.\r\n<LI>If you like it, it can\'t be bad for you.\r\n<LI>If you touch it, it is real.\r\n<LI>If you think it, it will happen.\r\n<LI>If you are honest, you will be happy.\r\n<LI>If it matters, you will respect it.\r\n<LI>If it dies, remember it.\r\n<LI>If you thank it, it will be grateful.\r\n<LI>If you miss it, it will miss you too...\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\n'),(2054,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2054_1.pjpeg\"><br>\r\n\r\n<UL TYPE=\"square\">\r\n<LI>If it protects you, it can\'t be bad.\r\n<LI>If you like it, it can\'t be bad for you.\r\n<LI>If you touch it, it is real.\r\n<LI>If you think it, it will happen.\r\n<LI>If you are honest, you will be happy.\r\n<LI>If it matters, you will respect it.\r\n<LI>If it dies, remember it.\r\n<LI>If you thank it, it will be grateful.\r\n<LI>If you miss it, it will miss you too...\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\n'),(2055,'<pre>\r\nwhere are we going?\r\ni can\'t see any signs, and\r\nyou aren\'t the best driver\r\n\r\nlet me take a look\r\n\r\nwe are not lost she said\r\nmaybe not\r\nmaybe we will get there today she said\r\nmaybe so\r\n\r\nmaybe so\r\n\r\nin the window there are forms \r\nin the window, there are faces\r\nin the  window, there is a ghostly face and look\r\nit\'s raining again\r\n\r\nup ahead, well\r\nthat\'s too much to think about, \r\nthat\'s just too much\r\nup ahead will take care of itself, yes sir \r\ndon\'t you worry about that, little pilgrim\r\nyes sir\r\n\r\nmaybe we are not lost she said\r\nmaybe we will get there today'),(2056,'greetings.  this is my first submission here at swinney.  it\'s a true story from a few years back. i figured it\'s as a good an introduction as any.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\n<p>It was a Wednesday morning. I woke up early, and I was feeling quite shitty. Literally. So I went to the bathroom and felt shitty. When I came back to my room, I still felt a bit sick, which was strange, because usually a trip to the bathroom clears all that stuff up. I started feeling worse.. I realized that I had to barf. I was completely unimpressed - I hadn?t barfed in a few years, and rarely ever do. I don?t really mind the actual physical act of vomiting - but still, I wasn?t impressed that I had to actually do it. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>So I went over to the sink in my room, and we spoke for a while. I figured that after I?d puked, I?d be fine, and okay to go to work. I got myself dressed properly, washed out my mouth, and headed off to work. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Once there, I still felt pretty horrible. I was sweating and close to passing out. It took me about a half hour to say ?fuck this, I?m going home?. I called for a cab, and went downstairs to grab it. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I figured I?d go over to the pharmacy to get some Gravol before the cab came. This is where a crucial part to my journey took place. See, I got a plastic bag to carry my Gravol in, and I kept it. I shake my head now thinking what might have been had I not gone to the pharmacy. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>So I came back to work, and the cabbie was waiting. I got in the cab, still not feeling well at all. We were pulling out of the parking lot when my face turned from grey to green. I thought to myself, ?there? s NO way I?m going to puke in this cab.. it?ll pass.. I?ll be fine?. We got on to the road. Two seconds later I said to the cabbie ?I?m very sorry, but I?m going to be sick?. He asked me if I wanted him to turn around and drop me back off where he picked me up. The quick vision of me barfing in a parking lot made me decline. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I frantically dumped the contents of my savior plastic bag on to the seat beside me. The cabbie was looking back at me through the mirror very nervously. Right before I started puking, I saw that the light at the intersection we were approaching was red. Great. Spectators. So I started puking. And puking. I couldn?t stop my body from making the heaving noises. It must have stunk really bad because the cabbie rolled down all the windows. We stopped at the light, and from the corner of my eye I could see people in other cars watching me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was at this point that one of the basic truths of life hit home with me. There is no dignity in puking. There?s nothing noble about it whatsoever. You cannot maintain any shred of dignity at all while you?re puking, and especially when you?re in a cab puking into a Shopper?s Drugmart plastic bag while other people are watching you. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I thought about these people while I was puking (trust me, I had a lot of time) and how I was going to be the common topic of conversation at dinner for a bunch of random people who I?ll never see again. \"So, I was at a stop light at lunch today and I saw this guy who was puking his guts out in a Blue Line. It was hilarious. You should have seen his face, and the puke coming out of his nose\" The barf coming through my nose was no good. I didn?t like it at all. Our brave cabbie, meanwhile, was rummaging around and handing me McDonald?s napkins over the front seat so I could at least try to keep his cab clean. I puked for quite some time. Normally, a situation like this forces you to swallow your pride, but in my case, it kept coming back up. I stopped puking (mercifully) about halfway home. I apologized to the cabbie. He was being quite polite.. he was trying not to let me see that he was plugging his nose - he tried to make it seem like he was just wiping it.. perpetually. When we got to my house, the fare was about seven bucks. I gave him fifteen for his tolerance. Poor guy. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Getting out of the cab, I realized that I had a bag of puke with me. What the fuck do you do with a bag of puke? Now, we?re not talking a plastic bag with a few wisps of vomit clinging to the insides. We?re talking a FULL, heavy bag. This thing had *pull* to it. There was no way in hell I was bringing this thing up to my room - you don?t just walk around with a bag of puke. What if someone stopped me and asked what was in it? So I just left it on my neighbor?s lawn. I went up to my room, got into bed, and barfed for the rest of the day. </p>\r\n\r\n \r\n \r\n'),(2057,'<p>Tonight they are making mushroom risotto and salmon. there are two varieties of cheese on the counter. oh so continental.</p>\r\n<p>all that\'s missing is my celtic lover. They say Wednesday is Hump Day because it\'s the day you are \"over the hump\" and all your anxiety and stress rolls as if downhill into the leisure of weekend. In oakland on broadway above the zebras and below the nocturnal vampire cats, wednesday in the bend of 580 and the curve of my ass usually  promises an entirely new geography.</p>\r\n<p>Instead, on this fine hump day, I will merely eat well. there is a lot of love in my tummy. I have been previously eating through my own stomach being stressy messy tessy coffee lady. All that is over tonight. I am released, like nina simone. leavin long messages on the telephone. /p>\r\n<p>she stopped a song during a recording to instruct: \"no, you pushin it. dont put nothin in it unless you feel it.\" Jay and Eve have nina simone on shuffle with chet baker and belle and sebastian and the cowboy junkies. it is a seduction scene. I feel.</P>\r\n<p>all my friends are fuckin aesthetes. eat drink suckle suck fuck snort rub chew chaw chaffe. as julie ruin after yoko ono says, sometimes the secret is, YES. Remember that commercial (I cant remember what product it was for) in which the metal guy yells into the phone, \"we\'re gettin the band back together MAN!\"? well, when eve and jay get together their form of \"jamming\" is cooking refined meals with dense ingredients preceded by smooth bourbon cocktails and accompanied by decent, but cheap costing, wine, preferably from the vineyard. </p>\r\n<p>Cheese is pretty much the staple of the house at any given moment. I still have memories of eve spreading boursin on a piece of bread with sundried tomato and smoked oysters on the beach - and justin taking a bite and asking, \"but the question is, is it rich enough?\"</p>\r\n<p>And tonight, I shovel and shovel, greedy for new love and old cheese and massages and planes. tonight I will eat a plane and fly on a fish. I complained to the kitchen that I was not going to have sex for a week. Eve mocked me. oh, you poor thing. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"dont cry for me, pussytina.\"</p>\r\n<p>I should have remorse for being so greedy, so ready for chocolate before dinner, so thirsty for the blood of endangered species. I don\'t. Im going to Milwaukee this weeknd and get shitfaced and demand that arwa paint my fingernails lilac and make elaborate toasts about the beauty of commitment and the necessity of family. Im going to fill up my whole body with poison and perfection and then fall asleep until thanksgiving.</p>'),(2058,'<p>Tonight they are making mushroom risotto and salmon. there are two varieties of cheese on the counter. oh so continental.</p>\r\n<p>all that\'s missing is my celtic lover. They say Wednesday is Hump Day because it\'s the day you are \"over the hump\" and all your anxiety and stress rolls as if downhill into the leisure of weekend. In oakland on broadway above the zebras and below the nocturnal vampire cats, wednesday in the bend of 580 and the curve of my ass usually  promises an entirely new geography.</p>\r\n<p>Instead, on this fine hump day, I will merely eat well. there is a lot of love in my tummy. I have been previously eating through my own stomach being stressy messy tessy coffee lady. All that is over tonight. I am released, like nina simone. leavin long messages on the telephone. /p>\r\n<p>she stopped a song during a recording to instruct: \"no, you pushin it. dont put nothin in it unless you feel it.\" Jay and Eve have nina simone on shuffle with chet baker and belle and sebastian and the cowboy junkies. it is a seduction scene. I feel.</P>\r\n<p>all my friends are fuckin aesthetes. eat drink suckle suck fuck snort rub chew chaw chaffe. as julie ruin after yoko ono says, sometimes the secret is, YES. Remember that commercial (I cant remember what product it was for) in which the metal guy yells into the phone, \"we\'re gettin the band back together MAN!\"? well, when eve and jay get together their form of \"jamming\" is cooking refined meals with dense ingredients preceded by smooth bourbon cocktails and accompanied by decent, but cheap costing, wine, preferably from the vineyard. </p>\r\n<p>Cheese is pretty much the staple of the house at any given moment. I still have memories of eve spreading boursin on a piece of bread with sundried tomato and smoked oysters on the beach - and justin taking a bite and asking, \"but the question is, is it rich enough?\"</p>\r\n<p>And tonight, I shovel and shovel, greedy for new love and old cheese and massages and planes. tonight I will eat a plane and fly on a fish. I complained to the kitchen that I was not going to have sex for a week. Eve mocked me. oh, you poor thing. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"dont cry for me, pussytina.\"</p>\r\n<p>I should have remorse for being so greedy, so ready for chocolate before dinner, so thirsty for the blood of endangered species. I don\'t. Im going to Milwaukee this weeknd and get shitfaced and demand that arwa paint my fingernails lilac and make elaborate toasts about the beauty of commitment and the necessity of family. Im going to fill up my whole body with poison and perfection and then fall asleep until thanksgiving.</p>'),(2059,'<p>Tonight they are making mushroom risotto and salmon. there are two varieties of cheese on the counter. oh so continental.</p>\r\n<p>all that\'s missing is my celtic lover. They say Wednesday is Hump Day because it\'s the day you are \"over the hump\" and all your anxiety and stress rolls as if downhill into the leisure of weekend. In oakland on broadway above the zebras and below the nocturnal vampire cats, wednesday in the bend of 580 and the curve of my ass usually  promises an entirely new geography.</p>\r\n<p>Instead, on this fine hump day, I will merely eat well. there is a lot of love in my tummy. I have been previously eating through my own stomach being stressy messy tessy coffee lady. All that is over tonight. I am released, like nina simone. leavin long messages on the telephone. </p>\r\n<p>she stopped a song during a recording to instruct: \"no, you pushin it. dont put nothin in it unless you feel it.\"  For the past few weeks, I have felt an elastic squeezing inside of me, tightening around my center and making every impulse feel forced, wanting to hide in my bed, wanting noone to ask me 4 shit, wishing I could summon the light hunger for everything at once that I usually walk through the world with. And tonight, I am greedy again.</P>\r\n<p>oh no, will i ever tire of chet baker? will i ever tire of  eating too much of the softest thing i have ever tasted?</p>\r\n<p>The other night Jay appeared by my desk with a grave expression and a trembling mouth. I thought someone was injured in a new accident. He widened his eyes and proclaimed, \"austin told me to eat the pears because they are ripe right now and will go bad in a few days and I ate this and its the BEST PEAR I HAVE EVER EATEN in my life and you have to do you want one? oh ill get one for you.\" the juice rushed over my teeth and dribbled down my mouth and chin. </p>\r\n<p>all my friends are fuckin aesthetes. eat drink suckle suck fuck snort rub chew chaw chaffe. as julie ruin after yoko ono says, sometimes the secret is, YES. Remember that commercial (I cant remember what product it was for) in which the metal guy yells into the phone, \"we\'re gettin the band back together MAN!\"? well, when eve and jay get together their form of \"jamming\" is cooking refined meals with dense ingredients preceded by smooth bourbon cocktails and accompanied by decent, but cheap costing, wine, preferably from the vineyard. </p>\r\n<p>Cheese is pretty much the staple of the house at any given moment. I still have memories of eve spreading boursin on a piece of bread with sundried tomato and smoked oysters on the beach - and justin taking a bite and asking, \"but the question is, is it rich enough?\"</p>\r\n<p>And tonight, I shovel and shovel, greedy for new love and old cheese and massages and planes. tonight I will eat a plane and fly on a fish. I complained to the kitchen that I was not going to have sex for a week. Eve mocked me. oh, you poor thing. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"dont cry for me, pussytina.\"</p>\r\n<p>I should have remorse for being so greedy, so ready for chocolate before dinner, so thirsty for the blood of endangered species. I don\'t. Im going to Milwaukee this weeknd and get shitfaced and demand that arwa paint my fingernails lilac and make elaborate toasts about the beauty of commitment and the necessity of family. Im going to fill up my whole body with poison and perfection and then fall asleep until thanksgiving.</p>'),(2060,''),(2061,'<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2061_1.pjpeg\"><br><br>\r\nNew York City is a city full of wonderful things, shiny opportunities, and unsurpassed uniqueness.  However, it can also be a city of extreme annoyances. You know, acts of terrorism, thugs, street preachers, getting mugged, and those damn lackey hustlers who always approach you on the street and ask, \"Hey! Do <i>you</i> like stand up comedy?!\"  Somewhere at the top of the <i>annoying list</i>, however, is our little law called <i>Opposite Side Parking</i>.  If you\'re one of the unfortunate souls who has to keep a car in the city, it predicates that you have to move your parked car from one side of the street to the other, every other day or so, to make way for the street sweeper trucks to come by; not that the street sweeping trucks do any good, but I digress...<br><br>  \r\nThe NYPD Traffic Patrol has developed a rather witty scheme that allows them to cash in on this car-moving phenomenon: they tend to lurk close behind the bulky, slow-moving sweeper trucks, ticketing every ensuing parker who doesn\'t obey the law, nailing you for something in the neighborhood of $30-75, all in the name of clean streets.<br><br>\r\nSo, after a while, the shrewd parker learns to go move their car an hour or so before the 8am opposite-side-in-effect-time, double-parking it across the street.  This involves sitting and waiting for some forgetful/late/lazy/naive soul (or an out-of-towner) to move their auto, while being poised to snag one of those sought-after spaces immediately in front of your apartment.  On oppostie side parking days (Mon/Wed or Thurs/Fri in my neighborhood), when you see someone exit the apartment with a book, a stack of magazines, the paper, cell phone, coffee or pillow, its a safe bet that they\'re heading off to do the bi-weekly drive-<i>wait</i>-park ritual.<br><br> \r\nSo, all premise aside, and on to the puke... I went out early one Monday to go move my car.  I had been sitting in my car for an unbelievably long 45 minutes when I was suddenly overcome by an odd feeling of <i>intense</i> nausea. To make a long story short (and somewhat less graphic), I quickly opened my car door and vomited right on the street, into the uptown gutter drain which luckily happened to be situated within my trajectory.  Tactfully checking to make sure I had escaped any neighbor\'s gaze, I looked up with red eyes and slobbery lips, to see standing right in front of me, a mirrored aviators-clad NYPD traffic officer, smirking, coming in for the kill.<br><br>\r\nNot only had I succumbed to an embarassing public display of sickness (right in front of the police, no less), but I also got a $35 ticket for being double-parked in an opposite-side zone. Who says the City isn\'t cordial?<br>\r\n\r\n'),(2062,'<p>\r\nnot many people like the show M.A.S.H, but it just so happens that M.A.S.H is my favourite show. Ive seen all of them a few times over now, but the whit and humour still makes me smile. i find myself interested in every episode. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhawkeye is the coolest person on earth. out of all the people on the show he is the one that makes me laugh the most.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nexcept for this one episode where one of his old flames is assigned to the 4077th and he makes out with her. the thing is she is married to some guy named duke or something. good thing the voice of reason; b j honeycut leans on hawkeye and basically makes him feel bad. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nten hours later.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni dont even remember writing this post. i wake up and its sitting here in the taskbar. crazy shit. blackout swinney sessions. scary stuff i tell ya. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntoday is kinda boring so far. no note hanging from my door of daily chores to keep me semi-busy. even having chores to procrastinate makes the day seem more lively. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the bright side. its a very nice day out. not to warm. not to chilly. autumn rocks in my opinion and for another two cents, its quite a colourful season to walk around in. trees can suprise you at this time of year. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim glad its not so windy out. wind would wreak havoc in this hood. there are a good number of old people living in this area. they dont get out much and not much raking gets done. in all of toronto, etobicoke has the largest number of parks and trees per capita. there are parks attached to almost all residential streets and if there isnt one, a bigger and better one is within walking/biking distance. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city approved a bill to spend something like 700million on bike lanes for major roads. the goal is to make the city available to anyone who wants to ride a bike. the city will also expand the large system of bike trails, running through the numerous ravines and flood plains in toronto. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nback to what i was saying before tho. im glad its not windy, because with the ammount of leaves on the ground now, a wind storm would most certainly turn into a leaf storm. wipers werent designed to battle leaves. it would be pandamonium. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na squirrel has pissed on me twice. not the same critter. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2063,'<P>Tonight I looked down at the translucent white skin of my thighs and saw between them the short, buttery brown feathers of a tiny sparrow. We were flying, high in deep drops and billows. Suddenly, the sparrow couldn?t fly anymore and plummeted-hard. A lake opened up below us, wide and black. It was cold. I don?t know what happened to the sparrow. I came up, choking from the cold and wet, knowing there was a shark. I never saw it. I could feel its navy blue presence. I thought it rapped into the small of my back. I thrashed, black water cold like mud. A long time. I might have died. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I grabbed onto the edge of a rock, it was big enough for me to balance on. I cut myself, skinning my knees on the wet rough edge. I looked down. A small naked shivering boy. The rock was barely big enough for all my toes. They were white blue, gray sandy grit rimming each toenail. The shark was still there. And up above me was a pier. Too far to reach, yet ominously close. Like the bleachers at a sporting event.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>A fishing line struck the water. Invisible plastic snaking below the surface. I could hear the sting in the air. And I was wet, cold and naked, my skinny limbs and tiny penis shrinking. Up on the pier was a long line of men, old or middle-aged, fat mostly. They all had their dicks out in their hands like fishing poles. Their eyes stared at me. They were jerking off to me. And the shark still circled around my toes.</P>'),(2064,'<p>Here\'s my puking story.</p>\r\n<p>Roughly two months ago, it was a friend\'s birthday.  Thursday night, being University pub night anyway, we decided to go out and celebrate.  My addition to the festivities was the purhasing of: one (1) 40oz. bottle of Colt-45 and two (2) 40oz. bottles of Olde English (OE).  Just to establish this fact early on, I had to work the next morning.</p>\r\n<p>Our friend, Greg, had never drank any kind of forty before so it was to be quite a spectacle.  We were chillin\' at Matt\'s place, watching some CKY3 and Jackass episodes to get us prepared for the evenings festivities.  At one point, Matt suggested that I go get Greg his present and I returned from the kitchen with an OE and a Colt-45.  I uncapped the 45 and passed it on, while beginning to make a dent in my OE.  Greg didn\'t really like the taste of his Colt-45, but we pressured him onwards.  I finished mine, he was halfway there on his, I got another then we had to go.</p>\r\n<p>Enter the Brown Bag.</p>\r\n<p>Upon leaving the residence building we were previously situated in, our friend Greg announced that he had to releive himself.  I urged him to do it on a tree in the middle of the sidewalk, in front of the Beckers.  He did.  That action sets the tone for the evening.</p>\r\n<p>We were proceeding to the bar in the very light rain and everything was all jolly fun.  We got to the corner CIBC bank beside the bar and I urged Greg to finish what was left in my last OE.  He downed it, left the bottle on the ledge of a bank window and not 10 seconds later, puked on the railing of the bar\'s patio.  We then walked over the smallish line and he said he had to puke again.  I began vehemently shouting that he puke on a tree, which he did.  One of the bouncers came over and told us that he wasn\'t going to let us in because our friend was puking.  We tried to tell him that he was okay now but he wasn\'t going for it and told us to come back in an hour.  So we went to a campus pub and waited there before making our way back.  I was in pretty rough shape by this time and when we returned to the first bar, the line was huge and the bouncer who talked with us before came over to tell us that they were at capacity.  Matt stepped up and tipped the guy what he wanted ($20, what a cheap bastard) to let us in.</p>\r\n<p>So, we\'re inside and it\'s all loud and seeing people know you and at one point I\'m sitting down talking with some people and I feel that intense desire to be standing.  You know that \"Nausea-begone\" technique?  Well after playing that game for a while I decided it was time to announce to everyone that I had to get out of there, or else.  Matt told me to sneak into his residence because the door to his unit and his room were open so I could get my bag and go home.</p>\r\n<p>I got tired of waiting around for people to go in the building that I could sneak in with and made my way to the closest subway stop.  There, I ran into Corey and chatted him up for a bit before he got on a streetcar to head home for the evening.  I get on the subway, still feeing really rough, and it begins moving.  It feels not right, but I\'m fairly confident that I can manage it.  I know I was trying to remember if I should be looking at the tunnel walls or the floor to avoid motion sickness but I couldn\'t remember and both seemed to produce the same sickening results.  Three stops later, we\'re at Union Station and I get out to vomit in a garbage can.  I managed to hold it in long enough until I couldn\'t see any people on the subway platform.  One thought was going through my mind as I puked into that garbage can: \"Oh no, I\'m <b>that guy</b>.\"</p>\r\n<p>I sit down and calm down while waitinf for three subways to pass in my direction before getting on the fourth.</p>  Two more stations and I get off to puke into a garbage can again (I was very happy for the TTC policy of placing garbages in the same position on every station platform).  I sit down for a few trains again and again make my way.  I manage to make it more of a distance this time, all the way to St. George, but this time I barely made it off the car.  I didn\'t even care anymore and was puking in front of people that time.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m only feeling worse at this point and I make it to the next station and have to get off again.  This time I realise that I\'m not going to make it home via the subway because the distances between the two stops before mine are huge and there\'s no way I\'m pukikng in a subway car.  So I get off at Spadina and begin to walk home.  At this point it was sort of lightly raining.</p>\r\n<p>The walk took nearly three hours and by the time I got home the streets looked like rivers it was raining so damn hard.  I was completely soaked, no piece of clothing on my body was dry and I was shaking I was so cold.  I puked once more during my travels, on the wall of Casa Loma, and discovered all kinds of crazy cool parks walking through weird neighbourhoods.  I know I will never find these parks again because I was basically picking a direction to walk in and then walking until I recognized my surroundings.  Most of the times when that happened, I wasn\'t in the place I thought I was.</p>\r\n<p>So, in the morning, I call my boss and tell her I\'ll be an hour late (she knew I was going out the night before) and I showed up only half an hour late.  My shoes were still completely soaked and I looked like complete crap but I made it.</p>\r\n<p>I have other good puking stories, but this is the most recent and the others are all really messy, involving highschool and drinking dangerous amounts of hard liquor in very small periods of time.</p>'),(2065,'<p>last saturday, i woke up at the hotel completely drunk still and wandered out to the pool.  on the way, i saw some of my bandmates eating breakfast at the buffet (the smell of which was enough to make me gag), and they snidely remarked that my hair looked \"great.\"  in fact, it looked like liz taylor\'s hair after one of her quaalude binges (i caught a glimpse of it bouncing puffily around in the window of the buffet room).  i proceeded to slap my way over to the pool area, weaving slightly and stopping on the way to coax one of those pepsi waters out of the reluctant machine.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>as i sank into the hot tub, my stomach protested and my head began to throb.  i sucked down the pepsiwater greedily and tried to ignore my body\'s protests that this was too much sensory input, too early in the morning, and there was too much alcohol in my system to take it in properly.  i leaned my head back and tried to relax.  soon i was surrounded by loud obnoxious boys trying to get me to do a cannonball into the pool.  i ignored them for a while, chugging down the rest of my water and trying to remain still.  then suddenly, as if of its own accord, my body went \"what the hell\" and hoisted itself up out of the boiling hot tub, staggered over to the edge of the pool, and sort of teetered in sideways.  the water was freezing.  it felt good on my skin, but my stomach started to churn ferociously and bitter saliva began to squirt into the sides of my mouth. i hurriedly got out, grabbed my towel, donned my robe, and ran out through the pool gate and around the corner as fast as my trembling legs could carry me.</p>\r\n<p>once safely out of sight, i let \'er rip onto a flowerbed. i watched in amazement as what appeared to be pure beer (diluted with pepsiwater, of course) raged out of my body like niagra falls.  i heaved a few times, just to be sure it was all gone.  then i straightened, surreptitiously wiped my mouth, and wrapped my towel around my head (like liz taylor in one of her more glamourous moments).  i sauntered back into the pool enclosure, suddenly chipper and voraciously hungry for sausage and eggs.</p>'),(2066,'<p>i want to talk about what the fuck is going on.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>shit. jack <b>fucking</b> shit. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>and you know what, i hate it. i hate that i have no job, no life. i hate everything right now, and damn well, i think i\'m justified. i\'m not sure if my facade has been so pristine in the past, but you know what, i\'m not putting up a screen anymore. i hate what my life is right now and i don\'t want it to be a sham. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>i\'m stuck in a terrible rut, with only a glimmer of hope to see the other side of this damn fucking hole that i am in. i just want out, but it feels like every time i try to crawl up the side i just dirt and rocks thrown at my face. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>i\'m so close to just giving up trying anymore and have been this close for a long time now. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>fine, fine you think i\'m whining like a spoiled brat (cuz damnit, I am one), but this just isn\'t the case now. i\'m not sure how any of you could feel in my position. i have to take one step down to get anything at all out of life and it\'s not something i think anyone would do easily or willingly. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>i\'m out here alone now, so it seems. I\'m glad i put so little faith in friendship in the first place because i damn well see the fairweather friends nowhere now. I\'m glad i didn\'t put my heart on my sleeve, cuz fuck, it would have been trampled on about ten times by now. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>so if i seem bitchy lately, to anyone that i might talk to, you\'ll damn well know why. cuz i just don\'t care to put a face on anymore. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>have fun, fuckers.</p> '),(2067,'<p>glenlivet scotch whiskey. it starts so innocently. the fire in your nose spreads to your chin, neck and slowly up your face into the top of your head. it steams out of you like you\'re a freshly baked bun. your head gets a little soft, too. push it in a little and it stays.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe last time i had any was 6 years ago. my housemate, (what was his name again?) a bug-eyed morose wanna-be writer sliding into the bitter dregs of his middle-aged life without fame or pussy, set up the card table. he suggested a night of poker. it was a saturday night, and i had nothing else to do. i shrugged. i brought out some liquor. we played. we drank. at some point, i got really pissed at him. he was caught by surprise when i started thumping my glass of whiskey on the table and calling him names. oh yeah, we were drinking glasses of the stuff. not shots.\r\n</p><p>\r\nson of a bitch.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncocksucker asslick asshole bumbleboy. nannyass frannylicker.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni yelled insults. he yelled insults. i grabbed the table and thumped it at him, spilling more booze. back and forth. how to up the ante? i throw my half-filled pintglass at him. it shatters, spilling strong liquor everywhere, and the glass spit up in all directions. <i>then</i> we got really fired up. i told him he had no soul. he looked at me. \"i have no soul?\" incredulous disbelief. dead silence. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni turn around and slam into my bedroom, pass out on my bed. someone puts a bucket next to my bed, was magically full of vomit when i woke up.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe amazing thing is, we both woke up sheepish, and mopped up the liquor and glass together the next day. we were ever so solicitous! would you like the dustpan? oh yes, please. thank you. there were apologies and mutual grimacing.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni still remember telling kim about that night- she cackles gleefully, \'you told him - <i>what</i>??\' and we both giggled in the living room together. the funny thing is, i hated his guts, but we actually got along a little better after that night. ultimately he proved himself a dick, but that was after i moved out.</p>\r\n'),(2068,'one of the things i\'ve been hearing a lot from a lot of people in diverse age groups and geographic locations is that they feel a lot of hopelessness and depression.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlots of those who read swinney are too young to remember the vietnam war, but i\'m not.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwar sucks. terrorism sucks more than war, but not by much.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe west was already heading for an economic recession before any terroristi decided to blow up the world trade center and make things a whole lot worse.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlike it or not, big business needs to make big money for everything else to work, good old reaganistic trickle down theories are sort of right in some respects.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthose of us doing the dotcom thing know that we pretty much lost our asses this past 18 months or so, and those of you who used to be employed in the fat cash venture capital will never end sort of industries suddenly find yourselves not only not rolling in the dough anymore, but many of you are wondering how the hell you are gonna pay the rent.<br><br>\r\n\r\nback in corporate america all the big businesses that wanted/needed to lay off some workers took advantage of the sept 11th bombings to cry broke and opened a flood gate of unemployed middle management folks with few options onto the job markets.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen there are a lot of people who used to have a fair amount of cash to spend on things they didn\'t need to survive suddenly unable to disperse it amongst the retailers of the world, things go downhill quick.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthose are facts, and they suck, but that\'s how it is.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat i want to say to you who are wallowing in desperation and depression is this -- there are solutions, both short term and long term, to your immediate problems.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you can\'t afford where you live, maybe it\'s time to move. it\'s a lot cheaper to live in small town america than you realize. rents in big cities are roughly 3 times higher than small towns.<br><br>\r\n\r\nconsider moving in groups, so you don\'t leave your friends behind.  if you have green hair, choose the friend with the least number of piercings in obvious places to negotiate the lease on a 3bdr/2bath/garage/fenced yard sort of neighborhood house in east podunk america.<br><br>\r\n\r\nonce you get moved, knock on the doors and meet your neighbors.  tell them your names and disarm them with your charm.  talk about your mom and dad and little brothers and sisters and grandmas so they will not be afraid you have been spawned by dope smoking aliens.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you have web skills, and you don\'t want to move, and are used to working for big companies in teams, consider getting together with some other unemployed folks and build your own company in your bedrooms.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen you are home for the holidays this year, go knock on the doors of every teeny business in your hometown and offer to build them a static 5 page website for a couple hundred bucks.  you can flip those babies out in no time flat and you don\'t need many to meet the rent.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndon\'t forget that celebrations are around for a reason.  for thousands of years depressed people have known that you can take time off from poverty by getting together with people you care about and have some fun.  play a game, have a singalong, dance.  do it together, bring potluck food. hug a bunch. laugh.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand if you are feeling really down, put it into words and send it out to the cybers.  i\'ve found that writing down my deepest fears and frustrations and making them public has a way of making them seem less important. maybe it will work for you, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlove and kisses, mom<br><br>'),(2069,'<center>ever shoot yourself in the foot?</center>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>i don\'t mean literally. if you\'ve shot yourself in the foot literally then you\'re possibly either an idiot or a psycho. </p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>a psycho, incidentally, is a bit of a misnomer. or a bit of a whatever. a psycho is actually supposed to be relatively harmless. now, a sociopath, on the other hand, is the dangerous type. a sociopath is one who is capable of feeling no remorse. so there you go.</p>\r\n<br>\r\nyeah. so i shoot myself in the foot. do i do it to wake myself up? or fuck, do i do it to fall asleep? who knows? who cares? but i\'m doing it right now. was i doing it earlier, in the club, drunk with the red lights flashing in my eyes? there was a girl with a red dress and a girl with chopsticks in her hair. where was i? talking about some things i did know : surrealism.....i forgot to tell her that dali\'s mustache was prone to his moods. it drooped sometimes for a reason, you know? like the clocks. but is that true or am i making this up? instead, i talked about dali\'s scatology, when i was thinking of rhinoceros horns. what does this make me? a foot-shooter, that\'s what. and so also i was talking about stuff i didn\'t really know about. i was re-culling images from long ago and trying to siphon them into word-molds. and the beauty of painting is, that can\'t really be done. and so as a former and future painter i have committed a crime. i am neither proud nor embarassed to admit that is not my first. and that\'s fine.</p>\r\n'),(2070,'<p>While walking home from work I saw people (note the pluralisation: meaning more than one) driving in their cars and picking their noses.  It seemed really weird/funny to me, to be witnessing this.  It\'s like the people thought that no-one could see them in their cars.  Protected, holed up inside their nasal exploration caves.  I saw them, though.  And I laughed to myself.</p>\r\n<p>I even saw one guy, who is a regular at work, sitting in his car, wailing away.  When he saw me, we gave each other The Nod and then he went back to his secret fun and I took another sip of my coffee..</p>\r\n<p>I can\'t believe I\'m writing this.  I\'m so tired that even though I want to have a bath, I\'m not going to, for fear that I will pass out and drown.</p>\r\n<p>I just noticed this: for some reason, the right brace/bracket and the pipe/backslash keys on my keyboard are really dirty.  Just those two.  I think someone forgot to include me on that particular joke.</p>\r\n<p>What?  Does this make any sense?</p>\r\n<p>No?</p>\r\n<p>How about this?</p>\r\n'),(2071,'<p>the stag pictured on the green bottle of jagermeifter king of unspecified opiates is reputed to be the infamous reindeer of the seven gables in masonick folklore and is said to represent the hunter/father god SPORKMEIFTER, who resided in Vallyaya Olympic Mons and irritated mortals by hurling down massive sporks unto the general populace for no apparent reason other than his own sick amusement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>where am i? what am i doing?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>dodging smart bombs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3:09 a.m. nine minutes into what a friend of mine called the \'hour of the spirits\'. i stay up late because i\'m a night person. the many graveyard jobs are too timelong away to use as an excuse, and an excuse isn\'t needed. sundays are especially weird. i lock in and get loaded and try to go for a walk and i try to ignore the daylight creeping in, bringing a harsh breakfast platter of starting cars and neighboring alarm clocks. why in god\'s name would you choose something that sounds like it\'s screaming \'fire\' when you could opt for music? my clock radio was cheap, but it could play tapes. i used to wake up to the vocal rantings of a middle-aged sometimes dreadlocked and dragged uber-freak of the highest degree from the u.k. anyway what i\'m doing is trying to keep that first workday at bay for as long as possible. and in the end it just makes it worse, having to cut things in a narrow area with alot of knives and fire and still feeling the latent waves of the smokin\' beersea but fuck i\'ll probably keep doing it for a while.</p>'),(2072,'<p>\r\ni helped my coworker with her photoshoot on saturday. she came up to my desk a few times last month with lighting and film and camera questions. this dancer lady who teaches at mills asked her to do a photoshoot of some of the dancers in her company. and kiran, the coworker, agreed to do it, despite having never used a strobe kit in her life, not knowing the first thing about film and not even owning a camera. this is where i come in. resident photographer. she used her receptionistic/secretarial charms to get me to sit down with her and explain some basics. we did this twice. and it was beginning to look like she was trying to talk me into just doing the shoot for her. finally i agreed to come along and help her set up but i refused to actually shoot anything. i hate shooting at someone else\'s shoot, especially in the studio.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwhen we got there, kiran basically had me set it all up and figure out a balance between stage lighting and strobe. i think it looked nice, actually. we were shooting seven dancers. mostly in pairs. a couple on their own. really posed. straight shots. some complicated lifts and jumps. pretty though. simple and classic but sort of off and weird. apparently, the dancer lady does a lot of stuff like that.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthere was one dancer who i instantly had the hots for and made sure to take my time lighting her so i could go up and stand next to her and take meter readings and chat a bit. her solo shot was of this fast turn in a dress where the skirt bit would come up. we made her do it over and over again to get exactly the right shots. one with flash and a slow shutter speed to get the blur and one really crisp and detailed. she turned so many times that she got all motion sick and ran out of the room to throw up. i felt so terrible that i ran out and got her a sprite. we sat outside for a while and got some air and drank our sprites. when she felt better, we came back in and she danced some more. i was impressed. she looked great through my camera. i think the barf moment really cemented my crush though.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni came back later that night to watch them perform and i actually liked most of the work. and that\'s rare for me at a modern dance performance. i was running late for a champagne date with jessica so i couldn\'t stay to talk to her afterward. i wish i had. i wonder if i\'ll see her again.\r\n</p>'),(2073,'<p><i><center>So, I\'ll see you in Nashville if I ever get out that way</ br>\r\nI\'ll see you in Austin but I won\'t have long to stay<br />\r\nIf you\'re ever out West, son<br />\r\nAnd you\'re feelin\' like slowin\' down<br />\r\nI\'ll see you around, \'round my hometown<br /></center></i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>So that\'s a little misleading as I don\'t have a hometown, but it did remind me of something I mentioned in the Ruta Maya smokeshop a couple of months ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sitting there with Dava, the girl who works the counter, and Steph, prior to when we started and stopped and started and stopped dating, I said that I thought having to slow down is why so many people are afraid of falling in love.  I think it\'s a basic tenet of almost any relationship that the two involved must slow down.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We all live life at such a high pitch nowadays.  Rushing from work to shows to meetings with friends to home for a couple of hours of sleep before we start again.  We\'re all so stressed about our social and professional standing that we never feel as if there\'s enough time in our lives to complete the things that need to be done.  We\'re flooded with information relentlessly.  The time we don\'t spend absorbing it we spend analyzing and trying to understand it--even if that\'s only lying in bed before falling asleep.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Relationships make us slow.  Not stop, but slow.  We have to take inventory of our days and decide what\'s really important and what can be cut out to make time for this new person, this Significant Other that our attraction dictates we spend time with, learn about, experience things with, and, if everything works out, make more concessions for. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Often, we\'re unwilling to take this step.  Probably especially when we\'re younger and it looks like there\'s so much more to the world.  Maybe there is, or maybe there isn\'t any more than giving up trying to close our hands around some distant, unforgiving trophy and \"settling\" for the person that\'s here in front of us willing to forgive the transgressions we\'ve made in the past in pursuit of that elusive award. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>That\'s a large question to me, especially with friends\' recent concerns about the lack of time (maybe it\'s the recent time-change that\'s throwing them off):  Is the pursuit of some grander illusion (which it may very possibly be) worth the expense of serious relationships only because one requires moving as fast as possible, maximizing every snippet of time, and the other forces me to slow down and enjoy the here, now, tomorrow, and this person beside me?</p>\r\n\r\n<p><center><i>\"So I\'m leaving for the last time  honey<br />\r\nI\'m never more to roam<br />\r\nGonna pack my bags a little heavy this time<br />\r\nGonna head my ass back home<br /></i><br /><br />--Charlie Robison, \"My Hometown\"</center></p>'),(2074,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2074_1.pjpeg\">\r\n<br><br>\r\nThey program their drum machines and beat-boxes; They sequence their obsolete analog machines; They pave textures on which the discussions of their bleak urban futures drive like the cracked motorways aproning through the Motor City. General Motors installs automation, robot arms to boost production, dispatching countless laborers from the assembly lines; crumpled souls collecting like abandoned cars in the crackled car-parks scattered through the urban sprawl like sterile, neuter seeds, cast to the changing winds, fallen wherever the fates lose interest,\r\ndoomed to rot in a land without nourishment.<br><br>\r\nDirt, whos oil-stained topsoil birthed unprecedented revolutions, engine revolutions, mass-produced vehicles to bear us on burning fossil fuels, to contrived destinations we never knew we had to reach.<br><br>\r\nAnd weâ€™re still not convinced, yet weâ€™re endlessly strung along on these manufactured dreams, hopes ephemeral, made flesh by Detroitâ€™s envy, working hands, calloused souls, sheet-metal minds crafting American Chariots in the form of streamlined magazine ads.<br><br>\r\nStill chained to the assembly line, our shared subconscious retooled by preprogrammed robots, efficient and calibrated to the eons that defy our sharpest human conceptions, propelled by the riches amassed from a market of ever replaced, perpetually re-abandoned autos, Detroitâ€™s greatest left to rot with bald tires, left to die with imported hands, greasy fingers long since laid off, men succumbed to hydraulic arms, automated welders, mechanical sprinting, senseless efficiency, time measured in time-clock decadence, not in human breath.<br><br>\r\nNot even in human tears, so real, which leak out of eyes that still remember the soreness of the welding arc, the burn of the topcoat primer fumes, the shimmer of meta-flake custom finishes, eyes averted to avoid the grit of titanium rouge, polished from carburated engine portals.<br><br>\r\nThis is our fate, blueprints drawn with the spilled blood of the united brotherhood of auto workers, perpetuated and circulated in a tangle of veins only as complex as our highways and weaving interstates crosshatched life-flow, etching our countryâ€™s dilapidation, taxiways for grounded Detroit dreams, mincemeat for Michigan lake-gazing souls.<br><br>\r\nToo few of us remain reluctant to abandon this technology we, as children, were intricately conditioned to embrace. Our world, it seems, is changing dialects, digressing, beckoning the new souls of its young citizens with freshly contrived horizons that are as blank as they are empty, as hopeless as they are bleak, and unprecidentedly soul-less. The staunch fingers of tomorrow gesture, their seductive â€œcome hitherâ€ enticements indicate futures paved with circuits, silicon dreams so minute and featureless that they  swelter to obsolete staleness before our jaded hands can even hope to grasp them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nDetroit has been abandoned for outcroppings of digital commerce, the crystalline fiber-optic linked splinters of silicon bi-ways. And our sociopathic digital pipe-dream churns out soul-less replicants, commerce-engineered, stock-option-fueled automatons, the assembly-line hip, who now populate a new era of decay and fleeting decadence, a digital sprawl so hopelessly fated, epicenters engulfing our land like lava flows,\r\ndissolving global borders into homogenous territories, all lacking the individual\r\nfeatures carried only by unique human individuals, funded by the sweat of labor,\r\nthe unflagging chase of tangible mechanical innovation, dreams made real on the assembly line.  Now machine etched circuit boards are \r\npacked, straight from robot hands, hard-wired with the substance of breathlessly tepid, post millennial aspirations; shiny commercial after-lives that even the most naive now see to be bitterly vanished.<br><br>\r\nHistoryâ€™s pilot-light still glows brightly enough, yet, to illuminate the trends of reality,\r\nstroking us with hopelessly soothing breaths, whispering reminders that speak in gears grinding,\r\nâ€œwe canâ€™t go backâ€. Revolutions and sparks, timed to spark-plugs and engine ignitions grunt out in diesel breaths, â€œthat which was once precious has been abandonedâ€.<br><br> \r\nAnd the abandoned souls of our Motor Cities worldwide collectively sigh in perpetual\r\nfrustration. And yesteryearâ€™s newest models, shiny highway cruisers of Detroit Ingenuity and American work-ethic, are left abandoned, in fields and on free-ways, each last drop of oil spent out in failed redemption, each last measure of horsepower consumed, parsing pavement and driving roads that lead nowhere.<br><br>\r\nSo we sustain our futile human hopes as best we can contrive, the willing among us sitting attentive as Detroit souls program that forgotten hope in sonic metaphors, weaving our collective dreamâ€™s rememberance with the tarnished fibers of the assemby-line, the residue of Detroitâ€™s mechanized soul, the automotive majesties that carried a generation of enraptured citizens towards goals that still remain reachless, and a syncopated utopia that perpetually disappears over the horizons of our endless network of highways.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2075,'yep, it\'s gotten a hold of me. i suppose it\'s not such a big deal since i do have a rather large amount of free time on my hands. it\'s not like i need to accomplish anything or nuture a girlfriend. but is it so bad that i spend all day every day i\'m not at work playing thps3?\r\n\r\n<p>hmm, i guess not. it just seems so wrong some how. it feels like a waste of time. it feels like i should go do something more constructive. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>in reality though, with the weather getting colder and wetter, i don\'t want to spend it outside. i\'d rather be inside basking in the warmth of my television.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>truthfully i could keep reading my book or write or even continue to try (desprately) to teach myself to draw.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but i can\'t. i must play. i must play more. get that extra combo, get the next goal, open the next level, new character ...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>neverending.</p>'),(2076,'<p>\r\n> > howz tingz?<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> tings been so much like so much, you know? some<br>\r\n> old some new some newly old. figger.<br>\r\n><br>\r\n>  you still part of the PROJEKT?<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> i kant talk bout that.<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> > where been onez mindset?<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> out there sometimes. trapped, then out there.<br>\r\n> spinning. for sure spinning.<br>\r\n><br>\r\n>   heard<br>\r\n> > any good chatter?]<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> near zero.<br>\r\n><br>\r\n>   forget FLIP, remember<br>\r\n> > PLASTIK!<br>\r\n><br>\r\n> as usual you comflagerbannbmit!!!! daw!!<br>\r\n> fliksdiaothjv-nah fajoerj. aoirgz!!!!!!!1<br>\r\n> ><br>\r\n> > c-<br>\r\n> ><br>\r\n> ~d<br>\r\n</p>'),(2077,'<p>\r\nso after i gave change to the homeless guy<br>\r\nand started to walk away<br>\r\nhe said<br>\r\nenjoy your tv set<br>\r\nand i <br>\r\nsaid<br>\r\ni don\'t watch tv.<br>\r\ni see pictures in my head<br>\r\nhorrible<br>\r\npictures<br>\r\nand then i ran<br>\r\nshedding and shredding my clothes<br>\r\nlike a demon<br>\r\nsearching for oblivion<br>\r\n</p>'),(2078,'<p>\r\nlast night she sat and yodeled while tying knots in cold spaghetti strings as is her wont.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmyself, i talked to the radiator :<br><br>\r\n\r\n\'o divine all knowing radiator in the sky that is not really in the sky but is in my apartment on the floor, please bless us with the simple hissing sound......\'<br><br>\r\n\r\ncourse nothing happened right away. there\'s a window of about 20 minutes or so during which time the divineness of the radiator must contemplate.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndoes it contemplate itself? is it existential radiator??<br><br>\r\n\r\nher toenails were atrocious. that\'s why i loved her.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit was a quiet night really. the radars remained featureless. it\'s during these times, though, that we must keep our most alert vigil. she looked at me. we remembered that storm on saturn not too long ago.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfourteen minutes later the radiator began hissing, with soft clanks.<br><br>\r\n</p>'),(2079,'<p>\r\nlast night she sat and yodeled while tying knots in cold spaghetti strings as is her wont.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmyself, i talked to the radiator :<br><br>\r\n\r\n\'o divine all knowing radiator in the sky that is not really in the sky but is in my apartment on the floor, please bless us with the simple hissing sound......\'<br><br>\r\n\r\ncourse nothing happened right away. there\'s a window of about 20 minutes or so during which time the divineness of the radiator must contemplate.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndoes it contemplate itself? is it existential radiator??<br><br>\r\n\r\nher toenails were atrocious. that\'s why i loved her.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit was a quiet night really. the radars remained featureless. it\'s during these times, though, that we must keep our most alert vigil. she looked at me. we remembered that storm on saturn not too long ago.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfourteen minutes later the radiator began hissing, with soft clanks.<br><br>\r\n</p>'),(2080,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe are sitting in the living room and watching television and drinking and smoking and he stands up. stands up. he stands up and grabs his glass and walks downstairs. opens the door and down the stairs and to his bed.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she watches. she watches him stand up and walk downstairs and i am in her line of sight because i am sitting between the door and her and every so often she would make a motion as though she was about to get up and then, deciding \r\nagainst it, she would resume her sitting position.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i am laughing! to myself i am laughing because I know, I know she wants to get up and walk down the stairs but, at the same time, not through me.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso i am laughing. to myself i am laughing and to myself i say hey, why not go outside and smoke a cigarette? go outside and smoke a cigarette and see how long it takes before she is downstairs. for kicks. try it.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso, i am outside smoking a cigarette.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand it takes about thirty five seconds.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nafter thirty five seconds i hear her get up and out of her seat and closing the door and down the stairs. I hear from outside.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nback inside and yeah, yeah she?s gone. she?s downstairs.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nshe?s downstairs and i am upstairs and i am pure burning rage inside. i am spitfire. i am the worst middle aged black death you have ever seen.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\noutside i am talking and laughing at things on the television loudly and i am burning up.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand back outside, for a cigarette. and no cigarettes and over to seven eleven for some cigarettes. and with cigarettes and smoking and walking back to the house and you know, this is kinda fun. why not do this more?</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso i am walking. with cigarettes and the strokes on the cd player headphones, and i am leaving holes in the sidewalk. i am walking with vengeance.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nfrom the time i arrived in san francisco i had slept with her four times. twice in the first two weeks and twice more scattered over four months. the second week I was here she sat me down and she said i?m late, i think i?m pregnant. pregnant.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe last time i walked around south city with something on my mind that something was her pregnant and me being a dad. and i said, you love her right? you can get married and raise the kid! and you two can be in love and together with her pregnant.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis time it is me walking around while she is fucking my roommate.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe thing i miss most about new york is not having a car. before i moved here i had a car and i could drive around anytime i wanted and turn the stereo up real loud and scream lyrics and just be happy. here i don?t have a car. here i have legs and sidewalk.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i am walking. i am walking and leaving holes in the pavement and listening to the strokes. listening to the strokes and i am trying my hardest not to sing, but sometimes, sometimes a word or two slips out and i look around real quick to see if anyone was around to hear me.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut then, then last night comes on and i am screaming out all the lyrics, top of my lungs, and the cd is skipping and skipping and i restart the song and scream and scream and it skips and skips and eventually i am holding the cd player in my hand as i walk so i can sing without skipping. i was quite determined.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nquite.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni loved her. for real. for real i loved her and we would talk about how we would move out of south city and into the city together and eventually move to new york and live in that city and then maybe to london or tokyo and just live and be in love.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nshe had been fucking my roommates for as long as i can remember. the one, the one she would fuck all the time back in new york. the other she would fuck whenever he was in town visiting from florida. she was fucking me too then. we were all doing too much e and (atleast for me) got caught up in the trying to experience excitement all the time thing.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\neventually she got pregnant by him. that?s how it ended. she got pregnant and had an abortion and that put the brakes on all the experimenting and mock excitement.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nhe took her down to planned parenthood and dropped her off and picked her up an hour later. he took her after with him to pick up his girlfriend and the three of them rode around in his jeep and eventually climbed some rocks. all the while he and his girlfriend would steal kisses while she sat in the back seat trying not to bleed on anything.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nYou might be thinking that i am making that up, but i?m not.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni found out about all this after the neil young concert that she and i went too.  i had been noticing her weirdness lately and i dragged it out of her. she told me that she had gotten pregnant by him and that he took her to \r\nplanned parenthood and had picked her up an hour later and then took her with him to pick up his girlfriend, and that later they drove around and eventually climbed some rocks.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nshe also told me that he had done nothing for her and would not even talk to her about any of it. the ultimate number one quote being, \'oh jezus, are you kidding me? you\'re still thinking about that?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut i loved her. for real i loved her. and i may not had realized it at that moment, but later, later i did most definitely. that whole thing was a nice kick in the ass that showed me how deeply i cared for her. and i told her. right before she left to come here i sat her down and told her that i loved her and i called her my angel and things like that and things were pretty ok.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso, walking. back to that.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni saw myself, when leaving the house, walking for miles and miles and listening to the strokes album over and over and over. i almost got on the \r\n130 and went to bart to go into the city. i almost saw myself going up haight and to their apartment and knocking on the door and, ?yeah, I know it?s late and i know you cant stand me anymore, but- listen!- I really need for you to just listen to me for a half hour, please.?</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut, you know, how would I get home? and anyway, they wouldn?t want to talk to me. so i walked.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwalked and walked and walked. down el camino. and the strokes! the strokes are fucking awesome!</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni almost didn?t come out here, to san francisco. i almost didn?t come to san francisco, instead i almost ended all my ties with everyone i knew cause i just couldn?t handle it. i almost gave up san francisco. for her. to prove a point.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe?re all out here now. they came out first, the two boys and the other girl and then later, her. i waited until may because my father had died the year before and i didn\'t want my mom to be alone for her first full winter.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand after she left and before i arrived she would call me all the time crying about how they would treat her and about how whenever she would try to bring resolve to the whole issue (still, since that summer, left unresolved) he would call her crazy and how he was turning the other two aainst her.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe three of them, they would team up. they would pick her to pieces.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthey would pick her to pieces and every time i heard about it i would write them email or get on the phone and yell at them and really, honestly I came this close to just throwing away everything- all my friends, everything- san francisco, everything- for her.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand all this time we would talk about our love and how we would move out to the city when i got there and forget all them crazies and just take on the whole world.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand all this time, unknown to me, she was still fucking them both.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand that was completely inconceivable to me. inconceivable. utterly fucking inconceivable.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut, whatever- anyway-</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni walk until i get to the grave yard and turn around. i had fully planned on walking much father than that (that being only about forty minutes one direction), but i remembered the last time that i walked past that graveyard at night i freaked myself out. i don\'t know if you know the one i am talking about, but it just goes on forever. blocks and blocks, all axises.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand the cd is over. eleven tracks and thirty five minutes and it is about to play again. but wait, fuck! i have that erick morillo cd with me don\'t i? and i can\'t help but think of swinney, and i\'m kinda writing this already in my head as i walk back.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i put in the erick morillo and the erick morillo starts out something like this:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'i don\'t know where we went wrong. maybe we need to stop and let the music take control. just close your eyes. and remember.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmy first night in san francisco i kissed her in jack kerouac ally, i was rolling and it was a very nice thing. the second night she told me that she needed a little more substance from her music these days, that sure, yeah, housemusic is fun and all, but.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nat the end of the first week she really didn\'t have that much time to spend with me, cause, you know, she had friends and stuff now. and at the end of the second week it was pregnant.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nat the end of the third week she was not. it was a false alarm.</p>'),(2083,'My Venus in Furs dance is over.<br>\r\nThe masochist haze is gone.<br> \r\nThe sky is my confession, I reclaim my<br> \r\nshoulder a thousand times.<br>  \r\nShe was never here.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\'m still walking a rigid passage.<br>\r\nTo earn a living thinking about her skirt<br>\r\nor the absence of it. Chasing Waifs in the<br> \r\nafternoon, like a bullfighter<br> \r\nAgainst butterflies, they will be defeated.<br> \r\n<br>\r\nI broke my promise not to suffer, and now.<br>\r\nI have to start all over to earn my solitude.<br>\r\n'),(2081,'<p>\r\nhi. you are the skechers girl right?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh im sorry. i didn\'t know there was more then one of you. i recognized you from one of their ads. im chiamattt.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnice to meet you scechers girl.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat brings someone like you to parts like these? you live around here? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou have a really nice nose. is it original? \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwell its certainly a good job. did it hurt?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nno, i don\'t have a tatoo. id assume if it hurt less then a tatoo a tatoo must really hurt.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nyou mentioned you had it done while you were at school. what did you take? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh i see. high school. wow. how did you pay for it?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh your mom did it. thats really nice of her. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh? and your dad is a lawyer? what kind? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ninteresting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso you wanna go have a coffee sometime?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh. ok. thats cool. see ya round.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>in the head</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe did have a nice nose. god what a nose. fuck her tho. they are all the same those girls. those boys. whichever side you stand on. there is no difference. stuck up girls. stuck up guys. they make the world go round in the most fucked up round ive ever seen. sex sells, but if you dont have the money to spend, you dont have the pleasure of time well spent.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe was looking at the other dude the whole time. the other dude in his gap raver styles looking as close to catalogue as you can get. these generic puzzles all look the same and when the same gets old they realize they have nothing in common and by then theyve had two kids. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nconflicting interests. believe the bling and forever be damned. power to the pimp man. power to the bitch woman. clash of the bling. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto conform to a specific idea belief and custom is to confirm to \"god\" that you have given up and have ceased to be rational. you have ceased to think for yourself. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey look so sweet on the covers and inside pages of the magazines. at the clubs. on the tv. all dolled up for the camera. the explanation only discussing the good, the love, the up coming marriage. three months later \"tragic\" shit happens. more of the same. generic marriage. generic seperation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npeople dont want to hear the truth anymore. they want instant gratification. they want what looks secure. they equate security with a fat pay cheque and let personal happiness take the back seat. the pressure cooker needs to let off steam at some point and those times ultimately come. for some they come at time where things can be changed. where life can be put back on track. for others, things become too drastic and suicide is the only answer. its never the correct answer, but at times like that, we must remember, that we ceased to be rational. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npeople ask me when will i grow up and understand that the world is the way it is because its the way it is. i come from the school of thought that asks people to realize that the world is the way it is today because we have ceased to care about it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto say i feel things are entering a phase of futility to me is an understatement.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe girl in the ad represents just one of many conversations i have with myself on a daily basis. in my head, i turn them all into fake and ultimately \"bad\" people. even the ones i meet do nothing to try and portray some kind of unique quality. the ones that do have been taken and for that reason they shall remain nameless. the taken ones. the ones who seem cool ultimately get added to the box of qualities i look for. they become part of the model. the model from which i look to for answers regarding my state of affairs. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n'),(2082,'<p>\r\ni was playing civ. its easy for me to get distracted playing civ. its a turn based game and so when its not my turn i tend to drift off into thought or zone out watching tv. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlast night, my cat kept jumping up and down from the window in my room. my room is in the basement.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went out for a smoke and decided to bring my flashlight and see what was getting his attention. I figured it would be a big spider. Something id really hate to look at.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe neighbour uses that side to do his smoking. he\'s a real anti-social dude. i was hoping he wouldnt be there. he wasnt and so i made my way to the well. we have our window wells covered with plastic things. they are screwed into the walls and make thunder noises when the wind picks up. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshining my flashlight down into the well i see my cat peering through the screen looking at me peering through the distored vision of the well cover. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlookin further, i see a little plump mouse crouched on the cill looking at my cat. im not sure if anything was said, but im sure my cat would have liked to take things outside.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npondering whether the mouse had a way out i decide to drop some pebbles and sticks down; trying to hit it softly so that it would scurry to safety. it scurried around in a fashion i recognized as being the walk of a drunken driver. after letting it display a few futile attempts at trying to climb out of the well i returned to the driveway to plan the killing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit was late. the rifle was definately out of the question. my target was lucky. it was small and fast. it was dark outside. my visibility compromised by the location and time of day. crossbow was out of the question. i needed something good for close range combat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had decided that any sort of spray would be out of the question. the wind had picked up, but with the anthrax scares i didn\'t want to cause a panic. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy tazer got dropped off the possible list due to the fact that the wells are lined with corrogated steel and well i didn\'t want to short out anything. i mean its only a mouse. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had decided on the only possible solution to this whole mess. i headed downstairs and got a few screwdrivers and my wire cutters. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfearing disease i put on a pair of white gardening gloves. they were a bit small and made my fingers feel much longer then im sure they are. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfixating the flashlight on the screws holding the plastic covering in the cold is easy. unscrewing this fucking screw wasnt. finally getting it off my temper had soared to a point where it would have been nice to have had something helpless to take the rage out on. it was then i remembered my mission. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni felt like darwin on the beach of some small island. a mouse is a small and simple creature. its worth on the scale of things might be miniscule, yet there seems no doubt its purpose serves the chain i sit atop of. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nreaching in. my cat the only witness. i grab the mouse, squeezing hard so that it could not escape the fate it so fittingly chose for itself when it got itself stuck in MY well. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe walk to the backyard was brisk. the mouse squealing in one hand, my orange black and decker flashlight in the other. i wondered if my neighbours might mistaken me for a thief. my mind raced back to the mouse. its small claws trying to loosen my grip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni found a nice spot in the yard. a spot where i could set an example for the rest of the wildlife i get in my yard. they think they can come in here and do what they want, well this is what will happens to those who stray off the beaten path. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni lowered my hand and freed the mouse, letting it scurry off into the fields behind my house, hopefully happily eaten by an owl some time later.\r\n</p>'),(2084,'<A HREF=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1928.txt\">Last time</A> I wrote about the \"stop\" phenomena, where bands will sing/say the word stop and then the song will stop for two seconds. An unoriginal trick of the same producer is what I theorized, but something that affected bands from U2 to Cibo Matto to Macy Gray.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nToday, on the eve of Thanksgiving, I offer another observation: The Scowling Cover.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe Scowling Cover is most often found on â€œalternativeâ€ rock stations (of course it baffles us how anything can be â€œalternativeâ€ for almost 10 years now with complete media saturation and usage in Mountain Dew ads). These are the stations where they have 35-year-old men playing DJ, doing their best Paulie Shore voice, trying to reach the heights of Sean Penn in â€œFast Times.â€\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n The bands they play are countless awful reproductions of the same blend of 311-meets-Soundgarden, a delightfully faux-AngryWM blend of hormones that can only be labeled â€œDate Rape Rock.â€ Recently on German television I saw a documentary on the growth of â€œRap Rock,â€ it was in German I had no idea what they were saying, but everything was clear as they drew a twisted bough from the Beastie Boys to Cypress Hill to Limp Bizkit and then on. These are the bands that make Green Day look like the James Taylor of songwriting, AC/DC of balls and the Jimmy Page of musicianship.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOf course youâ€™ve heard the Scowling Cover. Today for the first time I heard a cover of Soft Cellâ€™s 80â€™s classic â€œTainted Love.â€ Recently one of these bands, leaning towards the 311 side of the formula, did Michael Jacksonâ€™s â€œSmooth Criminal.â€ Last year it was â€œBlue Mondayâ€ by New Order. As the record labels handed MTV briefcases of kickbacks, they created a myth that Limp Bizkit broke on MTV Spring Break covering a Cure song (correct me if Iâ€™m wrong, â€œJust Like Heavenâ€). \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWhat is the predilection of these bands to choose the songs of bands who found an initial fan base in young gay men and girls who dreamed of finding a hollow-chested English boy to cuddle, a Morrissey of their own? The most obvious critic would point to a punk rock version of homophobia: See, hereâ€™s your drippy love song, homo! Or maybe it is sort of statement to fans on a musical level: See, we can take this faggy song and make it R-o-C-k! \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBut maybe, just maybe, itâ€™s pure corporate strategy. A cynical attempt to pick the most â€œleft fieldâ€ song to cover and â€œjust do it.â€ Punk rock embraces irony with a wink and the hand behind the back dangling that chain wallet with the corporate paycheck inside.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe formula then is this:<BR><BLOCKQUOTE>\r\nUnlikely, yet already established popular song <BR>[multiplied by] <BR>oppositeness of original author of song  <BR>[divided by] <BR>cookie cutter corporate â€œpunkâ€ band thatâ€™s covering it <BR>[plus or minus] <BR>tempo shift (up for 311-esque bands, down for soundgarden variety bands)<BR> <HR>=$$$</BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe origins of the Scowling Cover are vague, most likely the first example in Western culture goes back hundreds of years, to a composer creating a piano sonata and then some wise ass musician coming along and playing it with a curl of the back mimicking gravitas, hunched over the keyboard, only looking up to see if the parlor audience was amused to the point of snickering. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMore recently, in the jazz age, there was a different relationship between the author of songs and artists. Often songwriters would be famous on their own and never become artists. Many popular artists would never pen an original song, but would have a career based on covering â€œstandards.â€ Purely through interpretation of familiar tunes would an artist be able to display their voice, virtuosity and originality. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis relationship made an artistic leap in the 40â€™s with the arrival of be bop. Be bop, of course, is the incredibly fast style of jazz music that was a brilliant mix of incredible musicianship and humor. Solos would be played â€œat the speed of thought.â€ Something else happened to songs in be bop. Standards were reduced to chord changes, in which the recognizable melody would be played in a few bars and then left behind in a fury of playing. Sometimes the recognizable melody would be left out altogether. After improvising for weeks in basement jazz clubs, new melodies would be found and then the musicians would change the name of the song and take writing credit when it was recorded. New standards were created. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nPart of be bop culture was the humor, best seen in the wit of a Charlie Parker. The wink before going into an insane flurry of chord changes at what only a few years earlier would be an incomprehensible tempo. Pumped full of heroin, Parker must have greeted many of the old guard of jazz musicians at clubs, nodded in their direction from the bandstand and then took their careful compositions and nuked them with his genius. As the crowd applauded, half confused, the old jazz master clapping along so to not seem out of touch, Bird would give them that wink. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nA few years later, in 1960, Coltrane broke through commercially by covering â€œMy Favorite Things.â€ If you are familiar with the original (best seen in â€œSound of Musicâ€), you can be sure that the album version of this song, at over 13 minutes long, was a force to be reckoned with. Unlike the be-bop era of covering standards, Coltraneâ€™s version of â€œMy Favorite Thingsâ€ doesnâ€™t seem to have wit or irony. It seems completely earnest. He takes this light, frolicking show tune and pours his emotions into it. This is best seen in the live rendition, of which there are several versions on CD, some reaching into the 20, 25 minutes and half-hour mark. This song in a live setting is blistering madness. â€œMy Favorite Thingsâ€ goes from a cute list found in the original lyrics (â€œcream colored ponies and crisp apple strudelsâ€), to this symphonic â€œsheets of soundâ€ version which seems to encompass not only the actual range of favorite things one can discover in a lifetime, but the depth of emotion one can feel about them.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThen we reach the Beatles and the British Invasion. Many early Beatles hits, as well as one hit wonders of their comrades are American R&B covers. They took hits in the US, covered them and sold them back as much bigger hits. They discovered that kids would make the same song popular two or three times, because they loved the new sound so much. Of course Beatles versions of Chuck Berry et al, were cheerful, fun and in tribute. By the end of the 60â€™s this deep reverence would be turned into a pseudo-religion, as British blues rock bands such as Cream would mine ancient blues vaults for songs to re-interrupt with the kind of earnestness that begs for a sense of humor. Led Zeppelin with their shameless rip-offs of blues riffs and lyrics tossed in some of that much-needed humor and, with their logical approach to the testicle inflation of Willie Dixon songs, they became the greatest rock band in that musicâ€™s history.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWhich brings us to punk rock. Punk rock hated what had come before and created something new. Because many punk bands didnâ€™t have the musical skills to cover songs, there are fewer covers to discuss than came before and later. One memorable cover, and an important one for this discussion, is Sid Viciousâ€™ post Sex Pistols cover of â€œMy Way,â€ the Sinatra standard. This is probably the landmark Scowling Cover. This is probably the cover to which all the other Scowling Covers owe a debt. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe problem of course is that it is over 20 years later. Itâ€™s the same â€œMy Wayâ€ being recyled over and over. <I>Look at me, mom! Iâ€™m rebelling! Fuck you, Iâ€™ve got the keys to the car!</I> These bands with their cute sneers and the same old guitar riff all demand another Frente to come along. What is more â€œedgyâ€ what is truly more provoking than what you hear on the radio today is Rod Stewart doing â€œThis Old Heart of Mineâ€ and Phil Collinsâ€™ inspired take on â€œYou Canâ€™t Hurry Love.â€ And when a music fan says they would prefer Phil Collins, even Phil Collins screaming â€œtake a good look at me now,â€ to yet another Scowling Cover, it must be time for real change. \r\n'),(2085,'<p>I\'m at a turning point.</p>\r\n<p>Long ago, I started something and it was left alone for a long time.  It has been left alone for long enough that it holds near no importance at the current moment.  I still have unanswered questions but I no longer feel that they are appropriate to ask.  And not everything has been said and maybe it\'s for the best.</p>\r\n<p>Sometimes it\'s safer just to walk away and not face things.  It\'s weak, cowardly and slightly childish.  It\'s easier.</p>\r\n<p>You might like the answers to your questions and you might get the most terrifying response: you just might get the answers you want to hear.  What happens then?  What would you do?  It\'s certainly not what you expect and the unexpected is unquestionably frightening.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t think that I believe walking away and giving up is the best solution to the problem, but it\'s a short-term happiness and sometimes a simple, non-forced, smile on your face is reward enough.</p>\r\n'),(2086,'le  .o0O (holidays make me feel like i don\'t know nobody, and nobody knows me.) \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nword.'),(2087,'<p>i killed my doppleganger tonight. came home from work, tired, irritable. it was hunched over the keyboard, tapping out it\'s insane poetry. it didn\'t even try to dash for the window, as it usually does, disappearing behind ominous skyscrapers, trailing disturbing laughter. it just sat there and snickered, a knowing and insidious grin on it\'s face. it was getting too bold.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for the past six days the doppleganger had been haunting me. the first night i saw it, i wasn\'t even really surprised. i\'d come home, as usual, exhausted and beer-breathing, to confront my sallow and stricken self in the hallway mirror. i regarded my reflection, as usual, with a grim look-what-the-cat-dragged-in countenance. the reflection though,  (which i realized no longer belonged to me), was grinning. i knew for a fact i wasn\'t grinning. in my forebrain, activity was taking place. i was digging feverishly for primal knowledge, juggling this with increasing uneasiness. all it came up with, all my mind could offer me, was a sense of foreboding. dim recollections of legends that had been crushed under the feet of urban sprawl, blue-collar futility, and the endless parade of cocktails.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>confused, i chalked the apparition up to a lingering flashback. acid casualties get used to these things. i went off to bed, to entangle myself in a fitful rendition of sleep. over the following days my shadow seemed to follow me too closely on the street. and when i\'d get home, i\'d catch a glimpse of myself retreating via the fire escape, every night this escape was more reluctant than the last. danger signals went off in my head. i was getting the kind of shakes alcohol couldn\'t flatten. something had to be done.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so i killed it. i\'ll spare the details, it doesn\'t matter. it\'s gone. whoosh to the void with purplish smoke. not even a pool of blood to convince me i\'d done anything real. i\'d half-expected to be enveloped in an atmosphere of quasi-epic heroism, but there was nothing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now it\'s just me left. i am a mundane person. nothing much interesting to say. my life is a monotonous pattern. i must like it that way. no complexities, no disturbing surprises. no dopplegangers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am at peace. i am not happy, but i am at peace. i sleep ok.  i guess.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>somewhere out in the strange and mundane world, your doppleganger is walking, looking for you. an ancient clone shadowing you, embodied harbinger of death. it\'s right on the fringe of what we all reassure each other is reality, sniffing you out.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>it\'ll find you, and you\'ll have to confront it, an unexpected element of danger and mystery. will you kill it?</p>'),(2088,'<p>\r\nHi all.  Longtime reader, firstime writer.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI just saw Miracle on 34th Street.  What a well-made film.  It\'s also very practical, albeit in a very disintuitive way.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere\'s this theme of \"common sense\" running throughout the plot.  It\'s common sense that allows Natalie Wood\'s mom to achieve a position of authority and respect in what was (still is?) a man\'s world.  It\'s common sense, above all else, that mom is teaching her daughter.  And it\'s common sense that both mom and daughter have to abandon to get what they really want.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBelief is the antithesis of common sense within the story\'s framework.  During the film\'s climax, Natalie Wood can be heard saying faintly \"It\'s silly, but I believe it,\" and her mom signs the bottom of Natalie\'s letter to Kris Kringle with \"I believe in you.\"  \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt\'s cute.  It\'s movie-magic.  It\'s a great work of imagination.  And it\'s perfectly practical.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nCommon sense is a valuable asset, and it gets a lot done.  But it is very focused and demanding -- it demands that we know what comes next, that we know how to do something or get something, that we know what to do.  Common sense can (it doesn\'t always, but it can) get in the way of the much more powerful agent of belief, for belief allows us to delegate the practice and protocol of common sense to the Universe at large.  This is the same Universe, mind you, that organized you and me into being from a seeming void.  And I might add that (at least in my case), it did a far better job than I would\'ve if the task had been left to me and my imagination.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo if the essence of creation involves both instrument and agent, it would serve us well to remember that we do have a choice of whether to be that instrument, or that agent.  It would serve us well to remember that we can choose to be the instrument of common sense, or the agent that commands the Universe to use that common sense on our behalf.\r\n</p><p>\r\nChoose wisely, kids.  Happy holidays to all, and thanks for the light and not-so-light reading.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2089,'<p>striding in quick breathless greeting she would taste like cold night air and rain\r\nfinish this drink so we can get out of here sidelong appraisals which will echo \r\na few times later on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>out rain dripping hood up no peripherals just a voice walking where to?\r\ndown favorite bar avenue in through big heavy wooden doors iron clasps\r\ndrum n bass pounding the smoke? no, no breakbeats not really trance\r\ndoesn\'t matter can\'t remember just following her is all, upstairs\r\nperfect table i thought encircled loft overlook\r\nbut it\'s really loud she said yeah it is where else?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>back out check out closed down the road past crusty tavern return\r\ndoorman smiling like he knows smile back yeah we don\'t know\r\nupstairs gin & tonic she\'s right can\'t hear but like leaning in to catch \r\nthe words and her immediate air is there try to keep my smoke \r\naway last call lights come up have to remember time</p>\r\n\r\n<p>are you tired? no it\'s just early afternoon for me let\'s go\r\nwander again no late night open doors in this city\r\nyeah that place with the shark sign i think? roadmap menus\r\nthey haven\'t fully grown their bathroom graffiti yet \r\nback out again she\'s cornered by windows \r\nspain and germany? where\'s the accent from then\r\nsounds english mother from wales oh i nod</p>\r\n\r\n<p>well i think i have beer at my apartment\r\n(visualize two rolling rocks in refrigerator)\r\nnot sure i should ask but alcoholics will \r\nsay anything eventually where do you live?\r\ndown there i point at cluster of skyscrapers\r\nthat\'s not far she says and i\'m glad it\'s yes</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she walks as fast as me didn\'t notice then \r\nforgotten scarf wanna go back? no she says quickly\r\nphase and space of half-block silence\r\nnot uncomfortable but hard to read then\r\nconversation crops up again downtown lights\r\npass over freeway wistful travel thoughts</p>\r\n\r\n<p>apartment not the same now difference is good\r\ndid you do these? yeah just very quickly got tired\r\nof white walls staring back at me light incense\r\nwhat do you want to listen to? up to you\r\ndon\'t know what you have\r\nfeel slightly silly trying to decide\r\nrealize i don\'t know what i have either right now\r\ncurve in front of me always good default</p>\r\n\r\n<p>talk some more common thread kept going\r\nsitting side by side on futon she borrows blanket\r\nusual boy thoughts run alongside spoken thread\r\nbut oddly they stay at bay thinking no rush\r\nstill hard to concentrate on words for a while\r\ncrazy she\'s right there but am fine with listening\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen instantaneously it\'s seven a.m. \r\nand that\'s the night\'s end she gets up \r\nputting on the very cool black coat \r\nand furry collar cuffs she should be in \r\nsome european film stops to embrace\r\nplans for art museum on sunday and \r\nshe\'s gone girl-scent fades less rapidly</p>\r\n\r\n<p>eleven twenty two i think fuck the holiday i want art.</p>\r\n'),(2090,'<p>Dear Ms. Lâ€™Engle,\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nMy name is James Tyler Lee. I am an English student at the University of South Carolina, and sometimes a writer myself. I must admit, I have not read all of your books, nor even half of those available, but I greatly enjoyed the few that I have read. I first learned of your work as a third-grader, from my school librarian, who was always on the lookout for books that I might like. This was most likely to keep me from being disruptive in class, as I could be quite so, and quite often in those days! I remember being so taken with A Wrinkle In Time that I would read it during spelling tests; the teacher would call out the word to be spelled, and I would look up from my book and write it down. In retrospect, I guess I could have looked in the pages at hand for the correct spelling, but the thought never occurred to me (and I can only wonder about my teacher!). The book and its sequels had a powerful effect on me, though over the years I had all but forgotten about them, until tonight actually; I have gotten out of bed to write this, and to look up your mailing address on the internet. Thinking back on them, I am somewhat bothered.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThis series of books, and indeed most of your â€œchildrensâ€™ books,â€ seem to share the common theme of a young person or persons, different than most, separated from the herd as it were by exceptional intelligence and open-mindedness, coming of age and learning to navigate the rough waters of adolescence and so on. This is admirable-enough stuff, a fairly common sight in literature for young adults and children, part of a time-honored tradition. Judged strictly by the criteria of this genre, your books without further dissection stand up wholly and are filled with quality, in my humble and largely uneducated opinion. Butâ€”that is not what has kept me awake tonight.\r\n</p><p>\r\n I get the same feeling from these books as I did when I read Carlos Castenedaâ€™s work, or The Celestine Prophecy by James Redfield. I want to believe that what they are saying is true, or at least based in fact, because if they areâ€”then the world makes some kind of sense. There is in fact an underlying order to all things, something to strive towards, truly a â€œgoodâ€ and an â€œevil.â€ I so desperately want life to have meaning, even when the purely scientific (maybe pessimistic) side of me says â€œoh, itâ€™s only entropy, what has been unmade shall not be remade, all life is simply a chemistry assignment gone astrayâ€¦we are but particles of fuzz on a great rock in a void, vibrating and spreading out when the sun strikes us.â€ Surely you know the feeling, even if I am getting emotional and losing coherency as I often do, being only human and blessed or cursed with curiosity. Perhaps if I were more of a scholar I could sit down with your books, or those books, and arrive at a definitive conclusion for myself. At any rate, my question is this: Are your metaphysical concepts, like the Farandola mouse, or the Tesseract, or â€œnaming,â€ â€œkytheingâ€(sp?), the various nonhuman intelligences, are any of them based on anything other than your fascination with certain concepts in science, and your imagination? That is to say, have you seen anything like that in â€œreal life?â€ If not, I am no more or less bewildered by life than before, and I continue my quest to find meaning in it. If so, well then, I should like to know more. I could probably dedicate my life to pursuing some (any!) physical evidence of a â€œplotâ€ for human existence, a purpose for life outside of the biological imperative. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI imagine that you will answer this letter, and that you may have over the years answered a multitude of its cousins. Maybe you will tell me that these are questions that one does not ask, especially when the books are clearly marked â€œfiction,â€ according to the Library of Congress. At any rate, thank you for writing them, I enjoyed them, and the world, higher meanings notwithstanding, is a better place for your efforts.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSincerely,\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nÂ Â Â Â James Tyler Lee, Jr.                 \r\n\r\n                                                              \r\n'),(2091,'there are two kinds of people in this world: proud sleepers and guilty sleepers.'),(2092,'<p>Today has been all about the exciting Saturday morning fun.</p>\r\n<p>A woman came into work at around 8:15, ordered a drink and stood in the  side doorway, watching the street.  I told her that the drink was ready and as she came to fetch it, she informed me that a car was on fire just outside.  Sure enough, there was a car, parked on the Eglinton south west side of the intersection with Old Park Road, flames blasting out of the open hood.</p>\r\n<p>This is one of those events you don\'t see everday, if ever.  In fact, I know that I have never witnessed a car on fire before this morning.</p>\r\n<p>I saw the burning insulation fall from the hood and disappear in a fireball atop the engine block.</p>\r\n<p>I had never before called 911.  It was rather uneventful, though.  A call technician answered my phone call and hurriedly asked: \"Police, fire, ambulance?\"  I was slightly confused by the question and eventually said \"fire.\"  He must not have heard me correctly because he frantically blasted his question at me again, so I hurriedly threw my previous answer back at him.  After this, I was transferred to the appropriate operator at which point, I heard a familiar question: \"Hello, Fire Department, please state your emergency.\"</p>\r\n<p>I stated my emergency and was told that they were already on the way to the situation.  We thanked each other and thus concludes my first 911 experience.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s not nearly as dramatic without William Shatner narrating the whole affair.</p>\r\n'),(2093,'<p>something is wrong with the way things are going. i am concerned. concerned for myself mainly, but I have other concerns too.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am finishing up a year, as we all are. many changes this year huh? I think it all started in January. I was laid off around then, and about that time I became wise of the situation that many are in. days were long at first, and that worried me. looking for a job, not wanting a job. I started a relationship with a woman around that time. I think that i started that relationship so as not to feel so much pain. three months later, the pain was so bad that I had to break that up. I spent months sleeping much, drinking more, and smoking every day. i started drinking coffee again too. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni did get a job at one point, but then i soon realized I could make more money staying on the dol. the checks were steady, and i could do as i wished. benefits exhausted last month. it was a relief really. I went back to school, thinking that there was something there for me. I found a few things interesting, but then soon realized that I wasn\'t there for the right reasons. I am still in school, but it is not what I do with my time. I just go to class. I read the assigned material, i grasp what I am taught only I don\'t strive to achieve grades. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI went to california to meet and visit the swinney folk a few months ago. I was received by kat warmly. we shared moments that are engraved in my mind. i smile writing this. while on the beach I realized that I was not there to visit people, but to see the coast again. my stare reached out across the ocean, for miles I could see. i remembered being on ship, staring out at the world that you can only see from  ship. in my mind, i was thinking how nice it would be to be out there alone again. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI take advantage of friendships for what they are. if one friend likes to talk about depression, i listen and speak about depression. if another speaks of music, or computers, I participate in that conversation too. on rare occasions I start my own conversation. I like to talk about pain, i like to talk about survival. i think of how i can deprive myself of more. deprivation is my way of feeding myself pain. i enjoy this pain, mind you. I am not speaking of breakdown, though it might not be that far off either. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni started a conversation about celibacy a few weeks ago, about a friend of mine that has chosen to be celibate. I was getting ready to say something about how admirable that is, when one of the ladies (one i am fucking) spoke up about how stupid that is. I don\'t want to fuck her anymore. I don\'t want to fuck anymore. \r\n</p><p>\r\nyesterday, i watched movies. dj luna met me at the theater. I decided to watch another movie right after the first was over. I saw a girl looking at me as I waited to go in. i realized that she was alone just like me, though she had friends with her. I wanted to tell her that it was okay, okay to be alone if she wanted to. i was overwhelmed with the thought of spending another night fucking, so i didn\'t speak to her. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni really do believe that looking at lovely things gives you lovely eyes. i try not to look to long, for some strange reason. I capture images in my mind, of the way things are supposed to be but they rarely appear that way in reality. its a shame that flags are waving for the reasons why they are waving. i wish more people would open their eyes and see this all for what it really is, not what they want it to be. i wish the fag bashers would stop hitting, but I know that they won\'t because they are scared that it will happen to them. i wish that people would speak about things without filters, express pain and express love. at the point in which we can make love in the streets without guilt we will finally be free, but not every one can watch and participate in the orgy. some just refuse to enjoy it, life that is. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmaybe this is why I do nothing, say nothing. maybe I will stop doing this, just because I am scared that someone will like what I say, then I will have to explain to them that I don\'t know what the fuck I am talking about anyway. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI am awake, again, and this dream doesn\'t make much sense to me either.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI think a friend of mine is going to kill himself, but its hard to tell him not to. because i think maybe he might be better off that way. i can think of five or more people that are on the verge of suicide. i wonder this, if I were walking on the bridge and saw the man jump, would i turn around to watch him fall? would i of stopped him if I could? could I join him there on the rails and hold his hand as we both fall to never-never land? \r\n</p><p>\r\nI don\'t want to, nor do I think I should. but there is a part of me that thinks that it would be much easier than spending my time talking about it. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni wonder if any of you see things this way too?\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am not asking for help, I am not trying to offend. I am just trying to talk. trying to translate what is in my head, translate it into a better language. too many distractions, so many people, so much music. I can\'t seem to find a good roast of coffee anymore. afraid that everything has been touched by the plague of pain.</p>'),(2094,'<p>\r\ni have to be out of my current house in less than one week. none of my stuff is packed. my girlfriend has packed most of her stuff. we found a house. it is big and beautiful and accepts dogs with open arms. it is very hard to find a house to take an 80 pound dog. it is close to a mcmenamins and a couple delicious vegan restaurants. but just because we found a place, does not mean we have a place.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe have been looking for a month. i have scoured this city. i have seen houses for rent i could never afford. houses that i could never pay money to live in. i am behind on my homework. i have lots of math to learn. and quickly. finals are coming. and an essay. couldn\'t have taken fictional writing this term could i? oh, and then there is my game program. a very small game cause i am just learning how to code. well, i can script like there is no tomorrow. but C is a little different than PHP.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso now i sit and race to get things done at work that i have been putting off. i hope that something will smile on us and we will get this wonderful house. my brain throbs and i think that i don\'t have time to catch up and pack and find a place to live and all that other little shit that comes with everyday life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso, basically, i am in a state of suspended hope.\r\n</p>'),(2095,'<p>\r\ni have to be out of my current house in less than one week. none of my stuff is packed. my girlfriend has packed most of her stuff. we found a house. it is big and beautiful and accepts dogs with open arms. it is very hard to find a house to take an 80 pound dog. it is close to a mcmenamins and a couple delicious vegan restaurants. but just because we found a place, does not mean we have a place.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe have been looking for a month. i have scoured this city. i have seen houses for rent i could never afford. houses that i could never pay money to live in. i am behind on my homework. i have lots of math to learn. and quickly. finals are coming. and an essay. couldn\'t have taken fictional writing this term could i? oh, and then there is my game program. a very small game cause i am just learning how to code. well, i can script like there is no tomorrow. but C is a little different than PHP.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso now i sit and race to get things done at work that i have been putting off. i hope that something will smile on us and we will get this wonderful house. my brain throbs and i think that i don\'t have time to catch up and pack and find a place to live and all that other little shit that comes with everyday life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso, basically, i am in a state of suspended hope.\r\n</p>'),(2096,'<p>\r\ni wheeled \'round the corner, feet spinning. the air was fraegnated with that fish smell. the smell of the sea. it came channeling up through the methodical canyons downtown. i walked against it, inhaling, capturing sea air in my lungs, creating my own private tide. air and water, these are my signifiers, not that i\'m lacking in fire.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\never since i landed here, i\'ve loved that smell. i\'m not sure why. for the first time in seven years, i wondered what it was. was it the fragrance of rotting fish? or was it the smell of alive fish, swimming around out there...did the fish blow bubbles? i pictured fish-blown bubbles making soft explosions on the sea surface, the flap of gull wings driving the fish-bubble air through downtown.</p>\r\n<p>was i breathing fish-breath? or was the sea smell something else, the collective scent of skin from every creature that dwelt in the waters of the world.</p>\r\n<p>i wandered on, extrapolating, and wondered if a hungry child in china might someday get drunk off of my beer breath.</p>'),(2097,'The weather in this city has been unusual lately, going from those reluctantly sticky and humid mornings to evenings that chill with a breeze that slips through your skin and slinks to your your core, embracing the bones.<br><br>\r\nYesterday evening, nice and clear as it were (you could almost see the stars) I went to see a somewhat vague but interesting art exhibition.  It included some artwork by a friend of mine, so I felt compelled to go along when asked. So, I spent my evening languishing over some bland prints, some nonchalant sculpture, a few passe installations involving barcaloungers and plaster cows, and some seen-it-before varities of photography, as well as the requisite measure of college-angst-y paintings.<br><br> \r\nA couple of the people I went with were commenting that I looked somewhat dazed and bewildered, like I didn\'t know where I was.  This put me in a thoughtful mood, as I <i>did</i> feel somewhat strange: I can\'t explain it, so hopefully some attempt at writing it here will help me identify these feelings.  Something was just weighing heavily inside me, and at the moment, I didn\'t feel like being around many people, perhaps <i>any</i> for that matter, especially people who are pretending to like each other\'s cookie-cutter, tired-out art.<br><br>\r\nThings just didn\'t feel right, not there, not in the gallery, and certainly not with any of my friends.<br><br>\r\nI left the exhibition after a short time (mostly because this feeling was overwhelming me, but also because it can probably be justifiably argued that I simply don\'t understand enough about \"art\" to \"appreciate\" what I was seeing, and can\'t really pull off effectively pretending to). I walked a short way down to the river that runs right through the heart of the city, and sat on a bench smoking and watching people run along the path, skate, jog, walk with lovers, listen to walkmans. I\'m impressed by these people because they appear (at least superficially) to have some sort of ambition - everyone perfectly synchronised in their moment in time, all very mechanically going about their riverside activities, all exercising their muscles.<br><br>\r\nAfter that I went to Burger King to get food - it was nearly 8pm and I hadn\'t had dinner (and I\'d had a glass of wine, which was probably not helping).  Just as I was placing my order (fries and shake, as I eschew beefy Whoppers), a friend called to ask (rather exasperatingly) where I was. I explained, and asked <i>why it mattered so damn much</i>, as I wasn\'t (nor have I ever been) much of a contributing source for art anyway.  I suppose he decided he couldn\'t be bothered with my rant, as he just hung up on me as I was laying down the cash for my food. I know, its impolite to talk on the phone while in line for something, but...<br><br> \r\nSo, after I ate, I headed back down to the river to smoke some more, and when it began to get really dark, and somewhat more chilly, I went home.<br><br>'),(2098,'<p><font face=\"Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, san-serif\" size=\"2\">How lucky for her \r\n  no one has touched me in two years. I feel like an alien. No one respects the \r\n  fact that 95 % percent of the people on planet earth are complete idiots. She \r\n  does of course. Bless her heart. I want to drink her starlight. I want to listen \r\n  to <a href=\"http://www.blackfly.co.uk/thestrokes/lyrics.htm#bl\">Barely Legal</a> \r\n  really loud. If she would let me. I want to respect her. She knows my heart \r\n  is a box. She knows I\'m dying of loneliness. She knows it is a valid reason \r\n  to die. She is going to put a stack of comic books on my grave. My shadow glows. \r\n  Not enough of a reason to see her naked. </font></p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://www.fantagraphics.com/artist/acme/strip.gif\"></p>'),(2099,'<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2099_1.pjpeg\"><br><br>\r\nI want it even though its bad for me. Thatâ€™s a curious reality as Iâ€™m really quite averse to self-destructive behavior. Perhaps it was the way I was raised, or perhaps a mere product of circumstance, but Iâ€™ve perfected an certain expertise: the ability to abberate from my true nature at the drop of a hat, at the whim of convenience.  Or maybe boredom is my poison. I often feel so distant from any sense of ambition, such a product of my lifeâ€™s events (the traumatic and the redeeming, even, all render some final twist on my reality) that I dawdle, and veteran wheel-spinner that I am, Iâ€™ve grown attached to the phenomenon of my undefinably slipping treads; traction-lacking points of contact with <i>terra firma</i> that allow me no grip, no frictional coefficient, no essence of forward movement, vector-progress, or linear accomplishment.<br><br>\r\nI often find myself wandering between these parallel realities, and the walk is always quite nice. My two parallel tracks are so very similar, but are set apart by a substantial enough parallax to allow me a profound shift in perspective.  I currently assume that it is this new, pungently-flavoured, high-content perspective that keeps me regularly jumping track for more and more.  Iâ€™m so excited to break out of my quarter-century familiar paradigm, so refreshed by escaping through its safety-lock that I just canâ€™t resist.<br><br>\r\nBut, itâ€™s this paradox that exhausts me.  I certainly donâ€™t want to abandon my painstakingly crafted <i>Peter</i> reality altogether, but the luxury of the uncharted masses of my parallel world do always tempt.  On the other hand, I cant fathom the withdrawl that Iâ€™d experience if I were to attempt to wean myself from my exciting new world and its embedded views, associated thrills, and moments of enchantment.<br><br>\r\nSo, I often grasp both worlds, one in each hand, bridging the gap with my torso, my muscles burning with the frustration of constant suspension in this nether region, avoiding a wholehearted commitment to either. And each day seens new splinters, new subgenres and tributaries spring forth from each world. Its as if, upon waking each day, I must survey, catalog, and peruse the new acres of sociality and consciousness that have crashed into my reality as morningâ€™s tides subside.<br><br>\r\nI know Iâ€™ll abandon the shiny, new, empty-ish realities eventually, as I mature, and it will be a seamless, smiling process, full of good-riddances and bon voyages. But for the present, my feet arenâ€™t founded firmly enough on anything to make that leap.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2100,'up in the opium chamber<BR>we don\'t know it\'s wednesday<BR><BR>on the reel-to-reel:<BR>seven steps to heaven<BR>seven views of jerusalem<BR>seven<BR>siete simios<BR>seventh son<BR>the magnificent seven<BR>two sevens clash<BR><BR>eddie spots me<BR>while i do<BR>incline dumbbell flies.<BR>anna lays on the couch<BR>watching \"the other half.\"<BR><BR>\"that mario lopez<BR>is such a ride,\"<BR>eddie says.<BR>anna and i concur.<BR>i study mario\'s<BR>sweet little made-up face.<BR>\"i want dimples,\"<BR>i announce.<BR><BR>eddie and i<BR>do some more of his crank<BR>off the glass-topped end table.<BR>says he got it out in aripeka.<BR>probably had to blow a<BR>string of nasty bikers for it.<BR>it\'s good for fucking:<BR>makes your dick slightly numb.<BR><BR>eddie does a set.<BR>study myself in the mirror:<BR>gained weight while i was on zyprexa.<BR>thirty pounds in two months.<BR><BR>me:  i wonder what the poor people are doing today?<BR><BR>her:  living with their parents.<BR><BR>lay on the bench.<BR>bust out some presses.<BR>lay there, breathing hard.<BR>eddie pushes the strap of my tank top over,<BR>tongues my nipple.<BR>\"listen,\" i say,<BR>\"if you can\'t stand the heat,<BR>don\'t monkey with the thermostat.\"<BR>\"nigga, please.\"<BR><BR>he goes down<BR>to the bathroom.<BR><BR>me:  so, it\'s today, tomorrow<BR>and the day after?<BR><BR>her:  yesterday, today, tomorrow.<BR><BR>me:  i thought you said<BR>today was the first day.<BR>didn\'t you fuck him yesterday?<BR><BR>nothing.<BR><BR>me:  so, is he like your boyfriend now?<BR><BR>her:  he\'s YOUR boyfriend.<BR>what makes you think you got dibs?<BR><BR>me:  i guess that makes him OUR boyfriend.<BR><BR>last night at midnight<BR>i went out to the crossroads.<BR>middle of the state forest.<BR>buried a mojo bag<BR>full of lodestones,<BR>cemetary dirt,<BR>mum petals,<BR>a sacajawea dollar.<BR>came over the hole.<BR>now the baby\'s going to look like eddie.<BR>have his goofy little ears<BR>and panoramic mind,<BR>strut around and say \"axe\"<BR>instead of \"ask.\"<BR>have to get my spermas in there<BR>and duke it out with his.<BR><BR>he comes back up.<BR>we all hit off the vaporizer<BR>(lebanese blonde),<BR>undress.<BR>they kiss.<BR><BR>me:  do you think we could both<BR>fit up your cooch at the same time?<BR><BR>her:  with that little vienna sausage you got?<BR>sure.<BR>but we\'re not gonna try that little experiment,<BR>johnny.<BR><BR>afterward:<BR><BR>me:  what does \"savoir faire\" mean?<BR><BR>her:  expertise.<BR><BR>laying in a pile<BR>on the folded-down couch<BR>heart going boom-boom<BR>head feels like HAL<BR>after dave disconnected him<BR>car door slams<BR>raise up on my elbows<BR>look over the edge of the gable<BR>dad.<BR>he won\'t come up here.<BR>he\'s growing a beard<BR>for the christmas cantata<BR>gonna be a wise man<BR>i tell him he looks like grizzly adams<BR>eddie doesn\'t remember that show<BR>tells him he looks like grady<BR>on \"sanford and son\"<BR>he gives me a pretty wide berth<BR>ever since six years ago<BR>i knocked him out, took off his clothes<BR>tied him up<BR>whipped him with a black nylon rope<BR>til i saw an irridescent snake<BR>crawl out of his ass<BR>sam and green johnny<BR>told me to<BR>don\'t ever listen to those guys<BR>if you run across them<BR>dad and i<BR>laugh about it now.<BR>i wonder how he\'ll react<BR>to being a grandpa again<BR>probably kick us out<BR>we\'ll be boondockin\' it.<BR><BR>eddie:  who\'s that?<BR><BR>me:  bobo.<BR><BR>eddie:  who?<BR><BR>me:  grady.  did you lock the door?<BR><BR>her:  he won\'t come up here.<BR><BR>a flock of white birds<BR>flies over the dairy next door<BR>reminds me of when<BR>i got out of jail in san miguel<BR>took a cab out to the impound lot<BR>to pick up the car.<BR>the cops had stolen<BR>the wine i stole<BR>from the buffet place<BR>on calle de reloj<BR>planned on drinking it<BR>while i took<BR>the stolen ativan<BR>the farmacia valium<BR>everybody said<BR>get out of town<BR>now they\'ll shake you down<BR>for everything you got<BR>stood there in the lot<BR>impound woman looking on<BR>should i go north to real de catorce<BR>south to the federal district<BR>across the road<BR>a family of wild parrots<BR>landed in a tree<BR>this feels like home<BR>i thought<BR>why leave?<BR>moved my stuff<BR>out to marisol\'s house<BR>in la colonia<BR>stayed all winter<BR>got high with clifford irving.<BR><BR>anna\'s been reading swinney.<BR><BR>her:  could you write a book in a month?<BR><BR>me:  i could write a book<BR>in three days if i wanted to.<BR><BR>her:  why don\'tcha then?<BR><BR>me:  i don\'t want to.<BR><BR>if this doesn\'t work out<BR>will i want to try it again next month?<BR>i must be fucked up.'),(2101,'<p>\r\nits -3 with the wind chill right now. its going to get colder. much colder. not that the cancer can will mind. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ncancer can smells really bad. cancer can offers people the chance to smell what lungs smell like after about thirty years of smoking.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncancer can should be emptied regularly during the dry season. cancer can has notified me with fire three times regarding the level of waste within its belly. procedure regarding disposal of cancer can have subsuquently been changed, and due to these procedural changes fires have been avoided.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe smell of that burning material is putrid. However the smell of it decomposing is worse.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthere is an effect; a chemical reaction of sorts. when used cigarette butts get wet, the aroma they let off compares maybe with rotting flesh. cancer can scares the neighbourhood cats.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nrest myself assured the cancer cans absurd. he collects the filth. cancer can vomits when he tips over. a rusty spray of his cigarette vomit. hate is no doubt cancer cans motive. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen the leaves were trapped by cancer can i freed them. when cancer cans mouth swallowed maple twigs and swirly keys i instinctivly extracted them from cancer can, the fear of getting toxic sludge on the fingers constantly swirling around.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncancer can was once a proud coffee can. That is until the coffee ran out. There are perhaps pros and cons to being a can of any sort really. However being a cancer can, for a can, is like a human being sent to hell. heaven for a can is like being used by grandpa to collect screws and buttons. the lazy days of a pen holder can. For most can, being thrown out was often desired more than becoming a cancer can.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnevertheless, no one in the can world gave two shits about cancer can. he liked being who he was, and for that he became part of a sick and twisted subculture. a can subculture. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwhile cancer can lacks the social skills that other cans pocess, cancer can has never been accused of having a thankless job. like that dude who unclogs the public bathroom, cancer can resides on the bottom of that ladder we call status. cancer can is never clean. never well spoken. never invited anywhere. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncancer can literally hates the rest of the world and for this reason he is happy collecting cigarettes and letting them emit a smell similar to bile. cancer can will rust away into nothing. the only thing left; a pile of burning cigarette butts. \r\n</p>'),(2102,'<p>\r\nthe best way to deal with the possibility of embarassment is just to get it over with, out yourself, and move on. that said, here are parts of my pathetic 10k nanowrimo novel.\r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nchapter one: the day my heart fell out\r\n</p><p>\r\nit was about halfway through that year  that i had the dream where my heart was falling out. \r\n</p><p>\r\nsomehow, my chest  had been sliced open diagonally from neck to armpit, and my heart was sticking right out through the hole. it hung there, shiny and slick looking in my dream mind. it was blue and purple there in the dream, hanging out of my chest  like a toy.  i was going about my business as if nothing had happened. washing the dishes, that heart dangling from me. folding clothes, the heart attached by wires and bloody guts. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni wasnâ€™t bleeding, really, in the dream. it was as if i was a rubber doll and someone had cut apart my rubber chest  and neatly pulled the skin aside. but, god, it hurt me. it hurt me so badly in my dream that i felt the pain for days after that, in my waking life. at work i would clutch my warm chest, feeling the pain of that dream. a dream so real that the throbbing dullness, the cutting pain of the gaping heart wound stayed with me. i can still feel it now if i think about it too hard.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe fucked up thing is that at the time, this type of dream wasnâ€™t so out of the ordinary, and funnier, i didnâ€™t associate it with the events that were transpiring in my life. everything was broken but that was just how it always had been, and always would be as long as i was beholden to deb in the way that i had been for six months now.\r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen he woke up, he didnâ€™t remember most of the dream. all he knew was that he dreamed about her, yet again. he reached down to cradle his limp penis in his hand as he rolled onto his side. he stared at the door of his room, frowning.  he cupped the hand over his testicles. he sighed deeply and fell back to sleep.\r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen he was ten, he had found a centipede in the backyard. they are rare in this area and this one was huge. it was long and winding, a sickening shade of brown, it slithered and it was slick and shiny and dangerous and poisonous. he knew he must capture it, that it was rare and that he would  be revered. he didnâ€™t know this in words or in positive ways, he knew it instinctually that this insect presented a possibility for recognition and heroism. he worked hard and quickly to capture the centipede. it was so flat and wide, it slithered along, god it was magnificeint, oh he must have it. oh he must be associated with it, he thought, in his developing ten year old mind.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe grabbed a trashcan and stalked the centipede but it stayed near the edges of the walls, it fit perfectly in the ninety degree angle where the porch met the outer wall of their suburban home. the chase was on. he carefully aimed the upturned trashcan at the centipede. before it could reach the safety of the grass, he scared it out from the wall and slammed the trashcan down onto it.  it was so huge that he could hear it climbing around under the can, bonking into the sides of the can, trying to escape. he coralled it further into a tupperware container and finally into a jar. \r\n</p><p>\r\n...\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere\'s more, but it\'s all much worse than the above.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhave a nice day!\r\n</p>'),(2103,'<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2103_1.pjpeg\">\r\n<P>I do not know what it feels like not to want to sleep with someone I\'ve already slept with.\r\nThe road to those feelings interrupts my sequential talent.\r\nLong lasting fury is one feeling I still have.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nToday I want to ignore the dust. I want to ignore gravity.\r\nI want a sustained noise to come out of my bedroom.\r\nMy shirt says \"Kill Silence\". \r\n</P>'),(2104,'testing'),(2105,'<p>\r\nOnce, a very long time ago, there was a bottle. It was of average size and weight for bottles of its purpose, which in this case was being the container of a very fine French wine. Who owned the bottle, and how it came to be here is none of our concern; that in itself would make a very worthy story, but for our purposes, let us just say that it was even at the time very old indeed. \r\n<p>	While the wine itself may have been quite valuable, the bottle was discarded immediately after its contents had been consumed. Some people prefer to save their wine bottles as a memento or souvenir of a wonderful evening, but the owners of our bottle (and it is our bottle, since no one else has taken quite an interest in it as we have) saw for whatever reason to throw it out. \r\n<p>	In most cases, a used wine bottle does not serve much purpose in the affairs of ordinary people, and in fact you may be wondering why we have spent so much of our valuable time discussing it. This bottle, however, was very important. Because, dear friend, there was something of indescribable value inside, something of which the existence of the world, and, in fact, the entire universe, depends on--this bottle was filled to the very brim with Xanmth. What is Xanmth, you ask? That is a very good question. Xanmth is defined as â€œThat substance which has weight, but no sizeâ€, and also â€œthat which has depth, but not height.\" Any further description, sadly, would be pointless. One of the criteria for being Xanmth is that if you can define it logically, it must not be Xanmth. In fact, that is the only way to isolate it: you must know for certain, be absolutely sure, that you cannot be certain it is there or what it is. And then you have it! It is almost always a solid, except when it is a liquid, in which case it is almost always a liquid, when itâ€™s not a gas.\r\n<p>	This bottle, being itself very rare, and containing such a quantity of this rare substance, or none at all, or all that exists, or perhaps Diet Coke, you can never tell with Xanmth...this bottle was in an ordinary shop on an ordinary street, in, you guessed it, an ordinary town...\r\n<p>\r\nWell, maybe it wasn\'t so ordinary after all. It had green grass, and fluffy white clouds, and children playing on front lawns, and grownups <i>swearing</i> that they thought it was five already, and you know it\'s five <i>somewhere</i>. Despite all this, there was a strange electricity in the air, and many a young boy or girl saying bedtime prayers felt the need to mention eveyone by name, just in case. \r\n<p>\r\nnext week...the bottle finds a new home.\r\n'),(2106,'<p>my hip had been bothering me.  deep pain near the ovary.  spreading up the back, tingling and maddening.  felt like my leg was jammed up into the hip socket, pressure, but no amount of stretching and cracking was relieving it.  so we go (take separate cabs, all excited, both carrying sacred objects) over to the new place, all empty, echoing hardwood floors and new paint, bring our sleeping bags over, light jesus and mary candles, burn sage, drink champagne, toast our new life together, and exclaim over the beauty of the apartment.  we try to decide which of the large rooms will be our bedroom and which will be the living room.  he favors the larger front room, beautiful and open with french doors, as the living room.  i disagree but think it would be nice to sleep there tonight.  we lie down on our sleeping bags, in the candlelight, looking at each other, stroking each other.  the full moon rides the clouds outside the window.  despite the romantic setting, something feels wrong.  my energy is not mingling with his, i don\'t feel safe, i don\'t feel attractive.  my hips feel particularly wide and ungainly in my pyjamas.  i feel paralyzed.  pain wells up in my throat and suddenly i\'m talking nonsense, voice constricted: \"i don\'t think this is going to work.  i\'m not feeling love between us.  i can\'t sign the lease.\"  and on and on, i can\'t stop myself, i\'m saying awful things, things i rationally know to be untrue, but they <i>feel</i> true, and i\'m so upset and so full of conflicting surges of emotion that i can\'t even be near him any more.  i stand up, yank my sleeping bag out from under his arm, and stomp into the bedroom.  i lie down, seething.  the moonlight pouring in peaceful and blue over my raging, rigid figure on the floor.  i stare up out of the window, unable to think straight--am i moving tomorrow? if i\'m not moving in here, where am i moving to?  why am i so upset?  i notice that i feel more comfortable in this slightly smaller room, more contained, safer.  i call across to scott in the living room, \"this is definitely the bedroom,\" and hear him rustling around.  no answer.  i lie there, feeling the hardness and coolness of the floor under my back.  \"scott?\"  i call again.  \"can you come in here please?\"  he comes in, lies down, looks consternated.  i begin, in stops and starts, to explain myself, and realize that i\'m full of grief for a lost and disappointed and small part of myself and sadness and deep, deep fear that he doesn\'t love me.  i confess these fears, mumbling even more nonsensically than before: \"i\'m just scared.\"  and his eyes tear up and he says simply \"i\'m sorry if my defense mechanisms make me seem distant,\" and in one moment, my heart is open, and i\'m reaching out to him, touching his smooth neck and saying brokenly, \"but i love you just the way you are.  it just hurts so much.\" finally hearing the ring of truth in the room and there are these operatic sobs pouring out of me in rhythmic bursts, coming from way deep down...heart, solar plexus, hip.  the anger coming up like vomiting up something nasty.  convulsing.  knowing i love this human being next to me with a passionate and enduring love.  and his body feels real under my hands again, and i feel our energy meshing, and our lovemaking is phenomenal, synchronized, epic. </p>\r\n<p>when i woke up this morning, the pain in my hip was gone, body felt light, head clear, and i breathed in the delicious crisp air in deep gulps walking to work.  depression lifted.  grief that had been incubating in my womb, incubating into a big fat ugly anger baby--finally it had been born into the world in tears and love and orgasm.</p>\r\n<p>thank god.</p>'),(2107,'<P>\r\naren\'t i suposed to be learning some new programming or working on my motorcycle?  it needs to be shined more.  oil is caked on in a gew everywhere.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nafter work i went directly to bed.  i was almost asleep when i decided the light should be turned off.  after turning it off there was no more of the plaeasant dream that was fading in and out before the light decision.  sitting in the warm spot on the bed was not as comforting as before.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni made dinner. surplus tacos.  the same thing that i ate for nearly three years in college.  they cost about a dollar and a half a taco for the gourmet style with expensive cheese.  strait up with no chease, about sixty-five cents.  the black beans, the buttered flour tortilla, the optional cheese because i have a paycheck.  while cooking the black beans, add a few shakes of green tobasco for tang, and a few shakes of worchestershire sauce to make them costa rican style. \r\n</P><P>\r\nthe refrigerator has one taco cut in half and placed in a ziplock bag.  maybe after karate it will go to my stomach.  the time is six twenty.  karate is at six forty.  im getting ahead of myself.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nafter dinner i went to the garage.  a week has passed since last my bike had some work done on it.  parts are half neetly, half piled, in an area under the machine and out of the way.  boxes from my roomates moving are everywhere else. it is cold.  the light are dim.  the radio plays jazz oand gives the area some memories from elesewhere.  the old jazz radio show is my favorite.  a bolt on the neck, under the steering dampener is rusted in place.  there is a crescent wrench on the floor that will not screw wide enough.  somewhere the right size wrench sits, but not in this garage.  there is one in the attic at my mother\'s house.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nthere is a cold walk way between the garage and the house where all the snails live.  crunch crunch.\r\n</P><P>\r\nin the kitchen i put the new tea kettle on that i bought because i fell asleep and left the last one burning.  it nearly melted.  it was one of those kind that does not have a whistle.  i think that is a brilliant touch, tea kettles that have no whistle.  the new one has a whistle.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\negg pointed out, i am not tired of reading. i am tired of my own writing.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2108,'<P>what she said that night made no sense. what i\'d said, i suspect, made even less. i looked across the table at her, she was looking down into her cup, i looked around the room instead. a lot of people sitting around, looking like they\'d been there for hours, all men, all of them forgetting to take their coats off, or maybe cold...out through the windows, in the street, snow was falling, buses and cabs crawled slowly along, the flakes sticking to the windows and slowly melting their way down...\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni sat, moving my eyes but not my head, waiting for her to speak. i waited for what felt like a long time. i studied the creases in the skin covering my knuckles, the imperfections in the paint on the wall, the sparse, greasy looking facial hair on the kid behind the counter...my arms get cold and i reach behind me for my jacket. i pull it on, look around again, and realise i fit right in now, with the other men, huddled down into their coats...\r\nshe looks up and brushes away a strand of hair that had fallen in front of her eyes. she says: \r\n</P><P>\r\n\'i guess i can understand.\'</P><P>\r\n\'you can?\'</P><P>\r\n\'yeah. i hope you weren\'t expecting more...\'</P><P>\r\n\'no, no. i wasn\'t even expecting that.\'\r\n</P><P>\r\nit all ground to a halt then. all my next moves had been countered even before i could make them. it made no sense, and boy was i surprised. she guesses she can understand, when i sit here, nowhere even close to comprehending it myself. i almost started to laugh...\r\n</P><P>\r\n\'what are you smirking at?\'</P><P>\r\n\'nothing. i was just thinking.\'\r\n</P><P>\r\nshe was looking at me with the one-raised-eyebrow-\'don\'t-push-me\' look. i didn\'t know what to say to it. i just went on smirking. she picked up her cup, drank whatever was left in it, stood, and walked out. the door closed, and just then, all the other men sitting in that place looked at where she was sitting, and then at me. it was then that i realized we were all wearing the same smirk.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2109,'I\'ve been out walking around the neighborhood for awhile, and its been nice, what with this full moon and all.  The sky is unusually clear tonight, and it accentuates the moonlight, unlike the nights that are usually swallowed up in the city\'s night time halo of glare.<br><br>\r\nIts quite late, and the streets have finally grown quiet; you can even hear the breeze blowing the mist of gauzy night-time haze towards the Hudson River.  A perfect evening for a walk.<br><br>\r\nI just returned to my building, and saw my building\'s superintendant walking down the block, also, staring at the moist street\'s surface. We waved, as usual.  As we both paused to fumble with keys and enter the building, he, in his rich broken English, inquired if I could give him a second.<br><br>\r\nSure.  He proceeded to explain to me that, for some reason, he was underpaid this week, and that he has found himself completely broke, and its kind of weighing him down as tomorrow\'s his 48th birthday. He asked me if I could spare just a little cash.<br><br>\r\nI\'ve never been approached like this, from someone I know and see on a daily basis.  I didn\'t really know what to think or how to tactfully react, as after you\'ve lived in New York City for a while, you quit taking people at face value and begin keeping your empathies in check by digging for deeper, ulterior motives.<br><br>\r\nAlthough I\'m not one to usually ponder long before acting, I found myself taking an almost-uncomfortably-long pause.  I remembered that I had $15 on me, at least. But...?<br><br>\r\nI came to an interesting observation after taking a instantaneous passing glance into his eyes.  They were weary eyes, eyes that were doing quite a stoic job of covering some type of desperation, although I didn\'t know of what type. I noticed that I was handing him the cash I had on me; after all, I <i>could</i> afford to part with $15 bucks.  Maybe he might be in dire enough straits that that $15 could actually make a significant dent in his weekend, or this coming week.<br><br>\r\nPerhaps I helped him out.  Its also likely (although surely not a majority) that he just smooth-talked me out of a little pocket money.  I\'ll never really know, not that I actually care.  But, would it be a risk worth taking to not help him out when I clearly can?<br><br>\r\nHe looked at the ground and half-grunted, \"thanks. tell no one, ok?\", gripped the folded bills in his hand quite tightly, and jogged up to his place.  I think I made the right move.  I think I should give humanity the benefit of the doubt more than I do. $15 was probably a pretty small price to pay for some late-night epiphanies.<br><br> \r\n'),(2110,'<center><img SRC=\"http://members.aol.com/hitreetop/images/subway3.jpg\" BORDER=0 height=208 width=410></center>\r\n\r\n<p>In the last couple of months I have also been exploring the many cracks and crevices of San Francisco. I have met new friends and increasingly find it a wonderful place to live. I think what draws me the most to this city is the music. I can find good live music any night of the week. Most of it is within walking or muni distance, except for the Oaktown shows. Im thinking of going to see Phil Lesh and Friends for NYE. But, String Cheese and Galactic w/Karl Denson are also in the que. Sweet!\r\n\r\n<p>Unfortunately, I am still on the job hunt. I go to job interviews and they tell me under qualified.., for administrative work! I find this really funny because I have a BS in management and almost 10 years administrative experience. I guess there are people out there with masters degrees applying for the same crap jobs that I am applying for. I am giving myself one more\r\nmonth to find a job here. If I don\'t have a job by NYE I am putting notice on my abode and moving to another city with a better job market. I don\'t\r\nwant to do that, but I have no choice.\r\n\r\n<p>I have not posted here in a while. Actually, I swore I never would again. I think that was a rash statement. I apologize for any annoyance that I caused. I found that by posting on this site I was forced to improve my writing skills and improve or tap into creative thought. I appreciate any criticism whether good or bad because it causes me to step back and look\r\nin. I do not know any of you. Well, I did get to have lunch with a super cool Kat in Santa Cruz. Mmmmm, Boca burger! All of you seem like nice people. Of course everyone has their moments, especially me.\r\n\r\n<p>With your permission, I would like to submit an occasional post here.\r\nFor some reason, I am addicted.\r\n\r\n<p>~peace\r\n<br>t'),(2111,'<a href=\"http://slashdot.org/comments.pl?sid=24379&cid=2642775\">comment</a>, attached to <a href=\"http://slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=01/12/02/0150222&mode=thread\">story</a>.  <br><br><br>I fight that despair.  I don\'t always feel like that, in fact recently, I haven\'t felt that way very much at all.  But other days, I feel that darkness building behind my eyes, the corruption that I barely restrain from leaking out of me.  Those days, I wonder if other people can see the things lurking, burning behind my pale blue eyes.  Those days, like in Fight Club, I have the urge to destroy things, to lay waste to order and purity and goodness.  But instead, I think that I\'m winning.  Ever day the flowers get a little more sunlight, a little more water in the garden that is my cerebellum.  I build.  I create.  Tomorrow I will be a better person than I was yesterday, and the tuesday me is just a memory.  2000 and 2001 have been the winter of my discontent, but 2002 will be the spring and raging summer of my renewal. <br><br><br>[As a coda, I want to say thank you to all the swinneyites, from austin who I have met to all the rest whom I have not.  Sometimes I post silly things, but when I\'ve needed a place to talk or a place to listen, having swinney-dot-org has been really, really important to me.  Thank you.]'),(2112,'<P>after the five-day weekend, our work week starts.  for the next four weekends, we\'ll babysit paige on saturday, shoot at whitledge hall in tampa on saturday night and all day sunday.  we think we\'re really hip, part of the new vanguard of human being that refuses to work, just play.  i guess if this ever catches on, there\'ll be trouble, but it suits us for now.  for us, playing is the work.  hardest work i\'ve ever done.</P><P>harder than:<BR><BR>working at the rock mine<BR>the nursery<BR>the bank<BR>the funeral home<BR><BR>teaching gym<BR>substituting<BR>laying sod<BR>moving furniture<BR>cooking<BR>feeding lab mice<BR>caging chickens<BR><BR>being a janitor<BR>a tech consultant<BR>a hardware store clerk<BR>a newspaper freelancer<BR>a dishwasher<BR>a fraud<BR><BR>collecting disability<BR>drinking too much<BR>stealing<BR>being homeless<BR>pretending to be other people<BR>dealing<BR>selling myself<BR>going to jail<BR><BR>ripping off tourists<BR>being a junkie<BR><BR>getting clean<BR><BR>getting dirty again<BR><BR>going to school<BR>interning for a major label producer<BR>stalking a rock star<BR><BR>roofing<BR>laying tile<BR>building trusses<BR>and pumping out shithouses</P><P>paige is in the backseat with eddie, giving him the fisheye.  first time they\'ve met.  warms up to him soon, though, showing him one of her japanese-looking clogs.</P><P>paige:  i wear size twelve and a half, so that means I\'M twelve and a half.</P><P>me:  no, you\'re five and a half.  i wear size nine and a half shoes, but i\'m thirty-one and a half.  my waist size IS thirty-one and a half, though.</P><P>anna:  yeah.  tell that baby some more stories.</P><P>i want to play the george harrison mix tape i made, but the women veto me.</P><P>\"i was up all night putting this together.\"</P><P>anna:  then play it on your teeth.  i need my soul music, mattie.</P><P>she puts the radio on 101.5.  loud.  and sings along.  loud.  knows all the lyrics to all the songs.  very distracting.  i try to turn it down a little, thinking of paige\'s ears.  anna asks her if it\'s too loud.  no.  she\'s loving it, riding around with the big people.  the physically larger people, that is.  not much older than her emotionally, it seems, \'cause eddie gets bored after five minutes and decides he needs to stage a tickle attack on me.  scares me.  stomp on the gas and nearly rear-end somebody.  i pull over and turn off the radio.</P><P>\"you and dusty springfield here are gonna find yourselves walkin\' home if you don\'t watch it.\"</P><P>him:  jesus.  touchy, touchy.</P><P>we go to the drugstore.  paige wants me to buy her this toy gorilla that plays bongos and sings \"who let the dogs out?\"  she settles for a bag of dessert mints.  she\'s really into candy.  on the way to the park she and eddie sing \"who let the cats out?  meow, meow\", \"who let the cows out?  moo, moo\", etc.  she gets tired of that and tells us about nina in her class who\'s having a birthday party coming up soon but is only inviting girls who have lost a tooth.  paige has three that are loose but is worried that none will be out in time.</P><P>in the park, anna bends over to tie paige\'s shoe.  i nudge eddie.</P><P>\"look at that.  you put your thing inside her.  think about that.  you\'re so nasty, putting it into that thing.  how can you do that?\"</P><P>we start giggling.  she turns around.  we pull straight faces.  she looks away.  eddie sings under his breath:  \"moon river, wider than a mile.\"  we start giggling again.</P><P>hide and seek.  on the swings.  freeze tag.  red light, green light.  up in this thing they have that looks like a boat, paige and i look across the street at the back of florida boys barbecue.  terry is over there taking out the trash.  \"hey, garbage man,\" i yell.  he just looks at us.  paige says, \"do it again.\"  i yell it out, she yells it.  he gives us the finger.  \"ooh!\" she says, eyes getting big, \"naughty man.\"</P><P>we play basketball, changing who\'s on which team every five minutes.  tired, i sit in a swing.  anna sits in my lap.  i hug her.  eddie looks at what used to be the dairy queen across 50.  after they lost the franchise, it was the \"dairy stop\" for awhile, selling the leftover dairy queen products, then it closed down.</P><P>eddie:  too bad that\'s gone.  i sure could use a dilly bar.</P><P>paige:  i want a dilly bar.</P><P>i tell her no, i\'m not driving all the way to spring hill to the closest dairy queen just for that, we can get some ice cream at mcdonald\'s.  she pitches a little fit for a second, some tears.  we ignore her, talk about how it gets dark so early.  she calms down.  i ask her if she wants to go to mcdonald\'s.  she says she wants to play some more.  ok, i tell her, fifteen more minutes.  she chases eddie around.</P><P>sitting there in love, everything feels perfect, but it feels like a trap, like there\'s something depraved i\'m supposed to be doing.  things left undone.</P>'),(2115,'<P>\r\nso i landed a role in the local theatre\'s production of steel magnolias.Â  i would proudly say that this is the first \"non-commerical\" play i\'ve joined, but if the play\'s set in one place with six characters, all women, who speak of \"deep thoughts\" BUT the place is a beauty salon, the women say as many funny one-liners and with a sweet southern accent on top of that...\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nbut if you\'re in the l.a. area around january and february, you should come see it.Â  and not because i\'m in it - the director\'s a crazy whip-cracking ball of fuzzy warmth and the actors are good.Â  except the small problem about clairee\'s brooklyn-ness.Â  if she achieves southern grace, there certainly is a god.\r\n</P>'),(2113,'<p>Did you ever feel so secure in a situation that you forgot to look around and make sure everything was okay?  Have you ever been so complacent with the way things are going that you kindof just keep forging on obliviously?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That\'s been my mode for the last year or so.  If my watch isn\'t off, I was involved in a very close, mainly live-in relationship with a really nice girl for about 3 years.  From the beginning, we clicked, soul-wise, in a way I have yet to see duplicated.  From that first kiss, we pretty much knew we were made for one another.  Bliss ensued.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then, after a while, the haze of love dissipated just enough that we started to see that the outside world mattered just a little bit, and that we had better get our careers and life plans going.  We couldn\'t just spend the rest of our lives gazing into eachother\'s eyes, like the proverbial \'limpid pools\' spoken of in cheezy love poems, forever, right?  I only wish we could have.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>started to worry about money, status, cars, stock options..  Or, rather, I started to worry about that stuff.  She was a dyed-in-the-wool hippie, born and raised in the Pacific Northwest, so such things did not concern her.  She, in fact, thought my dot-com-vintage worries quite hilarious.  She would say, \"Who the hell cares if you live in some hip apartment, who the hell cares if you have a car that runs..  What matters other than love?\".</P>\r\n\r\n<p> I would always blurt out something stale and rigid like \"Well, I just feel like I want to be, uh, _cool_, or something...\".  Little did I know I was ignoring that which would cure all my ails now, would that I had a bit of it..  Love!  We had it in spades, we were made for eachother, and yet I was so blind, I never appreciated things the way I wish I would have.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She realized, over time, that I was nigh-on-insane, and that the bay area had rotted my once optimistic mind into something more paranoid, more fatalistic.  All of that time stuck in my car really didn\'t do me one lick of good.  My art went undone, my spirit went unquenched, as I searched for that next paycheck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What a freaking load of bollocks.  She went back to the northwest to finish college, and the idea was that we were going to stay together, even as we were apart, and that she\'d join me back here once she got done with her studies.  We drifted, ever so slowly, apart, over the course of a couple of short months.  She realized her place was up there amongst those trees, and that a lot of my troubles just weren\'t getting better.  She still loved me to death, but couldn\'t handle seeing me become a slave to money and status any longer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So she broke up with me, and what should happen, but all of a sudden, I\'m broken out of my complacency!  I see the truth of the matter!  I finally grasp just what it is I want and what I need to do to get it- and my depression lifts, as though it was a veil suspended before my eyes.  All of a sudden, I know what to do!  I know how to let go of all of this twaddle and just get on with being with the love of my life!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But it was too late.  I spent the next week writing voluminous tomes to her over email, trying to win her heart back with words and epiphanies.  She waffled back and forth a bit, always making it clear how much I meant to her, but that, at present, she couldn\'t see us continuing as a unit.  It was a week after the break that she informed me she\'d found someone new.  She\'d gotten together with him the day after she broke up with me.  I thought that was a bit much.  Then again, she has not been out of a long-term relationship for almost a decade..  She can\'t compute the thought of being alone.  Turns out I\'m not having the easiest time with it, either.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ironically enough, the guy was a displaced dot-commer, moving to Oregon out of desparation and a jones for cheap rent- just the kind of people Oregonians generally detest!  So I have to chuckle at that.  For all I know, he\'s a better man than I.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At present, I feel like I\'ve lost a soulmate.  Like my future has been ripped away and replaced with a swath of uncertainty.  I\'m facing the dating scene in the city- not fun.  I just can\'t believe that I let the machinations of the money machine around here do this to me.  I was not myself, for the better part of a year and a half.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In real life, I\'m a musician, a writer, a philosopher, a lover.  I never wanted to be a commuter, a corporate slave.  I had my individuality stamped out of me by the overwhelming homogenization that overtook the bay area during the boom of the dot-com times.  I valued money and security over expression..  Now I\'m paying the price.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At least I still have a job, though I\'d much rather have my cute Portland girl and no money any day of the week.  I hope that some new freedom comes from this dip in the economy.  When there\'s too much money around, it gets frighteningly easy to occupy yourself with inconsequential trash.  When you have no money, you often have to create your own fun..  Entertain yourself.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I\'d love to see us all express ourselves a little more.  They say good things often rise out of recessions.  Well, I\'m ready.  If we\'re not all going to be rich, we might as well get our passions back, eh?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s about time..</p>\r\n'),(2114,'<p>Last night Mrs. Skylark and I were on the bus and suddenly at the back this guy started badmouthing Jewish people. I\'m usually apt to grin and bear this kind of shit. It\'s the bus and you hear things, you learn to tune things out. \r\n</p><p>\r\nBut this guy just went on and on, getting progressively louder and more offensive until I couldn\'t take it anymore. I rose out of my seat, walked to the back of the bus and leaned down to him. \"Sir, I don\'t appreciate what you\'re saying about Jewish people.\" In a flash he lurched out of his seat, grabbed me by the neck and pushed me into the row of seats behind me. \r\n<p></p>\r\nHe didn\'t have my hard by the neck so instantly me hands went to his and pulled them down. I regained my balance and said, \"Look I don\'t want to fight you, just keep it down.\" And he shoved me again. There was some comotion, I heard my wife screaming for the bus driver and next thing I knew the big bus driver, as big as a bar bouncer, pulled the guy off me. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI went back to my seat while the guy screamed at me: \"Fuck you Jew! Fuck you!\" I didn\'t turn around again. My hands were shaking and sweating. \"Fuck you Jew! Elvis Costello! You can\'t even get your own look. Why don\'t you get your own look!\" I have dark-rimmed glasses and an old-fashioned hat. Mrs. Skylark and I had dressed up for dinner, so I was also wearing a skinny red 50s style tie. \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe bus driver thew him off the bus and explained to us that we should never take matters into our own hands, and instead tell him to take care of it. I wish I knew that was a possibility. I didn\'t even think about it really, just went back there and said something without thought to recourse. I\'m such a naive bastard. I thought they were just words and forgot they had they the potential to inspire a VIOLENT reaction. \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was a horrible experience. The man\'s face was twisted into a knot of ignorant hate. He\'s the kind of guy that was guily of complicity in WWII Germany...hell, he was the fucking Gestapo. It took a long time for Mrs. Skylark and I to calm down and I drank two martinis and a beer with dinner and, though we had a good time, I still didn\'t feel very at-ease. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI know you can\'t expect the world to be all postage stamps and Raisnettes, but why do people have to be that way? Didn\'t we learn anything from the Holocaust? Isn\'t hate too old-fashioned to exist? I\'m too liberal, to sheltered, to goddamn foolish-in-the world. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSo, tonight I can\'t sleep. I woke up at 3:30am with an upset stomach and now I\'m typing this. \r\n</p><p>\r\nUgh. Please somebody, tell me it will be all right. \r\n</p>'),(2116,'	They spoke because they could do nothing else.\r\n\r\n<p>	\"Yeah.  Nigel was fuckingâ€¦ mad the other day,\" murmured Fred.  \"Like, when Jessica was laying there on the couchâ€¦\"\r\n<p>	\"Yeah,\" nodded James, while sitting in semi-sleep-mode.\r\n	\"He came with this big beer can, she turns and asks him â€˜whyâ€™s that beer can so big.â€™\"\r\n<p>	\"Yah, hehâ€¦.\"\r\n<p>	\"He, like, points at her with this perverted face and goes â€˜Ha! Look a herâ€™. Fucking mad.  Like, everything the cunt says, itâ€™s eighty percent about sex.\"\r\n<p>	\"Remember he was standing behind her earlier doing that blowjob face.\"\r\n<p>	\"How come she doesnâ€™t get scared every time she sees him.\"\r\n<p>	\"Fuck, I almost fell down laughing, after the beer can thingâ€¦ I almost fell down and farted, I wanted to laugh so hardâ€¦\"\r\n<p>	\"Funny.\"\r\n<p>	\"Yeah.\"\r\n	\r\n<p>	They were sitting in a poorly lit bedroom at noon.  James, a victim of his own reckless behavior, was sitting numb in his chair.  There was no reason to bring menace of bright, white light from the outside into the room.  It would pierce them like needles.  In factâ€¦\r\n<p>	\"Close the window! Agghhhâ€¦. Needles in my eyesâ€¦ Close it you stupid fuck!\"\r\n<p.	Warm, dark and quiet was OK.\r\n<p>	Fred was on his back exhausted on an unmade bed, staring at the pattern of the ceiling.  A week of turbulence and excess was over.  They were determined never to go back to those times.  For a while.\r\n\r\n<p>	Fred: \"Not drinking this week.  Not until Friday.  My digestive system is in fucking civil war right nowâ€¦\"\r\n<p>	James: \"Stop lying to your fucking self.\"\r\n\r\n<p>	This conversation went on for a while.  Same answers.  Same questions.  With a little bit of blaming, a bit of violence, a bit of regret:\r\n<p>	\"Yeah man, just imagine Seth and Nigel walking into the house with two fucking cases of beer tonight,\" said James.\r\n<p>	\"God damn, that would be it, that would be completely giving up to the drink.  Kiss goodbye to a week of school.  Kiss goodbye to a semester of work,\" said Fred in an awkward sarcastic lament.\r\n<p>	\"Arenâ€™t you getting thirsty.\"\r\n<p>	\"Shut up.\"\r\n<p>	\"Yeah, like if you have a few beers tonight you wonâ€™t get up in the morning.\"\r\n<p>	\"Never.  I want to sleep for a month.  And I canâ€™t because my fucking nerves are shocked to hell by this fucking hangoverâ€¦ agghhhâ€¦\"\r\n\r\n<p>	Exit Jamesâ€™ room. The hallway to the bathroom is empty.  But turn right, and scoot into the living room to find a cemetery of cans and trash.  The place does not smell one third of what the kitchen smells like with the trash can uncovered.  This was the stage of a rough, uninspired, destructive, and long weekend.  Not turkey this Thanksgiving, but beer and drink and sandwiches and loud music.\r\n<p>	Exit the apartment.  Sidewalks, streets covered in fallen leaves and piles of newspapers that the neighbors were too lazy to pick up.  There was no one outside.  Not a soul.\r\n<p>The town was desolate during the break from college life, and thus a lonely weekend of self-destruction seemed imminent for the four youths, too poor at the time to embark on a \"real vacation.\"  For four days, drinking was constantâ€¦  They swam like ducks in a pond of golden, foamy, sparkling water.   And at the end of the weekend, the agony and the pain of their damaged and weakened bodies took them by surprise.\r\n<p>\r\nIt was then 9 PM.  Seth was done with work and probably headed towards home.  Lights were on in the house, both roomies sat in the couch, staring at the set , planning how to get around lab work and term papers that week.  The lights suddenly dimmed and they heard thunder.  James looked outside the house, scared.  He saw nothing.  Fred could see the fear in his eyes but did not understand it.\r\n<p>\"You look like youâ€™ve seen a ghost,\" he said.\r\n<p>The door was smashed open, as if a megaton bomb had exploded outside, and then, trotting in defiance, came the horsemen of the apocalypse.  Holding a Babel tower of light beer and showing a devilish grin, Seth pranced into the room.  Nigel followed, jumping into the couch and putting on loud music, and then jumping around the living room.  Fred laughed in amazement as Seth proceeded to put the beer in the freezer.  James stood, screamed and ran in despair to his room.  He jumped in and locked the door.  Violently he grabbed the phone and called Chang, the landlord.  Chang, unknowing of what was going on, picked up the phone and graciously greeted James.\r\n<p>\"Helloooâ€¦.\" said Chang.\r\n<p>\"Chang!!!!!  Thank God!  I need to tell you something!  Its about Seth and Fred!\" screamed James in despair.\r\n<p>\"Hoooâ€¦nooo chill down boy speak quiiiietly please.\"\r\n<p>\"Godâ€¦ How can I say this?!?  Remember that explosion last weekâ€¦ welllâ€¦ we lied!!!! That was us!!!  And remember what happened about the trouble with all the beer cans in the balcony, and we said it was the neighbors?!?!!?!?  That wasnâ€™t true either!!!!  We did it!  And Seth just came in with 48 cans of beer!!!!\"\r\n<p>Chang stood silent.\r\n<p>\"When we signed our lease, oh Godâ€¦. We were DRUNK!!!\"\r\n<p>James almost sobbed after that last sentence.\r\n<p>The cracking noise of three beer cans opening in unison brought a chill into Jamesâ€™ spine.\r\n<p>\"Oh, God!  Itâ€™s started again!!!! Iâ€™m sorry Chang!!! Forget us, just forget usâ€¦.\"\r\nJames hanged up.\r\n<p>\r\n	Chang hanged up the phone and looked at his wife.  He spoke.\r\n<p>	\"Must do something!  Must make difference in someoneâ€™s life!  I will.\"\r\n<p>	Chang exited his living room and into a huge walk-in closet.  There, he grabbed a black suit, which he put on, and later, from behind the clothes, produced a small chest full of ninja equipment.\r\n<p>	Dressed fully in black and armed to the teeth, the brave Chang ran swiftly and silently towards his dark brown Lexus.  He began the drive to the battlefield.\r\n<p>	He pulled over into the apartment complexâ€™s parking lot.  Chang noticed the light in Jamesâ€™ window, and heard the loud noise coming from the apartment.  Inside, there was hell going on.\r\n<p>	Fred sat in the couch chugging beer after beer, while Seth lighted his farts and told dirty jokes.  Nigel continued talking about sex.  James hopelessly drank a 16-ounce can.  In one of his finest moments in that drunken carnival, Seth dropped his empty beer can and flew into the air, landing headfirst into the couch and tipping it to its side with great force.\r\n<p>	Chang decided to act.\r\n<p>	He rapidly climbed the apartment wall with special ninja claws, getting to the window as fast as he could.  The youths, drunk and dangerously armed with light beer, looked demonic.  Chang whipped out of his bag a handful of ninja stars.   \r\n<p>\r\nHe broke the window with his left arm, and began to shoot them.  One by one, the beer cans were shot down.  Seth and Fred ran for cover behind a one of the couches.  Nigel, completely out of his senses and apparently drunk as hell, stared at Chang, not believing what he was seeing.  James felt the presence of the Messiah and got to his knees.  <p>Chang then produced tranquilizer darts.  Fred, Nigel, and Seth stared at their ninja foe.  Four shots marked the end of another night of endless drinking.  Armageddon had concluded.  Good triumphed over evil.  The apocalyptic horsemen lay asleep in the living room.  Quiet reigned over Changâ€™s kingdom.\r\n<p>\r\n	Chang jumped into the apartment and wiped his ninja suit.  He walked around the sleeping, drunk bodies of the tenants and went into the kitchen.  Chang took the spoils of war -stored deliciously cold in the freezer- downstairs, into his brown Lexus.  He sped fiercely through the road, knowing justice had been done and vice had been conquered.\r\n<p>\r\n	The day was saved.\r\n'),(2117,''),(2118,'<p>As I was drifting off to sleep last night I mid-dream envisioned a tank top/short-shorts set that I would like to have. It was a lilac-colored, terry cloth number that had bold white embroidered lettering across the breast, as on a sports letter jacket: \"DIRTY\". and then on the sporty short-shorts, a big white pocket stiched on the crotch-mound with a puffy green terry cloth wad of cash spilling out of it.</p>\r\n<p>And then I dreamed that austin was the bad guy in a (<i>robocop</i>esque) warehouse rumble between this gay guy and this arnold schwarzeneggar (sp?) type (austin) who had challenged him to a fight till death.  He tried to blast-shoot me with one of those military-issue over-the-shoulder minibomb throwers(I was the gay guy\'s girlfriend), and I rolled on the floor underneath the enormous fireball and ran out of the warehouse and up the side to watch the fight from above. I was trying to concentrate on the fight but I kept looking around the warehouse and thinking, \"wow this is a great space...the things I could use it for, hrm.\" Evenutally austin killed my gay boyfriend and I ran from the building.</p>\r\n<p>I have had violent or unpleasant dreams quite often lately. If we looked them up in the dream book I would probably discover that my sub-conscious thinks I feel out of control of my life. But this one didn\'t feel violent, it felt cinematic. </p>\r\n<p>  Last night I saw the new Coen, <i>The Man Who Wasn\'t There</i>, and it has wrapped itself tightly around my imagination. There are movies such as \"waking life\" which try to render the sensation of a dream directly, and fail. <i>TMWT</i> stays so faithful to its own artificiality that it conjures a dream-state without such literal reference. In this world, ordinary television and Life magazine are more mystical than trippy special effects.</p>\r\n<p>My two favorite words today are \"pinched\" and \"hanker\". \"Pinched\" as in \"your wife got pinched this morning\" as in \"your wife was arrested for embezzlement.\" When I saw <i>Miller\'s Crossing</i> for the first time, I walked around for days thinking, \"what\'s the rumpus?\" it just makes me feel snappy.</p>\r\n<p>\"Hanker\" has deep visceral resonance with my insides and my teeth and my sense of color and my perception of beauty. I atttribute this to a cheese cartoon commercial that used to come on during afternoon cartoons when I was a kid. It was a tall skinny cowboy who had nothing but an enormous cheesehead and two skinny legs for a body, clad in cowboy boots and a cowboy hat. He did a song and dance around the saloon, the chorus of the song being \"I hanker for a hunka cheese.\"</p>\r\n<p>Film noir is a world of hankering, men with dirty money, women with dirty pasts, lovers with dirty mouths and children with dirty intentions. A hankering that pulses through the thick shadows, surrounds your heart, enters your bloodstream and blurs your vision. And after you can\'t see, you might find that you like being blind. </p>\r\n<p> I wake from my bed by the freeway and look out the window. Some mornings I like at the sky and think of Hokusai\'s \"36 views of mt fuji\", remembering Micah\'s description of Hokusai\'s life: \"He would wake up and do nothing but create.\" I hanker for nothing else today.</p>'),(2119,'<p>Are you ready for the end of the year?\r\n</p><p>\r\nI think yesterday was my birthday, but I know it wasn\'t. I know my birthday was 9 months ago. So maybe yesterday was when I went to Austin. No, that\'s not it, either, that was also 9 months ago. Ok, let me try again to get it right. Yesterday my dad got married? Nope, six months past. Was it that my brother graduated from grad school? Getting warmer: that was 5 months ago. Oh! I got it! I moved into my apartment yesterday! Phew, it sure was humid as we moved all that furniture up sometimes two and sometimes three flights of stairs. No Jill, that was August. Get with it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThis has been the quickest year on record. Again, lots of moving around, lots of changing, lots of ideas and consequent failures. All things dynamic came to a grinding halt on 9/11, and only now is life slowly beginning to churn again. What about next year? Next month begins a new year. How odd it is that time is divided into groups with definite endpoints, such as months and years. How strange that at the end of December, the following day marks a new year, a cliched new beginning. What makes Tuesday so exponentially different from Monday? Why is midnight labeled 12:00 AM when the sequence of hours leading to midnight is labeled as \"PM\"? How can adolescents too young to handle alcohol at 20 years and 364 days old suddenly be so legally mature one day later?\r\n</p><p>\r\nI\'m welcoming the sensation and gravity of getting older. I\'m apprehensive for the calendar year that lies ahead of me because I\'m anticipating changes with which I\'ve never had to deal before. My mother may be engaged or married this year; or my brother, or even me. I\'m leaving Albany finally, with a careful and unimpulsive set of plans, and I\'m vowing to do nothing by the seat of my pants from here on out. Is this is New Year\'s Resolution? Not really. Just wishful thinking.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMy hair is long now and I feel like someone else. I\'ve become the girl who tucks her hair behind her ear to keep it out of her eyes. I\'ve become the girl who will scream bloody murder if she can\'t blowdry her hair each morning. How did this happen?\r\n</p><p>\r\nOne year ago...\r\n</p><p>\r\nOne year ago, I was taking finals for my first real semester of college. I was working for a health insurance company during the evenings, and I was living with three friends of mine in a large messy apartment. I had just learned to drive standard. I was planning a trip to Colorado with my boyfriend. My hair was short, my temper shorter. I moved 4 times last year. Drove two cars. One car accident. Two semesters of college. 3.64 GPA. Average of $240 in my checking account from month to month. $300 for rent, $150 for car insurance, $50 for student loans: every month of last year. My income was about $600 a month. I was getting by on the skin of my teeth, and then everything got worse.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI was fired. I moved out of my apartment and back in with mom, long-ass commute to school. My car insurance company dropped my policy, owed hundreds to DMV. Boyfriend left me, motivation left me, hope left me. That was how I ended my year. I had big plans for this year. I did big things this year, and look back on this year as the ultimate in experiences. I\'m not living like a pauper. I\'m happy, and more importantly, I have my motivation back. So maybe it... wasn\'t so bad after all.\r\n</p>'),(2120,'<p>\r\ntoday is my self appointed day of anti-slack. hence, i am running many an errand. some of these errands have to do with getting my application ready for <a href=\"http://www.reed.edu\">reed college</a>. i should probably stop mentioning how i\'m applying to reed, because if they reject me, everyone will know that too. this is a side effect of honesty: everyone knows the good stuff and the bad stuff.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni\'ve been digging through my file folders from the past few years, digging up high school transcripst and SAT scores (weak - 1200). in a folder today, i found a great thing. it\'s a printout of a \"career search\" test i took my junior year of high school. i remember, very clearly, getting great joy out of answering each of the questions for this test on a crappy old 286 in the school library. i find the results to be totally accurate, even today, and i remember telling that to my guidance counser. (her name was mrs shirley lips, and she had a round face and large glasses. short curly red hair framed her face. she told me i was a revolutionary, and laughed afterward.) she said \"you know what that means? it means you know yourself very well.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere are the results, reproduced to look as much like this old dot matrix printout as possible:\r\n</p>\r\n<pre>\r\nSelf-Directed Search  (SDS)\r\n\r\nScale scores entered:\r\n1. Realistic          5\r\n2. Investigative    5\r\n3. Artistic         12\r\n4. Social         6\r\n5. Enterprising         7\r\n6. Conventional        1\r\n\r\nGIS Occupations from More Detailed Occupations\r\nPHOTOJOURNALIST\r\nWRITER, SCREEN\r\nDESIGNER, COMMERCIAL\r\nDESIGNER, PACKAGE\r\nARRANGER (MUSIC)\r\nPUPPETEER\r\nDANCER\r\nDESIGNER, CLOTH\r\nMAKE-UP ARTIST\r\nSAFETY-CLOTHING-AND-EQUIPMENT DEVELOPER\r\nSCULPTOR\r\nWRITER, EDITORIAL\r\nWRITER, PROSE, FICTION AND NONFICTION\r\nCAMERA OPERATOR, FIRST (DIRECTOR OF PHOTOGRAPHY)\r\nCAMERA OPERATOR (MOTION PICTURE, TV BROADCASTING)\r\nCARTOONIST\r\nCHORAL DIRECTOR\r\nCHOREOGRAPHER\r\nCONDUCTOR, ORCHESTRA\r\nCREATIVE DIRECTOR\r\nDESIGNER, DISPLAY (OUTSIDE)\r\nDESIGNER, GRAPHIC \r\nDESIGNER, INDUSTRIAL\r\nDESIGNER, INTERIOR\r\nDESIGNER, SET (AMUSEMENT AND RECREATION)\r\nARCHIVIST\r\nART DIRECTOR (MOTION PICTURE, TV BROADCASTING)\r\nEDITOR, NEWS (PRINTING AND PUBLISHING)\r\nEDITOR, NEWSPAPER\r\nILLUSTRATOR\r\nACTOR\r\nMUSIC DIRECTOR (MOTION PICTURE & RADIO, TV)\r\nPHOTOGRAPHER, MOTION PICTURE\r\nPHOTOGRAPHER, STILL\r\nSET DECORATOR\r\nSINGER\r\nSTAGE DECORATOR\r\n</pre>\r\n<p>\r\ni have gotten some great ideas from this list! i should definitely become a puppeteer!\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2121,'<p>What just happened here?</p>\r\n<p>It seems that, all of a sudden, the only thing a nation has to do in order to fuck shit up abroad is to call another nation a bunch of terrorists and then publically decalre a <b>War On Terrorism[tm]</b>.  Oh, being on speaking terms with the USofA would be a good thing, too.</p>\r\n<p>Calling people names and then getting other people on your side to help beat on them?  What is this, grade three?</p>\r\n<p>Oh, and respect to the major media outlets for successfully making everyone forget that it was never discovered who was behind the 911 attacks but let that not stop us from going and fucking up some guys with cloth on their heads anyway.  What the hell is going on?  Maybe I don\'t know enough about politics or something but, the supid to smart ratio on this planet seems to keep leaning in favour of idiocy.</p>\r\n<p>It looks like Israel jumped on the WOT bandwagon today by declaring a <b>War On Terrorism[tm]</b> Arafat\'s supporters after comparing them to Osama crew.</p>\r\n<p>Okay, great.  Why can\'t we just go back to a time when people <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0086946\" target=\"new\">fought with dancing</a>?  I mean, I\'ll take a good Break-Off over a Holy War any day.</p>\r\n<p>But I guess I should be happy that at least it\'s not happening in my backyard and that someone, somewhere, is protecting my freedom to scholastically and financially take it in the rear.</p>'),(2122,'When she smiles, it makes me happy (really, it makes me happy when she\'s happy and smiling. really. most of the time even the smirk will do. even the smirk hiding the smile, watching it, it trying to break out. with her head in her hand, looking at you and)<br><br>\r\nhappy<br><br>\r\nwhen she smiles (when she smiles sometimes i feel as though i have just made love to the stars for hours and hours).<br><br>\r\nhappy! and really, i like being happy.<br><br>\r\ni like it when she smiles.'),(2123,'<p>red light means stop. </p>\r\n<p>i was sitting semi-comfortably in my piece of shit car with my sunglasses on \r\nlooking over my shoulder at the sharp dressed gentleman in the grey crown \r\nvictoria with leather seats. he was a pudgy sort of fellow with a flashy gold \r\nwatch, rings and neatly combed hair. his slightly tinted windows refused to let \r\nme see much else about him other than his gestures. this man was troubled, all \r\nthe tell-tale signs were there. he rubbed his brow with his left hand, massaging \r\nthe area above his left eye. he looked nervously and cautiously at the light as \r\nwell we all did waiting for it to allow us to turn left. it crossed my mind that \r\nperhaps he was just anxious, he had a headache, late for a meeting with client \r\nor coworker or hungry and waiting to get to his destination but i don\'t think \r\nthat was the case. he moved his hand down slightly to his mouth, he began to \r\nslowly tap his top lip with his index finger then he began to lightly bite his \r\nfinger lengthwise, he looked over to me for whatever reason... perhaps he felt \r\nme spying on him. i couldn\'t help but to look. his eyes told me it was nothing \r\nas simple as lunches, red lights or headaches. this man was deep in thought, i \r\nwanted so badly to roll down my window and tap on his and ask him what could be \r\ntroubling such an obviously wealthy individual like himself. <b><i>i just wanted \r\nso badly to know</i></b>. </p>\r\n<p>class warfare has always been part of my makeup, it stems from a deep \r\njealousy i have for things i don\'t have, things i won\'t have, etc. i\'m big \r\nenough to admit such things and i know full well it makes me extremely \r\nunattractive to others (but i always hope honesty can compensate for such \r\nugliness provided i keep all things in check). this gentleman, at this stoplight \r\nat this point in my life showed me that its all <b><u>meaningless</u></b>. it\'s \r\nall very simple. he was troubled, i am troubled... the only difference is the \r\ncost of the clothes. </p>\r\n<p>i stay pretty medicated these days, i really don\'t leave the house until i\'m \r\nwell dosed. i could blame the year i\'ve had, the decisions i\'ve made, or the \r\neffort (or lack thereof) that i put into my daily routine but in the end its all \r\nirrelevant... i fell off the wagon so to speak because deep down i guess i \r\nwanted to. i\'m actually quite good at recognizing myself as my own public enemy \r\n#1. the more scripts i seem to ingest the more self-absorbed i seem to get, this \r\nis perhaps the worst side effect of abusing.  i\'m don\'t think myself to be \r\na bad person, i think it\'s just time for me to wake the fuck up.'),(2124,'<P>SAMO IS A GANGSTER NOW.  RIDE AROUND IN A PHAT-ROLLING CADDY WITH GEORGE HARRISON AND MACKENZIE KING AND YOUR GREEDY GREEDY GRANDMAMMY.  HOT EUCHRE GAME WE PLAY AT THE STOPLIGHTS.  GRANNY SAYS HI AND STOP PLAYING WITH YOURSELF.</P><P>SAMO GOTS THE GOOD PAINT IN HEAVEN.  DANCE THE SAMBA ALL DAY LONG.  TELL JULIAN SCHNABEL THE BITCH OWE ME NOW.</P><P>SAMO TASTE YOU IN YOUR LITTLE ICBS AND IRCS AND ICBMS.  YOU TASTE LIKE CHICKEN.  GEORGE BUSH IS A GANGSTER NOW.  PERE ET FILS.  WE MAKE HIM RIDE IN THE TRUNK.</P><P>SAMO SAY DON\'T GO TO HELL ON A HOT WEDNESDAY.  OR CHRISTMASTIME.  ALL THEY GOT ON THE STEREO IS ANDY WILLIAMS.  YOU LITTLE DROOGIES BE GOOD.  I ART YOU LATER.  PEACE.</P>'),(2125,'<p>I think I feel worse than I look at the moment.  Not shaving for now.  We\'ll see how that goes.</p>\r\n<p>I was going to write about my Friday night with the film geeks but I chose not to.  Instead I want to talk about self-imposed isolation and perhaps get some kind of discussion on whether it\'s a good thing or not.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s exam time (for myself and most everyone else I know in any form of post-secondary education).  Exam time is a time of stress and deadlines and sleep deprivation and neglected chores and poor eating habits.  Exam time is not fun time.  Exam time is that time where everything about yourself is at it\'s weakest.  I\'m exhausted, my body aches.  I am constantly tired and am looking forward to when it\'s all over and done with.  I am looking forward to that time when I get to leave this place for a week and be home with my family; the time of year when I get my annual visit with who took different paths to today.</p>\r\n<p>This has been a really rough semester.  For financial reasons, it was necessary for me to work 36 hours a week on top of going to school full-time during the whole of September.  That resulted in one month solid without a single day break.  The latest I was allowed to sleep in was 7:16AM because that is what time I would wake up for 9am lectures and everyday I worked, I opened the store, which meant my being there at 5:30am on week days and 6:00am on weekends.  After the soul-fucking received during that month, I requested reduced hours at work and have been working 16 hours a week (Saturday and Sunday) and picking up the odd extra shifts here and there since the beginning of October.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve still been really, really busy since then and have not had much in the way of a social life.  There are people who actually live in the same city that I have not seen since school began.  People who I used to see with some regularity.  If you don\'t work with me and don\'t go to my school, chances are you hanven\'t seen much (if any) of me during the past three months.</p>\r\n<p>And now I\'m finding myself desiring that lonliness and thinking of all the people whom I don\'t want to see during this break.  Of course there are people I care for a lot and can\'t wait to have a chance to spend a little time with, but there seems to be more people CON instead of PRO in my mind.  I\'d been thinking about whether I really wanted to go home this holiday season at all and I did eventually decide that it would be foolish of me not to.  Not only do I get to spend time with my family in a (hopefully) stress-free environment but I get car access, free food and gifts thrown at me for my trouble.</p>\r\n<p>Even now, in the thick of it, I don\'t want to talk to people.  I don\'t want to see anyone.  A great number of things angers me lately and most of it is school-related.  I\'m not doing well, that\'s plainly evident.  Though, I don\'t know if cutting myself off from people is a good thing.  I want to sort through things on my own terms, I guess and it doesn\'t help that if I even try to reach out to people and seek some comfort or solace it doesn\'t happen for a variety of reasons.  So, if other people are letting me down, maybe I should just give up.  I\'ve been having these feelings regarding school, as well.  Something along the lines of: if everytime you get up, you just get kicked in the face, at what point do you just lie down and not move?</p>\r\n<p>Maybe sorting things out on my own terms is a good idea.  I don\'t know if I can, but I guess I\'ll find out.  I\'m tired.  I have an assignment that isn\'t going to debug itself and I should really get some sleep before my quality of work goes to pot.</p>\r\n<p>A fresh start and maybe a chance for a smile on my face.  It\'s only one more week and then I\'m free.</p>'),(2126,'<p>every year since i turned 17, this week leading up to my birthday has plainly sucked.  i always have these chipper, optimistic plans for a gathering or a party, and i casually mention it to everyone in my circle of friends.  then, as the date gets closer, the optimism shrivels up and dies away in my chest, and i start to curl up like a potato bug and wish that my birthday would just die.  i can\'t quite figure out why, although maybe a look at a few years past can help to solve the mystery.</p>\r\n<p>20th birthday: record rainfall and torrential floods up in humboldt. everyone was trapped indside. no one wanted to go out.  even my dad was marooned out in his swampy hole of a house.  i got stuck at laura skiles\' house in town.  her boyfriend a was surly indie guy who didn\'t have much of a sense of ceremony or celebration.  he got irritated with me and shut himself in the bedroom.  we watched jay leno and fell asleep.</p>\r\n<p>21st birthday: got way too drunk at sushi dinner.  between sake shots, boyfriend at the time confessed (this part is murky) that his ex-girlfriend had given him a blowjob at a party a few weeks before.  wound up running from the restaurant sobbing. don\'t remember getting home.</p>\r\n<p>22nd birthday: absolutely no recollection of 22nd birthday.  every year i was in college, it was during finals week, so i\'ll just venture a guess that it was during finals week and no one wanted to do anything.</p>\r\n<p>wow, this is depressing.</p>\r\n<p>23rd birthday: apparently i have blocked out all of my recent birthdays.  i am trying hard but all i can think is that this birthday curse not only causes the birthdays to suck, it also causes me to immediately forget them.</p>\r\n<p>24th birthday: had been living in the city for 6 months or so.  it wasn\'t exactly on my birthday, but a few days before, that my grandmother was visiting.  that was the last time i saw her.  mom brought all manner of worthless junk (fake tiny christmas tree, ceramic lighthouse) into apartment. forgave her.  gram\'s leg was hurting her and i rubbed it while we did a crossword puzzle, her murmuring the answers before i even could finish reading her the clue. she was a whiz at crossword puzzles. said it kept her mind sharp.  i loved her so much.  okay, so that birthday can\'t have been so awful.  i had a choir performance, which made me happy.  singing in the church at night with candles lit.  ok, so that one wasn\'t so bad, although i didn\'t party my ass off.  then again, i haven\'t partied my ass off on my birthday since i was 17.</p>\r\n<p>25th birthday: coming up this saturday. still haven\'t worked up the nerve to call friends and make sure they are coming over.  deep fear of the birthday curse prevents me from composing evite.</p>\r\n<p>i guess the secret here is to just make my birthday as fabulous as i want it to be.  hot spiced wine and candles and good music, and who cares if everyone\'s busy or it\'s raining or i just get drunk and dance by myself in my living room?  it\'s MY birthday, dammit, i should have a good time.  no pressure, just the freedom to make it how i want it.  i\'m gonna try to bake myself a vegan birthday cake from scratch.  and do art.  and just enjoy my one day in the year where i can say, THIS IS THE DAY I WAS BORN.  I\'M CELEBRATING THE FACT THAT I\'M STILL HERE.  THAT I MADE IT TO A QUARTER-CENTURY.  THAT I SURVIVED THE BIRTHDAY CURSE.</p>\r\n'),(2127,'<p>\r\nyeah so i got a new job. its an interesting job i guess. sorta unique to what all you might do. i work in a warehouse. a freezer warehouse. its -30C at all times in this hell hole. when you ride around on the carts the wind chill adds another -10 to the equation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe entire warehouse is one million square feet. the freezer is about one hundred thousand square feet. aisle upon aisle of frozen goods ready for the pickin. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy job basically involves building the skids that goto the various supermarkets we service. i carry upto 60,000 pounds a shift. its hard work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to the clothes i wear to work. my uniform consists of heavy snowpants i put on over my regular pants. they are sort of like long hockey pants. a thick jacket and a pair of gloves finishes off the uniform. hats are optional and are to be attained on your own. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am in so much pain right now. I had my first shift this morning at six am. its not so hard waking up and getting to work, but by around two in the afternoon, things get really sweaty and cold. chillz run up and down the legs and back. the sweat collected in the hair freezes and when you enter the warmup room, you have to shake it all out or the sticks of ice clinging to the hair will melt and run down your back; meandering around the crack of the ass. its quite uncomfortable at times. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni explained to the person who was training me that i was in ottawa going to university, taking political science. upon hearing that he began to introduce me to people around the freezer as the future prime minister of canada. i kinda wanted to kick him in the balls for saying that to everyone, but whatever...for the most part these people are simple. However, just because they are simple doesnt mean im better then them. when it comes to hard labour and speed, these guys got the job down pat. Im sure they were not as fast when they started, but watching them adds to the respect they deserve. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni like working with the rank and file. there are always a few that kinda suprise you, yet i havnt met one yet in the freezer. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin three months, ill be off probation and ill enter the union. i dont think im going to keep this job for three months. i really just dont think im cut out for that kind of work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just want all of you to know that this is not my career and that you should not think im selling myself short.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to that, next time you goto the frozen juice aisle in your supermarket, just remember that some sorry sod had to cart all that shit.\r\n</p>'),(2128,'<p>Sounds pour into my mind from unusual sources:</p>\r\n<p><ul>\r\n   <li>high-end, ceiling-mounted speakers</li>\r\n   <li>terribly low-quality speakers owing more to tin cans and string than to magnets and paper cones</li>\r\n  <li>a wooden pencil, rhythmically tapping a wooden desktop</li>\r\n  <li>the paticular clanging of cutlery and ceramics</li>\r\n  <li>busy traffic and the doppler effect</li>\r\n  <li>strange bodily noises from origins best left unknown</li>\r\n  <li>disgruntled voices and quietly-uttered obscenities</li>\r\n  <li>the loud din of a coffee shop, full of nighttime people and colourful discusions regarding whatever</li>\r\n  <li>the adventure in ambient music that is going to sleep</li>\r\n  <li>the relentless, disembodied pounding of a keyboard</li>\r\n  <li>that odd frequency your ears detect when a television\'s CRT is blasting out electrons</li>\r\n  <li>squeaky soles attached to shoes in need of replacing</li>\r\n  <li>annoying and stupid cell phone rings</li>\r\n  <li>the shuffling of indecisive footsteps</li>\r\n  <li>the scratching of fingernails against a stubble-clad kneck</li>\r\n  <li>the gentle hum of a refridgeration unit</li>\r\n  <li>the sounds of war no matter when you turn on a television</li>\r\n  <li>the running soundtrack courtesy of my brain</li>\r\n  <li>laughter with a friend not talked to for a very long time</li>\r\n  <li>the snapping of fingers to a catchy beat</li>\r\n  <li>the part satisfying, part dreadful thud produced by dropping a $1000 stack of badass textbooks and various programming texts</li>\r\n</ul></p>\r\n<p>During the holiday break, I plan on getting back into making tracks again.  It\'s been a long time and is one of those pleasures I wish I had more time to partake in.  I have successfully skipped the whole \"I want to be a DJ\" fad and with my lacking of dreams for becomming a Superstar DJ: I have neither the patience nor the resolve to learn how to mix.  But I can still produce and it looks like there are a lot of really cool new tools available since I last sat down to write something.  It will be fun toying around with them.  I might even see if I can get Impulse Tracker to play nice with winXP.</p>\r\n<p>Last year, during the holidays, I taught myself how to code BSD sockets and learned the ins and outs of MP3 frames.  This year, I\'m going with a little more artistic route.</p>\r\n<p>It\'s good to have balance.</p>\r\n'),(2129,'<P>\r\n1987 dodge caravan.<BR>\r\n187k mi. runs. $1400\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nThe Spiro family wagon, a Dodge Caravan with fake wood siding.  Its last five years were plagued by an orchestra of strange noises.  It never gave in though.  Sitting in a caravan, one got the feeling that they belonged in a procession of like styled cars.  You drive a Dodge caravan and you are <i>in the Dodge Caravan</i>, the procession.  All Dodge Caravans on the road are going the same place.  They are following each other and there is no leader.  To be an artist driving a dodge caravan is to be working Inside the system.  After ten years of family service, the interior smells like a camel.  musk oil. dust. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>The red and white for sale sign offers $1400.  The actual value of this machine is $850.  Six and a half weeks the Spiro family wagon sat at the intersection of Main and Marshall.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Mr. Spiro answered the phone.  He was watching the television.  Six weeks ago he left the old caravan at Main and Marshall.  Outside in the driveway, is a new 2002 Dodge Caravan showing the beginnings of the first Dirt it has ever touched.  The old caravan is no longer something Mr. Spiro thinks of.  If it was stolen, he would not notice until he happened by the intersecion it was parked at, and even then, he probably would not remember that it used to be there.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"...sure, 850 is fine...\"</P> \r\n\r\n'),(2130,'Isn\'t this always the way? I love him so much. When we lay in his little single bed together and spoon. He snuggles his head just so into my neck and I can feel his breath. When he falls asleep I watch his chest rise and fall. I watch his mouth, his hair, his body getting comfortable around me. I cuddle up against him and drift to sleep.\r\n\r\n<P>I want to hold his hand and hug him always and tell the whole world how happy he makes me feel. How I feel like my heart is smiling because of him.\r\n\r\n<P>I can\'t though. He doesn\'t want me. He just wants to be bestfriends. I want to be bestfriends. But I also want the world to know I\'m in love with him. That I\'m in love. I just want to smile and be happy and be with him.'),(2131,'<p>I lugged my ass out of bed at the god awful hour of 6 this morning, all in the name of overtime. Groggily I showered and dried, brushed my teeth standing naked in the bathroom. I wiped the steam off the mirror and took a look at myself. Just checkin shit out. Something didn\'t look right. It took me a moment to realize that the shiny silver horseshoe that usually lives in my left nipple wasn\'t there! Oh jeez! I searched frantically, the bed, the shower (and drain), the towel to see if was stuck in the nap. How in the hell can something like that FALL out without my noticing? Continued the search. The kitchen floor, all the floors, the rug. Damn. It was in there last night, I KNOW it. I was absentmindedly tugging on it, as I\'m prone to do, while standing naked in the bedroom trying to decide which clothing to put on. Fuck. Can\'t find it anywhere. The next question was, \"do I have any spare jewelry to put in the piercing to keep it from closing?\" Nope. All long lost. Okay. Now what? I really didn\'t want to just leave it. I love the adornment, don\'t want it to close up but can\'t afford to have it repierced. Hmm. I rememered reading once that fishing line can be used to keep the hole open. I awoke my lover (again) and asked him where the fishing line was. The loverly man went up to the attic to fetch it for me. I looped a length of it through the hole three times, to try and simulate the gauge. Tied it off and voila! A makeshift ring.</P>\r\n<P>Now I\'m at work, and it\'s starting to ache. I keep going into the bathroom to fuss with it; make sure that its not closing up or becomng infected. I think that\'s why it\'s starting to ache, but I suppose that it could be because I have a length of fishing line roped three times through a hole in my tender flesh. </P>'),(2132,'baasdfasfasrwe'),(2133,'<p>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2133_1.jpg\" align=\"center\">\r\n</center>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2133_2.jpg\"  align=\"center\">\r\n</center>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2133_3.jpg\"  align=\"center\">\r\n</center>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nhello there. hi. you asked to see some shots of those horses from a while back. here are a few.\r\n</p>'),(2134,'<p>Two weeks ago was a rough time for me and most of my friends at school.  All of us had multiple huge assignments due within the same three-day span.  For most of these assignments, we were working together and the stresses of hastily approaching deadlines and non-working code created an unpleasant environment, to say the least.</p>\r\n<p>It hurt.  It hurt a whole lot.  That was the week of pulling the doctor routine: catching three hours of sleep here and there whenever I could.  Most of us pulled all-nighters more than once that week.  I think that out of us all, Eli slept the least.  I guess his military training for sleep-deprivation came in handy, though and apparently the key is to eat like crazy because your body needs such a large energy intake to make up for not sleeping.  I wish I had of known this <b>before</b> the week was over, but regardless.  It was a rough week and one that we\'re all glad to put behind us.</p>\r\n<p>I took Thursday night off for myself basically because I had been killing myself for various assignments and needed a sanity break because my mind was starting to crack.  On Friday I decided to head into school in the afternoon to work on my graphics assignment (which I successfully received an extension of five days on) in the labs at school because I had to be downtown to go see <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0235327\" target=\"new\">Dark Days</a> in the evening and it wasn\'t a very far walk to the theater from campus.</p>\r\n<p>I was almost at the building with the CS labs when I looked to my left and saw four of my friends walking across the street at me.  They had all just come from demoing their database assignment to their prof.  None of them had slept the night before.  They were in good spirits and were on their way to the campus pub for beers.  I laughed with them and turned down an invite to join them for drinking with the excuse of no money on me (and not wanting to hit-up a bank machine) and the fact that I had come to work on my assignment.  All it took was one offer of a free beer to get me in the door (I\'m easy).  I had the seat at the head of the rectangular table, the opposite end against a wall.  Clockwise from my left were Matt, Zoltan, Greg and Eli.  I think we were luck to get a table.  The place was nice and packed for 1:00PM but it\'s that time of year, I guess.</p>\r\n<p>They were really excited about their DB assignment, especially the shell script which controlled everything.  They kept telling me that I had to look at the thing.  Apparently it was up to my standards of quality for being well-coded and it made me smile to hear them excitedly describe a shell script.</p>\r\n<p>We shared tales of sleep-deprivation and everyone compared how much sleep they had treated themselves to all week.  I was the bastard for sleeping about ten hours the night before while they were all whoring themselves for their DB assignment.  We just sat around and bull-shitted with each other.  We made fun of each other, threw around a cornucopia of Simpsons quotes and just enjoyed each other\'s company.  There were times when I just say back, clutching my CD binder and smiled the largest, most genuine smile I\'ve experience in months.  It was the most fun I\'ve had since school began again, that\'s for sure.  Similar moments were experience during the first week of classes when it was just a lot of drinking and shit talking outside on the patio of the campus pub before and after class.</p>\r\n<p>These moments are few and far between.  Those moments where you can sit back and just get so much joy from watching and listening to your friends.  I\'m sure we would have made a great beer commercial: all smiles and laughter and beers on deck.</p>\r\n<p>It was a very humbling and rejuvenating experience.</p>\r\n<p>When Desi showed up things got all crazy and I knew I had to get out before I got caught in a humour vortex and not get any work done.</p>\r\n<p>With a big smile on my face, I bid my farewell and continued, unimpeded, to my original destination.</p>\r\n<p>All of us are writing our last finals this coming Wednesday so I expect some more drinking will be incurred following the last one that day.  More of these <i>warm and glowing warming glow</i>-type moments to be had, no doubt.  I\'ll most likely have to skip out as I think I\'ll have an hour from the end of my last final before I have to go to work to work a closing shift.</p>\r\n<p>And as some marks for assignments are rolling in now, it appears that our enormous effort is paying off because the marks are high and in turn, so are my spirits.  This is a good way to enter the holidays.</p>\r\n'),(2135,'The Making of a Canadian Terroris I\'m a little concerned about terrorists in Canada, because if the Prime Minister and his cabinet get their way there will be a whole lot more of us just after the holidays. That\'s right I said \'us\' because I am one of the Canadians that will be a terrorist after the new trio of securitymeasures are put in place. You may have heard of them bills C-35, C-36, and C-42 if you have been consuming any Canadian media latelyFirst let me make a few things clear, I outright condemn the actions of the terrorists the U.S. I however have a civil libertarian slant to my politics. Not to mention that I have also been know to attend a protest or two in relation to international trade policies and their impact on common goods and industry regulation in my own country. This is why I\'ll be getting the label of terrorist as a late Christmas present from my own government.The silent majority out there is asking \'What is he talking about?\'. The explanation can\'t be found by looking at the new security bills one at a time it is the over all combined effect. This way when questioned about any one specific bill any member of the government still has\r\nplausible denial See if you can follow this one, first off C-35 expands diplomatic immunity and gives the RCMP new statutory powers \"to ensure the security for the proper functioning of any intergovernmental conference.\" Then the more infamous C-36 steps in and this is where things\r\nget messy, it defines interference with \'protected persons\' (the definition of which has been expanded in bill C-35) as an act of terrorism. Even threatening interference is an act of terrorism under C-36. Now harken back to the APEC fiasco, the government felt that the threat to Suharto\'s conscience from the demonstrators was interference - there has been some debate back and forth about the governments involvement here, it should be pointed out that the only thing being debated is whether or not it was the executive or judicial branch that came to this conclusion. To top this all off under C-42 - the Public Safety Act - the Minister of Defense is authorized to declare a specific area a military-security zone and expel people from it. \r\nOnce these bills all pass into law all I will have to do is declare my intent to protest in an attempt to disrupt any meetings and then by the books I\'ll be a terrorist. Our Beloved Jean and his cabinet ministers have assured us that the laws won\'t be used to suppress political dissent -\r\nlest I remind us that this is the same party vowed to get rid of the GST. And when asked his reasons for believing that this legislation wouldn\'t be used to suppress political dissent our fearless leader infamous for his personal choking of a demonstrator stated \"I just don\'t\".\r\nWith reasoning like that how could I take any of these bills as a serious threat. This doesn\'t even get into the massive curtailing of civil liberties under C-36 or the weak review clauses (only included after much uproar).\r\nYours Truly, An unemployed debt ridden university educated asymmetrical threat to the security of Canada Dv8'),(2136,'<p>I don\'t feel as well as i did about a week ago, I\'m undergoing a psychological change, I\'m sick of being a joker, I\'m tired of being laughed with and laughed at by other people. I am from this point taking a stand. I am who I am and if that means not conforming to societies expectations of me then so be it. I\'m sick of being stereotyped because of the way I look, I can get on with just about anyone, but now I really can\'t be bothered making the effort. Let people think what they want about me, it just shows their prejudice and how they have been conditioned by society.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What is the point in living? I have no answer to this question. Why do people fear the different? Why can\'t we all just get along? People fear change. But change is good thing right? Maybe not.  Why am I here? What purpose do I serve? What makes a person cool? Approval from others? Approval is something every person seeks. Do you like everyone? What\'s the point there will always be someone who dislikes you and people you dislike.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>If you had one wish what would it be? World peace? It could never be maintained. Are you selfish? Everyone is, its just that some are more so than others. If only i knew what purpose I was supposed to serve I would be content. Would I really be content? No. Why? My life feels empty. I doubt whether the void I have could be filled for at least a few years. Why so morbid? Its just how I fell. I\'m tired of society I need a release. Is this my release? Kind of. Kind of? I cry, I inflict pain upon myself, I isolate myself these are my releases. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Have you ever been truly happy? I don\'t know... Maybe. When have you ever been happy? I can\'t remember. Have you ever been in love? No. But I have felt strong emotions towards others. Like? My mother and father and siblings. Do you not love your family? I care for them. Do you love your family? I care for them very much, but there have been times when I have hated them all. Do you remember your first love. Vaguely. But my love was never reciprocated. Has your love ever been? Perhaps. Who from? My mother. What about your father? I believe he resents me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What do you look like? I am not comfortable with my appearance. Why not? If you ask anyone they would want to change something about themselves. What would you change? Nothing. I am who I am accept me for that. I wish I was considered attractive but so would everyone, maybe some people find me desirable others won\'t I don\'t care. Why? I\'ve been alone long enough, I don\'t mind being alone. Don\'t you mind? No. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Honestly life isn\'t that bad. What happens when you die? I don\'t know. But I am intrigued. Would you like to see? I am afraid. Afraid of dying? Yes. Why? I fear the unknown. So you are in effect like the society that you resent so much? No. Why not? you fear dying as it is unknown to you just like society looks at you in contempt because of your lifestyle. This is not the end...</p>\r\n'),(2137,'today i forced mark to take me to the mall.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark claims he hates to shop.  the truth is that he hates to shop for things not on his personal list at the store of his choice.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat the first place we went, he had a list, which he filled, then he decided that there were certain things a yeti might need.  he had already purchased a really lovely yeti surprise, but he found another.  then he found one for le that he was sure she needed.  then he found a bunch more stuff, and then when it came time to pay he did what he always does when he knows i am flush with internet invoice cash -- do you want me to put these on my card, or should we use \"your\" money???  lol! i already knew who was paying, because the mall was looming ahead, and he always thinks that if i spend a big bunch in one place, i will, like him, refrain from spending a big bunch somewhere else.  silly man.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere are two things that make the mall bearable for mark, one is the fucking picadilly cafeteria, the other is the hope that i will not actually spend any money while i am there on anything that he does not believe is an important purchase.  which is so pitifully hopeful, but flawed when i am on a mission.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni had on my list three silver chains. they are meant to hold silver camel pendants purchased in kuwait and mark was not happy because i will be sending them to strangers he does not know.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark and i have been married 29 years, he thinks that if he behaves in a certain manner, he can, without complaining about being in the mall, trick me into leaving sooner than i had planned.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlest you feel sorry for him, you have to know that we have lived in this town for 2 years, and he has accompanied me to the mall a grand total of 4 times, including today.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni know his game, but he doesn\'t know i know, which is one of the reasons i love him more. <br><br>\r\n\r\n the first place we stopped was the most expensive jewelry store there, which i knew whould be way overpriced, and they did not fail me.  $89 for a silver chain.  mark was rude, as expected. \"$90 for that?\" he opined loudly... to which i just nodded and told the clerk sweetly that it was more than i planned to spend. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni then moved his line of vision so that he could see the picadilly cafeteria sign, and he got happy and asked me if i wanted to have lunch there and i agreed.  once fed, i maneuvered him into the second most expensive jewelry store where the silver chains were only $49. he was incensed, and louder. \"no way in hell am i gonna spend that much money on a fucking stranger,\" he compained, forgetting that HE wasn\'t spending a dime. <br><br>\r\n\r\nhaving accomplished the intended result, i quickly moved down the mall to the place where i had originally intended to purchase the silver chains, his relief when i paid a mere $11.50 each was palpable.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni will return to the mall next week, sans husband, but the point is, he promised to take me to the mall, because for me, it doesn\'t feel like christmas unless we have been there once together and seen all the mothers and children and daddies and brothers and families, and mark actually enjoys remembering what it was like when the children were small and we were so poor and couldn\'t buy much, but we would go and dream together.<br><br>\r\n\r\non the road home, he made many more suggestions about purchases i should make for children that we love, and i laughed and laughed, because he hates to shop, but he loves to buy things.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfor mark, it isn\'t really christmas until i have wrapped the presents and stacked them all up with many ribbons and bows and then he counts to see how many for who and decides i must go out again for more gifts because there are not enough, and i love him very much, but it is exhausting to get him started and i have been doing this for 29 christmases now, that\'s quite a few picadilly dinners, huh?\r\n'),(2138,'<p>I said, \"I know thereâ€™s nothing I can say to make things different.\" And then we smoked a cigarette.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When you put your hand on my knee, when you reached for me like a dying man, my world fractioned. And later, in your bed, I remember thinking, \"Enjoy this. Enjoy it because you might never be here again.\" I did enjoy it. But I was there a hundred more times. A million times. But I was never very good with fractions. And I canâ€™t remember, was I there? Or did I just wish to be? And if I was there, were you? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Were you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The wind bangs on a window like a lover throwing rocks to wake me up, and I donâ€™t know if Iâ€™m really alive anymore except for the moments I wake up hungry. Not for food. I suck on a toothpick like Iâ€™m thirsty for the wood. For something to do. Itâ€™s 5 in the morning and thereâ€™s nothing but this toothpick for company. A toothpick and old words and the soft light that stretches out to me from the bedroom doorway. I think about giving in. It doesnâ€™t matter either way, so I give in. And in my dreams I feel the warmth of summer on my face and the weight of other peopleâ€™s hands holding me down so that I can learn to breathe under the water.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Iâ€™m under water.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Deep down in dreams. I live a fantasy played out, repeated until nothing is left. But there are always more dreams. And there is always more dreaming. Last night I dreamt we chased a cab for blocks. Fought with a dozen other dream time couples for the first cab, the second cab. We stopped a cab in the middle of the street, and before you could climb in, he surged and I was alone with only your voice on the outside of the cab screaming, \"Wait!\" When the driver slowed at a light I launched myself from the car. You were some blocks back, and I looked your direction and pulled a toothpick from my right coat pocket, and put it in my mouth. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>For a fraction of a second, I was afraid to be alone. And then it was over. And I was alone. Like I said, I was never very good with fractions, and I canâ€™t remember. Did I want to be there? I thought I did. So warm and so full and so sweet in your arms. But Iâ€™m not sure you have the strength to hold me under the water until I learn to breathe. And Iâ€™m not interested in drowning. </p>\r\n'),(2139,'Today is Monday. On Saturday I move into the house on Haze.st.<br><br>\r\nI have no lovers.<br><br>\r\nThe room at haze represents a blank canvas. A clean break. I am already populating the room with candles and plants in my mind.<br><br>\r\nI am nervous about living with new people. <br><br>\r\nI wonder where and how I will make my art.<br><br>\r\nEverything nebulous, I find it hard to enjoy my time with people, knowing that in days my life will be in upheaval. I am a homey girl, and I am leaving the best home I have ever had.<br><br>\r\nI dread going home for the holidays. Christmas represents to me not only the reinterpretation of cherished mysticism into a capitalistic swill-fest, but it burns deep into my awareness the love that exists in other families does not exist in mine.<br><br>\r\nOk sure, there is love there. It is a skewed, narcissistic love served with a bitter ladle. It has attachments and fine print. I dream of a holiday that sparkles like a cranberry plane of glass, untempered by the dim grey spectres of alcoholism, sexual abuse, divorce, decay.<br><br>\r\nI look forward to finishing December quickly and going boldly at the blank canvas of next year, spilling golden and green hues into the future.<br><br>\r\nI stand at the ready, a seasoned soldier prepared to fight for the sake of art, for the sake of humanity, and to forget myself. <br><br>\r\n  '),(2140,'I wrote this whole thing about wanting my holidays always to be like a sparkling cranberry pane of glass and it was really beatiful and dark at the same time and i was feeling patently sorry for myself again and i ended the essay with great socialistic pride by saying:<br><br>\r\n\"I stand at the ready, a seasoned soldier prepared to fight for the honor of art, for the sake of humanity.\"<br><br>\r\nand i was feeling pretty good about myself after that, and then i went to find an image to go along with my moment and as i was flipping around i acidentally hit back on my browser and *voop* my essay was gone. now i have gone all unstructured and without metaphor, and here i will take a moment to update you in case you were following along at home. mostly this is for my own benefit.<br><br>\r\ncurrent books:<br>\r\nfire : the unexpurgated diary of anais nin, 1934-1937.<br>\r\nparasite rex by carl zimmer<br>\r\nfree play by s. nachmanovitch<br>\r\npicasso by patrick o\'brian (slowly)<br><br>\r\ncurrent projekts:<br>\r\nmoving to haze.st in 5 days. nervous.<br>\r\ncreating a crew of artists, the visualist posse, possibly<br> to be called \"the bonus army\".<br> \r\nhumbling myself at the feet of work.<br>\r\nfucking ready to head boldly into work, possibility,<br> discussion, potentiality. 2002!<br>\r\nso symmetrical and balanced, a year of positive change.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlikes:<br>\r\ncreation, protein, juice, fruit, tea, cigarettes, boys in sweaters, est.00, lipgloss, jack dangers.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndislikes:<br>\r\ncavities, bad posture, depression, hopelessness, the feeling that no matter what i do i will never make art that really speaks to the people.<br><br>\r\nJohn D. Rockefeller commissioned a mural from Diego Rivera then had it destroyed after Rivera painted in an image of Lenin. What a waste.<br><br>\r\nNo matter how important my ideas seem to myself, they appear trivial to many people.<br><br>\r\nI want to bring people back to beauty. Not an idealistic beauty, but the kind that occurs after healing from scars. A worn beauty. A real beauty.<br><br>\r\nHow I go about this is another story altogether. Stay tuned.<br><br> '),(2141,'<p>People say love is like water. It washes over, it drowns you, runs past you like a river and makes you think of building dams.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sometimes I see people on their hands and knees licking love off the sidewalk like itâ€™ll never rain again. Trying to get it all before it evaporates. Binging because there isnâ€™t an even flow.  And I wonder why I donâ€™t get down on my knees, oblivious and uninhibited, just to get a taste. But then itâ€™s gone. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Just a tonguefull is never enough to do anything but make you notice your thirst, make you walk around for days raw and unfinished. And the last thing I need is to feel the seams that arenâ€™t airtight. It takes a long time to learn to wait out the spring and see if it all dries up in the sun, leaving you dusty like a ghost town. You get so thirsty. Your throat closes up and itâ€™s hard to breathe. But when the rain finally does come, comes to stay, and pools up at your feet and you lift love to your lips in your hands, you feel like you can drink for days. You can splash it on your face, wring your shirt out in it, float on it face down with your eyes open. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Greywater runs around the corner in the gutter and shoots over the edge of the sewer grating. Foamy from chemicals it picked up on its way down the block, opaque from use. And I think to myself, \"look at all that love that someone threw away.\" I wonder who poured out their soul and is down on their hands and knees trying to lick their own love back up before it all flows out to sea. </p>\r\n'),(2142,'<p>(the real title of this is \"untitled\". neat.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we\'re standing behind paris, holding down the night with pelican clamps and three rusty nails, the darkness snagging on edges and brushes of fine synthetic material. the weight will crack and burn them, the resulting smoke shade our eyes from you, lucky, tripping and sliding to your escape, planning the routes you think we can\'t follow; we always follow, to my right we track holograms and solidstate versions of you, watching your decoys with little attention, following the signal with distraction to your true shape, we stand behind paris, see you in bonn, tokyo, amsterdam...we weave a treaty to settle your unknowing hand, the car alarms explore your death in other countries, we hold our heads to the sound of animals dying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>days before this i saw melting birds fly low over lake water, turned to see the streetlights all bending in towards the pavement. the scent of cut grass, condensation on transparent plastic, some floating, seething image of dead parents. i wake to the voice on the answering machine, instructions to partner up and track you, at that point a meter-high hologram on slow rotate with descript text, later a faux-brunette peering through trees, skyscraper windows, dusted hallways with hungered childlike eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>society moved a step over today, we were able to bargain for data on your recent surgery, three months ago, amping your musculature in a dead new jersey alley/conduit office. you\'ll receive this document one month after your follow-up surgery, at which point we will see what you see, in every aspect, via circuitry, implants, and chips. microtoxin blades in your wrists, low amperage cams inside your eyes, chips, high-grade cortex grips in your head. none of it detectable, unknown to you until you receive this.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>how will it feel? my back is to you, as i write this. tracking you for months gives me no reason to guess where you will be, we understand all motives and missions you\'ve been given, even all your desires to abort or abandon, no matter how fleeting they may be. i know nothing of you. i don\'t know what you look like, the core of you, just the trademarks in what you do and what you leave behind. this is a trade involving too much disguise, changing the face and everything all too personal, the number of casualties losing themselves is staggering, you\'ll hold out maybe another year at most, i can feel the framework reducing from steel to brick to wood, soon to paper, then to ash. our intervention may extend this, or take you faster to that end...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i feel the dead skin peel a little painfully from my chin, the fragments caught under my thumbnail. i haven\'t seen this city, i spend too much time closing down the pathways you try to get to, misdirecting your current efforts, or just tracking you through narrow streets. i wonder about you. i wonder if you\'re as tired as i am. i wonder where we\'ll land after the next jump.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hold on.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2143,'this is a story i wrote over the course of a year a few years ago.  anyone who knows me here will have already read it, but most don\'t know me here, so that\'s why i\'m posting it.\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.inconduit.com/words/bm_frame/s_frame.html\">it\'s linked here off of my site</a> because it\'s too long to post as one single entry.\r\n\r\nclick on the right arrow to advance the pages. it\'s 13 pages in total.\r\n\r\nso yeah, if you\'ve been to my site, you may have read it, so i may be sorry for the repetition.\r\n\r\ni have other writing on my site in the brain section.  click around.  find stuff.'),(2144,'<p>\r\nI remember you now with your hand on the doorknob, not turning it, just standing still. Staring at the crack between the door and the jamb. I thought maybe you were going to leave and never come back and my heart raced a little at that. People are always doing that in fiction and on the TV. I guess people are always doing it in real life, but you never hear about it in our circles. People don\'t just disappear, go out for a pack of smokes or a gallon of milk, and never come back in our crowd. Instead they linger indifferently, slowly building up lies, and finding inconsequential things to put their gaze on so they can avoid each other\'s eyes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou stood there for a minute, maybe more, like you were checking for the heat of secret fire on the other side of the door, and then you turned around and walked back into the kitchen. I never had the courage to ask you if your lingering was indifferent or mindful. And how could I ask? When I knew I was just biding time myself until the weather changed. I can stand up straight because I never slid so low as to avoid your eyes, and technically I never lied, because technically, you never asked for the truth. I think you preferred not knowing. I think it gave you a sense of perennial freedom you needed to keep your hands from crashing down over your ears every time I opened my mouth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo, you went into the kitchen and I turned back to whatever it was I was doing. Maybe I was watching TV or maybe I was reading some last year\'s magazine I\'d stealthily slipped into my coat when I\'d gone out to get my hair cut. Or maybe I hadn\'t had my hair cut in months and was just staring out the window watching all the other people who\'d had the courage to open the door and step through. Slapping them high fives in my mind.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt must have been indifference for you, but I still don\'t know for sure if it was fear or excitement that made my heart race to see you with your face to the door. You know (of course you know) if you\'d have left, I wouldn\'t have followed. </p>\r\n'),(2145,'<p>i have a client that i love and care about on a personal level, but she is making me a crazy woman.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she believes that she pays for my time 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.  she simply does not understand how long it takes to accomplish the most mundane of web tasks</p>\r\n\r\n<p>every weekend, every holiday, every time i tell her i am going on vacation, she freaks out totally, for she is sure that her business will fail unless her website is updated daily.  which would not be such a problem except...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we\'re not talking about small changes, we\'re talking daily redesign of entry page, massive newsletter demands that would be spam if i would agree to do them, even fucking SUBSCRIBERS have their limits, you know?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>her favorite terrorization of me has to do with the search engines.  she wants to be number one.  she wants me to make her number one.  she simply doesn\'t understand why i can\'t do that, because she gets all these emails from all these people telling her that, for a small fee, THEY can guarantee her search engine placement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i told her last week that i was going to bill her for every minute she had me working, no matter what it was, and she does not believe me, i don\'t think, because the email tonight was all about WHO was in the search engines above her, and why SHE should be above them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have hit my last nerve, as it is 9:39 pm on the first day of what is supposed to be my vacation, and i have been dealing with these demanding demands most of the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if anyone knows of a reliable service for search engine manipulation, please email auntie@auntie.com, because i am not doing it anymore. the end.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>AND -- i am on fucking vacation, and i don\'t give a shit if the whole fucking server blows up tomorrow, i am not available for repairs, thank you very much.</p>'),(2146,'<p>\r\nthis morning i woke up with a really sore back, a cold raging on inside my head. I knew what i was to do but i still wasnt sure how to do it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsitting in the car with my mom before shift in the early morning is some of the most quiet times, but this morning conversation was brisk and a lot was said. nothing bad. nothing good. just conversation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni quit my job this morning. right after my mom dropped me off i crossed the road and walked towards my bosses office. he wasn\'t there so i spoke to the second in command. polite man. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni told number two that i had found a full time job and that it started immediately. I told him i was going off to become a negotiator. He said good luck and i think he meant it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwalking to the bus stop with a cigarette I smiled. In my conversation with number two, he had said something to the effect of \"and you were doing pretty good i hear\". Im glad i left there a good worker, and not as a dude who just didnt show up. Im glad i went in to do it. even though i lied about why i was leaving I didn\'t feel it was proper of me to \"bash\" those who this man would have to work with. Not to mention that anything i said wouldnt be unknown to the guy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nduring my last lunch break, i learned from some of my co-workers that on saturday one of the selectors had been threatened with a knife over a skid. I also learned that a dude shit in another dudes boots because he pissed him off. In retalliation, the dude with the smelly boots wiped his ass on the dude with the empty colon\'s lock. while the room erupted in laughter i said \"so they play with poo?\" the laughs stopped and i mentioned something about the leafs. I didnt wanna leave work with a shitty lock. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter a nice nap with my cat i got to work raking the back yard. the willows on the other side of the fence are still shedding and without any snow on the ground its nice to keep things tidy back there. i stopped to light a cigarette and check the time. It was second break over at the A&P freezer and after only four shifts i knew exactly what everyone there was doing. The forklift dudes were drinking beer and smoking joints in their cars. the full time selectors were at the far picnick table smoking joints and drinking molson king cans. leo and the dude with the blue jacket are talking about frisbee golf(which sounds fun as hell). everyone at that time would be sucking back three smokes before the shift started again. beebee would becoming out in a few minutes to tell everyone to get back to work and the beers and joints would go flying in all sorts of directions. i think some of them actually thought the beebee\'z didn\'t know what was up. what could they do? management isnt going to waste the time fighting with the unions to \"clean up the workplace\", when in all fareness management fuck up just as much. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n its cold time up in canada. everyone seems to be coming down with some sort of sniffle, flu, cough, runny nose. the news is boring. the indy news is boring. the world is fucked up. lets stop the war and get back to work. \r\n</p>'),(2147,'<p>\r\nEver get the feeling that the universe is trying to tell you something, leaving little clues in the strata of existence that in the corner of your eye and under your nose but mostly beneath your subconcious mind?  Little clues, that maybe seem related but try as you might, you can\'t connect or make any sense of?\r\n</p><p>\r\nIn the past day or so I\'ve caught probably an even dozen repeated-sentence errors, like where some sentence is perfectly grammatical and every word is spelled right, but it\'s repeated twice?  wtf?  Is time getting stuck in liitle loops where people are writing the same thing more than once without noticing it, little temporal whirlpools that encompass one person and their keyboard but not the server\'s memory?</p>'),(2148,'<pre>\r\nThere was a day\r\n	I burned you up.\r\nOne day out of a thousand\r\n	others that could have\r\n	called your name\r\n	to a crisp.\r\n	But this oneâ€¦\r\n	I chose this one day\r\n	threw you on the fire\r\n	like a pencil breaking\r\n	like a mudflap\r\n		catching mud.\r\n\r\n	And you were gone.\r\n\r\nThere was a day\r\n	I chose to let my heart\r\n	be free to love\r\n	be free to catch\r\n	another fire burning.\r\nThere was a day.\r\n\r\nThere was a day\r\n	I burned you up\r\n	and started my own fire\r\n	with gasoline and woodchips\r\n	with gasoline soaked lingerie\r\n	with gasoline to a crisp\r\n		I burned you up.\r\nAnd it could have been\r\na thousand other days.\r\nBut this oneâ€¦\r\nI chose this one day\r\nto throw you on the fire\r\nand you were gone.\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2149,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis is what happened on saturday:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwe went to a party.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\njen\'s wicked smart. my friend jen, she\'s wicked smart. she works for this bio chem company in south city, and on saturday they had their christmas party. it\'s a big company and the party was equally as big. and it was in the park.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis party, it was in golden gate park, at the california academy of sciences. it was a big party. huge. there were tents and food and fish and man, i don\'t know.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i wasn\'t even sure if i wanted to go! that\'s the funny thing. i had never been to the california academy of sciences and really, a party there didn\'t seem all too big of a deal. but, free food. that was why i went. when they said free food, i decided i should go.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nso, we went up to their apartment. to jen\'s apartment. we went up there and hung out for a while. we drank a little wine and ate a little cheese and listened to a little jazz. we hung out and smoked some cigarettes and eventually we took off.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis party, man. let me tell you about this party.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nwhen we got there and walked up those steps, man, i couldn\'t help but laugh. cause this place is huge. it\'s friggin huge. and i just kept giggling to myself, cause you know, there i am in my sweater/cargopants combo from jcpenny\'s and everybody else is in gowns and dresses and suits and shit. i don\'t know.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand all throughout the night that was the theme, you know? like, all through out the night, i kept turning to dug and saying, \'this is crazy man, we soooooo don\'t belong here.\' and then we would just laugh and sip our free drinks.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut, anyway. we walk in and sit down at a table underneath this huge tent thing in what, i can only imagine, is normally some sort of outdoor courtyard. there\'s pa speakers playing jazz and there\'s this fountain in the center and there\'s these chefs.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nsee, the chefs. yeah. the chefs are serving food. meat. roastbeef. and mashed potatoes and stuffing and these little eggroll things that tasted like pumpkin and nutmeg. and there was turkey and salad too.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut man, roast beef. roast beef! roast fucking beef. i mean, i\'ve been eating ramen and rice and liptons cup-a-noodle soup for the past month. and now i get roast beef? jesus. and the chef asked me how i wanted the roast beef. would you like it rare or medium? like, not only do i get the roast beef, but i get to decide how i want the roast beef.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni want it rare and i get it rare and finally we\'re sitting down. and we\'re eating and there\'s some guy that jen says is the ceo of the company, and he?s up speaking through a microphone. and no one is listening and we applaud for him at all the breaks in his speech that are there to allow for applause. and we\'re eating our roast beef.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand jen disappears. she disappears and reappears fifteen minutes later and she\'s holding this picture of her with the biggest fucking parrot you have ever seen. there it is, this parrot, sitting on her shoulder, in the picture. and she\'s says, \'i just had a parrot sitting on my shoulder, look!\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand there it is, in the picture. a giant red and yellow and green parrot, and jen.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand later we are walking around, dug and i. we are walking around after the roast beef and we are talking. loudly. \'dug,\' i would say, \'you know what this reminds me of? the hamptons, summer of 95.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand dug would reply, \'you mean, the year the yale crowd showed up and just made a mess of everything?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i would say, \'yeeees. it\'s always a shame when the lesser of the ivy leagues arrive, acting as though you own them something.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'mmmm,\' dug would nod. \'by the way, have you seen marcos?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'noooooo. but i did run into pierre earlier.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'pierre? haha. is he still wearing the beret? haha.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'mmmmmm.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'how droll.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'mmmmmm.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand then we got sushi.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand the sushi. sushi! there were stations. little bar type things all around the place serving sushi made right before your eyes. and the stations- this is just fucking classic- the sushi bar stations were setup all around the aquarium. so, you know, you could walk around and look at the fish and then eat them. it was great.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nyeah, so. we got sushi. and when we got the sushi, we started to notice the girls.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand the girls were fucking gorgeous. they would never talk to us, the girls. to dug and i. they would never talk to us, but god. they were beautiful.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthis one in particular. this asian girl. short hair kinda like faye from cowboy bebop with some sort of flower behind her ear. god. wearing this black dress thing with straps that crisscrossed in the back. looking all sophisticated and like the temptress. god.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand every time we would pass each other we would make eye contact and share a smile and then she would be gone. and, oh. my. god.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nother things from the party:</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthey had cat girls! they had girls that they paid to dress up like cats and run around and play with the partygoers. we actually got a picture with them. this picture already has a spot waiting for it on our mantle.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthey had giant snakes! they had these two guys with this giant snake who walked around, and who would allow you to put the snake around your neck as to create an excellent photo opp. jen got a picture with the snake as well. the parrot first. later the snake.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthey had free drinks! free drinks. they had free drinks and a dj rig and a dance floor set up in the african tikii room, allowing the white girls to dance drunkenly to madonna and new order amongst dioramas depicting the mating habits of the gazelle.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand really, that\'s why we left. the food gone and the dancing to the new order. we had had a little too much. so dug and i leave. and dug and i are drunk, off the free drinks. The free drinks and the roast beef.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand we are walking, leaving. the wind starts to pick up and we are waiting for the bus, and we are drunk.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand cold! hands in pockets. breath showing. and i tell dug, screaming, \'daaaaaamn that asian girl was fiiiiiine!\' and dug agrees. and we talk a little more and finally i say, \'you know- i wish i had my walkman with me, cause then i wouldn\'t have to listen to your mouth.\' and dug laughs and says, \'yeah, cause then, i wouldn\'t have to pretend that i was listening to you.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand really, this is our relationship. dug and i. we are boys. we make fun of each other.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand waiting somemore and i start tapping my feet, and i ask dug, \'you wanna see me shuffle off to buffalo?\' \'no.\' \'really?\' \'yeah.\' \'you sure?\' \'yeah.\' \'cause i can!\' \'yeah i know.\' \'no i can! really!\' \'uh huh.\' \'no no, watch.\' \'no.\' \'i\'m gonna do it!\' \'no!\' \'i\'m gonna shuffle of to buffalo! you watch me!\' \'NO!\' ?i?m gonna!?</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand then the bus came.</p>'),(2150,'<p>\r\nHe\'d been walking around with a canned ham in his pocket for 2 days. A small canned ham that fit in his pocket, though this was a coat pocket, and for a pocket it was on the large side. He could fit a dozen donuts in his pockets if he tried, and if no one cared about donuts that leaked cat hair and gray-green lint instead of raspberry jelly. He carried the canned ham because his father was a big fan of the salty hard packed food and he found himself patting the can through his coat at times, as it to comfort it. As if to say \"Not long now, Canned Ham. Not long now!\" From time to time as he walked the streets past the bottom floors of tall buildings he would stick his hand into his pocket to feel the hard surface of the tin, the way the flat sides met up with the crimped edges. The way the end of the tin deepest in the pocket was warm, while near the pocket opening it was cold as a popsicle. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nHis reflection in the shatterproof plate glass windows and revolving doors of the downtown office towers was only a little too wide at the hip on the right side. It could have been a book in his pocket, and no one stopped to wonder if it was. He was right in thinking no one would notice the edge of a tin of ham in this city full of strangers full of thoughts of themselves. It was two weeks to Christmas and everyone was covered in burly jackets and chunky scarves, and weighted with bags and bundles. He liked to keep his hands free though. He didn\'t buy clothes with small pockets.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSouth past the taller of the tall towers, he walked fast but with an even pace. Dodging the trailing scarves of those swimming against his stream, nicking the hip of a skinny woman in clicky heels with his can of ham. She almost lost her balance, skidded twice on one foot in a little hopping dance, and caught one arm on the edge of a Salvation Army Santa bucket, righting herself at the last second but flinging change into the gutter. Catching the blue glint of a Canadian dime before he caught her eye, he let himself imagine the bruise she\'d have later that night but, brought his mind back to the Canadian dime before he became aroused. He rushed ahead, eager to leave behind her sour look and the image of her rubbing her sore right hip with a black leather gloved hand. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe patted his ham and checked his watch as he stepped out of the business district. His father would be home now watching reruns on a lesser channel, puttering around the house, opening cans of soup, deriding the people on the street from the safety of his recliner, banging on the wall at the kids next door. Moving farther south and into the cheaper neighborhoods where the windows are covered with sagging blankets to keep out the cold, he slipped the can of ham out of his pocket for the first time in 48 hours. Bounced its weight back and forth between right and left. Tossed it in the air a few times dropping it once on a slender piece of lawn dangerously close to a pile of yesterday\'s dog shit. Looking around to make sure nobody had seen this horrifying near disaster, he slipped the tin back into his pocket for the last 2 blocks of his walk.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe light was on in the front room and the blue-white flash of the television refracted gently from beneath the edges of the curtains. His father was indeed home, and indeed, he was watching his programs. No doubt sitting in his duct taped recliner, sipping the remains of a past date soup can of oil splotched watery broth. Without hesitating, he pulled the canned ham from his pocket for the last time and set it in the middle of the worn doormat. Then he turned around and walked the way he had come. He thought hard about the Canadian dime, the woman with the bruised hip, his reflection in the revolving doors, the weight of his hands in his empty pockets. He thought of anything to avoid the loneliness of a long walk without a pocket full canned ham for company.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2151,'to me it seemed like<br>\r\nI was chained to the wall<br>\r\nnow I wave goodbye<br>\r\nwhile you\'re pulling me in<br>\r\ninto a faraway glass cage<br>\r\nbut I pretend I\'m somewhere else<br>\r\nand I just keep breathing<br>\r\nno matter how it breaks me<br>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat are you going to do<br>\r\nwhen you just can\'t take it anymore?\r\n<p>\r\nthe sun burns\r\n<br>the stars sail\r\n<br>the wind screams\r\n<br>singing a song'),(2152,'<p>i hate everyone today. i dont care if you did no harm, i hate you too. call it hormones, call it my cycle, call me psychotic, i still hate you, and nothing is going to change that.</p>\r\n<p>my friend christine called me last night, saying, \"Id love if you could come over and look at the space for the show im putting together.\" when is the show? this friday. i see. this means i have to do a site-specific piece in three days, is that right? yes. and this morning at mama\'s royal cafe stve noticed a flyer for a local art show. \"isnt this you?\" my full name printed for posterity on papers in oakland. hold me. </p>\r\n<p>last night my boyfriend babbled to me about some christmas party at his office. i told him to email me the details so that i could plan. he never did that and i sat at chat flapping in the wind for hours, wondering if I should take a 90 minute road trip to wander around town trying to use the force to find him. meanwhile, I was reminded that I volunteered to drive a friend to the airport at the crack of dawn and to knee surgery in the morning tomorrow. </p>\r\n<p>so naturally, Im a bit piqued. no, im not complaining, i make my own bed, thanks, its a free country. i mean, what should i expect from people? that they be organized? im a flake-o-rama, why should i be concerned with anyone else holding up any end of any bargain that wasnt explicitly agreed upon, cant i just be mellow, dude, laid back and lady-like, smile and wait for the director to call me in? </p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm just tired is all, and I want someone to surprise me with an act of godfearing originality, creativity, kindness, something, a gift of spontaneous poetry, something to live for, something to cling to humanity with, something to inspire me, is that so wrong? what was it that jay articulated the other day - \"magnanimous romance\"? is that too much to ask? something other than, \"oh uh im er hrm harm let me pick me butt for a few more minutes and um, yawn. listen to this funny anecdote about something thats going on in the world news. here\'s a link to the onion.\"</p>\r\n<p>Im that crazy freind you have who calls you up with a shrill string of expletives and hangs up and you scratch your head wondering, \"what did i do?\" I had a friend like that. she doesnt call me naymore. i miss her today. i could call her and screech, \"OH. my GAWd. listen to what this bitch did to me.\"</p>\r\n<p> the point is, I have to make some art before friday for my friend, who is taking some art history or curatorial class. and thank god im one of those people who has a fat reserve of ideas that i never follow through on, there is always a bank to draw from. SOmehow, I always find myself at home depot late at night procuring last-minute hardware items that make these dusty lightbulbs a reality.</p>\r\n<p>In the car I listened to several Thee Headcoatees songs on repeat. they are vindictive and sexy. I particularly repeat, \"<a href=\"http://www.eggthings.com/YoullBeSorryNow.mp3\">Youll be Sorry Now</a>\", for love of the poignant harmony at the line, \"FIRST YOU TAKE MY LOVE\". Two other moments of pop harmony that make me tingle inside: Lauryn Hill\'s <i>Everything is Everything</i>, \"flippin in the ghetto on the dirty mattress\", and Liz Phair\'s <i>Perfect World</i>, \"I know the girls/ that live inside your world\".</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2152_1.jpg\" height=\"150\" align=\"left\" border=\"1\">I have previously hated home depot. when i saw dave hickey speak at mills he joked about art installations that could be made entirely from materials you get at home depot. home depot is the pinnacle of tiring consumer experience. things in little bags where you cant reach them, images and names of things where they cannot be found, the things themselves. people who wont talk to you, people who wont help you, people who condescend to you, people who ignore you. long waiting lines to spend lots of money for a very small object, or no object at all. oh yes, its very Duchamp. </p>\r\n<p>arwa and joanne and I call home depot \"Big Ben\" after the scene in National Lampoon\'s European Vacation in which chevy chase in London cant get off the rotary in order to turn, and each time he rotates he points out \"Big Ben\" and \"Parliament\" to his kids. the constant unneccessaary rotation, the repetition. the tiring repetition. we would return to home depot 2, perhaps 3 times in one day, having forgotten or miscalculated the need for various exotic fasteners or drill bits.</p>\r\n<p>I am ashamed to say that I know the layout of the store like the neighborhood I grew up in. I know how to get straight to the section I need, and what I will find there. When my friends and I used to make things, we had only this store to patronize after 6 or 8 PM.</p>\r\n<p>And today, finally the repetition has paid off with the breeze of familiarity i felt for the smell of the place, the dust of it, the rudeness of the lights and the chaos of its classifications. Once inside the flourescent womb, once placated by rough music, I agreed to jump into the void and forget my anger.</p>\r\n<p>Why I make art is simple enough. It is merely one of the by-products of my body, like sweat or skin or blood or shit. there\'s nothing mystical about that. or is there? sometimes its all stuffed inside me and i cant get it out. art constipation. and then i become a very disagreeable person. cranky, you might say. </p>\r\n<p>I grapple with the production of my own fake bits of nature like a builder grappling with the materiality from which he must make a home. I\'ll be stepping on nails and cursing bitches on the way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>ps</b> i like the word \"careen\".</p>'),(2153,'i just finished Lord of the Rings. if anybody remembers my letter to madeline l\'engle, you might guess that i am kind of, not <i>upset</i> really, but, like, nostalgic for something that doesn\'t exist, or i should say something that didn\'t exist. so here\'s what i\'m going to do.\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m going to believe in Tolkien\'s version of history from now on. not pretend to believe, but believe. there were dwarves, orcs, wizards, magic, and elves. no one can change my mind, this is it for me, i\'ve finally found a metaphysic that works. i can analyze The Source\'s books letter by letter, and translate the words into a numerical code-matrix, where all the major events of the last ten thousand years can be found just by skipping a certain number of letters at a time, like every sixth, every fourteenth, and so on. i don\'t have to pray, i don\'t have to go anywhere, and i don\'t have to wear a special hat. \r\n<p>\r\nwhen you think about it, it\'s just as good as anything else.'),(2154,'<pre>\r\nI am a man who fingers \r\nthe lapels of sport coats for quality,\r\nwho squeezes tomatoes \r\nand thumps melons.\r\n\r\nI take new books off the  shelf \r\nand page through them, counting for\r\npagination inconsistencies.\r\n\r\nMy knees crackle like Rice Krispies\r\nwhen I stand up from a crouch.\r\n\r\nI inspect windshields for fractures\r\nand study menâ€™s hairlines on the subway\r\nin search of incipient baldness.\r\n\r\nI point out grammatical errors\r\nin grocery store banners\r\nand notice when a magazine photograph\r\nis slightly out of focus.\r\n\r\nThere is a small clump of gray hairs at my left temple \r\nand sometimes\r\na sudden pain in my right hip causes a limp.\r\n\r\nI have three sport coats of dubious quality.\r\nbut most people donâ€™t\r\nnotice, or care.\r\n\r\nI listen to conversations\r\nand pick apart the words\r\nto peer at the meanings underneath\r\nSo that, if weâ€™ve talked, I know you \r\nBetter than you think I do.\r\n\r\nThere are people I wish I didnâ€™t know,\r\nthat bore me, that have essentially nothing to say, \r\nbut are insistent with their vacuity. \r\n\r\nI am tempted name these people here:\r\ninclude addresses and phone numbers,\r\nto openly insult them and give you the opportunity to insult them as well.\r\nIf I did this,\r\nMaybe theyâ€™d realize my distaste.\r\n\r\nBut a poem is no place\r\nfor such pettiness. \r\n</pre>'),(2155,''),(2156,'<p>\r\nquitting my job was for the most part due to personal reasons. I wasn\'t happy there. Working conditions were cold and physically strained.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni am going to be frank when i say i live a very easy life. i freeload and while it does bug me to a certain degree, i have been so afraid to follow through with ideas and assertions that living off the money of other people seemed easier. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is exactly what happened to my uncle. perhaps the only difference between him and me, is that while he wasnt doing anything with his life he was doing hard drugs and banging chicks. through that he attempted marriage and had a kid. all things considered he didn\'t fuck up the people around him as much as he could have. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am quiet and reserved, yet those who know me cant see that. i DO open up to people and i do have things to say and yet when it comes to using those ideas and thoughts in a practical manner im at a complete loss. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas soon as i am confronted with any sort of real and tangable continuation of a plan, that plan is what i now like to call \"negatavized\". there is a steady path towards humility.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas comfy as my life is, that life is going to be completely gone forever if I dont get my head out of my ass soon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI currently have four types of debt. I Have a student loan. I have a credit card. I have a line of credit. I am overdrawn. I am not sixteen and these debts wont disappear. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy father has been a millionaire four or five times. he has also filed for personal bankrupcy four of five times. for this reason i refuse to take that course of action.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni take full responsibility for my fiscal error. I take full responsibility for procurment of funds for unjust cause. These were all conscious decisions that i made. I knew full well what I was doing, and so I am keen on trying make things right. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou see i respect and appreciate the people in my family regardless of their faults and fuck-ups. they are alright. i have good talks and times with them all. yet they all do things that hurt me in ways id rather not describe. nothing illegal. just pressures and ideas of direction. Ideas of lifestyle and ideas of an older society. Ideas im sure all of you can imagine...if not just fill in the blanks. nevertheless when you dig through all the rhetoric and bullshit, you unearth a fundamental idea, and when that is realized you expect them to look at you and say \"i told you so\".\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2157,'<p>It\'s midnight and a spotlight hits the clear green of a beer bottle the last band left sitting on an unplugged amp, beating the glare back into my eyes where I stand in front of you, there, on the stage. And you\'re singing. I have to close my eyes so I wont fall in love with you and your voice before the end of the song. I have to close my eyes because of the smoke and bourbon fumes. When I open them again yours are closed, so I scan the drummer, the bassist. They\'re eyes are closed too and they are rocking their little hearts out. And then it\'s your turn again. I catch your eye. To you I\'m just a girl in the audience. You have no idea who I am. It doesn\'t matter, because I\'m only there for your songs and a few bottles of beer. Mine come in brown glass. What\'s the difference? No difference. Green bottles, brown bottles, glasses full if ice and clear liquor. It\'s all the same.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I used to have a thing for musicians. Not by choice. They seemed to come at me like metal filings to a magnet, and who was I to say, \"No\" to someone with the potential to write me songs and knock me down with serenades? Who was I to say, \"No\" to all those someones? And for years I waited for my song and my serenade. For years. And I\'m still waiting. I am very patient. Even so, I\'ve been losing interest in possibilities.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Certainties. A hat, something to hang your hat on, clear words from your own mouth. Certainties are more desirable, easier to live with. I\'ve been writing my own songs. Quiet serenades played with cold fingers to the girl in the faded, under-stuffed easy chair. That\'s not to say I was never knocked down. Once I was hit so hard I couldn\'t get up for months. I might still be lying there, but I wipe my brow, I laugh too loud with the relief that he finally went away and left me for dead. I got up, and everything is fine. Maybe I laugh too loud or too often, but everything is fine. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>You swirl the ice in your glass with a short black straw, too narrow to sip through. It picks up that spotlight, turns the ice and gin into an iridescent cylindrical winter forest fire. And you know what that feels like. Hot and cold together tempting disaster. The very hot and the very cold together rushing at odd angles and creating chaos in a confined place. The avoidance of lukewarm. Anything but tepidness. I close my eyes again and listen to your voice, let the gin-bourbon bouquet fill up my sinuses and mingle with second hand Camel Lights. This day and yesterday. You and someone else. What\'s the difference? No difference. Everything is fine. Itâ€™s all the same.</p>\r\n'),(2158,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2158_1.gif\"><br><br>\r\nThe summer of 2000 was hot; air like lava smothered the city so heavily that you could virtually see the waves of boiling humidity roll up out of the harbor and lap over the crosstown streets askew, over the parks, drowning brownstones, filling the air with steamy breaths, with a symphony of air-conditioner clatter, coating windows with an ironically frosty-looking web of condensation.<br><br>\r\nI moved into some non-descript brownstone in Bay Ridge, finding my haven from the Manhattan rents of economic upswing in central Brooklyn, an hour\'s ride on the B train from your place, of course, but in a part of town ceaselessly bathed in the amber tones of the setting sun, the summertime breezes nonchalantly flowing with hissy salsa music and the smell of the fish market.<br><br>\r\nI was so proud that first day, when you braved the non-sequitur world outside your Upper West Side, rolled the rails and graced my move-in efforts with your overseeing grey eyes, the two of us sweating out saline pints while wrestling my life up the stairs, out of boxes, out of a tightly compressed car, you, inserting hints of yourself amidst my possessions, some clothes, some toiletries, teasers for me to conjure your presence.  We would be such failures as refugees, you giggled.<br><br>\r\nThat day was golden. Each day in Brooklyn was, the sun seeming to trace the perfect parallels of the long avenues to the east, the stark green girders of the Gowanus, winking into the traffic shadows below.  Dank to the core with humidity, we slunk up 5th avenue, deleriously smiling on the sight of the first air conditioned diner. \"This should be <i>our place</i>\", we agreed, over tepid coffee, with syncopated nods.<br><br>\r\n\"Lets buy an air conditioner\", you said. \"Or, we could just come here everytime we get too hot\", I laughed, as we settled further into our brown vinyl booth, immediately in front of the giant cooling unit.  We shared smiles over our $3 breakfast, sweaty hand-holding; the adventure of a strange new place, a maturing bond, an anchored connection, the joy of making love in uncharted new territories, the new world across the East River.<br><br>\r\nBut we never went back to our diner.  The days grew shorter as the weather cooled, and the train rides grew longer.  Often were the days I\'d walk past the place and look in through its dulled windows. I\'d see us, seeing endless reflections of your summertime smile in the window, remembering how my eyes burned in the ochre glow of your tightly stretched summer skin; seeing the kaliedoscope of possibilities, the hopes in our eyes, feeling the same goosebumps that were raised on my flesh somehow on that scorching summer day.<br><br>\r\nMy Brooklyn life has come and gone, fleeting and forgotten now; I\'ve found my tired niche back on the Upper West Side where the rent is higher, but the trains run faster, where tall apartment buildings obstruct the rising sun, block its setting, cast shadows incongruous with the parallel avenues and rectilinear cross-streets.<br><br>\r\nThis neighborhood has no soul, no diners, no shiny eyes, no hope.  Even though this neighborhood has you, our connection has gone dead, the hopes as forgotten as is the summer heat when the first snow falls.<br><br>\r\nSometimes, at night, I dream about that diner. Every night I dream of you, always waking, always gasping in the emptiness nestled next to me.'),(2159,'So much for my anonymity.  I guess this means if anyone is bored they can call me at work. (extension 123)'),(2160,'<p>Ich lebe mein Leben in wachsenden Ringen,<br>\r\ndie sich Ã¼ber die Dinge ziehn.<br>\r\nIch werde den letzten wohl nicht vollbringen<br>\r\naber versuchen will ich ihn.<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ich kreise um Gott, den uralten Turm,<br>\r\nund ich kreise jahrtausendelang;<br>\r\nund ich weiÃŸ noch nicht: bin ich ein Falke, ein Sturm<br>\r\noder ein groÃŸer Gesang.<br></p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2160_1.pjpeg\">\r\n<p>i walked a labyrinth in honor of my grandmother\'s death.  around and around we walked, silently, in the cool springtime sun. every step moving closer to the center, even as you turn your back and appear to be moving away.  a weaving together of souls as we walked, some quickly, some barely shuffling along, some fairly dancing, all of us meeting knowing eyes as we passed one another.  grief was with me, settled into my chest like black water, sloshing around in there, smoothing rough edges.  but under the blue sky my heart felt strangely light, my feet solid, tracing the steps i knew my grandmother\'s had traced many many times.  toward the end of her life she stopped being able to walk, so her daughters brought her a wooden finger labyrinth, carved spiral grooves in smooth wood.  she would sit in her hospital bed with her eyes closed and gently run her hands around and around the pattern.  she found grace before she died.  she would repeat the 23rd psalm over and over.  the lord is my shepherd, i shall not want.  he maketh me to lie down in green pastures.  he leadeth me beside still waters.  he restoreth my soul.  i shall not want.  i miss my grandmother.  she was a woman who knew romantic poems by heart -- keats, byron, shelley, longfellow, yeats -- and would nonchalantly quote shakespeare, working it into her children and grandchildren like oil into fine wood.  her fascination with the labyrinth as she neared death reminded me of this poem by rilke (my translation):</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i live my life in widening rings <br>\r\nwhich stretch themselves far as the eye<br>\r\nand while the last one may be out of my reach<br>\r\ni am certainly willing to try.<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i circle round god, round the tall ancient tower<br>\r\ncircle a thousand years long<br>\r\ni still do not know: am i falcon, or storm,<br>\r\n or a magnificent song? <br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>and life continues to move in circles, as is apparently life\'s wont.  there is a recurring theme, a fugue, iterations within subtle hints, correspondence, corollaries.  there is a purpose here, i feel its momentum building, feel it readying for its closeup, for a more central place on the stage.  there is a pattern being woven, mysterious and invisible but creating a web all the same which catches me at the strangest times.  going back in time to when my great grandmother was my age, and opening up my sensorium to the myriad possibilities for ways of seeing  that were so suddenly, synchronously offered...was just what i needed.  the world is new today.  on my winding way to the center i have taken a turn which clearly shows the true direction of the path.  toward transformation, wholeness, toward integration of opposites.  back to where i am from.  back to where i am.   </p>  \r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2160_2.pjpeg\"> \r\n<br>\r\nthank you.<br>\r\ne'),(2161,'<p>A building stares blankly across an empty road at another building. The other building stares back.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Columns and rows of cold glass eyes look outwardly, unblinking. It is quiet. The buildings are as content as buildings will allow their rigid awareness to bend.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Falling feet no longer litter the corridors of the city with unclean sound. Cars do not call out, lights do not hum, winds do not move.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The buildings stand solidly by. The air has a foreign flavor. The breath of the buildings is the source of this flavor. The buildings are breathing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They are breathing the air of progression, of change.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.inconduit.com/words/bm_frame/s_frame.html\">Anticipation</a>.</p>\r\n'),(2162,'<P>i sent out email to my family regarding the holidays: when i was leaving, when to expect me.  an hour later sitting in traffic my dad called.  he only calls when i have not called him in over two weeks.  lately i have been very busy.  normally i call my parents every weekend.  but last weekend my phone was missing in action.  during the week when i get the chance to call, or think of calling, it is usually around nine in the evening which is too late to be calling texas.  people are asleep because of the time difference of two hours.\r\n</P><P>\r\ndad said that he has not seen me in a year and at this rate he will only see me twenty or so more times in his life.  amy\\\'s dad told her the same thing last time he saw her.  i think that moves the instance into the class of perspective.  the perspective of the father.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni feel guilty for not being near my dad.  i am not allowed to say i love him more than anything in the world.  there are so many people and things to love.  i cant pick favorites.  i am tempted to make that conclusion though.  when he told me thathe would only see me 20 or so times before the average age of death for an adult male, i noted it.  when i got to egg\\\'s i looked on the internet for jobs in houston, texas.  just checking.  just checking for computer jobs in texas.  there really is nothing web oriented.  i would have to put on my suit and go door to door selling myself to marketing companies.  there are lots of entry level marketing jobs, business jobs.  that seems so foriegn to me.  i think i went into programming so that i could make more than minimum wage and avoid the business and marketing jobs.  i imagined myself working with a team of marketing people.  sitting in a meeting.  sitting at my desk.  trying to work, but not using the computer.  all i have done is work on computers for the last few years.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthen i think about living at my dad\\\'s house.  how it would go alright, but i would get depressed not having a social life.  i would become a lulu character.  i would dress up in izods and drive fourty mintues to work where everyone has tightly clipped hair cuts and wears perfume and cologne.\r\n</P><P>\r\nme and dad would go motorcycling on the weekends.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthat is very sappy.  i miss him.  im happy here.  i think i would be happy in many other places too.  but i dont think i would be happy in texas right now.\r\n</P><P>\r\nim still in my get out and see the world phase.  defining that phase is not an article i am doing today.\r\n</P>'),(2163,'<p>Early this morning, I was contemplating the world outside.</p>\r\n<p>While sipping my tea and observing the silent, snow-covered tress.</p>\r\n<p>The noises from the ground were crunchy and cold.</p>\r\n<p>If you held your breath, you could feel the air trying to float you away.</p>\r\n<p>I listened to the most perfect music and I thought of home.</p>\r\n<p>The world was so beautiful, completely buried in snow.</p>\r\n<p>It nearly hurt to look at with both eyes open.</p>\r\n'),(2164,'<p>SF, LA, NY, and the whole apple</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Little pink umbrellas<br>\r\nAnd there we are zealous,<br>\r\nFor all the attention<br>\r\nSmiling calmly.<br>\r\nEast of tension.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fingering our hair<br>\r\nWalking here and there<br>\r\nWe are neuroses <br>\r\nWe like it that way.<br>\r\nWe have psychoses,<br>\r\nWe donâ€™t know what to say.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Tight like my finger around <br>\r\nA circus wheel, I seem to feel<br>\r\nThe wheel bearings move<br>\r\nInside like clockwork<br>\r\nIs there a way to prove,<br>\r\nThat all people have one quirk?<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n<p>Say it down, say it low, <br>\r\ncome on, understand this flow, <br>\r\nI pray for strong words to punctuate the silent screams,<br>\r\nI hope to wake you from your attachment to liquid like dreams,<br>\r\nLife is one river you ride real and bravely, <br>\r\nitâ€™s the culmination, the epitome.<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Grave like flowers <br>\r\nEarnest for hours<br>\r\nHe gravitates <br>\r\nTo phenomenal heights,<br>\r\nSmiling down then, <br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>He is weightless<br>\r\nHe gravitates, <br>\r\nQuickly to earth.<br>\r\nNow engaged like dust <br>\r\nas he was at birth.<br>\r\nSmile down now, like some disgusting angel.<br>\r\nCome on, come on, <br>\r\nsee this from more than one angle,<br>\r\nWe are fragile, we are out of control,<br>\r\nSilence the dumb-box, as well as the radio,<br>\r\nConfide my heart, to more than one bird,<br>\r\nCome on listen, the wind speaks, I have heard.<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Say it down, say it low, <br>\r\ncome on, understand my flow <br>\r\nI pray for strong words to punctuate the silent screams,<br>\r\nI hope to wake you from your attachment to liquid like dreams,<br>\r\nLife is one river going along quickly,<br>\r\nitâ€™s the culmination, the epitome.<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>We drop down hands, invade foreign lands,<br>\r\nQuestion motives, Explain away derivatives,<br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>So here we are, arrogant little men,<br>\r\nPraying for little fruits, desperate for roots,<br>\r\nWe fear eroding away,<br>\r\nsigh, sigh, sigh,<br>\r\nMen, lie, lie, lie,<br>\r\ngoodbye sweet friend,<br>\r\nWhen there will be an end?<br>\r\nWhen the men say so.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Say it down, say it low,<br>\r\nlife is one river that can flow.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n '),(2165,'<p>Today had a flat surface like the fresh sweep of the sidewalk after a rain. Today was all flat surfaces and distances between points. The subtle lack of emotion in the places that aren\'t deeper than just beneath the surface. I know I wanted to hear your voice, but I don\'t know what it is I would have wanted you to say. Today I wanted things without knowing why or even exactly what things they were. It was a day for the edges of edges, the details of details.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There are some things that sound better when they aren\'t said at all. When I was only 8 or 9 I was worried about the obvious waste of spoken language. Tormented by the way people spoke unnecessarily. I agonized over what would happen if we used up all the words. I wondered if concepts would become more fragile, less exceptional, meanings become dull, ideas dark and worn from overuse. In all the clutter of the mundane small talk, would anyone still see the shine of worthwhile conversation, the glow of intelligence, the beat of emotion available in the mouths of the few and the lucky? I silently pleaded with my mother to stop the flow from her throat, imagining my hands into position over my ears. I was crushed by the ease with which so many people, everyone, let their mouths race like a river thick with winter run off. I thought if I could save myself from coffee table chit-chat, it might leave me with the ability to recognize more meaningful discourse. And now I find myself guilty of the very same mediocre tongue flapping bullshit, babbling almost incoherently and tarnishing meanings as much as the people who used to make me so uneasy. I can only frown and shake my head and go suddenly quiet. Become the unspeaking eye in a storm of words. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can hear the slice of the cars on the wet pavement. And I think maybe itâ€™s not too late. There is still time enough to study the flat surface and find the edges of edges, time to watch for the details of details, to listen to the things that aren\'t said at all.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2166,'<p>\r\nit finally became clear last night. i feel compassion for inanimate objects.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlast week someone left a PC on the grass. i named it \"abandoned technology\". today abandoned technology was missing; probably picked up by the trash collectors. i felt a small twinge of sadness.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am convinced that abandoned technology had discovered a purpose for itself, as it sat on the grass, around the corner from nirvana; waiting, unknowingly, for its uncertain demise. abandoned technology had a soul, a mission, and even its own will. now unplugged and free from the clacking of human fingers, abandoned technology, through either inanimate will or boredom (but not through hallucination of a passing tourist) chose its mission: to observe and catalogue the shoes of passing children.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnoting that children\'s shoe fashion had become increasingly outlandish in recent months, abandoned technology took it upon itself to notice each pair of tiny passing feet. abandoned technology groaned. open-toed platform keds, glitter baby gap tennis clubwear, oshkosh bgosh \'boarding boots for baby, pooh bear pumps, and, the most insidious of the lot: birkie jr\'s. \"to live at foot level\", mused abandoned technology, \"means to understand the full horror of image conscious parenting\". and in that moment, abandoned technology vowed to never produce children.\r\n</p><p>\r\nabandoned technology passed the days counting, observing, and cataloguing shoes. and musing. so much musing. so much musing, in fact, that it attracted the attention of a passing tourist. the passing tourist would have daily conversations (silently, of course, because inanimate objects don\'t have mouths or vocal cords) with abandoned technology. the two became friends.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe passing tourist would see abandoned technology twice each day. the first time, on the way to meal; the second time, on the way back from meal. some days, the passing tourist would go a different way. on those days, abandoned technology would miss the passing tourist. but on other days, the conversation would go much like this:\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"how are you?\"\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"quite well, thanks. and you? how\'s the wife?\"\r\n</p><p>\r\n\"the same. lovely as always, as you can see. how have the shoes been, hmm?\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nand so forth.\r\n</p><p>\r\non the days when the passing tourist would go a different way, he would long for the silent conversations with his unlikely discarded friend. he would wonder to himself how his friend was doing. he felt that the life of abandoned technology was, perhaps, lonely. the passing tourist would them berate himself for leaving his friend without the daily attention. and, later that night, he would resolve to pass by his lonely friend in the grass, by walking the usual way the next afternoon. this resolution would comfort the tourist, allowing him to sleep better at night, knowing that he could bring friendship and warm conversation to his new unlikely (and silent) friend.\r\n</p><p>\r\none morning, abandoned technology was gone.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen objects are discarded, i wonder if they wanted to be left alone, or if they were happier to live in service. kept as slaves, or loving pets. i will never know. this is why i feel bad for discarded objects.\r\n</p>'),(2167,'I\'ve been thinking about boys lately. Boys for me, or just the opposite sex for heterosexuals, and everyone for people who are bi, which must be terribly confusing. Let\'s move on though.<br><br>\r\nIn my collective experiences, I have learned that there is no hope for humanity. In no startling turn of events, I\'ve noticed a pattern in my life. And this pattern entails feeling alienated and alone. You don\'t need some sort of experience to bring out these feelings, although I\'m sure it doesn\'t hurt. I know that the girls that I talk to would mainly agree. But people are going to look at this and wonder, \"who jilted you?\"<br><br>\r\nLet me tell you, it isn\'t one. It\'s all of them. Right now, I\'ve never been farther from having a caring bond with someone who feels as committed to me as I to them. I am jealous of those who can find this. What is their secret? I suspect they have amnesia, are addicted to massive amounts of mind altering drugs, and are probably just generally good at optimism...<br><br>\r\nSomewhere along the way, I began to decide what I wanted. Sometimes, I come very close. I allow caring and trust to enter in the relationships I have. Usually what happens is I do this and the person is entirely not who I thought they were. Then, I fall apart for a while. I rely on my friends to pick up the pieces of my shattered hope, and then I find massive flaws in all of the trait in boy number X and I try to meet a new mr. X so as to narrow down the ever decreasing field of possible candidate for the recieval of my love.<br><br>\r\nLove is a touchy relationship issue. I suppose this is because everyone is afraid of being in love, or being loved and not have full reciprocation. In general I think fear accounts for 98% of what\'s wrong with humanity. In this case, ifear just sticks everyone in mental retardation mode. Everyone wanders around with this little pile of goo locked in their chest screaming bloody murder if anyone suggests that it\'s love. I have loved a few boys in my day. Coming to terms with this, in that none of them have stuck around long enough to really know it, is hard. I love a lot of people I\'m close to much more than I ever loved those boys though. Including those boys. If you\'re my close friend, if I trust you, if we meet on the street and accidentally touch hands while you pull me back from wandering into a car, and I look in eyes and see you there, then I think that is love. Of course, one second of love is amazing. Finding someone you can tolerate, and grow to love more deeply than a thousand ponderings, that makes you hurt to feel it, that is so intense that it\'s hate, that can make you cry or never let you cry is just. .. a dream.<br><br>\r\nI wonder if people who get married now think about these things. I wonder why people get married. I wonder so much about how things work, that it is obvious that I am a beginner.<br><br>\r\nIs it the best love the first time you feel it, when you\'re drowning in it? Or is it better when it slowly creeps its way along and drags you beneath the surface...<br><br>\r\nBut why am I sidetracked with love. I\'m trying to complain about all the stupid foolish things people have done to me in the past. Including picking the absolute worst timing, being completely ignorant, being anonymous, leaving, not understanding, and giving promises, trying to learn all about me in order to shut me down. Tearing me inside out.<br><br>\r\nTearing me inside out, and then making it up to me, and then tearing me inside out again, when the wounds have just got that fresh pink scar.<br><br>\r\nI can\'t even explain how it works anymore, all I know is that it\'s broken. Who the hell fixes this shit anyway?<br><br>\r\nAnd don\'t even get me started on the ones who are attracted to me, and who think I am something more than a pretty face. Let me tell you, that in my experiences, I am a pretty face. My face is a moderate one. It\'s slightly squarish, it includes blue-grey eyes, and a small square ended nose, with a line down the middle which sometimes looks like a bruise. I have averagely placed cheekbones, and a small mouth with small lips, and a small chin. My skin isn\'t totally clear, but it\'s pretty good at faking it most of the time. I don\'t wear make up for more than one day a week, but usually even less than that. That is part of who I am, When people ask if I am more than a pretty face, I am going to lie from now on, and say no.<br><br>\r\nMy biggest complaint, is that they always want to know you, and then when you show them who they\'re looking at, beneath that face, they can\'t see it, and they\'re wrong all over, and they think you aren\'t who you are. Or they see you, and then they run away.<br><br>\r\nWhen they come back, they\'ve forgotten. And there you are, with that fear again. And there you are.<br><br>\r\nI always tried not to be just my face. I hated my face. I used to have such a pretty face when I was young, that everyone I know thought was horrible. Covered with wide large glasses, and marred by strange teeth, and totally unwise to the ways of make up and totally precarious. When I was really a beautiful girl, they wouldn\'t give me the time of day.<br><br>\r\nI knew everyone\'s name. I cared about a lot of things. I cared about everyone else. I cared about myself, and I wanted to make things better, and I wanted to fight for the world and I wanted it all so badly that I lost myself dreaming.<br><br>\r\nWho wants to fight now. The only people fighting are the ones who want to be at the top. My world does not lay in a line. I want to see things, and I want to capture the world and take it as my own, and enjoy it in my soul and love it like it could give a damn. I am never going to be at the top. (if I get up to where they say the top is, I\'ll look at the view, and then leave).<br><br>\r\nSo. We\'ve now established that I am a hopeless dreamer who is secretly more than a pretty face, who has let people interrupt the world to try for a lasting bond, but has been let down, and pushed away.<br><br>\r\nWhy do I want more of this intolerable thing that people are so attracted to, that people seem to think is the part of life worth living. How do you make you love and passion into a family. How do you turn the one you love into the one you can always love, and how do you do it when you realize that you don\'t change them, they change you?<br><br>\r\nAt the moment, my spirit is broken, and my fear is large and looming. I have a plea. It\'s for myself, and no one else. Not foe those boys, the ones who only get close so they can push you away, ( too many times.. ) it\'s for myself, and for my friends, who I know are listening.. Don\'t settle for less than what you are. Don\'t lower yourself to meet their expectations.<br><br>\r\nThey don\'t know what\'s behind your pretty face.<br><br>\r\n'),(2168,'<p>\r\nOne of the networks (among the three television stations I receive through the air, this is just the right amount of television for me I think) was playing <a href=\"http://www.titanicmovie.com/\">Titanic</a> over the past two nights.  I\'ve always felt a special fondness for the RMS Titanic - I read <i>A Night to Remember</i> at a young and impressionsable age, after somehow discovering that she <a href=\"http://www.discovery.com/stories/science/sciencetitanic/220am.html\">slipped underneath the waves</a> the morning of 15 April, 1912 - exactly 61 years to the day before I was born.  Reading that led to reading a whole spate of books about disasters at sea.  A compendium of stories about ships lost on the Great Lakes, a number of modern tales of life raft survival after the sinking of small pleasure craft, one about the Andrea Doria.  I  was ten maybe, and somewhat dour taste in books I suppose.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn one of the last scenes in the movie, there\'s a series of smooth transitions from underwater camera shots, into the luminous but still a bit overly smooth looking CG model, proceeding then into a real set: the main staircase, where the two protagonists are about to be married.  That\'s the scene that did it for me the first time, and it did it for me again last night.  It has as much to do with the graphics as the romance; it\'s a good enough story, but Di Caprio\'s performance doesn\'t quite do it for me for some reason.  But the graphics do, perhaps because they reflect the audacity of the whole project.  It\'s a really big, ridiculous movie, and I enjoy it on levels that have little to do with art.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m a little extra mushy right now, due to focused dosages of relationship angst.  Two relationships that I thought were quite clearly and simply and easily evolving towards the actual spending of more time together simply aren\'t.  What is the point of dating 2 or 2.5 or 3 or however many people are happening to squeeze even vaguely reciprocal feelings for me today into their busy schedules if i still can\'t get a date for New Year\'s?  Fooey on being busy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOr, fooey on making relationships work between two moving targets.  I used to be \"busy,\" maybe I will be again.  I\'m considering putting on indefinite hold any future plans for emotional growth that might result in priority changes.  I\'ll be nice and stationary, I\'ll just sit here in my nice little cowtown and keep my same job and quit reading and maybe move to a slightly bigger place and get a nice hobby or two and that will be it.  I\'ll be a known quantity.  Someone wants to come like me and spend time with me, they\'ll know what they\'re getting, <i>ad infinitum</i>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m not sure I can quite complete the circle of the analogy here.  Something something foundering relationships something.  I\'m not sure they really are foundering.  Maybe they\'ll just have to be towed back to port and patched up somehow.  Or scuttled, dynamited so as to prevent them from becoming hazards to further navigation.\r\n</p>'),(2169,'<p>I got this email a few days ago:</p>\r\n\r\n<blockquote><FONT FACE=\"Courier New\" COLOR=\"#990066\"><pre>\r\nyeah i know i know i made no sense whatsoever\r\n\r\nso i had another nervousy type breakdown last night\r\n\r\nwatched BUFFALO 66 with Michael at 4 in the morning\r\n\r\ncouldnt go to sleep and I did my reeeEE-port and all\r\nstill\r\n\r\nI have some tights that haVE HOLES  where the feet go\r\nso i have no socks and these shoes always make my feet stink\r\nitch\r\n\r\ni shook my proff\'s hand\r\n\r\nit was good\r\nshe deserved it\r\n\r\nMike asked me if I was wearing a bra...\r\nI took it as an insult.......a flat thing\r\n\r\nhe said he was wondering if that was my usual perkiness or a bra\r\n\r\nlately i have been insecure.\r\nabout my relationship\r\n\r\ni did an oil pastel and he looked at it for a minute...\r\n\r\ni wanted at least 4\r\nthe arch of my right foot itches real bad\r\n\r\ni need somebody to kiss the arch of my right foot\r\n\r\npost this on swinney under Velourian Root\r\nemail TATANIASHAKES@UNDERWRITERS.COM\r\n\r\nthanks you\'re a bud\r\n\r\n</blockquote></FONT></pre>\r\n\r\n<p>The subject of the email was <FONT FACE=\"Courier New\" COLOR=\"#990066\">Re: Re: 771 </font>.</p><p>Originally, I wasn\'t going to comply with Miss Root\'s request, but.... I don\'t know.  The fancy just struck me.  Also, her whining to me about it Saturday night on the phone might have been a factor.</p><p>Anyways, I haven\'t posted anything in quite a while.  I\'ve been really low on inspiration, so I\'ll steal someone else\'s and put my name on it.</p><p>There you go bud.</p><P>btw, I. am. a. fat. Elvis.</p>'),(2170,'<p>So I finally quit/got fired(?) at my ass job at the bookstore. It was a good job at first, with a quiet store, nice people-- the owner was a little crazy, but it was okay. At first. Then came the strange requests that had nothing to do with my job. The calls from him on my days off from jail. The repeated efforts at explaining away the restraining order his wife put on him. Listening to his pathetic life story for the fortieth time, again.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo it came to an ugly climax on friday, while I was shelving books. I was tired of listening to him blame Islamic religion because his Islamic wife left him, probably because she couldn\'t stand him anymore. I flat out told him I didn\'t want to discuss it, \"it\" being the subject of whether Islam was evil. I was just sick of it. SICK. So I kept saying, No, I don\'t want to discuss this right now. No, I don\'t want to talk about it. Eventually I reached through his self-involved miasma and he left the store in a huff. That night, I got a voicemail saying that I was fired. Nice, huh?\r\n</p><p>\r\nHe just fired me on Friday with a vague promise that I could be kept on for a week if I wanted to. Well, I didn\'t want to. After being fired through voicemail, I wasn\'t in any mood to do him any favors. Then! After I drop off his key and borrowed books, he has the gall (!!) to leave me more voicemail cursing me out for not showing up for work. Look, bozo, you don\'t get brownie points for being stupid, so stop trying so hard.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThis is the same man who tried to get me to buff his hardwood floors in addition to working as a cashier. This is the man who called me from jail on my day off to weep for 2 hours because he got arrested for wife beating. This is the man who will not stop talking about himself, all the time, to whomever is closest. He doesn\'t let the fact that you have already heard the story a million times deter him.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIt\'s laughable. If you want to see my Dear Asshole letter, that I gave him instead of having to hear his voice one more time, <a href=\"http://www.weeb.com/asinine/\">click here.</a>\r\n</p><p>\r\nThank the fucking gods in heaven I never have to deal with him again. Man, I can\'t believe what I had to put up with for 8 dollars an hour! Why is it that the more you have to put up with, the less you get paid?\r\n</p><p>\r\nSheeeuit.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n '),(2171,'<p>\r\nin grade eight my teacher mr swanek taught the class lord of the rings. it was really fun. we made huge photocopies of the middle earth maps and coloured them. the books really got us going. we were a bunch of pretty rambunctious kids and we had a reputation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat was ten years ago\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis past weekend me and my old gradeschool/highschool friends had a reunion of sorts at my friend marks house. for many of us, it was the first time seeing each other in over five years. we got seriously drunk and beat the shit out of a wendies drive through because they wouldnt serve our carless asses. during that night conversation got going on the new lord of the rings movie and our grade eight class. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsomewhere in all of that we came up with a great idea. we dusted off the phone book and gave mr swanek a call. it was quarter to twelve at night if i remember correctly, and we ended up leaving a msg, with a number where he could get ahold of one of us. he called kevin the next day.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis morning i woke up to find an email in my inbox telling me that tonight was the night. the night we gathered as one again. as a good part of the grade eight class; including teacher.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe met up before hand at a place called montana\'s and had a few drinks and caught up. i really did feel young and very old all at once. it was a strange feeling, but a good feeling nonetheless. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe movie ended and we parted ways. a good good night for unemployed me. it did the body good. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2172,'My parents have cut down the tree that I had a fort at the base of.  That was the tree that I climbed all three storeys of when I was 16 in order to rescue my kitten.  That was the tree that dominated the view from our lounge window for 25 years.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnd now, on top of the stump of the tree that was is a silver cable car track.  That\'s right, we now have our very own ride.  My mother\'s excuse for spending $30,000 installing it was that she\'s tired of lugging 80kg gas bottles for her kiln up the steps, but i think they\'re just lazy. It seems so ridiculous to get a ride up our admittedly very steep garden path.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nStill, if you\'re ever in Wellington, you should come over and I\'ll punch in the pin number and let you have a ride.  '),(2173,'city i love you<br>\r\ncity rockers, feel for you<br>\r\nimmensely i sigh<br><br>\r\n\r\nagitprop lust for<br>\r\na city-life boy who might<br>\r\nnever hope to know<br><br>\r\n\r\nor love, between sighs<br>\r\nface nestled between my breasts<br>\r\ncity i love you<br>'),(2174,'                                                                     \r\n<p>stream</p>\r\n	\r\n<p>of mind flowing out and about onto tapping finger moves along the grey keyboard thing that lays in front of my weathered tired faceless mass of possibilities- only expecting something brilliant out of nothing riding away into the setting darkness of youth past by and now replaced by more knowledge bigger, better ways to see the same things over againâ€¦ driving toward existential peace wrapped up in tightly unified, codified character roles specified by and dictated by the very life itself that passes as we move between moments in time and wonderâ€¦ how on earth or not earth did we find ourselves in this place this time and singing always helps to move the darkness away so we all do it in different ways but we all do it- stopping when the idea must move- breaking habits and shaking out old forms and constructs, good fucks inside the mind but much less pleasantly described in real time where the variables swim in multiple directions and idealism gets washed away by the flow of complicationsâ€¦</p>\r\n	\r\n<p>outside the walls we cry up to someone or something on the other side, the high side where we canâ€™t see everything thatâ€™s going on but we feel it in a way that makes us want to collapse down into a position of submission and cry about times when we lost something and couldnâ€™t find it againâ€¦ always thinking forward moving wanting nothing but the it we canâ€™t define- settling down we move along the same track but slower now much more slowly and certainly then before when we saw big things through big eyed wondering about mostly everything, not caring so much but doing, feeling wandering wherever things happened to be happening at that particular timeâ€¦seeking trains once the darkness had come and sitting smoking talking not talking not knowing where the tracks went or how they split into many different, separate livesâ€¦</p>\r\n\r\n<p>desolate desperate desert air cold whipping by not so cold that I canâ€™t think about colder whippings from other areas of being there and hearing the warm sounds flow out from some glowing refuge where smoke curls high into the air and dogs would much rather stay inside- out of nothing here it comes now what the fuck am i now ginsberg? or kerouac? i did go across the country once but i couldnâ€™t tell if the spirit of kerouac was there or not maybe sometimes he was there and other times not really though i doubt it matters much at this point one, five, six, four seeping through and leaking into the small holes on the side of my head that take in the parts of life that canâ€™t be seen or felt- nervous habits always have a habit of making me nervous even when iâ€™m asleep.  where is the end?  where is the answer- itâ€™s blowinâ€™ in the wind thanks so much bob for that utterly and ambiguously useless aphorism since it really makes everyone feel so much better about the fact that the world is killing us off slowly one by one at least until something better comes along.</p> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2175,'<p>\r\nnot too long ago.  i was out with some friends enjoying coffee.  boring night.  our friend ken showed up with his wife and kids.  his daughter is 8, his boy  is 4.  ken and i took his kids to the pinball room so we could get rid of quarters and talk about construction work.  everything was fine.  he asked me about  my move, how was i going to surive in california.  i didn\'t have an answer other than i had to try something new or die.  i wasn\'t sure about dying...being  the correct choice just then.  we left his kids playing medieval madness and sat down at a table around the corner.  minutes later a teenage boy ran past us  in a panic.  i got up and signaled to some friend that trouble was up.  as we got ready to move, around ten police officers ran past us.  into the pinball  room.  where ken\'s kids were playing.  we followed them, hearts pumping uncomfortably fast.  what we saw was no good at all.  his boy was on the ground,  bleeding from his head.  his daughter was trying to pull him under a pinball table, and the police were trampling her legs trying to get the kid they were  chasing.  she was crying, but his son was unconcious.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"what the fuck is going on,\" ken yelled.  right then another cop grabbed me from behind and hurled me to the floor.  i wanted to fight back.  knew it was my  right to do so.  but i held myself in check.  refused to risk my future.  then they descended on ken.  one hit him in the head with his maglite.  ken fought  back, but never stood a chance.  they beat him to the ground, the whole time he was yelling about his children.  if i\'d had a firearm that night, i might  have seriously considered shooting those cops.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey arrested ken for resisting, impedance of a police officer, and obstructing justice.  they arrested the kid they were chasing, but released him later on  that night.  no charges were filed.  they arrested another friend of ours after he, speaking with ken\'s wife, mocked the eight cops who beat ken to the  floor.  they charged him with resisting and impedance.  both he and ken spent the night in jail.  they decided not to file charges against him.  ken however,  was charged, arraigned, and went to court.  twice judges threw it out, but the DA continued to pursue the case.  in the end ken lost almost ten thousand  dollars due to lawyers fees.  he went free.  his record was fully expunged.  the police involved were caught perjuring themselves during the trials.  they  were not charged.  some never even bothered showing up.  his son, who used to be full of wonder and quick to smile is now in counseling.  he cries at night.   i haven\'t seen him smile in over a month.  he\'s cranky and won\'t play pinball anymore.  ken\'s daughter had to have her legs stiched up.  she stopped with her  irish step dancing, and only rarely play her bagpipes anymore.  even at her young age, we all recognize the hardness that has grown around her eyes.  and we  all hope it doesn\'t last.  we were all very proud at how she kept her head that night.  trying to protect her little brother.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nken and i talked about it all the other night.  like it was no big deal.  the price you pay for questioning.  for believing in liberties most people don\'t  realize we even have.  a little something to get the ire up.  sipping our coffee.  eyes narrowed over the lip.  scanning.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere isn\'t much about this city i\'ll be missing when i leave.\r\n</p>'),(2176,'<p>An old man is asleep with a bird in his hand in front of the Mission Grounds. He\'s old and crusty with a bandana around his head that\'s holding gray straw hair in a style that makes me think exclusively of Vietnam Veterans. Wrinkled and dirty and I bet his eyes will have crusted by the time he wakes up. His other hand drips down to the ground at his side, but his left hand holds a black-feathered bird that looks caked in the same hazy dirt. It looks like a pigeon, but it can\'t be because I don\'t think I\'ve seen any all black pigeons around.  It sits in his hand, and I think maybe its dead because it doesn\'t move, and it doesn\'t fly away. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe\'s peaceful at least, which is maybe why the way too hip kid at the counter hasn\'t noticed him and come out to force him to get up and move into a doorway. The next doorway is already occupied anyway. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s still day, but the light is filtered by thin sheeted clouds and doesn\'t get very far before it gives up and starts to think about night time. So many more way too hip kids are on their way home from jobs they don\'t realize are the rest of their lives, or on their way to meet other way too hip kids, or just wandering around 16th street because they haven\'t thought of anything better to do yet. </P>\r\n<p>\r\nTowards the corner of Mission the gutters get thicker with paper and plastic and shit and people. Most not coming or going, but static. Stuck there by the direction of the wind or fate or bad luck. You have to go underground on that corner to catch the train. There\'s a long flight of piss covered stairs before you reach the safety of the blue vested ticket attendant. Smells of deeper meaner things you don\'t want to think about hit you before you can see the portal. They swirl around the heads of crazy fucked up people who\'re like demons outside the gates of hell with horns and tails. Their eyes roll back and forth looking for a fix or selling a fix. Some of them are in the midst of a fix. Their eyes roll faster or slower depending on what the devil made them do this time. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"You lookin sister? You lookin?\" one guy says, and all I can think of is Juice Newton. And then I feel somehow wronged by the trash and the shit and the people. And then I think, maybe the bird doesn\'t fly away, because of love.</p>\r\n'),(2177,'<P>on the phone with my mother this morning, and she said:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s hard to have to be more mature than the people who were adults when you were a child.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\'ve agreed to go to my father\'s mother\'s house for christmas eve.  i don\'t celebrate christmas, and don\'t really care about it, excepting for the giving of gifts to my friends.  i haven\'t seen my father\'s family more than twice in two years; i cut them off without a backward glance, and i\'m still angry with them about the situation that evolved into that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but my grandmother is not demonstrative, my father\'s younger brother is a racist/bigoted asshole, and their \"friends\" are all tremendously irritating and conservative.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so i\'m going to hold my breath and bite my tongue for four hours, and my father will think that the reason we all will get along is because god loves him, when really it\'s just that i don\'t want to make life any harder for my younger sisters, who see these people all the time and somehow manage to look past their politics and religion and the lack of decency in their treatment of other human beings, and see goodness.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it must be hard for my sisters to have to be more mature than me, even just as hard as it is for me to be more mature than my grandmother and my uncle, two of the people that i looked up to the most when i was little.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and i always feel awful and guilty and arrogant when i say things like that, because i feel like i am judging everyone, and acting like an i-know-everything-21-year-old, but that\'s not it at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it\'s this:  it hurts that i don\'t love them anymore.</p>'),(2178,'<p>\r\ni woke up around 11 today.  matt was frying bacon and sausage, fucking up the eggs again.  stumbled around trying to get  dressed.  went downstairs and sat, watched HCI, something about the Japanese during WWII.  during a commercial, matt brought  me breakfast.  \"i apologize to you heart in advance.\"  yeah.  kill that fucker.  we watched silently, until the commercials.   i gave him the dubious benefit of my opinions.  the next show came on.  this one about the japanese war crimes.  a couple of  times i cried.  this set the tune for the day.  we finished breakfast, i grabbed a beer.  went to the computer room, where yet  again i couldn\'t fix matt\'s troubles with his display drivers under Win2k.  steve called, asked if i was still going for pad  thai.  no.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut i was still heading in that direction.  had to pick up some things at the gaming store.  we drove over to oakland.  no  college broads out today.  too cold.  too rainy.   matt put on paul van dyke.  i liked it i guess.  but i wanted to hear banco  de gaia, which was sitting on my computer desk.  found a parking spot in front of the Irish store.  i skipped on in, matt in  tow.  picked up the gaelic dictionary which was suggested for learners.  almost grabbed a new scally.  seriously considered  taking one of the nice irish wool blankets.  matt checked out the crystal, thinking of giving something to his mum; talked  about buying his girl a sweater.  in the end he bought nothing.  i stuck with the dictionary. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncrossed the street, gazed longingly at yumwok, and walked into the gaming store.  spent sixty on some needed role playing  materials.  talked geeky talk with the fellows playing WH40k.  discussed picking up replacement counters for WiFFE.  went to  the hive, got coffee and water.  considered playing pinball, but ran into nicky.  talked to her about 9/11 for a while.  then  talked about stuff french prostitutes were known for in the early 20th century.  eh...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nclaimed a hug from tina for the twenty i left in her tip jar the other day, a handshake from joe; same deal.  talked about val  and her new fiance.  stupid little girl.  but a wonderful loving friend.  watched the time go by...talked about music...war...\r\nsome time later, i looked up and saw her again.  very nice indeed.  she had a long dress on, black with silver trim.  no  makeup or any crap like that.  her long black hair (something i normally don\'t like, long hair that is) shiny from the rain.   that half smile she wears.  no heavy scent,  just human.  i met her a while back now.  sure a hot broad; but too, intelligent  and interesting.  i\'d like to have....  but like most of them these days, she didn\'t like me then, and doesn\'t now.  i\'ve  gotten pretty used to being ugly physically.  but the discovery that all the worst in me leaks out for everyone to see these  days.  well, i don\'t know if i\'ll ever come to terms with that.  i...used to...not be this thing.  i don\'t know.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy fellow irish republican (as in the /irish/ republican movement) randy, came in and said hi.  sat down with her [what was  her name?]  matt, perked up, wanting badly to go hit on her.  he doesn\'t know i\'m interested [not that there\'s any point  pining for a woman who sees you as dirt] and wouldn\'t care anyway.  done it to me before.  you get used to it.  in time.  so  off he goes to their table.  leaving me alone with all that my life has bought; a few things to read, and alot of bitterness  and sad memories.  i play the part most of my friend want of me well.  rarely let my face express what i feel.  unless it is  rage or upset.  stopped trying to act pleasant or [gods forbid!] chivalrous years ago.  i play a stone to their pixie ways.   it fits them well.  and, if not well or pleasantly, it fits me.  it certainly help remove the fear of death.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe left later, where i gave matt\'s computer another serious talking to.  just like my wall, it wouldn\'t talk back.  just  stared into me and blinked once in a while.  matt gave up hope and went out.  \"she was smiling at me dude.\"  against all  instincts towards friendship, i hope he falls flat.  another new development in my life.  jealousy.  fucking perfect.  perhaps  when i\'m done, i can get involved with a death camp.  he left and i drank beer.  sat alone, fiercely wishing i had someone to  see, touch, talk to.  someplace to go.  some driving motive force to carry me through another long evening.  even as i write i  wonder if i should pack up and head out.  maybe jump off the bridge, save people some grief.  save certain loved ones living  in sf the trouble of seeing me as i am.  maybe switch to bourbon when the beer runs dry.  maybe go through my latest issues of   jane\'s defense weekly, looking for information i missed.  i\'d love to work out.  but until i get my blood pressure meds  again, i\'m not allowed to.  i\'d like to do alot of things as i write this.  and writing this is bottom of the list.  i know  listening to this lamb song isn\'t going to help things.  bittersweet.  like brett dying for a cause that wasn\'t his until he  made it so.  like speaking to my ex wife after many years, and finding that no, she never cheated on me after all the things  she said.  finding out that she\'s healthy and safe.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngeneral war is coming.  and for the first time i wish i\'d joined the military when i had the chance.  same time:  i\'ve been  looking into leaving my nation.  renouncing my citizenship.  but no other nation will take me.  i have no marketable skills.   being stateless [state dept. is blunt about it] is almost like being declared outlaw during the middle ages.  no friend  anywhere.  no safety.  you virtually cease being human.  almost as bad as being a mercenary.  it never bothered brett.  would  it bother me?  i don\'t much want to see the death that is coming.  or the shame of my nation as it extinguishes liberty,  justice, law, and everything else that is good.  i don\'t want to ever again tell someone, who\'s just watched thousands die on  television, \"i told you this was coming\".  or explain to ignorant friends or fellow workers whence comes terrorism.  i want to  curl back into the dream i held in my hand so long ago.  \r\n</p>'),(2179,'<p>i was in vancouver in september.  we were in the mountains, hiking.  well, not really hiking.  we were  more into building towers of dry riverbed rocks, piling them one on top of eachother.  then we were throwing rocks at each pile to see who could knock it down first.  the winner.  there were quite a few rounds, and i held my own.  nothing dominant, but i was respectable.  i even called one shot, like babe ruth.  we were about to start a new round after a stack had been built, but i stopped everyone, called a first shot drop, and delivered it.  the crowd went nuts.  i also missed a lot, badly. </p>\r\n \r\n<p>then we built about 17 piles and left them there.  ate some food, possibly smoked some joints.  to get back, we had to cross a giant fallen tree that bridged the gap between the mountain side and the dry section of the riverbed. a very cold, very blue, reasonably deep glacier stream ran beneath it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remember the feeling of things being phsyically bigger than what i\'m used to.  the scale of things.  rocks, trees, mushrooms.  the tree we crossed was long enough and wide enough to let us pass without much trouble. it was still a fair hike across it, and there were a few tense moments, but confidence is key when you\'re in those situations.  you have to pretend like you know what you\'re doing.  getting across that log is so satisfying because you reaffirm to yourself that you really don\'t need to know what you\'re doing, you only need to pretend you know what you\'re doing.   just like stacking rocks.</p>'),(2180,'<p>hello you all...how are you? me? i am fine, preparing for a christmas festival with my family of friends. we have a bamboo christmas tree thingy adorned with toy animals, miles davis ornaments, and lots of pretty lights... grillo is making italian food for us to eat, while woo just got through making us pancakes, money gripp is designing the coffee as we speak, sandy has it best of all - she is sleeping in the lounge room, gary is receiving love from his new friends - us. me i am making foto\'s thinking about my sally step, prepin for a 10 meditation course that i am leaving for tomorrow. i am thinking about friends like austin & egg, and all the other people i have fallen out of touch with for the past few months... new directions, new friends, a woman that sways when she sleeps, and family that i never knew i had... i am elated, on fire with love and passion for all - all of you...</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmeet some of my friends... sandy, garret, bart, dave, wendy, gary, aimee, cali...</p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2180_1.jpg\" height=\"500\">'),(2181,'<p>after we had crossed the tree and left the stacked rocks behind us, we went to a bus stop to wait for a bus to haul us back in to the city. we laid on the sidewalk and someone\'s front lawn.  we were tired.  after a while, a bus came, and we got on it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it began to dodge its way down the mountainside.  soon after we got on, we stopped again to pick someone up.  it was a girl.  she was pretty.  she came to the back, where we were all sitting.  there were five of us. i was sitting on the left hand side bench seat, facing the other bench seat.  the girl sat there.  i used the brim of my hat to to shield my eyes well enough to steal a glance from under it.  she was very pretty.  i can\'t exactly remember her face, but that doesn\'t mean i don\'t remember the moment.  she was dressed in dark goth like colors, but in more northern vancouver naturalist textures.  very simple, very plain lines and divides, navigating through a few layers.  her hair was shoulder length, dark, and i believe it was braided at the back.  she had a plain and beautiful face.  i remember more in physical detail about her hand woven burlap textured bag and wondering as to its contents more than her specific facial features because i spent more time looking at it, and not her..  eyes averted, not wanting to blow my cover.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>her dress was dark grey, and laid close and quietly on her body. but she looked elegant nonetheless.  no make up that i recall.  i looked across again, and caught her looking at me.  or she caught me looking at her.  her eyes were engaging in that simplistic sense.  it felt like she was looking at me plainly, who she really was by herself, and not a version filtered by a social shield.  our many mutual glances didn\'t last beyond the literal blink of an eye.\r\nthe bus wove its way down the mountainside.  it eventually stopped at the harbor station.  the girl got up in front of me.  people were exiting the bus. she was waiting to step down the stairs of the side exit and turned and looked at me.  i looked back and keep looking past the committal limit .. smiled the only way you can smile when you look at someone beautiful like that - reflection.  you don\'t really have a choice in the matter.  she kept looking at me for a lifetime (by intentional glance collision standards) and then returned my smile, for a split second.  it was enough to surprise me, and make me feel good.  i was a bit too surprised, apparently, and broke the gaze before her smile could complete what it was saying.  but i dug the first part.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she walked ahead of us.  we were all getting on the sea bus to take us across the harbor to downtown vancouver.  it wasn\'t arriving to pick us up right away, and we found some advertisement placed flying discs (frisbees with big holes cut out of the center, with advertisements for various websites on the top), so tomfoolery ensued.   we whipped them across the bridge to the sea boat dock, and caught them, and dropped them.  there were a few fancy throws behind backs and legs and such.  jumping between the rows of bars that during busy commuting times corrals and organizes the herds of people for their own safety.  it was a good time.  i think we were all looking forward to getting to the other side to throw them around in public again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>back to the girl.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the sea bus arrived, we got in, and took seats.  i sat seven or eight seats down from the girl (quite likely not by accident) and beside one of the friends in my group. we were all facing forward, watching vancouver get bigger, slowly.  we chatted a bit.   i played glance tag with the girl a few times.  i couldn\'t resist, mostly because it felt like i didn\'t need to.  we docked, exited, and resumed tomfoolery with the discs.  the girl got way ahead of us.  we made slow progress to the sky train, but we got there.  a few innocent commuters were hit along the way, but those are the risks you take using public transit these days.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at the skytrain platform, the girl was waiting for the train. i was happy that she was taking the train too.  i thought perhaps i had lost her.  the train came, but unfortunately, she got on in the car ahead of mine. probability had disappointed me like it does about fifty percent of the time.  i caught a few glimpses of her through the intra-train car doors while we were sliding along.  she was talking on a cell phone.  my guess was that she was going to a friend\'s place, or returning home from a friend\'s place.  calling to say she was coming.  mostly, we played with the discs and garth did trapeze stuff on the above head handrail bars.  we got off the train at an early stop, the girl did not, and she slid away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>more running and more acrobatic throws and catches at the bus stop area, beneath the sky train track, waiting.  there were a large concentration of people around.  i wondered where the girl was going.  we were loud on the bus, but good natured.  it took us up to broadway, and we got off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>running throws and catches down east broadway, across intersections over cars,  taking turns sending each other closer to home.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we stopped throwing for a bit of a breather (it was a long way) and i looked up to the line of the sidewalk, mildly uphill.  i saw her walking toward me and knew right away it was her.  i guess she\'d taken the sky train to a station further down the opposite end of broadway, and just walked from there.  i tried to straighten myself up a bit..  a was a bit messy from the tomfoolery.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was walking with my group of friends, but i was looking at her.  i was ready to reply to the conversation she had made with her smile on the bus.  as soon as she came close enough, i saw that she was smiling huge and beautifully, from ear to ear, and looked only at me, right at me.  it was a totally unabashed smile, and mine reflected it (again).  with that look, we both knew we were busted, and then we admitted it with our smiles.  thinking the same thing.  that\'s what hits me hardest when i remember that moment (which i do often) - the smile.  i felt like her smile looked.  it felt fucking good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we passed.  she rounded the corner just behind me, i turned around once and saw her through two layers of chain link fence while it lasted.  it turned into brick storefront, and we continued on our way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we ran home.  we were trying to beat a friend who\'d hopped on a bus to throw discs to us out of the bus windows.  i thought about the girl while i ran.  that last smile and look, how open it was, both ways.  no words needed.  (not like i would\'ve been able to speak anyway)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i vowed to talk to her if i saw her again, not out of regret, just out of common sense.  of course, i didn\'t see her again.  but, i was in vancouver because i\'m considering living there soon.   i hope to see her. we already know each other from that smile.</p>'),(2182,'BOINK BOINK'),(2183,'<p>\r\nSometimes, waking up for work is easy.  i think i can remember getting up, chipper, ready to make breakfast and my lunch,  watch the news and bullshit with my roomate.  today was not one of those days.  my usual christmas depression hit worse than  it normally does.  we had four days off, compared to the two we get most years.  my sleep schedule flipped, and i slept into  the afternoon and spent the evenings awake and ready for action.  then it all came crashing down around me.  i had one day to  shift to my standard schedule.  i failed miserably.  i was in bed, reading a book on evolution, wishing i had some water, and  wiggling my toes under the wool blankets.  then i got my christmas present.  a call from darwin and cyan.  talking to them  cheered me up, entertained me.  it also wound me up.  i wanted to go get thai food with them, not go the bed.  but seeing as  i\'m well over 1500 miles away...  i went to bed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni tossed and turned.  finally turned the damn light back on and dug into my book some more.  thought about some ideas cyan  raised.  in other words, i wound myself up further.  by the time i started yawning it was 4am.  that gave me a maximum sleepy  time of just over an hour.  i wake up, and my alarm is blaring.  my roomate is pounding on the door.  \"Asshole!  Wake the fuck  up!\"  I knew today was going to be cold.  I stumbled around like a moron, trying to get all the vital layers of clothing on.   somehow i even managed to tie my boot laces.  i grabbed my empty lunch box, and with a sigh out the door we went.  GEEEEZUS it  was cold!  The air seemed to pull my breath from my lungs.  My eyes closed against my will.  The sounds of Pittsburgh echoed  off the hills.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen we get to the shop, we\'re late.  not that it matters, we were still the third group to get there.  i could hardly see,  could hardly breath.  i am not ready for true winter.  i am a sad example of a new englander, i thought.  i work on loading  the van, while my foreman stands in the heated shop.  he only helps load when it\'s nice out and the boss is watching.  then  he\'s mister fucking helpful.  not so this morning.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"where are we going howard?\" i ask in the van, i add \"the duck\" under my breath.  his initials are HTB, which i claim stands  for Howard The Bastard.  he finds this amusing.  so do we all.  \"We\'re at Holy Trinity today.\"  Joy.  That fucking church is  cold and wet and the whole job is fucked up.  Whoever the architect is, maybe he should stop with the cocaine and PCP  breakfasts and get back to the Cheerios.  we stop all too soon, and it\'s time to unload the van.  i slip on some black ice,  slamming my head into the bumper of the van.  gotta love those hardhats.  i\'m too cold to blush as howard laughs, but i would  be any other time.  and so work truly begins.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe thing about the cold is...  your hands.  they stop working.  we can\'t wear gloves doing what we do, not that they help  much anyway.  at first it\'s just cold, and they work okay.  but when the burning starts, that\'s when you stop being able to  hold your tools.  simply wirenutting some 12 wire becomes a monumental task.  looking up, i can see a priest in his office.   wearing short sleeves.  looking oh so happy and warm.  it doesn\'t take long until i\'m barely able to work at all.  then i get  called inside.  where we have gargantuan natural gas heaters set up.  now my flesh begins to burn real bad.  from burning  cold, to cold, to warm, to that horrible burning you get when you\'ve been freezing too long.  my balls are sweating, i have  four layers on.  i begin to shed clothing.  my flight jacket, my hoodie; i roll up my sleeves.  up in the ceiling, where all  the heat comes to rub against the corrugated steel roof, it must be 90 degrees. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen, just as the burning stops, howard wants me to go back outside and unload a truck full of light fixtures.  100 light  fixtures, each 35 lbs.  and another 25 boxes of assorted materials.  dammit, it\'s bloody cold out there i bitch to myself.  so  out i go, the wind zooming in on my face, bringing with it snow flake daggers.  with my sleeves rolled up, i work outside for  over an hour.  but the cold doesn\'t bother me after the first few minutes.  something about accepting to the cold.  letting it  insinuate itself into my body.  loving it.  i move faster and faster as the time goes by.  other workers are staring at me,  some even laugh.  but i\'m going to get away from this dead fucking job.  while they go nowhere but to the bar, i will bask in  the warmth of good friends and new experiences.  i will never slip to their level of ignorance.  my escape is already planned.   all i need to do is survive five more months.  this website helped bring that about.  many a time it pissed me off, made me  laugh, whatever; but my future is engraved in the replies i\'ve recieved to my posts.  i think that\'s pretty fucking cool.\r\n</p>'),(2184,'<p>Often times, I get engulfed in a kind of quiet, deperate angst that doesn\'t feel very Romantic at all. In fact, it feels very Modern, cold, concrete, and dull. It feels like the whole crapshoot doesn\'t amount to anything at all, that we come from dirt and we end as dirt and the stuff in between is pointless, boring, full of needless anxiety, and probably too long. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>There\'s nothing artistic or productive or motivational about this distress, nor does it feel like something that medicating with or without the aid of a professional would help. To be frank, it feels like the one true gift of human self-consciousness -- the realization that I don\'t matter to anything or anyone at all. What insight! How blessed we are, to be able to realize our own insignificance. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>This despair is inevitable, so long as one pursues self-consciousness honestly, with all one\'s intellectual rigor, to its logical conclusion. We are animals, vectors of genetic evolution wrapped up in bags of survival-enhancing protein soup, with a curious mutation: we know what we are. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>Initially, this self-awareness is but a glimmer, easily misunderstood and conflagrated into a delusional sense of self-importance. God created everything, and he\'s a guy who looks a lot like me. Yeah, that\'s likely. I\'m the plucky underdog who, despite my humble beginnings and tremendous challenges, was born to rise to the very pinnacle of human society, and single-handedly save the country/world/universe. Uh-hunh. The universe all began as one single, unified force/matter that can be explained in a simple mathematical expression of our devising. Sure, that would be nice. We have had more than enough of this infantile anthropomophism, self-aggrandization, and woefully inflated sense of self-importance. Time to grow up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Growing up, unfortunately, unless I\'m missing something, leaves one wavering between feeling slightly giddy with meaningless and then pitching headlong into the pit of despair over this selfsame meaninglessness. Sometimes we yearn for a little less self-awareness and a little more self-delusion. But admit it, we\'re past that now, more and more, as a species. One by one, or a little group at a time, we make the leap out of teen angst and self-importance and sink deep into the darkest depths.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There doesn\'t seem to be any tonic that soothes this sting. The irony of the situation doesn\'t raise much more than a bemused smirk from the corner of the down-turned mouth. Spirituality, art, the soul, acheivements of science, business or politics all seem like so many ashes in my mouth. More pointless delusions of grandeur, more ways to kill the idle time before it kills you. There\'s no escape. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>So, what the fuck to do now? We\'re beasts, anonymous members of a species that would be itself forgettable were it not for its talent for extinguishing all life around it. Individuals of this species have a curious talent for realizing their utter interchangability, and this knowledge terrifies them into acting in irrational ways. Now what?</P>\r\n'),(2185,'<P>I had a dream last night about the most wonderful place on earth. Picture this:</P>\r\n<P>An old, revival type house, like a big old mansion on St. Charles. But when you walk in, it\'s just one massive room. 200 ft long, 100 ft. wide with ceilings towering three stories. Off to the sides are smaller rooms, like in an old cathedral. In the main room, the walls are just bookcase after bookcase reaching up to the top, in the middle are lower tables; all are covered with books. More books than I\'ve ever seen in my life. Maybe every book ever printed, because there are books on every subject I can think of. Most of them older, but some brand new. The room is so big, that you can easier wander around and get lost amid the stacks. The smaller rooms are reading rooms, with overstuffed couches, chairs and floor pillows. People quietly gathered, reading, chatting, laughing. Drinking hot tea curled up in a big chair. People that you knew, friendly people who were calm and pleasant and happy. Also there were animals. I want to think that they were roaming free, but I think that they were contained somehow; maybe they were just really well behaved. Monkeys, cats, birds, dogs... I think there might have been a snake or a lizard. Its hard to put into words, but the feeling of this place was one of utter contentment. Pure joy and the without the ability or desire to leave. I think that this will be my lifes work, to create this place. I don\'t know how it would work, because the books weren\'t for sale. (In fact, there was nowhere to pay for anything. No cash registers, no maney takers, nothing. Nothing electrical. Natural light. No computers or tv\'s...) I don\'t know how to fund such a place, or how to buy/create the building. I don\'t know if it could happen. Maybe it\'s not supposed to exist. Maybe it\'s just a dream place that I\'ve created that I can go and visit in my sleep.</P>'),(2186,'<p>i always loved this aspect of the law of inertia: that an object at rest tends to stay at rest, while an object in motion tends to stay in motion.  i find that if i am being lazy at any given time, i cannot even conceive of how to start doing the things i \"want\" to do.  in fact, if i\'ve been lazy for a while, i don\'t really even want to do anything at all.  i guess that\'s why we have this tradition of \"new year\'s resolutions\"--to jettison us out of our lackadaisical winter habits and into the spring.  if it weren\'t for the hopeful clang of the words \"new year\'s resolution,\" i don\'t think i would have much gumption to do much of anything right now.   but in order to gear up for the new year, i\'m maintaining a surprising level of activity and motivation.  surprising, that is, if you consider my lethargic, catnap-prone tendencies as of late.  nothing appeals to me as much as my bed, a book, and a cat on my lap.  i\'m not the type of person, usually, who sleeps a lot, but lately sleep has seemed like the most precious commodity i have available to me.  i still don\'t sleep as much as i would like, though, during the week. always one more minute stolen from the alarm clock. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>contemplating quitting the band.  we\'re on hiatus at the moment and it feels good to have more free time (not to mention a rest from all of the interpersonal mumbojumbo and their mostly quiet but pervasive sexism).  laying on the couch staring into space.  time to dream.  dreaming feels important to me today.  i\'m going to go home and cuddle up with some tea and thomas mann.  for christmas, i bought myself mann and anne carson and borges and jung and hillman.  i find everything written by anne carson riveting.  i also love henry james.  so satisfying to finish one of his wordy tomes.  it\'s amazing to read this rich, vocabularied prose that casually mentions things like getting dressed for dinner and horsedrawn carriages and \"rosettes on his shoes.\"  it enchants me and makes me want to go to europe.  i am definitely craning my neck, trying to see spain in my future.  possibly this may.  we\'ll see how things continue to go with the job search.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>today i applied for a job with the sf opera.  after a recent drunken palm reading that boded well for my financial future, i felt newfound hope for my chances in this seemingly impenetrable job market.  i find writing cover letters to be one of the most difficult tasks i have ever undertaken in my entire life.  i am sure that this job with the opera is much coveted, and that there are extremely over-qualified individuals out there who are, as i type this, being contacted for a condescending interview with the \"human resources assistant,\" who, in a typically contrary human resources fashion, refuses to post his or her first name anywhere on the internet, rendering it practically impossible to address him or her properly.  either you take a foolhardy gamble or you go with the obsequious \"dear sir or madam\" which i hate.  nobody says dear sir or madam.  this isn\'t goddamn 1910.  i wonder as i read those henry james books from that period, which always seem to be about people whose preponderant source of wealth is never revealed or even alluded to, what would my life have been like if i had lived in those days?  would i be a washer-woman, a fishmongerer, a whore?  what on earth would i have done to support myself?  or would i have married at fourteen and proceeded to sew myself blind (not to mention turn myself inside out having 12 children) by the age of thirty?  sometimes i permit myself the fantasy of being like one of james\' dubious heroines, rich just because, with beautiful dresses and a villa in venice in which to while away the winter.  but it always seems fraudulent.  if i had been unlucky enough to have been born at the turn of the century, i\'d have been dirt poor and no question about it.  emphasis on the dirt.  still, it\'s pleasant to dream about the travel trunks full of satins in deep green and crimson, the tortoiseshell combs, the letter writing, the Gilded Age.</p>\r\n<p>further dreaming: if i got even an interview at the sf opera, my grandmother (rest her soul) would be <i>so</i> proud of me.  i hope i get one, just for her.</p>  '),(2187,'<p>The rain, like a million diamonds, glittered in shards on your sleeve as we stepped out of the rain onto the bus. I couldnâ€™t take my eyes of the glory of your elbow, the brilliance of your shoulder. And I thought, thatâ€™s what beauty is. The light of a thousand drops of rain sitting silently on your coat sleeve in the glare of the overhead fluorescent lights. I had to look away and catch my breath.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>And you. You sat there perfectly unaware of the power you had over me by just being there covered in rain. The power you wielded under harsh lighting. Imagine the pull youâ€™d have had in candlelight, or unrelenting darkness. You had no idea. How stupid you were sitting there in the residue of raindrops. Sparkling into the city one block at a time. Shaking a few from your cuff at every jerk and shudder of the bus route. How ignorant of what you could have had me do or be or beg for. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I said nothing, and watched the miniscule puddle of rainwater form between your feet and slide in dirty rivulets under the front seats of the bus toward the door. I watched the beauty drain from your coat and pool up like gutter run off. And you never knew. And I never said a word. And in that small ride up hills and around corners, your beauty slipped from the world, and I let you go. </p>\r\n'),(2188,'So today I\'m bored and sleepy at work.  Something reminds me of Lynn Bender (I think it was qmail), and that reminded me of the crazy parties he threw down under the bank on the drag (\"crazy\" = \"black-lit, drunken exploding mixtures of science nerds, transvestites, strippers, bearded professors, and a chair nailed to a tree stump\").  I think I first saw austin at one of those parties, asking a question about how to get a modem to work with his linux laptop, or something.  This was in late 98 I think, and I was probably drunk on Jagermeister, so this could have been some other blond skinny germanic-looking person.  But frankly I\'m suspicious that it was austin.  Count this as sighting #1 for me, I think.  If that wasn\'t austin I met him for sure drinking coffee with Lynn over at Mojo\'s on the drag.'),(2189,'<p>She buys bibles by the case and hands them out to the Vietnamese women who do her nails. Told me that the Buddhists have an easier time converting to Catholicism because of all the â€œrulesâ€ and all that â€œfemale worshipâ€ that the two religions have in common. Says they still arenâ€™t Christian though, because they havenâ€™t accepted Christ into their hearts. But some of them get there. You never know, so she passes out the bibles.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I didnâ€™t bring up the varying forms of Buddhism or the fact that in my travels through more than a few predominantly Buddhist countries, I never saw much sign of female worship, and in fact, was lead to believe that more often than not in Buddhist tradition, females are found to be spiritually inferior to men, unable to reach enlightenment without first being reincarnated as men. And I canâ€™t right now think of many religions that donâ€™t have â€œrulesâ€ but who am I to argue when I feel my grasp of Buddhism, while comfortable and reverent, is so small. Skimmed from between the lines of meaning found in various paintings in a smattering of wats and temples.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œDo you think that Muslims, who follow Islam to the letter, who really follow it, do you think they are good people?â€ she says to me. I look at her and I say, â€œYes. I do.â€ And then they launch into a speech that Iâ€™m not sure wasnâ€™t rehearsed. They say to me as we stand around the kitchen island that Islam dictates that to be a good Muslim, you should kill all those who oppose Islam. They tell me that Muslims who die for their religion are told they will receive a special glory for their deeds, that they think the glory is bigger and brighter and better if they die in the act of killing innocent Christians. Or this is the gist of what they say at any rate. And I say, â€œI think there are those who are Muslim who are good people. In fact I have known them personally.â€ But they arenâ€™t swayed. Why would they be, they have rehearsed this speech. They have studied Islam at church! Read the pamphlets and everything! And when I mention this later to a friend, he says, â€œWhat about Sufism! What about Rumi!â€ and I just shake my head and donâ€™t know what to do. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think about how I could never convert to Buddhism because my upbringing, my mythology, is so firmly rooted in Christianity. How the myths and teachings of Buddhism are approached from another direction. How there is no way in my mind to correlate the two in a way that would make conversion possible in spite of the fact that the foundations of the teachings are often incredibly similar. And no way to forget these myths and legends and beliefs that have become a part of my living and breathing and being. I think of the women filing nails and nodding as she tells them about the glory of Christ. Maybe taking home the bibles and reading about Jesus before lighting some Nag Champa and asking Buddha to make sure they win the lotto.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She speaks as if it is only a matter of time before her work is done and everyone sees the â€œTruthâ€. But I know too much, my truths are not hers. Itâ€™s getting late, they lead the room in a rousing chorus of â€œHappy Birthday to Jesusâ€ and I get up and try to make it home before Santa visits.</p>\r\n'),(2190,'<p>it\\\'s six in the evening on a saturday, two nights before the end of the worst year of my life. i am poorly lit from above by an aging 40watt in a very unattractive multi-bulb hanging light fixture whose other bulb has gone missing. my glasses are smeared with the grease of some accidental touch, causing me to employ an unusual cock in my head in order to view the screen through a small section at the lower left corner of the lens that remains reasonably transparent. multi-colored whiskers cover my face and neck, as they usually do. there is a large black X on my left hand written with a magic marker, which is in the back left pocket of my now very soiled pants. filthy as my clothes are, their stench is bested by the rotten skin beneath them, the palpable smell of which would overwhelm even the uncivilized. the dessicated remains of a medley of vietnamese food from a storefront restuarant in a neighborhood where i once lived sits on the table to my left, its odors mingling with mine.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i notice the persistent cold seeping through the cracks in the old windows of my soon to be former home on the sweat-damp hair on the back of my head just above the nape of my neck. occasionally my skin does all it can to encourage me to find warmth by complaining with a bristling shiver. when i pause to think for a moment, i lean forward on my left fist and chew on the fingernail of the pinky attached to it. there is a pain in my lower back whose cause is unknown, but whose presence is familiar. my toes are curled up inside black nylon socks which aren\\\'t well suited for the footwear that surrounds them. these black and white shoes, along with my slightly sore feet inside of them, are folded underneath one of the rolling legs of the articulated chair i\\\'m sitting on. my weight gently causes the joint to rock every so often which causes a shirt i wore yesterday and two jackets which are all draped over the back of the chair to rustle along the ground in a satisfying way. from time to time my thoughts wander to a box of cigarettes -- nestled in the pocket of one of the jackets along with a bright pink lighter -- until the markered X and the unrelenting cold causes them to wander elsewhere.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>to the left of me on the standard office issue folding brown table i sit before is a small barrel cactus that i\\\'m not sure is alive or dead. it rests inside an appropriately sized orangebrown container which rests inside an inappropriately sized black ceramic pot that features reasonably whimsical and vaguely asian-looking patterns. on top of the soil and stones surrounding the cactus -- who has a name i cannot remember that was given to him by a stranger girl who met him at a party a year or so ago -- lay two red barrettes, one of them burrowing on its side as if to integrate itself with the soil, the other overturned and almost touching the cactus. they are the sort of thing cute young girls use to pin their hair in various ways that inevitably make them look cuter still. their previous owner left them on my bedstand as she lay beside me one night, the once-strong shimmer in her eyes visibly fading over the course of the brief stolen moments we shared together.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>angular and defiant rock-and-roll that i\\\'ve only heard once before plays loudly from a stereo i\\\'ve had since i was sixteen in the other room. the first time i heard it was while fucking the girl that named the cactus, who managed to remain a stranger despite the exchange. an unearthly green digital-clock glow reminds me that i\\\'m supposed to be packing and moving all of the objects surrounding me instead of telling you about them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>memories make it hard to get things done sometimes.</p>'),(2191,'I am alone again under the full moon in winter - dirty milk sky swirling overhead.  A thousand little diamonds flash like sugar in the first fallen snow of Christmas Eve.  Leafless black fingers aching, half dead witnesses point accusingly at Heaven - creaking and cracking like so much shipâ€™s rigging.  Alone again.  Alone again in kinship with the wolf: moving quickly at night, under the stars.  Baleful we cry.  The things that we love flee from us when we run to them.  And so we are alone again.  Why know beauty that we cannot reach, or love things that want us not?  Pity me Moon â€“ hide your bright face and leave us senseless to the dark.  Will any of you Host in the thin air answer me?  Why?  Ever cold and ever silent you answer me not.  And so I run: under the stars, in the face of the moon.  Alone again, in kinship with the wolf.    '),(2192,'<p>I have to work in the morning.  This directly translates into sharing a large bottle of weird Belgian beer with myself, losing interest in watching a movie and going to sleep stupidly early.</p>\r\n<p>Today I\'ve been pondering what the big deal with New Years is anyway.  It\'s just another night, really.  Why has everyone been so picky about urging me to go out to a party tonight or to at least do something other than sit at home by myself?  This year has been terrible for nearly everyone I know.  I was not fortunate enough to escape the loads of crap that the zero one was freely dumping.  I am glad that this year is over with.  I am glad that I and those I care about are all still here (for better or worse).</p>\r\n<p>I\'m going to be sleeping through the rollover and when I wake up and go to work, I shall do so with a smile on my face for I will have finished the year the same way I have lived it.  Alone.</p>\r\n<p>Whatever lessons learned and habits destroyed, I have come to some slow and saddening realisations this year.  I\'ve learned not to count on others and to not ask for anything from them.  Living your life from a certain extreme requires you to fully displace yourself to the opposite end in order to discover a mangagable mean.  I\'ve been too nice and have suffered for it.  I think 2002 will be the year of being an asshole and I shall dedicate it to my ego.</p>\r\n<p>With three hours and one minute before this year is finished in the EST, I\'m going to sleep.</p>\r\n<p>I deserve it.</p>'),(2193,'<p>So here I am, showing up on the first.  A momentuous day, the earth\'s birthday, and a day when many people are getting a bit introspective.  People are asking themselves the hard questions, making resolutions, and sipping water as they recover from the hangover doldrums.  And by people, I mean myself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I feel as if a wrench has been thrown into the gears of my life in the last month, throwing off schedules, routines and accepted facts.  What was once true is no longer, and a whole different landscape surrounds me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The wrench hit me on December 11th, at 5:50am, as I groped my way out of a cab, and arrived at the bus station.  The sidewalk there was  smeared with garbage from city litterbugs, and the hot-dog vendor was already selling from his breakfast menu-- hotdogs.  I held open one of the quadruple glass doors, and then passed through myself.  I trudged through the coffee mist, past the grumpy employees, out towards the Departures sign.  The bus was on the tarmac, suitcases being tossed into its belly, and a line of travellers shuffling slowly forward.  In a few short seconds, the line dwindled to nothing, the door gave a sigh and closed, and I was standing on the pavement by myself.  Belle was on the bus, fourteen hours from Cincinnati, slowly getting farther away from me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At that time, I didn\'t feel the wrench, didn\'t even know of its existance.  Four days later, I felt it.  My effort at optimism was shattered when I found the electronic plane ticket to Washington DC, bought by a Washingtonian named Philip, who I\'ve encountered before.  Belle accepted it eagerly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That night, I picked up the phone.  When she answered, I danced around the subject.  I sat, listening to static while the other line interrupted us, and desperately wished she would tell me.  Ohio was boring, according to Belle, and no, there were no exciting plans.  Why?  I answered that I didn\'t know, and soon hung up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The devil\'s advocate on my shoulder points out that I was snooping when I found the plane ticket.  I was hunched over at the computer screen, scanning through files and searching for names.  Why was I so suspicious?  Is my lack of faith an equal betrayal?  I\'d like to think not, but in truth, maybe things have been over for a long time now.  I\'m just another minor villain, undone by my own flaws, like everyone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So the wrench is with me now.  It\'s forcing me to explore new things, and to wonder if the girl walking past me has that inquisitive twinkle in her eye that I used to recognize.  My mind may be chaotic these days, but my hands are still warm,  my feet are still cold, and the air still feels crisp when I breathe it.</p>'),(2194,'fascination<br>\r\nendless distraction<br>\r\nfake light has flooded<br>\r\nyour heart again<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\nfive eyes<br>\r\nblinded with lies<br>\r\nloaned for suicide<br>\r\nwhat you gave up<br>\r\nfind it broken up<br>\r\nin a million pieces<br>\r\nall around the place<br>\r\neverything on fire<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\noverhead fan moving the air<br>\r\ntoo idle to figure things out<br>\r\nfeeling the fear of accepting<br>\r\nthe future resembles now<br>'),(2195,'i suppose the one big deal about watching the rollover happen is just to have the oppourtunity to restrospect about what shit has gone down the year before. look back, feel like shit and drown those feelings out with a lot of drinks.\r\n\r\n<p>i know i\'ve gotten the crap beaten out of me by life this year and i feel stronger for it. being laid off of two (good) jobs and being dumped by a very great girlfriend is just the highlights.\r\n\r\n<p> since 2k1 brought major changes to my life in a seemingly endless onslaught i\'ve decided that this year can\'t be all that much worse. too many things happened that i had no control over. i don\'t complain or whine about those things too much. they suck, but hell, that\'s life anyway.\r\n\r\n<p>this year i want to have the year of good change. the year of taking life by the horns and holding on for dear life, cause damnit, it\'s my life and i want control!\r\n\r\n<p>i know what i want, i know how to get there (well, for the most part) and i have the will to do it. now i just have to actually do what i say, think and desire.\r\n\r\n<p>the retrospection is a great thing. plans for the future can be made while looking back at all the mistakes you\'ve made in the past twelve months.\r\n'),(2196,'\r\nDixie thought he was beautiful. Patty took one look at him and pronounced, â€œArt fag.â€ She said that where she was from, in Newton, guys wearing converse high tops matched with London fog overcoats and black berets were a sudden infestation, like rats fleeing storm swollen sewers. â€œGod, ever since MTV started showing â€˜120 minutes,â€™â€ she said. â€œI bet he read â€˜The Strangerâ€™ because he thought it would make him as deep as Robert Smith.â€ \r\n\r\nAll Dixie knew was that he was different from every other guy on the campus of their backwater state college. Westfield State was a repository for every townie kid who hadnâ€™t possessed the skills for an athletic scholarship to a name brand school or the grades to make it into the state university. The boys wore blue jeans tucked into untied workboots, rails cut into the sides of their whiffle haircuts, and the girls wore sweatpants everyday, their hair tied into Pebbles Flintstone topknots. But the Art Fag wasnâ€™t one of them. It was true about the beret and the overcoat, and he had offset all that black with an ill-considered Hawaiian shirt, but he had sharp cheekbones and hair cut to fall long over one eye. It was his one visible eye she fixated on, as she stared at him sitting alone in the Dining Commons. He had blue eyes, like the blue her mother had given the paint-by-numbers Jesus Dixie had hanging on her dorm room wall. And it wasnâ€™t Camus he was reading for company, no matter what that smarty pants Patty said. It was Nietzsche. \r\n\r\nDixie also felt herself to be out of place at Westfield. She was short and bigboned, like her grandmother had once said. â€œItâ€™s not that youâ€™re fat, Dixie. Youâ€™re just big boned. Good strong farm people, thatâ€™s who youâ€™re from.â€ Her daddy had put it more bluntly. â€œWhy, youâ€™re just a cute lâ€™il olâ€™ fireplug, ainâ€™tcha honey,â€ he said. â€œDonâ€™t ever stand too long on the sidewalk now, some pupâ€™ll likely come by and mark his territory!â€ Dixie was terribly nearsighted, but too astigmatic for contact lenses, and had to suffer with thick lensed glasses, although she tried to compensate by secretly spending $200 of her babysitting money on a pair of fancy fashion frames. The bright red plastic of the glasses tended to unpleasantly offset the ashyness of her complexion, at least in direct sunlight, but she loved them because she thought they lent her an air of barely suppressed naughtiness, like one of the teachers in that Van Halen video. \r\n\r\nDixie was an education major, set to graduate that spring. Westfield State had originally been a teacherâ€™s college, and the School of Education was still the largest department on campus. Dixie had successfully made it through four years, her most notable achievement being the most anonymous student that ever passed through those hallowed halls. Half of her instructors would have been unable to pick her out a lineup as a former student. Still, Dixie was a dedicated student, and not above helping her classmates with an assignment or two when they just hadnâ€™t been able to get around to completing it, and she had made herself a few friends along the way. One was Patty, who in her own way was as odd as the Art Fag. Patty was obsessed with Celtic imagery and Druidic beliefs, and dressed in an idiosyncratic welter of Scottish tartan and Stevie Nicks inspired lace. She played the bagpipes, and toted them around in a haversack that also contained a collection of quartz crystals that wouldâ€™ve been the pride of most mid-sized geological departments, and a well thumbed deck of tarot cards. Dixie had been paralyzed with fear when Patty had first produced them, certain that Patty was a Satanist; she was only slightly mollified when Patty told her she was actually a Catholic. Patty was pretty, in a Buffy Ste Marie sort of way, but she was a virgin, like Dixie. She had confided this to Dixie after they had shared half a pint of Peach Shnapps, sitting on the floor of Pattyâ€™s dorm room, candles burning and scarves draped over the desk lamps. â€œI just donâ€™t like boys,â€ sheâ€™d whispered. â€œI mean, I like boys, but I donâ€™t like thinking about, you know, their things. Touching their things. Itâ€™s gross, right?â€ \r\n\r\n\r\nDixie changed the subject. She pushed the deck of cards, wrapped in black velvet, across the floor to Patty. â€œWill you read my future for me?â€ she asked. The truth was, Dixie thought about boysâ€™ things all the time. She knew the church said it was sinful, and that she was supposed to confess impure thoughts before she went to Mass, but she had read enough books to know that thinking about boysâ€™ things was totally natural and nothing to be ashamed of. Sheâ€™d had a terrible crush on Nick Rhodes from Duran Duran, and later on John Cusack in â€œSay Anything.â€ At 22 sheâ€™d never even kissed a boy, but sheâ€™d read Forever and Flowers in the Attic and The Story of O, paperbacks with fake covers taped on in order to smuggle them passed her mother, and she had a rich and varied fantasy life involving Johnny Depp and David Hasselhoff and a host of others. \r\n\r\nBut now here was this new boy, sitting not twenty feet from her in the Dining Commons, and all thoughts of David Hasselhoff and his broad shoulders and fine wavy hair were gone forever. But that twenty feet was a chasm, an abyss she could never cross. She could never just walk up to him and start talking. What could she possibly say that would get him to look past her squat fire hydrant frame and see the sylph ha! inside her? How could she explain that she saw that he was pure beauty and was the sudden new axis of her world? \r\n\r\nThe Art Fag stood up, shouldered his book bag and picked up a guitar case that had been hidden under the table. He took his tray up to the counter and then walked out the door, pausing at the message board mounted outside long enough to tack up a sheet of paper he pulled from inside his coat. Dixie waited until he was out of sight, forced herself to count to ten, then got up and dashed outside. \r\n\r\nâ€œHave You Always Wanted to Play Guitar?â€ the notice said. He was offering guitar lessons, $10 for half an hour, teach you to play whatever you want, heâ€™ll come to your room. Heâ€™ll come to my room! Dixie thought, and she rushed back inside to find Patty getting ready to leave. \r\n\r\nâ€œPatty please I need to borrow your guitar!â€ Dixie gushed. \r\n\r\nâ€œWhat? What is that?â€ Patty asked, and took the flyer from Dixieâ€™s hand. â€œOh god. You want to learn to play the guitar? Dixie, Iâ€™ll show you for free.â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œPatty. Please let me borrow your guitar.â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œSo you can spend ten bucks to hang out with that guy? Dixie, honey, if they listen to the Smiths, that means theyâ€™re gay.â€ \r\n\r\nBut Patty relented, and that night Dixie punched in the numbers for his dorm room extension with shaking fingers. When she heard him say, â€œHello?â€ she hung up. It was spontaneous and totally out of her control, like sneezing. She looked fiercely at the phone for a moment, as if it were at fault, then picked up the receiver again. This time she slammed it down before sheâ€™d hit a single button. There was black terror in her heart, and she felt like she couldnâ€™t catch her breath. She pulled herself heavily up from the floor and lay back on her bed, gathered Mr. Peyton, her stuffed rabbit, into her arms and rocked herself back and forth until she fell asleep. \r\n\r\nIt took her two weeks to get up the nerve to try calling him again. In the meantime, she watched him in the dining commons, seeing him sometimes at lunch, and sometimes at dinner. He was a freshman, and the freshmen boys and girls always seemed to sort themselves out rapidly into cliques and groups. Soon the Art Fag was sitting with a group of boys with fussily arranged hair and girls with too much eyeliner and blood red lips. But Dixie could see that he was unhappy. Even in the midst of his friendsâ€™ excitable chattering and loud laughter, he stayed still and silent, staring into the indeterminate distance as if brooding on the most profound and intractable philosophical problems. Dixie watched him, and she ached to kiss him until he forgot his sensitive suffering, if only for a moment. This would then segue into a delicious fantasia that began with him sitting on her bed, setting his guitar aside and leaning in close to kiss her ears, and ended with her sitting flushed at the dining table, a thin mustache of sweat across her upper lip and her cotton panties damp between her legs, a forgotten spoonful of jello in her hand, her friends laughing. \r\n\r\nShe called him that night and when she hung up she had no recollection at all of what she had said. Dialing had been like the slow ratcheting of a roller coaster climbing a rise and the conversation like the screaming freefall that followed. But she had inked a heart in the space for the Tuesday of the following weeks with â€œSM guitar lesson 7 PMâ€ inside it. \r\n\r\nTuesday came so fast, and then there he was, sitting on her bed, just as she had imagined it. They sat together and he taught her some basic chords, C, F, and G, and she was glad she had an excuse to stare at his hands, which were fine and feminine, and not have to meet his eyes, because she wasnâ€™t sure she could do so and continue breathing. Her head felt like a helium filled balloon, bobbing and rising, and when he reached forward to lightly press her first finger into service on the fiendishly difficult F chord she saw spots in the air and the seashell roar of the ocean filled her ears. \r\n\r\nâ€œSo, are there any songs in particular youâ€™d like to learn to play?â€ he asked. \r\n\r\nShe desperately opened and overturned every drawer in her mind, trying to come up with something he would think was cool. \r\n\r\nâ€œI really like that song â€˜Wanted Dead or Aliveâ€™ by Bon Jovi!â€ she said. \r\n\r\nâ€œYeah, that is a good song,â€ he replied gravely. â€œBut it might be a little hard for you to play at this point.â€ He brushed the long hair back from his eye and gestured at the Jesus paintings Dixieâ€™s mother had given her for wall decorationâ€” Jesus Walking on Water, Jesus with the Halt Man, Jesus Overturning the Tables of the Money Changersâ€”and said, â€œMaybe you know this one, â€œ and started to sing â€œMichael Row the Boat Ashoreâ€ in a reedy tenor, accompanying himself with muted strums on his acoustic guitar. It was actually sort of terrible, and Dixie was still sensible enough to realize it, but the thought that took precedence was Oh my god mommy would love him. \r\n\r\nThe lesson was over so fast. Dixie asked him to stay another half hour-- she was just starting to get the hang of it!â€”but he told her to practice hard, and if she wanted they could get together again the next week. He gave her a toothy smile before he let himself out and she waited at her window until she saw him emerge from her building and get into his late 70s tan Nova and drive away. Dixie changed for bed quickly and turned off the light. She crawled under the covers, then hiked her nightgown up around her waist and clamped Mr. Peyton between her thick thighs. She rolled over on her belly and ground Mr Peyton against the mattress until she came, pressing her face into her pillow and rolling the ends up around her ears to ensure no errant moan escaped. \r\n\r\nHe had a show called Jazz from Hell on the campus radio station. Dixie would turn her tuner all the way to the left every Wednesday night at 10 PM to listen to him play Jean Luc Ponty and Al Dimeola and Scott Henderson. She didnâ€™t really care for the music but she would sit through hours of wailing guitars and off time polyrhythms for a few moments of the Art Fagâ€™s slightly fey surfer boy drawl. Sometimes she would call him at the station number. \r\n\r\nâ€œWWST,â€ heâ€™d say, distracted by trying to cue up his next record. \r\n\r\nâ€œHi. Itâ€™s Dixie,â€ sheâ€™d say. \r\n\r\nâ€œOh, uh, hi, Dixie. Whatâ€™s up?â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œWill you play a song for me?â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œUh, sure. What song?â€ \r\n\r\nWhat song. What song. â€œI donâ€™t know. Something you like. Just please say itâ€™s for me.â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œUh. Sure, no problem.â€ \r\n\r\nThe next time she called him for a lesson he didnâ€™t pick up, and she left a message on his voicemail. He didnâ€™t call her back, so she assumed that the voicemail system, which had just been implemented that year, had eaten her message, so she called him again and left him another one. When he still didnâ€™t reply, she began to panic, wondering if he was ok and thinking of every terrible thing that couldâ€™ve befallen him, but this time when she phoned he answered and asked her why she was calling him over and over. She told him that she wanted to take another guitar lesson and he said he wasnâ€™t teaching anymore. He had joined a band in Springfield and didnâ€™t have the time. He said there were music stores downtown that could teach her \"Koombaya\" if she wanted, and Dixie heard the snicker in his voice. She said thank you, hung up and sat on her floor and cried for 20 minutes. \r\n\r\nThe next day at lunch Dixie could feel eyes upon her as she made her sturdy way to join Patty and the other Ed girls at their table. She was already feeling overheatedâ€” she hated early November, with the dank air outside conspiring with the premature central heat inside to make the sweat bead on her chin and darken her armpitsâ€”but it was worsened by the flush she could feel rising from her tight collar. The Art Fag wasnâ€™t looking at her, but most of his friends were, some looking away when she looked over, and some unselfconsciously gawking at her. She saw two of the prettier ones turn to each other and giggle, and then reach over and pinch the Art Fagâ€™s cheeks. He shook them off in embarrassment and shot an angry glance at Dixie. She snapped her head forward, fixed her gaze on the floor ahead of her and concentrated on getting to the table without tripping over her long coat. Patty didnâ€™t noticed Dixieâ€™s ferocious blush. She sipped her tea and said, â€œOh, I met your friend the Art Fag in the practice rooms at the Music Department. He wasnâ€™t as bad as I thought.â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œHeâ€™s worse,â€ Dixie said under her breath. She took deep breaths and tried to calm herself, but a red haze was settling over the room and all she could think about was pulling out the rock hammer Patty kept in her bag and walking over and pounding a half-dollar sized hole in the Art Fagâ€™s pretty skull. Patty was still talking and unaware that Dixie was controlling a homicidal rage only by digging her nails into her thighs. The deeper she gouged her fingers into the spongy flesh of her legs, the more clarity returned, until finally Dixie was left with bloody welts under the stretched and torn fabric of her tights and a head cooled and cleaned like a street after a terrible summer storm. The problem was obvious, actually. He simply didnâ€™t understand the depth of her feelings. If she could make him understand that this was not a schoolgirl crush, that she was a grown woman and that hers was a mature, ferocious, epic passion, then he too would be swept up by the hellfire gale that was driving her on. \r\n\r\nShe wrote him. She knew his cruelty was love masked by fear and she wrote to console him, to let him know that she knew he was afraid, and that it was ok to feel love, and that he should follow her example of bravery and find the courage to admit that he felt love, too. She wrote poems, sonnets, blank verse. She quoted John Donne and REM. â€œI thought that I saw you laughing, I thought that I heard you sing, I think I thought I saw you try.â€ Dixie went to the post office in downtown Westfield and got a P.O Box. She had five letters in her bag, letters she has left unsigned, and she carefully copied the PO BOX number into the return address. She disciplined herself and mailed them one at a time, one a day. Every afternoon for the next week she approached her residence hall mailbox in an ecstasy of anticipation and suffered a tawdry little death each time she was greeted by an empty mail slot. But one Friday afternoon, two weeks after she had mailed her first letter, she received a reply. \r\n\r\nHer hands tremored and shook like those of a woman four times her age. Dixie pulled the envelope from the box. She tucked her chin into her chest and scuttled up the four flights to her room. She fumbled her key in the lock, then threw her shoulder against the door. She dropped her bag, slammed the door behind her and and tore the letter open. \r\n\r\nShe had written him love poems. He had written her back in limericks. \r\n\r\nShe saw white noise. She smelled chlorine, like her mom was treating the water in the aboveground pool in her backyard. Her hands taloned and crumpled his response into a ball, and the smoothed it over her broad thigh and began tearing it into tiny little pieces. She thought about the vampires in â€œthe Lost Boysâ€ biting into someoneâ€™s head like an apple. She thought about biting the Art Fagâ€™s nose off. She thought about opening the veins in her arms, the thick blue veins inside her elbows that Red Cross nurses always complimented, and walking down into the Dining Commons and collapsing at his feet, so he would understand that being handsome was not a license for cruelty, so that he would see the damage his brutish callousness had caused. She held the shredded page to her face and cried until the ink ran and blotched her cheeks. \r\n\r\nWhen she finally looked up, her digital clock read 6:15 and her stomach gurgled discontentedly. The dining commons was only open for another fifteen minutes. It had started raining, and she dully kicked off her mary janes and pulled on her rubber duckboots. She shrugged her way back into her overcoat, got her umbrella from the closet and headed out into the freezing November night. \r\n\r\nThe cafeteria was nearly empty. It was the weekend, and many of the students usually piled into their friendsâ€™ cars for roadtrips back to Brockton and Carlisle and Swamscott. Dixie got a tray and stepped along the stainless steel serving line, reaching for whatever was available and moving sluggishly along to the next station. Bread, an entrÃ©e of meatloaf and cabbage, a side of corn, a side of mashed potatos, a salad, a piece of strawberry shortcake, another one, a slab of ice cream, a soda. She handed her mealplan card to the lunchlady at the end of line, who swiped it through a machine and handed it back. Dixie took her tray and started for the back of the cafeteria, carefully skirting the area where the Art Fag usually sat. \r\n\r\nShe stopped dead. He was sitting alone at a table at the very end of the Dining Commons. She almost hadnâ€™t recognized him without his stupid beret. He saw she was looking at him and his head snapped back down towards his plate. \r\n\r\nDixie wanted to turn around. More than anything, she wanted to drop her tray and turn on her heel and run out the double doors and through the woods and into the nearby Connecticut River and never surface. Instead she took a step forward, then another, a methodical plod that brought her to his table. She placed her tray on the table across from him and sat down behind it. \r\n\r\nâ€œWe need to talk,â€ she said. \r\n\r\nâ€œUm. We do?â€ he said. \r\n\r\nâ€œYes. I think you know what I am talking about,â€ she said. \r\n\r\nâ€œI donâ€™t. I really donâ€™t,â€ he said. \r\n\r\nâ€œYes, I think you do,â€ she said. Dixie was watching herself from a third person perspective somewhere in the air above and to her left. A silver cord wafted in the air between her two selves. â€œI think you know exactly what we have to talk about.â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œLook, I have no idea what you are talking about, â€œ he said, his voice tinged with desperation. â€œI mean, I donâ€™t mean to be a dick, but really, I was just back here, trying to eat my dinner in peace. I donâ€™t know what you want to talk about, I just want you to leave me alone, ok?â€ \r\n\r\nDixie felt the silver cord snap taut and she heard a sharp crack as she was reeled back in. Once again she saw through her own eyes. The Art Fag was staring at her, his expression both cold and disbelieving. She saw herself leaping at him, heaving her bulk across the table like a bull seal up onto arctic ice, and slamming his face into his plate of fishsticks and mashed potatos, flattening his Kevin Bacon ski jump nose. \r\n\r\nInstead she karate chopped her side of the table and swiped her loaded tray into the aisle, shattering glass and spraying strawberry shortcake all over an unfortunate girl who was sitting at a table adjacent to theirs. \r\n\r\nâ€œYOU FUCK!â€ she screamed. â€œYouâ€™re such a fucking asshole! NO WONDER YOU DONâ€™T HAVE ANY FUCKING FRIENDS!â€ In one deft motion, she stood up and extricated herself from the bench seat. She snatched her bag and umbrella from the floor, did a precise military about face, and began stomping away. She was half way to the door when she stopped. She needed to make him understand. She was walking away, but it was not over. It was far from over. She turned around. \r\n\r\nThe Art Fag watched in horror as Dixie came stalking back towards him. She felt deeply powerful, like one of Pattyâ€™s pagan goddesses, ready to rend and eat the flesh of men. She stopped beside him and leaned down into his face. \r\n\r\nâ€œYou might think Iâ€™m a fucking bitch now,â€ she hissed. â€œBut you havenâ€™t seen bitchy yet!â€ And she turned again and stomped out. She was glad it was raining. It washed the tears and snot from her face. She left her umbrella closed and smashed the garbage cans that lined the sidewalk. She saw the lights over the student union and she cut across the quad, her duckboots sinking inches into the saturated turf. \r\n\r\nHer glasses steamed as she stepped into the Bearâ€™s Den, the campus pub. She pulled them off and she squinted through the lank snarls of hair that draped her face. She pushed the tarty red frames back up her nose and stepped up to the bar. She ordered her dadâ€™s drink. \r\n\r\nâ€œGive me a Makerâ€™s, neat,â€ she demanded. She dropped her ID and a twenty dollar bill on the bar. Drinking her way through it took the better part of two hours, and when the last of the twenty was spent Dixie waited until the student bartender turned his back, and then stumbled out of the bar and back down the student union steps. \r\n\r\nThe rain had stopped, and the sky had cleared. The temperature was dropping and already the sidewalks and railings were icing over. Dixie skated lightly over the frozen cement. She had sojourned long and far on her whiskey trip, and had arrived at a place where a tenuous peace prevailed. Thoughts of the Art Fag had been temporarily muffled under thoughts of warm blankets and fluffy pillows and the attentions of the ever lusty Mr Peyton. \r\n\r\nShe turned the corner onto the street of her residence hall. As she approached the building she realized that the car parked in one of the handicapped spaces in front was familiar. It was a late 70s tan Chevy Nova; it was the Art Fagâ€™s car. Dixie felt something open wide in her chest, and she was sure if she pulled open her coat and her blouse she would see her heart and lungs and ribcage illuminated from within. He had come back to her. He had surely come to apologize for being such an ass, for being blind to the love she offered. She felt terrible when she thought how long he mustâ€™ve been waiting for her, while she was getting pointlessly drunk at the bar. \r\n\r\nShe was about to knock on the driverâ€™s side window when she froze, fist in midair. The windows were steamed, but she could see he was not alone. Dixieâ€™s broad smile melted like tallow held to flame. She began to inch away from the window, circling around the front of the car. The couple wrestling inside were oblivious to her presence. The streetlight shone into the front seat; she could see the Art Fag clearly. But the passenger side was in shadow. As he lunged across the seat he passed into darkness. But his adversary counterattacked, pushing him back hard against his door. Dixie took an involuntary step back. \r\n\r\nPatty. \r\n\r\nDixieâ€™s mouth opened and closed soundlessly. A sharp pain ran through her sinuses and a spasm in her eyes sent a sheet of tears down her cheeks. Giant hands pressed tight against her ears and began to compact her head. Dixieâ€™s gaze fell to the ground at her feet and settled on a chunk of concrete that had crumbled loose from the sidewalk. She bent and picked it up. She tested its heft in her hand, and then in one fluid motion, screaming â€œYOU SUCK YOU SUCK YOU SUCKKKKKâ€ she threw it at the Art Fagâ€™s windshield. It lodged there, suspended, the glass instantly cobwebbing from hood to roof. \r\n\r\nThe driverâ€™s side door flew open. The Art Fag took one step out. â€œWHAT THE FUCK!â€ he screamed. â€œWHAT THEâ€”what the fuck? Dixie?â€ \r\n\r\nPatty got out on the other side. â€œDIXIE what the fuck is WRONG WITH YOU!â€ she screamed. Around the corner a siren whined to life and a spotlight flashed on. \r\n\r\nDixie sprinted for the residence hall door. Not quite halfway there the short heel of her rubber boot skidded from under her and she went down hard, her momentum spinning and sliding her along on her ass until she slammed into the door. Her head smacked wetly against the glass, jarring her eyeglasses from her face. They hit the cement sidewalk and one of the lenses cracked. Her vision swam from the hard conjunction of impact and whiskey. \r\n\r\nShe groped for her glasses with one hand and flailed for the door handle with the other. She pulled herself to her feet and wrenched the door open just as the police car was pulling up alongside the Art Fagâ€™s Nova. â€œStop right there!â€ one of the cops bellowed, but Dixie didnâ€™t hear the rest because she was already scrambling past the second floor landing, desperately hauling herself along on the handrail and hoping she didnâ€™t slip on the wet stairway and break her front teeth on the concrete steps. She made it to the fourth floor landing, threw open the fire door and pounded down the cheaply carpeted corridor to her room. \r\n\r\n\r\nShe let herself in and closed the door. She propped herself back against it and slowly sank to the floor, her legs sprawling under her. She heard the elevator open, and heavy footsteps and the squawk of a radio reciting codes. There was a sharp report as the cop rapped on her door with his nightstick. The vibrating wood translated the knocks painfully to the back of Dixieâ€™s head. \r\n\r\nâ€œMiss Cavendish? Dixie Cavendish? Can you come out here please, Dixie? We need to speak with you,â€ the cop said. Dixie said nothing. There was muffled conversation and Dixie heard her RA say â€œI have a keyâ€ and then the cops were trying to force the door open against the bulk of Dixieâ€™s body. â€œMiss Cavendish?â€ someone said. â€œAre you alright?â€ \r\n\r\nDixie stirred and shifted her weight onto her hands and knees. She crawled away from the door and two cops walked in, male and female. The male cop helped her to her feet and said, â€œMiss Cavendish, we need you to come outside for a moment, we need to ask you some questions, please.â€ \r\n\r\nDixie just nodded mutely. The female cop took her other arm, a little bit more firmly than she needed to, and the three of them walked down the hall to the elevators, followed by Dixieâ€™s RA and the building RD and a small crowd of gawkers. Dixie wished they would let go of her arms long enough for her to brush her hair back from her face, to pull her coat back into place, but she didnâ€™t have the nerve to ask. They rode the elevator down and exited into the dormâ€™s reception area. They walked back through the double doors and out to where the Art Fag and Patty stood alongside the vandalized Nova. \r\n\r\nâ€œIs this the person that threw the rock through your windshield?â€ the female cop asked. Her blond hair has tied tightly at the base of her neck, and she let her right hand rest on the butt of her gun. \r\n\r\nThe Art Fag didnâ€™t say anything. He looked at Dixie, and then at Patty, and then back to Dixie. \r\n\r\nâ€œSir,â€ the female cop said, getting irritated. â€œWe heard the glass smash and we saw someone fleeing into the building. Is this the girl that threw the rock through your car window?â€ \r\n\r\nThe Art Fag started slowly shaking his head. â€œUh, no,â€ he said. â€œIt wasnâ€™t her. It was some kids. Some high school kids in hooded sweatshirts. I couldnâ€™t really see their faces. They threw the rock and then took off over that way, through the woods.â€ He pointed towards the woodline. \r\n\r\nâ€œThatâ€™s my roommate,â€ Patty said. â€œShe saw them throw the rock and ran inside to call the police. You showed up so fast!â€ \r\n\r\nThe cops looked at each other. The female cop let go of Dixieâ€™s arm and took a step towards the Art Fag. Her voice was strained. â€œAre you sure thatâ€™s what you saw?â€ she said. \r\n\r\nâ€œUm, yes, maâ€™am,â€ he said. He looked at Dixie again. â€œThree kids in blue sweatshirts. One threw a rock and they ran off that way. That girl was just walking into the building, and she saw it and said sheâ€™d call the cops. I mean, police. Maâ€™am.â€ \r\n\r\nThe male cop sighed. â€œSon, do you want to file a report on the matter?â€ \r\n\r\nâ€œNo sir, thatâ€™s ok. It was just some kids looking to have fun. I donâ€™t want to get anyone into trouble,â€ the Art Fag said. \r\n\r\nâ€œSuit yourself, son,â€ the cop said. He climbed into the passenger seat of the cruiser, and the female cop took the wheel. The Crown Victoria slowly pulled out, bubble lights still flashing red. \r\n\r\nPatty and the Art Fag stared at Dixie. She stared back at them. She took a handkerchief from the pocket of her overcoat and pulled her glasses off her nose. She wiped the steamy condensation from the cracked lenses, pushed her glasses back onto her face, and without a word turned on her heel and walked back into the building. She pushed her way through the small knot of girls in sweatpants and pajamas that crowded the lobby and took the slow elevator back up to her room. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2197,'<p>yesterday, as i was typing in my text editor, it displayed a message to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Happy New Year!\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it was the first time my text editor has ever communicated with me.  thank you, text editor.  you too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have a friend who sits on my ICQ contact list, waiting to be clicked on.  she lives far away, and in fact we\'ve never lived close to each other.  we cross paths a few times a year, sometimes for a few minutes, sometimes for an afternoon.  i\'ve never quite known what to make of her.  we went shopping for paintbrushes one day in ottawa, leaning against the biting wind on bank street.  she said things that surprised me, that i couldn\'t anticipate, and challenged me, and i realized that she was a good person to talk with.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>two weeks ago, we met again, and hung around queen street in toronto.  i mentioned that i wondered what things would look like without the photons bouncing around my eyes, and the same thing had crossed her mind too.  how would you paint that?  she tried on funny hats (one made her look like a french nun), and we drank juice next door at the java house, surrounded by garage sale decor.  a mountain scene was painted on the wall with sponges, and the light switches were out in plain view, tempting all the pranksters. (me)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i returned home, thinking how nice it was to spend time with someone who appreciates, enjoys, and worries about the same things as me.  when we rely on the keyboard to communicate, something is lost with every key we tap.  i sometimes find myself doubting the meaning of what i type, or doubting the sincerity of the person i\'m typing to.  is this person really a friend, or just an acquaintance?  do they really want to be here?  words mean so much more when they\'re accompanied by gestures, sounds, actions.  and i don\'t mean that guns\'n\'roses riff that plays every time a message pops up on the screen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>even silence means something in person, whereas silence on the internet means that your counterpart stepped out for a pee, or a cigarette.  i\'m not a luddite or a technophobe, in fact i\'ve been chatting with my friends for years.  as time passes, though, i realize that some things are far too important to trust to ICQ.  i\'ve also learned to appreciate my friends, who are mostly total opposites of myself, and to especially appreciate it when i run across someone who\'s kind of like me.</p>'),(2198,'<br><br>Dixie thought he was beautiful. Patty took one look at him and pronounced, â€œArt fag.â€ She said that where she was from, in Newton, guys wearing converse high tops matched with London fog overcoats and black berets were a sudden infestation, like rats fleeing storm swollen sewers. â€œGod, ever since MTV started showing â€˜120 minutes,â€™â€ she said. â€œI bet he read â€˜The Strangerâ€™ because he thought it would make him as deep as Robert Smith.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nAll Dixie knew was that he was different from every other guy on the campus of their backwater state college. Westfield State was a repository for every townie kid who hadnâ€™t possessed the skills for an athletic scholarship to a name brand school or the grades to make it into the state university. The boys wore blue jeans tucked into untied workboots, rails cut into the sides of their whiffle haircuts, and the girls wore sweatpants everyday, their hair tied into Pebbles Flintstone topknots. But the Art Fag wasnâ€™t one of them. It was true about the beret and the overcoat, and he had offset all that black with an ill-considered Hawaiian shirt, but he had sharp cheekbones and hair cut to fall long over one eye. It was his one visible eye she fixated on, as she stared at him sitting alone in the Dining Commons. He had blue eyes, like the blue her mother had given the paint-by-numbers Jesus Dixie had hanging on her dorm room wall. And it wasnâ€™t Camus he was reading for company, no matter what that smarty pants Patty said. It was Nietzsche. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie also felt herself to be out of place at Westfield. She was short and bigboned, like her grandmother had once said. â€œItâ€™s not that youâ€™re fat, Dixie. Youâ€™re just big boned. Good strong farm people, thatâ€™s who youâ€™re from.â€ Her daddy had put it more bluntly. â€œWhy, youâ€™re just a cute lâ€™il olâ€™ fireplug, ainâ€™tcha honey,â€ he said. â€œDonâ€™t ever stand too long on the sidewalk now, some pupâ€™ll likely come by and mark his territory!â€ Dixie was terribly nearsighted, but too astigmatic for contact lenses, and had to suffer with thick lensed glasses, although she tried to compensate by secretly spending $200 of her babysitting money on a pair of fancy fashion frames. The bright red plastic of the glasses tended to unpleasantly offset the ashyness of her complexion, at least in direct sunlight, but she loved them because she thought they lent her an air of barely suppressed naughtiness, like one of the teachers in that Van Halen video.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie was an education major, set to graduate that spring. Westfield State had originally been a teacherâ€™s college, and the School of Education was still the largest department on campus. Dixie had successfully made it through four years, her most notable achievement being the most anonymous student that ever passed through those hallowed halls. Half of her instructors would have been unable to pick her out a lineup as a former student. Still, Dixie was a dedicated student, and not above helping her classmates with an assignment or two when they just hadnâ€™t been able to get around to completing it, and she had made herself a few friends along the way. One was Patty, who in her own way was as odd as the Art Fag. Patty was obsessed with Celtic imagery and Druidic beliefs, and dressed in an idiosyncratic welter of Scottish tartan and Stevie Nicks inspired lace. She played the bagpipes, and toted them around in a haversack that also contained a collection of quartz crystals that wouldâ€™ve been the pride of most mid-sized geological departments, and a well thumbed deck of tarot cards. Dixie had been paralyzed with fear when Patty had first produced them, certain that Patty was a Satanist; she was only slightly mollified when Patty told her she was actually a Catholic. Patty was pretty, in a Buffy Ste Marie sort of way, but she was a virgin, like Dixie. She had confided this to Dixie after they had shared half a pint of Peach Shnapps, sitting on the floor of Pattyâ€™s dorm room, candles burning and scarves draped over the desk lamps. â€œI just donâ€™t like boys,â€ sheâ€™d whispered. â€œI mean, I like boys, but I donâ€™t like thinking about, you know, their <i>things</i>. Touching their things. Itâ€™s gross, right?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie changed the subject. She pushed the deck of cards, wrapped in black velvet, across the floor to Patty. â€œWill you read my future for me?â€ she asked. The truth was, Dixie thought about boysâ€™ things all the time. She knew the church said  it was sinful, and that she was supposed to confess impure thoughts before she went to Mass, but she had read enough books to know that thinking about boysâ€™ things was totally natural and nothing to be ashamed of. Sheâ€™d had a terrible crush on Nick Rhodes from Duran Duran, and later on John Cusack in â€œSay Anything.â€ At 22 sheâ€™d never even kissed a boy, but sheâ€™d read <i>Forever</i> and <i>Flowers in the Attic</i> and <i>The Story of O</i>, paperbacks with fake covers taped on in order to smuggle them passed her mother, and she had a rich and varied fantasy life involving Johnny Depp and David Hasselhoff and a host of others. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nBut now here was this new boy, sitting not twenty feet from her in the Dining Commons, and all thoughts of David Hasselhoff and his broad shoulders and fine wavy hair were gone forever. But that twenty feet was a chasm, an abyss she could never cross. She could never just walk up to him and start talking. What could she possibly say that would get him to look past her squat fire hydrant frame and see the sylph inside her? How could she explain that she saw that he was pure beauty and was the sudden new axis of her world?\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe Art Fag stood up, shouldered his book bag and picked up a guitar case that had been hidden under the table. He took his tray up to the counter and then walked out the door, pausing at the message board mounted outside long enough to tack up a sheet of paper he pulled from inside his coat. Dixie waited until he was out of sight, forced herself to count to ten, then got up and dashed outside.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œHave You Always Wanted to Play Guitar?â€ the notice said. He was offering guitar lessons, $10 for half an hour, teach you to play whatever you want, heâ€™ll come to your room. Heâ€™ll come to my room! Dixie thought, and she rushed back inside to find Patty getting ready to leave. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œPatty please I need to borrow your guitar!â€ Dixie gushed. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œWhat? What is that?â€ Patty asked, and took the flyer from Dixieâ€™s hand. â€œOh god. You want to learn to play the guitar? Dixie, Iâ€™ll show you for free.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œPatty. Please let me borrow your guitar.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œSo you can spend ten bucks to hang out with that guy? Dixie, honey, if they listen to the Smiths, that means theyâ€™re gay.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nBut Patty relented, and that night Dixie punched in the numbers for his dorm room extension with shaking fingers. When she heard him say, â€œHello?â€ she hung up. It was spontaneous and totally out of her control, like sneezing. She looked fiercely at the phone for a moment, as if it were at fault, then picked up the receiver again. This time she slammed it down before sheâ€™d hit a single button. There was black terror in her heart, and she felt like she couldnâ€™t catch her breath. She pulled herself heavily up from the floor and lay back on her bed, gathered Mr. Peyton, her stuffed rabbit, into her arms and rocked herself back and forth until she fell asleep.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nIt took her two weeks to get up the nerve to try calling him again. In the meantime, she watched him in the dining commons, seeing him sometimes at lunch, and sometimes at dinner. He was a freshman, and the freshmen boys and girls always seemed to sort themselves out rapidly into cliques and groups. Soon the Art Fag was sitting with a group of boys with fussily arranged hair and girls with too much eyeliner and blood red lips. But Dixie could see that he was unhappy. Even in the midst of his friendsâ€™ excitable chattering and loud laughter, he stayed still and silent, staring into the indeterminate distance as if brooding on the most profound and intractable philosophical problems. Dixie watched him, and she ached to kiss him until he forgot his sensitive suffering, if only for a moment. This would then segue into a delicious fantasia that began with him sitting on her bed, setting his guitar aside and leaning in close to kiss her ears, and ended with her sitting flushed at the dining table, a thin mustache of sweat across her upper lip and her cotton panties damp between her legs, a forgotten spoonful of jello in her hand, her friends laughing.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe called him that night and when she hung up she had no recollection at all of what she had said. Dialing had been like the slow ratcheting of a roller coaster climbing a rise and the conversation like the screaming freefall that followed. But she had inked a heart in the space for the Tuesday of the following weeks with â€œSM guitar lesson 7 PMâ€ inside it.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nTuesday came so fast, and then there he was, sitting on her bed, just as she had imagined it. They sat together and he taught her some basic chords, C, F, and G, and she was glad she had an excuse to stare at his hands, which were fine and feminine, and not have to meet his eyes, because she wasnâ€™t sure she could do so and continue breathing. Her head felt like a helium filled balloon, bobbing and rising, and when he reached forward to lightly press her first finger into service on the fiendishly difficult F chord she saw spots in the air and the seashell roar of the ocean filled her ears.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œSo, are there any songs in particular youâ€™d like to learn to play?â€ he asked.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe desperately opened and overturned every drawer in her mind, trying to come up with something he would think was cool.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œI really like that song â€˜Wanted Dead or Aliveâ€™ by Bon Jovi!â€ she said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œYeah, that is a good song,â€ he replied gravely. â€œBut it might be a little hard for you to play at this point.â€ He brushed the long hair back from his eye and gestured at the Jesus paintings Dixieâ€™s mother had given her for wall decorationâ€” Jesus Walking on Water, Jesus with the Halt Man, Jesus Overturning the Tables of the Money Changersâ€”and said, â€œMaybe you know this one, â€œ and started to sing â€œMichael Row the Boat Ashoreâ€ in a reedy tenor, accompanying himself with muted strums on his acoustic guitar. It was actually sort of terrible, and Dixie was still sensible enough to realize it, but the thought that took precedence was <i>Oh my god mommy would love him.</i>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe lesson was over so fast. Dixie asked him to stay another half hour--  she was just starting to get the hang of it!â€”but he told her to practice hard, and if she wanted they could get together again the next week. He gave her a toothy smile before he let himself out and she waited at her window until she saw him emerge from her building and get into his late 70s tan Nova and drive away. Dixie changed for bed quickly and turned off the light. She crawled under the covers, then hiked her nightgown up around her waist and clamped Mr. Peyton between her thick thighs. She rolled over on her belly and ground Mr Peyton against the mattress until she came, pressing her face into her pillow and rolling the ends up around her ears to ensure no errant moan escaped. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nHe had a show called <i>Jazz from Hell</i> on the campus radio station. Dixie would turn her tuner all the way to the left every Wednesday night at 10 PM to listen to him play Jean Luc Ponty and Al Dimeola and Scott Henderson. She didnâ€™t really care for the music but she would sit through hours of wailing guitars and off time polyrhythms for a few moments of the Art Fagâ€™s slightly fey surfer boy drawl. Sometimes she would call him at the station number.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œWWST,â€ heâ€™d say, distracted by trying to cue up his next record.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œHi. Itâ€™s Dixie,â€ sheâ€™d say.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œOh, uh, hi, Dixie. Whatâ€™s up?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œWill you play a song for me?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œUh, sure. What song?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nWhat song. What song. â€œI donâ€™t know. Something you like. Just please say itâ€™s for me.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œUh. Sure, no problem.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe next time she called him for a lesson he didnâ€™t pick up, and she left a message on his voicemail. He didnâ€™t call her back, so she assumed that the voicemail system, which had just been implemented that year, had eaten her message, so she called him again and left him another one. When he still didnâ€™t reply, she began to panic, wondering if he was ok and thinking of every terrible thing that couldâ€™ve befallen him, but this time when she phoned he answered and asked her why she was calling him over and over. She told him that she wanted to take another guitar lesson and he said he wasnâ€™t teaching anymore. He had joined a band in Springfield and didnâ€™t have the time. He said there were music stores downtown that could teach her \"Koombaya\" if she wanted, and Dixie heard the snicker in his voice. She said thank you, hung up and sat on her floor and cried for 20 minutes.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe next day at lunch Dixie could feel eyes upon her as she made her sturdy way to join Patty and the other Ed girls at their table. She was already feeling overheatedâ€” she hated early November, with the dank air outside conspiring with the premature central heat inside to make the sweat bead on her chin and darken her armpitsâ€”but it was worsened by the flush she could feel rising from her tight collar. The Art Fag wasnâ€™t looking at her, but most of his friends were, some looking away when she looked over, and some unselfconsciously gawking at her. She saw two of the prettier ones turn to each other and giggle, and then reach over and pinch the Art Fagâ€™s cheeks. He shook them off in embarrassment and shot an angry glance at Dixie. She snapped her head forward, fixed her gaze on the floor ahead of her and concentrated on getting to the table without tripping over her long coat. Patty didnâ€™t noticed Dixieâ€™s ferocious blush. She sipped her tea and said, â€œOh, I met your friend the Art Fag in the practice rooms at the Music Department. He wasnâ€™t as bad as I thought.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œHeâ€™s worse,â€ Dixie said under her breath. She took deep breaths and tried to calm herself, but a red haze was settling over the room and all she could think about was pulling out the rock hammer Patty kept in her bag and walking over and pounding a half-dollar sized hole in the Art Fagâ€™s pretty skull. Patty was still talking and unaware that Dixie was controlling a homicidal rage only by digging her nails into her thighs. The deeper she gouged her fingers into the spongy flesh of her legs, the more clarity returned, until finally Dixie was left with bloody welts under the stretched and torn fabric of her tights and a head cooled and cleaned like a street after a terrible summer storm. The problem was obvious, actually. He simply didnâ€™t understand the depth of her feelings. If she could make him understand that this was not a schoolgirl crush, that she was a grown woman and that hers was a mature, ferocious, epic passion, then he too would be swept up by the hellfire gale that was driving her on.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe wrote him. She knew his cruelty was love masked by fear and she wrote to console him, to let him know that she knew he was afraid, and that it was ok to feel love, and that he should follow her example of bravery and find the courage to admit that he felt love, too. She wrote poems, sonnets, blank verse. She quoted John Donne and REM. â€œI thought that I saw you laughing, I thought that I heard you sing, I think I thought I saw you try.â€ Dixie went to the post office in downtown Westfield and got a P.O Box. She had five letters in her bag, letters she has left unsigned, and she carefully copied the PO BOX number into the return address. She disciplined herself and mailed them one at a time, one a day. Every afternoon for the next week she approached her residence hall mailbox in an ecstasy of anticipation and suffered a tawdry little death each time she was greeted by an empty mail slot. But one Friday afternoon, two weeks after she had mailed her first letter, she received a reply.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nHer hands tremored and shook like those of a woman four times her age. Dixie pulled the envelope from the box. She tucked her chin into her chest and scuttled up the four flights to her room. She fumbled her key in the lock, then threw her shoulder against the door. She dropped her bag, slammed the door behind her and and tore the letter open.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe had written him love poems. He had written her back in limericks. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe saw white noise. She smelled chlorine, like her mom was treating the water in the aboveground pool in her backyard. Her hands taloned and crumpled his response into a ball, and the smoothed it over her broad thigh and began tearing it into tiny little pieces. She thought about the vampires in â€œthe Lost Boysâ€ biting into someoneâ€™s head like an apple. She thought about biting the Art Fagâ€™s nose off. She thought about opening the veins in her arms, the thick blue veins inside her elbows that Red Cross nurses always complimented, and walking down into the Dining Commons and collapsing at his feet, so he would understand that being handsome was not a license for cruelty, so that he would see the damage his brutish callousness had caused. She held the shredded page to her face and cried until the ink ran and blotched her cheeks.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nWhen she finally looked up, her digital clock read 6:15 and her stomach gurgled discontentedly. The dining commons was only open for another fifteen minutes. It had started raining, and she dully kicked off her mary janes and pulled on her rubber duckboots. She shrugged her way back into her overcoat, got her umbrella from the closet and headed out into the freezing November night.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe cafeteria was nearly empty. It was the weekend, and many of the students usually piled into their friendsâ€™ cars for roadtrips back to Brockton and Carlisle and Swamscott. Dixie got a tray and stepped along the stainless steel serving line, reaching for whatever was available and moving sluggishly along to the next station. Bread, an entrÃ©e of meatloaf and cabbage, a side of corn, a side of mashed potatos, a salad, a piece of strawberry shortcake, another one, a slab of ice cream, a soda. She handed her mealplan card to the lunchlady at the end of line, who swiped it through a machine and handed it back. Dixie took her tray and started for the back of the cafeteria, carefully skirting the area where the Art Fag usually sat.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe stopped dead. He was sitting alone at a table at the very end of the Dining Commons. She almost hadnâ€™t recognized him without his stupid beret. He saw she was looking at him and his head snapped back down towards his plate.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie wanted to turn around. More than anything, she wanted to drop her tray and turn on her heel and run out the double doors and through the woods and into the nearby Connecticut River and never surface. Instead she took a step forward, then another, a methodical plod that brought her to his table. She placed her tray on the table across from him and sat down behind it.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œWe need to talk,â€ she said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œUm. We do?â€ he said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œYes. I think you know what I am talking about,â€ she said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œI donâ€™t. I really donâ€™t,â€ he said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œYes, I think you do,â€ she said. Dixie was watching herself from a third person perspective somewhere in the air above and to her left. A silver cord wafted in the air between her two selves.  â€œI think you know exactly what we have to talk about.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œLook, I have no idea what you are talking about, â€œ he said, his voice tinged with desperation. â€œI mean, I donâ€™t mean to be a dick, but really, I was just back here, trying to eat my dinner in peace. I donâ€™t know what you want to talk about, I just want you to leave me alone, ok?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie felt the silver cord snap taut and she heard a sharp crack as she was reeled back in. Once again she saw through her own eyes. The Art Fag was staring at her, his expression both cold and disbelieving. She saw herself leaping at him, heaving her bulk across the table like a bull seal up onto arctic ice, and slamming his face into his plate of fishsticks and mashed potatos, flattening his Kevin Bacon ski jump nose. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nInstead she karate chopped her side of the table and swiped her loaded tray into the aisle, shattering glass and spraying strawberry shortcake all over an unfortunate girl who was sitting at a table adjacent to theirs.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œYOU FUCK!â€ she screamed. â€œYouâ€™re such a fucking asshole! NO WONDER YOU DONâ€™T HAVE ANY FUCKING FRIENDS!â€ In one deft motion, she stood up and extricated herself from the bench seat. She snatched her bag and umbrella from the floor, did a precise military about face, and began stomping away. She was half way to the door when she stopped. She needed to make him understand. She was walking away, but it was not over. It was <i>far</i> from over. She turned around.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe Art Fag watched in horror as Dixie came stalking back towards him. She felt deeply powerful, like one of Pattyâ€™s pagan goddesses, ready to rend and eat the flesh of men. She stopped beside him and leaned down into his face.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œYou might think Iâ€™m a fucking bitch now,â€ she hissed. â€œBut you havenâ€™t seen bitchy yet!â€ And she turned again and stomped out. She was glad it was raining.  It washed the tears and snot from her face. She left her umbrella closed and smashed the garbage cans that lined the sidewalk. She saw the lights over the student union and she cut across the quad, her duckboots sinking inches into the saturated turf. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nHer glasses steamed as she stepped into the Bearâ€™s Den, the campus pub. She pulled them off and she squinted through the lank snarls of hair that draped her face. She pushed the tarty red frames back up her nose and stepped up to the bar. She ordered her dadâ€™s drink.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œGive me a Makerâ€™s, neat,â€ she demanded. She dropped her ID and a twenty dollar bill on the bar. Drinking her way through it took the better part of two hours, and when the last of the twenty was spent Dixie waited until the student bartender turned his back, and then stumbled out of the bar and back down the student union steps.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe rain had stopped, and the sky had cleared. The temperature was dropping and already the sidewalks and railings were icing over. Dixie skated lightly over the frozen cement. She had sojourned long and far on her whiskey trip, and had arrived at a place where a tenuous peace prevailed.  Thoughts of the Art Fag had been temporarily muffled under thoughts of warm blankets and fluffy pillows and the attentions of the ever lusty Mr Peyton.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe turned the corner onto the street of her residence hall. As she approached the building she realized that the car parked in one of the handicapped spaces in front was familiar. It was a late 70s tan Chevy Nova; it was the Art Fagâ€™s car. Dixie felt something open wide in her chest, and she was sure if she pulled open her coat and her blouse she would see her heart and lungs and ribcage illuminated from within. He had come back to her. He had surely come to apologize for being such an ass, for being blind to the love she offered. She felt terrible when she thought how long he mustâ€™ve been waiting for her, while she was getting pointlessly drunk at the bar. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe was about to knock on the driverâ€™s side window when she froze, fist in midair. The windows were steamed, but she could see he was not alone. Dixieâ€™s broad smile melted like tallow held to flame. She began to inch away from the window, circling around the front of the car. The couple wrestling inside were oblivious to her presence. The streetlight shone into the front seat; she could see the Art Fag clearly. But the passenger side was in shadow. As he lunged across the seat he passed into darkness. But his adversary counterattacked, pushing him back hard against his door.  Dixie took an involuntary step back.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nPatty.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixieâ€™s mouth opened and closed soundlessly. A sharp pain ran through her sinuses and a  spasm in her eyes sent a sheet of tears down her cheeks. Giant hands pressed tight against her ears and began to compact her head. Dixieâ€™s gaze fell to the ground at her feet and settled on a chunk of concrete that had crumbled loose from the sidewalk. She bent and picked it up. She tested its heft in her hand, and then in one fluid motion, screaming â€œYOU SUCK YOU SUCK YOU SUCKKKKKâ€ she threw it at the Art Fagâ€™s windshield. It lodged there, suspended, the glass instantly cobwebbing from hood to roof. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe driverâ€™s side door flew open. The Art Fag took one step out. â€œWHAT THE FUCK!â€ he screamed. â€œWHAT THEâ€”what the fuck? Dixie?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nPatty got out on the other side. She was screaming too. â€œDIXIE what the fuck is WRONG WITH YOU!â€ Around the corner a siren whined to life and a spotlight flashed on.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie sprinted for the residence hall door. Not quite halfway there the short heel of her rubber boot skidded from under her and she went down hard, her momentum spinning and sliding her along on her ass until she slammed into the door. Her head smacked wetly against the glass, jarring her eyeglasses from her face. They hit the cement sidewalk and one of the lenses cracked. Her vision swam from the hard conjunction of impact and whiskey. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe groped for her glasses with one hand and flailed for the door handle with the other. She pulled herself to her feet and wrenched the door open just as the police car was pulling up alongside the Art Fagâ€™s Nova. â€œStop right there!â€ one of the cops bellowed, but Dixie didnâ€™t hear the rest because she was already scrambling past the second floor landing, desperately hauling herself along on the handrail and hoping she didnâ€™t slip on the wet stairway and break her front teeth on the concrete steps. She made it to the fourth floor landing, threw open the fire door and pounded down the cheaply carpeted corridor to her room.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nShe let herself in and closed the door. She propped herself back against it and slowly sank to the floor, her legs sprawling under her. She heard the elevator open, and heavy footsteps and the squawk of a radio reciting codes. There was a sharp report as the cop rapped on her door with his nightstick. The vibrating wood translated the knocks painfully to the back of Dixieâ€™s head.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œMiss Cavendish? Dixie Cavendish? Can you come out here please, Dixie? We need to speak with you,â€ the cop said. Dixie said nothing. There was muffled conversation and Dixie heard her RA say â€œI have a keyâ€ and then the cops were trying to force the door open against the bulk of Dixieâ€™s body. â€œMiss Cavendish?â€ someone said. â€œAre you alright?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie stirred and shifted her weight onto her hands and knees. She crawled away from the door and two cops walked in, male and female. The male cop  helped her to her feet and said, â€œMiss Cavendish, we need you to come outside for a moment, we need to ask you some questions, please.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDixie just nodded mutely. The female cop took her other arm, a little bit more firmly than she needed to, and the three of them walked down the hall to the elevators, followed by Dixieâ€™s RA and the building RD and a small crowd of gawkers. Dixie wished they would let go of her arms long enough for her to brush her hair back from her face, to pull her coat back into place, but she didnâ€™t have the nerve to ask. They rode the elevator down and exited into the dormâ€™s reception area. They walked back through the double doors and out to where the Art Fag and Patty stood alongside the vandalized Nova.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œIs this the person that threw the rock through your windshield?â€ the female cop asked. Her blond hair has tied tightly at the base of her neck, and she let her right hand rest on the butt of her gun.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe Art Fag didnâ€™t say anything. He looked at Dixie, and then at Patty, and then back to Dixie.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œSir,â€ the female cop said, getting irritated. â€œWe heard the glass smash and we saw someone fleeing into the building. Is this the girl that threw the rock through your car window?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe Art Fag started slowly shaking his head. â€œUh, no,â€ he said. â€œIt wasnâ€™t her. It was some kids. Some high school kids in hooded sweatshirts. I couldnâ€™t really see their faces. They threw the rock and then took off over that way, through the woods.â€ He pointed towards the woodline.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œThatâ€™s my roommate,â€ Patty said. â€œShe saw them throw the rock and ran inside to call the police. You showed up so fast!â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe cops looked at each other. The female cop let go of Dixieâ€™s arm and took a step towards the Art Fag. Her voice was strained. â€œAre you sure thatâ€™s what you saw?â€ she said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œUm, yes, maâ€™am,â€ he said. He looked at Dixie again. â€œThree kids in blue sweatshirts. One threw a rock and they ran off that way. That girl was just walking into the building, and she saw it and said sheâ€™d call the cops. I mean, police. Maâ€™am.â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe male cop sighed. â€œSon, do you want to file a report on the matter?â€\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œNo sir, thatâ€™s ok. It was just some kids looking to have fun. I donâ€™t want to get anyone into trouble,â€ the Art Fag said.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nâ€œSuit yourself, son,â€ the cop said. He climbed into the passenger seat of the cruiser, and the female cop took the wheel. The Crown Victoria slowly pulled out, bubble lights still flashing red.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nPatty and the Art Fag stared at Dixie. She stared back at them. She took a handkerchief from the pocket of her overcoat and pulled her glasses off her nose. She wiped the steamy condensation from the cracked lenses, pushed her glasses back onto her face, and without a word turned on her heel and walked back into the building. She pushed her way through the small knot of girls in sweatpants and pajamas that crowded the lobby and took the slow elevator back up to her room.\r\n<br>'),(2199,'Dear God, \r\n\r\n<p>How\'s it hanging, O Great One? Alright. How was your New Year\'s? Did you and Joseph do one too many kegstands? I hear that. What\'s that? Oh yeah, Dick Clark is really getting old, yeah I know. Death\'s door? Really? Interesting. Bin Laden too? Already there? But wait, why is he up there? Really. Who would have thought that under that beard is a champion cocksucker? Well, at least you got him up there for a reason. Yeah. Well, we all need a little head, even your right hand man, Jes. Hey, Yahweh. Who you got going for the Niner game next week? Really, beat the spread? Oh no, career ending? Oh. Well, he\'s got all offseason. So what\'s going on this week with you? Wow, even up in heaven, Bob Hope hosts a golf tourney. Jeez, Yahweh, you really know how to relax. Do you still gotta get up at five in the morning to golf up there? Oh, only fishing. That makes sense. Well, Big G, I gotta go cuz my doctor just gave me some more thorazine. Ta ta for now, You Master of the Universe, you. Stay black.</p>'),(2200,'<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmost lights off. i am sitting across from her and a pad of paper is between us. she told me that most people like to use the pad of paper to take notes, she said that most people forget a lot of things that she says and that they like to use the pad to scribble things down so they won\'t.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nnext to pad of paper, two candles lit. one green and one white. she says white is for truth, green is for healing. she has always been truthful she says, she has never held anything back.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand incense too. there is incense burning, it\'s smoke is clouding the room with jasmine.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand behind her are stacks of tapes leaning, and one is clearly labeled jesus christ superstar in thick red ink. the others, i can only imagine, are of the same ilk.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbefore- the ride over, the day grey- the strokes came on the radio. i took that as a good sign.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nmy mother was driving, she knew where to go. she had been in that house, with that pad and those candles and incense- she had gone and had talked, and she said that this woman definitely had the gift. the gift.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthe woman told me that she worked on voices much like card readers work on cards. she said  that, in order for her to get a correct reading, she would have to hear my voice more.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'so,\' she said, lipstick on her teeth, \'ask me a question.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni asked her if i was going to meet a girl.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand she smiled. and she said that, yes, i would indeed meet a girl. and that this girl, she would be named either sue or maureen- and her father named george (or jorge)- and that we would meet at a church. and that this girl, she would be the one that i would marry and that, together, we would have children (atleast two). she said that this girl, sue or maureen, would be a teacher and that she would not put up with any sort of drug use. she said that this girl would be the one to set me straight.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nshe also said that my grandmother was helping me out with that situation, that she was working on this from the other side. and right then, the idea of my grandmother networking with all the other grandmothers up in heaven, trying to find me a girlfriend, put on my face the biggest smile you have ever seen.</p> \r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\ni saw sarah later that night. we had fun. drinking coffee and shopping for books. catching up on things and laughing alot. and laughing alot is good, especially with old friends.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand while driving in her car i was explaining all of this to her.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i said, \'yeah, but she also asked me if i liked chinese food.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sarah laughs. and sarah\'s laugh is a laugh that closely resembles one that hello kitty would make if you were to surprise her suddenly with chocolate cake. it is very cute. it is a loud burst.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i said, \'yeah. chinese food. this lady told me that my mom and i were going to have chinese food, sometime in the future, possibly in san francisco.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'but really, you know? like, how is that important? and i\'m paying you sixty bucks for this?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sarah laughs again.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'and see, this is the weird thing: she kept coming up with the names of people that i hardly know, and she would tell me, very earnestly, that these people are destined to receive everything they want out of life, and that i should tell them that she said that.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'you know? like, friends of friends. people that don\'t even matter to me in any sort of way.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nlater on, sarah and i drink coffee in dennys. we are smoking cigarettes under bright white neon. and the place is packed. everyone back for the holidays and we see people we know, and we say hello to them and chat, and it is a pretty good time. talking and seeing people, drinking coffee and smoking, and eventually i am caught up on the lives of everyone that i have not seen and whom i care about in varying amounts.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nnic, nic is still in binghamton, doing post graduate work in comparative lit. and sam, sam is living in los angeles with alanna and working with music. and lori, well lori is still lori and she, yes, she is still together with zack. and brian is still together with lisa, and they\'re engaged(!), and he\'s getting better on the decks and he might start spinning out soon. and tom is still in la and is finding work finally. and the other tom (spelled t-h-o-m) moved back from boston and has changed his last name.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand other people too. tons and tons. and all of them have lives now, and they\'ve all grown up. and, for the most part, everyone is happy. and, mostly, no one has changed a bit except for the better.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand when they ask me how i am, i say good. and when they ask me if san francisco has been good, i say yes, yes san francisco has been good. and sometimes i throw a \'for the most part\' in to qualify. sometimes it\'s, \'yes san francisco has been good, for the most part.\' but sometimes there is no \'for the most part,\' sometimes it is just good.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand they all say, wow!, your hair, it\'s blonde. and i say really?, i hadn\'t noticed. and we laugh. and they all say it looks good, and some of them (i can tell) actually mean it.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand they all ask if i\'m spinning out, and i say yeah, you know, sporadically- but if i could just get my f\'ing demo done, i could probably do that more often- but, you know, i\'m a perfectionist and for the most part, too picky.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand when no one is there and it is just sarah and me, we keep catching up and laughing and drinking coffee with cigarettes.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i don\'t know how long it is until i ask, but i do eventually. i ask her about rhiannon. because rhiannon is someone who i have yet to see and someone who i would most definitely would liked to have seen (</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand rhiannon is someone who i have thought about, off and on, since moving. she is someone who i thought needed to be in san francisco- or somewhere like san francisco- or anywhere. anywhere but clifton park. she was just the smartest girl ever.</p> \r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\never. and whenever she would listen to you talk she would nod her head and close her eyes and smile for a second, and you knew- you just knew- that she cared.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand honestly, she was just the best friend. the best. and i eventually lost touch with her, because that\'s just what happens sometimes.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nbut san francisco makes me think of her. i remember once writing something about her putting everyone out here to shame. san francisco is warm and elegant like she was, with the same sort of sophisticated wisdom, you know? like, i don\'t read melville because i\'m supposed to, i read it because i like it.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nthere is a corner on market that i can see her standing on every time i pass by it. on the curb, the buildings behind her, her hair flowing in the wind. i was tempted to take a picture of it, to show it to her and say, \'here. you need to stand here. this corner was made for you. this is why you need to come to san francisco.\').</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sarah says, \'have you heard?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand i say, \'no, heard what?\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sarah says, \'rhiannon\'s a mommy.\'</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand believe me, if i was a cartoon, i would have done a spittake right then. i almost did one anyway, on purpose, in the spirit of the occasion.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\nand sarah says, \'i don\'t think she\'s very happy.</p>\r\n<p align=\"justify\">\r\n\'and it\'s shifty, cause if anyone deserved to get out of this town, it was her.\'</p>'),(2202,'<p>\r\nnew years eve was a few nights ago\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nim going to eat curry to clense my body for the coming year\r\n</p><p>\r\nlots of good things happened\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hope to have lots more good things\r\n</p><p>\r\nhere are a few:\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ get a job in a good location. maybe that is in the bay area, maybe it is not?  here are some locations:  oakland, san francisco, los angeles, san diego, singapore, bangkok, new york\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ learn a new programming language.  maybe java or perl.  im a little farther along in perl but java has allot of jobs to choose from  and in this bad economy, decisions can be dictated.  i hate that though.  NO DICTATING TO ME IN 2002!\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ related, i would like people to start giving me job offers through networking and not me pounding away on craig\'s list, not that i am unsatisfied with my current employer, i usually like them allot.\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ ride my motorcycle on a trip through the north west with some friends.\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ what was i thinking of?\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ oh yea, continue with karate.  this is important to me.  i think it is kind of like my lifetime achievement effort that could work where college failed.  rewarded discipline.\r\n</p><p>\r\n_ pay off my credit cards and destroy them.  put $2002.00 in the stock market and see where it is two years from now in 2004.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere are many things that i make a point to do. they are not goals for the year, but goals for life.  those are not included here.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsomebody said this has been a checkered year for many people.  it sure has been for me.  i think i responded well. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni watched <a href=\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0190590\">o brother where art thou?</A> over new year\'s  break and let me tell you, my boat has washed up on more than one shore. i never felt like i didnt have paddles though.  even at the mercy of currents, i steered my own ship.\r\n</p>\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2202_1.jpg\">\r\n<pre>[14:03] <*egg*> oh thank the lord</pre> \r\n</CENTER>\r\n'),(2201,'<P>A young woman is yelling.  Shoes - lots of shoes around me.  Try to figure out where you are, Pauli.  The floor is cold, and that young woman is yelling something again.  You didn?t really see the blackness coming, did you?  Ok, you?re moving as the young woman and her friend pull you across the floor and away from whatever was causing all the pain.  Oh that feels good; I?m a little kid again.  Pull me; I like this.  Oh my fucking God my head is burning.  What happened to my head?  Now you?re thinking Pauli.  Hey, this is a nice floor: slate or something.  This young woman seems nice too; she really seems to care.  Maybe she likes you?  No, she?s saying something about you being killed.  Uh oh, I remember: just go with the program and get the fuck outside.  Yes: act hurt.  The door is near.\r\n\r\nCold night air at last, and no one is following - they?re afraid to take it too far.  They all have wonderful futures ahead and they know it.  Ungrateful bastards: you gave them a wall of free art.  Have they found it yet?  Well, that carpenter?s pencil was in your pocket and they had that plaster wall - white and waiting.  Maybe the stick-man making pee-pee wasn?t your best work, but you were under pressure.  Wait a minute, you did show that one guy, Pauli.  They asked you to go, but Mike wouldn?t leave.  He was on the staircase when the trouble started, and you tried to go to him.  Yeah, that was it.  You kicked that guy in front of you when he wouldn?t let you get to Mike.  You were Bruce Lee for at least 3 seconds.  Who would have guessed that you?d get knocked down so easily?  Blame it on the liquor and their numbers; that?s it, yeah.  Oh shit, then your head was a soccer ball.  Thank God those fags had boat shoes on.  If that were a hillbilly bar you?d be dead boyo.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhat now?  Go find Michael, and that worm Nicki too.  Damn Nicki is always talking like a tough guy and then vanishing when things happen.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOh my head.  I?m a freaking elephant man.  Look at yourself you idiot - look in that mirror.  The right side of your head is fucking huge.  And you know that your jaw pops-out so easily.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nEverything that happened before was silly - all that cartoon pee-pee man crap.  But this, this new plan for our revenge, this makes sense: we just run up to the windows on their building and break them.  That?s the plan.  We?ll never beat all thirty guys there in a straight fight.  It?s got to be this way.  Everybody?s got half a mop handle and we?re ready to go.  No talking.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOk, good.  Now do it - quick!  I?ll break my window first; we?ve got everything divided up.  Oh my fucking God!  My stick bounced off the glass!  I?m playing the xylophone here!  Why won?t you break you fucking glass?  Stop making those noises and break!  Break!  Break!  At last!  And another!  You rock Pauli!  But?Oh fuck!  A Crown Vic is rolling towards us!  Run Pauli run!  Hold that fucking stick and run!  Jesus no, please no - the sound of feet running behind you, Pauli.  Why me?  It?s got to be the cops; middle-aged white guys don?t run at night like that for fun.  Run Pauli run.  Keep running; don?t turn around.  Oh fuck: I?m tired.  I?m too tired to run any more.  I need to stop and fight.  Just knock him down Pauli; you?ll make it.  Oh Jesus I?m going to prison.  You stupid fucker - you and your fucking cartoon pee-pee man that you tried to call an artwork.  Those fucking rich kids like Monet and naked chicks wearing chocolate syrup and not cartoon pee-pee men.  You asshole - you knew this was coming.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOh my fucking God: Michael.  It?s Michael and not a cop.  Oh thank you God - I?ll never do anything like this again.         \r\n</P>\r\n     \r\n\r\n     \r\n'),(2203,'<P>something is broken in my stomach this morning, making me shift and squirm in my seat.  my gut is rumbling in protest, and it feels like its trying to climb up my throat.  i\'m going to try to contain it, whether my pancreas is trying to go on vacation, or my body has cooked up some funny-coloured fluid overnight.  at least now i have proof that i don\'t like to get up before 8:00am.  i\'m allergic to the early morning.\r\n</P><P>\r\ntoday i called in and played hooky from school.  i\'ve only been back one day since the holiday break, and already i can\'t face the fluorescent lights and dirty pea soup-coloured walls.  and the plastic plants around the room, who are they trying to fool?  if we\'re going to learn in a canned environment, at least go the whole distance.  i\'d rather have a funky lego sculpture sitting in the corner than see those limp plastic leaves wave hello to me every morning.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nyesterday, the woman beside me fell asleep about halfway through the lecture.  the lecturer was standing right in front of her, and she was oblivious, sprawled out in the chair with her feet pointing in funny directions.  i said, \"i\'m next,\" and nobody had the heart to wake her up.  i can only assume that the speaker saw her as well.  he stopped speaking at one point, looked down at his notes, and said, \"well, we\'ve gone off on a tangent here.  i\'m not sure what we\'ve been talking about.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy favourite part of the class is the walk there and back.  about 45 minutes each way, depending on how fast i churn my legs.  i pass through toronto\'s little italy neighbourhood, where everyone has a garden, everyone makes wine, and everyone has a sicilian grandmother screaming in the house.  even the most dilipadated shack has a fancy stone fountain on the front patio, and iron grille-work surrounding the yard.  one house is tilted at an insane angle, on the verge of falling over, and it\'s been for sale for years.  i\'ve seen people standing in front of that house, just laughing at it, which made me laugh too.\r\n</P><P>\r\nso today, no walk.  today i\'m left up to my own devices.  thus far, that consists of staring at the computer screen, hoping some australians might be awake somewhere to keep me company.  i\'ve poked around on swinney.org, getting to know names and recognizing a few of them as people i\'ve met, or even been friends with.  i\'m here under an assumed identity, though, so i\'m torn between the desire for anonymity, and the desire to get to know some of you.  i want to be able to write here without having to censor my thoughts, but hiding my name is probably just cowardly.  oh well, i can\'t be courageous when my stomach hurts.\r\n</P>'),(2204,'<p>Sometimes when you are the type of person that alway lies to yourself, you get lost in your own web. You learn to relish the emotional fallout from your various relations. The problem being that this fallout is all of your own making, all of the fallout others direct at you seems impotent in comparison with that of your own making. Every once in a while though when you live in this sort of web you catch a glimpse of (I\'m tempted to say truth but that doesn\'t fit) the over all pattern of lies and thus you suddenly understand their workings a little better. This is the only time when the action of others and all their fallout seems to impact you and suddenly your masochistic pleasures are no defence.</p><p>It is hard to make sense of anything when this is how you are cognizing your existence. You are constantly second guessing yourself wondering if it is just you lying to yourself again. It does make you a rather practiced critic though.</p><p>The problems this existence gives birth to  seems to come from humanities need for structure though. The problem being that no social structure is taken into the individual because they can not be truly accepted as they/it may get in the way of or contradict some future lie, thus they can only be given lip service. So the individual in this state is left to try and find a structure within themselves that they can take solace in as so many others do in societal structures. Thus they come up with some half cocked notion such as this very one I have been explaining to make due until the next lie makes it irrelevant. Their life is lived in fear of the truth and the pain that has been distilled of its pleasurable contents that seems to accompany the truth.</p><p>Instead of a New Years resolution I have a New years request - I\'d like all my past to be offered some forgiveness of its trespasses</p>    '),(2205,'<p>When I wrote the lyrics that said you couldn\'t hurt me, I lied.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"And I\'m so unsurprised, I remember - I remember why I dream in black and white.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I sang about walking away in straight lines, I lied then too. I never meant to tell untruths, but it\'s often hard to live up to your own ideals. And harder to live alone. But we\'re all alone. All of us. You get used to it. You learn to live without. And to live with yourself. But you never learn how to stop getting hurt. You can pretend you don\'t hurt. And sometimes that\'s enough to fool everyone but yourself. But you never stop feeling.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You\'d never think you did me any harm. You there safe in your tower. And here I\'m the girl; I\'m supposed to be the Rapunzel who hides away waiting for someone to prove to me that they\'re worthy. But it\'s you in your tower, and it\'s me out here wondering why I\'m standing alone in the cold wind. Standing here with the long hair and firm breast wondering what it is that\'s missing. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"I don\'t really need that now, I never really did anyhow, I only really needed alcohol something that\'ll treat me ok, and wouldn\'t say the things you say.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ve asked before, \"what is it that\'s missing?\" but there\'s never an answer. There is never anyone who has an answer. Nothing is missing. No, it\'s just timing or location or what? Nothing. It\'s not me it\'s you. It\'s not you its me. It\'s nothing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Just goes to show, my continual decline.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can sense there\'s more to it than this. What the \"more\" is I don\'t know. You can\'t show me. That\'s plain enough.</p>\r\n'),(2206,'<p>\"This train\'s final destination is Daly City. This is a Daly City train.\" And I guess it is, because that\'s what the driver voice blurts out of the intercom, but I\'ve never gone past the stop at 16th. Go up an escalator and go out the gate, and go up an escalator again.  Assaulted at the top by sunlight that slices in sheets past the top of Wells Fargo and I\'m blind. I don\'t mind being blind, but I can still smell the smells that the pit of hell has to offer me. I don\'t know if the smells or sights are worse.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I walk past the French public toilet and the congregation that stands there to worship it, and nobody offers to sell me drugs today. Pedestrians, like me, and not like me, weave in and out between each other and the man who wobbles on his legs like a chicken doing that dance chickens do after you cut off their heads.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I walk past Pancho Villa and I almost go in but there are no seats left, and it\'s too hot, and there are too many people in line. I see one of those old and tired Hispanic women selling roses out of an old bucket that probably used to hold something like 5 gallons of mayonnaise, but no one is buying single roses wrapped in cellophane tonight. They\'re only buying burritos.</p>\r\n'),(2207,'<br>~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nWhy did Dr. Brian get the (Promis) computer contract? Meese\'s wife owned substantial stock in Dr. Brian\'s company and it is also alleged that Dr. Brian was being paid off for setting up the original meetings between the Iranians and the Reagan-Bush campaign team which may have led to the delay in the release of 52 American hostages until after the November 1980 Presidential election. A British Air Force officer who had allegedly witnessed Dr. Brian\'s sale of PROMIS software to Iraqi military intelligence in Santiago, Chile, was found hanged. His death was ruled suicide. - 1\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDr. Brian\'s name has been linked to the October Surprise and is a central figure in the government\'s theft of PROMIS soft ware from NSLAW. Brian\'s role touches from the Cabazon Indian scandals to United Press International. He is one of those low-profile key figures. \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nAnd, alas, his name appears again in the nation\'s behavior modification and mind control experiments. Dr. Brian was Reagan\'s Secretary of Health when Reagan was Governor. Dr. Brian was an \r\nadvocate of state subsidies for a research center for the study of violent behavior. The center was to begin operations by mid-1975, and its research was intended to shed light on why people murder or rape, or hijack aircraft. - 2\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nDr. Earl Brian, Governor Ronald Reagan\'s Secretary of Health, was adamant about his support for mind control centers in California. - 2\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nCalspan was founded in 1946 as Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory, which included the \"Fund for the Study of Human Ecology,\" a CIA conduit for mind-control experiments by Ã©migrÃ© Nazi scientists [and others under the direction of CIA Doctors Sidney Gottlieb, Ewen Cameron, and Louis Jolyn West]. - 3\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nAccording to mind-control researcher Alex Constantine, \"Calspan places much research emphasis on bioengineering and artificial intelligence (Calspan pioneered in the field in the 1950s).\" In his article, \"The Good Soldier,\" Constantine states:\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nas did riconoscuito...a guy named kumnick made an AI prog named Brainstorm that was a sort of supplement to promis...it was supposed to be able to PREDICT when someone (or an army) was going to go off by reducing personality traits to mathematical vectors...in the wake of the promis scandal, kumnick died...suicided?\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nHuman tracking and monitoring technology are well within Calspan\'s sphere of pursuits. The company is instrumental in REDCAP, an Air Force electronic warfare system that winds through every Department of Defense facility in the country. A Pentagon release explains that REDCAP \"is used to evaluate the effectiveness of electronic-combat hardware, techniques, tactics and concepts.\" The system \"includes closed-loop radar and data links at RF manned data fusion and weapons control posts.\" One Patriot computer news board reported that a disembodied, rumbling, low-frequency hum had been heard across the country the week of the bombing. Past hums in Taos, NM, Eugene and Medford, OR, Timmons, Ontario and Bristol, UK were most definitely (despite specious official denials) attuned to the brain\'s auditory pathways?.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nThe Air Force is among Calspan\'s leading clients, and Eglin AFB has farmed key personnel to the company. The grating irony - recalling McVeigh\'s contention he\'d been implanted with a telemetry chip - is that the Instrumentation Technology Branch of Eglin Air Force Base is currently engaged in the tracking of mammals with subminiature telemetry devices. According to an Air Force press release, the biotelemetry chip transmits on the upper S-band (2318 to 2398 MHz), with up to 120 digital channels. - 3\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nisn\'t Eglin very NEAR the cabazon res? \r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nFormer Army CID investigator Gene Wheaton also described a covert unit created by the highly secretive NRO (National Reconnaissance Office), which used assassination and torture to eliminate so-called enemies of the state. In 1985, Wheaton was approached by \"security consultants\" to Vice President Bush\'s \"Task Force on Combating Terrorism\" who were working for USMC Lt. Colonel Oliver North (who served under Shackley in Vietnam) and Associate Deputy FBI Director Oliver \"Buck\" Revell. \"They wanted me to help create a \'death squad\' that would have White House deniability to assassinate people they would identify as \'terrorists,\'\" said Wheaton. - 3\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nan individual that will remain unnamed did once attempt to convince me that everything i had been through had more to do with the NRO than any other agency...\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\"They used the assassination of President Kennedy as a prime example of how to develop the strategy for the assassination of a world leader as a conspiracy, while making it look like some \'lone nut\' did it.?\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\"The stronger a patriot you are, the more important it is to you that you do whatever is necessary for your flag, for your country,\" he adds. \"It makes you the most susceptible type of person for this kind of training. You are the ultimate warrior. You\'re out there to do for your country what nobody else is willing to do. I had no qualms about it at all.\"\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nlike gritz?\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nIn another version of the story reported by Ted Gunderson, an intelligence informant indicated that McVeigh was \"trained to work for the CIA in their illegal drug operations,\" then \"became disenchanted with the government, and voiced his displeasure.\" At that point he was sent to Fort Riley for discharge, at which point John Doe 2 \"was planted on him\" and \"orchestrated the bombing.\"\r\n<br>\r\nAccording to Gundersen\'s informant, McVeigh was a victim of the CIA\'s mind-control project, Project MONARCH.\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n~snip~\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nof all the supposed MK methods spoken of online, monarch most closely resembles my experiences\r\n<br>\r\nthe shoe-bomber had visited israel and had a british passport and mcveigh is said to have been in touch with british intelligence not much prior to the murrah incident...the israeli WTC connection - koresh spent a good deal of time in israel...hassidic jews kept me into MDMA for a time at a \'new age\' resort...before i knew it was the fuck-and-tell drug...\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\none more thing:\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nremember terry nichols?\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nhe was described at the scene and he was hauled in and he got off...seemingly the gubment has a vested interest in keeping the public from even thinking \'conspiracy\' - here is a name you will find with a moderate amount of research into the octopus: robert booth NICHOLS...and one more: John Phillip NICHOLS! - BOTH CLOSELY ASSOCIATED WITH RICONOSCUITO!\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nnotes:\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n1 - inslaw\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.sonic.net/sentinel/gvcon7.html\">http://www.sonic.net/sentinel/gvcon7.html</a>\r\n<br>\r\n2 - mind control\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.sonic.net/sentinel/gvcon8.html\">http://www.sonic.net/sentinel/gvcon8.html</a>\r\n<br>\r\n3 - the politics of terror\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://mindprism.com/__ocbpt/chpt2a.html\">http://mindprism.com/__ocbpt/chpt2a.html</a>\r\n<br>\r\n'),(2208,'<p>When I told you that you had introduced me to a seven year old Hispanic boy, and you said, \"This is my son.\" And that he had no name, you stood up, made as if to take a step, and sat down in the same place. I didn\'t see the look on your face. I didn\'t need to.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The dream started like any dream, strange and without any reasonable meaning. I was driving a large clunky motor home down an eight lane expressway. I knew it was an expressway in New York because it was my dream. It was an older motor home, late 70s, square edges, carpeted, needlepoint samplers swaying with the traffic. I drove up to a shopping mall and stopped outside the entrance. There was plenty of parking at the curb, in fact no one else was parked for the length of the block.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I stepped out onto a large concrete plaza and the manager of the large department store at one end of the mall walked out to meet me. She was wearing a Christmas red knee length skirt and a matching blazer over an ugly cream-colored polyester blouse. The suit fabric was dusty, nubby, clerical. She had on flesh toned panty hose and shoes that weren\'t necessarily leather. Her hair was done up in a flouncing series of loops that gave her a tight halo. She offered my father, who I hadn\'t known was present until this point, and I a tour of the store. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the store we followed the manager\'s hairdo through the departments. My father was with me, wearing a bunny suit made out of a synthetic sheepskin, just like the bunny suit I wore in <I>Winnie the Pooh</I> when I was in Junior Theater. The ears were tall and firm, and I know that because I was riding on his shoulders like I might have when I was very small. We followed her down the escalator and across the cheap floor tiles. It was not a very interesting tour, and she was not a very interesting tour guide. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>After the tour I walked out to the motor home to meet you. You were waiting in the back in the tiny wood grained RV bedroom. The nameless seven year old boy was sitting next to you. You introduced him to me as your son.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After I told you this dream, and after you stood up and sat down again, you were very quiet. I had to prod you. I wanted to know why you\'d be so weird about a dream that wasn\'t even yours. And so you told me. You said seven years ago, your girlfriend, who was Hispanic, had had an abortion. You said you still thought it was the wrong decision. You said you had only told two people in the entire world about it, and I was not one of them. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was not the last time I knew things you\'d done without being told, but it was the last time I was able to pay attention. The next time I knew things, they had much more to do with me. It was much easier to pretend I didn\'t know them at all.</p>\r\n'),(2209,''),(2210,'<p>Miss USA\'s PR lady called me \"da bomb\" today.  It was in a really sweet matronly sort of a way....\"Mickey, you are dabomb.\" (No exclamation marks or anything).  Since this was the first time I had been referred to in such explosive terms, I wasn\'t quite sure how to take it.  I think I\'m flattered, except that we were only working out the details of an interview I\'d be conducting with Miss USA sometime next week for my sports talk show to discuss her new gig as a bull riding commentator on TNN.  I gave her my fax number and she said, \"Mickey, you are da bomb.\"</p>\r\n<p> On second thought, I don\'t like being \"da bomb.\"</p>'),(2211,'<p>strange how you can feel two ways at once, or three or a thousand.  strange to feel so large inside, capable of holding miles and miles of pain, and the sky above it, the not-pain that always mirrors down.  everytime my heart breaks the space inside gets more vast.  someday maybe i won\'t be able to see the horizons anymore.  maybe that\'s what dying feels like.</p>\r\n<p>let me tell you how it happened last night:<p>\r\n<p>i walked home most of the way, appreciating the evening light and the clear air.  the rusty prints of ancient leaves on the sidewalk, layers of these bloody ghosts, a draining pattern underfoot.  back in the spring [i thought as i walked] the sun infused the tree with golden green sugarlight, the leaves uncurled and wavered in the fog, waved all the cold summer mostly unseen.  darker and darker with the shorter days, gold to brick to brown, and fallen and rained on.  and swept away by wind and broom, but. they left this riot of shades and shapes, sudden texture on the concrete.  swan song of the naked tree.  </p>\r\n<p>i walked home happy, noticing things around me, leg muscles stretching out nice long strides, breath. noticing when walking by windows that i looked peculiar.  peaked.  i never thought of that word in relation to myself until last night.  pale and peaked, dark dark circles under my eyes and hollow cheeks.  ghostly.  i stopped looking in the windows.</p>\r\n<p>hopped on the 22 at guerrero and began the long pulling up over the hill.  knew i was looking sickly, sat in the hard seat and fantasized about a hot shower.  a man got on at market street, a man with a big blue tarp bundle of a backpack, stringy blond hair, insistent voice.  he immediately began talking to everyone around him.  <i>man you know what time it is.</i> people ignored him, shaking their heads, as bus ettiquette seems to dictate.  ignore the wacko and eventually he will disembark.  <i>hey, how far is haight street?</i> this time his desperation directed at me.  i tell him, as gently as possible, that we passed haight about three stops ago.  he is hurt, shouts at the driver to let him off right now.  jostles to the front and demands that the bus stop RIGHT NOW.  next stop, we both get off, and i am left walking toward home, my heartstrings pulling back with this man who is so lonely that he asks strangers for the time repeatedly.  just some human contact.  and young people in this city have such a cool aloofness, no, no contact for me please.  i\'m in my own world.  i think i am now included in this category, although for at least a year when i first moved to the city i had no defenses whatsoever and was thus constantly being hassled by all energy-hungry people.  now it seems i have learned the trick of being invisible, that is, to keep all available energy inside, to be selfcontained and pure and unassailable.  still sometimes i am vunerable, though, like to this man.  i have a special sort of compassion for bums d\'un certain age, these so-called able bodied men who haunt the streets.  they remind me of my father.  my poor dad.  not a bum but close.  shaggy that way, addicted to cigarettes, sad.  so i see this man headed off to the haight for who knows what reason, and i wonder where he will sleep and if he\'s had any food recently.  still i didn\'t offer to help him.  well, my own heart was heavy.  so, there i was, walking toward home, feeling two ways at once: immensely priveleged and pristine and cold, and also feeling small and dirty and scared of the empty house. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i knew the boy would not be home.  i felt it.  i ascended the stairs, and when i saw the window dark, i started to cry.  i had not heard from him in two days.  i psyched myself up for the empty house and still i couldn\'t help crying when i walked through the door into the darkness.</p>\r\n<p>i cried steadily for the better part of an hour, during my shower (best place for crying, really), getting dressed, curling up underneath a comforter on the couch, little low sobs.  there was pain oddly high in my chest, not the normal heartache place but above it, on the breastbone.  i beat my chest experimentally, brain thoughtfully touching on that tribal custom women of many cultures have of beating the breast and tearing the hair in grief.  hm.  isn\'t that interesting. it actually felt good.  i\'m real.  <i>thud.</i>  i\'m here. <i>thud.  thud.</i>  i have a body and i am alive, even though my insides feel like they\'ve been through a meat tenderizing process.  whip chop beat blend puree.  i got up, paced, tried to read (anne carson\'s the beauty of the husband, an apt if painful choice), tried to watch tv.  off and on would feel a sudden welling of self-pity, would allow it to surface, would cry, would pet the cats again.  little guardians of sanity. </p>\r\n<p>hours passed this way, and my anger simmered over flames of pain.  i tried to think of strong acts, of destruction, could i break some dishes?  could i stab his computer? could i...but none of it seemed satisfying, i didn\'t really want to wreck my own house.  so i fantasized about cutting myself.  how it would feel, how i would bleed.  i could bleed all over the kitchen floor, that would show him, right?  i pictured the cats stepping in the sticky puddle and tracking it all over the house.  no good.  these ridiculous fantasies continued until i felt it was really time to go to bed. here i go, crying myself to sleep like in the country songs, i thought as i brushed my teeth.  jesus.</p>\r\n<p>laid awake for a long time, this the most torturous time, thinking i heard keys in the lock, feet on the stairs, creaking floorboards. drifting in and out of strange visions of needles being inserted into the center of an enlarged red whorl of thumbprint.</p>\r\n<p>he is home.  i smell him before any other sense is engaged.  i throw off his hands. what do you want, i snarl, not fully awake.  the clock says 1:49.  he speaks softly to me. i can feel his shape in the darkness, how graceful he is, how beautifully his soft shirt sits on his frame.  i feel raw and chopped-up inside.  but glad he\'s here. so here i am, unsuccessfully choking back more tears, feeling two ways at once: vicious wounded cornered-animal self, and relieved forgiving warming woman. </p>\r\n<p>he comes to bed and we wrap our arms around each other and i cry some more.  not sobs, just soft tears flowing down my face onto his chest.  amazing that i have any tears left.  drifting off to sleep i am completely wrung out, completely dry. i whisper into his velvet skin.  </p>\r\n<p><i> you\'re so beautiful.  you inspire me to forgive you.</i> '),(2212,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2212_1.pjpeg\"><br>\r\n<b>MTA Subway:</b><br>\r\nThis is the Bronx-bound 1 train, making all uptown local stops. The next stop is 110th Street, Cathedral Parkway. Stand clear of the closing doors, please.<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2212_2.pjpeg\"><br>\r\n<b>MTA Bus:</b><br>\r\nNext stop, 110th Street. Transfer available to the 1 train, the m104 and the m60 to LaGuardia Airport. Please step behind the white line.'),(2213,'<p>I rode up the freight elevator with Johnny.  Before we got to our floor we had to stop so that this woman and her monkey could get on the elevator with us.  She was cute.  The monkey was in this cage on a rolling metal cart and the whole cage/cart apparatus was half hidden underneath a black tarp.  Johnny wanted to see the monkey; he liked animals.  I knew enough not to ask to see the monkey - because I liked animals.  The cute woman pulled back the cage\'s covering and Johnny and I both got an eyeful.  The monkey had some sort of electrical socket grafted into its skull and there were a number of wires running out from it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"Cover that thing back up!\" I yelled.  The monkey shuffled to the corner of his cage; Johnny moved to the corner of the elevator; the woman scowled at me.  \"Don\'t raise your voice,\" she said, \"They are very sensitive!\"  Oh, that\'s right, I thought to myself, the monkey\'s biggest problem right now is my voice; thank goodness he has a friend like you - someone willing to wire him up properly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was never supposed to have any contact with the animals - Nicki assured me that I wouldn\'t.  \"Just move the material up there; make your cuts in the wall; shovel out the rubble,\" that\'s what he told me.  I knew it was wrong, but I needed the money.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Later in the day I ran the saw with the diamond-blade, watching the white plume of dust billow out from the cut and fill the room with silica.  All the other contractors complained.  I wished that I had a mask.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>But, the noise of the dogs bothered me the most.  Even with my saw running I heard the dogs screaming.  I don\'t know what the cute woman and her colleagues were doing to the dogs, but the dogs were in pain.  A group of dogs, somewhere on the floor below us, would bark.  Then one dog would scream.  It would scream a long time.  I wanted to go and kill the dog, and kill the people who were making it scream, and kill myself too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I tried to repeat to myself: \"I am not, I am not.\"  I tried not to exist.  I tried not to be me.  I loved the sky.  I loved the sun.  I loved the feeling of a clean wind blowing over me.  I loved the smell of the pine trees that grew around my Uncle\'s farm.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The whole place reeked of urine and fear.  I had sold my soul and body for a few dollars.  I didn\'t really think that I had a choice.  I had big hands and a strong back and not much else to pimp.  Johnny didn\'t know better; he never saw the sixth grade.  The people who were running the show didn\'t give a fuck about me or Johnny or any of the other animals.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I still cough a little more than I should.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s four in the morning now.  High above the city the animals in the lab are sleeping - sleeping in a monument to our great potential.</p>  \r\n'),(2214,'<p>\r\npestered for money for coke. saying no so many times in so many ways.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na fight insued and i found myself filled with rage, but still i cowered off down the busy side of the street. it felt good to get away from the voices and demands.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni sat on a bench and wrote some things down. a short list of my own demands. they are as follows. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>crazy glue. i fucking need crazy glue.<br>\r\nwho the fuck uses hotmail?<br>\r\nstudded punk bracelets are cool.<br>\r\nthere was vomit in the phone booth.<br>\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na pigeon sat down on the bench beside me. i found some crumbs in the bottom of my book bag and feeded the poor bird. pecking at the crumbs and bits of pencil lead the pigeon let a nice load out in the middle of the bench. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe stool had formed an almost perfect circle. what struck my eye wasnt bits of pigeon crap, but was a glint of something shiny in the middle of the dung. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbending down i found a stick, and with it i prodded the shiny object trying to determine what it was. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngold. it was gold. gold from a bird. how fairy tailish. i scooped the nugget out from the crap and put it on a leave. wrapping it up i headed on down to the eaton centre and washed it up in their very clean and nice smelling public bathrooms. what luck!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthats when i got hit over the head with a toilet seat. the bastards had seen me cleaning it. the nugget. the bad karma nugget. may the bastard that put it in his teeth choke on his teeth. at any rate. if it does end up in someones mouth, they\'ll never know they have pigeon shit in their mouth. \r\n</p>'),(2215,'There is not much in this place that I can understand. The first thing I see this morning is a strange place and strange people, people I feel I should know, but know nothing about. And now I\'m stuck on a Saturday morning watching a ridiculous Alec Baldwin/Whoopie Goldberg movie.\r\n\r\nHave you every felt no matter which way you turned, all you found was more confusion? How can I begin to understand people if I do not understand myself.'),(2216,'<p> There was a time that I felt there would be no one I could love. I could be fooled for awhile into thinking that the one I was with could be right \"the right one\". I was always looking for \"the right one\". I am not one for quick fixes or wasting time. I never wanted to partake in random dating or sleeping around. Somehow I fell into in anyway. Always believing that if I could just make him happy, he would love me, and he could be \"the right one\".</p>\r\n<p>I was a silly girl. What the hell was I thinking?</p>\r\n<p>It was time to move on. And that begun with finding somebody I could love. Not somebody who simply wanted me.</p>\r\n<p>Well I found that person. And quite on accident! I guess such a thing finds you in strange places, at strange times. At a rather young age, I suppose, I have found somebody who makes me feel happy when he\'s around. I\'m considering myself lucky.</p>\r\n<p>And this morning when I woke up and he was not there, again, I felt the pit of my stomach fall out. I know that I should move on, that it will only be a short time until his return, but I find myself wishing the weeks fly by, wishing I could be put in stasis until the time is over. Through this time, he encourages me to grow, and I feel an obligation to that. But it\'s been a long time since I have seen him last, and his vacation still has some time to go.</p>\r\n<p><i> the abyss resides in my home now, surrounding me with fog, where I wander without my heart, and I feel the glow faintly now, my love, keep safe...</i></p>'),(2217,'<p>you know how you always secretly suspect that there is someone in your town who is going to all the same rock shows as you, reading the same books, renting movies from the very same snarly punk kid at the video store only hours before you do, sitting at the same cafe but different tables, and it\'s just a matter of meeting this person?</p>\r\n<p>it\'s just a matter of having their face slip into your mental database enough times that, with each subsequent event, that person will appear more familiar to you until you finally say to yourself, \"how do i <b>know</b> him?\"</p>\r\n<p>i haven\'t met mine yet.<BR>\r\nbut i know his name.</p>\r\n<p>he responded to a salon personal ad i had, a personal ad about which i\'d entirely forgotten until 2 weeks ago when someone replied to my year-old ad and i was reminded of it and faced with the choice to either delete it or update it.<BR>\r\nit was the week between christmas and new year\'s. i was at my brother\'s house in north texas where i\'d just spent the holidays with my brother and his wife, my parents, and my 20 month old niece. conversation topics this christmas centered mainly on: cute outfits people had bought for my niece, toys people had bought for my niece, how great my brother\'s entertainment system was, my brother\'s motorcycle, my sister-in-law\'s stepfamily and how much they suck.</p>\r\n<p>i updated the ad, and got a slew of responses almost immmediately, most of which just weren\'t interesting at all.</p>\r\n<p>then:</p>\r\n<pre>\r\nHey there,\r\n\r\nI was glad to read your profile because I thought I was\r\n the only one who got slammed by the literature GRE. I \r\nstill don\'t fully understand what a synecdoche is. I \r\nmean, I thought I learned stuff in college but evidently \r\nnot...oh well.\r\n\r\n\r\nSo anyway, I just moved to Austin not long ago. I\'m \r\nactually glad to be back after spending the holidays with \r\nmy family (and turning 24. ugh.) I\'m sort of your typical \r\nEnglish-major-dork kinda guy...I love going to see live \r\nmusic, mostly indie rock, so I end up at Emo\'s and the \r\nMercury a lot. Otherwise, I read, write, and smoke too \r\nmany cigarettes. I think you can look at my profile if you \r\nwant, though as I remember my answers were a little \r\non the lame side. But if any of this sounds remotely \r\ninteresting to you, feel free to respond...or write me at \r\nxxx@yahoo.com.\r\n\r\n\r\nIn the meantime, take care.\r\n\r\nYrs\r\n\r\nMike\r\n</pre>\r\n<p>i stared at the response skeptically, as if confused as to what just happened, and wrote him back, and we have spent the last week in a thrall of emails and a state of disbelief.</p>\r\n<p>we have decided to meet, tonight, at a shearwater show, because they\'re a local band that we both like a lot, and were both at their show with silver scooter.</p>\r\n<p>i\'m trying not to have high expectations. if he ends up being a boy to go to shows with, that\'s just fine.</p>\r\n<p>i just wanted to share with you my afternoon of anticipation, questions, worry, elation. i wanted to extend that you.</p>\r\n'),(2218,'<P>last night, i was laying on my bed, the wrong way around.  my head was hanging off the end of the mattress, and my feet were warm, wedged between two pillows.  that\'s the way i sleep lately, with blood rushing to my head and a fuzzy feeling in my lips and eyes.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nnot so long ago, i didn\'t have room to sprawl out in bed like this.  i would have had an elbow in the ribs, and an admonishing grunt.  i would have fallen asleep without twisting like a contortionist, because my mind was at rest.  these days, my mind is awake, and my body usually follows suit.\r\n</P><P>\r\nlast night, i went wandering in the neighbourhood at 4:30am, enjoying the chill on my skin.  i sauntered along the side streets, going nowhere in particular, searching for the most circuitous route.  i could feel that everything around me was asleep, and being awake didn\'t feel so bad.  i passed a giant oak tree in front of a hundred-year-old house.  there was a squirrel up there, somewhere, snoring. \r\n</P><P>\r\nfurther down the street the sidewalk stopped, and i walked along an unpaved curb.  there was a lip of dirty grass, ending where a tilted fence blocked my view.  in the dim light, i could see rust-coloured paint weathering on the boards.  the fence led to a gate, also wooden, with an indication of a stone-strewn driveway in front.  someone had abandoned an austin mini there, resting on four flat tires.\r\n</P><P>\r\naway down the road, there were two rows of cramped garages, split by an alley.  each one was different, leaning on the others like a row of drunks.  i could imagine that they\'d been built with hands like mine, by people with no architectural saavy.  the pavement was rough, ungraded, with dirt patches everywhere.  garbage was piled in corners, and crispy leaves blew along the ground, following me.  \r\n</P><P>\r\neven though i\'ve never seen one, this place reminds me of the mexican barrios.  except our garages are their homes.  there\'s such a sweet odour of urban decay here.  things are slowly falling apart, not because of neglect, but because they\'ve seen so much life, so much use.  thinking of my bed, assembled from the box just a few months ago, it\'s no wonder i can\'t sleep there.  i\'d rather curl up on the ground, where so many footsteps have passed.  \r\n</P>'),(2219,'<center><p><img SRC=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2219_1.pjpeg\" height=289 width=548></center>\r\n\r\n<p>I was about to comment on joâ€™s article â€œ<a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2217.txt\">anticipation</a>â€ when my comment became entirely too long and I decided to post my own. I actually met two girlfriends online. The first was purely accidental, and the second was sought after.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Purely Accidental:</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Back in <a href=\"http://www.una.edu\">college</a>, one afternoon after reviewing my lecture notes and taking a few bong rips, I was casually surfing\r\naround on a site called <a href=\"http://www.sugarmegs.org\">Sugarmegs.org</a>\r\nlooking for <a href=\"http://www.phish.net\">Phish</a> bootlegs and this girl pops up on AIM. Her screen name was Deadhd1684 and I later came to know her as Jamie.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jamie was a couple of years younger than I was. We immediately hit it off with our mutual interest of <a href=\"http://www.jambase.com\">music</a>,\r\nrecreational drugs, and common location. We talked occasionally for a few weeks and then decided to meet. She came over to my house. I made her some herb tea and we talked for a few hours.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Over the next few days we continued to talk. We were attracted to each other and decided to give it a <a href=\"http://web.hulteen.com/hilary/\">shot</a>. We began to fuck. We dated for about 4 months. We fucked a lot. Actually now that I think back on the relationship, all we did was fuck. Well, we did fight and then fuck, eat and fuck, fuck all\r\nnight, fuck in the morning, take bong rips and fuck, eat <a href=\"http://www.lycaeum.org/graphics/art/blotter/pix.cgi?alice_front.jpg\">acid</a>\r\nand fuck, smoke cigarettes and fuck, shower and fuck, wash dishes and fuck, go to class and fuck in the janitor closet, have tickle fights and fuck, and fuck and fuck some more. You get the idea. For four months we were little sex machines.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And then, I started noticing that my friends were not around anymore. I started to feel isolated. I confronted one of my friends and asked\r\nwhere he had been. The answer was obvious, â€œWhere have YOU been!?â€ So then we started going out to friends parties. My friends quickly decided that she was not worth hanging out in our group. I could kind of see why. She was not all that bright. She would blurt out the wrong things at the wrong times and in the wrong <a href=\"http://politicalhumor.about.com/library/graphics/bush_bong.jpg\">places</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My friends started to avoid me because they knew she was always around. I was stuck in a dilemma. I didnâ€™t know what to do. I was not\r\nuse to this rejection by my peers so I broke it off. She went hysterical when I told her why. So much for the <a href=\"http://www.davidrachel.com/Script.htm\">honest</a> approach.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At first, the phone would ring and she would hang up when I answered. This happened about 50 times a day so I stopped answering my phone. My answering machine started recording these long messages of strange noises. Then I found some burnt things in my yard and it was over. Isnâ€™t\r\nit strange how fire stereotypically signifies the end of a relationship?  Its kind of like saying, â€œOK, thatâ€™s it buddy, your <a href=\"http://real21mt.audiovideoweb.com/ramgen/avwebbb1005/CJ_LE_56K.rm\">history</a>!\r\nSizzle Sizzle!â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I talked to Jamie once since then. I apologized to her for the way I had handled things and told her I was wrong for letting my friends dictate to me who was ok for me to go out with. She seemed very <a href=\"http://www.icehotel.com/english/Icehotel/galleri.htm\">cold</a>\r\nto my apology and did not offer me any peace of mind. I said goodbye to her and wished her happiness. She had five piercings.</p>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b>Sought After:</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was new to San Francisco in August of 2000.  I moved here not knowing a soul, determined to build an empire. Work at that time was easy to find and I found myself in a 9 to 6 job within\r\na week. Finding a place to live on the other hand was much more challenging. In due time I found myself <a href=\"http://www.cooley.com\">employed</a>\r\nand housed. So I thought, why not post an add on <a href=\"http://www.craigslist.org/best/2463364.html\">Craigâ€™s list</a> and see what happens.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>â€œMen Seeking Women: ISO <a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/thetropics/resort/5770/\">Hippie Chick</a>. Any out there?â€</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I actually got <a href=\"http://www.five.com\">five</a> replies. I exchanged emails with them a few times and eventually only one kept responding. She was in the same boat as me. New to the city and looking to make friends. Eventually we ended up meeting at Bitterroot for brunch and had a really <a href=\"http://www.tauzero.com/Rob_Tow/audio_recording.html\">nice</a> day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jennifer was from Oregon and had studied art and graduated from the University of Arizona. She didnâ€™t exactly meet the curriculum of my post for a <a href=\"http://www.hippy.com/hippyway.htm\">hippie</a>. Not that I am a <a href=\"http://www.hippynet.com/\">hippie</a>, but she was more generated toward pop culture instead of counter culture. Regardless, she was inclined to learn more about the counter culture. We started to hang out frequently going to concerts, movies, hikes, and the given <a href=\"http://www.hightimes.com/welcome.html\">recreational drug use</a>. I was not chemically attracted to her even though she had a cute face and figure. She was a very good painter and was very social. One night as I hugged her goodbye she kissed my neck. She crossed the bridge and being stuck in the moment, I kissed her. Right away I knew our friendship could be in jeopardy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We wound up dating for about two months. We mixed, mingled, and made new friends. Over the course of time I became <a href=\"http://www.hack.net/\">distant</a>,\r\nand she noticed this. At one point she wanted to know where our relationship was going. I <a href=\"http://www.utne.com/bNewPlanet.tmpl?command=search&db=dArticle.db&eqheadlinedata=Jose%20Bove%20108\">diverted</a> the question and changed the subject. She didnâ€™t talk to me for a\r\ncouple of days after that. We drifted apart.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The next few months I became engrossed in my job trying to make it a better <a href=\"http://www.worksucks.com\">place</a> to be. Jenn and met a couple of times for lunch. We got together at a <a href=\"http://www.phillesh.net/\">Phil Lesh</a> show in Berkeley and I met a couple of her friends, Deanna and Bridgett. Jenn was getting\r\nready to go to Europe to spend a couple of months backpacking. She had put in notice at her job and found someone to sublet her place. I loaned her my <a href=\"http://www2.webpark.cz/comedy/\">Letâ€™s go Europe</a> book.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A couple of months later I was getting ready to fly to Alabama to visit with my family and decided to go to <a href=\"http://www.sts9.com/\">Sound Tribe Sector 9</a> at the Fillmore before leaving for the airport at 3am. At the show I took some\r\n<a href=\"http://leda.lycaeum.org/search.pl?Terms=MDMA\">e</a> and began bouncing around the <a href=\"http://deoxy.org/def/ecstasy.htm\">vortex\r\nof peaceful vibrations</a>. During set break I spot Jenn, Deanna, and Bridgett coming down the stair. They see me at the same time. We hug and exchange stories. She explains that she is moving back to Arizona to work on her Masters since she <a href=\"http://deoxy.lycaeum.org/chimp/\">canâ€™t\r\nfind a job</a> here. I tell her that I am still searching. The show was great and we all had a great time. As I am leaving for the Airport Deanna winks at me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I get back to <a href=\"http://www.rockument.com/links.html\">San\r\nFrancisco</a> a week later, I have about 30 emails from Deanna. She wants to get together, so we start hanging out. Now I am in the same\r\nsituation that I was in before with Jenn. This time I made my intentions very clear from the start that I am not looking for a serious relationship. It didnâ€™t matter. I still got that <a href=\"http://www.oliveplace.com/beengooed/goomain.html\">awkward</a> email a couple of days ago followed by a nervous phone call <a href=\"http://be-in.com/12/highlights.html\">elaborating</a>\r\nher feelings. I donâ€™t want our friendship to end. She is a fun person to be around.  Unfortunately I am not attracted to her. The other night we watched â€œ<a href=\"http://www.geocities.com/Area51/Shuttle/4600/Alienpic/drewalien.gif\">Oh Brother Where Art Thou</a>â€ and laughed our asses off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This reminds me of gus\'s article <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2205.txt\">Plainclothes</a>, except I am the one in the tower waiting...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So this brings us to <a href=\"http://www.ltn.lv/~kat/vision/index.php\">today</a>. I am getting the silent treatment now as she steps back and looks at the situation. I hope that my <a href=\"http://www.mellowfx.com/video/guest/rave(cut).wmv\">friendship</a> is good enough for her.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2220,'<pre>\r\nOutside my window tonight it is snowing--\r\nand thatâ€™s exciting.\r\nFrom the inside it looks inviting.\r\n\r\nA few hours later it looks like itâ€™s melting,\r\nand thatâ€™s disappointing.\r\nIt\'s barely even morning.\r\n\r\nIt doesnâ€™t snow here much.\r\n\r\nDoes it snow where you live?\r\nDo you like it?\r\n\r\nSometimes. \r\n\r\nI always run out of breath faster when there is snow on the ground.\r\nWhich, like I said,\r\n\r\nisnâ€™t often.\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2221,'<P>I\'ve been contemplating lately. Contemplating making massive decisive changes in my life. I want to shake off this cloak of contemporary society and return to a more simple existance. One of hard work, sweat and deep sleep. </P><P>We\'ve had conversations; how to start making changes . What to do first. Listmaking. Early morning conversations about hydroelectic power harnessed from a river, or the possibility of purely solar. Conversations about how to live a live as removed from capitalism as possible. These conversations are frustrating to me. How badly do I want to create a life where I create everything that I need, from food to clothes to electricity. How difficult this life would be. It\'s funny, to be able to make a life that would be free from money, money is needed to make it happen. The land has to be bought. The seeds for the garden. The sheep or cotton for cloth. Bartering is possible, but again, a product is needed. </P><P>I find this frustrating, again, yet interesting. I\'ve read about people who have made it happen and I wonder at their inginuity and determination. And I wonder how they started. That seems to be the hardest part. </P>'),(2222,'<p>Recording the third part of the Richard Dorfmeister & Jazzanova set from WMF 2001 to my new MZ-R900DPC and relaxing with a couple Clementines and a large cup of coffee.  There is snow on the ground, for now, and today was the first day back to school after the Winter Break.  I\'m on academic probation (again) and I only had one class today: The Art of Diary Writing.</p>\r\n<p>Now, my school has this thing about liberal studies where in your first and third year, each semester you must take a liberal studies elective and the course must be un-related to your field of study.  Being a computer science major immediately removes any fun computer/technology classes from being picked.  For this semester my first two choices were The Art of Diary Writing and The Philosophy of Love and Sex.  I received my first choice and am one of five males in the class (two of them being friends of mine who have their own personal/journal sites).</p>\r\n<p>Apparently that for our final essay (35% of the final mark) we can do something which our prof, Liz, tried out last year.  She told us that we can write a diary online for two weeks and then write about the experience in an essay.  This made me laugh and Matt said to me, \"Fuck, two weeks?  How about eight months?\"  I replied with \"Eight months?  How about three years?\"  So, we laughed and all silently decided to take that essay option but then I got to thinking about what kind of fun I could have with something like that.  I thought that it would be interesting to go on a two week bender and chronicle the events of this experiment in a diary.  Sure, I might come out a broke alcoholic on the other end but it would be worth it.  I don\'t really think I can do this, though, because I do have four other courses (all necessary for attaining my degree) to worry about as well and a job.</p>\r\n<p>Responsibility is always ruining my fun, it seems.</p>\r\n<p>This semester looks good.  I\'m happy to be back into the swing of things, even though the break was welcomed, it\'s time to get back to business.</p>\r\n<p>I love my MiniDisc unit more than anything in the world right now.  I can\'t express with words how much I feel for this little unit beside me.  I\'ve been without portable music for so long since my headphones died and my walkman has been on the \"crapping out\" end of things for so long that it\'s like I\'ve had the shackles thrown off me and can now run free.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m in the future of music (piracy).</p>'),(2223,'<p>She handed me a warm bottle of Corona.  The top had been taken off the bottle at some previous date and 1/3 of the beer was missing.  I thought twice about drinking it.  She said: \"What\'s a matter?  Don\'t you trust me?\"  Nope.  But what the fuck, I thought to myself.  And I drank it anyway.  She asked me how I felt.  I said that my stomach burned a little, but otherwise I didn\'t feel too bad.  \"That\'s odd,\" she said, \"I put enough in there to knock-out a horse.\"  I looked at her and asked: \"Enough of what?\"  In reply she said: \"Let\'s go get a pizza.\"  So, we did.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She waited until I had the first bite of food in my mouth to tell me about her abusive boyfriend.  She warned me that he might come back to her place that evening.  \"Last time,\" she said, \"he kicked the door in.\"  The pizza started to upset my stomach in a manner not unlike the mysterious \"Corona\" had done.  But, I understood that she had been through some rough stuff and I worked hard to be nice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was feeling really tired.  So, we had some more to drink at a tavern down the block.  I took her home, and thought about asking to rest for a while before I started to drive again.  I had humped bricks for 12 hours that day, and I was shot.  Her comments about the boyfriend and the unconscious horse made me think twice though, and I decided to hit the road.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I woke up there was a little flame-red car with tinted windows on the highway in front of me.  I was traveling at about 85 m.p.h. and the little red car was doing about 45 m.p.h.  Well whatever else you do Pauli, I thought to myself, don\'t fuck-up anyone else. So, I hopped on the break pedal real hard.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I avoided the devil-car, only inches from my truck\'s hood, and I felt real high.  I didn\'t buy shocks for my old Toyota though.  In any case, high-speed cornering with an empty back end was not her forte.  The truck kissed the concrete lane divider and it was all over. I said a prayer real quick, and wondered what kind of injuries I would have.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I watched from about three feet above and behind myself as everything happened.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I locked my arms and grabbed on to the wheel.  God, I wish that I had a seat belt on.  My hands and arms held fast against the inertial force, but the steering wheel bent.  I was lifted up out of the seat and half-hurled, half-floated into the windshield.  My head blew out the glass like the business end of some metal stamping machine.  But, I stayed in the cab.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then, everything turned pure glowing white.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I woke-up for the second time that night I could barely see.  The worst thing, the thing that had to be dealt with first, was the tape deck.  Black Sabbath was playing and they wouldn\'t stop.  I reached for the knobs, but the knobs were missing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Toyota truck still ran despite it all.  So together we scuttled home very slowly; neither of us could stand to turn very quickly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I lay down on the couch and couldn\'t move the left side of my body for a week.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Corona and Black Sabbath were never a big part of my life anyway, so it was easy to get rid of them and their bad mojo.  I don\'t know what the Hell to say about the women though - my stomach is still burning.</p>\r\n\r\n      \r\n'),(2224,'between the walls</br>\r\nand the winds</br>\r\nthat carry me away</br>\r\nthere is an end</br>\r\n<P>\r\nthere are escape routes</br>\r\ntell me you know the reason</br>\r\nI do not know what I feel</br>\r\nthe wind smells of deception</br>\r\n<p>\r\ndeath is an easy thing</br>\r\n<p>\r\npulling vibration</br>\r\nstanding gray</br>\r\nbent and alone</br>\r\ngracious thunder</br>'),(2225,'<P>\"Lucifer\" is written on his shoe.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOn the top of his toe.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe dirt encrusted like jewels on his face, hands, nails,teeth. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nGrit between his toes, this is what I imagine as he sits across from me winking at the blonde, \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nshe waiting for the doors to open, \r\nhe smiling and nodding, \"you want to sit down,\" he\'s saying pathetically..\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\"No, no Im off...the next stop,\" and she looks away hoping for nothing more.  She enjoys the admiring looks but the attention is edging towards unwelcome.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHe grins, mind swimming in the uncertainty of her answer, seeing her like a fly in net as he looks through the gray fog of his eyelashes.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOne more tunnel. She must be thinking..One more minute..He continues and looks and touches his greasy hair and bites his dirty nail.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHe is thinking, thinking about what to do next. Can he stop her? \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHe wonders... When will he see her again.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nShe only shudders. And when she steps off the train, he laughs.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2226,'<p>Bangkok is crazy and dirty and loud and hot (oh so hot). Every time a light turns red the motorbikes filter to the front of the traffic, and every time the light turns green it\'s like the start of a crazy multi directional motocross race. The drivers wear baseball caps on their heads and flip flops on their feet for protection. Once in a while you\'ll see that one of them is carrying a helmet in their front basket. It\'s likely it does as much good there as anywhere else, the best being about as sturdy as batting helmets. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The city is covered with mangy dogs and tiny kitties. The dogs sleep anywhere, in the shade under a car, in the shade of a tree branch, in the shade of the Buddha. And they don\'t seem to mind if you trip over them or whether you notice them at all. Every wat (temple) is filled to busting with stray mangy dogs. The monks feed animals brought to them, so unwanted and dying dogs live on within their walls, infecting healthier dogs and providing the nearby communities with unwanted puppies. The kitties are all tiny little things, and generally in much better shape. Whenever I pet one I wash my hand before I touch anything else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is some really delicious food here, mostly from food stalls and vendors which people have told me I should avoid but, wow the food is so much better on the sidewalk than it is in the tourist places. I think I have eaten more squid and more pineapple in the last few weeks than I have in my entire life previous. My favorite is a small noodle soup cart on the street next to Khao San Road. The woman who runs it is skinny with short frizzy hair. She\'s there everyday by about 10 and she stays until late at night. For 20 Baht I can get a big bowl full of soup. I get to pick the noodles (fat, medium, skinny) and tell her if I want fishballs, fishcake, pork balls, little dumplings, or everything in my soup. I always order fat noodles with everything. In a doorway near the stall there\'s a woman who sells Thai ice tea for 8 Baht without milk, 10 Baht with. At 40 Baht to the Dollar, that\'s lunch for under 60cents. I crave the soup everyday.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We don\'t have too much time to kill this time in Bangkok, but yesterday we did some shopping in the wacky shopping centers. One of them, the MBK center is one of the weirdest places I\'ve been to. The fourth floor is almost nothing but different cell phone accessories. Sounds, faceplates, phone cases, and more. I\'m not sure there are enough cell phones in the entire world for so many accessories. In the aisles there are trinket vendors willing to bargain down for the right Powerpuff Girl watch or cheap suitcase. The stores are full of cheap jewelry, expensive furniture, and five in platform sandals. It\'s sort of Blade Runner meets Hello Kitty.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Next we\'ll go to Cambodia. To get to Cambodia you can fly for $75 or you can go overland for $17. The roads in Cambodia are supposed to be among the worst in Asia. Thatpart of the trip should only take maybe 6 or 8 hours but once there I\'ll get to see Angkor Wat, then onto Phnom Pehn.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2227,'<p>Bangkok is crazy and dirty and loud and hot (oh so hot). Every time a light turns red the motorbikes filter to the front of the traffic, and every time the light turns green it\'s like the start of a crazy multi directional motocross race. The drivers wear baseball caps on their heads and flip flops on their feet for protection. Once in a while you\'ll see that one of them is carrying a helmet in their front basket. It\'s likely it does as much good there as anywhere else, the best being about as sturdy as batting helmets. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The city is covered with mangy dogs and tiny kitties. The dogs sleep anywhere, in the shade under a car, in the shade of a tree branch, in the shade of the Buddha. And they don\'t seem to mind if you trip over them or whether you notice them at all. Every wat (temple) is filled to busting with stray mangy dogs. The monks feed animals brought to them, so unwanted and dying dogs live on within their walls, infecting healthier dogs and providing the nearby communities with unwanted puppies. The kitties are all tiny little things, and generally in much better shape. Whenever I pet one I wash my hand before I touch anything else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is some really delicious food here, mostly from food stalls and vendors which people have told me I should avoid but, wow the food is so much better on the sidewalk than it is in the tourist places. I think I have eaten more squid and more pineapple in the last few weeks than I have in my entire life previous. My favorite is a small noodle soup cart on the street next to Khao San Road. The woman who runs it is skinny with short frizzy hair. She\'s there everyday by about 10 and she stays until late at night. For 20 Baht I can get a big bowl full of soup. I get to pick the noodles (fat, medium, skinny) and tell her if I want fishballs, fishcake, pork balls, little dumplings, or everything in my soup. I always order fat noodles with everything. In a doorway near the stall there\'s a woman who sells Thai ice tea for 8 Baht without milk, 10 Baht with. At 40 Baht to the Dollar, that\'s lunch for under 60cents. I crave the soup everyday.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We don\'t have too much time to kill this time in Bangkok, but yesterday we did some shopping in the wacky shopping centers. One of them, the MBK center is one of the weirdest places I\'ve been to. The fourth floor is almost nothing but different cell phone accessories. Sounds, faceplates, phone cases, and more. I\'m not sure there are enough cell phones in the entire world for so many accessories. In the aisles there are trinket vendors willing to bargain down for the right Powerpuff Girl watch or cheap suitcase. The stores are full of cheap jewelry, expensive furniture, and five inch platform sandals. It\'s sort of Blade Runner meets Hello Kitty.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Next we\'ll go to Cambodia. To get to Cambodia you can fly for $75 or you can go overland for $17. The roads in Cambodia are supposed to be among the worst in Asia. And that part of the trip should only take maybe 6 or 8 hours but once there I\'ll get to see Angkor Wat, then onto Phnom Pehn.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2228,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2228_1.jpg\">\r\n\r\n<p>pop city</p>\r\n\r\n<p>police were at the nightclub to break up a fight.  carter was arguing with an unidentified woman, and <s>when</s> cops asked carter to leave the club at least 10 times, and reportedly even counted to three before arresting him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>carter was cuffed + put in a police car.  an eyewitness has said that <s>he</s> although he was not belligerent, he did begin to cry.</p>'),(2229,'<p>It\'s 4:24 in the morning and I can\'t sleep. She is in my bed again. This is really starting to get rather maddening. The extra body heat caused me to toss and turn for a few hours before I gave up on trying to sleep at all. The worst being that just before I got up I was lying there remembering other encounters when the body heat was was like an inviting warm blanket in the winter instead of a cloud of smog during a summer heat wave. Tonight all this drives me to thinking of others, memomries of the blanket instead of the smog.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I told her that it was over before any of this happened, a hell of a lot of good that seems to have done. Its my own fault though, I fit so well into that stereotype of males that can\'t exert their  will over their own genitals. I wish I could have the thing put up on charges for biological mutiny or perhaps treason against the host organism. Situations like this is why I am an asshole and my spirit guide if I were to have one would be your neighbors dog that merrily humps your leg oblivious to the futility of the whole thing. I guess not oblivious really, just dumb or forgetful.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>She left crying earlier in the evening, I thought it was done. I had told her it was done or so I thought, but before I even had a chance to leave she was back and everything took on a feeling of the absurd. Why is she back here? I was asking myself. My coping mechanism was to imagine myself as an actor in an old romantic black and white movie, unfortunately the director of this divine comedy didn\'t see fit to yell ?cut!\'. I could have suddenly relaxed and asked some movie set lackey to fetch me my scotch and cigar. I really wish the director had yelled ?cut!\'</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Before I get to caught up in some movie fantasy let me take issue with the notion of honesty as the best policy - you say to a person this will end up with both of us becoming filled with contempt and bitterness towards each other. Whats the reaction? \"I\'d like a double serving please and throw in a side order of emotional baggage from a previous relationship for yourself.\" It baffles me that all this can be put out on the table yet still people want to get some.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\'t have a large unit and I\'m not an especially good lover either. My personality is best described as split between a curmudgeon and visionary - and I\'m not a gold mind either. So whats the motivation then? I just don\'t get it.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Back to the situation in my bed - we ended up leaving the scene of the ?break up\' for lack of a better term, we aren\'t even in a relationship. She wants to come back to my place, it is written all over her actions and I am trying to figure out why. What\'s one more meaningless encounter? I make another attempt to talk about things while in transit - I\'m told to shut up. Am I this weak willed or am I just taking advantage of the situation for sex that I would be better off without.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The sex ends up being hard and confrontational. I begin having musings about this being some fucked up psychological thing on her part that makes her desire to have sex with someone that has no connection with her. That doesn\'t fit though cause she gets pissed when I don\'t put on an act of caring - it doesn\'t seem to matter that we both know that it is an act. Maybe she just doesn\'t register anyhting I say when I mention how this isn\'t working out.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>How did I end up in this situation to begin with? An initial attraction based on the physical and intellect. Somewhere along the line the intellect of hers decided to take a vacation and not come back. Can\'t say that I blame it - smart intellect. If only I could afford a vacation as well. Now the only topic of discussion between us is her constant rambling about how pathetic and annoying other people are - I\'m thinking ?have you had a look at us lately?\' Other then that the only other topic on the menu is ?us or lack of us\' and this one doesn\'t served too often because it seems to leave a bad taste in both our mouths.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Feel free to think I\'m a prick, ashole, etc. God knows I think it myself rather often. I just would like to find someone whose company is good enough we don\'t have to be having sex all the time to compensate. I\'ve had two relationships like this in my life both ending due to circumstances that were out of my or their control at the time.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I want to know why I let myself get into the sort of situation that I\'m in now. Why do I continue to have sex with a person whom I have told straight up that things are not going to work? It just causes more pain in the long run and I end up being a man-whore in my own mind. No wait, not even whores have more self respect - they at least demand money.</p><br>     '),(2230,'so here i am drunk good intro? .. eh yeah . .. what did i drink today eh 3 of the 5$ ones.. umm yummy. Plus i just had a toastada so my tummys all good... yeah i\'m back home imade it out to substance.. nice i like that placei t\'s so clean. Got to dance of course ya know.it\'s a great workout right/right.i left had t o come home sad yeah i like walking out when the lights come on when they kick us out. and i\'m all what do u mean it\'s only 2... wtf right that\'s the way to party oh yeah i gotta go to work tomorrow right right right yeah should get some sleeep but i\'m too busy writing runon sentences  in hopes that some one will read it and be and be and be i don\'t know smiling.. or something close to that at least is that too needy? laugh i mean smile. To laugh is asking too much. so just smile and realize your\'e not the only one othere people get wasted too. yup tha\'st right i\'m a wasted of beer.... when did i start drinking beer.. oh thta was just a side thougth prodon me. anyway substance was really goood i like that jointi need a laap top ... i\'ve decided tuesdays is going to be my nite.i\'ll just go chill at substance and write runon sentencees.'),(2231,'<p>A thin shy smile was always on your face, and an expectant look in your eye.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A little glimmer of light still sparkled inside you - all that was left of your innocent youth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You rosy-cheeked babe: you brought joy to your mother, a few brief years ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Green rolling hills of Kentucky and the smell of morning coffee were lifted, so warm and so moist, on every summer\'s air.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s a bittersweet wind that blows through Kentucky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were those people who should have cared for you - they died or they left you; you found yourself worse than alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You trusted no one; yet always you wanted love and attention.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You turned from one to another - no father, no mother.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You wore a pretty blue dress with pale wildflowers on it.  Your long dark hair was auburn in the sun; it framed your sad eyes that no one looked into.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Little lines like an old maid\'s grew in your features - little clues that no one searched for.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You learned to feel dirty - you wanted to please him, to please them, you just needed to please someone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You reached out in frail hope, and waited for the hurt; he never failed to bring it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Every morsel you tasted had a hook in it - such a good giver was he.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh you were still, ever so gorgeous; only you couldn\'t see it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You were more Irish than you knew.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You mailed me lyrics from Jessica Andrews: \"An empty house, a broken fairy tale, a hollow girl with empty arms...In my blue world, you shone like Heaven\'s fire, and left me cryin\' in the dark...\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When at last you could leave him, he gave you his child.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Such a good giver was he.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am still here, waiting for you.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2232,'<p>so yesterday my co-lo server went away forever.  along with it went my domain hosting and root access.  this is bugging me enough that i am telling swinney.org about it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>since i\'ve been online i\'ve lost access for different reasons - once i went about 9 months without a single pine session,  typing ls -la, or firing up vi.  i was fine with it.  then, i bought snakecharmer.net for two years (since renewed) and everything changed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i messed with stuff constantly - tweaking css and reorganizing directories and futzing with permissions.  i rarely updated actual content, at least at first.  it was fun.  i learned stuff.  then, one day, i moved on to a new server - a sun sparcstation5 that belonged to my friend.  It lived in a closet in an auto shop in reno.  it shared a rack with a CSU/DSU and a cisco box and a ricketly old linux server.  it\'s disks were noisy and clattered away in the dark. now it is sitting in a basement quietly.</p>\r\n<p>i miss having root on that ss5, but i miss my website more.  i liked pointless reorganization and poking at the database and trying to write php.  instead i post compulsively to web boards and to diaryland and to swinney.</p>\r\n<p>i do other stuff: i walk around at night with a discman listening to the flaming lips\' \"soft bulletin\" and i read and i bullshit with my housemates and go to the movies - but these are all things i did when my domain was up, too.</p>\r\n<p>i know lots of swinney people maintain websites and have root and program, so i need your input:  what\'d you do when you couldn\'t mess with your machine/site?  were the first few days sort of traumatic?</p>\r\n<p>being obsessive/compulsive sucks.  sometimes it\'s funny, though, when you think about it.</p>'),(2233,'paul and i walked down to the ambient ping from his building, down church street.  past the posters of muscly-men, past the fur-coat hookers and gay dance clubs.  the street-front staircase was nearly hidden from view, but inside i was reminded of an old train station.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nin the side room, musicians were on stage, one with a guitar and the other with a stack of flashing lights.  paul and i sat at the bar, talking, and tweaking our ears to the soundwaves.  we kept to ourselves, although i caught the eye of the girl with blue nail polish.  she was with someone, which was okay.  i didn\'t want to talk to her tonight, but it was nice to exchange a glance.\r\n<br><br>\r\nback on the street, i bid good-bye to paul as he returned up church street, and i caught the last subway home.  the car was empty, and full of dirt from the day\'s crowd.  loose-leaf newspapers littered the seats, while gummy footprints tracked a path to and from the doors.  i sprawled my legs across the aisle, enjoying the luxury of my own personal car, until a mumbling man stepped on board at queen.  we arrived at college street and i stepped off.\r\n<br><br>\r\nup the escalator, there was a woman.  she had a silk hankerchief pulled up over her mouth and nose, and black hair.  she was asian, in her 60\'s, and pulled a little shopping cart.  as i whisked towards the top, she stood there, arranging her things.  she looked at me as i passed and walked through the turnstile.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni turned the corner, down an empty hall, and began to climb the first flight of stairs upwards.  near the top step, i heard a voice from behind.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\"excuse me!  excuse me!  could you help?  it is heavy!\" \r\n<br><br>\r\nthe woman was standing at the bottom of the stairs, pointing to her cart.  her english was broken, and i walked down, feeling sympathetic.  i picked up the cart, and carried it up two long flights of stairs until we broke the surface.  \r\n<br><br>\r\n\"have a good night,\" i said, and she echoed me.  \r\n<br><br>\r\ni felt proud of myself as i walked past her, mentally checking off my \"good deed for the day.\"  i crouched at the corner, in front of the newspaper boxes, reading headlines and waiting for the streetcar.  canada to send 750 troops to afghanistan.  dolly the clone suffers from arthritis.  eggleton: canadians know how to fight.  i stopped reading, noticing that the woman was waiting as well, standing in the shelter of a doorway with her cart.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen the streetcar careened to our stop, and i looked back toward the woman.  i said, \"here, i\'ll help you lift that,\" and helped her aboard, waving my transfer at the driver.  she sat down four seats in front of me, with her cart in the aisle.  the rest of the car was empty, except for a man snoring in the back.\r\n<br><br>\r\ntwo stops later, the woman stood up, and began wedging the cart between two single seats.  i watched her, perplexed, and began wondering if she was one of the crazies that haunt late-night transit throughout the universe.  then she walked back to me, and sat down.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\"i know where you coming from.  i know you in the subway.  what do you want?\"\r\n<br><br>\r\ni was amused, thinking she wanted to pay me a reward. \r\n<br><br>\r\n\"that\'s alright, i\'m happy to help.  don\'t worry about it.\"\r\n<br><br>\r\n\"no, you see..  i know where you\'re coming from.  the men in the subway, i know why you do it.  what do you do?  how much?\" and she leaned towards me.  i\'d never seen lust in the eyes of an old woman before, but they gleamed at the next words.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\"i know what you do.  you tell me how much, i pay.\"\r\n<br><br>\r\ni leaned back an inch or two, and stammered, \"i\'m sorry, i think you have me wrong.\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nand she huffed a bit, stood up quickly, and wandered back to the front of the streetcar.  she sat behind the driver, casting me sidelong glances every stop.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen we finally pulled up to my street, i stepped down, out onto the street, and looked back.  she was staring at me, with a weird blank look.  i guess that\'s another thing to check off life\'s to-do list.  be mistaken for male prostitute-- check.'),(2234,'<p>\r\noh heavenly father. i know i am just one of the billions you have helped bring to this world yet i can\'t help but think of scripture when i feel that it is in some way your duty to bring me out of this hell i call unemployment. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have tried for so long to contact you in regards to my current plight. you refuse to answer my calls and it is virtually impossible to get any clear answers from those living in your houses; those same houses you so spiritually inhabit.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nall i ask for is a chance to prove myself within this arena we call life. think back to when your son was born. the daily ridicule and opression. if he had not been given a chance to prove to the world that he was rightly so your son, what would you have done? would you have pulled some strings? im sure you did at some point during his career. dad\'s always pull strings. well good dads do. apparently you arent so caring. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm quite sure being the CEO of the universe is a trying and arduous job. im sure much of your time is spent keeping \"the whole\" in motion, yet i find it quite irresponsible of you to let humanity make decisions for themselves. it is quite clear that we, as humans, are incapable of choosing good over evil. we think we can. we learn we should, but very rarely do we express our emotions in positive and good ways. much of our time is spent thinking of destruction, not birth. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni grew up listening to your word and your ideas and rules and for what? so some old tit can put a host on my tongue while commanding me to repent? repent what and why?  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngod. i am broke. i am eager. i am mad at you. you are such a fake bastard it pains me to think that people believe you actually deliver us from temptation. \r\n</p> '),(2235,'<p>Its about 3 days till Khmer new year and Iâ€™m here in sweaty Siem Reap (a couple miles from Angkor Wat). Everything here is sweaty. April is the hottest month in Cambodia, and so of course I have come here in April. You may think to yourself, \"Oh, she is a wimp, when I was little it was so hot in my part of the world! blahblahblah!\" But no. It is hotter than that. Oh it is so hot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And I was wrong about the pickup taxi from the border with Thailand at Poipet to where I am now. There are no trucks with covers and benches. There are 4wd pickups, regular pickups, and you pile your bags in the bed of the pick up, and then you sit on the edge of the truck in the hot sun and they drive you down the worst road in the world (might I add this road is also intolerably dusty) and you hang on for dear life. For 8 hours.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We pay about $10 more to sit <I>inside</I> a truck and it turns out that enough others getduped into doing this to fill a horrible little bus. As luck would have it, I get on the bus last so there are only crappy seats left, like the one directly over the engine with no headrest. This choice is better than the seat that tilts back and to the left at a sharp angle, or the chair in the door of the bus where they had propped the extra wheel. They suggest my friend sit with the wheel. He loudly refuses, and they let their own guy sit in the wheel in the miniscule amount of space thatâ€™s left. I figure it will be ok over the engine because itâ€™ll be right up by the driver and have good aircon vents. And it does have good aircon vents! Until the aircon breaks after hour three. For hours four through eight, I just sitting on the hot engine sweating my guts out.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And the roads, they really, really, really are awful. When you think of roads you think of streets. They are flat and they are straight or they curve but they are basically these even panels of blacktop that take you somewhere. Not here. Here there is some black top, sort of, in chunks where you can tell that it was once part of something that was relatively road like. The French apparently fixed up these roads in the 70â€™s or so, and no one has touched them since. They are now more like boulders planted in the dust. Next to the boulders are potholes, deep, deep potholes. It takes 8 hours to drive a distance that would take about 1.5 hours in the US. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>But... then we get here and after escaping the guesthouse they tried to force us to stay at (some arrangement between the horrible bus people and the annoying guesthouse people) we run away and find a better guesthouse next door. The people are extremely friendly when they arenâ€™t being extremely pushy. Our guesthouse folks refer to each other as Mom and Dad, and Mom is forever running over and offering us free pot. We donâ€™t smoke pot so she tries to give it to us to keep. We never take it. They give us a beer instead. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Angkor Wat is really something. Everyone should try to see it. I donâ€™t know if you should try to see it in April, but in some month where it is not so so so so very very very hot, you should come here, and you should walk around the temples and you should be totally amazed at these things. When you do come here, bring a lot of one dollar bills. You can pay for everything in dollars. You get change in dollars, its US dollars for everything. They say that over 60% of US currency is in circulation outside of the US, and now I believe it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am hoping that other parts of Cambodia arenâ€™t so expensive as this sort of touristy type trap, but Angkor is worth the trouble I think. Well, so far. I\'ve been a lot more frustrated by my week in Cambodia than I was in Thailand, for one thing the food in Thailand is about a million times better, and for another, its just very different here. I\'d say it\'s the farthest I\'ve ever been from \"home\" and I donâ€™t mean that distance wise. But... it is very very beautiful, green and lush and everywhere there are houses on stilts and friendly people getting ready for new year and singing songs and asking you for dollars.</p>\r\n'),(2236,'\r\n<p>We left Siem Reap and the temples of Angkor by speed boat to Battambang. Itâ€™s the dry season so only a speedboat can get up the river. It was better than the pickup/bus scenario... barely. We arrived at the lake just as it began to rain huge heavy raindrops, and they packed us into a tiny boat to navigate the floating village, and dropped us off at the speedboat stop. I had to pee, so I found their toilet, a tiny projection off the back of the boat surrounded by boards for privacy. The toilet itself is just two boards to put your feet on as you tinkle into the lake. They had us wait there for a few minutes for the rain to clear, which was nice of them considering there are no tops to the speedboats. Actually there wasnâ€™t much to the speedboats at all, a molded fiberglass hull with some raw planks balanced across it to sit on. It wasnâ€™t so bad. it only took about 5 hours.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The river, while shallow and muddy at this time of year was cool and refreshing, and seemingly full of children, every one of which stopped splashing or walking or whatever they were doing to wave their arms and yell \"HELLO!\" they say it in a clipped little accent more like \"HAH-LO\" and they donâ€™t seem to ever get tired of screaming it to anyone who looks like they\'ve come from somewhere else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Battambang was sleepy and quiet and hot (of course it was hot, everything is hot) so I spent a lot of time in the hotel room watching HBO and reading. We sort of gave up, and actually took a flight here to Phnom Pehn, pretty whimpy, but our butts were sore from the roads and the boats and the motos. The plane was pretty interesting. Some tiny outdated Russian built thing, with no safety speech at the beginning, and broken parts sort of bolted on or welded in place. It was a completely uneventful flight though, and now we\'re here, we\'re hot, and we\'re about to go see a nearby museum.</p>\r\n'),(2237,'Think of your rock steady life. A foundation you\'ve built from then till now. I have as a girl, had a long time disdain for other girls. They are weak, they are pawns, and they play into it a lot. Not all girls do it.<br><br>\r\nSometimes it is the threat of losing respect for those you have admired and loved the most that makes your heart ache, not the feelings you have on your own behalf. It\'s 3am here, and it\'s hailing. The little plinkety plink noises are enveloping into my state of discontent, and I long to re-examine and find solace in a place unlooked for, in my own strength.<br><br>\r\nOur bond, hers and mine, has been a long time in the making, and I will not give it up. She has taken care of me, and I wish that I could have warned her, because as a good friend, I could see her self destruction, and I knew that there was a catalyst waiting. But she suprises me, and I am left to thinking only about how I wish I could have been with her for these years, and so that maybe in my strength, she would have found her own growing too. It\'s funny how the situation can make one person grow, and another fall apart.<br><br>\r\nWithin my heart, I feel a pain tenfold as she falls apart, one occurance at a time. Seams unfolding, I am the one who is falling apart. It\'s all that I can do.  But I know that I\'ll be here to hold my parts together tomorrow, overtired. Feeling warm, and smiling in arms fresh and hands that sing with a gentle caress.<br><br>\r\nFor now though, I will think of your eyes, and your ways. Tomorrow is another day for me, and another wall for her.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2240,'                                 \r\n                                                                 jock me\r\n\r\n                                                                 I know so and so \r\n                                                                 and I?ve worked on \r\n                                                                 blah blah blah \r\n                                                                 and I went to the first \r\n                                                                 Yeah I was there before \r\n                                                                 anyone cared \r\n                                                                 I heard- saw -tasted it first \r\n                                                                 It was I \r\n                                                                 I was the one who made \r\n                                                                 up that slang \r\n                                                                 No I didn?t read it in \r\n                                                                 some comic strip \r\n                                                                 and if I did I wrote it \r\n                                                                 I drew it and I was the \r\n                                                                 one to put it out there \r\n                                                                 For the pubic \r\n                                                                 Ya know \r\n\r\n                                                                 Yeah that was me or \r\n                                                                 a close friend \r\n                                                                 a close \r\n                                                                 one indeed \r\n                                                                 He lived in my building \r\n                                                                 Sometimes his mail \r\n                                                                 would come to my place \r\n                                                                 and I?d hold it for him \r\n                                                                 That?s right we were close \r\n                                                                 Like family \r\n\r\n                                                                 Yeah everyone jocks me \r\n                                                                 I act like I care about what \r\n                                                                 you just said \r\n                                                                 Smile laugh chuckle and \r\n                                                                 nod my head \r\n                                                                 I?ll tell you \r\n                                                                 that your \r\n                                                                 special \r\n                                                                 Cuz I can tell that?s what ya \r\n                                                                 want to hear \r\n                                                                 I?m a crowd pleaser \r\n                                                                 And I?ll take you to dinner \r\n                                                                 Let you know about all \r\n                                                                 the people who ever \r\n                                                                 Visited that hole \r\n                                                                 Maybe we?ll see their \r\n                                                                 head shots lined up against \r\n                                                                 the walls \r\n                                                                 Maybe they left some expensive memorabilia \r\n                                                                 That I can sell on e-bay to some \r\n                                                                 Teeny bopper or middle-aged man in \r\n                                                                 Kentucky who thinks Ian Ziering \r\n                                                                 Really is a good actor \r\n\r\n                                                                 I?m not impressed not impressed at all\r\n                                 '),(2238,'<p>Karmic cleansing has left me feeling scoured. Clean yes, but scoured none the less. Having something akin to an epiphany in self awareness and needing to address the new perspective and all the issues it gives rise too, I dive right in. Apparently the epiphany wasn\'t so profound since it didn\'t give me the perspective needed to tread with caution. I get these notions in my head and they become all consuming things that must be addressed right now.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I find myself staring into the reflection of the moving streetcar in the darkened window of the shops passing by like some answers and atonement could be found in these shadowy images. I fear the inevitable need for human contact and the swelling desire to try and make it the most precious interaction. In response I choose the sensual rhythme of late night radio that speaks to me about the world I live in as a substitute for that contact at times. The radio doesn\'t listen to me but it is the constant reminder that I am not alone, the many faces that I pass day in day out don\'t do this for me. They all seem to be automata to me, so fractured from my life and experiences. The radio talks to me about the goings on in the world, the radio has seen the newspaper headlines that line my streets. I need to start turning off the radio and dealing with reality.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>When I turn off the radio I am confronted with the human interactions of the past that make me fear the human interactions of the future. I hear that expirements in intentionality have actually proven that intentions can influence things on some minuscule level, that isn\'t really reassuring. From what I have read there is a lot of debate related to whether these experiments are even stastically significant within the realm of science.</p><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2239,'<p>Underestimating people always catches one off guard. I have only really done so once in a situation that has come back to bite me. Its been made worse by adolescent social complications partly of my own making.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m confronted with mixed feelings. The underestimated has new found respect in my eyes and has also taught me a valuable lesson. I can be really hard to move, impact, stir, whatever but its defintely happened here. Then there is some anger at myself for being caught with my guard down. Sometimes when you allow yourself to think of people in a precious way and things can\'t work out for some reason or another you assume that the logical and rational aspects that have recognized this will do the de-investment work for you.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I can take some solace in the dark social mechanisms that have brought this about. Rooted in misery and ending in the same - should have know better, but I didn\'t. A lesson taught by a strict school teacher.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Actions speak louder then words - I have been told through out this experience. I agree that actions speak louder then words, but I would like to point out that they are no more resistant to falsehoods or paradox then words. The phrase that has been in my mind through all of this is rather childish but whatever - sticks and stones may break my bones but names will never hurt me - I think this is bullshit. I underestimated people in ways they are not aware of. Words have always been more hurtful too me, this is why my notions of the sacred and profane seems so out of synch with others.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The underestimated didn\'t seem aware of this but I suppose this is probably my fault. It seems increasingly easy to take responsibility for ones negative experiences. If I didn\'t do that then things wouldn\'t have happened like this, sorta thing. Something is flawed in this reasoning though, it simply leads to isolation into a fortress like mentality. Don\'t interact, don\'t make any decisions. I\'m not sure how to respond to it all. The whole experience has reminded me of the fact that people are defintely individual entities and work based on their own notions, which really I think I should know by now. Makes me question the notion of love versus mere circumstance as far as being the catalyst for creating connections between people. I\'ve always thought circumstance plays a larger role then most people care to admit, I\'d like to believe the opposite though. Otherwise we are all just roaming around using each other for our own needs as our circumstance dictate.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>What bothers me most is that I still want the underestimated to be happy and I really need to come to terms with the fact that I can\'t be a primary source of this. I hadn\'t been a primary source in a while but still doesn\'t make it easier. To pursue anymore dialogue related to these feelings though just seems to be self serving and maybe even disruptive to their happiness.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>It has been experiences like this that have made me drawback and start going through relations like some sort of consumer good. I can\'t do that this time, there needs to be some progress.</p><br>'),(2241,'<P>giftmas has come and gone. the lives of many turkeys have been claimed in the yearly feasting. it\'s all in the name of celebration. i mean, i truely do love being able to see my family all together in the same room. hell, they even get along!\r\n\r\nbut damn, everyone just turns into the world\'s biggest asshole as soon as it\'s all over. i guess everything is just a let down after all those toys and all that food. \r\n\r\nhell, i know i\'m an asshole because i\'m just downright broke as hell. seeing that diminished paycheque after all the time i didn\'t get to work during the holidays was just downright depressing.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni guess everyone else is just as fooled by the spirit of the season. it\'s fine and dandy and then the comedown hits ya. \r\n</P><P>\r\nserotonin depletion due to lack of decorations and lights sparkling on everything.</P>'),(2242,'<p>you stood inside the tree. like a forest house. a giant small home for a small giant. wonderful. giant. you. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>i wanted to climb it. but i wasn\'t big enough or strong enough. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>...i thought maybe you could lift me. but i was too shy to ask. so i went only as far as i could on my own. a few feet. but enough to feel like i was flying. and i almost thought that tree would lift me up itself. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>because the bark underneath my fingers tingled. like skin. and i wanted to kiss it. i wanted it to know what i felt. and that i knew it was alive.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>the rain came then.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it didn\'t matter. but the rain came. and that bark was wet and dripping like blood. or like honey. a thing to suck the marrow from.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i thought i might be afraid of falling. but when i looked down you were there. and even in the dark dark grey of stormy twilight i could see your eyes. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>shining.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>you laughed. maybe at me. i don\'t know. it didn\'t matter. because we were like kids again. revelling in the dirt and the wet and the quiet nature around us. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was still standing on the tree and said something. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can\'t quite remember because your eyes were shining so. but i said something about that tree and wanting it or how life had movement or some such thing. and you said \'it\'s flowing\'. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s flowing.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p><br>...and i knew exactly what you meant.</p>\r\n'),(2243,'<p>The King of Carlton street died yesterday, a creature of love and caring left. I  barely knew him other then that he had been a regular fixture on the streets of  my neighborhood for the short time I have been here. I stopped to read the writings that people have left in remembrance on the steps this man called home. Someone had provided some personal information about him, once a family man now estranged from them. He had been a loving person on the streets the accounts of various strangers tell me. He was always quick to try and cheer people up and tell a joke. I am amazed at this mans accomplishments in touching people especially considering his own circumstances and how unloving a street existence can be. In appearance he could have passed as the brother of Santa Claus and judging by the good bye messages left for him would have shared the title of saint.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p> Hail to the King - a hero to me </p><br>'),(2244,'<P>mommies and daddies, there is a grave disturbance in the force. somebody\'s main cannabis man got greedy and faked one oxycontin scrip too many, ended up in the honky-tonk pokey. it\'s a sad story, isn\'t it?  and that little pobrecito has other people\'s jones to worry about, too, hungry ids depending on him to come up with the goods, and nothin\' is panning out.</P><P>he\'s tried copping on the street and ended up with two little baggies filled with spanish moss for his troubles. he gave it another shot and got a close look at a .38, got lifted for $70, nearly shat himself.</P><P>things are looking bleak. the thc is starting to clear from people\'s systems, synapses that haven\'t been used in years are starting to arc again. too much of this could be dangerously productive.</P>i know you\'re wondering, \"what can i do? here i sit with my big city connections, my gorgeous piles of green before me; how can i help a brother out?\"  well, my friend, far be it for me to suggest using our glorious national post system for unseemly means, but somebody\'s (i don\'t know whose) address is: 22213 Croom Road, Brooksville, FL 34601. what you do with that information is your business.</P><P>and the damn thing works both ways. <I>you know it. you show it. if you had a garden, you\'d grow it.</I> grateful people have been known to send all manner of things in return: random CDs, original art, nude photographs of a certain handsome someone with a big fat dubois stuck to his lip, toothbrushes, arcane knowledge, maybe even a little cash money if the need arises. and someday we\'ll do the same for you.</P> '),(2245,'<p>what are you suppoesed to think?  why should i even think.  wouldn\'t it just be better to revert to my animal instincts?  let the laws of nature be my master.  even Rousseau found this better than being a slave, at the will of other powers leading you astray.\r\n</p><p>\r\nindeed, i remember, just a few months ago, in Japan, as I was sipping a AW rootbeer float, in the southern reaches of okinawa.  everything was careless, i had some intentions, to learn to read the language, high among them.  it wasn\'t so urgent to get on with it.\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2245_1.pjpeg\">\r\n<p>\r\nnow, if it doesn\'t move fast, i start to get paranoid.  why can\'t it be better, what is really under my control?\r\n</p><p>\r\nhow does one even get into a position like this?  i wrote a paper in high school once, where the main theme was \'what i know\'.  funny, indeed, to look back, and recall how much wisdom was in my naivety.  the crux of the argument;  the more i know, the more that i realize that i don\'t know.\r\n</p><p>\r\na wolf on the prowl, he sees his prey.  immediately he attacks, grrrrr, shlurpppp, sluppppp, blood mats his fur, but it doesn\'t concern him at all.  the satisfaction of his hunger calms him now.  perhaps he\'s feeling a bit good about himself as well.\r\n</p><p>\r\nright, so when i get myself into a position, where i fall in love with a married woman, i should really just stop thinking.  i can analyze, and can hypothesize, i should close my eyes.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe was afraid to even look at me, when i first met her.  staring right into her eyes, as I tested her out, i could see her want to dart like a rabbit.  my fangs showed themselves and I growled.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin my lair she was like a little girl.  \'ohhhh, what is this\' she asked coyly, as she explored my muscular haunches.  make me dinner, and do the dishes when you are finished.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut, then suddenly, the realization comes, that everything is so perfect.  why can\'t i hold on to this forever.  why couldn\'t time have stopped on January 6, early in the afternoon.  on the subway would have been fine.  even as we walked in the rain would have sufficed.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen i start to think.  i realize that i am sharing her.  how can this be?  i am the alpha male.  where is this scraggly excuse for a kitten, and why does he not scurry away!  \r\n</p><p>\r\nbut, is it right for me to expect him to scurry?  i am the transgressor in this situation.  thought turns to questioning.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin our context, i am in the wrong.  she is too.  what is wrong?\r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\'t worry about this question so much.  i have been desensitized by hundreds of years of precursors.  i feel no need to flagellate myself in the closet, like a puritan.\r\n</p><p>\r\nno, the question that i am troubled with stems from love for a woman of a different pack.  when i look out onto the desert, everything seems so close.  once the journey starts, however, the true dimensions of the situation expand beyond exploration of one feeble set of paws.\r\n</p><ul><li>\r\n1.  what should i feel, on the nights when she is not with me?  ordinarily, the idea of a girlfriend being with another man would enrage me.  but here, it is her place, to be next to him.\r\n</li><li>\r\n2.  can i ever trust a woman, met under such dubious circumstances?\r\n</li><li>\r\n3.  it is horrible to ever try to control another person.  nothing will ever cause more frustration.  but she wants to be told what to do.\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nTBC\r\n'),(2246,'<p>i sort of want to share this with someone, and swinney.org seemed like an ok place to do it.  i am not a terribly good poet, but i try to be sincere.  maybe that helps.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this was written after a night of wandering around southeast portland (hawthorne area) while listening to the flaming lips\' \"soft bulletin.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthere is an industry that bundles joy<br>\r\nin part and parcel<br>\r\nmetered by savants and city lights<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshowcased in sea rocks<br>\r\nand orange-juiced lips<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsweetwrapped in<br>\r\nsugar liquor litanies<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndelivered by sunburnt junkies<br> \r\ndropping bright bites by flashlight.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2247,'<P>Pendleton is my favoite cat in the world.  he is large and pink with white fur. he never moves.  he always sits in his bed.  sometimes he raises his head aloft and lets out a mighty, albiet quiet, \"mreor.\"  he is Pendleton.  here him roar.  the other cats, what do they care if Pendleton sleeps all day and all night.  they work around him.  Pendleton sleeps through it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>usagi is psycho.  Pendleton has a grip.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have never seen Pendleton picked up.  he reminds me of a catfish becuase his head is so huge.  he has a blockhead.  he just squeeked.  i think he is mad at me for making noise on the keyboard.  he wants me to observe the hour.  it is 8:16 AM. and no one is stirring, not even an unagi.  ix is supposed to be awake.  he said, you mind if i wake you up to get breakfast at 8?  i said no.  does this mean i can wake him up?  i could place a bowl of milk on his head.  the only thing that will get Pendleton to move is a bowl of milk.  Pendleton LOVES milk.  he starts meowering.  Pendleton will climb ix and drink milk from the top of his head.  this will wake ix up and we can go for coffee and food before the lines swell with yuppies and their conversations that are never very interesting.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Pendleton.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i\'ll get some pictures of him and post them.  you guys seemed to be in love with tub cat.  Pendleton is a good cat too.  although i dont believe he can fit an entire cat of cat food in his mouth like tubcat can.</P>'),(2248,'<p>\r\nI just found some newspaper article about a girl who was pregnant and didn\'t even know about it. She had her period as usual, just a little...less, but she didn\'t give it much thought. Her tummy wasn\'t even much bigger than usual, so she just assumed she had gained weight. Heh. One day her stomach started to hurt really bad, and she was taken to the hospital where they told her she was having a baby right away.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThis made me really paranoid. I have my period, but not...alot. I\'m so delicate and I make you hungry, I know, but...geez. For some reason I started believing I\'m having a baby. Stupid, yes? I have nothing better to think about.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nHaving a kid now wouldn\'t be the worst thing in the world though. I\'m in a very happy and stable relationship with the man I\'d like to spend the rest of my life with. How cheezy is that?\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI should do something better on a Saturday night than worry about a child I\'m not having in the first place. I should go out and get drunkish like normal 19-20 year olds.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThe end.\r\n</p>'),(2249,'<p>My father sang in the opera.  He never wanted a career as a soloist; he was happy to be in the chorus.  There was one real opera company in town - the Lyric Opera of Chicago - and he was a part of it for over 25 years.  That was the best thing about my father.  For him, belonging to the opera gave meaning to his existence in a way that nothing else ever did.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>My father hated the work that he did for money, and he drank a lot to forget about it.  He had grown up on the west side of the city in a neighborhood filled with Italians, and Jews, and Greeks, and Syrians, and Armenians.  It was a ghetto, really.  Chicago has always been like that - people who were \"the same\" lived together, whether they wanted to or not.  My father may have left that neighborhood physically, but he was always a part of it; the west side had shaped him, and made him who he was.  When you told someone in Chicago what neighborhood you were from, you told them everything about yourself: how much money you made, what race you were, what religion you were, what you ate for dinner, everything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The opera changed everything.  Memories of depression era urban poverty and untimely deaths were left behind as men and women came together to tell great stories through song and screen and costume. The mean stuff of daily life was forgotten, and Wagner\'s tales of long-dead heroes were remembered.  My father took me with him a few times when I was a kid.  I watched him, and I saw what took place behind the scenes.  I saw costumes being made; I saw sets being built; I saw the singers and the orchestra rehearsing.  I think that I understood why he liked those people: they were different.  No one made much money doing what they were doing, at the time, but they all came together to put on the show.  And a grand show it was.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The people who sang had voices that were loud, very loud.  It\'s not the sort of thing that is easily conveyed with the written word.  Their voices could be felt.  It\'s a bit like being at the symphony and realizing that the people who are playing are really in the business of moving air.  It\'s a physical experience.  As a child I was terrified by their voices.  This one fellow in particular - Julio - chased me around the make-up area when I complained that he was too loud.  He loved his big voice.  The all loved themselves.  The men had beards, the women had long hair, and the atmosphere was sexually charged; even as a kid I picked up on that.  Everyone was larger than life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When my father left the opera he rapidly grew decrepit.  The opera had functioned as something like a counterweight in his personality; when it was removed, everything that it had held in balance came crashing down.</p>        \r\n\r\n<p>I wonder if personal websites and journals are doing for other people what the opera did for my father: making them feel like they are a part of some great story that is constantly being told and re-told; providing them a way to escape the less than ideal circumstances in which they exist; offering them some sense of community.  Maybe it\'s about a little time and place to show people who you really are, and what you are becoming.  Graduate school has been doing that for me.  But, I might need to leave it on Monday.  I don\'t want to become what my father became.</p>  \r\n'),(2250,'<p>Pussy catâ€™s in <I>heat.</I></p>\r\n<p>I had no idea what the problem was when she started getting really friendly with everybody who walked in. Rolling on her back and purring. Rubbing her head against your foot as she wags her tail. I just thought maybe she was lonely.</p>\r\n<p><I>The cat is sexually frustrated.</I></p>\r\n<p>And sheâ€™s even more frustrated because the boy cat keeps chasing her and jumpin all over her. See, he <I>knows</I> sheâ€™s ready.</p>\r\n<p>Sadly enough, heâ€™s still a kitten, and just not mature enough to mate with. Which I can only assume to be <I>more</I> frustrating for her.</p>\r\n<p>Wandering about the apartment like a honeymooner without a mate.</p>\r\n<p>The other day I saw her licking herself. <I>â€œCleaning herselfâ€</I> Iâ€™m sureâ€¦</p>\r\n<p>Her purr is more like a moan. Actually itâ€™s more like whining. Makes you feel bad for her. Sheâ€™s frustrated as hell, poor thing. Staring up at me with yellow eyes, wondering, â€œWhen the hell am I gonna get laid?â€</p>\r\n<p>I keep asking myself that same question.</p>'),(2251,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2251_1.pjpeg\"><br><br>I did it. I invoked the call-signs of fated flights, the yahweh of faiths, the jargon of schemes with naÃ¯ve question-marks.<br><br>\r\nMy admissions carried me deep into the night but my omissions left me there.<br><br>\r\nUmbrae form penumbrae at intersections; their dark is heavy. This is me, crying, hideous. This is me swallowing my tongue.<br><br>\r\nI give into an agreeably greater good because it is in my nature. On the other hand, I never knew nature to be such a harsh task-master. If it were \r\nleft to me, I\'d have my miles to walk.<br><br> \r\nBut at this moment, all I have are these wordless words, and yesterday\'s miles haunt my callused feet.<br><br>But time marches on, and I respect myself more than to rely on simple memories for sustenance. <br>'),(2252,'<p>\r\ndearest sugar_butt,\r\n</p><p>\r\ni have always needed to understand things from the root of the problem.  \r\nyou can beat around the issue for a while, but unless you really examine \r\nwhat the problem is, you will always feel that you are missing something.\r\n</p><p>\r\nits frustrating to deal with people who can\'t see this, but then again, \r\nmost people are content not to examine their lives, and let it slip by.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\'t claim to have all of the answers.  in fact, the more that i \r\nread, the more that i can see, i still don\'t completely grasp.  but somewhere \r\nalong the line i can start to see different parts of the puzzle coming \r\ntogether.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin anycase, i think that you definitely can understand me, which i \r\nlike.  and with the 20 threads that you keep running, you often times prevent me \r\nfrom following darker paths of thought, that probably are not even worth \r\nthinking.\r\n</p><p>\r\nits kind of scary at first.  like the first time that i saw a deer \r\nbeing slaughtered.  at first, i didn\'t want to look.  but i was curious.  \r\nsoon enough, i peeked.  repulsive at first, but then i looked more, and i \r\nslowly grew accustomed to it.  after that, i started to pick out the different \r\nmuscles, and the organs.  finally, after that i was able to make the \r\nreal connection between a deer, the slaughter, and real food being put onto \r\na plate in front of me.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin that instance, it was something at first ugly, that grew to be \r\ninteresting.  other times, it isn\'t so clear cut.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbasically, we are creatures of habbit.  we find a way of living our \r\nlives that works.  at first when we are learning, we might have to think \r\nabout the act.  other times, we can just be conditioned by our environment (what \r\nwe watch other people do).  sometimes, however, even if the way we are \r\ndoing something is harmful to us, we just can\'t stop doing it, because we are \r\nso used to it, and we don\'t know any other way.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin any case, i am glad that i haven\'t scared you off (i think).  i \r\nreally like you, for so many reasons.  there are a million pretty faces in new \r\nyork.  but someone who is everything, the way you are, i have never \r\ncome across.  i sometimes feel like i am taking a big risk, by putting \r\neverything out in front of you this way, but if you can understand me, and still \r\naccept me, then it has been completely worth my troubles.  it will only serve \r\nto build a trust between us, and strengthen the bond that we are forming.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnixie_nix\r\n</p><TT>\r\n----Original Message Follows----<BR>\r\nFrom: sugar_butt (sugar_butt987006@yahoo.com)<BR>\r\nTo: nadnad_nixie_nox (nixie_nixie_nobbies@hotmail.com)<BR>\r\nSubject: some thoughts...<BR>\r\nDate: Mon, 14 Jan 2002 19:13:43 -0800 (PST)\r\n</TT><p>\r\ni think it was good that we talked today...  i\'m glad\r\nthat u insisted on talking about stuff that i\'ve been\r\navoiding all my life.  i\'m afraid i guess to face\r\nmyself...  i rarely think about topics such as these\r\nand just pretend that nothing is wrong and move on...\r\nat the same time, subconsciously they kind of control\r\nmy actions and decisions.  maybe i do try to exploit\r\ninto these areas with friends like Lucy and Lana but\r\nrarely we talk about it in such depth.  after all, we\r\nall got a ton of problems so there is no time in the\r\nworld to fully explore them.  i have to admit, i\'m\r\nmuch more sensitive and observant of others\' problems\r\nand feelings than of my own.  i always say that i\r\ndon\'t know what i want but deep down i probably do but\r\ni\'m afraid to pursue them or i don\'t want to know what\r\ni want...  like everyone else, i see myself in other\r\npeople\'s lives and problems and i vicariously discuss\r\nmy problems and fears through analyzing others\'\r\nissues...\r\n</p><p>\r\nand the funny thing is i don\'t even realize that until\r\ntoday...\r\n</p><p>\r\nwell, the conclusion is i\'m full of problems and\r\nissues although i\'m very good at pretending that i got\r\nnone...  u really opened a can of worms here...  maybe\r\nwe should just talk about fluff and go after all...\r\n</p><p>\r\nsugar_butt\r\n</p>'),(2255,'<p>The roommate came home around 8pm and I woke up to her pouring drinks for herself and then new boyfriend. He had just gotten back from a week long conference in Niagra Falls. As I stumbled out of my room the absence of lights with the exception of a few lit candles communicated to me that it was time to make myself scarce. A quick check of the fridge assured me that despite my hunger I was feeling too lazy to cook anything. In a matter of ten minutes I\'d showered, dressed, and headed out the door.<p/><br>\r\n<p>I walked across the street and returned the five movies that I rented last night - One porn, two romantic comedies, and three family movies. As I return them it struck me that they are a good reflection of my headspace at the moment and at 87 cents a pop damn cheap.</p><br>\r\n<p>Out on the street I was told by the flashing lights that my cinematic binge isn\'t over yet. I walked up the steps where the shrine to Larry the King used to be, I guess people are done remembering now. Having read Fuzzy\'s mention of his viewing of Ghost World, it was all made up for me. I sauntered on up to the ticket counter and with the swipe of a card I was in with some slight misgivings about not having eaten anything.</p><br>\r\n<p>The theater was completely empty with the exception of a punk looking couple that was sitting in the back row. I tried not to take notice of them, as they had already started the make out session. I was reminded of a highschool girlfriend who gave me a hand job in the middle of a crowded theater and tried not to laugh to loudly at my own memories as I took my seat in the front row. The couple looked up for a second and then got right back to the task at hand, I hadn\'t wanted to interrupt them.</p><br>\r\n<p>The movie started and I knew within the first few minutes that it was going to be good. I suspended my disbelief and for the next hour or two proceeded to watch the movie and fantasize about a love affair with the Enid character. It helped that in appearance she was the twin of Amber Bishop, the first girl I ever slept with (no sex just foddling of each other). A dam of memories was opened up - Spiderland acres, Shotmaker, Hillcrest shows, Dostoevsky, Hitch Hiking, and the Jitters club. Last I knew Amber had moved to the Yukon and was making a living as a cook in some lodge up there, she had always made fun of the Dostoevsky. The movie with its theme of a lack of ability to relate to 99 percent of humanity reasonated loud and clear. It reminds me that the social contract that I signed before coming into existence doesn\'t work in my favor in this regards.</p><br>\r\n<p>Clause II subsection V</p><br>\r\n<p>Although, the client will be brought into the world in an area of affluence - in exchange he  will only be given the ability to commune with a small percentage of the collective</p><br>\r\n<p>As the movie ends I was left with a desire to pull off the same thing Enid does. I find one big fault with cinema (or maybe it is a fault with me) it always fills me with desires that can only be fulfilled on the screen.</p><br>\r\n<p>Remember that punk couple, while with the movie finished I turned to the door to head out and I wasn\'t  dissappointed they were still going at it. During the walk out I tried to make myself believe that they had missed the entire movie, having been otherwise occupied.</p><br>\r\n<p>Standing on the steps having a smoke afterwards I was wondering if I had the will to go back to school - this time for something that I had chosen and wanted to do, instead of something with *good job prospects*. Funny how I am unemployed after five years of education based on *good job prospects*.  Lesson learned the hard way -  be true to yourself. My ruminating was interrupted by a couple on the steps in tears trying to figure out if they should stick together. I immediately thought if Larry the King was here he would be trying to cheer these two up. They looked good together and in the interest of aesthetics I hope they keep it together, there was obviously more then just aestetic though they were both bawling. I remembered different times I had been told I looked good or right with someone. Its a strange idea *looking right with someone*. It was always strangers that said it to me, probably cause they don\'t know the details it makes it easy for strangers to think in this way.</p><br>\r\n<p>Ending my evening in a 24 hour diner that many have warned me against, I have staff asking me for help with their crosswords. I figure my writing has meandered enough and its time to read some more of *Children of the Alley* or do some of the drawings I have been planning. Chances are I end up eating and doing crosswords with strangers though.</p><br> '),(2253,'<P>some notes on surviving without my girlfriend for the first week of\r\nher being gone.</P>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2253_4.jpg\" ALIGN=\"RIGHT\" ALT=\"yea right\">\r\n<P>_ pornography. i started getting really horny after a week.  today\r\nit is the worst, just terrible, the urge to go and seek out naked\r\nbodies getting dirty and thrusting all over the place.  unfortunately\r\ni am like seymour in <i>ghost world</i> when i am around porno stores.\r\ngeneral uneasiness and wish to be gone before i am seen in either an\r\nincriminating or a lustful manner.  even if i were to set out to find\r\nit, im not sure where i would go.  santa cruz either hides it\'s\r\npornography shops, or it is a genuinely wholesome community. yes sure\r\ni could go to the pack and save for a playboy, but what fun is in\r\nthat?  besides, there is something inherently pathetic about buying a\r\nporno and nothing else at the pick-n-save, or even worse, a porno and\r\na candy bar, or a porno and a coke.\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2253_1.jpg\" align=\"left\">\r\n<P>_ strange day dreams.  it started when i daydreamed my cock was a\r\nhuge thick mountain.  that was amusing.  then i dreamed her vagina was\r\njust as large, like the sky of clouds, or a huge pink valley flipped\r\nupside down.  this one was hard to recover from.  somehow despite the\r\nvast proportional chasm of this daydream versus reality failed to\r\ndiffuse the situation and it made me sweat with desire.  next came the\r\nenormous volcanic vagina that appeared to me on the car ride home.\r\nit was huge and red and was going to swallow me and then it turned\r\ninto a blooming flower that kept blossoming and blossoming, a\r\nnever ending non-cyclical bloom of folds of vagina, absolutely\r\nfantastic!\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2253_2.jpg\" ALIGN=\"RIGHT\">\r\n<P>_ then came the sitting at work.  sitting sitting sitting.  you\r\nknow how the internet is.  it is to porn what costco is on a sunday\r\nnoon, all you can eat samples of every cuisine.  but it is work.\r\npeople are everywhere.  there are cameras.  there are programs that\r\nscan the intranet looking for keywords in every requested url.  yes\r\nthere are ways to get around that but still i would not be in the\r\ncomfort of my own home.\r\n<a href=\"http://www.coeddormshower.com\"><img src=\"http://www.coeddormshower.com/images/coed%20logo.jpg\" width=400></a>\r\n<P>_ wake and wack.  in the absence of porn, sexual energy growing,\r\nthe best source for erotica is the dream world.  in those few minutes\r\nafter waking up but before remembering that i should be at work, there\r\nare large orgies of women, sometimes strange encounters with men, the\r\nmorning wood already prepared and waiting.  then when i get done, the\r\nshower is right there and cleanup is really a non issue.\r\n'),(2254,'\r\n<p> I\'m still in Cambodia, and it\'s still hot. In the last two days I have visited the Tuol Sleng Prison AKA S-21 and formerly known as Tuol Sleng High School, and The Killing Fields of Choeung Elk.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It is hard to imagine bustling Phnom Phen, the countries largest city, as an empty ghost town, but that\'s what it was from 1975 to 1979 when Pol Pot and the Khmer Rouge took power and drove all of the people from their homes into the fields to slave away for 16 hours a day with nothing in return but a small meal of watery rice. They abolished the people\'s culture, their religion, the monetary system, the schools, the hospitals. They destroyed temples and burned books.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tuol Sleng Prison is an ordinary building on an ordinary side street. I suggested we pay a guide to show us around here because I knew there would be more history than could be understood from the photos alone. Our guide was a soft-spoken very pregnant woman. She talked slowly with a heavy accent. She showed us first some private cells where important prisoners were kept and interrogated. Important prisoners were those who were in (or I guess formerly in) the Khmer Rouge themselves, or other officials. It didn\'t matter that they were previously one of the elite; their fates were exactly the same as everyone else. 4000 people went through Tuol Sleng, and only 7 or so survived. (The survivors are all still alive today and living in Phnom Phen).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The others were interrogated by specially trained Khmer Rouge who were picked as children for the job. All interrogators (who were also the executioners) were between 14 and 18. Our guide mentioned the records showing one had killed 300 prisoners by age 15. Many of them are also still alive and well and living whatever normal lives someone with that history might live. There were two groups of interrogators, the nice group and the hard group. You got the hard group if you failed to confess your allegiance to the CIA (though no one had any ties) or refused to give up the names of your fellow \"revolutionaries\". The nice group gave you water and cigarettes; the hard group beat you, sometimes to death, before returning you to your cell. The not so important prisoners might be kept 30 to a room, all with their ankles lashed together. Unable to do anything but lie on their backs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In some of the cells there are photos on the wall of one of the 14 prisoners that were still at the prison when it was liberated by the Vietnamese. The photos show them where they were found, and it\'s hard to imagine you are standing in the same room that was once the cell where this person was tortured to death.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Like the nazi\'s the Khmer Rouge kept records. They photographed every person who came through, and many of these photos are on the walls as well. They are of men and women and children. Some women with their infants in their laps, and all of them knew that this is the last stop before they\'ll be taken to the killing fields and bludgeoned to death. You can see it in their eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the last room on the tour is a map of Cambodia made out of the skulls of the prisoners who died and were buried on the prison grounds. As we were walking through, our guide mentioned rather offhandedly the place where her family was taken to work in the fields, and then how her mother, father, and siblings were killed and she escaped into Vietnam.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What do you say to that? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The killing fields are a ways off, and we got there by motorcycle (finally renting our own to be rid of the annoying moto drivers and their broken-down bumpy machines). At first you see only a large stupa (shrine) erected as a monument to all of the people who were killed there (over 8000). It looks unremarkable until you see that it is filled with shelves that are themselves filled with the skulls of the excavated mass graves. Tourists were having their photos taken in front of this, and I can\'t think of a more unsettling picture to have of yourself. After walking through the small part of the field that\'s been excavated I just sat and stared at the stupa. I couldn\'t figure out how to feel here. Were the ghosts of these people all around me? What did they think of my being here at this site? How could this have happened in my lifetime, without my ever knowing? Without my parents being outraged and yelling at the television about injustice? Everywhere you look there are holes in the ground that were once filled with over a hundred people each. There is a sign on a tree to indicate it was what the executioners swung infants against to kill them. They didn\'t dare waste precious bullets on anyone. Over where the graves have not yet been excavated, you can see bones and clothing sticking out of the ground.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And the whole time you just think about how these people these young boys, the interrogators who were killing their own families, their friends, their neighbors, and even those who may have been standing right beside them doing the same things the day before, not for being different or having another religion or really any good reason.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Overall between 2 and 3 million were killed in those 4 years in prisons and at killing fields all over the country, and this happened in my lifetime, this isn\'t some strange war from another time. I\'m not really sure what else to say about it, I just thought I should tell you the story.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2256,'Every year in the first or second week of January my allergies kick in.  But to what?  Wtf is blooming now?  Further, if it was something kicking the bucket and sending out spores, wouldn\'t it have done that already?  It feels like evil gnomes have snuck up on me in my sleep and filled my nose with superglue.<br><br>\r\nOh, and today when I was taking a walk for lunch a bird crapped on me.  Rather good aim, I must say, they managed to hit the bullseye of the center of an \"o\" in a \".com\" on my shirt.  Thankfully I had a spare tshirt in my car...<br><br><em>*SNNNNNuuuuuuurrrK!*</em> baaaaah!'),(2257,'<b>Sunday January 13th 2002:</b> \r\n<br><br>\r\nFeverishly searching for loose change and lint in the creases and folds of my antique pine framed sofa i stumble across several neatly packaged abstract concepts including dignity and abberation.<br><br> \r\n<b>Monday January 14th 2002:</b> \r\n<br><br>Soup <br><br>\r\n<b>Tuesday January 15th 2002: </b>\r\n<br><br>abberation takes hold \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2258,'<p>i was walking down bank street tonight.  before i left i smoked a few pipes, and was rather high. i was walking and giggling to myself, and felt the giggle in my whole body.  but i was so high that i got distracted from giggling, forgot what was happening, and suddenly half vomited - a dry heave. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i felt the acid creep up my throat, and half the smell of vomit followed it to whisper hints of barf to my nose. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> i was shocked.i had to stop and collect myself, because nothing like that has ever happened to me before. it was kind of like how in hitchhiker\'s guide to the galaxy you can achieve flight by throwing yourself at the ground and <i>missing</i>; the key is distraction, to forget.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but the outcome of my situation didn\'t measure up to flight.  maybe for the pasta i ate for dinner, but not for me.  it totally threw me off my pace - i was gun shy of giddiness - and i was more cautious about interior humor until i got to a pub to meet a few friends.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2259,' HOW TO TURN SIX $ INTO SIX THOUSAND: \r\n\r\nREADING THIS COULD CHANGE YOUR LIFE! IT DOES WORK! \r\n\r\nI found this on a bulletin board and decided to try it. A little while back, I was browsing through \r\nnewsgroups, just like you are now, and came across an article similar to this that said you could make thousands of $ within weeks with only an initial investment of $6.00! \r\nSo I thought, \"Yeah right, this must be a scam\", but like most of us, I was curious, so I kept reading. \r\n\r\nAnyway, it said that you send $1.00 to each of the 6 names and address stated in the article. You then place your own name and address in the bottom of the list at #6, and post the article in at least 200 newsgroups, \r\n(there are thousands). No catch, that was it. So after thinking it over, and talking to a few people first, I thought about trying it. I figured \"what have I got to lose except 6 stamps and $6.00, right?\" Then I invested the measly $6.00. Well GUESS WHAT!?... within 7 days, I started getting money in the mail! I was shocked! I figured it would end soon, but the money just kept coming in. In my first week, I made about Â£25.00. By the end of the second week I had made a total of over $1,000.00! In the third week I had over $10,000.00 and it\'s still growing. This is now my fourth week and I have made a total of just over $42,000.00 and it\'s still coming in rapidly. It\'s certainly worth $6.00, and 6 stamps, I have spent more than that on the lottery!! \r\n\r\nLet me tell you how this works and most importantly, WHY it works... Also, make sure you print a copy of this article NOW, so you can get the information off of it as you need it. I promise you that if you follow the directions exactly, that you will start making more money than you thought possible by doing something so easy! \r\nSuggestion: Read this entire message carefully!(print it out or download it.) Follow the simple \r\ndirections and watch the money come in! \r\nIt\'s easy. It\'s legal. And, your investment is only $6.00 (Plus : postage) \r\nIMPORTANT: This is not a rip-off; it is not indecent; it is not illegal; and it is 99% no risk - \r\nit really works! \r\nIf all of the following instructions are adhered to, you will receive extraordinary dividends. \r\nPLEASE NOTE: Please follow these directions EXACTLY, and $50,000 or more can be : yours in 20 to 60 days.\r\nThis program remains successful because of the honesty and integrity of the participants. Please continue its success by carefully adhering to the instructions. \r\nYou will now become part of the Mail Order business. In this business your product is not solid and tangible, it\'s a service. You are in the business of developing Mailing Lists. Many large corporations are happy to pay big bucks for quality lists. However, the money made from the mailing lists is secondary to the income which is made from people like you and me asking to be included in that list. \r\n\r\nHere are the 4 easy steps to success: \r\n\r\nSTEP 1: Get 6 separate pieces of paper and write the following on each piece of paper \"PLEASE PUT ME \r\nON YOUR MAILING LIST.\" Now get 6 $1.00  and place ONE inside EACH of the 6 pieces of paper so the coins will not be seen through the envelope (to prevent thievery). \r\nNext, place one paper in each of the 6 envelopes and seal them. You should now have 6 sealed envelopes, each with a piece of paper stating the above phrase, your name and address, and a $1.00 coin. \r\nWhat you are doing is creating a service. THIS IS ABSOLUTELY LEGAL! You are requesting a legitimate service and you are paying for it! Like most of us I was a little skeptical and a little worried about the legal aspects of it all. So I checked it out with the Post Office and they confirmed that it is indeed legal. Mail the 6 envelopes to the following addresses: \r\n\r\n#1) Sue Brown\r\n16 Cutlers Squear\r\nLondon, England\r\nE14 3RL.\r\n\r\n#2) Garr Jones \r\n1405 New Rodger Rd. Apt.BB8\r\nBristol, PA 19007\r\n\r\n#3) David Lee \r\n101 Russel St. Apt. \r\nWest Lafayette, IN 47906 \r\n\r\n#4) Dr Nokronenberg\r\n15 Plumpton Close\r\nLuton Beds,\r\nEngland\r\nLU2 8JU. \r\n\r\n#5) Charlie Dodd\r\n47 Gilderdale\r\nLuton Beds,\r\nEngland\r\nLU4 9NA.\r\n\r\n#6)Devjit singh\r\nA-2493,\r\nnetaji nagar,\r\nNew delhi\r\nIndia \r\n\r\nSTEP 2: Now take the #1 name off the list that you see above, move the other names up (6 becomes 5, 5 \r\nbecomes 4, etc...) and add YOUR name as number 6 on the list. \r\n\r\nSTEP 3: Change anything you need to, but try to keep this article as close to original as possible. \r\nNow, post your amended article to at least 200 newsgroups. (I think there are close to 24,000 groups) All you need is 200, but remember, the more you post, the more money you make! You won\'t get very much unless you post like crazy. :) \r\nThis is perfectly legal! If you have any doubts, refer to Title 18 : Sec. 1302 & 1341 of the Postal lottery laws. Keep a copy of these steps for yourself and, whenever you need money, you can use it again, and again. \r\n\r\n*PLEASE REMEMBER* that this program remains successful because of the honesty and integrity of the participants and by their carefully adhering to the directions. Look at it this way. If you are of integrity, the program will continue and the money that so many others have received will come your \r\nway. \r\n\r\n*NOTE: You may want to retain every name and address sent to you, either on a computer or hard copy and keep the notes people send you. This VERIFIES that you are truly providing a service.\r\n(Also, it might be a good idea to wrap the 1  dark paper to reduce the risk of mail theft.) \r\n\r\nSo, as each post is downloaded and the directions carefully followed, six members will be reimbursed for their participation as a List Developer with one pound each. Your name will move up the list geometrically so that when your name reaches the #1 position you will be receiving thousands of Pounds in CASH!!! What an opportunity for only $6.00 ($1.00 for each of the first six people listed above) Send it now, add your own name to the list and you\'re in business! \r\n\r\n---DIRECTIONS ----- FOR HOW TO POST TO NEWSGROUPS------------ \r\n\r\nStep 1) You do not need to re-type this entire letter to do your own posting. Simply put your cursor at the beginning of this letter and drag your cursor to the bottom of this document, and select \'copy\' from the edit \r\nmenu. This will copy the entire letter into the computer\'s memory. \r\n\r\nStep 2) Open a blank \'notepad\' file and place your cursor at the top of the blank page. From the \'edit\' menu select \'paste\'. This will paste a copy of the letter into notepad so that you can add your name to the list. \r\n\r\nStep 3) Save your new notepad file as a .txt file. If you want to do your postings in different settings, you\'ll always have this file to go back to. \r\n\r\nStep 4) Use Netscape or Internet explorer and try searching for various newsgroups (on-line forums, message boards, chat sites, discussions.) \r\n\r\nStep 5) Visit these message boards and post this article as a new message by highlighting the text of this letter and selecting paste from the edit menu. Fill in the Subject, this will be the header that everyone sees as they scroll through the list of postings in a particular group, click the post message button. You\'re done with your first one! \r\n\r\nCongratulations...THAT\'S IT! All you have to do is jump to different newsgroups and post away, after you \r\nget the hang of it, it will take about 30 seconds for each newsgroup! **REMEMBER, THE MORE NEWSGROUPS YOU POST IN, THE MORE MONEY YOU WILL MAKE! BUT YOU HAVE TO POST A MINIMUM OF 200** That\'s it! You will begin receiving money from around the world within \r\ndays! \r\nYou may eventually want to rent a P.O.Box due to the large amount of mail you will receive. If you wish to stay anonymous, you can invent a name to use, as long as the postman will deliver it. **JUST MAKE SURE ALL THE ADDRESSES ARE CORRECT.** \r\n\r\nNow, each of the 5 persons who just sent me $1.00 make the MINIMUM 200 postings, each with my name at #5 and only 5 persons respond to each of the original 5, that is another $25.00 for me, now those 25 each \r\nmake 200 MINIMUM posts with my name at #4 and only 5 replies each, I will bring in an additional $125.00! Now, those 125 persons turn around and post the MINIMUM 200 with my name at #3 and only receive 5 replies each, I will make an additional $625.00! OK, now here is the fun part, each of those 625 persons post a MINIMUM 200 letters with my name at #2 and they each only receive 5 replies, that just made me $3,125.00!!! Those 3,125 persons will all deliver this message to 200 newsgroups with my name at #1 and if still 5 persons per 200 newsgroups react I will receive Â£15,625,00! With an original investment of only \r\nÂ£6.00! \r\nAMAZING! When your name is no longer on the list, you just take the latest posting in the newsgroups, and send out another $6.00 to names on the list, putting your name at number 6 again. And start posting again. \r\nThe thing to remember is do you realize that thousands of people all over the world are joining the internet and reading these articles everyday?, JUST LIKE YOU are now!! So, can you afford $6.00 and see if it really works?? I think so... \r\nPeople have said, \"what if the plan is played out and no one sends you the money? So what! What are the chances of that happening when there are tons of new honest users and new honest people who are joining the internet and newsgroups everyday and are willing to give it a try? Estimates are at 20,000 to 50,000 new users, every day, with thousands of those joining the actual internet. \r\n\r\nRemember, play FAIRLY and HONESTLY and this will really work \r\n \r\n '),(2260,'we had the first snow of the season last night. it fell in flakes that were so little they made sky sugar. when i stood out in the snowfall, arms outstretched, face upturned and staring into the immense whiteness, when i quietly let the snowflakes slide their way over my body, i swear i was receiving a blessing. a benediction. \r\n</P><P></P><P> \r\nthe first snowflakes were not what i expected. there were no edges of ice slicing into my skin. they were neither hard nor sharp. they felt more like the feathers of a dove, like something fragile and holy. and oh each snowflake was so small. so small. yet, when trillions of them joined together, they transformed. they became a thing more powerful than any on earth. they engulfed the world in their smallness, this world that is so much bigger and stronger than them. \r\n</P><P></P><P> \r\ni keep picturing snowflakes as tiny tiny white creatures, floating through the air, their translucent parachutes billowing out behind them. and i keep thinking how once they reach the ground, they find their brothers and sisters, their friends and lovers, patiently  waiting. and how all these miniature snow creatures gather close and hold hands, how together they form themselves into this crystalline structure with an architecture so intricate it is impossible to see with the human eye.\r\n</P><P></P><P> \r\nthough it was friday night, there was no one else out. with everyone and everything inside, the snow fell completely undisturbed. there was not a single footprint, tire track or pawprint, on the surface of the entire neighbourhood. just pure unending white. i don\'t think i\'ve ever seen anything like it before. i stood there for a long long time, in the untouched silence. i stared and stared until the very texture of time itself changed. \r\n</P><P></P><P> \r\nas i watched the snow something inside me lifted. i believe that if i had raised myself on my toes last night, the wind would have picked me up, would have carried me along as effortlessly as it did the tiny snow creatures.'),(2261,'	tuesday, surgery. the destruction of a tumour hiding in my mother\'s chest. i don\'t know, don\'t care to know the colour of a tumour. but i make a hypothesis. it is black. all day at work, i stared at my knuckles. i drew shapes of the\r\njewel bright fish on the paintings in the hospital waiting room on the flow charts taped to my cubicle. i smiled pleasantly at coworkers, narrowed my wet eyes. i attended meetings, spoke when spoken to. people were kind, i tried to hold back the vomit. i cut out a picture of a killer with a knife stuck in his eyeball. i staggered among the commuters on the way home. my knee remembered an old injury, i had to start limping. the front door was unlocked. i got lost in our kitchen, thought i saw her shadow under the potted plants.\r\n</p><p>\r\n	9pm, the intensive care unit. my mother\'s lips are white. when she licks them, they get whiter. with her eyes fastened shut, one arm bent at an awkward angle, her hands hidden, she looks like venus. a drum filled with red liquid, too thin to be blood, too real to be cranberry juice, is connected to her by two translucent plastic straws. i can\'t see to what part of her body. she is under a blanket with three red circle stains joined into the shape of a bloody snowman on the corner nearest me. i can\'t tell if the pipes, bent and twisted into the world\'s smallest roller coaster, are taking the red liquid out of her body, or pouring it in. my mother\'s body looks like an empty envelope. this frightens me. i am no longer certain if the envelope is indeed empty, or just looks it. i try to wake her. she opens her eyes, looks right through me. then she asks me my name. i try to smile, and stroke the air above her veiny, needle stabbed left hand. the nurse warned us against touching skin to skin.\r\n</p><p>\r\n	walking to the car, on the way home, i know my mother is okay. because there is only stillness inside my ears. if something were to happen to her, even in my absence, i would *know*. i would sense that something had gone wrong without anyone telling me. but do not think this is a matter of faith. my faith is a small dying animal; if i were to believe in God, i would have to believe in magic. this is not a question of religion. or superstition. this is a Fact. the very instant a mother dies, her daughter is knocked off her feet, hits the gravel backwards, even if they are on opposite ends of the country. i am still standing.'),(2262,'there are times i am grateful for my poor vision.  i watch blurry rain drops with a strange hunger; dream of capturing a single imperfect droplet.  perhaps i could freeze my hands into a safety-cold nest for the raindrop, keep it with me always.  i would hold it close to my chest, a goodluck charm from the sky.   i try sinking my palms deep into the ice of the freezer in my mother\'s basement, hold onto the glassy sides for as long as i can, then run outside and cup them into a loose boat in mid air.<br><br>\r\n \r\nthe raindrops splash onto the wet pavement as if they were coins, the plump oval ripples swimming away from the centre like tiny silver fish.  they jump in a shower of spray in the mini waterfalls at the corners of street curbs.<br><br>\r\n \r\non the giant windshield of the bus, the droplets gather at the edges away from the insect-leg sweep of the wipers, and change colour with the traffic lights: red, green, yellow sparkles.  they are bigger than glitter but smaller than the miniature christmas lights flashing on and off in the bare december trees.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwithout my glasses, it looks like fireworks have settled in the branches. the rain polishes all the bits of coloured glass from broken beer bottles until they shine.   from a distance, the individual drops coalesce into a fine thin veil draped over the surface of everything, so that suddenly, suburbia looks both mysterious and pretty. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni start counting the number of drops that fall onto the lawn of our house.  i\'m not stupid.  i know they do not, in any way at all, determine the number of seconds left for my mother.  but she is dying, and other than making her favourite vegetable stir fry and rubbing her shivery hands, other than placing a cool rain wet cloth on her feverish forehead, there is nothing else i can do.<br><br>'),(2263,'From his window, Nikolai watched the stars fall out of the sky last night.  He stepped outside and began picking them up and  stuffing them in his pockets, but soon they were full to bursting.  It was a frigid night but he didn\'t want to give up and go back inside.  He dumped the first batch of stars in a pile by the stairs leading up to his apartment and went back for more.  It was late and nobody was around.  Nik found himself scooping up great quantities of glistening stars, still warm from passing through the atmosphere, and dropping them down his shirt for warmth.  He held the stars under his armpits and a soothing radiation seemed to penetrate his skin until he was warm from the inside out.  This renewed Nik.  \r\n<BR><BR>He piled the stars higher and higher by the staircase until the glowing mound lit his apartment building with a shimmering, beautiful light.  As he cleared the stars from the area, he went further, collecting stars from down the block and around the corner, sweeping the streets of celestial lights and bringing them back to his building.  The light was intense now.  Constant.  Like daylight.  But Nikolai didn\'t want to stop.  The stars had stopped falling over his block, but he could see them falling elsewhere over the city.  He fixed his eyes on a group of stars in the distance, they shook in their celestial moorings, dislodged, and wafted to earth like fairy dust.  Nikolai set out for them, intent and concentrating.  <BR><BR>He hiked through the desolate city, past the shuttered vegetable stands and package stores, past electronics repair shops and tiny insurance companies.  He turned down the countless streets.  Then finally he came upon an alley where the stars had collected.  It shone and shimmered as if God Himself resided among the discarded cardboard boxes and tattered newspapers.  Stars shone from the metal fire escapes and the lids of black metal dumpsters.  Nikolai found a canvas bag tucked between the dumpster and a wall and he went about the alley collecting his divine treasure.  He stuffed his bag, his pockets and dumped the stars down his shirt.  He took handfulls of the stuff and rubbed it into his hair until he too shone like something otherworldy, something holy.  When he had all he could carry he made his way back to his apartment.  <BR><BR>The morning light was just begining to crack the sky open and Nikolai wanted to get home before the city came to life and the vendors and commuters would catch him with his treasure.  He stepped up the pace and got home just as the sun crested the top of the buildings and filled his block with golden morning.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe pile beside Nikolai\'s house had gone black, like thousands of dead ants heaped upon one another: dead, motionless shells of life.  The glowing treasure in his bag was merely a conglomeration of the same.  His hands were covered in black coal-like smudges.  Nikolai stopped where he was, his feet as heavy as anvils.  The cold air hit him then, whipping down the street with merciless cruelty, surounding him like a whip, like a whirlpool.'),(2264,'call night hunter<br>\r\ni will listen at my window<br>\r\ncrawling out of my star wars<br>\r\nblanket and pajamas<br><br>\r\n\r\nout the window<br>\r\ninto the the dark world<br>\r\ntrees and spirits<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe will move <br>\r\nsilently<br>\r\nnight hunter<br>\r\ncall<br>\r\n'),(2265,'<p>Vince didnâ€™t know it was art, he just cleaned the room like he cleaned the other rooms, doinâ€™ his job as he would say later over and over, especially to the museum director. Staff scrambled to recreate the installation from photographs, tried to keep it <i>sub rosa</i> but it got out anyway of course. The ironic black-glasses-decaf-latte sipping crowd crooned -- a comment on the nature of art, how ironic, like ha ha ha. Itâ€™s art when each piece of trash is placed on the floor by an air quote artist but itâ€™s trash in the end when the janitor cleans the room? What the fuck? They say. But they will die young and unenlightened, that is our consolation. They listen to crap music and spend too much time drinking decaf lattes. Vince is having a beer after work with Dan, a letter carrier.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œSo you know, just doinâ€™ my job.â€ Vince begins to rotate his pint glass on the counter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWhoa. So like are you going to be fired?â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œNaw. Union. Itâ€™s a great job, I mean OK first location wise, I am like five minutes away. Out a bed in the morning, shower, one second it takes me to shave â€“ zing zing, there I am clocking in early. They like it you punch in a little early sometimes. Then thereâ€™s that whole union thing.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWhoa.â€ </p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWhoa what, you work for the government.â€ </p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œJust whoa. Some story. Fucking artists.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then Vince leans in a little bit, confession like. â€œOK, so weâ€™ve been pals for a while right?â€ Dan nods into his beer. â€œDonâ€™t tell no one but sometimes in the morning I shave my feet too.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYou got beautiful feet.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWhoa.â€</p>'),(2266,'she is a self-contained unit.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe is streamlined.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe moves her hips <i>just</i><br>\r\nright <br>\r\n<br>\r\n(she wears tight long skirts,<br>\r\nall straight and narrow<br>\r\n<br>\r\nand pulls her hair back shiny,<br>\r\nwith rubberbands).<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe walks with ferocious energy<br>\r\nand rockabilly twang.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe pounds the ground with sex.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe leaves holes.<br>\r\n'),(2267,'<p>Jury duty. It turns out that I have to go down to the courthouse. I always get called for criminal court. 850 Bryant Street, down by the jail. You have to go through a metal detector to enter, and the only cheap place to eat is McDonalds. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I arrive at 10:30, like I\'m supposed to, and I sit down and start reading the last chapter of The Fellowship of the Ring. At 11:15 they tell us we\'re lucky, we\'re getting an extra long lunch. But this isn\'t that lucky. What am I going to do for the next hour and a half? I go to McDonalds for an All American Meal, because dammit, I\'m an American. And I end up sitting outside the McDonalds on a cold cement bench in the January sun finishing the book. At 1:45 I go back inside and I\'ve just started on The Two Towers when they tell us we can go home, our service is complete for the next year. Waste of my time, but I\'m glad I don\'t have to sit around and try to convince a judge that I wont be able to pay my rent if they make me show up there everyday.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have to take the 27 Bryant bus home. It meets the 22 at 16th Street, where I\'ll transfer back to my neighborhood. I sit on a low ledge in the side of the building there at 7th and Harrison and keep reading. I can\'t pay attention to my story because a transit vagrant walks up and asks if I\'m waiting for the 27. I tell him yes. He wants to know if it\'ll go to Market and Duboce. I say no, you have to get off at Division then walk to Market... Or you can walk to Market here, and take any bus to Duboce. He misunderstands me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"What? Why would I take the bus if I wanted to walk? If I walked there I\'d already be there! I wouldn\'t need to take the bus!\" I can\'t figure out how to explain my directions, he obviously doesn\'t understand that this bus wont go to the exact intersection he wants. In fact, no bus goes to that EXACT intersection that I know of. He starts to berate me, to tell me that I don\'t listen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"You don\'t listen! You aren\'t too smart!\" he says over and over. \"I oughta teach you a lesson!\" he sneers. But I don\'t get too worried. I can tell by the BandAid on his cheek and the way he wont stand up straight and slurs his words that he\'s not a serious threat. I know I can outrun him if I have to, so I turn back to my book and wave him away. And as he keeps berating me and telling me I don\'t listen I turn back to my book and read about Merry and Pippin in the scary forest. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The bus comes much sooner than I expect for the 27, and we both get on. I sit down near the front and put my book away. The transit vagrant sees an off duty bus driver in the seat across from mine and demands to know if this bus goes to Market and Duboce. The driver tells him he should transfer to the 22 at 16th Street, and take that to Market and Church, one block away from his desired destination. The guy gets pissed! He doesn\'t want to go to Market and Church! He wants to go to Market and Duboce! The bus driver doesn\'t have any better luck than I in explaining how to get there, and the transit vagrant waggles his arm and points in my direction.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\"She LIED to me!!! She LIED to me! She said this bus went there!\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I make some mumbled remark and roll my eyes. Like yah, I get my ya-yas by giving incorrect directions to half drunken lunatics. That is my joy in life. I have so much free time that I give it to the world by offering poor directions to transients. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two stops later he gets off the bus. Nowhere near to his intended destination. For all I know he\'s out there right now asking people how to get the 14 Mission to Market and Duboce. I don\'t know what he needs to go there for anyway. There\'s nothing there but a Safeway, a Starbucks, and a Gap. Nothing for a transit vagrant with no cash and a gutful of unfriendly liquor.</p>\r\n'),(2268,'some part of my brain--a tiny sweet dangerously delicate part, small, very small, the size of a woman\'s nipple, perhaps--the part of my mind responsible for the human body\'s analogue of the sky\'s rainbows must have been switched off when my uncle first put his fingers between my legs. because my body is no longer capable of refracting physical sensation into bright bands of colour. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy uncle always adored my breasts. he would palm them, he would lick them, he would bite them, he would blow hot air over them, he would try to cup as much of them as he could in his hands and weigh them, admire their shape and size, and wonder aloud how it was possible for me to stand up without falling over. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni was thirteen the first time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nall the while, as he whispered into my ear what he would do to me this night, what he ached for, the intensity and strength of the urges my wicked body created inside him, all the while i kept picturing the curved knife in the kitchen in the drawer at the bottom of my grandmother\'s ancient iron cabinet. i could see the metal of the blade clearly in the dark, waving in front of my eyes, catching and releasing the light from the lantern above. i could feel the comfort of the heavy black handle inside my fists. i tried to imagine what it would be like to grab hold of first my right breast, and then my left, and cut them off. a couple of slashes of this huge sharp carving knife, just a simple movement of my wrists and my ribcage would be free of its burdens. i remember watching with longing as their little servant girl used it once a month to chop up all the cow and goat carcasses from the farm into chunks of red meat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nif i did it that morning, then that night he would come to me, as usual, quietly, tear away my blanket, unbutton my dress, and send his fingers searching in the darkness for breasts that no longer existed. he would come upon two raw bloodfilled holes gouged into my chest instead. what he would do then. how loud his howl would be. how hot his anger would burn. how\r\nquickly he would throw me away.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe used to burn the soles of my feet if i cried or cried out. he was careful only to bruise me in hidden places, the soft insides of my thighs, the shadowed hollow between my breasts. the places no-one else would ever see, let alone touch.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit was painful to walk. i taught my mind to strangle my tear glands until they asphyxiated, so that no tears could be born, let alone spill out onto my cheeks and displease him. any sound from my throat, no matter how small or unwilling, would propel the black lighter out of his pocket. silence became a bodyguard, a soleguard.\r\n</p><p>\r\neventually i realised, in a slow dull dead way, that there might be other consequences. but i didn\'t know anything. so i asked my best friend, who was older, riper, and had a boyfriend, how i would know. she promised me a period after \'it\' meant i was probably not pregnant. \r\n</p><p>\r\nin the bathroom two and a half weeks later i bent over and stared at my strange dark blood, utterly still, watching that single tear, which must have come from a tear gland that my mind had accidentally missed, splash near the stain on my panty. i stood there, hunched against the commode, barely aware of the dirt on the floor, and mouthed my first prayer, my first thanks, my hands pushing at those ugly despicable useless lumps. those things that started it all back then, and that end anything current, before it begins. those breasts that refuse to do anything but brood when they are caressed now, by someone i could love.'),(2269,'i\'m dying!  i\'m dying!  i\'m dying!<br>  \r\n<p>\r\ni\'m only in my mid twenties and yet daily, nightly, i feel this sense of my life ebb ebb ebbing away.  \r\n</p><br><p>\r\nwhen i was a little girl, about 10, I wrote \'i love life\'  on the bottom of a shelf in my closet.\r\n</p><br><p>\r\nwhat a great place to proclaim my love of being alive.  some write songs, some dance, some paint.  me, i write in dark little places that no one would ever think to look.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2270,'<p>I\'m such an old man.  The nephew came over today for a sleigh ride.  He had one of those long, red plastic toboggans that some factory somewhere pumps out by the thousands.  I though that he might feel cold sitting on the plastic; I looked around for something to line his sled.  I found, of all things, a burlap bag.  Now, this wasn\'t a burlap bag that I had inherited from my father or grandfather.  No, this was my burlap bag.  The nephew examined the images that were printed on the weathered-brown screen-cloth of the bag and decided that they were cool enough to sit upon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The next order of business was to find a bit of rope that was thin enough to fit through the eyeholes on the front of the sled, strong enough to withstand some pulling, and long enough to fashion into a harness for myself.  I would run, and he would give me better encouragement than any workout partner or training officer I had ever known.  I found the proper type and length of rope in the trunk of my Honda, along with a good variety of tools - strike two.  We were ready to go then, and we walked to our trail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Once we started moving, the nephew eyed the snow intently.  He discerned the target of his search half-hidden in a drift, and then ordered me to stop.  He pulled the perfect stick out, my little King Arthur, and then ordered me to proceed again.  Now we would have a good ride.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the summer I pulled him in a red wagon.  He let a stick, held loosely in his grasp, trail along behind us.  Sanding itself on the sidewalk\'s fine aggregate, the stick left a faint trail of itself behind us.  Neighborhood fences were all fair game; the clinckity-clack of wooden pickets or the clinkity-cling of iron fencing accompanied our tour.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wish that there were a way to move directly from uncle to grandfather, without the intermediate step of fatherhood.  Well, sometimes I wish for that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Movement can be a very reassuring thing - that sensation of being able to be borne by something or someone.  My mother spoke of using the family car to settle me when I was a babe.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I too remember as a child, blond like the nephew, holding a stick out into the water.  I was in a boat, moving, and a little wake in the water spread out behind me - marking for a while where I had been.  And then watching as the delicate landscape disappeared back into itself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Today was his day though; his wake was in the snow.  And I was perfectly content to see through his eyes - strike three.  I\'m glad I\'m getting older.</p>\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n \r\n'),(2271,'<p>lindsay is asking me why i was asleep, and i say, \"because that\'s what i do when i\'m not awake.\"  probably not the right answer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she has the right to complain, i don\'t deny.  after years of distant antagonism, lindsay has come back from the downtown dorm rooms and wants to go to lunch.  instead she\'s waiting for me, spending time with the bus station weirdos, and i\'m laying in bed full of fuzzy-headed apathy.  i answer in grunts, but my thoughts are winding and long, horrible run-on sentences.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when we were high school sweethearts, i started having stomach pains.  my mother found me in the bathroom one day, curled up in a fetal position, trying to suck the cold from the white tile floor.  i spent the rest of the day in the emergency ward at UH, getting poked and prodded.  four orderlies were assigned to hold me down while the sedatives took effect.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\'t remember much else from the hospital, but one thing i know:  that day was the first time i missed a date with lindsay.  it was the beginning of a pattern of operations, unplanned hospital stays, and disappointments.  teenage life was put on hold while my gut churned and ripped itself to shreds.  lindsay was understanding, but deep down she wanted a normal boy.  she knew that crippled and bed-ridden lovers were things for decrepit old women to worry about.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the last time lindsay came to visit me, my stomach was exposed, and she saw the lines of stitches criss-crossing my skin.  blue veins spider-webbed beneath the pale surface, and i think that was the breaking point.  i didn\'t see her again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now she\'s back, complaining into my ear about the no-smoking signs in the bus terminal.  i listen to her list of complaints, stifling a yawn, and then pause for a moment.  i tell her that if smoking is what she really wants to do, she should light up anyways.  \"after all, who\'s it going to hurt?\"  then i hang up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the next day i sat down with my grandfather, two hunched up old bodies on the sofa.  i asked him what women really want in a man, and he said, \"one with removable teeth.\"  i don\'t know if he was just being vain, but i cancelled my dentist appointment just in case.</p>'),(2272,'<p>Hashbrowns and veggiee dogs help me to greet the morning, instead of wasting away doing the backstroke in the swimming pool of sweat that my bed has turned into. The radio is croning away on the CBC, as I read the paper and come across an article about an all girl metal band that I promise myself to check out. Reasoning split half between a strong desire to hear some heavy music and a severe weakness for the imagery of women covered in tatoos and decked out in leather and harley gear. Reading on one of the band members alludes to this fact that if people don\'t like the music they often just stare - the band <a href=\"http://www.nowtoronto.com/issues/2002-01-17/cover_story.html\">views</a> it as a win/win situation. This however was not the real gem of my morning, it was a little later in the paper. I often don\'t pay much attention to horoscopes in a newspaper today was different... </p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m down with astrological signs related to certain character traits because in my experience I have seen alot of examples of these generalizations. I have to say though that I put more stock in people having their charts done then any of this pop astrology in newpapers. Today though, as I look up Gemini due to a lack of any articles of interest I lay my eyes and something that reasonates with me. There is mention of a *koan* a paradoxical riddle that is used to demonstrate the inadequacy of logical reasoning and to promote enlightenment. Being a sucker for paradox and knowing all too well that logical reasoning has landed me in my current situation of feeling like I\'ve made the *right* decisions, but have been left feeling unimpressed with where it has lead my life, I read on. </p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>1. Think with your heart and feel with your head. </p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>2. Thou shalt not bore God.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>3. Whom can you ask what you came to make happen in this world?</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The first one reminds me of some shortcomings in relationships and suggest to me a way of thinking that may allow me to pursue what will be fulfilling, instead of making more *right* decisions that are supported by conventional wisdom.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Not boring God has me in stitches, the massive amounts of drama in my life have suddenly been justified. I have a love/hate relationship with drama in my life. A lot of it seems to me to be rooted in adolscent behaviours that even the elderly still grapple with. The notion of someone watching all this be it god or someone else makes me feel a little beter and dealing with it not so tedious, I\'ll just remind myself it will make the next episode great viewing for whomever is watching. Life lived as a sitcom all in the name of divine ratings, angels and demons tuning into peoples lives like we switch channels. God as voyeur. This seems both scared and profane to me.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The answer to the third seem clearly to be myself and with that I decide to go back to the pool in my bed and do a few laps until I have to meet out of town friends this afternoon. Inspiring indeed - time to nap.</p><br>'),(2273,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2273_1.pjpeg\"><br><br>\r\nThis is my hometown.<br> \r\nSure, I saw it, first<br> \r\nfrom a naive cherished<br>\r\nimage; its as raw as the<br>\r\nrest of the city to my<br> \r\nmore mature eyes now.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTo a stranger, its all<br>\r\nsimilitudes. The dingy-dank<br>\r\nof my downtown Broadway<br>\r\ncould, to him, lead right<br>\r\nup the silver strip of <br>\r\n5th Avenue.<br><br>\r\n\r\nKudos for kindred spirits<br>\r\nand real images; the things<br>\r\nof dreams and the hearts<br>\r\nof lives lived out.<br>'),(2274,'<p>\r\ndear craig david.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat every turn i hear your music. in stores, on the radio, on tv, at peoples houses. in bars. oh god. the list goes on. you make me sick. you really do. you are so bling and yet you are only nineteen years old. you arent barry white dude and you arent gods gift to women, yet you have penetrated the canadian music charts and have made my life that much more full of violent thought. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had to leave a house party once because the people who were hosting apparently lost all their self respect and put your cd on. the apartment was straight out of your ikea style videos. ugh you make me sick. they made me temporarilly sick, but once rational thought moved back in it was decided that their once good taste in music had been put on my probation list.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni find it really bizarre to that your song seven days is seemingly based on the bibles creation story. god you\'re pathetic. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyours truely. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nchiamattt\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\np.s go to hell!\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2275,'<p>It snowed last night.  James and I stood outside, scarves wrapped around our necks grilling salmon with fennel, and eggplant sliced lengthwise and placed close to the flame so it would become nice and charred.  \r\n\r\n<p>The snow was coming down like confectioners sugar and melted on contact with the hot grill cover.  Mrs. Skylark and Mrs. James were both inside with big goblets of red wine, talking and listening to Ella Fitzgerald\'s greatest.  James and I amuzed ourselves by melting snowballs on the top of the grill.  At one point I took of the cover and leaned in close to inspect the fish.  It was doing nicely and would have to be turned in a moment or two.  I flipped the eggplant first.\r\n\r\n<p>James hit me with a snowball then.  My glasses went flying into the white powder.  I reached for them, got them with a hunk of show and pelted him good in the shoulder. We fought viciously for awhile, beaning each other with snowballs and running around the yard, half drunk on the Plumb Sake Martinis I\'d made earlier, and hooting like kids.  When we finally came back to the grill, which I\'d left uncovered, the salmon was ready to turn.  I flipped it using both the spatula and the tongs and we peeled of the skin with our fingers.  I spread the fennel (which I had previously braised with olive oil, balsamic vinegar, Itallian parsley and black pepper) on top of the fish, so that the flavors would permeate.\r\n\r\n<p>As it cooked we built a little snowman audience, threw more snowballs at the wall, watched them stick and fall.  We had brought grilling beers outside and we drank them as we stayed close to the fire.  \r\n\r\n<p>When we went back in to our wives, snow-soaked, bearing the cooked fish, we felt like primitive hunters returning from the kill, bearing a mighty prize for our brides.  \r\n\r\nThere was more wine, beer and finally coffee and dessert.  A good night.  '),(2276,'<P>This was a dream. moving as fast as an HK movie. Boy B and the girl are making a get away, running fast, from some trouble. coming towards Boy A who is waiting by the get away car. the girls seems injured, doesn\'t walk as fast as boy b. boy b stops kicks and punches the girl then runs away.  boy a comes to the aid of the girl, she tells him to go kick boy bâ€™s ass. he thinks she is dying. He runs to a nearby phone. the phone is dead. He runs madly around, finds a stranger, turns out to be boy bâ€™s older brother. FIGHT. boy a runs back with a car and saves girl.boy a and the girl hide in some other side town, a motel.  boy a has been leaving behind pennies with listening devices at boy bâ€™s house. boy b is smart and find them daily. during a recon mission to boy bâ€™s house boy a gets \r\ncaught. FIGHT. he runs awaybut leaves behind matches from the place they are staying at. A Night and a Day later boy a is seeking help, calls a friend, \"they could come get you at any time\" friend says. a hunch tells boy a to run home. boy b and bro go get the girl, they snoop around boy aâ€™s motel complex. The girl is anxious she thinks she hears people moving about the front door, she glimpses at shadows, she runs to  the back door, nothing, then from above a hand grabs her throat. boy a is running, he spots the car of boy b in the parking lot. He runs across the street, into some hood, he has hidden a gun in someone elseâ€™s apartment. He walks in in a state of madness, fury, yelling, making noise, he finds his hidden gun, the old lady who lives in this apartment is freaking out. Boy a prepares himself, he walks out, right into a pistol by his head, old man holding it, he asks for help, he joins him. A second old man comes around the corner and  fires a shot towards boy a thinking he was an intruder at the old ladyâ€™s apartment. The shot alerts boy b and his older brother who are running across the street. they start making a fast get away from the scene. They are running behind a black metal fence, boy a is too far to make a good shot, the girl can\'t hear him telling her to get out of the way, to duck, they get in a car, boy a and the old men are shooting madly, shouting like mental patients. boy bâ€™s car car crashes into something down the street and it explodes. The girl is done for. One of the old men tells boy a he will be ok, boy a looks him straight in the eye and shoots him, boy a in a rage shoots about the street neighborhood causing explosions at the store down the street, he disappears into the flames.</P>'),(2277,'<P>the sun rises to your back at yeti\'s house.  it lights the tops of the houses starting at the tops of the hills and working its way down.  the cars noise up at about 6, starting with the busses.  yoko and turbo kitty start about 6:30.  by 9 on sunday morning, yeti is still asleep and yoko has been perched atop the couch positioned to watch the intersection of guererro and 18th for quite some time.  she is not missing a thing.  i tried up christmas tree also overlooks the intersection giving the inside of the apartment a partched desert feel.  though it used to be a pine, now it is a juniper tree or some other dry desert sort.  i would even almost go as far as to say it could be a sort of cactus, not one like i have ever seen, but something of the family.  dark green, spiney, forbidden to touch.  i was thinking about heaving it out the window onto 18th in the wee hours of the morning, like 445 AM.  on the couch is an ampex monitor and computer.  it looks like a one piece item.  seth asked if i wanted any computers when i woke up.  not really but i would like to see them on.  what was an ampex computer\'s operating system like?  there is a huge HP at my foot that yeti said seth used to use to heat his apartment.  there are some old apple floppies that predate the first computer i ever had, an apple II which is in my dad\'s attic still, maybe.  maybe he dumped it out without telling me.  my god father bought it for me and my brother scott.  my dad was never a geek in the slightest.  he did not recognise the inherent *coolness* of computers until pentium came out and then on the sales floor of a computer outlet in dallas he said, these things are really getting fast!  at that point my only experience with computers came from an apple II.  in highschool we had a fleet of them.  elementary schools across texas still have fleets of them.  yeti is still asleep and i am antsy for some food in my stomach.   i stole a partial mug of seth\'s coffee without asking.  i do that, take without asking.  asking most often seems like a formality to me which the world could do without.  it is the rude barbarian in me.  i always try to ask for a few moments before i take.  \'i should ask\' i think to myself as i poor the coffee and begin to sip it while moving back to the couch.  there is a NeXT on the floor that i would like to see running.  i have heard allot about the NeXT os but i have never seen one running or even a screen shot.  i assume it looks like a standart xwindow system.  im probably wrong.  after all that would not be innovation.  maybe the innovation was not in the interface.  i will have to get seth to fire it up some day.  (wake up yeti.) i think i might just go home.  i am slated to hang with goat today though.  so if i did go home i would be right back up here.  life suckes for the santa cruzer.  i considered the other day that with all the work i am doing on specific types of web sites, libraries, i will be falling into the nitche of library web site management.  i wonder if any of those library jobs will come open in san francisco anytime soon.  i wonder where i would move to out here.  maybe oakland.  if i had to live in sf i think i would hide from the traffic.  i enjoy the country living of santa cruz.  the lack of action forces me to think about things other than being annoyed by noise and congestion.  oh that is not right.  i dont know what it leads me to.  i guess it leads me to not write on the web but once a month and when i do to not include any paragraph breaks.'),(2278,'My expectations for this film were lowered by the Bruckheimer connection, but evened out by Ridley Scott, who can do good work. I read the book, which brings some comparisons. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThis film walks a very fine line between a \"go see it\" and an \"eh.\" Anyone who tells you \"it\'s a renter\" is lying, because it wouldn\'t be good at home. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThis movie is 75% action with most every minute involving somebody being killed. It\'s engaging because it is loud and fast and semi-realistic. You will walk out thinking: Wow, that was a fucked up situation. You will not be left with much more, as you don\'t really get to explore the characters (though I\'m sure you will \"know\" the Tom Sizemore character, since he plays the same dude in every flick) and there is no plot beyond guys go in and things go wrong. War ensues.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nA few misses:<BR>\r\n-Ewan Mcgregor was nothing short of an embarrassment. I\'m a fan of his, but he can\'t speak like an American to save his life.<BR>\r\n-the writing that drove the above point home; when Ewan says \"Whatta bout a cuppa JOE\". <BR>\r\n-the writing to make a point. When they keep hitting us on the head with \"You know, we never leave a man behind.\" Or \"Remember, nobody gets left behind!\" Thanks for being my third grade teacher.<BR>\r\n-the direction and the writing. When Sam Shepard <i>yanks</i> off the sunglasses to tell the warlord, \"god dammit this is genocide!\"<BR>\r\n-the warlord guy. this aspect of the film reminded me of that Bond film from the 70\'s where Roger Moore hits Harlem.<BR>\r\n-slow motion. \"Noooooooo!\" It wasn\'t as bad as some films, but I dunno, after watching 10 guys fly sideways after RPG hits, why continue the slow motion?<BR>\r\n-slow motion. There are a few shots of the innocent people caught in the fight. Heavy handed slow motion of them turning their faces with sad looks. Visa ad or Nat Geo special.<BR>\r\n-50 shots of Sam Shepard hearing the bad updates, shaking his head, looking tired.<BR>\r\n-Josh Hartnett. He is Brad Pitt 2.0. Some acting ability, but he can\'t carry a film. <BR>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIn terms of book to movie comparisons, what is most disturbing is that the film tries to be very documentary-like, and succeeds. It is for the most part very accurate. The problem is when they leave those extra touches out that make war that much harsher. The scene where they are trying to find the vein is intense, but the book had many more moments like that. They showed the mob lifting one of the bodies up in the air from the black hawk, but they cut the famous CNN scene of them dragging his body through the streets. Would that have been harsh? Sure, but that is what happened. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFinally in the book they pointed out that they were killing children, boys with guns. In the film they have one scene at the end when one guy resists shooting a woman with a gun. After an entire night of mayhem, this moment was stupid.\r\n<BR> <BR>\r\nTo be realistic, there should have been scenes of children getting killed. Would that have been harsh? Sure, but that is what happened. I see little point having one of the most violent movies ever and then watering down that last 10%.\r\n\r\n'),(2279,'<P>i will now admit that when my father was in hospital for an investigation into his erratic heartbeats, i had the insane urge to have sex with my boyfriend. and i was furious. not at him. not at my father. not at anyone really. or well, at everyone. damn my father for not taking care of himself, for being weak. damn me for being vulnerable, for caring abot such a jerk. damn my boyfriend for all that he lacked. i was so crazymad. it was this all consuming furiousness that i had never been gripped by in my entire life, this blindeyed rage that balled my hands into ridiculous pink fists. it made me want to bite my boyfriend\'s lips until they bled. and maybe i would lick the blood, maybe i would just watch. i started swearing at my bed, at the door, at my boyfriend, at my sisters. i ripped up paper, but it wasn\'t enough. i needed to tear clothes, break things. i needed my boy to fuck me. and it didn\'t make sense, and i was too engulfed to be ashamed, and he was whispering explanations into my ear, telling me it was okay, when it wasn\'t, it was petty damn the opposite of okay. and maybe, now, a year or so later, i understand what happened that stupid dark rainy afternoon, but i still can\'t speak of it to anyone, least of all him. i can spew about it in this private-public place, where i\'m a stranger. you don\'t know me. i don\'t know you. perhaps by telling you all this, by screaming as i type, i can block out the emergency room, the sound of my lover\'s voice. </P>'),(2280,'perhaps you have fallen asleep. i can almost see you, curled up on your bed like a stray eyelash, your pale body shining like milk. your face is soft, wide, dreamy. i\'ve watched you sleep, i\'ve watched the way your palms open, like calla lillies. i\'ve resisted the urge to stroke the soles of your feet, to trail my hands across your arms and legs, the rise and fall of your chest. you sleep deeply, gorgeously, with great attention, as if rest was a masterpiece you could create brushstroke by brushstroke on the tissue paper canvases of your eyelids.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni try to comfort myself with the memories of our january nights, where the wind pulled the snow close around us, like a shawl, and i watched you paint with wisps of sleep. i remember the night air shone blue in the moonlight outside our window. ottawa was glittering with snowflakes. we skated on our way to buy groceries, the sidewalks long narrow make-shift ice rinks. back then i could watch you sleep without having to close my eyes, the way i must do now. the tears spill unbidden, crackling spines of cactii.'),(2281,'What i hate about relationships is that one person always, always needs the other person more. Over the course of the relationship, this may change. at the start, the boy worships the girl. the girl enjoys this, blossoms in the light he gives her. but she would be fine without it too. <br><br>then in the middle of the relationship, in the rich warm body of it, she starts to care, and care deeply, recklessly. he? his mind wanders, just a little. it is not that he has stopped loving her. it is just that he loves her differently now. need, more than desire, is on a sliding spectrum. and each member of the couple is always at a different level. the difference varies, the positions switch. but the space between the two is always there.\r\n<br><BR>Damn him for needing me less. damn me for aching for him like this. damn the world. and yet, the slow revolution of the earth continues beneath my cracked winter boots.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2283,'You\'re hundreds of miles away now, or so it seems.  It\'s wonderful really.  I\'ll do whatever I can to keep you that far from me.  I\'d build myself a house, deep in the forest, high up on a hill, with a big wall around it, just to keep you from coming near me. Snipers would patrol the back of the hill (just in case you thought of coming from behind), and they would \'take care\' of you.  No longer will you pollute my space.  I\'ve got too much protection now for you to hurt me.  I\'ve got an army to the left of me, and my mother on the right (she\'s the one to be scared of y\'know).  With all of this, who needs you?  What you need to worry about, is how you\'re going to penetrate my fortress once you\'ve realized what you\'ve done.  There\'s no taking back words now.  I\'ve found myself in a place of solace, I\'ve built myself a new home.  You\'re not welcome here.\r\n'),(2282,'<p>When I was 16 I got my first job at Carls Jr. a fast food restaurant in Buena Park, CA.  I started by ringing at the register, but soon moved back to the kitchen where the cool guys worked.  I washed trays and dunked french fries for awhile, but then got moved to the cookside where I assembled the burgers and chicken sandwiches as they came off the char-broiler.  \r\n\r\n<p>Even though I was sixteen, I was pretty inexperienced in the ways of the world, hadn\'t even kissed a girl, let alone gone out on a date.  I had fantasies, but pretty much left it at that.  I listened to heavy metal music, read Fantasy novels and played Dungeons & Dragons; that should tell you something about my personality development at the time.\r\n\r\n<p>Once in awhile, when someone didn\'t show up for work, or there was little to do back in the kitchen, I\'d get assigned to clean up the dining room.  Cleaning the dining room was grunt work, the newbies were always assigned dining room duty, either that or it was a punishment for some sort of wrong-doing.  But since I was on the low end of the cookside totem pole, if no one else was available, I\'d have to be the one to go out there and swab tables, clear trays and empty the trash.  Dining room duty was like Carls Jr. geek-work.  But I did it.  \r\n\r\n<p>My parents were making me work.  There argument was that because I didn\'t want to be involved in the social life at my high school, I should get a job.  I didn\'t want go to football games or school sponsored dances like my sister did, those things just disgusted and bored me.  So I applied at a few places, waited for a couple of weeks and had a job.\r\n\r\n<p>So there I was with a gray rag in my hand, collecting tables strewn with the wrappers of Carl\'s Double Famous Stars and Western Bacon Cheeseburgers, remnants of onion rings, wiping up traces of  Catsup and Chocolate Shake.  \r\n\r\n<p>There was a table of pretty girls in the back of the restaurant.  One of them especially appealed to me, she had short blonde hair and bright blue eyes.  But I was stupendously embarrassed. I was embarrassed by my brown polyester uniform and the shit position I was working.  By this time I felt I had earned the badge of the Cool Cook, but I couldn\'t flex any  culinary muscles tying up plastic trash bags and scrapping gum off from under the tables.\r\n\r\n<p>But after a little while I realized that the girls were watching me.  They were smiling over at me and beckoning for me come over.  I felt skewered by a pin.  \"ME?\"  I pointed at myself like a silly character from a move.  \"ARE YOU TALKING TO ME?\"  I mouthed.\r\n\r\n<p>The blonde nodded.   \r\n\r\n<p>I started over there.  She was prettier than I thought, and probably only slightly older than I was.  She had on a dark red blouse, a sliver necklace held a cut glass pendant right on her breastbone.  I remember feeling a little dizzy.  I\'d never had attention from a woman before so this was something new.  In my memory I have only a vague impression of the girls that were sitting there with her.  The edges of my vision were fuzzy.  I was trained in on only this one.\r\n\r\n<p>\"What\'s your name?\"  She asked.\r\n\r\n<p>I swallowed hard and told her.  \"Quinn.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Quinn?  That\'s a nice name.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Thanks.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"When did you start here, Quinn?  We haven\'t seen you here before.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"I usually work in the back.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Oh...Well, we think they should put you out front all the time, at least I do.\"  She reached out and slipped her arm around my waist and pulled me close to her.  I felt her warmth come through the polyester.  \"I think he\'s kinda cute, don\'t you?\"  She asked her friends.  \r\n\r\n<p>My mouth felt very dry and the light from the parking lot outside was hurting my eyes.  I was not at all sure where I was or if this was really happening.  This girl\'s arm around my waist was the closest I had EVER been to a woman, so the dynamic electric THRILL of it was just OVERWHELMING.  I was dumfounded.\r\n\r\n<p>\"When do you get out of here, Quinn?\"  She asked.\r\n\r\n<p>\"Uh...uh...9:00.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Well, we should come back and get you.  We should come back and we could all go do something tonight.  How about it Quinn?\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Uh...that sounds good to me.\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Well, that\'s what we\'ll do then.  We\'ll come back at 9:00 and all go out together.  Okay?\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"Okay.\"  Rather regretfully I had to leave her side and go about the room cleaning up other tables, but whenever I came by her she would smile up at me and her nose would crinkle and I\'d nearly lose my balance.  \r\n\r\n<p>When the finally left the dining room, their table was a mess.  They had left everything there, spent katsup packages, empty soda cups and crumpled french fry wrappers.  But they had also left a five dollar bill.  A tip.  A tip in a fast food restaurant.  \r\n\r\n<p>For me, this confirmed her interest.  Why would she have left me MONEY, if she didn\'t like me.  Money meant something.  Somehow I went through the rest of my day by rote, not so unhappy to have been assigned the dining room, certain that it was a mechanism of Fate that had brought us together.  I had been working in the kitchen for WEEKS now and just TODAY I get assigned to the dining room?  Goddamn it, this was MEANT to happen.\r\n\r\n<p>When my shift was over I punched out, shoved my polyester uniform into a locker and walked out into the cold night air in my shirt sleeves.  I looked around the parking lot.  There was my yellow Mustang, the cars of my fellow employees that I recognized, and a few others who must have been customers.  I did not see My Girl or her friends.  \r\n\r\n<p>I went over to my car and leaned up against it, almost wishing I could smoke a cigarette.  I imagined her pulling up at an moment, music blasting from the window, all the girls laughing.  \"Get in, Quinn!  Get in!\"  But of course that didn\'t happen.  You knew that already.\r\n\r\n<p>I got in my car with a sinking feeling in my stomach.  Of course that\'s how it was going to be.  Maybe she forgot, I said to myself.  The night sky was honing heavy over the parking lot.  The melancholy street lamps tried to hold it back, but it was no good.  I put my hands on the wheel and waited inside the car, turning my head first to one entrance into the parking lot and then the other.  \r\n\r\n<p>Some cars pulled in, the drivers got out of their cars and went into Carls.  I watched one place an order at the counter and take a seat in the dining room where My Girl and her friends had been sitting.  \r\n\r\n<p>I turned the key, kicked the engine over and pulled out of my parking place.  I started to drive away, but couldn\'t do it.  I parked in another part of the lot and just sat there.  It was 9:45.  I had been waiting for 45 minutes and I still didn\'t want to leave.\r\n'),(2284,'<p><i> Silence... </i></p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>It\'s 12:45 and I hear from you <i>now.</i> It was so quiet here. </p>\r\n<p>Two days, not a call, not an email, not a message. The abrupt end to our conversation on Thursday hadn\'t concerned me until now.</p>\r\n<p><i>Until all this silence.</i></p>\r\n<p>I suppose your new life is giving much enjoyment to you. And you are no longer thinking of me.</p>\r\n<p>Well, not as much as you used to, anyway. </p>\r\n<p>Not as much as I still think about you. </p>\r\n<p>Not as much as you tell me you do. </p>\r\n<p><i>And you tell me you do. </i></p>\r\n<p>Have you really been gone so long, that you have <i> adjusted?</i> Am I so naive that I would think that even after all this time, you would still wish for me, dream of meâ€¦ Like you used to claim you did. </p>\r\n<p> I still fantasize for you the way I did when we first met, those years ago. </p>\r\n<p> But you give me no sign to say you fantasize for me. </p>\r\n<p> Though you may love me, I think it not the same consuming love burning on my end. </p>\r\n<p>I am so far into the drug now that the thought of not waiting for you frightens me. Frightened like if <i> I </i> were to some day not choose to call you because I felt tired, because I was doing something else, because I just wanted just sit around, if that day would come, I would no longer be myself, no longer know who I was. I would awaken a stranger. </p>\r\n<p>Youâ€™re asking me right now, â€œWhatâ€™s wrong?â€</p>\r\n<p>It had taken you twenty minutes to hear it in my voice. </p>\r\n<p>You do not know, and in all fairness, I could not expect you to know. </p>\r\n<p>Still, I know in fact that you do not take me so lightly. What you feel for me is real. <i> Different but real. </i></p>\r\n<p>And I canâ€™t let go of that.</p>\r\n<p>Please come home. </p>\r\n'),(2285,'No, I donâ€™t have body odor.  Well, maybe I do.  But, that smell is from the Thai restaurant.  I sit in there for over an hour before every seminar.  The whole family in the restaurant knows me: Mom, Dad, and daughter too.  I donâ€™t even need to place my order â€“ everyone knows exactly what I eat: Pad Kee Mow [vegetables only], hot tea, and a plate of steamed rice.  Two or three times a week for the past five years I have gotten the same God damned thing.  Itâ€™s the only place in town that I feel at home in though.  \r\n\r\n<p>I just know that the less exotic guys in my program think that I smell.  I know that I do have a smell; but itâ€™s not Thai.  I think that Iâ€™m sour â€“ lemons, tomatoes, vinegar, and all the other acidic things that I eat must make me sour.  Maybe thereâ€™s a hint of salt and garlic too.  I never have had the nerve to taste it.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>When Nick and I would pick up our friend Steve we noticed that he carried a scent with him.  Sitting in a small, enclosed space like a car you canâ€™t help but take in personal odors.  Steveâ€™s whole family came over from Korea.  His father had done some sort of work for the Americans in Vietnam and they got citizenship in the States after that.  Anyway, when Steve sat down in the car an odor of broccoli and fish and garlic followed him.  One night, Nick brought it up and then Steve told us that we smelled too.  Iâ€™m guessing that we smelled sour.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Rogelioâ€™s family was from the Philippines; they didnâ€™t have a smell.  Sacâ€™s family was from Laos; they did have a smell.  Come to think of it, they all had fathers that did some sort of work for the American military.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The English [Manx] and the German [Swiss] people in my family had their own smells; they werenâ€™t sour at all.  They ate a lot of meat.  They were older, and they smelled like old people.  My Uncle smelled like the farm; even after he stopped farming.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My sisterâ€™s ex was from Spain.  She called the family home one day in tears, and told us that she couldnâ€™t take it any more.  We drove to pick her up at the place she worked.  As we all rode home together in the car and talked - slowly and gently, she told us about the problems.  One of the things that she said was that her exâ€™s smell wasnâ€™t right â€“ she felt repulsed by it.  She didnâ€™t say that he wasnâ€™t clean; it wasnâ€™t that at all.  He ate huge amounts of meat and cheese.  And something else was wrong too.  Something that didnâ€™t agree with her was coming out from inside his body and through his skin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Whatever it is that lets two people know that they are right for each other must have a lot to do with smell.  I think that you can get to know many, if not most, of the important things about someone through their words.  But, before I ever commit to anything again Iâ€™m going to get real close and inhale.</p>   \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n  \r\n'),(2286,'Ja-bloo-me<br> \r\nJa-blah-me <br>\r\na foo-fah hip & hop me. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI founda gringo with a key <br>\r\nhe said he\'d snitch on wayward me <br>\r\ni told him that he too would see <br>\r\nthat i was the queen of anarchy <br><br>\r\n\r\nthe spies you see could not believe <br>\r\nthat the silks and strings that they were to weave <br>\r\nwere spiky shorn and made us grieve<br> \r\nyes we\'re the ones who get reprieve<br><br> \r\n\r\nmake mine mustard to you i said <br>\r\ntoot it off before youre dead <br>\r\nbounce a ring upon my head <br>\r\ncause i\'m the dude that bakes the bread. <br><br>\r\n\r\nand from my atlas i bought a gun <br>\r\na warm and sticky hot crossed bun <br>\r\nthe miscreants we\'ll surely hum <br>\r\ncause i\'m the one who got the pun <br><br>\r\n\r\neskimo nuns began to beg <br>\r\nwith a lighthouse on the side of her leg <br>\r\ni shot it in and twisted the peg <br>\r\nlike my man gavin, i drank a keg <br><br>\r\n\r\nso gimme a call upon my phone <br>\r\nbe sure not to leave me alone <br>\r\ncause when you go i start to foam <br>\r\ndont leave me or i\'ll turn to stone <br><br>\r\n\r\ncome on and dance the police are here <br>\r\nwe\'ve got nothing left to fear <br>\r\nexcept our men, and some beer <br>\r\nwhom i wager, in sin, are queer.<br>\r\n'),(2287,'<i>Preface: i want to begin working on this story again. this is page six, which i wrote recently, after a long break.  if you want to read the first five pages, a <a href=\"http://www.inconduit.com/words/farmers/f_frame.html\">link</a> is here and also at the end of this entry. I have more hand written, i need to get my data entry on and type it into a text editor.  i\'m going to try to write the next parts of it as a serial here.  motivation.<br></i>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>six months had passed. the man lived only to sleep. he dug himself deep into sleep, more and more each day, with no regard for depth.  he wanted to see this woman who was dreaming in the old man. by now, he knew it was a woman and knew her in the way that you can sense the identity of an intimate partner\'s face near the back of your neck by the way that your ears burn.  no need to see.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he served his days in his job, assembling plastics ten hours at a time, four days per week.  he would spend most of his time half dreaming at work. day dreaming, but in the truer fashion.  it\'s exactly why most of his kind chose jobs like his - repetitive labor - knowing how to place your body on auto pilot while your mind is deep into dream is a valuable skill.  he knew of two other farmers in his factory, which was quite large, but knew of none outside of it. they lived solitarily by choice and sparsely by consequence. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>his dreaming at work was not as deep as at home - it expended much effort to maintain a functioning body while somewhat disconnected by dream. but the dreaming was still effective, just slower - equivalent to not having enough bandwidth.  regardless, it was certainly a preferable alternative to watching himself work.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>he had been able to grow his window into the old man\'s dreams at speed far faster than any other host he\'d had.  in that time he had watched the nondescript colors and shapes slowly begin to collect around each other, telling him more as the weeks passed.  he had always loved the slow and sensuous process of growing into a host\'s dreams, but this time he was a bit more anxious, wanting to see this woman.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he knew that this woman must want to be met because she had continued dreaming in the old man, when most likely she\'d be able to feel his new window growing.  from time to time he could feel windows of other farmers in other hosts of his growing their windows, but would always be abandoned after they detected that host was already occupied.  there are billions of people in the world, an infinite amount of hosts to visit in your lifetime.  his kind preferred solitude.</p>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://www.inconduit.com/words/farmers/f_frame.html\">the first five pages</a>'),(2288,'<br>\r\nclick shot knife<br>\r\nshowers diaphinous ice<br>\r\ncuts out cubes<br>\r\nof moneyshot gorgeous ahi<br>\r\nslick red and fatty<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwrapped in seaweed plastic<br>\r\nsewn in sticky rice<br>\r\nlaid out on kiln-fired crane pictures<br>\r\nrows metered like mormon architecture<br>\r\n<br>\r\nshe\'s got flaming june red hair and<br>\r\nshe swallows them<br>\r\none-by-one between<br>\r\n<br>\r\nconversations<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwrapping her lips<br>\r\naround ocean scents and grain</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2289,'<p>The Big Mac Index is used by economist in nations that are concerned about the value of their currency in global markets, it can be used to ascertain if a currency has been over or undervalued. Take the cost of a Big Mac in various different economies, convert said cost into a single currency, find the mean and there you have your bench mark. If your over the currency is inflated, if your under its deflated. The idea is that a Big Mac should cost the same everywhere. This has been used by various governments economists to help guide monetary policy and has even been talked of in the economist magazine.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This has done very little to help my faith in those players that are supposed managers of our local and global economies. I imagine walking by a McDonald\'s now with its so many billion served sign with a little add on sign saying something along the lines of *And now monetary policy advisors to governments world wide*.  Am I a crackpot? Does this seem absurd to anyone else?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It does add a little prestige to McJobs though, if one is working in such a organization and feeling a little less then significant just remember  - The Big Mac Index. Those burgers you are handling are helping to set global economic policies.</p><br> '),(2290,'<p>This morning after stepping through the turnstiles at Dundas station, basking in the scent of marijuana and high-end cleaning solvents, I suddenly got on some kind of introspective tip and felt a burning desire to just dump my brain onto some kind of storage medium.  After seating my ass into a chair in the room QE127 for a lecture, I quickly jotted down some notes in my Palm so I wouldn\'t forget what I wanted to write about.</p>\r\n<p>Early last week, Matt and I had been browsing around the computer district (College & Spadina) and he purchased a new 60GB HDD.  We had walked part of the way back to his place, but decided that we would rather hop on the College street car and ride to Church Street.  While we were waiting, we noticed a guy in the alley behind us and I thought he was smoking crack.  Upon closer inspection and noticing that he was just chilling in the alley smoking a cigarette I was disappointed.  I\'m not sure how I feel about that.</p>\r\n<p>Last night, while walking home from work, I was listening to the <a href=\"http://plaid.co.uk/\" target=\"new\">Plaid</a> disc in my MD and one track from <a href=\"http://www.warprecords.com/ography/release.php?cat=WARP54\" target=\"new\">Not For Threes</a> was playing.  It was the perfect music for the very light snowfall that was around me.  The snow was so light that you couldn\'t even tell unless you looked up at a streetlight to catch small flakes lighting falling into the beam.  After throwing all the Plaid albums on one MiniDisc and have listened to them all together in one solid listen, I can see how they work together as a trilogy.  There are subltle similarities and repetitions to each album on top of the unique sound which each album presents on it\'s own.  It\'s like verse to listen to each album one after the other.  I have a greater respect for Plaid after this and have been listening to nothing but this disc in my travels of late.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve been thinking lately about a question which you are asked many times throughout your life: What is love (to you)?  One answer which seems to work well is that love is when you like someone enough that you want to spend the rest of your life with them physically, emotionally and mentally.  That seems to work rather well and I think most people would agree that it is acceptible as a broad generalisation.  I think that I would like to say now that I have a pretty good idea of what love would be, for myself.  I think that love is being comfortable.  If you can feel totally at ease and comfortable with another person, no worries, no suspicion, no tension.  I love that word, tension.  I think that close friends exemplify this principle of feeling relaxed and comfortable.  Nearly everyone would agree that they love their close friends.  Well, I think the same applies in romantic relationships with any long-term prospects.  This definition works for me.  I like it and I think I will stick with it.  I\'m comfortable with it.</p>\r\n<p>Just some random output.</p>\r\n'),(2291,'<p>I\'m still, yes still, in Cambodia. I feel like I have been here forever, probably because I have been here forever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Last week we rented our own motorcycle and drove one of the few decent roads through the mountains (most mountains here are just small hills, but these were actually mountainous) to Siahnoukville. It took about 4 hours with two of us on the bike and one big giant bag strapped to the back. Siahnoukville was pleasant. It\'s the beach resort town of Cambodia. There were few tourists there, and only a handful of Cambodians hanging out in the surf. The water of the gulf of Thailand here is calm and unrefreshingly warm. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>The beach was covered in chairs you could rent for 1000 Riel a day (about 25cents), and an army of small children who were carrying woven trays on their heads full of snacks to sell at hugely inflated prices. We spent a lot of time sitting around drinking Angkor beers and buying snacks we didn\'t really need in order to placate the swarming urchins. They were pretty entertaining, and seemed quick to make attachments to tourists. One girl, Marie tied a braided thread bracelet to my wrist on our last day and cried to see us go. Some of the girls would cry their eyes out if you didn\'t buy from them. We weren\'t sure if it was real or not. Were they going to be blamed for the lack of tourism on the beaches of Cambodia? Some said they would get beaten if they didn\'t sell enough, and how could we know? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>There was one boy who was always naked running around the beach begging greedily for handouts of the very same overpriced snacks we\'d just bought from his comrades. It seemed odd that he was always naked, the only one naked, and I asked jokingly if his mother had to chase him around to try to get clothes on him. I was told very soberly by Marie, that his mother was arrested and jailed for trying to sell her girl children, and so the boy didn\'t have any family, no one to buy him clothes and put them on him. He survived on table scraps, tourist handouts, and the cold kindness of the other families.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After resorting it on the beaches, we headed to Kampot, getting to the bad part of the \"road\" just as a torrential thunderstorm hit. There was nothing to do but laugh and make a lot of muddy moto-cross jokes as the road became more and more a connected series of mud filled lakes and rolling hills. My shoes were squishy with mud, my pants were drenched and brown up to the knee. But Kampot was a nice small and quiet town, so peaceful we rested an extra day. And while the streets were brimming with overly aggressive dogs in heat, chasing the motorcycle to try and eat our legs, the guesthouse was new and clean and full of friendly people who spoke to us endlessly in Khmer. I have no idea what they were saying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>From there we struck out on day trips, and I still thank God for giving that motorcycle adequate suspension. The best of the trips was to Bokor National Park. You drive up this long and winding road (again I use the term road very loosely) to the top of a mountain and there at the top is an abandoned mountain resort town. You can still wander around the rooms and hallways of the formerly fancy hotel. It now stands in ruins, looking spooky and haunted. They tell you not to leave the paths here, not all of the landmines have been cleared. There are remnants of a Catholic church, a post office, some houses. All empty, all looted to bare walls by the Khmer Rouge or the Viet Kong or local hoods. Roofs are missing, walls tilt precariously, and no one yells at you to get out of the dangerous spots. Our other day trip was to Kep. \"Kep by the Sea\" is a mostly, but not entirely abandoned seaside resort town. The only thing of note was the extremely large pig, which was wallowing in the middle of what was supposedly the road. The beachy ruins aren\'t all that old and I wonder what this country would have been like, had they not so thoroughly destroyed themselves...</p>\r\n'),(2292,'I am happy.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThis never happens.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI am feeling glee, smiling.<br><br> \r\n\r\nStanding in corners.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWatching people, <br>\r\nanticipating my next movement<br> \r\nand their next movement.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nEnjoying the minutes. <br>\r\n\r\nWatching the sun rise along the shadows on my wall.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI wonder what you are doing.<br>  \r\nThe same? <br><br>\r\n\r\nDo you smile in the dark?<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd does it mean something like when a tree falls in the forest?<br><br>\r\n\r\nI take this time and capture it in my mind hoping it will not fade.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2293,'<i>This is the unfortunate result of extreme fatigue, alcohol, caffeine, a severe lack of anything serious to say, but a desire to try and say something anyways.</i><br> \r\n<p>Hippies try to spread the love, Nihilist are an exclusive club.</p>\r\n<p>Revolutionaries ask \'will it begin?\', Liberals are busy delving into sin.</p>\r\n<p>Conservatives focus on tightening their ties, Poor try to busy themselves by swatting at flies.</p>\r\n<p>Mothers hug their children tight, Soldiers ready themselves for a fight.</p>\r\n<p>Politicians accept their kickbacks, Businessmen invite secretaries into the sack.</p>\r\n<p>Lovers try to reconcile, Candystrippers mop up the bile.</p>\r\n<p>Media pundits start to spout, Gays are struggling to come out.</p>\r\n<p>Students are dreaming of success, Parents tell kids what is best.</p>\r\n<p>Protestors carry signs and chant, Economists start on some boring rant.</p>\r\n<p>Smokers light up their cigarettes, Athletes try to bet records set.</p>\r\n<p>Depressives contemplate suicide, Optomist enjoy a smoother ride.</p>\r\n<p>Alcoholics drink some gin, Garbagemen collect their bins.</p>\r\n<p>Friends gather in intimate places, Plastic surgeons make new faces.</p>\r\n<p>Critics attempt to analyze, Chemist struggle to synthesize.</p>\r\n<p>Foreigners come to a new land, Visionaries decide to take a stand.</p>\r\n<p>Vegetarians don\'t eat meat, Fetishists make love to feet.</p>\r\n<p>Environmentalist climb up trees, Tax collectors add up their fees.</p>'),(2294,'<center><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2294_1.pjpeg\"></center><br><br>\r\n\r\nRead back, and you\'ll see. I\'m stronger than a king. I\'m taller than a mountain. And I don\'t need you to tell me so. If I had a little bit of what I don\'t, I\'d have more than I needed. And more than enough to show you how it is. There is a mirror at my side, but not in front of my face, not there, because it\'s unnecessary. I know what I look like, as much as who I am. You know what I look like, but beyond that, you have no idea. You have no idea, and you are the weaker for it. I could burn holes into you with my eyes, and teach you how to follow the very path to yourself and to your future, if you were worthy, but whether you are or not, is little concern of mine. <br><br>Were you to come to me and say to me, \"This is your concern! Show me the way\" I might slow myself and stay with you. Show you where to go. Make your future into mine. But you move and think and have your own time and your own concerns. You live your own small pattern and forget even to give thought to divergences. But I will live all our lives. In the many directions you could go, I will live out the perfect direction, even as I live it without you. Even as I live without you, I live with you. There is no escape from this direction, and all directions pass through this place. I am the pass through the tall mountains, the bicep of the king.'),(2295,'<p><i>Hello hello hello hello\r\nCan you hear me?\r\nHello hello hello hello\r\nI\'m bangin\' on the fourth wall.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p> Hi. I\'m lost. Can you help me find myself?</p>\r\n<p> I last saw myself at age..... I can\'t remeber.</p>\r\n<p> I wonder, when did I stop lying to everyone else and start lying to myself?</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>Hello hello hello hello\r\ncan you hear me?\r\nGet me out of this reality, where I don\'t belong.</i></p>'),(2296,'<b><i>*i want to create something unabashedly beautiful*</b></i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nand i hope for it to be beautiful in all the senses of that word, beauty. not the lopsided definition we always use: that which is pretty to look at.'),(2322,'<pre>\r\nmysql> explain people;\r\n+-------+--------------+------+-----+---------+----------------+\r\n| Field | Type         | Null | Key | Default | Extra          |\r\n+-------+--------------+------+-----+---------+----------------+\r\n| id    | int(11)      |      | PRI | NULL    | auto_increment |\r\n| name  | varchar(255) |      |     |         |                |\r\n| email | varchar(255) | YES  |     | NULL    |                |\r\n| url   | varchar(255) | YES  |     | NULL    |                |\r\n+-------+--------------+------+-----+---------+----------------+\r\n4 rows in set (0.01 sec)\r\n</pre>'),(2323,'Soft footed, sleek bodied,<br>\r\nsilk to the touch;<br>\r\nwarm beating heart hidden<br>\r\nfrom mere mortal eyes.<br>\r\nIf you go looking for it<br>\r\nit hides.<br>\r\nBut go about<br>\r\nyour daily tasks<br>\r\nwith patience and small love;<br>\r\nignore it, stop searching,<br>\r\nand forgotten, <br>\r\nit will pad up <br>\r\nbehind you<br>\r\nand suddenly pounce.<br>\r\nThe dark fur of Joy<br>\r\nwill thus come and flash by.<br>\r\nAnd only the scratches<br>\r\nwill remind you<br>\r\nthat it was here <br>\r\nat all.<br>'),(2324,'<p>I can\'t fucking write this.<br>I am mentally uncapable.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t understand all these complexities and unrealised feelings.</p>\r\n<p>I am always right.<br><br>Always.<br><br>But I am especially right about negative things.</p>\r\n<p>Just once I\'d like to be wrong.<br>I\'m still waiting.</p>\r\n<p>I am doomed to live out my days in a horrible self-inflicting cycle.</p>\r\n<p>I find things that don\'t need fixing and break them.<br>I don\'t know how to fix them.</p>\r\n<p>I am a complexity magnet.<br>I could walk into a Kindergarten class and walk away with unsolvable problems.<br>Unresloved issues.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t deserve anything.<br>I want everything.<br>I get nothing.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t want anyone.<br>I want everyone.<br>I want them always.</p>\r\n<p>I don\'t know what I want.</p>\r\n<p>This is garbage.</p>\r\n'),(2297,'<p>The Strokes play in the background - Last night. I type away trying not to spill too much blood on the key board. Earlier tonight I was suffering from some sort of anxiety attack, the ex fiance called to talk about possible future plans. \"If you just move out to the west coast you can move in with me and I\'ll take care of all your needs until you get your feet on the ground. I know Robert he is a recruiter he could get you a job in no time.\" Trying not to be too much of an ass I just go on some rant about self respect and making your own way, what else can I say - if I go out there it is all over and it will be her and I for better or worse. We have a tendency when together to lean towards the worst.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I smoke like a fiend starring at the skyline out of my window contemplating my position in life. The pack of smokes with Carlos\' number on them serve as a reminder, if I could just be a gay man I wouldn\'t have so many problems trying to attract people to share my life with in an intimate way. He was one smooth mother, hanging out at the Bovine club comes up and asks me if the newspaper is mine. He just wants to check one article - Savage Love\'s column (all about a conservative muslim being scared his family will discover the homemade porn he made that has been posted on the net by an angry ex). After reading this he decides to be more direct and brings up our eye contact upon his entry to the club, I fess up \"yeah I was lookin at you, you were the only person I have seen tonight that walked in here smiling ear to ear all night.\" Reply \"You do blow?\" Me lying \"No. I\'ve done other things but I draw the line\" Suddenly I am reminded of <i>Johnny Cash - I walk the line</i> then as a few more neurons fire up and I take stock of the situation my thoughts wonder to <i>Johny Cash - Ring of fire.</i> He asks me some questions about what I do and finds out I\'m unemployed and ask \"You bi?\" before I can answer he looks at my face and say \"you don\'t have to answer that.\" The next thing I know he starts mentioning that he is a scout for male models and actors. Feeling flattered I can\'t help but chuckle to myself.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The wing man approaches his straight friend Luke (the name catches me off guard and I try to maintain composure) suddenly the two of them launch into this bit about the Celestine Prophecy and how every eye contact you make is an opportunity and you have to decide what you are going to do about it. Apparently James Redfield thinks you should try and at least talk to anyone you make eye contact with. Fuck, I\'m so amused I must look like a teenage boy watchin his first porno. Trailer trash Wednesdays at the Bovine are at the very least entertaining.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Sad thing is I\'m here with two girls, one of which I\'d be lying if I didn\'t admit my loins had a liking towards. Problem is I don\'t know her enough and after my most recent experiences my male admirer seems so much safer. He has already offered me a night out on the town \"don\'t worry about the money, I\'ll show you a good time - movie then drinks.\" I\'m left wondering if my advances on the female my loins have taken a liking too would sound the same way to her.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>More Beer Please! No Beer, well then gin will have to do. </p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>Soundtrack to my writing switches to <i>Southern Culture on the Skids - You make me walk like a Camel, Dirt Track Date, and Chicken and Gasoline.</i></p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>To skip ahead for a moment, my drunken stupor upon my return home finds me frying up veggie bacon for a sandwich and I\'ve sliced my hand open like I was the topic of some TLC surgery show. The roomate has bandaged me up and quickly retired, probably due to not wanting to listen to my rambling. Thus I turn to typing this all out to share with the Swinney crowd, while blood slowly drips onto the keyboard. I\'ll be sorry for posting in the morning and I\'ll find the keyboard disgusting but hey maybe it\'ll amuse some of ya.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I sing out some lyrics \"ride down to the real nitty gritty...\". Something tells me that neither my admirer Carlos nor the female my loins lust after are the type of person that could share my desire to be a successful young urban professional, while taking pride in my desired weekend activity of participating in some sort of muddy crash up derby event.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The girls called in the middle of my phone call with the ex fiance on the cell, a definite blessing. An excuse to get off the phone and out of the house all rolled up into one. Don\'t get me wrong the Ex is great she wants to see me happy and I reciprocate that feeling, she just hasn\'t realized it isn\'t very unlikely that we will be able to do that together.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\"Whole lot of things I ain\'t done yet... Never had sex in a hurricane, ran around naked in the pourin rain... but that don\'t mean that I\'d wouldn\'t like to.\"</i></p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The conclusions I\'ve drawn: I should call Carlos but not for a date, he wants to hook me up with a job; I should stay away from the previously mentioned female that my loins have taken a liking to, it\'ll end in me being rejected or feeling like an ass for some reason; I should not be posting anything on the net while under the influence.</p>         '),(2299,'you know that scene in that movie<br>\r\nwhere the people in the concentration<br>\r\ncamps are blocked into trains<br>\r\nand you can see their eyes<br>\r\nthrough the spaces between the rough<br>\r\nwooden planks of the carriages, and<br>\r\nthe thought of cows fills your mind,<br>\r\nmaybe the ones you saw on the fields<br>\r\nnext to fallowfield road as you drove home<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou know the tall handsome officer<br>\r\nin his perfect uniform, the buttons <br>\r\non his cuffs gleaming in the sunlight<br>\r\n(the sun always shines in these scenes)<br>\r\nyou know how the grey cloth on his arm shifted<br>\r\nas his muscles bulged as he lifted<br>\r\nthe pipe and squirted water against<br>\r\nthe side of the train, into their<br>\r\nunseen faces and all of a sudden a thousand<br>\r\nhands burst out through the gaps<br><br>\r\n\r\nand now the image of pale human flowers<br>\r\nsprouting from the soil of a train carriage,<br>\r\nfills the space behind your eyeballs,<br>\r\nand you watch as these hands, separate, alone,<br>\r\nthe bodies they are attached to hidden<br>\r\nbehind the side of the train,<br>\r\nstart grabbing at the water<br><br>\r\n\r\nwith such conviction you don\'t have <br>\r\nthe heart to shout out how impossible <br>\r\nit is to hold onto liquid, onto anything, <br>\r\nlike that. even life. then you remember <br>\r\nyour grandfather asking you, if you could <br>\r\nlive another day, wouldn\'t you?<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou remind yourself the screen is deaf <br>\r\nanyway, they wouldn\'t hear your warning,<br>\r\nas a thousand tongues appear higher up<br>\r\nabove the hands, and they\'re trying <br><br>\r\n\r\nto drink the water. just when they\'ve had a taste <br>\r\nthe camera swings to the officer\'s face<br>\r\nand closes in on his smile<br>\r\nand suddenly there\'s a cry, and you realise<br>\r\nit was all silent before, no sounds, just pictures<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou figure out, though you weren\'t shown it,<br>\r\nthat the handsome officer\'s ordered a subordinate <br>\r\nto turn off the water, and you picture the younger<br>\r\nsoldier\'s hands on the tap, his fingers moving<br>\r\n\r\nand you\'re well aware that this is just <br>\r\na movie, but you know this did happen, it will happen,<br>\r\nit is happening right now. <br>'),(2298,'<I><p>Ive got 3 weeks behind me just 10 more to go<br>\r\nAnd if I could see you id take off your clothes <br>\r\nAnd weâ€™d lie in the garden and watch the weeds grow.</I> â€“ mirah</p>\r\n\r\n<p>All I have running through my hot head are pop songs. Sweaty little love lyrics. Moments of effortless beauty happen to me when Iâ€™m alone, when I get the grace of a breeze or a wide-open space. When I catch a karaoke lyric from one of the outdoor stages, and I understand the thai, I am just a little bit more pleased with myself than when I can order noodles and be perfectly understood the first time. Justin said that he went to Akita, away from warm tits and familiar lights, â€œto challenge himselfâ€. As for me, maybe once a year I have to jump my fishy ass out of the water in order to let some caprice of nature suffocate me. And then I can breathe again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In  my imagination I am less of a warrior, and more of a willing victim of irresistible, painful luck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now I know how to get the big park, where I can pretend I am a sweet young coquette skipping forth to avoid the garden sprinklers flooding the path. Where I can sketch trees and sit in the shade eating gusâ€™s favorite ice cream treat. It is dairy-free, frozen taro inside with coconut milk on the outside. Licking the wooden stick I remembered another lyric,  Julie Ruinâ€™s ubiquitous â€œstay monkeyâ€</p>\r\n\r\n<p><I>Your love your know it tastes as sweet as cold coconut ice cream<br>\r\nNot knowing makes me happy; I just wanna wait and see.</I></p>\r\n\r\n<p>And from hearing that song briefly somewhere between my ears, a flood of memories associated with language and love gushed forth. I am often embarrassed at how much I relate memory to these simple little diddies. What bell in my brain is jingled by certain wails and words? There is somehow a direct chord of psycho-stimulation between my â€œnostalgiaâ€ button and my ears. Let the scientists translate that one. But I know Iâ€™m not the only one. And so does Cameron Crowe when he hammers 10 college pop hits through your brain without a breather in the audio foreground of all his rom-com-drams. He is telling you to feel, goddamnit!!! FEEEEEEEEEEEL! Feel it, feel it, feel the vibrations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Youâ€™ve seen the movie <I>Better off Dead</I> right? When the mother says, â€œIn our house weâ€™ve discovered that you donâ€™t NEEd to know English to speak the intahnational language. Heheh. The INTAHNATIONAL LANGUAGE. HEHEHEHâ€¦â€ pause. â€œLOVE. The language of LOVE.â€</p>\r\n\r\n <p>Whenever you see a Landmark movie presentation (likely a foreign sleeper or some indie about 20somethings sitting in a bar talking about homosexuality), that damn commercial comes on: â€œLe cinema est une langage universelleâ€ [sic]. But then what is love again? Make up your mind, people. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Everyone, particularly ad copywriters, wants to uncover the true â€œuniversalâ€ language. For if we find the key to that sacred little bit of public real estate lodged inside the noggen of Aborigines and potbelly pig traders alike, we can sell the one universal product to that universe of mankind. Coke has come close, hasnâ€™t it? Or if you are a linguist, you could trace that public space to the origin of all language cognition, and we could learn each otherâ€™s languages more quickly. </p> \r\n<p>My various non-sequitur mixed media revelations about the nature of memory and conversation and music and greetings was cut short by a figure I didnâ€™t see standing to my left, wearing a yellow jacket and a rain cap in the 90 degrees F heat, reaching into a tattered fanny pack at her hip. Before she opened her mouth to offer me a card printed with the words  â€œI am deafâ€¦â€ or to try and sell me candy, or to just beg, I said quickly â€œpuut pasaa thai mai daiâ€ (I cant speak thaiâ€) and she began to babble in me coherent English.</p>\r\n<p>â€œI fortune teller, lady. Lady, I can read palms and calculate your birthdayâ€.  She handed me a card that read her name and a message in both English and thai: â€œI Give You Many Gifts.â€ </p>\r\n<p>â€œI no lie lady I give you discount today you my first reading lady.â€ Thai people have such a deferent language that they look for some way to address a person politely, even though in every day American english it is more common to refer to someone, anyone, as â€œhey, you!!â€. Thus, I am often referred to on the streets as â€œMissâ€ or â€œMaâ€™amâ€ or â€œLadyâ€. â€œMaâ€™amâ€ is also just thai slang for â€œwhite woman.â€</p>\r\n<p>I told her that I have no baht. She persisted. â€œI give you many gifts lady I no tell lie!â€  She sat down next to me and started to ask me a series of personal questions, to which I mostly answered lies or â€œI donâ€™t know.â€ I didnâ€™t want to waste her time and I told her again and again that I wasnâ€™t interested. But being a victim of limitlessly â€œluckyâ€ circumstance, I began to think that maybe I would let her read my fortune. That perhaps she was a sage brought to me by the taro-ice cream people for more good tidings. I asked her how she learned her craft? at the wat. For how long did she study? Three months.</p>\r\n<p>Three months?! Three months of church tarot card lessons and Iâ€™m going to put my fate in your hands?? No way lady.  The luck ends here.</p>\r\n<p>â€œI give you many gifts lady just tell me what year you were bornâ€¦â€ 1976. â€œOH! Very lucky. Big snake!â€</p>\r\n<p>big snake? I thought I was a dragon. Now I knew she couldnâ€™t be trusted. I am a dragon, the most auspicious and luckiest in the Chinese zodiac, dammit. What the hell is big snake? I asked her:â€œI thought I was <I>gnu-yai</I>â€. I know the thai word for dragon on accounta  my mom, my aunt, my sister, my newphew and I all being dragons. Very auspicious. </p>\r\n<p>â€œyes, gnu-yai! Gnu-yai! Very lucky!!â€ and for the first time I realized by repeating the word that the thai for â€œdragonâ€ is translated literally as â€œBig Snake.â€ And that, when I imagine what a dragon looks like(from the numerous times in real experience that Ive encountered them), it indeed resembles a big snake.</p>\r\n<p>I have to remember not to think in terms of translation. My French teacher in high school used to bitch about it. â€œDonâ€™t ask me â€œquell est le mot pour MOSQUITO?â€ I guess you were supposed to act out a mosquito or put together some shoddy sentence like â€œwhat do you call that thing that bites and it itches and gives you malaria?â€</p> \r\n<p>And I remembered that I had pledged not to listen to my English-lyrics CDs while I study thai, that I would let thai music take over the gnarly knobs of my cognition that control daydreams. Iâ€™m not throwing away anything Iâ€™ve learned, just adding to my library of possibilities so that I might be flexible out of my element. That new words and music could soften my head, instruct my heart. In a new language I wonâ€™t remember how to miss you so much.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2300,'i stared at myself for hours last night. <br><br>i watched my patterns and sleep.  i sat inches away from my feet and saw myself breathe in and out while in the middle of a dream.  i slowly walked behind myself as i strolled through glass corridors and crystal blue water of lake michigan ran over my feet.  i ran beside myself when i got scared and looked up at the sky as it grew dark.  i stood quietly behind myself while i opened the door at the end of the hall and then i looked back at myself as i walked through.  i held my hand while time stood still just waiting for me to wake.  <br><br>i stared at myself for hours last night.  '),(2301,'when people ask if you\'re tired, they mean that you look like shit.'),(2302,'Red Dirt,<BR>\r\nEven when seen<BR>\r\nthrough the<BR>\r\nBlack Coffee masked<BR>\r\nEyes of Exhaustion,<BR>\r\nis still<BR>\r\nRed Dirt.'),(2303,'<P>\r\nthis room is killing me\r\nor maybe it\'s not<BR>\r\nit could just be my back<BR>\r\nI really don\'t think it likes me<BR>\r\nand I think I\'ve heard it plotting<BR>\r\nI don\'t know with whom<BR>\r\nbut there\'s a conspiracy no doubt<BR>\r\nmaybe it\'s you<BR>\r\nyou always do get closer to my back<BR>\r\nthan the rest of me<BR>\r\nyes the pieces are starting to fit<BR>\r\nstarting to fit myself around the bit<BR>\r\nI\'ll bite down for you forever<BR>\r\nuntil my teeth are dust<BR>\r\nand my jaw hangs loosely<BR>\r\nbroken several times over<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI didn\'t need to know this<BR>\r\nno clearance was given<BR>\r\nI shouldn\'t have been told this<BR>\r\nmost agents are more driven<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nif this is how it goes<BR>\r\nthen okay, alright<BR>\r\nbut my back goes with me<BR>\r\nso to what end will that serve you<BR>\r\nwithout your favorite part of me<BR>\r\nto get so very close to<BR>\r\nwould you make some love to my corpse<BR>\r\nyou might like that<BR>\r\nall of my good parts and none of the bad<BR>\r\nput on that hooded mask<BR>\r\nhide from me like I do inside you<BR>\r\nmy back is not going to like this<BR>\r\nthis betrayal of its plot<BR>\r\ndon\'t worry I won\'t tell<BR>\r\nI am the quiet one<BR>\r\nor so everybody says\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI didn\'t need to know this<BR>\r\nthe drugs are very strong<BR>\r\nyou shouldn\'t have been told this:<BR>\r\nI was broken all along<BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2304,''),(2305,'<p>i find that there is little to say these days. especially about my book. how many times can one say \"almost done,\" and keep on meaning it? i feel like papa smurf. more like a puppet, though, than a cartoon. when i speak i watch myself, and i wonder what that girl is talking about. so i try just sitting quietly. i used to have a lot to say, so i am not always comfortable with this. yesterday elly was sitting in my house and i went in the other room alone and wrote her email while my cat burrowed into her armpit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to be the cat. i want to curl up in everyone\'s lap and go to sleep. i want to just walk up behind the people i love and press my cheek against their backs. or kneel at their feet and wrap my arms around their ankles. this is why i like ezekiel; he is only a couple months old, and he doesn\'t expect anything from me but warmth. i hold him and we walk around in circles or curl up in the corner. i find i have lots to say to ezekiel, but it\'s all stuff like \"you smell so good. i am going to eat your head.\" i don\'t think ezekiel knows who i am.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there have been times, and i\'m sure there will be more, when i enjoyed moving through rooms kissing all the people i care for, savoring pull and draw. but kissing creates complications, and right now i like simple. i just want to press myself close to the people i care for, and close my eyes and smile, and notice where they are warm and what they smell like.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what is there to say? everything to say seems random. i can\'t remember what i thought i had to say. my notebook holds lines like this: \"today the narcissus shot up two inches between sunrise and sunset.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i love everyone so much it hurts, but i am so athrob with being that i cannot speak.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>maybe i will try holding hands. maybe i\'ll become a rampant silent hand-holder.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that sounds about right.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all the window-blinds have been closed for days, and this morning i glued a tiny ceramic turtle atop my monitor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but we build a fire almost every night.</p>'),(2306,'<i><a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2287.txt\">click here for previous page</a></i>\r\n\r\n<p>she had been waiting.  she felt comfortable in her favorite dream set.  the number of individual dream sets inside any given person was so high that it was impossible to explore them all in a lifetime.  even the host doesn\'t come close to dreaming in them all in their lifetime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she had found a dream from the old man\'s youth - a rural existence. grass, wild, and lots of it, a tree in the middle of it all, a warm summer evening. slow red light, still and unchanging, deeply satisfied with resting in her skin. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>the sunlight from the horizon, gently pierced it, and her whole body straight through, pinning her to the tree. she was sitting up against the trunk, dull furrows of bark pressing back against her. she thought of very little, and breathed even less.  she knew someone was coming. but she did not feel anxious.  she\'d never met another farmer in a dream before, and was curious as to how it would happen. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>she drew in a very slow breath. slow, but strong, reaching out to the grass around her and pulling the moisture from each green blade, their water collecting fine droplets on her lips as her breath passed through them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he had been able to see her clearly, but from a distance, for a few weeks.  she was always in that field, against that tree.  the window was wide enough for him to be able to smell the smells and hear the sounds.  even without the woman there, he would have found this place attracting.  for the past two nights he impatiently tried stepping through the window, but still could not quite feel firm soil beneath his feet, nor taste the quality of the air that always told him when he had finally made his way in.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>another night, to sleep, a pause; the window.  over a few small hills, he saw her there, and tried to step through again.  it\'s not like physically stepping through a window, but all the trappings of living in a physical body predispose you to their limitations, so it ends up feeling like stepping through a window from night to day, from vague darkness to acute description.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>he knew he would make it through tonight. from the beginning of the evening he could feel the heat of the red sunlight passing through the window and resting on his chest.  he followed its lead, closed his eyes, and pulled himself through.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he felt the sunlight wash into him as his body passed through the plane of the window, warmth continuously accumulating progressive thin cross sections of him, from front to back, until there was no darkness left.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he was through.  the ground came in to solid focus beneath his feet.  he was warm, and he had his arm over his eyes to shield them from the light - they always took a few minutes to adjust. </p>\r\n\r\n<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2287.txt\">page six</a>'),(2307,'Now... what y\'all wanna do?<br>\r\nWanna be ballers?  Shot-callers?<br>\r\nBrawlers - who be dippin in the Benz wit the spoilers<br>\r\nOn the low from the Jake in the Taurus<br>\r\nTryin to get my hands on some Grants like Horace<br>\r\nYeah livin the raw deal, three course meals<br>\r\nSpaghetti, fettucini, and veal\r\nBut still, everything\'s real in the field<br>\r\nAnd what you can\'t have now, leave in your will<br>\r\nBut don\'t knock me for tryin to bury<br>\r\nseven zeros, over in Rio Dijanery<br>\r\nAin\'t nobody\'s hero, but I wanna be heard<br>\r\non your Hot 9-7 everyday, that\'s my word<br>\r\nSwimmin in women wit they own condominiums<br>\r\nFive plus Fives, who drive Millineums<br>\r\nIt\'s all about the Benjamins, what?\r\nI get a fifty pound bag of ooh for the mutts\r\nFive carats on my hands wit the cuts<br>\r\nAnd swim in European figures<br>\r\nFuck bein a broke nigga<br>\r\nI want a all chromed out wit the clutch, nigga\r\nDrinkin malt liquor, drivin a Bro\' Vega<br>\r\nI\'m wit Mo\' sippers, watched by gold diggers (uhh)<br>\r\nRockin Bejor denims, wit gold zippers (c\'mon)<br>\r\nLost your touch we kept ours, poppin Cristals<br>\r\nFreakin the three-quarter reptiles (ahahah)<br>\r\nEnormous cream, forrest green - Benz jeep<br>\r\nfor my team so while you sleep I\'ma scheme <br>(that\'s right)<br>\r\nWe see through, that\'s why nobody never gon\' believe you\r\nYou should do what we do, stack chips like Hebrews<br>\r\nDon\'t let the melody intrigue you (uh-uh)<br>\r\nCause I leave you, I\'m only here<br>\r\nfor that green paper which lead you<br>\r\nI\'m strictly tryin to cop those, colossal sized Picasso\'s<br>\r\nAnd have papi flip coke outside Delgado\'s (whoo!)<br>\r\nMienda, with cash flowin like Sosa<br>\r\nAnd the latin chick tranportin in the chocha<br>\r\nStampedin over, pop Mo\'s, never sober<br>\r\nLex and Range Rovers dealin weight by Minnesota (uhh)<br>\r\nAvoidin NARC\'s wit camcorders and Chevy Novas (uh-huh)<br>\r\nStash in the buildin wit this chick named Alona (uh-huh)<br>\r\nfrom Daytona, when I was young I wants to bone her (uh-huh)<br>\r\nBut now I only hit chicks that win beauty pageants (ahahaha)<br>\r\nTrickin, they takin me skiing, at the Aspens (c\'mon)<br>\r\nUhh, gangsta mental, stay poppin Cristal<br>\r\nPack a black pist-al in the Ac\' Coupe that\'s dark brown (whoo!)<br>\r\nPinky-ringin, gondolas wit the man singin<br>\r\nItalian music down the river wit your chick clingin<br><br>\r\nto my bizzalls, player you mad false<br>\r\nActin hard when you as pussy as RuPaul<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nC\'mon, c\'mon, uh-huh<br>\r\nIt\'s all about the Benjamins baby<br>\r\nUh-huh, yeah<br>\r\n(repeat 4X)<br>\r\nIt\'s all about the Benjamins baby<br><br>\r\n\r\nUhh, uhh, what the blood clot?<br>\r\nWanna bumble wit the Bee hahh?<br>\r\nBZZZZ, throw a hex on a whole family (yeah... yeah yeah)<br>\r\nDressed in all black like the Oman (say what?)<br>\r\nHave your friends singin \'This is for my homey\' (that\'s right)<br>\r\nAnd you know me, from makin niggaz so sick<br>\r\nFloss in my 6 with the Lex on the wrist<br>\r\nIf it\'s Murder, you know She Wrote it (uh-huh)<br>\r\nGerman Ruger for your ass bitch, deep throated<br>\r\nKnow you wanna fill the room cause it\'s platinum coated<br>\r\nTake your pick, got a firearm you shoulda toted, suck a dick<br>\r\nAll that bullshit you kick, playa hatin from the sideline<br>\r\nGet your own shit, why you ridin mine? (uh-huh)\r\nI\'ma Goodfella kinda lady<br>\r\nStash 380\'s and Mercedes, Puffy hold me down baby!<br>\r\nOnly female in my crew, and I kick shit<br>\r\nlike a nigga do, with a trigga too, fuck you<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n(Big: Yeah, yeah, uhh, uhh, uhh!)<br>\r\nI been had skills, Cristal spills<br>\r\nHide bills in Brazil, about a mil to ice grill<br>\r\nMake it hard to figure me, nigga be, kickin me<br>\r\nin my asshole, uhhh, undercover, Donni Brascoe<br>\r\nLent my East coast girl, the Bentley to twirl (uh-huh)<br>\r\nMy West coast shorty, push the chrome 740<br>\r\nRockin Redman and Naughty, all in my kitty-kat<br>\r\nHalf a brick of yea, in the bra, where her titties at<br>\r\nAnd I\'m livin that, whole life, we push weight (uh-huh)<br>\r\nFuck the state pen, fuck hoes at Penn State (c\'mon)<br>\r\nListen close it\'s Francis, the Praying Mantis<br>\r\nAttack with the Mac, my left hand spit, right hand<br>\r\ngrip on the whip, for the smooth getaway<br>\r\nPlaya haters get away or my lead will spray<br>\r\nSqueeze off til I\'m empty, don\'t tempt me<br>\r\nOnly, to Hell I send thee, all about the Benji\'s\r\nWhat??<br><br>\r\n<b>It\'s all about the Benjamins baby</b>\r\n'),(2311,'<p>\r\nit\'s 9:02am and i am doing my morning ritual of milking my fullest breast into a colorful plastic baby bottle.\r\n</p>  \r\n<p>\r\nall night long ezekiel and i sleep belly-to-belly, his hands and mouth wrapped around my right breast.  he sleeps all through the night with the occasional exception of when he has misplaced my nipple.  it must be nice to eat and sleep at the same time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the mornings my left breast is full to bursting.  sometimes i wake up feeling a light sprinkling on my face.  this is my breast dramatically unleashing the milk.  i have very little control over that.  the best i can hope for is to apply pressure and stanch the milk.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin case you were wondering, the bottle holds a little over 5 ounces.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have 8 little bottles of milk all lined up in the kitchen freezer.  these bottles are like gold to me.  i can barter for my free time in milk.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe math goes like this:  5 ounces of milk is, roughly, 3 hours of time. so, 8 5oz bottles means that i could walk out the door right now and not come back for 24 hours.  for one whole day, i could be my own girl again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m plotting my escape.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou would, too, if you knew the crush of being everything to someone.  you start out your motherhood thinking that parenting is a partnership, that there will be two little birdies tending the nest.  one day you realize that you are the default parent.  your child is constantly with you.  you can\'t even get into the shower without asking the other little birdie to watch the egg.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndid you get that?  i have to ask jeremy to watch ezekiel.  nobody asks if the mother will watch the baby.  it is just assumed that she is.  i am the default parent.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feed ezekiel while he is cradled in my lap.  i hold his left hand in my right hand and i read to him while he eats.  we\'ve already gotten through the hobbit and have started the fellowship of the rings.  he humms softly into my breast and pets my skin with his free hand.  i sometimes think this is the best thing, ever.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nah, biology.  she is a tricky bitch.  oxytocin is the goo inside of you that releases the milk in your breast.  it is also the same goo that starts labor contractions and is the hormone that triggers orgasm.  i feed ezekiel and think of sex.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nezekiel pulls off of my breast with a wet smacking sound and treats me to his hammy smiles and vocal olympics before diving face-first back to the work of eating.  every single cell of him has come through me, first.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsoon jeremy will come into my room on his way to making the coffee downstairs.  i will hand him this bottle to put with the others in the freezer.  my freedom sits chilling in the cold in fruity plastic bottles.  i contemplate the mechanics of getting myself into the shower.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2308,'<p>After returning the bike we\'d rented to drive around the south of Cambodia, we took a small boat up the Mekong to the town of Kratie to see the freshwater dolphins. They were shy animals, and played a little game hiding from our boat, rowed by an old woman out into the middle of a wide spot in the dry season\'s shallow river. The dolphins migrated into the Mekong (and in Burma up the Irrawaddy) over the course of time, slowly moving away from the salty seawater into the fresh river water. We spent the morning arching our necks for glimpses of the curve of their smooth backs breaking the surface. By 10 am we were finished, and riding back to town on the little motos through roadside villages. I watched the kids run around in the space next to the road with their dogs, playing or on some errand for their mother. It struck me that though over 50% of the population of Cambodia are under 15 years old, and have no living memory of the atrocities that occurred in the early â€˜70s, the horror and violence of war is still running strong in their little veins. They smile and laugh, but there is a sour rage sitting just below the surface of their skins. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>There was absolutely nothing else to do in Kratie, even the food was worse than you can imagine. Restaurants were serving instant ramen, with foil pack included for seasoning, so we left and went back down the river to Kampong Cham. The boats you have to take up and down the river are a sort of metal tube with windows and some stifling seats inside. Anyone who likes themselves even a little bit will sit on top, which is the only way you\'d survive if the boat were to sink or flip. There are no stops except to pick up more people, and there is no toilet. The ride in total from Phnom Phen to Kratie is over 5 hours, but luckily it\'s only about 3 back to Kampong Cham. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Welll... usually 3.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This day the ride took a bit more time because twice we had to stop along side a small wooden boat in the middle of the Mekong, so workmen could load their cargo onto the boat. Not boxes, no, but pigs, live pigs! LARGE pigs! Hogtied! Grunting and squealing! Lifted by ears, tails, feet, whatever protrusion was most easy to grab, and tossed roughly onto the railing that ran around the back end of the boat where they were tied down. When the railing was full of squealing pigs, roasting in the summer sun, pigs were slung onto the top of the boat and tied into place near the passengers and other cargo. At one stop they untied a few pigs, presumably to sell to the locals, and just let them flop into the water. People in small boats rowed out and held pigs by the ears so they wouldnâ€™t drown, but a few of the animals werenâ€™t so lucky and slowly their heads sank under the surface, their bodies just floating limp. Iâ€™m guessing pigs probably donâ€™t swim too well even when they arenâ€™t hogtied. I also briefly reconsidered my love of bacon, but Iâ€™ve managed to get past that.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2309,'<p>In a time when dinosaurs walked the earth<br>\r\nWhen the land was swamp and caves were home<br>\r\nIn an age when prize possession was fire<br>\r\nTo search for landscapes men would roam</p>\r\n<p>Then the tribes they came to steal their fire<br>\r\nAnd the wolves they howled into the night<br>\r\nAs they fought a vicious angry battle<br>\r\nto save the power of warmth and light.</p>\r\n<p>Drawn by quest for fire<br>\r\nThey searched all through the land<br>\r\nDrawn by quest for fire<br>\r\nDiscovery of man.</p><i>- Steve Harris</i>\r\n<center><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2309_1.pjpeg\"></center>\r\n'),(2310,'so we watched Mothman Prophecies tonite...yea yea...we only went cuz it was a free screening for poly students....anyway sorta scared me...but in a funny way....like is richard gere doomed to dumb ass movies? should i send him a sympathy card? damn\r\n\r\nwhat im really scared of are clowns. think about it...why do they always wear makeup? why are they always smiling? CUZ THEY GOT SOMETHING TO HIDE.\r\n\r\nfor awhile i was scared of what was gonna happen after i graduated...where i was gonna work...where i was gonna live...do i care? yea i still do...can\'t lie...am i gonna freak out right now and start wearing my interview clothes everyday? hell no...i got rum to drink and a life...\r\n\r\njust no clowns. please'),(2312,'<p>I think that I learned too much today for my head is throbbing.  Little food input and my contacts were being obtrusive rather than transparent (both in look and feel), like they should be.</p>\r\n<p>I\'ve had a dull throbbing headache for most of the day.  The kind where it only hurts when you blink.  It seems okay at first, but try really hard to see how long you can actually not blink.  After you reach your own breaking point, a frenzy of eyelid fluttering ensues.</p>\r\n<p>So, the headache and learning too much.  After a rather long day at school and heading to work to finish reading Generation-X (so that I could get on with my life and do some school work), I was walking home listening to some minimal techno, all quiet like, so as to appease the headache monsters in my head.</p>\r\n<p>It was \'misting\' outside.  A slight smell of ozone and high moisture content in the air making street lights become highly diffused and their darkness-cutting cones become visible to the naked eye.  Everything was cool and wet and I was looking at the trees.  With all the photons moving around through the moist, ionic air, they looked hyperreal.  They were almost too perfect and I looked at them not as a natural formation but more as a mathematical abstract.  A computer-generated visual application of a theoretical algorithm.  A perfect-to-the-point-of-not-being-too-perfect fractal creation.  With just the right amount of genetically induced deviation so that the human brain doesn\'t feel like it is being deceived.</p>\r\n<p>That\'s the one problem with photo-realistic computer generated graphics.  They often look too perfect.  Most objects In Real Life(TM) have imperfctions and the eye is so used to them that they are automagically erased from perception.  This is the same phenomenon as mentally erasing telephone/hydro poles from your mind when looking at a street.  They are something you just expect to be there and do not waste time contemplating.</p>\r\n<p>It is the absense of the natural imperfections in objects which triggers alarms in your brain.  Your visual cortex lights up and you screw your face up trying to figure out what is wrong, without being able to put your finger on it.</p>\r\n<p>Tonight, while walking down the misty street, I took in my surroundings in terms of a 3D OpenGL application.  I thought of lighting component co-efficients and textures and polygons and I had one of those moments where you have to remind yourself that the things around you are real and not existing within a computer system.</p>\r\n<p>Things always look much nicer if you can break them down into a yes/no binary logic.</p>\r\n<p>I think that I learned too much today for my head is throbbing.</p>\r\n'),(2314,'<P>it must be the christmas episode<BR>the one where they left the little boy alone.<BR>they all went off to pidgeon forge<BR>like it\'s some kind of redneck valhalla.<BR><BR>he took<BR>one anna jarring<BR>one eduardo stokes<BR>to port of tampa<BR>to meet a turkish freighter.<BR>how did they look<BR>climbing the gangway<BR>the three of them?<BR>(2 1/2 of them? 2 1/9?)<BR>sad, mad, glad,<BR>or fearful?<BR>he didn\'t know.<BR>couldn\'t see their faces.<BR><BR>for them:<BR>5 and a half weeks<BR>of saltwater and gestation.<BR>stops in:<BR>pascagoula, galveston, tampico, veracruz,<BR>port royal, el bluff, cartagena, fortaleza,<BR>recife, salvador, rio de janeiro,<BR>monrovia, freetown, conakry, bissau,<BR>dakar, nouakchott, port etienne, arrecife,<BR>funchal, cadiz.<BR>trains to locarno.<BR>\"see you later on,\" they said.<BR><BR>for him:<BR>back in the dodge,<BR>back to the base.<BR>lasted one night<BR>in the empty house.<BR>all the lights on,<BR>woods full of coyotes,<BR>drank the cooking sherry.<BR>then went out for a case of grolsch.<BR>blasted \"the planets\".<BR>nothing on tv that late except<BR>\"charles in charge.\"<BR><BR>decided he\'d be better<BR>off in the city,<BR>took the greyhound<BR>back to tampa.<BR>left no traces.<BR>stops in:<BR>dade city,<BR>zephyrhills.<BR>checked into the Society room<BR>at whitledge hall.<BR><BR>whitledge hall<BR>burns down every night.<BR>just this side of flophouse.<BR>fleabag hotel.<BR>they take down your info<BR>to see if you\'re wanted.<BR>all the doors open into the hallway,<BR>so it\'s really no use to barricade.<BR><BR>installed a porcelain figure<BR>of the laughing buddha<BR>in a corner of room two-ten.<BR>mirror and a ceiling fan.<BR>basin and no a/c.<BR>clean sheets, baby.<BR>this room is one of the few<BR>with a bathtub and toilet.<BR>bare light bulb with a pull chain.<BR>no screens in the windows.<BR>found a love letter<BR>in a drawer<BR>from \"mildred\" to \"herschel\".<BR><BR>nominally, he\'s here to write.<BR>but you know he makes his own<BR>distractions.<BR>first night there<BR>amuses himself<BR>by removing every hair from his body.<BR>you\'d have to ask him why.<BR>shaves head and eyebrows,<BR>face and body.<BR>saves the pubes for last.<BR>the triangle goes easy.<BR>it\'s the balls you want to be careful with.<BR>used a little pair of scissors.<BR>no trauma, no blood.<BR>finished up behind the scroat,<BR>thought he was done.<BR>realized he