><BR>go back in to use<BR>the bathroom.<BR>think about an english professor<BR>i used to have<BR>who drank at the same bar in st. pete<BR>as jack kerouac did.<BR>this was in the late \'60s,<BR>right before he died.<BR>said he\'d go in for a piss,<BR>and kerouac would be standing in front of the mirror,<BR>admiring himself.<BR>he\'d say,<BR>\"not since valentino died,<BR>has there been such a beauty.\"<BR><BR>two nights later:<BR>new year\'s eve.<BR>alone in the room at whitledge hall.<BR>count it down on my watch.<BR>they say whatever you\'re doing at midnight,<BR>you\'ll be doing all year long.<BR>with a minute to spare, drunk, i start laughing.<BR>i regard the buddha laughing in the corner,<BR>little porcelain children crawling all over him.<BR>still laughing as the second hand crosses the twelve.<BR><BR>next day, wake up<BR>to the cell phone ringing,<BR>the bat signal.<BR>it\'s mom.<BR>on their way back from tennessee.<BR>they all have the flu,<BR>need me to be the nursemaid once they get home.<BR>i don\'t get sick that often.</P>'),(2313,'28000 cross the Bering land bridge<br>\r\n10000 hunt large Ice animals<br>\r\n8000 ocean levels rise<br>\r\n4000 hunt and gatherer on the Plains<br>\r\n100 B.C. farm<br>\r\n100 A.D. Anasazi emerge<br>\r\n500 trade pearls, copper, shells<br>\r\n1000 build mud pueblos <br> \r\n1300 leave cliff dwellings<br>\r\n1490 speak 300 different languages<br>\r\n1492 begin the hustle<br>'),(2316,'<p>\r\ni\'ve entered springtime a little earlier than everyone else around me.  the weather is grey and dreary today but i feel like my body is finally melting all its snow and attracting butterflies.  pregnancy shouldn\'t last 9 months.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m falling in love every 5 minutes.  i want to pollinate the world.  everyone is so sexy.  i can barely contain myself.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy pants are very magical lately.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\njeremy is holed up in his room with his desktop projected six feet tall on the wall while he tries to force his new laptop to duel boot windows xp and linux.  i think i can hear him crying.  if you were to ask him, he wouldn\'t be able to tell you why he is depressed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe says he wants to scream and he doesn\'t know why.  he says he\'s just going to do one last install.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe kids and i have been snuggling under my bed.  we put flower stickers all over the baby and laugh when he doesn\'t seem to notice.  sierra demands 5 uno games in a row.  i show no mercy in trouncing her.  better at home than on the streets.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsierra gets tired of playing with us and wanders off to play with her toys.  it used to be that i would go into her room and see a definate lab theme.  many of her experiments involved dissolving jelly beans in toilet water which she kept in vials on the windowsill. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbefore that she was counterfeiting money.  i\'d find replicas of my credit cards drawn in crayon and stashed at the bottom of her underwear drawer.  we\'d go places and when she caught me admiring something she\'d offer to buy it.  i should have let her, just to see what would happen.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe\'s moved on to the mortuary sciences now.  i could go into it, but it would be awfully hard to explain.  needless to say, several of her toys are reposing in state.  i pass by them and say \"wow.  they all look so life-like.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\njeremy comes into my room and i rip a random back hair off of him:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>me:</b> this is how long your back hair is now.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> oh jeeze, all of it?<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> yeah.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> (pointing at a spot on his back) is this something?<br>\r\n<b>me:</b>  yeah.  it\'s a zit.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> do you think you could -<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> no.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> all the other guys have girlfriends who -<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> no, they don\'t.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> you don\'t love me.<br>\r\n<b>me:</b>  you\'re right.  i\'ve often asked myself if i loved you enough to pop your zits.  i guess we\'ve answered that question.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> if you pop the zit i\'ll let you rip out more back hair.<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> no.<br>\r\n<b>jeremy:</b> i guess i\'m just going to have to deny you the pleasure, then.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neveryone is gathered under my bed now reading comic books.  sierra shares her coveted tick stash with jeremy.  between panels we discuss the impermanence of teeth.  ezekiel has two white nubs straining to break through his gums.  sierra\'s mouth is like a grand central station of teeth with arrivals and departures every few days or so.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy mom called yesterday to wish me merry christmas.  in mom-time, she\'s early.  her message was long and rattling and included her thoughts on the quest for perfect breasts. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have glimpsed my future. \r\n</p>'),(2315,'There was an odd sort of machine on the third floor of the jail.  When a prisoner had a broken jaw it was wired shut.  If a prisoner were to vomit while their jaw was wired shut, then they would drown.  The machine on the third floor of the jail was used for the specific purpose of sucking vomit from the mouths of prisoners.\r\n\r\n<p>The vomit sucking machine was kept on the third floor of the jail because that is where the female prisoners were kept too.  The only people who had broken jaws were women.  The women had broken jaws because the men in their lives hit them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The men who were held in the jail for domestic battery were not good inmates.  Domestic battery was a misdemeanor.  Serious criminals were felons.  The felons knew the criminal system and the correctional system better than most jailors did.  Life in a correctional facility is nothing if it is not routine; knowing the routine is a good thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Domination and submission was the theme.  Jailors wear shiny, black leather and give orders.  Jailors shout orders and put restraints on people.  Jailors handle cocks, and balls, and asses.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Inevitably, the men held in the jail for domestic battery were accustomed to manipulating their partners, and they attempted to continue their practices within the jail.  It is true that some felons employed force and the threat of force and deceit.  But, there is something fundamentally different about the character of a man who is willing to engage in an armed confrontation with another muscular adult male who is similarly armed, from the type of \"man\" who is willing to strike the gentler members of his own household.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some people enter their professions in order to gratify their desires in a socially acceptable way - some surgeons have an un-natural desire to touch and cut human tissues.  Some jailors behave in a manner very much like the people who practice domestic battery; they even laugh knowingly when engaging in discussions with the men who commit domestic battery.  It is a cowardly thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some sort of mechanism for correctly metting out punishment is missing in the American system; it is missing from most systems.  We seem to practice the wrong type of tolerance.  Vendeta is a difficult art.</p> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2318,'the whole day is ahead.  this happens seven times a week.  i wake up and have the entire day.  i do not feel like writing.  i feel like drinking a cup of coffee.  i feel like eating french toast.  goatie is driving over from the biggity biggity o.  meanwhile, pendleton is testing his claws on my chair.  you see, im at qq and xep\'s place.  this has been my home away from home for the weekend.  my getaway.  i have a parking spot directly out front of the box.  for breakfast i\'ll go to kate\'s kitchen like last weekend and dig around in some french toast.  maybe i will even get the one with granola and yogurt, and fruit on top.  right now pendleton is sitting strait up, alternately sleeping and staring at me with half eyes.   why wont he go back to bed?  maybe i fooled him into thinking it was time to wake up.  pendleton does not look happy when he is awake.  he looks like he is fighting the waking hours.  plump, milky, pendleton, with a pink schnoz.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin the next room, bcm sleeps.  i wake him up for breakfast and after three knocks he grunts.  after three more knocks he tries to sound awake, \"what is it?\" i tell him it is time for breakfast, \"No thanks.\" then five minutes later he is getting in the shower.  things do not sound great to him when he is asleep and then a few minutes later they sound fine.  which is good probably because maybe we will go to the park after and he made it clear yesterday that he would like to spend more time walking in the park.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n im hungry. i had better head on down there and wait for goat.  also i am hungry.'),(2317,'<p>it is a slight thing<br>\r\nrepetition and rhythm. <br>\r\nyou and i as one</p>\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2319,'<p>\r\nphone call from gord set the wheels in motion.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nweird dreams had been occuring all week. dreams about sex, dead bodies, various pink coloured things(things not always meant to be pink. ie. motorbikes), icb, sf, ottz, libya, dubya, and all sorts of other things im sure i dont remember. what i do remember is that my mom woke me up right in the middle of one of these dreams. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was in a mad max style dune buggy with a crew of about eight. from what i can remember it was some sort of race. a race somewhat resembling the plot of running man. we had just stopped at a gas station to fill up. for some reason it took a long time; people got split up-we had to \'git\'. i found myself stuck in a maze. perfect walls had been created using crumpled up cars. i found myself driving in circles with no place to go. my crew began heckling; throwing tomatoes and bottles at me. after shooting three of them i sensed my mom opening the door. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"david, there is a phone call for you.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nphone calls are the perfect way for a parent to get their sibblings up at noon. even when i refuse the phone call my mom will refuse to let me refuse. on the hard days it becomes quite the scene to those listening in on the other end. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"eye ohhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhh. sup davey!?\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntalked to gord for a bit on the phone while i began the wakeup routine. after opening my eyes i usually get out of bed and move my mouse, clicking the left button once to turn the monitor on. putting on the pants and socks from the day before i then head upstairs and have a cig outside in the driveway. glass of juice from the kitchen, piss, brush teeth, check email. add a shower in there and you have daves patented thirty minute wakeup routine.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncall dan and in three minutes arrange a meeting thirty minutes later. heading to the gas station with dan behind the wheel we find that the station is very busy. nine pumps full with nine cars waiting. we snag a nice spot behind an audi whose at the pump. three aisles down is a lady cleaning her rear window with a squeegee.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nchit chattin with dan for a bit i notice the lady is cleaning her rear hood. she is so meticulous with the squeegee. when she finally moves to the bumper i suddenly flip out. there were nine cars waiting to get gas and this lady has the gaul to start cleaning her car with a squeegee at a station that has a carwash!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni ask dan politely to let me yell at the lady. after sucessfully defending his window switch three times i gave up on any further direct action. Instead i reverted to venting all sorts of hidden frustration; masking it in my almost immediate hatred of this woman. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni became quite calm when the lady finally put the squeegee away and went to pay for her gas. we had by then pulled upto the pump; dan escaping to the task of pumping gas. in the back of my head i feared him doing the same thing with the squeegee just to spite me, but he did not. instead we  enjoyed a delightful, but short, drive through car wash.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe end. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2320,''),(2321,'<p>\r\nsierra is in the car.  she\'s been down there for over an hour.   i look out of my window and down into the carport and see her curled up on the front passenger seat singing into her comic book.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went down there a few minutes ago.  she has assured me that she is not ready to come in yet.  okay.  as long as she is happy.  and doesnt\' touch the emergency brake.  or gear shift.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nezekiel sits in my lap and watches me type this.  occasionally, he throws himself sideways and i catch him before he tips over.  he does it all without blinking, looking right at me.  how can anyone be so sure of being caught?  the trust is like icecubes down your pants. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce, i took some e at an outdoor ravey thing.  after i get past that uncomfortable stage where i think i\'m going to die of a brain aneurysm and i\'m no longer obsessing about keeping my heartrate as far away from the bmp that is attacking me from the speakers as i can, i get a little chatty.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\'d smeared myself across the grass and was gazing up at the stars in amazement.  the feeling i had at that moment was one i hadn\'t felt since i was a little girl playing in the creek behind my house.  it was just a dirty trickle, but it felt like the entire universe.  i had done battle against endless foes and won fame and love by that nasty water.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsuddenly, i had to know all about the adventure places of everyone around me.  it lead to some pretty amazing conversations with people i hardly knew.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you remember the places where you were king? \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2325,'<p>It is my dad\'s birthday today.  Talked to my mom on the phone and she reminded me of what I already knew.  There was a huge full moon last night.  I saw it here in California, out of the car window driving down the freeway with Ashley from the gym.  My mom saw it on her way home, driving up Route 4 in Vermont, with a great gash of jet exhaust plumes drawing a glowing frame of white beside it.  She seemed to be saying it meant something, at least to her, like it was a prescient symbol, a thing of beauty sent back to us from beyond.  She didn\'t say it in so many words, but she\'s been on the lookout for signs and symbols.  I suppose I have, too, but I haven\'t been seeing any.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'d say I\'ve probably been a bit depressed lately.  Friends have commented in the past that I\'m a pretty balanced person, even-keeled.  Depressions for me don\'t last too long and are never too severe.  When my Libra-rising scales get a bit askew, that\'s all it is, a slight imbalance, nothing dramatic.  I\'m drinking a lot, which has been pretty par for the course this year.  I haven\'t been waking up in any pools of my own sick, but it probably doesn\'t help.  I\'m not sleeping excessively, keep going to the gym every day because there isn\'t much else to do, and I\'m not over-eating, though I\'d like to sometimes.  No, depressive periods tend to be pretty mild in my world.  Just some glum days, mostly unproductive, and even though a friend is taking me to Hawaii in a few days I can\'t get very excited about it.  I\'m clinging a lot.  I sound like my mother when I ask Ashley where he\'s going, will he be home for dinner.  We\'re doomed to become our parents, at least in some ways, and I\'m big Momma around here.  Invasive, passive aggressive, cleanly.  But there\'s no job to go to or complain about, no love interests to obsess over.  Just a series of similar days, introspective and slow, but not lonely really, as long as somebody\'s there to cling to, which can be a heavy burden to bear sometimes, I guess, but Ashley\'s a good sport, and he has his clinging times, too.  The world doesn\'t hold a lot of promise lately, but it probably will as soon as I see Waimea Canyon, the beaches of Kauai, and smell some South Pacific air.  Lows like these often strike in the winter.  Seasonal-Effective Disorder.  And it\'s the fourth stage of mourning, right?  Except I don\'t think I experienced the other three.  </P>\r\n\r\n<p>My birthday\'s in a couple of weeks and Amy, my Piscean pal, won\'t be around.  Maybe I\'ll make a chocolate soufflÃ© and eat it in front of the TV.  Maybe we\'ll go bowling, Ashley and Arwa and I, and whoever wants to join.  We can each dress as our favorite Big Lebowski  character and get soused while rolling gutter balls and strikes, carelessly and without skill.  It\'s my mom\'s birthday, too, in a few weeks but she doesn\'t want to go anywhere.  She\'s got new stresses.  Managing debts and bills on her own.  It will take time to resolve and it\'s become like a new, burdensome pet she can\'t abandon.  She won\'t be able to relax, she says, and besides, she has no money for a vacation.  Not now. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>All this moaning would really piss my dad off.  It isn\'t his fault that I\'m depressed, of course.  He was quite considerate, really, hurrying along the dying process so it wouldn\'t infringe too much on Christmas.  It was tough, sure, maybe felt a little strange on Christmas morning without him, but it wasn\'t so bad.  Just unbalancing, maybe, surreal.  An absence you can\'t quite explain.  And then a long series of uncomfortable pauses when people hear the news and give the requisite apologies.  Now it\'s time to pull together and make life anew, but it\'s pretty chilly out.  I kind of want cookies, and some drugs to numb me, and a series of diverting videos which I luckily happen to have rented and are waiting for me in the next room.  </P>\r\n\r\n<p>Overly busy people are envious of those with such control over their days, people like me who can write self-indulgent personal essays like this and read books and watch videos and go to the gym.  On a whole, I should be thankful.  But the chemicals are off a bit.  Seratonin.  Some slight imbalances in the mixture, a vague pall over the world, the apartment.  </P>\r\n\r\n<p>Lulu\'s coming to get the cat the day after tomorrow.  He\'s shat upon his last duvet cover.  He meows and whines at us from his food bowl, like, \"Don\'t you understand?  My life is one long series of torments.  All I want is some of the canned food in my dry food like you gave to the dog.  And I want you to stop staying up till all hours.  And I want to understand what love is, because I never have, because I was abused as a kitten, or so they tell me.  And I want you to keep that fucking dog away from me.\"  He\'s going to become an outdoor cat and maybe get eaten by coyotes in the wilds of outer Los Angeles.  \"That\'s just what he needs,\" Brian says.  \"He needs a dose of fear in his life, a fight for survival.  Then maybe he\'ll realize what\'s important and stop shitting on beds.\"  Maybe, but some folks don\'t ever realize what\'s important, or they stay wrapped up in circular thoughts, in money worries and other crap until the day they die.  And lives get left unfinished, because there is never a clear beginning or end to things, unfinished business is the way of the world, and the cat is just another lost soul without a clue. </P>\r\n'),(2327,'<P>\r\nhas this happened to anyone else?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nwhen we moved here last june, we brought with us a small, simple, peach-colored telephone with a spirally cord.  he\'d had it before i even came into the picture, and it was always there to help us, or wake us up at 8 am with solicitors.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nanyway.  after a few months of living here, the phone started becoming senile.  we would push a key and nothing would happen.  we would push many keys and one would stick and it would keep beeping until we hung up.  sometimes a key push would register two beeps, like sticky keys on a keyboard.  finally, it was useless for dialing out.  we had to buy a replacement.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nthe replacement phone is black, and on the top there\'s a caller id screen.  at the time it was on sale for five dollars.  our now-totally-psychotic peach phone was banished to the living room, where we could still use it to take an incoming call.  the new black phone had the place of honor in the bedroom.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nquick segue to the present.  i spent yesterday through last night unpacking, plugging in, and de-crackhead-ing sam\'s new computer.  (i\'d go into how completely stupid i think windows xp is to anyone with half a brain, but that\'s for another day.)  to do all the unhooking of sam\'s old computer (which had also become senile and rather useless) and rehooking of his new computer, the phone line that connected the now-somewhat-new black phone to the answering machine and the computer was disconnected and then reconnected.  sam was away at the time, and i had a question for him, so i picked up mister dependable black phone and tried to dial the cell phone number.  no sound, then the phone seemingly hung up on itself without me touching a thing.  the next time i tried, it freaked out even more.  it took nearly ten tries for the crackhead to understand the seven digits i was giving it enough to dial me through to sam.  my goodness, thought i, what happened?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\ntoday i was at work (though i didn\'t work today, bleh) and decided to call sam to see how he was.  as soon as he picked up the phone (no dialing on his part, mind you), i heard a mind-jarring series of beeps, then the phone hung up on me.  child of satan, i thought as i fumed over not being able to use a rather simple and normally highly dependable machine to speak with whom i wished to speak to.  tried again and the phone behaved itself enough to let me talk to sam for a coupla minutes.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nmy question: why the heck is this happening?  this never happened to any of the crappy phones i bought for my bedroom at home, or to any phones my family owned (and believe me, there were a few), or even my roomates\' and friends\' phones (that i know of).  so what does this all mean?  is there something wrong with the telephone jacks here?  could some sort of strange telephone-surge fuck up our phones in this way?  and is it really worth calling up the phone manufacturers over a warranty for a five-dollar phone?\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nbeh.  just when you think about how nice modern technology is, the not-so-modern stuff freaks out.\r\n</P>'),(2326,'<p>It\'s bright and the workers are all coked up, jazzed about nothing in particular. They are loud, they\'re singing along with Spandau Ballet and Howard Jones. They\'re razzing each other about previous events. I question their ability to make me a safe and tasty hamburger, but I place my order and leave it to fate and sit down at the red formica table, on the green vinyl booth bench.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I like my burger medium, with american cheese. I like lettuce and mayo, mustard, and less than half of the onion slice. I eat the pickles separate. I eat slow. I shove fries into my mouth between burger bites. I drink water instead of paying for a coke. I use two napkins, and no straw.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Through the window I watch a guy in a pink overstuffed backpack cross the wet street. It rained earlier in the day, but now it\'s slowed to a soft drizzle and the black top has kept its glossy car commercial sheen. The silence between us vibrates somewhere between pregnant and uncomfortable. I let it sit, keeping my head cocked to look out toward the street. I twirl a greasy french fry. I hide a mess of ketchup, mustard, and onion on my plate with a napkin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I keep staring out at the sidewalk until the silence is uncomfortable without question. Later when we leave weâ€™ll walk too slow and prolong the inevitable but, it wont be until the next time I see you that we become close enough to say anything. That same night weâ€™ll become too distant to say more.</p>\r\n'),(2328,'I would like my cock back, please. When I was mentally castrated or psychically neutered in the most recent attempt at an intimate relation, my unit was left behind. Unlike the borrowed sweater or book that may hardly  be worth the awkwardness neccessary to get it returned, this is something that is important to me. I can see it now, the bloody stump testicles and all lying in the corner of her room - most likely buried and forgotten under some pile of dirty laundry.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\'ve only really lost it once before and I had to order a factory replacement, that took the better part of a year to be shipped to me. How you might ask does a mid twenties male misplace his unit? This problem tends to arise when the mid twenties male in question detaches the unit, so as to better facilitate othermeans of interaction with their intimate partner. It is not removed in every relationship (I\'ll be honest here) but in ones were some hope and desire of more then just the connecting of two pieces in a live flesh puzzle, exists. As you really don\'t have a lot of uses for the unit, while out on your own, in these situations it tends to be left lying around the places you and your intimate partner use it. This is how if you are not careful you end up losing it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI make no apologies - I like possessing and weilding my unit, it gives me a fair amount of pleasure and some others have said it wasn\'t that bad for them either. <br><br>\r\n\r\nNow upon my realizing that I had lost my unit I became very distraught. In a brutal misjudgement on my part I rushed down to the local pharmacy and grabbed one of the temporary models (located right next to the disposable razors). Needless to say as soon as I found myself in a situation to use the temporary unit, I realized why it was called a one shot model.<br><br>\r\nMy misjudgement becoming clear to me I decided to forsake the temporary models entirely. Unfortunately, the retrieval of the real deal does not look likely to happen. I\'ve already sent away to the factory for another replacement unit. I can\'t negotiate the return of the lost unit, as I fear she would find it and then mail it back to me peacemeal like some twisted scene out of a ganster movie.<br><br>\r\nWhen I get my factory replacement unit, hopefully it doesn\'t take a full year, I\'m not taking it off for a good long time. It seems that everytime I take it off I end up losing it along with other more important things.<br>  '),(2329,'<p>I\'ve been feeling alienated from the Swinney crowd for awhile.  Haven\'t been reading, haven\'t been writing.  Maybe it\'s all the new people but it\'s probably more due to the fact that I\'ve been feeling alienated from humanity in general, especially since New Year\'s.  My roommate keeps bugging me to snap out of my depression (she even invited a bunch of her friends over this weekend in an effort to force me to be social), but I\'m not depressed; I just don\'t feel like being social; people don\'t interest me anymore.</p><p>  I spent most of last year trying to make friends, trying to meet new people, trying to get laid.  I overcame (somewhat) some serious interactive inadequacies, an almost complete lack of social skills.  I fucked some models.  I took some drugs.  Attended some hip parties.  Rode in a limo.  Made some interesting friends.  (I even made one or two really good friends and reestablished some old connections that I had allowed to languish.)  It was a busy year filled with full-time employment, dance classes, and plans every Friday and Saturday night.</p><p>and now...</p><p>Well, I guess I\'m just tired.  I\'m not a party animal.  I\'m not a friendly, interesting person.  I\'m not \"cool.\"  I\'m sick of giving people fake smiles and insincerely friendly salutations.  I don\'t want to listen to some trust fund baby tell me bullshit stories about rafting down the Salmon River in Idaho.  <i>I don\'t give a fuck that Glenn Close just moved in next door to you!</i></p><p>When it comes down to it, I\'m a misanthropic, depressed loner.  I don\'t like people and people don\'t like me.  There\'s too much maintenance involved in keeping relationships, too much drama, too much bullshit.  I\'d rather sit alone in a darkened room making love to my computer.</p><p>Heh... maybe I <i>am</i> a little depressed.</p>'),(2330,'<p>Someone across the way was screaming last night.  It was a long sustained scream, high pitched, but not necessarily a woman\'s scream.  It would go on for about twenty seconds, then stop while whoever it was caught their breath, and then it would start again.  A disconcerting sound to say the least.\r\n\r\n<p>I got up and went to the window.  \r\n\r\n<p>\"What is it?\"  My wife asked sleepily. \r\n\r\n<p>\"Someone across the way is screaming.\"  I told her.\r\n\r\n<p>\"Why are they screaming?\"\r\n\r\n<p>\"I don\'t know.\"  I told her.  \"Why would I know that?\"\r\n\r\n<p>The building across the courtyard was dark save for one window in the upper right corner where the light was on.  Judging from the sound, I didn\'t think the screaming was coming from there.  I stood in the window for awhile, staring hard at the darkened windows.  Who could be screaming?  Why?\r\n\r\n<p>My attention was suddenly drawn to the upper right corner of the building across the way.  A woman in a filmy negligee had moved into view and she looked across at our building with a certain curiosity.  With the light behind her, I could make out the lines of her body under the thin fabric.  She had a long, lean torso.  Her breasts were full and round.  Her blonde hair seemed to glow.\r\n\r\n<p>â€What is going on out there?â€  My wife asked again, sitting up in bed.  She was very much awake now.\r\n\r\n<p>â€Someone is screaming.  How could I know more than that.  Someone is screaming.  It woke me up.â€  The woman in the negligee moved away from the window.\r\n\r\n<p>â€Should we call the police?â€  \r\n\r\n<p>â€I suppose so.â€\r\n\r\n<p>My wife took the phone from the bedside table and dialed 911.  And the screaming stopped.\r\n\r\n<p>â€Itâ€™s over.â€\r\n\r\n<p>My wife hung up the phone.  â€œHow do we know it wonâ€™t start again?â€\r\n\r\n<p>â€I guess we donâ€™t know.â€  I said.  The light turned off in the upper right apartment.  I pulled the curtains closed and came back to my wife.  â€œHow would we know that?â€\r\n\r\n<p>I got into bed with my wife.  She snuggled up to me, put her head on my shoulder and promptly fell asleep.  \r\n\r\n<p>I lay on my back with my eyes open, blinking into the darkness.\r\n'),(2331,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2334_1.pjpeg\"> \r\n\r\n'),(2335,'where I come from,<br>\r\nit does not snow.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut I remember her<br>\r\nblinking snowflakes from her lashes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis morning I saw a woman<br>\r\non the subway<br>\r\nplatform that looks like<br>\r\nI remember Mary.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmemory is a trick of time.<br>\r\nit is not something that <br>\r\nI can get up and trail away from, leaving<br>\r\nglasses and <br>\r\nlipstick cigarettes<br>\r\nbehind.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit is wound around my waist and tangled<br>\r\nin my hair, it drags <br>\r\nboxes and books, bottles and rags<br>\r\nalong with me.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nBut,<br>\r\nthe rings I wore<br>\r\nare tarnished now.  the passages<br>\r\nin the books have different meanings.<br>\r\nand, while everything is still there,<br>\r\nthe contents of the boxes are disordered.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I seek it, <br>\r\nI can\'t seem to find what I\'m looking for, and<br>\r\nwhen I\'m looking for something else<br>\r\nI find a bauble or memory that was elusive<br>\r\njust yesterday.<br>\r\nBut, upon finding it, I forget why I wanted it so badly.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo yes,<br>\r\nMary could have snowflakes <br>\r\nin her lashes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt makes perfect sense.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor that is the way I loved her.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIsn\'t it?<br>'),(2332,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã \r\n				\r\n	\r\n\r\n