orida, and soon after shot his twin brother for cheating with said woman. His spinstress wife in tow, he drifted around the Southeast, eventually moving to the Tennessee Valley where he birthed a bastard son, my father\'s father, who would eventually become a fiery Southern pentecostal preacher, forbiddenly marry outside his sordid dogmatic congregation, and birth my father, who would be raised in the rather bizarrely influential detritus of Southern congregationalism at its most beguiling.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBreaking free from such sequestered locales and closed-minded paradigms, my father would soon happen across my mother, artist and globe-trotting hippie granddaughter of a fiddlemaker and a seamstresses from the wilds of Louisiana and Rural Arkansas, freshly after the submission of her first husband to the agony of Hodgekins disease in the rural township of Mapo-dong, Mapo-ku, just out side of Seoul, Korea in 1971. My father had been her late husband\'s best friend in medical school, and his post-mortem condolences to my mother blossomed into rebound love. They settled in Chattanooga.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFast-forward a few years, skip over my father\'s inconsistent victories over manic depression and mysogeny, my parents two divorces and subsequent make-ups, my father\'s incarceration, and I was born; a peace offering to their reunification, the only son of two only children. Undoubtedly, all I got from my great-grandfather Americus was a genetic propensity to have large hands, feet and head. <br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter seven more years of battling the recurring demons that had plagued the earlier years of their relationship, my parents permanently parted in 1984.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSubsequently, growing up amidst my fellow 5,000 inhabitants in the one-police-car-town of Red Bank, Tennessee was nice. I learned both the reward of personal responsibility and the work-ethic that stems from being raised by a single Mom; this was especially so during the years she went back to school to get her degree and teaching certification. My school years were rather idyllic and uneventfully peaceful; weekend outings ranging from the nearby comparitively urban area of Chattanooga to the secluded virgin hardwood forests and pocket wildernesses of Dayton, weekends of work on my rusty $500 car to get it running again for the week ahead, acres of yard to while away hours with pets and friends, creeks that overflowed after the annual spring rains.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI an proud to have very few regrets in my life; I can count them on one hand, or rather, on a finger or two. I\'ve never regretted my upbringing, this much is certain. I\'ve certainly cherished the memorable vignettes of wisdom and experience that growing up down South afforded me. But, by the time college beckoned, I set my sights towards the attraction of urban living, towards institutions that could more effectively instill in me the trait of Universal Thinking that my Mom had tried so hard to embed in me amidst the constantly evolving but sometimes odd-thinking South.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI picked one of the best universities New York City had to offer, based on the fact that it had odd patches of grass on its campus as well as river-access- a rowing team that would allow me to quench my years-long habit of rowing, daily, watching the sun rise, and smelling those organically pleasing <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1060\">river smells</a>.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter the denouement of graduation, I returned to Tennesee for a year to teach and make my post-New York peace with my hometown. My responsibility fulfilled and the siren-song of my personal desires screaming with a din to drown out all other reasoning, I returned to New York City...<br><br>\r\n\r\n...where I\'ve been ever since, sitting daily at a job that allows me both the time and equipment to write stories like these for the world to read.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2334,'ag'),(2336,'<p>Uneventful hamburgers (and cheeseburgers) are a recurring theme in my life. I\'d like to provide some background. This will be all the explanation you get when it comes to uneventful burgers and tomatoes and fish. This was probably written in March of 1992. It is a <a href=\" http://kalliope.hypermart.net/pantoum.html\">pantoum</a>, every line is repeated. I was twenty years old, so you\'ll forgive me if it\'s a little melodramatic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>The Importance of Tomatoes and Fish</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was a cheeseburger night<br>\r\nwhen I gave you the importance of tomatoes and fish<br>\r\nYou sat next to me breathing<br>\r\nand I thought you might like to suck up some darkness.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I gave you the importance of tomatoes and fish<br>\r\nDid you give me the weight of steel? <br>\r\nI thought you might like to suck up some darkness<br>\r\nor play catch with the moon. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Did you give me the weight of steel<br>\r\nas we sat not touching under stark fluorescents? <br>\r\nWould you play catch with the moon, <br>\r\nor just sit and watch the T.V. flash? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>As we sat not touching under stark fluorescents <br>\r\nyou didn\'t look at me, <br>\r\njust sat and watched the T.V. flash. <br>\r\nI wanted to yank you out into the darkness. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>You didn\'t look at me. <br>\r\nI thought how nice it would be to arch with the willows, <br>\r\nI wanted to yank you out into the darkness<br>\r\nto where the tall flowers wavered in serenades. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I thought how nice it would be to arch with the willows, <br>\r\nbut you just sat there not even aware<br>\r\nof how the tall flowers wavered in serenades. <br>\r\nI guess you were afraid of the dark. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>You just sat there not even aware<br>\r\nthat I sat next to you breathing. <br>\r\nI guess you were afraid of the dark<br>\r\non a cheeseburger night. </p>\r\n'),(2337,'For your perusal and responses:\r\n\r\n\"The power in forgiving goes to the forgiver, not the forgiven\"'),(2338,'<p>turmoil continues, eddying around me and confounding every attempt at order.  one thing can be said for turmoil though, it makes one feel more deeply.  every emotion is lit up in ten-foot-tall neon letters, glowing and obliterating the scenery.  every mood swing tosses me around like the overgrown kid from grade nine gym class.  it rips my gym shorts to shreds.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>i\'ve been thinking about recent events, trying to shape them into an episode of \"this is your life.\"  no luck.  the flow of time eludes me.  i can\'t see a clear beginning, middle or end, and at this rate, i\'ll never make it on the show.  for example, how do i explain that i saw two cars driving the wrong way up my one-way street, in reverse, and i marvelled at human stupidity.  being in reverse somehow makes it okay?  i\'ve been thinking about that for weeks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the rest of my life is similarly fragmented:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>garth is moving to calgary and i couldn\'t even telephone him.  well, i could have, but i didn\'t.  i felt bad about not calling, but the receiver was always just out of reach.  and when he finally called me, i could hear the hurt in his voice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>nadine is terminally ill.  i found out the day after i met her, prancing about in a hawaiian lei and grass skirt.  she certainly didn\'t seem to be sick, but the video of hawaiian underwater eruptions made her exclaim that the ocean was bleeding.  we went to take our drivers license test together the next day.  it seemed an odd priority for someone who\'s dying, but i\'m glad she went.  afterwards, i stood beside her at the MTO payphone while she broke up with her lover.  after dinner, i watched as she had her nipple pierced, and then fell head over heels for the piercer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jenie came back to toronto for the weekend, ostensibly visiting me.  friday things were just like they used to be.  we were in a time warp that lasted until i left her house, out the front door into the chilly morning air.  the rest of the weekend was a reality check, struggling to reconcile the past and present.  we went rollerskating, and i saw her off at the bus station on monday night.  major deja vu.  the next afternoon she called to say that she was moving to washington DC.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are countless other vignettes, but i don\'t think they illuminate anything.  some friends i love more than they would believe.  some i\'m angry with, but i haven\'t said a word yet.  others i empathize with to the point where it drives me nuts.  but one common thread emerges, running straight through the tangle.  i\'m affected so deeply by the people near me, but the ones far away are like shadows sometimes.</p>\r\n'),(2339,'<p>She gave it to you for Christmas, that little charm on a chain you\\\'ve worn around your neck every day since.  Silver, of course, because gold doesn\\\'t fit intoher college student\\\'s budget, nonetheless radiating every ounce of her love.  (\\\"Love,\\\" you say.  \\\"She\\\'s just in love with the idea of being in love, and I can\\\'t bring myself to tell her otherwise.\\\"  I think you just like getting laid.)</p>\r\n<p>When I lay naked behind you, I still run my fingernails up your back and kiss your shoulders and try not to giggle at your little sleep spasms, but I can\\\'t even come close to your neck any more.  It\\\'s not that I don\\\'t want to touch you and taste you and kiss you there, tracing the line of your shoulder straight to your ear.  I physically can\\\'t do it.  My hand and that damn piece of tinsel are opposing magnets.  Every time I come close I can actually feel it pushing me away, a physical reminder that part of you will always elude me.</p>\r\n<p>When you\\\'re on me and in me and I\\\'m seconds away from leaving my body, that cold piece of metal dangles in my face, rhythmic gleams ensuring that I don\\\'t forget I\\\'m just a number or a day of the week.  I wan\\\'t to rip it off with my teeth, but instead I let it leave invisible burns on my face with each thrust.  (\\\"He complements my existence,\\\" she confides in me.  \\\"Emotional detachment is sometimes more painful than falling in love.\\\"  She doesn\\\'t know you just like getting laid.)</p>\r\n<p>What\\\'s funny is that before her \\\"I met the most amazing guy,\\\" and my \\\"I\\\'m so sure I\\\'ve just met my soul-mate,\\\"  before you won us both over with the same stories I\\\'ve since heard you share over the phone with countless other potential Wednesday nights and Sunday afternoons (after football of course), before all of that, I couldn\\\'t stand to see her cry.  I loved her so much that I hurt when she wept for whichever boy had used her last.  When I comforted her in my arms it took every last drop of strength I possesed not to touch her lips or promise that I would never hurt her that way.</p>\r\n<p>Now, when I see the sun catch something barely above your collar, I make mental notes about all the things I wish I could say the next time I bump into her when she\\\'s home from school or when she sends me an e-mail about how much she misses her perfect man and how much he loves her.  When that little basketball hoop bumps against my chin, I hate myself for wanting to see her eyes glisten when she realizes that she\\\'s just college breaks and random weekends, and even then she\\\'s sharing you with whomever else your  schedule allows.  But her tears won\\\'t slow the revolving door.  Even when the charm is long gone, I know I\\\'ll still feel its phantom cold kiss in every drop of sweat that falls from your chin to my face.  By then I won\\\'t even need a physical reminder.  (\\\"I don\\\'t care what you do in your free time,\\\" I say.  \\\"I just enjoy whatever time I get to spend with you.\\\" As convincingly as I may pretend otheswise, I know I don\\\'t <em>just</em> like getting laid.)</p>'),(2340,'<p>He enjoyed the trips.  As a child he always looked forward to that yearly trip.  It was never much fun to be cramped into a car with the rest of the family.  It was especially displeasurable to sleep in the car at highway rest stations in states where you were likely to hear the phrase \\\"Would you like grits with that y\\\'all,\\\" at some point during ordering breakfast.  He felt uncomfortable in those situations and couldn\\\'t sleep.  Too many foreign noises and a lack of suitable comfort brought on aggrivating insomnia.  That slamming of car doors, disrespectfully loud speaking volumes and the smell of petroleum products.</p>\r\n<p>It was worth it to get there faster, though.  The time saved by stopping for several hours in a rest station, versus spending a night in a hotel worked out to allow faster arrival at the destination.</p>\r\n<p>But even when they arrived he still had to wait.  Everyone was relieved and slightly stressed upon arrival.  Unexplained impatience did little to help matters and his family would scornfully reprimand him for his hyperactivity.  He was excited, though, because he knew it was near.  There was little time to wait but still it was painfully drawn out.</p>\r\n<p>He couldn\\\'t go by himself, either.  He had to be accompaied by a parent or guardian and that was perhaps the most insulting thing.  To have travelled great distances, to be only several minutes worth of distance from his true destination was a distraction.  Nearly unbearable.  He would never tell if confronted about his desire.  Eventually someone would accompany him simply to quiet him.</p>\r\n<p>Getting closer he could taste the salt in the air.  It was magnetic.  Biting through the air and drawing you towards it\\\'s source.  He could hear it before it was in sight.  This was the best part.  The tension which had been building was near to the breaking point.</p>\r\n<p>Finally, after crossing the peak of the hill, he could see his goal.  At full speed, he ran towards his goal.  There may have been other people around, he may have disturbed them but he didn\\\'t notice.  Suddenly, he stopped and stood very still.  If anyone could have seen his face, they would have noticed the hint of a smile on a mask of pleasure.</p>\r\n<p>As the water pooled and eddied around his feet, he watched as the crashing and receeding of waves covered his feet in sand.  He would stand there all day if he could.  Usually a distressed parent would drag him away, though, or his body would grow tired and pink from overexposure to the sun.  After his feet had disappeared, he understood something.  Something which for a decade he would not be able to fully understand or express.  Even before he knew about the ocean tides or the importance that natural erosion plays with respect to moving land around the world, he felt something.  He felt a part of the earth, his feet gone and legs being slowly devoured by the sand.  He would look out into the endless ocean before him and hope that some day he could pull his feet out from the sand and go forward into the water and never have to come back.  This was it.  This was the point of the trip and every year he would return to experience the same great feelings.</p>\r\n<p>And that boy, now grown, is still sticking his feet in the sand.  Unable or unwilling to make that plunge into the ocean before him.  He still dreams of a life floating out with the clouds and the horizon.</p>\r\n'),(2341,'<P>green tea is required to steep a couple minutes and no more.  oof said.  these are the dynamics of green tea brewing.  more time makes the tea bitter.  people who steep green tea too long do not know the dynamics of green tea.  oof said.  dynamics are swirling around in my paper cup transferring heat to my palms.  dynamics are breaking green tea down in my stomach and entering it into my blood stream.  likewise, they leave my brain and tell my hands to pick up the mug and put it to my lips.  mental dynamics.  a small accomplishment, drinking tea is. so much more satisfying than trying to reconcile the past.  yet they go hand in hand for one moment.  dynamics of reconciliation.  sip tea.  </P>'),(2342,'<p>\r\ndo you suppose that what we call humanity and the way we live our lives is so apart of us that it is futile to even try and stop it?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncan you imagine a world, where everyone knows how they should act and loses all notions of community?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere will be a day when the world is covered in people. parks will have to be used up. mountains will have to come down. the surface from afar will look like a one hundred sided dice.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni know that nothing like this will happen in my lifetime. im glad it wont. im glad i will still be able to go somewhere where noone is around. but thats my point. my point is, that there will come a time where science and all that cool stuff will determine what im fearing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have this crazy fear that the earth is, lets say, a piece broken off, a bigger piece. a fraction of a whole.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow we see this on earth. we see how species over time have learned to live and have evolved to meet the needs of each climate and ecosystem. blah blah blah. to bring this back to something way more specific, lets just say that us humans were the dominant species on every single one of these fractions. this isnt to say a jules verne type planet of gigantic crab and lobster isnt possible, but im only talking about the fractures of the whole where humans are the dominant species. im even leaving out the fractures of the whole from a galaxy far far away. only humans. xenophobic maybe, but im trying to keep it as unhokey as possible.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom each of these fractures lives a totally independent human race. evolving at the same rate, but perhaps with different ideals. our civilizations have evolved with an idea of god. with a creation story. on each one of these fractures cities would grow up differently and maybe more postively. our world has grown up with so much war and misery and greed. what if on other planets the idea of money never even arose. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim not saying our world isnt capable of good, its just kinda nice to be able to zone out and think of a better place than here. ya know? \r\n</p>'),(2343,''),(2344,'<p>\r\nwoke up at 7am. bright eyed and bushy tailed. spoke to the mother. the grandmother. the cat. shovelled the snow-ice-slush mixture off the driveway and sidewalk. i did the copemans sidewalk to because mr copeman had done our driveway with the snow blower yesterday when the shit had really landed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits so early no one is around. i dont like to play video games during the day, so i have noticed how useless my computer has become when there isnt another person at their computer. its very strange.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy cat is in his catbed trying to decide if his ear really is itchy, or weather he wants to lick his ass. his leg is spazzed out, sticking on some strange angle. he\'s funny. he decided on the ass. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits so early, i actually had to opt out of watching mass with my grandmother on tv. the archbishop was gracing us with his presence. whatever i said. i escaped to the driveway to smoke.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none of the biggest things i did this morning was go through my entire mp3 collection. a few weeks ago i filled my harddrives with everything backed up on music and deleted the crap keeping all my faves. here is the playlist i came up with. this playlist represents chiamattt at age 24, feb 1st 2002. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<pre>\r\nIggypop - passanger\r\nEast Flatbush Project - Tried By 12\r\nPaul Simon & Art Garfunkle - America\r\nBilly Bragg - New England (Acoustic)\r\nYo La Tengo - Green Arrow\r\nSunjammer-(Munen)\r\nSparklehorse - 15 - Sad And Beautiful World\r\nThe Cure - Killing An Arab\r\nJosh Martinez - Nightmare\r\nAnti Pop Consortium - 9.99\r\nSage Francis- Makeshift Patriot\r\nU2 - The Wanderer (with Johnny Cash)\r\nMountain Goats - Going to Georgia\r\nHefner - The Hymn For The Coffee\r\nBettie Serveert - Heaven\r\nThe Smiths - Asleep\r\nReagan Youth - USA\r\nKinks - Waterloo Sunset\r\nIAM - Shurik\'n - Samourai\r\nmilk_(knifehandchop_remix)\r\nSole - nothing fell apart\r\nSex Pistols - Submission\r\nBlack Uhuru - Word is Africa\r\nAnti-Flag - Angry, Young, and Poor\r\nTim Buckley - no man can find the war\r\nBad Brains - Revolution\r\n1962 - Three Blind Mice\r\nLucinda Williams - Ramblin\' On My Mind\r\nJosh Martinez - Exile\r\nBollywood Breaks - Tonight my love\r\nThe_Gino_Anthem_JFGino\r\nCloak A Dagger (Ver. 3)\r\nDJ APS - Chronic 2001 (Bhangra Remix)\r\nThe Smiths - Back to the old house\r\nScouts - Bleeding Hearts\r\nBilly Bragg -The world turned upside down\r\nPunjabi MC - Mirza - Surinder Shinda\r\nRun DMC - peter piper\r\nTemptation - Perry Como\r\nSaul Williams - Niggas, Way Back\r\nNon Phixion & Necro - I Shot Reagan\r\nB1 - Space Invaders Are Smoking Grass\r\nJohnny Cash - The City Of New Orleans\r\nWhiskeytown - Bottom of the Glass\r\nHefner - The Hymn for the Cigarettes\r\nroberta flack - No Way To Say Goodbye\r\nBuck65 - Memories Of The Passed\r\nJacob Miller - False Rasta\r\nVincent Gallo - I Wrote This Song For The Girl Paris Hilton\r\nMission Of Burma-This Is Not A Photograph\r\nSixtoo - Trapdoor\r\nBollywood Breaks - Temptation\r\nmy morning jacket - it\'s about twilight now\r\nVincent Gallo - Laura\r\neast meets west - over the rainbow\r\ntheenticer-n-siren\r\nNeutral Milk Hotel - The King of Carrot Flowers Pt. One\r\nApache Waria - wariah-guyana\r\nVelvet Underground & Lou Reed - Heroin (Live)\r\nVincent Gallo - Honey Bunny\r\nSunjammer-(Mediaburn_part2)\r\nPrince Paul - Pain (featuring Sha and Breeze)\r\nDr Alimantado - Best Dressed Chicken In Town\r\nPetula Clark - Downtown\r\nstan getz - Once Upon A Summertime\r\nI AM - Le dernier coup\r\nMorcheeba- Over And Over\r\nMogwai - Punk Rock\r\nSonic Youth - Bull in the Heather\r\nViolent Society - You\'re Gonna Fall\r\nVincent Gallo - Was\r\nPsychoFreud ft. Merciless - Wicked Choon\r\nPassi - Emeutes\r\nBillie Holiday - Strange Fruit\r\nViolent Femmes - Why Can\'t I Get Just One Kiss\r\nBent - Invisible Pedestrian\r\nJosh Martinez - theories\r\n</pre>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwell there you have it. morning morning morning. now im going to go sweep the hallway and kitchen floors.\r\n</p>\r\n '),(2345,'<p>\r\ni am in a cafe on haight street, called the horseshoe. this isn\'t my favorite place to go for coffee, but they have a pantheon (no, that\'s not the right word at all) of computers and i needed to print some resumes out so i can try and get a part time job (coffee shop, retail, reception maybe) while i tread water here in san francisco.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe girl behind the counter is playing some type of happy hardcore from a shitty tape deck. every high hat is muffled, the only reason you\'d listen to music on a tape deck like this is because you\'ve heard the music before and your memory fills in where the quality of the speakers leaves off. i wanted to write about this to all of you, because i wanted to tell you how happy hardcore is like a brute force mood changer. you can\'t help but feel good about it. this observation didn\'t seem like enough of a reason to write a swinney.org article. then i looked down at the table next to this computer i\'m typing at.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsomeone has left some handwritten poetry on the back of a flyer. obviously i am meant to share this with the internet. punctuation has been preserved, spacing is edited a bit for clarity.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<pre>\r\nBlood sickk honey suck\r\nIn little children\'s heavy heads\r\nmy dreams erupt\r\nWhile in my bed\r\nInnocence is dripping red\r\nIn dreams I walk with you \r\n            talk         \r\nIn dreams youre mine\r\nall of the time\r\nHeads on fire and drunken \r\n                  lights\r\nDays devoured by hungry nights\r\nIn love\'s secret Domain\r\nThis is mad love\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2346,'and the urge to reach out and touch you, as you sit quietly on the bench opposite, is overwhelming. i\'m glad you don\'t look up. we know each other, but just barely. i know that if i get up and walk over, you\'ll smile and invite me to sit. perhaps you\'ll even share your sub with me. but what will be crashing at the back of your mind, into your skull, where the bone meets your spine? will you feel sorry for me? i think so, and i couldn\'t bear that. so i lean back, against the tree, ignoring the shining greenblue roof and spire of the closest parliament building, and stare at you instead. not too intently, in case you feel my eyes on your skin, but long enough for me to be able to sketch you from memory later. perhaps we\'ll bump into each other when we get up to leave. if so, i\'ll pretend i only just noticed you. you\'ll never know the difference.<br><br>\r\n\r\neven though the grass is probably dry, in fact, it has been bleached to a pale, almost white green by the june sun, it feels damp beneath my skirt. it rained this morning, and as usual, i wanted to tear the umbrellas out of everyone\'s hands. the roads and pavements are still darker than they usually are. the water has not completely disappeared, i guess. i can still see the outlines left by other people who have sat here earlier. the blades have flattened into the shapes of their bodies. it looks beautiful, and i cannot bear to disturb it. a couple of men, ties loosened, are sitting on the steps of the office complex next to me, their cigarettes held loosely in their hands. the air near them is so hazy and heated from the sunshine, that the smoke melts into it almost immediately.<br><br>\r\n\r\nabout you. well, i like your hair best. it is a little bit too long for your face, but the most beautiful shade of black i have ever seen. i especially like the way it curls near your forehead, as if it is hiding. a woman in a short red dress walks past, blocking my view while i follow the curls with my eyes. she looks at me, and smiles. i smile back, of course, but i\'m uncertain. do i know her? if so, why don\'t i remember her? if not, why is she smiling at me? i close my journal and consider going inside. the sun has disappeared behind a cloud, and my arms are cold.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut the man with the tall striped cloth hat who carries a brightly coloured bucket and stands at the corner of bay and slater saying \"if you don\'t have any change, then how about a smile?\" suddenly appears and sits next to you. you look up, and instead of wrinkling your nose, let alone moving away, like i\'ve seen so many people do, you greet him.'),(2347,'<center><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2347_2.pjpeg\"></center>'),(2348,'<p>she rang the doorbell and stood on my porch, smiling in her black felt jacket.  the porch light was off, and there she was in the dark, smiling.  she was wearing a red sweater, and tight blue-jeans with a denim sash.  i opened the door, wearing my mesh-back hunting cap and aviators, and she began to laugh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>upstairs, we lay on my bed, giggling like a couple of kids.  we rolled around, elbowing each other, staring, and having a brief match of \"steamroller\".  i said, \"can i kiss you?\" and she looked away, giggling, and replied, \"you\'re not supposed to ask.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she wrapped her legs around my hips, and we moved together, blue jeans on blue jeans.  it was chaste, and i had to laugh each time my mouth filled with red sweater fuzz.  her skin trembled a bit, and felt burning hot.  i could feel her curves pressing through the fabric.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as the clock struck 2:00, we walked to bloor street, and waited for the all-night bus to arrive.  i kissed her, hands clenched in my pockets to hide from the cold, and trotted home through the streetlit snow.  i flopped back down on my bed, luxuriating in the disarray, and caught her scent on my pillows.  on the wall beside me, there was a big blue smudge of denim ink.  it was from her knee.  i shook my head and laughed again.</p>'),(2349,'<p>\r\nfew things are tighter than a baby\'s fist.  ezekiel grabs and pulls from his whole body.  fists clenched to whiteness by day open like night-blooming flowers while he sleeps.  he wants.  he is one aching ball of want.\r\n</p><p>\r\nas i watch, ezekiel turns red-faced and windmills his arms wildly.  grunting, grunting, a loud wet KABOOM comes from his pants and his legs shoot out in front of him in surprise.  he can do this two or three times a day.  you have to appreciate what it takes to commit so physically to the act of crapping yourself. \r\n</p><p>\r\njeremy boards a train for colorado.  when he returns we will all be one week older.  1/14th of ezekiel\'s life will have been spent apart from his father.  we will be completely different people by then.  we miss him something awful.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni occupy myself by seeing a different matinee movie every day.  i spend half the movie pacing the back of the theatre with ezekiel until he falls asleep.  i have brunch with friends.  i have people over to make dinner.  when the house feels especially lonely, i make www.m-w.com pronounce \'cunt\' for me over and over.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthree weeks before leaving, jeremy loses the key to our mail box.  he goes out there with his tools and persuades the little lock into giving up the mail.  i have many larcenous tricks up my sleeve, but lockpicking is not one of them.  jeremy empties the mailbox one last time before leaving.  we are both convinced that we will never see that key again.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nlater, i find the key in the dryer with a load of jeremy\'s clothes.  i write jeremy email about it but do not receive a response immediately, like i am used to.  with the time change, he should be answering my emails before i even send them.\r\n<p>'),(2350,'<p>\r\nit was good to see my friend rob. we travelled down the qew into guelph.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe venue was this place called van goghs ear. its a strange place. full of hoochy country bumpkin type college kids. it was very strange.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere were only three girls i \'had my eye on\'. two of them vanished almost as soon as they appeared. the third danced gracefully on the dancefloor. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni overheard a lot of things that made me feel kinda sick, kinda lonesome, and kinda angsty. it was small town canada and i could see how people grew up with such weird notions about the way society should act. what is acceptable and what is over the top. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni sat for moments thinking of all sorts of ways i could talk to the people around me. what set me back were the slurring and probing from the drunken losers around me. i have three cigarettes to three different patrons. all three of them acted as if they were trying to tell me they were acting fake and overly enthused by my gift of nicotine. to myself i thought about how even though i was unemployed i didnt bum cigarettes off people. i marvelled at how they could afford to consume drink after drink yet neglect the purchase of a pack a bones. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni watched the players make their moves and i laughed out loud to the people around me. i never let on to what i was laughing at, for what i was laughing at was so subtle a move it would have come across perhaps as being \'normal\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit was a night filled with bling. bling all around. i am glad i saw rob...but i am not glad i saw guelph.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2351,'<p>The day to day malaise that is my currently unemployed life spiked and dipped today. My body had been foreshadowing that something was going to happen all day. Its strange and rather hard to describe this feeling: nervous energy, a sorta out of synch feeling, or like the electro chemical system in your body suddenly decided to up the voltage on you. It builds up inside you until you feel like you could shoot the energy out of your hands like thunderbolta, Zeus style.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'d been bunkered down inside all day and was going a little batty, the roommate suggest heading over to her friends place to watch some movies. <i>Playing by Heart</i> is the movie picked, this movie contains a character that is uncanny in its psychological resemblance to my ex-fiance. I continue to watch and realize that this is no coincidence. I try to politely duck out as soon as the movie is done, I\'m wound up more now then ever. </p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>The street cars are broken and so I end up anxiously awaiting a replacement bus. It was on the bus that the spike occured. I watch as the bus slowly fills up with passenger as we go through a part of town with a few bars. Everyone is involved in their own conversations when slowly but surely a distinct noise can be heard. Someone most likely a drunk at the back of the bus started singing <i>Hey Jude</i>, within seconds all conversation has stopped and I watch as almost everyone on that bus starts to sing along. I half expected them to start some dance routine a la <i>Dancer in the Dark</i>, the few that aren\'t singing along all have grins so broad they almost look painful. For a moment I almost doubt my intuition about something being up with the ex, maybe this was the weird event that I was meant to experience - the source of all the weird energy. After <i>Hey Jude</i> they immediately jump into the sone with the line \'why do birds suddenly appear everytime you are near\' I\'m baffled by the whole thing. The entire bus is into it - memories of primary school field trips pop into my mind, but these are just a bunch of random strangers.</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I got off the bus feeling pleasantly suprised with humanity and smiling all the way home. Then the dip, my intuition had been right. I returned home to messages on my answering machine letting me know that my ex-fiance had collapsed and been taken to the hospital. A series of phone calls went out and when I finally got an answer it was some friend of hers that was at her apartment taking care of her. The friend filled me in on the details, looks like it is related to ovaries and cancer most likely. She is scheduled to go see a specialist in the next few days, as soon as a few more test results come back. The friend tells me she has just woken up and wants to talk a little. The first few minutes are questions about why I didn\'t call when I first got the messages about her being in the hospital, I never got those messages. I ask her to tell me what she was doing when this happened she says \"I was at my friends house watching this movie <i>Playing by Heart</i>\".</p><br>\r\n\r\n<p>I have to say the whole experience was a little unsettling, too much weird coincidence and then their is the strange occurence on the bus as well. I had been thinking for a few days before all this that if something didn\'t happen in my rather bland life, dominated by job hunting, that I was going to go mad. I didn\'t realize I was being allowed to coast a bit before being thrown for a loop. Just when I think I know what to expect.</p><br>         '),(2352,''),(2353,'thinking of <BR>\r\ncoming up with<BR>\r\nsome conclusion to <BR>\r\nthe questions that <BR>\r\nkeep popping up <BR>\r\nevery day <BR>\r\nmore than once<BR>\r\nalway of<BR>\r\nto what end <BR>\r\ni should spend <BR>\r\nthis time <BR>\r\nwhich passes <BR>\r\nwhether i want it to <BR>\r\nor not.<BR>'),(2354,'<P>S5 and O turned up sometime after 2am the other night.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We went to bed at 5:30am and got up too soon afterwards for adventures.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>When we were out I began to be convinced by the way that Olivia was describing things that I had been away for a long time, too.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I looked up at the Sky Tower and thought that it was the first time I\'d seen it in ages.  We went up to the viewing platforms in the zoomy lift and I walked around the circular platform, deliberately standing on the glass floor that the elderly women tourists didn\'t trust.  I thought that if someone had binoculars and looked up that maybe they could see up my skirt. O forgot to flash the security cameras after specifically asking the ticket-seller if she could. One of my contact lenses got mushed up in the top of my eye and I had bloodshot, weepy eyes.  To the bathroom to fish them out and put on my glasses and a Californian woman started babbling at O and I about the cruise ship she was on.  Back out again, I looked out and things felt very familiar and homely, but lost - like I hadn\'t been around for ages.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>This city is home to me and I love it, but I have only been living here for 14 months, so it is all a mixture of familiarity and wonder.  </P>'),(2355,'My creative writing class is becoming a strange experience. The teacher is only 4/5 years older than I am. He is quasi-irish, meaning when he wants to come off as cool, and when he reads poems, he might be any mick off the streets of Dublin, but when he isn\'t paying attention he is more like your average south carolinian. The class...meaning the students...it\'s hard to put into words. They completely buy mr. mcirishcoolguy\'s act, man, man, they\'re just... Their poems are just about poetry, just mixing prose in a blender, and spicing it with unconventional grammar--it has no point, it\'s goal seems to be NOT making contact with the reader. It doesn\'t say anything but \"Look, I wrote some poetry!\"\r\n<p>\r\nnote:<br>\r\nWhen I finished this article, and sat back, I decided to come back to this spot and say one thing about my fellow students: to be an outspoken \"artistic\" person around here is <i>in itself</i> a huge statement, and it\'s almost impossible not to adopt an us-versus-them mentality and start wearing black lipstick or something. So maybe I should cut them some slack.\r\n<p>\r\nI love music. I love to read, I love to write, i love classical, jazz, and rock, poetry, plays, and fiction. So why is it that everytime I sign up for something, like this class or my music classes, I meet for the most part people who are actively plumbing the depths of pretension, obtuseness, self-image, just bullshit bullshit bullshit? Where the fuck <i>is</i> everybody? You know, the other writers, artists, the other creative people who create just for the thrill of seeing something beautiful, who understand what it means to have vision, that it can\'t be about you, so much, you yourself, it has to be about the beauty of an idea, because you yourself are common, impermanent, and fundamentally we are all so much alike; more alike than we think we are.\r\n<p>\r\nSo yesterday, I was going to get the mail, and thinking about the poems that I had to critique for my classmates, trying to think of something constructive to write without getting into my little manifesto here, and I opened the box, and there was one envelope, leaning up against the inside of the steel box. Inside the envelope was an invitation to be an exchange student at almost any college or university in the US, for an entire year. So I am OUTTA HERE, I, oh my god, I have a whole new reason to live for the next 6 or 7 months, I\'m getting out of SC, life plans <i>intact</i>, it\'s like god reached down out of the sky, tapped me on the shoulder, and said \"Alright son, you asked for something like this.\" And he would be right, except I never could have imagined anything this wonderful, I feel like I\'m in love. So bring on the bad poetry, because I just got a get-out-of-jail-free-card. \r\n<p>\r\nSo where should i go? New York, LA, Vancouver, St. Thomas, Maui, Wyoming...Alaska? \r\n<p>\r\n<i>Adventure</i>'),(2356,'<br>Why are there so many songs about rainbows,\r\n<br>And what\'s on the other side?\r\n<br>Rainbows are visions, but only illusions,\r\n<br>And rainbows have nothing to hide.\r\n<br>So we\'ve been told and some choose to believe it,\r\n<br>I know they\'re wrong, wait and see.\r\n<br>Someday we\'ll find it, the rainbow connection,\r\n<br>The lovers, the dreamers and me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Who said that every wish would be heard and answered,\r\n<br>When wished on the morning star?\r\n<br>Somebody thought of that, and someone believed it,\r\n<br>And look what it\'s done so far.\r\n<br>What\'s so amazing that keeps us stargazing\r\n<br>And what do we think we might see?\r\n<br>Someday we\'ll find it, the rainbow connection,\r\n<br>The lovers, the dreamers, and me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>All of us under its spell,\r\n<br>We know that it\'s probably magic ............\r\n\r\nHave you been half asleep? And have you heard voices?\r\nI\'ve heard them calling my name.\r\nIs this the sweet sound that calls the young sailors?\r\nThe voice might be one and the same.\r\nI\'ve heard it too many times to ignore it,\r\nIt\'s something that I\'m s\'posed to be \r\nSomeday we\'ll find it, the rainbow connection,\r\nThe lovers, the dreamers, and me \r\n\r\n'),(2357,'i must admit that secretly, there was a tiny part of me, the little finger on my right hand, say, hoping that you could somehow sense i was writing this, and turn around, see me, walk over. as if my words had enough power to invoke your presence beside me, even from such a distance. \r\n<br><br>\r\nand when i shut my eyes for a second, and a shadow fell on me, the texture of which i could feel even through my closed eyelids, the inside of my chest did a funny thing. i hesitated, debating whether to open my eyes. i could hear the owner of the shadow, i knew it was a person. i\'ve never been a patient girl, so it was easy to make the decision.\r\n<br><br>what ran through my head, and so quickly, when i saw who it was? disappointment, of course, in the very first instant. then sudden relief, and a mere moment after that, the genuine feeling of welcome which widened my smile into one of true pleasure. i was delighted that it was someone i\'ve missed, and didn\'t expect to see, here in this place. it is good that it was not you. \r\n<br><br>but as we exchanged hellos, and apparently significant news, my watch started to burn on my wrist. i was so late i dreaded getting on the first of my two buses home. and it turned out that the two of you had indeed made me miss my second bus. which, by the way, runs only once every hour after 6 pm, even in the summer time. but i wasn\'t resentful, quite the opposite in fact, despite having to walk half a mile home because the petrol station was the closest the other bus could drop me off. that\'s what happens when you live in the middle of nowhere. the only thing i regretted was wearing silver sandals that look pretty but aren\'t that easy to walk in for long. \r\n<br><br>besides, the delay gave me time to think. God i love ottawa. i could never live anywhere else, like my sister said last week. funny to think i\'ve only been here for a few years. i love how this whole place blossoms every may. i love how winter covers the whole city in delicious frosting. i love how miniature sunsets are caught in the leaves in autumn. i love how every park bench in this city is a perfect suncatcher. this must be the effect of seeing all those giant maple leafs hung on the glass fronted buildings downtown. \r\n<br><br>i stop to watch the shiny little dark green leaves of the baby corn plants growing in neat rows in the fields before our neighbourhood. the feathery tips and the long stalks really do seem to be talking shyly with the wind.\r\n<br><br>lightning, without any rain or thunder, flashes periodically on my way home. the air tastes so good after the first bolt slices through the sky. it makes me wish i could remember how to do cartwheels. i\'d like to spin through the air and meet the jagged light, head on. when we drove past the huge lake that the express transitway curves around, north from downtown, i felt as if i could tiptoe over the silvery blue surface of the water. \r\n<br><br>i don\'t understand why people in cars must look at people who are walking along the side of the roadway. especially when there are acres of farmland and dangerous sky which draw one\'s eyes almost right out into the charged air. but i resolve to let nothing upset me today, and stick my tongue out at one man who really should be keeping his eyes on the road, if nothing\r\nelse, and run ahead on wobbly feet.\r\n<br><br>i pass some wildflowers with their heads turned upward to watch the lightning, and think about picking a few. but it doesn\'t feel right to disturb them either, so i simply stand at the back with the rest of the floral audience, almost believing that when the storm is over, the flowers will turn back to what they were doing before it started. i really wish i knew the names of these flowers. i\'m obsessed with names. i think it is because of my desire to hold onto every single person and place and event and object in my life. i need a name to catch and keep each thing. especially since the pocket of my memory has a giant hole in it. a hole that i am terrified is growing every day. and nouns, words in general, are the thread for the patch of cloth to slowly close it up. if i know what to call something, i can make it mine. names are powerful. with a name you can own anything. think of spells, of incanatations, of \'Let there be light.\' think of Adam up in heaven, naming the land-creatures, the air-creatures, the creatures of the sea. think of a girl called anastasia. and one called joan.\r\n<br><br>i wish your name had more syllables. it has some gorgeous consonants in it, particularly your surname, but they both end too soon. i love how you say my name, all four of the times that i\'ve heard you say it. i love the softness with which you say it, the gentleness which is so strange when it emerges from such a face, with straight features and angles and edges. you make my name sound smooth, lovely, lush. you make it sound the way i want me to be. '),(2358,'<p>I woke up late. My head feels like dogshit. I find a little piece of paper that my not-girlfriend wrote on a long time ago. I throw it away. I still smell like last night\'s dinner. A linguistics exam looms. And I can\'t find my watch. I pack my books and leave.</p>\r\n<p>I\'m just about at the train station when I\'m struck with a strong sense of <i>wrongness</i> about today. I get the feeling that all of my actions since I woke up have been attempts to prove to <i>someone</i> other than myself that the day is somehow just as okay as last Monday. But I have known since I woke up that it isn\'t.</p>\r\n<p>I turn around.</p>\r\n'),(2359,'<img src=\"images/2359_1.pjpeg\">'),(2360,'When the streets are wet<br>\r\nthe colors slip into the sky<br>\r\nand so I scribble with hunger ink<br>\r\nfor want of moxie or good sense<br>\r\na reflex, my melancholy,<br>\r\nlike gagging.'),(2361,'<P>andy frigon was born in a small dilapidated house on a corner of a dirt street with a lot and four large white paint chipped posts holding a sagging fence together.  the house wasn\'t too far from us, the year was \'79 or \'80 i think.  he was the second child of my aunt and sometimes in - sometimes out uncle.  i don\'t remember much of blake frigon except for the subtle fear he put in me when i was around him.  i can\'t even say if he and my aunt were actually ever married.  nevertheless, he finally abandoned  my aunt and two cousins a few years after andy was born as was later predicted by all those who were older than i.  to this day i have never asked andy if he remembered him.  my aunt was a wild child, the middle of three sisters, and now she was alone to raise two children.  she did as best as she could for as long as she could i suppose amidst the circumstances.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>sometime in \'82 (i think) she lost it, in a fit of anger towards the possibility of what life layed before her, she packed up her kids and clothes in her green rusty VW bug with the WRIF \"BABY\" sticker on the dashboard and headed north for isolation amidst the tallest of pines and biggest of lakes.  on and off welfare for the years that followed, jason and andy grew into their separate identities.  jason was the hellion, he took the worst qualities of my aunt (as well as the circumstances by which they lived) and andy was the loving and thoughtful one, he took the best qualities.  andy was loveable by default, you couldn\'t help not being enamored with him.  he was young, small, polite and had the ability to make parents act like complete idiots when around him.  jason had none of these qualities, at least they didn\'t reside on the surface like his younger brother, he was a great kid in his own right... it just took you longer to realize it.  unfortunate as it may be it was jason who had the unlucky position of being the oldest, and because of that he had to bear the brunt of my aunts anger towards life and men.  most likely enduring fits of rage that i can only imagine.  </p>\r\n\r\n<P>please let me note here that my aunt is capable of an awful lot of love <U>but</U> for every ounce of love lies equal amounts of bitterness i think and its important to understand she is not the villain in this story.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>now, nobody ever really saw what we all felt in our hearts was going on between her and jason and being a kid myself at the time (although older than my brother, jason and andy) i was able to understand that something was amiss.  it was because of this that jason in turn used andy as his avenue by which to expel some of the anger and hurt he had been subjected to.  he picked on him, beat him and hurt andy\'s image of himself deeply.  physically andy\'s stature didn\'t help him any either, he was always thin... weighing  maybe 60 lbs soaking wet for what seemed like forever.  he also had trouble with his speech, for the longest time he had an awful time pronouncing words beginning with the letter \'L\' which was both adorable (he was fond of telling my mom \'i yuv you aunt yinn\' whereas lynn is my mothers name) and painful because he was the subject to taunts by kids his age and even considered to be \'slow\' by a few of his teachers.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>as the years went by my aunt became more of an isolationist much to the dismay of my grandparents who wanted so badly to see their daughter and grandchildren, to my mom who wanted to see her sister and nephews and lastly to my brother and i because we wanted to see our only first cousins.  every visit would bring us a new glimpse of how the three of them were weaving in and out of life.  we would see and hear about my aunts many boyfriends, learn of her quiet alcohol and drug abuse, bear witness to jasons growing distance from us and andys growing need for acceptance and love.  we would learn of the different houses they were living in, how school was progressing and we would even dine together as somewhat of a family would.  when my aunt trish would come to town on special occasions (to complete the sisters), i would revel in all of these people converging on our little 2 bedroom duplex on hathaway street.  it was almost a family unit (albeit with no men due to divorce) just like the ones on television.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>on these rare trips down to \'the big city\' we really began to notice the difference between the two brothers.  jason was confident and content with his digs up north among the wilderness, andy was not.  which, because of his age and lack of knowledge of \'the city\' to begin with, made his being attracted to the constant motion and sights of detroit all the more curious.  when they would visit we would find little andy in his muscle t-shirt (showing off his toothpick arms), the jeans that were two sizes too big and the subsequent mesh belt tied almost around his waist twice to keep them on his tiny frame, fat-laced second hand hi-top sneakers, the messy hair, his sheepishly toothy grin that would emerge when he became secretly excited and his heart which was as big as the city itself just bursting with energy and wanting nothing more in the world than to just go out and see the sights.  his little brown eyes were never bigger than when he was in the car and looking at the size of the buildings, the expanse of the freeway system, the zoo, the parks, the radio stations blasting out the latest in funk and urban music and most importantly there was his mecca... the mall. </P> \r\n\r\n<P>andy was addicted to the popular suburban mall.  it wasn\'t hard to understand why, the mall contained all the objects not available to him in his rural life.  he would spend hours looking around, observing how people dressed and what they bought, consuming terrible food in the food courts, playing video games in the arcade where he could be heard using the word \'dude\' often as in - \'dooooooode, did you yust see that?\'.  finally he would make sure he knew what was popular at the time and how much he could get with the money he saved up.  he would buy the latest clothes, tapes and tapes of the latest rap music, the latest action figures, comic books and because we lived so close to the 7-11 his trips also included more candy than we as normal people should consume in a lifetime.  he would take all of this back north as his links to the city, these objects were his connection to a life different than what he was used to day in and day out.  from what i remember the artifacts from his trips down got him attention among his peers.  i get a smile when i think of little kids, i\'m talking kids from really low income families, all gathered around a chipped walkman taking turns listening to rap music... some for the very first time.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>collectively we all grew older and more distant in the years that followed.  we would catch a word here and there on how everybody was doing, sneak a visit north once in a great while and find them in a new trailer or home, with and without food to eat, man around or man not around.  it was difficult for us to observe such difficulties and not be able to help because at the time we had our own difficulties... all so similar in nature but not in geography.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>because of the separation that had occurred between the three sisters, the distance between the cousins grew as well.  when we saw them at my grandfathers funeral we had found ourselves awkward at a time when were supposed to draw together and mourn.  my aunt battled with my grandmother for rights to my grandfathers belongings further straining what was already a weak relationship and again the kids were the ones to suffer further.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so after many years and so much uncertainty, misinformation or lack of any information whatsoever regarding their lives you can imagine our surprise when we learned of andy\'s obtaining a full scholarship to the university of detroit.  little andy was going to a four-year institution, in the heart of the city he loved, on a full ride.  what surprised us even more was his intention to become a lawyer.  it was upon hearing this news that it finally hit us.  the spark we saw in his eye for years wasn\'t a playful spark although he was, it wasn\'t a mischievous spark although he was that too, it was what we all knew but never spoke of.  it was focus, an unsual and determined focus.  it was andy\'s way of taking all that he is, all that he grew up with and all that he wanted and using it to become something that was to never happen had he let life take its normal course.  <i>somewhere, somehow along the way andy became a man without anybody looking.</i>  he ended up graduating with a 3.8 and spent the last two years at U of D as president of his fraternity.  he is now enrolled in Law School pursuing his degree in corporate law, working almost 40 hours a week, maintaining a relationship with what i hear is a wonderful woman.  making his little family so full of pride in the process that it makes it almost impossible to relay the happiness we have for him, in ways that wouldn\'t belittle the effort (because of a lack of proper handling of words and emotion), to outsiders.  my aunt\'s life is now full of possibilties with steady job and steady man, his brother jason also is doing significantly better... i often wonder if his success wasn\'t paramount to their collective survival.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>with all this being said, i can\'t even begin to project the enormous admiration i have for this kid.  it\'s awkward when you meet somebody face to face again and find that they now have sideburns that are too long, their voice has changed and they now speak without pause, they consume beer instead of \'faygo red pop\',  they drive trucks instead of playing with them, are romantically linked to real girlfriends instead of imaginary ones and most important of all they posses not only goals but means within themselves to meet them.  </p>\r\n\r\n<P>andy, i love you kid.  thanks for the <A HREF=\"http://www.relaxone.com/e_mail.html\">e-mail</A>.</P>'),(2362,'Mia drown when she was eleven. It was only the first time and it didn\'t last nearly as long as the others, but still, it was long enough to remember vividly forever after. And it wasn\'t because it was the first time either, that the memory remained so strong. It was just the fact that she drown in her neighbor Diana\'s pool, and that no one did anything but watch.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMia is now 24 and nearing the end of her long short life. She loves flowers and children and wants lots of both, but ever since she drown, everything changed. The dreams she\'s had ever since have all been dark, red and black, and she realizes that there are no flowers and children in her future. Just death and water.\r\n'),(2363,'every since i learned the word \'fucktard\' from Brian C. Moseberger. then number of moments that i should spend cursing at strangers has increased several times fold.  i have even embarassed myself in front of my coworker paul whose son has down syndrome.  yes, FUCKTARD has changed my life.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndriving in my car, a bitchass fucktard is going 30 in a 35 mile per hour zone.  i shout, not whisper under my breath, \"PICK A GEAR FUCKTARD!\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwalking in downtown santa cruz to the movie theater, a panhandler begging for money whilst hopping on what is left of his legs, a single raggedly clothed pole, the other leg probably removed in some bout of frost bite from sub zero weather up north before he got the sense to move to THE MOST EXPENSIVE CITY IN THE UNITED STATES, \"whatever fucktard, eat a dong,\" i tell him with a moseberger-esqe charm.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnot that brian would ever do something like that, no he leaves his change out on the newspaper stand in front of the ethiopian dining destination axum of fabulously filthy lower haight.  yes, he is a generous soul who hates it when change is rolling around in his pocket.  just a note.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsitting at work late at night doing absolutely nothing put trying to force my hair to fall out with the power of thought.  i turn to God\'s favorite website, swinney.org, and give them a harty thought, \"austin.o0O(<B>EAT A DONG, FUCKTARDS!</B>)\".\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsome might think this is a rude habit, and besides that a very rude incarnation of the word fuck combining the usual sexual penetration ideas with a new twist on humans that share an y chromosome with apes.  AND THEY ARE RIGHT.  but i cant help it.  when faced with the vocabulary for verbal revenge when the stakes are high, one is bound to use it when the stakes are low, just in reflex or training.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nindeed, i had a dream last night that Enrique Florido, that scar faced bastard son of a bitch from south of the border was trying to start shit with me again just like in highschool and if i had known to call him a fucktard in highschool, the paradigm of austin eating shit for 4 years might have changed dramatically.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\neat a dong, fucktard.'),(2364,'<p>My two year old son said the \"F\" word to my mother last week.  The two of you who actually know my mother understand that she was less than thrilled about that prospect.</P>\r\n<p>After two weeks of work, the only progress I have made in cleaning up the wee one\'s vocabulary is to get him to substitute \"Oh, MAAAAAN!\" for \"Fuck.\"</P>\r\n<p>Unfortunately, he is so proud of being a \"good boy\" that he now emphatically tells family, friends, and complete strangers alike, \"Conner say Oh, Man word.  Conner no say fuck word.\"</P>\r\n<p>AAAARRRRGGGGHHHHH!  (But it makes me laugh every single time.)</P>\r\n'),(2365,'i like to be chatty with people in public places a lot.  when i was in sf last week, i went to my very favorite cafe and chatted amiably at length with the owner.  he liked me and i liked him and i think that the incident brought my child back to the more embarrassing moment of puberty, when she cringed repeatedly in my shadow while i chatted with complete strangers in bank lines and bus stops, on trains, planes, and seated at the next table in restaurants.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni like people a lot.  i like most people.  the ones i do not like are all classified as low life, low class, sonsabitches who deserve to die wallowing in their own vomit like the curs they are.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think it\'s funny that the children i embarrassed by chatting to all and sundry have grown up doing exactly the same thing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlife is a circle.  love is important.  affection for strangers is okay.<br><br>'),(2366,'<p>Never ever, under any circumstances, should you place your tank of amazing Sea Monkeys anywhere near your alarm clock.</p>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<p>Services to be held at the Dirt Devil funeral home as soon as the wet spot dries completely.</p>'),(2367,'<P>when i was in high school i ran with a small bunch kids.  for the most part we knew each other for years (2 guys in particular for the entire 12 years of school) often playing together on various neighborhood sport leagues and so forth.  a quiet bunch, we mainly clung to each other instead of opting for companionship within one of the universal cliques that exist in high schools everywhere.  we weren\'t popular, we weren\'t outcasts either... we were the guys in the background that left a collectively small footprint on the schools\' history.  we learned together, did drugs together, partied together and mourned together on a few occasions.  we also shared a mutual dislike for the high school we attended and therefore skipped many a class together as well.  the staff of the school looked the other way for the most part since drug and small time gambling busts and other issues of a more violent nature took up most of their attention.  we were also fortunate to have been in good favor with several of the staff who were more important to the running of the school than others.  in hindsight it was a great position to be in and one that i suppose i took full advantage of.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>sometime early on in 10th grade we were introduced to a small little polish diner called \'Menkas Kitchen\' about 15 minutes south of school.  a small building on a busy street that you would easily overlook if you were not searching for it.  it had maybe 15 spots around an L-shaped bar with beige vinyl stools.  4 brown tables in the center of the room with 4 chairs to each table.  completing the diner was 9 booths arranged against the front and side of the diner in the same L-shape as the bar area with large brown laminate tables.  long vertical fabric blinds blocked the morning sun coming from the east when pulled by the little broken and rusty chain.  the long grill was in the rear corner along the wall facing the bar.  this wall was full of carefully arranged dimpled glasses and bowls, silverware, towels, boxes of cereal, pies, cookies, various hallmark cards taped and hanging by their corners, pictures of newborn babies born to proud grandparents - patrons of the kitchen no doubt, a small television, a few clocks, a handful of bowling and softball league trophies with chipped gold finishes and dusty tin faceplates, a tattered radio with a hanger acting as an antenna (broadcasting the morning news with the beloved JP McCarthy on the mighty WJR AM 760) and a large display of Detroit Red Wings paraphernalia.  clippings, pictures, articles, banners and during the Stanley Cup run - a large silver drum-like canister made to look like the Stanley Cup itself.  the carpet was worn, the bathrooms often a mess and the seats had long since lost their cushion so when you sat in them you just planned to be there awhile as it took great effort to climb out of them when done with your meal.  it was home.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>Menka was the owner of the diner.  she was small, thick, spoke with a heavy polish accent and moved quickly as not to keep her trusty patrons waiting for their coffee or tea.  an passionate and sometimes rabid sports fan it was not uncommon to arrive at the diner during one of her loud and boisterous rants following a sunday football game, basketball game, hockey game, baseball game or other similar event.  during hockey season she would reach her fan zenith, often starting loud chants of \'let\'s go Red Wings\' throughout the morning as if we were sitting front row at Joe Louis Arena and Steve Yzerman himself had just taken the ice.  she had 2 waitresses to handle the floor, a feisty little polish woman named Mona handling the bar area and a cook behind the counter in the corner.  Mona spoke the most broken of english but possessed an uncanny sense of what you wanted before you even knew you wanted it.  she was our little personal \'Flo\' only with no witty \'kiss my grits\' tagline and deep southern accent.  all together Menka and her staff of 4 served breakfast and lunch 6 days a week to more people than i can even count.  </p>\r\n\r\n<P>as simple as the place was, the meal was even more so.  breakfast would cost you $2.10.  for this you would get your choice of two eggs, sausage or ham, hash browns, pancakes or toast and coffee, tea or juice.  they has plenty of extra items on the menu that you could order but it was some unspoken rule that you just never strayed from the \'special\', i\'ll take it a step further and say that you really never dared ask for a menu either.  you had the \'special\' like everybody else, the only option was really how you wanted your eggs and if you wanted pancakes instead of toast and vice versa.  the food was fine, the coffee was good and the people who ate there completed the entire package.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>the folks who ate at menka\'s were often elderly.  retired men and women with time on their hands and special diets that required attention.  some of the ladies and gentlemen arrived often unkept, on any given day you would probably find a woman with curlers still in her hair who lived close enough to throw on a long coat and trudge a block or so to get a quick meal and a cup of coffee then return back home to face the day.  a large percentage of the breakfast eating population at Menka\'s were of polish origin like Menka herself.  many of them saw it as a sort of social club where they could go and converse with each other, enjoy some toast and remark on current events... current events in Poland.  during the harshest winter months these folks would still show their dedication to Menka and her kitchen by walking or driving in the deep and muddy snow.  upon their arrival they would begin the process of removing all the layers that they had put on to combat cold.  off would go scarves, off would come overcoats, off would go flannel hunting jackets, off would go big funny hats revealing sparse covered heads in various shapes and sizes, off would go rubber boots opting instead to walk around in socks or slippers and lastly the regulars would finish the ritual by individually greeting their friends while often rubbing their hands together for instant warmth or applying some sort of balm on wind chapped faces.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>to say the population of the diner was \'old school\' would be an understatement of epic proportions.  these are the folks that \'built\' the school that housed the people we often refer to as \'old school\'.  grizzled big men with fists as big as hams, smoking big cigars and laughing at triple-bypass operations and severe intestinal difficulty.  their pudgy wives wearing tight clothing and heavy makeup as to mask their age talking about how their sons and daughters, grandsons and granddaughters, great grandsons and great granddaughters were doing amidst pots of coffee, messy breakfast plates and dainty cups of tea for those who felt they were a little bit better than the rest of us.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>one time i can even remember taking advantage of their age and attitudes for our own sick humor.  it was early on in our tenure as the resident high school kids that Ray edged up to the bar one morning and looked around as to make sure he had the proper audience.  Mona had not yet been able to remember our tastes and so after getting all our orders she finally made it to Ray.  Ray politely gave her the order and after being asked if he wanted cream or sugar with his coffee he looked left then looked right and boomed <I><B>\'no thank you ma\'am, i like mah coffee like i like mah women...hot and black!\'</B></I> with the deepest peckerwood accent he could muster.  jaws dropped, gasps were heard, eyebrows were raised, coffee was spilt and the course of the morning for the 25 or so patrons of Menka\'s kitchen was disrupted at such a notion.  i\'m can\'t remember exactly but i believe my lip required 3 days to heal from my biting it as to avoid laughing out loud. </p>\r\n\r\n<P>since some of the first names were difficult to pronounce due to the multitude of ethnicities we came up with little nicknames for the people who we talked to everyday.  nicknames we didn\'t actually call them as in a formal greeting but more just names we would use among ourselves outside of the restaurant.  there was \'smokey\' because of his cigars and cloud that followed him wherever he went, \'Chauncy Lover\' because this wonderful 80 year old woman often mistook my friend Chauncy as one of her own, \'petey\' because of the perpetual dark circle around his left eye that was never fully explained (like the dog in the little rascals shorts), \'maude\' cause she reminded us of Bea Arthur, \'4 links\' because he would order something and right before it would come to the table he\'d want double the meat portion and a host of others with names i have since forgotten but would remember in an instant if i happened to come across them again.  we loved these people and they loved us, the 5 or so of us were the adopted grandsons of the patrons and the kitchen itself.  we looked out for them by picking up a newspaper or two on the way and they in turn looked out for us.  numerous times in the middle of winter we found our cars dead in the parking lot after breakfast only to have big huge polish men come out into the wind, tinker with the engine and revive them so that we could make it to school.  </p>\r\n\r\n<P>how we were introduced to this place really is unknown since nobody can pinpoint exactly who took us there first.  the 5 or 6 of us just sort of arrived one morning at the door, came in and sat down and had breakfast among the regulars.  so fond of this place did we become that a system was quickly developed whereas if two or more people within our small circle wanted to skip first hour and grab a table at Menka\'s then you went, no questions asked.  if the phone rang at your house around 7am and the person on the other end just simply said \'Menka\'s\' then you knew in 15 minutes however ready you were, you could count on a ride to be in front of your crib ready to head south to the kitchen... again, no questions asked.  if you ran late you would forgo the shower opting instead for a meal with Menka, Mona and the gang.  of course in these circumstances your buddy would then hold the car while you dashed in the house afterward to take a quick shower before heading to school, we were not complete barbarians.  sick, hung over, lazy, fever, headache, broken limbs, runny nose, hell... you could have had some new form a intense bacteria eating half of your face it just didn\'t matter - you went.  if you made it back to school then you could count on hooking up with a fellow breakfast eater in the main bathroom sometime during 3rd period to do what was later coined as \'taking a Menka\'s shit\' almost like clockwork.  so regular were our collective asses that you could set your watch to the event.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>getting in trouble for skipping school was never really an issue because of the aforementioned \'friends in high places\'.  so if the mood struck, no matter how many times a week or how many weeks a semester you just went with it.  if breakfast ran long then you skipped second hour as well.  if you were feeling good and had an extra 2 bucks in your pocket then you got a take out plate for Amy\'s mom - Mrs. Zannetti who was always designated to watch the doors by the parking lot as to curb or deter the rampant skipping of classes by such hooligans.  by junior year we even started to pick our classes relative to importance and time of day to allow for such trips.  the less important class the earlier, the more important... you get the idea.  by senior year we opted to have no first period at all because we all had the proper amount of credits as required for graduation.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>it had become our spot, as much our own as it was to the others that frequented it.  every now and then we would invite an outsider to dine with us but for the most part it was just the 5 or 6 of us that would stroll back into school with toothpicks in our mouths like the punk kids we were day in and day out.  we thought of it as almost a secret, a refuge from cliques, high school angst and routine.  not a day goes by that i don\'t think of having breakfast at Menka\'s with those same 5 or 6 people.</p>'),(2368,'-the moon is a bone. <br>\r\n-what? <br>\r\n-it looks to me like a bone. <br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe smiles, slightly uncertain, never knowing how to react when she says things like this. he considers reaching over to take her hand, then decides against it.\r\n</p><p>\r\n-you know, the moon is just dead rock. people -- poets -- have all these wildly romantic notions about it. but it\'s just the moon. it just stores up light. you know?\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe continues looking up, without saying anything. \r\n</p><p>\r\n-i just find it, uh, overrated. i mean, it is not even shining on its own. it borrows light from the sun. <br>\r\n-so?<br>\r\n-well, its not an orchid or a closed eyelid or a goddess. it\'s more like a rechargeable battery \r\n</p><p>\r\nand he laughs and kisses her chin, her cheek, the corner of her eyes he\r\ncan reach beneath her glasses. \r\n</p><p>\r\nshe closes her eyes. her eyelids are the shield against the world. \r\n</p><p>\r\nhe takes off her glasses and kisses her eyelashes, as if her eyes closing was a signal. \r\n</p><p>\r\nsometimes she thinks her moods are a cat, a grey cat prowling about in her skull. he\'s the one that gets clawed the most, but he doesn\'t seem to mind.  '),(2369,'i thought it was going to be a hassle, you know the hopping around, the \\\"clank \r\nstep clank step clank\\\" on the concrete. i was afraid of summertime--time \r\nfor wear shorts, miniskirts, and my g-string bikini w/ one flip-flop. i could \r\nhear the jokes already: \r\n<p> <i>the obvious: \\\"arrggggh/where\\\'s your eye-patch?/what\\\'s a matter, shark got \r\n  your leg?/where\\\'s your parrot? etc.\\\" </i>\r\n<p><i>on santa monica blvd: \\\"stick your wooden leg up your ass, you candied/toffee/caramel \r\n  apple.\\\" </i>\r\n<p><i>ex-friends: \\\"that thing come w/ a kickstand?/hope you don\\\'t catch termites./bet \r\n  you swim in circles w/ that thing! etc.\\\" </i>\r\n<p><i>vacationing in australia: \\\"mate, you expect a woman with one wooden leg \r\n  to catch her own kangaroo?\\\" </i>\r\n<p><i>breakin\\\' the movie deleted scene: \\\"that b*tch can spin!\\\"</i>\r\n<p>maybe it\\\'s becoming fashionable these days or something because i haven\\\'t heard \r\n  one crack about my peg-leg. not even a peep, a snicker, a laugh... maybe a whisper \r\n  (that\\\'s because they\\\'re jealous of my wooden friend). i\\\'ve even seen wannabe \r\n  <b><a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2369_2.jpg\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">legbrace \r\n  pretenders</a></b> in marathons, supermarkets, post offices, dance-offs, and \r\n  panda expresses--all places i frequent. \r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2369_3.jpg\\\" width=\\\"203\\\" height=\\\"395\\\"><br>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><b>shake a leg</b> \r\n<p>i bet you all want to know how i lost it. it\\\'s not like losing your virginity \r\n  or even your car keys. it\\\'s something you remember the rest of your life. i \r\n  was 18 and a very successful cheerleader. i was doing kicks and cartwheels and \r\n  was strong enough to support the bottom of the pyramid by myself. anyhow, after \r\n  we had just won state for the fourth year in a row, brad (my boyfriend at the \r\n  time) and i decided to go out and tear up the town, you know, burn some rubber \r\n  w/ his \\\'82 pontiac f-bird. his ride had it all: red paint, t-tops, yosemite sam mud \r\n  flaps, and wings across the hood. he used to take me on the hood, but that\\\'s \r\n  a different story for a different time of day...<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n<hr noshade width=400 align=center>\r\n<p>\r\n<p><i>brad pulled up to a vette at a stoplight and started revving his engine. \r\n  i remember telling him, \\\"brad, please stop. it\\\'s so embarrassing when you do \r\n  this. besides, every time you drag you lose. you don\\\'t have to prove yourself \r\n  to me or to your car... brad, are you listening to me?\\\" </i> \r\n<p><i> brad gave me a macho look and continued to push his pedal to the metal, \r\n  making the guy next to use do the same. </i> \r\n<p> <i>\\\"i\\\'m getting out,\\\" i screamed. \\\"you\\\'re insane.\\\"</i> \r\n<p><i> brad grab me by the arm and said, \\\"nah, baby. you\\\'re staying for this ride. \r\n  you\\\'re my good luck charm.\\\" </i> \r\n<p> <i>\\\"brad if you don\\\'t let me go, i\\\'m going to cut off my leg!\\\" yes, it seems \r\n  like a stupid thing to say now, but it was the only way i could get through \r\n  to him. brad was such a numbskull, but he had a big c*ck and a hot ride.</i>\r\n<p><i> he laughed at me, which only made me boil. i couldn\\\'t keep making threats; \r\n  i was going to show him this time. </i> \r\n<p> <i>\\\"ah, you stupid b*tch,\\\" he yelled. \\\"you know how hard it is to get bloodstains \r\n  out of sheepskin???\\\" </i>\r\n<p><i> when i woke up, i was in the middle of the road, my leg propping my head \r\n  up like a pillow. the paramedics were there, one was telling me it was too late \r\n  to save my limb. i remember thinking that it was going to be okay, especially \r\n  w/ the sock hop next weekend. </i> \r\n<p><i> witnesses said that brad won the race, and, a few weeks later, brad called \r\n  me to say that all the other times he lost a race was because my fat ass was \r\n  holding him back. anyway.<br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  </i> \r\n<hr noshade width=400 align=center>\r\n<p>\r\n<p>that was all a very long time ago...\r\n<p>it\\\'s weird when i think about it, i mean, when i first put my \\\"foot\\\" down i was on a \r\n  pine peg and now, 12 years later, i\\\'ve upgraded to a monkey wood peg. i especially \r\n  love the monkey wood because it floats, helping me do my morning laps at the YWCA \r\n  pool. the doctors say i\\\'m in the best shape of my life and that if there was \r\n  a cheerleading team for a paraplegic football team, i would have something to kick \r\n  about. \r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2369_2.jpg\\\" width=\\\"258\\\" height=\\\"385\\\"><br>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><b>back that ass up</b> \r\n<p>for special occasions, like fancy dinners and charity events and celebrity \r\n  luncheons, i switch out my everyday peg to my oak peg w/ the honey finish. it \r\n  matches this kate spade bag i recently purchased from saks. i get many compliments \r\n  on it, and it helps me get laid very often. \r\n<p> sure, i\\\'ve got a limp, but who the hell doesn\\\'t these days? '),(2371,'<i>Jesus Christ Vampire Hunter<br>\r\nThe Spirit of Christ Impales you!<br>\r\nThe First Testament Says \\\"An Eye For An Eye\\\"<br>\r\nThe Second Testament Say \\\"Love Thy Neighbor\\\"<br>\r\nThe Third Testament... Kick Ass!!!</i><br>\r\n\r\nHumanity has reached the zenith in the film arts, well at least in the campy b-film genre. The childish way that some middle aged viewer walked down the aisle summoning up - his best demonic voice quoting the title, all the while giving the devil horns with forearms crossed, and sticking out his tongue upon completion like he was trying out to be a Gene Simmons replacement should have been enough forewarning. The plot contains everything you could possibly wish for:\r\nKung-Fu Action, Vampires, Lesbians, Lesbian Vampires!, J.C. Himself, Punk Rock Mohawk Sporting Priests, Santo The Famous Mexican Wrestler, A Cult of Aethists, and Cameo\r\nappearnces by God and The Virgin Mary.<br><br>\r\nBest parts about the film - partly funded by the Government of Canada\\\'s equavilant to the National Endowment for the Arts, shot entirely in Ottawa with a great epic Kung-Fu battle in The Dominion Tavern a watering hole unlike any other (the same tavern in which I shot a short biography of a tatoo artist) and it also includes three musical scenes.<br><br>\r\nGranted it is a niche film but it\\\'s experiences like this that remind me why I love film and want to eventually do some indie works of my own. Unlike, the regular pop culture film experience this outing is an event. The viewing audience cheers  as the protagonist J.C. engages Atheists and Vampires alike in Kung-Fu, they clapp along to the beat during one of the musical scenes (all original score by the way), and scream out ewwwh! and ahhh!\\\'s at the punky sound effects and squirting gore. This is as close as modern film gets to how I envision Shakespeare. That\\\'s right, Shakespeare. Think about it - Will\\\'s plays always gathered two audiences the upper crust in their booths and then the real people down in the pit, front stage. Those people weren\\\'t the quit audiences of today that file into little black rooms, they got into things.<br><br>\r\nMaybe thats the difference between theater and film, but even in theater today people are rather subdued. Sure, there are times when this may be necessary, perhaps during a serious drama, that doesn\\\'t change the fact that todays audiences seem to be lacking a little something in my eyes. At least they didn\\\'t tonight. Has anyone ever seen a standing ovation for a film? Granted the people you are standing for most likely wouldn\\\'t see it but the others around would.<br><br>\r\nThis gets me to thinking that maybe this is just symptomatic of numbness that seems to dominant in western culture. We are sedated in our viewing of cultural works, we don\\\'t react when our ideals are betrayed by our leaders, when events demand attention we react with bravado instead of courage, and this is a tangent that could be a whole seperate entry. Don\\\'t mean to be accusatory, just seems like our culture breeds passivity individuals to me. Take the leaders thing for example what do people do - write a letter <i>yeah that\\\'s showin\\\' em!</i> (read sarcasm). All right end tangent.<br><br>\r\nThe tangent really derailed me, however if for some reason you get the chance - this flick is worth checking out. Nothing religiously offensive really probably the best PR old J.C. had in a long time. I liked it anyway, enough that I wanted to share.<br><br>'),(2370,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2370_1.jpg\">'),(2372,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2372_1.jpg\"><br><Br> \r\n\r\n<i>\"you should write a post that includes that picture and then create a series in the new swinney.org series manager...\"</i>  -a. swinney<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe Place:  one (1) graffiti\'d <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2008.txt\">bathroom</a>, <a href=\"http://newyork.citysearch.com/profile/11351476/\">Plan-Eat Thailand</a>, Bedford Street, Williamsburg Brooklyn.<br><br>\r\nOn 7 Feb 2002, Peter wrote:<br>\r\n> ive actually never even been to this place before. it was in the<br> \r\n> women\'s bathroom and my girl mentioned it, so of course i had to <br>\r\n> go barging in to see for myself. if it makes you feel better,<br>\r\n> there were also ups for fluxfactory.org and 2xy.org, so i suppose<br>\r\n> that puts you in good company.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2373,'<p>Having given up everything that was difficult for her, Anna realized that there was nothing left. When she was a little girl, she began by trying everything. She was intelligent, as all the teachers told her parents, so school was easy-peasy and she kept up with her good marks. However, ballet was not so easy. And the violin was also a challenge. Skiing, skating, acting, the flute, the piano and a few years later, the oboe also fell into the \\\"difficult-category\\\". This left arithmetic... which was the next to go, and yoga, which was the last to go. And a strange thing happened. School ceased to interest Anna, and yet that was all she had left. She realized that the essays she was writing were as boring as fuck-all. Then, walking home one night from the university which she attended, she saw a headless, rubber blow up doll hanging in a dormitory window; its hands held above its head with coarse rope. The doll was in the image of a naked woman and its plastic, public hair was shaved into a perfect, vertical rectangle, which reminded Anna of a geometry class dropped long ago (for everything reminded her of something lost).</p>     '),(2374,'<i>What do you do when you are your biggest obstacle?</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nEmotions enraged engage each other like some grandeur grandiose group of gladiators.<br>\r\nRiddled with indecision, inconstant, indifference with no core, crux, or center.<br>\r\nTemporarily tempting time to trump the task to be ackled<br>\r\nWhistling while waiting, watching, wanting, waxing,<br>\r\nFuture formulas ferment<br><br>\r\n    \r\nRationality wrought right rigid wreaks wretched wrath<br>\r\nAbsent absolutism absolves abundant abusive absurdities<br>\r\nAuthentic axioms anger all acountants against accommodating acceptance<br><br>\r\n<i> Do they have Pepto-bismol for the soul?</i><br><br>\r\nDeny depth, decry decalogue, despense debauchery<br>\r\nGangly git gather glam girl groins<br>\r\nSalvation saunters satirically saving saucy succubi.<br><br>\r\n\r\nUmbrage unaddressed ultimately unravels ubermensch<br>\r\nTepid tolerance tests terrestrial termination<br>\r\nSling shot shatters sacred symbols<br>\r\nRecompse rewarded righteous rage rectified, recusancy reinvested<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>Accessing alliteration amuses audience?</i><br><br>'),(2375,'there are these people at <a href=\"www.rabble.ca\">www.rabble.ca</a> and they stole me.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am auntie.com, i have BEEN auntie.com for years.  not only did they steal my name, but they put it on the frigging television and i have been trying to get an ad deal with lifetime television for women for years and they never even answered any queries but they have this militant fem site on their teevee and i am so mad.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am beside myself with aggravation.  it\'s not the content of their site, that doesn\'t bother me.  what bothers me is that the STOLE ME!!<br><br>\r\n\r\ni need a shark of an attorney.'),(2376,'<p> Close your eyes, go on, keep them closed. </p>\r\n<p> Wouldn\'t want to you to see us? </p>\r\n<p> You and I aren\'t under the same sky? you?re praying that?s the truth. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> You get so sick at night, because you?ve heard what?s been going on. </p>\r\n<p> You just can\'t seem to read the paper anymore. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> You didn\'t want to know that we suffer like we do. You didn?t want to hear it, and you didn\'t want the truth. </p>\r\n<p> And you can\'t seem to read the paper anymore? </p>\r\n<p> You don\'t read the paper any more. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Your little bubble cannot exist without us. You know it, you can\'t stop it, and as much as you\'d like to help, you will not give up the bubble. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> We are worlds away. We are so forgotten. You\'d be happier if you don\'t know we exist? And the many of us here, we can just touch only glimpses of your untouched world filled with so few. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Go on and close your eyes, and we\'ll all go away. It\'ll be better if you just don\'t know? </p>\r\n\r\n<p> You don\'t read the paper anymore? </p>\r\n'),(2377,'Grandpa was a great guy; he was my favorite guy.  \r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa didn\\\'t speak English very well - but I knew that he loved me like no one else ever did.  Grandpa collected things for us to play with, and we used those things in our games.  He had big cardboard barrels in his garage, and we would take them out and roll around in them.  He got the barrels from the heavy industry in the area - they had been used to ship dry chemicals, and sometimes they would make our skin burn or itch.  But, it was all still so much fun.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>There was an oil refinery in town, and sometimes the air and water tasted of sulfur.  There was an ironworks in town, and we could feel the triphammer falling forcefully as we drove past it, in Grandpa\\\'s car.  There were rail-yards in town, and we could hear the rusty steel wheels screeching and boxcars coupling at all hours of the day and night.  There was a garbage dump in town, and we saw the methane torches burning bright and blue in the night.  The whole place was a factory.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The people in town were German, and Irish, and Italian, and Polish.  And almost everybody had a trade.  Everybody worked hard, real hard.  They built stuff, and moved stuff, and cut stuff, and baked stuff.  They did a lot of stuff.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa never got a lunch hour.  Grandpa had to eat raw dough while he was standing-up working with the bread.  Grandpa had the biggest hands in the world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa came to see us at least once a week.  He always brought things for us.  He brought cookies that he had made, a bottle of pop, and a piece of candy for each of us.  He took us out to eat every time he came to see us - no place fancy, but it was just about the only time that we ever went out.  It was a real event to go to the \\\'Yankee Doodle\\\' hamburger chain - back when they had Earth, Wind, and Fire paraphernalia all over the inside of the restaurant.  Grandpa and Grandma probably could have used that little bit of money for themselves, but they gave it to us instead.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa lived through one World War, and then escaped a second one.  Grandpa came to a New World, and made it through New York and into Chicago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa was always a bit naughty.  He\\\'d pretend to fall asleep at the wheel just to scare Grandma.  He\\\'d dump water all over the basement floor so that we could slide around on it - and make toilet plungers stick to it.  When everyone else told me to be careful, he\\\'d whisper in my ear: \\\'Go like Hell!\\\'  When pollsters called his home after an election to inquire about his voting behavior he told them that he was a Communist.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Things are a bit different in Grandpa\\\'s town now.  I don\\\'t think that the air and water are any cleaner - it\\\'s just that the triphammer doesn\\\'t fall anymore, and the boxcars don\\\'t roll as much anymore, and I know that the people there don\\\'t do as much stuff as they once did.  The only two things that I\\\'m sure that they still have are the oil and the garbage.</p>   \r\n\r\n<p>When Grandpa got real old he didn\\\'t drive to see us as often as he once did.  Instead, Mom would pick him up and take him back to our place.  One day while he was in the car with Mom, Grandpa fell asleep - and that time it wasn\\\'t a trick to fool Grandma.  Grandpa never woke up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Grandpa was a great guy; Grandpa was my favorite guy.</p>\r\n\r\n      \r\n'),(2378,'what is it son?<BR><BR>\r\nfather, i need.<BR><BR>\r\nyes?<BR><BR><BR>\r\n\r\ni need something in my mouth burning.\r\na smoke, a candle a firecracker for all I care. opium pipe, sparkler, punk, match, or corn silk-- something with flame and smoke.<BR><BR>\r\nmy lungs ache for it. my mind bends towards it like sunflower towards the sun.<BR><BR>\r\ni was born to it you see, a father with glowing ash in a middle winter\'s night.<BR><BR>\r\na reflected glow from the window as he rushed my mother to the hospital on a late summer\'s night.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nthe doctor slapped my feet and said \'it\'s smoker\'\r\nsmoke poured from my ears when i cried.\r\n<BR>'),(2379,'<p>The other night we had kids (16? 18? 22? I\'ve never been any good at estimating ages) show up at our door three times -- in the pouring rain -- to try to sell us magazines. The first one couldn\'t stop talking, and from what she said at first I thought she was a new neighbor of ours. Then she said something about being in a competition to meet as many people as possible in a given time period. Then she pulled out her little brochure and told me I didn\'t even have to keep all three magazines -- I could just subscribe to one, even if I requested three. I explained to her that the reason I was answering the door unshowered and unshaven in my pajamas at 4 in the afternoon was that I was unemployed, and so I really shouldn\'t be buying magazines. \"They\'re cheap!\" she said. My answer was still no, and I said goodbye and closed the door.</p>\r\n<p>My girlfriend answered the door the second time, because I\'d just gotten out of the shower and was totally naked. I didn\'t hear the conversation, but it was short; she doesn\'t believe in giving excuses.</p>\r\n<p>The third time was about 8:00 or so, while I was cooking stir-fry (mmmm, olive oil). What these people were doing hawking magazines in the rain all day I could not fathom. This one was a cute/cool boy of the kind I one-third want to fuck and two-thirds want to punch. I opened the door, took one look at him, and asked \"Magazines?\" He allowed as how yes, it was magazines, but \"I\'m not lying to you like the other kids!\" Oh really? I didn\'t get to find out what the story was there, because he instantly launched into begging me to let him buy me subscriptions himself -- supposedly he was just a couple shy of his goal. Maybe I\'m nuts, but I want things even less when the salesperson offers to pay for it themselves. When I refused this offer, he gave me this incredulous \"come on!\" look, half-disbelief, half-anger. I shut the door in his face, and immediately wished I\'d done so sooner, because it was then that I realized he\'d been smoking and the cancer-stick smoke was interfering with the heavenly olive oil aroma.</p>\r\n<p>What was this really about? The first girl said something about a trip to Cancun. Maybe she was \"lying.\" All I know is that I get the creeps when strangers come to my door and aren\'t bringing me a package I ordered. I got the creeps when I had to sell stuff door to door as a kid (it was oranges and candy, I think). I wasn\'t very aggressive and didn\'t want to be; I was very polite and always took no for an answer. This is partly a sort of mild agoraphobia, I guess; you wouldn\'t expect it of someone like me, maybe, but I\'ll come out of the closet and admit that I don\'t like Halloween and haven\'t since I was a little kid and the trick-or-treaters used to scare the shit out of me. When I was older I hated it because the kids at school hated me and would egg my house or write nasty messages in shaving cream on my dad\'s car, but also because the kids coming to the door were often older and looked like they could try anything. And I don\'t like it now because coming up with a costume and making it work is always such a chore, and I never live up to my own expectations.</p>\r\n<p>But I digress. I guess my point, uncontroversial as it may be, is that I really hate \"selling\" and I think it\'s one of the ugliest of the socially acceptable behaviors in our society. I hate selling even more than I hate being sold to. And I made some profound connection with regard to this phenomenon last night, maybe in a dream or something, some realization about how fundamental this is to our society now and how it\'s a key to what\'s really going wrong. I wish I could remember it.</p>'),(2380,'I rent a storage facility, that is also the home of two of my good friends. I sleep, and hang out with a woman that I am more than interested in. It can be great, sometimes it can be trying, but I can hardly seem to spend time away. When I go to work it is different, but I rarely go to work and nor does she. I think my friends here think I am going crazy, and so yah there are those that say that I should maintain a healthy distance so as to not burn the flame out - but what if things are going really good, and she and i can have handled little fits and all that? Is it fair to escape your emotions by not staying? The nights I have stayed alone have been great, but I would have loved to of been next to her as well. We talked about it today, seems as though we are on the same page with it. Tonight I brought over a change of clothes, in a bag. She smiled, I smiled. We made dinner, I helped. She is making a cheese cake right now, and she brought me a spoon full of cream cheese - she says she adores me, and I adore her.</p><p>Tomorrow I go back to work. I have been working as a teacher\\\'s assistant at the school for the blind here. Last year I worked in the dorms and hated it. I am loving the school part of it. I have found the degree path that I have been searching for. I have found that education is helpful to me, I like that part.</p><p>The other day I took a nap at my uncle\\\'s house. He and I slept for four hours in his bed, he snored and so did I. We put new shelves in his closet the other day. Sweat and a little pain - then we sat out on his back deck and admired the spectacular view of downtown Austin (as beautiful as you wanna imagine). </p>\r\n<p>I have two pairs of skivies under the driver seat of my car. My car has become a second closet. I have about three or four toothbrushes.</p>\r\n<p>All these places are home to me, I love them all equaly. I am broke but  I am happy, and full of home. Austin told me that he is going to come visit next month. He will have a bed to sleep in. Maybe he can convince Sandy to keep his feet warm.</p>'),(2381,'<p>\r\nive been playing one of those mega multiplayer role playing games as of late. its called <a href=\"http://www.darkageofcamelot.com/\">\'Dark Age Of Camelot\'</a>.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nas you can see from my screenshot my character has a nice rack. i play a huntress and my weapons of choice are the bow and a spear. you can pick what sorts of things you want to train in and you can even become a weapon smith, fletcher, craftsman, tailer, and im sure more. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing only lvl 18 i am kept away from the RVR areas. Realm Vs Realm is what makes the game kinda cool. when you first start the game you pick which realm you want to fight for. in each realm there are different classes of people. I just happened to pick hunter. there are about nine classes to choose from within each realm.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neach realm is at war...and when i get to a safe lvl(24ish) I can begin attacking the enemy. Its a really neat idea, because for the most part all other mmprpg games have been \'me vs computer\'. DAOC adds to the thrill i guess. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have a real life friend who has been playing a bit longer than me, so most of the stuff im wearing in that pic is hand-me-down stuff from him. He made me that bow for instance. when he started playing the game he joined a guild called \'wrath of fenris\'. by the time i got into the game the guild was pretty big and i had to actually \'tryout\' to become a member. I did and i got in. while i dont really give a damn about being in the guild, you are often running around  getting from one place to another riding horses and running in the wild. Its kinda cool after running for ten minutes to see someone who is in the same guild. they will pay attention to you more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is also the infastructure within that guild. being in a good guild means it has a good number of upper lvl players. having this means that the hand-me-down cycles come around a lot more often than when you arent in a guild. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe game is really fun when you group with eight other players and go after harder \'mobs\'. today for instance some lvl 23 and 24 players let me hunt with them and we were hunting shreiking willows and snow yeti\'s. its good fun. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngotta love the nickname. a nickname no one in the game even understands. \"dotcom? what does dotcom mean?\". i told a few it was the last name of a very important klingon soldier. $$ \r\n</p> '),(2382,'He always thought of himself as anti-authoritarian, his intuition had told him at an early age that most of those in positions of authority didn\\\'t know any more then others. It was probably around grade 6 that this really started to exert itself in his personality.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThe first thing to go was his church going, his family wasn\\\'t religious but he had been going with his best friends family since they met in grade 2. Every Sunday he would go, mainly he liked the singing, every couple of weeks there would be the shaking, too. The congregation would get really riled up, filled with the holy spirit they would say, then a few members would collapse shaking. He used to think it was better then T.V. but then he started to question. He lost his Christianity and church going by the end of grade 6 and started looking for something else.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSchool had been touch and go when he was young, they constantly switched him from gifted to special education classes, he was smart but had a speech impediment. The principal of the military school took an interest and decided to keep him in regular classes but had him spend one day a week just hanging out in the office. He used to like those days, he would drink juice the principal would have coffee and they would read the paper and talk politics together. It was a good substitute for his father always being away.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe beginning of highschool involved punk and industrial music. The public highschool decided to put him in the enriched stream, the speech therapy had finally paid off. Girls, oh my god, girls, highschool until grade 12 was a series of relationships centered around letters, similar taste in music, holding hands, and a little making out. The problem being that there wasn\\\'t much else to do in a small town, if he wasn\\\'t holding hands he was trying to find something entertaining. This lead to social companions that would bring on a break down in all parents except those with inhuman amounts of fortitude.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHim and his companions got involved in all kinds of activities. There was the recreational drug use, no big deal really, but the fighting was a bit of a problem as was his new found hobby of fencing the goods his companions B&E ring would grab on the weekends. He worked after school to make sure that the cash flow didn\\\'t arouse to much heat from his parents. School had lost all interest for him with the exception of art and drama class. Despite all his extra curricular activities he still managed to do alright in school and actually got the lead in a community drama troupe\\\'s play.<br><br> \r\n\r\n<b>Violence</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nThe fighting was never really a problem for him he had lots of practice. His old man would come home and as they got re-acquainted there would always be issues of control. It\\\'s funny cause it was also his father that arranged his first after school job for him making bullets in his Godfather\\\'s basement. His Godfather was military intelligence, he would be taken out on weekends by his Godfather and sometimes his father - they would train him. Hand-to-hand, handguns, rifles, semi-automatic but always tell him only in defence of self or loved ones. When he and his Father went at it though it became clear it wasn\\\'t only in defence. There was the broken coach from him being flung across the room, not too mention the one friend that had to be talked out of pressing charges cause his car had been attacked as it sped away from his house one night with him in it, a father attached to its roof smashing windows. The violence with his Father ended finally after one special encounter, in front of his Mother and Sister where his father had him pinned and was yelling \\\"Who is in control! Who is in control!\\\". He answered by ripping his head open using friction and the floor. When the blood finally began to flow it quickly covered his face and he replied \\\"I don\\\'t know who is in control\\\". The blood was all over his fathers face and his Mother and Sister were silent, that was it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nViolence wasn\\\'t kept inside the home though, the small town bred violence so brutal it would be the envy of any major metropolitan center. He had long hair and tended to wear long johns under cut off shorts with concert t-shirts all the time. People weren\\\'t as liberal in his home town as some other places. The sparring with his Father plus training actually ended up helping him a fair bit. In one such instance he was walking with his girlfriend in broad daylight. While walking through a cross walk a pick up truck stopped at the stop sign, one man jumped from the back another exited the passenger side both in the late twenties early thirties. He didn\\\'t think anything as they walked up to him and his girlfriend as he didn\\\'t know them at all. He went down with the first surprise punch, his girlfriend immediately ran with him yelling at her \\\"call the cops\\\", from his knees he launched himself at one knocking him into traffic on the street. He was up and grappling with the other faster then he could think. At the first chance both men ran, as a few cars had stopped and people were honking. This was just one of many occurrences of this kind. He wasn\\\'t innocent in violence though as there were a few occasions were he would get involved in things knowing what would come of it. Many of the previously mentioned social companions were in and out of juvenile detention centers and a few of the older ones ended up doing some time in the pen.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThis ridiculous behavior ended one night though when he had accompanied a few of these said companions on a trip to one of the bigger cities a few hours away. Him, two guys, and two girls had been drinking amongst other things and just generally walking around the city. It was past midnight when they all decided it was time to go, the car was parked in some multi-level parking garage. This is where everything went too far. They were being loud and a lot of curse words were used when they crossed paths with a middle aged man in a suit and tie going to his car. The man took offence to the language and told them that they should watch their mouths, this only egged the other two guys on more and they started swearing at the man. He made a comment about how he would make them watch their mouths and that was all the provocation they needed. The immediately went to work on the man, an empty mickey bottle from earlier that day used as a weapon, the man was unconscious pretty quick and they continued to work at him for a couples of seconds while he was on the ground, despite yelling from the girls and near the end he himself. They all hoped back into the car and went home, the man died from his injuries. Charges were brought against the two guys and the case went to adult court, they both did time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSince then there has only been one other episode and that was when he was working as a bartender during university. An older gentleman attacked his manager punched and then started strangling her, he came out of the dish pit to see five men sitting at the bar just watching. He reacted before thinking and when it was all over the attacker was taken by police and everyone filled out a report. The police told him not to worry, but there were charges brought against him anyway apparently he had fractured the mans skull. The bar had a restraining order against the attacker from before and had laid five charges some negotiating and charges were dropped against him.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThese days he just tries not to remember all the violence, but when it comes up in a conversation he can\\\'t stop himself. He tends to try to explain that you can\\\'t always just put a blanket condemnation on violent event until you know why it has happened. He also wonders if the motivation behind him doing that is trying to lessen the weight of his past.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Post Secondary</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nHis highschool years ended and he was expected to go on to post-secondary, it was made clear that there would be no help from his folks if the path was fine art or drama. He checked and he was told he wouldn\\\'t qualify for loans. He had always done his own thing regardless before but he didn\\\'t see any options for him if he was going to keep going to school. His parents picked a course of study that they thought would interest him and any excuse to get away from his home town seemed like a good idea at the time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt took five years to get the degree and in those year he had some of the best times of his life and some of the worst. Since he lost his virginity to a good friend in grade 12 he continued on a path of serial monogamy in his attempt to find some one to share his life with. He finally made some friends that didn\\\'t fill his parents with fear and concern. Academics went well he even received some awards in the field that had been picked for him related to critical theory. Profs started grooming him for grad school and his peers heaped praise upon him. He hadn\\\'t given much critical though however to what it was he would be doing when he was done, the job market tends to be more geared towards practitioners then critics. He had to work to get through school and near the end of the fourth year he realized his mistake and dropped out short of his honours. The help was cut off and the pressure was put on, friends and relatives both weighed in saying that there was nothing to lose by going on to get the honours. Faced with the idea of actually trying to find out what he wanted out of life he gave in went back for the last year. Instead of praise when he came back people talked about how he was burning out, one of his prof\\\'s tried to get him to go on some medication. He graduated and left town after lingering for several months due to what he thought was something more then just another relationship in his serially monogamous personal life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe languished for several months trying to figure out what he was suppose to do with his life. He started to remember highschool more and more back to a time when he had run away from his parents place for a few months in the summer. He used to sleep in the cornfields between his small town and the next one twenty minutes down the highway. His friends would give him rides to and from work and lend him their shower and washer when he needed it. He felt like he had lost himself and his way somewhere, maybe during those five year at university or maybe at the outset when he gave up on the art and drama. All he knows now is he really wishes he was back in those cornfields.<br>  '),(2383,'I like my neighborhood, however, I\'ve been experiencing the misfortune, lately, of living near a bustling section of Broadway.  Through the wonders of gentrification, many of the old bodegas, drug-stores and all-night diners have given way to more upper-crust clubs and bars and \"nicer\" restaurants.<br><br>\r\nThe cruel hand of fate recently took my neighborhood\'s last hold out- <i>Grandma\'s</i>- the only 24-hour greasy spoon that delivered, and replaced it with a cabaret-wannabe-jazz-joint called <i>Smoke</i>.  Smoke has nightly live jazz (of dubious quality), insanely pricey coffee, and... <i>stand up comedy</i>.<br><br>\r\nStand-up night must be popular at Smoke, as they seem to host it about 5 days a week.  To each their own.  However, lately, they\'ve been stationing rabblerousers at each of the four corners of my block; people (the actual comedians to perform, I understand) who flail around conspicuously and hand you a flyer for their show once they have your attention.  This is fine and good; NYC is known for its obstacle-course-like streets, tough to navigate through the sordid crowd.<br><br>\r\nHowever, alot of the comedians literally rush up to you and, with a melodramatic, overly-ennunciated flourish, ask: <i>\"Do <b>you</b> like stand-up comedy?!!</i>.  The first few times, I relied on that 6th urban sense, the ability to blow off passersby with a half head-shake and a subtly bothered smirk.<br><br>\r\nBut then they got more persistent.  Lately, its gotten to where I can\'t even get get to my apartment without being approched by the rabid comedians.  After months of walking past (and blowing off) the same purveyors of comedy, week after week, you\'d think that, in the name of tact, they\'d get the idea and stop hounding me, my neighbors, my friends.  But no, they\'ve upped the ante. They surround me, they go in for the kill.  <i>They want me to enjoy their comedy, dammit!</i><br><br>\r\nYesterday, I had an epiphany: I was blessed with a means to ward off the bloodthirsty comedians.  When approached by two comedians, and asked in stereo <i>\"Do you like stand-up comedy?!\"</i>, I simply replied: <i>\"No, I am German. I have no sense of humour\".</i><br><br>\r\nThey shrunk back with a confused look, and will hopefully stay retreated for long enough for me to conjure more ways to beat them at their game.<br><br>Someday, I wish to live in a normal neighborhood, where I don\'t have to <A href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1912\">retialiate</a> just to enjoy day to day life...'),(2384,'Thereâ€™s something out there for everyone, they say.  And I guess thatâ€™s what makes it difficult, the prospect of something out there, and the feeling that whatever is out there is keyed especially for me.  Cara was here again the other day, knocking on the doorâ€¦.I know youâ€™re in thereâ€¦.but I had just made a cup of instant and didnâ€™t feel like answering herâ€¦I know youâ€™re in thereâ€¦.and she pounded on the door like she had a hammer.  I sipped my coffee and turned the set low.  Eventually she went away.   \r\n\r\n<p>Maria brings the groceries, I pay her and she leaves.  She makes no indication of wanting to stay, though sometimes she hesitates at the door as if sheâ€™s afraid to touch the doorknob.  She hesitates only for a few seconds and then leaves.  I can go for several days without taking a shower, but I always take one on the days Maria is supposed to be coming with the groceries.  I clean-up the place a little bitâ€¦.mainly by throwing things into the bedroomâ€¦but I clean.  Maria says it smells in here and she wrinkles up her nose.  She brings me Lysol and other forms of deodorizer, and I use them before she comesâ€¦but it still smells in here, she saysâ€¦what do you do all day?  Maria wonâ€™t be by for several days now.  I have enough instant for three days or so, so I hope she remembers to bring the coffee.  I need a cup of coffee every morning or I get a headache.  <p>The picture on my set flickers.  I watch the morning news and I take notes.  I mark down the color and pattern of the newsmanâ€™s ties.  This is important to me.  I want to see if he wears a tie twice, if he repeats himself.  But after two months of watching I have seen no repeats.  I wonder if the newsman has a closet full of ties, or if there is a wardrobe department that prepares one daily.  It must be a strange life, being a newsman.  A newsman is famous from the waist up.  I have never seen his legs as they are behind the desk.  Where do newsman live?  Can you imagine living next door to a newsman?  I think it would be strange to see him mowing his lawn or clipping the hedges.  I donâ€™t think I would want to live next to a newsman  I wouldnâ€™t only see his legs, but I might see him in short pants.  The last thing I want to see is a newsmanâ€™s white legs.  It is interesting to count his ties though.  If he has a closet for them, it must be a huge closet.  I imagine a walk in closet full of ties.  I would like to go there and run my hands through the rainforest of silk ties.  I drink one cup of instant every morning.  If I need more coffee, I put more scoops into my mug.  I never drink more than one cup of coffee in the morning.  But if the newsman has a closet full of ties, where does he keep his suits?  How many suits does he have?  I will mark down in my notebook that I start keeping track of his suits.  Why didnâ€™t I do that before?  And if I am keeping track of his suits, I must follow with his shirt.  If I remember right itâ€™s usually white, but then it could be light blue.  I must follow his shirts.  <p>Cara was here the other day.  She comes by once in awhile now, less than she used to.  She calls me too, sometimes late at night.  Sometimes in the middle of the dayâ€¦just calling to see if youâ€™re okayâ€¦and though I seldom pick up the phone, I do once in awhile.  Oh, Iâ€™m just fineâ€¦what are you doingâ€¦nothing muchâ€¦.do you need anything?  â€¦Maria brings everything I need.  She will be by in a few days.  I hope she will bring the instant coffee as I am almost out.  But I ignore her most of the time.  I wish she would just leave me aloneâ€¦just go away and leave me aloneâ€¦.thatâ€™s what she wanted, wasnâ€™t it.  Why does she keep calling, keep coming over, on the pretense of her caring.  There is something sadistic in that woman, and I wish I realized it years ago.  Can you imagine seeing the newsman in short pants?  Wouldnâ€™t that just be ridiculous?  Heâ€™d probably be wearing black socks.  '),(2385,'the singer sings a song</br>\r\nlike he\\\'s telling a story</br>\r\nthe grounds of it are lies</br>\r\nthe boy listens carefully</br>\r\nlike it is talking to him</br>\r\nhe gets a high on listening</br>\r\nhe gets a high on anything</br>\r\nthat tells him<p>\r\nthere is something else</br>\r\nother that this</br>\r\nin the street a multitude</br>\r\ndancing in the rain</br>\r\nthe water is rising'),(2386,'<pre>\r\nThe road vanishes behind me\r\nAs I proceed.  So I move slowly;\r\nI need to treasure my memories.\r\n\r\nA truck passes and pulls to the side \r\nof the road Where dogs \r\nsleep, waiting for their master\r\n\r\nwho has gone off to the post office\r\nand general store to buy stamps \r\nor cans of beans.\r\n\r\nTwo men, dressed in blue coveralls,\r\nGet out and lift the dogs into the bed.\r\nThe dogs never stir, it as if they\r\n\r\nare frozen that way, curled into fur\r\nBalls, as if snuggling by a fireplace.\r\n\r\nThe men do not say a word to me,\r\nBut get back in the truck and drive\r\nUp the road, until it vanishes around\r\n \r\na far and invisible bend.  I know\r\nwhat will happen to those dogs.  \r\nThe men will split open their bellies\r\n\r\nand from them they will take \r\nthe finest office supplies: staplers,\r\nscissors, and ball-point pens.\r\n\r\nthe men will sew up the dogs and\r\nload them back into the truck.  \r\nwhen their master comes back \r\n\r\nto this spot where he left them, \r\nthe dogs will be fresh and as doglike\r\nas ever.  He will never detect\r\n\r\nthe strange scars underneath them.\r\nbut I will move on, past this place\r\nwhere the dogs should be returned:\r\n\r\nand it will vanish.  I will continue\r\ndown the road, and around that\r\nfar and invisible bend: \r\n\r\nand it too will be gone.\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2387,'<p>It was just one of those days.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s winter.  The sun shining through the skylights gives a false sense of security.  By just looking up one would think that the world outside is paradise with sun shining down all day and night.  By comparison, once out the door the world outside is cold, and full of slush.  Cold, wet, slush.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What makes this day different from any other slush filled day?  Are the streetcars making different screeching sounds today?  The raccoons fighting on the roof, they are still the same right?  Its February, the shortest month of the year, and rightfully so as it is the most dismal.  And today is just another day with yet another battle in the epic argument that is life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ever known you were about to get yourself into something stupid before you did it?  You can just sense it coming before it happens.  Not really explainable by logic, its just there.  This is the kicker, logic doesn\'t work here.  If logic was the all controlling, six years ago it would have prevailed.  But logic chickened out, and gave way to something more sinister.  And out of this came six years of fighting, yelling, screaming, and good times mixed in.  In public, private, on the phone or in the land of zeros and ones, it doesn\'t matter where it happens the result is always the same.  Sadness followed by happiness, in varying extremes of each.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Try forever to explain your point of view to some people, and they will never get it.  Others pick it up before you finish your sentence.  How about both these people in one?  Can that exist?  It does.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Something special must be around for people to be able to fight for 6 years, but through all of it remaining friends.  A wise person once said, \"That is the sign of a true friendship\".  Forgiveness?  Common sense?  Something special.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sitting, thinking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I hope the six year feud isn\'t over forever.  But it can\'t continue like this.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I will miss you.</p>\r\n'),(2388,'I had a spiritual day today. I killed people.<br><br>\r\nToday i read Roland Barthes\' <i>Camera Lucida</i>.  I was sitting at a pub while doing it.  he wrote this book to figure out for himself if photography had an essence.  instead of relying mostly on outward theory, he went looking for a photograph of his mother after she died.  he went through photographs of his mother, throughout her life, not finding her.  he finally came upon a photograph of her, taken in 1898 when she was five years old, with her parents and brother.  (any quotes here are barthes unless otherwise noted).<br><br>\r\n<i>\"There I was, alone in the apartment where she had died, looking at these pictures of my mother, one by one, under the lamp, gradually moving back in time with her, looking for the truth of the face i had loved. And i found it.\"</i><br><br>\r\n<i>\"of course, i was then losing her twice over, in her final fatigue and in her first photograph, for me the last; but it was also at this moment that everything turned around and i discovered her <i>as into herself ... ( ... eternity changes her,</i> to complete Mallarame\'s verse).\"</i><br><br>\r\nhe found death in that photograph, and continued developing his thoughts toward that end.  a photograph tells you what is no longer, projects reality to a distance that you cannot reach.  there isn\'t much meaning in that death, because photographs block memory.  a photograph, like death, is a violent derailing from reality->memory to reality->image.  the difference between your memory of a particular event in your past with no photos and your memory with photos to see - is that violent transition.<br><br>\r\n<i>\"We photograph things in order to drive them out of our minds.  My stories are a way of shutting my eyes.\"</i> - Kafka<br><br>\r\nand now to the beginning of the story; the beginning of today.<br><br>\r\ninstead of doing schoolwork, i decided to take photos.  my roommate skipped his classes, and modelled for me.  i had this idea before, tested it - you may have seen that entry here on swinney.  for over an hour in my sunny afternoon bedroom i took his life, usually 1/6 of a second at a time.  he fell asleep a lot.<br><br>\r\nthere was a point where he was pinned beneath an arrangement of blankets, and i had to take off his socks for him.  it was comically erotic.<br><br>\r\ni edited them, and posted them right away.  i have a lot to say about digital photography, but in short, <i>hooray</i>.<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2388_1.jpg\" ><br><br>\r\ni had to get out of my house, away from my computer (healthy overdose), to do some reading.  i went to the royal oak on bank street, like i often do.  i had the first quarter read.  i drank, ate, and read the rest there.  smoked cigarettes outside.  barthes discusses portraiture quite often, and especially of early portraits, old grainy photographs with faces that are taking the procedure quite gravely, acknowledging what they feel to be a serious event.<br><br>\r\nPerhaps without a past blocked with pervasive photographic imagery, these people could vaguely sense the significance of being photographed, and expressed it in their pose.<br><br>\r\n<i>\"A sort of umbilical cord links the body of the photographed thing to my gaze: light, though impalpable, is here a carnal medium, a skin I share with anyone who has been photographed\".</i><br><br>\r\nthen the shutter is the pair of scissors that cuts the cord, always prematurely, thus also sharing death with anyone who has been photographed.<br><br>\r\ni had two and a half pages left in the book when someone came up behind me, over my right shoulder.  i was sitting facing out the window.  i turned, and saw a short native woman holding a plastic bag asking me something.  i took off my headphones, and tried to make out what she was saying.  at first i thought she was slurring, but after listening to a few sentences it seemed more like a very thick accent.  she seemed old.  she was missing two front teeth, and the others weren\'t in good shape.  after a few tries, i understood what she was saying.<br><br>\r\n<i>\"is anyone sitting there?\"</i><br><br>\r\nshe was pointing at the seat opposite me.  there were other empty seats in the pub, and of course my first thought was that she wanted something from me.  i fought that off, and invited her to sit down with me.<br><br>\r\ni shook her hand, and the two guys sitting at the table next to me, who\'d been listening and watching the whole time, seemed surprised that i was friendly and asked her to sit down.  i asked her her name, and so i met lucy.<br><br>\r\nlucy is from baffin island, visiting ottawa, so she tells me.  i think i may have seen her around in the city, and she may not be visiting, but i don\'t doubt she\'s lived there.  i think it was her excuse for wanting to sit in the window - she claimed she got lost wandering, because she loves to explore new places, and wanted to sit in a window seat and watch people for a while.<br><br>\r\nit was quite hard to understand her.  she spoke slowly and thickly, with an accent of age more than anything.  she had a broken arm.  she felt old to me, but didn\'t much look it.  she offered to buy me a drink, and i declined, because i really was about to leave.  i stayed to talk with her for a bit. i told her that i was in school, and she was very happy to hear that. she reached out with her cold hand, took mine, and looked to my eyes with a fractured smile.  then she reached into her purse, (i remember her purse strap, looking like a quality purse strap, somehow identifying her as not a street person) pulled out a wad of twenties, and gave me one of them, as a graduation present.  by the way she did it, i knew i couldn\'t decline, so i accepted it and thanked her.  i bet the guys at the table beside me were quietly and suddenly second guessing their non participation.<br><br>\r\nlucy then went on to tell me that she\'s an inuit psychic.  for those of you who don\'t know, inuit are aboriginal people from the far, far north.  she pronounced it \"inoo\".<br><br>\r\nshe leaned in a bit and told me, slowly, that she could see through people.  it seemed like she was translating in her head while talking.  if you\'ve ever heard the inuit language, she sounded very much like that.  she said that she came to me because she knew i was a good person.  she looked at me deeply when she said that.<br><br>\r\ni kept going back to my book because i thought that she was going to just look out the window.  i soon closed it, and talked with her some more.  the fortune she told for me was pretty general, basically that i\'d have a lot of turbulence with work and profession, but i\'ll take care of myself and be well.  of course, that could apply to anyone, but if someone is paying <i>me</i> for them to tell <i>my</i> fortune, i\'m not going to complain.<br><br>\r\nshe ordered a pint of labatt blue (bad canadian beer for you non canadians oot there) so maybe she wasn\'t all that wise, but jokes aside, she was a very spiritual, somewhat sad old world person.  the type of person i had just been seeing in that book.  she may have not lived in their time, but i bet where she grew up, nobody even knew what a camera was.<br><br>\r\nshe went out for a cigarette (ottawa by-law: no smoking) and i joined her.  it was -25c.  she seemed comfortable.  she was talking to an old drunk guy.  i was packed up and ready to go.  we smoked, and i had to translate her for the old drunk guy.  his name was bob, and assured me that no harm would come to lucy once i had left.  i brought my camera, as i always do.  i asked her if i could take her picture.  she butted out her cigarette roughly, nearly burned her fingers, and decided the framing for the shot by moving over to a corner with a door and a window, and became very serious.  she wouldn\'t talk to me when i tried to talk to her.  before i even looked, i knew the face looking back at me would be from the same place as the ones in the book i\'d just been reading.  i took only one shot, used a flash to be sure.  it was strange.  normally i take a lot of shots in a situation like that.  but as soon as i picked up my camera in the pub to walk out the door, i knew what i\'d see, and that i could take only one shot, and then i\'d have to go; not sure why.<br><br>\r\nshe didn\'t want to have her broken arm in the photo.  it seemed she felt it would somehow ruin the picture.  she wouldn\'t talk, and stared right at me.  her demeanor was very respectful.  i don\'t really remember looking through the eye piece, focusing, nor pressing the shutter button, only the cold night conducting through the metal skin of my camera in to my cheek, and my breath floating in the direction that i walked after i killed her.<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2388_2.jpg\">\r\n'),(2389,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2389_1.jpg\"><br><br> \r\n\r\n<b>I.</b><br>\r\nShe gave me the watch for graduation a few years back; I was astonished at how her choice in styles conformed so closely to my tastes. <i>She really does know me</i>, I thought. I had never had a watch like this; it was so modern and pleasing to look at, so stylish; a man\'s watch. I recall very little from my college graduation; I spent most of that sunny morning steadily gazing into the crystal bezel, watching the loop of reflected sun glint off the watch-face, burnish off the brushed steel band, dreaming. As I peeled out of my cap-and-gown, she joked that she was going to have it engraved with some purposefully mushy message. She cloyingly mused that she didn\'t because she didn\'t want to stigmatize such a nice gift if we ever were to separate. We spent the next several hours holding each other close, reveling in the fact that we would, in fact, never separate.<br><br>\r\n<b>II.</b><br>\r\nEnglish is not my co-worker\'s first language. I admire him because he has become deftly fluent so quickly. I suppose survival\'s necessities are a good motivation. Linguistically speaking, he stumbles rarely nowadays, usually only on odd idioms, irregular verbs, or English appropriations of other foreign words. Today, he asked me what it meant when the <i>demons are exercising</i>. Suppressing a smirk, I asked him to clarify. He explained that he was reading about his favourite saints, and came across several instances of <i>exercising demons</i>. I told him that there was a great demon work-out tape that he should look into, a film called <i>The Exorcist</i>.<br><br>\r\n<b>III.</b><br>\r\nSometimes I forget where I\'ve parked my car. My car sees little use these days, tending to languish on the NYC streets, under the weather, only awakened for 5-minute bi-weekly circles around the block to find a new parking place, cylinders hiccuping and coughing just enough to obey the city\'s <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2061.txt\"><i>opposite side parking ordinances</i></a> long enough to evade another ticket. I should probably sell the car, as I spend more time and money on simply maintaining its parked-stasis than I actually do driving it or going anywhere these days; cars aren\'t so much a necessity in a city where everything is linked by trains as they are in places blanketed in urban sprawl and freeways. But, like so many other things, I keep the car. After wasting the past few Monday and Thursday afternoon hours walking the concentric blocks of my neighborhood searching for my car, I\'ve begun to enter the location into my cell phone to help me remember. I\'ve developed quite an alpha-numeric code to pinpoint its location between the numeric streets and phonetic avenues; this is fascinating, as it would probably be much easier to simply <i>remember</i> where I parked my car.<br><br>\r\nHowever, deep down, I keep the car in case she might ever want to use it. Its location, I punch into the phone she persuaded me to get so that she could be in touch at any time within this throbbing missed-connection city, this, the girl who bought me the watch, this girl who, like the fiercest of demons, I\'ve yet to exorcise.<br>'),(2390,'<p>sometimes I wish I was a friendlier person. i very rarely find myself in \r\nsituations like <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2388.txt\">this</a>. i \r\nsuppose the years of big city life have warped my sense of people. i almost never talk to \r\npeople on the street anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the only people that i have had a good chat with are those that moved \r\nhere from Out East. often new brunswick or nova scotia. almost always down \r\non thier luck. they\'re the only ones that have been kind enough not to \r\ninvade my space without permission. such genuine people, no pretenses apparent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>regardless i do often feel compelled to talk to more people i walk past, \r\nto see what stories they can tell. maybe i should stop listening to my \r\nhead once in awhile.</p>\r\n');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (2391,'the first time i fell into a panic about the arranged marriage after everything had been decided, i was watching the movie \'birthday girl\'. nicole kidman plays a russian mail order bride who doesn\'t speak english. in one scene she\'s all alone, in this foreign country, in a dark bedroom, with a strange man. <br><br>\r\nsuddenly there were all these granules of ice just a bit wider than my veins hurtling through my bloodstream. i could feel their edges poking out, rupturing my skin. it was like inhaling liquid nitrogen.<br><br>\r\nthat\'s me up there on the screen, i thought, my hands rubbing at the pain which suddenly burst inside my chest. i don\'t know anything about the man i\'m going to marry in just a couple of months. i\'ve only met him once. for an hour.<br><br>\r\nnow i wonder, was sex, the threat, the desire, the sheer mess of it, hovering in his mind as we spoke that sunny january morning at the chapters bookstore in such calm, measured tones? was he thinking, underneath all that nice clean logical discussion of bank accounts and the division of household chores, about typical reactions to a hypothetical crisis, was he thinking that soon we would be man and wife, and live together, and learn each other\'s bodies? i don\'t know.<br><br>\r\ni know i wasn\'t. it didn\'t even occur to me to picture him naked. to think about that part of married life at all. it was only while sitting in that theatre that it dawned on me what i am really letting myself in for. my skin is too dark for my blush to show. <br><br>\r\ni guess i\'ve been too dangerously lost in daydreams about interior decorating our first house, about the wedding ceremony itself, to worry about what will happen when our divergent lives merge in that sudden instant, like lightning.<br><br>\r\ni admit it. i am scared. though really i have no reason to be. what is the worst that can happen? i will never permit any man to beat me. and aside from such shadow monsters, what is there to fear? i\'m still trying to accept the fact that i\'ll be getting to know him _after_ our wedding. which is terribly risky. afterall, a wedding is supposed to be a once-in-a-lifetime event.<br><br>\r\nso what if we turn out to be horribly incompatible? it is very possible, considering the circumstances. but then i think, like a gadzillion other couples, including my parents, and his, we\'ll be able to work it out. we might not be living in bliss, but we\'ll be okay. we\'ll survive. and who knows? we might end up being very content.<br><br>\r\njust because you\'ve courted each other beforehand, even lived together, doesn\'t guarantee a happy marriage. and anyway, i believe we can never really know another person. think of all those people who discover brutal secrets about loved ones they\'ve known for decades. and not just brutal secrets. all sorts of things. a person\'s self is unknowable to anyone outside the person. <br><br>\r\nand are love marriages really any more successful? i don\'t think so. not when i look around me, when i read the newspaper, when i listen to my friends\' love lives. my marriage - an arranged marriage - is a risk, because marriage is a risk. no matter what kind.<br><br> \r\nagreeing to share your life, to belong with someone else, a human being, with all the frailties and fallibilities of mortals, well, it is a leap of faith. it is partly what makes us humans incredibly stupid, and partly what makes us incredibly magnificent. there\'s a reason angels do not marry. just as there\'s a reason apes don\'t. how wonderful we humans are, to be capable of so much trust, to be aware of the future, to know how much can go wrong, and to still try. <br><br>\r\ni\'ve given up my little girl dreams of love and happy-ever-after. they\'re not realistic. i know. i\'ve had boyfriends. and i\'ve had my wild and glorious affair. with a soulmate. someone i will never ever forget. ever. with someone i think i will be in love with, even when i\'m sitting on a rocking chair in a retirement home.<br><br>\r\nthe thing is, love is not enough. i mean, i will never regret the fireworks, the tenderness with which he kissed my eyelids, the spilt orange juice over his only set of clothes, the sharing of william h gass\'s literary essays...but, but. it is not enough. young love isn\'t. the stormy passionate desperate animal body love just can\'t sustain itself, let alone the couple. at the very least, love doesn\'t buy bread.<br><br>\r\nbut i still have no answer when yi asks \"so you\'re agreeing to a life without love?\" over our omelettes on saturday. why do people (my sisters, my best friend) think that just because i agreed to this arrangement, that i now have all the answers? i don\'t know if i\'m doing the right thing. can any one of us know that, in advance? don\'t we always find out after the fact?<br><br>\r\nwhat i do know is that there is always a trade off. whenever you choose one thing, you are giving up something else. when you decide to take a nap on Tuesday evening, you also decide to miss the latest episode of \'Buffy the Vampire Slayer\'. i had to think long and hard, and look deep within myself. i had to be painfully honest when answering some very tough questions. what do i want? what do i *really* want. now what i think i want. not what i should want. not what i need. not what i desire. not what my parents want for me. not what my friends want for me. not what my ex-boyfriend wants. not what my sisters want. not what society wants. but what do *i* want. no matter how embarrassing or shameless it is. and maybe i want security more than i want passion. maybe i want my parents\' approval more than i want to run away to ireland. if people want to judge my wants, let them. ultimately, it is my life. i have to do what i think is right for me. so i said yes. and i will jump right in, to this, my next adventure.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2392,'<p>\r\ncategory, classification, class, grouping, division.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni understand that to some degree we need classification in life. I understand that we need to bunch things up so that they become simple. but i do not understand why this type of thinking is instilled in every aspect of life. it frustrates me to no end. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen someone tells me a cat is more spiritual than a dog i laugh inside. there is only so much laughing before one is consumed by rage. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think to myself, how can a cat be more spiritual than a dog. how can a dog be more spiritual than a cat. its apples and oranges. its so basic an understanding.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni understand that every living thing has something to say. something to do. something to be. I understand that some of you wont eat other living things. i understand that some of us do.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni understand that we are all the same regardless of religious belief, colour, height, weight, gender, age.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni saw a piece on iraq, talking about how more people are going to the mosques because life there has been getting so bad. iraq used to be a pretty easy going place. women could walk free and goto school. sure, the government was a bit fierce, but society on the whole there was much more liberal then lets say saudi arabia(a friend of the democratic and freedom loving usa).\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit is easy to place faith in god. we let an institution catagorize our life, turning friends into demons and life into a routine. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen overall standards of living go up, the need for simplistic organizations go down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit frustrates me that we, on the whole, dont understand this. we cannot understand that the good of the whole is good for the individual as well. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndont tell me something is spiritual. everything is. everything at the most basic level gets life from the same energy. we are one. all of us. everything. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you like cats more than dogs so be it. i like cats better than dogs. dogs are cool too!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy does it have to be this way?\r\n</p> '),(2393,'cloudy head</br>\r\nsmell of death</br>\r\nfalse steps</br>\r\ndirty dreams</br>\r\nstupid scream</br>\r\nfake hair</br>\r\nlong nails</br>\r\ntongue bite<p>\r\nwhile I bleed with tears</br>\r\nshow me how you kill with a face<p>\r\n\r\nI\'m staring through a haze</br>\r\nlike I\'m waiting for something</br>\r\nanother moment of nothing'),(2394,'you know it\'s just the sound of the microphone</br>\r\nyou know it\'s like melting ice in a freezer</br>\r\neverybody sounds like buzzing receivers</br>\r\neverything comes and goes and means nothing\r\n<p>\r\nworthless talk filling the air</br>\r\nI\'m stuck here being so social</br>\r\nyou want light then smash the light bulbs</br>\r\nblue and red squeals poison the air</br>\r\nwe\'re chasing invisible things\r\n<p>\r\nyou could dissappear</br>\r\nfading into the air</br>\r\nlike televsion waves</br>\r\nfading into commercials</br>\r\ncan you see yourself</br>\r\nvanishing into oblivion'),(2395,'walk on splinters down the road</br>\r\nseeing everything as new</br>\r\nlooking at no one in a sea of people\r\n<p>\r\nthe past was once</br>\r\nnow it\'s late</br>\r\nthe past was once</br>\r\nnow it\'s late\r\n<p>\r\nsliding down the spiral\r\n<p>\r\nI wish to travel down the highway</br>\r\nbut now I\'m sinking in the ground</br>\r\ntomorrow you\'ll have to sweat in the cold</br>\r\njust sit up straight, smile as you walk\r\n<P>\r\nI looked into the eyes of happiness</br>\r\nand laughing at the end</br>\r\nshe spilled her blood on the floor\r\n<p>\r\nand kissed me goodbye on my hands</br>\r\nkissed me goodbye on my hands\r\n<p>\r\nsliding down the spiral'),(2396,'i just got done biting my nails.  i spit them carefully onto the tops of my fingers and then flung them into the trash bin sitting under the corner of my desk.  above it sits the little black no-name pc that refuses to have linux put in it no matter how many times we try.  the cheap, pale *Reflex 15\\\' monitor sits next to it.  it just sits there.  a blue dot on its side with a number written on it, a number indicating its place among the rest of the hardware in this office.  it sits there quietly, blank.  no input whatsoever, nothing to display.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m reading bobby burgess online, drinking what is probably my 6th or 7th mug of coffee today and listening to groove salad spill subtle electronic rhythms from two small cheap speakers - cones no bigger than 2\\\' in diameter sitting atop an ugly IBM box.  in front of me sits my trusty laptop, humming as it does all day, everyday.  connected to the net via stolen dial-up account and ghetto 21.6 connection.  its keys click when i type, it whirs into action when i want it to and it\\\'s lights blink whenever they feel the need.  i often imagine it silently gloating at the attention i shower upon it while sitting next to my company box.  in its hum i almost hear it mutter, \\\'no love for the box, mad love for me\\\'.  my belly is full from lunch.  brandon and i tried the new Lebanese place down the road and threw $8.88 away on a small buffet spread.  the chicken was warm, the lamb almost cold and rice was dreadful.  standing up to look around i see people mill about like people do here.  some people work, others wait for work.  i belong to the latter.  with no calls to make, with nobody\\\'s data to pour over, i just sit here.  quietly typing and staring blankly.<br><br>\r\n\r\nafter i leave this afternoon there will be a cleaning crew that will descend upon this office.  taking away waste, rubbing and polishing, making carpet stand at attention by way of vacuum.  they will move from cube to cube, they will spy on the results of an 8 hour day within a mid-size POS software company.  ethernet cable, broken pens, paper, crumpled notes and business cards will be the only witness to their work.  during their shift they will reach over candy wrappers, they will spy blinking voice-mail lights and discard styrofoam coffee cups with the rims bit off by bored developers.  at last my fingernails too will be gone along with the Cafe Gibron napkin containing notes written in haste while at lunch.  all of this will occur while i\\\'m at home doing nothing.  sitting quietly and staring blankly.  no input whatsoever, nothing to display.<br><br>'),(2397,'<p>today we left work for a bit.  we walked about four blocks in the drizzle until we reached 10th and market.  Zoe unlocked the giant padlock that guarded the service entrance to an old building.  once inside she locked it behind us.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Zoe is a cool brit who has been living in the states for about six or seven months.  she has duel citizenship and is my supervisor.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>we began to walk toward the back of the corridor.  a light flickered on and off as she struggled to open a door in the back.  it finally opened and we stepped straight into a scooby doo cartoon.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>you see, with flashlights in hand, we were there to explore the building.  eventually everthing there will have to be inventoried and removed.  basically, the whole the whole thing was vacant except for what was left behind when the businesses moved out.  the building is going to be torn down to build affordable housing.  it was abandoned in 1999 from what i gathered from looking at a lonely bank of america calendar that was hanging on a wall in one of the sections.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>some of the lights would flicker on, but most had long been burned out.  i felt like i was walking around after a holocaust or some horrible war in a wasteland were all of the people were gone and nothing was left but small reminders scattered here and there.  i picked up a rubber stamp that had someone\\\'s signature on it.  i wondered if it graced the tops of paychecks at one time and what the people were like who used it.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Zoe and i kept exploring.  we went through 3 floors;  we found a ceramics room, a room full of refrigerators, several rooms full of empty cubicles, a large stack of small wastebaskets, a turd on the floor, a room full of old computers, a room full of lightbulbs, a descending hallway with a hand rail that ended into nothing, a room full of office chairs, and more rooms full of random desks, filing cabinets, tables, and boxes.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>we talked as we worked our way through the maze to the exit.  she said that back in london a building like this would be an ideal squat spot and a door would have been left ajar for the right friends to sneak in and take advantage of the free space.  i totally agreed with her and also suggested that it would be an ideal spot to throw a party.  she told me that she had not had a chance to go to any of san francisco warehouse parties.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>i stepped on someone\\\'s baby picture.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>we finally edged our way back into the daylight.  we turned off our flashlights and stepped out onto 10th st.  ahhh... </p>'),(2398,'<p>Condensation slides in vertical rivers down the windowpane. It pools up on the sill and soaks into the curtains as he stares between the droplets at the roofs across the street. He wishes he had binoculars. Either to look farther toward downtown, or farther into the bedroom on the opposite corner. A big man in a wifebeater lives there, leans out the window to look down the block. If he had binoculars, he could check the wifebeater for grease stains. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>When the sun slides down behind the hill it will be time to go. And then it does, and it\'s time to go. He slips into his shoes, sticks his hand into a baggie full of rubber bands and snaps one onto his wrist. He snaps it as he jumps down the stairs, rolls it into a twisted ring as he walks toward the bus stop. Pulls it up and down his forearm while he waits for the bus. Examines its details while he rides. Its width varies, the edges are uneven, it\'s a muddled flesh tone with a dark smudge like charcoal or watered down ink. But only on one side. So he rolls it over with an almost inaudible <i>fwwwaaap</i> so that only the clean side of the rubber will show. No one even notices this. And why would they?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The bus lets him off by the beach, face first into a cold wind, and he rubs his upper arms with his hands to warm them up. There aren\'t many cars parked at the side of the road, and one or two get packed up with blankets and dogs as he watches, to drive away down the street back toward the city into the nameless mass. He checks is watch and looks toward the road that pours into the beachhead and runs his finger over the smooth rubber band as he waits. In half an hour he will give up and walk out to the water. Alone, if he has to. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>He walks out onto the sand avoiding a small pile of dog shit and the remains of a late afternoon fire pit, and stands at the edge of waves daring them to lick his toes. Daring the wind to sting his cheeks. He takes the rubber band between thumb and forefinger and shoots it like a gun into the ocean. </p>\r\n'),(2399,'<P>i bought a motorcycle off craig\'s list.  tonight i revisit the list to see what is for sale.  i thought of perhaps finding a jacket for egg.  but there was an add that said \"motorycle 500\". that was the title.  i thought to myself, hm another peice of shit japanese bike from the late seventies / early eighties that somebody wants out of their garage.  but i was tempted.  what if it was not?  what if it was an italian bike or a german bike?  what if it was something unfathomably cool like a 100cc sprint bike from the fifties.  what if it was a guzzi police bike or an old norton.  an old woman whose husband past away and she saw it out in the garage and just wanted to make room for who knows what.  or a mom whose son left the bike in her garage and then moved to australia.  she just wants it gone.  or it could just be a college student who used this bike, a yamaha or a kawasaki, to deliver pizzas with a rack welded to the back of it while he was in college but he just dropped out and decided that 500 dollars will get him as far as south america on a courier flight.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i follow the link and low and behold, it is a rad bike:  a 1972 bmw r60/5.  i have an r75/5.  koz has an r60/5, and two more r bikes.  dan has an r60/5.  xep has an r60/6 (i think) and an r100/7.  of the people i know that have bikes, more than half have bmw r series bikes from the seventies.  they are classic machines.  their looks are fantastic, especially the /5 bikes with their one piece instrument headlight pod.  xep likes to say that the /5\'s drum brake will lock up.  qq likes to exclaim of xep\'s r100/7, \"how can anyone ride this thing the way it shakes!\" that bikes sets off car alarms, as he illustrated one balmy summer san francisco day. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>my bike will perhaps one day set off a car alarm, but for the moment it wont go above 3500 rpms without shutting down.  it is a pretty thing that was all dirty and cold out in the so called \'hurricane alley\' of marin, you take the first marin exit off the golden gate bridge and go off to the right around the hill and a left up the hillside to a covered parking spot overlooking the north-east bay.  it sat there for two years in the rain.  it had rust all over the mufflers and the speedometer is rusted in place.  the handle bars are brown with rust.  if it had disk brakes, those would have been brown with rust but it has drum breaks, the kind that lock up.  now it looks somewhat presentable in most places.  i replaced the mufflers with shiny knew chrome ones.  i elbow greased the rust off the crome sides of the gas tank and the engine block.  the internals are where it needs the most work now.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have been riding it to work this week and last week get a feeling for just what is wrong with it.  i listen to one side and then the other.  when i am driving down the road, i feel the jugs -- bmw bikes have jugs that stick out the sides where the pistons do their work, very unlike any other bike -- trying to see if they are firing.  i  replaced the spark plugs that the previous owner had not bothered to gap, a procedure of adjusting the distance between the ceramic inside of the spark plug and the metal finger that arches over it.  it is specified to between .6 and .7 millimeters.  anything more can cause the engine to sputter when the spark fails to reach from the electrode that is in the middle of the spark plug surrounded by ceramic to the finger that stretches over above the electrode, in this case at a distance between .6 and .7 millimeters.  that did not seem to solve any problems with my bike.  i posted to a santa cruz bbs asking to borrow a timing gun and got a response, so perhaps a tune up that is definately in order will get the motorcycle running smoother.  that will happen this weekend.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>egg is excited to go riding around on it.  she wants it in good shape by april when she gets back from thailand.  i am uncertain if i will make that deadline, or my bike.  but two nights ago i was out in the garage with a beer listening to the radio and polishing some deposits off from under the gas take that was removed to put new spark plug wires when i sat back and had a moment of love for the machine.  i do not know it that well, and i have not ridden it much, but we are already sharing moments together out in the garage over a beer and the radio.  it is a good motorcycle just for that experience.  it may never be in great riding shape, but i doubt that.  i think it has nothing irreparable wrong with it.  but the road to getting on the road may be a long one.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>that bike on craigs list, the r60/5, i wonder who is going to buy it and sit out in their garage painting over the little dings and tuning the engine?  i hope it is somebody who needs a friend.  old cars and old motorycles are good that way.  they are always there when you have no plans.  they always provide that <i>something to do</i>.  tonight it appears that something for me was surfing craig\'s list\'s bikes of dreams.  i would <a href=\"http://www.craigslist.org/sfo/mcy/2905610.html\">go buy that bike now</a> if i did not already have one to tend to.'),(2400,' i.\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	shoals of fish pass through the face<br>\r\n	of the moon in Ottawa River;<br>\r\n	their opaque bodies dimples<br> \r\n	which appear and disappear<br>\r\n	in the width of a blink<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n ii.\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	the moon is a carnation<br> \r\n	tucked behind<br> \r\n	the ear of the river<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n iii.       \r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	the blue heron knows this moon<br>\r\n	is a ghost; it shivers and walks<br>\r\n	to the rocks on the opposite bank<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	the frogs are strangely silent, their<br>\r\n	green and yellow bellies rising,<br>\r\n	falling like the waves of the river<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n iv.\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	if it was winter now we could<br>\r\n	walk on the surface of the moon<br>\r\n	our hands full of leaves<br>\r\n	like green flags<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>                        \r\n v.\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	we undress each other under a silver maple<br>\r\n	and swim into the river. we learn moonwater<br>\r\n	smells of lemons and rain.<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	if my lover built a dam tonight, his beaver<br>\r\n	head bobbing in the water, perhaps<br> \r\n	he could prevent the moon from crossing<br>\r\n	the dark land on riverback<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	the moon is the gate we must keep<br>\r\n	closed as we lie here in the ferns<br>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n vi.   \r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	if river had consciousness, moon<br>\r\n	would be Paul Broca\'s map<br>\r\n	of the brain<br> \r\n<p></p><p></p>	\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n vii.\r\n<p></p><p></p>\r\n	in Ancient Persia sages pronounced<br>\r\n	the moon a cure for woman\'s curse,<br>\r\n	sleeping sickness, yellow fever. every<br>\r\n	night maidens would stand by rivers,<br>\r\n	their arms outstretched, their small<br>\r\n	bronze cups held to the moonlight<br>'),(2401,''),(2402,'The sun is slain and falls to the east<br>\r\nWhen will it learn?<br>\r\nThe tinking of the pole<br>\r\nCarrying the symbol of a nation lost<br>\r\nTakin\\\' in the world from a rickety outpost<br>\r\nOf the days of hostility<br>\r\nThe pinko dreamer is constrained<br>\r\nBy the very market economy that they deplore<br>\r\nTotal love is something lost on Capitalists<br>\r\nand it imprisons the true Communists<br>\r\nConservatives are only concerned with production<br>\r\nLibertarianism searches for an equality that doesn\\\'t exist<br>\r\nWhere can one find oneself?<br>\r\nWhere can one find truth in all this mess?<br>\r\nI refuse to roll with the punches of life<br>\r\nMy masochistic desires cause me to go rigid when I see one coming<br>\r\nAnd I walk the streets with a heavy brow and tense jaw, searching...<br>'),(2403,'<p><i><b>author\\\'s note:</b> this document was recently retrieved from a box in my basement, where it had been filed away and forgotten for years. it was written in a fit of bored desperation during my first office job in a city that was as yet still quite unwelcoming. i feel better nowadays. and it\\\'s better when read aloud, for some reason.</i></p>\r\n<p>i would like to make something very clear: the bus was not late. i was late. i had to wake up, thirty minutes past my alarm. i had to wash. i had to dry. i had to slip into my underwear, my trousers, my shirt. i had to tie my tie and retie it again. i had to shave. i had to get my suit jacket on. i had to pack my backpack, unpack my backpack, repack my backpack, put on deodorant, feed the cat, brush my hair, use the toilet. i had to walk down the stairs in the dark and turn the light on when i got to the bottom. i had to run back up the stairs, return to my bedroom, get my wallet and keys out of yesterday\\\'s suit. i had to leave my apartment building, go to my car, park it again. i had to walk the two blocks to the bus stop. and i had to stop: stop worrying, stop daydreaming, stop thinking all the time. i had to wake up.</p>\r\n<p>the bus was not late.</p>\r\n<p>i was late.</p>\r\n<p>entering the bus i felt as if i were being consumed, disappeared, wrenched out of my life and away. i stood among the other passengers and tried to ignore the dull heat, the stench of bad breath, of hair shampoo and perfume gone bad. as i stood there, hand on grimy bar, straining not to touch anyone else as the bus lurched forward and back like a drunk, i tried to locate the other office workers on the bus, the other suits and executive women. i do this every morning. we see each other, sometimes nod, sometimes look away in embarassment.</p>\r\n<p>i do not live in a neighborhood where white collars are common. we go underground by night, wear black and leather and lurk in corner bars or in our living rooms, always watching CNN with a glass of something strong clutched in one hand. we eat TV dinners, we order out for chinese, we stock up on prepackaged food. we listen for sirens. we wait for gunshots. we think nervously about that one black man on the bus, the one with the mean eyes. we think about fire and how it is lit. we think and think and think and everything slides by us, the hours are gone and it is time for bed. as we lay there, waiting, the night owl busses go by, making the room tremble. our eyes close. we go away. far away as we can. goodbye.</p>\r\n<p>i dream about the loaded gun i do not have.</p>\r\n<p>the city is large. it eats its young. i am young. i have been devoured alive by the city. there is nobody home. the phones do not work. the power has been shut off and the windows are blacked out with tape. the city is vast. i am so very, very small. </p>\r\n<p>this is my desk. this is my computer. this is my cubicle. this is my life. there is no escape. there is no point in complaining. what is, is. what will be, will be. i am a quiet person. i am a hard-working person. i am working hard at being very, very quiet.</p>\r\n<p>in these offices after hours i am hearing my heart beating. the dull throbbing roar echoes off the walls, rumbles through the floor, is sucked up into the acoustic tiles set into the ceiling. these days have been endless and i cannot breathe anymore. i am losing my self. i have been lost.</p>\r\n<p>please go away.</p>\r\n'),(2409,'<P>this is the very first swinney post of mine ever... and i am a tad bit nervous.  i have long been a voyuer on this page, but until today i never felt as if i had anything to say.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhat is it about heartache that makes you suddenly feel siginificant in ways you didn\'t before?  because it\'s universal or just because it is a solitary thing... hence it makes you self-important?  i worry that it is merely self-importance.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni met someone.  the anti-thesis of myself in many ways... there in lied the intrigue i suppose.  and whereas i am extremely direct and open person, loving and naive to an extent.... he is like beating your head against a brick wall.  how can two people exist within the same body?  and is it truly important for someone to act the same way towards you in public as they do in private?  i think i already know the answer to that though.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen do you just have to give up on someone?  after weeks of public abuse and private consolation?  after you liken them to \"having a screwdriver jabbed into my brain\"... ?\r\n</P><P>\r\ni am non-sensical right now.  i apologize.  what a way to begin.....\r\n</P><P>\r\n-x</P>'),(2410,'<P>this is the very first swinney post of mine ever... and i am a tad bit nervous.  i have long been a voyuer on this page, but until today i never felt as if i had anything to say.\r\n\r\nwhat is it about heartache that makes you suddenly feel siginificant in ways you didn\'t before?  because it\'s universal or just because it is a solitary thing... hence it makes you self-important?  i worry that it is merely self-importance.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni met someone.  the anti-thesis of myself in many ways... there in lied the intrigue i suppose.  and whereas i am extremely direct and open person, loving and naive to an extent.... he is like beating your head against a brick wall.  how can two people exist within the same body?  and is it truly important for someone to act the same way towards you in public as they do in private?  i think i already know the answer to that though.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen do you just have to give up on someone?  after weeks of public abuse and private consolation?  after you liken them to \"having a screwdriver jabbed into my brain\"... ?\r\n</P><P>\r\ni am non-sensical right now.  i apologize.  what a way to begin.....\r\n</P><P>\r\n-x</P>'),(2404,'<p>\r\nit is no secret that i wear the pants in my family.  i\'ve always been a tomboy with dirty fingernails and scabby knees.  jeremy has flounced around in a skirt for as long as i\'ve known him.  he will argue with me on the flouncing part.  on the gender-meter, we are well matched.  i can\'t wait to find out what ezekiel thinks about all this.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni\'ve always been highly mechanically inclined.  i spent several years collecting and tinkering with junker classic VWs.  the closest i got to being feminine during that time was when i swiped a roommate\'s eyelash curling contraption and macguyvered it with a rubber band into clamping off a length of braided fuel line while i was pulling an engine.  other than for that, i really couldn\'t tell you how to use an eyelash curler.  they look like little guillotines.  do you really want something like that close to your eyeball?\r\n</p><p>\r\na girl who spent the night at my house once asked me for some lotion after she got out of the shower.  the best i could offer her was orange go-jo.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit has always seemed to me that being girlish was something of a cop-out.  like you were buying into the dominant male/submissive female paradigm.  how can you run in heels?  how can you fight if you are afraid of breaking a nail?  you can\'t play kickball in a skirt.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nbut secretly, i am a pretty pretty princess.  i want flowers and candy!  i want love notes and garterbelts!  i want to twirl around in a circle and see my skirt spin out away from me!  i want to smell like springtime and drape my sexy underwear-clad form softly against satin sheets! \r\n</p><p>\r\nbut how do i reconcile my desire to be pretty with my utilitarian mentality? \r\n</p><p>\r\nmore and more lately, i\'ve been coming to covet items of girlishness.  i buy them and they sit sadly unused.  i\'ve got bottles and bottles of nailpolish that have never been on my fingers, dresses that hang limply in the very back of my closet, actual girl shoes waiting daintily next to endless variations on the tried and true scuffed black boot.    \r\n</p><p>\r\nas i type this, i\'m sitting here in a pair of beat-up overalls.  underneath, i\'m wearing the frilliest pair of underwear you can imagine.  my feet, which are stuffed into gym socks and then huge clunky shoes, are adorned with silver nail polish.  my fingernails are painted lavendar and are of a length that i am sure is not conducive to the opening of cans of soda.  \r\n</p><p>\r\ni\'ve been wearing colored bits of ribbon in my hair lately.  you\'d be amazed by all the little clippies and doohickies i didn\'t know how to work.  i spent an afternoon over at kristie\'s house learning about what to do with all this hair i\'ve been growing.  it\'s been almost two years since i\'ve cut it.  it used to be that i would just as soon shave my head as comb my hair.  now my head is a swarm of ringlets.  nothing is more romantic than a ringlet.\r\n</p><p>\r\nat the request of someone i like a whole lot, i wore a dress that showed my legs out in public.  he said he liked my calves and wanted to see more of them.  i believe i blew him off and made snide comments and generally stammered until i could change the subject.  i was secretly titilated!  despite the scoffing, i got around to it.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nas it turns out, i didn\'t have to climb a tree the entire evening.  it was like a crash course in sitting like a lady.  even though i felt like a cross-dresser, a girl named jen said my legs were sexy.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nimagine that!\r\n</p>'),(2405,''),(2406,''),(2407,'testtesttesttesttesttesttesttesttest'),(2408,''),(2412,'<p>he meowed, he purred, he ate, and cuddled.<br>\r\nhe cleaned himself with his little white paws<br>\r\nhe licked his anus clean.<br>\r\nhe\'d saunter up ontop your bladder on any early morn<br>\r\nand then make sounds like a dying cow <br>\r\nuntil you got up and filled his purple cat shaped food bowl.<br>\r\nif you couldn\'t find him stuffing his adorable little face<br>\r\nyou might try your bed where he crawls under the covers and sleeps all day.<br>\r\nif you\'re lucky, you might catch him licking his genitals<br>\r\nand boy, what a treat that is!<br>\r\nhe\'s just the cutest thing when he lifts his little hind leg up high in the air and bends down to his chubby and laps it clean.<br>\r\noh, fernando, so handsome in his tuxedo fur and his bow tie collar with little black bell.<br>\r\nding-a-ling-a-ling-a-ling...and i would say<br>\r\n\"oh, here comes my handsome little furball.\"<br>\r\nbut fernando would do the darndest thing on occasion<br>\r\nhe\'d shit and pee on you or me.<br>\r\nhe\'d shit on beds and chairs and clothes<br>\r\nhe\'d shit on carpet floors and flannel duvet<br>\r\nhe even shit on oof.<br>\r\npoor oof,<br>\r\ndon\'t worry buddy, you\'re not the only one<br>\r\nhe peed on me<br>\r\nand it was a whole lot.<br>\r\nhe peed in jay\'s brand new clogs and stank them up<br>\r\nthe incontinent furball sat next to his work with an eerie smirk and a glint in his eyes.<br>\r\na funny thing with fernando though<br>\r\n(and this i find most amazing)<br>\r\nevery time he dumps a load of fresh steamy poo on some unauthorized area<br>\r\nhe does a sort of trot, maybe even a gallop if you will.<br>\r\nhe\'ll gallop out of the room where he did his deed, and then continue to gallop around the house<br>\r\nas if to say, \"woo-pee, i shit a pile on your bed.\"<br>\r\nhe did this trot last night on valentine\'s day,<br>\r\nand right away, i fell suspect to his incontinent ways<br>\r\nand as i entered my room,<br>\r\nlo and behold, a fresh pile of poo<br>\r\nstill steaming and juicy<br>\r\nstinking up my duvet.<br></p>\r\n<p>i wish i knew a good taxidermist.<br>\r\nmaybe fernando and i will meet again<br>\r\nmaybe we won\'t<br>\r\nall i can say is <br>\r\nfernando will be the only cat i\'ll ever love<br>\r\ncause now i\'m a dog person.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2411,'i\'m embarrassed to admit i took advantage of the free Lover\'s chocolate latte coupon (the vanilla brownie fudge one) from <a href=http://www.secondcup.com/home.asp>Second Cup</a> with a coworker-turned-friend yesterday. the whipped cream and lindt chocolate shavings were sweet and rich and i just couldn\'t describe the combination of their flavour to you. this is yet another reason i admire <a href=http://www.austinchronicle.com/issues/dispatch/deep_focus/cuisines_austinrestaurants.html>food writers and restaurant reviewers</a>. it is ridiculously hard to transport the tang and honey of a meal through the sterility of words.\r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\nhowever, it has been confirmed: i cannot abide coffee - bitter but intense - all at the same time. i think maybe it confuses my taste buds. i couldn\'t finish the latte. actually, i barely started it. i took a few sips and had to give up.\r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\nright opposite the cafe there was a tiny little flower shop. the line of men at 4.45pm (just after work on Valentine\'s day, on their way home) was so long it stretched out far beyond the doorway of the store, and into the tiled passages of the mall itself. there were a few teenagers with their requisite baggy jeans, and lots of men in ties, their bald heads shining in the flourescent light. it was almost funny. i would hate to receive a last minute duty-bound bouquet on this officially bizarre day.\r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\ni don\'t know where my life is going. it is like i am in some sort of perma-fugue state. lately, i keep leaving work early; just taking off, without telling anyone, heading out on my own through the woods. i don\'t do anything. that\'s the point. sometimes i wander through the market by myself. other times i just walk through the streets of unknown neighbourhoods. it doesn\'t matter where i\'m going. i trust my feet. \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\nthe sky is all winter smoky, and breathing the february air hurts because it is so cold. the tips of my fingers go numb, as i take these confident long strides. if people were watching me pass by, they\'d think i had a specific destination in mind. \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\ni know doing this doesn\'t solve anything. but nothing helps, really. at least these wanderings give me a temporary reprieve, a delicate sort of peace, enough to let me go home able to face my parents, the slow voice of my soon-to-be fiance, for one more evening.\r\n\r\n'),(2413,'<p>\r\nI have searched for my family on the internet. I have found two things.\r\n<a\r\nhref=\"http://www.unlovedcrimes.com/wwwboard/messages/367.html\">this</a>\r\nand <a\r\nhref=\"http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Hills/9639/B24J.html\">this</a>.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nLeonard Ross Blodgett was my great grandfather. He, according to popular\r\nbelief, moved to San Francisco from Los Angeles. He married a local woman\r\nby the name of Veronica Devlin and depending upon which birth certificate\r\nyou believe, he was anywhere from 2 to 25 years her senior. Leonard was\r\nalso thought to have another, separate family in Los Angeles. When I found\r\nthe above link, I couldn\'t help but wonder if the woman who posted it was\r\nfrom that mystery other family.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nVeronica was one of 13 children, including two sets of twins. I know a few\r\nthings about a few of them and that\'s all. Leo and Leona were one set of\r\ntwins. Leona died as a child and Leo lived his last 20 years in San  \r\nQuentin, serving a life term for murder. He murdered a woman he was out\r\nwith on a date, according to my mother. Another relative insists that it\r\nwasn\'t his fault and he somehow ended up taking the fall for it. My\r\ndistant cousin Liam, who I believe is one of Leo\'s sister\'s daughter\'s son\r\n(whew!), joined Leo in San Quentin on a drug charge. Another of Veronica\'s\r\nsisters was married to a man named Mickey who was, among other things, a\r\nbootlegger, bookie, possibly mafia-affiliated small-time thug. The legend that surrounds Mickey is another entry/book altogether. He was a magnetic,\r\nfrightening, fascinating, dominating character in our family and the\r\nstories about him range from the sinister to the amazing to the truly\r\nbizarre.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nLeonard (everyone called him Jack, but since there are two other Jacks in \r\nthe story, i\'ll stick with Leonard for clarity) and Veronica had 4\r\nchildren: Betty, Joan, Jack (papa, my grandfather), and Putsie. Leonard  \r\nworked with Mickey running whiskey around town during prohibition and\r\nkeeping book out the back of a gas station somewhere near Van Ness. They\r\ntook bets mostly on the races at Bay Meadows Race Track, where quite a few\r\nof our relatives worked. (side note: Mickey eventually ran off with the\r\nowner\'s daughter.)\r\n<p>\r\nIn 1927, when Jack was 4, Leonard was found murdered at\r\nthe bottom of a stairwell, hit in the back of the head with a pipe. None  \r\nof the papers ran a story on it and according to the legend of Mickey, he\r\nhad something to do with that. Jack never knew his dad had actually been \r\nmurdered until the 70s when my uncle Mike did some research into family\r\nhistory in college.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the early 1930s, Veronica died as well. Papa always told me that she\'d\r\ndied of pneumonia. Just recently, Aunt Betty told me that she had been a\r\nprostitute and had died of an abortion gone wrong. She has never told Jack\r\nthis. With both of their parents dead, Betty, Joan and Putsie went to the \r\nconvent. Jack became a \"ward of the state\" and was sent to St. James boys\r\nschool on 23rd and Fair Oaks Streets (just up the hill from Guerrero). He,\r\nlike most of us in this family, had some problems so they sent him to a\r\nranch for juvenile deliquents in Ukiah. The place was run by monks. He\r\nstole one of their cars and drove it back to the city. Later they placed\r\nhim in a few different foster homes. As soon as he was old enough to work\r\nthe track, Mickey and his brother-in-laws took him in. When he was 18, he\r\njoined the Army.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ll move on to the World War II years in another installment. One of the\r\nmost interesting things, to me, about this family is the vast difference  \r\nbetween everyone\'s \"truths.\" For example: According to Aunt Joan, Uncle\r\nLeo had a ranch. According to fact, he died in San Quentin serving a life\r\nterm for murder. According to Papa, his mom sewed stuff for a living,   \r\naccording to Betty she was a whore. According to fact, she died when papa\r\nwas 7. According to Aunt Joan, she had one sister and one brother.\r\nAccording to fact, she neglected to tell her husband or her kids about\r\nPutsie, which put a damper on things when Putsie showed up at her doorstep\r\none day.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs I write this, I\'m trying to stay as clear as possible but there are so\r\nmany asides and digressions racing through my head, i can barely stay on \r\ntrack. For instance, I want to talk about Jack Campbell (known as either\r\nJack Who or Soup) but I can\'t remember how exactly he is related. I will \r\nsay this: he got the name Jack Who because he\r\ncame home late one night after being gone for a couple days, most likely\r\nat Bay Meadows spending his paycheck (which he got from working at Bay\r\nMeadows), and his wife jumped up in bed and yelled WHO\'S THERE!? and he\r\nsaid, shh, baby, it\'s Jack and she yelled back JACK WHO!? Of course the\r\nrest of the family caught wind of it and he never lived it down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore later\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2414,'<p>if i was a scientist, i would know why rain seems to leech the colour out of dog poop on the sidewalk.  but alas, i\\\'m no scientist, and this, among other things, will remain a mystery to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i live at 193 concord ave, also known as the blue house.  someone, somewhere decided that our half of the crumbling duplex should be painted ultramarine blue, so it was done.  the paint is peeling along the eaves now, revealing chunky red bricks underneath.  the front yard is small and surrounded by a homemade fence of painted iron pipes.  it\\\'s a \\\"low-maintenance garden\\\"-- basically a total mess.  strings of ivy snake through the overgrown grass and trees tilt madly, held in place by bungee cords.  but i like it.  our house has character.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>indoors, we have another low-maintenance garden.  piles of books, papers, and empty dinner plates creep across the floor like oversized snails.  one never knows where they\\\'ll be.  a gasoline generator sits in the middle of the living room, emitting an evil stench.  beside it, there\\\'s a stack of pine crates full of film equipment.  the crates are from a fish market, and they tilt wildly as i tiptoe past.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>amy\\\'s in the kitchen.  she\\\'s been stuck there all week, like the butt of some chauvinist joke.  she volunteered to cater for scott\\\'s film crew, feeding twelve to fifteen people a day.  piles of dirty and clean dishes cover every surface, and groceries spill across the floor.  amy is reading tolstoy at the kitchen table, waiting for muffins to bake, and frowning.  i\\\'d frown too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>nobody else is around right now.  scott is off shooting his film.  eli has disappeared again, and thom is at work (which is really his home anyhow).  for once the neighbours aren\\\'t on the front porch waiting to give us helpful household tips.  \\\"now that you\\\'re on your own, don\\\'t forget to turn off the porch light!  electricity is expensive you know.\\\"  the local madwoman is taking a vacation.  she\\\'s not outside posting cardboard signs on the lampposts, or spraying cars with soy sauce.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all is quiet.  and yet there\\\'s a hum in my molars.  everything is perched on the verge, waiting to explode into activity.  streams of ivy will shoot across the sidewalk, the generator will sputter into life, the oven alarm will ding, and the whole neighbourhood will bang down my front door.  i get this feeling every day around 3 o\\\'clock.  i think that even if i were a scientist, it\\\'s something i could never explain.</p>'),(2417,'<p>\r\nmy old aunt lulu, the black sheep of her mormon family, was a nutjob and a gambler, as well as a romantic and an animal lover.  she lived in reno to be close to the action, but she and her nasty chihuahua bobo also took several trips a month to the ocean.  one of her favorite hobbies was to collect sand dollars, which she would tuck away into her mile-long cleavage.  once home, she would nail the sand dollars to the wall using a single nail right through the center.  she had been doing this for years and had figured out a way to do it without shatering them.  when i visited her, she had one entire room decorated floor-to-ceiling this way, and she was working on another.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe and bobo lived alone (i am not sure if she was ever married) and they were both about as old and decrepit as you can get.  bobo was so old, in fact, that he sported a constant doggie boner which he no longer had the muscle control to retract.  before having company over for dinner one evening, i remember lulu taking bobo into the bathroom to splash cold water on his dong.  she called this \\\"making bobo presentable\\\".\r\n</p><p>\r\nof course, it only lasted for a few minutes.  my memory of the dinner is of several old ladies transfixed in horror as they watched an ancient and scabby bobo alternate between eating his own meal off of a tiny little place setting on the floor (complete with silverware and napkin) and \r\nlicking his erection with joyous abandon.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(my grandma suffered from a similar sick relationship with a turtle.  she had originally bought it for her garden, but became obsessive about taking care of it.  she made a little bed for it in the garage next to the water heater.  her and my grandpa used to take it on walks on leash through the neighborhood.  they are both in their 80s, so it was probably a chore to keep up with the turtle.  once, the turtle became very sick because my grandmother applied several coats of weatherproofing to its shell before the rainy season.  their neighbors finally came and liberated the wretched thing one day.  we all told my grandma that the turtle must have run away.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nlulu would make several trips a week to the casinos, where she would spend her day at the nickel slots.  bobo, being her good luck charm, rode along in her purse.  she was spoken to by the management at a couple of casinos because bobo would often lunge from her purse at anyone who was \r\nunfortunate enough sit at the next slot machine.  instead of leaving the dog at home, like they asked, lulu would try to sedate bobo with \r\ncocktails.  bobo liked jack and coke. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthere are few things in the world that are meaner than a drunk chihuahua, until you consider a hung-over chihuahua.  lulu would have to jumpstart \r\nbobo in the mornings with a little hair of the dog (ha!  i make funny!) so that the dog would be sleeping like a baby in her handbag.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\\\'t know if it was a poorly ventilated purse, a little to much booze or a particularly lengthy winning streak that did bobo in, but one day he \r\nwent paws up in her handbag.  as you can imagine, lulu was terribly upset.  bobo spent many months in the freezer and was visited several times a day \r\nby lulu.  lulu wasn\\\'t going to the beaches or casinos anymore, since she couldn\\\'t take bobo with her.  it was a pretty sad situation.  there is a \r\nphenomena that happens to really close elderly couples.  when one dies, often times the other is not far behind.   everyone was worried that \r\nsomething like this was happening to lulu.\r\n</p><p>\r\none day, she started going to the casinos like normal again.  apparently, she had sent the bobo carcass away to be freezdried into a life-like pose, which she carried around with her for many, many years until she died.  what happened to bobo after lulu died is sort of a family secret.  i\\\'m \r\npretty sure she wanted him thrown into the coffin with her, but i wouldn\\\'t be surprised if there is a chihuahua corpse sitting in a handbag in some \r\nsalvation army somewhere.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalthough i sometimes get the urge to dress the cats up in little lord fauntleroy outfits, i am hoping that i have not inherited this particular \r\nbrand of crazy.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n'),(2415,'They remembered walkin\\\' in and feeling the attention focus. You could say the gaze felt like heat or that the air was charged with electricity but, it doesn\\\'t really matter - you get the picture. The two of them would walk down into the\r\ncrowd both aflame and everyone\\\'s eyes suddenly turned into moths.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMost of these memorable moments had been proceeded by a sexual encounter of one sort or another. Usually in the upstairs coat room, the key to which the owner would give to them when they arrived, or if it was a particularly\r\nexhibitionist evening right out in the hallway. They were for these moments the closest this provincial city would get to celebrity or aristocracy and others would devour them with their eyes. It didn\\\'t matter the gender male or female people just simply wanted to get close to them, like maybe some of the glamour would wear off.<br><br> \r\n\r\nTheir dramatic stories seemed to circulate like bad rumors through the club scene. They had gotten together under circumstances most only find in movies. He had seen her one night at the club and like Babe Ruth turned to his friends\r\nand pointed saying \\\"I will know her\\\". She upon meeting him had asserted that \\\"she was for no one\\\". It was only after she walked in on him and another women that had followed him home, that she laughingly commented \\\"When your\r\ndone with this come up and talk to me.\\\" The following conversation didn\\\'t involve too many words, after which she promptly kicked him out of her bed and remarked \\\"You can\\\'t stay here honey, if I remember correctly you have a guest you have to entertain. Just no more guests after this, ok.\\\" That\\\'s how it all started.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThe next year was a blur as they were out every night and put on the list for more events then they could attend. They didn\\\'t pay for anything when they were out, it was as if they were at a friends house that wanted to make sure that\r\ntheir company wanted for nothing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHighlights included the night that some out of town drug dealer so far gone mistook him for someone that owed him money. Outside the club while the two were talking the dealer rushed him with a knife. He side stepped in time but before he could react she had stepped forward grabbed the dealer by the arm popped his elbow and then proceeded to push him back on his ass. People talked about that one for months. On Halloween they had both shown up dressed like aristocracy from Czarist Russia and were treated accordingly. They exuded a sensuality and eroticism that was\r\nthe envy of porn directors.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhere did it all start to turn like some dairy product that had been left in the fridge too long? Its hard to say maybe it was the first time she balked at his proposal of marriage or maybe it was later when she asked him the same and it was his turn to dig in his heals. His concern for upward social mobility at any cost and her desire for happiness regardless started to clash though.<br><br> \r\n\r\nBoth of them could tell something had been misplaced, they suddenly found that they weren\\\'t the flame everyone\\\'s eyes were attracted to any longer. Maybe they hadn\\\'t been right from the start but they hung on fighting, \\\"How could\r\nsomething that felt so right at one time not be meant to be?\\\" The struggle to recapture something was a brutal one and gave rise to so many negative emotions it was hard for them to even remember what they were fighting for. The same passion that had made them magical in the beginning had turned to the dark side and now filled them with contempt. They still strained to try and fix things driven by instinct or some other unconscious force but it didn\\\'t matter. When they watched others now they both found themselves asking if those people had been like they had been, did they know some way to fix things?<br><br> \r\n\r\nThe struggle consumed them both he fell into severe medical depression and a promising career evaporated. For her it registered in her physical health, doctors didn\\\'t give her very long. They had been each others undoing, but in the end they found themselves digging underneath all the contempt and negativity and remembering the moments when they had been everything they had wanted in each other.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThey were left wondering as each dealt with their own fallout if this was simply the price they had to pay to have those few moments that they did.<br><br> '),(2418,'<p> melbournes weather and that of victoria has been dominated by clouds this summer I have a theory about that!\r\nI heard somewhere that the antarctic is having a heatwave! well the fridge door has been left open and we are standing at the opening as the cool moist air flows toward us so that is why we have had a cooler summer I\\\'m giong to ask the beaureau ( i\\\'ve forgotten how to spell beurau) that is the weather b. and see what they think.</p>'),(2419,'my upstairs neighbor is a 40 year old hair rocker who lives all alone. his name is ralph. sometimes i hear the harmonic wailing of axl rose coming through the floor. recently i determined that ralph plays bass along to poison, guns n roses, and lynrd skynrd. i can tell from the thumping on the floor, in time to the music. i can only assume he his pumping a guitar pedal.\r\n</p><p>\r\none time i ran into ralph in the hallway. he was carrying his bicycle. he told me that he used to deliver large appliances to rich people. refrigerators and dishwashers. ralph is a nice guy but i can tell he\\\'s a little lonely. when i ran into him in the hall, he talked to me for a long time. he offered to teach me how to ride my bike defensively in the city. he said <i>i\\\'ve been riding my bike in the bay area for twenty years! i can show you the ropes!</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis morning, i knocked on ralph\\\'s door. the reason i did this is because earlier this evening all my housekeys fell off my substandard keychain and into oof\\\'s jacket pocket. the reason they fell out into oof\\\'s pocket is because he was carrying my wallet and keys for me. the reason he was carrying  my wallet and keys is because, as a stylish female, i often buy clothing without functional pockets. this morning at 3am oof harbored a  surrogate functional pocket. after the housekeys fell off my shitty keychain (the false carabeener style keychain), and into oof\\\'s pocket, i only retrieved some of them, not all of them. the key i neglected to retrieve opens the door to my apartment. unfortunately, i did not realize this until about five minutes before i knocked on ralph\\\'s door this morning.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso, i knocked on ralph\\\'s door because his window opens on the same fire escape as my window one floor below.\r\n\r\ni listened at his door first, because it was 8am, and i didn\\\'t want to wake him up. his television was on. i knocked. ralph came to the door in grey workout shorts and a tshirt. i explained myself and he let me in, gracious and vaguely hopeful seeming, which is his way.\r\n</p><p>\r\nralph\\\'s apartment is tiny like mine, only his is littered with sports memorabilia. he seemed eager to help me get out onto the fire escape but also nervous about his apartment. <i>don\\\'t mind the mess</i> he fretted, as he moved things out of the way of the window. <i>oh mine\\\'s just as  bad</i> i said, and looked around. <i>worse</i>, i amended, realizing that there was a clear path from his front door to his window, and his mangled oriental rug was clearly visible. clear walkways and visible floors are a hot commodity in my apartment. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni ignored his advice about how to get through the window (<i>so put your head through first</i> he prompted) and put one leg through first, then folded the rest of my body out into the cold, my back briefly comingling with a plant that resided near the window. i looked back in at him: <i>now we just have to hope the window is unlocked</i>. he peered at me from inside the frame of his window, a portrait of himself in his own apartment, stringy blonde hair and a big open helpful face:, <i>i have a set of picks. i\\\'m a locksmith.</i> i hadn\\\'t heard him on my way down the ladder: <i>you used to be a locksmith?</i> <i>no, i AM a locksmith.</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy window was unlocked, a wide open portal to the cozy mess beyond. i pushed it up and folded myself into window-body again,  rewinding the motion i had used to get out of ralph\\\'s window and playing it back. my interaction with ralph was cut off abruptly then, with thankyous yelled up the fire escape. having ralph around is pretty good. i actually really enjoy it when he plays GnR loud, and i can sing along through the floor.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2416,''),(2420,'<P>this week marked the beginning of that period of time where young people like myself find the need to be slutty, in order to speed up the heartache and moving on process that comes from ending a 3 year long, intense relationship.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni spent the evening with a guy friend of mine, not fully intending to let anything sexual happen. of course, this being the week that it was, i suppose it was inevitable, and before i knew it, he was in my bed. this was fine, until i stuck my hand down his pants and realized something that i am only now coming to terms with: i\\\'ve been ruined.\r\n</P><P>\r\nas many problems that i had with my ex, there were many things that i will always love about him, and one of those things was that he had a very large, very shapely cock. i\\\'m only now realizing that this cock will probably continue to haunt me for a long time, and has already caused for a somewhat awkward (for me, i would not be so cruel as to admit any of this out loud to the new guy) situation for me. upon reaching my hand down his pants, i had a revelation. from now on, and perhaps indefinitely, i will have to redesign my entire mental conception of penis size. right now, i don\\\'t feel that i really have any notion of what is \\\"big\\\" and what is \\\"small.\\\" perhaps this new guy i was with does not have a small penis, perhaps it is only avg. but i don\\\'t know, because all i am used to is big.\r\n</P><P>\r\nso the reason i post this is because i am feeling guilty and confused, on this rainy sunday morning. how long will it take before my archetypal Penis goes away? how long must i grasp a new cock and simultaneously feel the slow spread of disappointment through my veins?\r\n</P>