n error message?\r\n\r\n\r\nWarning: fopen(\\\"\\\",\\\"r\\\") - Error 0 in\r\n/sky/www/swinney.org/swinsite.02.06.01/swinsite/journals/vip/process_article.ph\r\ntml\r\non line 73\r\n\r\n                   [Message sent and copied to \\\"sent-mail\\\".]\r\n? Help       < MsgIndex   P PrevMsg      - PrevPage D Delete     R Reply\r\nO OTHER CMDS > ViewAttch  N NextMsg    Spc NextPage U Undelete   F Forward\r\n'),(2429,'outside a golden silence</br>\r\nwaiting for you to feel it</br>\r\njust light up your eyes</br>\r\nand maybe you can see it\r\n<p>\r\ntomorrow might never come</br>\r\ntomorrow is a stolen car\r\n<p>\r\nI regret something</br>\r\nthat has not happened yet</br>\r\na strange distortion of the blues</br>\r\nI feel sores</br>\r\nof wounds I do not have</br>\r\nwhat a treacherous way of killing me.\r\n'),(2430,'- kiss me</P><P></P><P>*kiss*</P><P></P><P>- i want to take your hand and put it under my shirt</P><P>- do you think that is a good idea?</P><P>- no</P><P>- then let\\\'s not think about it\r\n</P><P></P><P>\r\n<i>\r\n[2002-02-16, after midnight] </i>\r\n</P><P></P><P>\r\n</P><P></P><P>\r\nit is as if yesterday never happened. the lion has been buried in the backyard. weeds are already growing on its grave. </P><P></P><P>you\\\'re sitting on a cushion on the floor, in your favourite cool-band t-shirt and boxers. i\\\'m curled under a blanket in a thin striped tank top and mismatched pyjama bottoms. the darkness of the night is strangely comforting. i\\\'m twirling my fingers in my hair, and you\\\'re laughing. </P><P></P><P>last night\\\'s desire, jealousy, pain, chaos no longer exist. \\\'we\\\'re going to be okay\\\' you promise. i want to believe you. i put my hands on the ice cold window pane and nod, as silent as winter breath.\r\n</P><P></P><P>\r\n<i>\r\n[2002-02-17, evening] </i>'),(2431,'it is snowing wildly outside, the sky swirling out confetti in endless white crystal showers. the cars in our parking lot are sweetened with icing from boot to hood. i\\\'m tempted to go out there and fingerpaint on their windshields. i never noticed before, how curvy a car body is.\r\n<p></P>\r\nmy mouth tastes like chocolate. i\\\'m hovering, my palms tiny humming birds, in that space where one relationship ends and another is about to begin. last night i felt so desperately alone i crushed my wet eyelids into the corners of my pillow. my hair got tangled in the telephone cord and i gave into temptation and called him. but the international phone card screwed up, and i couldn\\\'t get through.\r\n<p></P>\r\ni\\\'m so childish. even though we are no longer lovers, i want him to choose me, always. i want him to think of the shape of my mouth, to remember that almond shaped birthmark on my torso that no-one else has seen. \r\n<p></P>\r\ni still can\\\'t believe he dispatched me into a blue sleeping bag on the floor of his dorm, as if i was just anybody. it almost drove me to leave, to knock on the doors of his housemates. i wanted to seek someone else, to trace my fingers over another man\\\'s chest, and lose myself under a stranger\\\'s blankets. \r\n<p></P>\r\ni went to the drug store after work yesterday. while paying for my prescription, i couldn\\\'t stop looking at the giant stuffed velvet red hearts, the tacky cards of cartoon giraffes with entwined necks, objects i\\\'ve always despised, and never never wanted, i swear. now there is a sour taste, right here, right under my tongue. the chocolate melts quietly at the back of my throat. \r\n<p></P>\r\nconnie and i were talking about joanne and her father today. i can\\\'t pass by any unfinished relationship, lying in a come-hither pile on the smooth glass coffee table, without reaching out to try to fit just one more piece in the right place. given enough time, i could singlehandedly solve every abandoned jigsaw puzzle in the world. \r\n<p></P>\r\nhappy valentine\\\'s day.\r\n\r\n'),(2427,'dong'),(2425,'<p>I\\\'m in an interesting place right now.  I can\\\'t quite put my finger on\r\nit exactly but something is changing either about me or around me.  I\r\nthink my ego is winning the battle for control lately.  Actually, I know\r\nthis and, frankly, I am both relieved and happy at the prospects of\r\nthis.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m not focusing on the negativity of things and I am just enjoying\r\nsituations, even the unpleasant ones.  Things that would knock me flat on\r\nmy emotional ass previously aren\\\'t even phasing me.  You can actually get\r\nthis in prescription form, I believe, but I\\\'m picking this up naturally.\r\nI don\\\'t even know where it\\\'s come from but I\\\'m definitely up and\r\nclimbing.</p>\r\n<p>Now, perhaps there is a downside to all this.  For instance,\r\nhistorically, I have cared a lot about the problems that my friends were\r\ngoing through and what-not.  But lately I don\\\'t care.  I\\\'m not bothering\r\nmyself with other people\\\'s shit.  I\\\'ve pretty much always taken a nomadic\r\nslant on these sorts of things and have never asked people for help\r\nbecause I honestly don\\\'t feel that anyone actually can offer me any help.\r\nInversely, I was always willing to offer some advice or a friend to lean\r\non, whatever the situation dictated.  However, I just don\\\'t want to\r\nbother.  At least for right now.  I find myself saying \\\"yeah, whatever\\\"\r\ninstead of \\\"that sucks\\\" most times.  I don\\\'t get involved of those \\\"Oh\r\nyeah?  I\\\'ve got more to do than you and I\\\'m broker than you and I have to\r\nwork this much and I have so much less time than you do\\\" self-defeating\r\ndick length contests.  Those fun conversations where everyone tries to\r\noutdo everyone else with how much is on their particular plate.  I just\r\ndon\\\'t fucking care anymore.</p>\r\n<p>I think I\\\'m cleaning out the closet that is my life right now.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m searching for change in the couch of my personality.</p>\r\n'),(2432,'<p>Later, after Pam tried on her Japanese silk, tailored pants ($25) where they keep trying to get her to buy more, we cut down a soi off Kao Sahn to get back to our street, Ram Bhuttri. We passed a little bar with Anglos drinking beers at tables and went on into the alley. Before too long, we passed a table where a man and a tall, slender woman were sitting. Except the woman was a rather feminine-looking man wearing a tube top and jeans. We barely got by the table and the woman stood up and started walking behind us. She made\r\nno attempt at being stealthy, nor was she making any overt moves towards us. Passing mom-and-pop\r\nrestaurants, homes, and the back doors of shops, we continued onwards. We both turned and looked back, casually and independently of each other, but this did not deter our friend.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"I think we\\\'re being followed,\\\" I said.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Yeah, it looks like that,\\\" Pam answered.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The soi ran parallel to a street we had walked\r\npreviously, where I had seen several side sois that\r\nconnected to the one we were currently on. Pam was in front of me so I told her to turn off the next time she could as the alley was only getting darker.\r\nAnother 20 feet and we turned between a couple of\r\nbusinesses and got out to the street. As we turned\r\nleft onto the sidewalk, we looked back to see the\r\nsilhouetted figure of our follower looking at us--long, straight hair, hand on a thin-strapped purse, and non-female hips cocked to one side--and\r\nthough we couldn\\\'t see her face, I really felt a\r\npissed-off vibe directed at us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We never felt like we were in danger; it was just weird. Drugs? A mugging? Or did we look like we wanted some action? Is that alley known for hookers? It is for us now. The only answer that made sense was a transvestite prostitute, of which we\\\'ve heard there are many here. There\\\'s a kind of subculture of homosexuals, transvestites, and transgendered individuals here, many of them freely co-mingling in the places worked by XX chromosomed women (naturally born women if that XX is wrong).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We know all there is to know about the Crying Game now.</p>\r\n'),(2433,'Iâ€™m not too certain where to start or what to say.  Several people that I know are going through, or have been through, very difficult times with their parents.\r\n\r\n<p>We were my Fatherâ€™s second family.  Both of my parents were older than most newlyweds of their era, but my Father was a good deal older.  He was 40 when I was born, and 45 when my youngest sister was born.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My Fatherâ€™s memories were of the Depression, and World War II.  My Fatherâ€™s Father was an American by virtue of the fact that my Fatherâ€™s Fatherâ€™s Father brought his pregnant wife to New York on one of their â€œimport-exportâ€ ventures.  They sailed from the Mediterranean to New York, and Sao Paolo in O Brazil â€“ Iâ€™m not certain where else they went.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were old stories about people being wrapped-up in rugs and dropped over the side of the boat mid-ocean, and warnings not to search too hard for the family past.  They were hooked-up with the Catholic Church somehow, and my Father used to brag about his Fatherâ€™s Jesuit education â€“ he could speak several languages, and he was supposed to be a pretty sharp guy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Family lived on the West Side of the city; â€œthose kindâ€ of people had to live in certain neighborhoods in Chicago.  Little Egypt danced with my Fatherâ€™s Mother and the other ladies when they got together.  My Fatherâ€™s Mother was as Celtic as the day was long, but she married dark.  So, she was dark too.  She learned their language.  She learned to cook their food.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think that my Father had a hard time growing up.  It took me a long time to ever consider the possibility that he had been young once too.  My Fatherâ€™s first marriage took a lot out of him, I think.  He tried to bring my half-brother into our home to visit.  That took a lot out of us.  My own Mother, displaying her infinite Christian forgiveness, attempted to tolerate the circumstances.  But, something wasnâ€™t right with my half-brother and I think that heâ€™s still being held by the state.  I donâ€™t know.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My Father went in the Army.  He was in the Engineers.  Most of the guys like him drove things or built things when they went in, in that era; thatâ€™s what they were supposed to be good at.  When he got out he went to work in the printing industry.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He sang in the Opera at night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dad drank a lot.  Grandpa said that it was his â€œIrish Bloodâ€ that made him do it.  Dad smoked.  When Dad got home from work heâ€™d lay down on the floor, propped up against the sofa.  There he would lie till he passed out.  His cigarette in hand or on the table, drink held tight and never spilled, he would lie.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I lifted weights and learned to fight.  One day he thought twice about yelling at me and settled for cynicism thereafter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dad took a real long time to die.  It seemed like forever.  His last round of surgery and treatments lasted for at least a year.  I never went to see him in the hospital.  I never went to the hospital until the last week that he was there.  God it was hard.  The last day of his life I got a call while I was leaving for work.  I drove to the hospital, in uniform, and said: â€œI forgive you,â€ and, â€œIâ€™m sorry,â€ to him before he died.  He was unconscious; but it didnâ€™t matter.  I had let it all go; everything that I had held inside I let go.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I started repairing things the day that he died.  Some of the things, like the neglected house, you could see.  Other things, like the hurt inside the members of my family, were less easily seen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We had the funeral for my Father on a Wednesday night.  Thursday morning I had to go back on duty.  A few months later, I let the job go.  It got real hard to do certain things after he died, and much easier to do others.  I found that I had a new set of priorities in life.  I had to take a real hard look at myself and ask how much of what I was doing was really for me, and right for me, and how much of what I was doing was a reaction against him.  I had to try to repair my family, and my familyâ€™s home.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Itâ€™s been a long road from there to here.</p>\r\n\r\n       \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2434,'<b>The <i>Downtown</i> Party Pack:<br>\r\n<i>randomly packaged in a manhattan portage bag</i><br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>subway fare\r\n<LI>illegal loft\r\n<LI>expired unlubricated condom\r\n<LI>methamphetamine\r\n<LI>roofies\r\n<LI>funky breaks compilation cd\r\n</UL><br><br>\r\n<b>The <i>Uptown</i> Party Pack:\r\n<i>conveniently shipped in stylish diesel bag</i><br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>cab fare\r\n<LI>rent-controled hovel\r\n<LI>rough rider condom\r\n<LI>40 oz. Olde English\r\n<LI>cocaine\r\n<LI>house compilation cd\r\n</UL>'),(2435,'<P>some clothes are perfect.  one day, two and a half years ago, a tall gay hispanic man named ... i cannot remember his name, took me out back  of my workplace and offered me a pair of his black wranglers.  they did not fit him anymore because he had grown a bit too wide.  he was a tall man, six three im guessing.  he wranglers had that long fit that allows them to bunch up on the boot.  so in addition to being four inches taller than me, he also liked his pants with extra length, these black wranglers he was offering me out of the trunk of his car were probably 7 inches too long for me.  i took them anyway, like any man, i wont pass up a pair of broken in wranglers.  especially black ones. \r\n</P><P>\r\nthey took a little getting used to.  wearing wranglers was never really my style though i had been known to slip into a pair of osh gosh b\\\'gosh, or dickies, or carharts.  i have never been a big levis fan.  they wear out far too soon.  that said, i also never made it my style to cuff pants with 6 inches of play.  at first i cuffed these new-to-me wranglers on the inside, giving them a sort of highschool vietnamese ganster look.  you know that one, white tee shirt, sag, clean ended jeans with neither a cuff nor a stitch.  i wore them like this with a pair of similiarly clean black airwalks.   \r\n</P><P>\r\nim not sure when it happened, but one day i cuffed the jeans to the outside.  maybe the shoes i had on were boots, or my black and red air jordans, or some sporty sneakers, but when i stepped out with the six inch cuff on my leg, i never went back.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni think it was dan who first said those cuffs were called rain pails.  something like:</P><P>\r\nDan, \\\"you trying to collect water?\\\"</P><P>\r\nMe, \\\"huh?\\\"</P><P>\r\nDan, \\\"in those pails?\\\"</P><P>\r\n(points to the cuffs)\r\n\r\n<!-- i really need to get dsl -->\r\n<!-- and a house with a bomb shelter in marfa texas -->\r\n<!-- and an international pick up truck from 1968 -->\r\n\r\nor maybe it was buckets.  anyway, i wear these jeans all the time now.  they are my prefered pair of pants.  i would love to have three more of them because they are black and i do the most dirty things in them.  i roll around on the ground and smear oil on them.  i drip gasoline all over them.  i fart in them while typing on the computer.  they can get pretty nasty.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<!-- i should write a story about the uniforms we had in highschool that absorbed farts. -->\r\n\r\nsome mornings i wake up and after showering look at these black wranglers in a pile bunched up on the floor all wrinkly and think, \\\"ew gross, you are not going to put those nasty things on are you?\\\"  and i put them on.  it is hard when you only have one pair. what am i going to do, walk around all morning in my red underpants while the wranglers go round the wash and dry?  no, so i wear them some more.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni made it a point to try, TRY and get some new pants that i would like this weekend.  i did not get very far.  the thrift stores in town were all there and i drove by them all.  the weather was not good and i just did not feel like the spirit of new pants was upon me.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nsecretly i have been wanting a monkey suit.  not that they are as versatile as the pants, but i could definately roll around in grease and nasty with a monkey suit on and then my wranglers would not get so filthy.\r\n</P><P>\r\nif anyone will send me a pair of pants, waist size 32-34 is acceptable.  i actually like my pants a little large.  im getting older you know.  34 is the larger size for me.  you know, just whatever you have laying around from when you were a 32-34 only now you are a 36, or 30, that would be great.  sometimes i think what makes some clothes my favorites is that i did not pay for them.  they just sort of came to me.  the spirit of pants, you know.\r\n</P>\r\n<!-- why the hell can not i get an internet connection?  fuck -->\r\n\r\n'),(2436,'For starters lets make it clear that I don\\\'t know the first thing about the full tenets of Humanist dogma or any such thing. I\\\'m just appropiating the word for my own use.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Scapegoat says:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nHumanism : A desire or belief that humans should be good to one another. Most likely tied to a belief that humans will make or break their own salvation. Any role the notion of a divine or supernatural being plays, being limited to a rhetorical device.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThat being said <i>my</i> humanism seems to be hemorrhaging a lot lately.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere does seem to be a certain lack of community in this belief system, especially when compared with friend\\\'s more established and institutionalized spiritual worlds views. Granted the notion of a large group of humanists getting together to make up for this seems a little lame to me. I just envision them all trying to come up with more rules and doctrine, that\\\'s not what I\\\'m looking for.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen there is politics, nothing else does a better job outlining how out of synch my beliefs seem to be. How do most traditionally relegious people reconcile their faith and what happens in their own lives and the world around them? <i>My</i> humanism causes me to be overly political at times but often it is politicking that trumps my faith. On days when this has happened I am pure poison and unreconcilable, in these states of frustration I often find myself wishing that <b>M</b>utual <b>A</b>ssured <b>D</b>estruction had actually played out.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAs I sit sipping sodas this Sunday afternoon in what should probably be a greasy spoon or maybe a wafflehouse, but is instead a place that has an ambiance more akin to a cold wet cave. I\\\'m not sure why but they never turn the heat on in this place. Pondering leads me to realize that this is probablt the way it for most people trying to hold a faith, I just need to accept this and work on trying not to be so poisonous on the bad days. Without <i>my</i> humanism though, I simply don\\\'t want ot get up at all and feel like a spectatotor watching a predetermined life unfold in front of me with no real purpose.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m not even really good at being a humanist according to my own definition but, struggling personally and politically for it seems to be the only thing worthwhile. Realizing this again is one small step in the right direction, time to wander around and find some humans that bolster my faith. Naive world view maybe but it is making <i>my</i> life worth living.<br><br>'),(2437,'I don\\\'t like <a href=\\\"http://www.harpercollins.com/catalog/excerpt_xml.asp?isbn=0880014768\\\">the \r\n  way things work</a> in the vicinity of my life. I don\\\'t <a href=\\\"http://www.poets.org/poems/poems.cfm?prmID=2226\\\">eat \r\n  poems</a> anymore. <br>\r\n  My <a href=\\\"http://www.deskmod.com/core.mod?valert=11:02PM&show=showskin&skin_id=4380\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">collage</a> \r\n  stares at me, weighing </font>the density of my sleep. <br>\r\n  My own endless silence is pushing against frustration, <br>\r\n  and it sounds like a few songs [ <a href=\\\"http://www.elbow.co.uk/first_03_lyrics.asp\\\">red</a> \r\n  | <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B00000I5JS/qid%3D1008757092/sr%3D2-3/ref%3Dsr%5F2%5F15%5F3/104-7224141-0803964\\\">How \r\n  To Fight Loneliness</a> | <a href=\\\"http://www.danah.org/Ani/ToTheTeeth/WishIMay.html\\\">Wish \r\n  I May</a> ] '),(2438,'<P>staring at slinky</P><P>\r\ntelekinesis possible?</P><P>\r\ncranial lobe cleaves</P>'),(2439,'<p>early morning *sniff*<br>\r\n<br>\r\nam i dreaming of beef stew?<br>\r\n<br>\r\ncat laid a steamer<br>\r\n</p>'),(2440,'<p>That\\\'s basically all I wanted to say, but maybe a little more permanently than a thoughtbox. :^)</p>'),(2441,'<p>\r\nIâ€™ve been dreaming of women more and more as of late. They make up more and more of the supporting cast. They are never women I know. They women in my dreams arenâ€™t perfect. Iâ€™m seemingly â€˜involvedâ€™ with most of them yet they do not represent â€˜my perfect girlâ€™. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy dreams often involve violent behaviour. This violent behaviour is always directed towards objects and men, but never women. The women in my dreams donâ€™t seem to do much of anything though I know they are there. I am always aware of their presence. I often kiss and have sex with these women but never have I dreamed of them showing any kind of involvement. They seem hollow and vacant, as if nothing is happening at all. They say nothing and do nothing. Iâ€™m quite sure Iâ€™m not forcing myself upon them, because I am often in situations where these kinds of things happen. Iâ€™ll wake up in bed with them in the morning and the kids will run into the room, or we end up at home after a dinner or opera. The fact that Iâ€™m not assaulting these women relieves me, yet I am confused as to why all these women donâ€™t display any emotion. Their deadpan faces never flinch and their eyes never blink.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is perhaps because I donâ€™t personally interact with women â€˜in the realâ€™ world much. I have for the most part lived life devoid of emotional contact with women. There are the few isolated cases that too this day I wish had never happened, though I donâ€™t know that the women in my dreams represent negativity. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTo be more specific, my life has been devoid of positive emotional contact with women. Perhaps this is why the women in my dreams remain empty and blank. I canâ€™t even recall these women ever even speaking to me.  \r\n</p>'),(2442,'i was laying in bed early this morning, staring out the window through a soft glow from the moon that came and went between the clouds.  it rained softly on cue when i noticed it getting darker and then ceased when the glow returned.  i was in sleep purgatory.  the state you find yourself when you are tired enough to sleep but can\\\'t.  you lay there in quiet understanding that getting up to <i>do</i> anything probably wouldn\\\'t be a good idea because your body wouldn\\\'t respond the way you would want it to and your mind just spins its wheels in the hopes of latching onto some thought that would follow you into dreams.<P>\r\n\r\ni was thinking about the day ahead and the day prior.  i was trying to find what exactly my expectations were of my days, of my time.  what did i need to know deep down, that the days were worthwhile or absolute.  i weighed my material expectations verses my spiritual expectations.  how much worth did i put into actual, tangible products of time spent awake and how important was it that it was (or wasn\\\'t) worthwhile to somebody, <i>anybody</i> else.  how important that i find peace with myself at night knowing what i had done or not done? <P>\r\n\r\ni sat up in bed and opened the window a little more.  the air was warm and moist, i remember not wanting to turn on any lights but i had to write things down.  i usually abstain from lists but this thought required one, this required i search within myself and put down exactly what it means to wake up each day, to live and then attempt to sleep that night.  the list was lengthy.  i surprised even myself.  \\\'this is me on paper\\\', i thought.  i really didn\\\'t find any hidden answers in the numbers and lines of needs, it was just a simple list.  i stared at the paper for a minute then realized that even though i was happy with what i had done it didn\\\'t reveal to me what i <i>wanted</i> from the day, it showed just what i thought i needed.  i started to fold the piece of paper, trying desperately to remember how exactly to fold the sheet into an origami bird in flight like i remembered being taught in grade school from kimo.  \\\'kimo spent days trying to teach me this\\\', i quietly muttered.  after 20 minutes or so i was furious at myself, the paper looked like a small misshapen triangle with ridiculous angles jetting from either side and not like the delicate little bird i thought i had burned into memory.  i gave up after 35 minutes or so and started to unfold the cheap lined paper and i knew exactly what i wanted out of the day. <P>\r\n\r\ni wanted the day to unfold like origami.  each fold representing a new experience or if not a new experience maybe rather my better understanding of an existing experience.  a simple fold could be a new acquaintance or a good effort to renew an existing relationship.  a tuck of paper might equate a great meal or a simple original thought.  the intricacies of the paper in its tucks and folds could be something as simple as keen observation of the intricacies of the day in its minutes and hours.  then at night we could fold the day up again, making something good in my head of what was 19 or 20 hours spent awake.  the day could become a paper animal, folded in 20 or so different ways each being different and unique.  <P>\r\n\r\ni remember crumbling the list, throwing it across the room this morning in the darkness.  a few hours later i stepped over it on my way out of the bedroom.  fuck.  now that\\\'s fitting.'),(2443,'<p>\r\nI have written before on how the stock market was used to accelerate growth and â€˜developmentâ€™ during the Silicon Valley revolution. The information age worker was charmed like a snake thinking their stock options were â€˜spreadingâ€™ the wealth. They did not encounter the hardships that a worker of the Industrial revolution would have had to put up with. They had no immediate reason to organize.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe saw firms spring up over night. Profits that could never be realized were expected and debt was ignored or hidden. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe immediate gains provided by stock options seem more lucrative than the long-term advantages of mobilization. Yet you cannot control what happens to stock. Those decisions are made in smoky rooms with antler on the wall. A place when asked if â€˜the planâ€™ would work, a quick response of â€˜thatâ€™s ok, Iâ€™m rich, ill plead the fifthâ€ is answered.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt has become so clear to me how the rich have somewhat legally played with the jobs and trust of so many hard workingwomen and men. While exploitation has always occurred, in previous cases it had been individual conglomerates. The â€˜stock for workâ€™ model became the benchmark creating a surge of activity in tech stock.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt even got to the point where we were able to donate stock to charities like the Red Cross, republicans, and democrats. Liberals and Conservatives. Speakers were paid to speak with stock, and infomercials abound with tapes and pc programs that help you â€˜day tradeâ€™.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis ongoing Enron fiasco is certainly a good example of this. We see a company so coveted and highly regarded. Then we see it crumble like the World Trade Centre. Perhaps the only settling thing about this mess is that they have not lay blame on 911.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe stock market needs to be regulated in a way that makes it hard for a group of people to inflate a stock artificially through shoddy accounting, make a massive profit, and then sell the stock and quit the job before the house of cards collapses. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPerhaps it is time we make it so you may not own stock for a company you work for. Perhaps when a corporation is investing in another corporation, things like this might occur less. If one could only own the stock; relinquishing all â€˜controlâ€™ over the corporation. No board of governors. No trusteeship. No majority ownership privileges. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe market would then rely on the trust investors have with a company rather than on what they claim will happen later. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA company that comes to life at a consistent pace is in my opinion much better than a corporation that mushrooms to life overnight extending itself too far because of projections.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhile Enron is probably one of many corporations using accounting tricks to inflate stockholder opinion, it is perhaps insignificant compared to how rich this â€˜war on terrorismâ€™ is making some people. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2444,'<p>\r\nMercenaries working for private military companies could be hired for international peacekeeping duties, the British government has suggested. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe long-awaited Green Paper says \\\"reputable\\\" private firms may be able to do a better, more cost-effective job than forces like the United Nations. (BBC 13/02/02)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe idea that private companies would be mandated to seize and control areas of land in the name of â€˜peace keepingâ€™ is preposterous. How can we allow questions like these into our governments?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIs it bad enough that we have privatized our schools, our hospitals, and our prisons? We have now thought up the brilliant idea of privatizing peacekeepers. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat if someone tomorrow proposed that we privatize the Toronto police force? Just think of the corruption that would occur. The idea wouldnâ€™t make it past the idea phase let alone the proposal phase. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe have some nerve asking a corporation to provide peacekeeping to areas of the world too costly for our attention. If the war on terror is so important then pay for it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(a response to â€˜Peacekeeping â€˜roleâ€™ for mercenariesâ€™ posted on the BBC News website)\r\n</p>'),(2445,'<P>my nails draw blood; the need to hear his voice curls my hands into small dead animals, the ones we close our eyes to when we see them ahead on the road. saying yes, giving in, showing up on his front door step in my pyjamas would bring the kind of relief i can\\\'t find in aspirin. but i lock my door and sit at my desk, a ridiculously out-of-place fountain pen shivering in my hand. i try not to look at the band aid. when i was a little girl my parents would tie my left hand behind my back to force me to use my right one. the right side of the body is noble and good. righteous. the left is weak, misshapen, wicked. gauche. i invent new forms of punishment for the weakness rising in my body, spinning in my throat. i pummel my pillow, my blue glass vase falls to the ground, and i leap up in bed at the sound. i sit on my hands, i don\\\'t allow myself a sip from the glass of water on my bedside table, no matter how thirsty i am. i look longingly at the blade on the dresser. i take long walks through my neighbourhood. it is still new. there are many houses under construction, their insides open like giant dollhouses. sometimes i rest my head on the cool metal of the scaffolding and cry without stopping. there are days when my faith is so shaky that God ripples in the air like the surface of a lake when there is a pebble-made breeze. days when a single bad stone could break through the surface and destroy the whole image. and God becomes tiny dots like those that make the static on a fuzzy television screen. perhaps the reception is bad. perhaps there is a malfunction in the equipment. i\\\'m not really sure why or how. i just know that i\\\'ve forgotten how to pray. i know i\\\'m supposed to fold my hands into the shape of a boat, i know that God can understand any language, and all the different kinds of silence, and the knots in my fingers. but every time i try, i fail. the scaffolding is gone. the tarp has been rolled away, tied with thick rope and thrown in the builder\\\'s old garage. the metal poles and ladders are rusting on the open land overflowing with brambles and dandelions. and there is no place to put my head.</P>'),(2446,'<P>slightly damp sand, and tiny shells, the shiny ones with outsides like the insides of oysters, coloured orange or brown or pink swirls. the feel of the solid, sun warmed steel on the back of my shirt and jeans as i slid down, and tumbled to the bottom in a tangle of arms and feet, mine and those of the friend who had let go an instant before i did. \r\n</P><P>\r\ndespite the million pleadings we never could wait to start a descent after the previous child had safely landed and was upright, toes curling in the sand at the foot of the giant red curve. the slides shaped like the first letter of my name, S, the name i was just learning to write, caught me the most strongly. \r\n</P><P>\r\nadults didn\\\'t, couldn\\\'t, understand. it was impossible to wait. my fingers itched painfully, fiercely, until only banging as hard as i could with tight fists on the bright yellow of the plastic ladders i had to climb, those unbearable obstructions to the scream-leading, hair-following headlong rushes below, could bring some small measure of relief. \r\n</P><P>\r\nimpatience was always propelling my girlbody too quickly forward, uncoiling the springs in my thighs and shins, until eventually i fell, exhausted, in the grass at the edge of the fountain. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni spent all my time on the slides. the swing set never held my interest. it could never take me up high enough. and the see saw merely teased, offering a brief taste of the air above a grown up\\\'s head, well beyond the ordinary short reach of my mouth, only to drag me down, bumping my bottom hard against the wooden plank rocking on its concrete base. \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut it inspired us to try climbing the palm trees in the centre of the park every afternoon, comparing the scabs and bruises on our bare knees afterward, giving all our best marbles to the proud possessor of the biggest, bloodiest wounds in an elaborate ceremony under the square green leaves of palm trees while sharing ripe mangoes. \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen evening finally came, the whispering and soft laughter of our parents, and other old people, would coincide in our ears with the musical bell of the ice cream man, chiming until we sent the littlest in our group, a girl, perhaps four years old, with chubby pink cheeks, and three irresistible front teeth, to cry and cry and cry until they gave in and bought all of\r\nus popsicles, the special ones, the ones in the shape of different characters for each of the flavours. a strawberry pink princess, roses in her fair hair. chocolate was a dark pirate, with a curling moustache the colour of his eye patch. \r\n</P><P>\r\nand lemon, my favourite, a jumping yellow clown, caught in mid air, laughing in front of the sun.</P>'),(2447,'<P>My good friend Amy had a daughter in 1996. Her name is Sally. Sheâ€™s my goddaughter, even thought there was never a baptism, and Iâ€™m not Catholic. Iâ€™m â€˜Auntie Stellaâ€™. </P><P>When Sally was born, I noticed immediately that her eyes were crossed. She had two wandering eyes, one so severe that the child was in effect looking at the side of her nose. She had difficultly controlling her eyes, but her father had a wondering eye when he was little and it went away by the time that he was 3 or so, so no one was worried. As Sally grew, it was obvious that something was not quite right with her. She wasnâ€™t as alert as most babies are, not very quick to either cry or laugh. She would mostly just sit and look. When she hit her second year, she wasnâ€™t walking yet, and wasnâ€™t trying to talk. She grunted a lot as a means a communication. She also was still in diapers, which I suppose isnâ€™t too abnormal, although her mother hadnâ€™t tried to potty train yet. </P><P>\r\nI was really concerned. Her eyes were worse and her behavior just wasnâ€™t normal. Her mother had smoked a lot of marijuana when she was pregnant and her father was addicted to both speed (methamphetamines) and marijuana. Amy confided to me that she was fairly certain that Sally was conceived when they were both high on ecstasy. Sally was raised for a few months with both of her parents. Her mother left her father when Sally was 2, from then on alone with her mother, who continued to smoke a lot of pot and drink excessively in front of Sally. Often, I would go to visit and Sally would be filthy, in clothes that sheâ€™d been wearing for days on end, covered with food. Her hair would be a mess. She reminded me of a child raised by wolves, grunting to communicate, dirty. Amy managed to somehow get Sally signed up for free experimental surgery for her eyes. The doctors sucked the eye out of the socket and re-aligned it, then popped it back in. Sally was supposed to have that eye bandaged, so that it could strengthen. Amy didnâ€™t make her. Her eye stayed weak, and therefore still misaligned. </P><P>\r\nOne night, I was to meet Amy at her small apartment so that we could have dinner together. I got there and the front door was open. Amy wasnâ€™t home; no one was except Sally. She was two and a half. She was in her crib. I stayed there and waited. Amy came home, drunk, about 45 minutes later. She wasnâ€™t impressed with my concern. She gave me the impression that she felt that I was being judgmental. She often would lock Sally in her room for hours when she was having company. Sally could be heard screaming from the road. </P>\r\nSally went to day-care. The people who ran the daycare center called Amy in one day to talk. They told her that Sally would start to bang her head, with her eyes shut, if the room got too loud, or if she was frightened. They told her that the last time that they had seen a child do that, the child had been sexually abused. (Amy was very liberal with who was allowed to baby-sit. If you were willing to watch her you were allowed to watch her. This included her husbandâ€™s drug-addicted friends) Amy didnâ€™t believe them. She felt that there was nothing wrong with Sally.  She also ignored her uncle, who was a child psychologist, when he wrote her a letter to let her know that he was concerned with Sallyâ€™s behavior. After being exposed to her at a family Christmas party, he felt that she needed some help. That she had some problems. </P><P>\r\nAfter a few years of watching Amy ignore her child, and after Child Protective Services (who had been called) didnâ€™t do anything, I stopped contact with Amy. I couldnâ€™t watch her do this to her daughter, my godchild. That was three years ago. I recently became in contact with them again. Amy has remarried and is pregnant again. Sally has been visiting a speech therapist, because she still canâ€™t talk. She sees an occupational therapist because her eyes are so bad, sheâ€™s almost blind. She has borderline mental retardation, whether from the neglect or from the drugs. When I found out that Amy was pregnant again, I was angry. I was very angry. But Iâ€™m hopeful that now Amy isnâ€™t doing drugs anymore (sheâ€™s actually a born-again Christian) that perhaps Sally will have a better chance. Perhaps everything will be okay for her. But I\\\'m still angry. </P>\r\n'),(2448,'this is just a test'),(2449,'<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2449_1.jpg\\\">\r\n<!--\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n-->'),(2450,'<p>i saw you on the other side of the street while trying to avoid the usual cadre of bums in our usual meeting spot. you were dressed as if ready for some sort of costume party; plastic, colourful, anime. your hair a firey rouge, yet different. longer, but tressed up so it appears almost the same as when we were together before. i crossed to say hello and see why you were dressed in such a bizzare way, why you were on the street looking for a cab-ride. you told me we could really just walk there from here. it was as i guessed, a costume party.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so we walked and talked and went into that art gallery where you always pointed out that strange carved wooden shoe. it was on the way, so why not.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we went up the stairs where it always sits and we discussed life, your destination and various other insignificant details we always bring up when we want to pretend we\\\'re playing catch up in each others lives. we stopped and inspected the ornate shoe.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>delicate, in the shape of a woman\\\'s heel, but somehow made into a miniature home complete with carpets and tiny chairs and tables the size of pink pencil-stub erasers. you were always enthralled by the sheer intricacy of the workmanship.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it was there that you foisted apon me the words that you so long had withheld, that you were too timid to tell me before. it was there that you told me why you hadn\\\'t made it to our friendship-only lunch just two days ago and why you couldn\\\'t explain on the phone. how could have you left it unsaid for so long? the many months had passed. it is strange, however, that you could leave telling me to our chance (or was it?) meeting on the street that night.\r\n\r\n<p>you told me you had been seeing him for quite a while now. i couldn\\\'t understand in my blinding tears; my hatred of him instantaneous. for so long i\\\'ve been wanting to let go, and finally, hopefully this will truely let me do so. but why, why couldn\\\'t have you told me sooner? so i could have earlier been able to just no longer hope and dream that some day we would be together again. the pain was able to be released in a salty deluge down my face.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you tell me of how you met, how he is a professional musician employed in the court of her majesty, the queen. oh yes, and that it was just your birthday passed a little over a month ago (like you needed to remind me) and that as a gift you recieved an even tinier replica of the shoe under glass that we are standing next to. he gave it to you. a symbol of the true gift, which sits here on up on a pedastel just out of reach, like you.</p>'),(2451,'I feel the weight of the flashes\r\n</br>\r\nof light around my darkness\r\n</br>\r\nfrom this empty pit I look up\r\n</br>\r\nit all has the stink of never\r\n<p>\r\nI can\\\'t stand losing you\r\n</br>\r\neverytime you\\\'re away\r\n</br>\r\nthe pain makes me numb\r\n</br>\r\nI cannot even move\r\n</br>\r\nbut I try to see the holiness\r\n</br>\r\nin keeping your poison away\r\n</br>\r\nyes I miss your electricity\r\n</br>\r\nI can\\\'t stand losing you\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve grown into a vessel\r\n</br>\r\nof hidden strains in the air\r\n</br>\r\nthat give me the passion\r\n</br>\r\nof letting myself go away\r\n<p>\r\nwhen you\\\'re ready for the kill\r\n</br>\r\nI will open my arms\r\n</br>\r\nand surrender my heart\r\n</br>\r\nand let you flow into me'),(2452,'<p>orion: seen recently: \\\"Where was your flag on Sept. 10?\\\" Heh. So true. These \\\"well now I guess I\\\'m a patriot\\\" people make me sick.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>chiamattt: there are police officers, firefighters, and paramedics all over the world. they have dangerous jobs. they live. they die.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Quoth Chiamattt: \\\"they live. they die.\\\"</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Right.  Now, some of those people had little boys and girls who called them: \\\"Mommy,\\\" or, \\\"Daddy.\\\"  Some of those people had husbands and wives who called them: \\\"My Darling Wife,\\\" or, \\\"My Darling Husband.\\\"  Some of those people were really good brothers, and sisters, and sons, and daughters, and fathers, and mothers.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Quoth Chiamattt: \\\"they have dangerous jobs.\\\"</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Right.  And when even one of them dies, the ripples of that event radiate outward through their families and communities.  The people who cared for them get upset.  You know: the people who called them: \\\"Mommy,\\\" or, \\\"Daddy,\\\" etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So, when a large number of them died quite unexpectedly, one day last September, then there were lots of ripples.  And there were lots of people who were upset.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>People fly flags for all sorts of reasons.  The first 5 comments on Chiamatttâ€™s article, \\\"stockmarket blues\\\" <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2443.txt\\\">dealt with</a> the connection between flag flying and the events of last September and jingoism.  I hoped to point out that some people left an American flag up, somewhere, as a memorial â€“ their reasons were, essentially, apolitical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yes: it\\\'s possible for mass demonstrations of any kind to be employed for purposes other than those that the demonstrators intended.  Clever political leaders might claim that the sudden appearance of so many symbols is a mandate for a certain type of action.  Whether it is remains questionable.  I\\\'m not smart enough to know the answers to many of the questions that surround this event.  Will the military action that we take provoke more such acts?  Or, will a failure to take military action encourage more such acts?  Everyone has their own pet theory that often seems to have less to do with this particular circumstance and more to do with their own political views.  Isolationists claim that the events of last September illustrate the need for the U.S.A. to withdraw from foreign commitments.  Interventionists claim that the events of last September illustrate the need for the U.S.A. to become more involved in global affairs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can\\\'t offer up any sort of blanket defense for the actions of every member of every element of the emergency services.  I can\\\'t explain why they all became involved in the work in the first place.  But, for that very reason, to make some sort of blanket statement about them, or the people who flew flags to remember them is, in my mind, to be guilty of a horrible form of prejudice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Stella left a story about child abuse: <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2447\\\">pity the child</A> was its name.  Can you imagine the emotional strain that is put upon the people who need to interact with such children and their parents on a daily basis?  How does one move from such a situation as that described in Stellaâ€™s article to pleasant chat with someone about directions to a particular location in the city?  \\\"Well,\\\" it will be said, \\\"if they can\\\'t handle it then they shouldn\\\'t be in that line of work.\\\"  But, aren\\\'t there economic forces that motivate people, and aren\\\'t there family traditions that motivate people, and isn\\\'t there still an honest desire in some people to try to do good things?  And when all of that has been said, if the people who do that work are completely emotionally removed from everything that they encounter then they won\\\'t be human anymore.  Do you want people in the emergency services to become less compassionate than they are now?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The real story of the events of last September, and of all human conflict, is to be found in the empty spaces of the families who have lost the people that they loved and needed.  And even more, perhaps, you can tell the real cost violence when you spend time with the people who have suffered traumatic amputations and horrible burns and who must now live with the pain for the remainder of their lives.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is it too much to ask people to just remember the dead and maimed, and set politics aside?  I\\\'m sorry, but I don\\\'t understand this at all.  Good people who were good parents were snuffed out like candles and their children are left without them.  And people who abuse their kids are still walking around.  I don\\\'t get it.  And I don\\\'t understand the need to turn it all into a political issue and rain piss down on the victims and their families.</p>\r\n\r\n  \r\n'),(2453,'<p>\r\ni applied for 4 jobs in the past 10 months.  most of them were so entirely ridiculous that i was relieved that no one called me back.  one was so insanely entertaining that i could not stop laughing as i sent in my resume.  that, of course, is the job i got.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin late january i sent a resume to a regional recruiter for a major, multi-national coffee chain.  i included this cover letter:\r\n</p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nHello [recruiter name],\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nI came across the listing for Assistant Store Manager on monster.com, and I would like to submit my resume with great enthusiasm.\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nI have a successful history of managing teams and tasks, as my resume attests.  With strong beliefs about customer experience, colleague satisfaction, and my responsibility to each, I consistently facilitate successful endeavors without sacrificing an enjoyable workplace.  I understand business practices and the delicate balance of efficiency and quality.  As well, I\\\'m quite passionate about caffeine!\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nI would love to discuss my qualifications further and explore how my management experience could best serve [coffee chain name].  Should you also be interested, please do not hesitate to contact me!\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p><i>\r\nSincerely,<BR>\r\n[my name]\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p>i made myself hiccup with self-amusement/self-loathing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i didn\\\'t tell them the caffeine i\\\'m passionate about is diet coke.  and i tried really hard to dumb down my resume.  don\\\'t get me wrong -- i\\\'m not stuck up at all, but going from tech management to retail management is all about nuances and customer friendliness.  my ability to bridge the gap between programmers and visual designers and the gap between Our Team and The Clients, is pretty insignificant when you\\\'re dealing with coffee beans and bottom line p&l.  quark be damned!  they want to know if i can manage an inventory of java jackets.  and you know what?  i can.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntwo interviews, one 45 minute wait while an interviewer was talking on her cell phone, one lost resume, two free beverages, and three weeks later, i have a verbal offer.  33.333% of my previous salary.  >30000% of my current salary.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis multi-national coffee chain\\\'s background check rivals the fbi\\\'s.  they have already contacted all my references.  they have called every previous employer to verify i worked there (even asking me for email addresses for ANYONE i worked with at a company that no longer exists).  they have completed my criminal background check.  they have called me to quiz me on what my relationship to each reference was, to ensure honesty.  it\\\'s pretty shocking.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nof course, i was totally spoiled in the dot com blitz.  no one really questioned hires on real abilities, let alone checked references adequately.  i remember my previous job\\\'s interview process as three-part pitch where i sat and they asked me questions similar to a junior high personality test and then explained how they would be the best match for me.  it was like the dating game, only instead of chuck woolery, we had a guy they called Chief Morale Officer.  luckily, i had skills.  or maybe i didn\\\'t.  with this sort of fairyland, who can tell for sure.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso fact of the matter is: i am becoming an assistant manager at a coffee chain.  some people think i should be a little embarrassed about it.  but i\\\'m totally not.  i mean, there is a fine line between knowing it\\\'s funny and being ashamed of it.  after ten months of teaching my dog very impressive tricks, painting every surface in my house (sometimes twice), and memorizing the day\\\'s tv schedule, i am looking forward to hanging out with surly baristas and wiping down tables.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neight weeks of training are in my future.  i am anticipating some really ridiculous situations.  it\\\'s really truly going to be a hoot and a half.  i can\\\'t remember the last time i thought about dress code or worked an eight hour day.   good lord, it\\\'s going to be an exercise in observational obsession.\r\n</p>\r\ni\\\'ll close with some email i sent to others regarding my news:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n------\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy lackluster enthusiasm for [megacoffee], inc. will offer a buffer zone of resentment.  i will not drink the koolaid, but i will propose a koolaid latte to patrons.  the best things about working at [megacoffee]:  good benefits, decent pay, um, uh, free coffee.  the worst things about working at [megacoffee]:  working with people who work there, hearing/speaking those ridiculous beverage names, and giving my shithead little couch-riding brother any opportunity to criticize me -- he says i\\\'m a sellout because they don\\\'t recycle paper cups.  \\\"i don\\\'t think mom and dad recycle those classy paper plates you dine on every night, tiger.  please refer to michael jackson\\\'s hit song \\\'man in the mirror\\\',\\\" i said.  \\\"you are so stupid,\\\" he said. \\\"i got rid of that tape in sixth grade!\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\\\'t care enough to feel guilty about quitting if i find it to be sucking hard.  there should be some good stories to be told, and i am very interested in seeing how the [megacoffee] training parallels my bob\\\'s big boy training.  (i made a specific point to add bob\\\'s big boy to my resume and say that my responsibilities there were to present and transcend the big boy brand, also doing morning prepwork such as cole slaw dishing and wrapping silverware in napkins.  they brought it up in the interview, too, so that must have been the deciding factor). \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin a nutshell:  it isn\\\'t pathetic because i admit it\\\'s pathetic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n------\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy parents sent me a blue mountain greeting entitled \\\"you\\\'ve got the world at your feet\\\" to congratulate me on my new job.  suddenly i feel as if i have reached new lows.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyesterday my dad said something about how i will be making coffee all day.  and i felt obligated to say \\\'you are mistaken, i will be pushing paper around all day\\\'.\\\' and he says \\\'so you will manage the coffeemakers.\\\'  and i said \\\'oh no, i am two steps away from those untouchables.\\\'  and he said \\\'oooh!  you\\\'re a muckety muck!\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n------\r\n</p>'),(2454,'<p>as we are making our oatmeal on the deck of my outdoor kitchen, on the edge of an oak tree canyon somewhere in the foothills of southern california, we are jolted by a booming sound. at first we can\'t believe just how loud it is, and sort of look at each other in amazement. there\'s one thing about a fairly loud-sounding stereo in an echo-prone canyon at 8:30 a.m. on a holiday. but this was as loud as a stadium-style concert, filling the dew-drenched canyon with the unmistakably annoying sound of 1970s-era classic rock. if it wasn\'t so annoying, in fact, its sheer volume would have been remarkable and maybe even admirable.</p>\r\n<p>was it tom petty, he wondered? could it possibly be bad company, i asked? it was one of those songs you have heard a million times but never know the name to. most likely it\'s been made into muzak and pumped out every 37 minutes in doctor\'s waiting rooms, office buildings and grocery stores, distracting you as you search for the peanut butter and embedding itself in your subconscious, making you hum it all day long even though you have no idea what it is. so we sit there, our oatmeal getting cold, marveling at the audacity these neighbors had. it\'s not as if they have not been rude before--no, quite the contrary. they are practically known around the neighborhood for their saturday-night-beerfest-eagles-greatest-hits-vol. 2- marathons. but this bad company-esque business at 8:30 a.m., this was a new low.</p>\r\n<p>the music eventually goes off; we look at each other and shrug. back to the oatmeal. then, from the unit next door (it\'s a duplex), comes a musical counterattack. blaring from the stereo of my whimsical neighbor  comes the abstract groove of mohammed mahmoud al tablawi (some kind of crazy islamic chanting?). uncontrollable laughter ensues from our side of the house. are we really experiencing this? he could have just yelled ``fuck you,\'\' but no. it\'s musical warfare, with each weapon of choice perfectly summing up the worldview and general disposition of either front. i ask my neighbor about it later in an email, and this is what he writes:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>``If this sonic terrorism continues we may \r\nhave to resort to ELF Technology to get them evil hippies under control.  \r\nThey won\'t hear a thing, but I can\'t wait to see their faces when the light \r\nbulbs start shattering, trinkets begin to creep off the shelves and the walls \r\nare violently vibrating.  If they can get their attention off their splitting \r\nheadaches, that is.  This is America.  We don\'t mess around.  God Bless Us.\'\'</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2554,'barely any light</br>\r\nbarely any light to see</br>\r\nwinds foreshadowing clash</br>\r\nwinds blowing against</br>\r\nagainst</br>\r\nagainst\r\n<p>\r\nsit and look at me</br>\r\nsit and don\'t speak</br>\r\nwrapped in white sheets</br>\r\ndon\'t get up and leave</br>\r\nplease</br>\r\nplease</br>\r\n<p>\r\na scream</br>\r\nis that you I see?</br>\r\nlights</br>\r\ncamera</br>\r\na scene that repeats\r\n<p>\r\nyou\'re not chasing me away</br>\r\nyou\'re not scaring me away'),(2455,'<p>\r\nHe said that Jingle Bells is the best song in the English language. If it makes people feel the energy of dashing through the snow, ears and noses going numb smashing into frozen pieces of world thrown up from underneath the horses\\\' hooves, snow-covered islands rising from the sidewalks and lawns, murky beasts hidden under a baby blanket of snow, dirt and disease and smelly office chairs mercifully occluded, laughing all the way...</p>He said that he was once invited to sing at the New College, some reception or something and brought the people to their feet with his song about Homer, the poet, you know, and then booted out and not paid, I guess they took offense to Omer. Brought them to their feet.</p>I\\\'ve had many guitars, he said, but they seem to break all at once. I had three guitars break in 2 weeks, he said. I can take a guitar apart and put it back together with my eyes closed. Got this one, found it in the park one day, some dreamer must have gotten too stoned and left for Portland too busy thinking about revolution and good coffee, bought a new string, just trying to get a string that wouldn\\\'t break on me, and lo and behold, Iâ€™ve got a base guitar. There was a song I wrote in the \\\'70\\\'s, love song, always writing about love or losing teeth or leather couches in those days. This song. Hoover was an innocent man, blamed for the Depression, didnâ€™t do nothing to deserve it. The beauty of the American people is that they need something ugly to succor them through hard times. Sometimes people come by here and act like I was born to entertain them, surviving on nothing other than their smiles. Put some coins in the box if you like it, spit on me if you don\\\'t. Just do something, for chris\\\' sakes. The song I wrote was about kissin\\\' and I just now learned how to play it, on my new base guitar. Iâ€™m going to do a song now, it\\\'s called Bombs</p>\r\n<p>Homer and Nikita up in a tree (Nikita Kruschev, thatâ€™s him)</p>\r\n<p>K I S S I N G</p>\r\n<p>First comes love, then comes marriage</p>\r\n<p>Then comes the baby carriage</p><p>Airplanes and bombs airplanes and bombs, thatâ€™s all they want to hear about these days.</p><p>He said, bombs and bums. Sunlight passes in and out of here, see me flirting with that light. Make a good photograph. This young guyâ€”took a picture of me. It\\\'s a nice photograph. Not because it\\\'s of me, the way he took it. If you walk down here, there\\\'s a restaurant, the Slanted Door. It\\\'s hanging up there, on the wall. So that I can watch the people while they eat, and have their cars parked for them by the Mexican boys out front. Forgetting to eat sometimes. Speaking of butts, I never smoked, never did. You won\\\'t catch me bummin\\\' a cigarette from the overdressed hustlers in their Prada heels. I asked a woman once how much her shoes cost. Then I asked her why she was walking in them on the sidewalk. Doesn\\\'t she know that bums pee there? </p>\r\n<p>He said, I\\\'m working on starting a welfare program. I\\\'m doing a lot of work on it. I know this citizen, this man, he goes all up and down this street for 2 miles, collecting cans and bottles and used Barbies and chicken bones. Now that\\\'s a real social service. I know half a dozen individuals that do a real service to the community but they won\\\'t pay them, \\\'cause they don\\\'t want to live in a house. Now that\\\'s discrimination. My office is going to be right here and Iâ€™m going to give out checks.</p>\r\n<p>He said, I never play anywhere but on Valencia. Used to play on Mission, but the schools, crowds, 200 people must have passed me by one afternoon, everyone laughing, must have made 17 cents. Here, people stop or they don\\\'t. Like me, I\\\'m getting thinner. Used to be a wrestler. That\\\'s why I have these arms. Where did I come from? Bakersfield, grew up there. My dad, now, he\\\'s a real character, but they put him in a home last year. He could tell you some stories. I like to sit in the sun on Saturdays, outside that cafÃ©. They give me coffee and I write songs. I got half a sandwich in this bag. I\\\'ll teach you how to fix your guitar, he said.</p>\r\n'),(2456,'<p>\r\nAll the job boards -- they failed me.  Or maybe I\\\'m a failure to them.  Who knows?  Who cares?  Point is, I need a job.  So do lots of us, I take it.  Here\\\'s something I discovered.  Maybe it\\\'ll help.  Or maybe it\\\'s just worth a shot.</p>\r\n<br/>\r\n<p>\r\nLet\\\'s suppose that you were a men\\\'s restroom attendant in your employed past-life.  Name of Deuce Bigalow, Sr., perhaps.  Anyway, you\\\'ve searched hotjobs, monster, dice, jobs-in-the-toilet.com, etc etc, to no avail.  Why not turn to the last bastion of the damned?  Google.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nType in \\\"men\\\'s rest-room attendant San Francisco.\\\"  Type in \\\"toilet jobs\\\".  Type in as many phrases pertaining to the shit job in question as you can think of.  Yes, this is the \\\'so simple\\\' but equally \\\'so difficult\\\' part.  But there\\\'s one distinct advantage that offers a trade-off to the difficulty of being creative:  google doesn\\\'t ask you to pigeon-hole yourself neatly into one of its preconfigured, all-encompassing job categories.  If you were Mr/Ms Tidy Bowl, then you\\\'d probably have to choose \\\"Other\\\" or some such on a job board.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow comes the really cool part (which, for all its simplicity, is still really nifty).  Scan the items google returns to you for names of companies that you think might, just might (Braveheart rhetoric intentional), employ shit-shovelers like you, or be somehow involved with shit shoveling to a greater or lesser degree.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThen, pop up a new google search window.  If this is an advanced concept to you (it is to my parents), ask a more tech-savvy friend of yours about how to do it (keeping in mind that tech people very often cringe at the mention of the word \\\"window\\\" and all of its derivatives).  In this new window, do a google search for the company name in question.  Go to their homepage, and look for \\\"Employment Opportunities.\\\"  You may even need to do a site search for \\\"Employment,\\\" before the desired page presents itself.  One would think (at least this one would) that \\\"Employment Opportunities\\\" would be conspicously displayed on site maps.  But alas, this is not always the case.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRepeat ad nauseum.  Copy and paste any info, job descriptions, email addresses, phone numbers, etc into an email to yourself.  Leave it marked as \\\"Unread\\\" when it arrives, and you\\\'ll have a big old string of emails in your inbox that document your job search.  This part of the process is particularly apt for modification, and need not be followed to the letter of this description.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was surprised at the sheer number of companies offering shit jobs, in my particular flavor, that had never hit the job boards.  For that matter, they had never been returned in google searches of the form \\\"shit job employment opportunities,\\\" \\\"shit job apply,\\\" etc.  This does not bother me.  The technical issues behind this anomaly (sp?) do intrigue me, but only to a limited extent.  Perhaps a contributor who is more technically-inclined than I will wish to shed light on this phenomenon.  Perhaps not.  I for one am one of those people who is perfectly willing to enjoy Google, without understanding how it works.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGood luck everyone.  Thanks for the late-night reading.  Hope this simple little trick works for you in some way.\r\n</p>'),(2457,'i\\\'m lonely today.\r\n\r\nit\\\'s sunny and cold out and i really want to be part of one of those couples sitting in gimme with a cup of coffee and the newspaper.\r\n\r\nand you know what the scariest thing of all is?\r\ni\\\'m starting to feel like i want to be domestic with someone. which up until now has represented the first step down a very dark path. this may be cause for alarm.\r\n\r\nbut maybe i\\\'ll feel better after i eat some bibimbap. what do you think?\r\n'),(2458,'<P>mom and dad have company.  friends from years ago.  i\\\'m called down to make an appearance.  after a few minutes, it starts to feel like an interview.</P><P>\\\"so what is it you do?\\\" they want to know.</P><P>\\\"play with myself,\\\" i very nearly tell them.  these inappropriate urges come upon me lately.  maybe it comes from flirting with death.  that giddy feeling you get.</P><P>i want to find an ape or bear costume, put it on, go downtown and stand on the corner.  grab my crotch with both hands and shake it at passing traffic.  also:  pat bald men on the head like benny hill used to, pinch the occaisonal ripening asscheek.  totally inappropriate.</P><P>the novel-ing continues apace.  an excerpt:</P><P>\\\"At night it gets dark.  Time away from the multitudinous eyes that look upon the evil of men.  I step out into the back yard.  In one corner, a cedar tree grows around a palm.  I like to think they\\\'re married, the cedar much older, Jewish, arms wrapped around his tropical mama.  She\\\'s a head taller than him.  \\\'My sweet oasis,\\\' he whispers.  \\\'My coffeecake, my raincloud, my psalm.\\\'  His suspirations float away on the wind, unheeded.\\\"</P><P>dinner:  lasagna, garlic bread, arrugula with red onion slices, mandarin orange, sunflower seeds and raw ramen.  cherry pie in honor of george\\\'s birthday.  (which reminds me:  \\\"george washington\\\" is really worth seeing.  beautiful lyric filmmaking.)  i sit by barbara, one of the guests.  her neck is peppered with little black moles.  i fight the urge to run my tongue over them, suck on the big one under her ear.  see how her husband would like that.</P><P>they\\\'re missionaries.  i can stand only so much jesus talk, but the husband did tell an interesting story about bangladesh.  said he came across a man who was herding pigs.  (hope he had the decency to wipe him off.)  hundreds of pigs, all asleep.  asked (in bengali) how he managed to get all the pigs to sleep in the middle of the day.  the man demonstrated.  he had a chant to wake them, a chant to put them back to sleep.</P><P>they brought my dad a gift:  a book with nice color prints, <U>birds of north america</U>.  it has entries on each bird.  i found their breeding habits amusing:</P><P>most of them are described as \\\"monogamous.  solitary nester.\\\"  every once in awhile, you\\\'ll find polyandrous, polygynous, or polygamous birds; birds who live in colonies, are gregarious, who mate for life or are promiscuous.  summaries of courtship and fucking behaviors are sometimes given:</P><P>\\\"female courts male by swimming beside him and chasing off other females; eventually male makes sexual advances.\\\"</P><P>\\\"during courtship, male and female will sit together and pass flower petals back and forth, share food, and rub bills.\\\"</P><P>\\\"male and female face one another and vibrate wings while singing.\\\"</P><P>\\\"birds copulate in air, with male and female coming together from opposite directions, locking on, and pinwheeling downward through the air, sometimes for more than 500 feet.\\\"</P><P>(by the way, isn\\\'t apolo anton ohno fine? wowee.)</P>'),(2459,'Causing trouble, no doubt.\r\nHope you can help....\r\n\r\n\r\nTUESDAY FEBRUARY 26, 8:30\r\n@ ELYSIUM, 707 RED RIVER\r\n\r\nExhibition - the Erotic Photography of <a href=\\\"http://www.eumorphia.com\\\">Eumorphia</a>.\r\n\r\n\r\nLinearB and <a href=\\\"http://www.elysiumonline.net\\\">Elysium</a>. will be hosting an exhibition of the Erotic Photography of <a href=\\\"http://www.eumorphia.com\\\">Eumorphia</a>.. Here\\\'s your chance to see Jher\\\'s work in the proper environment -- a dark red room full of hungry souls.\r\n\r\nI\\\'ve been a big fan of Jher\\\'s work for quite some time. Now that I don\\\'t have to spend all my time running some damn bookstore, I\\\'ve finally got the time to host a get together so everyone can\r\nsee his work. Since my long-time co-conspirator, JohnW, just opened Elysium, I decided to host the event there.\r\n\r\nIf you haven\\\'t had a chance to visit Elysium yet, I hope you can make it Tuesday. John has really done a heck of a job fixing up the old Atomic.\r\n\r\nThere will be NO COVER CHARGE during the exhibition.\r\n\r\nThere will also be a fetish show following the exhibition.\r\n\r\nHope you can make it.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nEumorphia\r\nhttp://www.eumorphia.com\r\nElysium\r\nhttp://www.elysiumonine.com\r\nLinearB/GeekAustin\r\nhttp://www.geekaustin.org'),(2460,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2460_1.jpg\\\">'),(2461,'woke up.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nfed the rat, fed myself toast with peanut butter and a cup of coffee. read a hundred pages of womens\\\' studies homework. smoked two consecutive cigarettes, then mentally flagellated myself for being a spineless willpower-less chainsmoker. reflected upon the state of my life in the shower, shaved my legs for the first time in weeks. sat in moist stupor on my bed listening to moon pix. played with the ferret. had heated discussion with rachel re: long distance relationships and how they are hazardous for the mental health. halfheartedly commenced thesis research. chose instead to watch mindless television.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nnow i\\\'m in the library, and the sexy italian grad student next to me is looking up helmut lang jeans on the internet.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhat next?'),(2462,'<center>\nAt the extreme of disharmony, the mind of Tao has been long buried away, and people don\'t pay attention to it;<BR>\nThis is disharmony resulting in <b><tt>isolation</tt></b>.<BR>\nWith the mind of Tao buried away and the human mentality taking charge of affairs, <tt><b>the influence of habit becomes one\'s nature</b></tt>,<BR>\nand <tt><b>doubts and ruminations come forth by the hundreds</b></tt>;<BR>\nthis is being like <tt>a pig covered in mire, a wagon full of devils</tt>.\n<BR><BR>\nIf you want to restore the mind of Tao, it is necessary to first understand the human mind.<BR>\nBut to understand it, it is important to see it:<BR>\nSeeing it <tt>as a pig, as devilish</tt>, is truly seeing the human mentality and how injurious it is.<BR>\nOnce you can see and understand it clearly,<BR>\nyour former use of the human mind without understanding is like first drawing a bow,<BR>\nwhile your later ability to understand and restore the mind of Tao is like afterward putting it down.\n<BR><BR>\n<tt><b>Without the human mind, you don\'t see the mind of Tao;<BR>\nwithout the mind of Tao, you cannot know the human mind.</b></tt><BR>\nUsing the human mind temporarily to restore the mind of Tao,<BR>\neven though the human mind is the <tt>chief of villains</tt>, it is also the <tt>chief in merit</tt>;<BR>\nit is not an enemy, but really a partner.<BR>\nOnce the human mind is seen and the mind of Tao is restored, at this point if you empty the human mind and activate the mind of Tao,<BR>\nand go on to solve disharmony, then yin and yang combine harmoniously.<BR>\nWhat can compare to that fortune?<BR>\nThis is taking advantage of the time to solve disharmony, inasmuch as disharmony must ultimately be reconciled.\n<BR>\n</center>\n'),(2463,'<center>\nAt the extreme of disharmony, the mind of Tao has been long buried away, and people don\'t pay attention to it;<BR>\nThis is disharmony resulting in <b><tt>isolation</tt></b>.<BR>\nWith the mind of Tao buried away and the human mentality taking charge of affairs, <tt><b>the influence of habit becomes one\'s nature</b></tt>,<BR>\nand <tt><b>doubts and ruminations come forth by the hundreds</b></tt>;<BR>\nthis is being like <tt>a pig covered in mire, a wagon full of devils</tt>.\n<BR><BR>\nIf you want to restore the mind of Tao, it is necessary to first understand the human mind.<BR>\nBut to understand it, it is important to see it:<BR>\nSeeing it <tt>as a pig, as devilish</tt>, is truly seeing the human mentality and how injurious it is.<BR>\nOnce you can see and understand it clearly,<BR>\nyour former use of the human mind without understanding is like first drawing a bow,<BR>\nwhile your later ability to understand and restore the mind of Tao is like afterward putting it down.\n<BR><BR>\n<tt><b>Without the human mind, you don\'t see the mind of Tao;<BR>\nwithout the mind of Tao, you cannot know the human mind.</b></tt><BR>\nUsing the human mind temporarily to restore the mind of Tao,<BR>\neven though the human mind is the <tt>chief of villains</tt>, it is also the <tt>chief in merit</tt>;<BR>\nit is not an enemy, but really a partner.<BR>\nOnce the human mind is seen and the mind of Tao is restored, at this point if you empty the human mind and activate the mind of Tao,<BR>\nand go on to solve disharmony, then yin and yang combine harmoniously.<BR>\n<big><tt><b>It is like encountering rain,<BR>\nwhich washes away the filth of all the pollution of the past,<BR>\nso one is restored to original wholeness and soundness.</b></tt><BR></big>\nWhat can compare to that fortune?<BR>\nThis is taking advantage of the time to solve disharmony, inasmuch as disharmony must ultimately be reconciled.\n<BR>\n</center>\n'),(2464,'<p>it\\\'s been awhile since my one and only post here on swinney... there has been much to say but i have lacked the words with which to say it.  even now i am not quite sure i will be able to convey my meaning.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>i have been facing the world from behind my hair, with sound blotted out by monster dj headphones.  sometimes i forget that nailbiting and music are the things in the world that can heal you the most.  but it\\\'s hard to heal when you aren\\\'t even sure how you\\\'ve been wounded. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>mainly i just spend these days hiding from daylight....</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i hate being one of those people who\\\'s life is centered around recent events, but having never before experienced the awesome power of an \\\"adult relationship\\\".  i am awed and crushed all at once... and it is taking a toll on my thought process.  it\\\'s made me wonder if maybe i am, in fact, the horribly naive person that i secretly fear i am.  how else can i explain being so easily led along by another person?  a person who... well a person who is unlike anything else i have ever encountered.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>so here\\\'s the question.. do i listen to the friends and observers who warn against this person... or do i go with the instinct that no one can see how amazing he really is?  the fact that there truly are two people living in his body... and most times the abusive tough guy characteristics win out.  how mean is too mean?  and when is it time to give up?  i have never given up on anyone in my life so i am new to this in every aspect possible... and i feel childishly stupid.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>and when will the wound stop smarting?  and how do i grow from this experience?  because if there is nothing else garnered, at least i know that people aren\\\'t always what they present to you.  and that saddens me.. because i like being genuine and i value honesty.... and i really don\\\'t understand why people feel compelled to do the opposite.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this post was far too long.\r\n-xtal</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"the more skin that you shed... the more that the air in your throat will linger when you call him your friend\\\"</p> \r\n'),(2465,'I used to go diving in the shallow river; the <i>Ideal Gas Equation</i> is repeatedly proved correct in such environs of shallow depths and subaquatic terrain affording shallow breaths, and often were the times I\\\'d put it to the test, diving maybe 40 feet to scan the gravel bottom, looking for murky adventure amidst ancient water-logged trees and mossy bridge abutments.<br><br>\r\nMy favorite haunt was the Walnut Street Bridge, or moreover, its deeply plunging limestone supports of massive hand-hewn cornerstones, stacked deep from the depths to high above the water. The riverbed beneath the bridge afforded a wide range of scattered debris, thrown from a century\\\'s worth of hands; the headlight fitting from a 60\\\'s era city bus, a rotary-dial pay telephone, flintlock parts discarded from disarmed Civil War casualties, antebellum cobalt medicine bottles.<br><br>\r\nOne day, I found a large hammer down there. The bridge was undergoing a major renovation after having sat to rot, its abandoned wrought-iron spans having been closed to traffic for a decade. There\\\'s nothing sadder than a town with such a beautiful dead bridge. But busy hands had been put to work repairing rust-scars and lime scale, replacing single-lane wooden roadway with asphalt, a modern day overhaul for yesterday\\\'s river-crossing nightmare.<br><br>\r\nUndoubtedly, some clumsy or absentminded worker had dropped the hammer from the bridge, into the water, as it was rather new and had not yet begun to rust. I retrieved the hammer from the depths and took it home, scrubbing the small bit of river grime from the texture of its rubber handle and buffing the slight cloud of riparian offal that hazed its silvery surface. <i>This is a man\\\'s hammer</i>, I thought, marveling at its magnificent weight, its cross-hatched anti-skip striking face, its long, sinuously curved claw-back. This was a professional grade tool, dropped by professional hands to be retrieved by an amateur diving teenager.<br><br>\r\nI used the large roofing hammer for years; often were the days I\\\'d retrieve it from the tool cabinet to hang a piece of art, to re-set a nail in the old wooden floor, to hang drywall in the attic; each time my mother would frown as I reveled in the pure overkill of using such a huge, weighty hammer for the smallest tasks. But, as any enlightened man knows, sometimes nothing feels more genuinely real than hefting a piece of pure, top-quality utilitarian steel, an implement so overly task-appropriate that its design is without both flaw and luxury. That was the essence of this hammer.<br><br>\r\nSeveral years later, I was on the same river, tending to a dock on the east bank, maybe two hundred feet upstream from the Walnut Street Bridge. By this time, my diving days were over, but the hammer remained a consistent part of my hardware repertoire. With subtle awe, I drew its seductive, ergonomic shape from my toolbox to sink a series of brassy ten-penny nails into the splintery ends of the marine dock-board.<br><br>\r\nAs right as rain, my hand fumbled, and the river fates took back their hammer. As cloudy, gassy bubbles struck the surface, most likely from where the heavy steel hammer made contact with the murky, muddy bottom, I somehow knew that the hammer had returned home, on some level. And I learned that sometimes, things are meant to be a certain way. You can intercede, interfere, make ulterior motives, reclaim, rescue; but sometimes things are just meant to be. Yes, my river-hammer taught me much, mostly that sometimes the heaviest tools can only be hefted by the hand of fate. \r\n'),(2466,'			now i can\\\'t stop staring at women\\\'s breasts - in the gym, in changing rooms, at the communal showers, under loose sweaters at the bus stop, while going down bouncy stairs, bound with handkechiefs and lace - oh they are everywhere. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\n			fat women with large breasts like mushrooms, thin ones with aubergines. black women, white women, pink nipples, brown nipples. old women with saggy drooping breasts, young ones with perky straight ahead breasts, nipples like a second pair of eyes.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n			women on the night shift are more likely to get cancer. its the light, the bright light at night, it screws up the hormones. i read about it. i read a study done over several years on a bunch of nurses.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n			do a damn mammogram. mash your breasts into pancakes between two glass plates.<BR><BR>\r\n			mammary glands. self-examination. lumps.<BR><BR>\r\n			a little introspection wouldn\\\'t hurt.<BR><BR>\r\n			my cousin had breast cancer, but big deal. she got it removed, i think of it as purged, she has gone through the fire, the scalpel, and she is pure. now she\\\'s a happily married mother to a baby girl. the baby girl looks boyish. my cousin cuts her hair close to the scalp, like she\\\'s a toddler-marine.<BR><BR>\r\n			i don\\\'t want to think of being breastless. i\\\'d be lopsided, i\\\'d topple over, i\\\'d drown. a mis-loaded boat capsizing and nobody cares, they arrive too late. aren\\\'t ships named as women? what life boats? <BR><BR>\r\n			or losing one breast. i\\\'d be only one-third a woman, no-one could touch me. not because people are bastards, which they are, let\\\'s face facts here, people are awful awful awful, but because i wouldn\\\'t let them. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\n			imagine a map of veins, in a pit the colour of a bruise, a bruise which never fades, and think of the scars, think of frankenstein\\\'s discoloured skin. grotesque. if part of you is a monster it means all of you is a monster. i mean, where does monster end and where does non-monster begin? you can\\\'t draw an outline around it, put yellow caution tape around your upper chest.  <BR><BR>\r\n\r\n			the question is, does sex equal death? i mean, am i actually admitting i\\\'d rather die than be without a breast or two? breast or two. i like how i\\\'ve phrased that. see, i can be casual-like. i\\\'m not obsessive about this. i\\\'m not one of those people who stares down at her cleavage and smiles. or one of those hideous chicks that examines the shape of her breasts and compares them to the sluts in men\\\'s magazines.<BR><BR>\r\n			a life isolated, touchless, isn\\\'t that worse than death?<BR><BR>\r\n			am i being superficial, crazy? don\\\'t blame me, don\\\'t hate me, i\\\'m just here waiting for the test results.'),(2467,'<PRE>\r\n\\\"... something with the mundane narratives of people\\\'s lives and\r\nthe idea of death which is central to living.\\\" \r\n\r\n			(a.t.)\r\n\r\nthere are different ways to cut a pineapple, it depends on the hands, \r\n	and the boti one uses.\r\nchachi, half blind, could do it so fast smoke would rise between her fingers.\r\nthe slices of sun transformed each blue plate into a monsoon afternoon.\r\nthe mouths of her nieces, and one nephew, retarded, \r\n	though our mothers always slapped their palms across our lips \r\n	when we used that burning word \r\nwere the source of evening. \r\nthe not-so-crazy old woman who lived in a hut at the edge\r\n	of the boys\\\' lake promised us whoever ate the core \r\n	of the fruit thirty-three nights in a row\r\n	would see the Holy Prophet in a dream.\r\non t.v. we saw white children fighting over wishbones, and surprise\r\n	scribbled onto our foreheads: the core of a pineapple, \r\n	the fat oval seed of a mango, these were worthy\r\n	of scratches and hair pulled out of skulls. \r\nwhen chacha had his heart attack, our eldest cousin\r\n	was given the center of the fruit every night.\r\nwe prayed silently, as we hung the washing out on rope\r\n	stretched thin between date and guava trees.\r\nthe children stopped playing ludo, and copied out their multiplication tables\r\n	up to twenty in silence; my sister, left handed, would smudge \r\n	the ink of the three numbers after the one she was writing.\r\na month and three days of cleaning the outhouse, gathering the still warm eggs\r\n	harvesting the rice from muddy fields, and milking our black and white cow\r\n	and big cousin saw His face, and woke up crying.\r\nthe retarded boy took off his clothes and walked out onto the road as the rain\r\n	fell in wide straight gushes, windows of water for airless rooms.\r\nwe followed him, our feet breaking through the ground, chests imploding, \r\n	chacha\\\'s pale face hovering in the rain drops\r\n	as if we had grown bee eyes.\r\njasmine petals hung like white stars in his hair;\r\n	he was chanting nonsense to split the earth\r\n	or stitch chacha\\\'s heart or make a new whitewashed home.\r\nwhen he saw us, his mouth opened so much i thought i would fall \r\n	into it, i could crouch inside his chest and live\r\n	a bent life under his ribs, away from chacha\r\n	and the starving children in somalia\r\n	the soldiers in bosnia, the suicide bombers\r\n	in palestine --\r\nbut he closed it quietly, shook the scented blossoms from his head; patiently\r\n	not answering the questions on our faces.\r\nhis wet skin had a sheen ours did not, and he took the small hand \r\n	our littlest cousin offered him with only the smallest of hesitations.\r\n</PRE>'),(2468,'<P>when you read this, you will think of me as a little girl. pig tails, chattery, all of that. but the older i get, the less i am what i used to be. and i guess you\\\'re always supposed to be just a little reminiscent of who you were. like when i used to think myself a passionate person. like when i wanted to tear from man the masks of pretension and morality. burn it. just get rid of those vices all together. but now, i have no pigtails and i don\\\'t give a fuck if man is pretentious. in fact, let him be. let him be ill and weak and happy because it\\\'s all about illness anyway, as both excuse and as justification for harmony. and woman, you can bleed and bleed and blame some more. suffer the thinkers of the past and try to find reason in your monthly decomposition. feel heroic for battling through all of the heat and pain. lean on your internal organs as you back-peddle through countless years of subservience and silence.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen you are sick, your spiritual health is supposed to become more intense, says nietzsche. you sweat and smell and in this omission of natural/unnatural waste, there you receive clarity that can be only revered. suddenly there is meaning in everything, from the spry little spring flowers, to the uselessness of an entire generation of man. men. you hope, but still, you die, and please, can\\\'t this just be a rehearsal? next time, i\\\'ll get it right. i\\\'m just kind of learning as i go, right now, you know? i was just kidding. i didn\\\'t really mean that. next time, i\\\'ll get you little whippersnappers. there will forever be a next time.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwell, i absolve you all. we\\\'re all god, didn\\\'t you know? i can absolve you and you can absolve me. no matter how decadent you become, no matter how stubborn and absolute you remain, where i inevitably have to come and pick up your scattered, disillusioned pieces, i will absolve you. but don\\\'t ask me to be faithful. don\\\'t ever even ask because there is no faith. there is no such thing as remaining faithful to any other human. the temptation is too great, the mind too expansive, and the real threat lies in the ability for the body to betray one\\\'s friends without even the voluntary acquiescence of it\\\'s owner. so stop trying. there\\\'s no sense in the futile.\r\n</P><P>\r\nyes. life matters. life is absolute. but enough already. deal with the existing imperfections and just get over yourself. go out, consume and dispose, and slowly, oh so slowly, realize and give in. you are obstinate, but to no end.  as alone as you are, as strong as you might be or may become, you don\\\'t matter. you are, still, innately and always, a slave.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni will always be that little girl, no matter how many times i do things that shock you or turn you on. and i will do them. i will continue to act and then regret and then act some more, because there\\\'s no need to think of the consequences. they don\\\'t matter. i control myself for a mere second compared to the amount of time that the rest of the world is in control of me. and the greatest trick anyone ever mastered, was that of accepting loneliness and silence. when you go out there, to wherever you might go, you are still alone. they are not with you, they are not experiencing the same things, and you will never find one of them who understands the way that you do. you will not find your soul mate because he does not exist. listen to kierkegaard when he says, \\\"if i conduct myself like others, i shall be a betrayer; if i separate from them, i betray myself.\\\" so i say, separate. be alone. your mind and heart are forever divided, a chronically divorced couple with no hope for reconciliation. so stop trying to force them into union, for there is no secret, heavenly rapport with the rest of the world or society. you\\\'re ill, you\\\'re bleeding, you\\\'re alone. admire the flowers, feel pain for the lost souls. but the only place you belong is in death, and in the covetous eyes of those who have yet to see the truth themselves.</P>'),(2469,'<p>undoubtedly I am a lurker. I post, but never comment. I read many posts, think of clever witty comments, sad sometimes, analogous to my own experiences, but somehow I don\\\'t comment. Ever. I\\\'ve been reading Swinney for almost a year now, and I have still never dipped into a comment train. I check back daily to see if anyone has commented on my post, hoping to start a discussion without really having to initiate something. I want everyone else to make the move. Never me. Is it pathological? Fear of rejection? The worm syndrome? The wallflower. I wanted to comment on Peter\\\'s most recent post. It is beautiful. You are one of my favorite writers on here. A little while ago, someone started a string about New Orleans. I too love New Orleans. I suppose, that I\\\'ve always been thought of as anti-social--and online I have even more freedom to continue to avoid interaction. No one can see me sitting here, writing comments and then pressing cancel or <--back.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i suffer from abortitis.</P>'),(2470,'<p>\r\nAnother beautiful day with mother and father, and little sissy!<br>\r\nAnd I have my sweetest baby, my sweetest doll!<br>\r\nThe meadow is so pretty this time of year,<br>\r\nMother always says. Soon it will be my birthday,<br>\r\nAnd Iâ€™ll be eleven years old!\r\n<br><br>\r\nIâ€™m almost a big girl.\r\n<br><br>\r\nMother says that when she was little,<br> \r\nOnly my age,<br>\r\nShe played in this same meadow with her baby dolls,<br>\r\nThey used to have such parties, she said!<br>\r\nSuch parties. \r\n<br><br>\r\nTea parties!\r\n\r\nFather says that he had the meadow cut two weeks ago,<br>\r\nand now it is the perfect length for playing in.<br>\r\nI think heâ€™s right, but these mushrooms everywhere!<br>\r\nThey grow in great big faerie-rings.<br>\r\nI love it when mother cooks mushrooms for us.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI think theyâ€™re my favorite.\r\n<br><br>\r\nNow we will go over to the stump,<br>\r\nWith the pool of water in the top--<br>\r\nA swimming pool for my little baby!<br>\r\nShe looks like she needs a swim, and<br>\r\nThe sun is hurting my eyes.\r\n<br><br>\r\nIt\\\'s so awfully bright!\r\n<br><br>\r\nIn goes baby, and doesnâ€™t she look pretty,<br>\r\nSwimming in her pool.<br> \r\nLook how well my baby swims!<br>\r\nLook at her kicking her arms and legs!<br>\r\nNow a centipede is swimming with her too.<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\nNow that we are the same size, heâ€™s not so scary.\r\n<br><br>\r\nWait! He is leaving too.<br>\r\nWait!<br>\r\nWait!<br>\r\nNothing to do but follow him, I suppose.<br>\r\nMaybe my baby is with him.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMaybe they are swimming together.\r\n<br><br>\r\nItâ€™s getting dark though.<br>\r\nThe sky is dark for some odd reason,<br>\r\nIsnâ€™t it supposed to be blue? What is that awful noise?<br>\r\nAnd this water!<br> \r\nMuch too cold for swimming!<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\nIâ€™m shaking!\r\n<br>\r\n<i>Get her arms! Her arms!</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nWhere on earth has my baby gotten to?<br>\r\n<i>For Godâ€™s sake, Harold, hold her!</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nWhere is the centipede? Where are mother and father?<br>\r\n<i>Why wonâ€™t she stop shaking?</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nWhereâ€™s my sweet baby?<br> \r\n<i>Sheâ€™s not breathing, oh my sweet baby...</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nWhere am I?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2471,'<p>i never thought i would live in southern california. growing up in the tiny hick town of oroville (about 2 hours north of sacramento), all we were taught was that those evil people in los angeles were sucking water from the big lake near our house (lake oroville: a central feature of the state water project). there were years when they got so greedy with their water-drinking that our dad had to forego taking us out on the speedboat for endless hours of waterskiing and fried chicken-eating, if you can believe that. now, 20 years later, there still are plenty of things to complain about when it comes to this crazy, sprawling place. but sunday was one of those days that makes it, somehow, just okay.</p>\r\n<p> it\\\'s probably 75 degrees out on the beach near santa barbara, the mountainous santa cruz island clear in the distance off the coast; the snowy plovers all fluffy and hopping around the bordering sand dunes; the waves big and steady, filling your head with that luxurious crashing sound. and, best of all, walking next to a boy who just happened to have a kite in the trunk of his car.</p>\r\n<p>we stroll down to the place where we were meeting a group of his friends from work, the sand slipping through our toes until we make it to the hard-packed wet stuff. it\\\'s an unusually low tide that day, so there is a lot of it, glazed with the amazing patterned texture that chic fabric manufacturers want you to forget occurs in nature.</p>\r\n<p>we sit and eat and talk and play with the world\\\'s cutest cocker spaniel and bounce around in the surf for hours. the entire day was so calm--there was barely a breeze--that i\\\'m sure the lovely kite would have to go back in the trunk unused. but sometimes, on the idyllic beach in southern california on a summer-like day in the middle of february, just at the very moment you want to fly that kite with the boy you are falling for, the wind kicks up for you. </p>\r\n<p>we unravel the crinkly, colorful streamers, he ties a clever knot to attach the string, and up she goes. and as he\\\'s launching that funny contraption higher into the atmosphere, it occurs to me: i have never flown a kite. it seems impossible, but it\\\'s entirely true. i have seen other people do it. i have read about it. and i DO know all the words to the gleeful song in \\\"mary poppins.\\\" but i have never actually held the thing in my hands, feeling it pull as it gyrates up there in the wind. mmmmm, what a great feeling.</p>\r\n<p> we take it down closer to the water and recklessly let the string out, even though we know it will be a huge pain to roll in. but we want her to go up up up --- soaring above the sandy bluffs to ensure everyone in the five-mile radius can enjoy it, no doubt thinking to themselves: those kiteflyers sure are lucky; what a perfect day for it. <p/>\r\n'),(2472,'<p>\r\nsoft skin of your hand on my hand<br>\r\nlimbs akimbo in the sand<br>\r\nthat day we played rodeo<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsunset glint <br>\r\nthrough a web of monofilament<br>\r\nyour laundry lay in mounds<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsmall portion in a bowl<br>\r\nsalt absorbed in your soul<br>\r\nhand to mouth with wooden spoon<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n(i miss your constant groans<br>\r\nsmall chuckles and giant moans<br>\r\nfrom the other side of the apartment)<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npolaroids tacked to the wall and arrayed<br>\r\nblurred landscapes, elegant decay<br>\r\nand a few of friends at the beach.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmounds of matter on the floor<br>\r\npaint stuck upon, layered, contoured-- <br>\r\nthe cat once curled on a pile of clothes.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nour bodies digress<br>\r\nminds pour forth less and less; <br>\r\nshuffled inside, scatterhearted and calm.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere will be new circuitry this year<br>\r\npasted on poorly and smeared,<br>\r\nand also, maybe, good eatin.<br>\r\n</p>'),(2473,'Kickin at the pavement trying to provoke some reaction, his thoughts turn to that nice battered up leather cowboy hat that he couldn\\\'t afford. It would go so well with the new image he would like to try and pull of, rockabilly sorta with some redneck chic for finesse.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe looks around wonderin why everbody in this damn city likes slacks and a black or gray three quarter length <i>coats</i>. His desire for a recognizably small town fashion sense has more then likely been manifest in some sort of polar response to all of this he imagines.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOne of the scenes from Midnight Cowboy with Joe Buck crossing the street enters into his mind and he kicks at the pavement again. He sure ain\\\'t no damn hustler, though. Asked for change on the street corner gets him to thinking, how long is he going to last? Only got a roof over his head due to generousity of an old lover and hasn\\\'t seen a job in almost a year now.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLast time he had a drink it was bought by an aquaintance and accompanied by some sorta talk about how the rough around the edges barabrian type wasn\\\'t really in these days. His mind was off dreamin about better times so most of it just rolled right off his back, he figured the guy meant well anyways.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe folks in the city that he feels the most kinship with are the punk kids on the streets, probably has more in common with them then those folks always wearing the <i>coats</i>. Maybe thats were he belongs, on the streets - an ornament in a city populated by <i>coats</i>. Doesn\\\'t seem like much of an existence to him though, better to try and make a run at things.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIf frustration grips to tightly maybe he\\\'ll rethink that bit about being a hustler, hell it gets to tight why stop at being a hustler? Havin\\\' marketable skills does you no good when the markets all dried up. Tiried of frustration and rejection maybe he\\\'ll just sit down on the pavement here for a little while, no sense in going back to the apartment and just sitting there all day.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNot even a minute passes before some faceless <i>coat</i> walks by and drops a bit of change on the pavement in front of him. The frustration and anger boils inside, up he gets kickin at the pavement scattering the change as he walks off. Damn <i>coats</i> just don\\\'t get it.<br><br>     '),(2474,'it\\\'s been months. i have been twiddling with html and font tags and lear gifs, and i finally threw up my hands in despair and shouted \\\'THERE HAS TO BE A BETTER WAY\\\' and threw things out the window. three days later, i sat down in front of my laptop with a bitter pugilistic look and started flipping through CSS tutorial sites. hey. hey look.\r\n\r\nthis isn\\\'t so bad. this stuff is still formatting, but it\\\'s more choices and you only have to do it once. armed with the shiny sword of knowledge, i proceed to hack at my html. two days later, clothes torn in shreds, hair smoking,  and pondering whether i should just use flash, i am staring at the margin-top: 100px; line for the 200th time, wondering why it doesn\\\'t work. damn this linear thinking. i am stumped. i am mad. i gnash. i growl at jeff. he runs away.\r\n\r\nthen i had my revelation. i don\\\'t have to do this myself. i have FRIENDS who do this for a living. they\\\'re suckers, they\\\'ll want to help me. i don\\\'t have to be a sweet html designer. i just want to show some dinky drawings and some clothes i made to the world. i want snarky book reviews. i want to put up pictures of me and my sweetie. sigh. is there something i\\\'m missing? a killer app? i\\\'m trying to modify css file with dreamweaver, but it\\\'s messy.\r\n\r\nwebsites weren\\\'t this complicated when i was a kid, dagnabbit. '),(2475,'<p>It\\\'s been awhile since i\\\'ve had anything online.<br> Before this i\\\'d removed as much of myself from the internet as possible, and now i\\\'m sitting in front of computers daily again i\\\'m getting dragged back into it.<br></P>\r\n<p>I dont know if this is a good thing, I mean it feels like i\\\'ve been \\\"away\\\" for so long.<br>Using computers nearly _every_ day of your life, then coming to a complete stop for a number of months really makes you aware of how much time is wasted on here.<br>Yet, i\\\'m back.. I can\\\'t keep away.<br>\r\nI feel like such a failure in most respects, and even in this way too.<br> My social life is almost non-existant at the moment. I\\\'m deeply in love, and spend all of my time with my loved one, so \\\'my\\\' social life becomes \\\'our\\\' social life.<br>\r\nI don\\\'t mind this, she makes me happy :)<br></P>\r\n<p>I have the feeling i\\\'m becoming more stable.<br>This is a good thing.<br> Throughout my life stability has been a real problem, i mean i\\\'ve gone through lots of shit previously, as everyone has in their own way.<br> I can\\\'t seem to deal with my problems as well as other people, but i\\\'m working on it.<br></p>\r\n<p>Life just has a way of catching up on me, i guess.<br> all the shit that i\\\'ve done in the past just keeps creeping up.<br> The girl im with at the moment has problems with my past.<br> I\\\'d love to ignore everything that happened before i met her, just take if from there, but she can\\\'t seem to handle my ex\\\'s.. my previous relationships.<br> I guess for that reason she doesn\\\'t trust me much.. and that sucks.<br>\r\nI mean, we\\\'ve been together for nearly a year, and still I feel like she doesn\\\'t trust me 100%.<br>Thats a feeling that just ain\\\'t right :)</p>'),(2476,'<p>How things can change in just one day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Just yesterday the sun was shining and spring was peeking through the clouds.  The world was full of life with animals and people busy with their day.  Everyone totally oblivious to their surroundings, just enjoying the pseudo spring that has come.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Cue the sunset, with oohs and aahs people gawk as if they have never seen a sun set before.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then, just one day later.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The blue skies have been replaced with something less content.  Snow is falling from the sky again.  People are hurrying to get where they are going, no sightseeing today.  This is the same city as yesterday?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s like two different worlds but only seperated by a single day.  And here we are, wandering in the awful weather.  From one shop to another.  Snow falling all around.  Quite a contrast from the day before sitting at home while people were enjoying the day at hand.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s hard to believe this is even happening.  It\\\'s like a weird dream, from which I don\\\'t know if I will ever wake.  One, from which I never want to return.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If this much fun can be had during such a dreary day, who knows what the upcoming change of seasons holds.  Hopefully many bouts of dejavu and nights in the summer breeze eating ice cream under the stars or what stars you can see with the overwhelming light pollution.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Friends change like the seasons.  But no matter what happens, you know summer is a maximum of three seasons away, and it can never come too soon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some things are worth the wait.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2477,'<p>\r\nBack on the bus and heading toward home. He sits facing forward, the seat next to him empty, his head tilted, resting on the plexiglas. There is an old woman in the seat directly behind him, singing to herself. She sings a line from a Rodgers and Hammerstein musical, he canâ€™t remember which, over and over. Itâ€™s South Pacific or Flower Drum Song or The King and I. Something to do with some country that rings the far side of the Pacific Ocean. The only word he can make out is â€œcockeyedâ€. He gets off the bus before heâ€™s even halfway home and steps into the glow of a neon-enriched fog. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe neon comes from the window of a bar that hugs the bus stop like a lonely drunk. A luminous Bud Light glow billows out from the window into the mist. Itâ€™ll be rain soon, but itâ€™s not rain yet. He steps toward the bar with his hands shoved far down into his pockets. As he reaches the door a woman flings herself against the glass shoving him to the side and rolls herself out to the curb where she falls to a crouch and hangs her head between her knees. He imagines her moaning to be singing, pretends he hears her say â€œcockeyedâ€ as he pulls his hands free and punches the door open. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe mist is replaced by a haze of smoke, Rodgers and Hammerstein by an old Pat Benatar song on the jukebox. He stands in the doorway while his eyes adjust and takes stock of the clientele. A little quiet, itâ€™s still early, only five or six people hunch over tables in the booths to his right. At the bar, one guy slouches on a stool, the left half of his ass hanging precariously over the edge of the vinyl. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe rubs his hand over his forehead and looks up at the ceiling like heâ€™s trying to decide whether or not heâ€™ll stay. He stays. He walks up to the bar and leans in with his wallet in his hand, waiting for the bartender. The bartender asks him what he wants, and without thinking he says â€œRubber bandâ€. The bartender just stares at him waiting for him to name a drink. â€œA Manhattan,â€ he says this time. â€œNeat... I guess.â€\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2478,'<pre>\r\nin the beginning\r\na man and a woman had a child\r\nand they called him tyler\r\nafter his father\r\n\r\nin the beginning\r\nthere are car seats\r\nthere is sun in my eyes\r\nthere are no words\r\nthere are no memories\r\n\r\nthere are suckers\r\nthere are orange juice cans\r\nthat became rocketships\r\nthere are beanbags\r\nthat become launchpads\r\n\r\nthere is a baby brother\r\nfor some reason\r\nfor some reason\r\nhe needs to be tackled\r\n\r\nthere is a father\r\nthere is a father who has a go-cart\r\nwho flies airplanes\r\nwho drives the emm gee tee cee\r\n\r\nin the beginning there are other children\r\nopen your mouth\r\nclose your eyes\r\nget ready for a surprise\r\nbad children\r\n\r\nthere is a mother\r\ndragging me by the hand across the yard\r\nfeeding me cake\r\nreading me stories\r\n\r\nthere are bicycles\r\nbig red shiny bicycle\r\ntraining wheels\r\nthere is a little house on a circle\r\nin the beginning\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2479,'<p>\r\nShe is sitting on the grass with her feet bent under and to one side of her, playing with an anklet made out of safety pins, the plastic kind that comes in bright primary colours. \r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"I hate people who smoke.\\\" </p><p>\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"You don\\\'t hate me.\\\" </p><p>\r\n\\\"I do. You know, when I was 16 I made up a bunch of Rules For My Life. Things like Never Intentionally Hurt Someone If You Can Help It. Never Ever Cheat Even In History Class Where It Is Just Memorisation. Never Steal Anything Or Try To Exchange Stuff Under False Pretences. Never Be Friends With Someone Who Smokes. I was serious about it too. If I found out that a friend had started smoking, I would stop being friends with them. I\\\'d cut them out of my life. Just like that. I was always a very earnest little girl.\\\" She starts laughing, a little embarrassed, already regretting admitting this much. \r\n\\\"Yes, I know. I\\\'ve seen the pictures.\\\" \r\nShe punches him.</p><p> \r\n\\\"What\\\'s that supposed to mean?\\\" \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\n\\\"Nothing. Nothing at all,\\\" he grins. \\\"You were adorable as a child. I particularly like the one of you standing on top of the slide with your arms outstretched.\\\" </p><p>\r\n\\\"I was trying to reach the sky.\\\" She looks at him, curious, \\\"You remember that? I showed you those photos ages ago.\\\" </p><p>\r\n\\\"But why? Why this pathology about people who smoke? Isn\\\'t that some kind of discrimination?\\\" he asks, quickly, trying to change the subject. \\\"What happened to your principles? Equality for everyone?\\\" </p><p>\r\nShe\\\'s aware of his attempt to distract her, but decides to let it go. \\\"Pathology? Not at all. Pathology implies that it is abnormal or irrational or something. But it\\\'s not. It\\\'s quite sensible, really.\\\" </p><p>\r\nHe lifts his eyebrows, but doesn\\\'t say anything. \r\n\\\"I can explain it to you-\\\" She breaks off, hesitates. He remains silent, somehow aware that if he speaks, she may not continue. She looks at him, brow wrinkled, trying to decide. \\\"I\\\'ll try to tell you, but only if you promise to just listen. Don\\\'t say anything, anything at all, okay? If you do, I\\\'ll kick you.\\\" </p><p>\r\nHe laughs and pretends to back away. He points to his throat, miming a question. </p><p>\r\n\\\"Yes, you can say something _now_.\\\" </p><p> \r\n\\\"Thank you. All right, I promise not to say anything while you speak. Go on, justify your crazy rule, if you can.\\\" </p><p>\r\n\\\"It was very logical to me to not have friends who smoke. You see, I\\\'d seen what it did to my father\\\'s brother. To a strong vibrant healthy man. It was almost ironic. He always exercised the most out of everyone in the family, and he was so conscientious about his diet, you know. Took far better care of himself than my father or any of his other brothers. Smoking was his one and only vice. And after battling with cancer for a scant six months, he looked like a skeleton with the skin left on by mistake.\\\" </p><p>\r\nHe\\\'s stopped smiling, and for some bizarre reason this makes her feel good, it spurs her on. \r\n\\\"I hated going to their house. Even with the lights on, it was dark. I hated sitting in the drawing room, on the expensive brocade couches, with my aunt\\\'s hand embroidered cushions at my back. I hated sitting there trying to get the too sweet fruit -- mangoes -- his favourite, down my throat. Especially after I found out he could no longer even eat solid food. I always wondered why they continued to buy mangoes.\\\" </p><p>\r\nShe pulls up some grass stems and starts tearing them into tiny pieces. \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\n\\\"I hated trying to act normal around my cousins. What do you say to someone whose father is dying? I hated feeling guilty about the fact that my father was perfectly fine. And most of all, I hated having to look my uncle straight in the eye and pretend everything was all right. Not being able to cry until the ride home. Every time we came back from a visit I felt as if something had broken inside me. So when my grandfather got cancer I simply refused to see him. My father never forgave me, but I couldn\\\'t, I just couldn\\\'t. I wanted to remember my grandfather the way he was to me, before the tumours got him. I was selfish, but in the end I won. I WON.\\\" </p><p>\r\nShe sucks in a breath, scattering the bits of grass on her lap. She tries to pretend that she\\\'s talking to herself, that he isn\\\'t there, though she can hear his breathing, steady, and calming. Even comforting, she thinks, and is irritated at this possibility. That theory her psych professor was always talking about last semester pops into her mind. She shakes her head free of it and continues, without quite knowing why. </p><p>\r\n\\\"In my mind my grandfather is still the man who let my little brother try to pull his white beard off after watching the christmas specials on tv. He\\\'s still the man who stood perfectly upright, back straight, white punjabi immaculately ironed, cutting up the sugar cane into pieces that were just the right size for our child mouths. He used to use the pillows on his bed as his ironing board. I\\\'d sit and watch him iron, my eyes following his slow, precise movements, and tell him stories. He used to make it look like a joy, not a chore. He treated everything like that. Once when I was about six or seven, me and my younger sister sat down and got some paper and decided to teach him English. We spent at least a couple of hours that morning, writing out the alphabet and making him copy the letters. I remember saying \\\'No No No, there\\\'s no loop on the tail of the y, Dada.\\\' and him laughing and trying to write them without the fancy curliques at the ends.\\\" </p><p></p><p>\r\nShe thinks he\\\'s smiling again, now, but she doesn\\\'t want to let him see her check. She starts to gather the grass fragments into a neat pile in front of her feet instead. </p><p>\r\n\\\"That same afternoon we rushed to Abbu and told him what we\\\'d done. We were so proud of ourselves. I can still feel the embarrassment engulfing my entire body when he told us Dada already knew English. That he\\\'d known it for fifty years. And yet he\\\'d pretended not to, just to please us. He\\\'d played along.\\\" </p><p>\r\nShe starts piling soil on top of the little pile of grass, crinkling her forehead in concentration. </p><p>\r\n\\\"He died while he was sleeping. My father cried for the first time in my whole life, in his whole life, as he told me how after they moved the pillow, Dada\\\'s head remained bent in mid-air, as if the pillow was still there beneath it.\\\" \r\nShe pauses for a second, her own hand in mid air, above the grass and soil. </p><p>\r\n\\\"Anyway, that was when I made the resolution never to maintain contact with someone who smokes. No matter how much I love them. So you see, I\\\'m not going to grieve for --\\\" her voice breaks again, and this time she doesn\\\'t even try to hide it. </p><p>\r\nShe sits there, staring at the ground, then gets up suddenly, dodging his arm. She starts walking toward the library. </p><p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m not going to get cancer,\\\" he says, quietly, catching up to her. </p><p>\r\nShe tries smiling, but her lips feel thick, funny. \\\"That\\\'s right, I forgot.\\\" \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'re immortal, aren\\\'t you? On top of being invincible,\\\" she shouts, before running suddenly, in the opposite direction. </p><p>\r\n\\\"I like your blue nail polish,\\\" he says to her back, when he finally reaches her. </p><p> \r\nAnd she can\\\'t help it, she starts laughing. Laughing as hard as she can, as loudly as she can. Two passers by, a couple, stop and look at her, obviously concerned.</p><p> \r\n\\\"I\\\'m fine,\\\" she calls out to them, and waves. \r\n</p><p></p><p>\r\nThe man shrugs his shoulders and walks on. But the woman stands there, looking at her, unconvinced. So she blows the woman a kiss, and says, \\\"I\\\'m going to Paris. I\\\'m so ecstatic I can\\\'t keep the happiness inside me!\\\" </p><p>\r\nThe woman laughs and walks over and hugs her. A total stranger hugs her. And just as suddenly, after wishing her well, the woman is gone. </p><p> \r\n\\\"I do hate you,\\\" she says, so softly he\\\'s not sure he heard her correctly. </p><p>\r\nHe coughs and she looks up. And wishes she hadn\\\'t. '),(2480,'<P>\\\"let\\\'s see, our records show your last visit to our office was in... 1997?\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I\\\'m at the dental office.  After years of broke-ness and bad oral hygiene and too much work I finally went back to school, got a loan and coverage under my parent\\\'s insurance, so I thought its high time to get my teeth checked.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And its a sad, brutal process.  Dental hygienists chastise me; \\\"There\\\'s no way I can finish the cleaning today. You\\\'ll have to come back tomorrow.  Do you eat a lot of sweets or something?  You\\\'ve got a lot of cavity action here.  And these old silver fillings, the one in the back is split in two, and oh this one\\\'s coming out. The doctor\\\'s going to have to take a look at this.\\\"  \r\n</P><P>\\\"So, um, how many cavities do I have?\\\" \r\n</P><P>\\\"I\\\'d say at least a half dozen.  Does that thing on your lip bother you?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\\\"What thing on my lip?!!\\\"\r\n</P><P>\\\"Its a blocked gland, its from constantly biting it.  Does it ever turn blue and swollen?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\\\"Noo?!\\\"\r\n \r\n</P><P>So my mouth\\\'s a rotting mess.  This has been a theme in my life the past year.   The year of me-decay. Nothing major really; just certain key organs have been affected.  More of a quality of life issue.  More of an omen of things to come.  \r\n\r\n</P><P>I blame life of course.  I blame early mornings and eating flakies on the way to work. I blame this goddamn protestant province I was born and raised in; the ethic that we should all work like dogs and never ask for a handout.  The ethic that you can\\\'t put enough sugar in food.  \r\n\r\n</P><P>I dream of the day I\\\'ll have enough time to brush three times a day and floss.\r\n </P>'),(2481,'<P>\r\nit has been incredibly difficult for me to stop smoking.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni have a highly addictive personality. the \\\'this is your brain on drugs\\\' commercial with the fried egg describes my tendencies really well, only it\\\'s not just my brain, it\\\'s my hands and my nose and everything else. especially with smoking, since nothing else seems to calm my nervous fluttering.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhile i was in paris, smoking was divine. everyone is a chainsmoker there, for better or for worse, so one can avoid the tsk-tsking of strangers when indulging. not to mention the fact that there\\\'s something about the city that calls for rampant self-destructive activity, as long as you do it with languid flourish. \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut i know smoking is bad for me, it\\\'s vile vile vile. when i was visiting my parents over christmas break i cut down a lot, in part because they are relentlessly critical of negative attributes and also because it is damned expensive. and i swear for the three weeks i was there, my breathing was easier, i smelled better and i felt clean.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni also developed an eye twitch.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe first few weeks i was back at school, i had it under control. i was kind of liking the new healthy me, and i started fantasizing about rock climbing and running the marine corps marathon. then i realized i have two months until my thesis is due, and my boyfriend broke up with me. i figure nicotine is a small pleasure i should allow myself if it means greater mental stability.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe thing is, i\\\'m really not enjoying it that much anymore. my camels don\\\'t taste that great, and i\\\'m increasingly conscious of the considerable dent it puts in my less-than-impressive budget. i guess i\\\'m just looking for an excuse to quit. i\\\'d look for a health-freak boyfriend, but it would be nice to come up with a reason by myself. \r\n</P>'),(2482,'<p>\r\na while ago i bought a very expensive pomegranate. pomegranates look especially beautiful split open but with the hard plainness of the round skin left on. because the contrast between the inside -- a tight bunch of tiny rubies -- and the outside  -- can stop a person from leaving the kitchen table, from going to another room. the red jewels shine almost to the point of transparency. each segment of fruit, smaller than my littlest fingernail, has a pure white centre. i learnt that my chin was made for the sweet deep juice to soak into its skin.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nmy parents are talking, again, of separating. i have no idea why this closes up my throat and hands. i\\\'m looking down at my fists and thinking i am not a child anymore. i\\\'m too old to be this upset. why is this bothering me so much? so i\\\'ve been attacking this mystery -- trying to unravel the pain and the clenching. what i\\\'ve figured out is that parents, despite their bad tempers, the rolling of tissues into balls while waiting in line at the bank or the flossing in the den instead of in the bathroom with the door closed, their off key singing as they stir fry the vegetables for dinner, their insistence on lecturing in a sleep-inducing monotone, despite their hundred thousand annoying habits, parents are comfort. they\\\'re safety. i can be all grown up. i can go out to work or leave for university. but, at the back of my mind is the knowledge that i can always come home. that there will be a home. a quirky, messed up, but steady home. the place you fill in for the section they have on every form after mailing address -- the permanent address. you know, the part where they ask: where can we get in touch with you, no matter what? what place will be there for you, forever?\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nfinally, fundamentally, perhaps even as the entire purpose of their existence, i think: parents are supposed to be proof of love. proof that love works. my arms relax a bit as i realise this. parents are evidence that two people can be interlinked, as closely as the fingers of two hands clasped together, and not kill themselves or each other. quite the contrary, in fact. they are supposed to make it look good. sweet. worthwhile. and on sunny days, they are supposed to make you believe that there is no better way to go through life -- that indeed, it is the only way to live. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you want to know what happens when your parents\\\' marriage starts to crack? well, it makes a peep hole, a little gap, through which you can see that love, forever-love, was a lie. a big fat shiny pink wrapping paper ribboned around it lie. wrapped with so much paper that the pink reached your eyes. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nafter a talk like that, the world starts to shift a little. not the earth of course, which continues orbiting at the same pace it always did, but my world, my particular world...my room in our house, my life: the doing of dishes while my sisters shout out what is happening on the tv in the next room so i don\\\'t miss much when i join them afterward, the going together to my little brother\\\'s soccer practices every thursday night, the eating of chicken sandwiches out in the living room - not having to stay in the kitchen, after dad leaves for the mosque -- that world -- it starts to shift. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nand why does everything still look the same? because nothing is. i think i\\\'d feel better, i really think i\\\'d feel better, if the kitchen table cloth changed colour. if the chandelier suddenly pulled itself off balance and landed in a thousand glints on the drawing room floor. if things started to reflect how different everything is now. if the waste baskets, the telephone, the washing machine, could change to match our faces.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nwhenever i see the two of them together now, at some party, where they must appear as a united couple, though his face is looking to the right, hers away to the left, the image of the pomegranate, split in two, superimposes itself on the scene. what was it my grandmother used to say? to make scrambled eggs you need to break the eggs? this doesn\\\'t make me feel better. i stand against the wall next to the potted plants, so I can see them though the tracery of huge leaves. the leaf shadows hide me as i watch over my parents and start trying to think of other examples where something has to be broken for something else to come into being. seeds. there\\\'s a good one. you can\\\'t have a plant unless the seed lets itself be torn open. i remember seeing it happen when we grew those bean sprouts in damp cotton wool placed in those cheap white disposable glasses on the classroom window sill. i think that was grade five or something. and you have to break way from your elementary school world to enter junior high. that\\\'s a pretty big change. does that count as a breaking? what about that old caterpillar closing itself into a coccoon thing. the sun eventually splits the chrysalis open and a butterfly emerges. this happens everywhere in nature. so it is quite natural, really. expected, and right, almost. now if only i could give a damn about stupid old eggs and butterflies and tadpoles and life being born out of wreckage. although i can\\\'t help stopping to wonder about a tadpole\\\'s tail. is there a huge pile of tadpole tails at the bottom of the ponds? or is it the tail that transforms into frog legs? i can\\\'t remember.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m blinking as hard as i can, unclosing my hands, furious at those stupid tear glands hidden somewhere behind my eyelids, as my mother starts to walk this way, calling my other name. most children in our culture have two names: an official one and a beloved one, the former being the formal one, the one on the birth certificate, the latter, the one everyone -- grandmothers, friends, uncles, schoolmates, cousins -- use every day. my other name means pomegranates.'),(2483,'I pulled up my inbox this morning as I have every day for the better part of the last two years, and saw that an old friend had written me. The subject line was simple, the name of a mutual friend\\\'s father. The message was also simple. She had written me to let me know he committed suicide Monday morning. I felt a knot in my belly, in the caverns that sit behind my belly button, yanking chains from my feet and my hands and my head and everything in between. My jaw, if I recall correctly, fell open in an unintentional Jim Carrey fashion.\r\n\r\nI glanced up from desk, and peered out over the tops of my cube. The sunlight of the day pierced me and my astigmatic right eye twitched slightly. The blinds on the window created lines on my retina that seared for minutes even after turning away from the light. Not everyone turns away, you know. I believe there\\\'s a sick pleasure involved with trying to stare into the sun for long periods of time. A \\\"what if? mantra that drives us.\r\n\r\nMaybe that\\\'s how he felt, too. What if? What if I could actually do this to myself? I would imagine his eyes were closed, but who\\\'s to know? Seeing is believing, they say, but there are times that purposely shutting something out is the only other real assurance that something is real. Very real.\r\n\r\nI can\\\'t seem to get comfortable today. I wish the arms could be removed from this chair. My wrists strain from the awkward angle the arms create in my hands. My eye is still twitching intermittently. I don\\\'t know what I expect to see, but I find myself continuing to peer outside at the glares on the cars and the parking lot lights that seem to stay on all day. Light, I think, can drive someone mad. So can darkness, I suppose, but there\\\'s a certain security that comes with the dark, when nothing has a shadow anymore, and your body fills an otherwise undetectable space.\r\n\r\nEven in the light, I feel undetected.'),(2484,'I remember a time when I was at an airport; I believe it was Heathrow, waiting for my connection. The airport was hot and steamy it was so crowded that you just couldnâ€™t move. It carried, the smell of peopleâ€™s sweat and smelly socks. It was far from pleasant, and all I could think of was the five hour delay until I got back on the plane for another eight hours. \r\nI remember looking around and I saw this huge obese guy, with a beard floppy hat, khakis and a fanny pack. This was the true epitome of what the rest of the world perceived of North Americans. I looked at this man and I started to visualize the life he must lead. \r\nI imagined one of excess, indulgence, gluttony, and obviously because of his expensive penny loafers, I knew that he was of some wealth. To me he represented all that was wrong in the world. The whole atmosphere quickly put me in a dark mood of retrospection and self-evaluation. I then decided that I would sketch this marvel that stood in front of me. \r\nSo I slumped to the floor pulled out my sketchbook and proceeded to draw. As I did this, the most amazing thing happened. I felt a thwack into my side. I had been blind sided by a curly haired boy of about 3-4 years of age. He wrapped his little arms around me and gave me the biggest hug that he could. He peered up to me with a wide-eyed cheese-eating grin on his face. He then proceeded to laugh, and then I started to laugh as well. The mother yelled to me from behind some baggage, â€œ He likes you, if he is bothersome just tell him to shoo.â€ â€œ No he is fineâ€, I replied.\r\nSometimes a little innocence is all we need to make the world right again. \r\n'),(2485,'I remember a time when I was at an airport; I believe it was Heathrow, waiting for my connection. The airport was hot and steamy it was so crowded that you just couldnâ€™t move. It carried, the smell of peopleâ€™s sweat and smelly socks. It was far from pleasant, and all I could think of was the five hour delay until I got back on the plane for another eight hours. \r\nI remember looking around and I saw this huge obese guy, with a beard floppy hat, khakis and a fanny pack. This was the true epitome of what the rest of the world perceived of North Americans. I looked at this man and I started to visualize the life he must lead. \r\nI imagined one of excess, indulgence, gluttony, and obviously because of his expensive penny loafers, I knew that he was of some wealth. To me he represented all that was wrong in the world. The whole atmosphere quickly put me in a dark mood of retrospection and self-evaluation. I then decided that I would sketch this marvel that stood in front of me. \r\nSo I slumped to the floor pulled out my sketchbook and proceeded to draw. As I did this, the most amazing thing happened. I felt a thwack into my side. I had been blind sided by a curly haired boy of about 3-4 years of age. He wrapped his little arms around me and gave me the biggest hug that he could. He peered up to me with a wide-eyed cheese-eating grin on his face. He then proceeded to laugh, and then I started to laugh as well. The mother yelled to me from behind some baggage, â€œ He likes you, if he is bothersome just tell him to shoo.â€ â€œ No he is fineâ€, I replied.\r\nSometimes a little innocence is all we need to make the world right again. \r\n'),(2486,'I remember a time when I was at an airport; I believe it was Heathrow, waiting for my connection. The airport was hot and steamy it was so crowded that you just couldnâ€™t move. It carried, the smell of peopleâ€™s sweat and smelly socks. It was far from pleasant, and all I could think of was the five hour delay until I got back on the plane for another eight hours. <br><br>\r\nI remember looking around and I saw this huge obese guy, with a beard floppy hat, khakis and a fanny pack. This was the true epitome of what the rest of the world perceived of North Americans. I looked at this man and I started to visualize the life he must lead. \r\nI imagined one of excess, indulgence, gluttony, and obviously because of his expensive penny loafers, I knew that he was of some wealth. To me he represented all that was wrong in the world. The whole atmosphere quickly put me in a dark mood of retrospection and self-evaluation. I then decided that I would sketch this marvel that stood in front of me. <br><br>\r\nSo I slumped to the floor pulled out my sketchbook and proceeded to draw. As I did this, the most amazing thing happened. I felt a thwack into my side. I had been blind sided by a curly haired boy of about 3-4 years of age. He wrapped his little arms around me and gave me the biggest hug that he could. He peered up to me with a wide-eyed cheese-eating grin on his face. He then proceeded to laugh, and then I started to laugh as well. The mother yelled to me from behind some baggage, â€œ He likes you, if he is bothersome just tell him to shoo.â€ â€œ No he is fineâ€, I replied.\r\nSometimes a little innocence is all we need to make the world right again. \r\n'),(2487,'<P>went to the doctor yesterday to obtain some mental health-related care. told her i was experiencing sexual side effects from the drugs i\\\'m on: zyprexa and paxil. i think it\\\'s the paxil that\\\'s doing most of it. got like zero sex drive and haven\\\'t come in about 2 and a half months. so she puts me on wellbutrin in addition to my current regimen. supposed to do the trick. we\\\'ll see. there are times when i don\\\'t take my meds, ease off them and give myself a drug vacation. might have to do that.</P><P>paige runs in here and sees me using the computer. \\\"matthew, you\\\'re always lookin\\\' at them internets,\\\" she tells me. (everyone in my family calls me \\\"matthew.\\\" my friends and lovers call me \\\"matt.\\\" i call myself \\\"trixie.\\\" \\\'cause, y\\\'know, i\\\'m so tricksy.) she\\\'s staying here today due to a bout of vomiting last night. she seems to have recovered.</P><P>let me tell you about trixie. listen to what he did: first, you know he spent nearly two weeks in the hospital recently for drinking, suicidal ideation, etc. what\\\'s the first thing he does when he gets out, after he\\\'s found the car he lost in a blackout state? goes to the 7-11 and buys a beck\\\'s tallboy, sucks it down, finds a roach in the ashtray and fires it up. that\\\'s exactly what he did. he thought it was funny, too. kinda graveyard humor.</P><P>for the next couple of weeks, he drinks a couple beers here and there, nothing serious. tripped once. weed was hard to come by. the parents swear nothing came in the mail for him. he was expecting a nice little package from his sole contributor in the weedathon he conducted. it never came. probly intercepted. some copper somewhere smoked it up. so, anyway, yesterday he goes down to tampa to the university of south florida to turn in his application for grad school and also to deliver his aunt\\\'s birthday present. the parents gave him $25 to assist him in this endeavor and filled his gas tank, seeing as how he was broke.</P><P>and what\\\'s the first thing he does? gets a quart of beck\\\'s. at 9:30 in the freakin\\\' morning. then another one. and another. the beer interacts with the meds, and pretty soon he\\\'s sloshed. manages to turn the app in, but misses the appointment with his aunt. supposed to meet her at a restaurant in brandon called \\\"sweet tomatoes,\\\" but was too drunk to find it. he couldn\\\'t navigate tampa\\\'s highway system. the cell phone was ringing every five minutes. his mother called and asked if he was drinking. he vehemently denied it. the aunt had to go back to work. she would have to wait until another day to get the coffeetable book of monet prints. it was her fiftieth birthday.</P><P>later he toured tampa\\\'s housing projects and rough neighborhoods in search of the elusive weed. lost $10 in a fake transaction. (wherein the money is taken, but none of the desired commodity is obtained, the seller of said commodity having hauled ass with your money) he looked a little further. all he had left to offer was $2 and his watch. rolled up on another brother. \\\"ain\\\'t no weed here. it\\\'s crack.\\\" still drunk, he wanted to smoke something. opium, old tennis shoes, crack, anything. something. got a $10 piece for his watch and money. eased down to a parking lot in ybor city, examined the crack. it looked like a small cube of wax. stuck it into the end of a cigarrette and lit it. didn\\\'t get too much off it. it could have BEEN wax, for all he knew.</P><P>sobered up and headed home. when he got there, the parents were off serving food to carnies at the county fair, trying to save their souls. when they got home, he told them he got lost trying to find the restaurant to meet his aunt, ended up in gibsonton. they seemed to believe him. he told of the doctor increasing his meds, how he felt hopeless most of the time, so the doc put him on an additional antidepressant. he left out the part about sex. they told him how pitiful the carnies were. he told them he had dreamt the night before that he was homeless. he had done no such thing. he is a consummate liar, perhaps even a compulsive one. the homelessness dream seemed to ameliorate any suspicions they might have had about his sobriety earlier that day.</P><P>what does trixie\\\'s soul look like? he was in the library today looking at art books. one of them contained a reproduction of a painting by matisse - \\\"the joy of life.\\\" the colors were vibrant, clashing, unfettered. in the center, a ring of figures danced in a meadow. couples embraced. women and men exulted in their nudity. he thought his soul might look something like that.</P><P>sometimes trixie scares himself. then again, he finds himself funny. \\\"son, you ain\\\'t right,\\\" he\\\'ll say. is he an alcoholic in training or just a human being? somehow, i think i\\\'ll turn out OK.</P>'),(2488,'<FONT FACE=\\\"Verdana\\\" SIZE=2 COLOR=\\\"#ff0000\\\">\r\n<P>\r\nmy boss is real dumb and I don\\\'t know what to do.</B></FONT><FONT FACE=\\\"Verdana\\\" SIZE=2> I just started here, but I can already tell she turned tricks to climb up the latter. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I am a design consultant. My boss, who is supposed to look over my work to make sure it isn\\\'t horrible before I make 10 foot posters, has no idea what she\\\'s doing. There\\\'s something terribly wrong with that picture.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>She gave me an assignment a week ago. So, being the hard worker that I am, finished 3 boards in one day. One of the senior designers looked over it and approved. It was perfect. I took it to my supervisor to have her proof it, and she told me to change the entire layout. The next day I do just that. I change the WHOLE damn project in one day (all three boards). I took proofs back to her with all the changes she suggested. She looked at it, and then grabs her double wide, super sized, bright red marker and starts making ridiculous notations on my proofs (I guess she doesn\\\'t like it)..... \\\"CHANGE IT AGAIN,\\\" she said in her blonde hooker on coke voice.</P>\r\n<P>um... okay. so, you get the idea. She keeps abruptly changing her mind everyday until yesterday afternoon. Now, on Friday, the project is due \\\"or else\\\"... I send the client proofs. and guess what? she hates it. she suggested that I make some changes. (which would make all 3 boards look EXACTLY how I had first done them) so, I sent her proofs on the first boards I had created. She loved them, for the most part, but wanted 1 or 2 minor changes, as all clients do.</P>\r\n<P>My supervisor (Dummy) wasted 4 days. I\\\'m getting sick of looking at these boards. She wasted about 2500 dollars of consulting and graphics creation time on the clients side, and probably some on our side too.</P>\r\n<P>I hope she gets fired. I want her job.</P>\r\n</FONT>'),(2490,'<p>The north is actually chilly sometimes. For once I don?t have to complain about how hot it is. Oh, it?s still hot mind you, but it?s not quite as hot as it was, and a few times I\\\'ve even had to wear long pants and a long sleeve shirt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One of the coldest spots was Doi Inthanon, where you can find the \\\"highest point in Thailand\\\" We rode up there on the motorcycle and looked at the view, mostly clouds, and saw a few fancy waterfalls. We also stopped by a local wat, Do Sutep (there are over 100 wats (temples) in Chaing Mai) that sits on the side of the hill and shows a stunning view of the city. While I was there, peeking into one of the shrines, a monk beckoned me in. He sang a chant, and splashed me with water, before a nearby guard tied a string around my wrist. (Monks aren?t allowed to touch women). The monk finished the blessing and in his choppy english let me know that it was a special blessing for my happiness. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>A few days later we went to another local wat for a night time flower festival. I?m not really sure what the festival was for, but it was surrounded by an incredible number of food and flower vendors and it was swarmed with people offering flowers and incense to Buddha. There were kids doing traditional dances on stage and rings of people going around tables and laying out offerings of thousands and thousands of flowers. In the middle near the front of the temple was a circular mound covered in dozens of hundreds of flowers and candles and incense sticks.  The smoke rivaled that of a beach bonfire, I had to leave and take a bunch of antihistamines before I could breathe again. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chang Mai is full of cooking and massage classes. I think too many hippies came in the 70s. There are ways to \\\"enrich\\\" yourself all over the place, and way too many guys singing folksongs and playing guitar. I decided to enrich myself and take a course in Thai Massage. I spent 5 days learning the basics in a room full of other sweaty Americans on vacation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After the class ended we packed a small bag and headed off to the town of Mae Hong Son, about 5 or 6 hours away over a couple of mountain ranges. There we stayed in a little bungalow on stilts and visited more huge waterfalls and tourist sites like the famous \\\"fishcave\\\" a hole in the rocks through which you can see (and feed bugs to) an underwater river full of iridescent carp. We also stopped by a \\\"Long Neck Village\\\". A hill tribe where the women wear rings around their necks that eventually collapse the neck muscles down so far that they look like their necks are about twice as long as normal. Most of the Karen are refugees from Burma/Myanmar, and they support themselves by selling handicrafts to tourists. They say they prefer the life of tourist attraction over toiling in rice fields.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now that it\\\'s rainy season, we found it is better to be off the roads by about 4pm when the afternoon rains hit. And as it is you have to spend a half and hour here and there hiding under shelters at the side of the road so you wont get too soaked during the brief showers.... It was under one of these shelters in a very remote part of Thailand that I ran into a woman who\\\'d just left her San Francisco dot.com job... There is no escape...</p>\r\n'),(2489,''),(3658,'<P>Two years ago M and I put our cats to sleep. We didn\\\'t do it purposefully as we dropped them off at a shelter,thinking they had good chances for adoption. Both of us sobbing were consoled by the words of the lady behind the counter. She had given us some paperwork to fill out-and since we were broke at the time had waved the 30 dollar fee. She assured us that \\\"them good looking cats\\\" would find homes.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>We came home then, to our new apartment. Cat free-no hair on the furniture or on our coats-we had a catless holdiday. And the year that followed was the same. I still get pangs of remorse-especially when Daniel\\\'s boyfriend-who works at an animal shelter reminds me that they don\\\'t keep them for 30 days...they usually euthenize in a week depending on the adoption prospects of the animal.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>So I vowed to myself, a few years back that if I ever stumbled accross two animals in need I would take them in. I would take them in no matter how broke and how used to my clean apartment I had gotten. I would take them with their flaws just like you do people...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>A month ago, M was doing laundry and some SOB had dropped off an entire litter of kittens into the bushes near the laundromat. Three, the bravest of the bunch, I\\\'m assuming, followed M and I to the end of the parking lot, across from our apartment. Two-the bravest of the brave-dared crossing the street with us. A neighbor offered to help us...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We decided to take the cats in-just for that night and in the morning drop them off at a shelter. The friendly neighbor had provided us with a little carry on to place the kittens inside when we cared for them overnight. That night was a sleepless one. M washed one-then the other. After they were dry-we put some flea meds on their coats and all four of us quarantined ourselves to the bathroom. Killing the fleas as they dropped off of them, and piling them up in the corner. Hours we spent doing this...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Now-one month later, we are battling an ear mite infestation. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>I do this because of that promise I made a while back. Not that it makes me a better person. But it makes it better-the pangs of guilt that come and go. I guess it makes my conscience rest-but it doesn\\\'t erase my irresponsibility with my two other cats. How I would have liked to have been able to see my other cats die...of old age.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We put animals to sleep when we can no longer stand their suffering. And although I am for euthenisation-for both animals and humans...I only think it should be an option after a dying phase has occurred...a suffering period. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>My friend Michael watched his wife die of cancer-for more than half of their marriage. But he held her while she slowly left-and some of those nights when he would sit next to her at the home...holding her wrist like thighs-he considers some of the best nights of his life. Michael is still a young man in my eyes-a very brave one too.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And so I apply this to animals. I apply this to my bird, who broke its neck and so I carried it in my hands while my mother drove me to the vet...of course it died. But I\\\'d like to think that although it didnt have a shot to take away the pain as soon as could be, it could see me and hear-maybe feel that I was there. My clumsy attempt at compassion. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Is it a cop-out? I think it is. I face the fact that my mother could be very sick-or maybe it\\\'s nothing...but if she is very sick then we will fight it together. I saw her take care of her father. Change his diapers, wash him, speak to him in a respectful tone only a daughter\\\'s voice carries. I\\\'d like to think I\\\'d do the same for all-if I could. Of course, it\\\'s easier to just not to-but that\\\'s the point. </P>\r\n\r\n'),(2491,'<p>i put a great deal of effort (read: money) into christmas 2001.  vintage ornaments & wrapping paper, careful attention to detail on the gifts i bought, etc. i tend to go a little overboard.  for my friends who are out of town, i try to get them something a little fun each year to keep things interesting. nothing dirty or corny, something useful and cute at the same time.  we lose touch off and on, so i don\\\'t usually get something overly personal; we keep our gift-giving cheap and simple.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>one of my friends is pretty darn well near perfect. beautiful skin, tall and graceful, seems to know exactly what she wants in life, great job, liked by everyone, straight-a student all through school... the type of person who makes you feel special (you have great conversations together, she truly cares about you and your life) and insignificant (ugly) in their presence. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>this friend was having a christmas party.  i was very excited to contribute to the potluck theme and anticipated seeing some old high school friends. i was also excited about another year of giving a gift she would appreciate. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i made a fabulous amaretto cheesecake for this party. apricot glaze. smothered in crushed almonds. i also bought a new dress online that had an asian print on it. it was a little bit big despite being the usual medium i wear.  my husband and i struggled to pin the thing together in the back, but that wasn\\\'t quite working. so i found black belt with velcro from a pair of pants and wrapped it around my torso right where the seam between the top of the dress and bottom met and closed it in the back.  it was makeshift but it looked pretty good.  i tucked a white flower behind my ear to play up my drab hair and my husband, the cheesecake, the belt and i zipped off to the party.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the party was terrific. great food, reminisicing, games and music.  i saw a lot of people i hadn\\\'t seen in years and it was great catching up. it was great to talk with people i went to high school with who were finding success in life - i tend to hear horror stories about deaths, unwanted pregnancies, etc. from my fellow alumni all too often.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>one slight caveat to about the first hour or the party: the belt on my dress kept coming undone.  i tried to fix it without being obvious, but just as soon as i would fix it, it would fall off. then it started getting obvious. i tried just taking it off, but the girth of the dress felt like maternity wear and i didn\\\'t want any rumors to start. i got realy self-conscious (thinking things like \\\"this is the first time in years some of these people have seen me and i don\\\'t want them to remember me as being high-maintenance\\\") so i went into the bathroom, took off my dress and (somewhere in the midst of all this a safety pin was procured) pinned everything together tightly.  i put the dress on then made some adjustments. it was working out well without the worry despite the fact that my hair was a mess by now and my flower was wilting.  i joined the group and felt much less paranoid about clothing.  the evening continued without a hitch. (no, i did not have my dress tucked into my pantyhose or anythign like that. this story is not over.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the night was winding down.  i was over my clothing woes, everyone loved my cheesecake and i felt confident among my really successful peers.  i stopped worrying about all those little things that were bugging me all night and just relaxed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>some nicely-wrapped gifts were sitting on a sofa-type table. these were the gifts people had brought for the hostess. mine was all wrapped in vintage santa paper.  i was priding myself on the fact that it was the most nicely wrapped gift on the table.  </p>the hostess opened the first few gifts (\\\"i told you guys not to bring anything for me!\\\").  my gift was nothing much, but i knew she would enjoy it - it was \\\"wash away your worries soap\\\" and some lipsticks with funky names like \\\"champagne queen\\\"... really simple and useful but the name gave it the kind of quirkiness i am notorious for.  before opening my gift, the hostess opened a gift from a friend who is an arts therapist. she opened the gift and it was the most beautiful thing i have ever seen.  the girl handmade her a prayer doll with a prayer woven inside. she had been inspired by a blind patient of hers to make this.  beautiful. everyone was awestruck. the hostess loved it.  my gift was last, and i felt a little silly after that beautiful gift was received, but i knew i could throw in a little humor and move on.  just as she was opening my gift i said \\\"oh, it\\\'s nothing much, but i hope you will enjoy it.\\\"  as she was unwrapping it, i kept thinking about how goofy this seemed compared to that beautiful, personal handmade gift she had just gotten, but i just tried to relax and stop worrying so damn much.  she opened the box and inside were the soap and the lipsticks.  she saw the soap and took it out and read the name with a smile.  then she smelled the soap. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>and then she sneezed. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>not once. not twice. i stopped counting her sneezes after twice. then she said \\\"thanks\\\" while sneezing. i think she finally stopped sneezing long enough to see the lipsticks i had packaged in the box as well, but all i remember was sneezing.  and i remember her quickly putting the soap back into the box. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>ah, just as i had shrugged off all of my silly worries of the evening, the night was topped off we me giving my perfect hostess friend a gift she was allergic too. mortification. at least there was some amaretto cheesecake left over. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2493,'<!--\r\n<p>hellllooooooooo textpad.  textpad is what i use to write most of anything that i write creatively.  it is the best goddam text editor out there for windows, so much so that i may actually even register it.  but i digress.</p>\r\n-->\r\n<p>hi. i haven\\\'t written in a while.  i wrote an email to a friend a few days ago that melted into the stuff i\\\'d been wanting to write about lately.  so here i am, on a sunday night, smoking, drinking oj, wondering how to begin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m going to paste part of the email i sent to him, unedited.  i read it over just now and most everything i want to say is there:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>-----------------</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s like being in love, brian.  (i always love how the level of the\r\ncorrespondence hushes when the person who\\\'s writing to you uses your name).\r\ncreativity.  i read a book called \\\'Solitude\\\' by anthony storr.  he\\\'s a\r\npsychologist, and a damn good writer.  he spends the book chronicling various\r\nlives of various famous creative people, mostly authors from earlier days.  he\r\nuses them as examples of the reason he refutes freud\\\'s widely accepted claim\r\nthat in order for a human being to obtain satisfaction in life, s/he must have a\r\nconsumated relationship with another human being.  (i.e. a wife, husband).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>instead, storr argues that it\\\'s the passion in the relationship that\\\'s the\r\nthing, and not necessarily the object.  quite plainly, he thinks its possible\r\nfor a human to be happy completely alone as long as there is passion in their\r\ncreative relationship.  i agree.  that\\\'s not to say i don\\\'t want a wife, or a\r\nrelationship, because those things i\\\'m quite open to.. but i like that idea of\r\nrelationships being the focus.  being aware of and trying to understand\r\nrelationships is a defining human characteristic, and that\\\'s what art is to\r\nme.    the relationship i have with the things i create have all the same\r\nattributes as a relationship with a girl..  elation, depression, suspension of\r\nlogic, and most importantly, trust. i trust the things i create.  it hasn\\\'t\r\nalways been that way..  i\\\'ve had to work on it for a long time, building\r\nfaith.  faith in what you create is trusting your relationship with yourself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> where will it lead? dunno.  i wonder if i\\\'ll ever settle in to a comfortable\r\nwork a day life and give up the creativity.  probably not, because it\\\'s a\r\nnarcotic, it really is, and i\\\'m addicted, and i don\\\'t ever think i can go back.\r\nit\\\'s quite honestly the only thing that\\\'s ever been able to make me feel\r\nconsistently good about myself - true, substantial good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i treat biking and running as creative activities.. do you?  again, it takes\r\nfaith, stronger faith, actually, because you certainly don\\\'t see the results as\r\nyou\\\'re doing it.  they\\\'re imperceptably slow, sensual sculpting of your own body through sweat and drowning mind.  through intense physical activity like the way that you and i run and bike, you create things in your body and in your mind that otherwise wouldn\\\'t have come to be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>-----------------</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>i get disappointed when i find myself only making things because they bring praise from others. ultimately i trust that i create things for the joy it gives me when i\\\'m creating them, by myself.   we all quietly know that praise is not hearty enough fare for subsistence in a lifetime alone.</p>'),(2492,'\r\n<p>a current experiment of mine is yielding results of remarkable proportions thus far. i put my dog\\\'s resume online saturday night (posted on a major job database website). i activated my resume only minutes after. at the 24 hour mark, my dog\\\'s resume had been viewed 3 times. mine had not been viewed at all. it is now nearing the 48 hour mark and my dog\\\'s resume has been viewed 5 times.  one kindly soul out there has viewed mine.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so many questions come from these sorts of results: are my skills not as marketable as, say, sniffing, licking, and biting... are these people who have viewed my dog\\\'s resume looking for dogs to promote their products or services (and if so, why haven\\\'t they emailed her?)... or are there (so far 5) people out there who are desperately seeking poopers? one wonders. and i am starting to get a little self conscious. do i throw something in my resume about my cleavage in between my job in a computer lab and my years of experience with HTML to get someone to look at my resume? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>stay tuned... my dog might have an interview in her future. and i wouldn\\\'t be surprised if she did.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2494,'<p>People like this always stand RIGHT in front of me at concerts.  This time, the scene is Gorky\\\'s Zygotic Mynci playing at the Great American Music Hall on Thursday, February 28, 2002.</p>\r\n<p>Imagine if you will the high school drama class production of The Virgin Suicides. We had Trip Fontaine and Lux Lisbon dirty-dancing in front of us. No joke. Earlier we\\\'d seen them swanning through the crowd between sets, just twirling around and being hippies, and we never dreamed they\\\'d get anywhere near us. Well, ha fucking ha, there they were, with a beer bottle and some other drink, Trip in a long coat with a buckskin man-purse and wavy Donovan tresses, and Lux in some lacy white thing with long straight blonde hair down to her ass. The 60s/70s costuming was really not so bad, and while the girl was a total schnauzer the boy would under other circumstances have seemed cute, though in need of a forced cheeseburger diet pronto. But for some reason Trip had it in his head that it was super-hippie-tree-child Sixties, man, to dance like fucking W. Axl Rose, so we had his skinny elbows jutting at our faces (did I mention he was a head taller than I am?) as he made like Davy Jones singing \\\"Daydream Believer.\\\" This was bad enough, but later on he and Lux started really getting hot and bothered. Trip set his beer bottle down (where it would later keep rolling at our feet and nearly trip M) and started to open his paper-thin button-down shirt to reveal (I can only imagine, not having any desire to look) his scrawny pale chest. Then he and Lux started grinding -- recall this is to GORKY\\\'S ZYGOTIC MYNCI! a funkier, freakier, nastier band has yet to be formed!! -- and later dancing all sexy-like face to face in such a way that Lux nearly shoved our friend B into her neighbors. M kept her fist out so that Lux would occasionally punch herself in the kidney, and I actually had to push Trip away from M at one point.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s cool that people enjoy concerts. It\\\'s great when they feel like dancing\r\nand love each other almost as much as they love ironing their hair. These are all life-affirming non-terrorist-being hippie-radical things that I should appreciate. Just because I happen to prefer standing stock-still at concerts like a caryatid in a tomb doesn\\\'t mean everyone should have to. But when I can\\\'t see over your flower-power tresses and you\\\'re taking up enough space for four to six people even though there are only two of you and you are in serious danger of hitting other people with your flailing stick insect arms and you are distractingly lame to boot, can I really be held wholly responsible for my burning desire to slip a poisoned needle into the small of your back the next time you sway a little too close to me? I\\\'d like to think not.</p>'),(2495,'<p>People like this always stand RIGHT in front of me at concerts.  This time, the scene is Gorky\\\'s Zygotic Mynci playing at the Great American Music Hall on Thursday, February 28, 2002.</p>\r\n<p>Imagine if you will the high school drama class production of The Virgin Suicides. We had Trip Fontaine and Lux Lisbon dirty-dancing in front of us. No joke. Earlier we\\\'d seen them swanning through the crowd between sets, just twirling around and being hippies, and we never dreamed they\\\'d get anywhere near us. Well, ha fucking ha, there they were, with a beer bottle and some other drink, Trip in a long coat with a buckskin man-purse and wavy Donovan tresses, and Lux in some lacy white thing with long straight blonde hair down to her ass. The 60s/70s costuming was really not so bad, and while the girl was a total schnauzer the boy would under other circumstances have seemed cute, though in need of a forced cheeseburger diet pronto. But for some reason Trip had it in his head that it was super-hippie-tree-child Sixties, man, to dance like fucking W. Axl Rose, so we had his skinny elbows jutting at our faces (did I mention he was a head taller than I am?) as he made like Davy Jones singing \\\"Daydream Believer.\\\" This was bad enough, but later on he and Lux started really getting hot and bothered. Trip set his beer bottle down (where it would later keep rolling at our feet and nearly trip M) and started to open his paper-thin button-down shirt to reveal (I can only imagine, not having any desire to look) his scrawny pale chest. Then he and Lux started grinding -- recall this is to GORKY\\\'S ZYGOTIC MYNCI! a funkier, freakier, nastier band has yet to be formed!! -- and later dancing all sexy-like face to face in such a way that Lux nearly shoved our friend B into her neighbors. M kept her fist out so that Lux would occasionally punch herself in the kidney, and I actually had to push Trip away from M at one point.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s cool that people enjoy concerts. It\\\'s great when they feel like dancing\r\nand love each other almost as much as they love ironing their hair. These are all life-affirming non-terrorist-being hippie-radical things that I should appreciate. Just because I happen to prefer standing stock-still at concerts like a caryatid in a tomb doesn\\\'t mean everyone should have to. But when I can\\\'t see over your flower-power tresses and you\\\'re taking up enough space for four to six people even though there are only two of you and you are in serious danger of hitting other people with your flailing stick insect arms and you are distractingly lame to boot, can I really be held wholly responsible for my burning desire to slip a poisoned needle into the small of your back the next time you sway a little too close to me? I\\\'d like to think not.</p>'),(2496,'i always stutter whenever people ask where i am from. should i start with my parents and each of their births in the green mouth of the sundar jungles deep in asia? for that feels like a possible origin. or should i begin with my own birth, in a third world medical school room, with students watching as the instructor taught them how babies emerge into this world. or should i tell them of my trip, as a mere twenty-nine day old baby in the arms of my mother, to england, where it rained all the time and i got fever every friday evening, my parents treated as old friends at the weekend clinic. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni left the country where i was born and only stopped to visit it, once every three years, for two months in the summer, to\r\nmarvel at the strange strong despairing men, the women with their eyes leaping out of their faces and falling into my conscience, caught and held in the snags there, the toddlers at their sides with bellies swollen bigger than their heads, their ribs sticking out like tiny ladders meshed against the skin under their upper chests, black flies at the corners of their wet eyes and runny noses, flies they didn\\\'t even slap away.<br><br> \r\n\r\neven with my hands against my ears i heard the noises: the rickshaw bells and the scooter horns, the begging and the frenzied sales pitches of hawkers. i stared disbelievingly at their little stalls jumbled haphazardly at the edges of the street, pineapples and star fruit and radios and yards of bright silk hanging over the gutters overflowing with human sewage. i gaped at it all, mouth open, purse ripe for the pickpockets laughing behind my back. i even wore a traditional embroidered salwar kameez and chappals instead of my usual blue jeans and shirt, and spoke in my mother tongue, but still the locals knew me for the tourist i was. <br><br>\r\n\r\nmy mother tongue -- what does that mean? the language of my mother, my beloved ammu? of my home country? but that\\\'s what i\\\'m trying to discover. where is home? the language of the country of my birth was difficult to decipher, though i learned it, speaking with a british accent neither of my grandmothers could understand. so my vacations there were just that, holidays in a foreign country, where once upon a time, i had been born. <br><br>\r\n \r\nbut we only lived in england until i was three, a preschooler in dresses made out of my mother\\\'s old saris. so i suppose i should skip that in my quest. where am i from? arabia? home for three quarters of my life so far. the place where i was convinced i\\\'d left my heart when we moved to this iceberg of a country. until a new heart grew out of the time spent looking at a sky that turned and stayed white for almost half the year, a heart that kept growing while collecting maple leaves in autumn and discovering satisfying sweetmeats known as donuts, waiting for the first winter day cold enough to freeze one\\\'s nose every year. i didn\\\'t know you could regrow a heart. but perhaps regrow is not the right word. it is a different heart, a different love.<br><br>\r\n \r\nnow i have received my red and white and gold certificate in a ceremony eerily resembling a graduation, only it was the judge in robes, not the citizens-to-be. a red carnation was pinned to my breast, everyone\\\'s smiles so radiant it was impossible for anything bad to happen anywhere in the whole world, for this one moment. relatives and friends cheering, a hundred cameras flashing, dozens of different voices singing O Canada and actually knowing the words. <br><br>\r\n\r\nand i feel a great relief. i have survived the climax. while every day since then has felt anticlimatic, i can\\\'t possibly complain. that day in the desert where we signed the application to immigrate here and all that it hurtled forward, into now, the packing up of a decade and a half, the selling of a car and all the contents of a house, or rather home, a previous home, but you never forget your first love, isn\\\'t that what they say, and moving to a different continent unimaginably far and a sixty hour journey away, where most people\\\'s skin was a different colour, a shade which matched the ice on the ground, and rooms were more likely to have lamps than lights despite the switches answering to our fingers, and people followed around their dogs in the park and cleaned their poop up using little red shovels, and they didn\\\'t have the usual cleansing fountains next to the commodes in their bathrooms, and houses were tiny, and not always white, and it was so green everywhere, even in the middle of the city, and changing to a public school from the convent, and then to university, making friends with strangers who\\\'d never heard of islands in the middle east, all of this wide expanse shaping itself into a concentrated little ball, a snowball, landing splat against the body of a loved one, only the body of a country, not a person. <br><br>\r\n \r\nso here i am, in canada, in ottawa, a place i didn\\\'t even know existed a few years ago, and kept spelling wrong. my uncle, when he spontaneously decided to surprise us with a visit, remembered we were living in the capital, and thus confidently booked a ticket to toronto. he thought we were pulling his leg when we told him it would take at least four hours to pick him up from the airport. the outside world kept persisting in the belief that toronto was the centre of canada. ottawa was a village on the outskirts. so how can anyone blame us for not thinking different, before we made our first tentative steps to discover the country we\\\'d decided on. the country that people warned us would be deadly boring, but hey, you can always leave and go next door, to the states, so in that way it is better than australia, which is the opposite of the rest of the world anyway. <br><br>\r\n \r\ni\\\'m so glad the answer to that question has finally been whittled down to a single sentence. where am i from? i\\\'m from canada, of course. and i\\\'m proud. and this is why, every year, i\\\'m going to go down to see the fireworks on parliament hill, despite the crazy summer thunder storms we get here, in this place, where i now live. afterall, it is what drives me, what is beneath most of what i do and say, the urge, despite my dark skin, my hair hiding under a scarf, my short decidedly female body, it is my reluctant but desperate wish to connect. perhaps even, to belong. strange to think the place where i most feared i\\\'d be an outsider, is the place which welcomed me the most graciously.<br><br> \r\n\r\nafter all these years of looking in, through the glass to the warmth and light and the laughter around the fireplace, here you see my gratitude at being embraced inside the house.<br><br>'),(2498,'<p>He said neat, but the bartender brings his manhattan on the rocks. He doesnâ€™t correct the error, just stares into the whiskey for a while. Ice in the bottom of the glass melts slightly and he watches while the cubes on top slip into the liquor. Thereâ€™s a moment of silence as the jukebox takes a break to load up Willie Nelson. He checks his watch and lifts the drink to his lips in one motion. Rummages around in his coat pockets with his other hand and dumps a crumpled nearly empty pack of cigarettes onto the bar. The bartender wordlessly slides an ashtray toward him. A sign above the register reads: NO SMOKING. Thereâ€™s a dirty rubber band in the ashtray with the used up butts. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>As he lights up the woman he saw moaning out on the curb lurches through the door and climbs onto the stool to his right. Neither the bartender nor the other patrons take any notice. She sits without speaking for a few minutes, then picks up the napkin ringed by his cocktail glass and winds it around her pinky. She leans toward him. He can feel her lips brush against his shoulder as she speaks. â€œWill you order me a drink? They never pay any attention to me in here.â€  Her voice is soft, she whispers so he canâ€™t tell if itâ€™s deep or high pitched.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He makes eye contact with the bartender and orders 2 more manhattans. The bartender shrugs and brings two more on the rocks. She looks straight ahead but smiles greedily and lifts the glass. He can see her in the mirror behind the bar. Dark haired and dark eyed, wearing a black turtleneck. It has soft narrow ribbing, looks expensive, cashmere maybe. He thinks she must have been cold outside with no coat. He feels cold just sitting next to her. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Normally this is where heâ€™d ask her name, but he doesnâ€™t care, doesnâ€™t want to know anything about her. He was stood up once today already, doesnâ€™t want any more trouble. He turns the other way, and pretends to look out the opaque window. She giggles and puts her hand on his forearm. She leans too close again. â€œCall me Maria,â€ she breathes. He sighs. â€œDonâ€™t worry! Itâ€™s not my real name.â€ </p>\r\n'),(2499,'such a slutty whore<br>\r\nvagina like a canyon<br>\r\nwhat was i thinking?<br>'),(2500,'			<P>The guy in the booth is the Mexican. I don\\\'t know if this is always the same guy. There must be more than one Mexican guy working at this toll. He might just as well not be Mexican at all. Just a non-black guy with a thin mustache. I\\\'m too damn busy when I arrive at this booth to notice more than a few details. Slow down, pass the lower gears, open the helmet screen, stop, check for neutral, grab the 2 bucks that I stucked earlier in the slot of the key, say \\\"How you doing?\\\" as clearly as I can, and hand the bills. They never say a word to answer me. But tonight something goes wrong: the guy says something:</P><P>\r\n- Poolout\\\'s low!\r\n</P><P>It doesn\\\'t make any sense to me. What is low? I don\\\'t hear well with the helmet on, the wind, and the motorbike engine on.\r\n</P><P>- What?\r\n</P><P>- Poolout\\\'s low!\r\n</P><P>The guy is barking at me. Usually, I give up after making them repeat once, but I am kinda worried now.\r\n</P><P>- I\\\'m sorry, can you say it again?\r\n</P><P>- Poolout\\\'s low!!!\r\n</P><P>Nothing better, but his eyes point somewhere ahead on the right, and there I see a police car parked on the highway shoulder. Oh yes, the guy means \\\"slow\\\", not \\\"low\\\". I am, as usual, a bit ashamed.\r\n</P><P>- Ah... Thank you!\r\n</P><P>He looks at me with no expression as I leave. On the bridge, I repeat to myself a few times:\r\n</P><P>- pull out slow, pull out slow,...</P>\r\n'),(2501,'<P>It\\\'s the first time I see an Asian guy at the booth. This is a surprise, though not bigger than to see him smilling at me. And he starts talking as well: \r\n</P><P>- ...! It \\\'s nice and warm.\r\nI didn\\\'t get a word of the first part. I kinda undderstand, though: the air tonight is warm, compared to these last weeks when I was each night freezing my ass.</P><P>\r\n- Yes, I like it like this!</P><P>\r\nThe guys makes a weird face, puzzled. I don\\\'t understand what\\\'s wrong. I just say:</P><P>\r\n- Good night!</P><P>\r\nHe mumbles something as I pull out. Riding away, I think again about this first part I didn\\\'t get, and understand he was talking about my motorbike suit...\r\n</P>'),(2502,'			<P>Ugo is working a few cubicles away from mine. He is Italian. Sometimes, when I come with the bus, he drives me to the Menlo Park station. At the Dumbarton bridge toll, he is marvellous. I admire him, I mean. Whoever is in the booth, Ugo always manages to sing (he\\\'s Italian) a \\\"Hey, how you doing today?\\\" Always smilling as well. Sometimes they don\\\'t say a word. Still he will say \\\"Thanks, have a good one!\\\", cheerfully. I am wondering what the dumb ones might be thinking about Ugo. Maybe \\\"such a jerk!\\\" or something. Anyway, I like his way too much, so I in deed started to try to imitate him. The problem is that I\\\'m of course not that good. Specially, when they ignore me, my voice faints, and my \\\"havagoodones\\\" tend to die at 2 centimeters from my mouth. Nevermind, I keep on trying.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Tonight, it\\\'s the black lady in the booth. One of them, I mean, I know two or three. She is rather nice, she always answers to my oyoudoings. And tonight she is so nice that she is faster than me to say:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>- Have a good one.</P><P>\r\nI am happily surprised but my english still allows me the basic answer:</P><P>\r\n- You too!</P><P>\r\nThen something goes wrong again: she laughs:</P><P>\r\n- Ha! I don\\\'t have a bike!</P><P>\r\nI don\\\'t get it at all. Why would she need a bike to have a nice evening or something? I say, puzzled:</P><P>\r\n- But I didn\\\'t mean the same \\\"one\\\".</P><P>\r\nShe twists her head a bit, and smiles again:</P><P>\r\n- OK, then I will have a ride in my van.</P><P>\r\nI was passing the 2nd gear when I understood that what she said in the first place was \\\"have a good ride\\\", to which I gladly answered \\\"you too\\\". Such a moron I made...\r\n</P><P>'),(2503,'<P>Riding from SF to NewArk at a good 75-85 miles an hour. No only me: every car around is going that fast. After Redwood City, the usual slowdown. Normaly, it lasts one or two miles, then gets faster again. Not today. Something is weird: it looks like the cars before me have a lot of space ahead, though they don\\\'t accelerate... Then I notice the police car on the second lane. Going 65 m/h sharp. Nobody dares to pass it. Or really slowly, looking straight in front of them, pretending strongly they don\\\'t know it\\\'s there. Thinking very loud and hoping the cop can hear their lie:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>- I am going 67 m/h, like everyday, man. And when I\\\'ll be far enough from you so that you can\\\'t see me well, like just after that hill over there, I swear I won\\\'t accelerate like hell to get rid of you forever.</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI am still young in this country, I didn\\\'t dare to pass the cop. I stay confortably hidden in the herd behind him. We\\\'re the sheep, he\\\'s our shepherd. And together we nicely move now at the incredible speed of 65. Compared to minutes ago, it looks like we are stopped, or played on a slow motion VCR. I start gazing at the things along the highway. It seems it can last for hours. I have an American idea: we should not use the leftmost lane, so that some ignorant newcomer might pass us all at full speed and get caught. It would be nasty, but maybe better than this hypocritical ballet we are all dancing now, the cop included. And what other solution do we have? Damned, the cop should go away, it\\\'s clear he missed his catch here! Marsh Road; my exit comes and sets me free from the herd.\r\n</P>'),(2504,'<P>thinking about you.<BR>i\\\'m sitting on the back deck<BR>flinging my grolsch empties<BR>into the woods.<BR><BR>remember that day on the river?<BR>drinking the lowenbrau we found in the basement?<BR>i put a pop-top ring on your finger,<BR>told you i\\\'d love you forever?<BR><BR>heard our song<BR>on the casino boat yesterday.<BR><BR>went to seaside<BR>when the boat got in.<BR>switched from rob roys<BR>to rolling rocks.<BR>programmed the juke<BR>to have something to drink to.<BR>old rock \\\'n roll\\\'s<BR>a good companion sometimes.<BR>the gandalf-looking bikers<BR>and their leathery women<BR>seemed to enjoy it.<BR><BR>always on my mind<BR>toys in the attic<BR>badlands<BR>green river<BR>friend of the devil<BR>paradise by the dashboard light<BR>take it to the limit.<BR><BR>woo hoo.<BR>party on down.<BR><BR>went to hooters for something politically incorrect to eat.<BR>fell in love<BR>with a blonde waitress.<BR>i\\\'ll have to offer her a part in a short<BR>to get into those little orange undies.<BR><BR>after some shrimp and fries<BR>went to b.t.\\\'s<BR>a gay bar on the river.<BR>at 8 pm<BR>they started playing bingo<BR>(proceeds going to an aids charity).<BR>the caller was funny:<BR>\\\"B-12: shot in the ass.\\\"<BR>\\\"O-69: dinner for two.\\\"<BR>only in florida<BR>will you find a bunch of homos<BR>sitting around the bar on a tuesday night<BR>playing five bingo cards at a time.<BR><BR>last night i dreamed<BR>i was a member of a church<BR>that had an ipo.<BR>all day long<BR>people were in and out of the sanctuary<BR>watching the stock price<BR>on a big scoreboard they\\\'d rigged up<BR>above the choir loft.<BR>my shares ended up being worth $285K.<BR>it was one of those flying dreams:<BR>i was riding a vinyl-covered wrestling mat<BR>high up above the stained glass windows.<BR>at the end of the day<BR>i asked a blonde girl<BR>to marry me.<BR>put a plastic ring on her finger.<BR>we walked out of the church<BR>and down the road.<BR>cars were parked on both sides.<BR>after a little while<BR>we were joined by a little boy.<BR>we named him timmy.<BR>while dreaming that dream<BR>i was the happiest i\\\'ve ever been<BR>in my life.<BR>the only problem was that the blonde girl wasn\\\'t<BR>you.</P>'),(2505,'<p> My thoughts on art are confused, blurry, changeable, but I\\\'m still going to present them in the conext of a few personal facts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I feel like artowrk is a commodity. Of course it is. Everything is a commodity in our culture, and no matter how much my professors, artist friends, and the people I look up to want their particular creation to be new, fresh, untouchable, penultimate, this really doesn\\\'t happen.  I was going to say anymore.. but I think I\\\'ll make it a blanket statement. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> I\\\'ll admit freely, I study art, and I\\\'m a painter. It\\\'s a thought that makes me cringe in a way. We\\\'re taught by many as artists that there is some higher cause within art. The art of creation, and behind that, idea, and concept. But what makes your concept worth knowing? I don\\\'t claim to know anymore. It all became about personal opinion for me, and since I still haven\\\'t figured it out yet...</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I know that I find value in contmeporary art, and in that I suppose many would agree, and some would disagree. Bringing in a little name dropping, my vague art history knowledge, I follow with most modern school\\\'s in considering the artists role not as a social commentator but as a cog of sorts. Like the constructivists \\\"sort of\\\" thought. People like Malevich (a constructivist. They existed in Russia just before communism won over, and were very pro communism, and wound up getting ousted by the communist government that their public art was intended to support.)  thought that art could be rendered into a pure fornm of creation. He called his ultimate emotional painting \\\"The Black Square\\\".I don\\\'t think the Black Square (appropriatelt named) is the ultimate in creation of pure emotion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> It\\\'s not really as if artists are stepping above it all to say these glorious and awe inspiring things. Sometimes the message is glorified, and hailed as genius. Some art that we love is not formally good. But that\\\'s a school of thought, so is feminism, and post-modernism.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The merit in art then, could be said to rely on it\\\'s recieval by the community in which it\\\'s presented. An indication of the time and culture which produced it, or rather, which produced the individual (or group) who produced the art. That\\\'s a concept of art. I think within our framework, the western one that is, art become a concept a long time ago. A structure that we use to apply to the creative section of creation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> My own opinion of \\\'art\\\' lies pretty weakly, and quietly as a questioning of all of this. Of a business-like \\\'world\\\' that I may choose to try to squeeze into, but also as this conceptual framework of moments where I realize life, and realize many other things, and have a concept of beauty. The wrong word, perhaps. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Language, words, is my last point I think. That art is a language of signs. Bad art/good art both fall into these catagories. Perhaps what makes good art good is a factor dependant on our ability to apply readings to the signs presented to us, in whichever form they exist. I think that, anyway.</p> \r\n\r\n'),(2506,'Boku wa ongakka dentaku katate ni\r\nBoku wa ongakka dentaku katate ni\r\nTashatari\r\nHiitari\r\nSoosa shite\r\nSakyoku suru\r\nKono botan oseba ongaku kanaderu\r\nKono botan oseba ongaku kanaderu\r\n\r\n'),(2507,'								<P>	I had to call my father. It was last week, or the week before. And then I had JM on the phone and he said that this wall-maker-breaker guys didn\\\'t give any update about what money he wants to do the job. So I should have called my father for that too, to ask him what he thinks about that guy. And I didn\\\'t feel like calling him and ask that. He might have think I called for that only. So I waited. And monday morning I had this awfull wake-up in the middle of the night. The back of my throat was filled with acid. I knew what it was, since this was the job of my father before he retired. I slept bad, and was anxious the next day. Again, I couldn\\\'t call my father to ask him about that. I checked on the web, and got more scared. On a medical website I got the scientific description will symptoms, lesions, operation details, etc. They say I should stop, it\\\'s incredible: alcool, smoking, chocolate, and lower the fat. Why chocolate, the bastards! The rest is easy, anyway I was tired of drinking alcohol quite everyday for a partial result since I could not get drunk enough to enjoy it. Tuesday went better. I asked H if I could be the one driving; being of the passenger seat in the morning in a hurry reminded me of when my father was driving me to school, always late. At the bus stop this highschoolgirl was there again. She passed right in front of me and spit on the ground, I couldn\\\'t say if it was a message. After I changed my seat in the bus she \\\'rearranged\\\' her boobs. So maybe it was a message. I noticed they were too big. I started writing something about advantages and disadvantages of big boobs. As I was walking towards work, Sondre stopped with his LeBaRon and gave me a lift. He was smoking in the car and listening to the radio. This reminded me again of my father\\\'s car.</P><P>\r\n\r\nBad day at work also; I tried to contact these internet stars for the second time. Last time was last year, I didn\\\'t feel like trying again since then. They are going to ignore me one more time; that\\\'s maybe how I can be sure that they are real stars.</P><P>\r\n\r\n											Yesterday night I indulged myself with half a glass of Bordeaux. And H bought 2 kinds of Swiss chocolate and I had a bunch of it, I couldn\\\'t help. I was scared to go to sleep. I made a few nightmares. I cannot be sure if it was real but this acid swamp was here again, in the back of my throat. At the bus stop this morning, there was a rather pretty girl. I\\\'m quite sure she was talking to herself as I arrived there. Dark hair. She kept on touching her hair all the time before the bus came. 15 minutes, with a comb, then with her hand. Walking towards work, there were dead worms all over the sidewalk. Yesterday they were alive; I was looking down all the time not to step on one. Stepping on worms is quite as bad as stepping on snails, I can\\\'t stand it. Dead or alive doesn\\\'t make a difference, by the way. And then, as I was again thinking about calling my father *soon*, a dead rabbit. A car had been rolling on it, maybe last night. It was flat as a fish. The back part was rather gone, but the front was \\\'fine\\\': the front legs, the head, the big ears, just like a drawing on the ground. I wanted to take a picture, too bad.\r\nIt\\\'s getting really time to clean the swamp. And call my father.</P>'),(2508,'<p>\r\nwhen I was a kid my dad would yell. oh how he\\\'d yell. he\\\'d yell in private and he\\\'d yell in public. it didn\\\'t matter where we were. and he wouldn\\\'t care who was there. he\\\'d yell in front of the guests visiting us. as if they weren\\\'t trying to politely avert their eyes. he\\\'d yell in the department store. as if he couldn\\\'t see the tight lips of the sales clerks and other customers. he\\\'d yell in the street. as if there were no passers by turning their heads, looking behind them, long after they\\\'d passed us. he would not wait until we got home. he would not wait until we were alone. no. he would yell now. right away. while the fury was still burning. while our mistakes were still fresh and soft. he wouldn\\\'t give them time to harden. then they could crumble away and he wouldn\\\'t be able to yell.\r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\\\'t think he ever felt the shame of our public scoldings. to him it was so natural, like leaves wilting the same moment the sun burns them: the link between action and reaction should be so close, one should not be able to tell where the action ends and the reaction begins. i have many memories of him yelling. while we were at a party. while we were shopping. while we were boarding an airplane. and most of all, while he was driving. \r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nhow could he not? we just kept making him so mad. and if he got particularly angry, if the flames were too much, he\\\'d stop the car and tell us to get out. he\\\'d just pull over to the shoulder of the road and wait until we slowly unfastened our\r\nseatbelts, too young to still the trembling of our hands, too weak to stop our tears from spilling onto our childcheeks, helpless. the detachment, the silent obeying, the facade of indifference, that came later. much later. \r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nso we would get out and he would drive away, and only my mother would look back, in obvious pain, but not enough to compel her to keep us. and we would stand there, trying not to sob too loudly, so scared. we would grab handfuls of air and stuff them down our throats. it was impossible to breathe. because he had left us and we never knew if this time it was forever, if this time he would not come back. is there anything more terrifying for little kids than their parents abandoning them in a strange place, with all the other cars containing all the other families, the normal families, whizzing by, the good kids, the children that never made their fathers angry, sitting buckled up inside, looking out at them through the car windows?\r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nand then suddenly, we found out that there was indeed something more terrifying. our loud LOUD shoutingly ear hurting loud father became an outline of silence. this was a pushing away like nothing else we dreamt. \r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nnow when we are bad he just looks, just looks at us with his eyes like that. no more screaming. no more yelling. no words at all. we\\\'ve never been able to stop being bad, though we try and we try and i sit on my hands and want someone to tie me to a chair so i won\\\'t do anything to hurt him or disappoint him or make him sad but i still do because good intentions count for nothing, they are less than nothing, they are weightless, they fly out from the pores of my skin and i am bad and he looks at me. he doesn\\\'t say anything. \r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nand i would give everything, every single thing, if he would just say something. sometimes when he catches us doing something wrong i want to stop and hold out my hand and have words, his words, if i could find them, if maybe I could just travel back to my childhood and steal some and bring them back to the present in my pockets. i fantasize about holding out my palm, with his words crouching there, small and black and shivering, and watching as he picks them up and puts them back into his mouth. \r\n</P> \r\n<p>\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nbut i can\\\'t and he won\\\'t. sometimes when he comes in and finds us being bad now i want to walk quietly to the hall and reach up and take the keys to the minivan from our pretty wooden key chain holder and give them to him. i want to ask him to take us driving. i want him to yell at us until the glass panes are covered with the steam of his angry breath. i want him to shout for us to get out. I want him to let out the fires. i want to be able to look away from his silent eyes.'),(2509,'<p>It was a typical Friday afternoon: I was parading around the house in my rainbow-colored diaper; I was singing along with the \\\"Oldies\\\" on the radio; and I watched the <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2493\\\">comments</a> flow like water on Swinney.org - then I saw it!</p>   \r\n\r\n<p><b>Peter :</b><br> \r\n\\\"i thought i was really funny too, until someone told me otherwise. heh.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh <a href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/\\\">Peter</a>, poor Peter - I used you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You see, Peter, I am something more than the immoderate ass that I appear to be.  You thought that my references to <a href=\\\"http://www.ohthehumanity.com/review.php3?ID=414\\\">Godzilla and Mothra</a> were complete nonsense, didn\\\'t you?  You thought that, like a bad little boy from the neighborhood, I was simply provoking you to experiment with more and more ridiculous and dangerous forms of mischief.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, the truth of the matter is that I am involved in a clandestine international organization.  <a href=\\\"http://www.edb.utexas.edu/faculty/scheurich/proj6/pags/pm101_2.htm\\\">Post Modernism</a> is a real threat, Peter.  And we are going to stop it.  The head of my order - <a href=\\\"http://www.math.utsa.edu/sphere/salingar/Charles.html\\\">Prince Charles</a> - has spoken publicly about the dangers of Post Modernism.  We have manipulated your consciousness via our control of the media - you have already been exposed to our <a href=\\\"http://www.stomptokyo.com/movies/godzilla-vs-mothra-64.html\\\">secret teachings</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0767823966/qid%3D1015616006/ref%3Dsr%5F11%5F0%5F1/103-1535394-9259817\\\">Learn more</a>.  Contact our <a href=\\\"http://tubcat.com/\\\">friends</a> - they can help you.  Consider relocating to <a href=\\\"http://randomhuman.com/\\\">New Zealand</a>, or Australia.  In the meantime, avoid the wrath of the Post Modernists.  Conceal yourself, even as we do.  Pretend to write for your audience, exclusively.  Write things like this:</p>    \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"I was walking around the SFBA the other day, looking for breakfast food, when I found a used cocktail napkin with something interesting written on it.  I picked up the napkin, and put it in my pocket.  I was lucky that I still had room in my pocket for anything - even a napkin!  I was already carrying a mango, and a map of Texas, a rubber band, and my favorite camera, and a part for my motorcycle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Well, I ought to say that, \\\'I thought,\\\' I still had room for the napkin in my pocket.  The napkin was the straw that broke the camel\\\'s back.  My pocket exploded!  Everything went flying.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The mango shot out and hit a laid-off I.T. employee right in the nose.  He yelled something at me about how things like that never happened in the place that he came from, and how he ought not to need to put up with it here - especially when he paid so much rent every month.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The motorcycle part flew into the air and then landed on 3 cups of this gay-ass coffee drink that some dude was carrying, knocking everything down.  He muttered something about how he spent Â½ of his life going back and forth to and from the coffee shop.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(Insert something about lesbian sex or personal hygiene here.)\\\" </p>\r\n\r\n<p>You are funny, Peter.</p>'),(2510,'<p>\r\ni think it\\\'s time we added TORONTO to the official swinney locations, since hool is posting from there too.  and isn\\\'t lotus from canada? maybe it should just say canada.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni may not be french, canadian, interested in postmoderism, or long winded, but i\\\'m going to post. i hope that\\\'s ok with everyone.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni really came here to reprint this gem:\r\n</p>\r\n<pre>\r\nDate: Fri, 8 Mar 2002 18:14:00 -0800 (PST)\r\nFrom: chiamattt <chiam@saturn5.com>\r\nTo: elly@elly.org\r\nSubject: hello there. \r\n\r\n\r\nhello there.\r\n\r\n\r\ni totally forgot what i ws emailing you about.\r\n\r\n\r\ndave.\r\n</pre>'),(2511,'<p>\r\nsomehow, sierra accidentially slams her head in the car door.  fate has decided that she will not pop her brains out into my bucket seats today; for some mysterious reason, she is wearing her bicycle helmet. \r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"i hate this fucky-shit helmet!\\\" she says.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe hasn\\\'t quite got the hang of cussing down yet, but what can you expect from someone who spends all day long hanging out with grade schoolers.  she can hardly be expected to gracefully bridge the stormy waters between \\\"booger\\\" and \\\"douche bag\\\". \r\n</p><p>\r\ni\\\'ve just spent the last half hour hanging around outside her classroom waiting with the other mothers for school to let out.  i sit in the middle of the herd observing how each woman there is sizing up everyone else\\\'s thighs.  we all check each other for the damages of motherhood.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\\\'t really know any of these women, so i\\\'m surprised when i get the occasional \\\"oh!  you must be sierra\\\'s mother!\\\" and then i am treated to a third-hand recounting of the day i happily submitted myself to the 1st grade beauty parlor or the time i came to the mother\\\'s day tea dressed up like a cowgirl.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthey get it wrong, of course.  i was going more for \\\"urban desparado\\\" than \\\"cowgirl\\\".  i guess it\\\'s really hard to get the nuance this close to sacramento.\r\n</p><p>\r\na mother looks up from loading her minivan full of tiny little commuters when she hears sierra cussing her helmet.  she turns to me, wide-eyed and waiting for my reaction.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"we don\\\'t say fucky-shit helmet, sierra.  we say stupid whore-mongering helmet.\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"oh.\\\" says sierra, throwing her backpack into the car.\r\n<p>'),(2512,'<p>anything that is in threes tastes good. i just had cucumbers, rice, and avocado. it tasted good because 1) it turned the rice into a pleasant spring green 2) there were three tastes combinations possible 3) the ideal proportion on my fork was three cubes of cucumber, 3 grams of rice, and three micrometers of avocado. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni like have three things on my fork. it\'s not acceptable to have two or only one. three. 3. th. ree.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou think i\'m kidding. ask jeff. i\'m not.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntonight at thailand house, we had the holy triumvirate of thai dishes: \r\n</p><p>\r\n1. param pak - spinach with peanut sauce and fried tofu <br>\r\n2. garlic flat noodles (\"that was just grease and salt\" - tom) with veggies,<br>\r\n3. garlic eggplant with a delicious basil sauce.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni put a little of each on my plate and was in heaven. it\'s all about proportions. the perfect proportion is usually 1/3rd of each, but this varies with degree of spiciness and temperature. also, color. the goal is to end up with a heavenly blend, in weight, texture, taste, color, volume, and aerodynamics. yes, it is a science, and i am the only one gifted with a degree.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni should take pictures of the perfect bite at each meal this week and see if i can\'t form enough loose hypothesi to qualify for my own religion. please start practicing, i\'m going to need a lot of minions. \r\n</p><p>\r\n'),(2513,'<P>it is four in the morning.  i guess i am not going to austin tomorrow i am actually going today.  i cant really go to sleep at this point with the music and the drugs and voices.  if i did fall asleep i might not wake up with enough time to make the airport in san jose.  fucking san jose.  i really try to make plans and stick to them.  they sound good when i make them.  like for instance why would i get a plane ticket from oakland?  i wont be in oakland.  i\\\'ll be in santa cruz.  so san jose airport is the closer.  now look at me.  im in oakland and i have to drive to san jose in the morning.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>im flying to austin tomorrow.  there is some things i reckon i\\\'ll do while im there.  i scrawled them out on a piece of cow hide with the end of a mesquite switch.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>eat breakfast tacos.  oof lent me three hundred bucks.  whereas before i had only twenty dollars and one cent to my name, now i can have breakfast tacos every day i am there and even migas with the love on them from magnolias.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>the bats.  the bridge over town lake, that bend in the colorado that was damned up and appropriated for the long boating pleasure of the texas capitol,  it aint a lake, it is a resevoir sort of, sort of a big ass crek.  at night the bats fly out of the bridge.  not night really, dusk.  they circle out under the trees that hang over the water scooping up gnats and mosquitos.  if you snap a camera flash you see hundreds of them for a split second, a frame of exodus.  then the hairy little mamilian cousins are gone and replaced with more.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>bar be que.  im not sure where this goal will take me.  in the hills west-central austin is the established County Line.  Beef ribs are to die for.  of course, you can get all that you can possible stuff into you guts and kill you.  possible location number two: the Salt Lick.  this place has no beef ribs, only pork.  and you can only get all that you can possibly eat and plug your guts and cause you lots of pain the next day.  there is no plate or side order.  once i rode with oscar collegio and bill in oscar\\\'s signature dallas cowboys chevy blazer.  it was colored blue and silver and had the star on the sides i think.  it is hard to remember.  that truck ruled the road.  oscar drove it to yale when he started medical school.  people cheered him in the streets he said.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>donuts.  mrs. johnson\\\'s doughnuts come hot off the conveyor belt and into your hands.  you can watch them bounce in the grease track and go under the sugar fall of ooze.  THE FOOD OF GODS. one time i was chewing the fat with gdey the owner, i think he is from pakistan, and i commented that he car was destroyed outside.  yes, he said, some people vandalized it.  a huge spider crack in the donut shop\\\'s front window was noticeable from inside, but i missed it from the outside.  from the outside all you see is steel mesh.  it is ok, he said, insurance will pay for it all.  but it was not ok.  you dont vandalize a man\\\'s property who makes donuts and serves them up every day at 6 am and 9:14 pm.  burn in hell perpatrators.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>im going to get a cup of coffee and sit out front of the quacks, the only one left, on 42nd if it is still there.  i think it was 42nd, in hyde park.  the coffee is weak there, just like i enjoy it.  a notch above garage coffee.  i had a crush on this czech girl who worked there once.  for a short while.  it passed.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>there is a sidewalk that goes from wheatsville food co-op to the slant floored crack house on 31st and speedway that i used to live at.  this side walk has lots of cracks and is uneven in many places.  i want to walk that side walk.  i might stop for a marguirita at trudy\\\'s texas star and overlook the park that i used to walk my dogs in every day and watch them run chasing squirrels.  then walk past the prebyterian church made of rock with the garden court.  on the sidewalk are stamps of the people who made each stretch.  johnson.  maufrais.  they are historical markers.</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>if i were visiting in the summer time i would go to barton springs and think about getting in the water and how agonizingly cold the water is.  it springs up from the ground at like 65 degrees all year round or something.  so it is kind of nice if you are naked and the air temperature is negative 10.  then 65 degrees starts to feel good.  but usually, 65 is cold.  it is especially cold when the air temperature is 100 degrees.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>where is my plane?  im so tired.  just pick me up here in oakland.  i\\\'ll go up to the roof and jump as hard as i can when you fly over.  i\\\'ll catch a wing or a tail.  right now i need to catch some sleep.</P>\r\n'),(2514,'<p><i>My cat left for the void today. I suspect she has taken her place at Odin\\\'s side. This is what I say about her:</i></p>\r\n\r\nWe found you in a garage in 1986.<br>\r\nNo name was given â€“ you were the Gray Cat.<br>\r\nYou defended the territory around our house well.<br>\r\nYou were even known to attack dogs who came too close.<br>\r\nAn honorable cat.<br>\r\nYou killed many animals in your time.<br>\r\nAnd you were a great hunter.<br>\r\nYou once killed a rabbit in the back yard.<br>\r\nThe heads of many mice and moles were placed on the front steps.<br>\r\nHeaping honor upon our house.<br>\r\nYou would often capture birds and bring them inside.<br>\r\nSo that they would fly around in the basement.<br>\r\nI saw that you were growing old.<br>\r\nI wanted to buy you a mouse from the pet store on your birthday.<br>\r\nSo that you may enjoy one last kill.<br>\r\nBut sickness came too soon.<br>\r\nYou groomed yourself fastidiously.<br>\r\nEven when you stopped eating.<br>\r\nSuch was your sense of dignity.<br>\r\nYou seemed to know that your time had come.<br>\r\nThey say you remained calm and reserved.<br>\r\nUp until the end.<br>\r\nAn honorable cat.<br>\r\nYou were just a creature and you had no name.<br>\r\nBut I can say, with great conviction.<br>\r\nThat I am proud of you.<br>'),(2515,'<p>Walking with your eyes open affords many opportunities for stealing infrastructure.  Say, for example a major telecommunications company is laying fibre optic pipes in your neighbourhood.  This would mean that there is at least one giant spool of large plastic tubes which contain those highly pure glass fibres.</p>\r\n<p>The boring machines tearing up sidewalks, back-hoes driving around and large trucks.</p>\r\n<p>One police officer on the scene at all times.  You\\\'re not sure what for, just that the cop always seems out of place and not doing much.  Probably just making sure that the back-hoe driver doesn\\\'t accidentally kill someone while backing up or something.</p>\r\n<p>A power station and the loud hum of the transformer coils, studiously using physics to make the electric grid work.</p>\r\n<p>Did you know that if you take a standard power cord (say for a lamp) and strip the covering and peel the wires apart so that they make a T shape you have effectively created a dipole antenna?  If you plug this cord into an outlet (and don\\\'t get electrocuted) you are now broadcasting a 60Hz signal with a wavelength of apprx. 5000km.</p>\r\n<p>Guys, don\\\'t put laptops on your laps if you want children someday down the road.  Cell phones are going to cook your brain.  In fact, if cell phone signal power weren\\\'t in the range of millidecible-watts, they would boil your eyes.</p>\r\n<p>Manhole covers (sewer and telecom), satellite dishes, car antennas, street lights, the little handle on your toilet, clean water and picking up your empty trash and recyling receptacles in the morning.</p>\r\n<p>Try not to think of it too much unless you want to get paranoid about how easy it would be for someone(s) to push on it and make it all fall over.</p>\r\n'),(2516,'I find myself out listening to bluegrass again, another step towards keeping my associate membership to AA. My bones are cold tonight and I\\\'m isolated despite having come out in a group. The band cranks out the noises of Leadbelly, Phil Oaks, and the occasional Elvis tune. The worn hardwood floor catches my eyes and it is while my eyes patiently trace the wood grain patterns of each planks that I start to dream.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>The sky is a dead grey in my mind and I can see a small gathering of people through all the mist down near the water around a bonfire. Immediately, I ask myself wouldn\\\'t they be happier in some different climate?<br><br>\r\n\r\nNorthern rock beach surfers, boys and girls alike are there, having driven in some cases an hour or two in some run down Datsun pickup to make it to this rocky beach. Such an odd group, where do they come from? The genetic lineage would seem to suggest a member of a polar bear club marries a California surfer. The whole idea of these people existing seems mythical to me. Watching them guards the human spirit from the hypothermia that they themselves risk in every outing. </i><br><br>\r\n\r\nMy little daydream is interrupted by the passing of the hat for the band. Tossing a few bucks in the hat, I find myself liking the idea of small bands of people in the more northern reaches, surfing rock beaches at near 0 degrees celsuis. Sure as hell beats the current past time, consisting mainly of booze cans. I think it is the dedication that such an act would require that appeals to me so much.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLeaving to go home the lyrics <i>I shot a man in reno, just to watch him die.</i> rung out as the door slammed shut behind me and I started dreaming again.<br>        '),(2517,'<p>\r\nerror inserting comment: INSERT INTO comments VALUES ( NULL, 2510, NULL, , \\\"chiamattt\\\", \\\"error inserting comment: INSERT INTO comments VALUES ( NULL, 2510, NULL, , \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"chiamattt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"error inserting comment: INSERT INTO comments VALUES ( NULL, 2510, NULL, , \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"chiamattt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"error inserting comment: INSERT INTO comments VALUES ( NULL, 2510, NULL, , \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"chiamattt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"error inserting comment: INSERT INTO comments VALUES ( NULL, 2510, NULL, , \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\