 chin with sweet syrup adhesive. i am in heaven.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbaklava comes from greece. i come from greece. what other things come from greece?\r\n</p><p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>philosophy\r\n<li>anal sex\r\n<li>political science\r\n<li>mythology\r\n<li>feta cheese\r\n<li>the olympics\r\n<li>white plaster\r\n</ul>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthank god for the greeks.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2568_1.jpeg\">\r\n</p>'),(2569,'<p>Tonight I engaged in the sweetest procrastination.  As much fun as writing an essay comparing the techniques used in my own diary with those of W.N.P. Barbellion, AnaÃ¯s Nin and Anne Frank is, going to see Jesus Christ, Vampire Hunter was more so.  Now I am writing this.  I think I\'ve come to the educated decision that I am not going to hand in this essay tomorrow morning in class.  At best, I\'ll drop it off with the English Department secretary on Tuesday and take my -2%.  At worst, add several days onto that estimation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am currently wearing my history hoodie, comfortably typing this into my laptop (a.k.a. The Paperweight) from the corner of the L-shape couch with Plaid coming out of my headphones in preparation for tomorrow night\'s show.  Strewn about me to my left are two notebooks; many stapled-together photocopied excerpts from books, journals, articles and guides; four books: two paperback, one hard cover and one a spiral-bound photocopy of an out-of-print book; several Mini Discs; headphone case (a.k.a. The Spaceship) and one black ball-point pen.  I think there is a Palm Pilot in there somewhere along with several remote controls.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s not enough to just sit down with pen and paper and start writing anymore, you have to build a small fort to act as a base of operations to get anything productive done.  Or at least I know I do.  By the end of this evening\'s work I will have effected a chalk-outline of literary detritus around my position on this couch.  Forensic scientists could measure the distance between a piece of paper and the edge of a couch cushion and no doubt would be able to tell me what it was I ate for my meal of the day this afternoon.  Crafty people those forensic scientists.  I wonder if they could tell me why my tree is dead?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While partaking in some post-film caffeine imbibing, a torn up cup-holder featuring the words: \"Caution, the beverage you are about to enjoy is extremely hot.\" was torn up into strips and bits and gave us the sentence \"about emely not.\"  Syntax and hidden meaning and pattern recognition and all that, I guess.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s not enough to just read the words given to you anymore, you\'ve got to be able to pick it apart and bring your own meaning and creativity into the mix.  But I still don\'t think Starbucks is the center of the universe.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I need a haircut.  My hair is immense.</p>'),(2570,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2570_1.jpg\"><br><br>\r\n\r\nThere\'s a public transportation phenomenon that happens here in Manhattan sometimes; when a particular subway line starts seeing higher numbers of riders, the <a href=\"http://www.mta.info\">MTA</a> puts more trains/cars into service on that line.  I\'ve noticed that this is particularly true on the 4/5/6 trains that run up the severely congested east side, as that line is the only subway in that highly populated part of town.  This phenomenon also becomes apparent during holidays, when it seems that the trains run every 2 minutes up and down the line to help the city cope with the excessive influx of shoppers, visitors, and holiday revelers.<br><br>\r\nI live on the west side, where many train lines run; here, congestion isn\'t a particular problem, aside from the usual moments of passing rush-hour crowds and such.  Lately, though, I\'ve noticed that traffic has profoundly increased on my neighborhood line, the 1/9,2/3 trains.  Perhaps this is due to some post-WTC trend; the 9 train no longer runs, as most of its stations were destroyed ca. 9/11 (none of the trains on this line travel to lower Manhattan since, incidentally); for most of the day, the formerly-express trains run local.  All these factors seem to somehow be leading to much more crowding on the trains, as well as the fact that my area is becoming more and more popular as a residential area.<br><br>\r\nSo, all this being said, the MTA has forced some older surplus trains into service, as they do during such times.  Now, running up and down the 1/9 route on both the express and local tracks are the old 1950s-era trains, the ones with the red cars.  It seems that many of these cars have been in outdoor storage for a long time; their windows have yellowed, and they\'re rusty all over, often having crackly, patchily primed skins that show damage right through the walls from years of corrosion.  Water damage on these cars is also quite obvious, and some tend to smell- not the usual dank, musky subway smell, but the aging scent of inertia.<br><br>\r\nThis morning, my assumptions about the surplus cars was verified; I caught the local to go to work, and it was one of the old red, rusty cars with dirty, hazy windows.  Inside, the linoleum floor was buckled, torn and stained.  But what struck me as interesting were the advertisements inside the train: An <i>Earth Day \'96</i> poster, as well as a profoundly outdated version of the subway system map, not showing any of the new lines or countless permanent route changes that have occurred in the past 6 years or so.  There was also an ad for the long-forgotten movie <i>Sleepers</i>, which proclaimed \"coming in June\".  But what struck me most were all the ads from the beginning of the dotcom heyday.  I counted no less than 5 ads for different internet-related schticks, most all of which are now defunct.<br><br>\r\nIt was an interesting step back in time, in many ways.  Who\'d have thought that public transportation could serve as a kinetic time-capsule, representing a specific slice of time in our increasingly distant past?  Quite a pensively reflective way to start the day, indeed.  '),(2571,'five dead geese. she didn\\\'t count the bodies. only joanne had such presence of mind, such fingers. mike noticed that one of the corpses - \\\"don\\\'t use that word!\\\" she wanted to cry - had its head under the sand. another of the birds was upside down at the edge of the pond, its light wing feathers jutting out of the surface of the water. </P><P></P><P>( <br>the smell of burnt skin<br>a bound pair of hands detached from their body found on a rooftop<br>ashes on pavement, on windshields, on faces and the backs <br>of necks, knees, the stark landscape of the body<br>blood trickling down a black shin<br>the body parts lovers had cradled in their palms an hour or two earlier<br>or perhaps scratched, quarreled with<br>)<br></P><P></P><P>they all woke up and got dressed and went to work and got off the bus this morning and the geese were dead.</P><P></P><P>wait. stop. pause. rewind:</P><P></P><P>yesterday afternoon, on the way home, she\\\'d left work a few minutes early so she could stand in front of the quiet silvery pool and watch the geese. how many were there? ten? fourteen? twenty? all splashing about, exuberant, almost as if they were playing some joyful bird-game.</P><P></P><P>wait. she thinks now. shivers.</P><P></P><P>were they playing? or were they thrashing? it\\\'s hard to tell between desire and struggle, isn\\\'t it? maybe they were fighting something even then, and she didn\\\'t realise.</P><P></P><P>well, it must have been sudden. even in the ice of death some looked as if they were waddling </P><P></P><P>on their way somewhere and in the midst of movement, keeled over. </P><P></P><P>they woke up this morning and found the bodies. litter on the beautiful landscaped pond in front of their glassy building, next to the designs made of rocks like speckled eggs or opaque glass, beside the bright red flowers that almost glowed in the afternoons. the bodies were all in different parts of the land and water, thoughtlessly strewn here and there - no decency, no tenderness--'),(2572,'<p>Upon leaving Amboseli National Park nine days after arriving in Kenya, we were not only completing our first safari in East Africa but also leaving Kenya, at least for quite awhile.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Elly and our latest cook, Nyerere, drove us from the park to Namanga, a small town thatâ€™s a main entry point via highway into Tanzania.  Elly made sure we navigated the emmigration/immigration paperwork hassle-free (visa - $30/each), and then bid us farewell; patting us on our butts and sending us on our merry way, so to speak.  We had already paid Come to Africa Safaris Ltd $30 each for a shuttle bus south to Arusha, where we were hoping to organize the next leg of our adventure, and where we would be greeted by an affiliated safari company and escorted to our accomodation of choice...â€no charge and no strings attachedâ€.  When we had agreed to this arrangement back in Nairobi weâ€™d suspected that the cost was a bit hefty, but weâ€™d also figured it was worth the convenience.  But as the saying goes...the plot thickened.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The â€œplanâ€ was that we would be climbing on to a bus at the border at 2 pm and arriving in Arusha in late-afternoon, at worse.  Getting into a strange town after dark in Africa was not especially appealing to us.  But somehow the arrangements got botched up and there were no seats for us on the 2 pm bus.  Whether this was the fault of Come to Africa, or of Davanu Shuttle Services, we still do not know.  But I must say in all fairness to the safari company that IF they were the ones responsible, it was absolutely the only gripe we had with them...everything else was virtually flawless.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So we waited.  Now all of you who have spent any time in a border town know fully well just how unenchanting these armpits of the world are.  Little to do but sit on a bench, read and people-watch, fending off the beggars and peddlars, we became increasingly irritated as the hours slowly passed.  If we had made our own arrangements we would been infinitely more patient, but we had paid top dollar for convenience, and worse yet, when we wanted to know what the hell was happening our contact from Davanu, a lady named Zaituni, was absolutely no-where to be found!  â€œWe could have screwed this up on our own for a lot less moneyâ€, we agreed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Finally...just before 6 pm (having never seen Zaituni again) we left on a bus and sped off to Arusha, contemplating the fact that for the first time in East Africa we were really on our own.  Up to this point someone had been holding our hands every step of the way.  The ride was magnificent.  First of all, the quality of roads in Tanzania is light-years ahead of Kenyaâ€™s.  It is certainly a country also saddled with extreme poverty, but the infrastructure appears to be in better shape.  Our view from the bus window was superb: a stunning wide-screen landscape with lush green, rolling grassland; tiny Maasai bomas and splashes of crimson as the guardians of their cattle drove their herds before them; a few zebras crossing the highway and a giraffe browsing from a tree.  And later I sat transfixed as Mt. Kilimanjaro appeared on our left, Mt. Meru (the second highest peak in Tanzania) came into splendid view straight ahead, and on our right - a brilliant blood-red sunset.  All was well again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We arrived in Arusha at around 8 pm and looked for our contact...Benjamin.  We soon saw a man holding up a big piece of cardboard with our names scrawled on it.  But then another man also held up a sign bearing our names, and people were tapping on the windows trying to get our attention.  Upon stepping off of the bus we were immediately surrounded and several men tried to grab our backpacks and stuff them into their vehicles, each insisting we go with them.  Since we had been given a contact name I yelled out, â€œWhoâ€™s Benjamin?â€.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>At least five men answered, â€œMe...Iâ€™m Benjamin!â€.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Back and forth it went for awhile, from one guy to another, each trying harder than the other to convince us that they were our Benjamin.  We were standing in the middle of a verbal pinball machine...and utter chaos!  It might have all been quite hilarious had it not been for the fact that we were tired, hungry, needing both a shower and a toilet, and feeling just a wee bit cranky.  It wasnâ€™t until the real Benjamin pulled out his photo ID card that we finally discovered who was actually Benjamin and who wasnâ€™t.  How they all got our names remained a mystery, but we were at long last headed to the guest house of AAH Tours and Safaris Ltd. - accomodation being provided to us free-of-charge with hope that we would subsequently hire them for a safari.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As Iâ€™ve  previously mentioned, safaris touring in East Africa is highly competitive.  In Arusha, a city of 350, 000 and an ideal base for most key destinations in Tanzania, there are well over 100 safari and touring companies.  Most are highly reputable operations, but some have sold their soul to the devil a long, long time ago and run absolute sham operations...these scoundrels would probably steal a penny from a blind leper if they could!  How to tell the difference?  Well first there is the Tanzania Tourist Board on Boma Rd which provides a â€œblack listâ€ of rogue and unlicensed agents, and you might stop in as we did to check up on who youâ€™re considering.  Of course the best option is to go with a company thatâ€™s been recommended by a reliable source, if thatâ€™s possible.  In any event look over the contract carefully being clear about whatâ€™s included, take a look at their vehicles, and if you can, meet the driver/guide before forking over your money.  Youâ€™ll have no trouble getting in contact with companies in Arusha, as touts from both the good and the bad ones are everywhere, prowling the streets, badgering you to visit their offices.  One even marched up to our table in a cafe while Heide and I were trying to relax over our coffees.  For me that was the proverbial â€œlast strawâ€...I jumped up and chased him out!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Even though we had already far exceeded our pre-trip expectations regarding safaris, Heide and I were very keen on not leaving Tanzania without visiting the renowned Ngorongoro Crater, famous for its uniqueness and beauty.  The next morning we spoke with Akhtar Hussein, director of AAH, about putting together a short trip without bankrupting us.  We settled on a two-day tour to both the Crater and nearby Lake Manyara National Park, which Iâ€˜d also heard good things about in my pre-trip research.  We agreed on $170 each and would leave the next day.  I should point out that Tanzania is more expensive for safaris than Kenya - why I am still not quite sure, but if you decide to go on safari to either country it is generally cheaper to make arrangements once there, rather than prior to arriving.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Located 130 km west of Arusha, Lake Manyara National Park is also in the Rift Valley.  It is not a large park and much of it consists of the lake itself.  Though not as spectacular and chocked full of such a vast variety of game as Masai Mara, we found it to be possibly the prettiest of all the parks or reserves we visited, as it is heavily forested and criss-crossed with small rivers...almost jungle-like in a way.  It is particularly well-known for its â€œtree-climbing lionsâ€.  Apparantly because of its density of vegetation the lions there sometimes climb up on to the lower limbs of the acacia trees for a better vantage point of their prey.  But we were pre-warned on the way there by our driver about the chance of seeing the lions in the trees, â€œThe odds are not good.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But none-the-less, less than an hour after entering the park we spotted three lions lounging in a massive acacia...father, mother and their two-year-old cub...as cozy-looking as any family unit could ever be.  We parked no more than about 4 meters away, so close I could actually count the flys crawling on the maleâ€™s paw.  They stretched, yawned, rubbed against the tree, and watched us watch them.  Sometimes it almost seemed as though they were posing for us.  Eventually several other vehicles pulled up and plenty of rubber-neckers were all bunched together observing them, but the lions seemed totally uneffected by all the attention.  After about 30 minutes of camera-clicking and awe-struck murmurring, we left them there...still yawning, stretching, and acting fairly bored with it all.  The â€œodds arenâ€™t goodâ€ huh?  Well so much for the odds!  But then again, as far as viewing game was concerned, weâ€™d been beating the odds the entire trip.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>By the way...figure on a minimum of two rolls of film per day on safari.  Absolute minimum.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lions have long been deemed the â€œKing of the Jungleâ€.  Now with all due respect to â€œpanthera leoâ€ (after all Iâ€™m a leo myself), my vote goes to the elephant as the bona fide king.  There is really no animal in Africa that fully rules their domain like â€œlaxodanta africanaâ€.  By far the largest land mammal on earth, the elephant clearly â€œtakes no shit from nothinâ€™ or nobody!â€.  Other herbivores will all mix with each other and share the savannah, amicably eating, sleeping, drinking, and so on all together.  But not the elephant.  You might say they have a bit of a â€œpersonal space issueâ€.  All other wildlife cut them a wide swath and stay off their turf, and â€œhomo sapiensâ€ are wise to do the same.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>All the safari drivers are well-schooled on just how close one can safely get to them and when to back off.  When threatened, elephants can quickly transform their massive bulk into a powerhouse of frightening aggression.  Fortunately there are tell-tale signals you can watch for - lifting their trunk, flapping their ears, shaking their head.  These all have significant meaning and will tip off the learned intruder on whatâ€™s probably going through the elephantâ€™s mind.  And usually they will false-charge you once, if not twice, before actually reducing you to mincemeat.  Usually.  Besides the obvious point that you should avoid a mother and her offspring (duh!), one thing elephants clearly do not go for is having their path blocked.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In Lake Manyara Park there are plenty of elephants, and due to the density of flora they can suddenly appear upon rounding a curve.  On one such occasion we found ourselves squarely in the middle of a herd of about a dozen crossing the road.  We stopped to watch and photograph them.  One young male which had already crossed in front of our vehicle was â€œfeeling his oatsâ€ a little that day, and he displayed his displeasure at our close proximity by spinning around, waving his trunk, and letting out a few shrill trumpets.  All good fun...no problem.  But while he had our full attention, another adolescent male about two meters behind us decided to play â€œI can top that!â€ and let roar a massive scream.  More than just a wee bit startled we turned our heads to look at him just as he false-charged the van, absolutely scaring the crap out of Heide and me!  I can still recall the blur of gray rushing directly at us.  Nothing like a little adrenalin rush for an afternoon pick-me-up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Our driver on this safari was Baba - not his birth name.  He did inform of us of that when we were formally introduced, but everyone called him Baba, a title of respect implying both father and teacher.  He had been a safari driver for 13 years and apparantly had trained, at one time or another, almost every driver in East Africa...at least it seemed that way.  Everywhere, and I mean everywhere we went, he was enthusiastically and reverentially greeted by name.  And not just by the drivers...in the villages...along the highway...at the park entrances...calls of â€œBaba, Babaâ€ constantly rang out.  You would have thought that we were traveling in an entourage with Nelson Mandela!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Baba is 45-years-old, quite short but tough and wiry, and always has a bright, infectious smile.  He also knows the land and all its creatures intimately...it was not just by good luck that we found the tree-climbing lions!  I asked him the same question I asked each of our guides, â€œWhatâ€™s the scariest situation youâ€™ve ever been in?â€.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>His quick response was, â€œI donâ€™t get scared anymore.â€  Now normally if anyone said this to me I would be very, very sceptical.  But I believed him, especially after he shared one particular story with us.  Knowing that many companies are expanding into Uganda to do mountain gorilla expeditions, I asked him if he had been there yet.  â€œYes in 1979...in the war.â€  At that time Idi Amin, the demented and despotic ruler of Uganda, arrogantly and unwisely decided to grab some border land from Tanzania (a mistake that would eventually lead to his downfall), and Baba was part of a militia unit sent there to kick his ass and get the land back.  â€œMy friends were getting killed all around me, but the Ugandans were too disorganized to beat us and we chased them away.  They dropped their weapons and just ran.â€, he laughed, as if survival was a casual matter.  I noticed that he didnâ€™t flinch a bit when the elephants got pissed off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That night we camped near Ngorongoro Crater.  Although weâ€™d experienced no rain at all so far in our trip, that night there was an absolute deluge, some water got into our tent, and in the morning the road to Ngorongoro was a complete quagmire.  4WD vehicles are mandatory for entering the crater and itâ€™s a damn good thing we had one.  Spinning, sliding, but always in control, Baba calmly drove onwards.  Descending one fairly steep hill we encountered a truck stuck directly in the road.  Never hesitating, Baba swung our truck up on to the muddy shoulder and whipped around the stricken vehicle.  But while doing so we slightly clipped the rear end of another truck backing up.  â€œNo harm, no foulâ€ apparantly, as we sped on, slalomming all the way to the bottom of the hill.  Never letting off the gas he turned and with a grin as wide as an elephantâ€™s ass and his characteristic high-pitched laugh he exclaimed, â€œI love it!  Oh, but this is easy...you should see how it gets sometimes!â€.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ngorongoro Conservation Area was established in 1959 and in 1978 was declared a World Heritage Site.  About 2.5 million years ago it was an active volcano, but when the cone collapsed it left a crater between 16 to 19 km across.  The rim is 2,286 meters high with the floor 610 meters below it.  Ngorongoro has been called  the â€œGarden of Edenâ€, â€œNoahâ€™s Arkâ€ and â€œParadise on Earthâ€, and with an estimated 30,000 animals residing within it, along with the Maasai grazing their cattle, it is truly something special.  The panorama of the crater from the rim is incredible, or so Iâ€™ve heard.  I have to say that since when we arrived the clouds obscured our view completely.  But as we started working our way down the narrow, winding, switch-back dirt road the spectacle unfolded below us, rather like a huge amphitheature.  Breathtaking is the only word I can come up with to describe it!</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>We leisurely explored the crater floor by truck, every vantage point dominated by a view of steep crater walls in the distance looming upwards...a lush dark-green that cradled us.  We did not see that many predators there, but the antelopes, gazelles, wildebeest, zebras, buffalos and other grass-eaters were plentiful, sprawled across the verdant plain.  We also saw a couple of black rhino, a few elephants, and visited two pools filled with basking hippos.  Lake Magadi has a few thousand pink flamingos wading in its shallow waters and there are many other kinds of water fowl feeding and flying around, including ducks, geese, herons, ibis and African jacanas.  But Ngorongoro impressed us not so much because of the wildlife, but moreso because of its hypnotically immense grandeur.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>It was during our lunch break at a picnic site in Lerai Forest inside the crater that I took a fantastic upclose photo of a Green (Velvet) Monkey eating a sandwich.  My sandwich!  As Heide was sitting in the Land Cruiser feeding her face, I grabbed her camera to capture this moment for posterity.  While I squeezed the shutter a monkey seemingly came out of no-where, hopped up on to the hood of the truck and swiped my sandwich out of my lunchbox laying there.  When he dropped to the ground I captured him in posterity...looking quite smug while chewing away.  Frankly I would have preferred my sandwich to the photo, but as the saying goes, â€œSnooze, you lose.â€.  Iâ€™m telling you these guys are fast...and bold!</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>A few minutes later another monkey climbed into a vehicle parked nearby as a woman ate her lunch inside it...imagine her surprise!  And I am embarrased to admit that that I  got caught snoozing not just once, but twice!  In an obviously devious, carefully crafted diversionary move, a baby monkey strolled up, stood erect and enticingly posed for me.  Standing directly next to my lunchbox I jerked up the camera...it was clearly a â€œKodak momentâ€ I just could not pass up.  Do you know that while I was photographing the infant monkey, the other one jumped back up and swiped my muffin from directly under my elbow!  I still had my apple and cookie, which from then on I tenaciously guarded, but that day the monkey ate a lot better than I did.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some people like to organize their own safari.  You can certainly do that; rent your own 4WD, bring your own camping gear, buy some maps and guide books and do your own thing.  And many do.  It certainly creates for you the freedom to go where you want to go when you want to and to stay as long as you want to.  But you definitely wonâ€™t save any money.  Even with the much higher cost of the two-day safari in Tanzania factored in, we paid any average of $64 per day, per person, for our eleven days (six parks in the two countries).  And when you figure in the entrance fees (average $25 to 30/day), the rental cost (probably about $50/day), petrol, food and all the rest, it makes no sense to do it on your own...especially when you add in the invaluable experience and companionship of the drivers, plus all the things that were done for us.  And what if you get stuck in the mud or sand, or get a flat tire 40 meters from a pride of lions?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Any decent guide book will provide you with an adequate list of what to bring to Africa, but Iâ€™d like to add a few items that might not be listed, and which I believe are invaluable:<br>\r\n\r\n<ul><li>bandannas â€“ many, many uses...bring a few</li>\r\n\r\n<li>lip gloss or chap stick â€“ easy to overlook, but important</li>\r\n\r\n<li>lots of plastic bags â€“ for your camera (dust is a problem) and any food youâ€™ll buy</li>\r\n\r\n<li>duct tape â€“ you can repair almost anything with it</li>\r\n\r\n<li>ear plugs â€“ trust me on this one</li>\r\n \r\n<li>collapsible rubber wash basin â€“ for washing clothes and flushing naughty toilets</li>\r\n \r\n<li>slip sheet â€“ made by folding a sheet and sewing up the bottom and part of the side</li>\r\n \r\n<li>aloe vera â€“ for sun burn, and for inside your nose to prevent dryness and nose-bleeds</li>\r\n \r\n<li>sarongs â€“ practical for skirt, towel, scarf and beach-cover for women.</li></ul></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Imagine pulling up in your car up to the entrance of Yellowstone or Yosemite Park in the U.S. and seeing a sign announcing â€œAdmittance: 25 Eurosâ€.  Wouldnâ€™t it seem a bit odd to you that foreign currency was preferred over local currency?  Yet in all the parks and reserves in Kenya and Tanzania entrance fees could only be paid in U.S. dollars.  These governments donâ€™t even want to accept their own schillings.  What kind of statement is this that theyâ€™re making about confidence in the stability of their currency?  At Lake Manyara Park, Baba used one of our travelers checks (TC) in U.S. dollars to gain our admittance, and Heide had to personally endorse it in front of the ranger at the gate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Entrance visas to both Kenya and Tanzania were requested in dollars.  And many, many hotels, especially in Tanzania, wanted payment in U.S. dollars to such an extent that we would get penalized with horrific exchange rates if we used schillings.  But hereâ€™s another kick in the wallet.  When we tried to pay with a TC they either wouldnâ€™t accept it or else would devalue it by 10 to 15%.  One bank in Arusha wanted a commission of $20 to change a TC into Tanzanian schillings.  So let me get this straight.  They want U.S. money, but they discourage visitors from using TCs, right?   Now you tell me...who in their right mind would go prancing around Africa with their pockets stuffed full of cash!?!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Regarding the issue of cost - in general itâ€™s anything but cheap in East Africa, especially in Kenya.  When shopping in stores, the same ones locals use, we noticed that many, if not most of the items on the shelves, could actually be purchased for less in Berlin. When you consider that in Kenya the average wage earner makes less than $75/month, it is hard to imagine how most people can afford anything!  Iâ€™ve found it much less expensive to travel in most of Eastern Europe than in East Africa, and if you want to upgrade your accomodation beyond the kind of places Iâ€™ve described, youâ€™re probably looking at paying as much as you would in Western Europe or the U.S....at least.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Our time on safari was finally over.  In addition to hundreds of photos, weâ€™d collected countless and priceless mental images so vivid, so dramatic and so remarkable that the imprint of these would be, if not indelible, certainly very long-lasting.  These images were of wildlife, landscapes, people...all of them unique to anything weâ€™d ever experienced before.  But Africa is like that - incomparable, intense and also thoroughly uncompromising!  There are such extremes and so many enigmas everywhere, it all both fascinating and maddening!  The payoffs are huge, but yet so are the demands of traveling there.  There was still much, much more for us to experience in East Africa, and some of it would be even more challenging....as you will see when we continue on with our journey.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Kwaheri</p>'),(2573,'<p> Before today, I\\\'d only ever been to one funeral. It was for my great-grandmother and rather than being something sad or tragic, the atmosphere struck me then as being accepting. Death, for a woman her age, was inevitable and seeing how she suffered through her last years with alzheimer\\\'s, was actually a sort of release. </p> \r\n\r\n<p> It seemed to me that death would always be like this, a quiet irrestible force, an accepted certainty. Other stories of death were always just that- stories. Whether it was death in a Shakespeare play or death played out on the daily news, it was fictional, make believe. I\\\'d experienced the reality of death once and that was how it was. </p> \r\n\r\n<p> Tangible moments escape me, strike me as no more than contrived happenings in the tapestry of existence. They slide past my field of vision and are given as equal significance as lines of poetry and lyrics from a pop song. Reality and fiction commingle and confuse. I am invulnerable. </p> \r\n\r\n<p> I feel numb. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> Something changed today. Today a friend cremated two of her stillborn children. They were over two months premature. I didn\\\'t want to go. I didn\\\'t have to go. I went. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> My friend was amazing and strong, made light conversation and smiled. She said she couldn\\\'t cry, and didn\\\'t, even when she took me to see the two tiny, shrunken bodies; even when the reverend described what it would have been like to hold those children and cradle them in her hands; even when her father and boyfriend carried the small caskets out of the chapel. </p> \r\n\r\n<p> She said she felt numb. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> I am sure now that I don\\\'t understand death. Words fail. Words are meaningless. Sobs and heaving shoulders and choked back tears are all that can describe it. It seems as though I\\\'ve suddenly become vulnerable. </p>'),(2574,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2574_1.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"antidisestablismentariansim\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\nThese are my hands.  they make me much of what i am.  your eyes are the first thing to sense, because light is immediate, but your hands make things real.  i don\\\'t know - maybe yours don\\\'t.  but mine do.  i\\\'d seen pretty girls long before i ever touched one, but the first time i did touch my first girlfriend - it was only then that i was able to understand why i was so dedicated to looking.  soft.<br><br>\r\nI hit with these hands, and i\\\'ve used them to prevent being hit.<br><br>\r\nThese hands have dexterity, because i love tactile things.  backs, keyboard, wire, camera, pencil.<br><br>\r\nThey used to be rough when i lived in a rural area in southern ontario.  rocks, trees, axe handles. They\\\'re softer now, until i start bending wire things with them.  they get cut up, feel raw, and i like it.  but mostly, they flutter across my keyboard.  i try to do everything without the mouse, windows, unix, or otherwise. i type quickly, and concentrate on them when i write, usually with my eyes closed.  i can tell from my hands, while i\\\'m typing, if i make a mistake.  the rhythm gets interrupted.  i can usually correct the mistake without opening my eyes.<br><br>\r\nThey are the only part of my body that knows every other part by touch.<br><br>\r\nI do most of my important things with my hands.  i feed myself with my hands.<br><br>\r\nI like to touch things that i can feel in and past my wrists, reaching halfway through my forearms.'),(2575,'<center><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2575_1.jpg\"><br>\r\n<b>I love fatty tuna.</b><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2575_2.gif\"></center><br>  \r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nThis wonderfully aromatic and tasty treat (which some of you might know as <i>toro</i>, of course, as we\'re cool with culturally diverse perspectives here), is considered a delicacy not only by the Japanese but also by sushi lovers around the world. So anyways, Fatty tuna is a major choice cut of the fish, that part around its tubby little belly, you know? As the name surely implies, it is one of the fattiest portions of the fish, producing a very jucy, melting effect in your mouth when eaten raw. Wow. It sometimes even leaves me speechless. But, I digress. Of course, besides being a tasty meal, fatty tuna has plenty of get-up-and-go health benefits:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Fatty tuna is dolphin safe!\r\n<li>Unlike many other nasty animal fats, fish fats are considered mondo healthy. In fact, fatty tuna is packed with omega-3 fatty acids, which are essential for brain and eye development!\r\n<li>Fatty tuna makes you smart (it\'s worked wonders for me)!<br><center><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2575_3.gif\"></center>\r\n<li>Major scientific studies have shown that eating fatty tuna may ease arthritis pain!\r\n<li>noshing on fatty tuna can help promote weight loss!\r\n<li>Copious fatty tuna consumption can lower the risk of cancer!\r\n</ul>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOther studies have indicated that people who ate fish less than once a week risked a higher chance of depression, so there you have it.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<center><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2575_4.gif\"><i>Eat more fatty tuna!</i><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2575_5.gif\"></center>\r\n'),(2576,'After all the terror unwound to digestible tolerances and the eyes of Manhattan slowly began blinking back vague signs of its charmed life, returning, I was having dinner with a friend who had experienced September\'s events at a closer proximity than anyone should have.  With tedious stoppages and awkward nonsequiturs parsing his tale, he pieced together an abstraction of his placement one morning in September, and how the ground shook and fell away, sprinting and sprinting before the sharp pains rained down on his shoulder.<br><br>\r\nHe explained that his overbearing sense of sentimentality is what gave him the clarity of mind, amidst the whirling protoplasm of humanity, concrete and glass, to stoop for a fragmented sliver of a second and retrieve the grapefruit-esque chunk of granite facade that slipped down from one of two very tall tower-tops onto his still-fractured clavicle.<br><br>\r\nPausing in mental inventory of his life in the intervening several months, he concluded that his possession of this fragment was ill-advised, carrying a weight so much heavier than its physical pound or two.  Palming his face, raking sweat from stubble, he sighed in dilemma; <i>i can\'t throw it away, and i sure as hell can\'t carry it around with me every day</i>...<br><br>\r\nTwo days later, it was pouring rain. Seemingly, spring\'s showers had finally found their way to Manhattan\'s shores despite the battered piers, windswept warehouses and unyielding ribbons of gray motorway that lick the rivers\' edges.  I dove into my foyer, umbrella-less in the gray torrent, picking soggy bills and banal missives from my gunmetal gray post box.  And there, in the bottom of the box, was the stone.  And there, crudely banded around the incongruously smooth granite chunk was the note; <i>carry a burden for a friend?</i><br><br>\r\nNo one but God trifles with the fundamental laws of nature; no source of power is adequate, no fleet of shiny flaming planes, no diabolical stabs of hate, no monsoon of shattered half-mirrored glass raining down on Wall Street can encroach on God\'s fated kinematics. No human force can bear the weight implied by a single, architecturally smooth fragment except that of friendship.<br><br>\r\nLast night, I held the fragment aloft on my right palm, not in prayer or offering, not in fastidious display, but alone in my basement. I observed the halogen-flare of the ceiling\'s lights around its smooth surface, and the matte refractions that slipped from the single jaggedy side where it was loosed from some oblique parapet. I probed its floor-cast shadow with my foot; made assumptions of its mass and momentum, envisioned it falling, falling.<br><br>\r\nThe arena of this stone is large; its compatriot parts now circling the globe, orbiting the earth\'s surface, my city\'s surface; everywhere. Through the atmosphere in postal mail they go, barges, dump-trucks, shipping containers, cargo holds bound for third-world scrap refineries; recycling plants, Pennsylvania forges, New Jersey scrap yards, Staten Island landfills; being dumped into the Huson river, the East river, the NY Harbor, the Long Island Sound; rubble, sitting in still-vacated apartments and Battery Park condos, still as vacantly embedded in flesh, still dusting the streets of lower Manhattan, still seeping into the subway stations with the human remains every time it rains.<br><br>\r\nNature is inevitable and relentless, unyielding to millions of metric tons of continually manifested debris; conservation of matter is perpetually at work in this system, this city, this planet.'),(2577,'<p>\r\ns.h. called at 7.35 pm last night. i found it amusing. we were wondering if he\\\'d call early, or be exactly on the dot or what. my dad had asked him to call at 7.30. i was doing the dishes, but my mom put the cordless phone on the tiny triangle shaped bit of counter by the sink. i went to the bathroom, washed my hands, and picked up the phone.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nit is funny. he really is a nice guy, a sincere guy. earnest even. and he was content to just talk casually, about eid and food and my mom\\\'s health. we talked about our cooking and eating habits for almost fifteen minutes, and i sensed that he could go on in this vein until our time was up (i had to go pick up my sister from campus after her tutorial at 8.30) and i felt a little bad for sort of interrupting the conversation with a \\\'let\\\'s get down to business\\\'. so, now i know the story behind him having an ex-wife, and a son, and why he didn\\\'t tell us *before* we proceeded with the marriage negotiations. and well, as far as i can tell, he is honourable, and made mistakes. and really, his past \\\'mistakes\\\' are almost equivalent to mine. though i don\\\'t regret any of my old relationships, and of course, i was more careful--more wary of the future and of consequences.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\ni had a good talk with my mom last night, after the call, and i dunno, i was feeling okay. then this morning, on the commute to work, my dad managed to make me feel like shit. it is very obvious that he goes out of his way to be kind, as he criticises me, but it still hurts me so much. and i just want to withdraw so immediately and so badly, that i almost considered just opening the car door, mid-traffic, and escaping. but of course i didn\\\'t.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\ni think i will get married to s.h., and the two ceremonies might even be on the original dates we agreed on back in january. and it is something good, and at various times i feel happy about it, or indifferent to it, or even a little scared. sometimes sad.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\n2002 is the year of the horse. which is my year. i told yi this, and she was surprised, she\\\'d forgotten i was born in 1978. and then the first thing she said was \\\'oh my goodness, you\\\'re not supposed to make big changes in your year. it is bad luck. especially something like getting married or changing a job. most people would delay those to another year\\\'\r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nbut i do not believe in this chinese cycle of years. i don\\\'t believe in horoscopes or astrology. i don\\\'t even believe, any more, in wishing on stray eyelashes or on the first star i glimpse at night. \r\n</p><p>\r\n\r\nand it looks to be a beautiful day. the sun was trailing glitter in the air this morning. i followed the tracks of wild animals in the dunes of snow near kanata. i have many reasons to be content. it doesn\\\'t matter that i am not feeling it right now. maybe the contentment will set in later - maybe i just have to give it time.\r\n</p>'),(2578,'<P>i\\\'m beginning to think the damn cicadas are living in my ears and my brother\\\'s gone and the little part of night i can see through my window is jagged with rain. i can\\\'t tell for sure with the broken glass and the missing bulb. i think about going after him, but i\\\'ve already got a blister the size and shape of a beetle on my left heel and no shoes that fit and the druggies are already out in the alley behind our apartment.<br><br>i\\\'ve hidden a bread knife under my pillow, and make a half-hearted attempt to push our purple velvet sofa against the back of the front door. one of the spiral metal bits from a spring sticking out of the yellow foam slices into my wrist and i let the cut drip for a bit, bored out of my skull.<br><br>the rain sounds like old newspapers swishing in the wind. right now he\\\'s probably vomitting in a corner. i pull my knees up to my chest, and rock back and forth next to the stains on the mattress. mango comes and curls under the chair in front of the night window and gives a little cat yawn. she\\\'s a stray that almost clawed herself out of the plastic bag he brought her home in.<br><br>i\\\'m too tired to move but i can\\\'t stop thinking of trying to find him. he deserves to be puking his guts out in front of strangers, i wish i had a cigarette, i wish there was more than half a lemon and a sour carton of chocolate milk in our fridge. there\\\'s some leftover fuzzy pizza in the trash, but i\\\'m not that hungry. i\\\'m not thristy either. i just want something to do.<br><br>there\\\'s shouting and the sound of something big, a coffee table maybe, being thrown against the wall. i can make out a few swear words, and then i hear the girl screaming. she\\\'s nice, she smiles at me when we meet in the laundry room, once she even lent me a quarter for the dryer, and she says hi when we cross paths on our way to garbage chute, but, but.<br><br>so her boyfriend\\\'s beating her up, and i\\\'m tired. i shrug my shoulders even though there\\\'s no-one in the room except mango who doesn\\\'t give a shit. she\\\'s pretty complacent, making a nest on one of his old sweaters. i almost wish she\\\'d pee on it. the t.v. is busted, or else i\\\'d turn it on, with the sound muted, and watch the pretty colours.<br><br>he is probably up and away, staggering somewhere now. maybe to nina\\\'s place. i\\\'m hoping a bus doesn\\\'t hit him, i\\\'m hoping nina\\\'s not home. tomorrow\\\'s the day we go to vancouver, and my wrist, my wrist is hurting.</P> '),(2579,'<p>so after almost 6 months of being pummeled by bombs, afghanistan is rocked by a 6.1 quake, and the aid workers are tiptoeing around trying not to get blown to bits by dislodged land mines.  walking yesterday in the obscenely delicious san francisco springtime, i wondered to myself, \\\"why don\\\'t huge earthquakes ever seem to rock the country clubs of michigan and vermont?\\\"  we truly are living in the heartland.  even though i\\\'m on a fault line out here, i still feel like it\\\'s some kind of sunlit, airy fairytale town where people don\\\'t run around strapping explosives to themselves and running into crowded restaurants.  listening to npr lately has my hackles up.  it seems there will be no end to the destruction, the desperation, the overt bullying and the tedious back and forth of sharon and arafat and rumsfeld.  rumsfeld--ugh!  i can\\\'t even listen to npr while i eat breakfast anymore--hearing his voice makes my stomach turn. his macho posturing and \\\"everythings under control--MY control\\\" veneer.  we all know this.  we all sense that he\\\'s an android kind of like the terminator only very slightly more diplomatic. </p>\r\n<p>the good thing about listening to npr and reading the paper is that it puts things in perspective.  i think i\\\'ve got problems?  every week, it seems, someone in california or texas  offs their whole family and then themselves.  this week it was a former deputy who shot his 3 stepchildren and his own five year old to death and then shot himself.  that kind of pain, while imaginable to me from an emotional perspective, still seems faroff in terms of actually acting out those impulses.  what drives a person to do something like that?  to take a gun into your ex-wife\\\'s home actually intending to kill your kid? to fly a plane into a building? to _explode_ yourself?  can you think of a more horrible way to die?   i have experienced some pretty deep emotional wounds, but i feel like the older and wiser i get, the more fluid i become with how i approach and navigate the pain.  more transparent, or able to let it move on through like a wave, and then be gone.  it washes you like a wave too. if you let it.  </p>\r\n<p>my heart is going out right now, all over the world.  to victims of bombings. to displaced people with no hope.  to people shuddering with cold or fever in a country that seems doomed to be destroyed by man and nature.  to mothers who have lost their kids to disease, or gunshots, or madness.  to my friend who labors each day under a depression so deep he can\\\'t look into his own eyes in the mirror.  to my roommate who falls asleep to the tv every night.  to my own self, struggling between love so deep it drowns me and jealousy so white-hot and enraged it burns deep down.  to all of us.  </p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m praying for some stability, in my own life.  some calm.  some soothing for this fevered brow, some appreciation for the garden outside my bedroom window, the sounds of birds in the morning, the taste of toast with jam.  i\\\'m praying for some stability in this city, that we can support each other and build some solidarity and let the arts thrive and the homeless people survive.  i\\\'m praying for some stability on this globe.  although everything changes, and i accept that. i just want to let a little comfort in here.  i want every human to be able to have that little golden warm spot in his or her heart, that knowledge that somewhere along the path we will find peace.  i guess i\\\'m praying for peace. </p>\r\n<p>i just want a little peace.</p>'),(2580,'<p>today i am exceedingly happy because, after at least 12 years of trying, i have finally won something on the radio.</p> \r\n<p>though such an achievement has always been a dream of mine, i strived for it most earnestly in high school and the first two years of college, when i was a devoted listener of the old live 105, bastion of modern rock radio. my favorite game was \\\"what\\\'s the connection\\\" (at least i think that was the name of it), which came on at 11:30 or noon or 1 or somewhere around there every day. they would play three songs that were, in some way, connected to each other. sometimes they were general themes; other times it would be things the artist or the song title had in common.</p>\r\nan example:<br>\r\n\\\"message in a bottle\\\" by the police<br>\r\n\\\"girlfriend in a coma\\\" by the smiths<br>\r\n\\\"our lips are sealed\\\" by the go-gos<br>\r\n(who knows the answer?! come on, guys...it\\\'s easy! and fun!)<br></p>\r\nafter the third song began playing you could start calling in to guess it, and at the end of the third song the british-accented dj would start entertaining people\\\'s guesses. for some reason, this thing was highly addictive, even when the prizes were lame. but after four solid years of playing, and almost always guessing the right connection, i could never ever get through. and that was not the only venue i tried for radio winning, oh no. there would be weekends when i would park myself in front of my little stereo and call in every chance i could to win tickets or whatever they happened to be giving away. even a pack of free cds i already owned would have delighted me! i never got through.</p>\r\n<p>until today. driving to my annual unpleasent doctor\\\'s appointment and listening to <a href=\\\"http://www.kcrw.org/show/mb\\\">my favorite radio show</a> i heard them announce a ticket-giveaway for <a href=\\\"http://www.musicomh.com/interviews/neil-halstead.htm\\\">a guy i really wanted to hear play.</a> i made a mad dash for the cell phone to dial the number, and of course was greeted by that inevitable depressing busy signal. i kept trying, even past the point when i thought, \\\"no way in hell am i winning these tickets.\\\" then--gasp!--ring... ring... ring. five minutes of ringing results in someone answering the phone, putting me on hold, getting my name and telephone number, putting me on hold, getting my address, putting me on hold, and then - finally - telling me i won.</p>\r\n<p>i was still on hold while waiting in the doctor\\\'s office. the nurse who led me in shared in my extreme excitment. she said: \\\"i sit at my desk every day listening to the radio and calling in, and i always wondered how, after all that time, i could have never gotten through.\\\" yeah, i used to wonder, too!</p>'),(2581,'It\\\'s good to know that I still have it in me to spontaneously skip out of responsibility to binge drink in an attempt to cope, cause I\\\'m a curmudgeon with real and imaginary life pressures. Today I did exactly this, I left my pivotal volunteer position with the environment group early (the only useful/interesting thing I\\\'ve done for them so far is run a display about fuel cells at the local car show) and then planted my ass in the neighborhood pub and tied one on. Who says I need to be in university to provide myself with an excuse to deal with pressures in such a mature and constructive manner? <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere has been a spike in agitators in my life most recently.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>The third delay in my beginning of a new job as a marketing and publicist for the National Film Boards Ontario Documentary Studio - bureacracy related delay I\\\'ve been assured<br>\r\n<li>Personal life complications - fighting the rising tide of feelings related to the younger sister of an ex-girlfriend (there may be hope for this to work out but, it is rather messy)<br>\r\n<li>Home front friction due to my overwhelming urge to decapitate my roommates ex, who is around a lot for clandestine purposes, with a blunt instrument and brute force<br>\r\n<li>General angstyness due to a serious over abundance of pretentious and pompous opinion and a serious lack of plurality in relation to living life in contemporary popular culture in those around me currently<br>\r\n<li>Lawyers that run the environmental group that, I can only assume due to my less polished appearance, treat me like my intellectual abilities are not much higher then a distant evolutionary throwback<br><br>\r\n\r\nThese may seem like little things but when keeping other issues at bay unemployment (almost a year now) and ailing health these are the things that send me over the edge, on to a little drinking binge. Last time this happened I found myself with alcohol poisoning, an upset mother, and several empty bottle of gin strewn across my parents living room (I was visiting the parental units when it occured).<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnother irritant that I forgot to mention in my nice little list is this. I live in a condo where I rent a room from a friends family. The condo building in which I dwell has 24 hour security that sits at the front door. I have lived in this building almost 5 months now, I am constantly stopped by security and asked to identify myself and state my business. This tends to happen at least every two or three days. I have recently taken to berating the guards if it is not their first offence. I have gotten a variety of excuses about not recognizing me. The moment of truth however came when a new guard recently hired actually grabbed me by the shoulder and received a bit of a shove accompanied by berating. When I asked why I seem to be constantly stopped going in and out of the building the new guard was honest enough to tell me \\\"You look like a shady character.\\\" I thanked him for his honesty and went on my way unsure as to how to react to my apparent shadiness. After a bit of reflection on matters I am now committed to making sure that the next place I live is in a bad part of town so that I can be among my own kind and not be hastled.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnyway as I sit less then a block from my luxurious and secure abode getting sauced my mind is engaged in a pitch battle convincing itself of the gains to be made by following the general rules of civility. Instead of, to borrow a phrase from my early and naive (non-pc) adolescences, going \\\"Balls Out\\\" launching myself into a carnival of carnage and carnal acts as I start bouncing around like a heated molecule inspired by Easy Rider nd Baise-Moi. Self destructiveness seems so trite these days though, especially with so many people the world over struggling. Being rationally aware of this doesn\\\'t seem to help me in coping with my feelings of frustration though. This is where the bar seems to come in after drinking enough all I will really want out of life is some tylenol or asprin.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m curious what long term goals aside from financial success/independence I could latch onto to give me some purpose for behaving properly. Temporal power? spiritual enlightenment? love of some sort? It\\\'s like I have A.D.D. when it comes to life goals.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNo lo tengo, Yo lo tengo,  Soy confuso<br>\r\nIch kann nie auf allem wirklich abrechnen<br>\r\nDesidero andare di nuovo alla scuola e studiare i linguaggi<br>\r\nDans un monde parfait peut-Ãªtre<br>\r\nI believe in Whorfian language theory<br>\r\n'),(2582,'<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2582_1.jpg\"><br><br>\r\nItâ€™s 4:42pm and I just got back from chilling a while in Riverside Park. You see, I got home after a long day at work today and found a bill in the mail; usually, this is no biggie, but like I said, today was a <i>long</i> day already. When I opened it up, my eyes confirmed what I already knew â€œ<b>Account Status: Delinquent</b>â€ before opening the envelope. Sigh. So this <i>wasnâ€™t</i> some odd trick of fate that my cell-phone had been ringing <i>temporarily out of service</i> to people all day, then.<br><br>\r\nWell, let me tell you what I decided to do: I decided to rekindle a perpetual springtime interest of mine and unpack the old nylon sail-kite that I picked up a few years back, in college,  for a few bucks from some sidewalk hustler who was selling obviously ripped-off Eddie Bauer-ish items.<br><br>\r\nI started walking in the direction of Riverside Park, finding a new route, a new trail through the shin-high roadside weeds that grow oddly in the cracks of the median of Riverside Drive. Frustrated at the sight of a catch of last seasonâ€™s kite crop in many of the surrounding trees, I sat down thinking, â€œI must reconsider my kite-flying aspirations hereâ€. Although, it really <i>would</i> be a lot of fun to reel out a huge spool of string, letting it sail up over the mile-wide Hudson and up into New Jerseyâ€™s airspace.<br><br>\r\nAnd sitting in the park in the shadows of the inert kites, lolling, anchored in the wind. Thereâ€™s lots happening in the park at 4:42 in the afternoon, you know? People walking, kids playing, bikers biking, smokers smoking. I was watching a small family walk downtown, past me over the cobbles; I suppose I was smiling. The kid stopped and waved to me quickly as he passed<br><br>\r\nA lot of beautiful women run in the park, also, it seems; the fact that I donâ€™t get out to run <i>nearly</i> enough proves my guilt in not having discovered this phenomenon sooner. All the bronzed Upper Westside beauties running to work a long grey-ish winter off their skinny frames. I think I recognize some of them, vaguely, from situations in college.  Maybe the park is a good place to come to meet women, who knows; after all, it <i>is</i> springtime.\r\nAs the sunrays slowly turned to shadows, I walked back up to Broadway and decided to see what was shaking in the bodega on my way back home. As I stepped into <i>La Rosita Corona</i>, a car passed, beaming â€œfunk master flexâ€ something-or-other into the afternoon air. Some man was singing along on the sidewalk as the door shut behind me, muffling his rasp.<br><br>\r\nI decided to pick up a Snapple and maybe something to munch on. I had to reach over some  very cold stacked cases of Red Bull as I had my heart set on something more chill, maybe mint iced tea. Its still damn cold outside, though. Too cold to be touching cold cans.<br><br>\r\nI also picked up a day-old doughnut at the register.<br><br>\r\nNow Iâ€™m sitting in my basement as the afternoon fades over the building tops eating my doughnut. Iâ€™m eating my doughnut on Good Friday, in the haze of the monitor and the lava-lamp, an early season icecream truck tinkling up the street. I make a call to Customer Service and pay my cell-phone bill; I donâ€™t know why it hadnâ€™t occurred to me to do this earlier, to use my credit card to call in a payment;  keying in my account number and payment with one hand, clasping a really deliciously moist doughnut with the other. <i>This doughnut stands for satisfaction.</i><br><br>\r\nAnd, I definitely plan to fly the kite later, when the situation warms up a bit.\r\n'),(2583,'<p>\r\n- kandrer is 33.<br>\r\n- kandrer is french.<br>\r\n- kandrer is a paranoiac, and he\\\'s proud of it.<br>\r\n- he\\\'s rarely proud of <i>himself</i>.<br>\r\n- kandrer might not be the nicest person you ever met. though, he might be the nicest paranoiac you ever met.<br>\r\n- he is a loop thinker: most of the time, when you stop thinking about something, kandrer is <i>still</i> thinking about it.<br>\r\n- when you forgot something for good, kandrer is <i>still</i> thinking about it.<br>\r\n- when kandrer forgot something, he will think about it <i>again</i> later.<br>\r\n- kandrer is 100% self conscious. that\\\'s the least one can say about him.<br>\r\n- kandrer thinks he\\\'s old.<br>\r\n- kandrer has spent many years wondering if he would one day become insane or depressive. he now thinks it won\\\'t happen. he\\\'s for sure too confident about this.<br>\r\n- kandrer will be married with H on sunday. he feels calm and happy about it, just like in a movie (a quiet movie). he forgot about the ring (damn!).<br>\r\n- kandrer is christian. and even maybe catholic. at least as much you can be when you don\\\'t believe in god.<br>\r\n- kandrer likes to wave at people, and likes to be waved at.<br>\r\n- kandrer often turns back to look at people as he goes away. kandrer watches people as they go away. kandrer is a romantic.<br>\r\n- kandrer has a superiority complex. he\\\'s ashamed of that and tries to hide it. sometimes, it doesn\\\'t show.<br>\r\n- thanks to his superiority complex, or maybe to a very few faithful friends, kandrer still believes that he is loveable; even though most people just cannot stand him.<br>\r\n- kandrer cannot express his feelings.<br>\r\n- kandrer likes to be ambiguous, hoping that his loved ones will understand him anyway.<br>\r\n- kandrer is sometimes a person as you and me. for example after a good night of sleep.<br>\r\n- sometimes, kandrer is tired of seeing his face in the mirror. changing his face for another would be great.<br>\r\n- kandrer would like to be a woman. women can dress with many different cool clothes. women\\\'s shoes, for example, look pretty on women\\\'s feet. kandrer is jealous of that.<br>\r\n- kandrer is scared to become old. kandrer is scared of many things. like hormones or nerves, or veins in his body.<br>\r\n- kandrer cries during sad movies. it seems to get worse with age.<br>\r\n- kandrer likes to talk to strangers. he also likes to argue about quite anything.<br>\r\n- kandrer was born in cannes, france. he wishes he was born in nice instead.<br>\r\n- kandrer has been abroad too long. 1 year in japan and 2 in the usa. now he hopes that he will still feel good in france.<br>\r\n- in june, he\\\'ll be back to france. in september, he will move to japan.<br>\r\n- kandrer likes to talk about himself. sometimes, but less often, he can listen too.<br>\r\n- kandrer said stupid things, many times, and he knows he will do it again. it\\\'s sad.<br>\r\n</p>'),(2584,'<P>\r\nthe holy order of pooing is not known in this land to travellers as it is in the residential environments of north america.  when i poop, i prefer to retire to the sanctum and consider all aspects of the event as they arrive.  there is easing into the comfortable need to poo.  then comes the break from whatever activity one is doing, and retiring to the inner sanctum.  next is comfortably taking the place of the king.  follow this by the right of passage.  then dropping the kids off at the pool.  the return to peace of mind.  second to last, the clean up.  and finally, easing back into the present.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen travelling, of course you have to poo eventually.  it is not something that i personally can ease  up on.  i dread the idea of pooing in this land.  i protract the event across a mental map of my two day schedule so as to not be cought of gaurd.  i may not be able to poo on my own throne, but i can at least plan to be on one that is not moving at 100 kilometers per hour, or one that involves me squating into a hole in the ground and trying to run a lid of water down my crack afterwards.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthere are several kinds of pooing environments here in thailand.\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ the rural poo invironment, basic: a hole in the ground.  there may be a bucket of water to run down your crack, there may not.  but there will not be toilet paper.\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ the rural poo environment, high tech: a toilet, there is no paper.  you have a bucket to pour water down your crack, and then to poor water into the toilet thus activating the flushing mechanism.\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ the moving toilet: these are on the V.I.P. buses which take you from city to city.  there is no toilet paper.  there is a bucket of water to pour down your crack.  the bus is moving and shaking.  combine this with trying not to sit down on the toilet seat.\r\n</P><P>\r\n_  the hotel toilet, problematic: there is toilet paper, but after you wipe, you will need to put it in the poo rag mini-trash can; try not to touch the lid.  when you flush, the bowl fills up, then it slowly drains.  there is no flushing action.  if your poo floats, it will never go down.  you have to use the sprayer which is like many found in US kitchens for cleaning dishes and spray the poo until it breaks up and releases the air, thus comiting to sink.  you may also use the sprayer on your bum hole.\r\n</P><P>\r\n_  the hotel toilet, functioning:  it may be too low to the ground to relax, or have a broken seat, but it flushes and is supplied with both the toilet paper, and usually the bum sprayer as well.\r\n</P><P>\r\nif you manage to make it to one of those safe zones ( rural poo environment--high-tech, hotel problematic, or hotel functioning ) you can count your lucky stars.  otherwise there is the pay toilet ( not reviewed at this time ) and the gas station toilet ( also not reviewed at this time ).  \r\n</P><P>\r\nif you need to locate one, say \\\" toilet \\\" not rest room or water closet.\r\n</P>'),(2585,'<p>Saturday\\\'s Agenda:\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>listen to the Squarepusher track <i>Whooski</i></li>\r\n  <li>shower</li>\r\n  <li>sleep for the entire afternoon</li>\r\n  <li>wake up when it\\\'s dark outside</li>\r\n  <li>clean my room</li>\r\n  <li>eat large dinner</li>\r\n  <li>attempt to fix VCR to facilitate the watching of Beat Street</li>\r\n  <li>proceed onward with Minix kernel hacking</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I will attempt to dream of road trips to Montreal and jumping in lakes.  I want to be able to control, or guide, my dreams.  Considering I have enough trouble actually remembering them at all, I don\\\'t see this happening anytime soon for me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Why control my own dreams?  Well nothing is ever as good as your mind can imagine it.  So I figure that if I\\\'m sleeping, I might as well have my brain working on something of my own choosing, rather than it dredging up whatever toxic sludge it may find from my subconscious.</p>\r\n'),(2586,'<p>i spent most of my childhood moving from place to place.  by age twenty i had been on 52 international flights, which pretty much explains why \\\"overhead bin\\\" was one of the first phrases to ever cross my lips.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i got a lot out of living overseas. in my mind, there\\\'s no substitute for taking it in, soaking it up, getting to know people and places and things first hand. i got to see the solidarity marches in warsaw and the collapse of the berlin wall; i made friends with gemma our filipina babysitter and learned japanese by watching television shows like sazae-san and chibimaruko. my awareness of the world slowly came about without my even realizing it, through seeing my father drive to work in a bullet-proof car in the philippines and being kept home from school in times when americans were likely to be targeted by terrorists. i  missed the 1986-1996 period of american pop culture, but all things considered i guess that\\\'s not such a bad thing. the important elements, like madonna and popples, made their way over the atlantic anyways.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it was a shock to move back to dc for my junior year in high school after ten years overseas. it was supposedly home, but it totally wasn\\\'t; i had no idea what the tv shows or trends or music were all about. i was unfamiliar with abercrombie and fitch, which poses a problem if you\\\'re a sixteen year old prep school enrollee. after years of moving, my sister and i were old hands at adapting, but adjusting to being back in our home country was probably the biggest challenge we had had to face. i eased my frustration by increasing my parents\\\': dating georgetown university students, sneaking out to the 18th street lounge, smoking cigarettes in the bishop\\\'s garden. just another suburban girl.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>until i was 17, the longest i ever spent in any one place was three and a half years. now that i\\\'m getting ready to graduate from college, after four years in a small town in upstate new york, i\\\'m definitely ready to get out of here. i don\\\'t really know what i\\\'ll be doing next, but i know this dissatisfied feeling, the itching of my brain.  it\\\'s moving time.</p>  '),(2587,'i am sitting in one of the many internet cafes in bangkok and in thailand as a whole.  it makes me believe the internet can really belong to the people.  though many probably cannot afford one baht a minute, the fact that networking 14 computers on a single 56k modem makes me happy.  all the better knowing that not a single one of these 14 computers paid for it\\\'s copy of Windows.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsitting here, i chat with friends back home.<BR><BR>\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n[02:08] wb> how is amy doing<BR>\r\namy is deathly ill<BR>\r\nshe is speaking gibberish<BR>\r\n[02:08] wb> from what?<BR>\r\n[02:08] wb> food?<BR>\r\ndont know<BR>\r\n[02:09] jim> shit, really?<BR>\r\nyes<BR>\r\nshe will go to her uncle\\\'s tomorrow and he will take her to a dr.<BR>\r\n[02:09] jim> poor kid<BR>\r\n[02:10] le> :( :(<BR>\r\n[02:10] le> make sure she drinks enough water<BR>\r\nthat is all i do<BR>\r\ni feed her water<BR>\r\nand food and read the paper to her<BR>\r\ni  am a good caretaker<BR>\r\n</BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<BR>\r\nlife is nice here in the white travellors\\\' ghetto.  it is like being at home insofar as i walk from my room, to the restaurant downstairs for coffee, across the street for food and two stores down for internet.  and of course, i am sitting here with the King\\\'s beer: Chang of Thailand, in a 32 ounce bottle.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin the back of the store, a dog is barking like a nutzoid and thai people are kicking it, teenagers.   dogs are all over the place.  bangkok is their home.  a dog sleeps in the middle of the sidewalk during rush hour.  they live not in packs but in little territories.  my space is from here to the end of the block, one dog with scares on its back says.  i think of one of those callendars of people with their dogs, or the ones with the weirmerieners (how do you spell that?).  these dogs are glamorous in their mange, toothlessness, limps and scares.  they lounge like classical models, goyas prostitutes, or monet\\\'s women in cafe goers.  especially cafe goers.  they are a single focus of sanity in the hustle and bustle.  imagine if you would your living room where you sit now, and place it amidst a manhattan intersection at 6 pm.  everything moves around you.  this is the domain of a the dog. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni should like to take their pictures, these glamorous bitches.   love them because they do not care if you do or do not.  what is called for is a coffee table book of large glossy photos to set next to your copy of \\\"Cats of Greece\\\" lounging around on sun bleached white timelessness.  this book is \\\"Dogs of Thailand\\\" in the mud and filth, with patchy hair and dirty grace.  someone-- quick-- advance me 50,000 baht on the publication.  that is not much money.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni wonder who would buy such a book.  certainly not me.  but then this is the internet age.  printing is no longer such a huge undertaking.  all one had to do is find some small press and befriend the proprietor, make him your bitch, and then ask a small favor.           \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\ni am almost done with my chang.  time to wrap this up.  jim tells me he is suggesting s.o to a judge at the webbies.  i guess this means i have to wait until they are over before i change the name and release the site to peter.'),(2588,'My pits emit my presence long before I become aware of it myself. Clad in a black t-shirt and jeans I dream of becoming a cult superstar for my pseudo tap dancing routines set to various electronic and post-rock musical acts. All the pivotal questions about life are drowned out by my own spastic rhythm as I tap dance my interpretation of the emotional content of their lyrics or lack thereof.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMy dance is unapologetic and takes a stance on a variety of social issues, it communicates my core to the audience in the most efficient means possible. The viewers make conjectures as to what goes through my mind as I move like a marionette. Few if any bring up golden Emma but quotes from her are one of the flashing images, as my means of thought switches from a language based process to images, when the rhythm reaches that critical point.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMy dance somehow communicates the state of the human condition in its paradoxical complex simplicity all the way from the darkness of war bred genocide and rape to the lightness of hugs and the intangible connection of love. It is the spoonful of sugar that lets the whole be digested by the viewer. <br><br>\r\n\r\nAn act of communication that is from the margins yet holographic in nature and thus a champion of the core. Its chosen meduim of communication is the attribute of intuition. The feeble attempts of the fool who doesn\'t know any better to provide a kernel.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2589,'<P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, we talk about the changes. on the news, we see a ferris wheel, motionless on a ramallah hillside. we talk about the people who just want to live their lives.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, we think we\\\'re falling in love. i make her some soup, masturbate before she arrives and put a little bit of the semen into her bowl. she will love me. we don\\\'t think about the morals.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, i play her some music:  vibraphone, reverb, and organ that sounds like it was made in some studio with blue satin on the walls.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, i\\\'m glad i have friends, glad she\\\'s becoming my friend.</P><P><B>after it rains, the air\\\'s so humid.<BR>you feel it wrapping around you when you step outside.</B></P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, my dad presents me with a monkey. four inches tall he carved it, holding out a cup for alms. i think he\\\'s trying to make up for yelling at me yesterday.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, i\\\'m going to plant some seeds out in the woods.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, i\\\'m going to make inadmissable statements on the internet.</P><P><B>i wonder if you can love a person one way and love everyone in the world with a more general love that\\\'s just as strong. if it\\\'s all the same love.</B></P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, i drive my big black car around town in the middle of the night. wave at all the cops. scowly cops.</P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, we listen to x and can and thelonious monk. what is this music, she says, full of wonder.</P><P><B>the facts we hate: we\\\'ll never meet.<BR>walkin\\\' down the road, everybody yellin\\\' hurry up, hurry up,<BR>but i\\\'m waiting for you, i must go slow.<BR>i must not think bad thoughts.<BR><BR>i must not think bad thoughts.</B></P><P>when it\\\'s summer in siam, we\\\'re gonna stay up all night talking about nothing at all, but it will mean everything to us.</P>'),(2590,'<P>back in california for a day and everything was going fine for a\r\nlittle while. then i looked at my bank statement.  it seems i was\r\ncharged 31 dollars to have 20 dollars cash back from my paycheck when\r\nmy account balance was $0.32.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe day wore on and i became more acquainted with why my job bugs me\r\nso much.  i had kind of forgotten and was almost happy to be back.\r\nyou know i would like to talk about it here but i know some people at\r\nwork read this stuff.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe day wore on and i listened to 3 songs of elliot smith and sank in to\r\na depression that fast.  often i swear to delete his music from my\r\nhard drive.  my body has a chemical reaction to the notes he plays.\r\nthey put me down.  i tried to counter the affects with Lord Weird\r\nSlough Feg.  i don\\\'t think it completely worked.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni got a speeding ticket before i left for vacation.  that fact alone\r\nmakes me want to move to sf and sell my car and thus quit my current\r\njob.  paying insurance companies to protect me is a stomach sickening\r\nsubject.  now i have to pay even more.  150 dollars a month just in\r\ncase.  i don\\\'t know how much the ticket is going to be.  life appears\r\nat times to be a struggle to get my head above water. financially, i\r\nhave been gurgling for years.  i practically have gills.  if there\r\nwere any justice in evolution gills would be mine.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe sky is overcast.  that never helps.  i will go to karate tonight.\r\nthat got me excited this morning but now i want to die again.  i want\r\nto wrap my car around a police officer.  or load it with explosives\r\nand drive it into the court house, or wells fargo, or go to the middle\r\neast in search of aladdin\\\'s lamp so that i might make the ending of\r\nfight club a reality.  99 red balloons.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhaving finished <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0679601996/qid=1017800127/sr=2-2/ref=sr_2_2/102-1656586-8620909\\\" target=\\\"amazon\\\">The Count of Monte Cristo</a> yesterday on the plane, i bought a new book today: <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0684865246/qid=1017798946/sr=8-1/ref=sr_8_3_1/102-1656586-8620909\\\" target=\\\"amazon\\\">Euclid\\\'s\r\nWindow</A>.  it is part of my new, \\\'maybe math is not so lame,\\\' kick. </P>\r\n'),(2591,'<p>i printed out the post and folded the paper in 8 to put it in my back pocket. i use to keep things in my jeans back pocket when i want to avoid to forget them. it\\\'s been a few days now since i carry this paper. i have been thinking about this every time i put my hand in my pocket to check what i have to remember; a few times a day. i talked about it with S, C, and several times with H and M. the questions i was asked were: <i>what can you do? why do you want to do something? did she ask for any help?</i><br>\r\nno great answer was found. maybe the best i\\\'ve came up with is <i>because i feel it this way</i>. hey, it works out all the time in the damn movies, dammit. would they be lying to us? yes, i know they are.\r\n</p><p>\r\nH is taking some couseling courses. they taught her to listen to what the \\\"helpee\\\" is saying, then to reformulate it without adding any personal opinion. they are suppose to let the helpee talk until he/she realizes where the problem is. it really looks like psychoanalysis to me. i could not stand doing that. there would be a point when i would say, i would shout, what the problem is. and i might be wrong. but right or wrong, the helpee won\\\'t believe/understand anything if she did not find it by herself.<br>\r\nit makes sense. i think about the \\\"map of the world\\\" they talk about in NLP. we can only understand things that fit in our inner representation of the outside world.\r\n</p><p>\r\na nice chinese proverb nearly about this: <i>he who is blind hears bad.</i><br><br>\r\nthe translation must be wrong; i have only a french version to start from: <i>qui est aveugle entend mal</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut, if she had no doubts, if nothing was wrong, she would not write about it, right?\r\n\r\n'),(2592,'<P>there is glitter piled high on the sidewalks\r\n\r\n<br>\r\na winter moon like a half closed eyelid\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nand wind that slips its silver fingers through my hair\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nwhispering the whole city is in love with me\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\ni cover my face with my hands\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nagainst the pale frosted shore\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthe Atlantic blooms and blooms again\r\n<br>\r\n\r\ninto a hundred thousand blue flowers\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nits beach lit up from the radiance\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nof their white centres\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ntonight i will grow cold enough \r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthat the snowflakes dropping \r\n<br>\r\nonto my bare body\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nwill not melt but stay\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nand freeze into lace for my new skin \r\n</P> '),(2593,'<p>I have no aptitude for physical activity of any sort.  This is probably due to the fact that my parents told me when I was very young that my muscles lacked the capacity of other kids, that no matter how hard I tried Iâ€™d have only 75-80% of the normal musculature.  Thus armed with this knowledge I proceeded into elementary school at a disadvantage.  At recess I did not run as fast as the other kids.  I missed the ball in kickball.  I was the first one out in dodgeball.  I was the last picked for basketball.  \r\n\r\n<p>I didnâ€™t blame the other kids for not picking me. Iâ€™d fall on my ass when I swung at a softball, trip over my own feet during flag football. I was a terrible player.  The other kids mocked me mercilessly when I missed a basket or skinned my knee on the blacktop.  If it wasnâ€™t for the fact that my teachers expected to participate, I wouldnâ€™t have.  During recess, when participation was supposed to be optional, I liked to wander off by myself and make up stories to amuse myself.  \r\n\r\n<p>My elementary school was divided into two sections, â€œthe little kid\\\'s sideâ€ for first through third-graders, and â€œthe big kid\\\'s sideâ€ for fourth through sixth.  Recesses for the respective sides was staggered, so that at any given time the teachers and lunch monitors would only have to deal with three grades.  Less bodies notwithstanding, us kids were still expected to confine our lunchtime play to the appropriate side of the playground.\r\n\r\n<p>One day I decided that the rules were unfair.  If I wanted to play on the so-called little kid\\\'s side I should be allowed.  Besides, there were no kids over there.  There were no structured games I had to play, no opportunity to be made fun of, so I went over to the swingset and laid across one of the rubber swings on my belly, legs and arms dangling into the sand.  I imagined that I was the prisoner of space aliens, being carried off to their planet for food.  At the opportune moment I planned to burst from my â€œmeat hookâ€ and lash out against the heinous beasts who planned untold horrors for me.  \r\n\r\n<p>I swung back and forth slowly over the earth below the swingset, my fingers dragging through the sand and dried clay, biding my time.\r\n\r\n<p>So engrossed was I in my fantasy that I did not realize that one of the lunch ladies had come over from the big kids side and now stood only a few feet away from me.  â€œYou there,â€  she said tersely.  â€œWhat are you doing over here?â€\r\n\r\n<p>I looked up with a shot, shocked to see another human on a spaceship where I had thought I was alone.  â€œUhâ€¦â€  I said.\r\n\r\n<p>â€œMr. Starr wants to see you in his office.  Lets go.â€\r\n\r\n<p>Mr. Starr was the schoolâ€™s principal.  He was bald, a little pudgy, and shorter than my dad, which would put him somewhere under 5â€™8â€.  \r\n\r\n<p>But his size didnâ€™t matter.  The principal was somebody you thought about with fear and trembling.  He was an administrator that really didnâ€™t have much to do with the students unless theyâ€™d done something grievously wrong.  Among the kids there were whispered legends of corporal punishment.  --Mr. Starr kept the paddle on a hook behind his desk.  --It was drilled with holes for less wind resistance.  --Carpet tacks were nailed through the paddle which Mr. Starr would brandish for the sole purpose of instilling his victim with pure, unadulterated fear.  Right before the paddle finally met bottom, he would flip it around to the flat side.  \r\n\r\n<p>There were not many accounts of kids who had met Mr. Starrâ€™s paddle.  Those who had told conflicting stories.  \r\n\r\n<p>So there I was.  I had transgressed the line between the big kid\\\'s and the little kid\\\'s sides and was now being called to Mr. Starrâ€™s office to, in all likelihood,  find out for myself what kind of paddle hung on that hook behind his desk.  \r\n\r\n<p>I followed the lunch lady, who I can only describe as tall, to Mr. Starrâ€™s door.  I suppressed the urge to turn and run.  Where would I go?  Mr. Starrâ€™s office had a door which opened onto the playground and a large window through which I could see a row of bookcases.  She knocked.  I heard a muffled â€œCome in.â€  She opened the door and guided me into the room where Mr. Starr behind his a desk.  A stack of manila folders stood on the edge of the desk.  He had one such folder opened in front of him on the desk calendar.  His name plate read merely â€œMR STARR.â€  I looked around for the paddle but didnâ€™t see it.  \r\n\r\n<p>â€œHere he is.â€  The lunch lady said.\r\n\r\n<p>â€œThank you Mrs. Boyd, that will be all.â€  Mr. Starr dismissed her.  After she left the room, Mr. Starrâ€™s face seemed to soften.  I stood there shifting uncomfortably from foot to foot.  â€œQuinn,â€  he said, and I wondered how he knew my name.  â€œWhat were you doing on the little kid\\\'s side?  You know youâ€™re not supposed to be there.â€\r\n\r\n<p>I felt a tinge of anger, a sense that this division of the playground was somehow unjust, that I wasnâ€™t really doing anything wrong by being there, that I had only violated an unfair rule.  But I did not have the vocabulary to express these thoughts.  â€œI was playing.â€  I said.\r\n\r\n<p>Mr. Starr turned and briefly looked out the window.  The California sunlight poured through, enclosing him in a square of light.  â€œWhy were you playing alone?â€  He asked quietly.  â€œDonâ€™t you have any friends?â€\r\n\r\n<p>I couldnâ€™t look him in the eye.  I looked at the books behind his head, his knick-knacks.  His college degrees hung on the wall.  \r\n\r\n<p>â€œI have friends.â€ I said.  Again, I lacked the vocabulary to say what I was thinking, that I didnâ€™t like to play my friendâ€™s stupid games, that I was more content by myself, in a world that I had created, where my storyline was perfectly controlled and I didnâ€™t need to catch a ball or make a basket to be a hero.  â€œI have friends.â€  I repeated.\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWhy donâ€™t you go play with them?  It would be better for you.â€\r\n\r\n<p>I just wanted to get out of there.  â€œOkay.â€  \r\n\r\n<p>â€œI donâ€™t want to see you on the little kids side again, do you understand?â€\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYes sir.â€\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYou are dismissed.â€\r\n\r\n<p>â€œThank you.â€  I turned and fled Mr. Starrâ€™s office.  All  I could think is that I had come close, but had somehow escaped the fearsome paddle.  \r\n\r\n<p>The day seemed exceptionally bright and I felt light and buoyant with my new-found freedom as I walked back to the big kid\\\'s side where my friends played basket ball, four-square, and a horrible game called â€œsmear the queer.â€ \r\n\r\n<p>I found a bench, sat down and watched the other kids play while I waited for recess to be over.\r\n'),(2594,'<p><tt>My genital multilations have not gone un-noticed by my local health worker Alan. Alan hasn\\\'t said anything. He never does. I\\\'m not sure wheter it\\\'s because he\\\'s afraid to; or whether it\\\'s just that he just doesn\\\'t care. After all it\\\'s only a job. A way to pay the bills. Alan has a prominent birth mark below his right ear, the shape of Papua New Guinea. It\\\'s one of those real noticable ones. Real deep purple. No. Magenta. He caught me staring at it. He didn\\\'t seem at all perturbed. I suppose he\\\'s used to it. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>It was just a routine check up, you know, the way they call you in every now and then. Next service in 6 months or 40,000 miles whichever is sooner. That sorta thing. I go, just because i have nothing better to do. Alan is always there. He\\\'s been my local health worker for the last four years. Alan never looks pleased nor displeased to see me. Alan is somewhat indifferent in everything he says and does. He wears alot of grey and speaks in a low dulcid tone. Alan is my local health worker. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>I have to take these tablets twice a day before meals. I don\\\'t eat twice a day. I told Alan this. He gave me a leaflet entitled \\\"Healthy Eating for Modern Lifestyles\\\". I put it in my jacket pocket.</tt></p>\r\n'),(2595,'<p>It all started with a feeling in my gut. This was many years ago. I\\\'m not sure \r\n  exactly when but let\\\'s say 1995. I was an akward teenager. No experience with \r\n  women whatsoever. Never been in a relationship of any sort. But i suddenly got \r\n  this feeling in my gut accompanied by overwhelming sadness and a yearning for..... \r\n  something. Someone? </p>\r\n<p>Looking back with experience on my side i can see that i was pining for a lost \r\n  love. That indescribable feeling of heartbreak that you have to experience for \r\n  yourself to really understand. I started to have odd, fragmented memories of \r\n  things, places, faces i didn\\\'t recognise. Things i didn\\\'t recognise but somehow \r\n  strangely did. An old fashioned black bicycle with big silver brake levers. \r\n  A 1930s/40s village in rural england, houses built with golden brown coloured \r\n  stone set off by the summer sunset. And most importantly, a girl\\\'s face. Age, \r\n  late teens, early twenties, blonde hair, dark-brown/black eyes. A feeling of \r\n  complete happiness. A train. A postman. Tears.</p>\r\n<p>At the time i didn\\\'t think much of them. You have to understand, these memories \r\n  came few and far between over the course of several years. It\\\'s only now i\\\'m \r\n  beginning to piece things together.</p>\r\n<p>For the longest time there were only fragments. Broken pieces of memories. \r\n  Until the dream. The most vivid dream i have ever had, both before and since. \r\n  This chick was hot! I won\\\'t go into the whole dream in detail but here is a \r\n  very brief summary of events:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>The dream starts with her face, smiling then laughing. The village postman \r\n    riding past on his bicycle ringing his bell greeting people as he goes.</li>\r\n  <li>Cut to opening a letter</li>\r\n  <li>Cut to leaving on a train with this girl standing on the platform, handkerchief \r\n    in one hand crying as she gets smaller and smaller and dissapears into the \r\n    distance.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>Read into that what you will. Young man sent away to war? I\\\'d rather not speculate. \r\n  I haven\\\'t thought about any of this for years. In fact i\\\'ve had none of the \r\n  memories for a long time. Until the other night when i felt that strong sense \r\n  of longing once again. And that beautiful face.</p>\r\n<p>Who was she? Who was i?</p>'),(2596,'<p>i must be oblivious to something when it comes to music...\r\n</p><p>\r\nlast weekend, i drove up to pittsburgh to see the white stripes. i believe the show sold out.  the venue was originally rosebud, but was moved to a larger venue next door called metropol (think multi-level bar a la club mtv).  i stood in the front row. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni was blown away by the first opening band, the soledad brothers.  a physical blues experience as well as a musical one. the drummer stood up while playing every so often while the guitarist/singer poured sweat.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe second band was kind of college rock (brendan benson and the well-fed boys).  but good, reminiscent of 1994, college rock.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe white stripes were better than i imagined. just simple, scaled-down, raw music and very little rock talk. actually, none that i recall.  they did such a great variety of songs. not just the newer, more \\\"radio-friendly\\\" tunes.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe day after the show i was at the white stripes website guestbook reading the entries. i saw an onslaught of posts from people bitching about the show and how it felt like no one was enjoying it and they were complaining about people not dancing and stuff. in some cases, all in the same breath with \\\"i saw your video on mtv.\\\" i didn\\\'t notice people being obnoxious, elitist or critical at the show, but i was pretty much just focused on the show and enjoying it.  i agree that the venue was really not that great, but the show from my vantage point was incredible.  i saw the show reviews from the following night\\\'s concert in philadelphia and there were about three times as many complaints from attendees bitching about not only the crowd but the band.\r\n</p><p>\r\n...i guess the oblivious part for me is... when i go to a show i am just there to have a great time. i am not into the whole \\\"rockstar on a pedestal\\\" or the \\\"oh god frat guys are here\\\" thing. if i spent the entire show worrying about people not dancing/the band seeming pissed or not doing enough encores/what types of people went to the show, i might as well go back to the hallowed halls of high school and overanalyze everything again. \r\n</p><p>\r\nas i posted on the ws guestbook myself: \\\"i\\\'d like to think [all of this analysis and categorizing of fans by other fans] is all mtv\\\'s fault\\\".  i remember when i\\\'d go to more obscure/lo-fi shows and feel so worried about not being as \\\"indie\\\" as everyone else. but as you grow up a little you tend to realize that hoardes of people wearing the same indie-fied wardrobe are about as original as the people they make fun: the hoardes of people wearing all gap and abercrombie clothes.  and you stop analyzing things like public events and start enjoying them more than people watching and criticizing. well, at least i do. some people seem to not be ready to embrace a show for what it is. and this was a 21 and over show, so i would assume that attendees wouldn\\\'t have had that high school mentality anymore.  maybe people would get into the shows more if they didn\\\'t rely on one video on mtv to base their whole view of what the entire show was going to be like. many people there who came because they saw the \\\"fell in love with a girl\\\" video on mtv probably expected the entire show to sound that way. barely any of the white stripes\\\'s other songs do.  \r\n</p><p>\r\ni am fed up with indier-than-thou and pretentious art rock attitudes just as much as the popular kids at the cool lunch table attitude lately.  but, they exist.  they just can\\\'t seem to co-exist. \r\n</p><p>\r\nsee you at the morgantown show!</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2597,'<p>i really want to tell you all how moss soaks up water. (so fast!) i want to talk about how clay-y earth crumbles into dust when you step on it. i want to tell you exactly how the compost is doing. it\\\'s doing really well. leaves are becoming dirt. trees are becoming dirt. lettuce stems are becoming dirt. when it becomes more dirtlike, i will make offerings of compost to the trees, kneeling at their bases. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to buy a pound of worms. they would be red wrigglers. they would go in the compost cut composting time in half. did you know chickens and gardens go together? they make nice nitrogen rich manure. my permaculture book has instructions on how to make a <a href=\\\"http://www.gsu.edu/~biojdsx/fowl/tractor.htm\\\">chicken tractor<a/>. this is like a chicken anus on wheels, that you drive around your yard, so it fertilizes wherever it goes. okay? okay. i seriously reconsidered being vegan at the thought of organic fresh eggs from my future chickens on my future farm. hmm!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to go over to steve\\\'s and identify every plant in his garden. did you know he has wild strawberries back there? they started coming up when i watered his wildflower seedings. they are really cute. the same kind of strawberry is growing in helsinki right now. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>he has a hundred year old juniper out there. and garlic. and spring onions. and a bearberry bush. and a new jersey tea leaf tree. good thing grizzlies can\\\'t afford the rent in dolores heights.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>are we the farmers or are the plants farming us?</p> i stole that thought from \\\'botany of desire.\\\' <- really good book.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"everything in a garden is connected to each other. the more relationships you have in a garden, the healthier it will be.\\\" - bill mollison, \\\"intro to permaculture\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>enough for now.</p><br>'),(2598,'<p>The last time I remember doing much of it, I was 16. I much preferred my â€™68 VW fastback, but the Chevy Nova (gold with white vinyl top) had a bench seat. I could slide to the middle. The fastback had buckets, a gearshift in the middle, those little heater vents that open right up to the engine there on the floor between the seats. Ray would put his arm around my shoulders. I would put my hand on his thigh. We never had sex, but we made out a lot. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Not even a year later, when Iâ€™d finally fallen â€œin loveâ€ like a snared tiger falling into a dark pit did I think to lean over and push my face into the driverâ€™s side. By then I had a 924. It wasnâ€™t as cool as youâ€™d think. And itsâ€™ buckets were divided by the E-brake, and the gearshift, and a plastic cubby tray. This boy had a Ford Fiesta. Donâ€™t ask me how I managed to fall for a guy with a Fiesta. I should have known better based entirely on his car, but I was only 17, and I was still a little naÃ¯ve about these things. Later he spent more time driving an old Datsun pick up. An old pick up is better, but still. A Fiesta! </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hindsight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were others, and thinking back, my god, what lame ass cars Iâ€™ve dated. I wasted my 2002 on a guy whoâ€™d never even gotten his license. The guy with the Dodge Colt would let me borrow it anytime, but at what cost? What if someone should see me in this small automatic hatchback with new age stickers on the back window? The Mitsubishi Mirageâ€¦ Once, parked on the side of the road in rural Pennsylvania I even did it in the front passenger seat of a crappy Geo Metro. Looking back, I am positive all of these hatchbacks horrified my father, the mechanic, the former race car driver, the man who stuck a Buick engine into a â€™64 Bug convertible just because he â€œwanted to be able to merge on the freewayâ€. Ohâ€¦ and then there was the guy with the Fiat. A Fiat for Christâ€™s sake. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were a couple motorcycles, and regardless of make and model, they were predominantly un-lame, but you canâ€™t kiss on a motorcycle. The seating arrangement just doesnâ€™t allow for it. And even if you lift up the face shield, and manage to slam your faces together, your lips arenâ€™t going to touch. They are sexy things, but they are not geared up for romance. Not even at stoplights. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>But yesterday, after 14 years of hatchbacks and bucket seats, I found myself in a big fucking Ford truck, retaliating for a flipped bird by meeting in the middle with a little making out for the benefit of the assholeâ€™s rearview mirror display. After 14 years, Iâ€™m at it again. But you knowâ€¦ Iâ€™d still really like to date a Corvette.</p>\r\n'),(2599,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2599_1.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\nThe <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=luddites\\\">Luddites</a> had it all wrong; machines are a beautiful thing. Eccentric as that statement might seem, its a rare child that isn\\\'t on the verge of being utterly transfixed when walking past heavy machinery. I remember once, as a child, going to visit a water-wheel-powered mill; I sat and watched the looms and spindles cycle, the warp caressing the weft. I sat for what approached an hour or so; it seems that my 7 year old attention span outlasted that of my 37 year old mother. I was transfixed by the machine.<br><br>\r\nDaily, I pass countless scores of children who seem drawn by some uncharted force to machinery. The springtime-afternoon park is a place where lots of landscaping, patching and road repair is happening; along with such work comes bulldozers and steamrollers, flatbed trucks and bucket loaders. The children flock to these. When freshly off duty at 5pm, the children climb on the machines, all careful to avoid the still-warm-to-the-touch exhaust stacks, all wary (thanks to their admonishing parents) of grease and dirt. Perhaps children have a sixth sense, one that appeals to the scent of diesel.<br><br>\r\nToday, I was walking down 5th Avenue. There at 68th street, major road-patching was happening. Awakened in me was some latent curiosity, some fixation. I walked over to the working back-hoe as it enchanted, disregarding the workers\\\' curios stares as I palmed the dense corroded steel of the machines, my oblong reflection in the shiny hydraulics, the matte sun-absorbing surface around the grease-fittings. The dump-truck pouring asphalt was equipped with a set of four telescoping hydraulics. I watched the steam jet out of the binding rings as the truck raised its bed. I watched the mirror-gleaming hydraulic rods appear from within each other, telescoping, raising, forcing the bucket to lay a fresh cubic ton of steamy, stinky pavement.<br><br>\r\nI palmed the side of the truck as it lowered. Its curious that I was allowed to approach the working machines so closely, but I registered a definitive look of sympathy from the sweaty workers. At first, I thought it was their resignation to the whiles of a passing eccentric; then, I realized it was the look of men wishing to share my enchantment, the fickle ability to love the very machines they earned their paycheck on; the return to that child-like naivety that allows them to find an energy within the hydraulics and moving parts, the enchantment of the mechanical, the soul of the machine. '),(2600,'Spring: The cherry <br>\r\nblossoms<br>\r\n<br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â burst <br>\r\ninto linens<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYou come <br>\r\n<br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â downstairs<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwearing <br>\r\ntheir fragrance<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSummer: So humid:<br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â your mouth under my dress<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAutumn: Our morning robes <br>\r\noff in a pile <br>\r\nof leaves<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOur afternoon<br>\r\nsweaters beside ciders  <br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe scent <br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â of evening is cedar<br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â closets (full-<br>\r\nÂ Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â Â bodied)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWinter: Weâ€™ll wear anything to keep <br>\r\nwarm where there is <br>\r\nice or<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhere there is <br>\r\neyesore<br>\r\n'),(2604,'<p><tt>This medication is making me feel drowsy. I spent all day yesterday staring blankly into the shadows of my dimly lit antique mock-georgian hallway from my vantage point abreast a comfy armchair. The seams are coming apart as the stitching begins to perish. Rotting. Decomposing just like we all do; every minute of every day. There is a patch on one of the arms, perhaps hiding a errant cigratte burn. The threads of my consciousness spiral backwards, unpicking themselves as they head toward an earlier, happier time. A time when i wasn\\\'t afraid. Wine, women and song were the order of the day. Fear was a concept foreign to me.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>My mind jumps in fits and starts. I am experiencing what you might call flashbacks. My akward sense of selfless pity boundlessly spinning out of control. Hurtling toward a abstract car wreck, in which i am only fatality. 2 seconds to impact and it\\\'s my 18th birthday. I wake to find a large Samoan gentleman shouting at me. Something about his daughter\\\'s honour. I feel ashamed as my dreams slowly trickle into the bedsheets. I remember the way my father used to beat me with a rusty steak hook till i was bruised and bloodied. My promiscuous mother sobbing into her morning coffee as she brushes away the dried tears from the night before. It felt good killing him.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Carla says i should chill out. It\\\'s not all that bad right? She says i have friends. Who? Alan, my local health worker? I really don\\\'t think he likes me. He\\\'s a very boring man. The blonde chick in the convenience store? Sure she always greets me with a wide toothy smile, she has one of those mouths with a high gum/tooth ratio, i can tell from the way she conducts herself in my prescence that i make her nervous. They say babies only really smile when they\\\'re scared. A kind of defence mechanism. Perhaps the same is true of adults. I would not like to be friends with the blonde girl from the convenience store, though i am happy to have made her aquaintance.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Last night, as Carla was leaving, she revealed to me that she had the accumulated semen of four men still swimming around inside of her from an encounter earlier that evening. She told me she was wearing tight underwear to keep it all in. Apparently she likes the feeling. I told Carla that she will die from AIDS by the time she\\\'s forty. She just laughed and pushed past me.</tt></p>'),(2601,'<p><tt>Carla came round last night. She was wearing the same old blue roll neck she always does. It fits her in all the right places, hugging her ample bosom like a joey to it\\\'s marsupial mother. It was a cold evening and it showed. Carla doesn\\\'t wear bra\\\'s. She says they restrict her femininity. I told Carla her breasts will be hanging by her waist by the time she forty. She just laughed and pushed past me. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Me and Carla are not an item if that\\\'s what you\\\'re thinking. No. We\\\'re just friends. We watched Sex and the City, drank some beer, popped some amyls. They\\\'re sold on my local news stand as <i>\\\"Room Odourisers\\\"</i>. I always imagine the look of confusion/surprise/fear/delight on an old couple\\\'s face as they sample the fragrance of their new room odouriser only to witness their ass holes dilating widly. I told Carla about my visit to the health center. It seems Carla and Alan know each other. In the biblical sense. It\\\'s a small world. Alan never struck me as Carla\\\'s type. Alan is a dull, dull man. No sense of humour. Carla said she agrees totally. Apparently Alan has <i>other</i> talents. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Carla brought me a book entitled \\\"Modern Lovemaking for Modern Satisfaction Volume II\\\" I didn\\\'t get chance to read it yet. I\\\'ve been busy. People watching takes up alot of my time. Perhaps this habit stems from my lonely chilhood years spent idly masturbating into copies of <i>Health and Efficiency</i> whilst flagrantly hiding in the bushes outside the local Catholic Girls School at kicking out time. I spent more time in those bushes than i did at my own school. Wrenheaton Comprehensive. What a dump. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>I left school with no qualifications. None at all. I spent my late teens/early twenties on a drug fuelled roller coaster of good times and bad trips. I visited the highest of highs and the lowest pits of utter crapola. My week long ether binges would have surely put and end to the story of Manny Swindle if it hadn\\\'t been for that kind hearted soul Carla Mezzatesta.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Carla is my shining star. My guiding light.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2602,'i thought we\\\'d invented it. that we were the first in the history of mankind. oh the certainty of a woman in love. but yes, i really believed that it was the two of us with our just created universe...<br><br>\r\n...the silver ghosts of wishes we give to each other through our mouths; the many ways to eat a pomegranate in private; the different secret codes for requesting a kiss; the words hidden in the fingerprint swirls of our hands that flower into being only at the touch of the other\\\'s bare skin; the new sounds we made to share the pools of fire within our bodies, the violent pain that tugs at a place just beneath our skulls; the inexplicable needs which cause people around us to wonder if they know who we are anymore...<br><br>\r\n...but there it was, staring up at me, in existence before we even met each other, already named. idioglossia. to be precise:<br><br>\r\nthe secret language between two people;<br><br>\r\nthe babble of babies; or the murmur of lunatics.\r\n'),(2603,'if you do it slowly and carefully, you can actually reach in and touch the soft eggy part of your eyeball. you have to give your brain ample warning though, speak to it in a soft authoritative voice, like a doctor, maybe, or a judge, calmly explaining to your body what you\\\'re going to do to it in advance. you make a half-whisper, hope out loud that your eyes don\\\'t slam shut before you can get your finger in there. it is kinda cool, actually, to have nature\\\'s built in missile defence shield, right within your own skull.<br><br> \r\nIt feels, it feels strange to touch the surface of your eye. it feels soft, slightly damp, solid but liquid at the same time. and your eyelid flutters crazily, a drunken butterfly. you can feel your own eyelashes brushing your knuckle, and you remember the time a small brown sparrow flew into the living room last summer, and got trapped near the cherrywood bookcase. and immediately, your hand remembers the exact feel of the bird\\\'s terrified wing beats, as if somehow that same sparrow was here again. <br><br>\r\nBut you\\\'re spooking yourself now, so you take your finger out from your eye, and examine your face in the mirror once more. one eye is perfectly normal. slightly odd-looking, this close, but still sorta normal. a blood vessel must have burst in the other, you decide, staring at the white part surrounding your pupil, the part that now looks like bloody snow. <br><br>\r\nAt first you think your left eye is melting. but then you realise it is just your tear ducts. and you smile. you don\\\'t know why you\\\'re smiling, exactly, it just seems funny. your pupils are so small, so dark, like little tunnels. <br><br>\r\nThat the whole world can fit into something the size of marbles amuses you. that you use your eyes to see your eyes amuses you even more. you can\\\'t really describe this to anyone, even yourself. but it is funny nevertheless. though your eye is still hurting. in fact, when a friend sees you, the first thing she says, even before \\\'hello\\\' is \\\'want me to take you to the doctor?\\\' <br><br>\r\nAnd all you do is smile.\r\n\r\n'),(2605,'<p><tt>Iâ€™ve always had a thing for schoolgirls. It was raining this morning, the day is dull and lifeless. A metaphor for my soul you could say. Spots of rain still cling to the windows, splitting this dull panorama into a hundred thousand microcosms of tedium. I spent most of the morning surreptitiously pondering the merits of pleated versus non-pleated skirts, whilst observing the groups of young girls arriving at the Catholic school across the road. My eventual conclusion was that the more important issue was that of length rather than that of pleats. Less is definitely more.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>My reverie was eventually broken by the ringing of the phone. The ringer on my phone is faulty, it sounds like a duck being slowly strangled to death beside a muddy riverbank. I answered, still pondering over the merits of knee socks and PE knickers. It was Carla. It seems that everyoneâ€™s favourite health worker, Alan, had found out about Carlaâ€™s little Menage Ã  Quatre and wants nothing more to do with her. Carla said she was coming over.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Carla showed up around noon, big black panda eyes, smudged lipstick, she looked a mess. She was somewhat distant. I thoght I might lighten the mood by discussing my theories on schoolgirl apparel. Carla says skirts are a chauvinistic construct of an outdated male-dominated society in collapse. I told Carla that sheâ€™ll wish she flaunted her youthful body more, by the time sheâ€™s forty. She just laughed and pushed past me.</tt></p>\r\n'),(2606,'<P>i am very ready to go home to a bed in san francisco.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>have you ever gone someplace for no reason?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>that is the strange feeling that i get when i go home everyday.  it is always dark when i get there.   i spend a few minutes looking at mail or the television. then i move on to some forced activity to dull the drone.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i know the same things happen all over no matter where you or i live.  however <i>i</i> have to travel extremely far.  no one could walk on foot what i drive.  if they never stopped, they could not catch up with me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>don\\\'t know what all this stuff is out there.  i hear it rattling and junking up.  strangers are yelling about the latest technology or chemical combination of elements.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>FANTASY</B></P>\r\n<P>i woke up this morning at 8:30 because the sun was too bright to continue sleep.  i took a shower and put some outside clothes on.  my blind fold sits on the door knob.  the last thing before walking out the door is PUTTING THE BLIND FOLD ON, NEVER LEAVE WITHOUT THE BLINDFOLD.  and do not forget the walking stick.  it is instrumental in getting where i need to go.  if i forget that, i am lost and will never find my way.  probably get run over trying to get to the coffee shop.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>i try to get past that fantasy but who could want more than to not want anything at all?</P>'),(2607,'<p><tt>Carla and I spent all day yesterday sitting in my darkened living room. She is depressed. I am depressed, but this you know already. My life has become a perpetual carousel of the dull and mundane from which I cannot escape. I am the horse shackled to my humdrum existence with rusty carriage-bolts, my non-conforming lead paint paring off, my wooden torso rotting away as I gallop aimlessly into eternity. The music plays on. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Carla stayed the night. She took my bed and I slept on the red velour sofa in the living room, which is remarkably comfortable considering itâ€™s condition. I bought it five years ago at an auction in Bexley Heath, it was in bad shape even then, with a big tear down one side. It was peppered with cigarette burns and miscellaneous stains, like the craggy, weathered face of an elderly gentleman. What tales could he tell? What sights, what occurrences has he been witness to? He cost me Â£25. I was the only bidder. </tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>Iâ€™ve been awake since 6.30AM. Carla is still sleeping. Iâ€™m gonna watch TV.</tt></p>\r\n'),(2608,'<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<P>dear peter (et all):</P><P>i\\\'m afraid i\\\'m not going to be able to make it to new york this weekend. i\\\'ve decided to enter a residential treatment program to try to curb my alcohol, drug, and gambling problems and the havoc they create in my life and in the lives of my family, friends, and bank balance.</P><P>flaubert said something to effect of \\\"be stable and ordinary in your life that you may be wild and original in your work.\\\" i still think my soul looks like the watercolors in \\\"the joy of life,\\\" but i have to find new ways to appreciate, express, and sustain its more feral qualities. i have to learn how to handle my life more gently. how to be kinder to myself.</P><P>thank you for the offer of putting me up. maybe i\\\'ll take you up on it this summer or fall. it remains to be seen if there will ever be a day when i smoke the occaisonal joint or have the odd beer without being dependant on them. maybe i just need to chill out a little bit. thank you for your compassion.</P>\r\n</BLOCKQUOTE>'),(2609,'<p>\r\na month or so ago, peter and i ran into a man i know at mission grounds in san francisco. we live here together in this hilly city, me, peter, this man, the people at mission grounds. we all live in this city together. we all know about the hills here.\r\n</p><p>\r\n sometimes i see this same man in my yoga class or in a cafe. i know some things about him. i know he writes, i know he\\\'s a sagittarius, i know he has an endearing stutter, i know sometimes he stops and knocks on the door of my other friend and asks her to come eat food with him. i have walked with him along the esplanade at burning man.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthat morning i was eating a second breakfast, actually, i had just wolfed a dessert crepe and it was something of a behavioral anomaly for me. nutella and banana.\r\n</p><p>\r\nas we sat with this man we know, ( though peter would probably not say he knows this man, even though they had a lively discussion about comic books that morning \r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n( <br>\r\nso lively that right after we left the mission grounds, we ended up at al\\\'s comics on guerrero, buying comics so as to retroactively fulfill the truth behind what we had been saying during that discussion, that we like alan moore, that we like mister mixilplikt.\r\n<br>\r\n)\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\n), the sun was shining in on us, into the window of mission grounds. the man we know told us: my friend has spent some years working on this eva hesse exhibit at sfmoma, i am obliged to go to the opening of the exhibit, to honor the work she has done. i made a quizzical face because i don\\\'t know much about eva hesse. the man explained some things to me. i don\\\'t remember. except some words: <i> german, emotional, lumped in with sylvia plath, female</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nlater, much later, as late as last night, i spoke with another person i know about the eva hesse exhibit. some words from this person: <i>it made me want to hurl</i> and then <i> the box full of tubes was amusing though</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand so i had all this input prior to this eva hesse exhibit at sf moma. someone else said to me: it is heralded as one of the best art exhibits in the country right now.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni must tell you now that i spent the last two days doing things surrounding one good spell of vomiting. i did things leading up to the vomiting (holding gut, moaning, being delerious) and then did things recovering from the vomiting (laying in bed, experimenting with food intake). today is my first day emancipated from doing service to the vomiting, so on a day like this, it\\\'s all art to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n(<br>\r\naside: <br>\r\n*le* hey yeti<br>\r\n*le* how do you spell mixilplikt<br>\r\n*yeti* MXYZPTLK!#@@!#$~~!@$$<br>\r\n)\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p><p>\r\n(as an editor, i feel my article could end here. if you feel the same way, you can stop reading now)\r\n</p><p>\r\nso i spent my first free post-illness day perusing the bizarre sculptural formations of one miss eva \\\"i\\\'m such a german\\\" hesse. i did this activity with someone i don\\\'t know very well but have known-of-known-not-really-known-liked-though for some time, who comes from boston (land of persecution). this person was very gentle with me in my post-sick state. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwe wanted to touch the sculptures. there were lots of  tactile-looking things, strings protruding out of canvas, fiberglass sculpture forests. organic shapes of inorganic materials. i thought of egg in moments, when i saw string sculptures and rope sculptures. oh i ached to know what egg would tell me about eva hesse.\r\n</p><p>\r\neva hesse died of a brain tumor. do you think it was from spending many hours playing with molten fiberglass?\r\n</p><p>\r\nother insights from today: sixth street is really too much. both \\\"ananda fuara\\\" and \\\"somaz cafe\\\" are closed on sundays. sarah got her hair cut. nothing is the same once you decide it\\\'s different, and what your friends think about art may or may not apply to you. also, i\\\'m not sure edward weston was meant to photograph people.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2610,'<br>\r\nOne of us leaves the other<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;everyday.<br>\r\nWho does<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;is unclear,<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;but she is the one <br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;who calls.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This time<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;he&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;is with her.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe silence&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;following her voice <br>\r\nis so&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;vast<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;and empty<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI can hear through it,<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;until it becomes<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;a crescendo of static,<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;a wall of no-sound<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;that drowns out her next words.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI have offered&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;in reply,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf only<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;her words<br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;could stay.<br>'),(2611,'<p>\r\n<b>incubation</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\na migraine begins when i\\\'ve neglected some body maintenance item. this\r\nusually means no morning coffee, or i\\\'ve waited too long to eat.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>self destructive behavior</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nnow i\\\'m reloading stupid websites. my shoulders are at ear altitude\r\nand my spinal column has taken the shape of a question mark. in other\r\nwords, i\\\'m sitting like the sweaty hunched over guy in the ergonomics\r\nchapter of the macintosh manual. his caption says \\\"NO\\\" in bold. next\r\nto him is the uptight secretary who has perfectly adjusted her\r\nworkspace to meet modern health and safety standards. i am not her. i\r\nam the other guy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nback pain becomes neck pain becomes head pain. but i ignore all\r\nwarning signs and remain stationed behind the computer, reloading\r\nlivejournal repeatedly. the computer desk is a self-abuse theme park.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>light and sound</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nall light becomes blinding. i feel every photon like a nail gun\r\nagainst my eye socket. i bury my head in the nearest opaque object to\r\nblock out the light. then i release a small mewing sound so that\r\nolivia knows i need her to do something. she is good about these\r\nthings because she also gets intense migraines. i point at the light\r\nswitch and grumble. this means \\\"sweetie could you please turn off the\r\nlight? i have a bad headache\\\" but it sounds more like \\\"nnrrrghhh -\r\ni can\\\'t - could you - too bright\\\". it is also at this point that my\r\neyes water and turn red.\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe will still be typing on her computer. this is when all sound\r\nbecomes deafening. each clack-clack from her keyboard reverberates\r\ninside my skull like the footsteps of godzilla destroying neotokyo.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>inflated need for physical contact</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am very simple: i need petting. lots and lots of petting. in fact i\r\nbecome very needy, and when not cursing the pain, i curse myself for\r\nbeing intolerable. i also construct and surround myself with elaborate\r\npillow structures. sometimes i\\\'ll convince the cat to be where i am.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>reconsidering rejected painkillers</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nlooking over the leaflet from my imitrex prescription, i am reminded that\r\n\\\"In rare cases, people have suffered seizures after taking Imitrex.\\\"\r\nno thanks, i\\\'ll sit this one out.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>trepanation</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthen i wonder about amanda fielding. do you think that hole in her\r\nhead really helps decrease fluid pressure against her brain? i wonder\r\nhow it would feel to drill a hole in my skull. i replay my memories of\r\nwatching her drill herself on video.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>pathetic appeals to a higher power</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis begins as vague promises to do yoga again. the only thing that\r\nstops me from going is habit. but going to yoga means my life is\r\nmigraine free so why don\\\'t i go? because i\\\'m fucking lazy, that\\\'s\r\nwhy. which means i deserve what i get. (and this is where my train of\r\nthought includes existential speculation.) i am lazy so my inaction\r\nresults in pain. is it self-inflicted or inflicted by the universe,\r\nand to what extent is this the same thing. if i am part of the\r\nuniverse, i at least deserve a vote. on the other hand, how can i\r\nclaim to hold reverence for all sentient life if i can\\\'t even be\r\nfucked to treat my own body properly. (anyway this continues on until i\r\nremember that there is ashtanga at the yoga place down the hill. i\r\ncommit to \\\"really go this time\\\".)\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>resolution</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nat some point it goes away. it could be an hour or two days later. i\r\nhave heard of salt or caffeine or sex as possible migraine cures. i\r\nhave heard all the folklore. what i really want instead is a heads up\r\ndisplay to replace the pain mechanism. it would be so rocking to be\r\na cyborg with blinking red lights and assorted readouts just\r\nbeyond your field of vision. beep boop whrr.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2612,'<p>\r\ni have a recurring dream about the same apartment. i\\\'ve never been inside it, never seen an apartment like it, it only appears in my dreams. it\\\'s boxy, tiny, a basement apartment. the door is in the middle of the wall with stairs down into the recessed living room.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the dreams there are always boundary issues with the place. it\\\'s missing a wall, or there\\\'s a door into someone else\\\'s apartment and the other person keeps coming in. in one of the dreams, someone kept putting trash and junk through the shared door.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlast night, the scene in the apartment was that i had left town for some reason. while i was gone, a person broke in and killed someone in one of the rooms. i wasn\\\'t the killer and i didn\\\'t know the deceased. the killer left the dead body in my bed. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni never see the body and i never see the mess of the killing firsthand, though someone else tells me all about it. how hard it was to clean up, and how they had to have the body hauled away. as the person tells me i can see their memories of the event in my own mind.\r\n\r\nmy room is cordoned off now, police line, do not cross. i visit my bed, the scene of the crime. i examine the blankets for blood or evidence of the killing but there is none.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncut to another location, same dream. i am in safeway with an old friend. he is yelling at me and i at him. the subject of the fight is this killing in my apartment. he doesn\\\'t believe what i say about the killing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nlater, i am in a bathroom with thousands of stalls. everything is stainless steel and it\\\'s very brightly lit. none of the sinks or toilets seem to work. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2613,'\r\n'),(2614,'<p>yesterday was one of those days when nostalgia sneaks into the brain unbidden, images from the past spilling into my present-day consciousness like my now-ness had sprung a leak.  this is a mostly pleasant occurrence, a springtime occurrence.  things get stirred up in the spring. everything smells fresh and new, the air is heavy with potential.  the trees in the park are blossoming feverishly, tearing off their clothes into the breeze.  riding my bike through all of this yesterday, i remembered what it was like being younger and not having such weight in my chest.  at the time, of course, problems i did have seemed plenty horrendous, but in retrospect my heart was light.  strange.  i had a sense of my heart in my body now as being older, having a sense of the gravity of life, the strength of will and of shoulders that it takes now to hold myself up under it.</p>\r\n<p>remembered thing: a scene from germany, when i lived there as a young woman and used to jog out in the green rolling farmlands, the sky almost always dark and ominous, listening to commercial radio and breathing in the silence outside my headphones.  the trees were bare and black and there were berry bushes with vivid red berries and light-filled water droplets all over them.  i remember coming over a rise of green and seeing the elderly couple i lived with walking toward me in the wind.  they both had long coats on and he had a walking stick.  they waved at me as they billowed along the road.  i felt all of a sudden like i had found myself jogging in the Shire.</p>\r\n<p>at the beach yesterday, watching people fly kites in the long afternoon. hypnotized by the sinuous tail in the wind, i felt a beautiful, sad longing.  <i>sehnsucht</i>.  a wish to see that which is not there.  comforting feel of working feet into the sand.  being at the oceanside soothes me like nothing else.  the waves gently cajole my breath into regularity, the soft colors of sky and sand and water blend together to lull me into beer-craving turpitude...after i drank the beer, though, i was ready to ride again, literally like a maniac. poised on the seat, foot cocked in the toeclip, looking sideways at my lover, i had a moment of hysteria: \\\"the past is behind me. i\\\'m going to ride <i>away</i> from the past now.  it\\\'s not in front of me, it\\\'s behind me.  i\\\'m riding into the <i>future</i>.\\\"  somewhere in the middle of this statement i had burst into tears. i started pedaling as fast as  i could, hearing his mumbled \\\"whew...psycho,\\\" fade into the past as i jettisoned myself insanely down the path, lungs burning satisfactorily, blossomed trees whizzing past in a blur of white.</p>\r\n<p>today, i don\\\'t know what time it is.  i\\\'m confused and dreamy.  i woke up with a full-on urge, determination even, to take modern dance classes.  i don\\\'t know if i dreamed about dancing, or whether it\\\'s just time, or what, but my body is calling to Terpsichore (or is she calling to me?) i quickly found a place and time where i can <a href=\\\"http://www.linesballet.org\\\">indulge my whim</a>.  '),(2615,'It was another balmy Georgia day.  Alexandra Merriweahter was engaged in her favourite past time teasing and flirting with George Hathilwayn.  George was blonde, nineteen and lived on another plantation not far from Eden, Alexandra\\\'s father\\\'s cotton planation. \r\n<BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"Why, George Hathiwayn! I bet that even if you could get a girl all alone, you wouldn\\\'t be brave enough to kiss her, so you can stop with all this...this... tomfoolery!\\\" exclaimed Alexandra, playfully taping her beau on the arm with her fan.  <BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"Well you know Alexandra, we\\\'re all alone now\\\".  <BR>\r\n\r\nGeorge leaned closer to her, moving his arm along the back of the loveseat that they were sharing.  She looked around.  It was true that there was no one in sight, but she could hear the darkies in the house setting the table for lunch.  Besides, although it was only noon, she could smell whiskey on George\\\'s breath and she hated to kiss a man who\\\'d been drinking.  But he was a very well looking man, having inherited his nordic good looks from his Norwegian grandmother.  His piercing blue eyes never failed to thrill her, but in her heart she know she was not in love with him.  <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nShe had grown up with George, but his father had sent him to boarding school when he was ten, and she\\\'d been sent to finishing school when she was thirteen.  Then, when she\\\'d returned at the age of fifteen, George had grown into an experianced man, while she herself had blossomed out and was on her way to becoming a beautiful woman.  Her black hair cascaded to her waist when the bluey strands were not caught up by a ribbon, and her flashing green eyes sparkled with the intensity of a vivacious explorer.  But it was not just her beauty that drew men to her in droves like moths to a flame.  Nor was it her luscious ripe body, firm breasts and a tiny waist that was reduced to nothingness by her wide skirts.  Men were attracted to her beacuase of the way that she thought it was all just a big adventure created just for her.  They were lured on by her coquettish tricks, laughing softly so that a man had to walk over to her to ask what was so funny, and fluttering her eyelids quickly, so that men focused on her sparkling emerald eyes. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nToday she was dressed in a bright blue muslim dress and a straw bonnet.  The dress\\\'s skirts were frilled and spread over wide hoops, and its sleeves were puffed and ended half way between her shoulder and her elbow.  George couldn\\\'t take his eyes off the creamy white swells that peeped daringly over the top of Alexandra\\\'s shockingly low cut bodice.  Although  he had seen plenty of black breasts among the slaves, and plenty of white breasts in the town whore houses,  there was something particularly enticing about Alexandra\\\'s womanly treasures.  None of the other girls in the county dared show so much, or at least their mothers wouldn\\\'t let them.  But Alexandra\\\'s mother had died in childbirth when she\\\'d been seven, and so she had only her black mammy for guidance.  And Alexandra\\\'s persuasiveness was well known throughout the area.  Indeed, sitting together on this beautiful sunny day, he suddently realised just how much he loved her.  <BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"Alex,\\\" he said breathlessly, \\\"will you marry me?\\\" <BR>\r\n\r\nAlexandra gasped.  No! It shouldn\\\'t be him asking! He was her best friend, her favourite beau, but as a husband?  Never had her heart been stirred by his touch.  Never had he taken her breaht away, except for now.  She sighed.  How could she say no without breaking his heart?\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nSuddenly she heard a mighty outburst of noise.  A second later her brother Nicholas came galloping up the driveway, his horse lathered with sweat.  <BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"War!\\\" he cried, \\\"we\\\'re going to fight the Yankees!\\\" <BR>\r\n\r\nGeorge scrambled to his feet.  \\\"what, is that really true?\\\"<BR>\r\n\r\nNicholas swung himself down fron the saddle.  Instantly Tobias the groom rushed out and caught up the reins of the exhausted horse adn led  him to the stables.  <BR>\r\n\r\n\\\"I\\\'ve just come frm the telegraph office in town.  Abe Lincoln himself called for volunteers!\\\"  <BR>\r\n\r\nAlexandra was shocked.  How could a war come and disrupt their tranquil life? '),(2616,'<p>\r\nI crossed over the border from Northern Thailand to Huay Xai, Laos the other day, and decided to take the Mekong route south to Luang Probang. According to travel book sources, you just head down to the dock along Mekong in the morning and talk to one of the cargo boat captains to negotiate a price for passage down the river. Not true! No longer the case! Now that Laos is jam packed full of tourists. Now they have a ticket office (of sorts), charge you up the butt, and put you on a tiny boat with somewhere around 30 other western tourists. By tiny boat, I mean this boat is about 5 feet wide and maybe 50 feet long, the back half is taken up by the engine and living quarters of whoever is running the show (and a little opening over the water in the back you can use as a toilet, which is far more than we have gotten on some journeys in the way of \\\"facilities\\\"). The rest of the boat is lined on either side, with a hard wooden bench. You get to touch at least two other people for the entire ride, or rather, there\\\'s no way not to touch at least two others for the two days of the journey. You rest your back on the hard, sharp corner of the edging of the boat. The scenery is beautiful, if you donâ€™t mind the occasional view of blatant deforestation, causing terrible erosion, pollution of the river, and habitat destruction.  </p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter the first day down the river, they pull into a tiny pit of a town on the side of the Mekong called Pakbeng where you are forced to stay in one of several shoddy guesthouses, and eat at one of only a few crappy restaurants. In a town ON the river, at a restaurant where I could SEE the river, I ordered fish. I was told they were out. This did not seem a good sign. I went to a different restaurant.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe passed the rest of that night drinking the best beer in Southeast Asia, Beer Lao. This is the national beer of Laos, and the Lao drink it morning noon and night. It was one young Australian\\\'s birthday, so I pulled out my travel guitar and a songbook and a few of us sang songs until the lights went out. Pakbeng does not have electricity. They run on generator power and the lights at our particular shoddy guesthouse go out at 10. No candles were offered to us to help us get up the stairs. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The next day was much the same as the previous. A dayâ€™s journey down the Mekong at a slow pace. The occasional speedboat passes at a clip, tourists in shitty helmets, drivers in their shorts and tank tops. Hit the wrong eddy in a speedboat and itâ€™ll crack in half. Run into another speedboat head on while youâ€™re rounding a corner and itâ€™ll crack in half too. The Mekong is dark with mud this time of year, and full from the rainy season. If your boat cracks in half, youâ€™re lost in the murk until you wash up on shore down the river in Vientiane. At a slow pace you run the risk only of being caught in a seemingly gentle whirlpool that slowly pulls and tears at your boat until it breaks apart into a looser collection of wood and machinery and tourists. But our driver kept his eyes open and at last, he brought us to Luang Probang, a very heavily touristed town full of silk hangings, antique opium scales, and silver. The food wasn\\\'t bad, even if the travel was hard. Over all everything is a bit more pleasant than Cambodia, but just as French. A nice benefit to having been a French colony is the availability of French wines and French bread. (Not to be as widely found as French fries however...)</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI spent a lot of time in my head during this part of the journey. But thatâ€™s another post.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith new friends made on the boat, we went to see one of the main tourist attractions about an hour up the road. A beautiful waterfall, so rich in minerals, that the water is bright aquamarine. There are several tiers of falls and at the bottom you can jump in for a swim. The water was freezing so I didn\\\'t make it in past the knee. I have a photo somewhere. Iâ€™ll try to post it.\r\n</p>'),(2617,'<p>It rained today.  The smell of ozone and, strangely enough, candles was in the air.  This has been the first official spring rain so far this year.  Last week the precipitation was a disgusting combination of sleet/snow/slush/hail which signalled the arrival of my birthday.</p>\r\n<p>I have killed.  Many have died as a result of my indiscriminate wrath.  The headlights of the southbound traffic on the Allen Expressway illuminated my crimes.  It wasn\\\'t hard.  I didn\\\'t even break stride and before I knew it dozens had fallen prey to a violent demise as a result of my actions.  I am taking this opportunity to admit these things I have done; confess so that I may be absolved of the guilt which wracks me even as I write this.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s amazing and liberating all at the same time to know that you are powerful enough to make a decision which can result in continued existence, or lack thereof, of another living creature.  You know, the power of Gods and all that?  It\\\'s true.</p>\r\n<p>I guess it was the rain which brought it out in me.  The water droplets were congregating on the lenses of my glasses, making everything appear like it does behind the windows of a car in the rain.  Lights were blurred, appearances dissolved through the refractive blobs.  It was like one of those bad drunk driving commercials where they place empty beer glasses on top of a car to show how drinking impairs your vision/driving skills.</p>\r\n<p>The rain certainly brought my victims to me, of this I am certain.  You see, if you know anything about fishing, you know that the best place to find worms is near when there is a large amount of rain that has recently fallen to the ground.  So with Telus laying cables throughout my neighbourhood, there are large sections of sandy soil where once there was grass.  The pathway along the Allen was littered with worms.  Even if I avoided one, my feet would crush at least two others.  The disproportionate reprecussions of what I thought was an act of kindness.</p>\r\n<p>So I admit it.  It was me.  Tomorrow if the rain is gone and the sidewalks become dry and light in colour, the remains (if any) of dead worms along the pathway will be from me.  Yes, I am guilty.  I was the one responsible.  Please don\\\'t condemn me for my actions.</p>\r\n<p>The only thing is, it\\\'s supposed to rain through until tomorrow afternoon.  Maybe I should just stay inside.  I don\\\'t know if I can control these murderous urges any longer.</p>'),(2618,'<p>Cries to the deep of the crowd. For me! For me! Would it have cut as deep as your wound if I had not left it alone.</p>'),(2619,'<i>\\\"I need to find a Kosher deli\\\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nThe slightly distorted voice on the other end of the phone belong to my Father. I immediately started wondering if my Father has decided on some sort of strategic maneuver in the grand spiritual struggle. My immediate family has never been very religious or spiritual for that matter with myself as the exception. I was a really pious christian from birth (as early as I can remeber) to about grade seven, going to church on my own of my own accord sorta thing with out the parental units or the sista. Highschool saw a conversion to paganism (of a druidic tradition) based more on a reverence for things celtic, I somehow felt that I had been brought up in a religion not of my background (I know this is taking things back a little far but I was a kid). Now I find myself struggling along in what is best described as humanism with a bit of lip service to paganism as far as iconography goes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnyhow, it turned out that my Father wasn\\\'t converting to Judaism at all. He was simply going to bring up some supplies for his friend in Thule, Greenland on his way to CFS Alert (the most northernly point of human civilization in case any one cares, and part of the NORAD infrastructure). Turns out there is this young woman in Thule who is originally from Sudan then emigrated to Denmark became a citizen and is now working for the government in Thule. She has dietary restrictions and food shipments only come in once a year so she has been wasting away up there surviving on what little she can, very devout lady my Father tells me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>\\\"I got a name of some guys butcher shop but do you know of a Kosher Deli close to were you live?\\\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>\\\"Sure, I can find something before you come to pick me up and we can grab what you need before we leave.\\\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nNow, it isn\\\'t not like I or my Father are particularly ignorant or intolerant of other religions but, we do tend to kinda have an attitude leaning towards religion/spirituality being a personal thing and that it should be  kept that way. Eases friction, try to relate to individuals instead of their indentity groups. This would be the reason that we did not realize the cultural faux pas we were about to make.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMy Father\\\'s friend is actually a young Muslim woman not a young Jewish woman he knew this but, didn\\\'t know that Muslims have Halal where as Jews have Kosher . This explains why the name my Father has written down is Halal. All my Father wanted to do was bring this women in Thule some food she will eat. What did we end up doing? Well because I got caught up in other activities I didn\\\'t track down the Kosher deli. My Father upon arrival had me calling a bunch of numbers he had gotten out of the phone book. I end up calling a variety of Halal meat shops and my father and I take turns asking in the voice of completely ignorant and earnest people, simply out to try and help another human being out -<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>\\\"Do you have any Kosher meats?\\\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nGiven the current geopolitical climate the responses to our query make sense now that I know what Halal and Kosher mean and the different faiths attributed to each. It was only after my Father got a little impatient and explained the situation to one fellow over the phone that the guy at one of the meat shops told him he had what we were looking for. We didn\\\'t get an explaination of our faux pas though, I checked this out after the fact when I thought to myself we recieved a rather strange response at the meat shop.<br><br>\r\n\r\n I hope my Father\\\'s friend enjoys her minced lamb. Me I\\\'m not so big on the meat. <br><br>'),(2620,'<p>Tossing and turning... thought\\\'s trying to fill my head.  Imaging sham, deceit, foul play... I\\\'m being unfair.</p>\r\n<p>I want it all to be un-true so I can perform the final execution with no guilt of my own fault.</p>\r\n<p>I hate distance, I hate cravings, I hate particularly that these hates of mine, I bury away.</p>'),(2621,'<p>\r\ntonight, in  an irish pub somewhere on the other side of the bay, i was momentarily lost completely to live music. as i looked down toward the floor, eyes as slits, last night\\\'s dream came to me. here it is.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am with a large group of people. a party, or gathering. we are indoors, it is brightly lit. round glasses filled with red punch sit on a white table. someone is wearing a plaid shirt.\r\n</p><p>\r\nat some point, we all begin to walk outside into the night. we walk and walk. there is comradery and joy. it is festive. no one is dressed for the out of doors. no one carries a bag or backpack. this is not a journey, only a short walk. but it seems we walk for a long time.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe sky, rather than being a sky, is a field of drying molten lava. or perhaps it is the blackest clouds you can imagine with the reddest sun shining from behind. black clouds lined with bright red. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwe reach our destination. there is a sculpture or monument here, in a field, made of orange lights arranged carefully in a pattern.  they are fire, then they are electric lights, then they are fire again. it\\\'s a pillar of sorts, but wide and rounded. we gather around it. \r\n</p><p>\r\nan old man appears. he says to us: <i>i knew you would all walk toward the sun.</i> he laughs and is pleased with himself. but it\\\'s not because he doesn\\\'t care about us, or because he\\\'s tricked us. it\\\'s because he knows he was right. \r\n</p><p>\r\nfin.\r\n</p>'),(2622,'<p>About 6 months I was evicted :(</p>\r\n<p>So I moved myself into a VERY EXPENSIVE last minute Bed & Breakfast and quickly searched for a new place to rent.</p>\r\n<p>The fruits of my search brought me this damn mouldy disgusting house which I am binded to by contract for 6 months</p>\r\n<p>It does however come with a garden which I have discovered is the local hot spot for the local cats..... it\\\'s spring need I say more.  Imagine yourself at 2am in the morning, trying your damned hardest to work and there\\\'s several couple of cats  right outside the damn patio copulating with cat yowls.  It sounds as if someone\\\'s in pain.</p>'),(2623,'<p>About 6 months I was evicted :(</p>\r\n<p>So I moved myself into a VERY EXPENSIVE last minute Bed & Breakfast and quickly searched for a new place to rent.</p>\r\n<p>The fruits of my search brought me this damn mouldy disgusting house which I am binded to by contract for 6 months</p>\r\n<p>It does however come with a garden which I have discovered is the local hot spot for the local cats..... it\\\'s spring need I say more.  Imagine yourself at 2am in the morning, trying your damned hardest to work and there\\\'s several couple of cats  right outside the damn patio copulating with cat yowls.  It sounds as if someone\\\'s in pain.</p>'),(2624,'i\\\'m telling you things i\\\'ve never told anyone else. the word, that unspeakable word, flutters in my throat, like a tangible thing. this isn\\\'t some fancy metaphor, you know. the word, the physical manifestation of the word, is real. i can feel the remnants of that old pain, brushing my neck. pain. not your hands. pain. oh i\\\'m tired of this! where are you now? i want to be able to forgive you for not being here, for driving me to seek solace in useless things like the curling orange leaves i walked through to the library, or the whiteness of the sky, and sometimes i can. but not today.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni constantly judge beauty against utility. i attempt to measure autumn, as if a girl could ever quantify this season, this love. they are the same thing to me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nbut autumn is disappearing. the maples and elms are heedless of my soft sad warnings...they spill their leaf-petals carelessly anyway. holly showed me how the trees in Meadowlands look like giant blossoms now. we walk among them as if we were miniature girls in a marvellous garden. she listed the names. \\\'see that maple tree? that\\\'s really an enormous carnation.\\\' i can\\\'t remember all the names now. goldfingers? lillies? it doesn\\\'t matter.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nthe trees. they aren\\\'t sentient, are they? they seem to be. they seem to be standing firm in their belief that it is good to give, give, give and keep giving, they pour out the entire contents of their hearts, caressing the ground with leaf upon leaf. they seem so sure they will always have enough, that more will come, that there is no point in hoarding. they don\\\'t worry. or maybe they don\\\'t even think about the future. maybe only we do. but how can we not? every second that whizzes by is one second closer to dying. we humans are aware of death. we know we are going to die. we just don\\\'t know when.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni didn\\\'t try to catch the snow on my tongue today. i just stood under the green canopy near Mackenzie and prayed. it had been so long since i had last prayed that it took me a while to find the words, and then to search for the recipient of those words.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nusually i pray to God-in-the-sky-somewhere. of course God is present everywhere. so this morning i looked at the ground. i sent my eyes deep deep down into the sweet-smelling earth. the fallen red and brown and yellow leaves were starting to melt and rot. how could decay smell so good? (your parable about love -- about dying brain matter smelling like flowers and creating love -- haunts me.)<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nit might be blasphemous, i suppose, to pray while looking in the direction hell might be. but i keep sinning, even when i try desperately to be good. i think maybe i am never going to be able to be sweet and right. no matter how much i try. so why should i keep struggling? isn\\\'t it futile? but my lips moved anyway. i needed to pray. i held out my hands in front of me. i held them out over the earth. i prayed.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\non sunday we went to the Gatineau National Park. we hiked around Pink Lake. the water was so still and green i was convinced i could step out onto it. yes, i know. i couldn\\\'t. but i thought it possible. the fall rhapsody around us, the entire forest changing, i was in ecstasy. my sisters snickered at my exclamation of joy, when we turned round the bend to catch a glimpse of the great fire of the sun floating in the dark green waves. they reached out and pulled me into their arms, smiling. i was glad they were with me. but oh how i missed you. i tried to bring you to me, with the sheer fierceness of my desire. but i failed.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nso i tell you what i had to hold in all these years. i tell you that which i will never tell anyone else because saying these things this first time was so hard i didn\\\'t think i could make it to the end, until the end came like a sudden wall rising out of the darkness when a person tries to make their way home in the middle of the night, but they\\\'re hopelessly lost. and they try not to be afraid, they try to open their eyes as wide as they can to see as much as they can, their arms held straight out in front of them, fingers feeling their way around. when it is dark it doesn\\\'t matter that i\\\'m not blind.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni breathed the full stop at the end of my last sentence. my fingers traced my cracked lips. i was as shocked as you were. and afterward, our silence hung like cobwebs over the telephone wire. we stood quietly, letting the wind come. the silk a spider weaves out through its own body is unimaginably strong, you promise me. i want to believe you. but distance constantly devours the fibres.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nyou once said \\\"don\\\'t worry, my lips are wet. you don\\\'t need lip balm.\\\" and since then i\\\'ve stopped carrying my bonne belle. because i am a maniac, afterall. love is temporary, i tell you, and you say hush, don\\\'t think about that. what else am i supposed to think about then? tell me. i am frantic today. my hands keep shaking, my classmates stare, their brows all scrunched up. my lips are so dry they hurt.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t know how to handle this you. i don\\\'t know how to keep myself safe from this you that you are when you are with me. you bind me to you, so tightly, with your gentleness. i grieve. you keep cracking open my wall as if it were a thin shell. you find your way under my skin.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nmy eyes started burning, as my mouth curved upwards. i pushed my chin in, my forehead knocked against the knife corners of the payphone, and i counted the seconds between my breaths. i was breathing less often, less deeply. soon the interval between each breath will be longer than a breath itself. you said i wouldn\\\'t die if you hadn\\\'t called me on friday. maybe. maybe. no doubt breathing is involuntary. eventually i would have to go to the bathroom. sometime, despite my efforts, my eyes would close and i would sleep. but i would still, on one particular level, be dead. dead. <br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\\'desire is at the root of all suffering.\\\' \r\n\r\n'),(2625,'<p>I remember August 3, 1999. I walked into the office supply super-store with criminal intent.  I needed a the stapler but had no money.  I stole the stapler because I needed it to staple the spine of the literary \\\'zine in which I self-published the poetry and short stories of my friends and acquaintances  (the quality of the writing left much to be desired, but I will not open THAT can of worms here). \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was a blue, sunny day.  Wisps of friendly clouds stretched across the sky.  Though it was warm outside and the weatherman had not indicated rain, I wore a long raincoat.  I walked with hunched shoulders and looked my held tilted forward, looking at the speckled linoleum floor.  I maintained a discrete lookout for store employees in their telltale red vests, keeping my head facing straight ahead and darting my eyes back and forth, using peripheral vision to spot any hint of scarlet.  If I saw something red, anything that moved while wearing red, I quickened my pace lest I hear the dread:  â€œIs there anything I can help you with today?â€  I was not approached.  If the staff at this office supply superstore was anywhere as attentive as they seem to be in the commercials, I should have been shuttled off to the police for even thinking about stealing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe aisles were crammed with the useful and superfluous things of the modern office; computers, digital answering machines, PDAs, reams of white paper, file folders, scissors, rulers, briefcases, stacking files, datebook organizers, leather portfolios,  rolls of bubble wrap, Kraft paper, highlighters,  boxes of paperclips, different colored rubber bands.  So much stuff.  At first I felt a little bit at a loss and because my mind raced with the variables of my illicit mission, I was confused, and passed through each aisle twice before finding the one with the staplers.  Once I confirmed the staplersâ€™ whereabouts , I was confronted with another American bounty: electric staplers, hand-held staplers, staplers that could penetrate thick stack of paper, colorful staplers, etc., etc. I stood there, scanning the rows and not really seeing anything.  Finally, I saw what I was looking for on the bottom shelf.  There were two of them.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nI stuffed a box of staples in each pocket and slipped a stapler up each coat sleeve.  They were long and clumsy.  I immediately sensed my mistake.  If I didnâ€™t change the arrangement Iâ€™d have to my escape looking like a robot who needed its joints oiled.  A dead giveaway.  But as I tried to shake the staplers out of my sleeve, two red-vested employees turned down my aisle.  One looked like a young Robert DeNiro, and the other wore Buddy Holly-inspired glasses.  Robert DeNiro carried a brown clipboard with ball-point pen chained to the top.   Their eyes met mine.  Busted. \r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œFinding everything you need today sir?â€  The young Robert DeNiro asked brightly.\r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œUhâ€¦â€  I paused waiting for them to lower the boom.  I was sure that the cops were already on their way.  â€œIâ€™m just looking for computer disks.â€  I managed to say.\r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œOh, sure.â€  Buddy Holly this time.  â€œWe keep those behind the counter.  Otherwise people try and steal them.â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œUhâ€¦â€  What did they know?  Did they know anythingâ€  â€œUhâ€¦thanks.â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nBuddy and Robert stood looking at me for a few minutes as if waiting for me to make a run for it.  I had to restrain myself from doing just that, imagining that they would gun me down, shooting me in the back, mid-sprint, before I even had a chance to get out of the aisle.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œSir?â€  Buddy Holly looked at me pointedly.  His eyes were a bright, trusting shade of blue.  â€œSir, is there anything else we can help you with?â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œUhâ€¦no, thatâ€™s okay.â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nâ€œThen, could you move a little?â€  I was still standing in front of the shelves of staplers.  â€œWe need to inventory this section.â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nHoly shit.  My theft was about to be detected.  As soon as they counted the staplers, theyâ€™d realize that the only two long armed staplers in the store were gone.   â€œWell, thanks a lot.â€  I said and began to move away.  I was convinced that they could see the staplers through my coat and that according to some strange law, they had to wait until I left the store with the stolen goods on my person.  I walked as confidently as I could, not moving my arms, looking to ditch the staplers as soon as I was out of their sight.  Red-vested employees seemed to lurk everywhere now, each one bright and helpful.  â€œIs there anything I can help you with today, sir?â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere was no place I could turn.  The staplers were heavy in my sleeves.  The boxes clearly misshaped the manner in which the fabric fell.  I walked like Frankenstein.  My heart beat wildly.  Beads of sweat trickled down from my hairline and into my eyes.  My eyes stung.  I tried to reach up and wipe the sweat away, but couldnâ€™t because of the staplers.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nAs soon as I stepped outside I felt that the SWAT team would swoop down on me.  I knew it to be true.  If I could just stay inside the store for until I could ditch the staplers Iâ€™d be fine.  But there was nowhere to go.  An unusual number of employees were milling about the store in pairs, each one carrying a brown clipboard.  Apparently I had picked inventory day to commit my heinous crime.  Every staff member was on duty.  There was no part of the store that seemed free and clear.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI walked past the remaining aisles and past the checkout counter with a kind of dumb resignation.  If I was going to get caught, I met as well go out proudly.  I made it to the exit and the automatic door opened in front of me with a slight hum.  This was it.  I stepped forward.  And out into the bright summer day.\r\n</p><p>\r\nNo cops came swarming out of the shadows.  There were no shadows.  Every shadow seemed banished by the bright sun.  As I got away from the store my heart felt as if it were about to tear free from my ribcage and take off running down the street.  When I turned down a block away from the store I shook the staplers free from my sleeves and carried them one in each hand.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI had done it!  The sense of accomplishment was exhilarating.  My heart slowed back to normal.  And such a wave of relief crashed down on my shoulders that I thought I would fall to the hot sidewalk.  All at once I began to laugh.  It was just a few chuckles at first, but they surged up from inside of me like an air bubble bursting from underwater.  The first bout of laughter incited another wave of mirth and before I knew it I was doubled over in the street, guffawing, holding the two staplers in one hand and bracing myself against a mailbox with the other.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe laughter overwhelmed me like nothing I had ever experienced before.  I laughed so hard that it became hard to take a breath.  My stomach muscles ached.  People in the street began to skirt around me as they would a madman.  I lapsed into deep, silent laughter.  My eyes watered.  My body shook spasmodically.  My legs buckled underneath me and I fell to the sidewalk, dropping the two staplers.  The sky was brighter now and the people doing their best to avoid me were blurry blocks of humanity.  I was shocked that no one stopped to help me, to see if I was okay.  I could be having an epileptic seizure for all they knew.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nMaybe I was having a seizure.   What it was it like to have a seizure?  Could it be any worse than this?\r\n</p><p>\r\nLaughter shook my body.  I gasped for breath.   I began to feel as if my spirit were leaving my body, that I was dying, that my ethereal soul was divorcing its corporeal body.  There were two of me, one laughing uncontrollably, stricken with a hysterical mirth, and the other calm, imperturbable, watching this event take place.  I was able to see my life whole, each collective year of my life was not a segment, but a single event, a blip in the long timeline of the world.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nThen all at once the laughter passed.  I found myself lying on the pavement near the mailbox as bustling people stepped over and around me.  After a few seconds, I got my bearings and rose to my feet, bent over and picked up the staplers and headed off toward home.  I had at least 5,000 magazines to staple.  I had to call my friends  for a collating party which would involve ordering a few pizzas and at least a twelve-pack of cheap beer.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nLucky I didnâ€™t pay for those staplers.\r\n</p>'),(2626,'<P>\r\nFrom a really young age I\\\'ve always been interested in things which could be\r\nconsidered metaphysical, philosophical, paranormal, or whatever you want to call\r\nit. One of my favorite books as a kid was a big thick coffee table book on\r\nastrology. Noone gave it to me but after looking at it and carrying it around so\r\nmuch it became mine by default. I could barely read any of it until I got older\r\nmostly. I was intrigued by the pictures and symbols. There were lots of prints\r\nof renaissance type painting relating to astrology, figures from greek and roman\r\nera mythology, astronomical charts, alongside modern celebrities of the 60\\\'s and\r\n70\\\'s with their astrological charts.</P><P>\r\nLater around age 13, by indirect mention of the word \\\"synchronicity\\\" in same\r\nnamed album by The Police I got into reading some of Carl Jung. This was\r\nfascinating and heavy psychological theory of collective unconscious and\r\nsynchronicity that was mostly not understandable. I still liked to spend a lot\r\ntime chewing away at the meaning like beef jerky. This then led me to\r\nperiodically visit in \\\"new age\\\" bookstores that had those sort of books. That\r\nled back into deeper study of astrology, eastern religion, buddhism, etc. and so\r\none to this day. </P><P> There is special meaning to the term\r\n<A href=\\\"http://www.crystalinks.com/synchronicity.html\\\">synchronicity</A>.</P><P>\r\nI\\\'ve had enough of these types of occurences and experiences to have already\r\nrun the gamut from \\\"wondering\\\", \\\"believing\\\", \\\"fervently believing\\\", to now more\r\n\\\"casually believing\\\" which is most appropriate I think. Initially, I couldn\\\'t\r\nwait to tell of any weird coincidence or whatever that happened. It was\r\nfodder. Now most of the time I\\\'ll keep it to myself. There\\\'s no point in getting\r\nall caught up in it or worked up over it. I have no rational or logical\r\nframework for what or why it happens. That\\\'s the way it should be I\r\nbelieve, and which it will always be anyway. Let\\\'s leave the compulsive analysis\r\nto mundane technical work.<BR><BR> That said however I now have the urge to\r\nshare a few recent synchronistic occurences:</P>\r\n<LI>Alannis Morrissete:<BR><BR>\r\nWhile watching a VH1 thing a couple of days ago on her I feel something of a\r\ncrush, not so much for her looks but her intelligence, dignity, affinity or\r\nsomething just about her. I came back from Japan for christmas of 95 and spent a\r\nnight in a hotel hypnotized by her video after a lack of TV and weed from\r\nthe previous year abroad. Then a couple of months later I was travelling\r\nthrough Bangkok and listened to the entire album at an internet cafe. That music\r\nlike nearly any other decent music of that time really stuck with me for\r\npersonal reasons of emotional susceptiblity during a life upheaval. I was not\r\ntainted by the relentless top 40 type exposure most others had.<BR><BR>\r\nSo in the interview she\\\'s asked \\\"What was been the place you\\\'ve most been amazed\r\nto see reaction from your music played?\\\" I\\\'m thinking of that time in Bangkok\r\nand start to think \\\"Thailand, Thailand, Thailand...\\\", hoping she\\\'ll say that for\r\nsome reason. Then she says something like \\\"Well one time I happened to be in\r\nThailand...\\\"  I felt goosebumps all over and thought \\\"whoah\\\". It was about a 3.5\r\non the metaphysical \\\"whoah\\\" scale.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<LI>catching up with the past:<BR><BR>\r\na couple of months I went into the vietnamese restaraunt I normally go to for\r\nlunch nearly every day. I ran into a guy from my last job who could be\r\nconsidered a \\\"mentor\\\" for me as far as programming goes and something of a\r\nfriend too. It was totally surprising to meet the guy and let him\r\nknow what was going on. I hadn\\\'t seen him in over almost two years.<BR><BR>\r\nThe next day I stopped by a typicial weekend haunt, the San Diego Technical\r\nBookstore. I happened to see just standing there when I see my best friend from\r\nhigh school, Nathan, who I hadn\\\'t seen in about five years. Major catching up\r\nfollowed. The most memorable thing about Nathan is that he was my \\\"drug\r\nbuddy\\\". I had more regular \\\"party friends\\\" to just drink beer and go to parties with. Nathan did a lot of more off the wall type stuff like hang out with homeless people downtown, and an assortment of drugs, especially acid and speed. At that same time the guy who sat next to me at my last job a year ago walked in. More catching up ensued from him simultaneously as my mind started to feel overwhelmed by this memory and experiential overload. Nathan saw the tripped out look on my face and picked up a geometric\r\ntextbook waved it back and forth in front of my face saying \\\"whoooaahh...\r\naaaahcid... heheh\\\" while I tried to maintain a respectable conversation about\r\n.NET with the other guy.<BR><BR>\r\n<TT>tangential notes: the guy I met at lunch is japanese, me and Nathan\r\nstopped at a japanese market next to the bookstore to get some lunch, his\r\ngirlfriend is half-japanese. Nathan himself is half-filipino, my current\r\ngirlfriend is filipino.I lived in Japan for a couple of years, and my first\r\ngirlfriend was japanese. (seems to be a big <B>asian</B> subtext\r\nto my life)  As I walked around the japanese market\r\nsurrounded by japanese people and language with my \\\"drug buddy\\\" It was like\r\nthree different phases of my life had decided to revisit at the same\r\ntime.<BR></TT><BR>\r\n<LI>swinney.org:<BR><BR> Took a renewed interest here, looked at the\r\ncode and posted a <A\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2615#15621\\\"> goofy story\r\ncontinuation comment</A>  about designer drugs, and southerners.<BR><BR>\r\nToday, inspired by S5\\\'s \\\"double soy mocha\\\" comment line within the swinney code\r\nI stop by a cafe for said beverage. Sitting down for a minute to have my drink I\r\npicked up a rollingstone magazine and then read an article about a teenage boy\r\nfrom Memphis dying of a designer hallucingen drug, \\\'ACL-T-7\\\'. I had never heard\r\nof this before. Swear to god.<BR>\r\n</UL> <BR>\r\n\r\nI\\\'ve known other people who also have had these types of occurences but most of\r\nthem are inclined to not even notice them, or if they do they actually don\\\'t\r\nwant to think about as it must shake up their understanding of reality and they\r\nprefer to be in a kind of denial about it. Others I know have never had\r\nthese experiences and would never believe in them either anyway.<BR><BR>\r\nThe conventional view of reality is that we are solitary objects, disconnected,\r\nfloating around space driven by material and egotistical desires. All events\r\nultimately random and meaningless, props for us all to inaccurately perceive.\r\nThe alternative backstage view is of the universal being, moistly connected in\r\none common nexus of perception and reality. Each life no matter how difficult or\r\nexhalted mere cell pulsations in the universal body. Each individual thought or\r\nimpulse the destroyer or creator of successive universes in themselves. The mass\r\nof life the same being playing hide and seek with itself, discovering that it\r\nis.<BR><BR>\r\n The reason why I don\\\'t like to think or talk about these kinds of\r\nsynchronistic occurences too much is that it would ruin it. As soon as someone\r\nwrites a book on ten steps to create synchronistic events it\\\'s ruined, as soon\r\nas you go from excitedly talking about it to bragging about it it\\\'s ruined, as\r\nsoon as you get into a debate trying to prove it it\\\'s ruined.<BR><BR>\r\nIt shouldn\\\'t be any more impressive than a sunrise or conversation is\r\nimpressive. There\\\'s no need to study or prove this any more than it\\\'s necessary\r\nto do so of your own pulse or breath.<BR><BR>\r\n So, I just had the instinct to want to\r\npost this. Maybe it\\\'s a bullshit reason or maybe it\\\'s not. Maybe it\\\'s irrelevant\r\nor maybe it\\\'s not.<BR><BR>\r\n Life to me tends to be a continually, confounding puzzle.\r\nI\\\'m not sure if it\\\'s maddening or awesome; a journey, a prison, an adventure, a\r\ntest, or just another day.\r\n<BR>'),(2630,'<p>\r\ni am not the sort of person who has the attention span for proper\r\ncollecting. I am, however, the sort of person who can\\\'t seem to throw\r\nanything away. It makes for an interesting combination which results in\r\noverstuffed drawers and teetering shelves full of ragtag collections of\r\npaper scraps, found photos, magazines, old t-shirt scraps, typewriter\r\nparts, a couple of bmw gearshifts, etc but no whole, cohesive collection\r\nto speak of. I\\\'ve always been fascinated with people who collect properly.\r\nMy dad has a collection of rare books and a collection of old cameras. My\r\nold roommate Jeffrey has a collection of vintage jeans, his favorites\r\nbeing the fioriucci with the pink top stitching. I got a paperweight for\r\nmy birthday from an aunt. I toyed with the idea of collecting them after a\r\nteacher of mine told me that Colette and Truman Capote had as well.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve attempted and failed at several collections in my life, including  \r\nbaseball cards (i have a folder of the complete san francisco giants\r\nline-up from 1986 as well as a few other favorites from the time in a box\r\nabove my closet), numbers (i had two large binders full of notebook paper\r\nonto which i would write numbers, in order, starting with one. i was\r\nsomewhere in the hundreds of thousands when i lost interest. this is a\r\ncollection i desperately wish i still had.), library cards (i would still\r\nbe working on this one if i hadn\\\'t lost my wallet containing all my\r\nlibrary cards from the various towns and cities i\\\'ve spent a little time\r\nin over the years.), and first editions (i have one, A Spaniard in the\r\nWorks, by John Lennon. I had another, a Victorian bible of fashion and style\r\nentitled The Secrets of Definitive Dress, but I gave that one to Jessica.)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe more I think about it, I haven\\\'t failed entirely at my quest for the\r\nproper collection. I have three antique typewriters, seven working\r\ncameras, and hundreds of letters i\\\'ve started writing, lost, then found\r\nagain after their expiration dates.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWhat have you been collecting, or trying to collect, or have accidentally\r\ncollected?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2627,'<p>Do you read me? Odd, coming from me, but there are rarer things and things stranger â€“ your eyelids, carefully removed and tender, sliced from the surface of our first impressions, what were they? Or canâ€™t you remember?</p>\r\n<p>Itâ€™s raining now, gurgling through the gutters and spitting at the window, he heard it would last through the night, but from where? A radio in the deli, spewing at the oblivious cashier, a half-hushed blurting on the bus, a glimpse of a soggy newspaper -- could you resist this knowledge if you wanted to? Or canâ€™t you remember?</p>\r\n<p>He abandons plans for an evening walk in lieu of this reluctant omnipotence, brews coffee instead. A magazine sleeps on the kitchen table â€“ itâ€™s about gardens, gardens around the world. Leaves strangely shaded, delicately shaped, cups of color filling glossy pages, wild or slightly overgrown gardens appeal to him most â€“ tufts of a tall (almost violet!) grass exploding from a rock face, the razor-sharp leaves of a vine asphyxiating the mossy cherub, designs overrun, forms made ambiguous.</p>\r\n<p>The coffeemaker completes its steamy hiccups, he goes to the livingroom to read. The book on the table is thin and soft, not at all imposing. He thinks itâ€™s very interesting â€“ he takes a sip of the coffee (too hot), flips for the dogear, opens to the proper page. The words float up to him, empty flickering in his eyes â€“ more of an indifference than an aphasia. It is fascinating, the book, but. He lies down and squishes into the couch, all comfy-like, yet the sense of irrelevance does not abate â€“ staring at the ceiling is ineffably more pleasurable than this babble, however delicious it may be, how informative it may have beenâ€¦</p>\r\n<p>Time quivers, loses focus. The ceiling fascinates him, he imagines himself a cartographer of chipped paint and plaster, and he wonders what it would be like for the book to be new. Or anything, for that matter, to be new. What it would be like for a conversation to be new, to thirst for information, concrete and detailed information â€“ what would it be like to really want it?</p>\r\n<p>Was there ever a time? Was there ever consent?</p>\r\n<p>Navigating the ceilingâ€™s floorâ€¦</p>\r\n<p>He canâ€™t remember.</p>\r\n'),(2628,'<P>\r\nthe path to my house is quite the jaunt. from the market of gumo dong i walk up a hill, following an open sewer covered by loose and constantly moving flagstones. \\\"dont step on the stones, or you will discover the rank underbelly of korea\\\" a friend will say. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are many obstacles on this path towards my apartment. the scooters zoom in and out of pedestrian walkers, beeping their way along the dusty streets. the dust comes from the steppes of mongolia, perhaps swirled up by the ghost of khan. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlittle children squat where flagstones are missing to relieve themselves and im sure ive seen students doing the same thing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe cats that live in my hood look ruffled and are prepared for constant war with the other animals. small pickup trucks will zoom down the streets with cages packed with dogs heading to market. i have learned that a scooter can carry thirty times its weight in produce and empty bottles, teetering and tottering as its driver makes a path through the madness of the market.\r\n(on a side note, i was sitting in a chair at the front of a bus going about a 110km/h down the highway towards a theme park and almost died when a car slowly pulled out of the right lane and into the lane we were driving in. it was close...so close life didnt have a chance to flash before my eyes. luckilly the next lane was empty and the bus driver jerked the bus into the next lane missing the car by only a few inches.)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npiles of burning plastic and paper send plumes of course and choaking smoke into the air making my throat bleed. it happens to everyone here ive heard: coughing up bloody brown mucus. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nat night the skyline is littered with bright red crosses. they are literally everywhere here. beside brothels, barbershops/rubntugs, and on the top floors of apt buildings. christianity has a grip on this country like ive never seen. perhaps more so than it did in quebec prior to the quiet revolution. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nseoul is only an hour away from one of the most heavily defended borders in the world. if war should occur, we would be the first to go. seoul would be hard hit, perhaps a million dieing in the first few hours. with a population of thirteen million, one million seems somewhat fair.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe bathroom in my apt smells of dead bodies and rotting cabbage. the door is kept closed at all times for good reason. as much as it is cleaned having pleasant smells is a pipedream. the pipedream being that my pipes lead directly towards the open sewers outside. unlike some places im unable to witness logs and debris floating towards god knows where, yet the odor still rises and is unbearable at times. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas much as my apt smells rank, it is virtually bug free. i have yet to see a spider or cockroach and for this i am forever thankful. apparently if there is a bug problem, men walk the streets spraying insectiside around like they did DDT in the 60\\\'s. for such a modern and fast paced country, there is so much room for improvement. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe market down the hill from me is full of food baking in the stalls, on the street, and among the many carts and stores. the sidewalks are busy, and scooters scooting makes keeping your head down somewhat impossible. there is little regard for pedestrian safety here, and for this reason walking and crossing the streets is a constant adventure. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnevertheless, the food is great, the people are friendly, and the beer and soju are cheap. cigarettes are even cheaper. its impossible to spend more than i make here, and for this reason, im able to do what i want when i want to. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom gumo dong, korea, this is chiamattt signing out. \r\n</p>\r\n '),(2629,'<p>I got Teddy a few months ago.  On Valentine\\\'s Day as a matter of fact.  It was my first Valentine\\\'s Day celebrated in any form.  He\\\'s called \\\'Phonicus\\\'.  He is soft, has ears that are slightly too large  for him.  Round ample black beady eyes, and a decent well created bum that I love sniffing and cuddling...</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><b><i>Anyone here have a thing for bottoms? Rear ends? Rumps?</i></b></p>'),(2631,'<p>Why are my articles not being printed out in Swinney??? People are writing... sharing.... and the world is going before my eyes and all I can do is \\\'watch\\\'.... watch and comment... no actual input.... </p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p><i> woe is me..... :( </i></p>'),(2632,'I swiped my card on the security lock.  The security guard grinned at me in a hysterical way but I ignored that as well.  The door chimed and I pushed my way in.  Suddenly I felt a presence behind myself and there was a ridiculous figure behind myself holding a pistol on my back.  The door closed and locked behind us.\r\n<p>\r\n	â€œStay calm, ok cheraâ€™a?â€ said the man, which appeared to me to be dressed in a white shirt, a black tie, dark blue pants and black moccasins.  â€œTake out 600,000 guaranies and put them in this bag.â€\r\n<p>	I proceeded to do this obviously because I feared for my life and because I wanted no later trouble with this imbecile who didnâ€™t have a choice but to steal peopleâ€™s money.  I soon realized the scumbag was nothing but a high-school kid.  I guess they grow larger and with gruffer voices these days, since the tobacco and alcohol consumption of these low-lives is being made more socially acceptable by their own alcoholic, chain-smoking parents who think that â€œoh itâ€™s not such a big deal.â€\r\n<p>	600,000 guaranies is the limit of money that you can get from these machines, that is, if theyâ€™re working.  What I was wondering was what the fuck the security guard was doing about this.  Nothing, probablyâ€¦  I just assumed they were all just accomplices and the guard was getting a percentage of the spoils.  The boy kept the gun firmly pointed at my back as I worked on the machine.  Finally the bills came out and I gave them in the bag to the fucker.\r\n<p>	â€œGive me your hands,â€ he said in a voice with which I could tell he was scared shitless, and I trembled because he could let lose of his finger and pull the trigger.  I didnâ€™t want a fucking punctured lung.  He tied my hands up with a nylon string and then he duct taped them.  Then he took me outside and he winked at the security guard.  The guard replied with something in guarani that I couldnâ€™t understand.  I would if I wasnâ€™t a stuck up middle class advertising snob.  But if I wasnâ€™t, Iâ€™d probably be selling lomitos or robbing people at ATMs.\r\n<p>	The kid took me quickly down the street, where he met his partner, another high school kid in dark blue trousers, white shirt and tie.  I noticed the badge he had on and it read â€œCNC.â€  Colegio Nacional de la Capital, the state-run high school.  What a coincidence, eh?  He was short and had the beginnings of what I think was a moustache.\r\n<p>	â€œOK, did he get 600,000?â€ he said.\r\n<p>	â€œYeah, itâ€™s in the bag, cheraâ€™a,â€ said the taller one.\r\n<P>	â€œFine, letâ€™s take him to the house.â€\r\n<p>	They took me two blocks from where we were and then they put a black garbage bag over my head.  I started to choke.\r\n<p>	â€œRun, you bastard!â€ they screamed.\r\n<p>	We started running.  They pushed me into some kind of house and they sat me down in a couch.  They took the bag off from my head.  The house looked like the poor boyâ€™s parents had died and he was left alone to care about himself.  A theory which, until today, I think is accurate.\r\n<p>	The punk came to me and started to speak to me.\r\n<p>	â€œOK lekaya, weâ€™re going to have you here for a month.  Every day weâ€™re taking you to the machine and youâ€™re giving us 600,000 until were finished with what you have in there,â€ he said.  â€œThen you can go home and if you tell on us weâ€™ll kill you.â€\r\n<p>	Oh, what a fucking brilliant plan, man!  Besides the fact that the limit is not per day but per transaction at the stupid machine, they werenâ€™t actually going to â€œcleanâ€ my bank account since this was a credit card, and itâ€™s not gold or platinum so weâ€™ll reach the credit limit in no time.  But I supposed that was OK.  The stupid bastard was the one who had the gun.  I just hoped he knew how to use it or else he could set a bullet accidentally ricocheting around the room if he pulled that trigger just a bit more.\r\n<p>	â€œYou got a wallet?  Give me you fucking wallet.  And your cell phone,â€ the tall one said.\r\n<p>	â€œTheyâ€™re in my right pocket, yeah, both of them, go ahead,â€ I said.\r\n<p>	He took out the wallet and took out my credit cards and Ids and money.\r\n<p>	â€œMarco Oviedo.  Youâ€™re fucking only 28 yearâ€™s old.  You look 50 with your big beer gut, he he.  You getting a bit bald back there, arenâ€™t you.â€\r\n<p>	I didnâ€™t mind he was making fun of me.  I wasnâ€™t even that fat.  Iâ€™ve never dieted in my life, and I donâ€™t really care.  What I really did think is that idiot needed to eat something before he disappeared.  And I wasnâ€™t getting bald.  Most of the men in my family are just a bit light haired in the back.  From them on I just kept quiet because I was intrigued with what these two knuckleheads had in mind.\r\n<p>	The short kid came into the room with two a large cups of tea and a bag full of hand sized breads.  The tall one grabbed one of the cups and three breads.  That sure isnâ€™t going to do much but fill his stomach.  He grabbed my cell-phone and tried to use it.  It was key locked.  He looked puzzled.  Finally he popped the question.\r\n<p>	â€œHey, how do you get this fucking thing to work?â€\r\n<p>	â€œItâ€™s key locked,â€ I said, â€œyou have to enter a password and press pound to use it.â€\r\n<p>	â€œWhatâ€™s fucking pound?â€\r\n<p>	I was mesmerized by his ignorance.  I figured that he had already stolen and used as many cell-phones to know what the pound key was.  Anyways I showed him, pointing with my nose, and then I gave him my password.  I didnâ€™t know who the hell he was calling.\r\n<p>	â€œHey Marcelo, whatâ€™s up?â€ he said.\r\n<p>	â€œShut the fuck up!â€ said the short one, spitting his tea.\r\n<p>	â€œWhat the fuck is wrong with you?â€\r\n<p>	â€œDonâ€™t say any fucking names, he could identify us!â€\r\n<p>	The tall kid looked a bit spooked and confused.\r\n<p>	â€œWhat? How could heâ€¦ OK, nambreâ€¦ Iâ€™ll fuck offâ€¦â€\r\n<p>	â€œGo use the fucking cell-phone to the other room!â€ the short one screamed.\r\n<p>	He went to the other room of the house.  Speaking of the house, it was a glorious mess.  The floors were tiled in a faint red and white, and there were clothes everywhere.  The guy who lived there had his bed in the main room, and all of his stuff was on top of the bed.  I could see the patio outside.  There was a set of chairs and a table with three liter beer bottles on top, including a couple plates they used as ashtrays.  Those bottle must have been there unreturned for a week.  The patio was covered in huge weeds that had grown because they never cared to cut them.  I wondered about the size of the rats that lurked behind the green bushes.\r\n<p>	â€œHey, what the hell do you work on,â€ the boy asked me.\r\n<p>	â€œAdvertising,â€ I responded.\r\n<p>	â€œAdvertising? You do ads for TV and stuff?â€\r\n<p>	â€œSometimes. Yeah.â€\r\n<p>	â€œDid you do that commercial with the girl and the monkey? That was sweet, man.â€\r\n<p>	I wasnâ€™t sure if I wanted to keep having this conversation anymore.  He started pestering me about the commercial, which I did not make, and started to give me ideas.\r\n<p>	â€œYou should do one with professional futbol players and have them kick balls around a runway while planes are taking off or something.â€\r\nI started thinking about what day it was and about my kids and their school.  Iâ€™m supposed to pay stupid bills and care for my job and all and I was going to be stuck in this stupid house for weeks.  I noticed they had a crucifix over the bed.  The short one was still having his food sitting on a chair while the other kid was busy talking on the phone or whatever.  I asked him if he goes to church often.\r\n<p>	â€œSure,â€ he said, â€œI go every Sunday and I take my grandma as well.  Itâ€™s important to hear Godâ€™s message, you know.â€\r\n<p>	Yes, of course.  Like it was doing any good to him.  I myself stopped going for years because I was tired of it and it would be much more useful if I used the time to watch movies or something.\r\n<p>	Time passed and I was bored to death.  I actually wished myself dead.  The heat was killing me and these bastards werenâ€™t gracious enough to offer me a bite to eat.  I wasnâ€™t really scared anymore because I figured these kids wouldnâ€™t shoot me if I tried to get away.  It was late in the afternoon and the neighborhood was quiet enough to hear a whisper being spoken.  Surely there were old ladies sitting in the sidewalks gossiping about someoneâ€™s daughter or talking about how safe it was in the city during the dictatorship.  Sure, it was beautiful during the dictatorship.  You couldnâ€™t even trust your own neighbors.  Nobody even tried to move a finger because you could get branded as a communist and get tortured and jailed for a day.  Thatâ€™s what my dad tells me, anyway, and he tells it like he was such a revolutionary while were driving in his Mercedes down the street.  Heâ€™s a fucking yuppie fascist.\r\n<p>	The kids started to listen to music.  The tall kid was unshirted and was drinking a can of beer, and smashing empty cans with a mallet.  Air in the room was extremely hot.  Afternoons donâ€™t get milder in these brick homes and sweat was pouring from my back.  The whole place got to smell really sweaty in fact, after a while.  The short kid was fumbling with what I could see was a pile of Iron Maiden and other heavy metal CDs.  â€œThe Number of the Beastâ€ was playing on his stereo.\r\n<p>Itâ€™s like a stereotype in hooligans. Itâ€™s either this or punk rock, and not even good punk rock.  The kid left his gun on a chair nearby and started bringing cans of beer.  My cell-phone began to ring.  The short kid grabbed the cell-phone, but couldnâ€™t open it because his hands were busy, so he just threw it against the wall and it burst open.  They started to laugh.  The tall one was extremely amused.\r\n<p>	â€œNderakore boludo,â€ he muttered as he laughed.\r\n<p>	Suddenly we heard someone knock on the door.  The short one got up and went to the door barefooted.\r\n<p>	â€œOuch! What the fuck!â€\r\n<p>	He had stepped on one of the beer cans that the tall kid had smashed on the floor and he was bleeding.  While the other laughed hysterically, he began to jump in one foot towards the door.  The door opened.\r\n<p>	â€œHey, I was just coming to get my bottles that you guys should have returned like a week ago.  I hope you havenâ€™t broken them, you silly drunks.â€\r\n<p>	This was the voice of the owner of the despensa the two kids frequently buy their beer from.  He pushed his way into the house like it was his own and almost made the short kid, who was standing on one foot and bleeding, fall.  From the look on his face I knew he was a bit surprised to find a guy in a suit tied and sitting down in the house of these two young punks.\r\n<p>	â€œHey kids, what the hell is going on here.â€\r\n<p>	The tall kid started to freak out while the short kid fell back on his chair.  I was waiting for the astonished storeowner to do something.  He was wearing clothing too heavy for the heat and I knew he was hiding some kind of weapon.  I think itâ€™s pretty logical that he should keep a gun if heâ€™s living in the midst of such scum and owns a store.\r\n<p>The tall kid reached for his pistol and accidentally pulled the trigger, and shot a bullet that ricocheted around the room so fast it sounded like laser was being fired.  Everybody ducked for cover, as if this was going to make any difference.  I think that at one point the bullet nearly missed my head.  I think it actually bounced off my head because I felt something hit me and it wasnâ€™t the wall or the floor or a chair nearby.\r\n<p>	The storeowner produced a revolver from his back pocket and pointed it at the two boys.  The tall kid reacted by pointing his own gun to the storeowner.  The three stared at each other and none of them knew what the hell was going on.  I was completely shaken by the gunfire and I was still ducking and still sweating in the couch, I thought that my bad luck was going to continue and the bullet would go through my head.  The two gunmen were still staring at each other while the short kid looked completely spooked.  His foot was still bleeding.  I wished the storeowner had shot the two and I hated him in that moment for hesitating.  I think Iron Maiden was still the blaring noise that came from the radio.\r\n<p>	I brought myself up and saw the perfect opportunity for escape.  I peered at the storeowner and I spoke to him.\r\n<p>	â€œHeyâ€¦ Can I go?â€ I asked.\r\n<p>	The storeowner rolled his eyes to look and me.\r\n<P>	â€œYeahâ€¦â€ he said, shaking his head. â€œGo ahead.â€\r\n<p>	With my hands tied, I crawled to the bed where I grabbed my wallet with my teeth.  Then I got up and I walked through the door.  I was exasperated by the rush the situation and stopped to breathe.  Outside I found a tree and I rubbed the tape and string that held my wrists together and I successfully broke them both.  I wanted to start running but everything still floated in circles around me.  It was starting to get dark, and there was a cool breeze that soothed me, but then I was afraid the combination of the wind and my sweat would make me catch a cold.  I walked down the street, surprised that no noise but the heavy metal music still came from the house.  That was when I heard two shots, and a few seconds later a third one.\r\n<p>I started running.  Fortunately there was a bus stop around so I tried to act like nothing happened and sat down on the bench.  I checked my wallet to see if anything was missing.  When the bus came, it didnâ€™t stop completely so I had to make a run to catch it.  I sat beside a kid going back home from school and he was drawing something in his notebook.  He reminded me of my own kid Miguel when he was about his age, always drawing and stuff.  I didnâ€™t know what the fuck he was drawing so out of curiosity I kept staring at the piece of paper, and then the kid gave me the scariest â€œquit giving me shitâ€ look ever.  His head was gently tilted forward, a frown on his face, and his eyes catching my own with such power that I just stared towards the front of the bus for the remains of my bus trip, trying without luck to breathe calmly and in constant rhythm.\r\n'),(2633,'<img src=\\\'http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2633_1.jpg\\\'>\r\n<p>\r\nÂ build me a rainbow<br>\r\nÂ Â so that i may see<Br>\r\nÂ what lies at its end<BR>\r\n</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>\r\nÂ Â but from there to before<BR>\r\nÂ Â Â Â is found only looking<BR>\r\nÂ Â Â and not any way back<BR>\r\n</p>'),(2634,'<p>Probably not the best idea to go see a film called Suicide Club alone, but there it is. Plans canceled, last minute, loud Le Tigre in the headphones and me alone walking down 2nd Street, standing outside in the line (\\\"we canâ€™t fit 300 of you in the lobby, please line up here\\\"), thinking about: do I get out the umbrella? It isn\\\'t raining that much. Man that is a lot of eye shadow, and not an attractive color on her at all. He is not talking about lawyer stuff and then saying how he is in a band. No way. Poser punk! Lawyers can\\\'t be in rock bands!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>An old woman came up to me holding a disposable plastic rain helmet in her hands, and asked me what the line was for and I gave her a very far away look and said in a low voice: this is the line for the Suicide Club. Her jaw did a half-grind and she straightened and then walked away. \\\"Oh,\\\" she said. Later I realized what that must have looked like to her: 300 assorted people in line to kill themselves? This used to be such a nice neighborhood. I hope she got as much amusement out of the moment as I did.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>OK, but the film, about which I can\\\'t stop thinking but and also about which I don\\\'t really want to write. Japanese with English subtitles. Spare and beautifully shot in low, rich reds, greys, blues. It opens with a ballet of trains, the camera weaving through the people and infrastructure in Shinjuku Station. But then 54 Japanese high school girls in uniforms leap from a subway platform at the same time. You see limbs crushed under wheels, blood splashing up onto train windows, blood shooting out on the platform.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But the creepiest shot -- the most haunting -- is before they leap and the camera pans the length of the platform, wall to wall people hands linked, at the edge, ready to jump.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then a musical number, a micro version of early Hanson, but all girls, and there are six of them. Or five. They sing a catchy song about e-mail called \\\"Mail Me.\\\" Turns out in addition to being that awful pop band everyone hates but secretly craves, they are responsible for the whole mass suicide fad, maybe. Or maybe people are just bored. But you donâ€™t find that out until later, after a lot more death and the Japanese version of a young Tim Curry strutting around a deserted bowling alley in another musical interlude, this time his own version of \\\"Sweet Transvestite\\\" -- complete with big chair, big hair, and Curry-esque legs-over-the-armrest-coy-I\\\'m-fucking-hot-and-I-know-it-bang-look -- with lyrics like \\\"because the dead shine all night long.\\\" Then somehow the film ends, after a weird D. Lynch moment in an ill-lit red velvet room with hundreds of baby chickens and a hooded man with the carpenter\\\'s version of a cheese grater taking giant slices of people\\\'s skin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One <a href=\\\"http://216.239.35.100/search?q=cache:npKm_d4Ug6cC:www6.yomiuri.co.jp/newse/20020307woaa.htm+Jisatsu+Sakuru&hl=en\\\">review</a> uses the phrase \\\"the profound ennui of contemporary existence,\\\" to background his discussion of theme (the reviewer argues that the film failed as commentary, being neither compelling nor coherent. Two stars.). I like that phrase. I am still thinking about the film, so I am going to have to disagree: I\\\'m still thinking about it, therefore it is compelling. Also lately the profound ennui of contemporary existence has been a lot on my mind, so the whole thing fit neatly together except there isn\\\'t a resolution I mean I still don\\\'t know what I think about anything, and also I had to go home alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After, on the subway platform waiting for a train, I kept putting my foot over the yellow line, replaying the opening of the film, getting right to the edge but backing away. Then I noticed that the woman who was standing in line behind me earlier was there in the station, alone also, and she still had the same bad eye shadow on except now that we were closer I could see that it was bright bright blue. The name patch on her industrial indie rock thrift store jacket said \\\"Steve\\\" in the awful cursive font found only on uniforms. Steve was listening to loud angry music and I really wanted to ask her about the film, talk about it in a non-committal non-creepy sort of way, except I did not. At the other end of the platform a girl in a long white belted wool coat was doing ballet, twirling, leaping. Her coat was flared at the waist and it spun out along with her, a half-second behind each of her movements.</p>'),(2635,'<P>\r\nI walked out to the car to get my back pack with all my books and this\r\nnotebook that I write in at night before I go to bed.  I live in a\r\nhouse in Santa Cruz:  home to all things slimy.  The local\r\nuniversity\\\'s mascot is the slug.  Snails dominate the landscape\r\noutside the house\\\'s entrance.  They do their snail thing over every\r\npart of the entrance area.</P><P>\r\n\r\nMy roommate Dave is a native of the area.  (He owns the house.)  He can\r\nwalk on a snail barefoot without flinching.  I can not do that.  I do\r\nnot hate snails, but I do not really feel compassion for them yet.  I\r\nthink  I eventually will.</P><P>\r\n\r\nMost of the little ones are gone now, probably stepped on or eaten.  I\r\nonly saw three big ones out there when I went to my car.  One was\r\nunderneath the walkway light.  It was slugging away.  They move.  It is\r\ndifficult to say how they move though.  Nothing on them moves, but as\r\na whole, they move.  They are eyes and body and shell.  This one had\r\nlarge eyes out in front of him (or her) on slick skin poles.  The\r\neyeballs on sticks move slowly from left to right.  The movement is\r\nsubtle.  The creature has an air of caution.  I suspect they have an\r\nexperience traveling similar to walking in the the dark.  I try to\r\nwalk in the dark as much as possible so I know what that is\r\nlike.</P><P>\r\n\r\nI decided to take a moment out of my busy schedule to observe the\r\nmovement of the snail bathed in orangie side walk lamp light.  The\r\nslimy and hard shelled creature seemed to be heading just off to the\r\nside of me.  I put my index finger to the ground in front of its\r\nhead.  The eyeballs swayed back and forth a few times then centered\r\nwith my fingernail between them.</P><P>\r\n\r\nAfter halting a few moments to get a good look at my obstacle presented\r\nbefore the two eyeballs, Mister Snail advanced slowly until one\r\neyeball  touched my finger\\\'s nail.  The eyeball curled back\r\nspasmatically in a recoil.  I am not sure if what the snail did counts as emotion.  \r\nIt just halted and cringed a little.</P><P>\r\n\r\nRe-composing its self, the eyeballs moved out to the sides of the\r\nsnails head and the body advanced and the snail stuck out its head and\r\nkissed my fingernail.</P><P>\r\n\r\nI very definitely felt some emotions.  I imagined the snail was going\r\nto see if I was tasty and perhaps bite my fingernail, then\r\nfiguring that as a path climbing my fingernail had much more promise\r\nthan slugging along the sidewalk, begin to advance up my fingernail\r\nto the cuticle and then up my knuckle.</P><P>\r\n\r\nThis just could not be tolerated.  I recoiled quickly after a moment\r\nof trying to be a tuff guy and brave new sensations.  Who can say that\r\nthey have knowingly allowed a snail to crawl up their finger?  No, no,\r\nI am sure snails are nice creatures and I could let one climb my arm\r\nwithout any sort of incident, but theory and practice being separate\r\nas they are, the light kiss of the snail was far enough down the road\r\nof experience for me.</P><P>\r\n\r\nI bid the snail good night with a shriek and a jump, and then tip toed\r\ninto the house in a manner that I felt would greatly reduce the\r\nsurface area between me and the sidewalk and also the chance of me\r\nhearing the awful crunching sound of snail shell under foot.</P>\r\n'),(3168,'<P><B>the morning routine</B></P><P>live, \\\"love minus zero/no limit\\\" lasts from the house to the church, long enough for a joint and a cigarrette.  you can appear unstoned if you hold your eyelids just right and focus on things appropriately.  secret of my success.</P><P>frank waits by the door of the maintenance room.  he\\\'s got a piece of toast with peanut butter and a coke inside for me.  ambushes me on the way to the carbohydrates.  \\\"she ain\\\'t been here for tree days, and she wants to know why the jungle room ain\\\'t vacuumed.  i\\\'m about to tell \\\'er somethin\\\'.\\\"  he\\\'s 71, white orbit of hair, sicilian by way of michigan, pad in his undies due to \\\"my cancer surgery\\\" that came in handy at homecoming when he spilled coffee in his lap \\\"and ditten even burn my gonjools.\\\"</P><P>chewing toast, i study him while he talks to me.  freda comes in.  she looks like a 67 year-old hedgehog.  homes in on me.  \\\"you never did that back brass.  what happened?\\\"  \\\"i left early wednesday.  ditten feel good.\\\"  frank kind of jumps a little bit, like he\\\'s been poked in the ribs:  \\\"who was here to <I>help</I> \\\'im do the back brass?  <I><B>i</B></I> don\\\'t do brass.  who does that leave?\\\"  i dig some pills wrapped in tinfoil from my pocket and take them with the rest of the coke.  androstenedione, tribulus, 3 hydroxycuts.</P><P>freda looks toward the toaster.  \\\"and what about the kitchen?\\\"  i point at the fellowship hall and question her with my eyebrows.  \\\"no, in the memorial building.\\\"  \\\"what about it? it was OK wednesday.\\\"</P><P>frank: \\\"come on.\\\"  we get in my car and drive to the gym, radio tuned to \\\"the gull, wgul. tampa bay\\\'s music of your life.\\\"  oldies.  i\\\'m not talking about the beatles and three dog night, either.  perry como, sinatra, and kay kyser, baby.  frank spots me on squats, the ass machine, leg curls, calf raises, the roman chair, hanging leg raises, sit-ups.  not much rest in between.  radio on the gull.  frequent trips to the water fountain.  after the lifting, twenty minutes on the elliptical stepper while he does the nautilus bicep thing and talks to the girl out front.</P><P>back at the church, we strip the floor in the fellowship hall.  freda and dave are elsewhere on campus.  radio bouncing sound waves off the linoleum.  benevolent kerosene smell of the stripper.  frank starts to get high from the fumes.  he used to be a painter, so he\\\'s used to it.  my grandfather claimed all painters were alcholics.  peggy lee comes on.  \\\"fever\\\".  frank scats along with it.  \\\"fev-ah.\\\"  \\\"mixxle bah row feeeee.\\\"  \\\"vah.\\\"</P><P>i can see out the windows at the east end of the hall.  an old white woman who looks like miles davis drives by on her way to the food closet.  then there\\\'s a mexican who looks like the cute hijacker.</P><P><B>lunchtime</B></P><P>i go into the memorial building looking for dave.  by the youth room, there\\\'s a wall of snapshots, with more in cabinets inside.  pocket the one with the shirtless teen boys in a tug of war at the beach.</P><P>dave\\\'s cleaning the girls\\\' bathroom upstairs.  \\\"she\\\'s here, dude.\\\"  julie\\\'s got a blue mustang convertible.  top\\\'s up in the breeze.  listening to \\\"forever changes\\\"  on the way to her apartment.  roll a joint.  push in the lighter by the ashtray, wait for it to pop out, spark up.  \\\"for better aftest,\\\" i mutter.</P><P>when we pass the high school, i yell at some guys at gym.  \\\"you got fine asses!\\\"  i don\\\'t care who hears me.  everybody knows this is nowhere.</P><P>we have tacos and iced tea.  dave\\\'s got the news on.  julie sits on my lap, on the sectional couch.</P><P><B>good afternoon</B></P><P>mop, strip, buff.  two more hours.  at break, he heads to the bathroom and takes off his clothes.  digs out the picture and sets it on the edge of the sink, leaning it against the wall.  tip of right index finger on right nipple.  left on left.  rub in circles.  he notices his face is finally starting to get tan.</P><P>gets some soap out of the dispenser.  left hand around his potatoes.  his grapes.  his cranberries.  his devilish eggs.  a little squeeze.</P><P>one of the guys in the photo is medium tan.  shiny rosy brown face.  full lips and cut abs and subtle smooth curve of cheek and jaw.</P><P>the jism is yellow against the white of the porcelain.  it seems so much better before than afterwards.</P><P>frank:  \\\"jeezus.  you were in there long enough.\\\"</P>'),(2636,'\n<H2>Morning Time</H2>\n<P>\nFluffy was a young man of 19 with a large fluffy head of blonde hair.\nThose that knew him thought of him as \"Fluffy\". Those that didn\'t know him that\nwell called him Fred. Fred Duvall.\n</P><P>\nAs was usual he awake first thing in the morning sharply at 8am with no\nassistance of a clock radio. He simply went from eyes closed to eyes open. He\nslept on his back on a bed with red silk sheets in torn white sweatpants.\nNormally he would vacantly look out through the gaps of the curtains that\nblocked the morning sunlight for a minute before getting up. After lying like\nthis for a moment he would slowly, but not sleepily, rise from his bed. Then he\nwould reach his hands straight up in the air to stretch. After a pause came a\nbrief moment where some kind of silent prayer was uttered under his breath.\nOften it would be, \"One, two, three, one, two, three... For there that I go and\nthere I shall be. Empty for none to see.\" Then he would walk to the bathroom to\nbrush his teeth and shower.\n</P><P>\nHis room had an assortment of illustrations on the walls of\nvarying sizes, some framed, others just plain paper taped to the wall. These\nwere drawings of sea otters done by himself. The otter drawings\nall had an exceptional level of detail to them. For example, it looked as if\nthe whiskers had been closely studied to get the exact layout and texture\ndone just right. Despite the detailed accuracy in some areas there was also a\nstrange element of cloying sentimentality to them where the eyes where enlarged\nemphasizing cuteness. Also the otters were in poses which would never be\nnaturally found -- an otter at the base of a tree lovingly gazing at a songbird,\nan otter following a little group of ducks with their mother in the lead, a\ngroup of otters all swimming on their backs in an synchronized\nswimming type arrangement. It was the mixture of detailed precision and maudlin\nexcess that gave the drawings an aching sense of sadness.\n</P><P>\nIf it was possible he\'d like to have an otter of his own, but he knew the space\nrequirments an animal like that would need so it was not a real dream for him.\nHis mind was practical like that. He knew that if he wanted to have an otter as\na pet his only chance would be to live near a river and hope to befriend one\nsomehow someday. He had already gone over this possibility, of one day getting a\nshack or cabin in a remote area where otters were and living to pursue that\ndream to it\'s end. In any case, for now there would be no use in entertaining\nthose notions. Maybe one day, but not now.\n</P><P>\nHe got up in his usual manner. After he brushed his teeth he heard a sharp\nrapping on the door. A husky voice bellowed on the other side clearly\naudible even from inside the bathroom, \"Yo, Flufs!\". Fluffy had a perturbed look\none his face. \"Coming.\" he replied back, even though probably his much softer\nvoice was not audible from outside the door. \"Fluffy! Get yo butt out here boy.\"\nthe outdoor voiced continued, in a familiar way much more than hostile or\nthreatening.\n</P><P>\nBefore Fluffy opened the door he let out a sigh and thought to himself, \"Now\nwhat?\". He knew it was Hank, but he didn\'t know why he would be around at this\ntime. He opened the door.\n</P><P>\nHank was a large black man with an outwardly indeterminate age range between\ntwenty five and forty that many black men have. He was dressed in\ncasual slacks and a leather jacket. Bright thick gold rings on his fingers stood\nout in sharp contrast to his dark skin.  \"Yo Flufeee, how is it going my\nman? What do you got for me today? You been good for me?\" he exhorted garrously,\nvigorously patting Fluffy\'s shoulder.\n</P><P>\n\"Now Hank, you know the rules and how it is.\", Fluffy began. His tone had an\npedantic quality to it. \"On this day and on every day... like you\nknow. I have my routine\" His voice rose to empasize the word \"routine\". \"This\nisn\'t your time to come over, now is it? Hmmm?\", he scowled not that seriously.\n</P><P>\nHank shook his head up and down nodding agreement but there was look of\nexasperation on his face. \"Yeah, yeah, Fluffs I know... about the \'routine\'.\nBut baby you got work with me on this. I got payments due for my last shipment.\nIt\'s no big deal my man. I need the money now. I just got to make some payment\non this shipment of really good turquoise that just came in and the mans coming\nover in an hour. C\'mon... Just give what you brought in and I\'ll let you have\nyour day big guy. \"\n</P><P>\nHank was a pimp, but he also made Native American jewelry on the side. He\nconsidered that his truer calling. They were both business models of his but it\nwas in making jewelry that Hank felt much more gratified. He lacked that hard\nruthless edge to be a very good pimp. A strange thing was that Hank\'s love in\nhigh school was native american. They would spend time after school walking to a\npark where they would lie on the grass and she would tell him all about her\ntribe and the ways of her people. Noone was sure what they talked about but\noccasionaly she would rise up to dance in a circle, which Hank would then\nrehearse only much funkier. During those days he would frequently say to nearly\nanyone that he was \"learning the ways of my girl\'s tribe.\" and then he would\ndo some hand movements and yell out \"Ay-yah-uh-uh\". It was best to never mention\nanything about that girlfriend to Hank anymore. It wasn\'t until after a few\nyears after the breakup did Hank develop the interest in Indian jewelry for\nreasons he kept to himself.\n</P><P>\nFluffy walked back to wad of money casually stacked in a coffee can on the\nfloor, got it and handed it to Hank. \"Okay. Here you go. Now I\'m going to be\nneeding some art supplies pretty soon. I\'m all out of canvas.\"\n</P><P>\n\"Fluffs, you allright bro. I got your back. You know it. I\'ll stop by art supply\nwhen I get some wooden dowels for my next project. Hey by the way there\'s going\nto a show coming next week. We gotta go, it\'s going to be off the hook. Yo,\nlater.\"\n</P><P>\nHank popped a piece of gum in his mouth and briskly returned to his maroon\ngold-flecked El Dorado which still had it\'s engine running the whole time. He\nstuck his hand out the window, slapped the roof twice and then gestured a peace\nsign to Fluffy as he drove off.\n</P><P>\nFluffy didn\'t wave back. Instead just disapprovingly shook his head saying,\n\"Yeah, yeah\". He turned to see that his neighbor\'s door had been\nslightly ajar. Behind it he saw a middle-aged Mexican lady, huge belly, sheer\nnightgown and all. She intently watched the El Dorado drive off. After it had\ndisappeared in the distance she turned her gaze toward Fluffy to give him a\ndirty look. She made a loud, disapproving clucking sound with her mouth and\nclosed the door.  Fluffy muttered, \"Oh man... Whatever.\"\n</P><P>\nThen he came back in and sat on the oversized dingy couch in his living room.\nHe sat for awhile thinking about his job as a male prostitute. This was one\nof the disadvantages of his work -- having your pimp come over unexpectedly to\ntake the money you made the night before. It wasn\'t really all that bad though.\nHank was an allright guy as far as pimps went. Just like anyone else, he\nstill had time before work do whatever he felt like.\n</P>\n\n'),(2637,'<p>After being in the hospital for three weeks you can tell when the I.V. is in right.  This one wasn\\\'t.  For all the care and attention being lavished on the needle insertion process the nurse might as well have been flipping a hamburger at McHospital.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nApparently she had some notion that she had the needle in the right spot because she decided to flush the line with saline or whatever that crap is that you use flush those things.  As she pushed the plunger down I said, \\\"hey, I don\\\'t think that\\\'s in right.  I can feel it burning under my skin.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s in right.\\\"  She didn\\\'t even bother to look at me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe got out the morphine and started to pump it into the line.  I\\\'d been waiting for it all fucking morning.  I loved that shit.  It did dull the pain a little but far more importantly, it made the pain not matter.  It was still there but with the morphine I could just sort of look at it and think about it like it wasn\\\'t really my pain.  I could see how beautiful it was.  It was huge.  It filled my body below my chest and with those nerves in my back so recently smashed and abraded it flowed out from my legs.  The air above my motionless legs hurt.  I could feel the air and it hurt.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith morphine onboard, I could just lay there and take it.  I could just experience it and take it all in from a distance.  It had been a little over four hours since my last dose and the pain was moving in on me again, crawling into the parts of my head that the dope had kept it out of for a while.  I\\\'d spent the last 45 minutes or so just watching the stained ceiling tiles over my bed and waiting for my next shot.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow I was getting the shot.  I could feel it coming out of the needle.  I could feel it burning around the tip of the needle and it was doing me no good at all.  When it\\\'s in the vein you can\\\'t feel it going in, you just know it\\\'s there.  Your supposed to feel it all over, not just where the I.V. is.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I can feel that burning too.  I\\\'m sure the needle\\\'s not in the vein.\\\"  I was polite.  Today, I find that impossible to believe but there it is.  I wasn\\\'t even mad at her.  I just accepted it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s just fine\\\", she said and then she left the room.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI looked up at the ceiling and thought about what I could do to distance myself from the pain that was getting closer.  I thought about what I could do, now that I wasn\\\'t going to be high, to stave off the boredom.  After five minutes or so of fruitless thinking, I looked down at my forearm and the useless I.V.  There were three oddly shaped lumps just up my arm from where the needle pierced the skin.  The largest one was a little over an inch long and about half as wide.  The morphine had puddled under my skin.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Yes?\\\", the nurse said as she walked in the room to respond to the little button I\\\'d pushed.  It was the same nurse as before.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"This isn\\\'t where the morphine should go\\\", I said.  I had my palm turned towards her so she could see the bubbles under my skin.  \\\"I\\\'d like it in my vein please.\\\"  She left the room to go and get a more experienced nurse.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI still wasn\\\'t mad.  That\\\'s just the way things were and I accepted it without comment.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m better now.  I\\\'m an inch shorter than I used to be and I\\\'ve still got a little pain but I\\\'ve learned to walk again and run and climb and ride and just about any other thing you can think of.  If I saw that bitch today, I\\\'d break her fucking jaw.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2638,'<html>\n\n<p>\nSomewhere under the ocean\'s surface in the South Pacific his mind was focused on\nwhat it meant to be clear. A discursive daydream of thoughts, memories, and\nfeelings dredged up in a mostly obscure and perhaps arbitrary manner.\nThe warm, salt water environment was helpful. It rinsed the accumulated crud of\npain and illwill. The constant slur and slush of the underwater experience was\ncleansing.\n</p><p>\nThe task at hand was simple: dive down to the coral and sand bottom; thoroughly\nsurvey what was there; possibly pick up any interesting items; return to the\nsurface for a breath, and then repeat. This was the backdrop to it all. The\nclamour of foreground reality had gradually faded. The eminence of the\nunderwater world came into being. Neptune reigned. Clots and masses gurgled\nwithin to expel what was there: diffidence and it\'s constrictive companion;\nreflexive carbon monoxide grimaces; stains on outer appearance; daylight hour\nlandmines; silent betrayal. These could not come out at once without a saline\nsolution.\n</p><p>\nA whole shell was a brief break. Discovery of nature\'s creation tugged either\nforward or backward. It was not clear, yet. Silence like an empty auditorium and\na distinct thought came forward to present itself within the silent obscurity,\n\"This looks like a good one.\" It was just enough to touch down again, to touch\nthe lifeline rope of routine, to not drift away in the tide. This interjection,\nthis thought object made a good enough interlude, like a jazz horn in an\nextended session signalling the beginning or end of something.\n\n</p><p>\nSeveral more cycles of the surface and descent continued. It took to time to get\nback to that place. Where was it ever anyway? There wasn\'t any point in putting\nany effort into anything. Anything worthwhile would drift in, and hopefully\nanything that needed eliminating would float away. Again, clarity was sought\nafter.\n\n</p><p>\nShould it be that clear perception is just the removal of obstacles preventing\npure light from reaching the destination eye? What about this could be thought \nout which doesn\'t come into place of it\'s own? Would it be clear like the\nremoval of obstruction? The past always seemed like an enormous obstruction, but\nit didn\'t seem fair that the vessel should be faulted for it\'s contents. What\nwas the purpose in tracing the trajectory of a fallen object? It remained\nlodged. What else could be done then? In this world nothing. This was not a\nplace for action or hard existence. What obstructions get chosen for removal and\nfor what reasons? Where is the unseen obstacle created by anothers removal?\n\n</p><p>\nThe grasping thoughts required a breath of air. Gasping and panting air was no\ngood in the water. Slight panting bobbing under the sky was a good break, a\ndrumfill for another descent, but it had to stop. The water was comforting of\nitself, and should not be expected to babble back. Back to the liquid awareness\nand experience of an organism in an element, without thought in deep blue\nsuspension.\n\n</p><p>\nFreedom was soft and furtive, but it remained for that time. The peace of the\nwater was there. He did not need to grab it or take it back in his pocket. That\nwas for the shells, rocks and bits of seaweed. Something happened and was\nsettled now. The cleansing was over and enough.\n\n</p><p>\nHe was ten feet from the surface. Directly above\nhis head the sunlight was visible in beams coming through a large clump of\nfloating seaweed. The tide of the waves above had a swaying influence as he come\nup from the jags of the coral reef. A short bouyant moment later and he was at\nthe surface finally. His head emerged allowing a deep short burst of inhalation.\n</p><p>\n\nThings of the surface were now audible. The splashing of the swell tips and the\ndistant sound of crashing waves came into consciousness. It broke the silent\nstate he had been in, but it was not unwelcome. Through his squint he could make\nout a large portion of the perimeter of the island. Treading water for a few\nminutes while taking in fresh air he thought how good things were. This was a\ngood time. At this moment his entire body acknowledged and appreciated it as\nmuch as his mind.\n\n</p><p>\n\"Ha... whew\", he let out a quick burst of air before eying a distant palm tree\nalready noted as the landmark to return to. If he wasn\'t in water he would be\nslightly sweating by now but he wasn\'t that tired. He began the forward stroke\nto get to the landmark; it was arm over arm, legs kicking, in time with\nbreathing and head turning. This took exhertion now. Minutes and several\ndozen yards later breaking waves should not be that much of a\nproblem for a competent swimmer but it still evoked underpinnings of dread.\nBeing dragged and helpless in the surf was a childhood memory that never left.\nHe kept swimming until he caught up with a swell just as it was about to break.\nHe felt the rush of water as the wave broke a few feet down and in front of him\nwhile he remained unperturbed on the other side of the falling water.\n\n</p><p>\nHis heartrate increased out of anxiety. He had managed to get past these\nwaves in the other direction just a few hours ago but it was daunting. He\nwilled his mind and body to tighten up against the comfort he resisted leaving.\n\"Just get back in\", he thought to himself very much directly conscious now,\nstruggling. The peaceful womb of the ocean was also a destroyer and drowner. It\nmade sense that whatever gave can also take back, but this was not a thought to\nindulge in just now. The worst think about was being unable to breathe, tossed\nlike a rag; desperate for air and panicked; limbs unable to find anything solid\nto steady flailing. And all along the surging and crashing tide relentlessly\npulling everything back to it\'s origin.\n</p><p>\n\nThere wouldn\'t be any danger like that though. A few minutes\nlater his feet finally found solid ground and he emerged backwards in waist high\nin water. It was time to take off the fins now. Trudging along through the waves\nhitting his knees finally he was on land again. Legs now strained against\ngravity. His skin felt the chill of the air. The roar of the waves coming\nback from the distance was a reminder and reassurance he was out of the ocean.\nStanding on the sand of the shore catching his breath he spotted the one who was\nwaiting for him. \"How was it?\", she asked with a smile and a gesture to sit down\nnext to her.\n</p><p>\n\"Good.\"\n</p><p>\n\"Did you find what you were looking for?\"\n</p><p>\n\"Yeah.\"\n</p><p>\n\"Where is it?\"\n</p><p>\n\"I don\'t know.\"\n</p>\n\n\n</html>\n\n\n'),(2639,'<P>lately it occurs to me<BR>\r\nwhat a long strange trip it\\\'s been<BR>\r\n-j garcia\r\n</P><P>\r\nhello.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m Chuckspeed, or Chuck for short.  Got the nickname shortly after high school - it was the speed at which driveline vibration kicked cassettes outta the deck in my Opel GT.  Just a shade north of a buck ten, if you were wondering.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m in my early 40\\\'s.  I like to tell stories. True stories.  Been all over the world and seen strange shit stories.  It\\\'s like they say tho - the strangest stuff happens within a few miles of home...\r\n</P><P>\r\nTake my education, for instance.  Catholic elementary/jr. high/high school.  Bound to mess damned near anybody up.  Especially sex ed.  Poor nuns couldn\\\'t handle it - hell, some of \\\'em hadn\\\'t seen their feet for years, much less their vaginas.  So they pass the ball (and other genitalia) off to good \\\'ol Father Ted.  Real regular guy, Fr. Ted was - liked hangin\\\' out with the guys.  So he gets in front of us, and proceeds to \\\'splain the birds and the bees.\r\n</P><P>\r\nRiight.  Like a priest knows what to do with a bling bling schwing.  He didn\\\'t have Colleen McDonald sittin\\\' next to him in math - she had legs a mile long, and hair down to her tight little ass.  Coulda drilled concrete with my pecker just thinkin\\\' about her. She ended up marrying some gas station jockey, moved into a trailer park, and had a bazillion kids. Go figure. \r\n</P><P>\r\nAnyway, Fr. Ted starts to \\\'splain the details of umm...procreation. As he\\\'s doin\\\' it, he starts actin\\\' all weird and shit - starts talkin\\\' thru his teeth, head gets all jerky, and veins start to stick out on his neck.  I\\\'m like whoa, man...Take a break or something.  Hell, he made it *look* painful just talkin\\\' about it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nCouple weeks later, Fr. Ted was gone.  Turned out he knew all sorts of neato things to do with a stiff dick, and wasn\\\'t afraid of trying them on for size, so to speak.  They shipped him off to some reservation out West, and that was the last we heard of good \\\'ol Fr. Ted.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSex ed by a child molester.  Don\\\'t hafta make anything up - life is too f\\\'n weird as it is.  </P>'),(2640,'<P><B>hospital kit:</B><BR>- 3 sets of clothes (washer and dryer available on the ward)<BR>- extra blanket (can also be used as yoga mat)<BR>- <U>to the lighthouse</U><BR>- <U>my antonia</U><BR>- notebooks<BR>- personal hygiene items<BR>- CD player with headphones<BR>- CDs: \\\"astral weeks,\\\" \\\"station to station,\\\" \\\"brilliant corners\\\"<BR>- statue of the laughing buddha<BR>- tiny totem animals: glass falcon, wooden monkey, plastic alligator and fish, horse with fake hair, mane and tail</P><P><B>you will be taking:</B><BR>- zyprexa (mood stabilization)<BR>- paxil (depression, social anxiety, generalized anxiety)<BR>- wellbutrin (to combat sexual side effects of paxil)<BR>- ativan (anxiety)<BR>- librium (cocaine withdrawal)<BR>- folic acid (for any incipient nerve damage from alcohol)<BR>- levoxyl (thyroid)<BR>(who thinks up drug names? i want that job.)</P><P><B>in your room:</B><BR>- 1 matthew<BR>- 1 bed<BR>- desk<BR>- armoire<BR>- oxygen outlet and other medical things above the bed<BR>- nothing to hang a sheet off of</P><P><B>anxiety attacks:</B><BR>yes, i\\\'m sorry, but you\\\'re going to get some. unscheduled. nothing to be done about it. just flow with it.</P><P><B>in the dayroom:</B><BR>- television<BR>- vcr<BR>- videos: \\\"out to sea,\\\" \\\"good will hunting,\\\" \\\"joe\\\'s apartment,\\\" \\\"high noon\\\"<BR>- wooden-framed chairs and sofas with vinyl-covered cushions<BR>- your fellow travelers. not a bad group, as a whole. mostly depressives and substance abusers. they tend to fart a lot, but other than that, i won\\\'t hear an unkind word against them.</P><P><B>the cafeteria:</B><BR>- tables and chairs<BR>(- if you arrange the trays correctly, four of them can fit on a table)<BR>- the window food is served through<BR>- the wingnuts from the other ward<BR>- cindy, who will throw her drink on you, hit you or cuss your mama. just ignore her.<BR>- elvis priestly. that\\\'s what he calls himself anyway. i don\\\'t think i know his name. fat, shaved head, around 45. sings elvis songs and asks if you want to fight him. feels your arm. \\\"ooh, big arms. you think i can take you? i think i can.\\\"</P><P><B>your schedule:</B><BR>3 or 4 am: get rocked out of bed by KILLER freebase dream<BR>3 or 4 am \\\'til sunrise:  write (or sit there. no getting up.)<BR>- sunrise \\\'til they call you for breakfast: yoga and qijong<BR>- breakfast<BR>- morning meds<BR>- community group: this is where you get to bitch about your air not working or voice any practical concerns<BR>- group psychotherapy<BR>- narcotics anonymous meeting. everybody says, \\\"hello, i\\\'m <U>(insert name here)</U>. i\\\'m an addict.\\\" i say, \\\"hi, i\\\'m matt. i\\\'m a human being.\\\" i don\\\'t like labeling myself.<BR>- the doctor visits.<BR>- lunch<BR>- stress management group<BR>- group therapy<BR>- art or music therapy<BR>- outdoor recreation<BR>- shower then shave while being watched by nurse\\\'s aide<BR>- talk to case manager<BR>- dinner<BR>- relaxation therapy<BR>- alcoholics anonymous meeting<BR>- watch tv in dayroom<BR>- visitation time<BR>- surf net or read until bedtime<BR>- nightly meds<BR>- go to sleep<BR>- p.s. - smoke breaks scattered throughout day and more free time on weekends</P><P><B>so who comes to see you?</B><BR>- mom<BR>- dad<BR>- grandma<BR>- blair</P><P><B>who\\\'s blair?</B><BR>- my girlfriend (one of my girlfriends anyway. the other one\\\'s chilling out in switzerland making my baby. maybe. chances are 50/50 it\\\'s mine. hi, anna.) she\\\'s twenty.</P><P><B>don\\\'t you think that\\\'s a little young for you?</B><BR>- not at all. emotionally, i\\\'m about 17, anyway.</P><P><B>what did you do for your birthday, you big aries stud, you?</B><BR>- mom, dad, grandma, morgan, paige and blair checked me out of the unit and took me to lunch at this greek place in town. i had shrimp baked with black olives, green peppers, onions, and feta cheese. yellow rice on the side. after the meal, the waitress brought out a square of flan with a candle lit in the center of it. everybody sang. it was magic, baby.</P><P><B>who hasn\\\'t been to see you in the hospital yet?</B><BR>- morgan, even though she works in the same hospital. i guess she\\\'s just busy. she came to my birthday lunch, though. that counts, i guess.</P><P><B>what are all those little animals you took to the hospital for?</B><BR>- i arranged them on the desk around the buddha figure as a kind of altar. they help with the writing and the living.</P><P><B>are you getting any better?</B><BR>- the proof\\\'s in the pudding, baby.</P><P><B>does writing scare you?</B><BR>-  yeah, it shits me up, but it also feels really good. gets my endorphin flowing.</P><P><B>what are you writing?</B><BR>- i\\\'m working on a novel. if i can\\\'t get started on it, i write SAMO grafitti in my notebook until the sun comes up.<BR><BR>- samples:<BR><BR>SAMO IS A GANGSTER NOW. RIDE AROUND IN A PHAT-ROLLING CADDY WITH GEORGE HARRISON AND MACKENZIE KING AND YOUR GREEDY GREEDY GRANDMAMMY. HOT EUCHRE GAME WE PLAY AT THE STOPLIGHTS. GRANNY SAYS HI AND STOP PLAYING WITH YOURSELF.<BR><BR>SAMO GOTS THE GOOD PAINT IN HEAVEN. DANCE THE SAMBA ALL DAY LONG. TELL JULIAN SCHNABEL THE BITCH OWE ME NOW.<BR><BR>GEORGE BUSH IS A GANGSTER NOW. PERE ET FILS. WE MAKE HIM RIDE IN THE TRUNK.</P><P><B>do you snore</B><BR>- i have been told i do. personally, i can neither confirm or deny this.</P>'),(2641,'<p>I can\\\'t sleep. I\\\'ve been wanting to write on SWINNEY.ORG (insert jay\\\'s beauty competition voice here) but I haven\\\'t had the guts. I think that\\\'s why I started writing on SWINNEY.ORG in the first place, as well as chatting all night and laying around in s5\\\'s naughty bits hot room with the human flesh SWINNEY.ORG namesake. I couldn\\\'t sleep. especially when I quit smoking.</p>\r\n<p>I can\\\'t sleep now.  All sorts of mental maggots are eating me alive. All day long I drift through interactions like a limp skanky puppy freshly punted out of bed by a bored teenage owner. sometimes I growl, sometimes I pitifully yowl. sometimes I pant with insipid optimism. It depends on my position on this particular cycle of pathos. </p>\r\n<p> By the end of my time outside of this grand country, I made proclamations. Especially in the heat of my tropical fever in which I had Kafka-esque hallucinations. No, it doesnt mean I dreamed I was a big bug. Thats not really what \\\"Kafka-esque\\\" means, you nimroid. It means a feeling of emptiness so large that it feels like 10 empty playgrounds right after dusk when you are too young to find your way home in the dark and everyone forgot to pick you up. Here\\\'s what I emailed to a friend:</p>\r\n<p><i>I got this freaky fever the week before I left thailand for a week. it was\r\na hallucinatory experience like they say it is, and it sapped me out, as\r\ndid the beaches, shopping malls, and repressive social mores of the entire\r\ncountry. the heat, mostly. heat. but it was not a hallucinatory experience\r\nlike I came out of it wanting to paint on large canvasses or write an\r\nopera, it was a cerebrally exhausting experience, like I came out of it\r\nwanting to eat a lot of peanut butter and never think again. I can barely\r\nread a novel.</p>\r\n<p>im just really fucking sick of everything man. now im boring myself. i\r\nlook forward to seeing you again and giving you a THAI MASSAGE. i hope you\r\nlike being brutalized.</p></i>\r\n<p>That was the most feeling I could muster in english words for today. Now the sun is coming up. Hello, lover, I want to say, but actually I loathe the entire event and I\\\'m not going to haul my mangy moth-eaten dog ass to the roof to have a poetic experience as I rage on the brink of madness watching the light change on the horizon around me. No, Im going to sit my ass here and maybe eat some peanut butter.</p>\r\n<p>Llike I was saying, I made proclamations. Proclamations that I would never travel again, or not for a long time. Not for a LONG LONG TIme!!! NEver go no place again. no backpacks, no trains,  just peanut butter. </p>\r\n<p>And yet at this hour, and at other magic hours when Im half-croaking, trying to hang onto the last morsels of sleep that the merciless lord was able to spare, I find myself fantasizing about leaving here immediately and going on somewhere else. </p>\r\n<p>The morning before now it was Boston. I want to eat lobster, plain and simple, crack it open with my hands nad dip into hot butter. Really most fantasies and repressions I express through food, it\\\'s because Im a Cancer rising. Whenever I feel too much for the world I eat my anger and other tedious emotions. </p>\r\n<p>Most of this angsty talking is made up and I will be asleep soon trying not to think about the Bill Paxton movie in which this scary man stabs his mother while she is hanging laundry. I wont keep seeing the splattered blood over and over, no I won\\\'t. or hearing that corny horror jolt music when he touches the demons and sees their sins. I will think of New York City where I can walk and talk with ye olde girles of yore and spend a long time in brooklyn in the morning putting on a lot of disgusting black eyeliner.  or Lawrence, Kansas. and oh the lightning storms in that part of the world when it\\\'s summer and you drive across.</p>'),(2642,'I have this recurring dream...<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m on the Barstow - to Vegas run in a \\\'57 Desoto Firesweep 2dr hardtop, midnight green.  I\\\'m hungry, which means I\\\'ve probably decided to go to Yolie\\\'s for Brazilian...When the mood hits you, ya gotta go.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnyway, I\\\'m about 20 minutes out of Barstow having just worked  thru a plug of traffic when this rat bastard weasel driving a \\\'74-ish white Trans-Am explodes out of the huddled mass of mundane transport behind me.  He gets real big in the rearview real fast, even though I\\\'ve got a quarter-mile lead at a smooth 95.  I catch a glimpse of the 455 - SD on the side of the shaker as he blows by at about a buck and a quarter.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m intrigued.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI disengage the tailshaft - mounted overdrive on the TorqueFlite, and ease into the throttle.  The punched - out 392 Hemi I\\\'ve dropped in the engine bay acknowledges the request with a  symphony of sound unique to vintage modified hemis.  A few ticks later, the \\\'Sweep has matched speed with the T/A, albeit with a judicious eighth of a mile leash.  The tach settles in just under 5500, so I kick the O/D back in...and watch.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe\\\'d breezed by a couple of loose traffic formations when we run into a tight plug consisting of the usual collection of RV\\\'s, semis, and the odd old fart oblivious to the fact there is a right lane...Normal Anti-Destination League stuff.  I hang back to see how the T/A handles this.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWith just a light tap of the brakes, the weasel dives in, and immediately starts sawing through the plug.  I hail from the \\\"run silent, run deep\\\" school of thought...You know, quiet lane changes, smooth acceleration...don\\\'t ruffle feathers on the way thru. Not this guy.  His lane changes are abrupt enough to upset the rear suspension after taking his lane, scaring the bejeebers out of the guy he just pulled in front of.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Idiot\\\", I think as he jams the T/A in front of a Winnebago and hammers the brakes.  The \\\'Bago is pulling a powder blue Escort, which immediately commences an ominous oscillation across the road.  The \\\'Bago manages to gather the Escort in, but not before some hapless Civic driver takes a trip through the local vegetation to avoid getting punted off the road.  Not only is the T/A driver a rat bastard weasel, he\\\'s psycho, too.  Time to give this guy a wide berth.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI hang at the back of the plug until I\\\'m certain the T/A is gone, then cautiously work though.  Doesn\\\'t take me long...most everybody\\\'s so shaken, they pull aside and let me by.  I get to the front of the plug, and the T/A\\\'s nowhere to be seen.  After a couple of miles, the memory of the incident fades, and I\\\'m back into cruise mode, save for shaving a few MPH of the top as insurance against encountering psycho-weasel again.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAbout 20 miles out of Baker, I encounter yet another slow-moving mass of traffic. This one is particularly pesky, as there is a double bottom tanker attempting to pass a car hauler.  The car hauler is going 62...The tanker is going about 62.1.  The process seems to take forever.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nI work my way up until I\\\'m directly behind the tanker.  I\\\'m getting impatient, because the tanker has nearly passed the hauler on more than one occasion, only to fall back.  I make the decision to pass on the right the next time there\\\'s enough room, so I kick off the overdrive, and punch up \\\"2\\\" on the shifter pod.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere it is.  The tanker pulls up, and I squirt into the opening. Then...<br><br>\r\n\r\nI glance in the rearview to check distance on the hauler, and I see something which defies belief.  Here comes the weasel, passing everything in sight on the left-hand shoulder!  He must have stopped at the rest area a few miles back.  For a split second, it doesn\\\'t register...When I finally get it, the rat bastard weasel is even with me on the opposite side of the tanker.  He startles the tanker driver, who immediately swerves into my lane, sees me there, and swerves back.  This sets up a killer fishtail on the pup, and it\\\'s obvious it\\\'s only a matter of seconds before the Eastbound 15 looks like a scene straight out of The Road Warrior.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI head for the shoulder, which is covered with marbles.  I feather the throttle, steering with the back wheels while I try to get ahead of the ever-increasing arc of the now-doomed tanker truck.  Amazingly, the momentum of the T/A carries it ahead of the tanker, where the weasel damn near loses it getting back onto the highway, gathers it up, and takes off...<br><br>\r\n\r\nBy now, I\\\'m PISSED. While occupied with unrepeatable thoughts, I get the \\\'Sweep going fast enough to pull ahead of the tanker.  The tanker makes one last swing and falls on its side as if to mock a dying elephant. As the tragedy unfolds behind me, I find myself focused on one thing...catching the rat bastard weasel.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI center the Desoto on the highway, and whack the throttle plates open on four Weber downdrafts sitting atop custom short-runner manifolds...I smile inwardly as the roar of induction rises to the challenge. Won\\\'t be long \\\'till I catch up...<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe Hemi is in high song now.  The Desoto is settling into the pavement,  with Virgil Exner\\\'s famous fins aided by a contoured belly pan which adds downforce at speed.  All of a sudden, the motor makes a burbling noise. I look at the tach...Shit!  7400 RPM!  Even with solid lifters and titanium rockers, the valves are gonna float...<br><br>\r\n\r\nIn the heat of survival, I\\\'d forgotten to shift out of second.  I punch up third, and the motor recovers.  I wind her out, and engage the overdrive.  Theoretically, I should be able to hit about 180 if the Hemi\\\'s healthy...right now though, I\\\'m not interested in theory.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m gaining on the T/A.  Fast.  As the gap closes, the weasel sees me.  A small puff of unburnt hydrocarbons signals he\\\'s going for it...I don\\\'t care, because I\\\'m still closing the gap.  The SuperDuty in the weasel\\\'s T/A is heavily modified, as he actually pulls away for a bit as his motor comes on the cam.  It\\\'s a narrow band, though...Moments later I\\\'m able to take back the gap as his motor runs out of steam.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nWe are hauling now.  The Desoto has a 150 MPH speedo from a Chrysler 300 E, and the needle\\\'s  bouncing off the internal stop.  The blood is pounding in my ears from the adrenaline buzz.  The world has condensed to a single object...The garish, over spoilered, \\\"Gee-I\\\'m-a-racecar\\\" ass end of the rat bastard weasel.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSpeed borne from anger is a dangerous thing.  For starters, the sensation of high velocity is lost...It is as if one is stationary, and the world is slipstreaming around you.  On top of this, all fear is gone, along with the dose of common sense which keeps us grounded in reality... <br><br>\r\n\r\nAs I enter the draft of the T/A, I\\\'m able to lift off the throttle a bit.  As another wave of anger washes over me, I decide to announce my presence with a \\\"love tap\\\" on the weasel\\\'s hindquarters.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"HERE\\\'S TWO TONS OF HIGHLAND PARK HELL!\\\", I scream above the wail of the engine.  He must have seen my contorted expression, as the rat appears visibly shaken.  He hunkers down over the wheel, staring straight ahead.  I have him on the run now.  I\\\'m not about to give up the chase.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe run.  Nose to tail.  A traffic plug appears on the horizon, and  is upon us impossibly fast.  We slow a bit, and cut through the plug like a hot knife.  I don\\\'t bother to check the mirrors...Figure if the rat made a hole, I can follow. <br><br>\r\n \r\nWe top a rise, and Baker appears on the valley floor below us. Normally, there\\\'s a CHP working the Baker stretch, but instinct tells me that the mess created 20-odd miles back has called in every cop in a hundred mile radius.  I continue to hang on the rat\\\'s ass.  By now, I\\\'ve got a strategy...<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe T/A\\\'s probably running a modified 455...a strong motor, capable of lots of horsepower and torque.  You can run one pretty hard but they don\\\'t like extended high-RPM use, which was exactly what this one was getting.  Even with lots of trick parts, they don\\\'t last long when they\\\'re taken into the nether regions of the RPM band.  On the other hand, the Hemi was one of the more perfect horsepower pumps ever built, and I had the advantage of 40 years of materials technology advancement to improve durability...soo...I decide to wait him out, and may the best motor win.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe charge up the Baker Grade.  It quickly turns into an uphill  slalom as we dodge traffic moving 100 MPH slower.  Right now, I\\\'m thankful for the Pirelli Pzeros and the lowered suspension from a donor Dodge D-500, as I\\\'m having no trouble sticking to the rat\\\'s tail.    My predatory instincts are running full-bore.  I\\\'m actually starting to enjoy myself...<br><br>\r\n\r\nAbout 2/3rds of the way up the grade, I lose a cylinder.  I lift.  I check behind me for the oil cloud which indicates a holed piston...nothing.  Therefore, I probably lost a rocker from the over-rev.  I get back in the throttle, but it\\\'s not as crisp as before.  I have to work hard to keep up to the bastard now.  Where I once had brute force on my side, I now have to plan every move to conserve the precious forward momentum earned.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe bastard starts to pull from me.  I struggle, but it gets harder to earn back the precious feet of asphalt lost. I begin to think about conserving what\\\'s left of the Hemi, when I notice a thin wisp of smoke from the back of the T/A....<br><br>\r\n\r\nAt first, I think I\\\'m trying to see things that are not there...But no, it\\\'s true!  Looks like the rat is overheating, and may have broken a ring or two.  If so, I\\\'ll be able to catch up...Sure enough, I\\\'m right back on him a few moments later.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI announce my return with another whack on his rear end.  By now, I can see him shaking as he furtively attempts to get the car to go faster.  He begins to beat on the steering wheel, as if this will goad the car into pulling away.  I smack him once more for good measure.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI can see the Clark Mountain pass just ahead.  At over 4500 feet with sick engines, both cars way down on power, we\\\'re still running a solid 130 MPH...Seems like crawling after the blast on the desert floor.<br><br>\r\n\r\nKA-WHAM!  <br><br>\r\n\r\nAn explosion rattles off the walls of the pass. <br><br>\r\n\r\n \\\"Fuckfuckfuckfuckfuck...I blew the motor\\\", I think as I lift and punch up \\\"N\\\" on the pod.  I blip the throttle, and stare incredulously as the tach needle responds.  I look up. The T/A is losing speed.<br><br>\r\n\r\nQuickly, I punch up \\\"D\\\", tip back in, and whip into the far left lane.  The Hemi responds.  I make a quick mental note to donate large sums of money in the name of the man who designed the heart of this wonderful beast.  I pull alongside the Trans Am. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe rat bastard weasel rolls down his window.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe expression of pain, suffering and utter defeat defies description.  For a moment, I feel sorry for the guy...He probably poured his soul into the dying hulk coasting towards the summit and its now visible CHP patrol. Then I remembered: This guy is the Rat Bastard Weasel.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOur eyes meet.  For a moment, dead silence.  I search for something to say.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Nice piece of lawn sculpture ya got there, boy!\\\", I say as I smile and wave.  The guy\\\'s jaw drops.  Mission accomplished.  I ease back into the throttle as the CHP pulls up behind the mortally wounded T/A, lights flashing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI get off at the Searchlight exit, just before Stateline...Don\\\'t want to take a chance on a border roadblock.  It seems the logic behind driving 300 miles for dinner has...thrown a rod, so to speak.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBy the way...I dream in color.<br><br>\r\n'),(2643,'<p>\r\nthe dude three terminals down from me is listening to bon jovi...how fitting for a story this crazy.....ITS MY NIGHT SOMETHING SOMETHING SOMETHING LAST FOREVER!!!!!\r\n<P>\r\nsaturday night/sunday morning. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe night was fun. cops stopped me and shane for wrestling on the street. somehow i had ended up spending close to 100,000won on alcohol,,,which in this country is hard to do. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme shane judy and geyoung(sp) get out of a cab, right in the heart of gumo dong market. as we get out of the cab we hear a woman screaming so me and shane go and investigate. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe find a couple of men hitting a girl sitting on the sidewalk crying. turns out later that one of the dudes giving the beats is her brother. we didnt know at the time so we tell them to stop. they stop only to yell at us. at this point the girls mom is there and both the mom and the daughter are trying to hit me and shane...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe decide to leave because... well its just not the type of situation we want to get into. with the whole family there im sure our broken korean wouldnt have done anything to help. apparently this kind of thing happens a lot here. one of the teachers at my school said he\\\'s seen a man hit his wife unconcious on the street and another grab at a baby and dangle it infront of the wife as if to say she cant have him/her ever again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe leave the scene and start walking home. ten minutes later the brother of the girl getting beat comes back down the road yelling at us waving a broom. as he\\\'s walking down the street he\\\'s hitting the steel shutters of the stores and making a lot of noise, both verbally and with his weapon. the situation was turning somewhat intersting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas he gets closer, me and shane stand our ground, telling the guy to relax in korean and english. he starts calling us americans, one of the reasons i think he wanted to attack us with the broom so much. we insist we arent americans, which doesnt seem to calm his nerves at all. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwalking closer he puts the broom through a phone booth window. stumbling back and forth he winds up on the street. dropping the broom he then proceeds to put his leg through the drives side window of a car parked on the street. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor some reason, none of this at the time freaks me out. im just standing there staring at the wild man saying im sure very rude things about me and shane. our korean friends keep telling us to leave, but me and shane decide to walk slowly, and backwards i must add, to make sure the drunk man didnt sneak attack us north korean style. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are commercials on the united states air force tv station that promote the idea that \\\"beer bottles are not a weapon\\\". the night of drinking had filled my pockets with various shot glasses, soju cups and other baubles stolen in my drunken giddyness. i had the shot glass cupped in my hand, ready to use in defense. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnothing happened, and no fight occured, but a phone booth has a hole in it, and some hard working korean had the window of his car shattered. im finding more and more that when koreans get drunk, their distrust of westerners becomes exposed and honest. this isnt a violent nation, and im sure the only reason something of this calibre occured was because he thought that \\\"the white man was once again trying to meddle in the business of a korean\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas much as i dont like this moral, the moral is, if you see something happening that you dont approve of(in a foreign land), just let it be. this isnt canada...and more importantly this isnt america. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce again, this is chiamattt reporting to you from s.korea. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2644,'<p>My eleven year old daughter was sexually assaulted last Thursday.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The kids that did it are bullies.  They\\\'ve been suspended a bunch of times - they do it to get extra vacation time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They\\\'re all in the fifth grade.  Fifth grade.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\\\'s a genetic anomaly in my family - we finish growing by age 13.  My older daughter (13) is done growing already. My younger daughter is not far behind - the body of an adult, the mind of a child.  It\\\'s not their fault - yet they feel it is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Level II sexual misconduct - touching with intent to arouse - that\\\'s what the preliminary police report says.  It happened at school.  School\\\'s counseling the the kids - but not my daughter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She\\\'s different now.  Wears loose clothing and baseball caps - wants to do boy things...wants the whole incident to go away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My wife?  PISSSED.  ANGRY.  FRUSTRATED.  We\\\'ve been told by the cops not to allow our daughters to be out in public unescorted by an adult.  They\\\'ve also told us to be on guard - the parents of the agressors may retaliate.  Hell, we all live in the same neighborhood! Wife wants the kids banished forever to some \\\'other\\\' district. Great. Push the problem off on unsuspecting parents across town.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>11 year old sex offenders - I\\\'m having trouble with this.  My wife wants me to get angry with her.  Am I capable?  Sure.  Truth is, though...I don\\\'t know what to feel.  There are times I agree with my daughter - i want it to *go* away.  I don\\\'t want to deal with it.  Then again, the kids have demonstrated an escalation of unacceptable behavior.  Somebody has to show these kids there are limits. I know how Pop would deal with it - he kicked the shit outta a neighbor whose dog bit my little brother back when we were kids.  The neighbor was a Detroit cop - Pop didn\\\'t care.  Dog wasn\\\'t a problem after that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Night after it happened, I brought home a single red rose for my daughter.  She put it in a pop bottle and took it to her room.  Afterwards, she came out and gave me a big hug.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what happens next.  She didn\\\'t do anything wrong.  She wants her breasts removed.  I don\\\'t want this in the papers or on TV, but the police report filed today may make it go public.  Wife wants me to write a letter to the superintendent of the school district, demanding expulsion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They\\\'re kids, dammit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But then again, her eyes tell a different story.</p>'),(2645,'<p>the length of my arm, from the smooth hollow where the ball of my humerus rests in its socket down to the tips of my fingers, this length could half encircle you.  fiery bolts zinging down from the spine. flowing up and down, as we lay under the window i carved with my hands.  the night pouring in through \r\n\r\nthe glass, blue-lit.  there are reds and golds, flowing both directions, our feet press together.  i \r\n\r\nhave warmed you.  your hands are always cool.  i feel your back as though we had the same tattoo, as \r\n\r\nthough life had shaped us the same, given us the same leanings and asymmetries.  from the beginning we were\r\nmirrors to one another, flesh gold and curved mirrors, the shining eyes, beauty and also fear.  mirrors \r\n\r\nthat press up against one another, creating infinite space between them, a hallway of emptiness, two \r\n\r\nmagnets now attracting, now repelling so violently that one flies against a wall and knocks down all the \r\n\r\npictures.</p>\r\n<p>i ate an apple while you fooled with the kitestring.  i concentrated on tasting it, on not paying \r\n\r\nattention to what you were doing or thinking or worrying about.  pulling my reds and golds into me, into \r\n\r\nmy saliva and my tounge, into the apple while i chewed it into me, its reds and golds flowing down and \r\n\r\nup, its sugars burning into the blinking of my eyelids, the swivel of my arm in its socket as i hucked \r\n\r\nthe core away into the blue field.  the wind was chopping at my head and i felt the sugar on my teeth \r\n\r\nburn.  pulling my hood around my face, still focusing on my insides, my darks and reds, i flopped down \r\n\r\nin the green itchy grass to examine the spirals and scales of weeds waving in front of me.  stuck to me.  \r\n\r\nmy toes cold and giggling in the breeze.  ears like seashells catching the melody of dogs\\\' owners \r\n\r\ncalling, of kids escaping into safe shouting distance, of the kite snapping and whirring like some \r\n\r\nferocious insect above me.</p>\r\n<p>later bed again, total exhausted stillness.  my insides blown clean by the sea, one sip of a martini \r\n\r\nsending my liver into gales of protest, so i set it down with its sunken olives and lie back staring at \r\n\r\nthe ceiling, content to watch the retinal gold ghosts floating up there. body humming and slack, sore \r\n\r\nmuscles resting inside skin, bones heavy.  feeling the whirlpool sinking into my chest, grey water \r\n\r\nrushing in there where the heart-pull sucks downward.  bringing the fire to the forefront, focusing \r\n\r\neffort on lifting up the reds and golds to the front of the body, to the heart, the tips of the breasts, \r\n\r\nthe sex, pooling gold beneath grey stagnant sucking wound of heart.  breathing fire up into the cold.  \r\n\r\nfire through the shoulders, through the wrists, into the palms, torches for arms, flashlights up to the \r\n\r\nceiling in the dusk.  forces at war in my chest. dark swirling in there with light.  sending soft pulses \r\n\r\nout and down through my fingertips and into your skin.  you resting sweetly in my arms.  utter repose as \r\n\r\nthe sky drains itself of color outside and the leaves whisper against the window.</p>\r\n<p>through the spine flow all the woven together rememberings of heaven and earth, the up and down current, and through my arms, making spots of color in my palms, flashlights in the darkness.  i shine the lights into your back while my arms circle around your currents.  we breathe together in silence.  i wrap you in the length of both my arms and encircle you \r\ncompletely, protect you from all harm.   </p>'),(2646,'I\\\'m being sued for unpaid medical bills and it all seems horribly important.  So rather than do my job, which I\\\'m not liking at all right now, I start thinking...\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nDid my fingernail come from?  Well, of course it just grew there on the end of my finger but where then did the actual material come from?  I ate stuff.  Mostly crap.  Hot dogs, ramen noodles (the cheap kind), mild cheddar, tortillas.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo then my fingernail is made of hot dogs and ramen noodles and so forth.  But where did that stuff come from?  I mean when you get down to it, my fingernail and my hot dogs and my ramen noodles are all made of stars and asteroids and shit.  The atoms that make up my finger nails are, according to current estimates, something like 13 billion years old.<br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n13 billion.  My brain is not anywhere near large enough to accomodate a number like that.  Earth is supposed to be about 4.5 billion years old (according to the bible it\\\'s about 6000 years old).  So you could say that my fingernail arrived in the general part of the universe about 8.5 billion years after the whole thing just went pow and started flying around all over the place.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe whole human race is just a little microscopic blip in time.  My life is not really worth considering (unless you\\\'re me) even in comparison to that little blip.  I guess what I\\\'m really saying is that my fingernail is more magestic than my consciousness.  At least it will be around longer... in some form or another.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nRight.  So back to my medical bills.  Those certainly seem more important than the work I\\\'m supposed to be doing right now for my job.  Taking into account the big picture of my life, the $6500.00 really isn\\\'t all that important.  Of course, taking into consideration the majesty and beauty of the time traveling tip of my left index finger, my life isn\\\'t so terribly important.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIn light of all this existential horror, how can I be expected to build a stupid fucking web site?\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI\\\'m sorry, this has been a total waste of time.'),(2647,'A blank document stares at him. It stares at him. It stares at him and he cannot meet its gaze directly.<br> He is frightened, he is frightened of the document. He thinks also it may be frightened of him too. He does not <br>know why either of them should be frightened, but none of that matters now. None of that matters now.\r\n<p>\r\nA month ago there was a blank document just like this one. It stared at him. Documents do not have eyes,<br> a voice says. They do not have eyes and canâ€™t stare at you. You can stare at them but they canâ€™t <br>stare at you. You. You. You. They canâ€™t stare at you.\r\n<p>\r\nDocuments can\\\'t stare especially blank ones. He is not insane. Not. Insane. He reads documents all day,<br> they do not read him. Documents cant read. No eyes.\r\n<p>\r\nNo eyes and the blank document stares at him, stares at him, stares at him, a BLANK document for <br>christs sake. A FUCKING BLANK DOCUMENT, a blank document, stares. At him. \r\n<p>\r\nHe puts it on the desk. It lies there quietly, staring. It changes colors. surprised? It does not <br>move. It changes back to white.\r\n<p>\r\nThe document sits there, it lies there, it stands there, staring. He stares back for once, defiantly. <br>He sees nothing. He looks away. It has resumed staring. It stares, it stares at him. He picks it up, he puts it in a <br>drawer. Closes the drawer. \r\n<p>\r\nCloses. Silence. Silence. Silence\r\n<p>\r\nSilence\r\n<p>\r\nSilence\r\n<p>\r\nWhite   <br><br>                                                                                            Â Â Â Â Light\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nBright								<br><br>		           \r\n\r\nÂ Â Â Â Light\r\n<p>\r\nWhite clock. Black hands. \r\n<p>\r\nHe does not see it.  It is in the drawer and he does not see it and cannot see it staring. He cannot <br>stare at it, and it cannot stare at the document. The document, documeeeennntttnnntttadsfhgerthgrhjrjyt<br>klyuio;ioâ€™piâ€™poâ€™poâ€™popoâ€™oipâ€™poiâ€™oipâ€™podocuuuuuuuuuuuumeemmmmmeeeeeeennnnntnntnntnntntntntntntnntntnttttttttttttttttttttttttt\r\n<p>\r\n.\r\n<p>\r\nis it\r\n<p>\r\nis it\r\n<p>\r\neven still there?<br>\r\nIs it humming? Is that the fluorescent white (white light) light? Hummmmmming?\r\nDocuments canâ€™t hum.<br> Documents donâ€™t have mouths. <br>Donâ€™t be silly. <br>Donâ€™t be so silly. \r\n<p>\r\nDon\\\'t be silly.\r\n'),(2648,'<P>last night owen rocked me to sleep as if i were a child, my forehead pressed close to his chest. i could hear his steady heart beneath the strong piano keys of his ribs despite the noise of the traffic. i decided to count the beats per minute, aimless at first, and then very precise, a measurement for my personal gravitational constant. a single number to hold me to this world. \r\n\r\n</P><BR><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nmost nights my body threatens to whirl off the planet when it enters dreamworld, reluctant to return. i tend to postpone sleep, wistfully but resolutely looking away from my pillow. i\\\'ve often woken up with all ten toes burrowed deep into the mattress, desperately holding on to my bed. but yesterday my eyelids were already trembling in the evening, clusters of cells whispering escape. \r\n\r\n\r\n</P><BR><P>\r\n\r\nearlier, at the hospital, i had refused to go in and see the bluish face of my father. it was owen who had to take the x-rays the nurse was holding out to me. i stared at her orange lipstick like it was a piece of tropical fruit. owen curled his little finger over my wrist with his right hand as he rolled up the blueprint of my father\\\'s new body in his left. when we finally got home, i wanted to throw them into the trash compactor, but he slipped them out of my hands, like we were playing a game, just a game, and pulled me to him. \r\n\r\n\r\n</P><BR><P>\r\n\r\nwe stood there in the kitchen like that, me leaning against him so that if he had moved i would have fallen. we stood together for a long time, letting the house get dark, not moving, the stark streetlights eventually coming on and passing in through the open blinds. i could only see parts of his body: an eyebrow, his left wrist, the front of his brown shoes. he kept whispering something into my hair, very low, over and over again. \r\n</P>'),(2649,'I learned how fragile my body is recently. I feel like an old lady who is afraid to walk alone for fear of falling. I have fallen many times in my life, but I never broken before. I am trying hard not to let the fear take over.\r\n<p>\r\nIt was so dumb. I only fell a few feet when the ladder pulled away from the loft bed,  I should have landed with some bruises that I could show off as they faded away, but something went wrong when the arm I extended to the ground made a horrid snapping sound, followed almost instantly by my screams. There is a direct line between my pain receptors and my vocal cords. I am not stoic at all. I screamed even louder when I tried to sit up and saw my forearm dangling in a grotesque way from the elbow. Fuck its not connected anymore and my other hand grasped the joint to try and keep the arm from swinging with every movement a trigger of more pain than I knew how to process. The screaming was replaced by moaning and hyperventilating as Chaya crutched around trying to get me help fast.\r\n<p>\r\nI have no idea how I managed to stand and walk to the car. I just wanted someone to make it better. Hysterical is the only word for my state. Squeaking, moaning, breathing short and fast, trying to answer questions the nurse was asking, thank god David could speak, it was beyond me. Then they pumped me full of painkillers, Demerol, Valium, Percoset, finally a Morphine drip through my IV. My breathing slowed and the pain, the arm, seemed like it belonged to someone standing on the other side of the room. I was trying to keep my eyes from permanently staring at the inside of my skull when they showed me the xray. \r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t remember most of what they said, just bits like \\\"multiple fracture, severe, we can\\\'t fix this here, surgery, admittance to hospital.\\\" I called my mom, middle of the night in Michigan, and with slow speech tried to explain what was happening.\r\n<p>\r\nThe next few days are a blur of awakenings in the hospital, friends faces and voices soothing, nurses and aides, lucid dreams of far off adventures, a morphine trigger happy finger, surgery, nauseau, pain, trying to speak but falling asleep in the middle of my words, feeling the fall over and over, waking and not knowing how long I had been there, time made no sense, then they took the morphine away and slowly the fuzz became thinner the pain became clearer and the reality of what had happened hit me. \r\n<p>\r\nI had broken my arm and my elbow into ten pieces. The surgeon had used two metal plates and 14 screws to put eight of the pieces back together again (there are always those extra bits you don\\\'t know what to do with, I just wished they had saved them for me). I felt like Humpty Dumpty only I had been put back together again. If I am lucky and everything heals right I should have <I>almost</I> normal use of my arm after months of painful physical therapy. \r\n<p>\r\nI am a one armed wonder (typing is a bitch) in a cyber black brace, numbed by a constant diet of painkillers. If I ever doubted that I had true friends I would no longer. I felt so much love flow my way, so many offers of help, so many people willing to sit by my side, to help me get back on my own. My mom flew out to clean my apartment. My boss paid me for the time I missed even though I was only a contract worker. My friends opened their home to me. I should feel wrapped in a security blanket of love, and most times I do. \r\n<p>\r\nBut now I know how easily one slip can happen.  I know how easily I can break. And it scares me.\r\n'),(2650,'I had been out of the Navy for less than a year. I lived in Myrtle Beach all throughout the winter and spring, beginning near Christmas. I was twenty. I got a job as a projectionist at the mall right away, to make a little pocket money. It didnâ€™t pay much but I couldnâ€™t think of anything else, it was the dead season at the beach. I worked a lot, because most of the other kids working there were in high school and couldnâ€™t work during the day. I spent many days and nights up there in the darkness, with a small table that had a desk lamp on it, and I would read for hours and hours. There was a catalog of film supplies that I read, and another large book with hundreds of movie description, little two- or three-sentence blurbs.\r\n<p>\r\n	One day at the theater, there was a knock at the back entrance door, the fire escape, and it was Randy. I had been reading the biography of Jim Morrison that day. Randy had somebody with him, I donâ€™t remember exactly who it was, and he had bought me a small, stone pipe, and some pot in a little bag. We were going to move to Florida together, Randy and I and his girlfriend, and maybe her sister and her little brother. We were going to wait tables, and I would go to college. That was the plan when I began my efforts to be released early from the service, and it was service all right. Serve all goddam day and every other weekend too. I was successful in my efforts and I got out two years early. Not a scratch on me or on my permanent record. I could probably join up again if I wanted. Every so often I have a dream that I have done just that, joined up again, and it is a horror dream, a my god what have I done madness dream. So I was out and now here\\\'s Randy on the fire escape, Randy says hey man, uh, you know that Florida thing, yeah, my dad wants me to come work at his car wash, what can I do right? Here hit this bowl, dude. \r\n<p>\r\nI was staying with Randy, and his girlfriend, and his girlfriendâ€™s mother, and her little brother in a two bedroom apartment right next to the mall. It was small but no one was ever there. I didnâ€™t have a key. I wonder what her mother must have thought about all that. Their family followed the religion, I donâ€™t know what itâ€™s called really, where they sort of worshipped this guy Meher Baba. That means precious something, I think. Anyway he was an Avatar, someone sent to bring peace to Earth, supposedly Jesus and Mohammed and whoever the Hindus have and so on all were different incarnations of this one guy, and most recently the guy was Meher Baba and he had a retreat in Myrtle Beach. \r\n<p>\r\nRandy moved back in with his dad and his dad\\\'s girlfriend. He broke up with Brandy, because his father thought she was strange. That was when, I believe, my best friend sold his soul to the devil. That, at least, was the end of any ambitions he ever had. It was also the end of us living in her mom\\\'s apartment.\r\n<p>\r\n	So lots of stuff happened there in Myrtle Beach, I was in love for a while and I smoked lots of pot, once I got sick eating mushrooms and freaked out a little, I got a job waiting tables in a seafood restaurant for about a hundred dollars a week, maybe. Maybe a hundred. When certain songs came on all the waiters would have to dance. I listened to the Grateful Dead, I listened to Phish, Bob Marley, Zeppelin, I did not learn to play the guitar. I learned a lot of things about people, and realized that they were exactly like I thought they were all along, only more tired and less ambitious. Anyway, this story is about the night when I ate the mushrooms and got sick. It\\\'s a good story. Stay tuned.\r\n'),(2651,'The night I ate mushrooms and got sick was a special night. I worked the lunch shift at the restaurant, ten till three, and went back to the apartment. Whoâ€™s apartment? Some guys. It was just the apartment, and it was free to me. I lived at Randyâ€™s fatherâ€™s house for mail purposes. So I entered the apartment and went into the guest bedroom you might call it, and there was Mayâ€™s pocketbook, and it had a clear driverâ€™s license window on it, and inside that window was a cigarette pack cellophane with about twenty hits of acid in it. â€œWhat the fuck,â€ I said to myself, and took two. This was at maybe 3:30. People began trickling in. At seven or so, May, Jamie, and Christine showed up, Christine was the girl I was in love with, and Jamie was the girl in love with me. They also had been tripping all day. I was lying on the bed, and Jamie threw herself on top of me, saying â€œThank God, Tylerâ€™s here! Weâ€™ve been tripping all day and we need you!â€ They were fucked up. They were all waitresses too. We played video games and watched TV and smoked pot all evening. We drank beer. Later, maybe ten , Mayâ€™s brother James showed up with a bag of the most enormous freakish dried mushrooms I had ever seen, and began chowing down. So I ate some. I guess I wanted to make sure that they would work post-acid, so I ate more than I should have. \r\n<p>	One hour later all hell broke loose. Inside my head that is. May and James and Jamie and I went outside. Christine went home. Normally that might have bothered me but not this night. I was way beyond all that. We walked across the parking lot and across the road, and there was an enormous oak tree, with limbs hanging low to the ground, lit from behind by an orange sodium-vapor street light. It was 11:30 pm. I walked in a circle around the tree. The limbs looked like blood vessels to an imaginary brain. They looked as if they were not just supporting the leaves, but thrusting them up into the sky, reaching up into the sky like giant fingers. Ever since then, when I see a tree, thatâ€™s what I see, fingers reaching up to the sun. James, poor James, heâ€™s had a hard life, began crying and ran back inside. May followed. Her real name is Meher Nas, which means â€œprecious dearâ€ or â€œprecious deer.â€ She also has had a hard life. Those two were not made for life here. She went inside to calm him down. That left me and Jamie, and we walked the half-mile down to the ocean. \r\n<p>\r\nWe walked between two high-rises, because to get to the ocean in North Myrtle that\\\'s what you have to do. There is a nearly unbroken line of them along the coast. It was low tide. It was very foggy too, I could see only about fifty feet in any direction. It was outrageously beutiful, and it was deserted, this being March. Jamie and I walked to the water, meaning to swim. It was as cold as ice, colder than ice. I was undaunted. I meant to swim in the ocean that night. Jamie had to wrestle me to the ground to get me to stay onshore. I was too out of it to resist, the trip just got stronger and stronger, pulling me into levels of abstraction that I had never reached before. I lay there in the sand with her, just lay there feeling her warmth, the wonderful girl-smell of her hair, the softness of her sweater. I thought to myself, \\\"Is this love?\\\" I thought to myself, \\\"What should I do?\\\" We got up, restless and needing to move, needing to smoke, needing something or anything. Everything. We walked like children, holding hands, both way too far gone for sexual things, sex seemed like such a small thing to me that night. We held hands and that was more than enough. From the edge of the ocean, all we could see was darkness to the east, and to the west enormous pillars of light, the high-rises, lighting the fog. \r\n<p>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s the edge of the whole world,\\\" I said.\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Yes it is, sweetie,\\\" she said, and kissed me on the cheek. I can still feel it.\r\n<p>\r\nI had wanted to become a hippie, or a hipster, or a beatnik, or what you may call it, I wanted to leave the military and live, and here I was. It was safe to assume that I was the first one from my family ever to experience life like this. Part of me will always be on that beach, with that beautiful girl, and her sweater and her cold thin fingers. \r\n<p>\r\nI became aware by degrees that it was cold. I felt something like a small baby kick inside my belly, a small baby of consequences and reality, and suddenly it was time to go back. Soon I would give birth to that baby. But this is not the end of the story, or even the beginning of the end, to quote Churchill, but simply the end of the beginning.\r\n \r\n'),(2652,'<P>Erich is from Graz, Austria, the same town where Arnold Swartzenegger was born.  He told me once that the Swartzenegger brothers had a reputation in his town for being bullies.  \r\n\r\n<P>When Erich was finished with high school, he wanted to study art.  But his father, a practical man, wanted Erich to learn a trade figuring that a trade would guarantee him a comfortably living.  Erich was packed off to hotel school where he learned the basics of the restaurant business. He was an able student and finished at the top of his class.   He got a job working as a sous-chef in a  fine restaurant, but didnâ€™t enjoy it.  Erich never wanted a practical life.  A practical life was boring.  In his off-time he was taking pictures incessantly, teaching himself new techniques and reading everything he could get his hands on, from photography texts to American novels.\r\n\r\n<P>Erich came to the United States to photograph the kind of lives he read about in Charles Bukowski novels.  His first day in NYC he was mugged and all his money was stolen.  Despite this he somehow found an apartment and got a job.  He stayed in the city for a few years working a variety of odd jobs.\r\n\r\n<P>After awhile New York became too much for him.  Erich decided that if he were truly to understand the American experience he couldn\\\'t stay in one place.  He moved up the east coast working here and there and finally came to Boston. By this time he had acquired a impressive amount of English, and though he sometimes he had to pause in the middle of a conversation to search for the word, it was pretty easy for him to communicate.  Erich and I worked together at a copy shop my first year in Boston.  It turned out that we had a lot in common; we liked a lot of the same books and music and had an common interest in the arts.  \r\n\r\n<P>He invited me over to his house and cooked a marvelous feast.  There was roast chicken and potatoes cooked with dill, brown bottles of German beer and ice-cold vodka in small glasses.  We ate and drank, laughed and talked.  After dinner he hand-rolled his own cigarettes.  We talked and drank until the small hours.  I soon found that Erich was egotistical, self-righteous.  He had his opinions and those opinions were 100% correct.  If I didn\\\'t agree with him, I was wrong.  But he was willing to argue.  For Erich, half the fun of having an opinion was the chance to talk about it.  By the end of the conversation, he\\\'d still be right of course, but we\\\'d both be that much drunker.  We became close friends.\r\n\r\n<P>Being at Erich\\\'s apartment was like stepping into a scene in a Dostoyevsky novel.  It was just so European.  He had renovated the place himself, torn up the linoleum floor and ratty carpet to reveal beautiful hardwood floors, stripped the old paint and refinished the 100 year-old moldings, polished the long-ignored brass fixtures. He had built his kitchen table from scrap wood, found antique chairs on the street and refinished them.  His apartment glowed with dark wood grain. Nothing in his house was made of plastic.  He even ground his coffee beans in a mill made of wood and iron.  \r\n\r\n<P>Sometimes when I went over to his place, Erich would tell me how he wanted to stop working stupid jobs and just open a cafe, but the American system seemed to work against him.  He just couldn\\\'t find a space and hang up a sign like he couldâ€™ve back home.  There were regulations and laws, licenses, insurance.  In Austria there were little cafes in farmhouses where you could drink cider and eat sausages that the lady of the farm made in her kitchen.  Why couldnâ€™t he do that here?  If he could open a cafÃ© he believed that he could make a comfortable living.\r\n\r\n<P>At some point Erich started dating a Japanese college student named Tomiko who was in the United States on a student visa.  One thing led to another and Tomiko became pregnant.  They were married in a brief but sweet civil ceremony at his apartment.  Tomiko dropped out of school.  \r\n\r\n<P>When the baby came, Tomikoâ€™s mother came from Japan to help out.  I visited once and met her mother; she cooked us a feast with supplies sheâ€™d brought with from Japan, afraid that she wouldnâ€™t be able to find proper Japanese food in the States.  \r\n\r\n<P>Not too long after Tomikoâ€™s mother returned to Japan, Tomiko lost her student visa because she hadnâ€™t attended college for over a year.  She would have to leave the country. \r\n\r\n<P>Tomikoâ€™s father owned a construction company, so Erich had a pretty well-paying job when he got to there.  But Erich was given the grunt work: heavy-lifting, bolt-turning, nail-hammering type of work.  The things that Erich considered interesting, plumbing or electrical work,  were relegated to Japanese employees.  In an e-mail Erich told me that his father-in-law treated him like a dumb mule,  and no matter how hard he tried, he couldnâ€™t get any respect from the other workers.   Part of the problem was, Erich didnâ€™t speak the language. That, and his forceful, indignant personality  clashed with Japanese sensibilities.  \r\n\r\n<P>Unsatisfied with his employment with his father-in-law, Erich searched for a place to open a cafÃ©.  He lived for free in a house owned by relatives and the money piled up.  In just six months, Erich told me, he made more money in Japan than he ever did in the States.  When he did find a space he would have the money to make his goals.\r\n\r\n<P>Erich found his cafÃ©.  It was a run-down building, but he set to work on it with the same intensity that he gave to his old apartment, changing what was essentially a Japanese place into his version of an Austrian cafÃ©.  It took a lot of sweat, but with the help of some of the people from the construction business, he completely renovated the place.\r\n\r\n<P>He opened the place one hot summer night.  People came in droves, desperately curious to see what he had accomplished.  The place filled up with so many bodies in such a small space, it became warm and stuffy.  Erich swung open the big windows in front of his restaurant to let in cooler night air.\r\n\r\n<P>At some point a three-inch stag beetle, the kind with big antler-like mandibles, buzzed in through the window and landed on the bar.  People fell silent.  This was very auspicious.  Very lucky.  Apparently stag beetles, once plentiful in Japan, are becoming increasingly more rare due to urbanization.   \r\n\r\n<P>Erich reached kept his hand flat on the bar and the beetle crawled onto it.  The room was alive with hushed voices.  Everybody wanted to touch the beetle.  Erich went around the cafÃ© and one-by-one his patrons touched the beetleâ€™s glossy black wings.  The men were bold.  The woman acted squeamish, making faces and pretending like they didnâ€™t want to have anything to do with the insect, but it took only a little encouragement for them to stroke the beetleâ€™s back.  \r\n\r\n<P>A Japanese businessman from the city was at the bar that night.  He offered Erich the equivalent of $500 dollars for the beetle.  Erich refused. The businessman went higher and higher, but Erich kept shaking him off.  â€œNo,â€ he said.  â€œThe beetle is NOT for sale.  Do you think Iâ€™m crazy?  Do you think I would SELL my good luck?â€   Erich walked outside, petting the beetle lightly with his free hand.  \r\n\r\n<P>When he thought he was far  enough away from the cafÃ©, he pursed his lips and blew a column of air under the beetles wings.  It flapped its wings  to regain its balance and Erich felt the beetleâ€™s prickly legs shift on his skin.  He blew harder, the beetleâ€™s wings spread and it lifted off,  wings buzzing.  It hung in the air for a moment and then flew off into the night.\r\n\r\n<P>That night Erichâ€™s reputation was set.  He became known as the man who would not sell his good luck.  His cafÃ© became successful, full almost every evening.  He still photographs, but he loves running his cafÃ©.  The fact that itâ€™s HIS place, that heâ€™s not working for someone else is what gives him satisfaction.\r\n\r\n<P>I saw him about a year ago when he came back to the states to finalize his citizenship papers (yes, Erich is now an Austrian citizen of the United States who lives in Japan).  He came to my house and I was proud to break out the vodka and the beer for him.  We listened to music and drank.  Thatâ€™s when he told me the story of the stag beetle, and his pleasure at his new found success.  \r\n\r\n<P>â€œIâ€™m big in my prefecture.â€  He said.  \r\n\r\n'),(2655,'I don\'t believe that everything happens for a reason, but I do believe that reasons happen from everything. I spend my time analyzing life as if it were a story, with symbols and themes and sometimes a moral. I live in the spaces between, where hidden meanings lie.\r\n<p>\r\nI have been thinking about my broken elbow almost exclusively for the three weeks since it happened. It dominates my thoughts and conversations, it has pushed all other concerns to the edges of my life. And besides boring myself and everyone else to death, I think I have finally figured out why this happened to me. \r\n<p>\r\nA teacher in high-school stated that all tragedy (of the Greco-Roman variety) occurred because of a fatal flaw in the hero, and that in most cases that flaw was hubris. The most obvious example, of course, is Icarus and the melting wings.\r\n<p>\r\nMy own hubris showed itself in the form of an excessive disdain for MUNI. I ride (or rode) a 1979 P200E Vespa. It is a bit beat up and could use a paintjob, but it can do circles around any bus. It?s the perfect mode of transportation in SF (except when it rains, so perfect is perhaps too strong a word)- you can bypass traffic jams and find parking anywhere. I would quiver at the thought of having to stand around waiting for a crowded smelly bus to take you not really where you were trying to go, when I could just pull on my helmet kick-start my scooter and get door to door motorized transportation. It was bliss. \r\n<p>\r\nI was cocky. I shook my leather-gloved fist at the gods of Public Transportation and mocked them.\r\nInstead of having a bus run me over on my scooter as one might expect from ancient gods, the modern variety were more subtle. They simply maimed me on my way to bed in such a manner as to prevent me from riding the scooter for months. Forcing me to face my demon, but not to blame it. To make me have to suck it up and figure out what bus goes where, and then stand out in the cold with my fellow transportationless souls.\r\n<p>\r\nMy only consolation is that the brace scores me a seat up front.\r\n'),(2656,'<p><b>meeting dad</b></p>\r\n<p><b>intro</b><br />\r\nwe left my father when i was very young.  one night he tried to go after my older sister, but my mother stopped him by putting herself in between the two of them.  for that he almost strangled her to death.  i think that was when my mom finally realized that if she didn\\\'t leave him, he\\\'d eventually kill her and then there\\\'d be no one left to protect my sister and i.  we left after that night and found a new place to live.  a few years later my mom found us a new dad as well.  life went on.  i grew up.</p>\r\n<p>---</p>\r\n<p>it was easter sunday when the phone rang.  my sister answered it, and when she left the room to carry out her conversation i figured it was a call about a job interview.  \\\"good for her,\\\" i said (all the while wondering why she couldn\\\'t just get these assholes to call her at her place instead).  to avoid the inevitable flurry of chattering between my mother and sister i went downstairs, intent on wasting a few brain cells in front of the television.</p>\r\n<p>my mother\\\'s appearance in the basement is always a sign of less than wonderful circumstances.  my mother hates the basement.  it\\\'s cold, it\\\'s where all the b-list furniture is kept, and she\\\'s convinced there\\\'s a colony of mice living in the walls.  my mother only enters the basement to clean or to corner me for a \\\"talk.\\\"  this time she came in with what i initially diagnosed as a \\\"the dog\\\'s just died, and i\\\'m trying to think up a story to make you believe we\\\'ve sent it off to live in a big green field\\\" look on her face.  she started off with a reaffirmation that she\\\'d never lie to me, and that she\\\'d feel awful if she didn\\\'t tell me this (<i>okay, where is the dog ...</i>).  \\\"your father\\\'s in the intensive care unit.\\\"  queue me with an annoyed look and thoughts of, \\\"no he\\\'s not, he\\\'s sitting in the kitchen upstair- ... oh.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>maybe in the back of my mind i\\\'d been expecting this all along.  maybe i hadn\\\'t.  i do know that i\\\'d been debating meeting him and putting it off for years.  do you know what your father looks like?  would you be able to pick him out of a crowd if you saw him on the street?  for over 20 years i had no idea who this man was.  i had no idea what his face even looked like.  i knew nothing about him, but i knew i hated him.  i didn\\\'t know what he looked like, but i knew i must resemble him.  i didn\\\'t even know why my mother had decided to marry him all those years ago, but i\\\'d seen what that marriage had done to her.  he was my father and i\\\'d spent most of my life wondering if i should meet him or not.  and now he was dying.</p>\r\n<p>i thought about it for awhile and finally decided to go see him.  my rationale was that the few faint memories i had of him wouldn\\\'t satisfy me for the rest of my life.  i had a lot of questions that needed answers, and i wasn\\\'t willing to let my own fears and insecurities hinder getting them.  i also wanted to be there to watch the fucker die.</p>\r\n<p>the next day as i was sitting in the car i found myself faced with a unique dilemma.  what exactly is the appropriate mood music for this sort of situation?  i had the full spectrum with me, everything from gabber to trip hop, with jungle, punk and rap in between.  i decided against gabber, as that would likely have sent me into a violent frenzy (and it\\\'s just tacky to kill someone on their deathbed, not to mention redundant).  trip hop also got nixed.  i was already miserable enough without beth gibbons wailing away and making matters worse.  i ended up opting for some mellow drum and bass.  my mother must have suffered from heart palpitations when she was pregnant with me, because breakbeats give me that \\\"back in the womb\\\" connection that a 4x4 kick does for others.  with the music issue in check, i headed off for sarnia general hospital.</p>\r\n<p>i was halfway to the i.c.u. before i turned around and walked out of the hospital.  i might try to be a strong person, but at that moment i turned into an absolute coward.  i went outside, sat down, and started to chain smoke with the tulips.  what do you say to the man that tried to kill your mother?  what do you say to him after all this time?  do you try to vent all the anger toward him you\\\'ve been harbouring for 22 years?  that\\\'s what i was trying to figure out.  in the end, all i accomplished out there with the tulips was a sore throat.</p>\r\n<p>my second attempt was marginally more succesful than the first.  this time i made it all the way to the i.c.u. doors before i faltered and turned around.  i walked into the visitor\\\'s lounge and bolted for the washroom.  after a few minutes of discussing things with the reflection in the mirror and dousing my face with cold water, i decided the only outcome of the situation that i was willing to accept involved me walking into the ward across the hall.  it was one of those moments when you knew your life would be changed forever in 30 minutes.</p>\r\n<p>i walked up to the doors of the intensive care unit.<br />\r\n<i>alright, find the intercom.</i><br />\r\n\\\"can i help you?\\\"<br />\r\n\\\"i\\\'m here to see john leblanc.\\\"<br />\r\n\\\"i\\\'m sorry, who?\\\"<br />\r\n<i>oh fuck.  that is his name, isn\\\'t it?</i><br />\r\n\\\"john leblanc,\\\" (with a voice of feigned certainty).<br />\r\n\\\"alright, come in.\\\"<br />\r\nladies and gentlemen, let the games begin.</p>\r\n<p>the first thing i did when i walked into the intensive care ward was lock my gaze on the nurse\\\'s station.  i was determined not to make eye contact with anyone in the beds around me.  i wouldn\\\'t lower myself to searching through the faces, trying to figure out which one i was there for.  the head nurse and i went into the consultation room and i explained who i was.  i asked what he had and she listed it off: sclerosis of the liver, pancreatis, stomach cancer, as well as a healthy dose of internal hemorrhaging.  she told me he had a tube in his abdomen to drain the blood, one down his nose to drain stomach fluids, and of course the standard issue iv.  i smiled and thanked her, and was turning toward the row of beds when i remembered something.  \\\"which one is he?\\\"  \\\"you don\\\'t know?\\\"  no, i don\\\'t.  welcome to a day in my life.</p>\r\n<p>when i walked over to his bed he was unconscious.  \\\"asleep\\\" isn\\\'t the right word to describe him.  wherever he was, there were no dreams there.  the nurse asked if i wanted her to wake him up, but i said i\\\'d rather sit and wait until he woke up on his own.  to be honest, i was hoping to be able to watch him die in his sleep right there in front of me.  i sat down in a chair and studied him for what seemed to be an eternity.  i had to take in every detail.  \\\"this is your father.  this is what he\\\'s become,\\\" i thought.  here\\\'s the portrait in my mind of my father on that day.  he looked pathetic.  his entire body was twitching and shaking.  he was so frail.  there were little flecks of blood caked on his mustache and around his nose where the tube went in.  his hair was matted together with sweat.  saliva had collected in the corner of his mouth and was beginning to congeal.  there was a surgical scar on his shoulder and it occured to me that i\\\'d never know what he\\\'d done to injure it.  there were cotton restraints tied around his wrists.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'d been sitting there since time began.  i had spent every second of my existence sitting in that chair, staring at that face.  my entire world revolved around that moment.  then his eyes opened and he looked at me.  suddenly my world changed.</p>\r\n<p>when he first opened his eyes and saw me, nothing immediately registered.  then they widened, and i knew he\\\'d put two and two together.  his head lifted off the pillow a bit and i nodded slightly, staring back at him.  father, meet son.  son, meet father.  the blank expression on my face felt so cold it actually scared me.  i\\\'d never looked into someone\\\'s eyes and felt that way before.  i walked to the edge of his bed and looked down at him.  i asked him if he knew who i was and he said yes.  he said he was glad to see me.  then he reached out and took my hand.  this brought to my mind another revelation: this hand was completely foreign to me.  i\\\'d never realized it before, but i\\\'ve got a mental contruct of all the different hands i\\\'ve held.  having this new hand in mine made me suddenly start recalling all the details of various hands i\\\'ve held over the course of my life: their size, their texture, the strength of their grip.  in retrospect i think it was just an escapist reaction, but it did manage to keep me from jerking my hand away at the time.  when he did let go i brought a chair to his bedside and we began a broken conversation.  whenever he\\\'d pass out i\\\'d watch the canister at the side of his bed slowly fill up with his blood and wait for him to wake up again.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m not going to recount what was said in the conversation i had with my father.  some things are meant to be shared with others and some things aren\\\'t.  that conversation i\\\'m going to keep just with me.  i will say though that he never apologized for his actions and i never forgave him for them.  in that respect i suppose we are both similar to each other.  such is life.  while i thought he looked frail and pathetic, i never felt any sympathy for him.  my sympathy lies with the woman his alcoholism and abuse turned my mother into.  the one thing i took from the time i spent with my father is that i must never become him.  i made a promise to myself and the family i will have some day to never be like that man.</p>\r\n<p>the last thing my father said to me was, \\\"why don\\\'t you go now?  it\\\'s no fun watching an old bastard die.\\\"  i couldn\\\'t agree more.  as i walked out of the intensive care unit i heard the nurse calling to me that she hoped i would stop in for another visit.  i never turned to tell her that i had no intention of ever returning to that ward again, and certainly not to see my father.  his brief appearance in my life was over, and i had taken all i\\\'d ever need from him.</p>\r\n<p>---</p>\r\n<p><b>outro</b><br />\r\nwhen i woke up on april 7th there was a message waiting for me on the answering machine.  my father had died at 9:45 that morning.  good riddance.</p>'),(2657,'<p>\r\nI spent some time watching by the window. The trees were eating outside. The sun was bright. I could see the dark side of their leaves, and sometimes the clear one, as the wind was making them shiver.\r\n</p><p>\r\nLater on, walking in the street, I passed by the old people residence. It was night then, I watched the lights through the windows, and the inside of some of the apartments. I thought about myself leaving inside one of these, being old. I guess people in there often see ambulances coming, taking away one of them to the next step, the hospital. Maybe they try to avoid noticing at all the ambulances.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMy stomach was giving me some pain, just like it had been doing during the whole week. I suddenly got overwhelmed by the over-complexity of the system my body used to make me aware of this stupid stomachache. So many nerve sensors, millions of neurons, and thousands of different kinds of chemical reactions, just to make sure I cannot ignore a fucking useless mild stomach pain. Thanks, God, thanks Nature!\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2658,'It has long been known that canned tuna is full of protein and omega-3 fatty acids that many scientists report may help in the fight against heart disease, play a beneficial role in lowering blood pressure and cholesterol, and can ease the pain of arthritis. But many researchers now believe that increased intake of omega-3 fats may also improve your sex life!<br><br>\r\nAccording to dietician Ellen Albertson, \\\"You can awaken a sleepy libido with easy diet changes.\\\" Ms. Albertson is the co-author of a new book, Temptations: Igniting the Pleasure and Power of Aphrodisiacs (Simon & Schuster, 2002), which provides numerous practical dietary tips for a better love life, including eating more tuna fish sandwiches.<br><br>\r\nPrevention Magazine summarized how tuna can improve a person\\\'s love life in its February 2002 review of the book. Prevention explains, \\\"If you use white albacore tuna [in your meals], you up your intake of omega-3 fats. More and more researchers now believe that omega-3s help ward off depression, surely one of the worst enemies of feeling sexy.\\\" The conclusion is that by eating more canned tuna you can uplift your mood, leading to higher sprits and in turn a better love life.<br><br>\r\nU.S. Tuna Foundation National Director Randi Thomas says, \\\"We usually tout the long list of health benefits scientists believe are provided by omega-3s fatty acids. But canned tuna improving consumers\\\' love lives? Now that\\\'s what we call a sexy idea!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2659,'<p>what you don\\\'t know is that i watch you leave.</p>\r\n<p>you kiss me and say goodbye and i mumble back to you. i watch the apartment get darker as you make your way out, flipping switches. finally the door closes behind you and i roll over to look at the glow in the dark stars on my ceiling, bright again because you turned on the lights to find your shirt.</p>\r\n<p>i lie there motionless for a few moments and then lean forward to nudge the blinds open with a finger.<BR>\r\nyou are getting into your truck, checking your pockets, pulling out a cigarette.<BR>\r\nyou cast a glance back up to my window.<BR>\r\ni drop my finger and fall back into the bed and listen to you start your engine, listen to you drive away.</p>\r\n<p>i get out of bed, turn on the lights, throw on a robe, scoop up the kitten in my arms, and pace around the apartment before beginning my nightly routine: wash face, brush teeth, check e-mail.</p>\r\n<p>what you don\\\'t know is that <b>this</b> has become my nightly routine, too.</p>\r\n<p>but i want you to stay.<BR>\r\ni always just want you to stay.</p>'),(2660,'Jamie and I walked back up the road towards the apartment. There were no cars on the road. Back then there was a dead season at the beach, and it became sort of a ghost town. Today, it is full of people year-round, minus me. We held hands and spun around in the middle of the road. Soon we came to the entrance, and in we went.\r\n<p>\r\nJames had calmed down. Everyone was watching him play the snowboarding game on the TV, and smoking pot. We smoked a lot of really bad pot then. Jamie and May and me sat on the couch. i remember seeing everyone\\\'s head floating a foot above their body, like a balloon. I remember looking at the TV and not being sure which was reality, what was on TV or everything else. Seriously. Jamie started to get a little personal, rubbing my chest and head, and I started to feel a little uncomfortable--remember, I was in love with the other girl. Silly me. I should have...well, if I started in on the \\\"should haves,\\\" this would end up being a novel.\r\n<p>\r\nI began to feel funny, in my stomach. It wasn\\\'t a big deal at first, just a little tightness around my waist. I took off my belt, and that helped for a little while. Soon, though, I became aware of a vague discomfort in my abdomen, not pain exactly, just a feeling like I was a bug molting and any second now I was going to break out of an old skin. This is not a normal situation for a mammal. I went into the bathroom, and tried to throw up. Nothing. I lay down in someone\\\'s room and tried to sleep. Well, sleep was definitely out of the question. Nothing helped, and I began to get scared.\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I was a kid, everytime I went to an amusement park and rode a ride, \r\nI would feel sick. not so much because the ride was exceptionally violent, but because I didn\\\'t have the option to stop. That\\\'s the kind of person I am, I guess. That feeling returned to me for the first time since I was 11 or so, except now I was fully aware that this ride wasn\\\'t going to stop for at least eight more hours. I went into the guest bedroom, and lay in bed, trying to relax and enjoy my trip, which was quickly becoming a nightmare. The pain grew worse, and I had a vision of my own death, there in a dirty bedroom among people I barely knew. I opened my eyes after a while, and saw an alien landscape, with four enormous stone towers rising into the night sky, lit by a green sun. Then I realized that I was looking at my hand, lit by the alarm clock on the nightstand. I prayed for God\\\'s help, \\\"please get me through this one night, God, and I\\\'ll never ask for anything again ever ever ever.\\\" It\\\'s funny how when something horrible happens, there is never any question about my religious views. \r\n<p>\r\nTo make a long story short, I survived. It wasn\\\'t easy. At several points, I considered going to the hospital, but I didn\\\'t want my family to find out what had happened, which they surely would have once they recieved my health insurance statement. I actually fell asleep for a little while, and when I woke up, I felt much better, or at least like I wasn\\\'t going to die soon. In the morning, I got up, with everyone else still asleep, sat in the old ragged cloth recliner i the den, and watched the sun rise, and thanked the lord for getting me through the horrors of the night before, and then I had a defining moment, right there among the beer cans and the pot seeds and the video games and the pizza boxes. Right there, right then, I decided that I had had enough. Enough worrying over everyone, their misdirected lives, their messed-up friends, their bosses, their jobs, their misery. I knew that I had done all I could to help them, consoled them over a thousand slights, given a thousand words of kindness and inspiration regarding their life choices, given fully of myself, lived to the extreme, loved, been loved, fucked it all up, fixed it all again, gone to the limit. I saw that there was nothing more to accomplish in this particular life, and decided then to move on. I was not one of them, as hard as I had tried to be. They knew it and I knew it, though I never had understood why and still do not. And here I am. And that\\\'s that.'),(2661,'In this last week there have been more events, then in the last six months or so. As my life accelerated this week I\\\'ve been able to start feeling more positive in my feelings towards the people and things around me. I suppose I am one of those people whom have a hard time divorcing selfworth from work. Selfworth being intimately tied to my perspective on the world at large. It could be any kind of work fast food, retail, roofing, policy analysis, or as it is this time documentary film marketing. Highlights from the first week in my now employed rejuvenated life included -<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>A member of my extended social circle giving birth to a healthy baby boy with the help of two midwives and a birthing pool. Names that have been suggested so far include Neptune, Zeus, and Malkamus<br><br>\r\n<li>Frantic media consumption and phone calls home to make sure that the 4 Canadian soldiers taken by allied friendly fire weren\\\'t old friends, ends up my Father is friends with one of the injured guys that stayed in hospital, in Germany but none of my old high school friends have been hurt<br><br>\r\n<li>My first official screening of a documentary (center left take on the FTAA summit in Quebec) at the new job, after which a panel discussion    it was torn apart by a variety of groups much farther left then the film<br><br>\r\n<li>Ex Fiance calls saying that her lay over on the flight to Paris has been extended to 5 days due to some mess up, seems a little shady to me and the rest of the week is spent trying to avoid contact (even went and filed a security report in my building as my roommate made it clear that she was not to step foot in the building)<br><br>\r\n<li>Friday afterwork beers with good company kickin off the first weekend of patio season<br><br>\r\n\r\nGood, bad, boring, exciting, or otherwise I\\\'m just glad to no longer feel like I am sittin stewin in limbo. '),(2662,'thursday night i was feeling like absolute crap. i had a fever and after trying to get to sleep right after work, i didn\\\'t fall asleep until midnight. and i would wake up every couple of minutes after falling asleep into delirium dreams. i was having dreams about bottle caps. twist-offs, pop-offs, you name it. weird. and i would wake up trying to catch my breath. it was frightening at the time. sort of funny in retrospect but at the time very frightening.<br><br> \r\n\r\nfriday morning i woke up and felt terrible but my string of fevers finally broke around 10am. i took little naps here and there but usually felt worse would i would wake up. i didn\\\'t mention that through all of this i was drinking fluids like crazy. so my friday was a little crazy - i fell asleep in the afternoon/evening then i would wake up and watch tv and drink water and juice. then i fell asleep at one point and woke up freaking out because the fever was back. i have been taking all of the medication the doctor prescribed (augmentin [antibiotic] and claritin) plus tylenol and dayquil. so i was just getting annoyed with having this fever and different symptoms coming into the foreground over and over. the fever finally broke again and i fell asleep. i woke up catching my breath again and had had another fever-dream. this time it was about a girl who looked like she should have been on little house on the prairie. and she was wearing a pioneer girl sort of outfit and mary ingalls glasses and next to her was this chart with two bars, one bigger than the other and this little girl was trying to explain something to me about a certain demographic in these charts. but i couldn\\\'t understand her or what the charts meant. i just knew the bar on the right was higher than the bar on the left. and the percentages were obscured.  <br><br>\r\n\r\ni didn\\\'t get sick often as a child, but when i <i>was</i> sick i would be sick for 7 or 8 days at a time and very much bed-ridden. i remember this one time when i was in elementary school... i was sick and my mom was home with me and i woke up and she was beside me and i had had a dream about big, bigger and biggest. how abstract. it was beyond comprehension. i remember what i saw in the dream but it goes beyond description. there weren\\\'t really any comparisons, the dream was just about big, bigger and biggest. i can still picture and feel what i felt when i had this dream years and years ago but i can barely explain it.  surprisingly this did not happen during the same week during which i managed to puke all over my white bedspread, throw up every time the phone rang to the point where my mother had to rip up the bathroom carpet, saw the sky turn orange for the first time and got sick when cheers came on.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni was sick at my grandparents house once when i was pretty young/still in the phase where i was not able or willing to make it to a toilet or othersuch receptacle to throw up (probably in first grade or so?) and i had this fever and i had this dream that ronald reagan was trying to hit me with a croquet mallet on their gravel driveway. all in the name of fever and delirium. my grandparents did have a gravel driveway and a lone croquet mallet which i used to (and this is not a dream) smash gravel with on the carport. i am not sure what freud would say about the ronald reagan part. <br><br>\r\n\r\nthere are many obvious reasons why i don\\\'t enjoy being sick, but i think the number one reason i don\\\'t like it is because of the things fever does to people. i have managed to be sick a lot lately due to weather changing drastically but all without being feverish. so usually sleep comes and i feel better when i wake up. but not when i have a fever. i know what falling asleep with a fever entails and it\\\'s a little scary sometimes. and hard to explain. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni tried to write a poem about fever dreams once and it could be read in different directions and it was called \\\"fever-dream\\\" but my professor told me that the title was so obvious. so i changed it to something else when i revised it for his class but i got it published by the college literary magazine with the original title and form.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2665,'<b><i><P>explain this to me</P></i></b> \r\n\r\n<P>my husband-to-be made a private declaration recently that still makes my head hurt. apparently, he has \\\'fallen\\\' for me. what does that mean? how can that possibly be? of course i\\\'m pleased, in some small shady part within. it is flattering. and it is infinitely more preferable that he is attracted to me, than the opposite. but?</P><P></P><P>look, i don\\\'t believe in love at first sight. appearances are practically meaningless, if not worthless. can you tell anything important about a person just by looking at them? can you tell if they are kind from the shape of their mouth? does the colour of their eyes tell you anything about their sense of humour? or how they react in a crisis? or what their attitude to money -- earning, spending, giving -- is? if someone has a sexy body, does this provide any information about their social values? about how they treat people? about whether they stick by their friends? </P><P></P><P>i\\\'m not saying i don\\\'t get crushes on gorgeous people. i think this is inherent, it makes sense biologically. i mean, for the future of the human race, we need to procreate. and thus, we have to find each other attractive. so there is nothing wrong in _lust_ at first sight. infact, it is right. natural. good. besides which, beautiful people are just aesthetically pleasing. but i think it is dangerous to mistake this attraction for anything more than it is. to believe an infatuation is love.</P><P></P><P>i mean, he\\\'s talked to me the grand total of what? four or five times? he doesn\\\'t know anything about me! he doesn\\\'t know who i am, what i am like, what my fears and passions are, what kind of a friend i am, what it is like to live with me, to spend time with me, on a day-to-day basis.</P><P></P><P>i have to admit that his voice is tender, that he is very lovey-dovey. and this is nice. it makes this whole process much easier, more pleasant. i\\\'m glad he\\\'s happy. i\\\'m glad, that for him, this is turning out to be something different than an arranged marriage. but me? i can\\\'t fall for someone after meeting them in person just once.</P><P></P><P>and now i\\\'m scared he is building me up in his mind, imagining me into someone i am not. that i will disappoint him once he does get to know me. </P><P></P><P>and God, i am slightly...unnerved that he knew what i looked like long before i knew he existed. still, i suppose that at least now i know he likes how i really look, random temporary pimples, glasses, ordinary clothes and everything, and not just how i look when i have to dress the part of a traditonal bride-to-be. i mean, i met him at the end of january. and since then, every time my family\\\'s gone to a party where he will be present, i\\\'ve been told to take care of how i dress. i still didn\\\'t dress up, because i\\\'m not a dressy kind of girl. but i didn\\\'t just throw on the first thing that came to my hand from my closet, like i used to before we were introduced.</P><P></P><P>so back when i hadn\\\'t known, i was just being the person i was, completely unaware that anyone was noticing. but he had been, apparently. for months. and he told me he fell for me the first time he saw me, in november. and this is reassuring. it means i don\\\'t have to look like someone i\\\'m not (the someone my mother thinks i should be/look like) for him to like me. he likes me, at least appearance-wise, just the way i am.</P><P></P><P>he said other things too, things that made me squirm in embarrassed surprise, compliments that made me blush. i can\\\'t even remember what i was wearing, what i was doing, during that halaqa last aumtumn. </P><P></P><P>weird.</P><P></P><P></P> \r\n\r\n<i>-lotus</i>'),(2663,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2663_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">'),(2664,'<p>\r\n<b>Exhibit A</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>All that piece, parcel or lot of land with the improvements thereon, situate, lying and being approximately two (2) miles Northeast of the City of Camden, in the County of Kershaw, State of South Carolina, fronting East on Hilton Street for a distance of One Hundred Seventy-Five and 6/10 (175.6) Feet, and extending back on its Northern boundary for a distance of One Hundred (100) Feet, and on its Southern boundary for a distance of Ninety (90) Feet; the with at the rearmost part measuring One Hundred Forty-Five and 5/10 (145.5) Feet, and being shown and designated as Lot No. 3 on plat of the W.T. Roberts, subdivision, on plat made by A.B. Boykin, Surveyor, of date March 8th 1950 and recorded in the Office of the Clerk of Court for Kershaw County in Plat Book 14, at page 116, and on plat of S. Tatterton, Surveryor, of date August 22nd , 1953 and recorded int he office of the Clerk of Court for Kershaw County in Plat Book 21, at page 101, and being bounded as follows: NORTH by Thirty Foot un-named Street; EAST by Hilton Street; SOUTH by Lot No. 2 and WEST by Lot No. 4.</i>\r\n</p>'),(2666,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2666_2.gif\\\">\r\n\r\n<P><i>p.s. i love egg.</i></P>\r\n'),(2667,'<p>a kid.  *almost* a year older; wait to grow up and do great things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in college.  study/drink/fuck; wait to join the real world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on your own.  solo gig in a cheap apartment - wait for ms/mr *right*.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>married with kids.  wait for them to grow up; hope they turn out okay.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>past middle age. training the kid that replaces you; wait for the pink slip.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>retired.  too much TV; wait for health to fail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>dying.  short breaths; cold skin.  Wait for the quiet comfort to come.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>so much waiting. when do we live?</P>'),(2668,'<P>\r\njust about two weeks after the attacks on the new york world trade centre, the girl leaves her monday night class, and walks to her car, and boom, the five of them are on her. \r\n\r\n\r\none is fat, and smells of sweat and doritos. he grabs and pulls her hair until the back of her head smashes against the concrete. the others don\\\'t let him touch any part of her body below the fine gold chain around her throat. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe skinny guy with his baseball cap on backwards unzips his shorts first. he doesn\\\'t take them off. the girl\\\'s screaming so boy number three, who is annoyed at his tan, at how his skin looks next to the girl\\\'s half naked body, punches her in the mouth, and then kisses her through the blood. he bites her lips, and grips her upper arms when she whimpers. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe fourth boy swallows back bile, and tries not to cry. he\\\'s smiling, and stroking what part of her thighs and stomach he can reach. the skin on her belly is unbelievably smooth. he never expected that, and his eyes start to hurt, to burn. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe boy with brown hair and brown eyes and a toothpaste ad smile, except one of his two front teeth is chipped, crouches down close to her feet, rips off her sandals, and licks her toes. she tries to kick him, rolls her head back and chokes on her tongue. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthe fat boy screams whoo, he\\\'s okay, he doesn\\\'t care, doesn\\\'t mind, knots his fingers into her hair, it looks like bananas in a net. she\\\'s stopped struggling. its easy for the next guy to get on top. he\\\'s surprised at how good it feels. all he wants is to damn this paki bitch to hell.\r\n\r\n</P>'),(2669,'<p>\r\nhere i am in the lone star state, visiting my mom and dad in small town texas. since i\\\'ve been here, where the trees are short and the wal marts are massive, i\\\'ve been preoccupied with how fat we are.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbasically, i\\\'m disgusted.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnot by the fat itself. bodies don\\\'t disgust me. <b>FAT</b> is a larger word here, it\\\'s a mnemonic, a bookmark in a vast volume of western culture. i\\\'m not concerned with the aesthetic of obesity. it\\\'s ok, be fat, but for godsakes, if you\\\'re gonna do something, know what you are doing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere\\\'s a mindlessness with which we consume. shoving what we can into our homes, our mouths, our children\\\'s mouths. we are blank receptacles, formless and yawning, <i>oh give, give to me, i want</i>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere\\\'s so much thoughtlessness i can\\\'t bear to breathe. all things are food, passing through us only  to be shat out later without thanks. our eyes and ears take it in complacently, process little, and move on.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday, i went  to some caves near my parents house. a nature-oriented tourist attraction. dynamite out panels of the earth and build a path through it. pad along the path, your big butt craving taco bell later, taking a picture of a rock, using your disposable camera. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy sister calls me while i\\\'m doing yoga, one half of my body stretched, the other one compacted. i\\\'m forcing myself to take time from staring numbly into my parents television, to ignore the fact that my mother is currently focused on what she cannot have, to ignore the fact that the salves for the human condition currently consist of refined sugar, fats, and plastics. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy sister, hurling her voice across fiber optics and other synthetic materials, tells me her disgust with the public school system. the kids who don\\\'t want to learn, how all they do is fuck each other and do drugs and mouth off to her. she is so angry. i say if something makes you angry, come out publically against it. i am saying, don\\\'t fucking involve yourself in something that disgusts you. i say, i don\\\'t know what to tell you, i just need to be doing yoga now. she hung up on me.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni walked around the house like that for a while, half stretched, angry.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere\\\'s a list of things my mom can\\\'t eat. her health is under moderate attack. instead of making a serious change to her life or examining the things that have gotten her to this point in her health, she is: smoking, consuming vast amounts of aspartame in the form of diet soda, eating sugar free pseudo junk food. the doctor tells her she needs to sweat. granted, i\\\'m not here all the time and she\\\'s going to be pissed at me for making generalizations about her lifestyle in a public forum, but she does nothing to break a sweat. i am worried about her but feel as though it\\\'s fruitless to nag at her.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the \\\"cave\\\" today, the tour guide compared the rock formations to food. \\\"the formations have carbonate in them, just like the soda you drink every day,\\\" and \\\"the formations are hollow, as if you had stacked cheerios on top of each other\\\" and more, ice cream cones, ice cream sandwiches, lemon merengue pie, food food food is the way to make looking at underground rocks exciting for our tired-of-walking population.\r\n</p><p>\r\nvisiting home means my father gets me various junk foods as forms of gifts, and  it just makes me want to cry, because i love him more than anything and there\\\'s no way to make him understand that providing me with cool ranch doritos is not loving. it\\\'s not loving because i\\\'ll fucking eat the whole fucking bag of them. i\\\'m in small town america and there ain\\\'t shit to do but eat, my friend. and watch tv.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<b>also, wal mart</b>:<br>\r\non the way home from visiting the georgetown downtown area, a quaint square packed with local business, my mom said: <i>i think all towns should have a downtown like that</i>. i said:<i>that will never happen as long as there is wal mart.</i> my father shook his head, disagreeing but not saying much. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwal mart is public enemy number one. everything in wal mart is trash. it makes trash, is trash, will become trash, or trash was a byproduct of its existence. walking into a wal mart is like descending into the vortex of pollution, entering into a moment in time where you can see pollution crystallize and glimmer briefly. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni have begun to entertain fantasies of what life would be like for americans if there were no more \\\"superstores.\\\" what if they just didn\\\'t exist? how would people adapt? what horror would replace these stores? \r\n</p><p>\r\nin the wake of these visits home i usually feel thankfulness and renewed joy about the ways i\\\'ve chosen to simplify and live mindfully, however small those ways are. even though my efforts to be a more deliberate and thoughtful person are surely lost as soon as i leave a room, i think it\\\'s important.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m not sure what i want out of society when i feel like this. i\\\'ve no prescriptions or cures, i think i just really want people to be thinking harder about what they do and why. about how they nourish themselves and each other. yes,  <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=978\\\">i just want you to have thought about it</a>.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<img src=\\\"/journals/images/2669_1.jpeg\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" height=\\\"375\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"><br>\r\n<font size=\\\"1\\\">(this photo was taken by <a href=\\\"http://perceptions.diaryland.com\\\">bobby</a>, who i\\\'m pretty sure has thought about what he does and why.)<font>\r\n\r\n'),(2670,'<pre>\r\ntoday the sun was warm and the air was cool\r\nthe leaves and the grass were extra green\r\ni had saltines and milk\r\ni read outside \r\nthe sun on the paper hurt my eyes\r\nthere were more bugs flying around than usual\r\n\r\ntoday class was five minutes longer than usual\r\nworkers were outside working\r\ni was inside schooling\r\nthere were no problems\r\nwe talked about god\r\ni wore a hawaiian shirt\r\n</pre>'),(2671,'<pre>Summer Palace\r\nKludholm, Kingdom of Dodheimsgard\r\n\r\nThe Twenty-Third of April,\r\nin the Year of Our Lord Two Thousand and Two\r\n\r\nMr Steven Pekoi\r\nDirector, Human Resources\r\nUSA Detergents\r\n471 Jersey Avenue\r\nNorth Brunswick, NJ 08903\r\n\r\n\r\n     Dear Mr. Pekoi,\r\n     \r\n     I am very excited about the possibility of leading your people;\r\nenclosed is my resume. As you can see, I am qualified as a professional\r\nlord and I have worked in several royal appointments since 1994. I am \r\nskilled in rhetoric, philosophy, swordplay, ettiquette and courtship. \r\nI hold esteemed ranks. In the practicable arts I am fully competent \r\nin strategy, deployment, field logistics, maneuvers &amp; formation and \r\ntactical diplomacy. Also, I have a regal countenance.\r\n     I hope to discuss working with your company in the future. I will \r\nrequire much wine, sweetmeats, servants, courtesans, hounds, hunting, \r\nan armory, an honor guard, fine chambers, et cetera, but note that I \r\nhave a great deal of experience with motivating others, and I would like \r\nto have the opportunity to command you in the future. I am certain that, \r\nwith respect to my experience and character as a gentleman of high birth, \r\nyour competitors will have little choice but to capitulate to the \r\nsovereign throne of your company, upon whose doorI will heap honor and \r\nprestige. Please review the enclosed documents; I hope to hear from you\r\nin the near future.\r\n\r\n                                           cordially,\r\n                                                              \r\n                                    His Majesty Erich Aldergraf von Bittensfeld\r\n                                    Grand Duke of Aldershaut\r\n                                    The Kingdom of Dodheimsgard</pre>     \r\n                                                \r\n                                                \r\n<img src=\\\"/journals/images/2671_1.jpg\\\" width=\\\"240\\\" height=\\\"343\\\">'),(2672,'<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\ngoat. it\\\'s austin. <br><BR>\r\naahhm... why- why ain\\\'t you picking up your phone? um, uh. come go see the movie Scorpian King with me cause you\\\'re egyptian, and it\\\'s about egypt... it\\\'s, ah you know, a historical narrative. you- you should want to see it cause it\\\'s talking- it\\\'s about your people.. Scorpian King.<br><BR>\r\nCall Me Back Immediately. PRONTO. <br><BR>\r\nok. bye...<BR>\r\n<br>and it\\\'s about my people too, cause i\\\'m bedouin. ok. lets go see it.</p>\r\n</BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<p> i love phone messages like this. for some reason this made me laugh and giggle for a while. even while stuck in 8mph traffic north of vallejo.\r\n\r\n'),(2673,'<p>hey, i\\\'m new in this part of the web. just thought i\\\'d say hi.</p>'),(2674,'<p>\r\ni made it out of my place quite late. i coundn\\\'t wake up. at one moment i thought i was awake, but i closed my eyes, just a second, and an hour passed. i was still sleepy while riding. dreaming about people\\\'s behaviors and their thoughts.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>i woke up with the sun. but i stood there without moving for a few hours. then it became warmer. warm enough to feel like wandering around. there\\\'s always some nice food to grab around here. you just have to go for it.\r\ni smelled something nice. over there maybe. as i was crossing the freeway to check it out, i had a glimpse of something on my left. my body was crushed to a flat black and red stain, except for a few parts of it that were blown away.</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\nwork was slight boring to medium boring, nothing special. remember to clean my helmet\\\'s screen.\r\n</p>'),(2675,'<p>I call it therapy. After the daily grind has left my nerves raw, or the neverending drama of life in and around a college, the only lighthouse of sanity shining through my gloom is the objectivity of a plain white background (to quote a friend of mine). The complications and stress of a country at war and in recession, under the control of a bunch of monkeys in the everlasting pursuit of their all-important \\\"shiny things\\\"; the entire situation is enough to really fuck up your zen. But with the ever-faithful and ever-bored net population in rapt awe at my makeshift soapbox, there is incentive to plod on through the muck. So each night I can spare from the grinding stone, and from my [ludicrous] social life, I spend drumming the incessant rythm of thoughts traveling through the medium of my fingers into the form of the written word (and the occasional grotesque gesture) for publication and instant gratification on my website. (Segway: www.twistedinsight.net). Otherwise, I take some strange pleasure in sending incoherent and patently bizarre emails to random aquaintences, begging them for insight into the meaning of life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If I can\\\'t laugh at myself, who else can I laugh at, judiciously? In that spirit, here are a few of my more interesting and less rational late night therapy sessions.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: elly (everybody loves elly - www.elly.org)<br>\r\nhow many drugs does it take to get to the tootsie-roll center of life?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: Kelly Dutra (greyicewater.blogspot.com)<br>\r\nYou\\\'re right about our primal patriotism tho...we\\\'re just itchin to blow\r\nshit up. Our situation is, as time usually shows, our own fault. This war is\r\ntoo damned convenient for the powers that be. That kinda concerns me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: Sam (www.insamnia.org)<br>\r\nick. ugh. bleh. These are the adjectives I would use to describe today. The worst part of it all is that nothing really unusual went wrong today....but that\\\'s the whole problem right there. This stuff usually goes wrong. Fight with Jenn, deal on sid gone bad... Now I am sitting awake staring into space/writing this/smoking and wishing I could remember how to sleep. I don\\\'t even want to describe the ungodly situation that last occurred...Suffice it to say that I can\\\'t decide which of my parts to kill, the side that wants to be selfish or the side that wants to help....They cannot coexist obviously and the stuggle between the two is fucking up my life. The increasingly dominant side of me wants to hermitize and live in a cabin mailing bombs to people. I want things to get hurt....I want things to die.....fuzzy things...things that look cute....things for which good still exists.... Most of all I hate being so goddamn petty. Maybe if I had real problems I would shut up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Gentle reader, the point of tonight\\\'s incoherent ramble (if I can determine a specific one) is to impress upon you that no matter how crazy you think you are, someone else is always pushing the boundaries of their own self image, considering that possibility \\\"If I were crazy, wouldn\\\'t it feel like I was sane to me?\\\" and thus \\\"Could I be actually crazy, and just not really notice it?\\\". And rest easy, because you can always use this little test. If you ever ask yourself those questions, yes you are insane. But don\\\'t bother with getting professional help, because the only person crazier than your kind of loon is one who understands you enough to \\\"fix\\\" your problems. Which leads me to the conclusion that has been looming over my shoulder and whispering in my ears, everyone is crazy and no one has any fucking clue what is really going on. It stands to reason, of course, if you really think about it. Each of us was jerked roughly into this existence, and none of us asked to be blessed with this dubious gift. Some of us decide to hate it and hold it against anyone here before us. Some of us hide from anything we can\\\'t easily comprehend and hope that we get from birth to death without causing too much of a ripple in history. Some of us try to take life all at once, and probably burn up in the process. No one can really say which of our paths is right, since they all start and end in the same place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So with this little literary endeavour I have calmed the raging storms in my mind once again, save for this persistent quandry. If answers only lead us to more questions, can the questions themselves be considered an answer? After all, getting there is half the trip.</p>'),(2676,'<p>\r\nwell im in fukuoka. i went to the hmv, looked at neato shoes at the nike store and got some japanese porn at the local 7-11.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city is awesome. from the air upon landing i noticed that unlike seoul this city has alot of greenery. lots and lots of trees and there are mountains on the edge of town. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had to come here to get a visa at the korean embassy and so here i am. im only here until tomorrow afternoon, but its been fun. im staying at a hotel thats shaped like a ship. its towers over a baseball stadium. the hawks. japan, like korea is baseball crazy. ive seen much less world cup advertisments here and its much cleaner and much more expensive than korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe country is short. the tables at the local wendies were like baby chairs. i felt so massive and weird. like korea you can smoke everywhere and anywhere, except inside buildings and stuff...although there are ashtrays all over the place in my hotel. the staff are all dressed as pirates and swabbies and well its really funny. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile trying to get to this comp, i asked a few pirates if thaaaarrrrr were any computers i could use. they called a few other swabbies and at almost a wink i had a motley crew on my hands. they were all trying to figure out what i meant by *internet with keyboard* (there is internet in my room, but you have to use a playstation 1 controller,,,and well ps1 is so two years ago!!!)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwell the lab is closing,...i must be off...but if any of you are ever in japan, fukuoka is a nice retreat from the bigger cities with lots of parks, nice people, and just as much to buy and look at. they even have a museum of gas here...whaterver that means...all i know...is that im glad there wasnt anyone sitting beside me on the plane here, cuz they:d haave tried to induct me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom japan, this is chiamattt reporting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh, and as a side note. WHAT THE FUCK IS UP in the middle east!!!!!! \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey better check themselves before they wreck themselves.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2677,'<PRE>\r\nYou get what you give...\r\n			-the new radicals\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\nI am alone in the house.  Before me stands the pink refrigerator, pulled into the center of the kitchen for this day it leaves the home it faithfully served for the past 39 years.\r\n\r\nA General Electric Model BG 13 TC, it was the absolute top-of-the-line model when Victor and Marie Thredgar purchased it on March 22nd, 1960 for the princely sum of $382.40.  A Chrysler engineer by trade, Victor carefully researched the purchase and chose a unit which would last the couple the rest of their lives...\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd it did.\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou see, Victor and Marie were a childless couple who moved to Royal Oak from Detroit in the fall of 1956.  They moved into a bright shining new house...a place they called home for the next four decades.  Careful and neat by nature, they meticulously maintained the little brick ranch on the corner lot until it was time to depart this world after a long and fulfilling life.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThrough it all, the refrigerator marked the passage of time.  Marie was especially proud of her kitchen centerpiece, the pink refrigerator with the bright gold anodized adjustable pull-out racks.  The envy of her neighbors, she washed and polished the appliance with such vigor the enameled surface is thin with the effort.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAlas, time exacted its toll on Victor and Marie...As it will to us all someday.  Victor died some four years ago, leaving Marie to rattle about the house until last year, when she became too feeble to live alone.  She moved out, and the empty house was put up for sale.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOur first introduction to the house was early this year.  We were struck by its condition...Walls straight and true, hardwood floors unmarred by passage of the footsteps of time.  The pink refrigerator stood proud and tall in the vacant kitchen, a bright spot in an otherwise drab room. We could feel the presence of its owners in care taken to preserve this house over 43 years.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWe looked at dozens of houses, but we kept coming back to the little brick house with the pink refrigerator, so:\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe bought Victor and Marie\\\'s place this past April.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe immediately pressed the old pink girl into service, even though we had a newer unit waiting to take up residence stashed in the garage.  So carefully had Marie maintained her pride and joy over the years that it didn\\\'t even need cleaning.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe became curiously attached to the old girl...it was as if she enjoyed the hustle and bustle of her new family of four, especially those little girl hands sneaking into her slide-out freezer for a Popsicle.  Although it can\\\'t possibly be true, it seemed like she ran cooler and quieter each time a piece of elementary school art was affixed to her shiny exterior.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAll the while, the new refrigerator waited...and waited in the garage.  I couldn\\\'t bring myself to do it...couldn\\\'t abandon such a fine old girl on the curbside simply because a newer and bigger unit was ready to take her place.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd then...A phone call.  While chatting with a business associate, she mentioned their firm had adopted a needy family for Christmas, a family in desperate need of...a refrigerator, of all things!  Even though I was a thousand miles away from home on business, I swore I heard our pink lady plead for the opportunity to serve...one last time.  I could almost hear her giggle when I said I had just the ticket to make the day.\r\n\r\nSo here she stands...tall and proud, quietly purring while she awaits a fresh batch of Popsicles and a bright coat of kid art when she takes up residence in her new home.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSo, Victor and Marie...Thanks for leaving such a wonderful place to raise our kids.  Thanks for taking care of this fine house...a place where, God willing, we will live as long and as happily as you have.  And Marie...thanks for preserving this grand old pink lady so that she may serve yet another family.\r\n\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2678,'<p>\r\nThe other day I stopped on my way to work: on the pavement, close to the wall of a building, at this place dogs love to piss a few drops on their way to work, \r\n\r\nthere were a few gummy bears. They looked so neat, I could see reflexions of light through them, colored in red, green, yellow etc. on the dirty pavement. It \r\n\r\ntook me a good part of the rest of my way to work to understand (I commute soo far, stupid me): I just stopped like I used to do when I was a kid; the beloved \r\n\r\ncandy fell on the hell\\\'s dirty floor and it\\\'s now forbidden to take it back: you have to leave it here, even though it still looks so clean and yummy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nA few days later, in our garage, as I was walking with H from the car to the stairs, I let a Pinky fall. Pinkies are tiny candies we buy in Japan; they look like Certs \r\n\r\nmints, except that the box is cute and the taste is fruty. I felt a bit upset to make it fall. I stopped to look at it: perfect tiny colored shape on the garage dirty \r\n\r\nconcrete. H had kept walking to the stairs, she didn\\\'t realize I was behind. She wasn\\\'t looking. <i>take it</i>, I thought. In a second, I grabbed it from the \r\n\r\nground and put it into my mouth. I caught up with H and started singing of happiness.\r\n</p>'),(2679,'<p>I call it therapy. After the daily grind has left my nerves raw, or the neverending drama of life in and around a college, the only lighthouse of sanity shining through my gloom is the objectivity of a plain white background (to quote a friend of mine). The complications and stress of a country at war and in recession, under the control of a bunch of monkeys in the everlasting pursuit of their all-important \\\"shiny things\\\"; the entire situation is enough to really fuck up your zen. But with the ever-faithful and ever-bored net population in rapt awe at my makeshift soapbox, there is incentive to plod on through the muck. So each night I can spare from the grinding stone, and from my [ludicrous] social life, I spend drumming the incessant rythm of thoughts traveling through the medium of my fingers into the form of the written word (and the occasional grotesque gesture) for publication and instant gratification on my website. (Segway: www.twistedinsight.net). Otherwise, I take some strange pleasure in sending incoherent and patently bizarre emails to random aquaintences, begging them for insight into the meaning of life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If I can\\\'t laugh at myself, who else can I laugh at, judiciously? In that spirit, here are a few of my more interesting and less rational late night therapy sessions.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: le (everybody loves le - www.elly.org)</p>\r\n<p>how many drugs does it take to get to the tootsie-roll center of life?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: Kelly Dutra (greyicewater.blogspot.com)</p>\r\n<p>You\\\'re right about our primal patriotism tho...we\\\'re just itchin to blow\r\nshit up. Our situation is, as time usually shows, our own fault. This war is\r\ntoo damned convenient for the powers that be. That kinda concerns me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sent To: Sam (www.insamnia.org)</p>\r\n<p>ick. ugh. bleh. These are the adjectives I would use to describe today. The worst part of it all is that nothing really unusual went wrong today....but that\\\'s the whole problem right there. This stuff usually goes wrong. Fight with Jenn, deal on sid gone bad... Now I am sitting awake staring into space/writing this/smoking and wishing I could remember how to sleep. I don\\\'t even want to describe the ungodly situation that last occurred...Suffice it to say that I can\\\'t decide which of my parts to kill, the side that wants to be selfish or the side that wants to help....They cannot coexist obviously and the stuggle between the two is fucking up my life. The increasingly dominant side of me wants to hermitize and live in a cabin mailing bombs to people. I want things to get hurt....I want things to die.....fuzzy things...things that look cute....things for which good still exists.... Most of all I hate being so goddamn petty. Maybe if I had real problems I would shut up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Gentle reader, the point of tonight\\\'s incoherent ramble (if I can determine a specific one) is to impress upon you that no matter how crazy you think you are, someone else is always pushing the boundaries of their own self image, considering that possibility \\\"If I were crazy, wouldn\\\'t it feel like I was sane to me?\\\" and thus \\\"Could I be actually crazy, and just not really notice it?\\\". And rest easy, because you can always use this little test. If you ever ask yourself those questions, yes you are insane. But don\\\'t bother with getting professional help, because the only person crazier than your kind of loon is one who understands you enough to \\\"fix\\\" your problems. Which leads me to the conclusion that has been looming over my shoulder and whispering in my ears, everyone is crazy and no one has any fucking clue what is really going on. It stands to reason, of course, if you really think about it. Each of us was jerked roughly into this existence, and none of us asked to be blessed with this dubious gift. Some of us decide to hate it and hold it against anyone here before us. Some of us hide from anything we can\\\'t easily comprehend and hope that we get from birth to death without causing too much of a ripple in history. Some of us try to take life all at once, and probably burn up in the process. No one can really say which of our paths is right, since they all start and end in the same place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So with this little literary endeavour I have calmed the raging storms in my mind once again, save for this persistent quandry. If answers only lead us to more questions, can the questions themselves be considered an answer? After all, getting there is half the trip.</p>'),(2680,'<p>I think that I was 19 when we met.  Her mother was Italian; her father was Greek and Irish.  Her parents were divorced.  She lived with her mother.  She had 4 brothers and 1 sister; they were all several years older than she was.  Her family wasn\\\'t exactly rock solid.  They were wealthy, very wealthy.  They owned a museum, and a country club, and God only knows what else.  All 4 of her brothers went to law school.  They worked for senators; they worked for major corporations; they did big things with big people.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I always worried about how materialistic she and her family were.  I fell in love with her anyway.  It wasn\\\'t that head over heels starry-eyed birds singing magical love; but it was love.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>We were a good pair, physically.  We had similar sorts of bodies, if that makes sense.  We both had large hands with long, clever fingers.  We looked alike; not in some creepy way, but we looked like a couple.  We looked like we belonged together.  I never grew tired of kissing her; her kisses were sweet.  You can be with someone physically and still, on some level, resent his or her face being close to your own; I never felt that way with her.  We were a couple, a real couple.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We shared walks together: long walks after the rain fell in autumn.  I still think of her when I smell the leaves turning brown after all the red and gold has faded.  You know, a part of me still loves her.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I struggled for years to make sculpture.  She was the one who talked me into going to the Art Institute.  When she left for the University of Wisconsin at Madison, I rode the Greyhound Bus to see her there.  Man, I spent a lot of time on the bus.  She would always talk me into going to see her, even if I didn\\\'t have money to do it.  \\\"Just come here,\\\" she\\\'d say, \\\"I have everything that you need.\\\"  Inevitably, when I got there she didn\\\'t have any food.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It has been a decade since I felt the weight of her body upon mine. I remember lying together on a beach in Evanston in late summer. It was warm enough that we felt good being there, but not so warm that the beach was crowded. The only other people within sight were a mother and her three children. As her children ran and played, the mother seemed only too happy to watch us and, I assume, recall her own youth or the youth that she wished that she had.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The wind was strong, that day on the beach; but the sun was strong too.  She felt warm, and our bodies would stick and then peel as we lay still and then moved. It\\\'s hard to contain your good feelings in a swimming suit.  And with all my naive expertise I asked her to marry me.  And she said yes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One day her mother called me to ask me to stop seeing her.  Her daughter, she said, needed to associate with people of her own caliber.  She was a part of an important family.  Madison had the right kind of people for her daughter to meet; I was just an ordinary guy who was struggling to make art.  I had no bright future.  I wasn\\\'t doing big things with big people.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Eventually, between the Greyhound and her mother, and because of everything else that I haven\\\'t written, we broke up.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>When summer rolled around I saw her back in our hometown.  She was really heavy.  She had shaved her head.  She didn\\\'t look well.  I learned more and more about what had happened.  It turned out that I wasn\\\'t the only guy that she had been sleeping with.  She had an affair with one of her professors, and with a few guys on the football team, and one of her classmates, and there was a guy who dealt drugs who was with her too.  Sometimes there were 4 or 5 different guys in the same day, or at the same time.  It turned out to be the guy who dealt drugs who got her pregnant.  He was Black.  Her family would never accept a child of mixed race, and so they told her that she needed to have an abortion in order to keep her inheritance.  She did, very late.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And so she came back home.  And I saw her there in summer.  She was taking some kind of medicine for something, and when I kissed her she tasted bitter.  I still loved her.  I tried to help her and heal her and when she felt better she found another guy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After a while I lost track of her.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Then one night I stopped at a supermarket on the way home from work.  My life was in pretty good shape at that moment: I was working as a sheriff; I had a new truck; I was healthy and strong and looking handsome.  And I saw her in line at the checkout ahead of me.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>She was even heavier than before.  She was with her husband: a balding guy who had dropped out of high school.  The two of them were still driving the same car that her parents had bought her after she had her abortion, years and years ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The whole story started 15 years ago; I\\\'m 34 now.  I\\\'m still not a very important person in the world.  I still don\\\'t do big things with big people.</p>'),(2681,'<P>This is a short, on what if feels like to lift metal towards the sky, away from the ground.</p>\r\n<p>The metal, maybe it\\\'s iron or maybe it\\\'s steel I\\\'m not sure, has a rough sort of beauty to it.  It comes textured in the grippy parts, not textured for looks but for feeling serious in your hands.\r\nMy metal is in two twenty five pound silvery shapes.</p>\r\n<p>Gravity wants to keep you trapped.  Gravity doesn\\\'t want to let go.  Gravity wants you to take the easy way out.  We fight gravity with every step we take, every breath, every blink.  When we touch the metal, we\\\'re helping the metal fight gravity too.  When you help others you get stronger inside.</p>\r\n<p>You breathe slow.  The first couple of repititions, maybe you creak a little bit, but your tendons and muscles and joints settle in after the first arcs from ground are complete.  Pretty soon you begin to feel everything working together, things waking up that are normally dormant, compression and relaxation gliding into each other.  Soon you feel as if energy is flowing through you, down your arms and out your hands, as if by thought alone a mountain could be moved.  Mountains everywhere may shudder a giggle of rocks falling, but you know.  You know.  Their day might come.</p>\r\n'),(2682,'<p>\r\nNarrative from last night\\\'s dream...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m in a small apartment, somehow even smaller than the shoebox I live in now.  Somewhat evocative of a tiny room off a courtyard that I stayed in in New Orleans once.  The room seems to be mine.  There\\\'s a green almost-loveseat that I\\\'ve been coveting, an alarm clock, a desk, not much else.  The power goes out, and I experience direct communication with some kind of deity.  It\\\'s as if I\\\'d taken some kind of\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.erowid.org/chemicals/ketamine/ketamine.shtml\\\">drug</a>, except I register some suprise in that I haven\\\'t.  The deity speaks directly to me, yet indistinctly.  Cut to the next scene.  Same apartment, some people (more indistinction - I\\\'m not sure who they are) are visiting.  I\\\'m looking at the web and I find a site with scans of original scores for Verdi operas.  The site is very beautifully done, almost immersive.  The scores aren\\\'t written in standard notation, but rather in some very expressive vaguely runic script, like a standard notation for raw emotional content.  At one point scrawled on the page next to this strange language is a note: \\\"This is where I tell the story\\\" - I\\\'m listening to the piece at that point and it\\\'s a heartrendingly beautiful cello solo.  I think \\\"Ah!  Now I get opera!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThats it, although it somehow seemed even more involved than that; I certainly wish I remember what the deity said!  Reminds me a bit of walking into a church in Zagreb, Croatia and seeing a whole wall covered with some kind of text that I\\\'d never seen before.  Or the <a href=\\\"http://www.rosettaproject.org/\\\">Rosetta Project</a>.  I\\\'m listening to <i>Aida</i> as I write this.</p>\r\n<center>* * *</center>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\\\'s something so basically wonderful about courtyards.  I was walking through one just a block and a half from the aforementioned shoebox this morning and I found a cafe that I didn\\\'t know existed.  As well as a store entirely devoted to gift baskets.  Finding new things after being somewhere for six months is practically the definition of good urban design.  This city will age well.\r\n</p>'),(2683,'<p>\\\"And when the event, the big change in your life, is simply an insight -- isn\\\'t that a strange thing?  That absolutely nothing changes except that you see things differently and you\\\'re less fearful and less anxious and generally stronger as a result: isn\\\'t it amazing that a completely invisible thing in your head can feel realer than anything you\\\'ve experienced before?  You see things more clearly and you <i>know</i> that you\\\'re seeing them more clearly.  And it comes to you that this is what it means to love life, this is all anybody who talks seriously about God is ever talking about.  Moments like this.\\\" - Jonathan Franzen\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2669#15979\\\">\r\nSomething</a> like that anyway.\r\n</p></i>'),(2684,'<P>if boredom is an alternate deminsion than this office chair, i have not been in the present.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>occasionally i practice the old art of office meditation.  sitting or walking though the cube farm taking in all sounds near and far.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>usually my head phones are on and i am listening to whatever i am not totally sick of.  some totally random to me heavy metal that bcm ripped, or some 80\\\'s pop that i nicked off of one internet mp3 archive or another that does not hassle me with any sort of upload download policy that i remember when i was new to the internet and had no friends.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have a white board.  todays message of the day was supposed to be \\\"<B>I HATE YOU</B>\\\".  there is supposed to be a message every day but every day it gets censored before the message is squeaked out.  offices,  stiffling me before i can even express myself in a reprehensible act.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i fantasize about retirement.  it goes like this:  i plot out the soonest i can pay of every single debt that i have from the age of 18 on to present that i still owe on.  usually i figure between 3.5 and 5 years depending on the climate of the economy.  then i start browsing websites with real estate in thailand, bolivia, alaska, mexico, guatemala.  then i tell egg about the place that i found that is the best and i will buy.  then i get bored.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i cant take it anymore about 2pm and drink a cup of hot cranberry herbal tea.  as it steeps, it reminds me of bloody tampons or bloody nose plugs in the toilet.  the red oozes off the tea bag and down to the bottom of the cup.  this amuses me for four minutes and then i drink it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>sometime between three and four o\\\'clock, i leave work.  usually i go home and make a snack or an early dinner and sleep for a few hours.  then go either to karate or back to work.  sometimes i sit in bed and read some fantasy novel from one to three hundred years ago.  i am reading <I>One Thousand and One Night</I> right now. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> i used to work on my motorcycle until i broke it to a point beyond my limited comprehension.  now it requires the purchasing of parts with money.</P>\r\n<P>i do not allow myself any money for the time being because of my current obsession with the 3.5 year path to starting my retirement plan.  every two weeks i have 5 dollars in my bank account from paying other people my paychecks.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>at this moment in the above schedule</B>, i just finnished the cranberry tea, the time is 1:56pm, and i am waiting for egg to arrive at my office from los angeles sometime between three and four.</P>'),(2685,'A new study revealed that eating fish such as canned tuna can significantly reduce a man\\\'s risk of sudden death from a heart attack. <i>note: this is not the same as sudden-death, say, in a hockey game!</i> The study, supported by the National Institutes of Health and the National Heart, Lung and Blood Institute, found that men with no evidence of prior cardiovascular disease cut their risk of sudden death by more than half simply by eating fish rich in omega-3 fatty acids. Who knows if this helps women; maybe women should eat more tuna, also. If you ask me, I\\\'d never date a girl that hadn\\\'t eaten tuna.<br><br>\r\nThe study (published in the April 11, 2002 New England Journal of Medicine) confirms what physicians have long hypothesized- n-3 fatty acids, found in fish and fish oils, have similar benefits to drugs used to treat abnormal heart conditions. The authors of the study concluded \\\"If the observed association is causal, increasing the intake of n-3 fatty acids by eating more fish or by taking supplements is an intervention that could be applied to this segment of the population at low cost and little risk.\\\"<br><br>\r\nIn addition to reducing risk of sudden death, the nutrients in fish, specifically fatty acids and fish oil, may play a beneficial role in lowering blood pressure and cholesterol, and can ease the pain of arthritis. Furthermore, pregnant women and their children benefit significantly from increased fish consumption, according to a recent study conducted by the National Institutes of Health.<br><br>\r\nThe American Heart Association (AHA) recommends that both men and women eat plenty of tuna- at least two to three meals of fish per week for its heart-health benefits. U.S. Tuna Foundation National Director Randi Thomas explains, \\\"one of the most convenient and affordable ways to meet the AHA\\\'s suggestion is by eating delicious, nutritious and affordable canned tuna.\\\"<br><br>\r\n<center>###</center>\r\n'),(2686,'<p>I hate lying. Mostly because I am really bad at the physical act of telling a lie (face turns bright red, eyes dart from side to side), but also because, somehow, I always end up making the situation infinitely worse for myself.</p>\r\n<p>the most vivid memory i have of this phenomenon was when i was about 11 years old, in junior high school. my mom forbade me to borrow clothes. who knows why i was subjected to such a random rule, but there it was. But we were poor and all of my clothes were hand-me-downs and i wanted nothing more than to wear my rich friendâ€™s cream-colored cashmere cardigan. i can remember this outfit so specifically: a blue sundress, tan sandles and the pretty cream-colored cashmere cardigan. i had to smuggle it in my backpack until i got out of the house, but happily donned it on the way to the bus stop.</p>\r\n<p>a few hours into the day, I managed to get a rather large smear of blue ink on the pocket of the sweater. I pleaded with the friend I had borrowed it from, but she told me that it was actually her sisterâ€™s, and that I would have to get it dry-cleaned.</p>\r\n<p>But I didnâ€™t have any money, of course, so I devised a plan to ask my parents for yearbook money, which I would then use to pay the dry-cleaning bill. It all worked out fine, until yearbooks came out. They wanted to know where mine was, and I forgot to think up a brilliant excuse beforehand. Needless to say, I choked.\r\nI canâ€™t remember the extent of the trouble I was in, but I know it involved being grounded for an obscene amount of time and subjected to extensive ridicule.</p>\r\n<p>â€œNever, ever lie,â€ my mom said to me. â€œYouâ€™re terrible at it, and they will always snowball.â€</p>\r\n<p>Clearly, 16 years have not helped me learn that lesson. I am stuck in a lie right now, made worse by my failure to come clean when I had the chance. And I still donâ€™t know: is it always better to tell the truth? Always, always? For now, Iâ€™m busy trying to catch my snowball.</p>\r\n'),(2687,'<p>\r\ni think i remember that Desmond Morris (1967. THE NAKED APE. Jonathan Cape, London) said 100 members is a maximum for a herd of monkeys. above this number, they will have to make up new social rules to avoid problematic closeness. that\\\'s what humans (naked apes) are doing in any city bigger than 100 people.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthese rules are complex, subtle. one learns them along the years, while growing up. kids are bad at these rules. for example, kids are unable to pretend indifference. if a new person comes around, they will look scared, or shy, or interested, or most often a mix of these emotions. kids cannot hide what they feel toward you. when they dislike you, it will be visible just the same.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalong the years, we improve our indifference skill. we will use it in many occasions to protect ourselves from unknown people. how to look natural while sitting next to this guy who talks to himself aloud. how to stair ahead while this beggar girl walks along the line of cars, along <i>our</i> car.<br>\r\nmore subtle manoeuvers are needed to deal with closer relationships, like co-workers. let\\\'s mention the simulated distraction used to pretend nothing is happening - while someone is just being fired - to preserve this nice friendly mood common in American workplaces.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin parallel to our indifference skill, we develop what i will call an indifference coping skill, which is not less useful than the former. with everyone around showing blank screens, day by day relationships leave us starving of emotions.\r\nhysteria and paranoia are, i think, two of these indifference coping skills. the first is a desperate attempt to break the glass and <i>talk</i> to people. the second is a self deluding habit to persuade ourself that their indifference is fake.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand now for my advice:<br>\r\nbe proud to be using <i>both</i> of these skills. talk to unknown people! also, talk about inappropriate matters with people you don\\\'t know enough. if they freak out, enjoy it! don\\\'t allow people to ignore you. your message will be:<br> <i>like me or dislike me, but <u>see</u> me</i>.<br>\r\nthis is for your hysteria skill. paranoia will fix the rest. someone who still ignores you after all you did against it <i>must be hiding something</i>: hate or jealousy. pick jealousy! this will lower your stress, and comfort your superiority complex in the bargain...\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>have fun.<br>\r\nMr. P</i>\r\n</p><p>\r\n(join Mr. P at the <a href=\\\"mailto:paranoiaClub@yahoo.com\\\">Paranoia Club</a>)<br>\r\n<i>your brain is maybe your best friend</i>\r\n</p>'),(2688,'<pre>\r\nthe grass imagines\r\n          what it is like\r\n                     to touch the sky\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2689,'<p>\r\nHad a conversation with someone tonight about the car\\\'s I\\\'ve had over the years.  I told him the list was about 35 - I lied.  Here it is in chronological order, more or less, spanning a period of 25 years:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n* 1970 Ford Maverick - crap\r\n* 1973 VW Super Beetle - gotta have one once\r\n* 1964 Ford-Falcon-Futura-Hardtop-Hand-Painted-Silver-With-A-Brush\r\n* 1964 Ford Falcon Convertible\r\n* 1967 Ford Fairlane 500 Convertible - stupid attack\r\n* 1965 Ford Falcon\r\n* 1965 Ford Fairlane - see stupid attack, above\r\n* 1964 Ford Fairlane\r\n* 1969 Chevrolet Camaro - see gotta have one, above\r\n* 1973 Opel GT - mini \\\'vette; dual sidepipes\r\n* 1973 Opel Manta\r\n* 1973 Opel Manta\r\n* 1972 Opel GT - okay, I was obsessive\r\n* 1973 Opel GT\r\n* 1972 Opel Manta Rallye - hey, a black hood\r\n* 1970 Opel GT - needed a bunch for spares\r\n* 1973 Chevrolet Nova SS - four wheeled weapon\r\n* 1963 Rambler Ambassador 990 Cross-Country Wagon - tres hip\r\n* 1978 AMC Gremlin - pure unadulterated crap\r\n* 1980 Ford Pinto - driven hard and put away wet\r\n* 1985 Ford Escort - brand new crap\r\n* 1986 Dodge Daytona - cool at the time\r\n* 1987 Dodge Shadow ES Turbo - blew engine and trans \\\'cuz I could\r\n* 1989 Eagle Medallion - french crap\r\n* 1990 Chevrolet Beretta GTZ - more new crap\r\n* 1983 Lincoln Continental - ooh...buttons\r\n* 1956 Chrysler Windsor - a pink four wheeled weapon\r\n* 1963 Mercury Comet S-22 - the vomit\r\n* 1971 Ford Maverick - three on tree with air\r\n* 1978 Oldsmobile Delta 88 - designed with a t-square\r\n* 1971 VW Bus Camper - mouse condo\r\n* 1972 Opel 1900 - maggie the opel\r\n* 1987 Dodge Diplomat - ex post copo\r\n* 1968 Cadillac Fleetwood Series 75 Limo - marital aid\r\n* 1993 Ford Festiva - licensed go-kart\r\n* 1987 Mercury Colony Park Station Wagon - cement barge\r\n* 1992 Oldsmobile 98 - dunno what I was thinking\r\n* 1998 Plymouth Neon - fast little shit\r\n* 1998 Cadillac Catera - an opel with chrome\r\n* 2001 VW Cabrio - the wife\\\'s new ride\r\n* 1970 Subaru Sambar Van - the kid\\\'s resto project\r\n</pre>\r\n'),(2690,'<p>He keeps recoiling. I have no agenda, just to touch the front of his tooth with my tongue. He has nothing to lose; he has just minutes ago brushed his teeth so I won\\\'t think him unclean or taste what the floss tastes sometimes at night after Indian food.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s so embarassing to be old, and sorry, and young, and in love. Beyond self-consciousness and shame is the place where each part of you is available without effort. After you have read and memorized all the poetry you can get your hands on, after you have taken every drug and danced to every song and named every phase of the moon in your private mutual made up language you want to advance even farther and improvise into the unknown. Maybe I\\\'m greedy. He does this thing where when I advance he laughs nervously and wiggles and blocks my access to him.</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s just that creative intimacy is in order, and I\\\'m up to the job, goddamnit. when butt sex and simultaneous orgasm are out of the way you wonder if there is anything left to the flesh that will teach you. I haven\\\'t tried to pick his nose, or lick his toe jam. I haven\\\'t tried to meet his mother, or read his credit card statement. I simply want to understand the content of his mouth. Is that so wrong?</p> '),(2691,'<p>\r\nat 5am the fire alarm in the hotel went off. i had decided that someone had fallen asleep in bed smoking. we stood outside waiting for the authorities to let us back in. the hotel staff brought us drinks and snacks. not much of an appetite at 5am though.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ngot back into my room and was unable to get back to sleep so i got dressed and went for a walk on the beach near the hotel. the beach was nice. the waves crashing against the concrete piers and wave breakers was nice. sat down on a peice of grass and watched the sun rise. tranquility at its best. a few men and women walked their dogs, resting at certain points to take in the view. i cant imagine anyone getting bored of the view...it was so breathtaking. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngetting lost in the subway is fun too, and that day i did. got on the wrong line, doubling back a few times trying to find the right connection. apparently the subway map i had been given at my hotel had been printed some time before all the expansion had occured. i found the airport and started all the procedures to get back to korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe flight was kinda bumpy so when we landed with a thud i thought to myself \\\'its good to be back on dry land again\\\'. got off the plain, passed some russian hookers/mafia girls on the moving walkway and headed towards immigration. thats when the hand was placed on my shoulder and i was asked to follow the police officer. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing seated in a small room with a couple of dogs at my side i started to get kinda nervous. did they happen upon my plot of smuggle back japanese porn? did the hotel notify them of my unpaid beer bill? what was up? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na man speaking english came in and told me i fit the description of an american cocaine smuggler. i laughed and showed him my canadian passport. to no avail. they made me dump my bags. undeclared duty free cigarettes bouncing all about. cd\\\'s, my camera, a pair of glasses with windshield wipers on them for shane, two piggy banks, and a japanese thomas the tank engine for my nephew, underwear, hat, maps, visa applications, and all sorts of other odds and ends littered the table; including my hmv bag chalk full of japanese porn.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas soon as my bag and pockets were emptied a dog jumped up on the table and began sniffing all the objects, pausing for a few moments at the underwear. i snickered, and so did the security guards. the dog wagged its tail as a sign of negative findings and lept back onto the floor. the other dog began sniffing me, wagging its tail the whole time. i tried to pet it but was told not to touch the dog. i obeyed. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nkinda frustrated, my captors started to ask me what certain things were. i knew the whole thing had become a joke when i was asked to identify my toothbrush. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni left the airport two hours after landing. frustrated and tired, i made my way back towards work, stopping for some food and one of them many local pc rooms to take a breather and rest before getting back to work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat made the whole thing laughable was that they never even noticed i was bringing in contraband pornography. they leafed through the magazines, shaking them i guess looking for baggies of white magic powder, but when nothing was found they just tossed the magazines back on the table. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the subway i daydreamed. i thought of how some pudgy american bloke was strolling through the airport with a pound of china white laughing at himself as he saw me being taken away by machine gun toting security personel. the thought made me laugh out loud.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nairports are fun. something new everytime. from geuhmo dong sk, this is chiamattt signing out. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2692,'...in three easy steps:\r\n\r\n1.	Observe \r\n[insert groundbreaking interpretations and insights here] \r\n\r\n2.	Criticize\r\n[insert overanalyzed but potentially thought-provoking revelations here]\r\n\r\n3.	Assumeâ€¦\r\nâ€¦that someone will buy this\r\nâ€¦that the girl who inspired you to write this is still with you at the time of publication\r\nâ€¦that the royalty checks will be received promptly\r\nâ€¦that an advertisement for your work will appear in a obscure, but very cerebral publication\r\nâ€¦that the cover for your book will contain all the colors of candy and not appear dry or arrogant\r\nâ€¦that your cat will not piss on your galley proofs\r\n'),(2693,'<p>It was moving day for someone.  I was looking at their stuff.  I had no idea who the stuff belonged to, but I was looking at it.  There, in front of me, were all the most intimate possessions of some complete stranger.  I really didn\\\'t have a choice: I had to look at them.  I was stuck in traffic on a road that I needed to drive down, and this stranger\\\'s stuff was there too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'ve helped a lot of friends move, over the years.  Almost everyone has a friend like me: someone who: 1) can lift heavy things; 2) has a truck; and 3) has an agreeable disposition.  At least once a month I help some friend or relative move something.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Moving, as the word implies, is an unsettling activity: your private space and your private things are made public.  The person being moved is forced not only to open their bedroom and bathroom to strangers, but those same strangers then handle the contents of those formerly private spaces.  The treasures of your boudoir hint at all the little stories that make up the whole of your life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The mattress is the tabloid journalist of the furniture world.  Seeing someone\\\'s mattress on the roof of someone\\\'s van prompted me to write this story today.  It\\\'s a difficult thing, I have found, to carry someone\\\'s bedroll, or futon, or box spring, in a dignified way.  Inevitably it gets put down someplace that it shouldn\\\'t have been put.  And strangers stare at it: looking for what might be a stain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s curious that we like to look at other people\\\'s things.  But we do.  In almost every neighborhood someone is watching: everything.  But, after a certain number of things pass in front of people they are no longer shocked by what they see.  We all have similar stuff, really.  We\\\'re all human, all too human.</p>  \r\n'),(2694,'<p>This isn\\\'t going to be a fun read.  If you\\\'re in a good mood please turn around right now because you don\\\'t want to read this.  With that out of way let\\\'s proceed, shall we?</p>\r\n<p>Everyone has secrets.  Those dark, long-term, and deeply-affecting secrets that you have never ever mentioned to anyone.  The kind of thing which usually you are able to push down and not think about but which every now and then floats up to remind you of it\\\'s existence.</p>\r\n<p>I have a secret.  I was thinking about this the other day.  I had written something about what I\\\'ve been up to lately and while I was walking home my mind began drifting.  I started thinking about something which happened to me roughly thirteen years ago and I started to want to write about it instead.</p>\r\n<p>There weren\\\'t any kids in my neighbourhood, growing up, which were my own age.  All of them were slightly older.  However, I still would hang out with these kids.  One of them, Ivan, lived just down the street from me.  His parents and two brothers were nice people.  Ivan\\\'s eldest brother, Albert, had a black Trans-Am (with the racing stripes and gold Eagle on the hood and everything).  I remember once, Albert letting me control the wheel in his car while he worked the pedals as we down the street.</p>\r\n<p>The middle brother, Paul, was babysitting for my parents, watching my younger sister and I when my parents would go out for an evening.  My Dad, like most Dad\\\'s I find, had a fairly sizeable collection of pornography (mostly limited to a collection of three-to-four years of Penthouse Magazine).  I can remember as a young kid sneaking into the closet where they were \\\'hidden\\\' and looking through them.  Not really knowing why, but knowing that I was doing something bad and not fully understanding what I was looking at (genetics and instinct, I guess).  I must have showed Paul (or he had asked, I\\\'m kind of sketchy on exact details from here on in) where my Dad\\\'s \\\'stash\\\' was located because I remember him looking through the mags.  I know we would look through them together after my sister was in bed some times.</p>\r\n<p>At this point in time I must have been 9-10 years old or something along those lines, so masturbation was pretty much out of the question; emotionally and biologically (if not physically, I\\\'m not really sure).  I don\\\'t recall how old Paul was at this time but he was probably around 15-17.  I know one night (that I can specifically remember), after my sister was in bed, Paul and I were looking through some mags when he stuck his hand in his pants and was moving it around while looking at the magazine he was holding with his other hand.  I think I asked him what he was doing, or some other kind of question which would have prompted him to undo his pants and slide them down a little to show me what he was doing.  I don\\\'t know how it happened but he had told me that I could touch his penis and I did.  He was showing me how to stroke it and I did for a bit but I must have realised that something was not right about what we were doing and I stopped.  I also remember going into the bathroom with him on several occasions but I don\\\'t think anything happened during those instances (remember, I\\\'m a little sketchy on specifics here).</p>\r\n<p>I wasn\\\'t forced.  It wasn\\\'t molestation.  He didn\\\'t once touch me.  I don\\\'t really know what it was.  I know after Sex Education and puberty and all those great things I would think back to what had happened and understand the gravity of just what had occurred during that period of time.  I never told anyone.  I realise I could have charged him with molestation and gone to court or whatever.  I never wanted to do that or tell anyone.  I just wanted to forget about it, really.  By the time I knew what had really gone on back then, Paul was long gone, moved away somewhere.  As it turns out, he was gay.  I found this out once when my Mom was talking with me and had said: \\\"Remember Ivan and Albert\\\'s brother Paul?  The one who used to babysit you and Jessica years ago?  He\\\'s gay now.\\\"  I don\\\'t know if I was surprised by this piece of information or not.  I can\\\'t remember.</p>\r\n<p>Now, why was I thinking about this the other day and why am I writing this now?  I believe that I can pin-point most of the psychological \\\'issues\\\' I have with myself to these events which happened so long ago.  Be it my insecurities, my fucked up feelings about sex and physical relationships with other people and my fears of letting people get in close to me.  I\\\'m not gay.  Things might be easier if I were and I know my Mom thinks I\\\'m gay so it\\\'s not like I\\\'d be disowned or anything that drastic if I were.  No, as it stands I\\\'m always falling for girls I can\\\'t have.  If anything this just frustrates me and reinforces the self-imposed block on letting people get close to me.</p>\r\n<p>This is the kind of thing people go into therapy sessions for and I\\\'m sure I could use it (fuck, who couldn\\\'t?) but I don\\\'t know.  I\\\'m still kind of confused about the whole experience.  I know that it shouldn\\\'t have happened and that I wish it hadn\\\'t.  I don\\\'t think it was very fair what happened.  And still, to this day, I have not told anyone this.  I\\\'ve never felt safe enough with anyone to tell them this (that includes my parents).  So why am I broadcasting this across the Internet for anyone (including friends) to read?  I don\\\'t know.  It\\\'s one of those things that\\\'s been kept down for too long.  It\\\'s the sort of thing you really just have to tell someone but how can you trust just one person?  This way, you know anyone can read it and won\\\'t be suspicious of who may or may not know.  But I know I will still keep it from my family.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what I hope to accomplish in this.  Re-reading this it seems like I might be making a bigger deal out of this than it really is.  I don\\\'t know.  It\\\'s not like I\\\'ve discussed it with anyone before to gauge a reaction.  I feel like a great weight has been lifted off my shoulders with this but we\\\'ll see how I feel in a few days about it.</p>\r\n'),(2695,'<p>\r\nMemories are often smells or touch for me.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nGoing along, living my life. Telling myself things, and all of a sudden a memory will come along. Welling up big inside my chest, me feeling like I might explode.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nLooking down at my hands, not my hands. These are not my arms. I am not me. Sometimes I am just a little bit of myself floating high above my head.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nWatching. Watching my feelings and movements. Looking down at my legs and feet. These are not how I remember my feet. Not my feet.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can almost feel it touching me. A lover. A summer night. I can hear gravel crunching under my feet. I am momentarily removed from the reality of the now and placed in the reality of then.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nI sometimes feel like I am caught between that world and this. My past world and my present.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nAnd sometimes, when the muscles are knotted up at the top of my neck, I forget who I am. Sometimes I forget that my hips are my hips, swinging full, walking me into walls. Sometimes I lose myself examining the hands down below me.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI do not know myself. Or maybe I do. I just know many selves, preserved in minute spots of time.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI wonder where my heart is. Both of them. The one I can feel rocking my chest. Out and in. Out and in. The one I can feel when I close my eyes and stop listening. Everything is high up when my heart talks to me, and reminds me of everything I forget, and reminds me to look down and see everything in it\\\'s detail. My little bits of paper, string, boots. Everything comes flooding in. A winter where I didnt clean for 6 months. My muddy boots from an excavation job that nearly killed me. Walking down an alley talking to a dear friend. Jumping, jumping, jumping.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy feet and hands tingle, and unconsciously I am feeling the tiny lines of my hand. My facial hair. The wood on the floor that reminds me of the benches I once sat on that were made of recycled bowling alley lanes.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I am sober and present, I wonder if I\\\'m just now post-processing these things from 5 years ago. From what seems to me like someone else\\\'s life.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSome days, I close my eyes and walk around the house hugging myself. Some days I put my hands under the water and feel all it\\\'s little drops as they touch my skin.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dont know how to share that time with people. I dont have words that could \r\ntell people my truth.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nI tell my story with my hands and with my body. I tell myself the truth, spreading my arms out, everything flowing past me, touching me, and I turn inside.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nI think I go to some place between thought and feeling.</p> \r\n<p>\r\nMy heart is telling me a story with my body, and I am learning to share that story with the ones I love.</p>'),(2696,'I have this thing about the month of April. Astrologically the month of April\nputs sun in Aries, the sign opposite my own which is Libra, six months away in\nOctober.<BR>\n<BR>\nApril of 1995 really kicked my ass it was the ending of a valued relationship,\nthe center of everything I had going; probably the hardest time I\'ve had\nyet. A stupid drunken mistake the previous April in 1994 was the cause of the\ninitial cracks which led to the final break up. These two years take the cake,\nbut it would take too much to get into it all here.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nIt was that April of 1995 that I looked back in curiousity at what I was doing\nApril 1993. That was the month I decided to move back to campus housing after\nbouncing away from some random roomates I ended up with. Moving back on campus\nwasn\'t necessarily that bad of an idea, but the expectation that I would\nrecreate my first year living on campus somehow was pretty stupid though. And\nthe dissapointment and isolation of that April certainly sucked.  No big deal\nhad happened before 1993 that I could remember though. <BR>\n<BR>\n\nApril of 1996 was tail end of an asian trip through Thailand and Bali after\nreturning from living in Japan the previous two and a half years. Thailand\nrocked, but Bali was mostly a drag. April 23 I was back home in San Diego for \nsome reverse culture shock. Overall a pretty good month that bucked the trend.\n<BR> <BR>\n\n1997 was fairly non-spectacular. Only a personal conflict at work when I got too\nmuch of an attitude. You should never presume an attitude of authority if it\'s\nnot backed up with anything. That\'s because in that case anyone (rightly) has\nthe right and priviledge to tell you to fuck off and you can\'t do anything about\nit. I really learned something from that. I don\'t tread that line of presumption\nand I don\'t let anyone pull it with me.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nApril of 1998 I was one month into a relationship that has yet not ended. If my\nfirst relationship was \"can\'t get ever get what you want\" this one seems to be\nmore like \"can\'t get away from what you want no part of\". Nothing of that clear\nsignificance I guess overall though.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nApril 1999 had serious work conflicts and pressures. Too many uptight people\nwho thought management by yelling was the way to go. I was brought in to a new\nposition to step up to the plate so to speak and wasn\'t prepared for the morning\nmeeting everyone else had been enduring. I\'m normally pretty laid back,\nbut something that gets me riled up is being accused of something I didn\'t even\ndo, especially if it seems like they\'re just being an asshole to blow\noff steam. I hadn\'t become familiar with this routine I would later call the\n\"morning grill session\". That\'s when the boss transmitted all the pressure of a\nlagging product deadline and escalating problems onto everyone below hime. Every\nmorning each person would report\nprogress, practically wiping sweat away after stating sufficient\nprogress or no new problems to deal with. I didn\'t quite understand the routine\nmy first day so I piped back a few remarks which then gave me a bulldog boss in\nmy face the next couple of weeks.\nNow everything under the sun was fair game, and I couldn\'t even walk past\nhis office without fear of some irrational accusation. Have\nyou ever been responsible for a software build process? When there are\ntwenty people relying on it everyday even friendly groups can get testy. It got\npretty bad with an aggro boss looking for any excuse to ream me. At one point I\nwould dial in at midnight to see if it worked and would then lie in bed at 2am\nmy heart pounding when I couldn\'t figure out what was wrong with it. I learned\nthat if you\'re lying in bed at 2am with your heart pounding unable to sleep \nworrying about work then your life is fucked.\n At 2am you should either be sleeping, drunk, or idlely\nwasting time on the internet with a slow measured heart rhythm. It was at this\npoint that I started looking for a new job. Eventually this wasn\'t necessary\nthough when the uptight boss finally just quit. What a relief. A month\nlater an even more uptight female nutcase left too and all was well once again.\nWhat a difference a month can make.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nApril 2000 I was sick. I\'m sick unfrequently, maybe only once a year or less. It\nstarted out as a head cold kind of thing. Then it moved to my ears and gave me\nearaches which required a visit to the doctor -- the only one in the last six\nyears. The medication which was some kind of hormone anti-inflammatory stuff\nthat did relieve the earache but the side effects were nearly as bad with body\naches and just wierd tired bloated feeling when I stopped taking it. The virus\nwasn\'t finished with me yet. It had also made its way to my eyes. I had to quite\nwearing glasses while I took eyedrops to make it go away.\nSince that time I gave up on contacts and I\'m still back to wearing glasses.<BR>\nAnother thing happened that month. Sometime the previous March I hooked up agai\nwith the relationship from 1998. Whether or not that was a good thing remains to\nbe seen, but it was significant.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nI was hired at a new place on April 2, 2001. Later that same month one third of\nthe employess got laid off. I can\'t really say whether choosing to work at this\nplace was really a mistake though. In any case that first month wasn\'t much of a\npositive experience. Mostly it was spent in a kind of limbo while people younger\nand less experienced than me gave me a lot of attitude cause they had been there\na whole year before me... wow. I was trying to figure out how I could fit in\nwith it all. I even wrote a couple of <A\nhref=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=822.txt\">angsty </A> <A\nhref=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=823.txt\">poems</a>.\nIt wasn\'t until June that things turned around when a someone (mentioned halfway\ndown\n<A href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=951.txt\">here</a>).\ngot fired in a divine act of justice.<BR>\nApril 2001 was also ultimatum time for the relationship. I can\'t and don\'t want\nto go into all details here, but trust me, it\'s a lot of dramatic turbulent type\nbullshit I can do without and which I\'m increasingly unable to decisively deal\nwith.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nIt was that ultimatum of last April which just came back to visit in \nApril 2002. \"By next year so and so is going to happen or else\", that was the\ngist of it. I don\'t have enough distance to really look back at this month since\nit\'s not entirely over, but I\'d say it\'s mostly pretty screwed. April is also\nthe month of four birthdays in my family, plus easter this year. I\'m pretty\nburned out on dysfunctional family get togethers.<BR>\nI\'ve also been unemployed since this February 12. It\'s already gone past the\nthat feeling of relief and freedom and moved into a more dreary monotonous\nexistence where I question what I\'m doing with my life a lot. Lately it seems to\nhave inspired some rounds of pot smoking and frenzied chaotic posts here on\nswinney.<BR>\n<BR>\n\nSo anyway, only two more days to go! We\'ve already moved into the\nplacid tranquil sign of Taurus and I\'m feeling better. I consciously decided to\nnot even begin looking for a job largely because of the fates of April.\nSuperstitious yes, but why tempt fate? I\'m hoping down-to-earth taurus will work\nnicely with my Mars-Jupiter-Pluto conjuction in Virgo and I\'ll\npleasantly plod along with some good practical progress. Let astrology be your\nfriend. Something like that, or maybe not. Who knows? I figure the fewer\nmissiles of flying shit hitting the fan would be best in any case. Job search\nday is today. I declared the week of April 28 as the start date for my job\nsearch proper. That\'s when the rascally, impetuous force of Aries should\nhopefully leave me alone and give me a break.<BR>\nI actually don\'t take any of this April superstition that seriously. You have to\nlive your life no matter where you are or what month it is. Besides even from an\nastrological perspective it\'s not so simplistically a matter of being all bad or\ngood. You need those hard times as much as the easy ones.\n<BR>Even so, it still sticks in the back of my mind though when all the shit\nseems to happen in\nApril.<BR>\n'),(2697,'<p>a boy wrote me a poem.  he wrote to me about moonlight and jasmine and something about clouds racing in the springtime night sky. i listened to him read this poem on the phone in his confident, lax way.  i felt guilty about having given in to temptation and kissed him.  i met him last tuesday late at night on the way home.  he approached me with a classic line about me looking like someone he knows. he was tall, he was young, he had charisma, i was enchanted, i invited him in. my roommates looked at me strangely. \\\"i met him on the bus,\\\" i said lamely.  he is one of those people who generate their own bright light. extraverted, handsome, loud, self-deprecating and arrogant at the same time.  i already have a lover, who is not tall but who has gravity to him.  so, no more kissing of the tall charming gangly smoothskinned boy,  even though it feels good. commitment means sacrifice sometimes.  for me, scott recently sacrificed the adoration of a tiny beautiful massage therapist.  i am not so good at sacrificing the love of strangers for the love of someone i know.  i like to count the love up like ducats, to hoard it under my bed, to keep it fresh and warm like bread under a napkin in a basket.</p>\r\n<p>relativity: my lover, a youthful thirtysomething, now seems completely grounded and sane and practical and sure.  unthreatened by the transgression my lips made, he talks reasonably about what it all means.  some oddball part of me wants them to meet.  to guardedly eye one another, the two handsome faces throwing sparks while i stand, the damsel waiting in white, guinevere between the king and the knight, looking hopefully from one to the other, hoping they won\\\'t spontaneously combust.  opposites. wiry virgo dark introvert and lanky blond saggitarian clown.  my two fantasies.  terrible.  who are they, really?  i am always asking myself how i can love so many people at once.  don\\\'t know, just can.  doesn\\\'t seem to be any limit on it as far as i can tell.  but these flings are just distractions from the lack of affection i doggedly insist on as my fate.  such an illusion, so convincing but utterly false.  at the quarter-century mark now i ready myself to attempt a true devotion to one man.  divesting myself of the aforementioned deeply ingrained habit of \\\"get it while you can.\\\"  learning to feed my soul by other means than kissing random cute twentyone-year-old strangers.  kissing random cute strangers suddenly seeming like a panicked last grab at the dessert tray before the restaurant closes for good.</p>\r\n<p>i forgive myself. i forgive myself for my blindnesses, for my mistaken terrors and hungers and flailings.  i deserve good things.</p>\r\n<p>best thing in the world, circa april 2002: lounging on plastic chairs in the garden, sun delicately massaging skin, ministry rocking the boombox, drinking champagne and orange juice and water and dozing off to sleep listening to idle conversations about love.</p>\r\n<p>also: dim sum saturday morning. cup after cup of tea.  asparagus and greenbeans and veggie potstickers and those little sweet sesame buns.  soothing soft carpet and murmurs all around.  nothing lifethreatening, only salty comfort offered at regular intervals on tiny trays.</p>\r\n<p>art feeds my soul.  music feeds my soul.  lying on my bed with freshly brushed teeth, my feet under a down sleeping bag and a cup of tea steaming on the nightstand.  walking through the park and breathing in the day.  Time To Myself. rejuvenating. it\\\'s all right to give up dessert sometimes.  the kitchen\\\'s actually always open.  you just have to be willing to cook for yourself.'),(2698,'<p>\r\nfriday was tims birthday. saturday was brandons birthday. sunday was my birthday. sunday night was the adventure. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe all met up outside the chicken hof near work at 7pm. the crew numbered six. we wandered down the street towards the river and found a nice place to have some samgipsal. samgipsal(im totally spelling it wrong) is a delicious thing for meat lovers. in the middle of the table is a hole where they put a nice red bowl of hot coals. ontop of which sits a steel grill. on the grill you throw on some rectangular cuts of pig, mushroom caps, and garlic. when the meat is done, you put it on a leaf of lettuce and add a bit of been paste, some julienne cut greens in a marinade and wrap it all up. yum yum. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe only korean with us on that night was brian. a korean national who speaks english really well. he was educated in canada and plans on going to the states in a few months to take his masters. he\\\'s a good guy. because he was the only one who spoke english he became our tour guide. where he was taking us i will never forget. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i think of hookers in korea, the only images that came to mind before sunday were the dodgy looking skanks who chill out on hooker hill in the various \\\"drinking rooms\\\" where they get men to order them drinks before leading them into dimly lit rooms in the back, down/up stairs. the women are all dressed in latest skank wear, as their clients are almost always westerners. where we were heading was a korean hooker section...far far away form hooker hill. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe got out of the taxi underneath a highway connection, the pollution biting at my lungs as i drew on my marlboro light. i really do need to get a surgical mask. nevertheless along the road are many little entrences to alleys, hidden away with what looked like those strips of plastic they have in the meat department in a grocery store. once past these smelly, dirty barriers my eyes were soon met by the shrill voices of women saying \\\"sexy sex sex. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin alleys maybe four or five feet wide were a myriad of stalls selling meat, spicy things, worms, beetles, squid, octipus, cola, beer, soju, you name it. on one side of the alley was the wall; the other side being a roadway. the opposite wall from a distance resembles a shiny glowing line of glass. However as you move closer you see that each window is a different brothel, with the ladies sitting on the floor in formation.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat set this place apart from hookerhill in etaywon was that all the women had these sullen looks on their faces. passing as many as a hundred of these places i noticed they were all for the most part watching the same tv show while they waited for their next client. they made sure their hair was in place, and put on large ammounts of makeup. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat amazed me the most about this area of town was that all the women wore the same thing. they all had on wedding dresses. for the most part all the dresses were white, but some places had them in lime green, red, and blue. it was spooky in a way. no words can describe how i felt. i became so nervous...looking at all the women sitting there either watching tv, doing their makeup, smoking cigarettes, or just staring out the window looking for their next trick. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat the entrances of all the places stood an older female, not dressed up in a gown, but rather in shabby clothes. apparently they were the ladies of the houses. they were the ones who did the bargaining, took the money, and organized who got who and all that. i wouldnt go as far as to say they were the pimps, because im sure the real power figures were men, keeping themselves shacked up in the back counting money. im also sure most of the places we past were gang owned.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\napparently these places are popular with young men heading off for their two years mandatory military training. what i saw were business men drunk as skunks swaying this way and that down the alleys. every so often youd see a man stroll out of a brothel sweat coming down his dead, and all the girls inside saying goodbye in really cheery chipper voices. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor the most part these women were good looking. they were clean, and looked very proper, no matter how improper their surroundings seemed or were. ive heard that a lot of these women are sold into the proffesion by parents not needing or wanting a daughter. its sad. i was sad walking the alleys. the only solution other than leaving my friends to forage on their own was to get shit faced drunk. and i did. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nme and tim, both feeling a bit out of place decided to get some soju and cola. forgetting about the cola i decided to just start drinking sojo. it did the trick, for a while. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbecause me and a few others didnt want to have sex, we decided to get a \\\"show\\\" and sex for the ones who did. we ended up in a back room of one of these brothels, my korean counterpart haggeling the price down to 200,000won for the six of us. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe room was smaller than my bedroom, which itself isnt the biggest room. six of us, six of them crammed into a 7x7 room with a large coffee table in the centre. \\\"when are they bringing the beer i thought\\\" as a very pretty korean woman sat down beside me in her wedding dress and put her arm around mine. thats when the chain smoking began. god i was nervous. it was like a bad dream in some ways. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na few minutes later, a broad in a lime green wedding dress came in, obviously borrowed from another establishment. she was the one doing the \\\"show\\\" for us. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas soon as she got in the room the gown was on the floor. standing their naked as can be she put a knife in her punany handle first and began cutting an apple; slicing peices up for us to eat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen the apple was cut the knife came out and in went a canle. she had us say \\\"ONE TWO THREE\\\" and on three she snapped the candle in half. wow!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncandle comes out, turkey baster with yogurt drink and beer goes in. she stands up and then squats over a glass half filling it with liquid. she passed the drink to shane to drink and as soon as she passed it to him, he passed it to tim, and tim then passed it to the next person and then to me. im drunk. what the fuck. i drank it. it tasted like yogurt drink and beer. nothing more cigarettes cant cure. \\\"man when the fuck are we gotting out of here\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter the whole \\\"holy shit you drank it\\\" blurbs finished a hard boiled egg, shell and all, ejected from the hookers nether region. offering the egg to shane, he lay it on the table...knowing not to even bother passing it around. \\\"WHAT THE FUCK!!!!!\\\". \\\"is that what i think it is?\\\", the answer from all five was a unanomous \\\"yes!\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe show ended....not five minutes after it started. the guys having sex went to do their thing leaving me, time, and shane in the room alone with one of the ladies. we explained that we werent having sex because we were married. it seemed like a good enough reason. i figured that acting religious in a country saturated to the brim with christianity was better then trying to tell that i didnt feel like sleeping with a possibly disease ridden female. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nten minutes later the other blokes returned and i gulped my beer and we got the hell outta there. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nid have to say that for me, that night was perhaps the strangest, most nerve racking night ive ever had to deal with. the women sitting with us were nice enough. you could see the girlyness in them and knew that when not at work they tried to live normal lives doing the things everyday people did. i thought long and hard about what had happened that night. trying to get to sleep was difficult. i kept thinking of what these women had to do for a living....and asked myself if any of them went to school, had friends outside of work, knew their parents, and so on. we had asked one of the girls with us if she lived at the brothel and she answered yes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nalleys of sorrow.\r\n</p>'),(2699,'<p>They didn\\\'t show up right away. We both had our eyes on the water for a long time while we hiked along the cliff, and we didn\\\'t say anything about looking for dolphins, but we kept our eyes on the water from the beginning of the little hike. Neither of us said anything, but I realized that we were looking for something incredible. THat desire ran ahead of us and shot out over the water.</p>\r\n<p>We weren\\\'t watching where we were going, we were only watching the water, and finally turned around to leave, kind of dejected, but I saw something flash at the edge of my vision, and we turned around and gasped. Somehow the message got down to where the dolphins were hanging out and they leaped.</p>\r\n<p>Maybe twelve dolphins.</p> \r\n<p>They took turns leaping along kelp beds. harbor seals joined them and goofed around in the middle of the group, and swam forward and surfaced while coasting, looking back with interest.</p>\r\n<p>One dolphin even decided to swim closer and surfaced inside of a breaking wave to check out the uprights leaving the beach.</p>\r\n<p>Did you know that dolphins will save a drowning human, if they\\\'re nearby when one goes down? What are the other conditions? They\\\'re in the right mood? Their DNA tells them to do it and they can\\\'t deny it? And does that mean that God couldn\\\'t have a part in that?</p>\r\n<p>They are so amazing as to be not credible or believable. They are \\\"in-credible.\\\"</p>'),(2700,'<P>Amassing possessions over generations is difficult when you have not been from anywhere in a few hundred years.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen I was home for the holidays, my dad showed me all the nicks and dings and broken legs on his wooden furniture.  Some heavy-handed movers cared little for these belongings that carried a high value to my dad.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"You carry and collect these things your whole life, and then they get destroyed,\\\" he said with a sad voice.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIn my room thousands of miles away from home, feeling lost and shipwrecked, looking at my belongings of books and a guitar, a motorcycle, and a jam box, floating in the salty brine while I myself bob up and down clutching a pillow, I reflect: why?  I often liken my life to a comet streaking though the sky leaving a trail of stardust:  books from college at my mom\\\'s in Plano, a truck with my brother in Dallas, a bicycle with my friend in Austin, a motorcycle in a yard in Austin, a stereo at a friends in Los Angeles, computers in a garage in South Pasadena, a box in a basement in San Francisco.  And here I am all wet in Santa Cruz looking at these items in the water with remorse for ever having tried to attain everything i wanted.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI never willingly gave it all up.  I just left behind what I could not carry, begrudgingly, but not the want.  The want I keep with me.  I keep with me at a 19% annual percentage rate in Wilmington, Delaware, or in my heart.  I know want like a Southern Baptist knows Jesus.  \\\"You have to know him in your heart,\\\" bellows the preacher!  He has Jesus at 19%.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nFixing the tables and dressers and odd old things that hold odd old stuff ended up being several thousand dollars paid for by the moving company that destroyed the items. my step mom said dad refused to pay them unless they fixed what they had done.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nAt the rate I was collecting books and computers and clothes and specialized hobby tools, if I never left them where I departed from, I would have a sleigh ready to outfit a small village of central Mexico with everything needed to grow up comfortable, equipped, and ready.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe solution is a time consuming one.  There are organizations that specialize in needing all this stuff.  A lending library down the street would love the encyclopedia of poetry and prose that I have not cracked in three years.  The Salvation Army would love to have my clothes that moved from city to city in the same laundry basket for a year and a half, and the shoes that I bought for the first interview that I had out of college and never wore again.  Goodwill would do fine with my old computers.  I even hear there is a recycling center someplace that will melt my computer monitor down and turn it into another computer monitor, or maybe a shelf.  Some things I could just sell; the really big stuff like the car.  The motorcycle I will keep because it is a dream.  The rest can sink.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGrandfather Swinney was a travelling salesman who forgot to come home.  I used to wonder if I passed places had he stopped at when I was driving on the highways in Texas.  He left behind cigarette tins and children.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGrandfather Graves was killed in a car wreck, but he worked on a train.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe great great grandfather of my mother was a sea captain.  That is all I know about him.  I don\\\'t even know what his name was, but I guess it was Captain Graves.  Floating out here, I half expect him to sail out of the white night fog and pick me out of the water with an oar.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBeing ship wrecked and bobbing up and down in the waves was my destiny all along.   I figure I had to be a comet, the should-be astrological sign for people who can\\\'t stop and leave a trail of want behind them.  And, I had a 66% chance of dying out here.  One can only accept that.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2701,'<p>\r\nairplane. continental flight 350, houston to san francisco. rows of seats, upholstered in a color of blue so dull that it registers as grey. blue: inoffensive, liked by all, or at least not noticed. bring back the seventies, when airplane seats were orange and pink.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am curled around my solar plexus. the winged tube of metal, plastic, fiberglass and fabric that carries me wobbles slightly. and again. i begin to have the thoughts i have on every plane flight. not fear, but something greater<b>:</b>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe understanding of what my life has been up until this point, what it will be frozen as forever in time if this plane explodes or falls out of the sky. i think of what i\\\'ve done, who i\\\'ve been, who i\\\'ll be remembered as, and what my death will be like should flames engulf me soon. generally at these times i feel fine about my life thus far. thus, the fantasy tends to revolve around the last moments before possible impending fiery death, and that death itself.\r\n</p>\r\nsometimes my hatred for the unnatural surroundings of the aircraft dominates my thoughts at this point, and i resent the idea of dying in such pitiful manmade environs. how dishonorable, to die surrounded by this horrid unoffensive blue upholstery, how shameful to become incinerated next to a woman in a business jacket and prada shoes.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday, however, i was so thankful to be alive, and to have the option to die, that i had no resentment for the possible location of the event. the plane wobbled, and i felt my breath on my upper lip, and i thought <i>thank you for this moment of feeling breath</i>. i felt slightly overheated, for i\\\'d been too lazy to take off my sweater, and the process of feeling the temperature rise in my body, of understanding it, was a precious moment. i was pleased to have feet, i was pleased to have a face, for that face to be touching air, for those feet to be wiggling toes in socks.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni was feeling what it was to be alive in the place in the sky i\\\'d chosen for myself. i\\\'d like to always be feeling it that much. perhaps i should live my life on a plane so that every moment feels as limitlessly detailed and intricately decadent as the moments i spent in the air today.\r\n\r\n</p>'),(2702,'<p>Desperate times force desperate decisions to be made; but hasty decision making, I have found, is rarely in one\\\'s best interest. The obvious exceptions are deciding to flee a burning building, run from the cops that are chasing you and reacting to other such immediate needs. My immediate needs seem to be necessarily drastic, but my intuition is sending up distress flares into my subconscious so that now I can\\\'t shake this nagging feeling I am doing something stupid.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am in a rut. For the last year I have been avoiding moving for personal reasons, or work reasons, or simply because I lack the adequate funds and don\\\'t like playing around without some kind of safety net. Adjectives describing my life for the past year include stagnant, monotonous, redundant, frustrating, destructive, indecisive, et al... I have lived in Charlotte, NC (or surrounding areas) for all of my remembered life, and have known for the last several years that I would be exiting stage left (westward) as soon as possible. Of course, as life rolls onward, my plans disintegrated, reformed and disintegrated again, leaving me stuck here for a little while longer each time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Originally, as a means of escaping my unstable nucleus family, I enrolled in UNCC in hopes of transferring further away from CLT as money made itself more available. Recession, it would seem, will be the most commonly uttered word during my second decade of existence, and thus money did not flow as anticipated. Once I ran out of money, I dropped out of college and tried working full time again (or as close to it as possible), thinking that surely the dot com crash would turn around in only a few months. Now I find myself with the least financial ability to escape, but the greatest necessity to date.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Human emotions can cause endless complication in one\\\'s plans. To let one\\\'s self be more than casually influenced by these troublesome chemical imbalances is costly. I learned this through means of experience, having fallen for someone very special, though not similarly motivated. For that reason, and that reason alone I stayed here, in this place I hate, for the last year. One day, very recently, I snapped out of my illusory world of pitiful self-deception and began the irreversible task of separating myself from this area, and from the person I stayed around for. Now I am going to pack my bags and get the fuck out of CLT.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dallas, TX, it seems, shall be my new place of residence, though in all honesty the Internet itself is my residency and anywhere I \\\"live\\\" is just a place to hang my carcass. But my physical being will be rooming with friends in the very flat state of Texas. Another thing I learned, this one some time ago, is that one\\\'s perception of their surroundings, becomes the nature of their surroundings, subjectively. My intense distaste towards CLT is based off my experiences there and my tainted perspective of them. Somewhere fresh and new, objectively untainted as of yet, will hopefully assuage some of my restlessness.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yet, there remains that last tendril of self-doubt; this lingering question of rightness in a situation without any clear delineation of right and wrong. Human drama bred in Hollywood studios misses the most important features of the human condition, contingency and subjectivity. This life is so royally fucked up that two people on opposing sides of an argument can both be concurrently correct and ethically conscious. Right and wrong aren\\\'t black and white matters, but a multi-dimensional grayscale with millions of factors and chaotic influences. It could be reasoned therefore, that my responsibility towards the outcome of my decisions is less than I might imagine, however I believe quite the contrary (which does not bode well for my peace-of-mind). So the deadline for moving approaches like a runway freight train, horns blaring and bright white lights burning blindingly, and at the last minute I decided to jump the track.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'ll guess I\\\'ll let you know if the train runs\r\nme down or if I manage to cross and find an open road waiting on the other side...</p>'),(2703,'<p>At one point in the middle of his <a href=\\\" http://www.jeepster.co.uk/cgi-bin/sound.cgi?l=JPR010-11-M3U\\\">song</a>, he just had to stop and giggle. He was standing on a stage, in southern California, looking out on the most beautiful sunset he had ever seen, in front of hundreds of very happy people. Bobbing, swaying, smiling. He was singing one of the best songs --- the one where the clapping makes all the difference. And though he has performed countless concerts in the bandâ€™s career, he has never â€“ not once â€“ played outside. What is with this giant green field in the middle of the desert? You know, itâ€™s not such mild weather most of the time in Scotland. And they sure donâ€™t have any of those funny looking trees, what do they call them? Palms?</p>\r\n<p>He was trying his best to keep his composure, sure, and got lost in the moment for a bitâ€¦He felt the breeze on his face, smelled the dryness of the desert, heard the people cheering. Then he looked up, and saw an addition to that gorgeous horizon. Two hot air balloons were landing somewhere in between the stage and the sandy-brown mountains. <i>hot air balloons</I>, all red and green and blue and very very real. And they had just landed. All of these happy people donâ€™t even know there are hot air balloons behind them. So he just had to stop.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Iâ€™m sorry,\\\" he says in his adorable Scottish brogue, brushing his hand across the sunset that seemed so much like a painting. \\\"Itâ€™s just so unbelievable. And look at the balloons, as well.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>Everyone turned their bobbing, swaying heads. then screams erupted.</p>\r\n<p>And he went on playing.</p>\r\n'),(2704,'It was a small bridge, as bridges go.  He found it often captured his attention on the afternoon walks the doctor ordered after the bypass surgery.  A lifetime of cautious moderation and clean living had not exempted his body from the ravages of time - the exercise was necessary to strengthen his aging heart. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHe had developed a route which took him to the western edge of his hometown.  After retirement, he chose to live (most of the time, at least) in the town of his birth.  Memories, he often said, were more valuable than all the gold in Fort Knox...this place provided wealth beyond measure.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe park bench at the edge of the creek was reward and respite for having made it halfway through the regimen.  He sat down heavily.  A hundred yards upstream was the bridge - he gazed at it again.  A simple structure of concrete and steel, it weathered the years far better than he.  Silhouetted against the low sun, the image evoked memories of an elevation drawn decades before.  Memory\\\'s floodgates opened...<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe remembered the day the commission came across his desk.  A junior engineer just out of school, he was eager to find a project to call his own.  He spent the first few months doing detail work, patiently awaiting the day when he would be given project responsibility.  His firm had been awarded numerous jobs that month, and it was by pure chance that he\\\'d even seen the RFP - it was wedged in a stack of specifications for another job.  The only reason it caught his eye was the location - his hometown.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe recalled pleading his case before the department manager, a large florid man whose very presence he found intimidating.  Much to his surprise, his boss let him submit on the job - provided it didn\\\'t cut into production.  It was only until much later he heard his boss let him proceed on account of there was no chance of success - \\\"the boy needs to learn the hard way\\\", was the assessment.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSurprised by the approval, he devoured technical journals in an effort to learn everything he could about bridge-building...He couldn\\\'t possibly let his hometown down.  It was the Depression, and his town needed the work the bridge represented.  He often read until 3:00 AM, ingesting the works of such great masters as Roebling.  In a flight of fantasy, he sketched a simple suspension design - light and airy, it would have been a graceful landmark.  Common sense prevailed, though, and the Tacoma Narrows disaster a few years later left him thankful that he never got past the conceptual stages.  Besides, his boss would have laughed him out of the office.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe recalled the exact moment when, sitting in a client\\\'s lobby, he picked up the article about shipyard welding practices, and how it drastically cut time and cost.  He remembered the tedious process of heating, fitting and hammering rivets, and the prospect of fabricating a bridge from weldments would shave thousands of dollars and hundreds of man-hours from the budget - never mind the finished structure was stronger and lighter.  He still felt guilty about taking the dog-eared magazine that day - perhaps it was time to fess up to the deed.  Later at home, he read and re-read the article, along with devouring all the references in the bibliography.  Inspired by the knowledge, he quickly drew a bridge not seen before.  Simple in execution, it embodied design elements seen in shipbuilding and Romanesque construction - a perfect balance of welded tension and cast compression in a time when his contemporaries relied on riveted arches of iron to span waterways.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe remembered the controversy and pitched arguments the design evoked.  His boss was especially negative - \\\"Son, we\\\'ve done it the same way for forty years\\\", was the refrain.  He was crushed when the man submitted a conventional design at an astronomical price, and humiliated when chosen to be in attendance on bid day.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThat night, he resolved himself to his fate.  He would submit the bid as planned.  He tossed and turned, thinking that all was in vain - he would never get to provide his town with the bridge of his dreams.  On bid day, he sat in glum silence as the bids were opened and his firm was clearly out of contention.  To his surprise, though, all bids submitted were over-budget.  He began to feel his ears get hot as he realized his design was substantially under budget.  After the bid meeting, he approached the county commissioner and quietly asked if he could have a moment of his time.  He tentatively proposed his version of the bridge, gatherning strength from his knowledge of the subject.  The man listened intently, and asked if he could see plans for the alternate design.  He remembered he had a set in the car. After a lengthy question and answer period, he left them with the commissioner.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe next few days were an eternity...He regretted leaving the prints with the county - his boss would have fired him on the spot had he known.  Late in the week, he got the call he was dreading...His boss had called him into the office.  Waiting for him was a group of managers he\\\'d only seen occasionally in the hall, including the man whose name was on the building.  He felt his stomach sink to the basement - this was it...He was going to be out on the street during the worst economic time of his country\\\'s history.<br><br>  \r\n\r\nIt was only until several minutes had passed that he realized he wasn\\\'t going to be fired - rather the group had assembled to understand the elements of the original and innovative design submitted as a voluntary alternate by a young member of their firm.  The senior managers pored over the design and decreed that it would indeed work as designed.  With their approval, the commission to build the little bridge began.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe old man smiled with the memory.  As chance would have it, the road that passed over the structure eventually became known as the Mother Road.  The specification derived from the design became the standard for all small bridges throughout the American Southwest.  He went on to design some of the biggest and most prestigious projects in his state, eventually becoming an executive in the company he\\\'d joined so many years before.  Over sixty years later, few of the projects hold the same luster and regard as that little bridge in his hometown. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHe stood and stiffly walked to the bronze plaque that bore his name.  He ran his fingers over it, and smiling with the memory of the dedication ceremony.  <br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Whatcha doin\\\', mister?\\\" a small boy asked as he stopped on his bike.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Remembering, son.\\\" replied the old man.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"What?\\\" asked the kid.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"The day I built this bridge.\\\" He smiled.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe kid\\\'s eyes widened.  \\\"WOW!  You made THIS?\\\" he exclaimed with wonder.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Yep!  Did it so you\\\'d be able to ride your bike to the other side.\\\" The old man said.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"WOW!  I gotta go tell my Mom!\\\" the kid pedaled off in a burst of energy.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe weight of age floated away from the old man...for an instant, he became the little boy riding home to share his discovery.  He proudly resumed his walk home...eyes moist from the realization of life\\\'s work.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2705,'I\r\n\r\n<p>You canâ€™t<br>\r\n(word)<br>\r\n(phrase)<br>\r\n(broken device) have<br>\r\nmuch more than <br>\r\nliverlight,<br>\r\ndrinking Aquavit<br>\r\nin the switchyard<br>\r\n(moon)<br>\r\n(punctuation mark).<br>\r\n\r\n<p>II\r\n\r\n<p>(proper noun) <br>\r\nnightglare:<br>\r\nthe angle<br>\r\nof the branch<br>\r\nreaching out<br>\r\n(stones)<br>\r\n(ballast)<br>\r\n(phrase)(enjambed line)<br>\r\nover cold white snow.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>III\r\n\r\n<p>(fearsome wine)<br>\r\nclimbed upon the<br>\r\nbreakwater<br>\r\n(verb)(forced issue)<br>\r\nand bale-eyed<br>\r\nwatched the<br>\r\nstars <br>\r\n\r\n<p>IV\r\n\r\n<p>(workmate)<br>\r\na tense element<br>\r\nacross the room<br>\r\n(fuckthoughted)<br>\r\nnightly dreaming <br>\r\n(verb)<br>\r\nof meshed limblike <br>\r\n'),(2706,'The party the other night<br>\r\none of my first where every couple was<br>\r\nnewly married, in their early thirties,<br>\r\nbut already falling into<br>\r\nthe habits they will cultivate<br>\r\nas they approach their <br>\r\nparentâ€™s age.\r\n\r\n<p>With no need to flirt anymore,<br>\r\nThe room quickly divided into<br>\r\nmen and women,<br>\r\neach nattering their own<br>\r\nlittle nothingness at each other<br>\r\nwhile drinking beer<br>\r\nor red wine <br>\r\nand staring<br>\r\ninto the mirrored corneas of their friends,<br>\r\nand grinning,<br>\r\nsometimes even laughing, <br>\r\nat their wit of their own reflections.\r\n\r\n<p>The men, it must be noted <br>\r\nwere of the worst sort: <br>\r\nout-of-work actors, grad students,<br> pontificators, painters, <br>\r\nand at least one poet. \r\n\r\n<p>Show-offs<br>\r\none and all.\r\n\r\n<p>One of the actors, <br>\r\nwhoâ€™s head seemed literally encephalitic by<br>\r\nTwo hat sizes (no joke!), said:  â€œI really must<br>\r\nTry out for Macbeth  It is a role<br>\r\n Iâ€™ve always thought I could do something with.<br>\r\nReally sink my teeth into, but of course <br>\r\nI may be a little young for what<br>\r\nThey want.  I think theyâ€™re looking<br>\r\nFor an older Macbeth.â€  \r\n\r\n<p>And the other replied with, <br>\r\nâ€œYes, you should go for it.<br>\r\nOh, by the way, there is this new play Iâ€™m <br>staging<br>\r\nThat Iâ€™m really excited about.  You could try out <br>for the part of<br>\r\nFaust.â€<br>\r\nâ€œFaust? <br>\r\nHa Ha!  <br>\r\nIf Iâ€™m too young for Macbeth then Iâ€™m definitely to young<br>\r\nfor Faust!â€<br>\r\nâ€œItâ€™s not the classic Faust, itâ€™s an updated version<br>\r\nby a young playwright.â€\r\n\r\n<p>I stared bitterly at my empty beer bottle \r\n\r\n<p>listening.  \r\n\r\n<p>I had nothing<br>\r\nto say<br>\r\nI was not an actor,<br>\r\nNor director, so I kept silent.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>I am just a writer of poems and stories<br>\r\nAnd I donâ€™t even like talking about that.\r\n\r\n<p>I turned my back on these\r\nmen<br>\r\nAnd went to the kitchen were I topped off a glass<br>\r\nOf Irish Whiskey .\r\n\r\n<p>I drank off the whiskey <br>\r\nand came back into the room<br>\r\nwith another beer.\r\n\r\n<p>The actors were still talking about parts they<br>\r\nWanted to play, <br>\r\nthe painters were describing paintings<br>\r\nThey wanted to paint,<br>\r\nAnd all of them were listing<br>\r\nThe great books that they had either read or <br>intended to read.\r\n\r\n<p>I just drank my beer.\r\n\r\n<p>But before I knew it<br>\r\nI was at the bottom of the bottle <br>\r\nand had to go back for another.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>In fifty years most of us would be dead.<br>\r\nThough, probably not our women who<br>\r\nHave somehow knitted <br>\r\na longer lifespan on this earth.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>In the kitchen I drank another whiskey <br>\r\nand took a beer from the fridge.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>This time I went over the women<br>\r\nAnd sat next to my wife, pressing my bored thigh<br>\r\nAgainst her warm one.\r\n\r\n<p>They were talking about something <br>commonplace:<br>\r\nWriting thank-you cards for wedding presents, <br> crystal knives,<br>\r\nChina patterns, spoons and toasters.<br>\r\nAnd even better, they <br>\r\nrecounted stories, <br>\r\ngood stories,<br>\r\n about how their drunken fathers <br>\r\nHit on their bridesmaids.\r\n \r\n<p>I just sat there with my beer and the women<br>\r\nWhile the guys talked<br>\r\nTheir serious talk on the other side of the room.\r\n\r\n<P>and I wasnâ€™t as bored as I was before.\r\n'),(2716,'<p>There\\\'s a man who lives across my street who walks like you and another man \r\non the radio sounds like you. They both help me think constantly of you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While I\\\'m still noticing my phone\\\'s not ringing they are proxies that keep me \r\nhoping and teetering.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I stay here and make things from stone and cloth and wood, sometimes crying as \r\nsparks flash and die.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As I see droplets of my heart sinking into sandstone, knowing you don\\\'t know \r\nthis is happening to me, I tell myself something\\\'s growing from this silly pain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I make these lovable things for other strangers to hold like I wish you\\\'d hold me.</p>\r\n'),(2707,'<p>\r\n<u>the notes:</u><br>\r\npere noel cone rouge blanc projette jouets couloir je suis petit cochon jette jouets de nul pqrt approche pluie de j puis disparait: je suis un j;petit cochon<br>\r\nbruit=musique lou-luu-lii; peur pluie, vais contre le mur; ombre sur le mur;passe;silence, je suis seul<br>\r\ncouloir circulaire; avance comme machine; face blanche etsphere rougre\r\n</p><p>\r\n<u>their translation:</u><br>\r\nsanta claus cone red white throws toys corridor i am little pig throws toys out of npwhere approaches shower of t then disappears: i am a t;little pig<br>\r\nnoise=music loo-luu-lii; fear shower, move close the wall; shadow on the wall;passes;silence, i am alone<br>\r\ncircular corridor; moves like machine; face white andsphere redr\r\n</p>\r\n(to be continued...)'),(2708,'<pre>\r\ni protected the idea of you\r\non a day when everything sacred\r\nthreatened to rise to the surface\r\nyour name would not rise \r\nto the surface of my tongue\r\nthe syllables and utterances that mean you;\r\ni kept them large and silent in the place below my throat\r\n\r\ni would not describe you\r\ni would not say your name\r\ni would not tell them what you are\r\n\r\nto conjour you into the mundanity of language \r\nis to invite disappointment\r\nthe motions of my lips, mouth, teeth \r\nand \r\nthe motions of my diaphragm, throat, vocal chords\r\n\r\n(of my breath: controlled, pushed out, sucked in)\r\n\r\ncould never create you in sound\r\nsounds could never add up to words that \r\ncould never add up to sentences, paragraphs that \r\ncould never add up to anything that did justice\r\nto the you that i know.\r\n\r\n\r\nto speak your name\r\nis to dishonor us both\r\nto name you\r\nis to abandon the truth\r\nis to become small, to make everything small.\r\nto give up.\r\nto die.\r\n</pre>'),(2709,'<p>i want to make movies of you all, lengths and lengths of film full of cigarettes, guitars, elvis, endless echinacea tea, red high heels, all the tiny puzzle pieces which remind me of my friends.  i want it to be like those old movies, no dialogue, just the moving images of people with an overlaid soundtrack.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you can sit on the stairs outside my house & you can stand knee deep in ocean & you can shoot pool & you can run through the streets of the city at night, wildly & ecstatically. we will play pulp & pink, pj harvey & pavement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it will be magic because when we are together, we are utterly invincible.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you all keep my world spinning, even when it threatens to wobble off its axis altogether; you speed it up until i can\\\'t breathe because i\\\'m so\r\nexcited by it all.  that\\\'s the way i like it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as we grow older, as we get caught up in other paths, other plans we will inevitably separate & move away from here.  we won\\\'t see each other so\r\noften, but i will always have the film i made so that when i miss you, any of you, i can put it in & remember how much i love you all.</p>'),(2710,'<p>The last article I wrote didn\\\'t make it onto the site for what I\\\'m sure are various nebulous reasons.  I\\\'m not sure what to think about it, so I decide not to think about it and wonder about it out loud.  Maybe someone else can think something about it for me.</p><p>I\\\'m moving.  I hate moving.  In fact, I hate it so much that I haven\\\'t done it yet, even though I was supposed to be gone from here and living in there on May 1st.  It\\\'s early on the 3rd and I\\\'m still here destined to pay rent on two apartments for a month, listening to <i>Damn I Wish I Was Your Lover</i>.  heh... There are of course the various nostalgic pleasantries involved in unlodging all your possesions from entrenched locations: finding $80 tucked under an old book cover, finally being in possion of my medical insurance card, reading old love letters lost in piles of clutter.  Those are the highlights so far.  Well, those and the chance to ponder the last year of my life.</p><p>The primary reason I\\\'m moving is that I got \\\"No-Fault Terminated\\\" from my job, and I need to chase cheaper rent, something my unemployment checks can cover after my severence runs out.  The path to my jobless state was long and sordid, a tale completely unto itself for another night.</p><p>It seems that I can never go over a year without completely uprooting my existence, tossing it into a mixer, and serving it chilled and well shaken.  At some point I need to stop running away from my life, I eventually won\\\'t have the energy to do it anymore.  I fucking hate moving.</p>'),(2712,'<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2712_1.jpeg\\\">\r\n<p>\r\n<b>in girum imus nocte et consumimur igni</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"we go round and round in the night and are consumed by fire\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2713,'santa claus is in the corridor. i see his back as he is walking away from me. there are toys everywhere on the floor. he is throwing them around as he walks. the corridor being circular, i decide to walk in the opposite direction to meet him. as i reach the last corner, i can hear him coming. the noise of the falling toys, and a music, like a broken music box repeating the three same notes: <i>loo luu lii loo luu lii loo luu lii...</i> i pass the corner and see him coming. <i>that is not santa claus.</i> a white cone instead of a face, with a red sphere at the end that could mimic a nose. and the toys, he does not <i>throw</i> them: they appear out of nowhere above him, propulsed forward. it doesn\\\'t walk either, it hasn\\\'t got feet; it floats or rolls toward me like a machine. fear. fear. fear. soon, the toy shower will pour over me. i lean against the wall and try to protect my head with my arms. it\\\'s passing. i see its shadow on the wall: the big cone-headed thing, and the shadows of the toys popping out in the air above it. and its insane music is all i hear.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>sudden silence.</i> i guess i passed out. i\\\'m looking at the empty corridor. the toys are gone too. now i am alone. but i am a toy, a little pig-shaped toy.</p><p>\r\n(to be continued...)\r\n</p>');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (2714,'<p>sometimes i just don\\\'t want to use the brakes.  i want to say whatever i want to say without thinking hard in advance about how i sound to other people.</p>\r\n<p>i believe that this is not necessarily a bad thing to do, so long as you don\\\'t do it every day or even habitually.</p>\r\n<p>today i would like to flame the assholes of the world, but time is short, so i will just make a short list of those who deserve flaming and maybe i will come back and target them with explicitly exquisitely exhortations to expire.</p>\r\n<p>clerks who ignore you in favor of your male shopping partner, people who tailgate, people who flash their brights in order to make you move to another lane, people who scream at their children in public places, people who grab small persons by their arms or ears or other apendages in order to force the small person to do their bidding, people who do not realize that children are small persons, not objects, not possessions, not whipping posts, people who judge others by their cars, houses, hairdos, makeup, body, or other visual methods, people who join mailing lists in order to get spam materials, spammers in general, people who judge others by the number of degrees or places where they earned their degrees or lack of degrees.</p>\r\n<p>dogs that shit where i intend to walk, people who call me on the phone when i am asleep or trying to eat or get laid, men who assume because i have bodacious tatas and are friendly to them, i would like to fuck them, people who pretend to be important because of the size of their wallet, people who think that the world owes them free stuff for any reason, people who steal my intellectual property, people who assume i am anything but what i am without bothering to find out if their assumptions are right, car salesmen, insurance salesmen, telemarketers disguising themselves as charitable organizations, telemarketers who change their names in order to pretend like they didn\\\'t call me before when i asked them to take me off their lists, mci, barbara who is a canned telemarketer who wants to sell me insurance, yahoo who is selling my information in tricky ways, and there is more, but i have to leave now.</p>'),(2715,'<p>Do not smile at the Broken Hearted Arsehole Robot.</p>\r\n<p>The metallic chameleon will return the favour in a different flavour.</p>\r\n<p>Interested?</p>\r\n<p>Do not attempt to interrogate the Broken Hearted Arsehole Robotâ€™s crisply kept packaging.</p>\r\n<p>The unravellings you entice will send a virus into your own file system.</p>\r\n<p>A mistake.</p>\r\n<p>(Takes ages to disengage from the Greener Pasture Syndromeâ€™s startup program.)</p>\r\n<p>When the Broken Record of the Origination of Manufactured Pain begins to play, do not listen.</p>\r\n<p>All Broken Hearted Arsehole Robot Crap must be disposed of appropriately.</p>\r\n<p>Press correct button and all information will be flushed away.</p>\r\n<p>Do not subscribe to the Stupidity of Understanding.</p>\r\n<p>Still interested?</p>\r\n<p>Repeat after me:</p>\r\n<p>â€œI do not want to be a Broken Hearted Arsehole Robot for the entirety of my lifeâ€.</p>\r\n'),(2717,'two brown fists landed on either side of the pillow i inhabited.  they remained motionless, perhaps grasping the strength they had never used. but no, that can\\\'t be right.  there.  they were flat, curled fingers face down upon the mattress.  each bone of his hands shoved its white self to the surface.  tan skin concealed the pale framework beneath, flawless, like the perfect christmas presents your parents wrap but you can never duplicate.  my own hands were straight at my sides, bulging out of my wrists with patches of wrapping paper to cover each hole.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsilence.  my shuddering breaths seemed encased in silence.  every gasp went ripping through the empty air. is terror a strong enough word?  i lay there playing a video of the day\\\'s events on the back of my eyelids. hours ago i\\\'d come searching for him only to have him jump out from behind my door.  i never flinched.  when his face was the first to show surprise, i calmly explained that i\\\'d been expecting a murderer to hide there since i was five years old.  nothing shocked me any more. now i was stiff & wide eyed under a person i barely understood.  my heart flexed uneasily in its cage, protesting this sudden fear.  i tested my voice with a simple request.<br><br>\r\n\\\"unlock the door.\\\"<br><br>\r\ni was vaguely aware that i could blame myself for this.  it had been my order that turned that bolt.  now i was sinking under the weight of my own decision.  worried that my mother would find us & send him away forever, i\\\'d made myself easy prey. there was no response.  instead the body on top of mine grew heavier, positioned to cover (or strangle) each of my limbs.  his forearms lay over my wrists, hiding the bright blue veins.  i was paralyzed. \\\"please,\\\" i whispered.  \\\"what if she comes home?  unlock the door.\\\"<br><br>\r\nhe turned his face away, resting his head between my breasts.  i breathed in swiftly but couldn\\\'t make my body small enough to shrink from that pressure.\r\nhe finally spoke.  \\\"no.\\\"<br><br>\r\nstunned, i tried desperately to meet his eyes as he readjusted himself to fit more deeply into my crevices.  i felt him press against me & was immediately sick.  he was moving slightly.  my body screamed & i tried to imitate it, but any noise i made was swallowed by his ragged gasps. i trembled elegantly in the grey bra he\\\'d called beautiful while he poured hot air down my neck.  (i should have known when it was the bra, not me, he praised.)<br><br>\r\nthen everything was cold again.  the statue rolled off of me.  in twelve wide steps he reached the door & threw it open. i crawled into the corner of my bed reciting <i>it\\\'s okay, you\\\'re still dressed, he stopped, it\\\'s okay</i> silently to myself.  knees tucked up to my chest, i let strands of brown & red fall across my features.<br><br>\r\nhe was returning now.  i closed my eyes on the image of him sitting down beside me.\r\n<i>it\\\'s okay</i> i repeated.  i lay there motionless, grasping strength i\\\'d never used.'),(2718,'<p>I found some tv\\\'s in the street and I was startled by the thought...</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Dad could fix these\\\".</p>\r\n<p>He\\\'d give us old tv valves, or we\\\'d steal them. Out in the banana patch they became space ships and treasures to be found by an unimaginable person of the future.</p>\r\n<p>Once, over savoury mince, peas and mashed potato he hit Mum on the head with a milk bottle.</p>\r\n<p>As the dinner time air hung heavy as lead Mum said...</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Will you girls eat your meat?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>He\\\'d take me out bush to chop some wood. With me on his shoulders and his steel toe shoes on his feet he\\\'d chase snakes, while I screamed with concern...</p>\r\n<p>\\\"It\\\'s alright\\\" he\\\'d say and I knew it was.</p>\r\n<p>Now that he\\\'s gone, one by one I share snippets of him with others.</p>\r\n<p>They smile or frown or look down.</p>\r\n<p>But the cancer that consumed his body consumed some snippets with it.\r\n<p>And from now on when I find tv\\\'s in the street, the thought will please...</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Dad could fix these\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2719,'<p>right now i\\\'m in that mode you get in when you\\\'ve secretly wanted a person for so so long and it finally gets to the point you can\\\'t take it anymore and you just want them to know, no matter what the outcome. and then you make sure that they find out, and now it\\\'s that in between \\\"anything can happen now\\\" stage. it\\\'s exciting and scary and you walk around with an idiotic smile on your face all the time. you also , if you\\\'re female anyway, analyze every single word spoken, gestures, movements, and actions from the person. do guys do this? i pretty much thought guys didnt. \r\n<p>\r\nso that\\\'s where i am right now and i\\\'m hoping that i can do it right this time. not repeat the same patterns i fall into and have let ruin potential real actual relationships for the past 4 years. i don\\\'t want to run away this time. i don\\\'t want to use the same excuses and i want to just finally let my heart be open and be really honestly close to someone again. i feel the potential this time and i haven\\\'t felt this in so long. \r\n<p>\r\nalthough knowing the way my life goes.... nothing will happen with this at all. it\\\'ll stay in this \\\"in between\\\" stage forever cos i won\\\'t be able to actually function enough to make anything concrete happen. \r\n<p>\r\ni mean it\\\'s already been a year. \r\n'),(2720,'<p>How do I know these hands are mine?</p>\r\n<p>After all the messages that identify them, the crossed lines and miscalculations, warm touches and other mysteries that have passed over and over these palms, what is left to transpire that they do not already know?</p>\r\n<p>Tomorrow is today with a different glove, Yesterday gestures from behind closed curtains.</p>\r\n<p>Now seems like the time to hold something.</p>\r\n'),(2721,'<p>My heart\\\'s been so many places, it\\\'s hard to imagine the magnitude required\r\nof those that will form my future.</p>\r\n<p>Picture a tall forest, sprinkled with white:  cold and miserable, stark and beautiful.</p>\r\n<p>I want to see such a place and still believe there\\\'s no-one else I can be.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2722,'<p>entertaining some<br>\r\norange-bus traveling hippies<br>\r\none, wayne, doesnâ€™t drink<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>slurp margaritas<br>\r\nin front of him anyway<br>\r\npatio dining<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>then beer at elsieâ€™s<br>\r\ntalk of frisbees and hiking<br>\r\nthey sleep on the street<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>sex before breakfast<br>\r\ndiscover cream cheese in eggs<br>\r\nis best thing ever<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>why wonâ€™t they just leave?<br>\r\nnothing against them, it\\\'s just<br>\r\ndoing nothing rules<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>hardware store is fun<br>\r\nboards, bricks, but no bed brackets<br>\r\nwe keep breaking it<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>he has books again<br>\r\nboxes finally unpacked<br>\r\nnow it feels like home<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>cartoon bondage chicks<br>\r\nart, architecture, sci-fi<br>\r\nred hook with dinner<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>grey, gloomy sunday<br>\r\nescape in convertible<br>\r\ndrinking in vineyards<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>fun while it lasted<br>\r\nbut pay for it in headaches<br>\r\nand dehydration<br></p>\r\n\r\n<p>spend rest of the night<br>\r\nwatching simpsons and cuddling<br>\r\nwhy must weekends end?<br></p>\r\n'),(2723,'finally after weeks of work i have perfected the ancient and obscure art of cooking with marijuana in an effective and money-saving fashion. what follows are my recipes, modified from various dubious internet sources and tested through trail and error, much to my frustration and expense. please feel free to share your own recipes, but rest assured that these will work properly. the gnome behind my eyeballs tells me so.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>Cannabis Butter</i>\r\n<p>\r\none quarter-ouce bag of schwag pot. i mean it can be nasty worthless bullshit.\r\n<br>\r\nTwo sticks of butter\r\n<br>\r\nWater\r\n<p>\r\nFill a saucepan about halfway with water. bring it to a rolling boil, but not niagra falls rolling. mildly rolling. place one, and then two sticks of butter into the boiling water. look at the pretty colors. then, place the picked-apart pot, seeds, stems and all, in the pot as well. boil for about 15-20 minutes or until the water etc. takes on a <i>slightly</i> brownish cast. don\\\'t worry, you can\\\'t really burn anything unless the water gets too low. allow the mixture to cool for just 2 or 3 minutes, and strain into a large container, taking care that it is shatterproof. pyrex is good. don\\\'t bother with cheesecloth, just a flour sifter or a colander with small holes will do. place the bowl in the fridge, and don\\\'t touch it for about 3 hours. don\\\'t try to cheat and leave it in the freezer for 30 minutes instead because you will forget it, if you are a big enough pothead to actually go through with all this. after a few hours, the butter will have risen to the top and solidified. use a spatula to skim it off the surface of the water. try to get big pieces. lay them wet side down on a layer of <b>3</b> paper towels on a plate. let it dry for 10 minutes or so, then you can freeze some and put some in the fridge. \r\n<p>\r\ndosage: 2 inches square, 1/2 inch thick. poof. wait at LEAST an hour before having more.<p>\r\nnext we make, with the same pot we used in the first recipe...\r\n<P><i>Cannabis Milkshakes</i><p>\r\ntake the pot which you just used to make the butter, and place it in your now-empty saucepan.  add one cup of cold milk and a small pinch of rosemary, and heat the mixture, stirring constantly, on low heat for about five minutes. sorry, i don\\\'t think soy milk will work, it has to be real fat. having thus extracted the remaining THC into the milk,  strain once more and pour the milk into a waiting blender. add one cup Breyers Peach ice cream or the body-friendly substitute of your choice (in which case i recommend adding a teaspoon of light olive oil) with fresh peaches. man, i bet the breyers would be good with fresh peaches mixed in too. add a half-cup of ice. pulse. tasty beverage, gets you pleasantly stoned until the butter is ready. \r\n<P>\r\ni think margarine will work, but not as well. besides, you only live once.'),(2724,'<P>i got out of the hospital yesterday at 3 in the afternoon and called home to see if someone could pick me up. nobody was there so i called a cab. i don\\\'t like to hang around after being discharged. i start thinking they might find a reason to keep me there.</P><P>so am i cured? who knows. here\\\'s something though: before checking myself in i took a rolling pin and flattened some hash into 4-inch squares and placed it inside the liner notes of 5 cds. i left them on a shelf in my bedroom and showed blair where they were so she could smuggle them in for me if i started jonesing too badly.</P><P>and i never felt the need for it. the cds are still on the shelf: old r.e.m., the b-52s\\\' second album, pylon, the glands, macha. athens rules, yo.</P><P>am i going to get high now? i think it\\\'ll be alright as long as i stick to cannabis and avoid coke and alcohol. those are the ones that do me in.</P><P>so the cab ride cost twenty-five dollars. i rode in the back feeling like the king of america and tipped the driver five bucks when we got there. i walked in and there was a message for me from pastor greg. he\\\'s the minister at my parents\\\' church. i called him and he came out to the house and prayed with me and showed me a little scripture. and i turned my life over to jesus fucking christ. i damned sure did. becoming a christian felt like the taoist thing to do. go with the ol\\\' flow.</P><P>now why would i go and do a thing like that? it was an earnest act. it was nothing i was pressured into. i didn\\\'t do it for my parents. sometimes a path presents itself and you follow it.</P><P>so can i praise jesus and worship santeria deities at the same time? plus practice mahayana buddhism or yoga or anything else i want to? i don\\\'t see why not. people all over the world do it all the time (especially those in africa and the caribbean and latin america).</P><P>maybe this is my future. to marry a nice christian girl and have lots of babies. get baptized and proselytize for the lord. i don\\\'t know. i\\\'m not sure how lucid a decision it was but it feels right in my heart if not in my head. i feel changed.</P>'),(2725,'There are many requirements<br>\r\nwhen it comes to writing a poem:\r\n\r\n<p>Some sort of formal craftsmanship<br>\r\nan attention to language, rhythm<br>\r\nand detail.  \r\n\r\n<p>A poem should accomplish something great<br>\r\nwith<br> \r\neconomy<br>\r\nand<br>\r\nprecision.\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m thinking now about the struggle<br>\r\nof poets I know<br>\r\nwho labor for months<br>\r\nover a few lines.\r\n\r\n<p>All I\\\'ve ever wanted to do<br>\r\nis carve reality<br>\r\ninto the page;\r\n\r\n<p>to capture life<br>\r\nin a few breaths.\r\n\r\n<p>A poem is<br> \r\nless difficult<br>\r\nthan a sculpture\r\n\r\n<p>but not everyone can do it.\r\n\r\n<p>So I am lucky.\r\n\r\n<p>As I write this,<br>\r\nthe television drones in the next room<br>\r\nand above my head<br>\r\nthe neighbors thump and moan.\r\n\r\n<p>This is the life of an<br>\r\napartment dweller;\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>of a<br>\r\npoet<br> \r\nwho has recently<br> \r\nlost his job<br>\r\nat a research company\r\n\r\n<p>and is now<br> \r\nat home<br>\r\nfor the first time.\r\n\r\n<p>**\r\n\r\n<p>I will not go back and revise this poem.\r\n\r\n<p>What I consider my best work<br>\r\nis written<br> \r\nstraight out,<br> \r\neach phrase<br>\r\nlies on the page<br> \r\nlike a<br>\r\nnaked woman. An\r\n\r\n<p>uncorrected proof.\r\n\r\n<p>**\r\n\r\n<p>The Chinese poets \r\n\r\n<p>did not feel the need to ascribe<br>\r\nto this scholarship<br>\r\nor that school of philosophy.\r\n\r\n<p>For them, there was the<br>\r\nbottle of wine<br>\r\nand a<br> \r\npiece of paper\r\n\r\n<p>So I think<br>\r\nabout Li Po\r\n\r\n<p>I unroll my scrolls\r\n\r\n<p>and I know that what I write\r\n\r\n<p>is poetry.\r\n'),(2726,'<p>this is going around on newsgroups..</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMTV-VIACOM contacts about MTVX\r\n<p>\r\nMTVX, the only 24-hour rock/metal/punk channel was replaced by MTV Jams\r\nthat plays nothing but rap and hip-hop.  Not everyone likes rap.  Find\r\nMTV and Viacom contacts here:\r\n<p>\r\nhttp://www.pitbulls.com/mtvx/\r\n<p>\r\nFLOOD THEM WITH E-MAILS, FAXES AND PHONE CALLS!  Sign the petitions if\r\nyou haven\'t already.  The petitions are emailed regularly to MTV\'s top\r\nbrass.   Please copy this and forward it to any lists, forums, chats,\r\nfans, etc. who want MTV X back!\r\n<p>\r\nhttp://www.pitbulls.com/mtvx/\r\n<p>\r\nnow, i work for mtv.. and i\'ve known about this for months. i was so totally hoping for backlash like this once the public caught onto the fact that when they saw all the advertisments for mtv jams \"coming in may\", they neglected to mention \"in place of mtv x\". thing is most people i work with are just as upset about this. in the area we work in, we have access to all the networks, and X was turned up 90% of the time. since jams has replaced it, i haven\'t seen it turned up once. everyone has said the same thing: \"mtv plays only rap, mtv2 plays only rap basically (cant forget 120 mins once a week at 2am on a sunday night), and now this\". to  top it off, we already HAVE VH1 Soul as well. so now mtv, mtv2, vh1 soul, and mtv jams are basically clones of each other, and not one network that plays rock/alternative/punk anywhere now.\r\n<p>\r\nit was very depressing last week when i was handed a list of about 10 pages of rock/metal videos and told \"you can delete these from archive now\", and given an equally long list of rap/hip hop videos and told \"these need to be dubbed in\"\r\n<p>\r\ni love the job i do.. but my company really has no clue. '),(2727,'<p>There is a yellow rose in a small plastic Rubber Maid drink container filled with water sitting on top of my dresser.  Last night she gave the flower to me.  Well it wasn\\\'t exactly like that, but is it ever?</p>\r\n<p>After the show we were all walking down Queen Street.  I suggested that we go to NASA because I had promised to take someone there months ago and never followed through.  We looked inside and pleasant-sounding hip hop was enticing me to go to it\\\'s source.  But the people who had a long bus ride and school in the morning ahead of them decided that they should go home right away instead of waiting for the last possible bus to leave the city.  After escorting the out-of-town quartet to the Greyhound station, we departed with no particular destination in mind.  That was when she suggested that we could probably use some more drinks.</p>\r\n<p>We ended up at this pretty cool little pub, who\\\'s name I do not know, I had been to once on Valentine\\\'s Day last year.  She said her dad would kill her if he knew where she was drinking because there was a picture of the Queen Mum on the wall.  One pint of Kilkenny and one of Tetley\\\'s, $6.50 each, both leaving $8 for our drinks.  Conversation of drugs, death, adventure, and music.  A sad man had come in and was trying to make small talk with the bartender who was trying hard to ignore him.  Not long after one of those rose peddler\\\'s came inside the front door.  Every time I see them inside a bar/restaurant I often wonder aloud/silently whether what they do is even legal.  We said \\\"no thanks\\\" and he went over to the sad man to talk with him.  We went back to whatever it was we were talking about when the rose man came by and placed a yellow rose on our table and said it was from the man at the bar.  She looked over at him and thanked him for the rose.  He had also bought four roses for the table of women behind him.  He told us that he bought the roses from the salesman because \\\"he looked sad.\\\"  I think maybe he was looking into the mirror behind the bar.  I picked up the clear plastic-shrouded flower and smelled it.  I told her it didn\\\'t even smell and she said that roses were losing their smell because of the cross-pollination and all that.  Genetics and n-generation dubs of audio cassettes.</p>\r\n<p>After we left, we continued walking and talking.  After we saw two homeless people accost city workers performing some late night road work she suggested to me that she should give her flower to someone.  I suggested she give it to me after which I said: \\\"You don\\\'t bring me flowers no more!\\\"  She thought it was a good idea.</p>\r\n<p>Now the flower is sitting on top of my dresser and just the outer petals are slightly beginning to wilt.  A brown tinting at the outer edge.  The flower that she gave to me last night.</p>\r\n'),(2728,'<p>what worries me is that you have become a constant in my life.</p>\r\n<p>i tried to let go of you.  breaking up with you was obviously not in your realm of possibilities.  you threatened to show up at my house the next day, claimed not to care whether i called the cops.</p>\r\n<p>i carried a useless two inch pocket knife for weeks after that, terrified into needing that false sense of security.  you never showed up, but the calls never stopped.</p>\r\n<p>i wonder sometimes what you told the operator was so important to convince her to break into my phone call.  as far as i know itâ€™s reserved for emergencies.</p>\r\n<p>i am rambling, arenâ€™t i?  away from tangents, back to my point.</p>\r\n<p>it has been ten months since i broke up with you.  the anger finally tapered off.  when you call, i donâ€™t bother to argue.</p>\r\n<p>your memory still echoes in my mind.  once i had to push away the boy i loved because a recollection of you made me afraid to let anyone touch me.  similar problems occurred later.</p>\r\n<p>my god, itâ€™s ridiculous.  what happened with you should have no effect on my current situations.  & yet it does . . . or doesn\\\'t.  to be honest, i don\\\'t know if it has finally faded.</p>\r\n<p>today i open my mailbox.  inside there is a letter from you.  i toss it into the garbage can without a second thought.  & this, this is normal.  this is every day.</p>'),(2729,'<p>I\\\'m cursed by a memory driven view<br>\r\nThat hooks and bites and pulls me through.<br>\r\nEmptied of fuel for this out-of-date pain<br>\r\nI sort through my baggage again and again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A clue must exist in some small pocket.<br>\r\nI trust there\\\'s a key that works to unlock it.<br>\r\nThere must be some things I fixedly know<br>\r\nWill adjust me from a perspective so low.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But knowledge is weak against such things.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s tainted by the sense it brings.<br>\r\nInsidious thoughts that I\\\'ve stored and sown <br>\r\nAre bittersweet weeds that have poisoned and grown.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If only I could sever threads<br>\r\nThat tether me to long past dread.<br>\r\nIf only this dark spotted flower<br>\r\nWould bloom with more exultant power.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Alas, to yield this smould\\\'ring heat<br>\r\nWould move me to a different seat.<br>\r\nDislodged from ancient bleary hall<br>\r\nI might not know myself at all!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As if imprisoned for a crime<br>\r\nMy darkness slowly passes time.<br>\r\nContent to live within a mime<br>\r\nMy secrets speak a silent rhyme.</p>\r\n'),(2730,'<p>He sits there, eyes downcast. Playing his guitar, singing those beautiful songs. The words pull on my innermost thoughts. Pulling me away from the present, into a world unknown. The words, each one as mesmerizing as the last, take me to places never before seen. Tricking me into a false sense of security, yet these insecurities vanish in his presence. His soothing voice has a calming effect.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The way the words trickle from his mouth, ever so smoothly. That melody, entrancing, takes me into a world of unknown. A world of imagination, a world of discovery. What the day will bring; inconceivable. Itâ€™s all new to me. I have no way of navigating. I ride it like a wave. No past experiences to guide me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>His eyes begin to rise. Meeting mine, no words can describe. The words go on, the music never stopping. His gaze, like fire, burns into me. I feel no pain, feeding from his words. Taking in what I once thought impossible. Breathing in a new kind of air. Air filled with a sense of hope. A feeling of belonging.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The words wind through my fibres. Tangling themselves towards my heart. Intertwined in my core, my being. I begin to feel like never before. His words go on. The guitar fills the room with sounds. Along come those magical words again. How he conceives them, a mystery. How I wish to say a word. A word with meaning, a word like his.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Those eyes burning deep. His smile unsurpassed by any other. I find myself fixated, unable to move. His soulful melodies allow me to drift slowly into a relaxed state of mind.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Breathe in, breathe out. I have to remind myself. My mind races a million thoughts, yet my heart only one. I meet his gaze once again. A smile plays on his lips. Playing that guitar so effortlessly. The words weave together. Iâ€™m transfixed.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Like sunshine on oceans, his eyes sparkle. Blinding me into oblivion. The scenery changes. No longer am I sitting in a crowded room. There is only him and I, looking at each other across the wide expanse. Still unable to move, I remain motionless. Willing myself to gain some kind of strength. The kind which allows movement. It escapes me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Still the songs go on. The words empowering my soul. My heart beating a thousand beats. Voids in my mind once black now filling with light. Minute details now magnified. My senses scream out to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>His words now engraved into my memory. The rhythm of his voice beating through my veins. Flowing from his soul, the words pour. The meaning of them known only to him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Slowly he rises from his chair. That smile making my stomach tie in knots. Tilting his head ever so slightly, he begins to walk towards me. I canâ€™t move. That feeling of butterflies rises. Slowly, the distance between us diminishes. His eyes sparkling ever so clear. His mouth sings, his hands play the guitar, yet I am surrounded by silence. My eyes begin to blur. I rub them frantically. When I open them he is fading. Fading into nothing. I call out to him. That smile never leaves his face. The words, the words are gone. I call out once more. Reaching out to him, all I feel is air. I scream.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Iâ€™m wrenched from the wave and pulled back into the present. No longer are my days filled with those words, those melodies, that smile. Taken away from me is that freedom. The unknown now a distant memory. As I open my eyes I come to the realisation, only in dreams.</p>\r\n'),(2731,'<p>Foggy beaches, buildings distant, driftwood, seaweed, saying nothing, leaning close, smelling  cinnamon? -- in your hair. I miss you.</p><p>The air here doesn\\\'t taste the same, not like smoke, not like food. All the buildings here are flat and simple. The people have round heads and move very slowly.</p><p>Nights at the bottom of Starry Gorge, the puddles and pools reflecting the sky, the sound of the city in the distance. So very dark down there, too dark to sleep.</p><p>It?s funny. Everything here is covered from the sky, these people fear the rain, they run like scared animals when it comes down. But they bathe obsessively in showers every day. Will I ever understand them?</p><p>On my way home to Wall dist., Auloon, sitting in an uncovered car, the rain whipping down, making fiery streaks in the florescent glow, the groundtrain screaming all the way. Much less comfortable; far more satisfying, far more alive. Maybe because I was returning to you?</p><p>The food here is bland. The people behave like children and don\\\'t speak my language. Sujwa\\\'ke de, keppu i\\\'ke. I miss you. Take me home.</p>\r\n<img src=\\\"./images/2731_1.jpg\\\">'),(2732,'<p>- the government gives me anything i want - booze, housing, food, but it doesn\\\'t give me the one thing i do want: love. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>- never love.  let someone love you, but never love them back.  if you love them, they will hurt you.  a woman will destroy you. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n-----\r\n\r\n<p>these are some of the words spoken to me by this man, about an hour ago.  i stopped in to a place for some pizza, and this man started to laugh at me.  he then told me he wasn\\\'t really laughing <i>at</i> me, just at my clothes.  he said i should be an officer and a gentleman, and should be wearing a suit.  i sat down to talk with him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he was nursing a bottle of beer that he was pouring into a styrofoam cup, sitting by that window. he was drunk, and slurring, but when he concentrated, he managed a rough eloquence.  he was basically a good man, but was now broken.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he kept commenting that i had green eyes.  he asked me if my teeth were my own (one isn\\\'t). he told me he was 72, and he urged me to live every moment of my youth.  i told him that i tried to.  he said, don\\\'t enjoy life, live life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and he told me about love.  he told me about people being afraid, and i agreed with him.</p>\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>- i have lost my dignity.  i haven\\\'t lost my pride, but i will lose it soon.</p>'),(2733,'<img src=\\\"http://inconduit.com/image/rand/neverlove.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"><br>\r\n<p>- the government gives me anything i want - booze, housing, food, but it doesn\\\'t give me the one thing i do want: love. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>- never love.  let someone love you, but never love them back.  if you love them, they will hurt you.  a woman will destroy you. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n-----\r\n\r\n<p>these are some of the words spoken to me by this man, about an hour ago.  i stopped in to a place for some pizza, and this man started to laugh at me.  he then told me he wasn\\\'t really laughing <i>at</i> me, just at my clothes.  he said i should be an officer and a gentleman, and should be wearing a suit.  i sat down to talk with him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he was nursing a bottle of beer that he was pouring into a styrofoam cup, sitting by that window. he was drunk, and slurring, but when he concentrated, he managed a rough eloquence.  he was basically a good man, but was now broken.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he kept commenting that i had green eyes.  he asked me if my teeth were my own (one isn\\\'t). he told me he was 72, and he urged me to live every moment of my youth.  i told him that i tried to.  he said, don\\\'t enjoy life, live life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and he told me about love.  he told me about people being afraid, and i agreed with him.</p>\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>- i have lost my dignity.  i haven\\\'t lost my pride, but i will lose it soon.</p>'),(2734,'<p>He opens the door for her, and she steps out into the drizzle. It still isn\\\'t quite rain. He walks out behind her, and she laces her hand through the crook of his elbow without resting her hand on his arm. She floats beside him, buoyant in the spit of the low clouds. He thinks she feels too soft, not her flesh, but her weight laced through his arm. He doesn\\\'t remember ever feeling anything so soft.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She stops at the second corner and giggles. Jerks her head to the right. She lives around the corner she tells him. He lets her turn, and follows her so he can look at her from behind. He watches her ass sway, starts to get turned on, and catches himself. He doesn\\\'t know what he\\\'s doing here. He\\\'s supposed to be on a date with someone else... He rubs his forehead, reminds himself that he was stood up. She didn\\\'t show. He keeps following as she unlocks the door of a run down second story flat and then he follows her up the stairs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nHe reaches for the light switch, and giggling, always giggling, she too quickly whispers, \\\"no!\\\" Then more calmly, \\\"don\\\'t turn on the lights.\\\" When his eyes adjust he admits the light reflected into the windows from the weather is cool and damp and perfect. She doesn\\\'t offer him anything to drink, or even a chair. She doesn\\\'t take his coat. He looks her in the eye and she pulls him into the bedroom without touching him. She pulls him with her eyelashes, with her lips, with the webbing of her fingers, from across the room.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He doesn\\\'t stop to think about his date now. He makes one quick mental note to blame the whiskey and steps toward her. For once she doesn\\\'t giggle. And as he touches her, her moans remind him of her crouch in front of the bar, of the old woman on the bus. The line from the Rodgers and Hammerstein song dances through his head. The last thing he remembers thinking is that the softness is going to kill him.</p>\r\n'),(2735,'you are in love. you are still in love, and it hurts.</p><p>\r\nshe doesn\\\'t love you anymore. you hope she does, but she doesn\\\'t. you know she doesn\\\'t - she told you - but you can\\\'t help hoping. anyway you need it too much. <i>well, she could change her mind, no?</i> you feel so down, so weak, and dirty. nothing good will happen to you if it\\\'s not her.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand you keep waiting for her. waiting for a phone call. you think about her all the time. she must think about you sometimes; she could decide to call at this moment. she wouldn\\\'t leave a message on the answering machine, though. she wouldn\\\'t do that. that would mean <i>\\\"call me back\\\"</i> and she told you not to call anymore. she\\\'s too proud for that. too fucking proud for anything like that. but she might call, yes. one time, maybe. you have to be at home at that moment, that\\\'s all.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou disable the answering machine. if this damn thing picks up before you she will hang up. \r\nwhen you sleep, you plug the phone in your bedroom. you\\\'re more and more sleeping during the day. at night you never feel like going to bed anyway. you waste your time, you watch the stupid tv. it gets a bit better at night, thanks god. then you read in bed. there is this pile of books close to your bed. all these books you wanted to read and you had no time. you were spending your time with her, never reading, and specially not in bed. in bed you were...<i>no, don\\\'t go there.</i> there is this pile of books close to your bed, and you thought yesterday that it would take you a while to read them all, even if you spend all your nights reading. and probably, when you\\\'re finished reading the pile, you will feel better about her. probably. or at least you will have read a few good books. yeah, you\\\'ll feel better for sure. one step at a time. don\\\'t think about the step, just walk. your body knows. read forward, finish the book. start another one. when the morning comes you don\\\'t see the sun light because you closed the shutters completely. you can hear the birds outside, then the cars of the first commuters. then you switch off the light and hide yourself in an empty and dark sleep.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand you <i>are</i> feeling better. it\\\'s a surprise. you wake up in the middle of the afternoon and feel like going outside. there is this stinking cheese you forgot in the fridge and it\\\'s now begging you to eat it. you\\\'re gonna walk outside and buy some nice warm bread to put it on. no time for shower, the bakery would be close. and you don\\\'t feel that dirty anymore. and you didn\\\'t think about her since you woke up... <i>good, but let\\\'s continue like this, focus on the bread, walk.</i> you go out. there is so much light outside. it\\\'s even warm. and the girl working at the bakery is smiling. she\\\'s kind of pretty; who cares, she looks nice.\r\n</p><p>\r\non the way back you think about calling a friend to hang out. or maybe a movie. you don\\\'t even know what\\\'s playing these days. last time you went to a movie... <i>don\\\'t go there.</i> you enter the hall of the building and climb the stairs. somewhere a phone is ringing. oh oh, isn\\\'t it yours? at the top of the stairs it\\\'s clear, it comes from behind your door. <i>it might be...</i> you hurry to get your keys and unlock. <i>her!</i> you leave the door open to rush to your bedroom. and the ring stops while you get there. you look at the phone now silent. you look at it a few minutes. <i>call her back, right now.</i> but you don\\\'t even know who it was. <i>you can\\\'t call her to ask if she called, you stupid!</i> you are angry now. angry about her for having called - or for not having called, just the same. but specially angry about yourself. <i>this damn phone...</i> you unplug the phone, put it in the closet, take your pocket knife, the bread, the cheese, and a jacket.<br>\r\nat the corner of the street, you call your friend from a public phone.'),(2736,'<p>\r\nExtascy, the fluid lights flickering<br>\r\nWith time frozen between the music beats<br>\r\nBeauty, in the form of a coy female<br>\r\nSo close to touching I can feel her heat<br>\r\nGracefully, she coils herself around a pole<br>\r\nAnd I smile to let her know I enjoyed it<br>\r\nTenaciously, I compliment her on her skill<br>\r\nAnd our eyes meet, hers set in cold blue stone<br>\r\nInnocently, I am simply enraptured<br>\r\nWho knew such a creature existed?<br>\r\nEffortlessly, she becomes all I see<br>\r\nAnd when the moment fades I awake<br>\r\nDelicately, but purposefully she glides away<br>\r\nAnd as the world returns to me I sigh.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2737,'<p>\r\nive been on a bit of a streak since i arrived in korea. Ive had the misfortune of being involved in a few mishaps. i didn\\\'t intend for all of these things to break, but they did. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\nshanes cell\r\n</b>\r\n<br>\r\nwe were wrestling on the roof of the school. i kept trying to put shane in a head lock but his slimy head kept me from getting a good lock. we were close to the door...it was raining outside, and in such a small place we kept bashing each other into the door frames and walls which had rude notes written with pieces of gipsum. a few minutes after my last class of the night started shane knocked on my door and came in to show me what had happened to his phone. the lcd screen had what looked like an ink blot trapped inside. in the constant pushing, pulling, and bashing his phone got caught in a most terrible crossfire. \r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\ntina\\\'s tooth\r\n</b>\r\n<br>\r\nit was when we were all heading home from a hiphop club called \\\"harlem\\\", in sinchon. tina was pinching my arms so i got her in a bear hug and we fell over. rolling on the sidewalk, she went over the curb and into the street. she was rolling in sewage, so i scampered up and away. when she got up after laughing for a few moments she said \\\"i think my tooth is chipped\\\". although I dont think i was to blame for this mishap, blame was laid on me, so im sucking it up. add it to the list i say. (as a side note, i lost my studded punk bracelet during that rumble)\r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\nshanes \\\"hammer\\\" lighter\r\n</b>\r\n<br>\r\nwe were shopping in myong dong checking out the shops, stalls, and literally shoulder to shoulder crowds. it was a busy place. in a relatively calm and open section i like to call the oasis, we found a dude selling lighters. all sorts of lighters. gun lighters, naked ladies, soccer balls, all sorts of unusual objects with only one feature; shooting fire. shane found a hammer shaped lighter. i actually used it to get a nail out of my wall at the apartment. one night after work, me, tina, and shane went for a beer at a local hoff. we got the beer, but no bottle opener. shane tried to use the hammer to do it and failed, so i thought id give it a try. while trying to pry the bottle open the lighter snapped in two. the only good thing that came out of that situation was the fact that shane now had a nifty little wire to electricute me with. that broke soon after but was not my fault im happy to report. \r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>silvia\\\'s camera</b>\r\n<br>\r\nall i know is that it worked before my cursed hands touched it. there isnt really a story to go with this one. i think its working again, having not been in my hands for a few hours allows for my presence to escape; relinquishing its capability of breakage.\r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>silvia\\\'s rucksack</b>\r\n<br>\r\nit was my turn to carry the bag. god it was heavy. it was filled with two new t-shirts, a national geographic, the story of beneton(the clothes dude) in korean, the broken camera, three u.s army k-rations(thai chicken, beef gravy and potatoes, turkey) *commercial resale unlawful*, a hoodie, porn mag, the source hip hop annual, and tomb raider dvd. i had repacked it to try and get everything to fit and when i had zipped it up, the zipper came undone. shane fixed the zipper. the bag was fixed for about two hours, and then when leaving a bar in hay wah shane noticed that it was ripped. i got blamed because of my previous breaking record, but i do not accept this accusation.\r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\na car mirror\r\n</b>\r\n<br>\r\nso we were coming out from a club in hong day and two girls had followed us out. i tried to get shane to get into a cab i had flagged, but he insisted that we go find some place to eat breakfast with the girls he had met in a club called G1. conceding defeat i followed them down the street. in korea they have these nifty mirrors on the backs of cars so that they can see how far or close the curb, other cars, objects are from the bumper. they are a cool idea i think they should implement in n.america. while walking down the street in my drunken state i decided to inspect one of the mirrors. the mirrors have hinges and so i thought id see what kind of radius the mirror had. as soon as i touched the mirror(without even inspecting radius) it fell off the car, hitting the pavement with a thud. we continued to walk and had a posse follow us. we turned left down a street, the posse going past us about ten paces and into a police station. the jig was up. entering the police station i got kind of nervous, but kept my cool nonetheless. it was a good thing one of the girls we were with spoke a bit of english. she helped us get the situation in check. the first four who had come into the station were the owners of the food tent the car was parked infront of. the second two were patrons who had been eating. they jibber jabbered with the police officer for about half an hour; the mirror being waved all around. it felt like show and tell. the mirror was passed from person to person to dispay the damage. it was obvious to me and thankfully the police officer on duty that the mirror had been broken to begin with. the four left and we were left there waiting for the owner of the car, who i think was finishing up his meal. about an hour later a well dressed man came in and began talking with the two girls, the police officer and shane. i was outside sitting on the steps drinking water. nausia was beginning to take hold of me. I just wanted the fucker to tell me how much i owed him. shane was also getting a bit frazzled and started saying \\\"ok have your lawyer call our lawyer\\\". haha. i was laughing so hard inside. i had given the police officer my name, but told him i had no phone and didnt know the address of my school(which was all true). I had no money on me, and finally the dude who owned the car ended up only wanting 40,000 won(40bucks). he was an honest and calm person. he wasnt trying to embelish the story or make things worse than they were. nevertheless, we finally got out of there a couple of hours after the mirror hit the ground and got a cab and went home. what a fucking night that was. haha. \r\n</br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim sure ive forgotten other things ive broken, but these are the main ones. i just hope my streak comes to an end. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce again, this is chiamattt reporting from seoul s.korea. \r\n</p>'),(2738,'<b>A Personality Snapshot In Time</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nAdjusting to self change can sometimes be more challenging then you initially anticipated. Especially, when you are confronted by something that reminds you of the old self. The difficulty of the adjustment can sometimes be in dealing with a complete lack of self doubt or self assuredness that comes with moving stages in your life and self. You are self assured in your position but get engage in dialogue by an opposing view. Suddenly, your self assuredness is pegged by others as insensitivity, closemindedness, or sometimes naivete.<br><br>\r\nThe Goat has been finding himself in this position more and more so these days. Thus, being crowned with the social persona of a slightly charismatic asshole - by some of those closer to me. Its almost like there is a level of guilt related to feeling assured when so many people these days struggle with rampant self doubt.<br><br>\r\nI am not saying that one can\\\'t be critical of oneself, but...<br><br>\r\nIt seems to me that if one has certain issues that they are hardline about and don\\\'t want to compromise for simple social niceties then they are an asshole or other previously mentioned labels. Just because I am unwilling to compromise myself does not mean that I demand everyone hold the same position that I do. I have major conflicts over issues with most of my closest loved ones, doesn\\\'t mean there is any less love in those relationships.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m accused of being oppresive, aggresive, and ignorant due to my unwillingness to compromise. The thing is that I don\\\'t want to get along at <i>any price</i>, there are somethings I don\\\'t feel like anyone or myself should have to tolerate. I don\\\'t think getting along at any price is a desirable notion. Where do people draw the line? Do people still draw the line? If they draw the line, how many times is it redrawn to avoid contensious issues?<br><br>\r\nWhat really seems to bother me the most though is being confronted by the likes of an older self, then realizing that you would have been just as condemning of your current self\\\'s assuredness.  \r\n'),(2739,'<center>\r\n<table width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"100%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td width=\\\"100%\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" colspan=\\\"3\\\">\r\n<p><SPAN STYLE=\\\"font-family: arial; font-size: 10pt;\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2739_4.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" border=\\\"2\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2739_7.gif\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" width=\\\"4\\\">my teeth are rattling inside my head. that\\\'s all i can feel. it\\\'s hard to breathe, and there is no light. i am pressed backward into my metal seat, we push through gravity, violently, upward. i could only collect enough money to put myself on a budget ship. i had heard of how Â Â Â  terrible they were, but i did not expect this. it seems to be one giant mass of metal, chairs and all. it shakes me unconscious during the take-off, before we even pierce the atmosphere.\r\n\r\n<p>i wake up, with my head against the porthole. at least i got a window seat. the ship\\\'s engine resonates deeply, making conversation with the strangers seated around me impossible. i feel it in my sinuses more than i do my ears. it\\\'s dark, no lights on the budget flights.\r\n\r\n<p>i decide to keep my eyes closed. for the time being, i can control reality by not letting it in. i contemplate leaving my planet. i never really left my city, these days not many people did. it\\\'s not like it was the apocalypse, but i didn\\\'t see any solution to the overpopulation, other than leaving the planet, so i took up the discounted rates that were partially subsidized by my government.\r\n\r\n<p>i left my family. was i close to them? yes. but they wanted me to go, to have a chance at something better, in their words. so i went. i start to think about their faces behind my closed eyes, just before i left for the loading dock. i wonder if i\\\'ll ever see them again - they know the settlement location i\\\'m headed for, and i will stay there for five years.\r\n\r\n<p>i think about the awful fight i had with my girlfriend when i told her that i was leaving. but i knew that i had to go.\r\n\r\n<p>i think about grass, and going to my grandpa\\\'s house as a child, laying in the \\\'grass room\\\'. it was a makeshift greenhouse, and he used it just to grow a small indoor lawn. it was the only grass i have ever felt. i was the envy of my friends.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2739_5.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" align=\\\"right\\\" border=\\\"2\\\">i think about the earth, my earth, growing smaller behind me. i want to see it, if i can, out of my porthole, which is relatively big. i open my eyes, look to my right inside the ship, and it\\\'s too dark to make out anything beyond mirrored silhouettes, people either sitting rigidly upright like me, or crumpled, still unconscious, like i was. i turn to my left to look out of the porthole, and i have no idea if we\\\'re travelling in any distinctive direction. we rumble with velocity, but nothing seems to move, inside or out. my eyes have to adjust to the utter darkness outside, littered with stationary punctures of light. as my eyes adjust, i feel a sense of dread from a depth i\\\'ve never before felt.\r\n\r\n<p>when i look out of the porthole, what i see freezes me. i am locked in wonder and sorrow. i can\\\'t distinguish individual stars because there are so many, they all just burn phantom patterns into my retinas, and i look ever deeper. i don\\\'t know what this means. i have nothing else in any experience that i can use as a comparison to try to understand what it is i\\\'m seeing. i don\\\'t know what this means. i am not filled with joy at the beauty of the stars; they are not beautiful. the blackness between the stars is impossible, it feels sinister, it pulls heavily on my eyes.\r\n\r\n<p>it is cold. the metal of the ship is clutching in the cold from outside, from space. i shake from the ship\\\'s resonance, and i shake from the cold. it feels like even my bones are cold, almost wet beneath my skin. how can i survive this for fifteen days? i blink once and it makes me realize i haven\\\'t blinked since i started looking out of the porthole. how long has it been? i am amongst nothing. there is nothing here that will support emotion. i feel that i am becoming part of the ship. turbulence hits, i bite my tongue, it bleeds, and i do not break my gaze through the porthole. i taste not my blood in my mouth but the blood of the ship, metallic, cold, sharply vital. i feel my bones seize into steel bars, extensions of my seat.\r\n\r\n<p>i cannot recall names or faces of friends, and i haven\\\'t blinked again. i\\\'m lost, and i cannot remember my name.\r\n\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2739_6.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"2\\\">\r\n</span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</center>'),(2740,'<p>i was a little depressed, driving in my car the other night, so i popped in a mixed tape one of my best friends in the world made for me recently. (he won me over when, only a few weeks after we met, he brought me a mixed tape. then he kept bringing them, with titles like \\\"the complaint mix\\\" and \\\"the life is getting better mix\\\"). this is what came on:</p>\r\n* le tigre, \\\"T.G.I.F.\\\" â€“ i sing as loud as i can along to this part: \\\"youâ€™re beautiful! and your boss is an asshole! and I donâ€™t give a shit what that dick thinks ... we will survive ...\\\"<br>\r\n* the strokes, \\\"someday\\\" â€“ you just canâ€™t help but bounce<br>\r\n* new pornographers, \\\"letter from an occupant\\\" â€“ i can\\\'t figure out any of the words to this song, but I scream it at the top of my lungs anyway<br>\r\n* magnetic fields, \\\"100,000 fireflies\\\" â€“ \\\"they remind me of your starry eyes...\\\"<br>\r\n* daft punk, \\\"digital love\\\" â€“ pure electronic happiness<br>\r\n<p>i rewound it to the beginning and listened again three times. i thought of matt, my friend who made the tape and who is now traveling through europe with a boy he loves, experiencing so much happiness. and of the time i saw the new pornographers play. they were such adorable nerds. and of my brother, who i danced with to the strokes at a concert in the desert. and of my old boss, who terrorized our office and made our lives so miserable until we finally staged a revolt and got him demoted, ha ha. and of this cute boy i like. i wish i could catch 100,000 fireflies for him.</p>\r\n<p>and i felt better.</p>\r\n'),(2741,'<P> So I havent written here in quite a while. ( cheering in background) yes, yes...well thank-you, love you too. What I have to bitch about today is the password one needs in order to talk shit about someone else\'s article? Ah, now I know no one will tell me. Oh well, beaucoup sadness for me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I also want to say that I am sick of flags on cars...get over it. You dont see palestinians sporting their flag EVERYWHERE they go. Shit. America is such a coddled country.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Now if only the made personality tests which told you which position you prefferred to fuck in...hmmmm.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And now...I much chew my lip and worry about grad school.( oh, have fun deleting this. SINCE ITS NOT GETTING POSTED...effing Switlerites!)</P>'),(2742,'have you ever gotten the feeling that you might be sick soon? like you may possibly throw up within the next few hours. that ever present feeling that no matter what you do to stop it, its unavoidable. the slow dread falls into place within your mind. the fact that you haven\\\'t been sick for over three years now and the record is about to be broken makes the feeling even worse. that taste associated with throwing up is a distant and long-gone memory. one which you don\\\'t want to re-visit in the near future. maybe the taste isn\\\'t so bad? is that even a possibility? probably not....hold on....'),(3034,'<p>thoughts and patterns of conduct have the most odd ways of repeating themselves.  around 4 in the morning i found out just how odd when the <b>exact</b> feelings i had months ago returned to plague me again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2002-02-08 / 12:49 p.m.</p>  \r\n   \r\n<p> it\\\'s been just over a month since i had any communication with my father.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve just spent the last few minutes staring at that sentence. my father lives 10 minutes away in a 3rd floor apartment in a large community full of big, bland, stucco buildings and i have not spoken to him in over a month. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i took a few minutes this morning to look at myself in the mirror. not for vanity\\\'s sake but rather to just review my facial features. i wondered if i looked like a 29 year old white male should look. i thought about what people see when they look at me and more importantly what i saw in me when i looked. have i grown into the face that stared blankly back at me? i studied my eyes for a bit, curious about what signals they were giving off to others. am i able to mask the quiet desperation my eyes show or is everybody on to my secret? my eyes looked tired, the skin around them appeared darker than i remember it being and i noticed small pillows of puffy skin hanging below them. i noticed a few wrinkles, i  saw that my flesh is slowly looking worn. i looked at the 7 days worth of growth and the lines in my beard, where it stopped and where it started. sparse in some spots, thick in others. hair with colors of light brown, red and black all over my jawline, chin and neck. a quick flash of junior high school memories cut through me recalling when the facial hair started and how i was thrilled with the fact that i appeared ahead of the others in the race of physical age. i checked the time and thought about spending the 10 minutes it would have taken me to trim it up a bit but decided to stare a bit longer. i reviewed my hairline for a second and in doing so caused a sudden change in perspective.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>it was at this age my fathers hair began to go silver, a few more years and his thick hair started to thin. i looked at my hair again, no silver and still as thick as it ever was. i scoured my features yet again only this time they seemed to change a bit. staring back at me now was stark evidence of my father and a bit of his father as well. i saw them both inside me and outside of me in ways that i had never seen before. the face staring back at me was now my fathers and at this age he was 3 years away from the divorce, life was starting to close in on he and my mom. i looked back and tried to see my grandfather, i wondered what he was like at this age and how he handled things. did he face desperation & confusion like me around this time in his life and in my fathers life before me? what did he say to his son as he faced the pain and anger that was entering his life? what does a father say to his son when they realize they can\\\'t help in ways they used to? was my grandfather happy with the decisions he made at this point? was he quiet and reserved, content with listening to the radio while watching sports with the volume turned off on television like i remembered him? or was he soft-spoken and reserved because of silent direction he took early on and everytime we saw each other he waged quiet war inside like i do? i wish i could ask him. i wish i saw more of him than i do my father when i look in the mirror. <i>my questions about my grandfather are better than <u>the answers i have about his only son. </u></i></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>over the holidays somebody made random mention of my depression and how they knew it ran in the \\\'whyte men\\\'. i don\\\'t know who said it or in what context but i remember hearing it second hand. \\\'ran in the whyte men\\\'. \\\'it ran in...\\\' as if to say \\\'it\\\' was like blood coursing through my veins and like the blood in my veins it was never to leave me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i love my father, i fear i will become my father. i\\\'ll never be rid of this.</p>'),(2743,'<p>\r\nwalking in downtown seoul is like walking in a rave. the people are shoulder to shoulder and getting pushed is a way of life. you just cant get mad or annoyed by it. people gotta get through to where they gotta get to. its the same on the subway. constant crowds, constant pressure, constant activity. i cant seem to figure out how people can sleep standing up and get nudged, but ive seen it. its not like everone sleeps standing up, but when i think of it i think of horses. ugh, horses. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is a thick smog in this city. im sure it doesnt compare to the smog people see in larger cities in india, or iran, but the smog is there and hangs like semi-transparent kleenex over the sky, diluting the sun during the day and the moon and stars at night. i dont think ive seen a star yet in seoul. ive looked, but the only lights in the sky i see flash. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe walk up the hill towards my house reminds me so much of telegraph hill in san fran. while i walk up and down the hill everyday, and have only walked up and down telegraph hill once, the comparisons are clear; it sucks. perhaps the walk wouldnt be so bad if after ever turn; every new house, a new definition of rank didnt waft into my nostrils. shit, fish, and garbage. the smell is everywhere in the neighbourhood i live in and while im sort of used to it, it fills me with visions of huge dumps and rotting flesh.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nive been reading a lot of news lately and well things look grim on the diplomatic front through and through. the middle east is anything but stable, even in countries known for stability. iraq is worse off now that it ever has sending saddam into a religious revival like never seen before. secular nationalism is almost gone; new mosques and religious papers being allowed day in and day out. its the only thing people there can use to cope. the word of god. poverty drives people towards religion and in an area of the world so poor and devastated i find it so hard to even listen to the europeans and americans speak of anything positive with regards to the situation at this point in time. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen will the majority cast aside their undying belief in past philosophies regarding the state and the individual? when will the majority think of \\\"good\\\" in terms of \\\"the whole\\\" and less on personal gain and personal stability at \\\"home\\\"? why didnt september eleventh strike a chord of change instead of a chord of defence? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe big stick doesnt always win, but what about the big tv?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni want to goto the rishi valley in india one day and revel in its revolutionary ideas and its positive outlook on problems. they dont look at problems as impossible. they look at them as challenges the whole community has a say in changing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noptimists of the world unite.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nidealists of the world unite.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut to unite isnt the whole answer. the answer is reasonable and realistic means of change and promise. not election campaign promises, but hardcore ideas that strike a nerve and create change and action. the kind of action the rishi valley is producing. not dividends. not efficiency. just positive change. realistic ideas create realistic endeavours, from which people can look at and smile. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsmile!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto quote billy brag. \\\'just because youre better than me, doesnt mean im lazy. just because your moving forward doesnt mean im moving backwards\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hate the stock market because its a purely individualist endevour with only one end result; the aquisition of funds. im all for individual art, individual expression and individual activity, but when that activity is for the pure gain of making more money i feel hurt and exploited. when the pentions of so many are hurt by the actions of so few, the idea of stocks become so barbarian to me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nstocks are barbarian\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncant sleep as of late. been killing the time reading fiction and books about red flags and corrupt regimes. so much is corrupt in this world and we cant even begin to make changes in places so in need of them when at home we sweep them under the rug like they were peices of confetti or dust. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlets just blame it all on lack of attention span. nintendo generation. coffee. bling bling. i blame myself. life is marginalized and divided at a span i cant even begin to understand. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits 2002 and we still arent fucking driving electic cars. who is to blame. fucking capitalism. fucking communism. fucking arms race. fucking oil tycoons who avoid change because change means new and costly investment. investment in the future is what i hear from all the oil companies, but all they tout is a \\\"cleaner\\\", \\\"safer\\\" product. why is it when i call up an oil company they have no answers with regards to my questions pertaining to investments in renewable resources, or investment in electic energy. why is it i read about the canadian government cutting its funding for wind energy but then handing over millions to help harvest oil in the tar sands of alberta. when will this end? when will i actually have a say in this \\\'democracy\\\'.\r\n</p> '),(2744,'asdfasdfasdf\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2744_1.jpeg\\\">'),(2745,'<p>Life really exists between the events that make up the important junctures of any person\\\'s timeline. The pleasure in living, which would, if anything, be the whole point to life, is in the \\\"going\\\" and not in the \\\"getting there\\\". When something really important does happen we end up merely going through the motions, following the plans we\\\'ve previously laid out and reacting only on instinct, according to plan. You say to yourself, \\\"I have decided to do (A) and not (B)\\\". Performing this action is akin to making a body part move. When you eat, your mind decides to eat and your brain acts on instinct telling your body what various actions to perform to accomplish the objective. Your hand feeds your mouth, which chews your food, etc.. When a decision is made on a greater scale, say the infamous \\\"life decisions\\\"; those that will segregate time between before and after that moment permanently in your memory, you act much the same. Time is spent analyzing and considering, then planning and finally acting. But the action itself takes little time in comparison and the rest of the time, while you\\\'re \\\"in limbo\\\", so to speak, you\\\'re actually living life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Much of the time I feel like my entire life revolves around getting from point A to point B successfully without fucking up too much else of the universe in the process. This would at first consideration seem a relatively simple task, however, my track record indicates that I have some abnormal difficulty accomplishing seeming simple tasks. This could be simply, \\\"the way we all feel\\\", or it could be that I usually end up doing things very differently from the status quo. Either way, I have accepted that I have to steel myself rather uncompromisingly towards accomplishing whatever goal I have made for myself. However, most of the time spent in between these actions is fairly bland, except for the nagging feeling I have that I should be working harder towards my goals.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Fortunately, I do stop and enjoy myself often enough that my views towards the meaning of life are reinforced. I was a very different person a few years ago, obsessed with merely accomplishing, not paying much attention to what it was like to get there. A few tumultuous years have left me with some new respect for how the grass looks on my side of the fence. Wherever I go, life will continue relentlessly to age me, teach me and tire me; if I never bothered to waste a little time along the way what would be the point? Life is over with in a damn hurry anyway, no reason to race to the finish line. It\\\'s not like there is a prize for finishing first.</p>'),(2746,'<P>\r\nI ate fried ice cream and Thai with Hayan on Austin today.  I told her how Austin Street always reminds me of Andrea Goosick.  Everyone says Goosick but ammu pronounces it \\\'Gaausikh\\\'.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nI?m sick of being affected.\r\nIt looks as if every aspect of my thinking, my behavior, my being here is modeled after a white standard.  &if its not, then its the result of my trying to escape that white standard.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nEspecially in this goddamn school. I\\\'m choking.<BR>\r\nI\\\'ve never before felt \\\'brown\\\'.  Brown.  Brrr-own, roll the r so it sounds even more foreign.  <BR>\r\nEh-  Who started this anyway?  Yellow - brown - white - black. <BR>\r\nor the variations: Chocolate, Mocha, Caramel.                 [Egg?]\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIts because I was raised American.  Even if I am, on paper, I?ve probably never been considered American.  After all, I\\\'m not white.\r\n<BR> <BR>\r\na-What are you? <BR>\r\nb-I\\\'m American. <BR>\r\na-I know, but where are you from?<BR>\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIt\\\'s this generation that experiences this racial identity crisis most keenly.  I\\\'ve grown up here.  I don\\\'t remember \\\"home\\\".  So what am I?\r\nThere is no such thing as gray matter.  There is no room for individuality.  You are first an Asian, then an individual.  You are first Spanish, then an individual.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIts as if you <U>have</U> to assume a race, its ingrained into your identity.  Mainstream, underground, who the hell cares, in America, you are your skin.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nAt times, I reconsider everything.  I think its just me, setting myself up with this psychological filter.  Like walking around with a racial brain tumor..\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIts not that being brown is a new discovery.  Its finding out the connotations that come with being brown.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI\\\'ve spent 10 years of my life in an INTERNATIONAL School.  There were 4 pure Europeans in our class and everyone else was everyone else.  I was still the only South Asian (I\\\'m from Bangladesh) but I never noticed... </P><P>\r\nAs children, we were living idealistically, where my skin even though 4 shades darker than yours, didn?t matter.  Difficult pronunciation of names and mother?s who had thick accents were just names and mothers.  It was nearly race-less.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nChildhood must be the only period where purity like this can exist\r\n</P>'),(2747,'<p>ultra happy, ecstatic, spinning around staring up at the stars. feeling cool air on hot cheeks & becoming invincible. untouchable, wanting to be untouchable. its not about you anymore, she\\\'s not really sure it was in the first place. for once its about her, its about what she needs and what she wants and what she is going to find. </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>& this is the way her mind works - be as honest as possible, then run away. its a gut reaction, its deeper than instinctual. where did she learn these behavioural patterns? this is always the way it happens, but what happens when she\\\'s honest with herself and then she needs to run? is that what is happening now? </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>the plug was pulled, the water\\\'s all run out, all she wants to do is smoke & sleep & walk the streets of this city, makeup that she doesn\\\'t wear smeared all over her skin like it was that morning. she\\\'ll stand on hopetoun bridge at dawn & wonder how it could be that she\\\'s able to embrace another day when she\\\'s still stuck in one many many ago.</p>\r\n'),(2748,'<p>Constantly slipping into a thought that the lives of those I see in the street, and around me in my mundane days, are somehow better and richer than mine, I only ever come around to a most distressing conclusion. I am grappling with quite a large and ungainly parcel of uncertainty, caused mostly by my senses of fear and dread for my own future, my own world which is yet to be carved.</p>\r\n<p>Which material should I carve it from? This is my stumbling block - indecision, breaker of all my plans, seer of nothing to fruition.</p>\r\n<p> I am slowly learning, sometimes too painfully and accompanied by terrible thoughts, that I can just be sad, let that part of me be and still all the while start to carve - there\\\'s no need for me to feel ashamed of my sadness. It\\\'s a beautiful part of me that constantly teaches me what my happiness looks and feels like.</p>\r\n<p>If I can remind myself constantly of this truth, then I am sure that great times may follow.</p>\r\n<p>I have become more tolerant of those who fight this seemingly well-meant demon with a god or another obsession.</p>\r\n<p>My life is craving an obsession, something that develops and nurtures me so that whatever occurs, there, always mine, it will always be (if not it then the next). Yes, perhaps the answer to one obsession is another, another and then another that serves me better, until an entire family of them appears at the foot of my bed each day to comfort me as I awake.</p>\r\n<p>I know the tools are here already, waiting for me to pick them up.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2749,'walking outside tonight.  a bit warm.  a bit breezy.  but certainly one of the nicest days this spring.  there\\\'s a long row of houses on my way.  i watched my shadow move as i passed under each street light.  i was trying to remember something and then realized that it never existed.  reminded me of the resistance of velco shoes.  those kangaroo tennis shoes, purple with velco closures.  and how we always got yelled at by our teachers for repeatedly opening and closing them.  there was a guy sitting on his front porch trying to impress his friends by playing the guitar.  he only knew two cords and proceeded to clumsily jump between the two with what i imagine he thought was rhythm.  every once in a while one of the men would holler something out to a passerby.  keep your eyes straight ahead, look at the ground.  don\\\'t let them know you noticed.  \r\nlater on there were two women sitting on the edge of a retaining wall drinking cheap beers and loudly discussing the events of their dates this past weekend.  i wonder if anyone would like to hear me discuss my events.'),(2750,'<p>I have three kids. Evan is seven. He wants to be a doctor when he grows up. He studies tae kwon do, builds and programs robots in our living room, and when he is concentrating really hard, he bites his bottom lip and ducks his head just like I do. Zoe is almost 5. She wants to be a ballerina, an astronaut, or a firetruck depending on what day of the week it is. She likes to dress up in frilly dresses, has faded purple hair, and when you annoy her, she glares at you and growls deep in her throat. Zane is three. She wants to be a pandabear or a kitty cat. She knows this is possible because I am a pandabear. She likes to type into irc from my lap, knows how to tie fantastic knots, and when she wants something, I bribe her by making her say \\\"me too!\\\" which causes me to melt. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Each of them is unique and different, since they are indeed each a person, and people are unique, and they have learned different things, at different times, in different ways. Each of them are the same and alike, since, they are all related, and were raised in the same environment, with the same rules and regulations and input. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>People sometimes forget that children are people. You remember in one way, but you assume they are like retarded people. Or people-to-be. Larval people. That they don\\\'t have opinions of their own, and you can arbitrarily make choices without consulting with them.  People have a tendency to skip over talking to children, especially the very small children. What could they possibly have to tell you? You?! With all your worldly experience and observations? Who has seen and done so much? Who has been around for so much longer then they have, what could insights could they possible have to share with you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In reality, kids have so much to tell you. Things you have forgotten, like how grass smells just before it is cut, and what it feels like to have your hands cupped around a hopping cricket. Things they have observed, like how every time Mommie gets frustrated writing at her computer, she goes into the kitchen to fill a cup with ice and stand for a few moments staring at the window. chewing and thinking, and how sometimes, with your eyes closed, the wind feels like a thousand fairies kissing your face. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Kids have jokes and stories. Some of them you have heard before, told yourself, can still recite verbatim. Some of them are new, made up, the product of what these kids, now, think is funny or scary or weird. Kids have a lot to teach us, but the most important lesson they offer, is about being a kid. \\\"But\\\", you say \\\"I was a kid, I know all about it!\\\" But do you really? Do you <i>remember</i>? Do you still hold the feeling of running full speed down a hill with your arms outstretched, swooping and bounding like an airplane? Kids today are not the same as when you or I were kids. Today they learn their ABC\\\'s from a computer keyboard, not from pictures in a book, and they learn about sex from prime time teevee instead of whispered confidences in the back of the lunchroom. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>My kid\\\'s are smart, educated, can articulate clearly and speak intelligibly. I have taught them to respect other people\\\'s opinions, but not to be afraid to converse about their own. How do you expect kids to learn to communicate, if not by doing so with those who have already acquired such skills? Kids love talking and sharing and interacting with people. Even the shy ones can be coaxed out, if you have a bit of patience and show real interest. Kids can spot fakes pretty well most of the time. My friend\\\'s mother always said \\\"It\\\'s important to let kids think for themselves, because if you teach them not to, they turn into adults that don\\\'t think for themselves.\\\"  </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2751,'<p>Gaining the hill, cars whizzing by, heading for his house on the highway, setting of the summer sun, honeysuckle bursting curbside, the air thick almost tangibly thick with the liquid scent, sky a dull green now, a few smudges of cloud and there, first a gable then a porch, it\\\'s his home on the highway...</p>\r\n<p>Screendoor swings open, throws his pack on the couch, Melissa\\\'s in the kitchen, protopancakes sizzling on the range, cigarette in hand, flopflops on feet, little column of smoke drifting sleepy into florescent overhead light, outside the cars hissing by one after the other in a poor imitation of the ocean. \\\'lissa gives a little wave.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Dinner?\\\" he asks.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Something like that,\\\" she says, voice low and tired.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"I\\\'m going to wash up.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>His house has a smell like an old library or church - the smell of soggy books, pipe tobacco, tar, sea salt, varnish, dried flowers, it\\\'s one of the things he likes the most about this place. For him, this smell is almost like a hidden feature that the realtors failed to consider, just as valuable as a good piece of furniture or a dryer in the basement or a better view. \\\'lissa would disagree, but he honestly thinks that the smell makes up for the proximity to the highway. A smell like this means that every event and every resident left his mark in the form of a contribution to a single ineffable scent, the scent of house.</p>\r\n<p>He runs the hot water. He likes the steam that comes up.</p>\r\n<p>Introduced to her one hot summer day, 16 or so, smoking a joint behind a Dairy Queen, walking around town with cigarettes and a six pack of flying saucers, making fun of people\\\'s pets. Long nights on the phone, lying in bed with the window open, faint buzz of a radio on her end of the line, smell of clover in the air, whispering adolescent dreams half-consciously, wet shady streets, secret meetings in the park, eight years ago and 30 miles away.</p>\r\n<p>He washes his hands, looks at his nails.</p>\r\n<p>Things accumulate over time. Once everything was blank, once the dream of renting a house and going to school and drinking on a back porch of their own was enough, it\\\'s more than enough when you love someone and things are fresh. But every unspoken regret, every unresolved argument, every unvoiced and unidentifiable resentment, it all begins to gather and weigh down without you realizing it, until the conversations become silent and toxic, until sex becomes an anonymous ritual, until being together is as lively as pouring warm beer down the sink.</p>\r\n<p>In the kitchen the light is off, the outline of a single sad pancake on the plate, its permiter marked by the streetlamp and the glow of the oven\\\'s digital clock. The door to the back porch is open, honeysuckle swarming in and drowning out the smell of the house, and she\\\'s out there, he knows. She\\\'s out there staring at the fence, he could be out there too, if he knew what to say, if he thought she could hear, if he knew there was something more.</p>\r\n<p>But the day is over -- it\\\'s time for bed.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2751_1.jpg\\\">'),(2752,'<P>elementary school, i get in a sword fight with foil type swash buckling swords in the library.  the battle starts  becuase the other kid is tired of being picked on for being fat.  i  wasnt the one calling him fat.  or maybe i was.  either way he wants to kill me.  my fighting skills are better than his.  i tell him fighting is stupid, but he keeps waving his sword at me.  im scared he is going to wound me and then kill me when i cant fight back.  so i wound him.  he wont stop swinging his sword at me.  i wound him again.  he wont stop.  during the fighting our swords are broken.  again and again they are broken, like  nibbling on a piece of dry spaghetti.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni decide to go for the jugular.  end it now.  but i cant seem to get at the vein.  i poke and poke, the kid says, \\\"what are you trying to do?\\\" between acks when i miss and just poke him in the adams apple.  he doesnt know i am trying to stop him by ending his life, or maybe he does and is ready to go.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni spin around him and get him in a headlock, sort of.  more like grabbing him by the horns and riding him down to the ground like a steer.  then i cut his jugular and release him.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe artery in his throat spurts blood violently, collapses and dies.  i grab his throat to keep the blood from emptying all over the carpet.  i dont want to get in trouble for making a huge mess. and drag him into the bathroom.  i prop him up against the bathroom wall at the end of the row of sinks and next to the r2d2 type white cylinder trash can.  he is oozing blood onto the tiles.  blood is running for the drain.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni realize i just killed him and there will be repercussions for this.  im going to go to jail for the rest of my life.  it was self defense, yes, but the reality of the carnage is too gruesome to be thought of as such.  i have blood on my hands.   \r\n</P>'),(2753,'<p>No, I didnÂ´t smile today. My lips formed that haggard expression to each new face I confronted, but I wasnÂ´t smiling. I was walking backwards in my mind, attemping to piece together the broken road of childhood dreams and near adult failures.\r\n</p><p>\r\nNo, no, I wasnÂ´t depressed or looped up on over the counters, nor was I engorged in emotion food, just hunched over the railing of my sanity. Pretty view from up here...isnÂ´t it?\r\n</p><p>\r\nReality is a funny thing to tampter with, when the person at the stick doesnÂ´t understand the gears. Oh yes, there are rules, and you will earn points, but where is the goal? </p>\r\n<p>Leaning back into the shadow of what once, I was forced to analyse what is. What is today? The fourth month and more in a foreign country, something that passed away like a sleepless drunken stressed fortnight. Since when did people stop using that word anyways? </p>\r\n<p>\r\nDidnÂ´t wear my walking shoes for this little journey. I scampered lazily through dirty hallways in slippers with moons and stars, and shaved my legs in the hope of a male attracted to my scent. I put on my rings with the hope of smashing them into a wall. I thought, hey, fuck society, today I will eat like a pig and just throw up like those adolescents with disorders. I second thought that one, while the bile was already rising below my sternum. Bang on your chest, he said, it will help. Wives tales. </p>\r\n<p>ItÂ´s all wiveÂ´s tales. Mommy, will I grow up to be an artist like you? Sorry darling, what were you saying? Art? Oh yes, I remember. See, I made those pictures before I met daddy, now I donÂ´t have time. Time, mommy, whatÂ´s that? ThatÂ´s when you grow up, and everything you hoped for in life suddenly gets over taken by the monsters of the world, the people that want your money, the people that want to steal that little person inside of you... One day you wake up, and your hand canÂ´t draw anymore because youÂ´ve washed too many dishes and stroked too many trees at night.</p>\r\n<p> Origami is always nice. Gets you away from the world where folds can be quietly made, and creases bought at a price high enough to make them even out. TV? Nice theory. Make the masses come to your sublime little fairy tales and what they believe life should be. Funny, how they donÂ´t realize (or do they?)they dictate every crime before it happens, every sexual fantasy before itÂ´s fufilled, they even tell you how to eat. Subliminal messages anyone? Origami?</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNo, I didnÂ´t draw today and wrote nothing or importance. I played with time, and let it slip away again. I spoke my native tongue, because there was something that said it can wait. Procrastination, my best friend. <-- It never yells, it never fights back. I didnÂ´t end my celibacy, I thought, hey, maybe another month...or a year, why not? So why did I shave between my legs then? I curled my hair, though I stayed in my room all day. I thought about becoming a writer later in life, and then realized, this is life, this is already later, I put off years of not writing to do something else. </p>\r\n<p> Dear Parents, do you know what your kids do? Those nights, any day from Monday to Sunday especially, your kids are in cars looking for a dealer. For a friendly face with a pacifier around his or her neck, checking up on the latest price for that new Star Wars pill of X. They are in their closets at night, watering those secret green plants and searching the internet for the newest kitched drugs to kill those precious honor roll brain cells. Did you know your daughter is sleeping around...with people YOUR age? Did you know that your son had Penthouse under his matress? Were you suprised to find cigarette butts and little tupperwear containers of vomit next to the beauty pagent (school) photos? Really? Well, fucking wake up. </p>\r\n<p> Why donÂ´t you start teaching your kids and taking them away from the world? DonÂ´t push their academics and their looks, try character development, teach Ã©m some more morals and color blindness... do a little something to stop the degrading declination of every generation thus spawned and to come. Jesus, you look at our generation and say \\\"Oh God, what have they become?\\\" ItÂ´s not what we did, itÂ´s what you did.</p>'),(2754,'<A href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org\\\"><img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2754_1.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\nThis past Friday night, I was aimlessly walking around Lower Manhattan, as I often do.  There seems to be such an energy in the air on such evenings that simply getting out and mingling with it is irresistable.  At some point, I passed this makeshift shrine on Avenue A and 5th street; it seemed to be an anniversary shrine.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTime seemingly stood still as I briefly perused the items left on the worn cement dais near the north side of the intersection; items of clothing, favourite knick-knacks and plaques, worn, yellowing love-letters all postmarked from the 1970\\\'s. And the photos... they were scattered all about, but with some longing order.<br><br> \r\n\r\nI stooped to get a closer look at the photos. All were of the same couple, and what seemed to be their children at different stages of growth; wedding photos, faded images of family vacations and reunions, party shots and candid shots posed on the streets of this very neighborhood. Each was inscribed, on the back, with bitterly mournful passages, mostly concerning lost love, the unfairness of death, and the pangs of lonliness.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI quickly placed the photos back as I found them, discovering that I could barely read them without being overcome with painful empathies.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI slowly walked uptown, towards the train, wondering what it would be like to have a shrine made for me on Avenue A.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(2755,'i never smile. it is kind of strange to other people. the other day i was in a bar with this girl, who was drunk and ecstatic, dancing around. she was very pretty. she was smiling the whole time. she came over to me, and pushed the corners of my mouth up trying to make me smile, but it didn\\\'t work. her smile lost a little of its power, and she said \\\"you\\\'re just miserable, aren\\\'t you.\\\" so there goes the possibility of anything coming out of <i>that</i>.\r\n<p>\r\ni can\\\'t smile. the orthodontist told me when it began, when i was about 14, that it was because my jaw sticks out too far, and keeps the corners of my mouth from pointing upwards and forming a true smile. when i try to do it in the mirror, i get only a sort of grimace. this makes me self-conscious, obviously; i hate taking pictures with a deep and abiding passion. i can\\\'t fake happiness, because for me to look happy i have to be really almost overwhelmingly happy and laughing, and usually people see a smile then even when there isn\\\'t one. sometimes i can be very quietly happy, and people think i\\\'m upset about something. from inside it feels like they should see happiness--my face muscles are in &quot;happy formation&quot;, my eyes have that smiley feeling, but sometimes it\\\'s not enough. and if i\\\'m really not having a good time, forget it. so to show, for example, a girl that i like her takes unusual effort and requires a whole different set of rules for me than for the average person.\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m sure that there are much worse things that can be wrong with someone. i could be sick or hurt or dying of cancer. but it just doesn\\\'t seem fair. so...smile for me. look in the mirror and do it, and see how even the most homely face can be made beautiful by such a simple expression, how easy it is to win someone\\\'s trust and affection just by smiling like you mean it.\r\n'),(2756,'<PRE>\r\n                            .xm*f\\\"\\\"??T?@hc.\r\n                          z@\\\"` \\\'~((!!!!!!!?*m.\r\n                        z$$$K   ~~(/!!!!!!!!!Mh\r\n                      .f` \\\"#$k\\\'`~~\\\\!!!!!!!!!!!MMc\r\n                     :\\\"     f*! ~:~(!!!!!!!!!!XHMk\r\n                     f      \\\" %n:~(!!!!!!!!!!!HMMM.\r\n                    d          X~!~(!!!!!!!X!X!SMMR\r\n                    M :   x::  :~~!>!!!!!!MNWXMMM@R\r\n n                  E \\\' *  ueeeeiu(!!XUWWWWWXMRHMMM>                :.\r\n E%                 E  8 .$$$$$$$$K!!$$$$$$$$&M$RMM>               :\\\"5\r\nz  %                3  $ 4$$$$$$$$!~!*$$$$$$$$!$MM$               :\\\" `\r\nK   \\\":              ?> # \\\'#$$$$$#~!!!!TR$$$$$R?@MME              z   R\r\n?     %.             5     ^\\\"\\\"\\\"~~~:XW!!!!T?T!XSMMM~            :^    J\r\n \\\".    ^s             ?.       ~~d$X$NX!!!!!!M!MM             f     :~\r\n  \\\'+.    #L            *c:.    .~\\\"?!??!!!!!XX@M@~           z\\\"    .*\r\n    \\\'+     %L           #c`\\\"!+~~~!/!!!!!!@*TM8M           z\\\"    .~\r\n      \\\":    \\\'%.         \\\'C*X  .!~!~!!!!!X!!!@RF         .#     +\r\n        \\\":    ^%.        9-MX!X!!X~H!!M!N!X$MM        .#`    +\\\"\r\n          #:    \\\"n       \\\'L\\\'!~M~)H!M!XX!$!XMXF      .+`   .z\\\"\r\n            #:    \\\":      R *H$@@$H$*@$@$@$%M~     z`    +\\\"\r\n              %:   `*L    \\\'k\\\' M!~M~X!!$!@H!tF    z\\\"    z\\\"\r\n                *:   ^*L   \\\"k ~~~!~!!!!!M!X*   z*   .+\\\"\r\n                  \\\"s   ^*L  \\\'%:.~~~:!!!!XH\\\"  z#   .*\\\"\r\n                    #s   ^%L  ^\\\"#4@UU@##\\\"  z#   .*\\\"\r\n                      #s   ^%L           z#   .r\\\"\r\n                        #s   ^%.       u#   .r\\\"\r\n                          #i   \\\'%.   u#   .@\\\"\r\n                            #s   ^%u#   .@\\\"\r\n                              #s x#   .*\\\"\r\n                               x#`  .@%.\r\n                             x#`  .d\\\"  \\\"%.\r\n                           xf~  .r\\\" #s   \\\"%.\r\n                     u   x*`  .r\\\"     #s   \\\"%.  x.\r\n                     %Mu*`  x*\\\"         #m.  \\\"%zX\\\"\r\n                     :R(h x*              \\\"h..*dN.\r\n                   u@NM5e#>                 7?dMRMh.\r\n                 z$@M@$#\\\"#\\\"                 *\\\"\\\"*@MM$hL\r\n               u@@MM8*                          \\\"*$M@Mh.\r\n             z$RRM8F\\\"                             \\\"N8@M$bL\r\n            5`RM$#                                  \\\'R88f)R\r\n            \\\'h.$\\\"                                     #$x*\r\n\r\n</PRE>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">'),(2757,'<p>\r\nhi there.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday, i drove a golf cart down to a big empty theater surrounded by trees. i helped my fellow production crew members mop the floor there. i have keys to every classroom on this campus. i eat well. on the pathways and sidewalks i stop for random hugs, induced by two days worth of group sharing workshops which, tho i wince at them sometimes, really did bring us together.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe live here for free, for six weeks or four months or some time period in between. we are fed or we feed each other. both, actually. it\\\'s not what you would expect. it\\\'s not an ashram. we are not silent, humorless, isolated, or even devout. it\\\'s just a community. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni always have someone to eat with. there are meditation gardens, tons of workshops and classes. there\\\'s internet and it\\\'s not what i DO, it\\\'s just one small part of my life here. more important is learning about these new people who surround me, and doing my work.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy work is like this: wake up 5:30am, go up the hill, open the doors to the sanctuary. ring the big bell. i haven\\\'t done that yet but i\\\'m totally looking forward to it. also, ride the golf cart around to unlock doors of other classrooms. or maybe i work at night and i lock up all the classrooms for the night. the best part of the job, though, is the intent to provide the most beautiful and harmonious space possible for workshop participants and faculty. it is a tedious glory: perfect circles of chairs, measured with footsteps and stunted geometry skills. even chairs and pillows off to the side must be stacked symmetrically and neatly. the point is to create spaces with the least amount of distraction for people who are trying to grow.\r\n</p><p>\r\nit can be quite meditative to carefully arrange chairs and cushions like this. it is also incredibly joyous to coast along paths in the golf cart saying hello to people and just generally spreading joy. staff here exists to create a nice structure for participants who are only here for a week.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni am pleased and happy. the food is fucking great.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalso, i have been wearing my maglite in its holster on my belt, in tribute to mister yeti radavich, my true love. also, i carry a leatherman.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyay!!!!\r\n</p>'),(2758,'i dreamed last night i was in jail<br>\r\nhandcuffed to a slimy wall<br>\r\nwith rolling-eyed lunatics gibbering next to me<br>\r\nup in a high, round tower with a pointy top<br>\r\nthey brought me there on rickety terror of a rollercoaster<br>\r\nyou could escape by cajoling the jailers to let your chains loose<br>\r\ni watched a girl leap catlike onto a slanting rooftop and slink away<br>\r\ni wasn\\\'t taking any chances at that point<br> \r\njail was strange, a dank and sexual place<br>\r\nthreats of violence looming on all sides<br>\r\npreoccupation with the body<br>\r\n...<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni woke up at one a.m. with a terrible taste in my mouth and a question repeating in my head:<br><i>\r\n\\\"why do you stay in prison when the door is wide open?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"why do you stay in prison when the door is wide open?\\\"</i><br>\r\ni stumbled to brush my teeth, shedding the clothes i\\\'d fallen asleep in<br>\r\n(i found them this morning, a trail to my bed).<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsinking back into the dream, i was escaping jail, as if on a field trip,<br>\r\ninto a vast colorful library where i tried to find a trashy novel to distract me while in jail<br>\r\ngetting snacks and costumes together so the time in jail wouldn\\\'t be unbearable<br>\r\npreparing myself to be in jail for a long time.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\neek.\r\n\r\n'),(2759,'<p>Amber glass and thick sunlight: there was<br>\r\nA pretty mobile <br>\r\nTurning, turning. <br>\r\nTasting blue, red, <br>\r\nAnd inhaling: <br>\r\nSoft, old, cotton friend. <br>\r\nSitting on the floor: <br>\r\nListening.</p>\r\n<p>Amber floor was made of wood and <br>\r\nMysterious black marks here and there. <br>\r\nLooking forward, <br>\r\nTelevision playing. <br>\r\nBreathing in<br>\r\nTattered sofa cover foam, <br>\r\nLeaning back: <br>\r\nStaring. </p>\r\n<p>Amber glass is a memory, <br>\r\nLike the pretty mobile turning, <br>\r\nCasting its colors casually. <br>\r\nThey danced for me. <br>\r\nI remember when I smell the linen, <br>\r\nMy Mother\\\'s voice, calling. <br>\r\nAnd the stories that she read: <br>\r\nI used to listen.</p>'),(2760,'<p>all of the following are sounds that drive me insane:</p>\r\n<p><b>the scraping of plastic spoon/fork against styrofoam container.</b><BR>\r\nthis offense occurs typically when someone is eating ice cream from a styrofoam cup and is scraping, scraping to get the last bit of ice cream from said cup.</p>\r\n<p><B>excessive sucking on a straw when glass is clearly empty.</b><BR>\r\nespecially when drinking a milkshake or similarly thick beverage, so the person sucks and sucks and gets nothing and then waits a few minutes, thinking that the beverage might have melted a little, and starts sucking again. dude, it\\\'s empty. get over it.</p>\r\n<p><b>the sound of a finger against the opening of an aluminum can.</b><BR>\r\nmy mother has this persistent habit of sticking her pinkie finger in the mouth of her soda cans right after opening them (presumably to lessen the fizz), and every time she does it, i wince, and she says, \\\"oh. i forgot,\\\" although the first few times that i told her how much i hate the sound, as a teenager, she light-heartedly chided me about being too sensitive, but seriously. it\\\'s a hideous noise.</p>\r\n<p><b>neck cracking.</b><BR>\r\nalthough the sounds of other popping body parts, like knuckles or ankles, is actually a vaguely pleasing noise to me, neck cracking nauseates me. possibly because the flippant tossing of the head in order to crack it is so unattractive to me.</p>\r\n<p><b>people laughing just a shade too loud at something that they are silently reading your presence, as if to say, \\\"oh please ask me what is so funny.\\\"</b><BR>\r\nespecially annoying in work situations when normal laughter wouldn\\\'t attract your attention in the safety of your cube, nor would slightly-louder-than-usual laughter, so the offending person has to laugh <b>exceptionally</b> loudly for you to hear it, and, as a result, you feel so <b>used</b>, so impressed upon. and although we\\\'ve all had the moments of reading something and being so overwhelmed with its hilarity that we helplessly guffaw, this type of laughter is forced and it\\\'s really the forced quality that irritates me, not the volume.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m sure there are several more that i can\\\'t think of right now, but these are the main offenders.</p>\r\n<p>what sounds annoy you?</p>\r\n'),(2761,'it seems as if my life is inevitably coming to a standstill. everywhere i look i see the same faces, the same expressions. am i to blame? or is it everyone else. i am consumed by the fear of not living my life. will i ever feel like i did when i was ten years old, running around with my best friends in the summer sun? laying on our backs looking up at the sky and making pictures out of the clouds. will i ever feel the innocence that i felt when i was twelve and had my first crush? the way my stomach turned into a pit of butterflies every time i walked past him; my face burning a deep shade of pink. i am consumed with the fear that my life is coming to the point where it can no longer be called a life. my mind tries to contemplate what my future holds, yet it is out of my grasp. is there such a thing as destiny? and if there is, do we actually have to work to achieve it. i\\\'m afraid that i\\\'ve already lost my chance to fulfill it. is it too late? or am i on the verge of discovering it?'),(2762,'<tt><a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org\\\"><img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2762_1.jpg\\\"></a><br>\r\nSubway tunnels are odd and uncanny places.  A <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2228#12731\\\">Pentecostal man</a> in my neighborhood says if you look into the tunnels, you put yourself at the whim of evil spirits...<br><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org\\\"><img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2762_2.jpg\\\"></a><br>\r\nSome people avoid looking out the windows of traincars passing through tunnels; they avert their eyes to the bright advertisements, or peruse the crowd within the car, careful not to make eye contact...<br><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org\\\"><img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2762_3.jpg\\\"></a><br>\r\nRed lights are ominous; red lights move through the tunnels, disembodied. Red lights streaking, red lights whirling in spiritual patterns...<br><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org\\\"><img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2762_4.jpg\\\"></a><br>\r\nGreen lights loop and whirl; green lights might be less evil than red lights, they say. Green lights also let the train leave the station, zipping quickly into the tunnel so that the brave among us can gaze through the windows at the passing lights...</tt>'),(2763,'i decided to force myself to make criticisms about others\\\' posts on swinney. this might be a bad idea. i might change my mind again later. but i feel this is what i have to do.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe feeling has grown slowly. at first, i was happy to read the nice comments <i>\\\"great post\\\"</i> etc. on many people\\\'s articles. i did some myself. i received a few with emotion...<br>\r\nbut i grew unsatisfied. and i will be leaving this country soon; i have to train myself back to the good old French negativism.\r\n</p><p>\r\nalso, many times i found it unfair that some good posts didn\\\'t get the comments one could expect. bad old recipes still work too good: mention sex or violence in your post and you will get all the attention.<br>\r\ni should have known that comments are not related to the quality of a post, as most of you must know by now.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso here is my deal:<br>\r\ni will make criticism everytime i find an occasion for it. on anybody\\\'s post. i even might be a bit harder on the people i appreciate the most (according to the rule).<br>\r\nyou can in return tell me to fuck off, or argue with me, or ignore my comments, or criticize my own posts. with this last one you you get an easy revenge, but this is my - now revealed - secret goal: improve myself by fighting.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand during the coming battle, never forget, that <u>i love you all</u>.\r\n</p>'),(2764,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡'),(2765,'I can feel the empty, hollow aching, longing,\r\nspiritually lazy stagnation of the blue collar suburb.\r\nWake up before you are over run by the outlet stores \r\nand fat-fried fast food chains that line every block.\r\nI can feel the urgent envy of the ghetto.\r\nSouls and bodies and families hanging by a string.\r\nWhere is God?\r\nNowhere to be seen in the long forgotten and ill maintained streets and souls and minds of the disenfranchised.\r\nI can feel the all too relaxed tension \r\nof the hip, happening hangouts of the urban centers.\r\nThe mix and mash, the gentle clash.\r\nThere goes the punker chick dressed in black,\r\nrings in her nose and anxiety heavy on her back.\r\nEveryone trying to be different and trying not to be judged, but everyone walking and looking \r\nand pointing and laughing outloud.\r\nLaughing at each other and laughing at themselves.\r\nIs this right?  I think I need separation.\r\n'),(2766,'<p>\r\nI\\\'m on the bus again, the N Judah. More of an electric trolley-like train car than a bus really. I\\\'m heading outbound, toward the beach. I\\\'m able to find a seat right away, though I have to move a small paper airsick bag from an airline I\\\'ve never heard of off of the seat before I can sit down. I\\\'m vaguely worried it\\\'s full of puke, but it\\\'s light and unscented as I scoot it into a crack where it falls through to the floor. I sit down and suddenly I\\\'m being confronted with the state of America. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere are fat girls on the bus.  One of them is unnaturally large in a way that makes me worried for her very immediate health, the others are heavy but within our modern society\\\'s limits. All of them are a little too loud. This is possibly because they are all a little too teenage. They discuss finals. One of them has a final on Wednesday at 7 a.m. Another says that is ridiculous! She loudly declares that she would protest a final at such an hour, she would picket! \\\"I\\\'D PICKET!\\\" she cries. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone else on the bus is quiet. They keep to themselves the way people on a bus tend to do. Some couples coo quietly at each other, some riders carefully pass comments from mouth to ear, or stifle coughs, or whatever it is that people do to make the white noise of the inside of a bus. A few smile bemusedly but silently at the girls. I lean to my left and prop my elbow on the end of the row of seats, and then place my hand on my forehead. My seat, the one that held the airsick bag, is next to one of the girls. I get to hear her outrage at early mornings, her discussion of class schedules and classmates first hand. I feel her bulging thigh against mine as it edges out of her seat.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI sit directly across the aisle from the girl whose health I question. I wonder about glandular disorders. I cannot find her neck. Her arms rival my thighs. Her eyes peer out from deep within. I avert my own eyes and check out the couple to my left. They have some grocery bags, and a combined total of forty eight rolls of toilet paper between them. But I cannot avert my thoughts. I am now worried about the health of a nation in which a nineteen year old student requires more than one seat on the bus. Where nineteen year old students who are twice my size whine about misplacing their twenty ounce bottles of Coke.</p>\r\n<p> I wince as we pass a section of town rife with shops and restaurants and the girls squeal and moan in unison and in hunger as we pass several pizza places. They do not salivate for the salad place, the sushi place, they want the pizza. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is the state of America, and I start to wonder if the future is going to eat us alive.</p>\r\n'),(2767,'<p>strange things happen when they are least expected -- a collection of thoughts may 20 / 02\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>wake up in january from a long nap but think you are in may.  wander the streets in may fighting the snow that wasn\\\'t here when it was needed.  we had to send our canadian troops out to fight because there was no snow to shovel like years past.  something is wrong here.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why worry about things you cant control?  chance meetings with people who share many common interests isn\\\'t an everyday thing.  people who can appreciate fine junk are hard to come by.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>---</p>\r\n<p> it was a snowy may day.  suprising since it was so warm earlier in the week.  after having a dicussion with a homeless man about isonews.com (how homeless people know where to get their warez will always be a mystery to me) a quick jaunt to the computer district is necessary.  it proves to be much harder to find obsolete cpus than first thought.  after meandering through all the stores in the area, it was decided that obtaining this chip was not meant to be for today.  see part two.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ever think that to solve cities garbage problems, you could just train birds to make their nests out of garbage?  sure you would have a few bird casulties to the war on drugs.  but overall these birds could really keep the city clean.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>are spys paranoid that they themselves are being spied upon?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>part deux.  to continue the story later in the evening.  we had arrived with our asses nice and toasted by the heated leather seats to our destination.  unimpressed by the crowd, but the company is great.  possibly smitten.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for all the things i do understand, there are at least one hundred thousand other things i don\\\'t.  i think girls top that list easily.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no matter how many stories you tell, they won\\\'t be able to change the truth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>troiseme - nicely toasted.  if you can stand our nicely toasted conversations, then i am impressed and would like to hear more.  a crazy city has produced crazy but likeable people.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fin -- this is just a collection of random thoughts take it at face value.  excuse spelling and grammar its, 6am and i haven\\\'t slept</P'),(2768,'This city that embraced me before I did,<br>\r\ntook me in and showed me some places<br>\r\nI\\\'d never seen or been,<br>\r\nis not feeling like such a friend anymore.</p>\r\n<p>Disguised in its magnanimous coating<br>\r\nof diversity and overwhelming output,<br>\r\nit no longer needs my need.</p>\r\n<p>Full of so many others,<br>\r\nit has drained me of my own spirit<br>\r\nand filled the gap with a noise and smell<br>\r\nthat has both numbed and sensitised me for too long.</p>\r\n<p>Can a place do such things?<br>\r\nLife here is perhaps too big for me.<br>\r\nIs there a simpler place to suit my simpler needs?<br>\r\nOr am I thinking merely of an adjustable scapegoat?</p>\r\n<p>Maybe I\\\'ve yet to find a way<br>\r\nto preserve and protect my own small world.</p>\r\n<p>Or maybe I\\\'m right to think<br>\r\nsmall worlds flourish best in small star systems.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2769,'<P>I always fall in love at the laundromat.  Not that I don\\\'t fall in love in other places, but every single time I set foot into a laundromat I know that I will fall in love at some point within the following two hours.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m not talking crushes here, but honest to God, heart thumping, earth shattering love.  Even on a slow day at odd hours, there is always someone more than willing to be the unwitting recipient of a spin and tumble\\\'s worth of my devotion.  I\\\'ve indulged in nerd love (glasses, slightly too slick hair, way too many monochromatic button-down short sleeved shirts), biker lust (grizzly gray streaked pony tail/beard, leather jacket, tattooed neck, basket full of grimy white t-shirts, a bottle of teddy bear brand fabric softener to take the edge off), even the occasional girl-on-girl pseudo porn college co-ed fantasy (laundry day sweat pants covering the last clean thong in the underwear drawer, sorority girl highlights pulled back into that neat/sloppy scrunchy thing I can never do with my own hair, never too hung over to sort colors and whites into their respective machines).\r\n</P><P>\r\nI linger while folding to draw out each affair as long as possible.  I steal glances of biceps and tummies and backs of necks through the plastic checkerboard of a conveniently placed laundry basket.  I imagine what we would do in our free time or how my head would feel nestled on their lap watching our favorite movie together.  My heart breaks each time one walks out the door, oblivious to my affection.\r\n</P><P>\r\nToday (it must be fate) I have fallen for the same guy for the second time.  He is so undeniably appealing in his normalcy that I\\\'m sure if I wait long enough I will catch someone else stealing glances.  Yellow shirt, non-athletic or even trendy baseball cap, glasses for the intelligent look, brown, clean-cut hair, non-descript white sneakers and an average-to-cuddly body type.  There\\\'s a touch of five o\\\'clock shadow on a very friendly face, and a cell phone on his belt that screams, \\\"I\\\'m a bit of a dork, but a busy one who likes to stay in touch with the world.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe\\\'s here with a woman, but they\\\'re doing their laundry very separately.  Washers in different rows, and absolutely no co-mingling of their clothes.  They almost don\\\'t even look like they\\\'re here together, except for the sharing of the fabric softener sheets.  Somehow I know I\\\'m much better for him than she is.  I would let him share a washer, and he would let me wear his shirts around the house.\r\n</p><P>\r\nHe\\\'s sitting down now, drinking a Pepsi, and I wish he would pick up something to read, so that I could gauge his interests.  Occasionally he says something to his woman about his job or a trip he\\\'ll be taking.   I think she\\\'s caught me peeking at him, returning my glance with a smug look of confident possession. She barely hears him, but I hang on every word.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAlas, my dryer is beeping for attention.  I fold my clothes and the fantasy is over.  I walk out the door secure in my knowledge that even though my heart is painfully swollen with unrequited love, within a week\\\'s worth of perspiration, dribbled food, and muddy hiking adventures with my toddler I will walk back through that door with a basket of clothes, ready to love again.</p>'),(2770,'<p>\r\nbefore i start this, i\\\'d like to mention that what i\\\'m about to spew out i know is most likely all the result of my own paranoia and esteem issues. my head knows this. living it is another thing. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni moved to new york a year ago. not the city, long island actually since that\\\'s where my job is. i grew up in small town michigan, and lived in about 4 other cities afterwards before i came to new york. since i\\\'ve moved here, i\\\'ve never felt more self conscious about my looks. girls here are anorexic looking. it also seems to be a requirement here that whatever you wear, it has to be tight, low cut, etc. fashion here is also different. in my hometown my friends are all married and don\\\'t even look at what they put on to wear. the same couple pairs of jeans and sweatshirts work fine. a couple months after moving here i found myself looking into my closet panic striken realizing i couldn\\\'t remember the last time i\\\'d bought any new clothes due to many years of being extremely poor, and fretting over the fact that i didn\\\'t measure up to the girls at work. </p>\r\n<p> it\\\'s amazing the change that comes over me when i\\\'m in the presence of one of these pretty girls at work. it\\\'s annoying when there has to be a guy there i happen to really really like and every time i see one of the girls appear i get mad that they exist altogether. which is silly, yes. some of these girls i\\\'m friends with and of course i wouldn\\\'t want them to not exist. but every time i look at one of the fake tan midriff showing \\\"i only eat one piece of celery a day\\\" girls my confidence plummets and i feel plain. that\\\'s what it is. most of all i just feel plain. </p>\r\n<p> i should restate though, that i know this is most likely only in my head. i\\\'ve been asked out since being here. guys have told me i\\\'m hot. i also realize i shouldn\\\'t make broad sweeping judgetments like \\\"all girls in new york are like this\\\". i know girls like this exist all over, and i also know that not every single girl in the new york/li area looks like this. but as i said, actually letting go of my inner voice of negative thoughts is another thing. </p>\r\n'),(2771,'<p>\r\nit wasn\\\'t long before i realized our true purpose among these people. i now feel it was the hands of the fates that caused our inclusion within this isolated group. \r\n<p>\r\nwe soon learned the history, the oral traditions. it was amazing how quickly we learned the language. it was as if we were born to know it. the vocabulary seemed to just pop into my head in magical ways. i wish there was some explanation that made sense, but there isn\\\'t. \r\n<p>\r\nlike all oral narration\\\'s, the details become fuzzy. each generation altering the truth of the past to bend to the needs and will of the present. it has been hundreds of years since the great cataclysm. it was taught to us in lower school. but these people, they have a much more colourful version than any textbook could illuminate. hard facts are nothing compared to first hand accounts, despite them being retold by four generations past. \r\n<p>\r\nfrom what i can infer, our new family was driven here by our common ancestors. these are where some details become strange. apparently they were giants. silver hulking behemoths toting sigils of light and fire. \r\n<p>\r\nof course, like many isolated and extradited people, they have their own saviour/god myths. it\\\'s almost a cliche now to think of how enthralling our fiery decent from orbit must have been. the orations that the elders pass on speak of the pair discovered in the flat plains. \r\n<p>\r\ni am not sure what we\\\'ll be able to do to save these people. in fact, i think they\\\'re saving us from the world we came from.'),(2772,'<p>Every so often someone will use those two magic words in close proximity to each other, you know: detachable and penis.<br>\r\nAnd I hear an old song begin to play in my head: <a href=\\\"http://www.wildcity.com/lyrics/detach.htm\\\">King Missile - <i>Detachable Penis</i>. </a><br>\r\nBut never until now has it occurred to me that this might be the answer to all the problems posed by my long-distance love affairs, and broken-hearted nights alone.<br>\r\nI could send that special part of myself around the world, to be with other people in a special way.<br>\r\nDo I have a paper to write?  No problem dear: I\\\'ll stay home, and send the penis.<br>\r\nYou don\\\'t feel like going out?  No problem dear: I\\\'ll stay home, and send the penis.<br>\r\nNot quite certain if you want a man around?  No problem dear: I\\\'ll stay home, and send the penis.<br>\r\nYou live 9000 miles away?  No problem dear: I\\\'ll stay home, and send the penis.<br>\r\nIt would have postage on it - stamps from exotic lands - like some steamer trunk from a bygone era.<br>\r\nLike a character from Dr. Seuss: It would go here and there.  It would go everywhere.  It would travel on a train.  It would travel on a plane.<br>\r\nIt would be a celebrity: a sophisticated fellow who was welcomed at dinner parties.<br>\r\nOh the stories that he\\\'d have to tell after we came together again!<br>\r\nAnd I would be so much more productive: He would go places and do things; I would sit here and type - undisturbed by the nagging thought that I should be in a relationship.<br>\r\nEveryone knows that the brain and the penis have never gotten along; it\\\'s like a cat and a dog living together.  <br>\r\nBiker guys could wear it on a wallet chain.<br>\r\nWives could keep it at home when their husbands went out of town on business trips.<br>\r\nAhh...the detachable penis.<br>\r\nSigh.</p>'),(2773,'<p>\r\nWhen I was here before<br>\r\nCouldn\\\'t look me in the eye<br>\r\nI\\\'m just like an angel<br>\r\nMy skin makes you cry<br>\r\nI float like a feather<br>\r\nIn a beautiful world<br>\r\nAnd you wish you were special<br>\r\nI\\\'m so fuckin\\\' special<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nBut you\\\'re a creep, you\\\'re a weirdo.<br>\r\nWhat the hell are you doing here?<br>\r\nYou don\\\'t belong here.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2775,'<p>\r\nme and shane were wandering around a department store complex called DOOTA.COM. yeah thats the name. the web is rampant here. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe head to a floor dedicated to accessories. a girly girls dream come true, and let me say this country is full of them. women with curfews like to buy things more then women who dont. heh. im sure there is a study somewhere in the annals of science or sociology that will have proved this. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na big fad these days in korea are little bobbles that hang from a handphone(cellphone). \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are stores that sell nothing but these things. you can get anything and everything to hang from your phone. sesame street characters, holeman, moshimoro, soju bottles, lights, lighters, tiny picture frames with gawdy rhinestones around, star signs, korean symbols, backward swastikas(dont know what they are called, but im sure the GI\\\'s from rednekville think they are a riot), bruce lee(numchucks n all), school girls(i have one), almost anything you can think of. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwe were browsing through one of the shops where i found a thomas the tank engine thingy. i picked it up to send to my nephew who is absolutely in love with anything thomas(i also got him a thomas the tank engine key chain that plays the theme song in japan. the coolest part is the japanese package, but im sure he\\\'ll care little for the squiggles). as a joke i say to shane \\\'i wish i could find an osama bin laden\\\'. next thing you know we see one. me and shane both picked one up. its absolutely grand. its a bust of the famous \\\'terrorBOOMr\\\'(thats what my kids call him). a few of the american teachers we\\\'ve shown him to got mad at us in their drunken states saying how tasteless it is an all that. when i told my mom i had osama hanging from my phone she laughed for a good ten minutes of my phone card time. i personally think its a riot. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen im drunk i talk to osama and have him talk back in what i think arabic sounds like. people get a real kick out of it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nother things osama related:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-there is a cartoon tv commerical here that shows a plane heading towards the wtc. then some superheroes fly to the front of the plane and leta newspaper land on the window, covering their view. the terrorists and the pilots start laughing at what they see in the paper, and then the paper flies off the window and they swurve to miss the buildings. then its switches to a view of osama sitting in a cave watching it all on tv looking very sad.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-the kids have figured out a way to origami a 1000 won bill so that the face of a once leader of korea looks like osama bin laden. its really neat, though i dont have one. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-the kids in shanes class hate bingo, but when you change the name of the game to osama bingo laden, they love it. kids are weird. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the political debate level, i get a real kick out people who get mad at osama hanging from my phone. they say things like \\\'but he organized and helped pay for the wtc attacks\\\'. i look at these people and say \\\'yeah, bill clinton helped organize the attacks against a pharmaceuticals plant in sudan, resulting in the deaths of about 100,000 sudanese because of the resulting lack of affordable medicine. you dont call him a terrorist.\\\" i also look at all the other \\\"heads\\\" that order the deaths of people around the world. sharon in israel, thatcher in the falklands, yeltsin/putin in chechnya...the list goes on. perhaps the only \\\"head\\\" the world has tried to make accountable is milosovic of serbia...and thats only because he\\\'s a serb. i mean who likes serbs right?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor me at least, having osama hang from my phone reminds me of all the double edged swords in this world. right vs wrong. might vs plight. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTHE WORLD NEEDS MORE TREES, LESS BUSHES\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce again this is chiamattt reporting form seoul s.korea. '),(2776,'i am going on what should be an interesting trip this weekend. tomorrow morning i will be leaving columbia, sc and traveling by car to the bustling metropolis of montgomery, alabama. what on earth would you want to do that for, you may ask? well, i am going to the alabama shakespeare festival, which really isn\\\'t a festival but a year-round theater (my teacher spells this <i>theatre</i> because he\\\'s a &quot;real&quot; playwright). there i will see a production of the two plays that we have studied min my Maymester class, namely Much Ado About Nothing, and a modern play by Doris Baizley called Shiloh Rules. these plays are almost as diverse a pair as you have ever seen, but both are comedies. mostly. \r\n<p>\r\nanyway, the whole point of this, besides filling all of you swinney readers in on the details of my playboy lifestyle, is that i want to write an article in anticipation of going, and then another article when i get back. i am going to describe the trip for you here in great detail <i>before I go</i>, and then describe it when i return, and won\\\'t it be fun to see what i got right and what i didn\\\'t!\r\n<p>\r\nFriday, May 24, 2002. I wake up at 8am, having packed my bag the night before and cleaned out the car. There is at least one good-looking girl riding with me, and one guy, and maybe another girl--i\\\'m not sure (That last part about who is riding with me is not conjecture). Everyone else is riding in a school van which will be white with the USC logo on the side. After deciding not to try to &quot;caravan&quot; all the way there, i.e. i don\\\'t want to follow someone\\\'s lead for eight hours, we will depart at 9:30 or 9:45.\r\n<p>\r\nThe guy and girl(s) will have brought cd\\\'s. Theirs will be different from mine, but hopefully compatible. the guy will have dave matthews, the girl some sorority girl music that is completely alien to me. i will have a box full of discs, from DB to Django Reinhardt to the new Medeski MArtin and Wood to Zappa. the music is the most important part of the drive for me.\r\n<p>\r\nabout two hours in i will attempt to ascertain the feelings of my passengers regarding me smoking a joint. they will be hesitant at first, then cave in. after all, they could have taken the bus. we arrive.\r\n<p>\r\nat the hotel, the guy and i get our room, and i begin mixing vodka-and-tonics in the little hotel cups. we go see Much Ado about eight pm; i am dressed much nicer than the other kids, in a sport coat with no tie, but i am wasted. the play will be incredible. afterwards, the other girls scramble to get the extra seat in my car that they wish they had reserved earlier, and we go out to eat and party.\r\n<p>\r\nday two- repeat. Shiloh Rules.\r\n<p>  \r\nDay three, sunday. driving home, whichever girl wanted to ride with me to go out will want a ride home, easing the gas cost. if it\\\'s the one i am thinking that it will be, she will have indy rock cd\\\'s which is fine. i like indy rock but i\\\'m still on Beta Band and Pavement, if you know what I mean. is that even the right term for them? anyway, i\\\'ll have some valium so it won\\\'t make any difference. take me in, and dry the rain...\r\n<p>\r\nso there it is! sunday night i\\\'ll write another article and see how close i got.'),(2777,'<p>It was in the fall of 1988 when my sister and I were sitting in the living room with some old family friends.  Their son was my age, and I had a huge crush on him.  I loved when our families got together because it was the only time that I got to see him.  I was wearing my new black jean skirt and t-shirt with a bright picture of a panda bear.  I worked for an hour on my hair and if my parents would have let me wear make-up when I was ten, I probably would have spent a lot of time doing that too.  We got out the Legos and began to build.  As I leaned over to grab a piece, my shirt slipped up a little to show my stomach.  Immediately my crush said, â€œI saw that, you have fat roles on your stomach.â€  I was crushed and utterly embarrassed.  So much so that I still remember it now, 14 years later. <p>\r\n<p>Little did I know that in years to come I would get all kinds of teasing.  As I put on a swimming suit for gym class in junior high, when I tried to wear the trendy  tight jeans in the 80â€™s or the one time when I wore leggings to school and one of my friends said, â€œYour butt jiggles when you walk.â€  I never had a boyfriend and always told myself it was because I wasnâ€™t pretty enough, wasnâ€™t skinny enough, wasnâ€™t shapely enough, wasnâ€™t cool enough.  I gave myself a lot of reasons for it, but I always came back to the only thing that I could change, my thighs, my stomach, my weight...  By the end of junior high I was ashamed every time I got dressed, so at the beginning of high school, I decided to â€œfixâ€ my body.  I joined the cross country skiing team, the track team, and the cross country running team.  I watched what I ate and attempted to skip as many meals as I could.  I always ate dinner because I knew that my parents would notice if I didnâ€™t.  I must have eaten enough so that my obsession wasnâ€™t noticeable to other people, because no one ever said anything.  Or maybe they were just too scared to say anything...  And I always ended up eating anyway, and then hating myself for it. <p>\r\n<p>By the time I started college, I had come to a peaceful place with myself.  I ate every meal and didnâ€™t work out 3 hours a day like I had in high school, and I even felt okay about the way I looked.  But soon, the â€œFreshman 15â€ started to set in.  Unlike high school, I no longer have the time or money to go into counseling or work-out.  Now, I canâ€™t remember a time when I have had a lower image of my body.  I feel lost and out of control.  Where did all of these feelings come from?  Why have I become so obsessed with my body?  What I want the most is to figure out what caused this problem so that I can fix it.  But there is no answer, just a jumble of stories, memories, advertisements, movies, models, friends, sneerings and self loathing.  Amidst the, â€œYou donâ€™t look that different than you did in high school,â€ â€œI still think you are beautiful,â€ â€œYou are still below the national average,â€ and my personal favorite, â€œYou still look just fine,â€ I have been unable to see myself as beautiful or desirable.  Iâ€™ve devoted quite a bit of my time trying to figure out what happened to me and why I canâ€™t shake these feelings.  I canâ€™t blame it all on that one day with the Legos and the fancy panda bear shirt.  The question is, why did I already care so much about my body when I was ten?'),(2778,'Pleasurable silence surrounds my senses. Not true quiet, but the naturally soothing kind. Just the tiny gurgling of a crisp forest spring, the pips and titters of invisible but all too noticeable avian creatures. Occasionally a breeze breathes by, causing an eerie hush for just a moment. I can breathe deeply, inhale the world of lucious greens and brown in.\r\n<p>\r\nIf I stare just hard enough I can imagine I can see the lichens row on these ancient rocks. Lifeless yet alive, the stones upon which the forest lays brings sturdy stability to the roots of the world.\r\n<p>\r\nI almost desire the kind of lifeless appearance of the moss covered boreal floor. I only truly reveals it\\\'s beauty when you just look a little closer. \r\n<p>\r\nThe closer you look, the more you see. A stoic glamour that is as old as time itself. The feeling of watching the forest open up it\\\'s new-found face is likefeeling the cool kiss of the breath of mother nature  blow across my cheekbones.\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s a funny thing, spring in this forest. It sometimes snows. Not the ferocious old man of winter, but the calm nymph of renewal reminding us the icy embrace of that man is not long ago held us tightly.\r\n<p>\r\nThis outcropping of rounded rock where I sit is more ancient than I can imagine. Older than my family, older than all our people. I can imagine, though, that the moss that almost sparkles like emeralds is even older than me.'),(2779,'<p>\r\nso, i have poison ivy. i don\\\'t know where i got it, really. it\\\'s a small patch on my hand. it oozes and weeps. pustules. i made some art about the poison ivy to honor it. that was remedy number one. i also applied various forms of calamine, benadryl cream, and dr bronners. dr bronners soothes best i must say. it dries the pustules. somehow i spread the poison ivy to a tiny patch right at the top of my ass crack. don\\\'t tell anyone that ok, i\\\'m kind of embarassed.\r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday someone told me that putting urine on poison ivy, or any sorta fungal skin infection like that, is good for it. so i pissed on my hand earlier. don\\\'t tell that to the next person using this keyboard ok? it was good, peeing on my hand. it was soothing. i think it helped. anyway how can you resist pissing on yourself when you\\\'ve been given full medical license to do so?\r\n</p><p>\r\ni think the rash is clearing up, though, regardless.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni met someone here who has almost the same astrological chart as me, down to the houses. that\\\'s pretty uncommon. talking to her is like talking to a slightly altered version of myself. we are both really into astrology. go figure. she is small, with narrow shoulders and less narrow hips. she carries a white cloth bag around sometimes. she holds it out from her body as if it contains something she doesn\\\'t lay claim to. i asked her, but usually it\\\'s only toiletries or food.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe theme, overall, is aquarius. community. \r\n</p><p>\r\n(at night i hear animals outside my tent. i\\\'ve been having some weird visions the last couple of days when i try to sleep. specifically of being stabbed many times in the back. i\\\'m not sure why. i think i\\\'m a little lonely or stressed.)\r\n</p><p>\r\n i have met four, count em, four, aquarians that i want to befriend. i didn\\\'t choose them because they were aquarians, it just kind of happened that way.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni\\\'m sorry for using astrology talk when it alienates so many people. don\\\'t be alienated. just send me an email and i\\\'ll explain myself.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthis weekend some chicks will be here talking about being chicks. powerful chicks. the workshop is called \\\"women and power.\\\" anita hill will be here. so will eve ensler of \\\"vagina monologues\\\" fame. my department will be doing some stuff around campus to help facilitate their existence here. i don\\\'t know what to think about anita hill. is she really a hardcore feminist or is she just milking it? maybe i\\\'ll let you know, after i see her speak this weekend.\r\n</p><p>\r\nok.\r\n</p>'),(2780,'<P>well i saw her today, i know i rambled and tried to cram everything in to one session\r\nshe suggested medication- never.\r\n</P><P>but maybe\r\n</P><P>i am anxious alot\r\n</P><P>migraines and ulcers, when does it end?\r\n</P><P>i say i would never hurt myself, but am i though with the anxiety? just slower? \r\n</P><P>the lonliness hurts, i feel empty, just going through the motions\r\n</P><P>where did the richness of life go?\r\n</P><P>did i let it go when he said i\\\'m leaving?\r\n</P><P>change the thoughts consciously\r\n</P><P>but i love him, i want him\r\n</P><P>how?\r\n</P><P>becoming comfortable being alone, with myself and my thoughts\r\n</P><P>i start a new position friday- new start?\r\n</P><P>i will not call i need to get used to not having him around\r\n</P>'),(2782,'<p>Iâ€™m pretty sure I have genital herpes.<br><br>  Maybe itâ€™s the Zoloft talking but I really donâ€™t care.  I\\\'m not even diagnosed yet but I\\\'m sure of it.  </p><br><br><p>While playing Amped at work I thought of how my life is going to be if I do in fact carry the blister-inducing virus that supposedly 1 in 5 americans have.  I think that its going to be a mixed blessing.  I amused myself thinking of ways of spreading it to people I donâ€™t know/like but at the same time Iâ€™m embarrassed of what my friends and family would think if they ever found out.  Iâ€™m also not to fond of the mandatory precautions that will need to implemented for future sexual encounters.</P><br><br>\r\n<p>\r\nThe next big question that needs an answer is where did I get it from?  I have had unprotected sex with people I donâ€™t really know plenty of times.  Yes, Iâ€™m stupid but Iâ€™m a man who loves the pleasures of the flesh.  I wonder if this virus was brought to my neck of the woods from over seas.  Could it have been the lovely young lass from Holland?  meh.  oh well.  It might have also been the pretty young thing from Montreal.  I guess Iâ€™ll really never know.</p>\r\n<br><br><p>\r\nIâ€™m just clueless what to say to kim.  I hope to god she doesnâ€™t get it.  Sheâ€™s dealt with enough lately.  </p><br><br>\r\n\r\noh well.  Iâ€™m young.  Iâ€™m stupid.  I know... at least flames didn\\\'t shoot out my dick.\r\n<br>'),(2783,'Two embryos meet in a crowded place.<br>\r\nShe\\\'s looking for someone he\\\'ll never be.<br>\r\nHe\\\'s looking at her and desperately wants to fuck and run.\r\n<p>Outside, middle aged rollerbladers make their polyurethane mark,<br>\r\ncarefully executing the maze of dog shit that everybody hates but nobody cleans up.</p>\r\n<p>Down the street a man reaches into his pocket for some change to give a stranger.<br>\r\nAs his hand emerges from his pure and lightest wool designer jacket, his  shopping list falls to the ground:</p>\r\n<p>Toothpaste<br>\r\nMilk<br>\r\nCereal<br>\r\nMethadone.</p>\r\n<p>Everyone here\\\'s neurotic, antibiotic, hypnotic, chaotic.<br>\r\nEveryone here\\\'s breaking down or cracking up.</p>\r\n<p>Awoken by her noisy neighbours, a lonely woman fondles her lost dreams in silence<br>\r\nwhile alien rain falls ineffectual onto a sealed street.<br>\r\nThe mystery of her pain is enchanting but still she ponders:<br>\r\nWhatever happened to three kids and a muddy driveway?</p> \r\n \r\n\r\n'),(2784,'Night was more night than ever.  Hours passed and everything fell asleep.  Only the tender breeze was awake.  Even though the room\\\'s light was on there was only black obscurity surrounding us.  You could hear that outside the wind was whispering throughout the empty streets.\r\n<p>\r\nA man was whistling in the street, walking down the sidewalks as he tried to make the thumps of his worries match the rhythm of his heartbeat in his solitude.  Meanwhile, the leaves, the birds and the moon listened carefully.  He looked towards his right, later towards his left for two seconds, and stepped on the asphalt.\r\n<p>\r\nWith authority, the machine turned right on the corner and taking the wind hostage.\r\n<p>\r\nThe man did not notice the car, which shone with the moon and cut through the atmosphere creating a vacuum, scarring the night.  The sound of the engine awoke him, while the impact was a soft push that lifted him into the air a few meters.  In a moment of liberty, the noise of his incarcerated soul turned into a human wail that lasted aproximately 8 seconds, the time he was in flight before his body plummeted into the ground and his skull opened with a silent blow.\r\n<p>\r\nHe lay there with an open mouth, his eyes still closed, and with a blanket of red supporting his body that expanded forever.\r\n<p>\r\nI looked up from my book when I heard the screeching of the car, imagining for a second the car crashing into a wall as a consequence of speed and recklessness. But when I listened to the scream, I felt my blood freeze.  And those were mere seconds.\r\n<p>\r\nBecause later, I stopped thinking and returned to my book, into my own silent and self-imposed prison.'),(2785,'<blockquote>\r\n<code>ON STAGE: A NEO HIPPY dressed in raggy clothes with a straw cowboy hat and a mason jar of water sits in a handmade chair. He should have a little desk (also funky looking) to set hit water and/or his feet on. We can hear an acoustic version of The Greatful Dead\\\'s \\\"Friend of the Devil\\\" in the background. The NEO HIPPY speaks to the crowd in a intimate/friendly fashion, looking people in the eyes, trying to make them see the substance of his Trip. </code>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nNEO HIPPY: I wanna sit down a minute and talk about how we\\\'re going to be. Not how we are, or how we were, or anything like that, but about how we\\\'re going to evolve from this particular manifestaion. I think we\\\'re doing pretty cool right now. On the whole we\\\'ve got some big problems, but we\\\'ve also got some pretty good opportunities too.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWhat I want to do here, just for a minute, is to imagine how things might really be different, might be better for us, if we evolved. Not if we got politicians to do something different, but how we, like you and me and all the people out there who are in our position, just regular people, could be a little bit different and make the world maybe a better place to live in.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nNow I\\\'m gonna say stuff like, \\\"in my world\\\" a lot. That\\\'s not because I own it, or because I don\\\'t want you to have a say about it. It\\\'s just becasue right now it\\\'s my imagination thing, and I\\\'ve never actually told anyone about this before. So don\\\'t get turned off because I say that this place is mine. Man, it\\\'s yours if you want it, and I\\\'d just love it if you helped me think of better and better things to put in it. I\\\'d love it if this we\\\'re our world. Anyway...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world, there would be a lot of things made of wood, and nothing would look cheap. Things might look shabby, run-down, or tarnished, but not that ugly brand-new cheap that goes along with those new buildings, most mid 90\\\'s luxury sedans, and cigarette butts. The kind of cheap that speaks of a total lack of passion or intention on the part of the creator.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThey built one of these cheap buildings across the street from my old apartment. It sticks out like a sore thumb - it\\\'s all mass produced and painted over with a veneer of boring convienences. The concrete blocks are all generic tan with pre-cut slots for individual air conditioners, waiting for their logo sporting cargo to be loaded in by non-union labor.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world apartments would be organic and non-mechanical constructions, the absolute opposite of the Marriot Marquis: different everywhere you go. They\\\'d have unfinished wood floors, unreliable heating and lots of natural light even on a cloudy day. We would use simple gas stoves and light them with stick matches, so you\\\'d smell propane and sulphur before you get your coffee in the morning.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world we would all drink beer sometimes, and we would make it ourselves with tools that were built to last.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world we would listen to music with soul, living by multicolored low-wattage lighting, lots of candles and lanters, and we\\\'d eat spicy food.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world, we would have free love. And I mean free -love-. We could have free sex too, but that\\\'s something different. People would be free to give and receive each other\\\'s love. It is my belief that in this world, let alone my world, there are many many souls we can love, but because love is so scarce and fragile in today\\\'s environment, we tend to cling to whatever we find with all our might.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world we\\\'d all know what love is all around, and we don\\\'t need to cling to anything. Not that we can\\\'t make commitments, but when we do so it is out of a desire to be committed and not out of a fear of being alone.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world, everyone would learn to cook at least one kick-ass dish. And we could share the food all around, with more of that home-made beer. The sense of taste would be recognized as a legitimate form of expression, not just a way to get people to plunk down an extra buck to super-size it.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world, there would be lots of street vendors, and even though practically nothing would be illegal, there would still be a thriving black market, because sometimes things need to be underground.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world conversation would be popular as a passtime, so would playing your own music, or telling stories.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world, we would all exercise and play with our minds and bodies liberally. Girls would feel free to masturbate and boys would feel free to cry. Everyone would create, and life would be a far more fluidly sensusal experience. We\\\'d all run around like Neal Cassady, shirts off, feet bare, just digging everything. Just saying yes. Yes yes yes.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIn my world people would have courage, and not feel the need to feel better than other people. You\\\'d learn at a young age that everyone\\\'s experience is different, and trying to value one against another is pure horseshit. People could say what was on their minds, having never had the idea that \\\"nobody wants to hear it\\\" planted in them. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy world, man... My world is a world of quality and generosity. You do what you do because you like doing it, and because you like doing it you do it well, and because you do it well it\\\'s worthwhile sharing with other people. It\\\'s a place where we really strike a do-able balance between creation and consumption.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd it \\\'doesn\\\'t have to be all peace and love man, there\\\'s room for fightin\\\' and competition and jealousy and hurt feelins and all that, but everyone would know that all those trips are just part of the big thing that is living. That\\\'s life, man, and you\\\'ve gotta at least try out all it\\\'s spices and take time to dig the different feelings they can bring on in you.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMan... life. The adventure of your lifetime. A long strange trip from two different bits of two different people, down the long and winding road, maybe a stopoff in the procreation station to start the whole show over, and then back to being plant-food. Spiritually put to rest.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t really think there\\\'s a world beyond in my world, no afterlife to speak of, but we\\\'d all know where we came from, so those past would be kindof living on somewhere as an echo of their cumulative energy, flowing around everything like the milky way flows around the brighter stars in a clear night sky.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nShit. My world ain\\\'t all thta different from this world. Only difference is that in my world people feel confidant about who they are and what they want and they know how to listen to eachother. You expand your consciousness a little bit, and it\\\'s not too hard to see how things can always be getting better all the time.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<code>takes a good long drink of water</code>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWell anyway, that\\\'s a little taste of what my world would be like. I don\\\'t know if you dig it or not, but even though I\\\'m kindof doing a character here, I\\\'m really serious about this. This is a real thing, man. Sombody\\\'s got to make the myths. We\\\'re all being sold on the idea that this is about good as it gets, and that\\\'s just something I can\\\'t swallow.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnyway, I\\\'m goin\\\' on too long. I just hope there\\\'s enough of us out there in this world so that what I\\\'m talking about doesn\\\'t stay my world. I want it to be our world. Thanks.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<code>finis</code>'),(2786,'<p>I run my hands through my hair, stimulating the nerves at the base of each strand and making my head buzz from the extascy. The roll is starting it\\\'s decent I think to myself, and settle into the couch for some uninhibited pontificating. So much is on my mind, but I seem to have less and less will to deal with any of it. I probably do this too much, too often, but there\\\'s really not much else to do in Charlotte.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Examining the elements of my circumstances always leaves me with the unavoidable conclusion that I acted/am acting both ethically justifiable and productive towards my goals. The tragic irony here is that in order to be true to myself, I have to alienate the people that helped me find said truths.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sweat drips from a loose tendril of my hair, onto the side of my nose and down my cheek like a tear. This is as close as I get to real tears anymore. I barely notice the drop in the sea of moisture building on my face. The room blurs slightly and pulses to the rhythmic beat; and a grin too faint to see flickers across my stony complexion. Time exists without motion in this place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Until recently, change loomed like a sea storm, turbulent and gloomy, over my horizons. But the closer I get, the more I realize that this change is a friendly shore for me. Land Ho! Slowly, the people around me are adjusting to this new reality; but they are a lot more affected by it than I expected. Of course, its always nice to be loved and missed; I guess I\\\'m still getting used to the attention. It feels very strange to be noticed and appreciated.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I feel too heavy to move. Someone turned up the gravity on this planet. But this is not why I roll. The weight of my thoughts seems tangible, sticking to me with persistence and dragging me down. I shift my focus across the room at the lava lamp. Didn\\\'t Dan say something about how the lamp works? Something about paint and saltwater? I wonder if that\\\'s just a stoner theory. I allow myself a quick glimpse back at my mental Sodom and Gomorra, and then turn my thoughts permanently elsewhere, for fear I\\\'ll turn to salt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My hands idly pick up my strings (glow sticks tied to shoelaces) and start twirling, my mind synchronizing my movements to the music. Comets of fuzzy light streak in quick-time orbit around my body, the arcing trails of blue seemingly washing away my concerns. This is why I roll.</p>'),(2787,'<p>Lotto is serious. This from feuding syndicate heads on a lotto chat forum. </p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\nName:Â Brett Sutton\r\nSubject:Â ReTurn out what?\r\nDate:Â 15/05/01 07:03:27\r\nGreetings From ACROOS THE TASMAN!\r\nFrom a MEG Lotto Player to SOME un-informed MUG Lotto Players in KIWI LAND\r\n*MEG* actually stands for Minority Educated Gamblers, whereas *MUG* actually\r\nstands for Majority Un-Educated GAMBLERS...Hee HAW!\r\nGidda! M8,\r\nWould of replied to you personally but seeing you R not Man enough to post your \r\nEmail Address in this Lotto Forum, can no do, so it must be here for all to read.\r\nThe Syndicate Club member Mr Daniel Barrie, in a posting, quotes me as making\r\nFASLE LIES about the Syndicate Club.\r\nHe started this Cyber War, and I will Bloody well finish it, like the Syndicate Club will\r\nbe finished very shortly-so there you go you stupid Dronngo!\r\nUnlike you! I know it is A New Zealand web Page Dedicated to Lotto, have been\r\nvisiting this site for many years-as far aback as late 1996, for the New Zealand\r\nLotto results. \r\nNot only for the Archive Search Engine at the Lotto Shack, but also for several Lotto\r\nPrograms that I use, and also send the data files to the companies, the supply the\r\nprograms-there you go!\r\n(Shot you down in flames again!) Hee Hee!\r\nDid you go and read the WARNING on the pages sitting at the Lotto Shack, I bet my\r\nbottom Dollar you didn\\\'t!\r\nI will post the URL here again. I will type in Upper Case, just in case your eyes and\r\njudgement is a bit clouded by living in the land of the LONG WHITE CLOUD, or\r\nshould that read LOW WHITE CLOUD HaHaHa!\r\nBut try and type in lower case if you can remember that. So here is the URL:\r\nWWW.LOTTOSHACK.COM.AU/WARN.HTM\r\n(By the way I have proof read the url to make sure there are no typo error\\\'s okay\r\nm8?)\r\nOnce you have read the entire page, then and only then be Judemental okay?\r\nBut probably you are a YELLA BELLY DINGO. and if you R an Ozzie living in NZ, you\r\nwill know what I mean.\r\nBut I have a gut feeling why you have not posted ur email address, and once again,\r\nI bet you my bottom dollar I am right.\r\nWhat bout it sport? Or R U to gutless to admit it.\r\nOtherwise go to the BACK of BOURKE!\r\nAlso view my postings at the TOP of the FORUM PAGE AS WELL.\r\nSyndicate Club\r\nSyndicate Club part 2\r\nSyndicate Club part 3\r\nEpecially the latest one,\r\nAlthough I live in Melbourne, I used some of my Lotto winnings to actually ring the\r\nDepartment of Fair Trading in Syndney New South Wales. To confirm that the\r\nSyndicate Club is being investigated-so there you go!\r\nWOW! WEE!\r\nAnd despite what Syndicate Club Mr Daniel Barrie says, that this club is a threat to\r\nthis advertised site, I can assure ALL DECENT KIWIS, it it is not a paid site, but\r\nactually funded out of my own pocket, and mainly from my Lotto Winnings and has\r\nbeen since it has been up on the World Wide Web, which goes back to June 1996.\r\nThat\\\'s even before any Official Lottery Commission in Australia, even had web\r\npages up.\r\nNotice the Lottery Commsion of New Zealand still have not posted their Official\r\nWeb site yet.\r\nBy why should they spend the money, when there are people like Jason Walker\r\nand New Zealand Pages and several other very good sites.\r\nThey are saving money.\r\nSo there you go BUDDY. Mate-Go and jump in the lake!\r\nOr better still Stick it up your jummper and smoke it.\r\nBut not being born nor living in Oz, you would not know what theses Aussie SLANG\r\nTERMS MEAN, would you?\r\n</pre>'),(2788,'<p>\r\nwe will eat at this thai restaurant on geary where one\\\'s got to take ones shoes off. it seems a big deal here but it\\\'s completely common in japan. in the mood for prank, i decided to wear old socks for the occasion, with the heel so worn out that my feet show through. maybe it brought me bad luck.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe are twenty minutes late, but the others arrive after us anyway: satako and emiko, both japanese friends of H, and john who is asian but not japanese; vietnamese, maybe. john and emiko plan to get married.<br>\r\nas we sit around the table i regret to be here. no doubt, it\\\'s gonna be boring. i drink my first beer the faster i can. the girls are talking in japanese. except looking at emiko, there are few things left to do. emiko is cute; she looks a bit like a doll, with bad teeth - i remember i asked H why emiko doesn\\\'t do something about her teeth. i try to get something fun from john. that\\\'s not easy. the place is noisy like hell and we have different accents. we repeat several times common questions and non surprising answers.\r\n</p><p>\r\n\\\"so you guys have been to japan for last christmas, right?\\\" i ask. i hope to get any kind of opinion about it. i wonder what other asians think about japan. \\\"how was it?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"oh, that was great!\\\" john answers. \\\"have you already been to japan?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"yeah, i spent one year there. this is where H and i met,\\\" i say, but i thought he could know this by now; this is the third time we meet... what the heck have we been talking about?<br>\r\n\\\"i loved the food,\\\" he continues. \\\"we\\\'ve also been to japanese bath. we had an outside bath, you know.\\\"<br>\r\ni nod. i <i>know</i> the bath. i had a bunch of them.<br>\r\n\\\"we were in hot water,\\\" john says, as i grab my beer and drink as long as i can without breathing. \\\"with just our head out of the water, and it was SNOWING!\\\" he exclaims.<br>\r\ni keep on drinking. the food is very good. i would like to have him tell me what emiko\\\'s parents thought about her marrying a non-japanese.<br>\r\n\\\"i love japan,\\\" he says. \\\"i had great food there.\\\"<br>\r\ni don\\\'t care anymore, i am drunk. soon after, i make a tiny mistake.\r\n</p><p>\r\na waiter comes. \\\"would you like something else?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"our soup didn\\\'t come,\\\" i say.<br>\r\n\\\"you want a soup?\\\" he asks. this place is definitely too fucking noisy.<br>\r\n\\\"no,\\\" i say. \\\"we ordered a soup already, but we didn\\\'t have it.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"for how many people would you like your soup, sir?\\\" he says, and he has an abnormally wide smile that makes me get paranoid in one second.<br>\r\n\\\"i don\\\'t know, for tree or four,\\\" i say, regretting that i must look so drunk and naive. \\\"we\\\'re gonna share.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"i can bring you a soup big enough for five to share,\\\" he says, still smiling. he\\\'s standing behind me so i have to twist my neck to see him; that makes his smile look even weirder. \\\"would that be OK?\\\" he asks.<br>\r\ni suddenly feel like i am the dalai lama in the movie \\\'kundun\\\': mao is talking to me with his sweet sleazy voice, and i have no way to fight against him even though i feel i am getting screwed.<br>\r\n\\\"yeah, yeah, just do that,\\\" i say, wishing that he could just leave me alone.<br>\r\nwhen he\\\'s gone, i am relieved and overexcited.<br>\r\n\\\"hey!\\\" i tell the others. \\\"didn\\\'t you find this guy looked like mao! he scared me... did you see kundun? i am scared by chinese since i saw that movie.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"i am chinese,\\\" says john in an apparently normal tone.<br>\r\n\\\"hem...\\\" i say. \\\"i mean i am scared by chinese from... china, you know... i mean over there... with the communist government and everything...\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"yeah, then you should say you\\\'re scared by the chinese <i>government</i>, not by the <i>chinese</i>.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"ah...\\\" i say. \\\"well... yes... that\\\'s rather like what you say.\\\"<br>\r\ni don\\\'t apologize. i didn\\\'t mean to say anything bad about every chinese in the world, including john. if i apologize, he might think i meant something bad.<br>\r\n\\\"oh,\\\" says H, coming to my rescue, \\\"but i thought you were from vietnam...\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"my parents came from china to vietnam,\\\" he answers proudly, \\\"to escape from the war with japan. but i have no vietnamese blood at all.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"you know,\\\" he says, \\\"i don\\\'t like the chinese government either, but as my father says, china needs the communist government. there are four billions chinese. without the communist government, china would get out of control.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"yeah,\\\" i say, \\\"i read somewhere that they use death penalty to make sure the people behave. they kill several thousand people every year, don\\\'t they?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"and china has problems with birth control, right?\\\" H asks.<br>\r\n\\\"yeah,\\\" i add, \\\"they used to kill baby girls. do they still do it?\\\"<br>\r\nhe seems to answer no, i am not sure. i noticed we\\\'ll be out of wine soon; H is fast and i have to catch up or she will get it all. the waiters are nervous now, they want to close. and then something weird happens.\r\n</p><p>\r\nemiko starts to cry. through her tears she talks about us leaving to japan soon, we won\\\'t meet again. we say stuff to comfort her. i kinda find it funny that she cries, it reminds me of high-school girls. i notice the three girls react differently to alcohol: H laughs, satako is sleepy, and emiko cries.<br>\r\n\\\"anyway, you\\\'re gonna be in tokyo,\\\" i say, \\\"and we\\\'ll be in osaka; it\\\'s only three hours away by train! we will meet again, for sure.\\\"<br>\r\nshe won\\\'t stop crying. john is padding her back. he earlier talked about going together to karaoke after that, but it seems out of the question now. we pay the bill. i go to the bathroom. when i am back she is still crying.\r\n</p><p>\r\njust before getting out of the restaurant, emiko turns to me, her face is red. \\\"you know, you should not be laughing at me.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"i am not laughing at you,\\\" i say. \\\"common, there\\\'s no big deal. you have no reason to cry, you\\\'re just sad because of alcohol.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"i am not drunk!\\\" she shouts. \\\"i know this is not your fault, you don\\\'t understand my feelings. that\\\'s because you\\\'re not japanese. japanese people would respect my sadness, they wouldn\\\'t laugh.\\\"<br>\r\nshe\\\'s all red and wet with tears now... i am a bit upset; i already heard that \\\"you\\\'re not japanese\\\" speech and i don\\\'t like it at all.<br>\r\n\\\"you see,\\\" starts john, and i guess he\\\'s gonna say something to calm things down, \\\"you need to learn more about japanese people. and i am sorry you have prejudice against chinese people too.\\\"<br>\r\ni stare at them both, astonished.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe waiters chase us out. outside, the wind blows like crazy. we have to shout, and it\\\'s freezing despite the alcohol\\\'s effect.<br>\r\n\\\"you know,\\\" emiko cries, \\\"it\\\'s really bad that you think these things about chinese people.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"if you say something bad about john,\\\" she says, \\\"i have to protect him, because he will be my husband.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"H, please!\\\" she cries, looking theatrically desperate and pointing her index at me. \\\"please say <i>something</i> to him!\\\"<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nand now it seems everyone is looking at me. i am wrong, all the way. i am the bad guy. i made the girl cry. i am the racist motherfucker. and this stupid freezing wind won\\\'t stop. and there is no way anything can be discussed here.<br>\r\ni don\\\'t know what to say. at some point, i laugh. later, i apologize, shouting. i try to talk directly with him, i want to say \\\"hey, i have nothing against you, nothing against chinese people, let\\\'s talk about it calmly later on, OK?\\\" but she puts herself between us, facing me, just like i were trying to have a fight.<br>\r\neventually i get completely upset. i walk around in circles, talking to myself, to anybody. \\\"it makes no sense!\\\" i shout. \\\"damn, she\\\'s just drunk! how come we\\\'re still here arguing? let\\\'s stop this crazy talking!\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"goodnight!\\\" i shout at them. \\\"goodnight, now!\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nshe\\\'s still crying as they walk to their car.\r\n</p>\r\n(to be continued...)\r\n'),(2789,'\\\"Cruelty occurs when people lose touch with the real world. In this nineties this is partly due to the fact that we are distanced from reality by a life we do not lead, therefore we are also distanced from one another. We experience the world through television and use drugs to enjoy social communication. We use machines instead of our bodies and then when our bodies fail us, machines keep us alive. When we do not feel life, we do not feel what it is to be alive. We do not feel compassion, our neighbour is invisible. When he suffers, we turn the television off or simply register a blank. Existence is a bubble we feel will never burst.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"We can\\\'t decide how to vote because we need a new party. We\\\'ve abandoned our world in favour of ourselves. We cover the ground in broken glass then take off our shoes, we need to look again. Unemployment is the final insult to the individual, mass production was the first. Our education system is wrong, it takes no note of the subtleties of human nature, it places more importance on the memory of an individual than how memorable an individual is. It does not nurture talent, but rewards those who obey and allows them entrance to an exclusive club. This is wrong. The best are wasted. This is why society is disintegrating.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Can\\\'t you see the spelling doesn\\\'t matter, because we are not saying anything any more, it\\\'s called the power of silence.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n-Bernard Summer(singer in classic british band New Order), sometime during 1996'),(2790,'<p>I have a billion things to do. Literally. I counted. Why did I count? So I can procrastinate a bit more. Does anyone else out there make lists (color coded with complicated symbols that I spent hours working out a key for) to procrastinate? I know I do. I have spent too much time erasing and rewriting on my white board this morning. Does it really need to have perfectly straight and aligned text? Yes. Because that way I can put off cleaning the bathroom for another ten minutes. Crossing things off one\\\'s list feels really good. Unless you are me. Hey, that line wasn\\\'t straight. Perhaps if I just erase and rewrite a few times and where is my ruler dammit I can\\\'t find the red pen, I can only cross things off with the red pen! I have the neatest to-do lists ever. To bad I never actually DO anything on them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am the procrastination queen. I am leaving in 24hrs to fly across the country, haven\\\'t packed or done laundry in a week, need to pack three bags for the kids for a week too, and have at least 3 days worth of errands to run today. So what\\\'s the first thing I do? Sit at the computer and check my email. Then check irc. Then all my friends journals. Then update my journal. And oh look, in that time I got new email that I just HAVE to respond to asap, and gee, isn\\\'t it time I cleaned out my inbox and before I know it it\\\'s noon and I am still in my jammies with two hungry children and a tormented cat. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I would rather do anything than what I am supposed to do. It doesn\\\'t matter if it\\\'s something I usually enjoy doing, if I have a deadline for getting it done, it becomes the most detestable task ever. Today I need to shop. Pick up some makeup that I am out of and a few essential items for my trip. Normally I love shopping. Today I equate it with putting rabid weasels in my pants. In fact I think I might need to do that first, anyone know where I can get a few rabid weasels? Better add that to my to-do list. Now where did I put the purple pen? Purple is for weasel related items.</p>'),(2791,'<p>there is a space in between dying and being reborn.  a waiting-time.  the part of your breath that is neither breathing in nor breathing out.  silence.</p>\r\n<p>i had the oddest but most profound moment on the bus this morning: i thought of dying, and what it will be like (maybe the white tunnel of light, maybe the candle\'s flicker, maybe velvet blackness, maybe who knows?).  i had a sudden moment of looking forward to death, not in a morbid or life-denying sense, but in this sense: someday we will unshoulder the burdens of being alive.  no matter what we have achieved or failed to do during our lives, we each get to go.  and i felt a little spurt of joy in my chest, where nothing much has happened for a while, at the thought of the ultimate freedom.  freedom from everything, even myself!</p>\r\n<p>it\'s true, all the new-agey stuff about angels and visions gets on my nerves, but there is always a valuable lesson somewhere in everything that annoys me.  one thing i like in the new age repetoire is the semi-self-contradictory practice (being self-contradicting in that it is usually materialistic, under the auspices of a spiritual mindset; this doesn\'t bother me but i think it\'s why i don\'t do it) of listing what you want and sticking the lists on your bathroom mirror, your car steering wheel, your computer desktop, and so on.  a superfly neo-pagan chiropractor i know does this, and swears it helps her create her own reality and fulfill her destiny and all of that.  i think it\'s great.  i rarely think about what it is i want.  i am suspicious of what i want.  but today, in light of my little happy death moment, i am thinking about what i want, or what i would want, as karen the voodoo chiropractor puts it, if abundance were abounding, if the stars themselves were in my grasp.  i don\'t know if i\'ve ever really given myself permission to think about what i would want under those circumstances.</p>\r\n<p>here\'s what i think i want:<br>\r\n-fresh fruit and vegetables, washed and prepared in an aesthetically pleasing way (by me or someone else, doesn\'t matter), every day.<br>\r\n-personal training every day like the stars have.  with massages. <br>\r\n-a house with a view of the ocean. and a large hot tub outside, also with said ocean view. and a sauna. and a yoga studio.  and an art studio, and a huge kitchen. all overlooking the ocean, of course.<br> \r\n-to be a yoga teacher.<br>\r\n-a really nice computer that feels good to use.<br>\r\n-all the music i want to hear, all the time<br>\r\n-lots of free time to take naps and dream.<br>\r\n-work that excites my spirit, engages my intellect, and gets my body moving.<br>\r\n-access to the great outdoors.  maybe this means a car.  a jeep? maybe this means not living in the city.  <br>\r\n-time and materials to build the art projects and record the songs that come to me while engaged in previously mentioned daydreaming and work.<br>\r\n-notoriety sufficient to make an alternative worldview immediate and palatable to the general american public--i.e. the power to make people stop and think about trashing their bodies, trashing the environment, consuming trash media and trash art.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as i type this, i\'m thinking about all the things i already have, material and circumstantial--colorful, riotous, lovely providential comfort of things.  it seems like the wants i have are not that farfetched, not that grandiose, not even that far off.  five years? ten?  okay, maybe not the yoga studio overlooking the ocean. or the personal trainer.  but everything else seems within reach.  maybe i\'m not thinking big enough.  other things i want:  to be a doctor of medicine.  to go to greece, often and with many flowing white outfits.  to write novels akin to those of f. scott fitzgerald in his prime, rife with symbolism and beautifully formed sentences.  to have the time to deeply understand the decisions made by politicians and filmmakers. to love honestly, to cultivate the ability to give love unconditionally.  o furious! o confine me not!  that is one of my favorite walt whitman poems, <a href=\"http://www.bartleby.com/142/22.html\">the one about madness and joy</a>.  i like \"to plant on you for the first time the lips of a determin\'d man!\"  oh, yes, i forgot to add that to my list: a determin\'d man.  planting determin\'d lips on me, planting potatoes and carrots in the garden behind the house overlooking the ocean.  squash and beans.  homemade stew in the winter.  fresh bread.  we both will chop the wood!</p>  \r\n<p>what do you want?</p> '),(2792,'<center>\r\n<table width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"100%\\\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td width=\\\"100%\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\" align=\\\"left\\\">\r\n<SPAN STYLE=\\\"font-family: arial; font-size: 10pt;\\\">\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2792_1.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"right\\\">i am still locked in the gaze of the stars and the nothing of the dark. there are no constellations for me to recognize, and i begin to make my own, assign each a meaning from my new personal astrology. i will a group of stars to form the shape of the ship i am in, and search intently within them in hopes of finding myself reflected in the ship i have become.\r\n\r\n<p>no moments pass. i am staring outward. i hear something that i haven\\\'t heard in what feels like a lifetime.  it\\\'s a voice, and it belongs to a woman.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"are you okay?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>i feel a breeze on my cheek, something foreign pushing against my legs, back, and head. my body is mine again. i blink, and my eyes hurt from it. i am laying on ground. the porthole of my ship, my eye, is gone, and i am bare and confronting a night sky. more breeze.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"are you okay?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>a hand on my shoulder. warm through my suit. <i>i don\\\'t know</i>, i say in my head, but my mouth can\\\'t manage the words.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"you have to get up. help is coming. we\\\'ll be fine.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>her voice is muddied. my focus dissolves from it and i\\\'m now very aware of swaying plant life nearby, moving with the same breeze that keeps breathing warmly on my cheek. we are in a clearing of some sort, but everything is too dark to make out detail. i am laying on a thick blanket of what feels like moss. it creeps out beneath me into the dark to meet a line of vegetation that i cannot see.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"where is the ship?\\\" i ask, quietly, slowly.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"we had to make an emergency landing,\\\" she says, her voice becoming more clear, soft and calming. she sounds young. \\\"the auto pilot forced us to land. it gave us instructions to get a few hundred meters away from the ship. we carried you here, and the others like you.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"like me?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"catatonic. you wouldn\\\'t move. your eyes were open, but you wouldn\\\'t stop staring at the sky. you still are.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>i am. i bring my gaze down again, and it\\\'s too dark to see her face. i am laying down, she is kneeling beside me, her hand still on my shoulder.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"there is a settlement nearby. we were routed here by the auto pilot. they know we\\\'ve landed, and they\\\'re on their way to pick us up.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2792_2.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"left\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2792_4.gif\\\" width=\\\"4\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" align=\\\"left\\\">i hear footsteps, softened by the lush moss that cradles my back. they come closer. i hear some voices, and i try to sit up. although it\\\'s too dark to see much, i feel like i\\\'m blacking out from rising too quickly. the voices shimmer from my ears, then come back together.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"put this on him,\\\" says a male voice, deep, not familiar in any sort of tone i\\\'ve ever heard. \\\"you\\\'ll have to wear one too,\\\" it adds.\r\n\r\n<p>his dark hands pass something to her silhouette, now standing above me. i gather that he\\\'s from the settlement, and he\\\'s arrived with some sort of vehicle. she seems hesitant, her shadow gesturing questions. he calms her, and she becomes silent, and stands still for a few moments. he leaves to address the other passengers that are strewn about, awaiting instruction.\r\n\r\n<p>she walks to me and kneels down. the wind rushes heavily through the leaves, making me think that they are big. of course i\\\'ve seen plants before, but i\\\'ve never <i>felt</i> this many, just beyond my sight in this darkness. moments pass slowly, because i have so much to take in. i look to her face and can only make out vague details - a kind mouth, long hair, not much else.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"we have to wear these,\\\" she says, matter of factly.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"what?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>she takes my hand and turns it over to face palm-up. i briefly enjoy the touch of her fingers, before she places something wetly organic and dense into my hand. it feels about the weight of a good throwing rock. but it\\\'s wet. and it\\\'s moving.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2792_3.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"right\\\">it\\\'s large enough to span the width of my palm. i feel very uncomfortable. it feels like it\\\'s trying to push it\\\'s way into my palm, under my skin. it\\\'s looking for something. i freeze and she watches calmly. she holds up her right hand to me and with the other, flicks on a finger light. it looks like a slug is attached to the top of her hand, burrowed in to the fresh IV holes that the ship used to feed us nutrients. it\\\'s stretched from the top of her hand to just past her wrist, but has flattened itself down considerably, so that it appears to be more of a dark and slimy mound of skin.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"what is it?\\\" is all i can manage to ask. it pokes greedily into my palm, an over zealous tongue.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"the settler told me that it is a \\\'transitional organism\\\', and that we\\\'d need them to survive the first few days here.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"where are we?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"i don\\\'t know.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>i used to inject a lot of things into my body.  i never really liked the act of injection, but the habit grew me an ability for tolerance, so i draw on that, and decide to trust her.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"what do i do?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>she tells me, and i comply. i pluck the slug from my palm and turn my hand over. i place it on the back of my hand, and it quickly finds the IV holes, tiny as they are. i watch my hand held up in front of my chest. i mentally  detach it from my body for the moment. habit. the organism pokes through my skin, wriggling with effort, pushing with its other end against the top of my wrist for leverage. it does not hurt, but i do not feel comfortable.\r\n\r\n<p>warmth absorbs into my hand, wet, quickly up my arm and into my shoulder, surrounding my neck, then into my face. i feel like i\\\'m sweating, but i\\\'m not sure that i am. i watch the slug begin to flatten out on my hand, dark and foreign. i ask the woman to turn off the light, and she does.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"hello,\\\" i hear.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"hello,\\\" i say to the woman. she looks at me oddly for a moment, and then recognition relaxes her face.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"yes, mine\\\'s talking to me too,\\\" she says, placing a gentle finger on top of her hand where the slug is.\r\n\r\n</span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</center>\r\n'),(2794,'<p STYLE=\\\"font-family:verdana; font-size:12;\\\">\r\nI have difficulty differentiating between shades of red and green.<br>\r\nConical imperfections labor my existence in a plane of hexadecimal colors.\r\n</p>\r\n<p STYLE=\\\"font-family:verdana; font-size:12;\\\">\r\nDo you and I see alike is the tireless question<br>\r\nTransposed into the existential key of B flat minor.\r\n</p>\r\n<p STYLE=\\\"font-family:verdana; font-size:12;\\\">\r\nAs introspective folk songs sung with melancholic British voices sound around me<br>\r\nWittgenstein uses his pink logic to shove me backwards over a kneeling Descartes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p STYLE=\\\"font-family:verdana; font-size:12;\\\">\r\nA megahertz mafia holds me at bay<br>\r\nProdding me for answers I cannot give. \r\n</p>'),(2793,'<p>\r\ni walk downstairs hoping for my first glimpse of you of the day, as usual. there you are. i walk by you and go over to brian so he can do the shift change thing. brian stays and chats for a bit, starts telling me about a new girl he met. i try to listen because i really like brian, but as always my eyes keep darting across the room to where you are. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nfinally brian leaves and i try to begin my work. at least none of the scheduling girls will be in until 10. i\\\'ve still got a few hours. not that this matters, this is my job. i\\\'m here to work. i have to remember how i was before i got this job, how broke i was, how i was going to have to move back home to nowhere, wisconsin because i couldn\\\'t afford to live. then i got this job. mtv. new york. it saved my life. i\\\'ve gotta keep focusing on that, the big picture and stop letting this one person completely mess me up. </p>\r\n<p>\r\naround 11 or so i go over to your area since i\\\'ve got to do some work over there. you start asking how my memorial day was. you\\\'re still using that \\\"cover up\\\" tone. the same one i use with you. we both hide. we both act completely ourselves with everyone else there, but put on our fronts when speaking. we\\\'re covering up what we both know. i want you. you knows i want you. i\\\'ve never told you to your face. you\\\'ve never brought it up to my face. so we do this cover up through smart ass remarks, wisecracks, and continual attitudes. today my attitude toward you is worse. i just can\\\'t be nice. but you just can\\\'t be normal either. \r\n<p>\r\nas i continue my work jen comes in and i catch bits and pieces of a conversation. apparently a bunch of them went to fire island over memorial day. i try not to listen to conversations here, since sometimes you overhear things you\\\'d rather not. eventually they all come down. here comes alison , anorexic alison who everyone had to point out to me he had/has a crush on last year. her face isn\\\'t all that, but the anorexic body makes up for it. sometimes i think guys are full of shit when they say they don\\\'t like \\\"super skinny\\\" girls, because i\\\'ve never once seen a super skinny girl not get a guy because he \\\"wasn\\\'t attracted to her\\\". ok. it\\\'s time for me to go on my break before i drive myself crazy with these thoughts and paranoias.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlater in the afternoon i\\\'m doing more work near you and i see lisa come over. lisa is another bony seahorse girl. she\\\'s gorgeous. guys at work have dated lisa and not been impressed with her personality, but to me it\\\'s glaringly obvious with the way she looks she doesn\\\'t need to even bother developing a personality. she just has to sit there and guys will fall all over her. again, i try not to listen to you conversation.. but it\\\'s literally impossible. i catch bits and pieces i try to block out so i don\\\'t have to know and i still end up hearing something that sounds like her asking you to go somewhere. still, i\\\'m famous for jumping to conclusions and making things up in my mind. so i try to not hear. </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni manage to avoid direct contact the rest of the day until i once again can\\\'t help overhearing you saying you have to leave work early now and something about \\\"taking her somewhere\\\". my mind immediately goes back to the conversation with lisa and now my head is spinning around. i\\\'m going nuts. i\\\'m mad at myself for letting this guy do this to me. a couple minutes later you ask me a question and as usual i all but bite your head off. i hear you telling denise about how every time you ask me something i\\\'ll get an attitude. i mean, duh. come on. you\\\'ve known for awhile now what someone \\\"clued you in\\\" to, and nothing has happened aside from the fact you started being bothersome in a pain in the ass kind of way at work, stopped being normal with me, teased me about everything for awhile. then the night we were both drunk at the bar with everyone and i got up in your face telling you why i wanted to get so drunk, and continually shoving my hand in your face in a \\\"go away\\\" gesture because i didn\\\'t want to be around you to feel how much i want you. and then you insisted on coming to the diner with denise and i and leaving the bar which is rare. and how i knew i\\\'d flat out do you that night if you asked. but of course, with the way my go nowhere slow ass moving life is, denise just dropped the both of us off at our cars and we went home. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nso then i assume after that you can\\\'t possibly not know now, since i all but admitted it that night. and do you know how hard it is to work 12 hour days directly around someone you\\\'ve wanted so bad, for so long, and don\\\'t have? and how i thought it would all be ok as long as you somehow knew. if you knew, and you weren\\\'t interested. fine i said. i told denise it would be fine then it\\\'s cool, just as long as you knew and you could decide what to do about it. what a load of crap that was. it just made me want you more. because your signals are as mixed as mine. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>so work ends and i race out hoping to not see you going wherever you\\\'re going with lisa. i still don\\\'t even know if this is true but from more overheard conversations it seems to be. my rational mind tells me that this is most likely nothing, and reminds me of the one hundred billion times with one hundred billion guys in the past i\\\'ve jumped to the complete wrong conclusions with. i\\\'m famous for paranoia. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nso i get home and decide to spill all this nonsense out on a website where no one in my real life knows i write on because i\\\'ve completely fallen for you, rob, and it\\\'s driving me crazy. </p>\r\n\r\n'),(2795,'<p><i>\\\"Be not forgetful to entertain strangers, for thereby some have entertained angels unawares.\\\" -Hebrews 13:2: </i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>A picture followed me today at San Pablo Flea Market in Oakland. I brought Juniper there to collect stuffed animals for our artworks. She uses the skin, I use the meat. </p>\r\n<p> I like to shop around the used media area, look through old family photographs and feel nostalgia for the lives of people I don\\\'t know. Gerhard Richter made a series of artworks out of photographs he found on the street, often with intentionally beheaded figures. They always look to me like pictures of lovers. Why else would you cut up a photograph?</p>\r\n<p>Pearl leapt into my hands. I know her name is Pearl because on the back of the photograph in blue ink was written, \\\"Dear Jr I Love you! your Pearl.\\\" The picture wanted me; I moved my hand toward another and Pearl stuck herself to my hand instead. I stared into her eyes, into the gap between her teeth, at her white dress. Who is Jr? Her son or her lover? Is it junior, or J.R.? Why would she call her lover \\\"junior\\\"? Everyone called my father \\\"junior\\\", including his sister and his first wife I guess.</p>\r\n<p>Oh junior, she is \\\"your Pearl\\\". yours. And here is an idealized and frozen piece of evidence, forever excavatable by the future historians of desire. But you have given her up to the anonimity of this art scrap mound, placed her immortal love in the same class as someone\\\'s old soccer team pics or a casual snapshot of a barbecue noone remembers. What did she do to deserve such a fate? Or is it me who is projecting some kind of importance on Pearl\\\'s expressed love in a world where people give love away and throw love away with the flippancy usually reserved for ripped gym socks?</p>\r\n<p>When I put the photograph back (I wanted it but I have been trying to minimize my crapload), turned around to walk out the door, she lept again, this time at my ankle. \\\"You dropped your pearl,\\\" juniper told me.</p>\r\n<p>I would not abandon her. I scooped her up lovingly and brought her with the stuffies to the man who was cashing. He squinted at the photo of Pearl and said, \\\"prrretty!!\\\" Then squinted back at me and held the photo up to my face, to suggest that Pearl and I bore a resemblance. \\\"close\\\", he commented.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"She\\\'s Chinese, I\\\'m Thai. But the Thai are very Chinese. people always ask my cousins, \\\'why you look like the chinese people??\\\' \\\"</p>\r\n<p> He cocked his head and shook it at me playfully, \\\"We ALL mixed up in here. What color your blood?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"In Malaysia there is a red coral very common to its reefs that is supposedly the blood of the goddess. When you look at it you are supposed to remember that we are all kin. That we come from the sea and will return. now that I think about it, maybe only those of us with red blood are kin.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>He began to reply a little more quickly and exuberantly than my attention span could match. It was early in the morning and I had been drinking. I\\\'m still a bit haggard at press time. \\\"We ALL connected like that, not just the animals but the plants and all too. You know, you are what you eat. and you are what eats you. Like, the Lion, if the lion eat you, the Lion The Man. we all belong to the food chain together.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Do lions eat people?\\\" I honestly had to ask myself this question.</p>\r\n<P>\\\"heh HYEeeeeah shit blessed it dont!! blessed you! blessed you dont be ate by a lion!\\\"</p>\r\n<p>He tried to explain to us that everything that goes into our bodies and comes out of our bodies connects us to the greater spirit, or WHATEVER YOU CALL IT. That if you \\\"stay natural\\\" you will be closer to these energies, but if you pollute yourself with toxins you will be estranged from them. he was blabbing as I tuned out at one point when Juniper replied, \\\"oh yeah we shouldnt a drank that bourbon last night.\\\"</p>\r\n<p> I had a Kazahkstani lady doctor in my thai class who gave me a similar schpiel. She said that when you take alcohol you block your head\\\'s reception to angels and magic. She wanted to reassure me that she was only using the word \\\"angels\\\" to describe WHATEVER IT IS, the force, or the god, or whatever is the magic that you need in your head. She wanted to reassure me that angels are real.</p>\r\n<p> I blocked my head from the angels because it was Ashley\\\'s buddy Mike\\\'s last night in town. Mike came over from upstate New York and renewed the joy in our household, mostly by yelling out soul tunes nonstop in our presence. Last night, drunkenly meditating on the name \\\"Mike\\\", I recalled the archangel Michael and his little bare white ass joyfully plucking at harps in the breeze. I recalled thinking that Bugs Bunny was hot. and then Lola tried to hump Juniper.</p>\r\n<p>And when Lola (a female dog) mounted, licked and wriggled her hips rhythmically against juniper<a female human)\\\'s thigh, I hopped up to Jay\\\'s library to remember this passage from Salinger\\\'s <i>Franny and Zooey</i>:</p>\r\n<p>\\\"All legitimate religious study <i>must</i> lead to unlearning the differences, the illusory differences, between boys and girls, animals and stones, day and night, heat and cold.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>After repenting extensively about the bourbon, we made our gesture to leave and our guest speaker broke his lesson, \\\"Hey, when\\\'s the next bourbon party, I\\\'ll come to that.....shit, I\\\'ll even buy it. so dont forget to invite me.\\\"\r\n<p>\\\"sure thing\\\" We made for the doorway.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"what\\\'s my name?\\\" he wanted us to remember him.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"huh? what\\\'s your name?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Mike.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>laughter. </p>'),(2796,'<P>It was bitterly cold that winter.  Cold and very dry.  The trees by the road in front of Marisol\\\'s house stayed white with the dust raised by passing cars.  Large black ants invaded the bathroom in search of water. They congregated under the rim of the toilet bowl and had to be flushed away each time before sitting down.  Once, post-flush, an ant swam back up through the hole to the surface of the water.  \\\"That\\\'s one tough son of a bitch,\\\" he thought.  \\\"I might let him live.\\\"</P><P>They lived out in La Colonia among retirees from America and Europe and South Africa.  Marisol\\\'s house was built around a courtyard with a garden in back.  She owned a macrobiotic restaurant in San Miguel.  In the mornings she would go into town early, leaving him in bed.  He\\\'d get up around nine and smoke and read, lounging in a chair in the courtyard under the eaves, wrapped in a blanket.  He smoked Montana cigarrettes, a Mexican product that tasted richer than American-made smokes.  He was a kept man, not ashamed to admit it.</P><P>Marisol had a large library, including everything publicly available that Hemingway ever wrote.  Novels, stories, memoir, reportage, and letters.  He fell in love with Hemingway that winter, fell in love with books altogether.  He decided he would be a writer.  As a college freshman, his heart was set on becoming an investment banker.  That was before a hippie girlfriend introduced him to acid, and the jock persona he cultivated in high school crumbled away.  As he read, it seemed like the birds in the jacaranda trees and cactus were singing in counterpoint to the prose.  Reading was a joy.</P><P>Marisol was an inveterate packrat.  Every drawer was a junk drawer.  She would make inscrutable things, crafty-type projects.  One room was half-full of wooden palm trees, as if she were planning a production of \\\"South Pacific.\\\"  He contemplated having a courtyard sale while she was away.</P><P>After the dinner rush at the restaurant was over, Marisol would bring her three year-old son Ivan home and put him to bed.  They would have a late supper and sometimes Tony Fuzz and his wife Mary came over.  They lived next door.  Tony was so nicknamed because he was so hairy.  The Fuzzes were from the Bronx.  After a couple of drinks, Tony would ask Mary if she wanted to go into town.  \\\"I never lost anything in town,\\\" she\\\'d say.</P><P>Sometimes Mary would go home and he\\\'d accompany Tony into San Miguel.  Tony drove one-handed.  \\\"In the states, this would be called drunk driving,\\\" he\\\'d say.  \\\"Here, we\\\'re just drunk and in the car.  They don\\\'t care.\\\"  They frequented El Gato Negro.  Women didn\\\'t go in there.  There was a urinal in the wall beside the bar.  There were pictures of Marilyn Monroe pasted around the mirror behind the bar.  When you ordered tequila, it was served in large brandy snifters.  It was a bar for cowboys from the countryside and workers from the sandal factory in town.  \\\"This is what you call \\\'Friends in Low Places,\\\' huh,\\\" Tony would say.</P><P>The restaurant was closed on Sundays.  Marisol amused herself with her crafts while he taught Ivan to curse in Spanish and English.  When spring came, they went to the bullfight and the illuminated parade of saints.  They joined pilgrims from all over Mexico for Easter mass in the cathedral.  Shortly after that, they had an amicable breakup and he went back to Florida.</P>'),(2798,'<P>the bottom line is i need to stick with taking my medication. but there are so many caveats. and i hate the word caveat and i hate medication. \r\n</P><P>\r\nit seems as though my life revolves around serotonin reuptake inhibitors these days. sometime after i turned 17 i started having up and down mood swings very rapidly but i never questioned it. i lived in a pretty volatile household where one day everyone was in hugs and then other times, and no one knew why or talked about it, there would be yelling and throwing things and crying. no sorry or mention afterwards, you just moved on to the next day and whatever emotion flooded the hallways in the house then.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni started taking medication a few years ago (late college) after years of being in wild stress mode with bouts of massive depression made not even slightly better by confusing tantrums and slight paranoia about everything.  and now here i am, sedate on my medication track.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nand now it\\\'s as if i sort of miss days of having swings of different, horrible-maybe-terrific-maybe-horrible-maybe-terrific feelings because, on this medication, i feel nothing. im so worried someone is going to die and i wont be able to cry because i will be in this robotic coma that the medication has given me.  and, yes, i have been on other medications but they have made me feel terrible and nauseous. and although this isnt the greatest medication, i havent had days of wanting to throw myself off a tall building followed two minutes later by me wanting to smile and eat ice cream with a puppy followed by wanting to tear out my eyes, etc.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand did i mention the effect medication has on libido?  \r\n</P><P>\r\nso i played around with my pills.  i decided to take them but only maybe once a week instead of once everyday. and this is terrible because once your body hits withdrawl (well my body i know does this), i feel achingly sick and dizzy and my brain feel like it is overprocessing everything i look at.  BUT when i start to get the medication out of my system - voila - there\\\'s some semblence of my sex drive. but, again, the withdrawl makes me woozy.  so i start taking the medication the next day. and then i notice the next day is euphoric - i feel terrific. and a couple days later im still feeling good. and then i dont take my medication then i start to get off of it and get back on, etc.  there is no real inbetween because this whole process is a bit of a trap.  if i take the medication everyday, i dont have many feelings but at least im not deeply depressed one minute and totally excited and nonstop chatty, rampling, nervous, anxious the next. if i dont take it, i am a mess of emotions that i cant control or understand. and i say things i dont want to say because they feel right at the time when i am in the height of an anger swing.  if i take the medication every couple of days i get a brief wave of my old self back for a day then it\\\'s back to the robot/medicated version of me. \r\n</P><P>\r\ntoday i am definitely in a mode that reminds me that i should seriously start taking my medication regularly. but once im back on it, i start thinking about how i shouldnt be on it.  c\\\'est la vie, je suppose - when my hair is long i want it short and then it\\\'s short and i want it long. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2799,'<P>what i write means nothing.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthere are people dying. do you understand? what have i done for them? & what have i done for the millions of girls & boys being raped, beaten, abandoned?\r\ni feel a responsibility to the entire world. i feel worthless.\r\nwhat i write means nothing, but i will change all that.\r\ni will print out my poems in tiny booklets tied with string. i will staple stories to town hall bulletin boards. i will make people angry. i will force them to listen. i will play beau sia & charles bukowski recordings at top volume outside of nurseries & when the workers ask me what i am doing i will say, \\\"i am teaching them about the world.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\ni will hand out fliers at concerts & high schools inviting people i\\\'ve never met to poetry readings in the city. no, i will start my own readings. we will begin anew in the suburbs with out small town elite. i will trespass. i will break laws. i will reach someone.\r\n</P><P>\r\n& at the bottom of it all, my name in size 8 font. pressing my existence, but asking quietly to be left alone. \r\n</P>'),(2800,'<p><font face=\\\"verdana\\\" size=\\\"2\\\">next week i will board a plane for denver, where i will attend the 22nd annual national barbie doll convention - rocky mountain mod.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen i tell people i am going to this event, where 1200 or so barbie doll collectors will hang out in a five star hotel for 5 days, they are often alarmed and/or amused.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m not a barbie doll collector.  i have a half dozen or so vintage dolls that are reminders of my childhood, buying dolls is not the reason i will spend more than i can afford to attend.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere will be a fashion show, in which many of them will dress in costumes that replicate barbie doll fashions from the mod era.  go go boots and op art and lime green and hot pink and twiggy and hippy dippy laugh in judy carne goldie hawn peace and love.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmost of the women i will see this week are middle aged, married, and slightly overweight.  their hair is grey or dyed to cover it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsome of the attendees will be male.  husbands or sons of the collectors. gay men abound.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere will be many more lesbians than anyone suspects. <br><br>\r\n\r\nmost are well educated, many are extremely successful in their chosen professions, or recently retired. <br><br>\r\n\r\nquite a few are artists.  needle artists who create fabulously intricate fashions in miniature, makeup artists who repaint and reroot the hair of the 11-1/2 inch plastic princess to make her look less like a barbie doll and more like something else entirely.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsome are curators of barbie doll history.  they collect pristine dolls forty years old in mint packaging and preserve them for posterity.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsome are business women who man the sales tables with carefully selected merchandise that they will make large profits from, profits that will allow them to stay in business another year.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhalf of those attending will have healthy discretionary incomes, the other half will have, like me, living on the edge of their checkbooks and credit cards in order to afford the $1600 or so dollars the week will cost them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmore than a few will be in wheelchairs or carrying canes.  most will have some sort of medical crisis to report since the last gathering.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey will tell me of their children, their grandchildren, their jobs, their hopes, their fears, the places they have been and the people they have seen.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe will share the sorrows of their lives, the deaths of their parents perhaps, or the loss of a child. this year we will hear from those who lost someone in the twin towers or in an airplane that fell to terrorists.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe theme this year will remind us of a time in history we share. vietnam.  some will be wearing their pow and mia bracelets.  <br><br>\r\n\r\ni expect we will remember those times when we were young and stood in lines of thousands, holding hands and singing about overcoming.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nremembering classes on how to be as heavy as possible when dragged through the streets by policemen.<br><br>\r\n\r\n remembering when we changed the world with the courage of our convictions.  we are strong, we are invincible, we are women.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthey will wander through the hotel wearing badges proclaiming their names and perhaps their email addresses, badges noting the name of the doll email groups to which they belong. <br><br>\r\n\r\nsome will drink to excess and most will laugh with great abandon more this week than any other week of this year in their lives.  loaded brownies will be consumed.  tokes will be smoked by those who have figured out how to get their stashes past airport security.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s about barbie dolls, but it isn\\\'t really.  it\\\'s about women.  women who find joy in the company of their peers and need an excuse to congregate with those who share common interests.  it\\\'s an affirmation of our value to ourselves and each other.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m not a barbie doll collector.  i\\\'m a people collector, and so are they.<br><br>\r\n</font></p>'),(2801,'This is not done by choice,<br>\r\nthis is not a decision I have made--<br>\r\nthis may not even be my real voice.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s hard to find the source--<br>\r\nand without it, to go on parade.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nTo take it all downtown, one more time.<p>\r\nYou may not understand this,<br>\r\nthis is not a sensible, safe thing,<br>\r\nmy arrow flying at you might miss--<br>\r\nas you remain unaware, hear only the hiss--<br>\r\nit may only make the air sing.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nIt may only make you turn your head. \r\n<p>\r\nI had a dream and you were in it,<br>\r\nyou were there for just a minute.<br>\r\nYou said hello--<br>\r\nyou had to go--<br>\r\n<p>\r\nYou were thin, and the beauty of your face frightened me.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nI am haunted by the ghosts of living people<br>\r\nI\\\'ve never met and never will.<br>\r\nYou yourself have appeared to me a dozen times,<br> \r\n<p>\r\nand still...\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nmy only defense is to come here, and leave these rhymes--<br>\r\na peace offering, to the gods of sleep.<br>\r\nHere they are, take them, I lay them at your feet. \r\n \r\n'),(2802,'<p><font face=\\\"arial\\\" size=\\\"2\\\">I can\\\'t deal with it anymore, it isn\\\'t something that can be talked about with ease. If I don\\\'t say something soon though I\\\'m going to burst. I feel like shit.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor the first time in my life I had to hire a person at work. 300+ resumes slapped down on my desk and I\\\'m told to find the best person, using intuition and matching up skill sets interviews are setup. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThree days later the interviews are done and the I\\\'ve made my selections. It is only at this point that the organization or lack of organization in which I work decides to tell me there are identity requirements. Now because someone else failed to ensure I was given the aproppriate information, I got to call some one back and back pedal on a job offer with a question of this sort \\\"Can you answer yes to the following, do you belong to any of the following groups...\\\" the answer that comes from the other end of the line is no. I can feel the frustration through the phone line, I apologize and say good bye. Thirty seconds later the phone rings and a frantic voice pleads with me \\\"What about sexual preference or identity? I\\\'m bisexual.\\\" I explain that it has to be something that can be classified as visible.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor me the next step was to go to my next choice which luckily actually filled the identity requirements. Should have been easy enough, right? How naive am I? It was a disability, a neural disorder that impacted muscle control causing a unique gait, that met the requirements. I made the mistake, after having to call my first choice back and withdraw the job offer, of running this new choice by those above me. My boss said she didn\\\'t feel that the new candidate would be capable enough. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI need this job for a few more months at least but everyday I\\\'m reminded of all this as the new employee comes buzzing by my office asking me for different tasks. I work in an organizational culture that is mired in identity politics, everything you say is not just representative of yourself but all your identifier groups aswell. Don\\\'t give credence to identity politics and your publicly labelled by others as prejudice, give it credence and become part of an organization culture that privately practices discrimination.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThe mandate of the organization I work in - socially progressive media production.</font></p>          '),(2803,'<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nsarah called me today.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nwhen i picked up the phone, she said \\\"hi.\\\" then, \\\"where are you?\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand i laughed a little bit and i said, \\\"here in the apartment, why?\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand she said, voice meek and shaking, \\\"have you heard anything?\\\" she started her next sentence with \\\"stoolie,\\\" and before she even told me, i knew that he was dead. i have come to always expect the worst.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\npeople around me have been doing this for the past three years. dying.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nnow, i expect it.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nmy father died two and a half years ago, during the winter of two thousand. right after christmas and the new year. he had a heart attack while shoveling snow. i preformed cpr on him outside in the driveway while my mother screamed into a telephone, trying to get an ambulance to come to our house.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nlater we sat in a hospital room in schenectady, my mother and i, with the door closed. waiting for word. eventually a priest came and sat with us, tring to make small talk. and all the while, me, i could still taste my father\\\'s cologne on my lips.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\na doctor came in about twenty minutes later. my mother took one look at him, and the look he had on his face, and started sobbing.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nshe said to him, before he could get a word out, \\\"don\\\'t tell me anything bad. i don\\\'t want to hear anything bad.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nthat summer, myron died. he was found floating in a river.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand still, there\\\'s no explanation. no one knows what happened. he had dropped off of our collective radar a couple of weeks earlier and then, he was dead.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nhis funeral was like a reunion. it was the first time in a long while that i saw a good majority of my friends that i knew in high school. it was also the first time that i truly felt like a grown-up.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\na year later a psychic told me that myron had killed himself. she told me that although the idea was pretty somber, it was all for the best. she said that he was happier now, and that i should be happy for him. take that for whatever it\\\'s worth.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand stoolie. he had an asthma attack a week ago and had been in a coma until today. sarah said that, up until now, everyone was \\\"hoping the best, but expecting the worst.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni\\\'m trying to figure out the feasibility of making it out to new york by this weekend. i would like to be out there for the funeral. but, i don\\\'t know if i\\\'m going to be able to do it. i don\\\'t know.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut i have come to expect it.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni have come to expect it because this is something that is possible. that your friends, your family-- the people that you love, all of them-- one day, will die. good people, people that deserve to live, they will die because that\\\'s what happens. and there\\\'s nothing you can do.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nthere\\\'s nothing you can do but love them, as much as you possibly can, and hope that it will happen further in the future rather than now, or tomorrow.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\na month ago i was laying in bed with malinda. on my back, with her head on my chest and her arms around me. and she was sleeping quietly, inhaling and exhaling shallowly. and me, i was wide awake. my arms around her, me staring at the ceiling.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand in my head i could see us as an old couple. us, laying in bed, like we were then. her with white hair. both of us wrinkled.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand she had cancer. i just knew-- in my head, in the scenario that was there-- that she had cancer. and i was laying, holding her, trying to fend off the disease. hoping that my love would keep her alive.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand i\\\'m not really sure what that means.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni\\\'m not sure. but it was the most vivid thing that i have experienced in a while.</p>'),(2804,'<p>I have a double life.</p>\r\n<p>Monday through Friday of last week, I was a hard-working professional journalist. Career-driven. Clean-cut. Interacting with the public in very traditional, very accepted, very mundane settings. School district offices. City halls. Job training centers. I talk to people, usually members of some government establishment. I write about it. It goes in the paper. You think people read it, and maybe it makes a difference. But mostly you have no idea.</p>\r\n<p>Saturday, I was a slutty schoolgirl with knee-high socks, black lace knickers and a blue hooker wig. I stomped to thumping beats that came dangerously close to breaking the cityâ€™s noise ordinance. I ingested some stuff that the cop I interviewed the other day would not be so happy about. It made me light. Loopy-headed. Very unprofessional. I talked to people, but I didnâ€™t have to write down what they said and worry about how I would translate it into readable prose. I just got to listen. This same thing happened last week. Five days of work in boring office, two days of traipsing around the woods; swimming naked in lakes; flailing to psytrance.</p>\r\n<p>I am sad and confused, because I just don\\\'t know which life I want. It seems easy â€“ skinnydipping and dancing vs. meetings and interviewing government officials? But itâ€™s not. There are so many fears, so many ways I second-guess myself. So many messages constantly telling me what I <i>should</i> want, or what I <i>should</i> do.</p> \r\n<p>Also, I still havenâ€™t figured out the other thing. Is it all about laziness and hedonism, as the mainstream wants us to believe? Or is it possible there is more there, something that is impossible to find without immersing my whole self into it. Something that requires more than having one foot in one world, and the other in a different one.</p>\r\n<p>I know a lot of people do this double-life leading. And they would probably say itâ€™s very possible to do both and be happy. For a long time I thought that, too, but more and more, Iâ€™m not so sure. Now I am wondering, what is the point? Why should i dress in sarongs and fishnets and dance like i am on the moon when, hours later, i have to give that up for the ugly reality that is on the other side of the door?</p>\r\n<p>At the party Saturday night I met another journalist with an undercover life. It made me so happy. I spent the whole night talking to him, building up to the point when I would ask him how he justifies the double life and whether he would ever shed his day job and put both feet into the other reality.</p>\r\n<p>Alas, he was just as confused as me.</p>'),(2805,'<p>\r\ni\\\'d like to take a moment now to assess the state of afairs in my life. it it troubling to me that i am not happy. very troubling. no matter now perfect everything in my life feels, somehow i just can\\\'t shed this feeling of impending dread. that something will just set the whole deck of cards toppling once more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have a job i (mostly) love. the work is challenging, the environment is fantastic (foosball anyone?). in short, i love working.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have a new girlfriend that i just love being around. she\\\'s smart, funny, fun to be with, makes me happy, beautiful, smart. wow, i just can\\\'t stand not being around her. i think i may have even fallen in *gasp* love with her! really that\\\'s just a whole other story, one i don\\\'t need to get into it here. *swoon*\r\n</p>\r\nthen suddenly one day, we\\\'ll say saturday for arguments sake, it just struck me: i\\\'m not happy. now, it\\\'s still unclear if it\\\'s just a temporary thing or just my desire for something else in general, but i feel like something is missing. I hate being unhappy.\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s as if my mind realized that i was comfortable and it needed to kick into gear and make me worry about something. something ... i don\\\'t even know what it is! before, i could quantify it. it had a name or a face. before, i hated my job (or lack thereof since that was also the case for awhile), now i love it. before, i hated being single, now i\\\'m not single so i can\\\'t scared of being alone.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel so confused. i have the comfort of a good life, the things i thought would get me back on the mental track. i know i don\\\'t have any stresses anymore, but why does it feel like i am stressed to the point of breaking?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2806,'<body text=\\\"#000000\\\" bgcolor=\\\"#FFFFFF\\\">\r\n\r\n<center><img SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2806_1.jpg\\\" BORDER=0 height=52 width=59></center>\r\n\r\n<font face=\\\"Verdana\\\"><font size=-1>\r\n<p>A couple of nights ago I had a very vivid dream. I was back in the house that I grew up in. I remember being in my room and setting up a bunch of computers. I folded some clothes and\r\nput on a T shirt and some boxer shorts that were too tight in the thigh. Then I was in my brother\\\'s room talking to him about our parents.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My father and grandmother were downstairs on a couch. They wanted me to go to church with them. I told them that I did not want to go because I did not believe the same way the people\r\nin the church did. Then I was on the front porch and I saw this girl sitting inside at a table wearing a yellow shirt and a white skirt that came to the knee.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was coming in the front door and she was smiling at me. She jumped up and ran to the door to greet me. I noticed that she did not have a head when she was not looking at me. She was just a body walking until she looked at me and then her head was there. By the time I opened the door she was in front of me. She grabbed me and began to kiss me. She put her hand on my crotch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This was a strange kiss because it felt like she was nibbling on my tongue. I stopped. Again, her head would disappear when she was not looking at me. I noticed a strange mark on her hand. I pulled her hand close and looked at it. It had a burned-like scar of eyes and a \\\"no\\\" symbol. I then asked her if she was a witch. She made a hiss and a screwy face and began to back away. I woke up...</p>\r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#006600\\\">Tease! Witch eyes\r\n<br>Stare a silent moan\r\n<br>Thirsty for a night ride\r\n<br>Inhaling serum, pheromone\r\n<br>Mouth to mouth sensual overload\r\n<br>Slow dancing tongue to tongue\r\n<br>Pang singing lusts melodic tone\r\n<br>Sirens love is a sadistic one\r\n<br>Bitten by an opiate kiss\r\n<br>Wistful looks of rape\r\n<br>Stung by long fingertips\r\n<br>Through lips they penetrate</font></font></font></p>\r\n</center>'),(2807,'<p>At first Iâ€™m not certain what Iâ€™m looking at. Curling strands of spaghetti bathed in white light, floating in zero-gravity. This lasts for a while. Iâ€™m disembodied and coming closer to one of the long filaments. I can feel that it is very large, but nothing else about it makes much sense to me. Itâ€™s thick, with alternating segments of light brown and off-white, and there is something very organic about it, as though it were more like coral and less like a man-made structure.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But as I get closer, and almost get inside of the strand, I realize that it is man-made and sort of like an escalator. We are standing (sort of) on these large platforms of what look like marble. They are massive, so I canâ€™t actually go from one â€˜stepâ€™ to another. I can only sit or stand on this thing. The stone is a cloudy beige-and-white, but Iâ€™m not even sure that itâ€™s stone, because it has some properties of a liquid. It is hard to the touch and completely solid, its edges sharp and perfectly uniform, but the motion of the escalator seems to be frictionless and without parts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I look up at the â€˜skyâ€™ which is past the other vermiforms. It is like a dome or sphere made of layered sheets of this coral-like substance, with slats and openings which let in amazingly bright sunlight and hints of blue sky. The walls of the sphere are slightly translucent, like holding a sea-shell up to the sun. It is also absolutely quiet, except for the sound of wind or a breeze high above.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My platform approaches a kind of plaza where Iâ€™m let off. Itâ€™s hard to tell how large it is â€“ an acre? I really donâ€™t know. There are a few other people there. Most are dressed in long white gowns and some kind of hat or headdress, and no oneâ€™s feet actually touch the floor. Everyone floats a few inches above the ground. Some of them are standing, others are sitting on long chairs eating things from small bowls. Their clothing is stiff and white, and theyâ€™re wearing some kind of white makeup, or maybe their faces have very subtle features â€“ it is hard to make them out, in any case. There are other people, though (slaves?) holding large feathered fans and sort of tending to the floating-ones. These people go barefoot and donâ€™t wear much clothing. Their skin is covered in some kind of dried white mud, and from this I get the impression (Iâ€™m not sure why) that it is taboo to touch them or interfere with their activities in any way. Both the slaves and my peers have a very distant and unreal quality about them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>From the people who are seated, I overhear part of a conversation. A woman says, about something I canâ€™t hear, that â€œitâ€™s the third time Iâ€™ve ever thought that.â€ I donâ€™t know what she means at first, but then it occurs to me that these people have perfectly accurate memories. It is obvious to me that they can remember every thought and experience theyâ€™ve ever had -- since birth -- with total clarity, and that this is somehow responsible for the civilization around me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I find another escalator-thing and rise higher (or lower â€“ sense of direction between the plazas seems relative). On my escalator-platform there is a slave and she is staring directly at me. Both the slaves and the others have very similar facial features, or some other quality, which makes them appear indistinct and distant, somehow intelligent but unaware, like looking at a statue. I canâ€™t remember what it is exactly, but there is a strong feeling of being in the presence of beings that are alive but also very much â€œnot thereâ€.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The plaza I come to is much larger. At this point the dream becomes difficult to remember. It is very wide, and I am witnessing some kind of sport. The floating-people are â€˜seatedâ€™ at two large tables. In the middle of the plaza, floating in the air, are swarms of things that looks like moths, except less distinct. More like fuzzy points of half-substance or smoke. This is somehow a game. I had been that day of what three-dimensional Go would be like, maybe it is inspired by this, because I get the sense that the game involves strategy and large shapes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I go to another plaza. It is very large, so that I canâ€™t make out the edges of it, like staring off into the ocean. There are large crowds of people there, and lots of discussion. I canâ€™t make any of it out. I remember their voices being watery and sounding like the speakers were farther away than they actually appeared to be. I got the sense of being in a very important place, like a library or courtroom. Something very important is happening and is always happening here, but I canâ€™t really recall anything distinct about what it might be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The dream shifts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Iâ€™m in my kitchen eating Honeycomb cereal. Itâ€™s delicious. I canâ€™t believe how good it is. Itâ€™s so good that I wake up craving it.</p>\r\n'),(2808,'<p>\r\nhey austin. thanks for calling me from the white stripes show last night. i woke up at 5:15am this morning. ten minutes later, rain happened. i sat in my tent, thunder crashing around me, and listened to the white stripes sing BUT THIS CAN\\\'T BE LOVE, CUZ THERE IS NO TRUE LOVE through the static and crackle and distortion of phone lines, cellular repeaters, thunderstorms, time, and coasts. if a morning awake at dawn in a thunderstorm could have been made any better, this voicemail did so.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni have to go and unlock some stuff now. can\\\'t use the golf cart in an electrical storm. trudge trudge.\r\n</p><p>\r\n-le\r\n</p>'),(2809,'<p>As I was growing up, my mother would repeatedly warn me about so-called \\\"thrill seekers\\\". This classification included anyone engaging in activities she presumed to be dangerous, unhealthy or in some other way risking the life one is \\\"blessed\\\" with. Though it is probably a little extreme, my subconscious image of my mother is frighteningly similar to that of Adam Sandler\\\'s character\\\'s mother in \\\"The Waterboy\\\". \\\"Drugs are the devil!\\\" \\\"Rollercoasters are the devil!\\\" \\\"Sex with the lights on is the devil!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Repression is rampant. Everyone is altogether too concerned about the potential dangers of living, and not concerned enough about the living itself. Life to survive is not life at all. After I learned that too much sunlight can give you cancer, I stopped worrying about dying and started worrying about how I could squeeze the most out of life. If life giving sunlight can kill us, then death is inevitable and shouldn\\\'t be worried about. The inevitable, by definition, cannot be avoided; thus worrying is ineffective, wasteful and probably destructive. Death is the great equalizer, giving exactly the same objective meaninglessness to all of our lives, which sets us free. Everyone dies, and it is our impending death that forces us to really live. Without the urgency of a temporary existence we would feel no incentive to experience, to act now. Death also drives us to create, to leave something which originated from one\\\'s mind for someone else\\\'s mind to interpret. By this reasoning, our life is our experiences, and our afterlife is our legacy. To make the most of our lives we should spend as much time experiencing the new and different as possible, and to make an impact on the world we should share our experiences openly. Any healthy body is stretched and excersized regularly, and I feel that a healthy mind undergoes similar stretching of boundaries.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Safety is an illusion, and a dangerous one at that. By accepting and adapting to the contingent, and often unfortunate, nature of events in this universe we can experience life without terribly endangering ourselves or each other. However, by rejecting the possibility of change, one leaves one\\\'s self very open to negative influences. Not everyone you meet will be honest and trustworthy, and assuming this can lead to unfortunate circumstances all too quickly. But to deny one\\\'s self the opportunity to expand one\\\'s horizons is just as unfortunate, and in general wasteful. Anti-smoking campaigns tell us that each ciggarette we smoke takes 3 minutes off our lives. Similarly, each opportunity we miss is essentially time off our lives; for our lives should really be measured qualitatively by how much we\\\'ve experienced. We cower inside our personal bubbles, our lives chained to the status quo. Our houses and cars have security systems, but no one even looks up at a car alarm anymore. Airport security is a liberalist\\\'s nightmare, yet there is no reason to assume that all this facism is actually stopping terrorism. With the entire world constantly on the brink of nuclear warfare, the day-to-day risks of living disappear like drops of water in an ocean. Lung cancer, liver damage and kidney failure won\\\'t be the problems of my generation; we\\\'ll all die in wars, disease, famine and pestilence from the rapidly deteriorating world around us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Exploration has slowed, almost to a halt, and we have no purpose, no goal, to call our own. We aren\\\'t braving the dangers of the Oregon Trail, seeking a better life with greater freedoms. We aren\\\'t fighting tyranny (we are still too complacent to realize who the tyrants are), and we most certainly aren\\\'t progressing as a society. Physically, our race is decently evolved; we have healthy bodies, and most of our really hard labor is accomplished for us by machines of our creation. However, it does seem that with the arrival of modern comforts, the thrill in living itself dissappeared quickly. Now, the majority of people are mired down in mediocrity and complacency, without a philosophy with which to govern themselves. Intellectual territory has been almost entirely unexplored since briefly in the late victorian era. Before that, the ancient greeks were the foremost philosophers in the history of the world. Socrates is the Buzz Aldrige of cereberal space, first landing on the moon and telling us what it was like to learn something new. And now its all but silent, the cries of \\\"Eureka\\\" still echoing quietly off the pages of the early philsophical works.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jack London believed that to be a good writer, one must experience what one is writing about. Only by aggressively seeking experiences can our lives be meaningful enough to remember. There is nothing left for my generation to adhere to holistically; the hope of surpassing one\\\'s means and opportunities all but totally destroyed. I feel that the powers that be are the powers that have been for a very long time. Religion, especially Catholicism preys on the weak-minded, and has been controlling the path of history since it\\\'s inception. Repressing people keeps them from being creative, which keeps them from feeling free to express themselves, which keeps them from sharing with others, which keeps society from progressing past this vicious cycle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Like with most of my parents\\\' lessons, I have adapted their message to fit more closely with mine. I have an extremely open mind, and quite voraciously hunt down new experiences. These experiences include numerous occasions on which I used some psychotrophic substance, either already illegal or sure to become illegal shortly. Even though society has deeply ingrained a fear of narcotics in most individuals, I believe the cautious and sensible use of supplemental substances can be highly productive and positive. I consider my actions to be experience seeking, because the environment and the external influences of the situation are what really makes the event entertaining. Thrill seeking would require an artificially extreme situation designed to overstep my boundaries in some fashion. And I have found, during my numerous experiences, that the drug is only a catalyst, and the \\\"thrill\\\" can only be found with the correct mindset. <i>Adventure, excitement...a jedi craves not these things.</i></p>'),(2810,'<P>me: can you tell me more about those gyrations?\r\n</P><P>her: excuse me?\r\n</P><P>me: the gyrations the preacher was talking about.\r\n</P><P>her: oh, we can\\\'t see the preacher from here.  could you tell me more of what he was talking about?\r\n</P><P>me: well, i wasn\\\'t really paying attention.\r\n</P><P>her: this is a prayer hotline.  do you want me to pray over you?\r\n</P><P>me: umm . . .\r\n</P><P>her: has anyone ever told you about the love of jesus?\r\n</P><P>me: not really.\r\n</P><P>her: well, god came to earth in the shape of a man.  that\\\'s why they called him emmanuel.  & nicodemus said to him-\r\n</P><P>me: who\\\'s nicodemus?\r\n</P><P>her: he was a prophet . . . ::15 minutes of jabbering here::\r\n</P><P>me: do you have all that memorized or are you reading from a paper?\r\n</P><P>her: i\\\'ve got it in my heart from knowing christ for 15 years.  ::scripture quoting for 10 minutes::  you see, god loves you more than any human ever could.\r\n</P><P>me: what about my boyfriend?\r\n</P><P>her: not even your boyfriend can love you that much.  you see, men are fallible.  i went between different men for most of my life.  i didn\\\'t get married until i was 33.  there is a god shaped vacuum in your heart that only god can fill.\r\n</P><P>me: a vacuum?\r\n</P><P>her: yes, that only god can fill.\r\n</P><P>me: in my heart?\r\n</P><P>her: would you like to accept jesus as your lord & savior?\r\n(at this point my boyfriend & i are doing our best not to crack up laughing)\r\n</P><P>me: can i . . . think about it?\r\n</P><P>her: of course you can, sweetie.  but satan is looking for you & he wants to stop you from accepting jesus . . . . ::10 more minutes of bs::\r\n</P><P>me: i\\\'ll think about it & call back later.\r\n</P><P>her: bless you.\r\n</P><P>me: . . . bye.\r\n</P>'),(2811,'<P>\r\nDinner comes with resolution sometimes.  On this particular evening, it arrived with two:  one in the appetite of a Mister Appendorf, the other in the depression of Mister Spindlechair.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nFor Mr. Appendorf, this week was marked by three significant events that combined brought him to the table more hungry than he would have otherwise been but certainly not more hungry than was allowed to a man of his size and appetite for life.  \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>Mr. Appendorf</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Being a young man of four and twenty, and a professional commanding a large salary that although unknown to the exact amount can be guessed to be ruffly four times that of Mr. Splindlechair as exhibited by his propensity to always carry eight times the money that Spindlechair carried, divided by his appetite which was at least twice that of his friend (and thus requiring an extra availability of cash).  this money though plenty had its price as Appendorf would readily tell any man, becuase in order to earn it, Appendorf admitted to being buggared on a regular basis by what he would often exagerate as every exective at the extremely large music division of the Suny Corporation.  Buggardry takes its toll on a man and the buggar only wanted more.  so this week, Appendorf quit by initiating a process of separation between he and his buggar lovers.  He did so by agreeing to three more months of buggary and, informally, the theft of a few thousand dollars worth of computer equipment from his office.\r\n\r\n\r\n<P><B>Evelyn Rosenthrall</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>To a man without a mistress, few things are more attractive than a beautiful woman.  The widow Evelyn Rosenthrall was a lady who was known throughout society in San Francisco as the most beautiful, and not available of women.  If her level in society was not alone enough to extinguish the young flame of a romance in any man\\\'s heart, her status as married would do the rest.  But her marriage it turned out was not perfect and her husband who had left the country on safari to Africa a year prior to the date was eaten by a lion and the news reached Evelyn three months ago.  In the mean time she recovered faster than most would have expected and never missed a day of her weekly ritual of attending the best offers in the realm of chic indie pop that the tender loin of her city could offer.  It was also renown that Evelyn Rosenthrall was as intelligent and quick witted as she was beatiful.  The paths of the widow Rosenthrall and Mr. Appendorf crossed rairly, a fact that may seem strange considering both had a consuming passion for fashion driven rocksters with expensive guitars.  They met as if by chance at the Festival of Indiepop and as it turned out, knew all the same people and loved all the same music.\r\n\r\n<P><B>Appendorf\\\'s Appetite</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Among Mr. Appendorf\\\'s appetites was the thirst.  For three days before sitting down to the meal, Appendorf did nothing but drink spirits and ale.  In the past twenty four hours he had not had even a morsil of bread, or a peice of gum.  He was hungry.  In general, his constitution did not allow red meats or dairy products, and they caused him a type of gas that is indescribable except to say it is like a death being born and the soul leaving violently by the most available means.  His choices on the menu were few.  They consisted of Fish, Fish, Tofu, and Fish.</P>\r\n \r\n<P><B>The Dinner</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Mr. Spindlechair sat across from Mr. Appendorf and watched the food come.  Fish after Fish after Tofu after Fish.  Appendorf admitted candidly, \\\"This is a large amount of fish.\\\"  Spindlechair agreed.  Fish was repeatedly delivered to the table on plates, in boiling pots, and in bowls.  Appendorf burped.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"You know Spindlechair, I don\\\'t think they are going to stop bringing us the fish,\\\" said Mr. Appendorf.  Spindlechair shifted on his seat.  They had ordered the Fish Dinner For Two.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"She is a fascinating woman,\\\" said Mr. Appendorf.\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"Yes, I completely agree.\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"We like all the same things, the same music, go to the same shows, know all the same people, it is like we live parallel lives. Yet somehow I never met her until last week,\\\" said Mr. Appendorf.  His tone was hushed in the large restaurant, a fact remarkable because of the loud environment.  He was speaking with a secret reverence for Rosenthrall.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"I have a difficult enough time avoiding people with whom I have a similar situation with regards to tastes and activities, except that i detest them,\\\" said the sour Spindlechair.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\\\"More fish?\\\" Appendorf laughed to himself.</P>'),(2812,'<p>today i am at the edge of the world.  almost everyone i love is scattered. sometimes our physical spaces overlap.  there is a feeling when it happens.  i can bridge my space to theirs in bitstream and analog wave. we can talk, communicate roughly.  there is something in physical presence that we cannot approximate.  not with telephone calls, not with email, not\r\nwith online journals.  it is a warmth;  it is fitting human beings together.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am thinking of my uncle, alone.  his body is detoxifying, scrubbing alcohol out of his blood.  i can hear exhaustion on the receiver.  he\r\npunctuates everything in deep sighs;  i imagine old liquor on his breath. he is shuddering from poison.  he tells me about how part of him wants to die, and how part of him wants to live.  i want him to live, and i tell him so.  we talk about watching lightning storms from the mesas in our hometown.  about how we would wait for heavy cloud, and when it came we would race monsoons and dodge flash floods.  we would sit, soaked, under downpours and watch lightning fork from sky to red clay hills around the  airport.  i remind him of how he felt, waving his crutches at the churning sky.  we talked about watching storms like that again sometime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am thinking about the woman i love.  she is so far away, now.  she is in a jumbo jet over the Mediterranean Sea.  she is an archeologist, and she is going to Crete.  i don\\\'t know what she is doing there - cataloging, digging, not sure.  she\\\'s not sure yet, either.  i think about her voice when she wakes up, and there is a window in her life when everything is now - she is not tied up with the future and past. she recognizes my voice, and whispers \\\"hi, baby,\\\" and i can hear her\r\nsmiling.  in those ephemerals, those moments, i forget that she is sick. i forget the dark spots in her lung, the c/t scans, the blood tests.  in\r\nthose moments she and i are forever.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think about those moments, the intangibles that we remember to suit us. the moments when everything is for all time;  how love and life get a second chance in the paradise of a heartbeat.</p>\r\n'),(2813,'<h3>1</h3>\r\n<p>So you set out one day to find out whatâ€™s making that<br>\r\ndrip,<br>\r\ndrip,<br>\r\ndrip,<br>\r\nnoise and you happen upon a metal box.  And itâ€™s a big, burly metal box with all manner of knobby knobs and pointy points set upon it.  And so you say to yourself: â€œAye, and I think we should have a look inside.â€  But sure enough you see that the liquid that it contains is<br>\r\nblack,<br>\r\nblack,<br>\r\nblack.<br>\r\nAnd an oily film casts flat rainbows across its surface.  â€œWhereâ€™s the pot of gold?â€  â€œWhereâ€™s my hidden treasure?â€  â€œDance for me you thieving leprechauns!â€  â€œDance!â€  The libations have been poured and the pretty, green bottles are all empty, and in a row â€“ you alcoholics know this picture well.<br>\r\nPlease,<br>\r\nplease,<br>\r\nplease,<br>\r\nâ€œListen, while I tell my story!â€  â€œLook, while I paint my pictures!â€<br>\r\nThatâ€™s how I like my coffee.</p>\r\n<h3>2</h3>\r\n<p>Mr. Germanos was born April 7, 1950, in Blue Island. He was a field engineer for Wausau Financial. He served in the Army during the Vietnam War.</p>\r\n<h3>3</h3>\r\n<p>You canâ€™t really write personal stories: as soon as anything leaves you it becomes something else.  And yet we persist in committing personal things to writing.  You can tear a few pages out of most great books and theyâ€™ll be interesting to read.  But only very rarely will those few pages elucidate the authorâ€™s purpose or allow you understand the structure of the text as a whole.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You experience life.  The things that you see, the things that you smell, the things that you taste are graven in your mind.  Somewhere, within the space that your body occupies, itâ€™s all hidden.  You wait; you think; and you even forget.  Everything rough and everything smooth, everything too heavy to lift, everything is in your mind.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And then someone tickles, or teases, or cleverly steals into your space; your hidden things are laid bare.  Pity you when an unfriendly stranger walks into the long, dark hallway of your past.  Some books are best left on the shelf; some stories are better not read â€“ especially aloud.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You exist; your stories exist.  But once theyâ€™re on the screen or on the page - separated from yourself - theyâ€™re not you, anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And so you wait, if you can, for a lover to hold you.  And you hope, if you can, for someone to take you and the story together.  And maybe, if youâ€™re lucky, in the quiet darkness where there isnâ€™t any time, more than flesh will pass in the space between you.  And then you will really know someone.</p>\r\n<h3>4</h3>\r\n<p>a cracking, a whacking,<br>\r\nitâ€™s childlike: distracting,<br>\r\nbright colors and sweet tastes,<br>\r\nthe piÃ±ata was donkey-shaped.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>walking alone in late autumn,<br>\r\nwith the big dreams of the stage<br>\r\nnow Iâ€™m just hanging around:<br>\r\nfuck me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there must be a movie â€“ or a book at least â€“ in all of this.</p>\r\n<h3>5</h3>\r\n<p>Thereâ€™s a big storm brewing in Chicago. The wind outside is violent at times; the first of the raindrops are starting to fall; thunder is rolling in the distance; lightning is flashing.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>And now the raindrops fall en masse. Iâ€™ve closed the bedroom window just a bit too late and my sheets are wet. The weather is different now than it was in my childhood: This past week we moved directly from winter into mid-summer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I donâ€™t know if Iâ€™ve told this story before, but Iâ€™ve had a few beers and I feel like telling it - so Iâ€™m going to.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was 17 years old my first semester at Uni. I was young. I wanted nothing so much as to leave home. And so I did. I didnâ€™t put too much thought into my choice of schools.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The year was 1985. I met Juliet at school. I canâ€™t remember what she was there to study. She never went to class. At the end of her first semester she had a 0.00 grade point average. She listened to The Cure and Modern English. Her parents had money. The two of us laid in bed a lot: watching cartoons, having sex, drinking, and sometimes smoking some pot. It was a terrible waste of time, really. She let her work go, altogether.  I was placed on academic probation after the first semester; I did some fighting and then was placed on disciplinary probation too. It was a waste. It was an opportunity that I let slip away. She kept writing suicide notes: threatening to kill herself if I ever left her.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Eventually, I got over her, and she got over it. Sure: she stalked me for a while.  But then she shaved her head, became a lesbian, and joined the U.S. Navy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My friend Eric had a girlfriend too. She was Cuban; she was hot; she was legally blind. The two of them broke-up and then she came to me to talk, and then hug, and then more. I was 18 by then, and still on probation at school. She and Eric were 19? In any case, Eric found out that the two of us had been together. So, he locked himself in his room and hung himself from a pipe that ran overhead.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was hard to finish that semester. Eric had a lonely funeral on the north side of the city on a cold, windy day in mid-winter.  I think that heâ€™s still buried up there at St. Henryâ€™s cemetery; God that sucked. How freaking stupid were we - all of us. I lasted 3 semesters, total, at that school; then I was expelled.  Oh well.</p>'),(2814,'<P>I\\\'ve decided to vent what I need to on here. Sure, I have a diary, but I can\\\'t bring myself to use it. Every so often I get the urge to spill my heart and soul into that diary, but all I ever end up doing is writing about miscellaneous bullshit which isn\\\'t even beginning to scratch the surface of my true feelings. \r\n</P><P>\r\nToday my best friend began to tell me how her dad died. She\\\'s always kept that subject close to her. I guess I\\\'m only now beginning to see the real her. I could see how much it was hurting her to talk about it. She had a complete look of sadness on her face. Then all of a sudden, she turned back into the chirpy girl I know and love and stopped talking about her dad. I didn\\\'t know how to handle it. Should I try to get her talking about her dad more, or should I just go along with her lead, and start joking around about our completely different music tastes? That was when it hit me, I do exactly the same thing. When I found out about my dad having an affair it completely ruined part of my life. I think about it every day without fail. I can\\\'t bring myself to talk about it because, well, it just hurts too much and I don\\\'t even know where to begin. I guess that\\\'s sort of what it\\\'s like for her. I know her dad being dead completely outweighs my situation, but the feelings are similar. I haven\\\'t told her about my family\\\'s secret yet. I\\\'m thinking I will soon though. Maybe it\\\'s time to start telling the whole truth and not sugar-coating my life, like I\\\'ve been prone to do.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2815,'<p>i really have to pee. but i felt like writing something first. i have no idea what to say. but scooby doo is on. and it\\\'s really boring. i hear music in the background. the clutter of sounds makes me wonder. \\\"what\\\'s going on?\\\" and i still have to pee.  but no matter. i\\\'ll just shake my leg. that helps. i had this friend that once told me it was harder for girls to hold their pee than guys. i thought about it for a while. then i just accepted it. makes sense i suppose.</p> \r\n<p>     this episode of scooby doo is confusing. and i still have to pee. but now it isn\\\'t as bad as it was. i hear that music more clearly now.</p> <p>\\\"it\\\'s the faint\\\" my friend says. \\\"post new wave.\\\" </p> <p>\\\"whatever\\\" i say. now i\\\'m gonna go to the bathroom.</p> '),(2816,'Will he leave me if I fart now?<br>\r\nIs my bottom nice enough?<br>\r\nIs he gonna break my heart now?<br>\r\nAre my hands too dry and rough?<br>\r\nShall I snuggle in behind him?<br>\r\nHow long will I take to fall?<br>\r\nDo I really want to trust him?<br>\r\nWas I gentle with his balls?<br>\r\nWill it feel this good tomorrow?<br>\r\nWill we ever take a trip?<br>\r\nWill he cause me heaps of sorrow?<br>\r\nDid I feel the condom slip?<br>\r\nIs he quietly rejoicing?<br>\r\nDid he spy me as I slept?<br>\r\nDoes he think I\\\'m just a toy thing?<br>\r\nHave I into trouble stepped?<br>\r\nWill this be a cruel secret?<br>\r\nDoes he like the way I kiss?<br>\r\nWill we meet for many weeks yet?<br>\r\nAm I going to be missed?<br>\r\nIs he wanting to escape now?<br>\r\nDid my toenails cut his feet?<br>\r\nShould I put on a nice tape now?<br>\r\nShall I soon have a repeat?<br>\r\nDo I share him with another?<br>\r\nIs he good enough for me?<br>\r\nDoes he have an older brother?<br>\r\nDoes he want to watch TV?<br>\r\nDoes my do my was I will I?<br>\r\nWhat will be what now what then?<br>\r\nAm I could I should I might I?<br>\r\nCan I have it all again?<br> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2817,'<p>we at a bar called the buck it. where my friend\\\'s band will play. they are drip. we are we at? setting up while a man brings us drinks. drinks and more drinks. and then they play. and the sound of a new uprising band fills the air. and no one but us seems to care. but it doesn\\\'t matter. cause we are all drinking. and it was all free.</p> \r\n<p>the band stops playing. and dance music comes on. and i want to play pool. but i can\\\'t. no i can\\\'t. too many people. so i forget about it. and watch as the dance floor remains empty except for the dance lights shimmering all over the place.</p> \r\n<p>why is no body dancing i ask myself. and i can\\\'t find an answer. and i get destracted as a lady in a bikini top walks by. what was i saying?</p> \r\n<p>so we then take off to drop off the band gear at a house. and of course we come back. because it is free drinks to us ALL NITE</p> \r\n<p>\\\"can you believe it? i\\\'m getting drunk in a bar\\\" the youngest of us. 17. he\\\'s real excited. and real drunk. and it will get worse.</p> \r\n<p>we are back and now i decide to drink whatever i can get. but not to worry. i drank water earlier. so i shouldn\\\'t get too sick. i get a white russian. and it is pretty damn good.</p> \r\n<p>skip to about 2 hours later. and 17 is still drinking. but why not. ITS FREE. so drink up i say.</p> \r\n<p>2 o clock. last call. 17 runs to the bar and gets about 3 more drinks. he\\\'s real happy. as are we all.</p> \r\n<p>time to leave. and i walk to the door. without trying to draw too much attention to myself. it works. and then a friend of mine got into some huffy with a guy at the pool table. glad i didn\\\'t play. they called him harry potter. i think cause of his glasses. we could sense that the tension was growing. but it was all calmed by my other friend. who hadn\\\'t been called anything. time to go now. i\\\'m tired.</p> \r\n<p>we go to 17\\\'s house. he\\\'s passed out in the back. that bastard. now we have to carry his ass to the room. but i stay. don\\\'t think i want to see what will happen next. so i wait in the car. potter and another guy come by. then this overweight dirty man comes strolling up the street. looking for a cigarette he says. but to potter. he wants more. you know what i mean. but luckily it was avoided. and potter and the other guy left. but not before he took a tape of tool.</p> \r\n<p>they ask us to come in. we do. we go through the back and we have to tiptoe very quietly up the stairs. 17 is huddled around a toilet and another drunk with a yellow shirt. is being helped to throw up in a bucket. the symbolism is uncanny. they\\\'re fine and they are both thrown on a bed. time to go. but 17 finds it right to walk us to the door. he does. and we leave.</p> \r\n<p>now i am home. and i feel great. not to mention a little hungry. and to my luck there is pizza in the fridge.</p>'),(2818,'<p> I don\\\'t always remember my dreams. Lately, my body has taken up the strange habit of waking up exactly in time to beat the alarm by about 2 minutes. When this happens, I am often jolted awake, as I was this morning. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> To be clear, my body and I are in a fight about how little I wish to work, and how much I want to sleep. It seems my body is trying to help out my job status, which I thank it for, while I slam it back into submission, and invite the hazy fog of broken sleep to come.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> As a result of this, I have been late for work by about 2 hours twice this week. I have also been have very vivid emotionally charged dreams. This morning, the chosen format of my mind\\\'s performances was nightmare.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My dreams are always some veiled reflection of my waking life. In them, I feel a concentration of myself into a scene, or a drama. I can often understand that I\\\'m dreaming, but usually I can\\\'t change my dreams, or take them over. As if I\\\'m trying to communicate with myself. Knowing it\\\'s just a message doesn\\\'t mean I can change what it says.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>While probing the depths of my communications with myself this morning, I was in a house, not unlike my own house, in that I was residing there in the dream; but totally unlike my house in that I imagined everything about it, including the eerie way that all the rooms other than mine were seemingly abandonned. All this in a few moments of electromagnetic activity, while my eyes fluttered, and perhaps I gave a muffled sigh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My dreams have recurring themes. The theme today was threat of attack. I can trace my dreams of this sort throughout my life, starting with a dream I used to have as a child living with my parents in small town suburbia.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> In my latest dream, I was unexplainably being pursued by a threatening man who was breaking into the window in the first floor of my home. I don\\\'t imagine it will ever be pictured by another the way I see it, the window at the bottom of a narrow stairwell, and the attacker using some sort of crow bar. His initial intention uncertain. I run, and suddently I am in an empty room, trying to call for help. 911. On hold. Waiting.. my cell phone in hand. Almost out of batteries. Fear that I may lose my connection with the other end. With help. The dream ends as I lay paralyzed with my feet and knees over me, back to floor/bed I\\\'m on, behind a door, hoping that all the burgalur wants is the TV in my room, and not me. My waking is initiated by the crackling of my cell phone, with a faint woman\\\'s voice offering me options. Press 1 for fire emergency, 2 for police attack...</p>\r\n'),(2819,'Sitting in this room, listening to the blender in the kitchen making my brother\\\'s birthday cake. It\\\'s kind of sad really. Everyone seems to have forgotten that it\\\'s my birthday aswell. No mention of a present, no mention of a cake. I sit and listen to people talking about how cool it will be to celebrate my brother\\\'s 21st birthday. They even re-paved under the verandah just so that we could sit out there and have a nice lunch. $2000. And it\\\'s probably going to be too cold to even sit out there. Waste of money? I think so. Yet I keep my mouth shut. Hold my feelings inside. Don\\\'t let any emotion show. I am forced to clean the house for the visitors coming, while my brother goes out with his ex-girlfriend to see a movie. Seems a bit unfair to me. Considering just the other week I was told by my so called \\\"loving\\\" brother that he had no respect for me and couldn\\\'t wait until he left home, then maybe he\\\'d finally get away from this family. Did he ever stop to think that maybe that\\\'s what I want too, but at least I have the courtesy to respect them all. Possibly he\\\'s just too arrogant and self-absorbed to realise that once he\\\'s lost me he wont be getting me back.'),(2820,'at twilight, the sun shines amber rays on  our street, signaling the children that they must leave. the old men with their floppy hats stare out at it, battling against it just before it sinks beneath the buildings and, warm, disappears, butter melting on the horizon. the old grey street whistles at night, pleasant lanterns adorning it like fiery jewels. the men play.</P><P>all of them, hurrying out to join their friends after such a long day. remember the heat of the passed sun, blurring the road and causing the trees to dance in the haze like tongues of flame. dry brown hands, like finely tuned instruments, aching and perfect in every way. ready to play, do their job. sway in the breeze like those old masters, irresistibly, feeling the vibrations, rough, in their arms. bear the scars of the trade proudly with hard calloused hands, scarred fingers from too many nights of far too much passion. blood pooling down before being noticed and then left alone, to dry, burgundy, in the summer sun. blood, like red lipstick, melting away and forgotten in the dark. </P><P>one, red haired, only stands simply to the side, smiling a bit, and bobbing his head up and down. causing this large brown hat to flap in the air. </P><P>the women, inside with the children, sing along with their men. voices  and breasts high and sweet. dancing on the air for a while, and floating. their high waisted skirts whorl around them as they turn, dancing as they cook. hair tumbling and they sing as their children eat, looking out to see the street from above, to see their fathers play again, and my how proud they were. the little boys play with plastic instruments, preparing for some other day. and the girls, they sing with their mothers and they dream, of course, of one day being married. of one day having a man, like their father, who would love them and hold them and say funny things like, aren\\\'t you the most beautiful woman in the world? and they dreamed of laughing then, and kissing him perhaps, only a little, and blushing, bright  pink, in the soft light.</P><P>then, of course there is the ever present smell of food in the air, mixed with perfumes of all sorts. the faint wet scent of musk and sweetness wasting in the air, making some long to be home again.</P><P>there then, were they who danced. the two, laughing daringly close. the old men watched as a skirt whirled, sharply when the two turned, stepping briskly in the other direction, frills swishing behind. it was another song entirely as they danced, they were perplexed, aroused. they flew. blue fabric stretched tight over a shining corset, chest heaving, another, all black smoothness, standing fixed as she spins. it was another thing to notice that she had almost quizzically light hazel eyes, that her hair, so dark, tumbled about her in chocolate ribbons that shone. another again to say that the other so dainty in her black, pressed persistently at her clothes, and that her eyes, like honey, seemed to flash occasionally of madness.</P><P>the thing to notice, was the slight rain as it wet the skin, and old men sitting out, patiently, with their instruments, looking on. drops of water sliding off of their hats and splashing against their shiny ebony shoes. then wives,  happy that they could close the windows now. the sound of a small melody in the splatterings of the tiny droplets as they fell on tin roofs and, sliding, crashed to the dirty ground. notice that almost translucent nature of the two now, pale and slick in this new rain and still spinning forever, stamping their feet as they did so. lips shining bright red berries. cheeks flushed, and clothes, clinging desperately to them.</P><P>it was then that it seemed that it felt a bit like the sun had come out in the rain, and everything, quite blue, was  almost surreal. the poor clouds, solid, and moving in the sky like painted pictures moved by hanging pieces of thread. it was almost like walking into a bar and having a kiss before dying.</P><P>'),(2821,'<p><font size=+1><b>1) facts</b></font>\r\n<br>\r\n<li>in china, in 1999: 1.246 billions people, 361 millions militaries (source <a href=\\\"http://www.infoairports.com/asia/china/stat.html\\\">infoairports.com</a>).\r\n<li>in china, in 2001: at least 2,468 people were executed, but the true figure was believed to be much higher (source <a href=\\\"http://web.amnesty.org/rmp/dplibrary.nsf/ff6dd728f6268d0480256aab003d14a8/46e4de9db9087e35802568810050f05f!OpenDocument\\\">amnesty international</a>).\r\n<li>with these numbers, and comparing to the 66 people executed in the US in 2001, china is proportionaly executing 8 times more.\r\n<li>emiko decided not to meet us anymore. \\\"we are too different,\\\" she said.\r\n<li>two days after this incident, i had the \\\"toy shower\\\" dream.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><font size=+1><b>2) ideas</b></font>\r\n\r\n<p><u>about emiko and john, and racism</u><br>\r\n<li>prejudices are common between chinese and japanese. for one part, because of WW2 during which japan invaded a good part of asia; for the other, because of a japanese idea that they are somehow superior to other asians. i would not be surprised if many chinese also had some superiority feelings.\r\n<li>i have no doubt that she overreacted because of her fears from racism against chinese. i agree with her feelings toward this.\r\n<li>deciding not to talk anymore, because \\\"we are too different\\\" is obviously a racist-like mistake.\r\n<li>it is likely that emiko and john will encounter many representations of racism if they move to either one\\\'s country. i feel sad for them, specially now that i saw how bad they can deal with it. they might prefer to stay in the US: racist or not, people here don\\\'t pay too much attention if you are chinese or japanese.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><u>about china products and boycott</u><br>\r\n<li>i first noticed the ubiquity of the label \\\"made in china\\\" on toys. cheap toys, especially stuffed animals, are often imported from china.\r\n<li>i decided to boycott chinese products sometimes after seeing the movie \\\"kundun\\\". i don\\\'t think of boycott as an efficient way of helping tibet. i would like to do more, if i can.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><u>about my racism</u><br>\r\n<li>it is usualy considered as racism to have any general negative idea or emotion toward a group of people because of their genes, culture or religion. being scared by a particular nationality is racism.\r\n<li>i try to cure myself of my racist ideas against chinese by learning more about them. three of my close coworkers are chinese. i always had excellent relationship with them. though, i am too scared to even start talking about their native country.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><u>about proudness/shame to be a citizen</u><br>\r\n<li>i don\\\'t see why anybody should feel proud of being born in such or such country. i suspect this feeling was insufflated into people\\\'s mind by governments who needed voluntaries for battle fields.\r\n<li>i am somewhat ashamed of certain things concerning france. i was somewhat ashamed to be french when france was performing nuclear tests in the pacific. i am somewhat ashamed when i see french politics on TV.\r\n<li>i wish that people from any citizenship, including chinese, will stop being proud of it without a reason, or at least will <i>also</i> be ashamed when there are reasons for it.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><u>about <i>socializing</i></u><br>\r\n<li>every time i met emiko and john, i have been more or less bored. if i were in france, i would have stop seeing them after one or two times. being abroad, i took the bad habit to accept more people into my social life. i can\\\'t pretend it\\\'s because i\\\'m more open minded: i think it just helps feeling less lonely; i can\\\'t stick to seeing only friends since i have so few of them here.\r\n<li>H and i use to laugh and say we have to <i>socialize</i>. i got used to it but in the back of my mind, i can\\\'t help feeling a bit like a prostitute. i am selling away my ethics for some second hand friendly relationship.<br>\r\n<li>when this incident happened, i thought i paid the price of my looser ethics. i should not accept to see people who won\\\'t forgive me any mistake. i always talk shit, at some point, so this <i>had</i> to happen someday.\r\n</p>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><font size=+1><b>3) toy shower, enlightened</b></font>\r\n<br>\r\nin my dream (see episode 2 in the series), this wasn\\\'t santa claus. this big red unstoppable machine coming to me was china. building toys at full speed, flooding the market. no face but a white cone. the red noze might represent alcohol (i\\\'m unsure of that one; a red disk inside a white area might as well be the japanese flag...).<br>\r\nwhy am i transformed into a pig? george explained me long time ago that the chinese character for \\\'family\\\' is a pig under a roof. or maybe just because that night, in their eyes, i was changed into a racist pig...\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2822,'<p><b><tt>5:16am.</tt></b>  The alarm on my clock-radio dutifully turns on to some random E-Z Rock radio station which tuned in well when I set the alarm last night.  I cannot stand the non-radio alarm setting as I am not a big fan of heart palpitations as my first waking physical action.</p>\r\n<p>I glance to my left and I can see light coming through the crack under my door.  More light than the rising sun filtering through the windows in my livingrooom would produce.  Pulling my head back, I glance at my glowing blinds to assure myself that the clock is correct and I have not slept through the beginning of my shift at work.</p>\r\n<p><b><tt>5:19am.</tt></b>  I finally make my way out of my bedroom after deleting three pieces of spam received during the night and skimming the beginning of an e-mail I will anticipate reading after work.  Upon opening my door I understand the cause of he light I saw earlier.  Mike has passed out on the couch watching the Italy and Croatia match.  I quietly laugh to myself and robotically go about my morning pre-work routine.</p>\r\n<p><b><tt>5:25pm.</tt></b>  Let\\\'s talk about dreams.  I am one of those people who, until corrected, stated they did not dream.  My ignorance past, I now say I don\\\'t remember my dreams, or very rarely do.  About once a month I have a vivid dream which I wake up and remember and will keep with me for a short time.  I enjoy other people\\\'s dreams.  I think sometimes that I am vicariously living out my subconscious through others.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ve always enjoyed Ali\\\'s dreams because they are very crazy and sweeping, usually involving her friends.  We\\\'ve also had interesting conversations about dreams usually centering around the oddity of hers and the lacking in memories of mine.  I like waking up in the morning in one of those weird and sleepy not-quite-awake-and-before-falling-asleep-again periods and having a dream described to me before falling back to sleep again.  I think it\\\'s one of those simple pleasures in life to have that available.  I don\\\'t wrack my brain trying to find out what another\\\'s dreams mean because ultimately it is their own thing and I will never have all the facts to make a correct deduction.  That\\\'s why I don\\\'t particularly buy dream analysis.  Dreams are unique to each individual and if I dream about toast on the same night as someone else does, chances are that toast will symbolize something entirely different for each of us.</p>\r\n<p>So now I sit and listen to the Tosca album Different Tastes Of Honey and if someone were to bring tea into my room for me right now, I wouldn\\\'t object to this course of action.  Soon enough I will go back to sleep to repeat another early morning of work.  I would like to wake up with memories of a dream, rather than to a passed-out roommate who tried, but failed, to watch a World Cup match live.</p>\r\n'),(2823,'<p>i kept walking. i walked for an amount of some time. i didn\\\'t know where i was. the day was hot. and i was hot. and i kept walking.</p>\r\n<p>i remember the greens of trees. and the cool shade which existed underneath. and i couldn\\\'t find my way. i saw a body of water. a pond, enough to cool myself in.</p>\r\n<p>i walked over and i was swimming. more floating really. and a lady appeared. standing at an angle which appeared as if she were standing atop the water. and the sun played upon the water underneath her as if a trail of glittery light danced out her feet. she motioned to me. though i didn\\\'t know what she wanted. i spoke to her. yet nothing seemed to come out of my mouth. no words. just silence. she motioned to me again. and she walked along the bank staring at me. i watched her as she watched me. and the glittery trail as if following her tailed along.  she smiled. as did i. and she stepped into the water. slowly gliding towards me. still smiling.</p>\r\n<p>she wore a summer dress of little flowers. no shoes. eyes as bright as the sun. with two small roses upon her ear. and shimmering long brown hair.</p>\r\n<p>and i watched her. watching me. floating nearer. she came to me and laid her hand on my cheek and spoke in a tone to low for me to hear. i saw her lips move in a motion as if saying \\\'wake up\\\'. and the sun became bright as a white blinding light. and i lay upon the ground near the pond. coughing. water pouring out from my nose and mouth. and i stared in bewilderment at a lady unlike the one i saw, speaking to me in a quick panicked voice. and she asked if i were okay.</p>\r\n<p>i was. and she left me on the bank. lying in the sun. wondering where the other had gone. and where she had come from.</p>'),(2824,'We are the ones who are Lost--<br>\r\nWe are the ones who are Beat--<br>\r\nWe are the generation of left-over foods.<br>\r\nWe are the generation of water-wings, and the shallow end of the pool.\r\n<p>\r\nWe have no wars, we have no struggle,<br>\r\nwe hurt only ourselves and have only ourselves to blame.<p>\r\nWe are the ones left fatherless\r\nby selfishness;<br>\r\nnot by bullets, and this is our secret shame.<p>\r\nSing to me of books and trees--<br>\r\nSing to me of the perfect gin and tonic--<br>\r\nWe do not have them, you have lost them,<br>\r\ngive them back.<p>\r\nGive them back.<p>\r\nTell me where to find the face of my father, so that I can gaze into its depths and see myself--<br>\r\nTell me where to go to find peace--<br>\r\nTell me where the jazz-men blow their horns in furious crescendos and decrescendoes--<br>\r\nWhere are the nights?<br>\r\nWhere are the faces?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhy do we fling codes at each other at lightspeed?<p>\r\nWhy are our main concerns so easily distilled?<p>\r\nWhat have you done to us?<p>\r\nWhat have we done to ourselves?<p>\r\nScream at me.<br>\r\nWrite to me.<br>\r\nCall me on the telephone.<br>\r\nE-Mail me,<br>\r\nShoot me with a gun, stab me with a spear,<br>\r\nhit me with a stone.<br>\r\nLie to me,<br>\r\nsing to me,<br>\r\nlisten to me,<br><br>\r\nbut<p>\r\ndon\\\'t just leave me here,<br>\r\nnaked<br>\r\nand<br>\r\nalone. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n '),(2825,'<p>That which I couldnâ€™t say.\r\nI wouldnâ€™t have anyway.\r\nAnd I shouldnâ€™t have by the way.\r\nSay what I happened to say.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She spoke to me.\r\nAnd I now see.\r\nWhat couldnâ€™t be.\r\nthat wouldnâ€™t be.\r\nthat shouldnâ€™t be.\r\nWas better left unsaid.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She wanted me to mend.\r\nBut I turned from her again.\r\nWhat could have been.\r\nWhat would have been.\r\nWhat should have been.\r\nWas that which went unsaid.</p>\r\n'),(2826,'As you grow older it becomes more and more difficult to lie in bed alone; that can, and should, be read in many ways.  You will grow older.  Make no mistake: Youâ€™ll grow older or you\\\'ll die.\r\n<p>In the half-waking mind lost bits of perfume and the musk of unwashed sheets confirm the immediate presence of a lover who is, in truth, only ethereal.<br>\r\nGrasping is vain.<br>\r\nInhale and begin:<br>\r\nSearch your hands, first, for the scent; it fades quickly.  Do you remember the texture of their skin?  Was it different than your own?  Did their lips part for you?  Did their eyes close for you?  Did they smile a satisfied smile for you?<br>\r\nAnd sometimes the bed feels crowded as aggressive specters provoke responses: wielding the memories of their former offenses they vie with the present for attention.\r\n<p>The bed is not a place for cool transcendence.<br>\r\nIt is, I suppose, like a pagan incarnation: The word becomes flesh.  But the word is eros â€“ and that makes it a heresy.<br>\r\nThe unfeeling bliss of detachment is as blood in a glass: the stuff of Noahâ€™s shame and Christâ€™s communion commingle in the ruby liquid of nirvana. <br>\r\nMy tactile satisfaction led to release, and surrender.<br>\r\nDeep in my cups I surrender again.<br>\r\nTrample your rotten fruit, my barefoot beauty, and make wine for me to drink. <br>\r\nParadise without sensation is closer than love without feeling.<br>\r\nI want it: Now.  Imminence.  \r\n<p>Once upon a time â€“ a time not so very long ago â€“ people were born and people died in bed.  People fucked and gave birth, people fell ill and died on hunks of wood and feathers bound up in cloth.  And between the bed and the kitchen table all of life was divided.\r\n<p>Allow me to fulfill our stereotype for you: Let me worry my hair back, again and again; let my eyes be black; let my skin be oily and olive and somewhat transparent; let my hands be big and filled with veins; let me be possessive.  And most important of all: Let me shift quickly.<br> \r\nMy mercurial countenance is indicative of my race.  I am prepared - quite spontaneously - to fall in love.  I am ready to love, and hate, and cry, and laugh; things canâ€™t happen too quickly for me now.  Presto.<br>\r\nPresto: I wish that you would appear, again.<br>\r\nI am Latin.\r\n<img src=http://www.art-and-archaeology.com/roman/pom29.jpg>'),(2827,'Its official.  I am now officially another number in a STD statistic.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI feel so bothered having this disgusting little virus dormant in my spine, waiting for an opportunity of vulnerability to re-emerge and turn my dick into a battle field.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI just wish I could to talk to more people that cared.  My parents are some of the most loving people in the world but I canâ€™t bring myself to tell them.  They are not stupid people, they know they have a 22 year old son living on his own in downtown Toronto who considers himself something of a playboy but we have never really talked openly about sex or some of the complications associated with it.  The news of a STD isn\\\'t really the fuel needed to start that conversation.\r\n<br><br>\r\nLori was really easy to talk to and it made me feel really happy how understanding she was.  Some of the other people that know havenâ€™t been as supportive.  I donâ€™t really blame them. Its going to be emotional being judged.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI went to the doctor on Sunday.  She basically said from what my sexual past sounds like to her that I probably got it from kim.  I wouldnâ€™t really be surprised.  Iâ€™m not really upset at her because for all I know I could have got it since the first time I fool around with someone years ago.  kim\\\'s got symptoms too.  wonderful.\r\n<br><br>\r\nHerpes.  What a fucking stupid word.'),(2828,'<i><p>Tears fall like acid rain<br>\r\nSearing skin,<br>\r\nLeaving smudges on the windows of my soul</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Your hurt becomes my pain<br>\r\nThe wound festers<br>\r\nCut out my heart to save the rest of me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dull black eyes, life escaping<br>\r\nAnd now exhale<br>\r\nLet the feeling slowly fade away.</p></i>'),(2829,'<p>\r\ni had this weird dream last night. a jealousy dream. i don\\\'t think i\\\'ve ever had one before. maybe it was from joking around with wayne about italian boys so close to bedtime. i don\\\'t know but it was funny. in the dream linnea came back from europe with a bunch of live 105 blink\r\n182 style guys. and the big one was her new pal and i was like hey what\r\nabout me. and she blew me off kind of but she brought them all over to my\r\nhouse. and so me and the big guy decided that we had to fight so we ripped\r\noff our studded belts and and started hitting each other with them like\r\nwet towel in the locker room style. strangely, i remember the first hit\r\nwith the belt against my calf feeling kind of good. the last thing i said\r\nin the dream was, hey this isn\\\'t fair, your belt is longer than mine.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-simon\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2830,'<P>\r\nfuck broken phones.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy phone isnt broken. blah. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am here in seoul. the heartland of korea. its like a wound this city. its like a big fat open wound. it bleeds to the world. the cup. what the fuck does the world cup mean?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter being nervous...my cab cost less than expected...so that i can afford this fucking pc time. fuck. one hour. thats all i can afford. im typing like ive never typed before. not in this state. fuck the SAAB club. its such a shit place. I never want to enter that place again in my life. its like a big sore in an area so cool and hip. a university area. fuck the white people that go there. i should be the only white person there. thats the way i think in this country. if there are white people i dont know in an establishment,,,i should not be there. is that good? is that bad? fuck beer. fuck gin and tonic. does it cloud how i think? does it cloud the way i look at society? no it doesnt. all it does is make me notice what I hate. i hate to see the things i do. i should not share them with you because you should not have to deal with such madness. If you want to learn of such madness just visit any place on earth frequented by whites in an area of the world no lived in on an everyday basis by whites. fuck white people. i fucking hate white people. well thats not true, but here i do. they represent everything wicked in this world. they bastardize all, and send to the graves everything that is good. they turn all into a comodoty. my cab driver was nice. he let me smoke in the cab. he smoked a \\\'tambay\\\' himself. when i saw him light it i said \\\'tambay mish-e-soy-oh\\\'. he laughed. i was ever so polite in my limited capacity and for this i think i got the the shorter route home. fuck. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city is such a bizarre thing. i have been thinking of \\\'the city\\\' as a concept a lot lately and for the most part i think its bad. i think its a place that people get lost. i think its a place people get lazy and get used to the \\\'way things are\\\'...and in that i mean complacent. apathy runs deep in a city. the class struggle i see on a daily basis makes me sick, and then when i enter a place like SAAB club it makes me sicker. i see the peope dressed all up in their bullshit and acting all bullshit and it makes me sick. i feel like puking. i felt like puking in the cab on the way here. i feel like puking in this place all the time. i cant even remember what i had for dinner. did i even have dinner? fuck i still have a half bottle of crap wine in the fridge when i get home. what the fuck is this bill at? still a \\\'chun-in\\\'. thats good. i forgot to get a \\\'jet-or-ee\\\' when i sat down so no \\\'tam-bay\\\' for me. all my kids say tambay bad, but i reply tambay misheeesoyohhhhh and they laugh. fucking kids.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlast night me and shane went shopping. we went to an area of town not seen in any part of the west. a place where wholesaler meets seller. a place no one my size should go. it was depressing. not in a depressing way, but only in a clothes buying way. fuck these small sizes and my large body. should i be to blame or should i just realize that this part of the world isnt as big as me. one size fits all in korea. whodathunkit. fuckers. shane got five t-shirts and a pair of shorts. i saw a lot of things id have bought, but they wouldnt have fit. fuckin sizes. why is it that all the aliens in movies are the same size. the only one bigger is the goddamn momma alien. do they have no concept of weight in science fiction? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFUCK THE WORLD CUP!!!!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbillions of dollars spent on a tournament that lasts only a few weeks. how about the starving? how about the people in need of medical treatment but who cannot afford it? how about education?.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni ramble. i ramble. i ramble. and for whom? i have been swinneyless in ages and i feel it inside. i feel it because i truely love the site and yet i cannot even begin to put things in words about how i feel right now, unless in the state that im in right now. fuck right now. fuck tonight. fuck going out. what the fuck for. to see things i dont much want to see and to do things i can do at home. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel like such a lamb. not the lamb of god, because i dont believe in god. if i believed in god i wouldnt have stole those two coolers from the bar tonight. fucking losers. puting a fridge full of alcohol behind a wall and having a smoke machine right over head. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo i care about the kids i teach? no bloody likely. i could care less. its a job and thats all. why do icare about teaching kids who dont really give a fuck.....\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe only person that gives a fuck is the parents,,,and the only reason the parents give a fuck is because they think that if the kids know english they will make more money. oh and status. fuck status. where the fuck did status get anyone. even the statusable OD on drugs. no drugs here. god i wish. fuck im rambling. i should go. i love korea, but im not loving korea tonight. i wish i was at home in my futon....cuddling with my cat. i miss my cat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni made the mistake of telling a dude(a friend) tonight my outlook on sex. i wont share that outlook with you right now because fuck im sick of justifying it to other people. im fine the way i am. fuck you for thinking im narrow minded. \\\'YOU DONT KNOW WHAT YOUR MISSING UNTIL YOU TRY IT\\\'. fuck that argument. \\\'HAVE YOU TRIED CRACK?\\\'. fuck you. its the same thing. \\\'SEX IS NATURAL...CRACK IS MAN MADE\\\'. i say sex today is man made, and the only reason sex is natural is because of procreation and lets face it....sex today is hardly about procreation. fuck sex. i hate it. i think its used and overrated. fuck you for thinking im wrong. obviously you have something good...and for those of you who think im down right crazy...well fuck you too. you are sheep...and you have been lied too...and you have given into the advertising and sales pitch. might as well join amway you goats...you horses...you dogs. it has a place,,,but for the most part that place has been broadened, and bastardized. i hate it. i hate lust. i hate \\\'want\\\'. i hate \\\'cravings\\\'. i hate \\\'not being able to\\\'. why should i add another facet to that ever growing diamond. FUCK FUCK FUCK.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim pissed off because people keep telling me im wrong. i might be in their eeyes...and thats fine. they can say it doesnt apply to them...and i wont say anything, but when they tell me \\\'IM\\\' wrong i take that personally and why? because its a view i hold. fuck you. fuck them. fuck all. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2831,'Nothing\\\'s cut and dried<br>\r\nOr black and white<br>\r\nNobody\\\'s labelled<br>\r\nWith wrong or right<br>\r\n<p>There\\\'s no steadfast rule<br>\r\nNo line that\\\'s been laid<br>\r\nNo absolute rhythm<br>\r\nOr tune that must play.</p>\r\n<p>Thereâ€™s no beginning and no end.<br>\r\nThereâ€™s countless hours to save and spend.</p>\r\n<p>There\\\'s tearing and smouldering<br>\r\nAnd grey and greyer<br>\r\nThere\\\'s endless options<br>\r\nAnd numerous layers.</p>\r\n<p>We can do what we like<br>\r\nThen despair or rejoice<br>\r\nFor we\\\'ll always be slaves<br>\r\nTo the fancy of choice.</p> \r\n'),(2832,'<p>My Greek things, my Greek things: reread, unread, brittle off-white pages on my shelf.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I stood in new foundations shoveling broken concrete in July.<br>\r\nI learned to balance the barrow with several hundred pounds in it.<br>\r\nYou hot sun: You became hateful.<br>\r\nAnd I became dark: my skin: brown, my heart: bitter.<br>\r\nI darkened; you called me, \\\"Brother.\\\"<br>\r\nBorn a blond-haired child I became a brunette.<br>\r\n\\\"Your hair and your mood grew dark together,\\\" my mother once said.<br>\r\nAnd so I burned in your midst.<br>\r\nYou taught me crime; you taught me the folly of possession.<br>\r\nYet still I labored for you.<br>\r\nI broke my bread; I rolled my wheel; and now it\\\'s summer again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>El Moustache was in circumference as great as his height.<br>\r\nHe appeared to hold a small, bristly broom head just beneath his nose.<br>\r\nAnd underneath his heavy arms two salty half-oval stains revealed the intensity of his labor.<br>\r\nHe stared in a stupid but benign way - like a cow.<br>\r\nI took pity upon him - my noble blood.<br>\r\nOh, I\\\'m such a fucker sometimes.<br> \r\nI thought: \\\"He must be Catholic and have a large family.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m going to wander around just a bit longer tonight.<br>\r\nThere\\\'s no liquor in the house; I don\\\'t know how I\\\'ll sleep.<br>\r\nWine is the best companion for the wide-eyed at night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wondered if the action of the slide would disturb my teeth.<br>\r\nI wondered if the bathtub would be the best place.<br>\r\nI thought about my belt.<br>\r\nI wondered about God, and Jesus, and my family.<br>\r\nI wondered if El Moustache thought of these things.<br>\r\nI saw him shuffle around in his untied shoes with that blank look on his face.<br>\r\nI think sometimes now that I should think like him.<br>\r\nI should be like him too.<br>\r\nI should wear a cow-like expression on my face.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And I wondered if his family made him happy at night.<br>\r\nBut maybe he didn\\\'t go straight home; maybe he cruised for whores in his Cutlass.<br>\r\nAnd maybe he hated himself; maybe he drank in the dark.<br>\r\nMaybe he had a hard time falling asleep.<br>\r\nBut I knew without asking that El Moustache wasn\\\'t ever in bed alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>El Moustache never woke a family at four in the morning to tell them that their daughter was near death.<br>\r\nAnd El Moustache never heard a woman being beaten by her husband.<br>\r\nAnd El moustache never had to worry about the young mother and her child who couldn\\\'t breath.<br>\r\nAnd El Moustache never had to inhale the stench of decaying human flesh.<br>\r\nAnd El Moustache never had to ask where the missing finger went.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Straight line!\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Single file!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I asked my own mother if there was ever a moment in her life when she felt content - as if things were as they ought to be.  She answered, \\\"When the children were born and they were all still young.\\\"  In some way or another, most people seem to arrive at the conclusion that life is about more than themselves.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sleep is such a teasing tart.<br>\r\nI wish that she would just want me, and take me all at once.<br>\r\nThe wine shouldn\\\'t really be necessary to bring us together.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Four bottles of beer and bed seems nearer.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s a certain funny feeling: like a death in the family.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s a sense of loss.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s a queasy, almost nauseous unease.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s the fear of being alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>El Moustache knows the feeling.<br>\r\nI know that he does.<br>\r\nBut he threw himself into something, and someone.<br>\r\nAnd he doesn\\\'t look back.<br>\r\nThe priest told him that God said it should be that way.<br>\r\nAnd his father and mother told him that it should be that way.<br>\r\nAnd his friends too: They told him it should be that way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You\\\'re a wise man, El Moustache.</p>'),(2833,'<p>lately i have been spending time trying to think of reasons why people don\\\'t like me. i realise this is a stupid thing to do, but it happens, you know?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>here are some of the reasons i came up with last night when i was going to sleep:</P>\r\n\r\n- because i listen to goldfm, which is all about horrible 70s and 80s songs. they play a lot of elo and america. i like this music. this makes me so unpopular. an outcast.<br>\r\n- my room is really really messy, quite a lot of the time.<br>\r\n- i love fleetwood mac. especially stevie nicks. like, i get emotional sometimes when i see stevie nicks, especially before she gave up coke. i also spend a lot of time thinking about lindsey buckingham\\\'s incredible pop guitar.<br>\r\n- because i have been going to therapy for 11 years now, since i was young, and i talk about it lots. i know this must annoy people alot. at least i assume it must.<br>\r\n- insecurity is so unattractive, isn\\\'t it?<br>\r\n- because i am spotty.<br>\r\n- because i keep silent most of the time. i just get nervous. i feel so small when i am around people who i think are rocking.<br>\r\n- because i have problems returning emails.<br>\r\n- because i truly show fear.<br>\r\n- because i am always sick.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>i thought of other reasons last night. i get to see a therapist tomorrow. i think i probably will look back at this article and feel cringey. but also, i like to be truthful about insecurity. i think that everyone feels it, at least sometimes. okay, goodbye.</P>'),(2834,'All around was silence. The eunuchs, the priests, had gone to sleep, purified and tired from the day. When they awoke, they would again go through the ritual before greeting the gods. Bathing, scrubbing, shaving, getting as clean as possible, for fear of divine retribution. The wab priests\\\' white linens always freshly pressed, crisp,  seeming magically to appear every morning in their rooms. On the small scroll the familiar hieroglyphic script said a name, Wehemmesu, the repeating of births. <br><br>\r\nA young boy in the center of the room moved quietly.  On his knees, he ground the red paste together from his pallette.  The colour of importance, the colour used to write the names of those with the Two Ladies names, the Lords of the Sedge and Bees.  Around him, the boy had long scrolls of new papyrus waiting for him.  He was delighted finally to be able to use these things freely, not having to ask about them, a learning scribe unable to write on such costly things for fear of even one mistake on the scroll, ruining it.  He wanted to make a record of himself.  As he leaned over the papyrus, he lightly ran his brush over it.  The ink spread evenly over the crushed and beaten reeds, bleeding under the slight pressure of the boy\\\'s trained hand.<br><br>\r\nCarefully he began to draw his profile on the papyrus.  His profile first, the eye of Isis - Horus\\\' mother - above his right cheek.  He drew his small, straight nose from the side. A long braided wig covered his ears, which he drew encircled with golden thread, long tassels hanging down from each braid. His chest faced towards him, the exaggerated breadth of it free from any sort of cloth but laden with golden necklaces and breastplates. One necklace, he imagined, was a solid golden scarab in laden with his name in the shiny glass used only by the Lord of the Sedge and the Bees and his family. Another a plate with a vulture stretched out on it, carrying in its talons the god Ra\\\'s sun disk. Each feather was individually linked to the other in small golden links, the greatest of precautions taken because of his importance. He drew himself still in his scribe\\\'s long starched skirt, but now he also wore the hide of a leopard around his waist, tail hanging down behind him.<br><br>\r\nAs Wehemmesu reached for his pallette again he knocked it over, spilling it onto the papyrus, destroying his painting. He watched in horror as the same material that he had worked so hard to make - had studied so long to be able to make - for the first time in his life spilt over.  His mentor had told him never to mix so much water into the pigment but he had never listened.  Enduring so many scars from his teachers until they realized how beautiful his script was when he used this nearly translucent mixture of pigment and water. <br><br>\r\nNow he regretted not listening to his more dogmatic teachers, not giving in to their demands of a more solid paste for him to use. An accident proof version of his own light ink. He covered his mouth with his hands now as the ink enveloped the drawing, trying as hard as possible not to scream as he knew his body desired to do.  It was as if it were a small pool of blood, slowly moving over the stone floor of the temple. Covering everything in sight, staining the white floor and now moving towards the edges of Wehemmesu\\\'s skirt. Now the actual blood began to flow with the ink on the ground.\r\nThe eunuchs would find him there, red ink on the ground, a painting of himself on the papyrus and they would condemn him. They would tell him that he had defiled their pure sanctuary and ask their idols for a judgement. All the while knowing that it was one of their own priests standing behind the golden idol, dictating his judgement. Then he would be banished from Kemet.  His father\\\'s ka would have no body to inhabit in his death and would haunt him and terrorize him for the rest of his life. All because of this little daydream of his that he could live in the palace as royal scribe. Now the blood was visible in the pool of ink in the floor, dark maroon shining, swirling. Wehemmesu\\\'s skirt had little drops of blood all around the front in a little pattern.  The drops formed the hieroglyph for the scribe in the whiteness of the linen. At the edges of the skirt the redness had begun to seep into the fabric, traveling upwards in little columns to his thighs. <br><br>\r\nIn his last bout of consciousness before fainting Wehemmesu tore at his soft wrist again with his hardened nails, tearing the last bit of flesh to let the blood flow evenly downwards without the wound closing again, as it had begun to do just five minutes after he had first opened it. Now the blood flowed like a fountain from his wrist and he imagined it now a fount of life. Still, then again, what was this but bad blood?  It needed to be drained from him. <br><br>\r\nWehemmesu\\\'s face was deathly white when he fell into the gleaming pool of blood and ink before him. His nails were broken and crusted over with blood when their small wrists fell from his shoulders.  As the fell, they left trails of blood there too, little red dots of blood against his brown skin. When the wab priests found him the papyri around him were crusted and stiff with the dry blood and crumbled when the other eunuches ventured to grasp them in their hands. More important now was the state of Wehemmesu, who by now had stopped bleeding but however was having much trouble breathing.<br><br>\r\n\\\"So then you are not angry with me brother?\\\" Wehemmesu looked down as he asked this, afraid of the wrath of his brother for his idiocy. Much to the amusement of his brother, Wehemmesu\\\'s eyes began to open widely as he began to stroke his head. He smiled. \\\"Why should I be angry about the fact that you so desperately like the colour of Ra that you would use your own blood as paint?\\\"  He gently kissed the little boy\\\'s lips as he struggled not to laugh at his gawking. \\\"However, I am a bit angry that you would leave everyone in Kemet for a simple drawing.\\\"  Again Merynebptah smiled and then sat in a small wooden chair next to Wehemmesu. The wabu had bound his wrists with reeds and linen, cleaned the gashes with scented water.  The faint sweet aroma floating in the room was more than likely the honey they used to dress his wounds. The floor had been cleaned before he had regained consciousness, and was again shining brightly, only slightly tinted by the ordeal. In the air, the lingering copper scent of the blood still wafted around the room, there being no breeze to clean the air like the servants had the floors.\r\nSmelling the scent again, Wehemmesu vomited on the floor, leaving another mess to be cleaned. That stench now filling the room, nauseating him even further.<br><br>\r\n\\\"I do not suppose you would like to stay here, Wehemmesu?\\\" Merynebptah lifted the small boy in his arms as he spoke.  \\\"I am going to take you to my room.\\\"  Rocking in the elder boy\\\'s arms Wehemmesu noticed briefly that the stars were shining down on him. The full moon looking down at him from the heavens, expectant. In the moonlight Merynebptah looked vague and ghostly, an apparition carrying him to his after life. He lay back in his arms, admiring him, smiling. Everything seemed strange, as if through a fog.  The grounds looking like otherworldly courtyards as they walked past.<br><br>\r\nThe wabu priests had tried to make him drink a sort of red dye when he awoke again, telling him that he had lost too much blood, that it needed to be replaced. He resisted them, insisting that the concoction was horrid, disgusting. They mixed the dye with his fish and wine until the next full moon shone. The wabu regularly changed his bandages, now mixing in malachite with the honey to make a thick green paste, every night they washed the wounds with scented water again, trying to keep them clean. Merynebptah had told the wab priests to ask Imhotep to give health to his brother.<br><br>\r\nMerynebptah\\\'s room gleamed in the firelight.  The little round table he used to hold his jewelry and makeup stood on the far side of the room.  His little jar of malachite open with its dipping stick leaning on it. All around the table little ornate boxes sat with their lids open, scattered about. Their contents melting in the heat, mingling with the faint musk of their owner left in the air. Perfumed unguents, ointments, tinted oils for the skin, little rings of scented wax. The scroll carrier\\\'s necklaces were strewn over the backs and seats of the small chairs in his room.  His white linen skirts, folded in boxes under his small bed.<br><br>\r\nThe little oil lamps flickered on and off every few seconds, warming Wehemmesu\\\'s skin periodically. The bath water lapped against him, leaving little drops to skate down his chest as the waters receded. He raised his arms out of the water to look at them.  The wrists were pink where he had cut them now. . . . only a bit sore. The droplets from his fingers lightly fell on his face as he moved them, sprinkling him as he leaned back in the pool.  He shivered.<br><br>\r\nHis brother was bathing on the other side of the pool, continually dousing himself in the holy water, making his dark skin glisten in the firelight. He scrubbed himself with a coarse cloth then, rubbing at his arms, legs, face. His long straight hair fell back on his shoulders in little damp bundles. His soft fingers were just beginning to wrinkle in the water. He stood then with the droplets cascading down his body for a while, and remembered himself and Wehemmesu.<br><br>\r\nThe elder boy walked toward Wehemmesu, smiling at him with laughter in his eyes.  Merynebptah sat beside him, touching his shoulder with the same cloth, rubbing it over him. He covered the smaller boy\\\'s body in a sweet-smelling oil, massaging him gently. Two tiny droplets fell on Wehemmesu\\\'s chest, trailing down before Merynebptah could reach them. When he caught them, he began to massage the extra oil into Wehemmesu\\\'s skin slowly with two of his fingers.<br><br>\r\n\\\"Merynebptah, the gods hate me.\\\"  His bother shifted a bit in the pool as he heard this, stopping his fun momentarily.  He looked down at the little boy, smiling at him again. \\\" Wehemmesu, there are no gods. There are only idols. In idols there is  power. The priests know this, and they hide behind them.\\\" Merynebptah turned to hover over Wehemmesu, bending slightly to kiss the boy\\\'s lips. \\\"Besides this, little one . . . I love you.\\\"<br><br>'),(2835,'<i>take this book and read it</i>, he replied in answer to her question, and handing it to her he said <i>it might change your life</i>, and it did, but not in the way he had intended.\r\n<p>\r\nshe read it, and her soul or at least a picture of her soul soared into the sky, and the future became not an endless dragging monotony punctuated only by dogs barking in the middle of the night and little sally\\\'s junior prom and her husband making partner and coupons and kitsch and when it\\\'s time to ask about prevarin, but an open endless fruited purple plain that stretched as far as she could see, which was so far, and her vision of life encompassed everything under the sun.\r\n<p>\r\nexcept him.\r\n<p>\r\nhe wondered why it was so. \r\n<p>\r\nsecretly, perhaps, he knew the answer, and maybe that\\\'s where the dream came from.\r\n<p>\r\nhe dreamed he was in the home of an old friend, from years and years ago who had a beautiful sister and in fact she was as beautiful as any girl he had ever seen, inspiring a sort of awe that in scope was somehow beyond the narrow band of the sexual. she was a girl he\\\'d never have and yet he conciously shielded her from his desire in his mind, to preserve her image, and she was not the only girl that did this to him but there were not many in his life. \r\n<p>\r\nin the dream she was sick. not sick in her body but very quiet, and her eyes glowed bright but hardly moved. she fixed him with a look that was sadness but a serious sadness like something... something might still be done....\r\n<p>\r\nhe asked her brother, his friend, what was the matter and the brother said something about a wreck, something being wrecked, and there was actually in the dream a pamphlet he had written about her condition because it was too painful to discuss in the open air. a lot of people dropped by to ask about her. he, i mean not the brother, i mean our he, he couldn\\\'t read it really because who can read in a dream? but he saw clearly O- and he said, <i>that\\\'s my blood type, if it helps</i>.\r\n<p>\r\nand they were on the beach next to the ocean and the sky was dark, and indoorsy dark, and the water was motionless like a lake and he dove in to find what was needed to rescue her. he dove into the dark and there was a concrete slab, and he took a deep breath and dove under again and struggled, and pried, and pulled with all his strength until it lifted up, and underneath was a small boy, maybe eleven, and the boy smiled at him in a strangely familiar way and said, <i>time to wake up</i>. \r\n<p>\r\nand he did.'),(2836,'<p>yesterday i went crazy. i\\\'ve done this only one other time. i can\\\'t explain what brings this on. i don\\\'t know if this stuff happens to alot of other people.</p>\r\n<p>the first time it happened i was in the process of moving from springfield, massachusetts out to long island and i was making the drive for about the 4th time in a week. i was driving through bridgeport conneticut which isn\\\'t that nice of a place and i ended up getting lost and on some weird highway i\\\'d never heard of and as everyone knows ct traffic is nightmarish and it all caught up to me and i pulled over and screamed at the top of my lungs incoherently for about 2 full minutes and pulled a fistful of hair out of my head in the process. i\\\'m pretty sure this incident happened as a buildup of the stress and frustration i was going through in trying to make a move completely with no help from anyone (since everyone seems to mysteriously be \\\"busy\\\" when one needs help moving i\\\'ve noticed), and also having no money so i had to make about 3 trips lugging my meager possesions in my small ass car. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nanyway so this didn\\\'t happen quite as dramatically yesterday but i still haven\\\'t quite pinpointed what was wrong. i mean, there were alot of little annoyances, and i also was supposed to go somewhere i didnt feel like going that night, but whatever it was actually built up to the point where i wasnt even walking straight and i actually walked up to my boss and asked if i could leave work early. this is a big thing for me. for one thing at my work they flat out tell you \\\"we hold it against you if you call off sick or get sick\\\". but for another thing i usually will never ever leave or call off unless i\\\'m close to death. i felt like a crazy person yesterday.  i just didnt want to be there. i didnt want to be around my friends. i wonder if this is normal. sometimes i feel like i have to drop out of society for a day or two and ignore phone calls and hibernate in my dumpy apartment and brood (which is what im currently doing)</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2837,'<p>there are two kinds of love scenes.</p>\r\n<p>the first is passion.  two bodies outstretched, tangled, entwined; a mass of limbs merging & twitching & reordering themselves.  skin melts with skin, sweat shines under eyelids, lips part to gasp & quiver.  legs, arms, breasts all sucking & sticking together as sensation runs rapidly through a station in search of the next train to jump.</p>\r\n<p>the second is silence.  an inch apart, two faces remain nearly motionless except the eyes.  those eyes shake madly, scanning the pair before them, all tremors & intrigue.  slowly the lips roll back their stones & are revived.  they shrug snow off their shoulders & curve into nervous smiles.  fingers awake & trace dimples, hips, clavicles.</p>\r\n<p>i think i prefer the latter.</p>'),(2838,'<p>\r\nwhy do i like her so much. i was asked the \\\"why\\\" question the other night and was unable to collect my thoughts and put them into words.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nid have to say that i like her because she is cool. to define cool in a more specific manner id point out to anyone who asks that i like her pretty nose, her artistic mind, and her sense of humour. I like the way she laughs. i think her voice is sexy. I like the fact that she isnt a girly girl. she\\\'s independent and seems good natured. to keep asking why is somewhat bullshit. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni left the group tonight. I had to get away. I didnt like the way the night had turned out and i didnt like the places we went to. It would have been selfish for me to complain so i kept my mouth shut and just rolled with it. \\\"boogie boogie\\\' did it in. It was a \\\"hard rock cafe\\\" style bar. it was so busy. not busy with people...i can deal with that. there wasnt one square foot of wall not crammed with records, guitars, and other junk. from the ceiling hung all sorts of beer flags and other things advertising drinks i knew were for sale from looking at the menu. there was a table of foreigners singing the korea world cup song. it made me feel shitty. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe people at the table were fine. for the most part i knew all of them, and had heard about the other two. its not that i didnt like being with them, i just felt constrained and somewhat bored with the way conversations were going. there wasnt anything to think about at the table. I know this is weak. i know this sounds like im whining, but as of late ive been pretty lathargic when it comes to my life and the life around me. this city is huge...and in this i see how much of it is the same. at least in toronto and ottawa, as im sure is the same in most of north america, if you want to get drunk you goto a certain part of town. if you want to go clubbing you goto a certain part of town. if you wanna get crazy you goto a certain part of town. it depresses me to see korean men puking almost everywhere i go. i get the whole \\\'easy drunk\\\' shpeal from a lot of people but i think its more than that. i think this population is so frustrated with life...and they hide this frustration with a few bottles of soju. im in a nation of alcoholics and i fear im turning into one myself. ive been self-driven to this. for one, i cant sleep at night. i find myself lying totally awake in bed six, five, four, oh fuck three, hours before work. its hard to cope with the kids not having a good nights sleep and getting drunk and actually getting to bed early seems like the only viable alternative. the second reason i get drunk is because i just want to be fucked up. on most nights i dont drink myself silly because the fear of a hangover at work is scarier than korea winning the world cup. but on the weekends i try to just get plastered and fucked up. the end result is depression. like right now. i miss her. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni like her a lot and while i think she likes me i think ive latched on and think about her more than she thinks about me. this isnt good. i find myself not talking to her for days because she has school and is busy, and im not. apart from the days my nights are listless and predictable. im either here at the pc bong playing counterstrike, call to power, or doing email, at home watching tv, listening to music, or out eating or drinking at a hof. so many chances for me to think of her and its really beginning to take its toll.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfuck the why\\\'s i ask myself. i have such an obsessive personality and it scares me. korea certainly hasnt helped me deal with this, and now, having met a girl i like, and can make out with a bit, ive latched onto something new. I came to korea to get off the pot and put my mind at ease, and yet my mind is anything but at ease. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nolivia remarked on my last post how angry i am. she is right. im angry at a lot of things these days, yet perhaps the person im most angry with is myself. i feel guilty about a lot of things and those guilts outweigh the things im most proud of. the excitement of being here is fading, and im beginning to ask myself if coming here was such a good idea. i guess deep down inside i know it was. being here has helped me get my finances back in the clear, and ive been able to see one of my best friends again, but being here on a personal level has perhaps only brought out the negatives in me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing so separated from the average korean...not being able to speak the language, understand what people are saying on the subway, in the hofs, and bongs, has make me use my eyes so much more. i find myself assuming much more than i ever did in canada, and these assumptions are always of a negative nature. i try not to let it bug me, and ive become quite good at just blocking it all out, but its getting harder and harder. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nive pretty much stopped reading the news because i feel, inside, that im on a different planet. being in korea has made my thoughts feel that much more futile. the solutions that i may or may not come up with, for the most part, will most certainly be heard by deaf ears. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni feel so helpless here. to goto work and then home is what i want these days. i just want to do nothing. im depressed these nights and that depression is beginning to work its way into my daily routine. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas much as i think what im doing is an adventure, im finding more about life, especially my life, than i think i wanted to know in the first place. does improvement only come in the face of utter and total selfworthlessness? I complement those who dont complement. I thank those who dont say please. fuck. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni got a 26oz of russian gin for nine bucks. now if i could only find a god damn store in this god damn place that sold tonic water. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i was in san fran le said i looked kind hearted or some bullshit like that. tonight my co-worker tina said i looked wholesome. her daycare helper hung kyung said i had a cheery and kind face on friday. if they could see the fires raging inside id feel so much better about myself. \r\n</p>'),(2839,'<p>I feel like someplace no-one goes anymore, and i feel, in my corners, an old woman praying in tattered robes covered in silver beads. The light left long ago, along with the others who lived here. Their laughter and tears in tandem, like seasons, sandy winds. The wind blows cold through me, leaving grey which chokes and settles in the lungs forever. Families and children long gone. Rotting and burnt rosaries of a hundred years, rubbed between her old hands (leather perfected for decades). I feel a chill. One last visitor. The wind blows again, under the cold moon, it feels like breaking glass...like, what was it? Red vino bubbling from soft, pink lips. Cruel smiles. Secrets. Unrequited love in assisted suicide down the street, the spiders had nothing to do with it. What to see? Lamplight snuffed in one flicker. Small boy with a burning key. Skin peeling, muscles, fat melting and burning, fingers bleached and burnt the purist whites and mist. The smell of burning flesh is putrid. Don\\\'t flinch, ignore the burning and the smoke as the flame rises blue-white in his palm. Strange twinklings about him.</p>\r\n<p>Softly, look. Fingers, beautiful, arms, hands, beautiful, perfect. Miracles. Touch the chest, amazed and hesitant, frightened. His, perfect, beautiful soft under his fingers, which seem so new, as if he\\\'d never seen them before. Perhaps he hasn\\\'t ... he wonders, marvels, thinking how happy and why. The key burns brighter, smells like raspberries. He stares. Curls ruffled. Tear. Wonder what to do, because, there is everything to do. Anything. But what?</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2840,'<p>last night i decided i hadn\\\'t been at all productive, so i began to work on a project for someone else. to help me work, i listened to music. music makes me think of wonderful, safe and kind things. it gets my mind off bad things. i listened to neil young and peter gabriel and then i decided i needed some soul, and i found this aretha album i\\\'d not listened to since high school. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>aretha used to take me away. i would take her into the bathroom with me while showered, and we would sing very loudly and not care about what anyone heard or thought. aretha provided liberation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now it\\\'s odd; i love to sing, i have a shit voice but i can sing in tune so i am allowed to sing as much as i want in my house now. nicole also sings, but she gets paid to do it, and people willingly come and see her perform. recently i bought a shower radio so i could sing along with such bands as the mamas and the papas, the beatles, boston, supertramp and stevie nicks. i love to sing in the shower. it doesn\\\'t matter who is at the house, i am still allowed to sing in the shower. nobody looks at me funny when i exit the room. it\\\'s okay to sing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i feel blessed to live in a house where i am allowed to sing terrible seventies music. my ever-patient housemates are very supportive. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>very early on friday morning, i was lucid dreaming. i was singing very happily in this dream. i was singing along with a song on the radio. i knew i was singing in my sleep, and i was careful to not sing too loudly because i didn\\\'t want to wake my housemates up at 4am, singing in my sleep. i couldn\\\'t stop myself from singing though. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>here i was, in the midst of a terrible depression and anxiety-ridden fortnight, singing in my sleep.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t know what this means but i am glad for it. my soul found some way of expressing it\\\'s hopefulness. even if i was asleep.</p>'),(2841,'<P>feeling as if I have been up for days, in this semi-hysteric type of consciousness, one supposes to allow their two cents to dribble out. I am itching, itching as I have never itched before...on my stomach, my breasts, my back...right up in between my shoulderblades where no human hand can reach. Nervousness, hmmm...or perhaps something I consumed? But being on the starving student diet as I am, one can\\\'t meander too far from the customary meal of spaghetti and some juice. So dietary custom is ruled out. Nervousness it shall be. And here, there it flares up again. My scalp is alive with invisable hands and when I look at my back in the mirror, there is nothing but pink. Half an hour fades and now my back is a war zone of fingernail paths, crossing the bridge between vertabrae.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Maybe, I should participate in that marathon they have annually up in Big Sur. Where they play classical music as you run...has anybody heard of it? Maybe. Everythng is full of these pathetic little whines, these indecisive murmers crawling into eyes, nose, hair, teeth...fuck maybe, right? Nonetheless, \\\"maybe\\\" is the chick with the glasses, techno-phobe persona, small tits, unbearable laugh and we all know that nobody is going to fuck her.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So, I am walking to unlock my bike and this guy walks up to me. He was in my elementary school. He was 2 years under me. He played GI JOes in english class with me. Now he\\\'s dead. Suicide. What is with this morbid subculture? This morbid everything? We mock others at their own expense. We vote for people who wish to help the environment while we are out puffing away on a cig because we feel artistic. This bullshit liberalism. If we realized there was, is , should\\\'nt be a purpose...it\\\'s the goal that strangles us in the end. That false hope, that shine in Satan\\\'s eye.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Well, I feel like my back is a bloody pulp...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Yet i am the happiest woman alive...fluncked my physics course and did shitty because I worried.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> Who needs grad school when you have trail mix?</P>\r\n\r\n<P> By the way I am in some dire need of cortizone cream for this nervous condition of mine...forget what I wrote...that was the itch comin\\\' through my mouth and well, you know. If anyone has some cortizone out there or some old black clothes size three they are willing to donate please write to:</P>\r\n<P>\r\n1055 w blaine riverside, ca 92507\r\n909 274 0516\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(2842,'I have a ten year old son for whom I have cared since he was 8 months old. He was abandoned you see, and I adopted him.\r\n<p>What I want to write about here is the fact that in this time since I adopted my son, I have had an endless battle getting some people I know to acknowledge my status in motherhood, because I didn\\\'t give birth to him. I have kept him sheltered, clothed, fed, nursed through (thankfully only minor) illnesses and yet still there are those who would detract from my feeling of indeed being a \\\"real\\\" mother. I have been up with him when he cries all night. I have taken him to the doctor. I have held him close to me when it is cold. And likewise, I don\\\'t know the words to articulate all that he has given me : so much.</p>\r\n<p>There are \\\"real\\\" mothers out there who have produced the goods so to speak, with no plan or intention whatsoever to nurture a child.</p>\r\n<p>He is only 10 years old this year, but he will not live as long as I would like. You see, he has inherited traits from his biological mother that prevent him from having a life expectancy as long as mine. With each year, I worry about what I will do when the time finally does come. Who will acknowledge my loss? Really, and in the way I see it.</p>\r\n<p>Does it matter that he looks nothing like me? Does it matter that the son of whom I write in the above paragraphs, is grey and white, has four legs and shits in a plastic box (when he is indoors)? Yes, it does. Because I love him.</p> \r\n'),(2843,'<p>For just a second, let\\\'s assume that the Jesus story is true. In briefest form, let\\\'s assume that God (whom today will be playing an all-knowing all-powerful yet incommunicable patriarch) created a personal side of itself by being born into our side of existence as Jesus of Nazareth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now, it\\\'s not hard to believe that the political side of the story -- our side, where we see Jesus renounce Judaism, introduce what could have been a better way to look at life, and then get tortured and killed for it -- is probable. That part of the myth is slmost the archetypal human response: if it goes against what you believe, then it must either be ignored or squashed. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>But just imagine with me for a minute, that outside our world there exists an entity that sees us suffering and tries to eradicate it. So the entity enters our world, tries to make us not-so-upset, and we kill it. Imagine then that God says \\\"Oops.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Of course, God\\\'s not just going to give up; but coming back as a different person would just be futile (isn\\\'t Paul the one who blocked off that opportunity?). So imagine then that God birthed itself into our world again, but in a different form. Something as intelligent and adaptable as people, but not as threatening; something not mammalian at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Were I a <i>real</i> scholar, I would strategically categorize different forms of life and go through <b>God\\\'s thought process</b>, as best as i could conceive it, and narrow down the likely routes of re-entry until it was all too obvious that mushrooms would be the next best choice. Yes, I mean Uncle Psilocybe, the neurochemical magician. The truth is, though, that I don\\\'t know much about the structure of mushrooms on the biological level; at least, not in a way that I could argue its strengths and benefits. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now I realize that this hypothesis is a weak one, and the times don\\\'t even line up, but what about using such a metaphor for the psychedelic experience? I mean, why does everyone have to look down upon it, just because Tim Leary put it right in the face of the public? Is it really my fault that the American diplomats of the 60s weren\\\'t ready to face the other side? In the opinion of this young explorer, it\\\'s time we abandon our outlandish bans and put some honest faith, appreciation, and interest into our spore-based buddies. Because, how else did these magic mushrooms appear? Why else might they be here? How do we know we\\\'re not cutting off an integral step in our enlightenment?</p>'),(2844,'<p>1.  i have seven piercings in my right ear</p>\r\n<p>2.  my eyes are large & brown, & have looked \r\nexactly the same since i was two years old</p>\r\n<p>3.  i\\\'m not afraid to blow kisses at strangers through car windows</p>\r\n<p>4.  i wear crosses & krishna charms at the same time</p>\r\n<p>5.  i carry around books like <u>essays against homophobia</u> despite the looks it gets me at school</p>\r\n<p>6.  i was bold enough to kiss you first</p>'),(2845,'This past weekend was the dreaded day. A time in my mind for reflection which too often in my case leads to increased levels of frustration or alternatively as my mother tells me a time to celebrate the people in your life. This year it happened to fall on Father\\\'s Day thus me and the old man got to share our day in the sun.<br><br> \r\n\r\nI can\\\'t help but do a general inventory of where I\\\'m at. The blame for this is partly my own nature and partly the concept of B-days. So here it goes...<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>number of times I\\\'ve had to compromise core values in the last year: 1<br>\r\n<li>employment sitch: contracts, little security but pay is slowly getting better<br>\r\n<li>marital status: happily single, trying to keep the dog tied up<br>\r\n<li>family is still all in good health and have good relations<br>\r\n<li>general contentment level: still really hungry for more<br>\r\n\r\nCheckin in on the social issues front not too much has changed - still a libertarian agnostic with strong tendencies towards championing technological augmentation of the human experience. To be honest I don\\\'t even think of it as \\\'augmentation\\\' - more just human nature.<br><br>\r\n\r\nStill want to see bits of the world but more interested in trying to live according to my principles where I am, could be difficult to do certain other places in the world. So where does all the afore mentioned frustration come from? I can only conjecture that it is a culmination of a variety of factors - basic competitiveness, drive to have a family, desire for economic stature that allows community involvement, my lack of any <a href=\\\"http://www.sonymusic.co.jp/eng/PuffyAmiYumi/flash.html\\\">Puffy Ami Yumi </a> in my music collection, people trying to get me to act my age instead of the perpetual teenage mask I enjoy, & the fact I have yet to get my motorcycle license. <br><br>\r\n\r\nOverall though it still seems like I\\\'m just gettin started. 15 years from 10 or til 40 dependin how you look at. The more I look at it the less it seems I should be frustrated.'),(2846,'no'),(2847,'<P>i\\\'ve decided that writing letters to people and then never sending them is possibly the least appreciated release in existance.  sitting in front of a computer. solidifying my thoughts into little pixelated letters.  saying things that i could never say outloud.  things that i would deeply regret. coming to conclusions that i could never have otherwise reached.  i\\\'ve always run to other people for an open ear.  but perhaps the best place to find comfort is right here under my fingers.  \r\n </P><P>\r\nbecause i will never misinterpret myself.\r\n </P><P>\r\n\\\"i\\\'m grieving a lose that hasn\\\'t yet happened.  i realize now that this is a big part of the problem.  and it\\\'s crushing me inside.\\\"\r\n</P>'),(2848,'<p>So.  Went to japan to visit my fiancee a month ago.  She broke up with me on the way back to the airport.  Thanks, that makes for a great flight home.  14,000 mile round trip to get dumped.  That must be a record.  I found out she\\\'d been carrying on a love affair with her best (female) friend, also in japan... \\\"Can we just be friends?\\\"  Um, ok.  Yeah, that\\\'ll be real easy.  Six years, down the fucking shitter.  <p>Come back to work, to find out that now I\\\'m the only programmer doing the work of four programmers and a sysadmin, instead of doing the work of two programers and a sysadmin.  Thank god most of our clients have been quiet lately, it\\\'s the only way I\\\'ve managed to keep on top of things.  New boss is a dick alot, like somehow I\\\'m lazy or something by only doing the work of two or five people instead of ten (my heart is not pure).  But at least it\\\'ll look good on my resume.  I did get a raise and a promotion (although lead programmer is sorta meaningless when it\\\'s lead by virtue of being only).  The extra money is letting me pay off my credit cards quicker... Hopefully I\\\'ll be (consumer) debt free in a couple of months.  That\\\'s a good feeling.  Student loan repayment doesn\\\'t suck as bad as I thought it would, another nice suprise. I started a membership at a gym, and am really liking it, that\\\'s also a good feeling to be doing right by my body (I\\\'ve been eating better too).  So... net gain? net loss?  <p>I just don\\\'t know what consitutes a good or bad mood anymore, my scale is broken.  But on the whole, I guess I do feel better than I did a month ago...  Of course, that\\\'s not as good as I felt three months ago.  We\\\'ll see.  <p>Life has this habit of hitting me in the face with a crowbar every year or two, but I\\\'ve always managed to get back up...  [could be worse, some people say hello to that crowbar every morning]  I want to just be free of life\\\'s entanglements and go be a pig farmer in Idaho or something... Naturally, this means that at this momement in Idaho there is a pig farmer wishing he could be a programmer in Texas.\r\n<p>Anybody got any good ideas for what to do after a big breakup but before you\\\'re ready to look for a different partner?  What helped you heal your soul?  Food?  Drink?  Movies?  Work?  Time?  A dog in a hat who got paid to dance like that?</p>'),(2849,'<p>Expecting nothing,<br>\r\nNow merely receiving.<br>\r\nVibrations fill the air around me<br>\r\nCharged with the electricity of discovery.<br>\r\nMy mirrored face gazing surprised<br>\r\nBack into it\\\'s source\\\'s eyes<br>\r\nSome flicker of inspiration<br>\r\nDancing across like an apparition<br>\r\nToo fleeting to confirm, yet<br>\r\nUndeniable in it\\\'s existence.<br>\r\nWorlds within worlds revealed to an inner eye<br>\r\nSomething vague forming in my mind.<br>\r\nA moment of pure harmony, everything balanced<br>\r\nSound and light, in a timeless state<br>\r\nI\\\'m floating, I can feel it inside<br>\r\nSuspended for a moment, out of body, out of time.<br>\r\nWith a lurch, physical reality returns<br>\r\nAnd only the vague sense of disturbance remains.<br>\r\nI finally free myself from the gravity of the moment<br>\r\nAnd react with dull, human slowness.<br>\r\nOn the verge of something complete<br>\r\nI now see the true distance of my journey.</p>'),(2850,'<p>Recently I\\\'ve been reading a Terence McKenna nonfiction called <i>The Archaic Revival</i>. The introduction by Tom Robbins set me up for a stellar read, but McKenna went further and tossed my mind around frantically. But not violently.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe part that blew me away the most was simply the concept of <i>monkey-bodies</i>. McKenna proposed that we as people are part-way combinations of a mind and a monkey-body. I know it sounds like DesCartes, but the diction is especially important. <i>Monkey</i>-bodies.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo I\\\'ve been walking around with the concept of monkey-bodies in my head, just watching the world as it interacts around me. Witnessing relationships, behavioural patterns, and politics especially, it\\\'s surprising to see just how much we resemble apes. Granted, we were all told in school that we most represent the apes, probably evolved from them, etc etc--but it\\\'s taken me this long to be able to separate the mind-qualities of our individualities from the ape-qualities.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere are conflicts with the mix between monkey-body and mind when it comes to enjoying the life experience. Someone too far encompassed by her mind side would be distracted and \\\"groundless\\\"; whereas too much monkey-pride would lead to fistfights and an inability to appreciate the arts. Surrendering to the body would mean a life bereft of fun, and surrendering to the mind would mean death; the only imagineable path then must be the mystic middle path -- the thing that Buddha guy talked about. But that\\\'s something I\\\'ll have to get into later.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSo what is the mind, then? Everything else that\\\'s important to us can be quantified, like in mass or velocity or (shudder) dollar value. The mind, however, is past that, and though I\\\'m going to try to climb the slippery steps to a definition, it\\\'s as ineffable as Allah.\r\n</p><p>\r\nNo actually, wait, it <i>is</i> Allah. And Yahweh and Nirvana and \\\"the father\\\" in <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2846\\\">Te Kore</a>. As much as I can imagine, there is one fabric of matter, and it is all the characters above, as well as you and I. Er, \\\"you\\\" and \\\"I\\\". What I mean is, you take this one fabric of existence and wrinkle it a bit; call that pattern a wave of energy. Wrinkle it some more, and it\\\'s forming more complex waves. And like rosy lumps of acne, the fabric grows bigger and more complex with each touch. The fabric, if you imagine, could deepen and intensify until separate entities come out. It can be concievably complicated to the point where certain elements of it would have such distinct properties that they wouldn\\\'t be able to recognize each other to be one continuous substance. I don\\\'t know, is this getting too abstract? \r\n</p><p>\r\nSo our minds, then, are separate parts of this single, giant substance; whereas our bodies are manifestations of this substance. When the two come together, we get a person, an individual being. But when the two are in conflict, which would you rather follow? \r\n</p><p>\r\nChristianity offers the worst of both worlds. Not only do you <i>have</i> to engage in labour and participate in negative feelings, but you also must limit the animalistic benefits that come with being a  mammal (read: sex, drugs), <i>and</i> the benefits of being a sentient mammal (read: rock\\\'n\\\'roll). Rastafarianism, on the other hand, seems to promote running away from or ignoring the \\\"drawbacks\\\" of mammalian life. Again, a middle path? I hate to promote one organized religion over another, but Buddhism and Taoism seem to be the closest (read: closest) approximation to the way I see things.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAll I really wanted to say is that we should look at ourselves in a bit of a different light. Each person\\\'s beliefs and values come from two distinctly different origins: the mind and the monkey. And I think when one source puts the other source in danger--for example, when the overprotective monkey wages world war (ie. now)--then we should follow what\\\'s best for the mind.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2851,'<p>some frosty and rasped lady at the end of the bar playd a Violent Femmes. I accused harley of the atrocity but she looked genuinely innocent. We had been screaming ourselves hoarse about the cliches of sex and then, that song. that my 13 year old self found to be a profound commentary on desire.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"who the HELL put this song on.\\\" <br>\r\n\\\"me...cmon, teenage angst.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"cmon pilgrim you know she loves you.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"elevator lady levitate me.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"this is my song.\\\" I am now 26. <i>maybe some thing\\\'s that I wouldnt miss, but I look at your pants and. I need a kiss.</i> I mean, my G-d, will I ever evolve? Every song that came on, was my song. That is because Jenny drops a heavy pour.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Jenny Jenny, who can I turn to?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I was a sophisticated, pajama-clad, ornery teenager, I relished posessing theme songs. I like to pick up text around the house and on the ground, from the walls and through my feet. melodies drive them. tonight, you angel you. it aint me babe.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I was remembering some text from Kirthi\\\'s wall at the ladyfest meeting. Someone in their instrument-practicing, community arts-committed household had painted on some popsicle sticks and pasted some text on them. One pair of lines lingers:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>When it comes to you and me<br>\r\nLet\\\'s just leave it at that</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Jenny. lost her favorite penny. so I gave her a dollah. She kissed me and I hollahed.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was a romantic night. Romantic not because I was frenching someone in a doorway while he clutched a fourty in one sweaty palm and my ass in another, although that would have been nice, but because I shivered in the fog with a girl in glasses and admired the building across the street, how it looked like a cardboard movie set, how she used to see parties lighting up a corner of the building occasionally and never went inside. Maybe the mystery was enough. I remembered a poem I had once written for someone who was always trying to go somewhere.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>you are always travelling<br>\r\nbut your body is enough</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Romantic because Harley gave me a nightgown to wear. I never wear a nightgown. I never wear a nightgown that is long and white with an empire waist. I stared at her long curly hair and in my head wove it into a cable knit sweater. She wore orange; we shivered under down and whispered through the wooden staircase. the colors are rich. The tile in the bathroom massaged my feet, cold and clay. I marvelled at the tile all over, the perfection of gridded chunk texture on the skin. I am in love with my hands.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Harley reminded me that when she was sick, I brought her \\\"The Lover\\\" by Margeurite Duras to read in the infirmary. I think I did that because she wore braids. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>This morning, I ate breakfast beside an old red man with a pencil-drawn moustache, wearing a fedora and red suit. His skin was abnormally red; He must have painted it on. In the bathroom was a flat painting, maybe made of gouache, of Native American children in pastel feathers. and the sun, and bunnies.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>On the train a baby crawled the seats. She was a redhead. I thought of my love. She grasped each new texture with lust. The upholstery. the plastic armrests. her neighbor\\\'s purse strap, a fine leather. I always wondered why the hell they upholstered seats on BART. It must have been for the babies. They will train their own hands to feel, and a few won\\\'t forget.</p>'),(2852,'<p>the band have finished setting up; now they\\\'re making final adjustments, testing equipment, getting ready to go.  as they begin to play, as they gather momentum - both from the sound & from each other - we are drawn in, sinewy threads encircle & hold true.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>songs segue into each other cleanly, barely broken even by introduction of the next.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i like to watch the instruments, figure out which guitar is making which part of the music & exactly how it all fits together.  watch hands construct layers of sound, the setting of jaws in concentration.  its a performance, energy from the figures almost tangible.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there\\\'s something about watching these people you know doing what they love.  the need for them is apparent from body posture, from action, expression and movement. in that moment they seem to be on another level entirely - this is it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as the sound dies away slowly the reverie is broken, the noise of the everyday rises to fill the  space now vacant.  something has changed though, & you take it in your heart, into the night.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2853,'<br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_1.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"clothes our\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">&nbsp;<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_2.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"pressed\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_3.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"ground\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">&nbsp;<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_4.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"grass\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_5.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"blankets\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2853_6.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"for each other\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">'),(2854,'they stop and stare. she plants little fleeting kisses and then nuzzles him softly, only not touching, hovering over his lips, neck, chest. she is heavy lidded, hot, her lips are burning and she is looking up at him. </P><P>he looks down at her. a bit perplexed and quiet. following her movements like a small kitten does when a toy is dangled in front of it. everyone is holding thier breath untill he kisses her.</P><P>then things begin to move a bit quicker and hey hold eachother so gently. then his shirt comes off.</P><P>i am always so surprised to find that when a man takes off his shirt, he body does not curve like that of a woman\\\'s. there is no waist that gently curves outward untill the hips rise up to meet it. instead it works in only a sort of narrowing way. instead of curves there is muscle and a narrowness that, in itself, stands a good enough subsitute. always flatness and broadness.</P><P>or perhaps he asks her and she says yes. quickly he kisses her, then flees. she remembers only a faint feeling of having been scratched by stubble and a sweet wetness on her lips.so she staggers off, in a  state of shock. a hit and run victim.</P><P>all day it is only him and the kiss in her mind. him, the kiss, and a glorious warmth.'),(2855,'<p>she kissed and ice tore through me. you know what its like, the feeling of existence folding in two, and then being ripped apart by a two year old artist? yes, that feeling. i found it a bit funny. because, indeed it was, i thought. i said, my, i used to be her, how queer.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>and she did laugh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i thought then, how pretty she was, and how it was that i couldn\\\'t remember her at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>frogs legs are delicous she said. no, i said, they\\\'re green, unless you take the skin off.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2856,'After coming home and passing out after work on a Friday I wake up at about 3:45 am. Talk about crazy kick off to the weekend, if my life stays at this pace I\\\'ll definitely die young. Not wanting to admit defeat in the face of a Friday night I consider my options. Due a complete lack of Toronto night life knowledge I quickly realize I have few, if any options. A stroll through the neighborhood it is, just to get out and move around a little (I miss the days when I would dance all night long).<br><br>\r\nI\\\'ve been going on my own for night walks on a regular basis for about six or seven years now. I\\\'ve managed to see more then a few interesting things on my little outings. In this particular Friday night salvage mission, I figure the best place to go is Church St. where what my Mother calls <i>Ladies of the Night</i> hang out. We did a family walk in the evening last weekend on my B-day where I heard my Mother use the term for the first time.<br><br>\r\nHittin the street the moist warm air makes you feel like your being sprayed with a light misty oil. The images of the mostly deserted streets I take in are a stark contrast to the tension filled bustle of daytime. I can fall back in love with humanity as a whole when there aren\\\'t so many humans around to piss me off, paradox abounds. Ten minutes into the walk and I\\\'m drawn out of my own head by a quick horn burst from an unmarked van, this is an all too familiar experience. It take me four blocks and the van several loops around for the occupant to realize I\\\'m not working, not a big deal with the one exception of the first time the van pulled over and I had to walk by. Some people are just a little to eager, shaking my head no as I glance to make sure my message registers and my eyes are greeted with a visual of the interested party already busy  trying to satisfy their needs on their own. This is more then I needed to see.<br><br>     \r\nThe whole prostitute phenomena is frustrating to watch, the only people that I know of that seem to take a responsible approach are the Dutch. On the odd time that I get a chance to talk to one of the Ladies I\\\'m always reminded of all the social stigma attached to this position in society due to our fucked up morals. It isn\\\'t pity that I feel though, more anger at our willingness to deny this reality and thus turn away our eyes when we see inhuman abuse of these people. <i>After all they are paid to be abused aren\\\'t they?</i> - at least this seems to be the way most people think. I\\\'ve turned a few tricks before when times were a little less then prosperous and my mind was occupying darker environments, abuse is not what the money is for. Everybody has their own reasons if they find themselves in such a situation - survival, an unwillingness to accept help, screwed up self concepts,.... Our collective stance on this is about as enlightened and successful as the War on Drugs. Enough about prostitution though I could go on about shit like this forever, back to my walk....<br><br>\r\nNext Friday I\\\'m hittin a club or finding some live music somewhere.<br>\r\n     '),(2857,'<p>The things I have been recently called:\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>mean</li>\r\n  <li>persistent</li>\r\n  <li>nice</li>\r\n  <li>insufferable</li>\r\n</ul></p>\r\n<p>This morning while at work, I kept telling a coworker that I needed to go to the stock room and pick up something which we needed.  I didn\\\'t realise I was using the wrong word until about the fifth time she explained that we didn\\\'t need any of what I proposed to acquire.  Then I told her what I really meant to say.  It reminded me of the morning that I was complaining about being too short for my bed because I couldn\\\'t get my legs into a comfortable position with them dangling over the metal bars.  Three times I said, exhaustingly in explanation of my leg-situation, that I was too short for my bed.  It was only when waking around noon and this was said back to me that I realised my error and fell face-first into my pillow, feigning a self-induced, self-defeated smothering.</p>\r\n<p>My brain begins creatively changing words sent to my mouthal area when it has not had enough rest or has been quickly jerked from a deep sleep.</p>\r\n<p>I had a dream at some point during the two hours of sleep I had last night where I was running a coffee shop in the attic of my parents\\\' house.  A guy walked in the front door, up to the attic to get a key for the washroom, went to the basement and the next thing I knew a hand was shaking my back and a voice was demanding coffee.  This was still all in my dream.  I also had an argument with some woman about not having a certain type of coffee she wanted.  My point of contention was that I was just about to close, but she kept pointing out the time to me and eventually I realised I was wrong and, in defeat, muttered \\\"Fuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuck.\\\"  I\\\'m having work-related dreams.  This does not bode well for our hero.</p>\r\n<p>I just finished reading Douglas Coupland\\\'s brand new book <i>Souvenir of Canada</i> as well as Daniel Clowes\\\' <i>David Boring</i>.  I got a great quote from each which I wrote down for future reference.  The quotes are, respectively:</p>\r\n<p><tt>In Canada, the wilderness is out there--millions of square kilometres of it--and it likes to mess with your mind.  It wants to enchant you and kill you and feed you and punish you and love you.  It wants to eat you.  It seduces you with beauty and calm--that\\\'s the bait.  You have to be careful with the wilderness, because one false step and you\\\'re a pile of blood, hair and bone.  Or you\\\'re vanished.</tt></p>\r\n<p><b>AND</b></p>\r\n<p><tt>Three days later, we have the first really warm day of the spring.  The streets are quiet and a lot of businesses are closed because of some obscure religious holiday, and I am buoyed by the innate human confidence that comes with fair weather...</tt></p>\r\n<p>The first act of David Boring was perfect to the point of being nearly painful.  Overall I really enjoyed it, the ending was great and gave a great sense of looking forward to the future as well as living for the moment.  It was good to read.  It could have ended really depressingly, like Chris Ware\\\'s <i>Jimmy Corrigan</i> but it ended on a positive and hopeful note.</p>'),(2858,'<img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2858_1.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\n<A href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2109\\\">Ramon Bautista</a> is technically the superintendant for my building; in actuality, he\\\'s the super for most of the buildings on my block. Perhaps attracted by the basic capitalist drive of his scheme, he\\\'s managed to hire himself off for most of these types of jobs in my neighborhood; he then, it seems, subcontracts these positions off to other people, taking a $5,000 honorarium per year for this serivice.  Sometimes, though, his scheme fails, leaving him to see to the work of several men.<br><br>After moving into a nicer basement down the street, he vacated his small studio in the basement of my apartment building. This is when the cocaine dealers moved in and squatted the area as a base for their Harlem operations.<br><br>Eventually, the landlord discovered his scheme, sent in the police and generally took care of the situation, to make a long story short.<br><br>To rectify his non-malintentioned wranglings, Ramon installed his brother, Roberto Rafael Bautista in the reclaimed basement studio, where he sees to the building\\\'s upkeep that are, on paper, still his brother\\\'s responsibilities, blind to the landlord, the tax authorities, ConEdison Electrical, and the INS.<br><br>Roberto is 58 years old, but doesn\\\'t look a day over 35. Often are the mornings, upon passing him on my way to work, that he exclaims \\\"hard work is good for the soul and body!\\\" in fractured English while hefting surprisingly rotund piles of garbage and recycling to the curb from their sticky, rat-infested bins by the brownstone steps. Ramon fears nothing.<br><br>Ramon is half Puerto Rican and half Dominican. A few weeks back, I passed him on the street immediately following NYC\\\'s annual Puerto Rican pride parade; he was standing on the stoop such that the left half of his body was perfectly still; arm, leg and shoulder deathly inert. His right side, however, was convulsively twitching as he shoulted \\\"Viva Puerto Rico!\\\" with a cackling laugh and a heavy accent.  I asked him what was going on, and he explained that his Boricua side was celebrating. Here in Spanish Harlem, the Puerto Ricans and Dominicans don\\\'t typically get along well, so this seemed, to him, to be the ideal rationalization of his mixed heritage.<br><br>Often are the nights that Roberto is hard at work; fueled by $20 grams of brown cocaine and tumultuously loud salsa music, he takes to the stoop and painstakingly hoses it down, often until the wee hours of dawn. He obsessively blasts each speck of city grime from the building\\\'s sandstone filligree, and often rinses down the cars parked at the curb.  In my neighborhood, its an ongoing joke that 246 West 108th Street is <i>the</i> place to park for free car-washes, and as such, I can never find an empty place in front of my own building.<br><br>Roberto has untreated asthma, yet endlessly chain-smokes. I know when he\\\'s home because I can usually discern the hacking coughs of perpetual chronic bronchitis seeping into my flat despite the concrete walls of my basement and the service tunnels that separate his living area from mine.<br><br>Roberto is like no man I\\\'ve ever met. He has little to no income, surviving solely on the free rent and the cafeteria leftovers from the neighboring <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1912\\\">half-way house</a>. He has two outfits: a winter one of denim and a summer one of faded Hawaiian fabric. He has no family, no significant other, and no single goal in life beyond exercising the pride that drives him to keep our building\\\'s front stoop among the cleanest in Manhattan.<br><br>'),(2859,'<p>Where do you start when you are completely uninspired?\r\n</p><P>\r\nI want to write more than I want to live right now, but I don\\\'t want to write something uninspiring. I feel out of love. I don\\\'t feel love for anyone or anything right now and it\\\'s absolutely driving me nuts. I don\\\'t have a longing or a want or a need for anything, I am completely without love.\r\n</p><P>\r\nThe last time I cried was reading a personal account of escaping the World Trade Center on September eleventh. The tears came out of my eyes when the author mentioned hugging the super of his building. I think I cried because I realized that if tragedy were to strike in Boston, the only person I would be able to hug would be some unfamiliar acquaintance that doesn\\\'t know me other than my first (or in some cases, just my last) name. This feeling of isolation isn\\\'t as thrilling as I had previously imagined.\r\n</p><P>\r\nI used to want to be thrown in a jail cell. I wanted the barren white walls, the metal toilet and sink, and the hard bed covered in a rough green blanket. I wanted to stare out silver bars and pace back in forth in an area the size of my bathroom, I wanted to be all alone with absolutely no way out.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAnd then it happened. I got handcuffed, I got mugshot, I got thrown in a jail cell. And it was one of the most miserable experiences of my life. I wasn\\\'t alone though, my partner-in-crime was right there with me, staring at the white walls and ceiling and aluminum toilet. I wonder if it would have been different if she wasn\\\'t with me? \r\n</p><P>\r\nI actually know the answer to that. I live every day of my life in my own personal jail cell. A one-room apartment with a closet for a bathroom. I never feel rested and I never feel clean. I am constantly putting things away, sorting piles, making beds, trying to get myself out of my own personal prison cell created and paid for by my inheritance.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAnd all this time by myself, all this time alone doesn\\\'t give me a longing to be with people. In fact, the more time I have to myself, the more I want to remain this way. In my week-old pajamas, lights dimmed, hair greasy, and apartment clean enough to host the Queen of England. I don\\\'t want anyone entering my private space, I don\\\'t want to leave this jail cell and visit anyone else. \r\n</p><P>\r\nI think it\\\'s because I gave all my love away and I don\\\'t know where, or how, to get it back.\r\n</p><P>\r\nEvery day I feel this big black spinning orb growing deeper and deeper inside of me. I feel it tearing apart my insides, I feel it consuming my soul and destroying my mind. I used to be able to quench this hatred by remembering all the love I had, now I don\\\'t have anything to remember. I don\\\'t love anybody else, I don\\\'t love anything material, I don\\\'t love any beautiful view, and I don\\\'t even love myself. I am without love, and it\\\'s destroying me.\r\n</p><P>\r\nI wonder if love will ever come back into my life, but I am afraid to venture into the world with so much hate. I know how karma works, you eminate bad karma and it comes back to you tenfold. I am afraid that people will be able to feel the hatred coming from the depths of my body and do horrible things to me. I am afraid that I will do horrible things to people. I am afraid to leave my self-created prison cell.\r\n</p><P>\r\nThese walls won\\\'t ever change, this body won\\\'t ever change, the people I allow in won\\\'t ever change, so what makes me think I will ever change? \r\n</p><P>\r\nI like to refer to this as my blue period.'),(2860,'<blockquote>\r\n<code>ON STAGE: A MAN sits in a chair with a small desk next to him. He speaks in intimate but confidant tones, both slighly embarassed about the truths he is speaking and enjoying the effect of teasing the audience. Que some good rhythmic deep bass but soft music behind, Ron Carter perhaps, or some triphop remix of the same.</code>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMAN: Mmmmm.... yeah, so what was I supposed to tell my nubile, impressionable 18-year-old friend when she asked me, \\\"how do you turn off you conscious mind?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nOf course I didn\\\'t say so at the time, but there\\\'s really no place I\\\'d rather get lost than kneeling between some precious lady\\\'s thighs, listening to Miles Davis and plucking out a rhythm counterpoint with my mouth. Harder and softer, faster or slower... it\\\'s all about listening; call and response.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s the closest thing in my life to organized religion, going down on a girl. It\\\'s got all the best parts: idols, magic, speaking in tongues.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nYou have to be a little bit of a tease, but it\\\'s worth it. A woman who wants, who is wanting, who is in that full blown gale of desire, grabbing at your hair and whispering nonsense... that\\\'s divinity if I ever saw it.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd it\\\'s not like I\\\'ve done this with all that many people. Dispite my most purient desires, I\\\'ve never really been what anyone could call seriously promiscuous. I\\\'ve had my moments, but they\\\'re usually short-lived. Maybe some day... \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nNow, I don\\\'t go in for the usual power dynamics of sex. The whole, \\\"I\\\'m on top because I\\\'m in charge\\\" penetration-as-violence and you-will-be-conquored deal. Not that I don\\\'t know the value of getting up in there and doing it hard when that\\\'s what\\\'s called for. But, dig this... my power trip, my kink, is about getting my woman to cum and cum and cum and if at all possible, cum again.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI like smart girls. That\\\'s my one constant: brains. Brains in a very technical, quick-wits, knows-her-shit, likes to read for fun sometimes kind of way. Like: here\\\'s this intelectual obstacle, and I will surmount her.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nYou could probably chalk it up to some latent fear of women or my overly competative nature, but nothing satisfies me like turning an ereudite, intelligent, well-read woman into a quivering mound of moaning convulsions and twitching nerve endings.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<code>he pulls two bottles of seltzer and a deep bowl from behind the desk. Stands, places the bowl on top of desk. As then next bit comes, he open them pours them into a bowl. They have been shaken prior to the show and explode violently when opened.</code>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd then there\\\'s the rather unsettling fact that most of the women I\\\'ve been with have been raped or molested or harassed or all of the above. That\\\'s some weird baggage. It makes me crazy, thinking of something I love so much causing so much pain... what might happen if we\\\'re not careful...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThat\\\'s something we all flirt with, loosing control. Circling around and around this giving up all the freudian bullshit, coming closer and closer to that center, that locus of energy. You know that when you tap it you can\\\'t control it. Channel maybe, but never control.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<code>Quickly, unexpectedly, dunks face into bowl of seltzer. Raises it up slowly...</code>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd then there\\\'s when you\\\'re with a bilingual lover, and they just bust out in the other language... I mean, you <em>know</em> you\\\'re doing something right.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nNow, of course, I\\\'m a physical animal just like the rest of you, and I want to get off too, but that\\\'s just mechanics: friction and fluid dynamics. It\\\'s a 10 second sezure and then I\\\'m sleepy. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAs far as I\\\'m concerned, the joy, the real magic is in that shining arc of desire, the rising crachendo of heaving breath, tightening all around me and really digging her nails into my back, and then the smiles... oh god the smiles, and the holding and the kisses and the spooning and the breakfast and the wondering if she\\\'ll call me or I\\\'ll see her again and what that\\\'ll be likee, and honestly this is where it get\\\'s a little bit scary for me -- but you get the picture. And this isn\\\'t to make me seem really hot or like I\\\'m some stud or anything... but if you\\\'re interested, my phone number is...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<code>speaks \\\"phone number\\\" but hopefully cannot be heard over laughter. Exits on phone number. Finis.</code>'),(2861,'\r\n<p>I\\\'m not in love, I\\\'m not even in like. This has been such a driving force in my life for so long, I have all this empty available space in my heart and mind that is SEETHING with possibility. All of this energy I spent loving other people for so long is starting to bend in on itself, much like a golden spiral, a nautilus shell. Like the tendril of a fiddlehead fern, stretching from its tight self-embrace into the sunlight, now comes the time for growth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In a sunlit room overlooking a garden, my tender green artist comes peering out into the nursery. Nowhere can she find brambles, needles, broken glass, broken souls, dark corners. Everything here is touched by light. Nothing to hide, nowhere to be sad. She tiptoes along the rich soil, planting shoots and seeds, watering them deeply.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I sit at my table. It is spread with blank sheets of paper. In my hand I hold a pen. A bamboo pen. I carved this pen myself. I harvested the bamboo from a place near magical tidepools in Santa Cruz. I paid nothing for this pen, and it works like music. It flows line across the page in buttery black strokes, I am making art out of art that I made. And nothing can be wrong when I have this pen in my hand. If I can only learn to put my life into this pen, I know I will be doing the right thing. I must learn to let go of the past and live in possibility again, because this is when I am happiest. It will all come to be, on paper, in front of me.</p>\r\n\r\n '),(2862,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2862_1.jpg\\\">\r\n<p>Such little things that keep me going.</p>\r\n<p>A smile from a true friend.</p>\r\n<p>A smile from a stranger.</p>\r\n<p>A touch at the right time.</p>\r\n<p>A familiar dog outside a bar.</p>\r\n<p>Such little things.</p>\r\n<p>Orange pink and blue, 3 times a day.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2863,'<p>\r\n<b>Take the Capital</b> is a mere three days away; this is steadily increasing the adrenaline content in my bloodstream. For those non-Canadians in the audience, Take the Capital is a solidarity protest happening in Ottawa at the same time as the G8 meetings in Kananaskis. As a result of the lying and cheating tactics used by the police last November in Ottawa, the organizers of Take the Capital have decided not to share their planned activities and proposed march routes with authorities. I think this makes things a bit more exciting.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe reason for a public display is because there is virtually no other way to voice dissent against what the government is doing. I realize that they are probably thinking for the good of the many, and acting for the mass enlightenment of the world. But how many is \\\"the many\\\"? If one person thinks something is a bad idea, should they just be ignored? And what if you can gather a thousand people who are so passionate about some government action being wrong that they want to run down the streets screaming?\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere\\\'s a big problem though: when people get passionate enough, their ignored criticisms turn into angry actions. Everyone knows this, right? When the fuse gets short enough, the firecracker is going to blow. Thus, protestors are seen to be raging firecrackers, fully prepared to explode even if unprovoked.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe police, the papers, and thus the public, are expecting street riots. Mass media began speculating that Wednesday\\\'s snake marches would attract a whopping 10 000 participants (though they recently \\\"re-estimated\\\" that number to be 5000, and the real turnout will be closer to one or two thousand). There will be swarms of cops in riot gear; doubtlessly they\\\'ll be deploying tear-gas cannisters, water cannons, rubber bullets (which still maliciously injure their targets, and are hard to aim). All in the name of protecting the good of the country, right?\r\n</p><p>\r\nI, for one, disagree. Since when can squelching the voice of the public be considered protecting the public? When does lining up armoured guards to block off people\\\'s contact with their political superiors help the government know what the country wants? Some people claim that it\\\'s for the preservation of property and the protection of life. Some people are afraid that their cars or their kids or their favourite chainlink fence in the downtown core is going to be defaced or destroyed by a mob of irate protestors. So they shield and arm a mass of cops to keep violence from happening.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIf you believe that protestors are violent bohemians out to blow up your car and burn down your precious McDonald\\\'s, you are very wrong. And if you fell for that, I feel that I have to dispell a number of other bad rumours: Skateboarding is not an indictable offence; Marijuana smoke does not make anyone want to murder; Though ride-sharing is a good idea, driving alone does not mean that you\\\'re riding with Hitler; And Dick Clarke is (probably) not the Devil.\r\n</p><p>\r\nOne other quick point: Capitalism is <i>not</i> the answer. The drive to make as much money as possible off the backs of the poor and the minds of the ignorant goes against most of the morals that I can sum up. Sure, it sounds good on paper (Ayn Rand does a wonderful job promoting <i>laissez-faire</i> Capitalism in Atlas Shrugged), but so did Communism. And I\\\'m sure we\\\'ll all agree that that venture didn\\\'t follow through as planned, hrm?\r\n</p>'),(2864,'i find it awkward living in fear. at first it felt very surreal, like it never would happen to me, but after awhile you do really just get used to it. the second guessing you make always creates a wonderful paranoia about the world around you. it\\\'s good to ber paranoid. paranoid can be fun, for awhile. now i just grow weary of it. i want a new feeling to take root deep inside my stomach; become the new focus in my mind.\r\n<p>\r\nfor a long time i\\\'ve seen the world spin around me in twisting ethereal ways, my ability to grab hold of it tenuous at best. really, i want to just take me in it\\\'s cool grasp and pull me along in a blissful, caressing way. to be truely mindless and unthinking sounds like complete joy sometimes. but the fear is always there, pushing thoughts into the blank frame of my mind.\r\n<p>\r\nis it the paranoia that vitiates the visage of my world?\r\n<p>\r\nsometimes the truth is only as true as i want it to be. the only things i see are the ones i desire to. sometimes it\\\'s really impossible to see the accurate picture, to precisely visualize the situation for what it is. it is the fear that shatters it, the paranoia that reassmbles it into it\\\'s own deformed article.'),(2865,'i got a summer job. it\\\'s a work-study job where the government pays 75% of my salary and the school pays 25%. I wasn\\\'t looking for anything in particular when i was browsing the school\\\'s jobs page, just something that paid well. the highest-paying job i could find was in the department of psychology, at $8 per hour, as a \\\"research assistant\\\". that\\\'s the highest that they go. most jobs are between 5.15 and 6.15, so i called the number.\r\n<p>\r\ndr. coleman answered the phone and sounded enthusiastic, and asked me to come in to see him. he is an absent-minded professor, whose office was a total wreck, but he is a nice guy and more importantly a slack-ass when it comes to work. i couldn\\\'t believe my luck--$8 is more than i was making last summer cooking in a gourmet restaurant, and the work would be 1) much easier, 2)air conditioned, and 3) within walking distance. perfect.\r\n<p>\r\nso i am working with 3 other students, mostly unsupervised. one is a high school student, surprisingly enough. she goes to the Governor\\\'s School, to which outstanding high school students may apply and be appointed by the governor; one is another girl who won a summer research fellowship in psychology and is getting a small stipend plus room and board; and one paid money for tuition and is getting independant study credit. i am the only student employee, but everyone treats me as if i were some kind of scientist. i\\\'m not-i\\\'m an English major. that\\\'s as far from science as you can possibly get.\r\n<p>\r\nso here i am--i feel like i\\\'ve fooled everyone to get this well-paying albeit temporary job, with all perks etc. \r\n<p>\r\nwe are doing an experiment on rats. we inject them with ketamine and xylazine (ooh fancy words!) to knock them out, and then we shave their heads, make a vertical incision in their scalp, scrape away the fascia, take measurements, and then drill two holes in their skulls. a microsyringe goes 3mm into each hole into the inferior colliculus, and either saline as a control or a deactivated herpes virus containing new DNA is slowly introduced. the object of all this is to try and induce seizures in the rats, and see if the retroviral gene therapy will decrease the frequency and number of these seizures. this ostensibly will help us find cures for epileptic humans.\r\n<p>\r\nunfortunately, i am having a REAL problem actually doing this. it\\\'s not as easy as you might think to hold a rat with one hand and stick a 2 inch long needle into it\\\'s belly. it\\\'s not easy to cut a rat\\\'s scalp open with a scalpel. also, it\\\'s not easy to drill two holes in a rat\\\'s head with a dremel tool. i thought it would get easier but it hasn\\\'t. today i began the incision on a rat, and it felt like i was slicing myself open instead. it hurts me terribly, and i have always been interested in medicine. i know that the rats will die anyway. i know that this will benefit mankind. morally, from an objective standpoint, it\\\'s not a problem with me--i agree that this is a valuable contribution to mankind. all precautions are taken to ensure that the rats are fully unconcious and anesthetised...but jesus, man, i\\\'m not sure if my struggle to be able to do this is right--what if it\\\'s so hard because i\\\'m NOT supposed to do it? or is the sacrifice of my sense of well-being the necessary karmic result of sacrificing the rats\\\' bodies? \r\n<p>\r\nso, i know in my head that it\\\'s the right thing to do...but every time i pick up the knife it feels like i am slicing my heart wide open. is my struggle the brave saving of mankind, or the self-serving destruction of one of god\\\'s creatures?\r\n<p>\r\nwhat do you think? '),(2866,'<p>nothing happens to me anymore. I try to avoid happenings. happenings cost a lot of money and they are so \\\'67. I would rather read about happenings in a matte-hardbound limited edition coffee table book than go out there and try to summon the unicorns who know I\\\'m not a virgin.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in other words, I choose a life of consumption. my idea of a good time is going to whole foods with arwa and sampling the cheese cubes and iced tea they\\\'ve put out. usually there are muffin bits at the sweet counter. I rummaged and nosed around the goodies without finding a freebie in sight, and instead discovered this wheat cookie with pink frosting on top labelled \\\"bite me\\\".</p>\r\n<p>I pressed on the frosting, and it ebbed and flowed with thumb! Hallelujah, It was soft! I dont usually like those sugar frosting type of cookies but I had to want something. I took it home. I crumbled and nibbled in the car. it tasted like all natural dirt. I complained about how gross it was and kept eating it.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"is it good?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"I don\\\'t know, I can\\\'t say it\\\'s enjoyable at all, but it\\\'s...compelling. the texture is...earthy.\\\" Maybe I should write marketing copy. Flipping over to the label marked \\\"Bite Me\\\", I discovered the source of this compelling texture. why....brown sugar, it is you whom i chase.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"is the church commercial?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>i looked at her and laughed.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"i mean, is the church building zoned as commercial and not residential?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i guess it\\\'s technically a non-profit organization.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yeah. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>well, it\\\'s the house of the lord.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh, well maybe its residential then.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2867,'<p>\r\nwoke up with a tick today. i did many things before i noticed it. i opened some classroom doors with my keys. i drove the golf cart around. i fended off a person who wanted to grump at me for not unlocking her yoga room before 7am. i looked at the sun; an orange circle in the sky behind gray clouds. i witnessed human meditation, as well as a meditating frog. i felt fine.\r\n</p><p>\r\naround 7am i took an illicit trip back to my tent, on the clock, holding the walkie talkie. my plan was to shower and eat breakfast. i am the only person working in my department this morning, so leisurely showers are permissable by me. i ended up having to hop out of the shower naked and have a discussion on the walkie talkie about water urns and tea. \r\n</p><p>\r\nnoticed the tick while in my tent gathering items for the illicit shower. i was doing my usual sort of sleepy self-pick thing, focusing on my arms, scanning my fingertips from elbow to shoulder, looking for impurities. usually this almost subconcious behavior reveals an ingrown hair or a blackhead, which i sleepily and joyously destroy. these motions are such a part of me that i do them without thinking. today i found an impurity with legs.\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>hello tick</i> i said, to the tick. i sighed into the slightly damp interior of my tent (a north face brand shelter, ordered from rei.com. there\\\'s a tag inside the tent that says THIS TENT IS NOT DESIGNED TO BE USED AS A LONG TERM SHELTER and i always read it and wonder what, exactly, they mean by LONG TERM. i mean, i will be living in the tent for two more months. for me, that seems like a long term, but these things are so subjective) and considered my options. i could have pulled the tick out right then, by myself. there\\\'s something lonely about removing a tick on your own, though. i did nothing. i was really hungry. i went to breakfast. \r\n</p><p> \r\nat breakfast, i was a rebel with an un-acted upon tick. generally there is a sense of urgency surrounding ticks. i actually felt fond of the tick. he was hanging on pretty tight. sucking away.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere is a lyme disease problem here. seven people on staff have already gotten lyme disease, including one of my favorite people here; a psychic israeli scorpio with a penchant for rosewater and chick music. i saw him this morning. i asked him how he was and he sat with me, telling me he was sucky. telling me he puked last night. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe lyme disease people have various stories of bullseye rashes, fevers, ticks, and achey muscles. their stories always end with \\\"but they gave me some antibiotics, so...\\\"\r\n</p><p>\r\neventually, in the dining hall, i was pressured into making the pilgrimage to first aid. the person who pressured me was a beautiful woman named keagan (kay-gan). she rolls her body when she talks. her body is voluptuous, substantial. her hair is long thick dreadlocks and she is a wonderful artist. keagan rolled at me, her head in a crooked expression, saying <i>girl, don\\\'t even front, get that thing removed from your body</i>. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni carried my breakfast down to first aid. katherine was there. she has a french manicure on her long nails, and a rather disproportionate amount of eyeliner considering the surroundings (trees, dirt, grass). she is great. she has spirit and a thick new york or new jersey accent. i saw her and said <i>katherine, you wanna pull this tick out of me?</i> and we were in an intimate situation then, her long nails employed removing my little bloodsucking friend.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe day has gone on without incident. i don\\\'t think i\\\'ll get lyme disease. this is tick #2 for me. it was very small. it\\\'s minute size has lowered my confidence as to whether i\\\'d be able to locate a tick if it should be, say, in my asscrack or armpit. i\\\'m not too worried though. these are crazy times we live in! ticks, hippies, wars, pollution. i will make it. \r\n</p>'),(2868,'<P>\r\ni would like to go for a long walk, but i dont feel like there is anywhere to walk around here.  it is all very hamster trail.  very circular.  very structured around here.  i want to go on an aimless walk and i am thwarted by city planning and fences.\r\n</P><P>\r\naustin, tx, was nice because the neighborhoods are old and compact.  you can walk around aimlessly along tree lined streets taking rights and lefts that you have never taken.  things dont curve around into themselves. they end.  they start.  they terminate at creeks where you can skip rocks or tightrope pipes across to the other side and keep going on a similiar street.  all the while there is history in the smooth concrete, ancient by modern standards, stamped with dates.  speedway 1917.  6th 1905.  45th 1946. the roman grid is a wonderful thing.  i miss it.\r\n</P><P>\r\narchitecturally i was fascinated with curved buildings once.  my professor said flatly, \\\"curved walls have been shown to make people dizzy.\\\"  it was a fad.  like geodomes and use once buildings like the planetarium.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni got over it and now i want to wander though san francisco city square up and down the steps of the large square boxes with pilars and cheezy freezes of The Workers, and lists of ancient greek writers on the new Asian History Building. (it was founded with Avery Brundage\\\'s donation of asian stuff, and is now housed in the old main city library building.)  i want to walk diagnally and perpendicular.  i want to juxtapose myself against the white granite.  i want to sit and smoke a thoughtful cigarette as people like wayne bremser jog up and down, zig zagging the stairs on an unmapped adventure threw the civic center.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmove me out of this organic community thing of coastal california.  the curving this way and that, one mile u-turns, and intersects with long curving elipse into the ocean.  i live on the jagged half circle of Monterrey Bay, i am beset with things not strait and incest.  it is taking its toll on my mind.  im dizzy, understand me!  dizzy!\r\n</P>'),(2869,'XXXXXXXX <BR>\r\nIntellectual Property Lawyers and Consultants<BR>\r\nPatent and Trade Mark Attorneys<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nhubris.co.nz\r\n<BR>\r\nSELLOTAPE &reg Trade Mark\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe act for Sellotape New Zealand Limited. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAs you may be aware, SELLOTAPE &reg is a registered trade mark in relation to our client\\\'s range of adhesive tapes and related stationery products. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe purpose of this letter is to ask for your co-operation to help ensure the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark is not misused by being used generically.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOur client has noted with some concern that your website refers to the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark as the generic name for adhesive or sticky tape rather than as the brand name for those products. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nA copy of the relevant website <A HREF=\\\"http://www.hubris.co.nz/journal/2000/august/aug22.html\\\" TITLE=\\\"for your information\\\">\r\npage</A> is attached for your information \r\nCan you please either delete any reference to the SELLOTAPE &reg trademark on your website or at least ensure that the trade mark is used correctly. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe following guidelines will help ensure that proper use of the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark is maintained:\r\n<BR>\r\n1.  The generic name for the product should always be used in conjunction with the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark.\r\n<BR>\r\nCorrect:  SELLOTAPE &reg adhesive tape or SELLOTAPE &reg tape<BR>\r\nIncorrect: sellotape\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n2. The SELLOTAPE trade mark should always be used in a distinctive form such as: SELLOTAPE &reg or Sellotape &reg or <I>Sellotape</I> &reg\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n3. Any reference to the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark should always feature the &reg symbol which signifies that the mark is a registered trade mark.  We appreciate your efforts to help ensure that the SELLOTAPE &reg trade mark is used correctly and look forward to receiving confirmation that you have amended your website.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nPlease contact us if you have any questions.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nYours faithfully\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nXxxxxxx\r\n<BR>\r\nAssociate\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nDirect Dial (09) xxx xxxx<BR>\r\nDirect Email xxxxxx@xxxxxxx.com <BR> '),(2870,'<P>\r\ni am listening to this band 7 Seconds.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nthey are rocking me.  i remember when i was in the 6th grade at haggard middle school in plano texas, collin, a punk kid with half a shaved head and 20\\\" bangs that twisted down allong his ever tilted-to-the-side head. who was skinny like a H addict... he had a 7 Seconds tee shirt.  i had no idea who 7 Seconds were.  i had never hear them.  i didnt care to go looking for them.  i was not a music driven punk.  i wasnt any punker than my older brother would let me get away with.  mainly i was just hanging out i think.\r\n</P><P>\r\nbut here i am how many years later, 13?, listening to this band on mp3 that collin had on his tee shirt.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni associate that tee shirt with collin because he always wore it and the day that trent was trying to express his undying love for the hot girl with the clear braces in an alley a few blocks from school. collin was there wearing the tee shirt with his head tilted to one side looking on with one eyeball.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2871,'According to science, all matter is composed of tiny, subatomic particles spinning inimaginably fast around nuclei equally as inimaginalby small. Our minds are not able to comprehend this as it is and therefore processes it into the solid shapes which we call our reality. So, therfore,  all that we see is based on our mind\\\'s perception of those little spinning particles.</P><P>Thus, if we change the way we think, we change our world. </P><P>If we are to change ourselves outwardly, we must do so inwardly as well.</P><P>Rational people do not expect the world to change to suit them.</P><P>Thus all major things are created by irrational people.'),(2872,'she always seemed to remind me of zen. eyelashes like fine long bamboo brushstrokes, eyes like ripples in a pond, the sort that would perpetualy have small frogs croaking around it. but she wasn\\\'t like that, she was more like the sound that a small toad makes upon hopping into a pond, passing happily into a sort of dark otherworld. i don\\\'t think she knew though. what with all the croaking, it\\\'s so easy to miss. someone once told her that she smelt like warm peas. </P><P>i think she would have smiled then, and kindly, kissed their cheek a bit. i don\\\'t think that\\\'s what she did. it would have been nice though.</P><P>i remember, that she liked a boy who was perpetually blue. i never found out why, but i liked blue and so did she. i liked that poem too...that one with the horn.... He didn\\\'t like frogs , something about them being to loud everywhere. He never told us if he liked toads,  we assumed not, we didn\\\'t think he liked ponds either.</P><P>at home, she wore her hair like black shiny licorice, only not as smelly, and she cried. </P><P>i never agreed with Him though, i always thought that she smelt like my mother\\\'s lady grey tea.</P><P>'),(2873,'<pre>\r\nthere is a moment set aside for me\r\na ripe moment, plucked, mid-dangle\r\na boiling moment, rolling up the side  \r\nof a living vat of stewing time\r\nthis moment was offered to me\r\nshiny, squalling\r\nwrithing in the palms \r\nof a thousand nameless deities\r\n\r\nwithin the gift of a moment: responsibility\r\ni am responsible for countless unspeakable tasks;\r\nresponsible for catastrophe & worship\r\nresponsible for decadence & horror\r\n\r\nand \r\n\r\nsomehow, with the chores of an intricate universe \r\nresting upon my briefly present shoulders,\r\ni must find the space and place\r\nto get you alone\r\nand tell you, \r\nwith my 6 billion tongues\r\nwhat it has been like for me\r\n</pre>'),(2874,'<p>watching.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>she has slung her ruby red coat across the back of the chair; he continues to wear his.  they eat in silence.  she pulls her bag into her chest and rifles through it, finally pulling out a soft pack which looks squashed.  fishing a cigarette from it she parts her lips and it is held precariously as she continues the hunt for a lighter.  after about three minutes, he sticks his hand in his pocket, pulls out a lighter & ignites her cigarette.  they still do not speak.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he signals for the waitress and orders two cups of coffee.  she wipes her mouth & picks up the newspaper.  they have not made eye contact with each other for several minutes and yet seem to know exactly what the other wants.  you long for this kind of connection with someone. it seems so highly desirable to be in a place where you can anticipate the other, where its not necessary to pour sound around your bodies for comfort.\r\n</p><p>\r\nyou flick your diary open to the next week spread.  you pencil in appointments, commitments, the family dinner.  in your peripheral vision you notice that there is stillness at the next table.  the pair are edgy, tense.  he grips her wrist, is trying to get her to look at him.  an urgent whisper across the short physical distance echoes loudly against her.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>avert eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his urgency isn\\\'t whispered anymore.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"lou...\\\"  no answer.  \\\"louey.\\\"  nothing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he stands up.  brushes off his coat.  \\\"louey, i have to go.  no more calls okay.  no more.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she refuses to look up.  studies the nails lacquered to match the coat.  it is only when he turns  and goes that she raises her eyes.  she watches him walk the length of the cafe, push through the door and check his watch.  it is this slight movement, an everyday action which breaks her finally and she sobs unabashedly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the waitress returns to the table balancing the two cups of coffee and louey takes them both, wiping at her face with the back of her hand.  once the woman moves on the tears resume, and you note under \\\"dentist 4.30\\\" - nothing is ever as it seems.</p>'),(2875,'<p>sitting on the train to the city the other day my friend and i met a woman who originally seemed a little nutty. it\\\'s sometimes weird when you\\\'re sitting next to your friend and you say something to them, and a random stranger near you also responds, as if you\\\'ve asked them as well, when in fact you never even realized they were there until they spoke. that\\\'s how this conversation began although i don\\\'t even remember what i\\\'d asked my friend in the first place. when the woman responded, i noticed she was editing a typewritten paper and i looked over her shoulder nosily to see what it was. something about latin america\\\'s economy which she started ranting to us about. i thought she must be a teacher and grading a term paper or something. then she mentioned she\\\'d been called at 2am the night before about this, and had to go into work at 3am, come out at 6 or 7, go all the way back to long island to feed her animals, then come back to the city. after hearing this i thought what kind of a teacher has that kind of schedule. she was actually a speech editor. as she rambled i became more and more impressed. she\\\'s lived all over the world. she lived in a hut in tibet. she started some sort of co-op singlehandedly in mexico. she has 8 kids and 12 grandchildren. she just came back from egypt 2 weeks ago and told my friend and i it was completely safe to go there despite our current reservations. people like this inspire me. this woman will die no doubt knowing she lived her life to the fullest. i want to be more like her. but i don\\\'t think living a life like this is something one can plan.</p>\r\n<p>so we walked off the train into penn station and went to buy some sunglasses. as i was reaching slightly over the woman who\\\'d been there before me to pay for mine, i was met with a rude mutter of \\\"are you going to say excuse me, or just continue to reach around me like that\\\". to which i replied calmly i\\\'d been thinking about continuing to reach around her. i was just as calmly then called a little white bitch. welcome to new york. what made it funnier is my friend had no idea what words had been said, and thought we\\\'d just exchanged pleasentries due to the mellowness of our voices.</p>\r\n<p>after eating at a place called tangs and walking around st. marks, we made our way to washington square park. nothing much was going on. my friend was disappointed because she told  me it was usually more exciting here. then again it was about 5:30pm on a tuesday. most people are at work, or leaving work, or doing mundane tuesday things, not lounging in the park because they felt like hanging around in the city for the day. but then a man came around to the middle of the park and gave us a show complete with poetry, swords, and fruit. of course it was marred by the guy behind us who decided to hurl obscenities during the entire thing. someone always has to ruin it. </p>\r\n'),(2876,'<p>\r\ni cant believe austin is going to beat me to the 200th post. damn it all to hell. i was going to write more but being drunk and tired had gotten the best of me...not to mention the korean rnb blaring a few stations down from me. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2877,'<p>I am now 19 years of age. To those who are in their mid-twenties and beyond, 19 is just another year, another day to some but to me a decade, a trial, a battle, a warfare and at times a blessing.</p><p>At my age, I learn and forget, so is that growing or just moving ahead? At times, I am so sensible and understanding, otherwise .... It gets hard, real hard. People look down at me and it feels as though they spit in my eyes and laugh. Just \\\"tpotoouu,\\\" right in my face. Talk about sanitary.</p><p>I run and it feels like I\\\'m walking, I speak and it seems like I\\\'m drooling, I sing and it feels like I\\\'m humming. It basically feels like I do the opposite of everything. I try to be nice but my kindness to one, offends the other.</p><p>I am a lesbian, what is so wrong with that? There seems to be no winning with people these days. They say that you have to look for happiness from within but most of what\\\'s within can\\\'t get out without help. Most things that are within got there from some human influence. Therefore pleasing people can at time be a necessity (does that make sense to you?).</p><p>They say \\\"People fear what they can\\\'t understand and hate what they can\\\'t conquer.\\\" What do you think about that.</p><p>I haven\\\'t anymore to say on that matter but I\\\'d appreciate your thoughts and opinion. '),(2878,'<p>\r\nive been reading about the \\\'dirty bomb\\\' a lot as of late. they arrested some dude who was apparently \\\'thinking\\\' about making one and setting it off in the united states. they make these things sound like some sort of doomsday device though last night on CNN(cnn viewers in the rest of the world tend to get more content then viewers inside north america) that there has been a big program inside the former soviet republics to manage and retrieve the waste capabale of creating a so called \\\'dirty bomb\\\'. there are a wide range of places one can look for such materials. medical labs, atomic ships, navagational equipment, and various other industrial garbage. Im not sure how well the united states, or canada for that matter, disposes of such materials, but apparently you cant walk fifty paces in the former soviet republics without tripping over the stuff. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin a study done by a georgian(the country, not the u.s state) scientist it was found that while the radius of a dirty bomb going off in a \\\'typical\\\' u.s city would spread to about a ten block radius, the actual number of people would die from the radiation would only equal close to ten thousand. while this is nothing to shrug off, the media is making these dirty bombs out to be much more dangerous. a dirty bomb is esencially a very \\\'scary\\\' thing, and secondly acts more as a device to make people sick than it does to kill them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to \\\'dirty bomb\\\' talk, ive been reading a lot of news regarding u.s fears of chemical attack. the anthrax attacks make this seem like a plausable fear, and in my opinion i think those fears are warranted, but not to the degree in which we are being told.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnow you all know that i am ultra-critical of the u.s and its role on the world stage, so i must tell you i am not happy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe united states has used chemical and bacteriological warfare in the past as its using it now. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nduring the korean war between the u.s(UN) and north korea/china, there has been more and more proof coming out that bacteriological weapons were used. infact by the late forties and early fifties, the use of chemcial and bacteriological warfare had been accepted and added to a list of \\\'first-strike\\\' capabilities. the use of chemical warfare is \\\'tragic\\\' than full scale nuclear attack(which was also an option being discussed during the korean war).\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nvietnam was no different. there is a canadian think tank research type thing supported by the canadian government who put out something called the \\\'hatfield report\\\'. The report states that upto 14 percent of south vietnam was contaminated with dioxin. it has been devastating the vietnamese population even up until today. the u.s used vietnam as a test case, infecting the south with the agent while leaving the north clean. it was a population divided in two, making the north and south \\\'good\\\' control populations. this is the way things are thought about in those smokey rooms in the pentagon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmore recently, the use of chemical warfare has been going on for years down in columbia. with the \\\'war on drugs\\\' still raging on, and billions of dollars being pumped into the columbian war machine. the u.s via the most helpful of columbian generals have been introducing biologically engineered fungi throughout most of the andean region in the hope that it will some day kill coca.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to the fungi they have been injecting, they have also been using a very strong herbicide(produced by Dow) which has been designed to attack crops. apparently from what i have read, even Dow has warned against using the agent in the way the military has chosen to use it, but then again, who gives a shit about columbian peasants?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are other ways to introduce bacteria though. as we are mostly people who live in areas of the world with garbage pick up, sewers, and treatment plants for water, it is easy to take what we have for granted. iraq too was part of this very class of states. they had clean water to drink, sewage treatment plants and other facilities to help the population stay clean, though during the war most of these facilities were destroyed by the war machine. so too in serbia in the kosovo conflict. while serbia is not under the same pressure and saction that korea is they have been able to somewhat repair these systems, and have had a lot of help. in iraq this is a different story. bacterial wasnt introduced, it was allowed to grow, fester, and gestate. there has been a serious increase in disease due to unclean water, non-existant sanitation facilities and so on. this in my view is bacteriological warfare. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis isnt to say the u.s is the only bad person. churchill gassed the kurds in the 40\\\'s as madman saddamm did in the 80\\\'s though churchill saved the british empire from the nazi\\\'s so whose going to get mad at him. the u.s and canada both tested ddt on its general populations in the forties and fifties. there is a lot written about that, and just two weeks ago a truck drove by the school i work at with men on the back spraying a very thick gas out of these large machines. the men had gasmasks on and what they were spraying is unknown to me. just made me think. even big business uses \\\'gases\\\' to change the way we think. i know for a fact that in canada a book store called chapters injects an aroma into the air system to make people feel more content while shopping. lets not be fooled. chemical warfare is all around, in many forms. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom a very hot and sticky south korea, this is chiamattt signing off. \r\n</P>\r\n'),(2879,'<p>A couple of months ago I dreamt that I was a new hire at IKEA. I\\\'m not sure why this would creep into my slumbering thoughts, because I don\\\'t live anywhere near an IKEA and I have absolutely no desire to launch a career in retail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As a new hire, IKEA managers instructed me to change into an appropriate \\\"work attire\\\" and meet them in the training room 15 minutes later. The company provided my work uniform free of charge as a \\\"benefit of employment.\\\" The uniform provided to me was a man\\\'s suit (perhaps a reflection on the somewhat conservative scandanavian design of the furniture, though I am not sure entirely). So, I dressed down, slipped into an oversized man\\\'s suit, and appeared at the training session, where I sat in a high-school desk and viewed an orientation video along with 8 other new hires in a spare, undecorated back room with white walls. We were all dressed exactly alike... I found the experience a little frustrating, because IKEA hired me as a catalog copywriter, and I could not imaging why they subjected me to the sales floor training video, unless to acqaint me with company culture in efforts to mold my future writings on their behalf. But really, I thought, how hard can it be to write for an IKEA catalog.... </p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<blockquote>BILLY is a long-time family member at IKEA - a storage system which has survived all times and trends simply because it offers such great flexibility and possibilities to extend and grow. Add shelves, doors, bookcase lighting, CD-storage or glass doors to fit your needs and collections. </blockquote>\r\n\r\n<p>After viewing the training video, I was instructed to remove my clothing and adjust my body into a \\\"furniture-like\\\" position. At this point, I protested, and the manager informed me that I was no longer considered \\\"IKEA material.\\\" I changed back into my jeans and t-shirt and left. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>How could a large, multinational furniture company refuse to employ me because I would not interpret their designs with my naked body?</p>'),(2880,'<p>\r\nthe city is a god damn lie. a community of infinately different obsessions, classes, and colours. In colours i mean ambitions, prospects, and chances. the city is a casino in anarchy; its inhabitants looking for the breaks, holes, and exploitable potential. the only reason the city exists is because of its capacity to give and take from/for so many in a reasonably small area. no city on this earth has the capacity to fullfil the \\\'needs\\\' of everyone and for this reason the city acts as halfway house for the downtrodden and unlucky. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas a cacoon, the city provides the nuritment and stability at a young age, but for the most part when one reaches that age of maturity, it is hard to break free of that shell. once in a city most stay in a city, and its this lack of movement and personal growth which makes a city so dismally harsh and stagnant. if one could choose between living by a rushing river of a stagnant marsh most would pick the river, yet a city is a marsh; for most life is stagnant and boring.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit often bothers me that the \\\'vibrance\\\' of a city is usually directed towards its \\\'night life\\\', \\\'touristablility\\\', sports teams, and its marketablility. the quality of a city is represented by the consumer products available, the number of restaurants, and hotels. the \\\'things to see\\\', and \\\'where to spend money\\\'. Never have I heard of a city as a good place to go and think. Never have i heard of a city glorified for its promotion of creative and intellectual modivations. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city acts as audience to indulgence, lust, and greed. the city acts as a framework to base life on, and towards this end we become cutthroat and loathsome. the city represents the individual and not the community at large. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas much as one might like to think that a city opens ones eyes to \\\'more\\\', the city only directs those eyes to view certain things. the city closes our eyes to more things than we think, and by the end of the day the only thing worth thinking about is \\\'making ends meat\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe believe the lies because as long as we seem better than \\\'them\\\' nothing can be \\\'that\\\' wrong. JUST WAIT!!! so much of the city is dead. so much of it rots and festers. we lay blame on those areas for all the problems, separating the good from the bad via property value, police coverage, and the ammount of trees. and yet we are unable to stop the spread of disease and crime. disease and crime dont pay attention to property value...they spread because \\\"i\\\" dont care. its \\\"their\\\" problem. thats the way we think in a city. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city is like a big church. the more people belie the more wealth is accumulated. However, like a church that wealth filters up and rarely down, leaving many bleievers disenfranchised and austricized from the \\\'whole\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe city lends little for personal development. the trend of a city are as much products that we buy rather than outlets that we seek. the city is as conservative and non-changing as a constitution. the city reacts at a snails pace...and for this reason it acts as prison. for the prisoners life is meek, but for those few wardens, change is as dangerous as ever. change equals risk and nothing is more dangerous than change; change for the rich \\\'must\\\' mean greater reward and when that reward cannot be guarenteed, it is seen as a disadvantage and set aside.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow sick we become when we let the city take hold of our emotions and immune system. we rely on the city for so much of what is not important in life but for what we insist is. the city inadvertantly creates many of its own problems because it has clouded the many; creating rifts in society, leading to frustration and negativity. that frustration and negativity ultimately leads to blame and division. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe only hear the bad because its best to stay away from it, but for so many what is bad is right infront of them. the bad is created by the individual to indulge the senses and make happy \\\'in the short term\\\' our increasingly long lives. i pity the city and i pity myself for being its slave. i cannot say ive cast aside those chains though im aware those chains are there. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2881,'<img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2881_1.jpg\\\">\r\nIts been one of those slow summer-time nights here; ones dark and heavy, where the sun seems to set really late and the nights are as humid as the days. It\\\'s a scorcher out there, as they say.<br><br>\r\nI just came in from an evening walk with my flatmate; I was sitting there reading some music magazine, he eating supper, when he decides he needs a smoke. So I join him while he hides his guilty pleasure from his fiance, a late night smoke when she agreed to marry him only after he gave up the Camels for her.<br><br>\r\nWe proceed around the block. Half a block <i>west</i> on w. 108th street, one block <i>south</i> on Broadway, one block <i>east</i> on w. 107th street, one block <i>north</i> on Amsterdam avenue where we make a left turn back onto our street and snuff out butts on the three sandy steps of the stoop.<br><br>\r\nI don\\\'t think I said anything the whole time; I did do some nodding though. I seemed to be pretty curious about the squeaky sounds my feet were making inside the grimy Chucks. I do remember him only talking- between huffy drags- about Mayor Bloomburg\\\'s decision today to end the recycling services for plastic and glass for budget reasons; terrorism costs alot of money too, I suppose:<br><br>\r\n<b>he:</b> \\\"Damn fuckin\\\' conservative liberal!\\\"<br>\r\n<b>he:</b> \\\"Promises not to raise taxes... and then this\\\"<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> [nod]<br>\r\n<b>he:</b> \\\"What, they could just tack a fuckin\\\' extra dollar onto what we cough up to the Feds, you know?\\\"<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> [double nod, trying only to match the pace of my step]<br>\r\n<b>he:</b> \\\"fuck that, I\\\'m going to set all my glass out for that homeless bro. At least he can make a buck or two. Thats more than I can say about myself in this fiscal...\\\"<br>\r\n<b>me:</b> [nod]<br>\r\n<b>cigarette butt:</b> <i>*snuff*</i><br><br>\r\nAnd I try to slide down the steep rail of my spiral staircase on the way in, but kind of loose my balance and shimmy right off the thing into my bedroom. And I hear him blame the smoky-smell on me, although she knows better, and she\\\'s sitting reading a bride magazine, and I shut my door on them as they kiss, and I sit down in front of the fan.<br><br>\r\nThat\\\'s tonight. '),(2882,'<P>(based in part on the story \\\"Locomotive-38, the Ojibway\\\" from <U>My Name Is Aram</U> by William Saroyan)<BR><BR>FADE IN<BR><BR>EXT. BACK YARD - DAY<BR><BR>LEROY VOAT, white, 20s, stands behind a wooden house, smoking.<BR><BR>Woman\\\'s voice (offscreen):<BR>Leroy!  Dammit!  Leroy!<BR><BR>He stubs out his cigarrette and goes inside.<BR><BR>INT. BEDROOM - DAY<BR><BR>MRS. VOAT, 50s, fat, lies in bed.  The bedside table is full of pillbottles.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Where\\\'s my lunch?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Whattayou want?<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Grill cheese sandwich \\\'n tomato soup.  An\\\' some Sanka.<BR><BR>He sighs, scratches his temple.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Think we\\\'re outta Sanka.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Well, go get some, dammit.  Hurry up.  Put on my stories \\\'til you get back.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>They\\\'re not on yet.  Too early.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Put the news on then.<BR><BR>He switches on the TV.<BR><BR>EXT. ROAD - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy rides his bike speedily down the road.  He comes across a white MAN riding a horse in the other direction.  Looks to be in his early 30s.  The man wears two sombreros, one on top of the other.<BR><BR>Man:<BR>Hey, Willie.<BR><BR>Leroy looks at him like he\\\'s crazy.<BR><BR>INT. KITCHEN - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy, wearing an apron, opens a can of Campbell\\\'s tomato soup, plops it into a bowl.<BR><BR>EXT. SIDEWALK - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy walks his bike along the sidewalk, admiring the scenery.  A car passes, honks.  He waves at it lazily.<BR><BR>EXT. PARK - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy enters the park, heads toward the library at one end of it.  The man from before is lying on the grass on a blanket, a sombrero over his face.  The horse is tied up to a tree with the other sombrero secured around its head.<BR><BR>INT. LIBRARY - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy sits at a table, reading.  The man comes in, passes one of the LIBRARIANS putting magazines on the shelf, reaches out, pinches her ass, keeps going.  The librarian whirls around, slaps at his arm, a little tickled, a little pissed.  His sombrero is hanging across his upper back.  He carries a backpack.  He comes over and sits at Leroy\\\'s table, across from him.<BR><BR>Man:<BR>Hey, Willie.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>My name\\\'s not Willie.<BR><BR>Man:<BR>Yeah?  What is it, then?  Puddentane?  Calpernia?  Heinekeplotz?  Hildapoot Jones?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Leroy Voat.<BR><BR>Man:<BR>No shit?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>No shit.  What\\\'s yours?<BR><BR>Man:<BR>Locomotive 38.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Locomotive....<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>38.  Yeah.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Uh-huh.  Can I call you \\\"Choo-choo\\\"?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I\\\'d prefer you didn\\\'t.<BR><BR>L-38 takes out three or four packs of Juicy Fruit and proceeds to unwrap most of the sticks and cram them in his mouth.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You want some gum?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>No, thanks.  I\\\'m tryin\\\' to quit.<BR><BR>L-38 looks around the library.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You lived here long?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>All my life.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah.  Americans find someplace they like and stay there forever.  Not us Indians.  Always on the move.  Scootin\\\' fools.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You\\\'re not an Indian.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>How do you know?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You\\\'re not anymore Indian than I am.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Whatever. (mutters under his breath) Ignorant.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You\\\'re about as Indian as I am American.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You\\\'re not American?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Nah.  Romanian.  I was just born in America.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Less hear some Romanian.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Less hear some Indian.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Pssh.  (waves him off)<BR><BR>L-38 looks around the library again.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Quiet in here.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Yeah.  It\\\'s what they call a library.  (waves his hand around, indicating the shelves)  Feel free to do a little research on the subject.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You know what?  I always wanted to make a lotta noise in a library.  Blast everybody outta their coma.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Go ahead.<BR><BR>L-38 gets one of those old flat cassette players out of his backpack and lays it on the table.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Got me some library music here.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Uh-huh.<BR><BR>L-38 climbs up on the table, reaches down to the cassette player, turns the volume all the way up and hits \\\"play\\\".  A Casio drumbeat and chord sequence erupts from it.  He does a kind of shuffling dance step on the tabletop.  The only other patrons in the library, two OLD MEN who are sitting together at a table, look up non-plussed.<BR><BR>L-38 (sings):<BR>Keep on rockin\\\' that ass!  Keep on rockin\\\' that ass!  Keep on rockin\\\', rockin\\\', rockin\\\', rockin\\\', rockin\\\', rockin\\\', rockin\\\', keep on rockin\\\' that ass!<BR><BR>The two old men look at each other.  One of them taps his temple, indicating that he thinks L-38 is crazy.  The other nods.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Woooooooooo!<BR><BR>The librarian he pinched comes trucking into the table area.  They see her coming, gather their things and run off into the stacks.  The librarian chases them through the shelves.  His song continues on the soundtrack over these antics.<BR><BR>EXT. OUTSIDE LIBRARY - DAY<BR><BR>The librarian chases them outside.  L-38 turns around and chases her back toward the library entrance.  She runs inside.  He stops, and he and Leroy catch their breath.  They sit on a bench.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Where\\\'dja get that tattoo?<BR><BR>L-38 studies him closely.<BR><BR>L-38 (straight-faced):<BR>Bucharest.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Where zat?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>(stares at him, pokerfaced) Alabama.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Oh.  They got any nice girls there?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>If you like country girls.  Now, downtown Bucharest, they got some sweet ladies livin\\\' downtown.  There was this one girl - what was her name?  Katie?  Krista?  Somethin\\\'.  Anyway she worked in one uh those bars they got down there. (dialogue gradually fades out.)<BR><BR>FADE OUT/FADE IN<BR><BR>EXT. STREET - DAY<BR><BR>L-38 rides his horse down the street, comes to an intersection, rides through it, against the red light.  A car comes along and slams on its brakes, nearly hits them.  Car and horse both stop in the middle of the intersection.  The DRIVER leans out his window.<BR><BR>Driver:<BR>Get outta the goddamn road!<BR><BR>L-38 shows him the bird.  The driver gives him the fist up the ass gesture.  L-38 puts his fingers under his chin, palm down, and rakes them forward, flipping them up when they clear his chin, Italian flick-off style.  The driver bites his thumb at him.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>(mimics the thumb biting) What\\\'s that?<BR><BR>A truck comes from the opposite direction and plows into L-38 and the horse.  They fall over.  He crawls out from under the horse.  It appears unconscious or dead.  He limps over to a drugstore at the corner.  Much commotion behind him.  Finally, the people get back in their cars and drive around the horse.  Leroy comes to the door of the drugstore from inside and looks out.  L-38 walks by him.  They don\\\'t acknowledge each other.  Leroy goes back inside.  L-38 limps to a payphone.  He picks up the receiver and jabs zero.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I wanna make a collect call to Norman, Oklahoma.<BR><BR>INT. DRUGSTORE - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy sits at the counter eating a banana split.  NANCY, the waitperson, white, early 20s, stands behind the counter chopping bell peppers.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Yeah, they\\\'re supposed to build an Appleby\\\'s where that Captain D\\\'s used to be.  Kind of expensive, though, aren\\\'t they?  Appleby\\\'s?<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>I told you I can\\\'t concentrate on this with you talkin\\\'.<BR><BR>He looks kind of dejected, continues working on his ice cream.  L-38 limps into the store, comes over and stands by the counter.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Hey, Willie.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I told you my name\\\'s not Willie.<BR><BR>L-38 digs in his pockets, pulls out some change, stacks it up on the counter into little piles.  He takes it over to the cash register.  Nancy wipes her hands, goes over to him.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Gimme four packs uh that Juicy Fruit.<BR><BR>They complete the transaction.  He counts out the money like it\\\'s all he\\\'s got.  He takes the gum over to the bar, sits down next to Leroy, proceeds to unwrap the gum and shove it into his mouth.  She goes back to chopping.  He studies the menu over her head.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Is that Cuban sandwich any good?<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>Yeah, whaddayou want - the six inch or the nine inch?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Is the nine inch your favorite?<BR><BR>She slams the cleaver down through a bell pepper and stares at him.  He eludes her gaze and checks out Leroy\\\'s ice cream.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>What\\\'s that you\\\'re eatin\\\' there, Willie?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>This is what they call a Royal Banana Special.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Gimme the same.<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>You got any money?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah, I\\\'m good for it, baby.<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>Let\\\'s see it.<BR><BR>He waves her off, disgusted.  Sits there chewing gum.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>That\\\'s too bad about your horse.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Ah, there\\\'s no place for an animal in this world anymore.  Just as well.  (pause)  So, what kinda car do you think I should get?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You gonna buy a car?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I\\\'ve been thinkin\\\' about it for several minutes now.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I didn\\\'t think you had any money.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Well, I do.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I thought you were poor.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>That\\\'s the impression people get.  Another impression they get is that I\\\'m crazy.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You didn\\\'t strike me as bein\\\' crazy, exactly, but I didn\\\'t think you were rich, either.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Well, I am.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You\\\'re fulla shit, too.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You believe what you wanna believe.<BR><BR>They sit there in silence for a second.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I wish I was rich.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>What for?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I\\\'ve been wantin\\\' to go fishin\\\' out at Mud River for three years in a row now, but I\\\'ve never had the equipment or a way to get out there.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Can you drive a car?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I can drive anything.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Have you ever driven a car?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Not yet.  (beat)  I\\\'ve never had the opportunity.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You mean to tell me you could get into a car and start driving?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>That\\\'s right.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Remember what I was tellin\\\' you outside the library the other night?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You mean about the Machine Age?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah.  Alright:  Indians are born with an instinct for riding, rowing, hunting, swimming, and fishing.  Americans are born with an instinct for fooling around with machines.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>But I\\\'m not American.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I know.  You\\\'re Romanian.  Just born in America.  I got all that.  But you\\\'ve been here long enough to count.  You\\\'re twenty years old, never been behind a steering wheel, and somehow you know you\\\'ll be able to drive a car the minute you get into one.  Typical American.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Pssh.  Drive a car.  Who the hell can\\\'t drive a car?  Easier than ridin\\\' a horse.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>OK.  If I go up the street and buy a car, will you drive it for me?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Sure.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>How much would you want for it?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You mean you wanna pay me for drivin\\\' a car?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Well, that\\\'s very nice of you, but I don\\\'t think I could take any money for drivin\\\' a car.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>(shrugs) So what kinda car do you think I should buy?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Ford? (shrugs)<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Is that the best kind?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You want the BEST kind?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Shouldn\\\'t I have the best?<BR><BR>Nancy looks at the camera like, \\\"Can you believe this shit?\\\"<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I dunno.  The best costs a lot.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>What\\\'s the best?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>C\\\'mon.  I\\\'ll show you.<BR><BR>They get up and make for the exit.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>So where\\\'d you get all this supposed money?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>The gettin\\\' place.  (beat)  I got some land in Oklahoma.  About fifty thousand acres.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>(disbelievingly)  Uh-huh.  Is this land worth anything?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>For the most part, no.  All except twenty acres.  We got some oil wells on those.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>\\\"We\\\" who?  You got a mouse in your pocket?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Me \\\'n my brother.<BR><BR>EXT. STREET CORNER - DAY<BR><BR>They have come out of the store and are waiting on the corner for the light to change.  The horse is still in the middle of the intersection.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>You can\\\'t just leave it there.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Why?  You peckerwoods got a law against leavin\\\' a dead horse in the street?<BR><BR>Leroy shrugs.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Listen:  I got more money than Croesus.  They\\\'re not gonna bother me.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>(skeptically)  Yeah.  All that money.  They\\\'re not gonna be botherin\\\' you about a dead horse.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>They\\\'re not gonna be botherin\\\' me about anything.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Oh, OK.  I know.  \\\'Cause you\\\'re so \\\"rich.\\\"  (makes quote signs in the air)  Whatever.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>No, they won\\\'t bother me because they think I\\\'m crazy.  You\\\'re the only one who knows I\\\'m rich.  You and that girl at the drugstore.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Well, your secret\\\'s safe with us.<BR><BR>INT. SHOWROOM - DAY<BR><BR>They check out a vintage Hudson convertible.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You think you can drive this?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I KNOW I can drive it.<BR><BR>A SALESMAN approaches.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>Hey there, Leroy.  What can I do for you?<BR><BR>INT. OFFICE - DAY<BR><BR>The salesman, Leroy, and L-38 sit on the usual sides of a desk.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>So neither one of you has a driver\\\'s license?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>That\\\'s not any of your concern.  \\\"Is the money green?\\\"  That\\\'s the only question you need to be asking.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>Well, I need to see some green money, then.<BR><BR>L-38 pulls out his wallet, extracts a business card, hands it to the salesman.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Here.  They\\\'ll take care of it.<BR><BR>INT. SHOWROOM - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy and L-38 sit inside the convertible, admiring all its accoutrements.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Look at this here.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Ooooo.  Muy rico.<BR><BR>The salesman comes out of his office, visibly shaken.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>Mr. ... ah ... Littlefeather, you need to sign a couple of papers.<BR><BR>L-38 opens the door and starts to get out.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Littlefeather?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>That\\\'s from when I was just a baby.  They call me \\\"Bigfeather\\\" now.<BR><BR>He gets out and heads toward the salesman, who waves him on to the office.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>Lois\\\'ll take care of you in there.<BR><BR>The salesman gets into the passenger\\\'s seat.  Leroy is behind the wheel.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>So you\\\'re gonna drive this then, huh, Leroy?  Can you handle a standard transmission?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Tell me a little about it first.  About this gear shift.  All the mysteries of this machine.<BR><BR>L-38 comes up and leans in the passenger side window.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Listen:  He KNOWS how to drive.  By instinct.<BR><BR>Salesman:<BR>Well, let me get the boys to move some of these other cars out of the way first.<BR><BR>He gets out and gestures for the other SALESMEN to clear the salesroom floor and the driveway outside of all the other cars.  L-38 gets in.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I think you have to do something with those pedals down there, too.<BR><BR>All the cars have been moved a safe distance away.  L-38 hands Leroy a set of keys.  He turns the engine over, and they roar out of the dealership at top speed.<BR><BR>INT. CAR - DAY<BR><BR>Nancy and L-38 are sitting in back, on opposite sides of the car.  The top is up.  L-38 fishes around in his backpack and finds a cassette, nudges Leroy, who takes it and slides it in the stereo.  The sounds of Lionel Richie, \\\"Dancing on the Ceiling,\\\" fill the interior.  L-38 plays air drums along with it.  Nancy just stares at him.  Landscape zips by.  After a little bit, Leroy turns down the volume and looks at Nancy in the rearview.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>How come you\\\'re sittin\\\' there in the back?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>\\\'Cause we\\\'re the passengers.  Chaffeur goes up front; passengers in back.<BR><BR>He winks at Nancy.  She just stares at him.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You\\\'re a fine driver, by the way, Willie.<BR><BR>L-38 does the \\\"come here\\\" gesture to Nancy with his index finger.  She leans over halfway to meet him.  He whispers something in her ear.  She bends over, gets her purse out of the floorboard area, opens it, pulls out the cleaver and shows it to him.<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>I\\\'ve got a little advice for you:  If you don\\\'t watch it, you and your pecker are gonna die and be buried forty years apart.<BR><BR>He sits back, quiet, chastened, looks at her warily.  He leans up and pokes Leroy.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Stop the car.  I think she wants to sit up there with you.<BR><BR>EXT. RIVERSIDE - DAY<BR><BR>The Hudson is parked by a river.  The top\\\'s down.  They sit at a picnic table.  Leroy is fitting together a fishing rod, going through a tackle box looking at the lures.  There is a cooler on the table, Ritz crackers and cheese on a paper plate.  L-38 opens a bottle of champagne and pours it into plastic Dixie cups, passes them around.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yessir.  Some cheese, some crackers, and some FINE sparkling WINE.  Get yourself on the outside uh summa that.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Champagne won\\\'t make you drunk, will it?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Nah!  The bubbles\\\'ll just make ya fart, is all.<BR><BR>A PARK RANGER pulls up and gets out.<BR><BR>Park ranger:<BR>Ya\\\'ll havin\\\' a little party?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Looks that way, don\\\'t it?<BR><BR>Park ranger:<BR>Who\\\'s gonna be doin\\\' the fishin\\\'?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Just me.<BR><BR>Park ranger:<BR>You got a license?<BR><BR>Leroy pulls a piece of paper out of his wallet.  The ranger looks it over and hands it back.  L-38 glances at Leroy\\\'s wallet.  Something in the picture section catches his eye.  He motions to have a look at the snaps.  Leroy gives him the wallet.  The ranger gets back in his vehicle, starts to pull away, pulls even with them, addresses them through his open window.<BR><BR>Park ranger:<BR>Ya\\\'ll take it easy.<BR><BR>L-38 is checking out the pictures.  He shows one to Leroy.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Who\\\'s that?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>My mama.<BR><BR>Nancy makes a sound like, \\\"Ooh, boy.\\\"  Rolls her eyes.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>What?  What about \\\'er?<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>She\\\'s crazier \\\'n a run-over dog, is all.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>No, she\\\'s not.  She\\\'s got bad nerves, is all.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah.  Me too.<BR><BR>He pours some more champagne, tastes it.<BR><BR>L-38 (sings):<BR>Ooooh!  What a feelin\\\'!  Ooooh!  Dancin\\\' on the ceilin\\\'!<BR><BR>EXT. RIVERSIDE - TWILIGHT<BR><BR>The sun is going down.  Leroy is fishing.  His hair and clothes look soaked.  He stumbles on something on the riverbank and falls in the water.  Climbs back out.  Nancy laughs at him.  They all look drunk.  Leroy tries to cast again.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You don\\\'t know anything about fishing, do you?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>What am I doin\\\' wrong?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Everything.  You ever fished before?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>No.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I didn\\\'t think so.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>What am I doin\\\' wrong?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>You keep fallin\\\' in the damn river, for one thing.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I\\\'m not fallin\\\'.  They\\\'re pullin\\\' me in.<BR><BR>He reels the line in.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Piss on it.  We need to get goin\\\', anyway.<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>Ya\\\'ll feel like goin\\\' back to the drugstore \\\'n gettin\\\' somethin\\\' to eat?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Nah.  I gotta get home \\\'n get Mama her supper.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Why don\\\'tcha let me take care uh that?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Huh?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah:  Drop me off at your house, \\\'n ya\\\'ll can take the car over to the drugstore for awhile.<BR><BR>Nancy:<BR>You feel like babysittin\\\' tonight?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Why not?<BR><BR>INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat is in bed watching TV, remote in hand.  She hears a car approaching, looks out the window, sees headlights.  She turns off the TV.<BR><BR>EXT. DRIVEWAY - NIGHT<BR><BR>The Hudson pulls up.  They let L-38 out.  He\\\'s carrying his backpack and sombreros in his hand.  He wears a light jacket on top of his shoulders, the sleeves hanging free.<BR><BR>INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat cranes her head to look out the window, hears the car door slam.  She lays there, quiet.  The front door slams.  She hears somebody coming up the stairs.  She leans forward to try to see out into the hallway.  L-38 walks in her room.  She stares at him in shock, then roots under the pillows and brings out a pistol, trains it on him.  L-38 puts his hands up.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I\\\'m a friend of Wil - Leroy\\\'s.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Yeah?  Where the hell is Leroy?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>He had a date.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>DATE?<BR><BR>She lowers the pistol, puzzled.  He sits down gingerly on the edge of the bed.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I wrote a song about you.  Wanna hear it?<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Who the hell are you to be writin\\\' me songs?<BR><BR>He stands up and puts his hands on his hips.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>It is I, the Dark Seductor of the Nighttime, come to call.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Oh, Jesus.  We got a live one here.<BR><BR>He fumbles around in the backpack, brings out the cassette player, places it on the bed and sits back down next to her.  He fools with the controls, rewinding the tape.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>It\\\'s called \\\"The Devil and Mrs. Voat.\\\"<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Uh-HUH.  INteresting.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Just a second.<BR><BR>He finds the right place on the tape and starts it up.  It\\\'s the same music from the library.<BR><BR>L-38 (sings):<BR>Oh, Mrs. Voat, the devil musta gave you those magical titties.  Oh, Mrs. Voat, and that FINE ass.<BR><BR>She slaps at him, slaps him all about the head and torso, jabs at the tape player, succeeds in shutting off the music.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Yeah.  Hit me again, you big scary woman!<BR><BR>She tries to hit him, and he catches her wrist.  They look at each other for a second, then embrace, kiss wildly.<BR><BR>INT. DRUGSTORE - NIGHT<BR><BR>Nancy and Leroy sit at a little table, eating sandwiches, not saying anything, exchanging looks.<BR><BR>INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat and L-38 sit propped up in bed, pillows behind them.  The covers are down around their waists.  Mrs. Voat wears a bra.  L-38 is naked to the waist.  Each is wearing a sombrero, and Mrs. Voat has on sunglasses.  They both stare straight ahead, looking flushed and a little stunned.<BR><BR>MONTAGE:<BR><BR>Day.  Leroy drives the Hudson through the countryside.  Nancy rides beside him.  His mother and L-38 are in back, both sporting sombreros.  Mrs. Voat wears her housedress.<BR><BR>Night.  Nancy and Leroy dine in a candlelit restaurant.  Mrs. Voat and L-38 occupy the next table over.  Everyone is dressed nicely.<BR><BR>Day.  The four of them walk through a parking lot.  They stroll through a museum, look at an exhibit of marine life.<BR><BR>Leroy, in a suit, sits in an office talking to a man behind a desk.  He is next seen wearing a uniform, assisting a customer in a large hardware warehouse Home Depot-type store.<BR><BR>INT. CAR - NIGHT<BR><BR>Leroy drives.  L-38 is in the back.  They turn down a side street and stop in front of an old hotel.<BR><BR>EXT. STREET - NIGHT<BR><BR>They get out of the car and head toward the trunk.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>So, I\\\'ll see you in the morning, right?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>No, I told you:  I\\\'ve gotta work in the morning.<BR><BR>Leroy opens the trunk.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Hey - THIS is your work.  Right?<BR><BR>They each grab two cases of wine out of the trunk.  Leroy sets his down and closes the trunk.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>But you\\\'re not payin\\\' me anything.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Oh, yeah.  Well, how much do you want?<BR><BR>They go over to the hotel\\\'s side entrance.  Leroy puts his load down again and rings a buzzer.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I told you:  I can\\\'t take anything for driving you around.  This is pure pleasure for me.<BR><BR>GENE, the night manager, opens the door a crack.<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>Yessss?<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Get outta the goddamn way.  This shit\\\'s heavy.<BR><BR>INT.  LOBBY - NIGHT<BR><BR>They get inside and set down the wine on the floor.  Gene and L-38 exchange mock dirty looks.  Gene does what he thinks is a war dance and chant.<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>Hi-ya-ya-ya.  Hi-ya-ya-ya.<BR><BR>L-38, deadpan, draws his finger across his neck in a throat-slitting gesture.<BR><BR>Male voice (offscreen):<BR>Hey!<BR><BR>Deeper in the lobby, in the chairs and magazines area, a MAN, Native American, mid-30s, rises off a sofa and comes toward them.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>Son of a bitch.<BR><BR>EXT. STREET - NIGHT<BR><BR>Gene, L-38, Leroy, and Jake, the man from the lobby, stand on the sidewalk by the Hudson.  Each man drinks from his own winebottle.  Jake circles the Hudson, admiring it.<BR><BR>Jake:<BR>What year is it?<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>\\\'Forty-eight.<BR><BR>EXT. BALCONY - NIGHT<BR><BR>The four men stand on a balcony on the other side of the hotel.  They drain their winebottles, apparently racing to see who can chug it down first.  As each finishes, he throws his bottle down onto the sidewalk below.<BR><BR>INT. HOTEL ROOM - NIGHT<BR><BR>They sit around a table playing poker, drinking from plastic cups.<BR><BR>L-38 (to Jake):<BR>Whatta you got?  Nothin\\\'.  Nothin\\\'.  Call.<BR><BR>They lay down their cards.  L-38 rakes in the pot.<BR><BR>L-38:<BR>I believe it was Sammy Hagar who said, \\\"Winner takes it all; loser takes the fall.\\\"<BR><BR>INT. HOTEL ROOM - DAWN<BR><BR>Leroy wakes up in the bed.  He gets up and looks around the room.  It appears deserted.  He peeps in the bathroom.  Gene is passed out in the tub.<BR><BR>INT. HALLWAY - DAWN<BR><BR>He leaves the hotel room, closes the door behind him.  He sees Jake and L-38 sitting in an open porch/balcony area at the end of the hallway, talking quietly.  He hails them, waves.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Hey, I\\\'ll see ya\\\'ll later on, OK?<BR><BR>They wave back.  He heads down the hallway toward the exit.<BR><BR>EXT. STREET - DAWN<BR><BR>Leroy unchains his bike from a light pole near the Hudson and rides away.<BR><BR>INT. BEDROOM - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy\\\'s room.  He\\\'s in bed, asleep.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat (offscreen):<BR>Leroy!  Dammit!  Leroy!<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>I\\\'m up.  I\\\'m up.<BR><BR>INT. DRESSING ROOM - DAY<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat sits in front of a vanity in a hallway off her bedroom.  She\\\'s doing her hair and makeup.  Leroy wanders in dressed in his sleeping clothes.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Where ya\\\'ll goin\\\' today?<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>Over to Dade City for lunch.  Hurry up.  We got reservations for twelve-thirty.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>OK.  I\\\'ll be back in twenty minutes.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat:<BR>You\\\'ll never make it in no twenty minutes.<BR><BR>He leaves.<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat (calls out to his back):<BR>Put somethin\\\' decent on before you go over there!<BR><BR>EXT. STREET - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy rides up to the hotel.  The Hudson isn\\\'t there.  He chains up his bike.<BR><BR>CUT TO:<BR><BR>Gene opens the side door.  Leroy\\\'s standing there.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Where\\\'s the Hudson?<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>They took the Hudson.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Who drove?<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>The Indian.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>They\\\'re BOTH Indians.<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>Not the one that was stayin\\\' here.  The other one.  His brother.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>When did they say they\\\'d be back?<BR><BR>Gene:<BR>WON\\\'T be back.  Went back to Oklahoma.<BR><BR>He shrugs.<BR><BR>INT. DRUGSTORE - DAY<BR><BR>Nancy is behind the counter.  Leroy comes in.<BR><BR>Leroy:<BR>Can I use your phone?<BR><BR>INT. LIVING ROOM - DAY<BR><BR>Mrs. Voat sits on the sofa with her purse in her lap.  The phone rings.  She gets up and goes over to it.<BR><BR>INT. CAR SHOWROOM - DAY<BR><BR>Jake stands talking to the salesman.  L-38 can be seen waiting in the car outside  Jake has his hands out like he\\\'s pleading with the salesman.  The salesman shakes his head.<BR><BR>INT. DRUGSTORE - DAY<BR><BR>Leroy hangs up the phone.  He walks over to the counter and sits down.  Nancy unwraps two sticks of Juicy Fruit and hands him one.  He puts it in his mouth.  She winks at him.  He winks back.<BR><BR>FADE OUT</P>'),(2883,'Tonight my mom, her boyfriend and I walked down the boardwalk in Brighton Beach. The air was thick and the accents were thicker.</p><p>\r\nIt was dark out, too dark to see the water, but light enough to watch the young lovers nuzzle on the park benches. I could hear the kids laughing and the radios announcing the baseball game. I smelled leftovers from the bright afternoon--hotdogs, peanuts and cold soda. </p><p>\r\nMy mom pointed out the cracks in the wood, \\\"This is what it looked like when I was a kid. We didn\\\'t have the nice patterened boardwalk; we got splinters and sunburns and we came back the next day for more.\\\" She pointed out the drinking fountains, \\\"Those are still the same.\\\" And the brought up stories of days long past.</p><p>\r\nShe kept reiterating, \\\"Several years ago, it was too dark and desolate to come here alone, it was a terrible place to be at night. But the Russians certainly have revitalized this area. It\\\'s beautiful again.\\\" I\\\'m pretty sure her boyfriend squeezed her hand just then. He also remembered coming home from the beach with blisters all over his feet.</p><p>\r\nWe stopped for a minute and leaned over the metal barrier seperating the wooden planks from the footprints the birds left in the sand. \\\"I don\\\'t remember the sand looking like this,\\\" my mom said. \\\"It used to be nice and smooth, there weren\\\'t marks like these.\\\" Her boyfriend nodded in agreement.</p><p>\r\nAnd we kept walking. We saw Coney Island in the distance, she described each of the rides we saw outlined in neon lights. </p><p>\r\nThe dogs barked and the heat returned, so we went back to the car. We drove past familiar roadways and storefronts--places from my own childhood. I\\\'ve always been a tourist here, visiting grandma or uncle or even mom. Now, it feels like home.'),(2884,'bah.\r\n\r\ni just used to be able to post as anyone.\r\n\r\nnow i have to register.\r\n\r\nnote: this is non-fiction.\r\n\r\noh well, i guess i will register now, and post something more useful later.\r\n'),(2885,'<P>i very nearly uploaded a couple of digital photos of myself at the beay and at the gaye holud. (the wedding ceremonies themselves were bizarrely pretty and almost hitchless)\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthe thing is, i\\\'m too scared. i like being faceless. i like hiding in the safety of words, the walls of this monitor. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nif i included the pictures, you would know what i looked like. and that would affect whatever perception you have of me. and i don\\\'t want that to change, based on my appearance.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand i don\\\'t know who would see these images. that lack of control, that <i>vulnerability?</i> freaks me out.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbesides which, posting these pictures seemed the easiset way to express what i wanted to about the event. laziness. just laziness.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni missed swinney.\r\n</P>'),(2886,'<p>\r\nhere\\\'s some comedy for you:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\naround 6ish, call from wb. \\\"you going to the party tonight?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"dunno, not sure if i\\\'m up for interacting tonight. why don\\\'t you see how it is and then call me.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwait til 8.30 or so. no call. get rugged up and walk over. make it about 90% of the way. realize i don\\\'t know the address. call swinney. no answer. walk home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nreturn home to answering machine beeps. egg\\\'s left a message, asking for me to come to the party. ponder. \\\"it\\\'s 9.30. should i go back over? sure. nobody will leave before 11.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncall a cab. wait on the front steps with a smoke. stop off at the liquor store for an oil can. reach the house, pay the driver, step out of the cab. the phone rings. \\\"it\\\'s austin.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"where are you?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"we just dropped off wb. had to leave. we were freezing our asses off.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwalk home. leave the oil can for a bum on top of a garbage can at 18th & church. arrive home again. zach wanders by. \\\"that was quick.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand people wonder why i don\\\'t go out more often.\r\n</p>'),(2887,'<p><a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2217.txt\\\">six months later</a>, tipsy on whiskey, we tripped up the steps to my apartment and headed straight for bed. watched <a href=\\\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0108515\\\">the war room</a>. went back to bed.</p>\r\n<p>this morning over breakfast -- veggie sandwich for me, veggie quesadillas for him -- he asked, \\\"you know you talk in your sleep, right?\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"what?\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"yeah, i would ask you questions and you would respond completely coherently but it was obvious that you were asleep.\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"what? what did i say?\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"like: \\\'how are you?\\\' \\\'fine.\\\' \\\'what are you thinking about?\\\' \\\'not much.\\\'\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"really?\\\" i don\\\'t remember any of this.<BR>\r\n\\\"yeah. i like to imagine you dreaming and i suddenly show up and ask you questions in your dream and you answer back.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>we talk about the shows that are coming up that we want to see, standing outside of <a href=\\\"http://motherscafe.citysearch.com/1.html\\\">mother\\\'s</a> flipping through the <a href=\\\"http://www.auschron.com/\\\">chronicle</a>; i want to see ugly casanova play sunday night, he wants to see radar brothers and monroe mustang on tuesday, we both want to see <a href=\\\"http://www.lostrock.com/index.php/features/046\\\">okkervil river</a> and swearing at motorists tomorrow night. tonight we\\\'re going to see <a href=\\\"http://www.mrsinus.com/\\\">mr. sinus theater</a> do karate kid. </p>\r\n<p>at my apartment, back in bed, his head on my chest and my fingers through his <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/2002/mike_lovejoys.jpg\\\">curly hair</a>, i tell him that he\\\'s my dream boy. and he is. </p> '),(2888,'<p>I read this today and thought of swinney:</p>\r\n<p><i>\\\"In late May, a sound-art piece by Max Neuhaus was reinstalled in Times Square, a seemingly odd location for a work whose subtle tones are easy to miss in the constant bustle and din of traffic. Sited under a subway ventilation grate at the northern end of a pedestrian island on Broadway between 45th and 46th Streets, the work, called </i>Times Square,<i> emits varoius sustained notes -- like the lingering tones of ringing bells -- using sound generators and a loudspeaker. Unwitting passerby might easily assume they\\\'re hearing the hum of subterranean machinery, if they notice at all.</i></p>\r\n<p><i>The work was initially installed at the site in 1977 and remained until 1992...</p></i>'),(2889,'<p>17% of users experience headache as a side effect. 23% feel nausea, which i have not, but i\'ve popped four ibuprofen in the last six hours and the headache won\'t go away.</p>\r\n<p>i think that he asked five times during dinner how i was feeling. i smiled each time and said that i was fine.<BR>\r\nfine but emotional. weepy: i have already cried twice today. having taken a double dosage of a typical birth control pill has hormones swirling around inside of me. </p>\r\n<p>i took the emergency contraception pill at 1 PM, i take the next at 1 AM.</p>\r\n<p><a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2887\">the condom slipped</a>. i <a href=\"http://www.cyclespage.com/\">know my cycle</a> well enough to know that i was fertile, practically verdant. and although i didn\'t feel anything and the position was not conducive to impregnation, he was worried.<BR>\r\nhe offered twice that night to take me to get emergency contraception. the next night, he offered again.<BR>\r\ni told him not to worry -- that i knew my body, and that i wasn\'t worried.<BR>\r\nhe went home.<BR>\r\ni paced for an hour. i got on the internet and researched, ending up at the planned parenthood site, reading the side effects of emergency contraception.</p>\r\n<p>when i woke up, i left him a message telling him that i was going to the clinic, going to get EC.<BR>\r\n<p>i was the only one in the waiting room, filling out paperwork. the doctor and the clinic assistants were discussing a coworker who\'d been giving shit excuses for why she couldn\'t come into work.<BR>\r\n(\"she said that she didn\'t know if she had cramps or food poisoning -- i don\'t buy that. those are two very different conditions. cramps are a localized pain. food poisoning -- you wake up vomiting. you know the difference.\"<BR>\r\n\"am i being too hard on her? i want you to honestly tell me because if i\'m being too hard...\")</p>\r\n<p>they called my name and i followed the doctor back to a room where he weighed me, took my blood pressure, asked if i had used EC before. \"no.\" he told me to go home, eat, wait half an hour, take the pill. twelve hours later: eat, wait half an hour, take the pill.</p>\r\n<p>as i was paying, i said that i wanted to schedule an appointment to discuss birth control.<BR>\r\n\"condoms just aren\'t good enough anymore.\"<BR>\r\n\"i heard that,\" chimed the girl standing behind me, holding the same packet of pills.</p>\r\n<p>i went home, ate, took the pill. did yoga.</p>\r\n<p>he called a few hours later -- he had just woken up and listened to my messages.<BR>\r\n\"how are you?\"<BR>\r\n\"i\'m fine.\"<BR>\r\n\"...\"<BR>\r\n\"mike?\"<BR>\r\n\"i feel awful.\"<BR>\r\n\"you shouldn\'t.\"<BR>\r\nwe talked for an hour, mostly my reassuring him. he wishes that he had gone with me, he wishes that he\'d been with me last night when i was worrying, he feels as if he has abandoned me: \"i don\'t want to be one of those guys, you know? my god, i\'d kill myself if i thought that i were one of those guys...\"<BR>\r\n\"i didn\'t feel abandoned. i felt supported. i knew that you cared for me, i don\'t doubt that for a second. i sort of feel as if every couple should have to go through this at least once, you know? to remember how really serious this decision is...\"<BR>\r\n\"yeah but why us? we <b>know</b> how serious it is, we are so careful...\"<BR>\r\nnot careful enough.</p>\r\n<p>after dinner, we go back to my apartment and are snuggling and he tells me how much he cares about me, how beautiful he thinks that i am, how glad he is that we have met.<BR>\r\nand i would go through what i went through today every day if that is what it meant to be with him. but hopefully i won\'t have to.</p>'),(3142,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡â€°Ã‚@'),(2890,'\\\"She\\\'s amazing,\\\" he said. She was as definite as if he\\\'d run his hands over every part of her. she wore a black skirt with pleats that hugged her hips so that her every movement would cause it to tremble, her blouse was tight and had, on each arm, bits of string dangling down to her waist. she laughed, holding her hands in the air, each nail painted a different shade of blue, small wrists adorned with bracelets of every colour, a small bag then jutting from her hip. her skin was cream coloured, her eyes dark, slanting a bit upwards at the outer ends. each earlobe unadorned, though the tiny lobes had been punctured for such a purpose. she had a good deal of black hair, which hung  straight over her shoulders and shone in the light.</P><P>\\\"I wonder,\\\" he said, \\\"how she dosen\\\'t seem to notice, how she isn\\\'t amazed at herself.\\\"'),(2891,'<p>where will i be in ten years? what will i have become?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i will be twenty eight. that\\\'s about the only thing i can say with certainty. will i be as principled and independent as i hope to be?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>will i have become another boring, mundane office worker somewhere, faceless and inconsequential? i\\\'ve no clue how i intend to provide my livelihood, but i don\\\'t want to be stuck in a meaningless position turning cogs and pulling levers. i want to create, to change, to improve & enlighten. or something like that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>will i have achieved what i want to achieve in ten years time? how many times will i have fallen? will i still have the will to get up?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>twenty eight. if i\\\'m ever going to get married, it will probably have happened by then. how many times will i have fallen in love? will i still be in love? will i still be as clueless about love as i am now?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and will my accomplishments reflect the kind of person i want to be? will my peers respect me? (will my peers be respectable?) if, in ten years time, i were to reflect upon my life, would i know the mistakes i\\\'d made, would i know i\\\'d done the most i could? how much pain would i remember, and how much of my past will i miss?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and what of my friends? what of my family? the people that matter to me most now, where will they be? who will i still interact with? there aren\\\'t many people i consider my friend that i want to lose, even over the span of ten years.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>will my mother still hate me? will my brother be self-sufficient? will josh turn out to be a good person? will he be successful? he\\\'ll be twenty two... john\\\'ll be thirty. my parents... will they still suffer each other\\\'s presence, or will they have chosen to suffer their absence? will i still worry about my family?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>will the years be as hard as i think they\\\'ll be?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in ten years time, i will ask these same questions. i still won\\\'t have the answers. that\\\'s kind of scary.</p>'),(2892,'my father died yesterday at 3:30am.<br>\r\naccording to Bradbury, 3am is the deepest hour of the night. much darker than the overrated midnight hour. at 3am the previous day has faded away in our memory, and the morning light of the next day seems out of reach. at 3am we\\\'ve lost all hope.<br>\r\nanyway, my father didn\\\'t give a shit about what time it was that night. he had been in a coma for three weeks. maybe it\\\'s like spending three weeks at 3am.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe young mortician was nice and pale as i expected. he was also skinny and filled with nervous tics. for example he would blink one eye while talking to my brother and me. a kind of tacky eye contact trick, considering what he was talking about. but it helped me focus on something else.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe all had a nice dinner at my father\\\'s place. my bigger brother, his wife, their baby, my younger brother, my father\\\'s girlfriend, H and i.<br>\r\nmy father\\\'s girlfriend cooked marinated chicken. H grilled some pepperoni, mushrooms, and eggplant. we had red wine and a gewurztraminer. we got a bit drunk.<br>\r\nmy younger brother failed his exams that same day. we didn\\\'t blame him too much, even though he obviously didn\\\'t work shit the all year.<br>\r\ni spent a lot of time making grinnings and sounds for the baby. she is so cute and she smiles all the time; i didn\\\'t feel like stopping. i named myself \\\"her retarded uncle\\\".<br>\r\nwe had a few laughs about old stories, like when my father had bought this bell to call my younger brother for dinner.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand now i know i have many other stories to tell. it has to be easy like hell to write sad, true, and maybe even beautyful things.<br>\r\ni was ready for my father\\\'s death. i was expecting it for a long time. even before he went in a coma. even before he had his heart attack a few years ago. so i wasn\\\'t shocked. but i failed to beware of the strong ties.  a huge part of my life is connected with my father. memories and much more. and he pulled me with him. i am now closer to death, connected with my dead father, and this ain\\\'t no spiritualism. the color of my world got darker, and this ain\\\'t no poetry.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnow people say \\\"sorry, etc\\\", and i hardly hear them. there might be a new language for me to learn. death as a foreign language.\r\n</p>\r\n<IMG align=\\\"center\\\" SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2892_1.jpg\\\">'),(2894,'The mannequin that Molls\\\' stepbrother kept in his dining room was common in certain parts of Milwaukee back in the 1940s and 50s. Great numbers of them were utilized as all-night desk clerks at the cheaper hotels and even as a rudimentary sort of 24-hour automated teller at some banking parlors, often in poorer, more run-down neighborhoods. They were prized for their honesty, efficiency, and durability, not to mention their ceaseless dedication to service and lack of need for a paycheck. They were even once promoted in the Dewey Deluxe RoadBadger\\\'s guide as examples of pure Milwaukeean ingenuity (1949 edition, pg. 225). But eventually, economic fluctuations and a general disdain for what came to be known among certain Milwaukee residents as \\\"plasterface service\\\" resulted in the decommissioning of the entire mannequin population. Today, most of the few remaining service mannequins in Milwaukee have been repurposed as a sort of offbeat type of kitchen oracle. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSteve\\\'s mannequin was named Tammy, and he spoiled her with blue chiffon wraps, luxurious brunette wigs, and Manolo Blahnik heels. At several points during our stay we asked her to divine the weather, and each time she answered \\\"rain!\\\" or \\\"hail!\\\" or \\\"plague of locusts!\\\" in the weird piping tones of a mockingbird. Her fingers would trace nervous gestures in the air and fiddle with her amber earrings, which were, of course, just painted on. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe were surprised and meekly grateful to find that Steve had reserved one of the few functioning guest bedrooms for our one-night-stay. Most of the other family members and friends had to make do with leaky air mattresses in dusty storerooms or ancient couches that were not now and would never be \\\"sofabeds\\\" in any meaningful sense of the term. Perhaps he was being generous because we\\\'d traveled from out of state to see the place. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nOur attic-level room was reached via very steep set of two or three stairwells and a concealed stepladder. The route was a bit confusing--not because it was especially circuitous, but because the view from the attic room\\\'s window seemed to imply that for all the distance we\\\'d traveled, we were somehow only on house\\\'s second floor. To save us the trouble of lugging our bags up those stairs, Steve sent them up via an antique dumbwaiter that he\\\'d renovated just in time for our visit. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe room was very hot with little visible ventilation (other than a ceiling fan). It smelled richly of both cardamom and mothballs. Next to the bed: a small bookshelf that contained, among other things, a huge picture-book about the Titanic, a color catalog of Faberge eggs, and a historical study of male nudes in painting and photography since the end of the 19th century. The dresser and oak chiffarobe were empty. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nEverything in the house seemed to fit within either a neo-Victorian or art-deco motif, with touches of the bizarre around the edges. I desperately wanted to explore it, but there wasn\\\'t time for it then. Steve\\\'s plans for the rest of the afternoon and evening required that we leave almost as soon as we\\\'d arrived. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe gathered together in the kitchen parlor, where Tammy was becoming increasingly agitated (it turns out you should never ask kitchen oracles to deliver prophecies regarding human relationships). Steve quieted her by drawing the glyph for \\\"calm\\\" on her forehead and then led us down another set of creaky stairs. We gathered on the third and final basement level, where a large and sleekly modern submarine bay had been constructed. It felt completely out of sync with the rest of the house, but Steve explained that the original sub bay had been excavated in 1921 and had become so plagued with rot and rust that it had become unusable and even dangerously unstable.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe sub was a rotund 12-seater commuter vehicle that Steve had rented for the occasion--he usually piloted a much sportier Trident Mark IV, but it was designed for a maximum of three people and an optional dog. We climbed inside and buckled into our seats. As the hatch slid shut above us, Steve popped in <i>Nina Simone in Concert</i> and we were off to the tune of \\\"Pirate Jenny.\\\" \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe drifted down through the airlock and were soon circumnavigating our way through the labyrinth of underground river tunnels that all lead, eventually, to the frigid waters of Lake Michigan. As everyone knows, most of greater metropolitan Milwaukee exists on a huge shelf of land that extends (quite improbably, but there you go) over the body of the great lake for the distance of approximately two miles. The shelf itself is riddled with tunnels that the residents of the area have used to shuttle about to other parts of the Great Lakes system since antebellum times, if not even earlier. Some of these tunnels are natural, while others were clearly engineered--and the identity of the engineers is still an open question, since the tunnels seem to predate even the existence of American Indians in the area. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThese days the entire SubMilwaukee Metropolitan Aquatic System (or the Underneath, as most of the locals call it) is, in its more commonly traveled areas, almost as busy as a ground-level freeway. Steve deftly piloted us through a small maze of clearly lit passages that eventually opened up into a vast black space. It was crisscrossed with arcs of lit lamps on strong cable that stretched off into the distance like strings of pearls. These lamps served as markers for the underwater pathways that all submariners of the Underneath were required to follow (with the exception of those who had obtained carefully regulated permits to venture \\\"off the lights\\\"). We could see the multicolored headlamps of of dozens of other fellow subs slowly gliding along the predetermined arcs on their way to or from various parts of the city. It was a beautiful, inspiring sight.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThis being our first occasion to venture into the sub-Milwuakeean depths, Molls and I kept our gaze focused eagerly out the round portholes like children on a field-trip. Steve did a good job of directing our attention towards the various schools of perch and trout that were illuminated in the sub\\\'s headlamps, and at one point we even got a glimpse of a rarely-seen freshwater Northern Manatee-which today is found only in the delicate SMMAS ecosystem. They are known as \\\"the old men of the lake\\\" to local children. Many believe they may exceed dolphins in cognitive ability and learning skills.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSteve pointed out various sights as we traveled onwards. We were only a hundred feet or so from the ceiling of the shelf, and one could easily view various structures that had been built to extend down into the water. One dome-like shape proved to contain a typical family living-room. Apparently the kids were home from school and already engrossed in a videogame, oblivious to the subaquatic world around them.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBillboards proved to be as ubiquitous in the velvety black depths of the Underneath as they were on dry land. A few of them advertised an archaeological-site-turned-tourist-attraction nearby that seemed to promote the theory that a lost tribe of Potawatomi Indians had managed to set up an underwater civilization in the tunnels of the Underneath. \\\"Oh, that,\\\" Steve said when I mentioned it to him. \\\"It\\\'s a lousy rattletrap ripoff hoax. Don\\\'t bother.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBefore long the water became visibly lighter, like dawn arriving to a midnight world. In a few moments more, we emerged from the shadow of the Underneath. We rose up slightly and found ourselves in a network of piers and airlocks. Steve gradually maneuvered us through it, eventually slowing to a stop and docking the sub into a device that held fast as it slowly brought it to the water\\\'s surface. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe hatch slid open and sun shone in. \\\"Great, the rain stopped,\\\" Molls\\\' brother Andy said, sounding relieved. We each took turns heading up the ladder to the dock. It turned out we were in a huge parking lot of submersible vehicles. At the end of the dock I could see a grassy park and the arts festival that was our destination for rest of the day, all presided over by the ungainly set of highrises that made up Milwaukee\\\'s downtown skyline.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSteve climbed out last--a good captain always makes sure he\\\'s the last one off the sub-and exclaimed. \\\"Okay! Who\\\'s up for a braut?\\\" It seemed like a rhetorical question to me.\r\n'),(2893,'<p>\r\nFirst it was Enron, then WorldCom, Tyco, Merck, Xerox, and Qwest. Now it seems not only are the giants not paying the bills and living upto the standards they promised investors, but today, one of america\\\'s smaller .com firms held a press conference indicating that profit numbers projected by auditors did not match actuality. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin an early morning press conference, swinney.org president austin swinney stood firm while shedding a few tears. \\\"my kingdom for a working motorcycle\\\" was what he said as he left the boardroom. there has been pressure from key investors for austin swinney to step down, yet it is still unknown who board members might see fit to replace him. \\\"we are looking at a few options\\\" remarked one of swinney.org\\\'s board members. \\\"we might just have a raffle\\\" said another. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmany investors, who stand to lose upto and perhaps beyond fifty six dollars have asked the house energy and commerce committee to prohibit any of swinney.org\\\'s top brass from leaving the continental united states. in response, vice president of FAQ, elly remarked that while she was unaware of any accounting iregularities, \\\"i did find myself shredding a shit load of paper last week.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to elly\\\'s puzzling remarks, saturn5.com, a logistics firm actively working with swinney.org to provide the best possible service remarked that \\\"while we HAVE been paid, i know that for the past few months ive been getting a lot of monopoly money. it just never struck me as something that would indicate financial problems.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwith confidence in the american business and financial markets realing from financial woes, does the current financial crisis going on within swinney.org shed light into the cracks even medium sized businesses can exploit? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\naccording to committee leader Rep. Billy Tauzin and Rep. James Greenwood, documents provided by swinney.org show profits exceeding fifty six dollars, when in all actuallity profits ranged between five and six dollars. Rep. James Greenwood went onto say \\\"is this just a case where they sandwiched the two profit numbers together, or is this a terrorist plot to ruin america?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter george bush jr\\\'s speech outlining a tough new rhetoric being proposed about proposing the new proposed laws regarding corporate accounting practices, austin swinney held up a book written by Vice President Dick Cheney entitled \\\"I dont even know what Halliburton does, but i made mad l00t\\\", and another written by george bush jr called \\\"stocks shmocks! im president!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen asked what the point was, swinney shrugged and pleaded the fifth. when told that he wasnt in a court room swinney pleaded the fifth again and ran away from reporters. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat happens to swinney.org is yet to be determined, as is the fate of many of americas other businesses, but at this time, swinney.org has had its trading suspended, and it is reported that its headquarters is now on fire. lets just hope their insurance company doesnt come out with loss reports.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2895,'<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"><br><br>\r\n\r\ni was just walking around the block with my flatmate, james, sort of jogging around the block as he smoked and ranted about the weather, new york politics, whatever...<br><br>\r\nwe passed the catholic church on the south part of our lap, west on w. 107th street; some local woman looking a little mellowed out, sitting on the steps of the church\\\'s little chapel, chilling on a hot night in spanish harlem.<br><br>\r\nshe bummed a smoke from james, which he handed over gladly. he offered her a light and she said \\\"not yet; i aint gonna fuckin smoke in front of the church!\\\".<br><br>puzzled, james said \\\"huh?\\\", to which she stoically replied, \\\"cause i got fucking respect for that shit...\\\", with a serious grimace.'),(2896,'<p>When I woke up today, it was in my head ... I swear it.  The residue of the house dream ... A fence  ... A dog ... A garter-snake green garden hose coiled on a freshly cut lawn ...</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Me inside my house, my hand around a bottle of pitch black stout so cold, it nearly takes my fingerprints when I try to let go.  There is an odd tilt to my head as I sit at a kitchen table.  Iâ€™m staring out of the window, breathing in that newly-mown grass through the screen...slight breeze tousling hair that could never be mine...knowing that wherever I go in my head that moment, I will be able to breathe.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Yes, there will still be toxic chemicals in our elements, rapes, murders, child molestation, infomercials ...</P>\r\n\r\n<p>But I...in that moment... will be able to breathe because I have finally learned the art of being still...of coming home and being able to stay there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This I dream on the morning of preparing to scout for boxes and make phone calls and try to prepare my sinewsbrainsbones to move...again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Blank...detached...no longer gifted.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I wanted to be home here.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>To redeem myself with a proper job and home and fillintheblank. If I stay, Iâ€™m homeless ... If I go ... Beholden.  No matter which, Iâ€™ll only be roaming.  And not in a romantic, big cat of the Serengeti kind of way ... Just that lost, hey brother can you spare a dime, yeh I know I smell, brand of wandering.  The kind of wandering seeking devolves into when it gets tired and forgets what the hell it was looking for in the first place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is no way to gauge which will render me more or less free at the moment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And so ...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I float. A careless, surreal bobbing...the sort that might render you seasick if you had your head on straight enough to give two shits.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The wreck of the Hesperus</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Has nothing on me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am neither sedentary farmer nor wandering hunter/gatherer, seeking sustenance on the steppes and branching outward into the peopleless world, replicating myself so that my genes might someday serve the purposes of some purveyor of dish soap, toothpaste, or soft drinks or to study anesthesiology under Drs Bass, Guinness, and Killian.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A most dastardly run-on sentence.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We evolved for this?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What was I saying?</p>\r\n'),(2897,'<p>300 mg</p>\r\n<p>of dependency</p>\r\n<p>with three meals</p>\r\n<p>a day</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why shouldnâ€™t</p>\r\n<p>i hate</p>\r\n<p>that it</p>\r\n<p>should make the</p>\r\n<p>difference</p>\r\n<p>i canâ€™t<p/>\r\n\r\n<p>unbearable breeze</p>\r\n<p>of a thousand doors</p>\r\n<p>closing</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fumbled keys</p>\r\n<p>lost down</p>\r\n<p>some manhole</p>\r\n<p>floating in</p>\r\n<p>a cityâ€™s sewage</p>\r\n\r\n<p>swerving blindly</p>\r\n<p>maps folded</p>\r\n<p>instead of rolled</p>\r\n<p>creased and illegible</p>\r\n<p>in all of the important spots</p>\r\n<p>as they</p>\r\n<p>blow out</p> \r\n<p>the window</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is this a near miss</p>\r\n<p>or a head-on collision</p>\r\n<p>or a murder</p>\r\n<p>or a miracle</p>\r\n<p>or</p> \r\n<p>what</p>\r\n\r\n<p>am i saved</p>\r\n<p>does it matter</p>\r\n<p>since a coma</p>\r\n<p>is just as</p> \r\n<p>good</p>\r\n'),(2898,'<img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2898_1.jpg\\\">\r\n<p>Bangin\\\'<br>\r\nOn the rocks, the big machine and the little men got forgotten.<br>\r\nCut down, by the rocks.<br>\r\nPainted all crazy, pissed on, hard and lonely they challenge the lake: daily.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My hand was on your daughter\\\'s back late one New Year\\\'s Eve; she felt so thin.<br>\r\nSomething frail in her manner set her apart.<br>\r\nThis Jewish princess did not belong in the barrio.<br>\r\nAfter-hours in the club, she got high.<br>\r\nThe bartenders, the bar backs, and the doormen smoked weed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Positioned horizontally, I saw her again in a mechanical bed.<br>\r\nAt the Rehab Institute, she still had the same air about her.<br>\r\nShe called politely from behind the curtain, asking me to wait while the nurse finished cleaning her.<br>\r\nThe circumstance was irrelevant.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had a little, pink rose in a cheap glass.<br>\r\nIt was from a greener place.<br>\r\nNo other flower has ever smelled so good.<br>\r\nI held it out for her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She was with a friend when the railing broke.<br>\r\nAnd the corner of a concrete step severed her spinal cord when she fell.<br>\r\n\\\"Sorry.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Back in the late 80\\\'s the slick blood of an anonymous victim formed a huge pool, shone under the arc light, and reflected the stars in the evening sky.<br>\r\nThe hot, old, brick pavers that poked through the street were a much deeper red.<br>\r\nEvery picture was high contrast: the light and the dark were clearly defined.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My grandfather edged his garden with brick pavers.<br>\r\nAnd gentle was the sun.<br>\r\nWay down on the south side, a few things were kept alive.<br>\r\nBut Carravagio stole in.</p>'),(2899,'        <p>Feeling the crush of exploding nanotechnology, ethnic cleansing, twisted souls struggling internally for the sake of  â€˜true artâ€™, and whining social outcasts who bastardize Nietzsche so they can blow away their classmates in the schoolyard, Iâ€™ve begun to think gently about Tibor Jankay.</p>\r\n	<p>Good old Tibor.  A Hungarian artist born in Bekescaba in 1899, he began to create art at 6.  He grew up happy, married, got deported by the Nazis, and gave his wife up for dead when they were separated on a train going to Auschwitz.  He eventually ran into her again while heading back to his hometown on foot after having dug his way out of a second death train with a rusty chisel.  They were reunited, moved to the States, and raised a lovely, well-adjusted child while Jankay enjoyed a long and brilliant career as a painter.  Christ.  I wasnâ€™t even aware artists were allowed to let their torment take a cigarette break let alone live happily ever after.</p>\r\n	<p>He had no therapist.  He was free of writhing angst.  His trauma had failed to take root and bloom black.  He had no need for psychotropic medications.  To date there has been absolutely no report of any tortured alter-ego that abused small animals or smashed things or raged hotly before completely caroming out of control and blackening eyes and bloodying lips. He was just a soft, happy, wizened painter who appreciated his incredible life and his white-hot talent entirely.</p>\r\n	<p>Every now and again I wonder what the post-modernist, Grinchlike critics might have to say about Jankayâ€™s works, but I soon come to my senses, recalling that putting any art even remotely of this nature in front of these guys is like putting anything except Life cereal in front of Mikey.  They wonâ€™t like it.  They hate everything.  Not that it would have mattered one whit to this laid-back genius who used to talk about the Holocaust the way we might talk about an experience with the flu.</p>\r\n	<p>Jankay is, indeed, an amazing phenomenon.  It is his life that makes me think that â€˜contented artistâ€™ doesnâ€™t have to be an absolute oxymoron.  It wasnâ€™t that he lacked passion or drive or inspiration.  It was that he let it flow through him without decimating his insides as if it were 30 molar sulfuric acid or twisting him up like an arm caught in the dasher of a washing machine.  He simply was.  A lofty aspiration for anyone, I guess.</p> \r\n	<p>I confess, Iâ€™m still young and cranky, but itâ€™s rather nice know that artists like Jankay existed. It serves as a much-needed respite from the stories of the lost and wretched smashed to emotional bits amidst the jagged rocks of harsh reality and their tumultuous genius.  That way I donâ€™t feel at all guilty when people assume Iâ€™m in constant turmoil because Iâ€™m broke and a writer and a fill-in-the- blank because frankly, on most days, with enough Heavy in me, I feel fine.</p>\r\n'),(2900,'<p>I think thereâ€™s a rule in some almighty handbook about becoming overwhelmed by the sensation that youâ€™re pissing away your life if you havenâ€™t managed something extraordinary by a certain ageâ€¦then thereâ€™s some pedantic bollix involving Alexander the Great and Mozart and Roland Garros and Jesusâ€¦</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I donâ€™t have that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I imagine because there was no promise of greatness, I canâ€™t be disappointed by the way this is all turning out.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This time last week I was in Maine.  This time last year I was in Glasgow (doing stints in Edinburgh).  A year before that I was in Texas, doing whatever it was I was supposed to do there.  Now Iâ€™m here, working out what my next great little thing should be. Somehow I think it will begin and/or end with my girlfriend showing me how to make soap.  I never can tell about such things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Stillâ€¦as of this writing, I have a comfortable chairâ€¦a brilliant chair that reclines and swivels most artfully at the slightest prompting.  For some reason, Iâ€™ve been thankful for it these past few days.  I was looking at it recently and noticed it was getting that familiar, broken-in look that makes me smile and renders me ragingly hopeful.  It is just the sort of chair you can collapse into after an adventure of any size or scope, and its embrace brings relief without exception although I only fear that in loving it, Iâ€™m damning the bolts to spontaneously strip and fall out so that the whole works (footstool included) collapses into a clunky, leathery heap.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I keep replaying the BoureÃ© and Hornpipe movements of  Wassermusik.  Iâ€™m like a crazy personâ€¦chomping a whiff-like cigar that\\\'s threatening to fall apart any moment, listening to these same two movements of Handel over and over.  Thereâ€™s a breeze every once in a while, and it billows the curtains which, in turn, brush my arm, or the hamstring I pulled attempting to trail run for the first time.  Itâ€™s all really quite dreamy since it just occurred to me that  Iâ€™m listening for answers in chords.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2901,'<p>\r\n\r\nI last wrote from Luang Probang. From there we all went to Vang Vienne, a 6 hr bus ride or so to the south. It\\\'s a nice little tourist infested town on the Mekong with about a bazillion caves you can hike around in and explore. Some are fixed up with pathways and lighting and they charge you a fee to enter, and some we found by accident. One had a sign that said, \\\"pay here\\\" but there was no \\\"here\\\" there, and no one around except for some local kids playing stickball in the swampy grass. We wandered in with our teensy flashlight and soon noticed we were accompanied by a local guy with a candle. I thought I should be frightened, a stranger following us into a dark secluded cave, but he just wanted to look around as we did. The cave turned out to contain a large underground stream. We waded into the cavern until it got deep enough to get our butts wet. I started to wonder what might be in the water that was too dark to see, that was surrounding us, so back we went, to the daylight.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere really wasn\\\'t much to the town, but I did find a sweet woman named Noi selling fabric in the market who made me some $5 pants. I wandered through and ordered them in the early afternoon. They were finished and perfectly tailored in less than 2 hrs. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAfter a few days there, we (along with our Laos traveling buddies) headed off to Vientienne, the capital of Laos. You wouldn\\\'t know it by looking at it, there isn\\\'t much going on there either. One day we stopped by the national museum, where I learned all about the evil \\\"American imperialists and their puppets\\\" and The Secret War in Laos. I had never really heard about any of this before, it being a big secret, but it is quite interesting. Did you know the US bombed Laos more than Vietnam? Or that the tonnage of Bombs dropped by the US on the Plain of Jars in Northern Laos exceeds the tonnage of bombs dropped by the US in both Europe and the Pacific in WWII? Or that there was an average of one bombing in Laos every 8 minutes for 9 years? And all because of the Communists... Who won control of Laos anyway. \r\n</p> <p>\r\nAfter chatting with a couple of girls we\\\'d seen on our earlier two day slow boat ride, we realized we should go back up to the north and see some of the more remote sites. The roads to these parts are unpaved for the most part, and by all accounts unpleasant.  So we headed to the Plain of Jars by way of the planes of Lao Aviation. Scary little planes rivaling both Royal Phnom Pehn Air and Air Mandalay for scariest plane flight ever.\r\n</p> <p>\r\nWhen flying into the northeast, you are amazed by the sight of hundreds and hundreds of bomb craters left there by the American bombers in the 70s. You can still see them because the Agent Orange has killed all of the vegetation, and not much has been able to grow there aside from harsh grasses unfit even to feed animals. According to our Plain of Jars guide Leon, it will be approximately 90 years before the land can once again sustain crops, trees, and life.\r\n</p> <p>\r\nThe Plain of Jars is sort of a mysterious Stonehenge type of thing, there are about 52 sites total, all with carved stone jars that range from regular small jar size, to about 8 meters high. You can only visit about 3 or 4 of the sites at this time. Most are off limits due to the fact that they are still cataloging the jars, and because there are still thousands of unexploded landmines waiting for North Vietnamese soldiers to wander past. Unfortunately these days there are more animals and farmers and children around than soldiers. Anyway, it was pretty neat stuff. We saw about 4 sites with Leon.\r\n</p><p>\r\nLater he took us to see some UXO\\\'s (Unexploded ordinances AKA landmines) on the Ho Chi Mihn Trail. We also saw remnants of Russian and American tanks, and went to a village where the people reuse old shell casings and artillery equipment for fences or houses and make all their silverware out of recycled airplane aluminum.\r\n</p><p>\r\nNext we flew over to the west a bit to visit Luang Nam Tha. This area is full of remote hilltribes villages, and we stayed a couple kilometers outside of Muang Sing, about a 40 minute walk south of a boarder crossing to China. We went for walks and found many happy hilltribe villages and got all sorts of rustic bracelets and crafts thrust into our faces. \r\n</p><p>At one village we showed up right as all of the men of an Ahka tribe were preparing for a spirit ceremony. They of course asked us to stay, and so we had to. (If you don\\\'t accept an invitation, they might think you\\\'re an evil spirit, which could cause all sorts of problems). We were invited had to eat with them, and drink Lao Lao, a really super strong rice liquor. It is unthinkable that we would not eat this special ceremonial food, as it was a huge honor to be invited. So down we sat, and traded Lao Lao for 20 year old Scotch. The meal wasn\\\'t so bad, except for the fact that they enjoy eating raw pigs blood on special occasions. The roasted chicken though, was tasty.</p>\r\n'),(2902,'<p>it is by pure luck that i should be awake and (vaguely) aware as others in our hemisphere doze and dream...some peacefully...some not so much so...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in any event, i remain in my guarded, conscious, slightly spicy nacho state...and while my once admirable brains seek true answers, my wit goes out shopping for a toupee as it seems to be tired of perpetrating the fraud of a combover.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was thinking of a cressida to bring to my troilus even though i have no troilus to start with.  i was also wondering if it were my lucky lot to always find succor in such a state?</p> \r\n\r\n<p>pleasantly pished ... relieved...sanguine... even though i have come to exist so far out of my natural element that i can\\\'t even see where i came from when i squint.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>i think it\\\'s that she makes the most curative bombay and tonics and (in spite of my toxic spines) she chooses to share them with me, her ginger company converting me into that which transcends what i was when everyone who was someone thought me to be something.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2903,'<p>\r\nits another one of those nights where im not to sure how much i can write. the money in my wallet does not equal much. i found myself bribing a taxi driver into taking me and my friends to an area of town close to where we were. the walk wouldnt have killed us, but it was raining and well we are lazy drunks. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe played the shooting game and i won two holeman keychains and then i saw this osama bin laden statue in the window so i asked how much it was and i paid the four thousand five hundred for it...and then found out that there was a matching george bush jr figure to go with it. picked that up too. they are classic. the osama is holding a model of the pentagon and an airplane...and the dubya is holding a model of the wtc. only in korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits been gin and tonic day since six this afternoon. me and silvia went to techno mart to get my camera, which was fixed, and then we went to coex in a weird taxi ride. we were originally going to goto chinchon for a beer but the taxi driver took the wrong exit on the highway and we ended up near COEX so we just went there. it was fun. after some window shopping we went to the 7/11 and picked up four cans of gin and tonic ready mix and a bag of chips and a roll of film so that i could try out my new camera. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe funny part was when i opened my film box the film wasnt in one of those plastic cans and the little tab that always sticks out of the film wastn sticking out. we drank gin and tonic and talked and kissed and just had fun. i ended up drinking three of the four cans and well lets just say i got wasted. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen i came home via taxi; my favourite way to travel in this land of smells and rain and kimchi, and i had a quick shower and then it was off the sinchon with shane and tina. we  went to our favourite bar with joannne and drank the plum soju we love so much and i hit my head on a low beam in the basement but all is good. i guess. i mean my head hurts even still, but im fine. the blood i thought was dripping from my head was only sweat. nine out of ten times i hate sweat pouring down my head, but at this one moment it seemed all too great. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngod im drunk. in addition to the plum soju, gin and tonics, and the bottle of tequila me, shane, chris, tina, tim, and some other dude shared at a bar called rolling stone, i think when i get home ill down a few HITE PRIMES and call it a night. god its hot here. god i hate my job more and more, yet i like this girl more and more so my time in korea seems all that more worthwhile, yet my job seems more and more crap. the taste of salt, lemon slice, and tequila is still in my mouth...even after having a gin and tonic at MATAMATA. the club was busy and hot. it was filled with white girls and it made me feel sick. i dont know why white people make me feel so sick here. i just know that whenever i talk to any of them i have these totally fake and superficial conversations...and i feel as though im some sort of backpacker travelling from resort to resort around asia. why cant these people be real. why do they have to cling so much to where they are from and how they talk and what kinds of conversations they have. at least when i have a superficial conversation with a korea, its more so because they dont have the english skills to have a more in depth conversation...but when someone who speaks english...starts off on some random conversation...they seem to all say the same thing. \\\'where are you from?\\\' \\\'what are you doing here?\\\' \\\'how do you like it?\\\'...blah blah blah. why not just talk to me about how shit the heat is or somthing. i dont need you talking to me in this conversation that YOU think is worthwhile while i cant wait until it ends. id rather talk to myself. id rather just not talk. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits the backpacker mentality. its the mentality that when you see something seemingly close to home...IE WHITE, you latch on and try and get to know the perosn. fuck that. im in korea. fuck white people. fuck their predictable ways. i see them and their ways and i know. i know what they are going to say and i know how they are going to act. its just so false and gross to me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat did i want to say? what did i really start writing for? do you know? i dont remember. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nburning school house. thats what it was. another day. more sober day. more indepth day. less angst day. less anger day. more anger day. who the fuck knows. tomorrow. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2904,'<p>A man crawls then walks: Uptown, Downtown.<br>\r\nSlippery human oil makes his poly transparent.<br>\r\nHis crazy eyes are all big and needy.<br>\r\nBony prongs, where fingers once were, gesticulate wildly: he\\\'s compensating for his unclear speech.<br>\r\nHe\\\'s an artist, you know, and he wants desperately to be understood.<br>\r\nWild hair - devil may care.<br>\r\nThe time is right for a sit-down on the concrete.<br>\r\nSomeone owns the lucky storefront; it\\\'s Vietnamese.<br>\r\nRelaxing, he fills his pants; the semi-circular head of an organic stream casually piddles out from the general area of his ass.<br>\r\nThe day\\\'s hot sun means only minutes of life for the liquid on the sidewalk.<br>\r\nAnd the funky dark blue dumpster stinking of fish and guts is the stronger of the area\\\'s smells.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s ok buddy.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s all ok.<br>\r\nFor nine long months your mother carried you.<br>\r\nAnd for years she dreamed about all the promise that you held.<br>\r\nHey: I love you still - in that casually disturbed way that made me remember you but not help you.</p>'),(2905,'<img src=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/2905_1.gif\\\">\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<b>barefoot</b>\r\n<p>i wouldn\\\'t be caught dead barefoot in new york<br>\r\nif i walked barefoot <br>\r\nI would probably <br>\r\nstep on <br>\r\ndog shit, needle, urine-soaked metrocard <br>\r\njackhammer debris, abandoned dot-com equipment<br>\r\nmustard squirted from a hot dog<br>\r\ntaxi receipt, <br>\r\nor i might offend bloomberg <br>\r\non his weekly <br>\r\n\\\"public transportation\\\" adventure. <br>\r\ni take that back. <br>\r\nmetrocards are plastic <br>\r\nso the urine would just pool on top <br>\r\nand squish through my toes.<br>\r\n</p>'),(2906,'down <br>\r\nfalling, he goes<br>\r\nonward<br>\r\nwithout reprocusion<br>\r\nfor his<br>\r\nacts.<br>\r\nthe boy is alone<br>\r\nstranded<br>\r\nfrightened of life<br>\r\nadrift in a sea of falsehood<br>\r\nprecious and fragile<br>\r\ndrowning under waves of pretense<br>\r\ngasping for air<br>\r\nlife awaits<br>\r\nand he stalls<br>\r\nchoking<br>\r\non his own insecurities<br>\r\ngoing<br>\r\nat a thousand feet per second<br>\r\nto nowhere.<br>'),(2907,'we laid in bed together and i wondered if i could cry. if you would hear the tear drops upon the pillow, if it would make sense if i just hid my face. i tired to tell you that it didn\\\'t matter. that no matter what i said i was proably lying... and i was infact, in your bed where everything that was right felt wrong. \r\nbut that was just a dream, a thought, a miss fired nothing that dissolved into the sweltering air. a fan blew my hair off my forehead and i slid futher into the crisp linnen blankets that were stained with mascara that i was too tired to wash off. stained with my lipstick that was kissed off while dreaming of the way it felt when your hands would touch mine. \r\ni wondered where i was going with all these night time thoughts that only brought me to a place of dark and unfiltered fears. i wondered why i wanted to wake you up. why my mind felt so numb and sad. i wondered why i couldn\\\'t sleep or why the pillow seemed to be a rock that stuck into my head and let my brains spill out and upon the crappy air in the mellow night that just didn\\\'t know how to pass. \r\n'),(2908,'<p>I was sitting at home alone when they came.  People that I have known, now know, and am coming to know.  Family, friends, enemies.  They came one at a time to my door.  They came inside and said kind words to me.  It took several days and sleepless nights for them to all visit.  Some stayed longer than others.  Some brought gifts taking many different forms.  Some brought great meals and ate with me until we had each eaten enough.  Some I no longer cared for and were just around long enough to show their face before leaving again.  Some just sat and shared a special silence with me.  A comforting presence saying more than clumsy words could ever impart.</p>\r\n<p>The last of them to come told me this.  I became saddened.  Despite the fact that I was tired and had not slept for days I did not want this event to end.  I was told not to be sad and to look back upon everyone who had come so far and to concentrate on that.  I was also told that only when this person left would it ever end.  This last person was not the best of friends nor the worst of enemies.  This person was one of those background noise people who just seem to flit about on the periphery of your inner circle: never the focus and never invisible, always just there.  The kind of person that you can spend an hour with but any longer becomes an exercise in pain ignorance.</p>\r\n<p>This person told me that as long as they were with me it would never end and I could continue living my life and seeing the people I want to whenever I could.  However, should I turn this person away, all the people I had seen during this great visitation would be forever gone to me.  That was the catch: be forever with someone I can spend no more than an hour with or give up everyone I\\\'ve ever known.</p>\r\n<p>And I cried.  For days and days I cried.  I stopped only long enough to remove eyelashes which had followed the current into my mouth.</p>\r\n<p>Finally, after many days of crying and after many days of being visited by people; tired and hungry, it was hinted at that my decision on the matter at hand was necessary.  I asked, no, I begged for more time.  My pleas were ignored and again my decision was requested.</p>\r\n<p>This was well over a year ago.  Not a day has gone by that I don\\\'t wish I could choose differently.  I truly did not know what the gravity of my decision would truly be.  Had I chosen the other option it would have most likely been the lesser of two evils as nothing could be quite as bad as this.</p>\r\n<p>I gave up everyone for the sake of my own comfort.  Thus the true cost of my selfishness was priced and paid for.</p>\r\n<p>In terms of a gift which keeps on giving, this was like the mistake which keeps getting worse.</p>\r\n'),(2909,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2909_1.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>I met the young Ms. Finn perhaps ten years ago; I\\\'m not very good with dates.  She and her sister appeared at the mouth of a subway tunnel along Ashland, near Division.  At that time, I gave rides to hitchers.  That was an easy thing to do: I had a truck.  Every potential passenger was given an appraisal upon my approach, and then directed towards the bed or the cab of my vehicle.  In a few cases, of course, I avoided eye contact altogether and just kept rolling.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ten to twenty years ago the near-west side of the city was home to a large number of people who made things that, while non-traditional, were still easy to regard as art.  It was common to see a man or woman in their early twenties walking down the city sidewalks with stuff: the big, bulky stuff that Chicago art used to be made from.  They were my favorite people to chauffer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Rainbo still reclines on the sidewalk there.  Low, squatting, and lethargic: it\\\'s a little bit of architectural heroin.  I saw Ken last week; he used to work the door.  He said that he\\\'s too old to do that anymore; now he tends the bar.  And a few months ago I ran into Ernesto.  He displayed a portion of his toy collection in the windows of the Rainbo during the holidays; he\\\'s closer to his pension than any scene now.  As the neighborhood changes around her, the Rainbo seems to remain just about the same.  And judging by the smoky interior, they haven\\\'t opened a window since I was there with Ms. Finn and her sister, perhaps ten years ago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ms. Finn arrived during the holidays.  And leather clad young Elvis was I.  We explored the city.  We drank in the Rainbo; Ernesto\\\'s toy collection was proudly displayed.  We took photos in the booth in the back of the club.  And now those pictures are, \\\"...something to remind me.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m between one thing and another, hanging upon things like memories.<br>\r\nI\\\'m postponing the end of what has been; I\\\'m delaying the start of something new.<br>\r\nThe clarity of winter nights passed away with the spring.<br>\r\nAnd hot Summer lazily takes me in.</p>'),(2910,'One day you\\\'ll work out why a stranger called you about a car and ended up being a man about a dog. <p>The little treasure that emerged from under that rust heap was worth so much more than its missed transaction. In such a short time so much happened to you, didn\\\'t it?<br>\r\nSo much happened to him, too. One day of happiness with you was worth the greasy wait.<br>\r\nNow, each time you see the signs in the backyard of where he was, where he is, you\\\'ll remember something about him that tells you something about yourself.<br>\r\nIs life about anything more than a stolen sandal and a full bowl of biscuits?<br>\r\nSomeone\\\'s going to love you no matter where you are, no matter what the price, no matter what it is you need. This knowledge was enough to set his tail aspin.<br>\r\nThe wiggly worm knew that knowing someone\\\'s going to be yours, yours, yours is not all there is to know, but it sure can feel good. And so it should.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ll bet you two bucks and a carbie spring this is true.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2911,'<P>today is a blank day. my heart doesn\\\'t hurt because it just can\\\'t anymore. i\\\'ve cried a few tears, but mostly i just feel numb. i\\\'ve looked for comfort, but haven\\\'t found it. so then i stopped looking. i miss your eyes. read through some old emails. thought about the way i felt when i received them. thought about the way i felt when i sent them. thought about the way i felt when i heard that message. when i saw her face. when i knew it was true. and now, when i believe it isn\\\'t. thought about the time we sat on the wall next to the water and talked late into the night. and how we couldn\\\'t figure out how to get home. and how the next morning things had changed. but not forever. just for a while. thought about the mosaics in the background. thought about the fabric that was worn thin from years of use. thought about the cracked wood and the metallic sounds. thought about 5 minutes doing the same thing. thought about 1 minute doing a million others. thought about laughing wildly, thought about crying desperately, thought about questioning. thought about bliss, thought about terror, thought about jubilance, thought about softness. thought about lips, thought about hands, thought about movement, thought about silence, thought about standards, thought about firsts, thought about competition, thought about compassion, thought about joy, thought about comfort. thought of your face when you didn\\\'t know i was looking. thought about your fears, the ones that only i knew. thought about pages of advice and spaces without. thought about the moment that i knew i loved you. thought about the moment you knew. thought about the confusion. thought about the moment when i could no longer see you, but could only hear your voice. the moment you fell asleep and all i could hear was your breath. thought about the grass beneath us. thought about the flashing lights above. thought about the steamy windows and the drive back. thought about the goodbye. thought about the hello. thought about the way you ran up to me, uninhibited, like no one could see you. thought about the first card and the last flower. thought about dinner on the balcony, looking onto our past. thought about pictures with large grins, and memories with exaggerated frowns. thought about the last note, thought about the used kleenex and the red eyes. thought about endless tears. thought about the last hug. thought about the first swing. thought about the last time. thought about the first. thought about the hot water, and the steam rising from our bodies. thought about the wicked laughter. thought about the calm afterwards. thought about the sultry playing, thought about the smoky room, thought about the rainy nights. thought about the confusion. thought about the late nights, the early mornings. thought about staying awake just for you, with you. thought about letting you sleep. thought about your laugh, thought about the way you said my name, thought about the silence. thought about questions. thought about age, thought about irony, thought about meaning. thought about the smells, the tastes, the images, the textures. thought about the tightness. thought about the constriction. thought about the phone call. thought about the screaming. thought about the disbelief. thought about the uncontrollable shaking. thought about the dryness, thought about the bleeding, thought about the crushing. thought about the end.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthought about you.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthought about trees with big bolts screwed into them which now carry on your life.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(2912,'<p>\r\nim so sick of my boss. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlet me fill you in on something. let me tell you something that made me so mad.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere are a few things that make me sick about working. its not the kids. its not the teaching. its not the city. its not the job. Its my boss. he\\\'s a dimwit. he\\\'s hollow and moronic and spacey and has the memory of a fruit fly. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1. he has been \\\'looking into\\\' getting the sink in my apartment fixed for almost six months. I have been here almost 4 months and shane has been at the school almost 6 months, and the sink has been broken the whole time. god knows how long it was broken before that. he keeps telling us he\\\'s looking into getting it fixed, or having someone fix it, or going over to the apartment to fix it. never once has anyone even bothered to look at the sink. we\\\'ve been told to be home at certain times, we\\\'ve been told something will happen. nothing happens. when we do the dishes a pool of dirt and mould tinged water covers the kitchen floor. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n2. he lost my degree. nuff said. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n3. Like i said. I have almost been here 4 months and I still dont have my insurance card. Im paying for it at each pay day, but as of yet i have been unable to get them to get me this god damn card. now they are telling me that its not them, and that its the company they deal with...but i just look at it as something they can just lay blame on without having to question their own work ethics. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n4. last but not least. The foreign teachers minus one work at least one saturday a month. Its only two hours in the middle of the day and while the time we have to work sucks, its only two hours so no one really makes a big stink out of it. in the contract is says that overtime will be paid at the pay period of each month. I finally got really snippy with my boss about this money. Its money i need. Its money i deserve. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith my calculations the school owed me 12 hours of overtime. i sit down with my boss in his office and he tells me that according to his records i dont have any overtime coming to me. i basically look him in the face and laugh. I tell him. \\\"what the hell are you talking about?\\\" \\\"have you not seen me here on saturdays?\\\" \\\"did you not ask me two weeks ago why i had worked three saturdays in a row?\\\" i think he could see the smoke rising from my head. he says something like \\\"before you say anything, let me explain.\\\" I pipe down and wait for his long winded answer.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe tells me that because i had taken two sick days, that my over time became null and void because of the missed work. i respond by telling him that my contract says that i have two paid sick days and that it had nothing to do with my overtime. he says something like \\\"yes thats true, but thats not how its done in america\\\". I look at him and say \\\"im not in america. im in korea. the address on my contract is korea. if i dont get paid my overtime im not working saturdays anymore. you are talking to me about apples and oranges. you think i give a shit about how things are done in america?\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe keeps bringing up how its not the way things are done and i keep bringing up how im in korea and that i have a contract and the contract says things he doesnt seem to be living upto. he then has to gaul to ask me why i need my overtime so much. i just looked at him and laughed. i said \\\"what does that have to do with you paying me for work that i did?\\\" I ended up giving him some sob story about my student loans and banks and blah blah blah. like i give a shit about lying to this fuckwad at this point in time. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe just keeps at me about how things are done in america and i feel like slapping him and telling him how much of a broken record he is. IM IN KOREA ASSHOLE. LIVE UPTO THE CONTRACT. IM CANADIAN. IM NOT AMERICAN. IM IN KOREA. LIVE UPTO THE CONTRACT. kept thinking things like \\\'wherezmynine\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat night i had dreams. i had a few really good dreams. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the first dream i tied a few sticks of dynamite around a molotof cocktail and launched it through a window and burned the school down. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni can remember seeing my boss getting to the school while i was hiding in a tree and watching the tears run down his eyes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the next dream i was a giant. a big giant and i pretended that the school was one of those fireworks. the \\\'burning schoolhouse\\\'. it wizzed and boomed and burned to the ground. oh how much fun that dream was; dancing and prancing around the burning school house of morons. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next dream set the school in the deep jungles of vietnam, circa 1974. I was a viet cong soldier who just happened to stumble upon an english school(cia front). With my soviet era flame thrower i filled the belly of the school with smoke and flames. kids, teachers, and management all jumping from the windows and doorways; their backs on fire. snickering in my dream as im sure i was in my sleep, i faded into the jungle never to return. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou might say things at work are a bit tense. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2913,'       you found what you weren\\\'t looking for\r\n       upon an accidental wave\r\n       the things that lash out honesty \r\n       aren\\\'t the thinigs you crave\r\n\r\n       bottled up in amber glass\r\n       and bobbing on the icy sea\r\n       hidden in the darkesst waters\r\n       far away from me. \r\n\r\n       and sitll i found that little glimmer\r\n       that blinded both my eyes\r\n       suddenly the thought of sight \r\n       exactly what i dispise. \r\n\r\n       keep on moving to the shore\r\n       to get from this rocking boat\r\n       one tracked mind of mine\r\n       to keep my head a float'),(2914,'\r\n               you keep on walking thinking that the world will be different on your next\r\n               turn, a click of the heel a brush of the hand... but your eyes just won\\\'t\r\n               adjust to the high contrast beauty that the sun seems to want to hide. in the\r\n               northwest, in the dark alley, on your back and without a care in the fucking\r\n               world. she is the only thing that seems like puke. like piss. like vinegar in\r\n               your every wound. she is the way that the hollow product pushing pimp\r\n               television likes to flicker, strobe light images of perfection in your mind\r\n               while all you want to do is rest. and you, you fuck face. you are the way that\r\n               the sun warms the carpet in the winter, the way that hot chocolate and popcorn\r\n               bring smiles to the fridged soul... if you believe in that kind of stuff.\r\n               because really what is love? does it really feel the way that we keep telling\r\n               ourselves it does. is it really flowers and hearts and smiles and kisses,\r\n               can\\\'t it exist in the tears that we cry, when the pangs of bitter fucking\r\n               anger cloud our eyes... when our lips touch the lips of others, when our hands\r\n               can\\\'t seem to find the ones they should fit with... when the smiles seem too\r\n               happy to be comfortable, when everything seems right but it isn\\\'t with the one\r\n               who holds your name inside their heart waiting for that moment when we can\r\n               say, oh i love you too... why... do... we... keep... on... moving in the wrong\r\n               direction only to end up in the right, why must she travel down the roads that\r\n               nobody wants to walk... that only a few feel like they can\\\'t handle without a\r\n               chaser. keep on screaming her goddamned name and wonder if she\\\'ll answer,\r\n               becasue this road isn\\\'t new to her, this road isn\\\'t the dead end it appears to\r\n               be, and if it is... then maybe she just lives at the end of it.'),(2915,'i wanted to be held as i cried. \r\n                     i wanted to hear that you still loved me.\r\n\r\n                     i wonder if you understand how hard it was to tell you\r\n                     of the baby that lied within...\r\n\r\n                     words that wouldn\\\'t slip across my lips, \r\n                     super glued inside my throat...\r\n\r\n                     i didn\\\'t know what to do. what to say...\r\n                     thinking, lying, smiling, crying. \r\n\r\n                     my soul dispensed and on the floor... \r\n                     part of you in me... growing, dividing, and thus-ly dying. \r\n\r\n                     blood: crimson, \r\n                     heart: broken, \r\n                     mind: unspoken...\r\n\r\n                     i didn\\\'t ever get to ask you... \r\n                     give up. \r\n                     abort, \r\n                     or keep... \r\n\r\n                     it wasn\\\'t our choice, it wasn\\\'t mine... \r\n                     rejected by my body...\r\n                     only with me a short time... \r\n\r\n                     who could i tell such a secret... \r\n                     to trust with what i had trouble telling you?\r\n\r\n                     and no matter how much pain i was in, \r\n                     you were to busy to notice... \r\n\r\n                     i kept it all locked inside... \r\n                     too scared of the looks of passerby\\\'s...\r\n\r\n                     rumors spread fast, the truth even faster...\r\n                     and for things to be said that i can barely utter...\r\n\r\n                     things that are whispered as i cry late at night...\r\n                     falling further away from you, from me, from life...\r\n\r\n                     too much for me to handle \r\n                     too much for you to see...\r\n\r\n                     why couldn\\\'t you just hold me? \r\n                     why couldn\\\'t you tell me it would be okay? \r\n                     why couldn\\\'t we make every thing alright?! \r\n\r\n                     and now... an undulating pain that resonates\r\n                     plagues my being, \r\n\r\n                     scarred... \r\n                     alone...\r\n                     nobody to listen... \r\n                     nobody who cares...'),(2916,'no reason to be sad... no reason to be sad.... no reason to be sad... it is\r\n            the season for no reason to be sad. \r\n\r\n            read. write. dispense all of your emotions onto pale blue lines. crisp white\r\n            paper and a ball point. read. write. live vicariously through paperback novels\r\n            and fictional characters. read. write. hide behind the comfort of a pen name. \r\n\r\n            you smell of discontent and anger. run down and out of luck... you my friend,\r\n            look of fuck. \r\n\r\n            what have you been doing with your time? the pleasure found in pain, you hide\r\n            in the warm boundries of fornication, apperations, amputations, aspirations,\r\n            pink carnations that adorn your grave. \r\n\r\n            change the way you look, you still read like a book... i turn the battered\r\n            pages to get to the end, despite the cloudy semen that holds the pages\r\n            together, smears the words and tears the brittle paper... you break in half\r\n            and not even the strongest tape can hold you together.\r\n\r\n            binding broke. shoe untied. eyes that can\\\'t quite focus, a hazy world in an\r\n            alaskan season... a temporary...but seemingly permenent midnight grips firmly\r\n            to the sky... living in the dark you wonder why, the story continues, the play\r\n            goes on, the never ending script that was tossed to the wind tumbles around in\r\n            the morning dew. \r\n\r\n            the pages run together, mixed emotions arrise... nothing is pure anymore.\r\n            nothing is in it\\\'s right order. \r\n\r\n            the end comes before the beginning.'),(2917,'<P>test test</P>\r\n<IMG ALIGN=\\\"left\\\" SRC=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n\r\n<P>that is right, this article was a test.  but i have been looking at it in the admin area, just sitting there.  \\\"test,\\\" it says.  \\\"test,\\\" i think to myself.  life is so full of tests.  i have one this week as a matter of fact.  a test to see if i would go after a 3 month contract in san francisco vs. my incredibly stable job that makes me feel like a monk sometimes, it is so stable.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have with me right now, a shot glass that is empty.  well, not really a shot glass, more like a glass glass, albiet a small one.  id say it is an easy double and a half if you filled it.  or a triple.  thirty minutes ago it was half filled with cuervo 1800.  i had a lime too.  i took the opportinity to remanice to my roomates about a trip to mexico i did once, and resolve to head down there again in the near future, down baja.  i havent been down there.  my story to my roomates was about the time i skipped out on work and went to a northern mexico town with a spring, <i>con mi amiga Jessica</i>.  she was stretching her spanish and i was along for the ride.  id send her out to get this or that so she could practice her conversation skills on anyone that crossed her path.  she was a talker.  we hitch hiked out of some border town that our grey hound dropped us off at.  shit if i know where *spit*.  on our way back, the truck we were in wanted to stop off at a still and get some mescal.  i bought a bottle for shits and giggles.  the stuff tastes like gasoline.  that bottle stuck with me for a good year.  on hot summer nights, i drug it out and passed it to the most drunk person around with a salute.  if they drank a shot, it was their last.  man that stuff was bad.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>well that is my story.  doesnt have anything to do with tests.  maybe testicles.  huevos.  cajones.  cause that mescal i had bought was so bad.</P>'),(2918,'<p>\r\ni leave on monday. heading west into the sunset. riding an iron horse through the cold thin air with my love beside me. got my laptop. got my camera. got my ipod. three countries in three weeks. that is if you can count a layover as visiting a country. we will be <i>international</i> travelers. we both have been to mexico, but mexico is pseudo-international. the other side of the world. that makes for the status of <i>international</i> traveler.\r\n</P><P>\r\nother side of the world\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe farthest off this continent i have ever been was hawaii. i wonder if this will help on resumes when the day comes to find a new job. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"i have almost no experience at anything, but i am an international traveler\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\ni should think that would be enough to sway things my way. however, this most undoubtedly would have little to no effect in reality. before i leave i must make business cards for my love and i. it will include merely our name and under our name will be:\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"experienced international traveler\\\"</P>'),(2919,'<p>He wakes up and doesnâ€™t remember where he is. The sun is blinding him, targeting his eyes from between two slanted slats of a dusty rusting window blind. He moves his head to the left and raises it slightly. As his eyes adjust he does not become less confused. The walls of the room are bare and dirty and olive drab. There are grease spots and here and there, lighter rectangles where once pictures hung. The floor is bare plywood spattered with paint and in a spot under the window, a remnant of carpet padding sits under 4 of what must have been more stubborn carpet staples.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He swallows and then suddenly feels afraid. He stands up too fast and spins on one heel to see what heâ€™s been lying on. It isnâ€™t as bad as he feared. A bare mattress with only a few spots and stains between itsâ€™ thin blue stripes. There is nothing else in the room. He reaches for the light switch, and when he flips it up, there is no buzz of electricity. He tries it a few more times until he believes it. His eyes widen as his arm sinks slowly from the light switch until it hangs uselessly at his hip. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The rest of the apartment resembles the bedroom. Worn green indoor/outdoor carpeting still covers the empty living room. A dinette set gently rusts in the kitchenette, itâ€™s chairs all stranded at odd angles except for one, which stands upright and at silent at the head of the table. He is relieved when he notices he is fully clothed in his own clothing. There is no sign of the woman who reminds him of Jackie O, and when he reaches the door, he sees only one set of footprints in the dust that lingers on the linoleum of the entryway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Out on the street it takes him a few minutes to get his bearings and find the bus stop from the evening before. As he waits he tries not to think about what might have happened to him. Instead he stares over the pitted blacktop into the pet store on the opposite side of the avenue. Thereâ€™s a sale on pet food in a green bag with black stripes. He strains his eyes to try to figure out if it is for cats or dogs. He reaches his right hand into in his pocket for a cigarette and feels a wad of soft paper. He pulls it out and flattens the wadded cocktail napkin into his hand. The name Maria and a phone number are written on it, in lipstick.</p>\r\n'),(2920,'<p>\r\nwhen you\\\'re there, i sleep lengthwise<BR>\r\nand when you\\\'re gone, i sleep diagonal in my bed.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s a love song. simple but effective. concise.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\ni have been in love with a girl for over two years now. trying to explain that love is anything but concise. i focus on making things small. my emotional state however is too large. there are too many complexities and connections. there are webs entangled within webs. one thought connects to an emotion which connects to a few memories which connects to more thoughts and so on. much to large to ever encapsulate. it is something i cannot show even to the one i love. how could one possible display something so immense? <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nso when she asks me <i>why</i> i love her, i try to explain when really i should just play her the song.</p>'),(2921,'<p>I was all set to complain.  I swear.  All my private planets had migrated into prime whinging conjunction, but that was shot to atoms when it suddenly occurred to me what a truly lucky bastard I am.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Perhaps not in the whizzing down the coast road in my quicksilver M3, contemplating my George Lucas-esque bank account sense of the word, but what the hell â€¦</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Here I sit in a house with those windows I like â€¦ the sort that nearly go all the way to the floor.  Through the east ones, I can see the garden and the freshly-mown lawn benefiting from a spontaneous rain shower that I splashed about in like some demoniac Nikolai Gogol might have written a short story about had he not been so busy penning the one about the nose wearing the overcoat.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It makes me want to worship the most mundane things as gods.  Toothpaste, fresh bread, laundry soap, ginger ales with crushed iceâ€¦the terror of an upcoming rent day noticeably absent as I balance my checkbook and find that I still have money left over to buy a new copy of The Sheltering Sky and a halfway decent expedition pack.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tonight I get to sleep in a bed (not on a floorfutonroofcarseattruckbed) on a full belly instead of an empty one.  Tomorrow Iâ€™ll have biscuits with marmalade and several cups of strong Ethiopian and float on whatever this is for as long as I canâ€¦or at least until the meds wear off.  Whatever the case, Iâ€™ll take it.</p>\r\n'),(2922,'<p>\r\nim lost.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nive had so much to drink. im lost. what pc bong am i in. what am i doing?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim so afraid right now. how do i get home?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe inside scoop? you want the inside scoop?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe inside scoop is that ive had a few bottles of wine and a few bottles of beer since i last spoke to s5 a few hours ago. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe conversation was good. the conversation was relieving...but when i got home, all i could think about was how she left without saying goodbye. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim sick and tired of the people around be being so inconsiderate. i say please and thankyou. i ask how people are doing...but not one asks me. fuckem. FUCK YOU!!!!!\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nMY boss doesnt even say please and thankyou to his kids. i listen to him. i listen to him. i listen to him. he doesnt even say please and thankyou , yet he tells me how much he thinks manners are imporant. FUCK YOU. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nshe left without even saying goodbye. she left for japan. yeah you know who you are. you know who you are. sorry doestn cut it. sorry is such a copout. i know. ive used sorry more than you have i can guarentee itt.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou are having fun. your mind is elsewhere and im here thinking about hwo much you dont think of me. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni watched a movie. i went on my roof. i thought about jumping, thank god my building isnt high enough. i know that if i jumped id land and just break something. i wouldnt die. how much i hate this life i live in. how much i hate these decades. how much i hate my standing. how much do you think i hate? i hate so much you cant even begin to understand. i hate so much. i hate so much. i hate more than you can even comprehend. i think about hate. i think about hate in so many ways. i think about hate in those who i kill in my daydreams, i think about hate in those who i make fun of and ithink about hate in the news i watch. i watch the hatred. i watch those i hate. i hate those i watch. i fucking hate. i fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck fuck without even fucking. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hate those who fuck. i hate those who think fucking is good. i hate those who hate. i hate the racists. i hate the wealthy. i hate the ones who think nothing is wrong with this world. i hate those who say \\\"thats just the way it is.\\\" i hate those who want to do nothing in life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni hate because the people i have mentioned think nothing should be changed. they think the world is the way it is because its fine and dandy. they think they can escape but when they cant they just do drugs and when they cant escape they pay for some retreat at some richy rehab centre and when they cant rehab they commit suicide and someone writes a book on how tragic a loss it was. the countless lives lost due to rich people feeling sorry for themselves. well i feel sorry for myself but if i killed myself id get maybe a single line in the obituary section and thats why id ont do it. not that i havnt thought of it. fuck. the older i get the less i think about it. is that right? is it right that as a young person i thought and tried to kill myself and perhaps the only reaosn i did not sucseed is because of some god damn rule of physics....fuck yarn. fuck tables. fuck yarn. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwomen are the main reason i hate life as of late. she left without even saying goodbye. what the fuck? i made her laugh. i made her feel nice. i said things to her she hadnt heard in a long time and yet she feels it necessary to torture me in this way. why? she forgot? she is sorry? she is sorry she says in a text message. WELL FUCK SORRY. WHAT THE FUCK DID SORRY EVER GET ANYONE. sorry is such a copout. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nive never asked for more than i deserved. i didnt even ask for sex. i didnt even ask for a blowjob. i asked nothing of her. the only thing i asked for was respect...and i gave it my all. i gave her what she deserved and now all i cant think about is how much of it she really did deserve. did she deserve the love i gave? did she deserve the honest things i said? did she deserve my morals?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nshe said one night that i was too nice. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nshe isnt the only one who has said im TOO NICE. \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nyet all i hear from women is how they want someone nice and who listens to them...yet on the other hand women who get into relationships always move towards the ones who treat them like shit...or who are hardasses and who have things to say...and who expect things. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwwell im sorry to inform all you fucking bitches. im a man...and yes i respect you...and yes im polite...and yes i listen...and yes i think what yo uhave to say is imporant...and no i wont demand anything of you...you ont want want to do. \r\nFUCKING SUE ME.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits obvious that women arent drawn to that. they are drawn to forcful, demanding men, who expect things. yeah wello fuck you all. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nim pissed off because its those who dont deserve squat that get it. its those who demand who get it. its those who are greedy that get it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe masses are stupid. the masses are BABO. the masses are the masses because they let those dimwits lead their lives. they let them control them, and they let them steal and take whatever they want. FUCK ALL OF YOU. FUCK THE WORLD. FUCK WHAT I DREAM OF. FUCK MY MORALS. yeah you heard me. fuck MY morals. where the fuck have they got me. yeah its so nice to say and think that my mom taught me to be mature and respect, and treat others as id like them to treat me, but where the fuck as that gotten me? fuck you. dont ever think that what im saying has anything to do with social standing or wealth. all im saying is that those morals...and those respects...and those polite behaviours have nothing to do with what women are looking for...and since im not gay and i AM looking for a girlfriend....im finding out more and more that women dont give two shits about that shit either. what they care about is a man who acts like a typical man....who has the energy and forcfulness to tell someone to fuck off. well im saying fuck off right now. where the hell are all of you? \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nam i so nice that you dont feel the need to say good bye because im so nice? do you feel that because im so good hearted that you think i know what you are thinking and for that reason you dont have to make any effort to make me feel good. all you expect of ME is to make you feel good. well let me let you in on a little secret of mine. isnt friends ship supposed to be a two way fucking street? isnt love supposed to be shared? isnt it? FUCK YOU!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim so mad. im so blah. im so depressed. im so drunk. im so lost. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngod knows how long its going to be before i hear from her,. and when i do, i know ill collapse and suck up to her. i know that ill say what i think is right to make things better, and if i dont have to do that, i know ill have forgotten everying it is ive talked about tonight. ill just let it sllide and welcome her back. why? because im so FUCKING nice. i dont hold grudges. according to LE im goodhearted or some bullshit like that. well fuck that. fuck that! what the fuck does that mean? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou may think im angry now...and you may think that im angry now that im in korea, but the only reason i havnt spoken out like this in canada as much is because in canada i smoke pot...and pot represses these feelings and makes me think about more creative things...yet here in korea...conservative capital of the world,,,drugs are non existent and all i can possibly do is drink. and drinking brings out the violence in people. drinking brings out the true frustration. men who beat their wives when drunk probablyu didnt even want to marry the woman in the first place. im no saying what they did was right,, but what im saying is that if those white trash individuals smoked as much pot as me and had as much of an education as me, they wouldnt have gotten married in the first place. fuckers. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntonight on the roof of my building i was thinking about the third world. i was thinking about one of my stupid ass coworkers was saying about some chick he \\\"knew\\\" had kids to make her welfare check bigger. well shut the fuck up man. you dont know what the fuck you are talking about. for one. the average woman would not go through nine months of labour to have a bigger welfare check and two, people give birth (in my opinion) because its a certain time in a females life where she can feel so happy. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwomen in africa dont give birth to nine women in their lives because they want a bigger welfare check....its because bringing a person into the world is a truely happy thing. no matter how desperate and deplorable life is....having a new person come out ofa womb is a GREAT and happy thing for a woman. its makes them feel good and it gives them something to love...not that the women in africa have anything to love other than the people they have braught into the world. ya know? i was thinking about this at length tonight when i was singing to myself on he roof. people always give me these notions of how third world women give birth so much because they are stupid...but i think they  give birth so much becase its the only thing that gives them any sort of happiness. when living in abject poverty., im sure its great to give birth and in that fleeting moment hope and wish the child makes it in life and does something with life. in the first world, when one gives birth they think about schools, and toys, and daycare, and all sorts of things only wealth can achieve, but in the third world its all about love. there is nothing more. abject poverty diverts all thinking towards love. love is powerful. loveis life. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nwell im first world. FUCK LOVE.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nLOVE in the first world equals money. its equals money because most people in the world think about love in terms of stability. they think about it in terms of monetary wealth and that makes me sick. FUCK YOU. FUCK THEM. fuck fuck fuck.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you understand how mad i am right now?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you understand how frustrated with the world iam?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you know how frustrated i am with friends who got postcards but never even mentioned to say thanks? do you understand how frustrated i am with people who got long emails from me, but only replied with four or five lines....if that? do you understand how i feel about the world? NO YOU DONT. NO YOU DONT BECAUSE YOU DONT GIVE A SHIT. YOU DONT GIVE A SHIT BECAUSE YOU HAVE WHAT YOU WANT...and whatever you think you want but dont have has nothing to do with love...and if it does...well then get into my war canoe and start paddling...because this canoe aint stopping. you gotta piss? lean over the side. you gotta shit? sit over the side. you gotta write an email? save it. no one cares. ive learned that. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nall im saying is that im getting closer and closer to the truth...and the closer i get...the further i get from communism. you are all so selfish. you are all so high on yourselves. fuck sharing. fuck respect. fuck the man/woman next to you....tyou gotta do what you gotta do...right? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<font size=\\\"6\\\">\r\nFUCK YOU\r\n</font>\r\n</p>\r\n'),(2923,'<p>Right now, I just keep thinking about the same thing over and over.  Youâ€™d think it would be something useful like worrying about the dispositions of my query letters or methodically tracing various plot lines and getting something down on paper as I desperately need to sell something else before I find myself homeless and reeking, wandering the streets with just a daypack and an insanely persistent case of ringworm.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But itâ€™s nothing like that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My recurring thought is that of some nameless woman.  She lives in my house, seeing me without my seeing herâ€¦perhaps sheâ€™s standing quietly in the doorway, watching my scarred arm or bent neckâ€¦youâ€™d think sheâ€™d be disgusted, but itâ€™s my head.  I control the horizontal.  I control the vertical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sheâ€™s observing me, unobserved, watching me pore over these horseshit manuscripts or seeking solace in my well-worn Stendahl or helplessly holding my head in my hands, tears threatening, molten lump in my throat, worrying a hole in my duodenum over problems I need not mention.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And softlyâ€¦gentlyâ€¦before Iâ€™m even aware sheâ€™s there, she would take the pen or the scrawled upon page or the battered text from my hands and come softly into my arms, whispering that it will be all right and that there is some bright and fundamental joy just beyond the burnt edges of whatever is troubling me.  She is all grace and assurances, and Iâ€™m suddenly reminded how much I donâ€™t deserve her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have no concept of what she looks like.  Her looks donâ€™t matter.  Only her tenderness does.  Her empathy.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>When Iâ€™m this tired,the actuality that this can never bloody happen won\\\'t even sink to a menacing depth.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Tonight I bet I sleep like the dead.</p>\r\n'),(2924,'<p>i just had one of the most interesting trips home ever.  my only regret is that i did not have my camera with me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i was taking a bus home from a friend\\\'s birthday bbq.  the good old number 14.  for those who know ottawa, this runs right down gladstone.  this runs through some sketchy areas.  you can see prostitutes there who are so nasty that they basically have track marks running up and down their faces.  this always gives a nice backdrop for some interesting voyages, but i think tonite takes the cake. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>first off, i notice this man sitting right at the front of the bus, directly down my row in front of me.  he was leaning his head sideways on the divider glass behind the bus driver.  his hair was a dirty brown, shoulder length and greasy looking.  it fell together in small groups, seemingly sticking to the side of his head.  it was as i noticed this that i realized this man\\\'s forehead was gigantic.  i mean, it was at least two foreheads tall. maybe even three.  and he just sat there, unmoving, resting his head on that plastick divider, with a look on his face that reminded me of the look on a dog\\\'s face right when they realize there are being put down. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>a few stops later this woman gets on the bus.  she was probably in her late forties, but her hair was a mixture between white and super light blonde.  i think it made her look older than she was.  now she was completely decked out in her sunday gear: a bright red dress with little white flowers all over it, with a white lace schawl over her shoulders, and to top it off, a big red bow tied into her hair.  she had this strange grin on her face that reminded me of the people out on day passes from the psychiatric hospital in my hometown.  anyways, she sat down on my side of the bus too, in the first row, right in front of Big Forehead. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>then this really fat guy gets on a while later.  it took him forever to get up the steps, and shuffle down the aisle (only to the end of the sideways facing bench right at the front, mind you) before he took an eternity turning around and slowly lowering himself (falling backwards) onto the seat.  he was wearing a black t-shirt and gray joggers, and was carrying a backpack and a walking stick. and a can of diet coke, which he was chugging feverishly. the comotion his entrace brought scared Woman in Red, and she jumped up and ran down the aisle about five rows before seating herself in a new spot.  and all the while, she still had that crazy grin on her face.  back to Fat Guy.  the look on his face seemed to be one of pain.  he looked troubled.  the thought crossed my mind that perhaps a doctor had told him to walk a certain distance every nite to help his heart, but he couldn\\\'t walk the entire way, and so he would cheat by taking the bus.  as he sat there, maybe he was contemplating having to lie to his wife, telling her about the wonderful walk he had.  maybe i watch too many movies. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>a few stops later, Fat Guy gets up to get off.  again this seems to be a great trouble, and takes forever.  and to make matters worse, there is a large group of people waiting to get on, that he has to bust through.  anyways, he gets off, and this group gets on.  they are a bunch of students from spain (is my guess.  i\\\'m pretty sure that was spanish they were speaking, and they all had these name tag type things hanging around their necks). i think they are in town for world youth day activities.  so i\\\'m watching them get on the bus, and all of a sudden, she walks on.  this girl is drop dead gorgeous.  absolutely stunning.  she looks quite similar to PenÃ©lope Cruz.  i can\\\'t seem to take my eyes off her.  i know i am blatantly staring, and i don\\\'t mean to be, but i can not take my eyes off this girl.  she sees me looking at her, and knowing i\\\'m completely busted, i just gave her a big smile.  she just looked at me and sat down.  even sitting i kept glancing over at her.  i could not get over how attractive this girl was.  a little while later, the chord is pulled, and that group starts getting off. PenÃ©lope waits to get up until the end.  i was keeping my eye on her.  as she walked down the aisle she looked right at me, and gave me this huge, wonderful smile.  lips parted, teeth showing, coming through even in her eyes smile.  and then she turned and got off the bus.  that smile made my night.  i\\\'m still smiling now thinking about her.  damn she was pretty. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh yeah, walking the two blocks from the bus stop to my house, this woman hops out of a car at a stop sign, screaming \\\"stop that car,\\\" and jumps in front of this old, beat up station wagon making a left hand turn onto the street she was stopped on.  she runs over to the car, rips open the driver\\\'s door, and starts screaming into the car.  i turned and walked away.  i had had enough for one nite, and i didn\\\'t want anything pushing PenÃ©lope from my mind. </p>'),(2925,'	<table width=\\\"500\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n	<tr>\r\n		<td>Â </td>\r\n		<td>\r\n			<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></center>\r\n			<br><br>\r\n			<p>i\\\'m always looking for people.  i walk through the streets and i weave myself through crowds of faces i\\\'ve never before seen.  i try to take in as many faces as possible.  i look deeply at each one, body too if i have the chance.  i try to invent instant life histories for each person in the short pulse of time that they are in my eyes.  i never remember individual faces. i look at hundreds per day.  i don\\\'t really try to empathize, and i feel no overt good will on an individual level.  but after a while i start to feel a tide roll in.  a collection of all those experiences of a human, or at least enough of them to weigh heavy in my head.\r\n\r\n			<p>i start to see repetition in patterns of body types, and i get to know the different periods, all existing concurrently. and i wonder about the person, how much the body they were given has formed their experience.  i see bodies and faces as representations of the infinite tangents of experience that are everywhere just eleven stories below my feet.  the architecture of bones and skin expressing their distant history and current life together.\r\n\r\n			<p>.  i can\\\'t see stars in this city, even on clear nights.  so i have to settle for faces to relax in insignificance.\r\n\r\n			<p>i think that\\\'s why i like to be alone.  to feel insignificant.  i have many good friends. they make me feel significant, and i need that.  but i like to spend a lot of time alone.  i don\\\'t know that spending more and more time alone is good.  i do not want to hide.\r\n\r\n			<p>how many people look at me when i\\\'m out walking the streets?  how many look deeply, how many remember me?  who thinks of me?  who has fallen in love with me at first sight?  who has noticed that my eyes are green?  who has wanted to hurt me?  who tried to know me through just one look?\r\n			<br><br><br>\r\n\r\n			<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n		</td>\r\n		<td>Â </td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n	</table>'),(2926,'<p><img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\"></p>\r\n<p>He gets happier about little things like his train engineer\\\'s hat than I remember being about anything but him in the past 5 years.</p>\r\n<p>In his 3-year-old toddler speak, \\\"Star Wars: Attack of the Clones\\\" becomes an enthusiastic  <a href=\\\"http://www.infoblvd.net/flansbmp/wackin.wav\\\">\\\"Dar Wars: Wackin\\\' da Kwones\\\"</a> (an infinitely better title, if you ask me.)</p>\r\n<p>He doesn\\\'t get frustrated when the <a href=\\\"http://www.infoblvd.net/flansbmp/oobie.wav\\\">new kitten</a> won\\\'t play baseball with him. (\\\"Oobie can\\\'t fit the glove, Mommy.  I juss weed him my baseball book\\\")</p>\r\n<p>Even though there\\\'s no such thing as a \\\"Your daddy knocked up his new girlfriend a month before he left your mommy and neglected to tell anyone he was having another kid until 3 months ago, so you\\\'re going to be a big brother\\\" book to explain what is happening, he is incredibly proud of his new baby brother who is probably being born as I write this.  He\\\'s oblivious to any of the emotional baggage that makes this impending birth anything less than the joyous moment it should be for everyone involved. (I, on the other hand, am having a little trouble with this one.  But I\\\'m <a href=\\\"http://www.infoblvd.net/flansbmp/brudder.wav\\\">learning a lot</a> from him.)</p>\r\n<p>I guess that\\\'s enough reasons for today.</p>'),(2927,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2927_1.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\nThrough decades of inordinate early weekday mornings, the neighborhood coffee-vendor has learned to read her patrons through the practicality of their breakfast choices. The working class demarcates the corner in a convoluted line progressing towards her tiny steel cart, and she hands them colorful steaming cups of black coffee which make humid whiffs of jetting foodservice steam in the summer atmosphere. Interspersed between the jean-clad, the laborers, the students, are the suited folk with their entourage of briefcases and communication devices, the individuals who mumble orders of Lipton and croissant, Sonja the coffee-lady handing out discrete measures of tea-and-cream that trail teabag strings and paper flaps.<br><br>\r\nSome choose the <i>Local</i>, her non-menu specialty that exchanges a dollar for two cigarettes and a small cup of plain black coffee; you supply the matches; a likely breakfast choice for the masses who tread the dusky morning streets inhaling wakefulness for the commute, a truly appropriate pick for this city, ground zero for anxious energy, recitative of the burbling that fills hearts and injects a longing throb into infinitely languishing summer days, calendar squares that drip with anticipation and never pass quickly, despite how anxiously hard you hope to preclude another summer day alone.<br><br>\r\nYou have an old soul, yes, which sprouts latent knobby growth like the deciduous gingkoes that line my block near the coffee-cart. The impulse of my desire for you unfurls like ancient gingko leaves, slowly and thickly through these summer days. Evening winds ruffle gingko growth, flat and fluttery, finally leaving them still in the morning moments when I walk past, inert and waxy and thick, lushly tactile and quietly magnetic to my curious fingertips.<br><br>\r\nThe gingkoes grow ramrod-straight in a manner dictated by strict eons of trial-and-error, evolutionary experiences having left such trees nothing short of thriving, each in their own appropriate place and time, having finally found the perfect soil to mingle roots with, a nutrient-rich foundation seemlessly compacted around their slim tubular base. In the fleeting moments between when the sun dawns and the street-lights flicker off, the soily strata whispers <i>\\\"push your long, thin branches to the sky... I am here below you to hold them plumb. Transduce star-crossed photons into rooty stimuli and dig deep into me\\\"</i>.<br><br>\r\nYou remind me of the gingkoes, heart-wood protected from millennia, never changing. Whatever chafing, anxiously energetic hands twirled around your trunk before mine are inconsequential. The only sounds now are the wind between your leaves, the contented click of the birds in your forking arms, and an increasingly compelling tree-song that sighs <i>\\\"be my soil, let my roots convolute endlessly in your ageless depths\\\"</i>.'),(2928,'Two months, thirteen days, fifteen hours, thirty-four minutes, and, right now, \r\nforty-two seconds into my marriage. Tick tock. Tick tock. Tick tock... \r\n<p> <u>SOME THINGS I\\\'VE DONE SO FAR</u> (more than once)<b><font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\" size=\\\"2\\\">*</font></b> \r\n<p> Hung pictures<br>\r\n  Cooked dinner so she could clean<br>\r\n  Hugged and/or held hands<br>\r\n  Took out the trash<br>\r\n  Folded laundry<br>\r\n  Said, \\\"C\\\'mon... let\\\'s go.\\\" Opened/closed windows/blinds<br>\r\n  Oiled door hinges<br>\r\n  Went to crappy movies<br>\r\n  Had \\\"people\\\" over for dinner<br>\r\n  Got in a fight<br>\r\n  Threw a pillow <br>\r\n  Programmed the remote/VCR<br>\r\n  Lit a candle in the bathroom<br>\r\n  Took a picture of the pigeon farm Fetched the mail<br>\r\n  Bought an item that claimed it was \\\"Great for Couples\\\" <br>\r\n  Purchased batteries<br>\r\n  Watched MTV<br>\r\n  Met the neighbors and lied<br>\r\n  Asked, \\\"What the fuck is that smell?\\\" <br>\r\n  Parked in the wrong space<br>\r\n  Got yelled at for yelling<br>\r\n  Complained about hair on the floor<br>\r\n  Made waffles and fresh squeezed OJ<br>\r\n  Used the blender to shake my wife<br>\r\n  Planted flowers<br>\r\n  Ate a banana\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\" size=\\\"2\\\"><b>*</b>Random sampling of daily \\\"highlights\\\" \r\n  </font>'),(2929,'<font size=\\\"5\\\">\\\"W</font>hen are you going to hang the pictures?\\\" my wife asks, \r\nhanding me the hammer. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"We don\\\'t have any nails,\\\" I reply, quickly \r\n  placing the hammer on my desk. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This has been going on for a few weeks now. \r\n  She wants the pictures hung so it looks like we\\\'ve moved in and are \\\"living\\\", \r\n  but I\\\'m not sure where I want to hang them yet. I mean putting a hole in the \r\n  wall is a huge commitment. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I try to explain this to her with a series of \r\n  knocks on the wall and pointing to estimated areas of where one of our pictures \r\n  might hang. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"No, no. I think it should go higher,\\\" she says. \r\n  \\\"And a little to the left. No, higher.\\\" \r\n<p> <i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Knock, knock, knock. </i> \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Nope, that\\\'s not going to work,\\\" I say. \\\"A \r\n  major gas pipe runs right down this wall and I don\\\'t want to have to explain \r\n  to the authorities how it was all <u>your</u> fault.\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"A gas pipe, huh? You just don\\\'t want to do \r\n  it, do you?\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Honey, it\\\'s more complicated than that... you \r\n  can\\\'t just rush into putting holes into the wall. You have to find the wall\\\'s \r\n  center. The walls do possess the power of Zen you know... and damaging that \r\n  could fuck up the entire aura of this... I mean, <u>our</u> household.\\\" I take \r\n  a deep breath, trying not to breathe in any of my bullshit. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Just get some nails....\\\" \r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"> <b>*** </b> \r\n<p> A few days later, I finally get around to going to the hardware store down \r\n  the street. It\\\'s walking distance, and, on the way, there are plenty of other \r\n  stores planted there just to distract me. There\\\'s a used bookstore. I pick up \r\n  a nice looking, hardbound copy of E.E. Cummings\\\' <u>The Enormous Room</u>. That \r\n  took thirty minutes. Next, I go into the pet store. We don\\\'t even own any pets \r\n  but the wife and I are thinking about something fuzzy. I price everything in \r\n  there to make sure we can\\\'t afford it. I make up different scenarios for different \r\n  pets that we might get. \r\n<p> <i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Bird: cage, seed, book on how to take care \r\n  of a bird, a another bird so the first bird doesn\\\'t get lonely, special wing \r\n  clipping scissors. </i> \r\n<p> <i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Cat: food, kitty litter, box, toys, catnip, \r\n  scratch post, a bell around its neck. </i> \r\n<p> <i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Fish: tank, more fish, maintenance, cleaning, \r\n  what if it falls over and breaks? Birds shit all over the place and don\\\'t belong \r\n  in cages, and cats... we\\\'ve just got a new couch. A dog? Nah, they shit too, \r\n  and hump easily offended people. It never fails.</i> \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Across the street, another store catches my... \r\n  erm, eye. It\\\'s called \\\"The Psychic Eye\\\" and there\\\'s some white-haired wizard \r\n  standing out front holding a staff with a cheap, twenty-five watt light bulb \r\n  on top. It\\\'s flickering on and off, and, before I know it, the wizard is inviting \r\n  me over. \r\n<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I blink. I don\\\'t usually like stuff like this \r\n  but I\\\'m in one of those<i> throw caution to the wind</i> moods. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Free readings... first fifteen minutes,\\\" he \r\n  says, sweeping me in the store with his blue robe. \\\"Go... forth... Rabbit is \r\n  waiting to tell you things you need to know....\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;He points to a closed door. \r\n<p> <i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Rabbit?</i> I thought, <i>Who the fuck is \r\n  Rabbit? Is this Leonard in Wonderland?</i> \r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"> <b>*** </b> \r\n<p> Rabbit wasn\\\'t a rabbit in the animal sense of the word, but she did have funny \r\n  teeth and a few whiskers sprouting out from her cheeks. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"How does it work?\\\" I ask. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Simple,\\\" Rabbit says. \\\"See the cards on the \r\n  right? The square, triangle, squiggle and star? All ya have to do is choose \r\n  and I\\\'ll read yer mind. As soon as ya choose, pick another card and ya can try \r\n  again. Every time I get one wrong, the spirits distance themselves from the \r\n  room. So please concentrate.\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I\\\'m not sure if shit like this works but I like \r\n  stars so I concentrate on that card. Rabbit closes her eyes and begins breathing \r\n  in and out of her mouth like she\\\'s about to push out a supernatural baby. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;More than five minutes go by and she\\\'s still \r\n  laboring over the card I picked. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"You\\\'re not concentrating on the card,\\\" she \r\n  says, peeking an eye open. \\\"Focus on a card...\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I play along. <i>Rabbit, I\\\'m picking the star \r\n  card. Star card. Star card. Star,</i> I think to myself. <i>Star. Star. Star \r\n  Jones. Sometimes Tracy Morgan plays Star Jones on Saturday Night Live. This \r\n  Saturday we\\\'re going to my parent\\\'s house for dinner. I hope my mom doesn\\\'t \r\n  make salmon. She makes that too much. I think I\\\'m going to buy her a cookbook \r\n  for Christmas. Shit, on the news this morning they were already mentioning Christmas. \r\n  It\\\'s July for god\\\'s sake. It hasn\\\'t been as hot this summer like it was last \r\n  year. Last year at this time I wasn\\\'t married-- </i> \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"I\\\'m getting a vision... keep concentrating... \r\n  yes... yes, yes, that\\\'s it,\\\" she says, heavily breathing in and out. \\\"Yes... \r\n  you are on a quest. A lifelong quest for something small, but the small things \r\n  mean a lot to the people close to you. Do the small things to create happiness \r\n  in your life and others. You will be happy with the small things. Those around \r\n  you are waiting... waiting... waiting...\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Waiting for what? C\\\'mon... I didn\\\'t come in \r\n  here for the free reading to get the mystic run-around.\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Focus... on the task at hand.\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I look at my hands. I look at Rabbit and wonder \r\n  if she has someone to go home to every night. I wonder if she watches MTV and \r\n  sneezes and blows her nose just like my wife. I wonder if she hangs her towel \r\n  after a shower or leaves it on the floor. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"I\\\'m focusing... I can\\\'t see the task...\\\" I \r\n  start to want to believe. \r\n<p> \\\"Focus... there.\\\" She points through me. \\\"It\\\'s so close. It\\\'s around your \r\n  head. The spirits are strong in the room. Your aunt Martha is here. She standing \r\n  to your right... ask her something.\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I look over my shoulder and say, \\\"Martha... \r\n  why did you keep drinking?\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Rabbit breathes in, gyrates a bit and translates \r\n  Betty\\\'s reply, \\\"The past is the past and the future is now. And, you\\\'re uncle \r\n  Dave never did a goddamn thing around the house... drove me to drink. It was \r\n  either continuing to put the glass to my lips or right through his stinking \r\n  face.\\\" \r\n<p><i>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Fuck,</i> I think, <i>we just got new glasses.</i> \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Rabbit opened her eyes. \\\"She\\\'s gone...\\\" \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"Shit, I have to go buy nails.\\\" \r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"> <b>*** </b> \r\n<p> Later on that night, we eat dinner like we always do--on the floor, careful \r\n  not to spill on her family heirloom carpet. Except this time it\\\'s a little different. \r\n  Right above us, on the wall, are a few pictures that we crookedly hung together. \r\n<p> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\"If you sit like this,\\\" she says, tilting her \r\n  head and dipping her shoulders closer to the floor, \\\"they don\\\'t seem that off.\\\"'),(2930,'He has that nervous loose joint feeling one gets when really fatigued, and an awareness of some illness trying to find a home in his body thanks to the grainy communiques being sent to him every time his throat swallows. Another day has passed with no more progress made in finding the end of endurement. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThis bit of self awareness and progress evaluation is followed in quick succession by stories of explorers, adventurers, frontiersmen, and even a few conquerors played on fast forward through his mind. His self exploration is the only adventure he has been left with, an internal frontier whose conquest is the driving force behind his daily awakening. He can\\\'t help wondering if the people of these stories battled the same thoughts as he did.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHis internal world has become extremely desolate lacking infrastructure the more he has pushed on and mapped out. Old friends show up from time to time but they seem to be ethereal ghost walking by his side. Every time this happens he thinks of all the old stories about ghosts and how it takes great effort on their part to make themselves seen and heard. Even as ghosts they have saved him and guided him safely through some of the dangers of this internal frontier which has made him always thankful for their efforts.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere was a time once, before setting out on any exploration when all these friends and other people he would meet where solid and real in his mind but as time passed most disappeared completely and the remaining others became ethereal. For a long time he was driven mad trying to think of the reasons behind all this in some attempt to stop it from happening. Slowly overtime however, came adjustment and he would continue on in pursuit of his ambiguous conquest. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIt had been so long now though with all these ghost that he couldn\\\'t be sure that his own mind wasn\\\'t playing tricks on him. Imagine years with only ghost as company, ghost that you could only interact with through great effort on the part of the ghosts themselves. It had been so long he could no longer remember why he had set out on this internal voyage. When he queried the ghosts they all had different and disjointed answers.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nThis was why the sudden presence of a real material other caused such fear and alarm. When he walked through the wilderness of his internal landscape he could see the other dodging the trees and hear the noise of their footsteps, none of the ghosts did this they simply floated through everything. With awe and terror mixing in the pit of his being he cautiously watched. <br><br>\r\n\r\nSo deep within his own mind how could this other be materially present in this place inhabited only by ghosts for so long? A mindstorm of noise and broken thoughts erupted making it harder to track the other presence but the idea of material contact fueled pursuit. Dulled memories started smashing into him with a sudden flash and the force of a wrecking ball. Memories of pleasure and pain which he long thought had been rendered impotent, memories of life with others.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFighting through the storm of his own mind and straining to track the other he forgot to heed the distance and before really being aware of it he had almost caught up with the other. The storm and his longing had obfuscated reason and he fell to the ground when unexpectedly a searing heat and pain took root in his leg. <br><br>\r\n\r\nA booming \\\"Stay away from me\\\" was the last sign of the other as he stared transfixed at his leg wound. The moment the blood from his wound touched the ground he perceived a flickering in all of the internal landscape of his mind...<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen he awoke next, where he had been struck down it was immediately evident something had changed. He could see his part of his forearm disappearing into a small rock on the ground near where he lay as if it was phased out. His leg was still hurt but he could stand and walk. It took only seconds to realize what had happened. The landscape of his mind had become ethereal or alternatively he was a ghost in his own mind. Either way he was that much farther removed from material existence.<br><br>   '),(2931,'<p>i\\\'m visiting my hometown of menominee, michigan.. a small town in the upper penninsula which is as different from new york (where i live) as one can get. the main difference i find is all my friends here are married and/or with kids, and all my new york friends are single. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nrecently my one single girlfriend remaining here moved to detroit, so i was preparing for more downtime here without her. tonight one of my married with 2 kids friends and i were going to go get dinner and see a movie. the movie was to start at 715. since i\\\'d been away with my family all day i told her i\\\'d call her when i got back. i called her at 615 and was told that it wasn\\\'t enough time for her to drive here (half hour) and get to the movie theater (10 mins) for the movie. that didn\\\'t make sense to me but whatever. so i said what about a 9pm movie. can\\\'t do. too late. the girl is 24 and a 9pm movie is too late. don\\\'t get me wrong, i\\\'m glad she\\\'s happy. i\\\'m glad she\\\'s happily married and loves her kids and all. nothing against her. but by the end of our conversation i was craving some single people, or at least people without kids here. so i looked up my friend valinda. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nvalinda is the girl i spent all my nights when i lived here with going out until the wee hours, causing trouble and having fun with. i haven\\\'t really seen her in 2 years. last i knew she got married last year to a guy we\\\'d spent many years doing the trouble causing with. so i figured i\\\'d look her up and see if i could scrounge up just a little bit of young people fun tonight. i went to her house and the first thing i saw was the baby carriage. she just had hers last month. she was my last hope. the only chance left i had here to be young and have fun in this town anymore. boo. the end. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(2932,'<P>her: so you\\\'re like god\\\'s boy now, huh?<BR><BR>me: yeah, his little fuckboy. (giggle, giggle)<BR><BR>her: is it anyting like scientology?<BR><BR>me: no, you can still think.  your brain doesn\\\'t shut down. i swear sometimes i have these fears that i\\\'m being brainwashed, but if brainwashing feels this good, let it take me.<BR><BR>her: huh.<BR><BR>me: so what\\\'s the weather like over there?<BR><BR>her: it\\\'s cool. little bit of rain. some cumulonimbus clouds up there.<BR><BR>me: you know what those are? i have no idea what they look like. my woman is smart. just be glad you\\\'re not here. you\\\'d be miserable. last week it was like 95 degrees, humid as fuck.<BR><BR>her: that\\\'s florida.<BR><BR>me: that\\\'s florida. so you still don\\\'t know what it is?<BR><BR>her: no, they did another ultrasound, but i want to be surprised.<BR><BR>me: that\\\'s not gonna be the surprise. the surprise is if it\\\'s gonna be chocolate or vanilla.<BR><BR>her: either way, blood flows, baby.</P><P>anna called today, out of the blue. everything\\\'s still up in the air with her and eddie and me. we\\\'re not sure if we want to try to live in a tripartite union or let the baby\\\'s paternity decide who ends up with who. whom. it makes me cry when i think about it, so i try not to. they\\\'re in vaxjo visiting her parents, who are way cool about the whole thing. unlike some people around here....</P><P>i\\\'m working at what was once my church, then only my parents\\\' church, now our church again. i\\\'m a janitor. it\\\'s part of my therapy, and they wanted me to get a job. i\\\'m becoming a christian, trying to develop a christ consciousness. i consider christ an avatar of krishna, so it all fits into the big picture, baby. don\\\'t be scared. little mattie ain\\\'t turnin\\\' fundamentalist on ya.</P><P>and it\\\'s the sweetest thing. it seems like i run a little bit toward god, and he runs a lot toward me. i never knew that life could feel like this. that could also be the drugs - they\\\'ve got me on three different antidepressants. six of one, half dozen of the other. i just know now that life can taste this sweet, so i can find the feeling again if i ever lose it. i see it as my mission to change the nature of the baptist church in america. i\\\'m a spy in the house of love (tee hee).</P><P>i\\\'m also campaigning these days to try to get the city to leave the cemetary open all night. part of ashtanga yoga is considering yourself a god. i picked shiva, and he hangs around cemetaries at night sometimes. maybe on the off chance works in a good conjugal session with his consort kali. and isn\\\'t that why america is a great country, has the potential to be an even greater one? isn\\\'t that why all those people died in wars, so crazies could fuck in graveyards? people have rights, you know.</P><P>so, anyway, life is good these days. if i let it be.</P>'),(2933,'<IMG SRC=\\\"%%file1%%\\\"><BR>\r\n<p>I took a long time to clean the condo by the lake; it had been good to me.  I realized only too late that I had made a key to the door behind which the memory lay: rearranging the bottles on the tile that surrounded the bath, I moved one whose scent reminded me.  I had, quite unwittingly, conditioned myself.  I stared at the bed, and the shower, and the kitchen island, and the sofa, and the little black bookshelf, and the poster from La Scala.  I stood in the threshold too long.  And then I closed the door.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had to park a long way away last night: I parked south of Lawrence; I parked south of Weiss Memorial.  I walked down to get the Honda.  I walked past dozens of homeless people camping in the park.  I drove back, and stopped in the loading zone; I made quick work of my books and clothing.  LSD was crowded, and a haze lay over the skyline.  I was sad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tomorrow, as the next chapter begins, I will leave Chicago.  I am going down.  And I am not lily-White: south of the Ohio River, I get nervous.  The dark, Yankee stranger: that\\\'s me.  I have lived in Chicago - save for Wisconsin summers on my uncle\\\'s farm - my entire life.  The only other place in our Union that I have spent a significant amount of time is - strangely enough - Kentucky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They produce coal, tobacco, and bourbon in Kentucky.<br>\r\nThey raise horses in Kentucky.<br>\r\nThey have beautiful women in Kentucky.<br>\r\nThey handle snakes in Kentucky.<br>\r\nThey made Hunter S. Thompson in Kentucky; they made <a href=\\\"http://www.loc.gov/exhibits/gadd/gtran.html\\\">Abraham Lincoln</a> there too.<br>\r\nEmmylou Harris sang about Kentucky - but she was born in Alabama.<br>\r\nLoretta Lynn and <a href=\\\"http://users.cis.net/sammy/eyesblue.htm\\\">Crystal Gayle</a> really are sisters; and they really are from Kentucky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As a child I explored Kentucky\\\'s Mammoth Cave; it was well named.  And as a teen I worked in the Appalachia Service Project in southeastern Kentucky.  The members of my team slept on a dirt floor, in the basement of a school. The little boy who lived in the home next door had ringworm.  It was during that summer\\\'s work in the ASP that I had an encounter with a group of the local men.  They drank homemade from an old, clear glass jar of some kind.  They watched me work.  They decided that I was - in their words - \\\"Jewish looking.\\\"  After forming a circle around me, they told me so.  I feared the worst, and my face flushed with blood: I turned bright red.  That involuntary response convinced them that I was White.  Yes: a stereotype was fulfilled.  In Appalachia there are insiders and outsiders, members of the community and strangers.  But, that experience was really no different than the one that might be had in certain neighborhoods in Chicago.  The message is the same in either case: \\\"You don\\\'t belong here.\\\"  Some cities display their hatred openly; others maintain a veneer of civility.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is a difference between rural poverty and urban poverty.  Here, in Chicago, leisure and decay are roughly juxtaposed: the contrasts between rich and poor are heightened.  There, in Appalachia, rolling green hills dirty the feet of the barefoot children.  But, the experience of wanting is the same.  Most things seem to be that way: under the surface they really don\\\'t vary too much from place to place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My friend is getting married.  I\\\'m going down to attend.  We\\\'ve spent the past two years in grad school together; we\\\'re the ones who bastardize Nietzsche.  My friend is one chain-smoking son of a bitch; and he\\\'s a vegetarian.  It\\\'s ironic.  We talk about cities a lot; we also talk about the many and the few.  We believe Plato\\\'s Socrates offers an ironic teaching about cities - it\\\'s in that same book in which he suggests that it\\\'s preferable not to look at things directly.  Having been raised with my mother\\\'s Christian faith, I entered into a program of study that I learned to be pagan - and isn\\\'t that an ironic word?  There is so much irony: I am taking a trip for the sole purpose of participating in a religious ceremony for a person with no real faith, to a place where I previously traveled to perform Christian works - only to be menaced because I was not perceived to be a White Christian.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am leaving my city.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And tomorrow I am going to drink bourbon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>[Moce.]</p>'),(2934,'<P> So I am supposed to be studying bugs for my final tomorrowbut I took my migraine meds a tad too far from the food was consumed prior to cuckoo-bashing in my frontal lobe. Now rather I have this gurgling of acid washing everywhere within my abdominal cavity.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> So what is this about?</P>\r\n\r\n<p> pain.</P>\r\n<p> That bending into the tiny wart we all once were, twisting appendages into just blobs of mass and stuffing stuffing stuffing...</P>\r\n\r\n<P> If I didnt have a time limit I suppose I should follow my educational lusts and become an entymologist. Then again, maybe I wouldn\\\'t make such a great bug doctor. Here I am, writing this mono-meaningful monologue to no one in particular...when I should be studying. Hmmm, and then my loved one lost his pseudo-wedding ring/ just there because I love \\\"The Lord of the Rings\\\"...kinda ring. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> Is it dead?</P>\r\n<P> No.Not if we find here.</P>\r\n\r\n<P> I think...personally is dead.( the ringof course</P>\r\n<P> I can\\\'t sleep until i find what I am missing So  I cant sleep, I cant study...where is the ring? It isnt some sentimental factor that drives me towards such anal behavior, but more of this materialistic LACK OF kinda thing.Now if I couldn\\\'t sleep due to a lack of freedom, or of food to go around, or of political honesty...I suppose that would be much more noble. But I am just merely anal.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P> So its dead.And maybe I am always at a loss for words clean clothes, dishes, knives, books, money,good music, film. </P>\r\n\r\n<P> I  havent slept well in years</P>'),(2935,''),(2936,'<p>\r\ni have decided to be \\\'sauceless\\\' on swinney. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe school took the kids \\\'camping\\\' for three days and two nights this past week. let me tell you about that. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwell we left the school on a tuesday morning. it was early. not as early as you\\\'d think, but as my lifestyle in korea usually has me getting to sleep at around 5am and awaking at 2 or 3pm, waking up at 8am sucks, as getting to bed before 5am is as hard as rubix cube. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nit was pouring rain. actually there was a serious downpour happening. streets were quickly turning into rivers, and if you can think back to forest gump, all four types of rain existed all at once. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nme and shane splashed to work for the expected time of 9:30, weary eyed. As expected, we didnt leave at the proper time. the bus was supposed to leave at ten, but we ended up having to march up the street a ways to get on a big grayhound style bus. we left at around 11. i had tried to have a nap in one of the small school busses the school uses to drive kid too and from school, but kids kept coming upto me and yelling \\\"DAVE TEACHER!!!!\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe bus ride was loud and unorganized. the chiefs all counted the kids and kept counting the kids for about twenty minutes until all the chiefs had decided that all the kids were there. this marked the start of what i felt was going to be a very unorganized and frustrating \\\'camping\\\' trip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\napparently we were heading to an island, but as usual no one knew anything about what we were getting into. all that i knew was that there was airconditioning, rooms, and an indoor pool. thats it. thats all she wrote. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthrough the monsoon we drove. after about an hour we stopped at a rest area so that the kids could goto the bathroom and the adults could smoke cigarettes. I didnt have an umbrella so i sprinted from the bus to a sheltered area which smelled like rotting cabbage. after finishing my smoke, i grabbed one of the kids umbrellas and sprinted back to the bus with them scampering behind me yelling all sorts of korean things in my direction. it was all in good fun. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nabout an hour later we arrived at our final destination. im not sure where the \\\'island\\\' was, but we might have crossed a river...im really not too sure. I quickly found out that what we were doing with the kids was nowhere close to the definition of \\\'camping\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs with the beginning of the trip, getting into the hotel was as organized as my sock drawer. kids jumping and playing all about with the chiefs clammering about doing god knows what while me and the other teachers just sat on lumpy couches trying to figure out what all the chaos meant.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhearing that the rooms were on the fifth floor sounded worse than it is. 4 is about as lucky as 13 is in north america...so buildings jump from third to fifth floor. what a relief!\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ni was staying in a room with shane and another english teacher. his name is john. we had matts to sleep on and pillows we swore were packed with bricks. after cleaning all the kids rooms out of extra matts we had created a comfy bed three matts thick. it was still pouring. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe entire plan for this \\\'camping\\\' trip depended on bright skies and dry conditions. no plan b had even been thought of. the only answers we were getting from my boss were \\\'im finding that out now\\\' and \\\'ill have to go find out\\\'. it was classic. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe indoor pool we had been told so little about turned out to be a bus ride away. so after getting settled in the hotel, we all clammered back onto the bus and headed to the swimming pool. hahaha. when we got there the roof has mysteriously disappeared from the swimming area. the look on my bosses face said it all. the laughter inside me was hard to contain. needless to say, they swam anyway. i didnt. when asked why i didnt want to swim i just told them that i dont like swimming in pools much because kids pee in pools and that it was all too dirty for me. in all actuality i didnt really want to get in the pool because i have quite the belly and im hairy and i dont really feel like having my kids make fun of me. i put up with the fat jokes enough from my friends here...i dont need to hear them from my kids...especially since im so paranoid id think every thing they say in korea was about me and my hairy belly. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe day wore on, and there was a lot of nothing to do. the kids were having fun, but only because they didnt have parents around, and the chiefs didnt really know how to contain them. I just stood around and took it all in.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas the night fell, the kids settled into their rooms. i had a few beers. i had a few shots of soju. then me shane and john went to all the rooms and beat the kids up with pillows and basically woke them all up again. what madness. one room attempted a counter attack and i chased them down the hall and cornered them. what i didnt know was that the floor was covered in water and so when i tried to stop i landed right on my ass and slid a few feet getting so fucking wet in water that smelled like it had been trapped in the ballests of DAS BOAT!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter changing me john and shane got to work playing chess. john is a junior master or some shit so he basically kicked my ass every game. im not much good at chess. about a half hour into the games one of the bus drivers came in with more soju so i had a few drinks with him and got pretty toasty. so did he. ten minutes after he left the room one of the female teachers came in and looked kinda shaken. apparently the bus driver had gone into her room all drunk and put himself between two of the girls and was sleeping with his arms around two of them. this did not amuse us one bit. John, who is a body builder type guy went into her room and picked the guy up by his belt and told him in korean that no boys are allowed to sleep in the girls room. we threw him into one of the boys rooms and left it at that. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na word about the bus driver. he\\\'s about 28 or so and looks really nerdy. this isnt that big a deal here in korea as a lot of men look like complete nerds, but this guy looks like he still has his clothes put on him by his mother. he\\\'s also really touchy feely with the kids at school. we didnt really take much notice of it at the school because its nothing sexual...we just thought he was playing around with the kids, but apparently he is really touchy feely with the same girls he tried to sleep next too with his arms around. this kinda freaked us all out, and the next day we had a meeting with the boss...and guess what happened? NOTHING! he told us all sorts of shit about how he was going to talk to the guy, but he only had his brother and law talk to him and so that meant nothing was said. brother in law has no backbone and the bus driver is his friend. what a situation.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni finally got to bed at around 5am. shane and john had left to go find food, walking down the dark road dodging drizzle. I decided to just try and sleep. sleeping on the floor takes getting used to especially when you dont have a pillow. im sure i could have taken these pillows to a human rights tribunal. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni went without breakfast for the two mornings opting instead to sleep. man i love sleep. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe day began alright, and then we found out we were going to a beach. a mud beach. a beach of clean mud. what did that mean? i was so looking foward to seeing this. we got on the bus again and took the fifteen minute ride to the mud beach. when we got there we found out it wasnt exactly a beach...it was more like a mud pit where the kids could slip and slide their way around slinging mud at each other. fun for the kids. a bore for me. i just stood on a concrete barrier and watched them for a while. shane and john had gone down to see what all the fuss was about and they too ended up slinging mud around, throwing people into the mud and having a good time. i went back to where the bus was and hung out with the kindergarden assistant and talked to her while the kids slung mud around. they must have been in the pit for a good two hours. it was hot out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas it was the second last day, and last night of the trip, the night was to include roasting marshmellows at a campfire. the hotel was surrounded by trees and rice fields so i had thought they had a place to have a kicking bonfire, but as we found out walking towards the fire was that it was in a bus parking lot and they used gasoline to get the fire going. yep. might has well just put a few sticks in an oil drum. me, shane, and john opted to goto the pc bong and play call to power II for the night. fuck the parking lot fire. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the last day i slept while the kids swam and watched a magic show. the trip was over and i couldnt have been more happy. we were around so much nature and all they had planned were things that could have been done in seoul. the mud pit thing could have been a day thing. it didnt seem like a camping trip at all. needless to say, i had fun off and on during the trip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\njust down the road from the hotel was a lot where somebody had carved all sorts of cool things out of wood and put them on display. it was really cool. i took almost two rolls of film. i hope the pictures turn out. in addition to pictures of some pretty cool carvings, i got a couple of shots of some of koreas biggest spiders. holy mother of god these things were big. scared the living shit out of me. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom co.kr this is chiamattt signing out. '),(2937,'in santa cruz this morning, bumming because of huge questions in my life about direction and future, i talked to yeti on chat.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nme: hey\r\n<BR>\r\nye: W00J!\r\n<BR>\r\nme: what are you doing today?\r\n<BR>\r\nye: Some errands!  How about you?\r\n<BR>\r\nme: coming to sf\r\n<BR>\r\nye: Great!\r\n<BR>\r\nme: i guess i\\\'ll leave now\r\n<BR>\r\nye: And come here!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand just when i was entering sf, on guererro off the 280 San Jose Ave exit, im at a light at 26th and G.  and LO, there is king buzzo of the melvin\\\'s Hair flopping in the wind.  and LO, there is King Buzzo of the Melvins, only my favorite band of all time.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni called yeti.  i called my brother.  \\\"I just saw my second superstar of the west coast!  First Ron Jeremy and now King Buzzo!\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhours later, an entire day, i am alone at yeti\\\'s computer after a long conversation with egg about the week.  Here.  Nestled in the stack of CDs at the corner of the desk is a rare copy of the Melvin\\\'s Lysol stamped [ORIGINAL ISSUE] with the following note:\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\nMelvins-S/t.  Orignially called \\\"LYSOL,\\\" BUT DUE TO INFRINGEMENTS CHANED.  ORIGINALL ISSUE WITH THE TITLE \\\"LYSOL\\\" SCRIBBLED OVER ON THE SPINE, AND COVERED ON FRONT COVER W/ STICKER.\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>i am cranking it waiting for his ass to get back from berkley so i can lock him up in the sensory deprivation chamber that i have constructed with the help of his two cats.\r\n</P>\r\n<CENTER><IMG SRC=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"></CENTER>\r\n<P>p.s. elizibutt milkcan spent the day in a sweat lodge with the indians.  she has wompum.</P>'),(2938,'<P>\r\nLately, Iâ€™ve been preoccupied with unsatisfying love affairs. I find that in the moments of self-effacement that follow their death I come to these suffering gems of wisdom that are worth the bitter diminishing hope in all the plumb-fucking. I call it that because I feel like I dive each time into the black myrrh of humanity, I come up with some kind of phantasmagoric ore worth sharing. Today I translate the sounds of the flesh for you that fill the space between us. \r\n</P><P>\r\nShe is hemming her jeans with loose pink thread, folding the cuffs over to hide the stitches, while I sit there in plain silence. What had I done to find myself here? I think of her eyes and the flowery babydoll dress she wore at the picnic where I met her. It was a moment that happened not to long ago. A moment that already passed, under a warm sun, with children busying about, and souring pasta salad and melting candy in a wicker basket. We both felt compellingly drunk on the mystery, the possibility of searching through one anotherâ€™s souls to find a union. It couldâ€™ve been only temporary, fleeting, or something that could be greater than both of us. \r\n</P><P>\r\nNow as I sit on her couch with her nephew close by I wait for words to come and save me from this time between words. Words to destroy the distance that exists between us.  Words to unravel the mystery of who we are.  I am waiting to find the voice to spill myself in front of her.  However, this is the point where if I reveal myself through words, all other barriers of mystery will collapse into similes that she will associate between me and her past lovers. I hate appearing so obvious or so ill-defined, unless it is on my terms, and as a narrator Iâ€™m allowed some creative leverage to even out the wrong-doings in life. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI am applying for the role of the fool. I shift positions on the couch and she does hers. Again, we are doing the pre-linguistic rituals that are setting things up. I fall in love with her like I fall in love with books, and old buildings -- it is just that easy for me. But, is the love fleeting or is there some truth and permanence in this fool?  I can tell she wants a relationship in which she can remove herself from me.  Itâ€™s as if in my gaze she can dangle herself like a pretty and untouchable object. I admit that my attraction to women goes as deep as their clothes. Women do not deal in words as much as they do in soliciting their bodies. It is a manâ€™s world written from his point-of-view, and I admit that a huge element of intrigue for me is waiting to see what she will look like under her clothes. I think that is when women reveal themselves to men. They donâ€™t do it in words those belong to us.  The mystery is distilled through their bodies that they think are vulgar and unlovable. The myth was conceived by us.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWe begin to make our big noise. When we discuss things and fill the space between us with words we hide our true intentions behind expression. \r\n</P><P>\r\n â€œI know how you are. You are the sensitive type. I can just tell by the way you look at me. I bet youâ€™ll masturbate about me after I leave. I bet you want to.â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œI think we all need distractions, we need amusement. Do you really think that Iâ€™m confusing life with fantasy?â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œRemember when I told you that it was nice to cuddle with someone last night. It\\\'s like you don\\\'t even listen.â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nIâ€™m sorry for omitting that part before, but I donâ€™t care for her half-boyfriend.  She told me all about him when we first discussed things before this last conversation. That was when we first revealed our ugly selves to one another. Her relationship had soured soon after her lover left for the city. He found music and she found herself out of place with a lust-less fleeting romance turned friendship. Why I ended up falling for a scorned women is something that I should know better about, but again like old churches and good books I am a fool for beauty. I fall for the allure of the object, to see whatâ€™s under her jeans. I try to convince her about how she should feel, so I litter my speech with invitations to forget him. I try to put together a winning argument by comparing her situation to one I similarly went through. That is why she is on my couch now and her half-boyfriend is not.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œAnd now youâ€™re here because you want something that isnâ€™t just a ghost you can chase. You know you live in a time-before. Iâ€™ve seen many people do it.  I came out here for a girl myself, and she got pregnant and I was forced to move on.â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œAre you implying that my relationship is dead?â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe isnâ€™t sure whether she sees a future with him or a future in him. She is walking on stilts with me quavering beneath trying to see her pantyline, however, I know her arguments are only something to prop herself up with, and she knows that she too has made some assumptions about life that have failed before. She hates to see the truth. She is here on my couch, her music career isnâ€™t blossoming, and she is a janitor by day. She lives with her half-sister, sleeping in the same bed because she doesnâ€™t have a room for herself.\r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œYou must think I am so desperate or something.â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nEarlier she told me what it was like for her in high school, how little confidence she had. In high school everyone feels hopelessly ugly. But, when I looked at her I only saw a beautiful expressive woman, not some undefined girl. However, people like her, like me, are perpetually recovering from our adolescence because we cannot completely reconcile being unloved by ourselves. That is why I am doomed to play the fool and she is doomed to be with a man who doesnâ€™t love her. That is what I wanted to see, a beautiful hurt girl because there is something beautiful in human misery. It has always compelled me to make myself and others feel just a bit more adjusted to the fucked-up-ness of life.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe are both more at ease when we talk about our families because we feel that their love seems unchanging. I remember how she carefully described them all. Her parents divorced when she was young and her mother now lives with a woman who is dying from throat cancer. Her father ruined her family by going off with another woman, and I think that maybe she would love herself more and be more definite about how she felt about everything if somehow she could reconcile this early childhood event. She is born from her fatherâ€™s adulterous rib, and this makes her prone to confused feelings of detachment and longing for permanence in her relationships.  Even familial love is not unconditional because our parents can fall-out-of-love with each other.  She now has only half-mothers and a half-brother and half-sister.  Maybe I will become her half-lover.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI remember that it was the Greeks who severed the spirit from matter, but I find that the two are linked. I watch my own body move towards hers, to hold it and console and adore it, to claim it as my own. A hand to the breast, cupping it, tender explosions of flesh searing under a tense spiral white light of spirit. Something in me awakens, itâ€™s like waves that push me towards her and I cannot reason with it nor stop myself. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe fool is bound to folly even though he knows better. I can feel a burning connection but, of what sort. Is it just my eyes finding the pretty untouchable object, trying to uncover its mystery? My eyes reason her as like a still apollonian figure, full of grace, harmony, and restraint but without arms to fight back with. What will happen when I remove the clothes and find out that my Venus is hurt worse then me, more vulnerable then before, that she is scarred or stretch-marked? I have a hard time distinguishing between her flesh and her soul. However, even in the fullness of that moment I think about my life and returning alone to my own half-ness and sheathing myself under the covers of my half-bed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe letter and words turn away from me shrugging great shoulders of indifference.  I slide my hand along her thigh, I beckon to the words, those distillations that I cannot live without. But they slip and transpose under their own alliteration; two tongues entwined time after time.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSo dedicatedly have I tried to personify those atomic bits: Jen, Jen J-E-N. They are all fragmentation from the whole and she is severed into bits. She is at once a breast, a brow, and a hand that I traced with my own thin fingers. I call for those moments of lucidity and await the perfect language to ensnare her with. But, like the creases on my couch she dissipates.\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe is gone home for the night.\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(2939,'<P>\r\nHe was self-conscious of his hands when marking papers. Hands: eight fingers, two thumbsâ€” all mucked up from marking. He read the papers with his fingers like they were written in brail. I suppose, he wasnâ€™t really reading, so much as enframing. Sitting at his desk hutch hunched over scanning with right pointer and right thumb touching left pointer, while right index touches right thumb making a crude rectangle view-finder to isolate the words. Why such an endless array of order and statistics? There had to be a pattern that could be given meaning. Heâ€™d look over his hands and make finger prints on fresh white pages.  When his sinuses bothered him heâ€™d raise his glasses, press down on the bridge of his nose pointer and thumb (right side), unknowingly leaving black prints that were covered up by his glasses.\r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€˜Iâ€™m a newspaper man,â€™ was the first, and not by any means last, lie, that he told the fresh-faced brunette stewardess whoâ€™d later become his wife.  So he stretched the truth to impress the woman. She had pert breasts that made him lift his eyebrows suggestively when she reached over to give him his air-tight, compressed package of peanuts. He made $9.00 per hour to read the papers. The technical title read, â€œnewspaper monitorâ€ for a small media operator that required him to scan and photocopy articles that contained naval keywords like fisheries, ports, marine stocks etc. Why not consider himself a newspaper man?, since his hands were bloody well stained by the ink each day, she can make her own conclusions. Sometimes people will make assumptions based on a small corollary of facts.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe newspaper man and his wife kept separate rooms.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHis hands were soft from the variety of lotions that he kept at work. The newsprint stained his skin, so heâ€™d scrub with tough soap mixed with grains of pumice stone, not a soft lanolin soap which admirably absorbs into the skin, softening it. All this work to remove the black ink that worked its way into the crevices and contours. After washing, his hands were left cracked and dry, so out came the lotion, which would make them supple enough to be the soft, warn hands of an old man. \r\n</P><P>\r\n One day he found a protuberance on the back of his left hand, that he scratched at inconsistent intervals.  \r\nâ€˜A bed bug bite.â€™ Conjecture on his part, but a good guess. As he scratched his protuberance swelled, becoming an annoyed white, with a concentric pink ring wrapping around its circumference.  His skin  dried from the scratching. Out came the aloe Vera.\r\n</P><P>\r\nFor days the conditioned worsened, until he feared the worst. The lump could be a pimple or wart that he picked up from that ruddy child of his cousinâ€™s making. To the newspaper man she was always oozing puss and acne; her skin an exploded minefield, or a crater-full moon. Well she actually only had one large pimple, ripe and erect, on her forehead when he visited, and he was forever left with an impression of uncleanliness. \r\n</P><P>\r\nHe asked his wife to remove it.  She opened him up with a clean slice of a disinfected exacto-knife.  He winced at the sight of blood. It was an autonomous response on his part. He was unconscious of the inner workings of his nerve pathways. She pulled out the contents of his bump with tweezers. What came out was made of cartilage, tightly compacted. His wife left the room in a hurry to gather herself, as her thought was disorderly, because of his exposed pink insides. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe innards of the body are not welcomed in most polite company.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe newspaperman played with the byproduct of his former bump, which was healing quickly. He realized that the cartilage, when played with, could unfold. When he was finished picking at the fleshy bits it opened up to ten centimeters in diameter. It was octagonal and its intricate pattern symmetrical. \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œIt looks like a snowflake,â€ he said. A rather large and fleshy one, but it did have the characteristics of a snowflake that a child might create by folding paper in half and then quarters and then cutting a delicate pattern for its design. He carelessly tossed it in his drawer, on old parking tickets and unpaid phone bills. \r\n</P><P>\r\n The next week at work was busy. A fishing vessel crashed into a portâ€™s docking lanes killing all the crew, who went down with the ship and its cargo. \r\n</P><P>\r\nSeamen to Sleep with the Fishes\r\n</P><P>\r\nTabloids do have a flare with words, he quietly muttered. His hands looked more gaunt and translucent than usual. He thought he might use a lanolin rather than the pumice filled-soap, that day.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe fixed his fingers in their viewfinder shape, and he went deliberately through the next articles enframing each keyword that he could see. \r\n</P><P>\r\nâ€œThe latest re[port] went by without a rebuttal from his Wor[ship], who is the committee whip  con[trolling] the de[bate]. The p[ark]s board hopes for a similar rollar[coast]er  ride for their  proposal to takeover the lus[h arbor]etum and its adjoining hiking trails, all [lands man]aged by the native band.â€\r\n</P><P>\r\nNeedless to say that his work suffered from momentary concentration lapses, which his boss did not appreciate.  He unconsciously wrung his hands and cracked his knuckles. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe newspapermanâ€™s mind was now occupied by another biological rumbling. He felt a sharp, jarring pain in his handâ€” the one that had the successful snowflake extraction. He clutched it with the other hand and recoiled them both to his chest to compress the pain into a tight ball. â€œA heart attackâ€ was his own thoughtful conjecture, but it could have been a brain aneurysm, or simply a muscle spasm. at the point of pain he always thought the worst. \r\n</P><P>\r\nHe was 35 years-old and an eater of fried foods.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHis lip quivered like a child about to cry. His hands motioned towards his chest to feel for a broken heart, but instead they had come upon another lump.  A large festering lump that protruded like an angry nipple.  Why hadnâ€™t he noticed it before? \r\n</P><P>\r\nHis eyes suffered from a indiscriminate myopia. He could only see what was directly in front of him, and most of the time it was his hands, whether enframing words or typing reports, he had lost the periphery to a narrow rectangle. \r\n</P><P>\r\nHis wife was out of town so he went to perform the extraction himself. She was seldom home, as she took  any opportunity to fly away from her landlocked marriage. The newspaperman kept his wife in a rectangle, similar to the one used at work. She cheated on him in Vancouver, Denver, and Montreux. She smelled the mountain air, she blessed the high altitude and fell into bed after bed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe exactoknife: a slice roughly comparable to the one made by his wife. A pair of tweezers for extraction. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThis time he slowly pulled out a hard, slippery white bulb, which sucked the skin around it as it made its way through the fine opening. Then another popped out and another, until he had forty in all. These bulbs were spherical and were attached to a string.  As he refocused his eyes, there were tears everywhere, he saw through the glassy pane of his cornea, a string of diaphanous pearls, that were unquestionably real, and not cartilage like the snowflake.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nOut of the corner of his eye he spotted a pendant of two silver wings on the nightstand. He thought about her pert breasts â€” a rise in his temperature, a tightly compressed pack of peanuts.\r\n</P><P>\r\nA gift of pearls to show her his affection. Yes. A very intimate example of how much he cared. Wellâ€¦ better than anything he could buy her with his small wage as a newspaper man. He thought that maybe she would live in the one bedroom, rather than the two that they separated themselves into. He liked this idea. He gave her the pearls when she came back from Pompeii. \r\n</P><P>\r\nShe asked if he stole the pearls, or what he was trying to do. He just beamed magically, his eyes matching the gleam of pearls, she flushed and reached out for his hand to unbutton her blouse. There was a lull in her schedule, and he made love like he was a real newspaperman.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAt work he felt rejuvenated. He shed his fleshy rectangle and led a risky life of freedom, reading words, then sentences, then paragraphs then articles. â€”A context emerged. His boss sent him glowing  memos and even took him out to dinner. They had drinks and called one another by their first names. At home his wife laid in bed with him and mentioned that they should fill the other bedroom with another soul. And most importantly, he now only used a mild lanolin soap.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOne day he noticed her breasts sagging. His broad hopes had deflated after their first child, his sexuality cooled to all time seasonal low. [Submerged] Those breasts had been weightless in the skies, but domesticityâ€™s gravitational pull dragged them downward. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe newspaper manâ€™s wife searched for desperate means to reawaken their long-slumbering marriage. She went for his testicles remembering the compressed peanuts she handed him many flights ago. When she did this bold gesture, he yelped in pain. Her move was so sudden that he could not respond to the stimulus with tact, his first thought was pain and then an automated Yelp! like a helpless puppy whose tail had been stepped on. She did not appreciate the his canine noise nor what she felt.\r\n</P><P>\r\nA sizeable lump, slightly larger then the other two testicles. A middler that parted the other two. Poor conjecture would say it was a phalanx of balls preparing to overtake the phallic overlord. \r\n</P><P>\r\nShe became worried and thought the worst. He then became worried and his lip autonomously quivered like it had when he discovered the second lump. On a behavioral level the pain-stimulus reacted this way: The hand cupping the third testicle, whose sensitivity to pressure sent a signal to the central nervous system, which relayed a synaptic charge that controlled the muscles in the mouth, including the tongue to make an involuntary gesture of disobedienceâ€” YELP! All three of his balls autonomously retracted during this progression of events.  Everything else he did was his brain cognizing. His fear, brought on by the external tension of his wifeâ€™s thought, caused his distress; another event, related but unique: the quivering lip, was really involuntary muscle twitches, as low level electrical charges fired inside his central nervous system, while his cognizing brain struggled to comprehend the possibility of death.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe went to the hospital and found that he lost most of his hair and his middler, when he returned home. He no longer washed his hands, which were becoming diaphanous like the pearls of his wifeâ€™s necklace. It was a strange side-effect of the therapy that led him to think that his time was passing. Before, when he could work, he would stare at his hands, blackened from newsprint, and scrub them to make them fresh and clean. The repetition of action made it seem like he was in a stasis. But ever since he dropped his fleshy rectangles, he noticed change. \r\n</P><P>\r\nHis hands were fragile and glowed so bright that they hurt his eyes. He felt pain, he considered his own finitude, and then he did what would be unthinkable before. He deliberately smashed his hands against the wall of the hospital room he occupied, not with an angry expression on his face, but with a thoughtful look, as if he might be testing out a theory. His hands  shattered into innumerable shards of porcelain   scattered on the floor. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe doctor called him a few months later to pay him a visit. His hair had almost grown back now, at least in patches, and he began to feel restless after being bedridden for so many months.  The doctor arrived at his bedside so suddenly that he had no time to fix his hair. Without hands, no amount of time could have helped him to get ready, but he like the others, would always love to gain a few minutes, if not a lifetime back. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe doctor didnâ€™t say a single word. He just opened his palms to show his patient what treasure he had recovered. The patientâ€™s eyes were expressionless, disinterested in the findings. He slid open the patientâ€™s drawer and placed his treasure inside, where a brittle, shriveled, snowflake once sat amongst unpaid bills and parking tickets.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n End.  \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2940,'<p>Inexplicably, Iâ€™ll surf the net and do reverse look-ups on phone numbers I used to have.  Odd to be in one place and wonder who belongs to the same digits that were yours five years prior.  It makes me feel as if the â€˜sâ€™ has dropped out of cosmic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lately, Iâ€™ve been remembering Rowena street and that cozy little house where I used to stay.  I remember Brando, the dog next door, and wonder why I never learned his ownersâ€™ names.  I remember Shiner Bocks and Winterson and Nabokov and The Chron on the living room floor never even missing my excess furniture or the television.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember the Ginger Man and a few pitchers of Anchor Steam Porter.  I remember the most unlikely sorority girl God ever made falling asleep in my lap, waking in time to hear me whispering to Computer Mike that I could die like thatâ€¦with her placid breathing and my hand in her hair.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember Maggie Maeâ€™s and the pitch black pints we used to sip with chocolates melting in our mouths and the skinheads I met at the Black Cat.  I remember Martian and Sasha and the twenty dollar tab I ran up and have yet to pay.  I keep thinking thereâ€™s a way to make that right.  Bad Black Cat karma simply canâ€™t be a good thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember interminable nights, agonizing over copy, electrified by caffeine at Insomnia and Little City and Ruta Maya (do these places still even exist?).   I remember turning vegetarian at Magnolia and learning to swing dance at the Continental.  I remember black beans and rice at Les Amis and having tapas at Louis 101 with a Canadian ichthyologist who didnâ€™t know me from Adam and chatted me up on the #7 Duval.  I donâ€™t remember where either of us was going.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember strange nights on the roofs of houses and of buildings and stranger days working at the Texas State Senate and Texas Monthly Magazine, attempting to make myself respectable.  I remember screwing it up.  A girl from Poughkeepsie fell in love with me shortly thereafter, and I remember screwing that up too.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember my parents driving down to visit one afternoon and meeting Kristi who intercepted them at their nice, conservative, Bible-beltmobile sporting pink hair, spiked leather dog collar, and a safety pin through her freshly pierced septum.  My father didnâ€™t know what to think, but my mother liked her instantly.  She found her to be exceptionally kind, and she\\\'s a much better judge of character than he.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Recently, a friend wrote to me, enclosing all the scoop on SXSW and asking if it made me homesick.  I confessed that it had, but that it wasnâ€™t necessarily a bad thingâ€¦like falling safely from a great height.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>She wanted to know if Iâ€™d ever missed Dallas with such intensityâ€¦</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I lived in Dallas?</p>\r\n'),(2941,'<p>I really thought he loved me.  I thought we were meant to be together, as though it had been handed down in some irrefutable edict from the Universe.  He was smart.  He was cute.  He was funny.  He was quirky.  He didn\\\'t believe in the status quo.  He came down from The Great White North to be with me.  We fought so many battles, such impossible odds to be together...skeptical family and friends, our respective self-doubts, INS.  Why are we broken?</p><p>Two and a half years of cohabitation and nearly two and a half years of marriage later, and here I sit, looking at a life become twisted and grotesque, as if seeing everything reflected in a funhouse mirror</p><p>He wants me around, running always in the background, like a virus-protection program on one of the computers that give him so much solace.  But he doesn\\\'t want me, no contact of skin on skin, of flesh made one; even kisses and hugs make him squirm with the discomfort of an eight-year-old boy.  I have found, too late, that the organic aspects of relationship displease and discomfit him greatly.  He appreciates my intellect, he claims, but my passions, my emotions, are to loud, to raw, to primitive for his delicate, logical, cerebral sensibilities.</p><p>He claims to eschew the status quo, to have no respect for the usual niceties, and for the traditional social contracts made between human beings...and yet, I am some sort of trophy.  Some untouchable treasure, like a pretty, poisonous snake that can only be looked at under glass, but never touched, or a porcelain doll, expensive and too fragile to ever be handled much in the physical sense.  He likes to take me down off my shelf, though, and play \\\'let\\\'s pretend\\\' whenever there are parental units or work friends to impress.  He knows I\\\'m valuable and impressive to other people, even though he can\\\'t quite figure out what to do with me.  And so he trots me out when he deems the situation to require it, and plays the doting husband.  And when the audience has gone home, he puts me back up on my shelf and closes the glass door.  Many times he shouts at me, like a child who grows frustrated and bored with a toy but cannot figure out yet how to occupy himself.</p><p>And so I sit upon my shelf and dream of the day when I can find a way down.'),(2942,'<img src=\\\"%%img01%%\\\">\r\n<p>I woke up in North Carolina; the maid was knocking on the door.  Four of us had been in the hotel room last night; now, I appeared to be alone.  It was 11:45 in the morning; checkout was at noon.  I was still drunk.  There was a terrible odor in the place.  I made my way to the bathroom.  The towel rack was bent, broken off the wall, and lying on the floor.  The towels were on the floor too; the floor was quite wet.  T\\\'s striped underpants and green shirt were mixed in with the towels.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I found an empty bag.  I stuffed it full of the detritus that covered the floor and furniture: the torn plastic and paper, the clothing, the bananas; it all went in the bag, and out in the trash.  I bunched up the towels and tried to wipe the floor clean and dry.  I re-mounted the towel rack - as best I could.  I made the beds.  The room looked pretty good; I still looked like Hell.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The maid opened the door.  I stood and stared at her: wearing nothing but my black underwear, it didn\\\'t seem like the time to talk.  She said that she\\\'d come back; nice lady, I thought.  I hopped in the shower, broke out my Lush soap, and did what I could do in five minutes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remembered J\\\'s reassuring voice.  J said something to me before the group left.  I wonder if it was important; it\\\'s hard to tell: everything that comes out of J\\\'s mouth begins with the same several words: \\\"Paul Germanos, I tell you what...\\\" Maybe, once in a blue moon, it\\\'s something else - something like: \\\"Paul, I tell ya...\\\" J is good people - one of West Virginia\\\'s finest.  I\\\'d kill for J.  But my dueling pistol remained unused on this excursion - despite the fact that our honor was offended.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The shower helped; applying mirrored glasses to my face helped more.  I went down to settle my account; that done, I hit the road.  As I drove I noticed that the terrible odor followed me.  I scratched my chin as I pondered its origin.  It was at that moment that I discovered - horror - that the fingers on my right hand were the source of the malaria.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The second time that I passed out I had been trying to push the chunks down past the stopper in the sink; it just wouldn\\\'t drain.  It was the Devil\\\'s own sink stopper and it wouldn\\\'t lift up, or pull out; it just sat there mocking me and holding everything in.  I was forced to bail.  The dinner was a lovely affair; it had been a decade since I ate beef.  I felt sorry for the cow; but I still ate it.  I wondered how much nutrition my body had been able to draw from the meal; it was obvious that not everything was digested.  I think that the salmon contributed more to the terrible odor than did the beef.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Things had gone well when we had nothing but wine; but then the beer came out.  And then N shared his secret: the Chairman Mao Watch.  His head had turned into something like a weather balloon; it was connected by a great length of sewing thread to the rest of his body.  As he spoke, I found my head floating away too.  And so we were on the same plane: head to head.  He explained that he had mailed money to the woman that he knew in China - he had just gotten back, himself.  He expected that the government would take most of the money; but enough would be left for her to purchase and ship several Chairman Mao Watches.  And then - this is the key - once people saw the watches, they would want more and more of them.  He laughed maniacally; but I believed him: it all made perfect sense - at the time.  After all, he was wearing some sort of silk gi.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I do believe that N was the first one to buy a round of Pabst Blue Ribbon at the bar; I\\\'m still not sure where the bar was - but I do remember that they played Metallica.  The mystery woman had positioned herself and her lady friend in a conspicuous place.  That wasn\\\'t really necessary: she had the most lovely ink in her arms - left and right - from her wrist to her shoulder.  Flawless ivory skin and a thick head of raven black hair - in combination with the art in her flesh - made her impossible to ignore.  She said that she had the work done in San Francisco.  She asked if I liked to party.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>None of my credit cards were missing; my cash was gone.  I had roughly six dollars left - counting change.  I knew that I needed three and three-quarter dollars in toll-money to escape via the West Virginia Turnpike.  The cruel irony of the situation was that I was forced to conduct the business of accounting in the parking lot of a Waffle House.  If I bought a waffle, I couldn\\\'t take the Turnpike.  I cried.  I wanted a waffle; even more so, I wanted coffee.  Life is cruel: I left for Chicago - sans waffle.  There were other options, of course, but none that occurred to me at that moment: the scope of my vision had narrowed.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Verily: I left more than I took.  I did things that I had promised to myself I would never do again - things that I had not done in a decade or so.  My time in Kentucky and North Carolina was a bit of time in purgatory.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ten hours into the return trip I stumbled across a Doo-Wap station on the radio.  I liked what they were playing: it was slow and soulful.  What I wanted - for myself - was someone to hold close: her hand on my shoulder, my hand on her hip, and our two hands clasped together.  I wanted her just like that: slow-dancing together, alone, at night, across the kitchen floor, as our radio played that Doo-Wap music.  If she wasn\\\'t \\\"The One\\\" that was ok: that wasn\\\'t a part of this vision.  After the purge I just wanted something and someone - real.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chicago is real.  I made it a point to re-enter the city from the south side - the place where I came into the world.  Up the Skyway from Indiana, I took in my city\\\'s lovely stink and sprawl.  Damn: she looked good.  I had a soft spot in my heart for Louisville\\\'s bridgework over the Ohio - especially when lit at night with those blue-purple floodlights - but nothing else that I saw on the trip really turned my eye or made my heart wander.  I was Chicago\\\'s boy.  I still am.  I always will be.</p>'),(2943,'<p>I did a sweat lodge when I was in high school.  It was in these peoples\\\' backyard at their house in the foothills.  These weren\\\'t Native Americans, they were middle aged hippies. </p>\r\n<p>My friend Tiff and me, nekkid, crammed up in this blanketed dome with several others.  It was pitch black and hot as hell.  humid too.  I guess that\\\'s the point. it\\\'s like blanching some broccoli, except it\\\'s you.  The red hot rocks kept coming in, offering a hint of light, and a bath of steam, one after another.  It got so hot and wet it was just barely breathable air.  A claustrophobic wouldnâ€™t have a chance.</p>\r\n<p>I remember my HNAMDGF (hippy-new-age-modern-dancer-girlfriend), Marika (huh, aus), persuading me to go.  I was uneasy about the whole thing.  Afterwards, I was glad I did it.  I will never do it again :).  Itâ€™s funny what people will do to remind them that they are alive and that they have senses.  From falling off a skateboard, to skydiving, to body piercing.  Then, there is taking a ritual from an indigenous culture and imitating it for whatever reason (I guess body piercing falls under that category).  People are funny because weâ€™ll think up just about anything to try.  I am guilty too.</p>      \r\n'),(2944,'detroit wishes me well, i know this deep down. she wants me to return to her streets, populate her structures and interact with her denizens. she wants me to take notice of the way she works, to remember what it is to live and play within her. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve been here now 4 days and even the simple act of weaving in and out of rush hour traffic (no, i have no place to go yet... i simply enjoy being a part of the mass again) makes me feel wonderful and assured. i miss the dirt, the grime, the heat, the attitude and most of all i miss the comfort i find in knowing <i>this is where i come from, this is where i might belong</i>. in ways i don\\\'t understand i let loose all the jealousy, the angst and the ill feelings at mere sights of familiarity. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i throw away the ideas of \\\'what-if\\\' and just embrace the concept of \\\'what-is\\\'.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>today in traffic i observed people, some wearing white collars, some blue with stains and some wearing no collars at all. i watched how they acted within their environments, within their space and marveled how most of them looked moderately content. en route to destinations home, work, friends houses, dinner - life. i thought about smiling at a few of them when our eyes met but thought better of it, instead i opted for the nod. the nod of respect, the nod of silent expression. the nod indicating \\\'i know you\\\' or in a way \\\'i am you\\\'. silent agreement that we are what we are and that whatever that may be at this point in time - we\\\'re fine with it because its all we have. in this city and its surrounding areas, i am happy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want so badly for this to be real, i want prospective employers to see this enthusiasm and wonder how this could translate into my working for them. i have but 4 days left. tomorrow means more faxes, more letters of introduction and more calls to be made. i worry about the mental fall if nothing comes of this trip and i return on saturday with nothing to show for my effort. i don\\\'t want life to revert back to its regularly scheduled programming and i don\\\'t think i could stand it much longer if that be the case. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>so, here i sit. once again i\\\'m just a son in his mothers house, a small home on the west side, connected to the net by reliable yet still very stolen ghetto-bandwidth connection. clicking away in the dark, finished with my work for the day and about to head upstairs to read. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am 9 minutes into my 30th year on this planet, i would love for it to be the most significant day in my history thus far - <i>the day i could be certain that i am returning to form, that i am returning home. </i>\r\n'),(2945,'<p>\r\nso this is it eh? no explanation why you arent speaking with me other than \\\'[you] cant handle it\\\'?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i got to korea i was coming to terms with what was going on in my head. I figured being single for a long time wasnt so bad. I figured that not being affectionate towards a female would be alright. it seemed, to me at least, that a man could go through life without having someone close. someone to care about on all those levels that men and women share. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthen i met you and your big brown eyes. when you spoke i stared at those beautiful lips and listened intently, at attention, to your wonderful voice. i dug the short hair, your round cheeks and sexy laugh. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI cant help but think the reasons for you not communicating with me right now are my fault. perhaps its just easier to blame myself than you, but regardless of whose fault it is, i have thought about the things ive said and done and i have been honest about everything. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the past couple of weeks i have listened and asked for advice on a range of subjects, including suggestions on why things didnt work out. Questions regarding how and why i lost you, and with all their input i cant help but feel frustrated with myself.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\na majority of the input i got regarding \\\'us\\\' was directed towards my attitudes regarding sex. they felt that sex was something that connected people more than anything in a relationship, and while they are probably right, im still my own boss and i feel as though i was justified. However i also feel as though i was not clear on the matter. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits not that i want to go through life without sex. this is not true. I just dont want to have sex right away, with just anyone. From what i have gathered from my friends, peers, coworkers, the media, and print, most men try to have sex as fast with a girl as possible. Perhaps this is because they have the desires and wants that sex creates in a man. the instinct/urge to have sex is a great thing.(i guess)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm twenty-five and id rather get to know someone and test the water\\\'s of the pool before diving right into the deep end. Thats why there was no pressure, and thats why I came off sounding \\\'too nice\\\'. If being \\\'too nice\\\' is a drawback then i guess things are messed up in the world. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI guess im old fashioned in wanting to care about the person more while having sex then caring about sex more than the person. Listening to other western teachers talk about sex, i hear all the bragging and insensitive tones and attitudes about sex and i feel as though they are beasts. Yet i also find that some women too want to be treated with a somewhat barbarian demenour, and i refuse to adjust to that level. if thats what you want then you were right to let me go. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor me, life is about compromise and diplomatic procedure. I wont pressure anyone into anything they dont want to do. I may have violent and critical thoughts about people and life, but as long as they remain thoughts, i will continue to be kind to the people i meet on an everyday basis. I love humanity, the world, and cats. I am optimistic of life in the long term, though the short term is awefully hard to comprehend right now. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin addition to the things mentioned earlier, perhaps the main reasons for being smitten with you were your worldly aspirations. You have style and a unique personality most korean women dont have. More than independence, you have the travel bug, and you open your eyes to other cultures instead of letting them pass you by. Unlike most koreans, you dont hate the japanese. Having an open mind is important in life, and this makes you a very beautiful person in my eyes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnevertheless, i must respect your desire to remain silent, and i must respect that desire without letting the fear and anger build up inside me. Be that as it may, regardless of the reasons for your silence, i will have a hard time forgetting about you, and the fun things we did, and im sorry it has to be like this.\r\n</p>\r\n \r\n'),(2946,'in a few days i will be 25. <p>\r\nit seems, these days, that my hometown is sort of drying up. it\\\'s strange to imagine a town <i>contracting</i>, at least it\\\'s strange for me, educated man, used to the sounds of progress, everything getting larger, louder, faster. except cellphones and laptops.\r\n<p>\r\nDillon, South Carolina. Had the distinction of being along a railroad, with a station. At one time, it was a center of agricultural trade, tobacco and cotton. The downtown area was growing. Five or six major churches, five or six schools, segregated by color. The first time I ever heard about that was in the sixth grade, when I asked a teacher why there was so much worn-smooth broken glass in the playground dirt. He said that it had been thrown at the school during something called &quot;desegregation&quot;, but he didn\\\'t want to go into it. The school with the glass in the playgorund, of course, had been black. The black high school. Can you imagine that? <i>The black high school.</i> Such words flowed effortlessly off of the lips of my ancestors, only about fifteen years before my birth in 1977. Maybe only ten. \r\n<p>\r\nMy girlfriend asked me the other day, <i>what do they teach you down here about the civil war?</i> That was pretty funny. She\\\'s from PA. Of course they teach us the same thing that they teach kids everywhere--among other reasons, because our state is too poor to write its own revisionist history textbooks. But I had never looked at it that way before, that they might teach us something different. Like we\\\'re German kids learning about WWII or something. Jeez. \r\n<p>\r\nNow Dillon\\\'s population continues to grow...poor people have lots of kids, and Dillon is no stranger to welfare mothers. Although, from what I have learned about inner-city areas, we don\\\'t have it so bad...there weren\\\'t so many pregnant girls at my high school, maybe three or four out of about 1000. The pregnant ones may not have been in school though. The more well-to-do continue to leave, and especially their children. Me. The only place to have a legitimate business or job is in Florence, 30 miles away, and a lot of people make the commute. To our hometown sensibilities, however, this is unnatural. 30 minutes seems like a long time to be in the car every day.\r\n<p>\r\nWhen Ike\\\'s Interstate was going through SC, it made a little dip to go through Dillon, courtesy of Alan Schafer, my best friend\\\'s great-uncle, who owned South of the Border, which some of you may be familiar with. He used his influence to determine the course of I-95. Later, he went to prison for buying votes. He is remembered more for going to prison. He died a year ago or so. The interstate, unfortunately, has not made Dillon a hotspot of commerce as was once predicted.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Dillon of my childhood, through the lens of memory, is a vibrant place, and the grownups are young and good-looking, and the cars are new. Now, everytime I go back, there is something else missing. It is an interesting parallel to my own loss of childhood memories; for everything I forget, something actually <i>is gone</i>... The parades are gone, the Festival on the Square with music and clogging competitions and scones made by Mormons I think...they don\\\'t have that anymore. They don\\\'t have me anymore. The lines of the questionable neighborhoods creep ever closer to my mother, my grandparents, as houses fall into disrepair, as their owners realize that the once proud structures look pretty pathetic these days, and maybe aren\\\'t worth fixing up after all.\r\n'),(2947,'<p>My trip home last week was exciting on many levels.  My sister wanted to come home for a night while I was there so on Thursday I drove to London to pick her up.  It was a great little roadtrip.  The way there consisted of me singing along to Modest Mouse songs while playing 160 Leapfrog with a BMW sporting Michigan plates.  160 Leapfrog was the name I made up for the game wherein 160km/h was the speed necessary for one of us to actually pass the other, althouh I usually took it up to around 170-180 just to add that little bit extra.  Anyone caught doing less than 130 in the pasing lane was quickly muscled out of there by the two of us.  It was fun and as I took my exit off the highway, I waved to him while he continued onwards.  I love excessively speeding on the 402.</p>\r\n<p>That night I went to Sean\\\'s place for some fire and lake swimming.  We built a fire on the beach.  Not only was it a semi-private beach but it was also a weeknight so we were completely alone on the beach.</p>\r\n<p>In one hand I carried a bottle of Stella Artois, three medium-sized pieces of split log in the other.  A leftover cigar from the previous night dangled pointedly from my slack mouth.  We walked down the stairs to the beach and the fact that each step was angled back slightly made it feel like I was falling backwards everytime I placed my foot on a new step.  With an uneven load on each arm it was a satisfactory test of one\\\'s ability to catch oneself while falling forward.</p>\r\n<p>Our fire was a nice one that took quickly and evenly.  I made a comment about my father\\\'s fire building skills (which I had witnessed the previous night) and his technique of squirting varsol on the fire until something wooden actually stays lit or, as I like to call it, varsolling the shit out of the fire.  Sean went to grab his bottle of meade and change into his \\\"trunks\\\" while I sat on the beach alone, tending to the fire and enjoying my view.</p>\r\n<p>We talked about a great many things.  Sean and I hadn\\\'t actually hung out since the day in April when we saw Panic Room and did the used bookstore thing before he left Toronto to go home for the summer.  In other words, we had a lot of material to cover.  My favourite part of the conversation was when I brought up something which has been on my mind lately, namely whether there has been one person who has been so influential in your life that you can pinpoint a lot of what you have become on that lone person.  I shared my thoughts on this and Sean asked me if I could pick out the happiest moment in my life.  I tried to think about it but I wasn\\\'t really coming up with anything.  I said that it was very easy to pick out the worst moments but the happiest were not really coming to me.  His thought was that you\\\'re always just expecting it to just be around the corner.  He offered a moment of perfection he had experienced and I shared a similar story.  It was good talking.</p>\r\n<p>When we eventually decided to go swimming it was probably 1am.  It had been cool the past few days and the lake wasn\\\'t as warm as it had been recently.  It wasn\\\'t cold however, the air was cooler than the water so that when a small wave rolled up your legs and the air hit the exposed wet flesh, you felt it fairly quickly and sharply.  It wasn\\\'t bad once you were actually in the water with just your head bobbing above the surface, though.  While we were still walking out, with the water below our knees, some knowledge was imparted to me:</p>\r\n<p>\\\"When you\\\'re in the water, keep moving.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Okay, why?\\\"  I was thinking that perhaps it was cooler than I thought and the movement was just to keep warm.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Well, we\\\'ve got those fishes that like to bite.  We didn\\\'t get the huge bug crop this year and they\\\'re hungry.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Oh, right.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>We swam out to where touching bottom meant your head was under the surface and floated around and tread water for a bit.  When we began to make our way back I thought I felt something like seaweed on my ankle and when I realised that there wouldn\\\'t be any where we were located, I felt a little nip on my ankle.  I also had another one bite me when we were walking out of the water.</p>\r\n<p>Overall my trip home was a success.  Two nights weren\\\'t enough, though.  I hadn\\\'t been home since February and I ceratainly didn\\\'t get to partake in any midnight swimming under a full moon in my parents pool on that trip.  One of those moments of perfection was also experienced while at Sean\\\'s place: sitting around the fire, the fire drying us of and sipping on a glass of meade.</p>\r\n<p>Yes.</p>'),(2948,'<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni called my mother on sunday, for a recipe. i wanted to ask her how to make macaroni and cheese.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nmacaroni and cheese. because, for some reason, as i got out of the shower that afternoon, i had a craving.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut, what i didn\\\'t want to do was just melt cheese over macaroni. i didn\\\'t want to do that. i wanted to make it for real. i wanted to make it the way my mom used to make it.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nso i talked to her on the phone, and she told me what i needed. she said it was easy, that all it takes is butter, macaroni, cheese, and milk.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut i told her, \\\"no, that\\\'s not what i want.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni said, \\\"i know that\\\'s how you use to make it for me back home but, that\\\'s not what i want. how did you make it way back when, when i was little? that\\\'s how i want to make it. like that.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nback when i was little, my mom\\\'s macaroni and cheese was bitter and sharp. it was white and not orange, and it was not salty or sweet. my mom\\\'s macaroni and cheese looked nothing like the macaroni and cheese that was shown in commercials or that was eaten at friends houses.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nback when i was little, my mom told me that her macaroni and cheese was her own creation. i remember her telling me that, unlike the way her grandmother would make it, she didn\\\'t like to put it in the oven because, she said, when you put macaroni in the oven, it turns hard and gets all crunchy.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n\\\"so,\\\" my mother told me, \\\"even though this is my grandmother\\\'s recipe, it has my own spin on it. so, i guess, that kinda makes it <i>my</i> recipe.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut my mother told me on sunday that i probably shouldn\\\'t bother with that recipe. she said that it was far too hard, and that it involved flour and milk and constant attention. she said that, for me, the easier way was probably the better way.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n\\\"besides,\\\" she said, \\\"you never used to like that white sauce anyways. you always used to say it was too bitter.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand she laughed, \\\"i remember! you always used to ask me why it didn\\\'t taste like velveeta!\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nyesterday, malinda was already home when i got there. already in bed, already asleep. softly and quietly, with the covers pulled and wrapped around her. she was already gone.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand when i walked in, shutting the door and seeing her, i quickly crawled in and joined her. erasing the day with that immediate heat of hers.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand we napped. in bed, with the television on-- with the windows open and the city pouring through-- we napped, cuddled up all warm. me with my head in my hand, watching her eyes shut sleeping. with her mouth slightly open, her occasional heavy breathing.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand eventually, i slid out of bed. eventually, i slid out of bed and walked barefooted to the refrigerator, taking out that big metal pot and putting it on the stove. eventually warming it up, trying to keep the noise of the stirring spoon to a minimum.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand eventually, i slid back into bed. eventually, with bowls newly reheated in hand.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand she says, waking up, \\\"i love mac and cheese!\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand i say, smiling, \\\"good!\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni say, \\\"this is left over from yesterday. for some reason, i really had a craving for it, so i called my mom up and asked her for her recipe.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n\\\"but, when i went to the store, i ended up buying vanilla milk by mistake; i ended up having to use extra butter and a lot more cheese to try and make the sauce creamier.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand she says, \\\"well, it tastes pretty good.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand i say, \\\"well, thanks.\\\"</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni say, \\\"i guess, even though it\\\'s my mom\\\'s recipe, it has my own spin on it, you know?</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n\\\"i guess, that kinda makes it <i>my</i> recipe.\\\"</p>'),(2949,'<p>\r\n7/26/02\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I was 13, half way through my 9th grade year, I got my\r\nfirst magazine job. Art Intern. One of my tasks was to work with this\r\neditor named Rita Aero. I don\\\'t mind typing that here because, as I\r\nfound out later, it\\\'s not even her real name. I got the job through Janet,\r\nmy mom\\\'s old best friend, who bought \\\'ludes from the same dentist as Rita\r\nin the late 70s. I had to teach Rita how to use her Mac and she taught me\r\nsome editing basics (proofreader\\\'s marks, line editing, etc). She had an\r\noffice fridge under her desk, inside of which was always a large half\r\nempty bottle of Cuervo and nothing else.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nTomorrow, I\\\'ll be 23. That gives me 10 years of publishing experience. How\r\nstrange. 10 years seems like a long time. Last night I had an age anxiety\r\ndream. I don\\\'t think I\\\'ve ever had one before. I\\\'ve had plenty of other\r\nkinds of anxiety dreams. Once I had one about my El Camino. I won\\\'t go   \r\ninto detail here but it was so intense that I woke up and biarfed. Weird  \r\nweird. Anyway, I\\\'m at my desk wondering if I\\\'ll get a birthday toast today\r\nat 3. That\\\'s usually the practice here at Dance Magazine. Once someone got\r\nJell-o shots. Last year, I got taken to a nice lunch. I\\\'m also thinking\r\nabout all the other magazines I\\\'ve worked for. Like a ten year  \r\nretrospective or something. At one magazine, I learned paste-up when I\r\nfirst started. Waxy glue and rollers and pencils and stuff. Then we phased\r\nit out and started sending digital files. In\r\nPagemaker and an early version of Quark. I kept the big white paste-up  \r\nsheets with the blue lines on them. Ralph draws comics on them now. At   \r\nthat same magazine, our production manager kept changing his name and also\r\nhad a collection of rotting fruit on his window sill. Once our shipping   \r\nmanager and our circulation manager got into a fist fight in the mail\r\nroom. At another job, two of our bookkeepers skipped with thousands of \r\ndollars of embezelled cashola. I remember being kind of jealous.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow, after a mostly painful but not entirely useless year and a\r\nhalf at Dance Magazine, I\\\'m getting ready to move on and start something\r\nnew. What a long year and a half. I feel old and grumpy. Grr. Well, that\\\'s\r\nnot entirely true. I feel old and grumpy from the hours of 9:30 to 5:30,  \r\nM-F. Otherwise, I\\\'m pretty alright. The other day I registered a <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.whoknew-magazine.com\\\">domain\r\nname for a fake magazine</a> so that a coworker of mine could interview an\r\nactor whose pants she wants to get into. I put up a fake masthead and a\r\nfake blurb about what the magazine intends to do. It got me wondering\r\nthough if i shouldn\\\'t rewrite an actual for reals blurb and try and make\r\nanother new magazine. hmm.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBefore I leave you, let\\\'s go through the gossip updates from the past few\r\nmonths at dance magazine. Hairy Man, our managing editor got fired for\r\nbeing a shlump. His affair freaked out. Then she calmed down. Then my mom\r\nstepped in as managing editor for two months. Now there\\\'s a new dude. I\r\nhave been waffling between calling him The Dude and Goatee. The new\r\nclassified ad sales rep is friends with my tattoo artist in New York.\r\nThat\\\'s cute. Katie, the girl who took over for my office crush, is now\r\nliving in the same warehouse as my girlfriend. There are like a million\r\ncute interns but this is their last week. Ah well. Perhaps the art dept.\r\nwill actually be able to concentrate.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore TK!\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(2950,'<p>\r\nOf late I\\\'ve been pondering life and society in general.  Generally I\\\'m pretty resentful of the way things are in this day and age.. the hate, the crime, the stupid ideals, the awful corporate organisations that control so much, the lack of ethics, and lack of respect for all living things.  Sometimes I just want to get away, away somewhere that I can appreciate the wonders of nature, and of life - that there is still something pure and simple and good to hold on to.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is why I\\\'ve made the decision to move to a remote beach, west of Auckland, New Zealand.  It could possibly be the most beautiful place in the world.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo I\\\'m living alone, in a small one bedroom cottage on an organic herb farm.  There is no telephone or electricity.  The house is completely solar powered and there\\\'s a potbelly stove for cooking, or gas if I desire.  Even the toilet is completely environmentally friendly.  The cottage sits at the top of a valley and is surrounded by rainforest, and looks out over the wild west coast ocean.  God this feels good!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy neighbours (and not in the traditional city right over the fence sense, these neighbours are people who live in the community) are also of like mind, they enjoy the serenity of being away from the city, they enjoy caring for the environment, and they look out for each other and help each other out if necessary.  Fortunately enough, they\\\'re all artists and musicians, so no doubt I will have a lot of creative people to talk to.  I also won\\\'t bother them with my songwriting, deep into the night.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo I guess I\\\'m stepping back a hundred years, to a time when clarity and simplicity reigned!  All I need to do is wait until I get back from Burning Man, and then I move right on in there.  I just hope living in this paradise will not make me resent the city even more.. as many people I love live there.  I don\\\'t want to develop some kind of phobia!!\r\n</p>'),(2951,'<P>\r\nSpindlechair sits at his desk.  A box within a box within a box.  He blacked out from something that left a bad taste in his mouth and woke up here at 10 a.m.  At some point between arriving at Last Rites, he arrived here on time.  People are working all around him in the rack of boxes.\r\n</p><P>\r\nComing to consciousness in odd places...\r\n</p><P>\r\nWhen born into this world as an adult, memory seems genetic.  A song playing, half way through.\r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\ni know we should probably be apart\r\n<BR>\r\nbut i cant always accept it in my heart\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nHe stares deep into the concept of 17th century abyss that left him wondering since ENG 341b Literature of the Enlightenment with Professor Barnow.</p>\r\n<P>\\\"What is this about the abyss that is mentioned so often,\\\" asked young Spindlechair.</p>\r\n<P>Dr. Barnow replied, \\\"What?\\\"'),(2952,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/2952_2.jpg\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>Introduction:</b><br>\r\nAlendronate, a third-generation bisphosphonate used in treating osteoporosis and more recently proposed for the treatment of osteogenesis imperfecta (OI), is known to reduce bone turnover by inhibiting osteoclast activity. Accordingly, there is a concern that fractures that occur during alendronate therapy will not heal normally. Our previous study using image analysis to evalaute bone density and callus area showed that alendronate did not significantly delay radiological remodeling of the callus in either the +/+ or the oim/oim mice, and that it may actually stabilize fractures at an earlier timepoint. In this study, we used Fourier transform infrared imaging spectroscopy (FT-IRIS) to investigate the effect of alendronate on bone quality by evaluating ultrastructural parameters of bone mineralization during fracture repair.<br><br>\r\n<b>Methods:</b>\r\n30 B6C3FE oim/oim mice and 20 B6C3FE +/+ wild type mice six weeks of age were used under an IACUC-approved protocol.  The +/+ and oim/oim were randomly allocated into 4 and 6 groups respectively with n = 5 in each. Alendronate was administered via a subcutaneously implanted Alzet pump (Alza Corp., Palo Alto, CA) at a dosage of 26 &#61549;g alendronate/kg/day to 3 groups of oim/oim and 2 groups of +/+. The other 5 groups served as control and received an equal volume of normal saline.  At 2 weeks into treatment, the right femora were fractured via a modification of the rat femoral fracture model.  An intramedullary pin (25 gauge) was inserted retrograde followed by creation of an open osteotomy of the midshaft of the femur producing a transverse fracture of the femur.  After fracture, the mice continued to receive alendronate or saline until they were euthanized.  Oim/oim mice were euthanized at 3, 4, and 6 weeks post-fracture and +/+ mice were euthanized at 3 and 4 weeks post-fracture.      \r\nDissected femora were fixed in 70% alcohol immediately after the sacrifice followed by embedding in PMMA. Sections  3 microns thick along the long axis of the bone were cut using a Reichart-Jung sliding microtome and tungsten carbide knife and then evaluated by FT-IRIS. \r\nData was acquired on a BioRad (Cambridge, MA) UMA 300A FTIR microscope with an FTS-60A step-scanning FTIR spectrometer and a 64x64 MCT FPA detector (Stingray Imaging Spectrometer) at 16 cm-1 resolution under N2 purge from a 400 x 400 Âµm2 region of the histological section, resulting in 4096 individual infrared spectra per region. Infrared vibrations of the mineral (biological apatite) and the matrix phases were monitored.  The ratio of the area of the apatite phosphate absorbance (900-1200 cm-1) to the area of the protein absorbance (1590-1720 cm-1 ) was calculated to obtain the relative amounts of mineral and matrix present (mineral/matrix), an indicator of tissue density.  The ratio of the area of the carbonate absorbance (840-890 cm-1 ) to the phosphate absorbance was calculated to obtain the relative amount of carbonate in the mineral phase (carbonate/mineral), an indicator of mineral maturity.<br><br>\r\n<b>Results:</b>\r\nFor the +/+ mice, the mineral/matrix was about higher in the alendronate group compared to the saline group at 3 weeks and 4 weeks respectively.  The carbonate/mineral in the alendronate group +/+ group was lower than that in the saline group at 3 weeks and 4 weeks respectively.  For the oim/oim mice, the alendronate group had a higher mineral/matrix than that of the saline group at 3 weeks. At 4 weeks and 6 weeks this was reversed and the alendronate group had a lower mineral/matrix ratio.  The carbonate/mineral in the alendronate oim/oim group was lower than that in the saline group at both week 3 and week 6 (p = 0.009), while there were no differences at week 4. At both 3 weeks and 4 weeks, the carbonate/mineral was lower in the oim/oim compared to the +/+ mice. At 6 weeks, the carbonate/mineral and the mineral/matrix were reduced in the alendronate oim/oim compared to the saline oim/oim mice.<br><br>\r\n<b>Discussion:</b>\r\nPreviously, based on radiographic analysis, we reported that the fracture healing of oim/oim mice lags behind the wild type mice [1]. Interestingly, in both +/+ and oim/oim mice, alendronate treatment resulted in larger and denser calluses at earlier time points compared to their saline-treated counterparts. However, remodeling did proceed to the pre-fracture state in all groups.The mineral/matrix is an indicator of tissue mineralization and the carbonate/mineral is an indicator of mineral maturity. Based on these ultrastructural analyses, there appears to be more mineral in the alendronate-treated +/+ mice than in the saline-treated +/+ mice.  However, the carbonate/mineral ratios indicate that this mineral may be less mature, and therefore could potentially result in biomechanically less competent bone.  \r\nIn the oim/oim mice, those treated with alendronate were more mineralized at week 3 than the group treated with saline.  This mineral was less mature in the alendronate group than in the saline group at week 3, however.  At week 6, the saline group had greater mineral than the alendronate group, and the mineral was also more mature, as shown by the difference in carbonate/mineral ratios between the two groups.  These data indicate that at the early stage of fracture healing, alendronate appears to help the mineralization of bone, even though the increased mineral may be less mature. This trend disappeared as healing progressed.\r\nOverall, this study suggests that alendronate may accelerate the mineralization of bone at an early stage of fracture healing, but the quality of bone seems less mature. In addition, the mineralization and mineral maturity of trabecular bone in fracture healing appears to be different between the oim/oim and the +/+ mice, perhaps as a result of the underlying pathology in the oim/oim mice.<br><br>\r\n<b>Reference:</b>\r\n1. Yang, X, et al. Effect of Alendronate on Fracture Healing in Osteogenesis Imperfecta and Wild Type Mice. 2001: 47th ORS annual meeting\r\n \r\n \r\n'),(2953,'<p>for some reason i keep shoveling in the mike and ikes.  probably because it\\\'s saturday, and i don\\\'t have to think about carbs or electrolytes or ginko or guarana at the moment.  my running shoes are in the closet until monday, and my strength training will limit itself to moving the 27-inch tv from the living room into the back so we can spend all sunday watching old movies in bed with the oscillating fan creating little breezes of relief.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>elz hates it when i eat mike and ikes. she knows everything about everything (really) and even more about what you should do to keep your body from collapsing on itself due to misuse and neglect.  she always wrinkles her nose when she hears them rattling around in that movie-sized mega-box ,sniffing the air as it becomes rife with the fakest fruit aromas the lord (or whoever) never intended. she likens their shape to thermophile bacillae, and it\\\'s easy to see her point.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>it means she cares about me.  i know this. i like it. tomorrow it means elz, me, all the old black and whites we can stomach, and mike and ikes until i start to feel queasy.  the very smell of them will probably turn her a bilious green, but i\\\'m hoping she\\\'ll get past it and kiss me anyway.</p>'),(2954,'<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">\r\n<p>I had been sitting in the living room of G\\\'s apartment while he gave tattoos to fellow skinheads.  G was a good draftsman and printmaker.  But, he hadn\\\'t owned his gun for any significant amount of time.  The guys that he was working on wanted really big - full back - tattoos.  G was learning as he went; and it showed.  The work that he was doing that particular night consisted of a major touchup to something that he had previously done.  Those guys had a great tolerance for pain - and his growing skill.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>G and I were buddies.  I had given him a piece of ash to make a handle for his axe: he had welded together a huge - and impractical - double bladed Gothic horror.  It was a prop; he was the shaman of his tribe.  G and his buddies were one step further removed than I was; or, more precisely, they were one more step in another direction.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was never really quite certain who was who in that scene: some people were racist; some people were anti-racist; and some people pretended to be something other than that which they really were.  A few guys had really crappy AMF bikes - more props.  Hair grew back; but the tattoos were supposed to be real.  One of my sisters wears my flyer\\\'s jacket now; my leather hangs in the closet - dusty.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The marks on our bodies, the clothing that we wear, and the pose that we strike: these things are supposed to reveal our affiliations.  I chuckle now as I sit at the bar and listen to the scoffing at the manner in which other patrons dress.  I have been told that Chicago lags behind the coasts and Europe in fashion, as in almost everything else.  The students from London always called Chicago a, \\\"cow town.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It seems as though there are two popular styles here, now: 1) the neo-hippy, almost childlike, rave look; and 2) the underdressed, neo-grunge, rock look.  Both flavors come with piercing and tattoos; take your pick.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Even the underground has its conventions: the sub-cultures that thrive upon their opposition to the dominant culture have their own mores.  It\\\'s a saw: there is conformity in rebellion - more so now than ever.  All hail Elvis and television.  All hail the evolving monoculture.  If most people want it, it must be right: that\\\'s what democracy is all about.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s an odd sort of generation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The physical separation that used to promote regional - and to an extent: individual - differences has been made almost irrelevant as a result of certain technological improvements.  Particular brands are recognized as having juice around the world.  The people who can afford those brands and technology, can be cool.  This too is a saw.  But it drives those who seek to differentiate themselves from the herd into ever more extreme activities.  We\\\'ve embraced the arcane.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>G finished at the school that we attended together and got a fine arts degree; I left.  G was working as a carpenter the last time I heard from him; I worked as a carpenter; the guy who taught foundry at that school worked as a carpenter too.  Most carpenters never go to school.  Most carpenters labor in anonymity.  The people who praise the people who labor in anonymity usually try to garner some sort of reputation for themselves - here in Chicago and elsewhere too; or so I have been told.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wonder, sometimes, who\\\'s really inside and who\\\'s really outside - and how you can tell.</p>'),(2955,'<p>i\\\'m tired of feeling this way and i cannot forget the butterflies that floated as ashes, black into the air and over verdent fields.</p>\r\n<p>it is the middle of summer and it hurts to go outside because the sunlight burns into your eyes from the white concrete. </p>\r\n<p>and inside i am sitting and hating me. </p>'),(2956,'<P><B><I>\\\"...there\\\'s something about a sunday that makes a body feel alone....\\\"</B></I></P><P>i can\\\'t believe it\\\'s august again. already. i asked my grandma if time ever slows back down, and she said, \\\"no, it just gets worse. just you wait. you\\\'ll see. and you won\\\'t have to wait long, either.\\\"</P><P>i take a look around at my life and ask myself how did i get here.  what am i? what am i doing? i\\\'m a systems engineer who scrubs toilets for a living. and it isn\\\'t really a living, just a 20 hour-a-week job. but it feels good, to work.  i\\\'m not drinking or smoking or gambling.  i\\\'m saving money for the first time since i saved my allowance to buy an atari when i was thirteen.  and thinking about getting an additional job.</P><P>choosing to work feels better, more integral to who i am, than relying on my parents or the government or crime to subsidize me.  and what remains of my artistic ambitions; what am i saving for?  why not just ask my relatives for money and deal coke and make another movie?  because the last time i tried that i produced a real piece of shit.  actual excrement.  at the time, i thought it was brilliant.  at the time, i was fucked up.  my brain chemicals, my judgment were skewed.  and i still owe $70K for the processing.  it will probably be released some day as a curiosity, a folly for the world\\\'s fair.</P><P>so now i start over again.  my address might as well be \\\"square one\\\".  i am saving my money to make a couple of shorts, the first on video, the next on film.  the second (\\\"locomotive 38\\\", screenplay of same posted here) will be submitted to festivals.  i will use them to get an agent.  i will remain confidently self-obsessed.</P><P>so i\\\'m just taking it easy, going to group therapy once a week, church twice.  i\\\'m working on my novel every day.  and i\\\'d like to say it\\\'s driving me crazy, i\\\'d like to be able to indulge in my insanity that way, but it\\\'s coming along fine.  nice boring life.  i read a book of hemingway quotes about writing, and he said the secret for him was to quit every day while he was going good, so that there would be someplace to start the next day.  if you write \\\'til you can\\\'t do it anymore, you\\\'ll empty the well and wear yourself out.  writing is an intensely physical process for me, regimented and accompanied by talismans.  i have to exercise to exhaustion afterwards or i\\\'ll be tempted to drink.  creation releases endorphins, and you have to deal with them or face a manic episode. i do anyway.  so i\\\'m feeling pretty good these days.  the medication.  the creation.  the yoga and the abstinence and the voodoo are finally kicking in.  \\\'bout fucking time.</P><P>if there\\\'s one little thing i could impart to you with this morass of an article, something i could include in here about you instead of me, it would be to encourage you to try and love each other and yourselves more deeply.  i know i\\\'m preaching to the choir here, but we are the ones who are the salt of the earth now, we are the ones who are going to have to clean up the messes.  growing up is scary shit, no?  but definitely rewarding.</P>  '),(2957,'<p>\r\nThe future to explore like a virgin garden<br>\r\nSoaked in peace and bliss.<br>\r\nThe past to deplore, the disdain will harden<br>\r\nHow do we get over this?<br>\r\nHow do we get over this?<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe long for that immaculate amnesia<br>\r\nA forever distraction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t touch you with this glove anesthesia<br>\r\nA forever attraction<br>\r\nWe long for that impeccable disconnection<br>\r\nA forever reaction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t live with this misplaced affection<br>\r\nA forever subtraction<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBlack apathy lives underground and around<br>\r\nThe guru doesn\\\'t care.<br>\r\nWe trusted once, now eternally confound<br>\r\nAre we always to err?<br>\r\nAre we always to err?<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe long for that immaculate amnesia<br>\r\nA forever distraction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t touch you with this glove anesthesia<br>\r\nA forever attraction<br>\r\nWe long for that impeccable disconnection<br>\r\nA forever reaction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t live with this misplaced affection<br>\r\nA forever subtraction<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLike the stars at night, like the whitest white<br>\r\nGive us the simple, give us a sample<br>\r\nLike the first invite, like the first delight<br>\r\nWe crave the beginning, shun the sinning.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe long for that immaculate amnesia<br>\r\nA forever distraction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t touch you with this glove anesthesia<br>\r\nA forever attraction<br>\r\nWe long for that impeccable disconnection<br>\r\nA forever reaction<br>\r\nCan\\\'t live with this misplaced affection,<br>\r\nA forever subtraction.<br>\r\n</p>'),(2958,'<p>When your watering the tomato plants make sure to water them on an angle with a light touch spray on the hose\\\'s nozzle. It really damages the leaves when you spray directly on them, they\\\'re fragile, and the harsh spray can make those tiny green bulbs fall off prematurely.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Well what do tomatoes do out in the wild? Do they only grow under a leafy canopy? Or do they brave it out against the pounding rains leaving only the strong firm red tomatoes to survive? To me if we let veggie darwinism take place those tomatoes we eat would be juicier and would not need the wooden stakes to hold them up. Human\\\'s are aiding these wussy red invalids.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What about that pest problem, with the grubs and cutworms chewing the spinach to bits? May be we can try to keep a couple of cups of beer near by because isects are always drawn to fermenting sugar.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The stupid bastards just dive right in and I collect like twenty out of the cups each morning. The leaves of the spinach still look like they\\\'re being decimated by some winged threat. I have to reevaluate.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> If we continue with another lesson in organic gardening, pest control can be most effectively done with boring household ingredients like a palmolive soft soap and mouthwash mixed together in a spray bottle to spray around the garden that acts as a deterrent for a number of insects.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Why am I doing this to myself? Did I have to take over this garden and do everything organic? Even the seeds cost extra, and the compost soil was a real bugger to stir through into a nutrient rich mulch. I still don\\\'t know how many flies I swallowed that day, nor how much more water I had to pour over the little green beasts. Actually organic gardening is harder on your soil than most people think. Even if you rotate the crops each year you still are tempted to use fertilizer on the them to make them look good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> Yesterday I dreamed about using the supergrow chemicals on my tomatoes so they could grow large as pumpkins. The cherry tomatoes have been great, but my beefsteaks have been flavourless and yellowy green. In the dream I just finished making breakfast when I heard some noise in the backyard. I\\\'ll kill those groundhogs! I ran out of my house with my hoe set to kill when I saw my tomatoes now fully grown to the size of a small child, with tiny legs scampering away into the brush near my house. Then I\\\'d shout and wave for them to come back, but they keep going and I just went back in the house, defeated, and slumped in my chair and ate the porridge I made earlier. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> The other day I came to understand why I\\\'m on the Gardening Ontario message board, swapping ideas with the middle-aged ladies about how to tackle our fungal problems best. I really care about them all. I want to see them grow and I want to nuture them out of their sick days and protect them from their enemies. The plants are fragile creatures who I nuture and then devour when they are old enough. I am 25 right now and I can identify with them better than most of my peers. I am unemployed and tending to my parent\\\'s garden after a university degree and some crap freelance jobs. The plants go down good, they are tangible victories -- a small consolation for my work. Some animals eat their young and I will too one day when they are tasty enough for a balsamic vinagrette.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>The garden is like a small oasis from my own problems. It has a small wooden boundary dividing it from the lawn and the surrounding flower bed. It is a world which I am in complete control of, and that\\\'s inspiring right now.</P>\r\n\r\n'),(2959,'<P>Everything seems to come together. In the end. A friend of mine attempting suicide with a revolver he bought in China. Only it really is a lighter. The many facets of attempted death and none of them apply.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Over the phone we ramble inconsistently about sex. About marriage. And how we both are in immense ruts built by our own demise. Now it turns out that perhaps some of us are more predispositioned to be at the root of our demise than others. I being one of them. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>My epilepsy being one of the main guns aimed at my depression. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>That and the fact that I have no sympathy for teenagers who get knocked up. For what what I give to be able to wake up one morning after a week or two of questions and to say to myself, perhaps I\\\'m pregnant? Thats not me.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>My chances for neonatal birth defects doubled than that of general population, vaginal hemmorhage at delivery, 1/3 possible increase in seizure activity during pregnancy...etc. The list goes on. Now you have either hopped on to another swintry or are hating me violently for being so self-absorbed.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>But we speak of motherhood here. And of the possibility of not experiencing it. Yet, I have gads of experience with friends who\\\'ve aborted, friends who are 25 and have 8 year olds...and then to hear that random murmur of regret come from their lips...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>It\\\'s irksome. Of course, there is a long list of all epileptics who have accomplished greatness; Dostoevsky, Van Gogh, Alexander, Joan of Arc, Socrates, etc. But thats them. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>So, all you \\\"possible\\\" mothers out there...females between the childbearing age...\r\nbe thankful.</P>'),(2960,'<p></p><p> \r\nit hurts when i see talented, inspiring people falter; when i see them put down their tools, the weapons of a good and fair fight. so wrong, so discordant, so jarring. \r\n</p><p>\r\nit hurts that minds slow or veer off course, that they become discouraged or unhappy when the artoxygen supply is polluted or blocked.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso many survive as other things, undercover or behind armour because they were hurt by bad press or loss or forces beyond their control.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni want to pick you all up & take you to a place where the electricity bills are paid and the rent is free. where there will always be wine in our glasses & songs in our hearts & you can create all day long, because you must. not because you want to, but because you must.  </p>\r\n'),(2961,'<p>I get on the F train in Manhattan shortly after 2am one Saturday night/Sunday morning, knowing that it will take me a while to get home to Brooklyn.  I take out my book, <i>The Unknown Errors of Our Lives</i> by Chitra Divakaruni and start reading.  I can tell a book being read on the train at this hour interests a lot of people, I notice several lingering looks in my direction.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have settled into my seat when a guy gets on the train and sits in one of the seats perpendicular to mine, brushing my leg and apologizing.  I murmur a response, but don\\\'t look up.  I am intent on \\\"The Blooming Season for Cacti,\\\" but I do notice himâ€”probably younger 20\\\'s, black, hip in a kind of artsy sort of way, hard to describe.  He has excellent glasses: transparent light green plastic in a near elliptical shape, slightly wider at the farthest points.  He carries a box of some sort of fast food, something very fried and as soon as he sits down, he continues eating.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The story has pulled me in, but I see that he is trying to read the cover of my book.  I get to the end and continue on to the next story, but it\\\'s like he notices even that because it\\\'s right then when he interrupts me.  He is soft-spoken and, I think later, probably not sober (how many people are on the train on a Saturday at that hour?).  I can hear half of what he says, I can understand half of what he means.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"What book are you reading?\\\" he asks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I show the cover.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Oh, of course.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Have you read this book?\\\" I ask him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"No.  It\\\'s just I couldn\\\'t see the title because your hand was over it.  I was trying to look, it was hurting my neck actually.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The next few things he says garner a \\\"Sorry?\\\" from me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Don\\\'t you know who I am?\\\" he asks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"No,\\\" I reply, laughing.  I don\\\'t believe for a second that he is a person I should know, mostly because of the mischievous smile on his face.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"You aren\\\'t familiar with my work?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For a moment I question if he\\\'s serious, then doubt again.  \\\"No.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Sorry, this isn\\\'t going to work,\\\" he says.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I just laugh at his response and look out the window, darkness in the tunnel.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But he returns to questioning.  \\\"What\\\'s this book about?  What\\\'s the thesis?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"It\\\'s a book of short stories,\\\" I explain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Why are you reading it?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I go into a somewhat convoluted explanation of how I am reading a lot of short story collections this summer, which brings up the fact that I write.  The conversation finally gets somewhat normal;  he replies that he writes too.  He\\\'s just started his first novel, he talks about his other writings.  Though I can\\\'t understand some of what he\\\'s said, I\\\'ve put my book away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am just about to ask him something about his novel, when he looks up and realizes the doors are open at Bergen Street.  He jumps up.  \\\"I\\\'ve gotta go.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I smile to myself and make eye contact with the woman sitting two seats down from where he was.  I give her a look as if to say, \\\"Wasn\\\'t that entertaining?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In response she gives me a wary look that seems to ask, \\\"What kind of nut are you to carry on conversations with strangers at 2:30 in the morning?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A few stops later I get off to transfer to the N train thinking, <i>the unknown errors of our lives</i>.</p>'),(2962,'my cousin Louise had a baby the day before my birthday. my dad asked me to fly up to Asheville, NC with him and my stepmother to see her and little Webb. isn\\\'t that the cutest name? Webb. my grandfather said, on hearing it for the first time, \\\"i guess they\\\'ll call \\\'im Spider.\\\"<p>\r\nthat\\\'s hilarious.<p>\r\n\\\"flying up to Asheville\\\" maybe means something a little different in my family than it might in yours. we took my dad\\\'s new jet. it isn\\\'t new exactly, it was made in 1974, but it\\\'s new to him. he\\\'s an airline pilot. this was my first ride in it since he got it, 6 months ago or so. i was pretty excited. preflighting checks- excited. engine startup- excited. taxiing- excited. takeoff- oh shit. all of a sudden, 25 years didn\\\'t quite seem like a full lifetime, if you catch my drift. that thing was jumping around like Michael Jordan and shaking like a wet dog. i guess you don\\\'t realize how <i>fast</i> you are going on a big jet like a 727, but on this thing 200 knots feels more like 600, and you realize that if things get sideways it\\\'s gonna be over quick. \r\n<p>\r\nbut oh my god! it was so incredible once we got up there. nevermind that my dad almost put himself in the poorhouse to buy it, nevermind how much it costs to operate it--it was all worth it to be able to see out of like 10 windows, at 16 thousand feet...wow. like...wow. the clouds were just <i>stroking</i> by, it\\\'s like you are in a van or something, a little tiny spaceship with comfy leather seats. i <i>have to have one.</i> it felt like i could have reached up and touched the face of God.\r\n<p>\r\nso, yeah. 25 years might have been enough.'),(2963,'<p>\r\nWe agreed to meet at La Dolce Vita in North Beach. I spent another hour debugging code and fielding emails, then took the elevator down and set out north on Kearny. I had half an hour to get to the restaurant, so I took my time, and tried hard to see the city that so many others had fallen in love with. I soon gave up. The city\\\'s fabled hills and bridges, bathed in translucent late afternoon California sunlight, were unnaturally gorgeous, pristine and soft-edged, like something created by the worker elves of ILM up in Marin. But down on the street there was hardly anything but squalor and madness. San Francisco, like many women, was best appreciated at a distance.\r\n<p>\r\nOn impulse I stopped at the Sanrio store and bought a wildly overpriced plush toy, an abstracted penguin named Bad Batz Maru. I stuffed him in my bag so that his head stuck out, and he stared impolitely at the sidewalk sleepers we passed on our way through Chinatown.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Destroyer was waiting for me at the restaurant, loitering near the entrance and smoking. When she saw me she smiled and crushed her cigarette under her sneaker. I kissed her on the cheek and we went inside. We had to wait for a table so we headed for the bar.\r\n<p>\r\nI ordered Maker\\\'s rocks and she asked for a gin and tonic. We both crossed our legs as we sat on our stools and her knee rested on my shin. \r\n<p>\r\n\\\"So, do you do that sort of thing often?\\\" she said.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"What?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Pick up girls at laundromats. You do that a lot?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Not when I\\\'m sober,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe sipped her drink. \\\"I see. Who\\\'s that?\\\" \r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Who him?\\\" I turned Bad Batz Maru\\\'s head so he gave her a long saucy up-and-down look. \\\"This is a friend from work. He didn\\\'t believe I\\\'d actually made a date with a girl without paying cash up front, so he insisted on seeing for himself.\\\"\r\nBad Batz Maru looked at me and then back at her. I supplied the wolf whistle. \\\"He thinks you\\\'re cute,\\\" I said. \\\"He says, \\\'<I>Hola, mami</I>, you want to take me home?\\\'\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"Fresh thing!\\\" I handed her the penguin. \\\"He\\\'s pretty cute too,\\\" she said. \\\"I want to bite him. Are penguins good eating?\\\" She made Bad Batz Maru look at me in alarm.\r\n<p>\r\nI laughed and noticed the maitre\\\'d waving us to our table. We switched to wine and managed to put away a bottle between the salad and the entrÃ©e. By the time the check arrived, we\\\'d finished a second and made a brave attempt at a third. I paid the bill and then we were back on Broadway, surrounded by tourists and sailors and ragged hippies hailing from the Land of the Lost. The flashing lights of a dozen strip clubs threatened to trigger epileptic seizures in the milling crowds.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Have you ever been in one of those places?\\\" she asked. She swayed against me.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"No, my mom won\\\'t let me,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe punched me on the shoulder. \\\"Don\\\'t be a dick. Let\\\'s go to one.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWell. What do you know. I turned and arched an eyebrow for the benefit of the imaginary audience watching at home. \\\"Which one? This one?\\\" I gestured to the open double doors beside us, garishly lit by hundreds of exposed lightbulbs. A hideous old broad who was probably younger than me, stick thin in a red teddy and 6 inch heels, waved me to come in. I grinned drunkenly at her and started pulling the Destroyer with me.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"No, not that one,\\\" she said. She started pulling me towards the street. \\\"We have to get a cab. It\\\'s on the other side of town. Mitchell Brothers.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Whoa, hey,\\\" I said. I thought furiously. Was Tsing Tao dancing tonight? Was tonight her night off? I couldn\\\'t remember. Fuck! \\\"That\\\'s far. Cabs are fucking expensive. We could tip out two strippers for the cost of getting over there. What about this one? The Garden of Eden. \\\'Put your serpent in our burning bush.\\\' That sounds good, right?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Don\\\'t be a fag,\\\" she said. \\\"Mitchell Brothers is the best. No pasties.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I had no idea I was dealing with an aficionado. How about this one? Preja Vu. \\\'You Ain\\\'t Seen Nothing Yet.\\\' That sounds good!\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I want to go to Mitchell Brothers,\\\" she insisted. She pushed me back to arm\\\'s length. \\\"Unless there\\\'s some reason you don\\\'t want to go?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nWell, yeah, actually, my ex-girlfriend dances there and it might be a little uncomfortable if she saw me walk in with-\r\n<p>\r\nRight.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\\\"Nah. No reason. Just lazy,\\\" I said. \\\"Give me convenience or give me de