is about to begin.  it won\\\'t let up until my early thirties.  in between are years of drug use and abuse, mental illness, homelessness, aimless wandering, crime and punishment.  but for now, on this march evening, things are ideal.</P><P>i\\\'m a sophomore on a full wrestling and academic scholarship, majoring in business administration.  i\\\'ve got a 4.0 GPA and a hippie girlfriend.  she\\\'s in a sorority; i\\\'m in a frat.  that summer, she\\\'ll introduce me to acid on a trip out west.  my worldview will shatter.  i will not return to college for nine years.  nobody is to blame.</P><P>but like i said, things are going great.  i\\\'m living in a dorm for athletes on scholarship.  my roommate tommy is on the track team.  we\\\'ve become best friends, and, luckily, our girlfriends get along with each other.  we both have similar hairstyles:  short around the sides and back, long on top.  i\\\'m sure you\\\'ve seen the pictures.</P><P>i have begun to suspect that there\\\'s a little sugar in my tank.  at first, it kind of freaked me out, but i\\\'ve gotten comfortable with it.  i\\\'d had similar feelings in high school before and after games and practices when all the guys walked around the locker room naked.  everybody had a bone on, so it either meant we were all gay or were just curious teenagers.  (they were probably all gay.)  tommy and i undress in front of each other all the time, and i think nothing of it.</P><P>but tonight things change.  tommy wants to know, \\\"just for shits and giggles,\\\" if i want to go to the university club tonight.  it\\\'s the local gay club.  i start to put two and two together.  tommy loves the smiths.  he\\\'s the one who turned me on to the smiths.  he has gay friends.  he\\\'s a great cook and is very neat.</P><P>we\\\'re sitting in our room.  it\\\'s starting to rain.  \\\"tommy, can i ask you a really personal question?\\\"  \\\"yeah.\\\"  \\\"are you gay?\\\"  \\\"no, i\\\'m bi.\\\"  \\\"oh.\\\"  \\\"tommy, can i tell you something?\\\"  \\\"yeah.\\\"  \\\"i think i am, too.\\\"  \\\"i know.\\\"  \\\"how did you know?\\\"  \\\"i don\\\'t know.  instinct, i guess.\\\"</P><P>i lock the door.  we take our clothes off and start to kiss and rub each other\\\'s nipples.  \\\"want to do the 69?\\\"  \\\"what do i do?\\\"  \\\"c\\\'mon, bubba, 69.  you know.\\\"  \\\"yeah, but with a guy....\\\"  \\\"just do what i do.\\\"</P><P>he puts on \\\"the queen is dead\\\".  we get on my bed, heads at opposite ends.  he rolls my dick between his palms and sucks on the head.  i follow suit.  he rolls his tongue around the glans and licks the tip.</P><P>yada, yada, we go see \\\"tremors\\\" later on at the dollar theatre.</P><P><B>lesson the third</B></P><P>he comes back up.<BR>we all hit off the vaporizer<BR>(lebanese blonde),<BR>undress.<BR>they kiss.<BR><BR>me:  do you think we could both<BR>fit up your cooch at the same time?<BR><BR>her:  with that little vienna sausage you got?<BR>sure.<BR>but we\\\'re not gonna try that little experiment,<BR>johnny.<BR><BR>eddie and i start beating off<BR>like a couple of chimpanzees.<BR><BR>eddie\\\'s got a good 3 inches on me.<BR><BR>anna puts on her yma sumac tape.<BR>she\\\'s the one who turned me on to yma sumac.<BR><BR>she sits on the sofa bed<BR>and pats her hands<BR>on the mattress<BR>on either side of her.<BR>we sit down.<BR><BR>her:  i want to try something<BR>i saw on the internet.<BR>it\\\'s called the jade dragon<BR>tongue flick.<BR>or something like that.<BR><BR>eddie:  oh, shit,<BR>anna\\\'s breakin\\\' out the good stuff.<BR><BR>she bends down<BR>and licks the place<BR>on the underside<BR>of eddie\\\'s cock<BR>where the head<BR>meets the shaft.<BR><BR>she does the same to mine<BR>then sits back up.<BR><BR>she looks at us and grins.<BR>we look at each other.<BR>i shrug.<BR>we all just sit there.<BR>eddie starts to hum<BR>the \\\"jeopardy\\\" theme song.<BR><BR>after about fifteen seconds,<BR>she licks us again,<BR>sits back and waits.<BR><BR>after ten minutes of this,<BR>we\\\'re both writhing<BR>in pleasure and agony.<BR>eddie can\\\'t take it anymore<BR>and starts to beat off.<BR>he comes all over my leg<BR>and the plastic sheet<BR>covering the sofa bed.<BR><BR>i wait it out<BR>and am rewarded with<BR>the best orgasm<BR>of my life.<BR><BR>me: huh.<BR>learn something new every day.</P>'),(2972,'<p>Boundless opportunity stares. The look is blank but somehow threatening, the look of possibility. It blinks menacingly, the white frame suggesting emptiness or purpose <i>or</i>. The blank blinking, swallowing, breathing,\r\nbreathing, breathing. Searching for a void to fill, a sauce to drape across the nothing, something to grasp, turn over, around, look under. Something to smell. All I can think about is your smell. I can\\\'t remember, don\\\'t want to remember, can\\\'t, won\\\'t, don\\\'t, the smell, the smell, the smell. Blinking, blinking, blinking, blinking, you\\\'re not alone, you\\\'re not alone, let me take you away, let me find what you have lost, let me, let me give you what you don\\\'t know. \r\nYou don\\\'t let me smell you. Pretend you can remember.</p>\r\n\r\n<center><p><i>Breathe</i>.</p></center>\r\n\r\n<p>Who left who, I left myself, she was left, I was gone, we\\\'re gone, it\\\'s gone. Returning only as whispers in someone else\\\'s ears and a sparkle in a dream somewhere in a bed that has never been made. Feel me run my fingers through the moments, feel me trip on the roots of the tree we planted with its branches unfurled grasping, groping, hoping, feeling. Trying to breathe. Search my face for something, something in the estuary wrinkles and floodplain cheeks, something to believe in. I\\\'m looking through you, or through myself, or not looking. I\\\'m going to go, time to move, somewhere beckons, something calls. I can\\\'t see but I feel the web coming along for the ride sticking, crawling, tickling, disintegrating into dust under my feet.</p>\r\n\r\n<center><p><i>Are my eyes open?</i></p></center>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what\\\'s happening but it\\\'s happening. It\\\'s here around me, in me, of me, making me, moving me, pushing, shoving. Something else is tugging, teasing, tossing memories at me that vanish before I can grasp them. Or after. Is it you? I turn to look but it\\\'s always behind me, I turn looking, hoping, turning, but there are only just hints exhaled as breaths on the nape of my neck. Suggestions of an idea, outlines of a\r\npossibility, a call for invention, creation. But there is no safety in an idea. Maybe it\\\'s not a good idea. You never know, or I never do. I never\r\ndo. But I am. Here I am, here, can\\\'t you see me?</p>\r\n\r\n<center><p><i>Hold on, hold on, hold on, wait, but, maybe if I just, oh, oh. OK. Ok. ok.</i></p></center>\r\n\r\n<p>But. Wait. No. <b>NO</b>. I am not afraid and I know what I want. If I could only just, here close to me, here with me in my arms. I don\\\'t want to look over my shoulder or in my dreams. I\\\'m tired from it, sleepy, sluggish, blinking, rolling over and over and over. You are in my sleep but I cannot smell you. That\\\'s why I know I\\\'m dreaming. Waking. Waking. Waking. Awake.\r\nSomeday I will wake and you will be there. Someday before forever, yesterday maybe, but never never. Blinking, blinking, blinking. I can\r\nwait. I just have to remember to breathe.</p>'),(2973,'too tired to care<BR>\r\ntoo tired to even begin to understand<BR>\r\nyour outlook so limited by your caged existense<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nhave you ever seen the real world?<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\ntoo tired to leave<BR>\r\ntoo tired to think<BR>\r\nthe mind grows lazy with lack of use<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\ntoo lazy to leave<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nyou\\\'d never survive<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\noutside<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nin the real world<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nyou know everyone here<BR>\r\nand they all know you<BR>\r\na one stop shop for your conformity<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nblend the rules<BR>\r\nlive a little<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\ntaste\r\n<BR>\r\nthe real world'),(2974,'sometimes you send me messages that i read and my<BR>\r\nthroat pulses. there must be an instrument in an MIT lab somewhere that could measure the infinite<BR>\r\nvariations within my body as i read your words. you sent me a poem, and it made sense, like a small piece of music.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\npicture a brief, quick kiss while the girl has<BR>\r\nher head turned away: she wasn\\\'t expecting it, it took her completely by surprise, the angle was all wrong, but now that he has given it to her, she\\\'s deighted. she can\\\'t stop smiling. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand today, today i feel beautiful.<BR>\r\nthere are dark crescents, shadows? under my eyes. my right cheek has a smattering of small red<BR>\r\nindentations. imperfections dot me like black stars. and yet, my face glows. i am beloved.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand i wasn\\\'t going to reply today, you know. yet<BR>\r\nagain, i was going to just read your messages -- my sustenance -- and then close my inbox. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsilence, withdrawal into my tight, comfortable,<BR>\r\nrestrictive but safe snail-shell, has been my modus operandi since, well, since i don\\\'t know when?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbut i had to, tonight. i had to. your words persuaded me. your love is the colour of evening.'),(2975,'<P>\r\nit is so hot, i can lie outside in my underwear and my body sweats as if in a marathon. and still, i sleep under a comforter, under layers of sheets and blankets. i need to feel safe.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nlast night my whole body was wracked with grief. i could not bear it, i felt like there were claws and fangs and tiny minefields trapped within, and all that came out was empty parched silence. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t know a way to reach out to the world. to tether myself to it.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni need to get away from here. i need to get up and go. leave. go somewhere far far away. escape. get on a plane. cross oceans. go somewhere where i don\\\'t understand what people are saying when they talk, where the foreign language makes irridiscent bubbles inside my head.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbut what am i saying? this is all a lie. it won\\\'t matter if i find myself in the shade of the pyramids, or in a snowstorm on the alps. it will still be all wrong. it is not this place.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni need to get away from myself. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni don\\\'t have a job. i don\\\'t go to school. i pace the one bedroom apartment angrily, warily, carefully. i am tired. i am tired. my life is just, i can see it, vividly, flowing down a dirty bathtub drain. i am wasting it. i don\\\'t know what to do. i am in despair.</P>'),(2976,'<P>\r\nhe calls me in the middle of the night, no, the end part of night, and he is crying. i always feel funny when a man cries. part of me wants to take him into my arms and comfort him, stroke his hair and whisper iloveyous into his ears. the other part wants to get away, as far away as possible.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nhe wants so much from me. and i am drained. i am empty. we are no longer in a relationship. but tell me the truth -- can you ever really be a friend to your former lover? \r\n\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t know anything anymore. sometimes we\\\'ll be sitting quietly together, a big mug of semi-sweet hot chocolate enclosed in our palms, and talking about thomas pynchon or david lynch, and i would never admit to him what i am actually thinking.\r\n\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni concentrate on his green eyes. i\\\'m not sure about this whole soul thing. i don\\\'t know what happens after we die. if there is some intangible airy part of us that escapes from the confines of our corpses at the gate of the grave, that floats up into the sky, up past the leaves of the tallest trees at the cemetery. i\\\'d like to think that there is more to life, to human beings, than what is here right now. i\\\'d like to think so. but i\\\'m not sure. still, when i look into his eyes, i feel like i am reaching something i would not otherwise reach. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\ni like to remember what holly says about this. that we\\\'re all energy. and energy, of course, can never be created or destroyed. only transformed. \r\n\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nit is funny how sudden desires flame through me, so that there is a physical pain to not tracing his too-fleeting dimple. and oh! sometimes, sometimes i have a desperate urge to kiss him. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\ni am shameless. i confess this to you. i despise people who cheat on their significant others as much as you despise them. but sometimes, sometimes when i am with him, i want to pretend there are no rules, no boundaries, no limits. i want to crush the walls around us, take his hand in mine, and run.\r\n\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni feel desire for him, but anger, too. i rage at him, and at what he conjures within me. i want to excise him from my life, burn him away. two months ago the doctors took ammonia, smoke, what looked like magic, to the moles on my mother\\\'s arms and chest. they made them disappear, so cleanly, so beautifully, i wept for days aftwerward.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nso tell me, when i talk about love, is it the same thing you think of when you think of love? is this attempt enough to encompass the sheer magnitude, the chaos? can it hold and contain something so fucked up and complicated that i sort of walk around it, on tip toes, making up lists, extricating little moments, giving you only small fragments. mere aspects of this enormous universe.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nonce he waited for me outside the b&b in a short sleeved t-shirt, the temperature ten degrees below freezing. and i saw how his face changed when he caught a glimpse of me coming toward him. of how he laughed, too far away for me to hear the sound, when i broke into a run. of how he burrowed his so-cold hands into my winter coat and under my dress. of how his face looked slightly blue in the moment just before he bent his head to mine.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nit is over now. it has been over for a while. even though i can\\\'t resist reading the love letters he continues to mail me before i destroy them. even though i call him, dream of meeting him, in a strange city, a few years from now. even though he yearns for me, and the longing in his voice is a temptation that makes my nails tingle.\r\n\r\n\r\n</P>'),(2977,'<p>it\\\'s hot as hell in here...even with the environmentally correct non-freon utilizing oscillating fan blowing past me, my fingers make a disgusting soft suctioning sound in the little curves of the keys because their tips are coated with sweat.  she thinks i\\\'m working, and i probably should be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she\\\'s lying over there.  in the pacific glow of a single lamp, asking me if she\\\'s a femme fatale.  legs so long you\\\'d need a map to travel their knuckle-biting distance.  she has the look of someone who knows too much but remains good in spite of it. it\\\'s inherent in her.  i\\\'ve never seen such a thing, and i revel in being amazed by it. she doesn\\\'t know it but she looks like a botticelli when the wind pulls her chestnut hair just so...she has no idea how lost i intend to get in it tonight.  how i plan to chart her perfect topography...the inlets and atolls and islands of her.  her hands are perfect.  not a botticelli but a degas.  they look as if they can do anything...everything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she makes me ache but in the best possible way.  she doesn\\\'t realize how safe she is with me. she can\\\'t believe it when i tell her, but it will be true just the same.  that\\\'s the tragedy of it. she thinks i\\\'m a shit...unruly and restless. she can\\\'t believe i\\\'d dash my ship against the rocks just to get to her. but i will.</p>'),(2979,'<p>\r\nI still see your secret splinters.<br>\r\nI thought they\\\'d gone, I thought they\\\'d withdrawn.<br>\r\nBut they hide in the cracks, they leave their tracks, they take their tax,<br>\r\nThose secret splinters.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd when I\\\'m not there, they all prepare,<br>\r\nTo cut me one more time<br>\r\nI enter their lair, completely unaware<br>\r\nOf the sharpness of their crime.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI still see your secret splinters.<br>\r\nI thought I got them all, I thought they weren\\\'t that small.<br>\r\nBut they find me every time, they drink my wine, they maintain your shrine,<br>\r\nThose secret splinters.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd in the night, they all ignite,<br>\r\nAnd pierce me once again.<br>\r\nSometimes the white, it takes delight<br>\r\nIn knowing it will remain.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI still see your secret splinters.<br>\r\nI thought I swept them away, I thought they\\\'d gone yesterday.<br>\r\nBut they still prick me, they prick my heart, they\\\'re your lasting work of art,<br>\r\nThose secret splinters.<br>\r\n</p>'),(2980,'Do you believe in stress?</p><p>\r\nI don\\\'t. I think it\\\'s all just some made up word to classify people who need a reason to explain why they are acting erratic. \\\"I drank too much last night, I must be stressed.\\\" Or, \\\"I killed my lover, I was under a lot of stress.\\\" Or, \\\"I yelled at you, I\\\'m really stressed.\\\" It\\\'s all just an excuse for acting poorly.</p><p>\r\nI finally gave in and decided to get myself one of those new hip and trendy things. Yeah, I got a psychologist. She\\\'s really nice and listens to what I have to say and asks the right questions and, really she just listens. That was all I wanted in the first place, somebody to listen. But she keeps telling me, \\\"You\\\'re under so much stress right now.\\\" </p><p>\r\nAll I do is go to school. I don\\\'t have a job, I don\\\'t pay bills (thanks for dying, uncle L.!) and I haven\\\'t been hanging out with people who bring me drama. The most stress I have on an everyday basis is riding the T (Boston\\\'s answer to a subway system) amidst all the families who decided Boston was THE spot for family vacation this year. </p><p>\r\nBut then we talk about emotional stress. My mom got married last week. She was my single mother for the formative years of my life. She was all mine. I could call her at 2 in the morning to tell her I couldn\\\'t sleep and she would just sit on the other end of the phone and listen. I could be in the middle of my period and decide I want to visit her in Brooklyn for the week; she\\\'d give up her bed for me. But now she\\\'s married and it\\\'s all changed.</p><p>\r\nMy therapist says that\\\'s a lot of stress to deal with. Oh, and my grandma and my uncle dying within six weeks of each other a few months ago is also a bit of stress. And let\\\'s not forget getting arrested, giving up friends, moving across country and not knowing where to call home. Yeah, she says I\\\'m stressed.</p><p>\r\nI liked that she was able to justify my erratic behavior, but I didn\\\'t think that it was all that truthful. I figured if she thinks I\\\'m stressed, then I can tell people I\\\'m stressed. They will cut me some slack and let me be crazy and unreliable, unsociable and neurotic; if I\\\'m stressed, people will let me be myself. </p><p>\r\nBut I didn\\\'t really believe all that psycho-mumbo-jumbo-medical-jargon. Stress? Pshaw. I\\\'m not stressed, I\\\'m unique. But then I spent the night with my head burried in the toilet. Throwing up everything I\\\'d eaten, and everything I hadn\\\'t eaten (woah, my intestines look really funny in the toilet!).</p><p>\r\nIt was 1am and I didn\\\'t dare call my mommy. I knew she\\\'d tell me to have some saltine crackers and a cup of tea. She\\\'d coddle me and I\\\'d fall asleep feeling better. But I didn\\\'t call her because she\\\'s married now. I\\\'ve been throwing up a lot recently. I\\\'m not bulemic. I just vomit a lot. I don\\\'t ever feel better until I talk to my mom for a little while. The crackers aren\\\'t what heals my stomach. No, it\\\'s my mom\\\'s voice simply giving me suggestions and saying, \\\"Oh my poor baby.\\\"</p><p>\r\nWhen I woke up this morning I called her and told her what happened. She told me to have some saltines and drink some tea and \\\"I wish I could help you feel better.\\\" I instantly felt better. Later on, I was wondering why I throw up so often.</p><p>\r\nIt finally occurred to me: Maybe I\\\'m stressed.'),(2981,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã \n\n\n\r\r\Z\ZÃ¿Ã€?Âªâ€œÂ¢â€°Ã¿Ã”pp~Ã¢u%??yk1Y#â€™>_hWÃ¬?ÃKÃ›â€¢JÃœFÂ²)$Ã”â€¡CÃºuXâ€” +1` â€šâ€¢5Ãœâ€ Â©Ã»M4;+Â¸*a4<EÃ£WÃ¯Ã¤Ã«:ÂªÂ°Å“Â¾ÂºAÃ \"OÃ–ÃÂ£>\Z(W`<5YHkÃ¾Å¾Â¢Ã·Å¡5]X])Â¯ÃŒâ€žÂ¢AÃ¿Ã¿tÂ¢Â¯Ã½Å¡&Mk*Ãž	8)?Ã›Ã vy43Â¢pxÃ›i=Ã‹hâ€“Ã·$Å¡OlÃÃ¥rÃžuÃÃ‰2tHÂ¦â€¦y4oJÂ¿Â¥7Â¥Ã¾:3Â´d7pZÃ‹jÃÃšÃƒww<|Ã¢QÂ¬Ã­Ã‰~â€”Ãš_QE?<uÂ¯[â€¢Ã¶Å“Â´Ã¡ÃŠÃ¥ZhÃ¥â€¡?~LÂ¾Ã¢ÃƒÂ¢T nÃ²\rÃ½[Ã½Å¡Å¾Â·Ã¡â€ºÃš:Ã–>Ã®â€¹O\ZDÅ gÂ¼JV6fcHâ€™OËœIM1Ã²^Ã©Å½?Â¾$?Â³I?Â¶a:PO1SÃ‡â€°_Ã·@Æ’Â¦\')Ã­Ã°â€™tâ€¡VlÃÂ¼e}ÃŠ{VUÃ¿|FÂµâ€¡Ãž~Ã¡Ãƒ{Q({â€°eÅ’B?Ã½Â·%Ãœ>Ã›Ã¸8HÃœjÂ·Ã½{Â±`Â¸â€™~Ã Ã…Ãƒ\Z,Â­m+Is,Ã‰Ã‚Ãª4x]6Ã‚ÂµcÂ¿ÃµÃµÃ’Ã§â€“hÃšÃ¬Ã®Ã£â€”;o?â€¢Ã•YÃŸÃœYÂ§S6Â®+*kÂµ)Âªâ€¡0Â¨z@ÃÂ´Â­wÃ•zxj`#-EvÂ¦ËœÂ½Ã‰?Â½Å hÂ³Ã¸xÂ½ÃŒÂ¥Å¡Ã±Å¾Ã”z~Â²Ã—Â«@}~hâ€°Ã¨Ã›t:Å¸\nÃ–Ã…dÃ¬Â²ËœÃ¨/Ã¬Â¤ÃšÃœ/8Å¾â€>D0Ã°e;0Ã°:ÃŽF^kÂµ-Â®lnâ€”Ã’Ã—&Ã—_ÃŒÂ­nâ€°\'?Ã²T5<5Â¸ÃŸKÂ«7Â°Ã’YÃ¬ÃŒÃ°ZÃ›Gâ€¹Â¹VGâ€“1 2â€%Å Â³M+ÃŠÂ |5;ÂµÃ½Ëœ^Ã¬ËœÆ’6rÃ¥Gâ€º+pÂª)OL\n??Ã¶z5â€”!Mh>:Ãš\"Å¾xÃ¡?Â¤â€˜â€š\"Å½LÃ¬Bâ€¦rI;Â¬ÃšÂ¸Ã¤;;.ÃšÃ«Ã©lÃ¥â€”\'0nÂ­â€šË†yx?zBË†~ÃªÃªIÃšÃ¸4jÃ¶Ã¯Ã¦67v,QZÃ–ÃµÃ‘Â¤Å )\Z)U_â€ºÃšâ€“â€™6+Ã¸â€“Â¼â€¡â€“â€œ&Ã¥Â«Ã·Ã«Â£\'+Â uv:\ZÃ—Â®Â´\Zjtâ€¡â€“?ÃƒAÃžz??Â«E5â€°Ã³Ã2â€¡ÃƒQÃ¬NÂ¨u?\rÅ’Â Ã¿Ã†9Â¢pRXÂ¤^Â±ÃŠâ€¡Ã”Å’<Å½Â¦L.Ã”SÃƒZÃŠqÂª;nÅ¡â€¢c;5â€žÃ…eÃ­>â€º#nÂ³Ã„Â§Å“dâ€™Â¯Å½?Â­6Â´Â¤g87yâ€¢Â¾?Â¼;_Â¼Â²â€“}Â¿rÂ¹ËœÂ¤Â·LÂ¤Ã˜Â«Â²Ã¥Vs*Å -Ã‡Â¤QË†Â¤Æ’Â®ÃºiuÃ˜ÃŽpdÂ¿4Ã³â€˜)ÂµÃ†ZÃ¶Â½Â´Æ’yÃ²Ãµw@Ã¸mÃ£\nÅ Â¶u=gâ€ºÃºÃœEÃœgÃ¥Ã²Aâ€“Â´ÃŒÃ¤Â³Y,Â®RÃ•â„¢Ã‘Ã¥â€$â€™â€¢+Ã´Ã±â‚¬?wÃ©Â«Â§Ë†kÃŠÃ´Ã®Ã‹â„¢Ã°Â¾Ã»C$Å ?5ÃŒ|Â©lj]Ã½%Y\nÅ \nWÃ•Â±Â¦ÂµÃ–;tÃ©wivnK&.Ã–ÃŠÃžÃ©gÅ¾Ãš5 dÃ¶Ã”Ã±Ã€}Å¡vgÃ»:Ã™u7Ã²Ã¨?ÃºiOW7wÂ¦BzÃ³7RVÂºÃÃÃŒNAÂ£Â®Â´?-Ã¼Ã™â€¡#}Ãœ=ÂµË†wOÃ¤â€”â€™ÃŠÃ·vÃ’1Dâ€“X5YÂ¨A1Ã±Ã°Ã