ZBor6Ã™|lÃ¡Ruq\rÂ¥Ãº,+Ã‡Å â€“Ã£ÃœAÃ¸Kâ€¹Â³Ã§w\'Ã§Å¸lÃˆ)â€ Ny+B?Ã›	7Ã¸29SÃºu};pâ„¢Å“â€ºÃ¿Â³Ã›]Â©â€žÃ­Â¬rÃ˜bmâ€ž1ÃŠi(â€™Â½7yu?Â¨xjÃ¶Â¶Ã®â€™ÃœÃ™{\\.Ã«-tÂ¡Ã’Ã‘Ë†â€ž|Ã’J(Â¯BÃŒ@Ã”Ãº\\>zÃ®ÃŒqZ`Ã¦ÃdÃ±Â¢I3Â±Ã³lâ€¡Â¸â€¦Ã–kâ€°EÃƒV/Å“R(Ã‚Â¯ÃŸÂ®â€œâ€žÃ‹Ã‘?/{r{Ã™Â¬Â¡Ã‰]Ã»Ã¸Â»F>[kÂ­Â³â„¢Å¾ITÃŽÅ’OÂ¸ÃŠÃ‹SÃWÃƒmbÃŸ*-ÃŠ~cÃ¢qâ„¢+Å“aÂ°Ã‰]_[0CÂ½Â¤Â®Â®YCUÅ“8Ã¯JÃ—SÃ“Âµ39Qâ€ºÂ¹Â¿1Ã²+Â·VÃŠÃœÅ¡4Ã—Ã²â€žzyÂªÂ°QÃ¾Â«jÃºsOnâ„¢Ãs\\Ëœ.>Å¡ÃµÃ©Ãªâ€š9eÂ»â€Aâ€¦-cÃ¾Ã´zÂ²pÂ¹Ã¥Â§?Ã„gi???Â®#Ã¿lÂ¥Ã°Ëœ!Ã³Haâ€žÃ‡Ã·Uu3Ã²Å¡4[ÃžWgÃ¿Ã¿'),(2982,'<P>\r\nThe sacred animal escapes classification. We could look it face to face without realising what it was, we could maim it and never realise it was a sacred animal. \r\n   </P><P>            Dr. Philpott and Dr. Levin stood at the entrance way to the ward with pictures of animals of various types. Dr. Findlater still persevered with inkblots, while almost everyone else was in thrall of the Philpott-Levin technique which, as far as Dr. Findlater was bothered to understand it, involved manipulations of the r-complex with images of animals. \r\n</P><P>\r\nthe cat still hisses and growls when she smells her on my clothes. even though the other cat is long gone.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe cat knows things. this is why i love her.\r\n</P><P>\r\n       He gazed at his reflection in one of the shiny black pixels on the cafeteria floor. â€œDeclan,â€ started Philpott, â€œyou should really look at the new paper Levin and I put together for the BJP. Youâ€™d be doing us a favour, actually. Your study of psycho-depressives using puppetry was really the inspiration for our work, as Iâ€™m sure Iâ€™ve told you. Eh? Levinâ€™s here now with the results. Declan?â€ Dr. Findlater returned his eyes on the floor tile in pretend reverie. What kind of reverie could it be, asked Findlater. One about your childhood, he answered. My childhood was boring, said Findlater. How about somebody elseâ€™s childhood? Somebody elseâ€™s? Oh, yes, that sounds good. \r\n               Prospero Findlater was an old Rorschachian. The master of the mucilage, the executor of the excrement, the guardian of the glob, anything which cannot be explained in terms of an indistinct blot of ink on a piece of card, or a runny, beeswaxy tesselation of shit on the side of a toilet, is innately unexplainable. No word of a lie. It was his tarot, and he set the omens with its mandala, which was totally out of control, spinning wildly in whatever direction the winds blew, the Rorshachian blot was very much like Northern Ireland, see what you want, nationalism as a psychodiagnostik. Amazing, really, that the entire conflict could be solved by stressing subjective projection, rubber bullet agnosia in the rising damp of clandestine training grounds south of the border in Dundalk or Leixlip or ... Breaking the tyranny of reading left to right.</P><P>\r\n	In 1985, Ireland was in the grip of it, parallax had replaced history, religion, birth-right, family ... in a shrine in Cork, a woman saw a statue move, in Sligo, and elsewhere. But the force of the parallax didnâ€™t stop there, entire Biblical scenes were played out, and statues continued to move, to walk even, Blessed Virgins roamed like vagrants, and took their places among the stray dogs. Stray dogs are the true parishioners, they slept at her feet and felt the drizzle of her tears. They saw her true devotional. </P><P>\r\nBut what about the blood stains, the massed crowds, the great plumes of fire and smoke rising like a diadem to sit atop the detonating force below like a fire-eater, the dopplering of screams amidst the frantic passel, the hysterics, people losing control of their bowels at the exact point a child, screaming, is thrown through a windshield by the massive force of the bomb, the blasts of metal on concrete, the apocalyptic haze, the quiet interiors of ruins? The gaseous, the liquid, the undefined, escape the doctrine of parallax. An explosion will constantly change shape, thwarting the frozen gaze, the explosion always remains in two dimensions only, it has no shape, no form, it is the ultimate Rorschach ... </P><P>\r\nTo give any shape a name is to ruin the perfection inherent in it, to sabotage its uniqueness with ubiquity, and this was what he hoped his patients would be able to experience as a means to getting them back into a state predisposed to the kind of ruination of spirit the world had to offer. Perhaps Northern Ireland was the one refuge for us old Rorschachians? The circle was perfect until someone named the shape, and decided it was not as perfect as they thought. To experience each shape as a once in a lifetime, unrepeatable piece of infinity, unconnected with anything repeatable, performable, demonstrative or iterable was both loneliness and bliss.</P>'),(2983,'<P>My morning bloomed and wilted with the usual. Thai iced tea to temper the humid morning, and a Lucky Strike - the lucky cigarette turned upside down, patiently waiting to be annihilated last, expelling good fortune with each genie wisp of smoke. Make a wish before you die. the boredom filtered in as my mushroom-cloud dreams skidded out on combat-booted feet and i took my first drag - suicide at its most mundane. A man ambled by â€“ polished briefcase complimenting the perfectly creased suit, and incongruously - red Converse. He winked and asked for a smoke. i held out my pack. i didn\\\'t notice until i reached for my second of the day, that he had picked the Chosen One. The upside down paradox of luck.</P><P>\r\nthat was when i began to seriously reconsider the thought that i was part of a story-if not a bejeweled fairy tale stricken with the curse of a lost luck-laden cigarette, than maybe an after school special with a moral or two. The whole iliveinastory idea came a few years ago on a Sunday.  i had unearthed the hidden rock encapsulating my grandmaâ€™s house key, and slipped in the door, secreting the key back into its nook, beneath the ferny leaves of the gardenia. A real plant â€“ unlike the rock. </P><P>\r\n	â€œGrandma, are you ready for church?â€ i yelled.</P><P>\r\nThatâ€™s right. Me â€“ the born again atheist. But that comes later â€“ the born again part, I mean. Anyway, there i was - dressed in a skirt about twenty inches longer than usual, with society sandals replacing the boots that iâ€™m generally clad in. The belly button and shoulders would soon be ensconced in one of grandmaâ€™s sweet old-lady sweaters â€“ the kind with the brash gleaming buttons, front pockets with carefully tucked, oft used tissue, and a tasteful lacey collar. Canâ€™t offend the octogenarians â€“ my grandma lives in a retirement villa, thus The Church Outfit. I feel compelled to balance out my shitty, slid-down posture i manage to degenerate into around seven minutes into the sermon despite my best intentions.</P><P>\r\nSo, there i was, clomping through the hallway praying that my grandma is still alive. Yes, yes, even back then I prayed for some things. Death scares me. Or rather, the coming upon it does- as I did once as a kid. Iâ€™ll get to that later.  So, the clomping serves the purpose to alert my grandma that i have arrived.  i donâ€™t want to startle her. Heart attack paranoia I guess, as irrational as that sounds â€“ but do I sound like a rational person? </P><P>\r\nSo, back to me going down the hallway.  I peered into her room. No one there. \r\nI heard some scuffling on the outside porch and careened around the corner to investigate, envisioning masked bandits. But itâ€™s only grandma on the porch. However, i realize that something is amiss, because there she is - bareasstotraffic.  Granted itâ€™s seventy and eighty year olds who canâ€™t see that well, but the occasional one with sharpshooter vision drives by.  And she was utterly naked. OK, so i am somewhat prone to hyperbole. She still had her fuzzy pink slippers on. </P><P>\r\n	â€œSheâ€™s sunning her bottom,â€ my mother informs me later matter of factly, since i did not want to intrude on any kinky game my grandmother might have had going with a neighbor.  Grandma doesnâ€™t like to explain too much about herself and i figured iâ€™d never get details out of her anyway.  So, beating a hasty retreat, as some people say, i stumbled back out the front door to reenter â€“ louder this time. As i am waiting later for my grandmother to garb herself in her Church Outfit, i am thinking about how this would make a great beginning for a story. Letâ€™s see. â€œMy grandma likes to sun naked, ass to the air, cares and other things to the wind â€“ to hell with propriety.â€  I promptly forgot about it, although the feeling mustâ€™ve lingered and transferred to the sermon later, which happened to be â€œnarrating our lives late in life.â€  Except for one thing - I am relatively early in life and i am constantly narrating my life. At a quarter of a century, i have found myself pontificating how i will look back on my life and wonder if i am wasting my time reading, watching movies, or engaging in conversation. I mean, whatâ€™s the point if we are all going to die anyway? Granted, some peopleâ€™s conversations incontrovertible structures of academia and are taught in history and literature classes.  But for me, with my inconsequential life, should i even bother reading Brothers Karamazov, or should i just read the cliff notes? My mental exegesis on Brothers K would escort me to the grave, never to be heard of again.  Who gives a shit about what i think?  And should i bother to study other peopleâ€™s stories, or just stick to my own?  And regarding Cliff Notes, is the story more important than the beauty and intricacy of script? And regarding me, is my life story better written down in elaborate, twisting phrases or is it better to simply jot down the skeleton of the story: talked to tall, wry humored man at coffee shop, smoked two cigarettes, worked on graduate school essay, traded smiles with jaded barista â€“ beautiful smile by the way â€“ the sky-brightening, sparkly kind. See?  i canâ€™t even construct a basic rib cage. Right there, I had the overwhelming compulsion to add flesh and write more about That Smile. </P><P>\r\n	Anyway, a story began to unravel in my mind that Sunday, continuing with the arm circles that my grandma performs naked as well, her breasts an unashamed pendulum.  i was shocked and a little proud of her audacity. I was busily writing this scene down in my mind. Remember, this was before i realized someone else was scripting my life. Otherwise i would not have bothered. Someone was out there - pen to paper, watching me. Scraps of grocery bags, bar napkins, and toilet paper that someone was scribbling upon before transferring them to laptop, bit by bit e-mailed to various acquaintances of my ghostwriter, monitoring the pedestrian poems of my days.  Poems carelessly typed replete with mistakes highlighted in yellow, or sometimes underscored in red, with that damned paperclip bouncing for attention in the corner with that fucking light bulb glowing in itsâ€™ wake. Do you know which evil office implement i am talking about? I think itâ€™s Microsoft or something.  But i digress, as usual.</P><P> \r\nI believe it will be years before i meet my creator. As i said earlier - that comes later.  But there is this near-tangible feeling that tells me My Creator is nearby, a voyeur - of me and my Lucky Strikes. Everyone has always said that i am extraordinarily intuitive as well as a good listener. I guess i shouldâ€™ve listened to the invidious warning burying itself in my gut that day; the one that murmured that my life was not my own.  Couldâ€™ve saved me a lot of trouble and bad story beginnings.  Instead, i blithely sang hymns like a good granddaughter. I remember singing Thy Lamp is a Path Unto My Feet that day, not knowing that someone else was lighting my path. A beacon torpidly guiding a vessel towards the rocks, blocking out the lighthouse endogenous to my soul.  And it wasnâ€™t God, i can tell you that now.    \r\n</P>\r\n'),(2988,'<p>\r\nno grass in korea. no sprouts. just dirt. just ground where large sized korean style roughage grow. they grow in the tiny patches of earth still exposed in this city. this is literally the concrete jungle. ive seen little of what all of nature is while ive been here.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen we took the kids to play soccer on two days, we used the playgrounds of two area schools. the large playgrounds with soccer nets in them were void of any green. just gravel. lots and lots of gravel. knees bleed when there isnt any of that soft, fresh smelling when cut, green. grass isnt allowed to grow. it is illegal to grow or pocess grass in seoul. im sure of it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe went to a park once. a \\\'real\\\' park. with grass. with trails. with benches. with trees surrounded by grass. paying to get into this paradise within the urban sprawl of seoul, i was so looking foward to laying down on the grass and just staring at the open sky.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe grass was walled off by little walls you could step up onto. I could have just gone up and hogged all the grass to myself, but i could not. there were about fifty people in the park, and not one of them was sitting on the grass. Not one of them was even sitting under a tree. they were sitting on the benches which were positioned so as to give you the most sun in the park. was there no relief from this void of green?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nit wasnt until we met the new and mysterious english teacher.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non a saturday the rotation on the two hour shift is shared between me, shane, and tina. it was tina\\\'s turn and so she was the first one to meet the new and mysterious english teacher. she said he was cool, new, and mysterious. something about him told her he was part of something big. big and mysterious. so naturally we went out to sinchon with him to drink. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe introduced us to a much more dark and sordid side of seoul by way of oratory transmission. passing him on the street one might not give him a second glance. he was as normal looking as the next guy, but there was definatley something about him. something new and mysterious. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter turning down an offer to go see the nazi hangout, we ended up in a voo doo bar. indie rock posters plastered one ontop of each other; knick knacks tossed here and there. it was full of burly white men. the kind you dont often see roaming the streets of sinchon. i had a gin and tonic and kept one eye on the burlies and one on my drink. this bar was new to me, and yes, very mysterious. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe voo doo bar was so mysterious we all left for a much less mysterious place. mowong is a safe house for the gumoe posse. they know what we drink and they know when to walk away from a table having a mysterious conversation. the walls at mowong are thick and unrelenting. what we say, not matter how mysterious, is safe there. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe hid our true intentions within the common english teacher brovado. we snickered and plotted. we argued and bickered. the group often bickers due to the mysteriousness of the situation. it brings out the worst in us. adding a new and mysterious person to the equation could have spelled utter and complete death for us all. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit was raining and the mood of the night had become less than appealing. the mystery of it all was too great and i felt compelled to flee for the safety of gumoe. however so did mr mysteria. He cleverly plotted with himself to invite himself into my cab, which would normally have been generously excepted, yet tonight, the night of mystery, the greatest mystery yet was what would happen in the cab. what would i endure? on donner on blitzen. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"gumoe dong ka jue-say-oh\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"koom-nam-she-jang ka jue-say-oh\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"yaaaaa yeahhhhh\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe ride was quite interesting. in the four years mr mysteria, had been working in korea, he had worked under assumed names and without any legal permission. he was a wild man, living in a strange land witout anykind of protection. what had he seen? who had he known? he reminded me in an infinately small way to the cia operative in the quiet american. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshane had discussed with mr mysteria how grassless the city was. the koreans have let green space as an urban planning concept evade them, and while they have set up designated green areas in seoul, they are few and far between. green spaces are like red light districts. if they are too far for the masses to take advantage of, then street prostitution and drug use will continue. the same goes for green spaces. as soon as those green spaces are out of the reach of the masses, a criminal element appears; the green element. it was an element mr mysteria reluctantly accepted membership. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe cab moved along the pavement like a marble moving down a plank being tossed and turned by a child. mr mysteria calmly poked his head up into the cockpit of the taxi and requested information from the cabbie. speaking korean, mr mysteria added countless dimensions to the events and happenings of which he was responsible. when he leaned back and turned to me i flinched in my seat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"he says he\\\'s not drunk. just tired.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni got out of the cab. mr mysteria stayed in and continued on towards the sex motel he was staying at up on the other side of the school. im glad i got out first. im glad i got away. it was good to be once again alone. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na couple of days passed by without any word from mr mysteria. apparently mr mysteria had met with my boss and they had both created distrust of each other. the deal was off and so was mr mysteria. last i heard it was japan. we knew he\\\'d be back in korea. there wasnt a doubt anywhere. i knew id be crossing paths with mr mysteria again; but what form that crossing might take i was still unaware. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwednesday. eta to the school for the third day of classes was around ten seconds. once again my mind on that walk to work filled with thoughts of green. of grass. of that fresh cut smell.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthose thoughts quickly dissipate as i enter the \\\'we can do it\\\' academy. instead of those cheery thoughts of green. of grass. of the fresh cut smell, my thoughts turn to the \\\'oh how i dislike the ground floor of this building\\\' kind of thoughts i have when i enter the school on a wednesday.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is a phone call for shane. someone calling himself \\\'turf\\\' is making a call on behalf of mr mysteria. im not sure what kind of negotiations went down that day, as i only became privy to this information towards the end of the day. However i knew of the operation. operation \\\"green-retrieval\\\". having grown up on the green enriched ground of canada, with lots and lots of green space per capita, i knew little of what kind of operation i was now committed to. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat the strike of ten to ten on my classroom clock operation green-retrieval had become official. it had now started. we packed our things and made sure we had what we needed, locking and loading just before escaping the \\\'dons\\\' place.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe knew we were not safe using public transit that night, so we hopped in a cab and made our way to eetaywon, an area of town notorious for its teethless russian hookers, starcraft junkies, long legs, cheap drinks, and sauced up military police. dodging in and out of shadows, both fixed and moving, we made our way to the seoul pub. the seoul pub was situated on the main eetaywon drag giving us a good sitrep whenever each other needed one. cautiously we made our way up into the pub; located on the second floor. with every turn we cut the pie like we did during that arms deal we undertook in beirut circa 84. we didnt want to take any chances. we didnt know who mr mysteria worked for. we didnt know if they had been cleared by the word on the street. we were walking into a situation blind. as we entered the pub my hand in reflex moved toward the back of my trousers. it wasnt until shane whispered the all clear that i took my hand from my back and put it back at my side where it would have naturally been. i was jittery. canned coffee and mysteria will cause hysteria if you dont watch out. it was a lesson i was learning as we went. it would not happen twice. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nturf was big fat and jollyish. he stood six five and loomed over us. i could have sworn he\\\'d been an mp back in the day, but as he shouted over the ricky martin it turned out he had been with \\\'the cause\\\' over on vancouver island during the whole klakwat sound revolutionary war. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhe was to be trusted.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit happened that he had grass for sale. it was natures black market he told us. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"they think they can put us down by taking away the earth. they think they can humble us into being sheep. sheep!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhis nostrils flaired and he kicked at the hardwood floor with his boots. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"EVEN sheep need to graise. even sheep need the earth!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'amen brother\\\' i thought as i sat down with a beer and lit a figo. pretending to check out the jukebox i kept an eye out for the nature police.\r\ncrafty sons-of-bitches the nature police are. dressed in the most convincing urban cammo youve ever seen, they pop in and out of walls with court orders so unexpetedly and without warning many other operatives in the business turf was in had fled to the safety of the united states and canada. not turf. he persevered. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe deal went down. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe finished our beers, like any other normal pub goer and left. you could feel the heat consuming the seoul pub. you could feel the eyes wondering what had just happened. operation green-rerieval was a sucess and we rejoiced once within the safety of the cab.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngot home. got out the glue stick. got out the lip balm. got out the scissors. and heaved on a phat  \\\"sigh of relief\\\" and snickered the rest of the night away.  \r\n</p>  '),(2989,'<p> Watching the Heimlich maneuver in action is both a fascinating and disturbing thing. It\\\'s not quite like how they show it on TV or in a movie. When you see it there, it\\\'s only a pale imitation. The force imparted upon the recipient is quite fantastic. I suppose it\\\'s the sense of panic that the hero feels in the time of delivery. It\\\'s a spectacular feat using the body itself to eject something that\\\'s causing choking. I could hardly believe it was happening when I saw my farther in the very same position.\r\n<p> At first out hero, who turned out to be named Bill, only tried to use his hands. A standard technique, used only on the smallest of choking objects. It was quite evident that this alone wasn\\\'t going to be enough.\r\n<p> I grew, along with the now expanding crowd of fellow restaurateurs, increasingly scared. In the panic someone did call for the paramedics, however I was too panicked to do anything but watch.\r\n<p> Finally Bill brought my dad\\\'s diaphragm to rest on the table\\\'s edge. This is the choice for expunging larger items using Heimlich\\\'s technique. It offers more leverage, can even be administered by the choking victim themselves, and allows the hero to deliver more force onto the diaphragm. Unfortunately, if done incorrectly, this force is also enough to crack ribs. This was now what I was fearing additionally to my father possibly asphyxiating.\r\n<p> Several thrusts and what seemed like hours passed. Bill kept screaming, my father\\\'s face began to turn purple. What had my dad tried to swallow? I had been sitting directly across from him, but now I could even remember what restaurant we were in, let alone what he, or eve I, had been eating.\r\n<p> And then something snapped. \\\"It must have been a rib,\\\" I remember thinking to myself. The fear subsided a little, at least I wouldn\\\'t have to worry about a rib breaking now, it happened. At this very same moment, something else must have come loose. Bill dropped my father to the floor, at which point my dad began to cough. A sure sign of air passing out of the lungs. He coughed again and the food that was lodged in throat tumbled out. He vomited, rolled over and the crowd moved in.\r\n<p> It pulled me in closer with it. At this point I could see what I was that had been choking good ol\\\' pop. God knows how he managed to get the whole thing in his mouth. Sitting in a puddle of undigested dinner was a whole spare rib.\r\n'),(2990,'\r\n<p>\r\nAt four forty two in the afternoon, Earnest, Shackleton and Partners called in a fifteen minute rush, a legal filing that needed to be on the nineteenth floor of the Federal Building on Golden Gate avenues before five o\\\'clock. \r\n<p>\r\nThere had been no tags on the board for the better part of an hour. It had been quiet all day. I had made less than forty dollars and the rush job would pay nearly half that. I thumbed the radio that hung from the shoulder strap of my messenger bag. \\\"One five seven for the Earnest Shackleton,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nThere was a burst  static interference as I keyed off. Pfunk, the dispatcher, said, \\\"Ah, ten nine? Who was that?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nThe radio chirped. Tony Express countercalled me. \\\"One three one for the Earnest Shackleton. I\\\'m at Sansome and Sacramento. What\\\'s your twenty, Marshall?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Sorry, T.X, I\\\'m at Sansome and Green,\\\" I was said. I was four blocks closer to the pickup.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Ah, ok.\\\" His voice was tinny on the radio. \\\"Dick.\\\" When things were busy, the messengers on our board displayed teamwork and selflessness that would make a Marine DI teary eyed. But when things were slow, we were like starving sharks prowling shallow coastal waters. \r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Ah, one five seven <I>Marshall</I> for the Earnest, Shackleton and Partners,\\\" Pfunk said. \\\"A fifteen minute <I>rush</I> to the court on Golden Gate. Your contact is <I>Akiko</I> on the fifth floor. Be advised, one five seven, that these high level negotiations have left you with thirteen minutes to complete this tag.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI was already hauling ass up Sansome street. \\\"One five seven, ten four,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nA minute and a half later I pushed my bike through the double doors that opened onto the spacious lobby of Earnest Shackleton. I propped my bike against the front desk and winked at the security guard, who half rose out of his seat, his mouth hanging open. By the time he found the voice to protest this astonishing breech of protocol I was already in the elevator and heading up to the fifth floor.\r\n<p>\r\nAkiko was waiting for me when the elevator doors opened. I hit the button for the lobby. She handed me a fat brown envelope and said, \\\"This has to go to the Federal Building on the nineteenth floor--\\\" The closing doors cut her off. I muttered, \\\"Yeah, yeah, yeah.\\\" It irritated me when clients, assuming I was retarded and/or stoned, belabored the obvious. \\\"Hey, I graduated magna cum laude,\\\" I told the elevator doors.\r\n<p>\r\nWhen they slid open I ran back into the lobby and grabbed my bike. The security guard was shouting, <I>\\\"You have to sign in!\\\"</I> but I was already through the doors and in the saddle. The cleats on my bike shoes <I>kshnakk</I>ed as they locked into the pedals. I looked at my watch. I had twelve minutes to ride seventeen blocks to Federal Building, pass through a security checkpoint, and get upstairs.\r\n<p>\r\nI took Battery Street at a full sprint, standing in my pedals. It was a long gradual slope back downtown and I shifted rapidly, running through gears until I was on my tiniest chainring and in danger of spinning out. I overtook cars going the posted speed limit of thirty miles an hour.\r\n<p>\r\nThe slope steepened to a hill after Broadway and I slalomed through thickening rush hour traffic. I came up hard on a lumbering Muni bus and was boxed in on my left by a slow moving grandma in an ancient Volkswagon Rabbit. Finally I couldn\\\'t wait any longer and jerked my bike to the left, standing in the pedals and hammering through the bare two feet of space between her front bumper and the back of the bus. I heard her brakes squeal as she overreacted and the petulant bleating of her horn and then I was a block away. \r\n<p>\r\nMy legs were sheathed with muscle and felt like steel springs. My lungs burned like I was breathing ether instead of air. I was a manual laborer doing low-paying, unskilled work, but I was elated by the effort and speed and hair trigger strategizing. I was rafting blacktop rapids. I was running with mechanized bulls. I surfed a roaring metal wave that threatened to pulverize me on the filthy concrete beaches of California St. I didn\\\'t care that I was making six dollars an hour. I forgot myself and all that was left was street <I>satori.</I>\r\n<p>\r\nI chased a late model Porsche into the right lane, preparing to turn on to Market and make the final sprint for the Federal Building. I checked my watch again. I had ten minutes to go. There was a flash of bright green under my wheels and I craned my neck to look back. A puddle of antifreeze disappeared under the bus behind me. Then I hit the dip where Battery met Market St and I pedalled hard into the turn.\r\n<p>\r\nMy bike slid out from underneath me, the tires slick with antifreeze. My feet popped out of the clipless pedals and I hit the macadam with a skull rattling thud. I skidded on my back across the trolley tracks that bisected the street. My lungs felt crushed and I couldn\\\'t breathe. I shook my head to clear my vision. There was a bowel liquifying <I>blaaaaaat</I> and I saw the Muni bus accelerating to beat the yellow light that threatened to close off the intersection. The driver didn\\\'t slow at all as he bore down on me, his horn blaring. I said, \\\"Ah, <I>fuck</I>\\\" and rolled out of the way. Then I remembered my bike, still laying alone and bereft in the path of the lumbering bus. I lunged for it, prostrate. I grabbed the front wheel, the spokes lacing between my fingers, and sat up and fell back, clutching my bike to my chest like a spindly aluminum baby. The bus roared by and screeched to a halt at the stop on the curb.\r\n<p>\r\nI groaned and struggled to my feet. No one stopped to help me. I staggered to the curb. My hips and spine ached. My elbows were ragged and bleeding. My legs were streaked with grime. \r\n<p>\r\nMy radio crackled to life. \\\"One five seven, one five seven,\\\" Pfunk said. \\\"Ah, Akiko is calling. They\\\'re very worried. <I>Progress</I> report, Marshall. What can I tell them.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Tell them I\\\'m walking in now,\\\" I said. \\\"Tell them it\\\'s all set.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Ah, one five <I>seven,</I> ten four,\\\" he said. \\\"Nicely done, nicely done.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI looked my watch. The glass was cracked and there were dark gray spots in the digital readout. It was four fifty two. I had eight minutes to make the filing cut off. I ran with my bike and leaped into the saddle.\r\n\r\nI charged every intersection on Market, heedless of the changing lights. I relied on Saint Christopher, patron saint of travelers and bike messengers, to provide security. I was risking my life to make someone else rich. I had a suicidal enthusiasm for capitalism. But it was more than that. I had committed to getting the tag done, and I was not going to fail. It was a matter of honor, which seems like a silly, made up virtue, but only to those who don\\\'t have it. I was Don Quixote on a roadbike.\r\n<p>\r\nI turned onto Golden Gate. The Tenderloin flashed by me. It was the rotting heart of the city. I dodged the junkies and stewbums that wandered into my path and pulled up outside the Federal Building. I locked my bike to a parking meter and ran inside, pulling my bike bag off and tossing it on the conveyer belt to be xrayed. I stood in line for the metal detector. I checked the time. Four fifty seven. I said, \\\"Fuck. Fuck. Fuck.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI went through the security gate and the warning buzzer went off, announcing the presence of unauthorized metal on my person. The uniformed security guard waved me over so he could run the remote detecting wand over me but I said, \\\"It\\\'s my belt!\\\", grabbed my bag off the belt and ran for the elevators. There a shout behind me and a closing elevator ahead of me. I jumped and slipped sideways through the sliding doors. I hit the button for the nineteenth floor and slumped against the wall. I pulled a bandana out of the cargo pocket of my shorts and wiped my sopping face. The suits in the elevator crowded away from me. \r\n<p>\r\nThe doors opened at four fifty nine. The cleats on my shoes scraped and skidded on the marble floor as I hobbled to the filing desk of the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals. I pulled the Shackleton filing out of my bag and slapped the envelope on the counter.\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman behind the counter peered over her glasses at me. She said, \\\"We\\\'re closed. No more filings today.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nMy stomach dropped. \\\"Lady,\\\" I said. I stopped and started again. \\\"Ma\\\'am. I almost got run over by a bus on the way here. My job is depending on this,\\\" I lied. \\\"Please. Help me out here.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman looked me over, at the sweaty grime on my face and the blood on my arms. She sighed and opened the envelope. She started rubber stamping the cover papers of each document. \\\"Does your mother know how you make your living?\\\" she said.    \r\n<p>\r\nI went to the bathroom and washed my face. I pulled my jacket out of my bag and pulled it over my racing jersey. I stowed my hat and hoped that I was disguised enough that the guard wouldn\\\'t recognize me on the way out. To be safe I exited through the doors on the Turk St side of the building and walked around the block to retrieve my bike. I radioed Pfunk that I was clean. He checked his computer and radioed back that there were no more tags on the board. \r\n<p>\r\nI rode back downtown,to the low wall at Sansome and Market streets that was the bicycle couriers\\\' unofficial break area, to wait for a tag. I killed half an hour reading the tiny copy of Merton\\\'s <I>Zen and the Birds of Appetite</I> I was carrying that day, while the other messengers laughed and shot the shit and smoked bowl after bowl of pot. \r\n<p>\r\nAt quarter to six I called in to Pfunk to tell him I was wrapping it up for the day. I unlocked my bike, stuck the U lock in the back pocket of my oversized black shorts, and pedalled off to meet Tsing Tao at the Stinky Gym.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Stinky Gym was what Tsing Tao called gymnasium in the basement of the Internation Center at Oak and  Van Ness. The International Center was a stately building, but once you walked past the faux Doric columns at the entrance and through the marble floored lobby and started down the stairs to the lower levels, you were assaulted by the combined stench of mildew, ammonia, and locker rooms that hadn\\\'t been cleaned since World War II. The air was humid from a swimming pool on the second level, and the iron plates and ancient cabled machines in the weightroom were covered in rust. It only cost twenty five dollars a month.\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao had asked me to teach her to lift weights shortly after we\\\'d arrived in San Francisco the year before. She\\\'s found a job waittressing at a local cafÃ© called the Bitterroot. It was the first menial job she\\\'d ever had, and she found her back aching and her legs shaky after days on her feet at work. One night we sat on our futon, and as I massaged her feet, I suggested she might have an easier time of it if she did some supplmental strength training. To my great surprise, she agreed. I already belonged to the International Center gym, so I brought her on a guest pass. She hated it and loved it and signed up the same day.\r\n<p>\r\nShe was waiting for me on the steps when I rolled up to the curb. I locked my bike to a no parking sign and painfully climbed the stairs. I kissed her and rubbed my cheek against hers, breathing in the smell of her skin. We had been together almost two years, and I was more in love with her than ever. It seemed like a violation of basic thermodynamic law.\r\n\r\nShe saw the raw scrapes on my arms and the bruises on my legs and said, \\\"What happened to <I>you?</I>\\\" I told her the story of the Shackleton tag and my Indiana Jones moment with the muni bus. She hugged me and said, \\\"<I>Oh my god.</I> Marshall, if you die on the job I\\\'ll fucking <I>kill</I> you.\\\" She kissed my cheek and hugged my neck. She said, \\\"Mmmmm baby what would I ever do without you?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"No worries about that,\\\" I said. \\\"I\\\'m too dumb to die.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nWe went inside and down to the locker rooms. I changed and went down one more level to the weightroom. Easy Pieces was working the heavy bag. He was a doddering Irishman in a watchcap, a wifebeater undershirt and plaid polyester leisure pants. He had leather gardening gloves on. He threw uppercuts at the bag, grunting \\\"Eeesh! Eeesh! Eeesh!\\\" with every punch. I said, \\\"Hey Easy!\\\" but he ignored me, reliving some fight staged long before my father was born.\r\n<p>\r\nThe Senescent Power Lifting Society was already hard at work, chalked up and strapped into their wide leather weight lifting belts. Towering old men with huge guts and athletic socks pulled up to their knees stood on either side of an Olympic barbell loaded with twelve 20 kilogram plates-with the bar, a load of over six hundred pounds. Under the bar was a four and a half foot tall samurai midget, his face contorted in a maniacal rictus. Each of his legs was as big as both of mine strapped togther. He sat back on his haunches, his thighs going past parallel with the floor, and then he exploded upward, the tendons in his neck vibrating like guitar strings. Veins flashed and pumped on every bit of his exposed skin. He said, \\\"HIIIIIIIIIII!\\\" and pushed the load until he stood upright. He took two steps towards the squat rack and his huge lifting partners picked the weight up off his shoulders and placed it back on the rack. Then they burst into applause. Miyamoto-san held the world record in two powerlifting events in the sixty-five and over bracket. I clapped along with them. Miyamoto-san\\\'s strength and virtuosity were almost supernatural. We cheered out of an atavistic urge to propitiate whatever it was that gave him such awesome power.\r\n<p>\r\nBig Mike looked over at me and said, \\\"Ah, there\\\'s the happy couple now.\\\" I turned and Tsing Tao stood beside me. \r\n<p>\r\nShe gave me a crooked smile. \\\"Are you ready for the big weights?\\\" she said. I said, \\\"Fuck yeah\\\" and we got to work.\r\n<p>\r\nSing loved lifting weights, and I loved lifting with her. Once she realized that she wasn\\\'t going to blow up into a vaguely feminized version of Jean Claude Van Damme, she abandoned the tiny two and three pound dumbells and joined me in my basic but intensive weight training regime. She was focussed and enthusiastic. We pushed each other. We had been meeting after work at the Stinky Gym three times a week for eight months, and it showed in the small muscles that knotted in her back and arms and the long lines of her legs. I would sometimes catch her admiring her new muscles in the mirrors that lined the weightroom wall. I\\\'d laugh at her, but I was so proud. She was more beautiful now than when we had first met. We were two years into our relationship, and sometimes I still got hard just looking at her trying to finish an exercise, jaw set, muscles trembling and straining.\r\n<p>\r\nThe old men of the Power Lifting Society loved her. She traded dirty jokes with Big Mike and Stewey and Hugo and made them promise to come visit her at the Bitterroot-which none of them ever did, to my knowledge. I wouldn\\\'t have held any dirty old man fantasies they may have had about her against them, but I don\\\'t think it was really about that.  She was the bright light of that rusty dungeon. Who doesn\\\'t like to turn his face to the warm sun on a cold day?\r\n<p>\r\nWe moved from exercise to exercise, working hard. Tsing Tao released the pull down bar and stood up from the machine. I took her place, changed the weight and started my reps. She said, \\\"I met this couple at the Root today. They seemed really nice. They\\\'re filmmakers. They\\\'re going to have a party on Friday and screen some of the movies they\\\'ve made.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI finished my set and stood up. \\\"That sounds like fun,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao tilted her head and stared at me. She said, \\\"You couldn\\\'t tell a decent lie to save your mom from a firing squad.\\\" She sat down at the machine and moved the pin to a weight twenty pounds heavier than the last one she had used. \\\"You should really try talking to people. They might not all be as smart as you, Marshall, but that doesn\\\'t mean they\\\'re not worth knowing,\\\" she said. \\\"I hate it when we go places and you just lurk around. It makes people uncomfortable.\\\" She stopped in midset and looked at me. \\\"It makes me uncomfortable,\\\" she said.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I try, Sing, I try,\\\" I said. I felt feeble. \\\"I dunno, when we go to parties and I try to talk to people, half the time I feel like we\\\'re not even speaking the same language. I try to get involved in conversations and people get this glazed look in their eye.\\\" I mimed it for her and she laughed.\r\n\r\nShe said, \\\"That, my love, is because nobody but you and Huston Smith gives a shit about the parallels between certain statements of Meister Eckhart and the view of reality described by the Upanishads.\\\" She knew me well enough to insult my interests with esoteric references. I knew she was right, and that pissed me off, but I still had to laugh.\r\n<p>\r\nShe finished her set and stood up again. \\\"We\\\'re not in Amherst anymore,\\\" she said. She wiped sweat from her forehead. \\\"We moved here, so let\\\'s make a life here. I\\\'ve been meeting the most interesting, creative people, and I think you would really like them if you gave them half a chance.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I will, I promise,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"You just need to loosen up a little, baby. A beer or two won\\\'t kill you, you know. It\\\'ll just help smooth things out a bit.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I know, I know,\\\" I said. I got back under the weights.\r\n<p>\r\nWe worked through exercises for the broad muscles of the back and the inverted V\\\'s of the triceps that wrapped the upper arm. We did sit ups and leg lifts and Sing barked at me like a little Eurasian drill sergeant. Sometimes I thought that maybe I had trained her a little too well. When we were done, our bodies cleaned out and exhausted, we showered and went to the Rainbow grocery to shop for dinner.\r\n<p>\r\nI had about forty dollars in the bank, and Tsing Tao had fifty dollars in cash she had made from the weekend brunch crowd. It had to last us for more than a week and a half. We wandered through aisle after aisle of gourmet hippy food. We bought a dollar\\\'s worth of pasta and some meat-substitute balls and a jar of organic marinara sauce for a total of five dollars and seventy eight cents. I thought of the Shackleton tag, worth almost twenty dollars alone, and tossed in a box of tofu ice cream bars. Sing tried to frown severely but failed. I shrugged and hip checked her and said, \\\"Hey man, life is short.\\\" She tried to give me a charley horse in return and we play fought all the way to the checkout line. We paid for our purchases and rode our bikes home.\r\n<p>\r\nWe rode up 17th and turned left onto our street. Tsing Tao waved to the prostitutes working the corner. We pulled up outside our place at 422 Capp Street. I carefully walked my bike through an exposed mine field of discarded needles and used condoms. I shouldered both of our bikes. Tsing Tao took the groceries up the stairs and unlocked the steel gate that kept our doorstep from being another semi-private pissoir.\r\n<p>\r\nOur apartment was little more than a large studio. We slept on a futon on the floor of a walk-in closet. We had another futon, folded into a couch, and a coffee table and a tv on an overturned milk crate, and that constituted our living room.\r\nSing put the groceries down on the tiny bit of shelf space next to the stove and put a pot of water on to boil. I hung our bikes on hooks I\\\'d mounted in the ceiling and then washed my hands and started frying up the tempeh balls.\r\n<p>\r\nWhen dinner was ready we sat on opposite ends of the couch and watched TV while we ate, the soles of our bare feet touching. I was usually too exhausted from riding all day to go out more than once or twice a week, and we were too poor to afford more than that anyway. Tsing Tao went out with friends from work occasionally, but I was always happiest when it was just the two of us alone together, renting videos or reading to each other. \r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao woke me with her hand on my chest. I didn\\\'t remember nodding off. My plate of pasta was gone. She kissed my cheek and said, \\\"Come on, sleepyboy. Let\\\'s go to bed.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI went to the bathroom to pee and brush my teeth. Then I turned off the light over the stove and the television in the living room and felt my way in the dark to our bed closet. I slipped under the sleeping bag we used as a blanket and found Tsing Tao naked and waiting.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Shocking!\\\" I said, and she kissed me through my grin. I slipped an arm underneath her, drawing her to me, and ran my other hand lightly over her taut nipple and the soft ridges of her ribcage. I touched her hip and her thigh and even in the dark she was a miracle of curves and angles. I kissed her throat and felt her pulse against my tongue. When I slipped an experimental finger inside her she gasped and arched her back.\r\n<p>\r\nWe had made love hundreds of times. I was too tired to be adventurous or inventive. I did the things I knew she liked. I was gentle at first, touching her like she was an instrument best played with tongue and fingertips. When I was rough it was because I knew it was what she wanted and when I was tender it was because I adored her. \r\n<p>\r\nI had no belief in god. Not in the sense of an omnipotent intelligence that had any interest in my well-being. How could I, after four years of studying religion in the academy? I had gone looking for something to believe in, something to take comfort in, and I had come back with nothing. But being inside her, watching her face when she came, hearing her breathe my name in my ear-for a moment, I believed. Divine grace was this girl in my bed. When I came, crushing her in my arms, I imagined my semen taking root in her. <I>There are worse things,</I> I thought. As we drifted off to sleep I rolled onto my side. Tsing Tao snuggled up behind me and threw her leg over mine. She whispered that she loved me, and I thanked god for my life. We slept like happy children.\r\n'),(2984,'<p>\r\nSince the beginning, living in Ireland has been a somewhat challeging experience. An uneasy feeling. The best way to describe it is this:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>Ireland is exactly the same as the UK only completely different</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut just recently i think i\\\'m beginning to understand something fundamental about living here. You have to change your way of thinking. Your way of looking at the world. That, i think, is where i\\\'ve been going wrong for the last 18 months.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGrowing up in a densely populated urban environment, you become accustomed to being anonymous. being rude to strangers is <i>ok</i> cus you\\\'ll never ever see them again right? In the city, you are nobody. A faceless face. This is not necessarily a bad thing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>The poulation of Ireland is a mere 5 million(ish).</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn a sparsely populated rural enviroment. A small town. A village. You <i>are</i> somebody, and everyone wants to know who. Thigs you do. Things you say. Things matter in a small town. This is not necessarily a bad thing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy exhaust fell off this morning. I took my car to an exhaust centre which is a customer of the computer company i work for.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>Hello Jamie</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPeople remember you. People don\\\'t meet so many thousands of people, thousands of faceless faces on a daily basis. So people remember you. This is nice.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need to slow down and accept that this isn\\\'t England. A McDonalds meal takes 5 minutes (you take a seat and they bring it to you). Internet access is through 1950s analog phone exchanges. Things happen more slowly here...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd that\\\'s ok\r\n</p>'),(2985,'<P>\r\nI lost God for the first time when i was seven.  Seven was a magnificent year for me.  I had been uprooted from the childhood warmth of Florida where plants thrived even hidden under shaded bushes by careful seven year old hands.  I was devastated to leave my pumpkin patch, but i had no choice. Thus, i was dumped into Midwest winter, snow men and all.  In Florida, i had plenty of friends and never once looked into a mirror, nor can i recall ever thinking about my appearance.  Introspection lay dormant until we left Florida, and ultimately, destroyed the last remnants of oblivious joy. After the move, i found myself huddled in the bricked corner of the playground of my new school, suddenly aware of my ugliness - my otherness. I was unpopular. The Ugly Duckling story in reverse.  Self-loathing aside, my seventh year was also a year for firsts. My first nuclear war nightmare.  My first bloody nose. My first year living without my mother.  My first dead body.</P><P>\r\nThe bellicose dreams began pleasantly enough with plump robins tweeting merrily away in luminous magnolia trees. Then the dream would go into slow motion.  Everything would disintegrate into ash, the way the TV showed death would be if a nuclear bomb ever exploded.  When the clouds exploded i could hear every screaming bird and smell the macabre scent of cindered flowers.  The smell of the blossoms carried over and became the smell of death when i was awake.  To this day, i abhor magnolias. At the end of my dream, I would be left alone in the world clutching my Strawberry Shortcake lunchbox, watching my hands turning blackly transparent.  Fucking bleak.</P><P> \r\nThe bloody nose wasnâ€™t that big of a deal except for the revelation that there are physical repercussions for simple misunderstandings. A boy had punched me because he thought i had cut in line at recess during an intense four-square game. I hadnâ€™t of course; my sense of fairness extends as far back as i can remember.  I was the pigtailed girl you made fun of for defending all the kids in the â€œretard class.â€  Anyway, it was bound to happen to someone since the boy was always drawing pictures of fighter planes and missiles â€“ he was born to sock people for no reason. Sometimes I wonder who he is now and if he still has that sticky-uppy red hair and a penchent for sketching death machinery.  And here is the moral of that story as i could figure out: from his actions, I learned to apply the Simple Misunderstanding Resulting in Injury lesson to the insipid wars i became aware of as i neared my eighth birthday. Countries were fighting against countries because of the misperception that people were cutting in line.  As for the bloody noses that spontaneously occurred after the four square incident, well, i grew out of them finally, and looking back, i am thinking that perhaps there is a link to the dead body. Iâ€™ll let you decide what My Creator was thinking. It would be a handy motif for a story, donâ€™t you think?  </P><P>\r\nAnd as for my mom, I donâ€™t remember her leaving, i only remember her not being there.  And also, that the dead body was a result of her not being there. The dead body was the culmination of all the events that year, and most importantly, the catalyst that killed off God. The apocalyptic nightmares, bloody nose, and my momâ€™s unremembered departure were only the topsoil substantiating Godâ€™s murder.  Rich layers of earthen evidence covered Godâ€™s casket, hammering it deeper into the earth each day.  </P><P>\r\nThe dead body. My prozac-enhanced father had begun dating a wildly energetic woman named C.  My dad would often leave me with Maura, my babysitter with her beloved frizzy ringlets. I loved her glossy lipstick and matching toenails.  â€œThe color of bloodâ€ she would joke, waggling her feathery eyebrows and winking her frosted green lids at me.  Her nail polish reminded me of the time i smashed into a break-in-case-of-emergency fire extinguisher casing and ran back to my house saturated in blood. There was something mesmerizing about the bold color of my blood, turning my inside outside.  The color is reminiscent of those vividly poisonous frogs i would see in biology books discussing â€œnatureâ€™s visual messages.â€  </p><P>\r\nâ€œBeware!â€ their slimy skin seems to shout.  Except is there anything scary about a screaming child covered in blood?  My mom didnâ€™t seem to think so.  By the time i got back to my house, i was drenched.  All the way down my Pac-man t-shirt and my yellow terry cloth shorts. It was proof that i was human, though.  i have doubted it often, but i am clinging to that bloodied vision of me for certainty of my humanity.  And now that i think back to Mauraâ€™s description of her color choice, My Creator, or MC as i have come to affectionately call him or her, seems to suspiciously enter the picture once again.  It would be handy symbolism for MC. Or all-too-obvious symbolism, at any rate.  MC is a cheesy writer.  But wait, thatâ€™s my life i am talking about.  </P><P>\r\nAnyway, Maura and i would watch cartoons together with a dented tin bowl of popcorn, although i donâ€™t remember her ever eating any of it. She was always zooming off to the bathroom during that Roadrunner show, and come back sniffling. I used to think she cried for Wile E. Coyote in there, and i would feel a bond with her because the bathroom was always my place of solace as well, although i didnâ€™t feel bad for Wile E â€“ he was at best, irritating.  Maura would race back and plop down next to me, meticulously picking up the lint and dust molecules only she could see in the carpet and make her miniscule pile of fluff, taking it with her on the way back to the bathroom. </P><P>\r\nOne Friday night when Dad and C were off to watch a movie, she didnâ€™t come out of the bathroom.  I peeked in after shoving past her nail-polished feet, which were up against the door. She was sprawled on the daisy-tiled floor, with a glossy lipstick smear under her nose. </P><P>\r\nâ€œMaura, you have lipstick on your nose,â€ i had laughed, and bent to wipe it off with a ghost of toilet paper that was lying next to her hand. She didnâ€™t laugh.  i realized that the lipstick was a lot runnier that it usually was.  And we all know what comes next.  Somehow i figured out it was blood. Real blood â€“ unlike her Cover Girl lip gloss. I ran to the phone crying, spilling the popcorn bowl along the way and adding a few more dents, and called 911 like my Dad instructed me to do in case of emergency. Two weeks later i was punched in the nose and the spontaneous nose bleeds commenced.  </P>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2986,'<P>\r\nI am directly responsible for at least three peopleâ€™s misery.  For those of you who cannot handle my anachronistic style, i shall give you hints along the way. For example, this particular narration takes place in the present. I am not seven, i am 25. i am sitting at my normal coffee shop, drinking my normal drink, and smoking my usual smokes. I just got off the phone with my ex-boyfriend, Dorian. Heâ€™s the first recipient of my guilt. He has no qualms detailing his tortured daynighteverysecondofhislife misery.  It makes me feel like shit. I moved out to California to help take care of my mom and grandma and to escape Dorianâ€™s cerulean-teared eyes for which i am culpable. Granted, everyone creates his own feelings of anguish, but i am the object of Dorianâ€™s despair.  Cupid is glaring at me from across the street for breaking his divine arrow. But hey, even demi-gods make mistakes, right?  Is Cupid a demi-god? Hmm. Heâ€™s a fatass, armed cherub anyway, so maybe i should watch out. So yeah, even cherubs fuck up every once in a while.  And i am busily making a mess of his work everywhere i go, it seems.  </P><P>\r\nSo, on the phone with Dorian, in between his wails of howlostheiswithoutme, he explores the creepy mystery of how i managed to inadvertently destroy my motherâ€™s happiness while intentionally trying to make her life easier by helping her care for the elders scattered around California.  Thatâ€™s the story line of the afterschoolspecial genre. MC has been busy plotting a made-for-TV movie of my life. i can tell. </P><P>\r\nSo that makes three.  Dorian, my mom, and her boyfriend, Dale. Um, ex-boyfriend. Thereâ€™s a chip-in-Dale. Oâ€™ i like to amuse myself.  Dale needs his own goddamned chapter because he thinks heâ€™s so unique.  I could write a book on the attachment disorder permeating that man, like a college psychology case study.  Me - i wish i was like everyone else. I guess i suffer from the same delusion that i am unique as well.  I guess we all do. Isnâ€™t that everyoneâ€™s secret fear?  That we are all alike?  I am unique, though.  I know i am unique.  Or are other people writing your story too?  Can you be so sure that you donâ€™t have an MC as well?  Iâ€™ll let you mull that one over. </P><P>\r\nAnyway, Dorian was wondering how he could ask if Dale had tried â€œputting the moves on meâ€ without sounding like the jealous ex. I can tell these things because in the same breath he asked if i was dating anyone yet. And no, he hadnâ€™t. Dale, i mean. I tried to explain the psychological crap behind perception and how simply my mom thinking that Dale wanted me pretty much screwed any chance of them ever trusting each other again. There was a lot of resentment there. On both of their sides. And then there was me. I was described as â€œthe catalyst, but not at faultâ€ for the End of Their Relationship, and other meant-to-comfort-me phrases such as, â€œwho knows, maybe it would have happened if you hadnâ€™t moved out here anyway.â€  Does wonderful things for the self-esteem. </P><P>\r\nSo, i move here to help, i completely and utterly destroy my motherâ€™s life, and as a result, have my mother depending on me for support.  Dale needs a friend, too because he is so allaloneintheworld.  Dorian needs my support as well. I am that pillar that holds everyone up except that i am the one that knocked everyone down in the first place. Itâ€™s insanely and tragically ironic. </P><P>\r\nSitting out in the creamy sunlight, I contemplate the last Lucky Strike in my pack, afraid to smoke it because my Lucky One had been taken by the Man in the Red Converse.  Is it bad luck to smoke a last one, which wasnâ€™t supposed to be The Last One? Where does a girl go for the authority on these matters?  And what the hell. Here comes Red Converse Man sans briefcase -  his suit is now crinkled. Iâ€™ll be back.</P><P>\r\nHmmâ€¦vaguely cute. Heâ€™s an unemployed computer programmer and he has been out hunting for jobs and a little weary for it. I tried to tell him that maybe he should try to get some different shoes, but he likes to advertise a bit of himself, he said.  Caught my eye, anyway.  Supplied my moniker for him.  Red Converse man smoked my last cigarette, so i guess iâ€™ll never find out what happens. Does that cancel out his luck from my Chosen One? Or does that kind of luck not transfer to other people. Iâ€™ll have to see what MC decides for me. </P><P>\r\nAt any rate, listening to my mom has brought me back to the way i handled the break up with Dorian. I see the asshole way that Dale has dealt with my mother, and i see that it is the same way i discussed the end of our relationship with Dorian. Prevaricating in order to soften the blow, whereas i prefer veracity for myself. Quite the hypocrite i am.  I told Dorian today i wasnâ€™t dating anyone, but i just accepted a date from Red Converse Man. His real name is Wade, by the way.  </P><P>\r\nDorian is suicidal, though.  And so is my mom â€“ thus Daleâ€™s way of cushioning and drawing out the pain.  I can relate.  I am an asshole.  And now that i am aware of MC - i am afraid MCâ€™s idea of adding drama and intrigue to my story would be to have Dorian kill himself.  I guess Mauraâ€™s death adds a dramatic twist, but who knows when MC took over from my Real Life.  Perhaps her death was not part of my story, but part of my Real Life.  That makes it almost inevitable that Someone will have to die.  And soon.  Life is not exciting enough for the readers, i feel.  And anyway, it seems everyone in my life has the desire to off themself. Someone is bound to die sooner or later.  Especially if my life is Made-for-a-TV-Movie.  \r\n</P>'),(2987,'<P>\r\nWade is 6â€™1 with these quirkysmiling plum-colored lips and wide green eyes.  Reminds of the book Wide Sargasso Sea for some reason. Never read it and not sure if that is even the correct title -but for some reason I picture the sea being the exact color of his eyes.  They are remarkable, really â€“ well, obviously since I just made a comment about them. So Wade and I are sitting down outside the coffee shop during that lovely crepuscular time of evening.  I love that word.  And yeah, I just wanted to use it.  Whatever - itâ€™s dusk.  I switched to Thai coffee because itâ€™s a little chilly and my boots just arenâ€™t keeping me warm enough.  Wade is wearing a cute thrift store t-shirt thatâ€™s a little tight and has chipped black nail polish. The sexiest thing on a guy -for me, anyway.  Heâ€™s talking about his paintings, which sound pretty incredible, although he seems a bit shy about the whole thing.  I am afraid to venture my theory about having a Life Scriptor and how I am a slave to some Creator somewhere.  Shit, for all I know, MC is sitting at that corner table in the cut off jeans and ugly white high tops.  Incidently, the corner table guy is scribbling madly on coffee cup sleeves and he keeps looking over this way.  Could it be him? I canâ€™t help but think it is a male person.  Who else would make my life this miserable but a man. Um, but back to Wade and the date.  He really enjoys the solitude of fishing and has this idea to thickly layer silvery scales upon a canvas with oil paint, and then watercolor the people he likes to paint - over it so that the metallic sheen shows through like a primordial reminder of where we came from. I like to think of myself as a fish sometimes. Makes me shiver. I told this to Wade and this seems to make his eyes twinkle more than usual. Maybe he thinks i am deep. They usually do â€“ guys I mean. They donâ€™t realize how shallow i am. How the fuck can he see through those disco balls of sea water anyway? Maybe he thinks i am deep. They usually do â€“ guys i mean. They donâ€™t realize how shallow I am. So. To make a long story short, the date was mellow, he was sweet, and we are planning to go out again.  He said heâ€™d call. \r\n</P>'),(2991,'<p>I was with my nephews craftmaking again this past sunday. They were young so I handed them a boxcutter to use to cut the cardboard. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Not too young to hurt themselves, but old enough to feel it\\\'s clean glide through the surface of the material.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There arms were quick and clean and impervious to scars it seemed. Their faces vacant of wrinkles of regret.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Puerile and prepubescent but could they make the cut when it mattered most.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>We made swords and shields guns and aeroplanes of cardboard and tossed the excess material into the recycling... I reached the boxcutter to the top of the highest shelf where their eyes could not see, and set to fighting them with paper weapons and paper planes to dive bomb our afternoon blues away.</p>'),(2992,'my horoscope says that today i will have trouble expressing myself with words. it\\\'s a good thing i don\\\'t believe in that--otherwise i might be in trouble.\r\n<p>\r\nschool is beginning again. the campus is overrun with young confused-looking faces. we are accepting 600 more freshmen at USC this year; like it wasn\\\'t crowded enough before. this is also the beginning of my senior year of college. looks like it will jusssst be getting unbearable as i am leaving. lucky me. i tried to fight my way through the throng earlier to buy books, but it\\\'s just too hot. i didn\\\'t even make it halfway across campus before turning back towards the lab. this year i will be smart--i will buy books first thing in the morning before most people are out and about.\r\n<p>\r\nthe new freshman kids are SO SURE about what college is supposed to be like. you can see it in their faces and hear it in their voices. some of them will ruthlessly beat their college experience into a reasonable facsimile of the experience presented on MTV (which is, to most of these kids, what the catholic church is to the Poles). they will rush to join a frat or sorority, keeping late hours performing degrading feats of bravery. my thoughts on the matter, from a distance--it\\\'s too bad for the new crop that shows like Fear Factor are so popular. my prediction: there will be lots of disgusting things eaten--and i\\\'m going to go ahead and call it now--sometime this fall, there will be a national news story about kids eating disgusting things and/or great quantities of something until vomiting occurs. i\\\'d like to rant about how much i hate those shows on tv, but jeez--it\\\'s just tiresome to think about it, isn\\\'t it?\r\n<p>\r\nsome kids may smell something foul in all these proceedings, not just greek stuff per se, but also in the formation of the cliques. this will lead either to the forging of strong character, or the constant questioning of one\\\'s worth. wait--maybe they go hand in hand. maybe you can\\\'t have one without the other. maybe. they will not find many like-minded classmates.\r\n<p>\r\nprobably the thing that bothers me the most about the &quot;new college culture&quot; is the total lack of emphasis on learning anything. courses are seen as either obstacles to be gotten over, or at best opportunities to show the rest of the class how smart one is. it\\\'s little wonder that some professors take such an adversarial position right away, but this unfortunately in my experience seems to be a vicious cycle, one feeding off the other.\r\n<p>\r\nso where does this leave me? my decision rather late in the game to go to law school puts me in the position of having to get A\\\'s in everything, which is distracting. i\\\'m 25. i feel like a ghost when i am at school, preoccupied, head down walking the sidewalks to class, seeing but a few familiar faces here and there. in and out quickly every day, then back to my lonely apartment. a true <i>student</i>--a seeker of knowledge--an observer. there\\\'s just no other way for me to keep my soul in this sea of conformity. this will <b>not</b> be the generation that changes the status quo.'),(2993,'my horoscope says that today i will have trouble expressing myself with words. it\\\'s a good thing i don\\\'t believe in that--otherwise i might be in trouble.\r\n<p>\r\nschool is beginning again. the campus is overrun with young confused-looking faces. we are accepting 600 more freshmen at USC this year; like it wasn\\\'t crowded enough before. this is also the beginning of my senior year of college. looks like it will jusssst be getting unbearable as i am leaving. lucky me. i tried to fight my way through the throng earlier to buy books, but it\\\'s just too hot. i didn\\\'t even make it halfway across campus before turning back towards the lab. this year i will be smart--i will buy books first thing in the morning before most people are out and about.\r\n<p>\r\nthe new freshman kids are SO SURE about what college is supposed to be like. you can see it in their faces and hear it in their voices. some of them will ruthlessly beat their college experience into a reasonable facsimile of the experience presented on MTV (which is, to most of these kids, what the catholic church is to the Poles). they will rush to join a frat or sorority, keeping late hours performing degrading feats of bravery. my thoughts on the matter, from a distance--it\\\'s too bad for the new crop that shows like Fear Factor are so popular. my prediction: there will be lots of disgusting things eaten--and i\\\'m going to go ahead and call it now--sometime this fall, there will be a national news story about kids eating disgusting things and/or great quantities of something until vomiting occurs. i\\\'d like to rant about how much i hate those shows on tv, but jeez--it\\\'s just tiresome to think about it, isn\\\'t it?\r\n<p>\r\nsome kids may smell something foul in all these proceedings, not just greek stuff per se, but also in the formation of the cliques. this will lead either to the forging of strong character, or the constant questioning of one\\\'s worth. wait--maybe they go hand in hand. maybe you can\\\'t have one without the other. maybe. they will not find many like-minded classmates.\r\n<p>\r\nprobably the thing that bothers me the most about the \\\"new college culture\\\" is the total lack of emphasis on learning anything. courses are seen as either obstacles to be gotten over, or at best opportunities to show the rest of the class how smart one is. it\\\'s little wonder that some professors take such an adversarial position right away, but this unfortunately in my experience seems to be a vicious cycle, one feeding off the other.\r\n<p>\r\nso where does this leave me? my decision rather late in the game to go to law school puts me in the position of having to get A\\\'s in everything, which is distracting. i\\\'m 25. i feel like a ghost when i am at school, preoccupied, head down walking the sidewalks to class, seeing only a couple familiar faces here and there. in and out quickly every day, then back to my monastic apartment. a true <i>student</i>--a seeker of knowledge--an observer. there\\\'s just no other way for me to keep my soul in this sea of conformity. this will <b>not</b> be the generation that changes the status quo.'),(2994,'<P>\r\nthe two times he\\\'s thrown something at me, i noticed how he aimed them wrong on purpose, so they arced in my direction but hit the wall to one side of me.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nonce it was the alarm clock, clanging like a bunch of silverware against the floor behind the bed. and the other time, the car keys, hurled against the bathtub as i stood in the shower, hot water steaming my skin, my hands over my breasts. i cursed the faulty bathroom lock, made sure my naked body didn\\\'t tremble. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthrough this relationship i have discovered, for the first time ever, that there is something alien inside me. something that could have a forked tongue, a red tail, two horns. i goad him, i provoke him, i know where and how it will sting the most. i keep testing him, pushing to see how far i can go.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nbut last night? last night i saw him actually lunge toward me from across the room, his fingers curling dangerously, his teeth glinting, before he swerved toward the lamp, knocked it over. the bulb didn\\\'t make a sound as it broke, or maybe i just couldn\\\'t hear it. the darkness was strangely welcome.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni might have muttered little prayers under my breath, maybe shivered a little. outside the sky was being obliterated by a storm. the lightning was forked, the kind that actually touches the ground. \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nand i just stood there, in silence, as he crumpled to the tiles, as he lay there, breathing hard, at my feet. \r\n</P>\r\n'),(2995,'write something. fucking write something.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nat the dinner table when i was eight years old, my then new stepfather, in our small kitchen in our new house with no plumbing, told me he was going to hit me.  i was dry heaving on a pierogi that i\\\'d been refusing to eat, until he yelled at me to eat it.  i didn\\\'t like their texture.  he told me if i puked it up that he was going to smack me.  he was very angry and his face was very red, a face i would often see over the course of their marriage, more and more frequently near the end. an anger awkward with loss of control. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni didn\\\'t vomit.  i swallowed it down and cried while i did it, my face was red like his but it was warmed by humiliation, not anger.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nin that house, on barnesdale avenue, there were venetian blinds on the inside of the front door.  they were beige, and they covered the window in the top half of the wooden door.  the door was so heavy that inevitably it would always slam shut with too much momentum, and the blinds would slap a decaying puddle of noise as they came to rest by measured collisions with the window frame.  it gave me time to reflect on the departure of whomever it was that had left the house.  usually i hoped it was my stepfather.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif my hands aren\\\'t on the keyboard, i can\\\'t write anything.  i just stare down at my hands, and i don\\\'t write, not even in my head.  in my head i guess.\r\n'),(2996,'<i>hi. i need some help with the drug part in this. if you can correct the slang or offer details that would add to the verisimilitude (thanks tuggy) I\\\'d appreciate your help.</i>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nWhen I sat down at my desk I found five emails from the Destroyer sitting in my in-box. I didn\\\'t bother to open them. I selected them and hit the delete key. \r\n<p>\r\nThere was an email from the Huffer I couldn\\\'t ignore. I had missed one day last week and two this week. He wanted to know if I was having health problems. He wondered if my absence was impacting the code overhaul.\r\n<p>\r\nI chewed on my pen cap and considered how to respond. I knew Bianca had covered for me and completed the code reviews that I was responsible for. I double checked the spreadsheets that charted the project\\\'s progress. Everything seemed to be on schedule. Bianca wouldn\\\'t tip off the Huffer that she was had been carrying me for the last week. I would make it up to her later. I wrote the Huffer that there was no need for worry; my superior management skills had ensured the success of the project.\r\n<p>\r\nI tried to get some work done but I couldn\\\'t concentrate. I felt feverish and hollowed out. I resisted a powerful urge to crawl under my desk and sleep for the rest of the day. Instead I surfed the web and thought about Tsing Tao and Hondo. I hated to admit it to myself but I was terrified of him. I thought about how easily he had pinned me against the wall, rapping my head on the concrete blocks, and my stomach cramped in fear. I thought boxing had inured me somewhat to violence, but no matter how ferocious the contest, it was a controlled and supervised situation. Opponents were evenly matched. There were pads. There were rules. Hondo gave me a little taste of prison shower savagery. I was lucky he let me walk away. I was lucky I hadn\\\'t left on a stretcher. Or in a bag.\r\n<p>\r\nWhat would happen if I told Tsing Tao? Would she even believe me? She loved him. She loved him. How the fuck could she love him? I remembered the look on her face when I saw them standing together at the art show. I tried to remember a time when she had looked at me the same way. The effort made tiny black flowers of loss fill my lungs like smoke. I couldn\\\'t catch my breath. Suffocating pain bloomed in my chest. She had never looked at me that way. Tsing Tao had loved me. But now she was in love. \r\n<p>\r\nShe was in love with a guy who had threatened to kill me if I came near her again. I had never been threatened with death by someone I thought might actually be capable going through with it. I decided to lay that aside for the moment. Being killed was too abstract. It seemed like something that only happened to people in movies. I decided I would subsitute the verb clause \\\"kick the shit out of you\\\" for \\\"kill.\\\" Getting the shit kicked out of me was something I understood very concretely. Having a clear idea of the consequences of my actions made it easier to think about how to proceed.\r\n<p>\r\nI sat up from my office chair slouch. I had to tell her what had happened. Whether or not Hondo was really capable of murder, he was obviously very capable of acts of unpremeditated assault, and Tsing Tao wasn\\\'t safe around him. No matter how much she loved him or how much he thought he loved her, at some point she was going to be the object of his anger. I had to keep her safe. I was so tired. I rubbed my face and reached for my phone. My hand shook as I dialed her number.\r\n<p>\r\nI called her cell phone instead of her house. I was afraid Hondo might pick up there. \r\n<p>\r\nThe phone rang four times. Then the line connected. I heard clicks and thuds as someone tried to get the phone to his ear.\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"Hi?\\\" Her voice was sleepy and thick.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Sing. Hi,\\\" I said. \\\"Sorry to wake you.\\\" My head felt light. My breathing was shallow and quick.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, Marshall, hey,\\\" she said. \\\"What time is it?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI checked my watch. \\\"Um, eleven thirty.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, <I>shit</I>,\\\" she said. \\\"Goddammit. Fuck. I was supposed to open the Midtown this morning at eight o clock. Mickey Big Mouth is going to fucking <I>kill</I> me.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Agh, that sucks,\\\" I said. \\\"I should let you go. I\\\'ll call you later.\\\" I was so relieved I looked to the ceiling and lip synched <I>thank you.</I>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, no, that\\\'s ok,\\\" she sighed. \\\"I\\\'ll get the same shitstorm whether I call now or in an hour.\\\" She yawned. She said, \\\"Oooooh my poor head. So what happened to you the other night? You meet some hot girl and run off to do your nasty little business with her? I didn\\\'t see you leave. I was pretty pissed you didn\\\'t bother to say good-bye, actually.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"No, no girls,\\\" I said. \\\"Bianca got a little too drunk. She wasn\\\'t feeling well so I took her home.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, right, Bianca,\\\" she said. \\\"She\\\'s cute. Not really your type. But cute.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"We work together. We hang out a lot. There\\\'s nothing else going on. I can\\\'t believe I\\\'ve never told you about her.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, maybe you have, I dunno,\\\" she said. \\\"I was pretty lit, I have trouble keeping track of your harem in that condition.\\\" I heard her stretching, shifting around in her bedclothes. I imagined her nipples poking through her wife beater. My cock started to rise and I stared at my lap reproachfully. \\\"OH!\\\" she said. \\\"When are you going to come pick up your painting, Mister Anonymous Art Collector?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI had completely forgotten about it. There was a Van Halen coke mirror hanging on the wall of my cube. I looked at my reflection and a cartoon jackass stared back at me. \\\"Who told you about that?\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"There weren\\\'t many paintings sold, and I recognized the address on the credit card slip. Hondo was so happy. Someone said Nicholas Cage was walking around the opening in disguise and Hondo thinks it was him that bought it. I figured you had your reasons for not telling us so I didn\\\'t say anything. But it was really sweet of you. Are you sure you can afford it?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI cleared my throat. \\\"Well, uh, it was expensive, but I thought it was amazing. It\\\'ll probably be worth a lot some day. He is really talented.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I <I>know</I>,\\\"  she said. \\\"Sometimes I feel lucky just to be around him, like someday people will be reading my name in some book about him. He says I\\\'m his muse.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI mimed blowing my brains out with my thumb and forefinger. \\\"That\\\'s great, Sing. But listen, um, there\\\'s something I need to talk to-\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n<I>\\\"WHAT?\\\"</I> she said. I pulled the phone away from ringing ear. A distant male voice rumbled. She said, <I>\\\"NO, I DON\\\'T WANT ANY, THANK YOU BABY.\\\"</I> Then to me she said, \\\"Listen, Marshall, I need to ask you for a favor. Can you meet me tonight with your truck? We can pick up your painting from Spaganga on the way.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"On the way to what?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I just need to pick up some stuff from a friend,\\\" she said. \\\"I thought it would be fun to hang out with you. We don\\\'t get to spend enough time together anymore.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'re right, you\\\'re totally right,\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe told me to pick her up at nine o clock in front of the Midtown. I said I would and hung up.\r\n<p>\r\nBianca\\\'s head rose over the cubicle divider. She said, \\\"So how\\\'s Mrs. Picasso?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI grunted. \\\"Good one,\\\" I said. I turned back to my keyboard and pretended to work. I brought up Tetris and <I>click-click-click</I>ed little blocks of pixels into place. When I glanced up again she was gone.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"So where were you yesterday?\\\" she said. She leaned against the divider wall. She wore a t shirt that was decorated with a picture of a girl riding a fountain of beer. Her skirt flew up over her waist. The legend was <I>Duffy\\\'s Bar. Where tail and ale meet.</I> She said, \\\"There\\\'s shit piling up, Marshall. I can\\\'t do this by myself.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I was sick,\\\" I said. \\\"A twenty-four hour stomach thing. I\\\'m better today. We\\\'ll get caught up.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"Is that a variation of Mad Cow? Twenty-four hour Wild Turkey disease?\\\" She waved her hand in front of her face. \\\"I can smell it on you. Do you use that shit as cologne?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI kept my eyes fixed on the screen. \\\"You\\\'re extra funny today,\\\" I said. I bashed the keys.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m worried about you,\\\" she said. \r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"Thanks mom.\\\" I didn\\\'t look at her. I said, \\\"I\\\'ve just had a bad couple of days. Everything is cool now.\\\" I looked at her and smiled. \\\"Really. I\\\'m ok. I was sick. But I feel good today. I worked out with the Kid this morning.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"So I heard. Don\\\'t you have a little brother you could beat up? The Kid is too delicate for you to take out your romantic frustrations on.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI glared at her. I said, \\\"What are you talking about?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Nothing, Marshall, nothing.\\\" She rolled her eyes and folded her arms over her breasts. \\\"You won\\\'t tell me what happened at Spaganga, and that\\\'s fine. But <I>helllooo</I>. We go to visit your ex-girlfriend and you come out with a black eye, crying, running home like a kid who got beaten up after school. I can do the math. I\\\'m a programmer, remember?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI was shaking. \\\"Bianca,\\\" I said. \\\"Go back to your cube, please. Before I tell you to go fuck yourself.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nHer mouth fell open. She stared at me for a long moment. Then she turned around and left.\r\n<p>\r\nAt nine o clock I pulled up in front of the Midtown. Sing wasn\\\'t on the sidewalk. It was raining again, and every doorway on Haight St sheltered two or three crusty punks, dressed in medieval rags and roadwarrior spikes, huddled together for warmth. Panhandlers clutched their hooded sweatshirts closed with one hand and held the other out beseechingly to the few souls that scurried by.\r\n<p>\r\nSomeone was rapping on my window. I started to say <I>No spare change</I> but it was Sing. She ran around to the passenger side and I let her in. She said, \\\"Sorry I\\\'m late.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said it wasn\\\'t a problem.\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"I was in the Toronado. Mickey Big Mouth read me the riot act when I finished my shift. He was like, \\\'You\\\'ve got responsibilities, I trusted you and blah blah blah\\\' and I was like, \\\'Fuck, man, I was a couple hours late to work, it\\\'s not like I smoked crack in front of your kid or something.\\\' He was practically screaming. The spit was flying. I was like, \\\'Thanks, I needed a shower.\\\' Anyway I didn\\\'t really feel like hanging around the Midtown after that so I went across the street.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Why did you oversleep so much? Were you up late?\\\" I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe rolled down the window and lit a cigarette. She blew smoke into the dank night air and said, \\\"Oh, you know, we partied a bit last night. Hondo has been in a good mood since the show. Due to you and your credit card, most likely. I mean, he knows he\\\'s good, but there\\\'s nothing like making a sale to boost your confidence. Some people came over and we did some lines and we stayed up really, really late talking.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"I see.\\\" I put the SUV into gear and pulled into traffic. I said, \\\"So where are we going?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao said, \\\"Ok, we\\\'re going to Spaganga to get your painting, but first I need to pick up something from a friend of mine. I have to call him to find out where to meet him. It\\\'ll be in the Western Addition, though.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI turned right on Pierce and we climbed north on wet, empty streets. I said, \\\"We could just park and walk over.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nSing looked at me and said, \\\"Um, where we\\\'re going, it\\\'ll be better if we drive.\\\" She took out her cell phone and punched in a number. She said, \\\"Hello? Bayonne? What\\\'s up. Yeah, we\\\'re coming by. Where should we meet you? Ok. Five minutes? Yeah. See you, baby.\\\" She clicked off. \\\"Ok. Turk and Scott,\\\" she said.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"One of San Francisco\\\'s lovelier neighborhoods,\\\" I said. Sing didn\\\'t say anything. She stared out the window. Turk and Scott streets formed the corner of one of San Francisco\\\'s uglier housing projects. When I was messaging I knew a kid who had been jumped for his bike there. He was new, just three weeks out of the Marine Corps. Turk Street inclined to a steep hill after Scott, and he had paused on his bike to contemplate the intimidating climb. A gang of kids had swarmed him and beat him so badly he spent the next two weeks in the hospital.\r\n<p>\r\nWe crested the hill at Alamo Park and descended into the Western Addition. When we got to Turk St I turned left and we came to a stop at the next corner. There was no one there.\r\n<p>\r\nWe waited for a moment in silence. Then Tsing Tao said, \\\"Let\\\'s drive around the block. I don\\\'t think it\\\'s smart to sit here.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI waited for the light to change, then started driving up the hill. The windshield wipers squeaked as the rain slowed. I said, \\\"Why are we here, Sing?\\\" but I already knew the answer. \r\n<p>\r\nShe bit her fingernail. It was bleeding. \\\"I just need to get something from my friend Bayonne. Hondo\\\'s friends went through all our coke last night. It was supposed to last us all week.\\\"  \r\n<p>\r\n\\\"How long have you been laying in a week\\\'s supply at a time?\\\" I said. \r\n<p>\r\nShe stared intently into the dark recesses of the passing housing projects. \\\"It\\\'s usually not an issue. It\\\'s always around. Hondo deals a little bit to keep the cash coming in,\\\" she said absently. \\\"Wait! There he is. Pull over.\\\"\r\n <p>\r\nI did as she said. Tsing Tao got out of the truck and hugged a kid wearing enormous pants and a jacket four sizes too big for him. The pants draped over his unlaced workboots in elephantine rolls. His baseball hat was canted to forty five degrees, like he had slept with it on and just woke up.\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao opened the rear door and slid into the back seat. Her friend climbed in behind her and closed the door. His denim pants and jacket were the same shade of forest green. Tsing Tao said, \\\"Drive, James,\\\" and grinned at me in the rear view mirror. \r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"Sing, what-\\\" and she said, \\\"<I>Go,</I> Marshall,\\\" so I did. I said, \\\"Where to, ma\\\'am?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Just around the block a couple times,\\\" she said. \\\"Marshall, this is my friend Bayonne. Bayonne, this is Marshall.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Hey,\\\" I said. I nodded to him in the mirror.\r\n<p>\r\nHe stared at me with sleepy eyes. \\\"What up, man,\\\" he said. \\\"Nice truck. It\\\'s tight.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"Thanks.\\\" My stomach knotted. I didn\\\'t like the situation at all. I couldn\\\'t believe Tsing Tao had let him in my truck. I drove around the block and watched them in the rearview mirror, occasionally scanning the road ahead. The interior light shut off when Bayonne shut the door, and I saw them in intermittent flashes as we passed under streetlamps.\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao said, \\\"So, Bayonne. Let\\\'s do some business.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne said, \\\"A\\\'ight. I got the product right here.\\\" He reached into his pocket and brought out several small vials. He held them out in his palm to Tsing Tao. Her nose wrinkled and her lip curled in disdain.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t want <I>rocks</I>,\\\" she said. \\\"I thought you said you had some good shit. As in <I>powder.</I>\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I <I>gots</I> powder,\\\" he said, putting the vials back in the pocket of his coat. \\\"I thought you might be interested in a more <I>economical</I> option. Like, you be getting more value for your dollar, you know what I\\\'m sayin\\\'? Like a Safeway club card and shit.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao said, \\\"Do you have powder or not?\\\" She sounded impatient.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I <I>said</I>, I <I>gots</I> powder.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Well let\\\'s fucking see it, Bayonne! Are we going to sightsee in the W.A. all night or what?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne said, \\\"Just chill, <I>damn.</I>\\\" He reached into his coat and brought out a another vial. He unscrewed the black cap and smiled at her. \\\"You want a little taste first? Try before you buy?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao said, \\\"Don\\\'t mind if I do\\\" and reached up to flip on the cabin light. Bayonne took out a hand mirror and a razor blade. He balanced the mirror on his knee and tapped out a small amount of powder. He chopped and separated it with the razor. He handed the mirror to Tsing Tao. He said, \\\"Ladies first.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nSing rolled up a dollar bill and stuck it in her nose. She placed it on the mirror and snorted lustily. She gasped and closed her eyes. Tears trickled from under her lids. She said, \\\"Oh, it\\\'s <I>good</I>\\\" and handed it back to Bayonne.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"You goddamn right it\\\'s good,\\\" he said, and did a line himself. \\\"Bayonne only stocks the best, you know what I\\\'m sayin\\\'?\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nI watched in open mouthed horror, my eyes fixed on the rear view mirror. I couldn\\\'t believe she was snorting coke in my truck. I couldn\\\'t believe she had brought me into this situation, hadn\\\'t even told me what was happening. I thought, <I>Oh fuck, Sing, what are you doing.</I>\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tai reached behind her and pulled out her wallet. The chain glowed dully in the dim light. She said, \\\"Ok, that\\\'s good shit, Bayonne. What did we say on the phone? One hundred even, right?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nHe said, \\\"Naw, baby girl, that shit is one seventy-<I>five.</I>\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nShe froze, her hand, holding a folded bill, hanging in the air. \\\"You\\\'re being funny, right,\\\" she said. \\\"We talked yesterday. You said one hundred. We <I>agreed.</I>\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne stared at her, his fist closed around the vial. \\\"That\\\'s right. We agreed <I>yesterday.</I> Last time I checked, that was twenty-four hours ago. Lot can happen in twenty-four hours. Fluctuations in the market an\\\' shit. It\\\'s a bull market for the product, and the price done gone <I>up.</I> To one hundred seventy-<I>five</I>. Might go up even higher before the market done closed, you know what I\\\'m sayin\\\'?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao looked like she was about to cry. \\\"You said one <I>hundred,</I>\\\" she said. She suddenly leaned forward and put a hand on my shoulder. \\\"Marshall, can you loan me some cash? I can pay you back tomorrow.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"I don\\\'t have any cash on me.\\\" I probably had two hundred dollars in my wallet.\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh, <I>fuck,</I>\\\" she said. She sat back and looked at Bayonne. \\\"Look, I can give you the hundred now and give you the rest tomorrow. You know I\\\'m good for it.\\\" She met my eyes in the mirror and then turned back to him. \\\"Come on, man.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne looked incredulous. \\\"Girl, I ain\\\'t the <I>di</I>amond center and shit,\\\" he said. \\\"You know that ain\\\'t how it works. I give you credit, and then the whole neighborhood be bangin\\\' on my door, tryin\\\' to get they shit on credit. \\\'Oh, I\\\'ll pay you tomorrow, Bayonne!\\\' \\\'Come on Bayonne you know I\\\'m good for it, I\\\'m your mother!\\\' Bull<I>shit.</I> Ain\\\'t <I>no</I>body getting credit from Bayonne.\\\" A slow smile crept across his face. He cracked his knuckles. \\\"Which is not to say we can\\\'t work out some sort of <I>payment alternative.</I>\\\" He shifted across the seat and put his arm around Tsing Tao. \\\"Can you think of anything, baby girl?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI hit the brakes. \\\"Ok, that\\\'s it,\\\" I said. \\\"Get the fuck out of my car.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne calmly reached inside his coat and pulled out a small revolver. He pressed the muzzle behind my ear. He thumbed the hammer back and the cylinder turned. I felt the click against my skull. He said, \\\"Shut the fuck up and drive, white boy. We doing business back here.\\\" He tapped the back of my head with the pistol. He said, \\\"<I>Drive</I>, bitch.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI said, \\\"Look, I\\\'ll give you the fucking money. Just get out.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t want what <I>you</I> got,\\\" he said. He grinned. \\\"I want what <I>she</I> got.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nI turned around in my seat and looked at Sing. I said, \\\"Don\\\'t. Don\\\'t do it.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, \\\"It\\\'s just business, Marshall.\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nBayonne stuck the gun in my ear. He said, \\\"Do I have to tell you again?\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nI had the nearly irresistable urge to pee. Instead I stepped on the clutch and put the truck back into second gear. I thought about the first time I had ever seen Tsing Tao naked. We were in my dorm room at UMASS. I was a twenty-two year old virgin but had lied to her about it. I sat in my desk chair. She stood in front of the wardrobe on the other side of the room. She started to undress, watching my face. She stepped out of her Birkenstocks and peeled off her t shirt. She unbuttoned her batik skirt and let it fall to the floor. Then she slipped her panties down her hips and kicked them off her ankles. She crossed the room and sat in my lap, straddling me. She kissed my eyes and unzipped my pants. When she pulled me free I came on her leg. Instead of acting disappointed she wiped the come off her thigh and rubbed it into her nipples. She said, \\\"Kiss me here,\\\" and I did. And like Lazarus, I returned from the grave of the little death. She sat down on me and said, \\\"Second time\\\'s the charm\\\" and gave me her crooked grin.\r\n<p>\r\nNow, in the back seat of my truck, Tsing Tao turned her back to the door and slid down on the seat. She pulled her knees up towards her chest and stuck her  hand down the front of her workpants. Bayonne moved over her like a deadly storm, rabidly kissing her throat, and she said, \\\"Wait, wait. I want to watch you stroke your big cock first.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nHe sat back on his heels and grabbed his crotch. \\\"Oh, you like that freaky shit, huh? A\\\'ight, a\\\'ight, I\\\'m wit dat, I\\\'m wit dat.\\\" He unzipped his pants and reached inside. He said, \\\"Baby girl I\\\'m gon\\\' show you the </I>real</I> anaconda\\\" and Tsing Tao kicked him in the face with her steel toed frankenstein boot. He grabbed his nose with both hands and said, \\\"<I>Bitch!</I>\\\" She had her other leg cocked, her knee almost to her shoulder, and then the two inch thick sole of her boot buried itself in his face and his head smacked off the door window. The safety glass cracked. The third time she hit him the window cobwebbed and he sagged forward, stunned. She reached across him and pulled the door handle. Then she kicked him again and he fell out of the moving truck and tumbled to the pavement.\r\n<p>\r\nTsing Tao screamed, <I>\\\"GO MARSHALL GO!\\\"</I> I tried to stomp the accelerator through the floorboard. The tires shrieked on the pavement. The passenger door flapped. There was a sharp report, and then another, and a hole as big as a baseball materialized in the middle of my windshield. I screamed, <I>\\\"FUCK! SHIT! FUCK!\\\"</I> and drove sixty miles an hour up Turk St with my head under the steering wheel.\r\n<p>\r\nWe went airborne at the top of the hill and when we landed, bouncing on the springs, I knew we were alright. I felt safe enough to be furious. I said, <I>\\\"What the fuck you fucking bitch what were you</I> thinking <I>how could you get me into that don\\\'t you give a fuck about me at all you fuck!\\\"</I>\r\n<p>\r\nSing was laughing in the back seat. I took a deep breath and screamed at the top of my lungs. <I>\\\"WHAT THE FUCK IS SO FUCKING FUNNY?\\\"</I> I said.\r\n<p>\r\nShe held up the vial. \\\"Things go better with coke,\\\" she said. Then she started sobbing. \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(2997,'there are four golden brown horses, all ripply muscles and dappled sunlight, in the fields beyond the wood of evergreens. i pull over to the shoulder of the road, and get out of the car. the horses whinny, their big nostrils widening. if i were brave, i would go up to them, my fingers would whisper hello into their soft fur.\r\n\r\nacross from the canal, cows, black and white quilts, placidly munch grass between small square bales of hay. the corn crops are just shiny leaves this month, just green waves the sunlight hurls itself into before it quietly drowns. there is no funeral for this sun, just a rosy banner painted across the sky, just a memory behind the eyes.\r\n\r\ni\\\'ve lost my voice. i can manage a hoarse word or two, but i\\\'ve started  carrying a little notebook and pencil around. the world is more difficult to love when i can\\\'t tell anyone about it. every syllable hurts in a new way. the back of my throat clenches, the way morning does as dusk makes its way over the planet.\r\n\r\nin the middle of june, the moon is a misplaced seahorse. scales like the edges of newly minted coins. the moon is silent, and yet, when you look at it, nothing else exists. '),(2998,'<P>\r\nthere are four golden brown horses, all ripply muscles and dappled sunlight, in the fields beyond the wood of evergreens. i pull over to the shoulder of the road, and get out of the car. the horses whinny, their big nostrils widening. if i were brave, i would go up to them, my fingers would whisper hello into their soft fur.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nacross from the canal, cows, black and white quilts, placidly munch grass between small square bales of hay. the corn crops are just shiny leaves this month, just green waves the sunlight hurls itself into before it quietly drowns. there is no funeral for this sun, just a rosy banner painted across the sky, just a memory behind the eyes.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni\\\'ve lost my voice. i can manage a hoarse word or two, but i\\\'ve started  carrying a little notebook and pencil around. the world is more difficult to love when i can\\\'t tell anyone about it. every syllable hurts in a new way. the back of my throat clenches, the way morning does as dusk makes its way over the planet.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin the middle of june, the moon is a misplaced seahorse. scales like the edges of newly minted coins. the moon is silent, and yet, when you look at it, nothing else exists. \r\n</P>'),(2999,'<!-- slapper night -->\r\n<P>\r\nSlappers were in full effect at the Fortune 100 tonight.  My attention was split between a conversation with Radavasquez, the antique road show where a small jewelry case was being denounced as rubbish, and four slappers  who were not wearing marina khakis, but slappers none the less.  I was trying to take my attention off the slappers, unsuccessfully.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhat were they doing there?  I don\\\'t understand the slapper wardrobe sense, or maybe I do.  They get their clothes from the pages of fashion magazines?  Slapper shirt by Shirtsforslappers, $170.  Pants  by SlappersAnon, $79.  Slapper shoes by Slappaho, $250.   They are an incarnation of a wriggley spearmint commercial.  They appear to each identify with a different television character.  Three of them look like a wardrobe from Friends.  One of them looks like Gidget. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThey had a body gaurd with them.  Someone from the marketing department.  I don\\\'t know what you call him.  He had a tee shirt on despite the chill in the air.  He was protecting his slappers, arm around one slapper, facing the other slappers.  The slappers ignored him completely.  They carried on with their annimated discussion of oh my god did you see what she was wearing today rolling eyes mouth freezing in contourted smiles.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen I squinted my eyes, they looked like pieces of individually wrapped candy availble in the bulk bin at Safeway.   Customers are on the honor system to put a nickle in the coffee cup for each piece they graze.    One of the slappers was a neopolitan.  Another was a orange taffee.  There was a root beer with a see-through wrapper and yellow lettering label, and a raspberry caramel chewy.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOn the way out the door  I considered bumping into the slapper boy as a signal that he was off his turf.  Radavasquez said the proper protocol for that is to bump into him slightly and whisper, \\\"fag.\\\"  That should do it.  Just enough to wound his insecurity, and let him know he was treading on the wrong socioeconomic turf.  The glove thrown at the face.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSlapper boy would have pounded my ass into the ground, like, wince, for sure.  Which is why I never do the bump, fag, thing.  It has happened to me in the past though before I could afford Calvin Klien underwear.  I still cannot take the offence in a Wild America bar situation.\r\n<!--\r\n  Since I was not at a bar in the Marina, but a shit bar in some no name San Francisco nether neighborhood, I thought some reciprocal gesture was in order.\r\n-->\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3000,'Today, I woke to sun streaming in between the slats of the blind, picked awake by the sensation of a single warm spot of sunshine on the side of my face. The wind blew berry, flower and shrub branches scratchily against the window above my head.<br><br>\r\nI quietly rolled out bed, leaving the girl folded in a ball amist the tangled sheets and piles of pillow, hair looking like the lovely tangle of pyrocanthia and sunrise outside, the cat smugly and gruntily snoring, mumbly rasps of goodmorning wishes and sighs of love drifting up from beneath the comforter.<br><br>\r\nI quietly pulled on items of clothing from the most proximal piles, selections from the foot of the bed, the handlebars of the big steel bike, the drawing-board stacked in the deskchair, and clicked the door behind me, stepping outside.<br><br>\r\nIt\\\'s chilly here, these August moments in California a few months ahead of what\\\'s surely my favorite part of the year, Manhattan\\\'s turn to autumn, the crisp aired days that fill with crispy leaves and changing colors, street-fairs and sweater-clad walks in the park. Such days are status quo here, it seems, even in these ante-autumn days before the leaves fully change and fall, before people start realizing that summer is over, and embrace the quietness of fall.<br><br>\r\nI step over to my car parked in front, next to the larger-than-life sunflower, and dig around in the front seat for my orange parka. Somehow, appropriate clothing keeps getting left in my car and this process has become a morning ritual; I suppose I\\\'m not used to going to the car every day; it feels so suburban.  Out front, I talk to the Russian construction worker refacing the facade of the school across the street; He\\\'s clad in head-to-toe paint-splattered coveralls. The only words I can place, as he shoves his hat up over his forehead with the handle of his brush are <i>\\\"Still feeling cold, huh. Maybe will open when sun comes down some, yes?\\\"</i>, while gesturing towards the 8-foot tall stalk with the same handle, in one fluid gesture from his sweaty, paint-flaked forehead.<br><br>\r\nI walk up Ada street, and confidently cross Sacramento, smilingly aware that traffic likes to give foot-bound folks the right of way here. They slow to a creeping pace, and you can see them waving you across with a free hand, though their tree-shadowed windshield. It feels good to trust traffic, among other things.<Br><Br>\r\nUp the street, past the obsessively and painstakingly manicured garden; each morning, the same lady is out front, spade or rake in hand, poking about the front ribbon of flower-bed, classical music streaming from her headphones, offering a river of smiles to every sun-drenched passerby. I cross California street, and Monterey Market on the left, where I pick up the plumpest, coolest, ripest and probably most delicious pear I\\\'ve ever eaten, carefully peeling off its copious labels of \\\"organic\\\" before crossing Hopkins to the coffee stand.<br><br>\r\nI request a <i>regular</i> coffee, then reconsider my New York vernacular before more fully describing my order to the slightly annoyed, nonplussed waitperson. Next, I\\\'m sitting on the patio there with a soy latte in one hand, a delicions piece of fruit in the other, a stack of random weekly local papers in front of me, the sun finally having reached its apogee above.<br><Br>\r\nBreakfast passes. I retrace my steps back to the house. Past hummingbirds nesting, past houses painted bright earthy hues, houses with stained glass, without black iron bars protecting them, past houses with gardens and yards and friendly inhabitants whispering hellos to me, a complete stranger, as I slowly walk past. I come around the side of the house, finding all the doors open to the luscious morning breezes, and step inside. The clothes go back onto the floor, the chair, the bike handle-bars, and I go back in the bed, pile in next to the warm girl who hasn\\\'t moved in hours, who\\\'s hair smells like mint, whos only movement is parting a slight happy slit of her right eyelid as I return. This is where I\\\'m supposed to be right now, <i>today</i>.'),(3001,'<p>We all make choices in life. We all make decisions. Every single day.</p>\r\n<p>What if you could go back to a pivotal moment in your own life and change something you did, something you said, a choice you made? What if our lives had System Restore Points like Windows XP? What if every single decision you ever made had an undo button? What if you could choos again? Would you take more chances?</p>\r\n<p>This got me thinking... What if I\\\'d never left England? What if I\\\'d never moved to Northern Ireland? What if I hadn\\\'t been a miserable bastard that night, and had gone out clubbing with the lads, and those ropey looking girls from Bristol that they met in Ibiza the month before? What if I hadn\\\'t stayed up all night on IRC for the first time in years? </p>\r\n<p><i>What if I hadn\\\'t met Caroline?</i></p>\r\n<p>Where on earth would I be now? I wouldn\\\'t be sitting in a tiny bungalow in the middle of fucking nowhere. I wouldn\\\'t be sitting in my girlfriend\\\'s parents\\\' living room typing <i>this</i> into a geriatric second-hand Toshiba laptop, that\\\'s for sure. Not that I\\\'m complaining, you understand. I <i>do</i> like it here. The air is clean, the rain is made of water, and the people are simple, friendly, and uncomplicated. It\\\'s like the 60\\\'s didn\\\'t happen.</p>\r\n<p>But what if I <i>hadn\\\'t</i> left that rainy little island for this smaller, slightly rainier one? What if I\\\'d stayed at that miserable little company I was working for, designing boring websites for senile old women and crazy Asian businessmen? What if I\\\'d continued to spend my days under fluorescent lighting, breathing hot stale air in a tiny internal office full of overheating web servers named after various star systems? What if I\\\'d persevered in trying to get a Micro$oft webserver to do anything sensible?</p>\r\n<p>But I <i>know</i> the answer to that. The company went out of business. I\\\'m not sure whether it was my departure that sparked the domino effect mass-exodus of most of the senior staff. I\\\'m sure that would\\\'ve happened anyway, after all, I was only a Webmonkey. One of two... You will be assimilated.... We are bored</p>\r\n<p>So then, what if I\\\'d taken that tech support job offer from <i>The Ideal Home Shopping Channel</i>? What if I\\\'d left my sweaty little desk job to pander to the computational needs of amber faced, cap-toothed, bouffant blonde tv types? What if I\\\'d been on call 24/7 looking after a multi-million pound, digital live broadcast system I knew nothing about? What if I\\\'d taken the job I bullshitted my way into?</p>\r\n<p>I know the answer to <i>that</i> too because the building burnt down. The whole kit and caboodle. The channel went off the air, and as an interesting sideline, one of the employees went missing and was later found dead in another employees garden shed. Which would, no doubt, have been interesting to have been involved in, though hardly the career move of the century!</p>\r\n<p>So am I happy with my choices? You\\\'re damn right I am. Northern Ireland is much more chilled than you might imagine. I have a neat little traveling PC repair job. It doesn\\\'t pay much, but the cost of living here is much lower than back home. I don\\\'t intend on settling here permanently, but I\\\'m gonna stay as long as this crazy little place holds my fickle, MTV-bred attention.</p>'),(3002,'<p>what the fuck am i doing out here? i\\\'m broke. i don\\\'t have a penny in my pocket, just an expired credit card. all my money was spent on 2-4\\\'s. how i love to hear those bottles break. smash against the walls like pieces of myself. become sharred and jagged to cut those that come to close. but there\\\'s nothing for me out here. nothing good can come from wandering west on the Queensway. turn back i keep telling myself. stop this before its too late. why are you leaving? what are you running into? they don\\\'t even have 2-4\\\'s where i\\\'m going. just bottles of blood that smash you against the wall. maybe i\\\'ll turn back at the next exit. maybe i won\\\'t even make it that far. but then it happens.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i am swallowed up in love lost.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she has been dead for two years. i met her when i was 16. we were playing touch football and i asked her and her friends if they wanted to play. i just wanted an excuse to touch her soft skin. i just needed a way to cup her seductive breasts. but she went for my brother instead. he was older and bigger. so i began waiting. i waited for them to break up. i waited while she slept in my basement after her parents kicked her out. and then love finally stuck at 18. we have two beautiful children that live in the city. but now she is dead. i have decades before i\\\'ll die. so i am waiting again. here\\\'s a choclate bar and a coke. you\\\'re out of the city now, where the cops won\\\'t arrest you.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>but the cops catch me in heaven.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i hope you don\\\'t mind me speeding. my ex-boyfriend didn\\\'t like me speeding. he didn\\\'t like camping either. now that i think about, i don\\\'t think he cared too much for me at all. but now i go camping all the time. i just came back from south africa where i wandered down to the beach where they tell you not to go. i have been in ecuador when they had the coup. i sailed through the evolution of humanity. but at 160 km/h we flew over a hill and stared at all of gods creation. and in evolution and creation there was one consitency. one love. it all became clear through the ontario bush. and then the sirens came. and we didn\\\'t talk again. and love was lost again. good luck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but when it seems lost, it returns.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t have a girlfriend. i don\\\'t need one. i guess i\\\'m just picky. i left once. windsor. what a fucking shithole. there\\\'s nothing for anyone there. so i came back i guess. here it\\\'s good. i\\\'ve been playing this one game for two years. i play with my roommates. they have noxima. they don\\\'t go outside. they\\\'re twins. i\\\'d shoot myself in the head if i was them. here i have it good. here i work. here\\\'s how you break into a car. here\\\'s how you fuck a girl standing up. here\\\'s some food.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>further and deeper into the origins of the earth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you don\\\'t mind that we drink? are you looking for your long lost girlfriend? do you want a drink? my father died of cancer too. but he came out of his coma. they were going to give him 14 years in the oklahoma state pen for being a fucking native. he went to vietnam instead and did two tours of duty. when he came out of his coma he went looking for vietnam. here\\\'s a drink for the road.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>through reserves in the back of a pickup truck. through thousands of years of rape. through divide and conquor. three more dollars and a few good tips. at last, a night under the stars. i dream of her and infinite love beyond human matter, where we trancend ourselves and become each other. where our naked bodies roll around in space until we become the stars. but she is not here. and so i rise in the morning and keep a close hand on my knife, ready to cut anything that moves to quickly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is truckers the rest of the way. farmers and white niggers. professional wanderers. through the flat landscape that never ends i can see the back of my head. i spend a night in winnipeg with drunks, pimps, hookers and drug dealers. i spend a night in calgary with collage kids. i spend a day at the side of the road dehydrated and broken. i spend a night at the ocean\\\'s bedside.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have been burned and humiliated. humbled and glorified. i lied and spoke the heart\\\'s truth. i took and gave away. i left my past for a trip in the future. and here nothing makes sense. everything is broken. everything has been destroyed countless times. i jump down the hole and lie down in the middle of the street and see things i will never touch and feel things i will never know. but here is where i belong. and here is where it will really begin.</p>'),(3003,'Miles away but I feel you right here<br>\r\nAlmost like you\\\'re never gone<br>\r\nAnd things around seem to disappear<br>\r\nBut I feel it in my bones<br>\r\nI wonder when you\\\'ll interfere<br>\r\nAnd bring yourself to reappear<br>\r\nBack to my raven nights without you<br>\r\nHear me now for I declare<br>\r\nMy own merge of space and time<br>\r\nI\\\'ve waited for platonic years<br>\r\nImpatience pinches me divine<br>\r\nHold me \\\'cause I\\\'m without fear<br>\r\nSteering while in second gear<br>\r\nI\\\'ve got a destination saved for you<br>\r\nThe difference is far and near<br>\r\nFeels just like the pull of whim<br>\r\nAnd things around are crystal clear<br>\r\nI can hear it in this violin<br>\r\nI wonder when you\\\'ll interfere<br>\r\nAnd bring yourself to reappear<br>\r\nBack to my raven life without you<br>'),(3004,'when the day has drawn to a close,<br>\r\nor later yet--<br>\r\nwhen the first rays of sun are appearing over the horizon,<br>\r\nor through the fog of early morning,<br>\r\nor as they increase their angle to shine from further and further overhead--<br>\r\nthis is when i think of you.<p>\r\nand later still, when they begin to dim once more<br>\r\nand lower themselves into the earth,<br>\r\nto be born anew \r\non the edge of another day--<br>\r\nthis is a reminder of your face.<p>\r\nwhen i hear the rivers of cars begin to flow,<br>\r\ncarrying their loads of lives in countless directions--<br>\r\nthe machinery of america roaring to life\r\nto disturb the slumber of those more fortunate--<br>\r\nit brings you to the forefront of my thoughts.<p>\r\nwhen the coffee-maker burbles and churns it\\\'s coffee-song,<br>\r\nand scents and sounds wake me from my dreams,<br>\r\nmy thoughts congeal around you,<br>\r\nthe seed of my lucidity.<p>\r\nand when i have run my race for one day,<br>\r\nand lay my burdens down in a basket,<br>\r\nand write my letters,<br>\r\nand lie down to sleep again,<br>\r\ni see you in the distance waving--<br>\r\nand feel your breath on my face as i let it all slide until tomorrow. \r\n'),(3005,'<i>more drugs. more porn. my mother would be so proud.</i>\r\n<p>\r\nI followed her back to the bar. She was drinking Budweiser too and we toasted each otherâ€™s impeccable taste.\r\n<p>\r\nThe DJ cranked the music to punishing volume. Someone droned bloodlessly about love tearing us apart, again, and legions of spooky kids twirled and wafted like wind-blown wraiths. I turned La Chupacabraâ€™s head and leaned in til my lips brushed her ear. I shouted, â€œWhereâ€™s the Kid?â€ \r\n<p>\r\nShe me pulled me closer to her mouth. <I>â€œWHAT?â€</I> she said.\r\n<p>\r\nI said, </I>â€œWHEREâ€™S THE KID?â€</I>\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, <I>â€WHO?â€</I>\r\n<p>\r\n<I>â€œTHE KID. MY FRIEND. YOUR BOYFRIEND.â€</I>\r\n<p>\r\n<I?â€OH!â€</I> she said. <I>â€œI DUNNO. Iâ€™M OUT WITH MY GIRLFRIENDS TONIGHT.â€</I> She laughed and lit a cigarette. It was illegal but the bar was wreathed in smoke. Thin black goves covered her gang-tattooed hands. <I>â€œAND THE KID AINâ€™T MY BOYFRIEND. NOT YET, ANYWAYS.â€</I> She grabbed my hand and moved towards the front of the bar. She tugged me up a narrow ramp and we squeezed past hipsters heading the other way.\r\n<p>\r\nWe found a booth tucked into an unlit corner near the entrance. It was quieter, and we could talk without straining our throats raw. La Chupacabra pulled out a zippo lighter emblazoned with a winged, beret-wearing skull and the legend <I>Death from Above.</I> She lit another cigarette and the candle sitting on our table.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nShe said, â€œSo who are you here with?â€ She watched my face and blew her smoke to the side.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œIâ€™m just out by myself,â€ I said. â€œI felt like dancing.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œYouâ€™re not really dressed for it,â€ she said.\r\n<p>\r\nI borrowed her cigarette and took a long drag. â€œI came straight from work.â€ \r\n<p>\r\nShe said, â€œI seeeeee. Hard day at the office, Marshall?â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œYou could say that.â€ I slouched down in my side of the booth and stared at her. I watched her thin white arm turn into her shoulder, bare under the thin strap of her black dress, and descend into the swell of her small breast. I felt a surge of lust followed immediately by the stab of a thousand tiny knives of guilt. <I>Sheâ€™s the Kidâ€™s girl,</I> I told myself. <I>What the fuck is wrong with you.</I>\r\n<p>\r\nShe tapped a small plastic vial on the table. She said, â€œOh yeah? I got just the thing, <I>papi.</I>â€ She grinned and arched an eyebrow at me. â€œI have to go to the bathroom. Donâ€™t you?â€ she asked.\r\n<p>\r\nI blinked several times. I said, â€œWhy, yes. Yes, I do.â€\r\n<p>\r\nWe got in line for the bathroom. La Chupacabra leaned back against me and my arm snaked around her waist. She turned and pressed against me, her hands on my chest. She put her face under my jaw and kissed my throat. Whiskey, lust and guilt mixed poisonously in my stomach.\r\n<p> \r\n\r\nThe bathroom door unlocked and opened, the bright flourescent light a wedge in the nightclub gloom. Several sexual polymorphs came out, clutching each other and giggling madly. Tendrils of sweet pot smoke trailed after them.\r\n<p>\r\nLa Chupacabra stepped inside, pulling me after her. She locked the door behind us. It was the womenâ€™s room; there was no urinal, but there was a small cubbyhole with a mirror and smooth counter of faux marble. She pulled a handfull of paper towels from the dispenser and hummed as she briskly wiped the counter down. She balled the wet towels up and threw them at me. I was too drunk to react in time and they bounced off my face. La Chupacabra then tapped out a small pile of white powder from her vial on the counter. She said, â€œGive me a credit card, <I>papi,</I>â€ so I fished one out of my wallet and she used it to chop up the few clumps in the pile and to separate it into four neat lines. She licked the edges of the plastic card, her tongue curling like an upside down question mark, and winked at me when she handed it back. She pulled a cocktail from her purse. Then she took out a wicked looking little knife and cut the straw in half. She handed me a four inch section and said, â€œLadies first.â€\r\n<p>\r\nI bent down to the counter and put the straw in my nose. I pointed the end at the beginning of a line and <I>schnuff</I>ed it up into my nose. Rivers and tributaries of nerves flash froze in my sinuses. My eyes watered and suddenly my brain was a stadium lit for nighttime play. I said, â€œOh, <I>shit.</I>â€\r\n<p>\r\nLa Chupacabra grinned and said, â€œItâ€™s good, huh? My boy Bayonne gets the good shit.â€ I backed away from the counter and laughed long and hard. She wanted to know just what was so fucking funny but I wouldnâ€™t tell her. Instead I backed away from the counter and invited her with a flourish to partake.\r\n<p>\r\nShe put one hand on the counter and leaned close to the surface. She vaccuumed up a line. She stood up and pinched the bridge of her nose. She said, â€œOh. Oh. Oh.â€ She bent back down and did another line. And the next. She smiled at me, her eyes teary. Then she charged.\r\n<p>\r\nShe knocked me back and we fell against the wall together. She grabbed my hair in both hands and pulled my mouth to hers. We gnawed and bit at each other. Her tongue darted in and out of my mouth. My hands moved from her hips to her breasts and back. I clawed at her and her skirt gathered and bunched in my fingers. It didnâ€™t seem to be a time for niceties so I went ahead and slipped a hand between her thighs and stroked her through her panties. She panted in my ear. I pulled her hair back from the side of her head and sloshed my tongue in her ear. She shivered and squeezed my cock painfully hard through my pants. I retaliated by slipping both hands inside her panties and putting two fingers in her pussy and one up her ass. She bit my lip like she wanted to chew and swallow it. She hung on long enough that I got scared and pushed her back.\r\n<p>\r\nPeople were pounding on the door. La Chupacabra said, â€œIâ€™m tired of this place. You want to get out of here?â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œSure,â€ I said. â€œWhere do you want to go?â€\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, â€œI could show you my poems.â€ She wrapped her arms around my waist and rubbed against me. â€œYou want to read some of my poetry, Marshall?â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œI would <I>love</I> to read your poems,â€ I said. \r\n<p>\r\nShe said, â€œYou gotta come over then. Theyâ€™re in my room.â€\r\n<p>\r\nI said, â€œWhoa, wait, come over where? Your house? I thought you lived with your parents.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œHeh. Not exactly,â€ she said. â€œMy parents own the house. They live upstairs. But I got my own apartment downstairs. Itâ€™s got its own entrance.â€ She squeezed my hand. â€œSo we can have some privacy.â€ \r\n<p>\r\nI leaned against the door. The pounding was continuous. The people waiting on the other side were getting desperate. I said, â€œMaybe we should just go to my apartment. I live like three blocks from here. We can walk there in five minutes.â€\r\n<p>\r\nLa Chupacabra looked annoyed. â€œNo, man, we got to go to my house. My daddyâ€™ll be pissed if he doesnâ€™t hear me come in before two.â€ She kissed me along the line of my jaw. She said, â€œCome on, Marshall.â€\r\n<p>\r\nI said, â€œOh, alrightâ€ and then we were back on Harrison Street climbing into the first of a line of waiting taxis. La Chupacabra sat forward to give the driver her address and then she snuggled up against me. Her ceaseless cocaine chatter ranged among the topics of working for Mrs. Bonaventura (generally decent, but she was a bitch about making her keep her workstation spotless), her desire to own a shar pei (all consuming), nearly making the second round of cuts at the Niner Girl tryouts (heartbreaking, but she knew in her heart it was her destiny, so she wasnâ€™t about to give up), her love for obscure New Wave (a taste she picked up from her older sister, who in 1983 was the first girl at Mission High to pick up the Madonna look and the first to shave the side of her head), her favorite bars in the Mission (the Kilowatt and the Five Hundred Club), and her favorite food (unagi nigiri).\r\n<p>\r\nWhen she paused to take a breath I said, â€œHey, Inez, are you cheating on the Kid with me?â€\r\n<p>\r\nShe said, â€œNo, man, no.â€ She rolled down her window and spit out her gum. She said, â€œThe Kid is super sweet, you know? I like him a lot. But we ainâ€™t going out or anything. He donâ€™t make me go--â€ She mimed shivering in desire. â€œHeâ€™s <I>too</I> nice. You know what Iâ€™m saying? But the first time I met you I was like, <I>Oh</I> yeah.â€ She climbed into my lap. â€œDoes that answer your question?â€\r\n<p>\r\nWhen we were a block from her house she told the driver to stop. I paid the fare and got out. She stayed in the cab. She said, â€œ2467 Harrison. Itâ€™s the blue house. My entrance is the little door on the right side of the stairs.â€ Then she pulled the door shut and the cab pulled away from the curb. I stared after it, dumbfounded. When it reached the middle of the next block the brake lights went on and I saw her tiny figure climb out of the taxi. A light went on in an upstairs window and then disappeared.\r\n<p>\r\nI shrugged and started walking towards the address she gave me. It was almost two in the morning. I was walking alone and completely shitfaced through a neighborhood that would give me pause on a sober day. The cocaine had supercharged a heretofore mostly inert tendency towards foolhardy recklessness. I only fell down once on my way to her door. I knocked tentatively, sure that her father was going to pull the door open. But it was just her, wearing only red panties and an ancient Cure tshirt. \r\n<p>\r\nShe said, â€œYou want a beer?â€\r\n<p>\r\nI said, â€œOk.â€\r\n\r\nShe said, â€œYou want to smoke some pot?â€\r\n<p>\r\nI said, â€œOk.â€\r\n<p>\r\nHer apartment wasnâ€™t much smaller than mine, at least in terms of floor space. It took up the whole basement of her parentsâ€™ house. The ceiling was low enough that I felt like I was in constant danger of scraping hair off the top of my head. Her parents had put in a rather nice hardwood floor, the wood blond and still very clean, and all the furnishings had come from the Ikea superstore over in Emeryville. There was a futon couch and a coffee table. A TV and VCR in a home entertainment center stand. There were paintings on the walls, and when I examined them more closely I saw they were actually paint-by-number Bible scenes. La Chupacabra saw me looking at them and said, â€œMy mom did all those. Well, except I did those two.â€\r\n<p>\r\nShe went into her bedroom and returned carr