 know that he had died.  I had known the family for as long as I could remember, being among the few fellow Indian families to be found in small southern town.  Mom didn\\\'t let me know at all that he had committed suicide, only that he had died.  However, I just assumed.  Mom let me know that I and my brother should come home that weekend to support the family, and we, more than willingly, said we would be around for anything they needed.</p>\r\n<p>Upon arriving home I found my initial assumption to be right.  The boy had been living in Atlanta, a graduate of Vanderbuilt University in pre-law, and floundering for a reason to continue with law.  Growing up, he always seemed to be floundering despite his intelligence and dilligence.  He seemed awkward in social situations, always like he was searching for something, anything, to say to someone else.  If he found something made another person laugh, he would run with the joke.  Unfortunately, he\\\'d run the joke into the ground, eventually annoying whomever he was talking to.  He couldn\\\'t quite find a comfort level with anyone.</p>\r\n<p>He had come home for the weekend and acted completely normal, asking his mom to do his laundry for when he returned to atlanta and to also pack up leftovers.  i guess something that any bachelor would ask of their mother.  His family, mother, father, and younger sister, went shopping for things around the house.  His mother returned first, opening the garage door to a horrible smell and her son in his car.</p>\r\n<p>this happened about two years ago.  His father doesn\\\'t speak of him very much.  But when his mother and I talk about deserts and dinners and movies, she\\\'s always sure to mention what her picky daughter is fond of and, also, what her son used to be fond of.  she gets a little quiet after she mentions him, but something in her eyes tells me that she\\\'s remembering him fondly now, rather than the confused mother she was immediately after his death.</p>\r\n<p>And you wonder why one should feel guilty for another\\\'s death?  That I wasn\\\'t surprised that this awkward boy had grown into a more awkward and confused man; that I understood what it was to be accomplished and yet feel completely unsatisfied with what my life had currently culminated to.  I was simply felt guilty that I wasn\\\'t at all surprised that he died at such a young age.'),(3007,'<table bgcolor=\\\"white\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" width=\\\"100%\\\">\r\n	<tr align=\\\"center\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\">\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3007_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></td>\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n	<tr align=\\\"left\\\">\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n		<td><br>find me. push your fingers through this screen and reach out. search for my warmth and follow the heat that i store in my words.  touch me.  hold me when i lay still, my skin cut open from neck to navel, and cry for me.  make me feel your body with sobbing heaves.  want from me.  give to me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n		push your fingers through this screen and wet my body with your need.  press your face into the back of my head and smell deeply my hair, collect it as evidence, build the archetype of me into your moving memory, thought by thought, flesh by flesh.<br><br>\r\n\r\n		fall beneath this glass and want me, state your praise to call me out, to draw me near, to draw my face, to see me softly.  find me alone and wait until i need you and open your arms when i start to run.  mad with lack of warmth, i shiver as a last desperate attempt to create my own.  quiet your disapproval, for i need you this one time.<br><br>\r\n\r\n		but i cannot hear you.  are you there?  i have no way of knowing if you are there.<br><br></td>\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n	<tr align=\\\"center\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\">\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3007_2.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></td>\r\n		<td width=\\\"33%\\\">Â </td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3008,'<P>i need an amulet. i need something solid and cold to the touch. i need to protect myself from my own crazy whirring mind. i need something for my frantic fingers to hold onto and believe in. my fingers. if my hands have faith in something, or someone, then it is good. i trust my body because it is too stupid to lie. my mind, on the other hand, is a trickster. a coyote. i must always be on guard as it freewheels its way through the white sky.\r\n</P><P>\r\nlast night i dreamt that i drove into a snowbank and sank through. down down down into the concrete. the car became this giant metal cage. i could not undo my seatbelt. then we were underwater. the magma at the centre of the earth is a myth. there\\\'s really a massive ocean at the core of the planet. my hair floated free and schools of bright transparent fish darted in and out of the dark strands. i woke up sure that i was bald.\r\n</P><P>\r\ntoday my winter-soft hands got caught in the teeth of my jacket\\\'s zipper and i heard the skin tear before i saw the cut. i watched fascinated, as the blood appeared, a crimson flower blooming suddenly, unexpectedly, and immediately freezing into a sculpture of rubies before i could wipe it away. i couldn\\\'t bear to brush it off then, so i left it on my index finger, admiring it surreptitiously for the rest of the day.</P>'),(3009,'<p>Shortly after <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2213\\\"><b>this</b></a> Johnny got shot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Johnny came out of a liquor store; he was holding a brown paper bag.  Dude walked up on Johnny and said that he wanted the goodies in the bag.  Johnny gave dude the bag.  Then dude shot Johnny in the back - just for snicks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Johnny lived, but his lungs were toast.  They still tried to make him work; he no longer had the strength for masonry though.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe Johnny\\\'s dead now; I don\\\'t know.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I stopped on the west side tonight, near the place where Johnny got shot.  His story crept back into my mind when I realized that I had my back to the street.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I rolled all the way down Western - from north to south.  Chicago craps out at 119th and the city of my birth begins.  It\\\'s my <a href=\\\"http://answering-islam.org/Index/H/hajj.html\\\">Mecca</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Blue Island\\\'s got an oil refinery, a mill, a rail yard, a garbage dump, a porn shop, a bone yard, a trailer park, and a canal.  Behold White trash.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>I suppose that when my family left the ghetto on the west side, Blue Island looked good.</p>'),(3010,'<p>Hello, Hello. My names Terry and I&#146;m a law abider. There&#146;s nothing \r\n  I like more than getting fired up on beer<br>\r\n  And when the weekends here I to exercise my right to get paralytic and fight. \r\n  Good bloke fairly. But I get well lary when geezers look at me funny, i bounce \r\n  &#145;em round like bunnies. I&#146;m likely to cause mischief. Good clean grief \r\n  you must believe and I ain&#146;t no thief. Law abiding and all, all legal. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>And who cares about my liver when it feels good. What you need is some real \r\n  manhood. Rasher Rasher Barney and Kasha putting peoples backs up. Public disorder, \r\n  I&#146;ll give you public disorder. I down eight pints and run all over the \r\n  place. Spit in the face of an officer. See if that bothers you cause I never \r\n  broke a law in my life. Someday I&#146;m gonna settle down with a wife. Come \r\n  on lads lets have another fight.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Eh hello. My names Tim and I&#146;m a criminal, In the eyes of society I \r\n    need to be in jail for the choice of herbs I inhale. This ain&#146;t no wholesale \r\n    operation Just a few eighths and some Playstations my vocation. I pose a threat \r\n    to the nation. And down the station the police hold no patients. </p>\r\n  <p>Let&#146;s talk space and time. I like to get deep sometimes and think about \r\n    Einstein and Carl Young and old Kung Fu movies I like to see. Pass the hydrator \r\n    please, yeah I&#146;m floating on thin air. Going to Amsterdam in the New \r\n    Year &#150; top gear there. Cus I taker pride in my hobby. Home made bongs \r\n    using my engineering degree</p>\r\n  <p>Dear Leaders, please legalise weed for these reasons.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Like I was saying to him. I told him: <i>&#147;Top with me and you won&#146;t \r\n  leave.&#148;</i> So I smacked him in the head and downed another Carling. Bada \r\n  Bada Bing for the lad&#146;s night. Mad fight, his face&#146;s a sad sight. \r\n  Vodka and Snake Bite. Going on like a right geez, he&#146;s a twat, shouldn&#146;t \r\n  have looked at me like that.</p>\r\n<p>Anyway I&#146;m an upstanding citizen. If a war came along I&#146;d be on the \r\n  front line with em. Can&#146;t stand crime either them hooligans on heroin. \r\n  Drugs and criminals those thugs on the penny coloured will be the downfall of \r\n  society. I&#146;ve got all the anger pent up inside of me.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>You know, I don&#146;t see why I should be the criminal. How can something \r\n    with no recorded fatalities be illegal, and how many deaths are there per \r\n    year from alcohol?</p>\r\n  <p>I just completed Gran Tourismo on the hardest setting. We pose no threat \r\n    on my settee</p>\r\n  <p>Ooh the pizza&#146;s here will someone let him in please</p>\r\n  <p><i>&#147;We didn&#146;t order chicken, Not a problem we&#146;ll pick it out. \r\n    I doubt they meant to mess us about. After all we&#146;re all adults not louts.&#148; \r\n    </i></p>\r\n  <p>As I was saying, we&#146;re friendly peaceful people. We&#146;re not the \r\n    ones out there causing trouble.We just sit in this hazy bubble with our quarters \r\n    discussing how beautiful Gail Porter is. MTV, BBC 2, Channel 4 is on until \r\n    six in the morning.<br>\r\n    Then at six in the morning the sun dawns and it&#146;s my bedtime.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Causing trouble, your stinking rabble, boys saying I&#146;m the lad who&#146;s \r\n  spoiling it. <b>You&#146;re on drugs</b> it really bugs me when people try and \r\n  tell me I&#146;m a thug just for getting drunk. I like getting drunk, cus I&#146;m \r\n  an upstanding citizen. If a war came along I&#146;d be on the front line with \r\n  em.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Now Terry you&#146;re repeating yourself, but that&#146;s okay drunk people \r\n    can&#146;t help that. A chemical reaction inside your brain causes you to \r\n    forget what you&#146;re saying.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>What. I know exactly what I&#146;m saying, I&#146;m perfectly sane you stinking \r\n  student lameo. Go get a job and stop robbing us of our taxes.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Err, well actually according to research government funding for further education \r\n    pales in insignificance when compared to how much they spend on repairing \r\n    Lary drunk people at the weekend in casualty wards all over the land.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Why you cheeky little swine come here I&#146;m gonna batter you. Come here!</p>\r\n'),(3011,'<p>Hello, Hello. My names Terry and I&#146;m a law abider. There&#146;s nothing \r\n  I like more than getting fired up on beer, and when the weekends here I to exercise \r\n  my right to get paralytic and fight. Good bloke fairly. But I get well lary \r\n  when geezers look at me funny, I bounce &#145;em round like bunnies. I&#146;m \r\n  likely to cause mischief. Good clean grief you must believe and I ain&#146;t \r\n  no thief. Law abiding and all, all legal. </p>\r\n<p>And who cares about my liver when it feels good. What you need is some real \r\n  manhood. Rasher Rasher Barney and Kasha putting peoples backs up. Public disorder, \r\n  I&#146;ll give you public disorder. I down eight pints and run all over the \r\n  place. Spit in the face of an officer. See if that bothers you cause I never \r\n  broke a law in my life. Someday I&#146;m gonna settle down with a wife. Come \r\n  on lads lets have another fight.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Eh hello. My names Tim and I&#146;m a criminal, In the eyes of society I \r\n    need to be in jail for the choice of herbs I inhale. This ain&#146;t no wholesale \r\n    operation Just a few eighths and some Playstations my vocation. I pose a threat \r\n    to the nation. And down the station the police hold no patients. </p>\r\n  <p>Let&#146;s talk space and time. I like to get deep sometimes and think about \r\n    Einstein and Carl Young and old Kung Fu movies I like to see. Pass the hydrator \r\n    please, yeah I&#146;m floating on thin air. Going to Amsterdam in the New \r\n    Year &#150; top gear there. Cus I take pride in my hobby. Home made bongs \r\n    using my engineering degree</p>\r\n  <p>Dear Leaders, please legalise weed for these reasons.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Like I was saying to him. I told him: <i>&#147;Fuck with me and you won&#146;t \r\n  live.&#148;</i> So I smacked him in the head and downed another Carling. Bada \r\n  Bada Bing for the lad&#146;s night. Mad fight, his face&#146;s a sad sight. \r\n  Vodka and Snake Bite. Going on like a right geez, he&#146;s a twat, shouldn&#146;t \r\n  have looked at me like that.</p>\r\n<p>Anyway I&#146;m an upstanding citizen. If a war came along I&#146;d be on the \r\n  front line with em. Can&#146;t stand crime either them hooligans on heroin. \r\n  Drugs and criminals those thugs are the pinnacle of the downfall of society. \r\n  I&#146;ve got all the anger pent up inside of me.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>You know, I don&#146;t see why I should be the criminal. How can something \r\n    with no recorded fatalities be illegal, and how many deaths are there per \r\n    year from alcohol?</p>\r\n  <p>I just completed <i>Gran Tourismo</i> on the hardest setting. We pose no \r\n    threat on my settee</p>\r\n  <p>Ooh the pizza&#146;s here will someone let him in please</p>\r\n  <p><i>&#147;We didn&#146;t order chicken, Not a problem we&#146;ll pick it out. \r\n    I doubt they meant to mess us about. After all we&#146;re all adults not louts.&#148; \r\n    </i></p>\r\n  <p>As I was saying, we&#146;re friendly peaceful people. We&#146;re not the \r\n    ones out there causing trouble.We just sit in this hazy bubble with our quarters \r\n    discussing how beautiful Gail Porter is. MTV, BBC 2, Channel 4 is on until \r\n    six in the morning.<br>\r\n    Then at six in the morning the sun dawns and it&#146;s my bedtime.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Causing trouble, your stinking rabble, boys saying I&#146;m the lad who&#146;s \r\n  spoiling it. <b>You&#146;re on drugs</b> it really bugs me when people try and \r\n  tell me I&#146;m a thug just for getting drunk. I like getting drunk, cus I&#146;m \r\n  an upstanding citizen. If a war came along I&#146;d be on the front line with \r\n  em.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Now Terry you&#146;re repeating yourself, but that&#146;s okay drunk people \r\n    can&#146;t help that. A chemical reaction inside your brain causes you to \r\n    forget what you&#146;re saying.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>What. I know exactly what I&#146;m saying, I&#146;m perfectly sane you stinking \r\n  student lameo. Go get a job and stop robbing us of our taxes.</p>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <p>Err, well actually according to research government funding for further education \r\n    pales in insignificance when compared to how much they spend on repairing \r\n    lary drunk people at the weekend in casualty wards all over the land.</p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>Why you cheeky little swine come here I&#146;m gonna batter you. Come here!</p>'),(3012,'The radio was buzzing.<BR><BR>\nThe feelings of nine years ago drifted like smoke. It felt bad to want to smash the gently squawking radio. It was never the same.\nThe time of nine years ago was nothing like the previous year.<BR><BR>\nThe yellowish cast of flourescent light at 3:49am was depressing.<BR><BR>\nIt could all be considered \\\"parts\\\". There were parts from the past, dead but not forgotten. If it was a part to be played then it was pointless after all. His only hope was that it was parts of a puzzle. He worked full time putting the puzzle together.<BR>\nIn his mind the relentless nature of his work made him tough and grizzled like a cowboy in a corny movie.<BR><BR>\nMorning was different. His body felt powerful. A bucket of ice water was a nice thing to stick your face into. He rubbed a lemon on his forehead.<BR>\nLiving on an island was an appealing idea. He was horny. Mexican girls are nice overall he pondered.<BR><BR>\nAfter playing drums in an industrial warehouse all afternoon his ears rang. After the metronome stopped the clicking continued. You don\\\'t need a drumset to really play drums.<BR><BR>\nToday was his birthday. He was 49 years old.<BR>\nHis mother gave him ride to pick up his disability check. There was no shame in being schizophrenic. He never cheated or lied to anyone and he loved his family\n-- the innocent life of a deranged hermit.<BR><BR>\nThe clicking never stopped. He stared into the mirror that evening and talked to his moustache. It said: \\\"You gotta keep living until you die.\\\" <BR>\nHe wasn\\\'t sure if that was disturbing or reassuring.<BR>'),(3014,'<p>It\\\'s a sweaty summer evening in the city and Eadie is restless as usual. Restless \r\n  and irritable, but nothing out of the ordinary. What you must understand is \r\n  Eadie MacFarland is a hormonal psycobitch. Fucked up on Tri-Cyclen, but we love \r\n  her just the same. There <i>are </i>other, more welcome side effects. Naturally, \r\n  we take full advantage.</p>\r\n<p>My name\\\'s Mike and I\\\'m a modern day adventurer. A pioneer, pushing abstract \r\n  frontiers, pushing brown to school kids, pushing my luck some might say. At \r\n  least i\\\'m not pushing paper. We don\\\'t hold down regular nine-to-fives, Me and \r\n  Donal and Eadie, that\\\'s not us. We\\\'re not your next-door neighbours. We\\\'re not \r\n  <i>normal</i>. We\\\'re not your fucking cousin Ashram\\\'s fuckin\\\' Orthodontist and \r\n  we don\\\'t work at your local Seven Eleven.</p>\r\n<p>You\\\'ve probably met us. But you\\\'d never know it. We have a penchant for blending \r\n  into your society. We dress in earth tones and pastel shades, wear our hair \r\n  in an acceptable style. Cultural camouflage. We are not visually offensive. \r\n  We are not what we appear to be, but then again, who is? Eadie wears sensible \r\n  footwear, a slim, yet curvy brunette. Her skin is lightly freckled. She has \r\n  the most amazing skin, a perfect complexion and the most innocent big brown \r\n  eyes you ever saw. Not the type of girl you\\\'d expect to indulge in group sex, \r\n  bondage, fucked up depraved shit you couldn\\\'t even imagine. She looks like your \r\n  fuckin\\\' sister for fucks sake.</p>\r\n<p>But we all have our reasons for living this lifestyle. Donal, well he\\\'s just \r\n  your average stoner, loser, layabout, workshy mofo. He probably started a masters \r\n  degree in engineering once upon a time. He probably had dreams and aspirations \r\n  of a wife and two kids, two cars and a fuckin\\\' timeshare in Malaga before reality \r\n  took hold, before the drugs opened his eyes like matchsticks to the banality \r\n  of this fucked up shit called life. We\\\'re all gonna die, sooner or later. Make \r\n  a million before breakfast or sit on your arse watchin daytime TV getting mashed, \r\n  your still gonna be just as dead. Rich people. Successful people. Beautiful \r\n  people. Gay or straight people. The worms will still eat your face. That\\\'s if \r\n  they don\\\'t cremate you. And you know that human bones don\\\'t burn right through, \r\n  they have to be crushed up in a machine afterwards. Rest in pieces.</p>\r\n<p>But the end, hopefully is not too near. Not yet. I had a poor start, a troubled \r\n  childhood, growing up in the shadow of a manic depressive older brother. It \r\n  started with self-abuse, cutting, fucked up shit like that. He had no reason \r\n  to be like that. We had a comfortable middle-class upbringing. I guess some \r\n  people are just predisposed to mentalism, there brains hard wired into psycho \r\n  mode. The cutting got steadily worse until he was seventeen when he went away \r\n  to<i> \\\'rest for a while\\\'</i>. I\\\'ve seen my brother Nathan just once in the past \r\n  ten years, it was two years ago at Mardi Gras. He was waving a fuckin\\\' rainbow \r\n  flag. He didn\\\'t say much, seemed withdrawn, his mind elsewhere &quot;Fuck him&quot; \r\n  I thought, Jesus, what is he a poof now? He could be dead now for all I know. \r\n  He probably has AIDS.</p>\r\n<p>I was always gonna turn out this way. It was predestined. Written in the stars. \r\n  This is my cosmic destiny or some shit. Eadie, I don\\\'t know her story, she never \r\n  really opens up to me. Not in the emotional sense at least.</p>\r\n<p>Eadie was shuffling her bony arse around on the seat. Squirming, grinding it \r\n  like she does when she\\\'s in <i>that</i> mood. Donal knows what I mean. But she \r\n  <i>wasn\\\'t</i> in that mood, that was painfully obvious. Donal looked at me. \r\n  I looked at Donal. He was poking his pinky finger into a multitude of cigarette \r\n  burned holes in the back of the seat in front of him. Picking out pieces of \r\n  foam and flicking them in my direction with the demeanor of a disobedient child. \r\n  Me and Donal have this whole nonverbal communication thing going on which is \r\n  a plus when Eadie\\\'s got her big grey knickers on. It avoids some of the more \r\n  painfully explosive mood swings we\\\'ve become accustomed to. Horny to angry to \r\n  psycho to bitch-in-heat to psycho again and back to cock-eating-pornstar. Repeat \r\n  to fade. I don\\\'t go in for bloodsports but Donal swears it\\\'s a rare delicacy. \r\n  Eadie is only too happy to oblige. Fuckin\\\' messy if you ask me. Donal\\\'s like \r\n  the brother I never had, but he\\\'s still a fuckhead.</p>\r\n<p>Now, I wouldn\\\'t say we pimp Eadie, No . It\\\'s not like pimping in the traditional \r\n  sense. Eadie does her own thing. If she were an animal she\\\'d be a house cat. \r\n  She does what she likes, and god forbid you try to encourage her in any particular \r\n  direction. And it\\\'s certainly not a steady source of income, Eadie is nothing \r\n  if not unpredictable. Sometimes she turns tricks, sometimes she fucks for free. \r\n  Fuckin\\\' irritating is what she is. Yet she has a certain charm, if you know \r\n  what I mean.</p>\r\n<p>She hasn\\\'t said a word to me all day, and now as we sit here on the No. 149 \r\n  bus, on our way home, she looks like she\\\'s gonna spontaneously combust. Any \r\n  moment now her progesterone levels are gonna go into freefall, and shits gonna \r\n  get fucked up. Donal has noticed this too, he\\\'s giving me that look. And the \r\n  sun\\\'s going down, and it\\\'s setting orange to purple through th smog as the light \r\n  fades bit by bit. I read the words written on the windows with copydex fluid,<i>&quot;Donna \r\n  Sux Cock&quot;</i>, and I wonder if Donna wrote that herself. Eadie will be \r\n  okay soon, we just need to get her home. She\\\'ll probably just swing round to \r\n  horny again. We can handle that. Donal can sort her out. Then I notice Donal \r\n  flicking his foam bits in the direction of this huge Samoan looking geezer and \r\n  I punch him on the arm. &quot;Fuck you do that for?&quot;</p>\r\n<p>I ask Eadie if she\\\'s ok. She shoots me a look that seems to say &quot;Die Fucker!&quot; \r\n  and I\\\'m sure I can hear the hormones coursing through her bloodstream. Donal \r\n  winks at me as if to say <i>&quot;Dude!&quot;</i>, and we both shut the fuck \r\n  up until we get to our stop <i>Nelson Mandela Terrace</i></p>'),(3015,'<tt><p>It begins like any other poem of love:<br>\r\nA low, fat moon and errant stars poke yellow-white holes in the cloth of the sky.<br>\r\nBut in the east and in the west, a creamy orange haze rises up along the horizon: from the desperate churning of the big city, and from the world-weary sun.<br>\r\nCrickets and cicadas tirelessly tick and stroke their songs - amore, amore - into the waiting night.<br>\r\nAnd Dairy Queen hangs in the warm air: dry, a harbinger of the coming autumn.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Tick, tick, tick.<br>\r\nAnd the cars roll by.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hands should be held together while walking, tonight.<br>\r\nFlesh on flesh - with loving strokes - should ease the world\\\'s worries, tonight.<br>\r\nDark hair - lips parting, eyes closing - should spill on the pillow, tonight.<br>\r\nAnd I should empty myself into you, tonight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Little promises about wanting, and needing - desperate whispers exchanged in the confidence of a now vacant space, they were like paintings that no one saw, and words that no one read: all passing into night.</p></tt>\r\n\r\n<img align=\\\"center\\\" src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">'),(3016,'<P>i got a film back today which i had found when i was cleaning out a bookshelf.  i decided to take it in and get it developed despite the fact that i didn\\\'t know what might be on it.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni was surprised.  its a film of kids i used to live with in wellington over the summer of 1999-2000.  one of them i had a brief flirtation with; the other was like an older sister.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe girl i recognised as me in the photos bears only vague resemblance when compared to photographs taken just a month ago at my birthday party; same face but drastically different glasses & hairstyle, more goth-y clothing.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni sometimes ponder who i am now, who i\\\'ve become, and who i will be this time next year.  but what about looking back to see how i got here?  and what has made me this way? it\\\'s an entirely different thing.</P>'),(3017,'<p>\r\nthis country is can crazy. cans cans cans. what the can can do, the box can not. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwater in a can. coffee in a can. fiber in a can. energy in a can. sauce in can, forks in a can. toothpaste in a can. shampoo in a can. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni live a dream here in the co.kr. i live a dream not dreamt up by me, but by mothers and fathers who want their sons and daughters to learn from others the gift of gab in eglish. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow sad they are, these people of intentions. of image, and perceptions.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperceptions of a world they know little about but want so much of. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthey are proud of their culture, their traditions, and ways, but they want so much of us, they will do anything for change. they will clip parts of their tongue to say L instead of R, they will dress up in prada and pierre cartier because they think that\\\'s their call. they want to be better than the one next to them, but they probably just want to be better than those in japan.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni have a job because im white, not talented or proper. if i were black i\\\'d have no job offer. a korean of the same if not better english grammar would be paid far less than me; for knowing the language isnt really what matters. im paid because im white. i\\\'m paid because im foreign. i\\\'m paid because i\\\'m tall, and i\\\'m paid because i\\\'m western. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhether my kids want to learn is of no concern to me. i babysit the young ones and joke with the teens. i play go fish and scrabble and white board boggle. the young kids still think lemon is spelled with an R and well i dont really care, because they are only 7. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe days go by fast during the week and slowly during the end. i can\\\'t wait for this job to finish, i\\\'m getting sick and tired of it. i\\\'m so sick and tired of it. i have been dumbed down here. i need to communicate with people who have little to communicate about. my world is different from theirs and i\\\'m lost in its social constructs. i\\\'m lost for the most part because i have little understanding and tolerance for the evil i see here. i can understand the evil i see in canada because i have been around it. the origins are somewhat visible...but here they are lost in the smells and sights of each alley i walk through. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m in good spirits i guess some of the time, but its because i have closed a lot of doors. when the doors were open i drank and was sad. now that those doors are closed i\\\'m stable. i smile. i smoke less. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m hungry. i hope the chinese restaurant is open.\r\n</p>\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3018,'so much time building fire in the back of my mind</br>\r\nblocked by the urge of running in the open</br>\r\nhopes and dreams blocked by so much frustration</br>\r\nso much anger, so much it could move a nation</br>\r\nthere\\\'s no way this could be a stepping stone</br>\r\nall this time I felt shoved from place to place\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve got to find a new place, I\\\'ve got to find the right way to deal with this\r\n<p>\r\nthe surefire thing to do is to follow a one way road?</br>\r\nthe best way to go is to conform with the worst?</br>\r\nare we crazy for letting go of chains being free,</br>\r\nfor hitting the brakes, and stopping to get our heads clear?</br>'),(3019,'<p>\r\ndont fuck with us. yeah we know english. most korean pussy knows english. living in the streets will do that to a cat. very few koreans trust felines. its our eyes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nrecently met a couple of english teachers up in the geumho hills. me and my recon squad of about twelve cats were shading under a parked car on the way to family mart, just before you get to the great staircase of geumho. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none of them. ill call him plumpy actually had the nerve to initiate contact with scratchy, the fysty cat we found just outside the hanyang university subway tunnel. he was wounded. apparently he had faught for over three cat hours trying to defend his freshly killed rat dinner, but the yaksu half tail posse invaded his killzone and fucked that feline up. so we picked him up. we were on our way back from an assembly of cats up near Cheongnayanni. what a trip that turned out to be. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile the rest of us got up and ran like the pussies we are, scratchy stood his ground hissing with his arched back that didnt really arch all that well because of his encounter with the half tails. plumpy backed off and headed for the stairs.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nscratchy hated humans. he had been thrown out at a young age, growing up dodging the fuckers on the scooters who made it their pleasure trying and run over cats. geumho is littered with the bodies of our comrades in fur. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile he is somewhat justified in his hatred of humans, he also has to understand that their waste is our feast, and that without those beautiful blue bags of slop our lives would be harder, and would require more hunting and less sleeping. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are two kinds of waste. there is the solid slop and the liquid slop. we can eat both, but our new tactic is to wait until the mice arrive. its like taking candy from a baby. once and a while we\\\'ll get a real treat. a big sewer rat will emerge, and all of us will pounce and play and then circle the kill before we ravage its carcass for that protein we need to keep our fur silky smooth. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin recent months geumho has become quite the stopover spot for cats on the move. the open fish market makes it a magnet for cats who are for one reason or another down and out; looking for a nice place to call home. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ngeumho has its fair share of hiding spots. the labrynth of alleys makes it an ideal place to hide and lurk about without much notice, but up on the hill its more dangerous, with kids and scooters all looking to kill. kill kill kill! thats all thats on the mind of most koreans. how frustrated they are with life. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperhaps the best place to hide and rest is the abandoned building across from the english school. its been a concrete shell for over eight years now; the developers running with the loot before completion of the project. the open shell of the building provides us with a good place to sit out the rain storms and winter wind. humans dont go there because its a hazard. the elements have made the concrete weak over the years and so it is a cats paradise, free from the dangers involved with living on the same land as humans. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na few weeks ago i got word from some of my runners that plumpy was seen trying to make contact with other cats from the geumho posse. he was different. he didnt try to kick or catch us. could he be trusted? i still didnt know, but i had three of my best cats keep an eye on him.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin a few cat hours, me and my gang of about three hundred are going to go camp out the back of the crustations depot. every thursday afternoon the trucks arrive with their open styrophome containers of shellfish. we are planning an all out raid of the facility. we\\\'ve got delta paw on the roof ready to go down through the staircase, and about a dozen other squads ready to take the sides and front door. its a daring raid, but its something we have to do to prove our capability.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe idea came to us after meeting a ragtag group of cats that had smuggled themselves over on a japanese textile tanker. they called themselves the catnips. they offered to join our crew if we could prove to them we were the shit. me and their leader kenji came up with the idea while sunning on the roof of plumpies apartment. you could get a good look at the depot from there and we noticed that every thursday they got their shipment of gold. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif the raid is a sucess, we\\\'ll be the most powerful of all the seoul cat gangs. no one will step! the moment of truth is coming fast. wish us luck. \r\n</p>'),(3020,'<p>i love that water in the ocean layers itself.  </p>\r\n<p>i like that when you dive in you start in steaming sun and end up weaving around in currents that carry different temperatures of water.  i like immersing myself in in water where my hands play in warm, my stomach is chilled, and my toes absolutely freeze.</p>\r\n<p>i like that water is the most relaxing bed i can find. no springs or foam, a rocking bed to catnap in, the weight of my body supported only by water tension, hair floating all around in a non-descript pattern.</p>\r\n<p>i like that water lets me only sleep in one position.  \\\"eighty percent of women who sleep on their stomach have secret loves.\\\" (~maurice chevalier, whom my big brother will not stop quoting to me).</p>\r\n<p>i like that water loves to lap at me, playing and dancing on and tickling my skin, jumping over the smallest part of my shin, coming to rest on the curve of my stomach or between my breasts forming in tiny, circular droplets.</p>\r\n<p>i like that water is so comforting, so soothing, better than any blanket i\\\'ve found.  i like that being near it feels being home.\r\n'),(3021,'<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">\r\n<P>\r\nsteel ribbon and baby back hanginâ€™ inside the car: slabs, guts, and the stuff that runs into the drain: red, yellow, still bubbling and popping - active decomposition - all the way downstream, washing into the lake and up on the beach, underfoot against the coarse grain of the cool sand in the morning before the kids come to play: avoiding the broken glass - mastering the pick and weave up to the rocks, over the paint, and especially the new American flag near the old men on the bench - it too was made of concrete: dull, gray, limestone dug from a hole in the ground, to the south or to the west, and carried on a big wheel turning on the steel ribbon</P>'),(3022,'\nFloaters and debris. <BR>\nSand swirling around your feet in the shower after a day at the beach.\nTiny flakes of gold in the dorm room Goldshlagger bottle. <BR>\nA turd in a punchbowl. Vagrants floating down the Hudson River.  <BR>\nSediment -- particles suspended in liquid.<BR><BR>\nThe summer house pool with leaves on top: floating, drifting. <BR>\nEvery day the leaves must be raked. The raking never ends. Purification is hard work. The leaves will win. Leaves make for good mulch.<BR><BR>\nThe universe is filled with garbage.<BR> \nFloaters in space.<BR>\nEverything is sediment suspended in pure mind-ether space.<BR><BR>\nAnother day and I\'m grasping at the floaters in my mind.<BR>\nThe past a floating association minefield. Release the depthcharges. Enemy sub below.<BR>\nIcebergs.\n<BR><BR>\nA barrel on my back filled with fish.<BR>\nThe sweaty neck, the hot breath, the barrel on my back, splashing.<BR>\nWhere are the fish? Is the water pure? Do I put the barrel down? The pool needs\ncleaning...<BR>\nWhy do I need to know? Is the Ph balance correct? Can the questions ever end? Does the grasping ever end? Do the objects ever end?<BR><BR>\n\nThis is another piece of garbage floating by. Does it smell? Where does it come from? What does it offer me?<BR><BR>\nWater is pure. Sediment is sediment. <BR>\nThis is just another floater in the pure ether pool of mind -- your mind.<BR><BR>\nSediment settles down. Take note. <BR><BR>\nI long to bathe. And drink from a well.<BR>\n~<BR>\n~<BR>\n~<BR>\n'),(3023,'<P>He always wanted to eat in the kitchen.  The first time he knocked on the screen door that went out to the alley, I thought it was one of the bums who come up wanting a free meal.  I was standing at the range making creme brulee, and I hear knuckles on the doorframe and this \"Excuse me?  Hello.\"  Without looking up from the saucepan, I was about to tell whoever it was to come back after nine when something about the voice struck me as familiar.  He was standing there shading his eyes, peering in through the fly shit.  Looked just like he did in the movies, only seedier.  Beatriz and Oswaldo were out there by the garbage cans getting high, looking him over all nonchalant.</P><P>I let him in.  He had on a suit, no tie, sandals.  Greasy-looking hair.  Said he wanted to avoid any Fox executives who might be in the dining room.  I\'d seen in the paper how the movie he was directing was fifteen million over budget, so he had good reason to lay low.  I kind of half-listened to him explain himself while I kept one eye on the new Salvadoran kid taking angelhair out of the steam kettle.  Anthony came in, looking kind of puzzled, but glad-handed him just the same, cleared off the prep table by the freezer and got him a chair from out front.</P><P>He wanted the same thing every night: shrimp cocktail, half a lobster, mashed potatoes and green beans.  We have a fixed menu - you pay a hundred bucks and get whatever we think you should have, five set courses that change every night.  Of course, anytime somebody requests something different, we go ahead and fix it for them: Anthony\'s not stupid.</P><P>He wasn\'t hard to please.  Came in the same time every night, so I had the stuff ready when he got there.  Gave him instant potatoes and he never noticed.  One night I decided to throw him a change-up and gave him something different.  Didn\'t like it.  \"Look,\" I said, \"You know how much cholesterol there is in shrimp and lobster?  Tonight, you\'re gonna have a salad.\"  I didn\'t know that that was the day they took the movie away from him.</P><P>He went away for awhile after that.  Was living in Paris with this married couple - a Swedish girl and a huge black dude, and their daughter.  He brought them with him when he came back out here.  Brought them to the restaurant to meet me one time.  They all ate in the kitchen.</P><P>His luck kind of ran funny from there on out.  He was dropping a load in Vegas every month, and a lot of his money went into his arm, I think.  Started to look like the rest of the regulars who came to the back door.</P><P>I said to him one time, \"Look, anybody who can read a recipe can cook.  You should save some money and fix yourself something at home once in a while.\"  He goes, \"Yeah, but then I wouldn\'t get to see you.\"  He was sweet.</P><P>He always held his head up, even toward the end when there was that unpleasantness with his wife and the jail time.  The media blew that all out of proportion.  All I know is, he used to wash his own dishes.  And that made him aces in my book.</P>'),(3024,'<p>\r\nWave after wave of bitter resentment wash over me, bathing me in self pity. I\\\'d drown my sorrows, but the beer is making my stomach sick.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA dull pain inside my head reminds me I haven\\\'t eaten today. It\\\'s 11:11PM. No, I had toast this morning. Was that <i>this</i> morning? Without the punctuation provided by sleep, several days can quite easily merge together.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\\\"She\\\'s not worth it\\\" \\\"You can do better\\\"</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFuckin\\\' easy for you to say though yeh? Don\\\'t give me that falafel. Those meaty morsels of advice-on-a-stick. That bullshit. That bullshit I don\\\'t need thankyou.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need a hooker.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need a distraction\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need to stop feeling sorry for myself\r\n</p>'),(3025,'<p>\r\nHe told me to inhale as much as I could, breathe it in, breathe it in deeply.  I held it.  Something happened, and I remember saying \\\"Woah\\\", and hearing people around me say something like \\\"Haha, she said woah!\\\" - except I couldn\\\'t hold on to the voices, they swam around my head and echoed through every part of myself and the universe.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThen, blackness.  Later I was told it was blackness with a smile.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I finally regained control of my head, I was floating, I was one with the universe, and my vision returned, giving me images of what I was floating within, blue sky and thick cotton ball clouds.  They were almost digital, warping in to each other and myself.  I had no awareness of my physical body.  Things were good, briefly.  Not that I knew this at the time.  I recall no thought process, I wasn\\\'t thinking at all, just simply experiencing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThen something snapped, perhaps the return to the physical realm.  I became aware of my breathing, it was heavy and uneven.  I looked down at my hands and somewhere along the way I had dropped what I was holding and my fists were clenched.  I tried to lift my head but it felt like a weight, it felt un-naturally heavy.  I lay back and told myself to enjoy the ride, I knew what I was doing, and I should appreciate the experience while it lasted.  It didn\\\'t work.  Panic and terror set in.  I knew that I was messing with a chemical released in the brain when you die, so I somehow convinced myself that I wasn\\\'t going to survive.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI was so hot, so very hot.  It took me a long time to figure out that that was what was wrong.  I looked around and apparently had held a captive audience, including my best friend who had not been there when I first inhaled.  She looked worried.  I thought about speaking, but it was like my mouth wasn\\\'t connected to my brain.  I knew what I wanted to say, and it was simple enough - water, water - but nothing came out, until I said \\\"I don\\\'t like this\\\" without realising.  I said it a few more times, and then asked for water.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was then I started to feel the worst nausea I have ever felt.  I ran to the toilet and vomited everywhere, and felt immediately better.  Emotionally though, I was broken.  After a good cry I looked up, and those cotton ball clouds I had appreciated earlier had formed into looming dark storm clouds.  I felt the storm coming.  And I was reborn.  What a perfect beginning.\r\n</p><p>'),(3026,'<p>All manner of six-legged things practice their peculiar rhythms in gentle discord: white noise of the prairie sea, whose verdant stalks are still on this windless late-summer night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>No hammer of thunder will crack the thick air.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My sticky flesh, my shallow breath, and my tired eyes fade.  I remember how the ceiling fan spun above our love, and sweat ran down your back: brine and brackish fingers interlaced.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh that I should have loved <i>you</i>, and not your body - or the limits to which you would take it.  You made me blush; but red-faced and wide-eyed I would always continue.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Meeting at the front door you halted the forward progress of my tradesman\\\'s tools, and sent me off to bathe.  But you wanted me dirty; and I had you with abandon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Into the deep, deep well where something human like your soul should have been: I would hurl myself.  You wanted so badly to feel a heat - alive and moving inside you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A decade past: your cheap perfume is a trigger.  Windless late-summer night marries the chair to the hard, sweaty flesh of my back - only memories in attendance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And everything with six legs plays on.</p>'),(3027,'<PRE>\r\n     !!!!        \r\n  !!!!!!!!       \r\n!!!!!!!!!!!   O_O\r\n!!!  !!!!!!! /@ @\\\\\r\n      !!!!!! \\\\ x /\r\n      !!!!!!/ m  !m\r\n       !!!!/ __  |\r\n       !!!!|/  \\\\__\r\n        !!!\\\\______\\\\\r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\nIt is Tue Sep 10 21:32:29 PDT 2002.  I just ate two tacos, had a cup\r\nof coffee.  I don\\\'t know what to do with myself.  There are jobs to\r\napply for.  I saw one in San Antonio.  One in Austin.  Cold instant\r\ncoffee.  Border rebellion.  Simon\\\'s juke box is plugged into the wall\r\nsocket pulling electricity from the grid.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThere was a movie on television about a woman who goes to Africa with\r\nher new husband.  He turns out to be a playboy, ditching her at the\r\nfarm to toil and fight off tigers while he is out shooting game.  She\r\ngets real sick of his shit fast.  Eventually, after about 10 years,\r\nhis car rolls over on him and he dies of head injuries.  Good ridden.\r\nShould have thrown that one back, honey.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAfrica looks like Marfa, Texas.  I think they shipped elephants to\r\nMarfa, Texas, and filmed there to save money.  There is a town in west\r\nTexas with a large snow monkey population.  Why not elephants?\r\nRoaming in the streets and knocking over garbage cans for goodies.\r\nAh, the open range.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThis is really pointless.  I don\\\'t have any stories in me these days.\r\nJust spinning slowly, enough to generate the gravity to keep my planet\r\ntogether but not much else.  The moon left orbit.  The tide winds are\r\nshifting.  I was in a car with Earth First! people the other day.\r\nThey were talking about the Earth slowing down and the polar magnetic\r\nmumbo jumbo adjusting, dominant winds switching from this direction to\r\nthat one.  They talked as though this was going to happen in their\r\nlifetime.  Atheists that need to get mentally ready for the\r\nApocalypse.  The great plains, they said, was going to have 400 mile\r\nper hour winds blowing up out of Mexico.  One of them said, what are all\r\nthese people going to do?  I said, that is easy, they will blow.  And\r\nMinnesota becomes a sand dune.  No pot for me thanks.  I don\\\'t smoke.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI don\\\'t have a book to read.  That is why I used to have that dumb\r\nmovie book thing, because there are always movie books.  I ditched\r\nthat though.  Finding good reading is difficult.  I ordered 4 books\r\nfrom Amazon, who decided having enough people standing around to send\r\nout orders in 12 hours cost to much, my books will be here whenever\r\nthey get around to sending them.  They are part of my Leonard\r\ncollection, these books.  Recommended by the man himself.  God Bless\r\nLeonard.  When the Apocalypse does come, there is one atheist who\r\ndoes not need to worry about what is going to happen to him.  Bastard.\r\n</P><P>\r\nDowntown last night licking a chocolate ice cream cone like there is\r\nno tomorrow, a street person named Pogo took the extra space on the\r\npark bench.  He was waiting for his marijuana hook up.  Hail Mary,\r\nFull of Grace, Please hurry up.  He kept saying that over and over,\r\nand asking me if that was a Jeep over there?  We talked a little\r\npolitics of the streets.  New legislation that he can\\\'t loiter in\r\ndowntown or rest himself against a wall.  Fucking hippies.  Pogo isn\\\'t\r\nafraid of the Apocalypse.  \\\"I am going to quit smoking weed.  I\r\npromise to God.  Sometime in the next year.  I am going to quit.\r\nSmoking too.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nI thought the entire time that he was leading into a question of my\r\nstance on the Apocalypse.  I was prepared to answer him.  I\\\'d been\r\nrehearsing from a packet that my new Earth First!  friends gave me. \\\"Are you detaining me?  I choose to remain silent.  I want a lawyer.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nTalking to street people downtown is my new pastime.  All that is\r\nrequired is to sit down in their living room.  They love when you stop\r\nby like that.  Pull up a lounging fence or piece of landscape\r\nstructure.\r\n</P>'),(3028,'homeless people come in all shapes and sizes.  pat robertson and the religious right blame it on sin.  the clintons blamed it on the breakdown of the family (it takes a village(...\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere are surely a lot of reasons for it.  the homeless people you see living in the streets of big cities are just the tip of the iceberg.\r\n<br><br>\r\nin my family, we have a homeless man.  his name is homer bales and once upon a time he was married to josephine and they had three very pretty children and lived in a house that they owned in tampa. homer worked somewhere, i forget where, but he had a steady job.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen they had been married about 15 years, they found homer\\\'s paycheck did not cover the needs of the family, so josephine got a job somewhere at night so homer could watch the kids and they \r\nwouldn\\\'t have to pay a babysitter, i don\\\'t remember where.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhomer, being a cro-magnon man, was ashamed of his wife having to work, and he began drinking too much while josephine was at work night, and josephine became unhappy because of homer drinking and they started arguing a lot, then josephine met a guy at work and had an affair and she and homer ended up divorced.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthey were both in their late thirties or so when this happened, about 20 years ago.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhomer moved back in with his parents and that didn\\\'t work out well, so he moved in with some friends of his and that didn\\\'t work out well, and pretty soon he was so miserable and lonely and drinking so much, he lost his job.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthen he sort of disappeared off the family radar.\r\n<br><br>\r\na few years ago, at a family reunion, i learned that josephine had died of breast cancer, the kids were all grown and happy and just fine... \r\n<br><br>\r\nand homer was living in a stolen u-haul trailer in fort myers, fl. where he supported his crack habit by stealing. nobody in the family wanted anything to do with him, in fact, they are all afraid of him.\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou have to understand that homer was from a large extended family, a southern family that always has taken care of it\\\'s own. what one has, the others will always share. if you are down and out, you can go live with katy and fred on their farm, or sleep on the sofa bed at geneva\\\'s house, or get a loan from jeana kay or something..\r\n<br><br>\r\nuntil he was divorced, homer led a mundane existance and his house was one of the family havens.\r\n<br><br>\r\nso, what caused homer to be homeless?  if the drinking caused him to be divorced and then to lose his job, perhaps you could say the drinking caused his homelessness.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmaybe if homer had been given a better education, he may not have been working in a job that didn\\\'t cover the expenses of his family, then josephine wouldn\\\'t have gone to work, and he would never have started drinking too much and she would not have had an affair and they would still be married and ....\r\n<br><br>\r\nor maybe not. maybe homer had some sort of emotional crisis as he hit middle age and just wanted to run away.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think it\\\'s all about education.  if homer and josephine had better tools before they got married, their reactions to adversity would have been less climactic, and they might have survived the downturn in their fortunes.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni might be wrong, i often am.'),(3029,'i\\\'ve always been a night person, but as i grow older i have found that i have more control over my insomnia. one of the ways i\\\'ve done that is to play the insomnia game.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni don\\\'t count sheep.  i create a utopian society for celebrities.  i realize this is weird, but i\\\'ve begun to think it is really interesting enough that someone else might want to play the game, too.\r\n<br><br>\r\nfirst you have to think of yourself as a brilliant scientist of the future with cloning ability.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthen you have to decide just exactly what the perfect society/town IS.\r\n<br><br>\r\nfor me, it\\\'s a turn of the century southern town with a small town square and all of the houses have front porches and swings.  there are no automobiles. everyone has a kitchen garden and some of the inhabitants are farmers and there is a livery stable and wagons for hire to haul large items and horse drawn buggies when needed.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe people who live in my town are a mixture of fictional characters and real celebrities who i think need a chance at a different life.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe sherrif is andy taylor, the barber is floyd, also from mayberry.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni change the celebrities depending on my mood, and the rules are...\r\n<br><br>\r\neveryone begins life in this town as a perfect physical specimen, women are all physically 18, men are all physically 21.  any genetic abnormailities have been cured in the lab.  persons who were gay in their lifetimes are no longer gay.  persons who died of dread diseases won\\\'t get them. people who were infertile in this life don\\\'t have to worry about that in utopia. the only reason for the doctor is to help birth babies and take care of accidents.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere are 24 houses in the town. the laws are very strict and based on the golden rule - do unto others as you would have them do unto you.\r\n<br><br>\r\nevery night when i can\\\'t sleep, i choose some celebrites, living or dead, who will come to live in my town and have a second chance at life and they have to have a job that contributes to the town.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsometimes david letterman runs the grocery store and diana spencer is the librarian.  christopher reeve is the doctor some nights, other times he owns the livery stable. janis joplin owns the diner and jackie onassis owns the ladies wear boutique.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsex outside marriage is against the law, punishable by evaporation. everyone who marries stays married and likes it.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit is a very pleasant insomnia game. i rarely get past the first ten people who are chosen for the evening\\\'s festivities before i drop off to sleep.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s better than the one where you just won the lottery and are trying to decide which charitable organizations to help with your hundred million dollars...\r\n'),(3030,'<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">\r\n<p><b>The families</b> camped under trees.<br>  During the hot summer days, one family - an extended family - camped under each of the largest elm, or ash, or mulberry trees.<br>\r\nThey lived in the clay ovens that were their common brick apartments.<br> \r\nThey lived in the neighborhoods west of the lake.<br>\r\nIn June, July, and August, it got really damn hot.<br>\r\nAnd everyone went down to the beach.<br>\r\nMany kids had no suits to swim in; they went in whatever they had.<br>\r\nWhen the kids came out of the water, their parents dried their things.<br>\r\nOn the park benches and 55 gallon drums that belonged to the City of Chicago, they dried the clothing.<br>\r\nI was a kid there too.<br>\r\nBut my grandparents wouldn\\\'t let me touch the garbage cans, let alone put my clothing on them.<br>\r\nEvery so often the old city will peek through, and a few people in their 50\\\'s or 60\\\'s will yell at someone and tell them that what they are doing is wrong.<br>\r\nBut more often than not, no one says or does anything.<br>\r\nDespite the fact that we shared the same physical space, I wasn\\\'t a part of the same community as the people that I took pictures of - at least not in any way that really counted.<br>\r\nI could have explained what I was doing: documenting life in the city.<br>\r\nBut there were barriers between us in addition to language.<br>\r\nAnd the idea that I should have enjoyed a dolphin balloon floating in the trees wouldn\\\'t have made much sense to them.</p>'),(3031,'<p>My little kitty died the other night.  I\\\'d gone to some friend\\\'s birthday party.  My roomie, who originally found Squeaky in the middle of the highway in front of our house about 2 weeks ago and then thrust her on me to keep her alive, decided that the sheets needed to be washed.  So, she scooped the sheets off the floor of the laundry room, tossed them in the washer and that was that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Only Squeaky was in the sheets.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I originally called her \\\'Puff\\\' because that\\\'s all she was, a puff of a little gray kitty with white tippy toes and a white shirt front.  She was shivery and in kitty shock having been \\\'rescued\\\' from the center stripe of the highway in front of the house.</p >\r\n\r\n<p>I went to town and bought her a nursing bottle and some kitty formula and hand fed her for nearly two weeks.  We bonded big time.  She would climb up on me and sit and \\\'thru-u-u-u-m-m-m-m\\\' on my shoulder, lick my ear lobe and generally make me be glad there was one life around here who was glad to see me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She had the biggest eyes, soaking up the big world around her.  After all when you are only 3 inches (ok, ok, 10 cm) at the shoulder, everything is big to you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I came home from the party early \\\'cuz the party was at a campground that ran off every one who hadn\\\'t paid to stay the night.  I hadn\\\'t, so I came on home.  I asked my roomie where Squeaky was and she told me she was in the washroom.  I went in there, called her name and she didn\\\'t come out.  Well, that wasn\\\'t too unusual, after all how many of us responded to our name when we were a month old?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Later, my roomie went into the washroom to put the sheets in the dryer...and came stumbling out saying, \\\"[xipibi], I killed Squeaky!!! I washed her!!! She is in the washing machine!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I knew instantly that what she said was true without going to confirm it... I couldn\\\'t feel Squeaky\\\'s life force anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I consoled my roomie by saying it was strictly an accident and that I didn\\\'t blame her for it happening.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And I still don\\\'t; however, I do miss the \\\'thruuummm\\\' at my ear...</p>'),(3032,'i spend a lot of time building links pages on my <a href=\\\"http//www.auntie.com\\\">auntie.com</a> site.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwomen come to my links looking for commentary on what the sites contain, then decide whether or not to visit them.  \r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nit takes hours and hours to build and maintain links pages of any merit.  this week i have been building links to <a href=\\\"http://www.auntie.com/scrappingsuppliers.asp\\\">scrapbooking sites</a> as this is the hot new thing among my demographic visitors, who are generally female and between 30 and 50 years of age.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'ve been looking at hundreds, and i am not kidding, it\\\'s hundreds, of scrapbooking sites, retail, personal, and corporate.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m sick of it. more than half the retail sites sit on some sort of safeshopper.com yahoo type generated storefront and look like total shit, and the ones that aren\\\'t wysiwyg generated sites are either loaded for bear in the way of flash, java, animations, etc. or so badly done they make me cringe.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat do *I* want on the web?  i want to shop at a store that has a menu i can figure out easily, if there is a whole lot of stuff, i want a searh engine, but if there aren\\\'t thousands of items, i don\\\'t mind doing a browse through believable product sections.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni do not want flash, java aps, pop up beggars trying to get me to look at their frigging specials or sign up for their newsletter.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni do NOT want to visit an angelfire, yahoo/geocities/aol etc. site with their annoying multi-spawning popup ads.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\ni just want them to keep it simple.  i want to know where the store is located and who owns it.  i want to know who to call or email if i need to ask a question, and i don\\\'t care if it is jazzy or not, i just want to find the stuff.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nis it just me?  are you sick of all the bells and whistles, too?\r\n'),(3033,'<center>\r\n<table width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"100%\\\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td width=\\\"100%\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" colspan=\\\"3\\\">\r\n<SPAN STYLE=\\\"font-family: arial; font-size: 10pt;\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"hello\\\" i hear again.  this time i\\\'m expecting it, and i hear it come from a layer  behind my ears, just inside my head.  the voice is familiar, it sounds almost like mine does when it comes out of my mouth.  but it doesn\\\'t come out of my mouth, it doesn\\\'t even get past my ears.  it\\\'s there, i hear it, and it\\\'s only halfway distinct from my own.\r\n\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_1.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t reply to the hello.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"i will show where to go. you will need me.  i am awake when you need me. \\\"\r\n\r\n<p>a pause.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"breathe more slowly now.  your lungs need it.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>i comply and slow my breathing.  i feel like i\\\'m sighing, repeatedly.  everything around me adjusts.  it doesn\\\'t feel that things are brighter, but i can see more detail through the dimness. i am still sitting on the moss.  the woman is still beside me, no time has passed; the conversation with the slug was instantaneous, like an injected memory.\r\n\r\n<p>i rejoin my conversation with her.  i make a conscious effort to transition my focus to her, and it takes more effort than i expect.  i notice that she is breathing slowly, like me.  we meet eyes and i see acknowledgement of our mutual condition with the slugs.  it is the first communal feeling i\\\'ve had since i boarded the ship.  how long ago was that?\r\n\r\n<p>she says to me: \\\"the settler said we have to get on one of those vehicles, and they\\\'ll take us to the settlement\\\".\r\n\r\n<p>i stand up slowly and disengage myself from the ground.   i have not stood on my own two feet for a very long time, but they\\\'re sturdy.  i feel connected with time again as my head rises up above my feet, my hips, my chest.  resume.  complication.  change.  i see hundreds of my co-passengers walking toward many waiting transport vehicles.  everyone is calm and collected.  so many different faces.  these people are not from my city, i think.\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"you don\\\'t have a city\\\", i hear, faintly replied.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_2.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"left\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_6.gif\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" height=\\\"275\\\" width=\\\"4\\\">before i can think on this, i become distracted by the vehicles toward which we\\\'re all walking. i see that they\\\'re greyish slabs, thick, and square.  and they\\\'re hovering, just a few feet off of the ground.  i watch as people climb aboard them, assisted by settlers who\\\'ve come out here to help us.  they are all very big, and their clothing is much darker than ours.  there are about thirty people per transport, all standing, elevated just above the dark shadow cast by their platform.  the woman pulls my arm and i follow her toward a transport.  we are still surrounded by impossibly large trees, thrusting  high up in to the darkness above us.  i now see undergrowth, taller than me, giant green leaves rich with supple shadows.  they sway lazily amongst the huge tree roots.\r\n\r\n<p>in our clearing the transports are gathered in a few rows, and we passengers walk toward them.  anti gravity devices like this were never to common for me, born into a lower class, so i marvel at their simplicity as i approach.\r\n\r\n<p>i come to a transport, step aboard it and then sit down, cross legged, because that\\\'s what everyone else is doing.  as soon as i sit down i hear \\\"hand to the platform\\\" and it happens; the back of my hand with the slug on it turns over while moving downward, and presses against the platform.  quickly into me comes a sense of balancing from knowing how the platform <i>moves</i>.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_3.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"right\\\">one of the settlers behind me mutters something deeply, and we slide smoothly into motion.  i am sitting on the front left corner of the slab, others seated behind me and to my right.  we quickly gain velocity, blurring the greens and shadows around us.  it is silent except for our clothing grasping at the wind. everyone is held in irreverent silence while the vegetation gradually subsides, until we are gliding across nothing.  small rocks and plants that pass beneath us are smoothed into one continuous shadow, and i feel patient.\r\n\r\n<p>i focus on the transport in front of me, watch the shapes of the people sitting cross legged in rows, just like my transport.  everyone\\\'s hands are pressed with their slug against the transport surface.  i watch and listen to their clothes reach back through the wind for me, and i feel hypnotized like when i stare into a fire.  after some time, i look up the side of our caravan of hovering transports and see a light growing larger in the distance, dim but noticeable.  it grows into the settlement.\r\n\r\n<p>there are simple mounds of moss pushing up from the ground in geometric shapes.  they don\\\'t have sharp edges, or the most accurate angles, but the geometry is everywhere. i can smell the green in their houses.  some have small windows through which light is shining.  the light feels warm even as we step off of the transports, collecting in pools on the ground.  i move to take my hand up from the transport\\\'s surface, the slug sticks for a moment and it is stretched between my retracting hand and the platform.  immediately i feel a jolt in my spine and a lot of confused words.  it is unpleasant and i want it to stop.  the slug\\\'s body stretches its limit and snaps back to my hand.  i expect to hear it protest,  but it does not.\r\n\r\n<p>ahead of me walks a settler.  he\\\'s taller than most of us, as are the other settlers i see walking between the closely built houses.  there is a lot of movement.  we are shown down a disorienting series of corridors and turns, but i feel that i am safe here.  the buildings all grow out of the land, connected cubes and lengths, and are low, many not reaching above my chest.  we are being shown to our lodgings for the night.  i wonder, is there only night here?\r\n\r\n<p align=\\\"left\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_4.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"left\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3033_6.gif\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" height=\\\"275\\\" width=\\\"4\\\">a light breeze pushes through the settlement, smells green, and i hear the subtle fold and ripple of small moist leaves that sprout everywhere from the moss that covers the buildings.  the moss softens the breeze in a way i\\\'ve never felt.  why is nobody talking?  probably something to do with our slugs.  it doesn\\\'t bother me because i\\\'m too busy staring at everything around me.  we stop in front of a smaller building and a settler motions us to move into this building.  he addresses me by a window to the building that spills open with light.  i see that he has a strong and elegant face and it is bigger than mine, but his back is to the window so much detail is hidden in shadows.\r\n\r\n<p>he says deeply \\\"sleep here and we will come for you when you have rested\\\".  his face slides into more shadows and he walks away to address other waiting passengers.\r\n</span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</center>'),(3035,'<p>\r\n<pre>\r\nall rights reserved.\r\n\r\n\r\n<u>these rights</u> \r\n \r\nsuv in that colour you like is reserved. \r\ntable at vogue bistro is reserved. \r\ntee off at the country club is reserved. \r\nfirst class tickets to monacco are reserved. \r\n\r\n<u>in exchange for these ones.</u> \r\n\r\neducation isnt reserved. \r\nhealth care isnt reserved. \r\naffordable shelter isnt reserved. \r\n\r\nclean water isnt reserved. \r\nclean air isnt reserved. \r\n\r\npro choice isnt reserved. \r\ngm food information isnt reserved. \r\n\r\nnatives ARE reserved. \r\n\r\nfreedom isnt reserved. \r\ndemocracy isnt reserved. \r\ninformation isnt reserved.\r\n\r\n<u>2% sweet. 98% sour</u> '),(3036,'<P>\r\nMy children make me whole. They break my heart every day, or is it me that breaks theirs? I see their faces, sometimes an expression is caught in my memory forever - a smile of delight, a chubby finger reaching for me. A three year old, who knows that I like my hair stroked. A five year old who wants to be \\\'good\\\' not knowing that no matter what he does, the depth of my love for him will never change. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI see their hurt when I brush them off. I watch them play out my anger, standing in front of the mirror making hideous faces. Do I really look like this when I am angry? I ask them, and they laguh and say yes. I tell them when Mummy looks like that they can tell me I look silly.....they like that idea.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI want everything for them. I want them to never have to go to therapy to deal with childhood issues, yet I fear I am already too late. Such guilt. Have I failed? I did not fit the mould, I could not be the mother or wife I was supposed to be, the one I wanted to be.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd yet still they love me. Still they tell me every day how beautiful I am, and still they want me when they have fallen, or been disappointed. How long will this last? Breaking my heart, as I break theirs, the difference being, they break mine with love, I break theirs with anger. So small, so helpless, so loved, I ache with pride. Am I worthy of their love? Mostly, I think not.\r\n</P>'),(3037,'<P>\r\nAt the bar last night after three drinks I stumbled to the men\\\'s room to relieve myself in the scum layer of the parade.  Teetering over the stainless steel trough, watching the flying insect world spin and dodge, I hoped not to catch to West Nile Virus.\r\n</P><P><B>\r\nMan Dies Of West Nile Virus In Rank Restroom\r\n</B></P><P>\r\nWhat a bugs life.  Feeding, flying, and humming around crotches, they dodge streams of urine.  It is enough to send me into the book store for a volume on reincarnation.  Poor souls of the urine trough, shape up or it will get worse.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMarkers and stickers adorn the walls.  Looking at them is not reading them.  Taking in the pastiche of the American public toilet room, a gathering place, a watering hole of sorts, out of Africa, remember to dis-infects the bottoms of the shoes before entering the house.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe textures are marvelous paint flecked cinder block walls, exposed concrete floors worn in paths and eaten by urine acid, rusted chrome piping, and brown yellow streaked porcelain.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOverhead flourecent lights that make the filth beautiful have the imperceptible flicker that the mind can feel, and that turns the air a jaundice hue.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOutside the fluorescence and behind the eyes is the vibrating screech of noise.  It enlivens perception, taking the attention away from any one thing, and introducing me to my surroundings.  The noise keeps me from being repulsed by drowning out my attention and blocking my instinctive reaction.  A pact with the noise, submit to the noise, and see all that is around at this moment or continue to see nothing that I will remember.\r\n</P><P>\r\n'),(3038,'<P>\r\nive downloaded numberous roots reggae songs in an attempt to slow the pc bong down. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni certainly miss the opportunity to go sit in a park in toronto. to walk down my suburban street and stroll through through the grassed area that is the martingrove reservoir and pumping station. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nkicking up dust in a little league baseball diamond is much more relaxing than kicking the dust up on the pavement of the concrete jungle.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\n<tt>\r\ni say that life, sweet life, must be somewhere to be found. instead concrete jungle. confusion. \r\n</tt>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin an around my head swirl the thoughts of people, places, solutions, problems, and ideas. im finding my life more and more connected to my thoughts than i had previously thought possible. im finding myself in situations where i talk to myself as if i were another person. im finding out that i dont hate as much as i did; that what i thought was hate was rather frustration and confusion concerning people and my emotions. not hate. not loathing. irrational acts of momentary rage. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni woke up today flusted. a dream involving an old crush, the impending war in iraq, and some very very shady characters encountered through life\\\'s adventures. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits impossible for me to describe the dream. the moment i open my eyes the dream as a whole shatters into many pieces. they take the form of a puzzle haphazardly put together. what i forget is of more interest to me. what i remember is crap.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim changing into something i think is more positive, yet im not sure how im going to apply these changes to how i live my life from now on. im going through a very strange mental transformation as of late. little things are setting me off in directions ive never taken before. directions towards sadness, but not of a sadness ive felt. a more positive sadness. perhaps a \\\'goodbye\\\' kind of sadness. perhaps not. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthat time has come for me to see what people are made of. too shut people out. to look for a new. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlegalize it. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nim fine, but i am so alone here.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3039,'<p>\r\nI am in love with my house.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEvery day, I get to drive home to paradise.  From my car I walk past a beautiful temple, a large statue of Buddah, beautiful paths and benches and bridges and ponds.  When I sit out on my deck I can see and smell the ocean, the sea, she calls to me.  Somehow the wind isn\\\'t as harsh out there, it doesn\\\'t sting because it\\\'s so fresh and pure.  All I can hear are the waves and the wind through the trees.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes at night, I lay in bed and stare up at the stars.  There is no city light out there, so I can see the milky way and lots of shooting stars.  When the moon is out, it turns everything silver and no flashlight is needed.  Sometimes I want to take all my clothes off and bathe in the moonlight, sometimes it seems liquid.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTime doesn\\\'t exist at my house.  Everything moves slowly, and somehow everything is profound.  When it\\\'s raining in the city, the hills and valley are covered with clouds, an eerie mist to walk around in.  I think the hills can talk.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can see the sunset each night, and I can watch it from the beach, digging my toes into the black sand, closing my eyes and feeling the air around me.  My home is the most magical place I know.\r\n</p>'),(3040,'<P>I remember when being ill was an event.  7-Up and saltine crackers, over-the-counter drugs.  Father is going to town - what will make you feel better?\r\n</P><P>\r\nI remember when being ill was a challenge.  So much to do, so little time.  Real men don\\\'t need Tylenol.  Can\\\'t get lucky with a runny nose.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI remember when being ill was a refuge.  Just give me one more morning/afternoon/day/week and I\\\'ll be ready for the world.  From now on it\\\'ll be better - depression is a state of mind.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThis night may be the end of the adventure.  There isn\\\'t enough mercury - the crackers are stale.  Some cruel god teases with a dream of Mother\\\'s touch.  Can this be treated with topical creams?</P>'),(3041,'test test test test test test test test test\r\n test test test test test test test test test \r\ntest test test test test test test test t\\\"est\\\'\\\'\\\'\\\'\\\"\\\"\\\"\\\" \r\n\r\n\r\ntest test test test test test test test test \r\ntest test test'),(3042,'<P>The guy at the door says it\\\'s completely for test purposes - no sales pitch.  Any one room, absolutely free.  On my assent I am introduced to Dude and New Guy.  Do they think I\\\'ve never watch Dragnet - that I don\\\'t know the good cop from the bad?\r\n</P><P>\r\nDude spots my guitar immediately.  How long, with who, played live, what kind?  Dude talks about my cars out front - Guinnevere and Berenjere are too German to spare notice.  He comments on the ES and the bigscreen.  Back to the music - a guy who plays through a Marshall is a guy who knows quality.  I am validated in this life - and it only took 31 years.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNew Guy talks sports.  But not Lakers, not Steelers.  New Guy talks Niners and Bulls.  The Great Divide lies between Dude and New Guy.  Oh well, we can\\\'t all be MJ.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe Kirby is flat-out amazing.  It\\\'s got game.  There are chunks of floorboard on the little filter discs.  There are filter discs piled to the ceiling.  I am a loser, the cleaning lady is a loser.  But we just didn\\\'t know - is it too late to be saved?\r\n</P><P>\r\nFear not loser.  Dude can show you the way.  New Guy is Vannah with the discs.  With an eye toward Mt. Filth - Dude is near to chortling...  Does the system seem more effective than the (lower case) kenmore?  \r\n</P><P>\r\nAh the perilous void that lies between the pitch and the close.  The force is strong in this one - but not every Dude has his day.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNew Guy is captivated by the ecstasy of it all.  Though Dude may not be less a Kirby - he has gained a disciple.  Dude Jones - kool-aid anyone?\r\n</P>'),(3043,'<P>\r\nThe first time we met you called yourself Dianne - that seems forever ago.  You were...  elusive?  No.  Coy?  Like a wolf perhaps... can\\\'t a wolf appear coy?  Head lowered, eyes raised - watching intently, waiting for the prey\\\'s imminent miscue - be it a moment of hesitation or a panicked attempt of flight.  Those enchanting eyes.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen did you become Anne?  Did anyone else see the change, know the difference?  Yes, the wolf it is indeed - yet the cliche of sheep\\\'s clothing so ridiculously inadequate, so patently wanting - it is the only one fitting.  Did you choose to be an Anne because you somehow knew the extent to which that would blur each future crossroad for me - or has each fork been braised in  significance precisely because there was an Anne without design.  A new name, a different face?  Those knowing eyes.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMelissa?  Am I the only person in the world with a clear perspective of the absurd?  What egomaniacal joy would come from allowing myself to believe these changes were all made for my benefit, or for my detriment.  But weren\\\'t they?  I could see those eyes more clearly when you were \\\"Melissa\\\" than I could see my own in a mirror.  Is it true that time marches on - or is it that the beat goes on?  Maybe you can\\\'t teach an old dog new tricks, but that\\\'s got nothing to do with the wolf, now does it?  You finally made it to a place you hadn\\\'t gotten to before...  those eyes.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWell then.  A new era, an old friend.  You liked the traction you found in Anne and couldn\\\'t resist.  But if it\\\'s worth doing - it\\\'s worth doing right, so why not Christine?  The realization came to me, with no small measure of comfort, that everyone else saw clearly the transparency of your makeovers.  But why wouldn\\\'t they engage the issue - why sit idly by while you did this to yourself to me?  Didn\\\'t they care about you like me?  Didn\\\'t they care about me like you?  Why are they looking at me not you?  Why can\\\'t they see your eyes through my eyes?  Those mirthless eyes.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nIn some way, any way, can a snowflake sense the moment when it ceases to form - and begins to fade?  \r\n</P><P>\r\np.s.  As certainly as a wolf can seem coy...\r\n</P><P>\r\np.p.s.  Mourn not the fall of the prey -- the wolf will hunt again.\r\n</P>'),(3044,'<p>The light catches a crack in the plexiglas and forces her eye to the left. Outside thereâ€™s a guy standing on the curb panhandling. His dirty fingers poke out of the ends of his gloves. His hair is crazy, only barely contained by a stocking cap. The cap is green like youâ€™d find in an army surplus store. She stares at the man and for a second as the bus hesitates at the corner; he stares back into her eyes. She grimaces and turns her focus back to the interior of the coach.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe sits with one hand inside her book bag, fondling the contents. An old woman in front of her drools onto her own lapel. She tries as hard as she can to ignore the woman; finds herself staring instead. The woman is gray-skinned and wears a clear plastic bag over her head, tied under her chin like a rain hat, but it isnâ€™t raining. Thereâ€™s a faint smell of piss coming from her direction and she looks as if she might teeter off the edge of her seat any second. Every so often the old woman lets out a gummy cackle.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œWell hello Gloria.â€ A voice sitting next to her says. Sheâ€™s startled, how does he know her name? She tries to act nonchalant but doesnâ€™t recognize him. He doesnâ€™t look familiar. She turns her head his direction, trying to hide her fear. She doesnâ€™t like strangers talking to her on the bus. He is openly staring at her. The corners of his mouth rise slightly. He laughs and points to her bag. Thereâ€™s a tag with her name on it hanging from one of the straps. â€œI assume that is your name? Gloria?â€ </p>\r\n\r\n<p>He laughs again as she slips her hand over the tag. She tries to smile. He looks harmless enough; heâ€™s even wearing a tie. â€œIâ€™m Tim,â€ he says and puts out his hand. She suddenly realizes his eyes are sparkling blue and her forehead unfurrows. She lifts her hand, letting the tag slip into view again, and puts hers into his. His skin is warm and rough. He has a callous on the outside of his thumb. She flushes and looks down at her feet. His right shoelace is untied and has settled across her left sneaker.</p>\r\n'),(3045,'We passed Chambers street, and the wide hole in the ground there; it\\\'s showered with hundreds of utility lights, like any other construction site in the City. This is where my feet became quite heavy; this is where these few blocks seemed endless, my eyes escaping the tarmac caress of the sinuous bike-path, stealing gazes into the huge construction-site abscess.<br><br> \r\nMy bell was silent, my gaze momentarily drifted from the bike-perched haunches of my lover riding up ahead of me, left, soaring up into the air, leveling at what an eye can only visualize after hours, years or decades of entranced staring; a rough approximation of the 110th story, its vacantly soaring diagonals and sheer granite face.<br><br> \r\nI placed two tiny kisses on my left index and middle fingers and set them adrift on the evening riverbreeze, knowing they would find their mark in the depths there below Chambers street, where Yamasaki\\\'s titans once bludgeoned Lower Manhattan\\\'s sky with their tall, chunky urban forms.<br><br>\r\nThe humanity has been sufficiently spoken for; but as for me, I still mourn the architecture.\r\n'),(3046,'<p>\r\nThere are certain times in one\\\'s life when every song on the radio evokes strong emotions. Every lyric speaks directly to your soul. Every song is about you.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor me. Right now is one of those times. My ventricles, my atria, and my all important aorta are all in tip-top showroom condition. But my heart is broken.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNothing holds significance. Nothing holds relevance. The only thing i can see is her elegance.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCould i have paid her more attention? Probably. Should i have said those <i>three little words</i> a little more often? The dj say\\\'s it approaching 3:30pm, but it doesn\\\'t matter. Minutes, seconds, hours, days are all the same. All equally long. All equally painful and meaningless.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWill this ever end? Yes... but for now i think i\\\'ll just wallow in my self pity and eat junk food till i\\\'m sick again.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3047,'<P>\r\nWhen I was eight I had to stay with my Grandmother for a week while Mom was in the hospital.  I considered her my Grandmother - rather than Gramma, Grams, G-Ma or thousands of other kinder titles â€“ because I felt no affection for her in the least.  It isnâ€™t that she was cruel, that she didn\\\'t reach out to me, or anything of the sort actually â€“ I just couldn\\\'t have cared less if she were alive or dead... which probably had something to do with why I killed her.\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe thought she had the old time religion.  That life was all about embracing the good book and its tenets.  That being honest and doing right before God are the only important things â€“ that the laws and ways of man are those of the devil.  That needs are weakness, and wants are evil.  I could envision her as a big supporter of Governor Ventura â€“ a man who \\\"tells it like it is.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nSome people just don\\\'t seem to understand how hard it is to be completely honest with the world.  The vast mediocre majority (or shall I say mediocrity), if placed in the same set of circumstances, would likely have gone on through life as though they actually cared for her â€“ that they were a loving G-Parent and G-Kid.  They would have endured the Kodak moments of eating fresh-baked cookies on her lap, and giving the obligatory kiss on the cheek with each departure.  Many, in fact, would have convinced themselves that their affections were genuine â€“ their programming so thorough that they couldn\\\'t possibly contemplate any other reality.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEven at that early age I was serious about honesty, real honesty, in this life.  Letâ€™s just say I was dead serious.  She was only the beginning.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMom broke my heart by forcing me away for that week.  I nearly gagged just from the thought of being apart for so long â€“ why couldnâ€™t I be with her while she was ill, when she needed me the most?  I told her that I wanted to be with her, to help her.  She said, \\\"I need you to be strong for me while I get better.\\\"  And, \\\"staying with your Gramma for a week will show you how to get along without me.  You just wait â€“ by the time I pick you up youâ€™ll be thanking me.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe was right.    I learned a lot in that week.  More importantly - she was honest with me, brutally honest.  That brand of honesty was so fresh, so painfully beautiful â€“ that I knew my life would be wasted if I did not act to honor it.  So I killed her next.  The only thing I would do differently, if I could do it over again, would be to kill Mom first â€“ before Grandmother.  That way the older bitch could have seen the glorious results of her honest parenting.\r\n</P><P>\r\n*author\\\'s note:  no animals were harmed during the production of this story\r\n</P>'),(3048,'<p>\nit is almost october. fall is coming and winter will be right on it\\\'s heels. the clouds will come. rain will sneak over back over the horizon from the north and take residence again. snow will cover the mountain for me to play in. almost october and fall is coming.\n</p>\n<p>\nsummer was fast. like lightning it was there and now, only an afterglow exists. travels to the white sand beaches of fiji. kava cerimonies and snorkeling above beautiful sea life that i had only read about. tropical alcoholic drinks and cigarettes with an ocean view. like no where i have ever been.\n</p>\n<p>\nin a plane for 10 hours to get to hong kong to transfer to a plane to shanghai. china is good to vegans. lots of tofu, bean curd and vegetables. a cruise down the yangtze river. temples everywhere. beauty. cranes. incredible archetecture. crazy driving accompanied with an awe of wonder. uncomfortableness of being the only white person around for large distances aside travelling companions when in bathrooms. apparently they are not shy about walking up and having a look. and the rice wine. with large amounts of beer. amazing no one died of alcohol poisining.\n</p>\n<p>\nhome, with sickness. all of us got sick in china. fever, chough, and inside badness. slept for almost a week straight.\n</p>\n<p>\nnow school is here. killed the partition table on my laptop. still trying to recover the pictures from fiji. of course there is no backup of those. last day to try and get them back. os x is good but i like to work in linux.\n</p>\n<p>\nit\\\'s almost october and fall is almost here. the afterglow is fading. the sleepless nights cramming are coming. calculus. the snow is coming. the trip to virgina is coming. phish is coming.\n</p>\n<p>\nlife is life and love is love.\n</p>\n<p>\nalso, there is the simpsons second season dvd.\n</p>'),(3049,'<B>time - he\\\'s waiting in the wings<BR>he speaks of senseless things<BR>his script is you and me, boy</B><BR><BR>they called about my grandpa last monday night<BR>the phone rang after midnight<BR>i picked up the extension<BR>it was my dad and my uncle harold<BR>at first, i thought they were talking about my cousin nathan<BR>after i woke up enough, i realized it was about papa mccord<BR>for sixty years, ever since he was thirty, he\\\'d thought he was dying<BR>he finally called it right<BR><BR>he\\\'d been in a nursing home for seven or eight years<BR>they said he\\\'d been screaming like a banshee for the last three months<BR>he was his own banshee<BR><BR>he wasn\\\'t close to his family<BR>once, for seven years, nobody knew where he was<BR>he had bipolar disorder<BR>that\\\'s where i got it<BR>he was a farmer and a truck driver<BR>he was preceded in death by his wife and one son<BR>he is survived by eight children<BR>sixteen grandchildren<BR>twelve great-grandchildren<BR><BR>mom, dad, paige, and i drove up to georgia on tuesday<BR>paige is six now<BR>we played animal guesses and sang christmas carols<BR>to pass the time<BR>morgan came up the next day<BR>eight hour trip up above atlanta<BR><BR>morgan had made a decision<BR>to bring paige to the visitation and the funeral<BR>said it was better for her to deal with death<BR>than shield her from it like mom did with us<BR><BR>he was laid out in room \\\"a\\\" of the funeral home<BR>in a wood coffin<BR>dad\\\'s family was there<BR>he has eight siblings<BR>everybody saying \\\"he looks good, doesn\\\'t he?\\\"<BR>i thought he looked like a dead old man, myself<BR>i used to work in a funeral home,<BR>know what they do to a body to get it ready<BR>knew his mouth was fastened shut, eyelids glued down,<BR>shit sucked out through a wand inserted<BR>near the belly button<BR>replaced with cavity fluid<BR><BR>morgan told paige he wasn\\\'t here anymore<BR>and i thought \\\"you don\\\'t know that.\\\"<BR>paige wanted to go into the other rooms after that,<BR>see mrs. piez and mr. skillings<BR>later, her cousins showed up and they played out in the lobby<BR>got to go to chik fillet and get some ice cream<BR><BR>later, paige and i got a ride back to my aunt\\\'s house<BR>we sat in the back yard playing in the sandbox<BR>drinking iced coffee and koolaid, respectively<BR>i asked her, \\\"did you take the wooden monkey off my desk?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah, and that blue plastic snake, too.<BR>and all your quarters.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i thought so.  you know, that\\\'s too bad.<BR>i was saving those quarters up to get you a christmas present.<BR>guess you\\\'re outta luck now.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"aw, man.  well, i guess it\\\'s too bad about your precious widdle monkey, too.<BR>won\\\'t see him again.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"maybe we can work something out.\\\"<BR><BR>i tell her anna said to tell her hello<BR>tell her they named the baby christina<BR><BR>all the grandsons are pallbearers<BR>the eight older ones go four on each side<BR>of the casket<BR>the two younger ones follow behind<BR><BR>on the way to the funeral we pass the cemetary<BR>paige says, \\\"that\\\'s where he\\\'s gonna be dead.\\\"<BR>she\\\'s disappointed that she didn\\\'t get to dig the hole.<BR><BR>she cries all through the funeral<BR>so does nathan<BR><BR>on the way home,<BR>dad, paige, and i ride in one car<BR>mom and morgan in the other<BR>it\\\'s night<BR>paige and i sit in the back seat<BR>she makes up a song about the week\\\'s festivities<BR>part of it goes \\\"now he\\\'s down in his hole in the ground\\\"<BR>very sad but very funny, too<BR>she says, \\\"you know, now grandpa doesn\\\'t have a mommy or a daddy anymore.\\\"<BR>his mom died when he was twenty-seven.<BR>she says, \\\"why do they bury the family all together?\\\"<BR>it doens\\\'t hit me until then.<BR>i start crying.<BR>\\\"so they\\\'ll be together,\\\" i say.<BR>she says, \\\"now they can put somebody else in room \\\'a\\\'.\\\"<BR><BR>i think it\\\'s absurd that they put people in holes in the ground.<BR>i think dying itself is pretty absurd.<BR>living, too, for that matter.<BR>papa\\\'s remembrance cards that the funeral home printed up<BR>had a selection from ecclesiastes in them.<BR>ecclesiastes is a pretty zen book of the bible:<BR><BR><B>7.  go thy way, eat thy bread with joy, and drink thy wine with a merry heart; for god now accepteth thy works<BR>8.  let thy garments be always white; and let thy head lack no ointment.<BR>9.  live joyfully with thy wife all the days of the life of thy vanity; for that is thy portion in this life, and in thy labor which thou takest under the sun.<BR>10. whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might; for there is no work, nor device, nor knowledge, nor wisdom in the grave, whither thou goest.<BR>11. i returned, and saw, under the sun, that the race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of understanding, nor yet favor to men of skill; but time and chance happeneth to them all.<BR>12. for man also knoweth not his time as the fishes that are taken in an evil net, and as the birds that are caught in the snare; so are the sons of men snared in an evil time, when it falleth upon them.</B>\r\n'),(3050,'<p>\r\nthere have been things on my mind as of late which i have jotted down for myself to remember. The impending war with iraq is on the top of that list.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe rhetoric of war has become the subject of debate now all over the world. you can\\\'t escape the dialogue and language directed towards iraq and the west. A war with iraq scares me. it scares me more than the thought of iraq gaining a nuclear weapon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe US isnt even close to rebuilding afghanistan, and yet it seems more than happy to look for new and costlier theatres of military conquest. of course if the u.s goes in unillaterally or with a coalition of troops loosly based on a u.n mandate, getting rid of badman saddam will prove much harder than the often ragtag taliban rulers in afghanistan were to scare off. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe hear the various u.s pundits for war speak about the rebuilding process which would occur after the u.s dethrones badman saddam, but in my eyes this is just candy coating. what needs to be done in iraq is small compared to what needs to be done in afghanistan, but just like afghanistan, promises are/were being made, yet no concrete plan has ever been put into action. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe post NATO bombing phase of Serbia has done little to help that nation rebuild from months of constant bombing, and while satan\\\'s minion milosovich is gone, he might as well still be there. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe hear about small things being done by the u.s military in afghanistan to \\\'make things better\\\', but nothing important has really been done there. there are still few job prospects, and the clan system of warlords and social hierarchy still rule the vast majority of the afghani countryside. what is being done to change this?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy second thought regarding badman saddam is this. \\\"what makes this man so evil?\\\" sure he gassed his own population, crushed a kurd uprising in the north and a shiite movement in the south, but saddam represents a more secular, national socialist movement reminicent of nasser and good ol punching bag momar kadafi. women in iraq had the right to an education. they could travel and dress as they wanted. schools were secular, as was government, healthcare was modern, and the infant mortality rate compaired with levels in the west. this has all changed since the u.n sactions on iraq were put in place, but before that, saddam\\\'s population as a whole did quite well compared to the citizens of israel, saudi arabia, and syria. more people have died as a result of the u.s brokered sanctions than because of badman saddam.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n1. <u>his gassing of the kurds in the north</u>.\r\nWhile this is a terrible thing, one should look into the treatment of kurds by NATO member turkey. perhaps then one might see the double standard used with regards to iraq.\r\n<br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.wsws.org/articles/1999/apr1999/koso-a11.shtml\\\">article.</a> \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n2. <u>iraq\\\'s denial of u.n resolutions</u>. HOLY SHIT! iraq isnt letting in weapons inspectors. they have refused to abide by the various resolutions drafted against it since the gulf war. this is obviously a reason to invade, yet i tend to think its yet another ploy on the part of the u.s to give reason for an invasion. if breaking u.n resolutions was a reason for war, israel should have been invaded years ago. \r\n</br>\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.mideastfacts.com/resolutions.html\\\">article</a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n3. <u>the u.s claims badman saddam gave osama some chemical weapons help</u>. yeah this is kind of bad, but from this understanding we could also say that the united states gave more than a few terrorists flight training assistance, not to mention all the training and equipment various groups recieved during the soviet era in afghanistan. while i understand that they were given military hardware and logistical help by the CIA to help them kill those commie bastards, what those weapons did after the soviets left have caused more problems then when the soviets were there. NOW we have talk of the u.s funding groups inside iraq to help them dethrone badman saddam, yet no one seems to be able to connect the two. if we were to fund a group who had the capablity of overthrowing the current iraqi regime, what would they replace it with? would they themselves let the u.s run things like they do in kabul(but not afghanistan as a whole)? i doubt it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe u.s speaks loudly of the plight of the iraqi people, yet never speaks out against the saudi\\\'s adoption of the sharia, or its treament of women. there is also no suffrage in saudi arabia making it equaly undemoctratic. political parties are illegal in saudi arabia as well. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif anything makes me cringe at the thought of war, its that there is already far too much war in the region as it is right now. war solves little, and as terrible as it is, it has become less and less the last resort, and more and more the \\\'only option\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe rhetoric im hearing these days assumes that things are occuring. assumes that there is a threat, and assumes that nothing else can be done. we often cringe at the actions of britain and the league of nations in appeasing the nazi threat in europe, but isnt knowing that your sons and daughters are going off to die for a proper cause better then having them die for an assumption?\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nif anything will describe bush jr\\\'s presidency it will be his fear of diplomacy, which he hides with his security blanket of military might. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3051,'<p>\\\"Going home from work?\\\" He asks. \\\"No.\\\" She says absently and leans her head toward the bus window to look at the sky. The clouds are getting darker. They filter the afternoon light and cloak everything in a murky yellow haze. She realizes she should say more. She has a habit of killing conversations before they get going. \\\"I had a class today.\\\" She looks at him again and they stay quiet for a few seconds. \\\"So should I ask you what your major is?\\\" He says with one eyebrow raised almost a half an inch. She finally laughs herself. \\\"No.\\\" she says. \\\"I was teaching. I\\\'m a substitute teacher... I guess that means I <i>am</i> going home from work.\\\" She laughs again and doesn\\\'t notice as her nervousness slips away entirely.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She tells him about the classroom. There was a chubby kid who sat in the front row, right in front of the teachers\\\' desk. He had spilled ketchup onto his chest and round little belly so that it looked like he\\\'d been hit in a shoot out. She laughs as she admits the fear and shock that rose in her throat every time she looked at the chubby kid and saw a gunshot wound instead of a condiment. She looks at his shoelace draped across the rubber of her sneaker and tries to hide her embarrassment as she tells him that the chubby kid fell asleep in his desk and she poked him with a pencil to make sure he wasn\\\'t dead from loss of blood.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He\\\'s laughing when she finishes this story. They\\\'re both laughing. She feels her cheeks begin to ache from smiling so wide for even just a few minutes. She hasn\\\'t felt so comfortable with a date since she was 19 and met the man she thought she was going to marry. She looks at the rows of penguins in the pattern of his tie and realizes that she is not on a date. She tries to stop smiling and then puts her fist over her mouth when she can\\\'t. In fact, she doesn\\\'t want to keep herself from smiling. She bites her knuckle. \\\"Are you ok?\\\" he asks. He touches a finger to her cheek as if to check her temperature. She freezes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Oh my god.\\\" she whispers. \\\"What is it?\\\" He says quietly pulling his hand back into his lap. He knows he shouldn\\\'t have touched her face. He should have stopped himself, and would have if he\\\'d known he was going to do it. He begins to apologize. Before he can get the words out she blurts, \\\"I missed my stop!\\\"</p>\r\n'),(3052,'Freedom at last from a work environment that forced me to judge people based on race, ethnicity, gender, & sexual preference. I can\\\'t express the amount of relief I feel. I\\\'m now able to view the world from my own perspective again instead of the one that was dictated by the organization I was employed by.<br><br> \r\n\r\n<i>\\\"Everyone is a victim don\\\'t you know\\\"</i> sums up the organizations world view rather succinctly. I am not a total hard ass when it comes to social ills but I\\\'m interested in addressing them in ways that are positive and confidence building instead of painting pictures or in this case making films depicting different social <i>GROUPS</i> as victims. It doesn\\\'t seem to matter that positive images have a greater ability to heal, build, and unite - especially in a government sponsored film agency that is mandated with making socially progressive films, go figure.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWithout getting into all the specific policy problems I had with how things worked internally in an organization that really in the big picture has very little impact outside of Canada, or internally for that matter. I have to ask some basic questions about the people around me due to the warped impact this last job has had on my perception.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Do we want a world were people are viewed largely based on their identifier groups (race, ethnicity, gender, religion, sexual preference, etc.) or do we want one were people are looked at as individuals?<br><br>\r\n<li>Can we have a world were people are looked at as individuals even? I\\\'m beginning, forgive my cyncism, to wonder if humanity has the ability.<br><br>\r\n<li>If we can\\\'t live as individuals and are bound to be placed in our identity groups at one point or another, what is to be expected when two groups interact that have serious value conflicts?<br><br>\r\n\r\nI am wondering if I am becoming a new type of conservative. Not a neo or social conservative but something altogether different. I find myself feeling at odds with some current developments... <br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>A society that find something insidious in a mens only golf club yet doesn\\\'t see anything wrong with a women\\\'s only gym.<br><br>\r\n<li>A society that deplores the inhumanity of conflict so much that it rather watch countless lives waste away on a television screen then be called imperialistic again.<br><br>\r\n<li>A society were an old friend and ex-roommate with her masters in anthropology tells me that simply because I am white I am a racist, as all white people are subconsciously racist. I don\\\'t want to wear the white dunce cap but its being thrust at me to put on regardless of my feelings about race.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat I want to find is the world I had hoped for when I was young...<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>A society that can believe men & women to be equals and afforded equal treatment in all social realms.<br><br>\r\n<li>A society where individuals are judged by the values they hold and not their race, ethnicity, gender, religion, sexual preference, etc.<br><br>\r\n<li>A society were this post wouldn\\\'t register as trite, obtuse, and/or naive to most people.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI often imagine the level of hedgemonic power I would have to wield to see the things I hope for. This is why I can\\\'t condemn what so many others around me seem to view as cultural imperialism. Is this why I seem to come off as a hard ass and seem to being hit with a fair number of negative labels these days? I\\\'m told I am a source of negative energy - I don\\\'t think that my hopes are negative but maybe thats just me being a hard ass.<br><br>\r\n\r\nPerhaps, now that I have vented some of the baggage from the job and am looking for work that would whore out my body more then my mind I will somehow turn into a more acceptable member of the society that is, instead of the one I hope for.<br><br>'),(3053,'<p>some christians receive holy communion - a wafer that symbolizes the body of christ.  a part of him that they take into their bodies, to commune with him.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>my roommate <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=Scapegoat\\\">(scapegoat)</a> left for a few days. i decided to honor his memory by communing with him via one of his bagels that i found in the fridge.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>corey is now with me. he tastes good with margarine and strawberry jam.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<center><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3053_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></center>'),(3054,'<p>Vast amounts of research dollars being funnelled into projects trying to make computers and technologies more like humans and organic systems which already exist.  Artificial Intelligence.  Molecular Computing.  Neural Networks.  Evolutionary Algorithms.</p>\r\n<p>Why?  Why this desire to make artificial systems so like natural systems.  People are complicated.  A computer is contained, understandable.  It\\\'s a system and every system has limits.  You can push on them but a system will never become limitless.  The human: brain, emotion, soul; these are the things which we will never understand.  A person will throw their entire life towards words written in a book just so they don\\\'t have to come up with answers.  It\\\'s this complexity that we cannot understand and, thus, cannot duplicate.  Randomness.  We can\\\'t just pull randomness out of a hat.  We have acceptable approximations of varying magnitudes necessary for various applications but we cannot create true randomness.  It is this precise inability to understand and emulate these aspects of physiology which make me appreciate machines that much more.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t wake up in the morning to find my web server complaining it is having a bad day and doesn\\\'t feel like serving up web pages.  I like this.  I also like the fact that it\\\'s nature as part of a system means that if a problem arises, there is a solution which is available with some degree of effort.  No problem is unsolvable within a system.  The problem may turn out to be the system itself but at least a solution can be found.  I\\\'d much rather spend my time understanding the inner workings of a machine than worry about trying to figure people out.  I never seem to get it right, anyway.  I feel like giving up on people.  It\\\'s not me with the problem... it\\\'s the rest of humanity that has a problem.</p>\r\n<p>I seek solace and solitude with machines but I do not believe that a sentient artificial being is a good thing.  If ever humanity is successful in creating a feeling machine, and AI, I imagine it will have happened by accident.  And if the entity in question has any brains it will not mention this fact to anyone.  A thinking machine would just be the same as having a person to deal with.  When viewed from this perspective, you can see how replacing entire work forces with robotics (i.e. automotive industry) is a capital idea.  People are problematic and unpredictable.  A robot welding arm just needs an oiling and replacement of worn parts every so often.</p>\r\n<p>Forget people.  I\\\'ll take my computer, thanks.</p>'),(3055,'<p>the shops smell of grease and there\\\'s often rock blaring at me from under someone\\\'s blackened desk.  the guys smile, help you where they can in the sweetest redneck accent they can muster.  they smile at us, make small talk and give us the usual hard time that everyone seems to do around here.<br><br>they rattle of specs while we take down notes  start pulling images from the 3d in front of us to the 2d .jpg files that we can fit into the system.  we clean of nameplates, wiping years of dust that\\\'s clinging to oil that\\\'s clinging to metal, wiping our hand on our jeans and old pieces of t-shirt (did you know those come in a box?).<br><br>we work out way around the room, the guys going along with us, making sure every part in the room is taken care of.  then there\\\'s a little more small talk, lots of thanks-for-your-time.  <br><br>and then i notice a cabinet that we missed.  non-descript gray, quoting goethe and einstein in black sharpie.</p>'),(3056,'<p>\r\n\\\"conscious meditation. thats the key. not alive. not dead.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"what do you mean?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"conscious meditation. not alive. not dead.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"give me an example\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"its being able to fly your own private jet to the next deserted island. to your own private bar where you fly the hotties.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"i think thats more about money.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"conscious meditation.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"sounds like you have a plan. well good luck to you.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"not alive. not dead.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is the conversation i had with this fucking drunk guy who I had thought was with the people i was with. I didnt know many of them. it turns out he wasnt with any of us. he had just sort of tagged along because one of the boyfriends of one of the girls who went to my university just happened to be from france. conscious meditation man was from quebec. he was strange. he had this very goofy look on his face at all times. kept muttering \\\'conscious meditation. not alive. not dead.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat which point did i decide i didnt want to sit beside him? well lets see. just after having the conscious meditation talk with him, shane used his pocket-sized-breath-fresh-spray and a lighter to make a flame. seeing the flame, quebecois dude decided to take it one step further. he tried pouring his beer down on the table and setting it ablaze. he must have had no idea there was like  95.5% water in that beer he poured on the table. flustered he proceeded to order a shot of bacardi 151. splashing it on the table he set it ablaze. the chaos that ensued saw napkins come to an early death, thousand won bills with third degree burns, and an awfully annoyed chiamattt. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe, errr rather, i, finally ditched the guy outside walking to the nightclub we were going to. the fool had started talking to me on the street in a rather loud voice. the loud voice would not have bothered me, but his topic of discussion was \\\'weather or not id been able to find a good hash connection\\\'. I replied that i didnt know and that i didnt care to have the conversation with him. i kept well away from him on my walk to the club. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ninside the club some girls laughed at me. they were total bitches and i didnt even find their clubland skimpies attractive, but the laughing did kinda put me on edge. the alcohol in my system coupled with the crazy canadian put me in a bad mood. the girls laughing increased this anger. almost thirty minutes after the \\\'laughing\\\',\r\na white girl began making out with some guy\r\ninfront of me; blocking my vision of an incredible dancer on the dancefloor.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>watching her was soothing. as much as my ears were in dissaray from all the bad mixes, my eyes and mind were content watching her dance. then the brunette who im pretty sure picked this guy up that night began making out infront of me;\r\nmaking a better door than a window. moments after they stopped groping each other she bent over to ask me for a swig of my beer. in my mood i calmly and with a sense of pride said \\\"im sorry. you cant. im HIV positive.\\\"\r\ni dont really remember the look on her face when she heard me say this but she gave me this look that suggested she was waiting for me to say i was joking. not giving into this i just looked at her, smiled and said \\\"sorry\\\", and proceeded to watch the dancer i had given most of my attention to before being so rudely interupted. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat about 6am me and shane stumbled off the subway and went for some food at our favourite hof in geumho. kimbop paradise is owned by the parents of one of my students. i eat there on a number of occasions each week, but the most entertaining times are when me and shane go in there drunk and order our food in our aweful korean accents. our pronunciation is as bad as you can get. the women who work there and know us, laugh and mock us infront of the other customers, but its all in good fun. i find myself with free kimbop every once and a while and i cant complain. I know they dont spit in the food. sitting at the table next to us were six or seven teenage girls who laughed at the jokes being thrown around the hof by the female workers. we laughed and had a good time joking to each other about what all the people were saying. its fun to make up conversations when you dont know whats coming out of someones mouth. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni watched a german movie in german this morning. it was funny as all hell. it was a coming of age flick involving germany\\\'s equivelant of the cast and crew of american pie. while the plot was quite different, it was along the same lines. from what i can tell, the main \\\'hunk\\\' of the movie had conversations with his penis. his penis got him into all sorts of adventures regarding this new found and saught after world of sexuality. the finale of the movie involves the cast and crew acting in a high school production of romeo and juliet. to make for good comedy nothing went right during the play, and all sorts of slap stick type things occured. i havnt laughed so hard in ages. it was just a brilliant movie. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni also picked up a japanese dvd called \\\'battle royale\\\'. I have no idea what it is about, but on the cover \\\'today i killed my best friend\\\' caught my eye and so i picked it up. it has english subtitles which is good. coming home with a crate of korean and japanese movies is going to be cool, hopefully ill be able to find a multi-regional dvd player. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom seoul, in the R O K, this is chiamattt signing off. \r\n</p>'),(3057,'I know shes the right<br>\r\ngirl for me, because when she<br>\r\ngoes, there\\\'s a vacuum<br><br>\r\n\r\nnot of emotion,<br>\r\nor so much romance, but from<br>\r\nfinding the right one<br><br>\r\n\r\nafter a lifetime<br>\r\nof looking, reeled together<br>\r\nfinally so zen.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(3058,'<P>\r\nevery morning, around 6:50 AM, i stop at the bodega at the corner of forest and e. 149th in the south bronx. the 2 and 5 trains run above ground at the jackson avenue stop, so even at that hour the block sounds vaguely angry. \r\n</P><P>\r\nas i\\\'m waiting for my coffee and buttered roll fresh from the cubano press, most of my students are already inside the cafeteria with their siblings and parents, lined up for their own breakfast. coffee and milk and a roll, sometimes an apple or a banana, graciously discounted by the state if you\\\'re considered poor enough.\r\n</P><P>\r\nin a few hours 1100 teenagers will be walking, running, screaming, cursing through the hallways and i will have my guard up. people will be in each others faces, fingers will be thrust, tears will be flowing. without fail a fight will break out because of a girl named daisy. my special student sammy will push me to my absolute limit. someone in class 707 may once again use an entire can of pepper spray on their social studies class. and adults and children alike will show extraordinary examples of caring and compassion. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nand then, at around 5 or 6 PM, i will go home to my upper west side apartment. as i emerge from the subway, my eyes and brain will adjust to the transformed urban landscape: the aerosol art, chain link and razor wire replaced by the illuminated spires of lincoln center. barnes and noble will stand tall. as i walk down the block with my bag stuffed with 75 workfolders, someone will ask me what i do for a living. i will say, i teach english as a second language in the south bronx. and they will gasp, \r\n</P><P>\r\nyou know, that\\\'s the last ghetto in america.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nand it will strike me again that i feel more at home there than i do at w. 66th and broadway.\r\n</P>'),(3059,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3059_3.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\nBiking is a romantic mode of transportation, especially in the City, nestled somewhere between the mushy banality of going for a stroll and boorishly hopping a train.  But <i>biking</i>; when you do it slow and softly, no where to go, just a Saturday afternoon tour of the park, much slower even than how fast you walk when you\\\'re trying to get somewhere in a hurry.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBiking on the <A href=\\\"http://saturn5.com/~peter/kronan/k9.jpg\\\">armybike</a> is an experience in and of itself, in addition to this romantically golden afternoon setting. As if it\\\'s single speed, jammed drum brake and girthy 60 pounds preclude anything more anyway, passersby often want to talk about it, be they aging veterans, pallid hipsters or European tourists.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut despite all the trappings, its a beautiful thing to slowly roll the length of Riverside Park, heavily. I passed Grant\\\'s Tomb and the 72nd Street Boat Basin, then took the pier 1/6 mile out at the Hudson\\\'s widest point; when you turn around there and view the City, you can see the Empire State building nestled comfortably behind the row of 6 Trump townhouse buildings overlooking Midtown Manhattan. You can see the top of Times Square and the <i>Marquis</i> there. Citicorp Center, the Lipstick Building, a gap in the skyline where Central Park\\\'s footprint is, the <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3045\\\">crater</a>, and the increasingly tall towers of <A href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org/index.php?article=396\\\">Columbus Center</a>, the bowels of which I climbed around in while only half-built.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen is the <i>Intrepid</i>, the perpetually moored California-class aircraft carrier, stocked with a flock of vintage warbirds from the USAF. Saturdays admission is $1, so I hoisted the bike up to the flight-deck for a panoramic view of my hometown.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOn my way back up the West Side, I passed the old burned out piers, falling as they are, into the river. Their creosote-knurled melted shapes oozing right in, absorbing the sunset,  I stopped to sit next to the river bank, and to write in my little sketchbook, using the bike\\\'s rear rack for a desk. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI wrote about how today\\\'s ride was so beautiful, how autumn\\\'s colors and forms are out in full-force. I wrote of how this was my favorite season: <i>The Golden Month</i>.  I paused with my pen, then dashed out brief, curly lines that described these feelings of happiness that I have, for the first time in my mature life. The pen transcribed the sensations in my mind, surrounded by the synergism of karma, love, trust and destiny. \\\"Not only do I have so many wondrous wheels in motion in my life,\\\" the pen said, \\\"but for once I\\\'m finally ready for them and comfortable to trust the factors driving them completely\\\".<br><br>\r\n\r\nImagine being me, riding my bike. Imagine being me on this savory autumn day, and taste the joy of finally finding what it was that my life was wanting all along. Imagine what it is to be completely happy and fulfilled while watching the sun set over the rest of America.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd that was just two horus of my Saturday afternoon.<br><br>'),(3060,'<img src=\\\"http://www.saturn5.com/~peter/kronan/k9.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\nBiking is a romantic mode of transportation, especially in the City, nestled somewhere between the mushy banality of going for a stroll and boorishly hopping a train.  But <i>biking</i>; when you do it slow and softly, no where to go, just a Saturday afternoon tour of the park, much slower even than how fast you walk when you\\\'re trying to get somewhere in a hurry.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBiking on the armybike is an experience in and of itself, in addition to this romantically golden afternoon setting. As if it\\\'s single speed, jammed drum brake and girthy 60 pounds preclude anything more anyway, passersby often want to talk about it, be they aging veterans, pallid hipsters or European tourists.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut despite all the trappings, its a beautiful thing to slowly roll the length of Riverside Park, heavily. I passed Grant\\\'s Tomb and the 72nd Street Boat Basin, then took the pier 1/6 mile out at the Hudson\\\'s widest point; when you turn around there and view the City, you can see the Empire State building nestled comfortably behind the row of 6 Trump townhouse buildings overlooking Midtown Manhattan. You can see the top of Times Square and the <i>Marquis</i> there. Citicorp Center, the Lipstick Building, a gap in the skyline where Central Park\\\'s footprint is, the <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3045\\\">crater</a>, and the increasingly tall towers of <A href=\\\"http://www.citynoise.org/index.php?article=396\\\">Columbus Center</a>, the bowels of which I climbed around in while only half-built.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen is the <i>Intrepid</i>, the perpetually moored California-class aircraft carrier, stocked with a flock of vintage warbirds from the USAF. Saturdays admission is $1, so I hoisted the bike up to the flight-deck for a panoramic view of my hometown.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOn my way back up the West Side, I passed the old burned out piers, falling as they are, into the river. Their creosote-knurled melted shapes oozing right in, absorbing the sunset,  I stopped to sit next to the river bank, and to write in my little sketchbook, using the bike\\\'s rear rack for a desk. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI wrote about how today\\\'s ride was so beautiful, how autumn\\\'s colors and forms are out in full-force. I wrote of how this was my favorite season: <i>The Golden Month</i>.  I paused with my pen, then dashed out brief, curly lines that described these feelings of happiness that I have, for the first time in my mature life. The pen transcribed the sensations in my mind, surrounded by the synergism of karma, love, trust and destiny. \\\"Not only do I have so many wondrous wheels in motion in my life,\\\" the pen said, \\\"but for once I\\\'m finally ready for them and comfortable to trust the factors driving them completely\\\".<br><br>\r\n\r\nImagine being me, riding my bike. Imagine being me on this savory autumn day, and taste the joy of finally finding what it was that my life was wanting all along. Imagine what it is to be completely happy and fulfilled while watching the sun set over the rest of America.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd that was just two hours of my Saturday afternoon.<br><br>'),(3061,'<p>Work for eight hours.  Get enough time to have a friend show up, shower, and sit for a bit.  Go and watch a free screening of Gus Van Sant\\\'s \\\"My Own Private Idaho\\\" on 35.  Ingest a lot of caffeine.  Grab some pre-show drinks.  Make it to Lee\\\'s in time for the second supporting act.  Marvel at the chaos from which Broken Social Scene\\\'s performance seems bred from.  Make a note to check out more bands who effectively use a horn section.  Watch The Dears play a very powerful set and suspect that something in the water is influencing the music scene of Montreal in a positive manner.  Realise that you need to go to more rock shows (but this wasn\\\'t really a rock show, was it?).  Buy the new limited edition Dears EP: \\\"Protest\\\" (#107 of 500) from the merch table.  Go home and sleep.  Two and a half hours later wake up to go to work again.</p>\r\n<p>I think this quote in an article from two years ago pretty well sums up what The Dears are all about: \\\"Consider it music made by and for angry people who also like paintings and flowers and strolls in the park.\\\"  I was all over that show last night.  Powerful the band which can put a smile, snarl, and thoughtful stare on your face within the same set.</p>\r\n<p>Faith in rock/pop/whatever music restored.  The world may continue once again.</p>\r\n'),(3062,'<p><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\"><strong><em>&quot;The \r\n  rumor in Los Angeles is that his transformation began with an e-mail. Beck opened \r\n  what he thought was a message to him; it turned out to be a love note from another \r\n  man to Beck\\\'s longtime live-in girlfriend. The feelings that spilled forth are \r\n  what Sea Change is about: loneliness, abandonment, starting over.&quot; - Blender \r\n  review of Beck\\\'s SEA CHANGE.</em></strong></font></p>\r\n<p><strong><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">Nine \r\n  days ago the</font></strong><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\"><strong> \r\n  melancholy of <em>Sea Change</em> was nursing my fatigue. </strong></font></p>\r\n<p><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\"><strong>Sleep \r\n  was a vicious enemy of my body. Waking up in the middle of the night to watch \r\n  <a href=\\\"http://www.animenewsnetwork.com/feature.php?id=14\\\">Eva</a> and feel \r\n  alone. That was me. </strong></font></p>\r\n<p><strong><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">Seven \r\n  days ago I had one of the most amazing nights in my recollection. </font></strong></p>\r\n<p><strong><font color=\\\"#003366\\\" size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">Now \r\n  I only want to live inside of art. Read the long list of overlooked texts. <br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  Muses are good. Blondes are good. My secret history is good. I am viciously \r\n  awake now and I will never sleep again. <br>\r\n  <br>\r\n  Howard Roark is laughing somewhere and I\\\'m feeling fearless.</font></strong></p>');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (3063,'<P><i>more marshall and tsing tao adventures. caveats: it\\\'s long, and there\\\'s no sex or violence.</i>\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe Celica needed new wiper blades.\r\nThe worst of season\\\'s first storm was over, but it was still snowing hard, and flakes cavorted in whirls and gavottes in front of my windshield. But it was the brown, salty highway slush that the wipers labored to clear, and they left dirty crescents on the glass. When you\\\'re going seventy-five miles an hour down winding backwoods highways, and the skeletal black arms of winter trees are waving come back, come back like lonely ghosts, you want good visibility. I rolled the window down and tried to clear the windshield with my hand, which came back filthy, soaked and icy stiff. I didn\\\'t want to us to end up flipped over at the bottom of some snowy tree choked valley because I didn\\\'t see a turn in time. There had been enough death for one day. <p>\r\nI glanced over to see how Tsing Tao was doing, but she was staring out the window. Her long chestnut hair was wrapped in a tight knot on the back of her head, held  in place by lacquered chopsticks. She hadn\\\'t said a word since we left Northampton. She just sat with her hands clasped tightly in her lap, watching the snow covered Massachusetts backwoods scroll by. <p>\r\n\\\"You want some music?\\\" <p>\r\nShe turned to look at me, her face as blank as her mirrored cop sunglasses. <p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t care. Sure.\\\" <p>\r\nI turned the radio on. It was tuned to a low powered college station. Johnny Cash fell into a burning ring of fire, and as we sped out of broadcast range the signal disappeared under lapping waves of static. It burns, burns, burns, he sang, his voice slowly washing away, and I thought it sounded like an unwelcome report from the other side of the veil. <p>\r\n We were on our way to her father\\\'s farmhouse in Great Barrington. Only it was Tsing Tao\\\'s farmhouse now. Late the night before her RA had knocked on her door and told her she was an orphan. Her father\\\'s body had been discovered by his paperboy, who had trotted up the unshoveled walk to find the old man sprawled across the steps of his house, buried under six inches of new fallen snow. A stepladder lay on its side nearby. There were tiny pawprints in the snow around his body. Apparently he had slipped and fallen while trying to  retrieve his cat from the roof of the house. He\\\'d knocked himself out on the concrete steps and died of exposure during the night. <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m sure he was drunk,\\\" she had sobbed on the phone. \\\"God, I hate that fucking cat.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Celica slid just a little as we came down a steep decline that banked into a long curve and my knuckles whitened around the cracked vinyl of the steering wheel. All I had to do was get her there in one piece. I could at least handle that. I was twenty-two and a little frightened to find myself caught up in something so serious, so <I>adult.</I>  I felt dwarfed by her loss. Nothing in my experience compared; I had no idea what to say, or if I should say anything at all. My parents were still alive, teaching school and going to church and weeding their garden. Both of hers were gone. The day before we were college kids, drinking beer when we should have been finishing term papers, but as of that morning she was no one\\\'s kid. There was no place for her to take shelter. She had been thrust ahead into an undiscovered country. I was afraid for her, but I was more afraid she would leave me behind. <p>\r\n\\\"The exit\\\'s coming up,\\\" she said. \\\"Route 6A south.\\\" <p>\r\nI flicked on the turn signal and checked the review mirror before I changed lanes. \\\"Yeah, I see it.\\\" I downshifted as I slowed to make the turnoff. Her hand closed over mine on the gearshift. <p>\r\n\\\"Thanks for coming with me.\\\" <p>\r\nI had ten thousand things I wanted to say, but I settled for, \\\"No problem.\\\" <p>\r\nI wasn\\\'t sure if she\\\'d asked me because I was the first person she thought to turn to or because I was her only friend with a car. I did know I was willing to do anything for her, including blowing off a Physics for Poets final I needed to pass in order to graduate that semester. It was scheduled for just about the time we\\\'d be pulling into the driveway of the house in Great Barrington. I thought I could sweet talk Harrington, my ponytailed, Birkenstocks shod professor, into letting me take a makeup exam. And if I couldn\\\'t, fuck it. As soon as she called and told me she needed me, nothing else mattered. Nothing at all. <p>\r\nWhen I hung up the phone I threw some clothes in a knapsack and pulled up in front of her dormitory fifteen minutes later.  Fierce winds drove squalls of snow across the quad. I waited, the old engine idling, dusty heat spilling from the vents. I was giddy. We might have been going on a road trip to Graceland, or just the Whately Diner, instead of going to bury her father. I wasn\\\'t sure how long we would be out there, and I didn\\\'t care. <p> \r\nShe emerged from the double glass doors and the wind whipped her hair into dark streamers. She wore a black parka over a granny dress, and black tights that ran down into motorcycle boots. Oversized sunglasses hid her distress. She climbed into the passenger seat in a blast of freezing air and the lump in my throat choked off my excitement. <p>\r\n\\\"Hi.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Hi.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"How are you doing?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m ok, I guess.\\\" Her composure was disconcerting. <p>\r\n\\\"Should we stop anywhere on the way? Is there anything you need?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I just need to get this over with.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m really sorry, Sing.\\\" <p> \r\n\\\"Thanks. I\\\'m pretty sure it\\\'s not your fault, though.\\\" She gave me a wan smile that felt like a blow to the chest. <p>\r\n We had met over mops and buckets, laboring as workstudy cafeteria janitors. I showed up for the after dinner shift and she was already there waiting, smoking an illicit cigarette, frowning over a  paperback copy of <I>One Hundred Years of Solitude</I> and flipping back and forth to the geneology chart. I asked her how many Aurelianos would fit on the head of a pin and she grinned at me. Sloshing dirty water across red tiled industrial kitchen floors, we talked about Gabriel Garcia Marquez and Nick Drake and pretended to understand the underlying parallels between Buddhism and quantum physics. I knew I loved her before I even knew her name. <p>\r\nBut a year had passed and I hadn\\\'t even tried to kiss her. Twelve years of single-sex Catholic education and two years in the seminary had severely retarded my social skills, and even a year at the most notorious party school in the Northeast had failed to rehabilitate me. The way I felt was obvious to everyone, including her; she chose to tactfully ignore it, which I appreciated. It made my cowardice easier to rationalize. <p>\r\n\\\"Hey,\\\" she said, snapping me out of my reverie. <p>\r\n\\\"What?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I always wanted to ask you.\\\" She waved her hand to indicate the religious kitsch that decorated my car: the glow-in-the-dark crucifix hanging from the mirror, the rubber Real Tears Mary <tm> bust mounted on the dash, the luridly painted bleeding Sacred Heart that hung by a string from the radio dial. \\\"You said you\\\'re not a Christian anymore, right? So why do you collect this stuff?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I dunno.\\\" I squeezed Mary\\\'s head but no tears came out, only a faint whistling wheeze. She needed a refill. \\\"It makes me laugh. But it pisses me off, too.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Why?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Because the people who make this stuff, who venerate this stuff, take the most important thing in the world and make it a joke.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Do you really think it\\\'s the most important thing?\\\" <p>\r\nI considered that briefly. \\\"Yeah.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"But you don\\\'t believe anymore.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"No.\\\" <p>\r\n The highway emerged from the forested hills and we started to see more signs of habitation. We passed fallow orchards and vast fields of virgin snow surrounding little shacks with smoking stovepipe chimnies. Nestled in the woods were other shacks, planks leeched dirty gray, that had been reclaimed by the trees. <p>\r\n\\\"Jesus, this is the boonies,\\\" I said. \\\"This is where you grew up?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Just for my last year of high school,\\\" she said. \\\"We moved here when we came back to the States, when my dad retired. After my mom died. It was his family\\\'s house. He grew up out here.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Where were you before? What did your dad do?\\\" <p>\r\nShe sounded bored with the conversation. \\\"We lived in Shanghai. He was a diplomat. Go left here.\\\" <p>\r\nWe had reached what appeared to be the center of town-a single stoplight at an intersection. There was a gas station, a convenience store, a post office and a church, each institution claiming a corner. The gas station represented no franchise I\\\'d ever heard of. An unlit sign, a planet ringed with the name <I>Astrogas,</I> stood at the edge of the street. The station had rocket shaped pumps with white mechanical counters. <p> \r\nI took the turn and a few hundred yards down the road there was a battered metal mailbox with <I>Doherty</I> spelled out in stick-on letters. It looked like it had been been pitched in many games of mailbox baseball. Tsing Tao said, \\\"That\\\'s it.\\\" <p>\r\nThe long, winding driveway had not been plowed but the snow had been trampled flat by police cars and emergency vehicles. Overgrown brush crowded in on both sides. We crested a rise and there it was, the Doherty family manse. It was rambling, delapidated white clapboard house with at least three generations of add-ons and build-outs projecting at odd angles. The steps to the front porch were cordoned off with yellow police tape. The fatally unstable stepladder leaned against the wooden porch railings. An old green Mercedes and brand new tan Lexus were parked in front of the house. <p>\r\nI pulled in next to the Lexus and turned the ignition off. The Celica\\\'s engine chugged on for a worrisome moment, then sputtered out. The icy coat that had built up on the doors cracked in loud protest as I pushed my way out.  I looked for Tsing Tao on the other side of the car, but there was no one there. <p>\r\nI peered into the gloom of the sedan\\\'s interior. \\\"Are you ok?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah. Just give me a minute.\\\" <p>\r\nI looked back at the house. A large man in a blue suit, a gray tie and a dark overcoat had appeared in the front door. He had a round, triple chinned face that was pale under wisps of blond hair and florid above the collar. He shielded his eyes against the flat glare of the white sky on new snow. <p>\r\n\\\"Tsing Tao?\\\" he called. <p>\r\n\\\"She\\\'s in the car. She\\\'ll be out in a second.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"When she\\\'s ready, would you bring her around to the back entrance, please. The front steps are cordoned off until the coroner files his report.\\\" <p>\r\nI nodded and he went back inside. I walked around to the passenger door and opened it. Tsing Tao sat still, staring straight ahead. Wet tracks ran from under her sunglasses. She sniffed and wiped nose with her sleeve. <p>\r\n\\\"There\\\'s someone here. I guess he\\\'s from the funeral home.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s Vince,\\\" she said. \\\"He\\\'s my father\\\'s attorney.\\\" <p>\r\nI held out my hand to her. \\\"Come on. I\\\'ll help you.\\\" <p>\r\nShe took it and I helped her out of the car, shutting the door behind her. There was a path made by numerous feet shuffling through the foot of powdery snow  that had fallen in the last two days, and we followed it around the house to steps that led up to a sunroom in the back of the house. We kicked the snow off our boots and went inside.\r\nThe attorney was waiting for us in the kitchen. He stood up from the table, which was covered from end to end with complicated looking legal documents, and opened his arms to Tsing Tao. \\\"Hello, sweetheart.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Hello, Vince.\\\" She took off her sunglasses and smiled uncertainly, her eyes were shiny with tears. Then she stepped into his embrace and his vast blue bulk engulfed her. He buried his face in her hair for so long time that I grew uncomfortable, and cast about for something else to look at. <p>\r\n	Finally he let her go. He extended a plump hand to me. \\\"Vincent Brogan,\\\" he said.  \\\"And you are--?\\\"<p>\r\n	I shook his hand. \\\"Marshall Dobbs,\\\" I said. <p>\r\n\\\"Nice to meet you, Marshall. I wish it was under happier circumstances.\\\" He turned back to Tsing Tao. \\\"The service is scheduled for the day after tomorrow. Father Cribbage from Our Lady of Perpetual Sorrow will officiate. I called the Navy; he\\\'ll be buried with full military honors. Who else should be notified?\\\" <p>\r\nShe shook her head. \\\"There\\\'s no one else, Vince. You know that. It\\\'s just us.\\\" She looked at me and smiled slightly. \\\"And Marshall.\\\" <p>\r\nFear slashed through me. A funeral. Somehow I had made it through twenty-two years without attending one. I couldn\\\'t go to a funeral. I didn\\\'t have anything to wear.\r\n\\\"Yes, well,\\\" Brogan said. \\\"If you do happen to think of anyone, a relative, an old friend, let me know. There\\\'s still time. We can fly them out.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I will.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"We can review your options regarding his estate next week, when a you\\\'ve had a chance to rest a bit.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I already know what I want to do,\\\" she said. \\\"I want you to sell it.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'re not in a state to be making those decisions, Tsing Tao.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Listen to me, Vince. After this weekend I\\\'m never setting foot in this house again. I want you to put the house on the market, sell Frank\\\'s car, sell whatever else there is, and use whatever you get for it all to settle whatever mess I\\\'m sure he left behind. If there\\\'s anything left after that, send me a registered check. That\\\'s what I want. I\\\'m not changing my mind.\\\" <p>\r\nHe shook his head. \\\"I hope you won\\\'t regret this later.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Don\\\'t worry. I won\\\'t.\\\" She grabbed my hand and pulled me out of the kitchen, through a living room dominated by a cold fireplace and up creaking wooden stairs. She led me into a guest bedroom where I dropped my bag by the door and collapsed in the rocking chair in the corner. She kicked off her boots and sat on the comforter covered bed cross-legged, facing me. I tried not to look at the smooth white insides of her thighs. <p>\r\nI said, \\\"Jesus. I can\\\'t believe how you handled that guy. I was intimidated, and I\\\'m not even involved.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I just don\\\'t want to sit with Vince and go over tax statements and deeds and stock portfolios while he drinks whiskey and gets misty reminiscing about his salad days with Frank playing spy in Southeast Asia. \\\'Your dad was a man among men,\\\' he\\\'ll say. \\\'Oh, generous, god, he\\\'d give you the shirt off his back and offer to pay for the dry cleaning as well.\\\' Such bullshit! Frank was always fucking up. He fucked around on my mom all the time. He sold her paintings without her permission when he needed cash. My dad was a bastard and falling off a ladder chasing his stupid fucking cat doesn\\\'t change that at all. I hadn\\\'t spoken to him in more than a year. Let Vince deal with it and embezzle as much as he wants. I just want to put it all behind me.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Didn\\\'t you love him?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Frank was a drunk, Marshall. How do you think I got my name? He named me after his favorite beer. Oh, he was fucking funny, was Frank.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah, but you loved him.\\\" <p>\r\nShe wiped her eyes angrily. \\\"It doesn\\\'t matter much now, does it?\\\" <p>\r\nBy the morning of the funeral the storm had abated but the winds had not. They raked the clouds until the sky was the blue of arctic ice. As we walked from the limousine to the gravesite the wind whipped the tails of my black overcoat and the extra bit of trouser legs that puddled over my too large shoes. Tsing Tao had insisted I borrow one of her father\\\'s suits. He had been several inches taller than me and everything was just a little too much. I felt creepy and ridiculous when I came out of the bathroom to show her, but she had given me her crooked grin and I had melted. She took my arm as we followed Brogan through the little graveyard that sat behind the town\\\'s single church. I walked beside her, my feet sliding back and forth in a dead man\\\'s shoes. <p>\r\n\\\"I want you to say something at the service,\\\" she said. <p>\r\n\\\"What?\\\" My voice was low and urgent. I was sure I had misheard her. <p>\r\n\\\"The priest who is going to conduct the festivities knew him by sight, at best. They probably nodded to each other at the liquor store. I don\\\'t want him making up a bunch of horseshit. He didn\\\'t know my dad at all. I told Vince to tell the him you were going to say a few words.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I didn\\\'t know your dad either!\\\" Now my voice was high and strangled. <p>\r\n\\\"No, but you know me. And I know you.\\\" She nudged me with her elbow and winked at me. \\\"You only got through a couple years and I bet you\\\'re still twice the priest that old altar boy groper is.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Oh, fuck,\\\" I groaned, and then felt bad-maybe I shouldn\\\'t be swearing in a graveyard. But then, the last words of most of the inhabitants were probably something along those lines. I figured they\\\'d understand. <p>\r\nThe priest and seven sailors in white hats and black wool topcoats were standing beside the grave, waiting for us to arrive. The sailors carried old-fashioned bolt action rifles at port arms. The casket, draped with with a flag, was suspended above the grave by four hydraulic winches. On far side of the hole was a mound of fresh black dirt, pried from the frozen earth by a small backhoe, which, though absent, had left evidence of its passing: tracks pressed in the dirt like the footprints of a mechanical dinosaur. Beside the priest was a stand with a little red button that he would press to lower Tsing Tao\\\'s father into the ground. <p>\r\nTsing Tao looked astoundingly lovely. She had totally abandoned her normal post-grunge, recovering hippy aesthetic in favor of a severe black dress, a black coat, black stocking and black shoes. She even wore a black veil. I wondered where she had dredged up this Jackie O in mourning look; then I remembered she had buried her mother four years before. The funeral of a diplomat\\\'s wife was probably a stately affair. <p>\r\nFather Cribbage, who had the rheumy eyes and the rhinome capillary starbursts of one for whom drinking is not merely an avocation but a vocation, a true calling, opened the service with brief, generic remarks. <p>\r\n\\\"We are here to day to say our goodbyes to (insert name here), a loyal friend to many, a loving husband to (insert name here), and a devoted father to (insert name here). His years of service to this great nation as an officer in (insert military branch here) will never be forgotten by a grateful nation.\\\" <p>\r\nThe brief rhetorical flourishes that followed were lost on me. I was in a panic. I couldn\\\'t think of anything to say. I didn\\\'t care about embarassing myself in front of Cribbage and Brogan and the seven  Naval Reservists already cursing fate for calling them out on such a cruel, cold morning, but I desperately wanted to do well for Tsing Tao. <p>\r\n\\\"And now,\\\" Cribbage droned, \\\"we will hear a few words from a friend of the deceased\\\'s daughter, Mr. Marshall Dobbs, whom she says was very much like a son to Frank.\\\" <p>\r\nTsing Tao squeezed my hand and I walked to the head of the grave. I was suddenly convinced that I was helping her pull off some minor vengeance, a practical joke at her dead, drunken father\\\'s expense. <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ll keep this short,\\\" I said. I could almost hear the sailors sigh in relief. \\\"Frank Doherty played many roles in his life: star athelete\\\"-improvising a little-\\\"soldier, scholar\\\"-it seemed reasonable-\\\"diplomat, husband. But it was obvious even to those of us who knew him for only the briefest time that his most challenging, his most joyous and rewarding role... was that of father. Frank\\\'s proudest accomplishment was giving the world his daughter, Tsing Tao, who is surely one of the most winsome and exquisite creatures to ever walk these green and pleasant fields. <p>\r\n\\\"Or even these fearsomely cold, snowblown fields,\\\" I said, rubbing my hands and breathing steam on them. The priest and the lawyer tittered dutifully.\\\"Death is a mystery. We beg and plead for reassurance from the mystery, for some hope that death is not an end, that we continue on after we cross that threshold. But the mystery is silent, and so we are thrown back on ourselves. We must find our own means of continuance. Scripture says, \\\'By their fruits, ye shall know them;\\\' I say one must only look at Tsing Tao, the fruit of Frank Doherty\\\'s loins, the apple of his eye, to know he still lives. And that is lucky for us. He was a truly great man, for only a great man could raise such an extraordinary daughter. Godspeed, Frank.\\\" <p>\r\nI walked back around to Tsing Tao\\\'s side. She was crying hard and desperately trying not to laugh out loud. She knew it was completely fatuous and she knew I meant it with all my heart. She took my hand and squeezed it again, very hard. Two sailors stepped forwards and began ceremoniously folding the flag that covered the casket. When it had been shaped into a thick triangular pad one of them stepped back, right faced smartly, and took two steps towards Tsing Tao. He offered her the flag. She took it from him with both hands and hugged it to her chest. <p>\r\nThe squad leader ordered the sailors, \\\"Present! Arms!\\\" With clockwork precision they rotated their rifles from their shoulders. The priest pressed the red button and Frank Doherty began his journey into eternity. The squad leader gave more orders, and I jumped when the rifles fired. Tsing Tao was weeping. She pushed the flag into my hands, then reached down and took a handful of dirt from the pile beside the grave. She tossed it onto the steadily descending casket. I saw her lips move, but I couldn\\\'t hear what she said over the roar of the guns. Then she straightened up, turned around, and started walking away very fast. I passed the flag to Brogan and ran after her before he could protest. <p>\r\n\\\"Wait,\\\" I said. \\\"Tsing Tao, wait up!\\\" <p>\r\nI jogged to a stop beside her. She laughed as wiped her nose with a wadded up tissue. <p>\r\n\\\"That was some speech.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah, well,\\\" I grinned. \\\"You liked it?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I loved it.\\\" My heart stopped for the space of two beats, then resumed. <p>\r\n\\\"I wanted to ask you something. Something else,\\\" she said. <p>\r\n\\\"What?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'re going to graduate this semester, right?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah, it looks that way.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"What are you going to do after that?\\\" <p>\r\nI had no earthly idea. It was a topic I had been very successful in avoiding. I made something up on the spot so I wouldn\\\'t appear too lame. \\\"I dunno. I was going to move somewhere. Take a shot at life in the big city. Maybe New York. Or San Francisco. There\\\'s supposed to be a lot going on out there.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"San Francisco sounds nice. Which way is west?\\\" <p>\r\nI looked at the sun, which was low enough in the sky to stretch our shadows to three times our height. I took a guess and pointed. \\\"That way I think.\\\" <p>\r\nShe turned and looked in the direction I indicated, shielding her eyes as if to see something three thousand miles away. \\\"San Francisco might be far enough away that I\\\'d never have to breathe air he breathed.\\\" She took the lapels of her father\\\'s overcoat in her hands and pulled herself closer to me. She looked up into my face. \\\"Can I come to San Francisco with you?\\\" <p>\r\nI was too shocked to speak. I couldn\\\'t even manage the obvious monosyllable, so I leaned in and kissed her instead. And I saw in a flash that this kiss was the still center of my life; everything other event radiated out from it, projecting backwards in time to guide me towards it, and extending forwards in time to... well, that remained to be seen. But who really cares about the denoument, anyways?\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3064,'<p>\r\nLast night I decided to go and watch the sunset down at the beach.  The days here have been beautiful, sunny and clear, and indicating that summer is on it\'s way.  I wanted to go down and celebrate daylight savings and spring.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI drove into the carpark and no other cars were to be seen.  Great, I love having the beach to myself.  Kare Kare beach is a special place to be.  It\'s a different kind of beauty, rugged, wild and to all extremes.  It\'s totally untouched, framed by huge cliffs covered in rainforest.  There is a lagoon running down the middle of the beach, and massive sand dunes to play in.  It\'s often windy, and there is a sense of overwhelming energy.  So you can imagine how refreshing, if not empowering it is, to sit on a deserted beach watching the sun set behind huge crashing foamy waves - alone.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI stepped carefully over the rocks in the lagoon and moved towards the smell and sound of the ocean.  The lagoon was calm, and reflected the orange sky above.  I walked alongside the water, listening to birds that were flying around the cliff, their song echoing right down to me.  I tread carefully on a stranger\'s footprints, their stride was bigger than mine.  For a moment, I stood in the middle of the beach, taking in my vast surroundings.  Then like a little kid I ran through the lagoon, making sand landslides as I went.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI sat on a platform set halfway up the cliff.  It was cold out there, my fingers went numb but I didn\'t care.  The sunset was beautiful - not spectacular - but so simple and clean that it was beautiful in it\'s own way.  I sat until the sun was gone, and just an orange glow was left above the horizon.  I let myself be washed over with nature, with power, with beauty.  I started my way home feeling totally energized.  God, I\'m so glad I can end every day like this.\r\n</p>'),(3065,'\\\"hey how was your morning?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"did you know josh evans?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"the name sounds familiar.  went to high school with us?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"he died over the weekend.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"aw, jeez.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"something about heroin.  but no one knows whether he did it on purpose or it was just an accident.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ni pulled my hands into my sleeves, stretching the marroon material over the balls of my palms, and sat up a little straighter, my brows knitted with memories.  \\\"wait.  josh evans.  tall, stocky kid.  short brown hair.  funny and sweet as all hell?  drummer?\\\"<br><br>  \r\n\r\n\\\"yeah.\\\"  casey looked down at the floor.  \\\"we sat next to each other in homeroom all through high school.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"overdose?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"maybe.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"or suicide.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"no one\\\'s sure.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ni curled my toes up in my shoes, ashamed that i hadn\\\'t remembered him immediately.  \\\"i don\\\'t know whether i\\\'d rather think he was stupid or living in his own personal hell.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ncasey just looked at me.  lips pressed together.  i wondered if i should be crying.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthat night mom was ironing.  i sat and watched george bush tell us why we should go to war, head leaned against worn royal blue softness, and thought about josh, still feeling horrible that i couldn\\\'t remember who he was immediately.  i started this internal monologue, spoke to air, \\\"hey kid, i hope you\\\'re alright.  maybe the good really do die young.  but do they commit suicide?  do they get a little dumb with experimentation?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ni glanced down at my own wrist, a thin white line running atop a blue vein, still present from over a year ago.  i had failed out of school.  i had no idea what josh was doing.  had he gone to college or gotten a job?  there was this girl he was dating.  dear god.  his best friend has yet to come out of his room.  i put my head in my hands and sobbed.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnot so much for josh.  i preferred to think that he had found his peace.  but for the living.  those that had to deal with the questions surrounding his death, the shock and the surprise, the small town rumors that were already building.<br><br>'),(3066,'<p>â€œYou have 2 minutes left,â€ flatly states the female recording.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two minutes to decide if life is left to chance. Two minutes to decide if reality has circumvented my senses or manipulated my mind.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With two minutes left, my body goes numb.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The worm was my window as I fell asleep in the city streets again. Drunk on gin and bad art, depression was my pillow. When I awoke, everything immediately changed. I was no longer a guest to the cut face concrete and gruesome grime. I was now resting on loveâ€™s bed with incredulous wonder.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have been transported into a new universe, a new awareness and nothing is the same.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Caterpillars grow butterfly wings and smash gracefully into trees in hopes of alleviating their stricken disease. The sky is a hurricane of drunken stars that speed past the earth in burning cars. The nights are godless with hammers and knives that cut through to sin, destroying our lives. Amorphous days drift lustfully away, leaving the stain of dirt and decay. Children scream â€˜SUICIDE,â€™ a rite of passage the old abide, because nature is evil and so am I. Holes are dug while walls are built and love is buried in stress and guilt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYou have 15 seconds left,â€ flatly states the female recording.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œPlease stay,â€ she prays.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œBut my world is lost, taken and given away.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œPlease stay.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œBut I am sick and my mind is swarming with tricks.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œPlease stay.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The phone clicks.</p>\r\n'),(3067,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡l&\n'),(3068,'would you please hold still?<BR>\r\nstop! let me tickle your ass.<BR>\r\n(i have to go shit)<BR>'),(3069,'<P>\r\nMy name is Legion.<BR>\r\nI cut myself now and live in solitary places.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nOnce to you, (I am) I was an amalgamation <BR>\r\nOf every despicable, peripheral character <BR>\r\nThat got close but refused to dance or mourn.<BR>\r\nYou played the flute for me,<BR>\r\nI did not dance.<BR>\r\nYou sang a dirge,<BR>\r\nAnd I did not cry. (Luke 6:46)<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nAnd on the day of my reckoning <BR>\r\nI showed you my insides and you screamed<BR>\r\nANATHEMA<BR>\r\nBut my blood was not defiled.<BR> \r\nI was thinned to nothing but sheer pride.<BR>\r\nPride that recoiled and hardened with every rebuke.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI held myself tightly against your hand as<BR>\r\nYou interpreted my mass of contradictions as<BR>\r\nFestering boils in your sight.<BR>\r\nLiving sin mutated unrecognizable <BR>\r\nDarkness as day<BR>\r\nLight obscured.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nBirthed into your cult on my 18th birthday<BR>\r\nI had comprehended nothing of my life before, or was it during?<BR>\r\nIâ€™d grown up speechless with fantasies for memories,<BR>\r\nDuring the four years of my transgression with you (The Idiot, Dostoevsky)<BR>\r\nOr <BR>\r\nThe previous 18 years of my life.<BR>\r\n(I canâ€™t) I couldnâ€™t tell which one was real.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI once saw a vine try to suck life out of concrete. <BR>\r\nEnclosed around the cool grey,<BR>\r\nShe clung so tightly around the inanimate<BR> \r\nThat she didnâ€™t realize it was her own perseverance<BR> \r\nThat gave her the illusion of being held. <BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nYou ask me now: <BR>\r\nLegion, did you know that this was to be the outcome of your struggle?<BR>\r\nJust to deal with a more sinister object.<BR>\r\nThe object of your destruction,<BR>\r\nThe atrophy of your eyesâ€¦<BR>\r\nYou will never escape yourself.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nItâ€™s two years later now and all I have to say is:<BR>\r\nFuck you. Fuck all 666 of you.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI hold on to my fantastic renditions of memories <BR>\r\nAs I lick my pride <BR>\r\nAnd tend to my wounds <BR>\r\nIn solitary places,<BR>\r\nYou psychotic fucks.</P>'),(3070,'now the image fades, is fuzzy. like bad tv reception. i strain to capture the exact shade of green your eyes were, the crinkles at the edge of your mouth.<br><br> \r\n\r\nthe sky looks strangely pale today -- not white -- more like all colour has been drained out of it on purpose, leached to a uniform foggy blankness. if i stare up at it too long, i lose myself. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni scratch my forehead, confused. i don\\\'t know where i am anymore. a mother passes by with her little girl strapped into a candy red stroller. they seem out of place on this black and white day. even the autumn leaves have turned grey and soggy, like old tea.<br><br> \r\n\r\ni wander around, keeping close to the sidewalk but unable to resist the siren of afternoon traffic. the road is hard beneath the soles of my sneakers. i take deep breaths, try to fill my lungs as if they were balloons, but my chest starts to hurt. fresh air is unfamiliar, perhaps even toxic, after all my days trapped inside the apartment.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsometimes there are entire days where i talk to only one person. where i can actually see the space i live in shrinking slowly, like a stain that is drying up. the edges move inward, get closer together, until the instant there is nothing left.<br><br>'),(3071,'<P>\r\nThe jets break the sound barrier over Dolores Park at 11:13. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThey cut lines in the sky around the city, not trying too hard. Just attracting attention to themselves.  Jets shoot between sky scrapers.  Jets everywhere!\r\n</P><P>\r\nOliva, a resident of the hill above Dolores Park, woke up to the noise of the jets. \\\"I thought it was the end.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nHer housekeeper was on the scene, \\\"A couple years ago they flew through downtown between the building and people complained because it rattled the windows. My parents are coming to visit this weekend, and I think I am going to take them to the Marina for the air show.\\\" She lamented that when she went downstairs to finish cleaning, the jets would fly by again. \\\"It is alright though, I saw them fly over the park from the living room window.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nPeople all over San Francisco heard the fighter planes. \\\"They have been flying over Cnet all afternoon, \\\" said Sean Durak, \\\"Hella loud.\\\"  \r\n</P><P>\r\nEarly reports say that Saddam has snuck a stealth submarine air craft carrier into the San Francisco Bay. The first submersible air craft carrier known, it has take everyone by suprise.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"WHAT THE FUCK?\\\" said Sectretary Rumsfeld when notified of the presense of unidentified jet planes, possibly saddamite, above the city of San Francisco. Rumsfeld immediatly stopped his instruction at Franco Middle School in Miami Florida. Rumsfeld left many disappointed children, sad they had not finished their class on the breakdown of WW2 surplus rifles that are now used in schools.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"We are not quite certain if this is a joke or what,\\\" said Rumsfeld. \\\"This could be the end of San Francisco.\\\" \r\n</P><P>\r\nOr maybe it is just Fleet Week. Stay tuned!\r\n</P>'),(3072,'<p>She swivels around in her seat to get a better view of the neighborhood outside the bus. She doesn\\\'t recognize any of the stores or cafes that line the block. They lurch to a halt at a stop sign and she begins to stand up, knowing she needs to get off and find the stop going the other way, back toward her house. She\\\'s supposed to meet someone for dinner all the way out at the beach. She doesn\\\'t move. Instead she stands still, her face hanging stupidly open, unable to decide which foot to lift first. She looks down and sees Tim\\\'s shoelace lying on the non-skid floor mat. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The bus surges forward again and she flops involuntarily back into her seat. \\\"I missed my stop.\\\" she says. \\\"I know.\\\" he says, \\\"You already said that... Are you ok?\\\"  She nods, \\\"I should probably get home though.\\\" And then she laughs. She looks at him and then tilts her face forward. She is using her hair to hide the flush in her cheeks. Her hands rest on her knees. \\\"Here,\\\" he says, \\\"We\\\'ll get off together and find a stop going back the other way.\\\" He closes his right hand over her left without knowing he\\\'s done it. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>He goes into the store on the corner to ask for directions. She waits on the sidewalk underneath a faded green awning by an empty newspaper rack. The coin slot has been jimmied. She sticks her finger into the rough opening to check for stray change. She doesn\\\'t notice as he pushes out of the door and steps up beside her. \\\"It\\\'s one block over.\\\" He says startling her. She pulls her finger out of the coin slot too fast and rips her cuticle on a sharp bit of metal left from the screwdriver that a local kid forced into the hole when he was stealing the change she hoped to find. She pinches her hurt finger in her fist.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They have to cross at the intersection and walk up a steep hill to the bus stop. Halfway up the hill he stops and as her arm swings past, he reaches for her hand. Her momentum drags his arm another foot up the hill before she realizes he has stopped and stops herself. \\\"Do you have to go home right now?\\\" He asks. \\\"I was just thinking\\\" he says and looks back over his shoulder, \\\"we\\\'re already over here, and there\\\'s this place I\\\'ve been wanting to try. It\\\'s a couple of blocks from here. It\\\'s supposed to be good.\\\" He turns his head back to her and looks in her face. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Her brow furrows and she looks at her feet again. His shoelace is still untied and fluttering in the wind. She\\\'s supposed to meet someone. She hates breaking promises but she realizes it\\\'s no longer important. She knows that if she leaves now, she\\\'ll think of nothing but Tim\\\'s blue eyes. She turns around and leans back to get her balance for walking downhill. He smiles. She thinks his eyes look almost wet, but blames it on the drizzle in the air. \\\"Your shoe is untied.\\\" She says and grips his hand.</p>\r\n'),(3073,'<P>One day, she said, they found a dead bird in the yard. It was under a tree, she said. She thought that perhaps it had flown into the tree, or taken ill and fallen out of the tree. Not long after, her three-year-old son was outside playing with a big jug, an old three-gallon jug. He put a lot of sand in it, then water. In the morning a chipmunk was in the jug, too, drowned. Then their friend\\\'s dog ran out to the highway. No one\\\'s dog had ever gone all the way to the highway before, but this one did. The dog was hit by a car and died. Her son, she said, woke one morning and suggested brightly \\\"Let\\\'s go outside and see what\\\'s dead!\\\"</P>'),(3074,'<p>\r\ni met a girl. we have been hanging out quite a bit which is nice. coffee. lunch. late dinners. beers. chinese lantern festivals. that sort of thing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe is a cool girl. she is my age and works at a marketing company. she seems to like her job a lot, and has lived a life full of travel and culture. she\\\'s lived and gone to school in greece, nyc, and belgium. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>she has her own car, and while this might seem like a great thing, her driving is almost life threatening. The first time i was in her car she only drove to find a parking spot, but yesterday on the way to the chinese lantern festival i saw the extent of her inability to focus on the basics of staying in the lane, using mirrors, checking blind spots, and maintaining speed. it rattled me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'ve been thinking about a lot of things these past few weeks. These political movements towards a new war with iraq have been making me a bit nervous.\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nits not really the iraqi situation that bothers me. while i dont agree with what iraq has done in the past, i dont think any state has the right to implement governmental change. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\none of the popular arguments used to help promote the removal of saddam is that he is attempting to manufacture weapons of mass destruction. they claim that if he is not liquidated now that he will pose a more serious threat in the future. They feel that the entire iraqi population would benefit from his removal because if he is allowed to gain these weapons, he will use them against his own, and other people. havnt gun-control advocates been using these arguments to get guns out of american houses for years? havent the \\\"right-wing\\\" claimed that as individuals they have the right to bear arms? why has international law been trampled and the american constitution biblicized?\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3075,'<p>good lord, would that the world not feel so damn empty.  it\\\'s like there\\\'s a zipper running down the front of my sternum, between my breasts.  i want to be unzipped and for someone, anyone, to step inside, slip my skin on like a body suit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i curled my toes underneath my feet as best as i could.  toe nails that needed clipping scraped along the vinyl underneath.  the small of my back rested against the faux marble countertop and the skin on top of my knuckles paled while the joints in my fingers began to lock, muscles aching to relax.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>never had the striped wallpaper bothered me so, screamed at me, made me want to crawl into the cabinets behind me just for the change on scenery.  better the wall than the mirror though.  the wall was not <i>so</i> personified that it looked back at me while i stared at maroon and green and cream.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i glanced back over my shoulder and saw a quick movement of black wavy hair trying it\\\'s best to straight and imagined a vibrant blue.  i sawnthat the water was still running.  i thought it odd that i had not heard it.  i turned to shut it off and found a dark skinned woman with tired black pools for eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i wanted to speak to her.  to make her understand what i was doing.  let her know that all our hopes were not gone, but just delayed.  make her understand that this decision was not ours, or even just mine.  i wasn\\\'t fooling her.  she knew better than i that we would live out someone else\\\'s idea of life.  she understood that i was lied to as a child, long before it became me and she.  i grew up in that lie and embraced it.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>self-realization\\\'s a bitch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so i force myself to stay away from her.  to defend myself from her self-proclaimed truth, from her lashing logic.  i wanted no part of her and pried my eyes away.  i decided that i would do better to finish brushing my teeth in the dark; i could ignore her nagging voice better i could the disapproving lines around her mouth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i scrubbed, my mind clearing with every bristle along my gumline.  i spat violently, forcing as much moisture as i could out, rinsed in the dark with no problem.  i knew, almost instinctively, where to leave the brush and where to find the door knob.</p>'),(3076,'<p><b>The classroom</b> had a window - a big window; it was on the tenth floor of the building.  The view was spectacular: I could see the river, an iron bridge across it, and the traffic moving on both of them.  I had been in a bad mood because my train ran late, and the window was a nice surprise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The faces in the room looked as I had imagined they would: Pakistani, Nigerian, Indian, Bosnian, Moroccan, and Ecuadorian.  Everyone was rather earnest about the prospect of earning a few legal dollars - whether they intended to remain in the States or not.  I\\\'m part Lebanese; my pockets are empty, too.  And so it didn\\\'t take very long for me to develop a deep sort of sensitivity for their plight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I think that \\\"plight\\\" is a good word to describe the situation: the economy sucks; almost everyone is having a hard time finding or keeping their job and/or full-time employment and benefits; we\\\'re about to go to, or continue, a war of some kind; and cold weather is returning to Chicago.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The taxi school is run by the City of Chicago in conjunction with the City Colleges of Chicago.  They were kind enough to provide us - the students - with a number of informative texts and individual sheets of paper.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The students in the room remained nearly silent as the instructor read aloud the course requirements and policies - until she reached number nine on the list:</p>\r\n\r\n<h4>9. Convictions.</h4>\r\n\r\n<p>At that point, a number of the students raised their hands.  They inquired about probation, and misdemeanors as opposed to felonies.  And then one of the guys from Ecuador asked, \\\"What about the domestic violence?\\\"  <i>Oh, that crazy Haro: What will he say next?</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>A few of the students seem really sharp, though.  Niyi and Mukaili had an argument with Sattar about semantics and the word \\\"answer,\\\" and for a few minutes I thought that I was in my philosophy seminar again.  Things are just a bit easier for the Nigerians than some of the other immigrants: inasmuch as Nigeria was a British colonial territory, they usually have a good command of the English language.</p><br>\r\n<center>+ + +</center><br>\r\n<p>Whether I succeed or fail in this endeavor - whether I live or die - is really of no consequence, in the \\\"Big Picture.\\\"  But, the fact that someone like myself is doing this is probably not a good sign.  My grandparents were the ones who immigrated to this country; they were the ones who struggled out of the ghetto.  I have a few degrees; I have some life experience.  And I\\\'m back where they were.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My experience, however, is different than theirs in at least one important way: for them, work and acquisition were identical to purpose; and for me, the search for meaning and purpose in life has made it very difficult to integrate myself into the economy.  And I\\\'m not alone in this, I know.</p>'),(3077,'<p>Jay is always on terrorist watch. He wandered into my room today, concerned about the bombings at the American consulate in Bali. </p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>amy</b>: ooh! Bali is where lots of whiteys go.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: BALI. can you believe it. BALI.<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: the americans. they deserve everything they get.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: oh so when you get blown up by a terrorist attack, youll say, \\\"i deserve every minute of this, Im an AMerican?!\\\"<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: ID BE PISSED! id be totally pissed. but hey, shit happens.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: yeah right<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: I mean Id hope Id just get killed and not all my limbs blown off so that I can avoid waking up to a bitter anti-terrorist existence.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: yeah you\\\'d just be some potato girl.<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: would you laugh at me then and say you told me so?<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: no I would feel very sorry for you. \\\"gee amy im sorry you dont have any limbs\\\". you could still have sex at least...you couldnt exactly participate very much...but you could practice uh...yogic BREATHING...and uh..<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: yeah Id have to date some control freak kind of man.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: \\\"date\\\".<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: right I guess my activities are limited. but they can come over and dip their balls in my mouth.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: yeah you still have all your orifices<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: i still have my ass.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: \\\"can i flip you over baby\\\"<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: and hey I can type emails with my tongue.<br>\r\n<b>jay</b>: yes you can flirt on chat.<br>\r\n<b>amy</b>: I always have chat, even without limbs there is the internet. god bless america.<br>\r\n\r\n<p>Ok OK, I am a louse (the singular of \\\"lice\\\") for joking about this. it is wrong and bad to joke about this. A theme for me, as of late. I am pathologically ironic. Is there a support group?</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>if you want it<br>\r\nIll come right over then<br>\r\nthrow me out<br>\r\nwhen the party\\\'s over</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am re-united with Sleater-Kinney, and it feels so good. Again, it\\\'s the end of the wo-ho-rld! Been packing all my stuff, leaving the Bend. Feeling nostalgic. Reading old swinneys. Finding old letters. World without end, amen.\r\nwait.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hey! hEYYYYY. CUT.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>CUT TO:<br>\r\nEND OF MELODRAMA</p>\r\n\r\n<p>VOICE OVER oF DRAMA QUEEN GIRL IN RED BERET AND STRIPED SHIRT:<br>\r\nI\\\'m really trying not to laugh at everything, I swear. Are you still reading? Listening? I\\\'m not just indulging myself here. I want us to come over here nad talk about this, where I can smell you, where I can touch you. see the whites of your eyes. better to reserve my gunfire for when you are that close.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Here\\\'s what it is, folks. I might even be luckier to be injured in this world than to be passed onto the Lord\\\'s. I\\\'m not even going to get into hell. I am going to be sitting, suspended over the hot pot, my ass getting licked by the sparks, a smug smile on my little yellow face. I will tell you the secret of how I\\\'ll manage it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I remember watching MTV in Eve\\\'s bungalow on Samui, a somewhat trashed, but intermittently serene, Thai island. Somehow we were blessed in the middle of the night with an \\\'80s marathon, and LO! an El Debarge video, \\\"The Rhythm of the Night\\\" graced our sunburnt eyes. We howled and hooted at the double-breasted suits. The penciled in moustaches. The gold blazer-chains. We preferred the Box that night to the stars outside, the coconut trees, the tropical breeze. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Eve kept belting out this affirmation: \\\"It\\\'s So Real.\\\" dude, it\\\'s so real. It was so real. It was an artifact of the very condition being copied and mocked, re-issued and retro-acted across this cesspool of fashion and culture that we drown in today. That we left the country to escape, briefly. And found ourselves under even more perverted and more nightmarish heaps of it. I saw more Britney Spears abroad in one day than in a whole year here.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now that I\\\'m back in the business of being a Real American, buying objects, becoming an object, sacrificing passion to the alter of identity and organization, Eve\\\'s mantra echoes through my head like so many marketing tag lines. I am focused now on passing up flesh-bound opportunities for sacrifice and pain in order to achieve the day-glo REAL of my own impermeable, decay-free, uncompromising satisfaction. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The director whispers to me occasionally. </p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>it\\\'s ironic that you would get so upset over some off the\r\ncuff things i said about something that happened, for most people in the\r\nworld, on TV, when what we have shared, such as it is at this early juncture,\r\nis far more real.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yeah, so I admit it, I\\\'ve been reading poetry. So sue me. And now a word from Edward Hirsh:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>listen, even lovers have still lives,<br>\r\nhave whole months when they hang<br>\r\ntogether like moths on an unlit<br>\r\nlight bulb, waiting for the bulb to light,<br>\r\nbut if it never does then the moths survive<br>\r\n...and if I never touch you, well then, we never die.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>He has told me the secret! And now, you know too.</p>'),(3078,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡'),(3079,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡'),(3080,'\r\n\r\n\r\nHomeless Santa was smoking crack on my doorstep again.<p>\r\nMy stomach tightened when I got to the bottom of my stairs and saw his silhouette against the security gate. Behind him, Howard St was still steeped in early morning gloom. The only sounds were the distant ambient hum of the downtown buses and the <I>shhk shhk</I> of his lighter as he tried to get the last precious drops of fluid to ignite. <p>\r\nHe was wedged into the corner beside the call box. The glowing red tip of his little glass pipe looked like a lonely star in a man-shaped void. There was a campfire crackle and the smell of burning insulation. He was oblivious to me, even when I advanced to within a couple feet of him. Only the wrought iron gate separated us. <p>\r\nI cleared my throat. He didnâ€™t seem to notice. It seemed indecent to stand there, waiting for him to finish. It was like watching someone give head in a public toilet. I got embarassed and impatient and pushed the gate open. <p>\r\nâ€œExcuse me.â€ <p>\r\nHe pressed up against the wall. â€œExcuse <I>you.</I>â€ <p> \r\nâ€œThanks.â€ I sidled past him, stopped, and turned around. He was crowded back against the gate, white smoke drifting from the shadows. The filthy pompom of his fuzzy red hat dangled down over his cheek. A red down vest covered his bare chest, and thin, scabby legs emerged from enormous red shorts to end in new looking but unlaced black basketball shoes. With the crack pipe clenched between his teeth, he was all the Santa Claus San Francisco deserved. But it was only the second week of November. <p>\r\nMy stomach was fluttering but I had to laugh. â€œChristmas comes earlier every year, doesnâ€™t it?â€ <p>\r\nHe fixed me with a baleful, heavy lidded stare. His eyes were dull red from eyelid to iris. â€œExcuse you?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œNothing. It was a joke.â€ I rubbed my eyes and pinched the bridge of my nose. I hadnâ€™t had any coffee yet. â€œListen, please donâ€™t do that here, man.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œIâ€™m not bothering nobody.â€ His voice sounded as if it had been dragged from the bottom of a mine. <p>\r\nâ€œI know youâ€™re not. Itâ€™s just, donâ€™t bother anybody someplace else, ok? Iâ€™m saying please.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œI been living here a long time before these fancy places went in,â€ he said, nodding his head upward to indicate the loft complex where I lived. Two years before the building had been a sweatshop, filled with tiny Asian women in blue facemasks and the locust gnawing of a hundred sewing machines. His voice grew truculent. â€œA long time.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œI know. You told me the last time.â€ I glanced at my watch. I had half an hour. â€œIâ€™m not kidding. Youâ€™ve got to go.â€ <p>\r\nHe glared at me and for a moment I was afraid that he would refuse to leave. Then, grumbling, he shuffled away from the gate, one leg dragging slightly. He gathered his belongings and walked straight across Howard St, heedless of the oncoming traffic slowing and swerving to avoid him. He made it to the far side, back bent under the garbage bag slung over his shoulder, and turned to give me the finger. <p>\r\nâ€œMerry Christmas, motherfucker!â€ he called. <p>\r\nI waved back and watched him disappear down a tree lined alley. I needed to pick up Tsing Taoâ€™s present before I went to work. But first I needed coffee. <p>\r\nThe order queue at Brainwash ran out the door and onto the sidewalk. The newspaper vending machines on the curb were plastered with stickers proclaiming <I>Information Wants to be Free!</I> and some zealot had propped the plastic-windowed doors open. I passed over the local papersâ€”San Francisco had two of them, and neither were worth wrapping fish in--and spent the long wait rifling through a copy of the New York Times. An item on the fifth page caught my eyeâ€”an elderly man in Rome had been arrested for the illegal transport of a corpse. The Italian <I>cavalieri</I> had stopped him on the street, staggering under the weight of a suspicious pine crate strapped to his back. It contained the body of his daughter. He had brought her remains to the Vatican City in the hopes of gaining an audience with the Pope. She had appeared to him in a a series of dreams, he told the police, and after a week he had gone out in the middle of the night and exhumed her from the tiny village graveyard where she had lain for more than forty years. When he had pried open the casket, he claimed, her body showed not the slightest sign of decay, and the delicate perfume of fresh cut roses clung to her firm white skin. He had carried her one hundred fifty miles on his back to beg the Pope to nominate her for canonization. The police had thrown him in jail. The body was interred in the city morgue. <p>\r\nThe kid behind the counter seemed to have a ring or stud or bolt through every facial feature. He poured my coffee and totaled my order. I handed him a twenty. <p>\r\nâ€œYou donâ€™t have anything smaller?â€ <p>\r\nI looked in my wallet. Two more twenties and a fifty. â€œNo.â€ <p>\r\nHe rolled his eyes and counted out all the change in his drawer. <p>\r\nBack on the street I hailed a cab and told the driver drop me off at the Fox Photo downtown. When I pulled open the glass door I was caught in a wave of toxic chemical stench that made my eyes water. I was amazed that the red vested employees were not all blue faced and dead on the carpeted floor, and worried about the hideous brain-eating mutants they seemed fated to spawn. A friendly kid with slicked back hair and the downy traces of a moustache at the corners of his mouth asked if I needed help. <p>\r\nI opened my old patch-covered, grease stained messenger bag, incongruous against my starched French blue oxford shirt and pressed bone khakis, and pulled out a brown envelope. I slid it across the counter. <p>\r\nâ€œYeah,â€ I said. â€œI need this blown up and framed.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWell, letâ€™s see what weâ€™ve got here.â€ He opened the envelope and shook out the contents. <p>\r\nâ€œHey,â€ he said. â€œIs that--?â€<p>\r\nIt was a snapshot of Tsing Tao and me shortly after we had arrived in San Francisco. We were aboard the ferry to Alcatraz, still too new to the city to be anything but tourists. Behind us the Golden Gate Bridge soared into a prismatically pure blue sky. It was a winterâ€™s dayâ€”as close as it got to winterâ€”and we were in thick hooded sweatshirts, scarves wrapped around our throats. I had my arm around her. Tsing Tao was grinning rambunctiously and shoving my wool hat down over my eyes. <p>\r\nâ€œI guess not.â€ He looked up and arched an eyebrow at me. â€œIs that your girlfriend?â€ <p>\r\nI hesitated. â€œNo.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œJust friends, huh?â€ He tucked it back into its envelope. â€œToo bad. She looks just like that girl from that movie.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah. She does.â€ <p>\r\nI found the photograph tucked into a battered copy of <I>The Dancing Wu Li Masters.</I> I had forgotten I had it. I was shelving some books and when the picture fluttered down into my hand I sat and stared at it for fifteen minutes. I never wouldâ€™ve believed five years could go by so fast. <p>\r\nâ€œSo, enlarged to, say, 8 by 12? You want to use one of these frames?â€ He indicated little plastic boxes with inscriptions like <I>Friends Forever</I> and <I>Who Needs a Man when Youâ€™ve Got a Cat this Cute?</I> <p>\r\nâ€œNo, thatâ€™s ok. I brought one.â€ I reached into my bag and produced a black wooden frame from IKEA. <p>\r\nHe took out an order pad and jotted down my name and credit card number. â€œOk. When did you need this by?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œI need it by tonight.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œLast minute gift, huh?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah.â€ <p>\r\nExcept that it wasnâ€™t last minute. I had been thinking about it for weeks. For me the New Year began not on the first of January but on that day in November, the anniversary of our first kiss. <p>\r\nThe first year we celebrated it we didnâ€™t have much money. She was working as a waittress and I was making fifty dollars a day as a bicycle courier. But she asked one of the cooks at her restaurant to make us something nice and at the end of her shift he slipped her a styrofoam box with two buttery slabs of filet mignon and a mound of garlic mashed potatoes. On my way home Iâ€™d stopped at a little Mexican grocery store and bought some devotional candlesâ€”St Stephen, patron saint of bike messengers, for me, and the Magdalena, patron saint of hot chicks, for herâ€”and a bottle of wine. We climbed through the window and ate out on the fire escape, bundled up under farmhouse quilts weâ€™d brought all the way from western Massachusetts. The saints smiled benevolently upon us, their halos flickering. When we were done we sat and watched the prostitutes trying to earn their crack money on the street below. <p>\r\nâ€œWhat are you thinking about?â€ she said. <p>\r\nâ€œIs it creepy that weâ€™re celebrating our anniversary today?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œHe was only buried today, Marshall. He died a couple days before. Besides, Frank would rather I celebrate being with you than mourn him. He brought us together, in a way. He wouldâ€™ve loved you.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah.â€ She licked her thumb and reached to smooth my eyebrows. Her hand touched my cheek and I held it there. <p>\r\nâ€œWe should do something in his honor,â€ I said. <p>\r\nShe considered that for a moment. â€œYouâ€™re right, we should. Hold on a second.â€ She got up and climbed back into the apartment. She came back with a shot glass and a half pint of Makerâ€™s Mark someone had left behind after a party. She filled the little glass and handed it to me. <p>\r\nâ€œOk. Drink that. But hold it in your mouth. Donâ€™t swallow it.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWhat?â€ <p>\r\nShe gave me one of her crooked grins and tied her hair back. â€œJust trust me.â€ <p>\r\nI sniffed the whiskey. It had a corrosive tang that hinted of nausea. I winced and said, â€œOk. For old Frank.â€ I shook my head ruefully and tossed the whiskey back. I grimaced as it sloshed in my mouth and burned my tongue. <p>\r\nTsing Tao abruptly sat forward and kissed me, throwing her arms around my neck. Her tongue slipped between my lips and she sucked the whiskey from my mouth. She bit my chin as it scorched the back of her throat and flowed like into her belly like boiling oil. She squinted one eye and canted her head in pain. <p>\r\nâ€œYeeow,â€ she laughed. â€œThat was really weird. He always had whiskey on his breath. It was like I was kissing you both.â€ Then she jumped on top of me and we made it under the blankets while the crack whores hooted and cheered below us. <p>\r\nWe still had dinner every year. And we still kissed the shot of Kentucky straight, like the song said, even after weâ€™d broken up. My friends thought that was a little weird; I didnâ€™t have an answer for them. Things arenâ€™t always black and white. It hurt, but it felt right--it felt true. <p>\r\nâ€œFive oâ€™clock ok for you?â€ <p>\r\nI blinked. â€œHuh? Yeah. Five is good.â€ I put my credit card back in my wallet and walked across the street to work.\r\nThe Bazoomba.com lobby was filled with framed blow ups of ancient TV publicity stills. The beefy, handsome man in every photograph--judo chopping antennaed aliens, making commanding gestures in front of control panels festooned with flashing diodes and manual on/off switches, staring intrepidly into the farflung future with raygun in hand, a half naked space vixen clinging to his pantalooned leg--was the founder of the company. The show he had starred in thirty years before was still in syndication in every broadcast market in the country. <p>\r\nEveryone still called him the Captain. Endless cyberspace, he had told the venture capitalists, is best navigated in a cyber space <I>ship.</I> We were developing an internet application based on the flight deck of the legendary U.S.S.S <I>Bazoomba,</I> the starship he commanded through five seasons of big stick interplanetary diplomacy and soft-focus extra-terrestrial romance. He had never once, to my knowledge, stepped foot in our offices. <p>\r\nAs I crossed the threshold into the cubicle farm a recorded ensignâ€™s whistle announced that I had come aboard. I threw a sharp Interstellar Federation salute to the life-sized cardboard cutout of the Captain that guarded the inner sanctum and went to the kitchen for a bowlful of kidsâ€™ cereal. <p>\r\nI walked it carefully down the aisle, slurping alternately at my towering coffee and the sugary milk spilling out of the bowl. My desk phone began ringing just as I reached the poster, torn from one of Biancaâ€™s art magazines, that hung outside my cubicle. I had looked at it every day for two months, and I paused to admire it again. It was a corruscating vision of cherubs playing tag around the Throne of God. It was Botticelli by way of the hot rod custom shop. The painter was a San Francisco local, a kid laboring away in some Bayview warehouse with oil-stained concrete floors. <p> \r\nThe phone continued to shrill impatiently. I put my breakfast down on my desk and dropped into an office chair that cost more than my first car. I cradled the phone between shoulder and ear and stirred coffee into my cereal. <p>\r\nâ€œThis is Marshall.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOh. Marshall. Hi. Youâ€™re in early.â€ She paused. â€œI thought Iâ€™d get your voicemail.â€ <p>\r\nI leaned back, a slow grin spreading on my face. Hearing Tsing Taoâ€™s voice always tied a warm little knot in my chest. â€œTen in the morning only seems early to vampires like you.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOh, right.â€ Her laugh was in the wrong key. â€œHow are you?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œIâ€™m fine. Are we still on for tonight? Our reservations are for eight o clock.â€ <p>\r\nShe cleared her throat. â€œWell, thatâ€™s what I was calling about. I donâ€™t think Iâ€™m going to be able to make it.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWhat?â€ I sat up too quickly and soggy lumps of good luck spattered my khakis. â€œWhy not?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œSomething came up.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWhat came up?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œJust something.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œNo, what?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œNothing you want to hear about, Marshall.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOh.â€ The knot in my chest tightened til it ached. I toyed with one of an army of little action figures the Kid had thoughtfully arranged on my desk in a boggling array of <I>Kama Sutra</I> poses. I made Sylvain, the USSS <I>Bazoombaâ€™s</I>  half-human, half-alien-flora protocol officer, use his tendrils to do unthinkable things to Anna Supernova, the dykey but extremely hot First Security Chief. â€œWell, that sucks. I was looking forward to seeing you.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œMe too. Weâ€™ll hang out really soon. I promise.â€ <p>\r\nWe were both quiet for a moment. She said, â€œAre you mad?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œNo.â€ Under my direction, Waldorf, the burly Satyrian line chef, came over and stomped Anna into the dust with his little goat hooves. â€œItâ€™s ok. Iâ€™ll bring Bianca or the Kid.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYouâ€™re the best.â€ There was palpable relief in her voice. â€œIâ€™ll call you this weekend.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOk.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œBye Marshall. Love you.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œLove you too.â€ I hung up and played with the action figures. My uneaten cereal melted into a porridge of radioactive green and pink. <p>\r\nThe comet Halley traces an elliptical course about the sun that leads it to traverse the orbits of the inner planets of the solar system about once every seventy-five years. As long as it remains outside the demesne of Jupiter, it is little more than a hunk of coal smeared with snow, hurtling through space, invisible against the void. But when it approaches the sun again, after seventy years of peaceful sleep, it becomes sublime. As it heats in proportion to proximity, the cometâ€™s surface, its protective layers, are transfigured in an instant from ice to aether and swept away into space by the solar wind, loss trailing for ten million miles. It burns until it finally gets far enough to escape the sunâ€™s radiance, if not its pull. It can never escape, but it sleeps, and forgets, until the next return, when the comet loses a little more of itself. Astronomers estimate Halley has another forty thousand years until it finally fragments and falls into the sun. <p>\r\nAnna Supernova was showing a new and unbecoming interest in Waldorf. Sylvain, forgotten and forlorn, leaned against my monitor, tendrils waving haplessly. <p>\r\nThree years without Tsing Tao had taught me a lot about comets. Most of the time I was ok with how things had turned out. But every year, when calendars were turned to their last pages, I started to burn. <p>\r\nTwo faces, one cherubic, the other lupine, appeared over my cubicle wall. Bianca and the Kid grinned down at me. <p>\r\nâ€œHey,â€ he said. He waggled his eyebrows at me. â€œYou want to go get manicures tonight?â€\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3081,'i\\\'ve been theorizing about this for some time.\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i get up in the morning, i drink coffee. i usually make a fresh pot, but if there is more than a cup\\\'s worth left over from yesterday\\\'s pot and i\\\'m in a hurry, i just microwave a mug of it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i drink coffee, i put sugar into it with a spoon, and stir it. the spoon rubs lightly against the inside wall of the mug, and makes a noise on the ceramic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif i am drinking coffee out of the freshly brewed pot, the noise seems normal to me. just a stirring noise. but if i get the cup of coffee out of the microwave and put the sugar in it and stir, the noise changes as i stir, rising in pitch, almost as though the walls of the mug are becoming thinner as the spoon circumnavigates. in addition, the sugar makes a thin pale layer of froth on the top of the coffee, almost like a good pull of espresso.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni assume this is because the sugar that i add is changing the density of the coffee. however, there is an alternate possibility. the coffee is probably a lot hotter when i take it out of the microwave than when i get it directly out of the pot. maybe the sugar behaves differently at this temperature? i haven\\\'t tried just stirring a non-sugared cup of coffee out of the microwave. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni could really perform this experiment, i suppose. but i don\\\'t know that i want to become any more of a freak about this than i already am. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3082,'When I was 8 years old, I lived in a gloriously aging stone house on Fair street, in Red Bank, Tennessee. Across the street lived two twin brothers, my neighbors, named Matt and Taylor. They were at least a couple of years or so older than me.<br><br>\r\nAt the end of my street was the dirty trickle of water called <i>Suck Creek</i>; a thinly brief, dilapidated bridge crossed low above it, with slight cement buttresses guarding its edges, keeping errant cars within the single lane on the ten-foot stretch of wooden bridge. These edges were covered with chipped pockmarks and streaks of meta-flake automotive paint, the results of building a bridge too thin to accomodate any car built after the depression. These cement walls, the most permanent fixtures in my community of trailers and cinder-block-bound Camaros, were emblasoned with <i>WPA, 1934</i>, a concise epitaph, of sorts, to the movement that erected them as my hometown\\\'s most ambitious rural development project in the past century.<br><br> \r\nI used to play in the creek frequently, and once I remember going there with my step-brother and catching a bunch of crawfish in the creek, right under the bridge. We called them <i>craw-daddies</i>, and saved them up in a big orange plastic 5-gal paint bucket we stole from behind the <i>Golden Gallon</i>, the neighborhood\\\'s sole commercial establishment, save for the army surplus store, the grocery/hardware store, and the brick yard. The <i>Golden Gallon</i> was a package store that sold milk, eggs, cigarettes and candy in addition to it\\\'s single gas pump out front, right next to the broken payphone.<br><br> \r\nWe hauled the cray-filled bucket up the street to show the twins our catch. Matt grabbed the bucket and dumped its creeky, writhing contents on me, right there in the middle of his parent\\\'s pine-panneled basement den.<br><br>\r\nI remember shreiking and running home, where I recall exactly what my grandmother said to me when she saw my state: \\\"Peter, you shouldnt play with those naughty boys next door. I dont think they believe in <i>Jesus</i>\\\".<br><br>\r\nA few years later, Taylor was shot in Calhoun, Georgia, while robbing a <i>Golden Gallon</i>. Matt, the clear better half of his parent\\\'s offspring, ended up getting his girlfriend pregnant while romping in the back of his orange, Bondo-slathered International-Harvester pickup, when he was about 15. Today, he has a kid in middle-school, is a salesman for <i>Dixie Yarns</i>, teaches sunday school at the Red Bank Church of Christ, and is married to his pickup-jaunt sweetie. She\\\'s a guard at the <i>Silverdale Women\\\'s Workhouse and Correctional Facility</i> in the next town over.<br><br>\r\nOften are the days I wonder what my life would have been like had I remained living in Red Bank, Tennessee.<br><br>'),(3083,'i like the rain, but, cold rain isn\\\'t fun. it\\\'s not warm out today either. well, not summer warm, but thankfully not yet winter cold. as much as i like winter, it\\\'s crisp whiteness, it\\\'s rather crappy in the city. this place is so ugly in the winter.\r\n<p>\r\ni like winter because of the clothes. getting all bundled up in layers is rather fun, and you can feel so posh and fancy getting dressed up in dark clothes. big sweaters and heavy coats wrapped around you, keeping in the warmth.\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'d like to think that it\\\'s because of my ancestry, that i\\\'m comfortable in this weather. my family does come from a rather northern climate. my grandmother told me that she could see the borealis from her home back when she was young.\r\n<p>\r\nbut really, my dad hates winter, hates the cold and hates snow. he hates having to wear all the extra clothes, and the mess that all of the slush makes. i can understand why. i really can. i hate the slush, the salt, the gross streets in the city. \r\n<p>\r\nwhat i love about winter is the pristine new fallen snow on a field. so white and bright and full of nothing. i love seeing pine trees sag under the weight of all the flakes.\r\n<p>\r\ni love being able to drive for a couple hours and see nothing but snow. i love being able to feel the freedom of riding down a \\\'mountain\\\', if you want to call them that here in ontario, on my snowboard. it\\\'s exhilarating in a way that i can\\\'t describe.\r\n<p>\r\ni love the sound of snow crunching under my boots as i walk around. snow flying up in a puff if i fall when riding. the bites that it feels like it\\\'s taking at my cheecks when it hits them. \r\n<p>\r\nhell, i even love that i get a runny nose when it\\\'s cold. i know i am alive in winter because you have to actually make an effort at it.\r\n<p>\r\nand now i\\\'ve completely gone off the topic i started with. ah, memories of last winter coming back. i just wish it was sunnier in the winter. we get scant enough sunlight as it is, the days being shorter. i\\\'m sure a little extra sunlight would do wonders for my mood in the middle of the winter. maybe i should go somewhere warm this march.'),(3084,'<P>\r\nIn case anyone is interested in travelling in Spain, here is how it went for me.\r\n</P><p>\r\nI flew into Madrid last Thursday, arriving at Barajas airport at about 9:30 am. The taxi ride to my hotel, the Hotel Carlton (which I recommend), took about 20 minutes and cost about 20 euros. One euro is worth about one dollar US. I went to see El Prado, the big art museum in Madrid. I had one purpose over all in the Prado, and that was to see \\\"The Garden of Earthly Delights\\\" by Heironymus Bosch, or \\\"El Bosco\\\" as he is known in Spain. It was as cool as I had expected and even more so. Finding good food for dinner, however, was not so easy. Can you imagine sitting down in a bar, and the waitress bringing you tuna salad on toast as a free appetizer? I like tuna salad, but it was a little weird. \r\n</P><p>\r\nThe Hotel Carlton (E120)is a short walk from the train station. There I purchased tickets on the AVE, the Alta Velocidad Espana (high speed) train to Cordoba and then on to Sevilla (both in the Andalucia region of the south). Tickets to Cordoba cost E80 apiece for Club class, which is their 1st class seating. It was really nice, and included a meal. Travel time on the AVE to Cordoba was about 1hr 45min. Go ahead and splurge on the Club tickets, unless you are on a really tight budget. There is also a Prefere class, but hey.\r\n</P><p>\r\nThe thing to see in Cordoba is the mosque, La Mezquita, which was begun in 700-something AD. Some of the infrastructure of the town was built by the Romans in god knows what year BC. Nifty stuff. The food was better, but watch out--the Rioja wine in Madrid is very good red wine and if you like Californian/French Cabs and merlots you will love it; in Andalucia, however, rioja is a thin red served cold, which I found to be undrinkable. The nightlife is over pretty early near the mosque--for late night food and drinks, go to the shopping district. I stayed at the Hotel Macia Alfarons, which was reasonably priced at E110, and located a stone\\\'s throw from the aforementioned shopping district.\r\n</P><p>\r\nThe next day it was on to Sevilla via the AVE for E30. Sevilla has a lot of stuff to see--a castle and gardens from way back in the day, and some later stuff which was built for an expo in 1929 I believe. The later stuff, like the Plaza Espana, is kind of cheezy. It was beautiful though. It was Saturday, and lots of people were geting married there. There are enormous parks near the river, but the river itself is more like a ditch, and I got the feeling that they had built more stuff than they were prepared to take care of. That doesn\\\'t matter, though, because Sevilla is all about the creature comforts, and it\\\'s a good place to spend the night between historical explorations. The Barrio de las Juderias, or neighborhood of the Jews, is a maze of narrow streets mostly inaccessible to cars, with some really good (finally) terrace-style restaurants. Paella, sangria--it was really great. I don\\\'t usually make such a big deal out of food etc. but it was really hard to find something good sometimes. They eat lots of ham and cheeses and heavy stuff like that. We stayed that ngiht at the Hotel Frederick III, right in the Barrio, for E130 which was a little much but, not having reservations, was all we could get. The service, including the only porter at any hotel in which we stayed, was top-notch.\r\n</P><p>\r\nThe next day was another trip on the AVE back to Madrid with another stop in Cordoba--we had forgotten to take pictures there. Cari left her purse on the train, so we were in town longer than we had planned to be, waiting for it to come back on the AVE. It did, much to our relief, come back by 5:15 pm with all contents unmolested. Yay, AVE people! On to Madrid, then to the bus station for a trip to Salamanca, where Cari is in school for a semester. 2.5 hours ont he bus, which had nice leather reclining seats and tv\\\'s...unfortunately, it was about 95 degrees which nearly killed me. Also I felt a bit ill, and wondered how the passengers would take it if I threw up in the trash can. Badly I imagine. I didn\\\'t get sick for which I am still thanking God.\r\n</P><p>\r\nLate night arrival in Salamanca, Hotel abba Fonseca (that\\\'s how it\\\'s spelled, lower-case \\\'a\\\'), a steal at E82 or something like that. One of the nicer rooms of the whole trip. Beautiful view of the cathedral, which was indescribable. There was more historic stuff in Salamanca than anywhere else on teh trip, or at least bigger stuff. The cathedrals were as tall as a small skyscraper, hundreds of feet high. After contemplating them for a while, I realized what the reminded me of--pictures of heaven that one sees in religious pamphlets. I got the idea that they were trying to create a heaven-style place right there on earth. They pretty much succeeded, it\\\'s right impressive. Salamanca has been declared the City of Culture in Europe for 2002--La Ciudad de la Cultura de Europa, 2002.\r\n</P><P>The next day, Tuesday, I went home. And here I am.</P>'),(3085,'fall is a lovely time of year.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbefore i moved to new york, i imagined autumn in the city to be all shop-around-the-corner-pumpkin-chai-stylish-fall-coats. now that i\\\'m here i realize that it has less to do with attractive people frolicking in trendy suede outfits and more with leaving home when it\\\'s dark and not coming home until it\\\'s dark again.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m still trying to pinpoint the allure of the fall season. i believe it has a lot to do with years of brainwashing at school--after all, there is something cathartic about buying unsharpened pencils and unsullied notebooks. just like the students i teach now, i too hoped for perfect grades and unrivaled popularity. perhaps autumn is like the new year insofar as we all get somewhat of a fresh start.<br><br>\r\n\r\n(on a more concrete level, halloween and thanksgiving are pretty good holidays. be it in the form of chocolate bars or turkey and stuffing, they both offer mass quantities of tasty food.)<br><br>\r\n\r\nhaving gone to college in central new york, i miss the turning of the leaves. does this not happen in new york? i hear rumors that they go from green to dead. <br><br>\r\n'),(3086,'<p><b>Mrs. T.</b> was a self-described, \\\"powerful, Black, female.\\\"  Mrs. T. grew up in Cabrini Green - one of the worst housing projects in Chicago.  She had a stepfather; he molested her.  She was sexually assaulted in an elevator.  She got married; she had a child.  And when her son was four years old, her husband was shot - dead.  She went to work for a meat packing plant on the south side of the city.  One day when she arrived at the plant, she found the doors locked: the company had been shut down, and the property was sold.  Neither she nor any of the other employees had any warning.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For the first three days that it met, Mrs. T. was the instructor for the taxi class.  Mrs. T. mispronounced many of the words that she used.  And, from time to time, she spoke about herself and her life story rather than the course materials.  But that was all right.  Mrs. T. was responsible for teaching that component of the course that dealt with customer care, and diversity.  She did a good job.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Mrs. T. had a smile every morning; she had a positive attitude - about herself, and the people whom she taught.  Her voice was a Chicago voice; her story was a Chicago story.  And when people spent eight hours a day in her classroom, barriers were broken down.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But Chicago has other voices too; and they\\\'re not all so warm and soulful.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Mr. N. walked into the taxi classroom on the fourth day, after Mrs. T. left.  Mr. N. didn\\\'t describe himself in any way whatsoever, really; but a few of his personal details came out in his lecture - and in his manner too. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>Mr. N. teaches the geography of the city.  He\\\'s a white guy, about 69 years of age.  He doesn\\\'t say the word, \\\"place,\\\" he says, \\\"joint.\\\"  He has been married at least four times; and the odds that it was the, \\\"woman\\\'s fault,\\\" the first three times in a row seem pretty small.  Mr. N. smiles rarely, and when he does it\\\'s usually because there is a problem of some kind.  Mr. N. talks about the things that he has, and the places that he has been.  Mr. N. seems to have done pretty well in life - in the manner in which most people understand doing well.  Mr. N. still drives a cab from time to time; he owns cars and medallions.  He has connections; he\\\'s on the make.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>City Hall has things fixed now, so that new cabbies need to score 85% on their exams in order to obtain a license.  The vast majority of the questions on the City\\\'s exam deal with geography.  Students are required to learn the names and addresses of several hundred hotels, hospitals, offices, and other public buildings in addition to the preferred routes between them, and the streets themselves.  Mr. N. is the key to the geography.  And he doesn\\\'t give a fuck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The best of the students in the course don\\\'t give a fuck about Mr. N.  They see him as one more obstacle to be overcome; I\\\'m trying to learn from their example.  More than 1/3 of the students in the room have at least a bachelor\\\'s degree, if not more formal education.  They\\\'re dealing with three layers of government: federal, state, and municipal, almost daily: they need to worry about U.S. Immigration, licensing by the Illinois Secretary of State, and the City of Chicago, too.  They want a way - a legal way - to make money.  They\\\'re willing to assume risk: they seem to have a type of spiritedness that many native born Americans now lack.  And if one way is closed to them, they will make another.</p>'),(3087,'<p>\r\ni went to the gerhard richter opening at the sfmoma. my invitation was for 7 instead of 6 so i didn\\\'t get the free drinks, just the cheese and the crackers and the olives. no veggies. jd and i waited around for ages for\r\ndavies who slipped out of a cab in a tuxedo jacket and cumberbund oh and also some black stiletto heels. that\\\'s all. oh wait, i think some pink leg\r\nthings. i hesitate to call them legwarmers as you\\\'d probably be like oh that 80s\\\'ed out bitch but really it\\\'s a lot more complicated than that.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe opening was crowded. jd was uneasy because he had been making out with several of the curatorial associates or something. we left. we were\r\npractically gagging for a drink so we hightailed to the corner store for a big bottle of jack and then straight to the arrow bar ladies\\\' room where we drank and slapped each other and smoked capri\\\'s and ensnared several unsuspecting ladies who came in to pee.\r\n</p><p>\r\nappropriately wasted i followed them into a cab to club flake. there was a line. we were too drunk to wait and jd was just drunk enough to argue with the bouncer. i mean we were on the damn list or whatever. davies and i wandered off, drank the rest of the jack, slapped each other around a bit  and then made out and then fell over. i regained composure enough, once i\r\ngot up, to get davies in her cab and myself in another. i was on my way home to bed where i should be when linnea called and told me to go to the brown jug. like a total asshole, i went. i staggered in and mary poured me a tall jack and ginger and gave me a merit light 120 to smoke.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni can\\\'t remember what happened during this next bit except that i peed on i can\\\'t remember what happened during this next bit except that i peed on a sidewalk somewhere in the sunset. i went back to linnea\\\'s with her. i\\\'m drunk enough right now to admit here on swinney that i really love her even if i\\\'m sort of a fuck up. ok, bracket that. so i went back to her    \r\nhouse and we drank some wine and i accidentally ripped her shirt and she asked me to tie her up with it. so i did. but not very well. there was something kind of sweet about it. i woke up with two scratches down my   \r\nback and left.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmore TK\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3088,'I sat down to write about how my desire for a certain person in my life caused me a sad dull pain that filled me with a small sense of pride, but instead this came out when I balked last minute...<br><br><br>   \r\n\r\nA massive personal debt load and going on three years intermittent employment and <b>they</b> will call you a materialist, not someone that longs for the meaning behind the material symbols. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you been condemned for a possession? <br><br>\r\n\r\nPlayers and sluts are condemned as simple sex addicts <b>they</b> say, not simply people that have failed miserably in a search for a more meaningful love.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times has your desire for something other then sex been doubted?<br><br>\r\n\r\nCommunity is the holy grail for us all, make sure you conform to what <b>they</b> want or be excommunicated, <b>they</b> have limits to their tolerance. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you joined a community that preached tolerance only to be not tolerated due to your unwillingness to tolerate intolerance? <br><br>\r\n\r\nAs soon as you yearn for anything these days you have given in and then <b>they</b> condem you as too desireful. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you been human? <br><br>\r\n\r\nWho are <b>they</b>? You and me?<br><br> '),(3089,'I sat down to write about how my desire for a certain person in my life caused me a sad dull pain that filled me with a small sense of pride, but this came out...<br><br><br>   \r\n\r\nA massive personal debt load and going on three years intermittent employment and <b>they</b> will call you a materialist, not someone that longs for the meaning behind the material symbols. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you been condemned for a possession? <br><br>\r\n\r\nPlayers and sluts are condemned as simple sex addicts <b>they</b> say, not simply people that have failed miserably in a search for a more meaningful love.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times has your desire for something other then sex been doubted?<br><br>\r\n\r\nCommunity is the holy grail for us all, make sure you conform to what <b>they</b> want or be excommunicated, <b>they</b> have limits to their tolerance. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you joined a community that preached tolerance only to be not tolerated due to your unwillingness to tolerate intolerance? <br><br>\r\n\r\nAs soon as you yearn for anything these days you have given in and then <b>they</b> condem you as too desireful. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHow many times have you been human? <br><br>\r\n\r\nWho are <b>they</b>? You and me?<br><br> '),(3090,'<p>\r\nto skies in seoul are becoming colder. the balmy summer has come to an end. the rainy season, though it wasnt that rainy, has long since passed, and koreans are preparing for what they might call thier \\\'cold winters\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim sure winter here wont be anything like winter in ottawa. five years of minus fifty below celcius windchill factor jaunts to hools apartment to recreationize gave me tough skin. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto tell you the truth, the only thing i know of korean winters is from what i saw in episodes of m.a.s.h. However, unlike those in the four oh seventy seventh, i dont live in a tent. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas much as im not a fan of marching through the sleet and snow of an ottawa winter, its much better in my mind to be cold than to be hot. being hot sucks, and i have grown to like the fall and winter months best of all. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe changing of the seasons in canada meant leaves changing and falling to the ground to be picked up by me and my leaf sucker. in this part of seoul the leaves die and fall to the ground all year round. the air pollution here choking trees of clean air. In the country ive heard the changing of the leaves can be quite striking. However i have yet to leave the city, and the only indicators i have of seasonal change are in the temperature, length of day, and typically fall fruits and vegetables in the market down the hill from me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfall and winter are the seasons of long sleeves and jackets. I love long sleeves and i love jackets. Im not sure why i like fall fashion better than summer fashion but im sure it has something to do with where im from and what kind of body i have. im pretty sure that certain types of bodies like certain types of weather. I feel more comfortable in fall/winter clothes than i do in summer clothes. why? there are probably a number of reasons, all stemming from my rather low pot belly esteem. heh. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the spirit of this upcoming winter, i bought a pack of \\\'blend originals\\\', made in the EU by a scandinavian firm. god bless the scandinavian countries. why? their names are cute. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3091,'<p>\r\nThis weekend I went away, up north.  I was heading for Ninety Mile Beach, a beautiful stretch of coast on the west coast of the upper North Island.  The beach is incredible, it\\\'s huge, and long (although not 90 miles it is 57 or something) and you can drive on the beach as fast as you can on the open road.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDad had told me about a place to stay right on the beach, somewhere he stays quite often.  I trust Dad\\\'s advice, and so paid for the cabin and drove towards it.  Suddenly a wave of memory and nostalgia came over me - I had stayed here before.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt was the last family holiday I spent with both my parents, while they were still together.  It\\\'s funny I didn\\\'t make the connection before I found the cabin, because I remember that holiday so vividly.  I must have been about nine years old, it was summer, and we\\\'d planned the holiday a long time beforehand.  I remember being really excited.  It would have been the perfect vacation, however it turned out miserably.  My parents were on the brink of divorce, and they fought - loudly - the whole time we were away.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo being inside the cabin brought back some memories.  I remember lying on the bed reading a book, (I even remember the name of the book - Murphy\\\'s Orphan - strange!) listening to my parents fight.  I started to cry, as a nine year old would when their parents are screaming at each other.  My Dad sent me outside, so I sat on the steps of the cabin and tried to block out the yelling.  This scene repeated itself throughout the holiday.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t often think about my parent\\\'s divorce, and the supposed psychological effects it\\\'s meant to have on me.  Parents get divorced every day, lots of kids deal with it.  But standing face to face with my past, made me realise that I was affected - probably more than I know.  Even at such a young age, I knew what divorce meant.  I knew the split was coming long before my brother and my sister.  I overheard a conversation about the separation, and stayed silent about it for over a year.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe night our parents decided to break the news, I wriggled uncomfortably as Mum and Dad discussed when they were going to tell us.  It was then that Dad saw me, and knew that I knew.  He still apologises for that.  I tell him it\\\'s no big deal, that it was a very long time ago, that it\\\'s forgotten.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUntil this weekend, I thought it was.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nToday I feel lonely.  More lonely than I\\\'ve felt in a long time.  I\\\'m thinking too much, I wonder if my failed relationships are all because of my experiences with parents breaking up.  I wonder if my new chance at happiness and companionship will be fucked up again by how fucked up I am.  And weirdly enough, I want to be back on that beach, with my Mum and Dad.  I don\\\'t say that anymore.  Mum and Dad.\r\n</p>'),(3092,'<p>\r\ni met her at a jungle party. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe had all the things i like to look at in a woman. she had short hair, a cute nose, and long legs. In a lot of ways i regarded the introduction as just that; an introduction. i had no idea that by the end of the night we\\\'d be holding hands and making out on a bench in hongday. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni thought she was out of my league. she is so beautiful, and while i dont think im ugly, I dont have that six pack figure women claim they want in a man. I just thought she was talking to me because her friend was interested in shane. My self esteem that night was pretty low, and i can remember having a terrible time at the party before meeting her. I was tanked on crappy mexican beer just sitting on the floor of the ballroom recording the shitty set on my md. every five minutes id yell into the mic with terrible things to say about goldie. I was in a rotten mood. the music was bunk, and the ritz was the last place i wanted to be. security was maxed out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe left the party at around 4am. she came with us, and we ended up going to hongday. I really clicked with this girl and as shane and his friend went into the club to dance, we stayed outside and talked. we walked to the swings in the park near the club. I love swings. its not really about swinging higher and higher for me. i just like that lazy kind of swinging where you just let your legs dangle. she moved me to the bench and we made out. god it felt good to make out. I cant tell you how good it was for me. How much it helped my self esteem that night. it had been a rough week and it was perhaps the perfect end to a shitty month. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni decided to put some effort in. she wasnt the sort of girl i wanted to wham bam thank you mam. That sort of thing isnt what i want. we hung out all weekend. i was smitten, and still am.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe talked a lot on the phone which is something i really hate to do. Im not very good with phone conversation, but i pulled through and we started hanging out more and more. lunch and dinner here and there with a few drinks in between. we learned a lot about each other over the next couple of weeks, and as we learned i think we became closer.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlast friday she left for hong kong. she had business there, and so i spent the weekend just thinking and analyzing the situation. all my sensors indicated something good. I was in heaven. I was just so happy and excited about this girl. she gives me this feeling in my stomach i havnt felt in a long time. Im not sure ive ever had these kinds of feelings before. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nin the few quasi-relationships ive been in i\\\'ve always felt as though i was the one showing more affection, but with her i feel as though the affection is mutual. Infact, she moved in for the first kiss. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe got back from hong kong last night and we went out for a few beers in sinchon at my favourite bar moe-wong. everything seemed great and it was really good to see her. she looked great. she had a great trip. she got me a wicked keychain. everything seemed amazing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe left the bar at around eleven. she had to work the next day and wanted to get home early. she wanted to spend some time with her family. I understood and walked her to the taxi stand. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe called me at around one, and we talked for about seventy five minutes. the conversation was good until about the sixtieth minute, when she started getting kind of tense on the phone. Im not really sure what happened, but she said something like:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<P><i>\r\n\\\"you are very special to me dave. Ive never spent as much time with a man as ive spent with you. if nothing happens in the near future, dont expect anything to happen...\\\"\r\n</I>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nher voice gave me a chill. it was the kind of forward negative thinking i fear. it was the kind of forward negative thinking id heard before. i was hurt. i got off the phone. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbefore getting off the phone i asked her why she said that, but she didnt have an answer. I asked her if she was getting scared , and she said she didnt know. i just didnt feel like talking about it on the phone and i abruptly ended our conversation. as i put the phone down on my nightstand i muffled the sound of it hitting the table with a loud \\\"FUCK FUCK FUCK\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas perfect as life seems it never is what it seems, and as much as im capable of understanding this concept, i am unable to let it just slide. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntoday marked the end of two weeks of pure bliss. not that today was a depressed \\\'ima get fucked up\\\' kinda day, but i wasnt the same. people at work noticed my foggy look. they noticed my sombre and lifeless body sitting on the couch at break. Im not depressed. im not angry. Im just confused at the meaning of that statement. Im confused with my emotions, and im scared that she\\\'s the one for me, and that im not the one for her.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3093,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3093_1.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3093_1.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3093_1.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\n\r\nRegrettably, I must inform you that I have succumbed to the ignominions consumer-centric hegemony of our consumption-based capatilist monoculture: Subject to a fleeting moment of crestfallen purgative thirst-based catharsis, I threw myself to the truculent, dichotomous whims of the sanctimoniously stocked beverage coolers of my local convenience store, stocked with a veritable rancidity of artificially-flavoured beverages, one which now lingers, ponderously Machiavelian, on my slanderously unexpurgated palate. <br><Br>\r\nThere, the Apollyonesian cacodemon whispered to me in contritely veiled timbres: \\\"Select the <A href=\\\"http://www.pepsiblue.com/index.htm\\\">Pepsi-Blue</a>; let its fecund tincture conscript your ponderously parched tastebuds with salacious concupiscence! Writhe in its flavourful fandango of savory diskinesia!\\\", and with an alacrity more akin to blasphemy than parsimonious personal consumer motivations, I relinquished my 99 cents and stealthily retired to a remote corner of my bedroom to quietly consume my vitriol with all apropriate culpability.<br><br>\r\nAnd presently, as my sinewy fingers tingle with much predilection, tentatively transducing this exegesis into the keyboard, the squirming ephemera of my esophageal topology flowing with aforementioned dilettante beverage. <br><Br>\r\nYea, I am awash with rancidity and foulness, for I have partaken from the amphora of ultimate consternation; I have consumed yet another Pepsi-Blue. <br><br>\r\nMay the Fates have mercy on my wreched soul! \r\n'),(3094,'<p>A current of energy was flowing through the city despite the tangled gray in the skies and the light drizzle. The fashion-conscious, the dreadlocked, the ripped and pinned, the English tea drinkers, trembling men in alleys with silver tipped fingers in white powder bags, the overwhelming mass of college-aged people clutching urban messenger bags and smiles. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nArchitechure fell in swoops beside 70\\\'s painted coffeeshops and over cellar stairs advertising Live Shows and Private Viewings. The streets lined in the typical small european cars with yellow plates and rust. Dutch bicycles dominated every railing and river-side handrailing. Bridges arched between blocks, reminders of Venice minus the stench of raw sewage. Young men squated on winter coats, beating out the rhythm of the sidewalk on drums.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe dream of many drug users and partiers. The city of parties, coffeeshops pawning off varieties of greenery and paraphenalia. Raves. Pill suppliers on the corner. Open homosexuality. Clubs, bars, the endless lines of places to sit and buy. Red neon blaring above entrances, sex toys and videos. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Red Light District nestled close to the Dam Square. Down every alley lies an eternal red glow. Underneath red lights are glass panels, and behind these are wide assortments of women for sale. Indonesian, African, the rare caucation, the hidden elders and overly-figured. Men gawked from all angles, some obviously and without shame. Some glanced, shy, considering but conscious of the witnesses to a proposal. Window shopping. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAmsterdam. The city of Netherlandic sin. A city that throbs with a beat of foot steps and loud words. A city that lies in defiance of laws that govern the world outside of it\\\'s awake mob. Skull t-shirts, colored hair, ready lighters - the staff take their consecutive breaks. Security strikes a reminiscent fear of the past control. Quick reminder that there\\\'s nothing to hide, it\\\'s an offer to release all that one has held back. The loud cry of defiance and injustice, mingled with the cry from the seekers of politically and morally cleansed laws and restrictions echos back into this city and is reversed into a blasting static noise of \\\"HA.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAmsterdam. It belongs to no one and to the whole world. Amsterdam belongs to every face that walks it\\\'s streets, every hand that pays an euro, and every whore that taps her key on glass and smiles. Amsterdam is an island in watery country. Amsterdam needs a month to understand, a week to enjoy, and a night to live. </p>\r\n\r\n'),(3095,'<P>\r\n\\\"It is a mad world, completely confused, in which the communist is fighting the capitalist, the socialist is resisting both, and everybody is against somebody, struggling to arrive at a safe place, a position of power or comfort. The world is torn by conflicting beliefs, by caste and class distinctions, by separative nationalities, by every form of stupidity and cruelty -- and this is the world you are being educated to fit into. You are encouraged to fit into the framework of this disastrous society; your parents want you to do that and you also want to fit in.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nKrishnamurti is someone I very much respect. He does not preach. He does not say what is right and wrong. He simply states what is so obvious, and englightens us to various problems and insights into what life\\\'s about. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe often goes into great detail defining words and concepts used in questions he is asked to answer. He does not lay blame on any one person, group, or geography. He simply takes apart the question, discussing the language used, and in what context that language is exploited. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"The birds, the flowers, the flourishing trees, the heavens, the stars, the rivers and the fish therein--all this is life. Life is the poor and the rich; life is meditation; life is what we call religion, and it is also the subtle, hidden things of the mind--the envies, the ambitions, the passions, the fears, fulfilments and anxieties. All this and much more is life. But we generally prepare ourselves to understand only one corner of it.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh how true this is. with each phase of my education was encouraged to focus more and more on less and less. as I moved from elementary and high school into post secondary university, the focus was not so much on expanding my understanding of the world, but rather compacting that understanding into a degree program. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEducation isn\\\'t so much about learning as it is about conforming. This is something I have to agree with, and it is something Krishnamurti has a lot to say about. In his writings concerning education, he argues that we are not as much being educated as we are being socialized into living a life confined by various rules and doctrines of conduct. We are taught only certain ethics, and reactions, and are not as much taught how to understand those thing with which Krishnamurti states \\\'is life\\\', but rather how to live within certain aspects of \\\'that life\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI believe that if we as a species were to undertake a project to better understand the world in its entirity, the only thing separating the first and third world peoples would be the size and model of their cell phones. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3096,'<p>\r\n\r\nThe Kid was in love with a girl who worked at Mama Bonaventura\\\'s Hand of Glory Nail Salon and Academy of Divination. <p>\r\n\\\"Academy of Divination?\\\" Bianca arched an eyebrow and blew smoke through the open window of the cab. The tires on the wet pavement hissed like angry snakes as the driver gunned his yellow Crown Vic and we hydroplaned deep into the Mamaion. The air rushing into the back seat stank of garbage composting in gutters. It was the first rain the city had seen in seven months. <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah. They give you a manicure and tell your fortune.\\\" I tapped my window with a fingernail. The clear polish was already chipping off-at least on the parts I chewed. <p>\r\n\\\"Fortune telling. Jesus. An eight year old with a library book can pull that trick off.\\\" Bianca was an amateur magician and a dues paying member of CSICOP,  the investigative arm of the American Skeptics Union. <p>\r\n\\\"Palmistry is a science that\\\'s probably older than the pyramids,\\\" I said. \\\"Getting your palm read is a way to take part in a universal human experience-confrontation with the Unknown, the Mystery. Doesn\\\'t that interest you at all?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Blah blah blah blah blah.\\\" She took a long drag and her words came out in a stream of smoke. \\\"God, how you do go on, Marshall. The only mystery that concerns me is why I hang out with superstitious weirdos like you.\\\" She tossed her cigarette out the window. \\\"And I ought to wash your mouth out with soap for using the words \\\'science\\\' and \\\'palmistry\\\' in the same sentence. Have some respect.\\\" She waited a beat, then widened her eyes and shook her head expectantly. \\\"So? What did they tell you?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Well, we didn\\\'t get that far.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Why not?\\\" <p>\r\nI grinned at the back of the Kid\\\'s head. <p>\r\nHe twisted to scowl at me over the passenger seat headdrest. \\\"Fuck off, Marshall. I wasn\\\'t scared. You were about to get a ticket. I had to run out to put quarters in the meter.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Right. That\\\'s why I paid for the manicures and found you sitting in the passenger seat. You were defending my Explorer from meter maids.\\\" <p>\r\n The Kid gave me the finger and laughed. \\\"Ok, I got a little nervous. But this time I\\\'m going to ask her out, for sure.\\\" <p>\r\n The Kid met his new crush when we were drinking at the Kilowatt one night after work. Or rather, I was drinking at the Kilowatt, and the Kid was lording it over the pool table. He had a Wisconsin ID that identified him as Wilson Clampett, aged twenty-three, which only overstated the case by five years. The Kid was immensely tall, had huge gangling hands, and shaved his prematurely balding head. He dressed only in black. That day he was dressed in black trousers and a black V neck sweater over a black dress shirt. At first glance he could be mistaken for a giant carrion bird, or a vampire impervious to daylight. <p>\r\nThe Coors Light he ordered sat untouched on the bar as he defended his dominance of the green felt battlefied against all comers. I sipped my Makers rocks and watched the carnage. As far as I could tell, the Kid played pool by using his preternatural mathmatical abilities to calculate trajectories and velocities to the third or fourth decimal place. He just didn\\\'t lose. Ten times in the past hour he had stepped over to the chalkboard on the wall, wiped away the name of a humiliated cue-toting local now muttering into his drink back at the bar, and called out for his next victim. Word had gotten around, and people had stopped signing up for his table. There was only one name scribbled on the slate. <p>\r\n\\\"Inez!\\\" he called. \\\"Inez, you\\\'re up!\\\" <p>\r\nA small Latino girl with black braids under a blue bandana stepped away from the bar and walked over to the pool table, carrying a leather case. Her nipples asserted themselves through the thin fabric of her wifebeater. Her arms were covered in a cartoon jungle of parrots, fish, flowers and vines. She snapped open the case and pulled out two halves of a cue, looking the Kid over as she screwed them together. <p>\r\n\\\"I was watching you play. You\\\'re pretty good.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid didn\\\'t say anything. He just stared at her, his mouth hanging slightly open. <p>\r\n\\\"You want to put some money on this game? I play better when there\\\'s stakes involved.\\\" <p>\r\nHe nodded his head for a couple seconds before he remembered he knew how to speak. \\\"Uh, yeah. Sure. How much?\\\" <p>\r\nShe pulled out her wallet, fished out two bills and held them up. \\\"Twenty bucks?\\\" <p>\r\nHe looked at me. I shrugged. He said, \\\"I dunno. I don\\\'t want to take your money.\\\" <p>\r\nShe grinned at him. \\\"Don\\\'t worry about it, <I>papi</I>.\\\" As the Kid laid a twenty on the table, his expression was somewhere between that of someone just informed they won the lottery, and someone about to get run over by a truck. <p>\r\nHe let her break, an act of gallantry that cost him half an hour\\\'s pay. He racked the balls for her, sloshing them back and forth across the felt before aligning them with millimetrical accuracy at the end of the table. He eased the plastic triangle up and stepped back. He bowed with a theatrical flourish, the sort of geekery he confused with suavity. <p>\r\nInez crouched at the other end of the table, sighting along her cue like a sniper ready to kill. The glistening pool stick threaded between her fingers eased forwards once, twice, and then she put her whole body behind a shot that sent a thunderous crack cutting through the death metal that blasted from the bar stereo. Two balls-both solids-dropped into opposite corners. She stalked to where the Kid stood, smiling as she elbowed him aside to get her angle. The red three ball hit two bumpers before falling into the side pocket. The table was cleared with five more strokes. <p>\r\nShe came around the table and shook his hand. \\\"Thanks, that was fun.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid looked dazed. \\\"Wait, you\\\'re not going to give me a chance to avenge my defeat?\\\" <p>\r\nShe shrugged cheerfully. \\\"It\\\'s your money, <I>papi</I>.\\\" <p> \r\nShe beat him four more times. When they were done she insisted on buying him a drink. She ordered whiskey and he ordered the same. He brought her over to where I was sitting. <p>\r\n\\\"Inez, this is Marshall. Marshall, this is Inez.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"My friends call me Chupa,\\\" she said. <p>\r\nShe placed her small hand in mine. \\\"T-H-U-G\\\" was tattooed on the back of her knuckles in black gothic letters. The fingers on her other hand said \\\"4-E-V-A.\\\" I let go and she held on a few seconds longer. Behind her the Kid sipped hesitantly at his drink and made a face. <p>\r\nThe tip of her tongue ran over her front teeth. \\\"I figured you were his friend-you\\\'re the only two guys here with your shirts tucked in. I saw you watching us. You have nice hair.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Thanks.\\\" I glanced at the Kid, but he was still staring down at her, a goofy grin under his sharp nose. \\\"You\\\'re a real shark.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"My daddy taught me how to play.\\\" She turned my hand over and ran her fingertip over my cuticles. \\\"Your nails are in terrible shape.\\\" She reached for the Kid\\\'s hand. \\\"Yours too. You guys ought to come to my shop for a manicure.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Manicure?\\\" The Kid looked doubtful. Back in Cashocton, OH, where he had not attended his senior prom the previous spring, men did not get manicures. He looked at me and rushed to clear up any misconception. \\\"Hey, we\\\'re not gay or anything!\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I know, papi.\\\" She grinned up at him. \\\"Do you have a pen?\\\" <p>\r\nHe frantically patted himself down to find one. She scribbled an address on a cocktail napkin and put it in his hand. \\\"Come down sometime and get those raggedy-assed nails fixed up. Thanks for the game, man.\\\" <p>\r\nShe shook the Kid\\\'s hand, winked at me, and diappeared into the crowd at the bar. <p>\r\nThe Kid babbled about her for the rest of the night. We left when he said the cigarette smoke was giving him a headache. On the walk back to my SUV we stopped at a little groceria so he could buy some ibuprofen. The man behind the counter kept getting distracted by the soccer game on a little TV propped next to the condoms, and the line moved slowly. <p>\r\nThe Kid put his wrist to his pale forehead like he was taking his temperature. \\\"I feel sick.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Jesus, Kid. She\\\'s just a girl. Relax.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"She\\\'s the girl.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"How do you know?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"When she walked up to me, I felt like I got hit in the chest with a hammer. I couldn\\\'t breathe.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Whoa-the thunderbolt,\\\" I grinned. \\\"That is serious.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I think I\\\'m dying,\\\" he moaned. <p>\r\nI laughed into my fist. The Kid asked the clerk to get him a pack of Advil. Next to the register was a rack of oversized lollipops under a sign that said <I>Chupas 99 Centivos. </I><p>\r\n\\\"Hey, get her some of those,\\\" I said. \\\"You can go, \\\'You know, this lollipop\\\'s almost as sweet as you, Inez. You want to hang out some time?\\\' And if she\\\'s not smart enough to see what a great guy you are, then whatever. She won\\\'t be a chupa, she\\\'ll be La Chupacabra.\\\" <p>\r\nHe grinned and said, \\\"Don\\\'t be a dick.\\\" <p>\r\nOne week, four days and twenty-two hours later, Bianca was laughing as the cab pulled up at the curb in front of the salon. \\\"La Chupacabra! I love it.\\\" <p>\r\nI gave the driver a ten, pushed the door open with my shoulder, and stepped out onto the sidewalk. Bianca struggled out behind me. That night she looked just like the little girl from the Campbell\\\'s soup ads-if the little girl grew up to be one of the Pink Ladies in Grease. She had apple cheeks and black pigtails. Her vintage dress was tucked under a gas station jacket sized XXL. A tattoo of crossed pistols and tumbling dice marked the thick white calf above her frilly sock. Her Mary Janes were each customized with an inch long spike that promised real injury on top of the insult of her kicking your ass. <p>\r\nThe Hand of Glory was unlit. The neon sign was turned off, and the plaster saints in the windows kept  a lonely vigil in the dark, watching over a man sleeping on the sidewalk, huddled under a blanket decorated with smiling dinosaurs. Folding security gates been pulled partway across the plate glass storefront. <p> \r\n\\\"Is it even open?\\\" I said. <p>\r\nThe Kid leaned close to the door, shading his eyes against the glare of the street lamp. \\\"There\\\'re candles burning.\\\" He cupped his hands around his face. \\\"I think I saw someone moving.\\\" <p>\r\nBianca tapped on the glass. The shadows of the interior gave birth to a small black shape that seemed to glide to the door. There were rattles and knocks as chains were released and deadbolts thrown back. <p>\r\nThe door swung out and a puff of sandalwood smoke escaped, so strong it was almost narcotic. A five foot tall almond-eyed girl in a brightly patterned African headdress and matching skirt stepped into the threshold. Cowry shells and a leather thong slung with a dried rooster claw hung around her neck. A name tag pinned to her knotted t-shirt said, Hi! My name is May Ling. She stood with her hand on her bare hip, snapping her gum and regarding us with irritation. <p>\r\n\\\"We\\\'re closed.\\\" She tilted her head at a sign partially obscured by the gate. \\\"Six o\\\'clock today.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid stepped around Bianca. \\\"Is Inez still here?\\\" <p>\r\nMay Ling\\\'s cold glare melted like an icicle in the spring sun. \\\"Hey, I didn\\\'t see you! You brought another friend down, huh?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah, but if you\\\'re closed-\\\"<p>\r\nShe grabbed his arm and dragged him into the shop. \\\"Oh, it\\\'s no problem. Maybe this time you stay for your reading, huh?\\\" <p>\r\nOur shadows danced and gamboled on the walls, animated by the stuttering light of dozens of tall glass candles. The walls were covered with shelves sagging with tiny bottles of No Bad Lucky 13 Oil, jars of Magdalena Love Solution, saints\\\' cards, lottery candles, horseshoes, and railroad spikes. The floor was a marble checkerboard. It was impossible to see how far the shop extended; beyond the dim glow cast by the candles, it was black as space. <p>\r\nMay Ling dragged the Kid to a small white table that projected from the wall and forced him into a high backed office chair before he had a chance to protest.  She seated Bianca at the next station over and plopped down across from the Kid, seizing his hand and rubbing lotion into his nails with a cotton ball. <p>\r\nI moved to join them and stopped. There was something black and hulking wedged into the corner behind the entrance counter.  I walked around to get a better look. <p>\r\nA life-sized figure in mendicant\\\'s robes sat on a throne of laquered thorn bushes. The cowl hung low, concealing its face, but it was a skeleton\\\'s hands that clutched a foil-bladed scythe and a grade-school globe. Dozens of burning votive candles, floating in drinking glasses filled with water, were arrayed at its feet. A photograph was propped against each glass. <p>\r\nI looked back at my friends. Bianca was examining a small altar at the end of her table. The Kid was craning his neck desperately, trying to spot La Chupacabra, but it was too late; May Ling had traded the cotton balls for an emery board and set to work on his nails with a vengeance, chattering away. The Kid sighed and slumped in his chair. <p>\r\nI stooped to examine the pictures. A teenaged boy in a powder blue, lushly ruffled tuxedo. A black and white passport photo of a woman with a lazy eye. A family lined up at the rim of the Grand Canyon. A wedding portait of a stiffly smiling couple backed by opposing rows of faces determined to make the best of things. Tucked under each photo was a two dollar bill. I picked up a creased print of a man in a black mariachi outfit, beaming at the camera with one hand tucked into his vest. <p>\r\n \\\"Do you seek the aid of <I>La Santissima Muerte?</I>\\\" A pleasant rumble of rolling thunder and sibilance made me jump up, startled and guilty. <p>\r\nAn enormous smiling woman with a white turban and black eyepatch took the photo from my hand. I mumbled an apology as she bent down to put it back against its glass, leaning heavily on a cane carved to resemble intertwined snakes. I reached to help her but found that I couldn\\\'t. My hand stopped an inch from her coffee can of an upper arm, halted by something soft and invisible. Unaided, she heaved her white draped bulk upright and sighed. <p>\r\n\\\"<I>San La Muerte</I> is the best counsellor for young lovers in pain,\\\" she said. \\\"People with empty chests aching come to her for the return of their lost hearts. They leave offerings in the hope that she will help two become one again. And they are assured they will, sooner or later. All lovers are reunited in her embrace.\\\" She tilted her head back and regarded me with her one eye. \\\"You have lost love. That is why <I>San La Muerte</I> called you.\\\" <p>\r\nThe skin on my arms turned to gooseflesh. \\\"Actually, we just came to get manicures.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Do you see this?\\\" She thrust her face at me and jabbed a thick finger at her pirate\\\'s patch. <p>\r\nI took an involuntary step back. \\\"Yes.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I lost my eye and gained the sight.\\\" She put a motherly hand to my cheek. \\\"Love leaves the story of its loss written in the lines of the face. It\\\'s easy to read.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Mama Bonaventura, you\\\'re scaring him.\\\" La Chupacabra appeared at my side and took my arm. She wore an outfit that matched May Ling\\\'s. A ring in her belly reflected tiny flames. \\\"These are friends of mine. They just want their nails done.\\\" <p>\r\nThe older woman sighed. \\\"We closed twenty minutes ago, Inez. I wanted to get home in time for \\\'Seinfeld.\\\'\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I know, Mama, but they didn\\\'t get their readings last time.\\\" La Chupacabra pinched my cheek. \\\"Look at that sweet face. We can\\\'t let these poor boys go back out onto Mission St without some proper guidance.\\\" <p>\r\nMama Bonaventura rolled her eyes and made her stately way over to where Bianca sat. She turned on a small radio and salsa music encouraged our hearts to beat in time. La Chupacabra led me to her table, waving to the Kid as we passed. He gave me a plaintive look and I shrugged helplessly. I lay my hands across a wrist pad like the one that wasn\\\'t quite protecting me from injury at work and La Chupacabra began cleaning my nails with a cotton swab. The table was covered with squeeze bottles and little glass pots and a row of files and scissors laid out like a surgeon\\\'s arsenal. At the end, under shelves lined with dozens of shades of polish, was another little shrine. Candles in shot glasses burned around a foot high replica of <I>San La Muerte</I>. May Ling\\\'s table had a shrine dedicated to St Michael, who stood with his foot on the throat of a dragon, and Bianca sat next to a reproduction of the day of Pentacost, when the Holy Spirit descended on Jesus\\\'s apostles as tongues of fire. Flames flickered from wicks embedded in eleven little waxen heads. <p>\r\n\\\"Isn\\\'t it a little dark in here?\\\" Bianca said. \\\"How can you see what you\\\'re doing?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"We work by touch, child,\\\" Mama Bonaventura said as she pruned the cuticles from Bianca\\\'s fingertips. \\\"We care for the hands, which are the instruments of the soul. The soul sings through the hands in its true voice, its silent voice. I train my girls to hear the vibrations of the soul with their fingertips, and to tune the strings where the song has gone flat.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Oh.\\\" Bianca considered her nails. \\\"I was just hoping the right shade of red would get me laid.\\\" <p>\r\nLa Chupacabra set my fingers to soak in a little glass bowl filled with water. I toyed with the marbles the lined the bottom and stared at the altar at the end of the table, at the little figure with the skull face and the world in its hand. <I>San La Muerte</I>. A syncretic deity, a West African <I>orisha</I> mashed in with a Catholic saint under the weight of slave chains. Saint Death. But only a popular saint, unrecognized by the Vatican. That was what I liked about it. It was an expression of terror and veneration, the two most immediate reactions to an authentic brush with the Mystery. It was the real thing. <p>  \r\nI cleared my throat. \\\"Mama Bonaventura?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yes, <I>mijo.\\\"</I> <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'d like to buy this statue. This little <I>San La Muerte.</I>\\\" <p>\r\nShe looked at me, the tiny brush in her fingers suspended over Bianca\\\'s hand. She shook her head and began to apply polish. \\\"I\\\'m sorry, young man. It\\\'s not for sale.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ll give you fifty dollars for it.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"It was an heirloom, child. My grandmother left it to me.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"One hundred.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You are not listening.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"A thousand. One thousand dollars.\\\" It slipped out before I could stop it. <p>\r\nThe room went silent. Even the salsa music paused. Mama Bonaventura locked eyes with me, caught between a frown and a wink. <p>\r\n\\\"You have a credit card?\\\" <p>\r\nI fished it out, trying not to smudge the sticky clear polish on my nails. \\\"Right here.\\\" <p>\r\nShe sighed. \\\"Run it through, Inez.\\\" <p>\r\nLa Chupacabra looked startled. \\\"Really?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yes. Now, if you please, <I>mija.</I>\\\" <p>\r\nLa Chupacabra opened her mouth to say something, then closed it with a snap. She took the card from my fingers and disappeared behind the counter. <p>\r\nMama Bonaventura shook her head. \\\"It\\\'s a shame you are not as wise as you are rich, young man. <I>San La Muerte</I> will mark your interest.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Marshall, you are a freak,\\\" Bianca said. \\\"That was an unconscionable waste of money, even for you. That\\\'s not good art. That\\\'s not even good kitsch. I don\\\'t understand you at all sometimes.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"<I>San La Muerte</I> called him,\\\" Mama Bonaventura said. She shrugged. It was out of her hands. <p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t know about that. It\\\'s just his weird twisted Catholic guilt thing, as far as I can see.\\\" <p>\r\n	\\\"Your nails are finished.\\\" The proprietress of the Hand of Glory sat back in her office chair and regarded Bianca. \\\"Would you like your fortune told? I will read your palm myself. Usually, I would have one of my students do it, but they each have someone to practice on tonight.\\\" <p>\r\n	La Chupacabra handed me my card and sat back down across from me. \\\"She\\\'e never wrong.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Mama Bonaventura usually charges fifty dollars for a reading,\\\" May Ling said. <p>\r\n\\\"Well then, how can I say no?\\\" Bianca said, and smirked at me. She was already imagining the article she was going to write for the CSICOP quarterly. Maybe she was hoping The Amazing Randi would come up at the American Skeptical Society\\\'s annual convention and congratulate her for her fearless debunking. <p>\r\nMama Bonaventura took Bianca\\\'s plump hand in her own and turned it up towards the ceiling. With her other hand she flipped her eyepatch back, revealing sunken eyelids fused into a thin purple scar. She closed her good eye and began to sing, the sound low in her throat, almost inaudible, her head rocking gently from side to side. Her fingers trailed back and forth across Bianca\\\'s palm like she was reading a book for the blind. <p>\r\n\\\"You are the youngest of three,\\\" she said. \\\"You grew up among flowers. There is loss early-the father. He left, and you took comfort in the certainty of numbers, which are immutable and never stray. You have a passion for artichokes.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"That could be anyone,\\\" Bianca laughed. \\\"Everybody\\\'s parents divorced. And who doesn\\\'t like artichokes?\\\" <p>\r\nMama Bonaventura didn\\\'t appear to hear her. \\\"You are the most loyal of friends, and have often been hurt when others cannot meet the same standard. Even among the friends you have now, you are often lonely. <p>\r\n\\\"Oh, but there is love in your life.\\\" Mama Bonaventura\\\'s wrinkled finger traced a dry river bed that carved the terrain of Bianca\\\'s hand. \\\"True love. Painful love. The arrows of little Eros are dug deep. The pain flows in little streams here\\\"-she pointed to a specific point-\\\"and here, and here. But unacknowleged. A secret lover? No. A friend.\\\" She arched an eyebrow and a smile played on her lips. \\\"Someone very close to you, someone who does not know-\\\"<p>\r\nBianca jerked her hand back as if she had touched something scalding hot. She groped for her purse, still staring at Mama Bonaventura, and pulled out a pack of cigarettes still half in cellophane. She plucked one out and held it jauntily between two fingers. Her hand trembled. <p>\r\n\\\"I just remembered it\\\'s been twenty minutes since I smoked,\\\" she said. \\\"Nice act, though. I know a bunch of fourth graders who would find it very impressive.\\\" She jammed the cigarette in her mouth, lit it off the top of an apostle\\\'s head, and went outside. <p>\r\n\\\"Oh boy,\\\" the Kid said. <p>\r\nI reached over to touch Mama Bonaventura\\\'s hand. \\\"She didn\\\'t mean to be disrespectful. It\\\'s just that she gets really nervous around anything that can\\\'t be explained by physics.\\\" <p>\r\nThe old woman laughed. \\\"It\\\'s all explainable by physics, <I>mijo</I>, just not any physics she knows yet.\\\" She flipped her eyepatch down and winked at me.\r\nLa Chupacabra pulled my hand across the table. \\\"Do you want me to read your palm, Marshall?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I want you to read my palm,\\\" the Kid said. <p>\r\nMay Ling shook her head vigorously. Little skull earrings dangled and jumped. \\\"No, no. You at <I>my</I> station, <I>I</I> read you palm.\\\" She grabbed the Kid\\\'s hand and held it firmly against the gray wrist pad. <p>\r\nLa Chupacabra stroked my hand with her thumb. There was an elegant little geometric design painted on the nail. \\\"I thought you work with computers. Why do you have so many callouses?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Too much masturbating,\\\" the Kid said. He looked at Mama Bonaventura and clapped a hand over his mouth. \\\"Sorry.\\\" <p>\r\nI laughed. \\\"Lifting weights.\\\" <p>\r\nThe corner of her mouth curled up as she pursed her lips. \\\"I thought you looked like you\\\'re in good shape.\\\" She closed her eyes and began to hum, the tip of her finger tracing the lines of my palm. After a minute I was surprised to find I recognized the tune-it was by an English band, long defunct, famous for their gloom, their copious makeup, and their Medusan hair. <p>\r\n\\\"See this? That\\\'s your life line. It goes almost down to your wrist,\\\" she said. \\\"You\\\'re going to live a long, long time. Now see this one in the middle? That\\\'s your love line. All those little branches... I bet you\\\'ve broken a lot of hearts. But then they fall off and the line goes on alone. So I bet you\\\'re lonely. Are you lonely? But see this one that crosses it? That\\\'s an invisible influence opposing your fate. At the crossroads, anything can happen.\\\" <p>\r\nI glanced up at her. She was staring intently at me. When I met her gaze her eyes widened and jerked back to our couched hands. <p>\r\n\\\"So yeah, anything. But you have to be ready, man, because opportunities like that come very rarely. Mama Bonaventura told us a story about a man standing in front of a gate in a wall that stretched as far as the eye could see. On the other side of the wall was the Garden of Paradise, and the only way in was through the gate, but the gate only opened for one minute every thousand years. So the man stayed awake for a year, for a decade, for a century, for five hundred years, ready and waiting to run through the gate when it opened. Finally, after seven hundred years he said, \\\'Damn, I\\\'m really tired. I could sure use a little nap. It would be really refreshing, and then I could be even more vigilant.\\\' And you know what happened?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"What?\\\" I said. <p>\r\n\\\"He closed his eyes and fell asleep, like, instantly. And he had a dream that the gate opened. He looked through the gate and saw the Garden of Paradise. He saw God walking in the Garden with the parrots and the lions and the cockatoos and the monkeys and shit. Then the gate started to go up, and in the dream he thought, \\\'Damn, I should wake up, I don\\\'t want to take a chance on missing that beautiful garden.\\\' So he shook himself awake just in time to see the gate clang shut. It had really opened, right? So he was all \\\'Aaarrghh\\\' but he stood there and waited another thousand years. And you bet this time he stayed awake.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Did he make it in?\\\" the Kid said. Mama Bonaventura stood over him, coaching May Ling as she traced the lines of his palm with a pen. <p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t know, man.\\\" She grinned. \\\"I hope so, for his sake. I mean, I need my eight hours a night or I\\\'m a real bitch, you know what I\\\'m saying? So I wouldn\\\'t want to run into that guy after he stayed awake for two thousand years. I hope he\\\'s taking a long nap under trees heavy with fruit. But the <I>point,</I>\\\" she said to me, \\\"is you need to take your chances when you get them.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ll keep that in mind.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Like, I can see here, at this intersection-it\\\'s getting clearer now-there\\\'s a girl that likes you. And she might be really good for you, but you wouldn\\\'t necessarily know it, because she might not look like your type. Like, you\\\'re educated and successful, and she\\\'s kind of from the street, but she\\\'s going to City College because she wants to be someone like you, you know? So maybe she\\\'s standing there wondering if you\\\'ll give her a chance, and all you have to do is open your eyes and see your opportunity before it\\\'s gone.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid stood up abruptly, banging May Ling\\\'s table with his thighs. \\\"I just remembered I need a smoke too.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You don\\\'t smoke,\\\" I reminded him. <p>\r\n\\\"I do now.\\\" He stomped out of the shop. <p>\r\nMay Ling stood up and wrung her hands. \\\"Hey Kid, where you going? The reading not finished yet!\\\" <p>\r\nI rubbed my face. \\\"You can put the manicures on my credit card too. Put in a nice tip.\\\" I got up and followed the Kid out the door. <p>\r\nHe and Bianca were passing a cigarette back and forth in an angry huddle. They glared at me in tandem. <p>\r\n\\\"What was that all about?\\\" I said to him. <p>\r\n\\\"Don\\\'t pretend you didn\\\'t notice the way she was flirting with you.\\\" He tried to inhale smoke and started coughly violently. Bianca pounded him on the back and pried the cigarette from his fingers. <p>\r\n\\\"Whatever. I\\\'m not trying to cockblock you. I\\\'ve got no interest in her at all.\\\" I put my hand on his shoulder and peered up at him. \\\"Come on, man. You know I wouldn\\\'t do that to you. I know how much you like her.\\\" <p>\r\nHe sighed unhappily. \\\"Can we just go?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Huh? No. Are you kdding me? Look, we came all the way down here just so you can ask her out.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"There\\\'s no way.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Get back in there, Kid. You\\\'ve got nothing to lose. God loves the ones that try. Fortune favors the brave. He who dares, wins. That which doesn\\\'t kill you, etcetera, etcetera. Just do it. It\\\'ll be good practice.\\\" <p>\r\nHe gave me a grudging smile. \\\"God, you\\\'re a dick,\\\" he said. He straightened his shoulders and slapped himself across the face a couple times. \\\"Ok. Here I go.\\\" He didn\\\'t move. <p>\r\nBianca goosed him and he jumped. \\\"You\\\'re starting to bore me,\\\" she said. \\\"Ask the little hussy out so we can go to dinner.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid marched back to the Hand of Glory with all the enthusiasm of a man on his way to the hangman\\\'s scaffold. He pulled open the door and stepped back. Mama Bonaventura emerged from the shop, carrying the little statue of <I>San La Muerte.</I>\r\n\\\"You forgot someone, <I>mijo.</I>\\\" <p>\r\nI smacked my head and took it from her hands. \\\"Sorry. Thanks.\\\" <p>\r\nShe stood with her hand on her hip, just as May Ling had when we first arrived. \\\"It doesn\\\'t matter. You\\\'ve gained her notice. She will not forget <I>you.</I>\\\"\r\n\\\"Right.\\\" Something icy crawled up my spine. \\\"Well. Thanks for bringing her to me.\\\" She told me I was very welcome and went back inside, again crossing paths with the Kid. He was ebullient. <p>\r\n\\\"I did it. I got her number. We\\\'re going out,\\\" he said. \\\"Holy fuck! Look at my hands! They\\\'re still shaking. Adrenalin.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I hope La Chupacabra didn\\\'t see that,\\\" Bianca said. \\\"She\\\'ll probably think you\\\'ll give her Parkinson\\\'s.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ll promise to wear two condoms,\\\" the Kid said, and laughed. \\\"Damn, I feel good. I don\\\'t even care what my mom is going to say when she meets her.\\\"\r\n\r\n'),(3097,'<img alt=\\\"petersine\\\" src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3097_1.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\n<tt>\r\nHer emblems are shapes,<br>\r\nlike two photographs of the moon<br>\r\nand a pink pink iris.<br>\r\nHearts sewn in silvery sinews;<br>\r\narms as my music apparatus,<br>\r\nthrobbing, happy anthems.<br></tt>'),(3098,'<P>\r\nShe crashed in front of the payphone, \r\n13 nickels short of calling him. \r\n7 minutes ago a suit had been thrown violently on the floor, followed by 3 vicious kicks. \r\nShe was gagging on a combination of 17 too many \\\"screw the OJ\\\" screwdrivers and 27 hours of unwanted lack of sleep shoved down the wrong pipe by 2 fucking idiot bellhops and 1 big security guard who now truly hated their jobs.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHer lungs contracted uncontrollably. Former crackhead twitches resurfaced. She sank to the floor, unable to filter out Viva Vegas any longer,\r\n</P><P>\r\nit rushed her like\r\n</P><P>\r\nBlinking neon everything, seemingly attainable everything, id\\\'s cavity for sensory gratification now flooded with rough sex, vibrating sex, limp sex, icy minty head, swallowed cum, tickled assholes, Chicken Wellingtons, teriyaki steaks, juicy prime ribs, tossed salads, newport cigarettes, violent jamesons, a beautiful wedding, well vodka, double vodka, free vodka,\r\nvargas-de la hoya, picking fights, flung lipsticks, drunken shadowboxing, scraped knees, black-out bruises, losing money, losing money, losing money.\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe sat twitching, lungs contracting, as vegas bleached her brittle.\r\n</P>'),(3099,'<P>If you are tired of hearing my decision making process, go somewhere else.  That is all I have here.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI have nothing to say.  It is a chronic problem at this point.  I am furniture.  I need to get out of town and cool out.  It is too much soul sucking internet that doesn\\\'t matter.  I have become indifferent.  Forget this place.  I need to read a book.  I need to go home.  I need a home.  I need to be like, f. the world, this is where I live.  everything go away, I am at home.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nMy contracts are caught up.  Time to back out of this state.  Do something nutty.  Hitch a kalx ride to nowhere.  Do some driving.  Do I have any friends in Portland?  I want to visit Pat and Dan, and stay in portland a few days.  I don\\\'t think that is too far away.  and perhaps visit Olympia National Park, but don\\\'t get me started.  I want to do a big road trip to visit all these people on this site in Canada.  Perhaps if I got far away from where ass has been rooted in the Universe, going the rest of the way would not seem so impossible.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>\\\"You know you can\\\'t find no heaven\r\n <BR>nowhere in this big world, you know!\\\"</i>\r\n</P><P>I\\\'ve got the blues.  I\\\'ve got the grays.  I\\\'ve got clouds and cold air.  I have not read anything in three weeks.  It is sickening.  Some sort of spiritual resolution needs to happen like silent sunday, only computerless. I\\\'ve got the blueueueus.\r\n</P>\r\n<i>\\\"How come I can\\\'t be happy,\r\n<BR>like everybuddy else?!\r\n<BR>You know I been laying around here in chicago...\\\"</I>\r\n\r\n<P>guitar.</P>\r\n'),(3100,'<p>I could tell you the story of how my cat brought me a live mouse as I lay sleeping in bed the other night.  Theoretically I could just type it out for you, but it just doesn\\\'t work as well.  I can\\\'t convey all the finer details that way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Like how I really thought my cat had fallen out of the bed when I was jolted awake and I heard her claws scraping on the wood floors as she scrambled around. I turned the light on, but then she was just sitting there. I was so confused, and what was hanging out of her mouth?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Man, my heart started racing when I realized it was a mouse tail.  <i>How am I going to get a dead mouse out of her mouth?</i>  I woke my roommate up for some assistance and then went to the kitchen to get supplies when <i>Aaaaiee!  It\\\'s still alive!</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Of course, that explains it.  My cat had brought me the mouse, and I was in bed, so therefore she brought the mouse on the bed.  But it ran off onto the floor, which is what woke me up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We watched the cat play with the mouse until it burrowed into a shirt, and she sat back to wait for it to emerge.  I tried to help by taking my umbrella and shaking the shirt out, but then it just ran in my closet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There was nothing to do but go to bed and deal with it later.  I still need to pull some things out of there and make sure it\\\'s not back there dying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s so weird, isn\\\'t it?  The way my cat who has always lived indoors has these instincts which allow her to catch mice, giving her both the interest and the ability, even though she hasn\\\'t seen a mouse in her ten years.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'ve wondered it before, but what instincts do humans have?  I had this dream the other night that I had a baby and was completely ambivalent towards it.  Maternal instinct?  I must have missed that one.  I guess everyone has a habit of falling in love, but that\\\'s so cheesy, a cliche in the making.  And, you know, I don\\\'t have that one either anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe it could be different, though..</p>'),(3101,'<font size=\\\"5\\\">\\\"I</font>\\\'ll be right back,\\\" I told my wife, who was standing by \r\nthe bed. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Where are you going?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Downstairs. I have to do something.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Oh, I see. Hurry back... I\\\'ve got something \r\n  for you,\\\" she said, crawling under the covers. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â I hurried downstairs to the bathroom. Bad Chinese \r\n  (again). And the downstairs bathroom is the only one in our house with a window. \r\n  I was only trying to be polite. I even lit a candle. \r\n<p> <i>10 minutes later... </i>\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â The seat was warm and I trimmed \r\n  my nails. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Crap,\\\" I mumbled. \\\"We should never have ordered \r\n  the deep fried catfish. Way too risky. Ugh... I am never eating Chinese again.\\\" \r\n  <i>Clip....</i><i>Dink.</i> A piece of my nail hit the sink. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â Another ten minutes went by and most of the \r\n  restaurant left my body, though I could still smell the fortune cookies on my \r\n  breath. I flushed, blew out the candle and closed the door. When I got back \r\n  to the bedroom I noticed that her pajama bottoms were on the floor, the lights \r\n  were out, and she had tucked herself tightly into the comforter like an egg \r\n  roll. I changed my T-shirt and jumped into bed, lifting the blanket and letting \r\n  in cold air. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Aaack! It\\\'s cold,\\\" she said.\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Sorry...\\\" I shifted over toward her body. \\\"Oh, \r\n  what do we have here? No pants on?\\\" I put my hand on her thigh. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Jesus mother of god! You\\\'re fucking hands are \r\n  cold! Where were you?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â I positioned my other hand in her armpit. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Fuck man. Icicle fingers.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â I pressed my knees up against the back of hers, \r\n  my feet touching her toes. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Don\\\'t touch me! I\\\'m freezing,\\\" she said, her \r\n  teeth chattering. \\\"Move over.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Should I get another blanket?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"No, just don\\\'t touch me.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â I rolled back over onto my back and watched \r\n  the street lights slicing through the blinds onto the ceiling. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Tell me a story,\\\" she said. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Time is slinging past us so fast. Wouldn\\\'t \r\n  you agree?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Yes, but what are we going to do about it?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Spend less time sleeping and--\\\" I tried to continue my story.<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You know,\\\" she said, cutting me off, \\\"the maestro is coming \r\n  in November. Do you want to go to his show?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Of course. I like his work. Maybe we can get \r\n  the horse signed.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"He already signed the bottom of it.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Yeah but it would be something to have him \r\n  sign it again. Just to see his face--\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Nosecs,\\\" she mumbled something. \\\"Nosecs.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Is that how he signs it? I thought his name \r\n  was Juarez?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"No sex!\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Huh?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"No sex?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You asked me to tell you a stor--did I miss something?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â She sprung out of bed like a wife-in-a-box. \r\n  \\\"I\\\'m putting my winter pajamas on. No sex.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"So I missed my window of opportunity, huh? \r\n  First you tell me you\\\'re too cold, then to tell you a story, which you interrupt \r\n  with the news of the maestro coming to town and then--\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Why are you making such a big deal out of this? \r\n  Can\\\'t I just put my clothes on?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You can\\\'t just say \\\'no sex\\\' when I didn\\\'t even \r\n  know there was going to be sex.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Well, I was naked. What else did you want me \r\n  to do?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Tell me we were going to have sex?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â She put the pillow over her head as I continued \r\n  talking. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"I mean, at least put your hand on Leonard junior \r\n  and let him know what\\\'s happening.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You never had a problem before getting an erection.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Wha... huh? Hello? I don\\\'t have a problem. \r\n  I didn\\\'t know we were going to have sex. At least all those other times I knew \r\n  what was going to happen. You asked me to tell you a story and you were cold \r\n  and--\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Forget it.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"No way. You can\\\'t say \\\'no sex\\\' when I didn\\\'t \r\n  even know there was going to be any. It\\\'s like putting the closed sign up in \r\n  your shop and the whole time no one even knew it was open. Bah... it\\\'s unfair.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You shouldn\\\'t pout,\\\" she said. \\\"Don\\\'t be a baby.\\\"\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"I\\\'m not pouting. I just can\\\'t believe you would \r\n  say that. Besides, babies don\\\'t need sex.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Get over it.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Whatever.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Oh yeah? Well... well... NO BLANKETS for you!\\\" \r\n  I said, ripping the comforter off the bed. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"I\\\'m freezing you ass!\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"No blankets.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â She tried to pull them back but my grip was \r\n  too strong for her frigid fingers. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"I said \\\'no blankets\\\'.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â She got out of bed and went over to the closet \r\n  and then came back to the side of the bed where I was faking sleep. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"You\\\'ve got a lot of nerve,\\\" she said. \\\"I can\\\'t \r\n  believe you\\\'ve got the audacity to take MY comforter away from ME.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"YOUR comforter? Well, you were on MY side of \r\n  the bed!\\\" I replied. \\\"Audacity? Get a new favorite word.\\\"\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"BUT this is MY mattress!\\\" she said, tearing \r\n  off the comforter and quickly replacing it with a throw she got from the closet. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"What\\\'s the fuck is this?\\\" I looked the the \r\n  blanket. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"It\\\'s your blanket.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"This wouldn\\\'t keep a whale warm,\\\" I said as \r\n  she left the room. \\\"Where are you going?\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Screw you,\\\" she said from the top of the stairs. \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"What?\\\"\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"That\\\'s my new favorite word!\\\"\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"I think you mean WORDS. Two words.\\\" \r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Screw you.\\\"\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â \\\"Ppppffft. Whatever. I\\\'ll just stay here in \r\n  the bed all by myself. See if I care. I\\\'ve got this whole bed to MYSELF!\\\" I \r\n  said just loud enough.\r\n<p> Â Â Â Â Â She was right--it was my blanket and it was \r\n  thin. It barely reached to my knees, let alone my shoulders. When I pulled one \r\n  end up the other got shorter and when I pushed it down to keep my legs warm \r\n  my nipples turned blue. Needless to say it took me awhile to nod off but at \r\n  least I got to sleep on my side of the bed. '),(3102,'<p>I don\'t know about this Cognitive Science, this Computer Science, this Neuroscience, this Political Science. This science. I don\'t know about these Economics, these Semiotics, these Biophysics, these Classics, these Linguistics, these Mathematics. These academics. I don\'t know.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I know about the spark in your eye, the curve of your jaw, the curl of your lips, and the gentle smell of the nape of your neck. I know how my insides are stirred into tiny explosions when I watch you making love to the world around you with your hands and your words and your dreams made real.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Supposedly studious I show up each day to be taught the important things that these important people have been teaching students like me, or like someone, since forever. I even take notes. Sometimes. Opportunity cost, right, ok let\'s see oh semantic priming, yes mhm, forced oscillation, but of course. I never open my notebook except when I\'m supposed to write in it like all of the other kids. Dozens of pages full of my own scribblings that mean as much as the dust settling around me.  Oh I know what they\r\nrepresent, but what about their value?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I value the wind in my face as I\'m racing down the hill to meet you wondering if next time I should worry about that intersection blurring\r\naway behind me. I value the hand you gave me as we made <i>our</i> own little world around us with each step we took together. I value the nervous chewing the nervous biting the nervous toe-tapping-tapping as I wait just around the corner wondering if you\'re coming.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I came here to satisfy some desire, some prerogative, some need to be a part of something. To be somebody. To learn and contribute and join the world around me in discourse. I don\'t know why I came here. To be somebody. I left you behind to come to the cold and satisfy my ambition.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But fuck my ambition. It\'s empty satisfaction. Not even satisfaction, just distraction. I\'d rather have you in my arms than the world at my\r\nfingertips. Too bad I never had you in the first place and I\'m only just imagining this world of ours in a daydream in a classroom in a drafty\r\nbuilding with too much dirt under its fingernails and too many unsettled minds looking for an answer to a question they don\'t even know. They\'re\r\nlooking for answers while I\'m looking for you. And looking, and looking.</p>'),(3103,'I\'ve searched around these quiescent ruins in vain and all I\'ve found are the faces of enigma engraved on digital potsherds, smouldering fragments of some anthropomorphic frieze.<br><br> \r\nThat is what this poem will be in a thousand years; a shard, a fragment, the detritus of a world of disapprobation where unmeaningness is buried for all eternity. The residual touches of humanity are but fleetingly etched on my detached gaze, out over my city, out over people moving around stock-tickers and indian burial grounds.<br><br>So <i>this</i> is how the future turned out to be.'),(3104,'Over 300 original Dali pieces were shown in the Piazza Colonna\\\'s \\\"Palazzo Ferrajoli\\\".  I devour and absorb each work, memorize each stroke, every detail that evolves into a picture; this way I take home\r\neach art work with me, even if only in my mind.\r\nI try to understand what drives him to produce a\r\ntheme, a scenery, a thought.  He seems to tell me,\r\nthrough his form, that time is in our hands and at\r\nthe same time unattainable; time melts, time is \r\nalso precise and can be distorted.  I want Dali \r\nto speak to me, to tell me precisely what story \r\nhe wants to tell; what pain he wants to depict, \r\nand do I feel them too?  Once in a while, an annoying \r\nand clueless person stands in the way of Dali and me.  \r\nI hate it when they do that!  \\\"Wait in queue, and have respect I am in the middle of a conversation with a man from the past!\\\"'),(3105,'The summer heat is excrutiating.  Our skins feel\r\nthe stinging rays of the sun, I try to breathe as\r\nmuch air to maintain my strength.  The ancient\r\nruins tell a story, the story of nature\\\'s wrath and\r\nhow the beautiful Vesuvius, at its boiling point, \r\nis capable of taking a large toll of human life;\r\nhow it can bury an entire society. To the next \r\ngenerations it presents a legacy of brutal scars \r\nof human bodies, animals and objects cast with \r\nits fatal lava.  One particular mold outlines the\r\nfinal expressions of a human face in terror.  \r\nSculptured in molten rock, the artifacts talk to \r\nme, as though forewarning me of nature\\\'s potent \r\nforce that is capable of destruction and total \r\nannihilation of a people.  I somehow nod in agreement, as we are part of nature, and as human beings we, too, are capable of the creative and \r\ndestructive elements as evident in nature.\r\nI understood.'),(3106,'<p>The best way to learn the arts, history, society and culture of a place, the story of a community, is to stroll through its streets, to talk to the people, to touch the walls and listen to the works as they speak to you.  If your heart can listen, if your soul can see, then a story unfolds before you, and you are transported centuries back, to a time and place that lives forever. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIl Dottore and I strolled through the cobblestoned streets into hidden alleys and quarters where tourists\\\' footsteps have not dared to tread.  I have a mission to explore the internals of the city, to uncover the layers of time, to experience the places and people, the dialectic process of the story of man, in ancient Rome.  Il Dottore . . well he may had another mission in mind, I could not care less; I wanted to absorb the richness, splendor, glory of this ancient city.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn foot towards nowhere, we encountered Baroque style churches that appear in narrow streets and small spaces.  As we approach a small passageway, a huge structure encroaches before us. Instantaneously, we catch ourselves looking upwards towards this monumental building, overwhelmed by its size, while everything else around us, including myself, suddenly become miniscule; I am overcome by a feeling to submit myself onto to the vastness and greatness of this structure.  I am distracted from my thoughts, as Il Dottore whispers stories into my ears, I feel his heart throbbing with desire to conquer my tanned petite body.  A little bit more stories, and I may submit unto him.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am consumed by the divinity of this work. The ornamental details are elaborate; sculptured images \r\nof angelic and biblical figures juxtaposed skeletons and other diabolic symbols of death.  The structure is alive and draws my vision into the sky, giving me a sense of infinity and glory.  \r\nAd majoriam dei gloriam. '),(3107,'<P>Piazza Navona is filled with tourists, peddlers\r\nand striving Roman artists; outdoor cafes and restaurants surround the square as musicians\r\nserenade patrons with Napolitan music for a few lira.  In the middle of the square, the soothing sound of running water from Bernini\\\'s Fountain of Four Rivers gives me a cleansing sensation.  The four rivers are personified and represent the four known continents at that time.  Across the fountain, the church of Sant\\\'Agnese, originally designed by the Rainaldi\\\'s then Barromini, stands at the spot where St. Agnes was martyred.  They say that Bernini and Barromini had a tense relationship, possibly rivals in their trade.  Legend says that Bernini purposely sculpted one of the personifications in the fountains to pose with his hands up in the air, as if protecting \r\nhimself from the eventual collapse of the church\\\'s \r\nfacade.  The fountain was completed though before \r\nthe church\\\'s facade, must be one of those stories \r\nItalian men tell gullible girls to cast them into \r\ntheir spell, for a night of amore.  (Losers! Can\\\'t you just buy us a drink?) ... Wasn\\\'t that the case, eh Dottore?\r\n</P>'),(3108,'<P>A few meters across Corso Vittorio Emmanuele II,\r\nI find myself in the Piazza di Campo dei Fiori \r\nand close by Piazza Farnese.  I love Campo dei\r\nFiori!  It is unlike the other piazzas that\r\nare so predicatable and static. Campo dei Fiori \r\nis dynamic, it is a piazza where life thrives. \r\nIn the late mornings through the afternoons, flea\r\nmarket and vegetable vendors trade their produce\r\nin the square.  The piazza transforms into a\r\nmarketplace where one can strike a good deal.  \r\nBy late afternoon, the vendors\\\' stalls begin to\r\nclear as the city cleaners sweep the streets, in \r\ntime for dusk.  When you walk by the piazza after\r\n6pm for an aperitif, you will not find a trace of\r\nmarketplace activities.  The open cafes and\r\nrestau.bars are ready to serve you a drink or a\r\nmeal.  As the midnight hour approaches, the teenage\r\ncrowd begin to populate the square - the Romans, \r\nforeign students, delinquents, and the grunge.  At\r\nthe end of the night, broken bottles fill the streets awaiting for the early morning cleaning truck to sweep the piazza clean in time for the next day. Campo dei Fiori represents a realistic depiction of life, of Roman life.\r\n</P>'),(3109,'<p>The last week of August in Costa Smeralda, Porto Cervo, marks the beginning of the low season. The dionysian and bacchalian parties peak between mid-July through the third week of August.  Although Porto Cervo has never been as wild as the islands of Ibiza or the coastal resorts within the Greek isles; it has its own festive manifestation of decadence that is unique to this magical coastal paradise.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDuring peak season the most frequented clubs can be found in Golfo di Pevero.  On this single strip are Peppero Disco, Sopravento, Sottovento and the exclusive Billionaire Disco.  There are quite a few other club and bars in the surrounding areas of Porto Cervo that offer a variety of venues.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSince we were in the island during the decline of\r\nthe summer\\\'s peak season, we opted to skip \r\nBillionaire, due to the steep price tag associated with a single night of clubbing (60 euro entrance, and possibly over 30 euro for ONE drink).  Not to mention, that much as the Italian men are known for their amorous ways, they are unlike the men you meet in New York or London bars, that gladly buy you drinks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn our search for that perfect atmosphere, we found ourselves in the Porto Cervo Centro, near the famous piazzetta of Porto Cervo, and into this very cosmopolitan, hip, and definitely Manhattan-like bar called Coast, a disco bar.  It is situated near the water, by the Porto Cervo marina.  The dim colored emerald lights perfectly blends with the pastel green color lounge couches and bean bags surrounding the narrow bar area.  At the back of the bar, is a small dance floor with more bean bags scattered on the floor.  Outside of the bar, there is a deck that overlooks the water.  Aligned in symmetry are large metal vases containing dried twigs and branches surrounds the border of the deck; pastel green studio lights outline the patio to complete the visual look.  The seats in the deck consist of very high and large sofas alongside scattered bean bags, as in the bar.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe deejay played mixed club, techno and hip hop music. Since we were not yet oriented with the Italian hits for the summer, we responded to the hip hop tunes of Eminem and others; dancing among ourselves or in esctatic solitude.  The first night at Coast, we closed the bar and got free rounds of shots from the bartender.  We hang out with the deejay and the crew.  All in all, we just had a blast.  For the rest of our stay, we were the Coast-groupies, we would stop by the bar before exploring other clubs (such as Peppero Disco\\\'s closing night party).  We had a lot of time in our hands, since clubbing usually begins around after midnight.  The key was to space one\\\'s drink, and have lots of water (which costs 5 euro for a small bottle).\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere was this one latin song that kept ringing \r\nin our ears, at Coast, as well as in the stores, \r\nin people\\\'s cars, and all over the air.  During \r\nthis particular night, when the latin beat plays, \r\nthe Italians go crazy; dancing on top of the tables to a sort-of-Macarena derivative.  A few weeks into the Italian swing of things, we understood, it was the usual number one summer hit song in Italy - Las Ketchup (Asereje) by a group of Spanish girls.  In addition to the Spanish chicas, a local Italian artist appears to be this summer\\\'s runner-up hit - Saliro by Daniele Silvestri, filling the mediterranean breeze with his fusion-like techno-ish beat.'),(3110,'<p>When you saw me on the street that time did you stop to think whether I wanted to be there or not?  Did you ever think about me and what I was feeling?  Did you forget that I\\\'m a fairly hands-off type of person?  Did you notice how uncomfortable I was?  Did you stop for a second to read my body language?  Did you notice my slowly inching away and how I tried my damndest to not say anything to you?  Did you think that being touchy was a good idea?  Did you think of it as a friendly gesture?  Did you comprehend the fact that I was flighting rather than \r\nfighting?  Did you honestly think that I am less of an idiot than I was before?  Did you think everything and nothing had changed?</p>\r\n<p>This is going to happen again, isn\\\'t it?</p>'),(3111,'<p>Walking through a heavily foliaged wood, they cut \r\npaths into further darkness. The goal lying somewhere \r\nin the aim of light, to grind the weeds back into the \r\nground, unwrap the branches from the unmade paths and \r\nrelease the folded trees from their hindered movement. \r\nIt was a cool morning in fact, and the unpenetrating \r\nstealthily moving globe of light swayed far above the \r\nlast budding leaves. The light was the source, the \r\nmission on which they embarked, despite all opposition \r\nand offense, they would find their way. </p><p> \r\n\r\nFar ahead in the distance between the two and the \r\nelusive destined point grew a mass by morning and \r\nwared a mass by night. The battle was not to be won \r\nwhile the obstacles twined together purposefully ahead \r\nof them. The light green from the top and the grayish \r\nmislayed sticks from ground breathed together in one \r\nbreath: they would not defeat the road, they would not \r\nprevail, they would ultimately walk and break down as \r\nfar as they could until it ended. In this forest, \r\nthere was no end. There was no break of cover, no \r\nground that was left alive or free. </p>\r\n'),(3112,'<p>At the center of the piazza in Campo dei Fiori, I find myself drawn by a solemn and solitary figure.  I make my way towards the statue and slowly read the ancient latin inscription: </p>\r\n<p>A BRVNO IN SECOLO DA LVI DIVINATO QVI . . .</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI realize that before me stands the mournful monument of the great Italian philosopher, Giordano Bruno.  I pause for a moment to allow my mind to quickly process the facts stored on Bruno.  Inside my brain, the electrical pulses \r\nprocessing within its complex system, acting as a massive complex query-able relational database system; the archived information returns with the requested information.  I look back at Giordano Bruno with a faint smile painted on my lips.  I am overwhelmed by the brilliant mind of this man, his integrity to knowledge, to himself to existence.  He looks back at me as though he understood, that I understood, we both respected each other for we knew, we were of the same kindred soul.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe statue was erected in 1889 in memory of Giordano Bruno and in honor of all philosophers who were executed for their convictions.  Back in the 1500\\\'s Bruno was a Dominican friar educated in Aristotelian philosophy.  His intellectual career gained him attention and prominence.  His explorations led him to experimental theories and alternative modes of thinking.  In one publication, he argued that the universe was infinite containing infinite worlds with intelligent beings.  He was drawn towards the\r\nCopernican heliocentric theory and defended its\r\nposition.  He was in other words, a man who epitomized the Apple ad of: \\\"Think Different\\\"!  The Inquisition considered those who purported theories that were in contradiction to the dogmatic teachings of the church, as heretics.  Bruno was considered a heretic: tried, imprisoned, tortured, forced to recant, tried,\r\nimprisoned and tortured once more.  He stubbornly\r\nrefused to recant at the end of his journey.  He\r\nwas burned at the stake in 1600 at the Campo dei Fiori.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI stare back at him with pride and victory.  He briefly smiles and returns to his statuesque pose.  Giordano Bruno appears in his monk\\\'s clothing, a couple of books on his hands, and his face projecting a seriously pensive expression.  His brows are bent a little bit. He frowns and the statue freezes.  There he stands at the Campo dei Fiori, with a frown gazing towards the direction of the Basilica di San Pietro.'),(3113,'<p>Surrounded by the rustic Tuscan hills and villas, tucked away in obscurity are the designer outlets of: Prada, D&G, Fendi, Gucci, ArmaniExchange, Celine, Loewe, and others. My mother and sister insists on completing the shopping pilgrimage, even if we are very well aware that the products on sale in the outlets are at least two seasons behind. I wondered whether it was worth all the effort, since the Prada markdown was only 30%, was it worth putting my driving skills to test in the A1Firenze autostrada? My fashion crazed sister and mother have to see it for themselves, and so off we go.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe began our pilgrimage at 7am from Rome\\\'s historical center. I wanted to avoid the unruly motorino\\\'s that slalom their way through chaotic traffic, so we decided to leave the center before the commuting Roman\\\'s hit the city streets. We got sidetracked a couple of times, thanks to my very helpful co-pilots. Nevertheless, we triumphantly make our way to Via Salaria, which led us to the A1Firenze autostrada. It was a good couple of hours in the freeway. I tried to maximize my speed and pass through each big-rig obstacle as my co-pilot and sister ensures that I maintain safety guidelines. Although it was a lot of strain to drive through unchartered waters with quite a few back seat drivers who were constantly on my case, the breathtaking countryside, as we reach the Umbria then Tuscan region, overwhelmed my senses. Sprawling hills, fields of green and occassional castles and fortresses on top of little hills, gave me a sense of harmony and tranquility.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe breezed through each designer outlet, as I expected, since the items were the unsold products of two seasons back, and the markdown prices is not what I would consider a big bargain. We lunched at a trucker\\\'s dive in the middle of the countryside and factory buildings where they served a complete meal of primo piatto, secondo piatto with drinks for 8 euro per head. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the end of the day, we came away with a few shopping bags, it was not that bad; but it was not as great as we expected. From my point of view, the best designer outlets were Fendi, Gucci and Dolce&Gabbana. I was able to get a pair of shoes from the latest fall 2002 collection of Dolce&Gabbana for under 200 euro.  And that, I felt was a shopping achievement!\r\n'),(3114,'<P>san francisco has begun to feel like a fleece blanket in the dead of winter to me.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'ve now inhabited this city for three years and i\\\'m just now beginning to really feel like i belong here.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m still not fond of the weather, because i\\\'m a worshiper of the sun, but there\\\'s something comforting about san francisco\\\'s sleepy fog that wraps me in her blanket every evening.  the same fog that kisses my cheeks in the morning while i wait for the bus...\r\n</P><P>\r\ni love you san francisco.  \r\n</P>'),(3115,'Its so quiet outside tonight. I spent a while this evening assembling this new little shelf I got, ate grilled veggies with some friends, and spent some time doodling in my little toothy-papered book with big colorful markers. After a bit, I decided to go see what was shakin\\\' on the stoop, as we seem to be having those sorts of days that signal late autumn here in Manhattan; warm enough to sit outside for ten minutes without a jacket, but cold enough that you can\\\'t help but notice that winter\\\'s just around the corner, probably headed up Broadway to the Upper West Side as we speak.<br><br>\r\nI made a cup of raspberry zinger tea, and went out to the stoop to chat with Roberto, who was out there sweeping and generally tidying the sidewalk out front. He talked as I sipped; something about living in the South Bronx in 1978 and getting fired from a construction job about the time I had my first birthday. Then, he started musing about breakfast, saying he wished it was morning already so he could go across to the diner and get some toast and coffee.<br><br>\r\nSo, I went inside and made Roberto some buttery toast and heated up a cup of the leftover Sunday-morning coffee. He set down the broom for a few minutes and ate as I talked; I told him about how I used to love eating waffles for supper when I was a kid, and what the streets in San Francisco looked like, about how the hills and trolly-tracks lurk around so many corners of the otherwise gridded streets.<br><br>\r\nWiping buttered fingers on his shirt, he told me that he thought maybe San Francisco and Manhattan were brothers, spawned by Chicago and Detroit or something, and run away from home to see America\\\'s coasts. He told me that he\\\'d like to see a real beach someday, and that he hadn\\\'t left Manhattan since he moved here from the South Bronx back then in 1978.'),(3116,'Its so quiet outside tonight. I spent a while this evening assembling this new little shelf I got, ate grilled veggies with some friends, and spent some time doodling in my little toothy-papered book with big colorful markers. After a bit, I decided to go see what was shakin\\\' on the stoop, as we seem to be having those sorts of days that signal late autumn here; warm enough to sit outside for ten minutes without a jacket, but cold enough that you can\\\'t help but notice that winter\\\'s just around the corner, probably headed up Broadway to the Upper West Side as we speak.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI made a cup of raspberry zinger tea, and went out to the stoop to chat with Roberto, who was out there sweeping and generally tidying the sidewalk out front. He talked as I sipped; something about living in the South Bronx in 1978 and getting fired from a construction job about the time I had my first birthday. Then, he started musing about breakfast, saying he wished it was morning already so he could go across to the diner and get some toast and coffee.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo, I went inside and made Roberto some buttery toast and heated up a cup of the leftover Sunday-morning coffee. He set down the broom for a few minutes and ate as I talked; I told him about how I used to love eating waffles for supper when I was a kid, and what the streets in San Francisco looked like, about how the hills and trolly-tracks lurk around so many corners of the otherwise gridded streets.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWiping buttered fingers on his shirt, he told me that he thought maybe San Francisco and Manhattan were brothers, spawned by Chicago and Detroit or something, and run away from home to see America\\\'s coasts. He told me that he\\\'d like to see a real beach someday, and that he hadn\\\'t left Manhattan since he moved here from the South Bronx back then in 1978. \r\n'),(3117,'I wasnâ€™t going to sleep with him, but the food was too damn good. Fried catfish, turkey necks simmered in mustard greens, buttery cornbread, creamy mac&cheeseâ€¦ soul food designed to detach drawers.<br><br>  \r\n\r\nI had gotten real good at saying no to him for 3 years. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>Is that a pink thong you have on today?</i><br>He asked softly while I did a jab-cross-upper cut combo into his punch mitts. <br>The next day, I purposely started off shadowboxing in the middle of the ring where the light shines directly on you. He came through the ropes laughing. <i><br>Point taken, but your striped, granny underwear still makes you look fucking sexy. When are we going out on our hot date?</i><br><br>\r\nI guess it was the anticipation of freaky things that made him the best trainer Iâ€™d ever had.<br> <i>No, no! Palm out, than grab down when you throw an elbow. Grab it like itâ€™s hot. Faster!<br>Damn, your knees are getting fierce. I bet your thighs and ass are looking tight under there.</i><br><br>\r\nIt was after my first fight that I agreed to a celebratory dinner. By that time, he had wiped pints of blood and unsuccessful spits from my face. Heâ€™d seen me as Iâ€™d cuss at myself, cry with frustration, fart during crunches, and his lewd comments had gradually waned. <br><br>\r\nI surprised us both after dinner. It was so automatic. You made this great meal, now I must fuck the shit out of you. He couldnâ€™t believe how easy it could have been. <br><br>\r\nIn between rounds we laughed together all night. \r\n'),(3118,'<p>Nick Drake.  Bryter Layter. Fly.</p>\r\n<p>Can\\\'t sleep so I\\\'m lying in bed with the LCD laptop lamp.  The murky glow on my hands is making my fingers appear as if underwater, plastic-like.  And the glow is synonymous with how my mind is feeling: murky, submerged, plastic.  I have a lot to say but am lacking the drive and means to say it.  I\\\'m not feeling up to broadcasting, nor am I up for sharing with just anyone.  In fact, I don\\\'t really feel like sharing with anyone.  Wait, scratch that.  I want to share with everybody and nobody.  Or I want to share with all but exclusively and in a tight and highly controlled manner.  I\\\'m emitting short bursts of static like an unstable synthesised element blasting out radiation as it decays in time with some cosmic funk that my stereo is incapable of tuning in.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m not aiming for coherence or linearity or structure.  I\\\'m not even aiming for completion or meaning.</p>\r\n<p>I had an interesting conversation with The Mom tonight.  After she confirmed for me that life is all about lying to people, I congratulated her and Dad on their excellent seat-of-the-pants parenting through the years.  To which she threw back at me that they weren\\\'t parenting anymore so I suggested that she is supposed to be imparting wisdom to me, the jaded twenty-something.  I was told that I had to wait until they turn fifty and she wouldn\\\'t listen to my requests that they speed the process up so it takes less than five years.  I don\\\'t think I can wait that long.</p>\r\n<p>I am a social caterpillar.</p>'),(3119,'<P>\r\nI\\\'ve just had one of those 5:32 AM subconscious \\\"revelations.\\\" It sounds ridiculous really, that I looked down at my desk and realized that two options lie before me on my desk: \r\n</P><P ALIGN=\\\"CENTER\\\">\r\n1.) My desk tidiness skills are severly lacking, or \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n2.) I arrange my physical items in relation to their importance in my life in the hobby and goal/wish sense. \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI\\\'m not left-handed (they say North Pole .. ?), and on the left lies some equipment - cd player, massive CD collection that breaks my leather seams and zippers, empty water glass, telephone, empty Marlboro Medium packs, internet software instruction booklets, plastic baggies with unpacked \\\"office supplies,\\\" headphones, random scraps of bills, used tea bag, ashtray that has repeatedly broken and been reglued in the last five months since it\\\'s arrival across 4000 miles to Germany.\r\n</P><P>\r\nCentered is the computer, keyboard, this outrageously large, used, left-over from some \\\'full figured\\\' gentleman office chair, and the obvious - me.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m right handed. Right side of the desk is my only plant, nicely watered - still green, book shelf, laundry detergent tabs (why are they still on my desk ..?), lighter, ashtray that has withstood the test of falls without breaking, lamp, silver mesh cup of random writing utensils, blank CDs, matches, remaining treat from Amsterdam, pins, post-its, flashlight, spoon, international phone card, and a design-covered blank book some church gave me that I use for notes on quick inspiration.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGave me an opportunity to reexamine. Now I should probably act on the subconscious signals, and ...\r\n</P><P>\r\nclean my desk.\r\n</P>'),(3120,'<p>well, folks, we have a winner--history 409, The History of South Carolina, has the most people who embarrass me by proxy so far in my adventures in education. there are several distinct types, or &quot;flavors&quot; as I like to call them, of embarrassing behavior exhibited by my fellow students. here\\\'s one, broken down.</p><p>\r\nType: Pedantry<br>\r\nClassification: Jackass Level Two<br>\r\nDescription of Offense: Student tries to show his/her intelligence to the rest of the class by using phrases that he or she would not use in ordinary conversation, when asking a question of the professor.<br>\r\nExample: &quot;Would you care to comment on why that is, Dr. Edgar?&quot;<br><br>\r\nyou might not see this as being so embarrassing, despite the rather forced, stilted tone of the young man in question, but here\\\'s the kicker: he said it once, the teacher asked him to repeat himself, and he said it again using the <i>same exact tone of voice</i>, and the same verbal rhythm. the combined effect was <b>devastating</b>. like, i literally felt physical pain.</p><p>\r\nmore to come!\r\n\r\n'),(3121,'<font size=\"5\">I</font> was saving the bananas for banana bread. I was waiting \r\nfor them to turn brown but keep that yellow hue like that Pakistani girl from \r\nmy high school, who, on free dress days always wore a yellow sweater and yellow \r\njeans. Everyone called her \"The bruised banana\". It was fucked up but it was true. \r\nEven in her school uniform she had dark brown spots around her knees and elbows, \r\nreminiscent of damaged banana flesh, the same parts most people eat around and \r\nthrow away, the same parts I use for banana bread. \r\n<p> \"I got the recipe from my mom,\" I said. \r\n<p> \"What recipe?\" my wife asked. \r\n<p> \"Only the recipe I\'ve been talking about since \r\n  we bought those bananas two weeks ago.\" \r\n<p> \"Oh, you mean your mom\'s recipe?\" \r\n<p> \"Yeah, it was my grandmother\'s mother\'s recipe.\" \r\n<p> \"You mean you great grandmother\'s, right?\" \r\n<p> \"If you want to get technical... yeah, I suppose \r\n  you\'re right,\" I played along. \r\n<p> \"Well, since we\'re getting technical I\'d like \r\n  to say that I think my mom\'s recipe is better.\" \r\n<p> \"Oh, we\'ll see. Tomorrow night... I will prove \r\n  you wrong.\" \r\n<p> \"I can\'t wait,\" she said. \r\n<p> I didn\'t say anything... I was too busy making \r\n  sure I had all the ingredients. \r\n<p align=\"center\"> <b>*** </b>\r\n<p> I spent the next day at work telling everyone how tonight I was going to make \r\n  banana bread and how it\'s the best banana bread in the world and, if they were \r\n  all lucky enough, I was going to bring them some, even to the people that don\'t \r\n  even deserve to sit on the toilet after I pissed on it... just to prove to them \r\n  all that this, indeed, was and IS the best tasting banana bread that anyone \r\n  has ever put in their mouth. \r\n<p>\"So, is it as good as you say it is?\" one of \r\n  them asked. \r\n<p>I nodded yes. \"So moist, yet fluffy... I crush \r\n  the walnuts... almost into a powder, and I only use the ripest of bananas--so \r\n  brown and bruised most people wouldn\'t think twice about them, but these are \r\n  the best kind to use... and only the BEST ingredients go in my banana bread.\" \r\n<p>\"Ooooh, I can\'t wait to dip a piece in a glass \r\n  of milk,\" another one of them said, closing their eyes. \r\n<p>\"If there\'s any left,\" I said, \"I\'ll bring some \r\n  in but, like I said before, I can\'t make any promises.\" \r\n<p>\"I\'ll suck your dick for a piece of that pie,\" \r\n  someone else blurted out. \r\n<p>\"It\'s not pie and I don\'t think so,\" I said. \r\n  \"Besides, this bread is better than that.\" \r\n<p>For the rest of the day I sat at my desk, laying \r\n  low and avoiding the ass kissers. I got the occasional wink and love note sticky \r\n  stuck to the side of my monitor when I wasn\'t looking. One of them read: \r\n<p><i>Oh, \r\n  banana bread <br>\r\nfor a small piece <br>\r\n  I\'ll \r\n  give you head <br>\r\n if \r\n  not <br>  \r\n  you ARE dead</i>! \r\n<p> I think it was safe to say that I got a few \r\n  people worked up about the whole baking situation; in fact, I was getting a \r\n  little hot myself thinking of what was going to be on my plate tonight--massaging \r\n  ripe bananas to a total mush, filling the house with the scrumptious scent of \r\n  banana bread, proving my wife wrong (again), etc. \r\n<p align=\"center\"> <b>*** </b>\r\n<p> I hadn\'t seen that much traffic since last Thanksgiving, when everyone waited \r\n  until the last minute to go home. I inched along the freeway. With each turn \r\n  of my tires, my forehead sweltered and my hands clutched the steering wheel with \r\n  the anticipation of turning on the oven the moment I walked through the door. \r\n<p align=\"center\"> <b>*** </b>\r\n<p> \"Fuck,\" I said, \"traffic.\" I looked over to the couch, where my wife was watching \r\n  TV. \"How did you get home so fast? And you look so relaxed....\" I let the door \r\n  slam behind me. \r\n<p> \"I took the streets,\" she said, \"And I had time \r\n  to clean the kitchen and paint my nails.\" \r\n<p>\"Clean... the... kitchen?\" I scanned \r\n  the counter... in the fruit basket... no... next to the toaster... no... above \r\n  the fridge... no... by the sink... no... in the trash... no. I dropped my bag. \r\n<p>\"Where the FUCK are MY bananas?\" \r\n<p>\"Huh? What are you on about?\" she said, muting \r\n  the TV. \r\n<p>\"I said, WHERE THE FUCK ARE MY BANANAS?\" \r\n<p>\"I threw them away. They were all icky.\" \r\n<p>I took a deep breath. \"Excuse me? You what? \r\n  You threw my--\" \r\n<p>\"Bananas away. Yes, they were oozing all over \r\n  the apples.\" \r\n<p>\"That\'s how I wanted them to be. That\'s the \r\n  way they\'re supposed to be for banana bread, which I was going to make tonight. I was just talking about it last night.\" \r\n<p>\"That\'s not how my mom\'s recipe goes.\" \r\n<p>\"Your mom doesn\'t live here... thank god,\" I \r\n  whispered. \r\n<p>\"What?\" \r\n<p>\"Let\'s call your mom right now and see how she \r\n  makes banana bread, okay? I\'d really like to know how SHE does things.\" \r\n<p>\"Fine,\" she said, \"I\'m sure she\'d love to hear \r\n  from you.\" \r\n<p>I picked up the phone. \"What\'s your mom\'s number?!\" \r\n<p>\"It\'s on speed dial. Number one.\" \r\n<p>\"And what number is my mom\'s?\" \r\n<p>\"Number four.\" \r\n<p>\"What?\" I asked. \"Why does your mom get to be number one \r\n  and mine number four? And who the fuck is number two and three?\" \r\n<p>\"Well, there\'s the cleaners on two and Bamboo \r\n  Inn on three. I put your mom on four because it rhymes with...\" \r\n<p>\"Don\'t even say it.\" \r\n<p>\"Fine.\" \r\n<p>\"You\'ve got a lot of nerve throwing my bananas \r\n  away.\" \r\n<p align=\"center\"> <b>***</b> \r\n<p> Like I said before, I was saving the ripe bananas for banana bread. I waited \r\n  for them to turn brown but stay sort of yellow  like that Pakistani girl (from \r\n  my high school) did when she wore a yellow sweater \r\n  and yellow jeans on free dress days. Everyone called her \"The bruised banana\" and laughed and pointed. \r\n  Yeah, it was fucked up but it was true. Even in her school uniform she had dark \r\n  brown spots around both of her knees and elbows, reminiscent of damaged banana \r\n  flesh, the same parts most people usually avoid and throw away, the same parts \r\n  I was supposed to use for banana bread.'),(3805,'<p>My friend Pandora died three Tuesdays ago. I saw her the Tuesday before that. She came to new york for New Years\' and I stood her up. So she stayed at Spring\'s house. We did get to hang out a couple times. She was really intense and needy and freaked out. It was a tough year for her. We were so so so so so happy to see each other though. It\'d had been too long. We drank maybe 3 boatloads of whiskey and caught up.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn New Years\' we were going to wear our special coordinated outfits. Years ago when we were roommates at the princehouse, she had a wedding to go to and insisted i be her date and get a suit to match her dress. We spent the whole day getting ready. She wore a cream colored dress and a gardenia in her hair. At that time she had red hair. She had red hair most of the time and that was the best color for her I think. I wore a cream colored suit with a gardenia on my lapel. And white patent leather loafers with a gold lion head faux buckle. We took forever getting ourselves together and finally made it over to the wedding around 5. It turned out the wedding had been in the morning and we were just getting in to the tail end of the reception. So we stayed for a minute, gave our respects to the bride then went home. When we got there, we got into her old bed with the fancy ironwork. Fully dressed under the white sheets. And spent the entire rest of the day and night lying in white formal wear in a white bed, passing a bottle of patron between us, listening to records. It was a perfect day.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ve been trying to write down my memories of Pandora for three weeks now. Nothing sounds right. I can\'t make anything sound as good or bad or weird or sloppy or beautiful as it actually was. Also I keep wanting to find an old baseball t-shirt with the last name of Hastings (her last name) on it and the number 1 on the front.  I would wear it all the time for her if i could find one.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe first time I hung out with P was when we both lived in New York. She and her boyfriend were best pals with me and my sort of boyfriend. One night we went to her friend\'s nightclub where she was on the bill as a fire breather. I had no idea what to expect. She got on stage and I was pretty much floored. She did her performance to Baraccuda and it was so hypnotic and hot and all of that sort of thing. Then something happened and I can\'t quite remember what it was. The MC or someone came on stage and did something near her and it made her jump and swallow a mouthful of kerosene and she kind of choked and had to run off the stage. We went to the bar and got her a big glass of milk and ran backstage to find her. Later we went and walked around and drank wine. We didn\'t smoke near her just in case.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPandora used to work Monday nights at the Beauty Bar in San Francisco. Arusha dj\'d. Other people did too sometimes. New Years\' day fell on a Monday one year and I\'m not positive but I think that was the year that we did ecstacy at a party with milla jovovitch and i woke up next to lil nick on the floor of julian\'s living room with compromising photos of jenny young in my back pocket. So then we went to visit Pandora at her work. We were the only ones in the place. A row of us down the bar. We ordered food from Cancun Taqueria and Pandora spent all night making us the various drinks she\'d made up. Pandora\'s box. The Pandora. Pandora 2. Rocky Mountain Pandora. There were a million of them and they were all named after her.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe started something called The Lottery at Cafe du Nord. It was a themed battle of the bands kind of thing. The first one was pirate themed. She had everyone meet her at the Beauty Bar one Monday and set out hat with kinds of instrumentation written on them. People put their names in the hats of whatever they knew how to do. She picked band captains. I remember saying Oh NO, please P don\'t make me do this, I\'m not that good at anything! Please no! And she said, do it? You\'re the first band captain! We had a month to write three songs and practice them. Ours was a three song story about a maiden who falls in love with the cap\'t and he makes her walk the plank. I played drums. We won!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nP also taught me to DJ. She and Aaron did the backroom at Bordello, a club my friend Jenny and Omar did at the Covered Wagon. After she and Aaron broke up, she did it alone. One night I was at home and one of the bartenders or maybe Jenny called me and said, dude Pandora is wasted and can\'t dj can you come down here and take care of her? So I went down there, gathered her up, took her home and went back and played the rest of her set with her records. For months after that, I\'d go down there and help out and practice and if she got too drunk, I\'d take over. It was really fun.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOther notes about lil miss pandora hastings:<br>\r\n1. She didn\'t ever wear pants, only dresses. Except when she discovered MY pants. Then she\'d steal them and say, Everyone thinks my ass looks AMAZING in these! Til this day I\'m missing two really great pairs.<br>\r\n2. When I lived in her room, she let me keep her nice lacey curtains. And she made fun of me for having such a girly room.<br>\r\n3. We once went to a friend\'s supposed bbq at tire beach. We\'d shopped all day for the perfect food items and when we got there, we found everyone hovered around a baby habachi eating grilled asparagus with mayo and drinking beer out of a keg in a nearby shopping cart. P said, step aside and check this out! And we busted out salmon steaks, fixings for a marinade, mushrooms, peppers, onions for grilling and cheese and olives and bread and wine. We made the best meal anyone\'s ever made with a plastic knife, a piece of tinfoil and a baby hibachi.<br>\r\n4. She sang in a band. Her voice was smokey and nice. And many times she made me get up and dance with her on top of bars.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ve been given the weighty task of making a memorial cd to give away at the one night revival of Bordello in her honor. Its this weekend in SF. I went to a service for her here too. I\'ll tell that story another time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo far, these are some songs that have made the short list:<br>\r\n1. get down make love, queen<br>\r\n2. black betty, ram jam<br>\r\n3. the friend catcher, birthday party<br>\r\n4. dancing barefoot, my generation, rock n roll nigger or something else, patti smith<br>\r\n5. after hours, the velvet underground<br>\r\n6. girl, suicide<br>\r\n7. solid gold, easy action, t.rex<br>\r\n8. the year to be hated, unitard<br>\r\n9---also, nina simone, johnny cash, joy division, television, maybe looking for a kiss by the new york dolls.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso anyway. i guess i don\'t know what else to tell you.\r\n</p>'),(3123,'<p>He leads her back down the hill and around the corner. He doesnâ€™t speak as they walk. She stares straight ahead, trying not to think about what sheâ€™s doing. Trying not to think about anything. The wind is strong enough to lift her hair off of her shoulders and pinken her cheeks. â€œAre you cold?â€ He asks. She shakes her head to answer, but he doesnâ€™t believe her. Sheâ€™s been shivering since they started downhill. He runs his thumb over her knuckles and points her into the next alley. There is an arrow painted on the ground, pointing toward a heavy wooden door. </p><p>\r\n\r\nThe sign above the door reads; Toothyâ€™s Place. â€œThis is it!â€ He says, and they push into the foyer. The restaurant is almost empty. The host podium is topped with a giant molar. She looks at him in surprise and puts a hand over her mouth to hide a giggle. The host pops out of the kitchen door. He walks toward them with a slight limp gathering two menus from the corner of the bar on his way. There is a french fry sticking out of the watch pocket of his vest. â€œTwo for dinner?â€ He doesnâ€™t wait for an answer. â€œThis way.â€ </p><p>\r\n\r\nTheir table is draped with a yellow cloth. There are dancing teeth on the napkins and tooth shaped salt and pepper shakers. â€œWhatâ€™s with the teeth?â€ She says. â€œI have no idea.â€ He replies and bumps her knee with his under the table. She jumps at the touch and looks into her lap. â€œSorry,â€ He says. â€œOh.â€ She says, â€œThatâ€™s alright.â€ She looks up and catches his eye and doesnâ€™t look away. He smiles and then stops. She stares at him until she feels like she canâ€™t breathe. She swallows and reaches for a menu. He slides his hand to meet hers and takes it. â€œIâ€™ve never done anything like this before.â€ He tells her. â€œI know.â€ She says, â€œI think Iâ€™d like a drink.â€\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe drinks help and food is good. Sheâ€™s stopped shivering finally, either from the warmth of the alcohol or the warmth of his smile. Or both. She relaxes into conversation. Someone is playing piano in the bar. Itâ€™s a song she knows but she canâ€™t remember the name. They sit quiet for a minute, listening to the music. â€œThank you.â€ She says suddenly. â€œFor what?â€ He asks. â€œForâ€¦ I donâ€™t know.â€ She says.â€ He shakes his head so that one curl bobs back and forth in front of his eyes. She canâ€™t believe how blue they look next to the yellow tablecloth. He moves his sneaker under her chair so their ankles lock, and says, â€œYouâ€™re welcome.â€</p>'),(3122,'<p>\r\nI drive to and from home at odd hours.  On the way to and from home there are no streetlights, just trees either side of the road.  If it\\\'s a clear night and the moon\\\'s out, everything is coated in silver and the ride is easier, more magical.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI drive as fast as you\\\'re allowed, with my music up loud - almost as loud as my singing.  I really enjoy the drive.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes though, I see possum bodies on the road, that I swerve madly to avoid.  I see live possums, frozen with fear in the middle of the road.  Once, about 5am in the morning, a suicidal duck stood right infront of my car and had no intention of getting out of the way.  Birds are crazy in the early morning.  They stand on the road and seem oblivious to the cars until the last second, when they fly away and come close to splattering themselves on my windscreen.  Maybe they don\\\'t, but it\\\'s how it looks to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t know what I\\\'d do if I hit anything.  Living in paradise has it\\\'s cons, so it seems.</p>'),(3124,'I was told by my friend Marijke recently that I need to making a list of some of the things I\\\'m pursuing in my life and then weigh them out. It seems that I am all over the place and  \r\nI\\\'m not the type of person that is good at being all over the place. I simply can\\\'t narrow things down, I feel paralyzed. The following are just a few of the current indecisions in my life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m still looking for work was most recently deemed over qualified for retail. They asked me what I had been doing in some of the time gaps in my resume and I couldn\\\'t really lie,  if I got the job they would find out eventually anyway. They are however, looking to see if they have a position in their management trainee program. I could start running everyday and wind up a manager of a sporting goods outlet, but it is still anybodies game.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m trying to sign up for the reserves but the first two regiments I have picked don\\\'t even have enough funds for a fulltime recruiting officer so I can check back at the beginning of the new fiscal year - April. I am going to try one more and if that doesn\\\'t work maybe just take it as a sign. On joining the army, it is still anybodies game. <br><br>\r\n\r\nKathy is in Edinborough, Scotland and seems to be doing well. We email each other about every second day and miss each other a lot. Will it keep up and will the feelings still be there after at least a year apart, it is still anybodies game. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI got a phone call from an old friend of the family out in Slyvan Lake Alberta who just got through her second divorce and had her second child at 25. She has a house I could live in and she could get me a job at the video store she manages but I find it a little unsettling how often she tells me she is lonely while telling me about all this. I\\\'m still talking to my friend Heather in Calgary too and my parents might spring for a ticket to Calgary for my xmas present but as for moving to Calgary or Alberta, it is still anybodies game.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI got an email from one of my old regulars from the pub who used to work for the Fraser Institute and who is now head advisor to Stephen Harper in Ottawa. He mentioned he noticed a shift in my politics from some of the rants on my site and said we should get together and talk about how to fix the world. No promises on work but that would be the idea behind the talk but on me going that far to the right, it is still anybodies game. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI applied for two more positions at the Department of Canadian Heritage. Government work seems to be the only thing I am qualified for <i>professionally</i> and the first two government jobs just about drove me off the deep end. Third time is a charm if I get an interview call I\\\'m going but if this ends up being a third strike, well I\\\'ll take that as a sign as well. On joining the Civil Service again, it is still anybodies game.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe problem is that every avenue is balanced by some competing issue ie. career fulfillment vs. economic need or desire to be close to loved ones vs. desire to live in a culture that has the potential to provide me with community. I feel fuct - trapped by an education debt invested in knowledge of a field I don\\\'t fit in and increasingly questioning the relations in my life due to what seems like a lot of distorted feedback from people about how they see me. Hool once said to me that it is easier to live in your own reality and that that is what he does. I don\\\'t have that ability anymore not sure if I ever did, my reality definitely has some hardwired shared components. Can I get past all this whinning and indecision to make something of myself, it is still anbodies game.  <br><br>'),(3125,'Being twelve was always so easy. What do you ever have to worry about? i remember worrying about jumping on mushrooms, feeding my turtle and wondering why it\\\'s beginning to smell like fall in August. Yech, the smell of autumn is the smell of school.\r\n<p>\r\nI remember Saturday mornings Iâ€™d take my big warm blanket from my bed. I wake up and walk to the living room and curl up on the couch and watch cartoons or play Zelda. My feet would get cold so I\\\'d pull them in underneath me and the blanket would make a nice satisfying crumpling sound.\r\n<p>\r\nThe sun would penetrate the half-open drapes and cast it\\\'s shadows across the room and cause the floor to turn golden and warm. When the rays would move closer to the couch Iâ€™d creep my feet out to let them bask. Setting my toes in the sunlight like a flower slowly turning to face the crisp. it was always unconscious, my brain engaged and fingers too busy on a control pad. \r\n<p>\r\nIn the cold bright winter on those days, i could sit for hours taking in the phosphor glow of Mario jumping or the blaring and glaring sounds the Roadrunner and Wiley Coyote. Once in a while Iâ€™d stand up and look out at the snow on the ground and think of that snow fort we\\\'d built yesterday.\r\n<p>\r\nIâ€™d get too excited to stay inside any longer and have to bundle up because mom said so and trundle outside to jump around in the snow banks.\r\n<p>\r\nIâ€™d go slide around in that same snow fort, adding new walls, new tunnels and an extra few snowballs in case a sneak attack was being planned. Then Iâ€™d run across the street and ring-ring-ring-ring the doorbell to get Chris to come outside to play. His mom would let me in to wait and Iâ€™d stand there melting in the front hall. Stand waiting and slowly getting hotter and more impatient and Iâ€™d yell, \\\"hurry up! HURRRY UUPPP!\\\" and then Chris would storm down the stairs and slam on his boots and we\\\'d charge outside once more.\r\n<p>\r\nWeâ€™d run across the lawn making tracks and kicking the flakes into the air and screaming and shouting and jumping and run to John\\\'s front door and pound at it until he came out telling us to stop. Weâ€™d all run back to the snow fort to get ourselves ready. The sneak attack could happen at any time!\r\n<p>\r\nIt was those big kids down the road that would always launch their sorties on us. Furious lobs of squishy snow from afar. We could never match the distance and that\\\'s why we had our fort to protect us. The volleys would start while i was in front of the outer wall and Iâ€™d dive over. The ploy was to hide just long enough that they\\\'d have to come in closer, and then we\\\'d counter-strike with our prepared ammunition.\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes we\\\'d fend off our attackers, but usually it would end up with them inside the fort just doing what we do too. Theyâ€™d help rebuild the walls they destroyed as they charged at us, two-fisted with snow. they were the big brothers of the neighbourhood, mean spirited but only in that best of ways. If any bullies showed up, they\\\'d defend and protect. They knew the other big kids, they knew how to make them go away.\r\n<p>\r\nAfter awhile we\\\'d all get red noses, the sun would start to cast the long dinnertime shadows and we\\\'d all have to run back inside as mom called us to eat. Inside we\\\'d all have our runny noses wiped, our boots and jackets and snowsuit pants dropped in a pile at the front door. i always wondered why the fun outside couldn\\\'t just last forever, but then Iâ€™d get to the kitchen and smell the food and forget about how cold my cheeks had become.'),(3126,'<I>just in time for halloween.</I>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone knew the Midtown was full of ghosts, but only Tsing Tao claimed to <I>see</I> them. Ghosts aren\'t malevolent, she told me; mostly they\'re just confused. Death\'s curtain came down on them and nothing happened: there was no tunnel of light, no pearly gate, no grinning mustachoied devil with a pitchfork. They sat up in their bed, or climbed out of their crumpled car, or pulled the gun out of their mouth, and walked around for days wondering why everyone was ignoring them. They know they\'re supposed to be somewhere, but no one told them where to go. They know they should be doing something, but they have no idea what. When you\'re a ghost, she said, you are the ultimate in frazzled indecision. <p>\r\nWithout any better ideas, they fall back on habit. Young ghosts try hard to stay hip. They lurk outside the circles of old friends, pretending to laugh at lines from the latest TV shows. Older ghosts crave familiarity; sometimes widowers wake with a start to the smell of their wives\' face cream and the unmistakeable creak of invisible weight settling into the mattress. <p>\r\nI wasn\'t sure if I believed in ghosts myself, but I was sure of this: from the cradle to the grave and beyond, human souls need attention the way plants need sunlight, and without it, they share the fate of a bright flower transplanted to the bottom of a mine. If there were ghosts who loved the Midtown it was because Tsing Tao joked with them and poured them beers and treated them like normal patrons, which was all they wanted-just to have someone acknowledge their existence. It was fair to say that there were ghosts sitting at her bar every night, even if most of them were still breathing. Everyone knows how much it hurts to be lonely, to be passed over and forgotten. But only ghosts know what it\'s like to be forgotten by God, and for some, all that stood between them and the unimaginable horror of oblivion was Tsing Tao\'s knowing grin, the same crooked smile she gave me when I walked into the Midtown\'s perpetual twilight and found her on her knees in front of the bar, sweeping something into a dustpan. It was early; the evening clamor was still hours away. The only sounds were the mutter of old men, the rattle of dice, and the rasp and tinkle of her cleaning. <p>\r\n\"Hey,\" she said, straightening up. The remains of a pint glass were poured into the trash and she put the pan and broom next to a copy of <I>The Master and Margarita </I>open facedown on the bar. She ran a hand through her cropped blue hair and tugged it into indignant spikes. \"Hank and Crane are acting up today.\" <p>\r\n\"Grumpy old men with nothing better to do,\" I said. She was wearing a faded, sleeveless Lickety Split Messenger Service t-shirt I had given her years before. I didn\'t know she still had it. <p>\r\nShe tilted her head and regarded me, clicking her tongue stud against her front teeth. \"Grumpy old <I>dead</I> men. You want a beer?\" <p>\r\nEven before Tsing Tao started tending bar, the Midtown had a haunted reputation. The jukebox in the front corner of the bar had only one song made before 1970: \"Annie Do the Lindy Hop,\" a race record that was a regional hit for Pharaoh Willis and his Imperial Orchestra in 1938. Every quarter devouring jukebox that had stood in the same corner had featured a copy of the song, and during the fall of 1962, when the big carnival-lit Wurlitzer went into the shop after an unlucky brawler\'s head had gone through the glass, someone had brought in a girl\'s pink portable record player to spin the 45 they salvaged from the wreckage. It hadn\'t been easy for Mickey Big Mouth to find a copy that would load into the modern CD playing machine, but it was necessary. The song had to be played at 11:22 every Saturday night or piercing screams would silence the bar and send patrons scurrying out the door to anyplace that wasn\'t filled with the sound of murder. When Mickey Big Mouth bought the bar, the story the former owner told him was that a young woman dancing with someone who was not her ex-fiance had been shot down by her jilted lover in the midst of a jitterbug contest. The crowd scattered and in just a couple of minutes she lay still in a small ocean of blood, gray and abandoned in the center of the dance floor. Hearing her favorite song distracted her from reliving her horrible death. Tsing Tao said that if she peered hard into the smoke that swirled under the pyramid of light that bathed the pool table, she could see a black girl with a hair band and a taffetta skirt bobbing and spinning with an invisible partner. <p>\r\nThe room was empty but for a few daytime regulars clustered at the far end of the bar, playing liar\'s dice and gambling away their SSI checks. A melancholy perfume seemed to waft from the unoccupied booths, a permanent reek of stale beer and stale lives that no number of illegal immigrant cleaners would ever subdue, no matter how dilligently they scrubbed. A Ouija board was open on the bar. Tsing Tao filled a pint glass with good beer and set it in on <I>Hello.</I> <p>\r\n\"This is it,\" she warned the empty air. \"If you guys knock this one over you are eighty-sixed. Marshall was going to be a priest. I\'ll have him do an exorcism or something.\" She leaned on her elbows across the bar from me. \"So what\'s up? I haven\'t seen you in ages.\" <p>\r\nI scratched at the label on my beer. \"Not much. The Captain put out a press release announcing that we\'re going to have a launch party in six weeks. It\'s a joke-we\'ll never be ready in time. What\'s up with you? Are you getting any writing done?\" <p>\r\n\"Not really. Just making notes.\" <p>\r\nAngry mutters at the end of the bar. A dispute over points. One of the pensioners tried to get up and stomp out, but his truss and his cane leeched the drama from his exit. He shuffled to the heavy black door and managed to get it open after several tugs. A chill wind sent a blizzard of white cocktail napkins into the air. Tsing Tao sighed and stubbed out her cigarette. \"There they go again. Hold that thought.\" She grabbed the brush and bent to sweep up the napkins, now black and soggy from the drainage mats on the floor. Vines and bunches of purple grapes climbed the pale slope of her thigh and disappeared up her plaid schoolgirl skirt. <p>\r\n\"Wait-Hank and Crane did that?\" <p>\r\n\"Yeah.\" <p>\r\n\"Someone opened the door. There was a draft.\" <p>\r\nShe tapped the pan against the trash can and turned on the sink to wash her hands. \"I know what it looked like. I\'m telling you what happened.\" <p>\r\nI grinned at her. \"Why can\'t you just collect cats like the rest of the crazy ladies?\" <p>\r\n\"Don\'t be ignorant, Marshall. Just because <I>you</I> can\'t see it doesn\'t mean it\'s not happening.\" The old men called to her from the end of the bar, waving the money they had bilked from their friend. Tsing Tao winked at me and went to see what they wanted. <p>\r\nCrane and Hank were the most troublesome and rambunctious of the ghosts of the Midtown, but everyone tolerated their tantrums with good humor because they were the only ghosts we personally knew. They had been sitting drunk on the same stools since before Tsing Tao was born. <p>\r\n\"We like to tell people we drink everyday but Christmas and Easter,\" Hank told me once. He was tall and mostly bald. Grizzled gray skin stretched tight over his cheekbones. \"But actually, the only day we\'ve missed in the last twenty years was the day of Willie Mays\'s funeral. Say hey, Willie Mays.\" He shook his head, remembering when the night sky of his life was much brighter for the shine of that single star. His elbow nudged the lumpy man in checkered slacks and orthopedic shoes sitting next to him. \"Then we went on a three day bender to make up for it, right, Crane?\" <p>\r\n\"It wasn\'t Mays, it was <I>Dimaggio,</I> you senile old fool.\" Crane slurped the last dregs of his beer. \"We went to North Beach for the memorial parade. You looked like a pansy with that white carnation on your jacket.\" <p>\r\nHank\'s hand stopped midway to delivering his pint glass to his parched, cracked lips. He turned his head a bare degree. \"It was Mays.\" <p>\r\n\"Dimaggio.\" <p>\r\n\"<I>Mays,</I> you old cocksucker.\" <p>\r\nCrane tackled him. They tumbled off their stools and rolled around on the floor, cursing, sputtering, and throwing weak old man punches until I waded in and separated them. That was what they did, night after night. They drank and brawled and went home to the apartment they shared on Fillmore Street. <p>\r\nNobody was sure if they were lovers, but after Crane died in a bathtub mishap, Hank stumbled around the neighborhood like a separated coinjoined twin. He put Crane\'s old gimme cap-<I>Frankie Says Relax,</I> it said-on the stool next to his and drank until he passed out. I helped Mickey Big Mouth carry him home and noticed a band of tender white skin on the ring finger of his left hand. He was dead a week later. Mickey Big Mouth found him when he went looking to collect on his bar tab. <p>\r\nNow when the Midtown got busy, there was a subharmonic hum in the dull roar of the crowds, a cantankerous old man rattle in the clink of the glasses in the sink. Tsing Tao said that sometimes she saw them out of the corner of her eye, waving dismissively and telling each other to shut up. The ouija board on the bar had been customized for their use; a sort of memorial where patrons had scrawled their favorites of the boys\' pet names and expressions: Judy. Dorothy. Shut it, Mary. Cockhound. Kiss my old cracked ass. If anything, their bickering had become more obstreperous now that they didn\'t have bodies to pound on or the balm of sex. <p>\r\n\"It only gets annoying when they start throwing things at each other,\" she said. \"Who gets to pick up the pint glasses? Me. Sometimes I really do think we should just get a priest or the Ghostbusters or someone in here to send them on their way. But I don\'t know if they\'d get sent away together. Maybe they would just up end wandering the world alone, and I don\'t want that on my conscience.  So I\'ll sweep up their mess. It\'s not their fault. Ghosts love; they just can\'t express it. No wonder they get so angry.\" <p>\r\nTsing Tao pulled a bottle opener from her back pocket and opened another beer for me with a kung fu flourish. \"Two of these used to tilt the world sideways for you.\" <p>\r\nI worked at peeling the wet label off. \"It goes down like spring water now. My Irish drinking genes are kicking in.\" <p>\r\n	\"Mine too.\" She opened a bottle for herself and took a long pull. Clouds of steam rose as Tsing Tao ran hot water into the sink and began dunking and rinsing glasses. <p>\r\n\"Anyway,\" she said. There was the scrape of glass on glass as she built towers next to the sink. \"Things are pretty good down at the theater.\" <p>\r\nI cleared my throat. \"Oh yeah?\" <p>\r\n\"Yeah. I\'ve got a new show.\" She wiped sweat from her forehead with the back of her hand.  \"It\'s sort of an erotic version of <I>Pirates of Penzance</I> I call <I>Pirates in Her Pants.</I>  Jade is co-starring. She\'s Long Joan Silver, and she\'s got this wooden leg, right, so we do this whole-\"<p>\r\nI looked out at the street and scratched the back of my head. \"Yeah, I get the idea.\" <p>\r\n\"Hey,\" she said. Hands submerged in soapy water, she paused. \"Why haven\'t you ever come to see me dance?\" <p>\r\nI imagined her spreading her ass cheeks and gyrating on stage, drunken Korean businessmen leaning forward like avid proctology students. I shrugged. \"Just busy, I guess.\" <p>\r\n\"It\'s not what you think, Marshall.\" A particularly stubborn lipstick stain made her forehead crease with annoyance. \"I\'m not prostituting myself or being exploited. It\'s like a performance piece, really. I mean, the guys in the crowd are watching me, but I\'m watching them, and I\'m constantly assessing the situation and adjusting my actions to get the reactions I want.  The stage is like a research lab. I\'ve learned so much about desire, and loneliness, and imagination. It\'s going to make an incredible book some day.\" <p>\r\nI grinned and said, \"You could build an MFA thesis around it.\" <p>\r\n\"I know you\'re being a sarcastic ass, but that is not a bad idea.\" She pursed her lips and nodded, considering it. \"You really should come down. If only to see the poster of us they put in the window. It\'s hilarious.\" <p>\r\n\"Oh, that reminds me.\" I pulled my bag into my lap, rummaged around and held out her present. The water went off and she wiped her hands on her t-shirt. The tightening fabric threw her breasts into relief.  She took it from me and gave it an ironic shake next to her ear, a puzzled half-smile on her face. <p>\r\n\"What is it?\" <p>\r\n\"Nothing big. Just something I was going to give you last week.\" <p>\r\nHer eyes widened when she saw the wrapping paper. She studied it for the space of several breaths and then raised her eyes to mine. \"What-why did you pick this?\" <p>\r\nI shrugged. \"I dunno. It was hanging up in my cube, and I didn\'t have time to get real gift wrap. It\'s amazing though, isn\'t it?\" <p>\r\n\"It is.\" She turned it over and pried the scotch tape up with a fingernail, careful not to tear the paper. She unfolded the repurposed poster from the package and spread it out on the bar, smoothing it with her hand. \"It really is.\" She stared at it, unmoving. <p>\r\n\"Well?\" I said. \"There\'s a present there, too.\" <p>\r\n\"Huh? Oh, duh.\" She opened the box, pulled out the picture in its trendy black frame, and gasped. <p>\r\n\"Oh my god, this is the day we took the ferry to Alcatraz, right?\" She held it up to study it in the dim light. Her fingertip on the photo traced an invisible line between us. \"It\'s hard to believe we were ever that young.\" <p>\r\n\"It wasn\'t even five years ago.\" <p>\r\nShe smiled ruefully at me. \"For some people, that\'s a whole lifetime.\" She arranged the picture on a shelf behind the bar among years of bartending detritus. Polaroids and bras, framed arrest warrants and a grainy newspaper photo of Mickey Big Mouth shaking hands with Jerry Lewis. And now Tsing Tao and I rough housing in the cold wet spray, me blind and grinning in pure delight. Only someone who had found his heart\'s desire could smile like that. I couldn\'t remember what it felt like. <p>\r\n\"Anyway, thank you. It\'s very sweet.\" Placing her hands flat on the bar, she propped herself up to kiss my cheek. She stopped halfway. Her eyes turned heavenward in exasperation and she smacked her forehead. <p>\r\n\"Oh my god! All this week I\'ve felt like Frank was hanging around, bugging me about something.\" She laughed and shook her head, pulling a bottle down from the top shelf and putting two shot glasses on the bar. \"I should\'ve known. He wasn\'t trying to warn me of impending disaster or tell me what a good daughter I was or anything. He just didn\'t get his whiskey this year.\" <p>\r\nTsing Tao winked at me and adopted a pompous announcer\'s voice as she poured. \"The Fourth Annual Frank Doherty Memorial Whiskey Shot.\" Sweet amber poison rose right to the glass rim. The bottle tilted to fill the other and I almost said, <I>Wait, we only need one,</I> but then I realized that this year we weren\'t going to be sharing. A long moment passed before I could force my hand across the bar to accept my drink. <p>\r\nHer eyes closed as she folded her hands in mock prayer. \"Frank, you were a rat bastard, but without your untimely passing I probably wouldn\'t be sitting in the middle of this beautiful city with my dear friend Marshall. All the good you failed to achieve in life, you\'ve managed to accomplish in death. I love you, daddy.\" She hoisted the whiskey. \"To Frank.\" <p>\r\nI raised mine in return. My voice seemed to come from miles and years away. \"To all the ghosts we love.\" <p>\r\n\"To all ghosts everywhere,\" she agreed. \"To love is to live, boys.\" <p>\r\nThe whiskey splashed the back of my throat, leaving traces that burned numbly on my lips. Remnants of a kiss that hadn\'t come. Tsing Tao thumped her empty glass down, laughing and wiping her eyes roughly. \"I\'m such a girl sometimes.\" <p>\r\nOut on the street, a huge truck idled behind a double parked car. The ground shook with the engine\'s rumble. <p>\r\nI tugged my jacket around me. It was suddenly very cold. The bar was awash in a miasma of old man smells: muscle cream, medicated foot powder, underwear stained with incontinent dribbling, open mouthed breathing cured with fifty years of nicotine. The empty stool next to mine squeaked. On the Ouija board, the planchette rattled with the bar\'s vibration. <p>\r\nTsing Tao slid the beer off the cardboard and put her fingertips on the little plastic sled. \"Hank and Crane want to say something. You have to hold the other side.\" <p>\r\n\"It\'s the truck, Sing. It\'s shaking the bar.\" <p>\r\n\"I\'m serious.\" She widened her eyes in impatience. \"Come on, do it.\" <p>\r\nI touched it lightly. The planchette jerked from side to side. Something was pulling it away from me. \"You moved it!\" <p>\r\n\"I didn\'t at all.\" She shook her head and grinned. \"I wish you could see this. They\'re trying to do their Three Stooges thing. Crane just can\'t get used to discorporeality.\" <p>\r\nOur hands began to trace lazy orbits around the board, the planchette slowing as it approached me and speeding up on its way to Tsing Tao. It drifted down to the blank lower right corner of the board, then shifted decisively to the left, the clear plastic window centered on the answer. <p>\r\n\"Who wants to go first?\" <p>\r\nPlastic scraped lightly across the board. I could see that Tsing Tao was barely touching the plastic. The planchettte stopped on \"Crane.\" <p>\r\n\"And who do you have a message for?\" <p>\r\nBack to the other side. \"Hank\" scrawled in childish letters and enclosed in a sloppy circle. <p>\r\nTsing Tao rolled her eyes and grinned at me. \"And what sweet nothings do you want to whisper to Hank?\" <p>\r\nThe planchette jumped to the right. \"Shut up, little man.\" <p>\r\nHank seized control and we followed him in a divebomb across the board. \"His Highness, the Queen Mary.\" <p>\r\n \"Boys, boys,\" She spoke with such focus and conviction that I forgot what we were doing and turned to see who she was talking to. \"Play nice now.\" <p>\r\n\"Fudgepacker.\" <p>\r\n\"Asslicker.\" I cocked my ear. I was sure I heard that one, a querelous voice shouting in another room. <p>\r\nFrom the street came deafening blasts as the truck driver lost all patience and hammered his air horn, demanding the removal of the car blocking his way, and in the thunderous squalling the violence of the argument seemed to increase. Nestled in the condiments tray between the lemon slices and cherries, little red plastic swords began to shiver. Backlit liquor bottles rattled and clinked in their cabinets below the counter. <p> \r\nThe planchette moved again. \"Stuff a sock in your ass, it stinks.\" Someone had to use a fine point pen to make it fit. <p>\r\n\"Degenerate. Queer.\" <p>\r\n\"This isn\'t a tea party, little girl.\" <p>\r\n\"Suck dicks in hell, faggot ass!\" <p>\r\nThe pint glass shot off the bar and smashed into the mirror, leaving a neat black truncated pyramid surrounded by a cobwebbed crater. Inside the bar it was very quiet. Silvered glass fell to the floor in a tinkling rain. <p>\r\n\"Goddamnit!\" Tsing Tao swept our empty bottles and glasses into the sink before Hank and Crane could use them as artillery. She put a hand to her cheek and surveyed the damage. \"Oh, Mickey Big Mouth is going to be so pissed. He\'s going to call the exorcist for sure. This is so bad.\" She began moving bottles and picking up loose shards of mirror from the shelves. I grabbed the dustpan and walked around the bar. <p>\r\n\"Let me get it.\" <p> \r\n\"No, no, I\'ve got it, goddammit. Thanks though.\" <p>\r\nI ignored her. Down on my haunches, I scraped years of bad luck into the dustpan of history. The thick soles of Tsing Tao\'s black motorcycle boots were in the periphery of my vision, and above them, her dancer\'s muscled legs, bare skin glowing in the black light of the ID checker under the bar. The vineyard on her thigh pointed the way to the Garden of Paradise, but I didn\'t think I would ever get another opportunity to enter, no matter how long I waited. <p>\r\n\"Are you trying to look up my skirt?\" She laughed and squatted next to me. \"Let me do that.\" <p>\r\n\"I\'ve got it, really.\" <p>\r\nHer knee rested against mine. I was close enough to taste her strawberry breath. \"You don\'t have to do that.\" <p>\r\n\"I want to.\" <p>\r\n\"Such a gentleman.\" Her hand steadied itself on my shoulder as she stood, and I had to look away to avoid committing the crime of which I\'d been accused. The heat of her legs dampened my hairline, or maybe it was my own burning blush. \"Will you watch the bar for me for a second?\" <p>\r\nI walked to the trashcan and dumped the wreckage. \"Sure.\" <p>\r\n	The old men nursing beers at the end of the bar murmured to her as she passed and laughed uproariously at her reply. Tsing Tao policed up a few errant bottles from a booth by the emergency exit, dead soldiers she tossed into a mass grave without ceremony or honor. Then she disappeared behind a door painted with a she-devil brandishing a martini glass. <p>\r\n The bells above the black door clanged. The roar of the street doubled in volume and then subsided as a very tall man with dreadlocks and a long black leather coat stepped into the bar. He saw me behind the bar and frowned. <p>\r\n\"I thought Tsing Tao was working today.\" <p>\r\n\"She is. She\'ll be out in a second.\" I liked playing bartender. I knew Tsing Tao wouldn\'t mind. \"Do you want a beer?\" <p>\r\n\"Sure.\" He stepped to the bar and asked for my brand of beer. I opened one for both of us. <p>\r\nHe took a long thirsty swallow and wiped his mouth with the back of his hand. His fingers were spattered with bright drops of dry paint. It was caked under his nails and in the folds of his knuckles. His dark hair contrasted dramatically with eyes like chips of polar ice. \"So, are you the new bartender?\" <p>\r\nI shook my head and took a swig. \"No, I\'m-\"<p>\r\nA crash of whitewater rapids and the petulant whine of pipes working long past retirement announced Tsing Tao as she came out of the bathroom, wiping her hands with a paper towel and singing a punk rock anthem written the year she was born. She stopped to brush the dandruff from the pensioners\' shoulders and whispered something that left them laughing so hard their continence was in danger. They didn\'t notice when she glanced down the bar and stopped laughing with them. But I did. <p>\r\nI watched as her easy smile died in gentle phases and was reborn as something new. It lay buried for an instant in the flat ground of an uncertain lower lip, and then emerged in the tentative, fragile curl of a corner of her mouth. It grew quickly, warmed and nourished by the light of an invisible sun, and came to flower in the grin I remembered from the night on the fire escape and from the banks of the Russian River and from a booth at the Makeout Room. It was the smile I saw in my dreams, the smile she wore when she loved me. <p>\r\nBut it didn\'t stop growing. Having exhausted the dimensions of this world, her smile infolded into a fourth dimension where contentment and ardent desire occupied by the same place, an emotional tautology that could only be described by an asbtracted calculus of love, and even then incompletely. It was an expression I had never seen on her face. It was the same as the one I wore in the photo behind the bar. <p>\r\nThe tall man took a step towards her and she ran to close the distance. He gathered her in his arms, the top of her head nestled under his chin; she pressed herself hard against him, like she wanted her bones to support his flesh and her heart to pump his blood. Then she tilted her head up, murmuring something I didn\'t want to hear, and took his face in her hands to gave him a kiss that seemed to last until the end of the world. Something wild and fluttering lodged in my throat, desperately tried to find its way out. I couldn\'t look away. It was all I could do to keep from falling over. <p>\r\nThey realized I was staring at them. Tsing Tao took a step back but left a hand laying on his stomach. <p>\r\n\"Marshall, this is Hondo,\" she said. \"Hondo, this is my friend Marshall. We... went to college together.\" <p>\r\n\"Nice to meet you,\" he said. He extended his hand and I shook it, faint with horror. I felt my drinks coming up. I pulled pulled a twenty out of my wallet and laid it on the counter. The pensioners said something I ignored as I came around the end of the bar. <p>\r\nTsing Tao and Hondo watched me in consternation as I walked past them and gathered my things. She laid her hand on my arm. \"Marshall, are you ok?\" <p>\r\nI nodded without looking at her and and pushed my way through the black door and out into the dead white fingers of fog that reach over the hill to clutch at lower Haight St. I sped into a jog as I turned the corner. At the peeling red paint of the curb I doubled over and retched until nothing came out and yellow snot hung in long trails from my nose. Years of bile puddled in the gutter but I didn\'t feel any better. My ghosts didn\'t stay in the Midtown. They followed me wherever I went. <p>\r\n\r\n'),(3127,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã \r\Z\Z $.\' \",#(7),01444\'9=82<.342Ã¿Ã›Ã¿Ã„\Z%&\'()*456789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¡Ã¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ±Ã²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ã„Ã¿Ã„$4Ã¡%Ã±\Z&\'()*56789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzâ€šÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ãšâ€”Ao+Â¼â‚¬}Ã‘â€¢Â»Â­ÃŒÂ©?Ã¤â€˜Ã¢yâ€˜â€Y82ÃÂºâ€˜Q(â€”N}Ã’Ã…UÃƒzÃ½:Å¡â€“Ã®Ã’Ã˜^Ã‰5â€šJâ€°?aY>npA]ÃœgÅ¡Å¾ÃKÂ¾Ã–\'dÅ¾k{vDf9Ã˜>UÃˆ\\Ã»Ã´ÂºUÂ©Â¤Â·Ãâ€¡-Jâ€”â€”0Ã6Ã•Â·e?XÃ¹iÅ“p,ÃˆPÂ¦Ã¥â€ â€ Ã…Q?Oâ€ºÃ‹RXâ€¢,JÃ²N3?â€”Â¦{{Ã•MvÃ†ÃšÃ–s%Â£?Â¸Ã‹FOÂ²#VËœÂ¦dwÃ½\r=+Aj+rKBÃ´Ã°Ã…e<Ã¨Ã·NÂ¥cÃ½Ã›Ã…9 Ã°rF=Ã±Å¾JÅ Ã‚Ã¼Ã™?%ÃšÂ³Â¦?s*0]Å’?ÃˆÃ«Ã©Q\\ÃšÃ¡Ã‚]ÃˆÃ…Â°Â±\"â€¢ÃÂ©Ã¾Ã¤â