¤Ã²3Ã“Â¡Â¬Â´Â¶â€š\rÃ’5Ã„Â â€™rÂ«â€¢.>Â¿Ã£DÂ¹g+â€¢ebÃŒÃ–Ã=Â¼r!_Â´@Ã»Â¾QÃŽÃ½Â â€˜Ã®ÃžÂ°Ã¸mÃ•Ã¨Â¹â€¹Ã½\"F*Å <Ã˜Ã•O9Ã¿(Ã£?â€BÂ¥Â¸Ã·Ã¤Ã´Â¬u?Å½~@?Å“Ã¾Â½6nÃ˜ÃºÃ’Â¬]ÃºÃ¾5V%Â²<Å¡?Â¤\ZyBA#Å“{Ã”t]Ã§Ã’ËœÃ¤â€˜ÃˆÂ¥Ã…#tÂ Â«H$iÃ¼qâ€“Ã‡Ã¦kbâ€¢Ã§Ã°Â¬]\'Ã¾A0zÃ¼ÃƒÃ°#ZÂ°;3c/jÃ¢Å¡Ã•?pz!Ã¤Ã­8ÃœIÃ®MÂºâ€ Ã¥Ã€Ã¼hÂ¥bÂ®Å’â€°mÃ…Ã“IWNÃ…Pâ€”Å¡Câ‚¬p>ÃªÃ£Â­wÅ¡$QÃ›Ã©p$H\n)Ã†;â€˜Å¡ÃƒÅ Ã’Ã–{_Â³Ã‹Ã²Â»*Å’cÃ©Å½â€¢Â¿mÂ²tÅ’\"â€¡b)Â¡Â¹kfâ€¦.nC~AÅ¸Ã¡Ã´>ÃµI4Ã‹Ã¸ÃŽFÂ¢IÂ Ã–Ëœâ€¢1Ã·â€¦;ÃŒOÃ¯?ÃŽÂ­{ÂªÃ‰Ã°Â¾Â¿xZdÂ§â€”|#â€”$?*ÃƒÃ³ÃˆÂ«SKÃ³Â£Ã™Â¨?â€cpcÅ¸ÃƒÃ¿[Ãœ<jA!#9Ã†zaÃ¹TiÃ·Å“ÃŽwÃŽ:â€œÃŠ$Ã“H8Â§o\"-KÂ£.gÃ‰bc,{Â²QS\rQÂ´Æ’8Ãšâ€¡rÂ¨?G=:Ã£8Ã§Ã–Â«m8ÂªÃ·Å¸,Ãœ?ME_TEXSqmno_Ã™Ã‡Ã¢â€ºÂ¨Ã©jÂ±ÃžÃ†ÃŒ?â‚¬fmÂ§#Ã¸88Ã‡cÃ­ÃŒ?vÃ¶Ã†Wâ€šÃ¡%â€¦Å 28ÃVzÃ•&?ÂªÃœÃ¨ÂºÅ“7Ã–Å’Dâ€˜Â°;s?Ãƒ9Ã‡Ã¿NÃ¢kâ€ºpÂ¹9pbÂ¤6Â¶Â«Ã¾Â¶Ã¬!â€žDÃ‘EVÂ¯Â©qÂ¤Â·Â¹?5@ÃÃ»{Ã¢MqÃ¡Ã·Ã¹gÂ¼Â»#?ÃÃŽÅ )Ã²_Â©2Ã‘Xâ€™%Ã°TctÆ’Xâ€?ÃPOÃ¥Ã‡Ã«[:/Å’Â´\\I6â€¹Â§Ãª'),(3128,''),(3129,'<p>it was kind, tonight. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>now i hear the birds saying goodmorning to the sun which isn\\\'t there yet, but it will be. i know this because the sky is changing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there were people here all night.\r\nuntil the morning.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i was scared, but then it happened and it was good. i am happy. yes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i look at my pictures of tipi\\\'s and think about mr booth whom i met on the train platform this afternon. about how he was full of understanding and willingness, and how he told me his secrets about the stars. also i think about cats. and about cleaning the house when i wake up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>all these wonderful beasts are around me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i love them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>they kiss me and hug me and let me be me. they understand when i must take photos and dance awkwardly. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>231 photographs were taken tonight.\r\nsome of them were just light. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>today, when we were in her car, i asked her if she was afraid of dying. i felt compelled to listen to her. she has much beauty within her. i wish i could make her see it more. but she is strong and brave and beautiful, so there is nothing to be fearful of.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>then she took me to the warehouse, where we sifted through pieces of japanese fabric and old kimonos until she found the right one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she said she should take me with her. yes, i say.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i trust her, innately.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she left her mask in my room. \r\nshe is shiny and i once had a doll with her name. her hair is blonde.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she left her mask in my room.\r\nit is a fox mask. she likes foxes; she told me of a painting she used to have, a fox dancing with a person in the snow. </p>\r\n\r\n<P>she left her mask in my room.\r\nit is made of felt. black and white and grey. i will wear it tomorrow. maybe all of the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>giddy. my belly and my heart tangle together, confused about the truth. what is real? i am too scared to want to find out. but i want to all to happen. the right thing. unless i choose the wrong thing, and that will only be good if the lesson leads me to the next thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>12 hours ago, i took the dog for a walk.\r\nthat was the end of something, and the start of the beginning. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>yes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>thankyou for tonight. \r\nevery one of you. </p>\r\n'),(3130,'\r\n<p>Last week I got a card from my mother, asking for my email address, she apparently â€œhas emailâ€ now and wants to connect with me electronically. I havenâ€™t heard from her in almost three years. The card is still on my kitchen table, and I canâ€™t quite bring myself to throw it out, although I also seriously doubt Iâ€™m going to write her back. Sometimes I wonder why people even bother doing these things, why they insist on making an effort at something that is so hesitant and awkward, why people canâ€™t just give up and admit that maybe they just shouldnâ€™t know each other. Anyway, this is what I was thinking about on my way to work this morning.</p> \r\n<p>And it is probably what I was thinking the moment I hit the dog. Iâ€™m guessing it ran out just as I was coming by, because I didnâ€™t see a thing, just heard it and saw it in the mirror. So I get out of the truck and go to see if thereâ€™s anything I can do, I guess. To be honest, Iâ€™m not even really sure why I got out of the truck, because Iâ€™ve never run over a dog before. Are you supposed to look for tags or something? Or call the police? Do people call police for hitting dogs?</p>\r\n<p>Anyway, the dog is still alive, but it doesnâ€™t look very good. Itâ€™s not even making much noise. I donâ€™t know what breed it is. It has long hair, white with some brown spots. Itâ€™s not very big. No collar or tags. There are no houses nearby, either, in fact Iâ€™m not even really sure what town this was in. Itâ€™s a back road that goes by an old train yard and a big overgrown parking lot and thereâ€™s not much else.</p>\r\n<p>I stand there for a while looking at the dog. I donâ€™t think it notices Iâ€™m there. Iâ€™m not sure though. Itâ€™s not bleeding very bad or anything, but its back half is really flattened and twisted in an odd way, itâ€™s not even trying to walk, and Iâ€™m pretty sure the dog is going to die. I donâ€™t know much about animals, but just looking at the thing, itâ€™s so bad that you just know itâ€™s not coming back.</p>\r\n<p>Iâ€™m feeling weird at this point so I get back in my truck. And I feel bad. I mean, I donâ€™t know how to explain it, itâ€™s not like I feel bad that it will die. To be honest, Iâ€™ve never felt very close to animals, and it was an accident. What was getting to me was the fact that this dog couldnâ€™t simply die, it couldnâ€™t simply be hit by a truck and die. I mean, ideally the dog would just live, have a home, chew on bones, etc, and it wouldnâ€™t even be an issue, but what was really bothering me was that if it gets hit by a truck, and has to die, that should be it. Dead. But instead it was tangled and panting a few yards behind me. I just donâ€™t see why that is necessary, and I really didnâ€™t know what to do about it at the time.</p>\r\n<p>So I threw the truck in reverse.</p>\r\n<p>I went back over the dog again, then a third time on my way out. Iâ€™m sure I got it both times and thereâ€™s no way it was still alive. Iâ€™ve never run over a dog before, and Iâ€™m not sure what the right thing to do in that situation is, but Iâ€™m fairly certain â€“ at the very least â€“ I didnâ€™t make it any worse. At best I cut its losses.</p>\r\n<p>And sometimes thatâ€™s simply the best we can hope for.</p> \r\n'),(3131,'<p>sometimes it seems that we are destined to repeat ourselves again and again.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe went to the Blue Martini to see the jazz jam session that they have every friday night, myself and matt. columbia isn\\\'t such a bad city, if you know where to go. knowing where not to go is important too. after a few more drinks, some good cheap cabernet, we called it a night, and i went somewhere that i shouldn\\\'t have gone, that ordinarily i wouldn\\\'t have gone. i left the Blue for Opening Break, the pool hall on assembly street, the pool hall\\\'s pool hall, with all the requisite neon lights. i was there for a couple of reasons, some i will admit and some i won\\\'t.</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni blew in, all cool and overdressed for sure, like leaving the hot tub and jumping in the pool, ordered beer, and there she was. i sat down with her friend, my friend, a friend by proxy who i wouldn\\\'t go to see only, to see in particular. her friend. should there be a paragraph about her? should i talk about the way she speaks, or looks, or her age? <i>res ipso loquitor.</i></p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe leaves, she\\\'s back, she didn\\\'t find what either of us were looking for, she\\\'s surprised to see me on her territory, she is drunk. she asks me again why i\\\'m there, suspicious, and i fall back on stock answers, you know. some dude, a young man, 18 or 19 or 20, has attached himself to her. he is fixated, he has been captured, no metaphor is necessary. he challenges her to come outside, he banters, he says she said she would beat his ass, and he\\\'s ready when she wants to come outside, come on, kat, you said you would beat my ass, lets go. come on. let\\\'s go outside. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nres ipso loquitor. the thing speaks for itself.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsuddenly, as i sit at the bar a stool away, she is up, she is unconciously standing next to my chair, she in her dim awareness has sensed a disturbance in the young man, she goes from distracted annoyance to the fuck-you stage in a moment shorter than a breath. she runs out. he follows, and there i am, sitting, 10 seconds, 20, and i get up and go. he really seems disturbed, though puppylike and harmless. around the building, in the direction of her dorm, under dark and frightening railroad track bridges, and she is out of sight. i see his blue sweater flash in the darkness, as he crosses from one wrong side of the tracks to the other, and i feel adrenaline, i get in the car. down the street. they are on a side street along the way to her room, he is grabbing her, she is pushing him, he is crying about his dead friend, dead of suicide. he claims he won\\\'t let it happen to her. they have just met that evening. she is crying and i pull up, i say come on, get in, it\\\'s cold. it\\\'s late. she looks at me with the first clarity of vision i have seen in her eyes all night, she looks me dead in the eyes and says, i\\\'m fine. it\\\'s cool. i\\\'ll see you later. behind a gas station, 2am, stranger in hysterics, but everything\\\'s cool. i felt an old, cold feeling in the center of my being; it has been years since i have felt it, but it has lost none of its power. i drive around the block, contemplate returning, and finally i have to.</P>\r\n<p>\r\nthere they are, in the same place, and from a block away through my open window i can hear her crying and yelling. leave me alone. why won\\\'t you leave me alone. what is wrong with you. i park and get out, and go all <i>man</i> on him, pushing him away, speaking comfortingly, hugging him, and when he fails to get the message becoming a little more aggressive. he says some incoherant last words and turns around for the bar. i have saved the day. she is crying and shaking. i try to give her my jacket, i try to hug her, i try to fix her. she looks me in the eye, and says...</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\\\"fuck you both.\\\"</i></p>'),(3132,'<p>He takes out his wallet when the waiter comes by with the check and hands it off without looking at the bill. The French fry in the waiters vest is now hanging limply to the right. It has left a grease stain to show where it\\\'s been. She tries to protest his paying of the bill and reaches for her bag. He grabs her hand again and prevents her from retrieving her purse from among her things. \\\"I\\\'ve got it.\\\" He says, \\\"I\\\'ll let you pay next time if you want.\\\" \\\"O.K.\\\" She blushes, \\\"Thanks... Again.\\\" \\\"You\\\'re welcome again.\\\" He says.</p><p>\r\n\r\nWhen they leave the restaurant it\\\'s started to drizzle. The world has faded into a dull outline of itself behind a thickening fog. She stops at the street corner to get her bearings and looks up the hill. \\\"I\\\'m not sure if I can find my way in this fog..\\\" Her voice trails off. She laughs nervously. He smiles and sticks his hand out into the street. The headlights of a car break through the fog and stop next to them at the curb. It\\\'s a taxi. \\\"Come on!\\\" He says, \\\"My treat! I\\\'ll make sure you get home.\\\" She smiles, climbs into the cab and scoots over to the far seat. He gets in behind her and sits down hiding a duct tape vinyl repair job. </p><p>\r\n\r\nThey ride in silence for a few minutes before she realizes she needs to give the driver her address. She looks out the window. The shadows of the houses dance for her in the streetlights. \\\"Gloria.\\\" He says softly and puts a hand on her knee. \\\"Gloria... Can I see you again? I want to see you again.\\\" She turns her eyes to her lap and stares at his fingers. The nails are clean, his pinky looks crooked. She wonders how he broke it and when. \\\"Yes.\\\" She says almost inaudibly, the words stopping inside her mouth. She clears her throat and turns to look at him. \\\"Yes.\\\" She says louder.</p><p>\r\n\r\nBy the time they\\\'ve exchanged phone numbers the cab has pulled up to her building. She looks at him and smiles.  \\\"Tim.\\\" She says. She bites her lip, \\\"I had a great time. And I really will pay next time.\\\" \\\"O.K.\\\" He grins. He stops smiling, reaches up, and cups her cheek in his palm. Without taking his eyes off of hers, he says to the driver, \\\"Wait here a minute.\\\" He leans across her reaching for the door handle with his other arm. Their faces almost touch as he deepens his lean to release the door latch. Her face reddens as she breathes him in. She slides out the open door and he follows her.</p>\r\n'),(3133,'<!--\r\n<p>In my eyes this dream was as vivid as real life, it felt long and protracted and measured something that was really going on inside on another level of reality or at least my overworked subconscious mind.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were two angelic creatures that lived under a big-top circus. It was a large circus tent, with music swirling uneasily out of pipe organs and the short intense drum solos beating away to the rhythms of the tightrope walkers and the trapeze artists.  These two characters of the dream could be considered angels in that they both have wings to make them fly and they are invisible to the crowds at the circus, yet they provided an integral role for all the performers. The two angels were both committed to their solitary actions because it is through those actions that they remained vital. A female angel and a male angel, both somewhat conscious of each otherâ€™s presence, worked dutifully at their given roles to protect the mortals down below on the circus grounds and high above on the highwire and trapeze.</p>\r\n-->\r\n<p>The male angel flew through the sky and around near the ground to ceremoniously perform his acts of charity. His long cloak fluttered as he moved about the crowds who only felt his presence as a gentle but cool breeze that passed over their faces in the tense excitement of the great performances. It was not his act of charity that he himself willed, but rather some other force that acted upon him to perform it. He glided wordlessly in the air with his hands held out, to give a gentle push to the performers that need pushing. Maybe there would have been a misstep, or a cannon pointed in the wrong direction: he was the intermediary between charity and mortality, whose function and vitality acted out of fate completely.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The female angel acted in a similar role to that of the male. She is most-often remembered by her soft almost purely translucent wings. She felt the male angel most strongly each performance and almost reached out for his long cloak as he passed performing his action. Her act was of the same quality of the male angel, accept hers was not a push, but a catch to gather up mortality in its last moment, to extend its life after lapses of concentration.  She too, was an intermediary, she was the axis between compassion and mortal soul. If she gathered the fallen up in her strong arms she would be granting them new life. Not a life perfectly the same, because sometimes her clutch came late, and the mortal flesh would have to suffer its fate in all the pain and gore of the circus. She suffered silently each time the flesh began to bleed. She was not cognizant of the fact that she acted out fate, like the male angel, she considered herself having freewill. Maybe her imperfection is what enamoured her to the crowds of mortals who clapped loudly when the performers survived the fall. They all prayed to the angel singing hosannas each soul she saved, despite the broken limbs and crimson pools that marked her wake. The male angel received no prayers of thanks or polite applause.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Each night and each day these performances went on, with new performers replacing the last days performers. The angels did their work, fulfilling their roles as intermediaries in amongst the music and colours and lights of the circus. If one of the crowd were to look a certain way, out of the corner of their eyes they might see the angels busy at work, but there is so much bustle in all the action that the quick subtle movements of the angels went unnoticed until the fanfare subsided and the music held its breath.</P> \r\n\r\n<p>The male angel was conscious of the female angel. Conscious of her motions, even though he couldnâ€™t see her directly. He imagined her as the trapeze artist, high above him swaying back and forth on the thin swing. His role was to go up sometimes to give that slight nudge to the performers, and when he went up he would really drink in their charm and understand their fear of mortality. Their nervous bellies glistened with sweat, the tremors of their lips in the great moment when they must make their transition and stare at the possibility of death.  And sometimes the female would catch her performers in mid prayer to her, which made her blush with some vanity, at other times the performer had committed themselves to the act of death and she helplessly watched them slip through her fingers. She never understood why she couldnâ€™t make every catch, and because of this, she thought herself flawed. Not like the male angel whom she imagined as a perfect graceful soul like her, acting in the same sense of compassion.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>One day she decided that she was tired of feeling imperfect. She saw the lights and colours at once, the pipe organ slowed to a hush, and then the drums beat loudly. She plugged her ears because see had never heard the noise in all the time she had been at the circus. There were small children everywhere in various costumes that dance and swirl about the ground floor. She found herself walking up to the ladder that lead to the trapeze. So many times she had wanted to experience this, and now she saw that her strong pale arms and legs were moving towards it; her red and gold sequence dress dazzled her eyes in the noxious blue spotlightâ€™s ray. Up she went, climbing each wrung and savouring each moment. Her wings were gone, her hair was wavy--the colour of saffron, she now had a fear of heights, a vertigo that imbalances her. She felt an emotion considered to be happiness, for the first time.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>As she sat on the trapeze the male angel went about his daily and nightly routines. He flew up to her initially unconscious of who she was formerly. When he looked into the eyes of this mortal, he found that he is overwhelmed with a feeling he considered to be fear.  In that instant when he moved to push her off the trapeze, he felt that paralysing fear grip him hard, he realized who she was and he found himself in amongst the crowds of mortals watching her fall. Standing in amongst the people he remembered with absolute remorse, all the little pushes heâ€™d given the performers -- pushes that most never got up from. He ran with fast and sturdy legs to the spot where she was about to land. He felt all his muscles tighten and the limitation of his mortal speed. He came to her just at the moment when she was about to hit the ground. He caught her in his arms and was crushed awkwardly under her weight.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>She held his head in her arms softly crying tears for him. He was in his last moments as the tiny rivulets of blood spilled from his lips. She said that she had always wanted to meet him. She knew that he existed as she once did, saving souls from death. However, she wondered why he intervened with her. She said to him that he shouldnâ€™t have risked his life for her. She wanted to be pushed and she wanted to fall from that high trapeze. This was her dream when she had been an angel. To give herself away to flight and simply fall so that she could be witness to an end. With this thought his eyes closed and he breathed his last breath. She wept tears now of joy holding his face tight to her breast and the crowds let out cheers and hosannas for the heroic man who had given his life to save the performer. She got up with the blue spotlight dancing on her pale flesh stained with his blood, and in her bedraggled costume she took one more fateful bow to silence the crowd.</p>  \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3134,'<p>her hand.  her hand clutching at my chest, us laying on the couch in our apartment, crying, her hand, she\\\'d never touched me like that before.  her going silent in a heave of tears, rolling in to me, pulling close with a fistful of my shirt, her face frozen by a wave of sadness, planted into me now, warm, wet, the livingroom walls watch us, silent, bare, i am crying but happy for the moment, i feel wanted needed in control, a momentary contrast.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his eyes.  my stepfather, his eyes, i have never seen eyes like this, he has lost his mind, attacking me, it\\\'s spring, we\\\'re outside in the backyard, he is mad because i wanted to borrow the car again.  his eyes are on fire blue they make my face hot, they are cracked and jagged at the edges, stained with rust.   he is yelling but i am calm i am in control, my heart is racing but i own these situations they are mine to make.  i tell him calmly to hit me, silence, his eyes they amplify and he punches me hard in the face, i am surprised and my jaw hurts and he jumps at me.  he attacks again heavy and awkward, i separate, stand away from him, i continue talking.   his eyes blue and stained with rust, hating me.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>my face, expressionless, projected here and now in front of me, searching for a third event to tie these two together, nothing, nothing, nothing.  my mouth and eyes closed not ever being able to really cry, not in years, a sneeze that never happens.  my mom, her voice matter of factly through the telephone telling me she\\\'s out of money, i cannot help her i want to help her i cannot remember what she looked like before the cancer struck.  there, crying briefly and incompletely i find no relief.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<center><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3134_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\"></center>\r\n<br>\r\n'),(3135,'<p>The entrance to her apartment building is well back from the street. It takes them almost a minute of deliberate heel and toeing, hands locked, heavy with sighs, to reach the steps. He pulls on her arm to swing her around to face him. She takes one step backward onto the first step. Their eyes line up with the increase in height. As they stare they each begin to tip slightly until their foreheads meet. They rest that way together, foreheads touching, without speaking. She feels a great need to swallow, but is afraid to move. She waits instead.</p><p>\r\n\r\nWhen she canâ€™t wait anymore she closes her eyes and swallows hard. She keeps her eyes closed and feels the warmth of his breath on her face each time he exhales. And then it stops. He is holding his breath. She canâ€™t stand the tension, so opens her eyes a crack. His eyes are closed now. Just as she peaks from beneath her lashes, his lips connect with hers.  She freezes, savoring the sensation. Her arm involuntarily sends her hand into his hair. She grabs a fistful.</p><p>\r\n\r\nAs her fingers wind themselves into his curls his lower lip drops enough to take hers between his teeth. She lets her tongue lengthen against the roof of his mouth and laughs thinking of the restaurant and the giant molar at the host station. He moves the hand heâ€™s holding to the small of his back and loops his arms around her rib cage. They pull each other tighter until neither can breath. Several strands of his hair are pulled out in her fist.</p><p>\r\n\r\nThey stand together like this for several minutes. When they stop kissing they stay pressed together cradled in each otherâ€™s arms. She lays her cheek against his shoulder her face against his neck. â€œI should go.â€ He says. She sighs. He pulls back and their hands slide down their arms and meet at the fingers. â€œIâ€™m going to call you tomorrow.â€ He says. She smiles, â€œGood.â€ He plants one last kiss on her check and abruptly turns and runs out to the street. She puts her hand up to her mouth, giggles, and covers her smile as she enters the foyer.</p>\r\n'),(3136,'Hey, does anybody else use livejournal \r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.livejournal.com/users/p3rlm0nk/\\\">like me</a>?  It seems like I use LJ for little random shrapnel-like bits and interacting with <a href=\\\"http://www.livejournal.com/community/austin_freaks\\\">local communities</a>.  If I had more meditative or creative posts, I\\\'d probably put them here.  But I haven\\\'t really been in that space recently.  I got a super-keen job back up in Austin, so the past six weeks or so (wow, it seems way longer) have been mostly moving and settling in... I am so happy to be where I am right now, this city is home to me.  Some people have been tickling my ear about SF, and maybe I\\\'ll flirt with her, but I think Austin will always be home even though I grew up someplace else.'),(3137,'<p>\r\ni just finished reading <a href=http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/tg/detail/-/0553375407/qid=1036571695/sr=8-2/ref=sr_8_2/103-0392778-1605409?v=glance&n=507846>ishmael</a> by daniel quinn. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter reading the book, i have so much that i want to discuss with people, but have no idea of where to start, so perhaps the people of swinney who read this book can help me?  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe first 3/4 of the book frustrated me so much that i almost abandonned reading it altogether, but when i finally made it to the end, i was really quite happy that i finished it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"ishmael\\\" discusses a really unique view of how one might interpret genesis in the bible and the evolution of man.  i\\\'m fascinated by it, but like i said, i have no idea where to start!!!!\r\n</p> \r\n'),(3138,'<p>\r\nI\\\'m very frustrated about how en vogue it is to talk about how you didn\\\'t vote.  It\\\'s mostly so, because I use to be one of those people, and it was mostly my ridiculous excuse for not keeping up to date with current issues.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYes, I understand that it\\\'s a person\\\'s right _not_ to vote, but what I don\\\'t understand is this argument: \\\"I didn\\\'t vote because of what happened in the last presidential election.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>The 2000 election wasn\\\'t pretty, I\\\'ll grant you that, and it also showed us just how broken our system can be, but today\\\'s election wasn\\\'t about how we\\\'re going to vote on a national level, it was about how we\\\'re going to vote locally.  In my opinion, these are issues that are important to vote on, because they directly affect you and yours!  Your vote actually does count!  I know it\\\'s hard to vote sometimes, because you look at what\\\'s presented before you and you don\\\'t want to vote on either because you don\\\'t like how it is now, and you don\\\'t like the changes they want to make, but think about how those changes were proposed from the beginning.  If you feel helpless in their hands, it\\\'s time to get out there and be a part of what you believe in.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis election really sparked my curiousity because of my interest in San Francisco\\\'s night life,the use of medicinal marijuana, and various programs for our ever increasing homeless epidemic.  Which are all issues that I care about deeply.  For the first time in my LIFE, I was excited about politics.  \r\n<p>\r\nMore important than voting I think, is actually getting involved with your local city government and seeing what\\\'s being planned in your community.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRecently, I worked at a non-profit in Oakland, and we were faced with the serious issue of the city of Oakland taking our building away from us.  I attended over a dozen city council meetings and became accutely aware of how issues in the city are dealt with.  It\\\'s fascinating.  Even if you don\\\'t want to get involved, it\\\'s really neato to see how it works!\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3139,'<p>\r\nSo where I live, there is no artificial light at night time.  It\\\'s so lovely, the milky way and the stars are all visable.. and when there\\\'s a moon out, it lights the whole valley up (and also charges my solar power!).  On nights where there is no moon, I need a torch to get around, because it\\\'s so dark you can\\\'t see your hand infront of your face.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUsually I keep my torch in my car, but the other night I went to reach for it upon arriving home, and realised I\\\'d left it in the house.  It\\\'s a five minute walk downhill and stairs to get to my house.  Needless to say I was pretty pissed with myself.  I got out of the car, flung my bag over my shoulder and started the downhill trek armed with only a lighter.  I\\\'ve forgotten my torch before and usually the flick of a lighter is enough light to let me see where I\\\'m going.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this particular night it was stormy and windy, so I was left standing at the top of my stairs flicking my lighter hopelessly.  I decided it would be okay to feel my way down with my feet.  It wasn\\\'t okay.  I made it to the straight landing and thought - so this is what it\\\'s like to be blind?  I didn\\\'t realise, but what I thought was the landing was actually the first step, and my right foot was dangerously halfway over the edge.  I went to feel for the step with my left foot, and the right foot gave way and I started to fall.  For a split second, images from my fall down a flight of fifty stairs came flooding back (that\\\'s another story), and in my panic I blindly reached out for something.  Anything.  Luckily I caught a tree and was able to steady myself.  All I could hear was my heart beating.  If I had fallen, I would have tumbled down about six ponga stairs and smacked into the closed wooden gate at the bottom.  Ouch!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\\\'s been some other hiccups in paradise.  Well, it\\\'s not paradise that\\\'s the problem, it\\\'s me.  I digress, I think I\\\'m trying to put off admitting to this mistake in writing so I\\\'ll just get right down to it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt home my range, fridge and shower all run off gas bottles.  I read all the instructions for the shower and it says the shower won\\\'t go really hot.. so for the first couple of weeks I was there I took lukewarm showers.. but with no problem.  I was all into being nature girl.  One night it was cold outside, so I turned the shower right up to HOT and expected a nice flow of warmer water.  Instead, it came out freezing bloody cold.  So I thought the gas bottle had run out, and so started taking showers at Sarah\\\'s house which is on the way to work.  Why didn\\\'t I re-fill the gas bottle?  Because I am the worlds worst procrastinator.  Something about lugging a huge bottle up to the top of the hill does not appeal to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnyway, I\\\'ve been trying to sort out an AC power inverter so I can run my stereo down there.  I called the last girl who lived there to ask her advice on that, and a few other things.  The shower topic came up, I told her my little story and she laughed and said \\\"Has no one told you?  Hot is cold, and cold is hot!\\\".  Oh god.  I felt like a complete dick, and couldn\\\'t believe that I hadn\\\'t clicked to that earlier.  I went straight home and tested it.  Voila, hot water!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis morning I took a shower.  I got up early so I could have an extra long one and enjoy it that much more.  I stood there for about five minutes, eyes closed, thankful for this small piece of luxury in my simple house.  Suddenly, the shower ran freezing cold.  Guess what?  My gass bottle has run out.\r\n</p>'),(3140,'<p>i discovered a simple pleasure in life today! my regular go-too-fast-skreetch-around-corners bus driver was replaced this morning by a new young lady, who not only greeted me, she waited for me to sit down before putting the bus back into motion.  i found myself completely shocked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s amazing what we just accept in life as \\\"adequate\\\".  i never realized that having a bus wait for you sit down would make such a difference in your day.  when she dumped us off at the train station, she wished us all a good day before the doors opened.  i told her, \\\"you are the best bus driver ever, do we get to keep you?!\\\"  unfortunately, we don\\\'t, because muni places bus drivers on different routes all of the time.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe little things do count.\r\n</p>'),(3143,'<P>\r\nin north america 11/11 isnt that big a deal, but over here in the windy and somewhat cold streets of seoul korea its PEPERO DAY!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits this day of the year totally invisioned by a company. LOTTE is a huge food and beverage company overe here...sort of like nestle. They make a lot of really good drinks i like to have almost everyday. But something else they make has all the kids going nutzo. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npepero is a cookie stick dipped in chocolate. there are a few different types of pepero but they all use the same concept. What differs in the pepero design is the length, width, and flavour of the pepero stick. The classic pepero design is a plain cookie stick dipped in plain chocolate, but there are peperos with strawberry chocolate, nutty chocolate, and ummmm i think thats it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npepero goes on sale just before the eleventh of the eleventh month, but on the day of the eleventh pepero sales go up im sure ten fold. Its sort of like \"pancake day\" in north america. On pancake day, all the kids want pancakes. Ya know? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso today ive eaten about three boxes of pepero minis, and about nine or ten of the massive twelve inch peperos. Im peperoed out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin my seven o\'clock class, one of my students gave me a big box filled with chocolates and pepero sticks. What really caught my eye was that some of the pepero packages were of beers. Cafri pepero, Heinekin, and \"budieiser\"(haha). Other packages featured \"pucca\" the \"funny love\" characters, and holeman; a japanese cartoon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nalso in the box were mini mock packs of ramien with chocolate inside instead of noodles. Mini cans of pocari sweat, caffe latte, coca cola, and korean milks; all of which were filled with crappy korean chocolate. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nit was a really nice gift from one of my students, and i was caught right off guard as i didnt get jack squat on \'teachers day\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\non pepero day, this is chiamattt signing out. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3144,'<p>\\\"come in\\\" said the doormat to the flea, but the flea was too small to see the relationship between the doormat and the door, and so he replied, \\\"no thank you, mr. mat, I prefer warmer pelts.\\\"   \\\"Whatever are you talking about?\\\" asked the doormat to the flea.  \\\"Why, Iâ€™d just rather not get cozy inside your coarse, cold fur, is all.  Itâ€™s nothing personal, just my way, you see.\\\"  \\\"Well, little flea, I do not see, but do as you like â€“ \\\'personalâ€™ or not Iâ€™d rather you get on your way.\\\"  \\\"Very well,\\\" huffed the flea, \\\"and to think he just invited me inside and now wants to send me away so soon!\\\" thought the flea to himself.  \\\"Some fine host you are!\\\" gruffed the flea as he pushed a little harder than usual with his two hind hoppers and soared high into space and through the open door.</p>\r\n<p>Now, time travels much slower for such a small little flea (as we have here) than it does for us.  What seems like a nanosecond of a jump to us lasts much, much longer for this little flea.  I donâ€™t know whether this little flea has a peculiarly slow perception of time or whether all little fleas experience it as he, but one thing is certain â€“ this little fleaâ€™s jumps are journeys to themselves.  Of course, not all of this little fleaâ€™s journeys are worth detailing, and with that digression I continue...</p>\r\n<p>So, where was I? â€“ oh yes, the little flea is soaring into space through an open door.  I forgot to mention, the door is open because it is one of those nice dusty, musky warm fall evenings when the sun strews soft yellow rays that pierce the veil that separates the unknown.  Upon takeoff, the little flea smelled toasted marshmallows with a high note of what he thought was persimmons wafting in and out.  His journeys often started this way, with a smell â€“ the product of that capricious sense who prides itself as the high priestessâ€™s own capturer of memories, creator of intuitions, and foreteller of whatâ€™s to come.  Little fleaâ€™s sense of smell had quite a line of blue blood running through itâ€™s veins, no doubt, and it once told the little flea that it operated in the princessâ€™ nose before her bout with the fever, but the little flea knew better to trust the words of a jealous sense.</p>\r\n<p>Little flea rose higher and the smell faded.  He saw the doormat growing smaller beneath him and watched the particles of ether pass by.  Sometimes the little fleaâ€™s favorite part of his jump was interacting with the particles.  They all had different crystalline shapes that reflected light in a unique manner â€“ or mood.  Sometimes the flea would pause and have a brief discourse with one or another especially interesting looking particle, but on this jump, the little flea was not interested with such trivialities - sometimes they seemed so meaningless.  And today he had much more on his flea brained mind...</p>\r\n<p>The flea landed hard.  He wasnâ€™t paying attention.  He shook his head and shook his feet and mumbled, \\\"bastard child of this damn century,\\\" as he glanced around.  \\\"Hello... hello... is anybody here?\\\"</p>'),(3145,'<p><b>In the nursing home</b>, residents were assessed in order to determine how functional they were; they were then assigned to particular wings of the building based upon their degree of functionality.  Those who lacked sufficient mental capacity to care for themselves lived in the wing of the building that I exited through in the evening.  The odor of ammonia was terribly strong there.  And one elderly woman was always positioned in an old, chrome wheelchair, in middle of the hallway that led to the door out of the building.  She wore a leather mitten on one of her hands; she screamed almost continuously: <i>\\\"Mee-mee-mee-mee!\\\"</i>  And as someone passed near her, she would reach out and try to grab them; she had a grip like a vise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It had been impressed upon me that it was my duty to make a difference in the lives of the nursing home residents.  I was 15 years old at the time, and I was compensated to the tune of $2.33 per hour.  Maybe, nice things waited for me in Heaven as a result of the work that I did; but I grew rather suspicious of the arrangement after watching the administrators of the facility drive home in their Cadillac cars.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There were, without question, a few residents who were cognizant enough to benefit from the time that I spent with them.  I remember, in particular, a group of three older guys: an Irishman, a Czech, and a German.  They all wanted to talk; almost every older person seems to want to talk.  It\\\'s as if they are desperate to pass on the story of their peculiar life, and in so doing find a way to live on after death.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Irishman: Pat, I remember the best; he was the best talker.<br>  \r\n\\\"Are you Irish, Paul?\\\"  Pat would ask.<br>\r\n\\\"Well,\\\" I would reply, \\\"One of my grandmothers did come from Liverpool, and her family was from the Isle of Man.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Oh sure, you\\\'re Irish then,\\\" Pat would counter.  \\\"Just say you\\\'re Irish.\\\"<br>\r\nAnd then I was a part of Pat\\\'s world, and a part of his family too.  And he shared his stories with me.  We had the same conversation more than once; but that was ok.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The consequence of a young, healthy person laughing happily around the elderly is that something like a transfer of energy takes place: older people remember the past and smile, and to a degree it heals them of the passage of time; and the young too change: they become a bit older - vicariously - as the gravity of the presence of the aged has an effect upon the vitality of the young.  Spending time close to people who have suffered a bit in life can be draining.  But, maybe that\\\'s a part of the process of maturing: a certain dampening of enthusiasm for frivolous things, and an ability to confront and embrace the experience of life as a whole: sickness and health, beauty and ugliness, youth and old age.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I found something good in the nursing home, in the form of the stories that I received there.  But I had my own family; and I had my own stories from them.  The residents who I worked with in the nursing home had their own families too.  In some instances, the elderly required too much care for their respective families to provide; but in many more cases, it was simply that no one really had the time, or energy, or desire to allow them to grow old in their own homes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The same sort of thing happened in jail: people who were a problem for one reason or another were all dumped into the same building.  An initial assessment and subsequent classification took place.  And instead of being able to tailor the conditions to each individual, some people got more than they deserved, others got less than they deserved, and no one really got treated like a human being.  The end result was the same as the nursing home: an unhappy place that smelled, and that families didn\\\'t want to visit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was hired for $25,000 annually.  And when I ran a floor I\\\'d watch 50 grown men sit on the toilet, take showers, and relieve themselves of their sexual tension.  I gave them their food; I held them for Thorazine.  I applied restraints; I removed restraints.  I was fair - and I was told so; guys shook my hand when they left.  I listened to people who no one else wanted to listen to; and I heard a lot of good stories.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Sometimes, when I walked my rounds I\\\'d sing.  On a quiet night, I\\\'d sing loudly when I walked through the kitchen.  And at night, in the kitchen, I always sang the same thing: <i>\\\"If you want my body, and you think I\\\'m sexy, c\\\'mon honey let me know.\\\"</i>  I thought that it was very funny to sing that song inside a jail; I amused myself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I learned, later, that as a result of the manner in which the plumbing and ductwork were run, the guys in booking heard me putting on my show.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I sang when I worked for the park district, too.  The guy who made out the district\\\'s maintenance budget counted upon a certain number of gallons of fuel being used each year.  And if enough gasoline wasn\\\'t burned during the course of normal operations, it was used in other ways.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And so, in Chicago, in late November, I was told to drive a tractor around various parks and fields and mow the grass.  There wasn\\\'t any grass to be cut.  And in fact, it was usually raining or snowing - or doing both at the same time.  I must have looked ridiculous: mowing invisible grass in a snowstorm.  But that didn\\\'t matter.  There was nothing to do except sit there on the tractor, exposed to the elements, laughing, and singing: <i>\\\"Well I had my carburetor baby cleaned and checked...\\\"</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\\\"...a damp, drizzly November in my soul,\\\"</i> wrote Melville.  This is the month in which my father would have turned 75, and in which I should turn 35, this year.  It\\\'s during this month that the winter sky falls like a lid on a coffin in the northerly parts of the northern hemisphere.  It\\\'s a gray time: a time of moral ambiguity and indecision.  The idea that there is some Grail out there - some noble cause, some good war, some job worth doing, or some fight worth fighting - seems fanciful.  Charitable acts are a fine thing when you or your parents have money in the bank, and you want to get a bit of the stink of the workingman about yourself.  But I\\\'m selling my car to pay the bills this month.  And the only thing that I\\\'m certain of is that it\\\'s high time to get to sea, again.</p>'),(3146,'<p>It is 4:30 by the time he reaches the bar and lifts his keys into the air again. He inserts the largest of them into the lock of an iron accordion gate and latches the grating into place at one side of the door. He exchanges the key for the next largest and the bolt slides with a hiss and finds its place with a chink of metal. He pushes the door open with his knee and locks it again from the inside. They don\\\'t open until 5.</p><p>\r\n\r\nHe steps behind the bar and flips a switch under the rim of the wood below the coffee maker. When he does, neon lights erupt in the window and outline the bus stop outside in blue and red. He checks to see how much ice is in the bin with his right hand as he lifts the coffee pot to the spigot with his left. He puts in a clean filter before pokes his finger into the button that turns the machine on. The button used to read: BREW, but only one leg of the letter R is left. He saunters over to the booths and lights a candle on each table with a beaten up chrome Zippo he pulls out of his left front pocket. Each candle spits up a tired thread of smoke from within bumpy red party glass.</p><p>\r\n\r\nHe lifts a clean towel from a stack under the counter and begins to rub the bar in circles. He didnâ€™t work last night, and the bartender who did always leaves the remains of wet rings in a line along the edge of the over polished surface. He picks a discarded rubber band out of the path of the towel and tosses it absently into a lime green plastic ashtray that waits next to the cash register.</p><p>\r\n\r\nAt 5 oâ€™clock he unlocks the door to the street. He stops in the doorway to adjust himself with a cup of the hand, swirling his hips almost imperceptibly to one side, and frowns. Heâ€™s forgotten something. And then he remembers. He walks over to the jukebox, leans forward, takes the drooping power cord from the floor, and slides the plug into the wall. It flashes into life and radiates a thin pastel glow that fails to break through the gloom. </p>\r\n'),(3147,'<p>My clothes smell like smoke and my breath like beer.  It\\\'s been a while.</p>\r\n<p>Tonight I saw a super special sneak preview of a new film called Adaptation at the University of Toronto\\\'s Innis Theatre.  Written by Charlie Kaufman, directed by Spike Jonze, starring Nick Cage, and Meryl Streep.  It\\\'s a film about Charlie Kaufman struggling to adapt Susan Orlean\\\'s best-selling non-fiction book The Orchid Thief into a screenplay.  What made this a super special sneak preview?  Well besides it\\\'s cost of $0.00 and the fact that the release date is December 4, Mr.\\\'s Cage, Kaufman, and Jonze were on hand to take part in an \\\"intimate discussion\\\" after the screening.  This consisted of them fielding questions and wanting to hear our reactions to the film.</p>\r\n<p>Of course there were a lot of really bad questions but they told some funny stories and it was a fun event.  Nick had been drinking all day and kept playing with his nose a lot, I don\\\'t know what\\\'s up with that.  Some of my favourite highlights were Spike looking at Charlie after answering a question only to receive a shrugging and silent mouthing of the word \\\"naw\\\" from Charlie and the story about why thanks was given in the credits to \\\"The Man At the Beach.\\\"  The film was great and comes highly recommended when it is actually released.  If you enjoyed Being John Malkovich then you\\\'re probably going to enjoy the Kaufman/Jonez pairing again.</p>\r\n<p>After the screening, Sean, Deb, Brian, a bunch of Brian\\\'s hometown friends (including his two brothers), and myself went to The Green Room for some post-screening food\\\'n\\\'drink.  Good times.  Good people.  Good lawd!</p>\r\n<p>It was a good night to kick back and have some fun, I needed it.  Plans were also made for me to get a haircut on Saturday (the first in 17 months) and go clean shaven (for the first time in three-plus years) in preparation for Family Photo Sunday.  Oh, so, Sean, Brian, and myself were watching some TV a few weeks ago when we saw an advertisement for Sears Family Holiday Portraits for $7.99.  We decided to check it out and made an appointment for this Sunday.  So we\\\'re going to buy really bad/good outfits from Value Village and head up to the Sears at the Yorkdale Mall for our appointment this Sunday.</p>\r\n<p>Sean also mentioned that he found out the volunteers and the floats for the Santa Claus Parade (also this Sunday) line up right in front of our house.  It\\\'s going to be a festive and fun-filled Sunday, that\\\'s for sure.</p>'),(3148,'\\\"No, sorry I donâ€™t remember him.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Yes you do. Remember I introduced you guys at Moeâ€™s? We were playing pool and he and Mehti were in the booth, remember? We were there for a few hours, Lydia. Donâ€™t you remember him?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Was I drunk?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Not when you first met him. You have to remember Roge. Thereâ€™s no way you could forget meeting him. He looks so unique!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Chris, I donâ€™t remember meeting Roge. Itâ€™s nice to meet you now though.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Yeah, same here.\\\" <br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"I canâ€™t believe you donâ€™t remember. I canâ€™t believe she doesnâ€™t remember.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n(Damn Chris. Shut up. Why the fuck did I say I didnâ€™t remember meeting Roge? Move Lydia. Go back to your table.)<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Nice seeing you guys. Iâ€™m working Thursday night, come by the bar if you get a chance.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"I canâ€™t believe how fucked your memory is. Whenâ€™s the last time you smoked crack?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Bye muthafucka. Bye Roge.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n(Damn Chris. I wanted to see Roge so badly again after first meeting him. In that first instance of talking to him I could see clear visions of how we would be. I could see how heâ€™d instinctively hold me if I was in armâ€™s distance. I could hear our future conversations, deep words pulling us deeper into each other. I could feel his chin on my shoulder as he came from behind to kiss my ear. I could see his face, intent with crinkled forehead, as I explained how I felt when I first met him. I could feel my stomach sore as heâ€™d make me laugh and laugh.<br><br>\r\nI canâ€™t believe Roge was in front of me after willing him to appear all week. Come visit me at work. Come visit me at work. Please come visit me at work.\r\nDamn Chris.)\r\n'),(3149,'Thinking about headaches, ...feeling ill and needing to run out of here.  But still sitting and wasting time.  Why?  Because I have it for once in these past two years or so.  Itâ€™s a great luxury to be able to do nothing but let your eyes dry out staring at a computer screen at 5:00 in the evening.  I knew a homeless vet for a few months about two years ago.  Smart guy, lived in the park and panhandled downtown.  He carried an expensive North Face backpack around with him, and explained it was the best deal out there since it had a lifetime warranty.<br><br>  \r\nDuring the time that I knew him he traded his first one in and showed up with an even nicer one.   He told me about how once he and his ex-girlfriend devoted their life to living \\\"like a warrior.\\\"  Living where every moment is a moment of practicing maximum observation and instantaneous but perfectly well thought out reaction.  He studied religion, but only to denounce it â€“ said he was working on writing a religious-philosophical treatise.  He told me a story about the apostles and jesus.  Said the apostles are the people or influences that come to you when you need help making a decision.  You, of course, are your own god and the bible stories about jesus are metaphorically stories about you and your own mind.  Told me to just sit still and quiet and think, and then the apostles will come.  <br><br>\r\nHe said they will all talk and give you ideas and as you go deeper into discourse with them you start to see the path you should take.  I often wondered if he was crazy, but he never seemed out of his mind.  He was one of the most consistently calm and rational people Iâ€™ve met, and kind.  He also told me how I should exercise (donâ€™t be afraid to lift weights! He said) and how I should eat (he always started the day with a big glass of milk.)  I trusted him in those regards because he was, or looked to be, in phenomenal shape.  <br><br>He worked out with UCSF athletic directors (near the park) and used the public track and equipment.  Also told me a little about growing up in Alabama.  Then he just never came back to his usual spot, and I havenâ€™t seen him since those 2 years ago.  He told me he liked living in the park, free and not controlled by anyone or anyone elseâ€™s demands.  I wonder where he went.  I wonder if heâ€™s still working on that treatise and if he still drinks milk every morning when he wakes, but I donâ€™t have enough time to look for him.'),(3150,''),(3176,'<p>\r\nToday I am going to turn down a \\\'dream job\\\'.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo I thought it was my dream job, until I was told I could not take my leave when I wanted it, but instead had to take three mandatory weeks over Christmas each year.  I have never done things this way.  I have a huge problem with Christmas holidays, mainly due to the fact that I am not a Christian, and feel wrong celebrating their holidays.  I don\\\'t celebrate any other religious holidays, and the events that mean something to me spiritually, no one else takes holidays for.  Anyway, I digress.  My normal routine is to take off once or twice a year to somewhere new, somewhere of interest to me, somewhere that will provide me with the life experiences that I crave.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow that this choice, this lifestyle of mine is threatened, I feel like this job is a huge plastic bag that I\\\'m stuck in and about to suffocate in.  It\\\'s really forced me to think about who I really am, and what I want out of this life.  I guess it\\\'s always been obvious, and perhaps I\\\'ve always taken it for granted.  But I\\\'m a gypsy and I have to keep moving!  I want to live in other cities, experience their culture and meet new and interesting and like minded people.  I want to see the magic that Mother Nature creates in other parts of the world.  I want to ride on my ignorance and knowingly be naive, and surrender to anything the world will show me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEver since I was little, I\\\'ve changed my mind constantly on what I want to be, what career to choose, what will make me happy.  Unfortunately, (or fortunately if you\\\'re me) I don\\\'t want a career at all!  I will wash dishes over losing all that time and all that experience.  I am 100% serious.  I know now that this is how Iâ€™m meant to live.. and I have accepted a life of poverty, a life of no material possessions.  They never interested me anyway.  So as soon as I figure out where to next, Iâ€™m selling up and moving on.  Again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo I have to break the news to the family business very soon.  I wonder what they\\\'ll think when I tell them that I\\\'ve decided to abandon all plans of being a designer to pursue a life of being a GYPSY?  Wish me luck! :)\r\n</p>'),(3151,'<P>\r\nOn Sunday I went for a rousing motorcycle romp through the countryside\r\nof northern California with the boys.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen I got home and was parking my bike a hot girl sidled up\r\nand said, \\\"Oooooo, what year is that bike?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"It is a 72\\\", I said.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"How big is it?\\\" she asked.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Seven hundred and fifty CC,\\\" I replied.  I sized her up and down,\r\nundressed her with my eyes, considered if she would weigh down the\r\nback of my bike too much, etc.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Oooo, that is big!\\\" she exclaimed as smoke rose from the two\r\nlarge jugs of the hot German machine.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Do you ride?\\\" I asked, thinking maybe it wouldn\\\'t matter since she\r\ncould have her own bike.\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe shot me an unmistakable glance that meant yes and YES at the same\r\ntime, but she added to the unspoken, \\\"I\\\'m not\r\nsure, why don\\\'t we get a room above Zeitgeist and find out?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nIndeed, this was an excellent proposed end to my sunday ride.  We\r\nwalked the 2 blocks to the Zeitgeist, got a room under the name Harley\r\nHam.  The room resembled a special bike garage with oil of love thinly\r\ncoated over everything.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOver the course of sex, we did the Motorcycle, the\r\nScreaming Eagle, rebuilt her back end, and changed her oil; and yes I attacked the corners with a set of new rubber. \r\n</P><P>\r\n Afterwards I threw her a shop towel and went down to the bar for a\r\nbeer.  Then I walked home.\r\n</P>'),(3205,'<p>\r\nI could watch her sleep forever. As she lays asleep on the sofa beside me, she looks so peaceful, so calm. While she\\\'s sleeping, all her problems, all her worries and fears evaporate like the cool morning mist, and she becomes a mere thing of beauty. Something to be cherished and appreciated like finest renaissance art. She is my Madonna.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI could watch her sleep forever. Her skin takes on a soft luminescence beneath these flickering fairy lights, and I wonder what she\\\'s dreaming, and if she\\\'s dreaming of me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI could watch her sleep forever. Her face is full of interest and I study it like a map of some far-away foreign land. The shape of her intricately plucked eyebrow, her long sweeping neckline, the small creases around her perfect eyes, all hold my attention in equal measure. Like a cartographer of old I painstakingly map her each and every perfect imperfection.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI could watch her sleep forever.\r\n</p>'),(3152,'no'),(3153,'<p>\r\n\"yongdong sagory chinaso, chopyonjay whengdan podo ayeso, nayreyo jewsayo\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"yongdong four road pass first crossway in getoff i want\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\'ve been getting off work early for the past three or four tuesday/thursday shifts. In my last hour I usually only have one student. His name is BumSung. He\'s a nice guy, but he takes school very seriously, and as a result english has been put on the back burner while he concentrates on other subjects.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGetting off early is great. Not because I dont like working, but because it makes it easier to see my special woman. As she has a \'normal\' job working nine to five, meeting during the week after work means meeting me after ten. Getting off early makes her more likely to say \'yes\', and this makes me happy. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI enjoy my time with her immensely. She can make me smile without even flinching a muscle. She can make me shy away, and she makes my pants tight almost as soon as I see her beautiful face. She has a great fashion sense and it adds to her beauty ten fold. She knows how to drive me crazy in all the right ways.(like when she wore a brown skirt, with high brown boots!)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter a few minutes of confusion meeting up with her near her work, we finally met and took a cab to a nice cafe near her house. The cafe has twenty foot ceilings and is really quite minimal. I like the feel of the place as it is decorated with all sorts of hand-made christmas ornaments. Not gawdy at all, just nice and cozy. I ordered a gin and tonic, a jack and coke for her. We sit and talk about all sorts of things, and tonight she taught me how to give taxi drivers directions to her house and work. In the past ive just had her talk to the cabbies on the phone, but as she thinks this is really \'jellyfish\' of me I had her teach me how to give the directions myself. I was rewarded with remembering various phrases with kisses.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIve been a very happy man this past month. I had a talk with elly about how these days im so optimistic and \'happy\', and how I was finding it incredibly difficult to write \'positive\' and \'optimistic\' swinneys. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI\'ve been negative and angry for so long in my life that writing in that way became almost too easy, and I ended up neglecting my skills at writing and trying to put my \'nice\' feelings down. I still am having quite a hard time putting all these new and exciting feelings into words, but im also trying to write more down at home. I try and write at least a few pages a week, describing the great and joyous things happening to me these days, and im finding that with each passage things are becoming more and more clear. This is something im quite happy about, as I wasn\'t that proud of my previous few swintries. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere have been a lot of great pieces as of late on swinney, and id like to be among them. I swinney.org more now then i do chiamattt.com. A community is always better than an individual...and in this I consider swinney.org to be more \'social\' and \'communal\' and well \'socialist\' and \'communist\'. Yes im still very much political and well all I can say is that a lot of my ideas are becoming like life; optimistic and cheery. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlong live bliss!\r\n</p>'),(3154,'<P>\r\neveryone needs a place to spew.  in eigth grade spew was my word of choice for anything gross (which i happily borrowed from one skippy squirrel of animaniacs).  however, allison bone..no, weaver.  the blonde, yes, weaver.  decided this was the most disgusting word i could use and kept telling me to shut up.  she started doing drugs at some point during high school, i\\\'m sure.  i was smart.  i waited until college.\r\n</P><P>\r\nof course the location wasn\\\'t always the brightest.  yes, let\\\'s put the bong on the picnic table right in front of the frat house on saturday night right after a party where we know cops are going to be swinging by to make sure no one\\\'s dead.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"son, is that your beer.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"why, no officer.  it\\\'s hers.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nof course i could only giggle and stare at my feet hoping that he couldn\\\'t see the state of my pupils.  i heard him laugh at me, \\\"dude, i\\\'m just fuckin\\\' with ya.\\\"  did an officer just say \\\"fuck?\\\"  someone yelled that the pizza had arrived and i forgot about the cop until the next day.  \r\n</P><P>\r\na frat house?  what the hell is that?  it was a boy.  i couldn\\\'t get away from my high school wrestler stereotypes so i dated another one.  i already knew all the moves and still lost every single wrestling match.  i was rewarded with really great sex.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwell, at the time it was great sex.  ripped underwear and a dress around  your hips kind of sex.  scars down his back kind of sex.  the first kind of sex that made you scream.  if he\\\'d left it at that it probably would have continued to be great sex.  but no, he had to bring in the \\\'c\\\' word.  he really does care about me, but no we shouldn\\\'t date.  we just got to college.  what a silly thing to do.  he said it first, so i let myself feel when he kissed me.  stop thinking about how great the sex is and remember the lines of his face and the freckles on his back.  instead of relishing in how wet he makes you, hold dear the cuddling that ensues.  sex was no place for emotions.  it became mechanical.  and therefore started to suck.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nhe lost interest in me emotionally when it did.  and he got filed under \\\'just like every other wrestler.\\\'  \r\n</P><P>\r\nbut, lord, they\\\'re good in bed.</P>'),(3155,'This is going to make me sound weird, and probably a little disturbing, but I think I am fixated with Austin Swinney. I keep thinking I see him everywhere I go, and there\\\'s just no logical explanation for it. He hasn\\\'t been staying in various backpackers\\\' around the far north of New Zealand has he? \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s not like this is some weird sexual fetish, or or even that I fancy him, or am in constant contact with him or anything (other than the fact that I sent him my zine). Maybe it\\\'s just cult worship.  Whenever I talk to O about coming to visit her and she says I can meet her friends, I\\\'ll mention that I want to meet Austin SwinnE. Yes, I stil pronounce his name wrong. Some things are hard to shake. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI haven\\\'t posted anything on Swinney.org for a very long time. I just thought I\\\'d submit this cos I\\\'m sure I just saw Austin at Auckland Hospital buying a cup of coffee.  '),(3156,'<p>\r\nI have often cursed this land called korea for having a totally crap music scene, but i have found two places that play some good music. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe first and less interesting place I listen to music is at home watching the fashion channel. They have a channel that just shows footage from the worlds various runway shows. Everything from lingerie, to ugly fur pants...its all there to see, but its also a very interesting place to hear some good music. I often find myself listening to the channel more than watching it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe second place ive found for good music is taxi\'s. taxi drivers have an incredible and diverse taste in music. While most of the cabs im in have talk radio on, more than a few have had some really interesting \"world\" music playing. Just the other day I was in a cab being driven by a very old man, his face all wrinkled up and aged. I never like getting into the cabs being driven by old men because they tend to be grumpier and less enthusiastic about driving westerners around. He had a tape playing that really got me grooving and bobbing in the back seat. I called my korean friend hyunhee and had her talk to the driver. I wanted her to ask the driver what the music was so that i could go hunting for it in the future. After about three minutes on the phone with this man he started to laugh out loud in a really jolly kind of way, and looked back at me and smiled. Once off the phone he cranked the volume to an almost deafening level and we both started bobbing out head. As i tapped my knee with my hand he tapped the steering wheel. We were bonding.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i paid for my cab and got out, I phoned my friend back and found out that the man had not been able to give her any information about the music. It was a tape he had made himself from music he found on the net. There were no lyrics and as i thought about describing it to myself all i could come up with was \"new age traditional korean style music hooked up to a wahwah pedal patched with some reverb and echo here and there\".\r\nit was just so styling. It wasnt as much driving music as it was chilling with the bong music. It was just great stuff. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis isnt the first time ive complemented a taxi driver on his taste in music. I listened to some really good bhangra in a taxi heading to my apartment in Ottawa from the airport, and in Toronto I listened to a lee perry bootleg while cruising at 150km/h on the queen elizabeth way. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIve also found that when i get into a cab, if the driver isnt listening to something \'western\' they either turn it right down, or shut it off. I always ask them to turn it up. Music is just wonderful. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3157,'<p>Brevity has never been my strong suit - I am the kind of person that, in sending a message across an ocean, would arrange an armada of tall ships to sail en masse and arrive in force. Delivering missives in circumstance that overpowered the meaning itself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But many times that\\\'s the wrong answer; using a hammer to thread a needle, the result is unfortunate often, and lacking in grace always.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So instead of the long heavy reply I first composed, simply this:</p>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<p>The idea of this relationship was comfortable. it was safe. It provided some measure of structure, however vague, that one could set out against a cold and chaotic world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And, being selfish, I\\\'ll admit - I desperately wanted the hope and vision  of a life that it provided - one that you don\\\'t know if I could be happy with, but that I want more than anything in the world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I want to see the world, its people, in all of their best good and their worst bad. I saw this relationship as the ticket out of a life lived in\r\ncubes, to a land where, with a strong companion at my side, two people could stretch the boundaries of existence instead of taking comfort within them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That\\\'s not a trip I can make alone - I lack the grand sense of vision, the sheer audacity, to fly in the face of the world unaided. I am good only at seeing exquisite detail and opportunity in the short term, and need someone else to look to the next peak. To tell me where the edge of the rain ends, and something new begins. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I saw us, somewhere in the future, as a team - one where I told you about the wonder of the earth, while you told me of the beauty you saw in the stars.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That was my dream.</p>\r\n</blockquote>'),(3158,'<P>\r\nIt is Sunday again.  I just finished my ride with the boys.  I was\r\npulling my bike up to park it in the usual spot, under the pathetic\r\ntree in front of the neighbor\\\'s house.  Out steps the neighbor and is\r\nsaying something to me over the thud of my large german machine.  He\r\nis about 5\\\'5\\\" and 50 years old with gross sort of hair.  His hair is\r\nthe kind that, well, covers his whole body, and all looks like pubic\r\nhair.  He is a man covered in pubic hair, a matt of brown, black, and\r\ngrey pubes covers nearly all visible parts of his body: hands, arms,\r\nneck, face, head.  His dog is a dirty ugly little long haired poodle\r\nof some sort.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI turn my bike off, flip my visor up and say, \\\"WHAT?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Are you living next door?\\\" he asks.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Yea!\\\" shouting through my helmet.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe gets nervous.  \\\"Well, I, We,\\\" and he motions to the surrounding\r\nvicinity,\\\" would like you to not park there on OUR tree.\\\"  He grasps\r\nfor some legal right,\\\"You know, you could get a ticket for that, they\r\ncould come through here and give you a ticket...\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Don\\\'t quote me the law!\\\" offended.  \\\"I am not parking ON your tree; I\r\nam parking NEXT to your tree.  If I get a ticket it is a far sight\r\nbetter than getting run into by one of these cars right here,\\\" and I\r\nmotion to the vicinity. \\\"Look, if your fuck ugly little shit dog wants\r\nto piss on this tree while my bike is parked here, that is fine by\r\nme,\\\" and I step off my bike.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"It is against the law!\\\" he says.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Oh yea?  What about this mushed up pile of dog shit right here, I bet\r\nthat is poodle shit.  It looks like poodle shit, that is what I said\r\nto myself this morning when I stepped in it.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe glares and flusters and twitches to the left and right as though he\r\nis looking for backup.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"What?!\\\" I shout.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe opens his mouth.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"WHAT?!\\\" I cut him off.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe pulls back into an Iron Monkey style attack preparation and tosses\r\nthe dog\\\'s leash to the side.  Fuck, I still have my helmet on.  That\r\nis no good.  I hold up the universal sign for \\\'Not Yet\\\'.  Then I take\r\nmy helmet off and set it on my bike seat, and take my cumbersome\r\njacket off, fold it, and set it on the bike\\\'s gas tank.  Then crack my\r\nknuckles and kick him in the inner thigh, grab the back of his neck\r\nwhile he is on his way down press his right cheek firmly against the\r\nhot exhaust pipe of the bike, and confirm this by landing my right knee\r\nin his back.  His skin fuses with the pubes on his cheek.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe screams, \\\"AAAAAAAAAUUUUUHHHHHHHH,\\\" and I pull his head three inches\r\nfrom the pipe.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"What?!\\\" I yell.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Okay! Okay!  I\\\'m sorry, park your bike here!\\\" he is really fucked up\r\nand convulsing into a writhe of pain.  I step up off his back, and\r\nsmack the dog one time to shut it up.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Shit, bitch, if anything happens to my bike, I am coming for your\r\ndog.\\\"  That is it.  I pick up the helmet and jacket, and walk inside.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Shiznits,\\\" I mumble, \\\"nice ride today.\\\"\r\n</P>'),(3159,'i found the following somehow, <a href=\\\"http://www.totse.com/en/drugs/otc/drugd.html\\\">here</a>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAcid (LSD)\r\n</p><p>\r\nAlso known as LSD, trips, loony. Wow, there is so much shit I can say about\r\ntrips, but I will try to get in all I can. Acid was legal up to the year 1963,\r\nthen it bacame outlawed. Why, since the army and the government were using it\r\nto test it on people to study the fuck, people liked it and started buying it\r\nand making it. Trips is the most fucked up drug in the world, I know, I OD on\r\nacid. People ask me how did you OD on it, well I really just bugged out big\r\ntime, and I was home so my parents called the cops and the paremedicts and\r\nshit. So, if you are about to take a few hits, make sure you take em at a safe\r\nplace and a place where no one can bug you. If you take hits at a non-safe\r\nplace it can make you have a bad trip. Along with tripin comes a very bad\r\nthing called paranoia. It kills me, I bug out so fuckin bad!. There are all\r\nsorts of diffrent acid, I mean different forms, it could come in blotter\r\n(piece of paper) Or it could be dropped on a sugar cube, but if you really get\r\nlucky, you get liquid acid, thats the best. Just a few drops in your eyes and\r\nyou are set for a while. Most acid trips last for about 10-12 hrs... Thats\r\nan average trip. But if you take 20 hits like me, you can trip for more then\r\na day or so. But you dont get a 2nd peek. A peek is when you really start to\r\ntrip face. When you take the hits, it doesnt kick in for about an hour or so.\r\nThen you climb up with the trip, and after about 4-6 hours or so you get to\r\nbecome the most triped out you will be on this trip. But like I said liquid\r\nacid is best, cause it works instantly. Wow, I love tripin, fuck I wanna trip\r\nnow so bad arrrr. A great thing to fuck with is when you start to trip face,\r\nwave your hand around you face, you should see trails. The name is very well\r\nknown, people talk about trails till this day. I sometimes judge my trip by\r\nhow good are the trails. And if you do enuff acid like me, you can get\r\npermenant trails and flashbacks. Now with flashbacks, no doctor can tell ya\r\nhow long these flashbacks are going to be happenin. For me I have been getting\r\nflashbacks for more then a year now, I mean they are not as bad as they used\r\nto be, but they R pretty cool. A cool way to get trails if you are not tripin\r\nis smoke a cigarette fast, I mean suck it down. Now you get a head rush, go\r\nnext to any sort of light and start waving your hands, I love that shit. I\r\nreally dont remember what LSD stands for, but who cares, as long as I get to\r\ntrip thats all that counts. It is not cool or safe to trip alone, always\r\ntrip with more then 1 person, so you can have a 3way conversation, I sometimes\r\nbug out if I can only talk to one person. I dunno, maybe its me. Anyway,\r\na cool way to waste the time after you take the hits, I mean when you first\r\ntake the hits, you wont get to trip for another hour or so, so while you are\r\nwaiting smoke a joint. You get stoned and then you remember you just took hits\r\nand you luv it. I do, Well I love every kind of drug there is. Even sex. I was\r\naddicted to sex way back, but I got over it, thanks to some lil\\\' fuckin chick\r\nthis story is not for you to fuckin know. Gee, you must be laughing now, yeah\r\nhe was addicted to sex. Anyone can get addicted to sex, if you have a lot of\r\nGOOD sex. Ok, lets go on boyz. Always listen to cool soft music when you trip\r\nlike drug bands, beatles, rush, all the good ones. And try to figure out what\r\nthe song means, every drug song has a hiding meaning that only people who trip\r\ncan understand it. I figured out the part about the Stairway to Heaven from\r\nLed Zeppelins movie. Dont ever make plans on staying at one place all night.\r\nIts not cool, you always need to move when you trip. Thats why I had such a\r\nbad trip, I triped at my house with 1 friend at night. Oh yeah, trip at nite.\r\nDuring the day you cant do shit, since trips last for 12 hours or so, you need\r\nto have something to do, talk to people and shit. Don\\\'t ever go to school all\r\ndosing. It is the most fucked up thing in the world. I always felt like I was\r\nstuck in class and couldnt do shit, so I jumped up and started to yell & shit\r\nin the middle of class. So, that was da last time I went to school all tripin.\r\nDriving while tripin is very cool, fun, but fucked up. It all depends on how\r\nbad I am tripin. Also, people say to me yeah I triped and I saw micky mouse.\r\nYEAH SURE! You dont see anything thats not there, besides the trails and some\r\nlines on the wall and dots. You dont see naked chicks or anything like that.\r\nIf you have any hits send me some in the mail, its so hard to get them here.\r\nThe best place to get acid is at any dead show. All you have to do is go up\r\nto anyone who has a tydine shirt and ask for some doses. You can get sheets\r\npretty cheap. I know. I didn\\\'t mind, of course all the time that I go, I\r\nnever get to see the show, since I would spend all my money on the \\\'cid.\r\nI dont think anyone can get physcly addicted to trips, I know I was addicted\r\nin some way, since I said to myself I had to trip everyday or else I would\r\nstop living. I think its only me, but dont be too sure man. Oh BTQ, drink alot\r\nof orange juice, it has some sort of acid that makes the LSD in your body\r\ngo up and make it better. When you take acid, the LSD stores in your brain\r\ncells & your back. So if you trip for a while, and then you get stoned, you\r\ncan bring back a trip very easly since pot burns and brain cells, and then all\r\nthe LSD gets realsed and shit. Its pretty cool, you get the best trails. Plus\r\nit can kill the flashback effects, but it hasn\\\'t for me. Watching TV is so\r\nfucked up, but its cool. Go out and buy those color posters, with all those\r\nfuckin colors, I have 2 of those in my room, and everytime I trip I love\r\nto just sit there and stair at em. Until I bug out and wanna get the fuck\r\nout of my house. But thats the price you have to pay to vegge. I always\r\nwonderd where acid was made. I met someone who was the rep. for some place\r\nfactory in California who makes it. Yea, they had a sales rep. Wow, so I asked\r\nhim for hits, but he doesnt carry the acid on him. So if any of you wonder\r\nwho makes acid, now you know. If you want to make quick bux of acid, there are\r\ntwo ways to do it. The first one is buy a sheet of acid. And charge five bux\r\na hit, I bought a sheet for a 100 bux, and I sold 10 hits and I made 50 bux.\r\nBut I dosed on the rest. So that doesnt work 4 me to well, but for some people\r\nit will work fine. Also a quick and easy way to beat people is to take any\r\nstamp, and sell to some acid head like me, and say its awesome trips man.\r\nOh yeah, Do not do bart simpsons hits! I heard the new ones are rat posion.\r\nThe ones I took were preety fuckin cool. I was buying cid at a dead show\r\nand I heard the announcment the dead made, I was outside and I heard it. They\r\nsaid to all the people who are trippin to stop taking bart simpson hits.\r\nJust thought I\\\'d warn you guyz. Shit, what else can I say about acid. Yeah,\r\nwhen you trip, your eyes become so fuckin huge, well I mean your eye pupils\r\nbecome big as hell, they cover up your whole eye. So dont stair into the sun\r\nor any bright light, it could fuck you up. You become sorta like a gremlin in\r\na way. Sex with acid just doesnt work, I admit I love sex, but when I trip I\r\nrather bug out then get fucked. Well, oral sex is cool, but not fuckin. Just\r\ndoesnt work for me. But once I come down of my peek I wanna get fuckin laid by\r\nevery chick I see, my girl friend dident like that. But who carez, I am a drug\r\naddict. With \\\'cid you dont really get hungry or anything, just a little bit\r\nthirsty, but not anything like the munchies. I mean last summer I triped for\r\na whole week straight and I only had a few bowls of golden grahms, and thats\r\nit. I was fucked, but I am feeling much better now. Now with acid, one of\r\nthe worst things about it is that you can never tell if its real acid or not.\r\nUnless you know the dude, other wise you are fucked. Do not ever shoot up LSD,\r\nthere is no point of it, cause if you have liquid acid, just drop it any where\r\nin your body, but it works best in the eyes. You can play a sicko trick on\r\nyour teacher, put a few hits in her coffee and sit back and relax. And then\r\nyou find out later on that the teacher bugged out in class. I must admit, it\r\nwas one of my best ones. Dont ever try to get to sleep, well you really cant\r\nsince your eyes will not close at all, but if you do try or fall asleep you\r\nmight not wake up and shit, I figured out that if you have a dream about you\r\ndying, the body might think its dead and shut down, this wont happen to any\r\none, just people who sleep and trip. Never take a flight anywhere when you\r\nare tripin, you bug out and think you are trapped on this fucking plane.\r\nIf I had the chance to be dosing for the rest of my life I would do it. But\r\nI havent got enough liquid acid to do a shot glass full yet. But I will\r\nsomeday man. For the first time tripers, you only need about 1 or 2 hits to\r\ntrip, since the tollernce of acid builds up like crazy. If you don\\\'t trip\r\nfor a while then your tollence will come down fast. But if you trip on 2 hits\r\none night, you can take 2 hits the next time and barly get off. It takes me\r\naround 5 hits these days to get off. So I really need some acid man. Also\r\nwith some acid, it could come double dipped, that means its twice as strong,\r\nor maybe tripled dipped. Thats what happen to me when I OD. I took 8 Tripled\r\ndipped hits. So the doctors told me its around 25 or so hits. I didnt think so\r\nOh shit, I forgot to tell ya how to take regular acid, if its blotter, just\r\nput it on your tounge for a few minutes, then swallow the hit. With Liquid\r\nacid, like I said just put it in your eye.\r\nOh yeah I forgot to mention that Jimi Hendrix goes great with acid, some\r\npeople might know that and some might not. Watch this video by D-lite. With\r\nall the colors and shit, its fuckin insane.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMe done now..\r\n</p><p>\r\nWriten by - Tripin Face\r\n(C)1991 Å“egions â€œf Å“ucifer/PHUCK, Inc</p>'),(3160,'BMVÂ±@//?( 0 ?\' O80_G@PG@`OO_HO`PP@?@//0/7@0  ?( @70@7?@70?/00/0?00?000/0?0??000/00(/0(/0(/0/00/00(/000   // Â â€”??Ë†â‚¬?â€¡??â‚¬?Ë†?â€¡?Ë†??â‚¬?Ë†â‚¬xpph`pg`ph`og_oh`woÂ°Â°Â¯OOOÂ°Â°Â¿Â¯Â¯Â°@(@\'O `?/Â¯H`// `__   Â°Ë†pÂ¿?Â¯â€¡p?o`?h`OOpG@â‚¬O_â‚¬O_o@OoGO?__Â ppÂ hoÂ woÂ°xoÂ°p_Â¯wo`@?Ã€Â¸Â¿Ã ÃŸÃ Ã€Â¿Â¿Ã€Â¸Â¿Â°Â¯Â°pË†Â°oÂ¯`â‚¬Â°p?Ã€ËœÃpâ€”Ã€â‚¬Å¸Ã€?Â§Ã?Â§Ãâ‚¬â€”Ã€â‚¬?Â¿?Å¸Â¿Â°Â°Ã€Ã€Â¿ÃÃÃ€ÃÃÃ‡ÃÃŸÃ‡Ã€ÃÃ€Ã€ÃÂ¸Â¿_@@O(?oO``@__8_`?O_0?HOp???0Â¯h_Â¯h_Å¸_PÂ¯w`Â¿xoÂ¿Ë†â‚¬Â¿?â‚¬Â¿â€”â‚¬ÃÂ ?_PÂ¯Å¸Å¸Â°Å¸Å¸Â°ËœÅ¸Â¿Â§Â¯`W_@W` ??x?wp?wp?x?x?â‚¬pp?â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬ppP@@@/0@7@@?