 ogo?7?0?@OW_OW_PW___`P_`PX`OW_P_`PX`PX`PX`__`__oPX`PW`PX`_`oPX`PW`PW_OP_PW`PW___`__`__`__`__`__`PX`PX`PX`PX`PX`PX`PW_PW_OP_OPP@HP@HP@HO@GO@@O?@O?@O??@?8@?8@?8@?8@00?/0?/(0 /////000007007?07?000000070070070000000/00/00/00//0//0//0//0//0//0//0////00/00/(//(/ \'/ ?po?oo?oo?pooXP        0\'/?/?@7??0??7??70@7?@7?@7?@7?@8??8?OXopâ€¡Å¸?Â§Â¿?Â¯Ã€?Â°Ã€?Â°Ã?Â¯ÃÅ¸Â°ÃÂ°Â·ÃÅ¸Â¯Ãâ‚¬Å¸Ã€?Â¿op?OG`@7O@0??0?/ /   `PP?wp?wp?wp?po?xp?â‚¬â‚¬PGO      \'/ \'/ \'/  /  / \'/ \'///0  @7?@8@@8@0\'0   PG@â‚¬pp?ppâ‚¬ppâ‚¬oo?pp?x?pp?wp?go00? '),(3161,'<p>My dreamless and deeply restful sleep was interrupted by the persistent ringing of the phone.  I stare at the hotel room\\\'s high\r\nceiling, blink my eyes, and listen to the sound waves of early morning Roman activity penetrating into my space.  I sit up, stretch my hands upwards, sideways and listen to the wooshing sounds of cars and vespas passing through Via Sistina below.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Pronto,\\\" I lazily answer the phone.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Ciao, sofia.  Buon giorno, come stai?  Ha dormito bene?\\\" responds the \r\nvoice at the other end on the other line.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Oh, ciao dottore, good morning to you too.  Yes, I slept very well, hmmmm... what time is it?\\\"  I responded in a sleepy, whispery tone; like the voice of a purring vixen after hours of passionate love making. \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Eh, it\\\'s 9:00am and I am going to my class.  Sorry, did I wake you up bella?\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"No, you did not,\\\" I lied.  \\\"I woke up a few minutes ago.\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"I give an exam this morning but will finish before noon.  After this, I am free to be with you and we walk around Rome, va bene?\\\"  \r\nIl Dottore speaks English distinctively but occassionally translates the syntax of Italian grammar into English; resulting in an awkwardly\r\nrhythmic and poetic prose.  Latin accents (and men) are that way - I guess, rhythmic, poetic, and yes ... sometimes sexy.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Ahh, hmm, ok va be\\\'\\\", I respond in a moaning tone.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Ok, we meet in the balcony where Mussolini spoke to the people of Italy.\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Is that the one across Piazza Venezia?  at the end of Via del Corso?\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Brava, exactly!  I see you there bella, a mezzogiorno, is ok for you?\\\" \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n\\\"Yes, ci sentiamo at exactly 12 noon, ciao,\\\"  I replied, I placed the receiver down and dragged my lethargic body into the shower.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n.....\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nA final splash of my favorite Monguesca baby cologne around my neck and tanned arms, I look into the mirror once more and smile.  I peek\r\ninto my purse to check that everything else I need is in place, it is.  Looking around my tiny room, I grin at how small a single hotel room\r\nis in European standards.  The closet space is a joke.  How did Audrey Hepburn manage to sport many trendy 50\\\'s fashionble outfits back in\r\nthe days, with just this tiny bit of closet space; I wondered.  I walk down the stairs of the Hotel Internazionale.  I grab every opportunity\r\nthat allows me to burn calories.  I rarely take the elevator, and I purposely chose the Hotel Internazionale to force myself up the Spanish Steps three to four times a day.  Without a daily workout, I get depressed; besides my vanity motivates me to maintain my sexy physique while consistently living up to the Greek\\\'s theory of \\\"a sound mind and a sound body\\\".\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI step outside and the hot summer air blended with diesel exhaust immediately awaits to warm-up my freshly showered body.  I struggle to survive crossing the street towards the Spanish Steps, whizzing through Smart cars, Fiats and Vespas.  I have over a few hours to shop before my date with dottore, a nice chunk of time to shop around Via Condotti and Via del Corso.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIn the morning, the Spanish Steps is beautiful.  Void of teenage loiterers and Japanese tourists hovering about with their hats and the latest video gizmo, the steps look immaculate as one descends towards the bottom.  This morning the Romans leisurely walk to work. Most likely they are restaurant or retail workers.  There are quite a few government bureaucrats, I notice.  You can tell who the bureaucrats are.  They are dressed in light cotton shirts, a jacket and a tie.  They walk in a cocky manner, attempting to exude an air of importance, as if wanting to \r\ndistinguish themselves from the rest of Rome as one who has the power to make its citizens miserable should one require some form of public service.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI make my rounds into the Prada and Gucci stores on Via Condotti.  It is like a pilgrimage for fashion afficionados to pay homage to the Prada and Gucci stores whenever around the Spanish Steps, even if one does not have any intention of actually buying stuff.  I, however, am a psuedo tourist (psuedo, since I carry an Italian passport with a United States alien card, and genetically carrying another continent\\\'s racial traits, which really makes me a true alien belonging to no state), and therefore have the right to actually go into a shopping frenzy.  \r\nThe only disappointment I have with Prada, yet again, this year, is the fact that they have not released a new laptop carrying case.  It has been almost three years now, and I need to replace my \r\ndilapidated Prada laptop bag.  Sometimes I laugh at myself when I realize that my persona also includes a portion of wordliness and shallow \r\nmaterialism.  Yet, I indulge; after all, I will not be young and beautiful forever.  I graced each store with my presence.  Some of them were even blessed with my efficiently decisive purchasing decisions.  I am a very competent person.  The task of shopping holds no\r\nexception to my precise nature.  In an hour or so, both my hands are filled with shopping bags.  My biceps are now happily working out.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nStrolling aimlessly along Via Condotti towards Via del Corso into the late morning, I gradually feel the influx of shoppers (American, Asian and Northen European toursits) and the locals, until suddenly I forgot that my surrounding was serene and quiet a few hours ago.  The little street is transformed into a bustling commercial marketplace for articles of clothings and fashion related merchandise.  At the intersection of Via Condotti and Via del Corso, the chaotic activities of the masses reaches its climax.  The overwhelming vision, sound and smell suffocate my senses.  People walking in all directions, taxis and motorrinos whirring by, horse carriages  galloping around, and the very strong scent of exhaust and horse manure fused into the thickening air.  I was starting to feel suffocated by the heat, people and the emptiness associated with materialism.  I briskly turn left on Via del Corso towards Piazza Venezia, hoping to seek refuge under Mussolini\\\'s balcony.\r\n</P>'),(3162,'<p>\r\nolivia didn\\\'t believe that thanksgiving is a big deal in the US. so we drove around just to look at all the closed storefronts and dark streets.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe quest for life began as a drive-by to see if our favorite chinese restaurant was open. i thought that certainly, the chinese would be there for us. but they were not. we also drove by rainbow grocery, the pillar of left-wing unamerican food values, but they too were closed.\r\n</p><p>\r\nas we drove around more, several patterns emerged: all chinese restaurants were closed, unless headless ducks were hanging in the window. all massage parlors were open. all porn related shops, dvd & magazine, were open. (the exception to this was Big Al\\\'s Porn Emporium, in north beach. even Big Al needs a day off.) all strip clubs were open, except mitchell brothers. all italian restaurants in north beach were open. all restaurants owned and operated by cults were closed.\r\n</p><p>\r\nstarbucks and mcdonalds were closed, to further the impression that giant, evil corporations care about family values too.\r\n</p><p>\r\n24th street in noe valley, also known as \\\"stroller valley\\\" or \\\"mommy valley\\\", was not only closed, but it was almost completely abandoned. i spotted four people total: 3 homeless men, waiting for the bus, and 1 bartender, pouring drinks for no one inside an otherwise empty irish pub. even the SUVs that normally lined 24th street were missing, but hopefully safe and snug in their garages. our favorite coffee shop was closed.\r\n</p><p>\r\na few blocks over, mission street offered its usual parade of sketch. but the mood was different tonight: it was more agitated, more drunken, more medieval than usual. bodies stumbled on and off the dividing yellow line, as if playing a game of traffic roulette.\r\n</p><p>\r\ngrant street in chinatown was closed, mostly, except for four tourist shops. you know the kind - those tourist shops in chinatown where you can buy plastic cable cars and san francisco shot glasses, usually run by a blind chinese woman. lucky for us, the israeli falafel shop in chinatown was open (!). and it was packed full of families. the men all wore a yarmulke, and in a strange way, i felt at home, away from the giant roasted birds of the day. i ordered 6 falafel balls, some grilled mushrooms, tahini, hummus, and cucumber salad; all prepared kosher, of course.\r\n</p><p>\r\nunion square, the outdoor shopping mecca of san francisco, was closed down. but you would never have known it. many of the more giant stores (like the gap) had every light inside and out turned on. and the shoppers were already out, flocked in front of macy\\\'s and saks 5th avenue. i wanted to get out and question everyone. what were they doing there? i thought about yelling something out the window as i drove by. i rolled down the window and leaned over but nothing came to mind. olivia suggested a few things to yell, but the moment passed too quickly. i wanted it to be something like \\\"nice suit\\\" or \\\"go home!\\\". oh well. if only i had had the megaphone.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe castro was mixed, as half the neighborhood was accepted into their life partner\\\'s family holiday feasts, and the other half rejected christianity, america, and everything in between. harveys was open, the cafe was open (but empty), the glass coffin was closed.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsparky\\\'s 24 hour diner was open, praise allah, because the coffee situation (see above) was becoming dire. as the counter man froathed our soy milk, i overheard someone else taking a delivery order over the phone. the other end of the phone ordered sparky\\\'s thanksgiving dinner for one, delivered to his home. turkey, choice of vegetable, gravy, stuffing, pumpkin pie, and something else. i thought this was depressing, but olivia remarked that we\\\'re all alone anyway. i\\\'m not sure if she meant it or just wanted to be melodramatic. i wish people didn\\\'t have to feel alone or depressed on the holidays, but also, i wish people didn\\\'t have to care about whether or not they were alone on the holidays.\r\n</p><p>\r\nin the end, i decided that dinner-for-one man was perfectly content with his situation.\r\n</p>'),(3163,'<P>christian fell from the sky tonight.  he landed on mr rong.  mr. rong\\\'s\r\nhead hit the floor.  he became unconcious.  paramedics were called.  they\\\'re\r\nboth in the hospital now.\r\n</P><P>\r\nfinal dress rehearsal.  everything had gone reasonably well.  last trick.\r\nlast trick!!!! of the entire show.  everyone dancing on stage lining up\r\nfor the curtain call.  dance dance.  right next to me, body on the floor.\r\nsecond body on the floor.  music stops.  lights come up.  everyone gathers.\r\nsomeone yells \\\"give them room!\\\"... we all step back... leave the stage...\r\nthe audience is given an apology and asked to leave.  back stage free is\r\nsitting on a mat in tears.  i sit down next to her and she holds me, sobbing.\r\ni am blank.  i am blank.  i am blank.  director comes back and tells us to\r\nchange out of costumes.  everyone silently glides along the floor.  shuffling,\r\nwhispering, shaking their heads.  doug is still out on the stage holding\r\nchristian\\\'s head.  after i\\\'m dressed i go and sit in the seats.  christian\r\nis ok, talking.  mr. rong has regained conciousness but hasn\\\'t moved.  i\r\nsit and stare.  more blankness.  blank. blank. blank.  i don\\\'t know what\r\nthe feelings are.  fear?  compassion?  concern?  i don\\\'t know.  people seem\r\nmore fussed about christian but it\\\'s mr. rong who\\\'s not moving.  he doesn\\\'t\r\nspeak english.  he\\\'s scared and confused.  everyone is scared and confused.\r\nwe\\\'re called back stage again.  director is at a loss for words.  just tells\r\nus the call on tuesday is at 9am.  asks us to rest during the weekend.\r\npeople start making their way home.  doug is standing now.  i give him a hug.\r\nhe only half responds.  my guess is he feels responsible.  he was the\r\none who was supposed to catch christian up in the air.  but it was christian\\\'s\r\njump that wasn\\\'t right.  no one is too blame.  no one is guilty.  we all just\r\nfeel it.  we all feel responsible.  what do i feel guilty for?  i feel guilty\r\nfor my inability to help.  that\\\'s what i feel guilty for.  it\\\'s exactly the\r\nsame as the time i saw a car crash into a motorbike.  i sat and stared while\r\nmy passenger leapt off to the rescue.  i am a perpetual deer in headlights.\r\ni find myself just standing, blank, not there.  derek the rigger walks up\r\nand says \\\"are you ok?\\\"  \\\"it\\\'s all part of the business, right?\\\" i respond\r\nsarcastically.  \\\"i guess so.  the last time i saw an accident like that i\r\ndidn\\\'t work for a year and a half\\\" he says.  i have no words.  there are never\r\nany words.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthere are never any fucking words.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthis is the closest i come to emotion.  i just want to know how to speak.\r\ni just want to know how to help.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni just want to know.\r\n</P>'),(3164,'<p>hi.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>tonight has put me i a rather reflective mood. \r\ni suddenly remember these two things.</p>\r\n\r\n<u>1. the buttplug, the toothbrush and the housemate from hell.<br></u>\r\ni lived in athens, ga, with a girl, her lover who eventually became her ex-husband, and a horrible woman who\'s cats shat and vomited throughout the house over a three month period, and she never once cleaned any of it up. the carpet was really badly stained. <br>the time was coming to move out of the apartment, and this woman became increasingly difficult to live with, not paying rent, leaving the stains in the carpet to fester, and leaving cat shit in her room to petrify.<br> my friend\'s lover soon to be ex-husband was rather fond of butt plugs, and would wear one during the day. <br>my friend, his lover, became so powerless to this awful woman, and evenutally the horrible woman left my friend high and dry without more rent, and with a massive amount of carpet stains thoughout the house. <br>but the important thing is this: just before this horrible woman left the house  the lover/futureexhusband cleaned his butt plug with her toothbrush, unbeknownst to her. so this horrible mean woman was brushing her teeth with a buttplug cleaner.    \r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<u>2. invisible energy.</u> <Br>\r\nfor as long as i can remember, objects in my presence have been able to move by themselves.<br>\r\nin my bedroom as i was growing up, and still today when i spend the night in my old room at my parent\'s house, the light fitting swings around in circles. constantly. without being touched or assisted. they have their own invisible energy. and i want to know about it. i want to be let in on the secret. the lampshade twirls, and i watch it and wonder if there is some magical energy beneath the earth which is telling the object to twirl in a spiral.<br>\r\nwhere does the movement come from? how come it never stops? doesn\'t it get tired? why does nobody talk about this?<p>\r\n\r\nthese two things are completely unrelated, i apolgise.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nok.'),(3165,'i met you one night when i was fifteen.\r\n<p>you were on the footpath with your car, your dog and your dancing.</p>\r\n<p>this is what you had left.</p>\r\n<p>and your spirit.</p>\r\n<p>you were the first person i met who had the virus. you led me to understand how this virus was different to any other in these times. </p>\r\n<p>and so i watched you dance your dance, and i sat down on the concrete and you danced upon the concrete like you knew how precious time is.</p>\r\n<p>and then i held and kissed you, and i rode back to my cushy suburban existance, back to emptiness, back to a world i felt was pointless.</p><p></p>\r\n\r\n<p>seven years later, working at the aids memorial quilt, i came across your panel.</p>\r\n<p> it said \"in memory of bruce\", and it had a drawing of you and your dog and your car, and you were dancing. and there you were. you had been freed of your boundaries, and you had left this life.</p>\r\n<p>i sat down beside your panel with a box of tissues, and i put down my flowers beside you, and i spoke to you and told you how you had taught me so much in a single night. by dancing for people on the footpath.</p><p></p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is 12 years later now bruce.i remember you. and i will remember all of you who i have worked alongside, all of you who have left, all of you who are still here and living defiantly.</p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>humans, we must not become complacacent.<br>there is so much more that we can do.</p>\r\n\r\nREMEMBER THEM ALL.'),(3166,'<p>\r\nthe emptiness, silence and desolation of solitude can bring astounding deafness into an only child\\\'s senses.... \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor almost seven years, i prayed for a sibling to break the monotony; to bring the endless darkness of my existence to an end .  .\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nafter years of longing, the stork of life obligingly gave into my persistent plea ... \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nat the crack of dawn, on the 29th of november, my young and peasant looking father, (who normally was nonchalant regarding affairs of the home), knocks into my room; with a grin, gently he tells me that I have a new baby sister.... \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy black and white world suddenly transformed into a colorful canvas of possibilities.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n. . .\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe events of the following years may not have been as i have expected; it was not perfect;  we fought, we yelled, we screamed, we kicked and scratched each other... i was torn; i wanted to play and love her, but we had our differences.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnevertheless..she has given me the love, the joy \r\nand the time a lonely child has always yearned for\r\nfrom a sibling... she was a friend, a foe, a confidant, a traitor, she also was a plaything to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni may not have given her what she has\r\nexpected from an older sister; in fact i\r\nhave constantly deprived her of the ideal\r\nshe may have wished for from an older sister.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntough!!  i may not have been the sister that you have wanted me to be...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n... all i can give you is my love.... from an imperfect older sister; a rennaissance, sometimes crazy and uncoventional older sister.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni give to you my soul - all that it is, and all that it is not.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom all that i\\\'m not, and all that i am . . .\r\ni love you, for all that you are.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhappy birthday, my little one\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3167,'<P>My mom likes to tell me how horrible I look. In her defense, I\\\'ll tell her if her hair looks dumb or her lipstick is gaudy. Today she said something which I instantly forgot (it\\\'s best to just to block these things out) but the first thing I thought of when she said it was; \\\'Gee- my mother is like a swinney article- Friendly Supportive.\\\' Sarcasm required.</p>'),(3169,'<i>Never give out your password or credit card number in an instant message conversation.</i><br><br>\r\n \r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">What\\\'s bob\\\'s surname mate?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">henderson</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">That\\\'s it, just trying to remember</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Who else works @ ITC that i\\\'d know?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">scott</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">adam</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Nick?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">nick p</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">think thats it</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">everyone else went to yousystems</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">tony?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">fired</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">and his wife left him</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">nooo</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">why?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">really?  Jeeeeeesus\\\'</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">yep!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">I mean F****n\\\' Jeeeesus</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">lol  </font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">when was this?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">lmao</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">LMAO?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">laff my ass off</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">dunno, i think tony only went a few months back</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">I think he went ages ago too.  I went to Chelsea Match with Julie and Claire earlier this year...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">She told me he\\\'s in a bit of a state.</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">really?! cool</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">cool that u saw julie.. not cool he\\\'s in a state!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">mmm...  Yeah.  I tried to bridge the old contact between Julie Myself and Steve/Dustin</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">(Hoffmann) lol</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">and you and scott</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Do you know what ever happened to Moorey?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">mooreys at yousystems</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Not Butt Pirates?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">lol</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">and has a girlfirend!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">noooo...  Who?  Is it Sarah Chennell?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">lol, nah but apparently she\\\'s fit... though he wont let me meet her!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">are you in contact with him then?  Mobile no.?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">yeh, still not seen him though he\\\'s always \\\'busy\\\'</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">but then again so would i be if i still had a teenage girlfriend!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">well...  he\\\'s got a missis ain\\\'t he, no time for anyone mate</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">teenage?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">18 i think</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">he\\\'s my buckin\\\' age</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">his numbers 077368*****</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">he\\\'s younger than me, old moorey</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">is he?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">yeh</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">you\\\'re 21 so how ol\\\' is he</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">he\\\'s 21 now i think</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">so do u hear much from julie?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">not since March/April time I think</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">i didn\\\'t realise you and julie really associated all that much at itc</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">mmm...  we\\\'ve seen eachother a couple of times now.  The first time, again was a Chelsea match, and Steve H + Karen were there</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">She\\\'s a top lass though.  I did piss her off at the last match, I turned up late and they (Claire & Julie) missed the first 10 minutes...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">That\\\'s probably why I haven\\\'t seen or heard from her lately</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">i saw dustin and plastic karen the 1st week i was back actually. bumped in2 them at sainsburys!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">lol</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">hey...  Sainsbury\\\'s...  Were you with Scott?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">lol</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Plastic Karen...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">as a matter of fact i was!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">we met up at the canteen.. lol</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">:0)  mmm...  Wish I was there mate.  I really do</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">just like old times</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">yup.  That\\\'s why I want to meet up with the ITC lot just before christmas...  Is it half-Day christmas eve?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">To see you and Scott...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">i hope so, im supposed 2b going away</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">where?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">sum hotel in kettering</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Oh yeah..?  With family?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">yeh</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">cool n tha gang</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">cool n tha gang?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Actor Samuel Jackson, as Jules, in Quentin Tarantino\\\'s 1994 BlockBuster PULP FICTION</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Sam L Jackson ^</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">was supposed to be going with caroline but now i think i\\\'ll just get stoned with my cousin jo and make fun of my family!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">stoned...  You Stoner</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">dont front like u dont be puffin on none o\\\' that weed muthafucka!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Carolines swapped a Â£300 a night christmas break for christmas in Turves! good move!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">why you be talkin\\\' bout\\\' her 4 man.  Foo</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Enjoy yourself Jay...  Hope that\\\'s ok for me to say by the way</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">hope what\\\'s ok?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">what I said about Caroline, stop talkin\\\' bout her </font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">nah ur right dude.</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">bitch be messin\\\' wit ma\\\' mind innit!</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">so, is sarah back home now or what\\\'s happening?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">she\\\'s back now mate.  Tom\\\'s on his own in Camden until Jan 03.  I think they\\\'re still together...  Not too sure what\\\'s happening there though...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">what happens in Jan 03?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">he move\\\'s in with me</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">and Laura</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">oh yeh u said before</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">so theyre trying the distance thing?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Forgive me, it\\\'s not my place to say, but...  Yes they are.  Not a very good thing to happen to a couple that have been living together for nearly 2 years...</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">tell me about it! hope it works out for them i really do. 2 years is a lot to throw away</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">well i better let you do some work cowboy!  yo\\\' i\\\'ll talk to scott, maybe the three of us hook up and remenisce yeh? around xmas time.</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">yeah, that\\\'d be cool dude</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">Smartness...</font><br>\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">scotts on medication now, so he can come out and play</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">is he alright?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">yeh... well u know what he\\\'s like</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">he\\\'s as right as he\\\'ll ever be</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">Let me know what\\\'s happening around christmas eve sort of time for drinks etc.  Maybe we can go to The Brewery Tap or summit</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">yeh brewery tap would be good. i\\\'ll email u closer to the time</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"red\\\">cool mate, make sure you do that.  I wanna see you again soon mate.  Missin\\\' ya.  Missin\\\' tha old times donchu know?</font><br>\r\n\r\n<font color=\\\"blue\\\">see you soon, maybe online in 30 minutes when I get home...</font><br>\r\n'),(3170,'when i was 15 years old, i lived in a little town in kentucky and dated a german exchange student who promptly fell madly, passionately, amazingly in love with me.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe are talking 1969 here.  last fall i had an email from him, he\\\'d found me in a web search.  he poured out his heart to me about how important i was in his life, etc.  i was surprised, to say the least.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe wanted to phone me, to get in touch, to talk about old times.  i was weirded out by the whole thing. mark was in kuwait and it made me feel strange. but, then again, he was a nice boy, and i was touched and flattered that he remembered the 15 year old diva i once was.\r\n \r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut, i told him i was very happily married, i was not comfortable striking up a correspondence with someone who had such strong feelings about the memory of me.  i also sent him a picture of the now 48 year old person i had become.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nthen it got weird, because i would not give him my phone number or address, only that i lived in texas.  he did a search on the web and found some poor person in dallas he thinks is me...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nand said - i see you live on holland avenue in dallas...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\ni just said, no, i don\\\'t, and i did reply to him again and this time i cc\\\'d my husband on the reply and let him know i was not interested in keeping up a correspondence.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nso, this morning i receive another email from him, a year later, again with reference to me living in dallas and he has seen on my web site that i am moving to germany next year...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe whole thing weirds me out.  i don\\\'t want to see some guy i barely remember who thinks of me as the love of his life, and i am quite sure that he is going to make it his mission to find me.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nand then i remember that i\\\'ve done something similar one time - looked up an old boyfriend on the web, but i never emailed him or anything, i just wondered if he was married and happy and successful, or, actually, i was hoping he was rotting in prison, the low-life sonofabitch..\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen does curiosity become stalking?  at what point is it time to freak out?'),(3171,'<p>\r\nI was reading through some journals. Not online journals. Just the journals of some of the students who go to the school i teach at.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\none of the journal entries in sung hae\\\'s book talked about walking up a mountain and looking down and being sick. it was well written, though most of the grammatical errors were very cliche. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\njoon youngs entry consisted of two things. \\\"today i star crafting and eated kimchi\\\". Not much there, and if you knew the kid you would not be suprised. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe best entry of the lot was this girl hah eun. In her entry she talked about going on a picnic with her family. They went somewhere(i have no idea what she wrote. it was very messy) and ate lots of food with her extended family. at the end of the entry she said \\\"mom was sick much. she drink many too much beer\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have two main ideas regarding what i think is an alcohol problem in korea. My first assumption was that koreans drank out of depression, long work hours, and a weird unsatisfied life. Im not sure this is all that true. My second and more recent assumption is that koreans as a unit have not had the time to get used to alcohol consumption like those in the west have. I think to those stereotypes of native americans(canadians) not being able to handle alcohol and letting alcohol consume their lives; making them angry, lazy, addicted, and violent. I dont agree with the stereotypes eitherway, but i think too an extent, korea is moving through the \\\"democratic\\\" phase so quickly, that people dont know when to say \\\"ok ive had enough to drink\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy brother told me a story once. He had been working in finland for a few months and said that because they changed the laws regarding the sale of alcohol, people were now spending their friday and saturday nights binge drinking. I cant remember the exact details of the story, but the point is that when something that has not changed for some time, changes abruptly over night, people on the whole cannot understand and control their responses. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis is making me think more and more about the legalization of weed and other drugs in canada. I think that if it happens it should happen over time, smartly beginning with medical purposes.\r\n</p>\r\n<P> \r\nin short, i think koreans really drink a lot, though over time i think this will cease to be as big a problem as it is these days. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3172,'Best thing recorded in history? \\\"The Shower Tape.\\\" \r\n\r\n90 minutes of my shower into a stereo mic.\r\n\r\nNo really.\r\n\r\n'),(3173,'<p>\r\nhave you ever considered what a curse it must be to be beautiful?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni find that this can be true for both males and females.  but, it\\\'s more clearly a problem with women, who are blessed, and have been, always.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsome women, are just so perfect.  the act of looking at them, is satisfying in itself.  if i find a picture that i like, of a female face, i can be captured by it.  i can lose hours examining it.  i look into paper at the eyes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that i am not alone in having this fascination.  it\\\'s innate.  i studied genetics in college.  i know of darwin.  perhaps it can be explained along these lines.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut, then what is beauty.  the definition is constantly changing.  and in certain cultures, there are clearly variations in likings.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnonetheless, there is usually a threshold, over which perhaps a certain fraction of the immediate audience is compelled.  \\\"yes, here she comes!\\\", they whisper when she approaches.  excited!  some will just sneak a peek.  others will glare.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit usually doesn\\\'t take too long for a girl to figure out where she stands.  she knows it, whether she likes it or not.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit has to be exciting.  to know that people have their eyes on you.  to know that people are interested in every move that makes.  even the accidental moves; leaning over to tell a secret, stretching to reach the top shelf.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis raw power that they notice, will eventually be harnessed.  we are all opportunists.  given two choices, who doens\\\'t like to have their way?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nvoila, power 101.  barely old enough to read and write, and already strong and mighty.  but, it is a poor twist of fate.  should we arm the cubscout with a military missile launcher?\r\n</p>\r\n<hr>\r\n<p>\r\nthese musings have a purpose.  understanding and acceptance.  i am in a relationship that i am quite happy with.  yet, i struggle with many of the frailties of man.  when i watch mtv, movies, or read magazines, there are nothing but pictures of lurid women.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy is it that these beautiful women that i have met in my life, all have such tragic tales?  disappointment, rejection, deception.  i can\\\'t stand the person that this creates.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the end though, it seems that these people have been cursed.  cursed by looks, that make them an object.  cursed by looks that gives them power too soon, when they can\\\'t handle it yet.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsome beautiful people do survive.  but, such a large percentage of them, are really filthy.  unwashed and mangled on the inside, such a complete contrast from what we see.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nam i just imagining things, or have other people experienced this as well?  what do you think of the explanation i try to make?  taking this further, we owe beautiful people the same sympathy that we give starving africans, and oppressed falun gong.\r\n</p>\r\n    '),(3174,'<p>In the comments to an earlier post, xep, being the magical person that xep is, <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3162#19207\\\">somehow divined</a> my association with the lovely and confounding Narcisse Dille of Santa Cruz, San Francisco, France, and Points Imaginary and Thus Uncharted.</p><p>I did my best to outline a few necessary facts, but didn\\\'t want to go on at length in the comments area. I also realized that my memory of the guy was a bit spotty (sometimes everything before the turn of the millenium seems hazy to me) so I asked Jonathan, my good friend and former university roommate, to help me fill in a few blanks. Jonathan\\\'s responses, however, were undeniably superlative to anything I could come up with, so I\\\'ve decided to pass them along unedited. What follows are his words, not mine.</p>\r\n\r\n<blockquote><p>1. He named himself after Dill in \\\"To Kill A Mockingbird.\\\" Only later, when his blancophagia wobbled uncertainly into\r\nAdam-Ant-qua-18th-century-highwayman-qua-\\\"stand and deliver\\\"-qua-\\\"le roi soileil\\\" territory did he affect the Franketic \\\"-e\\\" to his\r\nname. As you may recall, this evolved finally into \\\"Narcisse Dille.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2. He was born in Marin County -- I could tell you the exact city if I remembered the names of any cities in Marin County. I drove\r\nhim to his family pied-a-terre thereat; he refused to let me in, or even show me the exact location of his house. He was born John\r\nWalker Lemmes -- the fact that he was from Marin County and that his name was John-Walker-L-something made me think for a while he\r\nwas the American Taliban (not withstanding that Mr Lindh affected similar French royalty ideas about personal hygeine during his\r\nAfghan tenure).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3. He was kicked out of Kresge College due to complaints tendered by his apartment-mates that he was stockpiling rotting and\r\nhazardous materiÃ©l, to wit styrofoam, large sheets of plastic, pigeons, doves, and the occasional white Christian infant. I still\r\nremember when we were performing a ritual on one of his objets d\\\'arts and the bird actually took wing inside his little concrete\r\nroom lit by a single flourescent, fluttering in a mad prelapsarian panic and rousing a mighty gale that made all the wee bits of\r\nstyrofoam, blank paper, plastic wrap and tiny soap carvings stolen out of Elfland flurry madly about. We were listening to opera at\r\nthe time. John/Dill/Narcisse lay his heavily powdered hands on his heavily powdered cheeks and wailed, \\\"Coo! Coo! Coo!\\\" the entire\r\nwhile.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3a. He then lived in my dorm room, using my ID card (since I was too lazy) to go fetch food from the cafeteria for us both. Since he\r\nonly ate white food, natch, I subsisted along with him on a diet of rice, bean sprouts, corn kernels bleached with lime juice,\r\njimica and white asparagus. I think he was officially still an enrolled student during this time; only his housing arrangements had\r\nbeen abrogated. I also think he let his enrollment lapse during this time, which brings up to</p>\r\n\r\n<p>4. France. Yes, Dill went to France and came back Narcisse Dille. He was there only for a week or two; he was mugged at a Parisian\r\nmetro station on his first day there. He then told me that he spent the rest of his langour there waning in faux absinthe-withdrawal\r\non the metro tracks; over the years since then, that story has become embellished (so that I imagine it\\\'s now that he was saved and\r\nsuccoured by the Liberation, and aided them in their fight against Petain -- kinda like \\\"Top Secret!\\\", now that I think about it).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>5. He became a performance artist in Santa Cruz. I remember his first concert, all gaudily advertised with butterflies and\r\ninvocations of \\\"la Narcisse Dille.\\\"  People actually paid to hear him sit at a piano and very softly play all white keys.  To my\r\nmind now this reminds me of Morton Feldman and other vaguely contemplative irritating arpeggios that have become all the rage, but\r\ncontemporary reception then was of puzzlement, befuddlement, inchoate outcry and demand for refunds.  I think he refunded everyone\r\ngladly, since they had neglected to pay him in white currency.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>5a. After that concert he became associated with some outfit in San Francisco called Theatre Rhinoceros. I don\\\'t recall whether or\r\nnot this has anything to do with Ionesco, but I do think he would have made a beautiful bald soprano.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>6. Narcisse Dille was obsessed with the colour white. Regardless of whatever aesthetic or political or artistic credo he may\r\nmanifest as the rationale behind this, originally it was because John Lemmes was a dutiful goth and was in love with another dutiful\r\ngoth girl. Dutifully they dressed in black. When she dutifully broke up with him, he changed his name and decided never to wear\r\nblack again. Since he was such a dutiful goth, it was only logical that he thereupon dressed only in white lace finery forever on. \r\nHe tried to dye his hair white too, to purge his black hair of its gothitude and all its associations of girl, but whatever he did\r\ntransformed his hair the colour and consistency of exceptionally conductive copper wire.</p></blockquote>\r\n\r\n<p><b>A few other facts (from moebius):</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>When he first moved to San Francisco, Dille lived in a tiny apartment with a couple of women on the top floor of an apartment building in the Tenderloin. He lived in a hall closet, which he\\\'d redesigned to look like the heart of an enormous, silky spiderweb. Things were hanging from the ceiling. The main thing I recall from that visit was Dille\\\'s obsession with his new breadmaker, which was churning out new loaves on an hourly basis.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As far as I could tell, for most of his time in S.F., Dille essentially couchsurfed in the apartments of various women. They were all statuesque and uncannily beautiful, with some degree of vampy goth pedigree to their fashion. This was strange because to my eyes Dille had all the sexual charisma of a prepubescent eunich. According to him, the women generally found him at various events and locations around the city and essentially \\\"took him home,\\\" as if he were a lost puppy. Which, I have to admit, he often seemed to be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I last saw Dille around \\\'95, standing on the corner of Broadway and Columbus in the company of two or three tall, Nordic looking blonde women. I waved at him. He smiled winsomely back, with that same default expression of confused bemusement playing across his face, as if he wasn\\\'t quite sure whether he was awake or dreaming. The women pretended not to notice any of this. The light changed and one or another of them pulled him across the street into the pulsing crowd. And that was that.</p>'),(3175,'<p>do you ever magically look at the clock when it is 11:11? \r\n\r\n<p>i have been looking at 11:11\\\'s for at least five years now, and i occasionally meet other people who are also eleven eleven people. \r\n\r\n<p>i think we are a part of a secret society of beings who understand that there is some significance in eleven eleven. even if we don\\\'t really understand what the significance is. it just is significant.\r\n\r\n<p>other eleven eleven people understand each other. i like it. \r\n\r\n<p>sometimes i like to take photographs of eleven eleven on clocks. sometimes in the car. sometimes in my room. sometimes at my friend\\\'s houses.\r\n\r\n<p>i wonder if any of you are eleven eleven people. let  it be known. we\\\'ll start a club.'),(3177,'<center>\r\n<table width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"100%\\\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td width=\\\"100%\\\" valign=\\\"center\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" colspan=\\\"3\\\">\r\n<SPAN STYLE=\\\"font-family: arial; font-size: 10pt;\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>i turn to the open doorway, it\\\'s low, duck my head, step through, enter the building, lift my head again. the single room inside greets me, plain, blanketed with moss, mounds raised from the floor here and there in no particular pattern. there is a single light in the middle of the room in the floor. i\\\'m paused at the door, looking at the light. it grows out from the floor, radiates softly into the room. i walk toward it, weave through the mounds that brush the hips of my suit as i walk past. i come closer, the light gets brighter, my eyes strain and i squint. i can\\\'t see where it starts, it is not a bulb.  i kneel close to it, it surrounds my vision, white, white, quiet, no heat, i cannot see. i reach forward into it, my hand pushes into a membrane, like an eyeball, but much softer, less <i>there</i>.\r\n\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3177_1.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>i catch another wave of thought, where am i, where have i left, where have i come, i did not come here, i was transmitted here by my people, by our human history, in machines that they built with their hands and the hands they have never known, the hands that they have forgotten. i have been washed here by technology and i am stranded, clean, i cannot\r\nsee.\r\n\r\n<p>i am still kneeling on the floor and my loss in thought is broken, the darkness trickles in, i can see again, i feel hands clutching the lapels of my suit, lifting me up, i am heavy.\r\n\r\n<p>clarity quickly returns. other passengers shuffle past me, i rise to my feet, turn, and see it is the woman that has lifted me.\r\n\r\n<p>she says  \\\"you seem to like shiny things\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>humor. it catches me off guard. i look down at her hands, wondering how she lifted me up. i tell her that she\\\'s strong. she tells me that i\\\'m skinny.\r\n\r\n<p>again, humor. her tone is friendly but she turns quickly and walks away. i watch her walk to a nearby mound of moss. there are several of them, long and narrow rising out from the organic floor. how does she know to lay on the mound as a bed? how do the others know? they must have the same urge that i do to disassociate myself from the ground in this foreign place.\r\n\r\n<p>i lay in the mound closest to me. my leg brushes over the moss on its top as i straddle it. i lay back and put my legs up. i sink a bit into the mound, it molds to the shape of my body. i like this moss, and i think about this being much better than the ship. i fall asleep.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3177_2.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"left\\\">i am watching myself sleep. i am standing beside myself laying down. the others are doing the same. my sleeping body has no slug, my watching body does. i am watching and doing nothing else.\r\n\r\n<p>i wake up and feel my dream shift not so smoothly into my memory. it causes a lurch in my head, a brief headache, and it causes me to close my eyes. when i open them and look around i see the others are also waking up.\r\n\r\n<p>there is more light now, this must be morning, i look down and see the floor light is gone. there are thin windows in the walls and soft light reaches in through them. i can see everyone clearly for the first time since boarding the flight. most are younger, like me. grandpa, i miss you, it\\\'s not fair that the elderly are considered too poor an investment for emigration, less likely to produce children, more likely to die sooner. not fair, and it hurts me to understand the unfairness.\r\n\r\n<p>before we can stand up, the door opens, stronger light floods in, and a settler steps down in front of it. he says \\\"you will come with me to eat\\\".\r\n\r\n<p>my stomach is empty, it growls, i need to eat. our group stands up from our beds, we test our legs on this soft floor, i feel strong. we walk single file in silence to the door and the woman who helped me walks in front of me. i immediately look for hints of her body beneath her suit. i smile, glad that at least one thing is still familiar.\r\n\r\n<p>walk, feet, a few steps up, head down, i\\\'m outside. the light is bright and full, other passengers are walking out of other buildings. some are quiet, some are talking, it is good to hear human voices. settlers lead the way at a slow pace with loping strides.\r\n\r\n<p>i step to the side of the door to look around. so much green, everywhere, so many buildings. i am in a city, or a very large town.  settlement seems too small a word.\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3177_3.jpg\\\" width=\\\"270\\\" height=\\\"270\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" align=\\\"right\\\">no two buildings are alike, and it\\\'s hard to tell where one starts and another ends. they sprawl out from where i stand, all rooted in the ground,\r\ndifferent lengths and heights, but none much higher than my shoulders, i can see to the horizon through some long sight lines around me. i scan the landscape and begin to see that beneath all of these organic buildings are geometric shapes, everywhere, clothed, softened, hidden by the moss. a soft geometry of green. i look up to the sky and see only opaque mist, diffusing light upon everything below it.\r\n\r\n<p>a settler approaches me, i\\\'m still looking up, and he says \\\"follow me. you must eat\\\". i say nothing and follow him into the many that are already walking. \r\n</span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</center>'),(3178,'<b>Tonight (two, a time of two parts):</b><br><br>\r\nSecond, 2 Paragraphs (Pp.):<br>\r\n<i>to be done in two parts...</i><br><br>\r\n<b>1.</b> I hear phasing sounds:</b><br>\r\n<b>2.</b> I am being phagocytised by:<br>\r\n--the world (i,e. the man)<br>\r\n--unitarians<br>\r\n--the three scorpioed alarm<br><br>\r\n<i>...and all the little voices are calling to me and laughing out over the TV.</i><br><br>\r\nIt sounds like Shirley Temple, over a mix of radiant splendor and hashish grumble. The world strains as I feel the coffee of my calculator hitting a striking mix of poise, random pleasures hint of a coming forever tomorrow embedded\r\nin strife... yet soft like felt, the world spinning and I am dying and praising and heating up lamps for the soul of goodness.<br><br>\r\nNew paragraph here or this will roll on a mighty river of sea love. soul-swell, steady-state study safe space space space <i>in deep space</i> falling in a stirring blend of liberian army tempo.<br><br>\r\n\\\"Temporarily Great!\\\" said the president to me as I sang in electronic haste of spectrum finality to the forgotten mathemeticians of dogma.<br><br>\r\nThe good early world is etched upon your Tigris heart/Euphrates soul.<br><br>\r\nExcelent!<br><br>'),(3179,'the fulcrum point,<br>\r\nthe cornerstone,<br>\r\npoint x.<br><br>\r\nuniverse expanding from one point.<br>\r\nthis one point tends to change in my perception.<br>\r\nthe \\\"middle of the galaxy\\\" or my heart.<br>\r\ni\\\'ve awoke to san francisco rain.<br><br>\r\ncars make different noise as they speed by<br>\r\nwet streets seem clean<br>\r\ncoffee tastes good<br>\r\njazz mixes with raindrops in a perfect way<br>\r\neverything is kind of blue<br>\r\nits not the sad blue but the cool blue<br>\r\nazul fits better.<br><br>\r\ngood day to say prayers.'),(3180,'<p>The end of a bleached summer day.  Tumbling into the car for the trip home, there is sand stuck to our skin.  It glints and sparkles as we try to brush most of it out of the car door.  Even the best attempts will prove to be futile as for days afterwards the grains will be a reminder of the hours spent ankle deep in the dunes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Faces are flushed, sun-rosy.  The deep pink spreads, closely layered with the salt residue which sits atop the surface.  Hair tousled, our limbs lazily tangle across the confined space of the backseat.  The vinyl is almost too hot to touch, and damp towels are spread across to protect the backs of our legs.  The action scatters more sand across the floor to a soundtrack of rueful cries and laughter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The drive home is always much quieter than the earlier ride.  Tired eyes slowly close, lids too heavy to hold open.  The world seems a lazier place as dusk approaches.  There is perhaps a stop for vegetables from a roadside stall â€“ the first tomatoes of the reason, scarlet and smooth-skinned or a bag of crisp apples - or apple juice, an ice-cream; simple treats to soothe dry throats and bodies.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The memory of the water is still so close, the feel of the slippery, almost intangible water with a force untold behind it.  Diving beneath the waves; rising up and through them, itâ€™s a feeling like no other â€“ an almost-invincibility.  You work only against the current and your own weight; pushing against the tide but let the waves carry you into shore. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Once you arrive home, towels and togs are piled in a soggy heap on the bathroom floor as the shower steams the bathroom mirror.  The remnants of the day are cleaned from your body as hot water tries to replace sea with city.  Skin becomes soft and smooth once again as the salty powder dissolves and washes away.  The ubiquitous sand gathers in swirls around the plug hole.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Under the shade of glittering night the waves menace, sucking as a leech on skin does.  Sinister now, the depth of colour is not deceptive â€“ you know the danger on the flipside of the coin.  The rocks shine as the liquid slides back and away.  The dunes and beach are emptied bar a few figures illuminated by the moon.  Only indentations and ruffled surface area, the occasional litter and a forgotten belonging or two remain from the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The phantom feel of the water is a missing limb, a cry in the night, a half-seen vision in delirium.  Itâ€™s enough to wake you from slumber, to cause a habitual return to the shore to fulfil the ache and hollow in your bones.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Itâ€™s summertime and you know you will return.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3181,'<i><tt>Trees, Static:</tt></i><br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3181_1.jpg\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<i><tt>Trees, Kinetic:</tt></i><br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3181_2.jpg\\\"><br><br><br><br>\r\n<i><tt>...the world is so much prettier when it\\\'s moving.</tt></ii>\r\n\r\n'),(3182,'<P><B>CNN WAS ON IN THE OTHER ROOM</B></P><P>That was the winter they decided they didn\\\'t really need electricity.  It was disconnected the day before the presidential election,  on a morning when they had gotten up early, eaten, then turned on every light in the apartment and sat around playing euchre with all the windows open.  They were drinking Mexican beers from a case in the middle of an otherwise empty refrigerator.</P><P>The meter man came at ten.  He was muttering to himself.  They were using a big shell for an ashtray.</P><P>She put on her lucky ring.  Him: The phone doesn\\\'t work.  Her: Yeah, no shit.  It\\\'s cordless.  The one in here does.</P><P>The walk to Florida Power took them through downtown, across the river and out by the highway.  Their deposit was refunded in cash.</P><P><B>AND JOHNNY DEPP\\\'S A JUNKIE</B></P><P>There was a stream running through the bar, a channel cut into the marble.  Little plastic boats bounced by the shot glasses and change.  Her: Oh, yeah, Patrick Swayze.  He\\\'s superfemme.  Him: What about Richard Gere?  Her: Him, too.  Silence.  They looked at each other in the mirror.  Him: Randy?  The bartender was leaning back into the inside corner of the bar.  \\\"Same again?\\\" he said.  He poured them a shot of Sambuca apiece and took some bills off the pile.  Toward the end of the afternoon, she called Howard from the payphone in back.  Howard told her to wait until seven to come by.  Apart from an old man who had fallen asleep at one of the tables, they were alone with Randy.  They sat around smoking and talking until six thirty then poured themselves out into the night.</P><P><B>HOWARD SURE DOES KEEP A NASTY HOUSE, THOUGH</B></P><P>The policy is, she handles the money.  She could have fifty thousand dollars in her purse, for all I know.  We both had to shit by the time we got to Howard\\\'s.  Howard\\\'s got three bathrooms, so we were all set.  I finish up and come downstairs and they\\\'re talking about the election.  I\\\'m the only one out of the three of us that can vote.  They both have felonies.</P><P>Howard puts out a pretty good bag.  Nine grams for forty bucks.  Decent weed, nothing special.  We walk over to the in and out store and get some candles then pick up a pizza.  We get back in the apartment, stumble around in the dark.  She finds a plate, puts one of the white hurricane candles in the middle of it.  Puts match to wick.  The room lights up slowly with light leaking into the corners.  In the flame, she has cat eyes.</P><P><B>THE ALGEBRA OF NEED</B></P><P>Matt thought it would be good to go through a candle a day.  We\\\'d light it at dark, then read until it went out.  It would burn down to a puddle of wax with the wick on fire a second or two after the