onight.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, shit.  You were gonna do that anyway.\\\"</P><P>They laughed and passed the rest of the bottle of champagne back and forth.  At the dock at the Lido, the young man disembarked first then helped the young woman out.  She was wearing a thigh-length black dress and pearls.  The young man wore a light blue three-button suit.  He had taken the jacket off after the ceremony.</P><P>\\\"My God, is it hot,\\\" the young woman said.  \\\"The next time we get married, let\\\'s do it in cooler weather.\\\"</P><P>They found a bar and each had a Campari and soda.  The bar was half-full, and someone had left a week-old issue of the New York Times on one of the tables.  They traded the sections once they had read them.  They sat at the bar and drank Campari and soda as the ceiling fan above them moved the cool, smoky air.  The young woman smoked menthol cigarettes, putting them out in a heavy glass ashtray.  The young man ran his hand along her thigh and nibbled her earlobe.</P><P>\\\"Hello, Mrs. McCord,\\\" he whispered in her ear.</P><P>\\\"Good day, Mr. McCord,\\\" she murmured.</P><P>She turned her head and put her hand on the back of his neck and kissed him.  They sat at the marble bar and drank as the people emptied out towards dinnertime.  A man came in barefoot, wearing swimming trunks and a T-shirt.  The bartender yelped at him in Italian, and he left.</P><P>The young man felt abnormally good, like he had superpowers or something.  He ordered a shot of Sambuca.</P><P>\\\"Don\\\'t get drunk,\\\" the young woman said.  \\\"Remember your duties.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.  Let\\\'s go then.\\\"</P><P>They had dinner at a place in the middle of the island.  They both had hare cooked with chickpeas and pine nuts and morels in a brown sauce.  Afterwards, they walked around and had their picture taken in a photo booth.  Long after the black and white prints had been spit out, they sat in the booth necking.</P><P>The young man slipped his hand down the front of the young woman\\\'s panties and began to stroke her vulva.</P><P>He said, \\\"Let\\\'s get you out of that dress and take some more pictures.\\\"</P><P>\\\"We\\\'d better not.  Somebody\\\'s going to open this curtain any minute.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Alright.  This money\\\'s burning a hole in my pocket, anyway.\\\"</P><P>The casino was packed.  They had to wait for a spot to open up at the twenty-one tables.  When one finally did, they decided the young woman would take it.  The young man took a wad that would choke a goat out of his pocket and handed her some bills.</P><P>She made small bets, judicious and logical.  In half an hour, she had doubled her money.  They took a break and went for a walk along the edge of the water.  The stars were out and the moon was new and there were stars in the water.</P><P>When they went back in, the only spots open were at the high stakes table, where there was only one player.  They conferred on it and sat down.  The minimum bet was one hundred thousand lire.  The young man gave the young woman half the money he had on hand, and they each put ten million lire, more or less, on the felt in front of them.  The dealer counted each pile in turn and gave them stacks of grey and black chips.</P><P>On the first hand, the young man bet two million, while the young woman bet the minimum.  She got a king and a ten against the dealer\\\'s queen.  The young man, who had a six and a five, decided to double down and placed another two million behind his original bet.  He received a two.  He could not hit for additional cards.  The portly man in the far left chair busted, and the dealer turned over his hole card to reveal an eight.  He paid the young woman and put the young man\\\'s chips in his rack.</P><P>On the next hand, they stuck to their original strategy and bet a hundred thousand per hand.  When they won, they made their next bet the minimum, as well.  When they lost, they bet twice as much on the next hand.  The young man started smoking, ordering a pack of Gauloises from the cocktail waitress.  The room was smoky and loud.  A fight broke out at the roulette wheel.  They alternated white wine with coffee.  They took a break every hour, leaving their chips at the table and stretching their legs along the waterfront.  When they quit playing at three in the morning, they had won more than sixty million lire.</P><P>In the gondola on the way back to the hotel, they kissed madly.  He slipped her panties and hose down around her thighs and ran his fingertips lightly over her vulva.  He looked up and saw the gondolier grinning.  He didn\\\'t care.  He eased her lips apart and rubbed her clit with his middle finger.  She leaned into him, stifling her gasps against his shoulder.  He eased two, then three fingers into her and pumped them in and out.</P><P>\\\"God, you\\\'re so wet,\\\" he said.</P><P>\\\"Stop, stop.  I\\\'m going to come.\\\"</P><P>She pulled up her panties and arranged her skirt over her crossed legs.  The gondolier was looking the other way.</P><P>The hotel lobby was dark, lit by dim electric bulbs high above.  Holding hands, they went upstairs.  One of the doors they passed on the way to their room was open.  There was a man inside sitting up in bed, drinking from a bottle of what looked like Grappa.</P><P>The young man unlocked their room and carried the young woman across the threshold.  He laid her on the bed and turned on a lamp and turned to lock the door.</P><BR><BR><BR>Chapter 24<BR><BR><BR><I><P>The little old man was standing by the courthouse, at the corner of Main and Jefferson, on a Friday night around midnight.  The sky was cloudy, and the air was hot and humid.</P><P>Cars and pickup trucks stopped at the traffic light then continued on their way.  A white Bronco lit up by an interior purple light approached the intersection.  That looks like an aquarium on wheels, he thought.  A young man leaned out the passenger-side window.</P><P>\\\"Hey, there,\\\" the young man said.</P><P>It was the boy he\\\'d met at the bank.  He opened his door.</P><P>\\\"I\\\'m gettin\\\' out here, Bert,\\\" he said.</P><P>\\\"OK, Bubba.  Whatever.\\\"</P><P>He hopped out and shut the door.  The Bronco turned right, leaving them alone at the intersection.  The old man and young man shook hands.</P><P>\\\"What are you doing out here at this time of night?\\\" the young man asked.</P><P>\\\"I\\\'m out here every night.  You\\\'ve never seen me before?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I might\\\'ve.  I guess I just didn\\\'t know you before.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  Before, I was just another old guy wandering around in the middle of the night.\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, I didn\\\'t mean it that way.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I know.  I\\\'m just kidding.  So, what\\\'re you doing for the rest of the night?  You want to go to Vinyl\\\'s and get a beer?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'m not old enough.\\\"</P><P>\\\"How old are you?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Twenty.\\\"</P><P>\\\"And that\\\'s not old enough to drink?  Jesus.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You have to be twenty-one.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You just come with me.  We\\\'ll see what happens.\\\"</P><P>They walked past the courthouse, down to Main and Broad, and waited for the light to change.  Cars and pickup trucks whizzed by.</P><P>\\\"So what\\\'ve you been doing tonight?\\\" the old man asked.</P><P>\\\"Burnin\\\' the road up lookin\\\' for women.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Didja find any?\\\"</P><P>\\\"We were talkin\\\' to a couple of \\\'em at McDonald\\\'s.  We were on our way to meet \\\'em at this party outside of town.\\\"</P><P>\\\"And you got out to talk to me?  You\\\'re not too damn bright, are you?\\\"</P><P>There were three men sitting inside Vinyl\\\'s.  One of them was Vinyl Lewis, the owner and bartender.  Forty-five years old, he dressed like Mister Disco Gigolo of Nineteen Seventy-Seven.  Ed was on the stool next to him.  Ed was alright as long as he stuck to beer.  On the occaisons when he made the switch to liquor, Vinyl made sure to stay within reach of the baseball bat hidden behind the bar.  A taste of wood upside the head cured a lot of things.  And in the \\\"party room,\\\" Bud sat in a booth nursing a cocktail and reading <U>Up Above the World</U>.</P><P>The old man came in with the young man in tow and greeted Ed and Vinyl.  He ordered two Rolling Rocks.</P><P>\\\"Can you get this?\\\" he asked the young man.  \\\"I don\\\'t have any money on me.\\\"</P><P>They took the beers to Bud\\\'s table.  He ignored them until he finished the page he was on.</P><P>\\\"Sit down, sit down,\\\" he told them.  \\\"Either that, or we could all take our drinks into the men\\\'s room and get drunk in there.\\\"  Bud was crazy.  Walking-around crazy.  \\\"Who\\\'s this?  Who\\\'s this?\\\" he asked.  \\\"A schoolteacher come to fit my new bones?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No,\\\" the old man said.  \\\"This is my friend Matt.  He\\\'s writing a book.\\\"</P><P>\\\"What\\\'s it about?\\\" Bud asked.  \\\"Three honkies sitting around the bar on a Friday night?\\\"</P><P>Matt:  No, it\\\'s about a writer and his wife on their honeymoon.</P><P>Bud:  A novel about a writer?  Hasn\\\'t that been done before?</P><P>Matt:  I\\\'m sure it has.  But maybe I\\\'ll do it better.  Maybe I\\\'ll do it differently.</P><P>Bud:  Oh, yes?  And how will you do it differently?</P><P>Matt:  For one thing, the writer in the story\\\'s also writing a novel.</P><P>Bud:  Oh, yeah?  What\\\'s <U>his</U> book about?</P><P>Matt:  Three honkies sitting around the bar on a Friday night.</P><P>Bud:  Oooh, I love a smartass.  No.  Really.</P><P>Matt:  It\\\'s about a writer living in a dystopia.</P><P>Bud:  Oh, OK.  A series of nested boxes.  Is <U>he</U> writing a book, too?</P><P>Matt:  No, he\\\'s blocked.  End of the line.</P><P>Bud:  What a pity.  It could have gone on forever.</P><P>They drank slowly until the bar closed, then went to the old man\\\'s apartment and sat around talking and sipping Scotch for a couple more hours.  Somewhere in the world at that moment, there were women playing che