k, he walked his fingers across the vinyl cushion between them and touched her thigh.  She grabbed his hand and slapped at it playfully.</P><P>After the show was over, Danielle excused herself.  Matt said, \\\"There\\\'s something in my room I want to show you.\\\"  They walked past the nurses\\\' station.  There was nobody in there.  He shut the door of his room behind them.  They stood in the middle of the room beside the bed.</P><P>\\\"Can you guess what I want to show you?\\\" he asked.</P><P>\\\"I think I can guess.  Is it something long?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Long enough.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Is it something thick?\\\"  She touched his crotch and found his dick through the fabric of his gym shorts and underwear.  She put her fingers around it and started pumping.</P><P>\\\"Pretty thick,\\\" she said.</P><P>\\\"It gets thicker than that.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I bet.  Longer, too, I bet.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Let\\\'s go into the bathroom.  Kenny might conduct one of his famous room checks.\\\"</P><P>They went in there and locked the door.  A nurse with a pass key might come along, but it was unlikely.  He kissed her, and they felt each other\\\'s bodies.  He started to lift up the skirts of her gowns, and she stopped him.  \\\"Not yet,\\\" she said.</P><P>She took off his T-shirt.  \\\"You have a really nice body,\\\" she said.  She rubbed his chest and stomach muscles then pulled down his shorts and underwear.  He stepped out of them.  She took his dick between her palms and rolled it back and forth.</P><P>It was in her mouth.  She was sucking on the head.  She took it all into her mouth and swallowed a good three inches of it into her throat.  She had to turn her head sideways to do it.  \\\"So this is what they mean by \\\'being eaten,\\\'\\\" he thought.  He had a propulsive orgasm that made his knees buckle.</P><P>Afterwards, he took off her gowns and sucked her eraser-sized nipples.  She pulled the crotch of her panties to one side, and he fucked her on the floor, the gowns spread beneath her.  He pulled out at the end and came on her tits.</P><P>That evening, there was a phone call in the dayroom for him.</P><P>\\\"Hello,\\\" he said.</P><P>\\\"Hello, yourself.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Hey, what are you doin\\\'?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Nothing.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You\\\'re up late, aren\\\'t you?  What time is it over there?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Four-thirty in the morning.  I\\\'ve been staying up late lately.\\\"</P><P>They talked for a while more of inconsequential things, then</P><P>\\\"How much money do you have saved up?\\\" he asked.</P><P>\\\"Not much.  My rent\\\'s pretty steep.  What about you?</P><P>\\\"Not much, either.  Maybe around five hundred bucks.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Not enough yet.\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, not enough.\\\"</P><P>A man who called himself \\\"Elvis Priestly\\\" came into the room.  He was wearing a two foot-tall hat he\\\'d made for himself out of posterboard.  It was decorated with Bible verses and vague admonitions.  He wanted to use the phone, so they wrapped it up.</P>'),(3184,'<P><B>AND THEY WERE MAGNIFICENT</B></P><P>that was when they all lived in the hotel in oranjestad.  the coins were square and they were all very lazy.  around three in the afternoon, the excursion boat came in and the captain came into the hotel for a drink.</P><P>she was always on the beach with her top off.  good for her, he thought.  it turned out english was the third language she learned.</P><P>she was always down there by the slot machine whistling the beatles.  he told jake to ask her if he could buy her a drink.  they both liked \\\"raising arizona.\\\"</P><P>at the airport in merida, they were both strip-searched.  there was a bus.  there was a train.  then they were in the federal district.</P><P><B>PLUS ONE MINUS ONE</B></P><P>we stayed in mexico city until she got pregnant.  she had the abortion in pensacola.</P><P>we went to a.a. for a month in san miguel, then went to new orleans and broke the fast.  we made a pilgrimage to the heineken brewery in amsterdam.  we both got homesick around the same time.</P><P>it was at or around this time that i started writing.  i saved the pages until we went to brooksville, then put them in the safety deposit box.  it was just like this.</P><P><B>NESTING BEHAVIORS</B></P><P>the landlady was czech.  it was a triplex, the landlady\\\'s family living in the house and the landlady and the tenant in the apartments.  we used to say that she came in and tried on our clothes when we were gone.</P><P>the triplex had a courtyard in the middle.  we used to go out there and sit with the potted plants.  nobody ever bothered us.</P><P>the money came in every month from her parents.  we used to go down to the western union on the fifteenth every month, no matter where we were.  we stayed in the apartment for a long time, though.</P><P><B>THE CYCLE OF DAYS</B></P><P>they stayed there in new port richey until anna\\\'s parents cut her off.  sad days came.  with what they had, they made the rent with a little left over.</P><P>the electricity went first.  then the phone.  then they had a yard sale and were rid of the tv, the dvd player, the computer, the stereo, the cds and dvds and videos.  they hung onto the vinyl, even though there was nothing to play it on, and their clothes.  then the rent came due again.</P>  '),(3185,'<p>it\\\'s cold here. it\\\'s not freezing cold, like they say it is in the upstate, where they had all the ice storms. it took some people a week to get power again.</p><p>\r\nit\\\'s colder near to the floor. i will do anything to keep from studying. the exam is tomorrow. i will do anything not to study. i smoke pot, i listen to music, i play the guitar, i play video games, i try to watch a movie. i thought that when i got a DVD player, i would be entertained more often. my life would be fuller; there would be more to do. not so!</p><p>\r\nthe DVD player has become a black hole in my life. i can\\\'t watch any of my movies again at all, even on drugs. i have lots of them, too, at least 15. that seems like a lot. the only way i can watch one is to have someone else over, who hasn\\\'t seen it. for some reason, that makes it like new again.</p><p>\r\nanything. i would rather do anything than study.</p><p>\r\ndo you want to know what my home is like? it is in a large brick apartment building, one of a pair. my building, the one with the fire escape on the back, is much less attractive than the other one. together, they share a driveway that goes down between them, into a parking lot. they are two stories high in the front, and three stories high in the back.</p><p>\r\nmy apartment is on the 3rd floor, at the back. it has wooden floors. it has a lot of windows. too many, in the morning sometimes. there are clean clothes in the den, dirty clothes in the bedroom, and junk in the kitchen. there are some cool things to play with, but they get old. sometimes people come over, but not much.</p><p>\r\nhow about my life? i go to college. i have two more exams. one is tomorrow. it\\\'s the hard one. better go study. '),(3186,'<p>\r\nThe two middle-school girls were run over and killed by a U.S. armored vehicle June 13 on a narrow country road north of Seoul on their way home from school.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"The United States Forces Korea (USFK) should waive its criminal jurisdiction over the two servicemen,\\\" said the coalition comprising 150 local civic organizations in a news conference in front of the U.S. Embassy in central Seoul. \\\"Despite strong demand from Koreans, the U.S. military has kept mum on the issue.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe soldiers were not handed over to korean justice. instead they were tried in a u.s military court on one of the many installations around south korea. the jury was all male. it screemed injustice...for the koreans. Bush did not formally apologize, and the u.s state department just ignored the korean lobby.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe soldiers charged with the accidental murders have all been aquitted. One lawyer blamed the deaths on faulty communications equipment in the armored vehicle. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsince the aquittals, koreans have been very vocal in their anger towards the american military and its agreement with the korean government(SOFA). \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas generations pass, the memory of american soldiers defending south korea from the communist hoards of the north have slowly disappeared. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto many younger koreans, the american soldiers stationed here are rude, drunk, and problematic. \r\nsince the trials, some shopping areas in seoul have put up signs stating that americans are not welcome. in a popular club district signs with the same message state that american soldiers are not welcome. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey are fed up with what they call the american injustice system. In nations with u.s military personel stationed, it is easy to see those double standards.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn spite of all this anti-american sentiment, koreans are still eating up american culture. mcdonalds and burger king are still popular, and kfc and popeyes still seem packed everytime i pass one. There are starbucks and seattle coffee company outlets everywhere. american music, movies, tv, and products are adored. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlast weekend I went to the book store. The book store i goto is really close to the u.s embassy and on the day i picked to go get some books, a massive protest was being held. when i stepped out of the subway station I met wall after wall of korean police baracades. There were thousands of protesters listening to men on loudspeakers and what caught my eye was how many of them were holding starbucks cups. I laughed to myself and pictured anarchists going to mcdonalds for a number three value meal right before heading up to seattle for some ruckus. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat really annoys me about this whole anti-american attitude growing here in korea is that they assume that all white men and women are american. THEN, when they want to send their kids to an english academy to learn english they want american teachers. There is a double standard on both sides. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nkorea is strange.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3187,'<P>\\\"you\\\'re a strange man.\\\"</P><P>\\\"why?  why i gotta be strange?\\\"</P><P>\\\"you don\\\'t fall asleep after i\\\'m done sexing you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"you sexing me?  i thought i was sexin\\\' you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, no, little man.\\\"</P><P>the streetlight was throwing soft coral shadows through the blinds.  she traced the curve of his ear with her fingertip.</P><P>\\\"listen to this dream i had,\\\" he said.  \\\"i just remembered it.\\\"</P><P>\\\"shoot.\\\"</P><P>\\\"it was me and perry farrell and about forty women riding in the back of this semi truck.  everybody was naked, but there wasn\\\'t anything going on.\\\"</P><P>\\\"you better tell those truck bitches to leave you alone.\\\"</P><P>\\\"yeah.  welp, i gotta pee.\\\"</P><P>he got up and went into the bathroom.  the landlady paid the water bill out of her end, so that was good.  there was a cold shower, if you wanted one.  and you had to really want one.  he did his business with the door open.</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m a man,\\\" he thought.  \\\"i pee standing up.  hear my song.\\\"</P><P>he sat his bare ass on the sofa, lit a candle and started breaking up weed.  she wandered in with an acoustic guitar and parked it in the armchair.</P><P>\\\"play me somethin\\\', mommy.\\\"</P><P>she began strumming angular blues chords.  a breast peeked over the top of the guitar.  he rolled a joint and lit it off the candle.</P><P>\\\"what time\\\'s howard coming?\\\" she asked.</P><P>\\\"two o\\\'clock.\\\"</P><P>howard was buying their vinyl for a dollar an album.</P><P>\\\"how much will that leave us with?\\\" he asked.</P><P>\\\"after we get the money from howard, we\\\'ll have eight fifty.\\\"</P><P>\\\"ok, so that\\\'s this month and next month.  if we don\\\'t eat.  what else we got to sell?\\\"</P><P>\\\"there\\\'s the books.  the clothes.  and my earrings.</P><P>he indicated the guitar with his chin.</P><P>\\\"oh no, motherfucker.  it\\\'ll be a cold day before i sell this.\\\"</P><P>\\\"so with your earrings, that\\\'s a good three months.  rent, food, weed, cigarrettes, and candles.  anything else i\\\'m forgetting?\\\"</P><P>\\\"beer.  soap.  tampons.\\\"</P><P>\\\"we\\\'ll have to cut back on the beer.\\\"</P><P>\\\"we can try.\\\"</P><P>\\\"what happens once the three months are up?\\\"</P><P>\\\"you could get a job.\\\"</P><P>\\\"oh yeah?  why don\\\'t <B>you</B> get a job?\\\"</P><P>\\\"doing what?\\\"</P><P>he shrugged.</P><P>\\\"ok. three months.  we\\\'ll have to leave after that.  there\\\'s an abandoned house over on hayman road, back in the woods.  we could stay there.  we\\\'d have to pack water in, though.\\\"</P><P>\\\"and how will we eat?\\\"</P><P>\\\"the only other thing i can think of is to sell what we can and leave instead of paying the rent next week.  howzabout that?\\\"</P><P>\\\"i don\\\'t know.  ask me tomorrow.\\\"</P><P>\\\"ok,\\\" he said.  \\\"let\\\'s try to get some sleep.\\\"</P>'),(3188,'<p>At 5:06, as usual, a man he knows only as \\\"Slim\\\" trips through the door and walks a worn path to the second barstool from the right. Slim is one of those men who lost his shape slowly and carelessly. So slowly that he never noticed the tightening of his clothing. So carelessly he is unaware of how his gut hangs out below his shirt and over his pants. When he sits, his ass bulges dangerously over the edge of the barstool. \\\"Slim.\\\" Santiago says in greeting. \\\"Lookin\\\' good!\\\" Slim says and gives a thumbs up sign. He has does this at approximately 5:07 on every day that Santiago has tended bar at The Midnight Club.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSlim orders a Seven and 7 on the rocks and attempts to make conversation for a while before he gives up and fishes through the lint in his pockets for quarters. When he\\\'s found a few dollar\\\'s worth he sways over to the jukebox and punches in his favorite songs. He doesn\\\'t need to look at the song lists, he knows his favorites by heart. As he resumes his perch he begins to mouth the words to Stop Dragging My Heart Around.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe door opens again before the first chorus and Santiago is relieved to see two young couples enter the bar. He can avoid conversation with Slim if he\\\'s lucky. He smiles and nods them into the room. The men stand at the bar. The girls stay back, hiding in the haze near the booths. They are obviously underage, but it\\\'s early, and he knows how well the men will tip if he doesn\\\'t make any trouble. He sighs and takes orders. The guys just want beer. The women ask for sweet syrupy drinks that taste more like Kool Aide than liquor. As he makes the drinks he tells himself that the girls were corrupted long before they ever saw his face.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSlim stares uneasily at the men. When they lean toward each other to make a private joke about their dates, he swivels his head and looks the girls up and down. Slim catches Santiago\\\'s eye and grins. His yellow teeth shine with bourbon. He gives a wink to make sure his lewd thoughts are clear. Santiago winces. He steals a glance at the men to make sure they\\\'re occupied with their conversation and waters the down the drinks to ease his conscience.</p>\r\n'),(3189,'<tt>Look out over the<br>\r\nFDR Parkway, Southbound;<br>\r\nRemnants of pileup<br><br>\r\n\r\nscattered, remaining.<br>\r\nOnly eighteen smashed hubcaps,<br>\r\nmetaflake red paint<br><br>\r\n\r\nand burned tire-marks on<br>\r\nthe cement median there.<br>\r\nIrridescent oil<br><br>\r\n\r\noozing into the<br>\r\nEast River, shattered shards that<br>\r\ntwinkle, safety glass.<br><br></tt>'),(3190,'<i>not particularly emotional or action packed, but necessary.</i>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMarshall wondered sometimes what Wally Rafferty, the priest that taught him to box, would have thought of the unreasoning euphoria that seemed to have seized the city of San Francisco as the century drew to a close. In the space of just a few years the soul of the city had seemed to turn; the beats and the hippies, the poets and musicians, remnants of a time when the eleven mystic hills sheltered creative enclaves from the ravages of war, had been replaced by new civic archetypes: the techno-anarchist, the venture capitalist, the dotcommer. Expectations had changed. Girls and boys fresh out of college, who once wouldâ€™ve worked for free, for experience, now could be heard complaining over ten dollar Belgian beers that their first jobs were not paying them more than what their parents earned after twenty-five years of effort. Vast fortunes were made overnight as the rest of the country, shut out of the action by location and circumstance, tried desperately to buy in to the dream. Affluence was everywhere; money seemed free, like manna falling from the sky. But the young turks of cyberspace didnâ€™t regard themselves as particularly blessed; their decisions were based on obvious trends. To them, everyone who wasnâ€™t getting rich was either blind, stupid, or lazy. No one ever stopped to think how odd it was that an entire generation seemed to assume they wouldnâ€™t have to work past the age of thirty.\r\n<p>\r\nFather Rafferty, he thought, wouldâ€™ve shaken his head in disgust. â€œOnly assholes act on expectations,â€ he told Marshall, more than once. The priest was a squat figure with a pugâ€™s square, flattened nose, a halo of white hair, and his Roman collar flapping loose as he hunted Marshall around the ring. His words, punctuated by light, unerring jabs, were clearly enunicated; Father Rafferty had been a Golden Gloves champion in his South Boston youth, and didnâ€™t bother with speech impediments like headgear and mouthguards, at least for the likes of young Marshall. In his day, he said, theyâ€™d slid a bit of orange peel between  their lips and front teeth and considered themselves well-armored. He taught English and boxing to the boys of Our Lady of Infinite Forebearance Parochial High School, and even near sixty he still glided across the canvas like a dancer in top hat and tails. \r\n<p>\r\nMarshall slipped to the right, trying to avoid another nose-stinging jab, and instead Rafferty nailed him with a short, stiff left hook to the temple. Marshall saw a burst of electric blue sparks in a field of black and sagged against the ropes.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œHey! Are you ok?â€ Raffertyâ€™s brilliant white dentures tugged at the laces and he pulled his gloves off. He held up his hand, nails stained nicotine yellow. â€œMarshall. How many fingers?â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œFour.â€\r\n<p>\r\nRafferty folded his index and middle fingers back to his palm and shrugged. â€œClose enough. Now listen to me.â€ He reached into the breast pocket of his black shirt and pulled out a crumpled pack of Pall Malls. He fixed his collar with one hand, gesturing with an unlit cigarette in the other. â€œNow, see, thatâ€™s exactly what Iâ€™m talking about. You acted on your expectations, and you got knocked down. Donâ€™t anticipate, goddammit! A good boxer sees you doing that, heâ€™ll play you like Neroâ€™s fiddle. Just stay ready.â€ He jammed the cigarette in his mouth, pulled Marshallâ€™s headgear off, and dabbed blood from his nose with a towel, mumbling around the filter. â€œDonâ€™t try to guess whatâ€™s coming. Donâ€™t try to be clever. Stay humble, and react. And you gotta keep in balance so you can work off of whatever comes your way. Remember, every time someone throws something at you, thatâ€™s an opening. You gotta jump on it, cause it might not come again. Itâ€™s the same in here or out in the world. Expectation has a way of undoing you, because God doesnâ€™t like a smart ass. He loves confounding expectations. Remember that, Marshall, and youâ€™ll do fine.â€\r\n<p>\r\nRafferty wouldâ€™ve laughed long and hard at Marshallâ€™s friends and coworkers, all splayed out on oversized primary colored beanbag chairs and waiting patiently for the Huffer to arrive, because expectation was the very air they breathed. Every one of them knew someone who had become a sudden millionaire as a result of the investing publicâ€™s insatiable desire for all things technological, and every one of them presumed he would be next. All the world wanted to own a slice of the companies that were building the future. It didnâ€™t matter what the product was, if it generated profit, or if it even existed. People were hurling money at anyone who offered them the slimmest chance of drinking from Fortuneâ€™s tit. And the employees of Bazoomba.com knew that their product had the advantage of being made in the image of a man that for millions of people was the very face of the far-flung future. \r\n<p>\r\nâ€œThe thing is,â€ Bianca said, lounging on a giant vinyl lozenge, â€œit doesnâ€™t even really matter what the market penetration of Bazoomba really is. When the IPO goes through, itâ€™s the Captain people will be investing in. Not the application. Half of the investors will probably think theyâ€™re buying shares in an actual starship.â€\r\n<p>\r\nThey both had stock options that numbered in the tens of thousands.  Marshall said, â€œWeâ€™re going to be rich.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œYeah.â€ She shook her head in wonder. â€œWeâ€™re going to be fucking rich.â€ <p>\r\nMarshall had come to programming out of a simple desire to escape the lowest rung of the economic food chain. In and out of offices all day, envelopes in his bag, bike cleats clicking in marble lobbies, he had grown weary of being treated like a simple pack mule. Casting about for some new direction, he had bought an old PC to dabble on, but never accomplished much, having only the vaguest idea of its uses and too lulled by the comfort of his home life to really apply himself. But when Tsingtao left him, he saw it in a whole new light. What had seemed a life of romantic deprivation when it was the two of them togetherâ€”delivering packages, living in ghettos--seemed like a menial, dead-end existence without her, and the machine offered his one chance for escape. He spent two weeksâ€™ food money on a couple of teach-yourself-to-program books and shut himself away. Disappointed by religion and then by love, Marshall turned to work for solace. He was bemused to find, sunk deep in his beanbag chair a few years later, that in his desperation he seemed to have stumbled upon a career. Money had come, more than he had ever imagined, but nothing he had acquired had made up for what he had lost. <p>\r\nFor Bianca it was different. Programming was her art, and when she was working, she had more in common with Marshallâ€™s mystics than she wouldâ€™ve cared to admit. Presented with a problem no one else could solve, she would lock herself in a room for days, sunk in yogic concentration, and emerge with a solution so elegant that people tore bits from the printouts to carry as holy relics. For her a good piece of code was a thing of beauty, a Faberge egg of pure utility, and it pained her to hand over her little wonders to grinning businessmen with lacquered hair and greedy eyes. In her heart of hearts she hoped that someday schoolchildren would take field trips to see her carefully loomed strings of code, hanging in frames on white museum walls. <p>\r\nNo one knew why they had been summoned that morning, but the email had been accompanied by three red flagsâ€”the highest level of urgency. Three giant video screens had been wheeled into the conference room, and everyone had automatically arrayed themselves in front of the dark screens, like petitioners before an oracle. Rumors were rippling through the ranks. They were going to IPO early. The worldâ€™s largest software company was set to acquire them. The music television station wanted to do a reality show based on their company, but it would require everyone to live in the office, locked away from the real world and eating nothing but sugared cereal and beef jerky for eight weeks. The tight labor market was forcing the company to bring in trained monkeys to fill low level coding positions. The room buzzed with excited whispers. <p>\r\nâ€œI passed the Huffer in the hallway,â€ Bianca said. â€œSomethingâ€™s definitely got him in an uproar. He sounded like he was breathing through a clown whistle.â€ <p>\r\nThe Huffer was technically Biancaâ€™s boss, but everyone knew he relied completely on her technical expertise and corporate experience. Only twenty-six years old, Bianca already held a masterâ€™s degree in computer science and had been technical lead on two multi-million dollar projects. Prior to being named the Chief of Operations for Bazoomba.com, which was backed by twenty million dollars in venture capital, the largest company the Huffer had ever managed was the Kill Zone Arcade on the boardwalk in Santa Cruz, which was where the Captain had found him. <p>\r\nA Spacequest convention had drawn thousands of fanboys to the little seaside town for a three day orgy of pizza, sugared caffeine, and playing dress-up. The Captain, fresh from his third stay at the Tabula Rasa Recovery Ranch, had graciously accepted an invitation to speakâ€”for a five thousand dollar fee. As president of the local chapter of the Spacequest fanclub, the Huffer had enjoyed the privilege of introducing the Captain from the proscenium. The Huffer broke out in goosebumps as he watched the Captain bound up the steps to the stage and give the Bazoomba salute; he thought he had reached the zenith of happiness. But it was nothing compared to the paroxysm of joy he felt soon after, when the Captainâ€™s agent invited him for a personal audience. <p>\r\n	As he followed the Captainâ€™s agent backstage the Huffer began to feel light-headed. The agent knocked smartly on the dressing room door and then helped him to a chair that faced the great man himself, who was pouring over a macrobiotic cookbook. The Huffer sat alone, corpse-pale and sweaty, awaiting the Captainâ€™s attention. His lightheadedness was getting worse; he was almost swooning in his chair, and the world seemed to have drifted to the far end of a black tunnel. In fact, his airway had tightened up so much that the level of oxygen reaching the Hufferâ€™s brain was roughly equivalent to that found at the summit of one of the junior peaks in the Himalayas. In the Captainâ€™s presence, the Huffer managed to contract severe altitude sickness while still at sea level. Finally the Captain looked up and fixed him with his trademark single-arched-eyebrow stare. <p>\r\n	â€œWe hear you manage a hell of an arcade,â€ the Captain said. When the Huffer couldnâ€™t manage a reply, he went on. â€œWeâ€™ve also heard you were invaluable in organizing this terrific event. We were wondering if you might be interested in a new project weâ€™re undertaking.â€ <p>\r\nThe Captain gave him a mission. When the Huffer emerged from his meeting he was a changed man, like Moses with ashen traces of shrubbery on his robe. He had the hard, glistening eyes of a religious zealot, and the ramrod posture of a military cadet. It lasted for about a week. When the Huffer came through the conference room door that morning, brandishing an unlabeled black VHS tape over his head, he had long since reverted to his usual appearance. His caustically unfashionable combover seeded the slopes of his shoulders with dandruff, and his Members Only jacket was worn with no ironic intent. On the pocket there was a Bazoomba space academy brooch with two fingertip-sized marks worn in the gold plating--evidence that the Huffer made repeated attempts to report to an imaginary starship orbiting above. He took a surreptitious hit off his inhaler and turned to face the assembled employees. <p>\r\nâ€œI have here,â€ he said, â€œa personal message from the Captain to each and every one of you, delivered this morning by courier.â€ <p>\r\nThe room darkened and the three giant video monitors flickered to life, blue ready screens giving way to the unpopulated bridge of the USSS Bazoomba. The Captainâ€™s command chair sat empty at center stage, and one hundred eyes stared at the black vinyl seat of five seasonsâ€™ worth of intergalactic adventure. Stared and waited. The camera lingered and no one arrived. Thirty seconds stretched to one minute. Two. Silence thickened the air. Someone coughed. Emotional barometers dropped dangerously low as the room filled with the vacuum of anticipation, promising a storm that was released when, onscreen, the bridge doors slid open and the Captain emerged. <p>\r\nThe Huffer shot to his feet and screamed, â€œAtten-<I>hut!</I> Officer on deck!â€ The assembled employees stood and cheered, drowning out the Spacequest theme music that thundered from theater speakers mounted around the room. Bianca and Marshall followed more slowly, giving each other sardonic smiles. But bad attitudes notwithstanding, they stood with everyone else until the Captain settled himself into his familiar arrogant slouch and said, â€œAt ease, crew.â€ <p>\r\nThe Captain cut an imposing figure, even thirty years past his prime. His preternaturally thick mane was still lush and glossy, but gone metallic, as if his head had been chrome plated. And though the gut that had once been incipient had now grown pronounced, his shoulders were still wide enough that he had to turn sideways to fit through most doors. His shirt was cut to resemble his familiar officerâ€™s tunic, wrapped around the front and tied at the side. He voice was invested with all the authority of thirty years of daily reruns in every major metropolitan market as he leaned forward and said, <p>\r\nâ€œLadies and gentlemen, it is indeed an honor and a privilege to address such an accomplished and hardworking group. I will keep this as brief as I can, so that you can return to your mission critical tasks as quickly as possible. As most of you are aware, our efforts to develop a revolutionary new way to experience the internet have been supported in large part by our friends at the Nikkoru keiretsu. Mr Feng Shan of Nikkoru Corp has communicated to me that his superiors in the keiretsu are so enthusiastic about the Bazoomba Holographic Flight Deck that they have grown somewhatâ€¦ anxiousâ€¦to see a working version. So much so that Mr Feng Shan himself came to visit me at my home in Palos Verdes this week and asked to see a demonstration.â€ <p>\r\nThere was an anxious rumble in the crowd, an uncomfortable shifting, as though the beanbags had grown hard as church pews. <p>\r\nâ€œI reminded him that the scheduled completion date for the project was not until June of the coming year, and that the Flight Deck, due to a feature overhaul, was not currently in a compilable state. Mr Feng Shan then informed me, with much regret, that if we were not able to supply a fully featured, shippable version of the Holographic Flight Deck by the first of February, they will withdraw their support from our company.â€  <p>\r\nBianca seized Marshallâ€™s arm. They stared at each other, stricken. <p>\r\nOn screen, the Captain leapt from his chair, fists clenched, his agility belying his bulk. â€œOf course, Iâ€¦ protestedâ€¦ to Mr Feng Shan that this deadline would beâ€¦ quite impossible. After delicate, protracted negotiations, and an emergency video conference involving myself and senior officers of the Nikkoru Corp, Mr Feng Shan was authorized to make the following compromise: Bazoomba.com will unveil an alpha version of the Holographic Flight Deck at the Fairmont Hotel in San Francisco on February 1st of next year, followed by the release of a fully featured gold mastered version on the first of March. At 6 AM Pacific Standard Time, March the second, Bazoomba.com will make four million shares available in an Initial Public Offering on the New York Stock Exchange. After conferring with Dennis Norquist, our Chief of Operations, I gave the honorable Mr Feng Shan my word of honor that we would meet this deadline.â€ <p>\r\nThe Captainâ€™s final words were lost in the pandemonium that followed. The average blood pressure in the room doubled. Yellow acid poured into twenty-three year old stomachs already perforated with ulcers. Bianca shook her head and mimed putting a gun in her mouth. Marshall could only nod. In the dull roar of worried voices, he was sure he heard Raffertyâ€™s wheezing laughter. <p>\r\nâ€œIâ€™m going to kill him, I swear to god,â€ Bianca said. She snarled and swore in frustration, and for a moment Marshall thought she actually looked capable of homicide. â€œThat goddamned Huffer. How could he do this to us? I know heâ€™s an utter incompetent, but havenâ€™t I taught him anything? Eight weeks, really. Good god.â€ She looked disgusted, then sighed in resignation. â€œWell, someoneâ€™s got to straighten this bullshit out.â€ <p>\r\nThey followed the crowd drifting out into the hallway, then hung back just outside the exit, watching their shellshocked coworkers file past. The Huffer emerged, flanked on either side by braying, pastel clad flacks from Marketing. Bianca grabbed Marshallâ€™s sleeve and tugged him close to whisper urgently in his ear. â€œListen, stick close to me. Hold me back if it looks like I might lose control and crack his head against the wall.â€ She moved to cut the Huffer off. â€œHey Dennis. Do you have a minute?â€ <p>\r\nThe Huffer blanched. â€œActually, I have an important conference callâ€”â€œ<p>\r\nâ€œOh good, this will only take a second.â€ Her fingers sank deeply in the doughy flesh of his upper arm as she steered him into an adjoining conference room and closed the door behind them. <p>\r\nâ€œDennisâ€¦â€ Her voice trailed off. She rubbed her eyes and pinched the bridge of her nose. â€œDennis, did it even occur to you to consult with me before you told the Captain we would have working code in eight fucking weeks?â€ <p>\r\nHe tried to answer but all that came out was a squeak like two balloons rubbed together. <p>\r\nBianca shook her head and put a hand up to forestall any further attempt at answering her rhetoric. â€œLetâ€™s imagine an alternate universe where you did. This is what my parallel self would have told you: â€˜No, Dennis, there is absolutely no way we can have working code by the first of February.â€™ Our burn date was in June, Dennis. Twenty-eight fun-filled, action packed, sixty-hour work weeks from nowâ€”and with all the new features the Captain has been demanding, the schedule was already starting to slip.What the Captain has ordered is simply impossible. You have to explain that to him.â€ <p>\r\nThe Huffer turned the color of cigarette ash. â€œI canâ€™t.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYou canâ€™t.â€ Deadpan disbelief. <p>\r\nâ€œThe board has already extended a formal invitation to the Nikkoru keiretsu to join us for the launch party at the Fairmont. They have the Captainâ€™s personal guarantee. If we changed the date it would cause an egregious loss of face. Our cash reserves will barely carry us through til the alpha date. Mr Feng Shan has made it plain that if we push the schedule out any further, the Nikkoru will withold the rest of our funding. If they withold our funding, there will be no IPO. Without the Nikkoru, this company will cease to exist. All the work weâ€™ve done will be for nothing.â€ <p>\r\nBianca took an involuntary step backwards and sagged against the wall. â€œOh, fuck,â€ she said. She covered her face with her hands, straining to make out some path to salvation in the bright glowing shapes that played against her closed eyelids. The she pushed herself upright and began to pace the perimeter of the room, rapping her knuckles on everything with a wood grain. â€œOkay. Okay. Thereâ€™s no way we can give him fully functioning code. No way. But maybe we can mock something up.â€ <p>\r\nThe Huffer, who stood watching her in a hand-wringing frenzy, brightened. â€œYeah?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah. Okay, firstâ€”total feature freeze. No more java doohickies, no smell-o-vision, no more flavor-of-the-week horseshit that the Captain saw in some trade magazine.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œBut the I-Smellâ€”â€œ<p>\r\nâ€œNo, Dennis! Itâ€™s not going to happen. Not if you donâ€™t want the Captain to walk onstage in eight weeks, click the little button, and watch nothing happen while our friends from Nikkoru Corp take out their knives and ask you which finger you want to lose.â€ <p>\r\nThe Huffer looked like he needed to find a bathroom immediately. Marshall stifled a grin behind his hand. <p>\r\nâ€œOk, second.â€ Bianca stepped close to him, and the Huffer, though a head taller, seemed to shrink in his skin.  â€œI get total control of hiring and firing. If I need someone, they are hired on the spot. No Human Resources approval required. And if someoneâ€™s dead weight, and I say they go, they go. Okay?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWellâ€¦â€ But it was obvious the Huffer was sloppy with gratitude. â€œOkay. Iâ€™ll clear it with the Captain.â€ <p>\r\nBack in the hallway, Bianca asked Marshall to be candid with her. â€œDo we have any chance of pulling this off?â€ <p>\r\nHe folded his hands into his armpits and shrugged his shoulders, grimacing. â€œMaybe. But itâ€™ll be close.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah.â€ She stopped him with a hand on his elbow. â€œWeâ€™re going to have to split the teams, you know. Are you going to be ok running the show on your side?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah, Iâ€™ll be fine.â€ <p>\r\nShe took his arm as they resumed their walk back towards the lobby. â€œIâ€™m glad I can count on you, Marshall. I canâ€™t pull this off by myself.â€ <p>\r\nHe grinned at her. â€œNo sweat, <I>mami.</I>â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOh, Marshall.â€ The receptionist, clad in the tight, off the shoulder jersey, knee high black boots, and thigh-slit miniskirt of a Bazoomba communications officer, reached behind her desk and pulled out a taped-up box. â€œThis came for you. By courier.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWhat is it?â€ <p>\r\nShe rolled her eyes at him. â€œI just sign for them. I donâ€™t X ray them.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWhat did he look like?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œWho?â€ she asked, exasperated. <p>\r\nâ€œThe messenger.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOh! He was tiny. And he had this slouchy wool hat tucked behind his pointy little ears. He looked just like one of those cookie elves.â€ <p>\r\nMarshall knew him. His name was Lâ€™il Pete, and they had been friends, years before, when they both rode for Mercury Messenger. Lâ€™il Pete had had a girlfriend that he loved with all his heart, even as she ground him to dust with her apothecaryâ€™s pestle of a tongue. When she had finally left him, Marshall had supported Lâ€™il Pete as best he could. He bought him beers, and picked him up off the street when he tried to lay in the path of an oncoming bus. But then Tsingtao had gone, too, and Marshall went spinning off into a black emotional void like a spacewalker with a severed tether. He hadnâ€™t spoken to Pete since. The sudden realization of how much he had missed his friend aged his gait twenty years as Marshall took the package back to his cubicle. <p>\r\nIt was from Wolf One Hour Photo. Collapsing in his office chair, he opened and up-ended the box. The snapshot of Marshall with Tsingtao and its framed enlargement slid out into his lap. And sharp as a slap, he felt once again his disappointment as Tsingtao broke their date for dinner. <p>\r\nIt had to happen at some point, but Marshall had been lulled into believingâ€”into the expectation, he admitted to Raffertyâ€™s ghostâ€”that their tradition was inviolable. The first time it had come into question, almost ten months after they had broken up, Marshall thought that perhaps their anniversary dinner might be set aside, because Tsingtao had recently started seeing a crayola-haired, concentration camp-skinny country western singer named Cowboy Bob. <p>\r\nMarshall hadnâ€™t expected to run into them. Heâ€™d been a virtual recluse for most of the year, staying home to forcefeed himself the abstruse concepts of object-oriented programming, but on the spur of the moment heâ€™d decided to abandon his hermetic life for a night of music. Heâ€™d paid the doorman, swept the hood of his sweatshirt back, and there they were, not ten feet in front of him. Cowboy Bob was standing with his arm around her, and she was laughing and talking with the knot of beautiful people that surrounded them. A slow knife of jealousy entered just below Marshallâ€™s ribs and worked its lazy way north. He forced himself to look away, face aflame, before she caught him staring. He turned back to the stage where roadies were preparing equipment for the band. The crowd was filling in and soon he was invisible, one more faceless soldier in standard-issue hipster uniform. Staring hard at the plumberâ€™s crack the soundman revealed as he stooped to adjust a kickdrum microphone, Marshall told himself he was glad she was happy, while his teeth ground themselves to powder. <p> \r\nThen he smelled vanilla, strawberries, and cigarettes. A thin arm threaded its way through his and Marshall realized he hadnâ€™t escaped notice after all. <p>\r\nTsingtao was all chain-smoking insouience. â€œWhatâ€™s a guy like you doing in a nice place like this?â€ <p>\r\nHe cleared his throat. â€œOh hey. I didnâ€™t know you were here.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œLiar,â€ she said, scanning the crowd of boys in spiked hair and wrist bands and girls in pigtails and baby t-shirts. â€œI saw you looking over. Why didnâ€™t you come say hi?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œI didnâ€™t want to cramp your style. You know.â€ <p>\r\nShe gave him a sidelong stare, the corner of her mouth hinting at a smile, then said,  â€œHey, check this out.â€ Her cigarette flared with neon intensity as she took a deep drag. Then she pursed her lips and puffed out a series of perfect little smoke hearts. â€œPretty neat, huh? Mickey Big Mouth taught me.â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah.â€ The hearts drifted to the ceiling and were shredded by a draft from the opening door. <p>\r\nâ€œSo,â€ she said. She squinted at him through the smoke. â€œWhat do you want to do for dinner on Thursday?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œThursday?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYeah. Thursday.â€ She cocked her head. â€œYou didnâ€™t forget, did you?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œOf course not.â€ His stomach churned with hope and doubt. â€œButâ€¦ is that going to be okay with Cowboy Bob?â€ <p>\r\nâ€œYou think Iâ€™m going to blow our anniversary dinner off for some dude I met a couple weeks ago? Any guy I date is going to have to accept the fact of you in my life, Marshall. If Cowboy Bob doesnâ€™t like it, thatâ€™s his problem.â€ <p>\r\nCowboy Bob hadnâ€™t liked it, but they had gone out for sushi anyway, and then to the Kilowatt where Marshall knocked back a double whiskey neat and Tsingtao had sucked it from his lips, her hand in his below the bar rail. Two weeks later, Cowboy Bob was back on the dusty trail, singing his high lonesome song. <p>\r\nTsingtao had dated a few other guys since Cowboy Bob, but she never seemed serious about any of them. The enormous tribal-scarred bicycle mechanic had only lasted a couple weeks, and so had the pot bellied, elvis-coiffed bartender at the 500 Club, and the alcoholic ex-Navy SEAL who, in a rage after Tsingtao dumped him, had put six men in traction before the police tasered him into submission. They had come and gone, but Marshall was still around, enjoying, he imagined, a special place in her heart. So he could be forgiven if he had entertained a few fantasies about his future with her. In fact, it wouldnâ€™t be inaccurate to say he had been simply biding his time, waiting for her to satisfy her wanderlust, waiting secure in the knowledge that he was the man she loved, and that she would realize it too, sooner or later. Why else would she be so faithful to their anniversary, to the celebration of their history, their bond? But if that was true, what did it mean that this year she had called to cancel at the last minute, and that Marshall was left sitting slumped in his chair, staring at the present he hadnâ€™t had a chance to give her? <p>\r\nHe glanced up at the poster that hung above his monitor. The Beasts of the Apocalypse gamboled and napped in the four corners, while giggling cherubim played tag above ranks of the seraphim, dancing skulls wrapped in layers of wings. A tiny tetragrammaton, the name of the Holy of Holies, dominated the center, and painterly sophistry had surrounded it with a glow too intense to look at directly for more than an instant. It would do. Marshall pulled it off the wall and began folding it carefully around the black bordered photograph. He didnâ€™t know what her cancellation meant, but he was going to give her her present anyway. He silently promised Father Rafferty he would try to stay ready, because for once he had no idea what to expect.\r\n<p>\r\n \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3191,'<p>She stands at the window.</p>\r\n<p>Where is Marco? The sun has set, the sky dark blue, the street is quiet â€“ Marcoâ€™s street. Everyone is his friend, thus hers. He helps water the big plants the grandmothers pull onto the curb on Sundays. Free coffee and fresh fruit every morning at the place on the corner. The old man downstairs brings them tomatoes from his garden. The street is a nice one. But where is Marco?</p>\r\n<p>She draws the curtains and sits by the telephone. Once Marco told someone that he wanted her. People thought it would be a good idea, everyone knew Marco. Then he came one afternoon to have sex with her. He did this often and she hated him at first. But Marco never lied and never treated her badly. She loves him.</p>\r\n<BR>\r\n<p>A big blue tarp. Inside the tarp is Marco. There are several deep holes in his body. No one will ever know.</p>\r\n<p>The man looks at the tarp for a while, then he looks at the marsh. The water is dark and quiet, he hasnâ€™t gone fishing in several years. He wonders a little bit about when his ride will get here, but mostly he thinks about the marsh and the fish inside.</p>\r\n<p>The man looks at his watch, then he thinks about the young girl heâ€™s been sleeping with. She is much younger than him and she has small body. He wonâ€™t have time to see her tonight. He thinks a little bit about what she will do instead. Probably the same thing with someone else. Thatâ€™s the way it goes.</p>\r\n<p>A white car pulls up.</p>\r\n<p>â€œEverything ready?â€ the driver asks.</p>\r\n<p>â€œYeah,â€ the man says, â€œLetâ€™s go home.â€</p>\r\n'),(3192,'<p>A blank page, a blank slate, the whiteness I tend to associate with purity. Maybe it\\\'s because I pour salt first in my curries and sugar in my baking, these are the elements that I start from, I crave them in their various forms:</p>\r\n\r\nwhite table salt for din-din,<br>\r\nsodium to clog my arteries,<br>\r\nwhite-powdered monosodium glutamate to kill the hunger,<br>\r\nrefined white sugar for tea,<br>\r\nsugar cubes before the dentist,<br>\r\nfructose in my fruitty chewing gum,<br>\r\nglucose before a jog,<br>\r\n\r\netc.<br> \r\n\r\n<p>White, flashes right into my eyes as they open into the waking consciousness, when light spills into the dark cavern of the mind. The pupils dialate and salty liquid stretches across the soft bulbous tissue.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I think of the salt water of oceans and maybe the mother that gave birth to me. I swam in  salty amniotic fluid at some point-- I am a damn child of the ocean. I am also a borderline hypoglycemic. I need sugar in my system to feel balanced. I once used to eat sugar from the bowl when I was young to sooth myself into sleep. \r\nI like my whites to stay clean and clear and unfettered. They are simple tastes with no smells or any other associations.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>When we finished dinner and laid in bed we drew with crayons across a page in my sketchbook. I wanted it to be filled with colour, so much heavy colour: reds were the soft kisses on nipples when she was pulsing with attraction, greens for when we twisted together like ivy, purple for her poor sense of colour -- she thought red delicious apples were a canadian misnomer, ochre was for some village in Italy that she talked of while I buried my face into her neck smelling the mustard mixture of our sex, blues were the same conclusions -- she had a boyfriend that made this all temporary.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A behaviorist once summed up my love life in a few choice words. \\\"The sex was good for you, but how was it for me?\\\" I think I have to stand outside of myself, maybe involve myself in some other abstraction like contemplating the purity of white, because for me sex has never been simply about pleasure or indulgence.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>When I asked an ex-lover how I should approach the situation she didn\\\'t say a word. She sat beside me and watched her programs, and I felt ignored. Then in one glorious moment, during commercials, she ran her hands through my hair softly, romantically, soothingly, then in another instant, like a 30 second softdrink ad changing to a public service announcement about menopause, she tugged, then pulled my hair till it hurt. My eyes spilled with salty white tears.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I shook in my place, my hands held out in almost prayer like fashion. I once had a stalemate position, it was sex without feeling, but now I knew it was not true. My calm uncomfortability was now quaking with tears.<i>You are so sensitive</i>, she said. Her tug felt honest and white, like fresh bedsheets. I fell to sleep in her arms and woke up feeling my back knotted into a ball on her couch, with her young son jumping on me to play horsey.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Life did feel wonderful after that, colours poured everwhere. I was in love, that irrational RGB, CMYK spectrum. I ran to her house later that night feet firmly planted in the knee-high snow outside her window on the forth floor. I looked at her window and sent her a text message.</P>\r\n\r\n<i>look for me</i>\r\n\r\n<p>She ran downstairs and let me in. She asked why I was there at such a strange hour. While walking there I had an upsurge of emotion to confront her about all my new feelings which I had awakened to. We both came to no conclusions, nor happy endings. She was from Prague and we  told each other fairytales before we slept. I began mine with <i>Once upon a time...</i>All her fairytales were in Czech, and they began with the typical Czech beginning to fantastic tales: <i>Bylo nebylo</i>--<i>What once was, what never was.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We made love one more time after that night. Later, I laid under my sheets smelling the scent of stale sex. I finished the drawing we started once, filling all the page with colour so that no spot remained uncovered by the grease of my crayons.</p>   \r\n'),(3193,'<p>\r\nIt feels like I have been away from home for so long, though it feels like I just got here. Its a strange feeling thinking about going home. Though it will only be for eight days, Im nervous. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore and more people have told me about their trips home. They tell me how good it is to get off the plane and see people they havnt seen, and then swing from the \\\'back home bliss\\\' towards \\\'after two weeks I wanted to go back\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nN. America is as strange to me now as Korea was when I first got here. There are things I love and hate about Korea, but living here has been easy, fun, boring, and exciting. I miss my family and friends, but for some reason I dont feel as compelled to see them as I did when I was \\\'away from home\\\' at university. I feel as though im actually doing something, whereas in university I felt as though I was just away for a long weekend.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m in motherfucking korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI leave on friday. As much as I like flying, I am not looking forward to my fifteen plus flight home. Living in one of the last developed countries that accepts and even promotes smoking, Ive developed quite an appetite for tobacco. Being without for fifteen plus hours just doesnt make my stomach feel good. addiction is a tricky game. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm going home. Im not staying here. Im not sure I made the right choice. There is a part of me that wants to go home and be with loved ones for the holidays, but there is also a part of me that wants to be away from loved ones for the holidays. I am torn by my desire to break away from my roots, though I have what I feel are obligations to be with my family at the one time of the year where my family comes together and doesnt argue. My grandmother is getting old, and I have this dark feeling her holiday seasons are numbered, and so I feel it necessary to spend as many with her as possible. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can\\\'t wait to get home. I can\\\'t wait to get back.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3194,'<p>\r\nBack in the UK all of a sudden, working a nine to five, fixing computers, fixing printers, bullshitting. Finding it harder to adjust.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSpending every day sitting in my shiny new car moving in spastic fits metre by metre along some faceless parkway, motorway, sliproad, offramp. In the wrong lane again. Fuck you buddy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWerrington Parkway, Soke Parkway, Orton Parkway, Fletton Parkway.. and i\\\'m still in the same freakin\\\' city. i\\\'m... just... trying... to get... to.... London for christ\\\'s sake. The A1(M) is eight lanes to we start to move and off we go... </p>\r\n<p>\r\nHow i long for the quiet 80kph country lanes of Ireland.\r\n</p>'),(3195,'<P>Went to <a href=\\\"http://www.zen-satsang.org\\\">Satsang</a> last wednesday for the first time in awhile. Adya talked about how we either act out of love or fear. This made a lot of sense and I could recognize it in my own behavior much of the time, but I was trying to understand how that is embodied when (as in the present moment) I am *not* doing something that needs to be done (in this case, for example, my expense reports). What fearful impulse is at work when I turn to something distracting instead of the task at hand? The fear of boredom? The fear of being confined by something I\\\'ve deemed not-enjoyable? The fear of being subject to an experience that seems like less fun than other possible experiences which are available? It\\\'s not like doing expenses or certain other work-related tasks are that difficult. But is fear at work when I avoid them?</P><P></P><P>One part of me is telling me I want to do these things, I *need* to do these things, then another (obviously stronger) part is not doing them, doing other things. This may seem mundane, relating as it does in this case to expense reports, but I think this is one of the fundamental issues that we face as human beings. What am I supposed to be doing with what I\\\'ve been given? Surely it can\\\'t be that I should be wasting these talents and abilities. Surely there must be some \\\"right\\\" path or course of action, both in the larger arc of my life and in this particular circumstance. Then why can\\\'t I find it? Why is it so elusive to me and to all the other seekers I\\\'ve ever met? Both the people looking for answers in their jobs, lovers, malls and bottles or the spiritual seekers who claim to want to know Truth (I fall into both categories). The quest for a meaning for our existence seems so compelling; it seems to be the next question once you\\\'ve dropped the veil of protecion that ignorance or cynicism provide.</P><P></P><P>It\\\'s like, aha, I get it. I\\\'m not just little \\\'ol me all separate and alone in the universe. WOW! That is fabulous and I spent the better part of three years floating on a cloud of warm fuzzy feelings that seemed to arise in the wake of that revelation. But lately there\\\'s been a nagging awareness that I do still occupy a place in this relative world and a desire to know what, if anything, I\\\'m supposed to do in it. I feel caught in the middle of not being able to EVER go back to the self-as-center-of-the-universe way of living life, but feeling unsure about what follows otherwise. Adya once compared it the odd in-between feeling we have as we grow out of childhood, but before we\\\'re really adults. The toys we played with in the nursery aren\\\'t really fun anymore, and yet we\\\'re not quite ready to start engaging in the same activities as the grown-ups. So we dwell in this place that\\\'s awkward, yet *totally necessary* for getting from one stage to the next. </P><P></P><P>That\\\'s still how it feels and it\\\'s been this way (to greater or lesser degrees) since I first had some awakening. I want to embrace that true nature, and yet I\\\'m not quite ready to leave behind all the toys I had in my nursery. </P>\r\n'),(3196,'<p>\r\nMister John Jackson, the proprietor and master trainer of the Romantic Boxing Club, believed that love was the most subtle form of warfare, a struggle differing only in scale from the dueling of pugilists in the ring or the clash of armies on the field of battle. Standing in the doorway of his office, balancing a tea tray with one hand and twirling his duffer\\\'s mustache with the other, he watched Marshall and the Kid carefully as they shadowboxed through the long rectangular shafts of sunlight that cut through the dusty warehouse air. His expert eye assessed their condition. One was a shellshocked veteran, he decided, and the other, a raw recruit, his skull still bleeding from the barber\\\'s razor. Neither, he was confident, was beyond his help.<p>\r\n \\\"It\\\'s really starting to suck,\\\" the Kid was saying. \\\"We went to Fisherman\\\'s Wharf, we went out to dinner, we went bowling.\\\" He punched awkwardly at the air. \\\"And I walk her home and try to kiss her and she\\\'s like \\\'Oh no,<I> papi,</I> my daddy is watching through the window\\\' or \\\'Not here, <I>papi,</I> that\\\'s my brother and his crew.\\\'\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall paused his bobbing and weaving to look at him. \\\"So why don\\\'t you make your move somewhere private, like when you\\\'re at the movies?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I <I>tried,</I>\\\" he said. \\\"She went, \\\'Oh,<I> papi, </I>no, the Virgin Mary can see us.\\\'\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall pivoted to his left so the Kid wouldn\\\'t see him laugh. He empathized with the Kid, but he still thought his travails with La Chupacabra\\\'s atavistic Catholic prudery were pretty funny. And though he didn\\\'t want to hurt his friend\\\'s feelings, he was glad something had finally cracked the black glass that had slid between him and the world since he had visited Tsingtao at the Midtown. He grinned into his fist and nodded to Mister John Jackson as he passed. <p>\r\nAlthough he had the look of a circus strongman in hale middle age, Mister John Jackson was, according to his own reckoning, almost two hundred and thirty years old.. He was Marshall\\\'s height, more or less, but packed an extra fifty pounds on his frame in the form of cannonball lumps of muscle that strained the seams of his turtleneck sweaters. Celebrated in his day as a paragon of physical perfection, famous artists from all over the world had used him as a figure model. But he never recognized himself in any of their paintings, because they always gave his magnificent torso someone else\\\'s head, due to his Neolithic brow and ears like an amphora\\\'s handles. His mesomorph\\\'s physique had developed without effort, so it was not a source of pride, but he was terribly vain about his thick red handlebar mustache, which he groomed with wax til its tips could be threaded through a needle. <p>\r\n.Marshall thought he was more than mad, but Mister John Jackson was the best boxing coach he\\\'d ever had, so he accepted his eccentricities with a straight face. The Kid, who had only been coming to the Romantic Boxing Club for a couple months, found him a little frightening. In fact, he found everything about the club a little frightening: the vicious crack of leather on canvas, the grunts of the solitary boxers heaping abuse on the heavy bags, the unidentifiable stains on the exercise mats and the mingled smells of effort and mortal terror. <p>\r\n Mister John Jackson set the tray down on an antique sidetable, flanked by a highbacked upholstered chair, that stood next to the short stepladder that ran up to the ring. \\\"Tea, gentlemen,\\\" he said. \\\"To fortify your nerves.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid accepted a tiny porcelain cup from Mister John Jackson\\\'s enormous paw, the knuckles ridged and white with scar tissue, and took a hesitant sip. \\\"Are you sure I\\\'m ready for this?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'ve learned the theory, now it\\\'s time to begin the practice.\\\" Mister John Jackson poured a cup for Marshall and tucked a crisp vanilla biscuit next to it on the saucer. \\\"No need to worry. Marshall will make sure your first time in the ring isn\\\'t too harrowing.\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall punched the Kid lightly on the shoulder, making him sputter as he sipped his tea. \\\"We\\\'ll just play around. You try out your punching, I\\\'ll work on defense. Once in a while I\\\'ll throw something back to make sure you keep your guard up. It\\\'ll be fun, you\\\'ll see.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid didn\\\'t look convinced. Marshall reminded him a lot of his older brother John, and John had had a way of describing any activity that began in uncertainty and ended in pain as lots of fun. <p>\r\n\\\"From the sound of things,\\\" Mister John Jackson said, settling himself beside the table, \\\"your instruction comes just in the nick of time.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"How do you mean?\\\" <p>\r\nHe gestured for the Kid to come over and handed him a leather foul guard, which the Kid hiked up over his shorts until it resembled a thick red diaper. Mister John Jackson began fitting his gloves on him, checking the placement of his knuckles, pulling the laces tight. \\\"I overheard the discussion of your troubles wooing a young lass. You\\\'ve heard the expression, \\\'I\\\'m a lover, not a fighter?\\\' Bollocks. You can\\\'t be a lover <I>without </I>being a fighter. Every romantic entanglement is, in its inception, an adversarial engagement. Tell me, what did you feel the first time you saw this girl?\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid sighed. \\\"Fear.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"My point exactly. Facing down fear is every fighter\\\'s first lesson. Did your heart not race, and your face grow flushed, and your limbs seem terrifically heavy, yet also capable of vaulting you through the roof? Your body knew the immediate threat. Fight or flight, my boy, fight or flight. And what did you do? Did you scurry away like a mouse caught in the pantry?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I played pool with her. She won.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Brilliant.\\\" Mister John Jackson pulled red padded headgear around the Kid\\\'s shaven skull, cinching the strap beneath his chin. \\\"Stepping into the ring is the most difficult part. Now the match begins! You circle each other warily, feinting and shifting, not giving too much away, trying to discern each other\\\'s weak points. Each seeks to penetrate the other\\\'s defenses while remaining protected against the inevitable counterblow. Both seeking to land the shot that sends the other into a swoon of desire.\\\" He shoved the white mouthguard between the Kid\\\'s teeth and shooed him aside. Marshall stepped into his place. <p>\r\nThe Kid looked confused. \\\"I just want her to like me as much as I like her,\\\" he mumbled around the plastic. <p>\r\n\\\"A worthy ambition,\\\" Mister John Jackson said. \\\"But one, if communicated too ardently, that will lead to failure. The hell of it is that if our opponent falls too easily, our perverse natures then decide they were not a worthy opponent for us in the first place, and we immediately seek another to test ourselves against.The paradox of mutuality is that both parties must be convinced that God, in His grace, has gifted them with more than they deserve. In His infinite wisdom He has confined such ideal love to the works of the dramatists and the tragedicians. In the mundane world, we must work out more pragmatic solutions. She defeated you in the first round with her beauty. Now you must come off your cornerman\\\'s knee and best her with skillful manuvering if you hope her to return your affections as wholeheartedly as you\\\'ve given them. Now she must be convinced that you are more man than she has any right to hope for.\\\" <p>\r\nHe climbed up onto the canvas covered platform of the ring and stepped on the ropes so that Marshall and the Kid could enter. \\\"The squared circle encloses and concentrates the mysteries of life and death, and the greatest mystery, of course, the one that ties one into the other, is love. Fighters, because of the insights they\\\'ve gained in the ring, have always been as reknown for their poetry and cocksmanship as their pugilistic skills. If you want to learn how to love, first you should learn how to box.\\\" <p>\r\nThey waited for the round to begin. The Kid stood in the red corner, hands held high, knees uncertain, and across the canvas Marshall lounged against the blue vinyl turnbuckle, lulled by the humid jungle air of the gym. The ring was a place where demons came out to play, but also a magic circle that kept them confined. Comforted by long experience, he savored the tension. Then the bell rang and the time for thinking was over. <p>\r\nDespite their assurances, the Kid was terrified. He was almost a head taller than Marshall, but he was sapling slender, and Marshall\\\'s pressed blue shirts, he had been shaken to discover, concealed shoulders knotted with bullish muscle. They came out to the center of the ring to touch gloves, and Marshall immediately began a predatory stroll that traced the circumference of a circle slightly outside the reach of the Kid\\\'s long arms. The Kid tried to keep straight everything he had been taught. Chin down. Hands up. Sit back. So many subtleties to the stance, and he was supposed to move at the same time? It seemed impossible. The most unnerving thing, the Kid thought, was Marshall\\\'s eyes. They were nearly closed, mere slits, like machine gun emplacements in a concrete bunker. The Kid couldn\\\'t see anything of his friend in that cold, calculating stare. Marshall was suddenly a stranger,  bent on his destruction. He\\\'s going to kill me, the Kid decided, and started swinging. <p>\r\nThe Kid\\\'s windmilling aggression caught Marshall by surprise. He had to actively protect himself, slipping right and left, knees bending, weight shifting. The punches he couldn\\\'t dodge he caught on his gloves and arms, and they hurt; the Kid was putting everything he had into his roundhouse blows. <p>\r\n\\\"Here now,\\\" Mister John Jackson called out in his stentorian voice, its Regency lilt only slightly flattened by one hundred years in the New York Bowery. \\\"Lighter, Kid, lighter. This is just practice.\\\" <p>\r\n Marshall parried and jabbed; the Kid flinched like a firecracker had gone off in his face, then redoubled his wild punching, eyes closed, head turned. Marshall backed off and watched his friend swatting at nothing. He shot an inquisitive look at Mister John Jackson, who ignored him and issued calm instructions to the Kid. \\\"Don\\\'t turn away,\\\" he said. \\\"It just throws you off balance. Relax. That\\\'s a good lad. Nothing to be afraid of.\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid\\\'s demeanor had changed. His face was beet red, and his lips curled back in a snarl that revealed the flat white shield of his mouthguard. Marshall realized they weren\\\'t sparring anymore; as far as they Kid was concerned, they were in a fight. He tried to stay calm, backpedalling and circling out as the Kid charged, but each reckless blow his friend threw eroded a little more of his self-control. <p>\r\n\\\"Cool it, Kid, seriously,\\\" he said. <p>\r\nIf the Kid heard him, he didn\\\'t appear to care. The punches continued to rain down. The snarl had become a sneer, Marshall saw; with a jolt he realized the Kid thought he was <I>winning.</I> He was taking advantage of Marshall\\\'s largesse, his good sportsmanship, and Marshall\\\'s pride screeched in protest. He bit down hard on his mouthpiece. Blood seemed to ooze in the periphery of his vision. Distracted by his thoughts, he had made a mistake in his footwork that allowed the Kid to trap him against the ropes, and now he was being shelled by sixteen ounce ordnance that seemed to arc from miles away. Each explosive thud in Marshall\\\'s head brought more red into the world, his indignation hardening to rage, until finally he dropped under the Kid\\\'s winging left hand and hit him in the solar plexus as hard as he could. <p>\r\nFor the Kid it was like getting stabbed with a baseball bat. He was sure Marshall\\\'s fist had punctured his chest cavity. The air whooshed out of him and he staggered backwards, folded in agony. His mouthpiece fell out onto the blood and sweatstained canvas, tethered by a silver string of saliva, and a panicked roar blanked his thoughts as his whole being centered on the high pitched choking wheeze of his lungs\\\' desperate attempt to fill their vacuum. <p>\r\nMister John Jackson called time. Marshall, his heart clenched in a fist of guilt, crouched down beside the Kid. \\\"Hey, I\\\'m really sorry, are you-\\\"<p>\r\nThe Kid charged him. His padded head slammed into Marshall\\\'s chin, closing his mouth with a snap that would\\\'ve shattered teeth unprotected by an ounce of molded plastic. Marshall tumbled back into the ropes, the Kid on top of him and flailing away, eyes wet and red in berserker rage. <p>\r\n\\\"Here now! Here now!\\\" Mister John Jackson said. He seized the Kid by the arm, hauled him to his feet, and slammed him against the turnbuckle, pinning him there, spitting and straining, until the black clouds in his eyes began to clear. \\\"This is boxing, not some tavern brawl! There are rules. One stands one\\\'s ground with one\\\'s fists up. One does not tackle-it\\\'s not rugby, for God\\\'s sake.\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall scrambled to his feet, astonished. He spit out his mouthpiece and tore off his headgear. \\\"Jesus, what the fuck is your problem?\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid had stopped struggling. \\\"I\\\'m really sorry, Marshall, really.\\\" He looked like he was about to cry. \\\"I get that way sometimes. I don\\\'t like to lose.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Oh, no, no,\\\" Mister John Jackson said, releasing his manacle grip. He shook his head sadly while the Kid tried to rub feeling back into his arms. \\\"The point of the training is not winning or losing. That was the scientific approach to boxing. Romantic boxing is about understanding.\\\" He sighed and began to climb down from the ring. \\\"I used to teach a scientific method, a long time ago, until I learned that victory without understanding can be the same as defeat.\\\" <p>\r\nMister John Jackson had once been known as the Emperor of Boxing. It was a sobriquet gifted him by the poet Lord Byron, who was the most avid of the many young aristocrats who flocked to his flat at 13 Bond St to learn the scientific style of pugilism that he had developed. Though not a noble himself, Mister John Jackson came from a wealthy family of merchants, and the fancy Regency bucks found him more to their taste than the rough sort who were the traditional fistfighting experts. <p>\r\nIt was Byron that introduced him to  the girl that would cause him to abandon the scientific style. He had first seen her at the illegal matches that were held at St John of the Fields, undisguised, not caring that the flickering torches might reveal her identity and tarnish her reputation as a young lady of refinement. She met his stare,  smiling across the pit where some luckless chap was being pounded into the dust, and he stood stupified until Byron noticed his interest. He had brought her over, and without a hint of hesitation or fear of scandal she had enquired about lessons. Which was how she and her governess came to be in the drawing room he had refashioned as a gymnasium, their skirts trailing across the worn wooden floor as they laughed and  punched his outstretched palms with their tiny fists. She had asked if she might hit him once in the stomach, just to see what it was like, and when she grinned as her knuckles bounced off his rock ribs, he knew he was in love. <p>\r\nHe had courted her slowly, over the course of many weeks of chaperoned lessons, and was on the verge of confessing his true feelings when he saw her one night at a match, on the arm of Daniel Mendoza, the Champion of England. Mendoza was a dandy, with the long dark hair of a Moorish prince and a penchant for declaiming love sonnets with a Castillian lisp, but he had put down the last ten men that had challenged his title. Back in his flat, staring at the reflection of his square peasant\\\'s head with its low, sloping brow, Mister John Jackson despaired of winning the girl with his charms. There had to be another way to prevail. He challenged Mendoza to a match. Mendoza accepted. <p>\r\nIn the spirit of the Enlightenment, Mister John Jackson attempted to apply the new principles of science to his training. He interviewed the men Mendoza had beaten and consulted with engineers. He plotted with artillery experts and drew schematics on a chalkboard. At an autopsy he learned exactly where the spleen lay, and an astronomer taught him how the orbits of the planets would influence the power of his blows. He wrapped his fists in mufflers and tested his theories on hapless drunks. When the night of the match came, he was thoroughly prepared. <p>\r\nDaniel Mendoza entered the ring with a light blue scarf tied above the bulging bicep of his right arm. When they squared off to begin the match, Mister John Jackson saw that it was embroidered with her initials. It sealed the dandy\\\'s fate. The fight didn\\\'t last long, but it would\\\'ve ended sooner if Mister John Jackson had let Daniel Mendoza drop. <p>\r\n\\\"I seized him by his poet\\\'s hair,\\\" Mister John Jackson said, \\\"and I hammered him until his own mother wouldn\\\'t have recognized him.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"That was your scientific solution?\\\" the Kid said. <p>\r\nMister John Jackson looked uncomfortable. \\\"It was the best I could do at the time.\\\" He shrugged and poured them some more tea. \\\"If I had merely knocked him out, to awake the next day with the bruises on his pretty cheeks already healing, I still would\\\'ve lost. To achieve victory in the invisible arena of love, I had remove his advantage. I made him as ugly as me. It was the only way to win.\\\" <p>\r\nHe brought her the scarf as proof that he was, in the end, the better man. She took it from him and walked down to the ring, where she would use it to wipe the blood from Mendoza\\\'s face. <p>\r\n\\\"Only a barbarian continues the beating when his opponent is beaten,\\\" she said over her shoulder. \\\"Every blow you struck when he was already down was a blow you struck against me.\\\" <p>\r\nMister John Jackson thumbed a tear from his eye. \\\"She illumined my heart with love, and I\\\'d allowed him to come between us. Cast into shadow, I fell into a kind of madness where the end justified any means, and in my zeal to win I had gone too far. Instead of proving myself the better man in her eyes, I\\\'d become no man at all-an animal, a savage beast.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"So what did you do?\\\" Marshall asked. <p>\r\n\\\"Being a man of action and not contemplation, I followed through on the first impulse of every victim of heartbreak. I went home and hung myself.\\\" He took a sip of tea, his mustache glistening. \\\"But it didn\\\'t work. My neck is too thick, you see. I swung for a couple hours from a rope I\\\'d thrown over a roof beam in my flat, and eventually I grew bored and worked myself loose. But while I hung there, waiting for the end which never arrived, I realized the error of my approach. And I dedicated myself to developing a new style of pugilism that would prevent others from making the same mistakes that I had. Where once I had told my students, do what it takes to win, now I said: First understand the nature of the struggle. Then bide your time, know your distance, set up your shot, strike once, strike well, and end the contest with mercy. That was the beginning of the Romantic Boxing Club.\\\" <p>\r\nForty-five minutes laters Marshall and the Kid were downtown, waiting for Bianca at a table in an outdoor cafÃ©. It was unseasonably warm, and Marshall had taken off his leather coat and sat with his closed eyelids flaring red against the sun. <p>\r\n\\\"Marshall?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m really sorry about what happened.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I know.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m just on edge, you know? Work. And Chupa.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I think I might be in love with her.\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall\\\'s eyes snapped open. He stared at the Kid, who stared miserably at his coffee. \\\"Jesus. You just met her!\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I know.\\\" <p>\r\nBianca arrived, wearing a kilt and skull-and-bones barrettes in her dark hair. \\\"What\\\'s up, boys?\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall rolled his eyes. \\\"The Kid thinks he\\\'s in love.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"With La Chupacabra?\\\" <p>\r\nThe Kid pouted. \\\"Don\\\'t call her that.\\\" <p>\r\nBianca grinned at him. \\\"No sweat, <I>papi.</I> I\\\'m getting coffee. Anyone want another?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Yeah.\\\" Marshall fished in his pocket for a five. <p>\r\n\\\"No, I\\\'ll get it,\\\" she said. \\\"I owe you from yesterday. Oh hey.\\\" She tossed him a magazine, its pages fluttering in wingless flight. \\\"There\\\'s an article in there about that painter you like so much.\\\" <p>\r\nMarshall plucked it out of the air and examined the cover. Krishna the goatherd with his flute, serenading an adoring flock of blue Saturday morning elves. <I>Desacralizing the SacredÂ¸</I> the blurb said. <I>The Religious Visions of San Francisco\\\'s Sebastien Reich.</I> Marshall flipped throught the glossy pages until he found the article. More color prints, gods and archetypes in rapturous embraces and unlikely combinations. He turned the page greedily, wanting more. <p>\r\nBianca called to him from the order window. \\\"Hey Marshall. You want cream and sugar? Marshall?\\\" <p>\r\nHe couldn\\\'t hear her. For a long moment he sat frozen, staring at the magazine: no lungs breathing, no heart beating, no cells dividing, no synapses firing. Complete silence in a vacuum of horrified disbelief. <I>Stripper Goddess,</I> the caption said. <I>With creator, at right.</I> The painting was of Kali, the carbon black Hindu mother goddess of death, one leg curled around a chrome pole, one of her multiple hands raised in an invocation of the devil. She was wearing a white cowboy hat and nothing else. Below the hat, she had Tsingtao\\\'s face. And the credited painter, standing unsmiliing to the right, his arms folded over his long black leather coat, was Hondo. <p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3197,'here we are. i just wrote and deleted a long swinney article about the new Phish cd and how i thought that it was weird how it was hard to tell if they had gotten worse, but impossible not to note at least a fundamental <i>difference</i> about them. There are two possible explanations for this unexpected loss of some qualities, and promotion of others, that I oscillate between constantly: <br><br>\r\n1. they got silly, and don\\\'t realize that the album is bad. I can forgive that. <br>\r\n2. they got lazy, and put out a piece of shit just to collect more money. that idea breaks my heart in a way that it hasn\\\'t been broken since grade school.<br><br>\r\nThe first idea and the second idea have close to a 50/50 split in probability to my mind, and since there is no way to tell, not really, i naturally lean towards the first. It\\\'s kind of like with Stephen King; his stuff now is not so great. He wrote a book recently about writing, and what he considers good and bad, and then wrote a collection of stories that proceeded to violate most of the rules that he had just laid out. This freaked me out of course, but I could at least (sort of) attribute this odd lack of quality to the gruesome accident that he had, and which he talked about at geat length in the book about writing. i can imagine that a super-prolific writer of horror fiction, who is able to come up with horrible shit from his imagination constantly, might be a little fucked up in the head after living through something truly and deperately <i>horrible</i>, like being hit by a van. I can see how that might happen. <br><br>\r\nstill, nobody in Phish got hit by a van. Far as I can tell. <br><br>\r\nSo, like I said, I wrote and deleted an article about this subject a few minutes ago, because after i read it i thought it sucked. i thought about how everything i had tried to write here lately had failed, and realized that the question i was asking myself about my own writing was the same question that i had been asking in the article itself--why are these things i love not so great like they used to be? the only answer i could come up with was this: maybe there are just too many factors involved in the creative process for anyone to ever truly explain relative failures and successes, and maybe artists can\\\'t prevent the loss of their talent, or even know when it\\\'s gone, but--<br><br>\r\nyou\\\'ve got to admire the fact that the poor bastards keep hitting the \\\"submit\\\" button.'),(3198,'<P>\r\nI\\\'ve heard about this feeling so many times, everybody has, I guess. In songs, in stories, at the movies, in our daydreams, from our friends. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis beautiful man I am in love with, thinks that this feeling we have for each other is little more than a chemical reaction in our brains, a physical response to our personal triggers, whatever they might be. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nBut when I look at him sleeping, or watch him become engrossed in Star Trek, or laugh with him over things I know no-one else would find amusing, I have my doubts over his \\\'gland theory\\\'. And I have excellent reasons for my doubts.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt doesn\\\'t matter what he wears, it doesn\\\'t bother me that his house is somewhat less than luxurious, it doesn\\\'t matter that he has no telephone and it doesn\\\'t matter that he thinks there is no such thing as a soulmate. \r\nAnd to really prove my point, I\\\'m quite happy to be the proud new owner of a headscarf, a blue and purple silk scarf that is designed to be worn like my grandmother would have worn it, tied under the chin. \r\nIt\\\'s his thing. Odd, but then thats why I love him, it\\\'s not as though I\\\'m a National Party voting, right wing conservative myself, after all. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nNo way would I wear this thing if this was just a chemical reaction. No way. It\\\'s the biggest fashion disaster of my life. I look like Sophia Loren after an especially nasty encounter with a blade-happy plastic surgeon, or possibly Michael Jackson dressed up like Grandma.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnd yet I\\\'ll wear it, because I love him. I\\\'ll wear it because he likes it, and because I know that if I asked him to, he\\\'d get up in the night and make me cheese on toast, or snuggle me if I needed it. I\\\'ll wear it because he comes to visit me at work and cooks my dinner, and I\\\'ll wear it because he picks me wild flowers and gave me sunflower seeds to plant in my garden, all wrapped up in a handmade envelope with \\\"Flowers of Love\\\" written on it. I\\\'ll wear it because he sent a photo of us together to his mother, and because he sends me messages that make me sigh. I\\\'ll wear it because he introduces me to all his friends, and because he respects my right to my own lifestyle. I\\\'ll wear it because he makes me laugh out loud, and because he takes the time to explain things to me I never knew. Most of all though, I\\\'ll wear it simply because I like to see him smile.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOuch, to care so deeply almost hurts. Physical reaction. Bah. I mean, come on. A head scarf? For a 25 year old Kiwi agnostic who grew up in a house full of determined, leather-clad, bike riding,nuclear submarine stopping women, it has got to be more than a chemical response.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis is love. The kind I heard about in the songs, in the movies, and in the greatest books.\r\n</P>\r\n  \r\n\r\n'),(3199,'.'),(3200,'<p>\r\n\\\"hey sasha, can you go into that store and get me a copy of swank? if they look at you funny just say you are looking for hairstyles.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve been in Toronto now since friday evening.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nGetting through the vancouver airport I remember thinking \\\"they are too polite!\\\" Had to literally run full tilt to my connector flight to Toronto. \r\nAn RCMP stormtrooper younger than me \\\'needed to ask me a few questions.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"where you runnin off to so quickly?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"this is an airport.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWent through all the questions regarding what I \\\"did\\\" in Korea and blah blah blah. Airports are stupid these days. more car bombs go off every year but we dont have to go through checkpoints all over the place. its ridiculous. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nlanded in Toronto craving a smoke more than anything. as soon as I landed I knew it wasnt the same. I feel somewhat seperate. Like I did when I landed in Korea back in April, but this time it felt sorta more distant. More confusing. Perhaps because I remembered it as it was, and korea was something one hundred percent new. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve seen friends. I\\\'ve seen family. I\\\'ve seen the skyline from the kyoo-e double-you. I guess things are good. I do miss the open sewer up the hill though. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI miss my life in Korea more than I miss Korea. I miss my girlfriend, and shane. I miss teaching the kids. I so want to travel around asia and just loaf. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfrom zoli\\\'s crib in teedot this is chiam signing oot. \r\n</p>'),(3201,'<P>\r\nsitting in my parents\\\' living room makes me want to do bold things. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nfar far away from the routine of my daily life in new york, i feel safe and free of responsibility. tokyo is a big motherfucking city, and it\\\'s easy to hide from whatever or whomever. at least, if you\\\'re asian. have you read about the men who stay in their apartments for seven years at a time and never come out? \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhigh in the ivory tower, i  lurk behind the drapes and make eyes at mt. fuji.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni called him last night, crackly-voiced over the long distance lines and said, \\\"let\\\'s don\\\'t do this anymore.\\\" a last bit of affect plucked from noel coward or something like that. i could tell that he didn\\\'t believe me, \\\'cause we\\\'ve been through this one too many times. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nand what makes my tongue curl the most is the fear that i\\\'ll punk out when i\\\'m rereleased from the womb and spat out onto the asphalt. i worry that i won\\\'t remember how to be okay. i worry that new york is not the supportive type.\r\n</P>'),(3202,'<p>\r\nthe media in north america has it all wrong. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbeing in korea the past eight months i havnt at any time felt like south koreans were afraid of an attack from north korea. at no time have i noticed any kind of anxiety towards the norths nuclear weapons program. i just dont see koreans caring much about a member of the \\\'axis of evil\\\'. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy take, though it may be a bit naive, is that the north and south will join together again in a bloodless revolution from both sides. the revolution will be similar to that of the quiet revolution in quebec in the 1960\\\'s.  a gradual re-unification of two \\\'great\\\' nations. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfor decades now the south has \\\'relied\\\' on the u.s to facilitate an atmosphere of stability. having the u.s military there makes an attack from the north unlikely. the south has \\\'relied\\\' on the u.s military presence as a means of attracting foreign investment into the region. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim not sure the 86 olympics would have been in seoul had the u.s not had troops farting around with m-16 assault rifles. im not sure south korea would have enjoyed the economic growth and \\\'development\\\' they have worked so hard to achieve had the americans not had troops there. so much has happened in south korea since the end of the war, that people often take for granted all the \\\'perks\\\' of having a u.s soldier stationed on your shores...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthis said, im beginning to understand that south korea is perhaps ready to let go of uncle sam\\\'s hand and take a few steps on their own. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni see the american military leaving. i see the south continuing the sunshine policy, moving ahead with stalled plans to connect north and south by rail. i see that my kids still regard north koreans with the respect they show south koreans. they believe a korean in the north is as korean as a korean in the south. more and more think that the only reason there is a north and south is because of the \\\'cold proxy war\\\' between the united states, the u.s.s.r, and china. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe two nations will again be one. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe 40million south koreans will hold hands with the 20million north koreans and sing traditional songs.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe leaders will shake hands and do all those things politicians do when they are in public. once all the festivities die down they\\\'ll move to a private abode and discuss the future of korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n40million highly educated south koreans. an established manufacturing sector with sales all over the world. wired to the tits. old and new money. money money money. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand then you have the 20million north koreans, and 2nuclear warheads. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*poof* instant nuclear democracy with the capabilities of being a strong economic force in asia.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\njapan? step off!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nchina? step the fuck back!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\namerica? wussup!\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3203,'<p>\r\nI am on the edge.<br>\r\nI am on<br>\r\nthe verge of<br>\r\nthe urge to let it<br>\r\nout.<br>\r\nTo let it<br>\r\ngo.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is too little<br>\r\nmasquerading as too<br>\r\nmuch.<br>\r\nToo much for the two<br>\r\nof us,<br>\r\nmyself and my other self.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut<br>\r\nwe are one<br>\r\nman on the verge<br>\r\nand all we\\\'ve got to get by with<br>\r\nis whiskey and cliche<br>\r\nand iron mouthed distaste for<br>\r\none another.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTogether we can be victorious,<br>\r\nthey say.<br>\r\nTogether we can make<br>\r\na difference.<br>\r\nBut what difference does it make<br>\r\nwhen victory never tasted sweet to me.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOur course is set and<br>\r\nour mutually assured destruction<br>\r\nwill unite us.<br>\r\nTonight we\\\'re playing<br>\r\nto lose,<br>\r\nbut I don\\\'t think either of us<br>\r\nwill win.<br>\r\n</p>'),(3204,'The beauty of cliches - despite how often they show up, they never seem to fail at being unique in one way or another when it\\\'s related to a person. And this is of course, about a person. Someone new. A person very real. Someone that was right in front of me and looking at me as I wrote the first draft.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe way his eyes light up and whole face changes when he smiles, the way he moves his body at all times. Light hair, cut short. Blue eyes that speak volumes. Small cliches that make him exactly who he is outwardly. The warmth of that one person\\\'s arms being 90% perfect.. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s hard to imagine not falling in love with him, really. Past all the physical appeal he has, there\\\'s an inner part of him that shines through. <br><br>\r\n\r\nWe met on the internet, something I hadn\\\'t expected. I don\\\'t know how he found me or why I responded. We talked for a couple of weeks and decided to meet. I\\\'ve always been cautious about such things, as people aren\\\'t always what they portray themselves to be. He was though, and much more. He made me laugh. Things were comfortable, as if we\\\'d known each other for years already. <BR><BR> \r\n\r\nWe went to a party together, we danced, I met his friends and family, and we talked all night in bed together without doing anything but holding each other. Those are things I cherish. And things I remember. He proved himself to be everything I\\\'d hoped for and much more. This is the cliche story of boy meets girl and everything goes great. So we\\\'re together now, something I cleared up with one question outside of the train station at 10 pm, smoking our cigarettes and watching the clock. Our thoughts about many things click together in one unanimous opinion, we want the same things in life to a degree. I can\\\'t say I really know him from knowing him over time, but thus far I\\\'ve caught a glimpse. <BR><br>\r\n\r\nI also worry in some sense. We want to move in together and it\\\'s rather soon. Living with him could be wonderful, but I don\\\'t know yet. It won\\\'t happen for a couple of months yet, but soon enough. I wonder if my past bothers him and if his bothers me. I question why I feel like something was lost as soon as we made a commitment to each other. I want commitment and stability, and with it brought a falling feeling. I don\\\'t know if it\\\'s excitement I need and I think I don\\\'t get or if something is missing... <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nSo - this is just a rambling off. I care about him, I want to stay with him. I suppose my worries are normal, I don\\\'t express them to him and he doesn\\\'t express his to me. I\\\'ve only hinted at certain things because I think my worries are also a product of the beginning tension of worry. Worry can destroy something, and I don\\\'t want to give myself away to worries and destroy the potential at hand. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nAnother cliche - I\\\'ve never met anyone like him before. I\\\'m falling in love with him. It\\\'s terrifying in some sense, really terrifying. It\\\'s strange to jump into the future and think of yourself as married to a person and sharing living quarters and a bed, sharing friends and life, sharing everything. <BR><BR>\r\n\r\nIn all honesty, my fear doesn\\\'t stem from the change or the future, it stems at the end and the ending of us. Those things have to be thought of, I think. But I could be wrong. I\\\'m probably being irrational. I just want to make sure that any decision I make is the right one for now. '),(3206,'<p>i went down to charleston to stay with barrett and his girlfriend nicki at her house, on the 30th, and we went over to a beach house which some of her friends had rented for a few days.  college kids. barrett and i spent most of the evening talking about how pissed the owners were going to be with the rental folks, for renting to this group. we swerved home safely. nicki\\\'s roomate caroline was drunk. she\\\'d had some xanax. so we had to carry her inside when we got home. she lay down for a while, then came out into the den to smoke pot with nicki and me, and then went back into her room. barrett, nicki and i drank a bottle of this awesome sparkling pinot grigio, and we at this point were pretty drunk. when we get drunk, we are happy and silly and that\\\'s about it. so we\\\'re in the den (happy and silly), nicki\\\'s in the back, and she comes into the den upset, and says, \\\"caroline\\\'s locked in her bathroom, and she\\\'s been in there the whole time we\\\'ve been out here, and she won\\\'t answer me or open the door.\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe fact that nicki has said, \\\"she\\\'s been in there the whole time we\\\'ve been out here\\\" is the thing. at least 20 minutes, and i have definitely not been counting.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbarrett went into action, this being his home territory more than mine, and told nicki calmly to bring him a butter knife. she brought four steak knives. i already had my little leatherman keychain thing out though, and i used the phillips head to break the lock. bathroom locks break easily.<br><br>\r\ni am at his shoulder. caroline is lying face up, head bent at an angle, because of the way she is leaning against the outside wall of the tub. her eyes are open. she appears to be dead. <br><br>\r\nbarrett stood there for a moment, turned around and walked down the hall. i was certain that she was dead. i checked her pulse, and as i felt a faint thump, she began to mumble a little. barrett was in the den, looking for the telephone, when i came to tell him that she was all right. we put her to bed, making sure that her vital signs and temperature were in the green. nicki reassured us that caroline passed out with her eyes open \\\"all the time,\\\" and in fact, she often woke her up in the morning and saw her sleeping with them open. \r\n<br><br>\r\nat midnight, the next night, new year\\\'s eve, i kissed her, and said \\\"happy new year.\\\" she has no recollection of the incident. she tooted her horn, the ball dropped, and i was happy to see that. and i went over to the corner and wrote this.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3207,'<P>they stopped to rest under a big oak tree beside the road.  the day was getting warmer.</P><P>him:  It\\\'s about to get hot.</P><P>her:  At least it won\\\'t be fucking raining.</P><P>it had been raining for three days.  they\\\'d been stuck inside and had to cook in the fireplace.</P><P>he took off his backpack and found the canteen inside, took two sips and offered it to her.  she waved it away.</P><P>her:  I don\\\'t want your backwash.</P><P>he started to root around in the backpack.</P><P>her:  Whadja lose?</P><P>he continued looking through it.</P><P>her:  Did you get condoms?</P><P>he took a box of them out and shook it at her.  despairing of finding anything in the main compartment, he looked through a zippered pocket on top and pulled out a blue paperback book.  they sat there.  he paged through the book.</P><P>him:  Says here that relaxation is the key to psychic communication.</P><P>her:  Izzat right?</P><P>him:  I wouldn\\\'t shit you, baby.</P><P>cars passed.  teenage boys leaned out of one and yelled at the girl.</P><P>him:  Can\\\'t they see that I\\\'m sittin\\\' right here?</P><P>after a time, they got up and walked up the shoulder of the road.  they had another mile before home.  cars passed, usually giving them a wide berth.</P><P>her:  What kind of flower is that?</P><P>him:  This purple stuff?</P><P>her:  Yeah.</P><P>him:  I think it\\\'s called heather.</P><P>they walked on, silent and luxuriating in the silence, enjoying it comfortably like a cultivated thing.  they came to a dirt road and turned down it.  instead of sticking to the shoulder, they walked in the ruts.  he came up behind her and tickled her in the sides.  she giggled and slapped at his head.</P><P>a jet was flying high overhead, leaving a trail behind.  she pointed it out to him.  they walked along on the grassy hump between the ruts, losing their balance and stepping into the sand now and then.  to their right, there were acres of pasture land with occaisonal patches of forest.  the fields were dotted with solitary trees or groups of trees, and it was all enclosed and divided by barbed wire.  they couldn\\\'t see any cows.</P><P>to their left, there were thick pine woods behind a fence.  after a time, a tributary forked off the dirt road to the left.  they followed it into the woods.</P><P>the house, judging from the wallpaper, had been built sometime in the 70s.  it sat in a clearing, compact and low to the ground.  The pane of glass they had broken to get in the back door had been replaced with a piece of cardboard.</P><P>they kept their things in the living room in the front of the house.  howard had helped them move what they didn\\\'t sell.  there was a box of books, one of pots and pans and dishes, two boxes of clothes, and a first aid kit lined up against the wall by the fireplace.  the guitar, food, candles, matches, soap, towels, toilet paper, razors, toothbrushes, laundry detergent, papers, mementos, paraphernalia, pens, notebooks, and camera were stored under the front windows.  they slept in the middle of the room, near the fireplace, on a pile of blankets and pillows.</P><P>after a snack of sardines and crackers, they got high and opened a bottle of red wine.  they had switched from beer to wine and stopped smoking cigarrettes.  he tried to write while she laid on the blankets and read.  they finished the wine.</P><P>when the sun started to set, he put his notebook away and selected a can of vegetable soup and the oregano from the box of food under the windows, then got a saucepan and the can opener and took it all outside to the back steps.  he went back in and got some newspaper and twigs and finger-sized pieces of wood from the piles in the utility room and built a fire in the pit they had made in the back yard.  the soup he mixed with some water from a demijohn.  it boiled quickly.  they ate it with crackers and a can of pineapple for dessert.  and another bottle of red.</P><P>he heated water in a large pot and took it in to the kitchen sink and washed the dishes.  when it was dark enough, he took the two demijohns through the woods past the field toilet, on a trail he\\\'d cut deep into the woods, to a barbed wire fence that marked the place where the forest met the pasture.  he walked across fields under the rising moon, crossing fences, and filled the demijohns at a spighot beside a watering trough.  the cattle were resting under an oak tree.</P><P>when he got back to the house, she was reading by candlelight.  they shared some bonghits and opened up more wine.</P><P>him:  What are you reading?</P><P>her:  <U>The Red and the Black</U></P><P>him:  How is it?</P><P>her:  I don\\\'t know.  I\\\'m fifty pages into it, and nothing\\\'s happened yet.</P><P>he watched her read.  the candlelight flickering made tiny shadows jump beside her nose and mouth and eyes.  he decided she looked like a cat in the candlelight.</P><P>her:  Excuse you.</P><P>him:  For what?</P><P>her:  Breaking wind.</P><P>him:  I would never do such a thing.</P><P>her:  The hell you didn\\\'t.  I\\\'m surprised it didn\\\'t blow out the candle.</P><P>him:  That\\\'s right, woman.  Know my scent.</P><P>he watched her read.  she looked up and met his gaze.  he crawled over to her and held her head in his hands and kissed her forehead, kissed her closed eyelids, her lips.  she sucked on his lips.</P><P>her:  You should build a fire first.  We\\\'ll get cold.</P><P>he went into the utility room and got newspaper and lighter knots and kindling and stacked it in the fireplace.  when it was burning well, he took off his shirt.  they stood on the blankets and undressed.  naked, they stood on the blankets and kissed.</P><P>him:  Feel how heavy my balls are.</P><P>her:  I\\\'ve felt heavier grapes in the grocery store.  Little ones.</P><P>he sat down and pulled her down to him.  he sat cross-legged, and she leaned back against him, laying her head on his chest.  he twisted his head down and around, she turned hers to the side, and, as they kissed, he cupped her breasts in his hands and rolled her nipples between his thumbs and forefingers.</P><P>they laid on the blankets.  he put his hand over hers and guided it down between her legs.  she was already wet.  they both ran their fingers along her lips and clitoris.  he moved down and licked the clitoral hood, then closed his lips around her clit and hummed.  she rubbed her hands over his head and caressed his ears.</P><P>him:  Where\\\'d we put the condoms?</P><P>he rubbed the head of his penis against her lips and put on a rubber.  she straddled his waist, lowering herself down on him.</P><P>it was friday night.</P><BR><BR><BR>*           *            *<BR><BR><BR><P>on saturday morning, they took a bath.  after cereal with soy milk, he built a fire and heated a pot of water.  they had cleaned the tub with bleach and steel wool when they moved in.  it was big enough for both of them.</P><P>after dressing, they walked into town.  it took an hour.  the wind blowing made it cold.</P><P>they went to the first baptist church and sat on the slope of front lawn near the street.  people were massed along the sidewalks all up and down main street.  at ten o\\\'clock, his family arrived.  they all ignored him except for his niece, age six.  she came over and said hello.</P><P>the parade went by in fits and starts.  after the motorcycle cops came fire trucks and marching bands, floats holding the mayor and city council, shriners, church groups, horsemen, just say \\\"no\\\" to drugs, and santa claus at the end.  they all threw candy to the kids.</P><P>while the people began to disperse, they sat on the grass and chewed gum.  his family left with glances toward them.  they walked downtown and had lunch at a pizza place then went to the bar.  she stuck to wine while he downed shots of gin and vodka, which he started to call \\\"little white guys.\\\"</P><P>him:  Randy.  Set up s\\\'more them little white motherfuckers.</P><P>she tried to talk him into going home.  he programmed \\\"love is all around\\\" for nineteen consecutive plays on the jukebox.  she left when he bit another woman on the tit.  the police found marijuana in his pocket.  randy unplugged the jukebox.</P>'),(3208,'<p>\r\ni just peeled 6 cloves of garlic and some 30 shrimp. i artfully arranged a plate or two of cheese and one of olives. i cut a baguette and opened a lot of cracker boxes. i made some smoked salmon, dill and creme fraiche toasts and set them aside.  in a while i\\\'ll saute the shrimp in butter, white wine and garlic. it\\\'ll taste nice with the warm crusty bread action under a cloth napkin in this baskety thing. oh wait, breaking news, the trader joe\\\'s white cheddar popcorn is mysteriously lacking in cheddar flavoring. says megan, the birthday girl, \\\"it tastes like butt. can\\\'t you just run to Cala Foods or something?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy sister is in town for a few days from tulsa, oklahoma. while she\\\'s here, she\\\'ll turn 19. my mom picked me up from work thursday with explicit instructions to hang around the whole time so we could spend some quality time. i emailed my roommates that i had been kidnapped and they should send help. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s great to see megs but i think we\\\'re having a hard time actually spending time with each other. she showed me pictures of portugal and the shoes she bought there. we compared notes on the drugs we did over new years\\\'. we haven\\\'t really talked about much else, i don\\\'t think.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m trying to mask my bad feelings about her having breakfast with our aunt who doesn\\\'t seem to have any desire to see me. i can tell she feels weird about it too. my mom has been trying to avoid the topic but occasionally whispers to me, \\\"are you sure you\\\'re ok?,\\\" when we\\\'re alone for a second.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmegan\\\'s been dating her boyfriend for about a year now and they are living together. she met him around the same time i hooked up with my girlfriend. it\\\'s funny. neither of us have had too many relationships that have lasted any length of time, let alone at the same time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni had so many more things to say but the guests have started to arrive and i should put the shrimp on....\r\n</p>'),(3209,'<P>dear sis,<BR>tell me, was i missed<BR>on christmas day?<BR><BR>let me ease your mind:<BR>there\\\'s no rape<BR>in the county jail.<BR>they watch us<BR>pretty close.<BR>the only guys giving it up<BR>are the ones<BR>who want to.<BR>like my bunkmate<BR>magnotti says,<BR>i don\\\'t play footsie.<BR><BR>magnotti\\\'s sicilian<BR>by way of miami beach.<BR>he\\\'s a federal inmate<BR>doing seven years<BR>in the atlanta pen<BR>for bank robbery.<BR>he\\\'s down here<BR>on an appeal.<BR>(the county takes in<BR>federal prisoners<BR>to fill any empty beds.)<BR><BR>he doesn\\\'t like<BR>the \\\"moolies\\\" in our cell<BR>l.a. and felton<BR>don\\\'t much like him<BR>either.<BR>l.a.\\\'s a crack dealer,<BR>also federal,<BR>waiting to do twelve.<BR>felt\\\'s in for<BR>strong-arm robbery,<BR>yet to be tried.<BR><BR>i tried to tell them<BR>my pot was in<BR>three different bags<BR>because it was<BR>three different kinds of pot.<BR>evidently,<BR>they call that<BR>intent to distribute.<BR>my lawyer says<BR>they\\\'ll probably drop it down<BR>to simple possession,<BR>but for now,<BR>i\\\'m in the felony unit.<BR><BR>tell joe<BR>hello.<BR>tell \\\'im<BR>we\\\'ll play dominos<BR>again.<BR>someday.<BR><BR>anna comes<BR>to see me<BR>on visiting day.<BR>makes thursdays<BR>something to look forward to.<BR>we look at each other<BR>through plate glass<BR>and talk on phones.<BR><BR>saturday\\\'s<BR>movie night.<BR>you\\\'d like the ones they show:<BR>\\\"bob le flambeur\\\"<BR>dubbed into english<BR>and \\\"d.o.a.\\\"<BR>the guys in here<BR>can take \\\'em<BR>or leave \\\'em.<BR><BR>on monday mornings,<BR>they like to<BR>pull inspections.<BR>always<BR>on monday mornings.<BR>everybody knows<BR>to hide their contraband<BR>on their person<BR>come monday morning.<BR><BR>magnotti\\\'s<BR>saving up his klonopin.<BR>he gums it<BR>when the nurse<BR>gives it to him.<BR>he\\\'s going to<BR>take it all<BR>back to atlanta<BR>and sell it.<BR>or trade it.<BR>something.<BR>i don\\\'t quite understand<BR>the prison economy.<BR>he\\\'s got<BR>a rubber sleeve thing<BR>he\\\'s going to<BR>put them in<BR>and put up his ass<BR>for the ride home.<BR><BR>we start the buck<BR>on monday right after<BR>they toss the cells.<BR>we\\\'ve got a <BR>five-gallon thermos<BR>we hide in the mop room<BR>during the inspections.<BR>if they found it in our cell,<BR>they\\\'d confiscate it.<BR>buck is the fermented result<BR>of mixing water,<BR>bread, fruit, and fruit juice.<BR>we drink it<BR>on sunday night.<BR>it\\\'ll fuck you up.<BR><BR>l.a. and felt<BR>hang a blanket<BR>off the edge<BR>of felt\\\'s bunk<BR>and sit behind it<BR>on l.a.\\\'s rack<BR>and smoke a joint<BR>every morning.<BR>one of the guards<BR>brings the weed<BR>into the jail.<BR>whoever the top man is<BR>sells it in<BR>skinny little joints.<BR>l.a. and felt give<BR>ten dollars apiece for them.<BR>i\\\'m not sure how<BR>they work out the payment.<BR>they get me and graves high<BR>every once in awhile.<BR><BR>graves is our other cellmate.<BR>he\\\'s a rapist who thinks he\\\'s black.<BR><BR>tell joe<BR>hello.<BR>tell \\\'im<BR>we\\\'ll play dominos<BR>again.<BR>someday.<BR><BR>christmas day<BR>i was in the infirmary.<BR>it happened after lunch.<BR>in the morning,<BR>we\\\'d gone out<BR>to the exercise yard.<BR>it\\\'s divided into two halves<BR>with an enclosed walkway in between:<BR>there\\\'s a basketball court on one side<BR>and weights on the other.<BR>magnotti and i<BR>went to work out.<BR>\\\"who\\\'s on the other side?\\\" i asked him.<BR>\\\"ten thousand moolyon.<BR>and graves.\\\"<BR><BR>we went back to the unit<BR>for showers and lunch,<BR>then the salvation army came around<BR>and gave everybody<BR>toothpaste<BR>for christmas.<BR><BR>i started thinking about everything.<BR>told the guard i was going<BR>to commit suicide.<BR>jump off the top tier.<BR>they took me and my property<BR>to the infirmary.<BR>took my property and my uniform away.<BR>made me wear a paper gown.<BR>i was in there for three days.<BR>most boring three days of my life.<BR>won\\\'t do that again.<BR><BR>tell joe<BR>hello.<BR>tell \\\'im<BR>we\\\'ll play dominos<BR>again.<BR>someday.<BR><BR>tell joe<BR>hello.<BR>tell \\\'im<BR>we\\\'ll play dominos<BR>again.<BR>someday.</P>'),(3210,'<p><b>\r\n\r\n<p>(on the BART platform)\r\n\\\"WHOOOOOOOOOOOO!\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(on a BART train packed to Japanese commute levels)\r\n\\\"DUDE IT\\\'S GETTING HOT IN HERE, YOU KNOW WHAT THAT MEANS!!!\\\"\r\n\\\"Hey sit on my lap!\\\"\r\n\\\"NO WAY DUDE I\\\'M NOT A FAG, FAG!\\\"\r\n\\\"You\\\'re a fag!\\\"\r\n\\\"SHUT UP FAG!\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(outside of the Cat Club)\r\n\\\"HEY DANH YOU WANNA WATCH ME GO GET DRUNK IN ALLEY?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(entering the Cat Club)\r\n\\\"I\\\'ve never met a man with a lavender wallet before.  Congratulations.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(inside of the Cat Club)\r\n\\\"!@#$%^!!!!!!\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(walking to the Arrow)\r\n\\\"Hey hot stuff why you hanging out with that snowbunny?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>(on the BART train home)\r\n\\\"Uhhhh.... I really need to pee.\\\"\r\n\\\"YOU WILL NOT PISS ON MY HUSBAND!\\\"\r\n\\\"Huh?\\\"\r\n\\\"YOU ARE NOT GOING TO FUCKING PISS ON MY HUSBAND!\\\"\r\n\\\"But I!\\\"\r\n\\\"DUDE DO YOU WANT TO GET HIT?\\\"\r\n\\\"Uh!\\\"\r\n\\\"ANSWER THE FUCKING QUESTION!!! DO YOU WANT\r\nTO GET HIT IN THE HEAD?\\\"\r\n\\\"I uh...\\\"\r\n\\\"DO YOU WANT TO GET HIT, MOTHERFUCKER?\\\"'),(3211,'<P>\r\nMy New Year\\\'s Resolution this year was not to curse anymore. It is going to be a long road. When I first made it in Texas while visiting an old friend, implementing it was no problem at all. I was able to cut my cursing by 80%. Things were going great. I felt really good about it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMy rationalization was that my uncle had said once that his dirty sailor language had kept him back in life. He didn\\\'t get promotions beyond a certain point because his superiors didn\\\'t want to risk his mouth getting them in trouble.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI curse so much that I actually curse for other people, too. If someone says they are having a problem, then I supply them with curses to punctuate it with. I might do that for someone who does not appreciate it and who could affect my life with their decision making process.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen you don\\\'t curse you have to explain yourself more. It helps build vocabulary usage. No more string of expletives. Say what you really mean instead.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAfter arriving back in San Francisco, stepping off the plane, I realized immediately where the cursing came from as I waited from 10:40 PM to 11:30 PM for my bags to hit the luggage pickup carousel. I started cursing. When I got to the long term parking lot and could not find my car, I started cursing. When it took me 20 minutes to find a parking spot within 10 blocks of my apartment, I started cursing really bad.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBy midway through the next day, after trying to get hold of a California Employment Development Department officer to determine why I have not received an unemployment check for the last month, I really started cursing a blue streak.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIt was right about then I realized cursing allows me to get out negative energy that builds up. This city makes my blood boil. Everything is compressed badness.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI waited in line at the DMV for an hour this morning to get license plates for my car.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI waited in line at the Parking and Traffic Department for a parking permit so I don\\\'t get so many parking tickets. I had mailed off $90 worth of parking fees last week. $90 is 30% of the available cash in my bank account at the moment. A residential parking permit would keep me out of the DPT\\\'s talons. I waited in line for a good 50 minutes, standing. A bimbo made friends with a man farther up in the line and cut in when I was 4 heads to the front. When I finally got to the DPT agent, they said my lease agreement was TOO OLD. 2 years old is TOO OLD.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOn the way back from the DPT, on the Bart, a bum started cursing me out calling me a STUPID CRACKER MOTHER FUCKER FUCK YOU I\\\'LL KICK YOU HEAD IN YOU MOTHERFUCKER because I would not buy his Bart transfer for a quarter. I felt his spittle strike my face.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI retaliated, WHAT THE FUCK IS YOUR FUCKING PROBLEM ASSHOLE... etc.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe problem with not cursing is I, a godless individual, begin to internalize what I do not retaliate against. I started feeling like some higher power, God, is against me.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod sending me to stand in line.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod delaying my baggage claims.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod clouding my memory of where the car is parked.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod sending DPT to ticket me over and over again.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod lengthening lines at the DPS.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod sending bimbos to cut in front of me in a line that He already made really long at the DPT.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod and the DPT rejecting my lease because it is too old.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod setting bums against me over a quarter, after a really long and bad day.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI think I\\\'ll be back to feeling small again in a day or two after this recent episode with the homeless person clears from my mind. And, I\\\'ll deal with God again. Frustrations with God. Ideally I would work God out of the picture and gain a higher understanding of all the lists of frustrating and violating happenings around me. \r\n</P>'),(3214,'<P>i was coming down hayman road, and i see this hot little piece of split-tail walking on the shoulder.  she was hitting it pretty good.  had on a backback.</P><P>she had this <B>ASS</B>.</P><P>i took the cruiser out to spring lake highway and turned around, and by the time i get back to hayman, she had turned down the dirt road by pickett\\\'s.  i pulled up beside her.</P><P>i asked her, \\\"where ya headed?\\\"</P><P>\\\"up the road here.\\\"</P><P>wasn\\\'t nervous at all.  \\\"what kinda accent izzat?\\\"</P><P>\\\"swedish.\\\"</P><P>\\\"sweden.  no foolin\\\'.  what\\\'re ya doin\\\' <U>here</U>?\\\"</P><P>\\\"just living with my boyfriend.\\\"</P><P>\\\"uh-huh.  you live around here?\\\"</P><P>\\\"yeah.  just up the road here.\\\"</P><P>the one house on that road had been empty for at least ten years.  it didn\\\'t gel.</P><P>\\\"you guys just move in?\\\"</P><P>her eyes darted to the right.  nervous.  \\\"yeah, we\\\'ve been there about a month.\\\"</P><P>\\\"uh-huh.  so.  didja get everything you wanted for christmas?\\\"</P><P>\\\"i didn\\\'t want very much.\\\"</P><P>\\\"alright, then.  you have a good one.\\\"</P><P>\\\"ok.  you, too.\\\"</P><P>i turned the cruiser around and headed back out to hayman.  it didn\\\'t gel.</P><BR><BR><BR>       *         *         *<BR><BR><BR><P>i wait \\\'til it\\\'s good and dark and take the cruiser on up the dirt road where it forks off toward the house.  i stop about a hundred yards from where the house is and turn the lights off and get out.  it\\\'s light enough to see where i\\\'m going.</P><P>there\\\'s light flickering in the front windows, like from a fire.  i ease up and look in.</P><P>she\\\'s laying under a quilt reading a book.  there\\\'s a fire going and a candle burning on a plate sitting on the floor beside her.  off to the side, there\\\'s a bong and a lighter and a bag of weed resting on the carpet.  over against the wall by the fireplace, there\\\'s a few boxes full of books and stuff.  i don\\\'t see anybody else.</P><P>so, i walk around the house looking into all the windows, and it doesn\\\'t look like there\\\'s anybody else in there.  i trip over this axe resting up against the back of the house and stumble a little bit, but i don\\\'t go down.  i catch the axe handle before it falls to the ground, so it doens\\\'t make any noise.</P><P>after a look around in back of the house, i walk around front and knock on the door.</P><BR><BR><BR>        *         *        *<BR><BR><BR><P>i\\\'m deep into <U>the magic mountain</U>, very high, when there\\\'s a knock at the front door.  i get unspeakably scared.  for a moment, i don\\\'t do anything, just lay there frozen.  another knock.  i go over to the door.</P><P>\\\"who is it?\\\"</P><P>\\\"it\\\'s officer hughes.  i was the one talking to ya this afternoon.\\\"</P><P>i press my face up against the window furthest away from the door and try to see who\\\'s on the stoop.  it\\\'s that peckerwood cop from earlier today.  but there\\\'s no police car out front.</P><P>\\\"where\\\'s your car?\\\"</P><P>\\\"what?\\\"</P><P>\\\"WHERE\\\'S YOUR CAR?\\\"</P><P>\\\"i was afraid it\\\'d get stuck in all the sand.  uh, can you open the door, ma\\\'am?\\\"</P><P>\\\"just a minute.\\\"</P><P>i hide the bong and the weed in a box of clothes then unlock the door and open it.  it\\\'s the first time it\\\'s been opened since we moved in.  spider webs trail from the door frame to the door as it opens.  he eyes them.</P><P>\\\"can i come in?\\\"</P><P>\\\"i guess.\\\"</P><P>he comes into the entrance area, his frame seeming to fill the space.  he looms in that corner of the living room.</P><P>\\\"so you all by yourself here tonight?  where\\\'s your boyfriend?\\\"</P><P>well, he\\\'s in the pokey, but i don\\\'t want this guy to know he\\\'s gone.</P><P>\\\"uh, he stepped out for a minute.  should be right back.\\\"</P><P>\\\"uh-huh.\\\"</P><P>he flicks all the light switches by the front door.</P><P>\\\"ya\\\'ll having a power outage?\\\"</P><P>i don\\\'t say anything.</P><P>\\\"you\\\'re not supposed to be here, are you, ma\\\'am?\\\"</P><P>i don\\\'t say anything.</P><BR><BR><BR>        *           *         *<BR><BR><BR><P>i arrest her for burglary of a dwelling and trespassing and take her down to the car and leave her in the back seat.  after going back in and looking through all the stuff and finding the bong and the weed and some pipes and some other stuff, i add possession and paraphernalia to the arrest report.  i put out the fire and the candle and seal the doors and tape them up so i can tell if anybody else goes in there.  wait around another half an hour to see if the \\\"boyfriend\\\" would show back up.  she finally admits that he isn\\\'t staying there anymore.</P><P>i let her take the book she was reading with her.  the only other things she took were a passport and some money.</P><P>i didn\\\'t do what i was planning on.  don\\\'t know what made me change my mind.  something.</P>'),(3212,'<P>i miss him the most when i wake up in the night and turn to find him gone.  after that, i get mad.  mad that this is just the last in a long line of stunts he\\\'s pulled, mad that he\\\'s left me alone.  and i was mad long before he did this.  he could have gotten a job.  i would have if i could, but i don\\\'t have a work visa.</P><P>i get scared going through the woods at night for water.  when he took me with him, the forest was enchanting, nocturnal birdcalls and the stars in the leaves.  the cows are scary, too, but never a bother.</P><P>he\\\'s been in there for six weeks now.  his next court date is another three weeks away.</P><P>what i did on christmas day, by anna j:  i got high and took a bath.  ate kippers and a can of sliced new potatoes.</P><P>while i\\\'ve been waiting, i\\\'ve finished <U>the red and the black</U> and started <U>the magic mountain</U>.  after i put the candle out at night, i sit with the guitar and figure out \\\"badlands\\\" and \\\"bizarre love triangle\\\" and \\\"how soon is now?\\\"</P><P>he continues to write his book in jail.  he says he\\\'s burning himself down.  he says all his memories, all the funny things he\\\'s heard, the moves from all the games he\\\'s played, his preferences, the shoot-em-ups he\\\'s seen, the void, the circus, the funk, the song and dance of his life will all go into it.</P><P>i play a little game.  how long will i or can i wait for him?  there\\\'s a little over a thousand dollars left.  if i stay here, how long will the money last?  what if he gets sent to prison?</P><P>while i\\\'m in town, i call a travel agent.  i could get a ticket to stockholm for seven fifty.</P><BR><BR><BR>          *       *      *<BR><BR><BR><P>anna hasn\\\'t been to see me for two weeks, so i know something\\\'s wrong, but i can\\\'t do anything about it from inside.</P><P>finally, my court date rolls around.  they drop it down to possession of less than an ounce and give me time served.  almost two months.  they don\\\'t let you walk out of the court right then, though.  you have to go back to the holding cell and wait with all the bros for the proceedings to finish up.  then they load you in the van.  \\\"watch your step, gentlemen,\\\" the deputy helping us in says.  \\\"and i use the term \\\'gentlemen\\\' very loosely.\\\"  i just about told him something, but i was afraid they\\\'d slap some kind of charge on me somehow.</P><P>they process me out with the property i came in with:  street clothes, shoes, my wallet, and a pack of rolling papers.  and the twenty-three bucks leftover from what anna put in my account.</P><P>i catch a bus headed for downtown.  go see if howard\\\'s home.  i\\\'m not going to spend any of the money i have on weed, though.  god knows what\\\'s happened to anna, so this might be the only cash i have for awhile.  have to see if he\\\'ll give me a buzz on the house.</P><P>howard says he hasn\\\'t seen anna for three weeks.  he gets me high and fronts me a quarter.  he knows i\\\'ll get around to paying him for it sooner or later.</P><P>when i get to the house, there are yellow crime scene tape and seals on all the doors.  i start crying.  i break the ones on the back door and go in.  there aren\\\'t any chalk outlines or bloodstains anywhere.  all our stuff has been rifled through, and the bong and the cigar box with the pipes and other paraphernalia are gone.  and her guitar is still here.</P>'),(3213,'<p>The tables are arranged randomly outside the shop. I don\\\'t even know what it is, this shop, I guess coffee? Some sort of theme is involved. There has to be. The sun is glinting off the tops of the tables. The tabletops. Yes, there she is. It\\\'s shining off her sunglasses. She looks ridiculous.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Hi\\\", she says, barely audible. The tables are so bright. We sit for a few moments. She adjusts her sunglasses and asks:</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Do you want to see a movie?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>But I can\\\'t hear her. I only see her mouth open. I understand what she\\\'s saying but I don\\\'t hear the words -- I can only see her teeth. Bright liquid teeth. Maybe she hasn\\\'t even really asked me this. She might as well have. She could have said anything. I comprehend the words but don\\\'t hear them -- her teeth are white and clear, a little translucent around the edges, the bottom of her tongue is gray and metallic (like a lizard\\\'s), there is some sort of film covering her teeth, it\\\'s glistening in the sunlight, what is it? </p>\r\n<p>Curious, I lean over the table and press my mouth to hers. I suck, I suck on her tongue and press against her teeth. Her mouth is stale and familiar. I back away. She gives me the smile of an uncomprehending animal.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"You\\\'re so sweet.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>.</p>\r\n<p>Her brother was beaten to death with a folding chair in 1997. They never found who did it. She said it was like he was just gone -- it could have been anyone. He didn\\\'t look like a person, she said. It is as though once it happened, once they did that to his face, he simply vanished. He didn\\\'t even die. He just disappeared. What they buried could\\\'ve been anyone. This is what she said.</p>\r\n<p>.</p>\r\n<p>We return to her apartment and begin, I suppose, to see a movie. I had in my mind a theatre, but instead the situation seems to involve watching something on her small television in the company of a rotund and strikingly stupid roommate.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Oh I\\\'ve seen this before\\\", pipes the roommate, \\\"I think it was based on a documentary.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>She is one of these people who, rather than knowing anything about something, know instead nothing about almost everything and regard any type of criticism or commentary, beyond the totally inane, as a kind of affront against a body of knowledge she only assumes to exist. She\\\'s a chubby fraud who will latch lovingly onto any source of predigested information and engorge herself until she\\\'s confident she can parrot a line or two about something that might be of passing interest to another potential victim.</p>\r\n<p>Something inside me turns. I retire to the bathroom.</p>\r\n<p>Lock the door and get the water running. In the mirror my face looks the same way it did six years ago. It\\\'s the retro me. I\\\'ve remodeled myself to look the way I did when I was originally trying to model myself into something I would look like in the future.</p>\r\n<p>I vomit, weakly, into the sink.</p>\r\n<p>Again.</p>\r\n<p>Chunky splashing.</p>\r\n<p>Bilefilled treat.</p>\r\n<p>I stare at my sepia surprise and wonder, if you eat alphabet soup, if the letters will still be legible after vomiting them into a toilet a few hours afterwards.</p>\r\n<p>Another halfhearted retch and I\\\'m out of there. I leave the apartment without a wave or goodbye -- she will probably be offended and come up with something half-vicious to say to me when she calls tomorrow afternoon. I look forward to it.</p>'),(3215,'<p>\r\nJust the way she smiles<br>\r\nEverything she says and does<br>\r\nfills me with desire\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe makes me tingle<br>\r\neverytime she touches me<br>\r\nI miss her so much\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can see her face<br>\r\neven though she isn\\\'t here<br>\r\nHow i wish she were\r\n</p>'),(3216,'In approximately half an hour, my workmates and I are going to the pub. Why? Because today is my last day of work. I am not coming back here ever again. I\\\'m leaving the boring mundane job of being a Communications Administrator at the Medical School to become a Communications Assistant elsewhere, where i will be producing their magazine in a young vibrant environment. I am tremendously excited. But for now, I have to clean out my desk, make more copies of my <A HREF=\\\"http://hubris.co.nz/zine.html\\\">zine</a> while I still have access to a photocopier and maybe pocket some more pens. I gave my boss a bottle of wine. They didn\\\'t give me anything. They\\\'re all going to be made redundant soon anyways. But my point was, yay, I am leaving. Hooray. '),(3217,'<b>Background:</b><br>\r\nIkea has a hypothetical store in, say, Plymouth, which they will be closing so that they may open a bigger store a half-mile down the road in Columbus. Ikea Corporate determines that they should liquidate everything at their Plymouth store, meaning they wouldn\\\'t have to move it (and thus pass that cost on to you). A perfect case of common sense, if you will, practicality. Sunday is to be a theoretical clearence sale. Everything is a go at 60% off.  The supposed store opens to the public at 9a, but its employees must be there by 6a. Saturday night is an icy one in Plymouth, most probably a good night to stay in, if you will.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>The Incident @ Plymouth:</b><br>\r\nEmployee A gets to the store at 6a and is shocked to see a line already forming around the building. Employee A heads for the employee-entrance and learns that these folks have been in line since approximately 2a. Employee A might say something to the effect of: <i>\\\"I mean, I like Ikea as much as the next Joe. Hell, I even work there. But I wouldn\\\'t sit in the cold for 7 hours <i>to meet Jesus</i> let alone to get 60% off a floor model piece of build-it-yourself furniture.\\\"</i> Employee A is amazed to see that there are about 300 fellow employees there as well, who are all slowly getting started up for inventory. But imagine if you will that before too long, there\\\'s this big uproar. It seems that one of the line-waiters thought he saw people coming out with merchandise, so he took it upon himself to illegally enter the closed store, making his way to the front of the building and letting in all the folks who were waiting outside!<br><br>\r\nIn Plymouth, the people probably wouldn\\\'t leave, and would actually start rampaging, leaving Ikea to summon the police to come out and clear the store.\r\n\r\n'),(3218,'once when i was younger, i cut down a tree.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit was an amazing feat, it felt, at the moment the final fibres broke free from the mooring of the deep roots.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe axe was so dull it took the better part of a day to cut the trunk to a stump. now you may think it was because it was a large, aged tree. alas, this was not the case.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit was just barely passed being a sapling, really. young and springy under the edge of the worn blade.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndeep in the summer-time heat i swung the heavy head into it\\\'s brown flesh. once i realized what i had finally done the joy faded.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni had ended a life! brought short a final breath to another living thing. i had no need, no purpose in the act save the act itself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nof course my mother had been more concerned of my use of the rusted old axe than the withering branches left on the forest floor. i was safe. my grandfather had been a carpenter. he had taught me how to use it; how to cut a tree. it had been two years previous, much to the shagrin of my grandma and mom.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni felt empty sadness at taking a life and in the fact that i\\\'d never be able to tell the one that taught me this skill of my deeds.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit was for him and his memory that i had felled that young tree. it was a final release of the feelings pent up i had from his funeral. <br><br>'),(3219,'<P>the morning snapped open like a window shade.  he had fallen asleep on top of the blankets and awakened cold.  the fire had gone out sometime before morning.</P><P>the first thing he thought was:</P><P>i need to find a new bar.</P><P>* a bar where everyone has a nickname - duke or red or pops<BR>* a bar where they ring a bell every time they get a tip<BR>* a bar where the waitresses wear little red shoes<BR>* a bar where the jukebox has some steely dan on it<BR>* a bar where the bartender\\\'s got a gun<BR>* a bar with all-day happy hour<BR>* a bar with pickled eggs and pigs\\\' feet for sale</P><P>he couldn\\\'t think of such a place within walking distance.  he was 86\\\'d from the pinewood.  from the wheel and roo\\\'s and connections.  86\\\'d from the sail inn and the hilltop and the castle.  the reef.  the blue penguin.  zip\\\'s.  the seaside.  and that wasn\\\'t counting the restaurants.</P><P>he thought of spartan\\\'s.  he could go there.  he got high, got breakfast, cleaned up.  he looked around the living room and remembered the last morning he and anna had spent there.  when he had awakened, she\\\'d been lying on her stomach writing in a magazine.</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m doing a quiz to see if we\\\'re comapatible,\\\" she said.  \\\"i\\\'ve been filling in your answers, too, so far.\\\"</P><P>\\\"uh-huh.\\\"  he sat up and looked for the bong.  \\\"it should really work, then.\\\"</P><P>\\\"listen:  what kind of leisure activity do you like to do?\\\"</P><P>\\\"the kind where you take off your clothes.\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, it has choices.\\\"</P><P>she read them off.</P><P>he took a backpack with some clothes, two blankets, a small pillow, his notebooks, toilet paper, matches, hygiene items, a couple of books, and some sardines.  everything else he packed up and stowed in a far corner of the attic.</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m a bum now,\\\" he thought.  \\\"this makes it official.\\\"</P><P>he couldn\\\'t do anything about the tape and the seals on the door.  he left them and got out of there.  tied the axe to the side of his backpack, used the field toilet one last time, spaded dirt over the mess, left the toilet and the spade in place, hidden back in the woods.</P><P>the day was clear and cold, no clouds at all, just the blue dome of sky.  he was wearing a pullover sweater and a coat and was still a little cold.</P><P>\\\"it\\\'ll get warmer once the sun comes up,\\\" he thought.</P><BR><BR><BR>     *        *       *<BR><BR><BR><P>the man on the barstool next to him was drunk.  \\\"i\\\'ll tell you why puerto ricans are better lovers,\\\" the man said.  \\\"we don\\\'t just go in and out.  we also go around in circles.\\\"  he made a circle with the thumb and forefinger of one hand then stuck the forefinger of the other halfway through it and ran it around the inside of the circle.  \\\"think about that,\\\" the man said.  \\\"all the different ways you can stick your dick into a woman.\\\"</P><P>\\\"until you opened my eyes, i always thought there was just one way.  so what does that make?  two ways?\\\"</P><P>\\\"see, you learn something today.\\\"</P><P>\\\"yeah.  thanks.\\\"</P><P>he got up to leave and tipped the bartender two dollars, which was pretty good, since he was drinking fifty cent drafts.  the bartender handed him his backpack from behind the bar.</P><P>at the police department, they had no arrest record for an anna jarring.  try the sheriff\\\'s department, they said.  he would try them the next day, since it was a mile out of town, and it was getting dark.</P><BR><BR><BR>        *         *         *<BR><BR><BR><P>the library was crowded for six o\\\'clock on a thursday night.  they knew him there, knew his quirks and discounted the axe tied to his backpack.  a one-armed man sat at the table across from him.  books sat on the shelves like massed paper butterflies.  he opened his notebook and started where he left off.  he decided to write about when the hero, a thinly disguied version of himself, came to the library the day after getting thrown out of college for the first time, hungover, wanting to sleep behind the shelves.</P><P>he looked around at all the people.  they were perfect, perfect in their beauty and their ugliness, in their wisdom and deformity.  he, too, was perfect.  all that remained to do in this perfect life was to write it all down.  everything else would take care of itself.</P><P>when the libary closed at eight, he went into the park outside and scouted out a place in a corner of the park in a little alleyway between a toolshed, the teen hall, and the post office.  it was sheltered from the wind and isolated enough that no one would go back there.  he spread out his blankets and rolled himself up in them and fell asleep almost immediately.</P> '),(3220,'<p>columbia is cold, in the morning. it comes as a surprise, even this far into the winter, for the cold to sting my eyes and make them tear when i walk outside into the sunlight and cross the big street, four lanes, and enter the stream of students, tears welling in my eyes, refracting the sunlight. diamond shards of light, bright as the sun. i think i have more tears than most other people. last week it was 70 degrees.</p><p>\r\ni\\\'m talking to someone who has struck some kind of chord within me, one three five, resonant. i am close to tears of happiness. at this point, what\\\'s the difference?</p><p>\r\nat school. it\\\'s my last semester.</p><p>\r\nteacher today brings in slide projector. shows us paintings. oh yes. can\\\'t understand one without the other. studying literature is basically studying art history anyway. i\\\'m not very good at drawing or sculpture.</p><p>\r\ncold wind. tears. newspaper blows across red bricks, squirrels think i don\\\'t see them hiding behind the trash can. little birds. i am no longer a cigarette smoker, i can smell everything. i see the moment, and i put it in its paper boat and watch it bob and spin as it floats downstream. </p><p>\r\nevery day is an eternity. every day is a lifetime. every morning there is cold wind, tears, brightness. every night: warmth, comfort, and rest. '),(3221,'<P>officer hughes took her to jail.  he surrendered her property to a guard behind a desk who counted the money twice and put it in an envelope with her passport.</P><P>\\\"can you let \\\'er hang onto her book there?\\\" hughes asked.</P><P>after some conversation between the guard and his supervisor, she was given the book and put in an empty holding cell.  she laid on the wooden bench and read, drifting in and out of sleep.  four hours later, they retrieved her and took her mugshots and fingerprints.  breakfast came around four thirty:  a hard-boiled egg, two pancakes, syrup, and milk.  she ate most of it.  when the trustee came for the empty tray, she got the guard\\\'s attention.</P><P>\\\"can i send a note to someone who\\\'s in jail here?\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, that\\\'s not allowed.\\\"</P><P>around ten, two detectives from the sheriff\\\'s department, a man and a woman, came to interview her.  the man was short, balding, wore a tire.  the woman was matronly, slightly plump, with an open face and dark roots in her blonde hair.</P><P>\\\"does either of you have a cigarrette?\\\" she asked them.</P><P>\\\"you\\\'re not allowed to smoke in here, honey,\\\" the woman told her.\\\"</P><P>she rolled her eyes.</P><P>so where is it exactly that you\\\'re from?\\\" the woman asked.</P><P>\\\"sweden.  vaxjo.\\\"</P><P>the man was writing on a legal pad.  \\\"vaxjo?\\\"</P><P>\\\"v-a-x-j-o.  with two little dots over the a and the o.\\\"</P><P>the man showed her the legal pad.  \\\"like that?\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, they get two dots apiece.\\\"</P><P>\\\"oh.  umlauts.\\\"</P><P>\\\"also called diareses.  so you speak german?\\\"</P><P>\\\"some.  do you?\\\"</P><P>\\\"fluently.\\\"</P><P>\\\"well, i don\\\'t,\\\" the woman said.  \\\"how long have you been living out there?\\\"</P><P>she imagined she was smoking a cigarrette, halfway through with it.</P><P>\\\"since the first of december.\\\"</P><P>\\\"what were you doing out there?\\\"  the woman was chewing gum like she was grinding coffee beans between her molars.</P><P>\\\"just living with my boyfriend.\\\"</P><P>\\\"who\\\'s your boyfriend?\\\"</P><P>she looked at the woman for a second.  \\\"that sounds like a 60s girl group song, doesn\\\'t it?  \\\'who\\\'s your boyfriend?\\\'  maybe one written by carole king?\\\"</P><P>they just looked at her.</P><P>\\\"carole king?  c\\\'mon:  \\\'tapestry\\\'?  <I>it\\\'s too late, baby, now, it\\\'s too late</I>?\\\"</P><P>they stared at her.</P><P>\\\"i guess not,\\\" she said.</P><P>\\\"you had over a thousand dollars with you.  why were you staying out there?\\\"</P><P>\\\"couldn\\\'t beat the rent.\\\"</P><P>\\\"and what about the marijuana?\\\"</P><P>\\\"what about it?\\\"</P><BR><BR><BR>        *          *          *<BR><BR><BR><P>on their first night in brooksville, he took her to the hilltop.  they did vodka shots and drank grapefruit juice.  when they left, she was leaning against his arm and shoulder, and they were both laughing.  in his conversations at the bar, he had made fun of everyone he talked to without them knowing it, and she thought that was sophisticated.</P><P>\\\"you know bert?\\\"</P><P>\\\"which one was he?\\\"</P><P>\\\"the baseball player.\\\"</P><P>\\\"yeah.  ok.\\\"</P><P>\\\"he can tell you the speed limit of any road you\\\'re driving on.\\\"</P><P>\\\"anywhere in the world?\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, just around here.\\\"</P><P>\\\"and why izzat impressive?\\\"</P><P>\\\"well, sometimes you\\\'re driving and you don\\\'t know what the speed limit is, so you don\\\'t know how fast you can go.  they have speed limit signs whenever you first turn onto a road, but sometimes you miss one.  he knows the speed limit everywhere around here.  i don\\\'t know if he memorized them or if it\\\'s instinct.\\\"</P><P>in the middle of the parking lot, she pulled him around and kissed him:  the moon was waxing, there was broken glass underfoot, cars were streaming down the hill into the housing project behind the bar to score crack.  sometimes, kissing him was the only thing that would shut him up.</P><P>they went home and fucked between two mirrors hung on opposite sides of the bed.  you turned your head, and it looked like you were copulating into infinity, part of a hindu <I>deus ex machina</I> of identical lovers.</P><P>afterward, he got up and picked a 45 out of a box and put it on the turntable.</P><P>\\\"i want this to be the last song the dj plays at our wedding reception.\\\"</P><P>\\\"what\\\'s it called?\\\"</P><P>\\\"\\\'ever, ever, ever\\\'.  alan freed used to play it at the end of every show.\\\"</P><P>\\\"oh, yeah?\\\"</P><P>\\\"oh, yeah.\\\"</P><P>he turned the a/c down to sixty-eight and got back into bed.  after the record stopped, there was the hum of the speakers.  he turned the stereo off with a remote control.</P><BR><BR><BR>         *           *           *<BR><BR><BR><P>at six that night, an hour after supper, they took her down to a room where she showered and dressed in the uniform they gave her.  her clothes were taken from her.  she was placed in the women\\\'s dormitory, where she made her bunk and went right to sleep.</P><P>the next day, she played spades with three other women, read the newspaper and <U>the magic mountain</U>.  it was cold in the dorm.  in the afternoon, she was taken back up front to the interview room.  the male detective from the day before was back.  she sat at the table.</P><P>\\\"ms. jarring, it seems you\\\'ve overstayed your visa by about sixteen months.\\\"</P><P>\\\"shit,\\\" she thought.  \\\"i knew we should have gotten married.\\\"</P>'),(3222,'<p>I can still hear the scratched voices bashing mercilessly inside my head</p>\r\n<BR>\r\n\\\"So help us all-So help us all-So help us all\\\"<br>\r\nThe worn and tired record is skipping<br>\r\nIts crackles shakes like earthquakes<br>\r\nIts pops explode like mines<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nMy grandfather grunts as he painfully fixes the needle<br>\r\nLetters pour out of the gapping hole in his neck<br>\r\nBut they are too jumbled for me to read<br>\r\nMy grandmother sits silently<br>\r\nHer lips are removed from her face<br>\r\nAnd I stare desperately at the record player<br>\r\nPraying to be saved<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nWaves and currents rush violently through my brain<br>\r\nMy body is fighting in spasms<br>\r\nI piss all over myself<br>\r\nA sweet sickening smell<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe record entertains my grandparents<br>\r\nBut I to hear it backwards<br>\r\nThe narrator is tripping on his words<br>\r\nLost in confusion and turmoil<br>\r\nEqually scared<br>\r\nThe plunge is coming<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nI slip beneath the conscious<br>\r\nAnd drop onto the floor of my grandparents war home<br>\r\nI scream at the record player<br>\r\n\\\"Save Me\\\"<br>\r\nBut itâ€™s too late<br>\r\nI have slipped into this thing again<br>\r\n<BR>\r\n\\\"So help us all\\\"<br>\r\nCries the navigator<br>\r\nThe storm is vicious and vengeful<br>\r\nThe controls are broken<br>\r\nCommon sense lost and forgotten<br>\r\nFear seeps inside the crew<br>\r\nGnawing through composure<br>\r\nSweat floods their skin<br>\r\n\\\"Where in godâ€™s name are we headed?\\\"<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nAs panic swells<br>\r\nThe captain steps into the shadows<br>\r\nHis voice raped from madness<br>\r\nYelling commands from the darkness<br>\r\nUseless system checks<br>\r\nControl the mechanics<br>\r\nIn hopes of restoring order<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nBut in one sweeping gesture<br>\r\nA dark quiet still overtakes the ship<br>\r\nAnd comprehension fills buckets of blood<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe captain stares at his reflection in chrome wheels<br>\r\nTrying to catch a glimpse of himself<br>\r\nFinding only a stranger<br>\r\nHe stops lying to himself<br>\r\nTruth burns through skin<br>\r\nAnd destroys minds<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe power dies<br>\r\nThe crew stands stiff<br>\r\nLooking to the captain<br>\r\nHis face stricken with shame<br>\r\nRecognizing no one<br>\r\nTotally immobilized<br>\r\nAs fate plays its hand<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe ship collapses<br>\r\n\\\"Into the abyss we go\\\"<br>\r\nCheer the faceless children<br>\r\n\\\"Iâ€™ll eat your hearts!\\\"<br>\r\nScreams the captain to himself<br>\r\nCareful no one hears<br>\r\nProtecting his crew of skeletons<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nEvil is calm and quiet<br>\r\nBut its directions are senseless chaos<br>\r\nThe first mate pleads for life<br>\r\nThe captain stars back blankly through endless nights<br>\r\nFalling further towards the bottom of the sea<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nWater breaks into the ship<br>\r\nThe sea is swallowing screams<br>\r\nThe first mate goes overboard<br>\r\nNo one notices, no one cares<br>\r\nThe captain bows to the unknown<br>\r\nAnd the sharp terror of death takes control<br>\r\nUsing the captain as its vessel<br>\r\nRipping and rushing through the crew<br>\r\nThe captain kills them all<br>\r\nAnd they sink into death<br>\r\nAs the captain floats in pain<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe sun has never existed<br>\r\nThe air is a poison<br>\r\nWhen breathed it cuts the lungs<br>\r\nThe waves tear away the captainâ€™s flesh<br>\r\nViolently shoving him underneath<br>\r\nNothing but darkness<br>\r\nBruised above water<br>\r\nEmptied below<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThe mermaids cry<br>\r\nDeafening screams<br>\r\nThe world\\\'s weight<br>\r\nIts rot and decay<br>\r\nAll its promises of shit and piss<br>\r\nHold the captainâ€™s head under<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThat Saturday morning<br>\r\nI took the boys down to the beach for a walk<br>\r\nSam was babbling about space<br>\r\nEric was lost in thoughts of eternity<br>\r\nThere lay the captain\\\'s limp body<br>\r\nLying by the deserted rocks were the waves snap in<br>\r\nThe rest of the families paid no notice<br>\r\nContinuing to play in sin and sand<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nI left the boys by the bench<br>\r\nAnd checked his pulse<br>\r\nHe was still alive<br>\r\nMangled, broken and disfigured but alive<br>\r\nIn his eyes I saw that his soul had been eaten<br>\r\nI turned him over and buried his face in the wet sand<br>\r\n<BR>\r\nThen I took my sons home and killed them too<br>\r\nBashing their skulls in with a hammer<br>\r\nCutting my wifeâ€™s face with the kitchen knife<br>\r\nI didn\\\'t hear her screams<br>\r\nNor did I feel her nails dig through my face<br>\r\nBut I remember hearing the crisp crack of her skull smashing<br>\r\nAs I swung my hammer around and hit her with a perfect strike.<br>\r\n'),(3223,'<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<li>The Processes of Tolerance and the Deviant in Poonlarp\\\'s <i>Ten Digressions</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Poonlarp, <i>Ten Digressions</i>, and The Subaltern: Transforming Bourgeois Boundaries<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Mapping, Etching, Complicating: Racism in Poonlarp and the Random Identity of Politics in <i>Ten Digressions</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Poonlarp, <i>Ten Digressions</i>, and The Privileged: Destabilizing Transgendered Text<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Poonlarp Re-marking Erasure: <i>Ten Digressions</i> and the Resistance of Futility<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Excavating, Ungendering, Fetishing: Breath in Poonlarp and the Deaf Performatitivy of Notions in <i>Ten Digressions</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Boundaries and Fragmentation in <i>Ten Digressions</i>: Poonlarp Writing Random Voices<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Power and Theory in <i>Ten Digressions</i>: Poonlarp\\\'s Resistant Representation<br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Polyvocal Monotheism and the Sexuality of Gendered Production in Poonlarp\\\'s <i>Ten Digressions</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<li>Naming, Mapping, Interpreting: Poonlarp\\\'s Patriarchy and the Aesthetic Attraction of Bodies in <i>Ten Digressions</i><br><br>\r\n</ul>\r\n'),(3224,'<p>\r\nI like the way she smiles at me<br>\r\nwith sadness in her eyes<br>\r\nI like the way she says goodbye<br>\r\nI like the way she cries\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\\\'s something in the way she moves<br>\r\nwith elegance and style<br>\r\nThere\\\'s something in the way she proves<br>\r\nShe\\\'s loved me for a while while\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI like the way she cheers me up<br>\r\nwhen we are far apart<br>\r\nI like the way she opens up<br>\r\nher kind and caring heart\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI like the way she takes speed humps<br>\r\nat thrity miles an hour<br>\r\nI like the way she smells so sweet<br>\r\nJust like a autumn flower\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI like the way she looks at me<br>\r\nI love her skinny hips<br>\r\nI love the way she kisses me<br>\r\nI love her cherry lips\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut most of all I love her <br>\r\nfor the soul that is inside<br>\r\nTo know her heart belongs to me,<br>\r\nfills me with so much pride.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3225,'<P>i was dreaming i was in the ocean, naked, dog-paddling, drinking the water, when somebody shook me awake.  it was an old man with a beard.  people in town called him \\\"the can man\\\" because he pushed a grocery cart around and collected aluminum cans.  his cart was behind him.</P><P>he said, \\\"you\\\'re in my spot, bud.\\\"</P><P>\\\"there\\\'s room enough here for both of us.\\\"</P><P>\\\"no, there ain\\\'t.  getcher ass up.\\\"</P><P>i got up and grabbed my blankets and pillow and pack and went out into the park and sat on a bench.  it was windy out there and cold.  the sky was full with two thousand stars.  i took everything into the bathroom.  it was a one-person model.  locked the door and rolled a joint.</P><P>when i was finished, i wandered back into the park and went over to the alleyway.  he was snoring.  i went back in there and laid down beside him.</P><BR><BR><BR>         *          *          *<BR><BR><BR><P>i woke up to birdsong from the trees and squirrels chittering.  the old man was curled up against me.  i went out into the park and took inventory:  sixteen dollars and fifty cents, four cans of sardines, about an eighth of an ounce of weed.  the sun was lightening the sky over the bandshell.  i went into the bathroom and washed my face and rolled a joint and smoked it back on the bench.  when it got light enough to see, i got out my notebook and continued writing.</P><P>the hero had flunked out of school and taken the greyhound home.  he arrived at four thirty in the morning and walked home, stopping at first baptist church on the way and beating off in the prayer garden.  when he got home, he hid around the side of the house until they\\\'d all left for school and work.  the maid came that day and caught him sleeping in his room.  he left before they got home.</P><P>that night, he stayed with friends and got drunk.  the next day, he walked to the library, bleary-eyed and headachey and exhausted.  he couldn\\\'t go home.  where was his home?  he waited in the library\\\'s back room, laying down behind the shelves and dodging everybody.</P><P>when the library closed, he went out into the park and walked to what must be his home, the wooden house a mile out of town back in the woods that belonged to his parents.  when he got there, nobody was home.  there was a key under a fake rock on the porch.  he went in and ate something and watched tv.  his mother and sister came home and weren\\\'t surprised to see him.  the maid had told them he was around.  his sister\\\'s friend had been in a wreck and was in a coma.</P><P>he finished writing and put the notebook away.  words came to him from a traffic song:</P><BR><BR><I>if you had just a minute to breathe<BR>and they granted you one final wish<BR>would you ask for something<BR>like another chance?</I><BR><BR><P>he walked out to the sheriff\\\'s department, left his pack at the front desk, talked to a detective who told him anna had been deported to sweden a week and a half before.  he walked back to the park and rolled up the rest of his weed and smoked it in the bathroom.  he went out of the park and walked down howell avenue to croom road, crossed the highway and turned down the dirt road to his parents\\\' house.  the key was no longer under the rock.  his father answered the door.</P><P>\\\"hello, son,\\\" he said.</P><P>they embraced.</P><P>\\\"welcome home.\\\"</P><BR><BR><BR>END OF BOOK ONE'),(3226,'<P>\r\nThe day was a long one that started at 6:14 a.m. when I looked at the clock and thought about how bad I did not feel like waiting anymore for the sun, a feeling similar to Christmas day age 5.  I had to get up.  This is where my day started, to give some idea of the journey to where it ended.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMy eyelids are holding on to life, fluttering.  40 minutes ago, I was at a birthday party in San Francisco\\\'s lower Potrero Hill just before the ship building wharf.  The bottom of the hill it is.\r\n</P><P>\r\nFeeling nauseous from the eggs and bacon I had for breakfast, the pizza I had for lunch, and the eggs and bacon I had for dinner followed by sushi with excessive amounts of wasabi, two scotches, four beers, and a hostess cupcake.  I decided to leave the party.  I thought if I stood any longer, I would vomit.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOutside my bike was still warm from the ride to the party.  Earlier that afternoon, I spent three hours on a 100-mile ride south as a reward for finishing a solid half days work by 11 A.M.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe bike, an old BMW, started right up.  It will not start when it is neglected, cold, or low on gas.  Right now, it is feeling the love.  On a good start, it goes KAROOMPAPAPAPA, and it starts.  On a bad day, it goes kakakakakaka, and that is it for the battery.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAs I am about to leave, a friend walks by and says, \\\"I thought I recognized that piece of shit bike.\\\"  I am so out of there in a blaze of rusted through mufflers.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nThree minutes later and half way up Potrero Hill, the revs started to drop off, as they do when there is no more gas in the tank.  When I left the house, I considered stopping at the gas station but figured 4.5 gallons of gas from the start of the day ought to carry me through the 3-mile trip to lower Potrero.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhere is the nearest gas station, I thought.  At the top of the Potrero incline is a gas station for sure, a Shell.  The synapses in the brain that light up brain scans when a human prays come to life.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI plotted a course quickly, optimizing down hill slopes to the Shell.  I considered gas stations that might be farther but downhill.  30 seconds later there is no gas left to burn.  On the last cough, I flip to the reserves.  The reserves were dry.  They are always dry.  The gas evaporates from them.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe bike and I coast down Missouri Street. I consider the options.  I could lock the bike up and deal with it tomorrow.  This would involve walking a good mile and a half or two miles home.  Or, I could push the bike up the rest of Potrero Hill to the Shell.  I opted for the latter.  I took a sort of circumference of Potrero as my course.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nTen minutes into my effort, I vomit.  I did not want to dump my bike so I vomited through the face shield of my helmet and onto the left jug that holds the still hot cylinder.  The fumes of sizzling vomit wafted up with steam into the chill night air.  You have not lived...  the smell.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI put the kickstand out and leaned the bike to a rest, took my helmet off and vomited again, this time from the thought of vomiting on my helmet, mixed with the smell of the western sizzler fish omelet that I laid down on the bike\\\'s radiating cylinder.  A spatula came to mind and I vomited again.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAfter taking a breather, and feeling better, I pressed on.  Cramps snuck up on me.  I did not recall feeling that they were coming, just that they were there, in my stomach, legs, and shoulders.  I passed the bar called the Bottom of the Hill.  I laughed in a huff; this was not the bottom of the hill by a long shot.  I left the bottom of the hill twenty-five minutes ago.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nI tried to think of nice things to take my mind off the pain.  I dreamed of the German engineers who made my bike some 30 years ago.  They must have considered installing a fuel gage and decided against it.  That would be too much dead weight on the design.  Arians like me calculated efficiencies, envisioned options.  I vomited again lifting my helmet in time to clear it and landing a splash of various meals over the instrument panel as I hunched over the front of the bike pushing on it.   I thought if I ever get new plates for this bike, they would read \\\'SHIZA\\\'.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nFinally, I reached the road with the gas station.  I powered past a bus stop of people waiting to catch a bus home after midnight.  That would suck worse than this, I thought.  At the Shell station and panting, I filled the bikeâ€™s tank and wiped my helmet out with a complimentary paper towel.  I felt like a worm on way home laid limp on the bike.  Moments like these, I feel a warriorâ€™s soul connection to the bike.  This is my own Zen and motorcycle maintenance lesson.\r\n</P>'),(3227,'<p>\r\nLittle green bottles litter my floor. I sleep in clothes I have not washed in weeks and still she comes back. I am filthy. I am dirty. I dont care about the outside world. I dont care about the people around me other then the basic few. I need those few to stay alive. feeding of their energy and ideas. It isnt about money I keep telling people who I bump into everyday six days a week. They tell me its about the pussy, or the chingching of coins in the pocket. I tell them its about looking at the sky for part of the day without having to think about what you need to do in the next hour. I tell them its about dangling your feet in a bubbling brook with birds chirping around your head. They tell me im a fruit and that they think im fucked up because I think chavez had a good idea. Patience isnt a virtue anymore because it has been sold out to progress and development. No time. NO TIME. lots of regrets, but no time. Have to move. Have to move forward. Gotta race past those next to me until I cannot see them anymore. Fuck them. Gotta move. Gotta progress. Gotta type faster. Gotta drive faster. Gotta work harder. Gotta pay more. Gotta do more. Gotta rely on more and more of them. Life isnt about choosing anymore. Just a bunch of sheep trying to get through the same small valley. The valley that leads all to paradise and bliss and joy and perfection. No one chooses to goto that paradise, they are rushed into it by the hoards of others who think they are following the ones being pushed. A sick and vicious circle of death and depression. Infused into our brains we think of all the things we want and forget more and more about the things people need. People need things....individuals want thing. Standards are good when they are attached to things that are stationary. Like weights and measures, but when those standards are attached to people who do change the world gets fucked up and we shift from side to side without really ever giving \\\'balance\\\' and \\\'consistency\\\' a thought. we are miserable creatures living in a miserable world filled with miserable filth and stench, though through that dense fog of war we seem to maintain the ablilty to smile or laugh when someone passes wind. \r\n</p>'),(3228,'<p>\r\nHenry was trying hard not to stare. He dunked his mop in the yellow bucket, dropped the streaming strands of rancid rope into the wringer to twist out most of the excess, and then slapped it on the floor and resumed swabbing. Turning his back on the lunch room, he mopped out the corner with brisk strokes, pressing hard, trying to scrape away the grimy accumulation of years. He looked out the tall windows and tried to find an interesting cloud to get lost in. But the winter sky was nearly featureless, just an endless expanse of cotton batting, and the glare hurt his eyes. The mop went back into the bucket. He turned his head just enough to pretend he was looking at the clock high up on the wall, and there she was, a little girl with bristling blond hair tied into pigtails with pink ribbons. Her profile was familiar, but surely-\r\n<p>\r\nShe turned to giggle a whisper in a confederate\\\'s ear, and Henry sagged, his knuckles white on the mop handle. He knew that face. He had carried it in his heart as the very image of love since he was a boy. It was Jennie Richie, standing in the lunchroom line at St Bartholemew\\\'s Day School, waiting in line for a steaming plate of American chop suey. Jennie Richie, as bright eyed and red cheeked as the day she\\\'d gone missing, forty years before. <p>\r\n\\\"Are you all right, Henry?\\\" Father Cribbage was at Henry\\\'s elbow, his schoolboy-smooth cheeks set in a mask of concern. <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m fine,\\\" Henry said. \\\"I just got dizzy.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Why don\\\'t you take a long lunch today.\\\" The priest winked conspiratorily. \\\"Take it easy for a bit. I won\\\'t tell.\\\" <p>\r\nWas it a mortal sin to smack a priest? Henry wasn\\\'t sure, so he didn\\\'t risk it. Henry missed Father Pasadena, the old pastor, who had been twenty years Henry\\\'s senior, and someone Henry could look to for guidance. Seeking moral direction from a kid young enough to be his grandson made Henry uncomfortable. The new priests were just so damned idealistic, so sure that anything could be overcome with rigor and modern therapeutic techniques. They just didn\\\'t appreciate that sometimes men are chased by demons their whole lives. <p>\r\nIt was really Cribbage\\\'s fault that Henry was even working in the parish school. Henry had enjoyed taking care of the grounds of St Bartholemew\\\'s, outside and with ample to stop and investigate the clouds. He still didn\\\'t understand why Father Cribbage, that high school kid in a Roman collar, insisted he now needed an assistant. But then one day there was Tony DeCampris, boom box in tow, telling Henry that he was the new assistant and by the way, man, when was the first coffee break? Henry grit his teeth and didn\\\'t say anything. He lead him out to the cemetary that abutted the parish, where tey needed to reinstall an iron statue of the Black Angel of Palermo to its proper place. It had been left at the cemetary gates during the night, arriving as mysteriously as it had disappeared a few months before. <p>\r\nTony wasted fifteen minutes scouting for an outdoor outlet where he could plug in his radio. Some madman began shrieking about <I>Wang Dang Sweet Poontang.</I> Henry wanted to send the young man away, to tell him to go smoke cigarettes and play Pac Man or whatever it was he\\\'d rather be doing, but was chagrined to find he couldn\\\'t lift the seventy-five pound statue by himself. He grunted for DeCampris to come help him. Which the young man did, until a new song came on the radio, one he couldn\\\'t stand, it was <I>disco,</I> man, how could his favorite band have put out a <I>disco </I>song, disco <I>sucks,</I> and he dashed over to change the station, leaving Henry to support the entire iron weight of the Black Angel by himself. He managed to lever her up on top of the monument, and something went snap in his lower back, and the next thing he knew, Father Cribbage had moved him out of groundskeeping and assigned Henry custodial duties at the day school. No longer outside and dreaming alone among the clouds, Henry sat in a closet at a leftover school desk wedged in among the shelves of cleaning products and listened to the hilarious cacaphony of herds of children moving to and from lunch. <p>\r\n\\\"Why were you staring at me?\\\" <p>\r\nHenry started. The little blond girl from the lunch line was standing in the door to his office.  Birds of terror and guilt fluttered and crashed inside him, desperate for escape. <p>\r\n\\\"Pardon me, miss?\\\" he said. <p>\r\n\\\"In in the lunchroom. You were staring at me. What, do I have horns or something?\\\" She fixed him with a level stare of her own and snapped her gum. <p>\r\n\\\"Horns,\\\" he said. He swallowed hard, his throat clicking. He tried to chuckle. \\\"No, I-I like your backpack. It\\\'s neat.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s Pikachu!\\\" she said. She turned to display it more carefully. A bright yellow cartoon character, a banana crossed with a sewer rat, was strapped to her back. \\\"Do you like Pokemon?\\\" She pointed at Henry and smiled.  \\\"I ch-ch-choose you!\\\" <p>\r\nHenry jumped in his seat.  His shoe kicked the yellow bucket and dirty water sloshed over the sides. \\\"You choose me?\\\" he said. \\\"For what?\\\" <p>\r\nShe shrugged and laughed, the bright yellow straps hitching up and down. <p>\r\nHe got to his feet and ushered the little girl back into the hallway. They were islands in the streaming swirl of children running from the lunchroom out to the recess macadam. \\\"You can\\\'t be in here,\\\" he said. \\\"It\\\'s not safe. There\\\'re chemicals.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"What\\\'s your name?\\\" she asked. <p>\r\n\\\"Henry.\\\" Go back to your office and close the door, he told himself. Right now, Henry. Go sit among the musty old mops and the bottles of Simple Green cleanser and think about clouds. Right now. \\\"What\\\'s your name?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Jennie.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I used to know a Jennie,\\\" he said. His voice sounded like if came from down a very long corridor. <p>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s funny, I used to know a Henry,\\\" Jennie said, and Henry\\\'s heart tripped and stuttered and hammered hard to catch up again. \\\"We had a cat named Henry, but he got run over by a car. It was really sad. But now we have a new cat. His name is Asriel.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s... an unusual name,\\\" Henry said. He felt faint. <p>\r\n\\\"My dad named him that \\\'cause he tears around like a demon. He likes our new house. We just moved here. Why do the kids call you \\\'Creepy\\\'?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"They do?\\\" Henry croaked. <p>\r\n\\\"They do.\\\" She nodded. \\\"People shouldn\\\'t stare. It\\\'s not polite. Don\\\'t you think?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"No,\\\" Henry said. \\\"Not polite.\\\" <p>\r\nJennie looked past him and out to the playground. \\\"Anyway, I have to go. Nice talking to you!\\\" And she was gone. <p>\r\n\\\"Yes,\\\" Henry said to the door that banged in her wake.  He went back into his office. He sat in the tiny chair, meant for a bottom two generations younger than his, and rocked back and forth. \\\"Nice.\\\" <p>\r\nHenry rented a studio apartment in a tenement building across the street from the church. He probably could\\\'ve afforded something a little bit nicer, but he didn\\\'t drive, and couldn\\\'t abide being wedged next to strangers with their strange mouths breathing strange diseases on the bus. When the day was done he would shuffle across the parking lot and up five creaky flights of stairs to his room.  <p>\r\nThe floor was covered in decades of newspapers--some loose, some bound up in bales--and discarded, rusting, mold sprouting cans of Hornel chili with no beans, which Henry ate for breakfast and dinner every day. There was a TV somewhere, buried behind the stacks of newsprint, but Henry hadn\\\'t turned it on in ten years. The only other furnishings were Henry\\\'s single bed, which he made everyday before going to Mass, his drawing easel, and an old easy chair with stuffing attempting escape from every seam. <p>\r\nThe apartment was papered from floor to ceiling with colored pencil drawings of clouds. Stratus clouds layered like desert cakes, raging black cumulonimbous clouds, cirrocumulus clouds in flully nursery toy shapes... Henry loved them all, and he loved to draw them. Standing at his easel, sketching in the drifting shapes-and they always seemed soft at first, no matter how threatening his colored pencils would reveal them to be later-Henry felt a wonderful calm. If it wasn\\\'t the peace that passeth understanding that Father Cribbage talked about, it was something close. He had tried other subjects-dogs, nature scenes, clowns-but nothing worked. The only subject he could draw to his satisfaction was clouds. <p>\r\nHe felt like his brain was a sponge that soaked up the loveliness of the sky. It was all there in his head, every last wonderful fold and complex crenelation of atmospheric structures, and all he had to do was stand in front of his easel long enough, and angels would wring the sky\\\'s beauty back out of him so that he could enjoy it, even when things were at their darkest. His pencils would scratch at the paper and his thumb would smudge and blur the colors, cerullian blue fading into a royal magenta and into a delicate pink, like the interior of a clam shell, velvety soft like-and Henry would shake his head, very hard, and slap himself in the face to knock the bad thought out. He would concentrate very hard on the cloud emerging before him, imagining himself walking with angels along the gentle rolling slopes, and bad thoughts would recede. <p>\r\nHe\\\'d spent most weekend afternoons of his life on his back and staring at the sky, even when it was raining, even when it was so cold the snot froze in his nostrils. A tarp on the ground, or three extra coats, it didn\\\'t matter. And a little discomfort, he thought, was probably a good thing. Why else would the church pews be so hard, and why else would they make you stand up and kneel down so many times during mass, if a bit of suffering didn\\\'t have some salutary effect? After the Vatican Council, when St Bartholemew\\\'s had done some renovation and installed pads on the kneeling benches, Henry had started bringing a little board with him when he went to the 6 AM mass. He lay it on the pads and relished the way the wood chafed his knees and bit into his shins. It felt like penance. It was satisfying. <p>\r\nThat night Henry tried to draw clouds, and couldn\\\'t. He needed to draw clouds, and couldn\\\'t find any in his head. Being shut inside all day kept him from absorbing anything he could squeeze back out. He looked at his meteorological magazines for awhile, and his National Geographics with their dramatic desert photographs, but he couldn\\\'t get started. And when he closed his eyes to try to call one up from memory, all he could see was little blond Jennie cocking her head and saying, \\\"It\\\'s not polite to stare, Creepy. It\\\'s not polite at all.\\\" <p>\r\nTired and depressed, he spent the night in his armchair, a bottle of Wild Turkey in one hand, and a bottle of Pepto Bismol in the other. He missed the early bird Mass for the first time in years, and called Father Cribbage to tell him he was too sick to come in to work. <p>\r\n\\\"I hope it\\\'s nothing serious,\\\" the priest said. \\\"It\\\'s probably just a cold. You haven\\\'t been working around kids that long; they\\\'re germ factories. You\\\'ll build up some resistance.\\\" <p>\r\nHenry told him he certainly hoped so. He heated a can of chili on his hot plate and stood at the window to eat it, his forehead resting on the cold glass. He scanned the sky. It was the blue of arctic ice, and a fierce wind had ripped the fluffy white cirrus clouds into ragged streamers  that burned pink and purple as the sun traced a shallow arc towards noon. As the day brew brighter, Henry found himseld squinting more and more; the light stabbed through his eyes and on into his whiskey sore brain. His gaze kept falling to the playground across the street, where the children of St Bartholemew were sent to burn off energy that might inhibit their rote memorization of mathematical rules and vocabulary lists. <p>\r\nThe boys and girls all wore big puffy parkas over their green school uniforms. The boys wore green polyesther slacks, but the girls wore plaid skirts that ended just above their knees. Henry didn\\\'t approve. Even with their dark knee socks pulled high, it was too cold for exposed flesh that flashed as the girls ran back and forth, playing freeze tag, and red light green light, and red rover. The girls who had been tagged, who were frozen, truly looked freezing, huddling into their oversized coats and rubbing their knees together while they waited to be rescued. Henry felt indignant. It wasn\\\'t right, sending children outside to play on such a cold day. They should be inside, scribbling on manilla drawing paper and playing boardgames. But he knew that the teachers grew tired of the childrens\\\' shrieking, of the smell of so many little bodies in musty uniforms all enclosed in small spaces, and so they sent them outside in all but the very worst weather.  <p>\r\nThere was one girl in particular who appeared to be suffering. Her buck teeth were chattering, and she begged her friends come on, come on and unfreeze her, but perhaps she was a victim of some grade school ostracization, some schoolgirl cabal, and the other girls ran around her and did not tag her free. Henry knew what it was like to be left out like that--not just left out, but actively and cruelly ignored. The little buck toothed girl was beginning to cry, holding her hand out in vain. Henry wondered how he could help. Perhaps he would bring her a blanket and wrap her up tight. He could invite her in for a cup of good Campbell\\\'s soup he would heat on his hot plate, and they could draw clouds together, and she would see she didn\\\'t need those other girls--<p>\r\n\\\"Wow, and my mom thinks my room is a pigsty!\\\" <p>\r\nHenry whirled around as quickly as his truss would allow. His back screeched in protest. \\\"What! Who!\\\" He looked around wildly. Then he eyes fell on the little girl with the cartoon backpack. She was looking at him, her hand convering her mouth and trying to hide her laughter. \\\"How did you get in here?\\\" he demanded. \\\"You can\\\'t be in here!\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You left your door wide open,\\\" she said. <p>\r\nHenry looked at the door. It was quite shut, and both deadbolts were driven home. <p>\r\n\\\"No  I didn\\\'t,\\\" he said. But he wasn\\\'t sure. <p>\r\n\\\"Yes, you did,\\\" she said firmly. \\\"You didn\\\'t hear me come in. You were staring out the window. You looked like you were thinking really hard about something. What were you thinking about?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I was looking at clouds.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Oh. But you were looking down. Are there clouds on the ground? Let me see!\\\" She stepped next to him at the window. \\\"I don\\\'t see any clouds. Just kids.\\\" She looked up and gave him a sly grin. \\\"Were you being creepy again?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"No! I just-it\\\'s cold outsde. I was thinking the teachers should let the children play inside.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Uh huh,\\\" she said. She stepped away from him and examined the drawings that covered every surface of his apartment, even the door to the little bathroom. \\\"The teachers don\\\'t care. They don\\\'t mind the kids being a little miserable if it buys them a little peace and quiet. You sure draw a lot of clouds.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I like clouds,\\\" Henry said. He sat on his bunk and stared with miserable shyness at his folded hands.If the clouds were not down on the playground, they did seem to have filled his head. He felt muddled and old.  <p>\r\n\\\"You know what I think?\\\" Jennie said. \\\"I think you should draw a picture of me.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I like to draw clouds.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"No clouds today,\\\" she said. \\\"Not after the night you\\\'ve had. You should draw me. I\\\'m pretty.\\\" <p>\r\nA headache settle on Henry like a pair of tongs to his temples. Suddenly all he wanted to do was sleep. He wanted this little girl to go away. <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m very tired,\\\" Henry said. \\\"You should go back to school. I\\\'m sure your teacher is worried about you.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m sure my teacher is worried about you,\\\" Jennie said. \\\"You have to draw me, now.\\\" And when he didn\\\'t move, didn\\\'t look up: \\\" Now, Henry!\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"But I can\\\'t draw!\\\" Henry said. <p>\r\n\\\"Well, you can trace, can\\\'t you?\\\" Jennie said. \\\"Anyone can trace.\\\" She kicked at the piles of newspaper at her feet so that some flew into the air and some rustled across the floor. She stooped and snatched a loose page up. \\\"See? There\\\'s tons of pictures. Look, there\\\'s a picture of a little girl right here.\\\" <p>\r\nJennie thrust the comics page at him. She shook it in front of his face until Henry finally looked up and took it from her. L\\\'il Orphan Annie was busy trying to steal a top secret fighter jet from the Nazis. <p>\r\n\\\"Just trace her,\\\" Jennie said. \\\"But change the hair, I hate her hair. You can do that for me, can\\\'t you?\\\" <p>\r\nHenry looked at the comic, and then up at Jennie, who was staring at him expectantly, her hands on her hips. His head really hurt. He knew it would be wrong to drink in front of the child, but he couldn\\\'t help it. He reached for the bottle of Wild Turkey on his dresser. <p>\r\nJennie slapped it out of his hand. \\\"No!\\\" she said. \\\"You won\\\'t even be able to trace if you get drunk. It\\\'s got to be good. Come on, Henry. Do it for me, please.\\\" Her voice wheedled him until he got to his feet with a heavy sigh and shuffled over to the kitchenette, the comic page crumpled in his hand. <p>\r\nSomewhere in the junk drawer there was-yes. Scissors. Nearly rusted shut, but Henry opened and closed the shears a couple of times and the brown dust crumbled away. He lined the blades up carefully with the border of the comic and cut it from the page. He dropped the the remains of the newspaper on the floor and went to his easel. An unfinished drawing of a cumulonimbus cloud-a really good one, like the harbringer of an old time nor\\\'easter-was on the top of the pad, and he tore it off with a twinge of regret. Henry didn\\\'t like to leave a task unfinished. He raised a fresh sheet up and then lowered it over the comic panel, pinning it in place. <p>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s no good,\\\" he said, his black number 5 pencil poised over the pad. \\\"The paper\\\'s too thick. I can\\\'t see what I\\\'m supposed to trace.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Oh for pete\\\'s sake,\\\" Jennie said, and stamped her foot. \\\"Put it on the window. The light will shine through.\\\" <p>\r\nIt did no good to argue with her. Henry was vaguely ashamed about being browbeaten by a ten year old girl. But what good was it to resist? Women demanded and men obeyed. It had been that way since the beginning, it said so right in the first book of the bible. And Jennie was right. The flat winter afternoon light made the drawing paper translucent. He could see blank eyed L\\\'il Orphan Annie behind it, and, confusingly, a little bit of backwards typewritten text behind that. He squeezed the pencil between his calloused thumb and forefinger and the lead began to trail a dark streak across the cheap gray paper. <p>\r\n\\\"Put a bow in my hair,\\\" Jennie said. \\\"I always wear a pink bow.\\\" <p>\r\nShe was right. Tracing was easy, and anyone could do it, even Henry. It was easy to pretend that he was actually drawing such precise lines, the smooth curves and sharp angles in perfect proportion and distance. There was a line, and another, and another, and suddenly there she was, L\\\'il Orphan Annie, on his drawing sheet. Henry laughed out loud. <p>\r\n\\\"Keep going,\\\" Jennie said. \\\"Make sure it looks like me.\\\" <p>\r\nHenry took a deep breath and continued on. He started to trace the side of Annie\\\'s head, to capture her frizzy head of curls, but then he remembered he was supposed to be drawing Jennie. He drew two shallow arcs that meat at the top of her head, like the profile of a road disappearing between two hills, or a giant version of the way he drew birds when he was a boy. And then on each side he tried to draw elongated backwards s\\\'s that connected at the ends. <p>\r\n\\\"Is that supposed to be my <I>hair</I>?\\\" Jennie said, looking from past his elbow. Her breath fogged the cold glass. \\\"It\\\'s okay, I guess. But make sure you put in the hair ribbons.\\\" <p>\r\nHenry dutifully added in the hair ribbons, and even though he couldn\\\'t quite get the twist in the fabric right-there was a trick to it, but he\\\'d long forgotten what it was-they looked, he thought, pretty good. <p>\r\n\\\"What do you think?\\\" he said. \\\"A girl with pigtails and hair ribbons. She looks just like you.\\\" <p>\r\nJennie looked up at him without answering. But he could tell she was not satisfied. <p>\r\n\\\"Well, what else do you want?\\\" Henry said. \\\"You want to be riding a pony or in a rocket ship or something? I can\\\'t really draw those, but I could trace the plane out of-\\\"<p>\r\n\\\"No, Henry.\\\" She shook her head patiently, as if explaining a simple matter to a slow child. \\\"It\\\'s not done yet. Look again. It doesn\\\'t look like me yet, does it? It\\\'s missing something, isn\\\'t it?\\\" <p>\r\nHenry looked again, and he had to agree. Something about her expression wasn\\\'t right. Staring at his work in progress, he pulled out his pocket knife and whittled the tip of the pencil back to a point. He could almost see it, right on the page.It was like one of those what\\\'s-wrong-with-this-picture puzzles, but instead of extra incrongruities, he could sense something missing. Hands. There should be hands in the picture. The pencil lead flowed across the paper. But they weren\\\'t her hands. Man\\\'s hands. There was a hand on her shoulder, and one under her chin, like someone was consoling her, saying What\\\'s wrong, little girl? And then her eyes. Her eyes weren\\\'t big enough. He added in black pupils to the empty spheres of Annie\\\'s eyes, and that helped, but it wasn\\\'t enough. His traced and darkened the circles, going around and around, adding shadows underneath. Her mouth was wrong, too. She shouldn\\\'t be smiling, she should be laughing, her mouth opened wide, her tongue sticking out straight, her mouth wider, darker, the eyes popping out of her head, the hand under her chin, the thumb buried in her throat-<p>\r\nHenry looked at what he\\\'d drawn and screamed. He leaped back from the window, his heels slipping on the loose newspapers, and tottered backwards to land heavily in his old familiar easy chair, the springs groaning in complaint. The back shot down and the foot rest shot up, leaving him reclined and trembling. <p>\r\nJennie peered at the drawing. \\\"That\\\'s not bad,\\\" she said. \\\"That\\\'s pretty close, isn\\\'t it?\\\" She put her hands to her own throat and mimed being strangled, her eyes bugging out at Henry. Then she laughed. Henry felt a shameful wetness at the seat of his pants. He couldn\\\'t catch his breath. He felt long tingling lines run from the tips of his fingers up through his shoulders and thought that maybe he was having a heart attack. <p>\r\n\\\"Jennie,\\\" he managed to gasp at last. <p>\r\n\\\"Henry,\\\" she said, and gave him a knowing grin. <p>\r\n\\\"Oh my god,\\\" he said. \\\"Am I dead already? Am I in hell?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Henry, really,\\\" she said, looking around at his apartment. \\\"Hell is much better kept than this.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t understand.\\\" Henry clawed stuffing from the fraying ends of the armrests. \\\"Jennie? Are you <I>m</I>y Jennie?\\\" <p>\r\nShe walked over and stood beside him. She took his hand and smiled. \\\"Of course I am.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"No, you <I>can\\\'t </I>be.\\\" Henry jerked free of her grasp and buried his face in his hands. \\\"I don\\\'t understand.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"You don\\\'t need to,\\\" she said. \\\"All you need to do is draw.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I can\\\'t draw!\\\" Henry wailed. <p>\r\nJennie pinched the bridge of her nose in a very adult gesture of exasperation. \\\"We\\\'ve already established that tracing is good for a start, right. So we\\\'re going to trace. A lot.  Every time you find yourself at your window, watching the clouds, and then the brightness makes you squint, and you look down and start staring at the children on the playground, we\\\'re going to make a picture. Maybe we\\\'ll even make up a story to go with it.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Like L\\\'il Orphan Annie?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Sort of,\\\" she said, and laughed. \\\"Maybe they\\\'ll even make a musical out of it.\\\" <p>\r\nThat was how it started. Henry kept his job at the St Bartholemew Day School, and though the children were afraid of him, and the way he stood over his mopbucket sometimes, frozen in place and staring, they were not so afraid that they didn\\\'t come up with some funny new names for Old Henry. Sometimes he would find cartoons of himself that some particularly artistic fourth grader had scrawled on the bathroom wall with a stolen magic marker, and Henry would laugh and spray some Simple Green on the wall and wipe it away. Then he would go home and draw pictures of children being eviscerated and hung from hooks by their intestines. <p>\r\nOver time his methods improved. He let himself into the school late one night and stole an overhead projector from the AV room, which he wheeled, bumping and squeaking, across the parking lot and around to the service entrance of his apartment building. Jennie assured him that the overhead projector, which allowed him to project images at many times their original size, was necessary to the work, and therefore stealing it was only a venial sin. But Henry still couldn\\\'t sleep until took the bus to Ossington, three towns away, so he could confess his crime in confession. <p>\r\nThe drawings became more complex. Henry began clipping pictures from everywhere, from the comics page, from coloring books, from illustrated storybooks. Jennie would come visit him whenever he stood too long at the window, and she would look over what he had cut out, and they would sit on the floor and sort out the ones that best told the story. <p>\r\nBecause it was the Story that was most important.  Jennie told him about the Realms of the Unreal, where the brave Vivian girls fought the wicked Glandelians, who kidnapped children and forced them into slavery. Henry listened to her carefuly and copied her every word into marble coverd composition books he stole from the school supply closet. Again, venial sin, and confession in Ossington. Jennie came and saw him almost every day, and they would talk and argue about the course of the Child Slave Rebellion. Sometimes Henry would balk at depicting the horrors that Jennie would recount, the way the Dukes of Armantado would trample the rebellious children with their horses, or breed girls that had the ram horns and the private parts of boys, or mount captured child soldiers on pikes or hang them by their tongues, but Jennie insisted that he get in all down on paper, because it was all true. <p>\r\n\\\"And if people know,\\\" she said. \\\"If it\\\'s out in the open, then maybe, just maybe, it won\\\'t happen here. You have an important job, Henry. You have to record this secret history. You have to draw to keep the children safe.\\\" <p>\r\nHenry worked for years and years and years, but still never managed to record the entire story of the Child Slave Rebellion. He painted seven hundred and thirty four pictures, and wrote fifteen thousand pages, all describing in excruciating detail the awful things that men can do to defenseless children. It was awful work. Sometimes he discovered he had traced something all wrong because his eyes had been too blurred with tears. In the background of the worst scenes he always painted clouds, because their gentle rolls and pastel colors calmed him and readied him for the next horrific onslaught. But every time he thought he had finished, that there were no more horrors to document, Jennie would find him standing at his window, or pretending to tighten bolts on the monkey bars, and tell him there were still chapters to record. <p>\r\nOn his last day he tried to get out of bed, but Jennie was standing there next to his bunk, and she pushed him back down, even though she was still only a ten year old girl. \\\"Jennie,\\\" he said. \\\"Am I going to hell?\\\" <p>\r\nHer tiny fingertips pressed his eyes closed and she leaned in to kiss his gray whiskered cheek. <p>\r\n \\\"You worked so hard for forgiveness,\\\" she said. \\\"I\\\'m sure it will be enough.\\\" <p>\r\nBut she was glad he couldn\\\'t see the uncertain tremble of her mouth.<p>\r\n'),(3229,'<p>\r\nThe cavalry wasn\\\'t coming for Jack Harkness. The board members were staring at him expectantly, their faces doughy but their eyes flinty hard from staring too long into the abyssmal American appetite for the lewd and the inane. Past a certain age, Jack knew, you have the face you deserve. Looking around the the mahogony table was like looking into tweve mirrors. He sighed and admitted defeat. His five hundred dollar pen scrawled his signature across the bottom of the paper they had presented him, and that was that.  He was out.<p>\r\nIt didn\\\'t really sink in until learned that his key no longer opened the executive washroom. He stood there in the hall and jiggled the metal tab in the lock. His bowels muttered sullenly. Perhaps the extra cream in his coffee had been a bad idea. Grunting and fuming, beads of sweat popping out on his upper lip and between his chins, he twisted the key back and forth until he heard a muted crack. He found himself staring stupidly at the hilt of the key, still pinched between his plump fingers. The shaft protruded from the lock. Son of a bitch. <p>\r\n\\\"Nora!\\\" he said. \\\"Call facilities and tell them the lock to the executive washroom is broken. And tell them to turn the damned air conditioning on. It\\\'s sweltering in here.\\\" <p>\r\n \\\"I\\\'m sorry, Mister Harkness,\\\" the receptionist said. Harkness had found her at the cattlecar auditions for <I>Showgirl Assasins.</I> \\\"The temperature has been set to 72 degrees, as per President Carter\\\'s energy guidelines. Mister Van Stanton\\\'s orders.\\\" <p>\r\nVan Stanton. That cretin. Only fifteen minutes had passed since Harkness had tendered his resignation, and already that glorified little mailboy had the janitors come through and change the locks. Now Harkness, once the Vice President of Programming at NBC, the King of Jiggle Television, would be unable to sit on his teak and porcelain throne one final time. He\\\'d have to wait until he got home. <p>\r\n Back in his office he found another insult-a cardboard box sitting on his chair. They expected him to clean out his own desk! There was a note from Van Stanton taped to the side-<I>Dear Jack, Best luck in future endeavors. The network deeply appreciates your years of service. Etc, etc. PVS. </I>The runt bastard. Harkness went to the trophy case and began packing his plaques and statuettes. Ten years of bringing the American public what it wanted, and this was all he had to show for it. Ratings were down, the board said. Tastes were changing. Guns, feathered hair and boobs-ingredients Harkness had mixed and matched until half of the nation was watching his shows on Thursday and Saturday nights-were out of favor. The public didn\\\'t want their intelligence insulted anymore, Van Stanton had told the board. They wanted culture. Shows like <I>Cajun Angels</I> and <I>Hot Pants O\\\'Sullivan, PI</I> were declasse, an embarassment. Harkness had stared hard at his lap and imagined throttling Peter Van Staton\\\'s chicken neck with one hand. <p>\r\nHe thumbed the intercom button on his phone. \\\"Nora. Have my limo out front in ten minutes, please.\\\" <p>\r\nHe waited for a response. The speakerphone emitted a ghostly crackle as Nora held down the reply button, but didn\\\'t speak. Finally she said, \\\"I\\\'m sorry, sir. The network\\\'s car service is... not available. May I call you a taxi?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"A taxi?\\\" Harkness imagined himself as a cartoon character, the top of his head blowing free in a volcanic explosion, steam shooting from his ears, an air horn bellowing. He threw the box of trophies across the room. It smashed a framed photograph of Harkness shaking hands with a has-been actor who was now the governor of the state. Golden statuettes clattered to the floor. <p>\r\n\\\"Sir? Sir?\\\" the speakerphone chirped. Harkness had always liked Nora, even if she had never fielded his occasional passes. \\\"Are you alright?\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m fine.\\\" He sighed. He rooted fruitlessly through his desk, hoping to find a stale cigarette tucked forgotten in the back of a drawer like a minor archaeological treasure. He should never have listened to his doctor. \\\"Call me a cab. Thank you, Nora.\\\" <p>\r\n\\\"Very good, sir.\\\" Her voice had regained its normal robotic cheerfulness. <p>\r\nHarkness felt twenty years older than he had at breakfast that morning. He stooped painfully and swept the broken remains of his career into the brown box his backstabbing protÃ©gÃ© had provided. It wasn\\\'t easy. Too many free commissary donuts had left  Harkness unable to touch his toes. He took a moment to  straighten his vest and smooth the flyaway strands of sweaty hair that stood up from his bald pate like the strings of invisible kites. Then he went out to catch his ride home. <p>\r\n\\\"Oh, sir,\\\" Nora said. She opened her drawer and pulled out an plastic-windowed envelope. \\\"Mr. Van Stanton left this for you.\\\" <p>\r\nHarkness put his box down on her desk and opened the envelope. It held his severance check. A standard two weeks salary. He sighed. His lawyers would dispute the rest. He was too tired to think about it now. But there was another slip of paper folded into the envelope. <p>\r\n<I>Dear Jack-Sorry about how things worked out. You had your day, now it\\\'s time for me to have mine. Don\\\'t worry about what I said about highbrow programming being the wave of the future. I intend to have a very successful and lucrative career following in your footsteps. Everything I know, I learned from you. PVS</I><p>\r\nFrom somewhere deep south of Harkness\\\'s distended belly button came a subterranean gurgle that promised disaster. \\\"Nora,\\\" he said. \\\"If I might ask you for a favor. May I borrow the spare key to the executive washroom? It\\\'s been an upsetting morning, as you might imagine.\\\" <p>\r\nNora\\\'s eyes glittered with ersatz concern. \\\"Of course, Mister Harkness, \\\" she said, and handed him the key. Harkness took his box with him around the corner and down the hall to the executive washroom. It was two doors down from what would soon be Van Stanton\\\'s old office. By the end of the day the facilities staff would have moved his shelves of motivational books down the hall to the room that had been Harkness\\\'s seat of power. <p>\r\nThis time the key fit easily. There might be other executive washrooms in the future, Harkness mused. He was not yet an old man, and perhaps some other network might value his veteran\\\'s confidence, his vicious aptitude for the corporate infight. Van Stanton had gotten the better of him this time, but so what? <p>\r\nHe had treated Van Stanton like a son, but forgotten the ultimate resolution of the Oedipal drama. It was not a mistake he would repeat. The wound was deep, but not fatal, and Harkness was already looking forward to feeding Van Stanton cold pies stuffed with his erstwhile protÃ©gÃ©\\\'s freshly cooked children. <p>\r\nThe muted gurlging of his bowels had become a cthonic roar. Harkness reached into the garbage bin and pulled out the plastic lining. He shook out the damp, crumpled paper towels, and upended his broken trophies into the white bag. Harkness carefully worked the ends of the bag over the edges of the box. He undid his belt and dropped his trousers. Then he squatted over the box and relieved himself in a titanic burst that would\\\'ve intimidated Hercules in the midst of his Augean labors. He could\\\'ve sworn he heard the cries of forgotten actors and unpaid writers in the stuttering rush of vapors.  It seemed to go on forever, the moment expanding into a mystic\\\'s Eternal Now, and when time finally resumed he was suffused with an extraordinary feeling of well-being, like he had left ten years of gossip and chicanery and debauchery and betrayal behind him. He attended to himself conscientiously and then pulled the drawstrings on the garbage bag tight. <p>\r\nHarkness peeked out into the hall. No one was looking. He crept into Van Staton\\\'s soon-to-be-vacated office and aligned the box carefully on the green blotter. He tore a page from Van Staton\\\'s stationary-already, he noted wonderingly (such balls on the little putz, how did he manage to walk?), identifying him as <I>Peter Van Stanton, Vice-President of Programming</I>-and pulled out his own gold Bulova pen. DearPeter, he wrote. <I>Just a little something to help you get started with next season\\\'s programming. Yours, etc. J Harkness. </I><p>\r\nHe whistled his way down the hall and winked at Nora as he passed. He kept the key to the washroom.\r\n<p>'),(3230,'<p>\r\nJ. goes to Tijuana for three days. It was a class \\\"field trip\\\", but really - he was just out to get laid. Him being my boyfriend\\\'s roommate, that means we(the boyfriend and I) get an entire friggin room to ourselves for three days.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow...three days, a room, and a short fuzzy guy who really isn\\\'t too bad in the sack. Yeah.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nJ. gets back from Tijuana. I\\\'m upstairs on my computer, being social in a very geeky way. All of a sudden - the horrifying message pops up in an AIM window.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"BAAAAAAAHHHHH!!! WHY AM I COMING HOME TO A CONDOM WRAPPER BY THE MICROWAVEEEEE!!!!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOoops.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is distressing for a few reasons. One, we usually clean up pretty well after ourselves. And the sex the night before wasn\\\'t even THAT good, so it\\\'s not like we passed out immediately afterwards. I just forgot. It was a bit embarassing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo, I\\\'d been doing pretty well in upholding my horny, but fairly celibate reputation. Yes, it takes quite a bit to get in my pants, despite the fact that I\\\'m a huge flirt. And no, I do not know why I prefer to keep the whole \\\"horny virgin\\\" aura around me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEither way, whatever suspicions that J. ever had about us have crossed the line into \\\"GAAAAHH!!! I DON\\\'T WANNA KNOWWWWW!!!\\\" territory. I think he\\\'s still a bit peeved at me and the firsthand information that he really didn\\\'t need. He has yet to look me in the eye.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI tried to talk to him afterwards. He was very monosyllabic. \r\n</p> \r\n<p>\r\nOr maybe J. was just bitter he didn\\\'t get laid in Tijuana.</p>'),(3231,'<P>we\\\'re sitting in per\\\'s apartment on valhallavagen drinking tuborg and eating artichoke hearts in mustard sauce at four o\\\'clock in the afternoon.  per\\\'s daughter sabina is playing with a puppy on the kitchen floor beside us.  she\\\'s three years old.</P><P>\\\"what are we listening to?\\\" i ask.</P><P>\\\"eric dolphy.\\\"</P><P>\\\"i like it.  you have something right here.\\\"  i indicate a spot above my upper lip.  \\\"no, the other side.\\\"</P><P>sabina comes over to me.  \\\"can i have a drink of beer?\\\"</P><P>i look at per.  he shrugs.  i hold the bottle out to her, and she grabs it with both hands.  i help her with it, tipping it up so she can get a sip.  she takes a gulp.  \\\"ahhhh,\\\" she says and hands me the bottle.  she goes over to per, and he picks her up and sets her on his lap.</P><P>\\\"hello, person,\\\" he says.  \\\"how did you get to be a person?\\\"</P><P>\\\"i just was born.\\\"</P><P>she looks at me.  \\\"you want to play snakes and ladders, lady?\\\"</P><P>per laughs.  \\\"what\\\'s her name?\\\"</P><P>she shrugs.</P><P>\\\"anna.\\\"</P><P>while she goes off to get the game, per clears away the dishes and gets me a fresh beer.  we play on the kitchen table with the puppy running around between the table legs.  she\\\'s about to win when per\\\'s wife erika comes in.  she\\\'s got shopping with her.  i get up to give her a hug as per takes the groceries and gets her a beer.</P><P>\\\"per called and told me what happened,\\\" she says.</P><P>i offer to help them with dinner.</P><P>\\\"no, no,\\\" erika says.  \\\"play with the baby.  enjoy yourself.\\\"</P><P>sabina wins at snakes and ladders.  i ask her if they have a deck of cards.  she toddles off and gets one, and i show her how to play war.  we go through a few hands of that until she starts to look bored.</P><P>\\\"ya wanna play blackjack?\\\" i ask.</P><P>she nods tentatively.</P><P>\\\"ya got any money?\\\"</P><P>shakes her head.</P><P>\\\"how ya gonna play blackjack without any money?  don\\\'tcha have a little piggybank or somethin\\\'?\\\"</P><P>she goes into her room and comes out with a chromed piggybank, the kind whose front and back ends slide apart.  after we dismantle it, she\\\'s got a little pile of coins over on her side of the table, about a hundred kronor\\\'s worth.  i count fifty kronor in change out of it and put it in a separate pile then take a fifty-kronor note out of my pocket and ask her if i can exchange it for some of her money.  she eyes the note warily.</P><P>\\\"it\\\'s worth the same as this money here.  it\\\'s the same thing.  plus, look how pretty it is.\\\"</P><P>she agrees, and i scoot the pile over to my side of the table.  she takes the note over to show it off to her mum and dad.</P><P>with per helping her play, we take turns dealing, each losing a little or winning from the other then coming around to even again.  as i\\\'m about to give her a card, i see a glint of silver from between her lips.</P><P>\\\"hey, what have you got in your mouth?\\\"</P><P>she takes out a coin, shiny and wet, and holds it between her fingers.</P><P>\\\"don\\\'t put money in your mouth.  i almost choked to death on a fifty ore piece when i was little.\\\"</P><P>we play until dinnertime, putting our stakes in separate piles on the counter until after we eat.  for dinner, there\\\'s salmon, fried potatoes, spinach in cream sauce, and strawberries.  i help clean up, then we play some more.  when it\\\'s her bedtime, i give her all my change, and we put the piggybank back together.  she drops the coins into the slot one by one, setting the note to the side.</P><P>after per puts her to bed, we all sit around the kitchen table and talk for awhile, then i take a shower and put on one of per\\\'s t-shirts to sleep in.  they\\\'ve put sheets on the futon in the living room.  i lay there in the dark and listen to erika and per make love.  i am jet-lagged in the dark in this apartment on valhallavagen.  after an hour with no luck falling asleep, i turn on the light and select <U>hunger</U> from their shelf and start to read it again for the first time since college.</P>'),(3232,'<p>\r\nI promise, no more posts about any of my body parts after this one.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nBut anyway, I have boobs. A pair of them, in fact. They\\\'re decent boobs, really, but nothing terribly special. They\\\'re just, y\\\'know, boobs.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThey do change cup sizes from an A to a B during certain times of the month - so that can be a rather spiffy occasion.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nTwo nights ago, I walked downstairs to the dorm\\\'s lounge(I\\\'m a college student, devourer of shrimp ramen). The lounge is usually full of a bunch of giggling girls(and a few giggling boys) discussing fairly inane topics. They tire me.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nBut this time, the topic of discussion was one that - to use an overused idiom - struck pretty damn close to home.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nTHEY WERE TALKING ABOUT MY BOOBS.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nApparently they are quite firm. And perky. And very well sized in proportion to my 5 foot, 97-ish pound frame. And nice enough to be the envy of half of the girls on my floor.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nDo you have any idea how flattering that is?\r\nMy ego feels all warm and fuzzy. I am so friggin happy that a bunch of giggling girls like my boobs!  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI later got a request from one of the more courageous, and lees boobed, ones to touch them. I said sure. She cupped the right one in her hands and reaffirmed her decision.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nYay.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3233,'<p>\r\nhe\\\'s packing his bags and preparing to leave for the desert.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m packing my bags and preparing to move home to florida.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe packing is harder for me, as i must take with me six months worth of everything required to live my life as normally as possible... alone.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe go about our chore, speaking cheerfully of anything we can think of that is good about the news.<br><br>\r\n\r\n this is a game we have played all the years of our marriage, before he was in the army, during the twenty years he was active duty, during the four years he was retired, and more than ever, during the last three years, since he began working as an army civilian.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit\\\'s the pollyanna game.  today, as we make lists and discuss what to take with us to florida, we play it with fierce concentration and forced humour.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe watch the protesting populace on the teevee and we both nod and say how glad we are they don\\\'t live in iraq, for if they did, they would be slaughtered for their insolence.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni ask him if it upsets him that elly is among the crowds, and he smiles and says, no, that makes him very happy, because he knows that she couldn\\\'t be there if people like him weren\\\'t willing to pack their bags and go to the desert.<br><br>\r\n\r\n i laugh and say that my dad wouldn\\\'t feel like that.  he laughs and says my dad does feel like that, he\\\'s just not stupid enough to say it out loud to my mother.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthere are pictures of the suv the dead contractor was driving in kuwait.  he looks very closely at the teevee and says that is the same vehicle he drove the last time he was there.  he is glad it won\\\'t be in commission when he returns, the air conditioning didn\\\'t work.  we don\\\'t look at each other, there isn\\\'t anything pollyanna could say about sudden death.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe isn\\\'t in the army.  i am worried about whether or not he will have a weapon to protect himself.  he says he will have a sidearm he thinks, but not to worry, he will get one if there is combat nearby.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmatter of factly, we discuss what sort of body armor he will be issued as a civilian, whether or not his gas mask will have prescription eyeglass inserts, and the code words he will use to alert me as to his situation.  he says his will is getting updated on tuesday, he will leave everything to me, and then, if something happens to me, the girls.  i nod and say nothing.  i remind myself to find all of the insurance policies and carry them with me.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe go to walmart to pick up my insulin.  it\\\'s full of downy-faced soldiers with impossibly young wives, moving through the store with quiet desperation, the aisles are filled with their unspoken fear.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe stop as a train passes by.  hundreds of flatbed cars, empty now, going to the fort to pick up more vehicles.<br><br>\r\n\r\n i tell him that it creeps me out.  i remark that each of those cars represents two vehicles, each vehicle five men, each man has a family - a wife, parents, children, brothers, sisters, cousins, and friends.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe train is long, longer than any train we have ever seen.  we talk about how long it is, but we don\\\'t say that we know this is not the first train, this is the one that has already dropped cargo at the port, and we both stare at it, wondering how many more times it will make the trip, carrying all of those soldiers and their families with it.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni think of something to be glad about.  i tell him i am so happy i am not one of those frightened young wives, that i worry about them, they are not as strong as i am.  they don\\\'t know the rules.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe rules, he asks?  i don\\\'t answer.  he knows why.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe rules are - you never fall apart until they are gone and cannot see you do it.<br><br>\r\n\r\n</p>'),(3234,'<p>\r\nWe sat in a musty Starbucks, me sipping on caramel apple cider, him on a mocha frap. I never liked those mocha fraps. They made my head ache.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m sorry I didn\\\'t call you back. I was out.\\\" he said.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s okay.\\\" I muttered.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh. Okay.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Yeah. It\\\'s just that when I called your house this girl answered. And she said you were in the shower. And then she said you weren\\\'t home\\\", I said, in perhaps too accusing a voice.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Um...yeah. That was my sister\\\'s friend. They were having a sleepover. She didn\\\'t tell me you called until an hour ago\\\", he explains.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Oh. Okay.\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Gee, someone\\\'s suspicious today.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m not suspicious, just worried.\\\" Self defense. Of course I was suspicious. She had such a sexy , throaty voice. The kind that would do really well in porn flicks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Silly girl. You are such a bitch today.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe broke up a week later, though I couldn\\\'t quite accuse him of cheating ON me, when he was in fact cheating WITH me. There\\\'s just something so second hand about being cheated WITH.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s three years later now and we\\\'ve both grown up, him more so than I. He\\\'s married to a beautiful woman(not the porn voice one), and lives with three kids and a dog. The dog is named Max. He loves them, and has settled down into a life of blissful domesticity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd sometimes I wonder - what if I hadn\\\'t been such a bitch that day?\r\n</p>'),(3236,'<P>they feed me something, then they sit me down in the living room.  here it comes.</P><P>\\\"john hendricks told us you were in jail,\\\" dad says.  \\\"he said you were arrested in a bar, drunk out of your mind, with pot on you.  when i heard that, i knew it was just a matter of time before we heard from you again.  you do this over and over - leave here with the world at your feet, got no use for us, don\\\'t hear from you for months on end, then you get in trouble and come back here with your hat in your hand.  if you want to live here, there\\\'s going to be certain rules.\\\"</P><P>he ticks them off on his fingers.</P><P>\\\"you\\\'re not going to drink or do drugs while you\\\'re living here.  you\\\'re going to go back to the doctor and get back on some kind of medication if they think you need it.  you\\\'re going to get substance abuse counseling.  you\\\'re going to go to a.a. and n.a. meetings three times a week.  you will be willing, at any time, to go to the hospital and get a breathalyzer or a urine test.\\\"</P><P>he starts on the fingers of the other hand.  the onyx in his masonic ring glints in the lamplight.</P><P>\\\"you will get a job.  promptly.  you will give us two hundred fifty dollars a month room and board.  you will put the rest of your salary in a savings account in our name, so you can\\\'t get to it.  that\\\'ll go towards getting your own place.  you will attend church and sunday school every week.  and you will not stay here for more than six months.\\\"</P><P>\\\"that\\\'s fine,\\\" i say.  \\\"but i\\\'d appreciate it if you don\\\'t test my urine for forty days, \\\'cause i just smoked a couple of hours ago.  it stays in your system for forty days.\\\"</P><P>\\\"are you high right now?\\\" mom asks.</P><P>\\\"somewhat.\\\"</P><P>\\\"well, go to bed then.  it\\\'s no use talking to you if you\\\'re high.\\\"  she turns to my dad.  \\\"it\\\'s no use talking to him when he\\\'s as high as he is.  messed up on drugs.\\\"</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m not <U>that</U> high.\\\"</P><P>\\\"just go to bed, hophead.\\\"</P><P>my room\\\'s just like i left it two years ago, with the addition of an antique sewing machine my mom\\\'s storing in the corner.  there are cassette tapes, empty pillbottles, a calendar, a razor blade in a bottom desk drawer, shelves of books with porn mags hidden among them, condoms, dumbbells, a couch in the corner, old clothes in the closet, old underwear in the drawer, fingernail scissors, screwdrivers, a marshall amp, manuscripts, envelopes, a rosenquist print i got for my birthday, photos, ticket stubs, love letters, my graduation cap and gown, awards, carvings, a bible in english, a bible in german, audio components, a telephone, running shoes, swimsuits, an athletic supporter, batteries, paper clips, and a camera.  i open my pack up on the bed and put my things away.  the notebooks i carry to the desk.</P><P>i write about the hero\\\'s first days home after flunking out of college.  he wishes he was in jamaica or chelsea, amsterdam or ljubljana or mexico city, sedona or heaven or the moon, playing cribbage, laughing, looking into the abyss, learning spanish, dissembling, decompensating, listening or dancing or dying real slow.</P><P>he dreams he is an ancient sea captain, a thing of the sun and stars, water and air, his holds full of feathers and salt, gold and olives and pearls.  he would have a menagerie for a crew and a honeymoon life.  there would be ports, there would be sirens, and he would never be becalmed.</P> '),(3237,'<P>they feed me something, then they sit me down in the living room.  here it comes.</P><P>\\\"john hendricks told us you were in jail,\\\" dad says.  john\\\'s a public defender they know.  \\\"he said you were arrested in a bar, drunk out of your mind, with pot on you.  when i heard that, i knew it was just a matter of time before you came around again.  you do this over and over - leave here with the world at your feet, got no use for us, don\\\'t hear from you for months on end, then you get in trouble and come back here with your hat in your hand.  if you want to live here, there\\\'s going to be certain rules.\\\"</P><P>he ticks them off on his fingers.</P><P>\\\"you\\\'re not going to drink or do drugs while you\\\'re living here.  you\\\'re going to go back to the doctor and get back on some kind of medication if they think you need it.  you\\\'re going to get substance abuse counseling.  you\\\'re going to go to a.a. and n.a. meetings three times a week.  you will be willing, at any time, to go to the hospital and get a breathalyzer or a urine test.\\\"</P><P>he starts on the fingers of the other hand.  the onyx in his masonic ring glints in the lamplight.</P><P>\\\"you will get a job.  promptly.  you will give us two hundred fifty dollars a month room and board.  you will put the rest of your salary in a savings account in our name, so you can\\\'t get to it.  that\\\'ll go towards getting your own place.  you will attend church and sunday school every week.  and you will not stay here for more than six months.\\\"</P><P>\\\"that\\\'s fine,\\\" i say.  \\\"but i\\\'d appreciate it if you don\\\'t test my urine for forty days, \\\'cause i just smoked a couple of hours ago, and it stays in your system for forty days.\\\"</P><P>\\\"are you high right now?\\\" mom asks.</P><P>\\\"somewhat.\\\"</P><P>\\\"well, go to bed then.  it\\\'s no use talking to you if you\\\'re high.\\\"  she turns to my dad.  \\\"it\\\'s no use talking to him when he\\\'s as high as he is.  messed up on drugs.\\\"</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m not <U>that</U> high.\\\"</P><P>\\\"just go to bed, hophead.\\\"</P><P>my room\\\'s just like i left it two years ago, with the addition of an antique sewing machine my mom\\\'s storing in the corner.  there are cassette tapes, empty pillbottles, a calendar, a razor blade in a bottom desk drawer, shelves of books with porn mags hidden among them, condoms, dumbbells, a couch in the corner, old clothes in the closet, old underwear in the drawer, fingernail scissors, screwdrivers, a marshall amp, manuscripts, envelopes, a rosenquist print i got for my birthday, photos, ticket stubs, love letters, my graduation cap and gown, awards, carvings, a bible in english, a bible in german, audio components, a telephone, running shoes, swimsuits, an athletic supporter, batteries, paper clips, and a camera.  i open my pack up on the bed and put my things away.  the notebooks i carry to the desk.</P><P>i write about the hero\\\'s first days home after flunking out of college.  he wishes he was in jamaica or chelsea, amsterdam or ljubljana or mexico city, sedona or heaven or the moon, playing cribbage, laughing, looking into the abyss, learning spanish, dissembling, decompensating, listening or dancing or dying real slow.</P><P>he dreams he is an ancient sea captain, a thing of the sun and stars, water and air, his holds full of feathers and salt, gold and olives and pearls.  he would have a menagerie for a crew and a life like a cheap honeymoon.  there would be ports, there would be sirens, and he would never be becalmed.</P> '),(3238,'<P>\r\nSouth Korea is a newer democracy. Lets just call it a democracy. Is it? I have no idea. I dont know what goes on within the smokey rooms of korean business and governement. Ive heard stories of corruption and scandal, but for the most part I just see what goes on around me and make assumptions on that. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI live in the center of Seoul if you look at a subway map. It\\\'s a good but very dirty area of the city. The dirt isnt the kind of dirt that builds up over the years because of unchecked littering and decay. The filth that has built up over the years is of the exact opposite. It is the filth of development and business. The fish market that takes up the center of my area produces a lot of garbage. At night there is a rather large pile of paper, boxes, and cardboard. Its like a giant version of the pile of christmas wrapping paper and sweater boxes that builds up over the christmas holidays. This pile of waste provides for the collectors. The ones who collect paper, cardboard, metal, wood, and other junk from all the different piles of garbage within all the different areas of seoul. This nation really is still in the grips of a small business revolution. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn most major roads economics ressemble that of any other major street in north america. Body shops, 7-11, starbucks, and the Korean copy cats. But as soon as you walk down any of the tiny streets linking hundreds of medium sized apartment buildings you find a large number of family owned and operated stalls, cubbyhole shops, small markets, and random sundries.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the way up the hill towards my house are two small convienience stores. They are each about ten by ten in size and are jam packed full of beverages, bottled water, alcohol, cigarettes, junk food, and ramen noodles. There is little or no regard for what we call \\\'codes\\\' and \\\'zoning\\\' in the west. I don\\\'t really think they are disasters waiting to happen, but I feel sorry for the people who own these shops. They are older koreans, and they live in the shop. When they close they lean old doors and pieces of corrogated steel up against the tables displaying their booty and then sleep on the same benches they sit when they are open for business. Late at night, me and shane walk by one of the stores and are able to peer into one of them through a gap in the old doors and steel wall. The lady who smiles at us during the say is usually nodding off on the bench..sometimes her pillow is a bottle of water. Im not sure what \\\'class\\\' id put this woman in as she \\\'is\\\' a business owner and probably chooses to sleep where she works. This is something that a lot of koreans do. Id say that a majority of Korean small businesses are both places of commerce and castles. Working from home takes on a whole new meaning here. My mom told me that Toronto in the 50\\\'s and 60\\\'s was like that, but from what I can tell, this way of life has pretty much died in big city canada. It has most definately not died in Seoul Korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou can climb to my apartment from three different directions. The one I just mentioned is from the fish market and center of Guemho. The second is up the hill from a less gradient road that leads towards the subway station. Taking this road gets you past the fish market and speeds up the trek to the subway station considerably. The third route up to my house is up a 200meter staircase. Beside the staircase on both sides at the bottom of the hill are three run-n-tugs. Ive never actually gone into one of these places, but they are everywhere. The ladies who work in these establishments arent all that ugly, though they do look like old versions of ladies there age who arent rub-n-tub artists. Ive also never seen someone go into one of these places and so im not sure how much money they make and im definatly not sure what kind of lives they live outside of work. When I walk past they look sullen but nothing on their face displays sadness. Perhaps they are just bored out of their skulls. Being white, I dont really get smiles from them as these establishments dont really cator to the western crowd. Its just really strange seeing these kinds of places in an area of the city that is historically conservative. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs fowl as the smells in this neighbourhood are, I do like living here. Its close to work, and its within good distance to all the areas of the city I like to visit and see regularly. There is always life here. From when I wake up until about midnight you can hear kids playing in the streets, their apartments, and in the stairwells. When it snows the kids gather up cardboard and try sliding down one of the more steep access hills. The concrete has been so weathered, scraped, and walked on the surface for sledding isnt all that great, but their laughs will state otherwise. At night you can hear people singing in the streets drunk on soju and whiskey. Young lovers hide in the sidestreets holding hands and chatting quietly. This is what city life is. This is what I like about this city. The fact that there is no real downtown. The city has not been divided like the cities of north america have. There is a copy of almost all types of business in almost all of seoul. Seoul has no center. There are no skyscrapers, and there are no business districts. There are so many people here, that having a \\\'center\\\' of town would cripple transportation routes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nKorea is different and strange. However it is by no means \\\'the best place\\\' in asia. Not yet. \r\n</p>'),(3239,'<p>\r\nThe black Mercedes limousine went around to the back of the hotel, because its passenger was much too famous to enter through the lobby. It coasted to a stop and two enormous men, industrial freezers in funeral suits, hustled out. One of them stood lookout, and the other opened the left-side passenger door. From the back seat emerged Jackie Zhao, kung-fu star of all Asia. He hiked up the hem of his silk robe and moved towards the service entrance, gliding over the fetid puddles as though exempt from gravity. Inside, the kitchen-boys cowered like God himself had deigned to visit.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œWait! Jackie! Wait!â€ someone said. A decrepit old man stepped out of the shadows, shivering with cold. He shoved the sleeves of his patched and threadbare peasantâ€™s jacket up to his elbows and bowed three times, his right fist tucked into his knobby left hand.\r\n<p>\r\nOne of the black-suited bodyguards moved to toss him into the garbage, but Jackie froze him with one upraised finger. He regarded the old man with the blank stare of a stone Buddha.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œIt is I, your father, Number Five son!â€ the old man said. He looked cross. â€œWhy have you not contacted us in so long?â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œYou sold me,â€ Jackie reminded him. â€œTo the Peking Opera. Twenty years ago. For three sacks of rice.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œAh yes, yes.â€ The old manâ€™s head bobbed. â€œI had to. We were starving. We are still starving! We need your help.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œAt the opera,â€ Jackie continued, as if he had not heard, â€œwe trained from dawn till midnight without a break. Only after that did we eat. We sucked shreds of meat from stewbones, then curled up on the floor to huddle together until it was time to train again. If anyone made a mistake, they beat us with bamboo canes.â€ He closed his eyes and sighed. â€œIt was a terrible place. Sometimes I think about rebuilding the old Opera house, just so I could have the satisfaction of burning it down myself.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œBut you owe all your success to the Opera!â€ the old man said. â€œThey taught you to sing, they taught you to dance. They taught you kung-fu! They taught you to fly!â€\r\n<p>\r\nJackie nodded, his hand on his chin. â€œThat is true.â€\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œAnd therefore, by extension, your success is in part due to me!â€ The old man bared his teeth and extended his hands in supplication. â€œI am hungry. It is only right that you share with your father some fruit of the success he helped secure for you.â€\r\n<p>\r\nJackie considered this for a long moment. â€œThat is also true. A righteous son should share his wealth. But wisdom is wealth, as much as gold.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œMy opera master said, â€˜Give a man food, and soon he must beg for more. Teach him a skill, he will never need to beg again.â€™â€\r\n<p>\r\nJackie nodded to his bodyguards, who seized the old manâ€™s arms.\r\n<p>\r\nâ€œTake him up to the roof,â€ Jackie told them. â€œTeach him to fly.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n'),(3240,'<p>When Slim asks for another, he reaches for a short glass without looking. His fingers curl around the bottle of Seagramâ€™s and he splashes liquor through the air into the glass. It coats it from the inside and eases over the ice, a serene pool for the split second before the 7-Up gushes out of the nozzle. He brings his eyes back to the bar only to pinch a cocktail straw between his fingers and slide the drink toward the soggy cardboard coaster in front of Slim. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe door shudders as a wind pushes it inward. He lifts his gaze to the bell that hangs from the door, it doesnâ€™t let out a tinkle, but he feels a slight rush of cold air. He expects the air to smell like the moist night and low clouds of the neighborhood. Instead his tongue flattens into the bottom of his mouth, pressed against his lower teeth, heavy with the metallic taste he automatically associates with blood. He unconsciously lifts a cocktail napkin to his mouth and wipes his lips. \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe looks back to the door and squints, trying to see something that isnâ€™t there. One of the guys with the sweet drinked girlfriends finishes a story and the girls laugh in throaty gurgles. In the background, masked by the conversation and the jukebox, he hears the hiss of a nearer giggle and looks instinctively to the end of the bar. There is nothing there, just four empty barstools in a trail at Slims side.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHead titled, he concentrates on the sounds of the bar and decides that the jukebox speakers must need dusting. He shrugs and turns his back to the room to dig enough quarters out of the tip jar to play his favorite set of songs. As he leans for a rag on a lower shelf, he feels the cold air again, and this time hears the jingle of the bell. </p>\r\n'),(3241,'<p>\r\nLast night as I lay in bed trying to sleep I heard a terrible noise. Two cats had crossed paths and one of them didn\\\'t like that one bit. If you\\\'d have heard the rancid sound coming out of this cat in a dark cave, you\\\'d have turned around. The raquet went on for some time. Tossing and turning while being forced to listen to two cats squabble over some fish heads isn\\\'t the correct recipe for slumber. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was getting rather anxious. It was almost Five thirty, and It was getting brighter outside. I needed the silence I was used to. The sounds of high heels going down the stairs for work. The girl upstairs practicing her piano at eight am. At four are the garbage scavengers, at five is the rooster. Eight months here has turned into an accoustic routine for my ears. A routine that allows me to just turn off and sleep. but to have two cats interrupt this routine by squabbling over some scraps enraged me. These were not huggable cute house cats. These were ruthless urban killing machines. When they meow shit, they mean business. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt first I didn\\\'t know what side of the apartment the noise was coming from. There were two possibilities. The sound was either coming from outside my window, or the kitchens. I opened my window and noticed that the sound was not louder. Leaving my room for the kitchen I knew it was coming from down in the junk heap. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nThe two cats were about five feet from each other in the junk heap. The cat making all the noize was older, fatter, and black. It was quite literally a fat cat. It was a veteran of the streets. The second cat was beige, and much younger; more naive. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI guess the younger cat had stood up for itself. Not too sure. I thought back to those old loony toon cartoons with the cat singing on the fence and one of the \\\'popular\\\' characters would throw a shoe or empty bottle out to quiet things down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe junk heap below was a mess. I myself have added a few items to the junk below. Hair from the bathroom drain, old chicken carcas from the fridge, a few plastic bags full of garbage, and just recently, an old mop that broke. Its quite literally the best place in town to throw shit out. The yard is square and is on a slope. It measures roughly twenty feet squared. The entire yard is littered with the frames of old chairs, cracked kimchi pots, a broken laundry machine, some two hundred garbage filled black bags, old doors, and a rocky landscape void of any large clumps of grass. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPeering out the window I spotted the cats face to face close to the wall. one of the cats was in the garbage pit of the yard, while the cat being screamed at was in a rain water run off canal that surrounded three sides of the building like a small moat. It was quite a sound. The fat black cat was hissing and breathing fire at the beige smaller cat. It was now six. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy first choice of projectile proved to have been the wrong choice. I had grabbed at a seventy five percent used bar of Dove Moisturizing soap that had been given to me as part of a Dove gift set during Chew-sok. I lobbed it towards the cats and missed. It had landed quite off course in a mushy clump of weeds and mud. They recognized the noise of the soap but were not afraid. \\\"DAMN IT ALL TO HELL\\\" I proclaimed!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI needed something heavier. I needed something to make them stop. I decided on throwing a shoe down there to quell this feline uprising. Im not sure if the shoe came to mind because the shoe rack is close to the window, or weather it was because I wanted to scare the cats and shame them as well. According to Sihks, having a shoe thrown at you is the ultimate insult. Perhaps this was true with cats?!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI grabbed at one of the beige leather shoes I got for twenty thousand(18$US) won down the hill. I got what I\\\'d paid for. They were junk. Fit to join the junk below, and provide me with the means of silencing the cats. In addition, by choosing shoes as the method of projectile you automatically have two shots. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy first shoe hit dead on. I didn\\\'t see it hit the cat, but im pretty shoer(haha) it hit the black cat. Startled, the black cat scrammbled to another spot a little further from the small beige cat. I waited to see if the silence would continue. As I leaned out the window to get a better view of the situation, the cats were still staring each other down. I waited for confimation that the confrontation was over. It was so nice noticing the silence I had become used to and felt at ease again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nease was shortlived. The Black cat began to snarl again. I had to use my second shot right away. Grabbing the shoe I decided for a shot that would put the shoe between the cats. With what I thought was the correct spot I gave the shoe a firm toss. It landed off course, but landed on something loud sending both cats scrambling in opposite directions. \\\"BRAVO\\\" I muttered. I was proud of myself for having initiated this most noble of public services. I retreated to bed to enjoy some peace and quiet in the silence I\\\'d become used to. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3242,'<P>i don\\\'t fall asleep until four in the morning.  they all get up and go about their business very quietly.  whispering in the kitchen.  erika leaves for work.  sabina and her dad play in her room with the door shut, then they leave.</P><P>sabina\\\'s dad.</P><P>sleep; wake at noon.  see what\\\'s on tv.  softboil an egg.  call howard.</P><P>\\\"whoever this is is fixin\\\' to get killed,\\\" he says.</P><P>i apologize for calling so early and ask him to go down to the jail and tell matt what happened.</P><P>\\\"i\\\'m not going to the jail.  what if i have a warrant?  i\\\'ll tell \\\'im if and when he gets out.  when he comes around here.\\\"</P><P>\\\"he thinks he\\\'s getting out when he has his next court date.\\\"</P><P>\\\"when zat?\\\"</P><P>\\\"in a week or so.\\\"</P><P>i give him per\\\'s number and wish him well.  look at their photo albums.  there\\\'s one of per and i in ibiza.  seems like a hundred years ago.  us in florida, in venice.</P><P>he comes in, alone.</P><P>\\\"where\\\'s sabina?\\\"</P><P>\\\"at her play group.  whatcha doin\\\'?\\\"</P><P>hold up the album.  \\\"reminiscing.\\\"</P><P>\\\"do you remember?\\\" he kind of sings.</P><P>\\\"je me souvien.\\\"</P><P>he sits down on the futon beside me.</P><P>\\\"god, i was so drunk in this one,\\\" he says.  \\\"remember how sunburned i got?\\\"</P><P>\\\"yeah, even your scalp was burnt.\\\"</P><P>he chuckles.  looks at me.</P><P>\\\"anna banana.\\\"  wistful.</P><P>look at the album.  back at him.  he\\\'s still looking at me.  look in each other\\\'s eyes.</P><P>a silence as long as august.</P><P>look down.  our heads draw closer.  our lips part.  i turn my head away at the last minute, and he kisses my cheek.  look at him.  he grins.</P><P>\\\"hi,\\\" he says.</P><P>\\\"hi.\\\"</P><P>kiss him, mouths open, tongues gliding and snaking over each other.  i\\\'m wearing his t-shirt and a pair of panties.  he puts his hand over my left breast and brushes the nipple through the cloth with his thumb.  we kiss again.  he puts his other hand on my right one.  we\\\'re still kissing.</P><P>taking his mouth away from mine, he bends down and sucks my right nipple through the cloth of his t-shirt.  i put my hand down the front of my panties and flick my clit with my middle finger.  he sucks my other nipple.</P><P>i take off the shirt.  he undresses.  he has a better body than when we were going out:  planes and swells of sharply-etched muscle covered with clean smooth glossy skin.  i run my hands over his abs and rub his nipples.</P><P>he stands by the edge of the futon with his dick in his hand and rubs the head over my lips.  i wet my lips and close them o