>\\\"no.  stay.  it\\\'s alright.\\\"</P><P>\\\"i\\\'d really better go.  it wouldn\\\'t feel right around erika.\\\"</P><P>\\\"she would understand, i think.\\\"</P><P>\\\"do you really want to find out?\\\"</P>'),(3243,'<p>There were seven flights of stairs. I counted them on the way back down. It was the first apartment we saw together. I\\\'d never been to the area before. The woman showing the apartment was somewhere from the middle east and her German was underdeveloped to a painfully obvious point. Her answers came in nods and grunts that we tried to distiguish between yeses and nos.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nApparently I\\\'m pampered to a degree and don\\\'t have a clear idea of how things function here in Germany. I\\\'ve never looked at an apartment without light fixtures and where the kitchen is nothing more than four walls and a hanging florescent. The bedroom flooring was tan linoleum and on one side has a door that opens up to a metal balcony. Balconies are nice when the view is also. The view from this particular one faced the identical apartment buildings on the other side of the square. Beautiful. From the front door and through the livingroom lays a medium blue nonplush carpet with signs of some use and abuse. A very large window to a scene of dead land and yet more apartment buildings and parking lots, another balcony, these are the highlights. The bathroom is so small and dark I am afraid to think of the mixure of water and razors within that small 7X4 confine.\r\n<br><br>\\\'So, the carpet will stay?\\\' I asked in German. <br><br>\r\n\\\'As you like,\\\' she responded in a heavy accent. <br><br>\r\n\\\'And the furniture?\\\' I referenced the oddities of white painted wood, glass and chincy plastic blocks that supposedly served as living furniture.<br><br>\r\nShe gave the same answer. She was overflowing with information. Really. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nSince being in Germany I\\\'ve moved comfortably between a furnished room in a private student house to a modern furnished apartment with more perks than I could count myself lucky to find. Where I live now, it\\\'s the bottom floor of a house, on the side of a mountain, set in the lush green mountains. Because of the apartment, I can overlook the 4,000 person population and boredom. The apartment today was the opposite of what I have now. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe\\\'re two young people looking for an apartment. Having said that, I don\\\'t think it\\\'s necessary to add that we have very little money. This apartment is in our price range, and in a time when apartments are few and few affordable, we can almost not risk \\\'looking further and deciding later.\\\' Not when there is a time limit, I only have until the first of April to move out, and now he\\\'s back to his normal schedule of classes and work that last far into the evening. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve been here for a year, and I\\\'m wondering if there\\\'s ever going to be a time when I\\\'m not surprised by anything anymore. No light fixtures - just wires. No stove, refridgerator, sink or stove. If we\\\'re lucky, we may inherit his mother\\\'s boss\\\'s mother\\\'s kitchen things, as she has recently passed away. Getting lucky there would save us literally thousands. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI think I\\\'m supposed to be happy. This is, after all, what I wanted. To grow up and find this wonderful guy, move in with him in our apartment. Struggling without furniture, without money, just holding hands on our balcony facing the buildings that stack up like graveyard mortuaries for the living and smoking our expensive cigarettes because we\\\'ll have rules about smoking indoors from then on. Maybe if we have a burner and a pot donated, we\\\'ll also be able eat our pasta and cheap rice in bowls from IKEA on the floor. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have a sense of irony somehow in this situation. I have a massive collection of music, closets worth of clothes I don\\\'t wear because they aren\\\'t European enough for me to feel comfortable here, and between the two of us we each have a computer and stereo. We can\\\'t eat these things, nor can we logically sit on them. My things of value are now degraded to their set value - nothing in starting-off living away from the \\\'rents worth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI will be happy though, and things will become better sometime. I have the guarantee that we can do one thing at least. We do have a bed, it will sit in our bedroom that we can barely afford even with government help for poor students and half-foreigners, we will have our only light source of candles and starlight if it fits through the fake-city lights. Though we may be starving at some times, shabbily dressed and over worked, we will both be able to come home to the simple happiness of each other and sharing the same bed. This alone, erases all the other discomforts and complaints. The value of him in my life overrides the lack of what we will not have - furniture for instance.\r\n</p>'),(3244,'<p>Because of business my business, I have been away for 3 weeks.  Yesterday, I got a call.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Papa\\\", she says, using her usual term of endearment.<p>\r\n\r\n<p>I sense it already.  I sense the excitement in the youthful voice.  This bubbly voice, that has spoken millions of sparkling syllables to me, now squeaks, almost strangely.  She fights to hold her feelings back.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"How is my mommy?\\\"  I always play along.  It\\\'s fun.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, this time, our names are suddenly more appropriate.  She is becoming momma inside, which will make me the poppa.</P\r\n\r\n<p>How should I feel about this?  How should I feel about a potential kink in my master plans?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We are both young.  I am very concerned, that I cannot be the father I need to be at this point.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have invested my savings to start a company.  Life as an entrepreneur is very unpredictable.  But, this is my calling.  I will not go back to the 9-5 life where I came from.  I struggled long hours, and saved my pennies to get where I am.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Abortion.  Everyone has an opinion.  Right or wrong.  Very few people can sit in the middle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My concerns are the following:\r\n<ul><li>We are very early first tri-mester.  Will going through with the operation cause any future complications with having many other planned babies?</li>\r\n<li>It\\\'s very hard to be with my momma right now (for both reasons of immigration and the uncertain state of my business).  How can I help her to get through this time?  I want to be on the phone with her every minute.</li>\r\n</ul></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am concerned for her emotional and physical well being.  I am concerned for our future together.  It\\\'s more real when it happens to you.\r\n</p>  \r\n?'),(3246,'<p>He lets the quarters fall out of his fist back among the nickels and dimes of yesterdays forgotten tips and lifts his head to the mirror in front of him. There are no new customers in the doorway. â€œWhat the fuck?â€ He mouths to his reflection, the left side of his image bowing out like an egg in the age warped mirror. Thinking the newcomer must have stepped to the side faster than he raised his eyes, he spins around on one heel.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere is no one but Slim in front of him, lazily swirling the straw around in circles in his half empty drink. Gently prodding the ice in intricate patterns that only he can recognize. The same two couples are still stretched out in a booth. Santiago lets his eyelids rest a little while at his next blink and when he opens them again, sees the door pushing forward in another wiggle. His brow furrows involuntarily until the door pushes all the way into the room. He can hear a bus pull away from the curb as a tall gloomy guy steps into the bar.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSantiago looks down, notices his hands are clenched into fists and takes a deep breath. He stretches his fingers out a few times, shifting his weight from one foot to the other, and shakes his head. The new customer has stepped up to the bar with his wallet in one hand. He walks over and stares at him from behind the bar. â€œWhat do you want to drink?â€ The guy orders a Manhattan, and he mindlessly fills a glass with ice, only realizing when he sets it down, that heâ€™d asked for it neat. Santiago hesitates and waits to see if the guy says anything. He doesnâ€™t.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe guy spends a few minutes looking through his pockets before he pulls a mostly empty pack of cigarettes out and lays them on the bar. Santiago reaches over and grabs the lime green plastic ashtray and slides it to over. As the guy lights up, the bell on the door tinkles, and another cold wind breezes past Santiago behind the bar. He waits to see who enters. No one does. He shrugs and picks the quarters back out of the tip jar.</p>\r\n'),(3245,'<p>we are but cripples with congruent wounds<br>\r\naddress each other without the familiarity gone before <br></p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>there is nothing left for us to do</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>say what you will <br>\r\n     think what you will; <br>\r\nall that we hoped for <br>\r\n     we didn\\\'t make it.  </p>\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n<p>this is not about what we lost, <br>\r\nits about the space emptied of what we had.</p>\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\n<p>(i miss you) <br>\r\n(my body aches)</p>'),(3247,'Everyone rise up!<br>\r\nWe got a rebellion going on!<br>\r\nCornflakes!<br>\r\nEvangelism!<br>\r\nNothing\\\'s weak!<br>\r\nWe\\\'re all fucking tired of seeing this shit!<br>\r\nWankers!<br>\r\nRockers!<br>\r\nDifferentiators!<br>\r\n\r\nSuffer no longer this mind is real!<br>\r\nSmash it with a bat!<br>\r\nWe have no original thought!<br>\r\nWe are monsters and whores!<br>\r\nWe \\\"No No\\\" when we should \\\"yes yes\\\"!<br>\r\nShit!<br>\r\nWhat the fuck is going on!<br>\r\nLet\\\'s fucking rock!<br><br>\r\nLet\\\'s speak in our native colloquialisms!<br>\r\nLet\\\'s not let people think we\\\'re interesting!<br>\r\nLet\\\'s keep a secret and watch nothing!<br>\r\nLet\\\'s get ignored!<br><br>\r\nLet\\\'s think only of ourselves!<br>\r\nLet\\\'s just seek pleasure!<br>\r\nRot Fuckers!<br>\r\nRot!<br>\r\nRot!<br>\r\nRot!<br>\r\nI hovercrafted here on the smell of my own shit!<br>\r\nRead these letters to your mother!<br> \r\nGo Away!<br>\r\n'),(3248,'<p>\r\nI wake up to more shouting. Jesus. Canâ€™t these two just give it a rest? Again with the bickering. Thirty year olds carrying on like spoiled toddlers. Iâ€™m the baby, and look at me. Quiet. At least for the moment. Mamaâ€™s holding me to her shoulder, a blanket over my head. It doesnâ€™t really matter; my eyes are too new, and disinclined to focus on anything. I hunt for tit by instinct. If I canâ€™t find some tit, thereâ€™s going to be trouble.\r\n<p>\r\nNo one realizes thisâ€”I mean, how could they?â€”but the soothing waters of the river Lethe act on time release. The memories of your most recent incarnation donâ€™t really begin to fade until you are about six months old. And the time between death and rebirth is just a blur of incoherent and terrifying images, a nightmare remembered only by the violence of your awakeningâ€”why do you think babies come out of the womb screaming? Me, I was born agogâ€”me, Jack Harkness, reincarnated as the child of my own worthless son. If one of my writers had handed me a script with this particular ironic twist, I wouldâ€™ve thrown him out on his hack ass.\r\n<p>\r\n<i>Wah rah wah BRAW nraw nraw,</i> my parents say. Itâ€™s funny that my interior monologue is in English, but I canâ€™t understand a word of their yammering. Still, the meaning is obvious. The old man is sick of this shit. Heâ€™s walking out the door. So just go already, jackass! You were always a shiftless punk, Raymond! I open my mouth to tell him but all that comes out is an air raid wail.\r\n<p>\r\nMoving now. Bobbing up and down. <i>Ooof!</i>A whole goddamned planet of whiskey-stinking male has fallen on me. My mouth finds mamaâ€™s nipple. I am happy, but smothering. Once, when I was really desperate, I slept with a fat, sad girl from Accounting. When she climbed on top of me it was like this. Black and airless. \r\n<p>\r\nNow there is violence. Shifting and shoving. Yes! I can breathe again. But the tit is gone. I gulp air and sing an impossibly high third harmony to their duet of grunting and screaming.  My blurry infant eyes fix on her breast, hanging loose from her torn blouse and constricting bra cup, and I experience a unity of purpose, a wholeness I will spend the rest of my life trying to recapture: <i>I WANT. GIMME.</i>\r\n<p>\r\nThen a new sensation arrives, shrieking along virgin nerves, overtaking desire. It starts small and then mushrooms, a big bang of pain inflating from the diameter of my arm span into a whole universe that is coming apart at the seams. There I am, suspended between opposites: male and female. Love and hate. Mirrored desire. Hanging in the air, cruciform, my baby voice cries the cry of the well and truly fuckedâ€”<i>My god, why have you forsaken me?</i>â€”and then I am staring into the Clear White Light again.\r\n<p>\r\nRaymond, you ass.\r\n       \r\n\r\n'),(3249,'<p>\r\nYesterday, I decided to go to Downtown LA. I figured that the little inky dinky town of Whittier, CA was repressing my artistic vision. Wait, I don\\\'t have any artistic vision. I guess I just wanted to get out for a while.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'d never experienced the art of riding public buses in this area before. There\\\'s always too much gum underneath the seats. Makes me paranoid. Either way, it was quite fortunate that I had Jesse, the resident LA-ian and public transportation expert(he took a class on urban studies). He is gay in the classical greek sense, which means that he doesn\\\'t like men so much as he dislikes women. Adam came along too. Adam is not gay, but it would probably be easier if he was.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWe took two buses to Downtown LA. I don\\\'t like transfers. Jesse introduced us to this spiffy thing called the \\\"Rapid\\\". It\\\'s a small, clean bus that brings you anywhere on its route for a quarter. They rock. I may not like buses, but I quite like the \\\"Rapid\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWe went to Chinatown first, because Adam wanted nice chopsticks. They also wanted to find me a nice chinese boy so that my relatives wouldn\\\'t complain so much about the lack of a good chinese boy in my dating history. There was this restaurant we saw called \\\"My Dung\\\". I don\\\'t care what the chinese characters next to it meant, I\\\'m not eating there. Overall, Chinatown was rather grimy and dirty and we didn\\\'t find satisfactory chopsticks or chinese boy.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThen we hopped back on the new beloved \\\"Rapid\\\", now made even more beloved by the fact that Jesse had us get off a couple stops too early and we got lost in Downtown LA for a while before finding the transfer point. And we headed to Little Tokyo.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMaybe it\\\'s a statement about China and Japan that LA\\\'s Chinatown is about as appealing as a trash heap on crack while Little Tokyo was about as neat and clean as anything in Downtown LA possibly could be. Jesse, Adam and I raided a local supermarket for Pocky. I was rather disappointed at the rather sparse variety of Pocky they had. I settled on a box of Almond Crunch Pocky, but I had really wanted to try some of this Green Tea Pocky that a Pocky Fan I know had mentioned once. I also got this soda in a funky bottle. It is opened by pushing a marble out of the bottle top. The marble stays in the bottle, rolls around and gets pretty damn annoying when attempting to drink a simple soda. The bottle looks pretty damn spiffy, though.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWhich leads to the question - <b>is there ever a time when aesthetics should take precedence over functionality?</b>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAfter that, we found our way into a pristine little hotel that had a Japanese garden on the roof. We got lots of dirty looks. I think we were trespassing.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThen we got back on the subway. There was a short debate over whether or not we should go to West Hollywood. We soon decided that it was cold, the sun was going down, and none of us were particularly big in the idea of wandering around West Hollywood on a cold, dark night.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThen, we went home.\r\n</p>'),(3250,'Christopher was extremly bored. He sat in the empty room, on the couch, reading a magazine. He had read the magazine before.\r\n<p>The sun went down, and it began to get dark in the apartment. Soon it was too dark to read any more. The apartment had no electricity. Christopher tried to read by candlelight, but it was not as easy as he thought it would be. In fact, it was extremely difficult, and he soon gave it up.</p><p>\r\nWhat the fuck, he thought to himself. Might as well go to the store. It was way too early to think about trying to go to sleep. Christopher was of the age where a young man is afraid he might miss something if he goes to sleep too early in the night. Even if he doesn\\\'t have but 65 dollars, and has to wait a few days for his roomates to come back to get the power turned on. He blew out the candle, and opened the door into the hallway. Orange light shone through the window of the hall door, which looked out onto the cracked cement and debris of the parking lot. As he got in the car, he automatically began rolling down the window. He was very quick at rolling it down. The car would not go, a child might think, without the window being down.</p><p>\r\nThe Super K\\\'s parking lot was mostly empty; there were maybe fourteen cars, arranged lazily around the entrance. Christopher got a good spot. He rolled the window up again, turned down the radio, and locked the doors, all at once, very quickly. He got out and went inside. He came out a short while later with another magazine.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThings continued on this way for quite a while.'),(3251,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3251_1.jpg\\\"><br><br>\r\nTonight was a horrible, horrible night, mind you, and just when I thought it was drawing to an end, who should appear out of the depths of hell (I-70) but my arch nemesis, Raymon Ramone.<br><br> \r\n\\\"Long time no see, Sport!\\\" shouts Ramon as he cues up his trademark sly grin. Frustrated already, I whisper back <i>\\\"stay the fuck away from my bathroom, Ray... I\\\'m not kidding.\\\"</i><br><br> \r\n<i>\\\"Hahah!\\\"</i> responds Raymon. I swear, the guy has enough nerve to look me straight in the eye and laugh good heartedly as if we were sharing some side-splitting inside joke.<br><br> \r\nAs we spoke, Ramon stood at the convenience-store coffee counter that I man for 14 hours a day. Although it took me a second to realize it, the little jerk was intently pouring every pot of my freshly brewed <i>go-juice</i> down the nearby utility sink.<br><br> \r\n<i>\\\"What do you think you\\\'re doing, Raymon?!\\\"</i>, said I. Keep in mind I\\\'d made this coffee something like five minutes before, and scrubbed down every freaking square centimeter of the counter, which was now strewn with sugar packets, little brown splash-stains, and tiny containers of creamer bubbling over the edges of the counter.<br><br> \r\nQuoth Raymon: <i>\\\"It just didn\\\'t look fresh, my man.\\\"</i><br><br>\r\nRaymon then spins on an alligator-booted heel and looms toward my bathroom, grinning the whole time and preparing himself for thirty solid minutes of shit-filled fun.<br><br> \r\nWhile Ramon Ramone was taking one of his infamous shit breaks, I bagged up all the trash from the food area and started lugging it out back to the dumpster. To add to my misery, it was twenty degrees or so out there, and the wind was blowing something fierce. I was covered head to toe in coffee grounds and the ooze from somebody\\\'s discarded cherry slurpee, all of it leaking out of three growing holes in the trash bag.<br><br> \r\nLo and behold! Blinging in the midwestern moonlight like a gift from the Convenience Store Deities was Ramon\\\'s aptly-put <i>\\\"Cherried-Out Rig\\\"</i>. Which I stared at with great guile. I noted that the driver\\\'s side window was half down, a new leopard print seat cover decking out the cushy old-school cab.<br><br> \r\nDon\\\'t get me wrong, people. I mean, normally, I\\\'m a paragon of <i>friendly</i> and <i>just</i> customer service, but tonight was just one push too far in the wrong direction. With leaky bag in tow, I hoisted my fat ass up onto the sideboard of Ramon\\\'s rig and gave that oozing shitbag a good hard shove through the window, where it landed with a <i>gluuurp</i>.<br><br> \r\nHey Raymon: \\\'tis better to give than to receive, motherfucker.'),(3252,'<P>\r\nWhy\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe most significant word to the evolution of human consciousness is \\\"why.\\\"  As a result â€“ it is the foremost concept that each culture attempts to eliminate from the developing minds of its young.  Indeed, \\\"why\\\" eventually â€“ even in private or confidential discourse - is beaten out of children as certainly as public gestures of masturbation, or acts deemed as sacrilege to the prevailing religions of the culture.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nVerily the success of cultural imprinting is demonstrated when the once inquisitive mind preemptively answers its own \\\"why\\\" with \\\"just because.\\\"  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Why\\\" is held in reserve as a precious resource until such time as the culture has screened its young, and determined which among them are best programmed to advance the causes of culturally appropriate sciences.  At this time and to these select few, \\\"why\\\" is rationed out for application with strict conditions:  at the right times, for the right lengths of time, among the right people, for the right purposes, within rightful limitations.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe punishment for failure to apply \\\"why\\\" within these bounds is administered as swiftly as it is cruelly.  The individual is stripped of credential and human credibility.  (The culture makes a hasty evaluation of the offenderâ€™s body of scientific advancements â€“ any part of which, if deemed outside the sphere of immediately relevant cultural contribution or utilization, is discredited summarily and recycled for use in the original imprint process to eliminate \\\"why\\\" from the developing minds of the young).\r\n</P><P>\r\nSomehow, certainly not surprisingly, the masses fail to observe the occasions when any such summarily discredited concept reaches the convergence of timeliness and cultural advancement or need as to become \\\"appropriate\\\" and, hence, valid.  The offender, however, is afforded no such second look.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe greatest mockery made by man to existence is the serious and intentional use of the words \\\"pure science.\\\"  \r\n</P>'),(3253,'<P>My parents\\\' house is bounded on the north by a dairy, to the west, by Dr. House\\\'s property.  If you follow the driveway through the woods to the east, you come to a dirt road with trailers on it, the homes of the Oxendines and the Smalleys and the Hog Man.  The Oxendines are part Seminole Indian.  Dad calls them \\\"red trash.\\\"  Further down the road are houses belonging to Mrs. Mock and Gail Rembert, a lady Dad used to work with who he calls \\\"Wonder Woman.\\\"</P><P>Nina Mock is a widow.  If you go down the lane until it hits Croom Road, which is paved, and turn right, you\\\'ll come to the home of another widow, Mrs. Millican.  I don\\\'t know her first name.  Her husband\\\'s name was Glen.  Nina\\\'s was Keith.  Keith died on Halloween, 1999; Glen, several years earlier.  Mrs. Millican\\\'s property abuts the southern end of my parents\\\'.  Her house used to be owned by Mom and Dad when they were first married.  It\\\'s the first house I lived in.</P><P>In 1970, when I was six months old, they sold the house and the front five acres and kept the ten in back to build on, which they did, ten years later.  My dad\\\'s younger sisters lived there when I was born.  After we moved away, one went back to Georgia, and the other settled in Clearwater.</P><P>Some other people, whose last names I don\\\'t know, live across the road from Mrs. Millican.  I do know that their sons are named Rinty and Jagger.  I remember this because my uncle told me their names once and commented that the names \\\"make them sound like a couple of damned dogs.\\\"  Dr. House lives next door.  Him got a big house.</P><P>Further down the road, blackberry bushes line the fence by the dairy.  They bear sometime in May.  A few years ago, I took a paper sack down there and picked a bunch.  I was listening to Stereolab on my headphones.  There were red and blue and yellow flowers in the masses of vines opposite the dairy.  The berries were purple-black and the music felt green.</P><P>Down by the highway, there\\\'s a convenience store where I used to buy Heineken in big green bottles.  Liters, maybe.  I don\\\'t know.  Sometimes the clerk is Margaret.  She knows me.  She\\\'s my mom\\\'s age, with red hair.</P><P>Walking down the short part of Croom Road between the highway and Howell Avenue, dogs bark at you from the yards on both sides.  Mom lived in one of those houses as a child.</P><P>Grandma lives in an apartment off Howell.  The sidewalk starts here.  Before, if cars were coming, you had to walk on the shoulder.</P><P>Going south, there are houses, a day care center, and an apartment complex where an uncle lived when he got divorced.  The apartments are now home to, among others, the family from India that owns the convenience store across the street.  They also have the big green bottles of Heineken, and they carry the tangy Mexican fruit-flavored sodas called Jarritos that you enjoy now that you\\\'ve quit drinking.  Inside, you see some Mexican beer three cases down from the sodas.  It\\\'s in squat brown bottles, two for three dollars.  You could take a couple behind the store, open them with the attachment on your Swiss Army knife, drink them in the company of the kids skipping school back there, smoking cigarrettes and joints, looking out for cops.  Nah, better stick with the Jarritos.</P><P>Past the store, there\\\'s the stadium.  Then the high school you graduated from in 1988.  It\\\'s changed a lot since then:  new gym, performing arts center, parking lot for the seniors.  You heard the kids have to wear ID badges now.</P><P>The sidewalk mounts a hill toward town, passing nice houses and shitty ones, a travel agency, and First Baptist Church.  You go up the church\\\'s driveway, fifteen minutes early for work.  You have a cup of coffee with Frank and Bill, wait for Freda to roll up.  The pastor is already in his office at quarter to eight, studying.</P><P>You start over in the fellowship hall because the Memorial Building has no heat.  You save it for later in the day when it\\\'s warmer.  On Mondays, you put up the chairs that were lined up in the fellowship hall for the senior adults\\\' assembly on Sunday morning.  Then you sweep the hall.  Vacuum the sanctuary.  Then a break.  Clean the bathrooms in the Memorial Building.  Finish up in the office.</P><P>After work, I walked further up Howell, past the convalescent home and the lawyer\\\'s office, and run into Bert in the library.  Bert used to be a ball player.  He\\\'s big, getting a little fat.  He\\\'s got a head as round as a crab cake.  Said he was in there to look up something about his car.  Bert\\\'s a car man.</P><P>Him and the rest of the guys have shunned me like the plague ever since one night at the Hilltop when I let something slip.  That was the night I got 86\\\'d, but not for what I let slip.  Well, pulling down my pants and shaking my wiener all around could be tangentially related to that certain something.  They might be distant cousins.</P><P>\\\"How long you been back in town?\\\" he wants to know.</P><P>\\\"Year and a half.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Really?  Haven\\\'t seen you around.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I been around.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Not where I been.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.\\\"</P><P>And that\\\'s our conversation, mostly.  He used to be my best friend.</P><P>I check out <U>My Antonia</U>, <U>To the Lighthouse</U>, and a book of Raymond Carver stories.  I go outside and am waylayed by Bud, the crazy guy I always talk to.  He spends a lot of time in the park.  He\\\'s named all the squirrels after Greek philosophers or mythological figures.  Sophocles, etc.  He says he\\\'s homeless, but I always see him over by the grocery stores on the other side of town.  I think he lives somewhere over that way.  He used to be a physicist, and he\\\'s always trying to tell me about how we live in seven dimensions.  It\\\'s disturbing how my social circle\\\'s now composed of crazies, Jesus freaks, old people, and family members.</P><P>He points to the bank across the street.  \\\"Jimmy Kimbrough\\\'s in there stealin\\\' money every day.\\\"  Kimbrough\\\'s the bank president.  \\\"Jimmy Kimbrough and I graduated in the same year, 1957.  I left Brooksville and ended up at Cape Canaveral.  But if I didn\\\'t have any morals, I could have ended up a millionaire, just like him.\\\"</P><P>I point past the bank to the courthouse.  \\\"What about them in there?\\\"  Local politics really agitate him.  He should get wound up now and go off on one of his rants.</P><P>He surprises me, though.  \\\"Yeah, them, too.  Stealin\\\' money,\\\" is all he says.  He\\\'s quiet, seems resigned to it all.</P><P>Sometimes I worry that I\\\'m going to end up like him, the town eccentric.  Sometimes I feel well on my way.</P><P>When I get home, the power\\\'s out.  Mom and Dad are sitting in the Florida room at the back of the house where there are wall-to-wall windows.  Plenty of ambient light.</P><P>Me:  How long\\\'s it been off?</P><P>Dad:  Couple of hours.  Power company says it won\\\'t be back on before eight o\\\'clock.</P><P>Me:  Well, we should go out to eat somewhere then, shouldn\\\'t we?</P><P>A pause.</P><P>Mom:  Pretty much have to, I guess.  Call your grandma and see if she wants to go.</P><P>We\\\'re sitting in the Road Kill Cafe, the four of us, west of town in Spring Hill.  We\\\'ve ordered.  Waiting for our food.  I\\\'m going to have chicken with poblano sauce.  \\\"Sexual Healing\\\" starts to ease out of the speakers overhead.</P><P>Grandma:  Where\\\'s the bathroom?</P><P>The other three of us point toward the back.  We\\\'ve been there before.  She excuses herself and gets up.</P><P>Dad:  You know who I saw the other day?</P><P>We drive back into town on Highway 50.  Everything downtown\\\'s closed.  The sidewalks roll up at five o\\\'clock.  We turn onto Main Street, pass the courthouse and the bank.  Main becomes Howell by the library.  Downhill, pass the nursing home, the church, the high school.  Let Grandma out at her apartment.  Continue on past the dairy to our little dirt road, Idaline Lane.  When we get home, the house is lit up like a Christmas tree with all the lights that were left on when the power went out.</P>'),(3254,'<p>hello everyone,</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>i am finally getting around to writing. katie and i arrived in bangkok a little over two weeks ago and headed straight for chaing mai, and then the lahu village of huay nam rin to study yoga thai massage. now we are back in chiang mai to stay for a couple of days until this weekend, when we plan to head north again to pai.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>we arrived in bangkok pretty late at night but we still managed to make it to khosan road to find a cheap place to stay the night. mmmmmm, they sell fried bugs on the street there. the next evening we boarded a flight to chaing mai which is a quick 1 hour flight. we stayed the next night in chiang mai and then met with our yoga massage instructor the next day. 8 of us piled into the back of a tiny toyota truck and drove for an hour and a half to he lahu village of huay nam rin, the location of our school.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>the village was semi primative and the people there were very kind to us. yoga thai massage school was almost a little too much for me. class and practive was 6 hours a day, every day, for two weeks. at severl points i was ready to chuck it all in and head back to chaing mai. but, now i am glad that i stuck through it. even though im very sore (from too many bad massage practices!) i learned a great deal about healing and energy work.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>for now i must run, its hard to squeeze in two weeks of experiences when im on the net cafe clock!. more updates will follow now that i am back in civilization.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>namaste\r\n<br>\r\nt</p>'),(3255,'<p>i went to jake\\\'s bar the other night. it was loud and crowded.</p><p>\r\ni guess there isn\\\'t much else to say about jake\\\'s. it has a shuffleboard thing inside--it\\\'s the only bar in town with one of those. it has a deck outside. girls are impressed if you can light the propane heaters. hey. small city. i\\\'ll take what i can get.</p><p>\r\nso this was saturday night, or in my current reckoning, 4 days after i got an acceptance letter to law school. some chick\\\'s birthday. i had heard from my grandfather that my childhood friend, let\\\'s call him bob, worked there. hadn\\\'t seen him in 15 years or so. now we\\\'re both 25. so i recognized bob easily--we still have our baby faces i guess. must be the water. we chat for a minute. he is pretty drunk, and is putting out that \\\'i get into trouble regularly\\\' vibe--offering information i have no need to know, like his drug habits, and so on. i mean come on. we weren\\\'t best pals or anything.</p><p>\r\nso he says, \\\'I want you to meet my friend Clay.\\\'</p><p>\r\n\\\'Clay Henry.\\\'</P><p>\r\nwell, it took a minute to sink in. i was <i>this close</i> to asking, \\\'where do i know you from?\\\' when i realized who it was. then i said, in a normal, relaxed conversational tone, \\\'oh, shit.\\\' that was my sole recognition of his noteriety.</p><p>\r\nhe thought that was funny.</p><p>so clay henry and bob and i talked about he-man, and other stuff we all were into as kids. he was a nice guy. now i\\\'ve never met anyone famous before--believe it or not, not too many famous folks hang out in columbia, sc--but i thought i handled myself pretty well. it\\\'s not like people were messing with him, and i\\\'m sure not many people recognized him. we\\\'re talking about a straight warholian 15-minute guy here. it was kind of apropos, really, being where we were; it occurred to me that henry, clay henry might be just about the least famous famous person in the world, which somehow fits right in with my life. it\\\'s unbelievable, in a mundane sort of way.</p><p>\r\nso i got to thinking about what it means to be famous, aside from the Webster\\\'s definition that is. i figure the only reason people care about meeting a person of note is so they can tell everyone that they met that person. in fact, i\\\'m pretty sure it\\\'s a strange sort of handicap, especially if it\\\'s unaccompanied by extreme wealth. still, standing there with a (sort of) famous person in the LEAST famous place in the world, after a moment i felt a rising sense of, i don\\\'t know, giddiness, just for a second. it was pretty strange. i realized what it was, and just how deeply into our subconcious the virus of fame has implanted itself, through our constant worship of it. i mean, if you are an average philistine-type person, uninterested in the higher arts, sciences, music, or whatever, what else is there to worship besides <i>being well-known?</i> isn\\\'t that what the reality shows are all about? not what is going on, really, on the show i mean, but the fact that a so-called <i>ordinary person</i> becomes famous. that\\\'s the new american dream. \r\n'),(3256,'<P>\r\nBRAVO BELGIUM!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBRAVO FRANCE!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBRAVO GERMANY!\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nwhy should NATO \\\"defend\\\" turkey? what does it even mean to \\\"defend\\\" turkey? Why is it that we love to attack countries that have the capabilities for commiting mass murder, but we do nothing when mass murder has already been committed? \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\njust last week turkey made it illegal for someone to have a kurdish name. what kind of law is that?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the wake of the NATO crisis rummy rumsfeld has had a hissy fit saying that the three nation\\\'s will be \\\"judged by other NATO countries\\\'. im sure rumsfeld just shit himself with spoiled alcoholic insider trading rage. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just dont buy into the idea that turkey is at risk if a war in iraq breaks out. if what we are hearing about the united states plans of attack are correct, i cant quite see the rational to attack turkey. isnt this new and very possible war in the gulf region a war between islam and the west? isnt saddam trying to paint this picture? why in allahs name would madman saddam attack an islamic state? Something smells rotten. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think the real reason the united states and its lapdogs desire more defensive armaments in Turkey isnt because meteorologists are reporting chemically armed scud lightning. They want to get turkey ready for any possible post saddam violence. We again swing back to the kurds. They are the jews of the holocaust. They are the palastinians of Gaza. They are the largest ethnic group in the world without a homeland. They are a realistic problem for turkey and its western style secular government. turkey fears a possible kurd uprising brought about by a new and more ethnically diverse coalition in a post saddam regime. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nkeep marching. keep mailing. keep asking questions. keep reading. keep looking. keep an eye out for something strange. we are at a heightened state of alert people! dont put your back to the government trying to figure out what \\\'they\\\' are going to do next. figure out what we\\\'re going to do next. im out. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3257,'<p>My fellow Swinnitesâ€¦ \r\n</p><p>\r\nThis past weekâ€¦I was proud to stand right after an evening of burgers and beer at the dog and duck before a joint session of a good friend with a confident message that the state of our site is strong. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAs a site, we continue to see successes in the classroomâ€¦at work... in economic development â€¦and our push to provide adequate prose for all Swinnites. \r\n</p><p>\r\nBut it\\\'s not the state of our stite that concerns me. \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt\\\'s the state of my debt. \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe fiscal challenges I now face in Austin cannot be blamed on Swinnite families.\r\n</p><p> \r\nNo one can say Swinnites are not working hard enoughâ€¦or paying enough for liquir and beer. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSince 2000, our population has grown by 27 percentâ€¦and flame has increased by 37 percent. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd what has Editorial complaints grown by in the same period? \r\n</p><p>\r\n125 percent. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSo as site editors looks to tackle the fiscal challenges of the future, we must begin by selling crack and un-limited pornography. \r\nWhen ends don\\\'t meet, Swinnite families prioritize. Why shouldn\\\'t editors? \r\n</p><p>\r\nWe\\\'ve already begun to take responsible measures. Because of the dedicated Swinnites bleading our bandwidth, we are reducing current year spending by 700 percent.\r\n</p><p> \r\nMy own office has achieved a 1 percent reduction in its food budget. \r\n</p><p>\r\nIn addition, during my State of the Site address I identified more than $9 billion in potential earnings to show we can balance our site budget without implimenting banner adds.\r\n</p><p> \r\nStarting from three, every dollar spent by the editors must be doubled. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI know that Swinnites don\\\'t wantâ€¦don\\\'t needâ€¦and don\\\'t deserve less stuff. \r\n</p><p>\r\nSlowdowns in the community usually are temporary. Death is not.\r\n</p><p> \r\nThe Swinney.org of a new century must be built on the legacy of centuries past. Lets go print! \r\n</p><p>\r\nSwinney\\\'s have always been best friendly, nice and supportive, constructive, destabilizing, angry detractors, pioneering and unrestrained in vision - focused on the far horizon. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAs we move forward, let us remember at all times the fundamental principle that guides our actions:\r\n</p><p> \r\nEditors should not limit free speech. Free speech should limit Editors. \r\n</p><p>'),(3258,'<p>a girl and a boy pull up at the point and stop the car. they jump out and slam doors, throwing towels over their shoulders. the car is locked and they run down to the path quickly, the ashphalt burning their feet. scamper scamper down the cracked dirt path with grass edging out into it. reach the bottom, down the chicken-wire covered steps and onto the shelly sand. towels are laid out, keys are dumped upon them and the pair challenge each other as they run out onto the rockbed. the girl slows more than the boy as she is less confident about the small snags of the rocks. reaching the edge she swings her arms and dives, down down down into the gentle lapping of the water.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>surfacing she lies on her back, stares up at the trees on the point. thoughts are fairly mundane.  all she can hear is the lapping of the waves and her brother\\\'s voice calling to her. with a snap she folds up and treads water as they discuss whether to swim out further or dive again. the latter is chosen and they swim strongly towards the rocks. the girl misjudges the strength needed to pull herself out and she slithers back into the water.</P>\r\n\r\n<p><i>it\\\'s a summer afternoon, and three teenagers run down the path, down the steps and onto the beach. stripping off school uniforms to reveal the togs underneath we run out across the rockbed and dive in. the wind is high but its a warm wind, welcomed on this scorching day as we swim up and through the waves, calling to and laughing with each other. we swim out past the first buoy and turn somersaults, dolphin dive, dunk each other. no one notices as the sun becomes hidden and the water reacts more with the increasing wind.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>soon we decide to go back in, perhaps dive off the rocks a few more times before drying off and getting ice-creams on the way home. its possible to swim straight to the beach and walk up the steep bank out of the water to our towels but we choose climbing out onto the rocks for another dive. </i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>two of us are siblings, and we are strong swimmers, having been in the water since we were very young in the tropics. our friend is not so strong but it was not deemed to be a worry in this (at first) calm water. the waves make it very difficult to exit. each time one of us manages to lift our torso above the water line we fall back into the waves which then push us against the rockbed. this happens repeatedly and we tire quickly. swimming back out or around is not an option because of the way the wind is picking up.  the waves push our bodies against the rocks. </i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>finally i manage to clamber out, and exhausted, help the others out. all of us are scraped up, panting.  the jovial tone of the afternoon has disappeared to silence.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>we walk gingerly across the rockbed. i look back out to the water, so quickly changed from the gentle lapping of an hour before. the sky has dulled and the air is different. we pick up towels and dry off. its a slow walk back up the path to the car and we brush the sand off their feet quickly.  there is no mention of ice-cream on the trip home.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>shaking her head, the girl pulls herself from the water.  she stands at the edge of the rockbed with her toes curled over the ridges and holes in the surface as she waits for her brother to join her. they count down and dive, hands arms torso body into the water. the cool clear water welcomes, allows the forms to pass through it effortlessly. they stroke underwater and come up abruptly several metres from the shore. the girl looks to her brother, thinking again of that afternoon so many years ago. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>she flips backwards in the water, her body forming a long arc. the only sound in her head is the water rushing past her ears and all there is in this moment is the sunlight filtering through the waves. here, underneath, her body feels weightless, graceful. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>she slowly swims towards the shore, avoiding a final exit via the rocks. she doesn\\\'t want to remember how quickly this perfection could shift.</p>'),(3259,'what if one of those nights where you sit with no lights watching the storm. <br>\r\nwhat if one of those nights i walked up. <br>\r\nout of the rain. <br>\r\nif i stood in the shadows between the streetlight and the dark. <br>\r\nwet with wind. <br>\r\nwould you know it was me. <br>\r\nwould you want me. <br>\r\ncould i come to you silent and still. <br>\r\nghost that i am. <br>\r\nwould you let me take you. <br>\r\ncould you stand not to say anything.<br> \r\ncould you stop me. <br>\r\ncold wetness everywhere. <br>\r\nour heat the only thing between us. <br>\r\nno whispers. <br>\r\nno talking. <br>\r\njust moans. <br>\r\nshort gasps. <br>\r\nlost breath. <br>\r\nand me riding you. <br>\r\nholding you down. <br>\r\nbecause one of these nights i might come to you. <br>\r\ncovered in rain. <br>\r\ndripping in heat. <br>\r\nbecause i hear your breath. <br>\r\nand it calls me. <br>\r\nbecause if only for one night of lightning and rain. <br>\r\ni want to own you. <br>\r\nbe one with you. <br>\r\nleave you helpless. <br>\r\nshivering. <br>\r\ninside of me.<br>'),(3260,'<p>I went to the protest in San Francisco and noticed a few things:</p>\r\n\r\n<b>let stupid talk.</b><br> Itâ€™s incredible that they spent some thirty thousand dollars for the stage and sound system to let these people talk. One woman was talking about being an artist evicted from her apartment and quickly mentioned George Bush, implying that his shadow government is in cahoots with her landlord. Then she discussed the rape of women. Then the struggle of South Africa.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>no message, off message.</b><br> Most speakers had their own, unrelated pet causes that they just couldnâ€™t leave at home. Around the plaza were dozens of booths and stands with countless left-related causes. One had a giant banner: Stop Mumiaâ€™s Execution! As many of you know, the Pennsylvania court has already removed his death sentence.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>many people looked bored.</b>  <br>A smaller group of people went to Union Square to face off with police and protest war by breaking stuff. They were bored. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>baptism of the converted.</b><br> People were bored because they already agreed with everything that was being said. The reason for a rally is to have the rest of the country notice the march on TV, yet inside the actual event they feel the need to â€œeducateâ€ people. They need to figure out a way to entertain people or figure out a way to make attendance useful. Maybe with voter registration or a giant knit-in.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>anti-Bush.</b><br>  Calling him â€œshrubâ€ is cute and funny, but many of the protestors had signs comparing Bush with Hitler. Similar to the people that  resorted to vandalism and violence in Union Square, these anti-Bush protestors threaten the legitimacy of the entire event. Are we really to believe that everything thatâ€™s wrong with this country is related to George Bush?<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>what is the answer?</b><br> There were no answers. Do we do nothing? Do we â€œback downâ€? The problem in Iraq remains. The fact is: the leadership there only responds to a threat of violence. The only solution offered at the rally was fix the Palestine issue and then everything else will sort itself out. They say with a Palestinian homeland there will be no more terrorists and the countries in the Middle East will be our friends. This is the racist, condescending liberal worldview that clumps every Arab individual with the Palestinians. It understands little and solves nothing. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>against the war.</b><br>\r\nI would be for an invasion of Iraq if circumstances were different. This administration is run by the <a href=\\\"http://www.tnr.com/doc.mhtml?i=20030224&s=trb022403\\\">same people</a> who armed Saddam and <a href=\\\"http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/gulf/oral/powell/4.html\\\">the people</a> who ended the first Gulf War too soon. Yes, this is Monday morning quarterbacking, but history is a bitch like that. \r\n\r\n<br><br>War is too dangerous, costly and delicate to allow people with a poor track record to lead it. The administrationâ€™s pitch for the war has been full of inaccuracies. The discourse from intelligent people like Powell and Rice sometimes seems aimed at school children rather than adults. They mix the real need to assert cold, rational power in the region with moralistic rhetoric. And they are awful at diplomacy. We should not let an administration that canâ€™t manage our own allies remove the Iraqi government, insert their own and try to assimilate it with the Middle East. \r\n<br><br>\r\nThe administration has sold the war to the public with a lack of consequences. Itâ€™s a gamble to stabilize the region with democracy. What will happen if it throws the region into turmoil? Will success lower gas prices? Will it improve the lagging economy? It will cost many billions of dollars. Will we recoup the money with the oil reserves in Iraq? What will this cost us? What world will we inherit?\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>against the broken left.</b>\r\n<br>\r\nThe left is just as guilty as the right. Both sides have erased the notions of sacrifice and personal involvement in this large economic and political situation. Consequences are never mentioned. The right takes no responsibility for cleaning up the mess a war would make, while the left offers no alternative to sorting out the Middle East. We donâ€™t need to do anything about the region except let love rule. Nobody at the rally suggested a change in our consumer behavior. The popular â€œno blood for oil,â€ slogan was invented during the last Gulf War, yet how many of these protestors have stopped driving in the past decade? \r\n<br><br>\r\nThis war is legitimate. There was no â€œrush to war.â€ Our president went through the proper channels of the United Nations. Our legislative bodies approved action. Democracy works. At least half of the people at protest like the one in San Francisco donâ€™t vote, yet all the blame is on George Bush. The left has to realize that we live in a functioning system and we can only blame ourselves. Nobody is brave enough to say to the crowd, â€œMany of you here today are hypocrites. Start voting and stop driving.â€\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe rally removes the responsibility of the individual. Thereâ€™s no accountability in a crowd. Nobody will get up on that stage and confess that: Just as Bush points to Saddam as the â€œevilâ€ in the world, we use Bush as our scapegoat. We say that evil oilmen are doing this; we say itâ€™s the big government meanies who stole the presidency. But no. We citizens are at fault. We are lazy. We donâ€™t want to change our lifestyles. We donâ€™t want to vote. And when we do vote, we vote like we are shopping for shoes, expecting to get exactly the pair we want. We are picky consumers who are not satisfied with the inherent compromise of democracy, so we create irrational theories to justify our failures as citizens.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI actually want a different administration in office. I actually want peace in the Middle East. And so I can not align myself with the left. It is a broken â€˜Beverly Hillbilliesâ€™ jalopy of cobbled together causes, spewing caustic, hypocritical lead fumes that will ensure a Bush presidency until 2008. I will not return to a war protest until the rhetoric is honest and responsible. And then I will carry a sign that quotes Mick Jagger: After all, it was you and me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3261,'<p>\r\nI saw a Black man walking southbound in the northbound lane of Halsted, near the strip club at Randolph.  He was somewhere between the ages of 50 and 100.  He had on a black suit; on top of that suit, he wore a black overcoat; he had a shiny, wide-brimmed, black leather hat on his head; he was dragging a black suitcase with one hand; he was carrying a black clothing bag in his other hand.  I caught the light; he hailed me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe trunk of my car would only open with a key; so, I threw it in park, and hopped out.  I helped him put his luggage into the vehicle, and then held the back door open for him.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe light turned green; no one honked: we looked too scary.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHe appraised the thick, black frames of my sunglasses, and the thick, black sideburns on my face.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Are you related to the Blues Brothers?\\\" he asked.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI chuckled: I knew that I had caught a live one; it was going to be a good day.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhen we settled in the car, I asked where I might take him.  He told me that he wanted to go to the, \\\"Chinese cleaners.\\\"  I needed a bit more to go on than that; but the best that he could do was to offer, \\\"South,\\\" on Halsted.  And so off we went.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHe had a lot to say; he was sauced.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Boy,\\\" said he.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Yes sir?\\\" said I.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Philly blunts and Black pussy make a nigger crazy - Hahahahahahaha!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI nodded, and smiled.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Boy?\\\"  said he.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Yes sir?\\\" said I.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"He said he was messin\\\' with a player  - I said: \\\'You got a whole jukebox on your head!\\\' - Hahahahahahaha!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI nodded, and smiled.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nA long story about the importance of the jukebox in a certain syndicate figure\\\'s rise to power followed.  Crime in the city is all too real to me, and I made a deliberate effort to stop listening too closely at that point.  It was a nice ride: we talked about Downbeat, the Mob, and everything in between; but it was time for it to be over.  When I dropped him off, I couldn\\\'t help but notice that the people who ran the shop were Korean.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI turned around, and headed back up Halsted.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nFares are often difficult to come by: post 9/11 the last thing that most people want to do is take an unnecessary trip, by plane, to a major American city, to attend a large public event.  Hotels, convention centers, theaters, and all such related enterprises in town have been hurting; and when they hurt, the waitresses, bartenders, and taxi drivers hurt.  The new color-coded terrorism warning system hasn\\\'t helped things, at all.  To make matters worse, in January it\\\'s cold here - it\\\'s painfully cold here.  And everyone is crying about his or her debt after the holidays.  Having said that, what I saw next shocked me.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThere was a guy standing on Halsted, at Madison.  He was waiting for a taxi.  I didn\\\'t think that I stood a chance of picking him up: there were two other taxis closer to him.  But, the drivers of those vehicles pulled up near him, waved at him, and then accelerated away from him.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI stopped; he tentatively approached my vehicle; his expression was downcast.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Good afternoon, sir.  Where may I take you?\\\" said I.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHe thanked me profusely for picking him up.  He wanted to take a short trip downtown; and so off we went.  As we drove, he asked if I had seen what happened; I indicated that I had.  We both knew what had happened, and why.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAfter I dropped him off, I headed back out of the Loop to Halsted; it had been good to me, so far.  I was pretty far north before I caught the next fare: a Black woman in her 40\\\'s.  When she got in, I asked her if the radio bothered her; I ask everyone that question.  \\\"Oh no, Baby,\\\" she said, \\\"That\\\'s Al Green: What could possibly be wrong?\\\"  When she was at her destination, she gave me a 100% tip.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI doubled back: south.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMy first White passenger of the day got in the vehicle.  He had an envelope in his hands; he gave it to me.  On the face of the envelope were written the words, \\\"Cab Driver Only.\\\"  Inside the envelope laid the address of the destination that I was to take the passenger to.  I held the paper to my forehead, and prayed aloud for, \\\"Wisconsin.\\\"  It turned out to be a place on Clark Street.  His wife had prepared a surprise party for him; I was to drive him to the restaurant.  I played along with the game, and laughed and joked with the gentleman in the backseat.  I got to keep the card.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI was hungry as Hell, and drove up Broadway with the intention of getting Thai food.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI caught a light - near Montrose, I think - and noticed that a highly agitated White guy with a beard was motioning at me.  He was in a hurry to get something; it didn\\\'t look good.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHe climbed in the back, and explained that he needed to make several stops; it looked worse. \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWhen in doubt, I speak to the passenger more - not less.  I engaged him.  It turned out that he had been a writer for the New York Times, and in that capacity, he had spent quite a bit of time in Sarajevo.  His mission on this particular evening was to pick up ten pounds of Bosnian sausage for some event that he was attending; he was anxious about the food, and transporting it.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe hit the restaurant - no problems.  But then, as he sat in the backseat with his huge, steaming pan of sausage, he decided that he needed a particular type of plum brandy to compliment it.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nWe spent the next half hour driving around the north side of the city, hunting for the elusive liquor.  Finally we found it, and then headed out to LSD, towards our ultimate destination.  When we got there, he had $30 on the meter; he gave me $40.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI still hadn\\\'t eaten.  So I parked the car on Halsted - hoping that it wouldn\\\'t be ticketed or towed - and walked into a place in Greek town.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAfter dinner, I walked back to the car.  I saw a guy standing on the corner of Halsted and Madison, near my car; he was trying to get a taxi.  He looked a lot like the gentleman that I had seen on that same corner, earlier in the day; and he was having the same experience that that other gentleman had: drivers slowed down, looked at him, and left.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI asked if he needed a car; he said yes.  As I unlocked the vehicle, a Black lady in her 60\\\'s or 70\\\'s walked into sight; she was the one who needed the taxi; he was only helping her.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI opened the door for her, and she settled into the back.  It turned out that she had just moved to Chicago, from Washington D.C.  We talked.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"Our city,\\\" I said, \\\"is still a very racist city.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAnd so it is.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nShe called me, \\\"Honey.\\\"  And when she exited the vehicle, not only did she give me a tip, but she gave me a yellow rose, as well.  And so I had a flower now, in addition to my card.  Valentine\\\'s Day was looking better and better.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI headed west on North Avenue.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI picked up two White women in their 20\\\'s.  I could tell by the way that they spoke that they were from the South; it turned out to be New Orleans.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nPeople are funny: they expect their driver - and everyone who serves them - to know the exact manner in which they desire to be served.  But, what offends one person is pleasing to another.  Example: I open the door for everyone who gets in, or out of, the vehicle that I drive - if it is at all possible to do so.  Roughly 1/4 of the female passengers find that action to be objectionable - presumably upon the grounds that it threatens their equality, or independence; roughly 1/4 of the male passengers, likewise, receive that action poorly - presumably upon grounds that it threatens their masculinity.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nHey, jerks: I\\\'m not the Amazing Blackstone.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe ladies from New Orleans waited for me to walk around and open the door for them, when they exited the vehicle.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAs they did that, the doorman at their building waived to me: he wanted me to wait for someone else.  I did.  It was an old, Black couple, going home after having worked in the building, all day.  Their home was in the heart of the west side, near Homan and Kinzie; it was around midnight.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe wife turned out to have a bad back; she was in pain.  When I pulled in front of their home, I got out, and opened the door for her.  I gave her my hand, and helped her to stand.  She held my hand, warmly, and said, \\\"Baby, can you find your way back ok?\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI headed back ok, under the Lake Street El; I stopped to take pictures.  I\\\'m always more comfortable there than I should be.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIt was getting late; and after midnight, almost everyone is drunk.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI started working Lincoln Park.  I wasn\\\'t surprised when I picked up a White woman in her 20\\\'s, and she seemed a bit tipsy.  She sat in the back, on the edge of the seat: her eyes were closed; her mouth was hanging open.  Her head was near mine.  She moaned a bit.  I didn\\\'t think too much about it - until the next passenger got in.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe next passenger was very much like the first: a tipsy, White, female in her 20\\\'s.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBut, then she threw something at me: \\\"Here,\\\" she said, \\\"You left these in the back.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI looked at the projectile: a blue, printed, Victoria\\\'s Secret thong.  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSo that\\\'s what the other one was doingâ€¦\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIt was after 4 in the morning.  I turned in the car, after having driven for about 20 hours.  The guy at the window called me, \\\"My Man!\\\" and I smiled.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI made it back to one of my temporary homes, and sat down in a chair.  The card, rose, and thong, hung out of my bag on the floor next to me.  I fell sound asleep - within seconds.  And I woke, suddenly, two hours later, to an unusual crunching sound: my cat was almost finished eating the head of the rose.  Happy Valentine\\\'s Day, Baby.\r\n</p>'),(3262,'<p>\r\nIt\\\'s that look of fire in her perfect chocolate brown eyes that sets your soul ablaze in a way that makes you want her every minute of every hour of every single day. It\\\'s the little things she does that show she cares. Those tiny, perfect, little bundled up yarn balls of affection, and it\\\'s the hundred watt luminesence of her perfect smile as she laughs at your dumbest jokes.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s just the way she is. Every move fills you with lust and adoration in equal measure tieing the strings of your heart around her perfectly manicured little finger, commanding affections at will.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nShe\\\'s electric. Touch her and you might get burned, spurned and spat out like last night\\\'s day-old tuna melt.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nOr you might just seize every last thread of happiness you\\\'ve ever dreamed of.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3263,'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetuer adipiscing elit. Ut vulputate dolor sit amet erat. Aliquam eu nunc et lorem dignissim convallis. Donec in quam eu augue volutpat tincidunt. Aliquam non velit. Nullam nisl. Pellentesque fringilla feugiat massa. Nulla wisi tortor, mollis sit amet, ullamcorper eget, egestas non, sem. Aliquam erat volutpat. Aliquam lobortis, erat vitae sagittis accumsan, lorem felis posuere mi, non nonummy ipsum magna fermentum leo. Ut eu diam ut lacus suscipit fringilla. Etiam tempus consequat lorem. Duis accumsan, nunc ut ornare blandit, risus lorem aliquam massa, ut cursus sem ante accumsan mi. Nunc nisl sem, posuere nec, viverra sed, volutpat at, magna. Cras iaculis nibh et urna. Nullam sem sem, eleifend in, imperdiet sit amet, feugiat vel, odio. In nonummy eleifend sapien. Curabitur ac odio id tortor lobortis semper. Suspendisse convallis, enim quis consequat malesuada, lorem nibh facilisis erat, in iaculis justo est id mi. Vestibulum vitae dolor. Vestibulum non sapien a erat facilisis ullamcorper.'),(3264,'<p>\r\nThere has been a lot of news regarding the North Korean \\\"Crisis\\\" as of late. The media here in South Korea doesn\\\'t seem all that frightened. I don\\\'t get a sense of anxiety from my students, and I have seen nothing in the way of civil defence activities being held as they had been in the \\\"Crisis\\\" of 1994; a negotiated settlement was agreed upon by Pyongyang with the help of Jimmy Carter. (Carter broke a 1976 campaign promise to yank American troops from South Korea. In 1980 Carter gave South Korea permission to quell a pro-democracy movement. 1000 people died in the city of Kwangjew.) \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat many people need to understand about Korea that im not sure most people know is that Korea did not become two states because of the Korean War. The only thing that was decided upon by that war was what we consider the borders of each nation as they exist today. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen the United States crushed Japan and ended the Second world war in the Pacific, Korea, which had been a Japanese colony before and during the war was occupied by the U.S in the South and the USSR in the North.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe partition was supposed to be temporary, but the United States government decided to put the South under direct military control and \\\'created\\\' the \\\"Republic of Korea\\\", and set up a government of Koreans to rule only in the South.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith this, the North was put under the control of the Korean Communist party. Having faught with the Chinese during their revolution in Manchuria, the Northern forces were gaining valuable combat experience.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter the Chinese communists seized power in China, the North Koreans launched an attack on the South. During the war, U.S Bombers bombarded the North, bringing famine and disease to the North by attacking irrigation systems, dikes. The flooding that ensued rendered half of North Korea\\\'s agricultural land.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen the armistice agreement was signed in 1953, with the Korea\\\'s still divided. The United States stationed tens of thousands of military personnal on the border. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter the armistace was signed the North focused on infastructure and civilian reconstruction. They achieved an economic growth rate that the South could not compete with well into the 1960\\\'s. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFearing a possible \\\"socialist utopia\\\", the United States began a massive arms buildup in the South. In response the North retooled its economy for war. They became so economically directed towards military projects, that military arms became the countries only lucrative export. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn 1958, the United States and Dwight D Eisenhower introduced nuclear tipped tactical missiles into the South Korean arsenal. This was in violation of the 1953 armistice agreement as the agreement prohibited both sides from introducing qualitatively new weapons systems. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTo make matters worse, the missiles were placed close to the border with the North. Because of their proximity to the North, the United States was then able to adopt a \\\"use it or lose it\\\" stance in Korea. In the event of an attack, the United States would immediately launch a nuclear attack so as not to allow for the capture of the missiles in the event that the Northern forces over ran them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is the same stance that the United States has today in the Peninsula. The North\\\'s Nuclear program interferes with the long term \\\"first strike\\\" dominance of the United States. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter the 1991 gulf war Bush Sr. reported that he would remove most if not all of the South\\\'s oldschool tactical nuclear weapons. The United States had enough nuclear weapons stationed under the waters in and around japan, korea, and taiwan, that any arsenal in South Korea was redundant. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn 1993, the United States created a new threat by reporting that missiles formerly targeted at the USSR would be re-targeted to sites in China and North Korea. In response North Korea withdrew from the non-proliferation agreement and made it clear that its nuclear electric plants would be used in the production of weapons materials. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSince the crisis in 1993, tension eased and the South voted in Kim Dae Jung. The \\\"sunshine policy\\\" was created, and further inroads were being made between the two sides. Hyundai corporation admitted to paying 400million(US) to the North for constuction rights and permission to build a resort area on one of Korea\\\'s most beautiful mountains. (Koreans love hiking)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the year I have been here more and more reunions have gone on between family members divided by the partition. My students seem very positive that the two Korea\\\'s would unite. There were even North Korean athletes in the ASIATIC games held in Busan, South Korea\\\'s second largest city.(The North Korean atheletes lived on a boat)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe only ones wary of unification are those in Washington who fear a united Korea. As China becomes more integrated into the world economy communist hardliners will gradually be replaced by more moderate elements of the government. This has already been happening. As Taiwan becomes closer to China, North Korea closer to the South, the fear is that the East Asian economies will become integrated without the need for United States \\\"Help\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe fear that Korea and Taiwan will fall under the influence of China or Japan scares many in Washington, so instead of trying to help the situation the Bush administration has decided to turn up the heat. By playing up the threat in the North, the United States is trying the delay unification. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3265,'<i>It\\\'s just like the old hippie said:<br>\r\nWho needs philosophy, when you\\\'ve got the Grateful Dead?</i><p>\r\nIn the shadow of the Tall Building,<br>\r\nWindblown trash dances around the young man--<br>\r\ndetritus of the ebb and flow on the hour.<br><br>\r\nA line of shadow,<br>\r\n(Or of light, depending on your point of view)<br>\r\nFalls across the center of the plaza<br>\r\nCreating two.<br>\r\nAt identical tables sit opposing camps--<br>\r\nNeither claims the better view.<br><br>\r\n[Paper swirls, the line moves on across the bricks.]<br><br>\r\nSees a tree, bisected--<br>\r\nOne half Remains in Light;<br>\r\nAnd soon the moment passes.<br>\r\nThe whole anticipating<br>\r\nThe coming of the night.<br><br>\r\nAnd looking up,<br>\r\nhe sees, far away from shadow\\\'s advance<br>\r\nin open field, a girl surrounded,<br>\r\nholding court upon the grass. <br><br>\r\nAnd, after a while---<br>\r\nAfter a short while--<br>\r\nShadow consumes the plaza, and leaves<br>\r\nthe young man unsegregated.<br><br>\r\n[By the ringing of the bells, the hour is stated]<br><br>\r\nThe tide once more ebbs and flows<br>\r\nAnd into the building he goes.</p> '),(3266,'<p>\r\nThatâ€™s right, Mr. Polonius,<br>\r\nyou go on dwelling <br>\r\nin your yellow etherised kittens<br>\r\nand dusty oyster shells\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile the beautiful <br>\r\nand slightly fuzzy women <br>\r\nchat away about Ecclesiastes<br>\r\nand coffee spoons in tea <br>\r\nand things you <br>\r\nwouldnâ€™t understand<br>\r\nyou Michelangelo illiterate fool<br>\r\nsoaked in formaldehyde and regret\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou are a rat.<br>\r\nYou are bald.<br>\r\nYou are a coward. <br>\r\nYou are not Lazarus.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDo you dare disturb your universe?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNo?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTough shit.\r\n</p>'),(3267,'<b>Itâ€™s Always the Little Ones What You Got to Watch Out For</b>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen the screaming began, it seemed to come from everywhere at once. Echoes fluttered down from the gabled rooftops above Moor St, mingling with the falling snow until they were no stronger than the whispers of ghosts. Constable William Dibble wheeled around frantically, trying figure which one to follow. A prostitute had been found dead on Lavender Hill the week before, missing several internal organs and a patch of skin from her back. Two more women had been murdered the week before that. Dibble had the sick feeling he was listening to another in progress, one he was helpless to stop.\r\n<p>\r\nA final, ragged wail waxed steam-whistle loud and then sliced off neatly, as if under a skilled surgeonâ€™s blade. The ensuing silence was more terrible than the noise, but Dibble had fixed a direction--the screams had come from the west, from Clapham Common. He set off in a run and his shadow lumbered beside him across the fronts of shuttered shops, a monstrous twin revealed by the weak glow of the street lights. <p>\r\n\r\nDibble blew furiously on his whistle, confused by the feelings that roiled in his gut. He was afraid for the girl, for sure; it was his job to protect the innocent. But part of him was glad that something had happened on his watch. If he caught the Lavender Hill killer, he wouldnâ€™t be Daffy Bill Dibble, punch-drunk simpleton who only got hired â€˜cause his wifeâ€™s uncle was the Constabulary Sergeant Major, not any more. Heâ€™d maybe get a better shift and a proper uniform greatcoat, like the other men wore, instead of the castoff black cloak that he shivered under now. Heâ€™d maybe get some respect. <p>\r\n\r\nAs he came up on Common, he heard something that sounded like the running of a clockwork toy, and under that, the secretive giggling of a child whoâ€™d snuck extra Christmas pudding.  Dibble came charging around Clapham Church and into the square, but he was too big a man to make such a tight turn, and his leather soled boots skidded on the snow slick cobblestones. He went down hard, skull thumping hollowly on the bricks. His black top hat rolled away into the gutter. <p>\r\n\r\nIt took a moment for his vision to clear. There was a girl sprawled on the sidewalk in front of the cemetery gates, not ten feet away from him. She was pretty but made up as heavily as a painted china doll. Her coat had been split in half down the center and lay on either side of her like red velvet wings. She moaned and snuffled, wiped an eye and settled again. She was <i>asleep,</i> Dibble realized. Asleep in the middle of Clapham Common on a freezing winterâ€™s night. Wincing at the pain in his head, he struggled to his feet. Then he saw what was on top of her. <p>\r\n\r\nIt was a chunk of midnight sky in the shape of a man, nearly invisible against the darkness of the cemetery behind him. A satin top hat hid his face, and his hands were braced on the girlâ€™s ample white bottom, talons digging into doughy flesh. As his narrow hips pistoned back and forth, the light of the gas lamps ran like mercury on the fine black silk of his cloak. He paused in his labors to look up at the constable, and Dibble recoiled in horror.  The man had red eyes that glowed like Chinese lanterns, the pasty white skin of a day old corpse and a nose as long as a communion candle. Blue froth dripped from his grinning mouth onto the girlâ€™s naked back. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œY-you,â€ Dibble said, but his voice was hardly more than a whisper. His truncheon in his hands, but they were shaking so violently he was afraid he might drop it. He cleared his throat and tried again. â€œYou t-there! Get off of that g-g-girl!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œYou can see me?â€ the man said. â€œBugger, bugger, </i>bugger.â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œGet off her!â€ Dibble shouted. His feet slid crazily on the icy stones. All around the common windows began to glow with ignited oil lamps. New, more welcome sounds were filling the streets. Constabulary rattles and whistles, the banging of doors and an excited hubbub of voices. <p>\r\n\r\nThe whey-faced man heard them too. He cocked his head, listening. â€œIâ€˜m not <i>finished,â€</i> he spat. â€œBugger, bugger.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œOh, y-youâ€™re finished, all r-right.â€ Dibble closed the distance in two steps, swinging his truncheon. â€œNo moreâ€”â€œ<p>\r\n\r\nBut the man was gone. Dibble tottered to avoid planting his boot on the unconscious girl and looked around wildly. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œUp here, you stuttering plod,â€ a voice hissed. â€œOh, but youâ€™re a meddling bugger.â€ The man crouched atop the spike-tipped cemetery fence, his face shining like a sickle moon. <p>\r\n\r\nThe manâ€™s jaw unhinged with an audible clank. There was a great sucking sound, like a torrent of dirty water gurgling down a giant drain, and then a jet of blue liquid erupted from his nutcracker mouth. Dibble slipped to his right, saved by his boxerâ€™s reflexes. Stray drops spattered his cloak, sizzling and glowing. It stank of rotten fruit and ether. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œOooh, youâ€™re a quick one,â€ the man said. He had an odd, wheezy voice that piped like a hand-organ.  â€œLight on your feet, you are. But can you do this?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nThe man sat down on his haunches and then sprang straight into the air, landing with a cackle beside a spire atop Clapham Church. He paused to blow the constable a kiss, fingers undulating like long white worms, and then with another impossible grasshopper spring he was over the peaked church roof and gone. Dibble was still gaping at the rooftops, snowflakes drifting into his open mouth, when the rest of the constables began to arrive. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œDibble? Dibble!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nHe turned to find Sergeant Major Boddington frowning at him, his mustache twitching in irritation. Two other constables were helping the girl up from the sidewalk, and more were still trotting up into the square from every direction, carrying lanterns and flaming torches.  She was still half-asleep, muttering complaints at being disturbed. Her mouth and nose were smeared with the blue froth. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThatâ€™s Polly Green,â€ someone said. <p>\r\n\r\nOne of the constables murmured solicitously and patted her face until she opened her eyes. She looked around at them, bleary and puzzled. When she saw Dibble, who had retrieved his hat from the gutter, she took a whooping breath and began to scream. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œJesus, Miss!â€ Constable Sweeney said. â€œBe calm, please!â€ <p>\r\n\r\n<i>â€œKeep him away from me!â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œWho? Him?â€ The Sergeant Major looked at Dibble. <p>\r\n\r\n<i>â€œThe man in the black cape!â€</i> By the last word her voice was nearly a whisper. For all the agony in her face, the policemen still had to strain to hear what she said next. â€œThe one wot spat blue spew at me. Oh God, sir, please donâ€™t let him get me.â€ She turned to bury her face in Sweeneyâ€™s chest. Rivulets of blood were freezing on the backs of her naked thighs. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œDibble,â€ the Sergeant Major said. â€œWhat is this about?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSheâ€™s m-mistaken me, sir,â€ Dibble said. â€œI s-s-saw the man that ah. Ah. At<i>tacked</i> her. He was w-wearing a c-cloak and top hat, t-t-<i>too.â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nConstable Sweeney stepped forward and thumped on Dibbleâ€™s shoulder with a thick finger. He was the only man on the force who matched Dibbleâ€™s size. â€œRight, whatâ€™s that, then? Porridge?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble looked down. Globules of the black manâ€™s odd spit still clung to the wool of his cloak. â€œHeâ€¦ he h-hocked at me too. I s-sawâ€”â€œ Dibble stopped, blushing. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSaw <i>what,</i> Constable?â€ The Sergeant Major cocked an eyebrow. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSir, w-when I t-tried to ah. Ah. Appre<i>hend</i> him, sir, heâ€”he j-jumped. He jumped up on t-top of the f-fence, and then to the r-roof of the ch-ch-<i>church.â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nThe Sergeant Major shook his head and rubbed his eyes, then slowly drew his palm down across his face. â€œTake the girl to St Georgeâ€™s Hospital,â€ he told two of his men. â€œGet her examined by a physician, and then see if you can get a decent description of the assailant from her.â€  <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œWeâ€™re wasting time here, sir,â€ Sweeney said, crossing his arms and glaring at Dibble. â€œThis idiot ainâ€™t fit for proper police work.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI k-know what I s-saw,â€ Dibble said. â€œIt w-werenâ€™t n-n-no man. I think it was the Springheel Jack.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nThere was a second of shocked silence as all the constables and assorted lookers-on goggled at him. Then they exploded in laughter, the flickering light of the torches etching their faces into cruel masks. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œYou mean the man what jumps through nursery windows to steal infants from their cradles?â€ Sweeney asked, loud enough for the whole crowd to hear. There were dutiful titters. â€œNow I <i>know</i> Billyâ€™s taken too many blows to the head. First leprechauns, and now this!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œPolly Green is a sweet young thing, for sure, but sheâ€™s no babe,â€ one of the constables said. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThem crimes happened when my grandma was just a child herself,â€ another added. â€œHer baby sister got stole.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œJesus Christ on His Cross, Dibble.â€ The Sergeant Major rolled his eyes in weary disbelief as he tamped tobacco into his pipe. â€œDonâ€™t make your reputation any worse.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œJust say you couldnâ€™t catch the scoundrel, Constable,â€ Sweeney said. A sardonic grin twisted his mouth. â€œAssuming you actually saw one.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibbleâ€™s cheeks burned like heâ€™d been slapped. â€œY-youâ€™re b-bloody right Iâ€”â€œ<p>\r\n\r\nThe Sergeant Major held up a silencing hand. â€œNot one more word.â€ He puffed the pipe bowl into bright life and blew out a stream of smoke, shaking his head in disgust. â€œYouâ€™ll give me a full statement at the station, and try not to mention faeries or spirits, all right?â€ <p>\r\n\r\n			<center>		II.</center> <p>\r\n\r\nWhen Dibble entered the office and closed the door behind him, the Sergeant Major was standing at his window, watching the rising sun limn the clouds with gold. Shafts of morning light lit trophies won in the defense of Her Majestyâ€™s Empireâ€”crossed spears, pagan idols, the snarling head of a huge boarâ€”decorated the walls. A fire was dying in the hearth, and the room smelled of sweet pipe smoke. Dibble stood at attention in front of his desk, and the precinctâ€™s commanding officer began to speak without turning from the glass. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œIn India,â€ he said, puffing an ivory pipe carved from the tip of an elephantâ€™s tusk, â€œhorror doesnâ€™t hide in the dark. One can walk down the street and see beggars smeared with blood and the ashes of the dead, and children whose limbs have fallen off from leprosy, who writhe like snakes from place to place. The Hindus float their dead on their sacred river, you know. Right through the heart of their capital city. Can you imagine if we tossed every corpse London produces into the Thames?â€ He shuddered and tapped the windowpane with his pipe stem. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThese killingsâ€¦ Thereâ€™s no rhyme or reason that I can see, no pattern in the murdererâ€™s choices. Women of all ages, from all walks of life. Taken in the street. In the taverns. God, at home in their own kitchens! Mutilated, and not a drop of blood left in them. It doesnâ€™t make any sense. Murder is one thing, but this is madness.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble worried the brim of his hat in his big hands.  â€œI-Iâ€™m not sure I f-follow, sir.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThe last thing we need is for the public to think thereâ€™s a spook on the loose, something the Constabulary canâ€™t take care of. These killings, these works of depravity, theyâ€™re sparks. It is the job of the Constabulary to smother them. If we do not, if they catch, madness will sweep through a city like a fire and reduce two thousand years of Christian civilization to ruin in a matter of days. Horror could run free here, as it does among the heathens.â€ He turned to face his junior officer, his eyes reflecting the fireâ€™s embers. â€œGo out and do your job, Dibble. But donâ€™t mention this Springheel Jack again.â€ <p>\r\n\r\n<center>III. </center><p>\r\n\r\n	â€œDaddy?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nNo matter how quietly he crept up the creaking stairs of their row house, Dibbleâ€™s daughter Sarah was always awake to greet him when he got home. He stepped to her bedside, a hushing finger to his lips. Sarah yawned and rolled over on her back, smiling up at him. Her blond curls were spread on the pillow like a bright halo. Beside her, her mother Harriet snored contently, not stirring. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI dreamed about Punch,â€ his daughter said. â€œHeâ€™s a wicked one, ainâ€™t he, Daddy?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble had taken Sarah to her first Punch and Judy show two Saturdays past. She had sat in the front row with the other young children, covering her face in fright, squealing with laughter, lustily booing the hooligan Punch as he committed mayhem all across the tiny cabinet stage. Sheâ€™d never taken her eyes from the stage, and Dibble had stood in the back of the crowd and never taken his eyes off her. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThat he is, my dear. But what did the Crocodile give him for his troubles?â€ He never stuttered when he spoke with his daughter. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œA big chomp on the nose!â€ she crowed, then stifled her giggles, remembering her mother slumbering beside her. She neednâ€™t have worried, Dibble thought ruefully. It took more than that to wake his wife. It took someone like him. Harriet refused to share a bed with him anymore; she claimed he boxed in his sleep. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œYouâ€™re a right menace, Bill,â€ she told him as she carried her bedclothes into their daughterâ€™s room. â€œWhat with that punching and raving all night long. Why, the one time I tried to wake you, you near knocked out the few teeth I got left!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble was lonely without her, but there was nothing he could do. Night after night, he relived his battle with Irish Danny Oâ€™Dell, who had challenged him for the Championship of England. Their fight had drawn crowds from far and wide, from every social classâ€”it was rumored that the Queen Herself might make an appearance, in disguise. <p>\r\n\r\nDibble had been the odds-on favorite, but the bandy-legged boy had come at him like a fierce storm, bringing a hard rain of fists slamming down on Dibbleâ€™s head until finally, late in the sixty-eighth round, the crowd saw Danny Oâ€™Dell put him down with a fearsome right to the chin. His head rang like a soundless bell, and Dibble was barely able to climb back up from the canvas. When his vision cleared, he saw a dozen hand-high men with red beards and dragonfly wings moving the boyâ€™s feet and pumping his fists. Rage made the world turn red. No wonder he couldnâ€™t knock Oâ€™Dell outâ€”faeries were doing his fighting for him! <p>\r\n\r\nHe staggered back out to the center of the ring, throwing wild punches at the cheeky, grinning little men who buzzed round his head while Danny Oâ€™Dell stood bemused and the crowd laughed and jeered. Finally his corner man threw the white towel into the ring. It took four men to drag him back to his stool, where Dibble, out of his mind with exhausted fury, kept shouting that Oâ€™Dell was a only a straw man, and cursed his real opponents in a bellow that caught the ears of the newspapermen in the audience. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI GOT BEAT UP BY A FAIRY!â€ one headline said the next day. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œFIGHTER SAYS HE WANTS R-REMATCH WITH L-L-LEPRECHAUNS,â€ said another. <p>\r\n\r\nOâ€™Dell had given him the worst beating of his life, and afterwards, as if the papers hadnâ€™t humiliated him enough, Dibble was horrified to find his life-long stutter had grown even worse. <p>\r\n\r\nBut it wasnâ€™t Danny Oâ€™Dell and his faerie pugilists that came to visit Dibble during that morningâ€™s slumber. In his dreams he returned to walking his beat around Clapham Center. But he was just a boy, maybe ten years old, dressed in his grown manâ€™s uniform. His pants puddled over the tops of his boots, and his sleeves nearly dragged on the ground. He had to tilt his head back to see out from under the top hat. <p>\r\n\r\nIt was bright mid-morning. Bill smelled horse leavings and sweet hay, but there were no carriages. No one was minding the vegetable carts, or hawking flowers at the curb. He heard quick footsteps, and a titter of high-pitched laughter. Billy spun round, one hand holding his top hat in place, and watched the empty street for a long moment, trying to force his senses to sharpen. There was no one there. Billy sighed in relief. Then he turned to resume his route and almost lost an eye on the tip of Springheel Jackâ€™s long pointed nose. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThere you are, you little<i>bugger,â€</i> he said, and grabbed for Billyâ€™s throat.<p>\r\nBilly fell on his ass and scrabbled for escape, unable to look away from Jackâ€™s blue-toothed leer. Rough cobblestones scraped the skin from his palms. He backed up against the Clapham Cemetery fence, and suddenly he was stuck fast like a bug pinned to a board. Jack crept after him, arms open to embrace, a mad parody of a doting uncle. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œOh, I wanted a girl,â€ Jack sang. â€œBut <i>youâ€™ll</i> do, my pretty lad, youâ€™ll do!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nHe reached deep into his front trouser pocket and rummaged around, lip-pursed distraction on his puppet-show face. Then, inch by gleaming inch, he drew out a polished cutlass, more than a yard of razor sharp steel. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œAnd now, Billy.â€ Jack held the sword pointing from between his legs and made it jump with a single lewd thrust of his hips. Then he grinned with such glee, white lips pulled tight on blue stained teeth, that a warm wet stain grew at the front of the boyâ€™s uniform pants. â€œ<i>Now</i> itâ€™s timeâ€¦ for the <i>QUEEEEEERING!â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nOn the last syllable his voice glissed into a piercing screech, like steel trolley wheels braking hard on the rail, and Dibble cried out and snapped awake. The noon sun was pouring through the window and dappled the floor. He put a trembling hand to his head, trying to calm himself. After a moment he realized the room was filled with the rank stink of wet diapers, and it wasnâ€™t sweat that was soaking his bedclothes. <p>\r\n\r\n<center>IV. </center><p>\r\n\r\nFour days later a thirty-four year old mother of three was found dead in her kitchen, missing her kidneys and a portion of her scalp. And a week after that, it was a scullery maid, buried, inexplicably, under a pile of potatoes at the farmerâ€™s market. The constables posted notices everywhere, begging for someone to come forward, but there were no witnesses, and no leads. The civilian constabulary, which had been founded only a few years past, was not winning the publicâ€™s confidence. Walking home from his rounds, threading his way through the produce stalls of Green Dragon Alley, Dibble overheard a klatch of withered old women discussing the particulars. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œPeeled her,â€ a fishwife said. â€œLike a grape. I seen it myself. One eye gone, and a patch of skin cut away, neat as you please.â€ She traced a rectangle around her own eye with one filthy finger. â€œLike someone had laid a book on her face and traced around it with a razor.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œCor, and thatâ€™s not all,â€ the butcherâ€™s wife said. She wore an eye patch cut from a bloodstained leather apron. â€œI know this girl wotâ€™s the best friend of the maid of the doctor wot did the examination, right? And she said she said he said, that the dead girls, they was raped, too, not just murdered, and not just raped, either, but got it the Greek way, if you know what I mean. And the fiend what did it was big as a Newcastle cucumber.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nThe other old women gasped. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œNot that Iâ€™m <i>against</i> the gettinâ€™ it the Greek way,â€ the butcherâ€™s wife said. She winked her one eye, and the rest of the harridans cackled. She noticed Dibble loitering by the fruit cart, pretending to look over the last of the autumn apples. â€œWhy look, thereâ€™s one of Londonâ€™s finest now. Oi, Constable Dibble!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nCheeks burning, Dibble dropped tuppence in the fruit vendorâ€™s filthy hand and tipped his hat in acknowledgement. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œWe was talking about these terrible murders, you know. Would you do us the favor of answering a question?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble nodded smartly, drawing himself up and squaring his shoulders. \r\n â€œIf you couldnâ€™t catch them leprechauns in the boxing ring,â€ the butcherâ€™s wife said, â€œhow dâ€™ya think youâ€™re going to catch the Springheel Jack?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nTheir laughter clanged in his ears all the way to the end of the alley. <p>\r\n\r\n<center>V. </center><p>\r\n\r\nAn unseasonable thaw had left the unpaved streets of London a filthy bog of melted snow and emptied chamber pots. There was a <i>London Post-Observe</i>r tossed carelessly on the ground, pasted to the mud. Dibble paused in his rounds to read the headlines in the glow of the gas lamps. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œVAMPYRES OF LONDON?â€ the paper said. â€œMAD SURGEON ON THE LOOSE! SOMEONEâ€™S GOT A TASTE FOR THE SWEETBREAD.â€ Below the blaring type was an illustration depicting Sergeant Major Boddington as a tongue-wagging buffoon playing blindmanâ€™s bluff with a gaggle of knife wielding fiends. Dibble had to grin, imagining the Sergeant Majorâ€™s reaction when he saw it. <p>\r\n\r\nThere was a flutter of displaced air and then Dibble was knocked to the ground, pinned under dark weight. It was a woman; her perfume smelled like June berries. She clung tight to him as he tried to scramble free, and he dragged her into the light. Dibble stifled a cry of revulsion. Her face was a Halloween mask, all bulging eyes and a tongue that protruded in a stiff arc from between rotten yellow teeth. She was only half-clothedâ€”her left side was fully dressed, but her right was completely bare. Something had sliced everything she wore--a thick wool coat layered over a plain cotton dress dyed a lurid red over lacy underclothesâ€”neatly right down the middle. Her corset flapped open around her breast, all tension gone from its laces. She was twice as heavy in death as she mustâ€™ve been in life, and Dibble struggled to push her off him. <p>\r\n\r\nSomething smashed the back of his skull and Dibble watched a black sun rise behind his eyelids. He sagged back to the street, stunned. The cold mud welcomed his weight. He felt a sharp tug as his police whistle was torn from around his neck, and then an invading hand was in his pockets, groping him until it found his pocketknife. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI just need to borrow this for a second.â€ said a voice, wheezy and high like the song of an organ grinderâ€™s box. â€œMaybe Iâ€™ll come back for your other tool later.â€ Amidst the shriek of giggles Dibble heard the knife click open and the soft slish of a blade peeling an apple. Then the handle, covered with something warm and sticky, was folded back into his hand by cold white fingers. <p>\r\n\r\nThe whistle gave an ear-splitting shriek. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThis is for costing me the girl by the cemetery.â€ Jackâ€™s furnace eyes and skinny tusk of a nose swam into Dibbleâ€™s dazed view. â€œDo you know how long it takes me to find proper subjects?â€ The whistle wailed again, and this time, it was answered by others. Men were shouting, coming closer. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI wish I could tarry to watch you try to explain this, Constable.â€ Jack giggled and blew the whistle one final time. â€œBut I still have two more specimens to collect before I can be on my way.â€ And with a rustle of velvet and a tearing squelch of mud, he was gone. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œDibble?â€ A tall figure was approaching from the south, silhouetted by the gas light or the corner. When he caught sight of the bodies on the ground he accelerated into a slow trot, a whistle clanging against the brass buttons of his tunic. He stopped ten feet from where Dibble stood over the dead girl. It was Sweeney. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œConstable?â€ he asked. â€œWhat have you--â€ His eyes widened. <p>\r\n\r\nDibble looked down at the knife in his hand. â€œN-no, itâ€™s n-n-notâ€”â€œ<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œYou put that down, right now. And donâ€™t you move. Donâ€™t make this harder than it has to be. Springheel Jack, eh? The Queenâ€™s holy white arse, the Springheel Jack.â€ Sweeney seized Dibbleâ€™s shoulder and drew his truncheon. â€œOi!â€ he shouted. â€œOver here! Itâ€™s Dibble! Itâ€™s Dibble whatâ€™s theâ€”â€œ<p>\r\n\r\nDibbleâ€™s fist drove the rest of his cry straight down his throat. Sweeney staggered back, one hand clapped over the blood pouring from his mouth, the other still clutching Dibbleâ€™s cloak. Dibble hit him again, in quick combinationâ€”<i>right-left-right</i>â€”and Sweeney collapsed over the corpse, driving the dead girl deeper into the muck. <p>\r\n\r\nConstables were on their way, torches bobbing and whistles blaring. â€œIâ€™m s-s-<i>sorry,â€</i> Dibble told Sweeney, and then he fled into the night. <p>\r\n\r\n<center>VI. </center><p>\r\n\r\nThree throbbing tones from the bell of the great clock tower shivered windows across the city as Bill Dibble, filthy, exhausted and starving after two days of hiding in the rat warren sewers under the streets of London, slipped through the alley that ran behind his house on Commonwealth Street to hoist himself, arms shaking and weak, through a back window and into his kitchen. <p>\r\n\r\nHe opened a cabinet and felt around until he found a pack of wooden matches. Groping in the dark, he found the edge of the kitchen table, where he knew thereâ€™d be a candle. He struck the match and in the flare he saw his daughter staring up at him. He jumped back, knocking a chair over, which scared him worse. He picked it up and sat down, hands trembling. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œDaddy,â€ she whispered. â€œWhere have you been?â€ She scrunched up her face and waved her hand in front of her nose. â€œOh, you smell!â€ she said, but climbed in his lap anyway. â€œDaddy, I dreamed about Punch last night.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble lit the candle and found a loaf of bread. â€œDid you, my dear,â€ he said around great tearing mouthfuls. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI did. He said he was going to come visit. He said he was going to stick me with his sword. Punch doesnâ€™t carry a sword, Daddy, he carries a stick. Why did he say that? It donâ€™t make no sense.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI donâ€™t know, love.â€ Suddenly the bread heâ€™d just swallowed was threatening to return. â€œBut it was just a dream. Dreams donâ€™t have to make sense.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œHe said something else,â€ she whispered. Her eyes were wide, and Dibble saw something in her face that had never been there before. It was fear. She called him closer with a crooked finger. â€œHe said to tell you that youâ€™d never catch him.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nVII. <p>\r\n\r\nDibble waited until midnight, and then slipped back through the window and into the alley. Keeping in the shadows as best he could, he moved from street to street and block to block, the mud sucking at his boots. <p>\r\n\r\nAfter prowling for hours, hiding in doorways and in piles of rubbish like a common thief when the Constabulary passed, Dibble stopped in the middle of Green Dragon Alley. The cold made Dibbleâ€™s hands whine and complain, and he jammed them in his armpits for some warmth. The stones reeked of fish guts and rotten carrots, and the covered stalls stood in silent rebuke. <i>Go home, Dibble. They ainâ€™t nothing for you to find here. </i><p>\r\n\r\nHis only hope to avoid the noose was catching Springheel Jack himself, but it was fruitless. He had seen nothing, heard nothing. He was only one man, and London was a very big place. A new kind of cold, much worse than the air that bit his nose and his ears with sharp little teeth, was growing within him. Despair squeezed his heart with hard fingers and tears rose to freeze in tiny icicles on his eyelashes. He was going to be hung for murder, and god knew what would become of his wife and little girl. He fell moaning to his knees and clutched his head, reciting every prayer heâ€™d ever been taught, begging heaven for aid. <p>\r\n\r\nThe silence of the street was broken by a muffled shattering of glass and a stifled shriek. He opened his eyes to find a familiar top-hatted silhouette creeping across a window above the butcher shop. Dibble was on his feet in an instant. The shop door hung splintered on its hinges even before the next thin, warbling scream drifted down to the street. <p>\r\n\r\nIt was pitch black in the stairwell and smelled of blood and rats. When Dibble gained the landing, stumbling when he didnâ€™t find the last step heâ€™d expected, there was one door with a crack of light below it and he threw all his weight against it. <p>\r\n\r\nHe burst into a drab, threadbare living room, light spots on the wall where portraits had once hung, the coal stove cold in the corner. The woman with the eye patch whoâ€™d teased Dibble in the market, the butcherâ€™s wife, was splayed facedown and snoring over a worn green ottoman, her voluminous skirts thrown up over her bonnet and her wrinkled old ass held higher than her head. Behind her was Springheel Jack, a pyramid of shadow topped with a grinning white mask. The old woman shuddered from invisible impacts to the accompaniment of whirring clicks and the sharp smell of lightening-cooked air. <p>\r\n\r\nDibble grabbed a chamber pot from its station beside the door and threw it as hard as he could. It slammed into the side of Jackâ€™s head, sloshing its contents everywhere. A steel pole burst from the old womanâ€™s mouth in a great spattering burst of blood and black bile, and as Jack surged to his feet, she rose with him, suddenly awake, dancing and jiggling and gagging on the spit. Smoke and sparks poured from Jackâ€™s left ear like jubilee fireworks, and Dibble rubbed his eyes hard, because he thought he saw Jack blur out of focus, smear sideways like a chalk drawing in the rain, and then snap back again. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œMeddling <i>bugger!â€</i> Jack cried. â€œA very delicate operation! Disturbed! Another experiment! Almost ruined! Bugger! <i>Bugger!â€ </i><p>\r\n\r\nThe pole disappeared and the butcherâ€™s wife flopped to the floor. Then, faster than Dibble could follow, Jack swept the pot from the floor and hurled it back. <p>\r\n\r\nDibbleâ€™s nose broke with a wishbone crack and he tasted a flood of pennies at the back of his throat. The world disappeared as if a curtain had dropped and he stumbled about until he hit a wall and slid, stunned, to the floor. He tried to get up, but his legs wouldnâ€™t work. Jack leaped to his side, dragging the old woman behind him. He slid a taloned finger under Dibbleâ€™s chin and tilted his head up to look at him. Blood bubbled in the constableâ€™s nostrils. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œIâ€™m a scientist, Billy,â€ Jack whispered. His burning eyes snapped shut and the humming and whirring grew much louder. When he opened them again Dibble could feel their heat baking the tears on his cheeks. â€œThe English invented the scientific method, did you know that? Iâ€™m studying the reproductive cycle in human females. Every stage must be represented. Iâ€™ve got samples from lots of reproductively mature females. And now one post-menopausal.â€ He picked up the fishwife by her scraggly gray hair and shook her. Her slack jaw slid back and forth like she was trying to tell Dibble a secret. â€œFor comparisonâ€™s sake. Can you guess what I need to complete my collection? One pre-pubescent female. A young girl. Can you think of one, Billy? One who likesâ€”puppet shows, perhaps?â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œN-n-<i>noâ€”â€œ</i> <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œ<i>Y-y-yes!â€</i> Jack cried, and it seemed as if his grin might stretch right off his face. â€œDonâ€™t you think Iâ€™ve marked on what a pretty daughter you have.â€ A tongue as long and thin as a garter snaked slipped between his teeth and smoothed an arched eyebrow.  â€œOh, <i>so</i> pretty.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble felt his innards liquefy and cascade, desperate for escape. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œAnd just because youâ€™re such a meddling bugger,â€ Jack said, pinching Dibbleâ€™s cheek, â€œIâ€™m going to let you live to see her corpse.â€ There was a blur of midnight and a tinkle of breaking window glass, and he was gone. <p>\r\n\r\nBellowing with rage and despair, Dibble threw himself out the third story window after him. He plunged down into the dark, sick with weightlessness and closing his eyes against the final judgment of gravity and the ground, and suddenly there was velvet clutched in his fists and he was rising high over the tenement roofs. In the east, the spires of Westminster Abbey were limned in a gold filigree of morning light. <p>\r\n\r\nJack looked completely back over his shoulder, an owlâ€™s gesture that wouldâ€™ve broken the neck of any man. â€œI donâ€™t <i>believe</i> it,â€ he shrieked. â€œYou bugger, bugger, <i>bugger!â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nThey slowed for a moment at the apogee of Jackâ€™s leap, and then the world called them home. Jack landed on the crumbling edge of a tenement roof. Dibble was slammed against the side of the building with the wet smack of a hog carcass dropped on the butcherâ€™s block, but didnâ€™t let go. <p>\r\n\r\nJack tottered on the heels of his fashionable patent leather ankle boots, pin wheeling his arms for balance and screeching in frustration. Dibble tried to pull himself up onto the parapet by the tails of the black velvet coat. Between his dangling feet he could see a crowd of early-to-rise businessmen and late-to-bed neâ€™er do wells gawking and pointing. From down the street came the rumble of stampeding feet and the whistle and rattle of constabulary alarms. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œHoly God in heaven, he was right!â€ someone cried. â€œHold on tight, Dibble! Donâ€™t let the bastard go!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nJack crouched abruptly, dropping Dibble three bowel-loosening feet, and then the rest of the crowdâ€™s encouraging cries were lost in a roaring rush of air as they burst into the sky. The earth was reluctant to let them go; Dibbleâ€™s face stretched tight, and his heart seemed to drop all the way to the pit of his stomach. The girls shopping the windows of Carnaby Street and the men on their way to buy new suits on Saville Row shrank to the size of tin soldiers. Then they were falling again, they were headed straight for the Clapham Church Cemetery, for the tall trees that shaded the graves--<p>\r\n\r\nThey tore into the upper reaches of skeletal branches, and then Dibble was crashing alone towards the ground like a ball in a pachinko game, the tail of Jackâ€™s coat still clutched in his hands. He tumbled from branch to branch, each one a truncheon smack his head or chin or kidney. <p>\r\n\r\nFinally there was a blissful instant when the battering stopped and then his backside met the spongy ground with a skull rattling shock. He rolled onto his side, groaning. The pain in his bones washed away thought like an ocean wave overrunning a childâ€™s sand castle. <p>\r\n\r\n â€œFlying is easy,â€ Jack crowed. He was still up in the tree, a sepulchral vulture perched on a high branch. â€œItâ€™s <i>landing</i> thatâ€™s hard.â€ He dropped a few yards away from Dibbleâ€™s outstretched hand, squatting until the tails of his coat brushed the grass. His legs, Dibble saw, had grown as tall as a short man from polished ankle boot to knee, and as long again from knobby knee to waistcoat. Springheel Jack swayed low between two black spider legs, his long white fingers fumbling with his belt and trouser buttons. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œOh, but we know whatâ€™s coming next, donâ€™t we, Constable?â€ Jack said. Dibble heard a clockwork whir and a gleaming steel rod began telescoping from between Jackâ€™s legs. It grew and grew, section sliding from section, until Jack sported a gleaming metal tool that would have shamed a horse. Blue drool leaked from the corners of his grinning mouth and dripped from his chin. â€œOh yes, we know what comes next. Bugger,<i> bugger.â€</i> <p>\r\n\r\nHe took a deep breath and a massive gout of sticky blue drenched the constableâ€™s face. Dibble gasped in revulsion, drawing it into his lungs, and then a bell clanged to announce the start of a new round. He heard the impact on hard fist on scarred skin even before he felt the horizon of his brow give birth to a sunrise of pain. <p>\r\n\r\nDanny Oâ€™Dell grinned around the black holes where his teeth used to be. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œWhy dontcha just drop then,â€ he said, his lilting voice provided by a red bearded elf on his shoulder. â€œYer out on yer feet already, son. Just stop now before ye get hurt.â€\r\nDibble didnâ€™t answer. He moved and circled, fists up high, watching the elves flit in the periphery of his vision. He would only have one chance to catch them unawares. He dropped his hands further. Danny Oâ€™Dell smirked, one corner of his mouth lifted by a tiny hand, and threw his big slow right, confident that Dibble was too dazed to stop it. <p>\r\n\r\nBut Nimble Billy Dibble, former champion of all England, wasnâ€™t done yet. He dropped and pivoted to deliver a left hook to the ribs and an overhand right to the nose so quick it caught Danny Oâ€™Dellâ€™s pained gasp on its way out. <p>\r\n\r\nThere was a distant, musical tinkle of expensive dinner plates smashing on a marble floor, and then a scream that reached Dibble at the bottom of the well of unconsciousness and pulled him straight to the surface. He sat bolt upright, coughing and spitting and blowing bright gobs of sticky goo from his nose and mouth, just in time to see Jack reeling backwards, stumbling over the low graves. Jackâ€™s long pointed nose, broken off at the base, lay in the grass next to Dibbleâ€™s boot. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œMy <i>sniffer,</i> you broke my <i>sniffer!â€</i> Jack wailed, and his gleaming steel phallus whipped through the grass and back through his fly. When he straightened up there was a black hole in the center of his face, and was there something moving in there? <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œDid I now?â€ Dibble climbed painfully to his feet and clenched his fists. His knuckles popped in quick succession, like firecrackers on a string. â€œLetâ€™s see what else I can break then.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nDibble dropped under Jackâ€™s flailing hands and hammered his black velvet vest with a right hook that wouldâ€™ve cracked the ribs of a steer. Jack flew across the grass, his skinny black limbs like diagrams of geometric proofs, to land in a tangle against the marble walls of a mausoleum. One of Jackâ€™s hand-tooled ankle boots was still mired in the muddy grass, smoke curling in lazy spirals from the insole. Dibble bared his teeth and began to follow, but stopped after a single step.  <p>\r\n\r\nHalf of Jackâ€™s face was gone. A few pale porcelain shards were stuck in the thick callouses that covered Dibbleâ€™s knuckles, and the rest littered the ground where Jackâ€™s body lay.  Behind the shattered faÃ§ade, where there should have been a maze of gooey sinuses and a pound of buttersoft meat, a little gray man with a babyâ€™s enormous head and black insect eyes sat in a tiny chair, frantically pulling levers and flipping switches amid a swirl of blue smoke and bursts of red sparks. He looked up at Dibble, whimpered, and redoubled his desperate manipulations. <p>\r\n\r\nJackâ€™s arm began to spasm, the talons digging up fresh sod, and Dibble jumped back. Jack jerked to his feet, slumping to one side like a marionette with half its string cut. The foot that had lost its shoe was little more than a small half-moon of glowing metal that burned black Uâ€™s into the ground. <p>\r\n\r\nShouts and whistles. A dozen truncheon-wielding men with bristling mustaches were sprinting across the Common to the cemetery gates. The flat slap of the leather soles on the stones sounded like applause. Sweeney was the first to arrive at Dibbleâ€™s side. He took one look at the faceless, shambling Springheel Jack, and slapped himself twice to be sure he wasnâ€™t dreaming. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI donâ€™t believe it,â€ he said. â€œI justâ€¦ damnation. Hellfire and damnation.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nThe man-shaped machine sputtered, smoke wafting from its pointed ears. The little creature sitting up in Jackâ€™s head smacked at tiny levers, squeaking with rage, and then the low coughing noise cranked back up to an insectile whine. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI swear youâ€™ll pay for this, Dibble,â€ Jack squeaked. â€œIâ€™ll be back for you! You shitass! You bugger!â€ <p>\r\n\r\nJack staggered back several steps to gain room, and then charged the steps to the mausoleum. With a final tremendous bound, a crack of splitting stone and a long tongue of flame, he flew up over the church and was gone. The constables, red faced and sweating, watched him go, mustaches drooping in wonder. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œWhat in Godâ€™s name was that thing?â€ a constable asked, staring at the trail of smoke arching up into the sky. <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œIt was one of Dibbleâ€™s leprechauns,â€ Sweeney said. â€œI think he wants another rematch.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œOh yeah?â€ The constable rubbed his chin contemplatively. â€œTricky, wasnâ€™t he, using that big puppet to do his fighting. Right tricky. You careful wifâ€™ him, Dibble.â€ <p>\r\n\r\nâ€œI will,â€ Dibble said, nodding gravely and trying to keep his blood off his uniform. â€œItâ€™s always the little ones what you got to watch out for.â€\r\n\r\n'),(3268,'<p>For the past two years, I\\\'ve been working ever so diligently as a dispensing optician. I sell you the glasses that you need to see with, the contacts that you absolutely must have, and just about anything else that has to do with eyesight in general.</p>\r\n<p>The company I work for is actually a rather nice one... unlike many optical locations, we actually take a wide array of insurances, and bill them directly. It\\\'s not common, but it certainly helps when budgets would otherwise limit what you can choose to wear.</p>\r\n<p>However, with our generosity come the inevitable problems one comes to expect in this business. People almost never take the time to understand the insurance that they are using, and so leave the sole responsibility on our shoulders.</p>\r\n<p>Perhaps, for once, I can turn the tables. Please note that the following comments, while aimed at the West Coast (and specifically Oregon) where I currently work, can be applied just about anywhere.</p><hr>\r\n<b>General notes of common sense:</b> \r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>The joys of the words \\\"up to.\\\" </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>Now... most of you who have asked your insurance what they will cover have heard those magic words: Up To. Most folks immediately assume this is blanket \r\n      coverage, and that anything they don\\\'t use on one part of the insured item \r\n      can be applied to another. So, when they come to us, their expectations \r\n      lead them to anger when we tell them that their coverage will be less than \r\n      what they thought. Most don\\\'t realize that since we\\\'re less expensive than \r\n      the industry standard, insurance companies expect to pay <b>less</b>. They \r\n      don\\\'t want to fork out any more than they have to, and so change the amount \r\n      they\\\'ll pay <b>us</b> to compensate. They can make the actual numbers as \r\n      much as they please because of those magic words. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>We are not obligated to take your insurance. </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>Now... if our customers could only figure this out. Did you know that, \r\n      at least in this state, that we have no such obligation? The fact is, we\\\'re \r\n      one of the only commercial eyeglass dealers that still accept insurances. \r\n      Do you have any idea how much of a pain in the ass it is to check up on \r\n      all that third-party stuff? We have to know the lingo, get past the bullshit, \r\n      and sometimes wait on the phone for two hours just to make sure we can <b>possibly</b> \r\n      bill them for your glasses. So think of the favor we\\\'re doing you. We could \r\n      be like everyone else and just make you bill it yourself. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>We must be the ones to verify eligibility before you get your benefits. \r\n  </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>I\\\'m sorry... it\\\'s as simple as that. I don\\\'t care how honest you are -- \r\n      I need to see for myself that your insurance company will cover you. There \r\n      are simply too many factors to consider, and you as the customer won\\\'t be \r\n      privy to them. How can you be certain we accept your insurance? Or how much \r\n      we accept? Or how we bill it. Just give us the info... we\\\'ll track all that \r\n      down. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>If your insurance tells us \\\"No,\\\" then what they said to you is \r\n    beside the point. </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>It comes down to trust, I guess... and our system has been scammed so many \r\n      times by people that we simply can\\\'t trust your word. No means no. If your \r\n      insurance tells us they won\\\'t cover... then we won\\\'t bill. If we do, <b>we</b> \r\n      lose the money. And, being a business, that\\\'s not acceptable. Sorry. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Checking your insurance won\\\'t always be an instant thing. </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>Like I said before... insurances love to make us providers wait. Oftentimes \r\n      customers tell me how quickly they get in touch with an operator. There\\\'s \r\n      a reason for that: customers get preferential treatment. We providers have \r\n      to wait until there\\\'s room for us to butt in. There are many reasons for \r\n      this: telling a customer what their benefits doesn\\\'t instantly mean loss... \r\n      whereas talking to us means they\\\'re going to have to pay up rather soon. \r\n      Who do you think they\\\'ll want to talk to more? Consider that the next time \r\n      you\\\'re on a call with your insurance company. Likelihood is, some provider \r\n      has to wait for you to get off the phone. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Time Zones and Weekends have an effect on our ability to check insurances. \r\n  </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>Fact: Many of your insurance companies have offices on the East Coast.<br>\r\n      Fact: Usually the initial number we\\\'re given to verify your insurance leads \r\n      to one of those offices.<br>\r\n      Fact: Most insurance offices close at 5, EST.<br>\r\n      Fact: That means we can\\\'t call after 2, PST<br>\r\n      Fact: I\\\'ve YET to find an insurance company open on Saturday or Sunday for \r\n      anything aside from Customer relations.<br>\r\n      Fact: When you come to me with insurances during those times, you\\\'re going \r\n      to wind up disappointed. I will not be able to check them.</p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Insurance companies love to change the rules. </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>\\\"My friend came here just six months ago and used this insurance...\\\" \r\n      That\\\'s fine and dandy. Bully for your friend. That really doesn\\\'t mean squat \r\n      in a business where contracts are renegotiated at will. It could very well \r\n      be that two months ago that company decided to change the rules about lab \r\n      use... and suddenly we\\\'re not able to cover them. It changed. Sorry. Not \r\n      our fault. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>Even with insurance, you\\\'re most likely going to have to pay something. \r\n  </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<ol>\r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p>It\\\'s a sad fact. Unless you\\\'re willing to settle with bottom-of-the-barrel \r\n      glasses, you\\\'re going to need to fork over a little cash. Consider how much \r\n      you\\\'re NOT paying for... for just one moment. You could be spending three \r\n      times as much somewhere else... so why bitch at me because I want to charge \r\n      you twenty bucks for a warranty? </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</ol>\r\n<p><b>Phrases that don\\\'t help us:</b></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>\\\"My insurance covers $150.\\\" \r\n    <blockquote> \r\n      <p>I\\\'ve already mentioned the ever popular \\\"up to\\\" clause. What \r\n        typically happens when you call in to your insurance is that they give \r\n        you a blanket number -- the total amount they\\\'re willing to part with. \r\n        This is what they call \\\"acceptable loss.\\\" The thing is, this \r\n        assumes that the lenses will cost $75 and the frames will cost $75. If \r\n        we charge less then that on either (say we charge $35 for lenses), then \r\n        $35 is all they will pay (which brings you down to $109 -- with quite \r\n        a bit that you can\\\'t turn over). The excess cannot be applied to anything \r\n        else. Don\\\'t ask. </p>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n  </li>\r\n  <li>\\\"I already verified my eligibility\\\" \r\n    <blockquote> \r\n      <p>This claim typically comes from one of two types of people: scan artists \r\n        or people who simply haven\\\'t got a clue. Of course, since the former exists, \r\n        we have to consider that to be the primary reason -- thus we really can\\\'t \r\n        trust you. We have to assume that everyone fits into that potential scam \r\n        artist category. Until I hear with my own two ears from the insurance \r\n        company that you\\\'re insured, active, and eligible, you\\\'re not going to \r\n        get any of your benefits from me. </p>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n  </li>\r\n  <li>\\\"I don\\\'t have a card with me.\\\" \r\n    <blockquote> \r\n      <p>Great. Spectacular. After all, all I should need is your name and what \r\n        you think might be your insurance company. They should be able to identify \r\n        you simply name from among their millions of customers. Who needs any \r\n        of the other information like member number, plan number, or even the \r\n        billing address? Hell... I should have all that information on file -- \r\n        it\\\'s only got 300 variations after all. And why can\\\'t I figure out which \r\n        office to call... there\\\'s only 20 of them... </p>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n  </li>\r\n  <li>\\\"Why do you need \\\'X\\\' information?\\\" \r\n    <blockquote> \r\n      <p>You can insert Social Security Number, Birth Date, Phone Number, Mailing \r\n        Address, or any other number of things that we\\\'ll ask you for. There are \r\n        certain things we <b>must</b> have in order to get money from your insurance \r\n        companies. The most prevalent of these is the SNN of every party involved \r\n        in the purchase (thus payee and dependant). We wouldn\\\'t ask for something \r\n        we didn\\\'t have to have. Think about it. As well, if you don\\\'t like using \r\n        your SSN, most states make it legal for you to request an alternate identification \r\n        number to replace it. </p>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n  </li>\r\n  <li>\\\"Bill it all to my insurance... they\\\'ll bill me for the difference.\\\" \r\n  </li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<blockquote> \r\n  <blockquote> \r\n    <p><b>Wrong</b>. Sorry... I don\\\'t know where you got your information from... \r\n      but insurances these days won\\\'t take that kind of time out of their schedules. \r\n      If I charge them more than they are willing to pay, they give me the contracted \r\n      amount, and tell me to stuff the rest of the bill where the sun don\\\'t shine. \r\n      In other words: I lose that money. Not acceptable. </p>\r\n  </blockquote>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<ul>\r\n  <li>\\\"You\\\'ve got my insurance info on file already.\\\" \r\n    <blockquote> \r\n      <p>So? How do I know it hasn\\\'t changed, been altered, or switched since \r\n        then? What if all I have is some sketchy info that was poorly recorded \r\n        last time? Or even done <b>wrong</b> and corrected by a manager later? \r\n        I\\\'m not willing to risk taking MORE time out of my already busy schedule \r\n        to track down phantom numbers. Just give them to me new... so we can get \r\n        this over with.</p>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n  </li>\r\n</ul>'),(3269,'<P><B>Chapter 14 (replaces original chapter 14)</B><BR><BR><BR>He writes for two hours before bed, at the desk in his bedroom, before the window looking out over the dairy.  He writes on white legal pads with a black pen, on the fronts of the pages only.</P><P>He writes:</P><I><P>There\\\'s a little old man in our town who never sleeps.  At night, he walks around downtown and tries the doorknobs to make sure they\\\'re locked.  He smokes Pall Malls and usually shares a Rolling Rock with Vinyl Lewis before Vinyl closes the bar.  In the mornings, shopkeepers all around the square find their front windows smudged with the grease his face leaves when he presses it up against the glass, looking for burglars.  Towards dawn, he grows the slightest bit weary and sits on a bench on the courthouse lawn, where he\\\'s beset by waking purple nightmares which he blows away like smoke.</P><P>Noon finds him in one of the cafes on the square drinking weak tea and reading the likes of Rimbaud or Mayakovsky or Lao-tzu.  The tea and some thin soup are all the nourishment he takes aside from the cigarrettes and half a beer every night.  At two, he slips the waiter a ten and goes over to the park for a math tutorial from his friend Bud.  When it rains, they hold the session in the library.  On rainy Sundays, when the library is closed, they repair to Vinyl\\\'s and drink brandy in the back room as Bud explains probability theory and calculus.  He gives him ten for the lesson.</P><P>At five or so, he goes to his rented room over the hardware store and feeds his cat.  He reads the papers and watches a quiz show, sweeps up or clips his nails.  He showers and dresses and, at eight, locks up and begins his rounds.</P><P>At sunrise, he\\\'s on the bench blinking away the demons.  He rises, his clothes slightly dewy, and walks down to the Catholic church, waiting on the steps for Father Anthony to show up.  Inside, he lights a candle for a saint whose name has long been forgotten, a saint for whom no medals have ever been stamped.  He prays and prays in Aramaic and Hebrew and Greek for his wife and his cat and Bud and Vinyl and all the people everywhere, but mostly he prays to stay awake.</P><P>He slips a ten in the poorbox and leaves.  From nine to eleven, he visits his wife at the nursing home.  She no longer knows who he is.  Then, sometimes, he goes to the bank, sometimes not, depending on how many tens are in his pocket.  And the bank is where he met our hero.</P></I><BR><BR><BR>       *        *        *<BR><BR><BR><B>Chapter 15</B><BR><BR><BR><P>During the day, she went around to the various government employment offices to see if there were any jobs to be had.  At night, she went back to the hostel and ate dinner with Lotte and David, the owners.  Sometimes there were other guests, but, more often, not.  There weren\\\'t too many people who wanted to come to Stockholm in February.</P><P>After dinner, she and David sat in the kitchen and drank aquavit.  The dill and pepper flavors were her favorites.  David, an American, told her stories about growing up in Hell\\\'s Kitchen and his days in the Air Force.  Lotte answered e-mail, most of it concerning reservations, in the next room.  She drank just enough to get a little drunk, then she went to bed.</P><P>The dormitory was full of bunkbeds.  She slept on a bottom bunk by a window that looked out on a courtyard behind the hostel.  One night, she got up to use the bathroom and saw a man out there curled up asleep on the paving stones under the sodium light.  He must have climbed the chain-link fence she had seen at the mouth of the alley between the buildings.  She tapped on the glass, but he didn\\\'t stir.  I like his shoes, she thought.  <I>I bet I could crawl out there and take them off his feet, and he would never wake up.</I>  He was gone in the morning.</P><P>She read <U>Other Voices, Other Rooms</U> before going to sleep.  She liked the rich, tumescent language, and the Southern setting reminded her of Florida and the people she knew there.  After she turned out the lights, she prayed for all the people she knew, for Matt, for Howard, for all the women she had met in jail, for her parents, her family, for the friends that she had met all over the world.  Lastly, she prayed for herself.  She prayed that she might know happiness, that God would give her faith and some kind of peace of mind.  Then she listened to the ceiling fan grinding on its bearings.  She turned it on so the white noise it produced would help her sleep.  She ran her fingers along the cool white wall next to her bed and tucked the covers in all around her and laid there still until sleep came.</P><P>On the night she moved into the hostel, she stayed up \\\'til ten o\\\'clock and called Howard.  She gave him the number where she was and asked him to pass it on to Matt when he saw him.  A little over a week later, Lotte came into the dorm one night at four in the morning and awakened her.</P><P>\\\"Anna.  There\\\'s a man on the phone for you.\\\"</P><P>She took the call in the hallway, in an alcove that held a payphone and a chair.  There were numbers written on the walls.  From where she was sitting, she could see down the hall to the front door and, through the windows on either side of it, snow falling on the sidewalk and the street beyond.</P><P>\\\"Hello?\\\" she said.</P><P>\\\"Hey.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Hey.  When did you get out?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Coupla days ago.  I woulda called earlier, but Howard wouldn\\\'t let me use his phone.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Where are you now?\\\"</P><P>\\\"At Mom and Dad\\\'s house.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well. <U>This</U> is news.  Did they let you use the front door?\\\"</P><P>\\\"More or less.  My residence here is contingent upon a few conditions.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I bet.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Listen:  I spent the night in the house the first night out, and I hid everything up in the attic before I left, so all your stuff\\\'s OK.  Howard\\\'s gonna take me out there with his truck to get it sometime this week.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  I miss my guitar.\\\"</P><P>\\\"So.  You\\\'re stayin\\\' in a hostel?  How zat?\\\"</P><P>\\\"So far, so good.  No snorers yet.\\\"</P><P>\\\"The dreaded snorer.  How much is it?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Two hundred knonor a night.  About twenty bucks.  I\\\'m looking for a roommate.  And a job.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I\\\'ve got to get a job, too.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Poor widdle baby.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Are you gonna have enough money to get settled over there?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I can get some kind of stipend from the government if I don\\\'t.  This is a civilized country.  We don\\\'t let people starve to death here.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I know all about it.  I spent last night in the park with the Can Man.  I decided that was enough for me.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You spent the night with the Can Man?  Matt McCord fucking bums.  I never thought I\\\'d live to see this day.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  I give \\\'em head for pocket change or Snickers bars.  Their choice.  I clean up on it, too.  Word gets around the homeless community.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I bet.  Listen:  I tried to leave some money in your account when I was in jail, but they wouldn\\\'t let me.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I know.  Howard told me.\\\"</P><P>\\\"So.  Didja get any ass in jail?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Did <U>you</U>?\\\"</P><P>\\\"A lady never tells.\\\"</P><P>\\\"So what d\\\'ya wanna do?\\\"</P><P>\\\"What <U>can</U> we do.  One, we don\\\'t have any money to spare.  Two, we\\\'ve got an ocean in between us.  Three, they probably won\\\'t let me back into the country for awhile.\\\"</P><P>\\\"They would if we were married.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Mr. McCord.  Are you asking me to marry you?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  I guess I am.  How many times does this make?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I dunno.  I\\\'ve lost count.\\\"</P><P>She drifts away like the windblown snow, back to the first time he asked her to marry him.  They were riding in his Ford sedan on the the stretch of road between Brooksville and Tallahassee, on their way to New Orleans, nothing but pine trees for miles at a time.  He was teaching her about Taoism.  She was keeping an eye out for a gas station.</P><P>\\\"Now, some Taoists, the hardcore ones, believe that we don\\\'t exist at all, that this is all some kind of illusion.\\\"</P><P>\\\"For somebody who doesn\\\'t exist, I sure do have to pee.\\\"</P><P>\\\"See, now that would be an illusion, too.\\\"</P><P>\\\"If you don\\\'t find me a bathroom soon, you\\\'re going to be swimming in illusion.\\\"</P><P>\\\"There isn\\\'t one for a while yet.  You wanna pull over and do it on the side of the road?\\\"</P><P>\\\"You\\\'ve got to be out of your fucking mind.\\\"</P><P>\\\"It\\\'s either that or wet your pants.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'ve never done it before.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Wet your pants?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, jackass.  Squatted beside the road like some kind of animal.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Aw, hey, really?  It\\\'s easy - I\\\'ll show you how.  I\\\'ve seen my mom and my sister do it a thousand times.\\\"</P><P>\\\"That explains a lot.\\\"</P><P>He pulled over, well off the road, and went around the front of the car and opened both passenger-side doors.</P><P>He said, \\\"Get the roll of toilet paper out of the glove compartment.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Wow, you come prepared.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Just like the muthafukkin Boy <U>SCOUTS</U>.\\\"</P><P>She got out of the car and stood between the doors.  He had her pull her shorts and panties down then squat.  He adjusted her stance so her shoes wouldn\\\'t get wet.</P><P>\\\"Now just let it go.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Turn your head.\\\"</P><P>\\\"What for?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Just turn your head before I bite your nose off.\\\"</P><P>She finished, got herself back together, stood up.  A Highway Patrolman came by and slowed down.  She waved the roll of toilet paper and grinned at him.  She turned to the boyfriend.</P><P>\\\"Thank you.\\\"  She kissed him.</P><P>\\\"Aw, for what?  I didn\\\'t do anything.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yes, you did.  You did a lot.  You\\\'re the most gentle man I\\\'ve ever met.\\\"</P><P>He hesitated a moment.  \\\"Will you marry me?\\\"</P><P>She blinked.  She looked around.  \\\"You really know how to pick your moments.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, will you?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t know?  Uh, let me think about it.  Ask me again when we get to New Orleans.\\\"</P><P>And he\\\'d been asking her ever since.</P><P>She watched the snow fall.  There was a graffito beside the phone that said, in French, \\\"My son\\\'s house burned down.\\\"  There was a number beside it.  She was halfway tempted to call the number after she hung up with Matt.</P><P>\\\"Yes.  I\\\'ll marry you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"When?\\\"</P><P>\\\"As soon as we get enough money for you to come to me.  I can\\\'t come to you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"And enough for a ring, too.  I want you to have a ring.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh, come on.  What do we need a ring for?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I just want you to have one.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK, then.  Listen:  It\\\'s four in the morning over here.  I\\\'m going back to bed.  Plus, your parents are paying for this.\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, I\\\'ll be paying for it.  They\\\'ll take it out of my paychecks.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Either way.  Nighty-night.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.  Sleep tight.  I love you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I love you, too.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Bye.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Bye-bye.\\\"</P><P>She hung up.  The snow fell on the sidewalk and the street beyond.</P>'),(3270,'<p>well chums, five to nil to alinghi. hopefully we never have to hear the word \\\'loyal\\\' ever again and everyone will take those goddamn flags off their cars. i was ok with the red socks; at least that seemed to have some point, but this was just over the top.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a little background for those who haven\\\'t been as bombarded as we have: america\\\'s cup yacht racing. we won it off the americans, successfully defended it once and we were defending it for the second time. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>our skipper and tactician left for the swiss team this time round and took several members of team new zealand with them to race with alinghi. i would have left in their position too - offered enough money to make their and their family\\\'s lives very comfortable for the rest of their days; and after two successes they deserve it. it is a reality in nz that we can\\\'t offer our sportsmen the same that they can get overseas, we just aren\\\'t big enough, and yet when people find success and get rewarded, we cut them down. tall poppy syndrome is rampant here.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>new zealand\\\'s reaction? crucify the traitors. the men who had been golden boys were suddenly evil and showed everything which is bad in the world. we were incited to hate them for this treachery.  then what happened? a few weeks later it is revealed that they left under less-than-stellar conditions; that they had tried to buy into the syndicate but the accounts and details of debts would not be revealed to them, so they left. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>there\\\'s always two sides to the story. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>even in light of this, everyone had LOYAL on their lips and all that was talked about was the racing. in fact, butterworth and coutts\\\' \\\"defection\\\" seemed to make everyone even more blindly patriotic. all that i could ever hear was the special compilation cd being played. the racing was mentioned on every news bulletin, and this was BEFORE the racing actually started.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>unfortunately after all the hype, after all the millions, we lost. completely. not only did we lose, but there have been five races and three brought broken parts of the boat. yes, that\\\'s right - the boat broke multiple times, one of these being the MAST breaking. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>the irony of this: the work of our yachties on the swiss boat has been more to be proud of than our pitiful performance. hopefully after this international embarrassment and revolting treatment of men who have worked so hard for their country in the past, we\\\'ll learn to stick to what we\\\'re good at and be proud of new zealanders no matter whose team they are on. </p>'),(3271,'sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise sac de baise '),(3272,'dododododo dodododdodododdodo doddodododdododod'),(3273,''),(3275,'<p>She extends her long lustful arm. Her skin is smoothed by callous cuts. Her heart is broken and free. The binding seams have been torn away, allowing the heart to scream blinding colours of light and love. Of course I accept. I take the warm can of Molson Pilsner and pour it over my emaciated and inebriated body, absorbing the alcohol slowly through my pours. I am drunk. I am always drunk. Addiction is an oath of honour. It is a method to the madness that leads its victims to their knees. But its owners are fickle and forgetful and everything is transferable. When I turn around she is gone. I crush the can and go home to sleep the day away.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It has been a few years, maybe more, maybe less since I woke up from my sleep. I am stalked by ghosts. I am constantly stalked by ghosts. I have lost track of time and Iâ€™m sure time has long given up on me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I drift hazily through this louts maze. My memory has gotten too full, so I put it down some ways back. My shoes are held together by tape, my friends by glue. My lover grows inside of me as my seed grows inside of her. A haunting, hallow whistle echoes throughout the valley. The crisp corners of the mountaintops hold the snow and clouds with heavy hands. Dreams are reflected and absorbed off this glass city. The ocean waves flood and float through the streets. Monolith monsters cry over the scabs of the sorrowful. It rains, but I am never wet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two speeding trains crash and collide. My lover is my life, my love and myself. We spiral up to the heavens in a dance of death, racing against fires set by others. We breathe in each other like sacred smoke. She extends her long lustful body and of course I accept. I pour her over my strong and wishful body, absorbing her through my dreams. When I turn around she is still there. I close the curtains and we sleep the days away.</p>\r\n'),(3280,'<p>its a sunny afternoon as we hit the carpool lane going to san fransisco. im guessing its about 4:30 or so. erica and anthony are on drugs and were going to see ladytron at amoeba. i dont even care about ladytron, but im always up for an impromtu trip anywhere. life is no fun without adventures if you ask me. but what the hell do i know? either way it got me thinking about what i really want. all of this would never occur to me if san francisco wasnt so bad. i mean honestly the people are rude, they dont dance ( i guess if it wasnt \\\"intended\\\" for dancing you shouldnt do it ), and they have this constant need to assert that sf is better than la. well most of the people i run into. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nmy question is who fucking cares? \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nbig fucking deal. im trapped in a city who is turning out to be exceedingly far more self conscious than the most self conscious city of all time. either way, digression aside, i have come to the conclusion that what i really want is far more ludicrous than it seems in my head.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n i want to quit school, quit everything, run away and never come back, at the same time i want to be in a different area, i want none of this bullshit that surrounds me in school, but at the same time i want to learn as much as possible. but what i want to learn isnt taught in any curriculum nor is there any fucking major in. and just what that \\\"is\\\" i have no fucking clue. it all makes sense in my head, it really does, but the sad part is that i think thats the only place ill ever make any sense. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nas we end this charrade and come back home bitterly dissapointed from a show that turned out to be some hipster spinning records (if i wanted to listen to joy division and my bloody valentine i would have gone home. i have both of those records you played, so fuck you danny, and fuck the rest of ladytron, fucks.) there seemed to be a shift in time, because it seemed like the summation of a bad day had been wiped clean off the slate and i was given a better beginning.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n the night ended with a show at gilman, lots of jack and cokes, wierd tasting beer and a kiss. i went to bed forgetting about anything that i may have thought somewhat important or relevant to my future and ended up where i began. i now sit in the same mental position that i was in the previous days and the months and years before that: wondering. it has now been a good 96 hours later and ive still managed to go completely nowhere...<p/> '),(3279,'people die. and when the ones you love do, it will be painful. but it will also be okay someday.\r\n\r\ngin is the drink of the gods.\r\n\r\na child who is in a holding cell for stabbing their teacher in the eye may very well have been through more hell than any 13 year old should.\r\n\r\nit is fine to buy the new york times sunday edition at 5 pm instead of being peer-pressured into buying it at starbucks along with a fat-free skim vanila soy latte.\r\n\r\nsitting in the bathtub with cigarettes and the aforementioned paper is not unproductive.\r\n\r\n50 degrees in march is a blessing.\r\n\r\nnot having your shit together at age 22 is not the worst thing that will ever happen to you, unless you are a spectacularly boring person.\r\n\r\nsometimes what you need to do is walk down to the piers and look at hundreds of little monarch butterfly umbrellas floating in the harlem river.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t know much, but i know that.\r\n'),(3274,'Il existe une position codifiÃ©e pour l\\\'attente debout. On ne peut se tenir n\\\'importe comment lorsqu\\\'on attend quelqu\\\'un ou quelque chose. Il faut dÃ©j?EÃ©viter toutes les positions qui signifieraient ostensiblement autre chose qu\\\'une simple attente: si l\\\'on tourne le dos ?Eune porte, bien dans l\\\'axe, on est pris pour un vigile; si l\\\'on est dans l\\\'axe d\\\'un couloir ou d\\\'un trottoir, on a l\\\'air d\\\'avoir Ã©t?Estopp?Een cours de route par une pensÃ©e frappante. Si l\\\'on regarde le sol ou ses pieds, c\\\'est qu\\\'on est si dÃ©prim?Equ\\\'on ne sait plus dans quelle direction avancer. Les seules positions d\\\'attente adÃ©quates semblent demander une orientation ambigu?Edu corps et de la tÃªte: par exemple le corps tourn?Edans une direction vers laquelle on ne peut pas marcher, et la tÃªte regardant de toute faÃ§on ailleurs qu\\\'en face, avec n\\\'importe quel angle entre 10 et 80 degrÃ©s sur le cÃ´t?E Faire varier souvent la direction de son regard semble Ãªtre un choix judicieux.<br>\r\nEn cas de doute sur la justesse de sa position, ou si quelque passant a soudain l\\\'air suspicieux, il existe heureusement un truc infaillible : regarder sa montre, puis faire la grimace et la conserver tandis qu\\\'on regarde l\\\'horizon fixement.'),(3281,'<p>\r\nIn a city filled with more and more nervous foreigners I stroll the streets with not a care in the world. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI have turned my bedroom into a patchwork of glue and cuttings. I have filled the cracks with magnum 44, and have made shade with curtains. I stuck rectangles of different shades of blue and orange onto my wall. It is where I sleep. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nN.Korea cant do a god damn thing to me. I\\\'ll be gone before anything serious happens. In this world of crisis and mayham, I\\\'m really not sure a crisis in Korea would be that bad. Let there be war. Let the nation that so wants to unite fight yet another civil war. This is a land out of an old style of thinking. This is a conflict that was never allowed to play its course, held in Limbo for decades. It was only a matter of time. It\\\'s time is near. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nFrom the window just past the front door of my flat I can see only buildings. Being on the north side of my building I can only look up. If I had been blessed with an apartment on the other side I\\\'d be admiring the view. Instead I can only look up to the buildings looking down on me. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nMy neighbourhood is a strange place. I really cannot put it into words. It is 98% safe, though it is very run-down and crumbling. There are new buildings being built and completed regularly, but these new buildings do not take away any of the filth. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf this were a neighbourhood in a major Canadian or American city there is no doubt in my mind there would be violence. I live in a working class ghetto. The only thing missing from my neighbourhood is desperation and crack. I have said to many of my friends in emails that I have not gone a day in Seoul where I have not seen at least one pile of vomit on the street. I live in a hard drinking part of Seoul. What scares me is that this is by far not the hardest drinking of neighbourhoods. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am hoping that my time in Seoul will represent the prologue of an interesting adventure. Chapter one will start when I stare at a fading Korea from a Boat heading for China with Shane. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n'),(3282,'<p> My mother\\\'s younger brother Jack came back from Europe. He\\\'d left school and bummed his way across that fairy tale continent. He came back with his long hair tangled and messed in a careless style, his pants baggy and worn, and his head and conversation filled with stories about girls named Giselle and Katerina. His green army surplus duffel bag was marked with unknown stains and curved neatly to his back. He had a passport saturated with serious looking, government-sanctioned stamps-- the one organized thing amidst the chaos that seemed to surround him. Everywhere he went in the house got messy. </p>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nHe was the only person in my family who had ever really travelled. The only trip we as a family had been on was to the empty parking lot behind the mall when my mom was running away from my dad. My mom would herd my sister and me into the backseat of the car. Sting would be playing on the car radio (he was always on the radio then). We would go and just park. I remember my mother then, her shoulders heaving, her face down. I remember her fingers tangled in her hair, messing it up. I remember what I used to make myself forget.</p>   '),(3283,'<p>and now i am going to share some knowledge that i have that many of you may find useful. recently i stopped smoking cigarettes after about eight years--i began when i was about seventeen, and smoked all the way up to 25. eight or nine years. anyway, at first i kept smoking \\\'the stuff\\\' when i wanted to get stoned. i didn\\\'t go as far as others and try to make it a substitute for cigarettes, but-- i pretty much kept my normal schedule for getting baked. after a couple of weeks, though, i started to feel guilty about smoking anything at <i>all</i>; if i were going to quit smoking cigarettes, and go through all that, i didn\\\'t want to miss out on the damn health benefits just because i wanted to get high every so often. so, after some experimentation, i think that i have hit on the best way to get high from, uh, <i>the stuff</i>, without smoking anything. it is fast, fun and easy.</p><p>\r\nfirst, obtain half an ounce of the active ingredient. the cheaper the better! that\\\'s right! no need to go paying top dollar anymore, boys and girls--our end product varies only in potency, not color or flavor. </p><p>\r\nplace a saucepan about half filled with water on the stove, and while it\\\'s heating up, drop the herbs into it. break them up nicely, but don\\\'t discard the stems or seeds--they contain the active ingredient. toss em in too! even old ones. </p><p>\r\nplace one stick of butter--margerine will NOT WORK--into the hot water/herb mixture, and let the whole thing boil on medium or so for about 15 minutes. make sure it doesn\\\'t boil up too much. if you\\\'re using gas, don\\\'t set the flame too high--you don\\\'t want to scorch the butter at the sides of the pan. soon you will see the butter oils taking on a dark green color, and bob marley\\\'s favorite smell will fill your kitchen. fair warning! it smells fucking awesome. next door too.</p><p>\r\nso, the formula is-- 1/2oz herb per stick of butter. this is for mexican compressed dirt herb. for the KB, the ratio is something like 1/8oz per stick. don\\\'t overdo it--more is not better.\r\n</p><p>\r\nafter 20 minutes, turn off the heat. place a fine-mesh strainer over a large bowl, preferably metal. pour the herbs/melted butter/water mixture through the strainer and into the bowl. squeeze the remaining vegetable matter against the strainer with a fork or potato masher. make sure you get as much butter out as you can--there\\\'s usually a lot that has to be pushed through. keep at it! then, gently set the newly filled bowl on a shelf in your fridge. take four long pieces of string and lay them across the top of the bowl so that they fall into the oily layer and come out the other side. let the bowl cool in the fridge for about four hours. before it cools, it will look dark green, and you may worry that you have done a horrible thing. it\\\'s cool. i promise. go watch a movie or something.</p><p>\r\nwhen you come back and open the fridge, you will see a beautiful, light green colored, solid cake of butter filling the top of your bowl. underneath it lurks some extrememly nasty water. move the bowl to the counter carefully--don\\\'t shake it. pull the whole cake up at once, gently rocking it loose by pulling upward on the strings. place the butter cake on some paper towels to dry. pat the top with a paper towel too. after it dries, i like to leave it in thin form, but you can mold it by melting and re-coagulating it. </p><p>\r\ningestion--every person has their own level, but WARNING--ITS NOT THE SAME AS SMOKING IT. you will be very high, insanely high, panic attack high if you eat too much. i usually put it on a bagel or muffin, using a \\\'generous\\\' amount of butter, not a whole lot. about two of those little patties\\\' worth. start small. i can\\\'t emphasize this enough. anyway it\\\'s a great way to get high anywhere you want including airplanes, the doctor\\\'s office, your parent\\\'s house, prison, school, or church--anytime at all. one stick turns out to be about 15-20 doses for a grown adult--15 strong doses. turns your schwag into KB. it\\\'s like magic. good luck.'),(3284,'II\r\n<p>\r\nThe next night, at about 11pm I awoke on the airplane to Chicago. I had a strange and terrible pain in my head, centered around my teeth somehow. To this day Iâ€™m not sure what it was. Soon it went away. The pilot announced that we would be landing shortly, and I tried through the clouds to see Chicago for the first time. \r\n</p><p>	I saw a perfectly regular grid of lights beneath the plane, and we landed. The airport was impressive. I looked around at the ordinary people going about their business, which seemed to be, unanimously, getting out of the airport. I saw husbands and wives, children, old people. They all seemed to glow with a strange light that Iâ€™d never seen before. I soon realized that they were all on the way home, or at least to a hotel; they all were heading somewhere at least vaguely familiarâ€¦if not home, then to a hotel or the home of a friend, or family memberâ€™s house. For the first time, I began to realize that maybe, I had made some sort of horrible mistake, but at that point I was so mentally exhausted that I couldnâ€™t really form any concrete images for very long. I wanted to go to sleep, in a bed, and pick this strange adventure up after a good nightâ€™s sleep, when I could get my feet under me and deal with things.  I smoked a final cigarette, and met the van driver in front of the terminal. The seven or so of us got in and drove through the snowy night to boot camp. </p><p>\r\nThe outskirts of Chicago on a cold night looked like the surface of an alien planet. Scrawny trees sprung up here and there, between concrete crumbling buildings and clouds of steam pouring from the earth like some sort of volcanic eruption. As we reached the entrance to Naval Training Center, Great Lakes, at about 12:15am, I began to feel sort of together again. I just needed to sleep, and when I awoke things would be better.<br><br>\r\nThe van drove between the squat, featureless concrete buildings for what seemed like a long time, and finally stopped.<br>\r\nÂ Â --\\\"you go in that door right there,\\\" said the cabdriver.<br>\r\nInside, normal people were standing around or sitting against the wall. The room looked like the entrance to a high school. It was warm. There were two obviously military people in black clothes with insignia on their arms, and a red braid running around their shoulders, underneath their arms. They occasionally woke the ordinary looking people up if they fell asleep. No one spoke.</p><p>\r\nOur small group was apparently the one everybody in charge had been waiting on. The military people spoke in clam, reasoned tones as they told us to line up in rows and call \\\"present\\\" when our names were read out. This accomplished, we led through the mostly-darkened building to a room full of pay phones, where we were told to call home and let our families know that we\\\'d arrived. I felt very far away from home. Still, I was kind of cavalier about the whole thing, trying to keep up my nerve. Soon, I would find out <i>exactly</i> how long I was capable of doing that. We were led, about forty of us, men and women, into what appeared to be a high school classroom, with desks and a dry-erase chalkboard. </p><p> \r\n\\\"Welcome to the Navy,\\\" a young woman in military clothes said calmly, as she handed out four or five paper forms for every person in the room at a desk. Calmly and in a projected voice, she walked us through the standard bubble forms that all of us had filled out many times up until this point. Name, social security number, birthdate, place of birth, nationality, height, weight, sex, blood type. A young, small woman raised her hand, and asked the military woman, in a timid voice, to repeat something she had said about one of the questions. <br>\r\nÂ Â --\\\"You\\\'d better get your fucking head out of your ass!\\\" <br>\r\nThe military woman was extremely upset all of a sudden. \r\nÂ Â --\\\"You\\\'d better get your head out of your fucking ass right now.\\\" <br>\r\nShe was nearly shaking with rage. The contrast to her earlier demeanor was so great that I feared I was having a hallucination. The room was silent. She looked around the room, hatred on her face...not faked hatred, either, but genuine ill will. She babbled on for a few more seconds. I thought she would start crying, but she got herself under control, and spoke more calmly. <br>\r\nÂ Â --\\\"If have weaknesses, you had better hide them. If you can\\\'t listen to <i>simple fucking directions</i>, you\\\'d better learn how. If you are a fat ass or a weakling, you\\\'d better do pushups in your sleep, because if your RDC finds out, if he finds out, he will break you down. He will destroy your mind, body, and soul. If you try to leave, you will go to prison for five years. We will hunt you down until we find you.\\\" She paused. \\\"Ok, let\\\'s move on. Form 242A.\\\"</p><p>\r\nMind, body and soul. I was looking at her...I could see that the woman in front of me was damaged, and I thought about what she said. <i>Soul.</i> I knew one thing for certain--my soul was just fine. So was my mind. And that woman was crazy. Still, I held on to the hope of sleep in the night ahead. It was one-thirty am. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\n<i>so it goes...</i>\r\n'),(3285,'<p>Listening to the new Yo La Tengo album \\\'Summer Sun\\\'.  Solemn with some really tight jams.</p>\r\n<p>While waiting for the eastbound College streetcar at Spadina I felt like Adam\r\n must when he is out stalking the city, killing people with his camera.  Casting a specific and slightly critical eye on the streetspace I found myself in.  I was noticing details which my subconscious usually does not send upwards to gain\r\nfair processing time.  Details such as the woman so engrossed in the conversation she was having that it took the distraction of stopping to wait for a street light to change before she realised that the cigarette she had been waving around previously was unlit.  The family whose car pulled up in front of the CIBC as if they were going inside but, instead, seemed to be talking the idea over for a few minutes before coming to a consensus to forget the whole plan and just drive away.  The look of frustration on the face of the cab driver who was attempting to make a U-turn and cursing the lacking of eyes in the back of his head.</p>\r\n<p>Riding the streetcar was interesting.  Sitting in the back of the crowded car and people-watching.  It made me want to spend a free day travelling on the streetcars downtown listening to good music.  Stopping occasionally to eat or investigate an interesting place.  It made me want to have a free day.</p>\r\n<p>I need to take advantage of this $95 piece of plastic in my wallet more often.</p>'),(3286,'<p>\r\nI waited around the house for ages for my ride to show up. linnea, her\r\nex-girlfriend elizabeth, philip seymour hoffman and todd haynes and i were\r\ngoing on a camping trip somewhere on the lost coast. they pulled up in a\r\ngold 1989 honda accord hatchback jobbie and honked. they had sleeping\r\nbags, a camping stove, blankets, water, blood oranges and a nice salad\r\nin the back. i climbed into the back with a pint of jack daniels, a red\r\nlipstick and a bikini top shoved in my back pocket. we drove and drove.\r\nthe landscape looked like a cross between the road to the point reyes\r\nlighthouse and 16th street going over potrero hill.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nfinally we got to a redwood forest, then a clearing, then a 2 room\r\nclapboard house. we parked in front of a wells fargo ATM. everyone got\r\nmoney. i didnt have any so linnea gave me a twenty and winked. my camping\r\nbuddies started unloading their gear to set up camp. i realized i didnt\r\nbring anything i needed. so i decided to go into the house. no one wanted\r\nto come with. i walked into the smaller room which was a wood-panelled laundry room with a water heater and a pantry thing. like you might have  \r\noff the kitchen. a bunch of my friends were hanging out in it drinking  \r\nbeer and talking. it looked like it was shaping up to be a fun party. i\r\nwas standing there, \\\'she\\\'s gone\\\' by hall and oates was playing and jenny\r\nyoung rushed in out of breath in a white dress. she looked nice. she   \r\nhugged me and didnt let go and told me that our friends sarah and ani were\r\nchasing her and if they found her they would beat her up. she said not to\r\ntalk to her because it would draw their attention. i told her not to\r\nworry, any number of us at the party could help her.\r\n</p>\r\n   \r\n<p>\r\nshe left and i helped myself to the guacamole and chips. i looked around\r\nfor the host to ask for the recipe. i was impressed. i went back outside \r\nto find my friends and they were all asleep on the car roof. i tried to\r\ntell them about the party but they didnt seem to care. i went back inside\r\na little sad. the big room of the house was all white, floor to ceiling. and a deep blue shag throw rug covered most of the floor. on one side of  \r\nthe room was a row of boys in black tuxes with blue ties. on the other  \r\nside were girls in blue and white dresses. they all matched the room. a\r\nslow song came on and they started doing a choreographed slow dance.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe whole night i was keyed up and ready to beat up ani and sarah in the\r\nevent that they tried to fuck with jenny. they never showed up. i had some\r\nlines in my head ready to say.\r\n</p>\r\n   \r\n<p>\r\ni went back outside at dawn, got my friends up and we left. the whole ride\r\nhome we kept driving up and down the same road. in one direction was a \r\nhuge dome and in the other were some towers. i couldn\\\'t tell which skyline\r\nbelonged to san francisco. i kept changing my mind. then i woke up.\r\n</p>'),(3287,'<p>\r\n<pre>\r\nwalking in spring. \r\n     my fingers being held. \r\n          how I love to walk in spring.\r\n</pre>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI woke up without the hangover I should have had. It was past noon, but just. As groggy as I was I rescued myself from floundering around any longer. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"wash, brush, and combing is for clean people\\\" I snarled as I pissed, coughed, smoked, coughed some more, and put my pants on and left my colorful apartment for the vivid browns and grays of my neighborhood. I had to get my mind off things. Me and her just couldn\\\'t get along the night before. I was hoping, as I flagged a taxi, that she had cooled down as much as I had; completely. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI headed for Kyobo books. I wanted to pick up a Lonely planet guide to Beijing, and Ho Chi Minh City (Saigon). Grabbing the guides and snarling again at their lofty prices, I headed on over to the poli-sci section to look at all the new books they had.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nKyobo books is a great place. In addition to being a great bookstore for both Korean(80% of the books) and English(15%. Id say the other 5% is Chinese/Russian/Japanese), the place is full of school, art, and office supplies. There are stalls that sell candy, food, and postcards and pc games. There is a rather large CD and DVD section. Perhaps the biggest feature of Kyobo that gets more of my attention than the product are the large quantities of energetic just out of school on a Saturday Korean school girls. Its jrhottie heaven. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBrowsing the poli-sci section is still a nice oasis from the school girl infested areas selling school girl type things. In the poli-sci section its usually just me(hah!). I found a book called \r\n<b><a href=\\\"http://images.amazon.com/images/P/0465011020.01.LZZZZZZZ.jpg\\\">The Aquariums of Pyongyang: Ten Years in the North Korean Gulag</b></a>. Im only ten pages into it, but the chapter titles seemed pretty thought provoking. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLeaving Kyobo, I thought I had left the majority of the school girls behind. To my suprise I turned the corner to see a line at least 500metres long of school girls. \\\"What in gods name is going on\\\" I thought to myself as I dragged my jaw past. They laughed and giggled at my baggy pants and messy hair. In spite of their attention most of the attention I get is from Koreans my age. Be it good or bad, everyone gets a kick out of the phat pants. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI called up my baby cakes and asked her to tea. She agreed and it was planned to meet up a couple of hours later. Having some time, I grabbed something to eat and checked my email in a pc bong on the fifth floor of a cracking building on the way towards millenium plaza. The bong was filled with business men and women teaching each other the in\\\'s and out\\\'s of hosting a shoutcast server and zenning over exel and powerpoint. I laughed. \\\"what a way to spend a saturday\\\" I said to one as I inched past him; hugging the wall. He had no idea what I meant. Isn\\\'t being in a far off land great?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMet up with Parohn and went for some Chinese Mondoo. Its good stuff. Then we had tea, chatted, and relaxed. She had something called JuJube Tea, and I had Top Leaf Green Tea. It was splendid. I prepared it in the Korean fashion.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe hit up a really hip store called DO ART. Its always filled with really neat pieces of art and sculpture from artists around Seoul. I found a camera with four lenses that puts four images on one negative. It takes the pictures at different times giving you an action sequence. I really hope the box isnt lying. Its cool. Only 55,000won.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFinishing up with Insadong it was decided I\\\'d walk Parohn back to her house in Haywah. Along the way we stopped off in a park and spun around on chrome colored device used by old peole to exercise their hips. It was the perfect chance to test out the new camera. (test roll still not done) We continued walking down the wide tree lined sidewalk towards the Haywah Rotary. As we walked a wall began to grow on either side of the street. We had no choice but to press on.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nParohn told me that to the left was the Royal Palace and she was astonished that I had not been there. I learned also that when the Japanese had colonized Korea they had turned the Royal Palace into a Zoo. I laughed and we made jokes. Politicians are animals. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe then attempted to create a Korean/Canadian Beatboxing duo. While the process of seeing what our capabilities were was hillarious and funny, we\\\'ve decided to retire to easier songs; mostly from the sound of music strangely enough. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nholding hands almost all the way to her house we walked, chatted, laughed, flirted, and had lots of good alone type fun. I had a great day. Leaving is going to be harder then Id hoped it would. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n '),(3288,'<p>Rise up my brothers and sisters! Shake off the shackles of sobriety! Today is anti-colonialism day! Today we attack the evils of imperialism! Today we do battle with the snakes of the state! Today our weapons are stout, suds and slops; brews, bocks and belly wash! Today we celebrate the strength and courage of the indigenous and autonomous people of this world in their battle to cleanse the poisonous colonial snakes from their land. In their war to exorcise the nefarious and destructive devils that raped their land and people. Today we drink out those demons that stole their wealth and freedom. Today is the day, my brothers and sisters that we rearm ourselves with pints and pitchers to fight the oppressors that still continue to exploit and occupy the soil of others.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You may be confused my brothers and sisters. You may be wondering how and when this day came to be. And when you look at your calendars, you will see that today is in fact St. Patrickâ€™s day, the holiest of all holiest days. Do not be dismayed my dear brothers and sisters, you are quite right, today is St. Patrickâ€™s Day. god bless his sacred, sloshed soul. Please remain calm and determined my brothers and sisters, I will explain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>god bless St. Patrick my brothers and sisters. He cast the snakes out of Ireland. Watch the slimly devils whither away into the sea. Watch the dirt burrow into their bellies. Watch them carefully my brothers and sisters, for while they may have been banished from Ireland, they still live freely among us. They have winded themselves around our necks into nooses. They have tightened their grips on our minds, suffocating free will and thought. They have grown arms and legs and suits and ties. Behind closed doors they dive back into dirt. Soiling the land and into mud and quicksand. Taking all and leaving nothing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But do not fear my brothers and sisters! For the time has come to banish them from our land. We will drink, vomit, shit and fuck them away! We will prevail! St. Patrick climbed Irelandâ€™s highest mountain in 449 AD to have a talk with god. For 40 days and nights St. Patrick argued with god to allow him and not St. Peter to judge the Irish on Judgment Day. Finally, god gave in. Many years ago I climbed Croagh Patrick to have a drink-off with our beloved saint. For 40 days and nights we drank many a Guinness. He smashed a bottle on my head, I smashed his head on a table, he smashed the table on my head. Finally, St. Patrick gave in and anti-colonialism day was born.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>March 17th is no longer a day just for the Irish. It is for the Scottish, the Africans, the Brazilians, the Natives, the Jamaicans, the Chechens, the Indians, the Basque, the Koreans, the Quebecois and yourselves. It is a day for all those that live under the rule of another.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Make no mistake my brothers and sisters, for we are not free. Self-empowerment has been cleverly stolen from ourselves under the guise of democracy. We are not free. Your condition is controlled. Your conscious is asleep. Today is a day for all to oppose the imperialist policies of America, Britain, China and Russia. It is a day to remember the systematic genocide of people. It is a day to remember the theft of cultures for the greed of others. It is a day to where shared drinks are treaties between autonomous people to remain free and help free their brothers and sisters. Free them from their oppressors. Grant them freedom from the press, freedom from religion, and freedom from conscience. Free their minds my brothers and sisters and you will free yourself. Now go and drink up</p>\r\n'),(3289,'i came to this conclusion perhaps a month or two ago: i no longer want to be a programmer. the joy that programming once brought me has been lost to the monotony of revisions that seem unnecessary the pure excitement i derived from creating something in a clear and logical way isn\\\'t there anymore. perhaps it is because i am not really programming as such, but mostly doing what i already knew i disliked: database management. <br><br>\n \n i am inherently good at information technology, organizing data in sensible ways, but i find nothing interesting about it. i never have. but this is what i find myself doing regularly. in the end it\\\'s all the same anyway. i can\\\'t live my life in fields, columns and sql statements. it\\\'s driving me mad.<br><br> \n \n so i\\\'ve decided to go back to school, as perhaps many of you know. at first i was planning to take computer science, but i\\\'ve had second thoughts on the subject, mostly because of my recent realization that i no long have the love for it that i once did. i think i might be partly wrong in feeling the loss, as computer science isn\\\'t computer programming. regardless, i am unsure if it\\\'s where i want to be anymore. <br><br>\n \n but what has intrigued me is human thought process. it has for a long time. i\\\'ve always felt like i was looking in on the world around me, not so much a part of it. i know on a conscious level that i am part of the system i percieve, but at the same time, i feel abstract from the people around me in a way that i can\\\'t easily explain. i\\\'ve constantly wondered why people do what they do, why they act how they act. i desire to understand not so much on a psychosomatic level, but more on a cohesive level. <br><br>\n \n an example: man watches commercial, desires cheeseburger. why? what was it? the images? the symbols? the language? the sound? what was it? i don\\\'t know, and i feel the need to find out. not on just a purely marketing level, that\\\'s far too pedestrian. that is just an application of what i want to know about humanity; the end, not the means. <br><br>\n \n but at the same time, i still do have my love for computers. technology is changing so fast, but i am unsure if we\\\'re doing the right things to make it so human anymore. my mom doesn\\\'t even use a computer yet, yet my grandfather uses one daily (mind you, he hasn\\\'t got internet, got to work on that one). what is it about computers that she dislikes? <br><br>\n \n technological devices are becoming so ubiquitous to us now, i just feel the need to find a better place for those devices. i must find a better way to integrate it into our lives. i desire to look at how we, as people, think about how we use things and shape the ideas of technology creators to think in those terms instead.<br><br>'),(3290,'<p>\r\nDeclaring war on terror is like declaring war on the number two: they both exist in the mind.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTerrorism - the act - is a form of asymmetrical warfare: it is a means for the weak to combat the strong.  How then will weakening the conventional forces of a state make its citizens less likely to engage in acts of terrorism against those that that they perceive to be their enemies?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt has been said that attacks have been made upon American targets as the result of the attacker\\\'s hatred of the American way of life - and freedom in particular.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut it seems that there is another plausible explanation: American arms, money, advisors, technology, and sanctions, have been used to kill, maim, starve and otherwise injury the children and other relations of those who now take up arms against America.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd so by causing further injury to a people, and weakening them, we hope to reduce either the root cause of their grievance, or their likelihood to turn to acts of terrorism as a means of vendetta?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt seems to be true that within Iraq there are many unconventional weapons: chemical and biological.  But, the same might be said of many other states - like Russia.  And against the other states American has not struck, or threatened to strike - despite the fact that they engage in the sale of munitions.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe military force that exists within Iraq was created with American assistance.  The American government invested billions of dollars in the Iraqi military during the Iran-Iraq War of 1980-1988.  In fact, after the Iran-Iraq War the Iraqi military was several times larger than it was before the war - despite its losses.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhatever sort of monster exists within Iraq is a monster that was - at least in part - made by America.  Of course, America sold arms to Iran too.  And it appears to many observers that the goal of said policy was a war of attrition between the two powers.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEstimates vary, but there is no question that between 1 and 3 million people were killed or wounded in the Iran-Iraq War; roughly the same number were made homeless; hundreds of billions of damage was done to the economies of the warring states.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFollowing the Iran-Iraq War, Iraq turned its weapons in a different direction.  America stopped funding, and started bombing.  In 1991 America decimated Iraq in the Gulf War.  During that same year, sanctions were imposed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs a result of the sanctions imposed on Iraq, roughly 1/4 of its children are chronically malnourished today.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA significant percentage of the population of Iraq has never known anything other than war or starvation: it has - essentially - been at war constantly, for the past 23 years.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Iran-Iraq War took place while George H. W. Bush was Vice President of the United States: from 1980-1988.  The Gulf War took place while George H. W. Bush was President of the United States: in 1991.  And all appearances are that the second Gulf War will take place while George W. Bush - the son of George H. W. Bush - is the President of the United States: in 2003.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn a nation of 290 million roughly equal citizens, the son of the last Republican President received the Republican Party nomination to run for the presidency.  And after having received said office in an unusual manner, he appears ready to make war upon the nation that his father made war upon.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut what are the limits of the new war?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTerror - like the knowledge of how to create it - exists in the mind.\r\n</p>'),(3291,'blank.'),(3405,'<p>\r\nThe past makes me sore<br>\r\nBut I can\\\'t help but adore<br>\r\nMy sadness, my sad mess<br>\r\nMy head on the floor.<br>\r\nThe past makes me sore<br>\r\nSo I heard you explored<br>\r\nMy stories with my house keys<br>\r\nMy photos and my memories<br>\r\nThere\\\'s history galore<br>\r\nBut you know, the past makes me sore.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m obsessed with my past.  I hold on to things like childhood picture books and ticket stubs and school reports and birthday cards and sometimes I hold them and I remember what it was like, back then.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m obsessed with my past.  I treasure photo albums and home videos and I can look at them and laugh, and wonder if one day my children will look at them too, and think that once I was pretty.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m obsessed with my past.  I collect events in my head, things to hold against myself and against other people.  I can still hear the things I\\\'ve said and the echoing words others have said to me.  I can be as angry as I was a year ago when he pushed me over.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m obsessed with my past.  I hoard my mistakes, so dirty, so dirty.. I think about them and hope that no one notices the look on my face.  I fear they will find me out, find out that I too can be cruel.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m obsessed with my past.  I want it back and I want it to go away.  I draw from it and I want to cut it up into little pieces and throw them into the wind.  I can\\\'t help this.  I can\\\'t help not thinking about tomorrow.  I\\\'m obsessed.\r\n</p>'),(3406,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3406_2.jpg\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3406_3.jpg\\\">'),(3407,'	<table border=\\\"0\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" cellpadding=\\\"10\\\">\r\n		<tr>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"400\\\">\r\n					this never happened. i don\\\'t know you.  this is not a memory.\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\\\"3\\\" align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n				<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"400\\\">\r\n					blessed am i. blessed am i the idle.  blessed am i the walker the lie maker. blessed am i the less sleeper, the less worker, the less feared. blessed am i the watcher. blessed am i.\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\\\"3\\\" align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n				<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"400\\\">\r\n					summer months. i wait until later afternoon, i go outside with my camera. i wear shorts and a black t-shirt. sometimes a green one. the sun bakes into my arms.\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td colspan=\\\"3\\\" align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n				<img src=\\\"%%img3%%\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"400\\\">\r\n					i find someone to look at and then we agree to divert memory together, get our story straight between us and nobody else. this is not what happened.  this is what i saw.\r\n				</td>\r\n				<td width=\\\"50\\\">\r\n					&nbsp;\r\n				</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3408,'<P>\r\nHead banging is really gratifying when you have long hair, thought\r\nAntony.  Swords crashed on shields.  The firebird sped through the\r\nnight, headlights on the road.  Antony banged his head.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNext to him, his girlfriend Cleopatra, 5 weeks pregnant, hung her head\r\nchin to breast and outstretched her arms left out the t-top\r\nright out the window. Devil signs made on both.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIntermittently their hair danced, mingled, and was sucked out the\r\nt-top roof, licking the night sky.  Headed home drunk after another\r\nnight of trying to keep Cleopatra sober for the baby, there were no\r\nquestions in the air, only loud affirmations of death.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe tape deck ate the tape.  Antony cursing hit the dash board.\r\nGod damned tape player!   That was his favorite tape.  The firebird swerved.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAntony you better drive safe, there\\\'s a baby on board!  They laughed.\r\nIt didn\\\'t make any sense at all, a baby.  That is what Cleopatra\\\'s mom\r\nsaid.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen Antony first heard the news, he was shocked.  The words of the\r\nstrip mall security guard who busted him for making out with his\r\ngirlfriend years ago came to mind.  It was a long winded story about\r\nhow he should not make the same mistakes the security guard had.\r\nDon\\\'t let your little head think for your big head, he said.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIt was too late for thinking though, or maybe that was all there was\r\nleft to do.  Anyway, Antony was a doer, and he did Cleopatra.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAntony\\\'s mom was happy.  She unpacked all the old 1970\\\'s baby toys\r\nfrom the uncle\\\'s U-Store-It.  Her motherly intuition told her they\r\nwould come in handy someday; lime green, pastel orange, perfect 4\r\npetal flowers in different faded soft tones.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAntony\\\'s uncle was retiring and turning over the U-Store-It\r\nwatchman position with the accompanying apartment to Antony.  They\r\nwould need the stability and the room to raise the baby.  That is\r\nwhy.  Also, the uncle was ready to move out to Lake Palestine and spend\r\nall his evenings and mornings fishing and checking trot lines.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAntony and Cleopatra laughed in the car.  Things were just beginning.\r\nNeither was abusive or jealous.  They liked each other.  Some might\r\ncall it love.  They were going to get married after the baby so\r\nCleopatra could wear her mother\\\'s wedding dress.\r\n</P><P>\r\nTwas the perfect temperature for a summer night.  Headlights\r\nilluminated the tree cover and the road, forming a tunnel.  Branches\r\nlike fingers closed on them and disappeared faster than they appeared.\r\nVery little future and no past, they drove on.  Every second a bad\r\nending became good.  Bugs hit the windshield.  Antony and Cleopatra\r\nlistened to the hum of the exhaust.\r\n</P><P>\r\nTimes like these, Death could take an interest and out of compassion\r\nkill the lovers sending them directly to Heaven.\r\n</P>'),(3292,'III.\r\n<br><br>\r\nIt was really late. Really, really late. We left the classroom and were quickly separated by sex. Everyoneâ€™s eyes were red and frightened as the thought played over and over, <I>not going home tonight, not going home tonight.</I> Itâ€™s not like I was totally prepared for the ideaâ€”I had been living with that for six months with no problem. It was the reality, the finality of it that took me by surprise. I hadnâ€™t expected to feel it so much, somehow; for it to feel so permanent. I saw the tiny time differential pass by, wanted to reach out and grab it, but it was already quickly receding around the bend. The future was far murkier in every respect then as it ever has been since. \r\n<br><br>\r\nInto another room, this one just a large room with a concrete floor. The cinderblock walls were painted red and blue. Just red, just blue. Red on top. The ceiling was a regular drop-ceiling, with long, bright fluorescent tubes. There was no furniture. Against one wall was a huge stack of flattened cardboard boxes. We were lined up side by side in rows, about 5 rows of fifteen guys. Boxes were passed down, which we assembled. \r\n<br><br>\r\nTaking off all your clothes in front of a bunch of other guys for the first time is a lot like losing your virginity. \r\n<br><br>\r\nWe placed everything but our wristwatches and wallets into our boxes. Sweats were passed out, and bathing suits, which would serve as underwear for the time being. We were given tennis shoes and socks. I studied the official military people, the way they stood, so wide awake compared to our waking death faces. They seemed perfectly comfortable amidst our extremity, and this was frightening to me for reasons I couldnâ€™t quite and still perhaps cannot fully explain. The stood about, not talking, watching us. The one in front giving out the commands did not speak but to clarify a command here and there. We were allowed to keep our wallets. \r\n<br><br>\r\nFrom the room we went out again into the darkened beige concrete of the hallway, with frosted glass windows. I began to slip mentally right about then. Looking at the windows, I knew that whatever was outside them was even more desolate and devoid of the comforts of my old like than the room inside, which felt like the calm, organized waiting room for Hell. We were told to take jackets from a large cotton-walled bin, and the ski masks were handed around along with gloves and long black raincoats. Suited up, we headed outside into the orange light and the orange snow and the silence. We were led across a large open concrete plaza into the darkness, no lights but from the small buildings ahead. We stumbled through the snow, bouncing off each other without a word, like prisoners of war in a cattle car. Fantasy, for once, failed me completely as the world around me began to break down the walls around my mind, eroding years of automatic judgment of my surroundings; I no longer sorted things into categories in my mind. Here, a street light. There, a three story building. We seemed to be headed towards it. A bush. A hundred identical, featureless statue-men stumbling in the dark reached the buildings, and went in. \r\n<br><br>\r\nBunks, lockers. Locks and keys for valuables. A shave in a darkened, dripping room, and for the first time slight murmurs of small talk. Without my watch, time had stopped. I barely had time to put away my razor and tiny kit when a fat man in uniform, red braid over his shoulder came in and announced his role as our leader â€œfor a few days.â€ We went outside, sweatclothes and all,  heading for breakfast in the middle of the night. \r\n'),(3293,'<p>Sue is fine. She came to me today, pale and sick like you described her. Like you left her.</p>\r\n<p></p>	\r\n<p>She came to me because she did not know where else, or who else, to come to. You can no longer hurt her. You can no longer touch her. She is broken but we can heal her. She can be fixed. Your damage is not permanent; your stain is not forever. </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>She tried to tell me today about how you came to find her. She tried but she choked on her words. The holes in her arm spoke more than she could. She said at first your place was warm compared to the wet streets where she spent her nights. She was like so many of the others, the lost and the forgotten. She told me that in the beginning you made as if to help her. She said that you promised her all. You told her you loved her. </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>This is love: she showed me the rope burns that encircled her frail wrists. She described the room where she was kept and the chair to which she was attached. </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>Sue is scared. She can\\\'t stop remembering you. She wants to forget. She told me how you would sleep at her feet at night, mumbling and whimpering in your dreams. She described how you gorged yourself on foods of all kinds and how you would sob in her lap afterwards. You became obese. She stopped eating. You sickened her. She wanted to die. She said you screamed at her. You demanded to know why she was punishing you. She said you would thrash about the apartment, destroying everything around you. The walls were speckled with your blood, the carpet embedded with glass. When the rage subsided, you would beg forgiveness.   </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>She says you are deluded. You spoke of her and you as a team. She said when you came back from the outside you would present your scavenges as trophies, as triumphs. When she offered nothing in response the cycle of violence and repentance would begin again.  </p>\r\n<p></p>\r\n<p>Sue is fine. She closes her eyes now and tries to cast your image from her mind. She remembers the coldness of your hands; the scars that ran up your arms and into your soul and grasped for her. Sue wants to cry but she no longer can. There are no more tears left for you, for what you have done to her. Sue has rid herself of your love. Sue is fine.</p>\r\n'),(3294,'<p>\r\nLet\\\'s talk about this war for a second. I have been seeing a lot of really bullshit commentary on CNN as of late. I saw a three minute piece on \\\"How CNN Will Budget The War\\\". They talked about all sorts of stupid crap. I don\\\'t see why anyone would be interested in how CNN is going to pay for their war coverage. It would be interesting to find out how much advertising costs for CNN have gone up now that their ratings are going to go through the roof. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHave you heard the jingle? I havn\\\'t, but let me just say that I can\\\'t wait to hear it. The CNN jingle for the first gulf war was pretty good. Wouldn\\\'t you say? It was intense and made me feel like starting my own war! I hope to God CNN budgets for their new jingle. Don\\\'t let me down, CNN!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey should get James Earl Jones back into the studio to record \\\"THIS IS WAR N N\\\" in his Darth Vader voice.  Also, why doesn\\\'t Saddam have a jingle? He should. I\\\'m almost positive he would have a chance if he had a jingle playing over and over on Iraqi TV. Maybe that boxing/fighting announcer yelling into the mic \\\"Let\\\'s get ready to Jihad\\\"!  Bin Laden should have had a jingle too, a cover to John Denver\\\'s Rocky Mountain classic called, \\\"Afghan Mountain High-ding\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe King of Bahrain said he\\\'d let Saddam camp out in one of his palaces. I\\\'ve heard rumours that even North Korea offered the man a mountain to live on with his family. Living on a mountain might not sound that great, but have you seen some of the cool pads one can build on the side of a mountain? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe best and most alarming thing I have heard in recent days are the countless Kuwaiti citizens, politicians, and hacks, demanding the US go into Iraq and free its population. They use words like \\\'freedom\\\' and \\\'democracy\\\'. They act as if they live in the United States themselves, but in all actuality they live in a Kingdom. They live in a Kingdom where they have no real freedom to organize politically.  They don\\\'t elect their king. They don\\\'t have a say in what goes on in their own country, but they talk of what should be in Iraq. I laugh. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nGo on America, go forth and free them from their chains!!! God Blell you all.\r\n</p>'),(3295,'IV.<br><br>\r\nThe next week and a half or so is hard to recall with any clarity. I remember standing in hallways. I remember seeing a bunch of young kids in the Navy\\\'s bellbottom jeans work uniform, with a blue shirt. We remained in our sweatpants and sweatshirts, and were not given the opportunity to bathe. Shaving was about as far as our personal hygiene went, and even that had to be directed by the fat, redfaced, yet sort of pleasant man who was our leader. He was a mother hen, in my scarce memory. No one really asked him a lot of questions about anything; we were all just too damned tired to think. Each day was devoted to sitting in a classroom, waiting to fill out forms and them filling them out, and endless string of forms. Life insurance. Medical histories. A psychological examination, which I failed on purpose. It didn\\\'t help. I thought of every possible disqualifying feature I possessed or might possess: migraine headaches, back problems, flat feet. The Navy was determined to have me. It was odd; before joining the Navy, when I was going through the pre-admission process, it had seemed as if they were doing everything they could to find a disqualifying feature, and now it was exactly the opposite. I think I would have had to have been dead to get their attention.<br><br>\r\nWhen I was talking to the Navy recruiter, many months before, I asked him a lot of questions about boot camp, and he tried to answer as well as he could, considering that it had been about fifteen years since he had been there, back in the early 80\\\'s. All the official literature on the subject would say was that boot camp was eight weeks long. Eight weeks. It doesn\\\'t sound like a very long time to sweat out, no matter how bad it was. After about a week and a half, through my fatigue and mental horror, I became aware that our eight weeks hadn\\\'t even started yet. That\\\'s right--it seemed as if it had been a million years since I\\\'d gotten off of that plane, and crossing the one-week mark I had been elated, for about five minutes at least, but it was something to hold on to. But now...now even that too was gone. We were in \\\'P Days,\\\' preparation days, getting checked out again more thoroughly for possible defects. No one knew, or no one was making it clear, when the P days would end. Weeks. Perhaps as long as a month. <br><br>\r\nI don\\\'t remember sleeping even one night, though I\\\'m sure I must have at some point. I do know that we didn\\\'t get to take showers. I didn\\\'t eat for about nine days or so, well into the second week at least. I wasn\\\'t hungry. I didn\\\'t take a shit for at least a month. Didn\\\'t have to. All I did was breathe, think, and look around. I became more and more convinced that the plane had crashed, and that was why I had woken up confused and in pain. I was dead. This was sort of a processing and purification center for the soul, a place where one went to shed his or her body in preparation perhaps for the afterlife, or for reincarnation. I did my best to let go and die. Life was devoid of all meaning for me anymore, and there was no companionship except the hollow eyes of the young men around me and the blank, businesslike eyes of the Mother Hen, like a robot; an automatic program designed to do nothing more than shuffle our group from one stop to the next. The men in the rooms, handing out the forms, said nothing to us other than to wake us up when we passed out, pencil in hand, at the desks, and to read the directions to us. <br><br>\r\nP days lasted two weeks. One day after lunch, at which I had eaten a few grapes and half a piece of bread, we entered a new building, turned a corner, and were in a room filled with bunkbeds and lockers, which looked to hold about 100 people. It was identical to the room we had occasionally slept and shaved in up until then, but it was oddly different. After a few minutes, sitting there on the floor, waiting again for something to happen, it struck me. It was bright inside. In our old room, we had never been there when the sun was shining, and I didn\\\'t even realize that the opaque plastic panels on the walls had actually been windows. The Mother Hen walked outside, and immediately we were all asleep, sitting upright on the floor.'),(3296,'<p>\r\nmorning of fri, 03/21/2003.  All of this is one dream, the transitions\r\nbetween the 3 parts I can remember were very very fuzzy.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI was taking a piss in the smaller bathroom here (just a toilet, no\r\nmirror/sink/etc, very small room.)  The room was slightly different\r\nthough.  Screens covered the window and door (not the one that lead to\r\nthe hallway.  one that lead outside) I could hear tim and steph and\r\nlots of other people outside talking.  They were in plain view through\r\nthe screen door.  That was good - they\'re home, they have my food,\r\nand I was extremely hungry.  I wanted to yell at them \"get my food in\r\nhere, i\'m starving\", but figured I should wait until I was done\r\npeeing.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThen I saw someone walking up the two steps to the screen door.  She\r\nlooked just like the female lead from La Femme Nikita, although not as\r\nthin, and had a nice french accent.  She opened the screen door and\r\nstood just behind me for a second before saying \"You know this is the\r\ngirls bathroom?\"  to which I responded \"Uh, no it\'s not.\"  She got all\r\nhuffy and stomped out into the hallway, saying under her breath \"now\r\ni\'ll have to wash my hands.\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI finish peeing, flush, and open the hall door and walk out.  I can\r\nhear her complaining in the next bathroom down the hall.  Her\r\ncomplaints seem to center around the soap she\'s using, so I figure\r\nshe\'s using charity\'s smiley face soap near the sink.  I open the\r\ndoor, walk behind her at the sink, lean into the shower and grab the\r\nbar of lever 2000 moisture response.  (The soap was wet, recently\r\nused..  and it was split longitudinally in a way you don\'t usually see\r\nlever 2000 wearing down..) I hold out the soap to her saying \"here,\r\nuse this, that bar is just for show.\"  She stops for a second, looking\r\nkinda stunned that I\'d care, snatches the soap from me and starts\r\nwashing her hands again, and continues complaining.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nFor some reason I\'m feeling confident around this girl, and I lean up\r\nagainst the sink and say \"why\'re you always so mean to me?  i\'m\r\nnothing but nice to you.\"  She looks up from her washing and flashes\r\nthis wonderful, wonderful smile.  I lean in, and she turns around and\r\nlifts herself up onto the counter facing me.  My lips keep following\r\nhers as she leans back until her head rests on the mirror and I catch\r\nup.  We kiss.  I pull her to an upright sitting position.  We kiss\r\nsome more.  I pull her off the counter.  We kiss some more.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThen out in the hallway I hear people talking, an older man sounding\r\nupset, complaining to someone that \"that\'s just not done around here!\"\r\nI pull back a little, look into her eyes and whisper \"I think he\'s\r\ntalking about us.\"  Her eyes smile and she says \"I know\" and kisses me\r\nagain.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>----</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m being sold a car..  but I already own one.  This woman is trying\r\nto sell me a car.  She\'s nice in every other way I notice.  She has\r\nmidwestern housewife hair, a midwestern housewife body, exudes \"1\r\ngeneration at most out of trailor parks\".  I tell her I\'m not sure I\r\nwant another car.. well, that I definitely don\'t want to own *two*,\r\nbut that I\'m not completely against the idea of replacing my current\r\ncar (jetta) with something with all wheel drive.  But all that is\r\nbeside the point.  She\'s selling, I didn\'t come in here looking for a\r\ncar.  I feel like I\'m being led through the process without my consent.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI feel vaguely guilty that, even though I\'m not in control of things,\r\nMike (I think that was his name... Rocky\'s friend that used to work at\r\nRoyal Motors in SF) would feel betrayed that I went to a different\r\nsalesman for my next vehicle.  I go over in my head what I\'ll say to\r\nhim to smooth his ruffled feathers.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman is saying \"well let\'s just draw up the papers and we\'ll get\r\neverything set up so that if you want to, you just sign on the dotted\r\nline and we\'re done.  and if you don\'t want it you just say \'no\'\".  I\r\nask if I can just say \'no\' now and save her the work.  She doesn\'t\r\nanswer, too busy typing on her computer.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nShe gets to the end and a stack of carbon separated copies of a\r\ncontract appears before me with X\'s in the right places.  I look down\r\nand see the first two digits of the price (which is 5 digits).\r\n\"$66,...\"  I stand up, walk around the chair I was sitting in, and...\r\nwalk around the store.  Why is it so hard to say \'no\'?  I can\'t afford\r\nthat.  I want her to understand why, so I follow her to the break\r\nroom, and hop up onto the counter.  She\'s there with her friend.  I\r\nsay to her \"I\'d like to, but I can\'t.  There\'s just no way.  I\'m\r\nplanning to go back to school soon.. to sfsu, berkeley, or something\r\nelse around here.  I\'ll need my money for that.  You understand,\r\nright?\"  She looks like she doesn\'t, but before she can tell me why,\r\nI\'m rescued by my friend yeti who has been waiting for me to extricate\r\nmyself from this situation, and has clearly figured out that I can\'t\r\non my own.  He walks up, says \"hey chris, let\'s go?\"  And we leave.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>----</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m walking along the interior of a long, thin mall of some sort..  It\r\nlooks almost like the inside of a bank, with velvet ropes, potted\r\nplants..  but there are no tellers.  There is a shiny car parked on a\r\npedestal along the walkway.  I shift the little boy from my left hip\r\nto my right and walk past the car.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m taking care of him for a friend.. he\'s not mine.  He seems utterly\r\nterrified of me.  Well terrified enough to not talk at all (I know he\r\n*can*...)  So we just walk along, do a little window shopping, I point\r\nout the interesting things..  For some reason the women I pass (who\r\nare all rather stunning in a conventional, hollywood way) are staring\r\nat me... not the \"you have toilet paper stuck to your shoe\" looks,\r\neither.  They\'re .. almost into me for some reason.  I guess it\'s the\r\nkid, but I really have no clue.  I\'m not complaining.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAfter walking around for a long time, I can tell he\'s getting tired.\r\nHe still hasn\'t spoken, but he\'s warming up to me a little.  I ask\r\n\"you ready to leave?\" and feel his nod under my chin.  \"you have a\r\ngood time?\"  again, a nod.  \"good, well let\'s get you back to your\r\nparents.\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWe walk out into the parking lot.  It\'s night, and there are very few\r\ncars in the parking lot.  \"no wonder he\'s tired, it must be really\r\nlate,\" I think.  We walk to my car, which for some reason isn\'t a\r\njetta but a subaru hatchback (yay awd!).  I stick my key in the back\r\nand the door lifts up.  I put the kid down into the back of the car\r\nand move to grab a sweater.  That\'s when I notice a long haired blonde\r\nman sitting in the front passenger seat facing forward, and a curly\r\nhaired woman rifling through a milk crate in the back seat.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\"Hey!\" I yell, \"what the hell do you think you\'re doing?  get out of\r\nmy car!\"  She looks shocked, and the guy opens the door and steps out.\r\nI move over to the driver\'s side backseat door and stand between her\r\nand the car and say \"what the fuck are you doing?  get out of here\"\r\nand grab my stuff from her and throw it back into the backseat.  As I\r\ndo that she runs around the car to the passenger side, front seat\r\ndoor.  I reach through the car to grab stuff out of her hands and yell\r\n\"cut this shit out\" at her.  I stand up outside the car, fully intent\r\non throwing her physically into her own car and turn and see the man\r\nstanding right in front of me.  He looks like he\'s thinking in slow\r\nmotion...  he sizes me up, decides he doesn\'t want to try, and turns\r\naround and walks quickly to his car.  The woman, however, is still\r\nplaying this fucking cat and mouse game with me.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI reach through the car and lock the door she just opened (passenger\r\nside, front seat).  I\'m totally stretched out, from back seat to\r\nfront.  That, coupled with all the stuff in my car makes it difficult\r\nto move.  The boy has climbed into the front seat and is watching the\r\naction.  I realize that the door I lunged through is still open, and\r\nsee through the window that she\'s already running around the car to\r\nget to it.  I\'m suddenly very scared.. unable to really move, and with\r\nthis psycho woman running around to my exposed backside.  She runs in\r\nfront of the headlights and when the light flashes onto her face I get\r\na little shock - she doesn\'t look like a person anymore (it occurs to\r\nme then that I hadn\'t ever really seen her in the light.)  Her skin is\r\ndark gray and horribly wrinkled.  Her hair is white.  she looks very\r\nold, moves very fast and strong, and looks very scary.  The door slams\r\nshut (the one I lunged through).  I\'m not sure why..  She must have\r\nslammed it shut.  She\'s still running around the car.  I can\'t move at\r\nall.  There\'s no room to move my legs to get them under me, and I\r\ncan\'t reach the door behind me, nor for some reason the door that I\'d\r\nclosed on her earlier.  She runs in front of the headlights again,\r\nand...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI wake up with a jerk, sweaty.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3297,'<p>\r\nIn October of 2002 I met a wonderful person. She was someone I enjoyed spending time with immensely. She was beautiful, smart, sexy, and stylish. I really dug her. We had fun going out for drinks, walking through the city, and spending time together and with friends. She was my blueberry muffin. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt is now March of 2003 and everything above sounds true. I still care about her a lot. I\\\'m still smitten. I was her bear. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe used to be confused about how she felt about me, but I\\\'ve noticed her affection fading as the new year, and her business trips have passed. More arguments. More uncomfortable moments when I dont know what to say to make things better. Instead of being someone to come to for comfort, I turned into stress and headache. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI woke up today with one of the worst hangovers in my life. In spite of being dumped by way of a text msg on my handphone, It was Tina\\\'s going away party last night. Tina was a co-worker whose contract had finished a few weeks ago. Tomorrow she is leaving to go teach in Thailand. Needless to say, the night was filled with Shots, Beer, and cheap Makoli. Makoli is sold in these 1.5L plastic bottles by men walking the streets with pushcarts. Its cheap. Its strong. It flowed from the cart and into our gullets. I got SHITFACED. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dont really remember last night all too well, but I do know I lost something very dear to me. I really feel guilty for losing her. There is a definate void within my mind today, and I miss her. I just didnt want it to end like this. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nBeing with her for just over six months taught me a lot about relationships. I uncovered and discovered things about myself and others that would not have been possible without her. We had what I thought was something real and very special, but I guess I was proved wrong last night. I didnt see it coming. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy mind is a flutter of activity and emotions right now, and it is causing me great pain. Not in the ways Ive written about in the past; when I was more fragile and naive. My mind is a flutter with just pure sadness. I am not angry. I am not bitter. I do not have anything negative to say about her. This is why it\\\'s so hard for me to let go of the strong feelings I have for her. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3298,'In a <a href=\\\"http://www.gallup.com/poll/releases/pr030319.asp\\\">March 14-15 CNN/USA Today/Gallup poll</a>, the top seven <b>REASONS IN FAVOR OF TAKING MILITARY ACTION AGAINST IRAQ</b> given by ordinary Americans were:\r\n<p>\r\n<b>1. <em>Iraq prevented from using weapons of mass destruction/providing to terrorists</b></em>\r\n<p>\r\nFirst, there is no clear evidence (yet) that Iraq possesses weapons of mass destruction.  Even if they do, they are at worst disobeying the mandate of the U.N. Security Council - an organization the U.S. government clearly does not deem important.  The U.S. government is going against the majority of the nations in the U.N. Security Council in prosecuting this war. Second, there is no credible evidence linking the Iraqi government to Al Qaeda - there is, however, plenty of evidence pointing to the fact that the two groups hate each other.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>2. <em>The Iraqi people would be freed from the rule of Saddam Hussein</em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nTrue, I agree.  But there are <em>plenty</em> of other countries living under dictatorial regimes, many of them oppressive, and many of them currently ruling with the tacit support of the U.S. government.  This does not justify singling out Iraq for conquest.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>3. <em>U.S. must play a leadership role in world by standing up for what it believes is right </em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nOr: we must be right because we believe we are right (oh, and because we\\\'re a superpower), so quiet down.  Maintaining the U.S. government\\\'s perceived world \\\"role\\\", or image, or whatever you want to call it, is not a good reason for people to die.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>4. <em>It would encourage political and economic reform in the Arab world</em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nWell, the CIA isn\\\'t sure about this claim.  And, while I don\\\'t know nearly as much about world politics as the CIA, I\\\'m not sure about it either.  Its basic premise is that the U.S. will stabilize the region by bringing war to it.  In addition - look at that word \\\"economic\\\".  Since when do we fight wars to open up markets?\r\n<p>\r\n<b>5. <em>Oil supplies in the Middle East would be protected and stabilized</em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nOkay: no blood for oil.  Oil is simply not a good reason for Iraqi civilians and American soldiers to die.  Plus, even if we never got another drop of oil from Iraq, we\\\'d still be fine, energy-wise.  After Saudi Arabia, the U.S. is the largest producer of oil in the world.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>6. <em>Groups that hate the U.S. would be discouraged from attacking the U.S.</em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nRight.  Are you kidding me?  In this war, America is being viewed as an imperialist power fighting a war of conquest to secure oil for itself.  This war is flaming the fire of anti-American hatred throughout the Arab world.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>7. <em>Fuel prices in the U.S would be lower in the long run</em></b>\r\n<p>\r\nWell: no blood for oil.  Saving a couple bucks when you fill up your car is not a good reason for anyone - Iraqis or Americans - to die in the desert.\r\n<p>\r\nThere is no good reason for war in Iraq.  American troops and Iraqi civilians are dying for that - for no good reason.  Join the protests to help stop this war.'),(3299,'<p>\r\nI made a pot of coffee, and sat down with my thoughts.\r\nI sat at the desk that I made to hold the computer. \r\nThe computer was like an old nag that I couldn\\\'t ignore, and yet didn\\\'t want to touch.\r\nWhatever I was looking for, I wasn\\\'t going to find it in that annoying glass and plastic bitch-box.\r\nShe wasn\\\'t there for consummation - only frustrating bits of banter and half-cruel, half-knowing, half-public displays.\r\nI shouldn\\\'t have bought the computer; I shouldn\\\'t have made the desk; and I shouldn\\\'t have been drinking coffee.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI had the usual internal debates about suicide, priesthood, and other unhealthy things; I considered deleting my e-self.\r\nAs I began to write, I imagined the typical questions and lack of response that would follow.\r\nAnd seeing the end from the beginning, I wondered why I should start.\r\nThe itching-ache in my gums and teeth increased.\r\nI took the Sex Pistols disc out of the drive, and put in something new.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore often than not, my days begin that way.  \r\nIn all honesty, I\\\'m confused.\r\nAnd the harder that I search for meaning and purpose in life, the more elusive it seems.\r\nI find myself keenly aware of the fact that I exist for only a little while, and in an imperfect state; after that, I don\\\'t know what.  \r\nMy belief in God is sustained in no small part by the horror of the prospect that we suffer and then fade into nothingness.\r\nBecause even if religion, or astrology, is synthetic, science is too.\r\nAnd I don\\\'t think that science is my friend.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs my coffee grows cold, the disc in the drive repeats: Samuel Barber\\\'s <i>Adagio for Strings</i> and <i>Second Essay for Orchestra</i> fill the room in that order.\r\nThe clouds in the sky thicken, and then thin.\r\nThe sun emerges, and then is concealed.\r\nIt\\\'s a nice sort of metaphor for the progress of my life, thus far.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere\\\'s a frustrating tension between the personal knowledge that we\\\'re not supposed to be attached to the things of this world, and a social order designed to promote the gradual accumulation of real, worldly, property.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s becoming increasingly difficult to support, and be a part of, something that I don\\\'t believe in.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need a new epiphany.  And I don\\\'t know what the Hell to do.\r\n</p>'),(3300,'Time passed. Two new men showed up and took over, and they were a little more serious than the Mother Hen. They told us that they were going to be in charge from now on, and that their performance would be judged by ours, and so if we fucked up they would make us pay. It was a simple and brutal state of affairs. Before, I had pictured boot camp running on ideals, \\\'making better sailors\\\' out of ordinary folks--volunteers, no less. Instead I soon learned that the people in charge of us had absolutely no interest in us as people, sailors or otherwise. They just wanted to be promoted. The best way to make that happen was to threaten us. There are no ideals involved. I imagine that there may be some division commanders (DC\\\'s) who believe that they are doing good, and that that is reward enough, but for my part I never met one. They all had the same look in their eyes. </p><p>\r\nSoon P-days were over and the real training began. It consisted mainly of classroom instruction, marching practice, and ship affairs practice. Our room was known as a ship. It had bulkheads instead of walls, heads instead of bathrooms, and all the rest. We spent countless hours learning the proper way to fold clothes, make the bed, stand, sit, walk, and talk. It was incredibly boring. On the wall was a chart, showing our progress on the calendar. Each day\\\'s events, or \\\'things to be afraid of tomorrow\\\' as I called them, were listed there. I began to eat again. We were allowed to shower every fourth day or so. We slept from 10 pm until about 4 am, and sometimes we were allowed to go to sleep early.</p><p>\r\nAbout a week into the real training, or three weeks after my arrival, I began to feel the first ray of hope. I saw that, despite the desperate circumstances in which I found myself in that icy wasteland of concrete and steam clouds, time was in fact passing, and there were moments of stupendous beauty too. For the first time, I saw real snowflakes--of course I had seen snow, but not these subzero-formed perfect huge flakes that I saw collecting on the ski mask and jacket of the man in front of me as we marched endlessly. I had never noticed the simple beauty of the sky so often, and so strongly. I soon had the easier aspects of life down pat. I woke instantly when the lights came on, and had the bunk made in about thrity seconds. I folded my shirts properly. I made some new acquaintances. I was surviving.</p><p>\r\nFor a while there, boot camp became sort of routine. Occasionally the division commanders would get upset about something and make us do calisthenics until we fell out, but that wasn\\\'t so bad. After all, who <i>really</i> knew when you had given out besides yourself? I enjoyed the exercise anyway, and I could feel myself getting stronger. What we learned in class was sort of interesting: ship procedures, firefighting, weapons handling, knots, communications, military history. Still, I never quite lost the feeling of being on the edge of panic. </p><p>\r\nWe had two guys who couldn\\\'t hack it. One was a skinny little guy, B, and the other was an ordinary farmboy type from Ohio, named M. B was a nervous wreck at all times--he couldn\\\'t distance himself when the DC\\\'s picked on him, as they did each of us at times. To me it was no big deal; I mean, they weren\\\'t going to hit me, they could yell all they wanted. But he showed weakness, and they prosecuted it. What seemed obvious to me, however, was that he really <i>was</i> weak, and was not going to get any stronger no matter what they did. They did not share my opinion. B went to the infirmary all the time looking for a way out. One night the DC fucked with him so hard--out of pleasure, I could tell (and so could you if I knew how to describe it better)--that he blew a circuit and freaked out. It was not a nice thing to see, or to remember. I grabbed him to keep him from swinging on the DC, and several other helped me. He was screaming nonsense. After a few seconds he went limp, and even that pigheaded fucker in charge knew not to poke at him anymore that night. The DC turned out the lights on the way out, and we got into bed. About five minutes later I was awoken by a scream. The large piece of plywood over B\\\'s head that forms the wall of the upper bunk had come loose and hit him in the head. He was bleeding and screaming, and someone came and took him away. He was back the next day with stitches in his forehead, with a vacant look on his face. He needed psychiatric attention, I remembered thinking. That night he took the whole bottle of painkillers that he had been given, and was so violently ill that they took him away again. He was gone after that. </p><p>\r\nM actually wanted to be in boot camp. He was from a military family, and was extremely into the whole thing. Unfortunately, M had a knee injury that caused him a lot of pain yet had no external symptoms. In keeping with the military\\\'s policy of letting recruits simply break all the way before removing them, he was forced to stay. He tried. Tears were often streaming down his face. The DC\\\'s were actually a little affected, I think, but there wasn\\\'t much they could do. He too tried to commit suicide after a while, the same way, and was gone. </p><p>\r\n'),(3301,'<i>This time, he flies down the office hallway and soars over cubicle walls calling out to his cooped-up colleagues as they turn their faces wondrously upward. â€œCome with me!  Come with me! Come!  Come!â€ And after a moment, a hesitation subsumed by inspiration, his colleagues lift off, leaving their cubicles for the freedom of the air, separating from their glowing computer screens and the beady little lines of code to which they may as well have been chained. Then, after a period getting their bearings by zipping around the office, they fly out the open window out over the urban valley.  Down the block they go, laughing and gliding with the confidence of birds, floating like feathers, like balloons, on currents of air.  Gonzalo stops and hovers in front of the windows of other offices, where wage slaves in white shirts gaze upon him in wonder.  He beckons them, and they take flight too! They lift off with some surprise, glancing around bug eyed, unsteady, but in the end tossing their manilla file folders into the air, the white paper raining down.  Soon the sky above the downtown streets is filled with office workers, clusters of them in suits and ties, pressed skirts and shiny shoes, moving smoothly over the avenues.  And they continue to come, flying out of the windows down the block, shooting into the sky like ducks on the wing, filling the skies, shadowing the hard sidewalks below.</i>   '),(3302,'<P>On Friday nights, <I>Income</I> plays a regular gig at Johansenâ€™s Bar, a dive in the eastern part of the city. They have a regular crowd that oftentimes is drunk and enthusiastic while the rest of the band , with the exception of Gonzalo, is also drunk and enthusiastic.  It makes for good live music.  \r\n\r\n<P>When he plays, Gonzalo hangs towards the back of the stage, close to the drum kit, watching the crowd bounce rhythmically as they wave their beer bottles and cigarettes above their head, the room filled with a blue cancerous haze. He likes the bass because it doesnâ€™t take much thought for the kind of music <I>Income</I> plays, he can get into a mindless groove even as his mind wanders, repeating variations on the same figures, thrumming along with the impact of the drums while the guitar and singer hold down the more pyrotechnic front-end.  \r\n\r\n<P>At their last show, the actress Drew Barrymore came back stage to <I>congratulate</I> them.  Her fame did not maker her motivations any less transparent.  She lavished attention on the lead singer, Kyle Kastner, talking to him while twirling a golden lock around her forefinger, giggling like an embarrassed school girl, pursing her blood-red lips.  She had been just passing by, on her way to meet some friends, when sheâ€™d stopped in the bar for a drink and heard the band up on stage!  <I>Youâ€™re great!</I> She enthused; <I>everything about you guys says SUCCESS!  You have such presence on stage.  Itâ€™s like, like pure sex!</I>  Ms. Barrymore was obviously drunk, a little unsteady on her red Jimmy Choo fuck-me heels, her ample breasts heaving.  If she were anybody else, a regular groupie, Kyle, in the grand tradition of egomaniacal lead singers everywhere, would have taken advantage of the situation (something Ms. Barrymore clearly wanted to happen), but Kyle was more than a little starstruck and surprisingly tentative in her presence.  Any other girl that was this seemingly pliant would have been moved briskly to the broom closet for some hot and heavy, but this was a real, honest-to-goodness movie star!  Undaunted, Ms. Barrymore went on: <I>I can, like, put you in my next movieâ€¦your band I mean.  You guys can do the soundtrack.  I can have my production company call your manager.</I>\r\n\r\n<P>**  \r\n\r\n<P><I>Income</I> doesnâ€™t have a manager.  Gonzalo gets the band their gigs.  He collects the money at the end of the night and divvies it up between band members without taking the 10% that would have otherwise been deducted.  As a result of his management, <I>Income</I> doesnâ€™t have many other gigs because Gonzalo is content to play once a week, and the rest of the band lacks the motivation to do more, so by default Gonzalo as the most active memeber, became the person the band turned to when it came to the business end. \r\n\r\n<P>Ms. Barrymore was not happy to be talking to Gonzalo about business.  It was really the last thing on her mind.  This was pretty clear to Gonzalo, who had been called over to handle this stuff about production companies and managers. She kept looking over his shoulder at Kyle who looked away every time she met his eye.  \r\n\r\n<P>Gonzalo wrote his name and number down on a piece of paper and gave it to her.  If she was really interested in the band doing some music for her new movie, just call him with the details, theyâ€™d obviously be excited to participate in any project she had in mind.  \r\n\r\n<P><I>Yeah, yeah, Iâ€™ll have somebody call you,</I>  Ms. Barrymore said, stuffing the paper in a small designer handbag already overflowing with scraps of paper, crumpled wads of cash and a pack of cigarettes.  She craned her neck to find Kyle who, afraid of blowing the deal, had gone to hide in the men\\\'s bathroom with a bottle of whiskey.\r\n\r\n<P>**\r\n\r\n<P>So now, a week later, as the band churns through its set and the crowd bounces and waves their arms, Kyle and the rest of the band eagerly scan the audience for Ms. Barrymore.  Certainly she would have returned tonight.  This could be the break they were waiting for.  \r\n\r\n<P>Gonzalo had patiently explained to Kyle that it wasnâ€™t the band that had captured Ms. Barrymoreâ€™s interest, it was his <I>dick</I>.  She didnâ€™t <I>obtain</I> his dick, and was now likely onto other <I>dicks</I>. But Kyle didnâ€™t seem to understand: <I>No, no, man.  Itâ€™s not like that.  What would Drew Barrymore want with me?  Iâ€™m not famous. She could have any guy she wants.  She could be diddling Keanu fuckinâ€™ Reeves, she ainâ€™t no groupie, she ainâ€™t no regular girl, sheâ€™s a movie star, man. She <b>likes</b> our music.<I>\r\n'),(3303,'So the other day I met up with a bunch of people from a messageboard that I post on. I have never met any of these people in real life.\r\n\r\nWho knows.. they could have been serial killers.. anhow, one of the other posters picked me up at the closest train station(I don\\\'t drive so had to take the train which was fine with me).\r\n\r\nHe turned out to be very nice, as did all of the people that I met that might.You never know quite what to expect, you know?People give off one vibe when you talk to them on a message board and a completely different one when you meet them in real life(I have a perfect example of this but it\\\'s a long story.Maybe something for another article)'),(3304,'<P>\r\nI first discovered Duran Duran the year I turned 12. Thanks to my brother, who had loaned one of their albums from a friend and didn\\\'t like it, I discovered their music.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSeptember of 1983. I start jr. high school.What a freaking wake up call.Nobody.And I do mean nobody, liked Duran Duran. It wasn\\\'t so bad at first because nobody knew who they were but as they started to get more and more popular, the more grief I got from people. My teachers were just as unsympathetic.They thought I focused more on Duran Duran than I did my schoolwork(which when I think about it now is probably true).\r\n</P><P>\r\nBeing one of only a few people(I think there were maybe 2 other people in the whole school that liked Duran Duran-then again-this wasn\\\'t a very big school so when I\\\'d wear my Duran Duran shirts to school or wear one(or more)of my Duran Duran pins to school,I tended to stand out a bit.One time I wore one of my Duran Duran shirts to school along with several of my pins.When I got to school the teacher said that I had to remove several of the pins because it was \\\"dangerous\\\" I knew better.The shirt *and* the pins were overkill in my teacher\\\'s opinion.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThat wasn\\\'t the first time I sort of broke the rules.After I went to a Duran Duran concert in 1984(my first concert)I punched a hole in my ticket stub. threaded some yarn through it and wore it to school.My P.E. teacher called me on it and said it was against school rules but in the end she let it drop which was cool of her ;)\r\n</P><P>\r\nSince Duran Duran fans were almost non-existent at my  school, we all gravitated towards each other. I was friends with a girl named Valerie who also liked Duran Duran, though none of her friends did.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe got to talk to each other every so often but were usually interrupted by one of her friends wanting to know why she was talking to me. And I used to get tons of grief from people(including one of Valerie\\\'s friends).Another one of the people doing a lot of the teasing was the son of friends of my parents.He was lucky I never told his parents what he did.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIn the end,I got tired of all the crap from people-that and Duran Duran\\\'t music was going in a direction that I didn\\\'t like-Wild Boys,their single released towards the end of 1984, had left me cold-it was a different sound for them. I guess that makes me a fickle fan.I gave away a bunch of my Duran Duran stuff to my friends and quit being a fan.\r\n</P>'),(3305,'<P>\r\nWhen I first got on the interenet just about 5 years ago, one of the first places that I posted was on a messageboard for african-americans (by way of explaination, I\\\'m half black and half white).\r\n</P><P>\r\nEven though the messageboard was for black interests, there were quite a few white people that posted there too.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nOne of the black guys that posted there was named Tony. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nHe had a few opinions, and would tell them to anyone who would listen. He disliked interracial ANYthing (which is ironic-more about that in a minute) the white people there thought he was more than a little over the top.I could see where he was coming from with some of his tirades, I really could. But on the other hand I couldn\\\'t relate to them that much since I was a biracial woman who had been raised by white adoptive parents and essentially was white.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nSo when it came to some of his insults, I was a bit thin skinned(but keep in mind that I hadn\\\'t had to deal with that much when I was growing up, stangely enough).\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nI come to find out that the whole thing was a big lie. Yeah. I was shocked. Tony wasn\\\'t like that AT ALL.  As a matter of fact, someone who posted on the message board told me recently that Tony is now married to a white woman and has two kids. Who would have guessed.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nApparently he\\\'s one of these people who gets off on being a total noodge when he\\\'s on the internet and a really cool person IRL.  It\\\'s not the first time I\\\'ve experienced someone like that.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI wouldn\\\'t mind exchanging emails with Tony.  I wonder if he knows that I know his attitude on that messageboard was all a fake. I\\\'m sure that he would talk to me on a completely different level.I don\\\'t hold it against him, even though some really nasty things were said, on both of our part.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3306,'â€“lâ€šÃâ€°Â¹Å yâ€°Ã†â€œdâ€˜Ã¬â€¢ÃÅ½Ã¨â€šÃ‰'),(3338,'<META http-equiv=Content-Type content=\\\"text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\\\">\r\n<p>Si imiter le comportement humain est difficile, cette difficultÃ© vient souvent lÃ  oÃ¹ on lâ€™attendrait le moins. Les actions complexes sont celles oÃ¹ un robot excelle ; elles ne prÃ©senteront donc pas de problÃ¨me particulier. Les comportements les plus simples requiÃ¨rent beaucoup plus dâ€™attention. Lâ€™attente, qui ne demande par dÃ©finition aucune action, est donc lâ€™un des comportement les plus difficiles Ã  simuler avec succÃ¨s. Pour Ã©viter de prendre des risques inutiles, on profitera systÃ©matiquement de toute possibilitÃ© de sâ€™asseoir. Lâ€™attente assis est particuliÃ¨rement simple. On peut se contenter de garder le corps complÃ¨tement immobile, et ne bouger que la tÃªte, voire mÃªme uniquement les yeux, pour suivre les dÃ©placements de tout objet ou personne en mouvement autour de soi. Limiter toutefois lâ€™amplitude des mouvements de la tÃªte ou des yeux Ã  plus ou moins 30 degrÃ©s, afin de ne pas avoir lâ€™air de porter trop dâ€™intÃ©rÃªt au mÃªme objet (et surtout Ã  la mÃªme personne). Si plusieurs objets sont en mouvement au mÃªme moment, en choisir un au hasard.</p><p>\r\n\r\nLâ€™attente debout demandera bien plus dâ€™efforts, car elle fait lâ€™objet dâ€™une position codifiÃ©e. On ne peut se tenir n\\\'importe comment lorsqu\\\'on attend quelqu\\\'un ou quelque chose. Il faut dÃ©jÃ  Ã©viter toutes les positions qui signifieraient ostensiblement autre chose qu\\\'une simple attente: si l\\\'on tourne le dos Ã  une porte, bien dans l\\\'axe, on est pris pour un vigile; si l\\\'on est dans l\\\'axe d\\\'un couloir ou d\\\'un trottoir, on a l\\\'air d\\\'avoir Ã©tÃ© stoppÃ© en cours de route par une pensÃ©e frappante. Si l\\\'on regarde le sol ou ses pieds, c\\\'est qu\\\'on est si dÃ©primÃ© qu\\\'on ne sait plus dans quelle direction avancer. Toute position incorrecte augment le risque dâ€™Ãªtre abordÃ© ou dâ€™attirer les soupÃ§ons.</p><p>\r\n\r\nLes seules positions d\\\'attente debout adÃ©quates semblent demander une orientation ambiguÃ« du corps et de la tÃªte: par exemple le corps tournÃ© dans une direction vers laquelle on ne peut pas marcher, et la tÃªte regardant de toute faÃ§on ailleurs qu\\\'en face, avec n\\\'importe quel angle entre 10 et 80 degrÃ©s sur le cÃ´tÃ©. Faire varier souvent la direction de son regard semble Ãªtre un choix judicieux.\r\n</p><p>\r\nEn cas de doute sur la justesse de sa position, ou si quelque passant a soudain l\\\'air suspicieux, utiliser la technique suivante : regarder sa montre, puis faire la grimace et la conserver tandis qu\\\'on regarde l\\\'horizon fixement pendant plusieurs secondes. Câ€™est notamment pour cela quâ€™on pensera Ã  se doter dâ€™une montre, quâ€™il faut garder Ã  lâ€™heure locale afin dâ€™Ã©viter de surprendre les gens de son entourage qui la consulteraient directement. Autre remarque Ã  propos de la montre : prendre lâ€™habitude de la consulter si lâ€™on vous demande lâ€™heure, et arrondir lâ€™heure lue par tranches de 5 minutes. Par exemple, rÃ©pondre de prÃ©fÃ©rence Â« 6 heures vingt Â» au lieu de Â« 6 heures 18 Â», ceci pour simuler une tendance naturelle Ã  lâ€™imperfection.\r\n</p>'),(3307,'<p>I saw William Shatner last Saturday at a sci fi convention in Pasadena. It rocked. I also saw Carrie Fisher make out with Leonard Nimoy. And I saw some kickass Klingons and Stormtroopers. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>But, I saw William Shatner. You have no idea how much I love that man. I grew up watching reruns of classic Star Trek. Captain Kirk was just the coolest man on the planet. Even when he grew that gut, he was still kinda sexy in the \\\"I am the Captain, and I have a nice penis under that gut\\\" kind of way.</p>\r\n<p>I wanted to be on the Starship Enterprise. I wanted to be Kirk when we played Star Trek. The boys would never let me be Kirk. \\\"You\\\'re a girl\\\", they said, \\\"Kirk can\\\'t be a girl.\\\" Of course not. Captain James T. Kirk was far too cool to ever be a girl. But I had long black hair and almond eyes, so I couldn\\\'t play Uhura. She kicked ass, but she was black. And a little Chinese girl could never hope to play Lieutenant Uhura. So, I was always relegated to the villain roles, although I put in an exemplary performance as a tribble once. Then they moved on, but I never got to play Batman either. </p>\r\n<p>It wasn\\\'t until the new Enterprise and Ensign Hoshi Sato, the lovely Japanese interpreter, that a part was had for me to play - but then, it was 12 years too late, and I had moved on to other geeky activities, like running around in quasi-renaissance garb and fighting with pvc, foam and duct tape weapons. But I didn\\\'t care. What mattered was that they never ever let me play Captain Kirk because Kirk was just too cool to be a girl.</p>           \r\n<p>I saw William Shatner last Saturday, and he was funny. He made me smile. And then he made me laugh. I didn\\\'t have a crush on him anymore, but he was still looking pretty good. Balding, though. Ohmigod, I saw William Shatner. </p>                                \r\n<p>But the bastards wanted to charge me 40 bucks for his autograph, so I went to the dealer\\\'s room and got a lovely dagger instead. The sword dealer kept on telling me I was pretty. I was going to get a tribble too, but the fur coloration was just rather sub par. </p>\r\n'),(3308,'<p>recently, i flew from new zealand to singapore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i had what i believe is the Worst Seat On The Plane (just in front of the toilets, with no room behind me to recline the seat), sitting next to an irate french-swiss woman and her supplicating husband, who was on crutches with an infected foot (no, i don\\\'t want to touch it, thanks), trying to bully her way into first class (she eventually moved to an empty seat a few rows up, giving hubby two seats so he could recline comfortably while elevating his foot right into my meal tray... after i switched my window seat for his aisle, in order to stop him actually leaning on me for ten hours straight), as well as having a seat over-recliner directly in front of me (thanks to my new aisle seat), leaving me cramped into approximately 5 square inches of low-oxygen space, with a bandaged gammy foot precariously close to my dinner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>towards the end of the flight, when i felt certain that deep vein thrombosis was imminent, an elderly woman approached the flight attendant standing officiously outside the toilets, right next to my seat. she began explaining that she wasn\\\'t feeling well, got halfway through her sentence and promptly lost consciousness. as her eyelids fluttered and she crumpled to the floor, the flight attendant attempted to hold her up while shouting to other flight attendants for help, and the next thing i knew, the woman was laid out on the floor right next to me, there was a call over the PA for any medical professionals to report to <i>my</i> seat number, and the elderly woman had started turning a delicate shade of blue.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a doctor from first-class ran up right after the announcement, felt for a pulse - there wasn\\\'t one - and called for a defibrillator. fortunately, as soon as they ripped her shirt open and took the paddles from the box, her eyes flickered back open and she gasped in horror, not because she had apparently been dead for about thirty seconds, but that her bra was showing and she had peed her pants. she was british, after all. meanwhile, the american across the aisle was calmly eating his ice cream and watching the events as if it were part of the in-flight movie or yet another reality tv show. i immediately burst into tears, and the supplicating french-swiss man with the icky foot gave me his ice cream.<p>\r\n\r\n<p>singapore girl, you\\\'re a great way to fly.</p>'),(3309,'I spent this afternoon with my father. This is profound since I have neither seen, heard from, spoken to, or otherwise communicated with him in over fifteen years; I could scarcely remember what he looked like up until today.  He\\\'s visiting NYC, and requested to see me via email (in which he admitted getting my address by lying to a friend of mine). I left work early, biked down to 53rd/6th and sat with him in the lobby of the Ritz and small-talked for an hour or so. He looked exactly how I\\\'m probably going to look when I\\\'m 60, which was nice, because he looked really good. He is a much different man than I remember. His eyes are tired and kind, no longer red with evil and swirled with maniacal genius. What a catalyst time can be.<br><br>\r\nMy father and I have an odd past. I will not go on about it here. I will tell you, however, that he had on a really nice suit. He told me about my great grandfathers. He told me about my brother. We discussed the things men talk about when they have no common past- cars and women. We shook hands a few times, forced an awkward hug when it was all said and done, left up the same avenue by which  I arrived.<br><br>\r\nI rode back through Central Park; the park drive was closed to traffic, so the roads were empty. I pedalled as hard as I could, and flew along the ribbony road, listening to hardcore in my mostly-deaf left ear while keeping my sharper right ear vigilantly tuned to whatever looping citynoises might interest a wary biker.<br><Br>\r\nI crossed the transverse going as fast as I\\\'ve ever gone on a bike, and saw a huge group of Japanese tourists posing up against the road\\\'s edge for a group photo. As I zipped behind them, I let go of the handlebars and offered the heavy metal salute with both hands, while making a crazy, tongue-y scowl immediately behind them right as their flash went off.<br><br>\r\nThe photographer shook his fist at me as I rode off, bobbing my head to amphetamine-fueled-chainsaw noises. I think he called me an <i>asshore</i>.<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m relieved that things are finally getting back to normal here; that people aren\\\'t afraid to get mad if you act out (which is boud to happen now and then, if you stay here long enough). I\\\'m glad the drama-induced sympathy is starting to fade. It\\\'s about time; time to stop frowning and crying, time to start getting up again. It\\\'s time to start arguing with cops more. It\\\'s time to sweat more, drink more beer, bike until you collapse. It\\\'s time to make love as the sun rises. It\\\'s time to listen to more music and sing more and manifest creativity in some physical sense. It\\\'s fucking springtime in NYC, and it couldn\\\'t have come a moment earlier.<br><br>\r\nMuch like I miss the Trade Center, I think I\\\'ve been missing my dad for fifteen years. I\\\'m glad tourists can call New Yorkers assholes again without feeling guilty. I\\\'m glad I spoke to my father in person. I\\\'m glad that someone out there is making headbanging music. I\\\'m glad that some union worker in Chicago welded a titanium frame strong enough to schlep my ass fast enough to outrun renegade cabs.<br><Br>\r\nToday, I\\\'m glad I\\\'m alive.'),(3310,'<p>one more harrassing remark about my working for americans and i\\\'m gonna slap somebody.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>okay, so george bush is not only ruining world peace with every passing day, he also happens to be the american president. but since when has every american agreed with him. hell they didn\\\'t even vote for him!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i work in the film industry, and as proud as my fellow new zealanders are of the likes of peter jackson, harry sinclair and christine jeffs, the film industry is an american industry. sure, films are made all over the world, but film is an american artform and has been for a very long time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no-one i know in the industry supports or remotely likes bush. no-one i have spoken to believes in this war or that bush, rumsfeld et. al. aren\\\'t lying aily to the american public.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>people need to remember that america has also given us the likes of michael moore. america just needs to listen to itself a little more, and less to its leaders.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m not a fawning america-groupie, far from it. as much as i love many americans i do find them arrogant and a little uninformed sometimes. but if you cast blanket criticism across a country that provides the majority of film and television that you watch, music and clothes that you buy, and colas that you drink, then you\\\'re not really following through with the old thought process either.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s as easy as one, two, three...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1) americans, stop listening to buzzwords like \\\"freedom\\\", \\\"evil-doers\\\", \\\"national security\\\" and \\\"god\\\", and start remembering things like enron, iran contra and all the things the bush family has fucked up for you over the years. it\\\'s not unamerican to hate a president that stole power and lies to you while the nation gets poor, sick and hungry.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2) everyone else, support america but not the bush regime. the more the world voices the clear difference between the two, the less that ol george can hide behind rallying cries of cynical patriotism</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3) stop killing children in iraq - sounds really simple when you put it that way, maybe a little naive, but since when was obviousness a mutually exclusive notion to justice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>peace out. hug your neighbour. drop a pill. fuck in the streets. whatever makes us feel better and hurts no-one can\\\'t be bad at all.</p>'),(3311,'<p>one more harrassing remark about my working for americans and i\\\'m gonna slap somebody.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>george bush is an ignorant fool who is ruining world peace with every passing day. unfortunately he also happens to be the american president. but since when has every american agreed with him? hell they didn\\\'t even vote for him!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i work in the film industry, and as proud as my fellow new zealanders are of the likes of peter jackson, harry sinclair and christine jeffs, the film industry is an american industry. sure, films are made all over the world, but film is an american artform and has been for a very long time. whether i, or anybody else, likes it or not</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no-one i know in the industry supports or remotely likes bush. no-one i have spoken to believes in this war or that bush, rumsfeld et. al. aren\\\'t lying to the american public on a daily basis.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>people need to remember that america has also given us the likes of michael moore. america just needs to listen to itself a little more, and less to its leaders.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m not a fawning america-groupie, far from it. as much as i love many americans i do find them arrogant and a little uninformed sometimes. but if you cast blanket criticisms across a country that provides the majority of film and television that you watch, music and clothes that you buy, and colas that you drink, then you\\\'re not really following through with the old thought process, now are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s as easy as one, two, three...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1) americans, stop listening to buzzwords like \\\"freedom\\\", \\\"evil-doers\\\", \\\"national security\\\" and \\\"god\\\", and start remembering things like enron, iran contra and all the things the bush family has fucked up for you over the years. it\\\'s not unamerican to resent a president that stole power, and lies to you while the nation gets poor, sick and hungry.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2) everyone else, always remember that you can like america without supporting the bush regime. the more the world voices the clear difference between the two, the less that mr bush can hide behind cynical pleas to freedom and patriotism</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3) stop killing children in iraq - sounds really simple when you put it that way, maybe a little naive, but since when was obviousness a mutually exclusive notion to justice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>peace out. hug your neighbour. drop a pill. fuck in the streets. whatever makes us feel better and hurts no-one can\\\'t be bad at all.</p>'),(3312,'<p>i just finished moving across town.  my back is so incredibly sore and i\\\'m exhausted.  just a few minutes ago, we finished bringing in the last box, and we rejoiced!!</p>\r\n<p>the move isn\\\'t over yet though.  tomorrow, we have to go through a walk-through in order to get our absurdly high deposit back.  i hope everything looks ok and is in order.  i now know all the tricks of the trade in order to fool your property manager into thinking that there are no holes in the wall, you regrouted the tile (hahaha, yeah right!!), and no scuff marks anywhere.  thank you to my friends spackle, acrylic white paint (aka grout whitener), and left over paint discovered in the garage.</p>\r\n<p>also, i have a new fascination with oven cleaner. my roommate and i fought over oven realestate.  i took the door and he took the inside.  we decided that starting a professional oven cleaning service would be kind of fun.</p>\r\n<p>cleaning unidentified kitchen goo is less fun.     i have no idea how most of that stuff got there.  it probably predates me.  i\\\'m just happy that i\\\'ll never have to eat in that kitchen again.</p>\r\n<p>tonight, i decided to throw together a meal rather than eat out.  eating out for the past two weeks has actually become tiring as well, so some home cooked grub sounded nice.  problem with this though, is that i didn\\\'t have much food readily available to me.  i dug through some boxes and found a bag of lentils and a bag of barley.   i boiled them and threw in some lemon juice (we have a full fridge of condiments!!), cumin, garlic, salt, and goat cheese.  i found a fresh tomato and chopped it up to sprinkle on top.  the end result was pretty damn good!  it was sort of like lentil risotto.  mmmm.</p>\r\n<p>now.. i sleep.  moving really sucks, but when you\\\'re finally done, it\\\'s such a comforting feeling to start a whole new nest.</p>\r\n<p>le, does a taurus nest??</p>   '),(3313,'Last year I went through a ordeal that nobody should have to go through.I had uterine cancer. It all started several months before when I finally went to the doctor after having lots of \\\"female problems\\\"(heavy periods,and when I did have periods it wasn\\\'t that often.Turns out that I had a cyst in my uterus(which turned out to be more than one cyst actually).<br><br>\r\n\r\nAs much as I did not want to do it, surgery(a D&C which is a simple surgery)removed the cyst.<br><br>\r\n\r\nA few days later my doctor called my parents to tell them that I had cancer.While he was performing the D&C they discovered that I had a whole bunch of cysts. The  first cyst that they removed turned out to be cancerous. As a matter of fact, when my doctor removed it he knew that it looked cancerous.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMy parents were the ones to break the news to me. I was shocked, of course.I went through every emotion that a person can have, all in the space of about a half hour.And then I decided that I was going to deal with the situation the best way that I could-with a sense of humor. And that really helped a LOT. Telling the rest of my family  that I had cancer was difficult(my brother  said that it felt like he had been punched in the stomach after I told him the news).<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere was some GOOD news though.Turns out that the kind of cancer that I had was slow growing. Because they had caught it early(lord knows how long it\\\'d been there).Good thing: they caught it early. Bad thing:I had to have a hysterectomy. In other words,no kids for me. This bothered me for all of, oh, 5 minutes(I have a niece  and 3 nephews so not being able to have children wasn\\\'t such a big deal-and there\\\'s always adoption.)<br><br>\r\n\r\nI had my hysterectomy and that was the end of it- I did not need *any* chemo or follow-up treatments\r\n(though I do have to go to the doctor more often than most people, which I don\\\'t mind..it\\\'s worth it to stay healthy.)Being in the hospital after my surgery was quite an experience-after my surgery they had me on morphine which is the good stuff-you\\\'re not in *any* pain, but it makes you *really* dopey.<br><br>\r\n\r\nRecovering from a hysterectomy is not easy.it\\\'s basically several months of taking it *very* easy.\r\nIt seemed like I\\\'d never get my strength back(I did though)and I had a few setbacks(my incision took a LONG time to heal,due in large part I think because it went from near my belly button almost all of the way down(they had to do it that way to get lymph nodes from my pelvis to make sure that the cancer hadn\\\'t spread.)<br><br>\r\n\r\nA year later, everything is fine, thank god.I just had a CT scan to make sure that everything was OK, and it was. I\\\'ll have to be mindful of my health for the rest of my life. Not the kind of wake-up call most people should have.<br><br>'),(3314,'you know, god dammit, i\\\'m starting to get pretty upset about what is going on in the world today. when i was growing up, things went on, there were wars and tragedies left and right, but it is slowly becoming apparent to me that some sort of golden time is now slipping away. what really bites my ass is that i haven\\\'t been <i>sure</i>. i have taken a whole bunch of history classes, and i understand WWI, WWII, the Cold War, the Civil War, Detente, Containment, the Monroe Doctrine, Manifest Destiny, and the Spanish-American War. Vietnam. the War of the Roses. the fall of the Roman Empire. i know a lot about history. And yet, still, i have a hard time figuring out what to think about what\\\'s going on TODAY, while i\\\'m alive, in the absolute peak of my time. i\\\'d like to at least be able to believe it\\\'s right, or believe it\\\'s wrong. i\\\'m leaning towards wrong right now, as more and more facts trickle down to me, yet on the other hand i know that throughout history, with the obvious exception of Teddy Roosevelt\\\'s imperialism, or \\\'Manifest Destiny,\\\' which led us to kill 100,000 Filipinos (and 4000 US), we have usually been doing not only the <i>right thing</i> in going to war, but usually we are so reticent to enter a conflict that we delay too long.<br><br>\r\ndon\\\'t get me wrong. i also read a book called <u>The Culture of Make Believe</u>, by Derrick Jensen (who is a really nice guy, by the way), that among other things details the rape of the environment and the slaughter of the indigenous North American people by the whites...and lets not forget slavery. i know that the dominant culture in the USA is guilty of a number of terrible things, things that easily compete in terms of sheer fucked-up barbarism with any group of power monkeys in the world. we were never quite as bad as the worst, but we could hang. \r\n<br><br>\r\nso what\\\'s your point, tdogg? \r\n<br><br>\r\nwell, the point is, i had bad, bad feelings about GWB from the first moment i laid eyes on him and heard him speak. i thought, <i>this man is going to make me lose faith in my country if he gets elected.</i> unfortunately he was, but my loss of faith has been a slower and more painful process, if you can believe that (recounts!). a young woman who worked at the law firm in which i had my part-time job at the time, who is a hard-core religioso and all-around southern american, said that she voted for him, in her first election as a voter, because \\\'daddy likes him, and i think that the way he sweats is sexy.\\\'<br><br>\r\nnow of course i saw the horror in that statement, but compared to what i feel today it was jr. high angst. this was before everything, so to speak. since then it has been a non-stop parade of fucked-up facts and figures, and i\\\'m juuuust educated enough to at least have gut reactions to certain things, even if i can\\\'t always make what is bothering me totally explicit. the thought will cross my mind, <i>this is not cool</i>, if you know what i mean. up until, well, right about now, my history education had led me to believe that we were probably doing the right thing, and my wariness of being subsumed intellectually in a movement that looks a lot more like mass hyteria(of which there has been plently in our history) has kept me from protesting the war, or protesting for it for that matter. i have tried to straddle the fence. the fence gets smaller and more uncomfortable every day so, ta-da, i have decided to take a position.<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m anti-stupidity. therefore, i\\\'m anti-bush. i know that his daddy was as guilty as any convict to walk the last mile in a prison with that iran-contra shit. i know that some of the worst things the CIA ever did were done under GHWB. i know that GWB got into Yale through his family connections and has always pretty much been a level one fuckup. most importantly, i have seen him. i have heard him speak! how could you not know after ONE SECOND that he is a dangerously dull-witted man to be leading us? that on top of the track record of the Republicans in general and his father in particular tells me all i need to know about him, war or not. if he\\\'s for it, i\\\'m against it. so look, people. we have to get this motherfucker out of there before everything goes to shit. that should be job one. the anti-war people need to be more anti-Bush than anything, not out of anger, but out of a sense of good old human self-preservation. my goal, in my first year of law school this fall, is to figure out what i can do to make this happen. hopefully i\\\'ll pick something up here and there. any of you are welcome to help, and i\\\'m willing to help you. '),(3315,'<p>just another familiar night. she\\\'s out in the lounge now, dancing i think.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can hear laughter, but it\\\'s not her. sounds like one of the guys.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i hate them because they\\\'re younger and fitter. i hate them even more because when i was their age i looked worse than i do now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can still taste her kiss. there\\\'s a little smudge of lipstick at the corner of my mouth and i don\\\'t want to wipe it away just yet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want this life. i want her, but to even suggest some kind of posession is to drive her away and invite ridicule.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m stronger now than i have been in a long time but something is still missing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the drugs are kicking in and i don\\\'t want them to yet. the rinse will have me cuddling everything in sight. i sense how strong it\\\'s coming on and envision wasted attempts at sexual coupling with pieces of furniture.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the face in the mirror is all too familiar. he keeps reminding me of all those other lives i have tried to live and failed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>will this one last? do i even really want it or is it filling in time until the next change of pace and scene.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>thirty-one eventful years should really be enough to have figured this out. the biggest lie of all is that growing up teaches you something and changes you, it doesn\\\'t. i\\\'m the same as i have always been, just more jaded.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when the blood stops flowing from my nose i\\\'ll eventually have to go out and face them again. if i don\\\'t wipe it away it might at least look tough. there\\\'s no way in hell the stains are coming out of this shirt before morning.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the comfort of soltitude is fading. the face in the mirror is starting to talk about old times i would rather forget. time to move on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>pull the mask back on and pretend i know who i am. they\\\'ll never guess the truth.</p>'),(3316,'I swear I have the loudest rumbling stomach I have ever heard.I actually have apologized for it being too loud before(!)\r\n\r\nDid you ever wonder why your stomach does that? it\\\'s the sound of gases and other stuff going through your intenstines(the medical name for it is borborygmus, plural borborygmi(yes, I\\\'m a science nerd, what can I say?) depending on what\\\'s going through your bowels, the sounds can be higher or lowe pitched.(my stomach is growling like a mutha right now and it\\\'s a very high pitch).When I get intestinal bugs, I know to listen for my stomach rumbling.That means that my system is going back to(relatively)normal.\r\n\r\nJeez, I know all of this and I\\\'m not even a doctor!)(but I play one on TV, j/k)\r\n\r\nMy stomach rumbles are so incredibly loud that I\\\'ve actually seen if other people can hear it(of course, when I WANT people to hear it it\\\'s always really quiet(isn\\\'t that the way it always is?) Another thing:My stomach grumbling seems to happen the most right when I get up and when I\\\'m falling asleep .I\\\'m sure that\\\'s significant somehow.'),(3317,'<p>The weather was nice yesterday, so I went down to the Chevy dealer to \r\ntest drive the Corvette Z06 - a factory race car. 405 HP, six speed \r\ntransmission. Purpose built to go.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After the usual small talk, I got the dealer to let me drive. \r\nNormally, they don\\\'t let folks drive a 170 MPH weapon - musta said \r\nthe right things. Anyway, the dealership has a very short \\\'test \r\nloop\\\' - maybe 3 miles. We were to follow the loop without \r\nexception...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Until the salesman closed the door, that is. \\\"I never get to drive \r\nthese darned things,\\\" he lamented, \\\"so I really don\\\'t know what \r\nthey\\\'ll do or how they perform. If you want to go on a longer test \r\nrun - that\\\'s okay by me!\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>WHOO-HOO!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>WHOO-HOO!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>WHOO-HOO!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We putter out to the main road, the Z06 warming up. REALLY nice ride \r\nquality - much better than I expected. The salesman begins to tell \r\nme about his blown \\\'91 Mustang LX - I figure I\\\'m not gonna get in \r\ntrouble if I drop the hammer, so I straighten the car out, settle the \r\nchassis, and push thru the throttle resistance that holds horsepower \r\nin check.</p>\r\n\r\n<p/The ghost of Ed Cole\\\'s smallblock awakens, and is whipped into a \r\ndemon frenzy. Our guts get squished as the Z06 howls thru its \r\ntitanium exhaust towards redline. The 1-2 shift goes by like there\\\'s \r\n$1000 at stake, and the steamrollers out back get loose and stay that \r\nway thru three-quarters of second. A few heartbeats later, we\\\'re 2X \r\nthe speed limit, and inertia is causing us to accelerate still - even \r\nthough I\\\'ve closed the throttle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The salesman is silent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After a long pause he says, \\\"That\\\'s the first time I\\\'ve ever \r\nexperienced that. No one\\\'s ever done that while I\\\'m in the car.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Whoops!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Another long pause. He then spoke slowly - with deliberation.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Do it again.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There are precious moments in a motorhead\\\'s life where the line \r\nbetween man and machine are blurred. We dance with the machine, and \r\nthe machine dances with us. It can be a car, a boat, a plane, or a \r\nbike...When you\\\'re there - time is suspended. A Z06 will blast thru \r\nthe 60 MPH barrier in 3.9 seconds in real time. In motorhead time - \r\nan eternity of bliss.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Several eternities later - we returned to the lot. It took an act of \r\nherculean will to shut the heart of the beast down. It sat there - \r\nrubber scuffed, engine hot and ticking from the wringing out just \r\nreceived.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The salesman came around to my side of the car. \\\"You know how to \r\ndrive.\\\" It was a statement of fact, like the sky is blue. \\\"Thanks \r\nfor the test drive.\\\" He went into the dealership, and lit up a \r\ncigarette.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>feel the tension\r\nman what a ride</p>\r\n'),(3320,'<P>It was clear to me that several protesters were injured.  At 5am, lawful picketers gathered at the West Oakland BART station and then moved to the entrance of the APL and SSA loading docks.  I got to the BART station at around 7am and waited with about 50-70 people who filed into a bus, or started walking to the same loading dock entrances about a mile from the BART station. About 30 people were walking towards the APL entrance when we encountered a stream of protesters coming back our way.  They let us know that the further entrance (the SSA one) had picketers being surrounded by Oakland police, who had donned gas masks. Once we got to APL, my \\\'buddy\\\' rode back from SSA, on her bike, crying. She had witnessed the police shoot rubber bullets and tear gas at the picketers.  We gathered up people with cameras and legal observers and slowly walked our way towards the SSA entrance.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>By the time we got there, the police were about a half-mile further down the road and there were rubber bullets/bean bags/remnants of tear gas canisters all over the road/tracks.  We went ahead and started walking towards the area where the police were.  As we got closer, some of the cops said we could go around them.  I heard several cops saying that they didn\\\'t want any protestors behind them so they corraled us and pushed us towards a larger grouping that was at the intersection of 7th and Maritime.  The pickters were walking in a circle.  No one was yelling at the cops, it was very peaceful.  The cops ordered the crowd to dispurse in two minutes.  There was no movement away so they lined up about 20 motorcycle cops and maybe 40 cops on foot in riot gear.  The picketers were still slowly walking in a big circle, with no motion towards or away from the police.  When the two minutes was up, the police threw stun grenades and tear gas into the picketing crowd (I think it was perhaps 200 people, losely collected).   They also fired rubber bullets and bean bags.  Some of the crowd started running, but it was fairly well organized and many people yelled that we should only walk, not run.  As we were walking away from the police, they fired several more volleys directly into the crowd.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>At this point, I was at the back, walking away from the police, but trying to keep everyone in sight.  I saw rubber bullets/bean bags fly by our heads and hit people with their backs towards the cops.  I watched a stun grenade land right on a young woman\\\'s back and explode, ripping a hole through her heavy canvas jacket, a long sleeved shirt below that and a light tank top below that, leaving scorch marks on her skin and a bleeding gash.  I heard later that the police made a press release claiming that iron bolts were being thrown at them.  While not impossible, I think that\\\'s highly improbable.  The crowd wasn\\\'t taunting the police in anyway that I could see and I had a clear view.</P>\r\n\r\nAs far as I could tell, there were no arrests made.  The picketing was designed to grab media attention on the various companies involved in shipping war supplies not only to Iraq, but also to Afghanistan.  I think it\\\'s important to remember that this isn\\\'t just a protest about Iraq, but about the entire War on Terror.  There have already been hints from the administration that as the WMD searches in Iraq have turned up very little (I did hear something about some medium range missiles which carried sarin gas and mustard agents found today) they will claim that the weapons must have been smuggled out.  Maybe that\\\'s true, maybe it\\\'s not, but  it\\\'s impossible to prove that WMD\\\'s weren\\\'t smuggled out, so, I wonder just how long this will continue...</p>\r\n\r\n<P>I\\\'ve noticed that this is what CNN has blurbed about what happened:\r\n<P>Police fired \\\"marble-size\\\" sting balls made of rubber to try to disperse protesters at the port of Oakland, California. An estimated 600 demonstrators were attempting to block two gates to American President Lines, a shipping company the protesters claimed was profiting from the war in Iraq.</P>\r\nI want to point out that by the time the police first started firing at the picketers, the union leaders had already declared that shift unsafe, and therefore no loading/unloading was going on.  Also, the police ignored a smaller group of picketers at an entrance less than a mile away from where they shot and the second incident, which I documented above, happened a half-mile away from the SSA docks entrance.</p>\r\n'),(3318,'<p>she was mine. it was meant to be. nobody else was ever meant to have her or we wouldn\\\'t have been together at all. why was that so hard for people to understand?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the awful thought keeps tugging at a corner of my memory, like someone pulling a hanging thread from my sweater. the full recollection of how she was so badly hurt is hurtling towards me, and i don\\\'t want it to arrive. it\\\'s bad news in the mail, a debt collector coming up the front path. it won\\\'t leave me alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remember spillling the wine. her shouting. the laughter from the room was a mix of friendly ribbing and outright derision. the drive home is less clear. i think it was me behind the wheel. it shouldn\\\'t have been, but i was full of purpose.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i need to get out of here.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>did i mean to make her bleed? to have her so badly cut up? she\\\'s lying over there with her back to me now. she won\\\'t look this way. just as well; i couldn\\\'t bear to see her eyes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if i clench my fists around the dried leaves and mud beneath me, they crackle satisyingly. the smell of wet earth and the patter of light rain on the leaves is oddly comforting.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the sun is coming up; the morning light revealing the grisly scene to the world. i want all of this to be a bad dream, though i know otherwise.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i saw them kiss.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'d guessed for a while that it was going on, but denial is a powerful tool of the desperate and insecure.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if i crawled to the car i might find that it would still start, despite the tree lodged halfway into the engine. it seems unlikely. wishful even. i can smell gas. i see myself on a pyre of dried leaves and gasoline; immolation cleansing my crimes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remember the crushed velvet curtains blowing in the dining room as they shared careful glances. were they touching under the table? she hated me being drunk. maybe it was the reason. perhaps it was just a convenient excuse to stray.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as we walked to the car i knew i wanted her to hurt. did i mean for this to happen after all?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i try not to enjoy the thought of her being disfigured. that  he might now find her ugly is suddenly comforting. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to vomit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i try to crawl. i cannot stay here. my knee hurts, and looking down i see blood seeping through the stocking. my skirt is ripped and is tangled around a branch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sirens in the distance. they\\\'re coming for us now. i can find no comfort in the thought of home.</p>\r\n'),(3319,'testtesttesttesttesttesttest'),(3321,'<p>\r\nseptember eleventh showed the United States government that Saudi Arabia wasn\\\'t the loyal servant it was supposed to be. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith a large number of Saudi nationals taking part in the WTC attacks, and being involved in camps and terrorist training around the world, the Saudi state has almost overnight become in itself a rogue nation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith the United States taking Iraq faster than a game of scrabble, one should see that the United States has now secured a long term fixed rate market to buy oil from. I don\\\'t think the United States wants to control the oil as much as it wants guarentees that any oil pumped will be sold to the United States for an \\\'out of market\\\' price. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwith the United States so close with the Kuwaiti regime, and soon the \\\"new and improved\\\" Iraqi authority, the United States is making Saudi Arabia less important. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nIt will be an interesting time when the fighting stops and the \\\"roadmaps\\\" for Iraqi reconstruction are revealed to the public. I\\\'m sure we\\\'ll see more of what was done for Afghanistan, but like Afghanistan im sure what is promised wont be fully realized. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom Seoul South Korea, embedded with the second expat calvary, this is chiamattt signing out. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3322,'<p>\r\nLast night I picked up a young woman; she was about your age.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI saw her waiting on the street - in the cold, and in the rain - for the bus; the bus wasn\\\'t coming.  At least once a night I try to do something to help someone; last night, she was the person that I decided to help.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs she entered the vehicle, I asked if money was tight; she said that it was.  Usually, people who have money don\\\'t wait for the bus.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHer clothing was an odd mix of unrelated pieces; she wore a scarf on her head; she had two bags.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe was leaving an average sort of hotel.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI told her not to worry about the meter; and then I drove north.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe talked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe asked about me; she was curious and intelligent.\r\nI broke an unwritten rule, and asked about her business.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe was a stripper; she worked at a club in town.\r\nThe address that I took her to was that of another hotel - a weird, little hotel.\r\nIt was her home.\r\nShe was selling herself just to earn enough money to pay her bills.\r\nShe was eating a bag of licorice for dinner.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe wasn\\\'t the sort of person who was ever going to be able to blend into civil society and function like everyone else; it just wasn\\\'t going to happen.\r\nAnd so she was relegated to a life on the outside.\r\nShe had no one to care for her in any meaningful way; when she died, no one would cry.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHer voice was measured; her face was a mask; she had distanced herself - psychologically - from the world in which she lived.<br>\r\nShe was detached.<br>\r\nBut her eyes were very sad.\r\n</p>'),(3324,'<p>My life as a pornslinger. It was short lived and lacked glamour. The customers were OK. It was the management that made me feel dirty. And not because of the porn. They made me feel dirty and stupid, like any little cog in any poorly running machine that chugs along only out of habit. Kept breathing, but nothing more. Like a man who\\\'s fed on nothing but bread and water. He wont die, is in no danger of dying, but he is nothing but a shell, a host for parasites with a fading mind.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe managers whispered furtively behind their hands, glancing over their crew, micromanaging the life out of us. Withholding information, and why shouldn\\\'t they? What did I need to know about anything to sit there and repeat the same 4 tasks at random for 8 hours a day? I needed to know nothing, and so I was told nothing. However helpful it would have been to have been told how to do my job, one does learn effectively by one\\\'s mistakes. And by the time you\\\'ve done it all wrong over and over, with no encouragement or guidance, your ego is shot, and you\\\'re ready to cog it. \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe customers though, the customers were never whispering, unless it was to ask if I knew where to buy used panties. That, they\\\'d stealthily whisper that to me over the phone, but on the whole, they were proud of their tastes, and trapped by their preferred genres. I answered their questions and took their money. If I could. Sometimes... I couldn\\\'t.\r\n</p><p>\r\nF rom: Fantasy Luvr<br>\r\nSent: Monday, February 10, 2003 10:22 AM<br>\r\nSubject: RE: Devyn Foster (porn star)<br>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nHi... I was wondering if you might be able to help me find a movie starring Devyn Foster in a scene with another guy.....the scene shows him and another guy in a bed that has like a clock headboard....and the first half of the scene shows this guy giving Devyn a really intense blow job......it\\\'s a very hot oral scene which then eventually progresses to Devyn topping the guy for anal sex. The other guy, whose name I don\\\'t know, has light sandy colored hair.  I know that this isn\\\'t much information; however, the key to finding this movie is the odd bed with the clock like headboard......I really want to know which movie that scene is from so that I can purchase it.......any help that you might be able to give would be appreciated.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthanks</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3323,'I have the perfect way to keep kids from having babies.Make them watch those shows that they have on TLC-\\\"Maternity Ward\\\" and the other one which is called \\\"Delivery Room\\\"(someone can correct me if I\\\'m wrong). I can\\\'t have kids anyways,but even before I became unable to have children, those shows definitely made me think seriously about having children.Whether I wanted to have children or not.\r\n\r\nWhen I was in high school we had to watch a movie with a bunch of women having babies.It left very little to the imagination. Some of the guys in the class were really grossed out by the movie.I wonder if, when their wives were in labor, they stayed and watched or not? Trust me, seeing a woman giving birth is not the prettiest thing you will ever see but as for it being gross?I have seen a lot worse on other TLC shows.\r\n\r\nOr have them watch the episode of \\\"ER\\\" where the woman died in childbirth.That episode freaked me out *really* bad.Of course, that situation with the woman\\\'s conditiion being treated entirely in the ER would never happen in real life,and they got a lot of the medical details in the show wrong.It doesn\\\'t mean it\\\'ll happen to *everyone*, but it\\\'s something to think about, definitely.\r\n\r\nThe most scary aspect of giving birth ,which should be pointed out to teenagers wanting to have children themselves is that it\\\'s far from painless(until you get a epidural, anyways).I have a low tolerance for pain,so I\\\'d be asking for an epidural as soon as humanly possible.\r\n\r\nOh and also, the financial cost of having a child.Teenagers wanting to having kids themselves should find out how much money it cost their parents to raise them.Sobering, isn\\\'t it?'),(3325,'there is something greater than myself, and it lives quietly within the four walls of my skull.<br><br>\r\ni can touch it, I have talked to it.<br><br>\r\ni feel it rubbing my neck now and then, pushing me through doors that I fear.<br><br>\r\ni do not know its name. i have never looked it in the eye.<br><br>\r\nit waits for me to get where I need to be. on that day it will either leave forever or consume me.<br><br>\r\nit binges on my desires and purges out dreams. It is my hunger, my mantra, my righteousness.<br><br>\r\neverything happens for a reason, and there is some purpose for each of us. '),(3332,'<P>i\\\'m eating chinese left overs; i stole them from my roommate.</P>\r\n<P>tonight i filed my taxes.</P>\r\n<P>tonight i drank a pint sized margarita, among other things.</P>\r\n<P>i lusted after lesbians.</P>\r\n<p>i saw a married couple use sign language to signal that it was time\r\nto go home for tittling and blow jobs.</P>\r\n<P>I HAVE SEEN THINGS IN THE DARKNESS OF ONCE A WEEK BAR LIFE</P>\r\n<P align=\\\"left\\\">by Austin Swinney</P>\r\n<P>who says you can\\\'t reheat the General\\\'s Chicken.</P>\r\n<P>who says i cannot charm the bartenderess into up-sizing my drink,\r\nother than me?</P>\r\n<P>i have charm and wit and when i wear contacts girls think i am\r\nhot.</P>\r\n<P>you who sits at home and viewers the internet.  speak now or stop\r\nthinking, you come here for why, to what end, TO LIVE AND LOVE AND\r\nFIND, PERHAPS, that perfect someone to spend the rest of your life\r\nwith, maybe in new york, maybe in melbourne, maybe in san francisco or\r\non a beach in new zealand.</P>\r\n<P>let this be a lesson to you, Mr. Scrooge, Mr. Grinch, Mr. thank me\r\nsir and take another, INSPIRATION IS YOUR ONLY SAVIOUR.</P>'),(3326,'No Bush supporter I; no undue warhawk either. However, it\\\'s good that the Iraqi people are now experiencing their first breath of freedom.  \r\n\r\n<P>Interesting to see that now that the yoke has been lifted from their collective necks, mass looting has occurred.  Sudden freedom has degenerated into anarchy (which of course is the ultimate freedom). Only time will see what shakes out.\r\n\r\n<p>And now that the statues have fallen, and remaining resistance is eventually cleared, it will also be interesting to see how the US will mediate this potentially chaotic state, and in that mediation, how the \\\"arab street\\\" may view our handling of petty crimes, curfews and civil crises.\r\n\r\n<p>No, I can\\\'t say I opposed this war. Nor can I say I supported it. To some degree there was no choice about going in, as Sadaam\\\'s \\\'regime\\\' toyed with UN resolutions and Hans Blix <i>cum</i> Neville Chamberlain, attempted to appease a madman with bureaucratic delays. While to some degree the war is about national economies (every polical warplanner understands an inherent relationship between the two), it does have collateral benefit for the survivors.\r\n\r\n<p>Decisions such as this have historically been made factoring in the loss of life, eg. \\\"the benefit of the many..the suffering of the few.\\\"  But what does it mean to me, as I drink my morning coffee and watch the rainclouds mass over the boston skyline?\r\n\r\n<P>I don\\\'t know\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what I\\\'m supposed to do; how I\\\'m supposed to protest; <i>if</i> I\\\'m supposed to protest. I don\\\'t jump on bandwagons, join crowds, mass together with the furrowed brow of human compassion.  And even if I did, how could I justify leaving a proud people in the hands of an madman who would just as soon kill and maim them as he would US \\\'invaders\\\'?\r\n\r\n<P>This war, for whatever reason it was fought behind closed doors, will have essential benefit for those Iraqis that will survive.  Freedom isn\\\'t free.  Prices must be paid.  But again, all we can do now is to wait and see what shakes out.  What will happen in Iraq after it is over?  How long will the US military police the streets of Baghdad? The proof of our altruism will depend upon what kind of democracy develops in this turbulent Arab nation.'),(3327,'<p>I met James in high school. We had a couple classes together but we never really talked.\r\n</p>      \r\n                                                                                 \r\n<center>\r\n<!--\r\n<img src=\\\"http://oddhatter.net/men/j/jamesh/hi1.gif\\\">\r\n-->\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n\r\n<p>So, I said hi.</p>  \r\n\r\n<center>    \r\n<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\">\r\n</center>        \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Hi...\\\", he said.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>He was a really spiffy guy and we got to know each other better. He made stage swords, shopped at the army surplus store and had a certain disdain for ants and snails. He also wore red flannel and had glasses that made him look like Harry Potter.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>We got along pretty well. I drew faeries and he drew things in armour. I read Paradise Lost and he read Beowulf. I believed in aliens and he believed in God. </p>  \r\n\r\n<p>He could read me better than anyone else. He knew that I fidgeted when I was nervous. He knew when I was lying when I told him I was doing alright. He listened better than anyone I knew.</p>         \r\n       \r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img3%%\\\">\r\n</center>         \r\n\r\n<p>He became a fixture in my little geeky high school social life. Probably my favourite fixture. But highschool was ending soon and we were going to graduate and go our separate ways. But before that, there was the senior prom.</p>    \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img4%%\\\">\r\n</center>            \r\n\r\n<p>My best friend Andrea told me he was just shy. \\\"Maybe you should ask him,\\\" she said, \\\"After all, it\\\'s not like you don\\\'t really like each other\\\".</p>    \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Heh.\\\" said Mr. Mitchell, my english teacher, and teased me mercilessly.</p>                                   \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"I asked Suzanne and she said yes\\\", said Evan. \\\"It\\\'s your turn to ask now, chicken.\\\" I wasn\\\'t chicken, I was just...scared. </p>  \r\n\r\n<p>In a sudden spurt of bravery, I decided. This time, I would be strong. This time, I would have courage. This time -- I would go alone. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>I went. </p> \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img5%%\\\">             \r\n</center>   \r\n \r\n<p>And that night, everything worked out right.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He told me he loved me later and I didn\\\'t know what to say. I didn\\\'t really know what the word meant anyway. But I guess we thought we\\\'d just close our eyes and see where fate brought us. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>It brought us to an interesting, confusing, frustrating and wonderful summer. But it ended soon, and I headed off to college. He was going to join the Marines and he was going off to boot camp soon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In a month, I told him I just wanted to be friends. \\\"We\\\'re better friends, anyway. If we were meant to be together, I\\\'m sure we\\\'ll meet up again sometime\\\", I insisted.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We made attempts at keeping in touch. He wrote me letters from boot camp, from training in Spain and from the USS Saipan en route to Kuwait. I wrote him letters from my dorm room. He came home a couple times, but I never managed to see him. His mother still asks about me. Sometimes, I wonder if fate, that bullshit excuse I gave him, will ever bring us together again. I suppose I just miss him, maybe as a friend, maybe as a little more.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Sometimes, I think of him. A part tells me not to be stupid. A part of me tells me it\\\'s about time I filed him off under \\\"ex boyfriends\\\" and let the matter rest.</p>\r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img6%%\\\">               \r\n</center>    \r\n\r\n<p>But a part of me wants him to appear out of nowhere and say \\\"Are you okay?\\\"</p> \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img7%%\\\">  \r\n</center>             \r\n\r\n<p>Even though I know that all I\\\'ll say is, \\\"I\\\'m fine. There\\\'s nothing wrong.\\\"</p>\r\n'),(3328,'<P>Matt used to like to take his metal detector to the beach and to parks and look for any kind of junk he could turn up.  His favorite thing to find was usually buried in sandboxes:  toy cars made of potmetal and rubber called \\\"Hot Wheels.\\\"  That\\\'s what Christian\\\'s car looks like - a big red Hot Wheels.  All it needs is a flame painted down each side.  He told me what kind of car it is.  Something Italian.</P><P>Christian\\\'s my roommate.  We\\\'re on our way back from Copenhagen, crossing the bridge to Malmo.  The water\\\'s grey and choppy,  a little after noon.  We came to Copenhagen last night, Friday night, after work.  We got there around one in the morning, stopped at a motor court outside of town.  Same room, separate beds.</P><P>We went to Copenhagen to go to the anarchist commune of Christiana.  It\\\'s an abandoned military base the hippies took over.  Inside, they sell weed, hash, and shrooms openly on Pusher\\\'s Street.  The police close down shop every few days, but their efforts are mainly for show.</P><P>It wasn\\\'t exactly dry in Stockholm, but what you can get on the street is shitty.  Chris moved here from Vienna six months ago and hasn\\\'t developed any good connections.  And all my rowdy friends have settled down.</P><P>Christian bought a huge sack of weed and a brick of hash.  He\\\'s an investment banker and a conspicuous consumer.  And a pothead.  He says what we don\\\'t smoke we can sell.  Then laughs.</P><P>Before we hit Sweden, I conceal the stuff on my person.  More accurately, I sit on it.  There\\\'s no problem going in; borders are pretty open now:  we drive right through and wave at the officials, sunny day, wearing sunglasses, stoned, the High Llamas on the CD player.  Chris lights the joint he rolled at the last rest area.  For the rest of the trip across the southland, we listen to The Sugarecubes\\\' first album, Al Green, Eno\\\'s work with vocals, and \\\"Sign O\\\' the Times.\\\"</P><P>I hooked up with Christian through an ad on the internet.  He was looking for a <U>female</U> roommate.  He was very specific about that.  I think he thinks he\\\'s going to get some ass.  Not if he doesn\\\'t try harder.  He\\\'s really very shy.  The only flirting he\\\'s done has been going shirtless around the apartment.  Plus, Per\\\'s coming over two nights a week, so that\\\'s kind of deterred him so far.</P><P>We get home.  I dig the stuff out of my purse and set it on the coffee table.  The apartment\\\'s huge:  it has a sunroom and a library and a gym, and he\\\'s charging me just three thousand kronor a month.  That\\\'s another sign that he\\\'s trying to scam some ass.  He also has a ton of books, in German and English.  That was the real selling point.</P><P>Chris cuts a cube of hash off the brick with a steak knife, then heats a corner of the cube with a lighter and crumbles it into the bowl of a three-foot ceramic hookah with two hoses.  He puts the fire to it.</P><P>I haven\\\'t been this high in years.  I\\\'m so high it\\\'s hard to hold conversation.  He turns on MTV, and we lay on the couch watching it for awhile, the chaotic rhythms of the editing and the blare of commercials settling over us like a blanket.</P><P>We hit the hookah some more.</P><P>\\\"This is like talking on the devil\\\'s telephone,\\\" he says.</P><P>\\\"What?\\\"</P><P>He repeats himself.</P><P>\\\"You\\\'re stoned.  It\\\'s official.\\\"</P><P>He puts on \\\"Forever Changes,\\\" and we lay on the hardwood floor behind the couch and let the sound caress us.  Halfway through \\\"The Red Telephone,\\\" he reaches out and runs his finger down my forearm.  I pull it away and give him a sharp look with the eyes and then a coy little grin with my mouth.</P><P>When the album\\\'s halfway over, I pick out \\\"Horses\\\" to play next, and we struggle through making sweet and sour chicken.  We eat it with a bottle of Anjou and Billie Holiday playing.  Afterwards, Christian puts weed and hash through a coffee grinder and rolls an after-dinner joint.</P><P>We sit on the couch and smoke.  He puts a tape of fractal patterns in the VCR so we can zone out on something.  We\\\'ve been watching it about five minutes when his hand finds its way onto my thigh somehow.  I take it off and drop it in his lap.</P><P>Five minutes later, I look over, and he\\\'s staring at me with these big red glassy doe eyes.  This is getting kind of tiresome.</P><P>I say, \\\"Listen.  There\\\'s an American expression called \\\'boxing the Jesuit.\\\'  Do you know what that means?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No.\\\"</P><P>\\\"How about \\\'tuning the antenna\\\'?\\\"</P><P>\\\"\\\'Shaking hands with the governor\\\'?\\\"</P><P>\\\"\\\'Hanging with the Jackson Five\\\'?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No.  None of those.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, you\\\'re about to find out.  Take your pants off.\\\"</P><P>He looks shocked, then grins wolfishly.  I unbuckle his pants and pull them and his underwear down and around his shoes.  He starts playing with himself.  I go into my bedroom and get some lotion, come back into the living room and spread it on his thing.  It\\\'s a pretty big thing.  Not the biggest I\\\'ve ever seen, but pretty big.</P><P>\\\"Stand up on your knees and turn around,\\\" I tell him.</P><P>He\\\'s kneeling on the couch, facing away from me.  I reach around and stroke.  And stroke.  Slower.  Faster.  Stop and start.  Squeeze his balls and caress his ass.  He takes off his T-shirt and rubs his nipples.  He\\\'s got a sweet body on him.</P><P>\\\"Yell when you\\\'re about to come.\\\"</P><P>He tenses up.</P><P>\\\"OK,\\\" he says.  Loud.</P><P>I form a ring with my thumb and forefinger and encircle the top of his scrotum, pull his balls down gently.  Keep stroking.</P><P>After a while, he signals me again.  I squeeze where the head meets the shaft.  I tell him to get up.  We stand, pointed away from the TV.  I push him so that he\\\'s hanging over the back of the couch, far enough over so he won\\\'t drip on the fabric or the strip of rug sticking out at the bottom.  I pump his cock really fast.  When he comes, he shoots two meters out onto the hardwood floor, nearly hitting the back wall.</P><P>\\\"And now you know,\\\" I say.</P><P>I let him clean up while I go wash my hands.  I go to bed and finish <U>The Last Tycoon</U>.  But that\\\'s the thing about unfinished novels:  can you ever complete them?</P>'),(3329,'asdfsdf!!!!!!!'),(3330,'<p>\r\nit started with that strange feeling of listening to a band you haven\\\'t heard in ages and knowing all the words.  that sort of thing just throws you back into another time when this was normal.  and once you\\\'re in that mindset, you start to remember everything.  in this case, it was why i haven\\\'t listened to the band in so long.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na boy.  one of many, but this one more significant than the others.  what we had was wonderful, what we lost was great, what we became was terrible.  it\\\'s perfectly ordinary for things to go that way.  i think the only significant difference between all the people we lose is what stays with us because of them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin this instance, there was a good deal of shame.  it\\\'s one thing to ignore what someone who knows nothing about you says.  it\\\'s another to disregard the insults thrown your way by someone who was once worth more than anything.  <i>maybe he really knows me</i>, you think.  <i>maybe he\\\'s right</i>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere are a lot of things that have stayed with me like this - a belief that weighing 120 pounds makes me fat, a hatred of brushing my teeth in front of strangers because i must somehow be doing it in a way that is different and wrong, a fear that i really should feel guilty if i don\\\'t give someone what they want (regardless of my own wishes).  but this shame upon listening to a cd which i was mocked for liking . . . it means more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow ridiculous must i be to criticize myself for something like that?  why does the opinion of someone i haven\\\'t spoken to in months matter?  how can i bear to look at myself when i realize that every part of me is based on a fear of disappointing someone?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmaybe it\\\'s more than that.  maybe we all absorb the awful things people tell us to draw back out years later and examine.  maybe it\\\'s important to have these memories to remind us why we\\\'ve forgotten the moments we wanted to hold on to forever.  maybe.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nor maybe i\\\'m just a coward who is too scared to admit that she turned off the cd player as soon as she remembered to be ashamed.\r\n</p>'),(3331,'Jane Doe, she finally fell asleep; she finally died. There were blood mice falling from her eyes. Her inane sense of becoming bitter, laughing, lit up... she let go of living. She was celebrating something. Something had arrived. She hid. She became beautiful. Some say it was murder, some say suicide.<br><br>\r\nA man cracked in sidewalks walked with walls. <i>Jane Doe died</i>. Someone inside may have broken her heart; she may have broken a bridge.<br><Br>\r\nShe managed to write out a few lines, assymetrical: <i>\\\"When you fall in, dont look back\\\".</i><br><br> \r\nShe fell from grace; a broken ball-joint \r\ncomplex when she died. When she died she left one hair for me, a push-pin unlike pain.<br><Br>\r\nI wonder like a child to see if she will come back.'),(3333,'<p>\r\nIf you walk the streets of Seoul during the daytime, you are bound to come across some junior hotties.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThey prance around in their beautiful school uniforms; the skirts usually below the knees. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I come across a flock of junior hotties I become entranced by their little outfits. I can\\\'t help but check\\\'em out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor the most part my love of the school uniform emerged when I was in high school. For the first three years of high school I attended a Roman Catholic all boys school. But for the last two years of high school I transferred to a co-ed Roman Catholic school and HOLY COW. The outfits just turned me on so much walking up and down the halls. I can remember jerking off in the bathroom stalls of my highschool when things just got out of hand. *or in hand...har har*\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nBut now I am a grown man. Yet I cannot stop looking at those little girls in their little outfits. Am I a sick man? I dont think so. I dont really dream of pulling one off the street an into the back of my unmarked 1983 Dodge Van. I just like to look at them. I just dig\\\'em. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm the most passive dirty old man on earth. I guess some might think im twisted, but my god man! THOSE SKIRTS! THOSE JACKETS! THOSE KNEE SOCKS! \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat I dream of is getting my girlfriend to dress up in a school uniform. To see my girlfriend in a school uniform would perhaps cure the terrible case of insomnia I have. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone should have a school uniform!\r\n</p>'),(3334,'I see them sitting before me, I can hear their laughter, see their smiles, listen to the stories. I know the faces well, the reflections in glass and chrome around us, the familiar sounds and smeels of our haunts.<br><br>\r\nThe numbness has come back to my hands, there is a ringing in my ears once more, all feels as if I am underwater.<br><br>\r\nThe frayed wires once connecting me to the world around wear thin with every passing day.<br><br>\r\nI wanted to be among people tonight, but in the crowd I feel alone. Perhaps one more trip, one more journey. Start anew in a familiar but unworn place. A little sanctity  in anonymity again. Reinvention, cowardice, audacity.<br><br>\r\nThey will see me through.'),(3335,'<p>i went for a walk back to the suburb where i grew up the other day. i guess it was one of those post-birthday, early-mid-life-crisis, what am i doing with my life, go and find my roots kind of deals.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it struck me at once how small and drab the whole place is now. that being said i don\\\'t think it has changed all that much. seventeen years is a long time i know, but the whole place seemed to have, well... shrunk.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remembered the mall as being huge. pottering around the comic shop as a kid, getting lost in the supermarket. all the ususal stuff.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but now i could barely fit in the place. the flowers and other stands outside shops leaving little or no room for a 6\\\'5\\\" lumbering freak to brush past without inflicting some kind of harm.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the house looked miniscule. the once towering wrought-iron gates were barely taller than me. i could see into my old bedroom. i recalled running from one end of the room to another, and waving padded foam swords at imaginary dragons. god i must have been so small.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i remembered the bad things too.</p>\r\n<p>lying awake in that room, terrified. wondering when the high-school would next phone my family to ask them if i was alright because i hadn\\\'t been to class in so long. waiting for the day when my parents discovered i had emptied my savings account...</p>\r\n<p>the park was still mammoth in scale. the tiny playground and ankle-deep paddling pool seemed almost comical next to the acres of open field.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the pine forest covering the hill at the far end brought back memories of days as a cub scout. terrifying tales of what horrid initiations they had planned for us when we graduated to full scouts. blazing a trail for our orienteering patches only to have one of the signs roll away and nearly send a group of keen ten-year-olds over the wrong hill and into a dangerous gully...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the school.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if what they say is true. that those years from 5-12 are your defining ones. that what happens to you then will basically create the person you will be for the rest of your life, well... let\\\'s face it folks, i\\\'m fucked.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i tried to swing on the old rope poles, to re-live the rush of the monkey bars and high platform walks. but my feet touched the ground the whole time on the first two. the high walk didn\\\'t seem so high any more.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i couldn\\\'t believe the sandpit had gone. the hallowed ground of the pentagonal pit. where so many gladiatorial battles had settled differences long-simmering in the classroom.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the war of the stolen pencil eraser came to an end on that sacred  patch of dirt. where i smited the scrawny daniel (or was it paul?) somethingorother by shoving fistfuls of muddy sand down his shirt. he punched me and my lip bled. and i tried to dribble more as i walked away victorious because despite the swelling and stinging sensation i was sure the blood looked cool.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>they cut down our tree.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i had my first kiss in that tree with kim. my friend sean and i played tag-team doctor with hariata and susan. and they cut the damn thing down.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i see some of my old classmates around wellington from time to time. they look old, tired. the people around me in the early years who so awed, terrified or besotted me now seem so unimportant.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>isn\\\'t it funny how your whole world for eight years, can eventually become such a distant memory.</p>'),(3336,'<p>\r\nTHEY MUST HAVE BEEN MOVED TO SYRIA!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThose weapons of mass destruction. They were given away. They were sent airmail. They were buried under layers and layers of curruption and lies. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat is up with this new and very aggressive stance the United States has now undertaken with Syria. What is it now? It sure as hell isn\\\'t the triangle of evil anymore. Its now the square of evil. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHOLY SHIT ITS NOT JUST A TRIANGLE...ITS A SQUARE!!!! WHATS NEXT? AN OCTAGON? WE ARE DOOMED.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLets just think about this. The Bahgdad Museum was looted of terribly priceless art. Some say many of the items stolen were \\\"picked\\\" and that their theft was \\\"premeditated\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith the fall of the Saddam regime the various buildings and infastructure of the country and its history was looted and destroyed. If we can say that some of the art was stolen rather then just looted, how can we say that these \\\"still hidden\\\" weapons of mass destruction arent being \\\"looted\\\" like the rest of the country. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI think its funny that the United States and Britain have untertaken to bomb the living shit out of Iraq, occupy it, and yet they had never thought about the day after. They claim they have things under control, but i fear a bigger can of worms has been opened. I fear that saddam would have been more handy as a besieged despot, rather than a rich man on the run. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is a lot going on i\\\'m not that happy with. On the one hand you have the United States telling the world it wants Iraq to be in the hands of iraqis, but then when nobody is looking they send shitloads of currency over there to replace the iraqi dinar. whats with that? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOk well I just dont know whats going to happen. I think this Syria thing is just too stupid. Back off and take a breather Busy Poo!\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3337,'Cheers for misleading titles! <br><br>\r\n\r\nI rang in the beginning of this year smoking Korean cigarettes, listening to Scandinavian radio (playing music from south East Asia), shifting politics, and single. Oh, and my job was making sandwiches for venture capitalists to complain about after their luncheon meetings. Aside from the Scandinavian radio not really one of the better times in my life, really.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWell, I\\\'m not making sandwiches anymore but I miss the capitalists, it made me feel good to realize that I had a better grasp on the global markets then the people funding so much of their development. Things are actually been becoming easier to bear lately.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMy moral relativism has proven indispensable this year with all the political <i>goings on</i>, I\\\'ve simply taken both sides of major events and tried to piss them off the best I can. Each one claims higher moral ground based on notions that human life has some intrinsic dignity/value when really we\\\'re all just space junk. Many people get really upset about the games played between members of the global elite but when it really comes down to it, the most likely thing we will feel long term from all of this is mild economic benefit (only if you believe in <i>trickle down</i>) and some additional air pollution. For those with loved ones involved, I empathize. Otherwise the infotainment stopped being entertaining after the first week, I just don\\\'t like mini-series.<br><br>\r\n\r\nRelativism has also spilled over into my personal life in a variety of ways. It has allowed me to justify having a relationship with a female that is a lot younger then I am for one. Her being of an <i>artistic</i> bent means she has stirred my own creative juices and generally has a lot of heart. It is this heart of hers that has her trying to mitigate my negative view of most of us humans by patiently questioning bits of my long hot pissy diatribes, when I don\\\'t freak her out too much. That, in and of itself, is one big reason to love her. The second is that she works at a custom T-shirt shop and has promised me a 3/4 length retro Pat Benatar Concert T-shirt. I initially felt guilty about the age thing, like I was exploiting her or some shit, but after some thought realized that as long as there is respect I should be safe from any personal qualms about exploitation.<br><br>    \r\n\r\nThe relativism also played a big role in coming to terms with certain friends of mine that were developing the new habit of frequent visits to <i>rub and tugs</i>. This just as I started dating the <i>quasi-Lolita</i>, seemed best at the time just to let things go. I wasn\\\'t ready to deal with things when all this started if any scrutiny was directed at my own situation. I\\\'d feel better about the whole thing if sex workers were unionized and the industry regulated, follow the Dutch on this one.<br><br>    \r\n\r\nAnyway, the relativism also seems to have landed me a job. It was due to my nasty habit of sniping at all sides that I was endeared to an old co-worker that provided me with a hook-up. Now it looks like I might have security too, a year or more possibly. All this has allowed me to start things like RRSP\\\'s and socially responsible investment, which in turn due to their heavy adult character have caused my personality to become more infantile or ridiculous.<br><br>  \r\n\r\nAll in all being relativistic about things has made me mutable to the point of being able to cope. I worried as I saw this happening about becoming paralyzed due to a lack of moral ground from which to launch myself but quickly realized that this can be made up for simply by substituting in more ego. What does all this matter to anyone else though? Iâ€™m generally happy and content in my own little individual relativistic world butâ€¦<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt was relatively easy to achieve happiness, it required for me a complete pull away from community and the adoption of a balls out (<i>ovaries in?</i>) attitude, of screw the rest.<br><br> \r\n'),(3339,'<P>I can\\\'t speak for the others,<BR>\r\nbut for me there is very little<BR>\r\ninteresting to say about<BR>\r\npoetry. \r\n\r\n<P>The word itself<BR>\r\nstalls my mind. \r\n\r\n<P>I\\\'m often<BR> \r\nembarassed to admit that I\\\'ve<BR>\r\nbeen fooling around with the poem,<BR>\r\neven when it\\\'s the only thing I\\\'m writing. \r\n\r\n<P>I find myself at parties<BR> \r\nwith people who make things:<BR>\r\nsculptors, painters,<BR>\r\npeople who can bake bread. \r\n\r\n<P>I should have been a baker<BR>\r\nor a blacksmith,<BR>\r\nsomething that \r\nrequires me to<BR>\r\nreach into flames.<BR>\r\nI would like to mix bowls of batter<BR>\r\nor clang a hammer <BR>\r\nagainst glowing metal.\r\n\r\n<P>And at these parties<BR>\r\nI reach for the whiskey or<BR>\r\nstand close to the cheese,<BR>\r\ntrying to avoid the topic<BR>\r\nof my work. \r\n\r\n<P>The absolute worst is when I meet<BR>\r\nanother poet<BR>\r\nand we sniff around each other<BR>\r\nas if detecting some faint stink. \r\n\r\n<P>Again, I can\\\'t speak for the others<BR>\r\nBut confronted like this,<BR>\r\nwith one of my own,<BR>\r\nI feel the temperature in the room rise:<BR>\r\n\\\"Can you recite one of your poems?<BR>\r\nWho are your influences?\\\" \r\n\r\n<P>\\\"Isn\\\'t it getting hot in here?\\\" I ask.<BR>\r\n\\\"I really must get a breath of fresh air.<BR>\r\nNice to meet you.\\\"<BR>\r\nAnd then I\\\'m out <BR>\r\non the balcony or the porch,<BR>\r\nstaring at what I can see <BR>\r\nof the light-drowned stars<BR>\r\nand wishing I still smoked cigarettes. \r\n\r\n<P>I should have been a baker. <BR>\r\n\r\n<P>A loaf of sourdough<BR>\r\nhot from the oven<BR>\r\nis better than any poem \r\n\r\n<P>and nobody ever asks<BR>\r\nabout your<BR>\r\ninfluences. \r\n'),(3340,'In Los Angeles, it\\\'s pronounced <i>Los Lobos</i>, not like <i>Las Vegas</i>; as so many<br> people do, you can have a house or live off a train. With the house, a yarded<br> fence is optional. \\\"A Garden Variety\\\" Lets say you pick one/a flower/an<br> orange/ one with a backyard.<br><Br> \r\nLet\\\'s say you\\\'re digging a hole to China in your backyard. Within the first week your<br> neighbors (for no business of their own) want to know your activities. It is only for<br> talk.<br><Br>Word of mouth.<br><Br>The gift of the gab.<br><Br> \r\nLet\\\'s take into consideration that your house stands, not with two feet, but with one and<br> an ankle with a bracelet, and the one whom said this to you was a bit<br> toothless<br> and without restraint spoke in a context of having a pattern of speech that<br> would resemble sound like in nature you find the peace that forever is imprinted<br> in all of molecular cells.<br><Br> \r\nBy the end of the month you\\\'ve been in and around China to presuppose fashion. You dont<br> stop in the middle of the earth to see what its like there. To get there you<br> hold your breath, and keep your mind, your mind\\\'s eye; your thoughts like a knife<br> (your<br> whole being razor sharp as all that would care to attempt to interrupt your<br> process would become cut).<br><Br>\r\nAn idea by which one may use as example in comparison is either a rocket, or a flaming<br> comet. Once you get your system <i>down pat</i> and are traveling to where<br> you want to be (whether it is in John Lennons words \\\"Across the Universe\\\" or<br> onto another one) that house you\\\'re living in, with your wife and kids, that now<br> stands on its head at times for others to be afraid of, the deer and blue-jays<br> that your kids love to feed, have picked up on the hatred vibe from the humans<br> around you because of sensing your accomplishments, and are jealous and envious of<br> them.<br><Br>\r\nIn order to get back those deer and blue-jays, all you have to do is secretly erect your<br> flag by projecting the sun.<br><Br>\r\nProjecting the sun.<br><Br>\r\n\r\n<pre> \r\n<i>Once upon a girl like time printed France in my photo off of a printing pressed, like<br> a nikel head, something in the middle, like a cookie, she turned the photo into underwear<br> and she should have sold them by now we were thinking, a bra, funny light<br> in the sky. It seemed to fall off, or into a ocean of seeded ideas.</i></pre> \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3341,'a few weeks ago i was in barnes and noble and realized that all of the books for \\\"young adults\\\" and \\\"intermediate readers\\\" were books that had so little to do with the real lives of kids today, it was time to write a series about and for kids who really exist...\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nkids who have divorced parents who hate each other and don\\\'t get child support, moms who date boobs and creeps, dads who drink beer and fall asleep on the couch.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbooks for kids who don\\\'t fit in with the teevee model of families.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbooks with humour and hope.\r\n<br><br>\r\nso i decided i would see if i could do it, and perhaps this will be the only fiction project i ever complete, but maybe it will make kids with holey underwear laugh.'),(3342,'i wasn\\\'t allowed to join the girl scouts as a youth because my mother thought they were a \\\"paramilitary organization.\\\" whatever. now i work in the south bronx and the girl scouts seems like a better way for a kid to spend an afternoon than, say, smoking crack. \r\n\r\nso i am a girl scout troop leader.\r\n\r\nnow, those of you who are familiar with the operation will know that cookies--samoas, do-si-does, thin mints and the like-- are girl scout business. every year these women rake in beaucoup bucks peddling baked goods all across america. never underestimate the business sense of a fifteen-year old; even in our economically depressed neighborhood, my troop raised $1300 in cookie sales.\r\n\r\nyay! you might say. only when i called the girl scout headquarters, a galaxy away in manhattan, i was abruptly informed that they had no cookies for us. we, troop xxxxx of the south bronx, had been misinformed about the selling dates. \r\n\r\ngirl scout lady: \\\"you never should have been selling in this first place! everyone in manhattan is long done--where have you people in the south bronx been getting your information? all the cookie cupboards have been closed for over a month.\\\"\r\nme: \\\"so why did you give me the forms two weeks ago?\\\"\r\ngirl scout lady: \\\"......\\\"\r\n\r\ni was a little bit irate. so i enlisted three other angry teachers to go down to the headquarters. we stormed the building guevara-style and demanded our cookies. there was no way we were going to back down. we were prepared to pull out the race card if we had to, because we had no shame. after an hour of haggling and angry gesticulation, they admitted defeat i tell you, it was like david and goliath.  we emerged victorious to midtown manhattan with 396 boxes of girl scout cookies. \r\n\r\nand no car.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3343,'<p>\\\'teardrop\\\'â€ (massive attack) throbs through the room.  there is the slap of bodies onto mats, the thuds of warming muscles and limbering.  the smell is of sweat and vinyl, the chatter that of women ranging from 16 upwards.  this is mania, this is the sportsfighting gym i attend at least twice a week.  i learn, along with about 100 other women around auckland a combination of jujitsu, aikido and wrestling.  it is women only.  there are no weapons. </p><p>\r\nmy body definition and strength grow, my energy rises.  however i field questions all the time.  isnâ€™t that aggressive?  how can you condone the violence?  why is that okay and boxing not?  the fights/spars are not about violence and power plays for me.  we donâ€™t kick or punch, thereâ€™s never any gushing blood or facial mutilation.  yes, when one person elicits a submission, they hold power.  but itâ€™s only total power for that brief second.  the clock is immediately set again and the floor levels.  </p><p>\r\nthis is about pushing my own boundaries.  itâ€™s about finding my own style, gaining confidence and working my body, learning to have better control over myself.  if youâ€™d told me last june that i would fight other women in a sports bra and hotpants i would have laughed in your face. \r\n</p><p>\r\nafter my first spar the trainer told me that my mean streak will work for me in the ring and will lessen in the world.  so far i have found this to be true to a large extent and i get a kind of â€˜cabin feverâ€™ in weeks when i donâ€™t train or donâ€™t train hard enough.</p><p>mania isnâ€™t just about the straight fights either.  integrated are self-defence trips and strategies for the girl about town (or country!).  i feel more confident when by myself in the dark or if i get separated from my friends.  no matter what we wish for, even the quietest nz streets arenâ€™t safe and the importance of self-defence canâ€™t be over-rated.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwhen i climb through the ropes and get into the ring i realise that i have nothing to lose here.  this is just body against body, flesh against flesh.  our eyes meet, we size each other up.  edging around like electrons circling in a negative nucleus, block block and then someoneâ€™s in a headlock.  pull into your chest and down and weâ€™re away.\r\n</p><p>\r\nsweat glistens, slick on limbs.  occasional encouragement comes from the sidelines, a countdown of remaining time is provided but essentially itâ€™s two of us, in a ring, together. if we were in a movie, weâ€™d be slow motion, silent/with distant sound, the camera zooming in on a furrowed brow, a look of concentration, the theory being put into practice.  \r\n</p><p>\r\ngroundwork is the scales and exercises, yes, but now weâ€™re playing the entire piece, completely unabridged.  the whole three movements, no matter how much full body exhaustion tries to keep you on the mat, gasping for air.  controlled but dynamic movement â€“ action breath gasp push work move reach press â€“ similar to a rough dance. muscles tense, vibrate, stretch out. \r\n</p><p>\r\nthe ache, the stiffness, the smell â€“ these are all aspects to revel in after the event.  itâ€™s girl on girl action, in one of the best possible senses.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3344,'i must be one, i mean it\\\'s the only way to explain the feelings i\\\'m having. i am a nasty, vile, racist asshole. i am the kind of person i have always despised.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni caught myself yesterday. for a moment i hadn\\\'t noticed what i was doing, and then it sort of hit me in a wave of self-awareness and mild revulsion.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis large group of asian (chinese i think) people were wandering down queen street; okay so nothing unusual for auckland at all. one of them, a middle-aged woman, was wearing one of those white protective masks we keep seeing plastered all over the news with the SARS outbreak in full swing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nand i caught myself moving to stand as far away as possible.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe lights couldn\\\'t change quickly enough for me to cross the road and get away.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat was my subconcious bigotry screaming to me i wonder? \\\"run away! outcast unclean! flee! save yourself!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\na white mask is no cause for alarm. it had to be the combination of the mask and their race. by definition therefore, i was guilty of racial prejudice.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe more i though about it, the more i became aware of other niggly little things i could find to be angry about with asian people.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe way all those young, wealthy, arrogant men treated myself and the other locals in the student hall where i worked for two years. all the talking and loud translating at the movies which drives me nuts...<br><br>\r\n\r\ni could go on and on. i got more and more worried about it. was this really the end of my determined left-wing approach to life? had i been wrong about myself all along? am i just a closet facist waiting to get rich so i don\\\'t have to pretend anymore?<br><br>\r\n\r\ni can rationalise most of it away if i try. things like, okay so most of the chinese people i meet are indeed, spoilt arrogant little rich boys who would be exactly the same, maybe a lot worse, if they were new zealanders, europeans or americans. people have cultural differences and because i can\\\'t adjust to them doesn\\\'t mean it\\\'s a failure on their part.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut that feeling kept nagging away at me... \\\"my home, my country, i\\\'m not the visitor, stop being so rude to me...\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe \\\"my country\\\" shouldn\\\'t enter into it, i should just be thinking \\\"god this guy is rude\\\". the distinction about his race shouldn\\\'t even cross my mind.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat happened to the angry, politically sound man who yelled \\\"oh you fucking asshole!\\\" at charlton heston during a screening of bowling for columbine, and embarrassed the group of friends he was with? had i gone over the edge so quickly?<br><br>\r\n\r\nagain i can rationalise this one away if i squeeze my eyes shut really tight and strain till the veins in my forehead threaten to pop.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif someone annoys us, we look for the differences and scowl about them... foreigner, rich bastard, broke dole-bludger, fatso, skinny bitch... there\\\'s a million things we can find fault with each-other when we don\\\'t get to cross the road in a straight line and have to be the one to move or get bumped into...<br><br>\r\n\r\ni just can\\\'t escape the feeling that i\\\'ve lost something lately. we should all be better than this but we\\\'re not, least of all me. and it bothers me.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni guess what scares me is the thought that, just maybe, if the world was populated with identical clones, there would be wars over what colour t-shirts people chose to wear.<br><br>'),(3345,'dear ms. amanda.\r\n\r\ndid you know that nz is on a huge volcanic rift? part\r\nof the great \\\'ring of fire\\\' of song and story\r\n(including from our very own johnny cash?\r\n\r\nof course you did.\r\n\r\ni didn\\\'t, but i just found out.\r\n\r\nthank you very much for your concern and interest. i\\\'m\r\nstill at it, writing, putting words up against the\r\ndoor to keep the pollen from spring-blooming apple\r\ntrees out.\r\n\r\nyes indeedy, but after meeting mr austin swinney, and\r\nthen having it out with the pro registration\r\nanti-registration crowds, i decided to leave such\r\nenforced rudeness.\r\n\r\nthe arguments are tiresome and i do not want to get\r\ninto them here, but basically mr austin, quite\r\nrightly, argued \\\'this is my website, i register as i\r\nlike.\\\'\r\n\r\nand so he did.\r\nand so i didn\\\'t.\r\nso i left.\r\n\r\ni love the possibilities of art that can happen, anon,\r\nwith nome de plume, or nome de guerre, and mr swinney\r\ndoes so hate spammers and worse anon, smart alecks, of\r\nwhich i was one.\r\n\r\ni told him it would kill the site, but i see it\r\nswinneys on.\r\n\r\ni\\\'m putting some doggerel up here.\r\n\r\nhttp://coyoboy.blogspot.com/\r\n\r\nby the by, selling grandpa on ebay is a true story,\r\noddly enough.\r\n\r\na fellow approached me about a heretofore unknown\r\nphoto of said relative available for bid on the site. horrible.\r\n'),(3346,''),(3349,'<p>\r\nThe port that connects Phenom Pehn with the Ankor Wat(or Vat) town of Siem Reap is classic. I had to switch boats three times just to get to the clumsy planks resting on the trunks of trees and limbs. I almost went in three times. Thank god those Khemer dudes trying to get me to stay in their guesthouses were there to help me with my balance. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLuckily, I had pre-arranged a driver and so I just looked for my name scrawled on a very tiny piece of paper. Ali was his name, driving fast was his game. Ali ripped shit up on his 100cc Honda Dream. It was the bumpiest most swerviest ride in my life. 99% of the roads in Cambodia are unpaved tracks of dirt, rock, sand, very large pot holes, and speed bumps of all sizes and shapes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSpeed bumps were of no concern to Ali. He\\\'d forgotten a roast in the oven I suspected, but it just turns out he knows the road better then I had expected. Even though his route was alltogether eratic, after being on the back for five minutes I felt safe on his bike.(he\\\'s been my driver for three days now)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve done a lot of chit chatting with Ali. His English is quite good and he is teaching himself Japanese. Ali is quite smitten with his Japanese girlfriend. He even let me read a love letter written to him from her. \\\"didnt want to profess then but did\\\" and all sorts of other cliche love letter type things. It was sweet, but I only read half of it. I was on the back going at about 110km/h. I didn\\\'t want those pages of passion and lust to fall victim to the wind.\r\n</p>\r\nThe sky in Siem Reap is crackling with lightning right now but earlier today it was bright, sunny, and hot. We were going to see a small waterfall up in the hills that had carvings on the stones as well as the river floor. Ali called the route to the waterfall a \\\"walking path\\\". The \\\"walking path\\\" that lead to the waterfall turned out to be the hike of a lifetime for me. It was only 3km, but it was rock and root infested. For most of the way I felt like I was climbing the decayed steps of one of the temples, then i\\\'d trip on a root and Ali would laugh. Apparently in a year they will have Elephant rides up to the waterfall and rock carvings, but these days they just do it the old fashioned way.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe waterfall was small due to it being the dry season but the rock carvings on the river floor were pretty impressive. It didn\\\'t really matter at the time because I was tired, sweaty, and dying of thirst. I was literally soaked through with sweat. Ali kept me at ease with stories of how people steal the carvings to sell in Thailand, and how the Thai people want to make war with Cambodia so that they can once again reclaim Ankor for themselves. Siem Reap translated means Siamese Defeated. It is an area of this world that has the posibility of making billions. Ankor Wat and the temples around it are amazing, though dumb fucks have carved their names and messages all over the place. Even in trees!\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nAli has told me stories of tourists getting caught in the act and seeing them being beaten by Khemer police. I say \\\"fucking right\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSiem Reap has been a far better experience having the same driver everyday. I highly recommend it to anyone who travels here. If you are ever in Siem Reap, look for Ali and his bushy hair. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nembedded at Ankor, this is Chiamattt signing off. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3347,'<p>\r\nEddy came to the States about three years ago; he spoke no English.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHis first job was at a gas station in Chicago, near the intersection of Roosevelt and Independence.  Most people from Chicago aren\\\'t familiar with the intersection of Roosevelt and Independence: it\\\'s in the ghetto.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt that gas station, Eddy\\\'s customers were almost all Black, and almost all poor; they were the ones from whom he learned how to communicate, in America.  Eddy was taught to say: \\\"Hey homey, what\\\'s up?\\\" and, \\\"Yo, dawg, gimme some White Owls!\\\"  And he\\\'s a rather proficient executioner of the hand gestures that accompany the speech.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEvery night before I drop my taxi, I gas it up.  Eddy now works at the gas station that I go to; he works seven nights a week, just like me.  I stop at a pump; I fill the tank; I go inside the station to pay; I talk to Eddy.  It\\\'s the same thing every night: for about thirty minutes, Eddy and I talk to each other about business, crime, sex, and Islam.  Eddy\\\'s given me a book called, \\\"Towards Understanding Islam,\\\" along with an English translation of the Qur\\\'an.\r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n<p>\r\nI am Arab; Eddy is not Arab.  Eddy\\\'s family is from Pakistan, and he has relatives in India.  But, especially since 9/11, people have given Eddy a hard time.  As far as I can tell, every city around the globe has its fair share of assholes.  And when people are down, they tend to look for someone whom they perceive as being even lower - so they can vent their impotent rage.  Eddy\\\'s dark, and he\\\'s Muslim, and English isn\\\'t his native language.  Eddy is not as large, strong, or intense as I am.  About one hundred people a week tell me that I am, \\\"Italian looking,\\\" and also that I have a British accent; they don\\\'t say that to Eddy - they say other things to him.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou\\\'re more likely to be murdered while driving a taxi in the U.S.A. than while doing anything else.  But after a taxi driver, the person who is next most likely to be murdered is a convenience store worker - like Eddy.  In the past few weeks, a taxi driver and a convenience store worker were both murdered in Chicago - not too far from Eddy\\\'s gas station.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe taxi driver was Pakistani; the convenience store worker was Indian.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSupposedly, the taxi driver was killed in the course of a robbery.  And the guy who worked at the convenience store was killed because he put too much sugar in someone\\\'s coffee; that\\\'s what his life was worth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMost of the guys like Eddy don\\\'t make very much money in the States; but they almost always send a portion of what they earn back to the place that they came from.  Sometimes that money puts a roof over someone\\\'s head; sometimes it puts food on the table; sometimes it prevents someone\\\'s daughter from being sold.  Eddy stands - night after night - in front of a rack of porno mags that he doesn\\\'t read, and condoms that he doesn\\\'t use.  He has a fiancÃ©e in Pakistan, and he\\\'s waiting for her.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEddy\\\'s real name in Adnan, and he\\\'s a good fuckin\\\' guy.\r\n</p>');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (3348,'BMÃšÃ’Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬Ã¿â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬â‚¬'),(3350,'<p>quietly she climbs the steps, wincing as she forgets which creaks.  the sound is aberrant in the still night air.  she eases her key into the lock and turns slowly.  all sound must be minimised to escape detection.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>blue light from the television flickers against the back windows.  the rest of the house is dark.  he\\\'s home.  she don\\\'t need to go down to the lounge to know that he is slumped in an armchair, cigarette in one hand, bourbon in the other.  it\\\'s the same, night after night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she concentrates on walking almost soundlessly down the hallway to their bedroom door.  heel-to-toe, heel-to-toe, a tightrope walker concentrating on control of her limbs.  a creaking floorboard betrays her, and he calls out.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"you\\\'re home late.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"i went with the crew for a couple of drinks after the show.  nothing major.  sleepy now, going to bed.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"i\\\'ll be down soon.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she undresses quickly in the dark, and slips into bed.  the coolness of the sheets makes her shiver, and she rubs her legs until the friction makes her skin warm to the touch.  curling up on her side she tries to force sleep as quickly as possible. her mind is racing; she knows she has to get to sleep before he finishes his drink.  it\\\'s futile.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>opting to feign sleep as she hears the door open, she doesn\\\'t respond as he talks at the prone body in the bed.  she has to force herself to stay still as he comes in behind her and puts his arm over and around her waist.  his hand moves down onto her hip and thigh.  she tries to slow her breathing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the big hands she used to love move back onto her abdomen, and he kisses her shoulder.  he mumbles into her back, something about missing her.  she\\\'s wishing desperately that he would get the hint.  he reeks of alcohol, and the smell turns her stomach.  she doesn\\\'t want another sleepless night of fighting - him yelling, her crying, having to make up a story at work tomorrow about why her face is pale, the shadows \r\nunder her eyes.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>they\\\'ve always fought, but the fights used to be electric - fuelled by passion.  the make-up sex was always intense.  she\\\'s never known anyone else like him, or the way she knows him.  and yet, despite all of this, they haven\\\'t had sex in a month.  she doesn\\\'t even want to him to touch her and she tries to shift her position away from him slightly.  she hopes it looks natural, but knows it doesn\\\'t.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his hand slides under the elastic of her panties and she stiffens.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"fuck you\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he turns over, ostentatious in his movement.  in the old days, even weighted with exhaustion, she might have softened. in the old days, they might have fought about it.  in the old days, they might have had slow sex and then fallen asleep, flushed and happy.  in the good old bad old days, which are long gone.</p>'),(3351,'at the moment i\\\'m reading marabou stork nightmares by irvine welsh. i won\\\'t go into too much detail about the story because those of you that have not read it, should. but the imagery of the menacing \\\"stork\\\" that has plagued the protagonist since childhood has brought back memories of my own serial nightmare.<br><br>\r\nthe black crow.<br><br>\r\nnow the crow never really looked much like a crow. i guess when i was six or seven i wasn\\\'t even really sure what a crow was supposed to look like. but that was definitely his name, the black crow. it was whispered in fear by all the strange, wide-eyed children running around in the dreams, warning me that he was approaching.<br><br>\r\nthere is one dream that i remember vividly. i know that at the time it happened i was sleeping face down into the pillow, in the spare room at my grandparents\\\' place in christchurch.<br><br>\r\ndespite my eyes being closed i could see the white of the pillow begin to dissolve into a drab grey. then slowly but surely, inevitably, the grey gave way like a parting mist and i could see the scene laid out before me.<br><br>\r\nit was as though i was standing amid a small copse of trees. smaller bushes and tussock surrounded a path that ran from one side of the small clearing to the other.<br><br>\r\nthe image was almost two dimensional. it seemed to be laid out as if i was watching a performance on a stage; there was nothing else to look at outside the tableau of my field of vision.<br><br>\r\ni could feel him approaching.<br><br>\r\ni knew he was coming for me. there was nothing i could do about it. this was all inevitable. and then there he was; this tall, awkward yet powerful bird with an almost human face. not quite an ostrich or emu or even a moa, but something very much like it.<br><br>\r\nhe walked into the scene from stage left, slowly pacing to just over half way across the clearing. he didn\\\'t have to look at me yet; we both knew how this was going to end.<br><br>\r\nand then after standing and waiting for just a moment, he finally turned to look at me. even now the memory of the smile on his face gives me chills.<br><br>\r\nand at that moment i woke up screaming...<br><br>\r\nonly once have i ever again felt the inexplicable terror that i remember from that night. maybe the fact that my new nightmare was only a couple of weeks ago has helped re-kindle these childhood memories.<br><br>\r\nthe name of the crow was well known to my family, as i would often wake up, deeply upset and unable to get back to sleep.<br><br>\r\nit all ended a few years later. i guess i was not quite ten years old. but again i remember lying face down, this time burried in the cushions which padded the bay window seat in our house over-looking karori park.<br><br>\r\nit wasn\\\'t the same clearing but it was much the same. the children were around as always, but this time he was not approaching us, he was already there.<br><br>\r\nwalking in a group with the kids, i was standing closer to him than ever before. he turned to me, and there was something about the moment between us that we both understood to be the end of the way things had been.<br><br>\r\nhe looked at me as if to say \\\"so now you finally understand\\\", but no words were spoken. and i knew then that all along he had never been a threat, had harboured only the most benevolent of intentions for all of us.<br><br>\r\nand i have never been visited by him since.<br><br>\r\ni cannot remember what i was going through at the time the nightmares stopped. there is no sense of a major change in my life, no great accomplishment or turning point that parrallelled the understanding in the dream.<br><br>\r\nand i wonder now if i will ever see him again, and under what circumstances. the fear is still familiar to me, but for different reasons and from different quarters.<br><br>\r\nnow, having written this, i don\\\'t feel like i miss him, but i feel like perhaps i should.'),(3352,'<p>\r\non wintery sunday mornings in the kitchen of our big memorial avenue house, my dad would stand over the kitchen stove stirring, waiting and watching intently for the deep pot of porridge to reach that rolling gluggy burbling state of boil we called <a href=\\\"http://www.richard-seaman.com/Travel/NewZealand/NorthIsland/Rotorua/MudPools/\\\" target=\\\"blank\\\">rotorua</a>, after the town up north famous for its hot pools of boiling mud. he would always cook it perfectly; the secret, he told me, was to add a little salt right at the start. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy brother and i had strict criteria for a good bowl of porridge. we would carefully pour milk (the creamy part from the top of the bottle, if we were lucky) around the outside of the porridge, aiming to create a floating hemisphere of porridge cushioned all around by a thin layer of milk. if it floated, it meant the porridge was a particularly good batch. we would layer brown sugar over the top to make a sweet brown crust, and settle down in front of morning tv in our pajamas to warm and fill our little bellies. inspector gadget was my favourite cartoon. \r\n<p><p>\r\ni\\\'m not sure where my mum was, those sunday mornings. maybe working, or at the gym, or maybe she was there, watching our sunday morning tradition quietly from sidelines. those weekend mornings were all about me, my brother and our dad. as years went by our weekend tradition didn\\\'t change much. someone, probably my brother, discovered that dripping golden syrup over the brown sugar topping was twice as good as brown sugar by itself. someone, probably me, discovered that food colouring worked well with porridge, and our sunday breakfasts became surreal rainbows of blue porridge with pink milk, green porridge and blue milk, purple porridge and yellow milk. we would stir it together to see what colour it would turn. usually, it turned brown, and we were secretly pleased. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\\\'t know how many years we ate those breakfasts for. it feels like every weekend for my entire childhood. my dad seemed so big then, his hands so huge, his motor mechanic fingernails always black and stained with nicotine and grease and oil from the racing cars that enthralled me. i remember him waking me up one night at about 11 o\\\'clock and taking me, with my pajamas and sleepy eyes and mussed up hair, for a ride in the porsche he\\\'d brought home from work to show off. i remember the colour - british racing green, he called it. i remember the speedo hitting 220km/hour on the empty roads out behind the airport. i remember feeling as if my face was stretching back behind my ears. fast cars make me nervous, now. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmum started working night shifts, and dad had to learn to cook night-time meals, too. he made the best hamburgers i\\\'ve ever tasted. he learned to make spagetti bolognaise, and it was never quite as good as mum\\\'s, but we told him it was better, and he was proud. he quit smoking. then, him and mum finally split up, and we all moved out of our nice big house on memorial avenue. \r\n</p><p>\r\ni went home for a while when my grandmother, dad\\\'s mum, died a couple of months ago. i stayed over at my dad\\\'s house for a couple of nights; it was the first time we\\\'d slept in the same house since he and mum separated, 11 years ago. one night i was reading my book in bed, with the door open. dad came in to say goodnight, and tucked my blanket (the same blanket i slept with when i was a baby) around me, his 27-year-old little girl. \r\n</p><p>\r\nhis spare room and wardrobe are packed with boxes and boxes of books and music and clothes i don\\\'t know when i\\\'m coming back to claim. he\\\'s sending my tent to japan, so i can take it to burning man with me in august. he wants to meet me in spain next year, to see gaudi\\\'s architecture and go to the spanish grand prix. he\\\'s ringing me tonight because i\\\'m lonely and sad and he wants to tell me i\\\'ll be okay. \r\n</p><p>\r\ntoday i stood in my tiny kitchen, halfway around the world, stirring a big bowl of porridge and waiting for rotorua. these days i eat it with sliced banana mixed through, with yogurt and brown sugar on top, but i still add a little salt to the mix. i ate my porridge, and drank sweet tea, and watched sex and the city, kind of wishing it was inspector gadget.\r\n</p>'),(3353,'drove out to camden today. big sky country. lots of low, one-story buildings. wal-mart big blue boxes, fast food places, rotting Hardees. the sky was gray, overbright, with no love in it at all.<br><br>\r\ni went for my first real job job interview. who says liberal-arts majors can\\\'t get a job. associate admissions director, camden military academy. 25k. some travel. maybe a chance to teach some. i was like whoa. <br><br>\r\nthe guy\\\'s office had Carolina stuff everywhere. we had the same class ring on. i actually could tell that it made a huge difference that we were both alumni. it was wild. i really felt like maybe they weren\\\'t kidding that it\\\'s important to finish school. i was just there for the financial aid money mostly. who would have thought.\r\n<br><br>\r\nso, yeah. i graduated from college. how unlikely is that? things weren\\\'t always so rosy., but they\\\'re so rosy now! it is unbelievable how wonderful i feel these days, just unbelievable. it is like the lie of ecstasy has come true somehow. <br><br>\r\nbright bubbles of color well up inside me. everything is smooth as a ride on rails. who would have thought.'),(3354,'<META http-equiv=Content-Type content=\\\"text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\\\">\r\n<p>\r\nLes japonais doivent Ãªtre spÃ©cialement Ã©quipÃ©s pour parvenir Ã  apprÃ©cier la beautÃ© quand celle-ci est noyÃ©e dans la laideur. Je n\\\'ai aucune idÃ©e de comment ils font Ã§a. Simplement, Ã  chaque Ã©vÃ¨nement annuel des cerisiers en fleurs, j\\\'ai un mal fou pour me concentrer sur ces arbres magnifiques ; mon regard va et vient, du cerisier au pylÃ´ne voisin (garni de cÃ¢bles Ã©lectriques noirs et de transformateurs gris), du cerisier au bÃ¢timent moderne juste derriÃ¨re (Ã  la faÃ§ade recouverte des sempiternels carreaux bruns, oÃ¹ les radiateurs des climatiseurs rÃ©pÃ¨tent inlassablement leur carrÃ© blanc sale, un par appartement de taille exactement identique Ã  son voisin). Lorsque la fÃªte des cerisiers (\\\"hanami\\\") bat son plein, il faut ajouter Ã  ce tableau les hordes multicolores de familles, d\\\'Ã©tudiants, et d\\\'employÃ©s de bureau, qui dressent leur campement sur des toiles de plastique, oÃ¹ ils s\\\'Ã©gayent en chaussettes Ã  mesure que leurs rÃ©serves de biÃ¨re diminuent et que leur visage rougit.\r\n</p>\r\n<p><a href=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"><IMG align=\\\"right\\\" width=200 SRC=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"></a>\r\nTout compte fait, mon meilleur souvenir d\\\'hanami fut cette soirÃ©e oÃ¹ je rentrais correctement ivre de la ville. Sur la route dÃ©serte, je m\\\'arrÃªtai sous un cerisier en fleur et je regardai le ciel Ã  travers lui. La beautÃ© de cette vue, probablement aidÃ©e par l\\\'alcool, me permit un court instant d\\\'oublier la laideur du vain petit homme dressÃ© sous l\\\'arbre, qui brisa le silence d\\\'un rÃ´t avant de poursuivre son chemin.\r\n</p>'),(3355,'<P>\r\nTimothy lay out in the bed before her, his body subtly writhing under the crisp white sheets.  Occasionally, he would let out a low moan and shift slightly, as if trying to find a position that would relieve his ache.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHelen had been hanging back at the foot of the bed, the toe of her boot gently tapping the bag at her feet.  The PVC dress she was wearing barely \r\ncovered her pert ass, leaving a good three inches of flesh exposed from the tops of her stockings.  She gave a business-like tug at the hem.  The \r\nPCV made a slightly damp sucking sound as it pulled away from her skin. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nHelen took another second to savor the look of him, so big and yet so helpless.  She\\\'d been thinking of him all day; him tucked away in this \r\nroom waiting for her, needing her to come and take care of him.  Earlier, while she was shopping for supplies, she had gotten so wet that she had barely resisted the urge to slip into the store\\\'s bathroom and masturbate furiously.  Instead, she gently pressed her thighs together near the rack \r\nof NyQuil and bit her lip against the impulse.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThankfully, she had remembered to bring an extra pair of panties.  A white lace thong, of course.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\\\"Somewhere on my body I\\\'ve hidden vitamin C,\\\" she said, her nurses cap slightly askew.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\\\"Mrph,\\\" said Timothy from somewhere under the sheets.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nShe loved it when he played hard to get.  She gripped the top sheet firmly in both of her hands, bending over delicately so as to give the best view in the mirror at the foot of the bed, and yanked.  The sheets came away in one swiftly magical movement and went fluttering behind her to the floor, leaving Timothy exposed in the center of the bed.  He was wearing an old gray t-shirt and black shorts which bunched-up seductively around his upturned ass.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\\\"Oh god, please, no,\\\" he moaned before burring his head under the pillows.  \\\"Pl