  \r\n</p>'),(3364,'<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img3%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img4%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img5%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img6%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img7%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n       \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img8%%\\\">\r\n</center>  \r\n    \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>  \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br> \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<center>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img9%%\\\">\r\n</center>   \r\n<p>\r\nyou only need two fingers.   \r\n</p>'),(3366,'<p>he was already feeling bloated, but the need to take one more bite wasn\\\'t going to go away, so he just caved in.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>one more night alone but, of course, the junk helped.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the luke-warm, greasy big mac quickly dissolved to a masticated pulp in his mouth, filling out his cheeks. his chest and throat cramped as he swallowed; a sure sign he was over-doing it again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>this wasn\\\'t normal behaviour. he knew that. it\\\'s not true when they tell you that recognising your problem gets you half way to solving it. not when your problem is self-esteem.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>seeing the problem just highlighted how much of a loser he was. why try loving yourself when you know you\\\'re not worth it, right?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no, normal people didn\\\'t sleep all day, cowering from daylight and going into a cold sweat when the phone rang. normal people didn\\\'t jump out of their skins if there was a knock at the door.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>normal people didn\\\'t sit in the shower to eat hamburgers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the spray made the buns a little soggy, but mostly they weren\\\'t out of the container long enough to get wet before they were stuffed down his throat.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the half-flat vanilla coke helped make it feel like it was dissolving, and of course the belching helped.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he was sweating, which was perfectly normal, and the water easily took care of that. the hard, slippery tiles beneath him were strangely comforting. tangible, unbreakable, so unlike himself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>why don\\\'t you get a job at starbucks while you\\\'re waiting? or packing supermarket shelves? you need to show people that you can stick at something, because so far you\\\'ve done nothing with your life.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p>there was a bad cramp developing in his neck. he was too tall to be able to stretch out in the bottom of the shower stall, so he would just slump in the corner, knees tucked half way back to his chest.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his arm was sore and there was a nasty bruise just above the elbow. but all this was pretty standard fair of course, so it wasn\\\'t going to worry him any more than anything else that was going on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>besides, the ritual of self-destruction was almost as cleansing as the shower itself.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>you\\\'ve let me down so many times now. you have zero credibility. it\\\'s always muggins here who has to come in and bail you out. well i\\\'m not just a walking check-book here to clean up your mess. are you listening to me?</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p>he wasn\\\'t greatful for the hot water, although he probably should have been. it was the only thing in the old house still connected. although the power was cut off about three weeks after he moved in, the water was heated by gas; they hadn\\\'t quite got round to cutting that one off yet. he\\\'d probably be evicted before they did.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the place was too cold at night. he only had the one blanket, and he couldn\\\'t sleep in his clothes because otherwise they just smelled too bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so the shower stayed on all night. a lot of the time he would sleep in it. the heat and the weight of the water like a warm, scratchy blanket.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>of course the junk helped.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he was throwing up now. vile chunks of half-digested burger and soda streamed down his naked torso, making him heave again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he kept dry-wretching long after it had all come up. he turned his face to the stream of water and filled his mouth, pulling his gums back so the spray lashed over his stained teeth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the running water covered the tears nicely. and of course, the junk helped.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>fuck you, i\\\'ve had enough of this. if your father can\\\'t get through that thick head of yours then i don\\\'t know how i can. i\\\'m leaving.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p>the sun would be coming up soon. he could already hear the first few strains of the morning traffic. the shower also helped mask the sounds of the people outside. it was good to him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the junk had helped, but it was beginning to wear off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as the last of the vomit drained from the stall, he sat back and clumsily pulled the needle from his arm. it took him a while to undo the belt buckle, with both his hands now numb.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yes the sun was cresting the hill across the park now. the real world was awake and walking around. he could already feel the judging stares, the faces frowning in disgust.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and he got a sense that, very soon, the junk just wasn\\\'t going to help anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and then one way or another, it would all end.</p>'),(3365,'<p>\r\nWe woke up in the early afternoon and had a light lunch in the hotel restaurant. It was already getting hot outside and I was not looking forward to the heat. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe grabbed a taxi to the train station, sorted out our tickets to Surat Thani, and exchanged some money. We had a few hours to spare before the train left so we grabbed a drink and went down to sit in the shade of a tree beside the river that flanked one side of the station. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDeciding to go for a walk, we headed over a bridge and walked along the river on the opposite side. We passed many cars that had been long since left to die. Their rotting skeletons lined the street, collecting garbage and critters. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFurther down the street we came upon a few men fishing the river. I cringed at the thought of eating anything pulled out of the shiny gasoline coated waters. I calmed myself down hoping the men were anglers, and not fishermen.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe crossed another bridge and ended up on the Station side of the river again. We had decided to head back to the station and make use of the large fans they had set up in the main foyer. Instead, we came face to face with a medium sized yard filled with old train cars. We thought we had come up upon a museum of sorts. We walked around the train cars, inspecting the military one more than others. Who-da-thunk they put guns on trains? Good thing Thomas the Tank Engine never went that route in life. In addition to having old traincars, we happened upon a basketball court and played 21. Shane won, and a man came out of one of the cars and told us that we were disturbing the orphans. Apparently the yard was an orphanage. Who-da-thunk they put orphans on trains?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTuk-Tuk\\\'d to the station, stocked up on water and other things, and headed into the madness that was boarding the train. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe station platforms are filled with carts selling anything and everything at prices much lower than the station chain store. I hit myself. I must remember the carts. I must remember the carts. There is a funny contrast in carts. There are the carts selling things for a mainly Thai demographic, and then there are the carts that specialize in Backpacker fair. I like to think I dont goto the Backpacker carts, but I inevitably do once and a while. Brotha\\\'s gotta get toilet paper!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFinding lucky car 13 proved to be a bit hard. The cars were littered with old numbers from systems and logistical adventures of days gone by. We finally figured it out and took seats. We were in a sleeper car and just outside Shanes window hoses poked out of the train expelling liquids of unknown origin onto the gravel bed below. Our seats were at the end of the car next to the bathroom. We gave up trying to keep the door shut, as men and women of all ages darted past in both directions selling tidbits of what was being sold on the carts outside. The smell was interesting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOur train left thirty minutes late. Our tickets said the journey would be close to six hours long, but according to Tina\\\'s information the ride would be more like eight or nine hours. I didn\\\'t care. I like trains and having a sleeper car meant I could lay down and perhaps sleep. I wasn\\\'t worried. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe pulled out of the station and clackity clacked our way South. I was excited and all that. We were all giddy. It was good being back together with Shane and Tina. The trio from Gumho. My happiness wained as we started passing the slums that hugged the tracks. I would not even dare call them shacks. More like inadequate shelters. Kids splashed and played in the water. Dozens of bored men and woman all under 30 sat on unused tracks, piles of wood, bridges, and crates. They were all waiting for something to happen in their lives, but it was all to clear that some of them were losing hope. They would wave. They would smile. Once and a while you\\\'d see a finger instead of a hand and a \\\"fuck you\\\" instead of a \\\"hello\\\", but for the most part I was staring at a sight I had never in my life seen so closely before. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLike most cities, the slums thinned out as we left the city, though anytime we passed through another city, slums would line the tracks from both directions. It was a sorry state of affairs in a country that has had so much time to improve and so much international support. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt nine oclock a dude came by and erected all the beds. It was a comfy setup and I was content. My night consisted mostly of reading, heading to the doors for a smoke, and laying down with my eyes closed. I could not sleep. There was something inside me that wouldnt let me. Excitement. Anxiety. Something. I wasnt sure. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThirteen hours later the train arrived. Holy shit it was a long ride. Tina in all her haggling glory got us a taxi and we headed on into Surat Thani, stopping at her house to shower. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTina was late for work and dashed in and out of the shower and then off to work. We left soon after and sorted out some boat tickets to the islands of Koh Samui, Koh Tao and Koh Phangan. From Koh Tao we planned to take a boat further North to the mainland city of Chumphon.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nSurat Thani was a sleepy town and I couldnt wait to get to a sleepy beach. The next stop was Koh Samui, and I coulnt wait. \r\n</p>'),(3367,'<p>\r\nthe grayness has been here for days.  the fog and gloom hover around me, slowly closing in.  i\\\'m starting to forget what a sunny day looks like.  outside children are crying, their howls carried onwards by menacing winds.  i can hear the rain, dripping off the awnings.  the beat is \r\nirregular, in both pace and volume.  it draws me in, until it is all i can hear. it is all that i am.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthe pain in my back began before i can remember.  shifting between a lingering, dull ache, and the sharp jab of a needle.  it shoots its contents up my spine, bringing with it more than discontenment.  i can feel its progression through my vertabrae, one by one.  slowly injecting its poison.  i shift my weight perpetually.  useless.  there is no way to find that delicate balance, that station to make the pain disappear.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ninside my head my mind races.  synapses fire in the billions per second.  they will never slow down.  i am a car crash waiting to happen.  passengers fingers clench down on the seats, knuckles as white as virgin snow.  if i slip, how many will fall with me?  which lives am i \r\nresponsible for?  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwho will take responsibility for my life?\r\n</p>'),(3368,'on the desk: rolling machine, ashes, pipes, scratched cd cases, altoids, valium, bojangles cup, pellet-gun pellets, plates, papers, hemostat, passport, stamps, pellegrino bottle filled with tapwater, .22 pistol, contact lenses, books, stapler.<br><br>\r\nin the morning, i was dreaming<br><br>\r\nin the dream there was a girl i know. how common. still. there it is. part of my brain is, apparently, unreasonably in love with her, or with some idea of her. the actual her is unloveable, that is to say her life is arranged in such a way that she is unapproachable in a practical sense. i have stayed at her house. any time we might have spent together, alone, she was either passed out from drinking, or sleeping it off. she spends a lot of time in the bedroom and the bathroom. would she want to drink so much if i loved her? if i could lie there with her, on sheets and pillows, and lay my hands on her stomach, on her face, on the curve of her hips?<br><br>\r\ni do not think i love her in life, but in the dream i did. i took chances. it was not a sex dream, but a love dream. i penetrated not her body but the enclosure she keeps around herself. in the bed with her, in the shower, behind doors and curtains, under wet water streaming with my arms around her legs like a small child, with her pubic hair pressed softly against the side of my cheek, i loved her. <br><br>\r\nwhen i awoke the sun was shining on me through the windows. i did not know what day it was, or what my plans for the day were, or whether or not i was hungry. '),(3369,'<p>after nearly seven weeks living out of a suitcase in a backpackers\\\', and having to share a room with seven strangers every night, i can honestly state that i have the mixed-dorm-etiquette thing sussed. most situations that arise can now be dealt with in the appropriate manner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for example, if someone was drunk and made a complete twat of themselves the night before, but is generally a nice person, one should only inform them of their unfortunate behaviour if they request details. otherwise, simple brief comments such as \\\"you were a little bit squiffy last night i think\\\", or \\\"how\\\'s the headache?\\\" will suffice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>or, if you find that a teenage girl has dropped her security teddy bear from the top bunk while sleeping, it is best not to try and return it until she is awake. suddenly waking up to the sight of some strange six-foot-five bearded freak leaning over her a with a cuddly toy may cause her some alarm. best just to leave it on the floor by the bunks and explain things to her in the morning.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>one situation did arise today however, for which i have no hard and fast rule. it has in fact left me stumped as to what i should have done...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are two lovely english girls that arrived early this afternoon staying with us for a couple of days. i had not yet spoken to them, but like me they had crashed for some sleep at around three.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>one was still asleep, the other just rising as i shambled out of bed. we chatted about this and that and played the game of keeping polite eye contact to avoid staring anywhere else because we were only wearing underwear.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>me being polite to avoid looking like a sleaze; she looking away so as not to go blind from the pale horror.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a few moments later as i turned to go to the bathroom, i was admiring the tattoo in the small of her back when the awkward situation came up. i said nothing, but now i am wondering that, if she notices sometime this evening, what will she think of me for not saying anything? that i thought it funny? that maybe i thought she wanted it to be there?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>even worse, perhaps she goes to the playboy mansion party at the globe bar and everyone is staring at her without her ever being any the wiser...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i feel bad for not saying anything, but how would you broach the subject with someone you have only just met?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>is there any polite way to point out to someone that they seem to have a cartoon of a small ejacluating penis drawn on their back in ballpoint pen.....?</p>'),(3370,'<p>\r\nThis period, from midwinter to midsummer,<br> \r\nseveral months into my new employment,<br> \r\na year or so since my father died and while<br> \r\neveryone talked about peace and war,<br> \r\nshall be remembered in the following flashes:<br> \r\nfirst, driving through the tunnel<br> \r\non Yerba Buena island to emerge into sun,<br> \r\ngreat towering puffs of clouds and the grand<br>  \r\nwestern span of the Bay Bridge, its gray cables<br>  \r\ncrisp against the blue sky, shutting off the news<br>  \r\nto sing along to Bacharach tunes or Eminem<br> \r\nwhile checking the mirror and changing lanes;<br> \r\nsecond, sitting dazed in a small office like Philip<br> \r\nMarlowe\\\'s, down a long quiet hall of doors,<br>  \r\nwaiting to hear from this or that new crush,<br> \r\nstaring out at Union Square and clicking refresh,<br>  \r\nrefresh.  This period shall be characterized by an<br>  \r\nongoing unrest familiar since high school, a longing<br> \r\nnow colored by the jadedness of too many first dates.<br> \r\n(In this period I became more aware of myself<br> \r\nin my body, of the mechanics of nutrition,<br> \r\nand slowly lost my love handles, which<br> \r\nno one ever loved.) During this period<br>  \r\nI came to like mornings more, and to dislike<br>  \r\nsinking the other lonely hearts who awaited<br> \r\nmy replies.  I got to play refused and refuser,<br>  \r\nmore often than I could standâ€”but I drank<br> \r\nbourbon at night, and I stood it.  I repotted herbs<br>  \r\non the fire escape and took pleasure in solitude,<br>  \r\nsinging in the car, laying spread across the bed<br> \r\non Saturday mornings, cuddled in the comforter.<br> \r\nDuring this period certain evenings were unbearable,<br> \r\ngod became an obstinate boor with chipped teeth,<br> \r\nchemicals roiled in my head, pulled me floorward<br> \r\nand I felt deadly unfit for affection or care.<br> \r\nStones slung from arms and neck, I have been<br>  \r\nalternately drowned in a blue sea and<br>  \r\ncalmly aware of my own gravity, occasionally<br> \r\nfloating to the surface and gasping at the sun<br> \r\nwhen the slosh of time and emotion stills and<br>  \r\neveryone I have ever loved becomes a single<br> \r\nunknowing object, palmed, polished and adored,<br>  \r\nthat lifts me more than not, keeps me coming back to<br> \r\nkiss it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>During this period I roasted chickens, grilled lamb<br>\r\nchops and New York steaks, and shunned potatoes<br> \r\nand grains.  There was a fluttering in my chest<br> \r\nthat the doctor dismissed, and ulcers that arose<br> \r\nout of household jealousies and a bilious disgust<br>  \r\nwith world affairs.  This was neither a period<br> \r\nof great productivity, nor of the shame of<br> \r\nnon-production.  Attempts were made,<br> \r\nmornings were given over to the fervor<br>  \r\nof poems and afternoons sailed by across<br> \r\nstrings of words retouched and rearranged.<br> \r\nSolitude was both friend and melancholy aunt,<br> \r\nwhile I read fine paragraphs and my heart rate<br>  \r\nrose; while I stood on a hill in Berkeley looking<br>  \r\nout on the entire bay, singing to myself; when I was<br>  \r\nfound dropped in the grass, another organism able<br>  \r\nto be crushed, or able to tarry on over anthills,<br> \r\nof a piece with the anthills and the ants and<br> \r\nthe rained upon leaves of grass, forever growing,<br>  \r\ncells splitting, falling apart each night only to repair and<br> \r\nreassemble into someone else, a few cells off<br> \r\nin the morning, different in a pattern of breaths<br> \r\nand one fresh synapse able to join two disparate<br> \r\nthoughts that have been waiting years to be joined,<br>  \r\nwhich will revise the entire system, if and when it fires.</p>\r\n'),(3371,'<p>She called to say the check<br>\r\nfor what she owed me<br>\r\nwould be late, the timbre<br>\r\nin her voice wavering. She<br>\r\nsaid she could cover<br>\r\nthe rent but not the<br>\r\nrest, and she was sorry.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I said okay, because<br>\r\nthere was nothing else to say<br>\r\nand at least the rent would get paid.<br>\r\nShe would get the rest in two weeks,<br>\r\nher next payday.  I said sure.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSomeone I owe a check to<br>\r\n(whose feelings I hurt once)<br>\r\nwould have to wait,<br>\r\nbut I hung up the phone and<br>\r\nwent back to my work,<br>\r\nwhich barely covers the bills<br>\r\nbut I am treading water<br>\r\nand maybe next year<br>\r\nthe weight will feel like less.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My boss has a mortgage and<br>\r\na company car and long-term<br>\r\ncare insurance.  She has a daughter<br>\r\ngoing to college in the fall,<br>\r\na lithe, tall girl with an interest<br>\r\nin public policy and a ponytail.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My mother wrote to say<br>\r\nthat she could not afford to<br>\r\ndo anything exciting during my visit<br>\r\nbut that we would make do.<br>\r\nI said what do we ever do?<br>\r\nI said we could go to the park,<br>\r\nmaybe drive someplace and<br>\r\nhave lunch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yesterday I put<br>\r\nhalf a tank of gas in the car<br>\r\nand went to the beach.  The waves<br>\r\ncrashed loudly against staunch<br>\r\nrocks, sprayed my face and chest<br>\r\nas I scoped out new loves.<br>\r\nI scaled the cliff to the path above<br>\r\nand then, sunglasses off to clean them,<br>\r\nwildflowers are blooming guilelessly<br>\r\non my left, trembling together,<br>\r\nthe ocean on my right, molten,<br>\r\nflat, bright with the late-day sun.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Afterward I walked the dog<br>\r\nto the store and bought a steak<br>\r\nfor dinner, and placed sliced yams<br>\r\nand asparagus in a pretty ring<br>\r\naround the meat on the grill.<br>\r\nA half-empty bottle of wine<br>\r\non top of the refrigerator which,<br>\r\nthough I would not buy it again,<br>\r\nwent down easy and brought<br>\r\nsleep on in the lazy way<br>\r\nit should slink on,<br>\r\nabove a flurry of forgotten<br>\r\nworries, on a full stomach,<br>\r\nwithout much thought<br>\r\nof the day ahead.</p>\r\n'),(3372,'<META http-equiv=Content-Type content=\\\"text/html; charset=iso-8859-1\\\">\r\nParfois, on se rÃ©veille au milieu de la nuit et on ne peut plus se rendormir. Quelque chose va mal ; par exemple on a mangÃ© avant de se coucher tout un tas de choses qu\\\'on n\\\'aime pas vraiment, comme de drÃ´les de morceaux de poulet dans la salade, et de drÃ´les de morceaux de viande â€“ Ã©tait-ce du boeuf ou du porc ? â€“ mÃ©langÃ©s Ã  une assiette de nouilles translucides. Ou bien peut-Ãªtre les <i>shu-mai</i>, au contenu jamais bien certain ? Enfin, rÃ©sultat, on se rÃ©veille, on a un peu mal au ventre, et il est Ã  la fois trop tard pour espÃ©rer vomir et trop tÃ´t pour espÃ©rer avoir la diarrhÃ©e.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nEvidemment, Ã  ce moment de la mÃªme nuit, d\\\'autres choses se passent. Par exemple on entend quelque part, trÃ¨s loin, le bruit des pots d\\\'Ã©chappement dÃ©biles de quelques <a href=\\\"http://www.bosozoku.de\\\"><i>bosozokus</i></a> â€“ on se dit une fois de plus que la connerie de l\\\'homme est sans limite.<br>\r\n\r\nOu alors il y a ce type qui s\\\'entraÃ®ne encore au <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3372_2.jpg\\\">kyudo</a>, enfin au tir Ã  l\\\'arc quoi, sur le terrain de la fac qui est juste derriÃ¨re chez soi. Oh, il n\\\'est pas bruyant, et loin de lÃ . Et si on n\\\'avait pas Ã©tÃ© rÃ©veillÃ© par le mal au ventre, on ne l\\\'aurait certainement pas Ã©tÃ© par le bruit de sa flÃ¨che, qu\\\'il dÃ©coche, Â« tac Â», avec la rÃ©gularitÃ© d\\\'une horloge psychotique, toutes les deux ou trois minutes.<br>\r\nOu encore il y a cette bicyclette qui descend la rue, et dont les freins grincent tout le long. Et on se demande avec espoir si ce n\\\'est pas le type du kyudo qui a enfin admis qu\\\'il est temps d\\\'Ã©courter cet entraÃ®nement stupide car dÃ©sespÃ©rÃ© â€“ mÃªme s\\\'il ne dort pas de la nuit, il n\\\'aura Ã©videmment pas la prÃ©paration nÃ©cessaire pour la compÃ©tition de samedi prochain. Alors on attend encore une minute, puis deux, et on commence Ã  espÃ©rer, puis trois et on se dit qu\\\'il est bien parti, l\\\'imbÃ©cile persÃ©vÃ©rant.<br>\r\nÂ« Tac Â», fait sa flÃ¨che Ã  ce moment lÃ .<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nOu l\\\'un de ces oiseaux au cri impossible, tel qu\\\'on se demande mÃªme si c\\\'est bien un oiseau. Ou n\\\'importe quel autre bruit, inidentifiable. Enfin, Ã  cause de ces choses, qui se passent au dehors de ce mal au ventre, on reste Ã©veillÃ©, et on regarde l\\\'heure de temps en temps, affichÃ©e de faÃ§on visible, tellement visible, sur l\\\'Ã©cran de la chaÃ®ne hi-fi. On imagine peut-Ãªtre que la diode Ã©lectroluminescente fut inventÃ©e par un type qui ne pouvait pas dormir la nuit. On se demande s\\\'il Ã©tait japonais.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nIl arrive un moment oÃ¹ l\\\'on se dit que, tant qu\\\'Ã  penser des trucs sans queue ni tÃªte, autant se lever pour Ã§a, et tenter d\\\'Ãªtre un peu plus constructif. Par exemple en allant aux toilettes â€“ avec un faible espoir de vomi ou de diarrhÃ©e.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nLe moment oÃ¹ l\\\'on se lÃ¨ve est un moment dÃ©cisif. Une fois debout, on se retrouve seul, et la seule chose qui tiendra compagnie d\\\'une faÃ§on sympathique Ã  ce moment lÃ , c\\\'est la culture.<br>\r\nBien sÃ»r, lÃ  encore, la forme sous laquelle se prÃ©sentera la culture est imprÃ©visible. Par exemple, elle peut apparaÃ®tre sous la forme du cafard gÃ©ant reprÃ©sentÃ© sur la couverture d\\\'une Ã©dition de 1958, au Livre de Poche, de La mÃ©tamorphose de Kafka. On n\\\'a pas forcÃ©ment besoin d\\\'ouvrir le livre â€“ on sait ce qu\\\'il y a dedans. Ce livre est sur la petite table des toilettes depuis quelques mois, et l\\\'on a du mal Ã  lui imaginer une meilleure place. Lui-mÃªme en a certainement vu d\\\'autres : imprimÃ© Ã  Paris il y a 45 ans, sa trace s\\\'est perdue jusque durant l\\\'hiver 2000, lorsqu\\\'on l\\\'a dÃ©nichÃ© avec surprise sur Church street, San Francisco, chez un bouquiniste ouvert tard dans la nuit.<br>\r\n\r\nPar peur d\\\'apparaÃ®tre futile, on n\\\'a pas vraiment envie d\\\'expliquer pourquoi, mais on sait qu\\\'on est maintenant attachÃ© Ã  ce livre. Le gros cafard sur fond rouge de la couverture est devenu sympathique. On aurait du mal Ã  s\\\'en dÃ©faire. On se dit qu\\\'on est comme Ã§a, on dirait. On s\\\'attache Ã  des choses sans importances et on les emporte avec soi pour les prÃ©server de l\\\'oubli. C\\\'est peut-Ãªtre seulement Ã§a, la culture.<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\nOn commence Ã  Ãªtre plus rÃ©veillÃ©. Les idÃ©es explosent dans tous les sens, comme si le feu prenait dans la poudriÃ¨re. Non, un peu de rÃ©alisme : les idÃ©es fument, font quelques Ã©tincelles, puis s\\\'Ã©teignent comme des pÃ©tards mouillÃ©s. On se relÃ¨ve du siÃ¨ge des toilettes, oÃ¹ nulle dÃ©livrance ne sera permise, on le savait bien. On s\\\'Ã©nerve un peu. On se dit d\\\'un coup que si les hommes cessaient de s\\\'attacher Ã  des stupides cafards sur la couverture d\\\'un vieux bouquin, on pourrait peut-Ãªtre scanner Kafka une fois pour toutes, le coller sur un CD rom et sur internet, et puis bon vent !<br>\r\nOn pense Ã  toutes ces choses qui menacent de disparaÃ®tre. On se dit que pendant qu\\\'on y est, on pourrait scanner les bouquins dÃ©crivant la grammaire de tous les langages existants, puis les jeter Ã  la poubelle et se mettre tous Ã  parler l\\\'esperanto. Et les baleines ? on se dit. Les baleines, on n\\\'a qu\\\'Ã  sampler leur ADN correctement, puis on organise une grande battue dans tous les ocÃ©ans pour s\\\'en dÃ©barrasser.<br>\r\nOn repense Ã  Georges W. Bush, qui vient d\\\'offrir Ã  Chirac des livres sur les indiens d\\\'AmÃ©rique. Quel message a-t-il pu vouloir faire passer ? Â« Regarde, au dÃ©but, un peuple, un problÃ¨me ; ensuite, je le rÃ©duis Ã  presque rien, si bien que je peux le faire entrer dans un livre ; puis j\\\'offre le livre Ã  quelqu\\\'un, et me voilÃ  proprement dÃ©barrassÃ©. Â» Ou encore : Â« Tiens, je te donne mes trois livres sur les indiens ; il faut que je fasse de la place dans ma bibliothÃ¨que pour les livres sur les musulmans Â».<br></p><p>\r\nA ce moment, on rÃ©alise qu\\\'on aura bien du mal Ã  se rendormir. On ne s\\\'est pas assez mÃ©fiÃ© de la culture. On se dit que la prochaine fois qu\\\'on se rÃ©veille en pleine nuit, on ne prendra pas de risque : on allumera la tÃ©lÃ©.\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3372_1.jpg\\\"><IMG align=\\\"right\\\" width=200 SRC=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3372_1.jpg\\\"></a>\r\n</p>'),(3373,'<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\"><b><font size=\\\"4\\\">wednesday</font></b></font></p>\r\n<p>\\\"what time\\\'ll you be here friday?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"dunno, i\\\'ve got a few things to do first. might not be till late in the \r\n  afternoon\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"what sorta <i>things?</i>\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"just things\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"i hate it when you act coy. it makes me suspicious\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"you don\\\'t trust me\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"i do\\\"</p>\r\n<p>*PAUSE*</p>\r\n<p>\\\"so what exactly <i>are</i> you doing on friday that\\\'s gonna take you \r\n  all day?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"it\\\'s a surprise\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"i hate surprises. tell me\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"no just wait till friday\\\"</p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\"><b><font size=\\\"4\\\">thursday</font></b></font></p>\r\n<p><b><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\">&lt;</font></b><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\">:IM \r\n  SO UPSET THE SURPRISE HAS GONE WRONG&gt;</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\">&lt;:DONT WORRY BOUT IT. WAT WAS IT \r\n  ANYWAY?&gt;</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\">&lt;:NOTHIN I JUST WANA 4GET BOUT IT. \r\n  I CUD CRY&gt;</font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Courier New, Courier, mono\\\">&lt;:CHEER UP ILL C U 2MORO&gt;</font></p>'),(3374,'<p>\r\nThe port at Surat Thani was modern, clean, and had areas for you to collect yourself in the shade. Shane and I had a little more than a half an hour to wait. We sat and chatted with some bloke who had recently purchased some land on the Island of Samui and was planning on building some Bungalows. He seemed to want to keep quiet about his plans so I thought to myself that he wasnt really building bungalows. He was probably some guy who was using that as his shtick. People have shticks when they go travelling. Shane and I used bags of ice as hats. That was our shtick. Even the packers thought we were insane. fuck\\\'em though. It was hot. The least of my worries was trying to look like a hip packer.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThe ferry was great. Even the stinky bathroom wasnt a squatter and they actually provided toilet paper. I was astonished. The trip from the port of Surat Thani to Koh Samui was roughly forty-five minutes. When we docked, I at once noticed the big signs and advertisments welcoming me to a very corporate and material island. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs much as I was let down seeing all the billboards and advertisments, the island itself was beautiful. Tall tropical trees, lush vegetation, and the noise of insects and birds warmed me up to my surroundings.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShane and I grabbed a tuk-tuk(pickup truck style) to the ville of Lamai. We were staying at a Bungalow community called the \\\"Lamai Chalets\\\", and once there we became instantly comfortable staying there. The rooms were cheap and accomodating. Nothing but gheckos in my room. \\\"keep up the good work\\\" i\\\'d encourage everytime I entered or left. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOne of the employees at the Lamai Chalets was a Ladyboy by the name of Den. Den was nice enough but was rather flirtacious with both Shane and I. It was all in good fun. We played with it and nothing serious every came about. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe stayed at the Lamai Chalet for about a week and met some pretty interesting people. In addition to Den, we met a really cool young lady who was working at one of the beach bars a five minute walk down the sand. Her name was Tik, and while she said she was seventeen, I had my doubts about that claim aswell. I think she just wanted to keep the packers out of her pants. She also claimed to be from the moon and was saving up her money to study English. Her uncle owned three of the bars around us and so Im sure her family was doing just fine. She was planning University and had many of the same goals and asperations of those at her age in Toronto when I was there. I pitied her plan, but she was a really cool dudette. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDates and times from Koh Samui arent foggy due to any sort of intoxication. Being there put me into this state of nothingness. I just waded in the water for hours and chatted with Shane and others. Women would walk by offering massages, hammocks, drinks, food, and boomboom. The sun and moon became my clocks and days just became another total on a reciept. I didn\\\'t care what day it was and CNN was nothing but background noise during a chess game with Shane. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNevertheless, when something called Friday came, we knew Tina would be on her way to see us. We were both excited.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTina arrived at the Lamai Chalet just one hour before Shane and I were to make our first and only drug transaction. What a process. What stress. At no time in my life will I ever put myself into such anxiety over getting a few grams of the mary jane. I mean \\\'what the fuck is the big deal\\\' I kept asking myself; soon after remembering that over one thousand people had been killed without trial in Thailands \\\"war on drugs\\\". Tina, Shane, and I sat on some rocks jutting out from the waves. The noise was fantastic. We sucked back cheap Thai beer and me and Shane smoked a bunch. We were leaving for Koh Phangan early the next day, so we had a night of drinking coctails and beer at the bar Tik worked at. I discussed the twelfth planet of Niburu and our links with an ancient reptillian race with a middle aged teacher from the Isle of White. She was glued to what I was making up from tidbits of conspiracy theory and imagination. Giving her my website I told her she\\\'d be able to find out all of the information she needed. She wont. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe next day we all got dressed and left for Koh Phangan. We were excited because Koh Phangan was supposed to be the chiller of the two islands. The place where cops didnt choke you for smoking reefer and you could buy mushroom shakes on the beach. I was looking forward to all of this as I strolled onto yet another ferry. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs soon as we stepped onto the dock at Koh Phangan we were bombarded with people trying to get us to stay in their Chalet, take their tuk-tuk, and buy a map. It was terrible. I was really shaken by the ordeal. They were the most aggressive I\\\'d see on my entire trip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe ended up heading to a bungalow that one of Tina\\\'s friends had recommended to us. My throat was a bit soar and the sun was a lot hot. Our chalet ended up being on the top of a large hill. After carrying my two bags up probably four hundred steps I collapsed on my bed feeling ill. Something had happened to me in a period of only hours and I didn\\\'t know what it was. I feared SARS. My bones ached. My throat was killing me, and all I wanted, and ended up doing was sleeping. I slept for like 28 hours. I didn\\\'t eat. I didnt goto the bathroom. I didn\\\'t even smoke a cigarette. I was just naff. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat Shane and Tina did I still dont really know. They had fun with some of Tina\\\'s friends and did things on the beach and at bars and all that. I missed it all and slept. The next day I struggled to get ready. I had to build up enough energy to carry my bags back down the hill and into the small town to find a TukTuk to the port. I wanted to goto the Hospital, but the only one I trusted was on Koh Samui. We stopped in at a cafe and had some food. I just had a plate of fruit and a coconut smoothy. I couldnt even finish the plate of fruit my throat hurt so much. I slept with my head on the table as Shane and Tina got some last minute things done. Shane sent home some extra clothes that both me and him didnt need and Tina went down to the beach for a quick dip. I just slept. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the TukTuk ride back to the port I was riding beside a japanese tourist that shane had begun flirting with. I think even Tina was flirting with her. About ten minutes into the ride I felt something on my neck. Grabbing it and looking I found a once inch scorpion in my hand. I threw it onto the floor of the pickup and it crawled into the japanese womans hats. I kicked her hat and didnt explain. I was sick. They just said \\\"he\\\'s sick. he wont hurt you\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI remember getting to the hospital. From the TukTuk to the hospital Im really not sure what was going on. After the TukTuk ride I was blacking out as I walked. I didn\\\'t make any of this known. I didnt want things to seem more serious than they were. I didnt have something that would kill me, and I didnt want them to worry. SARS and all. We got to the Hospital and as I walked up the ramp I calmed at once when I saw a couple waiting to have their motorcycle crash wounds taken care of. The germans were in rough shape. Skin was everywhere. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI got my two dollars worth of pain killers and anti-biotics. I told them I could only take Cipro and so they gave that to me. After the second pill I coughed up fluid ounces of phlehm and felt 100% better. I had had tonsilitus. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe headed to another town on Samui and stayed in a bungalow community called the pearl oyster inn or something. I was still groggy. It was during the walk up their driveway I started coughing up fluid ounces of throat garbage. I wanted a room with air con. I was hot and feeling ill and weak still. My apetite had returned. I think it was my birthday. Who knew? I sure as hell didnt, though Shane did remind me and we had a beer together. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe stayed at the pearl oyster for two nights. I was feeling almost back to normal when we headed back to the port to take a ferry towards the mainland town of Chumphon. From there were to head back to Bangkok by Train, and on towards Cambodia. I was glad to be getting off the Islands. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the TukTuk from the Pearl Oyster to the port we passed a giant Tesco\\\'s complete with a KFC. It was in the exact configuration of every other giant business park in Toronto. Im sure in two years there will be a paylesshoes and a bulkbarn. \r\nMany of the other travellers I spoke with voiced similar concerns, though others just didnt care. Ive heard Id have liked the north of Thailand more, but as I dont have the Luxury of going there right now, I\\\'ll just say that Southern Thailand is ripe with exploitation, poverty, wealth, and sex. It wasnt all that different from what I would see in Cambodia, but it was in their model for promoting it that I felt like a complete outsider. It was fun. Dont get me wrong, but the fun had only started, and there was so much more to be had. \r\n</p>'),(3375,'<P>We\\\'re on the front lawn of the church, raking.</P><P>Bill goes, \\\"I\\\'m a custodian, not a yod man.\\\"</P><P>In a screechy voice, I go, \\\"Mommy, my pussy hurts.\\\"</P><P>\\\"What?\\\"</P><P>\\\"That\\\'s what we used to say when I worked in kitchens, and one of the guys complained. \\\'Mommy, my pussy hurts.\\\'\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  Watch you don\\\'t get busted in the mouth.\\\"</P><P>He\\\'s smoking a cigarette with one hand, half-ass raking with the other.</P><P>\\\"She got us out here rakin\\\' in AYY-prul,\\\" he says.  \\\"Don\\\'t no leaves fall in Ayprul.  Ayprul is when the leaves staht <U>growin\\\'</U> on the trees, man.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  She\\\'s crazy.\\\"</P><P>He says, \\\"Watch this,\\\" takes a last drag, flips it onto one of the leaf piles.  Little flames spring up on the side of the mound.</P><P>I say, \\\"Put that shit out.  You\\\'re gonna get us in trouble.\\\"</P><P>He goes over and stomps it out with his big feet.</P><P>He says, \\\"Y\\\'always worried about gettin\\\' in trouble.  Fuck \\\'er.  She knows this job don\\\'t mean shit to me.  I was lookin\\\' for a job when I found this one - I can sure as fuck look some more.\\\"</P><P>We rake in enjoyable silence for a little bit, then Frank comes up on his golf cart.  He and Freda each have one.  His is a convertible - he took the roof off.</P><P>He says, \\\"Hey guys:  getcher leaves bagged \\\'n come with me.  I got an assignment for yuh.\\\"</P><P>We put the piles of leaves into garbage bags and set them at the edge of the property.  Bill gets into the other seat in the cart, and I perch back where the clubs would go.  We haul ass across the parking lot to where a van is parked behind the church bus.</P><P>Frank says, \\\"I wantchuhs to take the van out to where this lady lives - I\\\'ll give ya the directions in just a second - go out there and pick up some stuff she\\\'s donatin\\\' to the church.  Now.  I want botha yuhs to be careful:  she\\\'s bathouse crazy, \\\'n she\\\'s on the lookout for a man.\\\"</P><P>I say, \\\"I don\\\'t have a license.\\\"</P><P>Bill says, \\\"Yeah.  Me, neither.\\\"</P><P>He gives me a set of keys and says, \\\"Well, I\\\'m givin\\\' yuh a Frank Cona license.  Just don\\\'t get pulled over.\\\"</P><P>He tells us how to get there, and away we go.  We get out onto the street, and Bill pulls a huge hog-leg joint out of his pocket.</P><P>He goes, \\\"Smell this.\\\"</P><P>Oh, Lord, the flesh, he is weak.</P><P>Select Events in the Remainder of Matt\\\'s Day,<BR>Friday, April ____, 199_</P><P>12:11  rolled down windows of church van, smoked out with Bill</P><P>12:23  listened to last half of \\\"Kashmir\\\" on church radio in church van in stoned silence</P><P>12:26  held discussion with Bill about whether they looked stoned or not.  Bill contended that if you hold your eyelids just right, it doesn\\\'t matter how red your eyes are - people will overlook it.</P><P>12:31  arrived at home of Elizabeth Panter.  She was standing in her front yard waiting for them.  When she saw the van approaching, she started waving her arms wildly.  Parked van, got out.  She looked them over and announced they looked like they\\\'d been \\\"huffing some good green.\\\"  Panic-stricken, they denied this.  She said, \\\"Never try to snow an old snower.  Ya\\\'ll get me high, and I won\\\'t mention your condition to anybody.  <U>Okay</U>?\\\"</P><P>12:33  assembled in shed behind Elizabeth\\\'s trailer for a session.  She had a bed and dresser in there and explained to them she was sleeping in there because she had too much junk in her trailer.  Bill sat on the bed and rolled a joint using a \\\"National Enquirer\\\" for a rolling tray.</P><P>12:42  smoked out.  Did not get much higher.</P><P>12:51  loaded lots of worthless crap into the back of the van, took off.  When they left Elizabeth, she was laying on the bed.  They told her goodbye, and she held up her hand and waggled her fingers at them.</P><P>1:22  arrived at church.  Unloaded crap into storage room at end of office complex.  Avoided staff.</P><P>1:34  left church.  Walked to bank.  Cashed paychecks they had been given earlier in the day.</P><P>1:47  talked into having a few at Spartan\\\'s by Bill.  Set out walking.</P><P>2:23  arrived at Spartan\\\'s.  Began drinking Heinekens at the rate of one every twelve minutes at a cost of two twenty-five per.</P><P>3:37  played haphazard game of eightball with Bill.  Continued with the Heinekens.</P><P>4:58  consumed double shot of Sambuca.</P><P>5:17  more pool with Bill.  Switched to fifty cent drafts.  Programmed \\\"L.A. Woman,\\\" \\\"New Kid in Town,\\\" \\\"My Back Pages,\\\" \\\"Travelin\\\' Band,\\\" \\\"All My Rowdy Friends Have Settled Down,\\\" \\\"Mama Tried,\\\" and \\\"You Can\\\'t Always Get What You Want\\\" on the jukebox.</P><P>6:13  missed dinner at parents\\\' house.</P><P>6:25  smoked sloppily-rolled joint behind bar with Bill and brunette waitress.</P><P>6:49  switched back to Heinekens.  Engaged brunette waitress in conversation about Bob Dylan.</P><P>7:22  programmed \\\"Badlands,\\\" \\\"He Stopped Loving Her Today,\\\" \\\"L.A. Woman,\\\" \\\"I Walk the Line,\\\" \\\"Sympathy for the Devil,\\\" \\\"Green River,\\\" and \\\"Shelter from the Storm\\\" on jukebox.</P><P>7:47  asked bartender to see if the two blonde girls on the other side of the bar would let him buy them a round.  Turned down by blonde girls.</P><P>8:00  missed start of A.A. meeting.</P><P>8:39  smoked mondo gigantico joint in back room of bar with Bill, two blonde girls, brunette waitress, bartender, and two guys in cowboy hats.  Watched Bill mack on one of the blonde girls.  Watched Bill almost get his ass kicked by one of the guys in cowboy hats, who evidently was going out with the blonde girl.</P><P>10:26  was subject of phone call between father and uncle, who is a deputy sheriff, in which father asked uncle to keep an eye out for him.</P><P>10:41  consumed two shots of Sambuca.  Asked brunette waitress if she wanted to dance.  Offer rejected.</P><P>11:19  consumed two shots of Sambuca.  Got into argument with Bill.  Bill wanted him to chip in for cost of weed smoked.  Bill left.</P><P>11:57  consumed two shots of Sambuca.  Began to black out.</P><P>12:21  ordered two shots of Sambuca.  Served one by bartender.  Blacked out totally.</P><P>12:29  had conversation with blonde girl (not the blonde girl going out with the guy in the cowboy hat.  the other one.) by pool table.  was taken into stall in ladies\\\' room by blonde girl.  fellatio ensued.  emerged from blackout for the occaison.</P><P>12:43  blackout continues.  Cut off by bartender.  Told bartender to fuck himself.  Left.</P><P>12:44  began walking toward downtown.</P><P>1:19  strolled through park.  Encountered Bud behind the library.  Called him a \\\"crazy squirrel fuck.\\\"  Sat on bench.  Got drink of water, used bathroom.  Sat back on bench.</P><P>1:33  passed out on bench.</P><P>1:47  rolled off bench onto ground.  Did not awaken.</P><P>2:35  spotted by City of Brooksville Police Officer Rick Peters, who was patrolling downtown.  Failed to regain consciousness despite Peters\\\' attempts to revive him.</P><P>2:49  paramedics arrived.  Laid on stretcher, taken to hospital.  Came to en route to hospital.  Still in blackout.</P><P>2:57  arrived at emergency room.  Blood drawn.  Blood alcohol level assessed, drug screen performed.</P><P>3:13  ordered to be held for observation for two hours by E.R. physician.</P><P>5:36  parents called at number on old hospital records.</P><P>5:54  father arrived at emergency room.  Driven home by father.  Passed out in bed.</P>'),(3376,'<p>\r\nlook me in the eye<br>\r\ntell me that those boobs are real<br>\r\noh look, see you can\\\'t\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nmy friend gary c<br>\r\nspotted them a mile off<br>\r\neyeing up your tits\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nhere are a few points<br>\r\none of them is not on straight<br>\r\ncan i touch you there?\r\n</p>'),(3377,'<p>She said \\\"Swinney\\\'s out in orbit!\\\" <br>\r\nI thought he must be flying high,<br>\r\nUp there circling the planets,<br>\r\nA real stratospheric guy.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWhat she really meant was one day<br>\r\nWe were sitting at the bar,<br>\r\nAnd Swinney, he kept calling her<br>\r\nTo come by in her car.\r\n</p><p>\r\nShe meant Swinney\\\'s at the Orbit Room,<br>\r\nNot up in the night sky.<br>\r\nBut I preferred to think of him<br>\r\nOut in space. I don\\\'t know why.\r\n</p>'),(3378,'<p>i find myself surrounded by them, marked by them, troubled by them, but still remember them fondly. They sometimes plague my existence, as I wonder what sorts of evil miscalculations my own biological bits have made. I think of science, and of clouds of smoke rising in and out of towns, in and out of lungs, as I drift in and out of consciousness, worrying for them in my dreams. They are filling up my consciousness now.. fluttering in my stomach, withering like monarchs before their time- not butterflies but beautiful like them. Too many flights of fancy lost. I am tired for them, and for me. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI am fine. I await my own sickness and I trouble myself with worry and with doubts. A human can get over just about any trouble if they think they are doing a service, saving a life, righting a wrong. I lifted the enourmous building off my childlike psyche after the trauma subsided. Shock died down, and my consciouness quited. They who told me they thought I would find my path with God were left to spin their webs and paths of subjective truth alone. I will no longer parry along with those who live in those worlds. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are things in life that become vague over time. Thoughts, memories. Dates become a large blurs as experience becomes a new symbol to be translated, rethought, re-valued and exchanged in new ways. And so I am fine with the lives of other behind me. Sick and tarnished polkadot hats have danced off the heads of those who held me captive and into the future they dance with a new significance. Of course I am fine, I am real aren\\\'t I? <br><br>\r\n\r\nMy story remains captive within me. Never really surfacing to an epiphany of truth. Never really ready to be told, but ever present, reiventing, like I have done, and as they will do when the time has come for them to leave me and their place in things, to find something new. What they will find is as unsure as a story told about a lie that is a half truth. I am fine with that. </p>'),(3379,'<P>\r\nStanley woke up in his third floor apartment.  3am.   A great energy\r\ncalled him to the stair case.  Part dream, part biological chemical\r\nsuper-charge.  He put on his slippers and shuffled through 2 rooms and\r\nthe kitchen.  Shh, shh, shh, the rear of his slippers dragged as he\r\nwalked.  clock, clock, clack,  he unlocked the bolts on the door.\r\nree-eee-eee, the door opened.  squeak, squeak, squeak, the mouse cried\r\nand it stared up at him wondering in a mouse way, what are you doing\r\nstanding there? You should be in bed.  Rat, said Stanley,\r\nmisinformed.  Drat said the mouse.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nStanley considered for several seconds what to do.  Mice do not fit\r\ninto the world of domestic life.  They exist, but they are unwanted\r\nand have no political influence.  They might as well not exist at all,\r\nright?  Stanley weighed. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI could step on him.  Barbaric.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe mouse considered his options as a smaller creature.  Run for the\r\nstairs.  Run for a hole in the wall.  Run in mad-mouse zig zags; that\r\nalways confuses the humans.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m tired, thought Stanley.  He decided that whatever subconscious\r\ndeep sleep epiphany pulled him out of bed to see this mouse, must be\r\nover.  Can I do nothing?  reeekunk, the door shut.\r\n</P><P>\r\nshhah, the mouse sighed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nShh, shh, shh, Stanley\\\'s slippers dragged across the floor on the way\r\nback to bed.\r\n</P><P>\r\nTribble, tribble, tribble, the mouse went on its way.\r\n</P>'),(3380,'<p>\r\nno. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHip people have hip things to do. They have other hip friends who do hip things. Im a bottom feeder. I find a hip, latch on and cruise for the ride. I look for the host. The host hip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have done absolutely nothing this past week. Well thats not true. I have gained weight, and I think I have a new chin because of it. Im bored with what I guess is my life, but I think im just bored of Korea. Another year. Another year. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can\\\'t watch tv. I do, but I shouldnt. It makes me angry. Watching CNN this past year has taught me that they update the news about as often as I do my webpage. If you sit infront of the tube for maybe twenty minutes you are caught up. Turn it off. Put some roots reggae on the system and dance in your clean FILA socks. Thats what I do. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dance. I dance at home in my room. I sway to reggae, hop to rock, and bob my head to the chillin sounds of hiphop and drumnbass. Reggae is what I love these days. It fills the void with messages of peace love and humanity. There is no love in this humanity I live I think to myself as I walk down the street watching honest children do dishonest things. The bored over educated youth. fifty four percent of university graduates in this nation are unemployed. This does not bode well for those with families and debt. The average Korean has seven credit cards. HOLY CHRIST. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI look out my window past my dead Tiger lilly and sprouting erb plants. They arent tall enough to be dangerous yet, but soon enough I will have to invest in a grow light, and get operations moved inside. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNothing for a week. BAH!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone around me has interests and things to do these days. I am left in their dust contemplating what will really make me happy. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf I were to stop the violence in the middle east would I be able to put that on my resume? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIRAQ: where in the fuck are those weapons of mass distraction? Apparently there are some in Tiblisi, but none in Iraq. The process of rebuilding Iraq is slow and forgotten. I find myself asking \\\"what about Afghanistan\\\" whenever I read or watch an article about iraq. Nothing is improving there...no matter how much they say it is. How can you liberate a nation while occupying it. I see no lasting improvements. As soon as the U.S leave, things will hit the shitter. Its going to be messy either way. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPALASTINE/ISRAEL: The roadmap to peace is a joke. Who the fuck does dubya think he is? Its a little more complicated than meetings and ceasefires and all that bullshit. Until I see some cash flowing, and the average standard of living rise among all palastinians, NOTHING WILL CHANGE. Peace in the middle east will be gernerational. It takes time and money not summits. We often think that HAMMAS is a terrorist group and that they are bad. What we dont realize is that HAMMAS is like the RED CROSS with guns. If the Palastinian authority set up and handed out charity like those associated with HAMMAS did, Im sure there would be less support for HAMMAS...but as it stands...when you need food you goto them, not to ABBAS and his minions.   \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIRAN: holy shit! pro-democracy demonstrations? Motobike riding islamic warriors. Civilian nuclear weapons production. Its a mess. Let\\\'s not invade.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNORTH KOREA: INVADE!!!!!! INVADE!!!!!!!!! this place is a shithole in total need of some good ol fashion warrage. In a new survey of 1000 chinese citizens, only fifty four precent of those questioned said they would support CHINA if they were to aid N.Korea in a war against the United States and the West, for a second time. I say who gives a fuck. Those 1000 citizens probably got laid off by YAHOO during the bubble bust. Im sure most of CHina doesnt give two shits what CHINA does if North Korea gets invaded. I sure as hell cant wait. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIm so horny, but in a totally a-sexual way. I would just love to have someone in my bed to fondle and play around with, but this is not so, and so I just leaf through my Japanese spokemodel magazine and ponder life with a castrol racing girl. she is hot. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI woke up at five am, had a bowl of cereal, smoked a cigarette, and went back to bed. I woke up again at noon and smoked a cigarette. I went back to bed and woke up for work at two pm. lazed around the apartment for an hour and then walked to work, phoning in a food order on the way. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nIt has been raining a lot. The rainy season. Last night as it came down making plinkity plink noises through my window I watched Tom Green make a fool of himself as the guest host of the David Letterman show. It was funny, but in a \\\"if I wasnt watching this id be drooling in bed\\\" kinda lathargic manner.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nneed money. hate money. need money. hate money. need money. hate money. need money. hate money. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nSo this is where I stand when it comes to life right now.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3381,'<p>\r\nPardon the title, it is a bit misleading. This weekend, I will be taking my first baby steps into the realm of cosplaying â€“ a bizarre activity involving people pretending to be characters straight out of Cartoon Networkâ€™s Toonami programming.\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIâ€™m not an anime connoisseur, not one bit, not at all. I watched a heinous amount of Robotech when I was a kid. And I loved it dearly but really, that was about it. Later, I managed to catch a few episodes of the ubiquitous Sailor Moon and a little bit of Gundam Wing, just because everyone was saying that it had five hot cartoon boys in it. But, when it comes down to it, I canâ€™t tell one wide eyed blond chick with a Japanese name apart from another. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd yet, this weekend, I will be putzing around a convention dedicated to said and similar characters, and dressed as one, no less.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI am unsure what convinced me to plop down 50 bucks for a convention regarding a subject that I am not terribly interested in. Perhaps it was the promise of a panel on leatherworking and vacuum forming. And I suppose I couldnâ€™t resist the temptation to go to yet another convention â€“ the very word evokes an aura of lovely, lovely geekdom, for what itâ€™s worth. I am going alone. None of my friends have consented to stoop to such a degradingly â€œgeekâ€ level. I know people who are going, of course, but for the first time, I will be without someone to hide behind, or someone to fake an entertaining conversation with. I will be alone, with no other choice but to find similarly kindred spirits, although looking for people who arenâ€™t really into anime, but are dressing up for the hell of it may be a bit scarce at an anime convention.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI have seen pictures of this quirky activity, of course. I have seen pictures of 14 year old girls dressed(though that might be a very ambiguous word) as half naked nymphettes. I have seen overweight men in Sailor Moon outfits. I have also seen the painstakingly accurate costume recreations that I donâ€™t doubt cost a couple months of my meagre pay. It makes me cringe, and smile, and nod and want to fade back away into my shadows of slightly less charismatic geekdom. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nBut I have spent a week transforming myself into a character from a console game that debuted in 1995. Lucca, from Chrono Trigger, to be exact. I have crafted a helmet out of Model Magic and foam. I have wrestled with my sewing machine, switching its needles until it was placated. I have cut up multiple belts, pounded in rivets and broken two of my motherâ€™s Dremel bits. On top of that, I somehow agreed to throw together yet another costume from a movie I have not watched, of a character I barely resemble to help someone I barely know complete a group picture. And â€“ why? All for my innate love of costuming? No â€“ all for the sake of being yet another â€œgeekâ€ in a crowd of japanese schoolgirls, tall scrawny men in uniforms and scarcely covered cat-girls. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI have never shyed away from any expression of â€œgeekâ€. Somehow though, I suspect that this is actually a gathering of model wannabes who eat Pocky for lunch and use words like â€œkawaiiâ€ and â€œbakaâ€ and add cutesy suffixes to each otherâ€™s names. No, I am not that kind of geek. Give me my stinky, caffeine addicted LAN party boys. Give me my LARP-ing group, complete with duct tape and foam weapons. Give me a theatre and a soundboard and I will do my job. Give me Shakespeare, give me T.S Eliot, give me badly written Harry Potter fan fiction. Give me Counter-Strike, give me Neverwinter Nights, give me a wireless network to hook up. If I am to wear a costume, give me a Jedi Robe and a lightsaber to defend me by, or a wench-bodice and a pint of ale. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nBut I am afraid I do not understand this particular breed of â€œcosplaying-anime-geekâ€. The hordes of people that do not shun company, but gather around in endearing little groups for photoshoots. The kind of geek that willingly parades in front of a camera. The kind of geek that has seen sunlight. The kind of geek, that from what I hear, gets LAID at anime conventions. No, I am not that kind of geek. It is a brave new world that I shall set foot in this weekend and Huxley be damned(or maybe not), I would much prefer it if I was a lot less sentient. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIâ€™m not sure what this â€œessayâ€ is. Perhaps it is a plea. Perhaps it is merely a statement. Perhaps it is the last note I will ever write, for if I am taken away by a large tentacle monster and raped repeatedly in all my orifices, you, my friends, will know that I did not go gently into that good night. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3382,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡'),(3383,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã Ã¿Ã„\Z%&\'()*456789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¡Ã¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ±Ã²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ã„Ã¿Ã„$4Ã¡%Ã±\Z&\'()*56789: