turdy wooden door that could be locked.  He left his clothes and towel on a chair in the corner outside the shower.</P><P>He had just finished soaping up when there was a knock at the door.  \\\"We don\\\'t want any,\\\" he called out.  There was a grumble and a shout in a foreign language from the other side.  He rinsed off and dried.  He was nearly dressed when someone pounded on the door.  He said, \\\"Just a second.\\\"  There was no reply.  He slipped on a pair of engineer\\\'s boots and gathered up his towel and the leather case that held his toiletries.</P><P>Just after he unlocked the door and turned the knob, the door exploded inward, kicked open by someone on the other side.  He was knocked against the wall and fell to the floor.  A wiry little man in laborer\\\'s clothes sprang into the room and kicked him in the stomach.</P><P>\\\"Just a minute,\\\" he groaned, as if calling for a time-out.  The man shouted choked guttural words at him.</P><P>He tried to get up.  The man kicked him in the side of the head.</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t have anything,\\\" he said.  \\\"I was taking a shower.\\\"  The man kicked him in the head until he lost consciousness.</P><P>He came to when the man cut his throat.  He screamed and grasped his neck with both hands.  Blood squirted out between his fingers.  The man searched him, found the key to his room in his back pocket, and left.  He passed out again shortly afterward.</P><P>Somebody was rubbing his belly.  He opened his eyes to find himself under a sheet.  A female voice crooned, \\\"Mein Schatzie, mein Schatzie.\\\"  He clawed his way from beneath the sheet.  He was lying on a stretcher in a hospital hallway.  A smiling old woman in a formless black dress was standing beside him.  He felt his neck.  It was bandaged.  He was naked.  His chin and parts of his left side were covered with blackish dried blood.  There were needle wounds in the crooks of both arms, at the veins located there.</P><P>The old woman said, \\\"Come mit me.\\\"  He was surprised to find himself, without a word, wrapping the sheet around his middle and standing up.  He almost passed out from the effort.  He followed her outside.  She hailed a cab.  After a silent ride, they arrived at an apartment building in Flatbush.</P><P>Upstairs, she bathed him and brought him some worn clothes that smelled of mothballs.  When he had settled into a bed in the front room, she fed him soup.  After he was done, she set the tray with the soupbowl on the floor and sat by the bed.  Peering at him, she brought the wooden chair closer to the bed and took his hand gently.</P><P>\\\"They tried to safe your life,\\\" she said.  \\\"They couldn\\\'t do it.\\\"</P><P>He stared at her.  For the first time since awakening, he tried to speak, and she hushed him.  She patted his hand.</P><P>\\\"Don\\\'t try to talk.  You don\\\'t haff enough energy yet.\\\"</P><P>She looked off into the corner of the room, seemingly distracted by something in the curio cabinet there.</P><P>\\\"You vill stay here mit me now.  Rest avile.  You can try to sleep, but I fear sleep vill not come.  Sleep vill nefer come again for you.\\\"</P><P>At first, he concentrated on the sound of her voice and tried not to think about the words she had said.  Her voice reminded him of the sound of leaves being walked on, leaves rustling and soft rain.  Something about it was feral and omnipresent.</P><P>During the first days, he felt like he was losing his mind, but he grew accustomed to not sleeping pretty quickly.  Now time wasn\\\'t broken into the convenient units of day and night, but continued endlesssly as one long day.  He and the old woman, whose name was Hilda, spoke quietly to each other and went shopping and to church every morning.  \\\"We pray to St. __________,\\\" she told him.  \\\"You must do zis efery morning, or you vill die.\\\"  She taught him prayers in old languages.  He memorized them phonetically and never learned what they meant.</P><P>She never revealed how she brought him back to life, and he never asked.  It was good enough just to be alive.  They played chess and cards, ate well and listened to the radio at night.  They subsisted on money her husband had left her.  After two weeks, she clipped the stiches on his throat with a pair of fingernail scissors and gently pulled them out.</P><P>One morning, he cut himself shaving.  The sting and the brilliance of the blood against his skin, the nick\\\'s proximity to the wound and the memory of the wounding jarred him, seemingly awakened him out of some dreary reverie.  What was he doing here?  Why had he left the hospital with the old woman?  Why had he stayed with her for so long?</P><P>He left, snuck out while she was in the bathroom.  He went back to the hotel on Broome Street.  They had kept his things in a porter\\\'s closet behind the front desk.</P><P>His mother fainted when she opened her door and saw him there.  The police had said he had been killed and his body gone missing from the hospital.  He told his parents that he had awakened under a sheet and wandered the streets in a daze for two weeks before he\\\'d come to his senses.  The headmaster at the girls\\\' school where he taught was also shocked to see him.</P><P>Two years later, he married a young woman he had met at church.  They had four children:  two girls, two boys.  He taught for thirty-five years and retired to a small town in Florida.</P><P>In time, he went back to the old woman\\\'s apartment in Flatbush.  No one answered the door.</P></I><BR><BR><BR><P>Chapter 21</B></P><P>I found all these 16mm films in the back of the storage room and asked Ms. Larsen, the librarian, if I could show one to the kids.  She told me to pick out one I thought was suitable for the kindergarten classes.  I chose one on Hans Christian Andersen, dusted off the projector and ordered a bulb for it.</P><P>It was ready the following Friday.  Ms. Larsen ushered the first class of the day in and had them sit on the floor in the middle of the library.  I rolled the projector out on its cart, and they were all eyes.  \\\"Ooooh,\\\" they said.  \\\"What\\\'s that?\\\"  I told them what it was and showed them how it worked, pulled enough of the film off the reel to show them the tiny pictures on it.  One little girl said it was like what they had at the movies, that her dad had held her up to look in the little window at the back of the theatre, and it was like that.</P><P>At lunch that day, I sat with a fellow aide named Karla, a dark-haired girl I\\\'d gotten to know, and discussed our respective plans for the weekend.  We decided to meet up Saturday night at a rock club downtown.  Talking about mundane things with excitement, it seemed like we\\\'d known each other for a hundred years when, really, it had only been two weeks.</P><P>When I got home, I ran on the treadmill and took a shower, smoked a joint and clicked through all the channels on the television.  Christian got home at six with a paper sack of take-out Thai.  We ate and got high then sat around watching music videos with the sound muted and Brian Eno on the stereo.</P><P>\\\"Anna.\\\"  He looks in my eyes.</P><P>\\\"What?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I hear turkeys in the hall closet.\\\"</P><P>We laugh riotously.  This is a joke we tell each other to celebrate the fact that we\\\'re stoned.  It must be slipped into the conversation subtly, so that the other person does not expect it.</P><P>We sat in, watching \\\"A Face in the Crowd\\\" on video and eating grapes and cookies.  Somewhere around ten-thirty, we ran out of hash.  Chris called Luz.</P><P>We found Luz, a Spanish girl, through a guy we met in a club.  For a couple of weekends, we took our Christiania pot and hash to bars and clubs, met people and got them high, asked if they knew where we could get more once we ran out.  At Anhedonia one night, this guy said he knew a girl who delivered.</P><P>She buzzed the buzzer forty-five minutes later, and I let her in the door downstairs.  She had long brown hair with red highlights and big eyes that seemed to find pleasure in everything they looked at.  Her shirt was tight and mustard yellow.  She was carrying a vintage \\\"Six Million Dollar Man\\\" lunchbox which, after sitting on the sofa, she put down on the coffee table and opened to reveal seven or eight cakes of hash bundled in plastic wrap.</P><P>\\\"Which one\\\'s the best?\\\" Chris wants to know.</P><P>\\\"That depends on what you want to get out of it.  Do you want to get really stoned, just fucked up, or do you want something nice and mellow that you can deal with?\\\"  She held up a yellowy-brown chunk.  \\\"Or do you want something that\\\'s good for, you know, fucking?\\\"  She dropped her head shyly.  She lisped.</P><P>I think Christian blushed a little.  He said, \\\"We want to get really stoned.\\\"  He turned to me.  \\\"Right?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  I guess.\\\"</P><P>She handed him a black rectangular block.  \\\"Five hundred kronor.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I can only give you half of that right now,\\\" he said.</P><P>She looked confused.  \\\"What do you mean?\\\"</P><P>He laughed and patted her arm.  \\\"I\\\'m only joking with you.\\\"</P><P>I think the effort of speaking English must have been making him lightheaded.</P><P>He paid her.  We asked if she\\\'d like to stay and hit the hookah with us, but she had places to be.  Chris took the plastic wrap off the hash.</P><P>Five minutes after smoking, we\\\'re lying on the couch.  If feels like my bones and eyeballs are floating in little pools of milk, and there\\\'s an exquisite aching in my stomach, pleasant and welcome.</P><P>\\\"Christian,\\\" I say with great effort.  \\\"I think there\\\'s something in that.  That isn\\\'t just hash.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I think it\\\'s opiated.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Is that a good thing or a bad thing, do you think?\\\"</P><P>He doesn\\\'t answer.  I lay there for hours, perfectly content to stare at the lights blinking on the stereo.  We fall asleep with Eno still on \\\"shuffle.\\\"  All night long, I dream in cloudy pastels with mossy, foggy synthesizer chords in the background.</P><P>On Saturday afternoon, we\\\'re lying on the couch, smoking the black hash.  I\\\'m half wishing I had a cigarette.  We\\\'re watching \\\"9 1/2 Weeks.\\\"  Mickey Rourke\\\'s getting ready to fuck when somebody buzzes the buzzer.</P><P>It\\\'s Per.  He has a gym bag with him.</P><P>\\\"Erika\\\'s thrown me out,\\\" he says.</P><P>\\\"Well, don\\\'t think you\\\'re staying here,\\\" Christian practically shouts.</P><P>\\\"And why can\\\'t he, exactly?\\\" I ask.</P><P>Chris mutters something to himself.  \\\"I was only kidding.\\\"</P><P>I say, \\\"Sit down here and tell me what happened.  Did she find out about us?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, she doesn\\\'t know about us.  I\\\'ve been speculating in stocks with some of our money without telling her, and the broker sent something to our apartment instead of using my post office box.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh, that\\\'s nothing.  She\\\'ll forget about it in a few days.  Just chill out here with us, and let her cool off a little.  You can call her in a day or two , and I\\\'m sure everything will be fine.  In the meantime, smoke some of this hash and tell us what you think.\\\"</P><P>Per says he\\\'s never seen \\\"9 1/2 Weeks,\\\" so we rewind it to the beginning and watch it through again, each of us lying on one leg of the U-shaped sectional sofa.  Afterward, Chris wants to watch \\\"The Waterboy,\\\" and I\\\'m for \\\"Andrei Rublev.\\\"  Per breaks the tie and picks the Tarkovsky.  It\\\'s about five-thirty.</P><P>At nine, I get up to get ready to go out.  I ask them if they want to go with me, but they\\\'d rather lay there and watch MTV.  Chris won\\\'t let me use his car, so I take the bus to the club.</P><P>At Metro, it\\\'s a mixed crowd:  sleek night creatures share the room with the unpurpled masses, working class types just out on Saturday night.  Half of them are listening to the band, a power-pop outfit from Norway, while the others try to talk above the music.  I find Karla at the bar, facing the stage, a highball glass nestled in her hands.  She\\\'s wearing a black and mauve pullover and a white skirt.</P><P>\\\"Hey,\\\" she says.  \\\"What do you want to drink?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Whatever you\\\'re having.\\\"</P><P>\\\"This is just orange juice.\\\"</P><P>\\\"That sounds good.  I\\\'m so stoned, a drink would probably just be asking for trouble.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Got some good stuff?\\\"</P><P>\\\"At home.  My roommate does.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You wanna go back there in awhile and smoke a little?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Maybe.  Ask me in an hour.\\\"</P><P>We listen to the band.  They traffic in tiny luminous dots of melody and broad strokes of guitar noise and crunch.  They\\\'re good enough to make you notice and then regret that they aren\\\'t really great.  We listen for about half an hour, then Karla asks if I have to use the restroom.  I finish my orange juice and follow her as she gently makes her way through the crowd, skirting the wall.  Once inside, we wait in line and both go into the first stall available.  She goes first while I study my nails.  When we switch places, she digs a little vial out of her handbag.  It\\\'s half-full of off-white powder.  She unscrews the cap:  it has a miniature spoon attached to the underside.</P><P>She dips the spoon in and brings some out, closes off one nostril and sucks the powder up the other one.  \\\"I call this my little brother,\\\" she says.  \\\"You want some?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, I gave up coke a long time ago.\\\"</P><P>\\\"This isn\\\'t coke.\\\"</P><P>I have a vision of a time when I was a teenager, before I was fucking, a day at the beach in late summer, potatoes baking in a fire in the sand, bodysurfing and sand up your buttcrack somehow when you take off your swimsuit.  Of a time before complications.</P><P>From the time I take a hit until we get out of the restroom and head upstairs, Karla half holding me up, I think of the first time I got drunk and the first time I got high, the summer after that time at the beach I was thinking of.  I wonder what those kids are doing now, where they are.</P><P>We settle into a couch, sharing it with a blond Goth couple who are making out down at their end.  The girl has on a Sisters of Mercy T-shirt.</P><P>I look down through a guardrail at all the people on the floor and on the stage and  sitting at tables and at the bar.  What if they were the only people left on earth?  Would the race survive?  What if this was the only place, and no one ever left here, and no one new came in?  Would that be heaven, or would it be hell?</P><BR><BR><BR><P><B>Chapter 22</B></P><P>\\\"Get up.\\\"</P><P>I just want to sleep.  Please leave me alone.  I\\\'m sorry.</P><P>This is how I know I\\\'m an alcoholic:  I don\\\'t get hangovers anymore.  If I can sleep it off, I\\\'m alright.</P><P>Crack an eyelid wide enough to check out the clock.  Eight.  Saturday morning.  I can remember snatches of being in the ambulance and in the hospital.  I lay still with my eyes closed, hoping he\\\'ll go away.</P><P>\\\"Get up.\\\"</P><P>I fling the covers off, swing my legs around and sit up on the edge of the mattress.  Fight off the urge to puke.  I\\\'m still wearing the clothes I worked in yesterday.  My contacts are dried out.  I can feel one of them start to slip out of my eye.</P><P>\\\"Come eat something.\\\"</P><P>Pancakes.  I force one down.  Drink two glasses of orange juice.</P><P>After a silent breakfast, he goes to work on the hardwood floor he\\\'s putting in the living room.  Instinctively, I go in and stand beside him, watching him take a measurement.  He looks up at me, looking slightly disgusted, then looks back at the tape.  I look around.  A photo of Tim (18) and Morgan (13) and I (15) is hanging over the piano.  It was taken the summer Tim got killed in a car accident.  We look wholesome and cornfed, despite the fact that I distinctly remember getting high with Tim and Gretchen Covington the morning before this picture was taken.  We look like we\\\'re in an ad, selling something.  Orthodonture, maybe.</P><P>\\\"Go get something to write with.\\\"</P><P>I wander into the kitchen and find a pen and a piece of paper.  He only asked for something to write <U>with</U>, but I know he\\\'ll want something to write <U>on</U>, too.  Everything smells like beer.  Beer, and a burning odor, like my head\\\'s on fire.</P><P>Back into the living room.  Give him the pen and paper.  He measures from one wall to another and writes something on the paper.  Look at the photo over the piano again.  Tim\\\'s smiling his trademark smile.  Just the left side of his mouth.  I think of the first time I got drunk.  It was with him and his girlfriend and his friend Jay.  I start crying.  Sobbing.</P><P>Dad says, \\\"What the hell\\\'s wrong with <U>you</U>?\\\"</P><P>I wipe my eyes with the palms of my hands and snuffle back a snotter.  \\\"I miss Tim.\\\"</P><P>He\\\'s quiet for a second.  \\\"I miss Tim, too.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I wish he was here right now.\\\"</P><P>He doesn\\\'t say anything.  I stand there crying.  Crying so hard it makes my throat hurt.</P><P>He says, \\\"Did you ever go to the doctor?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Why not?\\\"</P><P>\\\"\\\'Cause they\\\'ll just put me back on that stuff that makes me feel like I\\\'ve been lobotomized.  And then I\\\'ll gain fifty pounds and start sleeping all the time.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, sometimes you have to take the bad with the good.\\\"</P><P>\\\"There\\\'s too much that\\\'s bad about it.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Well, you need something.\\\"</P><P>\\\"What I need is to be locked up in a jail for crazy people.  For crazy drunk people.\\\"</P><P>I start crying again.  He ignores me, slides some flooring into place.  I sit, then lie, on the concrete.</P><P>\\\"You\\\'re gonna get tears in your ears.\\\"</P><P>I sit up.  \\\"What?\\\"</P><P>\\\"If you cry lying down, you get tears in your ears.\\\"</P><P>\\\"No shit.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Don\\\'t swear.\\\"</P><P>I wonder if I\\\'m 86\\\'d from Spartan\\\'s.  The only way to find out is to go in there again.  Sometimes they forget about it after three or four months.  Shit, I\\\'m thinking about drinking again already.  Something\\\'s wrong with me.</P><P>He tells me to get another box of flooring.</P><BR><BR><BR>     *       *       *<BR><BR><BR><P>Now we have Paige to make us laugh, whereas before we were very serious adults.</P><P>They come in through the Florida room.  Morgan\\\'s carrying a basket of dirty laundry.  I take it from her.</P><P>\\\"Thanks.\\\"</P><P>She goes back to the car for more stuff.  Paige flits around the doorway to the kitchen, watching Grandpa put the floor together.</P><P>She says, \\\"Guess what:  we\\\'re gonna lose an hour tonight.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh, yeah?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, we\\\'re gonna have daytime savings.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Day<U>light</U> savings.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  That.\\\"</P><P>Morgan comes back in with a mesh bag full of laundry.</P><P>Paige says, \\\"I just can\\\'t understand this daytime savings.  I\\\'ve never had to do anything like this in my life.\\\"</P><P>Morgan says, \\\"You\\\'ve done it every six months.  You just never knew it before.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I just never cared.\\\"</P><BR><BR><BR>     *     *      *<BR><BR><BR><P>This is it, my fatigue and my depression pressed together like two flowers in a book.  I\\\'m going to sleep well tonight.  The day goes by.  When lunchtime comes, it\\\'s awkward again, with Mom avoiding eye contact with me and Paige enjoying herself, not knowing anything\\\'s wrong.  There are Easter decorations on the table:  tall, thin Modigliani-looking ceramic rabbits in Sunday clothes.</P><P>I tell Paige, \\\"When you were little, like two years old, you used to call these \\\'rats.\\\'\\\"</P><P>\\\"You were trying to say \\\'rabbits,\\\'\\\" Morgan says.</P><P>Paige chews on this.  She picks up the nearest rabbit and looks at the underside of the base.</P><P>\\\"Have you seen your dad?\\\" I ask her.</P><P>Her head sags a little, and her face clouds over.</P><P>\\\"Yeah,\\\" she says.  \\\"He\\\'s OK.  He\\\'s livin\\\' with his friends.\\\"</P><P>We\\\'re finished eating.  I ask Paige if she wants to go out in the backyard and play on the swingset.  Once outside, she starts jumping all over me, trying to wrestle.  I tell her to take it easy.  I can see them through the jalousied windows, still around the glass table in the Florida room.  Talking about me.  Deciding what to do with me.</P><P>I can hear the phone ringing faintly.  Mom gets up from the table and goes over to it.  Looking at the rest of my family, mom, dad, sister, what remains of the family I used to have, it feels like I\\\'m dead.  I feel like Tim, here by the picnic table where he used to sneak out at night and get high.  I\\\'m not a part of them, not a part of anything.  It feels like I\\\'m going to start crying again.  Go ahead and cry, you.  You thing.</P><P>Mom hangs up.  She says something to the other two, then steps outside and calls us in.</P><P>She says, \\\"Mary\\\'s coming.  Go get cleaned up.\\\"</P><P>I beat off in the shower.  I feel better then worse then better again.  I shave and get dressed.  Due to the drip of semen still draining slightly, the tip of my dick will fuse to the inside of my underwear and will hurt like hell later when I have to tear it loose from the fabric in order to pee.</P><P>We assemble in the living room.  Mary and Jeanette and Ed arrive.  Mary and Jeanette are sisters, Mom\\\'s cousins.  Mary and Ed are married.  Mary can barely walk.  Ed helps her down from the passenger side of the van, and we make our way into the house.</P><P>Ed tells us about stopping at the racetrack in Bristol on the way down from Michigan.  Mary interjects something every once in awhile, but you can\\\'t understand her.  She sounds like a very drunk person.  Beyond drunk.  Something\\\'s destroying her brain.</P><P>Mom remembers the family get-together the Christmas before I was born.  She was six months pregnant, and Mary wrapped a gift for me, labeling it \\\"To:  Who-sie What-sie McCord.\\\"  I felt like crying again.</P><P>They leave after an hour.  As Ed helps Mary up into the van, Jeanette hugs us and whispers, \\\"They say she\\\'s got less than a year.\\\"</P><P>Mary waves at me as they drive off, maybe remembering me as a baby.  I start crying and can\\\'t stop.  Paige can\\\'t understand why I\\\'m upset.  God, I\\\'m tired.</P><BR><BR><BR><B>END OF BOOK TWO</B>  '),(3385,'<P>A young woman and young man went to a diner on a Saturday morning in September.  The diner was called Alice\\\'s Restaurant, and it sat on a little hill across the street from the Greyhound station.  Inside, its walls were painted white, and a calendar from a local bank hung beside the door, above the cash register.</P><P>They took a booth in the smoking section.  The placemats were made of paper and were printed with ads from local businesses.  The sugar and the salt and pepper sat in a group at the edge of the table along with a bottle of Tabasco sauce and some maple syrup in a little glass pitcher with a metal lid.  Through a rectangular window at the back of the dining room, they could see the cook, a pale man with jet black hair and a grey goatee.</P><P>The young woman lit a cigarette and watched the smoke curl upward.</P><P>\\\"There\\\'s nicotine stains on the ceiling,\\\" she said.  She spoke with an accent.</P><P>\\\"Hmm,\\\" the young man said.  He was busy studying the ads on the placemat.</P><P>The waitress came to give them their menus.  She had a tattoo of the Zigzag rolling paper man on her left forearm.</P><P>\\\"Thanks, Margaret,\\\" the young man said.  He didn\\\'t open the menu, looking out the window instead.  There were two men sitting on the bench in front of the bus station.  One of them was drinking a Coke, which he presently finished.  He walked around the side of the bus station and threw the can in the weeds.</P><P>A blue sedan pulled into the restaurant parking lot.  A fat man got out.  He went around to the other side of the car and got a little boy out of a car seat in back.  When the fat man drew nearer to the restaurant, the young man said, \\\"Oh, shit.  Here comes a guy I bet I haven\\\'t seen in seven or eight years.\\\"</P><P>The fat man and little boy came in the glass door by the cash register.  They stood there, holding hands, perhaps waiting to be seated.  Evidently, they did not know it was a seat yourself type of proposition.</P><P>The young man called out:  \\\"Little Billy Smoot!\\\"</P><P>The fat man looked annoyed, then saw who it was and beamed.  \\\"Hey!\\\" he called.</P><P>The young man slipped out of the booth and went over to them.  He hugged the fat man.  He grabbed the side of the fat man\\\'s stomach and shook it.  \\\"What happened?\\\" he asked.  \\\"You got a little jiggle action going on here.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t know what happened.  It came up on me all of a sudden.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I bet it did.  Are you living back in town now?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  We\\\'ve been here for three weeks.\\\"</P><P>\\\"And where were you before?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Tampa.\\\"</P><P>The young man looked at the little boy.  He looked to be three or four.  He had grey eyes and curly hair like his father.  \\\"Who\\\'s this?  Little Billy Smoot, Jr.?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, this is Chad.  Chad, say hello to Mr. McCord.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Hi.\\\"  Soft little voice.</P><P>\\\"We\\\'ve got a six year-old daughter, too,\\\" the fat man said.</P><P>\\\"Wow.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Did you get married yet?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, not yet.  Came close to it a couple of times.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Don\\\'t hurry into it.  Do you see Bert and them a good bit?\\\"</P><P>\\\"No, not so much anymore.  Bert\\\'s working out at the mine ever since he quit baseball.  He\\\'s up at  The Hilltop bending his elbow just about every night if you want to see him.\\\"</P><P>They exchanged phone numbers, writing them on the backs of brochures they took from on top of the glass case the cash register sat on.  The young man took the fat man and little boy over to the booth where the young woman sat smoking.</P><P>\\\"This is Anna,\\\" he said.  \\\"This is Bill, and this is Chad.\\\"</P><P>They shook hands.  The little boy was shy; he wouldn\\\'t shake hands.</P><P>\\\"Very pleased to meet you,\\\"  the young woman said.</P><P>\\\"Same here.  Where do you come from?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Sweden.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Sweden.  So how\\\'d you end up here?\\\"</P><P>\\\"This evil man here brought me.  This man full of mischief.  He told me such big lies about this town.  He made it sound like the garden spot of the Western world.\\\"</P><P>\\\"It <U>is</U>,\\\"  the fat man and young man said simultaneously.</P><P>\\\"Bullshit.\\\"</P><P>They talked for a little while more, then the fat man and little boy went to a table in the non-smoking section.  The waitress brought the young woman and young man their food:  Spanish omelette with toast for her, pancakes with bacon for him.  Two juices.  Two coffees.</P><P>\\\"Do you feel like walking home?\\\"  he asked her.</P><P>\\\"Yah.  Sure.\\\"</P><P>When the waitress came with the check, he said, \\\"Margaret, we\\\'re going to leave the car here and pick it up later on.  Is that alright?\\\"</P><P>\\\"What the hell you askin\\\' me for?  I don\\\'t have any say around here.  Ask Tommy.\\\"</P><P>Tommy was the cook.  And the owner.  He said it was alright.</P><P>They walked over to the other side of the restaurant and said goodbye to the fat man and little boy, who were going to get haircuts after they ate.  The young man paid the bill then went back to their table to leave a tip.  He got a toothpick from a dispenser on the counter by the cash register, and they left the diner.</P><P>They walked through the parking lot down to the sidewalk and headed north.  The young woman shook a joint out of her cigarette pack and lit it.  She inhaled and handed it to the young man.</P><P>She asked, \\\"Why is he called \\\'Little Billy Smoot?\\\'  He isn\\\'t short.\\\"</P><P>\\\"He is called \\\'Little Billy Smoot\\\' because when we were in high school on the football team, all the guys used to walk around the locker room naked, and Bill had the smallest dick out of any of us.  I didn\\\'t name him that.  John McRae did.  Clarence Maura had the biggest one.  He was this short little black guy.  We used to call him \\\'Sport.\\\'\\\"</P><P>\\\"If you aren\\\'t going to hit that, then hand it to me.</P><P>He sucked on the joint and passed it to her.</P><P>\\\"Why didn\\\'t he get mad when you called him that?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Because he knows I love him.  I\\\'ve known him since we were in third grade.  Matter of fact, we were best friends in the third grade.  And the fourth.  We all used to call him that.  It\\\'s his name.  He got used to it after awhile.  He knows we all loved him.  I think he was proud of it, somehow, after awhile.  He grew into it, so to speak.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I think it\\\'s mean.  Very mean.\\\"</P><P>He shrugged.  On their right, there was a small engine repair shop with a Husqvarna sign in the window.</P><P>\\\"Look.  Husqvarna,\\\" the young woman said.</P><P>\\\"Is that a Swedish company?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t know.  I just like saying it.  Husqvarna.  Husqvarna.\\\"</P><P>They passed a Chevy dealership.</P><P>\\\"I wonder if they could order a tractor there,\\\" the young man said.</P><P>\\\"Tractors again.  You got tractors on the brain this morning.  What the hell would you do with a tractor if you had one?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'d drive it up and down Main Street.  With a little orange triangle on the back.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  Don\\\'t expect me to ride with you.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Oh, come on.  I\\\'ll drive and you can wave at all the people.\\\"</P><P>Next to the car lot, the Department of Motor Vehicles occupied a squat brick building set back off the road.</P><P>The young man said, \\\"Remember when we came through here that time and that man and woman were out in the parking lot beating the shit out of each other?\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah.  That the first day I was here.  I thought, \\\'What in the hell have I gotten myself into?\\\'  You\\\'d never see anything like that in Sweden.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Yeah, I know.  It\\\'s a civilized country.\\\"</P><P>When they got home, there was a light blue airmail envelope in the mailbox addressed to the young woman.  She opened it and read through the letter inside.</P><P>\\\"It\\\'s from my mother.  They\\\'re cutting me off.\\\"</P><P>He stared at her for a second.</P><P>\\\"Well, shit,\\\" he said.  \\\"What are we gonna do?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t know.  You could get a job.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I\\\'ll be <U>damned</U> if I\\\'m gonna get a job.  I\\\'m not about to get a job.  Why don\\\'t <U>you</U> get a job?\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t want to get a job, either.  Besides, I don\\\'t have a work visa.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You could <U>get</U> a work visa.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Nah.  Even if I had a work visa, I still wouldn\\\'t want to get a job.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I need a beer.  You want a beer?\\\"</P><P>\\\"It\\\'s ten o\\\'clock in the morning.\\\"</P><P>\\\"So freakin\\\' what?\\\"</P><P>\\\"You\\\'re breaking your rule.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I know.  But these are special circumstances.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.  Yeah, get me one.\\\"</P><P>He went into the kitchen and came back with two brown bottles of Mexican beer.</P><P>\\\"We could sell the car,\\\" he said.\\\"</P><P>\\\"You serious?\\\"</P><P>\\\"What else are we gonna do.  It would tide us over until something else came along.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I don\\\'t know.  Ask me later.\\\"</P><P>He went to use the bathroom.  When he came out, he couldn\\\'t find her anywhere in the apartment.  Then he happened to glance out the window and saw her in the front yard.  She was reclining in a lawn chair under the grapefruit tree, the beer on the ground beside her.</P><P>He went outside and stood over her.  Her eyes were closed.  Sunlight passed through the branches of the grapefruit tree and fell along her body in little squares and dots and blobs.</P><P>She opened one eye and looked up at him.\\\"</P><P>\\\"I decided to get some sun,\\\" she said.</P><P>\\\"Well, you\\\'re not going to get much sun under this tree.  You\\\'re just getting pieces of the sun.\\\"</P><P>\\\"Maybe pieces is all I want.  Maybe I\\\'ll settle for pieces.\\\"</P><P>\\\"OK.  Suit yourself.\\\"</P><P>He went inside and got another beer.  There was nothing on TV.  He thought about calling somebody, then changed his mind.  He sat on the couch and leaned his head against the back of it and stared at the ceiling.  There were cobwebs up there.  She came in after awhile and sat down beside him.  She tickled him in the stomach.  Maybe it would be alright.</P>'),(3386,'<p>Santiago is distracted as he punches in his jukebox choices. His thumb slides over the smoke stained numbers and presses them down with a soft click. The record arm whirs back into life and slips the next song under the needle. He doesnâ€™t notice that the tune is not one he intended to play. The kids in the booth glance over in unison, disgusted by the twang of Willie Nelson. He ignores them and steps quickly over the gritty floor tiles.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Back behind the safety of the bar, he resists a subtle but persistent urge to reach into the drawer next to the register and take a Camel Light out of the package he keeps there. He isnâ€™t yet aware he wants a cigarette in spite of the fact that he his hand is cradling the Zippo in his pocket. He stares hungrily at the brass drawer pull. Ice shakes in a glass at the other end of the bar distracting him. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>When he looks up, the guy points to his empty and holds up two fingers. He doesnâ€™t ask why the man wants two drinks instead of one. He doesnâ€™t care as long as the bill gets paid. Out of the corner of his eye he watches the guy talk to himself for a while, then get up and push himself out of the bar. â€œWeird one.â€ Slim says, cutting through the smoke. â€œYou mind?â€ He points to a full Manhattan the guy left behind. Santiago shrugs. Slim hops awkwardly off of his stool and leans his left arm out to swoop the glass to his spot. â€œDonâ€™t mind if I do.â€ He says and wiggles his eyebrows at himself in the mirror behind the bar.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When the door jingles again, Santiago checks his watch. He knows itâ€™s her without turning around. He fights to control a smile that slinks up the sides of his mouth and threatens his ears. He reaches into the drawer for a Camel, lighting it as he turns around. Her dress is the color of ink stains. A slit shows her right leg almost to the hip. The smoke from his cigarette wraps itself around the scent of her perfume. â€œHello.â€ She says with the lilt of a Yugoslavian accent. They both ignore Slim as he leers at her thigh and whistles under his breath. â€œSarah.â€ Says Santiago. He grips the bar with his free hand and lets the grin gain a little ground.</p>\r\n'),(3387,'me too.\r\n'),(3388,'<P>\r\nthis morning i ate an egg sandwich and reflected on the fourth of july.\r\n</P><P>\r\nas it happens, the nation\\\'s birthday is also my father\\\'s. (incidentally, my birthday is new year\\\'s eve, and my sister\\\'s is christmas day. my mother has had fantasies of somehow finding a category in the guinness book of world records for \\\"family with the most birthday coinciding with holidays\\\"). within our small and self-referential family unit, therefore, the fireworks and fanfare that take place around the world have always been specifically for us.\r\n</P><P>\r\nbut because we are also, as they say, a foreign-service family, patriotic recognition of july 4th has always been in order. americans abroad tend to do it up big for the day, especially if they are government bureaucrats. as a result, my sister and i attended myriad receptions and \\\'block parties\\\' taking place within barbed wired walls. \r\n</P><P>\r\nthe absurdity of it all never really registered, i don\\\'t think. we lived in countries of great poverty and political strife, but the fact that we were inside our protected enclave sipping pink lemonade was simply a testament to the greatness of america: we offered hotdogs to the armed guards at the gate.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy sister and i had no real idea of what america was. we had not lived there since our birth; our heady fantasies were made up of malls and proms and saved by the bell. (armed forces radio and television even replaces commercials with public service messages: i was sixteen before i saw a coke commercial, but i can tell you what to do if you are taken as a prisoner of war.)\r\n</P><P>\r\ndespite that, or perhaps because of it, i worshipped the barbeque, the parade, the peppy navy band tunes. one of the best parts was the face-painting booth, where you went in bland and emerged magnificently star-spangled. i waited anxiously for them to play the anthem so i could proudly put my hand over my heart.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ntonight i will stand on my brooklyn roof with my friends. we will talk about childhood and work and sex and life; we will drink quite a lot and smoke a couple american spirits. when we hear the booms sounding over the east river, we will raise our heads in awe towards the blooming night sky.\r\n</P>'),(3389,'They say: \\\"deliver us from evil\\\". Should life be so easy as to say some \\\"magic words\\\" and have everything sorted out. \r\n\r\n\r\nThey call it: \\\"wishful thinking\\\". Thinking seldom gets us anywhere. Thinking and not doing -- a million and one ideas never put on paper, never put into action. Thinking -- just sitting under the shades and watch the sun rise and set, drooling all over the floor to some ungenerous X-rated thoughts.\r\n\r\n\r\nThey call it: \\\"unhealthy mind\\\". Just watching the clouds march past. Maybe the brain is waving goodbye, bags all packed up and nerves all shot.\r\n\r\n\r\nThey name me: \\\"crazy\\\". A million and one names. I\\\'ve lost count -- never wanted to count in the first place.\r\n\r\n\r\n\\\"Pushover\\\". That\\\'s what I call them. Then words failed me, as it did the day before, and the day before it, and countless days before them -- lost for words.\r\n\r\n\r\nSo, I continue staring at the sun rise and set, rise and set. So, I continue to stare at the clouds marching. Who would have thought they\\\'re just screensavers?\r\n\r\n'),(3390,'<p>The afternoon commute home on public transit is a compendium of abnormality.  Look at the Oriental man sitting in the corner rocking to and fro, muttering his words with a look of desperation.  His son got caught cheating while gambling at a makeshift casino set-up illegally in the local public libraryâ€™s basement.  He is upset that his son got caught and that his family now faces an uncertain, possibly dangerous, future.  He swears that Triad gang members with dragon tatoos on their shoulders are walking with baseball bats outside their home, ready to bust in and beat the whole family for impropriety.</p>  \r\n<p>One black brother is itching to bounce his ball. â€œWhat is up with peoples always all up in my face?â€ he thinks while tightening the grip on the ball.  He is pushing 175, is six-six, and he is only 16 years-old.  People are always fucking with him.  His parents, his teachers, the ref who gave the borderline foul call that made him lose the game to free throws in the dying seconds.  He was given a half-hour lecture from his coach, who discussed his attitude and court awareness and the futility of losing his temper in critical game situations. He called him just another delusional ghetto kid.  â€œMy game is money like Garnettâ€™s,â€ he tells himself.  He is making others nervous next to him.  He widens his legs beyond necessity, pressing against the business man beside him, who doesnâ€™t want any trouble.</p>\r\n<p>The business man commutes everyday to different bookstores across the city where he drinks chai lattes, reads foreign newspapers and sits dully otherwise as he kills eight hours.  He was laid off months ago but he canâ€™t tell his wife who recently received a promotion at her work that puts her in a higher income bracket than him, even when he was gainfully employed.  The shame of it all is that he is lactose intolerant and he will have to switch to soy lattes, which tastes like fish-milk, if fish had tits.</p>\r\n<p>The Oriental man lets out a queer-sounding laugh after a Kleenex sticks onto another passengerâ€™s shoe.  The woman walks around none to aware of her recent footwear accessory.  His startling laugh collapses back into Chinese curses against his idiot progeny. The woman with the Kleenex on her shoe has a tense and uncomfortable look on her face. After a mundane afternoon at the office she decided to go shoplifting at the drugstore near her work. She had never done it before and it showed, resulting in a store ban and a resulting charge for theft-under. How will she look her kids in the face after getting caught for slipping 5 dollar earrings in her purse? She reaches into her bag for tissues as tears form and her nose runs.  She could care less about the charge.  Is her husband really sleeping with her best-friend?</p>\r\n<p>Across from me I see a middle-aged man looking at the panty-line of a Catholic schoolgirl who is chatting with her friend about the next school dance.  The pervert is sweating enough to form a ring around his shirt.  He is of medium build with thinning hair.  He looks like the prototypical closet pornographer and probably could pass for the serial rapist- type that lives in quiet residential communities, mutilating pet cats and luring kids through Internet chatrooms.  He has those square glasses; bifocals that make his eyes look way too small for his face. He has large hands and bad polyester pants that ride higher than his socks exposing a small comical stripe of white flesh.</p>  \r\n<p>There are thirty of these commuters all pressed against me, breathing my air, making the thirty degree day feel closer to hell. The windows are open yet the air is a stagnant, stifling, acrid, smell of rotting flesh.  I have abdominal pains and I feel like vomiting on everyone.  I have to get to the doctors by three, which I will be hard-pressed to make if this driver doesnâ€™t stop the twenty mile-an-hour tedium and start moving faster than â€œcrawling-and- falling-the-fuck-overâ€ speed.  I canâ€™t even see the windows.  I just see flesh and the ubiquitous ads for menâ€™s sexual dysfunction juxtaposed with ads for homeless shelters.  I am starting to drift into a waking dream through this lack of oxygen.  The only thing that stops me from slipping into a standing coma is this sudden jolt, as the bus presses forward and appears to go over something large enough to go thump, alive enough to yelp.  We all look at each other for an instant, recognizing that the bus may have run over a dog.  At least I think so.  I am sure some of the other people think so too.  But that shining moment of togetherness, when we married each other in a second of violent afterthought, dissipates.  The next stop most of us get off, spreading out in our different directions, towards our separate lives like, water being absorbed by a paper towel.</p>\r\n<br>     \r\n*		*		*\r\n<br>\r\n<p>â€œI canâ€™t take him anymore, his utterly bland gabardines, his lies, and his complete lack of direction.â€</p> \r\n<p>She is in one of those moods where it doesnâ€™t matter who is in the room with her, she will tell all in one cathartic maelstrom that would make a Shakespearian monologue seem tired compared to her ostentatious speechmaking.  Detail is what sets my wife apart, but succinctness is her failure.  The poor radiologist-intern has had no training for this.</p>  \r\n<p>â€œI know this whole thing sounds ridiculous, but I have had my lawyer prepare a court order for all this. He simply has gone too far.  The man should be seeking professional help and not having a wife as his therapist.  He has consumed me for years, and now that I want to break free he wants to swallow my entire soul.  I swear to God, I have lost myself in all this shit.  My mother, of course she warned me, but I have always followed my heart to dismal ends.  You know, I make a six figure salary, yet I still keep falling for losers. I am some kind of source that they migrate to.  Heâ€™d better show up itâ€™s nearly quarter-two.â€</p>\r\n<p>In I walk, two minutes early.   I look at her court order and I announce myself to the reception lady.  She nods my existence, makes a note on her clipboard and informs her keepers behind the swinging doors.  My wife for once is wordless; probably shocked that I would comply so easily.  She only had to go to court once for this, and I didnâ€™t even think to appeal.  Okay, technically speaking she is my wife, till I get the papers this evening.  We are in the process of a divorce which has gone on now for years.  I think she has all that she needs.  She has my limited-edition clubs, which her new husband probably uses to chuck golf balls into the Grand river.  She has the kids, and she has garnished a portion of my wages claiming that I ruined her chances of pursuing a medical degree.  Truthfully, she did have a Bachelor of Science, but she, nor me, had any at the money to pay for medical school, even minus the one-year old she raised while I worked.  After toddling through motherhood as an at home daytrader she is now an autodidact economist making tons of side money doing television spots for confrontational TV news shows.  Sheâ€™s beautiful and laconic, a sultry mix for private endorsement -- a vision for housewives across North America, as she jockeys for my retirement fund.</p> \r\n<p>Unfortunately we end our relations in a doctorâ€™s office, with only a radiologist as our intermediary.  The radiologist is from Hong Kong and she speaks English with a tender accent.  You could tell she was an over-achiever who arrived in the country maybe two years ago and had hired a speech pathologist to help her pronunciation.  She has a slight contusive accent which makes her stiff dialogue sexy, in a strange way turning me on.</p>\r\n<p>â€œWhat is normal for you? How often do you have a bowel movement, when, and how much?   \r\n<br>â€œWhen was your last bowel movement? Describe what it was like? Was there any blood? \r\n<br>â€œDo you feeling of fullness near the rectum? \r\n<br>â€œDo you use laxatives or enemas regularly? \r\n<br>â€œIs this constipation a recent change in your normal habits?â€</p>\r\n<p>I broke the silence with my first response.</p>\r\n<p>â€œWell no. I havenâ€™t gone for weeks actually.  I donâ€™t think I need to anymore.â€</p>\r\n<p>The wife breaks in: â€œSee what I mean? He is hiding something up there isnâ€™t he?  You are a sick, sick man that needs a lobotomy.  Donâ€™t worry, I will tell the children you were shot in a drive-by gang killing.  I think it sounds reasonable and honorable considering what youâ€™ve become.â€</p>\r\n<p>â€œMarion please be civil. If you are going to be here, please refrain from making it a spectacle.â€</p>\r\n<p>The radiologist interjects: â€œWell Mr. Brady, although having another family member present is higly unorthodox here, the court order does say you gave her consent. The x-ray does show some abnormalities in your stomach.  The abnormalities appear to be non-edible items.  Have you been consuming anything other than food?  You see, this is what may be causing the obstruction that is making you to be constipated.â€</p>\r\n<p>My wife tears the x-rays from her hands while my radiologist informs me about keeping hydrated and having regular bowel movements. My wife yelps with glee after looking over the image scan, a sound not too unlike the one I heard on the bus earlier that day.  She dons her headset and autodials her lawyer on her cell. She waves the x-ray and describes the whole situation. She makes two rapid kiss sounds and tells him that she would contact him after she had the doctorâ€™s note.</p>\r\n<p>â€œYou son-of-a-bitch, you swallowed my grandmotherâ€™s ring, the kids rubber scissors, mother\\\'s provincial spoon collection. Jesus you swallowed everything you could get your hands on didnâ€™t you?â€</p>\r\n<p>For weeks I had been reticent as the final divorce papers were being processed.  My wife was out for everything, and I wasnâ€™t about to give into her. She has taken everything that has mattered most to me. My life is a shell, hollow and meaningless since we split.  Her people were coming for everything and I felt damn near helpless; so I began to swallow.  I started with deeds to the house, safety deposit box keys, family photos, I even took a hammer to our wedding tape and crushed it into chewable bits. I was alright for some time, eating the occasional cheese slice and drinking next to nothing until yesterday when I got the pains.  I got the pains after my lawyer called.  Since swallowing our life I have felt better emotionally, but I have undergone some changes.  I have evolved. I discovered that since I consumed my former life I can now tap into the shit of others.  Every person I look at I am exposed to their miseries.  People on the bus ooze sad stories, and I hear them all boring into my consciousness like so many dull pencils poking at my skull.  Now that my wife has found me out everything appears to be coming undone.  Truth is a loosening feeling in the extremities.  No matter how hard you hold onto the things, eventually you will lose a grip in the end.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3391,'<pre>\r\npoetry, erase\r\npoetry, erase\r\npoetry, erase\r\n\r\n\r\nbackspace\r\n\r\nsend\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3392,'<P>\r\nI say invade Iran and North Korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is this big fuss right now concerning the evidence pertaining to the war in Iraq. I say \\\"boooooooo\\\". Normally I would be all for all the negative talk, but for once I agree. I agreed with the invasion of Iraq. I\\\'d agree with an invasion of North Korea. Im not sure I\\\'d agree with a war in Iran. That is a tough one for me for some reason.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIraq. Didn\\\'t everyone let out a big sigh of relief when they saw that the actual war was over? I did. This violence we see on the news now isn\\\'t all that suprising. Instead of whining about the lack of evidence and the budget of this invasion, America should really be asking ol Dubya to take a few valiums and let the U.N put a peacekeeping force together. Why the United States is hesitant is obvious. Nevertheless, when all the oil deals are made, roads built, textbooks re-written, i\\\'m sure the next generation of Iraqis will hear tails of how the Americans defeated one of Iraq\\\'s most destructive leaders in their modern history.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nNorth Korea. Here is a country that spits nuclear threats on its state owned radio station. The nation is a mess and the ruling elite know it. They know they have no chance. They have offered to reunite with S.Korea and set up a parlimentary system on the condition the United States leaves the peninsula. However conditions concerning the possible pardoning of Northern leaders from any sort of legal action is something many dont want to see happen. Ol Kimmy up there in PeeYang let two million people die of starvation because of his nukebitions. This is a country that has done everything but put ads in the New York Times looking for nuclear components, technology, and ideas. If one were to look into the \\\'actual\\\' North Korean economy, one would find that it is almost all \\\"gray\\\" and it is almost all criminally organized. China, Japan, Australia, have all seen a rise in North Korean criminal activity. I say fuck this past 50 years of diplomacy and just do it already. China wont do shit because they want the Olympics. Russia is broke. South Korea would be pissed off! The irony of this country I live in is sometimes overwhelming. It needs to end, and it needs to end soon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIran. why not? Let\\\'s test out the nutron.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOhhhhh say can\\\'t you see? An American Empire that stretches from Afghanistan to Iraq? U.S troops on the borders of Pakistan, Russia, Saudi Arabia(Saudi Aramco), Syria, Turkey, Kuwait, and some of the Stans. From quagmire to quagmire. '),(3394,'<p>\r\nInside those fields and orchards there are currents and ambitions. Flocks of flying creatures flap wings of desire and creative folly. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the confines of my mind a universe exists outside any other. Solar systems of anticipation cluttered with planets of opinion and tactful scrutiny. Surrounding a cluster of knowledge and kindness, are fragmented moons of assumption and astute judgment. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOrbiting the planet logic are satellites of upset and disturbed emotions. A small bi-plane zooms by at lightspeed with a banner that reads \\\"defective happiness can\\\'t be cured\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMr. Function relaxes on the sofa and his mind pipes in images of panic and cold feet. Dreams involving the viciousness of man towards man. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNever in his life had he seen a flower that did not bloom blue. What was it about those organisms that made him so unhappy. Do not pay heed to those worries and wishes. \\\"they\\\'re not worth the time\\\" she said to him in a very garbbled and magic mumbo jumbo of sounds. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTime had brought vast roadways of invisible weakness to his universe.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDemolition has resumed. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3395,'<p>i felt it as soon as i got out of the cab. those warm prickly sensations, running up and down my spine. i was home, it felt good, but there would be consequences and reckonings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>staying at a backpackers again and being a visitor in my own town. stranger in a not-so-strange land. it dawns on me that i have been on the road for six months to the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it took me a while to re-adjust. that deceptively sunny weather that hides the freezing cold so well. people i know everywhere. no solitude. no anonymity.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>faces. memories. issues. nowhere to turn. but i\\\'m home, and even the worst things can be tolerated. i have a strength here that i cannot explain.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when i got to the theater it started... the angry half-staring glances. familiar faces looking at me when i turned away, pretending to ignore me. forced anger, attempted justification of outrage...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck you all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>they say living well is the best form of revenge and they are almost right.... success is the best form of revenge when garnished with a healthy sprinkling of your detractor\\\'s own failures.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>someone once asked me how i knew i was doing what i really wanted. the only answer i could give was that on bad days, i never wanted to give it up. even at its worst, it beat a good day trying to be something else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there\\\'s a little more to it now. i can see it in the faces of both friends and detractors. that little hint of recognition, when they realise that i am becoming what i always said i would be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>then the long, drunken nights out with friends began. gossip. dancing. cuddling. near-misses which were one drink away from one-night-stands and red-faced mornings-after.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it may be a bold claim to say that one knows the meaning of life at the tender old age of 32, but i think i\\\'m fairly certain that i now know it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt>find what you love doing, what you want to be, and go after it with everything you have. treasure your friends and never give up on them. don\\\'t worry if you have bad days, everyone does, it doesn\\\'t make you special. you are what you say you are, if and only if you really believe it.</tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p>now if i saw that in a movie or read it in a book, it would make me vomit. but thinking it to myself, knowing it rings true, makes me feel good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why is that?</p>'),(3396,'<p>my little brother came by today on his way back home from where he used to go to college before he failed out. i say little but he\\\'s bigger than me. but one time he was smaller, way smaller, when i was nine and he was six or i was five and he was two. or when i was two years eleven months and mama brought home a small baby after being gone for a long time, before which i couldn\\\'t sit in her lap and she wasn\\\'t much fun to play with and couldn\\\'t run fast. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwe went out to eat. he told me all about how he and his old college friends watched horror movies, four of them and there was a theme to the selection and how the friend who brought the movies wrote all their reactions down on his website like MST3K and it was called HorrorFestaCon Two. i said it sounded like fun and that they had transcended geekiness and come right \\\'round again to something cool. the restaurant bill was reasonable and it was good to have a beer on sunday here in the blue law bible belt.</p><p>\r\nback at the house he told me all about something called warcraft 40k and how he had an idea for a webcomic series based on it and how it would be funny and how if only he could draw he\\\'d do it. i listened and gave advice and generally observed him and it was good to see him get excited about something like that, like i get excited about things. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nso i reminded him to study and do well at this his fifth or sixth try at making a go of it in college, this time at home away from friends and distractions and his computer just broke so no video games and dillon sc as of yet has no broadband connection. he is jealous of me and my toys but i hope it keeps him motivated to become a great physicist like he wants to be or a webcomic writer. whatever he does i\\\'ll always give him nice things and be generous and give him my old computer to play on because he is my baby brother flawed like me and i love him.'),(3397,'<p style=\\\"font-family: Courier New, Courier, monospace; font-size: 9pt\\\">\r\ni have to go teach a class in a half hour or so.  it\\\'s shortly after sunrise in houston.  days, weeks, months, spin and spin and spin.  i was here last year one time.  it\\\'s not so bad.  i\\\'m a sucker for a good breakfast and the hotel has this thing where you stick a card on your doorknob with your breakfast order and when you wake up, it\\\'s outside your door. except for i forgot what time to tell them to leave it, so they had to call me while i was ironing my shirt.  i don\\\'t have one of those at home.  an iron.</p>\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: Courier New, Courier, monospace; font-size: 9pt\\\">i think i\\\'m lonely sometimes.  but then i think that lonely is just what we call the condition of not being ecstatic.</p>\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: Courier New, Courier, monospace; font-size: 9pt\\\">i just opened the curtains.  i\\\'m on the 10th floor. my dad always says, \\\"houston, boy, isn\\\'t that a dirty town!\\\"  there\\\'s lots of grit on the window outside.  it hasn\\\'t been washed in a while.  the view looks out directly on the compaq center.  my cab driver says the rockets have played their last game there.  he says after the comets are done, they\\\'re going to turn it into a church.</p>\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: Courier New, Courier, monospace; font-size: 9pt\\\">morning is the best part of any day.  i get up a 6 most mornings and walking to the bus-stop as the sun is rising.  i like that. it\\\'s like the ants say, \\\"sometimes you can\\\'t see the blade of grass for the micro-fibers.\\\" if i were ten-stories tall, i\\\'d probably phrase it differently.</p>'),(3398,'<!-- bckwndw_sm.JPG. please make this picture the link to the page of thumbnails -->\r\n\r\n<font face=\\\"courier new\\\" ptsize=10>\r\n<p>\r\nthey built a 1957 robot electronica ghost town and left it <br>\r\nwith rockets\\\' documentary echo. <br>\r\ni want to stand in the hangar until every ablative shield that holds me up <br>\r\nhas burned away and i can see the sky <br>\r\nfor what it is. we are so old of what it isn\\\'t not</p></font>\r\n<center>\r\n<!-- 1 hanger fr -->\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_14.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_7.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n<!-- 2 hanger em -->\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_13.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_6.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n<!-- 3 bckdrkc135t.jpg -->\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_8.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_1.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n<!-- 4 hangerclt -->\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_12.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_5.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<!-- 6 engine23 -->\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_10.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_3.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n<!-- 5 btwn23_8.jpg -->\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_11.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_4.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<!-- 4 bckdrkc135t.jpg -->\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_9.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3398_2.jpg\\\"></a><br><br>\r\n\r\n<!-- 7  bckwndw -->\r\n\r\n</center>\r\n'),(3399,'<p>I look in my photo collection, and I realize that the last time I took any pictures was Thanksgiving last year. I like to take lots of pictures, and I used to be that guy that people would get pissed at for snapping picy\\\'s all the time.  I go back in time, looking at October and the funny Halloween pictures. I see how my hair used to be. If I go further back, I think there is a picture of me with a beard. I look like a freak in it. I can think back to the times when I took the pictures.\r\n</p><p>\r\n I remember taking mind photos in Montana, with Austin. I remember him telling me that if I stared long enough, and thought about it hard enough that it would be etched in my brain. It worked for that picture, though I think the picture that is etched in my mind is the picture of the back of Austin\\\'s head while he was taking a mental picture of the mountains. I remember that we had a fuck of a time on that hike, and that I probably will never go back because those cops in Montana might still be looking for me. I did take a picture of one of the cop cars that pulled us over on that trip. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI remember what pictures meant to me, they had hidden meanings and value. I don\\\'t look at them the same today. I was looking through a book on the Harry Ransom Center in Austin, and it sparked a memory of mine that goes back to one of my first memories. The photo was the first photo ever printed, of a building somewhere in France. Nah, it wasn\\\'t like the photos was particularly artful, just a side of a building. The memory it sparked was when  I was a little boy, sitting on the fence in my backyard in Albuquerque, NM. It was October, in the morning. The air was fresh, so it must have been early morning. I am looking up, way off in the distance is at least a million hot air balloons painted in the sky. I don\\\'t know that I knew what they were, but I remember it being spectacular to me. Something I had never seen. Such a colorful sky.</p><p>\r\nAs with most memories, a moment of sadness overwhelms me; the fun is over. No more playing on the playground. No more ice cream at midnight. There are these stages in life that we go through, like waiting to get into high school, out of high school, into college, out of college, into a job, oh please, please retirement. Retirement isn\\\'t the only thing to look forward to, there is success as well - the good ol\\\' american dream to follow. </p>'),(3400,'The subway is a challenge that not everybody is up to.  Upon arriving at my local station I know of only two spots where I can stand so that the doors are guaranteed to open directly in front of me.  I need to get on the subway first.  Standing there as the subway car slows to a stop in front of me makes me feel smarter than the others on the platform.  Why donâ€™t they pay enough attention to observe where the doors stop from day to day?  The doors always stop in the same place, give or take a couple of feet, and I am always there to step on ahead of everyone around me.   I need this little bit of satisfaction in the same way I need at least one cup of coffee in the morning.  \r\n\r\n<P>When I come down the subway stationâ€™s stairs in the morning, I can see my spot as if a big â€œXâ€ was painted on the ground.  Once in awhile, somebody is standing in my ideal spot.  This irks me.  I feel as if I have special claim to this spot on the subway platform.  It is the door that will line up with the exit when I reach my destination, allowing be to make a beeline to turnstiles on my way out.  But when someone else is in my spot my plans are dashed.  I am frustrated.  Why is that bastard standing in my spot?  He couldnâ€™t know that it is the exact spot where the doors will open, could he?  Who besides me would pay attention to such a detail? \r\n\r\n<P>To counter this Iâ€™ve pinpointed a back-up door.  Itâ€™s not as ideal as the first because it doesnâ€™t line up perfectly with my exit at the end of the line.  Anyway itâ€™s more important to get on the train first.  My final destination is a crossroads where hundreds of people either work or transfer to other trains.  People more neurotic then I crowd around the carâ€™s doors ready to bolt as soon as theyâ€™re open.  Iâ€™d rather wait for that to settle down before making my attempt.  Even with my brief delay I still get through the turnstiles before the larger horde further down the train.  There are less people to deal with at my point of origin so my position on the platform is everything.  I guess the difference is that at my point of origin I have control over the circumstances and at the end of the line I am in the hands of fate.  \r\n'),(3401,'<P>My morning meal is 90% ritual and 10% habit.  By the time I get off the subway on my way to work, Iâ€™m hungry.  Iâ€™ve heard that some people are able to function ably in the morning, easily incorporating the making of coffee and breakfast part of their morning ritual.  Iâ€™ve never been like that.  Since early adulthood, except for weekends, all my workaday breakfasts have been purchased from delis, breakfast counters or bagel shops (depending on what is nearby my current workplace).  \r\n\r\n<P>Lately my breakfast has been a toasted sesame seed bagel with a slice of cheddar cheese from the local chain of bagel shops.  Thatâ€™s Finagle A Bagel to Bostonians.  They donâ€™t make the best possible bagels.  Finagleâ€™s bagels are not as <i>dense</i> as bagels should be.  Theyâ€™re puffed up, light and airy, as if the bagel baker injected air into the raw dough.  I imagine the baker in the backroom with a bicycle pump, inflating the bagels to the edge of bursting.   Itâ€™s difficult to find good bagels in Boston â€“this ainâ€™t New York after allâ€“ but there are better bagel shops around.  These joints, unfortunately, arenâ€™t on my way to work.\r\n\r\n<P>My breakfast habits run in cycles and I feel this bagel â€˜n cheddar cycle nearing itâ€™s end.  Before this, my breakfast was a plain bagel with a scrambled egg and Swiss cheese purchased from a Greek deli.  The bagel here was even worse than Finagle.  Those bagels came out of plastic bags and had a stale, chewy quality.  I should have stopped eating them when I realized that it wasnâ€™t a fluke, that the bagels were consistently bad, but when after my first day at the deli, the counter guy knew my order by heart.  I walked in the second day and he broke into a big grin.  He pointed at me and said:  â€œPlain Bagel, scrambled egg and Swiss, right chief?â€  Itâ€™s a pleasing thing for people to know your breakfast order; you feel connected to something more than yourself, even if that thing is only a Greek deli.  I began to enjoy my breakfasts.  To some degree I even began to appreciate the chewy plastic-bag bagel.\r\n\r\n<P>I ate that breakfast for a few months, and every morning the counter guy pointed at me and called out my order.  I liked this especially when the deli was crowded and customers on line in front of me had to recite their orders aloud, but when I came up to the counter he had already rung up my order.  It was always the same price.  I paid with exact change.  I felt that the counter guy and I had some kind of unspoken camaraderie, a circumstantial friendship.  We didnâ€™t even know each otherâ€™s names.\r\n\r\n<P>One day when I went into the shop, my counter guy wasnâ€™t there.  A young woman with dark hair and a silver nose ring stood in the place where I was used to seeing my guy.  I had to say my order for the first time in months.  It was slightly disconcerting, but I shrugged it off.  Maybe he was out sick.  Maybe he was on vacation.  But the next day, he wasnâ€™t there either.  Nor was he there the day after that.  Finally, I asked the young woman what happened to the regular guy.  â€œWhatâ€™s his name? Maybe heâ€™s working a later shift.â€  I told her that I didnâ€™t know his name.  â€œIâ€™m sorry then, I canâ€™t help you.  Maybe he quit.â€  She paused.  â€œAre you going to order?â€ \r\n'),(3402,'<P>Truth be told, Iâ€™m not a very social person.  Excepting my wife, I have no close friends to speak of.  \r\n\r\n<P>Itâ€™s not that I donâ€™t like people; itâ€™s just that I donâ€™t put much value on friendships.  Perhaps its because I invest so little in them, but most of my friendships have been more like passing acquaintances.  Iâ€™m not willing or able get to know people in any deep way.  I can talk about slight things with others â€“things like music, politics, philosophical books or moviesâ€” but I canâ€™t see the point of talking about anything beyond that.  Why would anyone care about the interstices of my life?  Why would I want suffer through the life stories of others?  I have enough problems of my own.\r\n\r\n<P>I do have one person that, though I havenâ€™t seen or spoken to him in several years, I consider a true friend.  His name is Russ.  \r\n\r\n<P>Russ and I grew up together in California, and being that we both were social misfits, teenage punk rock rejects that couldnâ€™t flirt with girls let alone talk to them, we bonded together in unique solidarity.  We drifted away from our closest social group, guys who were more socially adroit than us, and formed a sort of partnership in which we could talk about anything and everything that came us: namely girls and how to go about getting them.  \r\n\r\n<P>The California suburbs are like suburbs anywhere, boring and discontenting for restless teenagers limited by a lack of money and independence. As a result of being bored, Russ and I started a communal story for which one of us would write an entry, draw an accompanying cartoon and then give it to the other for his installment.  The story itself starred ourselves as the heroes of a grand cross country adventure in a world populated by people we knew.  It was surreal and funny, each of us trying to outdo each other in pure absurdity.  In one episode, a friend of ours who we didnâ€™t like very much, was transformed into a huge anthropomorphic penis which we drew as graphically as a cartoon would allow: a scribble of pubic hair, delineated veins, and a dribble of urine leaking from the tip whenever the penis was unduly emotional.  The story never came to a conclusion and it rambled on for hundreds of pages before we finally wound it down.  \r\n\r\n<P>After high school, I went on to college and Russ took a job at a warehouse store.  I tried to convince him that he should go to art school, but Russ would have none of that.  He had hated school and was glad to be done with it.  \r\n\r\n<P>I went to school close to home so were geographically close and we saw each other often. The fact that I was a college boy and he was a working stiff didnâ€™t separate us; we were close enough friends to transcend the cultural barriers that separated our two worlds.  But I was disappointed he didnâ€™t want to go to college.  The state university I attended wasnâ€™t hard to get into and, though Russ hadnâ€™t done great in school, he hadnâ€™t done especially badly.  He would have been accepted easily.  Russ was (and is) one of the smartest people I know.  Sensitive and artistic, I wonder what would have happened to him had he gone on to college instead of entering the blue collar workforce.\r\n\r\n<P>Russ lives in Colorado now.  I live in Boston.  Heâ€™s been alternately a cinnamon roll baker, warehouse forklift driver, a house painter and a furniture mover.  He worked for a a meat packing company and a construction company. By contrast, Iâ€™ve worked as a bookseller, a copy shop attendant, a secretary at an architectural firm, a legal secretary, an assistant editor for an art magazine, a media analyst for LexisNexis and a web editor for a technology consulting firm.  It took me a few years for my education to finally click in and put me in a position where I was more than just a flunky.  I like my position now, and though Iâ€™m writing about something as dull as technology, at least Iâ€™m writing.  Those secretarial jobs almost killed me.  I felt that I was squandering my talents.  Being around educated professionals, some of which were younger than me, made me feel as if I were wasting my life.  I didnâ€™t learn much at either of those places, however I did develop a deep hatred for both lawyers and architects.\r\n\r\n<P>The last time I spoke to Russ I had called him because I was getting married and I couldnâ€™t think of anybody else to be my best man.  I had no friends; no people that I felt were close enough to me.  I had acquaintances, a few drinking buddies, but I had no friendships with what I felt was the necessary gravitas. \r\n\r\n<P>At the same time I had been trying to reach him, Russ was trying to get in touch with me.  He and his girlfriend of several years had just had a baby girl, and he wanted to tell me about it.  It was an amazing coincidence, that both of us were going through life-changing moments at the same time.  I congratulated him on the baby, and he congratulated me on my impending marriage.  In the end, Russ couldnâ€™t be my best man because he didnâ€™t want to leave the new baby and, to be honest, he didnâ€™t have the kind of money necessary for the trip.  He was flattered that I would think of him, but it just wasnâ€™t possible.\r\n\r\n<P>Several years have passed now and I find myself thinking about Russ today.  I miss him, but to some degree Iâ€™m confident that weâ€™re still friends, that if I were to pick up the phone right now and call him weâ€™d be able to pick up where we left off.  Weâ€™d be friends in the way we were years ago.  Another part of me fears that phone call.  What if I we have nothing to talk about?  What if the time has widened the distance between us an impassable gulf? \r\n\r\n<P>Iâ€™ve heard it said that men donâ€™t need friends other than their wife.  I believe that a manâ€™s rejection of friends after marriage is something hardwired into the masculine psyche.  I tend to question men who have strong bonds with other men, especially if theyâ€™re married.  Itâ€™s a foreign concept to me.  \r\n\r\n<P>I love my wife. Sheâ€™s my best friend.  Iâ€™m able to talk to her in ways I could never talk to a man, and the way I see it, friendships without that kind of depth arenâ€™t really worth having.  Iâ€™m not content with the superficial nature of most friendships, but at the same time Iâ€™m unwilling to go any deeper.  Itâ€™s not that I donâ€™t want to take the risk, I just donâ€™t see the worth in it.  I have my wife now, who needs anything more?\r\n'),(3403,'<P>Truth be told, I\\\'m not a very social person. Excepting my wife, I have no close friends to speak of. \r\n\r\n<P>It\\\'s not that I don\\\'t like people; it\\\'s just that I don\\\'t put much value on friendships. Perhaps its because I invest so little in them, but most of my friendships have been more like passing acquaintances. I\\\'m not willing or able get to know people in any deep way. I can talk about slight things with others --things like music, politics, philosophical books or movies-- but I can\\\'t see the point of talking about anything beyond that. Why would anyone care about the interstices of my life? Why would I want suffer through the life stories of others? I have enough problems of my own.\r\n\r\n<P>I do have one person that, though I haven\\\'t seen or spoken to him in several years, I consider a true friend. His name is Russ. \r\n\r\n<P>Russ and I grew up together in California, and being that we both were social misfits, teenage punk rock rejects that couldn\\\'t flirt with girls let alone talk to them, we bonded together in unique solidarity. We drifted away from our closest social group, guys who were more socially adroit than us, and formed a sort of partnership in which we could talk about anything and everything that came us: namely girls and how to go about getting them. \r\n\r\n<P>The California suburbs are like suburbs anywhere, boring and discontenting for restless teenagers limited by a lack of money and independence. As a result of being bored, Russ and I started a communal story for which one of us would write an entry, draw an accompanying cartoon and then give it to the other for his installment. The story itself starred ourselves as the heroes of a grand cross country adventure in a world populated by people we knew. It was surreal and funny, each of us trying to outdo each other in pure absurdity. In one episode, a friend of ours who we didn\\\'t like very much, was transformed into a huge anthropomorphic penis which we drew as graphically as a cartoon would allow: a scribble of pubic hair, delineated veins, and a dribble of urine leaking from the tip whenever the penis was unduly emotional. The story never came to a conclusion and it rambled on for hundreds of pages before we finally wound it down. \r\n\r\n<P>After high school, I went on to college and Russ took a job at a warehouse store. I tried to convince him that he should go to art school, but Russ would have none of that. He had hated school and was glad to be done with it. \r\n\r\n<P>I went to school close to home so were geographically close and we saw each other often. The fact that I was a college boy and he was a working stiff didn\\\'t separate us; we were close enough friends to transcend the cultural barriers that separated our two worlds. But I was disappointed he didn\\\'t want to go to college. The state university I attended wasn\\\'t hard to get into and, though Russ hadn\\\'t done great in school, he hadn\\\'t done especially badly. He would have been accepted easily. Russ was (and is) one of the smartest people I know. Sensitive and artistic, I wonder what would have happened to him had he gone on to college instead of entering the blue collar workforce.\r\n\r\n<P>Russ lives in Colorado now. I live in Boston. He\\\'s been alternately a cinnamon roll baker, warehouse forklift driver, a house painter and a furniture mover. He worked for a a meat packing company and a construction company. By contrast, I\\\'ve worked as a bookseller, a copy shop attendant, a secretary at an architectural firm, a legal secretary, an assistant editor for an art magazine, a media analyst for LexisNexis and I am presently a web editor for a technology consulting firm. It took me a few years for my education to finally click in and put me in a position where I was more than just a flunky. I like my position now, and though I\\\'m writing about something as dull as technology, at least I\\\'m writing. Those secretarial jobs almost killed me. I felt that I was squandering my talents. Being around educated professionals, some of which were younger than me, made me feel as if I were wasting my life. I didn\\\'t learn much at either of those places, however I did develop a deep hatred for both lawyers and architects.\r\n\r\n<P>The last time I spoke to Russ I had called him because I was getting married and I couldn\\\'t think of anybody else to be my best man. I had no friends; no people that I felt were close enough to me. I had acquaintances, a few drinking buddies, but I had no friendships with what I felt was the necessary gravitas. \r\n\r\n<P>At the same time I had been trying to reach him, Russ was trying to get in touch with me. He and his girlfriend of several years had just had a baby girl, and he wanted to tell me about it. It was an amazing coincidence, that both of us were going through life-changing moments at the same time. I congratulated him on the baby, and he congratulated me on my impending marriage. In the end, Russ couldnÃ¢â‚¬â„¢t be my best man because he didn\\\'t want to leave the new baby and, to be honest, he didn\\\'t have the kind of money necessary for the trip. He was flattered that I would think of him, but it just wasn\\\'t possible.\r\n\r\n<P>Several years have passed now and I find myself thinking about Russ today. I miss him, but to some degree I\\\'m pretty confident that we\\\'re still friends, that if I were to pick up the phone right now and call him we\\\'d be able to pick up where we left off. We\\\'d be friends in the way we were years ago. However, another part of me fears that phone call. What if I we have nothing to talk about? What if the time has widened the distance between us an impassable gulf? \r\n\r\n<P>I\\\'ve heard it said that men don\\\'t need friends other than their wife. I believe that a man\\\'s rejection of friends after marriage is something hardwired into the masculine psyche. I tend to question men who have strong bonds with other men, especially if they\\\'re married. Those kind of friendships are a foreign concept to me. \r\n\r\n<P>I love my wife. She\\\'s my best friend. I\\\'m able to talk to her in ways I could never talk to a man, and the way I see it, friendships without that kind of depth aren\\\'t really worth having. I\\\'m not content with the superficial nature of most friendships, but at the same time I\\\'m unwilling to go any deeper. It\\\'s not that I don\\\'t want to take the risk, I just don\\\'t see the worth in it. I have my wife now, who needs anything more?\r\n'),(3404,'He lay there for a while, afterwards. He was sweaty and a little out of breath.</p><p>\r\n\\\'Well, it\\\'s really nice to see you again, Amy,\\\' he said.</p><p>\r\n\\\'You too.\\\' She paused for a moment, then attacked him with her pillow, the only one on the bed. \\\"Fucking asshole,\\\" she said, laughing, then serious all of a sudden she kissed him again. It was good. She was a good kisser.</p><p>\r\nSensual lovely girl, oh, oh mercy. <i>If there is anything better don\\\'t tell me</i>, he thought, <i>I don\\\'t want to know</i>. His stomach filled with liquid happiness for a moment, a soft fluttery feeling, <i>love, love, love</i>. He felt her watch him as he got out of bed and went into the kitchen to get two beers and a paper towel.</p><p>\r\n\\\'Two beers and a paper towel,\\\' he said, and grinned to be funny. \\\'Only the best fo\\\' mah bitches,\\\' which brought on another attack, tempered out of respect for the beers.</p><p>\r\n\\\'Oh god,\\\' she said, \\\'where did you come from?\\\'</p><p>\r\nHe took a long swallow of cheap American beer and lay back with his head on her stomach, feeling hipbone ribcage on either side, feeling good sweat and tiredness wash over his body, feeling with his whole body, thinking only with his skin (cool, ceiling fan) and with the muscles and sinew underneath. <i>I would make a good tennis racket</i>, he said to himself, and it sounded so good inside his head that he said it out loud, too, and fell asleep from the fan breeze and car noises outside, but not so asleep that he would ever forget how she stroked his head that night, over and over and over.'),(3409,'<P>\r\nHis name is Tae Gue. He is an exception, but not far from normal. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe is fifteen and has seen one movie in his life. He is forced to be dedicated. He is forced to learn. He is forced to memorize. His eyes are constantly tired and when asked if he has friends he says \\\"yes\\\", but you know it\\\'s a lie. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe doesn\\\'t know anything about being fifteen. He doesn\\\'t know what he is intersted in, and he doesn\\\'t know what it means to have fun. He goes to some kind of school seven days a week and never has more than an hour during any given day to just sit back and chill. If you even attempt trying to find out what he does in his fee time you find out quickly his time is filled with either school, church, sleep, or homework. In fact, Tae Gue has so little to talk about I often let him go without speaking. Listening to him speak about his \\\"life\\\" is terrible, and I know he hates doing it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI feel really sorry for Tae Gue. I see him hitting the age of twenty five with no social skills whatsoever. He\\\'ll graduate High School and University with great grades, though he will be next to useless in the \\\"real\\\" world. Like many Korean men, he dreams of a life working for a big company in a stale and monotonous office building.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nNobody in their teens should be without intersts, imagination, desire, and lofty goals. And yet I see signs of this among almost all of my students.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNo wonder so many Koreans embrace religion.\r\n</p>'),(3410,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3410_1.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3410_2.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3410_3.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3410_4.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3410_5.jpg\\\"><br>\r\n\r\nWe saw Times\\\' Square with no lights on.<Br>\r\nWe slept on the roof.<br>\r\nWe watched people exit the stranded subways through grates in the sidewalk.<br>'),(3411,'What is this city Columbia, to which I have recently attached myself?</p><p>\r\nTo me, Columbia is the familiar that once was unfamiliar; a few years ago, though I grew up only 100 miles away you could have dropped me off almost anywhere in the city, and I would have been completely lost. Gervais Street, Huger Street were names that meant very little to me. Assembly Street. Harden Street. Now they make up the very backbone of my life\\\'s geography, orienting me to the local and the far-flung, as Columbia\\\'s main streets are laid out in a grid with Main running directly north/south. In Columbia, I can point to anywhere on the globe. </p><p> \r\nThe people in Columbia are a good representation of the population of the South as a whole; someone recently characterized it as a \\\"New South\\\" city, meaning (supposedly) that it holds on to its traditions while accepting the new. This may not be entirely inaccurate. Gangs, drugs, real <i>news</i>--we have these things, but not to the extent of other places, due in part to our cultural backwardness. Hence the sometime fanaticism of the conservative South, which treasures the status quo above all else, though the liberal community continues to grow and become more vocal. Some move to embrace a more forward-thinking ideology, but the \\\'liberal as pantywaisted fool\\\' remains to a certain degree a common archetype in the middle/upper class-southern collective unconscious, which can be observed in its most obvious form in the Greek community of the University of South Carolina at Columbia.\r\n</p><p>\r\nColumbia is the best place to watch the times change for the South; sure, Charlotte, Raleigh, Atlanta have more people, more money, and more influence--but by these very qualities they have lost the distinctly Southern aura that envelopes my city. They have been forced by their economic growth to conform, whereas in poor Columbia, we don\\\'t have that problem, as much as it seems we\\\'d like to. Still, we make our way towards the future with one eye closed, from the hangover of last night, the hangover from history, from wars and slavery and poverty, one Hummer, one MBA, one trip to San Francisco at a time.'),(3412,'<p>\r\nEvery moment we\\\'re apart lasts forever. Seconds become hours. Hours, days.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen we\\\'re together time darts past us like an express train to nowhere in particular, stopping at nowheresville.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEvery moment we\\\'re apart hurts a little bit more\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe see each other less now\r\n</p>'),(3413,'<p>\r\nYou walk down the road from my school and you get to a large four way; a sa(4)goree. Crossing the road on the same side I begin my climb upward. Its not so bad from this side of the mountain. The road follows the mountain upward in a bow like fashion. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the base of the mountain I pass the aja-maz(married women) working in the fruit store and hair salon. They seem to always know when im passing as I make eye contact with them almost daily. Up past the construction site of a very large church and towards the spot a man confronted me while I was walking my now ex-gf down to catch a cab. Up. Up. Up. Yup, up all the way to the 4th left. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHanging a left, you are abruptly confronted with a very steep slope. When it rains, the tar they use to fill the cracks becomes slippery and one must use caution. Depending on the rate of rainfall, you might have to deal with a swift moving current.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHalfway up the profound gradient the smell is overwhelming. Handling sewage and rainwater is the duty of one pipe in Gumho, and my street provides the mountain with its pipe down towards the river. Because of the rainy season, huge steel grills are placed across all roads every so often. If you stood and watched the mung flow by, you are amazed at how much shit and piss is produced by the people of this mountain neighborhood. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce up the steep slope the road levels off and its flat all the way to my apartment. I can walk from work home in about six minutes comfortably. Its really not that far...just steep.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI see lots of life on my street. Like most areas in Seoul, the ammount of young children running about and playing is pretty high. Its the elementary and up aged kids you never see doing anything but walking somewhere with a backpack. There are a few kids who use the street infront of my building as their playground. Their mob usually consists of about four kids, but on some days, that bubbles to almost twenty. My only relationship to them is that they say \\\"helloooooooooooo\\\" and I say \\\"huggajibbagoogoo\\\" or something to that effect and jump around and make them laugh. They are funny kids. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis past weekend there was a fire in one of the buildings just down the road from me. At the time I was cleaning the storage room out and thought the smell of smoke was dust and other crap rotting in my storage space. When I heard the sirens, I knew shit was going down, so I grabbed a can of beer from the fridge and went upto the roof to see what was going on. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI got upto the roof and did a walk of the perimeter to see where the action was. It was just down the road; maybe six buildings away.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhile there was smoking coming from the building, it really wasnt all that thick. I was nervous when I heard the sirens. I live in a really dense neighborhood. Buildings literally hug the sides of other buildings. The possibility for serious fires is high I\\\'d imagine. It\\\'s no wonder buildings here are framed using concrete, walls are made of concrete, and just about everything else is made of concrete. My nervousness increased as I watched a good number of koreans dash from their own houses with fire extinguishers of all shapes and sizes. In addition to that, I saw at least a hundred fire fighters, fifty police officers and another group of men and women armed with video cameras filming the whole thing.(Korean \\\"officials\\\" with video cameras are almost everywhere. Cops video tape demonstrations, soccer games, and all sorts of other events. I think is creepy and scary.)\r\nThe smoke stopped and I was relieved. I went back down and finished cleaning out the storage room, which looks great now!\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThis morning as I was looking out my window, I noticed a couple of women who live across the road from me. One is a grandma, and one is a granddaughter. They opened the gate and climbed the stairs to their apartment. The grandma moved all the way to the door, and the granddaughter took her shoes off and climbed in through a window. A few seconds later the door was open or grandma. It was cute and I smiled. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA beautiful white with gold splotch cat likes the yard next to my building. It likes to sun itself just beside the red pepper plants near the traditional kimchi pots. It is a beautiful thing staring down into the overgrown garden, with kimchi pots glistening with rain reserves and the yellow blossoms of the unknown vine draping over the blue gate. It is a lush green plot with spots of colour inhabiting its terrain. It is a tiny piece of unkept nature. Something you really dont find much of in Seoul.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt night the blue flicker of Televisions can be seen eminating from most apartments. By midnight the silence is intermittently broken by drunk men and women walking home. By four that silence is broken by garbage trucks, scooters, and automobiles. By six Gumho is once again alive, and by nine so am I. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom a very wet Gumho, in the city of Seoul, this is Chiamattt signing off.\r\n</p>   '),(3414,'<P>The night was cold and Luis had a handgun in a basket of flowers. He stopped for a drink at Blarney Rock Tavern and the few locals at the bar looked at him and his darker skin, the color of cafe con leche. They looked the wicker basket of delphinium sitting on the barstool to his left and then at the shiny brown pebbles of Luis\\\'s eyes. \r\n\r\n<P><I>Whatya doin\\\' boy, sellin\\\' flowers?</I> An old Irishman with a tweed newsboy cap spoke from the corner barstool. Luis remained silent. \r\n\r\n<P><I>Hey, BOY are you sellin\\\' those flowers? </I>\r\n\r\n<P>Luis sipped at his whisky and pretended that he hadn\\\'t heard. The whisky burned down his throat and into his belly. \r\n\r\n<P><I>Hey! I asked ya somethin\\\'. Are ya selling them flowers? I could surely use some fer my wife. She don\\\'t like it when I come home lit.</I> \r\n\r\n<P>Luis finished off the whisky, set the glass down, peeled off a few bills and tossed them on the bar. The bartender was watching a baseball game on the TV that was bolted to the bar\\\'s ceiling. Yankees and Red Sox. \r\n\r\n<P><I>Hey, dimwit,</I> the Irishman went on, <I>can\\\'t you hear me? I asked you a goddamn question.</I> \r\n\r\n<P>Luis stood up and hefted the basket. Through the purple flowers he could see the cold glint of gunmetal. \r\n\r\n<P><I>Hey, I wanna buy some flowers!</I> \r\n\r\n<P>On the TV, the inning ended and the game went to a Coors Light commercial. \r\n\r\n<P>The game thus interupted, the bartender came aware. <I>Pipe down Ian,</I> he said with authority. <I>If you\\\'re going to sit there you need to leave my <b>paying</b> customers alone.</I> \r\n\r\n<P>Luis made eye contact with the bartender, hefted the basket again, and nodded slightly to acknowledge his gratitude. \r\n'),(3415,'I fogot put in the title to my last post:  can somebody do it for me?\r\n\r\nHere it is:\r\n\r\nA Quick and Almost Brutal Story'),(3416,'<p>\ndo you see the fire? it burns on the mountain across the plains. i found it the day before last, during my travels to the delta of sangre. the smoke was blistering the air. heat poured from the mountain, pooling on the valley floor. steam drifted from the tall grass, hanging below the thick ceiling of smoke. after reaching the delta i found my legs were hairless, covered with small festering blisters. i followed the taksam mountain pass to get home. though the distance from fire was great, the soft winds left the smoke stagnated in the air. my lungs felt shallow and heavy creating great lead weights attached to my legs. sleep was fraught with dreams of iron fists engulfing my throat, squeezing with all their might. today my lungs still produce black tar.\n</p>\n<p>\nit does appear to be moving south. every hour i have come back here to observe the progress it makes to the valley. now, i see, there is no progress left to be made. the acrid smell of the burning grasses is our last warning. we must warn, pack, and move on. perhaps north, to the salty air and cool rains of the sea.\n</p>'),(3417,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã \n	\n\r\r\"\Z)$+*($\'\'-2@7-0=0\'\'8L9=CEHIH+6OUNFT@GHEÃ¿Ã›Ã¿Ã„\Z%&\'()*456789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¡Ã¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ±Ã²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ã„Ã¿Ã„$4Ã¡%Ã±\Z&\'()*56789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzâ€šÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿ÃšGBÃ‚Â¹iâ€¢@NOAÃ’Â 7Ã€ÃÃ‡~Â´Ã˜Ã‹Â®Ã†?Ã¼IÃÃ©PÂ·Ë†YÃBkâ€™2Â¸â€¡Â¶xÂ¦â€ oÃ¯â€˜@t^*?2Å½Ã¡Ã¾zÃ–ÃÂ®Â»Ë†Â¤VNâ€ºÂºÃ¢Â¼Ãœ?|Ã“Ã¢Å¡HtNTÃ»\Z(Â£Å :ÃqÅ¾:RPÃ“â€™Wâ€°Â¾W#Ã©LÃ¼(Â \r;mIÃ¢ÃŽÃ¢zÅ Ã‘Å Ã¼IÃ•â€”Å“Ã±\\Ã9I`ÃWÃ“;psÃ¯VV]Ã¤ÃŸâ€˜Â®zÃŽRÃ„Ã—ÃµÂ¿g	â€˜?=Ã¥@\ZÃ¶â€˜Â¡Â»Ã¾5hÂ Å½Å“zÃ’Ã›Ãƒâ€˜Ã(Â¢â‚¬\n(Å¾Â Q@Ã¨tÃ†mÃO_Â¥hÃ›XC<^b`qÃ“9Ã…=,<â„¢?UÃ£Ã“Ãžâ‚¬5Ã¬Ã¢,Fâ€”Ã¦Ã…cÂ¿jÃ¤/~â‚¬Ã¥Â­.>SÃ™Â»WKkÂ¨Ã‚Ã â€¢â€ºÃ¥Ã­Å¡Â¼5â€¹hâ‚¬WaÅ’wÃ­@=Å¸â‚¬]Â¦Ã„Ã³eG`+}Ã¼=Ã¶;.Ã‚Â¯Â¢ÂµdÃ±.â€ºd?w8Â½+\ZÃ¯Ã„Ã’>Q	ÃÂ³Ã—Ã²Â Ã‡Ã°Ã¹â€¢?â„¢>9Ã©Å¾kZÃ›Ã‚Â¶â€š\"Ãžjâ€™={Ã–}Â¼ÃpÃ¡Ã¾b~ÂµÂ¹l%;wdÃ§Â¦hÂ¶Ã–^SÂªBVâ€zYfÂ­kÃ©:[Â¸â€žVÃ³Ã˜Â¢Â @Â ÃÅ’â€¦Ã§5Ã’Ã…gAÃ‰zV?$Â¹_#Ã¾Ã­kÃš]YÂ¡EWesÃžÂ¨In$Â¶?Ã«PÂµâ€â„¢ÃœÂ¼}(vÃ¢Râ€”ZÃˆÂ­Ã†~Â¢Â¡Ã¬Ã¼Ã€{Ã–0Å eâ€”vÃ£â€˜Ã‡^ÂµIÃ–gB~VÃ¦â‚¬5Ã®nâ€žÅ½Ã¤Ã«Ã½ÃšÂ£5ÃšÃ²Â±ÃƒÃ–ÂªGtv\'Å¸Ã²jÃ’Ã›1Ã–â‚¬)Â­ÂºÃœÂ±$Â°>Â¹Â­%FÂ¨=ÂªÃ”VyÃÃ‡Ã«ZÃ–VCÅ Ã¾â€Ã¶Ã¡xÃ¼Ãª&ÃyÃ«@ÃOzazjYFÃ°>Âµ#tÂ¨â€¹ÂªÃ³Ã¯@(Â¢â‚¬1u@[wÂµrÃ—1Ã¢LÆ’]vÂ¡Ã‹Ãƒ?Â¥sjÂ¾i?@\râ€¦F@Ã¬}jilÂ·?Å½@=j8iÃ£Å¡Ã“â€¡k&ÃžÃ´VÃž?â€ F}iÅ¡â€ Å¾Â»/Ã²Â«Ã Ã™HÃ£Â¬eeHÃ‡Â¥sâ€™F>Â´Ã¦=Â¿<fÅ¡[Ã«Ã‡Â©Â ÂºÃŸÃŒTlÂ§=Ã¸Ã¢?Ã¦â€šGÃ´Â£pÆ’Ã–â‚¬#â€°Â¢f*PÂ¯?Ã³ÃˆÃ¿Ã—â€“ÃY	Ã§Â¶*Â¡Â´%Â±ÃÃ£@FY~\\Ã˜Ã”Ã‘Zâ€”aÃem1ÃˆRAÃ½+BÃ–ÃŸâ€˜ÃÃ·â‚¬,iÃ¶Ã¡?ÂºÅ Z?Ã½Ãž:\ZÃ¥nâ€¢wâ€œÃÃ”ÃªÂ¯Ã¢{ÃÃ‘$jÃŸ+â€™ÃŸâ€¡jÃ–â€“Å¸hÂ»Å’Ã¾5Ã–ÃƒÃ…Ã¡XÃ¶Py-Â¸Â©Ã¦Â¶Â¹Ã±Ã¨Ã±NÅ¸ÃªYOÃ·Â£pÃƒÃ²Ã«R5Â¥Ã®â€¡Â±Ãƒ4Ã‘?!TÃ¤Â¯Â¥lÃ¸zKâ€“uÃ›rÃˆz27ÃŒ=Ã½Ã«Âª:uÂ¤Â°Ã‹$nÂ¾aÃ¿Ã¦<T3lcÃEpÂ¢Â¥2lÂ¦oÃ™Ã€ÂªÃ²KÃ˜uÂ lÃ‡fs?YÃ³Ã‹Ã²â€˜Ã\nÃ¬WÃ:â€¦Â´Ã˜Ã%Â¾zÂ¤Æ’zÃ¾}kÂµJa*Å HÃ¥Ã¾Ã¨Â ÃFâ€™c!hÃšÃ£h!J>Ã–Ã€Ã«Å’Ã—OÃ¢Ãy_jImÃ®Ã½TsÂ®Ã€Â¨nâ€ Â¼â€¢Æ’mF`+Â¡ÂµÃ±%ÃµÂ¤-lÂ³â€“â€°Â¸â€6qÃ©Æ’@Æ’,Ã’Â¼Å½Â©Â¶TÃ‡Ã¯eÃ›ÃwÃ‚Ã´?â€¦ajÃºuÂ½Ã•Â¸XÂ´GÅ¸3Ã¯0Ã·Ã²GÂ·jÂ«Â§Ã¸â€ Ã‡Xm?CÃ©iâ€œÂµÂ¿Ãœ=?ÂµkoÃ„,Y	Ã¹Ã”?Â¨Ã®(ÃÂ®`Â¼Ã‘Â¯VHÃ¤(Ãƒâ€â€˜xÃˆÂ®ÃŸÃƒÃŸÃ¶â€žÂ·Ã–Ã—Ã˜\\ Ã¿Ã´Â«#ÃŠTÅ½Ã„PP:Ã”Ã@Ã—Ã•ÃŽ!Â½PÃÂ®?<v?Ã©oÂ¾9WÃ½^:5zÂ§â€ 5Ã”Ã”Ã¬R\\Ã¼Ã{Ã—Å½Kq,Ã±Ã‡?â€Ë†aÂ°Â­Â¿ÃªÃÂ§j!Å¸*NÃ´Â pÅ½Ã¡HÃ¡â€ÃºÂµoÃ­Å ?Å“â€“Ã´Â®bÃŽÃ´Ã·?*Ã Â¸ÃœÃˆ8Ã‚Ã‡Ã‘EpÂ­Å’Â¸O>Ã•5Ã…Ã‚Ã¹\',+N>d2Ã®ldÃ±Å¡Â¹Ã³Ã¶?OÂ¦hÂ»LÂ°3>I9Ã¢Â¯Ã™^Â«7\'ÃžÂ©Â¥Â»?Ãž^;Ã¦Â â€“ÃŠH>hÂ¾Ãµâ€¡â€™Ã‰?ÃˆuÂ¥â€žÂ»Ã‰Â¶0dâ€¢Ã¾PuÃ¶Â«Ã³Ã‰\"m%eOÂ )tyXjf`Yâ€ž1â€“Ã¾Ã®8Â Â­tkÂ¹â€”ÃŽÂªâ€š	?ZÂ¶Â¾Ã”Â¥Å’Â´[%Ã‚yÅ’ÂªN@Ã·Â®â€™d[dË†mÃ¡TÃ­<Å¾kzÃ¡#â€šÃŽÃ·ÃˆFPâ€˜Ë†ÃŽ$ÃŽ9Ã¾tÃ¥Om=â€ºÂ£KÃ„Ã˜ÃœÂ¤Å’dzÅ Ã¬Ã´?X]Ã›â€™Ã„:?Ã¶?Â£Â¡Ã­&;Ãº\Z?Ã†{KfgwÅ PÂ²?IÃ£<Ã†Â¹8.Ã®tÂ»â€¢â€“hÃOÂ§Ã©@Ã´Ã¯Ã¥ÃŽâ€™ÃŽhÃâ€šÅ’}(:xÆ’Ã¿Â²ÃŠÃ‚t&=Â«â€¢Ã®hâ€ºeÂ·â€™{â€°UÅ“Ã®mâ€¹Ã²tÃ­R\\XC4ÃÃƒo(bÃ¢Â¬vÅ¸ÃˆÃµÃ©WtÃdÅ¡Ã³Q?Å’â€šÂ¹ÃºTÂ·Â¾\\Ã‰su<kÃ‡Ã‰Â©ÃŽÃ´Â \rhÃ¡â€ yDÃªÂ¦ â€¡l*xÃ‰ÃºÃ¿'),(3418,'<p>\r\nThis man is frustrated sexually. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n<P>\r\nOnce, when I was in class playing UNO with the kids I thought of how much the kids had to learn.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI wondered how many of them would endure and cope. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI wondered how many of the girls would marry just because they thought they needed too. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI imagined young boys seeing their old fathers beat their scared mothers. Would they replicate? \r\n</P><P>\r\nI have been hard on them in class as of late. My attitude isnt jovial. I am impatient and edgy. I find it hard sometimes. Teaching. Im teaching English to kids who for the most part dont care about learning English. English to them is like Korean to me. Difficult. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI am lonely and frustrated. The sexual anxiety of virginity was so much easier. Oh how retrospect sucks. Its your own way of saying \\\"i told you so\\\".\r\n</P><P>\r\nMy morale makes teaching more of a chore than something fun to do.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI like my job. I like who I work with, and I like the kids. I just dont have the energy or mindset right now to sit through them stumble through readings and vocabulary. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI am very much a robot in class. Under the aircon I perch, cursing and muttering to myself.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3419,'<p>you can already tell that it\\\'s going to be one of those long-remembered moments. the view in front of you is totally unremarkable; you\\\'ve seen it maybe a thousand times before. but tonight, the lights of the city through the rain-spattered window feel different.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you already know that this may be the last time you will look at them for a long time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s been building for a while. have you been avoiding seeing your friends because you had things to do, or were you already sowing the seeds of change? will it be easier to forget them now that you have not cradled yourself in the comfort of their friendship for so long?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and tonight was the final test. seeing just exactly the right people one last time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the flirt was there, looking as good as she ever did. but whatever the spark between you had once been, it is now either gone or insufficient to hold your interest.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the one-nighter floated around, giving you those knowing little looks, now so much like fingernails down a blackboard. another shot now holds no interest. the defeat of being turned down seemingly inconsequential in the scheme of things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and the hippie friend. he\\\'s there. i mean where else would he be? tolerating your worst moods with his ever-present air of calm. infuriating you with his impossible optimism. helping you out of every jam not because he wanted anything in return, but simply because that\\\'s what a good friend does.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yes, him you will miss.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and so as you zip up the last bag, and count the small pile of coins on the dresser one last time, you know that your trip is starting again and at once nearly over. this will be a new beginning of sorts. it\\\'s time to re-write your entry in the book of life one more time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>maybe this time you will be content. maybe this time you might be a little closer to liking the face in the mirror. maybe this time you won\\\'t rock yourself to sleep with a pillow clutched in your arms, taking the place of that special someone you have yet to meet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>maybe this time will be the last time you run. but you kind of doubt it.</p>'),(3420,'<P>The World Has Not Changed, but the globe keeps spinning.  It has been said that the only thing to count on is change, but that\\\'s a partial truth.  Change is inevitable, but the human condition is constant.  The struggle for identity is what drives behavior, whether it be constructive or destructive.  However, to be constructive takes dedication while being destructive is quite easy.  (It takes years to build buildings but only a moment to knock them down.)  Understanding the human condition is the key to understanding ourselves and where we fit in this world.  True happiness isn\\\'t found in things.  It is found in people.  We must condition ourselves to not accept material objects as happiness.  We must condition ourselves to stop being sold.  We must accept that nothing changes unless we change ourselves.  Plant the seed of change.</P>'),(3421,'<p>Thirty steps to the piano. Halfway there I remember you. I am glad to have lost you. You were a traumatic limb. Thanks to you I search for perpetual amnesia in her lips.  I was lucky to find her. She was looking at me like something you find, laying somewhere halfway used, and now youâ€™ve run out and donâ€™t know where to find it. She was astronomy, a subject I didnâ€™t want to study. Good thing she was only interested in a crash course. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>First thing she said, the morning after, was that she was allergic to gravity. I knew the trance was never going to end. To me she was as interesting as the first musical instrument made on the moon (long story, it was one of her constant motifs to dream about the moon).</p>\r\n\r\n<P>â€œWhat the hell, we have nothing else to looseâ€ was never the prologue to my evening until now. Improper sleep hugs me deeply. Iâ€™m on fire when she giggles. To remember her briefly, on my way to being defeated by the world again, makes the day worth it.</p> \r\n\r\n<P>â€œLife wasnâ€™t worth the violence or crumpled paper it was written onâ€ </p>\r\n<P>Bowie</p>\r\n'),(3422,'<p>\r\ni just flew into phoenix az for what i believe to be the first time. it is amazingly hot here. i have driven through arizona three times at least but it never seemed quite so bad. i think it\\\'s because driving from san francisco, you go through climates of ever increasing temperature until you reach the zenith of panting dry heat, which at this point i can only assume is phoenix. i\\\'m sipping a double maker\\\'s mark on the rocks and as far as i can tell it\\\'s still morning. i didn\\\'t have any coffee earlier in hopes of sleeping on the plane. it didn\\\'t really work. i left a bag of some sort of prescription muscle relaxers i found at my parents house on the kitchen table. i am hoping to find their stronger and more plentiful sisters either here or in tulsa, my next stop. otherwise i\\\'m not sure how i\\\'ll make it to chicago let alone new york. i\\\'m on assignment for dance magazine and also myself. shooting dancers in midwest cities, including my sister at the tulsa ballet.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\non the plane i was thinking about how afraid of flying i\\\'ve become. i didn\\\'t used to care much at all. in fact i think i may have enjoyed it. even now the prospect of sitting around doing nothing much for a few hours sounds appealing. most people freak out over takeoffs and landings. but not me. statistically, a plane has higher chances of malfunctioning or losing control during a takeoff or a landing. i\\\'m not afraid of statistics. i\\\'m afraid of feelings. and in mid-air three hours into a flight, it becomes more and more possible in the pit of my stomach that the plane will just unexpectedly fall from the sky like a rock. during a takeoff i stare fixedly at the wing and the ground speeding below it and think there\\\'s no turning back now, if we crash i just have to deal with myself. if so, let\\\'s hope it doesn\\\'t take long. and during a landing, i\\\'m so relieved to be back and fascinated with the world that\\\'s coming back into focus slowly. today i said to the man next to me, look what is that weird big water? and, i think everyone has a swimming pool in phoenix. he said, can you blame them? i really like when you land near complicated freeway mazes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe views that comfort me most are:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n1. anything that is swimming pool water colored, like the round markers on the phoenix airport runway, the cement blue island in downtown los angeles, and swimming pools themselves, especially if you look down and see hundreds of them below you, all slightly different.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n2. round concrete structures, including the guggenheim, those twisting parking garage ramps, and spiral staircases.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n3. when you see nothing but brown shapes connecting to each other and then suddenly a pink one flashes by and disrupts the crushing boredom.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n4. the drink cart.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n5. i\\\'ve never flown across an ocean before. the thought makes me nauseous but i also find that exciting.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe are beginning our descent. viva oklahoma!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nairborne, this is simonfk signing off.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nps., we just went though a big cloud.\r\n</p>'),(3423,'I still miss them.  This morning, I got off of work and rode my bike down to the Trade Center site to leave an offering. The National Guard, still flanking the site, seemed a bit perplexed as to what to think about me, the sadly frowning man on the bike, bearing prayer etched origami and a bundle of flowers much more gaudy and expensive than I could rationalize for anything else.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter all, it was just a couple of buildings; a couple of people, some political mistake that happened two years ago.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'ve said it all before. I truly miss the World Trade Center. Some may think it strange, having this fetishistic emotional response, this sadness, this mental malaise revolving around scenes, repeated endlessly in my mind. But, I\\\'ll say it again... some profound part of my heart permanently broke this exact morning, a few years back, when I, with my own eyes, saw the most boldfaced symbols of my hometown collapse in a shower of steel and stone and blood.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nI will never forget standing in the middle of the highway, watching the second tower fall, traffic chaotically ripping past me inches away, not honking. I remember seeing an old man crying, dead bodies hauled to St. Luke\\\'s in shopping carts, a catatonic man in a suit, bloody and covered with dust, staring at his feet, locked motionless on the Stuyvesant overpass.<br><Br>\r\nAnd today no one is talking, the city is quiet. This morning, at the fated minute, someone started crying at work. I sat down my work equipment and just got up and walked home, unable to catch my breath long enough to bike the commute.<br><br>\r\nEmpathy is lovely and meaningful, yet only those eyes that saw the horror in real time might ever understand. The rest should say prayers, light candles, visualize calmness for us... whatever it is you do- just keep NYC and its people in your thoughts today.\r\n'),(3424,'<p>The system is spinning at an enormous rate; the stress upon the center is immense. One snap and the entire system could collapse. The central powers must do everything they can to uphold some sort of stability in its fifty habitats. These habitats are torn, tattered, and threadbare holding on only to some amount of hope and a bit of familiarity. To regain the balance it once had, the hub must realign its spokes, readjust its boundaries, and tighten its borders. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThe plan has been implemented. To ensure that our goal is reached, we must reiterate a few points. Start with the big ones first, the smaller ones will fall in line if they aren\\\'t already. Attack what is personal to them, take over their issues, undermine their morals, smear their reputations; and if they continue to win, then replace the system that says that they won. Goes as far as changing the rules mid battle. Redefine their boundaries, give them a sense of power within the system but don\\\'t let them get outside those set boundaries. Incite a riot among them and let them fight themselves. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNot to worry about those outside of the system, we have taken care of them with bread and circuses. They have been fattened like pigs, and quite entertained. Production may be down, but for the most part they are all content and quite happy slurping life threw the thin red McDonald\\\'s coffee straws we have implanted. As long as we can maintain, for now, the fuel they provide for our causes are quite sufficient. We have a number of events in the past few weeks to increase their complacency. One might note that the recent event at the Mall event helped sedate thousands upon thousands of would be skeptics.  The Mall has been a great success, and should be noted for future engagements.\r\nAmazing what a few explosions, lights, and cameras can do!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe are happy to report that the media, with a few exceptions, is now under control. The key here is to give them just enough from the other side, yet lacing it with mild undertones of influence. Writers need to ensure that this does not come across as an attack, yet allowing the readers to form the conclusions that we want them to form and let them think they have formed this opinion all by themselves. Be aware that there may be sleeper agents within; therefore all editors must be highly screened.  As long as interests are diverted we should be safe. If need be, we can utilize on of our entertainment agents to cause a stir. Remember to have your requested reviewed by all internal committees; we don\\\'t want anything thrown back in our faces. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs for the exceptions, nearly 99% are working for us. You will receive a list, by usual methods, as to who and who isn\\\'t working for us. We have a few that new enlistees\\\' that would never be expected. Yet there are a few that we cannot divulge, and this is for the best interest of system. Continue with protocol for any alarming individuals, you will be notified personally if a threat is turns out be positive, if negative proceed with protocol. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe are quite proud with the efficiency of you all, and are excited about the up and coming events you have all planned, and this comes from straight from the top. Keep up the hard work.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3424_1.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\n'),(3425,'<p>the dream is more exciting than the reality.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni dreamed that there were two girls of high school age. i wanted to be close to them, nothing more specific. we were in a dark land with misshapen houses, indefinite light sources, sliding glass doors. they wanted to go to myrtle beach. i wanted to spend the night in bed with them, and was thwarted. their conversation drifted towards hanging out with a rough-looking guy from Florence, and in desperation i told them i could take them. dream dialogue:</p><p>\r\n\\\'I can take you to the beach. The only problem for me is, I\\\'ve been there like three hundred and fifty times.\\\' pause. \\\'i guess you could make the argument that three hundred fifty-one wouldn\\\'t make any difference though.\\\'</P><P>\r\ni can\\\'t remember what they said. a short while later i was jumping from stone to stone, flat stones laid in a yard to make a walkway. it was kind of dark but i could see fine, and there was the feeling of an ocean nearby. a moment of anxiety over the high school girls erupted and faded; after all, i would be shipping out with the navy for six months the next day.<p></P>\r\ni must have been drunk when i reenlisted...i couldn\\\'t remember why i\\\'d done it, but i was very sure that i had. \\\'Haven\\\'t I already graduated from college?\\\' i asked myself. i thought that i had, but wasn\\\'t i working on a second degree? had that come up when i was signing the papers? i was confused about the details, but one thought loomed large--how do I get out of it <i>this</i> time? i ran through the various ways to get out of the military: mental distress, being gay; would they still work? what if they didn\\\'t work? fear blossomed inside me. </p><p>\r\ni looked over at the lawn, beside the trail of stones, and saw where i had taken a small pickaxe to the grass of the yard and written \\\'KICK ASS\\\' in the grass. this is what had gotten me into the military the first time, writing that in the grass with a pickaxe, i thought to myself. i looked closer. the words had been dug out of the grass in shallow trenches, filled with white sand, and it had been so long ago since i had dug them that the grass was beginning to grow back, and fill them in.</p><p>i lost interest and started to wander back to the house. i was upset at the thought of spending four years in the military, that there was no way you could get out <i>twice</i>; upset that the girls were gone, and we wouldn\\\'t be spending what was now my last night of freedom rollig around on the big bed i had seen through the window, and the only one left in the house was the old lady who was trying to keep us apart; i wonder who she is? and my last thought was, </p><p>\\\'how could you have done it, when you had all those dreams warning you not to?\\\''),(3426,'<P>When you are sitting on the couch, laughing hysterically about how tragic and dark your life is, you never really think for a moment that maybe timing is about to kick your arse.\r\nTiming used to be my friend, but now, timing is my foe.<BR><BR>\r\nWith tears rolling down my cheeks, I lean towards the phone and pick it up with my right hand.<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"Hello.\\\"<BR><BR>\r\nIt is my brother #2, man of a thousand daily phone calls.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWhat is it today? What joys of life will he bring to me aurally? Will I hang up on him, or will he hang up on me?<BR><BR>\r\nI get it wrong.<BR><BR>\r\nIt appears that brother #1, man of a thousand eccentricities, has attempted to set himself on fire.<BR><BR>\r\nI laugh hysterically as it is the only reaction that I can muster.\r\nHe passes the phone onto some other do-gooder of a family member, who promptly hangs up on me.<BR><BR>\r\nTheir technique is just like his. The timing is perfect. Maybe I should acknowledge that my foe, even in it\\\'s darkest moments, has it\\\'s charms.\r\n\r\n</P>'),(3427,'<P>\r\nAnd then the whole sentence dribbled out of his mouth, like rogue toothpaste onto the front of your pyjamas.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"Sooooo, I have like a girlfriend now.\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI looked down at the table first.\r\nThen I blinked lazily and intentionally rested my fingers on my chin. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI knew he could see through my reaction.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI wanted to bite my bottom lip.\r\n<BR>I wanted to give him a fake smile.\r\n<BR>I wanted to let my eyes go all glassy, for me, not for him.\r\n<BR>I wanted to yell at him and scream \\\"WHY DIDN\\\'T YOU FALL IN LOVE WITH ME?\\\"\r\n<BR>I wanted to lean back in my chair, so that I could show him how relaxed I was.\r\n<BR>I wanted to fall into a little hole in the ground.\r\n<BR>I wanted some more chilli sauce for my noodles.\r\n<BR>I wanted to be honest and genuine.\r\n<BR>I wanted him.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"Excuse me waiter. Can I have some more chilli sauce?\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI scratched an imaginary itch on my left eyebrow, and then proceeded to act as though his announcement made me feel warm and fuzzy inside.\r\n</P>'),(3428,'<p> \r\nOh those fucking churchies are everywhere on this rock. They hand you all sorts of crap in the subway, and on the steps of the bus stations. They preach into old guitar amps and come by as you stand on the subway.\r\n\r\nJust the other day I saw a monk walk up the subway car throwing a card on a bunch of unexpecting laps. The old monk cant talk. No one gives him trouble. To my amazement, he walks back to where he started and begins collecting the cards again. My amazement hightened when I see people handing the monk \\\"chun-wons\\\" (roughly a buck). I saw a mom hand the card to her young son. She wanted nothing to do with that monk. Perhaps just not her denomonation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Female Korean teachers have all been turned to the church. They are infact religious freaks. They eat it up. Why? I have no way of being certain, but im sure the fact that they are unmarried women over thirty have something to do with it. They have failed in their parents eyes to fullfull their cultural \\\"job\\\", and have turned to god for forgivness. One of our teacher\\\'s even went so far as to say that Jesus Christ was her boyfriend. This same teacher left the school just last week to head off to Switzerland for four years to live in abject poverty with a band of nuns. She says she is going to become a \\\"Missionary\\\", but I think instead she\\\'s going for those sexy black and whites. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf you count the crosses from the roof of my school you will reach the number nineteen. Startled you are, this does not even compare to the number of reverse swastikas you see as you walk the streets. These houses of worship are everywhere, though tucked away better than the big red glowing crosses hanging about. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis evening after work while I was sitting on my bed \\\"crunching some numbers\\\" there came a few soft knocks at the door. \\\"what sooth!\\\" \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndisplaying my paint stained oversized boxers, an old stuzzy t-shirt with the sleeves cut off, and a mop of all directions, I walked towards the door and let out a \\\"who is it\\\". I heard a female voice and prepared for the ultimate of annoyances; my old roommates sex fiend ex-gf. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI opened the door and freaked out about six or seven churchies. Bibles in hand they all stepped back and looked all together astonished and rattled by what they saw in the door. I said in a loud booming voice \\\"well hello there! how are you today? COFFEE!?\\\" I beckoned them inside. Oh the confusion on their faces. It was priceless. I felt as though they had become something of a joy for me. One of them spouted something in Korean. I heard the word je-shush three times.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfickle as I am I tired of them quickly and looked at them sternly and said \\\"Im with amway\\\", closing the door as I fumbled \\\"anee-ka-sayoh\\\" (bye bye i think) and \\\"shil-a-ham-ne-da\\\" (keep up the good work). \r\n</p>\r\n '),(3429,'My cat is walking around the house naked.\r\n\r\nHis collar lost in the jungle that is my bedroom.\r\n\r\nWhat looked like spilt coffee yesterday, is now a stagnant lake full of crocodiles. He walks deliberately around the edge of the lake ensuring that he doesn\\\'t roughed up by one of the snaggle-toothed residents.\r\n\r\nThe newspaper spread ardently across the floor is now a bed of quicksand with hissing snakes at the perimeter. Each step results in a satisfying, yet macabre sound.\r\n\r\nThe flea-dirt ridden bed spread is no longer the unglamorous resting place for the apathetic cat. It is now the top of a monutain, drenched in sun, and safe from aforementioned pests.\r\n\r\nThe beast that seems to rule this habitat is no longer typing incessantly at the humming computer. Rather, the sound is that of an approaching inexperienced army. The army has not yet however mastered the art of stealth, and crudely approaches with it\\\'s alternative fanfare.\r\n\r\nThe feline sits atop the mountain gazing across it\\\'s domain, wondering at what time the trolley will call with afternoon tea.\r\n'),(3430,'<p>\r\nHe drew me a picture on the wall. This man. This drunk off his ass man. The Bastard sketched out his plan to turn South Korea into one big grid of Office towers and appartment buildings. He sputtered in his sojo scented breath that there was to be no building under nine stories. He talked of how there was this huge fellowship of developers and land owners who were all for the idea. I stabbed the man nine times in the gut and walked out of the bathroom. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn my way home I passed a black and white cat sitting on the knot of a clear blue garbage bag full of old orange juice and pepsi bottles. It stared at me as I dashed past. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the way past the shack that sells nothing but dried red peppers, I witnessed a man crouched down anxiously shaving his face with only a pot of water and a mirror that im sure was older and more fragmented than his life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI steered clear of the main street and opted instead for the back alley that runs along the rear of all the businesses that face the main strip. Walking briskly as I always do I passed three men all lined up against the wall pissing. I wonder if they too were part of the fellowship. I moved on. Going further and deeper towards home I saw in the corner of my eye a young boy with his pants around his ankles peeing into a storm drain. His mother was patiently standing by. He noticed my glance and as I glanced again, this time with a smirk on my face, he was looking upto his mom. Looking down at him only letting out a \\\"gwun-chuna\\\"(Dont worry about it). I moved on. I moved on with a smirk on my face.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the base of the hill, I was home free. It was just a five minute climb up this steep side of the hill. I began to trudge on. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI needed to get home. I needed to write a letter explaining why the man was murdered. It would be a shame if his death was reported as a robbery or vandalism or something perhaps even more absurd.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI lit a cigarette on that hill like I did climbing the telegraph in SF. It was Lumen instead of Camel Lite Wides, but the taste was still energizing. It dawned on me in my mad dash home that I had two cigarettes in my mouth. I needed to write the letter. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMaking that final turn towards home, I tried to think about what I\\\'d write about. I felt like just spitting on a piece of paper and mailing that to the press. Would that suffice? Doubtfull. In this group think and herd mentality nation I pretty much have to spell shit out with a crayon. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI didn\\\'t have any crayons, but I had this really cool pen I procured when a student left it behind in class. It was sparkle ink, and the particular model was im sure over the 2000won range. It was a perfect selection. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nClimbed to the third floor and unlocked the door. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSat down to write the letter. Wow, it was hard. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat was his name? What was the name of the place I stabbed him? FUCK!!!!!!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nok. must addapt. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<tt>\r\nTo Whom it may concern. \r\n\r\nThere was a man killed in the bathroom of a really stinky bathroom in Kumho Dong in Seoul. At roughly 10:30pm September 16th. \r\n\r\nI would just like to entrust with you some information. This man was not killed in a petty theft, or random way. He was killed because he represented to Korea a serious threat. He was a vile man, who wanted to turn Korea into a wall of concrete and steel. He, and his fellowship of capitalist dogs want nothing more than to make bucket loads of money at the expense of Korean society, culture, and perhaps even sexual orientation. His plan was simply too fucked up for Korea in general. \r\n\r\nI must warn you. \r\n\r\nStop the fellowship or I will...\r\n\r\nThank you. \r\n</tt>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI felt productive. The sparkle ink looked fantastic and I just felt really swell about things. </p>\r\n<p>\r\n'),(3431,'The new word we came up with is BUDISSY. it\\\'s a combination of BUTT, DICK, and PUSSY. ex: <i> example</i> butt+dick+pussy=BUDISSY. example being:\\\"shit,bitch, it smells like budissy in here!.\\\"'),(3432,'BUDISSY: <i>noun</i> butt+dick+pussy. a conglomeration of the three words. <i>ex.</i> shit, bitch, it smells like BUDISSY up in this place.'),(3433,'<p>I\\\'ve become convinced that things-with-motors have the metallic equaivalent of biorhythms.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Let me explain:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Item - Been driving the same car for 3.5 years now.  TOTALLY paid off (yay) with less than 80K on the clock.  Maintained with a mechanic\\\'s zeal and driven like it was stolen.  Never dirty; arrow-straight body panels...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Until some goober backed into the driver\\\'s door.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bad chi, man. can feel that bad moon a risin\\\'...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Shortly thereafter, I\\\'m driving Maggie the Opel (a robin\\\'s egg blue 1972 Opel Ascona I\\\'ve never had the heart to part with after a decade ove ownership) down I-696 at highly extralegal speeds.  How extralegal?  Well, no speed limits in the country have three digits, but Maggie\\\'s speedo was in that sector.  Anyway, being middle aged, I think to myself, \\\"Geez, maybe 100+ MPH inna 31 yea-old car isn\\\'t the sharpest idea.\\\"  Two hours later at lunch, the front suspension breaks as I pull outta my parking space at work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>More bad chi. This shit comes in waves, I tell ya - time to duck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a few days later, I get a cancellation notice from this tightassed excuse for an insurance agent.  Seems they fucked up auto deduct on my hotrod scooter (Got this Honda Passport that will do nearly 55 MPH on the straight and level - you wanna see God, try doing the double nickel on a 70cc scooter) so they said sayonara to my policy - Five freakin; bucks a month. Bitch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hold on, Edna - I can feel the big one a-comin\\\'...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Last weekend, I\\\'m out on the Dnepr - a Dnepr is a decomissioned Russian military sidecar motorcycle. Damned thing\\\'s runnin\\\' as good as it ever did - schweet with a captial schwuh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On a cloudless bight blue Michigan day, the damned thing decides to go cannibal on its timing gear.  The engine makes insanely obsscene sounds as metal parts never destined to touch make contact, and Lucy the Dnepr falls eerily silent in the middle of four lane traffic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Ohh...Forgot to mention that the lanmower engine went bad, too - a few weeks earlier.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>EVERY damned thing I own that burns gas is either bent, broken, or sidelined.  EVERYTHING.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I need a new car.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3434,'<P>Iâ€™ve lost my motivation.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnd Iâ€™ve been looking for it ever since. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nWhen I find it, Iâ€™m going to bottle it and sell it. I figure thereâ€™s a pretty good market for motivation. And Iâ€™ll be able to charge whatever I want for it, because of high demand. Iâ€™ll package it in amber coloured bottles and sell it over the counter at supermarkets and pharmacies. It will be a high end product, like Crest White Strips, where only one display package sits on the shelf next to a sign that says, \\\"ask at the counter for details,\\\" \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nIâ€™ll call it Motivator. </P>'),(3435,'<p>\\\"WRENCH WEEENCH!\\\" The kids yelled as I walked to school. I clenched my already clenched jaw and shook my four mental fists at them; I\\\'ll show em! I\\\'ll show em that 2 extra arms that end in wrenches are COOL and a GOOD THING and one day, THEY\\\'LL BE SO SO SORRY. I didn\\\'t cry on the way home. Well, maybe a little.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBut skip ahead 20 years and here I am, sitting in the mud, taking apart four irrigation valves at once, one in each hand. I can replace 4 antisiphon floats and gaskets at once, swap out 15 solenoids under a minute, adjust 4-8 flow controls while blowing bubbles and whistling dixie. What do I care that I have to wear serapes and can\\\'t remember which wrist I put my watch on? I have them in every different color. The serapes.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMy parents are really proud of me. They keep mentioning how they are so glad they didn\\\'t throw me in the river with the kittens. I have a tattoo of them, you know. the five kittens. They run around my arms, eternally alive, in the moment, free. They survived. They\\\'re me. I could have been them, and they could have been me.\r\n</p><p>\r\n(tonight, i could have been lying on my back struggling for an hour to open these valves up, lying in the mud, trying to open a valve before the light was completely gone, one leg braced against the fence and one against a wooden post, but no, it went as easy as pie. my jeans didn\\\'t even get completely drenched. i didn\\\'t even get bitten by three mosquitos tag teaming it for my forehead)\r\n</p><p>\r\nAnyway, I hope you\\\'re well. \r\n</p><p>\r\nKiss the dogs for me.\r\n</p><p>\r\nLove,<br></p><p>\r\nweeb</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3436,'<p>\r\nI wasn\\\'t looking at her through the sheets of wood because I needed help. I was looking at her because she was hot. I was switching the chairs in the PC room I had just entered. For some reason the Married man who usually lets me be with a Hello hasn\\\'t been around as of late. In addition to his wife, two new women have started working the counter. They seem to be friends of the married man\\\'s wife. They are also cute. Possibly because I see them mulling about installing patches and fixes to some of Korea\\\'s favorite MMORPG\\\"Z. Tech friendly women. At any rate, I switched the chairs and glanced at her through the sheets of wood that separated the smoking from non-smoking section. Her \\\"small business owner radar\\\" was on full effect and she knew I was doing something. When she caught me looking through the crack she was using to see what I was doing it was all over. I knew she was going to get up and check on me. Sure enough. Three seconds after having switched the chairs she had positioned herself at an angle I couldn\\\'t have seen her at and leaned over for a split second to make sure I was sitting down doing the things I was here to do.  I don\\\'t know if most people would have noticed or cared, but I caught her glance with my \\\"paintball-periphery paranoid nervous shy guy\\\" radar on.\r\n</p> '),(3437,'<p>music played in my head constantly. there was no music from outside, except the constant sound of thbe cadence caller. he sang one-a-ya two-ooo, three-a-day fo-oh, ah one two, a one two a-three-ay-four...</p><p>\r\nbut inside there was all kinds of music, everything i had ever loved up until then. pearl jam, the dave matthews band, phish, blind melon; and my own lonely, uncomposed instrumental rambling. we woke up at night and went to bed long after the sun had gone down. we ate three times a day, and that\\\'s about all i remember after the fight and the attempted suicides. there were classes on something every day. i closed off the outside world to a pinprick of light, drifting in my own self-induced coma. i adapted to the siberian prison, the concrete and snow and ice, the black-clad recruits covered from head to toe so that only their eyes showed. i looked longingly at the walls and fences of our little compound. it was by no means a big army base, just about 10 square blocks of squat buildings and streets, and always covered in snow. there was nothing beautiful but the sky, and the snowflakes on the person in front of me which i examined when the train came to a stop. the train being our marching formation. </P><p>\r\nthen, it was week five, service week. i had been looking forward to this for a while, with what little conscious thought i allowed myself. service week meant that everyone in the division would be assigned a job. there were ab out five jobs helping out in various offices, but these went to the recruits who spent their time and evergy jockying for some sort of position within the boot camp environment, like trusted inmates in prison. i was a cipher. the other 80 or so of us went to the mess hall. </p><p> \r\nwhich deserves it\\\'s own chapter. '),(3438,'<p>i am sitting in a noodle repository!</p>\r\n<p>i am thinking of you... </p>\r\n<p>if you walked in the door </p>\r\n<p>right...</p>\r\n<p>.....now</p>\r\n<p>i would tell you these things:</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>i am the irritation you feel when your pretty blue nokia gets crackly reception on an important call</p>\r\n<p>i am the unsettling paranoia you get when you\\\'re out in public and people stare at you the whole day and you wonder if you\\\'re sporting an incriminating stain somewhere that you can\\\'t see</p>\r\n<p>i am the frustration spawned by the unruly lunchtime burger that will <u>not</u> stay together while you eat it</p>\r\n<p>i am also the seagull-shit mayonnaise in the burger</p>\r\n<p>and on your clean work shirt</p>\r\n<p>and your tie</p>\r\n<p>i am the embarrassing stutter and drool you develop when talking to a perfect 10</p>\r\n<p>mostly, i am the worst day of your life. you know, when you were at 8 playing in the sandpit, and the fat kid pushed you into the pee-corner? and you were wearing a grey tracksuit? and then you cried in front of your whole class? and peed yourself because you were so upset and embarrassed? and your mother couldnt pick you up so you had to walk a half mile home covered in dark and fragrant stains?</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>yeah hi thats me. i\\\'m actually everything you hate about yourself</p>\r\n<p>stop loving me. i want to be alone.</p>'),(3439,'<P>This picture pretty much sums me up.  The flower represents my inner patch.  Although below it lies a deep crater, and I know I\\\'ll never be the same,  I still like bring forth some beauty to the world.  So, I plant a seed, and even though I know i shouldn\\\'t,  I often times water it with alcohol and marijuana.  The star represents my hopes and dreams.  Which was brought closer by my patch.  Also, they often seem much closer than they are, but when I look at them I feel like I can just reach and grab them.  I am the Earth.  So simple from afar, but so complex at ground level.  Full of beauty, sorrow, hatred, love, compassion; I am a person like every other, yet completely different from any one you know.\r\n</P>\r\n<img width=400 src=\\\"%%img01%%\\\"><BR>\r\n<img width=400 src=\\\"%%img02%%\\\">'),(3440,'Everybody says my life is in my hands.  However, much like a clock trying to grasp time. I can not find my fingers.  I feel the most I can do is point at it, and stare as it passes by.\r\n\r\n'),(3441,'Ok, if you read this when it was with the clock one. Yeah, I took it from there cause it wasn\\\'t as good.  It had the same sorce of insperation, but that\\\'s about it.... Yeah I used to write songs..\r\n\r\nI find my life with out meaning, since she left it\\\'s all so demening.  She loves me as a friend, yet all I see is the end. It\\\'s like sliced wrists instead of the throat, just a slower sinking boat.  No chance of staying afloat, for happiness there is no hope.  When I think of the times we had I sigh, and have the oh so bitter sweet cry.  It fills me with empty meaning, which is a step up from complete nothing.  My oceans have all dried, and my flower shrivled and died.  I go to the therapist session, so I can merly survive this depression.  My day has left and night reigns supriem, sorrow is the king.  But night devines the day, so I awate dawns first ray.'),(3442,'<p>whilst living on the road, one picks up many essential survival tricks. it\\\'s a cruel world out there, and if you want to \\\"rough it\\\" you have to guard yourself against those who would prey upon your innocence, and use your inexperience and misfortune to prolong their own nasty, underhanded existences.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>staying in communal accommodation, such as a backpackers, there are some very basic precautions one should take. for example, carefully placing all valuables in a small lockable bag and securing it to the bed, with the key around a chain on your neck is always a good plan. a money belt is a simple yet effective precaution against most thefts.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>not coming home stone-cold drunk on vodka and undressing from the door all the way to the bed, scattering wallet, keys and watch to the four winds, is also a suggestion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sadly though, every now and then an individual comes along that can thwart even the most paranoid and miserly of travellers. this morning we met once such cunning individual.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we do not know his name, but shall refer to him hence as \\\"the world\\\'s greatest catburglar\\\"...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>our story begins at around 2.25 this morning (forty minutes ago as i write this) when i rose from a deep and restful slumber on my ripped PVC backpacker mattress, to the soulful sounds of large european men snoring, and an all-female gang war outside my window. ahhh, the sweet life of the road.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i heard the doorknob to our room rattling, which at that hour was nothing out of the ordinary, and looked up to see which of my friendly roommates was coming home. perhaps the lovely swedish hottie i met earlier...?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there in the doorway stood the instrument of our doom. the tall, dark figure silhouetted against the light was clearly very skilled in the art of stealth, subterfuge and cunning. our peaceful travellers\\\' innocence was about to be torn from us by an instrument of purest evil.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the world\\\'s greatest catburglar was carefully attired in an outfit so devilishly cunning, that it would never arouse suspicion. the big floppy woolen hat and blue and white floral board-shorts were clearly selected for their camouflage and anonymity value.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the loud flip-flopping jandals at first seemingly so wrong for the task were, i realised, meticulously designed to put one at ease.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what can men do against such reckless cunning?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>immediately putting his ninja training to use, twgcb dropped to the floor with a thud and kicked the door closed. he then feel into a deep trance, brilliantly feigning unconsciousness, even going so far as to pretend to snore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the large hulking threat of twgcb then proceeded to commando crawl from backpack to backpack, rummaging clumsily over locks and buckles, ferreting out his prey.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>terrified, i meekly called out a plea for help.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"hey mate, can we fucking help you or what?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>instantly twgcb fell back into the snore-trance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fearing for my life, i grabbed my meager valuables and headed for the door, fleeing with panicked breaths into the corridor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>after telling the night receptionist, who said he would call the cops, i snuck back into my room, driven by my fear for the lives of my fellow dorm-dwellers (and having nothing at all to do with the fact that i had left my new discman behind, nothing i tell you!).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>twgcb had by this time hidden himself half under one of the bunks. well, i say hidden, maybe delayed would be a better word for it, as his considerable girth may have had something to do with him stopping for a moment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i lay on my bed for ten or fifteen minutes, as twgcb drifted in and out of the snore-trance-camouflage-thing, every now and then reaching a hand up to try and grab something off either my own or my neighbour\\\'s bed, and each time beaten back just enough with my terrified cries of -</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"hey bud, don\\\'t even fucking try it. fuck off.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his merciless response of a drunken thumbs up followed by more snore-trancing struck fear into the hearts of us all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>finally the police came and rescued us. it turns out that not only had twgcb been doing this on a fairly regular basis, but is also the chef at a local hotel, where i will not be dining any time soon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have to be at work in five hours. i am tired, crabby and drinking ginger beer. good night one and all.</p>'),(3443,'<p>His breaths are short and shallow; the nurse says she doubts he will last the night.  He can\\\'t speak - attempts to mouth soundless syllables, but nothing is heard.  His eyes, bloodshot from the infection, shout volumes of fear.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She enters the room and sits by his side.  She clasps his hand, and concentrates on the frightened eyes before her.  The sights and sounds of the room are stripped away - they do not matter, for she is the Soul Catcher.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She\\\'s seen more death than birth, yet she is young by calendar years.  She\\\'s tender to the touch, but stronger than steel.  She tends to a task that most men fear above all, standing tall and unscathed before Death\\\'s cold wind. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Soul Catcher learned her skill by accident - tending to a relative no one cared to visit in the final hours.  Her schooling taught her the physiology of passing, but the event awoke something else in her - she knew the moment of passing, and became the Soul Catcher. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Spiritual things do not come easy for me - I\\\'m a pragmatist by nature.  The process was explained - my reaction was skeptical, at best. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>His breathing changed.  The Soul Catcher did not blink, so intense was the concentration.  The passage of time froze as the life of the man wound down. Slowly, almost imperceptibly, the man began to pause between breaths.  The pauses melted into momentsâ€¦thenâ€¦</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The air became thick,  the room darkened perceptibly - even though lights were on.  The man\\\'s chest fell one final time.   They say the eyes are the window to the soul - this is true, for the eyes lost something. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The change was amplified in the Soul Catcher.  There was a sharp breath, and her face became the mask of a woman giving birth.  The darkness of the room seemed to concentrate on her features, as if light was being drawn in.  She held her breath and birthing mask for several seconds, then exhaled.  The room brightened, the air thinned. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"He\\\'s gone\\\", she announced in a matter-of-fact voice, \\\"go get someone to pronounce.\\\" </p>\r\n\r\n<p>She was right - he was dead.   The color drained from his features, and his eyes receded into their sockets.  Moments later, he was a man of wax.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Into your arms I relinquish my spirit. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I thought these word were literary license - They are not. </p>\r\n \r\n\r\n'),(3444,'<p>Date: Sat, 11 Oct 1997 16:39:41 PDT</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have eloped with your last e mail and am presently living in a small whitewashed villa on the coast of Costa Muerte, where the dead rise from the sand and the sea is a sea of dreams and yea, even the dreams have dreams within which are most brightly coloured and sing softly of the days Before. the Days of Ignorance and Naivety. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Days before Knowledge. of the Old World, where there were so many earthly joys to still be discovered. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had a dream I was a child again, but in some far away place in a far away time. Perhaps it wasnt a dream...a recollection? someone else\\\'s recollection?....and it was twilight..there was a large gathering of people waiting..waiting for something...it was medieval..possibly pre- medieval..and a group of men came walking towards us dragging a Great Beast on a chain. Gasps of wonderment came from the crowd who drew back in fear, their eyes wide. the scent of musk and roses from somewhere. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Beast was led into our midst, and it was the most beautiful sad creature, snarling and frothing at the mouth as it was beaten with sticks and choked upon the chain to further incite its rage and woo the crowd\\\'s attention. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>And then I was looking down upon myself..i was the only one standing there with the Beast. And it looked at me through its humiliation at the hands of man, and told me its name was Bear, because that was the name that man had given it, and until then, it had no need of names for they were not important in its world. Below its mask of rage there was peace and in its eyes that of sadness at being discovered and torn from its land of serenity and of bear-like things and dragged into our world with our fears and hates to forever bind it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was afraid, for it was telling me this, but through its eyes and it was as though I had known it somewhere before. it then slowly dissolved before me, much like the Cheshire cat, until its eyes burnt a hole in the air and there was nothing but blackness and me. small and standing in the dusk in a dirty white cotton dress.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fade to black. wishing for a life that held new wonders, wishing for a piece of that old world where there was so much to find and be inspired by. where tales of distant lands and mythical beasts would fuel the dreams of the children. Magic is dying. Hope is dying. Love is dying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Awaken</p>\r\n'),(3445,'<P> \r\nI puked in an elevator today. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI stepped into the elevator at the Daewoo apartments  building 102 on the 19th floor. I got to about the 17th floor and the elevator began to fill up with a thick white fog. at first i thought it was smoke and that I was going down into a fire. I began to panic and sweat. I began to think the worst. I began to frantically press buttons on the wall of the elevator I could not see. It did not matter. Nothging changed. The fog consumed me and the interior of the elevator. It was not smoke. It was mosquito and cock roach killer. I knew they sprayed it on the streets but had no idea they sprayed it ino the buildings. I inhaled it for a good ten minutes. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI puked in the elevator. my eyes were watering and red. i coughed and swore. I tried to look at my hands ,but I could not see them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI ran out of the building from the first floor into the blinding light of day and breathed a sigh of relief. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nI hate everything right now.\r\n</p>'),(3446,'<p>if you are ever bored or depressed, there\\\'s a place in auckland, new zealand you can go for an endless source of entertainment. yes, hoyts village cinemas (or is it it village hoyts, oh who cares) on upper queen street in auckland can provide you with hours of fun and frivolity.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>no, not the films they screen, but the staff behind the counter, whose tacky little waistcoats belie the awesome intellect that lurks beneath. even the shortest of conversations can provide one with literally days of amusement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: hello friendly brain-surgeon in a tacky waistcoat, could you please tell me if the 8.50 session of \\\"confessions of a dangerous mind\\\" is allocated seating?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: yes. ahhh, errr... no it isn... oh wait, yes, no, ummmm..... yesssssssss, no it isn\\\'t.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(slight pause)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: that was a no, right?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: yes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: would it be possible to reserve two seats?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: sure.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: oh great. could i please have two adults, centre seats, four rows from the front?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(slightly longer pause)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: is that okay?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: you can\\\'t actually pick seats.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: i thought you said i could reserve tickets now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: yes, you can buy tickets now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: buy them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: yes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: but not reserve the seats.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: not as such, no.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(sigh)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: right.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(a look of pure devious cunning comes across his face and he leans in as if to impart a great trade secret, hitherto unknown to the movie-going masses)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: if you get here earlier, you can pick your seats when you sit down.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(longer pause - then the evil in me couldn\\\'t resist...)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: fabulous. is that new?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BS: umm, errr.... i think, maybe... wait i can ask the boss...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(moves as if to walk away)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ME: no! no! that\\\'s quite okay. i\\\'ll just try and arrive early and \\\"pick our seats when we sit down....\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(we exchange the filmic equivalent of a freemason\\\'s handshake as i leave)</p>'),(3447,'My dicks burn\\\'in and she\\\'s not satisfied,<br>\r\nI can\\\'t explain it\\\'s like a fire inside.<br><br>\r\nI go down south to spit on the fire,<br>\r\nThe tongue tries to tame it but I\\\'m startin\\\' to tire<br><br>\r\nTo break the stereotype I didn\\\'t start this,<br>\r\nIn addition to the burnin\\\' I gotta take a piss.<br><br>\r\nSo many rules they should make a manual,<br>\r\nThrow a training seminar it could be annual.<br><br>\r\nIf she can\\\'t even get there on her own,<br>\r\nHow does she expect me to hit her home?<br><br>\r\nThe journey should be one we take together,<br>\r\nNot one were I\\\'m workin\\\' forever.<br><br>\r\nThis has happened too many times,<br>\r\nIt\\\'s about as pleasurable as payin\\\' fines.<br><br>\r\nOh what\\\'s this? she\\\'s startin\\\' to stir, <br>\r\nCould it be? She\\\'s beginnin\\\' to purr.<br><br>\r\nOff she goes soundin\\\' like chimes,<br>\r\nThe question is now Do I get mine?<br><br> '),(3448,'<p>\r\nAlana. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI woke up today to find some hate mail in my inbox and when I checked out what I had said the other night while on a bender I really couldnt believe I had said it. I am a fucktard. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was selfish in belittling your loss. It was a terrible thing for me to do. I had no right to barge in and run my mouth like that. I forget all to often that pets ARE part of the family, and that just because they are smaller doesnt make them any less important.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am reminded of all the pets I have met over the years and even the ones I have lost myself. I am so sorry. I really am. I hope tinker, wherever he/she is can forgive me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI really am truely sorry. Please believe me. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDave. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3449,''),(3450,'I wish that I could be like a greyhound, who runs to the end of the track in the quest for the electronic rabbit.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nRather, I am the turtle with some obscure congenital growth that results in a whole lot of plodding, but not much else.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOh pity me dear reader, for I am a girl of a million and one excuses and distractions, not to mention, more than my fair share of \\\'je ne sais quoi.\\\'\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nEach day when I wake, my quest is crystal clear. And each day when I drift off, my  mind fixes on the fresh beginning that will be tomorrow.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nMy word, how I suffer from this human condition.'),(3451,'<P>\r\nIt is not that I don\\\'t desire some form of relationship.  In fact, the past few months have been, quite possibly, the most satisfying in every aspect of my life.  I finally have the financial independence I\\\'ve craved, the solitude of a one-person apartment, the respect of my request for distance, and a man who seems to have no wish but to very, very nice to me.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s not him that I don\\\'t love, so much as the co-dependent twit of sorts that I feel I now am.  I pine during nights away from him.  When he realized that his brother had lied to him about our dating years ago and took hours to sort out how he felt about that, first a walk and then a nap; I cleaned, thoughts racing through my head of how I could deal with him not wanting to kiss me anymore, lamenting ever being attracted to his brother in the first place.  But wasn\\\'t that how we met?  The only reason my present love and I had spent any amount of time together was that his brother and I had become best friends that happened to have slept together some years ago.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI find myself regretting the slut I used to be.  It had never bothered me till now.  I wanted every ounce of sex I had.  I used them as much as they used me.  I liked the drunk abandon of it all.  And most of all I liked not giving a rat\\\'s ass.  Till I thought maybe that it might bother him, that he might think less of me.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnd it bothers me that I regret what I used to take pride in, mostly because should that fantastic boy not care about me so much I still wouldn\\\'t care, consider my sexual history (and my incredible luck in the health department) a track record rather than something to be ashamed of.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHe knows.  \\\"Just because it happened doesn\\\'t mean I have to like it.\\\"  Once he asked how many, and I could only bury my head in his chest and tell him that I lost count somewhere in my sophmore year.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI wonder what he thinks about all of this hullaballoo that no longer happens.  He is fully aware in what aspects he\\\'s changed my life.  What, in my head, I ran through to get myself to this point, to let myself love him so completely, to allow myself to wonder if this is what they keep talking about.  Is this the stuff that lifetimes of love are made of?  Will this last longer than a year even?  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"I think I want to marry Aaron Sorkin.\\\"\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"The show\\\'s not that good.\\\"\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"Yes!  It is!  You\\\'ve just never watched an entire episode.  When I get the DVD of the first season you\\\'ll see.\\\"\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"You don\\\'t really want to marry Aaron Sorkin do you?\\\"\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"...Can we still have a doughnut wedding cake?\\\"\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"Ice creame filled doughnuts.\\\"\r\n\r\n</P>'),(3452,'<P>The morning itself had gone unseen again for a week, hidden by the roof of an old yellow bus. The sun rose and the sky, it blossomed and bloomed behind it in silence. The children were sleeping and it would be inconsiderate to yell then of its coming. \r\n</P><P>\r\nBeing asleep, the children dreamed. They remembered being rocked by clouds before birth and the distinct echoing of voices there. Tracy, she dreamed of being touched all over by soft plump hands that laughed for her; this laughing though, made her pull away from them, although she wished to hold them near her forever. She wanted to kiss one of them in the center of its palm, wanted to make it tickle, make it blush. The very thought of it made her heavy lidded.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"But boys are so scary.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nAbout her fingers: they were long and so almond shaped that they looked so very much like cinnamon candy placed in a dish. They twirled, with great dexterity, shimmering pieces of gum artificially flavoured to taste of unnatural things. She would, with these fingers, play gently on her lips. Parting them with their tips so that her mouth, moist and red, would open to them softly and envelope them in its warmth.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Did you see it?\\\"\r\n</P>'),(3453,'<P>She told me that she loved me Tuesday morning over coffee and rice. My bowl had been full as the moon and I was admiring this mound of whiteness. She over there, was looking at me pensively and tearing her lips with her front teeth, worrying me because they were so brightly pink that id wanted to do this myself. Then, contemplating these colours of white and brown (our coffee was served always with cream and honey) she told me with such a force in her eyes and trembling all over. It was so sweet, I thought with her necklace trebling, that I kissed her jaw mournfully. Her tears I kissed too, when they ran down her white cheek. For her I smiled and held her jaw before me, pulling her lips to mine, staining them red.\r\n</P>'),(3454,'<p>big shiny supermarket, thou makest me a timorous shadow of myself. see how anxiously i pilot my space  trolley on the shortest route back to the car? see? </p>\r\n<p>i try not to think of the thousands of fingers that have clutched the sticky handle. we have this oblivion, this somewhat romantic mindset that everything we touch in communal places we have got to first. we feel no fear.  you didnt see the fat man before you slip his hand down the front of his corduroys and scratch frantically did you? oh thank god, neither did i.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>WONT SOMEONE THINK OF THE GERMS?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to get it on in the coffee aisle with the stock boy. oh stretch for that top shelf, you mop-haired, cow-eyed smiley thing that i can not investigate. my perfect lover would smell like coffee all the time. he would sweat latte. he would sneeze mocha and his tears would be as the finest espresso liqueur. he would glow dark amber in the sunlight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>of course there are pregnant staggering women. heaving under the weight of fresh children, selecting stale vegetables. they\\\'ve both seen better days. i always imagine them birthing onto the overpolished floors in one determined gush. ( *cracklespeaker* CLEAN UP IN AISLE THREE! ) born into a world of bright lights and silly uniforms. seems there\\\'s no escaping that. no wonder we\\\'re all messed up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>by aisle 5 i\\\'m losing it. my polite acquiesces (as rabid shopper after rabid shopper runs into the back of my ankle/takes my trolley accidentally/takes the last can of beans from in \r\nfront of my hovering hand) are wearing thin. smile fading. jaded grimace setting in. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I WILL NOT BE POLITE AND YOU CAN\\\'T MAKE ME</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the \\\'essential looped hits for sanity destruction\\\' collection squeals out in mono over \r\ninvisible speakers. in the three years ive shopped here, belinda carlisle is always waiting \r\nfor me. with her circles. and her sand. always. id cry if i wasnt busy examining the eggs \r\nfor minute flaws and squeezing the breads.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when i was six i peed in the back of the car because my gran was taking so long with her shopping, and i was too scared to get out and use the supermarket toilet. this neurosis has \r\nbeen with me a lifetime, so i guess theres no shaking it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ps. online grocery shopping #1</p>\r\n<p>pps. also, the small local organic food shop owned by the terminally happy chinese lady. she glows, you know.</p>'),(3455,'<P>\r\nI am browsing this Craigslist.org forum Casual Encounters when I come across this odd add: \r\n</P><BLockquote>\r\n<P>\r\n<B>Title: Blame it on Cain - w4m - 27 </B>\r\n</P><P>\r\nDon\\\'t blame it on me... \r\n</P><P>\r\nIt\\\'s nobody\\\'s fault, but we need somebody to burn. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nSigh. Boy who gets this, gets me...all of me. \r\n</P>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<P>\r\nAnd I start thinking. People bring this burning upon themselves, because judgment is engaged by the individual, and the outcome is the burning. \r\n</P><P>\r\nCain was expelled for killing his brother and punished as an outcast for life. \r\nLiterally, I donâ€™t think we need people to kill their brothers. Figuratively, I do think we need people killing other people. Take Arabs versus Israelis; that hot man on man action fills the news papers and people love to talk about it. It is like putting the lions on the Christians. Damn fun! \r\n</P><P>\r\nIn a more general sense, as a society, I was walking down the street yesterday night and thinking that we donâ€™t need social outcasts. Social outcasts are not a goal of our society. Social outcast are a by product of having an ideal (which we may or may not have to have). People are conditioned to take their position in society early on just by what they receive from others in terms of adulation. I was thinking of it in terms of Pavlovian conditioning. For every beautiful person that gets a compliment, there are dozens who drop in desirability. Each party fulfills their role better, acts more the part, as the Beautiful or the Undesirable depending on treatment by people that surround them. And for those of us that offer compliments, we enjoy being lower, appreciate things that are perceived as more beautiful, though in some cases by elevating others one can gain some elevation himself. \r\n</P><P>\r\nMaybe we donâ€™t need to burn anyone, we just need to burn ourselves. Perhaps the real casualty in this is the Beautiful who burn themselves to be more beautiful. Two come to mind: People who develop eating disorders, plastic surgery disasters.\r\nI think you could turn that around anyway you wanted it. You could replace the Beautiful with the Ugly, Rich, or Poor. Any direction that someone makes a judgment about someone else, the result is burning brought upon himself. \r\n</P>\r\n'),(3456,''),(3457,'<p>\r\ni wish i were you parohn.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nyour hot boy/girl friend. the way she/he loves you. so many of us do not have that...and yet you take it for granted.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni just live. i just wake up and goto my classes. I goto the apartment blocks and make my money. i goto the school and make my salary. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe one i loved dumped me and it has turned me sour and angry...to the point where old friends dont respect me anymore. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto the point where people who once talked to me dont talk to me anymore. to the point where i do not understand. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy life is in disarray. my life is in a slump. my life is like so many out there...not unique, but not happy. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow many of you are unhappy with your jobs?\r\nmy job is what keeps me sane. its the outside world that drives me to the wine and depression. its the outside world that keeps me on that shit and its the world i once knew that makes me crave things i once had. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni know some of you are angry at me. i know some of you are questioning my motives and actions, but you must believe that they are the actions and motives of a very sad man. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou all know what sadness is. i dont have to spell it out to you in a metaphore or lovely poem. i know you know what it is in your own words. I know you know what it is with regards to your own worlds. its shit and it must stop. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim constantly put on hold because of french toast, juicing machines, and early mornings.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nits painful, but its reality\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni long for the life i once had. i long for the life you have. i long for a life. i am in pain.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo you understand?\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3458,'the shower has always been a special place to me, even when i was a little kid. when i was 8 or so, i used to pretend that if i hit the right combination of shower wall tiles, secret compartments would open up. sometimes i would pretend that little tv screens were in there too, and i could dial up my friends in their showers and we could make secret plans. like in a movie. <p>\r\ni don\\\'t really know why being in the shower was so special to me as a kid. i remember especially liking my mother\\\'s shower, because it was a \\\'dedicated\\\' shower, for lack of a better word. it wasn\\\'t a shower-tub combination. it was made of green tiles, floor, walls and ceiling. i would spend large amounts of time spitting water up onto the ceiling of the shower and catching it on my tongue when it fell. drops would run together and fall. right before they did, they caught the light a certain way and you could tell that that drop was an instant away from falling. when they fell off of the light, they were hot. this was endlessly amusing to me. <p>\r\none time, when i was about six years old, i shaved all the hair off of my arms and legs with my mother\\\'s razor. i started with just a little bit off one arm to see what it was like, and it was neat. pretty soon i ran out of hair. my mother was pretty mad about that. it hadn\\\'t occurred to me that anyone would notice that sort of thing, and i don\\\'t think anyone else did. was it during winter? i hope so, because it really <i>would</i> have looked weird, had it been during the summer.<p>\r\ni remembered all this this morning, in the shower. the shower i have now is pretty rad. it is really old, with black and white tiles. it has a tub. it also has a window. it\\\'s really odd, that window right there in the shower. it is just an ordinary window like all the others in the house, with those ugly off-white cheap blinds. the management has thoughtfully put a second, much shorter shower curtain bar up across the window frame, and supplied a \\\'custom\\\' cut-off shower curtain to cover the window.  <p> when i was a kid i used to try to sneak all kinds of things into the shower. i used to read in there, play handheld video games, sneak in food--the shower was my own personal space, inviolable. the shower was where i learned to mark the passing of time. each time you step into a shower, you know it is a new day. every new day, you remember that you had that thought before, and you can get a glimpse of how much time has gone by. for example, since i shaved my hair off, i have gotten into the shower about seven thousand three hundred times. if you count one number per second, continuously, that is about five days of counting. <p>\r\nsuch a long time ago! and yet, what has really changed since then? still, every morning, i turn on the hot water, then the cold. i wait for the water to get to the right temperature, checking with my hand. i make a seal with the curtain. nowadays, i look out into the parking lot. <p>\r\none day, i will have secret compartments in my shower, with a tv screen and a phone. \r\n'),(3459,'<P><i>I know this is a departure from the site into BBS format and everything but this is important.</i></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Is anyone doing Nanowrimo this year?  I\\\'m thinking about it.  Possible genres:\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>erotic action\r\n<li>spy action\r\n<li>strait up romance novel\r\n</UL>\r\n\r\n<P>What are you thinking about writing?'),(3460,'<p>\r\ni can hear the people upstairs from me fighting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit is sad because I can also hear their children crying.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nits reminds me of my first memory.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe only memory of living with both my parents is of them fighting. My brother and I were sitting on a couch and I was crying and my brother was hugging me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI remember only my dad pacing back and forth yelling at my mom who stood with her arms folded. thinking back im sure she was justified.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nlast night i had a dream my arm caught on fire. I kept putting it out and it kept bursting back into flames. I finally just took the shirt off and put it in the shower and soaked it in water. \r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nmy arm caught on fire. \r\n</p>'),(3461,'<p>\r\ni woke up today and laid in bed. I thought about getting up and woke up again after continuing the dream i had been having. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe dream took me all over the world. I can remember shooting people in the Vietnam war. we were all lined up on a small ridge waiting for them to come from the jungle. they did and we began firing. I could feel the bullets hitting them and then I fled down the road looking for more to kill, but i found myself in a busy bangkok market. I was walking down a street and a person i once knew in university was standing in front of me and said \\\"those cigarettes are fifty cents each\\\" and i walked past and said \\\"and i am smoking two of them\\\" and people around us laughed. I kept walking and the people I had been following dissappeared. i continued in the direction I thought they went. I saw them dissappear on the bank of a river. I looked where they were going and it was in a tunnel. I didn\\\'t know if i\\\'d fit in the hole but i did, and kept down the tunnel. It led into a wonderful house filled with many rooms, staircases, and tunnels. I looked for them but they were no where to be seen. At one point three enemy troops came around the corner and I was forced to shoot two of them and stab the other in the face. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npanting, i sat on a bail of hay. I looked at a picture of my exgf that hung from a locket around my neck and shivered. it was cold in this strange house. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni looked for an exit but all the windows were boarded up and I could not find any doors leading outside. I tried going back to the tunnel, but marines kept coming in through it, and there was no way I could get back. They kept telling me \\\"why go back, this way is the fight\\\". I had nothing to say to them. I felt trapped. I was trapped. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthere was a terrible pain in my head when I woke up. I was covered in sweat and I felt more tired than before I went to bed. I need help. I truely need help. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3462,'<p>\r\ni shit my pants a few minutes ago and i dont care. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhy should i? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nim sitting here in my own shit just staring at a computer screen. i feel like a deflated party. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI just wake up and things are fine and then i work and then i drink.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit is a routine i just cant shake these days.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe truth? the truth is im hurting inside and out. the truth is, i cannot begin to control the feelings inside me and I just explode with anger at times. my kids all notice it. when they are copying things down i find myself cupping my face with my hands just waiting for something to happen. who gives a fuck if they are speaking korean in class. I dont. why the fuck are they learning english anyway? most of them wont even leave korea. what the fuck use is it? status. their parents think that if they learn english they are worth more. worth more to who? the family? fuck it. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI just walk around. I just look at them and then when they turn towards me I look past them and at something else as to not let them know I am looking. They are no fools. They know. Do they feel sympathy? hell no. they have their own problems. they have their own debt. they have their own lonliness and anguish. They have their own tech pop electoclash to contend with. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI go out on saturday night and get loaded looking for a fight. I dont find fights, but I look for them in my secret world of angst and supressed horny afterglow. I relieve those addictions with downloaded race queen videos and futurama episodes. I just quell the demons, but do not put them to rest. what am i to do? my actual friends are scattered and none of them live within a reasonable flight distance away. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe ones that used to talk to me dont anymore. They think i have lost it and they are right. they feel no need to question or antagonize me. they just let it be and let me idle and shame myself within the world i have created for myself out of pure lonlieness and guilt. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\neverything goes the way it does, and for me that direction is down. I live in hell, and I suppose i deserve it, but do i really? \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI just want to be loved. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3463,'I was coming home from a night out with friends and I witnessed a man beating up a girl. I stood there after he finished and waited. He hit her again and I pounced. I ran at him and kicked him a few times in the chest and balls. He went down but his friends pulled me back. They came at me with umbrella poles and I pulled a bottle out of a near by crate and hit one of them in the head stopping him cold in his tracks. the rest of them stopped and I walked forward. The boyfriend came at me again with a frying pan and hit me in the back of the head. good thing I was drunk. I swung around and smashed him in  the face with an empty beer bottle sending him to the floor with a bloody face. I spit on him. His girl friend seeing him on his ass came at me and I pushed her away. She was angry at me, yet he was the one beating her. I left when i heard the sirens. I cant understand this place. I really cant. my head hurts from the frying pan. there was broken glass in my hair when I got home. I took a shower and now im going to bed. I\\\'m just so angry. \r\n</p>\r\n<Pre>\r\n:sheneman: sounds like a cultural misunderstanding\r\n:chiam: no\r\n:chiam: seeing a womean get beat up is not cultural.\r\n:chiam: i dont give a fuck\r\n:chiam: maybe barbarian misunderstanding\r\n:chiam: but not cultural\r\n:sheneman: haha\r\n:chiam: no one deserves that\r\n:chiam: i dont give a fuck where you live\r\n:sheneman: yeah, true\r\n</PRE>'),(3464,'<p>An hour later, when the cocktail waitress shows up for the busy part of the night, he walks out of the bar. Sarah is leaning against the bus stop in the drizzle, the glow of colored lights dancing off beads of precipitation like a magic show in her hair. Her lips lift up as she sighs his name, â€œSantiago,â€ but the smile does not reach her eyes. He stops in front of her taking shallow breaths. He looks down at the toe of her shoe and says, â€œHi,â€ then takes her left index finger in his right hand and delicately pinches the first knuckle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They walk together, her finger trapped in his hand, turning right when they reach the corner. They walk passed dark shops, the muted flash of televisions sputtering out at them between the slats of a dozen shuttered windows. She lets him pull her closer until they are walking almost on top of one another, and then he pulls her into the doorway of a Laundromat, his wallet chain clacking against the aluminum frame of the mail slot. The thick mist hides them like a curtain as he buries his face in her neck.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When they finish she leans against him, her head resting against the glass of the door. He lights a cigarette and they pass it back and forth, its tip glowing red as the edge of paper turns to ash. â€œI knew you would come.â€ He says, and then looks at her. He feels an overwhelming sense of relief and looks up at the fog where the stars would be if he could see them. â€œThank you, thank you, thank you.â€ he says silently to himself, disgusted by his sentiment. â€œDid you?â€ She asks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He hugs her closer and resists when she shifts her weight to stand on her own. â€œWe go back to the bar now, yes?â€ She says. He sighs and puts his arms around her waist, steering her back to the sidewalk. When they reach the door of the bar she stops. She is not going to follow him inside. â€œYou wont stay?â€ He is surprised. â€œNo.â€ He frowns and looks into her eyes for a sign of what she wants. He sees nothing but the smudge of mascara. Her eyes are locked doors. In one a tear wells up and refuses to fall. His heart stops. She looks away. â€œWell tomorrow weâ€™llâ€¦â€ She puts her hand on his mouth. â€œNo.â€ She says and shakes her head slowly. She backs up toward the curb with her arm out still blocking his mouth. With her other hand she waves a cab to the curb. She turns around and opens the door. He feels like heâ€™s in a movie. His stomach rolls and settles in his boots. She doesnâ€™t turn around as the cab pulls away, and he doesnâ€™t look up to see if she has. Santiago turns around, counts to ten, and plunges himself back into the bar.<p>\r\n'),(3465,'<P>There was this 4\\\"8\\\' guy that looked like a power lifter at IN-N-OUT Burger.\r\n<BR>He had sunglasses on inside the place to accent his intense meanness.\r\n<BR>I was thinking, \\\"Man I hope that guy doesn\\\'t sit next to me and while I am sitting at the bar thing, waiting on my food.\\\"\r\n<BR>He sits down next to me.\r\n<BR>Sets his drink down and it falls over.\r\n<BR>I\\\'m like, shit is about to hit the fan\r\n<BR>But no, he contains the anger.\r\n<BR>Rights his drink.\r\n<BR>Opens the wrapper on his burger  and yells\r\n<BR><B>\\\"SHIT!\\\"</B>\r\n<BR>And pounds the table, drink falls over.\r\n<BR>Picks the drink up and sets it on the tray to take it back to the counter and return it.\r\n<BR>As he is walking over, drink falls over again and rolls off his tray onto the floor.\r\n<BR>I thought he was going to explode!\r\n<BR>His face was all red.\r\n<BR>I think it was a shake, not a drink, so it didn\\\'t exactly spill\r\nout or anything like that, instead a thud.\r\n<BR>But the bottom was crushed at an angle and wouldn\\\'t sit.\r\n<BR>He spend the next 15 seconds trying to get the shake to stand up while he is saying something to the register girl.\r\n<BR>People are everywhere, it is lunchtime chaos, and then I hear him yell, \\\"I SAID I DON\\\'T WANT ONIONS ON MY ANIMAL!\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3466,'I remember the first time that I meet you. It was your smell that put me off. Something unfamiliar, slightly spicy, and oh-so-pungent.\r\n\r\nYou didn\\\'t seem to care about this stench, as you were hanging with the cool kids.\r\n\r\nIt was only a matter of time, and you and I were starting to see a reasonable amount of each other. The cool kids didn\\\'t seem to mind about this, in fact, they welcomed the intrusion.\r\n\r\nI flirted with you so much at one stage, that you were almost mine right then. But we resisted each other, again and again, only ever being \\\"acquaintances.\\\"\r\n\r\nBut that soon changed.\r\n\r\nIt got to the stage where you were my first thing in the morning, and my last thing at night. You were often the highlight of my weekend, though I never told my co-workers on monday mornings.\r\n\r\nWe would go places together, and we would more often than not, be unwelcome. Was it your pungency? Or was it my obsession?\r\n\r\nWe became alienated by society, and slipped into a state of reclusion together.\r\n\r\nThose were golden times my friend, and I thank you for those.\r\n\r\nBut now it is time for me to say goodbye. I now have a reason to not see you anymore, and for once it makes sense to me. I can hardly believe it myself sometimes.\r\n\r\nWho would have ever thought that I could exist without you?\r\n\r\nGoodbye.'),(3467,'Sometimes I wish I had the genius gene that you have Dad, then I could write poems that stick in the minds of people forever.\r\n\r\nLike when I was 12.\r\n\r\nSimply\r\nThe\r\nAbsolute\r\nLength\r\nAnd\r\nCircumference\r\nThat\r\nIntrudes\r\nTowards\r\nEarth.\r\n\r\nHow does it feel to epitomise the phrase \\\"son of a bitch?\\\"'),(3468,'<p>Drink up baby, I\\\'ll listen tonight<BR>\r\nalone in a chair bathed in blue airport light.<BR>\r\nNo need to talk or explain how it was<BR>\r\nto hate your own life more than anyone does.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Strum your guitar to words burnt and gray,<BR>\r\nthe loss of your loves, the pressure of days,<BR>\r\nand maybe that morning, after one final glass<BR>\r\nof Johnnie Walker Red, you gazed at your last</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Los Angeles sky, the sun warm and white,<BR>\r\nand saw there was nothing, no will or might<BR>\r\nleft in you this year. Your sad eyes shut,<BR>\r\npraying to rise once the strings were cut.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I sway to the swoon of your melodies,<BR>\r\nthe timbre of your sorrows so pretty<BR>\r\nsliding into my ears as I board a plane out of<BR>\r\ntown, to chase down a fresh hope for love.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\\\'s anguish enough to go around<BR>\r\nand some good men are damnably bound<BR>\r\nto be heaped with more than their share,<BR>\r\nto drown disappointments and try not to care</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that just being alive in a pliant brain,<BR>\r\nbent toward the ground, is hard to sustain<BR>\r\nunless love survives and the burdens are light.<BR>\r\nYou were not wrong and you were not right.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Drink up with me now, and we\\\'ll both get high,<BR>\r\nstare up at the sky, and I\\\'ll never try<BR>\r\nto doubt your reasons or assign any blame<BR>\r\njust \\\'cause you chose to forfeit the game.</p>\r\n \r\n'),(3469,'<p>\r\nI long for the\r\n<a href=\\\"http://elly.org/this/older/002705.html\\\">\r\n\\\"world only kissers can enter\\\"</a>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt has been a long time. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s just me and my Sansachun. Wine made from the wonderful Sansa. The fruit bearing broad leafed plant belonging to the rose species. It is red and has a pleasant scent. According to the bottle it is good for treating weak stomachs, backaches, cuts, and with 13% alcohol; quite good for depression.   \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nOn November ninth my sexy exgf leaves for Italy to begin an educational adventure. I think about her a lot still. She was such a big part of my life at one point, and I have yet to get over it. I am over her as a person, but I have yet to fill that hole that was created by us parting ways.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs cool as it is being away from home living and working in a far off land it is hard. The friends you make at the beginning inevitably go back, or move onto other places. As far as other expats go, going to the clubs and spots resembles a high school cafeteria, with cliques and already established friendship circles. I find it difficult making friends here. I find it even more difficult making korean friends here, though I have recently hung out with one of my adult students. He is a cool guy, but he is married with children. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI see so much beauty on my walks during the day. I see so much beauty from the bus and subway windows. I cannot communicate. I dont know what to say.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI realized yesterday that the old man I buy my Sansa from felt sorry for me. I had tears in my eyes on my short walk home. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3470,'<P>\r\nI bought my Laramy Jeans in Grade 11.  That was almost seven years ago, and these jeans have been everywhere Iâ€™ve been in that time.  Not that I wore them everyday- but most adventures were undertaken with the beautifully worn denim caressing my butt.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWe scaled steep coastal mountains, relaxed into sun warmed sand, and retuned to civilization muddy but intact.  We crammed for final exams during university, slouched in the smoky res cafÃ© until three a.m.. We attended poetry readings and visited art galleries.  We slid into greasy spoon diner booths after long nights of drinking.  We danced all night, and sometimes all morning long- seemly inseparable to the naked eye.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nThrough thick and thin, through good times and bad, my Laramyâ€™s were there for me.  The denim relaxing and fading with age, the cuffs fraying slightly, shrinking up in the wash, relaxing into a perfect fit after a few minutes of wear.  Opening the closet door, they are the proffered arms of an old and true friend.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBut as is the nature of these things, my Laramyâ€™s are beginning to show their age.  Still possessing a careworn beauty, the rich dark blue has faded almost white where seams rub together.  There is a small but steadily advancing army of holes, in the crotch, near the fly, along the outside seams.  And the cuffs have frayed to the point where they could be mistaken for the pants of some grubby kid- the kind whose cuffs follow them like a forlorn and ragged animal through the street.  Ah, yes my dearest. The end is nigh.\r\n</P><P>\r\nYet, after such an intensely satisfying relationship, I canâ€™t just abandon my Laramyâ€™s to the whims of an unknown fate.  No Goodwill store, or back-of-the-closet treatment for these denim gems.  Iâ€™m going to frame them, and hang them on my wall.  Theyâ€™ll be with me forever, a worn blue reminder of a successfully mischievous youth.  \r\n</P>'),(3471,'<p>...unless, of course, you throw in something ridiculous like \\\"But what if you\\\'re lying on a bed of jellyfish?\\\"  That\\\'s just silly.  For the sake of argument, let\\\'s just say that we\\\'re in a padded room...wall-papered with Victoria\\\'s Secret catalog pages.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now I\\\'ve never actually been in this state.  I ate bacon this weekend, and was at some point not wearing any clothing, but not simultaneously.  Why I didn\\\'t think of it at the time?  I\\\'m apparently not as quick-thinking as I think.  As I\\\'ve never experienced the two at the same time, let\\\'s look at each individual case.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Naked:  State of mind, my ass!  Take your clothes off sometime and kick it in front of the tube watchin\\\' old school Buffy.  Enter bag of Twizzlers and soft kitten purring at your side.  Better yet, take your favorite thing to do: hiking, drinking, driving.  Now throw a little naked in there.  That, my friends, is what life is intended to be.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bacon:  Do I really need to explain this one?  I can see explaining naked.  Not everyone in the world is comfortable with that one.  Unless you\\\'re among one of the religions that frown upon eating pork products, take everything good in the world (peace, teddy bears, Victoria\\\'s Secret models) and put it into a food.  Bacon.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Together?  You\\\'ve got it made.  You\\\'ve either got bacon making the naked that much better, or the naked making the bacon that much better.  Either way, you\\\'re better off.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Go make it a naked bacon day.</p>'),(3472,'The relationship has left me with an emotional hangover. The type you get when you drink to many of those sweet cooler drinks and wake up with a shish kebab skewer nailing your head to the pillow. The memories are still so sickeningly sweet. The process of emotional deprogramming has commenced, hurrah!<br><br>\r\n\r\nMaybe it was because she was six years younger in her early twenties, maybe it was an identity clash - she was a little clichÃ©d bohemian Iâ€™m a slightly obtuse curmudgeon. Maybe it wasâ€¦ Maybe it doesnâ€™t matter. Despite what ever the is reason is that I couldnâ€™t continue, she is still a hell of a lot better then most people out there and has a heart aimed in the right direction. She does need to learn not to hit though - I could see this causing a bit of a problem if she ever dates someone that is a little less tolerant then myself.<br><br> \r\n\r\nDamn she had a sweet presence though. Wish it had worked.'),(3473,'<p>\r\ni take an interest. i wonder how things are where she is. her new adventure. an adventure that was a subject of much of what we talked about when we were a thing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni saw her one night while i was sitting on the computer. she replied to my query with an imature \\\"fuck you\\\", and i felt kinda shocked inside but didnt let her know it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe cant make me hate her. she cant make me think less of her as a person. I think she is special. Maybe not to me...anymore, but at least she will be to someone someday. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe told me once, that she broke up with all thirty of her ex boyfriends by being really mean and rude to them. I will not fall for that trap. I will not lash back. I will not let her get under my skin like that. she is still something that I think about day in and day out. Not as often as I did when we were \\\"friends of a friend\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nmy roommate really digs his girlfriend. she gave him a talk tonight about how she couldnt see him anymore because her parents had set her up with some korean man. She had gone on a whole bunch of \\\"blind dates\\\" with men her parents had set her up with. He told me of how she could not see him anymore because of this. THe last guy she went on a blind date with liked her, and so her parents were all giddy and excited...even though she didnt like him. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nthe world is fucked up and i have become less and less caring towards custom and tradition. I think there is always room for change and I just cant see how what was once good for a set of parents is good for their kids. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI feel as though I was pushed aside for reasons unknown and having nothing to do with any action I might have done, partaken in, or been responsible for. I think I was just cast aside because something \\\"better\\\" was on the horizon. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ndid she rattle me? did she put me down? sure she did. But am I going to let her know she did? no. I cant do that. I cant let her think she achieved anything. Is that imature? sure it is. I know I know nothing about relationships. I know I know nothing about life. I know i Know nothing about the way the shit works over here. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhell is where you make money. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3474,'\\\'time-line it,\\\' says professor boyle, esteemed of criminal law, \\\'at what point was there preparation? at what point did this become an attempt? when she got in the car? when she hid behind the bush? when she pointed the gun? when she put her finger on the trigger?\\\'</p><p>\r\nno one in class knows the answer to his question, as likely neither does he really, a tax attorney for some number of years reading about three pages ahead of the class in the criminal casebook, his second year teaching the subject. both of us sweating as surely mrs. shirley smith was, there behind the bush gun in hand, preparing to shoot her husband, waiting for him to come out of <i>her</i> house, that <i>woman\\\'s</i> house where he was, no doubt, sweating, in the heat of darkest july, fifties electric fan oscillating left to right and back again, loud as a jet engine in the room, covering the sounds up. sweating and sliding in and out of <i>that woman</i> like a mechanical device, raining saltwater down on her, her own personal thunderhead raging as she could do little more than watch. she was rather immobilized. mrs. shirley smith too sat very still, sweating onto the grip of her husband\\\'s thirty-two caliber pistol, not a lot of knock-down power as they say but pretty much fatal nonetheless. the front of her fifties blouse begain to soak through. he came out of the door. the storm was apparently over. shirley stepped out from behind the bush, her knees popping, stomach boiling, legs shaking, fighting now not to kill her husband, only to stand up, to raise the gun. she got it about halfway up before he, veteran of gun wars as he was, took it from her without really thinking about it. \r\n</p><p>\r\nwell, mr. lee? which is it?</p><p>\r\npreparation, or attempt?</p>\r\n'),(3475,'Mark was my partner in Latin class, and by the time senior year of high-school rolled around, he had been for four years.  No one liked Mark. He was obsessive compulsive. He was somehow a subtlely pompous asshole while being very socially naive. He was also an utter genius.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhich was why I chose him for my partner in Latin. We made straight A\\\'s term after term. Mark translated our assignments with the passion of a paranoid pefectionist while I took special pains to slightly move each item on his desk from where he had painstakingly placed it at the class\\\' start, each time he turned around or looked away. His watch, his pen and pencil. I liked to turn pages in his notebook.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMark was our high school valedictorian.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSenior year, rumors began spreading that Mark was a little odder than we had thought. Rumors about what was going on during the long hours he spent, cutting class and hanging out in the shrub-lined senior parking lot, rumors that could destroy you at an all-boy\\\'s school. They said Mark, well, liked <i>the cars</i>. That, you know, he had a little quirk.<br><br>\r\n\r\nApparently, he fucked them every which way but right.  Pipes and grilles, bumpers, the works, selecting only the best bitches from the rows of luxury autos that most of the overpriviledged prep-school boys drove.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMark gained alot of popularity from these rumors; the brilliant badass quirk who scandalized the parking lots during lunch. He came out of his shell, he became much more sociable. Mark was actually getting cool.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOnce, he told me about a special Audi twin-turbo that he had explicit passion for, saying that although he loved her sinuous curves and lines, it was really her twin-turbo tailpipes that he loved to fuck.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMark gave the most eloquent graduation speech, then headed off to Cornell, where he was to study physics, classics and math.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI dunno, as time passed, I occasionally thought of Mark. I would see him now and then when our schools competed, or at some party.  The more I saw him fitting into his newly created, normal, popular college-guy role, it all sounded pretty sensationally scandalous to me,  that he was sexually attracted to <i>cars</i>; unreal, and just the case of a very sad, insecure teen finding definition and popularity through a string of rumors that was most likely intended to ridicule him.  Smart, just like Mark.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen, I heard he was dismissed from his college after he was witnessed repeatedly molesting fellow student\\\'s cars, and disobeying official warnings to stop. They say he was last seen running into a forrest on the shore of the Cuyahoga, and hasn\\\'t been heard from since.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhich might explain why no word of him ever turns up in our alumni newsletter.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(3476,'<P>\r\ni was supposed to fly to las vegas today, from toronto. i went to the airport.  the shuttle bus drove me through the fog on the city, i listened to sad guitar music.  at customs, i was taken to a separate waiting room because i didn\\\'t have return ticket. it was supposed to be purchased for me after i got there, couldn\\\'t decide on a return date. i told them i\\\'d stay about a week.  i also said i didn\\\'t have a job.  i didn\\\'t say anything about freelancing because i have no way of proving it.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni waited alone, george bush grinned at me from the wall. \r\n</P><P>\r\ninto the interview room. everything is antiseptic colors, generic office menacing in monotone. lots of waiting. i am told that i cannot be allowed to travel into the US because i don\\\'t have a return ticket. and my luggage is too small, just a small backpack and a camera bag, carry-on size. with no full time job here, i\\\'m told i have no reason to return to canada. i\\\'m told i could just slip into the US and disappear, a parasite on the social system. i also didn\\\'t have an exact address of where i was staying, another red flag i am told. i didn\\\'t have one because i was being picked up at the airport. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni was escorted out of the customs area, back into the main departure level. i saved some cash by taking the bus and subway home, an hour, more sad guitar music. i called my aunt\\\'s fiancee, who\\\'s paid for the tickets for me to come meet him, and visit my aunt. he rebooked the flight, with a return.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni fell asleep on the couch because i woke up so early to catch the flight. the phone woke me up, i got off the couch and went back to the airport, just barely catching the shuttle bus thanks to a fast cab ride to the stop. i went back to the airport, had a full itinerary printed including my return flight in five days, and the exact address of my aunt\\\'s home in vegas where i would be staying.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni was quickly pulled aside to the waiting room again. this time it was busy, five people were waiting with a bored nervousness set in their faces. two of them talked, a thirty something and a twenty something, strangers but friendly canadians to each other. the older had a daughter\\\'s birthday he might miss. the younger was going to work in the states. i looked at my watch and told them i\\\'d be missing my flight too.  we laughed and made faces that read: fuck it. one person per half hour was being processed.  through an open office door i watched a military cut immigration official be very serious. i was sure he\\\'d interview me.\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe two friendly canadians got processed, first the older and then the younger, the younger waved a goodbye and a nod to me when he left. i was alone for a few moments, then a russian man walked in. i knew he was russian as soon as he came in, burly, bearded, but stylish in dark clothes with pleated pants. his shoes looked not north american, that weird fashion quality that can only come from other places. they were black leather dress shoes, but with a half pointed half steel toe.  he took out his cell and talked into it with a russian accent. \r\n<BR>- i\\\'m 99.9% sure i won\\\'t make it\r\n<BR>- golf, yes, we\\\'re supposed to meet in beverly hills\r\n<BR>- i will have to go back\r\n</P><P>\r\non the wall across far across the room from my seat was a 9/11 poster. the text read \\\'we will never forget\\\'. the top right corner of the poster was drooping down over the image of the burning towers like negligent modesty. the corner was weighed down by a hunk of stick-tac. \r\n</P><P>\r\na half hour after my flight departed without me, i was called into the same room i had been in before, seven hours earlier. a female officer takes on my case, her name is kurkowski. \r\n</P><P>\r\nkurkowski hits me rapid fire with pointed questions, one after another, overlapping my responses with another question. kurkowski asks me questions that are wrong to gauge my response. i give the same story, saying i\\\'ve tried to correct the problems i understood to have got me denied the first time. she asks me why i\\\'m travelling so light. i tell her i\\\'m going to vegas, i have many t-shirts and shorts and a pair of pants for five days. kurkowski goes through everything in the bag. kurkowski disappears for at least twenty minutes. i pass the time staring at the poster in front of me that informs me everything is being recorded with audio and video. i put on a boring show.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen kurkowski returns, she is even more terse than before. she tells me i am \\\'TOTALLY unfit\\\' for travel in the US. i am told i have no home (i rent), no money, and no job and for this reason i have no business being in the US. i have no reason to go back to canada, i am told. i want to ask why i wouldn\\\'t want to return to where my girlfriend, friends, and family are, where i won\\\'t be shot and if by some chance i am i won\\\'t have to pay for the medical care to heal me. i want to ask why i would go hide in the US to be even more poor with no legal right to work. \r\n</P><P>\r\ninstead, i stare into kurkowski\\\'s eyes as she bleets this information into my face. i hold her stare and suppress a happy smirk.  how good it is to find these intimate moments, when i\\\'m supposed to be feeling bad.  i wonder through her eyes at what happens in her life when she\\\'s not kurkowski. she tells me if i try to gain entry into the US again without 52 weeks\\\' worth of pay stubs and a copy of a rental lease, my belongings will be confiscated, including a car should i happen to be in one.  a long, LONG time, says kurkowski.\r\n</P><P>\r\nagain, i was escorted out to the departure area. immigration was kind enough to autograph my boarding pass with a big black INS in slashing strokes. i showed off my ticket to an agent, and she set about refunding the price to my aunt\\\'s nice fiancee. i hope he gets his money back. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni took the bus again to the subway station. i found the one cigarette i\\\'d brought with me, borrowed a light from two girls who were too young to be smoking.  i smoked it outside under light rain, between two no smoking signs on the bus platform. i walked into the subway station, down the stairs, an asian girl kissed a black boy\\\'s forehead and touched his face.  some sikh men stood facing the subway train that would soon come, small laughs fell into a conversation, we all waited for the train together, and when the doors opened i heard thanks and excuse me, felt a few smiles, put on my headphones and listened to sad guitar music, happy to be back in canada.\r\n</P>'),(3477,'<p>the world today is kind of different from the past. but it\'s really not if you think about it. yesterday we didn\'t know about things happening another place so there was no way for us to have an opinion. today we know but we just don\'t care anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>why is that?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you\'re here you like the written word, otherwise, why do you come here? because of thoughts of people you know, or don\'t know? you sip the essence of their sentences, since they choose to write. it makes you laugth, think, smile, maybe release a tear? but as with anything, it has to come from somewhere. the words have to be put together and made into coherent sentences by someone before they can affect the minds of their readers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just with some basics in life experience it\'s easy to understand the trouble silence cause. and Swinney will soon die, according to statistics. so speak up, about anything. and I know I\'m new here, but I\'m not, I\'ve read Swinney for a long time. and I recently decided to make a move, to contribute. because of austin\'s <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3332\">Dear Swinney</a> and etienne\'s <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3245\">wounds</a> and other.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if you\'re out there like I were, come in here. help to make Swinney prosper again. like always, speak up and tell your opinion, speak up and make new connections, get new friends. there are too many stories untold and too much of silence, everywhere.</p>'),(3478,'I imagine I got the phone call between nine and ten Saturday morning. <br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œCould I please speak to Mr. Vall?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œPardon? Whatâ€¦â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œIs Mr. Vall?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œYou mean Wall?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œYeah, Vallâ€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œSure, Iâ€™m here what can I do ya for? Iâ€™m just waking up.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œVell, my name is Jevanuh and I am calling on behalf of the Vene Real Karma collections agency.  Are you aware that you have some rather massive outstanding karma debts?â€ <br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œWhat!? I have no idea what you are talking about?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œVell, Mr. Vall I assure you that this is quite serious. Did you really think that all your luck vith the ladies vas based on your looks and charming personality? You my friend have been running a karma deficit not too mention your debt and due to the specific nature in vich your karma has been used Vene Real Karma Collections acquired your Karma debt and vill from now on be handling all you Karma transactions.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œPardon Me? Jevanuh, That is what you said your name was right? I am at a bit of a loss here karma collection? You really expect me to believe this sort of thing exists?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œVell actually sir in your case you can believe it or not but your karma is going to be garnished or perhaps even cut off regardless. In an attempt to cut back on bureaucracy The Powers That Be, Inc. have outsourced their collections vork to private firms such as ourselves.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œJevanuh, So what exactly do you want from me? I was sleeping when you called for cryin out load.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œVell, ve vill have to decide on vhat area of life you are villing to make up your karma deficit in. Ve expect a balanced karma vithin one fiscal year. Obviously no further positive karma of any form will be dispensed  by Vene Real until such time as you have shown good faith in your attempts to reform.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œYou didnâ€™t answer my question Jevanuh.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œVell you have options, may I suggest a life long illness of some sort? It is the fastest and easiest way for you to make up your karma debt. One lump sum is taken care of at the onset of the illness and then the balance is extracted over the remainder of your life according to our celestial accounting formula. It vouldnâ€™t be anything too serious. Vhat do you think?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œDoes it mean that I get to go back to sleep and you promise not to call back again?â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œYes.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nâ€œFine letâ€™s do it up, Iâ€™ll take narcolepsy please.â€<br><br> \r\n\r\nClick.    \r\n'),(3479,'Weather makes me think\r\n\r\n\r\nlife is full of missed opportunities....\r\n\r\nconversations we never finish, gestures we forget to make, glances we never meet, promises we never keep \r\n\r\nlost, all lost, to the stealth ticking of time.\r\n\r\nOnward, onward, onward \r\n\r\nit snowed this morning\r\n\r\nwinter reality, at last.\r\n'),(3480,'<P>\r\nThis past week, I have been swept up in a confluence of events involving strangers.  I continue to sift the ashes of my memory for deeper meaning...  \r\n</P><P>\r\nEvent number one was perpetrated by a 20-something Goth chick with a bad attitude. She soaked me with the entire contents of her beer one evening, as I stood minding my own business at the bar.  Angrily, I asked her what in the hell she was doing, but she merely turned without a word, and walked away. Later she came over and drunkenly explained that she was aiming for her friend, but had not felt any obligation to clarify the situation. Then she wanted to fight me after I refused both her justification, and the cheers she offered me.  Should I have merely acquiesced and clinked bottles with her? The beer, stiffening my hair as it dried, warned me against such charitable gestures. We looked at each other long and hard, but no comprehension passed between us.  Then she walked away.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEvent number two involved some bad timing and some bad luck.  Running late to meet a friend, I hopped on the streetcar, only to have it rerouted due to an accident. Off I went, and into a cab, whose driver remarked cheerfully on the weather- very cold and rainy- as I stared out the window, willing the lights to change faster.  Sometimes this city really gets under your skin.  Then as I got out, I broadsided a bicyclist with the cab door, sending her flying about four feet. She landed on her back, on the sidewalk, and stared up at me in shock and reproach.  As I usually ride my bike- this rainy day being an exception- I knew I had committed a cardinal sin in the biking world.  I did not look before I opened the door.  There followed much commotion, as the cab driver weighed in on my side (She didnâ€™t even see you!), other bikers rushed to aid their fallen fellow (This happens at least once a week in this city. Damn drivers!), and people passing by gathered around to indulge in their ubiquitous lust for drama.  The cyclist was shaken, in shock, but physically unhurt.  I suppose if she had hit the door head on, she would have been much worse off. I was not feeling much better myself. With apologetic tears shamefully leaking from my eyes, I implored her to let me know if there was anything I could do.  No, she was fine, she said. Just go. I pushed through the small crowd that had gathered, and fled down the sidewalk, away, away, away from it all.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMaybe the moral of the story is that sometimes life is unavoidable.  No matter how good you are at pretending you\\\'re invisible, life always seems to find you. And then it makes you hit people with car doors.\r\n</P>'),(3481,'<p>and the rain hits, a plane squadron battering the city. </p><p>it brings an extra arsenal: the rolling, deep thunder is the ground patrol and quick lightning is on a search and destroy mission, a rogue fighter darting in and out. sirens pierce the air and the sky is black. </p><p>having conquered, clearing the streets and taking prisoners it is gone again - down country, out to sea. we change clothes, dry skin, and look to the window in the still light of absence.</p><p>you don\\\'t notice what is always there until it\\\'s gone, but you cannot fill this space. it will change itself, over time. </p>\r\n'),(3482,'<p>Wednesday morning, day before Thanksgiving<br>\r\nand I am skimming fat from turkey stock.<br>\r\nMy roommate passes by on the way<br>\r\nto the bathroom, scowling at the scene.<br>\r\nA stockpot of boiled necks and wings<br>\r\nthat never flew a day in their lives.<br>\r\nHe only eats fish now, and says things<br>\r\nlike \\\"How\\\'d you like your dead flesh?\\\" <br>\r\nas I sit on the couch with my dinners<br>\r\nof meats.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s been two years to the day<br>\r\nsince I fled California on a redeye<br>\r\nto Vermont to watch my father die. <br>\r\nWe brought him some dinner but he <br>\r\ncouldn\\\'t eat it.  He said we didn\\\'t understand<br>\r\nhow sick he was, so sick that he did <br>\r\nnot want to listen to music, or hear<br>\r\na story, or take pleasure in anything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Just now I became distracted by an image<br>\r\nof him&#151;mouth agape in a frozen snore&#151;<br>\r\nand I missed the sink, spooned a puddle <br>\r\nof golden fat onto the counter.<br>\r\nI sponged it up, rinsed out the sink, <br>\r\nand commenced making lists for the feast,<br>\r\nallotting oven time to various dishes, <br>\r\nanimal and vegetable, imagining as I stared<br>\r\nreverently at the pages of a food magazine, <br>\r\nthe well composed photos with their<br>\r\nglistening pies and roasts perfectly garnished<br>\r\nand lit like sculpture, what it could possibly <br>\r\nfeel like to flee the body,<br>\r\nto leave behind the flavor of crisped<br>\r\nskin, pools of potato studded gravy,<br>\r\nthe smell of warm bread,<br>\r\nwondering if maybe we shouldn\\\'t all<br>\r\nbe giving up one thing at a time, one sensual<br>\r\njoy each year just to prepare,<br>\r\nor if we shouldn\\\'t keep feeding each<br>\r\ncheap craving, lean back in our chairs<br>\r\noverstuffed and tired again, <br>\r\ngreedy \\\'til the day the hunger leaves us <br>\r\nof its own accord.</p>\r\n'),(3483,'<p>Wednesday morning, day before Thanksgiving<br>\r\nand I am skimming fat from turkey stock.<br>\r\nMy roommate passes by on the way<br>\r\nto the bathroom, scowling at the scene.<br>\r\nA stockpot of boiled necks and wings<br>\r\nthat never flew a day in their lives.<br>\r\nHe only eats fish now, and says things<br>\r\nlike \\\"How\\\'d you like your dead flesh?\\\" <br>\r\nas I sit on the couch with my dinners<br>\r\nof meats.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s been two years to the day<br>\r\nsince I fled California on a redeye<br>\r\nto Vermont to watch my father die.  <br>\r\nWe brought him some dinner but he <br>\r\ncouldn\\\'t eat it.  He said we didn\\\'t understand<br>\r\nhow sick he was, so sick that he did <br>\r\nnot want to listen to music, or hear<br>\r\na story, or take pleasure in anything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Just now I became distracted by an image<br>\r\nof himâ€”mouth agape in a frozen snoreâ€”<br>\r\nand I missed the sink, spooned a puddle<br>\r\nof golden fat onto the counter.<br>\r\nI sponged it up, rinsed out the sink,<br>\r\nand commenced making lists for the feast,<br>\r\nallotting oven time to various dishes, <br>\r\nanimal and vegetable, imagining as I stared<br>\r\nreverently at the pages of a food magazine,<br> \r\nthe well composed photos with their<br>\r\nglistening pies and roasts perfectly garnished<br>\r\nand lit like sculpture, what it could possibly<br>\r\nfeel like to flee the body,<br>\r\nto leave behind the flavor of crisped<br>\r\nskin, pools of potato studded gravy,<br>\r\nthe smell of warm bread,<br>\r\nwondering if maybe we shouldn\\\'t all<br>\r\nbe giving up one thing at a time, one sensual<br>\r\njoy each year just to prepare,<br>\r\nor if we shouldn\\\'t keep feeding each<br>\r\ncheap craving, lean back in our chairs<br>\r\noverstuffed and tired again, <br>\r\ngreedy \\\'til the day the hunger leaves us <br>\r\nof its own accord.</p>\r\n'),(3485,''),(3486,'I donâ€™t dream about falling in love with girls or women anymore. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIâ€™m not to sure when it happened but the change hasnâ€™t been that recent. The dreams used to be really clichÃ© but pleasurable none the less. The girl or woman was always the best and in my dreams I was in a relationship with her. We were best friends and great lovers, we would change and grow with each other, the dreams would come including a foggy sense of history with this person. Her presence helped me to mitigate my more self destructive tendencies and made all those things you hate in life a little easier to bear, someone to watch over and be watched over by. As much a I believe in the importance of making oneself a strong individual there was always a feeling of a type of completeness that eludes you when you are on your own. Sometimes the dreams would just be the two of us hanging out sometimes they would be a little more steamy.<br><br>  \r\n\r\nI would wake up from these dreams not disappointed by my re-emergence into my waking reality but filled with a hope of what could come to be.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThis doesnâ€™t happen anymore, now things are differentâ€¦<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow I dream of lust filled encounters with strangers, often prostitutes or exotic dancers - sex workers of one kind or another. There is no love in the encounters but there is the physical kind of pleasure one might expect. The feeling of completeness isnâ€™t there - more just a feeling of release, it also seems that the lust is fuelled in an odd way by the distance between myself and this new envisioned partner. Instead of looking out for each other it is a feeling of mutual usage. Addressing the physical needs of the now with no regard for the long term sustainability of the self.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I wake from these dreams I usually just have to take care of morning biological processes, quite often a headache accompanies.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI want to know why my dreams have changed. I want to find my old dreams, I want to know where they went.'),(3487,'<p>I woke up trapped in the misery of my job - ordering high school kids to stop chatting with each other and just run their registers. I would retrieve, with an inexplicable urgency, new cartons of eggs when a customer had picked one with broken shells. Price checks. Lunch breaks. Counting drawer after drawer. Without knowing why, I put my all into this piddly job.</p><p>This was not meant to happen, and I know that. It was all a case of bad timing. I quit that job, in the less than a minute, after waking one day appalled by the smell of bacon, bearing an ugly pain in my stomach, and in the clutches of the grumpiest mood ever to befall me. I moped for days, reclusive and testy. I couldn\\\'t sleep - I wasn\\\'t tired - but my boredom made it difficult for me to stay awake.</p><p>This continued for two weeks. And then the question was posed: \\\"Baby, do you think maybe you\\\'re pregnant?\\\"</p><p>A hot fire welled within my belly, an anger mixed with hopelessness and fear. So we bought the tests. The lines were more than visible - they practically exploded from the viewscreen. Then it was my turn. I practically exploded, too.</p><p>Something inside me moved that day, and far be it from me to say exactly what it was. But the timing - the bad timing - caught up to me with a suffocating quickness, and then came the questions. What now? What do I do now? I would continue to withdraw, slowly, as body and mind duked it out. I closed down, waiting for the answer. Then suddenly, I knew.</p><p>I knew what to do, and realized I\\\'d known it all along.'),(3488,'<p>I woke up trapped in the misery of my job - ordering high school kids to stop chatting with each other and just run their registers. I would retrieve, with an inexplicable urgency, new cartons of eggs when a customer had picked one with broken shells. Price checks. Lunch breaks. Counting drawer after drawer. Without knowing why, I put my all into this piddly job.</p><p>This was not meant to happen, and I know that. It was all a case of bad timing. I quit that job, in the less than a minute, after waking one day appalled by the smell of bacon, bearing an ugly pain in my stomach, and in the clutches of the grumpiest mood ever to befall me. I moped for days, reclusive and testy. I couldn\\\'t sleep - I wasn\\\'t tired - but my boredom made it difficult for me to stay awake.</p><p>This continued for two weeks. And then the question was posed: \\\"Baby, do you think maybe you\\\'re pregnant?\\\"</p><p>A hot fire welled within my belly, an anger mixed with hopelessness and fear. So we bought the tests. The lines were more than visible - they practically exploded from the viewscreen. Then it was my turn. I practically exploded, too.</p><p>Something inside me moved that day, and far be it from me to say exactly what it was. But the timing - the bad timing - caught up to me with a suffocating quickness, and then came the questions. What now? What do I do now? I would continue to withdraw, slowly, as body and mind duked it out. I closed down, waiting for the answer. Then suddenly, I knew.</p><p>I knew what to do, and realized I\\\'d known it all along.'),(3490,'<p>I woke up trapped in the misery of my job - ordering high school kids to stop chatting with each other and just run their registers. I would retrieve, with an inexplicable urgency, new cartons of eggs when a customer had picked one with broken shells. Price checks. Lunch breaks. Counting drawer after drawer. Without knowing why, I put my all into this piddly job.</p><p>This was not meant to happen, and I know that. It was all a case of bad timing. I quit that job, in the less than a minute, after waking one day appalled by the smell of bacon, bearing an ugly pain in my stomach, and in the clutches of the grumpiest mood ever to befall me. I moped for days, reclusive and testy. I couldn\\\'t sleep - I wasn\\\'t tired - but my boredom made it difficult for me to stay awake.</p><p>This continued for two weeks. And then the question was posed: \\\"Baby, do you think maybe you\\\'re pregnant?\\\"</p><p>A hot fire welled within my belly, an anger mixed with hopelessness and fear. So we bought the tests. The lines were more than visible - they practically exploded from the viewscreen. Then it was my turn. I practically exploded, too.</p><p>Something inside me moved that day, and far be it from me to say exactly what it was. But the timing - the bad timing - caught up to me with a suffocating quickness, and then came the questions. What now? What do I do now? I would continue to withdraw, slowly, as body and mind duked it out. I closed down, waiting for the answer. Then suddenly, I knew.</p><p>I knew what to do, and realized I\\\'d known it all along.'),(3491,'exams are halfway finished. after tomorrow, at five, they will be three-quarters finished. \r\n</p><p>\r\nmy composure these past few weeks has been, to say the least, at times a bit shaken. the words \\\'consumed with fear\\\' spring to mind. about twelve days ago, i was driving out to get a haircut and i was using my cell phone to try to find a psychiatrist who wasn\\\'t booked solid. i couldn\\\'t. i felt manic, like i had to jump from thought to thought, errand to errand to keep my mind off of the looming specter of exams. i was a classic case.</p><p>\r\nthe simple fact was, i had no idea how well i was prepared compared to everyone else. it\\\'s so hard to tell. there are no grades until the final. i slept a lot. i drank a lot. i, in short, did nearly everything i could, i felt, to ensure my own destruction. then the last two days left to study, i got busy studying. it wasn\\\'t really that much material. Property: easements, deeds, mortgages, real estate brokers, title insurance, real estate contracts. that\\\'s not that much to remember. it\\\'s not that much to remember.</p><p>\r\ni could do that, right?</p><p>\r\nthe day of the first exam, Property. open on LAW SCHOOL, OUTDOORS. three men stand around in the sunshine. it is a bit chilly, but bright. all three seem well-composed, talking about relatives, holidays, where to go skiing. opcasionally one will mention something law related; perhaps an anecdote. TYLER smiles, mentions having seen a neat new car the other day. </p><p>\r\nwe shot the shit, making jokes. the other two guys were the two i considered best-prepared, hardest-working. they were calm, cool, collected. i struggled to emulate them. i grinned and made small talk. inside, i wondered with some detachment if i would be able to go inside and take the exam--if i would physically have the strength to propel myself through the double doors and up the stairs, to room 230. at ten minutes \\\'till, they picked up their bags, and i followed suit. \\\'i am a fraud,\\\' i thought to myself, \\\'a fraud, a liar, a waster of twelve thousand dollars this semester alone. i do not belong here.\\\' \r\n</p><p>they went inside, and i followed. so far, so good.</p><p>\r\ni sat under the flourescents, too jacked up to feel anything. i watched everyone talk, laugh, move around, like a condemned man might watch the spectators at his execution arrange themselves in their chairs, awaiting the show. placing their purses on the floor carefully. plugging in laptops. arranging pencils. a man in a suit walked in, and handed out the exams. </p><p>\r\ntime was called, and we opened our exam booklets. a collective sigh of fear rose from everyone\\\'s lips, nearly simultaneously. my friend Maloof, sitting next to me, said, quietly but audibly, \\\'oh, fuck.\\\' the first question was four pages typed. a man had died, leaving no will. he was survived by a wife, a son, a grandaughter. he owned two adjacent properties. the granddaughter had built a cabin on one. there was an access road crossing the other piece leading to the cabin. the wife bought the son\\\'s interest. he gave her a deed. the wife sold the whole thing to a company without even contacting the granddaughter. she gave them a deed as well. the company had title insurance and a real estate broker. the broker got a commission. the company built a factory on the land. the factory blocked the access road. some other shit happened too. everyone sued each other. who gets what?</p><p>\r\ni began tentatively, outlining some basic things on scratch paper. i started typing, just like i\\\'m typing now. when i looked up, an hour had passed. i had written most of my answer. i was doing it. i was answering the question. the rest of the test passed in a blur of relief. soon, the end was in sight. i pressed on. i revised. i edited. i cut and pasted. i burned, and printed. </p><p>\r\nafter the test i was emotionally drained, but whole. my vision was slightly blurry, and the sky was overcast. it was cold. i knew i had missed things, overlooked things, made errors, but i knew i had made at least a C. at the very least. i had passed. i would be able to pass my other exams, if this was any indication; in fact, at the huge drinking party that night, the word was that Property was the hardest. this thought was repeated in various forms, by various people. </p><p>\r\ni had thought i was beyond this sort of anxiety in life. what-do-you-know.'),(3492,'does growing up mean you have to grow tired?'),(3493,'<p>\r\nDeep got old.<br>\r\nI musta put on some elses hat.<br>\r\nI musta read something good and wanted to live in that moment.<br>\r\n\\\"But I don\\\'t feel mediocre?\\\"<br>\r\nI don\\\'t like most common things, but maybe I should.<br><br>\r\nOnce I was shown beautiful things. <br>\r\nelegantly simple things.<br>\r\nNow these things are too complex.<br>\r\nDid I drink too much?<br>\r\nDid I think too much?<br><br>\r\nI thought we had an an agreement here Truth!?<br>\r\nI do my best to find you, and you don\\\'t try as hard to illude me.<br>\r\nI did everything right, I went through all the motions.<br>\r\nI lauded and embraced the esoteric,<br>\r\nsought obscurity,<br>\r\navoided mediocrity and all things \\\"mainstream\\\".<br><br>\r\nBut I really DON\\\'T like television...<br>\r\nI really DON\\\'T like their music...<br>\r\nI really DON\\\'T like their food...<br>\r\nnot because of how it looks sounds or tastes, <br>\r\nbut because I have seen heard and tasted it all before...and its old.<br><br>\r\nbut now, so is \\\"deep\\\"...<br>\r\nDeep got old.<br>\r\nSo maybe then I am just a mediocre man with exotic tastes.<br>\r\nNot a exotic man with mediocre tendencies.<br>\r\nWhere to go from here? <br><br>\r\n...Buy a new hat?<br>\r\nI dont even like hats.<br>\r\n</p>'),(3494,'Jeremy Kittledown, at fifteen years, is not your average boy.  Something lurks within him that his parents canâ€™t see, that he wonâ€™t share with his teachers, or at the once-a-week session with his therapist.  The therapist, a mudflap of a man with plastic round-framed eyeglasses and a furry brown caterpillar for a mustache is easily snowed, he doesnâ€™t know Kittledown at all.  Kittledown has the therapist thinking that heâ€™s depressed because he doesnâ€™t have a girlfriend.  The therapist thinks thereâ€™s this girl that Kittledown likes by the name of Katherine Fenton.  The therapist thinks itâ€™s Katherine Fenton\\\'s blonde hair and cheerleader skirt that have Kittledown all hung up.  The therapist thinks that its Katherine Fenton\\\'s status as one of the popular kids precludes Kittledown â€“ a reader of science fiction novels, a player of Dungeons and Dragons â€“ from even speaking to her.  Itâ€™s a class thing.   The therapist chalks Kittledownâ€™s depression up to teenage lust, swirling hormones, unrequited love.  A teenage thing.  An everyboy thing.  A universal thing.  He, the therapist, recalls his own high school longings and reapplies them here.  The therapist, who should be able to divorce himself from his own memories, tells Kittledown to direct his affections to another girl, a more accessible girl, someone in his same social group.  Youâ€™re a good-looking kid, affable, friendly, funny, thereâ€™s gotta be some girl already out there that likes you, if you just think about it a little.  Isn\\\'t there any girl that seems to look at you differently, who saves a seat for you at school assemblies, who copies her notes for you when youâ€™re absent for a day?  There has to be someone like that, not a cheerleader, but maybe someone from the band? Kittledown played trumpet in the school band.  Maybe someone from the drama club? The therapist remembers his own Katherine Fenton, named Laura Hillbury, an aspiring actress, not a cheerleader, but for all intents and purposes the same, startlingly beautiful, a mane of auburn hair and dark soulful eyes, an impossible girl, a girl who charmed her male teachers, a girl who walked two inches above the ground, a girl too much for the pedantic, poetry-reading boy that the therapist was in high school.  The therapist strokes his mustache.  Maybe school isnâ€™t even the place to look. There must be plenty of girls in your church group that you could pursue, Jeremy.  Nice girls, virgin girls, girls with loose-fitting clothes and too much makeup â€“ Christian girls. Christian girls, Jeremy.  Donâ€™t you want a nice Christian girl?  Forget Katherine Fenton, the therapist urges as the caterpillar twitches under his nose, sheâ€™s out of your league â€“  thinking about her will only lead to grief.  '),(3495,'<P>it is after noon, close to sunset in fact, and i\\\'m sitting here in my red pyjamas, the sort of expensive ones from sears, but worth it because they\\\'re so soft. they have little snowflakes embroidered here and there. they make me feel safe. i\\\'ve been sitting at my computer since about 8.30 am this morning and now it is 4.30, working on this stupid essay. i did take bathroom breaks, but, that\\\'s about it. now i\\\'m starving, but there\\\'s nothing to eat in the house. except, well, bread and cans of tuna. okay now we both know what i\\\'ll be eating.\r\n</P><P>\r\nnow i am feeling panicky, i do not have enough time to write everything i want to write. and putting stuff in apa format is taking FOREVER. i have to worry about margins. about whether the initial goes before or after the surname when the person is an editor. about using \\\"and\\\" in a citation, but & in a reference list. ugh. also, i have lost a bunch of my notes. also i am not so good at this. also i am so so so so slow at editing. why is my life revolving around this essay? because my entire course grade depends upon it. \r\n </P><P>\r\ni want to swear, but i\\\'m trying to cut down. yeah, like smoking. from 2 packs a day down to 1 and then maybe, eventually none. i am also cramping. i hate having my period. every month it is almost blissful for 25 days. so much so i forget what it is like. gah.\r\n </P><P>\r\ni am also still feeling very guilty for my part in breaking my best friend holly\\\'s cup. it was a very cool unique black ceramic cup, part of a set her dad had gotten her for her birthday. very striking when she set the table for dinner with the matching plates and cutlery. i had a very long convoluted dream overnight that i went searching everywhere, in all these different countries, in tiny little Arab souqs and Indian bazaars and Canadian flea markets, for pretty black cups. i could\\\'t find any, and i woke up sad.\r\n </P><P>\r\ni will have a big fat party on friday night. maybe just me and honey, but it will be a party. to celebrate that this thing is OVER. God. i\\\'m thinking i\\\'ll probably end up pulling a damn all nighter. oops. one cigarette is okay, isn\\\'t it?\r\n</P><P>\r\ni envy holly her new 9-5 job. weird how i miss things i used to resent. i hated cubicle land. she\\\'s the english major, and she\\\'s working at dell. i\\\'m the computer science grad, and i\\\'m working on a social psych paper, in grad school. still, it would be nice to have (more) money. yep. friday is soon. friday at 4pm to be precise. then i hand this baby in, and forget about it. try to forget about it. i\\\'ve decided i\\\'m just going to do my damned best, and no-one, not even me, can ask for more. haven\\\'t yet decided whether i want to pull an all nighter. i guess i\\\'ll decide based on progress achieved by 11pm tonight. hmm.\r\n</P><P>\r\njust got half a dozen emails from hollygirl. oh this is like treasure, gold coins and sparkling silver. i know i\\\'m not supposed to be checking my email, especially since i\\\'m crushed under the weight of a huge deadline (DEADline), and even more so because she already told me she would be too busy to email me today, but i couldn\\\'t help it, and apparently she couldn\\\'t either, because i have tons of delicious meesages from her. and God, she\\\'s a superhero. no-one, not my honey, not my friends, not my sisters, no-one has been able to cheer me up this week (before this essay i was stressed/depressed about that stupid part time human-technology interaction contract presentation -- which went well, as in we didn\\\'t blow it, but which went badly, as in, the management didn\\\'t like our design, and wanted us to redesign it, and my lab supervisor out of the blue suggested we\\\'d have it done before christmas! more work!!) not to mention severe acute holly-withdrawal symptoms, and i was just down down down, another reason, btw, for not emailing/calling/contacting any of my friends. i\\\'ve been hibernating. anyway--\r\n </P><P>\r\nanyway, she made me laugh outloud with her little bum ritual clarification. and it was the first time i\\\'ve laughed in at least a week. she\\\'s got bestfriend superpowers.\r\n </P><P>\r\nmy honey has been very sweet, and has given me much comfort and hugs whenever i want, and sometimes without me asking. (despite my training, he\\\'s not so good at the mind reading part) and he\\\'s bought me chocolate, and is very earnest and loving and understanding, and mostly hasn\\\'t risen to the bait when i provoke him in my crankiness, but he doesn\\\'t often make me laugh.\r\n </P><P>\r\nGod it feels so good to to see my fingers flying (that\\\'s right, i\\\'m weird, i look at the keyboard not the screen when i type) anyway, it feels so good to be writing after so long, my thoughts rushing out like crazy water, and i feel as if i\\\'m conjuring up an old holly-shamima time. you know what my favourite pic of us is? when she held out and stretched her long long arm and took a photo herself of the two of us in that dinky little park on rideaucrest, near my parents\\\' house and a.j.\\\'s house, and we were sitting on the grass, and we were happy, actually happy (a mircale considering how moody the two of us are, and how rare pure happiness is) and we were both wearing our rainbow toques. i have to dig that up and pin it up where i can see it. of course, i\\\'m 110% sure i\\\'ve lost that toque.\r\n </P><P>\r\neverything\\\'s gone. i keep thinking of holly\\\'s comment, brief, but it hit me, somewhere in my chest, of how i\\\'m grown up now (and not so easy to buy snow day presents for [she celebrates christmas, i celebrate eid, so we combine it into snowday. the first day the snow falls and _stays_ on the ground we exchange our presents, and it usually happens sometime in between eid and christmas]) and geez sometimes i hate the fact that time is unstoppable. it is like a physical force, isn\\\'t it? i mean, i look forward to things, like holly\\\'s wedding, or finishing grad school, heck even little mundande future events, but i don\\\'t like time passing. i want it back. i miss things. i miss my undergrad, as miserable as parts of it were. so maybe, maybe, in the future i\\\'ll be wanting this time back? this very time i\\\'m compaling about right now? should i stop complaining?\r\n </P><P>\r\nnah. that would be sane, sensible, logical. \r\n </P><P>\r\nwhat i miss are holly\\\'s toes painted with blue nailpolish. and those damn sour cream and onion chip lunches we used to share.\r\n</P>'),(3496,'Weâ€™ve been given time and potential, donâ€™t abuse it.<br>\r\nIt doesnâ€™t matter what you have if youâ€™re not going use it.<br>\r\nYouâ€™re knowledge and ability are nothing with out will.<br>\r\nThe view will all be clear once you reach the top of the hill.<br>\r\nSeen by the third eye; closed by inhibition. <br><br>\r\nLonging to be opened by pure intention.<br>\r\nIâ€™ve found mine to be innocent ambition.<br>\r\nBut use your own reason to spark your own ignition.<br>\r\nBecause it can be finished if you can begin.<br>\r\nThe end is outside and the start is within.<br><br>\r\nWeâ€™re all the same, even if you lead the leaders.<br>\r\nAnd youâ€™re just as important if you feed the feeders.<br>\r\nSo quit worrying about the chain, bottom to top.<br>\r\n\\\'Cause when it all comes down to it, weâ€™re just one in the pop. (ulation)<br>\r\nAnd  vanity always  murders life and will.<br>\r\nYours or others, it will kill.<br><br>\r\nRealize only survival is necessary.<br>\r\nAnd that life is planetary.<br>\r\nSo if we canâ€™t do it, something else can.<br>\r\nDonâ€™t be so arrogant to think the only hope is man.<br>\r\nJust do it, if itâ€™s through yourself or the masses.<br>\r\nIf you did it from the beginning, or back from the ashes.<br><br>\r\nThere are so many roads you can turn on.<br>\r\nTo play your roll as knight, bishop, pawn.-<br>\r\nPick your moves and make your name.<br>\r\nWeâ€™re all just pieces in this life game.<br>\r\nA  well placed pawn can kill any king.<br>\r\nAnd at the end of  board it can be anything.<br><br>\r\nYou can always save yourself, itâ€™s never too late.<br>\r\nYour true destiny is to decide your own fate.<br>\r\nBut what ever you do, what ever your choice.<br>\r\nMake sure that you donâ€™t push your voice.<br>\r\nA will that bends, is a will that lives.<br>\r\nOne that prospers is one that gives-<br><br>\r\n-and accepts knowledge for the taking.<br>\r\nBecause weâ€™re all part of the making.<br>\r\nSo donâ€™t prejudge, especially on looks behalf.<br>\r\nFor, prejudice divides our world in half.<br>\r\nThe only differences in the races are those we perceive,<br>\r\nAnd the ideas passed on to those we conceive.  <br><br>\r\nI wish it were as easy for everyone else as it is with me.<br>\r\nToo realize the truth and enjoy life with a childâ€™s simplicity.<br>\r\nThe only differences between ingenuous and ingenious are u and i.  (you and I)<br>\r\nBut we let greed mixed pride overcome life and people unjustly die. <br>\r\nUnjustly but not unnecessary and certainly not in vain<br><br>\r\nWe can only grow stronger after we live through pain.<br>\r\nThe only way to truly beat death is to accept it.<br>\r\nNot to fear, loath, push, hate, or regret it.<br>'),(3497,'I got on the train at 50th Street, just north of Times\\\' Square.  It\\\'s cold here in the winter, and my first priority upon boarding the train was to quickly stake out a seat near a heat-vent and settle in. I spotted one in the middle of the car, and puffed up my jacket like a big downy bird getting comfy. The train started rolling, and I busied myself with the usual games; scoping out the other strap-hangers and checking my reflection in the car window to see if my hat is on at the correct angle. By the time I looked up, we were pulling into Columbus Circle.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThis tired looking guy about my age got on there. In the great cast of characters constantly strutting their strife-filled pageant on the stages of the NYC Transit, this guy would be the one they called <i>The Artist</i>.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe had in his right hand a cardboard subway advertisement; some ConEd public relations blitz. In his left was a silver speedball paint-pen, which he was shaking maniacally. He asked me something. I reached into my breast pocket and flicked off my tunes. He asked me again... where was I getting off? I was getting off at 110th Street, which I told him. He mumbled that he\\\'d be getting off after me, and chuckled the words one two five<br><br>.\r\n\r\nHe asked me if I\\\'d give him a couple of bucks if he could draw my portrait on the back of the advertisement, with the aforementioned pen, before we got to my stop. I grinned and gave him the nod; affirmative. He may not have noticed that we had just sailed through 86th Street, but I had. This was a wager I so could <i>not</i> lose. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>The Artist</i> set to work like a greased lightening bolt from a subway-scented thunderclap. Before the cap hit he ground, he had already set to paper the curve of my hat, my forehead and where the brim covered my left eye, but the southpaw subway sketcher kept moving the paper to get a better angle. Between his constant shuffle and the commuters passing between us with their damned packages and strollers, I didn\\\'t get a good look; I hardly got any look at all. And didn\\\'t he need to see <i>me</i> to draw me?<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe drew, drawing fiercely. He sketched so hard the silvery tip screached along the paper\\\'s shiny, slick surface with a squawk. <i>The Artist</i> primed the pen into his hand and, as silver ink dazzlingly dribbled from his cupped fingers, streaked and shaded his sketch around the eyes and the fuzzy jacket collar. As we rolled into my stop, he gave magical life to the marriage of ink and paper, his silver-shadowed fingerprints rolling in every crevice over my caricatured nose and lips, the hollowness of my eyes, the tinted grayness of wintery despair clinging to everyone on the train like the fetid city grime dripping from our sneaker soles.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe flipped the paper towards me, beaming. He had drawn a simple but <i>uncanny</i> line drawing likeness of me. I dug it down to my eyebrows. Was he more than the usual stiff hawking doodles to eternally bored souls on the train? I was having thoughts in my head like where I was going to hang this drawing in my house, the great story I could tell my wife about the circumstances around it, and that maybe I should give him more than the three bucks in my chilly hand.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBing, Bong. The doors open and I quickly step up with the wad of crumpled ones, ready to clap them into his silvered hand, but he\\\'s rolling up the sketch and sticking it in his bag. But he\\\'s reaching out for the dough. But... I\\\'m confused. He tells me that yes, he <i>finished it</i> before my stop, just like he said he could. Now could I please give him his bucks? Which I did, muttering, ok, I get it, still blankly watching the subway doors scissor shut, as he rides off laughing, his voice camouflaged by the conductor\\\'s staticy station-stop announcements on the oscillating PA.<br><br>\r\n\r\nDid you ever notice that the trains get emptier as they go uptown?  It\\\'s because that by the time you get into the triple-digit streets, everyone\\\'s gotten off but the boricua kids, the working-class blacks, the poor students, the mamasitas and a shotgun spray of hoodrats. And the suckers. And you. And all the other extras, who you probably never thought counted. But amongst us tonight in New York City walks <i>The Artist</i>, who just got paid. And everyone that gets paid is happy when they finally get off.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3498,'<P>Bah.  What did Kittledown care about Katherine Fenton?  He had only mentioned her because herâ€™s was the first name to come to mind.  She sits next to him in Algebra, in his first period, but he has no real interest in her.  Sure, he had imagined fucking her.  Of course he did, who could resist? Everybody knew the girl put out.  But she only put out for a certain type.  Kittledown didnâ€™t realistically think heâ€™d ever get into those well-worn panties which were probably embroidered with logos like the drawings on the side of WWII fighters, a football helmet for each of the boys who had fallen victim to the allure of her snatch.  \r\n\r\n<P>Sure he had imagined fucking Katherine Fenton. But her unattainably wasnâ€™t enough to pull him down.  Regarding Katherine, his fantasies were enough to sustain him.   Nonetheless there was enough raw lust there to make the therapis believe his performance.  But in the end, Katherine Fenton was a distraction, a flag heâ€™d dropped for the therapist to pick up in his  a-ha! moment.  Suspecting a girl to be the root of the problem, the therapist could pin the source of Kittledown\\\'s depression to the wall so he could point at it, deprecate it and diffuse its importance.   \r\n\r\n<p>For three months now, Kittledownâ€™s parents had picked him up at school and dropped him off for the 50-minute-hour, a short hour for which the therapist received $100.  A whopping $100 for nodding and saying I see, go on, for recommending Christian girls, for saying that prayer and meditation were a fitting treatment for Kittledown\\\'s supposed emotional problems.   \r\n\r\n<P>Kittledownâ€™s parents are Orange County Christains, evangelicals, fundamentalists, believers that every word in the Bible is the inspired word of God. Therefore theyâ€™ve hired a Christian therapist who Kittledown suspects may not actually be a therapist, and instead is probably a pastor who has studied counseling as part of his calling â€“thatâ€™s right, <i>calling</i>, as if God himself had stepped into the therapist\\\'s dreams and told him that he should go into therapy - God as guidance counselor, God as academic advisor â€“ because all Kittledown learns about in the way of therapeutic tools is religious aphorisms - prayer is the answer, confess all you problems to Him, and He will cast a light onto your path.  \r\n\r\n<P>Kittledown, who had learned about psychotherapy primarily from Woody Allen movies, had expected talk of Oedipus complexes and obsessive compulsive behavior, bipolar personality disorders and chemical imbalances â€“ but all he got was <i>Christianity</i>, how fucking un-Woodyish.  \r\n\r\n<P>Kittledownâ€™s parents make Wednesdayâ€™s therapy sessions into family nights out, taking him from the therapy sessions to Dennyâ€™s where they order breakfast foods for dinner.  Kittledown gets the Grand Slam breakfast with bacon and sausage, scrambled eggs, hash browns.  He doesnâ€™t really think too much about what his parents order.  Pancakes.  French toast.  Coffee with cream and three sugars.  \r\n\r\n<P>The waitress (usually the same waitress â€“ named Patty â€“ with a row of white teeth and a smile that would make Julia Roberts jealous) always sets Kittledownâ€™s Grand Slam down in front of him before his parents get their meals.  How old could she be?  Nineteen, twenty at the most?  Thatâ€™s only 5 years older than he.  Patty is almost his age.  Or, more accurately, Kittledown is almost Pattyâ€™s age.  To him, he and Patty are the same, related, cut from the same cloth.  In his mind Kittledown will someday get a job at this Dennyâ€™s, a bus boy, a waiter, a cook, and he and will Patty fuck in the employee break room, they will screw in the freezer, he will lie her back on boxes of frozen meat and her breath will come hard, puffing, vapor clouds condenscing in the cold air.  \r\n\r\n<P>He thanks Patty for his meal, asks for more water, and when she smiles and says <i>sure thing, honey</i>, Kittledown feels that a secret message passes between them, a coded message of understanding is slipped in under parental radar.  \r\n\r\n<P>How did your session go today?</i> his parents ask. And Kittledown tells them that it went okay, that the therapist thought it was productive. Kittledownâ€™s parents nod and smile with the belief that their money is well-spent.  \r\n\r\n<P>If the therapist could search Kittledownâ€™s room, if he could lift up the mattress and find the porno mags stashed there, <i>Hustler</i> and <i>Leg Show</i> and <i>Swank</i>, heâ€™d assume that he was right.  Pornography is the gatekeeper to a boyâ€™s adolescent sexuality.  They are symbolic of unrequited lust, tokens of an attempt to satisfy frustrated desire. \r\n\r\n<P>The pormo mags are easy for Kittledown to swipe from the liquor store.  Heâ€™s been doing it for two years now and has it down to a science.  \r\n\r\n<P>The candy rack is on the other side of the magazine rack, so loiter about the candy rack as if trying to decide between a Snickers or a Marathon Bar or a Milky Way.  Keep an eye on the clerk who, in the summer months, watches baseball games on a flickering black-and-white just below the counter.  In the winter itâ€™s football.  The games are usually distracting enough, but donâ€™t trust that the clerk is fully involved in them.  The clerk will look up periodically, to see what your are doing over there for so long because nobody takes that long to choose a goddamn candy bar, but keep looking at the rack of candy, take your own sweet time, pick them up and pretend to read the ingredients.  You are a connoisseur of candy, a goddamn gourmand.  But watch the clerk.  Donâ€™t take your eyes from him.  Your time will come.  Others will come into the store, they will buy things: packs of cheese crackers, potato chips, pretzels, six packs of beer.  Still, wait.  It isn\\\'t your time yet.  When the clerk turns his back to retrieve a fifth of Wild Turkey for a customer, thatâ€™s it.  Slip around the rack to the magazines and slide the porn under your shirt.  Then, to throw the clerk of the trail, buy a pack of candy bar.  Get the fuck out of there you goddamn con artist you.\r\n'),(3499,'<P><TT>got out of the hospital<BR>the monday before christmas<BR>with nothing but my car,<BR>computer,<BR>and a quarter of weed<BR>in the glove compartment.<BR><BR>looked all through the car<BR>and couldn\\\'t find<BR>the title.<BR><BR>pawned the computer<BR>for two hundred bucks<BR>and hit the casino boat.<BR>stopped when i\\\'d won<BR>a thousand and fifty.<BR>used the fifty for<BR>drinks and tips<BR>until it was time to go.<BR><BR>i think i\\\'ve perfected<BR>my progressive betting system.<BR>it works as long as<BR>i don\\\'t get drunk<BR>and start making <BR>crazy wagers.<BR>it works best on roulette.<BR>you pick red or black<BR>and stick to it,<BR>bet a single unit.<BR>double your bet if you lose.<BR>bet a single unit if you win.<BR>it works better<BR>with two people:<BR>one on red,<BR>one on black.<BR>if you\\\'re playing that way,<BR>and you\\\'re a girl and a boy,<BR>(or two girls or two boys, i guess)<BR>the dealer thinks<BR>you\\\'re just being all cutesy,<BR>betting against each other.<BR>never suspects you\\\'re cheating.<BR>is it cheating, though?<BR>i could gamble for the rest of my life<BR>and make a decent living,<BR>but i don\\\'t want to do that.<BR><BR>after the shuttle brought us back,<BR>i went to the gay bar.<BR>after awhile,<BR>some straight people came in<BR>and sat down<BR>at the bar beside me:<BR>two girls and a boy.<BR>kristi and jamie were girlfriend/boyfriend.<BR>annette was a friend of kristi\\\'s.<BR>five minutes after meeting me,<BR>annette said<BR>she\\\'d suck my dick<BR>better than any man ever could.<BR>i told her it sounded like a challenge,<BR>but i\\\'d pass.<BR>we sat there,<BR>drinking the expensive drinks.<BR>i confirmed that they were straight.<BR>\\\"and you\\\'re gay?\\\"<BR>kristi asked me.<BR>\\\"i\\\'m bi.\\\"<BR>she drifts off to jamie,<BR>and they hold a whispered conversation.<BR>i talk to annette for awhile.<BR>i ask them all if they get high.<BR>they say they live<BR>right around the corner.<BR>we finish our drinks<BR>and walk over there.<BR><BR>we smoke and go back.<BR>it\\\'s bingo night.<BR>kristi gets drunk<BR>and keeps shouting out<BR>\\\"oh, sixty-nine!\\\"<BR>which pisses the queens off to no end.<BR><BR>the four of us<BR>are the hottest people<BR>in the bar.<BR>jamie tells me,<BR>\\\"we\\\'ve been looking<BR>for somebody like you.\\\"<BR>\\\"well, shit, man:<BR>here i am.\\\"<BR><BR>kristi and jamie and i<BR>fuck on jamie\\\'s waterbed.<BR>he\\\'s got a t.v. so big<BR>it takes up a substantial piece<BR>of the room.<BR>i had warned them<BR>that it would change<BR>their relationship:<BR>kill it or make it stronger.<BR>they were ready to gamble.<BR><BR>i left them as they slept.<BR>(for some reason,<BR>sex doesn\\\'t put me right to sleep.<BR>it kind of wires me<BR>for awhile.<BR>i\\\'ll hang out, putter around<BR>eat something,<BR>and then the supersonic coma.)<BR>i closed jamie\\\'s bedroom door.<BR>annette was on the sofa<BR>watching mtv2.<BR>she offered to suck my dick<BR>for forty bucks.<BR>i said,<BR>\\\"i wouldn\\\'t give you ten bucks<BR>on a good night.<BR>and did you not hear<BR>what just happened in there?<BR>i\\\'ll be lucky<BR>if i can get it up<BR>for a couple of days.<BR>come see me wednesday night,<BR>and i\\\'ll give you<BR>six bucks and a breath mint.\\\"<BR>i walked back to the gay bar<BR>and set about<BR>finding a hotel.<BR><BR>cheap hotels are the best ones.<BR>paid $150 for a week at travel inn.<BR>it\\\'s a place on the highway<BR>near the casino boat<BR>and the gay bar.<BR><BR>cable t.v.<BR>hot water<BR>no phone,<BR>no heat.<BR>slept on top of the covers,<BR>rolled up in a blanket<BR>i got out of the car.<BR><BR>the next morning,<BR>i started writing again<BR>for the first time<BR>in a few months.<BR>the sex had seemed<BR>to jump-start something.<BR>the infidels<BR>were still there,<BR>waiting for me.<BR>i started a story<BR>about the time<BR>matt\\\'s grandfather died.<BR><BR>that was tuesday, december 22.<BR>that afternoon,<BR>i drove to brooksville<BR>and called kiki,<BR>bought three hundred dollars<BR>worth of crack<BR>and an ounce of weed.<BR>drove back to the motel<BR>and started smoking.<BR>watching mtv2 with the sound turned off<BR>and listening to jackie wilson,<BR>it took me five hours<BR>to finish the crack.<BR>by then, it was nine-thirty.<BR>i left the room<BR>and walked down the road<BR>to a convenience store<BR>to get some beer<BR>to ease the come-down.<BR><BR>put the rolling rock on the counter.<BR>i was sweating<BR>and shaking uncontrollably.<BR>the cashier was middle-aged, dumpy<BR>a yin-yang tattoo on her left forearm.<BR>her nametag read \\\"kristi.\\\"<BR>\\\"are you OK?\\\"<BR>she asked.<BR>\\\"yeah.  i think i\\\'m coming down<BR>with something.\\\"<BR>\\\"here,\\\" she said.<BR>\\\"i\\\'m going to give you something.\\\"<BR>she took a little stoppered bottle<BR>from under the counter.<BR>\\\"here,\\\" she said.<BR>she shook a few drops<BR>of something brown and viscous<BR>onto my finger.<BR>i thought i was supposed to<BR>lick it off.<BR>\\\"no,\\\" she said.<BR>\\\"rub it in the middle of your chest.\\\"<BR>it kind of tingled<BR>and smelled like peppermint.<BR>\\\"there.  you feel better now, right?\\\"<BR>yes.  yes.  sure.<BR>\\\"you should really<BR>take better care<BR>of yourself.\\\"<BR>\\\"i know.\\\"<BR>i asked for a pack of philly blunts<BR>and a lighter.<BR>she frowned at that.<BR><BR>they say a large component<BR>of addiction<BR>is rebellion.<BR><BR>back at the motel,<BR>i did some push-ups<BR>and sit-ups and<BR>walking lunges.<BR>smoked a blunt and<BR>drank two beers and<BR>took three ativan and<BR>a shower.<BR>felt better.<BR>drove over to<BR>kristi and jamie\\\'s<BR>and sat around for a long time<BR>talking and smoking.<BR>had sex and went home.<BR><BR>wednesday, i was on the boat all day.<BR>won seven and change.<BR>that night,<BR>i waited on the fantail<BR>for the shuttle to come.<BR>\\\"you moon,\\\"<BR>i thought.<BR>\\\"you faint suns.<BR>water and birds.<BR>i feel like<BR>a paper man<BR>cut out<BR>and pasted<BR>into this landscape.<BR>i stood back there by the rail<BR>and smoked a joint<BR>and counted my money.<BR><BR>it was twelve-thirty<BR>when i got back to the motel.<BR>jamie and kristi<BR>had told me<BR>they were going to jamie\\\'s mother\\\'s<BR>in lake<BR>land<BR>for christmas.<BR>i watched mtv2 for awhile,<BR>mused on the fact<BR>that r.e.m. had sold<BR>over sixteen million copies<BR>of their greatest hits album,<BR>almost entirely in europe.<BR>remember the new year\\\'s night<BR>i called michael stipe<BR>at two in the morning,<BR>having gotten his number<BR>from john keane\\\'s<BR>digital address book<BR>while i was interning for him.<BR>wonder if he\\\'s home.<BR>wonder if he\\\'s up.<BR>wonder if he\\\'s changed<BR>his number.<BR>then i start to ponder on<BR>something else.<BR>i get out the yellow pages,<BR>turn to the e\\\'s.<BR>e is for<BR>escort.<BR>there\\\'s one called<BR>\\\"no limit.\\\"<BR>that\\\'s the one for me.<BR>call them<BR>and stress that<BR>i want a fine blonde girl<BR>under twenty-five.<BR><BR>i take off my clothes<BR>and look at myself in the mirror:<BR>one of the scars<BR>from the gall bladder surgery<BR>that the doctor said<BR>would fade away<BR>hasn\\\'t faded away,<BR>and it\\\'s fucking with my definition.<BR>i put my clothes back on and wait.<BR>half an hour later,<BR>there\\\'s a knock at the door.<BR>it\\\'s quarter after three.<BR>she\\\'s blonde,<BR>she\\\'s fine,<BR>and she looks to be<BR>under twenty-five.<BR>she\\\'s tan, too,<BR>which is a plus.<BR><BR>she comes in.<BR>i ask her to sit down.<BR>she sits on the bed.<BR>we go through the routine:<BR>\\\"are you affiliated<BR>with any law enforcement agency?\\\"<BR>\\\"no.  are you?\\\"<BR>\\\"what\\\'s your name?\\\"<BR>i ask.<BR>\\\"no, wait,\\\" i say.<BR>\\\"can i call you kristi?\\\"<BR>\\\"sure.\\\"<BR><BR>i put on my<BR>bobby \\\"blue\\\" bland tape,<BR>and business is transacted<BR>at the rate of one hundred dollars per hour.<BR>i come four times in six hours.<BR>i don\\\'t think she comes,<BR>but she fakes it pretty convincingly<BR>the first time.<BR>fuck her once in the pussy,<BR>once in the ass.<BR>she sucks my dick<BR>and then jacks me off<BR>on christmas morning.<BR>i don\\\'t put my mouth anywhere on her,<BR>don\\\'t kiss her.<BR>the blowjob is uncovered, though.<BR>gotta make some sacrifices<BR>in the name of ...<BR>in the name of what?<BR>dunno.<BR><BR>after bobby \\\"blue\\\" clicks off,<BR>i put on \\\"off the wall.\\\"<BR>\\\"isn\\\'t this michael jackson?\\\"<BR>she asks.<BR>\\\"yeah.\\\"<BR>\\\"you still listen to him?\\\"<BR>\\\"yeah.  you mean after the child molesting stuff came out?\\\"<BR>\\\"yeah.\\\"<BR>\\\"that doesn\\\'t matter to me.<BR>art\\\'s separate from the artist.<BR>i mean, i still read celine<BR>even though he was an anti-semite.<BR>i still watch elia kazan\\\'s movies.\\\"<BR>she looks at me blankly<BR>for a second.<BR>\\\"oh, yeah, <B><I>on the waterfront</I></B>,\\\"<BR>she says.<BR>\\\"we studied that in my film class.\\\"<BR>\\\"you\\\'re a film student?\\\"<BR>\\\"yeah.  i want to be an actress.\\\"<BR>\\\"well, you\\\'ve got a good start.<BR>that was some acting job you put on a second ago.\\\"<BR>\\\"oh, that.  that wasn\\\'t acting.\\\"<BR>\\\"yeah, sure.\\\"<BR><BR>we listen to gang of four<BR>and smoke a couple of blunts<BR>toward sunrise.<BR>\\\"it\\\'s christmas,\\\"<BR>i say.<BR>\\\"i\\\'m not going to see my family today.\\\"<BR>\\\"why not?\\\"<BR>\\\"my parents and i had a fight.<BR>they won\\\'t let me have my money.\\\"<BR>\\\"for what?  to spend on weed and whores?\\\"<BR>\\\"hah.\\\"<BR><BR>we\\\'re lying in bed.<BR>the room smells like<BR>cum and pussy.<BR>i say,<BR>\\\"i keep having these persecution dreams.<BR>somebody\\\'s always trying to<BR>make me feel guilty in them.\\\"<BR>\\\"i used to have those, too,\\\"<BR>she says.<BR>\\\"you know what got rid of them?\\\"<BR>\\\"what?\\\"<BR>\\\"i learned to love my life.\\\"</TT></P>'),(3500,'<p>i have a exam on monday, financial accounting. beware. but i couldn\\\'t care less right now. i might have lost a friend, a french girl, who i really loved. and i just want to proof-read, correct and send my letter to her. i don\\\'t want to study, although i have to.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i took a bath before, in my appartment under repair. and these strange sound came from the air-valve. joy or masturbating i first thought, but seconds later definitely sobbing. suddenly stopped, then came back. words i couldn\\\'t hear. no one replied, maybe she talked to herself, or on the telephone. but it\\\'s kind of odd to talk on the phone in the bath-tub, isn\\\'t it?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\'i hate\\\' you, maybe. \\\'i hate you, i hate you, i hate you, i hate you, i hate you, i hate you, i hate you, i hate you\\\' lower and lower \\\'til she ran out of breath. that was all i got. and the water as she moved, rippled \\\'round her body.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think it was the girl below, but it could as well be a girl above, if there is a girl in the appartment above. i saw the one below some weeks ago. i saw her legs, imagined the rest \\\'round the corner. she had a knee-length skirt, she was probably a brunette. she was probably gorgeous. i feel sorry for her.</p>'),(3501,'\\\"Cornbread!  Is the answer!  Is the moment!  That everything happens, cornbread!  Is so golden!  Do-do-do-do-do-do-do, cornbread...\\\" (sung to the 3-2-1 Contact theme song)</p><p>\r\nMy sister is sometimes amazing.</p><p>\r\nToday, I went bra shopping.  I got lacy things.  Some sleazier than others.  It\\\'ll be glorious the second they\\\'re worn -- I know I\\\'ll be pulling off my shirt for everyone one to see them.  The world\\\'s either seeing too much or not enough of my tits.  I think not enough.</p><p>\r\nMy world would be much more complete with many lacy underthings.  And a crockpot.  Maybe a lacy crockpot.  Do they make those?'),(3502,'I\\\'ve realised that of all of my friends only I and one other person don\\\'t fill our sentences with \\\"fuck this... fuck that.... fucking blah.... fucking blah blah blah.\\\" And it\\\'s made me think.<br><br> My friends have the vocabulary of a confused ten year old. Their converstaions consist of mindless dribble which I find numbing and they insist on inserting \\\"fuck\\\" into each and every sentence like it\\\'s madatory that it is heard a million times a day. Yet, I sit, listen and pretend to care.<br><br>Their so-called intelligent conversations remind me of my first year at high school when everyone was new and didn\\\'t quite know how to act. We were out of our element and I\\\'m beginning to wonder if that\\\'s the case now.<br><br> I want to go out and meet new people, forgetting the friends i have now, but then I feel like a shit friend. Maybe I am, well I am, but I figure that if I\\\'m becoming resentful to these people, then isn\\\'t it better to get away from them for a while and maybe come back when they\\\'ve grown up? But then I wonder if they\\\'re ever going to grow up. They\\\'ve been like it for five years, why change now?<br><br> So I sit, listen and pretend to care while at the same time all I can think of is how much they annoy me and piss me off. It\\\'s getting to the stage where their voices make my skin crawl. Karma is going to come and kick me in the ass one day for saying this kind of shit. <br><br>'),(3503,'just came back from cold mountain, which was a very\r\ngood movie. not excellent, but good. i have a weakness\r\nfor minghella. he makes shiny movies. but there\\\'s\r\nsubtance beneath the slick surface. cold mountain made\r\nme think of love, of how it really is a mysterious\r\nenterprise, and almost impossible to explain. how it\r\ndoesn\\\'t need words. how it changes the texture of\r\ntime. and of course, war. not so much a polar opposite\r\nof love, maybe just on a different latitude. of how\r\nbrtual men are. of how iraq has become a footnote. or\r\nnoise rather than signal. of how easy it is to tune\r\nout suffering. of how messy abd bloody and intense\r\nhand to hand combat was. of how people fought and\r\nfought and fought until they forgot what they were\r\nfighting about. of how using the past tense for the\r\nlast sentence is ironic.\r\n\r\nhave watched lost in translation. both bill murray and\r\nscarlet johansson are treasures. i loved that movie.\r\nit was so interior, for lack of a better word. and so\r\nsurreal it was deeper than real. it wasn\\\'t just about\r\na bizarro country, it was about being adrift in\r\ngeneral. lost lost lost. misunderstood. and i loved\r\nhow it protrayed a complicated relationship between an\r\nolder man and a younger woman, and no bedroom scene!\r\nand how it was about longing. and wanting to escape\r\nthe utter doom of being lonely. and of someone getting\r\nit. just getting it. no questions. just standing in\r\nthe elevator, in solidarity. and it occurs to me, just\r\nnow, as i type this, how close the word solitary is to\r\nthe word solidarity.\r\n\r\nGod, i love movies. and if watching mvoies truly is a\r\nsin, then i will go to hell. i need to immerse myself\r\nin a movie just so i can ... i don\\\'t kknow what to put\r\nhere, but it is essential and you will understand, so\r\nit is so easy to put fingers to keybaord and let\r\nloose, especially when it comes to ART, namely\r\nsustenance.\r\n\r\nhave also read speaking with the angel, an anthology \r\nby nick hornby to benefit a school for autistic children \r\n(his son is autistic) i own it, in\r\nfact, and some of the stories are gems. am currently\r\nreading the bitch in the house, a feministy collection\r\nof angst about how rotten it can be to be a wife,\r\nmother, writer, woman. although, strangely, i am no\r\nlonger angry or suffocating and i feel like an alien\r\npeeking at a world i used to inhabit once, but no\r\nlonger. well. i am an alien. free falling in space\r\n(now what a sensation that would be, huh?) but it is\r\nonly temporary. i\\\'m sure i\\\'ll land deep into the\r\nrage/sadness soon enough. c\\\'mon, it is the engine that\r\ndrives me.\r\n\r\nmy relationships are like thermometers measuring the\r\nquality of my life. right now they are sunny and life\r\nis beautiful.\r\n\r\noutside it is still cold and the wind slaps us with\r\ntiny white hands.\r\n'),(3504,'<P>\r\njust came back from cold mountain, which was a very\r\ngood movie. not excellent, but good. i have a weakness\r\nfor minghella. he makes shiny movies. but there\\\'s\r\nsubtance beneath the slick surface. cold mountain made\r\nme think of love, of how it really is a mysterious\r\nenterprise, and almost impossible to explain. how it\r\ndoesn\\\'t need words. how it changes the texture of\r\ntime. and of course, war. not so much a polar opposite\r\nof love, maybe just on a different latitude. of how\r\nbrtual men are. of how iraq has become a footnote. or\r\nnoise rather than signal. of how easy it is to tune\r\nout suffering. of how messy abd bloody and intense\r\nhand to hand combat was. of how people fought and\r\nfought and fought until they forgot what they were\r\nfighting about. of how using the past tense for the\r\nlast sentence is ironic.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhave watched lost in translation. both bill murray and\r\nscarlet johansson are treasures. i loved that movie.\r\nit was so interior, for lack of a better word. and so\r\nsurreal it was deeper than real. it wasn\\\'t just about\r\na bizarro country, it was about being adrift in\r\ngeneral. lost lost lost. misunderstood. and i loved\r\nhow it protrayed a complicated relationship between an\r\nolder man and a younger woman, and no bedroom scene!\r\nand how it was about longing. and wanting to escape\r\nthe utter doom of being lonely. and of someone getting\r\nit. just getting it. no questions. just standing in\r\nthe elevator, in solidarity. and it occurs to me, just\r\nnow, as i type this, how close the word solitary is to\r\nthe word solidarity.\r\n</P><P>\r\nGod, i love movies. and if watching mvoies truly is a\r\nsin, then i will go to hell. i need to immerse myself\r\nin a movie just so i can ... i don\\\'t kknow what to put\r\nhere, but it is essential and you will understand, so\r\nit is so easy to put fingers to keybaord and let\r\nloose, especially when it comes to ART, namely\r\nsustenance.\r\n</P><P>\r\nhave also read speaking with the angel, an anthology \r\nby nick hornby to benefit a school for autistic children \r\n(his son is autistic) i own it, in\r\nfact, and some of the stories are gems. am currently\r\nreading the bitch in the house, a feministy collection\r\nof angst about how rotten it can be to be a wife,\r\nmother, writer, woman. although, strangely, i am no\r\nlonger angry or suffocating and i feel like an alien\r\npeeking at a world i used to inhabit once, but no\r\nlonger. well. i am an alien. free falling in space\r\n(now what a sensation that would be, huh?) but it is\r\nonly temporary. i\\\'m sure i\\\'ll land deep into the\r\nrage/sadness soon enough. c\\\'mon, it is the engine that\r\ndrives me.\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy relationships are like thermometers measuring the\r\nquality of my life. right now they are sunny and life\r\nis beautiful.\r\n</P><P>\r\noutside it is still cold and the wind slaps us with\r\ntiny white hands.\r\n</P>'),(3505,'<p>Gwaltney virginia-cured bacon-- 2 pieces<br>\r\n2 grade A large white eggs<br>\r\n3 medium sized baby portabella mushrooms, cut into quarters<br>\r\n1/4 cup chopped onion<br>\r\n1tsp/1 clove minced garlic/garlic powder\r\n1tbsp butter<br>\r\n1tbsp milk<br>\r\npinch of salt<br>\r\npinch of pepper<br>\r\npinch of chili powder<br>\r\nshake of generic italian seasoning -- oregano, etc<br>\r\ntabasco<br>\r\n1/4 cup shredded cheddar cheese</p><p>\r\ncook the bacon, lengthwise, until it seems almost brown. set it aside on paper towels.<br><br>\r\nrinse the still hot frying pan under the tap to get the grease off. put it right back on the flame.<br><br>\r\nput the butter in the pan. put the onion and mushroom in there after it melts, and stir em around with your spatula. mmm, butter. <br><br>\r\nthen put in all your spices: salt, pepper, chili powder, and italian seasoning. stir occasionally.<br><br>\r\nnow break your eggs into a cereal bowl, add the milk, and stir them well until the yolk and the albumen are close to being one.<br><br>\r\nwhen the onions and mushrooms have shrunk and turned brown, add the garlic to the pan.<br><br>\r\npush the onions and mushrooms into a pile shaped roughly like a rectangle. add at least three drops of tabasco, but not more than six, to the pile--not the pan surface itself. allow this to cook for about a minute. if smoke rises from the tabasco sauce, do not breathe it in. it will irritate your air passages.<br><br>\r\nstir the egg/milk mixture a final time, and dump it in the pan. mix it together with the vegetables, and then spread it all out to about 2cm thick. <br><br>\r\nadd the cheese, and crumble the bacon right in there. stir.<br><br>\r\ncook until the mixture is still a little wet, but nothing is runny. try not to overcook it. serve on a plate with a small mound of ketchup on the side. for extra goodness, stir some tabasco into the ketchup. good accompanying beverage choices are orange juice, gatorade, milk, or champagne. follow with marijuana, plus video games or movies until digested, then go for a bike ride. take your camera.  \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3506,'<p>It\\\'s funny, last night I was thinking how much\r\nI miss eating seafood,\r\nhow long it\\\'s been since my arrangement of tongue and teeth\r\nscooped out the entire inner workings of an animal.\r\nJoyous destruction of small misshapen forms\r\nholding always the taste of the sea\r\nno matter how far away \r\nwe are from that great wide watery cradle.</p>\r\n \r\n<p>Today I am clouded with grey inland thoughts\r\nthese days rolling past the office windows in endless waves\r\nsmoke rising from roofs where no fires burn,\r\nthe exhaled breath of buildings\r\ndrifting past the patchy snow-strewn earth\r\nand the bare and silent trees.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Let this wind carry me out to sea.\r\nI will not return.</p>\r\n'),(3507,'<center><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3507_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"15\\\"></center><br><Br>\r\n\r\nHave I told you about my old orange plastic phone? If you place the receiver in its cradle upside down, it looks like an orange bull, a taurus telephone.<br><br>\r\nIt really is a shame, at least in terms of aesthetics, that the rotary protocol is no longer in fashion.  It speaks of an era where gaudy, colorful American design was as resonant as the duophonic bronze bells inside this shiny plastic beast; it has a four-pronged tail. It looks that it has been adequately used for many years.<br><br>\r\nIt bears the number (904) 651-0139. The area code is that of a town in Northeastern Florida, directly south of the Georgia border. Imagine, an orange phone from Florida. I doubt the phones in Georgia were peach. <br><br>\r\nThis phone was made to sell to people who took pride in such devices. Now, it is only a functionally-obsolete work of art, sitting pretty on its reed mat like a streamlined toro.'),(3508,'As conscious fades and subconscious is disclosed.  <br>\r\nI see true beauty as my eyes are closed. <br>\r\nFor this is a beauty for my third to show.  <br>\r\nA beauty riddled in truths above and below.  <br>\r\nMy eyes move rapidly as the thoughts begin.  <br>\r\nCreating a world around me a world with in.  <br>\r\nA world for my everlasting mind, <br>\r\nso my tired body may begin to rest.  <br>\r\nAll of my thoughts merged molded, shaped sculpted, <br>\r\nsymbolically upholded begin to manifest.<br>  \r\nI see the tops of the mountains, I feel the cool breeze.<br>  \r\nI smell itâ€™s aroma, and hear the wind through the leaves.<br>\r\nThis taste in my mouth is so sweet.  <br>\r\nI since my soul may finally be at peace. <br>\r\nSo I grab this moment, I squeeze it and hold it.<br> \r\nIf I remember in the morning Iâ€™ll never let go of it.<br>\r\nThe way I could just clear my mind, <br>\r\nand make my body completely weightless.<br>\r\nFree to fly away from my crimes, <br>\r\nmy greed , my vanity, my fear, my hatred.<br>\r\nAs I look down at the scenery, <br>\r\nI see oceans of flowers to the sea.<br>\r\nI wish I could share this with the one I love.<br>\r\nI look up and see her face in the stars above.<br>\r\nSo I pray for just one kiss.<br>\r\nTo feel the ecstasy of her glowing lips.<br>\r\nAs my wish was granted.<br>\r\nI felt my body become enchanted.<br> \r\nIt feels so real, it canâ€™t be fake,<br>\r\nBut my dream starts to fade, my eyes open and I wake.<br>\r\nI try to close them back and relive it once more, <br>\r\nbut I find that is for subconscious to restore.<br>'),(3509,'<P>The poems of my early-to-mid twenties Concerned a certain woman, named CF who, two years my senior, took take my virginity, a bitter wafer I was all to glad to have removed from my mouth.  \r\n\r\n<P>Iâ€™m not an obsessive type, or at least I didnâ€™t think so, but recently I did Google searches with CFâ€™s name, a uniquely spelled version of a common name, with extra letters, Hs and Ys where they shouldnâ€™t be, where nobody in their right mind would put them. Therefore, when I typed her uniquely-spelled name into Google, it came up, approximately 25 pages of digital record.  \r\n\r\n<P>The first thing I discovered was that CF had married and changed her last name.  I found a cache of live music reviews she wrote for a local L.A. magazine.  Even with the different last name, it still had to be her; such was the spelling of her name.  I went through the archives and read each review.  They were adequately written, but without much spark or creativity: lackluster, out-of-pocket praise of a bands instrumentation and stage presence.  In the end, the reviews werenâ€™t satisfying.  They were too outwardly focused, never said anything about the writer.\r\n\r\n<P>As I dug deeper into the Google findings, I found a web page devoted to a band she had joined after we broke up.  There was a photo of the band on stage and below the picture the caption stated the bandâ€™s lineup.  The guitaristâ€™s last name was the same as CFâ€™s new one.  Here was the man she had married.  I scoured this site, but nowhere found another reference to CF.\r\n\r\n<P>Moving on, I sifted through handfuls of musicians websites that quoted from her various reviews.  But then I struck gold.  Her name appeared in somebodyâ€™s blog.  I read an entry that referred to CF as the maid of honor at a wedding and traced that blog back through the links until I came to the mother load.  Her husbandâ€™s blog.\r\n\r\n<P>At least I thought it was her husband.  This person had precisely the same name as the guitarist in the band photo I mentioned earlier.  But as I read through the entries, I couldnâ€™t find one that mentioned CF.  In fact, I found references to his girlfriend who had a different name than CF.  I kept digging though, slogging through this strangerâ€™s life.  I found out that he worked for UPS, that he liked to play hockey, that he brewed his own beer.  Not very interesting stuff.   Maybe this wasnâ€™t her husband; maybe the last name was just a wild coincidence.\r\n\r\n<P>When I was about to give up my search, I found the entry that kept me going.  He said he was a single father.  He published his sonâ€™s first name.  I read the rest of the site, but didnâ€™t find any references to CF, but the fact he said he was a single father kept sticking in my mind.  I used his links, cross-referenced to his girlfriendâ€™s blog.\r\n\r\n<P>His girlfriend was a very poor writer, a chronicler of moods and vagaries, on-the-job bitches, and true love for her boyfriend.  But, suspecting that I was on to something I read her entry oeuvre.  After reading hundreds of entries I came to the entry that opened it all up to me.  One Valentineâ€™s day when the guitarist and his girlfriend had a special night planned, things went awry because he couldnâ€™t come over until CF picked up the son.  There I had it.\r\n\r\n<P>After I had confirmation I was able to put it all together, I could assemble a timeline of what CF had done after she left me, when she met this guy, when she had married.  She was divorced from the guitarist but had kept his name.   I stumbled across more entries about her, entries that vaguely insulted her, but claimed to be holding back because they respected her son too much.  I pieced together a ragged picture of CFâ€™s life.\r\n\r\n<P>For some reason, now, I still visit her ex-husbandâ€™s blog everyday.  I visit his sisterâ€™s blog, his girlfriendâ€™s blog, his best friendâ€™s blog.  I know more about them then I do most of my friends.  I canâ€™t stop consuming these lives.  Is this wrong?\r\n'),(3510,'The poems of my early-to-mid twenties Concerned a certain woman, named CF who, two years my senior, took take my virginity, a bitter wafer I was all to glad to have removed from my mouth.  \r\n\r\nIâ€™m not an obsessive type, or at least I didnâ€™t think so, but recently I did Google searches with CFâ€™s name, a uniquely spelled version of a common name, with extra letters, Hs and Ys where they shouldnâ€™t be, where nobody in their right mind would put them. Therefore, when I typed her uniquely-spelled name into Google, it came up, approximately 25 pages of digital record.  \r\n\r\nThe first thing I discovered was that CF had married and changed her last name.  I found a cache of live music reviews she wrote for a local L.A. magazine.  Even with the different last name, it still had to be her; such was the spelling of her name.  I went through the archives and read each review.  They were adequately written, but without much spark or creativity: lackluster, out-of-pocket praise of a bands instrumentation and stage presence.  In the end, the reviews werenâ€™t satisfying.  They were too outwardly focused, never said anything about the writer.\r\n\r\nAs I dug deeper into the Google findings, I found a web page devoted to a band she had joined after we broke up.  There was a photo of the band on stage and below the picture the caption stated the bandâ€™s lineup.  The guitaristâ€™s last name was the same as CFâ€™s new one.  Here was the man she had married.  I scoured this site, but nowhere found another reference to CF.\r\n\r\nMoving on, I sifted through handfuls of musicians websites that quoted from her various reviews.  But then I struck gold.  Her name appeared in somebodyâ€™s blog.  I read an entry that referred to CF as the maid of honor at a wedding and traced that blog back through the links until I came to the mother load.  Her husbandâ€™s blog.\r\n\r\nAt least I thought it was her husband.  This person had precisely the same name as the guitarist in the band photo I mentioned earlier.  But as I read through the entries, I couldnâ€™t find one that mentioned CF.  In fact, I found references to his girlfriend who had a different name than CF.  I kept digging though, slogging through this strangerâ€™s life.  I found out that he worked for UPS, that he liked to play hockey, that he brewed his own beer.  Not very interesting stuff.   Maybe this wasnâ€™t her husband; maybe the last name was just a wild coincidence.\r\n\r\nWhen I was about to give up my search, I found the entry that kept me going.  He said he was a single father.  He published his sonâ€™s first name.  I read the rest of the site, but didnâ€™t find any references to CF, but the fact he said he was a single father kept sticking in my mind.  I used his links, cross-referenced to his girlfriendâ€™s blog.\r\n\r\nHis girlfriend was a very poor writer, a chronicler of moods and vagaries, on-the-job bitches, and true love for her boyfriend.  But, suspecting that I was on to something I read her entry oeuvre.  After reading hundreds of entries I came to the entry that opened it all up to me.  One Valentineâ€™s day when the guitarist and his girlfriend had a special night planned, things went awry because he couldnâ€™t come over until CF picked up the son.  There I had it.\r\n\r\nAfter I had confirmation I was able to put it all together, I could assemble a timeline of what CF had done after she left me, when she met this guy, when she had married.  She was divorced from the guitarist but had kept his name.   I stumbled across more entries about her, entries that vaguely insulted her, but claimed to be holding back because they respected her son too much.  I pieced together a ragged picture of CFâ€™s life.\r\n\r\nFor some reason, now, I still visit her ex-husbandâ€™s blog everyday.  I visit his sisterâ€™s blog, his girlfriendâ€™s blog, his best friendâ€™s blog.  I know more about them then I do most of my friends.  I canâ€™t stop consuming these lives.  Is this wrong?\r\n'),(3511,'<p>\r\nHave you ever really, .. I mean, really,\r\ntried to answer the question: what is love?\r\nAt some point in your life, did you ever wonder\r\nwhether love is real, a myth, or just another\r\nword?  Is there a single answer to address\r\nthe immortal ponderings on the nature of love?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the past we created many ways of depicting\r\nlove.  Love is manifested in mythological characters, in novels where writers immortalize love through stories of young lovers ... love has been characterized in many ways, more than once, to be both tangible and intangible, as a synergistic phenomenon between two entwined souls.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Using the scientific model, others have tried\r\nto explore this further with objectivity in mind\r\nand rational tools at hand.  Crenshaw\\\'s \\\"The Alchemy of Love and Lust\\\" investigates the mystery of love and sex by understanding the chemical soup base .. the basic elements that drive human sexual and emotional behavior.  What about the argument of nature vs nurture?  How can chemicals, per se, serve as the fundamental basis for characterizing love?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Evolutionary theorists argue that the motivation for selecting a particular sexual partner stems from the tendency of the human species to evolve through natural selection, attraction towards another stems from the chemical soup base\\\'s influence in an attempt\r\nto choose the most eligible mating partner that could potentially reproduce a better offspring.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Survival instincts may have further encouraged the need to commune and stay together in order to feed and nurture the young offspring. It made sense during the days of sticks and stones. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Given today\\\'s highly complex economic, social and geo-political structure; the evolutionary and survival principles, in and of itself, as it applies to sex and love, appears to be obsolete.  Why?  Advancement of technology today has allowed man to manipulate DNA attributes for the purpose of reproducing a better human species.  The equal economic purchasing power of both male and female negates the need for dividing the tasks for nurturing, protecting and feeding the young.  A highly paid professional single mother can simply hire a nanny to nurture her child, purchase food in a convenient store, and afford a secure habitat to nest herself and her offspring.\r\nThe evolution of human society has evolved into a complex socio, economic and cultural interdependent system.  It has changed the basic premises and assumptions necessary to define the origins of sex and love. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>How can I fathom the essense of sex and love? Among others, it can be understood from a scientific method, from a systems method, from observing emergent behavior within a system. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have traversed through the cycle of falling in, getting bored, falling out .. of love.  I had my first kiss, I broke up, I was engaged, I broke up, I was a single mother, I broke up, I got married, while married I had a few lovers, I broke up, I got bored, I got divorced.\r\n\r\n<p>In every love affair I experienced, it seems like\r\nthe nature, the feeling, the concept, the chemicals .. of love was not of the same material or form.  Love has manifested itself to me, not as a single and consistent entity, but has presented itself as unique experiences each time.  Love did not have a face, nor a touch, it did not have a shape, nor a consistent form.  The attributes of love, for each experience were not the same, the feelings and intensity were different.  I sought to find out what exactly has been discovered to-date on this phenomenon.  The \r\nstories, conjectures or conclusions did not suffice to provide me a unified theory of love.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> My search through books and empirical experience has led me to conclude that love does not exist. It has no form, consistency or shape.  It defies rationality and therefore it has no logic.  The logic of love is nonexistent. Love in itself does not exist because it does not have a\r\nconsistent universal attribute.  How can we tell we are in love? We can\\\'t, with certitude.  It is the utopia of human emotions.  Have we dared fool each other all this time, to think that at point in time we were in love, ... or at one point in time, it is possible to fall in love? </p>\r\n\r\n'),(3512,'<p>Do we really need to have a soulmate? Where does the predisposition towards intimacy after sex, really come from?  What is the nature of love? Does it exist at all? Is it a just a term created for the sake of identifying a series of sensations resulting from the fusion of chemical elements flowing through the brain\\\'s synaptic receptors during a single moment of passion?  Why are we attracted to certain types of people, and not to all?  Why are there sexual hangovers, as opposed to having sex -> getting it done with a handshake and a smile..., next?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Attempts to conceptualize and understand sex, love and feelings have plagued thinking man since the dawn of his existence.  The answer to the question of love and sex has been exhaustively explored. The infinite quest has led to no succint answer.  It is as vital as questioning\r\nwhy we exist and what lies after our existence.  \r\nThe key to understanding the mystery of man\\\'s essence lies in the question of why we exist? why we love during our existence, and why do we cease to exist?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The question of existence and death, I would defer to the metaphysicians.  I challenge you with the question of love.  What is love? </p>'),(3513,'<p>I decided a year ago that sex, love and relationships is detrimental to my goal of achieving a rich and full existential life.  There are a lot of mysteries to be unraveled ... history, philosophy, physical sciences, political sciences, archaelogy, sociology, mathematics, art, literature... oh so much knowledge to be gained ! It is not worth paying the price of \r\nknowledge deprivation for a lifetime of boredom and routine, or a many single nights of orgasmic pleasure. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Suffice it to say that the young should experience the joy and frustrations, the magic and disenchantment, the sublime and divine qualities produced during moments of passion with another mate.  However some of us reach a point in the mating game, when you just wake up one\r\nday, gaze at yourself in the mirror, and ask, \\\"What is the point of it all?\\\"  Whether it is a boring marriage or a string of one night stands, the mystery of the beloved gradually deteriotes into the humdrum existence of stagnant relationships, betrayal, deceipt, and the ultimate\r\nrealization that underneath the mask of novelty, you have just unraveled another human body with all its frailty, weaknesses and hang-ups.  Those qualities in the beloved that has sent tingles through your spine has turned into the same qualities that gives you goose bumps.  The nihilism of love looms into your relationship\\\'s core and destroys the hopes of finding everlasting solace in his arms.  In doing so, it destroys my search for everlasting solace within my own existence.</p>'),(3514,'<p>Around the final years of my marriage, I had intense and passionate affairs with a very geeky Roman scientist and a very young British programmer.  Perhaps due to the lack of intellectual growth and stimulation in my marriage, it was a period in my life where discussions over Minksy\\\'s mind-hive theory or analyzing the structures of the Baroque architecture as it applies to the history of ancient Rome and the church, had an aphrodisiac effect on me.  In both love affairs, I was \r\na sexual goddess devouring the poor geek gods into a fantasy world they have not dared to imagine.  It must have been the affair with the Roman doctor where I almost believed that I have finally encountered my soulmate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Our minds have merged into this cosmic\r\ndance where mental and sexual intercourse were \r\nindistinguishable. I still remember the first kiss... our first kiss.  We were strolling through the Tevere one evening, in silence and awe absorbing the beauty of the Castel Sant\\\'Angelo.  He gently whispers the story of Tosca, of the great love of two lovers that\r\npreferred death over a lifetime of being apart.  Amidst the mystery of the aeternal city, he gently grabs my hand and with the other circles my waist and gently kisses me.  I submit without hesitation, I feel various forms of electromagnetic particles dancing inside of me.  The waves of electricity had elicited sexual desires, I felt moist, vulnerable and ready to take him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I am not sure what caused these illusions of love to fade like a passing cloud.  Was it the wrong timing, the distance apart?  Was the intellectual attraction not sufficient to sustain a potential relationship that could have successfully flourished?  I met him a few years after that evening in September.  We were sitting in a cafe by a modern art museum near \r\nthe Gianicolo.  I still remembered just feeling repulsed by him. I wanted to immediately go back to my hotel and explore the Roman night life by myself.  I am still perplexed as to how and why my feelings have changed.</p>\r\n'),(3515,'<p>Being single, free from the highs and lows of being in a relationship is an exhilirating experience.  I indulge myself on lazy misty afternoons of coffee at the Cafe Greco in North Beach while reading a book on the life of Che Guevarra. Other days, after boxing class, I aimlessly stroll through the Civic Center farmer\\\'s market to watch the vendors sell their fresh produce and search for the best priced eggs, mushrooms, tomatoes and flowers. The sense of freedom and aimless afternoons to one\\\'s self \r\nis a priceless experience.  Although there are evenings when a sudden surge of nostalgia overwhelms me.  I succumb to the void and loneliness, bury my head against my pillow and \r\nwallow into the slumber of restful sleep.  The next day, the rays of morning sun penetrate into my skylights, I gently rub my eyes, I stretch and wonder, is it a workday or do I have to go to the gym?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have a full set of friends to cater to the various aspects of my personality.  One group of friends are married, stable and conventional;  another set of 20something couples and boxing\r\nbuddies; a whole lot of friends from the high-tech industry, male, platonic buddies; and a handful of intellectual-minded friends whom I spend evenings watching a mind-intoxicating\r\ndocumentaries and discussing of everything until the wee-hours of the morning.  My work is fulfilling, one could say, though I would not deny the experiences of the ups and downs of corporate life.  Nevetheless, my books are the ultimate form of escape, in addition to sleep.  I cope with the world this way.  My own routine, my own chaos, I take full responsibility and answer to no one.  It is nice to reach a state where men, boys or silly crushes are of a foreign concept to me. This is the way my life should be.</p>'),(3516,'<p>That morning I jumped into my closet to check if I had a decent suit to wear for the day.  Working as a geek in high tech, I had no use for suits and business clothes. When I moved out of my house into my new loft, I threw my collection of Italian suits, and settled for boyish tapered shirts, albeit of the brand Sisley, Mango, and Zara.  I was lucky to keep a very hip Mango jacket that my girlfriend gave me.  I kept it for nostalgic reasons, today, I had the need to sport a business look.  I had a couple of business meetings and a presentation in front of over fifty geeks in some famous software development company.  Though I usually do not wear makeup, I was obliged to slightly paint my face, at least to boost my confidence, though I have prepared my materials the night before.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was just another geek fest.  I walked in with a sincere smile painted on my face.  I usually feel that I am an actor on stage when I attend these geek functions.  I love my high tech role, but I prefer to physically be situated  inside a lab or a cold data center and interacting via email or IM.  Face to face interactions at work where geeks and business people mix make me uncomfortable, so I play acting to survive occassions such as these.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Though everything was blurry and vague to me, I could not help but notice at the corner of my eye, a tall figure in jeans.  Somehow I felt his \r\npiercing stare towards me.  I think I remembered smiling back at him, as I did to everyone in the room.  This person followed me after the presentation into the breakout area.  He introduced himself, and I remembered noticing him, he had an international look, an interesting accent and a curious demeanor.  I don\\\'t remember what I felt after I left the building, but I know that he left a slight imprint in my mind.  Though I made a mental take note: he was attractive and sexy. I had to return that evening for the reception with another colleague.  For a while, he was nowhere in sight.  I was surprised that I even noticed he was nowhere in sight, why did it occur to me?  Did I look for him?  While sipping our drinks and chatting with other people, he suddenly appears out of nowhere and I think I felt relieved to know that he was there.  We talked for a quite a while, and I remember enjoying the conversation, until my colleague pulls me over to another group; only to leave me a few minutes later. I felt stuck and vulnerable. For the first time, I felt out of control and a little nervous.  I hastily concluded my current conversation, looked at him and in a sheepish way waved goodbye.  I felt I had to rush out of the door to avoid being alone with him.  I had a sensation of being scared but I did not understand what I was afraid of.  I thought of him on my drive back home.  His interesting accent came from years living abroad as an ex-pat.  He currently lives far away, in some exotic country where the people live with the rhythmn and breeze of its neighboring ocean.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had to go back to the geek fest the next day.  The thought of possibly bumping into him motivated me to wake up bright and early.  He was nowhere in sight during the meetings and I noticed the disappointment inside of me.  I did not admit it, but I knew I was disappointed.  At some point, I \r\nplanned to leave early in time to make it to my boxing class.  Each time the door opened, I hoped it was the tall bohemian looking guy in jeans.  As the time drew nearer to the end of the afternoon, I psyched myself to look forward to a great workout.  The door opens again, and there he was .. he walks right in, and everything around me froze, the background became blurred.  My eyes were lenses that immediately focused \r\non his image as he walked passed me.  He smiled.  A few minutes after he walks across me, he walks back and sits beside me.  I start to float \r\nin the air.  At this time I was not consciously observing myself or noticing that I was gradually being sucked into the trap of the vicious \r\ncircle of the mating game.  At the end of the evening we had a brief group discussion and someone suggested dinner in a nearby restaurant.  I knew myself, I was attracted to this guy, I should get out of here, now.  I respectfully decline dinner and half-heaterdly dragged myself, slowly, and out of the room. It gave him enough time to catch up, and repeat the words, \\\"dinner?\\\".  I was fighting with myself at this point, and I said, \\\"the city?\\\", and \r\nhe said, yes.  And that was it, I victimized myself!  I was the predator and I was the victim.  I was equally responsible for the subtle seduction, and I ask myself, why?  Why was I attracted at that moment in time, to him?'),(3517,'Pedaling the wet streets, itâ€™s fast or nothing fast or be damned, taking the tight turn onto Arlington street, sliding my bike between the parked cars and the traffic I try to duck my head below a truckâ€™s mirror but donâ€™t make it and my helmet slams into the ticky-tacky thing, shattering the glass, but by head unaffected, I pedal on, the crack of the mirror\\\'s plastic, the shattering of the glass, now in my short-term, something Iâ€™ll talk about tonight at the bar.  The superiority of the bike, of the body, victory of man-over-machine  Pedal, pedal â€“ keep the eyes alert â€“ pedal hard.  <br><br>\r\nFrom Arlington itâ€™s a broad left turn on to one way Charles Street, a funnel point for vehicles coming from three different avenues who step on the gas out of spite for bike couriers, the dull roar of their engines muted by the humidity, but ever-present behind you, at your side, in front, and you must be ready for the sudden lane change or abruput stop.  In Boston the way violate the direction of a one way street is to throw it into reverse and back up againt traffic, the thought processes seemingly being thus: <i>cops will think youâ€™re going forward because your car is pointed forward.</i><br><br>\r\n\\\"Donâ€™t use a turn signal because youâ€™re giving information to the enemy.\\\"  Cabs make left turns from right turn lanes, cut in front of oncoming traffic, use crosswalks as traffic lanes.  Bike couriers are invisible or, if seen, treated like bike riding street demons that rose up borne on the sewer gas.  In their eyes we are the spawns of the dark, sewer rats that smell little better than our greasy-eyed kin.  But you fly!  You fly!  Tower workers don\\\'t understand.  Those of you behind the wheel don\\\'t understand! Youâ€™ve got a dispatcher yacking in one ear, your eye on the traffic and a pedestrian will step off the curb, first instinct is to swerve, but be the roar of the engine you know that youâ€™ve got a SUV the size of a Kansas city mule at your elbow.  But you fly!  You fly!  Still, the SUV speeds up and cuts you off,  Fucker.  <br><br>\r\nThe only thing to do is slow it down, slow it down.  When the SUV passes, fall in behind it and in front of the BMW Coupe, your back wheel a scant 9 inches from his plastic bumper.   Bastard on a cell phone probably doesnâ€™t even see you, but fit it in there, slide to the side of the road.  Then.  Doors opening in front of you.  Potholes in the streets.  Dogs taking a shit in your lane.  Amatuer Riders coming right at you, against traffic.  Never fucking swerve into traffic unless you know what is or isnâ€™t at your elbow.  But don\\\'t worry if you\\\'ve been doing this for a while, after a few months your an old hat, a veteran.  If youâ€™re gonna die, though, gonna die within your first week.  Nearly half of Bostonâ€™s bike messenger deaths are newbies out to pick up a few bucks. So for those first few days, take it easy, don\\\'t push yourself.  <br><br>Being a dead bad ass isn\\\'t gonna impress anyone.'),(3518,'<p>We decided on sushi in my neighborhood restaurant. In restrospect, I wonder if we unconsciously chose \\\"my neighborhood\\\" restaurant,\r\nas it conveniently set the stage and mood for the evening.  We drove through the traffic stricken lanes of highway 101.  For a while I lost his trail, and I admit it crossed my mind to lose his car, to avoid the potential outcome of our adventure.  The voice of reason was faint at a time when I needed its conviction for I found myself maneuvering through the bumper to bumper traffic in search for his car.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Walking towards the restaurant, we engaged in casual conversation. Gradually, I was drawn into the evening without trepidation.  His soft-spoken\r\nmanner eased away all my qualms and anxiety. He was tall, lean with a well-defined body.  His faded tan complemented his rock star like wavy hair and the tight fitting jeans perfectly outlined his firm and perfect butt. I have never been one to admire a sexy and hot guy, but I was just in awe to see that intelligence and geekiness can exist in a beautiful body. I have preferred men with intelligence over those hot looking babes. Physical attributes were an option; there was a point where I arrived at a generalization that hot looking men were not mentally gifted.  \r\nThus, in the past I have always associated geek with mediocre or pathetic looking men.  Though, it is possible that my memory fails me tonight in \r\nremembering his looks, while I write to describe this man, whom I have spent an entire evening with, and has managed to cause a few days worth \r\nof disequilibria in my self-contained and stable world.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So what has transpired from sushi to the next morning that led me to take a quick side turn back to giggly-path?  There were too many events and subtle details crammed into a few hours within that single evening.  I don\\\'t even\r\nremember the small talk we had during dinner, we simply had fun.  My defenses were down, I was in my hood surrounded by the familiar faces of the waiters, bus boys and staff of the restaurant.  I was content to be with the easy going person beside me.  After dinner, we made our way into this new hip neighborhood bar.  The place was empty when we arrived.  We immediately checked out the place: the VIP room, the upper dining area, admiring the vertical space and layout of the joint; we were like two kids prancing about exploring a stranger\\\'s house, discovering its every detail. Later on, we moved to a secluded and cozy spot by the oriental fountain. We talked a lot, laughed a lot, touched a little, and suddenly a flurry of making out moves simply transpired. We explored each other physically,\r\nsubtly, gently, and in a strange way, very sweetly.  It was divine and I did not give a damm about anything else in the world, I was happy with him now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy chemical elements were definitely in active state.  Intensified by the brewing mix of our natural biological substances and a few glasses of wine, I was vulnerable.  The inevitable had to happen and I invited him back over to my place for a cup of coffee. How lame can that be?  Should I have just said, \\\" sex? \\\" ... No, I had to say, \\\" coffee ? \\\".  Why have we developed social protocols that tend to disguise motive in the surface, albeit intention is implied and clearly understood?</p>\r\n\r\n<p> The events that transpired after we hang out in the bar seem hazy to me now.  Was it because I was intoxicated when we left or simply because many things happened in a short span of time? Being with him was effortless. I am afraid to say, I felt like I have known him all my life.  (Aaaaaaaaaay!  Sofia, I cannot believe you said that!)  We walked out of the bar and chatted with the club bouncers, the people on the streets, carousing aimlessly until we reached my place. While inside my living room, my heart melted when he walked towards my bookshelf. He immediately recognized one of my favorite authors - Ayn Rand. He passed my basic litmus test! We had wine, talked, explored each other metaphysically and physically, we had coffee, played boxing in the kitchen (yeah, sparring, he had prior martial arts training), explored each other more. He slowly rubs himself against me, I turn around to gently kiss him, and together we voyaged into the unknown realms of passion. As we approached the peak of our rising crescendo, at that single instant, we were one.\r\n\r\n'),(3519,'<p>Have you ever experienced crawling into consciousness from the safety of peaceful slumber, and for a few seconds you are at a state of nullness. For a while your mind lurks in void and then gradually it finds words to ask who you are and where are you? I carefully opened my eyes the next morning and found myself in that very strange state. Upon realizing what happened the night before, I wanted to vanish back into nothingness.\r\nI felt confused, ashamed and uncomfortable when I realized that another person slept with me that night. I did not know what to do so I lay motionless and pretended to sleep while the rest of my senses were trying to figure out if he has left yet or not. I was unable to process anything, all I wanted was to be alone. The angel of sleep must have rescued me, carrying me with her wings and gently tucking me back into unconsciousness. I woke up an hour later and blankly stared at my surroundings.  I was beginning to process last night\\\'s events but the ringing of the phone disrupted me.  I never pick up my landline since they are usually intrusive telemarketers.  The answering machine picks up ... and a voice .. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Hi Sofia, this is Paolo, we had dinner last night? I drove back to your ....\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I did not hear the rest of his message, the phrase \\\"dinner last night?\\\" kept ringing over and over in my mind. Hello?  Dinner last night?  I thought I had an intoxicating, thrilling, frightening, sublime evening.  Dinner last night?\r\nI walked over to the machine to listen to the rest of the message:\r\n\r\n<p>\\\" i realized that i had your credit card from last night, you had uhm, given it to the bartender for our tab, i paid the tab and picked up your card ..\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh, yes I remembered leaving my credit card at the bar and he must have taken it from the bartender.  He called me several times after\r\nthat.  I did not answer the phone.  I don\\\'t know what I was thinking, but I just wanted to forget the whole thing ever happened.  Prior to leaving for work, I checked email and noticed a note from him: </p>\r\n\r\n-----Original Message-----<br>\r\nFrom: Paolo Maradonna [paolo@softwareco.com<br>\r\nSent: Friday, February 6, 2004 7:26 AM<br>\r\nTo: Sofia Valera [sofia@storageco.com]<br>\r\nSubject: Sofia: last night<br>\r\n \r\n<p>hi. I hope you got to work OK. I\\\'ve left you a couple of voice mails and stopped by your apt. I have your visa card from last night. As it turns out, I will be leaving tomorrow early so we should figure out how I can get it back to you. \r\nGive me a call or send me email. I\\\'ll keep trying to reach you. </p>\r\n \r\n<p>I had fun last night. You are indeed a very interesting person. </p>\r\n \r\n<p>paolo </p>\r\n\r\n<p>--------------------------</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Suddenly it hit me, he is going back to his far away home.  I don\\\'t know what I felt.  Many things, in one way I was relieved to remember \r\nthat he was in-transit, just passing through.  It makes it easier for me to label last night as merely a \\\"one night stand\\\".  Somehow that made it feel better overall.  On the other hand, I would never know, at least for that single evening, if he had felt the same intensity as I did.  I think I was sad, for sure I was perplexed, but I think I was sad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I cut through the inner streets of the city and zoom\\\'ed right into 101. I was to meet him before work to pickup my card. I was excited to see him again, even if it was to say goodbye. I wanted to find out how I would feel when I saw him.  I slalomed my way through the freeway determined to reach my destination.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>How can I describe that final moment?  I grapple to find the words to describe the strange melancholy feeling that overtook me when we briefly exchanged goodbyes, I took my card and wished him a safe flight. I wanted to throw myself into his arms and squeeze his body against mine.  I don\\\'t know if he felt the same way.  I guess I will never know.</p>\r\n'),(3520,'<p>hello<br>\r\nhi<br>\r\nhiya<br>\r\nI\\\'ve been trying to find you<br>\r\nhello<br>\r\nreturning your msg<br>\r\nGetting back to you<br>\r\nI\\\'ve missed you<br>\r\nlook closer<br>\r\nprivate pictures of me <br>\r\nlook closer<br>\r\nhello<br>\r\nOK, so we finally came out with it, ready?<br>\r\nCheck this stock out<br>\r\nTim wanted your approval on this.<br>\r\nOops, my dress slipped off.<br>\r\nGetting back to you.<br>\r\nYour check is waiting.<br>\r\nCan I tell you a secret?<br>\r\nCongratulations, you\\\'re a winner!<br>\r\nCan I tell you a secret?<br>\r\nlook closer<br>\r\nIs your money safe?<br>\r\nI love it from behind<br>\r\ngetting back to you<br>\r\nCan we talk about boosting your sexual prowess?<br>\r\nUpdate: please read<br>\r\n100% free access<br>\r\nBelieve it or not it works!!!! <br>\r\nMake her beg!<br>\r\nQuick & Easy Debt Consolidation<br>\r\nIncrease your size and performance now!<br>\r\nNeed a low-cost lawyer?<br>\r\nWant to lose weight while you sleep?<br>\r\nthere are less than 48 hours remaining <br>\r\nTiming is everything!<br>\r\nYour check is in the mail!<br>\r\nI\\\'m HOT NASTY AND READY!<br>\r\nFuck me harder!<br>\r\nShoot it in my mouth!<br>\r\nI\\\'m Hot Horny and Slutty!<br>\r\nI saw how you did her.<br>\r\nFuck me hard!!<br>\r\nThat was fast.<br>\r\nTiming is everything!<br>\r\nCan I tell you a secret?<br>\r\nYou can add 3 inches in 3 weeks.<br>\r\nEveryone approved!<br>\r\nBAD CREDIT OK!!<br>\r\nBills killing you?<br>\r\nGet your BIG 12 inch COCK RIGHT HERE!<br>\r\nLet the Government make you rich!<br>\r\nMore money in your pocket!<br>\r\n19 year old sluts getting nailed!<br>\r\nYou\\\'re not allowed to see this.<br>\r\nReally Raunchy Adult Viewing!<br>\r\nMaybe too uncensored for you?<br>\r\nOops my dress slipped offâ€¦<br>\r\nThese teens want YOU!<br>\r\nYou don\\\'t know what you\\\'re missing!<br>\r\nlook closer.<br>\r\nNasty sluts just for you!<br>\r\nCan I tell you a secret?<br>\r\nWhat\\\'s hot, pink, and juicy?<br>\r\nPussy getting pumped by carrots!<br>\r\nMaybe too uncensored for you?<br>\r\nHot and live!<br>\r\nNAKED FARM GIRLS!<br>\r\nYoung girl sucking horse dick!<br>\r\nI\\\'m sorry.<br>\r\nPlease forgive me.<br>\r\nAre you unhappy with your penis size?<br>\r\nDo you want larger breasts in six weeks?<br>\r\nSmile, free gifts are coming!<br>\r\nGET HUGE TITS IN DAYS LADIES!<br>\r\nSPENDING TOO MUCH ON PERSCRIPTIONS? <br>\r\nImportant update<br>\r\nWork at home and make great money!<br>\r\nUpdate: please read.<br>\r\nReduce monthly payments 25-50%!<br>\r\nUpdate<br>\r\nReduce monthly payments 60%<br>\r\nOffshore secrets revealed!<br>\r\nBoost testosterone 245%!<br>\r\nGrow your dick 3 to 4 inches!<br>\r\nNow is the time to refinance!<br>\r\nGet Paid to Shop and Eat!<br>\r\nGreat sex in a bottle!<br>\r\nNothing valuable is Free<br>\r\nWow, free cell phone!<br>\r\nAmazing mortgage rates!<br>\r\nReduce monthly payments 70%!<br>\r\nTreat me like the slut that I am!<br>\r\nChild custody battle?<br>\r\nExtra cash now!<br>\r\nFuck me harder!<br>\r\nYou know how I like it!<br>\r\nI saw how you did her!<br>\r\nYou want relief from those aches and pains?<br>\r\nFuck me hard!!<br>\r\nBills killing you?<br>\r\nFuck me harder!<br>\r\nlook closer!<br>\r\nUncover the truth about anyone!<br>\r\nFuck me now!<br>\r\nBeen turned down?<br>\r\nDo me now!<br>\r\nYou can meet your wife here!<br>\r\nYou\\\'ll profit if you read this!<br>\r\nHome alone tonight?<br>\r\nEliminate debt!<br>\r\nMake your traffic tickets disappear!<br>\r\nElimate fat!<br>\r\nShoot it in my mouth!<br>\r\nThe Ultimate in Hardcore!<br>\r\nI\\\'ve been waiting for you!!!<br>\r\nWhere have you been?<br>\r\nYES FUCK ME HARD NOW!<br>\r\nYOU ARE THE STUD I\\\'VE BEEN WAITING FOR!<br>\r\nhello!<br>\r\nhi<br>\r\nheya<br>\r\nhey<br>\r\nYour loan has been approved.<br>\r\nI love you.\r\n<br>'),(3521,'<p>I was searching, and lo, found mention of my handle here long after my last post.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLong ago, some people here told me to get an online journal.  So I went and did just that.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nI didn\\\'t quit swinney in disgust, sometimes I still miss it.  I just began to write in another place.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nAs said in the fairly recent post in egg\\\'s \\\"forts\\\", I am in San Francisco.  I was indeed fed some tasty treats by two lovely Swiss ladies on xmas. \r\n<p>\r\nI see le\\\'s mention of my morality, and wonder how she can define it as rigid when she\\\'s never really known me at all.   \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnyway, I\\\'m around.  Today being an exceptionally boring day off, I thought I\\\'d stop in a say so.\r\n</p>'),(3522,'<p>\r\nToday being an exceptionally boring day off, I discovered that someone ratted me out.  Yesss, I am in San Francisco.  I was fed delicious food by beautiful Swiss women.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor the record, I liked swinney.  I *still* like swinney.org.  I just took the advice of some people and got myself an online journal.  So far from quitting here in disgust, I honored swinney and the people I knew here by heading off and writing massive amounts of... er... stuff.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'m around.  It\\\'s easy to find me if you want to.\r\n</p>'),(3523,'<p>\r\nI\\\'ve been in SF, what, nearly two years now.  Most of that time has been, of course, dedicated to work.  And what a job.  I\\\'ve been shot at twice; attacked with knives, syringes, masterlocks on the end of chains, and even a bicycle tire.  I\\\'ve been punched and kicked.  It\\\'s all good clean fun here in the gutter.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy job has two major aspects.  I arrest people, and I conduct investigations.  Investigations are fun, they keep my mind busy.  Running around arresting thieves and the like... is not so fun.  It does however, give me a special view of this city.  (To be completely honest, SF is hardly the only city I work.  I also enjoy time in:  Oakland, Castro Valley, Redwood City, Pittsburgh, San Rafael, Antioch, and many more besides.)\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThat being said, this story is one of the more unique ones I\\\'ve lived through.  In a way, it wraps all my experiences up into a little package.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMy partner at the time was a huge black guy.  This is important to understand, because it makes what happened a little more amusing.  He had just gone to lunch, leaving me to work on my own.  Less than five minutes after he left, I watched as one of the largest black men I\\\'ve ever seen walked through the entrance.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis made me edgy, you see, because I\\\'d actually seen this man earlier in the day.  He\\\'d stolen some stuff, but my partner and I didn\\\'t have everything we needed to make an arrest.  This time, I promised myself, I\\\'d get him.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nSure enough, he quickly moved toward some electronic crap, jostled a little old man out of the aisle, and without so much as a glance around, he concealed several items within his jacket.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow I tried to imagine the possible outcomes.  I tried to select the best possible one, considering all the factors known to me.  That is to say I chose not to make an arrest, but simply to get the stuff back from the guy.  Armed with my trusty two way radio, badge, and my handcuffs, I waited for him to attempt to leave.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs he approached, I craned my neck in order to watch his eyes.  He was really big dammit.  I asked him to return the stuff.  I offered to let him leave.  He shoved me back.  (this is taking place in a very public place folks, so pay attention.  notice how well i control my temper.)  I spread my arms, and again asked him for the stuff.  This time he handed me two boxes (of four that he had concealed).  When I asked for the other two, he punched at my chest.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI sort of swiveled as that giant ham fist of his came at me, and I knocked his legs out from under him.  He dropped to the ground.  I began telling him to calm down, that he was under arrest, and that continuing to fight me would turn his crime into Robbery, a felony.  Man, he wasn\\\'t having it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nHe got up, and came for me.  So I again slipped off his line of approach and took him down.  I was actutely aware of the many people watching this happen.  I was most especially aware that this guy was at least twice my size.  Having taken him down again, and not having the option to just start beating the crap out of him, I mounted him and began the agonizing process of trying to lay a nice control hold on his wrist/arm.  Agonizing you see, because the guy was bloody huge, and strong.  Far stronger than I.  All during this time, I continued to ask him to calm down, submit to arrest, and not make a bad situation worse.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYah, right.  He wasn\\\'t having that.  \\\"I\\\'m gonna fuckin\\\' pull on you bitch!  I get up, you dead!\\\"  And so on.  I had doubts that he was actually armed, but you know... better safe than sorry.  I went the simple route and just put my weight on his arms.  Holding him down.  Then I heard this shrill, obnoxious, and vaguely feminine voice.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"You racist fucking pig.  Get off of him!\\\"  I swiveled my head to see what moron would say such a thing.  And sure enough, there\\\'s a mid-40s white woman, dressed in a sort of neo-hippy outfit.  Her face was livid with rage.  I fancied she was frothing at the mouth.  I could feel her hatred for me across the five feet seperating us.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWith this woman screeching like a loonie, other people approached.  Er..lots of other people.  Soon I was surrounded by a crowd of gawkers.  So the manager begins explaining what happened.  How I\\\'m a company officer, how I\\\'d been attacked while trying to prevent a crime, etc.  To which our intrepid neo-hippy replied, \\\"I don\\\'t fucking care, there\\\'s a better way to solve this!\\\"  followed by the requisite, \\\"You\\\'re a racist fucking pig!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFive minutes in, and I still can\\\'t control this guy.  I\\\'m getting tired.  And the neo-hippy has found a supported in the shape of a small white man in his late 20s.  Now they are both screaming at me.  When they are told that the police are on the way, they only grow more agitated.  Frankly, I was getting angry now.  I really hate being called a racist.  And I hate stupid people.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTen minutes in, the lactic acid buildup in my muscles is causing me frightful pain.  I still can\\\'t talk sense into the fool beneath me.  I can\\\'t control him.  And he\\\'s still threatening to do nasty nasty painful things to me when he gets up.  Then it happens...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI feel a painful scratch down the back of my neck.  Man, it hurt.  It drew blood.  I felt two pairs of hands on me.  Then I was standing, and the guy was standing and then running.  When I whirled around to see who\\\'d assaulted me, and put me in danger.  Yeah.  It was the neo-hippy bitch and her friend.  The woman ran, and before I could stop her, she got onto a bus heading north.  Seething, I grabbed the guy.  \\\"You dumb motherfucker, I\\\'m arresting you for assault.  Don\\\'t you know not to interfere in an arrest?  Huh?\\\"  He only smiled as if he were so very proud to stick it to the man.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThat\\\'s when the cops finally showed up.  They caught the big guy down in Muni.  He fought two of them so hard, they just beat him in the legs with their batons until he collapsed.  In disgust I hurled the young man with the irritating smile away.  Five minutes later, while I was giving my initial verbal statement, my partner comes up.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"What happenin\\\' Dave?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso I told him.  \\\"damn, I always miss out on the fun stuff!  But you my nigga Dave, I\\\'ll have you back next time.\\\"  We laughed together. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Al,\\\" I told him, \\\"I\\\'ve only been a nigga for a few minutes, but already I hate those white assholes.\\\"  That managed to get more laughs.  Even the cops chuckled.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThat same week, I was attacked by a junkie wielding a dirty syring.  I stepped into his reach, wrist locked him and tossed his sorry ass down.  He had a record of such things, but the DA doesn\\\'t like to prosecute people with AIDS.  (he also has HEP-C)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI helped my partner fend off a 15 year old who decided to fight him off with a skateboard rather than submit to arrest.  Someone had told him we can\\\'t use force to arrest minors.  Stupid stupid people.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI chased a drunk Irish lad into a crowd of what turned out to be his friends.  When I armlocked him, they all turned on me with threats and began pushing me.  My partner ran to the resuce, and together we dragged my prisoner into a locked room.  That\\\'s when the crowd of drunken wannabe Irish kids stormed after us, smashing things and scaring the hell out of the little old ladies.  Ahhhhh... thank you St. Patrick.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nSo it comes down to this.  San Francisco is a beautiful city.  But you\\\'ve got to have a certain amount of money to enjoy it fully.  Unless you manage to run in social circles that somehow distract you from what\\\'s happening at the level of the gutter.  I\\\'ve seen it all, and in short time.  I don\\\'t have dancing, sex, booze, drugs, or much of anything else to take my mind off of it.  Every day that I live through what will be another story, I get a little more bitter.  It\\\'s not all tolerance and joy and love out here.  It\\\'s mostly hatred, anger, death, destitution, back alley blowjobs, stepping in shit, lies, and the walking dead.  San Francisco is a crummy place.  It\\\'s also wonderful, even to me, sometimes.  \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3524,'What is the fastest and easiest way to shake out the memories of a forceful and synergistic encounter that sparked the flames of veiled emotions; a brief and steamy rendezvous between two kindred, passionate souls?  How does one treat a sexual hangover? Why am I plagued by some sort of romantic malaise that seems to bear the same symptoms experienced by victims of unrequited love?  Why am I experiencing this absurdity for a stranger that I hardly know, for he, who I will never see again? The deluge of sense experiences have somehow impaired my ability to act within the bounds of reason; its powerful energy has shaken the very foundation of my existence; it has lifted my body from the surface of the earth and defied the fundamental \r\nlaws of gravity.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI shook myself: \\\"Wake up from this madness, Sofia! ... can\\\'t you see, you are dramatizing a simple fuck; for god\\\'s sake it was a mere sexual handshake! He is so not worth it, he is not that gorgeous, anyway.\\\"  And there it hit me, he is so not worth it! No one is so worth it, worth my lamentations, my wasted unproductive evenings of repeatedly rewinding the what-if scenarios of non-existent possibilities.  The revelation led me back turning to the right juncture; straight back into the arms of reason.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThose streams of endless questions plagued my soul for days to come. The profound absurdity of that mental exercise has once again brought\r\nme back to questioning why we love during our existence?  I challenge myself, to seek within the depths of my cold stone heart; what is love?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nDo I really have the audacity to define the nature, the essence, the \\\"thing-in-itself\\\" of an abstract concept called love?  Love has manifested itself in many ways and forms.  Do I dare to search for a unified theory of love? Is it possible to define a unifying theory that could lay the basic premise to define love and sex as it impacts human behavior?  What drives a person to be attracted to one and not to all?  Why do we have the need to be intimate after sex; why do we hope to find a soul mate?  Although I would not deny that there is a powerful force that drives the mating game; a force that is compelling to influence the choices one makes where the desire to have sex is mutually selective, and sometimes mutually exclusive.  I am not sure whether man can ever develop a robust and elegant unifying theory of love. Robust, to the extent that its premises are irrefutable and unbreakable; elegant, to the extent that it can be universally applied to all cases of love \r\n(ie., romantic, patriotic, parental, etc.).  As I further contemplate on the nature of love, I remember one great man\\\'s ponderings, very long time ago, as he delved into the nature and essence of gravity:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n\\\"It is inconceivable, that inanimate brute matter, should without the mediation of something else, which is not material, operate upon and\r\naffect other matter without mutual contact. ... ... Gravity must be caused by an agent acting constantly according to certain laws; but whether this agent be material or immaterial, I have left to the consideration\r\nof my readers.\\\" <br>\r\n- Isaac Newton - Sir Isaac Newton\\\'s Mathematical Principle of Natural Philosophy and His System of the World, trans A. Motte and Florian Cajori\r\n(Berkeley: University of California Press, 1962) Vol I, p.634 <br>\r\n<br>\r\nCenturies later, Einstein\\\'s general relativity elegently identified the agent of gravity, as the fabric of the cosmos.  Is it possible for the invisible force of love, that is not of a material substance, to cause a great attraction between two human beings that drive them to act upon each other?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nPerhaps it is time for me to burn the midnight oil, in search for the unbreakable theory of love.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n....<br>\r\n<br>\r\nYou may ask, how can one recover from the aftermath of unrequited love?  What is the quickest way to eradicate the concocted hopes and fantasies created by one\\\'s minds and false expectations?  There are many ways to mend a broken heart.  However, the fastest route is to face the adversary and feel the pain to a \r\npoint of exhaustion.  With one\\\'s eyes closed, a deep breath, dive deep down into the bottomless pit of despair and allow the tragedy to flow into every vain of the body, into the core of your heart.  And there one will find an appalling, almost beastly image of the once-esteemed beloved.  When pain and the self becomes one, suddenly an air of indifference soothes, numbness prevails and then you realize, I have hit rock bottom!  There is no further down; the only other way, is to rise back up, cleansed, freshed; \r\nanew.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nDriving through Columbus Avenue towards downtown is one of the most romantic scenes in San Francisco.  The Italian cafes and shops align perfectly along each side, while old paesans hover in groups sipping their espresso, reading the pink pages of â€œGazzetta allo Sportâ€.  With passionate gestures, almost shouting, expressive faces, they spend the afternoon arguing vehemently over their favorite calcio team, whether it is Juventus, or Lazio or Roma that is currently leading the Seria A tournament.  Right before the City Lights bookstore, I glance to my left and wave at Maurizio, the Steps of Rome Trattoria manager, who is preparing for the nightâ€™s dining patrons.  It is always comforting to see the stable elements of my very romantic city; to know that there are things that are predictable, that remain the same.  As I pass through the Vesuvioâ€™s, and the Coppola building, grey clouds and fog appear in strokes across the sky; the pyramid encroaches my view. The city lights begin to light up; defining the contour of San Franciscoâ€™s skylight. I feel exhilirated and very happy.  I just experienced a moment of romance, of joy, of love, with myself.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI glance over to the passengerâ€™s seat and notice that my son has fallen asleep.  He always falls asleep when we are driving.  Well, I guess it is a long drive from my parentsâ€™ place in Ocean Beach, where he stays some days of the week, back to our place in the city.  The next light turns red, and I gently caress his hair.  I watch his beautiful expression as he peacefully sleeps.  I smile at myself and admit, Sofia, love exists \r\nin all its splendor, in all its glory.  Here I am, touching an entity, a product of love.  This boy was generated from a moment of ecstasy, extreme passion, and that passion lives; love resonates in him.\r\nI have experienced an epiphany that did not address my search for the unbreakable theory of love, yet, that will suffice for now.  I am suddenly filled with vibrant joy and passion; the beautiful city lights of San Francisco heightens my experience;  I marvel over the romantic moment I am spending with myself, â€¦ with my son by my side.  I look forward to other romantic evenings in solitude, with my beautiful friends, with my son, with myself.  I love myself and I love life; although I may be a mere speck of dust in this unfathomable universe, I will take the risk \r\nto love, to love in an effortless, synergistic and rhythmic fashion. It is futile to search for the unbreakable, Iâ€™d rather spend my moments living. Perhaps one day, I will take a chance again, to allow the fabric of the cosmos to shake my foundations and lead me into the realms of uncertainty.  Not today, maybe one day. . . One spring day, perhaps, the perfect love will appear, out of nowhere, walking through the horizon, towards me.<br>\r\n'),(3525,'Today I recieved this in my spambox:\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<pre>\r\nHello,\r\n\r\n\r\nI am creating a web directory, The-Insight.com, and would like to include \r\n<b>your website Swinney.org</b> under the <b>\\\"health/chinese medicine\\\" category</b>. \r\nIf you\\\'d like to be added, please follow this url: \r\n\r\n<A href=\\\"http://www.the-insight.com/add.cgi\\\">http://www.the-insight.com/add.cgi</a>\r\n\r\nWe shall put all our efforts into having your link up in less than 24 hours; \r\nand if you find our site useful for your visitors, please add a reciprocal link. \r\n\r\nLink Back : <a href=\\\"http://www.the-insight.com\\\"> The-Insight.com </a> - A \r\nSpirituality Web Directory.\r\n\r\nThank you very much,\r\nJames T. Monaghan\r\nwebmaster \r\n</pre>\r\n</blockquote>'),(3526,'I come out, sit & see, smoke some.<br>\r\nWet sweat. These towers, beasts of beauty.<br>\r\nlit, lighted, the beast of man<br>\r\nbuilt by god, my friends.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s so sick. Im so small,<br>\r\nmajestic height of fall<br><br>\r\n\r\nI might as well, now<br>\r\nI might as well.<br>\r\n'),(3527,'<p>i\\\'m mid sleep, the imaginary strains of a song</p>\r\n<p>- you seem so out of context, in this gaudy apartment complex,a stranger with your door key explaining that im just visiting, and im finally seeing, why i was the one worth leaving -</p>\r\n<p>filtering into my dream. (im putting the song on now, to set my mood, okay? okay)</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m dreaming of something to do with the guy with creepy eyes on the \\\'dont drink and drive\\\' ad. he has a wheel brace in one hand, and is zipping and unzipping his jeans with the other. i think he wants to hurt me.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m still asleep. thinking of the article in the newspaper about some kids in a car accident over new years. they all died except for one passenger who was trapped. upside down. nobody found their car for three days. you can bet your ass that kid is six degrees of separation from the kid he was before. six degrees. maybe seven. fuck, i hope that never happens to me (how do i make sure that never happens to me?) i\\\'m living it then. upside down. crusted in dried blood and piss-stench and flies and -</p>\r\n<p>i think i scare myself awake at this point. there are effeminate screams coming from the lounge. my sleep brain and waking brain attempt a feeble meshing of selves. waking brain wins out and propels my legs obediently to the bedroom door. hands to the door frame.</p>\r\n<p>EMERGENCY! EMERGEN..oh GOD ! </p>\r\n<p>the screaming gives way to silence and quiet sobbing sounds.</p>\r\n<p>i peek out and am greeted by my boyfriend, sitting cross legged on the floor, looking akwardly over his shoulder at me with an expression of what i can only describe as pure sadness. his body has the kind of frozen rigor one gets when a large bug with too many legs lands on their bare skin.</p>\r\n<p>his voice returns slowly, a pitiful homersimpsonesque \\\'getitoffgetitoffffgetitOFFFF!\\\'</p>\r\n<p>it is then that i notice the cascading splash of poo.</p>\r\n<p>i look from the end of the poo trail to the squirmy baby he has pinned down with his clean hand. hmm! from baby to mat to shoulder to length-of-arm to thigh-to-knee to the two feet of carpet behind him</p>\r\n<p>sleep brain and waking brain both instantly agree that this is Funny and collapse both of my legs in laughter while baby and father look on helplessly</p>\r\n<p>i continue to laugh. the stale overnight air has a slight fruity tang, complementing the vibrant pumpkin-soup poo-hue so artfully decorating my lover.</p>\r\n<p>we clean up. me in stitches, he in ashamed fury, the baby blissfully unaware of everything. he turns the shower onto \\\'nuclear\\\' and disappears. i attempt to get baby settled for a little more sleep.</p>\r\n<p>but</p>\r\n<p>she has other ideas, oh yes. </p>\r\n<p>gaseous emissions (which would be the envy of any late night blokey booze-n-beans session) pour forth, making a mockery of the cute animals adorning her nappy. sleep brain sighs and hands over the reigns to wake brain again. i put baby on the change table, get clean clothes and wipes and cream and.. yeah exciting stuff..</p>\r\n<p>anyway, i take the nappy off cautiously, and because im really short-sighted, the light is lowered, and im still not awake, i bend down to eye level. squinting, i go to wipe her clean and it gets me right in the EYE.</p>\r\n<P>i have POO in my fucking EYE</p>\r\n<p>its like all the worst jpgs of stileproject and rotten.com rolled into one. i am so awake now i think my heart has stopped. i claw feebly at my eye with a scented huggies wipe, hoping the absorbent bunny designs are everything the packet claims they are. then, i stop to listen...</p>\r\n<p>the shower is still running, i think im safe. </p>\r\n<p>no one ever has to know about this. </p>\r\n<p>its our little secret, okay? okay.</p>'),(3528,'A kept secret is heavy, corrosive like acid. It corrodes your brain until it finds a way out.\r\nThe heavier the secret, the more it should stay hidden. A heavy secret is also the one that burns your brain faster to try to get out.<br><br>\r\nThe secret gets out by telling. Telling a secret is like giving a weapon. You would rather give a weapon to a friend, since you know he won\\\'t use it against you.<br><br>\r\nIn turn, the given secret will start corroding your friend\\\'s brain... until your friend finds another friend. The friend of your friend might be your friend as well.<br><br>\r\nA lead-like heavy secret pierces people like a bullet. Few can hold it very long without burning to tell it away.<br><br>\r\nAs the secret travels fast, and gets farther from you, it eventually reaches people who are not your friends. Hopefully, they will be far enough from you so that they don\\\'t even know who you are. Your secret will in that case have become a <i>story</i>.<br><br>\r\nIn the worst cases, the secret might randomly travel through people and suddenly bounce back at you. You get hit.<br><br>\r\nHow can a secret be back after such a journey? It can if it carries your name. Suppose your name is engraved on the bullet. A story is an anonymous bullet. A secret with your name on it becomes a homing bullet. Do not carry a secret together with a name.<br>\r\n'),(3529,'<p>\r\nI woke up slowly today.  Somewhere inside my head, a voice was asking me, \\\"Are you sure you\\\'re going to be in a good mood?  Because if you\\\'re not, just fucking go back to sleep.\\\"  Last night, before I went to bed, I promised myself I\\\'d be in a good mood today.  Today, I swore, would be a good day.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nEventually I decided that yes, I\\\'m feeling positive.  I\\\'m going out there today to make the most of things.  I skipped breakfast as is the norm, did the shower part, the dress myself part (ah, to have someone to dress me, what a joy that would be!), and walked my arse right out of the aparment.  So it wasn\\\'t beautiful outside, but I did have my voter information pamphlets to read on the bus.  Things were looking good already.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>  \r\nThe wheels on the bus...  I read my pamphlets, made some preliminary choices, and looked forward to lunch.  One at work, I smiled as I said hello to my coworkers.  Something, they no doubt thought, is decidely odd about Dave today.  And so they were correct of course; I\\\'m not one to demand a positive attitude from myself very often.  The day would be good, I just knew, because I have a good book to read by an author who fascinates me.  It would be good because I\\\'d eat something tasty from my friend\\\'s restaraunt, and read my lovely new book.  It would be good because I like the people I work with (mostly), and my boss is working his ass off to make sure I get promoted again.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIf I possess any one skill, it\\\'s probably looking at people and figuring out whether they are any good.  As far as fitting into my life that is.  I certainly can\\\'t tell when a day will be pleasant.  Pleasant-day-prediction is a talent I absolutely do not own.  More\\\'s the pity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy first (and as things turned out, only) arrest of the day was a twofer.  Two juveniles.  They are both a blessing and a bane.  Bane because no one likes to babysit kids while waiting for angry parents or police to show up.  Blessing because parents have a high percentage of payment of restitution, and that makes me look good.  (Also, some kids are really pretty neat.  I met a girl from Afghanistan once, and she was really interesting.  Just made a mistake.  That\\\'s what being a kid is about, right?  Then there was the girl who spoke four languages, and seemed to have memorized almost everything Albert Camus ever wrote.)  Fastforwarding a bit...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nS1 (that\\\'s Suspect #1), had a bad attitude, and offered some very strange and contradictory stories.  After some agonizing, I decided to turn her over to the police.  While we waited, S2 was released to a very obnoxious mother, and passed out of my life.  It took almost an hour, but eventually the police were arsed to come deal with things.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThat, of course, is what ruined my life.  I\\\'m being glib of course, it only ruined my day.  The two officers who responded to the call... They don\\\'t like me.  They seem to hate that I\\\'m white.  That I\\\'m a man.  And that I force them to do their job sometimes.  My instinct when they show up, much like the junk dealer\\\'s on the corner, is to run and hide.  I can\\\'t however, since for all intents and purposes, I\\\'m the boss. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow look, I tried to be nice.  To be apologetic.  I tried to be helpful.  I\\\'m not such a bad person you know.  Look, I write poetry!  Look, my best friend at work is black!  Look, I speak in simple declarative sentences!  No.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am a bad bad BAD person.  My industry exists solely to wreck the peaceful days cops so enjoy.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo they ridiculed me.  In public.  Not only in public, not only in front of more than 50 people, but in earshot of the suspect.  I pleaded, they ranted.  I spoke, they yelled.  Finally, I collected myself.  Fuck you I thought.  I\\\'m not a fucking piece of shit junkie to be treated this way!  (And frankly, junkies, as much as I usually despise them, should not be treated as I was either.)  I stood up straight, and made it clear that I was tired of this.  They got very, very angry then.  And when I stood my ground, they called their Sargeant.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThree.  Count them folks!  Three fucking hours later, we actually got around to having the police speak with the suspect!  The poor girl probably had to piss, she was probably going mad with boredom!  I was in a rage.  I called my boss, and we plotted.  Things are going to being happening now.  A law abiding citizen who makes a legal arrest with good probable cause should not, repeat:  NOT, be called a piece of shit.  Sorry if you disagree, wait, no I\\\'m not.  I didn\\\'t deserve it, and I\\\'ll gladly call anyone who says I did outside and do something to them that will earn me some nasty names.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe end result is my day was wrecked.  I was so angry I could hardly see.  The remainder of the day was spent documenting and researching every instance where I, or a coworker has been subjected to abusive language or behavior by the SFPD.  And now that I\\\'m home from work, I have the distinct displeasure of writing a report based off the notes I spent four hours taking.  *sigh*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLet\\\'s see.  I\\\'m being sued by someone I was friends with, and later got fired for doing things that one should not do.  My partner, or former partner I guess, is about to be fired for his outrageous web based stunt, trying to force a policy shift.  I have to interview like 25 job applicants.  (Man, that\\\'s the worst.  These people seem to crawl from under rocks, with their stories of being arrested for domestic abuse or how they like to smoke weed.  Dumb fucks!  Maybe this industry is wrong for you, you know, if you wanna get high.)  I\\\'m constantly on call to go downtown to speak with our legal counsel about an ongoing case.  My poor boss is going crazy, and getting all depressed, and he\\\'s about the most stable person I\\\'ve ever known.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen you get right down to it... I love my job.  No matter how often I try to tell myself I hate it, I love it.  It\\\'s the challenge I suppose.  Or maybe I\\\'m just so unhappy, such a miserable fucking bastard, that I can\\\'t help but adore anything that ruins my days.\r\n</p>'),(3530,'<p><img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" height=\\\"2659\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></p>'),(3531,'<p><img src=\\\"merchant_overseer.jpg\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" height=\\\"2659\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></p>'),(3532,'<p><img src=\\\"merchant_overseer.jpg\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" height=\\\"2659\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></p>'),(3533,'<p><img src=\\\"merchant_overseer.jpg\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" height=\\\"2659\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></p>'),(3534,'dad. dad. dad. yes, it\\\'s true, i resent him. loathe him at times, but still, can never escape him. want to run screaming whenever he enters the room. but no, have to sit, listen to every condescending word that pours from his mouth. my mind constantly races with ideas of how i can untangle myself from him. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni want to move to some far and distant land where i don\\\'t have to hear his voice, see his face, where everything he has ever said or done is just a faint memory surrounded by a haze. that\\\'s my dream that will never become a reality because my mind is filled with all his words. they are like the soundtrack to my pain. every word stabs at me like a thousand needles and there is no way to stop the pain. even when he\\\'s long gone, dead and buried, i won\\\'t forget. <br><br>and it\\\'s this that makes me so cynical and bitter about everything in my life and everything that goes on around me. it\\\'s like i\\\'m going to be stuck in this black hole for the rest of my life, sinking deeper and deeper without any way of pulling myself out. this is what makes me want to scream.'),(3535,'<P>I hate golf. I hate golfers. And golf clubs. And golf courses. I hate nearly everything that pertains to the game of golf. But, for some reason, I love golf balls. I love their smooth dimpled surfaces. \r\n\r\n<P>When I was sixteen I went to a driving range and, after hitting only four balls, I stole the bucket of 36 remaining golf balls. That was twenty years ago. I\\\'m 36 now, and I still have those golf balls. But, here\\\'s my quandary. Should I go to another golf course and steal another 36 balls? If I don\\\'t have more golf balls than 36, will I live past another birthday? With another 36 balls, I could make it to 72. That would be a good age. \r\n\r\n<P>I never expected to make it to 36 --I was a drunken and suicidal teen-- but it must have been the golf balls that did it. \r\n\r\n<P>The bucket of golf balls presently sits on the mantle of a fireplace which doesn\\\'t work. My apartment is a chopped up version of what must have been a large South End apartment.  The grand and beautiful mantle, carved from heavy marble, mocks the blocked-up flue. Back in its day what fires must have blazed here! Now it is useless. If I built a fire in this fireplace the smoke would back up into the apartment and asphyxiate me. If I broke through the blockage in the flue, the carbon monoxide would kill my upstairs neighbor. So the fireplace remains a sad place in my apartment, a stopped up reminder of the stasis in my life. My old girlfriend, who said that the fireplace was bad feng shui, bought four votive candles and placed them in the fireplace. Last winter we would light them and sip wine in front of our \\\"fire,\\\" but we since broke up. I haven\\\'t lit those candles for over 4 months.\r\n\r\n<P>In those last four months I have not only lost a girlfriend, but my job as well. It was a good job. I worked as a researcher for a public relations company. I spent the day calling journalists and asking them about the beats they covered and then wrote reports that profiled the individual journalists and the publications for which they worked. By my standards that was a good job. It was not to last.\r\n\r\n<P>My division was acquired by a multinational, which promptly laid-off the staff and moved the offices to suburban New Jersey. I received a severance which was more of a kick in the ass than a pat on the back. Now, every two weeks I fill out a card to reassure a government agency that I\\\'m looking for work. I answer the same three questions every week. In the last two weeks were you willing and able to work? Yes. In the last two weeks, did you actively seek work? Yes. In the last two weeks did you work? No. That\\\'s the way it goes. Yes. Yes. No. Yes. Yes. No. Yes. Yes. No. Yes. Yes. No.\r\n\r\n<P>This has been the last four months of my life. I don\\\'t even have to read the questions anymore because I know the order of the answers.  If the government agency were to suddenly change the order of those questions, hundreds of people would be unable to claim their unemployment benefits.\r\n\r\n<P>In college, money seemed irrelevant. I was an English major, reading the classics as a way of learning how to write. I didn\\\'t think about what kind of job I would get when I graduated.  I didn\\\'t make any real plans. \r\n\r\n<P>There was a guy named Gary in my Abnormal Psychology class --which I had taken to fuel my ideas for future characters-- who was also a Pastry Chef at the Ritz Carlton restaurant. Before class, Gary and I sat on the patio of the school\\\'s coffee stand, roll cigarettes with Drum Tobacco and light them with Swan Vesta matches. \r\n\r\n<P>Gary had beautiful female friends that sometimes sat with us. At the time my love life was otherwise occupied by an Irish girl named Mary, but it was still nice to sit on that patio with beautiful women, smoking hand-rolled cigarettes, gulping espresso and not thinking too seriously about the future. I was going to be a writer, even if I had to starve and scratch a living from meager jobs. \r\n\r\n<P>When I graduated, I took a job in the stockroom of a bookstore, which was just enough to keep a roof over my head and food in my stomach. Even after I moved across the country, more of those kinds of jobs followed. \r\n\r\n<P>At one job, all I did was run a Xerox machine. I turned in 17 hour days without writing at all. I had less than $100 in my savings. \r\n\r\n<P>Eventually, one thing led to another, and I found the well-paying researcher job I mentioned above. Now that it\\\'s gone, and with my savings accound rapidly shrinking, all I can think of is money. How am I going to pay rent when Unemployment runs out? How can I maintain a telephone bill, a gas bill and an electric bill? Why didn\\\'t I take college more seriously, learn a profession, become a lawyer? A doctor? I recently heard that a childhood friend had become a doctor. He is doing post-graduate studies at Johns Hopkins. He was always more practical, a man of science, where I was fanciful. All I know how to do is write. \r\n\r\n<P>And to think I could have been a pastry chef.\r\n\r\n<P>To some degree I feel that I\\\'m a writer by default. I am a writer because I am not a travel agent. My dad especially wanted me to be a travel agent. I think he liked the idea of free travel. As a kid, my best friend\\\'s older brother became a travel agent, and his parents regularly took free trips to Bermuda and Greece. \r\n\r\n<P>I do not know why my dad didn\\\'t want greater things from his son. Some parents want their kids to be doctors or lawyers. More enlightened parents may hope for a college professor. Parents who are dreamers dream of their son as the President of the United States. My father wanted a travel agent. \r\n\r\n<P>Sometimes I dump the golf balls out of the basket and count them as I toss them back in. There are still 36 balls. The number has not changed. I don\\\'t have a TV, so, when I don\\\'t feel like reading, I count the golf balls. The balls are smacked up and dinged from their life on the driving range. \r\n\r\n<P>If I were to rescue another basket of balls, I could put it right up on the mantle next to this one. Logically I know that golf balls cannot extend one\\\'s life, but that doesn\\\'t make me any less afraid in my 36th year, each damaged ball a symbol of a year from my past. This yellow one with the surface scratches is 1992. The white one with the red stripe and a gash is 1986. \r\n\r\n<P>Today, I could not effectively steal a basket of golf balls as I no longer have a car. Back when I took these it was easy to slip them into my car and make a getaway. Now, I\\\'d have to walk down the street with the basket and get on the bus.\r\n'),(3536,'A while ago in the desert, a tattooed                   boy                 named                 Happy was walking along, minding his own business. Happy looked and dressed like                 Elvis Presley.                 Suddenly, he saw Bevis, who was                 homely                 and looked a little like                 Donald Duck.                 Bevis proceeded to steal a bald boy\\\'s shoes. The boy\\\'s name was Phil.<br><br>\r\n   \r\n \\\"Stop, you                 bad person!\\\"          Happy yelled out. But Bevis started to run away.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHappy chased Bevis                 for 3 miles.                 Bevis could run fast and seemed to be getting away. But then in a final        great effort, Happy          told Bevis to slow down                 and surprisingly caught the scoundrel!<br><br>\r\n\r\n Phil was so happy, that he          jumped with joy.  <br><br>             \r\n<hr><br><br>\r\n\r\nA long time ago                 in                 Hong Kong,                 a                 thin                                              girl               named                                                       Marge was walking along, minding her own business. Marge looked and dressed like                 Mother Theresa.               Suddenly, she saw Bevis, who was                 homely                 and looked a little like                 Adolf Hitler.                 Bevis proceeded to                 smash               a                 bald          boy\\\'s                 shoes.                          The boy\\\'s name was Elvis.<br><br>\r\n \r\n\r\n \\\"Stop, you                 stupid punk!\\\"          Marge yelled out. But Bevis started to run away.<br><br>\r\n\r\n    \r\n\r\nMarge chased Bevis                 for a long time.                 Bevis could run fast and seemed to be getting away. But then in a final        great effort, Marge          took a shortcut                 and surprisingly caught the scoundrel!<br><br>\r\n\r\n    \r\n\r\nElvis was so happy, that he          kissed Marge.    <br><br>           \r\n'),(3537,'<p>it\\\'s wanting to write something purely for the pleasure of it, for the first time in months.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the tingling in your fingers that makes it hard and yet oh so pleasurably trippy to type.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the doof doof doof that you will hear for days.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the hugging of sweat-soaked strangers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the artificial empathy that is anything but fake.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the amazing reassuring rush of something as simple as a writers\\\' website coloured shades of grey, where you know there are always beautiful creative people.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the total lack of hostility to the one you nearly married, the one you wish had a better life, for her little girl\\\'s sake.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the safety in knowing that that part of your life is over.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the certainty that many people are going to be utterly sick of reading the words \\\"it\\\'s the\\\" if they got this far, but feeling too huggly and warm to care.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the only pleasurable way to spend a teeth-grinding, ear-splitting legsinagony night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the rush of randomly distributing lemonade ice blocks to total strangers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the comforting feeling you get when you go to help someone up off the dance floor, and at least one other person is also trying to help.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s looking at physically beautiful people purely for the aesthetic, and not really caring if you go home alone.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s knowing that there are things in this world that george w. bush\\\'s cronies can\\\'t ruin, no matter how hard they try.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s wanting to take george w. bush out dancing and give him a hug.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s thinking of friends in other towns, and not missing them, just feeling their love from afar.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s loving my father unconditionally, even with all that we have done to one-another.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s thinking about writing to my sister and apologising.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s apologising to the homeless guy when he begged you for some of your beer - but you were carrying a can of sprite.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s thinking about going back and giving him five bucks so he could enjoy himself for just a little while, no matter what he did with it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s having the honesty not to claim that you did give him the five bucks, merely that it was a smiley afterthought.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s the total inability to realise that you have been prattling on for far too long.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is the etiennes and wendys of this world thay you should have caught up with for coffee much sooner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s thinking about people half a world away that are special to you in an escape-to-another-land-every-now-and-then kind of way. the le\\\'s and austin\\\'s of this world. the ones you want to meet but do not, lest you burst the bubble on their special place in your heart.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is, for the first time i can remember on a night like this, not a fear of the coming morning and the harsh clarity of day that comes with it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is knowing that yes, this is a feeling brought on by chemicals, and it too may pass.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is the bittersweet tingle of wanting to hug the world and cry.</p>'),(3538,'<pre>\r\nPOPSICLSHO: I caught a flounder once in Ocean City, MD\r\nleemantyler: neat\r\nleemantyler: thats good eatin\r\nPOPSICLSHO: i gave him back to mother earth\r\nleemantyler: i did too\r\nleemantyler: in my o