e that web fall. Fuck that bitch. Punch that bitch. Be that bitch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>See that gypsy whore grab my cock. Reach inside for my cock. See that pussy walk. Rub that pussy against my cock. Supersex that gypsy whore. Supersex. Supersex. Gypsy whores want my cock. Rub against me. Hands inside me. Hands inside my pocket. Fuck my cock or fuck my wallet. Gypsy whore ainâ€™t got no teeth. Make my cock bleed. Make my cock need. You love my cock gypsy whore. Suck my cock. Be my cock. Punch that gypsy whore in the face. Fuck that gypsy whore in her place. Fuck that asshole. Make it bleed. Fuck that gypsy whore. Be that gypsy whore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>See that fucker pissed on wine. See that fucker steal whatâ€™s mine. See that fucker kick that bitch in the face. See that fucker suck on waste. See that fucker kick that bitch in the face again. See that bitchâ€™s friends huddle in their den. Boot to the fucking face. Smack down. Beat down. See the gypsy whores show their tits. See that fucker. Be that fucker. I said beat that fucker. I said be that fucker.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>See that fucker in the eye. See that fucker bleed and die. See that fucker swing that bottle at your head. See that fucker good and dead. Duck down. Be down. Stop that fight. Be that fight. See that gypsy whore grab my cock. See me unable to stop. See my cum. See my blood. Be my cum. Be my blood.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>See that fucker kick that bitch in the face one more time. See that bitchâ€™s friends stand up this time. See that fucker get punched. See that fucker swing that bottle at your face. See that broken nose. See that blood pour down the staircase. See that gypsy whore grab that fuckerâ€™s cock. See that fight no one stopped. See that wine drip down my spine. See that pussy on tv. See those cocks make her bleed. See that cum cover her face. See that blood put blood in place. See blood. Be blood. Be blood. See blood. See that bitch scream in need. Be that bitch. Be that fucker. Be that gypsy whore. Be that pussy on tv.</p>'),(3540,'I start university in a week and a half. March the 1st. I\\\'m not sure how I\\\'m feeling about it. I\\\'ve had 3 and a half months of holidays where all I\\\'ve done is become completely involved in reading and surrounded by music. Sure, tv has popped in every now and then, along with various outings to the beach, but in general, i\\\'ve done nothing. I figured I deserved the break. Year 12 was the hardest year, academically, of my life. The pressure of end of year exams only heightened any feelings of anxiety and stress that I had. <br><Br>When my last exam finished on Novemeber 14 I felt a strange sense of freedom. Like my life was somehow changed forever. Now I see that it was just the release of a year full of stress and pent up emotion. I slept for the next week, waking only to eat and shower. The year had exhausted me that much. I was a zombie. Now my 3 months is up and the prospect of study is making my mind swim. The uncertainty of whether I actually want to continue study or quit and get a job is making me feel sick. Then there are the moments when I realise that it\\\'s just my pure lazyness which is propelling me to have these kinds of thoughts. I have wanted to study at university since I was in year 5. <br><br>Now that the moment has arrived the stage fright is making me doubt everything I have dreamed of. It\\\'s the fear of the unknown. The fear of failure. Now I take a deep breath and try to prepare for what is meant to be one of the most exciting times of my life (as people are constantly telling me). I think I\\\'m going to take their advice, let myself go and enjoy everything that comes along with the unknown and unexpected in the hope that this fear and dread that is rising in my stomach is actually just nerves and excitement. Who knows, maybe I will have the time of my life.'),(3541,'<p>Your eyes and your ears and your<br>\r\ntongue above all, sunlight on the snow<br>\r\nand the colors at dusk, lavender and<br>\r\nsalmon and eventually the palest blue.<br>\r\nThe creak of maple trees at night and <br>\r\nthe break of light each morning when the<br>\r\ncoffee\\\'s on and the dog is wriggling to go<br>\r\noutside.  The earthen spice of fresh coffee<br>\r\nand the bright, hapless bark of the dog<br>\r\nbecause he\\\'s ill-trained and his spirit\\\'s<br>\r\nnever been broken.  Your rituals and royal<br>\r\nablutions, the floral cool apothecary<br>\r\nscents of your lotions and shampoos, the <br>\r\nmorning foundation and straightening<br>\r\nof hair, the familiar motion of the brush<br>\r\nin congress with the dryer, the gel cleansing <br>\r\nat night, toning elixirs and moisture creams<br>\r\nand eye caviar for fine lines. The dog there <br>\r\nagain waiting to drink water from your glass <br>\r\nbefore bed.  The books that fill the in between,<br> \r\nand the sublime power of a perfect sentence, <br>\r\nwhich is enough to save us all if we let it.  <br>\r\nThe lyrics on the radio, at once trite but <br>\r\nenlivening, the yearnings for love and <br>\r\nthe power of goodbyes, a melody you hum later<br>\r\nin the day but cannot identify, every old<br>\r\nYeah yeah yeah and Baby it\\\'s you that crooned<br>\r\nyou to smiles and blessed an afternoon. The<br>\r\ncautious way you turn corners in the car<br>\r\nand break slowly approaching a stop. The<br>\r\npassing glance of a stranger that does not<br>\r\nseem so strange.  The sense of purpose <br>\r\nwalking down the sidewalk to the bank,<br> \r\nor down a grocers aisle at lunchtime,<br>\r\nand the small whim that strikes when <br>\r\nthe berries look fresh, or the bittersweet<br>\r\nchocolate\\\'s on sale, which gives way to a cake<br>\r\nthe morning after next.  The process of baking <br>\r\nand the satisfaction of its chemistry, the results <br>\r\nbrowned and risen as the goods of ancient ovens,<br> \r\nbut with a lightness only achievable with an <br>\r\nelectric mixer. The press of a foot on high-<br>\r\npile carpet and the graceful shape of a well-made<br> \r\nshoe, the kind you can\\\'t wear anymore but is still<br> \r\nlovely to look at for its architecture.<br>\r\nAn awareness of yourself in time that arrives<br>\r\nlike an extended float on water, neither quite still<br> \r\nnor in motion, but unable to sink as you may<br> \r\nhave feared on a cloudier day, the weight of rain<br> \r\nliable to change your buoyancy.  The rainy<br> \r\nSundays spent at home with old movies where<br> \r\nthe couple always gets together, stays together,<br>\r\nand loves each other effortlessly in good clothes.<br>  \r\nThe merciful loss of time that a movie brings,<br> \r\nthe lifting out of yourself that you\\\'ve always loved.<br> \r\nThe summer nights with a cocktail when there\\\'s<br> \r\nno work the next day and the crickets chirp<br>\r\nloud over the din of wind in the maple leaves.<br> \r\nThe featherbed down at the bottom of despair,<br> \r\na welcome place to lay your head for a few<br> \r\nhopeless hours before climbing up and out<br>\r\nto iron sheets and look at pictures of pastry.<br>  \r\nThe memory of a motorcycle ride when your hair<br> \r\nwas longer, with a boyfriend or your draft-dodging<br>\r\nbrother, and the moment you gave up on fear and loved<br> \r\nthe roar and the wind.  The roar and the love that<br>\r\nare with you still.  The pleasure of wearing<br> \r\nsomething new, seeing yourself again, redressed.<br>  \r\nThe taste of a rich sauce simmered with aromatics<br> \r\nand wine, the layered flavors of a wine perfectly suited<br> \r\nto the dish, the seconds after the first sip when the<br> \r\nbrain is beholden to the tongue, cuffed and dedicated<br> \r\nto the exercise of taste.  The mellowing that arrives<br> \r\nwith age, the softening of tannins that occasionally<br>\r\nfeels like progress.  The easy sleep that comes after<br> \r\na day of work, and the next morning, always the<br> \r\nnext morning, the dependable arrival of the light,<br> \r\nthe coincidences and tiny joys you can\\\'t help but<br> \r\nnotice because they never stop, no matter how<br> \r\nabsent they may have seemed just the day before.</p>\r\n'),(3542,'I\\\'m not too excited about this story but I just wrote it for an assignment and it\\\'s all true.  Please be amused at my jackassery and rest assured that I don\\\'t do this sort of thing anymore.\r\n<br><br>\r\n-------------------------\r\n<br><br>\r\nIâ€™m generally quite easy to get along with and smart enough to avoid escalating a tense situation.  The last time I was in a fistfight, there had never been a George Bush in the white house.  Iâ€™m quite forgiving and Iâ€™ve even been known to volunteer my time to rehabilitate sick baby seals.  Really.  In fact, I was on my way to the Marine Mammal Center in Sausalito on the day that I went uncharacteristically berserk and participated in a violent and useless public display of stupidity. \r\n<br><br>\r\n	It was January of 2003 and the morning commute traffic on the San Francisco Bay Bridge was surprisingly light.  With its low off-road gearing, my motorcycle was not pleased with the pace of traffic and, at 65 miles per hour, I was certainly not the fastest moving thing on the bridge.  I noticed something of the corner of my eye and I was surprised to look down and find a shiny black bumper only inches from my right foot despite the fact that I was in the left side of my lane.  The brand new Corvette passed me as close as it could without actually damaging its paint job and then, just to make sure I got the message, the driver waved his middle finger at me through his back window.  Apparently Mr. Corvette felt that 65 miles per hour was just not fast enough and that I was in need of a near fatal admonishment.\r\n<br><br>\r\n	It wasnâ€™t the finger that really pissed me off.  It wasnâ€™t the fact that he could easily have killed me.  It was his smug certainty that he could get away with it that released my inner psychopath.  If I were standing in a line somewhere this guy wouldnâ€™t just shove me out of his way and give me the finger.  Heâ€™d get in line and wait like everyone else.  Once heâ€™s in his little midlife crisis sports car, itâ€™s a whole different story.  Heâ€™s invulnerable.  He thinks he can do whatever he wants and nobodyâ€™s going to say a word.   Suddenly Iâ€™d become the vigilante offspring of some unholy union between Evel Knievel and Charles Bronson and it was my duty to disabuse this wingnut of his sense of impunity.  I revved the guts out of my poor little Honda trying to keep him in sight.  I saw him take the first San Francisco exit and followed him onto the city streets.\r\n<br><br>\r\n	I threaded through the congested traffic and found my target waiting for the light to change at an intersection.  I immediately began to spew an unending and mostly incoherent stream of obscenity.  The balding personification of evil inside the car responded with his own obscenities and a familiar hand gesture once heâ€™d recovered from his surprise at finding me next to him.  Rather than waiting for the light to turn green, as he had evidently intended to, he lit up his tires and kept them spinning as he took off around the corner.  Unsure of exactly how I wanted this thing to end but definitely not satisfied with the yelling Iâ€™d done so far, I took off after him.  Just after the corner, he stomped on his brakes.  As I sailed over the top of his car I considered what kind of worthless coward youâ€™d have to be purposely cause a motorcycle accident.  I cleared the car and landed on the pavement next to the carâ€™s left front tire.  I was on my feet immediately and well beyond angry.  I ripped the side view mirror off his brand new car and threw it on his hood.  When he tried to get out of his car, I slammed the door shut on his legs and kicked the crap out of the door hoping to break it and his legs at the same time if possible.  He managed to get all of his appendages into the car, squealed off down the street, and stopped near the end of the block.  For a moment, I regretted not letting him get out of his car and I hoped that he would return so I could beat him to death in the middle of the street.  Then I looked around.\r\n<br><br>\r\n	There were two pedestrians standing on the corner.  They were completely motionless, a woman in a business suit holding a tiny plastic phone and a guy in the ubiquitous blue shirt and khaki pants uniform of the San Francisco business-guy.  Their expressions were identical.  They were both staring at me with their mouths open and their jaws hanging as low as humanly possible.  My anger was replaced by embarrassment and shame as I now saw the entire incident from the point of view of an uninvolved bystander.  I had seen myself as a righteous avenger but what I saw now was two lunatics engaged in a meaningless violent confrontation in the middle of the street.  Corvette guy returned and, after a little more verbal incivility, asked if I wanted to get the police involved.  I figured that he would have been slightly more at fault but I also understood that the law wasnâ€™t going to have anything nice to say about my behavior.  I made sure there was no significant damage to my bike and then I told him to go.\r\n<br><br>\r\n	As it turned out, my motorcycle wasnâ€™t broken but my hand was.  I just didnâ€™t notice right away.  The following month of sitting around with my hand in a splint gave me plenty of time to contemplate my stupidity.  Sure, the guy in the Corvette was a jerk but I could easily have let him go and immediately ended my involvement with him.  Instead, I chose to escalate the situation.  If Iâ€™d paused to think for a moment, I would have realized that the most likely outcome of this kind of nonsense would be injury, jail, or worse.  Iâ€™ve always done my best to ignore rude people.  Since this incident, Iâ€™ve learned to ignore rude people in cars as well.  \r\n'),(3543,'<p>two categories.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>killing time:</b><br>\r\n\r\n- walk the long way to the bathroom. there and back.\r\n<br>- \\\"public radio\\\" section of itunes \\\"radio\\\"\r\n<br>- blogs.\r\n<br>- work on freelance projects\r\n<br>- rage at anyone who will listen on chat about how bad shit is\r\n<br>- walk the long way to the bathroom. there and back.\r\n<br>- shop online.\r\n<br>- laps around cube farm, to and from printer.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>good times to get a private moment to poo in the women\\\'s room:</b><br>\r\n- mid morning. everyone is actually working.\r\n<br> - during \\\"normal\\\" lunch hours. everyone is paying 10 dollars for a salad.\r\n<br> - sometimes there is a window mid afternoon but it\\\'s unpredictable. \r\n\r\n</p>'),(3544,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡ho he was and my family knows everything about me. All this time and all these feelings and no one in my outside life knew this was going on. Conscience motivates so many things in my life, and Iâ€™m a firm believer now, that a lie to yourself is an askinâ€™ for karma troubles.\r\nA few months after Tim left I got a call from Neil. Neil was someone from my past that I wished I had crossed the same lines with, but never did. He felt the same, back then, and we kept our dignity and uncompromised our integrity by accepting that we were not supposed to be together. I had not heard from him ever really and I cannot say why he called, except that he was thinking of me when he turned single. I was uncompromised to him. Little did he know that at the same time he called me, Tim became single and wanted me to move to Montreal. I chose Neil. \r\nI can say with honesty now that any relationship that begins as an affair is, in time, as good as dead and I never called Tim back. How do you expose yourself to situations of moral degradation and expect it to turn out happily ever after. I started dating Neil and have been with him for almost five years. The question I ask is would I have chosen Tim over Neil if I had not the influence of stability and honesty from my family. I think I would have sacrificed my integrity for him and this makes me furious. My family sees me as an honourable person and I grew up without question of this; being able to see the situation for what it was justify s this for me now. I still think of Tim from time to time, and imagine that he is happier not having had to deal with what I picture could have been a volatile and drawn out relationship with his infatuation. I know I am. \r\nq*q*\Zq*lq*'),(3545,'You\\\'ll start by putting on some music, <br>\r\nsomething motivational, Sleatter-Kinney say,<br>\r\nor maybe some old Michael Jackson.<br>\r\nSinging along word for word, you\\\'ll begin<br>\r\npicking up the pieces that have scattered,<br>\r\nthe entropy that surrounds the bed and<br>\r\ndesk.  A blip on the screen leads to an<br>\r\nhour on chat with seven people who <br>\r\nall wish you were nearer, back in Cali,<br>\r\nback in bed beside them on a Sunday,<br>\r\nback in the car getting high and <br>\r\nwaiting for the world to end.  Noticing<br>\r\nthe hour lost, you re-commit to the task<br>\r\nat hand, staring sadly at a pile of <br>\r\nwrinkled pages in your lap.  Ink words on<br>\r\none page link beautifully to some idly<br>\r\nscratched lines on another, a single<br>\r\nthread of human scrawl stretching<br>\r\nthrough the leaves of paper across the<br>\r\nfloor like a swimmer over lakes, to<br>\r\nthe box of paint, the jar of glass glitter,<br>\r\nand some artfully manipulated surfaces,<br>\r\ntextures alive as marsupial skins, color<br>\r\nless a method than a love, a means<br>\r\nto a visceral end.  A novel on the floor<br>\r\nasks to be opened, and the language<br>\r\nis so lovely that another half hour<br>\r\ndisappears and it\\\'s almost time for<br>\r\ndinner.  You strip the bed and this<br>\r\nfeels good.  Papers are put in piles,<br>\r\nseveral folders labeled, the world<br>\r\nordered thus and your anxiety abates.<br>\r\nPhone rings and an invitation for <br>\r\ndrinks in two hours.  You say yes,<br>\r\nyes because there\\\'s no compelling<br>\r\nreason to say no, yes because it\\\'s the<br>\r\neasiest word to say, yes to say yes.<br>\r\nLater that night, in bed with a boy<br>\r\nwhose hand on your skin is charged<br>\r\nwith precious neurons, your sheets freshly<br>\r\nwashed, you stare at the newly cleared<br>\r\nspace beneath the desk, a trapezoid of dust<br>\r\nframed and glorious in the dim light<br>\r\nfrom the air shaft (the palace at 4 a.m.),<br>\r\nand as the breaths on your shoulder turn <br>\r\nrhythmic, and a gust of night air slides <br>\r\ngently over bricks past the window, <br>\r\nrippling the panes, you\\\'ll be lulled by <br>\r\nthe marvelous mess, every sentient<br>\r\ndetail just as astonishing, just as rare.<br><br>\r\n'),(3546,'It took me a month to gain the courage to look at her for more than 3 seconds at a time and a month more to smile in her general direction. Six months later I am waking up to the sound of her breathing softly next to me. Just being near her makes my chest tighten and I canâ€™t help but smile at this. As I crawl out of bed I can hear her murmur incoherently. She does that as she sleeps. Knowing this is what astonishes me. Nine months ago I didnâ€™t even know her name and now I wake up beside her daily. I now know her habits, to the point that I know what sheâ€™ll do when she wakes up. Sheâ€™ll rub her eyes, sit up slowly and look around the room. When she sees me sheâ€™ll smile, saying â€œmorninâ€™â€ in a groggy voice. This is the part of the day that I cherish the most. Iâ€™ll walk to the bed, sit down and sheâ€™ll wrap her arms around my waist, resting her head on my shoulder. The smell of her hair will rise to greet me as I gently kiss her on the head. Weâ€™ll stay like this until she turns to look me in the eyes and smile. I never knew that being with someone like this could make my life so utterly different from what it used to be but at the same time the most powerfully emotional and satisfying time of my life. Sitting here, writing this while she sleeps is surreal, yet a reality that consumes me daily. Every day I am amazed at the little things she does. The way she runs her hands through her hair and hiccups when sheâ€™s excited. Itâ€™s the way she looks everyone in the eyes when sheâ€™s talking or listening to them. How I catch her smiling at me when she thinks Iâ€™m not looking. Itâ€™s when she hugs me and holds my hand as we walk down the street. The warmth of her body as she lays next to me. Little things like these make me realise how much I love her. I knew I loved her three months ago but couldnâ€™t say it until recently. I had never know a love so pure and strong. I was scared. When I told her this last night she simply smiled and said â€œI knew.â€ It still surprises me that sheâ€™s in my life, that in a few minutes sheâ€™ll wake up and our day will begin. I finally know what true love is.'),(3547,'<p> \r\nListen up!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am not a violent person. I do not agree with fighting, but when I am pushed, I am pushed. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nWe started off fine. we headed to a club called MOWONGGWOWON and I thought It was fun. There were cute girls and there was room to dance. The music was good, and I was just really comfortable there. \r\nBut it didn\\\'t please the people I was with. They wanted to go somewhere where the girls were taller and plentiful. They wanted to go somewhere busy. who am I to argue? \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe headed to club MB; a hiphop club. It was just so busy. OMG.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nIt was packed. SOOOOOOOOOOOOOO PACKED. I could not move. I had to piss and so I made my way to the bathroom. Inside there were all sorts of men styling their hair and taking up space. I yelled at them. \\\"ok, you look good. get the fuck out.\\\" they listened. There are few Koreans that wont listen to a six foot two fat man. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI pissed and felt good standing alone in the stall. It was locked and I sat down and tried to collect myself. I thought I did. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI moved back through the crowd. I found a couple of my friends and told them I had to go. It was just too much for me. I couldnt breath. I couldnt handle it. I made my way back to where we had stashed our jackets and bags. There was a korean man standing in the way. I tried my best to try and get him to move aside so I could get my jacket. He would not move. I tried again. I pointed to the jackets, motioned with my hands that I wanted to get my jacket and go, but he was just trying to show his girl he could stand up to a foreigner. I punched him in the face. I grabbed his hair by the ears and pulled him down and kneed him in the face. He fell. I stepped over him and took my jacket and turned around to find one of his friends there. There was a security guard there just watching. I looked at him and said \\\"I want to go home\\\". He understood, but the Korean friend did not care. He jibber jabbered some stuff to me, but it didnt matter. I kneed him in the balls and when he bent over from the pain kneed him in the face. He fell over. I walked past and the guard made room for me. On the way out I found my friends and told them I had to go. They didnt know anything of what went on. I didn\\\'t bother telling them. I just had to get out. I had to get out of that hell hole.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3548,'<P>Kyleâ€™s office was what the contemporary parlance termed a â€œcubicle farmâ€ and it possessed all the negative characteristics that the name implies.  All cosmetic attempts to make the padded dividers look like actual walls had failed and the relentless grey/pink walls retained a distinctive labyrinthine effect.  \r\n\r\n<P>Now, as Kyle wended his way through them, his heart thundering in his chest, beads of sweat breaking out at his temples, he was greeted by his workmates who lolled at the entrances to the cubes of their neighbors, drinking highly sugared coffee and chatting about their weekends spent â€œat the Capeâ€ or â€œin the mountains,â€ places for which Kyle would admit his hated only if pressed.  In the past, if somebody swigging their syrupy coffee/cream mixture appeared at the mouth of his cube and talked vacantly of their weekend getaways, Kyle merely nodded and grinned and talked the good talk that was expected of him, dredging up questions about <i>Samâ€™s Fried Clam Shack</i> and <i>Myrnaâ€™s Bed & Breakfast</i> from past conversations, not actual experience.  So, head down now, shoulders hunkered, feeling as if his thumping heart were a black hole sucking his bodily matter into a spiritual drain, Kyle attempted to get through the cubical maze with as little human interaction as possible, staring at the small-pile carpet under his feet and heedlessly plowing down the padded hall with barely a nod to this pinstriped suit or a faux-grin to that paisley tie, employing a non-verbal grunt or a rhetorical â€œHey, howâ€™s it going?â€ to transform the needy conversation of his coffee-slurping colleagues into a â€œHe must be having a bad Mondayâ€ response, providing the business casual automatons with only safe and non-engaging banter that would allow him to make it to the bathroom unmolested by the artificial friendliness of workaday banalities.\r\n  \r\n<P>Nevertheless â€” as always â€” the occupant of one cube has always been enough to slow Kyleâ€™s pace, stalling him mid-thought, arresting him in the middle of his busy day and redirecting his normally piss-poor attitude with a lighter touch, and so she does even now:  <i>Jenna Parks</i>. \r\n\r\n'),(3549,'<p>\r\nHi there.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nWhen you go to bed at eleven oâ€™clock it is difficult to sleep in on a Sunday morning. In fact, the only easy thing to do is wake up before morning and listen to the sun creep in. With practice, you can see the point where the sun would command the room and stir you from your slumber. There is a subtle but immediate change in energy flow. It is quite beautiful. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThree days ago it snowed. A freak march snowing. Ten centimeters in about three hours. The disaster started an hour before I was to leave work. I say disaster because I dislike snow. I donâ€™t hate it. Snow is beautiful from afar, but when I am trudging through it in my sneakers and baggy pants, my life becomes sodden. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThe natives loved it. It was something they donâ€™t quite see all that often. They were jumping all around the sidewalks catching flakes on their tongues, throwing chunks here and there. What a glorious site it was for them. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nAs usual I was standing out front having a cigarette between classes. My roommate came out to inspect the situation; his eyes twinkling with excitement. Heâ€™d entered the state of mind the natives were in.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI cringed at a snow flake melting on the back of my neck and decided to head back in. Just as I was turning around to go back inside the academy a student threw a ball of slush at me. It slid with pin point accuracy into my pocket. The shit almost killed my hand phone. When I say â€˜the shitâ€™, I am referring to the student; who scampered off into a bus as I muttered my way back into the academy.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nA week before the snowing I saw something else of great value. A serious Geumho Pussy Crew brouhaha.  I was standing in my kitchen making some tea when I heard some serious meow hissing. Letting my tea steep I opened up the window near the door and gazed out to see what the pussies were up to. There were about twelve cats on the street. I have no way of knowing, but I am thinking a bunch of playas were negotiating a limited supply of love. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI took a breather back to the kitchen to light a smoke and grab my tea. The situation had changed quite dramatically when I got back to the window. The group of twelve had split into two camps. One camp ran down the street off out of site. The group of five stayed put. Then just as I was thinking it was over, a cat darted onto a small ledge and onto the roof of some empty shacks. The other cats followed. The lead cat crawled into some sort of hiding spot, the others circling around hissing and flaring their tails at each other. â€œoh this is goodâ€ I thought to myself.  \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nAs I watched these cats quarrel, the streets remained empty. It was as if everything around me had stopped for this show to come on. You could see the hierarchy on that corrugated roof. The smaller cats sat and watched from a safe distance, while the two dominating cats hissed and spit at each other. Still there was no physical contact. It had been a twenty minute symphony of insults. As if it had all just been a waiting game, one of the cats stepped on something that wobbled and the other one pounced. A ball of screeching fur rolled around on the roof for about two seconds. The climax occurred when the two cats rolled off of the roof with some pieces off wood. The drop down from the roof into the side alley that leads down the hill is about two stories. The cats landed fine, but the wood made a very loud clatter. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThe noise brought people out from their buildings. An old lady who was coming up the alley was screaming something to another old lady. Apparently the piece of wood missed her by only a few meters. A good twenty minute drama unfolded with angry men knocking angrily on the doors of the building from where the wood came from. Luckily no one was home, and things died down. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3550,'I started thinking today what my life would be like if I didnâ€™t have close family connections. What if no one was interested in what I was doing? What if there was no one in my life to motivate good things? It makes me think that I would have made different choices in my life.  Everyone needs a person or persons around them to make them feel connected to something. What if I didnâ€™t have that? Iâ€™m not sure if I would be in school right now and I think I probably would have stayed with someone that treated me poorly and supported differing moral beliefs. What a compromise. Maybe I would have ended up on a talk show exposing my titties to show him I cared. Mmmm, maybe not. This leads me to think about Tim. <br><br>\r\nI met Tim in my first round of post-secondary schooling, and was really my first friend in a new city. We hit if off immediately and justified our friendship, by the fact that both of us were with other people. I think when you have so many things in common with someone and you need to spend time with them, exploring their intellect, you will inevitably face physical exploration as well. This is what happened to Tim and I. I was with someone who was arguably living through my goals and aspirations and he was with a girl, so different from him, Iâ€™m floored that they ever spoke to one another. I really felt like I could share anything with him, and only because I felt he could with me. \r\nIâ€™m going to go out of my way to state for the record that I have never cheated on anyone before this time and will never again. It was exhilarating, liberating, wonderful, and wrong. I donâ€™t regret any of it and I have grown from it exponentially. The problem with this situation is that I ended my prior relationship to be with him, but he never did the same. I would have dumped the other guy eventually, so I canâ€™t say Tim was the only reason why I cheated, but he felt he \\\"still owed\\\" it to his girlfriend of three years, to stay with her. Big red flag here. I wasnâ€™t looking for someone to marry or have kids with, I just genuinely enjoyed being in his presence. This relationship went on for about a year, including bringing his debt to my house, to \\\"show her that we were just friends\\\" and end any suspicions. I had to even endure watching them kiss and hug and then when she was out of the room, Tim expected me to respond to him like always. I am clearly the biggest loser for putting up with this behavioural pattern, but I spent most of the time with him, and she was only around once or twice a week. Note: It feels horrible to be the \\\"other woman\\\".<br><br>\r\nTim eventually moved to Montreal and I stayed to continue my schooling. (Iâ€™m not sure I would have done this had I not been thoroughly influenced by my family.) He would come down now and then in expectation of a quick romp, and I truly missed his friendship. I missed making music with him, I missed getting high with him, and I missed playing around innocently building up feelings that could not be contained. I was not I was in love but I missed everything about him. The reason why I bring him up is that my family never really knew who he was and my family knows everything about me. All this time and all these feelings and no one in my outside life knew this was going on. Conscience motivates so many things in my life, and Iâ€™m a firm believer now, that a lie to yourself is an askinâ€™ for karma troubles.<br><br>\r\nA few months after Tim left I got a call from Neil. Neil was someone from my past that I wished I had crossed the same lines with, but never did. He felt the same, back then, and we kept our dignity and uncompromised our integrity by accepting that we were not supposed to be together at that time. I had not heard from him ever really and I cannot say why he called, except that he was thinking of me when he turned single. I was uncompromised to him. Little did he know that at the same time he called me, Tim became single and wanted me to move to Montreal. I chose Neil. <br><br>\r\nI can say with honesty now that any relationship that begins as an affair is, in time, as good as dead and I never called Tim back. How do you expose yourself to situations of moral degradation and expect it to turn out happily ever after. I started dating Neil and have been with him for almost five years. The question I ask is would I have chosen Tim over Neil if I had not the influence of stability and honesty from my family. It frightens me to think I would have sacrificed my integrity for him and this makes me furious. My family sees me as an honourable person and I grew up without question of this; being able to see the situation for what it was justify s this for me now. I still think of Tim from time to time, and imagine that he is happier not having had to deal with what I picture could have been a volatile and drawn out relationship with an infatuation. I know I am. '),(3551,'I had a dream about a guy I worked with, last night. It was not erotic or wonderful, just strange and emotional. We were supposed to get married but nothing had really been planned even though everyone was already there. People were upset by the suddenness and there were so many loose ends. Something happened and I backed out, so did he and all was good, we agreed to be friends. Its weird how you have dreams like that and donâ€™t question things like, why am I marry this guy?, Iâ€™ve been dating some else for years. As it turns out I think I was a victim of television effecting what you dream. Yep, Iâ€™m not proud to say I was watching \\\"My Big Fat Obnoxious FiancÃ©\\\". Although Iâ€™m not sure how Scott scored that role cause heâ€™s not fat or obnoxious. On the contrary heâ€™s a really nice guy. (Yet I can think of a few guys that I dated that could fit that part perfectly).<br><br>\r\n I remember when he started working at the store. Scott was hired for installs and I was in sales. I recall mentioning to one of the girls I worked with that I thought he was attractive. He was not a GQ sort of guy, but he looked more like Hugh Jackman in X-men. He had different teeth, they werenâ€™t bad or offensive, they were intriguing and if you watched him talk he would expose them just enough that you could see his eye teeth were subtly pointed.\r\nI found out a few days later that Scott went to the same school I did and had the same internship after school as I did. For some reason I began to feel uncomfortable around him. I had graduated three years before him and I was experiencing anxiety over that fact that I was working in retail. What would he think of me for accepting such a low-end job after redeeming my diploma? This troubled me for a while and I avoided him at all costs, attempting to remain unnoticed. <br><br>\r\nDespite my best efforts I failed. I was at work bringing a sub box back to the bay, it was heavy and huge and just as I opened the door and was putting it down, and I wiped out. Not a trip, or a stumble, but a nasty uncontrollable fall. The kind when you know your face is all scrunched up anticipating the impact and as you fall, your limbs take on an unnatural series of motions, not landing neatly hands down but on your shoulder or elbow. They asked if I was ok and were straining to keep from laughing. I think getting up right after a fall like that is just as bad as the fall. Your face again revealing pain and shock and you donâ€™t quite have total control over your body. I tried to regain my composure by remaining on the floor, a flawless transition to sitting with my knees against my chest and arms wrapped around my legs; they had all seen it happen so I felt if I had jumped up right away without skipping a beat it would seem as though I was embarrassed, and I was. <br><br>\r\nOnce I got over that, the natural progression turned to the question why was HE here. Well, after chatting it up a few times I realized that we were both using our current jobs as a boost to something else we really wanted. I respected him for this and I think it was reciprocated. I find it quite frustrating thinking back to the days of highschool when we were all told that as long as you get a good education youre good to go as far as work is concerned. What a load of crap that is. I have friends who have masters degrees and BAâ€™s and are still working for less than 35000 a year. I just wish we would acknowledge the fact that it seems you have to sell out for an MBA before you have any control over the money you make. Maybe Iâ€™m just bitter about the fact that Iâ€™d have to move to the states for a \\\"good\\\" job. No offence but I like my country. It would seem I have gone through the motions of setting up a career and am being dumped at the altar by a saturated job market.\r\n'),(3552,'ÃÃÃ Â¡Â±\ZÃ¡. I had graduated three years before him and I was experiencing anxiety over that fact that I was working in retail. What would he think of me for accepting such a low-end job after redeeming my diploma? This troubled me for a while and I avoided him at all costs, attempting to remain unnoticed. \rDespite my best efforts I failed. I was at work bringing a sub box back to the bay, it was heavy and huge and just as I opened the door and was putting it down, and I wiped out. Not a trip, or a stumble, but a nasty uncontrollable fall. The kind when you know your face is all scrunched up anticipating the impact and as you fall, your limbs take on an unnatural series of motions, not landing neatly hands down but on your shoulder or elbow. They asked if I was ok and were straining to keep from laughing. I think getting up right after a fall like that is just as bad as the fall. Your face again revealing pain and shock and you donâ€™t quite have total control over your body. I tried to regain my composure by remaining on the floor, a flawless transition to sitting with my knees against my chest and arms wrapped around my legs; they had all seen it happen so I felt if I had jumped up right away without skipping a beat it would seem as though I was embarrassed, and I was. \rOnce I got over that, the natural progression turned to the question why was HE here. Well, after chatting it up a few times I realized that we were both using our current jobs as a boost to something else we really wanted. I respected him for this and I think it was reciprocated. I find it quite frustrating thinking back to the days of highschool when we were all told that as long as you get a good education youre good to go as far as work is concerned. What a load of crap that is. I have friends who have masters degrees and BAâ€™s and are still working for less than 35000 a year. I just wish we would acknowledge the fact that it seems you have to sell out for an MBA before you have any control over the money you make. Maybe Iâ€™m just bitter about the fact that Iâ€™d have to move to the states for a â€œgoodâ€ job. No offence but I like my country. It would seem I have gone through the motions of setting up a career and am being dumped at the altar by a saturated job market.\r\rl\n'),(3553,'I had a dream about a guy I worked with, last night. It was not erotic or wonderful, just strange and emotional. We were supposed to get married but nothing had really been planned even though everyone was already there. People were upset by the suddenness and there were so many loose ends. Something happened and I backed out, so did he and all was good, we agreed to be friends. Its weird how when you have dreams like that you donâ€™t question things like, why am I marry this guy?, Iâ€™ve been dating some else for years. As it turns out I think I was a victim of television effecting what you dream. Yep, Iâ€™m not proud to say I was watching â€œMy Big Fat Obnoxious FiancÃ©â€. Although Iâ€™m not sure how Scott scored that role cause heâ€™s not fat or obnoxious. On the contrary heâ€™s a really nice guy. (Yet I can think of a few guys that I dated that could fit that part perfectly).\r\n I remember when he started working at the store. Scott was hired for installs and I was in sales. I recall mentioning to one of the girls I worked with that I thought he was attractive. He was not a GQ sort of guy, but he looked more like Hugh Jackman in X-men. He had different teeth, they werenâ€™t bad or offensive, they were intriguing and if you watched him talk he would expose them just enough that you could see his eye teeth were subtly pointed.\r\nI found out a few days later that Scott went to the same school I did and had the same internship after school as I did. For some reason I began to feel uncomfortable around him. I had graduated three years before him and I was experiencing anxiety over that fact that I was working in retail. What would he think of me for accepting such a low-end job after redeeming my diploma? This troubled me for a while and I avoided him at all costs, attempting to remain unnoticed. \r\nDespite my best efforts I failed. I was at work bringing a sub box back to the bay, it was heavy and huge and just as I opened the door and was putting it down, and I wiped out. Not a trip, or a stumble, but a nasty uncontrollable fall. The kind when you know your face is all scrunched up anticipating the impact and as you fall, your limbs take on an unnatural series of motions, not landing neatly hands down but on your shoulder or elbow. They asked if I was ok and were straining to keep from laughing. I think getting up right after a fall like that is just as bad as the fall. Your face again revealing pain and shock and you donâ€™t quite have total control over your body. I tried to regain my composure by remaining on the floor, a flawless transition to sitting with my knees against my chest and arms wrapped around my legs; they had all seen it happen so I felt if I had jumped up right away without skipping a beat it would seem as though I was embarrassed, and I was. \r\nOnce I got over that, the natural progression turned to the question why was HE here. Well, after chatting it up a few times I realized that we were both using our current jobs as a boost to something else we really wanted. I respected him for this and I think it was reciprocated. I find it quite frustrating thinking back to the days of highschool when we were all told that as long as you get a good education youre good to go as far as work is concerned. What a load of crap that is. I have friends who have masters degrees and BAâ€™s and are still working for less than 35000 a year. I just wish we would acknowledge the fact that it seems you have to sell out for an MBA before you have any control over the money you make. Maybe Iâ€™m just bitter about the fact that Iâ€™d have to move to the states for a â€œgoodâ€ job. No offence but I like my country. It would seem I have gone through the motions of setting up a career and am being dumped at the altar by a saturated job market.\r\n\r\n'),(3554,'<pre>\r\ni remember it as i choose to.\r\n\r\ni remember lying there\r\nstroking her hair from root to tip--\r\nseven years\\\' worth.\r\n\r\nno split ends, and she\r\ncouldn\\\'t stand the sight of it wrapped around her brush.\r\n\r\ni had to clean it off for her.\r\n\r\nher hair was three feet long,\r\nand got subtly lighter towards the end\r\nand was highlighted.\r\n\r\nas i ran my fingers through it, lying there\r\nwith the cat, too, \r\nupstairs in her apartment, \r\nisolated as astronauts--\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nceiling fan, refrigerator noise, \r\nmexican food, marijuana,\r\nposters of jim morrison\r\nand john lennon\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nstarting from the root,\r\nyesterday, last week, last year,\r\nseven years\\\' worth.\r\n\r\nit seemed like such a long time!\r\n\r\nbut it has already been six since then.'),(3555,'<pre>\r\ni remember it as i choose to.\r\n\r\ni remember lying there\r\nstroking her hair from root to tip--\r\nseven years\\\' worth.\r\n\r\nno split ends, and she\r\ncouldn\\\'t stand the sight of it wrapped around her brush.\r\n\r\ni had to clean it off for her.\r\n\r\nher hair was three feet long,\r\nand got subtly lighter towards the end\r\nand was highlighted.\r\n\r\nas i ran my fingers through it, lying there\r\nwith the cat, too, \r\nupstairs in her apartment, \r\nisolated as astronauts--\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nceiling fan, refrigerator noise, \r\nmexican food, marijuana,\r\nposters of jim morrison\r\nand john lennon\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nstarting from the root,\r\nyesterday, last week, last year,\r\nseven years\\\' worth.\r\n\r\nit seemed like such a long time!\r\n\r\nbut it has already been six since then.</pre>'),(3556,'I have been pre-heating for some time now. You may find yourself behind the wheel of a vehicle that is heading straight on for a dead end bridge. Mine is a slow and patient burn, examined, and analyzed to ensure proper and warranted distribution. You may even find yourself at the head of a very large desk directing hundreds of people and managing millions of dollars. It takes much to enflame me and I am proud to say that I have never burnt any issue in my life. You may find yourself alone. I have a very determined and monitored temp sensor. You jeopardize this.<br><br>\r\n\r\nRegrettably you find yourself justified, and I find you pathetic.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Throw your hands in the air if you think youâ€™re a PLAYER!\\\"'),(3557,'Stealthily weaving my cart through crowded aisles, I came upon the housewares and cleaning products section. As I strolled past the air fresheners, I remembered that the spray can we have at home was feeling rather depleted the last time I used it.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nScanning the rows of brightly-colored metallic cylinders, I noticed several hand-written signs on neon orange paper taped to the shelves. There were four of them, three of which said things in variants of Spanish (or should I say Latin?). One was in English. \\\"DO NOT SPRAY.\\\" I can understand the logic behind the store\\\'s request. It\\\'s like picking a handful of grapes out of an unpurchased bushel in order to \\\"try them out\\\". But with scents named \\\"Mountain Rain\\\" and \\\"Tropical Breeze\\\", how is the consumer supposed to know what they smell like? Does a tropical breeze actually have a distinct odor? Coconuts? Salt water? Tequila-riddled vomit? <br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Okay\\\", I thought to myself. \\\"I\\\'ll respect their wishes. Besides, there are two store employees stocking the shelves not even 15 feet away from me. That would almost be insulting to put them in a predicament where they\\\'d have to uncomfortably ask me to stop when the signs clearly say, in a variety of languages no less, not to do so.\\\" But I\\\'ll be damned if bizarre mental images didn\\\'t immediately spring into my head. Images of me nonchalantly spraying excessive amounts of aerosoled particles high into the air as the employees to my right stared blankly... spraying it under my armpits, shooting it in my mouth to give it a taste test... <br><br>\r\n\r\nAm I the only one who has sudden flashes of utter stupidity? Did my insanity slip out of the bag again? Anyway, I got the Tropical Breeze and it doesn\\\'t taste half bad.\r\n'),(3558,'it is easy to see why grey \r\nis the smallest colour \r\nwhen you wake up at the end of march \r\nto closed-in sky, \r\njust as woolly and ill-fitting \r\n(it won\\\'t cover your body no matter \r\nhow much you tug) \r\nand just as harsh on the softness of your cheeks \r\nas the old blanket your husband \r\nrefuses to throw out. \r\nthick icy sharp layers \r\nof snow on the ground! \r\nit seems outrageous. \r\ninappropriate. \r\nblasphemous. \r\nhaving to wear \r\nlong johns the day after \r\nthe spring equinox! '),(3559,'So there is a woman that I pass on my way to and from the subway. She sits in the same place cross-legged against a phone pole, quietly accepting spare change and offering tattered copies of an outreach newsletter. I donâ€™t notice.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHer face is aged and drawn, and she seems unhealthy and soiled. She never makes eye contact and maintains an emotionless expression, occasionally pulling in her lips and squinting when change is haphazardly tossed into the can that sits next to her. I didnâ€™t realize until today how much I can gage my daily mood based on how I react to her.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor example: When things are chaotic and I am stressed, I find I am overwhelmed with frustration when I see her. \\\"Why canâ€™t she get a job? Who does she think she is expecting people to support her laziness?\\\" Not a politically correct response, I know, but Iâ€™m being honest. <br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I am feeling low, I imagine that she is actually quite rich, but her family refuses to institutionalize her, so they let her do what she wants during the day. <br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I am happy, I want to help her up off the ground and haul her into the cafÃ© across from where she sits and watch her devour anything she likes from the menu.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I feel lost, I picture her as an unseen prophet of sorts; maybe I will be the only one to know that she is really there to save everyone from their sorrow. <br><br>\r\n\r\nToday I am tired, and I see her purely as the street being she is. It seems she is fixed to that place as much as the pole she rests against. I have never once contributed to the can she carries, and always find myself looking away when I pass her. I guess if I donâ€™t look, no one can scold me for blatantly refusing the few coins that float around in my pocket on a daily basis. I think on my way home today I will offer my smile, and add to her takings.<br><br>\r\n'),(3567,'from the twenty second storey, the buildings downtown glow <br>\r\nlike the forgotten embers of a once roaring fire, it takes my breath away, and I imagine that the whole world must<br> dream of being consumed here  <br><br>\r\n\r\nthe view I have looking out dares me to follow the tracks that force <br>\r\ntheir way in, <br>\r\nbut itâ€™s so much nicer to watch the city breathe from afar <br><br>\r\n\r\neveryday that passes tucks away another memory I have of you. \r\n'),(3560,'Dear C-\r\n\r\n<p>Itâ€™s been over 10 years now.  Where did the time go?  Iâ€™m sorry if this letter finds you unwilling to hear me, though I would understand if that was the case.  The last time we spoke, it was not in the most favorable of terms.\r\n\r\n<p>Nonetheless, something still compels me to write.  We spent less than a year together, and thought I donâ€™t know what memories you keep of those months, I still think fondly of them.  So perhaps Iâ€™m writing merely to repair the bad picture of myself with which I left you: a whining, sniveling man who didnâ€™t have the sense to just straighten up and move on.  \r\n\r\n<p>I guess rock lyrics have something right in that you never forget your first love.  As much as it surprises me now, thoughts of you still drift into my mind.  And with those thoughts comes a deep sense of regret, a recollection of what a child I was, and how desperately wrongheaded most of my actions were when our relationship ended.  If I didnâ€™t write this letter, I would feel that I left something very crucial undone.  \r\n\r\n<p>It is impossible and useless to catch up, to piece together the patches of separate lives lived.  But Iâ€™ll fill in a few details.  I no longer live in California, I left the West Coast in 1994 and have been living in Boston, Massachusetts ever since.  In the next year my wife and I hope to move to New York City.  \r\n\r\n<p>Thatâ€™s right, my wife.  Her name is E.  She is a fashion designer.  Weâ€™ve been married now for 4 years.\r\n\r\n<p>Currently I work as a writer and editor for a high tech research firm in downtown Boston.  I have an office on the 17th floor with a window that overlooks Post Office Square, a small urban park in the middle of the financial district.  Sometimes I wonder how I got here from the snot-nosed leftist that I used to be, working smack-dab in the middle of American capitalism and making a living at it.  Funny where life takes you.  I canâ€™t claim to be in any way more conservative than I was back then, but practical things have won out and today I understand what it means to work for a living.  Real life isnâ€™t all tea and roses, though it certainly seemed like it was back then.\r\n\r\n<p>In fact, Iâ€™ve worked a variety of jobs since I came to Boston.  I worked in a copy shop, first running a machine and later as a quality control supervisor.  I worked as an office manger for an architecture firm, a legal secretary for an insurance firm, an editor for an art magazine, and a researcher for a PR firm.  I was a temp, a software tester, a telephone operator, a file clerk and a receptionist.  Iâ€™ve answered phones for travel agencies, wrapped client gifts for financial companies and much more.  In a way, Iâ€™m still searching for my perfect place in life, but then again, who isnâ€™t?\r\n\r\n<p>E and I live a good life together.  Weâ€™re both â€œsort-ofâ€ vegetarians.  That is, we eat fish, but no other kinds of meat.  We used to live in the middle of the city, but getting an apartment in Boston that is big enough for both of us, and Eâ€™s business is impossible.  Rents in the northeast are higher than almost anywhere else in the country.  We go out to dinner two or three times a week, go see some music at least once a month, and catch a movie or a play whenever something interesting comes out.  We donâ€™t have a car, or a child, so the bare expenses are relatively cheap allowing for a pretty good lifestyle otherwise.\r\nWhen I came to Boston, I left everything behind, almost literally.  I brought one suitcase full of clothes and that was it.  Now I have stacks of books, a substantial CD collection, but other than a few notebooks next to nothing remains from my life in California.\r\n\r\n<p>I am different now, yet I am the same.  Itâ€™s hard to sum up what that means, but I want to say it now.  I have new concerns, new thoughts, new interests.  But I am the same.  I would say that throughout life we only change a little, and weâ€™re lucky if we have the wherewithal to do that, for most of us remain the same throughout our live.  I am only a little different than I was when you knew me. \r\n\r\n<p>I guess what Iâ€™m saying is that somewhere along the line I learned that people who were once close separate and will never meet again.  Paths diverge in the woods and former couples take opposite directions.  I was too young to understand this all those years ago.\r\n\r\n<p>I guess thatâ€™s it.  Iâ€™m sure there could be more to say, but I donâ€™t know what it is. \r\n\r\n<P>Best, Quinn \r\n'),(3561,'<p>\r\nI move through space, towards a destination that is not my own.\r\nMore than a year passes - my ass still screwed into the seat of the machine.\r\nCeaseless in motion, unseen wheels revolve while siren lights flash in the blackness.\r\nTime melts like Dali; the clock ticks away meaning.\r\n8 to 24 hours a day, 6 to 7 days a week, the shifts run together - spinning.\r\nUp the city, and down the city, I look for strangers at night.\r\nUnder hood, the V-8 runs hot and thirsty as rats and shadows slip back beneath dumpsters - hiding from the broad, orange face of the sun.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need 2,000,000 cents to get back to zero.\r\nI have the meter.\r\nAnd the meter turns: 20 cents a click, 160 cents a mile.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Two years.  At the end of this month I can finish repaying my student loan, or pay my taxes.  It\\\'s a cruel choice, really.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"How do you like the job?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"It all depends on who\\\'s in back.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Good line, hooker.  But how \\\'bout right now?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"I hate 9 out of 10 of these fucking people; I live for that 1 in 10.  We had our last real fire in 1871; I\\\'m telling you: it\\\'s time for another.  But seriously, on a good day the taxi is the best thing that I\\\'ve ever had; on a bad day things were still worse in the jail.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"You must see some crazy shit.  What\\\'s the wildest thing you seen?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Sex, violence or comedy?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Just tell me another story...ok?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I was downtown, near LaSalle and Randolph - or something like that.\r\nA well-groomed White guy in his 40\\\'s hailed me.\r\nHe\\\'s wasn\\\'t likely - statistically - to be a problem; so, I didn\\\'t pay much attention to him.\r\nHis trip was short, only a few seconds long.\r\nI pulled up to his stop - still inattentive - and reached over my shoulder for the money.\r\nHe put a bill in my hand.\r\nOnly then did I realize that something was wrong - because the bill was soaking wet.\r\nThe guy saw me pause.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Don\\\'t worry,\\\' he said, \\\'It\\\'s only blood.\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI looked down: it was blood.\r\nThe guy had several fresh knife wounds, and the mess was everywhere.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"What happened?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Well, I acted like nothing was wrong, and made change.  He tipped me.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"But what happened?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"I don\\\'t know.  I only see the middle of the movie.  He left in a red sport utility vehicle; I cleaned the floor, seat and door; five minutes later someone else was in back.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"This seat?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Yep.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Oh my God!\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Every fluid that can come out of a man or a woman has come out in the taxi, while I\\\'ve been driving.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"That is so gross!\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Better than solids.  Listen: Last night there was a group called \\\'OAR\\\' up at the Aragon Ballroom on Lawrence, near Broadway.\r\nWhen the show let out, four 18 year-old girls got in the taxi and asked for a ride all the way downtown, to a luxury residence on Michigan Avenue.\r\nIt should have been a good fare, but I knew that something went wrong during the trip.\r\nAs the girls walked away, I saw that one of them had a huge wet stain on the back of her pants.\r\nI put two and two together, broke out my flashlight, and checked the rear of the vehicle.\r\nSure enough, while her friends sat beside her, she\\\'d urinated on herself - leaving a puddle on the seat and floor.\r\nI stood dumb: not only had she done it, but she had failed to acknowledge it in any way.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[A week later, a second woman pissed the taxi.  When I started driving, I wouldn\\\'t accept dogs in the vehicle; now I do.  Yellow Snow Score: Dogs 0, Humans 2.  Enough said.]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy immediate practical concern was that I was out of action until I could go someplace and get the car cleaned up.\r\nI hit \\\'Not for Hire\\\' and drove off.\r\nThree blocks away, two obnoxious yuppie drunks flagged me down.  As a group, White children of privilege in their 20\\\'s are far and away the worst passengers.\r\nThis pair had liquor in their hands, on the street; they were going to be bad customers for someone.\r\nI shouldn\\\'t have stopped - but I did.\r\nThe yuppies sat right down, oblivious, and began soaking up the pee.\r\nI dropped one of them, and then made a second stop in Boy\\\'s Town for the other.\r\nBut before I could go \\\'Not for Hire\\\' again, someone else walked up to the taxi and grabbed a hold of the door.\r\nA portly gay man of sinister appearance...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"\\\'Sinister\\\' appearance?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Yeah, he was wearing a leather vest, a black velvet cape, and a monocle...\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"\\\'Monocle\\\'?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Yeah, like Colonel Klink from Hogan\\\'s Heroes.\r\nAnyway, there were a number of other empty taxis in the area, but it was as though the pee was calling to him, and I couldn\\\'t wave him off.\r\nWell, I dumped him and then turned around to get the car cleaned; but right away this lady in a bus shelter started waving at me.\r\nShe wanted a ride to the South Side, which was fine.\r\nAnd I didn\\\'t feel too guilty, because I was sure that the Colonel\\\'s cape had absorbed what the yuppies had missed.\r\nWe got to the South Side; she didn\\\'t have any money.\r\nWe hit a gas station; I dropped her, and doubled back.\r\nAs I headed north, up south Michigan Avenue, I saw something moving quickly in the shadows cast by the cars parked on the street.\r\nI slowed down to get a better look, and saw a small, happy face smiling back at me.\r\nIt was a Black midget, chugging and puffing as he hauled ass, on foot.\r\nHe had already run a mile from the housing projects on 35th, trying to make it downtown in time to hustle people leaving 2am bars.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Hey Big Man,\\\' he said, \\\'Free ride?\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo I let him in.\r\nHe stood on the back seat, bouncing up and down in a very high-spirited manner: boing-boing-boing.\r\nAnd it struck me that he was the only one to avoid the pee.\r\nThen as we passed the Marriott, he spied a crowd and squirted out the door before I could bring the vehicle to a complete stop.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"You\\\'re making this up!\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Nope.  That\\\'s a typical night.  Think: I do nothing but pick up random people after sunset, on the streets of Chicago.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Is it ok if I smoke?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Go ahead - light the car on fire.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Tell me more...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFour passengers rode with me: three to the rear, one to the front.\r\nNavigating through heavy traffic, I spoke to the man who was sitting beside me.\r\nContrary to law, the car had no shield.\r\nAnd when my head was turned, someone reached forward and stuck something into my right eye.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSnap.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI get a regular partner.  \r\nI call her Cindy Lou.\r\nShe\\\'s a 2000 Ford Crown Victoria Police Interceptor, and we\\\'re in a relationship together.\r\nBefore we go out, I slip bits of folded paper into a crevice at the top of the instrument panel - one for each warning light that happens to be flashing.\r\nI pull the release, and then pry open the hood; I\\\'ve always got a screwdriver handy.\r\nI check the vital fluids, on nights when I care.\r\nAnd then I throw down two cushions - one of mysterious origin.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[I suspect that the garage manager found the cushion in an alley; but, after all this time, I don\\\'t really want to know.]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI love Cindy Lou like Mike Mulligan loved Mary Anne: she\\\'s not the newest, or the best; but we work together, every day.\r\nShe always gets a bath and perfume.\r\nSometimes, at the end of the shift, I\\\'ll climb on top of her while we\\\'re pushing into triple digits on the Kennedy.\r\nAnd even then, she\\\'ll give her back end a little wiggle, drop down a gear, and break Hell in the road.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Do you know what I did?  I just shot my wad.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Pardon me, sir?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"I just shot my wad; I got a blow job.  You can get one upstairs at the Bijou.  Do you know what the Bijou is?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s a theater that shows films about men, for men.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Yeah, well, you can get a blow job upstairs.  Usually.  I\\\'m not saying it\\\'s a sure thing.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"I see.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Now I need some candy.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"You bet.  There\\\'s a White Hen a block away.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Here: Keep the change.\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"That\\\'s a rather generous tip.  Are you sure?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"It was worth it just to look at you.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Still with me?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"Yeah.  Tell more...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Outside Roditys, in Greek Town, two women flagged me down.\r\nIt was typical, enough.\r\nI was focused on the road: my left hand draped over the wheel, my right hand resting on the passenger\\\'s headrest.\r\nAnd then I felt something soft moving against my right arm: one of the women was stroking me, as she spoke.\r\n7 years ago, I was told, the two of them had arrived in the United States with only their Summer clothing.\r\nToday - this very day - was the anniversary of their departure from the Czech Republic.\r\nThey wanted me to take them to a place on Kinzie - a place that they had been to, 7 years ago.\r\nBut we found it gone, and so they had me choose their next destination.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI parked opposite \\\'my club\\\' and stood outside the car, leaning on it, thinking, watching them walk away.\r\nThey were fun - and they tipped well.\r\nI was sorry to see them go.\r\nAn odd creaking noise brought me back to the present moment: the driver\\\'s door was falling shut.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[I had parked on an incline...]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI felt 2 of my fingers being crushed, and heard the rapid movement of a vile stream of profanity pouring out of my mouth.\r\nOne of the women spun around, and came running.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'I am a bartender,\\\' she said.  \\\'Let me take care of that.\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSure: (1) ER MD; (2) Paramedic; (3) EMT; (4) Fireman; (5) Bartender. \r\nBut she did a good job.\r\nAnd then she asked for my number.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAn hour later, my cellphone rang; she actually called back...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Paul?\\\' she said tentatively, \\\'I want to make a proposal:\r\nWe are not hookers, or drug people, ok?\r\nWe just enjoy your company, and we think that it is a sign that we have met you, tonight.\r\nYou are our Lucky 7.\r\nAnd so we want you to spend the night with us.\r\nWe will pay you: $100.\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe business about lucky numbers, signs and fate appealed to me; it was a slow night, anyway.\r\nWe began a short tour of their West Side neighborhood bars.\r\nEach club that we went to was more seedy than the last.\r\nThey called me \\\'their Frenchy.\\\'\r\nAnd I, for my part, smiled, laughed, and tried very hard to avoid contemplating such things as the origin of our moral valuations.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the end of the night we arrived at an apartment that belonged to one of the women.\r\nWe all drank more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Do what comes naturally, you two...\\\' said the apartment owner\\\'s friend, as she left.<br>\r\n\\\'Paul?\\\' said the other.<br>\r\n\\\'Yes?\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Please be with me...\\\'<br>\r\nWe took off most of our clothing, and lay down in her bed.<br>\r\n\\\'Paul?\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Yes?\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'You are mafia guy, or drug dealer, or something.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'No.  Well.  No...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Tell me what happened to you...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'I\\\'m going to go.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Please stay with me...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Goodnight...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Just go, then - and don\\\'t say anything...\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCindy Lou was waiting.\r\nIt was six in the morning.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes, like a helix turning there\\\'s an illusion of movement - but it\\\'s what you\\\'ve already seen, returning.\r\nWhen everything is compressed, ant-sized in the rear view mirror, that\\\'s the feeling of being free: timeless in the space between here and there.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"In need of surer ground, I picked up my mother, and went to see Greek Tina at the diner.\r\nA pork chop, 4 eggs, two pieces of sesamee toast, fried potatoes, and two pots of coffee filled my gut.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Jesus Christ, I never eat this kind of crap anymore...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'PAUL!\\\' my mother yelled, \\\'Stop saying JESUS!\\\'\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Are you Italian?  Sicilian?  How big is your penis - because we\\\'re looking for a third person to join us...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWith equal parts skill, intuition, and luck we subsist.\r\nBehind the wheel now, we might have been hunters, cowboys or fishermen; here it\\\'s a matter of following different herds and schools.\r\nThe taxi is a link to a more antique mode of being - especially nocturnal.\r\nIt\\\'s all sorcery: the numbers, the constant turning, the prediction, and the draw.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI find myself alone in a place with people that I do not know, having been thrown together by the greater hand of fate.\r\nIn some cases, we share ideas about what is good; in other cases, we are enemies.\r\nIn the taxi we coexist, or we separate.\r\nWe always separate, in the end.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Do I look like I just killed someone?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[The correct answer is always, \\\"No.\\\"]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"A young woman hailed me as I turned the corner from Chicago, westbound, onto Damen, northbound.\r\nI stopped; she climbed in the back.\r\nWith a great flourish, she put the back of her hand to her forehead, and sighed deeply.\r\nIt seemed an overly-dramatic gesture, and I imagined an actress from <i>Gone With the Wind</i>.\r\nAs I turned to look at her, I couldn\\\'t help but notice that one of her eyebrows was substantially longer than the other.\r\nShe asked for a light.\r\nI don\\\'t smoke, but I always carry matches.\r\nI handed her the book.\r\nShe took out 5-7, and twisted them into a phosphorus-tipped rope.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n~WOOSH~\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA little ball of flame lit her asymmetrical features.\r\nI drove, and listened.\r\nShe played with the shaven spots in her raven hair, and twirled her skeleton earring as she spoke about witchcraft and depression.\r\nI politely refused her invitation to take a break from work, and drink wine in her company.\r\nShe asked again, several times.\r\nIt was obvious that she needed something, but didn\\\'t know where to go.  So I gave her time and space to talk.\r\nBut it went on too long.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Where\\\'s home?\\\' I asked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe - a White girl - gave me an address in the ghetto on the West Side, in between Kedzie and Pulaski.\r\nUpon our arrival, she exited the vehicle and laid face down on sidewalk.\r\nI gave her another 10 minutes or so, but she wouldn\\\'t respond to any reasonable suggestion.\r\nIt was a Friday night, pushing 1am.\r\nSmall groups of young Black men wandered by, offering to take her down to the corner park and rape her.\r\nOlder Black women walked by, called her honey, and told her that she needed to get up and go.\r\nShe asked everyone for more fire; but she didn\\\'t budge.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere was a descision to be made: (a) leave her there and drive away; (b) stay and be her special friend; or (c) dial 911?\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe longer that I am hidden from the sun, the more my appearance changes.\r\nPassengers guessed Italian, at first, then Russian or Native American, and now Black Irish.  It\\\'s not that their guesses are so far from the truth, but rather that I never ask them to guess; they say things, unprovoked.  And then regardless of the ethnicity they suppose, they attribute the stereotypes of that group to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore than once someone screamed: \\\"You\\\'re a fucking Jew!\\\"\r\nAnd the ride ended quickly.\r\nNever having met me, never having spoken to me - or communicated with me in any substantial way - they have ideas about me: who I am, what I believe, and what my life experience has been.\r\nThose prejudices govern our interaction.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the moment, most people embrace the character: Irish Paul, the story-telling, street-wise, cabbie.\r\nThey solicit murder; they ask for butt sex; they try to get me to deliver narcotics; they tell me that they love me.\r\nThey talk about their marriages and their children; they talk about coming to America; they talk about their victimization.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce in a while, someone asks where something is in the City.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt night, the interior of the taxi is an intimate place - a potential space defined by its occupants.\r\nIt\\\'s a battery of rapid sexuality and violence [or banality, in turns] discharging its contents as quickly as some invisible switch is flipped.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I\\\'m glad to see there isn\\\'t some fucking Arab driving this cab...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I started the car, and turned on my cellphone.\r\nI had a message, waiting:<br>\r\n\\\'PAUL!  It\\\'s a voice from your past...\\\'<br>\r\nI hadn\\\'t heard that voice in years; it hadn\\\'t changed.\r\nIt was my ex; she found me on the net.<br>\r\n\\\'If you called me - and you did - then someone died, or you got a divorce.\\\'<br>\r\nShe had finished her PhD in Neurobiology at Chicago, and then moved out to the West Coast; but, shortly thereafter, things began to fall apart.\r\nWe kept talking.<br>\r\n\\\'At the convention in New Orleans, I drew the Emperor card for you, Paul; this was meant to be.\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDays turned into weeks; weeks turned into months.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*TIME OFF*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"One day she met me at the airport.\r\nI wore my best cowboy shirt, and the leather coat that she remembered.\r\nWe passed through a redwood forest, down to a hidden place between San Francisco and Santa Cruz - climbing quickly to the peak, laying off and descending in free-fall around the tight radius turns.<br>\r\nDown, down, down...<br>\r\nFalling back to earth...<br>\r\nWe passed over the mountains to the coast.  And as though a curtain were pulled swiftly, the Pacific appeared - a boundless day broken open before me.\r\nI breathed it in again, at last: Ocean.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI woke in California: her daughter jumping on the bed, her cat at my feet.  \r\nShe was beside me.\r\nIt was good to remember; it was good to be understood; it was good to have a home and mate again.<br>\r\n\\\'And here I had thought that you\\\'d be a balding, alcoholic cop...you\\\'re freakishly well-preserved.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Human blood.  Joke.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'You do remind me of Angel.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Angel?  I am so tired...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'I know: I couldn\\\'t wake you...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'When I take a day off to sleep, now, I sleep 18-36 hours straight.  I have got to leave the taxi.  I have got to leave Chicago...I need someplace clean, and green.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Paul, one way or another, the taxi is coming to an end.  In many ways it\\\'s the perfect job for you; but it\\\'s served it\\\'s purpose.\r\nYou know, I think that when we split you were suffering because of all the weird shit in your life; and in my opinion you had classic symptoms of PTSD/OCD.  If the navigation that you do at work has resulted in an elargement of your hippocampus, that\\\'s probably been helpful.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Art-making, like OCD, is an attempt to gain control over a portion of one\\\'s environment.  It\\\'s all about spatial relationships.  No?  I\\\'ve read a few articles that dealt with structural changes in the brains of taxi drivers...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'This all determined the course of my research: you, and lions too.\r\nYou\\\'re like you were, but better.  I like the man that you\\\'ve grown into.  Paul, do you realize I was only 16 when you took me to the CroMags show, and had me drink beer in the alley?  You gave me my first cigarette.  I\\\'m still smoking!\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'And I took you to the initial release of <i>Blue Velvet</i> too.  You make me sound like a big jerk; but maybe it\\\'s because I was bad at being good that you still love me.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Don\\\'t be stupid.  You were THE male figure in my life.  You are a virus that infected me: I am still rethinking our conversations; I look more and more like you; my daughter has your personality.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Ouch.  Hey I was young too - and things were NOT easy.  D\\\'you realize we started almost 20 years ago?\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Now I wonder if the past ten years were wasted; could it always be this good.  I\\\'m still crazy about you.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'That\\\'s cool.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'No, I mean crazy like <i>Fatal Attraction</i>.  Virus.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'OK, that\\\'s less cool.  And you\\\'re a materialist.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'You\\\'re a mystic.  You should be a cult leader.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'I\\\'m opposed to your use of animals.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'And you\\\'re not going to support me with my band either...you never did.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'On the bright side, I\\\'ve gained 5-10 pounds in a week with you; you are such an Italian woman.  You\\\'ve got the best hands; let\\\'s just keep the knives well-hidden, darling.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'You need to lie to me more.  And never mention anyone else.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'This has to do with the knives again, doesn\\\'t it...\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo: Tacoma? Seattle? A little house for the family? Maybe just wait ten years, again?\r\nI left California with a gift: a small wooden box.  In the box were two locks of hair: one hers, and one her daughter\\\'s.<br>\r\n\\\'She really loves you.  And you\\\'re at your best when you\\\'re with her...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'It\\\'s one of the few times that there aren\\\'t any questions about how to be, or why...\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Paul, you broke my heart...\\\'<br>\r\nAnd so I placed the small box in the large box that holds the other good and painful memories - until I pass, and the person who follows me finds the things, not knowing what their importance was...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"As I headed up Ogden, I saw a figure standing in the intersection at Grand Ave.\r\nHer face was a mess; it was obvious that she had been crying.<br>\r\n\\\'Hey...I\\\'m a working girl, ok?\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'What\\\'s the situation here.  Tell me what happened.\\\'<br>\r\n\\\'Yes: a situation.  I\\\'ll give you a blow job if you get me back to the place that I need to go...\\\'<br>\r\nWe talked: Every night she got in a car with strangers, drove someplace, and then hoped to be paid.\r\nShe was alone; the police weren\\\'t responsive to her.\r\nI nodded; it sounded familiar.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nJust before I found her, she had been ripped off and left in an alley.\r\nThe upsetting thing, she said, was that her work was so \\\'intimate.\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'How could someone just take that and not pay?\\\' she wondered.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe looked 19-20, and said that she was from Wisconsin.\r\nI had a little bag - a makeup kit - that a wealthy woman had left in the car a few months ago.\r\nI try to return everything; but sometimes I try very hard, and other times I don\\\'t try very hard at all.\r\nI practice something like distributive justice.\r\nI knew that a homeless woman or prostitute would be thrilled to have $100 worth of cosmetics.\r\nThe makeup kit sat in my bag, waiting for the right person to receive it.\r\nI had a box of smokes too; someone else had left those behind.\r\nAnd there was a cup of water and ice that a young couple had brought to me.\r\nI felt pretty good as I gave her the makeup, and cigarettes, and water.\r\nIt seemed meant to be: I was there, then, to give these things to this person.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut she complained that they wouldn\\\'t buy her drugs.\r\nSo I gave her $5 too.\r\nWhat could I hope to do?  Be kind, treat her with respect, nothing more.\r\nSucker?  She would be lucky to survive the year.  We\\\'re the boys; they\\\'re the girls.  The girls work so hard.\r\nAnd the more that they work, the more teeth they lose, the more burns they get, and the more difficult their life becomes.\r\nUntil one day they are so ugly that they can\\\'t work anymore, and they have to beg, or die.\r\nThe two of us talked about the people who were robbed, killed, and went missing - and the fact that no one knew.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe smiled, lit up, and passed out - stone cold - with the fag burning down into the flesh of her fingers.\r\nI looked back and saw her in Piccasso blue, as the arc-light warped through the glass of the taxi windows.\r\nMost of her was already dead, her face like nothing so much as that of a young person I had seen in a casket.\r\nWe got to her corner.\r\nAnd there, waiting, was her pimp.\r\nShe craddled her presents like a babe, turned up her head, and walked past him.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI saw her on the southeastern edge of the park, in the nights that passed - until she disappeared.\r\nI would like to think that she found her way back to her home in green Wisconsin; I would like to think that...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRoughly 40% of Chicago\\\'s residents are Black; the legacy of racially-based slavery continues to shape the character of the City.\r\nFewer than 60 years ago, between WWII and the Vietnam, one quarter of the country\\\'s entire African American population moved - from agricultural communities in the South to manufacturing centers in the North.  \r\nDuring that same period of time, in response to what was purported to be an inadequate supply of affordable living space, public housing \\\"projects\\\" were built in Chicago - projects like Cabrini Green.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[They were named for a nun who dedicated herself to the poor: St. Frances Xavier Cabrini.]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPeople from Mississippi and Alabama moved directly north - to Chicago - bringing their culture along.\r\nBlues and Rock were born. Chess Records came into being in 1950.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ve got a black cat bone; I got a mojo too; I got the John the Conqueror root; I\\\'m gonna mess with you...\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd then, gradually, the industrial jobs went away.\r\nGang activity and drug sales increased; the \\\"projects\\\" proved to be very difficult to police.\r\nPublic housing grew vertically: every new building was taller than the last; and the problems grew proportionally.\r\nPeople were left - segregated - in high-density units, unemployed, and ill-educated; in the Black community here, there is a <50% dropout rate over the 4-year high school term.\r\nIt must be the case that close to a million of Chicago\\\'s residents function on a poor grade school education.\r\nSince the year of my birth: 1967, at least 600 people per calendar year, every year, have been murdered in Chicago - despite being within seconds of what is, arguably, the finest emergency medical care in the world.\r\nFinally, in 2003, the number of murders in the City plummeted below the 600 count - all the way to 599.\r\nIn 2001, 666 people were murdered in Chicago - more than in any other American city; Chicago lead the nation again, in 2003.\r\nChicago\\\'s murder rate is still higher than any other American city with a population over one million.\r\nAnd over 90% of the murder victims in any given year are, \\\"non-white.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCity of Chicago:<br>\r\nU.S. Census, 2002 estimated population: 2,886,251<br>\r\nReported murders: 646\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCanada:<br>\r\n2002 estimated population: 31,496,800<br>\r\nReported murders: 582\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCity of New York:<br>\r\nU.S. Census, 2002 estimated population: 8,084,316<br>\r\nReported murders: 580\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNew Zealand:<br>\r\n2002 estimated population: 3,908,037<br>\r\nReported murders: 67\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"I saw him waiting at the opposite end of the block.  In the dim light, he resembled a young Santa Claus.\r\nAs I drew closer, he appeared to me to be standing upon the deck of an ocean-going vessel: he rocked and reeled, back and forth, side to side.\r\nSure enough, he raised a thick arm and hailed me.\r\nI stopped; he tugged on the rear door, and fell into the seat.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI turned round, and began with my usual greeting and query: \\\'Good evening, Sir.  Where May I take you?\\\'<br>\r\nHe looked me square in the eye, and in a very serious tone he said, \\\'Grooog!\\\'<br>\r\nI paused, and searched my memory for any sort of street or building that might bear a name resembling, \\\'Grooog.\\\'  But, I was stumped.  As poorly as many people pronounce, \\\"Goethe,\\\" it never sounds quite that bad.  And so I waited for his next utterance...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Grooog...train!\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWell, I had something to work with; I began to recite the names and locations of the various trains in town.  But poor Grooog\\\'s brow became furrowed, and I could tell that the letters and numbers that I sent flying through space in my speech did nothing but confuse him.  And so I waited, againâ€¦\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\'Grooog...train...P-r-u-d-e-n-t-i-a-l-B-u-i-l-d-i-n-g...\\\'\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGrooog was terribly sad, and on the verge of tears.  He wanted - desperately - to communicate with me.  He wasn\\\'t trying to be difficult; he had simply filled his Santa belly with more beer than any two normal Santa bellies could hold.\r\nPoor Grooog: I left him at the Prudential on Randolph.  And he walked off in the wrong direction...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA young Puerto Rican couple - a boy and a girl - stopped outside Eddy\\\'s gas station.  \r\nThe boy came in, and asked to use the toilet; he did, and then he went back outside to his car.  \r\nThe girl came in and made the same request.  While she was thus occupied, the boy returned.  \r\nHe asked Eddy for one of the plastic flowers that sit for sale on the counter.  \r\nAnd then he opened the bathroom door and gave his girlfriend the flower - putting her on display while she sat on the gas station toilet.\r\nThe whole thing was so romantic, and they were both filled with such passion, that they decided to stay in the bathroom together, and engage in a physical expression of their love.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEddy got bumped off nights, and I lost touch with him.\r\nHis replacement - also a Pakistani guy from the southern city of Karachi - was a total dick.\r\nI started going to a new station, just down the street.\r\nThe night guy there was Pakistani too - but he was from the northern city of Peshawar.\r\n\\\"Those people rape cute boys like me!\\\" said Eddy.\r\nThree times in the course of the first week that I went to the Peshawar station, the night guy there was robbed: someone entered the station, held a handgun to his stomach, and cleaned the place out.\r\nI decided to try a third station, and then a fourth; but I never found another Eddy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI miss Eddy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"A few nights ago, on the way back from Hyde Park, I helped a family that was heading to 42nd & Cottage Grove.\r\nAn older guy and two little girls were on the corner, waiting for the bus.\r\nThe smaller of the two girls had some sort of problem with the whole of her face.\r\nShe needed glasses, but the only pair that she had to wear were terrible, thick, and made for an old lady.\r\nNo one loves these people; no one cares.\r\nYet they were all very thankful, and polite; they had manners, and courtesy of a type that the \\\'better\\\' classes are supposed to possess, but which they rarely display.\r\nAnd the littlest girl waved to me when I left, saying: \\\'See you again soon.\\\'\r\nThings like that make me cry.\r\nWhat did she do to deserve her lot?\r\nSo many of the children here begin life with physical or mental disabilities because of the drugs that their mothers used while they were pregnant, or the violence that was done to them.\r\nIt\\\'s not that dealing with such things is so hard, alone.\r\nBut dealing with the next group of people can be - when the next group chatters about their wealth, complains about nothing, and then tips me in pennies.\r\nI find myself, more and more often now, simply tired, and isolated.\r\nIn spite of whatever education and experience I have had, and in spite of how cynical I have become, my ideas about the way that things ought to be, and my hopes for people, are still surprisingly stupid, and childlike...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI roll my cage around the City, and some nights I go home defeated.\r\nTired - but sleepless - I put out seed for my birds.\r\nThey wait for me in the morning: a link to the sanity of the free, green world that must exist someplace else.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI keep turning the wheel.\r\nOver time the routine is of necessity stripped bare: I wake, make a pot of coffee, and take the coffee to the dumbells.\r\nI hit the dumbells like jail, and then turn the key that sets the shift in motion.\r\nTime and money usually allow 2 meals per day, but sometimes only one; these are not the fat years in my life.\r\nEntertainment is a 13 inch black and white television that keeps me company infrequently, at the end of the day.\r\nAnd the old tabby cat is always there.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEvery week a guy waits for an envelope with $300 in it.\r\nIf I don\\\'t make the $300 for him, I lose the car.\r\nAnd then I\\\'m on the hook for gas, washes, coffee, parking tickets, etc.\r\nIt\\\'s about $500 per week - all told - that I need to make for other people, before I make dime one for myself.\r\nThe taxi, I discover, is a form of debt peonage.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEven so, things have changed for the better - relative to the first dark months of Winter, post 9/11.  No one counts my lease now; and I don\\\'t stand in a line anymore.  I throw an envelope down on the garage manager\\\'s desk; we hug and slap backs.\r\nI have a key for the car that he owns.\r\nIt\\\'s a different way of doing business - and I like it.\r\nWe are hated; but behind the veil there are high acts of love, comedy, and drama in the manner of The Guild of Thieves and Assassins - betrayed only sometimes by the manner of our dress, the stories that we tell to outsiders, and those moments when honor and utility are thoroughly confused.\r\nWe are brothers in a family - as long as I keep making money.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\\\"He shall be a wild ass of a man, his hand against every man and every man\\\'s hand against him; and he shall dwell over against all his kinsmen.\\\"\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"It was after 2 in the morning.\r\nI drove north on Clark.\r\nWhen I hit Lawrence, I saw three figures standing at the edge of the cemetery: two men and a woman.\r\nThe woman yelled, and ran into the street.\r\nBefore she got to the taxi I knew two things: (1) she wasn\\\'t right; and, (2) she had an amazing rack.\r\nHer hair was bright orange, and her skin was as pale as snow.\r\nThe black dress that she wore suggested her occupation was prostitution: easy on; easy off; tied at the top; nothing underneath.\r\nWhen she sat down in the back, I was overwhelmed by the cheap perfume that she wore.\r\nI was correct about her mental state, and also that she employed her sexuality.\r\nBut I had forgotten to include the place of our meeting in my initial estimation of her character.\r\nThere were ongoing problems with Satanic activity in the area,\r\nincluding the recovery of human remains at the roller rink across the street from the cemetery.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe two of us were in the taxi together; but we were not alone.\r\nShe laughed in a voice that did not belong to her.\r\nHer presence was like a gate to the void.\r\nIt\\\'s a difficult thing to explain to anyone without experience in ritual magic or exorcism.\r\nShe was dangerous.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe met my eyes in the rear view mirror, letting her coat fall from her shoulders.\r\nIf her face appeared drawn from distress, her figure was much younger.\r\nIt was obvious that she had implants.\r\nShe began to manipulate herself in a well-practiced manner - her hands sublty tracing her charm.<br>\r\n\\\'I\\\'m a good girl,\\\' she said, \\\'I don\\\'t fuck or suck.\\\'<br>\r\nAnd then she laughed that horrible laugh, again.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHer comments and antics continued, as she urged me to drive faster.\r\nSuddenly, she called for a stop.<br>\r\n\\\'Master lives here,\\\' she said.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI told her to take the ride for free.\r\nShe leaned forward to expose herself; her hands resumed their former work; and she repeated her telephone number twice.\r\nI drove away quickly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFive minutes later I picked up a young Black guy; he was going to rob me.\r\nThe speed limit was 30.  By the time I hit 90, he paid me to slow down.\r\nI got lucky.  Even so, my stomach was burning after the encounter - coming on the heels of cemetery expedition.  I pulled over to the side, and took ten minutes out.\r\nBut then I made the mistake of going back to work; things run in streaks.\r\nA young gang-style Latin couple got in the back.  They had a violent domestic dispute over a pack of cigarettes.\r\nI dropped the car - and I had a drink...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*CLICK*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI pick up everyone.\r\nI go everywhere in the city.\r\nI can\\\'t control anyone but myself.\r\nFor now, I am here: turning the wheel.\r\nAbsent meaning, movement suffices; the trick is to keep moving.\r\nStill, I can\\\'t help but wonder what this was all about.\r\nMy name is Paul Edward Germanos, and I drive a taxi at night, in Chicago.\r\n</p>'),(3568,'i would slip you into my to do list<br>\r\n\r\nor in a drawer, the middle one, of my desk<br>\r\nthe one nobody opens, not even thieves<br>\r\nslinking inside, in the middle of the night<br><br>\r\n\r\ni would put you on my dresser<br>\r\nunframed (looks more natural that way)<br><br>\r\n\r\nor maybe sit you on top of the pillows<br>\r\ninstead of my fluffy little polar bear<br><br>\r\n\r\ni would hook my little finger round the fender<br>\r\nand pull the greyhound bus back to the parking lot<br>\r\nbefore it left st catherine with you<br><br>\r\n\r\ni would stretch the limits of this city<br>\r\nso you were somehow contained within its skirts<br><br>\r\n\r\ni would put you in my address book<br>\r\non a page where no-one but me would look<br>\r\nperhaps the letter Q, or maybe W, just never U<br>'),(3562,'I have a car. Itâ€™s Grey and Swedish and the only thing in my life I wonâ€™t give up on. Itâ€™s the first and only car I have ever owned and I intend to play God on its ass for as long as I have two hands and a friend with a lot of tools.<br><br> \r\n\r\nI remember shopping for my first car. My dad came with me to supervise and offer advice, so we went to the Saturn dealership where one might purchase an \\\"inexpensive reliable automobile\\\". As I walked slowly along the rows of colourful cookie cutter cruisers, I saw it. It was beautiful. Like nothing I have ever seen in my life. It was a 1991 Saab 900s, and I now believe in love at first sight because of it. It was also the only car I could afford that had leather interior, a sun roof, and the most wonderful rubber spoiler that dipped up from the back window. Pivotal moment.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI have had the car for about four years now, and every stall, every squeal, every symptom of trouble is a call to my will. I learned very quickly that this is not a car you want to take to the shop, unless you want to be monetarily violated, and laughed at. I bought a shop manual and the rest is history. <br><br>\r\n\r\nOil in my coolant led me to change the head gasket a couple of years back and I have never since felt so much pride and power as I did when I got her back on the road. My knowledge of mechanics is limited, to say the least, but I know my car. I know every belt and gasket, every sensor and valve, every hose and plug, and I feel intimately connected to every stutter and groan. <br><br>\r\n\r\nShe died last week. Excessive oil consumption led me to believe she was experiencing leaky seals. They blew as I was crusinâ€™ 160km/h on a rurally placed highway. Zero compression, on piston #3. (sigh, blubber) But do not fear I have my exam week coming up, lending much time to spend rebuilding, reboring, and you will be better than ever. <br><br>'),(3563,'Perched now, on the eve of so many things<br>\r\nthe apartment unpeeling its possessions<br>\r\nflinging them loose, scattering physical reminders, talismans of who we were.<br>\r\nThe act of picking and choosing imbued with a sense of the sacred,<br>\r\nMy holy quest to choose and pack<br>\r\nto strip away the dust and dirt,<br>\r\nand lock everything safe within this box.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat shall i choose to accompany me?<br>\r\nWhat small comforts do i keep?<br>\r\nOh the world is wide<br>\r\nand i am such a small thing in it.<br>'),(3564,'<PRE>\r\n_Things I have lost_\r\n\r\n        This is a very long list. Randomly then:\r\n        Numerous gloves;\r\n        My Grandmother;\r\n        Two strong chances\r\n        at love;\r\n        A beautiful body;\r\n        My annual bus pass;\r\n        My father\\\'s\r\n        father;\r\n        One sock each\r\n        from several pairs;\r\n        My lunchbox which was just the right size\r\n        and had a purple lid;\r\n        A schoolmate who liked fast cars\r\n        too much;\r\n        My little red alarm clock\r\n        which used to glow in the dark\r\n        and travel everywhere with me,\r\n        the only one\r\n        I\\\'ve ever found\r\n        that can pull me out of\r\n        the cave of sleep;\r\n        My girlhood;\r\n        The capacity to draw;\r\n        My good scissors;\r\n        My motivation;\r\n        My 95.105 textbook\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n_Things I am losing_\r\n\r\n        I shall go for the easy laugh and first say \\\"My mind\\\".\r\n        You can also add \\\"Sanity\\\" to the list.\r\n        \\\"Time\\\" of course.\r\n        This lecture on artifical intelligence as I write all this.\r\n        My other best friend. (I can\\\'t talk about this just yet.)\r\n        My love of and belief in words.\r\n        My mother tongue.\r\n        A secret that makes me feel beautiful.\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n_Things I have lost and found again_\r\n\r\n        This too is a long list.\r\n        A brand new and very expensive CD;\r\n        My winter jacket;\r\n        Bus tickets;\r\n        Many loonies;\r\n        A blue sweater.\r\n        On Saturday I lost my other wallet\r\n        which had about $100.00\r\n        or so of cash in it.\r\n        I was terrified I\\\'d left it\r\n        on the bus we had just gotten off from\r\n        on our way to Dunn\\\'s for a long overdue lunch.\r\n        \\\"What would you do if you got on the bus,\r\n        walked to a seat, sat down and found an orphaned\r\n        wallet with all that money and absolutely no ID\r\n        inside it?\\\" I cried to L.\r\n        She ignored me and started looking through\r\n        the million pockets of my backpack.\r\n        I was frantic. Lunch seemed like an unnecessary thing.\r\n        I was so upset I stood there\r\n        paralyzed, while she asked me to empty my jacket pockets.\r\n        \\\"I\\\'m sorry. I have to go back to the bookstore.\r\n        I might have left it there by mistake.\r\n        You don\\\'t have to come all the way back with me.\\\" I said.\r\n        But she smiled and said \\\"It wouldn\\\'t be as much fun\r\n        to have lunch without you.\\\"\r\n        The cashier had my wallet! Someone found it\r\n        on the floor in front of the poetry books.\r\n        I could have hugged him.\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n_Things I want to lose_\r\n\r\n        Here, just the usual: weight, worries, other things\r\n        with \\\'w\\\' in them;\r\n        My acne;\r\n        My anxiety;\r\n        Fear;\r\n        Certain memories;\r\n        The need to pretend\r\n        ...\r\n\r\n_Things I should never have lost_\r\n\r\n        My oldest uncle (who I lost before I was born\r\n        surely that has to be some kind of record?);\r\n        The best lip balm in the world;\r\n        An intense love of rain;\r\n        Old friends who gave part of themselves\r\n        to make me;\r\n        The gold locket my parents gave me;\r\n        My wooden box of love letters and birthday cards;\r\n        What my faith used to be\r\n        (the big strong shining structure\r\n        which held up not just me\r\n        and all the people i loved,\r\n        but the world itself)\r\n        I still have it\r\n        in a smaller form\r\n        (a piece of it, say a wooden block\r\n        from the scaffolding\r\n        used in the original structure\r\n        which protects me\r\n        from monsters in the dark,\r\n        slipping on ice,\r\n        accidents from getting worse)\r\n        ...\r\n</PRE>'),(3565,'I quit smoking cigarettes. Like that, just quit. What helps is, after a couple of weeks of not puffing, stepping outside and <i>really</i> sucking down a smoke... like, dragging so hard that you get that wicked head-rush, which does well to remind you why you were quitting in the first place. It\\\'s sort of like smoking probation.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nIts a quiet Friday evening, and I decide to do just that. I grab whatever stale smokes we happen to have around and shuffle down the five tightly spiralling flights to the street, and step out to have a calculated smoke. Reinforcement therapy to remind me why I\\\'ve quit. You know how it goes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo I get to the front door of my apartment and step out onto the sidewalk facing 9th Avenue like I have a million times before. I say hi to my fellow smokers at the gay bistro next door and watch neon yellow cabs zip by as they drag the metal pull-down over the bike shop across the street with a metallic rumble. Boom.<br><br>\r\n\r\nYay, a smoke. My first in about a week, give or take. A reminder. A fetid remnant of my addiction.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI give the box a shake and pull out a Parliament, eyeing it menacingly, knowing it\\\'ll jolt straight to my brain the nauseous reason why I\\\'m quitting... the headrush; conditioning. Yuck. How many of these I\\\'ve smoked before so tastily, I don\\\'t know. It can\\\'t be good for you.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhile contemplating the cigarette, disdaining the taste and scent its sure to leave on my newly non-smoker palate, this dude steps up. This dude in a black leather jacket and slicked back hair. This dude that\\\'s most likely on an excursion from Jersey, chatting on his cell, hitting me up. He\\\'s <i>dying</i> for a smoke, he says, and asks if I could spare one. I throw on my disaffected New Yorker face, and scowl, rather begrudgingly aquiescing... it shows, etched on my face. <i>\\\"Whatever, here...\\\"</i> the look of impatience that comes from freely distributing one from a pack of 20 cheap cigarettes that stilll cost you around eight bucks in this fucking not-so-bourgeoise city.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nAnd he says, as I zip out the Zippo and spark it... <i>\\\"Yo, man, I can hit you for a buck or something\\\"</i>, figuring I\\\'ll be socially minded and say <i>\\\"Oh, its ok, here!\\\"</i> and just hand him one. Fuck that, I could really use the buck, and I say so. Honesty is a virtue, and this city ain\\\'t cheap; even a buck can by you supper in Hell\\\'s Kitchen, if you\\\'re as broke as I am.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nSo he hands me a crumpled dollar, its top right corner missing, which I sheepishly stuff into my pocket along with the rest of the smokes before handing him the cigarette I was just about to light up myself, before darting back into my building, dollar safely in my pocket, cigarette unsmoked, head-rush unearned.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nHealth is an important factor, but I\\\'ll quit smoking any day as long as the cash is flowing. Fuck, if I can pass off aroud 60k more smokes in this manner, I just might be able to pay off my student loans.'),(3566,'<pre>\r\ni awake,\r\nhallucinating from \\\'hydrocodone,\\\'\r\nwhatever that is. \r\n\r\nit infiltrates my dreams, \r\nplaying moving pictures--\r\n\r\nmonsters,\r\nwhales,\r\nand elephants--\r\n\r\npink,\r\nyellow,\r\nand green.\r\n\r\nthere is no pain, and \r\ni float above the mattress,\r\nthree inches in the air.\r\n\r\n</pre>'),(3569,'<p>death is the greatest equaliser in the entire world.  no matter who you are, when you are dying, you may have a different level of care from me or her or him but essentially, a human body is a human body and they all work the same way and they all die the same way.  it seems so easy to lose life, whether its taken from you or you remove yourself from it.  your body naturally fights for survival and yet, sometimes the forces against this are too much.</p>\r\n<p>we saw \\\'mystic river\\\' last night and though it wasn\\\'t the most quality film i have ever seen it struck chords with me about death and friendship, mostly death.  the murder of the 19 year old and the complete standstill the lives of everyone she knew came to afterwards took me back to march last year and the complete standstill my life came to for nearly a week, and who knows how long it was until it went back to \\\'normal\\\'. </p>\r\n<p>after a death, nothing matters.  you call out of work, school, commitments.  it conquers all.  it\\\'s all you can think about.  you can\\\'t stop crying, shaking.  you\\\'re in the supermarket and you want to sit down and bawl.  you\\\'re on the bus and you want to hug old ladies who are being ignored by the stranger next to them as they talk because they are lonely and you fucking LEAK.  you sit and stare into space, shredding tissue after tissue in your hands.  you can\\\'t finish sentences and your eyes are glazed by grief and tears.</p>\r\n<p>it\\\'s worse than anything else i have ever experienced, and it makes me scared of it happening to me. it seems a pathetic fear, intertwined with hating how little actual control i have over the wider sphere of my life.  i can\\\'t stop bad things happening to people i love and  i\\\'m scared of my mother dying, no matter how fucked up our relationship is sometimes.  i\\\'m scared of my brothers and friends dying and basically i\\\'m scared of anyone i care about in the least dying.  those who are gone i miss badly enough. </p>\r\n<p>die dying died death.  it\\\'s so final.  i don\\\'t believe in reincarnation and its only in moments of abject sentimentality that i hope that people who are gone are watching over us.  the closest i have to any life beyond death theory is that of the  whatever-those-aliens-are-called in SLAUGHTERHOUSE 5 (vonnegut) - all moments exist simultaneously, therefore no one is dead because they are simply existing in moments gone before which are happening at the same moment as their death.</p>\r\n<p>expletives will not save us.  money in the bank will not save us.  beauty truth and art cannot save us becuase when the reaper comes you are alone and you will make other people feel alone because you have left a void in their reality whether you ever damn well wanted to or not.<p>\r\n<br><p><i>...sans teeth, sans eyes, sans everything.</i></p>'),(3570,'<P>\r\nI opened the Neil folder in my email this morning. We officially broke up yesterday and contrary to what has occurred from time to time in the past, I think this time itâ€™s for good. I wrote to Lynn and told her about whatâ€™s going on. I hate telling my friends about relationship issues because they often work themselves out. I mentioned that it doesnâ€™t hurt as much for some reason this time. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThen I opened the Neil folder. \r\n</P><P>\r\nIt contains numerous emails detailing our relationship from as far back as 2002. It includes the period of time when we last broke up, and emails following when he regularly declared his love. I read one from about three months ago. It was not all that noteworthy but his words were meaningful, and honest. Iâ€™m going to miss my best friend. Things between us were not like most relationships. We were close, I could spend countless hours with him and him with me and we would never tire of each others company. I thought about the Coral Reef Inn and all the good times we spent at the Pinery or the beach. He was truly genuine in bed. We never got physical when we were having issues, and it was apparent that when we did he truly did love me. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThatâ€™s how I figured out that we were having problems. I came down to visit him in March. I hadnâ€™t seen him in two months, yet when we retired to bed he couldnâ€™t have been further away from me than if I was still in Toronto. //*Neil your an open book, and so predictable. You think Iâ€™m not supportive of you, I think that youâ€™re angry with yourself. I just wish you could have the balls to flat out say that you donâ€™t want to be with me. After more than four years, MSN? Man thatâ€™s weak.*// We argued and quarrelled when I was down last, about my cousin and how when they were dealing with all kinds of shit, he says I did nothing. He fails to note that I paid the three month back rent that got him out of the situation. I wonder if he plans on paying me back. He said that I donâ€™t have what heâ€™s looking for in a wife, but didnâ€™t want to break up with me. Why did I open the Neil folder? I think that Iâ€™m supposed to be given a chuck of time in order to deal with all of this and figure out a game plan. Unfortunately I have exams next week and I donâ€™t have time to deal. Iâ€™m supposed to be studying right now. Iâ€™m not supposed to be typing on the computer. What about my MTH exam? I refuse to experience the pain right now. Iâ€™m going to close the Neil folder and maybe even delete it.\r\n</P>'),(3597,'t'),(3571,'<p>this has as good a chance of reaching you as anything else would.</p><p>\r\ni haven\\\'t seen you in three years. we talked once by phone since then, in 2001 i think.  </p><p>\r\ni remember the summer i spent nursing a crush on you, and landscaping LB\\\'s farm. i used to stand half naked in a field, with grass juice and mud covering me, with no one around for a mile, and think about you, and how pretty you were, and how i loved to sit in the dark with you and smoke pot, and feel the air conditioning, an d look at you. i thought about your hair, and the color of your skin, and the shape of your mouth. i thought about your name. Sarah Margaret. Sarah Margaret. Sarah Margaret.</p><p>\r\ni almost made myself sick. i wanted you so much that i couldn\\\'t act. i was paralyzed. i just wanted to be around you, and i wasn\\\'t sure how you might take it. it sounds simple now, but it was not so simple then. </p><p>\r\nthat fall you went back to school, and had a new boyfriend just like that. i met him once. he was like me in a lot of important respects. </p><p>\r\nand since then? you graduated a while back now. when we talked on the phone there was a new guy, and you were waiting tables. now i don\\\'t know where you are, what you are doing, or who you are with. i don\\\'t know what kind of car you drive, what kind of house you live in, if you have seen any good movies, if you still get high, or what you do for money. </p><p>\r\ni do wonder sometimes. sometimes it keeps me up. not too often. \r\n\r\n   '),(3572,'<P><B>*** what i wanted to know about sex (before) ***</B></P>\r\n<P>\r\nwhat is it like to lose your virginity? what is it really like? do you become a different person? or the same person with a new skill, like being able to ride a bike, suddenly, without the little training wheels? </P><P>\r\n\r\nhow much does it hurt? on a scale of 1 to 10 say (where 1 is a sweet sharp paper cut to 10 where you would rather die than continue with the agony of gallstones)? \r\n</P><P>\r\nexactly how much do you bleed the first time? actually, do you really bleed or is that just a scare tactic, a myth, to stop young girls from trying sex? \r\n</P><P>\r\nso how much blood are we talking? do you stain the bedsheets? is it like when you have your period? or is it just a teaspoon of blood? a few drops? \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhat does it feel like to have sex? really, what does it feel like? is there anything else that is comparable?\r\n</P><P>\r\ndo you stop masturbating once you have sex?\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhat does a penis look like?\r\n</P><P>\r\ndoes anyone ever get bored of sex? like if you do it a thousand times?\r\n</P><P>\r\n<B>*** what i didn\\\'t know about sex (after) ***</B>\r\n</P><P>\r\nexactly where the penis goes. i didn\\\'t know i had what i had down there. seriously. pretty amazing.\r\n</P><P>\r\nit is not just the first time that sucks. the first several times do. you wonder what all the fuss is about. sexsexsex obsessedmedia obsessedworld now confuses you. what is the big deal? i could do without it. easily.\r\n</P><P>\r\nit gets better. whoa, does it get better. and get better fast. (just keep practicing)\r\n</P><P>\r\na penis is beautiful. i kept hearing that it looks funny, even ugly. that it smells weird. but oh, i find it beautiful. \r\n</P><P>\r\nmaybe i\\\'m weird? \r\n</P><P>\r\nbananas and cucumbers do not do it justice. \r\n</P><P>\r\nit continues to get better, better than you can imagine, as years pass.\r\n</P><P>\r\nyou need to know yourself, your own body and mind, as much as you need to know about the mechanics of sex.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand learn about his body too.\r\n</P><P>\r\ncreative people such as yourself and your lover often discover a whole range of new positions by themselves. they even think they invented them. until they pick up their first sex how-to book.\r\n</P><P>\r\nsex is mysterious. \r\n</P><P>\r\nsometimes it is ordinary.\r\n</P><P>\r\nafter all this time, you still can\\\'t disentagle why it is magic, and why the opposite.\r\n</P><P>\r\nyou can be surprised by what turns you on.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni love my body. i am proud of it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhen you eat fresh fruit and veggies and whole wheat grains everyday, and go jogging three times a week, sex is different.\r\n</P><P>\r\nsometimes when you think about it, even now, it seems as though this is the most bizarre thing in the world. a man\\\'s what? inside my what? shouldn\\\'t that be dangerous? that sounds dangerous. and freaky, if you ask me. \r\n</P><P>\r\nwait, you mean this, this, THIS is how we make new people? isn\\\'t that strange? and a little bit disgusting? \r\n</P><P>\r\nisn\\\'t it shattering? when his fingers are in your hair, and you have to close your eyes because he is so beautiful you can\\\'t stand looking at him?\r\n</P><P>\r\nit feels goodgoodgood. every millisecond feels good. \r\n</P><P>\r\nno. there\\\'s nothing like it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nyou float. you\\\'re half off the bed and half in the air. now you understand. now you know why.</P>'),(3573,'<p>for the past five nights i have dreamt that my alarm clock is recording the private grievances of strangers for me to hear and i am able to save the recording by pressing snooze. then as i go in and out of sleep in the       \r\nmornings, my alarm clock relays these messages to me.</p>                 \r\n                                                                 \r\n<p>i want to know, am i nuts? do i get a new alarm clock? am i a superhero?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it doesn\\\'t help that i told hillary this and she told me that her old college roommate was crazy and when she didn\\\'t take her anti-psychotics regularly enough, her alarm clock talked to her too! </p>  \r\n\r\n<p>also when my friend was in the psych ward, she said that people were often very matter-of-fact and nonchalant about the weird things they saw or heard.\r\n</p>                                                                   \r\n                                                            \r\n<p>any ideas?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i should also mention that about six months back, i had another dream that lasted five days. Each night, as i slept, i boarded an airplane the size of a small city or cruise ship. I had my own room. Coincindentally, Bijoux Phillips was also on board and had taken up in the room adjacent. I would hang out in my room and read. I\\\'d browse through hallways lined with shops. Then suddenly, without fail, the plane would start to jerk back and forth violently, pitching this way and that. It would become apparent, as i stood in Scoop NYC waiting to try on a pair of Seven jeans, that we were crashing and there was nothing to be done about it. So we\\\'d crash. Every night for five days, we\\\'d crash into a suspension bridge. The plane\\\'s wings would catch the ropey suspension part of the bridge and glide down in a slow motion fireball. This dream series preceeded my cross country flight by 2 days. The day of the flight, I grabbed my late grandfather\\\'s suitcase packed with a few clean polo shirts and a pair of levi\\\'s and headed for Penn Station and a cross country train trip to San Francisco in a sleeper car.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ps. i\\\'m totally serious.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n------\r\n</p>\r\n<p>my sister\\\'s response to the above email questioning my sanity:</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyou want my professional opinion? hmm...you are fucking nuts. not really. um well, i don\\\'t realy know what to say. what is it telling? what kind of messages? i guess that if you got a new alarm clock that one might not be able to record such messages, but i am not really sure. have you noticed any new abilities that would indicate you have become a superhero? can you      \r\nleap tall buildings in a single bound? can you shoot spiderwebs out of your palm? i don\\\'t think you are nuts. have you told mom this? she might be able to help. she is the one with the third foot and everything. let me      \r\nthink on this. i will get back to you.....</p>\r\n\r\n<p>can anyone help?</p>'),(3574,'Itâ€™s another weekend and Iâ€™m so happy with where Iâ€™m headed. I think. I have only one complaint, and itâ€™s that I canâ€™t rely on anyone right now. This year has certainly started with a bang. Friends are turning vindictive, loved ones are fucked up; everyone in general is going crazy. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIâ€™m doing well in school and Iâ€™m even going on vacation! Thatâ€™s new for me. Iâ€™m actually starting to build some momentum in my life and people around me are not pleased. Why does it have to be this way? Itâ€™s bound to fall Iâ€™m just not sure if Iâ€™m at the 270 degree mark or the 90 degree mark. Both are a climb but one will surely be less disappointing. I need sushi, stat.'),(3575,'I\\\'m trying to figure out just what is happening with me and it is funny because I can\\\'t do that without you.<br><br>\r\nHaving just seen you again for the first time in several years a whirlwind of emotion has filled my being. We are both with others now and your situation definitely seems more stable. I love how I always consider you to be the unstable one out of the two of us, I guess thats just me projecting myself onto you. Your partner is pretty successful and you have admitted to really loving him. Honestly, I am both happy and sad -glad to know that you have found some one but sad that through some twist & turn of events we weren\\\'t brought back together in a way that that someone could have been me.<br><br>\r\nYou degrade me constantly now - knowing that you have the right after what happened and also that I find it an easier form of affection to receive then the mushy stuff.<br><br>\r\nYou still made time to see me...<br><br>\r\nI still love you...<br><br>\r\nWe talked about this and you know.<br><br>\r\nYou know that I\\\'m a tight ass and have already denied myself happiness once because of my messed up notions of what I am must do in this life. I must leave my mark somehow, so I\\\'ve foolishly dedicate myself to pursuing certain forms of power.<br><br>\r\nYou already knew this wasn\\\'t how it worked. I look at what you achieve in whatever community you are a a part of and you have already far outstripped any mark I have left.<br><br>\r\nA few people know or have ideas about the reality of this situation and I am constantly told that I have to let go of you completely to be able to move on. When we talked you said that you had done this and I don\\\'t hold any ill feelings towards you for it.<br><br>\r\nThe truth is that I don\\\'t ever want to let go or move on. We where together in a way that I will never be with anyone else.<br><br>\r\nWe may not be \\\'in love\\\' with each other still but there is no doubt that we love each other. I want you in my life forever.<br><br>\r\nI imagine visiting you and your family in the years to come. I imagine sharing a lot of lifes events with each other and helping each other along the way (granted it has been a little lopsided so far - I owe a great debt to you). I want whomever we end up with as partners to share our bond.<br><br>\r\nI am stubborn and it has cost me dearly in many ways but every so often it pays off - I can achieve rare things. I want the bond between us to be part of my mark that I obsess over.<br><br>\r\nThis is a highly inadequate explaination of my feelings but it seems that tears and a tight chest come alot easier right now then words.         \r\n'),(3576,'<pre style=\\\"font-family: mono, Courier New, Courier\\\">\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nagain\r\nall over again\r\n\r\nthe part\r\nwhere i get stuck\r\nin your thighs\r\nthe part\r\nwhere my voice gets stuck\r\nin your throat\r\nthe part\r\nwhere i sleepwalk\r\nall day long\r\n\r\nall the old\r\nprotestant\r\nprotestations\r\nevaporate into the channel\r\nthe fast groove\r\nthe quick line\r\nthrough the apex\r\nthe obsession back straight\r\n\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nagain\r\nwonderfully again\r\noh happy happy again\r\ni love you\r\nagain\r\nwith your five letters\r\nand your second chances\r\nand your revisitations\r\nwith your first kisses\r\nthe second time around\r\nwith your mind-numbing\r\nintoxication\r\nwith your stupid-fying\r\neffects\r\nleaving me all\r\ngrunts and phrases like\r\ni\\\'m so fucking hot for you\r\nbaby\r\n\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nto make sense\r\nto come round to the\r\ntop of the hour\r\nwith the chimes\r\nall the lame rhymes\r\nand the kid\\\'s room\\\'s floor\r\nthe sea of\r\nempty boxes\r\nand bratz doll clothing\r\nand stuffed cats\r\nand the random piles\r\nof clean laundry\r\n\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nto be fun\r\nwith the breaking\r\nand show going\r\nand karaoke\r\nand contests\r\nand wrestling\r\nand cheesy\r\nacoustic guitar versions\r\nof hair-band songs\r\n\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nin the spring\r\nand if it ever ended before\r\ni can\\\'t remember\r\ni can\\\'t see past holding\r\non to this now\r\nwith a fist so tight\r\nand nothing gold can stay\r\nand if you love something, set it...\r\n\r\nand i\\\'m the boy\r\nwho doesn\\\'t care\r\nabout anything\r\nbut the contour\r\nand condition\r\nof his heart\r\nin your relief\r\noff-center\r\nin your relief\r\nagain\r\n\r\nit\\\'s all beginning\r\nand i\\\'ve got a taste\r\nin my mouth\r\nand a tension\r\nin my tummy\r\nand a buzz\r\nin my ears\r\nand a smile\r\neverywhere\r\n</pre>'),(3577,'Every morning my mother wakes me up with tea and buttered scones for breakfast. The scones are little round things with points on them that crunch when I bite into their surface. I eat the little glistening things while she prays in the living room. The trees outside take on a dusty bluish tint as the sun come up and Benny Hinn preaches though out television. I have heard his deep southern voice at this time for so long that it is nearly indistinguishable from the dusty tint of the trees.<br><br>\r\n I dress myself now and I pick out two long pink socks to put on under my jumper. I pull the jumper over my head and button each button (then buckle each buckle of my Mary Janes which are small and black and gleaming with polish). My father takes me to school when the sun has gotten itself up properly. His car is a bright crimson red that reminds me of a crayon, it has been around for as long as I can remember. Every morning I measure myself against it. I am still not tall enough to see over the top of it.<br><br>\r\n I like to look out the windows at the people passing by and wait to see if they notice I am staring at them. There are big men that always smile at me from high up in their trucks. They waves as they drive away and I notice that their trucks are always so dirty that I canâ€™t see the stickers on them anymore. I can only tell that once they must have been new and blindingly white in the clear morning sun.<br><br>\r\nOur nuns are Franciscan and wear little caramel brown habits. Under them, their heads are shaven and on their fingers they wear little wedding bands. We saw one get married once, she knelt in front of the altar, looking up with her lips open and rapture on her face. I wondered what to do, for she was sitting there on the floor and noone was helping her. The nuns take us to church every morning. They make us walk in two straight lines, one of boys, another of girls. As we enter the church we blink from the darkness. The stained windows donâ€™t let the light in and the only things visible are the melting candles on either side of the aisles. The altar is illuminated by whole tiers of candles behind it. Above the altar is a golden sculpture of the Lord, dying on the cross. I wondered every time why he was so thin and why his ribs showed above the cloth at his waist. And I wondered where the wound at his stomach had gone. We shushed each other with hissing noises because each laugh of ours echoes down the aisles like the ringing of bells. We are silent though, passing a gated alcove. The nuns told us that inside was the mummified had of a saint and that it rested in a box covered in gold. On the other side was a Byzantine idol of the Madonna. It was always so dark inside that we could never see but I would squint and sometimes see a yellow flicker of light from within.<br><br>\r\nWe are made to sit very close together in the pews, one boy next to one girl. They tell us to kneel and the church resounds with the knocking of wood on its stone floor as the prayer pads fall out of their places. Father watches over us and smiles as the choir sing and a boy all in white swings a burner full of myrrh over us.\r\nNext to me sits a boy with dark black hair. He has long thin eyelashes and a thick Spanish accent that makes him move his little lips in the strangest ways to talk to me. He smiles and flashes his teeth like a wolf- they are so sharp that the others accused him of vampirism. He smelled always of the rose oil he applied every morning and his white pressed shirts hung over his gleaming wrists like a shroud on his pale skin.  There was nothing uncommon in his black eyes, but in the darkness of the church they seemed to reflect in them a sort of light over his eyelashes that could not have been there.<br><br>\r\nIn class the nuns would let us have half an hour of free time if we were all good. We couldnâ€™t talk and were not to leave our seats, but could play, silently, at whatever we wished while the nun sat at her desk reading the bible with its tissue thin pages.<br><br>\r\nThe boy sat next to me in class too and would write, compulsively in his journals. It was like a competition because I too would write and we would scribble in our notebooks for nearly the while time, looking at each otherâ€™s hands and eying the pages and pages of letters that we had put down with our number two pencils. He would smile at me sometimes as he finished, and we together would put our notebooks slowly away under our desks. I liked to play with my fingers then, pretending that this one was a woman, doing laundry or going out for a walk; and that one was a man, sleeping or playing a game. He liked to watch my fingers as they posed and traveled in little paths around my desk. His fingers would imitate mine, walking or dancing on his desk.  His women were more violent though, his men more spiteful, more angry. <br><br>\r\nHe made his hands kiss each other, rubbing the backs of their palms together.  One would walk around to a room, gesturing with a pinkie finger for the other to come. It would undress the other, slowly and carefully with its pinkie and thumb. Then, it would push it to lie down on the desk, an imaginary bed, and the two would move together as one hand with fingers intertwined. He flashed his sharp teeth at me then, mouthing words with his pink lips that I could feel hitting me, laden with his accent. Those words were an invitation.<br><br>\r\nLeaving the church is blinding. Even at night, the darkness outside is never as dark as the darkness inside the temple. We blink then, stepping into the light and we walk to school in two straight lines. <br><br>'),(3578,'<p> \r\nThe girl in the reflective window this morning\r\nwalking along side me\r\nis a stranger to me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The dark eyes. The long face.  The shadows that define the body, instead of a body that creates shadows. Hair that\\\'s beautiful, falling like water over the sunken eyes, listlessly lifeless.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I do not know her, and I wonder why she is following me.  And why all of Her thoughts have made their way into my head. </p>'),(3579,'<p> I don\\\'t want to drag you down to where I am. \r\nYou\\\'ve been here before.  You know the smell of the dungeon.  The lack of hope. the darkness.  You know the demons that circulate between the bars, and freely enter your brain, piss on it, chew off a few old memories and taint them with distorted new ones.  I hate my life, and I hate where I am.  My only solace seems to be isolation - my greatest gift to my friends is to not see them, so they don\\\'t become tainted with my darkness.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>But that doesn\\\'t work, because they\\\'re my friends.  So I see them, and I lock the darkness up deep, deep inside me where it cannot touch them, cannot tarnish them.  And I appear to the world like a ray of light and sunshine, hope and peace and good will.  I am perfection.  I am beautiful and funny and witty, smart and attractive, intelligent, a little socially awkward.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p> But why?  Why am I socially awkward, you might wonder!  Because of these demons.  Because it smells like acrid puke down here, and I hate it, and I wish I could escape my body forever and flee.  I think I\\\'m protecting the world from my stench.  I shower frequently to rid myself of it, to smell artificially clean and fresh to the world.  You love that fresh, clean smell.  And you love me.  And perhaps you\\\'ll even follow me. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> I don\\\'t want to drag you down to where I am. </p>'),(3580,'<p>Some mornings, you know, I just don\\\'t care.</p>\r\n<p>And the world spins around me, violently, more like a merry-go-round on crack.  And the music is riotous in my ears.  The kids\\\' hair is flying about, whipping them in the eyes, and they\\\'re clinging to the brass poles of the donkey, screaming, blood drained from their delicate little baby fingers.  But the music screams louder than they do, with obnoxious ebullience, drowning out their terror, and making it sound like joyous thrill. After all, a child\\\'s shriek is a joyous sound! To those who don\\\'t know any better.  Let\\\'s go tickle the children, to make them shriek more, and have more fun. </p>\r\n<p> And that\\\'s when I don\\\'t care.  Why?  Why fight the centrifugal force?  Why hold on?  Why pretend that this music is fun, and that your face, twisted into a contortion of pain and fright, is actually a big fat smile?\r\n</p> \r\n<p>  There\\\'s one thing I can hold on to.  That every damn morning this world is still turning.  So I pull myself out of bed, and grab on to the shiny brass pole, and scream for my life, and hold on. </p> \r\n'),(3581,'<p>this is a test, because I wrote a really nice poem, and it\\\'s lost, and I hate that. </p>'),(3582,'<p>Do you know the sound a cockroach makes? \r\nI don\\\'t - I\\\'ve never heard them, only swept them off the kitchen table when I left the pizza box and a tipped-over bottle of Corona sitting out overnight. But I think it would be like a timid \\\"screech!  screech!\\\" </p><p>Do you know the sound a cockroach makes, when you sweep them off the pizza box, sitting on the table covered with dust?  I think it would be like a \\\"scuttle, scuttle.\\\"  Their asses drag on the floor as they walk, you know.  That\\\'s why they\\\'re such disgusting creatures.  </p><p> Do you know the sound, when you finally get a cockroach firmly beneath the sole of your vinyl boot, and you push down firmly pivoting upon your heel, and the green juice of cockroach blood goes shooting out to the left, making a gooey stain on your already-stained floor board?  Do you know that satisfying \\\"Crunch\\\" of a million crunches, as every plate of his miserable exoskeleton is busted, every antenna is detached, every arm is dismembered, every eye is exploded, stomach juices flowing out uncontained, the death of a Cockroach.  </p><p> Do you know that sound?  The sound a cockroach makes? </p><p> That was me, when you shut your mouth and grabbed your bag, and walked away with my heart in your back pocket. You bitch.'),(3583,'Goodbye to the steady pace of this city,<br>\r\nGoodbye to the streetcar drivers ringing their bells like madmen,<br>\r\nGoodbye to the CN Tower, that great silver cock rearing above the penetrable city,<br>\r\nGoodbye to dark coldness, dressed smartly in its black peacoat,<br>\r\nGoodbye to the sick, sad lake,<br>\r\nto the smoke, slush and sighs of winter,<br>\r\nHello my new life! <br>\r\nCome, I embrace you with all my naive idealism held high<br>\r\nOh adventerous, wonderful life, hello.<br>\r\n\r\n '),(3584,'The comfortable complacency of the hostel, its inhabitants flung out into the city by day,\r\nand retrieved carefully again at the end of light.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI wake each morning with the springs of the lumpy hostel bed etching knots of pain into my back. I wake in your fort of bedsheets and blankets, untangling myself from this den of inquiety, where I teach you what the word \\\'seduce\\\' means, tracing understanding onto your skin with my lips and fingertips.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI wake each morning half expecting to find you gone, having followed your sense of lust and adventure to another bed. But this morning I woke up to find myself tangled in your arms, bedsheets, legs, your insistant mouth searching, your face sweet with sleep- such a blank canvass on which to trace the fine shapes of love and life.<br><br>'),(3585,'<P>Hanging out in Mission/Fashion valley on a Saturday is really the lamest experience of all time.  I\'m doing the desert wildflower thing tomorrow.  I\'m outside my hotel room on the stairwell while the maid cleans up my room.   This confirms in my own mind my commitment to writing from outside the box.  A couple just walked by while I am surfing the internet, listening to Sheryl Crow on the speakers.  She is singing about soaking up the sun.  I just bought this to get my head in The Game.  Crow is an attempt yours not-so-truely to BECOME southern Californian.    Smoking a cigarillo, I\'m feeling the SoCal hybrid Hunter S. Thompson 2004 vibe.  All I need is a Halloween rubber bald spot and a convertible BMW.  These days, had he done it all over again, I\'m certain he would have had wi-fi for his laptop and a directional antenna for dropping in on his neighbor\'s internet connection instead of a .357.  He would have had a convertible BMW instead of a T-Top Firebird.  He would still be hopped up on a drug cocktail though--consider that it would be a coctail of prescribed housewife drugs, corporate productivity drugs, and norcal weed.  He would be the extraverted productive worker, always on the sunny side of life.  He would be the crazy yuppie IT contractor getting paid too much for too little.\r\n</P><P>  \r\nThis is a transcript of the Maid encounter with Austin trying to mumble appropriately around his cigar, ashes falling on the laptop\'s keyboard:\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nAustin (thinking he would start speaking spanish now): Hola\r\n<BR>The Maid: Hey, should I do your dishes?\r\n<BR>Austin: I wouldn\'t touch those if I were you!  \r\n<BR>The Maid: Yeah, I just started today.  I think they want me to.\r\n<BR>Austin: You have no idea how horrible those dishes are, rotten bastards!\r\n<BR>The Maid: \'K\'.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEarlier in the day, my experience at Baja Fresh Burrito Mall Store emphasized that living in Tijuana is preferable to this.  As much as I like skinny white girls in capris and shabby-chic single stitch embroidered baseball jerseys, filthy mutts vying for my leftovers are more appealing.  It has been said that all this mall community stuff is humorous when smoking pot.  I wouldn\'t know.  Hunter would definitely know.\r\n\r\n</P>'),(3586,'<p> Dear ...  dunno.  Letter head.  Thingie I\\\'m writing to, </p><p> Hey, how goes?  Let\\\'s small talk for three paragraphs. </p><p> One: weather, anyone? </p><p> Two: Food.  Mmmm hungry.  dinner plans? </p><p> Three: Pop culture time!  throw out a name, I\\\'ll catch it and throw it back. Your Hunter S. Thompson take on my Cheryl Crow. and we\\\'ll mingle with the stars by association to them on a first-name basis.  I\\\'m cool.  You\\\'re cool. and we know it cuz we both listen to 94.9 </p><p> Life. work. toil. exercise. news. families. happenings on. what I saw on the street today. You doing good?  Yeah, yeah, I\\\'m doing fine.  Life is just fine.  Fiiiine.  God, you\\\'re a golly good friend. We\\\'re having fun.\r\n\r\n</p><p>  Now.  What was that all about?  Let me tell you the dream I had when I was awake just now.\r\n\r\n</p><p> I was trying to remember my childhood, because so much of my memory has vanished -- gone to the blown neural fuse.  And after some time of sitting in the darkness, remembering shapes, and scattered incidents. I realized.  I\\\'m a weakling.  I\\\'m a weak little kid who never learned how to fight back.  How to swear.  How to tell dirty jokes, or be cool on the playground, or put other kids down to make me feel superior.  I\\\'m just a weak, wimpy little kid.  Like a wet noodle. The noodle that bends doesn\\\'t break. It\\\'s a great way to avoid conflict. I never fight back.  I never face a foe.  People tell me all the shit they want to, everything that\\\'s crass and rude and offensive and critical and manipulative and unreasonable, and I don\\\'t argue.  I hold my head like a golden vault, with my tongue locked safely inside, right next to my heart.  And I nod agreement, and walk away, firmly disagreeing, and resolutely refusing to combat over it. Even refusing to open my eyes and see it.  And then I work on immediately erasing it from my memory, so it can never, ever hurt me. </p><p>\r\nSo there it is, maybe.  an idea.  I just wish I could identify why all this anger and solitude and dark pain traces the outline of my heart these days.  Some say the answer is locked away in your childhood.  But seems to me that while I was busy locking myself in safely from the vicious world around me, I was busy locking the answer out.\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\nYeah, well, gotta be running now.  Sure has been great chatting with you.  Let\\\'s catch up sometime, huh?  You\\\'re really looking great.  Take care of yourself now.  Hope to see you soon.\r\n\r\n</p><p>\r\nLove.\r\n</p><p>\r\nPepon\r\n\r\n'),(3587,'we live our lives, confronting, searching, challenging.. and it all we fucking ever do. where is the laid back, yet rich life i am looking for. rich in terms of feelings, friends and  carpe-diem-days.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe romance of the road trip, the escape from the humdrum existance.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni have never forgotten the girl i met two years ago, and a half-year before that. the feelings she made me experience. not love, but with the same gentle touch. like a soul-mate, she seemed.\r\n<br><br>\r\nour ship really seemed to be different. but i guess others too would say.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni got the song from which the title above is taken played for me today. and it inspired me somewhat to my roadtrip-feeling. i like the feeling of flying, to feel the breeze.. but then you land, and things get back to normal.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni have felt like shit for three days or two. the depressiveness come and goes. i have thought of it as a focus problem. a problem deciding what is important and what is not. but it is not like that at all i am afraid. it is stuck inside me. it will not disappear.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe sum is apathy, melancoly. still moments come and go.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nif only i could figure out how to fill the emptyness...\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<i>april 26th, 2004 (21:48)</i>'),(3589,'There\\\'s a whore on my back, and her teeth are around my neck.  I should be enjoying this.\r\n<br><br>\r\nBut sex is spare, and violence growing ever more tiring.  Who made me for this?  Me?  Does it matter when I tell myself, \\\'oops.\\\'  \r\n<br><br>\r\n____\\\'s face is still healing, and he\\\'s grinning.  sooner or later he\\\'s going to wake up and remember that those scars aren\\\'t going away.  should i have killed the man who did it?  the law would have been on my side.  legal murder was right there, waiting for me.  and i went with my morals.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe best part of the job is the worst.  and the stuff that makes up for it... isn\\\'t any better.  i\\\'m the friend waiting to stab you.  that\\\'s my job.  i\\\'ve gotten exceedingly good at it over the last year.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m the way out of the anxiety.  i\\\'m the way into the jails and prisons.  through lies or violence, i get my way it seems.  a gentle voice, an improbable scenario.  am i really interrogating them, i think they wonder.  but i am.  i\\\'m seeing more than they want to believe.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s all very strange.  my only joy and my greatest misery.\r\n<br><br>'),(3588,'<p>My girlfriend of nearly two years broke up with me in March.  We\\\'d started to break up last July, when I discovered she and a friend of mine had been having a small but clandestine love affair via email and IM.  Flustered but committed, we slowly healed from that and it seemed all was well.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>We spent the remainder of a breezy, beautiful summer together, and an introspective but in some ways pretty magical fall.  In January, we took off to Europe for our second annual two-week vacation, filled with rapturous music, back alley food revelations and discoveries aplenty.  It seemed that things were going to work out after all.  We had always loved one another madly.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Unbeknownst to me, she and my pal had kept up correspondance even as they promised not to talk for a while, and she arranged their first meeting in person (amorously) for the day when we got back from our trip- when I would be at a trade show my work had sensitively assigned me to.  I literally stepped off of the plane from London and boarded one to Anaheim.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She was oddly distant that week while I was gone..  She never seemed to be able to specify what she was doing, where she was, without a great deal of thought, \\\"um\\\"s and \\\"uh\\\"s..  I thought nothing of it.  If anything I am a totally blind person when it comes to deciet.  I cling to the concept of people being honest far longer than it pays me to, most of the time.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>In February, I found out about she and my friend having started up their little e-tryst again, and that the conflict in her heart was making it tough for her to stay in the relationship.  I was not, however, told that they had actually met up <i>while I was away</i>.  I, in my typical fashion, threw myself to the floor to grovel and forgive and promise to forget, only please, I said, don\\\'t leave me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She agreed to stay, but we decided that us living apart for a while would be a good step forward for us.  We had moved in together only 3 months into our relationship out of financial need.  Both of us had fallen quickly into the codependant trap, and lost a lot of fire in our individual passions.  Despite the hardship of her moving away, I was very happy, despite the fact that I should have been angry, or at least, offended enough by the situation to leave her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She hastily moved to a room in San Francisco, which really set me afire looking for a place out there.  It had been a near-lifelong dream to live in the city, and I\\\'d been languishing in warehouses in West Oakland for 4 years..  Time for a change.  This seemed like the time to do it.  I found a place in SoMa that was cute and affordable, and started a two-weeking lobbying process to land it. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>All the while I was on this wicked uncertainty-generated adrenaline high, and I\\\'m thinking, \\\"Oh my goodness, I\\\'m going to be able to live in my favorite city and in my own place only a mile away from hers..  ..and then someday maybe we\\\'ll share it and it\\\'ll be awesome..\\\".  I started looking at the Ikea website for ideas for new furniture- a couch, a dining room table, dresser, how exciting!</P>\r\n\r\n<p>A few days before I moved into my new place, she and I met with the landlord so I could sign the lease and she could see the place.  I signed it with gusto, and she gave me crap about \\\"out-housing\\\" her already, as she had a room and I a 2 bedroom apartment.  As we left, I turned to her, half-sheepishly and said, \\\"Do you like it?\\\".  She looked at me and said, \\\"Well, yes, of course, but the real question is do <i>you</i> like it, because what I think doesn\\\'t count in this situation.\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Caught a little off-guard, I stammered, \\\"Well, honey, I mean, I\\\'m just asking you as a friend, and, you know, we\\\'d talked before about maybe moving back in together someday, so I just wanted to know what you thought of the place..\\\".  \\\"Uh, well, I wouldn\\\'t really think too far ahead just yet, but yes, it\\\'s lovely, anyway, could you give me a ride to our place in Oakland?  I need to pack some more stuff\\\".</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Somehow I still didn\\\'t get it, and I soldiered on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At Ikea that afternoon, I was buying a couch, and as we often did, (being little cocooning loves) I called her to see what she thought of what I was considering.  Again, I got not a light-hearted \\\"Oh yeah babe, that\\\'s really cute!  I say go for it!\\\" but a, \\\"Really that\\\'s *your* perogative, sweetheart.  The whole point is that *you* get to pick what you want, don\\\'t you see?\\\" dismissal.  The disparaged frown on her face was audible.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On my way back from dropping off the couch I chose of my own volition, I called her, and was summarily broken up with.  She explained little, just said that it was over and that that was her final decision.  I scrambled home to find her there, crying softly on the bed.  What has changed?  I asked.  Everything, she said.  How, why?  You haven\\\'t even moved out yet! I argued.  It doesn\\\'t matter, it\\\'s just not the same, she said.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>Time stood still for a while..  My insides turned to quick-set concrete.  My blood ran cold, then dry.  This lovely relationship I\\\'d tried so hard to bolster as it bled to death slowly for half an irreplacable year had finally breathed its last.  And why, before we\\\'d even had a chance to try out our new, separate-living configuration?  It simply didn\\\'t add up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For a couple of weeks after the breakup, I didn\\\'t talk to her.  We had a couple of quickly aborted attempts at friendship too soon after, where a night watching movies and making dinner dissolved into one or both of us crying inconsolably and a pledge to not contact one another for a while.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I even had breakfast with my former friend to try to heal things up.  He implored me to ask him anything, anything at all.  I asked, \\\"Have you two had physical contact of any kind?\\\", and \\\"are you pursuing a relationship now?\\\".  The answer to both of these was a vastly reassuring, incredulous, \\\"No, of course not!  I couldn\\\'t let that happen.  I mean, we have interest in eachother, but it doesn\\\'t make sense to do anything right now, so soon after the breakup and all that drama, you know?  It just wouldn\\\'t be right\\\". I smiled meekly into my french toast and a little weight lifted from my heart.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>One night after a quite nice, emotionally uneventful dinner at my new place, she checked her email from my computer, and then left to go to a party.  When I sat down to do some checking of my own, I found her email account still open.  Breathless, I pondered- should I read?  What will I do if I find something?</P>\r\n\r\n<p>I read, and I read, and I found out some very unsettling things.  She had cheated on me with him while I was gone at the tradeshow.  They\\\'d been sending lovestruck, sprawling tomes of poetry and history and all of the bookwise passions they shared to one another for months- far before our breakup and even before our trip abroad.  The most recent email thread talked about sex, marriage, kids, plans for the future and musings about past relationships, all written in aching detail and the floweriest of prose.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I cried and cried, but soon the sadness gave way to anger.  I called both of them and left searing, volcanic, vocabulary-stretching missives of pain, broken trust, and misplaced love and time on their answering machines.  I had so much to say that it took me two messages per person to get it out (30-second limit be <i>damned</i>).  To my surprise, he flipped, and told her to stay away, that he couldn\\\'t take the drama anymore.  He quickly found a girlfriend and summarily brushed her off.  She\\\'d lost both of us.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>..and I became her support structure.  I listened to hours of moaning about the loss of myself and my friend, pushing back anger and being the best friend I could.  Eventually that subsided, but we remained close friends.  We became so close again during these times that we became lovers again, slowly, cautiously.  A hug here, a snuggle there..  A stolen kiss, a patently offered and accepted kiss..  Over the course of a few weeks we were back into things completely, in a physical sense.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>I, surprisingly, was happy in this situation.  I had a great lover.  I had moved on to the point where it didn\\\'t bother me that we both had other love interests, and we both got to relive some of what used to make us such a firey perfect couple- physically, anyhow.  Also, it lessened the pull I felt towards tawdry flings, because I was already getting what I needed from this very convenient arrangement.  I thought little of the consequences.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had to end this second relationship one week ago, when she started to go tangibly crazy every time she heard of me hanging out with somebody female.  It didn\\\'t seem to matter to her that I wasn\\\'t seeing anyone and that these girls were truly \\\"just friends\\\".  The very thought of me creating even emotional connections with other women sent her into apopleptic fits, and it would take hours of explaining away to abate it.  I felt shortchanged.  I started to feel guilty when I\\\'d plan to see one of these friends, and I hoped to hell that they wouldn\\\'t write glowing things about our nights together in their blogs, all of which she\\\'d started to comb for information.  She, however, saw no problem in her continuing to long for and pursue my friend, and giving constant updates on her progress.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>The moment where it broke for me and I knew it was over was when she said, \\\"You know, for some reason it doesn\\\'t bother me when I see other people, but when you do it totally freaks me out..  I wonder why?\\\".  This was said, mind you, without a hint of tongue in cheek, and the question was not meant rhetorically.  I ended it the next day.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>The day after, she started hanging out with my friend again.  I haven\\\'t seen or talked to her very much since.  I have done my best to be so busy that I don\\\'t care.  I have been so busy being busy that I have scarcely had time to regret anything or to indulge in belabored, milky flashbacks.  It\\\'s been kinda nice, actually.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We\\\'re planning to meet up tonight, to make sushi.  We\\\'re going to make dinner, talk for a bit, then I\\\'m going to give her a ride home.  As I turned off of my exit on my way back from work, today, I started randomly daydreaming about her body, and how it felt to touch it.  I saw her, naked, floating wispily in midair, blonde hair flowing in impossibly beautiful waves.  I thought of the part of her side just above her hip, where it curves just so..  As I did this, I unconciously moved my right hand through my steering wheel, and slipped my hand around its curved back.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>For just a millisecond, my heart jumped and I was holding her, feeling her irresistable geometry in my hand.  Just as quickly as the vision came, it was gone, and I was left fondling my steering wheel.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>In a way, I can liken my missing her to a recovered heroin addict missing his fix.  Though I know her presence is corrosive to me, she haunts my dreams and every unattended silence in my day, unless I forcibly push her out.  At the time of this writing, she seems to be flaking on our sushi plans.  I\\\'ll never learn, will I?</P>'),(3590,'I ride the Metra train into Chicago most weekeday mornings.  The morning commute is usually silent, somber even.  On the way in, I almost always take the opportunity to lose myself in a book, savoring the last 30 minutes I have to defer reality setting in.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThings haven\\\'t changed much in 11 years.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI make my way through the crowds of people streaming from the platform and through the revolving doors.  I watch other people teeming in relative silence on their way to their destinations, and most times I glance into the dark glass by the now-defunct Fannie May Candy store and catch a glimpse of my reflection, always checking to see if I have truly morphed into just another face in the respectable business crowd.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI see some people walking with odd gaits or limps, and count myself lucky to still be young enough to not have that type of hindrance to look forward to during my morning trek to work, while other people jostle and push me and others around me, jockeying for a better position in the lemming parade.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThere is no real mystery to where we are going.  It\\\'s the same jump off the cliff every morning.  No need to rush.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI cross over the Madison Street bridge.  It\\\'s always the same homeless guy and the same two people seling the Red Streak and the Red Eye, dumbed-down, colorful, \\\"hip\\\" papers for the younger crowd that marketing research has discovered hasn\\\'t the attention span to deal with the \\\"regular\\\" newspapers.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m a court reporter.  It\\\'s my job to record all the horrible things can go awry in the world of man and machine.  As I cross the bridge, I often wonder, with every bus-induced jounce of it, if that will be the blow that undoes it so that it collapses and we are all pitched into the dirty river that I can see beyond the guard rail, but even more tellingly, through the cracks between thin metal wafers of the access doors that I occasionally walk upon.\r\n<br><br>\r\nSometimes there are homeless people on the bridge, panhandling.  There\\\'s always at least one on the far end of the bridge by the Civic Opera House.  Sometimes their pitch is plaintive, sometimes it\\\'s just plain aggressive.  Sometimes I am moved by sorrow and pity to give them some money.  The angry ones anger me in turn.  I cannot save the world.  Once I gave a few dollars to a homeless man on the bridge, and the next day he chased after me, expectantly when I did not repeat the performance, as though by giving him money that one time, I had somehow entered into a contract I was obligated to fulfill.\r\n<br><br>\r\nThe pendulum swings back and forth in my mind always.  If I give money to these people, am I truly helping someone out?  Or am I simply enabling crack addicts, drunkards, and people who\\\'ve found out that panhandling is more profitable than a 9-to-5 job?  Sometimes I don\\\'t care and I give, regardless of if I know very well the money\\\'s going to go to support a habit.  A few cruel twists of fate, or less, and I could easily be the woman begging on the bridge.\r\n<br><br>\r\nBut then there are the times when I see a really aggressive panhandler by a restaurant, giving a plaintive spiel about how they need to eat.  So when I go in, I buy and extra meal and hand it to them on my way out.  The few times I\\\'ve done this instead of giving money, I\\\'ve noticed a distinct sullenness in the recipient, as though I\\\'ve somehow gypped them.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nThe juxtaposition is jarring:  People panhandling while men in business suits and women in high heels walk by, ignoring them as if they do not exist.  A lot of people are on cell phones.  A lot of them just look vacant and preoccupied.  It\\\'s weird when I recognize people I\\\'ve never met or even spoken to before.  Some I recognize from my train line over the years.  I see attorneys who I recognize on the street, and they don\\\'t see me, and I wonder that if they did, would they recognize me in return.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI hear people on their cell phones or talking with walking companions.  Most of them are talking about business stuff, whether it be law or accounting or some big deal they\\\'re about to make.  I wonder if they wonder about the incongruity of their speaking about their deals while they blithely pass by a homeless person huddled against a building.\r\n<br><br>\r\nI wonder if they\\\'ve ever realized that we all keep jumping off the same cliff day after day after day after day...<br><br>'),(3591,'<p>It\\\'s midnight.  The hotel lobby is shined so bright that the chairs are sitting upside down, asking me to turn myself inside-out and join them in the reflection world.  The chandeliers are dimmed.  Nonsense.  They have crystal chandeliers with candles, but they\\\'re glass, and the candles are light bulbs above white plastic tubes. So the management simply adjusts the voltage for us red-eye customers who don\\\'t sleep well in stuffy hotel rooms. </p><p>\r\n\r\nYou could say I\\\'m livin\\\' the life.  I couldn\\\'t. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI should be working.  I should be sleeping. or exercising. Or out drinking and dancing. Living. Yes, that\\\'s it.  I should be living.  But it\\\'s 1:30 (I was lying), and I\\\'m sitting on a couch that\\\'s too comfortable, in air-conditioning that\\\'s too cold, with my laptop on my lap and my feet on a mahogany table. I\\\'m wide awake, and wishing I were drunk, so there would be a reason to go back to my room on the 12th floor, and pass out on the starched linens. \r\n</p><p>\r\nNow I\\\'m wondering how to make a point of this article.  How to tie it together, have a punch line and a moral - a tidbit of thought for you, gentle reader, to hold between your mandibles and suck some juice of existence out of.  But what could it be?  There\\\'s a beeping noise coming from somewhere, like a utility truck stuck in nightmarish reverse all night long.  Maybe it\\\'s the alarm going off in a room without a guest.  Maybe just a bug planted by terrorists to drive us crazy, so we kill ourselves and give the Taliban a break from their labours.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nNothing is real here.  The palm trees outside the window are illuminated in yellow sulfer lights, so they match the color of the drapes - a gentle bile-green.  They blow in the wind, but instead of being tropically pleasant, it looks like a scene from a TV drama: <i>The revenge of the un-dead palms</i>. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd I\\\'m down to a 10% battery, so even this article has to be left in the same state as the hotel lobby.  void. cold. shiney. inverted. artificial. \r\n</p><p>\r\nPray, sleep, please come and release me away from this tropical paradise.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3592,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã \n	\n\r\r\"\Z)$+*($\'\'-2@7-0=0\'\'8L9=CEHIH+6OUNFT@GHEÃ¿Ã›Ã¿Ã„\Z%&\'()*456789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¡Ã¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ±Ã²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ã„Ã¿Ã„$4Ã¡%Ã±\Z&\'()*56789:CDEFGHIJSTUVWXYZcdefghijstuvwxyzâ€šÆ’â€žâ€¦â€ â€¡Ë†â€°Å â€™â€œâ€â€¢â€“â€”Ëœâ„¢Å¡Â¢Â£Â¤Â¥Â¦Â§Â¨Â©ÂªÂ²Â³Â´ÂµÂ¶Â·Â¸Â¹ÂºÃ‚ÃƒÃ„Ã…Ã†Ã‡ÃˆÃ‰ÃŠÃ’Ã“Ã”Ã•Ã–Ã—Ã˜Ã™ÃšÃ¢Ã£Ã¤Ã¥Ã¦Ã§Ã¨Ã©ÃªÃ²Ã³Ã´ÃµÃ¶Ã·Ã¸Ã¹ÃºÃ¿Ãš9Ã‡Ã–Â¢3@Ã’yq3Â±Ã£â€“Ã†+Å Â¤Ãâ€?}Å’Ã·Â°Â¸Â¤Â§wÂ¤Ã…t	EQÅ \\QÅ \\Qp\n(\"â€°Â¯â€¦â€¹+â€¹hÃ¤RÃ§Â¨ÃÂ®^Ã«Ã‡WawÃšÃ˜Ã‚ÂªO{â€™xÃ¿RO`?Ã‚â€ºÂªh7Ã–:tÃqhÃˆÂ²Å’Ã‰â€¢ np:Ã¦Â®)Â½Ã„Ã¬Â¶2Ã£Â¸â€œ1Â»A,Ã0+IÃ·@ÃµÃµ\'Å¡Ã™_	Ã«ÃÃ­â„¢#Â¶gÃ€2Â»Å“Â¨Ã¾Å¸â€¡ZÃÃ’-\"Ã“?<J7Å¸Ã¢Ã‡Ã˜?m=Ã„Ã’BÂ 6[â€œ?ÃÃ¢khÃ˜Â§MÂ£ÃŽn4uÃ“Â®Ã§Â·Vâ€¦â€“6;Â²Ã„Ã?Å½:qZ67+6Ãˆâ€º\r) !\'Ã§Â°9Ã·j_Gr`ID;Ã–6%ÃŠÅ¾}ÃsPÃžM-Ã¬;PÃ£?yÂ¢ÃœÃ‹R$Â¹edzÂ¼,^Ã™Å½[hÃœsÅ¾qÃ)ÂªÃš\\Ã­sÂ§$Ãnâ€“Ã›Ã™Ã±Â±l\rÃ„rOsÃ–Â¬Ã—Â«N