Ã½Ã—Ã˜~Ã¿Ã¤Ã®!2Â³ËœQÃ’Ã™â€ eÃ©Ã©â€œÃ“5Ã:mBW)6Â£tÂ¤Ë†Ã£â€°1Ã¤Ã¤{Å½*Ã•â€ â€¦gÂ¨Â©Å¡HJMÂ¸\rÃ‘Â¼cÂ©Ãªâ€žÂ¼â€ºÃ—Â½t\ZH	hÂ Â¸C!,2pI?Ãˆ`VvÂ±w3Ã¯aÃ’Â´Ã«Dâ€ 1<nÂ§Rl1Ã§Â¨#â€¢jÃ²KiÃ£Ã™\nÃ…/ÃŽHâ„¢:Ã Ã˜Ã¿IÃœÃ¶kÂ¹Ã¨pÂ¥Ã„pÃ›Â¤f\"Ã±)pFWÅ“Ã­Ã‡|ÃºÃ’ÃœÃ›#>Ã·@C\rÂ®qXÃºnÂ¥qim-ÂµÃ¤Lna?Ã¯H_Â¯Ã¦&Ë†Ã‚ÃžY;Â·â€¢Â»Å¸Ã¿Â¢FÃˆÃ­\"Å½Cg?ÃŒyÃ­Ã’Â´Â£?Â®TÂ¾Ã¸mÂ­Â¥Q\'â€”nâ€¦|ÃÃ¶[ÃÃµÃ‰Ã§=8Ã¹Ã‡â€ºÂ ;,Jâ€˜Ã•Ã¾CÅ“FsÃ¹\\U}\no:ÃŽH3â€“â€°Â·.ÂºÃºÃ¹Ã¼ÃªÂ¢\rÂµÂ±Ã’Ã‚?Â¨ÃªRÂ·Å¡Ã‘Ã»|;Ã†Ã¤â€˜0Ã©â€¢Ã³5ÃÂ®Ã•Â¾Ã³c@Â®jk;â€ž+ÃŒNeÂºâ€˜Ã½>aZ1ÃŽÃ·Âº)Â¼Â¢Â¼3gAÃ§â‚¬B	Ã»Â¥8ÃˆIW<ÃºnÃ­Ã¸Ã–Ã™Â¶ÂµÂ¶pcÂ´?XÃ¿'),(3595,'The glow of the street lamps burns through her basement window.  Clothing is scattered here and there in non-descript piles of clean and dirty. The TV at the end of her bed is dreadfully dusty and there are several glasses sitting on the desk, ringed from the evaporation of whatever was last in them. The desk is littered with empty pens, debit receipts scattered around the monitor, unlabelled minidiscs, and half empty compacts of cheap makeup that have been around for far too long. Pictures on the walls remind of better times, smaller waists, and fulfilling relationships that have since past. <br><br>\r\n\r\nEverywhere she sits, is uncomfortable; too hot, too cold, too familiar. The phone wonâ€™t ring tonight and thatâ€™s just fine with her, she prefers her thoughts not to be interrupted. Tonight is one of those nights that one might as well write off; cause there is just nothing that can be done that would inspire activity. Music needs to be made, assignments need to be finished, clothes need to be cleaned, sit-ups and lunges need to be done, none of then will. Instead with a heavy head and tired eyes she surrenders to the uncomfort of her bed. Maybe she is getting sick, that might explain the ache. <br><br>\r\n\r\nStaring at the ceiling she realizes the absence of someone. She is in her position but he is not to be found. She closes her eyes and imagines things as they were, times that seemed important and moments that passed with too much ease. Memories of recent, forced guilt, the touch of his hand on her shoulder made her roll over and pretend to sleep. Why did she do this? There is nothing more in this world that she wants than to be in his space and welcome with all her heart his closeness. She is cold and divided, calculatingly jealous. There has never been a need for any of it, he indeed loved her, and not just that but planned everything around their time, and was sincerely devoted to her comfort and happiness without ever giving up himself. Some people just love easier than others. The touch of her warm hand mimics his motions and for a brief time he is sent back to her with rampant lust. She will call his name only to be alone when she shudders and opens her eyes. She pulls the blanket over herself and rolls on her side toward the wall. Markings and scuffs of passion along the wall force her to roll onto her stomach and fill her pillow with tears. There wonâ€™t ever be anyone like him, she is certain of that. <br><br>'),(3598,'t'),(3599,'t'),(3600,'t'),(3601,'t'),(3602,'Iâ€™m on my own, something that hasnâ€™t occurred to me in quite some time. It is confounding how much the human mind acquiesces to feelings of comfort and stability, and even more so how it challenges forces against it. Maybe itâ€™s good to have the rug pulled out from us every now and again. If you donâ€™t you end up sinking into emptiness and complacency. How can two young and promising people drag each other down into doubt and despair? I just wish for being able to look at each other in a crowded room or an empty forest and know you have someone on this planet that sees things the way you do. I have always paired my love with respect and that will never change. I just wish I could know when I have found another who is searching for the same. Itâ€™s so easy to think back at people you knew than it is to look ahead at people you havenâ€™t met, but there are few things that I will be able to experience and not think of you. <br><br>\r\n\r\nHot sand beneath my toes and the blazing sun on my hair,<br> \r\nThe sound of silence and isolation from unpopulated areas, <br>\r\nThe rhythmic lapping of water onto a shore, and walking for eternity along the edge of Lake Huron,<br>\r\nThe feeling of perception and reference regarding many archived moments and remarks,<br>\r\nThe feeling that I deserve nothing but the best and should demanded nothing less, <br>\r\nThe smell of you all over me, and the warmth of sheets from a bed occupied for many hours, <br>\r\nOrdering obscene amounts of take out just because you can,<br>\r\nThe marks teeth leave on just about any kind of food, <br>\r\nA cold refreshing Heineken on a hot afternoon, <br>\r\nSpending long cold evenings smoking in front of a fire, <br>\r\nA perfectly executed British accent, <br>\r\nThe bleachy smell of a freshly cleaned hotel room, and its promise of privacy,<br>\r\nThe feeling that I couldnâ€™t be with a better person in this world,<br>\r\nLaughing, 	and laughing, 	and laughing with each other.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt is impossible to disregard nearly five years of experiences, and arrogant to think that none of it was worth while. Iâ€™m not looking to forget any of this, only to lament at not being able to share it with you anymore.   <br>'),(3603,'<p>I thought that I had something to add to the matter, but I guess I was wrong. I can\\\'t stand goodbyes\\\' and break ups\\\'. Like the lyric of <i>A Letter to Elise</i> by The Cure I cannot forget the moments where everything was perfect. Anyhow, things change and those moments maybe never come back even if you hold on and wait for them to. To move on and carry only those memories that keep you goin\\\' might be the best solution, often so.</p><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m working on a script, but I\\\'m stuck. I cannot decide whether I want a happy ending or not. I had an idea to make an alternate ending, but it is harder to write without knowing the end right now. But a particularily sad movie maybe needs an open kind of ending at least implying happiness to not make the whole movie a travel in dark and dispair. I think Magnolia.</p><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>The questions about my ending of life used to interest me more in the past. The question is; is the best way to die to do so after all happy days, remembering the past with joy and happiness.. Or is the best way to die, doing so hit by a bus on the very happiest day of your life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The second sounds absurd, but if it ain\\\'t the choice of most people, isn\\\'t that the same as telling those people ending their carrier on the top of it that they\\\'re givin\\\' up the race in advance..</p><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>Nothing is that simple as of yes and no, of course. I\\\'m just playing with my thougts. As for the first part of this text, things usually work out fine. As for the second, we\\\'ll see. As for the question in the subtitle of the text, I do not know.</p>'),(3604,'These lakes are indeed great. Even Lake Ontario seems deep and menacing if you find the right pier in Oakville. I love how the sky showcases the waves and ripples, and light dances confidentially upon them. Absolute power. There are few things as stabilizing and humbling as standing upon the forefront of a great and beautiful, endless living blue.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt is like a drug, making you think of nothing but its movements and presence; so much darker and mysterious than any ocean. It gives you just the right amount of confidence to conquer it and the right amount of intimidation to remain in awe. There are so many memories I have shared with the rocks and kettles that are scattered along these shores; the soft sand and how it felt on my naked skin. I truly could not live without water around me. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI have a picture that a friend took of me when we were out exploring an area we had never been. Iâ€™m looking up at the sky that is drowning in sunlight and my face and hair are glowing against the strong blue of the water. When I feel like crap I stare at this picture and remember the smell and warmth of that autumn afternoon.<br><br>\r\n\r\n Lake Huron is by far the loveliest, with its unmistakable sunsets, violent and unpredictable storms and the feeling it gives that there is no one else on this planet but you. The absolutely most important luxury to me is having access to my own piece of this bliss. It presents itself as my manifested path away from everything, accessible at anytime. <br><br>\r\n\r\nGrand Bend is amazing in the winter. The waves rise up on the shore and freeze together making mountains of ice that shelter the shore from foreboding winter winds. I love sitting on the pier and watching a storm build out on the lake, waves crashing against the corrugated metal banks that support the concrete pier and the lighthouse. <br><br>\r\n\r\nToronto has a water front, but Iâ€™ve yet to experience it. Too much is going on around it to appreciate its grandeur. I prefer to be as isolated as possible, to feel surrounded by it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe St. Clair River is thought to be a polluted mess, and as much as this is true, nothing can beat watching the boats flow out ominously into the lake from under the bridges. I love the water there, it swirls and heaves with challenging currents, and the blue glow from the bridge kisses the waves, created by passing tankers, that lap up to my feet dangling from the rock shelf above. It is here that I remember faces and places and moments of my youth.<br><br>\r\n\r\nErie reminds me of stories I have read about still waters that push the horizon out to the end of the earth; seas that from shore seem so peaceful and go on forever. The excessive blue is only mimicked by the black soil rich with old memories and the broken backs of many men. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThese lakes are indeed great and I feel comforted by how they surround the land where I live; a power and beauty that I never want to be separated from. It is with these images that I dream about during the day that I am able to endure the things in my life that would surely pull me under.\r\n<br><br>'),(3605,'it looks like everything is set. i am going to be a personal injury, worker\\\'s compensation, medical malpractice, and products liability lawyer. i got a summer job with a successful firm, which has a high probability of becoming permanent barring any outlandish behavior on my part. which is not impossible, but growing increasingly unlikely. </p><p>\r\ntoday, i purchased professional attire on a large scale. my grandfather, the family court  judge, gave me the money. he\\\'s excited.</p><p>\r\n\r\nif you had met me five years ago, this possibility would not have occurred to you. nor to me. five years ago i was a college dropout, recently fired from a job waiting tables in a shit town in south carolina, working at a car wash and living on the owner\\\'s couch. i was not really ok with it, but i made the best of things. that is how i ended up at this point, just by making the best of things. if my child ever asks me, \\\'daddy, why are you a lawyer,\\\' my only honest answer would be \\\'it just kind of worked out that way.\\\' and the way it worked out, is the story of the dramatic part of my life. </p><p>\r\nin a very real way, though, i am far, far more fortunate than my peers, average age 23, who did well in high school, were reasonably popular and well adjusted through college, and simply rolled into law school on momentum. i have never taken success for granted. </p><p>\r\nbut now, i sort of can. this is having a strange effect on my psyche. i was once afraid because the future seemed so uncertain, and this fear has now <i>transmuted</i> itself into fear of certainty. it is kind of like dying. the knowledge that i will probably end up doing this for most of my life brings with it the realization that i will <i>not</i> be realizing all of the other potential life tracks which, only five years ago, were just as likely as this one. </p><p>\r\nthough i feel more secure, i also feel the loss of freedom. and so i ask myself, \\\'what would make me just feel ok with everything?\\\' </p><p>\r\napparently, buying shirts and ties isn\\\'t going to cut it.</p><p>\r\nwho said, \\\'there are no second acts in american lives?\\\''),(3606,'<P><FONT FACE=\\\"COURIER\\\">\r\nok, so\r\n<BR>i was at the buckner show drinking pabst\r\n<BR>i had three\r\n<BR>i\\\'m not drunk, but i am def. tipsy\r\n<BR>i leave the show\r\n<BR>my car\r\n<BR>i get in my car\r\n<BR>i head home towards this really steep hill that sharply levels out\r\n<BR>and i am acquainted with going down it really fast\r\n<BR>and the joy that brings when it levels out\r\n<BR>this time i am thinking: it is midnight, nobody is around,\r\n<BR>i\\\'m going to try to catch air going up it\r\n<BR>so i gun the civic\r\n<BR>as i reach the apex and take to the air, i pass a copper\r\n<BR>\\\'oh fuck\\\'\r\n<BR>i\\\'m going to get a DUI and reckless driving\r\n<BR>i take the IMMEDIATE left after the cop goes down the hill i just flew off of\r\n<BR>the cop can\\\'t see me or where i am going\r\n<BR>it is a dead end not even a block in, like a quarter block dead end\r\n<BR>\\\'oh shit\\\'\r\n<BR>i flip a bitch, the cop passes\r\n<BR>i take a left onto the road i just flew off of\r\n<BR>just as the cop is flipping his own bitch\r\n<BR>i basically did a square dance with the cop\r\n<BR>and he didn\\\'t realize it was me\r\n<BR>\\\'whew\\\'\r\n</P></FONT>'),(3607,'<PRE>\r\nok, so\r\ni was at the buckner show drinking pabst\r\ni had three\r\ni\\\'m not drunk, but i am def. tipsy\r\ni leave the show\r\nmy car\r\ni get in my car\r\ni head home towards this really steep hill that sharply levels out\r\nand i am acquainted with going down it really fast\r\nand the joy that brings when it levels out\r\nthis time i am thinking: it is midnight, nobody is around, i\\\'m going to try to catch air going up it\r\nso i gun the civic\r\nas i reach the apex and take to the air, i pass a copper\r\n\\\'oh fuck\\\'\r\ni\\\'m goiing to get a DUI and reckless driving\r\ni take the IMMEDIATE left after the cop goes doen the hill i just flew off of\r\nthe cop can\\\'t see me or where i am going\r\nit is a dead end not even a block in, like a quarter block dead end\r\n\\\'oh shit\\\'\r\ni flip a bitch, the cop passes\r\ni take a left onto the road i just flew off of\r\njust as the cop is flipping his own bitch\r\ni basically did a square dance with the cop\r\nand he didn realize it was me\r\n\\\'whew\\\'\r\n</PRE>'),(3608,'<!--\r\n<P>Acid Reflux Disease and Wells Fargo Bank and Belching Burping Bronx Cheer</P>\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<P><B>I</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Leonard walked into the Bank on payday with his pay check.  Lazy Leonard.  He didn\\\'t want to fill out the bank\\\'s deposit form.  He handed the bank teller his check, his driver\\\'s license, and his ATM card.  Leonard let his mind drift in historical thoughs of the wild west spurred by the stage coach that sat in the middle of the bank lobby.  The teller interrupted his History Channel moment by handing him the blank deposit slip.  Immediately offended, Leonard looked not to the teller\\\'s face, but to the teller\\\'s name tag.  It read MANUEL as in fill out your own damn check deposit form, biotch.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>II</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>Leonard was walking down the East/West sliver of ghetto that runs through paradise, or \\\'Pair of Dice\\\' as he likes to think of it.  The sun was shining and going down, bathing the little East/West ghetto strip in a lovely deep shade of sunlight.  The shadows were begining to get some legs.  A trolly headed back to ghetto land was pulling away from him to his right.  Ghetto people after a hard day of servitude to the Man mill about on the left side of the street, waiting in the nice light for their train home.  Everything was picturesque in a sort of Even Dirty People Are Pretty In The Eyes of God way.  Filled with this joyous inner glow while standing at a street corner looking over his shoulder to the right, the magnificence of God, Leonard belched in prayer.  Then looking to his left saw a woman, disgusted by his reverence.</P>\r\n'),(3609,'<p>It happened like this:</p>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<p>My trainee and I stopped by a Magic 8-Ball.  Feeling the mystic energies in the air (ahahaha), I decided to check on that most important of questions in my life today.  Namely, Will I get laid in the next five years?  The answer slowly broke through the dark blue fluid contained within - <b>Yes</b>.  My trainee reached across and grabbed the Magic 8-Ball from me, \\\"Will it be by a man?\\\"  The Magic 8-Ball had my back - <b>Don\\\'t Count on It</b>.  I snatched the Magic 8-Ball back from him with a glare.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"Will I get laid within the next year?\\\"  <b>No.</b></p> \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Within two years?\\\"  <b>Don\\\'t count on it.</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Within <i>three years?</i>  <b>My sources say yes.</b>  <i>whew!</i>  By then I was getting worried.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>So there it is, sometime between 2 and 3 years, I\\\'ll get laid again.  However, later I felt darker mystical energies (hahahahahaha) and dared to ask that question which I should not:  \\\"Will I have to pay for it?\\\"  <b>Yes.</b>  The word loomed cruelly behind the thick plastic circle, bobbing gently in the dark blue fluid.  I ran away terrified, giggling maniacally.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"It\\\'s hard to be so ugly my friend,\\\" I told my trainee.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>\\\"There there,\\\" he patted my shoulder, \\\"at least some hooker is going to bang you.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On the way out, feeling even darker energies (bwahahahahahahaha), I stopped and stared at the Magic 8-Ball.  It looked so happy and smug in its packaging.  I wanted to tear it out of its plastic bubble womb and smash it on the floor.  But I dared not.  Instead, I asked one final question:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Will I kill myself within three years?\\\"  The score crashed into a crescendo, then halted, becoming silent as the words were revealed.  <b>You may rely on it.</b></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I dashed headlong from the store into the street, filled with dread and a sort of giddy hunger for Otter Pops.  My trainee jogged after me, waving his cell phone and threatening to call the medics. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>So here\\\'s the contest, based on the information available to you in this story and wherever else in my journal you choose to look, write a brief (less than a billion words please) scenario on my death.  If you win, I\\\'ll see to it that the video of my final days is left to you in my will. </p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Note:</b>  This post was ripped straight from my livejournal.  I normally don\\\'t do that, nor do I take my old posts from here and put them there.  However, after writing it, I thought that it might be fun to share a different sort of experience with my friends and etc at swinney.  Uhhhh... I guess feel free to create amusing or cruel scenarios on my death.  It certainly can\\\'t hurt.  =)</p>'),(3610,'test'),(3611,'<P>Iâ€™m about to embark on an adventure Iâ€™ve managed to avoid thus far.</P>\r\n<P>\r\nIâ€™m considering renting a basement apartment.\r\n</P><P>\r\nFirst of all Iâ€™m not a huge fan of people living above me, and not just in a house, but Iâ€™ve never like it in apartment buildings either; I was always at least second from the top.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNext point, I used to work down town in an old building, and the basement housed a number of canine sized rodents. Hmm, I can enjoy and extra two legs in my bed but four is a bit much to ask. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nContinuing, I hate spiders and this basement has a very high spider potential. When youâ€™re on the twenty-second storey the only spiders you have to worry about are the ones escaped from terrariums. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nLastly, I would have to get used to the seven foot high ceilings. Iâ€™m 5â€™11\\\" and that doesnâ€™t leave all that much room for my melon, there is also an air duct that runs the length of the main room, and could deem fatal in a moment of attention breach, or intoxication. Seems like a nice place though, the people who own the house reek of hippie-gone-yuppie Vancouverites, and itâ€™s totally self-contained.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nIâ€™m sharing an apartment with two other people in a great high-rise, but will eventually have to move anyway, cause their getting married and entering the life of condos. There is a certain comfort in being above the world. When you look down you can see things going on, things happening, I can even see the lake and on clear days New York state. Perhaps this is a false reality, cause Iâ€™m not really down there contributing.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nMaybe the call of solar exposure will pull me up from below to venture out more often just for the sake of  \\\"being out\\\". \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nMaybe being a basement dweller suits me? \r\n</P>'),(3612,'after putting in my first full week at the firm, and in the past couple days getting to do some really interesting work on some cases, my outlook is starting to improve. i\\\'m starting to see that what we do for people is a really good thing. we help people get what they\\\'re entitled to when they get hurt, physically or otherwise. we don\\\'t charge anything up front, and our firm from what i can tell puts as much effort into the smaller cases as we do into the more potentially lucrative ones. and i have also learned that even the small cases are a huge deal to the clients. i mean, if you\\\'ve been an unskilled laborer for 25 years and your back goes out for good, man, you\\\'re really fucked. you need that 350 a week to pay for food and medicine. <p>\r\nand i can\\\'t even really fault the defense side of things; they are just trying to make sure that the claimant doesn\\\'t basically steal something that they\\\'re not entitled to. so being a lawyer is good! it\\\'s hard, mentally taxing work that benefits society all-around. what else could i really ask for in life? '),(3613,'<i>this is the result of a writing exercise: make up definitions forthree unfamiliar words (agapetae, sciophilous, extispicy) and incorporate the them into a story in a way that subtly conveys the mock-meaning to the reader. </i>\r\n<p>\r\nDear Bernard,\r\n<p>\r\nDespite your most dire predictions, I managed to arrive on Coronus without having my constituent atoms scattered across the parsec of space that separate us, and also avoided  emerging from the jumpdoor with someone elseâ€™s legs and genitalia in place of my own (although I do appreciate your pointing out that virutally anything along those lines would have to be an improvement. Brother, you are the soul of wit). If it makes you feel any better, rest assured that your lurid stories lodged in my head until I was compelled, as you knew Iâ€™d be, to ask the jump attendants about them. And of course they enjoyed a hearty laugh and my expense and assured me that reports of tesselation accidents were, in almost every case, greatly exaggerated. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAnd now look where I am! Coronus, Diamond-of-Light! And I, a simple missionary brother in way, way over his head. The order allowed me the rest of the afternoon to stow the few belongings I was allowed to bring (oh! For a snifter of cognac and the collected works of Mere Dupret!) and then the next morning I was introduced, without ceremony, to the first non-human sentient creature Iâ€™d ever seen outside network panoptics. Was I frightened? Well, no, but still, it was a cognitive shock of the highest order. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nOne can be <i>told</i> that the agapetae are more concept than object, but itâ€™s still deeply disorienting to enounter an intelligence that exists in your imagination as much (if not more so) than it does in three-dimensional space. It didnâ€™t help that to all outward appearances, Jean Pierre (as my client preferred to be called) was at first glance, little more than a blurring of whatever stood behind him, a point in space where oneâ€™s eyes seemed to develop a sudden and painful myopia. Thus when addressing him I find myself involuntarily closing my eyes, and that, of course, gives rise to the fantastically vivid mental imagery and disorienting emotional responses that make the agapetae so interesting to our superiors. Immersed in visions of lost loves and savior figures, itâ€™s all to easy to forget that the gentle creature before you is actually a fearsome predator that slavers (so to speak) for oneâ€™s soul, and that to surrender to its siren call is, as Doctrine and Practices assure us, to consign oneself to a most hideous and non-metaphorical Hell. And if that was not bad enough, his Jesuit guards are on limbic-enhancing drugs that  make them nearly psychotic with rage over infractions of order as minor as farting out of turn. So of course you can imagine that I, with my delicate digestive system, have already had a few hair-raising encounters. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nJean Pierre, with typical agapeta disingenousness, insisted to me (in the form of my own sainted motherâ€”<i>extra</i>-typical  guile!) that he was an emissary from his people. A missionary, much as I am! At that point I wouldâ€™ve applied red hot pokers to his eyes for his heresy, as Doctrine instructs, had I not been distracted by the pleromatic bliss of a nursing infant, and had he eyes in which to place said pokers). He showed a diabolic grasp of Doctrine as he made reference to sciophilous and claimed that he and his cohorts were on a similar mission of outreach. â€œOne child of All extending a hand another,â€ he said (speaking metaphorically, I assume, as he had no hands I could see), and I couldâ€™ve screamed and rent my robe in horror to hear such blasphemies emerge from the mouth of Cecilia Louise, a doe-eyed ingenue with whom Iâ€™d once held hands the summer before I entered the seminary. Oh, I can imagine you tut-tutting, Bernard, but not all of us came out of the womb sure of our vocation.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œThereâ€™s only One Truth,â€ I reminded him coldly. â€œSciophilus is rooted in Gospelâ€”our Gospel, unique in all time and space. Itâ€™s our duty to spread the Word to all creation.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œOne Truth, of course,â€ the wicked creature replied. â€œWhoâ€™s disputing that?â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWhat followed was a curious exegesis on both the Gospels and <i>philisophi sciophilus</i> as seen from an alien perspective. I will spare you the details, Bernard; suffice it to say that Jean Pierre was an unreconstructed Gnostic, a Platonic Idealist in extra-terrestrial drag. He insisted that all consciousness (and here he seemed to be implying that consciousness can be easily equated with the immortal soul, as if dogs and dolphins had souls as well!) has a universal source, and that source seeks to make itself known. He claimed that the agapetae had in fact once had corporeal form but had since left them behind in a sort of salivific evolution. Agapetae are, in effect, us, a thousand generations removed. The crucial difference was that his ancestors had had the wisdom to lay aside their intellectual differences and embrace actual experience. As the colonists of Coronus had already done. As he invited me to do.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nCouched as it was in the figure of the Savior himself, hanging on the cross with blood and water streaming from his side, it was a convincing argument. So convincing, in fact, that I ordered him killed. And then, for good measure, I ordered the guards that had overheard us to kill each other, which they did all too happily. All that remains is the cleansing by fire of Coronus itself, which, if the flares over the horizon are any indication, has already begun. Soon Iâ€™ll add myself to the pyre, and gladly, at that. Heresy is a virus, Bernard, to be contained at all costs. God speed you on your way. <i>Extispicus triumphent. </i>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nI remain your\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nSebastien\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nP.S. Iâ€™d sell your mother for a snifter of cognac and the collected works of Mere Dupret! Here they c\r\n'),(3614,'i had a hard time coming up with a title for this. i came up with \\\'boring backstory a real writer wouldn\\\'t bother a reader with,\\\' but it didn\\\'t fit. also, \\\'please don\\\'t feel obligated to click on this, because it is just me rambling on about myself.\\\' yeah. it also wouldn\\\'t fit in the box. \\\'attempt to provide context, and hopefully meaning, to some stuff i wrote so my imaginary friends will like me better.\\\' etc. </p><p> \r\ni had a crisis about a year ago. i was almost finished with undergrad. i was reading modernist fiction. it was assigned. i won\\\'t bore you with a long description of what modernism is supposed to be, all i will say is, WWI fucked up a lot of people in the head. and they said, \\\'is this all really worth it?\\\' and by \\\'this,\\\' they meant \\\'life.\\\' no one had ever really asked that question before, or at least no one who had gotten my attention before. because, i guess, they hadn\\\'t been assigned. and most of the writers said in their writing that they weren\\\'t sure. Hemingway said \\\'yes, life is worth it because of sensory pleasures and intellectual satisfaction,\\\' but he sort of took it all back in the end, when he killed himself in the living room. the modern comparison is Kurt Cobain, who ssid \\\'yes, life is worth it, because it feels so good to rock out.\\\' which apparently ended up not being true for him, when he killed himself in \\\'the greenhouse above the detached garage of the residence.\\\'</p><p>\r\nso i had me a little ol\\\' crisis. yee-haw. life was definitely either \\\'good\\\' or \\\'not good.\\\' the specter of somehow figuring out that it was \\\'not good\\\' transfixed me. it was as if i thought i was walking along a flat plain, when all along i had been on the edge of a cliff. seeing that gaping death-chasm deeply affected me, and i began to see its echo in various works of art. </p><p>\r\nit was a while before i began to entertain the idea that life might actually be sort of neither one. good nor bad. that it changes every weeek, or every day, or maybe by the hour.</p><p>and back to the lawyer thing. is deciding to do something that requires a huge commitment of time and energy and thought and of the limited hours of my life a good thing? or is it a bad thing? <p></p>\r\ni guess maybe there are just good days, and i should try to make as much of them as i can. and rock out. '),(3615,'<p>\r\n\r\nWith an imperious wave of my hand I ordered the orchestra to begin playing, and the sweet strains of â€œAn der Schonen, Blauen Dounaâ€ filled the little sub-cellar. Before setting to workâ€”and it was work of a most agreeable sort, chastening a terribly handsome Italian lad who had the temerity to sass meâ€”I took a moment to check my appearance. Because after all first impressions are everything, and though this impetuous (but pretty, oh, those dark eyes, those long eyelashes, and of course Iâ€™d had him stripped naked so that I could enjoy the contrast of his snowy round buttocks [scoops of vanilla ice cream!] with the deep color of his muscular laborerâ€™s back [caramel! Delicious!]) young soldier, this prisoner of war, this young buck had already formed hisâ€”of me, little Klodwig, in his frock and powdered wig, with his little belly and one leg shorter than the other. Heâ€™d sneered at me when I offered him the chance to be my personal valetâ€”<i>my</i> personal valet, which would have left him only a bare degree removed from the body of the Holy Emperor of Prussia himself! He sneered, the wretched boy!\r\n<p>\r\nBut now, lash in hand, I was to be born anew in his eyes. I would cleanse him of his hauteur, reduce him to slime, make him grovel at my feet and beg for a second chance. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œApologize, underling!â€ I cried. â€œBeg forgiveness!â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nOf course he was silent. Of course! And that was how I preferred it. Had he broken  right away, had the mere threat of pain been enough to crumple the tin shield of his pride, I wouldâ€™ve been sorely disappointed indeed.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAch, Blauen Donau! How I loved that tune. My feet moved in time across the cobblestones, with their chiaroscuro of blood and bile dried in the cracks. <i>One</i> two three <i>one</i> two three, my velvet slippers whispered across the stones. One two three one two three <i>One! </i>And my leather belt cracked through the air, and rising across the Italianâ€™s rump (have you even seen the Taj Mahal? A snow white dome, trimmed in gold, precious and perfect. Picture it in miniature. And twinned, of course)â€”a red welt. Oh, the tears that rose in my eyes along with it! How hurting him pained me! And yet! Though every muscle in his body locked in agony, though he pulled tight like a bowstring against his manacles, still he defied me. He did not cry out! \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSpeak, slave! Apologize for your insolence. Speak!â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nBut no! Only the lilting violins spoke. I stepped closer to him, close enough to smell the sweat on his ribs, to feel the heat rising from his skin like air from an opened bakerâ€™s oven. I had to be sure. No! He was not crying. Not even locking his teeth against a whimper. Just breathing, snuffling, snorting like a stallion out studding in the fields. Enraged, I danced backâ€”so graceful, if only my old ballet master could see me now, in my element!â€”for another blow.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAnâ€¦ derâ€¦ scho..nen oh! Blauen Donnnau! Ah, Strauss! I danced, with my long leather strap for a partner. And a flirtatious partner he was! Always trying to leap from my grasp, reaching to caress the adorable stranger writhing in chains. But I was not happy. No! For though my whip cracked and sang, my Italian did not deign to join in. Red stripes criss-crossed the furrows of muscle on his back like party gift ribbonsâ€”put a bow on him! Silent he remained, like an Englishwoman doing her marital duty, as the beating went on and on. But his suffering was nothing compared to my own!  My eyes stung, my stomach cramped, I was afraid Iâ€™d have to run up three flights of stairs to sit on my brass chamber pot. How selfish could he be! How heartless! Couldnâ€™t he understand that his obstinacy was breaking my poor heart!\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWith a cry I dropped my lash and ran to his side, intending to beat him mercilessly with my bare hands. But I was weak, weak with desire. My fists thumped against the slabs of muscle in his shoulders, his back, until I grew exhausted and fell against him, sobbing. The slickness of his skin! Like a medicinal salve, like a heated oil. I begged him, (and here I must abandon my mother tongue for my sweet captiveâ€™s unwashed blather, German has no word for whisper) <i>sotto voce</i>, â€œJust apologize, Italian, and then I can let you down. Please, just say youâ€™re sorry.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nThen he responded. He let loose a furious torrent of gibberishâ€”his native tongue, I suppose. And then, as if to tease me further, he proved he wasnâ€™t an utter barbarian by saying in low German (the language of those mountain dwellers in the south, who are Prussian only by virtue of some cartographerâ€™s pen, sharing as they do the African coloring of their Italian cousins), â€œStuff a sock your ass, <i>Honheit.</i> Itâ€™s been stretched to wide. I can smell it from here.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWhat a cry of terror I let out then! So loud that the orchestra actually dropped a beat of the waltz in shock, and then stumbled for a measure before resuming Straussâ€™s greatest work. For I knew then that he was too proud, that my beautiful Italian would never show proper self-abnegation in the presence of his betters, and that Iâ€™d have to put him down. But such plans Iâ€™d had for him! My hands fell to his hips, and I curled myself against his back, pressing my nose hard against his spine. â€œOh, Italian, Italian!â€ I whimpered. And as if on their own, my hands wandered forward, exploring the rippling plain of his belly, and then traveled south, to the grassy lowlands there. â€œYou donâ€™t even have to say it all,â€ I continued in a whisper. â€œJust show the barest politeness.â€ Straightening up, bringing one hand up to stroke his smooth face (but leaving the other where it was, which had the fortunate effect of bringing my little steam ship to dock in the inlet between his antipodean cheeks), I stood on my tiptoes and bellowed for the other prisoners to hear, â€œIn my mercy, I require but one word, Italian! I will make you an offer. I will say, â€˜Italian, would you like me to grant you clemency?â€™ and you have but to say â€˜Please,â€™ and all will be forgiven. â€˜Please!â€™ Not â€˜Please, <i>honheit!â€™</i> or â€˜Please, my celestial master!â€™ Just please! That is the extent of my mercy.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAnd all the while my hidden hand had been busy. It roamed through the wild tangle, that jungle run riot between his legs, stalking prey I was sure would be prodigious, but even I, who have been on many such hunts, and have collected many, many trophies, was unprepared for the leviathan that lurked unseen. I had been merely in search of the snake of the Garden, who could lurk unseen in the branches of a tree. Instead I found in my grasp Jormangund, the Midgard serpent, in whose coils the whole world rests! I had called him my stallion in jest, and how shocked was I that my playful nickname barely did his endowment justice! After Iâ€™d made its acquaintance, Catherine the Great herself would be calling me to court to report my experience. But was I intimidated? No! While I bellowed my challenge, I skillfully took matters in hand. He flinched awayâ€”coy Italian!â€”but then, finally, lowed long and low, like a bull steer. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nDear reader, gentle readerâ€”it didnâ€™t take long. This was a young man, after all, barely more than a boy (actually, in some respects, a bit more than three boys!), who had been confined away from the comfort of women for more than four months. We dance in time to Strauss, my delicate little feet shuffling in steps, and still keeping the downbeat, but with lashes of a decidedly gentler nature. With my other hand I because to beat time, and the orchestraâ€”smart fellows!â€”caught my cue. As my hand slowedâ€”from a sprightly waltz tempo, to the walk of a man with a cane, to the swing of a clock pendulumâ€”the tune slowed in tandem, as did my hardworking other hand. The Italianâ€™s breathing grew tortured, shuddering. One might even say he wasâ€”sweet vindication!â€”whimpering.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œNow, beautiful,â€ I said, as my strokes grew even slower, though my grip tightened until I mightâ€™ve been holding onto a cavalrymanâ€™s saddle pommel for dear life. â€œSay the word, and I shall release you.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nNo answer, still. Mulish boy! One, two, three. One. Two. Three.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSay it!â€ I hissed.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nOne. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nTwo.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nThree.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nA thin whine slid between his fat wet lips. My heart! It leapt like a milkmaid dodging the cowpox. And yet, with all the iron discipline that fueled my meteoric ascension my exalted post, I was able to hold back, and counted, to the tolling of a distant bell,  \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nOneâ€¦\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nTwo â€¦\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nA strangled cry escaped the cage of his clenched teeth, a lovely coloratura wail that wouldâ€™ve done a Vatican choirboy proud. â€œPLEASE! <i>PLEASE</i> HONHEIT!â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nThree. Ah, success. \r\n<p>\r\n'),(3616,'<p>\r\n\r\nWith an imperious wave of my hand I ordered the orchestra to begin playing, and the sweet strains of â€œAn der Schonen, Blauen Dounaâ€ filled the little sub-cellar. Before setting to workâ€”and it was work of a most agreeable sort, chastening a terribly handsome Italian lad who had the temerity to sass meâ€”I took a moment to check my appearance. Because after all first impressions are e