>\r\n\r\nThen he responded. He let loose a furious torrent of gibberishâ€”his native tongue, I suppose. And then, as if to tease me further, he proved he wasnâ€™t an utter barbarian by saying in low German (the language of those mountain dwellers in the south, who are Prussian only by virtue of some cartographerâ€™s pen, sharing as they do the African coloring of their Italian cousins), â€œStuff a sock your ass, <i>Honheit.</i> Itâ€™s been stretched to wide. I can smell it from here.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nWhat a cry of terror I let out then! So loud that the orchestra actually dropped a beat of the waltz in shock, and then stumbled for a measure before resuming Straussâ€™s greatest work. For I knew then that he was too proud, that my beautiful Italian would never show proper self-abnegation in the presence of his betters, and that Iâ€™d have to put him down. But such plans Iâ€™d had for him! My hands fell to his hips, and I curled myself against his back, pressing my nose hard against his spine. â€œOh, Italian, Italian!â€ I whimpered. And as if on their own, my hands wandered forward, exploring the rippling plain of his belly, and then traveled south, to the grassy lowlands there. â€œYou donâ€™t even have to say it all,â€ I continued in a whisper. â€œJust show the barest politeness.â€ Straightening up, bringing one hand up to stroke his smooth face (but leaving the other where it was, which had the fortunate effect of bringing my little steam ship to dock in the inlet between his antipodean cheeks), I stood on my tiptoes and bellowed for the other prisoners to hear, â€œIn my mercy, I require but one word, Italian! I will make you an offer. I will say, â€˜Italian, would you like me to grant you clemency?â€™ and you have but to say â€˜Please,â€™ and all will be forgiven. â€˜Please!â€™ Not â€˜Please, <i>honheit!â€™</i> or â€˜Please, my celestial master!â€™ Just please! That is the extent of my mercy.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAnd all the while my hidden hand had been busy. It roamed through the wild tangle, that jungle run riot between his legs, stalking prey I was sure would be prodigious, but even I, who have been on many such hunts, and have collected many, many trophies, was unprepared for the leviathan that lurked unseen. I had been merely in search of the snake of the Garden, who could lurk unseen in the branches of a tree. Instead I found in my grasp Jormangund, the Midgard serpent, in whose coils the whole world rests! I had called him my stallion in jest, and how shocked was I that my playful nickname barely did his endowment justice! After Iâ€™d made its acquaintance, Catherine the Great herself would be calling me to court to report my experience. But was I intimidated? No! While I bellowed my challenge, I skillfully took matters in hand. He flinched awayâ€”coy Italian!â€”but then, finally, lowed long and low, like a bull steer. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nDear reader, gentle readerâ€”it didnâ€™t take long. This was a young man, after all, barely more than a boy (actually, in some respects, a bit more than three boys!), who had been confined away from the comfort of women for more than four months. We dance in time to Strauss, my delicate little feet shuffling in steps, and still keeping the downbeat, but with lashes of a decidedly gentler nature. With my other hand I because to beat time, and the orchestraâ€”smart fellows!â€”caught my cue. As my hand slowedâ€”from a sprightly waltz tempo, to the walk of a man with a cane, to the swing of a clock pendulumâ€”the tune slowed in tandem, as did my hardworking other hand. The Italianâ€™s breathing grew tortured, shuddering. One might even say he wasâ€”sweet vindication!â€”whimpering.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œNow, beautiful,â€ I said, as my strokes grew even slower, though my grip tightened until I mightâ€™ve been holding onto a cavalrymanâ€™s saddle pommel for dear life. â€œSay the word, and I shall release you.â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nNo answer, still. Mulish boy! One, two, three. One. Two. Three.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nâ€œSay it!â€ I hissed.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nOne. \r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nTwo.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nThree.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n\r\nA thin whine slid between his fat wet lips. My heart! It leapt like a milkmaid dodging the cowpox. And yet, with all the iron discipline that fueled my meteoric ascension my exalted post, I was able to hold back, and counted, to the tolling of a distant bell,  \r\n<p>\r\n\r\nOneâ€¦\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nTwo â€¦\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nA strangled cry escaped the cage of his clenched teeth, a lovely coloratura wail that wouldâ€™ve done a Vatican choirboy proud. â€œPLEASE! <i>PLEASE</i> HONHEIT!â€\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nThree. Ah, success. \r\n<p>\r\n'),(3617,'<P>Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer</P>\r\n<P>Worker bee worker bee<BR>\r\nWorker because they pay me<BR>\r\nAsbestos Cancer of the <TT><3</TT><BR>\r\nIs what they paid me<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nUnder flourescent lights<BR>\r\nSorting envelopes like bolts<BR>\r\nBusy bee busy bee<BR>\r\nBusy because they pay me<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nDying in the street<BR>\r\nWith dirty feet<BR>\r\nHumming this song<BR>\r\nWhile I worked all day long<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nBusy bee busy bee<BR>\r\nCancer is what they pay me<BR>\r\nBuzzing all day long<BR>\r\nUnder flourescent bzzzzzz<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3617_1.jpg\\\"><img width=300 src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3617_1.jpg\\\">'),(3618,'<P>Ever since I started suing my employer for giving me Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer, I have felt better about dying.  I\\\'m getting mine, then I am going to die.  I\\\'ll be happy knowing, in my grave, that that money didn\\\'t sit in some account somewhere getting ready to pay for 1% of some corporate swine\\\'s house!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>  People say money can\\\'t help you when you are dead, but that is bull.  I set up a trust fund to put flowers on my grave, fresh ones, exotic ones, every day for eternity or until the collapse of civilization and everyone dies of Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer and they sue each other first, class action lawsuit style.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I remember in the court room, this expert tried to tell me that I wasn\\\'t dying because of their Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer agents.  I drifted off thinking about the agents of Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer in their dark glasses.  Every day on the way to work I would hand them my badge.  They would smile in that scaley sort of leaden toothy smile with flakes of asbestos chipping off and falling into their coffee.  I should have known that this Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer was coming when I started there. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>The money was good, and I didn\\\'t think Mesothelioma Asbestos Cancer would be so bad while I drove my Porsche to work or up the coast on the weekends.  All the blue sky and clean air with the T-Tops down can\\\'t help me now that my heart has toxic cob webs running through it.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>So I got myself a lawyer and I got them back!</P>'),(3619,'<p>\r\nTHE OLD WOODCUTTER:<br>\r\nHark! Young ears, bend to my words.<br>\r\nA sad story, a tale of warning I have for you<br>\r\nA present, if you will, a gift of wisdom passed<br>\r\nFrom an old man who carries an axe no longer, but only<br>\r\nLove and concern for you, my children.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nListen as I tell the tale of little<br>\r\nRed Riding Hood, a girl from a village<br>\r\nNot far from here, no, separated from our own<br>\r\nOnly by the Black Woods to the west. A journey<br>\r\nShe was called upon to make, not far to go, bearing <br>\r\nA sweet cake for her sweet ailing grandma.<br>\r\nNot so different from those chores you perform <br>\r\nEvery day. So attend me closely, little dreamers,<br> \r\nListen to the dangers she faced. Learn from her mistakes, <br>\r\nTo save yourself and your kin from broken hearts and<br>\r\nBlack veils of mourning.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nCHORUS: <br>\r\nOur village had long been ravaged by<br>\r\nThe Big Bad Wolf, who with his wily ways <br>\r\nAlways managed to escape the pursuit of hunters.<br>\r\nCunning was the Wolf, but lazy, with a taste for <br>\r\nThe plump flesh of our well-loved children.<br>\r\nWe, the Hausfraus of Gottinsdier, at night could keep them <br>\r\nSafe, by locking our doors and bolting the windows,  <br>\r\nBut what of the sunlit hours, when we were busy with work, and <br>\r\nCurly-haired young kids must be free to roam and play? <br>\r\n<p>\r\nMOTHER: <br>\r\nMy dear, word has come that <br>\r\nYour beloved grandmother has been very ill.<br>\r\nCome, put on the lovely red cloak she made you,<br>\r\nAnd take this honey cake, and this little pot of butter, <br>\r\nOver the river and through the Black Woods to <br>\r\nHer house at the edge of the forest. I know that<br>\r\nNo matter what ails her, when she sees your cherubâ€™s <br>\r\nFace in her window her heart will grow glad and strong again.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED: <br>\r\nOh, poor grandmother! Itâ€™s a terrible thing<br>\r\nTo be ill and alone; if only weâ€™d learned sooner of her plight! <br>\r\nI am small, but I am quick, Mama, <br>\r\nIâ€™ll be by her side and feeding her cake within the hour.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nMOTHER: <br>\r\nLittle one, your heart is in the right place, but remember <br>\r\nHow easily distracted you are by things of beauty, as they are like mirrors<br>\r\nFor the delightful counternance God has granted you.<br>\r\nFields of flowers, pretty songbirds, brooks where water gathers<br>\r\nAmong stones to laugh, these you should resist. <br>\r\nAvoid them, or else youâ€™ll lose the better part of the day, and <br>\r\nPoor grandma will spend even more hours alone. <br>\r\nAvoid as well strangers on the road, who may charm you with<br>\r\nHoneyed words, but who will steal your time as certainly as perfumed flowers.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED: <br>\r\nHave no fear, Mama! Once my little<br>\r\nFeet are on the path to grandmaâ€™s house, nothing shall distract me.<br>\r\n<p>\r\nCHORUS: <br>\r\nLittle girls are easily swayed by sweet perfumes<br>\r\nAnd mannered ways. <br>\r\nWoe to the girlchild who lacks the wisdom<br>\r\nTo distinguish a gentle sheep from the<br>\r\nDisguised, slavering wolf<br>\r\nIn a dead sheepâ€™s skin.\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nBy Godâ€™s eyes, look at the little morsel<br>\r\nTrundling up the path to me on two plump little legs!<br>\r\nAnd in her handsâ€”cake and butter. Zounds, I say,<br>\r\nA feast on the hoof<br>\r\nFor the Big Bad Wolf! But it wouldnâ€™t do to take<br>\r\nHer in violence, though I long for her flesh;<br>\r\nNot with husky woodcutters<br>\r\nAnd their sharp axes<br>\r\nDoing their brutal work so close by, and all<br>\r\nToo easily would they transfer their violence from the <br>\r\nUnprotesting oaks to my very reluctant throat!<br>\r\nBut wait! Such dismal musings have given me an idea. <br>\r\nLet me slink, belly low to the ground, to their camp. Let me<br>\r\nBorrow a shirt, and trousers, and a hat, and an axe. <br>\r\nLet me slick back the matted fur from my brow, \r\nand pick the gristle from my teeth. Now, indistiguishable<br>\r\nFrom a kindly woodsman, she will not fear me.Charm, I think, <br>\r\nIs the lure I need to draw this dumpling from the path <br>\r\nTo a place where our business can be transacted with discretion.\r\n<p>\r\nExcuse me, miss! Where are you off to, at such a tear?\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nKind woodsman, Iâ€™m to visit my grandmother, she is ill and needs company.\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nWhat a fine girl you are! You look oâ€™er warm in that cloak, however,<br>\r\nAnd in need of water. Is it still far to go?\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nNot far to go now, it is beyond that mill you see there, <br>\r\nAt the first house in the village.\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nGod bless you, child, and your good heart! But<br>\r\nSurely that is not all youâ€™re bringing her?\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nSir?\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nA cake and a pot of butter seem a meager offering for<br>\r\nA poor old woman convalescing alone. What will she do when <br>\r\nYouâ€™ve gone? Who will keep her company? Pâ€™raps youâ€™d <br>\r\nBest prepare bouquets of flowers to brighten her home<br>\r\nWhen youâ€™ve withdrawn the shining light of your lovely face. <br>\r\nA poor substitute, itâ€™s true, but better than nothing.\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED: <br>\r\nOf course you are right! The sweet smell of<br>\r\nFlowers will do much to blot out the stale reek of her <br>\r\nOld womanâ€™s fevers. Thank you, sir, I will gather great bunches<br>\r\nOf flowers that will brighten every dark corner of her cottage,<br>\r\nAnd leave no place for the shade of untimely death to bide his time.\r\n<p>\r\nCHORUS:<br>\r\nFoolish child, do you think we speak only<br>\r\nBecause we love the sound of our own voices?<br>\r\nIf only youâ€™d listened to your poor mother!<br>\r\nNow your goose is cooked.\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nQuick as a flash did I cover the distance<br>\r\nBetween that serendipitous conversation and<br>\r\nThe wretched shack where the old crone lives. <br>\r\nBefore I passed the mill I looked over my burly shoulder <br>\r\nAnd the red hood was still stooped over the daisies<br>\r\nGrowing wild in the field. A little extra time was all I needed!\r\n<p>\r\nA knock on the window will summon the old wretch.\r\n<p>\r\nGRANDMOTHER:<br>\r\nEh? What is it? Whoâ€™s there!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nWitness clever me, piping like a child: â€˜It is I, Grandmother,<br>\r\nLittle Red Riding Hood, come to bring you consolation and <br>\r\nSweet honey cakes as your brittle bones prepare for their final rest!â€™\r\n<p>\r\nGRANDMOTHER:<br>\r\nEh? Eh? I canâ€™t hear you. Pull the bobbin, and the latch will go up.\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nSo I do, and it did, and before another minute was past Iâ€™d<br>\r\nCrunched the dried-up carcass between by powerful jaws and <br>\r\nSwallowed Redâ€™s Grandma whole.\r\n<p>\r\nGRANDMOTHER:<br>\r\nEh? Help! Itâ€™s dark in here!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nWitness, now, the evidence of my exceeding genius! Into the<br>\r\nOld womanâ€™s nightgown, into her kerchief, into her bed did<br>\r\nI spring. And I pulled the bedclothes up just under my eyes, <br>\r\nSo only the red silk tied oâ€™er my pointed ears did show. Just in time!\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED (knocking):<br>\r\nGrandmother! Answer your door! Your granddaughter has come to keep you\r\ncompany!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nPull the bobbin, child. The latch will go up, and you may enter.\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED: (entering)<br>\r\nOh, my aching heart. Your voice, gone hoarse and low, <br>\r\nShows the ravages of your disease, Grandma!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nBlack wings of death surround me, my dear! And I <br>\r\nHave grown so cold. Come, warm me with your youth and vitality.<br>\r\nTake off your clothes and <br>\r\nWarm my bones with your young skin.\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nPoor, poor grandma! The least I can do is ease<br>\r\nYour final sufferings. But goodness, does the <br>\r\nApproach of death always bring such <br>\r\nEngorgement of form? What big arms youâ€™ve developed!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nAll the better to hug you close, pet.\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nAnd what big legs youâ€™ve grown!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nAll the better to for you to clasp tight between your own, precious girl.\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nAnd with the ravages of age, what long teeth fill your kissing mouth!\r\n<p>\r\nBIG BAD WOLF:<br>\r\nAll the better to eat you up, morsel!\r\n<p>\r\nLITTLE RED:<br>\r\nHelp!\r\n<p>\r\nCHORUS:<br>\r\nSilly girl, who spoke to a courteous stranger, and heeded his kind-seeming<br>\r\nWords, see the fate youâ€™ve earned! For the world is full of wolves, <br>\r\nSome hairy and gross, some smooth and fair. But no matter what their <br>\r\nAppearance, theyâ€™ve all one goal: TO EAT YOU UP!\r\n<p>\r\n'),(3620,'<p>Yesterday I felt adult <br>\r\nbecause I flirted in front of<br>\r\nmy mother.  He was a friend <br>\r\nof a friend, and perhaps he <br>\r\ndid not realize that when our<br>\r\nforearms touched I was telling<br>\r\nhim that he could have me,<br>\r\nat least for a week or two.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The wind was blowing over<br>\r\nthe Sonoma mountains<br>\r\nand we were just done<br>\r\nwith a day of fine wines.<br>\r\nHe preferred white,<br>\r\nand knew his limits.  I prefer<br>\r\nred, and have never known <br>\r\nmine.  At the picnic table<br>\r\nunder the eucalyptus and<br>\r\nsycamores, as we spread<br>\r\nsoft cheese on a baguette<br>\r\nhe smiled and with my<br>\r\nsmile back I tried to say<br>\r\n\\\"I\\\'ve been looking for <br>\r\na while now, firm in the idea<br>\r\nthat I would not take<br>\r\nthe leap willy-nilly this<br>\r\ntime, but today you seem<br>\r\njust bright and wry<br>\r\nenough, your jaw remind-<br>\r\ning me of a boy I loved<br>\r\nin college who never<br>\r\nloved me backâ€”if that<br>\r\nkind of everyday itch<br>\r\ncan ever be called love.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Mom made a friend,<br>\r\na young woman at the<br>\r\nchampagne place who<br>\r\nsaid she should try <br>\r\npersonal ads on the internet<br>\r\nbecause they\\\'d worked for her.<br>\r\nBack in the car,<br>\r\nshe was smiling and<br>\r\nprattling on like she <br>\r\ndoes when she\\\'s a little<br>\r\ndrunk, about the election<br>\r\nabout the weather, and as<br>\r\nI glanced in the rearview<br>\r\nat the young man along <br>\r\nfor the ride, gliding<br>\r\ndown the 101<br>\r\ntoward home with the<br>\r\nwindows open,<br>\r\nI thought of the thousand <br>\r\nhours spent like this, <br>\r\nmy father at the wheel,<br>\r\nme in the backseat,<br>\r\nand both of us listening<br>\r\nhappily<br>\r\nwhile she talked.</p>\r\n'),(3621,'This is a question for moebius...\r\nHave there been any recent sightings of Narcisse Dille?  I too knew him, quite well. It was around 1994. I have recently begun to worry something may have happened to him. '),(3622,'<p>A nice girl and an handsome boy each have a relationship, but as they meet and get to know each other they find attraction. They start to play with small things, glaces turn to gazes, and he writes her small notes on paper that are not poetry but funny short things on a paper you\\\'d love to get from an handsome boy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At a party a long-hug turns into kissing. Two weeks later a gaze turns into long-kiss and some short sweet tenderness without the act. Three weeks after that they walk home together after a night out, and what have happened before repeats.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Last step will probably follow next time, but might not due to extrogenous forces. You do not know.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now; at which step should they leave their old time loved ones in favour of their affair in order to upheld some credibility?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At which step would you as ones partner be able to forgive?</p>\r\n'),(3623,'<P>Good artists of Swinney.  You will notice the google ads on this website and think, what good are they?  Well here is your answer:</P>\r\n\r\n<CENTER><P><B>The First Swinney.org Foundation Promotional Art Contest</B></P></CENTER>\r\n\r\n<P>YES!  Swinney.org is doing a *star* search.  This is an advertising art contest.  We need some new blood to suck around here.  There is a <B>$200</B> purse.  You probably won\\\'t make this kind of cash begging or stealing purses! Use it to buy Popov, feed the new baby, donate to the local crisis center that you go to.  Don\\\'t feel bad for stealing from Swinney.org, it is Google\\\'s<SUP>*</SUP> money!  They greciously donated it!<SUP>**</SUP> (<i>or was it the people who clicked on them ads?</I>) This is the first time I have EVER seen a low rent internet shack give away money like this.  Just think, you don\\\'t even have any real competition!  \r\n\r\n<P><B>Here are the rules:</B></P>\r\n\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>Art must fit on one of..\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>a matchbook with three sides: front, back, and inside.\r\n<LI>a matchbox / any size\r\n<LI>a one/two sided paper coaster\r\n<LI>a one/two sided post card\r\n<LI>a one/two sided book mark\r\n<LI>disposable paper coffee cup sleave\r\n<LI>??? suggest something!\r\n</UL>\r\n<LI>must clearly contain the URL swinney.org somewhere on it\r\n</UL>\r\n<P>Here are some example advertising items.  Not to limit your imagination!  But in case you don\\\'t read english and have dollar signs in your eyes:</P>\r\n<CENTER><P><a href=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"><img width=50 src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"></A> ...or... <a href=\\\"%%img2%%\\\"><img width=50 src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\"></A> ...or... <a href=\\\"%%img3%%\\\"><img width=50 src=\\\"%%img3%%\\\"></A> ...or... <a href=\\\"%%img4%%\\\"><img width=50 src=\\\"%%img4%%\\\"></a></P></CENTER>\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>Have the arts submitted by <B>July 15, 2004</B>.  That is plenty of time to brainstorm even if you are busy!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Post your questions below.</P>\r\n<P><FONT SIZE=1><SUP>*</SUP> Google is NOT in any way affiliated with this contest.<BR>\r\n<SUP>**</SUP> Google didn\\\'t donate anything.  The money is coming from the Google ad click-thru revanue, though.</P>'),(3624,'<p>A twist of fate, and I came undone.\r\n</p><p>\r\nA child on a swingset, who doesn\\\'t like to pump back and forth monotonously, but instead twists herself around like a snake, slowly rising from the ground as her sandals drag in the sand to keep her from going backwards.  up and up.  tighter and tighter.  Usually, she looses her grip to the earth, and the swing releases its angular momentum, and she\\\'s jerked in rotation back to an untwisted sitting position. \r\n</p><p>\r\nBut not today. \r\n</p><p>\r\nThis time she kept twisting, and twisting, and the ropes became taught: 200 pounds of tension on line the diameter of your thumb.  Until, *SNAP!* it burst, and she tumbled from the memory of the playground, and cracked her head on truth. \r\n</p><p>\r\nShe always enjoyed twisting on swingsets, rather than pumping back and forth, like all the others. </p><p>\r\n\r\nThere\\\'s really no use in asking, \\\"Why.\\\"</p>'),(3625,'<p>                                                                             \r\nWhile I was in Thailand, one of the recommended tourist events was              \r\nthe full moon party on Koh Phangan, essentially, a monthly rave on a            \r\nbeach.  In some sense, this recommendation is a lie.  While every               \r\ntourist knows about it, no one that you talk to ever seems to have              \r\nactually gone to one.  However, since I was in Koh Phangan at the               \r\nright time, and with a very flexible schedule, I thought I should go.           \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nPer recommendations, I arrived a couple days early in Haad Rin and              \r\nfound a room.  Places were already filling up, but I still found a              \r\ndecent, if pricey (by Thai standards, anyway) room.  I spent the next           \r\ncouple of days in Haad Rin, twiddling my thumbs.                                \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nHaad Rin has two main beaches, but the smaller one is pretty much just          \r\na landing point for boats, and the larger beach, where the party is             \r\nheld, is dirty.  The garbage washing up on it discouraged me from ever          \r\nwanting to swim there.  And because of my melanin-deficient flesh,              \r\nsunning myself was not an option for me.  However, I stayed reasonably          \r\nentertained simply by drinking beer on a busy corner and watching the           \r\nyoung flesh parade past.                                                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThis parade was near constant.  Koh Phangan is a little too remote and          \r\nrough for most suitcase tourists, who probably wouldn\\\'t be too                  \r\ninterested in a rave, anyway.  So the artificially swelling population          \r\nis almost entirely composed of young backpackers.  The sexes are                \r\npretty evenly divided.  Whatever your orientation might be, there\\\'s             \r\nplenty of fit young flesh to be seen.                                           \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI did in fact flirt with a couple of lovely young women during this             \r\ntime, but only received this place\\\'s standard kiss off line: &quot;maybe        \r\nI\\\'ll see you later at the party.&quot;  It\\\'s well understood that               \r\neverything is very temporary there.  With the exception of the monthly          \r\nfull moon party, no one really stays in Haad Rin.  While it is a nexus          \r\nfor travel on Koh Phangan, it\\\'s much more of a way point than a                 \r\ndestination.                                                                    \r\n</p> \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nEventually the party evening rolled around.  I decided to stay mostly           \r\nsober, although I did have a beer before, and a glass of wine with,             \r\ndinner.  Some of this decision was paranoia...I was traveling on my             \r\nown, and don\\\'t have anyone to look out for me.  However, the larger             \r\npart of it was that for some reason I just didn\\\'t feel like drinking,           \r\nthat evening.                                                                   \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nHaad Rin\\\'s population expanded dramatically as evening arrived.  The            \r\nstreets filled up.  The moon was full.  Just before 11 PM, I began to           \r\nmake my way to the beach.  It was slow going as the streets were so             \r\ncrowded, but eventually I arrived at my destination.  The beach was             \r\npacked, but thinned out a bit towards the ocean.  It became clear that          \r\nmoving along the ocean was the fastest way to travel between the                \r\ncrowds clumped up against the beach resorts.                                    \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nEach of these rather densely built establishments was blaring some              \r\nform of popular music: britpop, house, r\\\'n\\\'b, trance, and drum\\\'n\\\'bass,          \r\nto name a few.  Because of personal preferences, I ended up                     \r\nspending most of my time in front of the drum\\\'n\\\'bass resort, &quot;The          \r\nOrchid&quot;.  The jungle and dnb they were playing wasn\\\'t particularly         \r\ngood, but it was still the best offering on the beach.                          \r\n</p>                             \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAlthough sober, I behaved much the same as the intoxicated crowd                \r\naround me.  I wandered aimlessly, I flirted, I danced, I sat in the             \r\nsand and talked.  I pissed in the ocean, to avoid the lines and the 10          \r\nbaht toilet fee (about 25 cents, the cost of a bottle of water).  I             \r\nwas willing to suffer one or the other but not both of these                    \r\nindignities.                                                                    \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThe partying crowd were mostly tourists.  Although, about ten percent           \r\nwere Thai.  Most of the partying Thais were non-locals though.  The             \r\nlocal population was far more entrepreneurial, selling booze, mixers,           \r\nwater, and all manner of party trinkets.                                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAs the evening progressed, men took advantage of intoxicated women,             \r\nand ladyboys took advantage of intoxicated men.  All in all, a good             \r\nparty.  Although, observing such situations soberly caused me bit of            \r\nexistential despair.  However, at some point it dawned on me, almost            \r\nliterally in fact, as the sun was just starting to come up, that this           \r\nwas exactly what they had come for.                                             \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI\\\'m not sure I got what I came for.  That\\\'s a common hazard when one            \r\nisn\\\'t really sure about what that is.  However, watching the rather             \r\nunspectactular sunrise over the Pacific, the place I was in, both               \r\nphysically and mentally, was someplace new, and that in itself made             \r\nthe experience worthwhile.                                                      \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n                                   \r\n'),(3626,'<p>                                                                             \r\nWhile I was in Thailand, one of the recommended tourist events was              \r\nthe full moon party on Koh Phangan, essentially, a monthly rave on a            \r\nbeach.  In some sense, this recommendation is a lie.  While every               \r\ntourist knows about it, no one that you talk to ever seems to have              \r\nactually gone to one.  However, since I was in Koh Phangan at the               \r\nright time, and with a very flexible schedule, I thought I should go.           \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nPer recommendations, I arrived a couple days early in Haad Rin and              \r\nfound a room.  Places were already filling up, but I still found a              \r\ndecent, if pricey (by Thai standards, anyway) room.  I spent the next           \r\ncouple of days in Haad Rin, twiddling my thumbs.                                \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nHaad Rin has two main beaches, but the smaller one is pretty much just          \r\na landing point for boats, and the larger beach, where the party is             \r\nheld, is dirty.  The garbage washing up on it discouraged me from ever          \r\nwanting to swim there.  And because of my melanin-deficient flesh,              \r\nsunning myself was not an option for me.  However, I stayed reasonably          \r\nentertained simply by drinking beer on a busy corner and watching the           \r\nyoung flesh parade past.                                                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThis parade was near constant.  Koh Phangan is a little too remote and          \r\nrough for most suitcase tourists, who probably wouldn\\\'t be too                  \r\ninterested in a rave, anyway.  So the artificially swelling population          \r\nis almost entirely composed of young backpackers.  The sexes are                \r\npretty evenly divided.  Whatever your orientation might be, there\\\'s             \r\nplenty of fit young flesh to be seen.                                           \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI did in fact flirt with a couple of lovely young women during this             \r\ntime, but only received this place\\\'s standard kiss off line: &quot;maybe        \r\nI\\\'ll see you later at the party.&quot;  It\\\'s well understood that               \r\neverything is very temporary there.  With the exception of the monthly          \r\nfull moon party, no one really stays in Haad Rin.  While it is a nexus          \r\nfor travel on Koh Phangan, it\\\'s much more of a way point than a                 \r\ndestination.                                                                    \r\n</p> \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nEventually the party evening rolled around.  I decided to stay mostly           \r\nsober, although I did have a beer before, and a glass of wine with,             \r\ndinner.  Some of this decision was paranoia...I was traveling on my             \r\nown, and don\\\'t have anyone to look out for me.  However, the larger             \r\npart of it was that for some reason I just didn\\\'t feel like drinking,           \r\nthat evening.                                                                   \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nHaad Rin\\\'s population expanded dramatically as evening arrived.  The            \r\nstreets filled up.  The moon was full.  Just before 11 PM, I began to           \r\nmake my way to the beach.  It was slow going as the streets were so             \r\ncrowded, but eventually I arrived at my destination.  The beach was             \r\npacked, but thinned out a bit towards the ocean.  It became clear that          \r\nmoving along the ocean was the fastest way to travel between the                \r\ncrowds clumped up against the beach resorts.                                    \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nEach of these rather densely built establishments was blaring some              \r\nform of popular music: britpop, house, r\\\'n\\\'b, trance, and drum\\\'n\\\'bass,          \r\nto name a few.  Because of personal preferences, I ended up                     \r\nspending most of my time in front of the drum\\\'n\\\'bass resort, &quot;The          \r\nOrchid&quot;.  The jungle and dnb they were playing wasn\\\'t particularly         \r\ngood, but it was still the best offering on the beach.                          \r\n</p>                             \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAlthough sober, I behaved much the same as the intoxicated crowd                \r\naround me.  I wandered aimlessly, I flirted, I danced, I sat in the             \r\nsand and talked.  I pissed in the ocean, to avoid the lines and the 10          \r\nbaht toilet fee (about 25 cents, the cost of a bottle of water).  I             \r\nwas willing to suffer one or the other but not both of these                    \r\nindignities.                                                                    \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThe partying crowd were mostly tourists.  Although, about ten percent           \r\nwere Thai.  Most of the partying Thais were non-locals though.  The             \r\nlocal population was far more entrepreneurial, selling booze, mixers,           \r\nwater, and all manner of party trinkets.                                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAs the evening progressed, men took advantage of intoxicated women,             \r\nand ladyboys took advantage of intoxicated men.  All in all, a good             \r\nparty.  Although, observing such situations soberly caused me bit of            \r\nexistential despair.  However, at some point it dawned on me, almost            \r\nliterally in fact, as the sun was just starting to come up, that this           \r\nwas exactly what they had come for.                                             \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI\\\'m not sure I got what I came for.  That\\\'s a common hazard when one            \r\nisn\\\'t really sure about what that is.  However, watching the rather             \r\nunspectactular sunrise over the Pacific, the place I was in, both               \r\nphysically and mentally, was someplace new, and that in itself made             \r\nthe experience worthwhile.                                                      \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n                                   \r\n'),(3627,'<p>\r\nMy mind was a lazy mess of conflicted thoughts about things I often try to make optimistic. I related to fewer and fewer peope during those days. The knowledge of a serious watershed in lifestyle was emurging on the horizon. I was worried of the things I was not sure belonged to worrying and outside of all that I was living a completely normal, often boring life abroad.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI woke up today singing a song. The lyrics were something like \\\" ohhhh morning cigarette where are youuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu, I feel like a puff puff puff puff ohhhhhhhhhhhhh youuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu \\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nChanged underpants and went to the post office to send junk home. Oh what a few weeks. At that point you know you are going home, the city you live in becomes alive and exciting again. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nThe walk home from lunch with SangRae. The buses wheel exploding only feet away from you just a ten yards from where you turn to climb stairs. Knocked me to the ground it did. Knocked the old lady to the ground it id. The tire pieces shattered a window, and injected themselves into the old ladies arm. Her blood mixed with the black powder which covered both of us. I was fine. THe sound of it was absurd. Unknown to me until right then was a sound louder than an ak-47 in cambodia. But there it was. Should I have thought about Basra or Najaf? No lets not go there. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou can walk down the street on a nice sunny day and just relish in the beauty that walks by you. There is so much of that here. I feel so safe and secure in Seoul. It really is a good place, and well BOOOOOOOOOYAKA, it has been fun.\r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nIt was my last day at work a few days ago. A few of the kids were sad; they handed me small presents and little notes they had wrote wishing me a safe trip, tips to improve my \\\'thunder hair\\\' and thanks. It was nice. I can\\\'t say I wont miss those little rapskallions, but its time to move on. It\\\'s time to at least make an attempt at moving into something that I think will be a lot more rewarding and thought provoking. \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\nI have so much to say, but even more to do. I land in Vancouver on June 2nd; Toronto on June 6th. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBUSH IN 04. WAKE UP MORE AMERICA!\r\n</p>'),(3628,'the ritual begins in late afternoon, always after the sun has passed highest in the sky for the day.  i live in and beneath and above concrete but i follow the sun.  <p>the sun wakes me up late in the day as a dog impatient to eat.  when sunlight passes through my eyelids it means i could be photographing it, so i wake up, i want to start looking.  by around eleven or noon each day the sleep mask i have to wear to sleep slips off, and it\\\'s time to look again.  time to see things, time to think about them.  i love getting up for the day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can tell what kind of photo day it\\\'s going to be before i open the blinds.  i start the day, open the blinds, look out at what the light\\\'s like, check the cloud cover, give rain the stink eye, get excited when there\\\'s sun and not a cloud to be seen. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i do miscellaneous activities like work, administrate, eat, for the hours before later afternoon, keeping an eye on the sky outside. i live on the eleventh floor of a south facing building in downtown toronto, with a wide open view over and through the city and out to the lake. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s nearing the summer solstice, when the sun creeps higher every day and stretches the day out a few minutes more.  it gives me the luxury of walking out to shoot photos later and later each day.  if it\\\'s a photo shooting day, meaning any day that isn\\\'t raining beyond a light drizzle, i leave an hour to three hours before the so-called \\\'golden hour\\\' time.  in late may as it is now, i start getting ready to leave the apartment between four and five o\\\'clock.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fetch the batteries from the charger, swap out the used one and pop in the charged one.  put on some music.  clothes, always the same thing, same pair of pants, same black hoodie, black ball cap.  same pair of shorts if outside is warm, and rotating t-shirt.    </p>\r\n\r\n<p>shoes.  minidisc and a few selections in the back pocket.  at this point it can become a terrible limbo, wanting to just be outside already, not going fast enough, never going to get there, i have dreams about it.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>apartment key and cash in zipper pocket.   photo cards to hand out to strangers in side pocket.  hat on, brim low.  i fish the cord and the minidisc ear buds up and under my shirt, through the neck, and seal them into my ears.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>grab the camera off of the table, throw the strap over my head around my neck, shut off the music, one last mental checklist.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at this point, i sometimes smoke a huge bowl, and sometimes i do not, but this is not that story.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>head out the door it clumps behind me, step down the carpet in the hallway to the elevator.  turn on the camera, check if i have downloaded the pictures that are currently on the card, if so, delete the contents of the card, watch the progress bar progress while waiting for the elevator.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i check exposure settings while the elevator glides me down to street level. thumb and forefinger fly across buttons and dials.  by the fourth floor i have the camera set for the first light i\\\'ll hit outside.  make sure minidisc is cued up and ready to start, but not playing yet.  wrap the neck strap around my wrist and let the camera rest in my right hand.  the weight feels so good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>bing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>across the shiny floor to the light, press through two sets of doors and i\\\'m there. i only start the music then, to begin walking and looking.  anticipation burns warm in my chest, the sunlight touches warm into my skin from afar.  here.</p>'),(3629,'los cabos - may 2004<p>\r\n. . . . . . . . . . .\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n..background..\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nI had no expectations, none at all.  I booked my beach vacation for the end of May to take advantage of the Memorial Day holiday, and thus saved on the meager 15 vacation days allotted to corporate US employees.  With no expectations, I was almost sure that I would not encounter any romantic adventures in the coastal resort of Baja California.  First of all, due to its proximity to California and the rest of the United States, and from a demographic standpoint the travelers will definitely consist of Californians and the rest of the mainstream Americans.  I have nothing personal against them (who am I to say, I who now carries a dual passport:  one from the European Union and the other a United States passport), but growing up in an expatriate and multicultural environment in Southeast Asia, travelled to Europe since I was 16, educated in the States, worked in high-tech and traveled more during the dot-boom and bust; I am quite embarrassed to admit that I have high expectations where cosmopolitan environments are concerned.  Being a daughter of a Foreign Service diplomat, as well as being a worldly free thinker who fancies everything from Aristotle, Nietzsche, Neruda, Lorca, Che Guevarra, Richard Feynman, Minsky, Vertosick, Brian Greene (I have his autograph!), and list goes on.  In contrast, my interests span further, of course, thanks to my sister - J.Lo, Prada, Gucci, Fendi and Italian fashion.  Cabo, was not really the ideal place, that could potentially cater to my worldly, intellectual, high-tech (they don\\\'t even have candy-painted RJ-45 cables) and cosmopolitan needs.  My idea of a real vacation is in Porto Cervo (as I had in the summer of 2002), basking under the Sardinian sun by day; dancing to the latin beat (Las Ketchup?) at the Billionaire\\\'s Club by night.\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\nAs a background, am of the late30s/early40s range, but I would say, not\r\nquite looking my age at all, a free-thinker, single mother (but financially independent - via my career and parents who graciously supports my beautiful 13 year old son), eclectic is an understatement, ... unpredictable, yet a very energetic, get the job done, ideal Silicon Valley geek.  (I have quite a few trophies of \\\"Professional Services Consultant of the Year\\\" types of awards).  At work, I am one of the geeks who enjoys sitting in a cold Data Center where I am one of the boys.  At a shopping mall I revel to enjoy being with my sister and girlfriends where I can be one of the girls.  Finally, while in the gym and boxing classes - well, I try to be like Ali, (at least in my dreams).\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\n.. my emotional state of being ..\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\nI have been divorced for almost a year, after about I think 7 or so years of marriage. Was it a happy marriage, in retrospect, I am not sure.  I think it was the right thing to do at that stage in my life where my career was blossoming, the times were great especially for geeks in the industry when we were a part of the wave that peaked during the internet boom.  Life was great until the bust; though I was never a victim of lay-offs, I have been unhappy due to my romantic and idealistic approach where work, academics and thinking is concerned.  I have a heart and soul of a scientist and artist, a forceful and energetic zest to create beautiful things whether it is troubleshooting a network problem, software installation problem, writing an essay, or teaching a geek class.  During the bust, I was forced to look within,\r\nthus realizing the state of my marriage.  My thoughts then, could be summarized as follows:\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\n\\\"Have you ever really, .. I mean, really, tried to answer the question: what is love?  At some point in your life, did you ever wonder whether love is real, a myth, or just another word?  Is there a simple answer to address the immortal ponderings on the nature of love?  In the past we created many ways of depicting love.  Love is manifested in mythological characters, in novels where writers immortalize love through stories of young lovers ... love has been characterized in many ways, more than once, to be both tangible and intangible, as a synergistic phenomenon between two entwined souls.\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\nUsing the scientific model, others have tried to explore this further with objectivity in mind and rational tools at hand.  Crenshaw\\\'s \\\"The Alchemy of Love and Lust\\\" investigates the mystery of love and sex by understanding the chemical soup base: the basic elements that drive human sexual and emotional behavior.  What about the argument of nature vs. nurture?  How can chemicals, per se, serve as the fundamental basis for characterizing love?\r\np>\r\n</p>\r\nHow can I fathom the essence of sex and love?  Perhaps it can be understood from a scientific viewpoint, from a systems perspective, or from observing emergent behavior within a system.  I have traversed through the cycle of falling in, getting bored, falling out, of love. I had my first kiss, I broke up, I was engaged, I broke up, I indulged in frivolous liaisons, I broke up, I got married, while married I had a few lovers, I broke up, I got bored, I got divorced. \r\np>\r\n</p>\r\nIn every love affair I experienced, it seemed that the nature, the feeling, the concept, the chemicals of love were not of the same material or form.  Love has manifested itself to me, not as a single and consistent entity, but has presented itself as unique experiences each time.  Love did not have a face, or a touch; it did not have a shape, or a consistent form.  For each experience, the attributes of love were not the same; the feelings and intensity were different.  I sought to find out exactly what has been discovered to-date on this phenomenon.  The stories, conjectures or conclusions did not suffice to provide an unbreakable theory of love.\\\" \r\n\r\nFor almost a year, I have beem happily divorced.  I have not felt sublime happiness as I have experienced in this period of my life.  Yes, there was a single set-back in the beginning of the year, where I had a powerful one-night stand encounter (yes, we do have each other\\\'s contact information); but as I always do, I got over it ...\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n.. back to Cabo ..\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nMy expectations were immediately validated from the beginning:  the flight was filled with frat-boy type of groups chugging pints of beers or shots of tequila, while still on the ground in SF.  Thank god for my CD-man and my hiphop music - I drowned the environment with either my hiphop CD or my Festival Bar 2002 - Italian hits CD.  I then closed my eyes and imagined I am elsewhere.  Upon landing, the dry and arid mountain landscape drew closer, I blasted my CD playing Euro-pop music and pretended that I was about to be in my Mediterranean coast.  My body soothing calm and relaxed, while frantic travelers maintained stress waiting in line, eager to go wherever they were going.  I was chilling in solitary bliss and so looking forward to my planned agenda for the rest of the day - quick unpacking, a few hours in the beach, shower and exploring the town of Cabo San Lucas to find a perfect local dive.\r\n\r\nThe first day turned out as planned.  I had the added bonus of finding a hip and young clothing store for very cheap.  I found two perfect light jeans mini-skirt, exact style as those sold by Miss Sixty, for very very less, like 80% less?  That find, in itself, deserved a treat of a nice dinner and a margarita.  Back to the hotel, a bit buzzed, I excitedly changed into my cute mini skirt!  It looked sooooo perfect and hip, very European, albeit, the skirt was made in Mexico, and the sexy top I purchased from the Haight.  I had to go to the local bar, at least to parade my new finds, and get a bit more buzzed for a good night\\\'s sleep.  \r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nThe choice to go to the bar (as opposed to nesting safely in slumber) led to the disruption of my solitary weekend confinement.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nAt the bar, I ended up joining a group of disparate travelers:  a girl who stayed for a few more days after her fiancÃ©e flew back to So Cal for work, two brothers waiting for their two other buddies on a for the boys bonding at the golf-course weekend.  It was a great evening where everyone chatted with each other and thereafter went on his or her merry way.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nThe next day was a full day of sun.  It was exactly what I needed to regroup my mind from the stress associated with corporate world; not to mention the difficulties Silicon Valley in general has experienced since the bust.  I was not a geek this weekend, but an invisible being in need of sun, nursing a hangover, and struggling to read the story of Cicero.  By the late afternoon, the girl I met the night before came over for a chat.  The chat turned into dinner plans, turned into strolling through the local plaza of San Jose, turned into going back to the hotel bar, and turned into hooking up with the four golfing buddies and agreeing to party in the local college-like in-place called: Squid Roe.  They were all generic guys to me, in fact reminded me of the comraderie feeling I had working at this Austin-based Software company in the late 90s (my favorite company throughout my life).  I was excited to get that feeling again, sort of like a Sales-kickoff, let\\\'s party with the boys feeling.  Most of them were from Houston and the other from Chicago.  They were:  Skidder, Wayne and Aaron.  I did not catch the name of the other one, ... he struck me a bit ... like a little spark.  Just a little spark; why, because, I was not looking forward to going to the club with the hopes of meeting other men and dumping them, the thought I toyed around with earlier, (I qualified my agreeing to go with them with the line, oh, and I guess I can just cab it by myself, if I get tired and want to go home, right?).  I somehow felt relieved and at ease with his presence.  All six of us cramped into the car, and for some reason, I felt committed to party with them all night.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nHow can I describe Squid Roe?  It\\\'s not different from the in-clubs you find in Austin\\\'s 6th St., New Orleans\\\' Bourbon St., and all the other coastal or party streets within the United States (including Hawaii and surrounding Caribbean spots).  The guys were perfect gentlemen and very nice.  He, on the other hand was extra nice (or was I simply filtering this perception) ... though we all danced with each other, this particular guy and I were together most of the time.  He whispered flirtatious comments into my ears, attempted to kiss me then gently kissed my neck; and on a couple of occassions rapped a sweet poetry - that rhymed in hiphop rhythm.  I was not drinking much - 2 glasses of beer for the duration of the night, since I was paranoid on how the bathroom situation was going to be (considering the mass of people and the drinking crowd).  I wanted to be in control.  When he had a chance, he caressed my legs through my tight jeans, wrap his arms around my waist from behind, and at one point, I sat in his lap ... all in a jokingly, yet meaningful manner.  We all drove back at around 4am and I consciously avoided walking near him to ensure that I would not be tempted to lead the flirtation further.  Besides I did not think it was proper since we were all in a group, and did not seem like good group protocol to have some hookup with the other, felt incestuous.  \r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nHow can I describe him?  He is on the heavy side (not the flat 6-pack stomach criteria, I just recently appended to my growing list of an ideal soulmate\\\'s characteristics).  Yet, he carries his weight so well.  He is African American with a bit of a goatie, dark curled lashes, a sweet, sparkling and naughty smile.  He moved so smooth and danced with perfect hiphop and Latin rhythm.  He is cool and gentle, a sweet teddy bear.  The adjective gentle rings over and over again in my ears, because that was what he was - gentle.  Earlier, he told me that I was \\\"sunshine\\\", because my smile radiates sunshine.  Very corny, I would say, but even the coldest of hearts could not help melt, such as mine, when he whispered that into my ears, and I looked straight into his eyes. \r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n.. a one night stand ..\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nI spent breakfast and the rest of the morning of the day after with the girl.  She was leaving in the afternoon back to LA.  We were nursing our weary bodies from a bit of a hangover and nonstop dancing.  We talked about girlie stuff, it was a refreshing experience as being a geek in the Valley, most of my friends are men.  I have a few girlfriends that I can relate to from a female perspective, it was refreshing to get in touch with one\\\'s female side, once in a while.  We talked about the night before and laughed at how funny that we forgot that guy\\\'s name.  The whole time I wanted to tell her about what I felt for that guy whose name we forgot.  But she and I were mere strangers; I was unable to really tell her that something happened that night (albeit in a very strange manner).  How can one succinctly describe in words [concept of word evolved into existence for the purpose of denoting a thing, a physical thing] a vague instinct, an invisible connection that occurred without any referential point to any material object or event?  After she left, I had hopes of bumping into them, however, my rational mind forced myself back into solitude.  I decided to have a nice cocktail and appetizers on a hip restaurant by the cliff.  It was a great moment - beautiful sunset, a couple of glasses of exotic flavored margaritas, my digicam and my thoughts.  It was empowering to think, feel, react and converse with one\\\'s self, without having to deal with another being.  It is scary and at the same time exciting to observe how comfortable I am to be alone, almost devoid of self-consciousness vis a vis what others may perceive of me.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nI walked around the hotel estate for a while.  The dusk, the ocean, the waves, and a little buzz in my head, led me back into the hotel local bar for a few more drinks.  After 3 days of abstinence from checking email and the Internet in general, I surfed for a bit hoping to get sleepy and shed off the hopes of bumping into them again.  By this time alcohol has clearly influenced my ability to think clearly.  I was toying with the idea of just calling him.  Before she left she bequeathed unto me everyone\\\'s room numbers.  She was the social coordinator for the weekend and had all the room number information on us all.  I had a bit of a struggle going on in my head.  Hell, get it over with, instead of living to regret and trying to fantasize on the what-ifs, just go back to your room and pickup the damn phone!  I have experienced both situations where I have succumbed to desire and where I successfully parried the thought.  In both instances there was a sense of loss, yet at least where I succumbed, there was always the satisfaction of knowing how that experience felt.  At the same time, I know I was a coward for relying on alcohol to jumpstart the courage and gain strength to pickup the phone and call him.  I called him.  I had two room numbers:  Room 1639 - nope, not here... (drats, I was soooooo ashamed!), Room 1638 - yes one moment.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n.. from midnight to 4am ..\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nI don\\\'t know what we talked about on the phone, I was in an intoxicated state.  Then I finally said, \\\" Want to walk on the beach? \\\".  \\\" Yeah, give me 10 minutes, I\\\'ll meet you by the elevator. \\\"  He walked out of the elevator in his very hip gangster look, I just wanted to grab him and squeeze him tight.  I actually did.  For some reason I really enjoyed his body, it was not as fit as mine, but so comfortable.  We walked hand-in-hand on the beach.  What did I feel was it really him, or was it the moment?  I think I had the need for intimacy, to caress and hold someone else\\\'s hand; to feel needed and wanted; to share intimate thoughts, to feel secure and close to someone else sexually and emotionally.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nBy the way, I found his name - it was Gary, I believe, Gary, short for Garrett, or was it Carey.  \r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nWe went back to my room thereafter and made out.  Though I think I turned him off right away by proudly handing him a 3pack of condoms.  It has been years since I have played the mating game, only one encounter since my divorce, so perhaps my dating skills are a bit rusty.  We talked a lot, in between making out.  I liked to caress him.  Kissed his full lips - it felt so good to kiss his full lips and smother him with kisses all over his face, his chest, his panson (little stomach pouch).  I am not sure if I have even kissed a man with beautiful full lips, it felt so good.  His eyes had dark curled-up lashes.  He had a tender stare that revealed his gentle soul.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nI was surprised to know that he was already in his early 40s.  He seemed younger, they all did, but then again, he thought I was younger too.  He is divorced, as I am, with 3 children, I found this fact a good point to consider as a possibility, since he is already done with the family-thing-experience.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nOur experience left me with a feeling that he was of a kindred soul.  He had a very calm, soothing and chill-out sort of a demeanor, a nice balance to my type-A fast-paced personality.  Though my rational mind incessantly pushed its stubborn voice reminding me that he does not possess the all inclusive attributes of an ideal partner: physically fit with a six-pack, intellectual, spiritual, sense of humour, serene and zen-like, free-spirit, etc.  Does love really require the logical AND operator in between each criteria to guarantee success?  How many months and years of trial and error, debugging the code of love, before the all-inclusive one is found?  No wonder marriages fail, love falters and people waste their lives in a relentless search for an ideal that is impossible to attain.  No wonder, I have managed to maintain a cold, cold, steel heart.  Yet, I sensed he has wisdom, like perhaps he\\\'s worth a try.  Is it because he was altogether a new experience for me?  He left at 4am, and when I woke up the next day, while nursing a hangover with tons of liquid, a morning of silver-shopping therapy in the town and a sumptuous local mariscos meal, back to the hotel pool, I caught myself thinking of my brown teddy bear, with a smile painted on my face.  However, I tried to brush-off thoughts of him.  While in a conversation the night before, he mentioned wanting to visit me in San Francisco, I panicked.  Why is it that when a romantic opportunity presents itself, I get scared?  I felt the tinge of suffocation, like I was being restrained.  \r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nWhile dozing off with my CD blasted, laying on the pool, I see two legs approaching, I gradually look up to see red shorts and my man in his smile.  I looked up and there he was, sheesh, I wanted to hug and kiss him but he was not alone, he was with his buddies, I felt embarrassed.  I had to make a split-second decision here - one more night with the possibility of pursuing this encounter with the risk of being hurt or should I just fade away, vanish and hide, so that the only recollection he would have of that evening will be memories of the hours of hugging and caressing and exchanging sweet nothings?  I chose the latter.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nThey dropped by to say hi and they were to hangout by the bar.  If I stayed the whole afternoon, perhaps we\\\'d hook up for drinks later, and so on.  After a few minutes, I escaped into my room, took a shower, went to town, back to my room and slept until the next morning, the checkout morning.  With my CD blasted on my ear I packed my bags and prepared for my flight back home.  I saw my phone indicator blink, and realized my phone is ringing!  I ran to pick it up, it was too late, the other line hang up.  Like a zombie I headed straight to the lobby, to the shuttle and into the airport terminal waiting for my flight back, with hopes of still bumping into him, so I could have given him my email, my number, remained deep in my soul.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\nThe plane has touched down in SFO, and now back to reality.  All I have were those tender memories, no last name ... just:  that his name is Carey/Gary/Gareth?, he\\\'s from Houston Texas, he golfs, he is 43 years old, is divorced with 3 children, he stayed in Room 1638 at the Westin Regina in Cabo San lucas between May 28 and May 31, and just that fleeting moment.  I guess my Mexican vacation left more than an alcoholic hangover, a sexual hangover, or some form of romantic hangover.  It will and should fade soon, but for now, I think I could have fallen for him.\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n\\\"brown_thunder, i miss you already.\\\"\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3630,'There is an unrelenting noise that cracks at my ears as I wake during the night. Itâ€™s the crickets, and under the chorus of hazy squeaking is the ascending creek of tree frogs. The air that is pushing through the open window is sweet and cool against my damp skin, and briefly I forget where I am. He is sleeping next to me. He is warm and quiet and I am mesmerized by the rise and fall of his back as he breathes toward my face. He is familiar and exciting, and a heaviness I have always equated with sadness fills my chest and forces my breath. There is no where at anytime, on any level I would rather be than here. I am excited to see him tomorrow and tell him about what I have been doing, about what I hope we will be doing. Eager to hear his humour and references of a culture we have cultivated together, I slowly role over and let the incoming breeze cool my thoughts. I anticipate a wonderful day. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe morning rises in the shadowed sky and I am robbed of what I thought would be a perfect onset to a likewise day. Instead I am greeted with indifference and like the air last night a sudden cold chill smacks awake every cell of my being. Something has changed. I have not changed he has not changed but what we shared has changed. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI am capable of thinking about times without him, but to remember the subtleties of a powerful spring evening with the same thought that I am afraid to be forgotten is blasphemy at its greatest. Like tunnel vision I see nothing but happiness until the box is promptly and unexpectedly lifted up around me. Never did I think I was capable of such overtly foolish and unrealistic thoughts as the ones I forever rhymed in my head and my heart for so long with him. Did it never exist at all? Was it really all in my head? Can four years pass with certain complacency dressed up as love? I question all of my thoughts right now. I question all of my judgments right now. <br><br>\r\n\r\n It would be nice to have the box back, even just for a while.'),(3631,'<p>                                                                             \r\nI\\\'m at the Full Moon Party in Haad Rin on Koh Phangan, Thailand.                \r\nEight to twelve thousand, depending on who you ask, backpackers have              \r\ndescended on this beach for this monthly all night party.  I\\\'m at the           \r\nOrchid resort, who are providing Drum\\\'n\\\'Bass to their slice of the              \r\nbeach.                                                                          \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI glance over at the stairs.  I see the suit first.  A tight                    \r\nElvis-in-Vegas-style electric blue PVC jumpsuit with a plastic zipper           \r\nrunning from the wide lapels to the crotch, unzipped just enough to             \r\npart the breasts.  I haven\\\'t even focused on her face yet, and already          \r\nI\\\'m entertaining thoughts of playing with that zipper.  I arrive at             \r\nher eyes, and this pretty blonde is checking me out, too!                       \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nShe looks a lot like Paris Hilton.  Wait.  Could that be her?  No.              \r\nWell, maybe.  It doesn\\\'t matter.  She\\\'s hot, and she\\\'s checking                 \r\nme out!                                                                         \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nShe turns around to say something to her friend a step or two below,            \r\nrevealing that the entire back of the jumpsuit is entirely made of              \r\nblack transparent mesh (and that she\\\'s wearing only a G-string                  \r\nunderneath).                                                                    \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI simply must approach.                                                         \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAs I make my way towards her, the friend wanders off.  I note she is            \r\nfumbling with some locally purchased novelty oversize spectacles made           \r\nfrom semi-flexible raver glow rods.  This is my opening.                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nMe: Hello.  <BR>                                                                \r\nHer: Hello. <BR>                                                                \r\nMe: I think you should wear them on your head like sunglasses.  <BR>            \r\nHer: Here, you can have them.  <BR>                                             \r\nMe: No, see. I mean like this. (Demonstrates sunglasses as hairband             \r\npositioning on self).  <BR>                                                     \r\nHer: mm-hmm. <BR>                                                               \r\n</p>            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI return the glowspecs to her, just as her friend returns.  Although            \r\nshe doesn\\\'t need to, she turns her back completely to me to talk to             \r\nthe friend.  The message is clear: we are done.                                 \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nSo, I make my own 180 degree turn and wander off, trying to hold my             \r\nhead high.                                                                      \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nA bit later, I ask myself again, was that Paris Hilton?                         \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nI think that it was.  In addition to her resemblance to the one I\\\'d             \r\nseen on teevee (and the speed with which she summed up and dismissed            \r\nme), both she and her friend were dressed an order of magnitude better          \r\nthan everyone else around.  Their clubwear clearly didn\\\'t come out of           \r\na backpack, unlike nearly everyone else\\\'s outfit on this somewhat               \r\nremote island in Thailand.                                                      \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAnd besides, it makes for a better story, that way.                             \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n                 '),(3632,'    It\\\'s peculiar to me how things seem so beautiful at ugly times in life. It\\\'s almost as if people know your inner being without even knowing you at all. Sometimes, people say that nobody care, nobodies listening, but, I swear, the people who know you just feel you when you\\\'re at a desperate time, regardless of how much you communicate. My best friends from high school stopped by tonight to catch up on the past year of life. Everything changes but nothing remains like the imprints people leave on your soul. <br><br>\r\n     It\\\'s weird how I honestly feel like I don\\\'t miss him and the next minute my heart feels torn to shreds. Have I honestly been putting off having a broken heart? I mean, is that possible to do? I love the simply crooked things we did together, in fact, I miss them. I often think about our unborn children that may never be. I even find myself missing them too! Today, I was waiting to be escorted from the airport and I saw two children, twins, darker babies, both girls, grabbing for their mother. As my brother drove off, I was overwhelmed with lethargic notions and nostalgia. What was it that could such an effect on me? I miss those feelings of hope. Sometimes I feel like the baby, reaching but not communicating exactly what it is I want or need. Perhaps I need to overcome this. I cherish those babies, for with them I shared a piece of my being I will never feel to share with another.\r\n'),(3633,'\r\n<p>5 am light reminds me softly that there\\\'s still time to sleep, so relax.  Enya is on the CD player, but it promises not to disturb.  You don\\\'t know it, but you glow in the dark.  Physically, you look how I feel.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The morning is chilly, but not cold, and weâ€™re cozy inside.  The room is in semi-disarray, but itâ€™s hidden by semi-darkness.  There are understated layers to your bed.  None acts alone, but all contribute to comfort when I lay down.  I have never felt more at home in my life.</p>  \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3634,'\r\n<p>\r\n5 am light reminds me softly that there\\\'s still time to sleep, so relax.  Enya is on the CD player, but it promises not to disturb.  You don\\\'t know it, but you glow in the dark.  Physically, you look how I feel.\r\n</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The morning is chilly, but not cold, and weâ€™re cozy inside.  The room is in semi-disarray, but itâ€™s hidden by semi-darkness.  There are understated layers to your bed.  None acts alone, but all contribute to comfort when I lay down.  I have never felt more at home in my life.\r\n</p>\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3635,'<p>\r\n5 am light reminds me softly that there\\\'s still time to sleep, so relax.  Enya is on the CD player, but it promises not to disturb.  You don\\\'t know it, but you glow in the dark.  Physically, you look how I feel.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nThe morning is chilly, but not cold, and weâ€™re cozy inside.  The room is in semi-disarray, but itâ€™s hidden by semi-darkness.  There are understated layers to your bed.  None acts alone, but all contribute to comfort when I lay down.  I have never felt more at home in my life.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3636,'A friend of mine threw into my lap a question I never realized would end up having such an impact on me. He briefly stated that with all the good technology etc. that surrounds us why are we always able to project something better? Why are we dependant on an ever expanding and perpetual upgrade of our lives? Due to the elevated levels of alcohol in my system and lack of substance in my gut, I blew off this bullshit answer about how its money that makes us think for better things, that itâ€™s the self-serving commercial attitudes that there is always something better; I donâ€™t think thatâ€™s what he was looking for. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThis I surmised later on was a huge fucking cop out. Money is always the answer you give when you havenâ€™t given the question too much thought.<br><br>\r\n \r\nThe big issue and I donâ€™t know if he intended this, is the need for betterment floods every aspect of my life. I was on the subway heading to school when I realized this. This ideal seems to poison relationships, propagate self-loathing and inspire occasionally necessary change. I moved to Toronto again six months ago, and again I have left behind all the people that care about me in search of something better. Why is this? Is the thought that even if Iâ€™m alone and relatively unhappy, justifiable as long as Iâ€™m pursuing something bigger? Is it wrong that there are those out there who are happy with their service jobs and factory positions are happy with where they are? Or maybe itâ€™s only wrong because we are so quick to judge them because of it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nEveryday people enter and exit the subway system with me and I never felt before now how all of them I am certain are here for something better also. <br><br>\r\nBetter jobs, \r\nbetter lovers, \r\nbetter clothing, \r\nbetter coffee, \r\nbetter music, \r\nbetter movies, \r\nbetter friends, \r\nbetter cars, \r\nbetter home furnishings, \r\nbetter expectations to be and know you are better than you were. \r\nOr at least attempting to become better.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nI had prior to this new period of my life carefully and methodically stacked and stabilized everything I had always wanted and what was important to me. I had a fairly predictable future, and without much notice and reminiscent of games of Jenga we used to play, a very significant block was prompting removed. Now I find myself scrambling for some sort of semblance of my life.  Things were misplaced that should never have been, and as a result I have again thrown away many of the good things in order to \\\"better\\\" my life and myself. At least thatâ€™s what youâ€™re supposed to think.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI have spent much time in thought over this, and occasionally I change my mind, but the question \\\"Why do we insist on looking for something better?\\\" has a fairly simple answer; one Iâ€™m afraid to admit has the ability to unravel my expectations of where I am now and one so disappointing I wonâ€™t even write it down. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIn a moment of hopelessness and a renewed state of mind, I realized now how much I was enjoying myself with him and that pitcher of Stella, and how its not about me scrambling to fill in the gaps of my life but enjoying the gaps as well. Perhaps itâ€™s not about being better just doing things better. <br><br>\r\n\r\n....whether he knows it or not ( and maybe he will now), he has refined my thinking about my life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThank you.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3637,''),(3638,'<p>how do i tell you that i love you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>how do i show you? how will you know me?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i make these photographs to see you.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>my voice, some space, your ears.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yesterday i sat at a street corner, took a break from walking, sat on a hunk of concrete. the sun was falling, i watched all the people walking by. nothing happened, i breathed, watched, nobody remarkable passed, it was warm and i felt the sweat on my t-shirt touch my skin again. i was content, and could only know it afterwards. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>a rope from my mouth to your ears and a secret between us, don\\\'t look at me to understand.  a blink and you\\\'re painted behind my eyelids to consider and fade, i won\\\'t see you again. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>it is then that our hands drop to our sides and watch wondrously the sky that is a mirror too big for us to find reflection. but for each other we stand still and gaze up, not across, sleeping in movement that sways us, not knowing we want to hear words.</p>'),(3705,'<P>	\\\"Are we in your story?\\\"</P><P>\r\n	She doesnâ€™t know that, as she says this, Iâ€™m writing down her every word. </P><P>\\\"Yeesssss...\\\" I pronounce, as I copy down her last sentence.\r\n	</P><P>She gives a short laugh. \\\"Okay.....\\\" She and her friend go into her room.\r\n	</P><P>Jehnna, Amyâ€™s roommate, streams energetically down the hall, and says, \\\"You like workin\\\' in the hall, donâ€™t you?\\\"\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Well, itâ€™s not work, as it is usually defined...\\\" I speak in vain. She has already gone into her room. A bevy of friends follows her. Amy is not among them. Damn.\r\n	</P><P>Itâ€™s noisy now, and hard to write. Harry, from upstairs, walks over and says, \\\"Hey Cory.\\\"\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Hey.\\\" Itâ€™s all I care to say in response.\r\n	</P><P>Jehnnaâ€™s friends, along with Harry, now open up the door to Saraâ€™s room.\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Sorry if weâ€™re interrupting anything,\\\" (I guess that means he is her boyfriend) \\\"...but we just wanted to drop by for some conversation...\\\"\r\n	</P><P>Now the voices crescendo, and itâ€™s even harder to write. I contemplate talking my existing notes and finishing in my room. Then I realize why Iâ€™m out here: Amy. (*sigh*...)\r\n	</P><P>Some guy whose face I recognize (but whose name eludes me at the moment) asks, \\\"Were you kicked out of your room?\\\"\r\n	</P><P>I look up from my work, wondering if heâ€™s addressing me, or one of the other passers-by.\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Were you kicked out of your room?\\\" he repeats. I guess he is talking to me.\r\n	</P><P>\\\"No...\\\" I donâ€™t feel like explaining my task again. Satisfied (I think), he turns away, off to do whatever the average anonymous person does on a Friday night.\r\n	</P><P>Harry walks out of Saraâ€™s room, followed by the rest of the cabal. He looks over my shoulder, his curiosity piqued. He sees nothing legible, (as my handwriting is atrocious) and moves on.\r\n	</P><P>No, I havenâ€™t been kicked out of my room. Not by my roommate, anyway. Graeme is acting in a play tonight, a French play for the Tintamarre society, and wonâ€™t be back until later. Lately, Graeme and	I have taken to speaking French in our room. I need the practice, if I hope to work as a bilingual tour guide on parliament hill this summer. My French is atrocious (like my handwriting).\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Hello, Cory.\\\" Beth walks into the hallway with some friends (that is another inconvenience of writing: when the muse calls, friends must be forsaken). I simply nod in response. I once had a crush on Beth, for many of the same reasons I now have a crush on Amy. The reasons are seven: Do, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La, and Ti. Music; both are musical people. Whereas I simply am a gifted amateur in the craft, they are full masters. I both envy and admire them. In fact, I remember sitting in this very spot, writing a poem, The Lament of Odysseus, inspired by listening to them practice for the chamber choir. If I could put a time index on when my crush on Amy began, it wouldâ€™ve been then.\r\n	</P><P>Why then, you ask, no more crush on Beth? Letâ€™s just say that that was ended by circumstance.\r\n	</P><P>I had found a group of friends at the beginning of the year. Beth, Lindsay, Gordon, and Brian. And Graeme. Sort of. We were a clique, one that I wanted to remain a part of. It soon became painfully obvious that the four were the kernel; I was excess baggage. So was Graeme. One night, sitting at a table with Brian, (always the diplomat, the voice of reason, on whom I counted, until then) Brian said, \\\"Cory, it seems like you and Graeme are kind of shadowing us; itâ€™s not that we donâ€™t appreciate your company,\\\" (bullshit. I know when Iâ€™m not wanted. I have never been very socially conscious, and neither has Graeme. But I know when Iâ€™ve been shunned.) \\\"... but we really need our space.\\\" (Meaning Brian, Beth, Lindsay and Gordon.) After that, things grew cold, and Beth and the group became casual acquaintances. Oh well. I suppose Iâ€™m not the best friend in the world either... perhaps Iâ€™m to blame. Had I alienated them? Had I not assimilated well? What made them reluctant to accept me? So far, the only friend who I know wants me is my muse...\r\n	</P><P>Amy. Oh God. Here she is. I quickly cover my work, to prevent her from seeing her name.\r\n	</P><P>\\\"Homework in the hall?\\\" She gives a pretty smile. I grin like an idiot.\r\n	</P><P>After this, she walks into her room, and I safely release my work from my chest, where I had clutched it in hiding. Maybe I should drop this, go in there, ask if she wants to go to the toonie movie at the Vogue... last night was Halloweâ€™en, and she, Harry, Sara and I went to see the Rocky Horror Picture Show. That was almost a date... wasnâ€™t it? I donâ€™t know... was it? I just donâ€™t know...\r\n	</P><P>She just walked out. Damn. She makes some small talk about supper with somebody walking past. Damn, I should have gone in there! Why donâ€™t you release me, muse? I do need friends of the physical world, after all!\r\n	</P><P>Amanda, who lives diagonally across the hall, walks out of her room and asks, \\\"How come youâ€™re workinâ€™ in the hall?\\\"\r\n	</P><P>I sigh. A deep, frustrated sigh. Why am I working in the hall? Why arenâ€™t I out there, like everyone else, living their Friday night? I couldnâ€™t explain everything to Amanda, about Amy, and the muse, or my friends (ex-friends, call them what you like), so all I can do is sigh, a despairing, angry sigh, the sigh of a man imprisoned by his muse.\r\n	</P><P>She walks away, as puzzled as I am. I decide that now is the time to end this, to break my bittersweet embrace with the angels, and seek the bittersweet embrace of humanity. To embrace humanity until I become weary of the real, and seek the arms of the muse once more...\r\n	</P><P>I laugh.\r\n	</P><P>A laugh of joy and pain.									</P>														'),(3639,'I walked into a Burger King the other day, and explained to the woman behind the counter that I had  accidentally misdialed Fiji. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nShe said she couldn\\\'t give me instant credit. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nI said, <i>\\\"But AT&T always did!\\\"</i> <br><Br>\r\n\r\nShe blubbered, <i>\\\"But youre not dealing with AT&T!\\\" </i><br><Br>\r\n\r\n<i>\\\"Well I am <b>NOW</b>!\\\"</i> I said as I stormed out the door.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nThen, I walked to the nearest payphone, called the operator and ordered a Whopper.<br><Br>'),(3640,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3640_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"11\\\"><br><Br>\r\nWe lost Charles, our favorite blonde dwarf hamster who looks like a tiny, tubby teddybear with pink ears and nose... The Big Daddy, the aging patriarch of our hamster family, he\\\'s been having some health problems (he\\\'s getting up there in hamster years), so we were slightly more concerned for him than usual when we noticed he had escaped his cage through a tiny opening in the lid, a seemingly acrobatic feat for such a chill old cutie like Charles. Oh no... ham on the loose.<br><Br>\r\nWe\\\'ve had hams escape before... they generally turn up in less than a day when my eagle-eyed wife spots them scurrying across the floor or scampering towards the little bowls of food and water we surreptitiously leave on the floor as enticements to bring them back from their wayward journeying. If they don\\\'t turn up after a day or so, though, well, that means they\\\'re lost for good, escaped into the infrastructure of our building, our city, oblivion.<br><br>\r\nCharles escaped on Sunday afternoon. As of Thursday, four days later, he hadn\\\'t turned up despite our prayers, little dishes of food and water and abundant worrying. Poor Charles. We were resigned to the loss of our favorite ham, and rather profoundly sad about it. For three days, it was as if a dark haze of depression had stubbornly settled over our house. Many were the times I\\\'d pass the studio and see Sine gazing forlornly into the empty hamster cage, face filled with sadness, reddened cheeks pressed into white palms.<br><Br>\r\nThursday morning was cold and sprinkly, so I took the train to work. As we pulled into the 135th street stop, I crossed my legs and felt something move in the toe of my left shoe. I was slightly bummed, figuring that the innards of my 25 year old green suede Keds was finally wearing out. Easily irritated by lumps in my shoe or sock, I pulled off my shoe (conspicuity in the subway is underrated) to see what was going on down there... and found Charles. Yep. Stuffed into the one-size-too-large abundant toe of my shoe. Good thing I didn\\\'t bike, the toeclips would have been problematic.<br><Br>\r\nWith a literal shout of \\\"HOLY FUCK!\\\" that got the attention of the entire train car, I scooped the body of my tiny certainly-dead hammie out of my shoe, right there as the train pulled out of 135th street; a troubling prospect for anyone, no matter how you slice it. Charles\\\' body was horrendously dirty; his silky blonde hair was matted, sticky and blackened. His paper-thin left ear was a crushed red mass; his pallid pink skin showed through the several patches of missing hair. One of his eyes was swollen completely shut. No signs of life at all. it was just about to be too much for me to handle; I am somewhat sensitive about pets dying, even moreso when retrieving such from my shoe... on the subway going to work.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nBut then I saw a tiny, miniscule little breath. Then a nose twitch. Then one eye vaguely opened with the odd charm that only a beloved pet coming back from the shock of being at the verge of death can have. Charles was alive!<br><Br>\r\n\r\nRather than waste the time backtracking home, I decided to simply take Charles into work with me. Hell, i work at a hospital, and had everything on hand that I could possibly need. Sprinting from the 168th street station into my office, a fading Charles cradled in hand, I noticed him squirming some, showing the various signs of life that are sparked by being rescued from certain death in your master\\\'s shoe...<br><br>\r\n\r\nI brought Charles in and immediately washed him and cleaned his woulds with betadine; he has a chronic infection on his belly that I was certain would abcess after being exposed to whatever muck he tramped through while on the dole. Although I was certain he was beyond mere dehydration, he refused water, which I was sure would be his key to quickly recovering. Mustering the rodent-injection techniques gleaned from my previous job, I gave Charles 15cc\\\'s of saline subcutaneously, as well as a surely appreciated drop of ketamine, and set to making him a nest of shreaded paper towels in a box here in my office. Before I could even get him situated in his box, he was already walking around some, hobbling on his exhausted, injured little legs.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nSomewhat hydrated, pain-free and clean, Charles began to immediately animate. He started nibbling lightly on a bit of my coworker\\\'s doughnut and taking sips of water from a tiny paper dish I rigged up. Charles was very quickly on the mend...\r\n<br><Br>\r\nI decided to give him some time to convalesce in peace and calm down a bit before subjecting him to another 40 minute train ride home; the tiny ham-brain can only tolerate so much shock in a day. I placed him snugly in his box with some water soaked pieces of pita bread (which he ate every single slimy bit of as the day quietly passed) and hid the box in a warm, quiet corner of my office and tried to get some very preoccupied work done.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nAfter lunch, I heard him scratching around in the box. Looking in on him, I discovered that he was \\\"bathing\\\", doing his typical hamster trick of smoothing his ears and whiskers down with his front paws, looking content and happy for the first time in quite some time. I decided to leave work early and take him home.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nWhen I got to the house, I found Sine standing in front of a sink of dirty dishes, frowning, moping. I said <i>\\\"hey, I brought you something from work\\\"</i>, which I often say before presenting her with a piece of candy or some other triviality, and she looked over at me like <i>\\\"oh well. ok, then\\\"</i>, still visibly depressed about the loss of Charles, unaware of his day of shoe-bourne oddyssey to 168th street and back.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe only face more animated than hers, when I placed a very tired but very happily alive Charles in her hands, was Charles\\\' face itself, when first opening his single unswollen eye after being retrieved from my shoe. It was an answered prayer, of sorts, a rare beautiful thing you have to stop and appreciate as it unfolds, a feeling of joy so fleeting and hard to put into words.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe fed Charles, got him comfy, and purchased for him a new, much larger space-age hamster house (with a securely locking door and glow in the dark wheel), in which he is now comfortably snoozing, most likely in his favorite old coffee-can, his look-alike blonde baby boy skirting around him in the cage.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nCharles is a happy hamster, and we are happy people thanks to his return. I\\\'m not sure how he got outside our apartment to where we store our shoes by the front door, nor why he chose to climb into my shoe, but I\\\'m glad he did... its what brought him home.<br><Br>'),(3641,'<p>Last year Mark had to go to war with the 3rd Infantry Division.  He was gone five months, and when he returned, we had to go to Ohio for school, and then we moved from Texas to Georgia in September.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe decided that we would try my living in our house in Florida, 5 hours away,  two weeks a month, and in Ga two weeks a month.  It looked to be working out pretty well.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen my Dad died.  Unexpectedly.  In his sleep.  On Christmas morning.  I doubt I will ever have the words to explain the last six months - except to say that I spent them in a fog of grief and sorrow, and after 50 years on this earth, learned to cry for the first time.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI was never a weeper.  Most women are, but I never was.  In my entire adult life, I probably cried a dozen times.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTears became my daily companion.  Gut wrenching sobs like you see on Lifetime, Television For Women...<br><br>\r\n\r\nFinally, a couple of weeks ago, I seemed to recover.  The tears are still behind my eyes, but they don\\\'t drip out so much.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut then...<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>the Abyss</b> <br><br>\r\nMark got orders to return to Iraq.  He leaves next week.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHe\\\'s an Army Civilian. This means he works for the Army, not a contractor, and he isn\\\'t going to get rich because he goes there.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo, you gotta be wondering - if he isn\\\'t gonna get paid big bundles of bucks, then why the hell is he going?<br><br>\r\n\r\nBecause of the robots.  There are these robots that go out and find bombs and mines and stuff, and then they detonate them so people don\\\'t get killed.<br><br>\r\n\r\nEvery day people are getting blown up by these IED things.  Not just soldiers - but lots of people, little kids, old ladies, people trying to rebuild their lives suddenly get their legs blown off.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe terrorists put the bombs in things like remote control cars, in things that don\\\'t look dangerous, which makes it even more likely some non-combatant (as the Army calls them) will get gutted for being curious.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere are only a few people who know how to teach other people how to use, repair, and maintain these robots.  Mark is one of them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s a job that will save lives.  He is going to go over there and try to do that.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>I shouldn\\\'t have to explain...</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nI understand that a lot of people blame politics and/or oil money for the war.  I\\\'m the first one to stand up and defend the right to have an opinion.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut I\\\'m also old enough to have lived through Vietnam.  Mark and I both protested the war in Vietnam, loudly, often, in groups, and on marches.  We have never veered from our belief that it was wrong.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nBack then, rabid anti-war as we were, we separated the politics of the war from the people who fought it. <br><br>\r\n\r\nNot everyone did.  Ask any Vietnam Vet what it was like back then.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI see a disturbing trend as elections grow closer,  a trend eerily reminiscent of those days when it was considered cool to spit on soldiers and call them babykillers.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLast year, when I told people my husband was in Iraq, you could see the sympathy in their eyes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow, when I tell people he is going to Baghdad, they invariably ask me why he doesn\\\'t quit his job.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m old enough to correlate electioneering and public sentiment with the reaction.  But what about those who aren\\\'t?<br><br>\r\n\r\nIntelligent people have a responsibility to themselves, and the rest of the world, to employ tact when expressing opinions.<br><br>\r\n\r\nStand up and be counted.  Make sure you vote.  Support the politician of your choice.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut don\\\'t ever forget that the men and women who are over there getting shot at deserve respect for the job they do.<br><br>\r\n\r\nDon\\\'t forget that you can never take back words, written or spoken.  You don\\\'t know who is listening or reading.  Somebody\\\'s wife, mother, father, son, daughter, brother, sister, friend...<br><br>\r\n\r\nProtest responsibly, and with compassion.<br><br>\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3642,'<p>\r\nOne of the things I find the most interesting about 4th of July celebrations, teevee specials, and newspaper spreads is this - there is very little talk about the historical events surrounding our independence.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNot too many people have ever actually read the <a href=\\\"http://www.house.gov/Constitution/Constitution.html\\\">Constitution</a> - although most people think they know what it says, most of us are a little surprised when we actually read the thing in it\\\'s entirety.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLike the Bible, the Constitution, and particularly the <a href=\\\"http://www.house.gov/Constitution/Amend.html\\\">Amendments</a> (Bill of Rights) to it are subject to interpretation by the the reader.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOur entire political and judicial system is built upon these interpretations.  Mostly by people who want to interpret in a way that supports whatever point of view they individually hold.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTake a few minutes and read it.  You will be glad you did.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow, if you aren\\\'t already registered to vote - go get registered.  Then, when the day comes, go vote.  Never assume an outcome of an election.  Remember that the outcome of the presidential election that is going to change your daily life.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s the outcome of the county commissioner, school board, judicial, tax appraiser, and other seemingly unimportant office races that are going to regulate your individual daily lives.  Pay attention to who is running, and what their opinions are.  It is more important than you think.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThis has been a public service rant by, thank you for your time.<br><br>\r\n\r\n</p>'),(3643,'<P>\r\nA Little New Media Collective Named Snuggles. Free Speech for Sale and Droplifting â€“ by Kerry (bleek) Swinney\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAttention shoppers! As you peruse the aisle of your random CD rack you may stumble upon a jewel case with an eye-catching image and a generous helping of tracks from a variety of artists, most or all of which youâ€™ve never heard of. If youâ€™re in a store with alarmed packaging around the CDs, youâ€™ll notice that the one in your hand is as unencumbered as an angry feminist at a bra burning. Interesting. Go see what the clerk has to say about it, ugh.... The kid doesnâ€™t know anything and canâ€™t find any info on the store computer. Not only that but the bar-code doesnâ€™t seem to work either. Maybe you want it anyway and they pull a price out of the air and on you go. Perhaps the track by Evolution Control Committee caught your expert, indie eye and you took the gamble, or perhaps you never gave it another thought. \r\n</P><P>\r\n<a href=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"><img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\" width=400></A>\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\n	Whether you like it or not youâ€™ve become a participant in the phenomenon know as \\\"dropliftingâ€™. A word coined by Richard Holland (aka Turntable Trainwreck) which is meant to describe the opposite of shoplifting, bringing in the goods and leaving them for no sane reason, except to perpetuate a kind of dadaist publicity stunt. Holland, not being picked up by a label or predicting not to be picked up, would discreetly drop his discs in stores, you know.... donâ€™t quit yer day job. Around the continent shifty characters are lurking around (usually) major department stores and CD racks droplifting the two thousand existing Free Speech for Sale compilations under the appropriate file or even right there in the top-seller display rack. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	A loosely knit collective known as Snuggles (Negativland fans will recognize the reference from the  \\\"U2\\\" single)  â€“ \\\"New Media Collective\\\" are behind the compilation of re-contextualised advertisements, cut-up, processed, remixed and generally fucked with. The Snuggles list is at least 9 years old and originally began as a Negativland discussion list. List members have taken the DIY ethic on in a funny yet combative or cynical strategy;</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\\\"The album\\\'s source material is copy written from several advertisers and producers who would charge us way too much money to legally clear the samples, if they are even willing to negotiate at all. The content of this album would likely offend them to the point where they wouldn\\\'t want it to exist, as it criticizes their very industry using the same clever tricks they use to sell their products. Frankly, we don\\\'t feel any of them deserve to get paid a second time for the recordings they\\\'ve already been overpaid to produce. More importantly, I feel what we\\\'re doing is making new art out of their old art, and furthermore, we should be allowed to be financially compensated ourselves for making such art! This is also one of the subtle ironies of this project.\\\" â€“ freespeechforsale.com\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nBut hey, now that you know the secret, donâ€™t be a patsy, all the tracks and more are available at the freespeechforsale.com site! . In fact Free Speech for Sale is the third in a series of compilations from the Snuggles group, the first two being named \\\"The Droplift Project\\\" and \\\"Dictionaraoke\\\" , not to mention various other side-projects and online give-aways from many of the artists. The Press kit explains it like this: \\\"With this recent compilation, we want to show the public the harmlessness of appropriating samples from commercials: harmless because the samples will not advertise anything...though they may say something altogether different, it just won\\\'t be the message the advertiser intended us to hear.\\\" explains Every Man. \\\"William S. Burroughs did it with real text in the \\\'Naked Lunch\\\', Andy Warhol did this with his \\\'Brillo Box\\\' sculpture and his \\\'100 Soup Cans\\\' painting, and we\\\'re doing the same thing in Free Speech For Sale with audio.\\\" The web-page designed to closely resemble the \\\"As Seen On TV\\\" site was built by Snuggles member Pimpdaddysupreme, who also works with audio-art troupe Workshoppe Radio Phonik, both of which appear on the FSFS CD.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThe CDâ€™s curator is a multi-talented computer systems administrator who goes by the alias Everyman. With other members of the list in various fits of flight and fancy, Everyman stepped up to the task of bringing Free Speech for sale into fruition. According to Everyman the project began being kicked around as early as 2,000 right after the Dictionaraoke compilation. Youâ€™ll find Everymanâ€™s contribution to the CD under yet another alias, the Button, which is derived from his late-night, 3 hour experimental radio show Press the Button on WRUW in Cleveland. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	After mailing hundreds of CDs around to interested droplifters, Everyman realized he hadnâ€™t even started his role in the CD giveaway but remedied that on a road trip from Ohio to the Burning Man festival in Nevada. \\\"... I droplifted some of these in the Flying J store by Interstate 80, and Iâ€™ve been doing this at every single Flying J stop. I drop 5 to 10 of these in the CD section which is right in front of the check-out counter and theyâ€™d never notice. I did this day after day after day while driving out (there), and at one point when I was standing in line I heard a trucker behind me picking it up and looking at it and he said the name of it, you know, he said (adding a southern drawl) \\\'Free speech for sale? This must be somethinâ€™ new because Iâ€™ve seen this at every goddamn truck-stop for the past 2 days!â€™ I was trying hard to stifle my laughter\\\". Stopping at the same locations on the way back from Burning Man, Everyman found that half of the Discs had been picked up. Either bought or stolen, and thatâ€™s the nature of the game, really. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	The website offers a place for droplifters to write stories of their exploits; where theyâ€™ve set the discs (ideally in a \\\"hot new\\\" items display somewhere), and hopefully soon, a victim... I mean lucky individual will post something about buying the disc. The stories may include something like this example from Snugglesâ€™ member The Former Yugoslavia; \\\"The other night the copy of FSFS that I dropped off at Target (Asheville) was still on the same end-cap more than a week later, only it had been moved in front of the new Lionel Richie CD. So I tried to buy it. Surprisingly, there wasn\\\'t much of a struggle - I got sent over to the customer service desk, they checked their database and couldn\\\'t find any matching products, so they sent me on my way with it... only to have me turn around, come back in the store and \\\"redroplift\\\" it (this time on a \\\"popular music\\\" end-cap in front of one of the registers). Fun fun fun...\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	Of course nobody really sees dime one from this experiment in nonsensical marketing, so why do it, and why take appropriated material and rework it to be something funnier, ironic and consequently more entertaining? \\\"Steve Hise discussed this a little bit,\\\" Everyman ventures to explain, paraphrasing audio artist and Detritus.net owner Hise \\\"where thereâ€™s different levels of plundering, thereâ€™s the subversive level, like why would an artist plunder, would it be for subversion or would it be because its so neat because itâ€™s so new, its against the law? You know, Iâ€™m doing this because itâ€™s wrong, just like some people smoke pot because itâ€™s technically illegal and they argue that if it got legal itâ€™d probably cut the amount of pot smokers in this country in half. A lot of people would try it just because itâ€™s legal but then it would just fade away.\\\" This, he says, likely makes up about 75% of the Snuggles collective.  \\\"...Then thereâ€™s the level of plundering thatâ€™s like \\\'I want to do this because I want to make a statement and I use recycling as a tool for my art and not necessarily because I only rely on recycling as an art form.â€™\\\" \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	Steve Hise (a contributor to Free Speech for Sale, etc.) told me about his involvement in the Snuggles group, which he said was fairly minimal at this point because of his own focus on the art of appropriation in audio and visual. His website Detritus.net hosts a number of on-the-go projects, news and issues around creating through plunder, or like the website says; \\\"Detritus is a gallery, a village, a library, a studio, a news service. Detritus is keeping watch on relevant events and developments, and always adding more content to its pages.\\\" Among the fine artists Steev calls friend, and may do the occasional collaboration with, are People Like Us, The Tape Beatles, The Evolution Control Committee and Wobbly just to name a few.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	 I asked Steev Hise about a recent speech he gave at the Electrofringe festival and got more about the question of why;  \\\"Electrofringe is a yearly electronic art festival in Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. I was invited and attended in 2001. (see electrofringe.org for more details ) My talk there was basically about using cultural recycling as a form of cultural activism. I also discussed the history and purpose of Detritus.net, and the 3 \\\"stages\\\" of cultural recycling, in my view. What I mean by stages are sort of modes of operation or rationale in artists that engage in cultural recycling.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nIn other words, why do people do it? The stages are:\r\n1. The Pleasure of the Intertext<BR>\r\n2. The Glamour of Theft<BR>\r\n3. Cultural Activism.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\nHe continues; \\\"These stages are not necessarily in that order for everyone, and often someone will drift back and forth between them or they co-exist in 2 or 3 stages at once. The first is about simply enjoying the act of cutting and mixing pre-existing cultural artifacts. It\\\'s fun.  The second is about exulting in the sort of outlaw chic of knowing you are \\\"stealing\\\" intellectual property. The third is using cultural recycling as a tool to convey meaning and hopefully make positive change in culture and society.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	Everyman admits that, to the Snuggles group, making a track compelling without the humour and irony can be difficult but valuable; \\\"... Sam Stevenâ€™s Cyberphobe (\\\'The Year of my Nervous Breakdownâ€™) CD where he included 9-11 samples, \\\"...me and David Dixon of Stark Effect were discussing that very concept saying that the one thing that the Snuggles artists use a lot is irony. It makes it cute, it makes it funny and does make it have a greater impact, but the one real hard thing to do is sometimes make a track without the usage of irony. Itâ€™s like the real artistic challenge. I felt that Cyberphobe did just that. Itâ€™s really cool, itâ€™s not funny but itâ€™s still entrancing to listen to.\\\"  \\\"Negativland has some tracks like that. My favorite on is off the album Free, which is called \\\'The Gun and the Bibleâ€™. Itâ€™s chilling, I mean the Bible, this loving, peaceful thing in the name of God and religion, which stands for the ten commandments, \\\'Thou shalt not killâ€™ but thereâ€™s so many people killed with a gun in the name of religion.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	Whether the tracks on Free Speech for Sale are ironic, silly, surreal, stupid or all of the above, they are unarguably compelling. Certain tracks and strategic edits are hard to shake from your mind sometimes, i.e. Tim Maloneyâ€™s \\\"Shatner\\\" with William Shatner informing you how to choose a career in \\\"TV VCR repair or TV VCR repair or get a degree in TV VCR repair\\\", Stop Childrenâ€™s \\\"Medicine Head 24 Hour\\\" in which  \\\"...one daily dose provides 24 hours of headache, diarrhea and abdominal pain.\\\" Evolution Control Committee mixes pineapples with Dippidy doodoo. Social Securityâ€™s touching \\\"Hi, Bye\\\" with the advice \\\"For free help call home.\\\" Toward the end of the disc some of the artists bring FSFS to an almost creepy end with darker audio-art collages. Itâ€™s truly a novel compilation with ingredients for the experimentally minded and thinking industrial fan.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	If that werenâ€™t enough, the website includes several bonus tracks from these artists and other potential Snugglers. \\\"I donâ€™t think the number has stopped because people keep making them\\\" Everyman says, \\\"I guess itâ€™s not clearly defined but weâ€™re always accepting bonus tracks. If you some idea of making a song out of a commercial then by all means, you know, thatâ€™s what the bonus site is for.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n	To get involved in droplifting, while the discs last, or to find out more about the Snuggles collective, go to the website at freespeechforsale.com \r\n</P><P>'),(3644,'<p>\r\nI saw from alah what was his plan.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndestruction and blame. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI lift up my head from such a shame and knew it wasn\\\'t the society that was to blame.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3645,'<P>\r\nJust when I thought I should probably start looking for some work to subsidize my schooling and ever present need to enjoy a movie or good sushi, I got a job. It was an email, simply stated it asked if I wanted part time work. Talk about ask and ye shall receive. Itâ€™s a position here at my school and instead of posting the job and sorting through countless applications, the head technician decided to just ask around. Iâ€™ll chalk much of my luck up to the fact that I am a flaming redhead in a sea of Asian and middle-eastern students, so perhaps Iâ€™m easy to remember. I am also female in a sea of guys so Iâ€™m even more of a sore thumb; the token woman thatâ€™s a token red-head. I was once told by a strange little man I didnâ€™t know that I was part of a -visible minority- and that I should be paid more in my work. He also stated that redheads stress out too easily and that I should be allotted more vacation time as well. As much as I disagree with the implications the subsequent bonuses would be nice. Perhaps I should notify my new employer about this. \r\n</P><P>\r\nItâ€™s strange how people react to my hair. A former boyfriend of mine would hold a small strand of it in his hand when we were lying down and baffle at the fact that it grew out of my head. I have been approached many a time by older men with pitiful pick up pitches like, needing to see if I had green eyes to match my hair or ask if redheads really are \\\"fiery\\\". This is usually followed by a roll of my eyes and an exit in disgust. Of course there is always the drunken question of whether of not the \\\"curtains match the drapes\\\"; which is my favourite. I was even told once that because I was a redhead I must have a whorish disposition. It was stated that brothels are synonymous with redheads and it wasnâ€™t just coincidence. Hmm interesting statement, perhaps Iâ€™m missing my calling. Iâ€™m really not sure what motivates men to share these sort of things with me, I donâ€™t think they mean to degrade, I donâ€™t think these guys are smart enough for that. Is my hair really that different? You know, I never really thought about it. If Tom Robbins is correct, maybe I am part of a superior race!\r\n</P>'),(3646,'<p>Heh... yeah... I think Incitor is a pompous ass, and I\\\'m currently laughing at myself about that thought because I know I\\\'d be way too chicken-shit to say that to his face.  So is the power of the internet: You can say things about people to them without being close enough to get punched in the face.  Besides, from what I can figure out about the man, I really like him, and he should know that if he remembers anything about me.</p><p>So, why am I here apparently insulting Incitor?  I\\\'m not sure.  I kinda lost interest in Swinney a couple years ago.  It got crowded.  Some of the people I liked stopped writing as much.  My life changed, and I didn\\\'t have as much time for this anymore.  But I still stop by occasionally to see what is going on, and the most recent visit here resulted in some fresh stuff from Incitor.</p><p>Now, Incitor was one of my favorite people on this site.  Probably because he reminds me of a lot of people from my past - people with a stoic sort of intellectual arrogence; people who look at you and think, \\\'I\\\'ve seen and understand so much more life and death than you ever will;\\\' people who court violence without even realizing it and yet have a profound sense of compassion and morality.  He also reminds me of a life I used to always skirt around the edges of without ever getting close enough for it to bite.  Luckily, I was smart enough to realize how much of a pussy I am.  I\\\'ve known quite a few who weren\\\'t that smart.</p><p>This isn\\\'t about me though.  This is about Incitor.  or Dave.  or whatever people call him.  How does it feel?  How does it feel to have an entire article written just about you, Dave?</p><p>heh...</p><p>So, why did I write this whole, long thing in the first place?  I guess, it was just to call Incitor a pompous ass... just some strange, uncontrollable urge.  Of course, once I got that down I had make sure he understood it wasn\\\'t meant to be insulting in any way.  Then I had to explain my strange fondness for the guy.  eh... so it goes... hope it wasn\\\'t too boring.</p>'),(3647,'<P>Iâ€™ve been having dreams about you. Theyâ€™re the ones that when you wake up you feel totally empty and betrayed from a cruel conscience that doesnâ€™t know when things get inappropriate. Like a friend you havenâ€™t seen in a long time and old regrets are unmasked with questions of a time youâ€™d rather forget about. \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhy is my brain doing this? \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe dreams are really nice. Theyâ€™re dreams that lend themselves to fictitious reunions and warm embraces, dreams that forget the past and only remember the good things, dreams I never want to wake up from, and dreams where youâ€™re proud of me and what Iâ€™m doing and we find the company of each other stimulating and important. Conversations that never really took place, words I have spoken that splash against the back drop of this artifice painted as you. This is just fiction and I will justify your role as nothing more than my brain unable to purge your image in substitution of another. Old data that lingers within my mind. It could never be you; theyâ€™re holograms and murmurs of time that swim in and out of my scope of vision. \r\n</P><P>\r\nIt makes me sad when I wake up to again experience the loss of intimacy and familiarity that comes with being with someone for long periods of time. Sometimes I want to protest all my former beliefs in meaningful relationships and just plunge into the anonymity that is the one night stand, but I know that just as a hangover from a satisfying stupor of intoxication, you canâ€™t just do these things and expect no consequences. \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhy do I care about the consequences? If I choose to go out and get fucked who am I disappointing? You? I guess there is still a part of me that is lost in the illusion that your thoughts are my thoughts, and itâ€™s becoming more and more apparent that I can no longer ignore it. Like the tennis shoes strung up and dangling from the power lines on Murray St., it just no longer fits the picture. My feeling is that these dreams are a physical response to people around me and my brain has just not caught up to what my heart threw away some time ago. Either that or my mind is swindling me into thinking Iâ€™m still in love in order to request the rush of endorphins it hasnâ€™t seen in so long. \r\n</P><P>\r\nNote: Submit indictment to brain for tyranny. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThere are a lot of things that confuse me right now and people who likewise make me question and hesitate. I have never been a person who acts before thinking and in staying true to form I will patiently wait for my cue. </P>'),(3648,'<P>Alright, Iâ€™ll give you something to write about. </P>\r\n<P>\r\nThere exists in me something that is truly wonderful and surprising. I rarely get the opportunity to use it, especially lately and there are few people on this planet who know anything about it. Itâ€™s my absolute happiness, absolute contentment, absolute sincerity, and my absolute true self. It may seem Iâ€™m blowing this out of proportion as Iâ€™m sure many people have this thing, but weâ€™re talking about me here. Itâ€™s my true generosity and understanding, my exposed intellectuality. No matter what events transpire or pull me away, this thing is my one way guaranteed ticket back to happiness. You see its hard to understand where the loop folds, cause Iâ€™ve only been around the returning end of this cycle a couple of times in my brief life, but I think I\\\'m now able to recognize the pattern.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe only variable in this equation is you. Iâ€™m not so sure you are interested in knowing me on this level. I am certain that you have this thing in you as well and I get excited thinking that I may eventually become very acquainted with it. Itâ€™s the way you notice things, the way you are able to isolate the things that are significant and how, with much precision, you are able to capture the honesty of what is around you, and you make me laugh. So in going against the spirit of so many things I wish I could do or say, but never have the balls, Iâ€™m to climb my ass out onto a very unstable limb...\r\n</P>'),(3649,'<P>Hi.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>New super login has been added.  The way it works is, when you login your IP address is stuck in with your username.  When you close your crappy Safari or Mozilla browser and your session is obliterated, you would in the past be shit out of luck when you come back and have to login all over again.  Now if you come back from the same location as before you will be automatically logged in assuming your IP address is the same as it was before.</P>\r\n<P>Where this will not work:</P>\r\n<P>It will not work if you login at home on your laptop and then take your laptop to work and open the browser and expect to be logged in automatically.  Your IP address changed and you have to log back in.  I didn\\\'t feel like coding in multiple location support.  Maybe some other time.</P>\r\n<P>What if I don\\\'t want to be logged in:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>If you don\\\'t want to be logged in, then logout.  Logging out will dump your IP address.  Next time you come  back from wherever, you will have to log in again because your IP will not be there to verify against.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>What if I don\\\'t want to play your games?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Tuff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>What if I don\\\'t want you to have my IP?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Don\\\'t give it to me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>What if I don\\\'t want to be logged in automatically?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Maybe later I\\\'ll make this an option but for now, you must submit.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Rock on Chicago</P>\r\n<P>Rock on London</P>\r\n<P>Pirate Radio Kicks Butt</P>'),(3650,'<P>\r\nI walked into the bookstore and left my cell phone number inside a copy of Cristina Garciaâ€™s \\\"Dreaming In Cuban\\\". If I wanted to be funny, I would of left it inside Nicholson Bakerâ€™s \\\"Vox\\\". This is how she wanted to meet.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Llorando\\\" from the soundtrack to \\\"Mulholland Drive\\\" was playing when the phone rang.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"Hi\\\" she said.<BR>\r\nHer voice was a replica of Hope Sandovalâ€™s. <BR>\r\n\\\"Hello\\\" I said.<BR>\r\n\\\"This is the part where we avoid having a Mia Wallace sort of silence\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"Indeed\\\"</P>\r\n<P>\r\nShe came over. Before me I saw excatly what I was waiting for. A complication. Too long I\\\'ve been living without turmoil, which does not leave room for a very productive anlge to my ambitions. The first thing she said to me was \\\"Am I fucking charming?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nShe woke me up the next morning:\r\n</P><P>\r\n- What do you do on Sundays?<BR>\r\n- I try to forget that my soul mate exists.<BR>\r\n- Maybe I\\\'ll come over next Sunday.\r\n</P><P>\r\n====================================================\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"It\\\'s hard to imagine existing without Lou Reed.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n<BR>\r\n\\\"I just love the way Lou Reed\\\'s concerts feel-expectant, uncertain. You never know what he\\\'s going to do next. Lou has about twenty-five personalities.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nCristina Garcia, \\\"Dreaming In Cuban\\\"\r\n</P>'),(3651,'<p>\r\n<b>\r\nfriday, may 28, 2004:\r\n</b>\r\nit was a dark and stormy night, and all hands were below deck.  well, no, not really.  actually...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni paid my last bill!\r\necstatic, and debt-free, i bought a dozen punk/hardcore discs from the late 70\\\'s and early 80\\\'s  - splurging on cd\\\'s for the first time in my life.\r\nthus emboldened, i started telling people to kiss my ass.\r\nthe effigies and black flag played along.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand then it happened: austin swinney and google.\r\nafter leaving my first comment for austin, i headed back to work - brashly crooning <i>devils whorehouse</i> along with the misfits\\\' glenn danzig.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnothing could touch me, until...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(1)\r\n</b>\r\ni walked into the alley, and found my car gone.\r\nmy heart beat faster and faster as i searched the neighborhood - to no avail.\r\ni peered through the windows of the company garage; but she wasn\\\'t hiding in there, either.\r\noh god, someone had stolen my cindy lou!\r\nfucking swinney: if he made things happen that quickly, he was totally mobbed up...maybe his real name was faustino swinnelli?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni broke out my cell and dialed chuck e. cheese, a.k.a., chucky, a.k.a., c.k., a.k.a., chakib - the garage manager.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"chakib!  my car is missing!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"easy, paul.  did you check the neighborhood, and the garage?  maybe derrell did some work and...\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"yes, i\\\'ve been looking for 30 minutes.  the car isn\\\'t here.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"shit.  ok.  i\\\'m out driving #3027.  i\\\'ll be right there...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni paced the ground in front of the darkened office.  the gravel betrayed his approach.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"paul, my brother, what\\\'s up?  don\\\'t be angry.  there\\\'s a new driver here.  maybe he had a problem.\r\ntake 3027 tonight, tomorrow everything will be fine - i promise...\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"a new driver?  maybe he had an accident, or got killed.  i feel bad, now.  ok, till tomorrow, no worries.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"tonight, paul, we\\\'ll look for that fucker.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut we found nothing  in the city...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n[car #3027 had 297,000 miles on her; she did not handle like a ferrari...]\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next night i walked into the alley, and found my car gone.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"c.k. it\\\'s paul.  i\\\'m calling you from 3027.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"3027 - why?\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"MY CAR IS STILL MISSING!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"you sound pissed.  what do you want to do?\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"i\\\'m going to belmont and western to file a police report.  someone is doing something in that car, and it isn\\\'t me.  if my car is involved in a crime, the detectives will gank ME from my nest.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"ok dude, i\\\'ll meet you there.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe police refused to take my report.  i was criminally liable for the car - but they insisted that it was a civil case.\r\nc.k. showed up.  the two of us badgered them.  and then they took the report.  i was covered.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe next night i walked into the alley: my car was there - and so was chuck e. cheese.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"paul, dude, ummm...the new guy was the one who took your car...\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"HE WHAT!?!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"ummm...yeah...he wants to talk to you...\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"fuck him.  he is a liar and a thief.  i need to be compensated financially.  actions speak louder than words.\\\"\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"he says that he\\\'s not going to pay...\\\"  \r\n<br>\r\n\\\"he is going to pay me, or i am going to take the money from him.  it\\\'s that easy.  \r\nand i\\\'d be a bitch to sign another lease with you - until he is gone, and i have my money.  \r\ni am not going to roll over on my stomach for either of you.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand - shock - my demands were met: they gave me $100 for each day the car was missing, and the \\\"new guy\\\" was gone.\r\ncurse?  what curse?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(2)\r\n</b>\r\neverything seemed right - until i checked the car.\r\nlike a cow abducted by aliens - the husk of which was returned to a farmer\\\'s field - \r\nmy cindy lou came back with no fluid in her!\r\nfrom washer wiper resevoir to motor oil pan, not a drop of liquid remained.\r\nthe dark forces were still swirling...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(3)\r\n</b>\r\nkeys get tucked in your belt; that\\\'s what i was taught.\r\nthe first day that i worked in the jail a sergeant grabbed my keys and stuffed them in my pants - behind my belt.\r\nthe same thing happened on the second day.\r\nand then on the third day i decided that i didn\\\'t want the sergeant\\\'s hands in my pants anymore, so i put the keys there, myself.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe night after cindy lou was rehydrated, i drove a routine shift - until i felt it time to lose my own fluids.\r\ni parked my taxi, and i tucked my keys behind my belt; old habits die hard.\r\nthen i walked to the 24-hour bathroom, a.k.a., the 24-hour starbuck\\\'s.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni stood in line, waiting for my turn.\r\nupon gaining entry i faced the toilet, and then: JINGLE-KLING-KER-BLOOP!\r\nmy keys fell straight into the porcelin hole - \r\nthe place the hookers, addicts, homeless, mentally ill, and garden variety drunks pumped their bilge.\r\nmy keys were in the TOILET.\r\nno god, no.\r\ni am not here, and this is not happening: the thing that had to be retrieved, from the place that i could not stand to retrieve it.\r\nTHE CURSE!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe other people in line knocked on the door.\r\ni had no time.\r\ninto the water dove my truest friend in the world: mr. stabby, the screwdriver.\r\nout flipped the keys - into the sink.\r\nthe horror...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(4)\r\n</b>\r\nthe night after i recovered the keys from the toilet seemed normal enough - until i parked the car at the end of my shift.\r\nit was then that i discovered she had a flat tire...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"fuck this.  fuck the taxi,\\\" i thought, \\\"i am the only person who maintains or cleans this thing.  i baby her, and i am screwed - continually.  someone else can change this fucking tire...it\\\'s after 5 am, anyway.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(5)\r\n</b>\r\nat 6 am my cat greeted me at the door - not in a loving way, but, rather, demanding to be groomed.\r\nif i don\\\'t comb her before i leave for work, and immediately upon my return, her personality goes to hell.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"listen baby,\\\" i told her, \\\"daddy has no loving left in him, tonight.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand strangely - as though possessed - she walked away.\r\ni sat at my desk with a big glass of wine, and put my head down.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\na loud noise woke me: \\\"BZZZUP!\\\"\r\ni looked to my left and saw a brilliant orange flash illuminating the interior of the room beside me.\r\n\\\"hey! what the fuck!\\\" i yelled as i ran in at top speed - half expecting to see a martian firing a ray gun.\r\nwhat met me instead was the cat running at top speed - in the opposite direction.\r\n<i>\r\nsomething was burning, and the power was out.\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy cat had decided to punish me by peeing on the wall, as evidenced by the wet stain that ran - 12 inches off the floor - into the ELECTRICAL OUTLET.\r\ni did what i could with the melted plastic and steel, and went to bed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>\r\n(6)\r\n</b>\r\nthe next night i woke, and turned up the buzzcocks\\\' <i>something\\\'s gone wrong again</i>.\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"you are *this* close to becoming a violin,\\\" i warned the old tabby cat, as i left for work.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni walked into the alley and found the car sitting with her flat tire still unchanged.\r\ni looked at the trip meter: she had been driven 120 miles in that condition.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nonce upon a time i had tried not to care.\r\ni had tried to beat the other drivers at the game of filth: not cleaning the car, imagining that at some point they would be forced to do something.\r\nbut as objects both enigmatic and ghastly accumulated, i was always the first to break - tossing away the half-gnawed bones, hair-filled combs, and other detrious.\r\nand so it was with the maintenence: a flat tire?  \r\n<br>\r\n\\\"OHARE! OHARE!\\\"\r\n<br>\r\ni was only fooling myself