at the fuck was she on about?  \\\"We\\\'ll make a story about what we would do tonight.\\\"  Damn me, but I couldn\\\'t say no.  The idea reminded me of the bit in _Jaws_ (the book) where wossname and wosshername get together and talk their way through a fantasy.  That appealed to me.  Maybe because I like to write, and I fancy that someday should I not suicide, I just might publish something worth reading.  Maybe I liked the idea because it was perfect fiction, the sort of thing that your friends will figure to be a lie when you talk about it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She said, \\\"You\\\'d have to drive -\\\" and when she saw the question on my face, she hastily added, \\\"Well there\\\'s enough space in your van for us to fuck if we couldn\\\'t make it to my place.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Mmmmm,\\\" I thought to myself.  To her I just nodded my head and grinned a little.  Fuck it I thought, in a year I\\\'ll probably be dead...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"I\\\'d have you hike up that skirt of yours as we drove, it\\\'d show off your legs, and that\\\'s good.  Better - for me anyway - I\\\'d have you play with yourself - get good and wet.  The van would smell like sex, coppery, exciting.\\\"  She blinked, but I wasn\\\'t done.  \\\"You play, and periodically, have me suck on your fingers, tease me a bit with your taste.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Ohhhh, you\\\'d like that?\\\" she asked me softly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Fuck yeah - psyche me up for when we got to your place - for the part where I can get down and taste you proper - until you\\\'re begging me to stop.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She reached underneath the table then, squeazed my left leg.  That made me angry.  \\\"Don\\\'t touch me,\\\" I said.  She withdrew her hand.  I don\\\'t know, but I swear she looked disappointed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I realized then that it was late, and I was into too deep for the night.  I got up and she followed.  We paid the check, and walked to her car.  I said goodnight, she reached out as if to hug me maybe, or kiss my cheek or something.  I just told her I\\\'d call soon and walked away.  Why should her I give her anything of myself, anything she could offer me would just be a fucking lie.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m too drunk to try and write about how tonight made me feel, I\\\'m amazed I got through the important parts.  I\\\'ve got my brand new bottle of KY-Ultra Gel.  I\\\'m going to kill ten-thousand kittens tonight.  Then I\\\'m going to pass out.  What a life.</p>'),(3654,'<p style=\\\"font-family: georgia, verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; color: #333;\\\">austin swinney kicked me off this website many months ago.  it might have even been years ago, it seems that long.  but here i am, submitting an article.  rebellion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: georgia, verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; color: #333;\\\">why would i rebel against austin swinney?  could it be that i wish to spite him for moving away, even though i barely saw him while he lived here in san francisco (my fault, i know) or could it be instead that i just have a rebellious streak?</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: georgia, verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; color: #333;\\\">the reason i am submitting this article for consideration by the swinney.org moderator teamâ„¢ is because basically i miss swinney.org.  i remember feverishly reloading wishing to read the latest tidbit from someone i knew or someone i\\\'d never heard of.  swinney.org was a revolution of intimate bloggery and the experience of it has never been duplicated in my life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\\\"font-family: georgia, verdana, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; color: #333;\\\">swinney, i\\\'m sorry i called someone here boring or stupid.  can i come back now?</p>'),(3655,'<P>I am so tired it seems like my mind is trying to distract me from the world and trick me into sleeping. I find myself closing my eyes in class and the professorâ€™s voice becomes entangled in my random thoughts and persistent dream sequences that blind the inside of my eyes. Like a raving addict, I find myself searching my daily routine for every possible break that could lend to a few minutes of shut-eye. I feel totally gross cause I slept through my alarm and had no time to do anything but throw on some fresh skivvies. Even the make-up on my face is day old. (I am that which should be bagged and sold for a discounted price.) They say that you shouldnâ€™t wash your hair every day because it strips your hair of essential oils. Well, I guess theyâ€™ve never seen my head neglected of much needed 2in1. The world lends itself to my hazy drowning fatigue and I can hardly wait until Iâ€™m on the subway sleeping.</P>'),(3656,'<p>my job is sort of a mystery to people.  i get alot of \\\'wait, what the fuck do you do again?\\\' and \\\'what the hell kind of job is <i>that?!</i>\\\'  when you get to the core of things it doesn\\\'t become too much simpler.  i am not a cop (thank the fucking gods), i am not a private investigator (although sometime next year i will be, at least i\\\'ll have the license), and i\\\'m not a security guard/officer/whatever.  i\\\'m a little like all these things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Like a cop, i spend lots of time a) arresting people and b) doing paperwork.  Like an inspector/detective i do interrogate or interview people (the difference is hard to define sometimes, but believe me, there is one).  Like a PI i investigate people, i surveill them, and i use the results to help someone (usually my boss). like a security something something, i get no respect, i am poorly paid (although i\\\'ve had what, four raises this year so far...), i am mostly unarmed, i have (usually) no backup, and my authority is the same as yours (unless you\\\'re a felon).  i work in plainclothes.  most of the time i look like i\\\'m homeless.  i was born ugly, and i make myself even uglier to do my job.  i don\\\'t have to, i just like having an excuse, after the fact, for being so ugly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i flirt with girls at work, which is funny, since i am incapable of doing so outside of work.  i guess it\\\'s easier to do while i\\\'m working since it helps me get information out of them.  that pretty little thing i was talking to last week?  this week she\\\'s being arrested and terminated for theft.  don\\\'t get me wrong, i like alot of the people at work.  some of them are just plain cool.  others come from weird and wonderful places all over the planet.  one owns a Thai restaraunt here in \\\'frisco.  another cooks the best Indian food i\\\'ve ever tasted.  i\\\'ve got work-friends from England, Nigeria, Russia, Iraq, the Phillipines, South Africa, Egypt, Syria, Italy... shit, all over the fuckin\\\' place.  and, as much as my job permits, i adore them.  i love their accents and skin tones, i love to listen to their native language(s), and i really love hearing about their homelands.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>some of the people i\\\'ve fired, or helped to fire...i wanted to cry when it went down.  i didn\\\'t want to believe they were thieves.  and i didn\\\'t want to have them gone from my life.  i find it perverse that my boss tells me i need to learn to be less outgoing.  since outside of work, i\\\'ve made only two \\\'friends\\\' in California since I moved here.  people don\\\'t like me, generally, and generally, I don\\\'t like people.  understand, i want to like them.  i just can\\\'t.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>here\\\'s the part where i tell you that i can\\\'t like people - and that i don\\\'t know why.  but i\\\'m not going to lie to you.  i have trouble with people outside of professional life because i\\\'m a contrary little asshole.  because i\\\'m opinionated, over aggressive, depressed, and because i haven\\\'t been laid (or even kissed) in nearly five years.  it makes me cranky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at work i\\\'m like a deeply flawed superhero.  i show up somewhere, and people are pleased to see me.  i\\\'m going to help them.  protect them.  they never realize that sometimes i\\\'m there to wreck their lives.  nor do they see how sometimes i\\\'ve been looking forward to seeing them for a month.  there\\\'s this girl down in San Jose.  she\\\'s not pretty, but she\\\'s beautiful.  and she speaks five languages.  she\\\'s funny, and has a cruel sharp wit.  i look forward to working near her, because she entertains me, and according to her, i help \\\'stave off the boredom\\\'. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>so by now you\\\'ve all figured out that i only write stuff that\\\'s basically a long winded complaint, right?  and you\\\'re wondering, \\\'when the heck is dave going to start bitching?\\\'  no worries friends, i won\\\'t disappoint.  in fact, if you knew me better, or if you\\\'re as smart as i like to believe, you\\\'d notice i really already have been whining.  hey, i\\\'m good at it.  like masturbation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>picture a place where all people, no matter their color or class, seem to be trash.  white trash, black trash, etc. etc.  hell, yes?  no!  this place exists.  i call it San Jose.  i hate San Jose.  my interaction with people in that fucking city seems to always be negative.  (obviously i\\\'m leaving out my co-workers here.  and also all the cases that disprove my mean spirited generalization.)  i work poor areas of course, so my view is warped.  but seriously now, <i>please stop breeding and drinking alcohol right fucking now, oh you trashy trashy San Joseans!</i>i mean shit, there\\\'s not even any Tang around, although there are two liters of Jarritos guava...so maybe i should relax a little.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what\\\'s the point, right?  i know, i\\\'m rambling.  i\\\'m drunk again, wheeeeee, ten night straight.  all part of my incredibly smart plan to cut all my friends out of my life and become an alcoholic.  but, the point is this:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i may be a really terrible human being.  sometimes i feel as if i\\\'m not human at all.  but in my job, i try to be a decent person.  i don\\\'t mock or insult the people i arrest; that is as long as they are being cool with me.  i\\\'m not perfect!  i don\\\'t claim any authority i don\\\'t have.  i don\\\'t beat people to a pulp unless a) i need to in order to protect myself and b) they give me no other choice.  i even had one guy thank me for giving him first-aid and bringing him water - and that was after he tried to stab me with a knife - and i had an total adrenaline dump and pounded the hell out of him until he was unconscious.  i\\\'m proud that unlike so many people, i\\\'m able to go from peaceful to violent and back to peaceful easily, as the situation warrants.  i don\\\'t hurt someone once they stop trying to hurt me.  anyway...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i try to be nice, and understanding, when the cops show up.  i know they\\\'re job is hard.  i know they don\\\'t wanna deal with the $1.95 theft i\\\'ve called them over.  but i\\\'ve got duties, and policies to follow, and liability to prevent.  so while technically, they can just fucking suck it - i try to be helpful and polite, and very understanding of their own frustrations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>today the new super hot Russian chick told me, \\\'how the hell did you put up with that?  i would have slapped him.\\\'  she wasn\\\'t referring to the guy i\\\'d arrested.  no, she was talking about the cop who answered my call.  the guy was rude, insulting, and ultimately went so far as to run me against the database - in front of the suspect!  just to spite me.  through it all, i just smiled and tried to be polite.  tried to reconcile our differing views.  finally, i gave up and stopped smiling.  i just looked him in the eye and said, \\\'i need to speak with your sergeant.\\\'</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i didn\\\'t win, but i didn\\\'t lose.  the sergeant basically told me to suck it up.  he backed his boy to the hilt.  but he also made it clear that i would NOT face such treatment again.  *shrug*  the whole time though... before the sergeant showed... i was thinking about things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the officer who was being a prick, you know, he didn\\\'t even have an expert pistol badge.  so i ran our respective skills down in my head.  i thought to myself, \\\'i am a better shot than you, with rifle and pistol; i know more about explosives and chem-bio weapons; i\\\'m a better interrogator than you\\\'ll ever be; i probably have far more hand to hand combat training and experience than you; i\\\'m better with a sword, a knife, or an impact weapon; and if i\\\'m willing to end my life here and now asshole, i can kill you before you even get out a call for help.\\\'</p>\r\n\r\n<p>knowing these things, i felt better.  it\\\'s how i could keep smiling as my pride took such an unbelieveable beating.  now look, i realize none of this says anything good about me.  i don\\\'t care.  what matters is the result.  to those who aren\\\'t privy to my thoughts, i looked like the worlds most reasonable man.  surely that counts for something?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>someone high in my department, a woman i really respect and enjoy speaking with, promised to get me a hooker when we go to Las Vegas for the big meeting.  she\\\'d better pay up, i have a cool book of hers as hostage.  oh, damn.  she has one of mine.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m too drunk to remember exactly what i wanted to say.  but uhhh...if anyone is interested in seeing my book collection, feel free to head over to whack.org/~incitor.  warning, it\\\'s a gargantuan page, because i haven\\\'t broken it up by genre or anything yet.</p>'),(3657,'<P>I once sold my soul to a friend. We were in highschool and our morality was guided by your average adolescent apathy, too much <I>Kids in the Hall</I>, and cigarettes.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>At first it began as a contest, to see who could collect the most souls. I, being the typical non-competitive person I am, fell out of the race early, handing him my soul on a piece of paper as stepped out.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Recently, Ive stayed up nights wondering what weight my soul would garner. I talk to god these nights and my dead relatives. Im trying to gain some points in my favor.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>See, Ive come to a point in my life where the cigarettes, the apathy and all the <I>Kids in the Hall</I> episodes just dont matter. I guess Im growing up. Im also wishing that whatever they found in my mother\\\'s breast would go away-and my worst fears remain just that...fears. </P>\r\n'),(3659,'<p>This time I intend to be less silly<br>\r\nabout it and what it all means.  I will <br>\r\nrecognize it as the gift and torment <br>\r\nit always is, and I will not get <br>\r\ncaught up in the romantic idea that<br>\r\nI can not live without it.  I will try<br>\r\nto remember that romance must<br>\r\nbe an organic thing, grown in silence<br>\r\nand proper soil, with a fortuitous<br>\r\nblend of light and food.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Two morning doves have nested<br>\r\nin a pot on the fire escape that<br>\r\nused to hold a lemon verbena <br>\r\nplant.  The soil and dried leaves<br>\r\nbeneath the birds, the twigs with<br>\r\nwhich they\\\'ve made a bed, still<br>\r\nsmell of tea and soap. Most days<br>\r\none dove hunkers there, warming<br>\r\ntwo white eggs, her eyes open<br>\r\nto the dangers of my watering can.<br>\r\nThis morning he was back, nestled<br>\r\nbeside her facing west while she<br>\r\nlooked east, neither of them <br>\r\nsure why they do this, or how <br>\r\nlong it will last, but both confident<br>\r\nthat something good will come of it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I believe in it insomuch as<br>\r\na jaded faith is possible.  I will<br>\r\nunderstand it as something<br>\r\nobserved and not made, a surprise<br>\r\nthat lies in the unspoken, the intuited,<br>\r\nthe imperfect moments when neither<br>\r\nof us knows what to say.  I will <br>\r\ncovet it, but not too tightly,<br>\r\nreeling it in and letting it unspool<br>\r\nlike a kite string, staring in<br>\r\nfar off wonder at the flying<br>\r\nkite, tethered but ready to<br>\r\ndisappear should the wind get too<br>\r\nstrong, or should my hands get<br>\r\ntoo tired.  I will accept it the<br>\r\nway Margaret Fuller accepted<br>\r\nthe universe, as the flawed and<br>\r\nlovely sum of unknowable parts,<br>\r\nas the earnest will drawing two<br>\r\nsouls close, each mostly strange<br>\r\nto the other, but dear.</p>'),(3660,'<P>\r\n..Looking for his blue eyes, and his blue jeans, I had a dream, a memory, and I didn\\\'t know him, but in my dream they accused him of being wrong, and I didn\\\'t know who he was, but I knew..\r\n</P><P>\r\nHe was taller than me and looked down on me with Extreme Love and he had on a blue shirt to go with his blue jeans and his brown jacket.. And I felt close to his chest to see he was Strong and rested my head on his chest.\r\n</P><P>\r\nand even though they accused him of being wrong, for a split second, I was outside of the dream, because then he said \\\"I. LOVE. YOU.\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nand I knew I would pursue it, I knew I would pursue him. \r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd I think it quite odd the adventures that led up to this night, rinsing brushes, playing in paint, drawing symbols of the everlasting spiral, drawing down into itself. to find what i was looking for. using that one everlasting color (blue) just to seek him and find him, looking for his eyes. Which at times are larger than his head itself. it was like he stepped out of the dream just to tell me himself. And I\\\'ve never felt anything like it before. there\\\'s no reason for anyone to be taken for granted. and he had silver cuffs behind his back as he climbed into his jeep- back in the dream. And it is time for you to Live, and be Art, and BE a poem, here I am strategically written on everyline, folded up, burned, recycled, the phoenix on every. single. line. Of ALL the things in the universe, you\\\'ve got to accept that there Are certain things which Only I can explain..i found you all through a dream. Perhaps I Am dying. I used to write poems and call them songs all the time. Paintbrush Child. Painbrush Color Child. Child who expresses himself in one shade of color: green. I imagine the soft golden yellow stars and the swifty loops. There is a reason for you Being Today, and there is a reason for the is. Our love is the soft summer leaves, which are now golden.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd all your love is through me, again, perhaps I make it possible... and within that split moment of reality between dream and undream, you were Real. Real to me. And I\\\'ll never forget that, and I will love you. and all your love is on my dressers to clear up my room..with the sweet mini rainbows, without all the colors but only rasta, never be afraid, for the Color is Sweet!\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnd the music IS inside you, and you will feel it today, I can feel it today,\r\nand you no longer must pass out or sell your sweet music heart to people any longer\r\nFor the spirit is with you and loves you very much, more than anything\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nbright glorious, starChild of Passion\r\n</P>'),(3661,'<P>Dear Friend, \r\n<P>It has been years since you have contained the least insinuation of talent. I know you too well; \\\"I see your tormented color, the steep form of your storm.\\\" Forget about a Winterson romance, deliver yourself from this Kafka nightmare, and find a cure for the Dostoevsky sickness. I can\\\'t help you; I\\\'m not an alchemist of valentines. Don\\\'t be a victim to admiration, stop blaming inaction on some weird shade of shy. When in panic you want to return to her and the way her eyes overlooked you constantly in the dark. Don\\\'t even dream about it. Wake up. </p>\r\n \r\n<P>Dear xxxxxxy,  \r\n<P>I felt sick at first but I was actually waking up from numbness. It was your existence, it makes my guts hurt ( and I need that, I suppose.) I\\\'ll stop painting a new coat on the rancor in my heart when you stop lying your heart out. Only then will we recall our past and sing it like a karaoke song. Iâ€™m your most important footnote, that song you keep listening to over and over. And the ugly irony is that we miss our insanity, it was pure blissful tension. this is how I came to realize that I need you, in some shape or form</P>\r\n \r\n<P>Dear E.,   \r\n<P>I\\\'m I writing you or one of your past personalities? I doesn\\\'t matter now. Around this time of the year I start thinking about you and that summer. You waiting around for me, in your shorts with holes through them. I still picture you jumping into the pool from your roof.</P>  \r\n \r\n<P>When I had you in my room we should of acted out that dream about the the church of  cosntant living through touch. Instead I was worried about you. I sure needed to relax back them. I was way too responsible. I think you were amused by my intensity and silliness.</P> \r\n \r\n<P>The last time I wrote you I was destroyed, damaged and diffused. I\\\'m sure you just assumed I would get better. I did of course.</P> \r\n \r\n<P> You\\\'ve been gone so long I can\\\'t ask you to tell me about me, or to help define me. I look foward to seeing you again. Just like the last time you will ask â€œHow goes your obsession?â€ but this time I will reply â€œWhich one?â€ </P>\r\n '),(3662,'<P>\r\nImagining that I have a japanese grandmother who raised me in the trailors of the deep south, I concocted what was sure to be a new hit on the white trash food circuit:  Mac & Chee & Edamame.\r\n</P><P>\r\nIn preparation, I purchased frozen edamame from <B>Whole Foods</B>, and <B>Annie\\\'s Shells and Real Aged Wisconsin Cheddar</B>.\r\n</P>\r\n<center><img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"></center>\r\n<P>\r\n<B>I considered the order of operations.</B>\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe mac\\\' takes 4 minutes longer then the \\\'mame.  I thought about adding the \\\'mame in with the mac\\\' while it was boiling, but considered that the temperature change caused by the frozen \\\'mame being introduced into the boiling mac\\\' would throw the mac\\\'s cooking time off resulting in overcooked mac\\\'.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAfter dumping the mac\\\' in the strainer, I start melting butter and heating half-and-half in the mac\\\' pot.  When it is warmed, I add the mac\\\'.  Then add the chee packet that came with the mac\\\'.  When that is blended into a slurry of mac\\\'n\\\'chee, I add the \\\'mame.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nFor kick, some crushed pepper is recommended.  For formal presentation, add some shredded white cheddar on top, then a pinch of pepper.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe overall flavor ended up being a sort of cheese omelet with pepper.  The mac\\\'n\\\'chee is what it is.  Everyone should be familiar with that flavor.  The added edamame brings the eggs.  Edamame tastes like eggs.  That was a suprise to me.  I has noticed the fatty flavor before, but with the mac\\\', it really comes together as egg.\r\n</P>\r\n<center><img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\"></center>\r\n\r\n<P>\r\nWell, that is it for this week.  Tune in next week for ... oh, not gonna tell you!  Tops Secret!\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3663,'<p>                                                                             \r\nOne overcast spring morning in Moscow, Idaho, I was headed towards              \r\none of my undergraduate classes, and I saw one of the saddest things            \r\nI\\\'ve ever seen.  I was walking along the sidewalk towards the asphalt           \r\npath that runs between Paradise Creek and West Park Elementary, a           \r\ngradeschool positioned right next to the university.                            \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nA chinese mother dropped off her little girl at the beginning of this           \r\npath.  I know they were chinese because the beautiful embroidered               \r\nsilk patterns on the girl\\\'s white jacket and blue jeans, had a                  \r\ndistinctively chinese sensibility about them.  The little girl                  \r\nprotested to her mother, lost the argument, and then resignedly began           \r\nto trudge toward the elementary school.  I couldn\\\'t understand a word,          \r\nbut from the gestures I understood completely.  She was arguing about           \r\nher beautiful clothes.  She hated them.                                         \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nAnd as a couple classmates of hers ran by ignoring her, suddenly I              \r\nunderstood why.  And it broke my heart.                                         \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThis beautiful little girl simply wanted to fit in.  That she was               \r\ndressed more beautifully than anyone, including adults, that I had              \r\nseen in months didn\\\'t matter to her at all.  What did matter to her             \r\nwas that her clothing was just another wedge between her and the rest           \r\nof the children at school.  It was already hard enough being a little           \r\nchinese girl in Idaho, and she didn\\\'t want to stand out any more than           \r\nshe already did.                                                                \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nHer parents, being students just scraping by, probably couldn\\\'t afford          \r\nto buy her some regular blue jeans and tee-shirts, even if they understood her               \r\nproblem.  And worse, they were probably peeved that she didn\\\'t                  \r\nappreciate the truly beautiful wardrobe that they had likely hand-sewn          \r\nfor her.                                                                        \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nBut the tragedy for me went beyond that of this particular little               \r\ngirl.  The particulars, though extraordinarily tragically beautiful             \r\nfor me at that moment, were not the source of the deep sadness I felt.          \r\nIt was the more general situation that they described, that really got           \r\nto me.  </p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nThat she lacked the ability to see her own beauty, because she was              \r\nrelying on the opinions of others.  That these others were ignorant,            \r\nand insensitive (and likely more than a little jealous).  And yet,              \r\nthat theirs was the only opinion that truly mattered.  That to please           \r\nthem, she would have gladly cast aside something beautiful, something           \r\nthat made her special.  And that she really would have been happier             \r\nfor having done so.                                                             \r\n</p>                                                                            \r\n<p>                                                                             \r\nMy heart now heavy, I made my own way toward class, wondering just how          \r\nmuch of my own beauty I\\\'d thrown away over the years.                           \r\n</p>'),(3664,'<b>Sour Grapes:</b><br>\r\nI dozed off on the train on the way to work this morning. This is not hard to do, especially when you\\\'re really tired and it\\\'s so warm and quiet and rocking back and forth up in there. This morning, I had a very brief dream whilst on the express train (easier to fall asleep on- less stops to jostle you awake) that went like this:<br><Br>\r\n\r\nI was the contestant on a television game-show entitled <i>\\\"Sour Grapes [We Got A Winner!]\\\"</i>. The set was very similar to <i>The Price is Right</i>. It had a theme song that went something to the effect of:<br><br>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nWhat if they brought you some \\\'gators?<br>\r\nWhat if they sewed you new pants?<br>\r\nWhat if they cooked you po\\\'taters?<br>\r\nWhat if they flew you to france!<br>\r\n</blockquote><br><br>\r\n\r\n...and had women dressed in charcoal gray Armani suits with matching monochrome shirts and ties, wearing pink sunglasses and boots, one who entered walking two alligators on a rhinestone leash, one carrying a bright fluorescent orange pair of cargo pants that were like 10 feet long, one with a steaming pot of Dutch potatoes, sprinkling herbs into the pot, and the last carrying a giant cartoon poster of the Eiffel tower with a happyface and beret on it. One also had a poster with the show\\\'s logo on it, which was apparently a big clump of pink and gray grapes, all of which had big frowning cartoon faces on them. These faces, however, would start to animate and smile and sing when you answered a question correctly... and would chant <i>\\\"We got a winner!!!\\\"</i> in a shrill lil hamster voice.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nThe first qustion they asked me is <i>\\\"what does Snoop sound like?\\\"</i>. I had a cup of ice on me in the dream, and I got a big mouthfull of ice and crunched it up with my teeth really loudly into the mic and said <i>\\\"*thats* what Snoop sounds like... just chillin, you know?\\\"</i> and I was rewarded for my answer by one of the women starting to feed me the potatoes. I forgot the next couple of questions (which were similarly random), but I lost the pants and the gators... I pulled into my stop and woke up before the trip to France was up for grabs, unfortunately...<br><Br>\r\n\r\n<b>The Levitator:</b><br>\r\nI was the only person in the lobby when I walked in to work today. The elevator arrived, just for me, it seemed. Which is nice, as I work on the 12th floor, and inevitablty spend several hours a month on the elevator, waiting patiently for all the doofuses that get on to ride it to the second floor where the hold the doors and talk to their cloying secretary friends and load Xerox paper onto the elevator, etc. This morning, I get on, and the doors begin to shut. Immediately, an arm intersects the closing doors, opening them again, and on steps a guy. He smiles at me, sheepishly, sort of apologetic for impeding my solo ride, most likely empathetic, cause everyone hates the <i>\\\'elevator bandwagon jumper\\\'</i>. I consider flicking him on the nose for delaying my now un-solo elevator ride. Of course, he presses \\\"2\\\", cause that\\\'s where the admin offices are, and that\\\'s where all the lazy doofuses get off with their styrofoam coffee cups and briefcases and tacky ties and executive decision makers. Every freaking day. The doors start closing again. Another arm, they open. On steps <i>another</i> guy and he presses \\\"3\\\". This cycle repeats SEVEN times, all told, until the elevator is full, and I\\\'ve stood there on the freaking elevator for about 5 minutes while 7 other people have opened/shut/opened the doors, gotten on, flashed a HR-department smile and pressed either \\\"2\\\" or \\\"3\\\". Seven people. They all got off on 2 or three. You\\\'d thnk they\\\'d never heard of the stairs. <br><Br>\r\n\r\n<b>The Login:</b><br>\r\nI just typoed my login at work as \\\"teh twin sdico wokrftoce\\\", followed by three similarly munged variations of that, until the machine decided to punish me and lockup for putting in the wrong l/p three times in a row. Now, I have to remember a 16 digit numerical code I haven\\\'t used since January 5th to get into the system. Remembering this is gonna be fun. I asked my cow-orker to gimme his code, and he leaned over my shoulder and read aloud: <i>\\\"teh twin sdico wokrftoce! hahahaha!\\\"</i> and laughed at me. I\\\'ve seen him take the elevator th the 2nd floor before too. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>The Plastic Pencil:</b><br>\r\nSo my cow-orker just got in, and I asked him to log me in so I could reset the shizza. Before replying, he holds up a pencil and says <i>\\\"hey look, this pencil is made of plastic!\\\"</i> and shakes it rapidly in front of my face. This perplexes me. You know those cheap pencils that are made out of that odd wood/resin/plastic conglomerate? Its one of those, and he\\\'s bending it and shaking it, staring at it, smiling and giggling and staring at me. This strikes me as something that would seem rather odd even if I weren\\\'t sleep-deprived.<br><br>\r\n<blockquote>\r\nWhat if they did you some favors?<br>\r\nWhat if they cooked you some clams?<br>\r\nWhat if you rode elevators?<br>\r\nWhat if you sang in a band!<br><br></blockquote>\r\nI really need to get some sleep.'),(3665,'<p>They say it corrupts, and it can\\\'t <br>\r\nbuy you love, but certainly we all<br>\r\nagree that it isn\\\'t all bad.  I give<br>\r\nsome (earned using words, created<br>\r\nfrom nothing) to the video store clerk, <br>\r\nwho spends it on macaroni and gin<br>\r\nat the corner grocer. The grocer, <br>\r\nafter counting that day\\\'s till, buys<br>\r\nrims for his truck, and pays the propane<br>\r\nlady.  The rim man, who is down on<br>\r\nhis luck (his lady left him, and he<br>\r\nwas never his mother\\\'s favorite), <br>\r\nbuys cigarettes, juice, and a box<br>\r\nof cookies at the same grocer, <br>\r\nand an ounce of weed, which <br>\r\nshould last him through the<br>\r\nmonth.  The weed dealer, who <br>\r\nhordes cash in his basement and <br>\r\ngrows his own food, only participates <br>\r\nin the economy when a new video<br>\r\ngame comes out.  The propane lady, <br>\r\nwho makes her living delivering vapors <br>\r\ndoor to door, has a father in a nursing <br>\r\nhome, and a son whoâ€™s too shiftless <br>\r\nto do well in school.  She buys a cake <br>\r\nfor his birthday, yellow with chocolate <br>\r\nicing like he likes, some razors for her<br>\r\nlegs, and pays the rent on the condo<br>\r\nwith the rest of her paycheck and <br>\r\nthen some.  Her landlady, who wears<br>\r\ntoo much gold and not enough<br>\r\ndeodorant, buys herself a new TV,<br>\r\nbecause the old one broke, got all<br>\r\nstaticky and colorblind, and<br>\r\nshe doesn\\\'t have much to do since<br>\r\nher husband died last year.  The<br>\r\nsalesman at the TV store is psyched<br>\r\nbecause it\\\'s his first job and so<br>\r\nfar, between the cell phones and the<br>\r\nair conditioners (it is July), his<br>\r\ncommissions have been pretty good.<br>\r\nOn Friday he buys three rounds of<br>\r\ndrinks for his friends at the bar,<br>\r\nand later goes music shopping,<br>\r\nbecause his new stereo is begging<br>\r\nfor fresh tunes.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The salesman pays his taxes, which<br>\r\nare routinely swept from his check<br>\r\nbefore he receives it, and these <br>\r\nabstract dollars fly imperceptibly<br>\r\nacross the continent as shapeless <br>\r\nbits of data, a surge over wires,<br>\r\nbounced once, twice from a satellite<br>\r\nand added to a pool that is both<br>\r\nmetaphysical and occasionally seen,<br>\r\nunderstood mostly in rows of<br>\r\ndigits on screens and the occasional<br>\r\nbound report delivered to the Senate<br>\r\nBudget Committee.  His cents are <br>\r\ndivided many times, fractions <br>\r\nexploded like confetti<br>\r\ninto myriad federal programs.  A<br>\r\nwoman named Maura who <br>\r\ndigitizes books for the Library of <br>\r\nCongress is paid her weekly <br>\r\nwage.  Paperclips are bought at<br>\r\nthe Department of Health and<br>\r\nHuman Services to help sort the<br>\r\nmany pages that account for<br>\r\neach human served.  The chef<br>\r\nat the White House orders wine<br>\r\nfrom California for a state <br>\r\ndinner, and three one-thousandths<br>\r\nof a cent go to help rebuild a<br>\r\nschool outside Omaha, destroyed<br>\r\nlast week in a tornado.  Two dollars<br>\r\ngo toward the purchase of a<br>\r\njet that flies below radar and<br>\r\nis capable of delivering bombs<br>\r\nthe size of tool sheds.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Several cents make it back to him<br>\r\nindirectly, some months later.<br>\r\nA farm subsidy provides him<br>\r\nwith corn on the cob, three<br>\r\nfor a dollar.  The old woman<br>\r\nin the apartment upstairs gets<br>\r\nher disability check in the mail<br>\r\nand bakes a batch of cookies, <br>\r\nof which she gives him a dozen<br>\r\nto share with his new girlfriend<br>\r\nwho reminds her of her sister,<br>\r\nnow dead, when she was that age.  <br>\r\nAnd the remainder of his uncle\\\'s<br>\r\ngovernment pension, from forty-<br>\r\nthree years as a mailman, is<br>\r\nwilled to him, and with it he<br>\r\ntakes the girl on a trip to the<br>\r\nSouth Pacific, and puts a<br>\r\ndown payment on a house<br>\r\nwhere he will spend the next<br>\r\ntwenty years doing his level<br>\r\nbest to keep his head above<br>\r\nwater and his kids<br>\r\nentertained. </p>\r\n'),(3666,'<P>We are talking now of summer evenings in Knoxville Tennessee in the time that I lived there so successfully disguised to myself as a child.</P><P>...It has become that time of evening when people sit on their porches, rocking gently and talking gently and watching the street and the standing up into their sphere of possession of the trees, of birds\\\' hung havens, hangars. People go by; things go by. A horse, drawing a buggy, breaking his hollow iron music on the asphalt: a loud auto: a quiet auto: people in pairs, not in a hurry, scuffling, switching their weight of aestival body, talking casually, the taste hovering over them in vanilla, strawberry, pasteboard, and starched milk, the image upon them of lovers and horsemen, squaring with clowns in hueless amber. A streetcar raising its iron moan; stopping; belling and starting, stertorous; rousing and raising again its iron increasing moan and swimming its gold windows and straw seats on past and past and past, the bleak spark crackling and cursing above it like a small \r\nmalignant spirit set to dog its tracks; the iron whine rises on rising speed; still risen, faints; halts; the faint stinging bell; rises again, still fainter; fainting, lifting, lifts, faints foregone: forgotten. Now is the night one blue dew.</P><P>Now is the night one blue dew, my father has drained, he has coiled the hose.</P><P>Low in the length of lawns, a frailing of fire who breathes...<BR>Parents on porches: rock and rock. From damp strings morning glories hang their ancient faces.</P><P>The dry and exalted noise of the locusts from all the air at once enchants my eardrums.</P><P>On the rough wet grass of the back yard my father and mother have spread quilts. We all lie there, my mother, my father, my uncle, my aunt, and I too am lying \r\nthere... They are not talking much, and the talk is quiet, of nothing in particular, of nothing at all in particular, of nothing at all. The stars are wide and alive, they seem each like a smile of great sweetness, and they are very near. All my people are larger bodies than mine,... with voices gentle and meaningless like the voices of sleeping birds. One is an artist, he is living at home. One is a musician, she is living at home. One is my mother who is good to me. One is my father who is good to me. By some chance, here they are, all on this earth; and who shall ever tell the sorrow of being on this earth, lying, on \r\nquilts, on the grass, in the summer evening, among the sounds of the night. May God bless my people, my uncle, my aunt, my mother, my good father, oh, remember them kindly in their time of trouble; and in the hour of their taking away.</P><P>After a little I am taken in and put to be. Sleep, soft smiling, draws me unto her: and those receive me, who quietly treat me, as one familiar and well-beloved in that home: but will not, no ,will not, not now, noter; but will not ever tell me who I am.</P> \r\n'),(3667,'<P>Having been married now for almost 2 years, and having stayed at home and gone to school for those 2 years may sound like many a woman or man\\\'s dream come true.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>Trust me it isn\\\'t.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Something gets taken away. Or put to sleep.\r\nOr strangled. Something that made life not safe is gone and instead I know what every fucking day of my life will turn out to be.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And that\\\'s a very depressing idea.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Another depressing idea formulated on a question is exactly how much worth do we give to the jobs women do when they stay at home? </P>\r\n\r\n<P>Ive recently discovered that staying at home has not only a.) driven me insane-but it has also b.) sucked any drive outta me.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>There are so many things I want to do...and so many are shot down because we\\\'re in this thing called marriage. Don\\\'t get me wrong. It was a good thing...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>...and then I remembered what I was like when I was 19. Sure there are certain aspects of being that age that I don\\\'t miss. But I see something in that girl, a luster that\\\'s missing now. Life has become a repetition of sequential events.</P> \r\n\r\n<P>Life has become boring. So I.m getting my nipples pierced. and it took me 2 years to realise this.</P> '),(3668,'<p>a moment or two before, i\\\'d been barely able to keep my eyes open.  now i\\\'m chasing someone down the sidewalk.  i shouldn\\\'t be - too tired, too worn down.  we\\\'re both at a sprint, my badge is hanging from my neck, the sound of voices is all around me.  some part of me knows that i\\\'ve become a spectacle.  a disgustingly ugly white man running flat out after a shady looking character wearing a black coat much too heavy for the weather.</p>\r\n<p>some of us love it, that bizarre feeling that we are somehow stuck in an episode of Cops.  some, like me, hate it.  i don\\\'t want anyone to see me. ever.  even when i\\\'ve done something that saved a life, or caught a real criminal, rather than some petty shoplifter, i\\\'ve always felt like an idiot and an asshole.  i\\\'ve never felt like a hero, even when by the standards of most people, i was for a short while, exactly that.</p>\r\n<p>i fucking hate that feeling.</p>\r\n<p>i am running up a hill, after this piece of shit who punched at me and took off the second i badged him.  i am tired, i am tired of the blur of wide eyed faces around me as i run.  i am sick to death of the poem i somehow scribed across the inside of my eyes, which colors everything i\\\'ve seen for days.  i\\\'m going to catch him, he didn\\\'t realize how fast i am.  i watch his hands, and sometimes i watch the back of his head.  i see the side of his face as he turns his head to the left.</p>\r\n<p>\\\'fucker, you\\\'re going to cut across traffic,\\\' i think.  the fucker cuts across traffic.  my eyes flick over, test my chances, in an instant.  it looks more dangerous than it is.  maybe i need to believe that as my right foot comes over and lands on the street.  something - people - a couple, catches my eye as i throw myself among the fast moving cars.</p>\r\n<p>i don\\\'t know why, but i see them so clearly, standing out of the blurry mess that is everything around me.  a tall white man, and a relatively short asian woman.  holding hands.  happiness pours out of them, bounds across the road, and hits me square.  my jaw tightens.</p>\r\n<p>i hate them immediately.  i hate their beauty, for they are indeed both quite attractive.  i hate their oozing joy, as if it were a mortal affront to my very existence.  in seconds i\\\'ve fantasized of ripping their love, their bodies, and their minds to pieces.  i want to destroy them.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m running.  they are gone, and my mind may be far away now, but my eyes are only for this asshole running ahead of me.  i\\\'m close.  so close now.  i reach out -</p>\r\n<p>when i get him i\\\'m going to wreck him.  i\\\'m going to beat him to within inches of his life.  his blood is going to cover me.  i\\\'m going to tear his disease ridden heart out and consume it while the world watches.  monster!  monster!</p>\r\n<p>i connect with him, first the left hand, then my right.  three seconds and we\\\'re going to go ass over teakettle into a group waiting to catch the bus.  i don\\\'t drive into him, but rather drop down, letting my legs just give out.  i am dead weight, climbing up his back, and we are down.  he shouts something, i do too.  i can feel my badge on my back now.  we\\\'re rolling.</p>\r\n<p>as we roll i\\\'m wrapping him up, getting my legs ready, forcing myself to be above him.  i\\\'m going to murder him, and it\\\'s going to feel so good.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m on top of him, like i\\\'m jumping his bones.  i lock him up, flip him, control him.  i shout something about arresting him.  i want to shoot the crowd watching me.  assholes.  look away from me!  he goes slack and i ease up.  i do the right thing.</p>\r\n<p>the only part of my mind that i can still take pride in exerts itself.  already the red haze is pulling back.  my anger is dispersed, to hide again in the darker parts of my brain.  i can hear the sirens.  the shouts of my backup as they gain our position.  it\\\'s over.</p>\r\n<p>i go back to a dark room, where the light comes from only the machinery of voyeurism.  there is no reward, not even within myself.  i will go home and speak not a word.  i will not eat.  i will drink until i pass out, having resisted the urge to call upon my Lamashtu.  tomorrow i will do something else.  and there will be nothing, no reason, no hope of reward for my labors.  there can be none, when i can\\\'t stop finding only oblivion inside my heart.</p>'),(3669,'<p>i was walking on dundas street, past kensington market, through the park on bathurst.  i walked past the huge public pool and there was public pool yelling, and splashing. i came around the corner and saw a fence full of kids on the inside arguing with an old man on the outside of the fence.  a large blue ball was set before him, i approached, wading into the yelling.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>the kids ask me to throw the ball back to them, the old man urgently insists that i not, claiming it\\\'s his and he just paid forty dollars for it.  the kids yell that he\\\'s mentally ill, and if he indeed paid forty dollars for this ball, he is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me he says</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me forty dolla, if you throw</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just please throw the ball back the kids plead</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i pick up the ball and the old man shrieks.  i throw it over the fence and the kids cheer.  the old man sets upon me, really upset, really believing that was his forty dollar ball thrown to the kids.  he yells over to his even older friend sitting on a rock nearby, who starts yelling at me too.  he charges me, a slow old man charge but still really pissed off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>back the fuck off i say to him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me, that my ball he says to me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i turn around and walk away and he gives old man chase, with his partner bringing up the rear.  i walk out to the path that leads through the park under the trees, and the old guys shuffle after me.  my lead increases quickly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>come here! come here! coward! you want to fight! come here! coward! says the old man, again and again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>even when i\\\'m hundreds of feet away he\\\'s still giving chase with his friend, and the wind whispers coward in my ear with a old man\\\'s chinese accent.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"oldballguy_450.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3670,'<p>i was walking on dundas street, past kensington market, through the park on bathurst.  i walked past the huge public pool and there was public pool yelling, and splashing. i came around the corner and saw a fence full of kids on the inside arguing with an old man on the outside of the fence.  a large blue ball was set before him, i approached, wading into the yelling.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>the kids ask me to throw the ball back to them, the old man urgently insists that i not, claiming it\\\'s his and he just paid forty dollars for it.  the kids yell that he\\\'s mentally ill, and if he indeed paid forty dollars for this ball, he is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me he says</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me forty dolla, if you throw</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just please throw the ball back the kids plead</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i pick up the ball and the old man shrieks.  i throw it over the fence and the kids cheer.  the old man sets upon me, really upset, really believing that was his forty dollar ball thrown to the kids.  he yells over to his even older friend sitting on a rock nearby, who starts yelling at me too.  he charges me, a slow old man charge but still really pissed off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>back the fuck off i say to him.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you pay me, that my ball he says to me.</p>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\">\r\n<p>i turn around and walk away and he gives old man chase, with his partner bringing up the rear.  i walk out to the path that leads through the park under the trees, and the old guys shuffle after me.  my lead increases quickly.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>come here! come here! coward! you want to fight! come here! coward! says the old man, again and again.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>even when i\\\'m hundreds of feet away he\\\'s still giving chase with his friend, and the wind whispers coward in my ear with a old man\\\'s korean accent.</p>\r\n'),(3671,'<p>They just don\\\'t make parenting books that deal with the kind of crap I\\\'ve gone through with Conner\\\'s dad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Last year, he got arrested for third degree burglary. He helped a friend rob our local Pizza Hut. Not being literate enough to understand the irony, they made off with salad, bread, and dough. Three slang terms for money and not a single dollar bill in the process. But what you steal doesn\\\'t lessen the fact that you stole stuff, so he got a couple of nights in the county lock-up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then there was the great lice-capade of 2004. I spent three months sterilizing my home and still picking Conner up from daycare every time he returned from Dad\\\'s house. We finally sent him away to my parents home (a lice-free zone) long enough that when he went back to Daddy\\\'s house and came back with lice we could pinpoint the source.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Soon thereafter we had the \\\"Cunabice City\\\" saga. Some massive lapse of good judgement allowed Dad to believe that there was absolutely nothing wrong with letting a four year-old play Grand Theft Auto: Vice City on the old Playstation2 (followed shortly by instructing Conner not to tell me, knowing exactly how well Conner has always been able to keep a secret. (Sarcasm)). After a couple of weeks of daily questions about whether or not it was o.k. to \\\"slice a ho\\\'s throat\\\" and a lunchtime conference with one of our city\\\'s finest, I hopefully managed to negate any potential psychological damage.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(Did I mention that during all of this time I\\\'ve been getting a bank breaking $25 per month in child support?)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Well, the (alleged) events of this weekend pretty much take the cake for me. Conner had reported (to his teachers and to me) that he saw Daddy hit his girlfriend this weekend. It wasn\\\'t just \\\"Daddy hit Carla,\\\" but a detailed description of a domestic abuse situation that would make any Lifetime Movie screenwriter jealous of its attention to detail and dialog. While Conner has been known to tell a fib or two to push people\\\'s buttons, this story is so far out of his frame of reference that I have a hard time discounting it. Of course, Daddy denies everything, but I can\\\'t toss the situation aside so easily.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What kills me the most is being put in the position to have to doubt my kid over something so serious. If it\\\'s true, then I\\\'m concerned for Conner\\\'s well-being. There\\\'s not much out there more damaging to a kid\\\'s psyche than witnessing domestic violence. If it\\\'s not true (which, again, I highly doubt), then I have to wonder what on Earth would make a 5 year old fabricate such a vivid story.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dr. Spock......Dear Abby.......Bueller.......anyone?</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3672,'<p>First you hear the softly loudening sound of crickets.  Next you hear the shakers sounding like a very specific type of lawn sprinkler.  That\\\'s what we would refer to as atmosphere.  Then guitars...</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Tonight is a very Green Arrow night.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>The night in question being very hazy with a threating-to-be-full moon seeming to glow along with the city through the high concentration of water molecules stuck in the air.  The night feels content and safe.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Do you want to go for a walk to your favourite bench?  There\\\'s a full moon out and the city is glowing.  It looked great on my ride here.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>And yet you were already thinking the same thing, weren\\\'t you?  A cigarette or two while wearing your super star pants and sitting on a dewy bench.  Listening to the sounds of the streetcars and watching the pulsing building.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"This song makes me sad.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Because of the crickets?\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\\\"Mmm.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>At a later date, after the initial introduction and familiarisation process.  You said that the song makes you realise that you don\\\'t hear any crickets in the city and the longing it inspires for where we\\\'re from.  The crickets are a very specific sound from where we used to be and the lack of their presence makes for an interesting concern: the unconnectedness with nature even when sitting on an old bathrub surrounded by foliage and fatty raccoons lumbering about.</p>\r\n<p>And for me the song is more of an emotional perfection.  The smell of a lawn being watered by an automatic sprinkler system late at night, or just wandering around with near-zero artificial sound interference.  It\\\'s that reminiscence of those times as a child when summers lasted so long that you would forget what school even was.  It connects with me on a very deep level and I still maintain the statement I made several years ago: \\\"This song is, for me, summer.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>But I don\\\'t know where the crickets are and I wish that I had an answer for you.  I hope that being able to go places where we can find them can be good enough.  If they refuse to come to us, we are going to have to make that effort.  I think the saddest thing for me is the knowledge that if, when finished writing this, I turned off my computer and my fan and I sat listening attentively to what is happening outside my window, I would not hear any crickets either.  And that is a very lonely feeling.</p>'),(3673,'<P>\r\nI never noticed they were gone. For some reason Yo La Tengoâ€™s Green Arrow had been targeting an emotional nerve perceptively for the last little while and I couldnâ€™t figure out why. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThen I realized that there arenâ€™t any crickets anymore. The epitome of summer evening sounds no longer exists were we live. We sit out back on the smoking tub and strain our ears for any recognition of the singing air. There is nothing here, the occasional plane, the odd police siren, and maybe a dog bark, but thatâ€™s it.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI listen carefully as the music that is singing me to sleep creeps up slowly like the warmest summer evening. I have childhood memories of family farms; long since sold and severed. The memories are of evening suns that descend rapidly from high skies and highlight the thick summer air thatâ€™s filled with floating seeds of flowering trees and dandelions; the smell of hot grass in the cooling air. Land that beyond the ominous swaying willows, spans without interruption out to the glowing horizon far off in the distance.  Long hot days end with the sweetest sense of security and the freshest cloudless cool evening, encroaching. Everything exudes a clean smell; like the suns bleaching rays burning off the lands stale humidity as it sets. Iâ€™m sitting out on the porch in my pyjamas, freshly washed skin radiating heat from the sun soak of the day. The wind blows the trees that dance in front of the brightest setting sun and send moving flashes of light and dark streaking across my closed eyes. It is here bathed in the setting sun that I remember dreaming of an aching and unknown future; all elements of childhood called to inventory and feeling truly happy for unknown reasons. The shadows of landscape grow longer and longer and dim stars grow brighter with each passing minute. The singing air floods my ears and echoes around the buildings and trees, filling in the growing darkness with noise. \r\n</P><P>\r\nSo where did they go? The neighbourhood Iâ€™m in has enough green to accommodate raccoons and birds, but no crickets? Is it from the mild summer? Are they just not here? When I am home I will find a field at the end of my town to lie in at the end of the day and think of the droning sliding guitar that so perfectly accompanies this place in my mind, and I mean to bring you there with me.\r\n</P>'),(3674,'<P>\r\n<B><I>\\\"No, barkeep, cut me off.  I\\\'m fighting crime tonight!\\\"</I></B>\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou\\\'ll never hear me say that.  People think the Green Arrow is a strait arrow.  Think again.   When I put on those green tights and pick up my bow and quiver, I\\\'m drunk for sure.  Alchohol: Liquid Courage.  That is what I call it.  And it helps me relax so my arrows fly true.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe other night I was hanging out on top of the Roost (my favorite bar) waiting for some crime to shoot arrows at.  I was drinking a scotch.  Mmmm, scotch.  Tastey scotch.  Across the street I see an altercation happening between a group of kids and an old man.  The kids lost their bouncy ball over the fence and the old man wouldn\\\'t give it back.  He was belligerent, saying it was his ball and he paid $50 for it.  Clearly the old man was insane.  Nobody pays $50 for a bouncy ball.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThings heated up.  I downed the scotch and grabbed my bow.  I was going to teach this old man a lesson.  I\\\'m pulling back on the arrow, saying a little prayer, when up walks this shaggy teenager.  He confronts the old man and starts taking pictures of him in jest.  He takes the ball and throws it to the kids.  The old man is furious.  The old man starts calling this shaggy teenager out, \\\"You coward, you steal an old men\\\'s balls!\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe kid is snapping away with the camera.  All the sudden, the table has turned and it is this kid\\\'s ass I am aiming at.  Teens today do not respect their elders, God damnit.  Some super hero needs to teach this kid how to show some respect.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI\\\'m taking aim...  sort of leaning into it, one knee bent.  I must have had too much to drink.  Jesus saves! I slipped.  I slid bumpety bumpety bump on the roof shingles and off the edge, two stories into the bushes.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI am an archer super hero, and I am an alchoholic.\r\nThere, I admit it.  This court appointed AA is BS.  Can I go now.\r\n</P><P>\r\nRandom member in the folding chairs:\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"You\\\'ve got a serious problem man, you look like a pedophile in the Peter Pan outfit.  Who are you kidding?\\\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nHey asshole, I avenge lives.  If it weren\\\'t for people like me, all you would have is parametics picking up the pieces.  Piles of pieces here and there.  I make sure the people who break people pay!  Wait what am I explaining this to you for?  You will never understand!  You powerless mortals always criticize.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\\\"At least we don\\\'t act justified.  You are an alchy, you said you admit it but you don\\\'t.  You think it makes you a badass!\\\"\r\n</P>\r\n<P><B>(pluck) *THWAK*</B></P>\r\n<P><B>(pluck) *ZIT*</B></P>\r\n<P>Take that you ...\r\n<P><CENTER>TO BE CONTINUTED</CENTER></P>\r\n<P><CENTER><img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\"></CENTER></P>'),(3675,'<p>\r\nIt was 1 am. He parked down the street, about a block, and walked to her grandparents\\\' house. Her car was not there. Of course it wasn\\\'t. There was a dirty white Buick Regal in the driveway. It was dark in the house. \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe paced around the yard for about five minutes, and decided to wait. He sat against the oak tree and thought of what he might say when she arrived. The humidity permeated his tshirt and made it hang limply from his body, and he felt the seat of his pants slowly soaking through. \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe had been in Myrtle Beach for about two months. He had other plans, to begin with, but nothing went the way it was supposed to. He was young enough for this to be a surprise. He made about ninety dollars a week working at the restaurant, smiling and serving food to tourists.\r\n</p><p>\r\nOne thing he was not, was a tourist.\r\n</p><p>\r\nBefore the waiter job, he had been a projectionist at the movies in the mall, and before that, he had been in the Navy. In the Navy he had been very lonely. He had been in love with two girls. One was a coffeeshop waitress and a college student at Old Dominion, who had loved him too. She made eyes at him, and moved her body close to his, played with his necklace. He was afraid. The semester ended and she moved to New Jersey. The other girl was in the Navy. He was less afraid, with her, but being a girl in the Navy she was constantly surrounded by other boys, and men, and he was not yet a man. And so he was not enough for her. And so, his heart in beautiful, refracted broken pieces, he told the Navy that he was a drug addict, stole his CDs back from her, and decided to move to the beach. \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was 2 am. In the quiet retirement neighborhood, no cars came by, no pets were barking. No lights were on save the orange sodium streetlights. He began to feel a little cold. His argument was taking shape inside his head, and he waited for her.\r\n</p><p>\r\nWhen he began working at the restaurant, he gradually acquired a group of friends. Soon he lived with them. He was always more comfortable in a group. They smoked a lot of pot together, played video games, and watched television. He slept on the couch at the end of the night, woke and did his laundry in the morning and then left for work . He ate about 500 calories a day. Before work he would ride around in his car listening to the Grateful Dead. He made about ninety dollars a week, and contributed about forty of that to the marijuana fund. The rest paid for gasoline and less often, food. He ate at the restaurant and had it deducted from his small waiter\\\'s paycheck.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nHe felt as if he were really living! He loved his life there, being young and unattached to anything. He never called his mother. \r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was 3 am. He was not sleepy. He waited for her, under the tree, feeling himself breathe in and out, and watching the japanese maple tree move slowly back and forth in the breeze. \r\n</p><p>\r\nOne day she started work at the restaurant. She was not classically beautiful, and she had an air about her that he had never before seen in a person. He was instantly and fully in love with her. He knew that he loved her, because he instantly forgot about his broken heart, the Navy, all of it. \r\n</p><p>\r\nShe beame a part of the group. She came over to smoke with them every night, to watch the boys play video games and make jokes. she fit in with the girls, and they all loved her. She lived with a boyfriend. She was from New York State. She dressed like a boy. When she finally brought her boyfriend over, he was not what they had expected. Soon, she left him and moved in with her grandparents. \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe two of them had an understanding, They were both escapees on a grand adventure. \r\n</p><p>\r\nAt four, he awoke, disoriented and aching. He gathered himself off the ground. He knew where she was, who she was with, and why. It was real, true tragedy, and he was intelligent enough to hold all of it in his mind at once. He felt something give inside himself. It was such a delicate and intricate tragedy that it made him weep with the greatest joy and the greatest sorrow he had ever felt. \r\n</p><p>	\r\nAll that he owned was in his car, down the block. He started the car, and headed west. He stopped in Conway to have breakfast, and, his eyes watering, he drive to his mother\\\'s house. He decided that maybe it was time to go to college.</p>');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (3676,'<p>My step-father always hated this one song - I don\\\'t know the name - that one where an irritating voice croons about how \\\'if you can\\\'t be with the one you love, love the one you\\\'re with.\\\'  He really hated that song.  It brings a smile just remembering how he\\\'d tell me, when I was a kid, that I should never live by that sentiment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Summer - it shouldn\\\'t have been such a big deal to me, I knew I\\\'d be dropping out of highschool in the fall.  But it was big, it was what we forever after just called \\\'The Summer\\\'.  The first time we all had jobs, which actually made it all the better.  Most important, it was the first summer where some of us had cars.  I fell in love for the first time, the virgins all finally got laid (except for poor Mikey, who had to wait for three more years or so), friends were made who didn\\\'t go to our school, and it was the first summer where we spent the majority of our time out doing stuff.  For me, it was the first where I barely got high, the others all spent vast periods under the influence of... everything.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was the neverending two or so months where we\\\'d meet up at Rocky Point, and drive to Bicks and drink coffee and goof off until 6am.  In the round booth on slow nights, all over the floor at every available table on the busy nights.  I beat the shit out of Adam Sh. on the pier while he begged me to let him go.  I showed my friends the horseshoe crabs which mated in the surf at night, shining my very first maglite on them.  Chuck and I pulled a male off a female, and were greatly amused when it when absolutely bonkers.  These were, probably, the best times of my life.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Chuck and I did our \\\'zine, <u>Thank you for not smoking</u>, with the little comic about an alternate universe where we sold our parents into slavery for a starship, and where my locker at school, called the \\\'locker of ill repute\\\', held only a six pack of Red Dog and stairs leading into my secret lair.  We liberally ripped off Stig\\\'s Inferno - maces hanging on walls \\\'in case of giant bats\\\'.  I invented the lead character, Bob the Goldfish, who was based on a stuffed fish I wore hanging from my fatigue jacket (who was actually blue with a black stripe).  Bob, with the huge fold out penis (and machinegun), who floated around, and got pissed whenever anyone noticed he wasn\\\'t gold - \\\"it\\\'s a recessive gene fluke asshole, and I\\\'m touchy about it!\\\"  We travelled to the planet of the Norse gods, and got our friend Laci installed as the Goddess of Skinheads.  We all got arrested on the Planet of Feminists, and escaped only after Bob seduced the guards.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We\\\'d sit in Chuck\\\'s basement, playing Magic or working on the \\\'zine, while smoking Captain Black from the hooka Chuck\\\'s dad had brought home from Turkey.  We\\\'d drink Jolt, or Merlot, or sometimes Flying Dog Doggy Style Ale.  We worked the kitchen with Big Chuck, brewing up beer.  At Bicks we\\\'d play Mortal Combat or the old Neo Geo machine in back, after which we\\\'d repair outside for a brawl with college kids.  We got into sword fighting that summer.  Some got into the militia.  We began the \\\'big green wall\\\' at Tom\\\'s place, made entirely of empty cans of Gennessee Cream Ale.  Tom bought his first AK-47, and shot a hole through the wall which penetrated the main water pipe into his house.  I nearly drowned in the swamp running from cops who weren\\\'t chasing us after all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That summer was when we created the prostitute who wouldn\\\'t put out, and the other favored inside joke - \\\'windy out here, eh?\\\'  We met Gail, who should have hated people like us, but didn\\\'t.  Most of us fell in love with Gail at one point or another.  I got the first date, but Chuck got the first kiss.  We snuck in CCRI and played Vampire or pinball.  Gail took me to a Depeche Mode concert, where later I got the second kiss - and a little more.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But Gail wasn\\\'t anyone\\\'s girl.  She was her own woman, even then when she was only 16.  She was neat, I miss her still.  I had my own girl, the only one accepted by my crew as a true skinhead, which was cool.  At least it was at the time.  We had these tremendous fights I remember, honestly I found them kind of enjoyable.  There was the time I freaked out and told her to stay away from me, not even call me, for two weeks.  By the time I was done ranting, she was throwing up in Mike\\\'s bathroom and crying and begging me to stay with her.  \\\"Shut the fuck up and don\\\'t talk to me for two weeks!\\\" I screamed as I stormed out the door.  Two days later we were fucking on the roof of my dad\\\'s house, having been unable to get inside as I\\\'d lost the key.  The end of the summer though, she really did stay away.  She had three jobs by then, and was working non-stop.  I never got to see her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'d love to sound all noble now, tell the world how when the time came to cheat on her I struggled with the decision at the least.  Yeah, right.  I hadn\\\'t been laid in who knows how long, she was never around, there wasn\\\'t even time for a nice back seat blowjob.  So I met Kelly one night at a party in NK, we hit it off, more or less.  When I met her again, I knew I was going to bang her.  For a skinhead especially, there\\\'s something really hot about a cute girl with a shaved head.  Every guy\\\'s eye was on her, some of the broads too, but she was wasting her time talking to me.  Heh heh.  We got to Bicks, the full crew, and she sat next to me.  While the conversation - most of it just remembering our best fights - went on, she sat there and rubbed my cock through my denims.  Sometimes she snapped my braces, stinging my nipple.  Finally, the night got boring for me.  I looked over at her, and she smiled almost shyly.  I thought that was really funny.  Everyone knew she\\\'d been the biggest skinhead fucktoy ever, at least back when she was 14.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I took her hand, or she took mine maybe.  A couple of the guys clapped me on the back as we headed out.  There was no good place to go, so we walked in the mist to the little graveyard.  She asked if I had a condom, and I laughed.  I sat her on some crusty old monument, ran my hand up her skirt.  I loved the feeling of the heat coming off her skin.  She did stuff to me probably, but I wasn\\\'t paying attention.  I got her panties pulled to the side (they were really girly, which made me laugh as well), got some fingers running over her labia.  Even as a youngster, I tried to not be overeager - anyone who was friends with Tom and Crazy John tried, those guys thought every chick they\\\'d ever fucked was the tightest thing ever.  It took me two more years before I found the heart to tell them it was just \\\'cause the girls weren\\\'t aroused.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We were young, she was wet quickly, and just as quickly telling me not to \\\'put it in\\\'.  Fuck that I thought, laughing all over again.  And I penetrated her.  Oh it was sweet, warm and - well, you know the feeling.  She didn\\\'t bitch or complain, she just started fucking be back.  Somewhere in the middle of things, I remembered my girlfriend - my first love.  I think the hot flash of guilt put me that much closer to my orgasm.  I don\\\'t know if she came, and I didn\\\'t care at the time.  I finished my bit, got the rest of her clothes off, and started again.  The next time was on the cold wet grass.  We cuddled and watched a police cruiser drive by the graveyard, got dressed, and spent the remained of the night walking around with her on my arm.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yeah, I\\\'d like to say it was hard to cheat, but it wasn\\\'t.  Some sexy little slitch with a shaved head showed up, and plainly offered her body up.  I didn\\\'t sit and ponder, I didn\\\'t agonize over consequences, I didn\\\'t ask anyone if they thought it was right.  I took her to the graveyard, and I fucked her.  I came inside her, three times I guess, and I didn\\\'t stop to think about pregnancy or the clap or AIDS.  I also never saw her again, except one night...it might have been her, but someone was beating on her - if it was her.  I just stepped around them and got my beer.  My girlfriend did eventually find out, I don\\\'t know how.  She punched me and slapped me, cried and screamed and had snot running down her face.  I just laughed.  Later that day, she gave me the best blowjob, and I forgave her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know if she ever forgave me.</p>'),(3677,'<P>\r\nSo when does it change? When will I outgrow having to fake an orgasm? The truth is I have only had the pleasure of penetrable peaking once and the guy I was with was clearly irritated by the effort. From then on I laid claim to the fact that he had broken me in and so it came easier. (or I) Not true. Once, only once in my life have I experienced this. I feel cheated and afraid to tell the person Iâ€™m with that Iâ€™m high maintenance in that department; so I moan on and appropriately curl my toes and bite my lip. \r\n</P><P>\r\nIâ€™m really very good at it. I can vary it up based on the position and make a point to even intermittently flex the muscle down there that swallows whole and empties his throbbing member. Its not like the guys Iâ€™ve been with were lacking in any way, in fact the last guy was terribly large, but no dice. Iâ€™m starting to think itâ€™s a powerful mental block. Now donâ€™t get me wrong I really do enjoy the act but it would be nice to finish the race before he heads off to the bathroom, and Iâ€™m left to polish myself off. I understand the delicacy of the male mind especially when it comes to that sort of thing, and I think thatâ€™s mostly why I do it, but Iâ€™m bound to grow weary of it. \r\n</P><P>\r\nI need some serious advice stat! Is it possible that Iâ€™m faking it because if he cums and I donâ€™t then it becomes a sort of power thing, I would say this is most definitely the case when its not offered to finish me off. Resentment builds through stuff like that and I would rather give a good performance than to have him thinking that Iâ€™m thinking hes an ass for not caring if I cum, and I will. So if I wail away and rack my body at just the right time I remain in control of the situation. Isnâ€™t it true \\\"Iâ€™m responsible for my own orgasm!\\\"?</P>'),(3679,'<p>Grocery shopping at the Berkeley Bowl today for a meal befitting the death of Julia Child, I ran into a little boy who made me think kids aren\\\'t so bad. I was standing in the checkout line, pushing my basket in front me with one foot.  In the basket were fennel and heirloom tomatoes and lettuce for a salad, some scallops for quenelles, which were mentioned in Julia\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.nytimes.com/2004/08/13/dining/13CND-CHILD.html\\\">NYT obit</a>, and some chocolate for a souffl&#233;.  The mom in front of me was straight-up Berkeley, with long red and purple hair, no distinguishable breast support, Doc Martins, babooshka, cart full of vegetables.  She moved aside and started unloading the cart and the little boy squished in the little boy seat looked right into my eyes and said, \\\"Hello!\\\"  Then he puckered his lips and made a kissing noise, so I sucked up my grown-up embarassment and returned the favor.  \\\"I want to kiss you on the lips!\\\" he screamed.  He then turned to his mom and screamed, \\\"I wanna kiss him on the lips!\\\"  He was laughing now, I think maybe slightly aware of the taboo, but maybe not.  He just kept saying it.  \\\"Can I mom, can I?  I want to kiss him on the lips!\\\"  The mother was smiling, and maybe just sort of shook her head while she kept unloading pineapples onto the conveyor belt. He must do this alot.  This is what I love about Berkeley.  As much as the winds of change might be starting to rustle the old staid leaves of this grand republic again, there aren\\\'t too many places where a little boy could scream this to a grown man and not have his mother shush him, or tell him that he shouldn\\\'t want to kiss a man other than his father. This is not to say that little boys should go around kissing men, but you get what I mean. It was cute, he had no censor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Anyway, the boy was full of love.  After deciding that I was a lost cause, and after his mother forbid him from climbing out of the kid seat until they got outside, he moved on to flirting with the boys in the next line over.  One had just been talking about New York and probably just flew in from there. He was marvelling at how they don\\\'t sell tabloids at the Berkeley Bowl checkout, they sell yoga magazines and \\\"D.I.Y.\\\"  They noticed the little boy, with his overalls, Doc Martins and his black knit skull cap and were like, \\\"I like your style, bro.  Nice hat.\\\"  The little boy quickly replied, \\\"You like my hat?  You want to see my bottom?\\\" and he proceeded to lean over to the side so we could all get a good look.  This went on for a bit, the boy obviously pleased with the reactions of the audience, and he had to repeat the joke a few more times just to make sure he got all the mileage he could out of it. </p>\r\n<p>\\\"Did he just ask us if we wanted to see his butt?\\\"<br> \\\"Yeah.\\\" <br>\\\"You wanna see my bottom? Here it is!\\\"<br>\\\"That\\\'s okay little man. We\\\'ve seen enough.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He\\\'s growing up in a liberal (or libertine) household, and god love him.  He renewed my faith in the future. I was loud like him once, a little fey and a little embarassing for my parents when I let loose in public. But I got tamped down at some point. It was New England, after all, and I didn\\\'t like the tone in my uncle\\\'s voice when he called me a fruit.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t want kids. I came to this conclusion a while back, and I reserve the right to change my mind, but mostly I\\\'m too overwhelmed with how much there is to get done, how many things there are to see and do as an adult, and family life sounds a little tedious to me.  It\\\'s hard enough to get creative work done, or to keep a love relationship going, and I guess kids give you something to do together, as a couple, a shared project.  But I\\\'m chock full of neuroses, at least 70% of which seem to come directly from my mom, and I\\\'d rather work these out in my own time rather than pass them on.  No kid needs me as a parent, as cool as I might be occasionally, making them cookies and letting them watch whatever they want on TV.  I\\\'d probably get drunk and leave them someplace like I\\\'ve done with several jackets, or maybe I\\\'d just get annoyed with them too often and they\\\'d grow up resenting me because I was a self-involved parent who never had enough time for them. In any case, I stand by me decision, but that li\\\'l fella today, nobody\\\'s gonna shut him up.  He\\\'ll be the wacky one everyone loves in high school who wears a funny suit to the prom.  He probably won\\\'t be President with that mouth, but everyone loves a flirt.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3680,'<p>Grocery shopping at the Berkeley Bowl today for a meal befitting the death of Julia Child, I ran into a little boy who made me think kids aren\\\'t so bad. I was standing in the checkout line, pushing my basket in front me with one foot.  In the basket were fennel and heirloom tomatoes and lettuce for a salad, some scallops for quenelles, which were mentioned in Julia\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.nytimes.com/2004/08/13/dining/13CND-CHILD.html\\\">NYT obit</a>, and some chocolate for a souffl&#233;.  The mom in front of me was straight-up Berkeley, with long red and purple hair, no distinguishable breast support, Doc Martins, babooshka, cart full of vegetables.  She moved aside and started unloading the cart and the little boy squished in the little boy seat looked right into my eyes and said, \\\"Hello!\\\"  Then he puckered his lips and made a kissing noise, so I sucked up