esnâ€™t realize what kind of day I have had, any other time I probably wouldnâ€™t care but his lack of consideration at this moment heats my face. I can make it down town in about 45 minutes; you guys arenâ€™t going to wait? After receiving explicit directions to a place thatâ€™s practically impossible to miss I hop on the bus and head to the subway station. I donâ€™t sit, its not cause there arenâ€™t any seats, but because I know that if I take my bag down from my aching shoulders I wonâ€™t want to place it back when the time comes to leave the bus. I find temporary relief from creeping the bag up my back a bit and resting it between my ass and the window.  I make a note that itâ€™s a good time to have a \\\"shelf\\\". I close my eyes and dream of being on the droning subway with at least a few minutes to myself to ponder the day and cool down from my worked up state. The problem is that I know why they wanted to go early, they need to sign mortgage papers tonight and think nothing of excluding me to get theyâ€™re dinner in. This is clearly not how either of them normally behave but in my head right now I spin a whirlwind of overreactions to my brother and my predictions that he will look disapprovingly at me when I arrive at Milestones with all my bags and a tool box. I plan to have a comment ready for this. \\\"Is that really the first thing you are going to say to me?\\\", and I will add biting sarcasm to it. However he greets me pleasantly at the door to the restaurant and helps me with my bags. \r\n</P><P>\r\nAs I get off the subway bags in check MP3 player in position and music at an acceptable volume I hike up the stairs to University Ave. A beggar mumbles something and extends his hand and I am glad to be removed from the sounds of the street and the abyss of noise. I feel that my headphones are containing my sanity at this point. I am walking down Queen and a construction walkway is ahead of me perfectly organizing the pedestrians in a neat flow of human flesh. I enter and must adjust my stride with the flow of people. I race into a theory that would amplify the output of this sorry flow and the thoughts that I am increasing the resistance with all my bags and inversely affecting the current. Ahhhh! Think of nothing. Think of nothing. As I am nearing John St., I nearly reach my peak of tolerance with the slow and short gaited people around me. If I just throw my bags around a bit I could produce a pathway that would let me through. Of course I donâ€™t and guiltily hang behind a small girl holding hands and walking with her dad. \r\n</P><P>\r\nWe finally get seated and I am greeted awkwardly by the wait staff as I produce my load and try to find storage for it around the booth. Theyâ€™re out of prime rib, again Iâ€™m irritated. My mother talks of a philanthropist that spoke to their union and she seems suddenly very passionate about humanitarian issues of Africa. A debate ensues where my mother blames \\\"us\\\" for not getting involved to help with the AIDS epidemic. I wonder for how long this issue has been around and she praises the speaker for organizing the distribution of AIDS medication. I argue that itâ€™s their governments own fault for the neglect and that they could so easily control this sort of thing if they just educated their people. Now normally I would be the first to offer a hand but today I have the whole help yourself attitude kicking and so I come across as a selfish and self-proclaimed insighter to global issues. I seem to have all the answers, why didnâ€™t I on the exams? Jane advances the conversation about how transparent the USâ€™s intentions are in the middle-east and that if they truly were fighters for democracy that they would be all over Africa by now. I am annoyed again and hate the anti-Bush propaganda as much as the anti-terrorist shit. I donâ€™t like who I am tonight and I feel restless and wrong for some reason. They give me a ride home and I know I will be waiting for someone. Someone that might just turn this night around.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI am here and typing and its 5:48am. I have officially been awake for twenty four hours as of ten minutes from now. I am pantsless and hot from tossing around with him. I need a fan, and a good nights sleep. I guess thereâ€™s time for that later. The hair around my face is flat against my head and the desk is shaking from my incessant leg wiggle. The sun will be rising soon and I should get to bed. He is there. My catharsis never comes but I do and so does he and I know things will look differently tomorrow when I have had time to sleep. I hope to lose my bitch on by then.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3687,'There is nothing like the buzzing silence of realizing that you have said or written something unforgivable.\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s white static and it only serves as a reminder of where you can never ever return to.\r\n\r\nWishing you hadn\\\'t said it only compromises things, because you always said it. They know it as well as you. \r\n\r\nNow, I guess I make some coffee-even though it\\\'s beyond late. It\\\'s nearly two. I\\\'m not dressed. The thought of leaving my home is paralyzing. Of actually having to force conversation...\r\n\r\nSo I\\\'ll take a nap instead.'),(3688,'<P><B>(Reporter asks what the album title refers to.)<BR>WAITS:  Rain dogs are the ones you see wanderin\\\' around after a rain.  Ones that can\\\'t find their way back home.  See, the rain washes off the scent off all the mail boxes and the lampposts, fire hydrants.</B></P><P>i\\\'m dreaming i\\\'m in a cafe with friends from the high school class below mine.  chet and dana and eric and merry and rob.  friends i\\\'ve lost track of or irreparably pissed off.  they\\\'re wearing those \\\"mofo senior\\\" t-shirts that they wore on their first day of school.  they got away with that for about five minutes.  eric told me about it that afternoon when he came out to hill \\\'n dale to buy some weed.  the twenty-three year-old woman i was living with late that summer was dealing pot.  i was waiting to go to college, thinking i was big stuff.</P><P>in the dream, nobody recognizes me.  i buy a bottle of veuve cliquot at the bar, take it outside, sit by the door and drink two thirds of it.  go inside and sell the remainder back to the bartender.  she slaps a seven-dollar bill down on the counter.  even in my dream state, i suspect that it <I>might not spend too good</I>.  i realize i have relapsed and get sad.  closing time comes, everybody leaves.  helicopter shot of the parting lot:  vast.  i see what\\\'s evidently my truck out at the far edge.  i crawl over a low-slung fence and move toward it.  the lock for the door\\\'s located on the wheel well.  i get in and put the key in the ignition.  it won\\\'t start.  i wake up.</P><P>four fifty-three.  seven minutes before the alarm.  what day is it?  friday.  i pull on some underwear, make the bed, do an hour of yoga on a blanket on the bedroom floor.  move into the living room, put on a clash mix cd, fast forward to <B><I>white man in hammersmith palais</I></B>.  (with that song and with <I><B>clampdown</I></B>, the intro is ten times better than the rest of the song, but the song is still good.  the hollies\\\' <I><B>long cool woman (in a black dress)</I></B> is like that, too.)  i eat some oatmeal, brush my teeth, shave, wet my bangs and fix them like i want them.  put on a sleeveless cotton t-shirt, a pair of cut-off sweatpants, and some reeboks.</P><P>jamie picks me up at six thirty.  we go to the ymca out by the highway and work with the free weights for about forty-five minutes.  i\\\'m not obsessed with my body anymore.  well, not <U>as</U> obsessed.  i don\\\'t want to be perfect anymore; i\\\'m content with having a nice body:  fit.  jamie\\\'s pretty much perfect:  he\\\'s all cut up, well-proportioned, got about thirty-five pounds on me.  now, i think he justs wants to get <U>big</U> as fuck.  i don\\\'t think that\\\'s attractive, though, being excessively muscular, and i need to tell him.</P><P>we change and do laps in the pool for half an hour.  (i swim rather than do the treadmill because i blew my right knee out fooling around [wrestling] with a teammate in may of my sophomore year of college.  running hurts.  cycling feels good, though.  it works the joint.  and slow squats are ok.)  we shower.  it\\\'s one of those open showers with lots of nozzles.  when there are other guys in there, jamie and i avoid looking at each other.  this morning, a latino guy comes in right after we do.  i check him out surreptitiously and catch jamie\\\'s attention and point over there with my eyes and a twitch of my head.  he looks at him then looks back at me, and we make the \\\"Impressive!\\\" face at each other.</P><P>jamie shaves and dresses for work.  i put on clean underwear and socks, a clean t-shirt and pair of shorts.  we drive back into town.  he parks in the public lot downtown, and i walk the four or five blocks back to my apartment.  jamie\\\'s a civil engineer, does consulting work.  christie (i know how her name is spelled now.  when i wrote the previous article in this series, i hadn\\\'t seen her name on a piece of mail or anything.  the only actual \\\"kristi\\\" i saw was on the clerk\\\'s nametag.  [and that\\\'s her name.  it isn\\\'t short for christine or anything.]) has a degree in american literature.  she taught a year of high school english after graduating.  she was teaching seniors, which was a mistake, because she was twenty-two, and they were seventeen.  there was some ugliness involving one of her male students, and it kind of soured the whole teaching thing for her.  there isn\\\'t much else you can do with an american lit degree besides teach, though, and she wants to do something with her life, so she\\\'s going to the university of south florida for her master\\\'s.  wants to teach college.  i ask her what happens when one of her students stalks her again, and she says that now she\\\'s got me and jamie to beat the hell out of him.  i point out that she had jamie <U>then</U>.  he was a wimp then, she says.  he was about that big around.  she holds up her pinky.  and the kid was a football player.  (i thought, wait \\\'til i see him.  i\\\'ll tell him christie told me about some kid bothering her that year she was teaching and why didn\\\'t you do anything about it?  see what he says.  i look over at her, at the place where her neck curves into her shoulder, the blinds laying ribbons of light and shadow across her neck, her face, the pillow.  we were in bed on a weekday afternoon, talking and listening to the oldies station.  i was about to turn the radio off and go to sleep.)  christie\\\'s twenty-seven; jamie\\\'s twenty-six.  they\\\'ve been together since their senior year of college.</P><P>breakfast:  soy protein shake, oatmeal, an orange.  morning meds:  geodon (decrease psychosis), lithium (stabilize mood), lamictal (stabilize mood), wellbutrin (decrease depression), buspar (decrease anxiety), levoxyl (replace thyroid hormone), full-spectrum multivitamin.  (you like that? - \\\"decrease,\\\" not \\\"eliminate.\\\"  i have to do other things to achieve balance:  a good sleep schedule, the right diet, exercise, yoga, try to avoid stress, don\\\'t drink, don\\\'t use, don\\\'t gamble, go to meetings.  other stuff.)</P><P>i watch channel ten until ten-thirty, at which juncture i drink another protein shake, eat an apple, load <I><B>substance:  the singles 1980-1987</B></I> into the discman, lock up the apartment, get the bike from where it\\\'s chained up around the side of the building, ride to main street, across the river, northwest up river road, past gloria swanson\\\'s old house, to avery drive, out to the highway to psychotherapeutic services of florida.  p.s.o.f. provides me with supportive case management.  they\\\'re funded by the state and are charged with trying to keep the mentally ill out of the hospital.  (case management costs medicaid much less than hospitalization.)</P><P>i go to substance abuse group therapy on fridays.  everybody meets at the p.s.o.f. office, and we take a van out to green key beach and talk for awhile around a picnic table.  everyone in the group is mentally ill and chemically dependent.  that\\\'s called a dual diagnosis.  i\\\'m just about the highest-functioning of the group\\\'s members.  that isn\\\'t saying much, but i can pretty much pass for normal.  people who don\\\'t know me (don\\\'t know my history) can\\\'t tell i\\\'m mentally ill.  and i hope to move beyond that label someday.  like \\\"alcoholic\\\" or \\\"addict\\\" or \\\"compulsive gambler\\\" (or \\\"normal\\\" [ha.]), it implies that you\\\'re fundamentally flawed, not a whole person.</P><P>bike home, get there a little after one.  check the mail:  electric bill.  ninety-seven dollars.  jeez.  it\\\'s a small apartment, but i\\\'ve got two window a/c units that eat up the juice.  i get $584/month in disability checks and $117 in food stamps.  the checks go to my sister; she handles my money.  the rent\\\'s four hundred.  p.s.o.f. pays $250 of that.  then there\\\'s electricity, phone, water/sewer, garbage, gym membership.  and the food stamps run out 2/3 of the way into the month.  plus, i\\\'m on probation for pot possession, so that\\\'s $120/month for cost of supervision, court costs, and a drug test.  also need money for soap, etc., toilet paper, laundry, bus fare, air for the bike tires....  what there is no money for:  eating in restaurants, movies, cds, books, cable, internet.  i go to the library for books and net access.  i have a tv which picks up one station, the local cbs affiliate.  i have an old computer that i use for word processing.  a guy from a.a. is going to give me a used vcr so i can watch videos i check out from the library.</P><P>p.s.o.f. is going to pay a company from tarpon springs to polish my systems engineering skills.  in addition, they\\\'ll teach me how to repair computers and about routers and switches, etc.  i got my mcse four years ago but never worked in the field, really.  i don\\\'t remember much about the coursework.  and then once i get retrained, my felony conviction is going to be a barrier to employment.  i\\\'m not going to worry too much about it, though.</P><P>lunch:  protein shake (the reason i\\\'m consuming all this fucking protein is that i lost 30 pounds [virtually all of it muscle because i didn\\\'t have much bodyfat] late last year/earlier this year in a matter of weeks, and i\\\'ve been trying to gain it again.), leftover cous-cous and vegetable tagine (chickpeas, carrots, zucchini, green peppers), tomato/cucumber/black olive/basil salad, a pear.  lunch meds.</P><P>i put five cds in the changer, on shuffle, find my blue notebook, write for four hours, switch the cds hourly.</P><P>first, i listen to:<BR><I><B>live through this<BR>blue<BR>the scream<BR>pod<BR>exile in guyville</B></I></P><P>then:<BR><I><B>the pretenders<BR>the kinks are the village green preservation society<BR>shoot out the lights<BR>los angeles<BR>daydream nation</B></I></P><P>then:<BR><I><B>marcus garvey<BR>the secret life of plants<BR>return of the boom bap<BR>cosmic tones for mental therapy/art forms of dimensions tomorrow<BR>america eats its young</B></I></P><P>lastly:<BR><B><I>the rise and fall of ziggy stardust and the spiders from mars<BR>reckoning<BR>zen arcade<BR>nothing\\\'s schocking<BR>berlin</I></B></P><P>the writing goes slowly.  i keep going back, fixing stuff, when i should just plow ahead and finish it.  i have an outline; i know exactly what happens.  i can contemplate it in its ideal state, but the translation into reality is messy.  i think i\\\'m making it harder than it is.  i find i have to write every day, make it part of my metabolism, or i\\\'ll end up not doing it for weeks.  and creativity makes endorphins, so you have to adjust for that.  i do yoga after i write to cool out.  the novel\\\'s going to be good if i can ever finish it, which, at the rate i\\\'m going, should take another twenty years or so.</P><P>at five-thirty, i do half an hour of yoga, drink a protein shake, eat an apple then bike through downtown, behind the restaurant i washed dishes for a few years ago, past the kung fu school where i want to take qixong lessons when i have the money, down grand boulevard to the church which is hosting tonight\\\'s a.a. meeting.  (my weekly schedule:  monday - gamblers anonymous, tuesday - dual recovery anonymous [for those with a dual diagnosis], wednesday - a.a. men\\\'s meeting, thursday - aftercare at the rehab, friday - a.a. speakers meeting, saturday - narcotics anonymous, sunday - narcotics anonymous.  all meetings are in the evening.)</P><P>as i said, tonight\\\'s meeting features two speakers, one of whom is ed, who happens to be one of the techs at the treatment center.  he tells of being homeless in new orleans, having a moment of clarity and finding an a.a. meeting.  when he got there, he had one shoe and shitty pants.  one of the guys asked how he was.  he said he was just fine.  the guy said that from where he was standing, ed looked like he was anything but fine.  and so he began his journey back to health.</P><P>i reflect on what brought me to my current situation.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>new year\\\'s eve.  i\\\'d been holed up at the budget inn since before christmas, going on the casino boat daily or nightly, winning almost four thousand altogether.  on the 26th, i\\\'d given sri, the motel owner, $500 for thirty days.  i had seen christie and jamie on christmas night and on the nights of the twenty-sixth through the twenty-ninth.  during this time, i was smoking pot and drinking a litle beer.  when i didn\\\'t have anything else to do, i\\\'d go to the library downtown.  i used their computers until i got my laptop out of hock two days after i\\\'d put it in.  it was still configured for my parents\\\' isp.  all i had to do was find a local dial-up number.</P><P>on the thirtieth, in the afternoon, i started smoking freebase with a prostitute i would know only as velvet.  by midnight, we\\\'d been to the dope man\\\'s twice and spent $900.  i had about $2,800 left.</P><P>new year\\\'s eve.  we had had sex once when she arrived the previous afternoon.  nothing since then.  we run out at around ten in the morning on the thirty-first and have to wait until after one for her dope man to answer his phone.  we\\\'ve been doing some of the oxycontins she had brought with her.  just to stay evened out a little.  we drove down to tarpon springs to get the coke and came back to the room and cooked it with ammonia to get the impurities out.  the first hit is so good i almost puke.  immediately, i want another hit.  we run out around nine-thirty that night.  she wants to go get more, but i tell her it\\\'s very important to me to spend new year\\\'s eve with my friends, and all i want to do for the rest of the night is smoke weed and drink.  i take two of her oxys.  i give her some money, and she calls somebody to pick her up.  she\\\'s been awol from the escort service for about thirty hours.  the - dispatcher? - madam? - will not be pleased.</P><P>i had about an eightball hidden in the toilet tank.  i just did bumps off of that, trying to maintain a nice, even buzz.  smoked a couple of joints and got ready to go out.  locked up the room, the last room of the motel.  (the office was at the other end.)  walked north up u.s. 19, over the bridge across the pithlachascotee river (affectionately known as \\\"the cotee\\\"), people down there fishing on the bank by the pilings, a little girl playing in the dirt as her father fished.</P><P>jamie answered the door.</P><P>i was like, \\\"hey.\\\"</P><P>he was like, \\\"uh, hey (really sarcastic).  where you been?  maybe i don\\\'t wanna know, huh?\\\"</P><P>me:  whadda ya mean?</P><P>him:  do you have any idea what you look like?</P><P>me:  huh?</P><P>him:  go look in a mirror.  get outta here.  go home.  go wherever it is that you go.</P><P>i went to the gay bar.  danced with a cute blond guy, did bumps in the bathroom with him.  touched each other\\\'s pee-pees.  a shot of vodka - a large tumbler full.  very fucked up.  another shot.  black out before midnight.  i don\\\'t remember a countdown, anyway.</P><P>merged back into consciousness in a jail cell.  never a good way to start your day.  i was alone.  there were some papers on the aluminum bench next to me that said i had been arrested for possession of marijuana (11.4 grams) (a misdemeanor) and possession of drug paraphernalia (rolling papers) (also a misdemeanor).  the box on the arrest report marked OCCUPATION said \\\"MOVIE PRODUCER\\\".  just like that, with the quotation marks, like they didn\\\'t believe me.  (ha.)  the report said i had been stopped, on foot, near the intersection of grand and tennessee on suspicion of auto burglary.  (god knows what i was doing.)  search revealed pot and papers.  no mention of cocaine.  no mention of my car.</P><P>the other piece of paper is an inventory sheet.  what was taken from you.  clothes and effects.  says i checked into the hotel with twenty-six cents.  no mention of the roughly twenty-two hundred american dollars that should have been with it.</P><P>they come by looking in the window at me every once in a while.  once they decide i\\\'m ok, i guess, they put me in with some other knuckleheads.  a couple of hours later, we get marched down to a courtroom where, by remote hookup, a judge starts letting people out on their own recognizance left and right.  me, too.  i guess there wasn\\\'t much room at the inn.  <U>some</U> relief, anyway.  not much.</P><P>it took a couple of hours to get processed for release.  in the interim, they gave us lunch:  a baloney sandwich, a hard boiled egg, an apple, milk.  then they gave me back my clothes and posssessions:  wallet with driver\\\'s license, social security card, auto insurance card, prepaid calling card; twenty-six cents; car keys; motel room key; cheap digital watch.  there were two payphones in the jail lobby.  seeing as how i didn\\\'t have fifty cents, i was going to use my calling card to get a taxi to take me back into town, many (i don\\\'t know how many) miles away, then jump out once it got near the motel.  the phone blocked my card, though.  just as well - probably would have ended up making a round trip back to jail.  i walked it.  i headed for downtown.  once there, i started looking in the area around grand and tennessee and found my car in ten minutes.  when i got back to the motel, it was around five-thirty.</P><P>took a shower.  vaguely hungry, not wanting to think about my situation, i fell asleep.  awakened by knocking at the door.  after nine.  velvet.  told her what happened.  using junkie ingenuity, it didn\\\'t take us long to figure out what to do.</P><P>we cleaned out the car:  blanket, cds, tool kit, maps, beanie baby monkey my niece gave me that was sitting in the back window.  put everything in the room.  saved all the word and notepad files i wanted to keep to disk then deleted them from the hard drive.  i called kiki.  luckily, velvet had a little money, so we were able to get some gas and some mcdonald\\\'s.  drove to brooksville, to kiki\\\'s fortress on school street, off of martin luther king, jr. boulevard.  after some haggling and blunt-smoking, in a living room full of brothers with hard eyes, i traded my car and laptop for a pound of cocaine, two ziploc bags of oxys and five hundred in cash, mostly fives and tens.  kiki had one of his associates, a young man named terrence, drive us back to new port richey.  he brought along a too short cd.  i don\\\'t think velvet liked it.  we stopped for gas, and i tried to talk terrence into paying for it, told him kiki would reimburse him. no dice.  we got into the coke on the way home, doing bumps off the corner of my driver\\\'s license.  a couple of months later, a woman i was in rehab with who also used to buy from kiki heard from her boyfriend, who was in jail in hernando county, that kiki was in the boyfriend\\\'s cell block, headed for prison.  i wonder if they confiscated my car.</P><P>that was the night of the first.</P><P>we attempted a semblance of routine and normality the first couple of days.  the afternoon of the second, we ran out of bottled water.  the stuff from the tap was brownish, and we had powerful thirsts.  since we were running low on ammonia, too, we took a cab to the nearest drugstore, down the highway at main street.  we bought all the lighters they had, got two gallons of milk and some dixie cups.  (a little milk, occaisonally, was the only nourishment i had for the next ten days.)  i dragged us into the internet cafe in the strip mall behind the drugstore to use the computer for a second, both of us going into the bathrooms every five minutes either to do bumps or with the shits.  before too long, we sensed the staff and other patrons knew something was up and were probably about to confront us.  we repaired to the front of the drugstore and called another cab.</P><P>that night (cnn was constantly on.  no other channels.), i got an attack of the shits.  velvet was in the bathroom.  i banged on the door.  she was like, \\\"hold on.\\\"  i was like, \\\"you need to hurry up.\\\"  she didn\\\'t come out, and she didn\\\'t come out.  i was about to lose it.  i tried the knob:  it was unlocked.  i went in there, and she was shooting up.  i got on the john and was like, \\\"what are you doin\\\'?  oxys?\\\"  she was.</P><P>i\\\'d sworn i\\\'d never use intravenously again, but, at that point, my resistance to temptation was pretty low.  she had two rigs; we each used one.  they probably got mixed up, but she had a water bottle half-full of bleach, and i made sure i used it every time.</P><P>not too long after that (it\\\'s hard to pinpoint dates and times.  it was just one big burst.  days and nights came and went.), i barricaded myself in the bathroom, taking all the drugs and equipment in there with me.  put towels down on the shower floor and sat on them, naked.  (i stayed naked.  it didn\\\'t have anything to do with sex; sex was the furthest thing from my mind.  when i did a lot of coke, i became really sensitive to anything touching my skin, and clothes scratched.  they drove me crazy.)  the boombox was in there, playing <I><B>beggar\\\'s banquet, let it bleed, sticky fingers, exile on main street</I></B> on constant repeat.  (stones is what the troopers listen to.)  i made her come in there with me when she wanted to smoke or fire some oxys.  she had clothes on.  she had a hooters t-shirt on.  claimed she used to waitress there.  then came a dancing gig at lollipops, a titty bar up near the county line.  then she was at club 54, a downmarket titty bar.  then she started working for the escort service.  and, now, she was embarking on her new career as a crack whore.  and really, she wasn\\\'t even whoring, strictly speaking, since there had only been that one time before we started smoking.  and that crossed my mind.  and i looked around and saw the supply dwindling and figured out it would last longer if there was one less body to process it.</P><P>we were in the bathroom.  i told her our arrangement was finished and she needed to leave.  she was like, \\\"what?\\\"  i got up and stood between her and the drugs, and i was like, \\\"get your shit and get out.\\\" i took a step toward her and she backed up into the main room.  she got her purse from the table.  she opened the door and turned around and said, \\\"i\\\'m going to call the police on you.\\\"  i said, \\\"go ahead.\\\"  she said, \\\"ok, then, give me some oxys.  and my works and my spoon.  and my bleach.\\\"  i said, \\\"fuck off.\\\"  my jeans were hanging over a chair near the door, with my shirt in the seat.  (no underwear, though.)  she grabbed the jeans and took off.  i started after her then realized i was naked and ran back into the room and tore into the duffel bag looking for shorts or pants or underwear.  i should have kept going.  by the time i pulled on a pair of shorts and got outside, i couldn\\\'t see her.  i ran behind the convenience store.  she wasn\\\'t back there.  i went inside the store and asked the clerk, an older guy, if a blonde-headed girl had come in.  said she hadn\\\'t.  said i couldn\\\'t be in there with no shirt on and bare feet.  i checked the women\\\'s restroom, anyway.  i ran back outside and checked behind the strip mall next to the store.  nothing.  i figured she was gone by then and went back to the room.</P><P>it\\\'s just as well:  if i had caught her, i might have battered her.  i don\\\'t hit women, but, in that state, mad as i was, i might have.  and if i had done it out by the highway, some good samaritan would have stopped, and, between the nudity, the mutual assault, the theft, the prostitution they would have inferred from the money and the nudity and the motel room, the public intoxication, the drugs, and the paraphernalia, i can come up with thirty or so charges off of the top of my head that we would have collected between us.</P><P>i felt a slight twinge of pain when i thought about the money (over $400), but i looked at all the hits there were left to be had and forgot about it.  of the driver\\\'s license, etc., i thought not at all.</P><P>i secured the main door and stayed in the bathroom for many days.  after a little while, i started seeing people come out of the walls.  among other things.  that stopped, and i kept on.  it would come back, then go away, and, after awhile, it was constant.  i didn\\\'t shower or shave or brush my teeth.  i went out into the other room and looked in the mirror:  i was beyond cut.  you could see all the striations of muscle.  the ribs were prominent; the cheeks were sunken.</P><P>the oxys ran out first.  the cocaine anesthetized any withdrawal symptoms i might have had.  then the coke ran out.  i crashed and slept for what felt like a very long time.  it was like one of those supersonic sleeps you have after you trip.  times ten.</P><P>it took me a while to wake up.  when i finally did, i sat on the side of the bed for half an hour, trying to get it together.  i found my watch:  three in the morning on the 12th.</P><P>i turn on the tv.  cnn.  sit on the end of the bed and watch.  get up and get the remaining gallon of milk out of the mini-fridge, open it up and smell it.  pour some in a dixie cup and smell it.  decide against drinking any.  sit back on the bed and look around, a quick inventory:  duffle bag with clothes and toiletries; backpack with books, cds, 3 1/2 inch disks, a notebook; boombox; tool kit; drug equipment in the bathroom.  what i don\\\'t have:  drugs, money, medication (hadn\\\'t taken any since leaving the assisted living facility at the beginning of december), food.</P><P>there\\\'s nobody i can turn to.  family doesn\\\'t want anything to do with me.  lovers rejected me.  no friends.</P><P>there isn\\\'t anything left.</P><P>when you do a lot of cocaine, then stop suddenly, the withdrawal does something to your neurotransmitters and your pleasure centers.  you can\\\'t stop crying.  deep, deep depression.  i tell myself it isn\\\'t real, that it\\\'s just chemicals in your brain, but it doesn\\\'t do any good when you feel like that.</P><P>watch cnn.  can\\\'t stop crying.  sun comes up.</P><P>can\\\'t stop crying.</P><P>can\\\'t stop crying.</P><P>the crying stops.  i turn off the tv and look around the room.  the curtains block out the morning light.  i sit on the floor and think for a long time.</P><P><I>i want out.<BR>i want off the boat.</I></P><P>(we learned a little bit about jung in rehab.  aside from supposedly being the first clinician to propose that perhaps the only cure to addiction is a spiritual one, he was known for putting forth a theory about the duality of man, how we have light and dark sides that are in balance in a healthy person, but not so in an addict.  in addiction, the dark side takes over; chaos reigns.  the light side is overcome by the neverending shitrain of misery and destruction the dark side is causing.  suicide becomes the last desperate act of the light side in its effort to save the whole.  death becomes preferable to the suffering.)</P><P>i straighten the room up, make the bed, pack the appropriate items into the backpack.  brush my teeth, shower, shave, put on clean clothes.  put the dirty clothes that are lying around into a garbage bag i have for that purpose.  gather up all the drug equipment (except for one of the needles and the bleach ) and put it in the garbage can; take the can outside and empty it in the dumpster.</P><P>go over to the convenience store.  the clerk is involuntarily taken aback when she sees me.  her eyes get big, and she jumps a little.  it only lasts a second.  i\\\'m like, \\\"kristi, lemme get a rolling rock.  i just want to taste it.  please.\\\"  she\\\'s like, \\\"get the hell outta here.\\\"  me:  either you let me have it, or i\\\'ll just take it.  her:  you\\\'ll go to jail, too.  i go back to the cooler, get a six-pack, haul ass out the door, run behind the store, behind the motel, circle around the far end and back to the room.  she has no idea that i\\\'m staying at the motel.  the cash register is situated so that you can\\\'t see the direction the customer came from.</P><P>i drink all six beers in about twenty minutes then dig around in the pocket of the backpack and come up with three quarters.  throw the bottles and the cardboard container in the dumpster and walk up the highway, across the river to a philly cheese steak place with a payphone outside.  no phonebook.  great.  go inside and ask to use theirs.  the guy throws it up on the counter.  \\\"knock y\\\'self out.\\\"  find the number for the hospital.  repeat it to self until memorized.  \\\"you gonna order anything?\\\"  \\\"no.\\\"</P><P>ask to speak to a doctor or nurse.  on hold for seven minutes.  the counterman comes out and looks at me then goes back in.  a woman\\\'s voice finally.  \\\"what would happen if i injected bleach into one of my veins?\\\"  got the answer i want.</P><P>back in the room, i put on <I><B>astral weeks</I></B>.  it\\\'s just about my favorite album, i guess.  i sit on the end of the bed and listen.  it\\\'s the first time i\\\'m trying this.  i\\\'ve thought about doing it, threatened to do it, but never tried it.  i have a sense of detachment about the whole thing, like it isn\\\'t really happening.  when <B><I>the way young lovers do</B></I> comes on, i get some water in a dixie cup and bring it to the table.  fill the syringe half full of water, half bleach. so it won\\\'t blow the vein out and short-circuit the procedure.  don\\\'t bother checking for air bubbles.  pick up the needle and sit on the bed.</P><P><B><I>madame george</B></I> comes on.  george ivan morrison (that\\\'s who <A HREF=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2796\\\">marisa\\\'s son</A> is named after.) singing about a drag queen.  when the song\\\'s over, i raise the needle (by the way, do you like how this is called \\\"home\\\" yet categorized as \\\"travel\\\"?  i knew you would.  i\\\'m writing about spiritual travel, not physical.  and that\\\'s how i justify <I><U>that</I></U>) and stick it in that big, ropy vein that goes around the outside of your forearm (if you can\\\'t see it - trust me:  it\\\'s there.) and push the plunger in.  it BURNS going in.</P><P>immediately, i feel excruciating pain up my arm and in my chest then in my stomach.  i jump off the bed and stagger to the door, unlock it, open it, go outside, head toward the office (no phone in room).  wave of pain throughout my body.  i collapse.  later, i learn somebody on the highway saw me go down and pulled into the street past the motel.  if it weren\\\'t for them....  people around me.  loud voices.  turns out the needle\\\'s still in my arm.  ambulance, paramedics, police.  fading in and out of consciousness.  questions shouted at me about drugs.  can\\\'t talk.  they inject me with something.  i manage to say, \\\"BLEACH!\\\" as loud as i can.  in the ambulance.  emergency room.  people around me.  woman in a white coat.  the woman says are you the one who called earlier and asked what would happen if you injected yourself with bleach.  i croak, \\\"yeah.\\\"  she says, \\\"nice goin\\\', asshole.\\\"</P><P>asleep.  somebody grabs my ankle and twists it like they\\\'re trying to grind the bones together.  simultaneously, i jerk my leg back and open my eyes.  it\\\'s jamie.  \\\"motherFUCKer,\\\" i call him.  i\\\'m in the hospital, on a heart monitor.  \\\"hey.\\\"  \\\"hey.  well, i called your dad.\\\"  \\\"who didja tell \\\'im you were?\\\"  \\\"i said i was a friend of yours.\\\" \\\"yeah.  real good friend.  thass alright:  he knows.\\\"  \\\"well, to start with, i told \\\'im you were alright.  then i told \\\'im what happened.\\\"  \\\"what\\\'d he say?\\\"  \\\"he wasn\\\'t surprised.  we talked for a pretty long time.  (pause)  do you know how many times you\\\'ve been in the hospital since you moved back to florida?\\\"  \\\"quite a few.\\\" \\\"eightteen.  your mom\\\'s been keeping track.  she\\\'s got it written down somewhere.  all of \\\'em for either being so drunk the police thought you were a danger to yourself or for threatening to kill yourself.  i got news for ya:  they weren\\\'t surprised.  they wrote you off a long time ago.  your dad said he\\\'s been expecting a call from the police saying you were dead for years now.\\\"  i start crying.  \\\"listen:  cryin\\\'s not gonna help anything.  what you\\\'ve been doing... for how long?  how long\\\'s it been goin\\\' on?\\\"  \\\"since i was sixteen.\\\"  \\\"sixteen.  that\\\'s more than half your life.  you\\\'ve been driving these people completely crazy for close to twenty years.  because they love you so much, and they don\\\'t know how to help you.  they\\\'ve done everything they can think of, and none of it\\\'s worked.  listen to me:  you.  have.  to.  change.  i wouldn\\\'t be telling you all this if i didn\\\'t care about you.  i wouldn\\\'t be down here if i didn\\\'t care about you.  so don\\\'t cry.  you\\\'ve got a lot of people on your side.  everything\\\'s gonna get better from here on out.\\\"</P><P>he said my family would come see me.  they didn\\\'t.  i was in the hospital for three days, in the psych ward for eight, and rehab for 89, and they didn\\\'t visit me once.  christie and jamie visited me in all three places.  during my time in the treatment center, they came every sunday and wednesday and stayed at least a couple of hours.  it was during these visits and the phone conversations we had that i really got to know them.  i didn\\\'t know them that well before.</P><P>i don\\\'t know why my family didn\\\'t come.  maybe they didn\\\'t want to see me in that environment.  maybe they didn\\\'t want to admit to themselves that i was in rehab.  (i don\\\'t see why not, since they already had seen me in jail and in the psych ward.  you would think they would look at rehab as something positive.  maybe it was all just so distasteful.)  i can see my sister not wanting to have to explain to my neice what was wrong with me.  paige doesn\\\'t know i was in jail or that i\\\'m crazy or have drug and alcohol problems.  i don\\\'t want her to know.  she worships the ground i walk on, and i spend a lot of time with her, when i can.  her father left her and my sister and rarely sees her.  i think she sees me as a father figure, and i don\\\'t want to ruin that for her.  when i was in the adult living facility, morgan told her it was my apartment.</P><P>rehab was alright.  it was stressful, but it was something that had to be done.  the thing i remember most fondly was a conversation i had with tim, one of the techs, also a recovering alcoholic.  he was what they call a low-bottom drunk.  when he reached his bottom, he was drinking sterno, etc.  i think my bottom was pretty low, too.  anyway, he said recovery would change everything for me, that there would be bad days, but, on the whole, things would be more good than bad.  he said, \\\"get on the boat, and we\\\'ll go for a ride.\\\"  i was so proud of myself for sticking it out.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>christie and jamie pick me up in jamie\\\'s navigator.  i put my bike in back, and we go to a steak place.  they\\\'re both carnivores.  i get the salad bar and a baked potato.  i make the salad heavy on the garbanzo beans, which i had at lunch, but i don\\\'t mind.  when the potato comes, i mash it up with my fork and take it up to the salad bar, pour olive oil on it, go back to the table and salt it.  jamie goes, \\\"what the hell is that?\\\"  \\\"potato, olive oil, salt.\\\"  \\\"you\\\'re weird.\\\"  \\\"yeah, but i\\\'m cute, though.\\\" the bill comes.  as he reaches for his wallet to leave the tip, he says, only half-jokingly, \\\"i wish you\\\'d kick in something every once in awhile.\\\"  i\\\'m like, \\\"whadda you want?  some government cheese?\\\"  of which i have none, but would be eligible for, should some come along.</P><P>we get back to my apartment a little before nine.  there\\\'s a truck in my driveway.  i tell jamie to park on the street.  there doesn\\\'t appear to be anyone around who belongs to the truck.  there are some tools on the cement in front of it.  we go in, and i wait for christie to use the bathroom then use it myself.  i\\\'m about to call the landlord about the truck when there\\\'s a knock at the door.  guy about my age.  asks me, \\\"did you see the beer i left by my tools?\\\"  \\\"what?!\\\"  \\\"there was a twelve-pack  right over there.\\\"  and i thought, \\\"jesus christ.  this is all i need:  somebody leaving beer in my driveway.\\\"  \\\"no.  i haven\\\'t seen it.  we just got here, and there wasn\\\'t any beer over there.  by the way, who the hell are you, and what are you doing here?\\\"  \\\"i\\\'m doing some work for mike.\\\"  mike\\\'s the landlord.  he goes, \\\"did you see a guy with a beard?  maybe he took it.\\\"  \\\"like i said, we just got here.  i didn\\\'t see anybody here when we rolled up.\\\"</P><P>about that time, a car pulls in behind the truck and a guy with a beard and a cracked-out-looking woman get out holding beers.  soon, the first guy has a beer, too, and they\\\'re all walking around the yard, going around the side of the building, etc.</P><P>i go back inside and call the landlord and ask him why there are people here outside my apartment drinking beer at nine o\\\'clock at night.  he lives a block away.  he comes over, and they finish what they\\\'re doing (moving a washer that had been sitting in front of the apartment since i moved in.  the city told the landlord to move it or pay a fine.) and leave.  (this landlord is a piece of work, by the way.  he\\\'s a short little gimpy bald italian man.  when i moved in, i had an agreement with p.s.o.f. that i would pay first, last, and security out of the disability checks that had been accumulating since the beginning of the year [going to my parents\\\' address], and they would pay the next two months rent.  when they gave me the first check meant for him, they included with it an i.r.s. form for him to fill out with his tax i.d. number or social security number.  he didn\\\'t mail it back to them, and when they called to find out why, he told them he hadn\\\'t filed income tax in thirty years and didn\\\'t want the government to know he had rental income.  well, at least i have something to hold over his head should it come down to it.)</P><P>so jamie looks through the cds, and i\\\'m seeing what\\\'s on channel ten, and christie\\\'s looking through the refrigerator.  she finds some orange juice and pours a glass.  jamie holds up a cd.  big star.  <I><B>third/sister lovers</B></I>.  the rykodisc release, the one with the people lying on the beach in a pattern that looks like the sun.  he puts it on.  christie sits down on the couch and checks out the tv.  <B><I>big brother</I></B>.  there are two hot guys on the show:  scott and drew.  we comment on this.  one hot girl:  diane.  jamie leans over the back of the recliner and watches.  he drifts off toward the bedroom.  christie and i look at each other, and she reaches over and turns the tv off.  i stand up and go over to her and help her up.  we hold hands and walk to the bedroom, letting go when we get to the doorway.  jamie\\\'s sitting on the bed, taking his shoes off.  i have difficutly getting one leg out of my shorts and almost lose balance.</P><P>i won\\\'t give you the play-by-play.  i\\\'m trying to be a little more subtle these days.  (but i reserve the right to get nasty.  should i absolutely have to.)  i\\\'m trying to learn how to write erotica, not pornography.  i\\\'m reading <I><B>the art of love</I></B> by ovid.  (advice for men on how to woo women.  and vice versa.)  unspeakably erotic.  but very tasteful.</P><P>i will tell you about the music, though.  they\\\'re deadheads.  conservative in appearance, but bona fide.  and they\\\'re very picky in their tastes.  they\\\'ll want to listen to the dead or some band like them, like phish or widespread panic.  (they thought i was the balls and berries there for a little while when i told them i met dave schools, widespread panic\\\'s bassist, while i was interning for john keane.)  there are other bands, but i forget their names.  and they like reggae.  but that\\\'s it.  nothing else.  i mean, i like the dead just as much as anyone, but i don\\\'t listen to it all. the fucking. time.  that\\\'s what i listen to when i\\\'m at their house.  and jamie\\\'s got all these bootlegs, so they never run out of material.  (they smoke a lot of pot, too.  i\\\'ve asked them not to do it around me and to not be stoned around me.  i see it in their little eyes, though, sometimes.)  (one thing i picked up from all the dead music recently that i had never noticed before was that line in <I><B>truckin\\\'</I></B> that goes \\\"hang it up and see what tomorrow brings.\\\"  that\\\'s a good philosophy to have on hand when you have a bad day.)</P><P>when we\\\'re at my apartment, i try to expose them to other music.  much of it they don\\\'t like.  albums that we all like:  <B><I>gp/grievous angel, the gilded palace of sin, music from big pink, the basement tapes, john wesley harding, third/sister lovers, dusty in memphis, in the aeroplane over the sea, yankee foxtrot hotel.</I></B>  and reggae.  that\\\'s what we listen to over here.  mostly.  i put on <B><I>indeterminancy</B></I> one night.  they deemed it trippy, but said you couldn\\\'t fuck to it, then went and put something else on.  hey, i thought, you can\\\'t fuck to <I><B>aoxomoxoa</B></I>, either, but i manage.</P><P>anyway, business was transacted, and all the troops were tuckered, and everybody was snoozin\\\', and it was still early in the night, since christie hadn\\\'t rolled me over yet because of my snoring.  (she\\\'s a light sleeper.  jamie\\\'s like me:  they could drop the bomb, and we would sleep through it.)  there was a knock at the door.  i checked the clock:  11:20.  tried to find my shorts in the dark, got them on, went to the door.  two guys, early twenties.  they were both about jamie\\\'s size.  maybe a little bigger.  the one in the hat said, \\\"is tyler here?\\\"  i said, \\\"i don\\\'t know any tyler.\\\"  \\\"i saw his truck here tonight.\\\"  \\\"oh, that guy with the truck?  no, they left.\\\"  \\\"when did he leave?\\\"  \\\"about ten o\\\'clock, i guess.\\\"  \\\"what was he doin\\\' here?\\\"  \\\"he was doin\\\' some work for the landlord.\\\"  \\\"what kind of work was he doin\\\'?\\\"  \\\"they were movin\\\' a washin\\\' machine.  and he was doin\\\' somethin\\\' in that room around the corner back there.\\\"  \\\"where was he gonna go when he left?\\\"  \\\"i don\\\'t know.  the bottom line is, he\\\'s not here now, so i\\\'m gonna hafta bid you gentlemen goodnight.\\\"  \\\"don\\\'t make me whip your ass.\\\"  \\\"yeah?\\\"  \\\"yeah.\\\"  i step back and get the aluminum bat i keep behind the door and go back out on the stoop.  \\\"oh, look at the pussy gotta pick somethin\\\' up.  you better put that down before we take it away from ya and put it up your ass.\\\"  i step toward him and say, \\\"c\\\'mon.  get some.  getchoo some.\\\"  we stand there for a second eyeing each other.  jamie appears in the doorway in a pair of boxer-briefs.  immediately, they figure it out and start going, \\\"c\\\'mon on, ya punk-ass faggots.  i say, \\\"if i\\\'m a faggot, then a faggot\\\'s gonna cave your skull in tonight.\\\"  jamie steps out beside me.  the taunts go back and forth.  it looked like it was going to be a battle royale like they have on wrestling sometimes.  no, i guess a battle royale is every man for himself.  it was gonna be me and jamie and The Bat versus the young\\\'uns.  i felt our chances were pretty good.  The Bat was going to be the equalizer.  we were out there about thirty seconds more, just talking trash, nobody wanting to make the first move, when something behind me captures their attention.  it\\\'s christie.  she\\\'s wearing a t-shirt that goes down to her upper thigh.  they appear momentarily transfixed.  now they don\\\'t know what\\\'s going on, if we\\\'re all roommates or what.  i say to her, \\\"call the police.\\\"  i turn to the young men:  \\\"we can stand here talkin\\\' shit \\\'til the cops come if ya\\\'ll want.  or you can take your pussy asses home.  <U>ooooorrrrr</U>, you can come get some.  why don\\\'tcha come get some, you little bitch?\\\"  i was really wishing he\\\'d make a move, because, while i abhor violence and try to avoid it whenever possible, when it\\\'s time to swell a motherfucker up, it\\\'s time.  they walked off toward the apartment complex across the street, calling us names.  we went inside and closed the door.  christie was looking through the blinds.  i ask, \\\"did you call the police?\\\"  \\\"not yet.\\\"  \\\"how long were you gonna wait?  \\\'til they jumped us?\\\"  \\\"i knew you guys\\\'d be alright.\\\"  in retrospect, we probably would have gotten our asses stomped.  i don\\\'t imagine jamie\\\'s much of a fighter.</P><P>i call the landlord and tell him what happened, and he says he\\\'ll talk to tyler about it.  i\\\'m sweating.  i go in the bathroom and wipe off with a towel.  we go back to bed, but i can\\\'t sleep because i\\\'ve got this huge adrenaline pump, so i just lay there and watch them sleep, bathed in the light coming in the window.  i\\\'m on the outside, jamie\\\'s in the middle, christie\\\'s over  by the wall.  jamie talks in his sleep.  he goes, \\\"ten nine eight seven six five four three two one.  NASTY!\\\"  does that twice.  then later on, something about \\\"the meteor in the morning.\\\"  then, \\\"my lawyer\\\'s out of town.\\\"  around four, christie gets up to go to the bathroom.  i tell jamie he talks in his sleep, and he asks what i was doing up.  christie comes back, and jamie decides he has to go.  i decide i might as well, too.  i follow him in there and show him how to make a cross with the streams, like my friend ben and i used to do at age five in the nursery bathroom at church.  something really erotic about peeing with another man.  we all get back in bed, and i turn off the alarm and get up at five-thirty, close the bedroom door behind me, wait for dawn, which will occur when the list of sunrise times i got from the internet says it will.  when the clock on the stove says it\\\'s time, i switch out the eggplant that\\\'s on the altar on top of the fridge with the fresh one that\\\'s been <I>in</I> the fridge.  requisite prayers, supplication, praise, chants, thanksgiving, hidden invective, light the candles, put out the candles.  this has to be done every three days.  i put on a pair of tennis shoes and walk the old eggplant up the street, over to central, down to the circular man-made lake in the middle of town, close my eyes, and throw it in.  turn around, open my eyes, walk home.  i don\\\'t know if they sink or float.  i go down there only at dawn, and i never see any in the water.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>this relationship i\\\'m in has its ups and downs.  it\\\'s exponentially more complicated than it would be if there were only two people involved.  first, you\\\'ve got the relationship itself, the big one, the three of us.  we all get along fine.  however, within that triad, there are three smaller relationships:  jamie and christie, christie and i, jamie and i.  whoever is the odd man out in these pairings gets jealous of the other two being together.  we should be above that, but it happens.  it\\\'s a natural reaction.  case in point:  i was over at their house a few weeks ago watching the yankees-red sox game with jamie.  christie was at the library at u.s.f. and was going to pick up dinner on the way home.  jamie\\\'s laying on the couch with his head in my lap.  i\\\'m playing with his hair.  he\\\'s got brownish-blond hair, parted on the side, average conservative haircut.  it\\\'s the top of the third; the red sox are trailing 3-0.  currently on the mound for boston is bronson arroyo of brooksville, f-l-a.  (younger brother of serenity arroyo, the critically acclaimed fellatio enthusiast.  hi, serenity.)  i explain the hometown connection to jamie.  \\\"big fuckin\\\' deal.\\\"  i flick his earlobe.  yankee\\\'s third baseman alex rodriguez is at bat.  bronson hits a-rod with a pitch.  rodriguez moves toward first base, staring at the pitcher.  then you can see his mouth say, FUCK YOU.  (somewhere near us, a car door slams.)  jason varitek, the catcher, goes to a-rod and gets up close to him and they talk shit for a second, then varitek puts his hand on rodriguez\\\' face and pushes him.  the field erupts; players come off both benches; there is a melee.  the front door opens; christie comes in carrying a bag of groceries, her backpack slung over one shoulder.  she says, \\\"oh, this is cozy!\\\"  storms off to the kitchen.  i go in there; jamie stays on the couch.  he must be smarter than me.  try to talk to her.  won\\\'t listen to reason - remind her that that\\\'s how jamie feels when she and i are together, that\\\'s how i feel about them.  no dice.  she\\\'s pretty frosty the rest of the night.  all we do is watch dvds.</P><P>i\\\'m with each of them more than the other knows.  keep it on the down low to try to prevent the jealousy.  then there\\\'s the unspoken rivalry jamie and i have (probably subconscious on his part).  he and i are both alpha male types, so there\\\'s this constant psychosexual pissing contest going on.  that goes along with sexual identity issues.  one night, christie and i were in my kitchen dancing to <B><I>more, more, more (part 1)</B></I> by the andrea true connection, one of my favorite songs (the lyrics of which are ironic in light of the fact that andrea true eventually became a porn actress.  and i\\\'ve always wanted to hear part 2.).  jamie was sitting on a barstool watching us.  i said, \\\"jamie.  come dance with us.\\\"  \\\"i can\\\'t dance.\\\"  <B><I>young americans</I></B> came on.  i said, \\\"you\\\'re a gay man.  you should be able to dance.\\\"  \\\"i\\\'m not gay.\\\"  \\\"you\\\'re not?  then who the hell was suckin\\\' on my wing-wang last night?  who was suckin\\\' on mister frank king the ding-a-ling?  that wasn\\\'t you?\\\"  \\\"i\\\'m bi.\\\"  \\\"i would propose that when you\\\'re fuckin\\\' a man, you\\\'re gay.  you can be bi the rest of the time.\\\"  christie goes, \\\"well, when he\\\'s fuckin\\\' me, he\\\'s straight.\\\"  \\\"you hear that?  when you\\\'re doin\\\' the do, you\\\'re one or the other.  the rest of the time, you can be bi.\\\"  \\\"kind of like the uncertainty priciple, huh?\\\"  \\\"more like schrodingers cat.  sort of.\\\"  \\\"what about when i\\\'m fuckin\\\' both of you?\\\"  i turn to christie:  \\\"can we get a ruling on that?\\\"  \\\"survey says:  bi.\\\"</P><P>these triune things usually degenerate into one of the couples leaving the third party and going forward on their own.  this one is holding up pretty well, though.  but i can\\\'t say i\\\'m in love with them.  well, not as much as i have been with other people.  (and i don\\\'t see it lasting longer than a couple of years, after which i\\\'m going to get married and start a family.)  but we get along, and we laugh, and even though they don\\\'t get all my jokes and references, even though i\\\'m smarter than they are, even though we\\\'re not of the same generation, it just feels good to have somebody there to rub your head.  to care about you.  and if that\\\'s all there is to it, i\\\'ll take it gladly.  because i know i\\\'m not an easy person to care for.  i\\\'m a case and a half.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>MY DISEASE(S):</P><P>primarily, i have a chemical imbalance.  different doctors have diagnosed it differently.  it\\\'s been called bipolar disorder, bipolar disorder with psychotic features, schizoaffective disorder, and paranoid schizophrenia.  the only time i hallucinate or get delusional is when i\\\'ve been up for days or when i\\\'m under greater than usual stress.</P><P>second, i\\\'m an addict.  and i can become addicted to anything that makes me feel good.  the food, sex, exercise, spending, and internet addictions i\\\'ve learned to control.  i can do that stuff in moderation, and it\\\'s just fine.  i can\\\'t go near alcohol, drugs, or gambling.  those will create crippling chaos at the least and devastation and death at the worst.</P><P>when you have a dual diagnosis, you have to bring both halves of it under control, because they feed off each other.  one makes the other worse in a cyclical manner.  that\\\'s why it was good to be in treatment.  i was getting my meds on schedule and building up a period of abstinence.  when i left the assisted living facility in early december, i stopped taking the meds.  thus precipitating the rapid decline.  i\\\'ve been stable and abstinent now for seven months.  if i keep it up, the doctor says he can start weaning me off the buspar and lamictal.</P><P>(the one reservation i have is marijuana.  i know i can\\\'t touch alcohol or coke or opiates or any of those body drugs again.  i\\\'ll die.  but some part of me believes that i can smoke pot and be alright.  they said in rehab that if you do one drug, it will create a craving for ALL the drugs you used to do, due to the receptors for the different substances being close together in the brain.  i don\\\'t know if that\\\'s true.  i\\\'m not a scientist.  but i think i\\\'ll wait awhile.  the tricky thing is that once i get off probation, i won\\\'t be tested.  i\\\'ll be hanging around christie and jamie, and i\\\'ll have to make some decisions.  knowing what they know, i don\\\'t think they would let me.  i would have to do it on my own.)</P><P>i am full of hope.  i think that not only will i survive:  i will thrive in sobriety.  i have on my shelf, among other books:  <I><B>post office, the sound and the fury, great expectations, crime and punishment, naked lunch</B></I>.  written by, respectively, an alcholic, an alcholic, a manic-depressive, a compulsive gambler, and a drug addict.  i am all of these things.  while two of these boys weren\\\'t playing in the same league as the other three, they all were (as far as i know) active in their diseases throughout their writing careers.  and if they could do that stuff while hamstrung by addiction and mental illness, then i think i can do at least a half-ass job straight and healthy.</P><P>(by the way, i think bukowski is eminently readable.  he\\\'s funny as hell, and his body of work, as a whole, is about nothing less than man triumphing over adversity and succeeding in spite of himself.  he may have had his faults, sure, but art transcends that.  you never get too old to read him, and those who say you do would, i think, tend to be kind of po-faced and anhedonic.  but that\\\'s just <U>my</U> opinion.  that\\\'s how <U>i</U> roll.)</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>when i was booking talent for a coffeehouse in brooksville several years ago, i was loafing around on the job one day reading a national music and lifestyle magazine, and i happened upon an article about an alternative film festival.  it said the jury prize winner needed completion funding.  i thought to myself, hmm, the boyfriend of the woman who owns this coffeehouse is a real estate developer with, no doubt, some spare cash sitting around.  i could introduce him to the film\\\'s producers and then take a piece out of the resultant deal.  that\\\'s what producers do:  they broker talent and money.  by that logic, i would be a producer.  (and that\\\'s basically all the credentials you need for the position.  just say you\\\'re one, and you are.)</P><P>the article was written by a man who usually traffics in absurdist vignettes.  i called information in new york, and, luckily, he was in the book.  we chatted about his work, then he gave me the director\\\'s number in l.a.  i talked to the director, who referred me to the producer\\\'s rep.  (this is a guy they hired, as far as i can tell, to get funding and/or a distribution deal.)  he said they were looking for fifty thousand.</P><P>they fed-exed us a package containing a prospectus, a budget breakdown, and a videotape.  the picture was basically finished.  on viewing the tape, it was apparent that they needed to re-time the negative, so that would cost, but the money they wanted was mainly for music rights.  i think they were being naive, because they had put all the gimcrack alternative hits of the day on there, and there was no way $50K was going to pay for it.  but that could have been overlooked, that could have been dealt with, the money would have been found if the film was good, was at all commercial.  but it wasn\\\'t.  it was absurdist, but not enough to qualify as Art.  it wasn\\\'t funny enough.  the acting was half-ass, and the plot was stupid.  it starred two young men who were scions of industrial families.  their last names were the names of giant multinational corporations.  you\\\'d think they could have bought a better movie for themselves.  one of them appeared briefly in <I><B>boogie nights</B></I> then gave up acting.  the other played the villain in a flop sequel and other bit parts.</P><P>well, it didn\\\'t work out.  the project was shelved, but i stayed in touch with the director.  he went on to make a movie starring one of the second bananas from a sitcom.  it failed to get distribution.  at that point, he switched agencies.  he then directed a film for which one of the leads won an academy award.</P><P>last year, i came across a site for a graphic artist whose work i thought would make a good animated film.  i e-mailed him, and he gave me permission to try to set up a deal with the understanding that he would write and direct.  i hadn\\\'t spoken to the director since before his last film was released.  the number i had for him had been disconnected.  i called his agent and was told he was about to start shooting his current project.  they referred me to the production office.</P><P>i never got to speak to him.  the closest i got was his assistant.  i submitted the project to see if the director wanted to executive produce it.  (by submitting it, i mean i referred them to the artist\\\'s work.)  the assistant told me the director was producing something at the moment and wasn\\\'t enjoying it.  she said he suggested i contact his studio and say he referred me.</P><P>the development executive i talked to looked at the website and liked what he saw but wanted a story outline or a script before he would commit to anything.  so the ball was in the artist\\\'s court.  it dragged on for a few weeks.  he said he couldn\\\'t find the time to work on it.  and there it sat.  i contacted him when i got out of rehab, and he said he was busy with other projects now but might want to do the movie \\\"someday\\\".</P><P>i was doing all of that while i was in the assisted living facility, using the computer at the public library and a prepaid calling card.  looking back on it now, if it had progressed at all beyond the talking stage, the jig would have probably been up, because i didn\\\'t have a production company or the money to fly out to l.a.  that shouldn\\\'t have mattered, really, because the recognition of quality was there and the determination.  i still have the creative instincts and the persistance (and connections) a producer needs.  all i\\\'m lacking is the money.  it wouldn\\\'t take a <U>whole</U> lot, either.  if i had $20,000, there\\\'s a good chance i could turn it into two to three million within two years.  $20K would pay a lawyer to set up a production company, and there would be enough left over to option the rights to a book or script.  nobody\\\'s going to give me that kind of money, though.  i\\\'ll have to work for it.  but i\\\'m kind of allergic to work.  the nine-to-five variety, anyway.  somehow, it just doesn\\\'t appeal to me.  what i\\\'ll probably have to do is write for it.  the novel isn\\\'t going to be done anytime soon, so the easiest thing to do would be to write the most cheese-ass commercial script i can think of.  and take it from there.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>i live in downtown new port richey.  from u.s. highway 19, you turn east on main street, turn north at madison street, and i\\\'m at madison and pennsylvania avenue.  i live in the duplex on the northwest corner of the intersection.  mine is the apartment on the left.  the yard has three date palms in it:  mama, daddy, and baby.  skirts of dates on the ground circle the trunks.  if you go east on pennsylvania, across madison, there\\\'s a nursing home on the corner, and then there\\\'s the treatment center.  the back side of it.  the front\\\'s on indiana.  monroe st. is on the far side of the rehab.  if you go south one block, to central avenue, you come to the triplex i lived in three years ago.  i lived in one of the apartments around back.  at the beginning of november, 2000, i got my disabilty checks, paid the rent, then went on the casino boat and lost the rest of the money i had.  i thought to myself, i have some money tied up in something or other.  what is it?  it came to me.  i had the electricity disconnected on election day.  i went to the florida power office and collected my deposit.  the next morning, i strolled down to a diner on main street.  i asked the waitress, who\\\'s our new president?  she said, it\\\'s the strangest thing:  we don\\\'t know.  i lived for two months in that apartment with no electricity.  just pure insanity.  i never want that kind of chaos in my life again.</P><P>you come in the front door (the only door, now that i think about it), and you\\\'re in the living room.  double dresser against the right-hand wall, my clothes in there, stereo components on top, two speakers on either side of it, eight crates full of cds beyond the far speaker.  picture window on your left (in the same wall the door\\\'s in).  tv on a stand in front of it.  Big-Ass Sectional Couch along the left-hand wall, curving around, L-shaped, the short leg of the L coming out five feet or so, facing the tv.  it has two recliners and a sofa bed.  coffee table in front of the couch.  white tile throughout the apartment except for bedroom (carpet).  behind the living room is the kitchen.  bar as a partial partition between.  door to a closet on the left.</P><P>on top of the refrigerator:  (mirror hung on wall above fridge.)  piece of red velvet.  on top of that:  statue of our lady of candeleria, statue of saint barbara, wonder woman figurine, red candle, purple candle, brown candle, string of red mardi gras beads, nine black horsehairs coiled into a spiral and laminated, four pennies heads up, arranged in a square, four pennies tails up, arranged in a square, a small wooden thing between the two squares with a slot in the top in which a penny sits on its side, white chicken feathers, a dish of locust shells, a wax plum, a bunch of wax grapes, an eggplant, a tiny piece of paper, laminated and turned face-down.  (on the paper is printed \\\"Our name is Legion.\\\"  it was cut out of a bible.  the bible was then burned in a galvanized trash can and the ashes buried.)  keeps the troublesome characters away.  i put it all in a box under the sink when my family comes over.  christie and jamie don\\\'t know what to think of it.  they don\\\'t say too much about it.</P><P>bathroom:  i never have got a liner for the shower curtain, so i take baths.  the mirror on the medicine cabinet has waves in it, so it\\\'s like one of those funhouse jobs.  little alcove between the bathroom and bedroom.  there\\\'s a bookshelf in there.  on the top are pictures:  one of my family circa 1976 - dad and mom in back - tim (nine), me (six), morgan (four) in front, left to right - tim has the same smile he had all his life - tim and i have matching red and black cowboy shirts, matching beatlesque haircuts - dad has a hairstyle like latka gravas (scary to think i\\\'ll soon be the age he was in that picture).  picture of my niece, one of my grandparents, one of my dad with his brothers and sisters.</P><P>the bedroom is roomy.  queen-size bed in one corner, computer desk in the other, two speakers along the wall, rest of the floor available for yoga.  on the other side of the eastern bedroom wall is my neighbor\\\'s bedroom.  i know it\\\'s there because friends of mine rented that apartment four years ago.  i have never laid eyes on these neighbors.  and they are quiet as churchmice.  if they have sex, they must do it in the kitchen or something.  i can imagine what they think of the goings-on on my side of the wall.  they\\\'re from czechoslovakia, so i guess they\\\'re used to laying low.</P><P>let\\\'s take a look at the tenant:  5\\\'10\\\", 187 lbs., slight scoliosis (makes the muscles bunch up slightly in the left middle back).  i\\\'ve got brown hair with blond streaks, cut it myself:  3/8\\\" on top, 1/8\\\" on the back and sides, 3\\\" bangs in front.  (i color it.  [christie does, really.]  naturally, it\\\'s brown with flecks of grey.)</P><P>my eyes are blue.  i\\\'ve been told i have beautiful eyes, which is nice because eyes don\\\'t age.  they can be somewhat intense-looking at times, too, even crazy.  there\\\'s a scar through my left eyebrow that i earned late one saturday night at the village pub downtown.  i was running my mouth, and this guy hit me and split my eyebrow open.  it bled like crazy.  i went home and passed out, and it greeted me in the mirror in the morning, a half-inch gap between the sides of the eyebrow.</P><P>i used to look like a girl.  during The Final Escapade, when i lost all that weight, my face kind of caved in.  i gained some of the weight back in rehab, but i look different now.  i still look pretty hot sometimes.  i don\\\'t know: sometimes i think i look like a dog.  depends on the light, depends on the angle, depends on the mirror.  i shouldn\\\'t worry about it so much, right?</P><P>hairwise, the only hair i let grow on my body is my head hair and my eyebrows.  everything else gets shaved.  i don\\\'t worry about whether somebody in the shower at the y notices or not.  they can deal with it.  i don\\\'t have any tattoos or piercings.  the only thing i have along that line would be a brand:  a half-inch triangle and x on the lower right-hand side of my right asscheek.  there are four small fading purplish scars on my abdomen from my gall bladder surgery.  one is right under my navel.  i haven\\\'t been laying out this summer, and my natural skin color is fish-belly white.  my face is a little ruddy, though.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>the hurricane came.  i tried to prepare my apartment:  getting everything up off the floor was about all i could do.  i put everything i wanted to save off of the hard drive onto zip disks and moved the tv and stereo and computer away from the windows.  i called the landlord and asked him if he was going to put any plywood up, and he didn\\\'t think it was that serious.</P><P>my sister picked me up the night before it was supposed to arrive.  she lives near the water.  she said she got what she absolutely couldn\\\'t do without out of her apartment and said goodbye to the rest of it.  she arrived at my place with my niece, their cat, clothes, laptop, important papers, and all the photo albums and the videos of paige.  a long time ago, my grandmother told us that during a fire, whatever, get the people out first and then the photo albums if you can.  everything else can be replaced.</P><P>it was expected to hit early in the afternoon of the next day.  we went to the parental home in brooksville.  dad was ready for it.  he was an eagle scout.  he had i don\\\'t know how many gallons of water, canned goods, two tanks of propane, a generator, twenty gallons of gas.  every once in awhile, it\\\'s a relief to know you\\\'re in good hands.  i don\\\'t handle most stressful situations well.</P><P>mom and dad had recently gotten a puppy.  it had never been around any other animals.  morgan and paige\\\'s cat hadn\\\'t either.  the morning of the hurricane, my grandmother arrived with a friend of hers and the friend\\\'s two cats.  who had never been around any other animals besides each other.  also, my aunt and uncle came over (mom\\\'s brother and his wife).  if the storm came our way, we were all going into the basement.  and if it arrived, i predicted the animals would FLIP out.  who knows who else.  me, probably.</P><P>it turned away from us, though.  the gathering got more festive after that.  i\\\'d never been to a hurricane party sober or with my family.  (but i can see why my friends and i used to drink like fiends, though.  it was stressful.  then again, anytime a hurricane <U>might</U> have been heading our way, we threw a party.  just another excuse, really.)  we ate lunch and then casted about for something to do.  my niece wanted to see some of the super 8 movies.  they were all on videotape (my grandfather and uncle took all of my granparents\\\' super 8s and all of my family\\\'s, and while grandpa ran the projector, my uncle videotaped the screen.  and grandpa added a commentary track.  the videos look like shit, but they have the bonus of grandpa talking.), but paige liked \\\"the movie part of it\\\".  we got out the projector, but couldn\\\'t find the metal filebox all the reels had been in.  we looked everywhere.  we found three metal boxes but no film.  mom said, \\\"well, we\\\'ve been cleaning out a lot of stuff.  maybe they got carried to the dump.\\\"  i started crying.  i went outside.  it was all a bit much.  the wind was blowing pretty good out there.  light rain.  after awhile, paige opened the front door and yelled, \\\"matthew, we found \\\'em.\\\"  (my family calls me matthew.  everybody else calls me matt.  [a couple of people call me mattie occaisonally.])  they were in the coat closet in a couple of shoeboxes.</P><P>i ran the projector.  grandma called me george chalker.  \\\"let\\\'s go, george\\\" when i was slow changing reels.  (g.c. was the projectionist at the old dixie theatre downtown when my mom was growing up.  said he used to sit up there in a sleeveless t-shirt, smoking a cigar, up above the balcony where the black people had to sit.)  the movies spanned the era from 1967, when tim was born, to the early eighties, when dad quit the state division of forestry and went into business with a friend of his from high school, making mulch, and soon found himself ass-deep in money, some of which he used to buy a videocamera.  kodachrome images.  babies coming home.  birthdays.  parades.  old people disappearing.  new people showing up.  weddings.  vacations.  disney world.  tim and i in little league uniforms.  first day of school.  picnics.  dogs and cats.  easter egg hunts.  morgan in a green dress.  (flower girl, we decided.)  people we couldn\\\'t identify.  cousins come to visit us.  christmas.  this is who we are.  this is who we are.  we see you there in the camera lens.  remember us.  this is who you are.</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>in rehab, we had to pick a higher power if we didn\\\'t already have one.  i decided if mine was anything at all, it was love.  the counselor, a jesus woman, got excited when i told her about it.  she told me about how the scriptures were essentially a series of lessons in love.  since then, i\\\'ve been looking for examples of love as i pass through the days:</P><P>christie and i are watching something on the independent film channel, waiting for jamie to come home from work.  he arrives, and i hang out for a little while then get up to go bike home.  he walks me to the door.  we get out on the porch, and i motion him toward me, conspiratorially.  \\\"listen,\\\" i whisper, \\\"there ain\\\'t nothin\\\' left for you.  i took it all.\\\"  joking, for me, is a form of love.</P><P>in the parking lot of the apartment building across the street, a little boy rides one of those scooters.  his mother, nearby with a stroller, stands talking to a neighbor.  the boy looks like he\\\'s about three.  they must be going somewhere:  his mom\\\'s got him dressed up - navy shorts and a yellow polo shirt, hair combed.  the scooter\\\'s bigger than he is; the handle\\\'s a foot above his head.  he abandons it and goes over to the stroller.  he jumps up and down, dances crazily, plays peek-a-boo, tries to entertain the baby.  he runs around, falls down, starts crying.  his mother comes to pick him up.</P><P>night comes.  in the parking lot across the street, four or five young men stand around talking.  they\\\'re dressed identically:  low-slung shorts, sleeveless white cotton t-shirts, baseball caps.  it looks like they\\\'re passing around something, keeping it down low.  a car arrives with a fanfare of bass.  it has lights inside the wheel wells, so that great rotating wedges of light are cast on the side of the building.  the young men have apparently never seen this phenomenon before because it excites them considerably.  the driver jumps out, and he is congratulated heartily on his choice of customization.  though these guys would never admit it, they all love each other fiercely.</P><P>an older woman and man, sixtyish, stand in the parking lot in paint-stained clothing.  they must own the apartment building or take care of it.  they\\\'ve been fixing up one of the units that has been recently vacated.  they\\\'re washing out brushes and one of those roller trays.  the woman\\\'s holding the hose, and her husband\\\'s agitating the brushes.  suddenly, she steps back and puts a finger over the mouth of the hose and soaks him down.  he gasps from the shock of the cold water.  they both laugh.  he wrestles with her and gets the hose away from her and wets her, too.  they both stand there and laugh.</P><P>i\\\'m at the library a lot.  it\\\'s three blocks south of my place, at madison and main.  there\\\'s a homeless guy named bill who hangs out on a bench out front.  i stop to talk to him whenever he\\\'s there, give him some money when i can spare it.  he\\\'s crazy, drinks to control the symptoms.  which was exactly like i used to be.  i was homeless for about a year and a half around florida.  (ran out of money one time in mexico and tried begging.  the police got the consul to ship my ass back to the states real quick.  they didn\\\'t like that.)</P><P>he\\\'s on the bench.  i say, \\\"bill, tell me how they got \\\'bill\\\' out of \\\'william\\\'.  where the hell did the \\\'b\\\' come from?\\\"  he says, \\\"ya got me by the balls.\\\"  we talk for awhile.  he\\\'s about half-drunk.  apropos of nothing, he says, \\\"y\\\'know, abraham lincoln was a republican.\\\"  i say, \\\"yeah, i know.  but i don\\\'t think he\\\'d be one today.\\\"  \\\"probably not.  (pause)  it\\\'s like a war, isn\\\'t it?  each side thinks the other\\\'s evil.  i don\\\'t buy it.  you can\\\'t tell me all those republicans are evil.\\\"  \\\"they\\\'re not evil.  i\\\'m sure they\\\'re good people.  they just do some fucked-up shit and have fucked-up priorities.\\\"  \\\"they love their children just like everybody else, though.\\\"  \\\"i know.\\\"  \\\"we should all get along better by now.\\\"</P><P>it was one of those things that makes my brain hurt, like contemplating eternity.  i said, \\\"you want to go get a coke?\\\"  \\\"how about some boone\\\'s farm?\\\"  \\\"you know i can\\\'t do that.\\\"  \\\"why.  tell me why.  nobody said you had to drink any of it.\\\"  \\\"yeah, but it would be hard not to.\\\"</P><P>we walked up the colonade in front of city hall and waited to cross the street to the no-name convenience store.  bill goes, \\\"i bet i go in here ten times a day, but i\\\'ll be damned if i know what to call it.\\\"  \\\"i guess it doesn\\\'t have a name.  nobody seems to care.\\\"  we go inside.  the little brown man behind the counter sees bill, and his eyes get big.  he says, \\\"no, billy.  you know you can\\\'t be in here.  get out.  (he points to the door)  get out.  i\\\'ll  call the police.\\\"  bill says, \\\"why doesn\\\'t this place have a name?\\\"  \\\"(indignant) it has a name.\\\"  \\\"what the hell is it?\\\"  \\\"main street food mart.\\\"  bill stamps his foot:  \\\"then put up a goddam SIIIIIIIIIIIIGN!\\\"  the guy comes out from behind the counter.  bill heads outside.  i get two cokes.</P><P>back on the bench, bill tells me he\\\'s thinking about moving.  why?  says he\\\'s \\\"gettin\\\' tired of the scene.\\\"  \\\"what scene is that, bill?  the drinkin\\\'-boone\\\'s farm-behind-the-hindumart scene?  the sleepin\\\'-in-the-woods scene?  you know what the man said:  wherever you go, there you are.\\\"  \\\"which man was that?\\\"  \\\"buckaroo banzai.\\\"  \\\"shit.\\\"</P><P>*     *     *</P><P>they say everybody loves a happy ending.  like:  the war\\\'s over.  the baby lived.  the storm missed us.  the good guys won.  juliet and romeo capulet are happily married somewhere outside of verona.  we get a postcard from them every once in awhile.</P><P>and it feels like a happy ending.  i know it isn\\\'t the end of things, but i feel happy.  overall.  all i know is that i haven\\\'t felt like killing myself in a long time, and my life isn\\\'t in chaos.  and that\\\'s enough for today, at least.  i sit in my little apartment and look out the window at the world, a world i\\\'m a part of, with people in it who love me and people whom i love and everything is beautiful and everything is right and everything is true and everything is good.</P><P><B>you\\\'re gonna make it after all.</B></P>'),(3689,'<p><i>...a woman, even a whore, is never a dog. </i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>If I could show you the beauty behind that moment you\\\'d understand better what followed, I\\\'m certain. The sight of her mouth around me was erotic, of course, but it wasn\\\'t what I concentrated on. In my mind, I was watching her come out of the bathroom, her hair wrapped in a blue towel, toothbrush sticking from between her lips. I was remembering the way her hand sought mine just before her orgasm, <i>faked? not?</i>, and gripped it, while her other hand tore at my hair and pushed my face against her. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>For whatever flaws she has, this wonderful, gorgeous woman, was sucking on my cock, and her eyes were watching mine. Whore, perhaps heartless, startlingly empty, but complex - funny, intelligent, amusingly crass. <i>Where does pleasure come from?</i> Whether she truly wanted me or not, found me attractive or not, for whatever reason she was there with me. Giving me something gentler, nicer women had never cared to offer. To her maybe I was not a monster at all. Did she see in me exactly what I saw in her? Was this about protecting those who can\\\'t be protected? <i>Is this what the love urge becomes after hope is lost?</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>She came up for air, smiled at me, and I tugged her hair. Brought her face to mine, held in my hands, and kissed her softly on the lips. She smiled and reached down between my legs.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Wait,\\\" it came out more harshly than I\\\'d intended.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Are you -\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Shut up a moment, please,\\\" I was out of my mind, I had no idea what I wanted, needed to say, \\\"I uhhh, I like you. Alot.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She just smiled.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wasn\\\'t done, although maybe I should have been, \\\"I don\\\'t know what I\\\'m saying, okay? This is hard. I like you and I want to make you happier than you are. I don\\\'t know that I can, even if it were in any man\\\'s power, I\\\'m probably not him. Okay? I know that.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Her eyes were staring into mine, I felt like crying. Without alcohol to cloud my brain, this was far more complex emotionally than I\\\'d expected in my heart. Her smile was fixed a little maybe, as if she were struggling to keep up with what I was saying. Well, so was I.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"What you\\\'ve done, I don\\\'t care about that, fuck it, I don\\\'t care. You\\\'re not that person in my eyes, you\\\'re someone else. Special. Beautiful. Uhhh...shit, what the fuck...\\\" She kissed the side of my mouth, were her eyes watery, or were mine?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Hmmm, you\\\'re not who you think, you know Dave.\\\" She kissed me. \\\"I think you\\\'re a great guy.\\\" </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Uh, yeah, well.\\\" <i>She\\\'s blind if she thinks that.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"We\\\'re having fun, let\\\'s just keep that going. Let\\\'s see what comes, okay honey?\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That is the sort of scary shit that makes me want to scream in terror. I coul