<BR>felt like i was the only person on earth<B>.</B><BR>on the corner of grand and main<BR>a clock mounted on a building<BR>was stopped at eight forty-five<B>.</B><BR>i stood there for several minutes<BR>watching the traffic light change<B>.</B><BR>a police car came along<BR>and turned east on main<B>.</B><BR>i started home<B>.</B><BR>at central and jefferson<BR>a mid-sized black dog<BR>stood on the corner<BR>and started barking when it saw me<B>.</B><BR>i crossed to the other side of the street<B>.</B><BR>it charged me<B>.</B><BR>i ran from it<BR>to jefferson and pennsylvania<BR>down towards my apartment<B>.</B><BR>it gave up<BR>halfway down the street<B>.</B></P><P>today after i write<BR>i put on my shoes<BR>put on my lightning cap<BR>go to the library<B>.</B><BR>stay there a little while<BR>check out <B><I>the graduate</B></I><B>.</B><BR>on the way out<BR>i pass a little boy and his mom<B>.</B><BR>around three<B>,</B> wearing a cap<B>,</B> he looks kind of like i did<BR>when i was his age<B>.</B><BR>i bring my hand up<BR>and waggle the bill of my cap<BR>at him<B>,</B><BR>kind of like a greeting or a salute<B>.</B><BR>he reaches up<B>,</B><BR>pulls his cap down over his eyes<BR>and then back up to its original position<B>.</B><BR>his mom laughs<B>.</B><BR>he was just imitating me<B>.</B><BR>makes me wonder who or what<BR>older or larger than myself<BR>i\\\'m imitating<B>,</B><BR>not realizing that the gesture<BR>is more complex than i think<B>.</B></P><P>that little boy<BR>is going to be an old man<BR>someday<B>.</B><BR>me<B>,</B> too<B>.</B><BR>sooner than him<B>.</B></P><P>besides christie and jamie<BR>and my family<B>,</B><BR>everybody i know<BR>is in recovery<B>.</B><BR>we\\\'re all living<BR>one day at a time<B>,</B><BR>trying not to relapse<B>.</B><BR>you see people dropping like flies<BR>all around you<B>,</B><BR>friends going back out<B>.</B><BR>for me<B>,</B><BR>i\\\'m quite sure<BR>that using again<BR>would result in my death<BR>sooner rather than later<B>.</B><BR>i consider myself lucky to be alive<B>.</B><BR>sometimes i feel like<BR>i was the gypsy in that rosseau painting<BR>asleep with the lion passing behind him<BR>really close<B>.</B><BR>and sometimes i feel like<BR>i was the lion<B>.</B></P><P><P>on the way to the grocery store<BR>one curve in the road by the park<BR>is under three feet of water<B>.</B><BR>three kids are swimming in it<B>.</B><BR>i say<B>, \\\"</B>watch out for the alligator, boys<B>!\\\"</B><BR>they look shocked for a second<BR>then the largest of them says<B>,<BR>\\\"</B>ah<B>,</B> fuck off<B>.</B>  there ain<B>\\\'</B>t no alligator<B>.\\\"</B></P><P>the latest forecast<BR>has ivan hitting my neck of the woods<B>.</B><BR>i think god is determined to<BR>fuck<B>.</B>    matt<B>.</B>    up<B>.</B><BR>that\\\'s alright<B>.</B><BR>god plays his games<B>,</B><BR>and i play mine<B>.</B></P><P>so it goes<BR>in between things<B>:</B><BR>waiting for the next storm<BR>the next attack<BR>the election results<BR>temptation to strike<BR>death<BR>or maybe something really good<B>.</B></P><P>i get home from the library<BR>check the mailbox<B>:</B><BR>nothing<B>.</B><BR>hell, i think<B>,</B><BR>i say a day without a bill in the mailbox<BR>is a good day<B>.</B></P><P>'),(3690,'<p>I had a love, all by myself <br>\r\nand alone I loved it much. <br>\r\nIgnoring warnings for my health, <br>\r\nI let it jangle me with such<br>\r\nfits of angst, ruin my sleep,<br>\r\nand destroy fine afternoons,<br>\r\nall because I can not keep<br>\r\naway the artificial swoon</p>\r\n\r\n<p>of well imagined romances.<br>\r\nThey were lovely, all the meals<br>\r\nwe did not share, sly advances<br>\r\nof hands and lips that revealed<br>\r\nangles of hips, the heat of thighs.<br>\r\nAnd I loved just as much the days<br>\r\nwe did not fight, volleying whys<br>\r\nand wherefores and the thousand ways</p>\r\n\r\n<p>with words we had each been wronged.<br>\r\nNothing but time will console <br>\r\nme for the loss of that prolonged<br>\r\nhigh, the pretty lie of fated souls.<br>\r\nI shut my eyes, thought of school,<br>\r\nof all the years I\\\'ve been this fool,<br>\r\nrevering faces, arms, charm and wit,<br>\r\nand the other person <br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;                  has nothing <br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;whatever <br>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;to do with it.</p>'),(3691,'<p>Dear Chechnyan fighters, islamic jihadists, and all others who seek to achieve your goals through the killing of innocents:\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am a student in the United States. I am studying to be a lawyer. My goal is to help people who have been injured to be compensated according to their injuries. I am 27 years old. I am young, yet I am a student of philosphy as well and of the human condition. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy heart and soul ache with yours; I understand that for you to commit such acts, or even to contemplate such things, you must have been wronged very deeply. I, even as a child of privilage in the United States, have a heart too, and I know how unhappy I would be were something bad to happen to those that I love. \r\n</p><p>\r\nYour pain is greater than the worth of my life. I know only that I want this anguish to end, finally, and that I want for us to move forward holding hands as brothers and sisters, mothers and children, fathers and sons. We all bleed the same red blood. We may not pray the same; we may not eat the same foods; we may not speak the same language; and still, we are all human beings, placed upon this planet for an unfathomable purpose. Still, we feel love. Still, we bleed the same red blood. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI am working to change the things that I can from my humble position. I can only hope that these words provide some measure of comfort to you in your struggle to assert your own rights to live in the way that you choose, individually, as we all do whether within a nation, a family, or a circle of friends, and so long as we do not infringe the rights of other to do the same. That, I believe, is how our creator, whomever and whatever it may be, and whatever its purpose, would want us to carry on. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI feel that our forefathers sought the same goal as I; the Founding Fathers of the United States, the Christians, the Imams of Islam, the Rabbis of Judaism; and all the others of which I remain woefully ignorant at this point in my life. At the core of their writings we find suggestions on how to live our lives upon the Earth, how to treat each other with kindness and to always act in such a way as to promote the ideals of dignity and freedom. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy plea to you is this: let us stop the killing. I pledge to you that I will work my utmost to change the government of my own country to accomodate the needs of all, rich and poor, nationalized or foreign to the United States--indeed, I see no difference. For what use is it to gain all the property of the world, to control and dictate the lives of others...what good is it to become powerful,if there remain starving children in the world, and impoverished persons, whether materially or spiritually? Please, can you not work towards these goals in your way, without the killing? It serves only to polarize, to create lifelong enemies, to tear down what, in our more lucid moments, we have built together. Tell me what more I can do! \r\n</p><p>\r\nA wise man of my country once said, \\\"we are here to help each other through this thing, whatever it is.\\\" My brothers and sisters, I am on this Earth to help you. My own life is weak, small, and of little consequence, but ask me and it is yours to command. Let us erase the imaginary boundaries that our Fathers drew, and become one world where children may play and adults may love each other, where we may play music for each other, where we might eat together and hare the joy of each others\\\' company. Let us strive to live to serve the ideal which our Creator surely must have had in mind--for, surely, for the Creator to have gone to all the trouble of bringing us into existence, the Creator must have had a higher purpose in mind than for us to fight each other and cause each other pain beyond measure. '),(3692,'<p>Dear Chechnyan fighters, islamic jihadists, and all others who seek to achieve your goals through the killing of innocents:\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am a student in the United States. I am studying to be a lawyer. My goal is to help people who have been injured to be compensated according to their injuries. I am 27 years old. I am young, yet I am a student of philosphy as well and of the human condition. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy heart and soul ache with yours; I understand that for you to commit such acts, or even to contemplate such things, you must have been wronged very deeply. I, even as a child of privilage in the United States, have a heart too, and I know how unhappy I would be were something bad to happen to those that I love. \r\n</p><p>\r\nYour pain is greater than the worth of my life. I know only that I want this anguish to end, finally, and that I want for us to move forward holding hands as brothers and sisters, mothers and children, fathers and sons. We all bleed the same red blood. We may not pray the same; we may not eat the same foods; we may not speak the same language; and still, we are all human beings, placed upon this planet for an unfathomable purpose. Still, we feel love. Still, we bleed the same red blood. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI am working to change the things that I can from my humble position. I can only hope that these words provide some measure of comfort to you in your stru