pain beyond measure. '),(3695,'<p>Dear Chechnyan fighters, Islamic Jihadists, and all others who seek to achieve your goals through the killing of innocents:\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am a student in the United States. I am studying to be a lawyer. My goal is to help people who have been injured to be compensated according to their injuries. I am 27 years old. I am young, yet I am a student of philosophy as well, and of the human condition, and of history. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy heart and soul ache with yours; I understand that for you to commit such acts, or even to contemplate such things, you must have been wronged very deeply. I, even as a child of privilege in the United States, have a heart too, and I know how unhappy I would be were something bad to happen to those that I love. \r\n</p><p>\r\nYour pain is greater than the worth of my life. I know only that I want this anguish to end, finally, and that I want for us to move forward holding hands as brothers and sisters, mothers and children, fathers and sons. We all bleed the same red blood. We may not pray the same; we may not eat the same foods; we may not speak the same language; and still, we are all human beings, placed upon this planet for an unfathomable purpose. Still, we feel love. Still, we bleed the same red blood. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI am working to change the things that I can from my humble position. I can only hope that these words provide some measure of comfort to you in your struggle to assert your own rights to live in the way that you choose, individually, as we all do whether within a nation, a family, or a circle of friends, and so long as we do not infringe the rights of other to do the same. That, I believe, is how our creator, whomever and whatever it may be, and whatever its purpose, would want us to carry on. \r\n</p><p>\r\nI feel that our forefathers sought the same goal as I; the Founding Fathers of the United States, the Christians, the Imams of Islam, the Rabbis of Judaism; and all the others of which I remain woefully ignorant at this point in my life. At the core of their writings we find suggestions on how to live our lives upon the Earth, how to treat each other with kindness and to always act in such a way as to promote the ideals of dignity and freedom. \r\n</p><p>\r\nMy plea to you is this: let us stop the killing. I pledge to you that I will work my utmost to change the government of my own country to accomodate the needs of all, rich and poor, nationalized or foreign to the United States--indeed, I see no difference. For what use is it to gain all the property of the world, to control and dictate the lives of others...what good is it to become powerful,if there remain starving children in the world, and impoverished persons, whether materially or spiritually? Please, can you not work towards these goals in your way, without the killing? It serves only to polarize, to create lifelong enemies, to tear down what, in our more lucid moments, we have built together. Tell me what more I can do! \r\n</p><p>\r\nA wise man of my country once said, \\\"we are here to help each other through this thing, whatever it is.\\\" My brothers and sisters, I am on this Earth to help you. My own life is weak, small, and of little consequence, but ask me and it is yours to command. Let us erase the imaginary boundaries that our Fathers drew, and become one world where children may play and adults may love each other, where we may play music for each other, where we might eat together and hare the joy of each others\\\' company. Let us strive to live to serve the ideal which our Creator surely must have had in mind--for, surely, for the Creator to have gone to all the trouble of bringing us into existence, the Creator must have had a higher purpose in mind than for us to fight each other and cause each other pain beyond measure. '),(3697,'<P>18. virgin. never been kissed. never hugged someone passionately. socially retarded; sounds like it. and yet, i\\\'m not. parents aren\\\'t religous freaks. sure, the subject is pretty taboo around here. it\\\'s frustrating to say the least. people assume because of the way i look that i\\\'ve had boyfriends. not the case and i don\\\'t understand why. nerves are a factor, but i\\\'m at the point where i\\\'m ready to put that all behind me and go with the moment. problem, no-one to share that moment with.\r\n</P><P>\r\nshort guys are fascinated with me. they have to realise that they have no chance. sounds shallow but i don\\\'t care. maybe because i know they haven\\\'t got a chance, i can be myself and not worry what they\\\'re thinking. being a virgin at this age doesn\\\'t bother me so much as the never been kissed saga. \r\n</p><P>\r\n18 and never been kissed. \r\n</P><P>\r\nit\\\'s not even funny. just sad. friends tell me to be patient. screw that. patience isn\\\'t my best quality, and yet i\\\'ve had to wait this long. a friend once told me to just go up to a guy in a club and say \\\"hey, i\\\'ve never kissed a guy. wanna be the first?\\\" i just laughed, she was being serious.\r\n </P>'),(3698,'<P>\r\njesus fuck it is hot. like 100. and humid. like 110.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m baked beans.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m corn on the cob.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m steamed cabbage.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m the indian food baffet.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m an oiled down porn star in the heat of the moment.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m the hood of a cop car sitting in traffic at 3PM in los angeles.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m writing about love with a water based felt tip pen and my words are obscured by dropplets of sweat.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'ve got baked groin ala powerbook.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m sterile wearing whitey tighties in the summer.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m starting to drift off as the evening air washes over my naked sweating body.\r\n</P>'),(3699,'<p>i want to show you how i used to see the world.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3699_1.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>and how i see the world now.</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3699_2.jpg\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>it is important for me to share this with you. because if i don\\\'t, i will carry this with me for the rest of my life, alone. this is the first time i have tried to express this loss through imagery.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>just understand</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that is all.</p>'),(3700,'test'),(3701,'<p>Well here we are then. The past three weeks have seen my life turned completely inside out. Black is white, all is forgiven, and the future seems at once filled with hope and impending doom.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It all started in the last week of August, I guess. My migraines were getting steadily worse which was incredibly frustrating because I\\\'d had them beaten for so long. The visits to the Osteopath seemed to be having less and less effect, and it was becoming very hard to concentrate on work.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I was spiralling into depression;  this was all too familiar. The headaches have in the past cost me both jobs and relationships, and after a year in which I finally managed to make some inroads into my career, I felt like it was all about to unravel.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I started to feel very nauseous, and a couple of times I was violently ill. The first was a rather spectacular showering of the walls and floor of our bathroom at the flat, which I was then too dizzy to clean up myself, so I suffered the humiliation of having my very fastidious flatmates mopping up after me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then it was the ill-fated visit to a corporate video client in Pakuranga, when I had to ask the cameraman to wait outside with me for five minutes before we went in, and the car-sickness subsided. Not to be outdone on the return trip I threw up all over the front passenger seat and out the passenger window, as we rocketed down the motorway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Knowing that something was seriously wrong I visited my boss\\\' doctor who gave me a referral to a neurologist and sent me home.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It is at this point that things went from bad, to much much worse.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>First, I woke up in hospital.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One of the few things I could remember was that I\\\'d had some weird dream where I was wandering around our flat, totally unable to find my bedroom, and getting very frightened and frustrated.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My Uncle was with me in the hospital and was grinning, apparently greatly amused that I could rememeber nothing of the previous day. My father was already booked on a plane to come over, apparently.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had no idea what was going on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What had transpired, evidently, was that I had arrived home from the doctors, and was about to head over to my boss\\\' for the night so we could work on a corporate project, when I\\\'d become very disoriented.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I had called my boss, Sonali, several times, and had scared her with just how \\\"drunk and out of it\\\" I sounded. She\\\'d phoned her doctor to find out what was going on, and he in turn had called an ambulance, which picked me up from home.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The ambulance driver had had some difficulty getting me into the ambulance, as I kept trying to \\\"find my room, I have to find my room\\\" (hence the dream I guess).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Once at the hospital Sonali had refused to let the doctor discharge me, which he wanted to do. By all accounts I was rambling and vacant, merrily chatting away with my boss\\\' four-year-old daughter and continually responding to any and all questions with the word \\\"absolutely\\\" whilst doing my best to keep my feet on the wall and fight any and all attempts to stick a needle in me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Just as well she didn\\\'t let them release me.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It transpired that my blood sugar levels were at 1.5, when they should have been over ten.  Low end for a diabetic is 4...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When I was lucid enough the next day, and shortly after my father arrived, they obtained my consent to perform a CT scan. I remember being wheeled backwards under lights; a sense of dizziness and slight nausea, lots of bright lights and white shiny surfaces. This all just seemed to be <i>happening</i>; it was a fait-a-complis.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Well they found a growth. I remember feeling almost ambivalent when they came back with the news and told me oh-so-matter-of-factly. It just felt like \\\"okay. right. what happens next?\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s known as a Pineal Tumour. It is located right in the center of the brain, and covers the Pineal gland, which produces cerebro-spinal fluid. The worst part of it is that the tumour is blocking the drainage of the ventricles in the brain, and the fluid builds up within the head literally to bursting point.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Apparently I have had this growth for a number of years, It may or may not be the cause of all the headaches I have suffered since I was 16 (I\\\'m 33 now).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Had they sent me home in the state I was in, I very likely would not have survived the night.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>They gave me a local anaesthetic, drilled a hole in my skull and inserted a drainage hose. The EVD drain I would be attached to for the next ten days would stop my brain from drowning, while they figured out a course of action.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And as I lay there, my father being more supportive and forgiving than I can ever remember, my job over for now, all my furniture moved out of the flat and my whole life on hold - none of it seemed real; I mean how could it be?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\\\'s a lot more to the story so far, and even more still to come. I\\\'ll post some more here as I am able, but right now I need to sleep.</p>\r\n'),(3702,'I pass barren lots<br>\r\nand yards that line the tracks and the backyards of industry; itâ€™s hard to think this area so familiar. <br>\r\nAcres and acres of land, revealed from this noisy rail car, flash by. <br>\r\nThe back farm lands, <br>\r\nuntended shrubbery, and fences <br>\r\nbarely able to hold themselves up, let alone keep something in; <br>\r\nI feel as though Iâ€™m in a foreign country.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBy me goes more old tires and derelict equipment. <br>\r\nLarge ditches are filled with old reeds and dirty rags of vegetation. <br>\r\nFrom the height of the rail car I can make out the highways I have traveled <br>\r\nso thoroughly, <br>\r\nI never thought of them so far away. <br><br>\r\n\r\nEverything is wet from the recent storm <br>\r\nand water gathers in the fields like slicks of mirror, or puddles of glass.<br>\r\nA lite fog along the tree line makes me think of cold evenings in isolated places <br>\r\nand how it feels like a long time ago. <br>\r\nCreeks and brooks swell from the fallen rain <br>\r\nand now and then the trophy of just such a storm stands ragged and broken in the middle of a field; <br>\r\nawkward and amputated, disfigured from the force. <br>\r\nThe green of new life glows through the haze of this overcast evening. <br>\r\nUneven farm roads expose themselves briefly as I pass, <br>\r\nand an otherwise uninhabited place is shaken by our speed and drone. <br><br>\r\n\r\nThe shiny rail next to the train is the only thing outside of the car that doesnâ€™t seem to move and pass with fury, <br>\r\nbut steadily follows alongside, <br>\r\nperfect in its design. <br><br>\r\n\r\nCows hurry away from our peaceful disturbance.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI miss the harmonic wail of the train passing through towns like the one I grew up in. <br>\r\nLate at night it comforts and reminds of its presence. <br>\r\nThe rubble of an old station in Strathroy, <br>\r\nan old bicycle lies next to the fence that quarantines the fire laden destruction. Its chimney <br>\r\nstands tall and alone all the way up defying the buildings defeat from flames. <br>\r\nBroken up concrete,<br>\r\nPiles of asphalt, <br>\r\nBillboards and water towers, <br>\r\nDeserted trailers and broken wooden skids, <br>\r\nHeaps of deconstructed buildings and un-toiled land, <br>\r\nMore patchwork fields and another distraught tree that looks to be giving me the finger.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat country am I in?'),(3703,'<p>\r\ndude -\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGlug, glug, glug.<br>\r\nChop, chop, chop.<br>\r\nBeer and karate.<br>\r\nAss-kicking machine.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<PRE>\r\n     !!!!        \r\n  !!!!!!!!       \r\n!!!!!!!!!!!   O_O\r\n!!!  !!!!!!! /@ @\\\\\r\n      !!!!!! \\\\ x /\r\n      !!!!!!/ m  !m\r\n       !!!!/ __  |\r\n       !!!!|/  \\\\__\r\n        !!!\\\\______\\\\\r\n</PRE>\r\n<p>\r\nthere\\\'s an outside chance that this will all work out, and i won\\\'t need to take another nut job.\r\nmaybe i\\\'ll just get a job, for once.\r\nyou know what it\\\'s like when you\\\'re defiant, you can\\\'t stand to take a subordinate position in a hierarchy, and you\\\'ve got a bit of a temper?\r\nright.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmotorcycles through mexico.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npaul.\r\n</p>'),(3724,'<p>\r\nI just want to begin by saying that I think the appointed president of the United States right now stole the election and a incinerated American rights and freedoms.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen George Dubya was inaugurated he was the first American president to be heckled as he made the traditional journey to the white house. Yes there were protests. Yes there were people pissed off that the election had been so easily stolen...but after a few weeks the laziness I am talking about set in. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLets first look at what I said. \r\n</p>\r\n<p><b>\r\n\\\"lazy fucks who like to blame others for the problems\\\"</b>\r\nBlaming someone else is always easier than actually looking inside and finding out what the actual reasons for the problems are. It is like this with so many things. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe French didnâ€™t single handedly boycott the U.N resolution to help George Bush Jr. Colonize Iraq. There was a serious coalition of the unwilling at that time, but the Media and Spinners picked France, because they have been historically easy to disagree with. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLetâ€™s blame the central and South American drug lords for the drug problem in American, instead of looking at \\\'why people are using drugsâ€™. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLetâ€™s blame Afghanistan and Iraq for a problem that stems for the most part out of a corrupt and self centered foreign policy. A foreign policy that has killed more people than the 911 attacks by a tremendous amount. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLetâ€™s blame the terrorists for invoking legislation that restricts and destroys freedoms long used to support the idea that America is/was truly free. \r\n</p>\r\n<p><b>\r\n\\\"Bush is what happens when people become so fucking consumed.\\\"</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen the population becomes more consumed with sports, survivor, idol, and all that other junk, it is easy to see how the current administration played a good portion of the American public into thinking what was going on was \\\'justâ€™ and \\\'resolvedâ€™. Sure there were protests, and grumbles, but nothing like what a European \\\'democracyâ€™ would have done. The only real media attention with any sort of critical thinking took place in the newspapers, and on the internet. But letâ€™s be honest. Nobody reads anything other than the box scores and infotainment sections anymore.   \r\n</p>\r\n<p><b>\r\n\\\"American politics and American civil society has been corrupt since before pearl harbor.\\\"\r\n</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe corruption is terrible. For so long now the education of the populous has been neglected, infrastructure stretched, and government has done nothing for the rank and file. Never before have we had an administration so heavily invested within the chairs and boards of the military industrial complex. It boggles the mind that this sort of thing happens. In Italy, a media mogul was elected president and the population had serious doubts. He was forced to relinquish control of his Empire. He is scrutinized over by not only his own people, but those of the other EU states. Not so in the United States. Again, there are grumbles, but at the end of the day nobody really cares. Not caring is a sign. A sign of depression. Depression leads to Laziness. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf you would like to read the complete drunken slur, check it out (swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3720)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNow here is what the others said to me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p><blockquote>\r\n\\\"matt : the united states of America is the greatest endeavor in, the highest achievement of human history, and it will be just fine. Not everyone is lazy. Not everyone is corrupt. There are good people on the left and the right who are full of hope, who will do whatever it takes to make things right and make things better. I, for one, am not going anywhere. Iâ€™m going to stay here and do whatever I can, however much or little that might be.\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou sound crudely patriotic. \\\"The greatest endeavor and highest achievement in human history\\\" What are you talking about? If Corporate Capitalism is what you are talking about then yes, the United States has proved itself. But if you are talking about education, health care, the government, and laws regarding narcotics, immigration and taxation, then you my friend are clearly mistaken. I also do not think there have been any \\\"serious\\\" endeavors by the U.S public in a long time. What has the public lobby changed in the last fifty years? What have they fought for? Sure there are good people. People can be good and lazy. People can be law abiding and lazy. People can be corrupt and lazy. My point is, that the American public as a whole isnâ€™t as politically aware or involved like other new and established democracies. The fact is, in my comment I was speaking of the Electorate at large. In your response you mention that there are \\\"people who will do whatever it takes\\\". The U.S didnâ€™t even begin thinking about pulling out of Vietnam until the average working class American thought the war was wrong, and until the average working class American starts paying attention to the people and the issues, the American electorate and the system with which it embodies will be forever lazy and corrupt.    \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\\\"one candidate is better than the other, and I desperately hope he wins, but if he doesn\\\'t, there is still hope, and there will still be people who will do their utmost to ensure that democracy stays alive in this country.\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhere are these people? How did they ensure that homeland security would be respectful of the law? Where were they when the Patriot act was passed? Where were they after the election was stolen and a President was appointed to rule, by people appointed by the presidents father a few years prior? I still donâ€™t know who these people are, and how they are actually affecting the general electorate and the way they do or donâ€™t vote. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\\\"your ill-informed, grad student blanket generalizations are wrong and are not helpful in the least. if you don\\\'t have anything constructive to say, then please just shut the fuck up.\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGeneralizations?  But didnâ€™t you just say \\\"There are people who . . .\\\"? Oh and wait, above this comment you said \\\"well, see the problem is that half of us are enlightened and the other half is either brainwashed, religiously fanatic, greedy, or scared.\\\" Which of the TWO categories do you belong to? You are just as guilty of being general, and perhaps the only reason you accuse of me of it is because you did not agree with me. Pure and simple. Next.     \r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\n<blockquote>\r\n\\\"lin I think you could say that chiamattt represents the rest of the world, the majority of which now seem to feel this way about Americans. Anyone with two cents knows that defining the entire American culture in one way or another is just a blanket statement that cannot take into account the myriad of differences that exist.\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou said it better than I did. Thank you. I was not speaking of the entire American experience. There are great artists, musicians, philosophers, poets, writers, inventors, and yadda yadda in America, but if those who are good at one thing, do not spend a little bit of their time trying to find out what is going on in the rest of the American experience by reading, discussing, and VOTING, then I once again apply the label on them of being lazy. It would be rude to spit on the graves of soldiers who lost their lives fighting in a corrupt war, so would it not also be rude to spit on the graves of those who fought long and hard for the right to vote? The right to assemble? The right to discuss? When more than half of the electorate does not, or is not permitted to vote, there are serious problems...and as long as more than 50% of the American electorate does not maintain that right, I am justified in saying that the majority (50% +1) is lazy. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\\\"griece to chiamattt : yeah chiamattt, all Americans are lazy. so are all the faggots. And niggers and kikes. fuckin Catholics! Damned women! fuckin Yankees! Pansy ass redneck white trash! Flaky assed Californians! fuckin flyover state hicks! Damned homo Canadians! fuckin towel heads! muthafuckin injuns! Chinks! Yellows! Dykes! Euro trash! Aryans! Nazis! Christians! ASSHOLES ALL!\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOk, I donâ€™t know where you came from, and I certainly donâ€™t know what you are talking about. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\\\"please realize that making such sweeping epiphanies against any group- Americans or otherwise- isnâ€™t really cool nowadays. This isnâ€™t 1950- we try to be a little more civilly minded nowadays\\\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHuh? Isnâ€™t cool? Have you not been reading the news? Are you that blind to the way the world works \\\"these days\\\"? To oust Saddam the U.S invaded an entire people. To get rid of the Taliban, they invaded and entire nation. The \\\"French\\\" are against America. Itâ€™s everywhere.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the end my point is. Yes, perhaps I was being a bit general, though in the end, I still feel that until more than 50% of a nation votes, and until the considerable wrongs have been corrected by a considerable part of the populous, speaking together, and for action, then I will continue to think that for the most part, the American electorate is lazy.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3704,'<P>My german shepherd is a porn star. It started with his surfing the internet while I was at work. He found a porn site of german shepherds doing white chicks. The appeal is obvious.\r\n</p><P>\r\nYou know how dogs sniff each others butts. Well imagine your dog is like that with white chicks. You can\\\'t bring any girls home. You can\\\'t take him for a walk during the day. He will pull your arm off when women walk by. You absolutely cannot let him off the leash in the Dolores Park on a sunny sunday.\r\n</p><P>\r\nHe likes blondes. I don\\\'t blame him. I like them too. He likes the ones that smell like lilacs, especially, or any kind of perfumed blonde chicks. The difference between him and me is that he can smell the real blondes.  He isn\\\'t fooled by the fakes.\r\n</p><P>\r\nI\\\'m sitting at a cafe by the dog park.  A Paris Hilton look alike strolling down the street in a turquoise tube dress with multiple belts, pointy fuck-me pumps, and, judging by the Dick\\\'s pinned back ears, no underwear, walks by.   The Dick pops a lipstick and starts putting it on.   Any good smelling marina-slapper-preppy blondie causes this activity.  I turn my head and look away. It is too embarrassing. I just ignore it.\r\n</p><P>\r\nI was at the 7-11 the other day and left him outside. He had a full chub digging up this poor girl pumping gas at her car. She didn\\\'t know what to do. Ask him to wear a rubber?  She was barking, but her point wasn\\\'t getting across.  I have tried kicking him in the balls, like in that movie Garden State when the German, not unlike the Dick, starts humping emo-boy in the hospital waiting room and crash helmet girl comes to the rescue.  (The main difference being The Dick isn\\\'t gay.)   I tried this and the Dick bit me.  I\\\'d do the same if I was getting it on and somebody kicked me in the balls.\r\n</p><P>\r\nI started calling him \\\'the Dick\\\' shortly after the practice of running up blonde girls started. \\\"Did the Dick get you?!\\\" I try to introduce humor into it. The charm of the chub rubs off when the dick sticks his head under their skirt and starts licking. At first, they think it is cute. Then they realize this dog isn\\\'t just fucking around.  \r\n</p><P>\r\nI called a pet therapist. During the appointment, the Dick started referring to white girls as \\\'bitches\\\'. The Dick says this openly now.  The therapist addressing it asked him, \\\"Do you hate women?\\\"  My grip on his collar had relaxed.  He sprung from my hand, onto her desk, and started touching up her lipstick.  The receptionist heard the initial screams of surprise and came in to help me pull the Dick off the therapist.  Unfortunately, the receptionist was a blonde as well.  The resulting threesome was more than my nerves could stand.  I waited in the lobby for it to end.\r\n</p><P>\r\nThis happens sometimes.  The women like it.  I\\\'ll be trying to get the Dick down, and they both start fighting me off.  When that happens, I walk away.  The Dick can find his own way home.  Fuck him.  \r\n</p><P>\r\nHe has a dog tag collection now since I removed the dog tags I gave him.  I don\\\'t want people to know he\\\'s mine.  I wonder where he goes at night when he jumps the back fence.  I wonder until he gets home with a new dog tag.  His \\\'bitches\\\' give him different names: Don Juan, Wilt Chamberlain, Bill Clinton, Panic, Dammit, Toshok.  Those are just some recognizable ones.  There are a bunch of other pet names.  The tags have addresses from all over town from women who hope that he will come back.  I take the tags off and put them on his trophy ring.\r\n</p><P>\r\nAt first, all the tags were within about 15 blocks.  But the Dick found out about <a href=\\\"http://friendster.com\\\">Friendster.com</a> recently and his radius exploded.  He comes home now with dog tags from 30 miles away.  He\\\'ll be gone for days at a time.  But when he gets home, it is as though nothing happened.  I wake up and he is lying in the kitchen next to his dog bowl.  My only consolation is that the Dick doesn\\\'t speak English and doesn\\\'t know how to write.  He can\\\'t communicate to them where he lives.  I can already hear the neighbors complaining about a stream of blonde women jumping my fence at night and generally keeping everyone awake.\r\n</p><P>\r\n\r\nOne morning he came home covered in <a href=\\\"http://suicidegirls.com\\\">SuicideGirls.com</a> stickers.  I had seen fliers around town for some kind of naked girl circus.  I presume that is where he was.  Odd though, he doesn\\\'t usually get off on the tattooed and pierced girls.  A copy of <a href=\\\"http://www.bmezine.com/\\\">BME</a> showed up on the back of the toilet the next week, pages all stuck together.  Shortly thereafter, the Dick went out and got a Prince Albert.  He was whimpering and licking it for a week.\r\n</p><P>\r\n\r\nIf anyone has any advice, I\\\'d like to hear it. I\\\'m at my wits end with the Dick. I\\\'m going to investigate getting him a vasectomy. The last thing I need is a bunch of blonde german shepherd puppies on my door step.\r\n</p>'),(3706,'<p>there are gaps in the city, bits that didn\\\'t quite fit into the equation.  the space between buildings, the alcoves.  they weren\\\'t divisible by four, by six, by any real number.  cigarette butts and wet autumn leaves crush into the angle where the planes intersect; spaces where the pebbles shine with rainwater.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>at the height of the day, the sun is directly overhead and shadows are cast sharp and angular.  as the afternoon wanes, the light cuts through sideways and silhouettes are elongated on the sidewalk.  it is usually in this, the softer \\\"golden\\\" hour, that i love the city with a pang, a punch in the gut.  </P>\r\n\r\n<P>the city holds movement and trajectory, purpose and intent.  they weave in and around but snag on the gaps - the gaps where the story begins.</p>'),(3707,'DEAR SIR/MADAM, <br><br>\r\n\r\nTHIS IS A PERSONAL EMAIL T0 YOU ONLY. I AM THE SON OF SPAM SPADE, THE FORMER LOAN OFFICER OF NIGERIA, WHO WAS KILLED BY A RUSSIAN LADY WHO WANTS TO MEET YOU. HIS MORTGAGE RATES WERE SUPERB, BUT HIS PENIS WAS TOO SHORT, SO HE ORDERED 60,000,000 VIAGRA PILLS AND BEFORE HE COULD TAKE THEM HE WAS ASSASINATED BY 5 MILLION EMAIL ADDRESSES AT NO COST TO YOU. NOW I HAVE THESE 60 MILLION VIAGRA PILLS AND SEEK YOUR HELP IN TRANSFERING THEM TO YOUR ACCOUNT. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI AM USING THIS MEDIUM T0 REACH YOU. HOWEVER I HAVE NO OTHER CHOICE AND THIS IS STRICTLY CONFIDENTIAL, YOUR CO-OPERATION IS NECESSARY. YOUR RESUME WAS SELECTED AS BEING HIGHLY QUALIFIED, BUT THIS IS NOT SPAM! THIS IS FREEDOM FROM DEBT - CUT YOUR MORTGAGE IN HALF WITH OUR AMAZING REMOTE-CONTROL PASTA POT. THIS OFFER IS FREE. YOU HAVE NO OBLIGATION TO DELETE IT AT ANY TIME. <br><br>\r\n\r\nY0UR PILLS ARE BEING HELD IN AN INEXPENSIVE DIGITAL CABLE DESCRAMBLER BOX GUARDED BY TEENY TITS CARRYING EXPLOSIVE LOADS. THE LOWEST RATES ARE LOCKED IN, ALONG WITH YOUR FREE GIFT. THESE PILLS MUST BE TRANSFERED TO YOUR ACCOUNT USING A HAND-HELD ORGANIZER WHICH MAY BE OBTAINED FROM A FREE GOVERNMENT-AUCTION WEB SITE. BY VIEWING PHOTOS OF SINGLES IN YOUR AREA, INCLUDING HORNY WIVES, YOU CAN LOOK YOUNGER, LOSE WEIGHT, AND INCREASE YOUR STAMINA WHILE DRIVING PRE-QUALIFIED CUSTOMERS TO YOUR MULTILEVEL PROGRAM AND MAKE OVER $4000 PER MONTH GUARANTEED. THESE PILLS WILL NOT ONLY MAKE YOU LOOK 3\\\" LONGER, THEY WILL GET RID OF YOUR FLEAS AND TICKS FOR GOOD! SIMPLY COPY ANY DVD TO YOUR FREE INTERNET DOMAIN AND BEGIN YOUR ANTI-AGING PROGRAM TODAY. <br><br>\r\n\r\nTHE PLAN IS SIMPLE. Y0U WILL USE NORTON SYSTEMWORKS (A $300 VALUE FOR ONLY $38.95) TO PURCHASE 37 MILLION SHARES OF TONER CORP STOCK. THEN YOU WILL MEET ME AT THE WORLD\\\'S SMALLEST DIGITAL CAMERA WITH $30,000 IN CORAL CALCIUM. WARNING: DO NOT TAKE ANY HUMAN GROWTH HORMONE OR YOU WILL NOT QUALIFY FOR YOUR FREE GIFT. ONCE YOU RECONNECT WITH YOUR HIGH-SCHOOL FRIENDS, YOUR FREE CREDIT REPORT WILL BE SENT. WITH THIS REPORT, YOU CAN BUILD YOUR OWN ONLINE CASINO. ONCE INSIDE THE CASINO, YOU WILL SUBSCRIBE TO KARA\\\'S FREE FAN CLUB, WHERE YOU WILL BE ENTERED IN A DRAWING TO PROTECT YOUR IMMUNE SYSTEM. DO NOT FAIL TO DO THIS, OR I WILL REDUCE THE SIZE OF YOUR PENIS BY THREE INCHES! KARA\\\'S WEB SITE WILL POP UP 6,000 PAGES, ALL OF WHICH YOU WILL HAVE TO CLOSE BY CLICKING THEM. FOR EACH ONE YOU CLICK, TWO MORE WILL APPEAR. AFTER YOU COMPLETE YOUR ONLINE DEGREE (IN 8 MONTHS OR LESS), I WILL SEND YOU A HOT AMATEUR MP3 THAT WILL CONSOLIDATE YOUR DEBT USING ONLINE AUCTIONS. NOT EVEN CNN, CBS, ABC, NBC, OR OPRAH WILL KNOW OUR SECRET! <br><br>\r\n\r\nN0W Y0U WILL BE READY TO RECEIVE THE PILLS. YOU WILL USE THE AUCTION SITE TO FIRE YOUR BOSS AND CLAIM YOUR PORTABLE DVD PLAYER FROM THE SECRET ANTI-WRINKLE AGENT. NEXT, BOOST YOUR CELLULAR RECEPTION AND DRIVE OUR TEENY LOLITAS WILD WITH HUMAN PHEROMONES MADE FROM PRE-OWNED INKJET CARTRIDGES. YOU WILL TRADE THE PHEROMONES FOR A SINGLE GEORGE FOREMAN GRILL FILLED WITH A FREE THIRTY-DAY SUPPLY, WHICH WILL ENLARGE YOUR PACKAGE EVEN FURTHER. THE HERBAL INGREDIENTS IN THIS PACKAGE WILL INCREASE YOUR REVENUE PROJECTIONS BY 3\\\" AND CLEAN THE INNER WALLS OF YOUR INTESTINES. REMEMBER, THIS IS NOT A SLEEP REMEDY, THIS AMAZING PRODUCT TARGETS THE BACTERIA THAT ACTUALLY CAUSE SNORING! <br><br>\r\n\r\n0UR PSYCHICS ARE STANDING BY TO TAKE YOUR ORDER. GIVE THEM YOUR CREDIT CARD NUMBER AND WAIT FOR THE BEEP. YOU WILL BE GIVEN THE ULTIMATE SCIENTIFIC BREAKTHROUGH AND A FREE CREDIT CHECK. DEPOSIT THIS CHECK IN A PRE-APPROVED ACCOUNT, AND YOU WILL TAKE HOME 40% OF THE PILLS. <br><br>\r\n\r\nTHIS IS A VERY SIMPLE TRANSACTION. THE RESULTS ARE GUARANTEED. THIS IS A COMPLETELY SECURE INVESTMENT. I HOPE YOU WILL HELP ME AND MY FAMILY AS WE ARE IN DESPERATE NEED. PLEASE REPLY ASAP. <br><br>\r\n\r\nSINCERELY, <br><br>\r\n\r\nPOONLARP'),(3708,'Just as before, the ants had been busy. Boy howdy, had they been busy! My eyes traced the erratic two-lane highway of insects up and across the wall from where they came through the gap in the window frame. Yup, just as I suspected. They\\\'d taken a new route to the last target I had found them swarming over just prior to my nap. <br><br>\r\n\r\n  I had only been asleep for a couple of hours, but it seems that had been more than enough time for them to marshal their resources and strike back. They were making it clear that this was no longer a standard foraging mission. No, not even close. Under normal circumstances, their typical response to the blow I had delivered to their forces would have been to cut their losses; send out scouts to locate a new resource/food cache that presented a lower potential risk. Nothing personal. No grudges. \\\"Jus\\\' tryin\\\' to make it in the jungle\\\" my stepbrother used to say. \r\n  <br><br>\r\n  But here they were again, tramping all over the one thing in the kitchen that I could not view from a perspective of \\\"benevoent-cousin/creature-who-can-spare-a-morsel-for-a-fellow-animate-carbon-based-lifeform-strugglng to \\\'make it in the jungle\\\".  Yeah, there\\\'s food and then there\\\'s *FOOD* which I spelled P-I-Z-Z-A! They had retaken the pizza, swarming over half the pie. I didn\\\'t care, I was NOT gonna let them have it. I would not surrender this time. Nor would I allow their numbers to intimidate me. Nay, I would not stand by and allow this invasion, and they could stuff their tiny dreams of occupation up their tiny exoskeleton asses! I grunted and nodded to myself. As G. Bush Sr. had said, \\\"this aggression will not stand!\\\" The phrase still didn\\\'t make any sense to me, but it always caused a flood of testosterone and adrenaline to surge into my blood and brain. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  This was War. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  I prepared for my next strike against The Bug. I winced as I sat down at the coffee table/lab bench, the skin of my thigh stretching and then breaking open the still painful cluster of lesions caused by the Fire Ant assault. Just as well. I looked at thewounds and saw that they were once again draining, the yellow pus-like discharge oozing from half a dozen of the angry red lesions (I had forgotten to clean the wounds when I woke up - too busy with the \\\"morning\\\" fix) no matter. I proceeded to carry out the mission at hand. I carefully prepared an especially concentrated mixture of purified water, ketamine and meth-amphetamine (approximately 15% purity - unusually high for kitchen-lab speed), all the while humming one of those nameless, discordant little tunes  - tunes I\\\'d never heard before. They were eerie, like something composed for a Theramin. It seemed to self-generate in my throat or maybe it was merely my lips humming the anthem of some bored Loa, come to \\\"ride\\\" my tattered soul for a while, slumming in the corporeal. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  The thought of the word \\\"Soul\\\" ALMOST brought back to mind the REAL terror in/of my life, or rather what bits of it I had left to myself after this latest \\\"Lost Weekend\\\" of a month that had... NO! I wasn\\\'t going to listen to reason - not even if I was the one talking. Nosiree Bob! I tapped the last tiny air bubble out of the syringe and quickly tied off before my Id could force me to take a glance at bare, naked cold reality. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  I registered in a tortured but serviceable vein and as I pushed down on the plunger, watching the visual countdown as it slowly passed the progressively diminishing graduations on the side of the rig, I exhaled and detected the slight but distinct odor of ether on my breath (deposited in my lungs by that same half-a-CC of blood I had just been watching through the hazy plastic of my syringe) and felt the various muscle groups of my upper body start to relax as the fire started to gather in the tissues of my heart and mind. Bring it on, Id! I shouted in my mind, in a moment, I wouldn\\\'t care again - for a little while, anyway. The time was coming when the dosage levels necessary to occlude my so-called objective reality would reach O.D. levels. I\\\'d have to stop and detoxify then. The curse was still upon me and overdoses merely disabled or otherwise damaged my mind/body (in new and diabolically creative ways), but would not kill me. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  No, I wasn\\\'t suffering from some youth-slash-invulnerability delusion; I simply could not bring myself to an end. Not for more than a few minutes, anyway (this is actually one of the non-fiction aspects of this tale). And as recovery from the O.D. induced comas was more painful that the reality I was trying to escape in the first place, I didn\\\'t take it to that level anymore. But those thoughts were rapidly being washed away with another hapless horde of neurons. As vision slowly replaced the gray rush-haze, I was once again blissfully ignorant of anything that made me feel anything. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  All that remained was the Obsession, and today, that meant the ants. <br><br>\r\n  \r\n  I recalled that, In Our Last Episode -12 hours ago- just before I had gone to sleep (more like \\\"passed out\\\"), I had stepped up my attack to include The Torch - a Bernzomatic cordless soldering iron/welder. It was a bit over the top, considering the diminutive stature of my opponents - even the cats stopped their lickin\\\' and bitchin\\\' for a moment to stare at me with expressions of apprehension that seemed to say \\\"Wow. I knew he was bi-polar and a bit of a hot head, but...Jesus!\\\" They all flinched when I kicked The Torch on at full power. Sure, I felt a little guilty, standing there with a 6-inch flame jetting from my hand as I approached my miniscule nemeses. After all, they were just critters whose continued existence was dependent upon the cessation of some other critters existence.  My pace slowed as I thought \\\" Hmm... Kinda like us...\\\"'),(3709,'<PRE>\r\nphotography=# create table widget (id SERIAL, name TEXT);\r\nNOTICE:  CREATE TABLE will create implicit sequence \\\"widget_id_seq\\\" for serial column \\\"widget.id\\\"\r\nCREATE TABLE\r\nphotography=# insert into widget (name) values (\\\'dog penis\\\');\r\nINSERT 299508 1\r\nphotography=# insert into widget (name) values (\\\'ass fuck\\\');\r\nINSERT 299509 1\r\nphotography=# insert into widget (name) values (\\\'shit crap\\\');\r\nINSERT 299510 1\r\nphotography=# insert into widget (name) values (\\\'nutz\\\');\r\nINSERT 299511 1\r\nphotography=# insert into widget (name) values (\\\'666\\\');\r\nINSERT 299512 1\r\nphotography=# \r\n</PRE>'),(3710,'<P>\r\nMy boyfriend<BR>\r\nMakes me cups of tea,<BR>\r\nHe laughs,<BR>\r\nAnd says he feels so free. <BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nHe stretches out like a cat in the sun,<BR>\r\nSmiles, and says that Iâ€™m the one. <BR>\r\nLooks like an angel when he sleeps,<BR>\r\nMurmurs, whispers thoughts so sweet. <BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nMy boyfriend<BR>\r\nSits there in his chair. <BR>\r\nEyes glazed over,<BR>\r\nDoesnâ€™t hear. <BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nWants the pain to go away,<BR>\r\nThinks it might be here to stay. <BR>\r\nStarts to cry, and shout a lot. <BR>\r\nBlames the rain and dirty pot.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nInspired,  desiring,  easy,  warm. <BR>\r\nTired, despising,  cluttered,  torn. <BR>\r\nHe feels so much,  he doesnâ€™t feel. <BR>\r\nMy boyfriends,  theyâ€™re a package deal. </P>'),(3711,'<P>\r\nYou know Iâ€™m really glad I donâ€™t see the world through your eyes; I think I would probably shit myself. Occasionally I will catch a glimpse of your perspective and my stomach rolls with anxiety. \r\n</P><P>\r\nLeaving your house is not a big deal. Going for coffee?... not a big deal. Admitting that you fucked up royally but are willing to hurl yourself over the road block?...yep you guessed it, not a big-fucking-deal. Things have been so blown out of proportion that you anticipate everything to do the same. The cycle begins here because then you resign yourself to the idea that things will eminently get worse and they donâ€™t, so then you suck, cause why didnâ€™t you anticipate that. So you beat the hell out of yourself and the more you do this the better things get and eventually your shit becomes a self fulfilling prophecy. There you go, not as complicated as you thought!?\r\n</P><P>\r\nI used to buy into it, you know. The moodiness, the self deprecation, the apprehension, I used to think that it was how a successful person behaved to get beyond what was required of them, but itâ€™s not. None of this is as hard as you think it is. You cannot pretend to be overwhelmed by the expectations and justify your dull ambition with it. Its not expectations fault, its yours.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI think its time you turned around to see just how far away from your original goals you are. \r\n</P>'),(3712,'<p>In third grade I had this teacher named Mrs. Martinez and she was really mean.</p>\r\n<p>We went to the Petrified Forest on a field trip and at the gift shop I bought one of those cool pencils that\\\'s 1/3 normal pencil and 2/3  plastic clear casing full of small shiney rocks - with an eraser fitted in the top to keep them from spilling. This pencil was my most prized possession. I used it on all of my spelling and math tests.</p>\r\n<p>Everyone in my class hated me because I was from Washington State which was a foreign country. They told me I had an accent and no one let me get on the balance bars during reccess. </p>\r\n<p>This one day the kid next to me took my pencil during the quiet reading hour - spilled the rocks all over the floor. I don\\\'t remember what happened next except I think I must have yelled  or frowned because then Mrs. Martinez called in my parents for a parent-teacher conference and she told my parents that I was \\\"difficult and haughty\\\" and that I didn\\\'t talk like a normal child and should be moved to a \\\"gifted\\\" class in another building. Sounds like a positive thing but my mom says she spat out the word \\\"gifted\\\" like I had leprosy or demons and so I got moved to Mrs. Yingst\\\'s class where we made Hopi dwellings out of mud on the carpet floor and got to dress up for parties every Friday.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>I think that was the only time I saw my mom really angry as a younger kid. She didn\\\'t like Mrs. Martinez, she said, because Mrs. Martinez didn\\\'t like me. </p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m thinking of this because while I was in Illinois a few summers ago my mom, dad and I went to Pere Marquette State Park and my dad bought me one of those pencils and just now as I was getting my glasses out of my purse I dropped the pencil and it made a mess on the floor. But I got all the rocks back in and even added some pebbles I had in my Virgin Atlantic earplug case. The eraser has hardened and the pencil tip is all worn, but I like to keep it around to look at sometimes. The pencil has been in England and all over the west coast, and I\\\'ll bet that guy from third grade is still living in Arizona.</p>'),(3713,'Ã¿Ã˜Ã¿Ã Print InfoResolutionCopyright Flag\r\r\rÃ¿Ã€Ã›XwÂ¶Ã”;g<Â£oaz\ZqoÃ©â„¢ÂµÃ´Â¬Â¼Ã¡Ã©1XÃ§\n\\tsÂ«Ã–ÃŠÂ·{Å¸Â·wÃÃ½ÃµG;Â¢}gnEÅ½Â£Ã”ÃŒÂ VÃªrXÃºÃ£ÃšCÃ±Å¸YÂµÃŒÃµkÂ¯cÂ¨â„¢â„¢vÃŸâ€¦_MÂ©Â¦ÃŒÅ’Ãª,Â¥â€™aÂ¬Â²Ã›2Â®{Â¾?4nÃµÂ·k1Ã©eOÂ°ÃžÃ–Â°â€”Ã„?ÃŸ3Â»Ã©~Ã§Ã¸~â€¦fÃ‹,Ã¥)Âº\rÂ¢Ã…â€œ6YÃˆÃ€Ã‹â€¹Oâ€â€“ÃœqÃ“lÂ»Â¤Ã¥Ã™Å¸â€œ?mW=Ã‡*Ã’Ã­â€¢}Â¢}G:â€¹ÃÃ­ÃˆÃ´Ã®?Â¾Æ’?Ã¾kÃ”\\?VÃ©xÂµ[8npqÃºTÂ¹Â®nÅ¸Å¡ÃªÃœÃ¿-Ã©]:Â¶Ã˜Ã¦Ã—Ã©Ãv5ÃŽÃœÃ¨\rsK]-sâ€¹~Å â€”Ã­Å¾g*Â®OÃ§SÂ­gÂ²ÃŽâ€˜â€”GNÃˆÂ¡Ã™â„¢,Ã»=%Ã–ÂµÂ¾Â©Ã´Å¸}Å½Ã·mÂ¯Â¢Ã»Å¸Ã½OÃ´Å sWÃŠ~Ã†fÃ–Â½ÃŠSÃ§Ã?Â¨Wâ€°Ã“lÃ¶o6Ã“â€°[Ã¡Â»?lÅ’Â®Â¡[M?Â·Ã“Ã»Ã¿\r\r\rÃ¿Ã€Â¦ÂºnÅ¸â€Ã¬LL6dÂ¸Â¾ÃŒÃ›â€”â€™HiÃŸÂ¥Ã¿#Ã­Ã6Å 2Â¨Â¿kr?pkxÃ“Ã·ÃšÃ¯kâ€˜]Ã–Ã¦â€¹iÂ¯Å¡â€¦Ã-qfÅ“Ë†Ã­Ã»Â©ÃªÃ«7Å’KÂ±[}uâ€šÂ½Ã‚gÂ±Â¯Ã·PÃ¢?<10Â¸8Â¡/GÃ½Ãº}Â«â€˜â€¢ÃŒLâ€šxÃ¸2GÃµÅ“}Â£ÃœlÃ´Ã¼ÃÃšÃ®~mLyÃ˜\\Ã›Å¡!Ã€??Å ÃŽÂ¦Å“Â¬lCÃ”hÂ»fÃ›=\"3Ã³Ã¼ÃÂ¨Ã¯Ã«WKÃªÃ€Ã†f)Â°CÃ¬ÃœÂ£Ã¹*8=VÂ¼L\'bÂºâ€ ÃœÃ½Ã#iiOÃ«{R#I?ÃªÃŒÂ£Ã²Ã¾Å¸Ãµbâ€œ#>3(Ã†_Â­Å¸ÃÃ¾OÃ´xÃ¥Ã‚Ã˜Ã€ÃªÃ·fdWâ€¡â€º[2iÃ›Â¸Â´\r9YyÂ´Â·2ÃªeÂµÂ¼ÂµÂ¿Â ÃžÂ±â€°?.ÃÃ‚e7Â£Å“Ã Â²Å¾Ã§Â½ÃŽ{ÃŽÃ§8â€™Ã¢Hâ€™Jv*$Å½$|Â¼QÃ¹Â¿zÂ¢Ã‡Å¡#%â€“`â€œÃ‡Ã?â€”Ã·xÂ¥RÃ‰%Ã«!%?{?ÂµÂ¯Ã­dÃ›â€š]Â¾SÂ¿ÃŒÃ¿Ã“[SÂ¬Ã†Â½Ã’Ã˜ivÃ¦Ã«Ã¤Â±~Â­Ã¢Ã¢ggÃºyâ€¢ÃºÂµ6â€¹.,?Â²YÂ¶7mÃ¾Â²ÃœÃ©ÃŽiÃÂ¨	Ã·h?Â¨Ã¥?Ãµ@Ã”Å“Nâ€˜â€¡tÃ½Ã¬Iâ€™DÃ£Â£Å½RÃ Ã†~ieâ€œâ€ NÂ«:oÃ•â€¹Ã‰Â­Ã¸Â¥Â¤â€ºYcÃƒâ€žkÃ¬Ã—Ã©9&Ã }^Â²Ã]] XÃ·c}GIÃ¸Ã«Ã­Ã¾[â€œcÃ”Ã¼â€¹TÃ»ÃžA!Â¾07)4Ã£ÂºÅ“Å“â€™Ãº[â€ÃƒMâ€”Ã”Km`?ÂºwÃ·Ã˜â€¦#.8>g CÃœÅ¾Å¸Ã–Ã¹â„¢Ã›Ã’Ã¾Â¬UMWÃ•Æ’NKoÃÃ¯e?saÃ´ÃžÃšÃÂ»ÃÃ¯AÃ»\'Ã•ÃŽÃ%â€¡Ã»O*lÃ©ÃŸÂ²Âº\'MÃ©Ã¦Ã–ÃŸÃ¨6Ã€Ã›XkÅ¡^^Ã‡Ã¾oÂµÃˆÃ¸?AËœÃµzÂ¦&IÃ¶XX5Ã¾NÃ”Â«Ã?AÃQÃ‰2MÃžICN)]XWnÂµâ€Ã¾ÃŠÂ­Â¾Â£Æ’7Â¾DËœÅ¾TÅ¸Ã“Ã¾Â¯W?h*Â£ÂµÃ»7:yÃ›<Â£Ã£]Ã“ÃŽU[1Ã¬ÃžlnÃ’lqÃ¼Ã¤sdÃ¡â‚¬]qkÃ”kÂ»Å¸Ã»gÂ­Ã¿Ã¶ÃµÂ»ÃÃªÃ®Ã¿Ã‡Ã¢=Ã“CÂ½Â¯/sDÃˆmÃ â€¹Ãž?Ã¼Â¦Â­Ã¥}QÃ¿Râ€°nÃˆÅ’?â€c>!\\QÃ¹Â¢ÃºwNoFÃ©Ã”Å“l;Âªkâ€¹Ã‹MÂ¡Ã†OÃ’:Â»Ã¹+?ÃªPÃ¾Â¨u:Å¸?MÃ‡Ã–ÃžÃ«@Â®Ã²Ã²ÃÃ¯ÃµoÂ­ÃŽÃšÃ‹V\'I8XÃ¾Â®^aÃ½6Ã–Z9Ã·ÃŽÃ§ÃUÂ©Ã‰}bÂ¯+Â Ã˜,9>â€ºÃƒâ€ºÂ¨iÃ·Å¸ÃºMfÃ…^RÂ¬â€š=Ãœ>kâ€0Å“Ã¿Ã±SÃ­KÃªÂ¯Ã”nâ€hÂ­Ãd[Å’Ã‡zâ€”Ã˜Ã¸Ã¼Ã©.Ã¶ÃŸÃº:Ã—?WÃ”Ã²zÃ®uÂ½K*Ã»Ã‹Å½Ã‰?{{?Â§â€šJ	Â¨}N{Â \"CÂ´Ã¥u8DÃ’Ã¶Ã¬nÅ½â€“LA.Ã¤oÃ¾Ã‚Ã¥Â°Ã‹kÂ°?Â²NÂ ?@ Ã‹\\Ã ÂºÃ¾â€ºEÃŒÂ­Â¾Â±.vÂ¥Ã…Ãš?Ã‡Ã³Â·â€ Ã®Â§Ãƒ\"e-<5Ã½ÃšÃ¿dÂ¬Ã·W{Ã´5Â¿aÃŠÃ“â€œ_Ã¥)\'Â ~Â£â€œÃ±Â¯Ã²â€â€œÃ¯Ã“Ã¾Ã½Ã›Fâ€¡ÃžvÃ¿!<%KÂ½Ã‰w)Â£Â¸DmÅ’=Ã£Ã¢?Âµ^Ã…Ã€Ã[oÂ¸[Ã¿Â·1Â¬kÂ¹nÃ²Å¡zÂ¬Å“3,51?aZÂ¼ÂªKÂ·Ã½â€ºÃ“Ã¿'),(3714,'<p><i>actually, nevermind.</i></p>'),(3721,'<P>\r\nI was having a drink with Brandon at a bar called \\\"milk\\\". We were drinking copious amounts of kiwi and soju. Brandon is an idealist, and lets things bother him too much. Nevertheless, he reminds me of myself in High School. Heâ€™s a cool dude. We taught at the same school during my first tour of duty in Seoul. Halfway through my contract he left for California to do his masters. He was at school training to become a diplomatic translator. His English is great and he often beats me and Shane at scrabble. \r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3713_1.jpg\\\">\r\n<p>\r\nAfter some political discussion we started talking about music. He asks me if I like Alicia Keys and I shrug and say \\\"not really\\\". \\\"She is coming to Seoul this Wednesday\\\" he says with a twinkle in his eye. \\\"oh you are a big fan of hers?\\\" I ask. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe begins to tell me that he plays the piano and really likes her piano skills. He says heâ€™d love to see her play live, but that the concert is too pricey. \\\"How much is it?\\\" I wonder out loud. \\\"Its a hundred thousand won\\\"(87usd) he says with horror. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThatâ€™s when I was reminded of my good friend Alex and his exploits getting into shows in Korea for free. Heâ€™d just make up some fake back stage pass and go to the concerts with an entourage of Russian women. It usually worked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI knew we wouldnâ€™t have the Russian women available to us, but I figured we could come up with another plan. I proposed that I be the photographer, Shane the reporter, and Brandon our translator. Weâ€™d be journalists from the Globe and Mail in Toronto doing a piece on the Seoul music scene for a series of stories about music scenes from around the world. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe next day during a terrible hangover I collected some photos from the Korean sites promoting the concert and slapped together a press pass. Then I went to the office and printed three of them out on the Xerox color printer and then to my second job and laminated them. Oh how glorious they looked. I was confident the plan would work.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn Wednesday, after work I rushed home to collect my gear and meet up with Shane and Brandon. They were both in Suits complete with attachÃ© cases. They had the passes hung around their necks and it was all looking so good. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGetting into the cab we went over some minor details regarding our stories and blah blah blah. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe entered Millennium Hall at Central City Centre and confronted the Security guard. He quickly saw that the Press Passes we were wearing had a BMG logo on them and called someone over. Turns out the dude was the rep from BMG. He asks Shane who he spoke to. \\\"I have no idea, my editor arranged this for me. I was told to speak to a Mr. Lee. Thatâ€™s all I know.\\\" BMG rep, looking puzzled, says \\\"where are you from again?\\\", and without even a pause Shane replies \\\"Weâ€™re with the Globe and Mail.\\\" During this whole time Brandon is translating this all into Korean just as he had been trained to do in California. It was beautiful. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOur passes were quite similar to the ones all the reps and vips were wearing, though smaller. The rep just passed them back to us and said in English \\\"sorry to keep you waiting, enjoy the show\\\". \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo that was that. It was all so anti-climactic. I stayed for twenty minutes and left. Alicia Keys was putting on a good show, even though her brand of music isnâ€™t quite my cup of tea. Nevertheless, the plan worked, and Brandon got to see Alicia play the piano without shelling out a hundred bucks. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt was all good fun. \r\n</p>'),(3722,'<P>\r\nI am tempted sometimes to forget all of it. That is the reason why I donÂ¡Â¯t or didnÂ¡Â¯t say much before. Ever since the tub the other night, IÂ¡Â¯ve been thinking about how it goes. I should not have said so much. I gather that itÂ¡Â¯s really not that important to you, its just words about the past, but contrary to my blow offs it is more than that, much more. I havenÂ¡Â¯t done anything with this and I am slowly realizing that my place of storage for all of this is horribly crucial to what happens between us. I made much effort to avoid the topic but my indulgence has left tiny bits lingering in my distractions. \r\n</P><P>\r\nSometimes I wish you would say more. Quoting David Gordon Green is appropriate but doesnÂ¡Â¯t seem all that genuine. I still feel like there is a side of you that IÂ¡Â¯m not familiar with. I could be wrong though, it wouldnÂ¡Â¯t be the first time. I wish sometimes you werenÂ¡Â¯t so close, so that I could get all of this out with a big messy cry and not be bothered by it. There are things in you I wish I knew. Those nasty little bits that made you form perceptions and built you up to who you are. I donÂ¡Â¯t want to pick at scabs for the sake of doing so; itÂ¡Â¯s for my desire to know you completely, and to get rid of all of this clutter in my head. Things have been so comfortable so far and it makes me wonder why. Behind every smile I give you, is another smile so do not confuse this with my wavering on anything; I only want to make the smile deeper. \r\n</P><P>\r\nand I always wonder why I furiously bite my nails. (humph) \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(3715,'<img src=\"%%img1%%\">\r\n<P align=\"center\"><B>Peter Rhodamine, New York, New York</B></P>\r\n\r\n<HR>\r\n<img src=\"%%img2%%\">\r\n<P align=\"center\"><B>Austin Swinney, San Diego, California</B></P>\r\n<HR>\r\n<img src=\"%%img3%%\">\r\n<p align=\"center\"><B>Sean Dreilinger, La Honda, California</a></P>\r\n<HR>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3719_1.jpg\">\r\n<P align=\"center\"><B>Peter Rhodamine, New York, New York</B></P>\r\n<HR>\r\n<img src=\"%%img5%%\" width=504 height=378>\r\n<P align=\"center\"><B>Annabel Chong, London, England</B></P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3715_7.jpg\">\r\n<P align=\"center\"><B>Alana Jonze, Melbourne, Australia</B></P>'),(3716,'<p>â€œYou know, one time I was elected to be the leader of a secret organization, bent on overthrowing the world government, and replacing it with a government of intellectuals.â€</p>\r\n	\r\n<p>â€œUh huh.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYeah, but it mostly turned into an excuse to play Dungeons and Dragonsâ€¦â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She leaned against the bar, overawed by my manly story of intrigue and bravado. She sipped her drink, looking forward at the crowd, turning her ear toward me, obviously to better hear my sexy exploits.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œWe called ourselves the Syndicate. Yeah, I wasnâ€™t actually the leader, it was more of a triumvirate; we had a whole hierarchy worked out, and a motto and everything!â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYeah?â€ She cranes her neck. I think Iâ€™m finally getting a rise out of her!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œIt was in Latin!â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œHuh.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She gets up, and walks away (probably to get her coat, so we can go back to my place and make sweet, sweet lovinâ€™). Iâ€™m in the zone now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh, hugging another guy, is she? Playing hard to get? Well, Iâ€™ll just have to do the same!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œGo ahead! I donâ€™t care! Iâ€™m the leader of the Syndicate, dammit! I donâ€™t need you! I am the Dungeon Master!â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>She pretends not to hear me; though I know, by her faked laughter, and the way she sticks her tongue down that guyâ€™s throat, that itâ€™s tearing her apart. I just have that effect on women. I turn to the bartender.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œYeah. She canâ€™t get enough of the C-Money.â€</p>\r\n\r\n<p>â€œIâ€™m sorry, Iâ€™ve never heard of that drink.â€</p>\r\n'),(3718,'photo attached'),(3719,'photo attached'),(3720,'<P>\r\nDear religious fundamentalists and others of faith who are voting for our President incumbent George Bush.  I am sitting here in my liberal democratic, good and decent home pondering the incalculable, how a man with such a terrible record as President could possibly be re-elected.  How the polls show Bush and Kerry, neck-in-neck and the outcome terribly close. How 50% of the country actually supports the swine, the unintelligible baboon that has alienated us from the rest of the world to support his own zealotry (not to mention his pocketbook).  I sit here and ponder how it could be so and I come up with very little of sense.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nFriends have told me that Bush has a vast support group in the moral majority, the religious right and others (perhaps less fundamentalist) of faith but it baffles me that these people could not see how unchristian and unholy our drunken, coke-snorting retard of a President is.  Other friends have suggested that Conservatives and Republicans are driven by racism and are out to protect the sanctity of being white and rich.  However, there is also a strong block of African American voters who support Bush because they, too, are Christian and oppose gay marriage (and other rights that America should protect).\r\n</P><P>\r\nSince I cannot make head nor tail of the logic behind this tremendous difference of opinion I will throw out my opinion in hopes that it may sway a few of these so-called Christians to come-to-Jesus, as it were.  I understand that some of you believe that George Bush has been chosen by God to lead this country but have you ever thought, for one small moment, that perhaps he is batting for the other team?  No, Iâ€™m not calling him a lesbian, I mean, what if his right hand man isnâ€™t God or Cheney but the Devil himself.  Wouldnâ€™t that be ironic?  All you frickinâ€™ righteous weirdoes out there think youâ€™re voting for this GREAT guy and youâ€™ve all been tricked by Mr. Evil himself, that guy with red skin and horns whoâ€™s waiting down there in that hot place for the lemmings who can only do what their pastor tells them instead of thinking for themselves.  As a side note, I suspect that the real people going to heaven are the Atheists like me who think hard about critical issues of morality and right vs. wrong while making choices bases on hard wrought personal beliefs, NOT dipshits who pretend to be highly moral and then go out and hire hookers and snort coke.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBut I digress...\r\n</P><P>\r\nBack to Satanâ€™s duplicitous genius. Think about it, the nation is torn apart ideologically, the prospect of a never-ending crusade against the Muslim people is imminent, nuclear annihilation is a distinct possibility. Bush and his horny-headed team are working day and night in secrecy attempting to reduce our country from a land of freedom and democracy (hello, our founding principles!) to a land of dictatorship without separation of church and state (hello, another fundamental quality of our government!!).  Now, I may just be a heathen, a-religious yahoo but none of this sounds very GOOD at all.  I would reckon that most Christians donâ€™t want to die a nuclear death as much as the next guy.  However, I do understand the power of faith and the willingness to die for oneâ€™s cause or religion.  I can understand people supporting Bush if they believe it is the will of God, but the problem is, it ainâ€™t!  God lost a bet or something because what Bush is doing AINâ€™T Godâ€™s work.\r\n</P><P>\r\nLetâ€™s recap: Jesus Christ is NOT in the White House folks, we may as well refer to it as the Red House because Satan is at the helm, not the Almighty that you all are banking on.  Thatâ€™s right, you all think that Bush was appointed by God, anointed to the highest position in all the land to save your worshipping asses and conduct the world according to Godâ€™s will.  BOY ARE YOU STUPID.  I donâ€™t see anything Godly in the works of our President, all I smell is sulfur and Iâ€™m pretty sure that hump on his back is a pointy little tail.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3723,'<p><em>I wrote this over two years ago.  Resurrected for your pleasure.</em></p>\r\n<p>I wasn\\\'t going to vote today [April 30 2002]. The guy had a ~ 66% approval rating a month ago, and it wasn\\\'t likely to dip below 50%. I also didn\\\'t know which way to vote.</p>\r\n<p>As I walked out the office to get some lunch, I changed my mind and decided to vote. I was 28, I\\\'d never seen elections take place in this country, and I was curious about the procedure.</p>\r\n<p>The nearest polling station was just a couple of streets away, in a relatively poor and crowded neighborhood. I walked down there and found a large tent. A guy was sitting up front at a desk with some papers, and a few other people were sitting in the back, eating lunch.  There were no lines of people waiting to vote or anything.</p>\r\n<p>me: Is this a polling station?<br>\r\nguy: Yes. Do you have id?<br>\r\nme: Yes. (pull out my id card)<br>\r\nguy: Okay, you need to go to the other station. \r\nthis one\\\'s for people who don\\\'t have cards.<br>\r\nme: Okay, where\\\'s that?<br>\r\nguy: Right down that alley. (points)</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what the procedure is for people who didn\\\'t have id cards. Maybe they needed to show birth certificates or something.</p>\r\n<p>I walked down to the end of the alley, and there wasn\\\'t anyone there. I looked around, and noticed a \\\"polling station\\\" sign right next to an open doorway. A ha. Somehow I expected a crowd, or something. I think the polling station was in a school (did the kids have the day off?).  I walked in and saw two armed guards eating lunch, and saw some more people in the back where the polling station was. I walked over there, and gave a guy behind a desk my id card.</p>\r\n<p>He looked over the card and placed it on the desk next to another guy. He then asked for my hand and marked the back of my thumb with some ink. Just a small thin mark on my skin next to my thumbnail.</p>\r\n<p>The people clustered around the box were actually voting. The guy with my id card was taking down the card numbers, and having people sign (or, alternatively, place their thumb prints on) a piece of paper. He\\\'d then hand them a ballot and they\\\'d stamp one of two circles with their entry, and place the ballot in the box.</p>\r\n<p>This was all done in clear sight of everyone. I found that odd -- there were no separate polling \\\"booths.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>The ballot had two circles -- a white one with a cross mark, and a green one with a tick mark. I got my ballot, and I stamped in the white circle. The guy who handed me the ballot made a noise, as if to say \\\"oh, you\\\'ve made a mistake!\\\" but then caught himself. He smiled. I smiled. I put the ballot in the box, picked up my card, and walked out.</p>\r\n<p>The next day, the <a href=\\\"http://www.economist.com/agenda/displayStory.cfm?story_id=1100672\\\" title=\\\"the economist\\\">news</a> reported a large turnout (um...), and that a ridiculously high number of people (like, 95%) had voted in favor of el presidente.</p>'),(3725,'<p>\r\nSo for a third year in a row, the school I work at built a haunted house for the kids of the area. It was a lot of fun, but boy oh boy it was a lot of work. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe kids started off in one of the classrooms on the ground floor by watching the first half an hour of the new â€˜dawn of the deadâ€™ movie. From there they lined up at the end of a hall that leads to the second floor. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe school is in a building that used to be a hospital, so to get to the second floor there is a ramp. From the ground floor to the second floor we used refrigerator boxes and built a tunnel. We covered all the walls with cardboard. At the end of the tunnel one of the teachers stood shrouded in a dark cloth to scare the kids. The tunnel was perhaps the scariest part for them. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom the tunnel the kids went into some rooms that we do not use. We filled the rooms with things that glowed, screamed, and dropped down when there was noise. Some pretty cool stuff really. The rooms were perfect because they lead into each other and exited into another ramp area and into the second floor classroom area. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI was in the first room. The kids came up in groups of three or four. The rooms were almost pitch dark. We had hung a few really low flashing lights in each of the three rooms, but because their eyes were not accustomed to the dark yet, they could only see shadows and the glowing objects. They were scared shitless walking through there. As soon as they were all in the first room, Id throw a big ball of shredded newspaper at them jump out and grab their legs. I didnâ€™t make any noise, but they sure did. My god. The screams. The shrieks. The convulsions of pure terror. It was great. With some of the kids, Iâ€™d hang on to their ankle for as long as 30 seconds. I didnâ€™t make a sound. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom their they headed into the second room, where two of the Korean teachers were hiding. When the kids were in their, they would jump out and let out a shrill scream and flash a light on their blood painted faces. I think a kid shit his pants. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom their they trembled their way into the third room where we had clothing stuff with newspaper hanging from the ceiling. A couple of other teachers were in their with big plastic hands that they used to brush against the kids. HOLY CRAP they hated it. Hahaha. Then they entered the ramp area and thinking it was over let their guard down. Two more teachers jump out there and scare the crap out of the kids for the fifth and final time.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt was great. Some of the kids who think they are tough shit just collapsed on the ground crying. It was really great seeing them put in their place. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBecause the students of the school could bring friends, there were a lot of extra kids that I didnâ€™t know. Lots of bad language and a lot of really rude and terrible things being said to some of the kids who were there just trying to have fun. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIâ€™m a pretty big guy compared to these kids, and I donâ€™t care when they say I am fat. I just rub my belly and say â€œelephant babiesâ€. They laugh and itâ€™s over. One of my students is a pretty big girl for a twelve year old. She was in line waiting to go into the tunnel and I was talking with her while I drank some water. Then a boy who didnâ€™t go to the school comes up to me and says â€œwhy you talk to girl pig?â€ I was really shocked. The girl looked completely saddened by it. Not to let this kind of thing go on, I did a quick look up and down the boy to make sure he didnâ€™t have a hand phone or mp3 player around his neck and simply poured my water on his head in a swift motion of my arm. Then I said â€œIâ€™m a pig, but you are a wet PONG-GU(fart)â€. He was embarrassed so much. It was great. I felt great. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3726,'\r\n<p>It was another one of those Black Days. Dark sky. Darker thoughts. Body operating the car on the quick jump up to the City from Belmont. \\\'Done it a million times.</p>\r\n<p>But different, today. Unique in it\\\'s category of commute. This is one of those days where I\\\'m in the midst of executing. Committing. <p>Perpetrating. An Act That Cannot Be Taken Back. Oh, sure. I perform Irreversible decisions all day long beginning with whether to come to full consciousness (I\\\'m always getting suckered into the wrong answer on that one), and ending with whether to turn off the light/TV as I\\\'m drifting off into theRealm Dreams (short, low-budget morality plays written/directed by my unruly and frequently enigmatic subconscious).</p>\r\n<p>Ah, but an Act That Cannot Be Taken Back is one where:</p>\r\n<p>a) Ya know it\\\'s gonna hurt good and proper for as long into the future as you care to give even a side-long glance at.</p>\r\n<p>b) Ya probably already know a good hour or two prior to The Act itself, which of the two choices your likely to make.</p>\r\n<p>And it ain\\\'t the right one.</p><p>So Ya figure that, \\\"Gee, I\\\'m a rational being, and I should make a concerted effort to convince myself that the high road may be hard, but the benefits are...\\\"</p>\r\n<p>Meanwhile, my -currently- dominant aspects respond with: \\\"Yeah, yeah. Sure, sure. High Road, benefits, sure...\\\" whilst rocketing the \\\'89 Cutlass Supreme Two-door Coupe down a well-worn path that would have been as deep as a luge ramp, had rubber any effect on asphalt.</p>\r\n<p>\\\"...and you\\\'ll se just how pleasant a day you can have just window shopping in the Haight rather than...\\\" the Angels of my better nature drone on.</p> \r\n<p>What?</p>\r\n<p>That little guy in the red suit with the pitchfork, sitting on my right shoulder, helping me park the car just up the block from where the Dealers hang out? Dealers. Dealing out losing hands via slight-of-hand to the unschooled unwary and Well schooled but donâ€™t-give-a-fuck-no-more alike. Dealers hand a winner again!</p>\r\n<p>Wake up, little girl/boy. The house ALWAYS wins. Didnâ€™t your Mama and Papa teach you that? Go cry on someone elseâ€™s corner!</p> \r\n<p> Oh, that little guy in the red suit with the pitchfork? he\\\'s being just as courteous a listener to the guy on my left shoulder as he can be, offering positive comments and all, like: \\\"Yeah, window shopping. Maybe a cappuchino, eh?\\\"</p> \r\n<p>But there\\\'s always just a leetle bit more of me left over to see through the mental three-card-Monte I\\\'m playing on myself. Yup. My game and I\\\'m the mark. But I can never pull it over on myself COMPLETELY. W.S. Burroughs wrote: \\\"Hustlers of the world, there is one Mark you cannot beat: the Mark Inside\\\".</p> <p>He\\\'s right. <p>Even though I go through with an act that appalls even me, I can no longer prevent myself from seeing the emotional/mental/circumstantial train wreck I\\\'m choreographing/performing. And with quiet, desperate, fear I accept that another deep cut has been rent into the wound that never heals. My will, seemingly paralyzed and thus, willing-to-allow this horror, as I watch the engines race down the tracks (on my arms) toward mutual destruction in over-cranked slo-mo as hand grasp hand, not in friendship or even nodding acquaintance, but to exchange monetary resource for a concoction cooked-up from (literally) a half-dozen items from under the average suburban home\\\'s sink and maybe a few from the garage.</p> <p>All for the purpose of introducing it directly into the blood stream. Once that\\\'s accomplished, the flailing bodies and luggage, the soup tureens and the cooks in their soon-to-be-not-so-white uniforms cart wheeling comically (at least for this slo-mo rush occluded moment) through the air toward the rest of the wreckage, seem more like a blend of ballet and poetry... until the next Jones.</p>\r\n<p>But in-between \\\"treatments\\\" and That Which I Canâ€™t Take Back, the wound aches, it jabs the memory centers of my brain and reminds me of what can now join the ranks of unspeakable acts Ivâ€™e performed that can\\\'t be undone. People whom I\\\'ll likely never have contact with again who were friends. Their names and our shared experiences condemned to the photo Albums and video tapes of my personal \\\"Jacob Marley\\\'s \\\'Chainâ€™ Of Events.\\\"</p'),(3730,'<p>I am looking at the screen,</p>\r\n<p>Can not write anythin.</p>'),(3731,'<p>i stood in a bus shelter. it was surrounded by construction barriers. i looked down through my camera at the concrete at a stencil that had been sprayed there, on an open patch where autumn leaves did not lay. i kicked aside a few loose pages from a newspaper. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>a man walked into the shelter and looked down at where i was looking. in a french accent: what are you taking a picture of? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>this stencil on the ground.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he gazed at it and said</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it looks like bird, with the wings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i nodded without agreeing. i shot another frame. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>the french man said to me with a grin, i am not happy because this is my place for smoke crack. i told him i would leave shortly, and did. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i stepped out of the shelter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he slid down to the space between the shelter and the construction barrier. my eyes left him crouched and digging into his pocket with no great dexterity.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><center><img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"> </center></p>'),(3727,'...But with a little more acceleration, you can feel pieces of yourself ripping free in the inevitable drag of your mortal frame in the face of such raw, titanic velocity. A touch more...oohhh, soooo verrryyy close to the outside of the envelope, that ultimate edge you don\\\'t come back from - at that streaking existence between light and non-being - that\\\'s where you can tell that the bits tearing loose in that inevitable attrition war of speed vs. drag, are bits of your soul.'),(3728,'I woke up in the morning and remembered Yesterday,\r\nRealized I am the heaviest memories carrier\r\nUnwanted memories weight is unspeakable\r\nUnderstood, that Today will be even scarier\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3729,'<PRE>\r\nI woke up in the morning and remembered Yesterday,\r\nRealized I am the heaviest memories carrier,\r\nUnwanted memories weight is unspeakable,\r\nUnderstood, that Today will be even scarier.\r\n</PRE>\r\n\r\n'),(3732,'<P>\r\nWarm room, and throats, and beats from Hands. The context of this is meant to astound.\r\n<BR>So are the notions going around. \r\n<BR>My ice is gone and so is my rye. \r\n<BR>I love that Iâ€™m caught with you under the ground. \r\n<BR>The bottle is empty from drowning the sigh, but there seems to be more that is getting us high.\r\n<BR>The air in here is desperately found, \r\n<BR>And we are swallowing everything near,\r\n<BR>In attempt to prevent that which might interfere. \r\n<BR>This might happen you know, I know you do; pulses of the softest waves, pulses of you.\r\n</P>'),(3733,'<p>It happened for the first time a couple of weeks ago. I thought I heard a female moaning but when I tried to catch it again, it was all silent. The night after, and almost at the same time, I heard it again. I was standing in my bathroom brushing my teeth and my parents, brother and sister was visiting. But neither this time the moaning was distinct, I could be imagining or it could have been a newspaper-page rubbed against a window. Like someone would be cleaning their windows close to midnight. After that second time I have not heard a moan, until today.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If the other noises were made around 11PM this was right before 4PM. And it was very distinct. The orgasm-soon-to-be must have been huge, else she was one of them pornstar-exaggeratedly- groaning-cunts..</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When my neighbour, I\\\'m not sure if she is the same person as the moaning and groaning one, one year ago <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3500\\\"> cried and sobbed while taking a bath</a>, I did not mind her sharing her emotions. But groaning and sobbing are different from each other. I do not want to hear my neighbour groaning due to satisfaction while fucking her boyfriend. I am not living in an apartment to share the intimacy of my neighbours private- and sex life.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>Since I do not know if it is the girl below or the girl above, I cannot really put a note in her mail drop telling her.. Or, I could drop a note to both, telling each girl to lower her voice if she is the one groaning. But I do not really consider that option.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The other solution would be to put a note up at the notice board in the entrance of the building. Maybe I could state what I want to tell under the headline <i>Information on Sexual Matters</i>.. But since that note probably would be teared down by some old lady, I would not know if the message went trough. And I am not sure the rest of the people living in the house would appreciate a note like that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The ultimate revenge might be to play some hardcore porn at high volume with the sound output connected to my sound system. But that would in turn probably freak my own neighbours out too..</p>\r\n<p>What do you think?</p>\r\n<p>Maybe I have to live with it.. ?</p>'),(3734,'...to Tyler\\\'s \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3675#21593\\\">Youth</a>\\\"...<br><br>\r\n\r\n(Yes, Ladies and Gentlemen! Observe as, once again, the Human Sponge demonstrates his singularly bizarre and grotesque talent of Self Absorption. One moment he\\\'s there and then...PRESTO! He\\\'s made you all disappear!)<br><Br>\r\n\r\nYes. That\\\'s it exactly. It comes back to me in a dream every now and then. One of those really well directed ones where I never suspect it\\\'s anything but real life. At the end, when I wake, I\\\'m feeling the ache - HAVE been feeling it, to judge by the large, non-drool pool on my pillow. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nThing is - for all the heartache these dreams bring back up, I\\\'d have them every night if it were mine to say. GOD! It\\\'s pretty darn pitiful that a grown man feels more alive unconscious than awake - and I think that\\\'s the hook - with the reliving of that ache comes the reliving of that unspeakable, indescribable joy! Oh, the \r\nrapture I felt just walking around downtown, Chinatown, window shopping, walking, talking, reading a book drinknig coffee - whatever! That she was there was all I needed, it seemed. And, because of what \\\'life\\\' had been for me prior to that, I was nearly always bemused, amused on the verge of giggling, wondering what minor MAL-functionary in the bureacracy of the functional Universe, what wingless Clarence-the-Angel, had fucked up and allowed me/her into eachothers lives, me into her heart.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nBut then the inevitable happened, inevitable ONLY because all things must change - the only guarantee you get from this life - and, although I was bright enough, sensitive enough to see and feel what must come, come to be endured, I was like a doctor having an apendectomy -I knew at least as well as any one else what was to come, but when the anesthesia wore off - YIKES!!! It hurt ten-fold more than my body was willing to endure.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nNeedless to say, weak, cowardly and unwilling to suffer pain of the soul as I was, \\\"Physician, heal thyself!\\\" was not the battle cry in the face of those slings and arrows. Uh-uh. Cupid\\\'s got a new gun, boys and girls, and I ain\\\'t got no flak-jacket for this kinda shrapnel. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nSo, I made a lateral move, I became my own anesthesiologist - a profession I have a tendency to return to now and then. Turn the dial up to ten! I don\\\'t want to hear anguish of my own thoughts, the replays of my own memories, distractions were weak, the gravity of her presence in my mind, my heart -the heart of my life, she was- was too powerful. All thoughts, all stimuli, and all thelies I told myself about the GREAT BIG WORLD AND WHAT IT HAS TO OFFER! All this slid in an ever increasing, elliptical spiral towards the heart of the pain, the pain in my heart. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nAh-HAH! EUREKA! EXCELSIOR! VISITACION VALLEY! Turn the gas knob round a bit more, breathe \r\nthe ether! a little deeper! spike the vein and away goes the pain! Well, it\\\'s still there in the end but hey! <br><Br>\r\n\r\nThen, I can just go out, steal yer Sony (sans doubt, sans conscience), and VOILA! We\\\'re back in business, the business of oblivion, the business of dodging Cupidic darts...Whups! There they are in my heart. No matter! they\\\'re no more than decorative quills, my badge of dishonor (Yeah! Dis On Her!) when I\\\'ve put that hotdiggiddydamnthishitsgonnamakemyheartburstmyskullexplodetaketherigoutmearmwhenihitdafloor in to my vascular system, I don\\\'t hear her. I don\\\'t hear me. I don\\\'t hear you. I don\\\'t hear those who care, not even my own conscience (not that the poor, starved neglected bastard could speak above a whisper at that point),  nor for that matter do I hear the shouts, curses and oaths of my oh-so-loud selfish little fears, hatreds, lusts or anything else in this rat cage of a life but- - - - - - - - - - - thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, FLURB!!~ thump! FLURB!~ ratatatatatatatatatatat....!!!   FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP!FWUHMP! AHHHH! All I hear, all I feel, all there is, is my superchargedjetfuelfromunderthesinkingredients blood. Blood makes noise. Blood and beats. Blood and beats are now. Not then, back or fore. There\\\'s now. no more. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nWhew! Where the Hell\\\'d that come from? (rhet.)\r\n'),(3735,'<p>your heart is not-quite-racing with pleasure and adrenaline. your stomach feels mostly empty, clenched tight and too high. it\\\'s going to take a long time for sleep to creep from your head down the warm length of your body. \r\n</p><p>\r\nyou turn your head and look at the stranger lying next to you, meeting their eyes with a startling spark of recognition (it sends a little <i>ping</i> to the base of your spine), softening in an instant to a gaze of gentle, curious affection and bemusement. \r\n</p><p>\r\nyour lips are connected to your heart, perhaps, your skin to your smile. \r\n</p><p>\r\nhis secret thoughts make your toes curl. you hold your breath to imagine the possibilities.</p>'),(3736,'<PRE>\r\nGreen and spaced out\r\nItâ€™s a beautiful morning on the farm\r\nWhere\\\'s your track-lighting now, chicken-run?\r\n\r\nWeâ€™re here to give you milk\r\nwhole or non-fat\r\n(soy need not apply)\r\nYou can choose which cow we use\r\nThen kill\r\nYou can choose which body part of yours is expendable\r\nWhich one you will alter first.\r\n\r\nWide and formica and splendid and white\r\nItâ€™s a beautiful morning in the office\r\nStainless steel everything\r\nSo cold on the floor that your sweaty feet stick and pull\r\n\r\nWeâ€™re here to give you surgery\r\nLips, tits, hips.\r\nYou can choose which eye you want plucked\r\nSliced.\r\nWhich one will gnaw off your botox-filled cheeks.\r\nEyebrows, you have completed your final expression.\r\n\r\nI brought my laptop so itâ€™ll be easy to record.\r\nWe can write down everything.\r\nDocument our thievery and boredom.\r\nRemark how cleverly weâ€™ll look back on our past.\r\nOne day.\r\nOne day.\r\n\r\nYour boots are soft.\r\nYour determination is soft.\r\nYour bones are soft.\r\nSoft, not unlike frail wood.\r\nSplintering and unstable.\r\n</PRE>\r\n'),(3737,'I\\\'ve been an 1111 freak for 20 years.<br><br>\r\nI started attending  LA Avengers arena football games 3 years ago. Before i knew it, I had a small part that was showed weekly on the Big screen whenever I went to the games.\r\nAfter that year I was looking at a newsletter from the Avengers. I saw the address of Staples Center in Los Angeles is 1111. Anything positive that will happen to me will be as a direct result of setting foot in Staples Center.<br><br>\r\nI now have season Clippers Avengers Laker tickets. Lots of sports shows are taped in Staples Center. Tom Arnold is always there at the Best Darn Sports Show, Fox Sorts Network studios right there at Staples. Does Fox TV have something to do with the 1111 number?<br><br>\r\nI might be an  1111 guy looking for a 1111 girl.<br><br>\r\nWhat do the any of these teams have to do with 1111? Lakers Clippers Avengers Sparks Kings X-Games...  all in Staples Center.<br><br>\r\nOne guy I work with has an 1111 birthday. Will he do somthing to me someday? Will I become a millionaire on the 11th day?<br><br>What does it all mean?\r\n'),(3738,'<p>well here we are then; the next step. it\\\'s been a surreal few months, that\\\'s for sure.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>since last we met, dear swinney-ites, i\\\'ve been jabbed, poked, prodded, cut up, injected and irradiated more times than i care to count. for someone that used to be so afraid of needles as a child, that he would insist on having his teeth drilled for fillings with no anaesthetic (what the hell was i thinking!?), times have certainly changed...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when this whole thing started i was determined to keep a diary of everything that i was going through. some sort of noble, matryr-like catalogue of horrors that might enlighten the masses and help fellow sufferers, or whatever. but the more i saw of other people going through the same thing, and the more people gave me their sympathy, i felt like a fraud.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so instead, i think i\\\'m just going to rattle off a few memories that really stick out...</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* buzzz. grrrr.</b> lying on my back under local anaesthetic as they drilled a hole in my skull. this is a very strange experience; rather like being at the dentist, with the strange numb vibration being in the wrong spot. i could hear the grating of the bone and the sound of the drill, but it was remarkably easy to get through. i think if i\\\'d had to watch someone else do it i might have fainted though.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* orange carpet vision.</b> shortly after the drilling, with a drainage tube freshly inserted into my brain, i blinked and everything i looked at was suddenly covered in a thick-textured orange material. now this freaked the hell out of me, not least of all because the doctors had absolutely no explanation for it. the \\\"carpet\\\" texture-like stuff subsided over the next couple of days, but the orange held fast. it wasn\\\'t until i was given synthetic morphine for my headaches that it went away. i literally blinked and it had gone. the doctors still had no explanation for this one.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* the visit from blue.</b> one of the last people i ever expected to drop in to see me was blue. a crass, sexist, over-the-top producer / director who rents the facilities at work from time to time. when i first met him i couldn\\\'t stand him, but he\\\'s one of those guys that kind of grows on you after a while.  with so many people being wonderfully nice, and so much sympathy and understanding around me for nearly two straight weeks in hospital, blue\\\'s unique persona was actually a breath of fresh air. \\\"well, you look fine to me... get the fuck out of bed and do some work you lazy prick.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* the metallic taste.</b> radiotherapy of the head tastes like metal. imagine licking a 9 volt battery (go on, you all tried it when you were kids), while being strapped face down in a head harness for thirty minutes, and having people draw all over your spine with magic markers. now you\\\'ve pretty much got the whole experience. it\\\'s usually at least a few hours before the headaches and dizziness start; the treatment itself gets quite easy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* horrifying portents of the future.</b> radiation therapy makes your hair fall out. this i was prepared for. what they don\\\'t warn you about is that it starts to come out unevenly and gradually. i had the horrific experience of seeing my hair begin to thin out across the top of my head, to the point where i now know what i\\\'ll look like when i\\\'m 75. it\\\'s not pretty. i hope the bald look is in around 2046.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>* truly terrifying.</b> off all the things i saw in the radiation oncology department, i think one thing will stay with me forever. two parents, faces blank and unfeeling, resigned to what they were going through. the father carried a small screaming bundle, wrapped in a blanket, the mother walking dutifully next to him. from under the blanket came a chorus of shrieking wails and screams. the baby could not have been more than six months old. next to the father walked an orderly, wheeling along a drip-feed machine, with four bags of saline and other medication. the machine was connected to the crying blanket-bundle with four long plastic hoses. i think this has to be one of the scariest things i have ever seen, and i feel like through all of this i\\\'ve had it pretty easy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so now it\\\'s just a matter of sitting around and waiting for my next set of test results just before christmas. all going well i should be able to return to work in the new year. i feel like i\\\'ve cheated or something. i mean, my god, i had an inoperable tumour in the middle of my brain, and it had metastisised and spread to my spine. now i\\\'m just tired and have to work my way back into the real world (assuming that the test results are okay of course).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>am i really this lucky?</p>'),(3739,'<p>The world is too much today <br>\r\nand a kiss like that could undo me. <br>\r\nLast night you gripped my hand tightly <br>\r\nand turned with a smirk to say <br>\r\n\\\"Let\\\'s have us a secret affair.\\\" <br>\r\nNow I\\\'m buried in spreadsheets and war dead,<br>\r\nreplaying every word we said<br>\r\nand the absurd bright blue of your stare.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I wonder if the jolt was created,<br> \r\nfate manufactured by chemicals, <br>\r\nor among those greater pushes and pulls<br>\r\nthat leave us unburned, and sated. <br>\r\nThe answer does not matter, perhaps, <br>\r\nand we should just admire the flood, <br>\r\na fresh chain of circuits wired in blood <br>\r\nsparked between tongues and kneecaps. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>I liked that littlest moan when I kissed<br>\r\nyour neck, and how you shoved me against the car.<br>\r\nI can\\\'t stop wondering where you are,<br>\r\nand if I shouldn\\\'t stop analyzing this.<br>\r\nThere will always be imperfect loves,<br>\r\nand at the moment you\\\'re spoken for.<br>\r\nWe crave the battlefield but not the war&#151;<br>\r\njust a few more jolts, and a few more shoves.</p>\r\n'),(3740,'Oh girl. The night cries out for you <br>\r\nThese soft clouds, bright stars, <br>\r\ncold grey stones weep for you this night.<br> \r\nWe have been robbed of you, <br>\r\nof your vitality and laughter, <br>\r\nof your quiet moods and soft moments.<br> <br>\r\n\r\nWhat is this strange world we inhabit? <br>\r\nWords fail me utterly. <br><br>\r\n\r\nFor you i wish a thousand thick carpets to cushion your fall, <br>\r\nOr a thousand wings to bear you up, <br>\r\nto carry you through countless sun-drenched days, <br>\r\nEach clear moment strung like amber beads <br>\r\non the delicate chain of time <br>\r\nI wish you could grow old, wise, and filled with peace. <br><br>\r\n\r\nOh girl. You have entered a dark garden, <br>\r\npassed through a gate where we cannot follow <br>\r\nWe wait outside, smoking barefoot, <br>\r\nThe cool stones beneath our feet <br>\r\nwet with the rain\\\'s tears. <br><br>\r\n\r\nWe wait outside, and we cannot wake from this dream of life. <br><br>\r\n'),(3741,'<P>\r\nSo in the midst of my hoop jumping to get into co-op, I found myself in a room, with eight other people, being interviewed. A simulation of a job interview, pretty much just to see if you can converse in the English language. I managed to be one of the few in the room born to this country so I think the interviewers thought they would throw me some of the more challenging questions beyond the typical: \\\"What is your favorite class?\\\"etc. The first one I got as they cycled through the row of students was, \\\"If you were an animal what animal would you be, and why?\\\" I was half expecting them to start chuckling along with me as this was my initial response. After realizing they were serious and all eyes on me, I cleared my throat and choked down my laugh, to search my head for an actual response. What the hell is the answer to this question? I threw out some lame ass thing about being a bird and was shocked to see them nod and smile amongst each other. \r\n</P><P>\r\nMy brother got asked at an interview once, if he could pick a celebrity that best fit his personality, who would it be, and why? Heâ€™s a lab coat toting, conservative, fraternity, smart guy, and after answering one too many of these such questions; blurted out, \\\"Busta Rhymes!\\\", the interviewer asked \\\"And why is that?\\\", he then responded with \\\"...cause Iâ€™m CRAZY!\\\" shook his head about, then promptly got up and left. I couldnâ€™t get this story out of my head, and was dying for them to ask a similar question. \r\n</P><P>\r\nNext was, \\\"If you were part of the first group of inhabitants on another planet, what would you not like to bring, and why?\\\" The first part of what!!?? There are just too many inappropriate answers to this question that as I mentally scanned and cross out various retorts that promote my passion for lo-brow, I found I could not think of anything interview worthy. Since then I have asked this to various people and got some pretty quality answers. So here is my question to you. What would you make a point of not bringing if you were making the move to a different planet? \r\n</P>'),(3742,'<P>six7nine: black reeboks?<BR><BR>x: hey, what\\\'s up?<BR><BR>six7nine: nuthin\\\' nuthin\\\'.  just living life, i guess.<BR><BR>x: be glad you are not in retail during the holidays<BR><BR>six7nine: had a bad shopping experience?<BR><BR>x: no we\\\'re in the middle of the achewood orders rush it is crazy here<BR><BR>six7nine: yeah, i wish i had such bad luck.<BR><BR>x: \\\"when the gods want to punish us they give us what we want\\\"<BR><BR>six7nine: \\\"once they get what they want, they\\\'ll never want it again.\\\"<BR><BR>x: unless they (me) only ask once a year and then have a year to forget how mad it was<BR><BR>six7nine: i think i\\\'m about to commit an act of literary fraud.<BR><BR>x: I hope it\\\'s at least from something good<BR><BR>six7nine: well, i had an idea for this high-concept screenplay (prison break story set in hell), but it isn\\\'t executing well.  and my parents are selling their house in the spring, and i have sentimental attachments to it, and i want to buy it.  it\\\'s a screenplay by an alcoholic writer who did a couple of things for hollywood.  unproduced.  only copy is in the rare works section of a university in the southeast.  thinking about going there and reading it and changing the plot and dialogue enough so that it won\\\'t be recognized.  i don\\\'t think it would be recognized, anyway.  i could probably just put my name on it.  i don\\\'t imagine there are many people outside of academia who\\\'ve read it.<BR><BR>x: Well, you\\\'ll always get called out eventually on something like this, but what some call plagiarism some call homage. Anyhow, many great dramatic frameworks are reworked slightly all the time.<BR><BR>six7nine: well, it would be fraud.  case closed.  i would have to change it.  and it probably wouldn\\\'t get bought, anyway, if \\\"closer\\\" tanks.  bears a similarity to it.  so dot dot dot can you divine the status of \\\"achewood:  the filmic juggernaut\\\"?<BR><BR>x: truly i hadn\\\'t considered the project since last we spoke of it, I\\\'ve been branching out into original art and retail more<BR><BR>x: need the money sooner, of course...need the creative efforts to definitely pay off in the near-term kind of thinking. we\\\'re expecting a small one soon.<BR><BR>x: a screenplay is a gamble and a huge one at that<BR><BR>six7nine: what if somebody would give you some money if you could commit to doing it in, say, the next five years or so?  oh, wait, you\\\'re expecting?  well, mazel tov.  congrats.  yeah, it would be a gamble, but i think there are people, like i said, who would probably give you some money now without even a story.<BR><BR>x: Well if money was lined up to support the project then yes, I\\\'d be both flabbergasted and interested in learning more. Are you really that charming?<BR><BR>six7nine: i could talk the ears off a donkey.  yes, i think it is possible.  do i have your blessing to try?<BR><BR>x: with whom?<BR><BR>six7nine: lion\\\'s gate.  again.  still.<BR><BR>x: after my recent book deal fell through I am once burned by dealing with people who aren\\\'t me.<BR><BR>x: was that the studio that did Cabin Fever?<BR><BR>six7nine: yes.<BR><BR>x: Haven\\\'t talked to eli in a while. I wonder if he works with them still. do you know his people? I figure name dropping is good. He wears achewood Ts in a lot of his press photos.<BR><BR>six7nine: he would be the person i would go to first.  his agent.<BR><BR>x: sounds like you could get a foot in the door. what\\\'s his name?<BR><BR>six7nine: jay baker.<BR><BR>x: http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0303816/business\r\nCabin fever made a boatload of money for them<BR><BR>six7nine: yes, i saw it, and was not really impressed, but he is very well connected.<BR><BR>x: it was a good exercise in the genre<BR><BR>six7nine: it was a good premise but fell apart halfway through.<BR><BR>x: how so? the disease killed everyone, like it was supposed to.<BR><BR>x: plus, don\\\'t watch movies like that with such a careful eye<BR><BR>six7nine: i would have to watch it again to tell you, can\\\'t remember, but that\\\'s what i thought at the time.  yeah, you\\\'re right, wasn\\\'t meant to be great cinema, was it?<BR><BR>x: it was meant to be fun and gory, and it was a pretty good first effort, I have to say.<BR><BR>x: I would be interested in working more with comedy and not so much on scaryness.<BR><BR>six7nine: you mean you or him?<BR><BR>x: me<BR><BR>x: he is good at scary stuff<BR><BR>six7nine: yeah, everybody\\\'s got their niche.<BR><BR>x: I would never actively seek to watch horror films but I enjoyed it well enough<BR><BR>x: plus they had tons of free Guinness at the opening night in SF<BR><BR>x: \\\"premiere\\\"<BR><BR>six7nine: i won\\\'t watch scary, SCARY stuff.  it premiered in sf?<BR><BR>x: no, it was the Sf premiere though. drumming up publicity<BR><BR>six7nine: is it going to be a boy or a girl?<BR><BR>x: can\\\'t tell yet<BR><BR>six7nine: if i had one, i wouldn\\\'t want to know.  would want to be surprised.<BR><BR>x: it\\\'s a surprise whenever you find out, I view<BR><BR>six7nine: yeah, my sister says it\\\'s a different ballgame.  kids.<BR><BR>x: there are no shortage of people who want to tell expectant parents that. So - movies. You would like to tell Jay Baker that the creator of Achewood would be interested in developing a screenplay?<BR><BR>x: I guess it\\\'s a \\\"spec\\\" screenplay?<BR><BR>six7nine: and would probably like eli involved in some production capacity.<BR><BR>x: definitely, we talked about perhaps collaborating on something once<BR><BR>x: how do you know this jay baker?<BR><BR>six7nine: i\\\'ve never spoken to him.  i just know he\\\'s eli\\\'s agent.  what i would do is call and ask if jay reps eli in his producer\\\'s capacity.  the only muckety-muck i\\\'ve talked to is john sacchi at lion\\\'s gate.<BR><BR>x: hey, if you want to do the cold-calling...what\\\'s your financial interest acting in that capacity?<BR><BR>six7nine: right now i\\\'m not worried about the financial interest.  that will take care of itself along the way.<BR><BR>x: how do you see your position if the project continues to advance successfully?<BR><BR>six7nine: as a producer of some sort.  associate, co-, whatever.  really i would be doing whatever it took to help you out and make sure the thing went smoothly.<BR><BR>x: And why would you want to be that guy?<BR><BR>x: I\\\'m not grilling you, I\\\'m just asking the base motives, you know.<BR><BR>six7nine: well, producer is basically a creative position.  i\\\'m smart, i know what works, maybe how to fix what doesn\\\'t.  i just want to be involved in the joy of making this thing.  midwife, i guess.<BR><BR>x: So why is Achewood your choice? (I feel like I\\\'m giving a job interview)<BR><BR>six7nine: because it\\\'s funny as hell.  it resonates.  people get a kick out of it, and it takes them out of themselves.  uh, because i\\\'d be better with funny than i would with scary?<BR><BR>x: Did you like Mr. Show?<BR><BR>six7nine: i\\\'ve never seen it.  but i like david cross.  chicken pot pie.<BR><BR>x: What about Woody Allen?<BR><BR>six7nine: yes, woody\\\'s one of my touchstones.  did you see \\\"the front\\\"?<BR><BR>x: No, but I just watched Sleeper and it blew me away. Have you seen \\\"The Office\\\"?<BR><BR>x: I mean, I watched Sleeper again for the first time in 15 years.<BR><BR>six7nine: no, but somebody said it was better than \\\"seinfeld\\\".  i have a hard time believing that, but it sounds good.  yes, i like cerebral humor.  i like adam sandler, too, so who knows.<BR><BR>x: You have not seen two of the most important comedy productions in the last ten years if you have not seen those two shows!<BR><BR>x: I highly recommend them.<BR><BR>x: What else can I ask you...<BR><BR>six7nine: if i like mel brooks.<BR><BR>x: you have to like Mel Brooks, as a human<BR><BR>x: Do you think the major Achewood characters are solidified enough to work well in a screenplay?<BR><BR>six7nine: ray, yes.  roast beef, yes.  lyle, yes.  teodor and philippe, perhaps.  rest are less realized.<BR><BR>x: Of the major players, which one do you think needs the most character development?<BR><BR>x: Do you follow their blogs? I have been able to develop a lot of their personalities more quickly there.<BR><BR>six7nine: teodor.  he is a real unknown.  there\\\'s a lot to him, especially in his journal, that suggests there\\\'s something else there we don\\\'t know about.<BR><BR>x: yes, I don\\\'t have his backstory completely visualized. I will write my character sketch out on the back of a check from Lion\\\'s Gate, perhaps!<BR><BR>six7nine: i would say teodor is the most like you.<BR><BR>x: comparing my blog to teodor\\\'s, you mean?<BR><BR>x: we both seem to cook and otherwise be general-purpose characters<BR><BR>six7nine: no, comparing the personality (you) that is expressed through the strip, etc.<BR><BR>x: how can you get my personality through the AIM window?<BR><BR>six7nine: i\\\'m an artist.  i\\\'m a student of human moves.<BR><BR>x: that was critically vague<BR><BR>x: What do you think the plot of an Achewood movie should be?<BR><BR>six7nine: i don\\\'t know.  i know a lot of people through the internet and feel i know some of them better than my associates in real life.<BR><BR>x: I have felt that about Internet-only acquaintances.<BR><BR>six7nine: plot?  well, you can either have a quest or a variety show.  plot-driven or altmanesque.<BR><BR>x: hm, those are the only two options?<BR><BR>x: I\\\'m no master of the craft, you tell me.<BR><BR>x: Ideally I\\\'d like to craft the thing like a Bertram Wooster novel.<BR><BR>x: It\\\'s where Seinfeld and David learned their tricks<BR><BR>six7nine: i don\\\'t know.  i\\\'ve never finished one.  my best efforts so far have come from just starting and seeing what\\\'s next.<BR><BR>six7nine: i must look into this wooster personage.<BR><BR>x: Wodehouse, the author, is the most brilliant comic writer I can think of. Douglas Adams said he owed it all to him.<BR><BR>six7nine: was he a satirist?<BR><BR>x: of the human experience...but with a lighthearted, bright outlook.<BR><BR>six7nine: yes, i like optimism.  \\\"it\\\'s all good.\\\"<BR><BR>x: Find yourself a Bertram Wooster novel immediately. You will probably thank me.<BR><BR>x: P.G. Wodehouse is the author.<BR><BR>x: OK, I\\\'ve got to get back to making tomorrow\\\'s strip. Thanks for popping in again with this movie idea.<BR><BR>six7nine: okee, will do.  need to go.  shall i contact the appropriate parties tomorrow?<BR><BR>x: as you wish. Please keep me apprised of your progress.<BR><BR>x: Best of luck with that.<BR><BR>six7nine: over and out.<BR><BR>x: ciao</P>'),(3743,'<P>What do you do when you find that special someone and they are borderline scitzo?  Part of them is scared of you, part of them is drawn to you, part of them wants to hurt you just because.  Is it so easy to walk away when you have been shown what could be, even though it\\\'s not what is at the moment? I\\\'ve lived this dilemma off and on for over 7 years now.  I grow weary of chasing the dream and have learned some hard lessons.  I just recently started to remember who I am and have started to follow my personal code again with a fanatacism that would do religious fanatics proud.  I have discovered that my patience and endurance for the back and forth are slim to none although I can feel his every waking moment echo inside me though we are not together.  How do you sever a bond like that and accept being alone? Am I making any sense?  Probably not, I never do to anyone but me.</P>'),(3744,'<P>my friend patrick came into treatment about a month after i did. 21 years old. kind and funny and smart. a fellow junkie.<BR><BR>brian, my roommate from rehab, called friday afternoon and said that pat\\\'s girlfriend found him dead in his apartment thursday night. overdose. i saw him at the narcotics anonymous meeting sunday, and he seemed fine. maybe it was the first time he used since he got clean. that would make sense. (but of course, the whole thing is senseless.) when you\\\'ve been clean, cognitively, you know you can\\\'t do too much, but the addict in you wants to go for the bellringer, so you try to gauge it just right. but it\\\'s not really an exact science.<BR><BR>i\\\'ve overdosed twice. luckily, there were other people around. these days, i have to keep death in the forefront of my mind. it has to inform all my decisions. i don\\\'t want to die.<BR><BR>he liked hip-hop.  he liked too short.  he liked tool.  he worked with his father (also a recovering addict) repairing air conditioners.  brian used to tease him, tell him he looked like a monkey.  (he did.)  he used to tell brian he looked like a gorilla.  (brian does.)  he would cheat at spades and cribbage if you didn\\\'t watch him.  he wrestled for a little while in high school, before he dropped out.  we used to spar a little bit in the laundry/lounge area in our dorm.  he kept wishing there were fine girls he could gawk at in rehab, but there weren\\\'t.  (hell, we ALL wished for that.)  he called the women we did have \\\"a bunch of muskrats.\\\"  he was incredibly witty and profane.  he and his girlfriend were going to get married next year.  he wanted to have a family and have a good life.  i don\\\'t know.  the dark side.  it\\\'s hard to cage.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>well, i got my ass beat. (you knew that was coming, didn\\\'t you?  sooner or later.)  that\\\'s one of the occupational hazards of posing as a badass -  every once in awhile, somebody calls your bluff.<BR><BR>c & j were having dinner with jamie\\\'s boss saturday night, and i was supposed to meet them at their house at ten.  got home from the meeting at nine-thirty, headed out on the bike towards the highway.  it\\\'s all sidewalk, so i wasn\\\'t worried about anything.  got as far as the best western (hotel), and a car comes out of their driveway just as i\\\'m crossing it.  it happened in slow motion.  they stopped just in time.  i coasted down the sidewalk a little bit and got off the bike.  it was a small car, i don\\\'t know what.  woman driving, man in passenger seat, two kids in back.  the guy had his window down.<BR><BR>i said, \\\"no harm, no foul, huh?\\\"<BR><BR>the guy said, \\\"that\\\'s right.  you just watch where the fuck you\\\'re goin\\\'.\\\"  hillbilly accent.<BR><BR>i said, \\\"YOU\\\'RE the one who almost hit ME, you ignorant cracker fuck.\\\"<BR><BR>he said, \\\"well, i tell you what....\\\" and fumbles for the seat belt button.<BR><BR>i said, \\\"that\\\'s right, sir.  step out of the vehicle.  come right over here.  let your kids see you get your ass kicked.  that\\\'s somethin\\\' they\\\'ll remember for the rest of their lives.\\\"<BR><BR>turns out he was a karate man.  some kinda man.  some kinda martial arts.  he explodes out of the car and runs the hell up to me.  i think i was in shock.  i put my hands up to defend myself.  about ten feet from me, he kind of does this step-jump thing and kicks me in the side of the head.  i put my arm up, but it didn\\\'t help anything.  i go down.  he kicks me in the side.  kicks me in the head again, same place as before.  i\\\'m out.  next thing i know, i\\\'m on the sidewalk, the car\\\'s gone.  feels like somebody peeled my onion.  don\\\'t guess you should call a man out in front of his kids.  probably a mistake.<BR><BR>wheel the bike up the driveway, go in the lobby and ask the clerk to call 911.  go to the emergency room.  bike gets put back in the office.  call jamie\\\'s cell.  police.  c & j get there.  get an mri.  (i wish i had a hundred dollars for every mri i\\\'ve had.  i\\\'m surprised my head doesn\\\'t glow at night.)  get some x-rays of abdomen.  mild concussion and bruised kidney.<BR><BR>back at the hotel, look for car and/or people matching my recollection.  none found.  decide they might have been eating at the restaurant and not guests.  police say they will check back later.  to c & j\\\'s.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>jamie got drunk last week (no, week before that).  i was asleep with the bedroom door shut.  (no a/c on, cool weather that night, window open, but, you know, The Fear)  it took christie calling three times for me to wake up.  she said jamie had gone out after work for drinks with terry, fellow engineer, said he\\\'d be home by seven.  (they aren\\\'t drinkers.  wine with dinner occaisonally, and that\\\'s about it.)  she called him at eight-thirty, said he\\\'d be home soon.  again at ten-thirty.  wasn\\\'t answering his phone.  she called me a little after midnight.<BR><BR>we went to jilly\\\'s, freddie\\\'s, the reef, boulevard beef and ale, the boulevard lounge, the village, jackie\\\'s place, sail inn, the german experience, alex\\\'s place, the gay bar, and the cotee river lounge.  he was at the last place we looked.  (isn\\\'t that how it works structurally?  something\\\'s always the last place you look?  by definition?)<BR><BR>the navigator was outside the cotee river lounge, right up the street from their house.  it\\\'s nominally a biker bar, but there are other people who go in there.  we enter, and all the eyes go to christie.  jamie\\\'s at a table in the corner by himself, nothing to drink on the table, just sitting there.  i figure he might have been cut off.  we say, \\\"c\\\'mon\\\", and i guess he thinks that\\\'s a good idea.  he\\\'s walking very stiffly, like drunk people do when they\\\'re trying to compensate for the jimmy-legs.<BR><BR>he isn\\\'t saying anything.  i tell him, without preface, to give me his keys or i\\\'ll whoop his ass.  get him into the passenger\\\'s seat of the navigator.  christie drives her mustang on home.  i drive us back to their place.  i say, \\\"you doin\\\' alright?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yes, asshole.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you better not start it.  it\\\'s not hard to beat a drunk man down.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i\\\'m not drunk.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i call bullshit on that.\\\"<BR><BR>inside, sitting around the table, he\\\'s hungry, wants some toast and jelly.  we ask if he\\\'s tired, if he wants to go lay down.  doesn\\\'t.  christie goes to bed.  we sit there talking about dogs, dogs we used to have.  he gets up, looks in the fridge.  i get up and stand by the counter.  he closes the fridge and comes over to me.  he reaches in his pocket and slaps a blunt down on the counter.<BR><BR>he says, \\\"you know what that is?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i can make an educated guess.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i paid twenty-five bucks for that.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, i hope it\\\'s good.  why\\\'dja buy it?  don\\\'t you have weed here?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i dunno.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"put it back in your pocket or in your box in there or somethin\\\'.\\\"<BR><BR>he pockets it.<BR><BR>he says, \\\"matt.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"mattie.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what?<BR><BR>\\\"matthew.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what, motherfucker?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"whatta you do, man?  what goes on?  whatta you do?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"about what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"just what d\\\'you do?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i live my life.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"some life.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"hey, fuck you, man.  it\\\'s the only one i got.  someday it\\\'s gonna be different.\\\"<BR><BR>he puts his hand on the back of my head and then puts the other one around my waist, and we embrace.  hug hard.  hug some more.  i pull back enough to look him in the face.<BR><BR>i say, \\\"you wanna know a secret?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you\\\'re about to get your shit pushed in.\\\"<BR><BR>he laughs.  \\\"no.  i\\\'m.  not.  you are.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i doubt you could get a stiff neck at this point.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"don\\\'t.  you.  be.  so.  sure.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"no, i\\\'m just jokin\\\'.  it isn\\\'t gonna happen.  you\\\'re gonna go lay down now.\\\"<BR><BR>he acted like he was going to resist at first then i guess thought better of it.  hugged him some more.  got his clothes off and got him tucked in, and christie was up by then, and we all talked about when we were little, about christmas.  he passed out fairly quickly, and we waited a little while to make sure he was out, then she took me home.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>david has designs on becoming a pot dealer.  says a guy, friend of a friend, will sell him a pound for five hundred bucks.  i tell him, first off, he doesn\\\'t have five hundred bucks (lives with his grandmother, does fuck all, has a little job with a tree service once in a while.)  i said, even if you did have it, you would be getting burnt.  a quarter pound of regs around here goes for three, three-fifty, somewhere in there.  there\\\'s no way you could get four of those for five hundred.  if it really is a pound for that price, it would have to be shitty, shitty weed.  you don\\\'t want it.  they\\\'re trying to rip you off.  he remains determined to pursue this career path.  i tell him, don\\\'t bring any of it or anybody around here.  i don\\\'t feel like going to prison so close to christmas.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>i walked to the convenience store one night last week to get a soda.  walked past a jeep, saw two women, the backs of their heads, didn\\\'t pay any attention to them.  got even with the door on the driver\\\'s side (if it had a door), and a woman\\\'s voice says, \\\"hey.\\\"<BR><BR>i turn and look:  two women, look to be in their mid-twenties.  the driver is unbelievably gorgeous.  she\\\'s wearing shirt, shorts, adidas watch cap.  her skin has this dewy glow.  her face is about halfway-tan with a pink undertone.  incredible legs.  brownish-blonde hair.  (and i have a weakness for girls who drive jeeps or volkswagen bugs.  they\\\'re a class apart.)<BR><BR>her friend is not so hot.  but i would not throw her out of bed for eating crackers.  no i would not.  she wasn\\\'t hot, but she\\\'d do until a hot girl came along.  (actually, she <U>was</U> hot.  in any other situation, she\\\'d be the hottest woman in the room, but she was outshone by her friend.)<BR><BR>the driver says, \\\"you like to party?  you wanna come party with us?\\\"  i notice there\\\'s a case of bud lite in the back seat.  for some reason, girls like that will, a lot of times, drink bud lite.  dunno why.<BR><BR>i came to another of those crossroad moments, in more than one way.  i say, \\\"well, i don\\\'t party, but i\\\'ll give you my number if you want to get together some time and have dinner or whatever.\\\"<BR><BR>she says, \\\"well, nobody says we <U>hafta</U> <U>party</U>.  i dunno, we could sit around \\\'n drink kool-aid or somethin\\\'.\\\"  her friend snickers.<BR><BR>i think, <I>ho, shit, this is a cat of another breed, entirely.</I><BR><BR>crossroad moment again.  i say, \\\"listen, i haven\\\'t had a shower; i need to shave.  i\\\'m really not fit to socialize.\\\"  i watch as my hand, seemingly of its own volition, lifts up from my side, moves over and comes to rest on her thigh.  she looks down at it, up at me, kind of grins and clutches it, lifts it up and holds it between her palms.  i kind of shake it back and forth a little.  all in the space of maybe five seconds.<BR><BR>i say, \\\"how about a raincheck?\\\"  exchange numbers.  karyssa.  and i think to myself, <I>matthew michael, isn\\\'t your life complicated enough?</I>  i guess not.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>jamie\\\'s birthday was sunday.  that night, we went to tampa, to bern\\\'s, a world-famous steakhouse.  not much for me to eat at the world-famous steakhouse.  had a salad and some bread.  told jamie december 5th was also the day that mozart died.  he said i was morbid.<BR><BR>told them it was also the anniversary of the u2 concert at tampa stadium in 1987 where my girlfriend and i had a fight,  and she and my friend bert started making out during los lobos\\\' set (during \\\"will the wolf survive?\\\", if memory serves), much to my consternation and that of the girl bert brought.  we were all so fucked up.  we went back to our room at the motel six by the interstate (bert was already eightteen), and bert and my girlfriend passed out in one bed and the other girl (julie?  judy?  something.  saw her in gainesville five or six years later, and she didn\\\'t remember me.) in the other and me with a blanket on the floor.  we were awakened around four in the morning by them having sex.  the other girl and i left and went to steak and shake and smuggled in jack daniel\\\'s and put it in our shakes.  i didn\\\'t hold anything against bert.  i never did.<BR><BR>later, back at theirs, we\\\'re making love.  do it slow.  but you can hardly stand it.  but it\\\'s so good.  listening to a mix cd i brought.  lush stuff.  in between.  <I><B>crying</I></B>.  <B><I>lloranda.</I></B>  (christie:  this is from <B><I>mulholland drive</I></B>, right?  jamie:  we just heard this.  me:  it\\\'s different.)  lie there and just listen.  it\\\'s better than drugs.  some things are better than drugs.  <I><B>in dreams</I></B>.  (christie:  i\\\'m sensing a theme here.  jamie:  what was this in?  we saw it.  me:  <B><I>blue velvet</B></I>.  jamie:  oh, yeah.  that movie was so trippy.  the other one, too.  both of \\\'em had me trippin\\\' out.  me:  trippin\\\' out.  yeah.  i\\\'d like to see you trippin\\\'.  please.  jamie:  you just ask christie right here if you want my credentials.  me:  yeah.  i\\\'ll take you down to mexico, to real de catorce, sometime and see what you\\\'re made of.  that\\\'s where you separate the men from the boys.  jamie:  you can\\\'t do that anymore.  me:  well, i don\\\'t know.  i\\\'ve got a prescription for an antipsychotic, anyway.  that would be like an insurance policy.  jamie:  yeah.  i think you\\\'re policy\\\'s been canceled.  no refund, either.  they laugh.  <I>i can\\\'t see them there, anyway, in that city on a mountain in the middle of the desert.  with the galaxy in their eyes.  some bridges you don\\\'t want to cross.</I>)  <I><B>lovers of today</I></B>.  <I>i\\\'ll never be a man in a man\\\'s world.</I><BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>brian came and got me sunday afternoon.  sue and manny were already in the car.  the four of us are the only ones who are still clean and sober (and alive) out of the group of fifteen to twenty who went through treatment at roughly the same time.<BR><BR>up to st. michael\\\'s.  sat there a while.  casket up front, closed.  his family came in.  jesse looked over as they went by and nodded at brian and i.  he knows us.  sue and manny are strictly alcoholic.  his family seemed to be holding it together.  mother sniffling.<BR><BR>the mass.  didn\\\'t know what to do.  just did what brian and manny did.  they were raised catholic.  i thought, <I>he would have picked different music</I>.  his brother got up to say something, got about a minute and a half into it, voice breaky, and broke down.  priest put his arm around him.  he sat down.<BR><BR>mass lasted about an hour.  graveside service for twenty minutes.  then the four of us got some coffee and talked for an hour.  and then it was over.<BR><BR>after so much life, so little death.</P>'),(3745,'It just occurred to me that I want to share these experiences, crazy just like life that happened to a real person, me. Selfish I know, and don\\\'t really care.  It covers about 6 months of my life and I figure I\\\'ll continue it from there.  Read it or not, I just feel better knowing that maybe just maybe someone out there is hearing my story.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>7/22/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s odd to be writing in a journal again as I haven\\\'t used one for years.  Life is very frustrating these days. The only really  nice parts at this moment are my kids and Dexter. Funny to say that as he used to be the evil black hole in my life sucking in everything good and leaving behind pain, rage, and anguish.  I\\\'m trying so hard not to become bitter and cynical but today is harder than some.  I have my parents contstantly nagging about cigarrettes, food, why are you with Dex, Dow this, Dow that, the kids got left here while you were gone, spend more time with us etcetera.  Right next door is my exhusband who is irate because I\\\'m back with Dexter.  From him is either glares or ignoring me and he can\\\'t seem to make up his mind whether he\\\'s accepting that it\\\'s over, fighting to save his marriage, or he hates me and it can\\\'t be over soon enough. I think we\\\'ll skip the friends as they aren\\\'t active in my stress levels for the last few days.  I can\\\'t explain to myself why I\\\'m back with Dex except to say that he makes me so happy.  I can be completely myself, he takes care of me and I have this hallpass from the Creator that states that this is my path for now.  Should any of these things change, I will follow the path laid before me then too. Gotta cook lunch will write again soon.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>7/27/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nDow and Scott came into Hardee\\\'s Sunday while Dex and I were there with the kids.  It was so uncomfortable.  Mikel was yammering away all excited to see them, if looks could kill I think I\\\'d have died on the spot.  They left.  I saw Mark Ferris at M & H last night and pretended not too as I figure he is Dow\\\'s friend and God only knows what he\\\'s told him.  I\\\'ve written off Jen and Tawny, I\\\'m very hurt and angry about their behavior.  My parents are making demands as usual but at least Justin and Jeanne and I are speaking again.  We\\\'re supposed to get together Wednesday and hang out.  I\\\'m really looking forward to it. I took my physical for the hospital job today cross your fingers and pray I get it.  The only time life seems to hold joy any more is when I\\\'m with Dex, he makes everything brighter, even when he\\\'s being bitchy, HEE HEE.  So far so good. Hopefully soon we\\\'ll have our own place.  Have to check on Squeaker, catch you later.  Dow just picked up the kids.  Fuck it, I don\\\'t know why I bother.  I tried to tell him about the kids and he shone me on.  I told Mom he can deal with her from now on as he won\\\'t listen when I try to tell him about the kids.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>August 16, 2004:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nDad and I had a huge fight about a week ago, so I\\\'m living at Sharon and Bernie\\\'s now.  I went to pick up the kids on Sunday for church and Dow asked how long I was keeping them.  I told him until around 1pm.  He told me to talk to him when I was ready to spend some real time with them and slammed the door in my face.  What did he think I was doing Monday through Friday??So I called that night and left a message for him to let him know that I\\\'m not watching them while he works anymore.  I will take them on my Monday through Friday nights off from 3pm until 9pm and every other Saturday and Sunday when I have them off.  Told him I\\\'d pay $150/month child support and that I expect to have them from 7pm this Friday until 9pm this Sunday.  I hate it because I didn\\\'t want to be a part-time parent but I don\\\'t see that he left me any choice!!  Well later, gotta catch the bus.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>9/3/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nWell, I got the job and things are still good with Dex and I.  Every day is something new.  We tweak together about once a month.  I don\\\'t know what happens to me, I become wild, savage.  I am so spun right now that I\\\'m probably going to ramble for awhile, but at least this way I AM FREE!!  To write what I really think, my confusion, my fears, my walls.  The biggest problem Dex and I are having right now so far as I can see is who he knows I am on the inside and him trying to push, pull, drag her out.  He\\\'s causing a, well, hurricane is too mild a word but it will have to do for now, inside of me.  I feel like I\\\'ve been, not half dead per say, but numb for my whole life and then he came back to me this man that I loved so much he damn near destroyed me and offered me all of my secret dreams, happiness, dark desires willingly without judgement or any pattern that so called normal people could possibly perceive.  I am on sensory overload after so long without REAL passion for life, now it consumes me at odd moments every day and it terrifies me.  What if I let go, show not Dex because he knows, no maybe even him and show them all the me locked so carefully away, what if then like now at my most vulnerable point emotionally, spiritually I should be destroyed.  I see who I am and what I have become and I hater her, spiritless, spineless, passionless and yet I cling to her because with her I cannot be hurt.  So the harder he pushes for my true self, in a panic, I fight terrified to truly feel and yet I want it so badly.  I wage this war inside every day.  I fell stupit trying to tell Dex that I am so reserved because I am keeping myself under tight control because I feel like I\\\'m flying apart.  I accept his pulling, pushing, dragging of my inner self because I want her freed too, but then I become afraid so I pull away from that as hard and as far as I can and the storm rages higher.  Even thought it\\\'s a very painful process, I know I cannot become free without him.  How do you bare your deepest, darkest, most terrifying sould and say to a man that feels every single time he cause that pain, how do you tell him to ignore that feeling he gets at your pain and to continue because it is what you need, what you must have to stop your inner soul from fading into nothingness??  I try to explain why I am so cold and remote but the true words won\\\'t come and instead comes from my mouth useless justifications and pitiful excuses.  What will become of that person should he become sick and tired of the outer me?? How will I survive if he should just throw his hands up in the air and walk away?<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>CAPTIVE:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nI can see the person inside I want to be,<br>\r\nI can see her beauty and she is just out of      reach.<br>\r\nBut outside is the one who survived, the strongest one left,<br>\r\nAnd she cries out to her sister, in pain, afraid and bereft.<br>\r\n\\\"What will become of me if I lose control?\\\"<br>\r\nNightmare visions she paints of suffering untold.<br>\r\nCowering inside her primitive cage, she shies away,<br>\r\nFrom her laughing carefree self of yesterday.<br>\r\nWhen there was freedom, joy and a nonchallant heart,<br>\r\n\\\"How do I find the light,\\\" she sobs,\\\"after so long in the dark?\\\"<br>\r\n\\\"What will become of me?\\\" she screams as she come undone.<br>\r\n\\\"What will become of the Captive One?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/4/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat a great impression to make at a new job that I really want; 2 absences at the last minute and one day being two hours late!!Dex and I started in at about one o\\\'clock this morning nothing but hard thrusting anal, awesome that was.  Then from like 2am to when I woke up nothing but giving head.  Had to be from like 4am on in my sleep.  Snapped out of a daze to realise it was now 6am.  Fuck me, supposed to be at work!!  Lightheaded and dizzy I threw on my clothes, called work to let them know was walking and didn\\\'t know how late I was gonna be.  Got clear up to 4th Ave S before I realised that there was no way I was going to walk 32 block in less than 2 hours by then it was 6:30am or so.  My bank opened at 8am so I figured I would e better able to handle residents etcetera after a cab ride than a 2 hour walk uphill most of the way and I wouldn\\\'t be any later either way.  I finally got to work at 8:37am and everyone understandably was pissed.  However due to the incredibly hateful attitudes of the nurse, I\\\'m wondering if a)the gut from West didn\\\'t pass the message on or B)they think I still live 3 blocks away.  I asked the nurse who I reported to about my break and maybe I came across wrong because she got an even bigger attitude than before and said I could report to her.  I asked if it would be all right if I went and she asked if I would stay until 12:30pm, why not so I said OK, and started serving lunch.  Now it\\\'s 12:45pm and I still have 4 showers to give, vitals, and charting, oh well, serves me right for being late.  Better get to work and pray this migraine goes away sometime sooner than never.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/18/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nSo here I am again running out of energy and about to get more.  I used a stick for the first time successfully lat night and I understand now why Dex likes it so much.  I don\\\'t have quite the same as he told me about, but close.  It\\\'s like a freight train rushing through your body until suddenly it washes over you and all you can do is take deep breaths and quiver with exhileration.  Bear with me as I\\\'m trying to follow three lines of thought at the same time, four it you count the stick.  Okay, first shocker.  Dex asked me last night how I\\\'d feel about doing Jason. Out of nowhere this crazy bitch tells him sure I can do that.  I\\\'m really kinda scared of who is being released but I\\\'ve already told you all that.  Can you believe I\\\'m not going to back out even so??  Next fear of the moment, shall we??  Every time something new comes along like this I get a warning signal from what feels like the instinctive level.  Maybe fals alarms from the Captive One?? God I hope so, because I can\\\'t think of the other possibilities without cringing in horrified disbelief.  I\\\'ll get to that in a minute.  Part of me rages every time I think about these horrible possibilities that she doesn\\\'t give a fuck about unknown consequences, that she wants to fucking live free and do we believe in this love or not?? Do we have faith in the Creator and the path we swore to follow NO MATTER WHAT HAPPENED ALONG THE WAY or not??  I tend to agree with her, but I am ever cautious, especially where Dex and Chant are concerned, overly so??  Captive One just keeps whispering her horrible stories of what could possibly be actually happening here and sometimes caught off guard I listen and I have to admit that I become afraid.  I try to shake it off but it keeps coming back, echoing softly, whispering of a pain so deep I\\\'m pretty sure it\\\'ll kill me, I\\\'m sure that\\\'s what Captive One wants, but acknowledging that doesn\\\'t stop the unexpected tidal waves of panic.  So here is what keeps haunting me.  It\\\'ll be incoherent, terror usually is.  What if this wonderful man I\\\'ve come to worship with my soul is a creation of Chant?  What if he is meant to gain my trust, my soul, free me into this Hybrid of Gypsy, Jade, and Sunni, only to slowly tear her apart and watch me die??  Even now writing this I can feel the cliff dropping feeling in my stomach.  Not that any of these fears will change the direction I am walking on this unknown path to which I was led.  I swore an oath to my Creator and if I should be torn apart walking this path then so be it and fuck the consequences as Hybrid is so fond of saying. I\\\'m just hoping that reading these fears I write about will make them less intimidating like thinking of a horrible nightmare in the clear light of day.  All right already, ENOUGH.  Good news.  I think I\\\'ve figured out part of TANNON NAZGUL, TANNON=teacher.  ALmost had the meaning for NAZGUL too but it escaped me before I could put it to memory.  Next as Dex and I chatted today about Chant and the committee and his students, he was talking about \\\"the one\\\" who would teach him something he didn\\\'t know among other things.  Inside where I feel these things was a click louder than any I heard before including when I saw the raven and started this insane journey in the first place.  I think it means that I am that one.  All day I\\\'ve had the compulsion to look at him and say \\\"teach me, I am yours as you are mine\\\"  Ah here comes the Captive thought.  What if he thinks I\\\'m only saying this because of what he told me happens between him and his students, to prevent it, as if I could.  What if he says No Way in Hell.  And even as I thought about this before I wrote Hybrid informs me that we shall continue to tell him this until he agrees to do it.  And yet again I feel that overwhelming kick in the ass from the Creator aolng with that unyielding sense of purpose.  You know, the one that tells me I\\\'m going to do this even as I shake with fear and inside me Hybrid grows stronger and states in her calm tones of acceptance, Come what may, so be it.  Well there it all is my piddly ass concerns when there are starving children in Africa.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWith love from Hybrid and the soon to be gone Captive One. <br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>The Hidden Path 9/18/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nI look in your eyes and I see the man who will be,<br>\r\nYou may think him leashed, but you are becoming he.<br>\r\nI am afraid and yet firm with resolve,<br>\r\nAs I feel him join you and even now you evolve.<br>\r\nI pray he will love me as much as you did and bear me no ill wish,<br>\r\nI know he bides his time and his memory is long, yet I hunger for that dangerous kiss.<br>\r\nYou spoke to me of students and of your quest for the one,<br>\r\nI felt it then that I am she, but I cannot say the words lest I come undone. <br>\r\nI gazed cautiously at him behind your eyes,<br>\r\nI saw that you didn\\\'t yet know but he was not surprised.<br>\r\nA struggle within as the Captive One throws a righteous fit,<br>\r\nHybrid seizes control and speaks to you both as one, \\\"So be it.\\\"<br>'),(3746,'It just occurred to me that I want to share these experiences, crazy just like life that happened to a real person, me. Selfish I know, and don\\\'t really care.  It covers about 6 months of my life and I figure I\\\'ll continue it from there.  Read it or not, I just feel better knowing that maybe just maybe someone out there is hearing my story.\r\n\r\n7/22/04\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s odd to be writing in a journal again as I haven\\\'t used one for years.  Life is very frustrating these days. The only really  nice parts at this moment are my kids and Dexter. Funny to say that as he used to be the evil black hole in my life sucking in everything good and leaving behind pain, rage, and anguish.  I\\\'m trying so hard not to become bitter and cynical but today is harder than some.  I have my parents contstantly nagging about cigarrettes, food, why are you with Dex, Dow this, Dow that, the kids got left here while you were gone, spend more time with us etcetera.  Right next door is my exhusband who is irate because I\\\'m back with Dexter.  From him is either glares or ignoring me and he can\\\'t seem to make up his mind whether he\\\'s accepting that it\\\'s over, fighting to save his marriage, or he hates me and it can\\\'t be over soon enough. I think we\\\'ll skip the friends as they aren\\\'t active in my stress levels for the last few days.  I can\\\'t explain to myself why I\\\'m back with Dex except to say that he makes me so happy.  I can be completely myself, he takes care of me and I have this hallpass from the Creator that states that this is my path for now.  Should any of these things change, I will follow the path laid before me then too. Gotta cook lunch will write again soon.\r\n\r\n\r\n7/27/04\r\n\r\nDow and Scott came into Hardee\\\'s Sunday while Dex and I were there with the kids.  It was so uncomfortable.  Mikel was yammering away all excited to see them, if looks could kill I think I\\\'d have died on the spot.  They left.  I saw Mark Ferris at M & H last night and pretended not too as I figure he is Dow\\\'s friend and God only knows what he\\\'s told him.  I\\\'ve written off Jen and Tawny, I\\\'m very hurt and angry about their behavior.  My parents are making demands as usual but at least Justin and Jeanne and I are speaking again.  We\\\'re supposed to get together Wednesday and hang out.  I\\\'m really looking forward to it. I took my physical for the hospital job today cross your fingers and pray I get it.  The only time life seems to hold joy any more is when I\\\'m with Dex, he makes everything brighter, even when he\\\'s being bitchy, HEE HEE.  So far so good. Hopefully soon we\\\'ll have our own place.  Have to check on Squeaker, catch you later.  Dow just picked up the kids.  Fuck it, I don\\\'t know why I bother.  I tried to tell him about the kids and he shone me on.  I told Mom he can deal with her from now on as he won\\\'t listen when I try to tell him about the kids.\r\n\r\n\r\nAugust 16, 2004\r\n\r\nDad and I had a huge fight about a week ago, so I\\\'m living at Sharon and Bernie\\\'s now.  I went to pick up the kids on Sunday for church and Dow asked how long I was keeping them.  I told him until around 1pm.  He told me to talk to him when I was ready to spend some real time with them and slammed the door in my face.  What did he think I was doing Monday through Friday??So I called that night and left a message for him to let him know that I\\\'m not watching them while he works anymore.  I will take them on my Monday through Friday nights off from 3pm until 9pm and every other Saturday and Sunday when I have them off.  Told him I\\\'d pay $150/month child support and that I expect to have them from 7pm this Friday until 9pm this Sunday.  I hate it because I didn\\\'t want to be a part-time parent but I don\\\'t see that he left me any choice!!  Well later, gotta catch the bus.\r\n\r\n\r\n9/3/04\r\n\r\nWell, I got the job and things are still good with Dex and I.  Every day is something new.  We tweak together about once a month.  I don\\\'t know what happens to me, I become wild, savage.  I am so spun right now that I\\\'m probably going to ramble for awhile, but at least this way I AM FREE!!  To write what I really think, my confusion, my fears, my walls.  The biggest problem Dex and I are having right now so far as I can see is who he knows I am on the inside and him trying to push, pull, drag her out.  He\\\'s causing a, well, hurricane is too mild a word but it will have to do for now, inside of me.  I feel like I\\\'ve been, not half dead per say, but numb for my whole life and then he came back to me this man that I loved so much he damn near destroyed me and offered me all of my secret dreams, happiness, dark desires willingly without judgement or any pattern that so called normal people could possibly perceive.  I am on sensory overload after so long without REAL passion for life, now it consumes me at odd moments every day and it terrifies me.  What if I let go, show not Dex because he knows, no maybe even him and show them all the me locked so carefully away, what if then like now at my most vulnerable point emotionally, spiritually I should be destroyed.  I see who I am and what I have become and I hater her, spiritless, spineless, passionless and yet I cling to her because with her I cannot be hurt.  So the harder he pushes for my true self, in a panic, I fight terrified to truly feel and yet I want it so badly.  I wage this war inside every day.  I fell stupit trying to tell Dex that I am so reserved because I am keeping myself under tight control because I feel like I\\\'m flying apart.  I accept his pulling, pushing, dragging of my inner self because I want her freed too, but then I become afraid so I pull away from that as hard and as far as I can and the storm rages higher.  Even thought it\\\'s a very painful process, I know I cannot become free without him.  How do you bare your deepest, darkest, most terrifying sould and say to a man that feels every single time he cause that pain, how do you tell him to ignore that feeling he gets at your pain and to continue because it is what you need, what you must have to stop your inner soul from fading into nothingness??  I try to explain why I am so cold and remote but the true words won\\\'t come and instead comes from my mouth useless justifications and pitiful excuses.  What will become of that person should he become sick and tired of the outer me?? How will I survive if he should just throw his hands up in the air and walk away??\r\n\r\n\r\nCAPTIVE\r\n\r\nI can see the person inside I want to be,\r\nI can see her beauty and she is just out of      reach.\r\nBut outside is the one who survived, the strongest one left,\r\nAnd she cries out to her sister, in pain, afraid and bereft.\r\n\\\"What will become of me if I lose control?\\\"\r\nNightmare visions she paints of suffering untold.\r\nCowering inside her primitive cage, she shies away,\r\nFrom her laughing carefree self of yesterday.\r\nWhen there was freedom, joy and a nonchallant heart,\r\n\\\"How do I find the light,\\\" she sobs,\\\"after so long in the dark?\\\"\r\n\\\"What will become of me??\\\" she screams as she come undone.\r\n\\\"What will become of the Captive One??\\\"\r\n\r\n9/4/04\r\n\r\nWhat a great impression to make at a new job that I really want; 2 absences at the last minute and one day being two hours late!!Dex and I started in at about one o\\\'clock this morning nothing but hard thrusting anal, awesome that was.  Then from like 2am to when I woke up nothing but giving head.  Had to be from like 4am on in my sleep.  Snapped out of a daze to realise it was now 6am.  Fuck me, supposed to be at work!!  Lightheaded and dizzy I threw on my clothes, called work to let them know was walking and didn\\\'t know how late I was gonna be.  Got clear up to 4th Ave S before I realised that there was no way I was going to walk 32 block in less than 2 hours by then it was 6:30am or so.  My bank opened at 8am so I figured I would e better able to handle residents etcetera after a cab ride than a 2 hour walk uphill most of the way and I wouldn\\\'t be any later either way.  I finally got to work at 8:37am and everyone understandably was pissed.  However due to the incredibly hateful attitudes of the nurse, I\\\'m wondering if a)the gut from West didn\\\'t pass the message on or B)they think I still live 3 blocks away.  I asked the nurse who I reported to about my break and maybe I came across wrong because she got an even bigger attitude than before and said I could report to her.  I asked if it would be all right if I went and she asked if I would stay until 12:30pm, why not so I said OK, and started serving lunch.  Now it\\\'s 12:45pm and I still have 4 showers to give, vitals, and charting, oh well, serves me right for being late.  Better get to work and pray this migraine goes away sometime sooner than never.\r\n\r\n9/18/04\r\n\r\nSo here I am again running out of energy and about to get more.  I used a stick for the first time successfully lat night and I understand now why Dex likes it so much.  I don\\\'t have quite the same as he told me about, but close.  It\\\'s like a freight train rushing through your body until suddenly it washes over you and all you can do is take deep breaths and quiver with exhileration.  Bear with me as I\\\'m trying to follow three lines of thought at the same time, four it you count the stick.  Okay, first shocker.  Dex asked me last night how I\\\'d feel about doing Jason. Out of nowhere this crazy bitch tells him sure I can do that.  I\\\'m really kinda scared of who is being released but I\\\'ve already told you all that.  Can you believe I\\\'m not going to back out even so??  Next fear of the moment, shall we??  Every time something new comes along like this I get a warning signal from what feels like the instinctive level.  Maybe fals alarms from the Captive One?? God I hope so, because I can\\\'t think of the other possibilities without cringing in horrified disbelief.  I\\\'ll get to that in a minute.  Part of me rages every time I think about these horrible possibilities that she doesn\\\'t give a fuck about unknown consequences, that she wants to fucking live free and do we believe in this love or not?? Do we have faith in the Creator and the path we swore to follow NO MATTER WHAT HAPPENED ALONG THE WAY or not??  I tend to agree with her, but I am ever cautious, especially where Dex and Chant are concerned, overly so??  Captive One just keeps whispering her horrible stories of what could possibly be actually happening here and sometimes caught off guard I listen and I have to admit that I become afraid.  I try to shake it off but it keeps coming back, echoing softly, whispering of a pain so deep I\\\'m pretty sure it\\\'ll kill me, I\\\'m sure that\\\'s what Captive One wants, but acknowledging that doesn\\\'t stop the unexpected tidal waves of panic.  So here is what keeps haunting me.  It\\\'ll be incoherent, terror usually is.  What if this wonderful man I\\\'ve come to worship with my soul is a creation of Chant?  What if he is meant to gain my trust, my soul, free me into this Hybrid of Gypsy, Jade, and Sunni, only to slowly tear her apart and watch me die??  Even now writing this I can feel the cliff dropping feeling in my stomach.  Not that any of these fears will change the direction I am walking on this unknown path to which I was led.  I swore an oath to my Creator and if I should be torn apart walking this path then so be it and fuck the consequences as Hybrid is so fond of saying. I\\\'m just hoping that reading these fears I write about will make them less intimidating like thinking of a horrible nightmare in the clear light of day.  All right already, ENOUGH.  Good news.  I think I\\\'ve figured out part of TANNON NAZGUL, TANNON=teacher.  ALmost had the meaning for NAZGUL too but it escaped me before I could put it to memory.  Next as Dex and I chatted today about Chant and the committee and his students, he was talking about \\\"the one\\\" who would teach him something he didn\\\'t know among other things.  Inside where I feel these things was a click louder than any I heard before including when I saw the raven and started this insane journey in the first place.  I think it means that I am that one.  All day I\\\'ve had the compulsion to look at him and say \\\"teach me, I am yours as you are mine\\\"  Ah here comes the Captive thought.  What if he thinks I\\\'m only saying this because of what he told me happens between him and his students, to prevent it, as if I could.  What if he says No Way in Hell.  And even as I thought about this before I wrote Hybrid informs me that we shall continue to tell him this until he agrees to do it.  And yet again I feel that overwhelming kick in the ass from the Creator aolng with that unyielding sense of purpose.  You know, the one that tells me I\\\'m going to do this even as I shake with fear and inside me Hybrid grows stronger and states in her calm tones of acceptance, Come what may, so be it.  Well there it all is my piddly ass concerns when there are starving children in Africa! HEE HEE.\r\n\r\nWith love from Hybrid and the soon to be gone Captive One. \r\n\r\nThe Hidden Path 9/18/04\r\n\r\nI look in your eyes and I see the man who will be,\r\nYou may think him leashed, but you are becoming he.\r\nI am afraid and yet firm with resolve,\r\nAs I feel him join you and even now you evolve.\r\nI pray he will love me as much as you did and bear me no ill wish,\r\nI know he bides his time and his memory is long, yet I hunger for that dangerous kiss.\r\nYou spoke to me of students and of your quest for the one,\r\nI felt it then that I am she, but I cannot say the words lest I come undone. \r\nI gazed cautiously at him behind your eyes,\r\nI saw that you didn\\\'t yet know but he was not surprised.\r\nA struggle within as the Captive One throws a righteous fit,\r\nHybrid seizes control and speaks to you both as one, \\\"So be it.\\\"\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3747,'Had to take care of my kids, clean house etcetera. So here is my journal, to be continued, or continuing soap opera written by crackheads, depending on how you choose to see it.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/18/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nTo continue with this endless circle my thoughts have been running in ever since I heard that word described as \\\"Teach\\\".  I think I may finally have the meaning (it only took me 14 years, at this rate I\\\'ll never be complete) of those words.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTeach the Soul Catcher.  The other that comes to me feel like secondary meanings but only seem to echo when the words are written backward: Learn the Warrior.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m thinking the second one may be wishful thinking partially because no one would ever call me a warrior, but when I say the words and think the meaning it echoes inside me.  But not as strong as Teach the Soul Catcher.  Now let\\\'s hope that it doesn\\\'t take me another 14 years to figure out what the hell I\\\'m supposed to teach him!!! I can feel them gathering together inside.  From what I feel from them Jade and Gypsy (always got along well) have just about convinced Sunni to merge with them.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nI just set everything up with Jason and I\\\'m excited and nervouse and scared to death.  The partial Hybrid is already plotting and Captive is railing and pleading and whispering but she is outnumbered on this.  I\\\'ve only got one life for now and by the Creator I am not going to cower in the shadows.  I don\\\'t want to lose Captive, she does have her uses too.  I will drag her kicking and screaming into this joinin.  Anyway, have to talk to Dex about all of the mystic word stuff.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/19/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nDex and I talked about this all night and I will try to share the sequence of things so I remember later as these revelations fade so fast.  Let\\\'s start where I showed Dex the set of words and the other and what he told me.   The one is teach.  But the mystic version is an imprint from another person of all their knowledge and experiences.  The other is soul catcher, Chant confirmed that for me as Dex didn\\\'t quite grasp what I was trying to say.  The reverse is learn.  The mystic version is a spell that enables you to put a piece of your core in an open space with the compulsion to \\\"Learn.\\\"  The bonus to this casting is that you can use teach to get an imprint of yourself and send it to the piece of core so if you ever lose control you can rebuild yourself.  The other reverse word is Mystic, not warrior, although in this case I sense it is similar but more.  The mystic version of this is the spell for compulsion.  In talking to Dex about this we went on to when I shattered.  Here is basically what happened there from what I can piece together from me, Dex, and Chant.  My core spirit recognized it\\\'s mate and so did Dex\\\'s.  My spirit embraced the whatever it is that happens between us and he was terrified.  So he repeatedly pushed me away.  The problem with this is that once my spirit sets itself upon a path it tends to stay there, resolve, purpose if you will. So to get around him I kept sending portions of my core in the hopes that he would see the sacrifice in this act and come to me where he belonged.  This went on for months.  FInally, right before I shattered my core sent a last ditch effort.  Logic told Dex accept or refuse are your only 2 options, he refused.  Logic and the other\\\'s in Dex\\\'s \\\"committee\\\" set up some kind of wave resistance that sent this very large piece of my core slamming back to me.  The resulting vibrations caused me to start to disappate to go back to the Creator.  Chant told Dex \\\"You\\\'ll never have to worry about her again.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nDex:  Good, she\\\'s going to back off.<br><br>\r\n\r\nChant:  No man, that little girl is dying.<br><br>\r\n\r\nDex:  I don\\\'t want her dead, I just wnat her to go away.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nChant apparently (or maybe it was Logic) gathered everyone up and wnet over to me and formed a type of hand in hand circle holding me together.  Chant was trying to piece me back together, I told him I didn\\\' want to exist without Dex.  Chant:  Look Baby Doll, I could give a shit less for you but that boy ain\\\'t gonna make it without your presence so my job is to make sure your ass stays here.  Form a wall, and hide there, I\\\'ll put the faces in charge and you can pretend not to exist forever as far as I care.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOr something like that.....<br><br>\r\nDex went about his happy way making his miserable life.  Gypsy, Jade, and Sunni freaked out because the core was hiding in shadow and refused control.  So Captive was created and she took over.  Many theories we discussed about the book, the words, what it meant, etcetera and I kept trying to lead him to realizing that I am his next student.  I\\\'m pretty sure I am \\\"the one\\\", but we\\\'ll leave that little revelation for later.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFrustrated beyond endurance that he wasn\\\'t getting it I told him \\\"What, do I need to draw  you a picture and connect the dots for you?\\\"  Dex says Chant said the same thing just then.  Logic dropped \\\"When all is one then the radiant companion.\\\" on Dex.  Dex says that\\\'s the first time he\\\'s done that in the presence of another person.  Anyway to make a very long process short.  Here\\\'s the gist of what needs to happen.<br><br>\r\n\r\n1)Dex needs to have all but positive and negative do the spell and imprint themselves to him.  The only reason I can see for this is that although I don\\\'t trust Dex, I will allow the others to guide me as they saved me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n2)Dex takes me as his student(his way??) and teaches me the combined abilities of all of them that I need to become a Mystic.  Or maybe he just has to do the other spell, then cast the first spell into it.  ANyway I know I am to receive the abilities of all, just not sure how.  Then we will have evolved, I think and our spirits should be able to entertwine.  <br><br>\r\n\r\nSeparate Thought:  There are no morals only the Spirit and the Unspoken Laws.  Choose your path and live it well.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOkay, talked to Dex, that isn\\\'t it so going to stop thinking in terms of Dex that way for several reasons.  I feel like a complete idiot.  Once I told him the before page he calmly explained why it wasn\\\'t possible for him to be my Guide.  Also fuck this shit, God I am so frustrated!!!  I\\\'ve only ever been this upset with myself twice before in my lifetime!!  The first time half asleep, I thought the most beautiful poem I\\\'ve ever created, finished it in my head, sat up to write it down and it was gone, but I still remember the overwhelming sense of well-being it evoked as it was created.  The second was when Will gave me that damn riddle.  I kept mulling it over and over in my mind, knowing that I almost had the answer.  Now I\\\'ve gone and made a complete ass of myself to my old man, informing him of how I was his next student.  I guess if nothing else I\\\'m getting that stupid arrogance back.  Also Dex isn\\\'t a Spirit Catcher so Chant can\\\'t be.  AAAGH.  SO square one definites:<br><br>\r\n\r\n1) ******-teach(imprint)<br>\r\n2) ******-spirit catcher<br>\r\n3) ******-Learn(storage for piece of core)<br>\r\n4) ******-Mystic Warrior(compulsion)<br><br>\r\n\r\nFrom Logic:  when one is all then radiant companion.<br><br>\r\n\r\nRandom advice from ?:  Choose your path, live it well.  There are no morals only the spirit and the Unspoken Laws.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI can feel the \\\"clicks\\\" in my life happening one after another and coming closer together and I know yesterday was a major turning point that I was supposed to get but somehow am not quite able to.  Comprehension is just out of reach and this is too important to tolerate the situation easily.  Fuck it, I\\\'ll leave it for awhile and hopefully the answer to this crazy equation will to to me in my sleep.  Ok, just a few more random thoughts.  I\\\'ve often thought of Milt and the meeting with him and of the oath I gave that night.  His final words to me were you\\\'ll come to me when you\\\'re ready.  Yesterday while I was caught up in the whirlwind of revelations I made a random connection between Dexter and Milt.  A vivid recollection of Milt saying that he didn\\\'t want to have sex only to \\\"play\\\"(like Dex, this wasn\\\'t a betrayal to him) and pressured me to the point of pissing me off (Dex too).  I remember thinking he must be a guide too.  Dex told me about his calling.  I want to ask him questions about it.  Like what happens when a Guide and Seeker are bonded and the Seeker is called to another Guide as a student?  Is it the same as what he said would happen when a student finds him??  But after that last go around I don\\\'t think I have the stomach for it!  Enough, I think I\\\'ve given myself a migraine.\r\nP.S.  Since the quote from Logic was for me, I think it most likely that so was Chant\\\'s (BLUSH) I bet he\\\'s laughing his ass off....Oh well.<br><br>\r\n\r\nUntitled<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s okay to cry, okay to be me,<br>\r\nIt\\\'s okay to laugh and it\\\'s all right to be weak.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s okay to live my life as it should be lived,<br>\r\nIt\\\'s safe to give someone all there is to give.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s good to feel and to be overwhelmed,<br>\r\nIt\\\'s even better if I can do it and be held.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s safe to lean on someone\\\'s shoulder,<br>\r\nTo take the leap and who cares about the drop.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s safe to be free and safe to come home.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s all right to be intense, to see and do what they cannot.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s all right to have fun, to test the boundaries of myself.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s wonderful to explore and find out who I am.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/21/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nOkay, now that I\\\"m a little calmer.  I am a dumb ass.  Bond ties two people so logic says if Dex can follow that bond, so can I, so there should be no need for anything more as once I figure it out I should be able to travel it too.  As for the rest it will come when it\\\'s ready and no forcing of the pieces in my brain is going to make it come any faster.  I think I broke the wall on Sunday, so hopefully all I need to do now is piece myself back together.  I can feel some of the changes already.  I think the key is full-hearted acceptance of myself.  My strengths and weaknesses, the things I like, the things I don\\\'t, all of it.  Dad should be happy, I forced my way through the \\\"tunnel\\\" inside myself.  When I decided to break down the wall, I was almost there when Bernie came out so I thought I failed.  Talk about pissed!!  So now, not only did I have cramps but a migraine as well and nothing to show for it.  Later I was trying to get by him to get a pill, he tapped me on the head with a thing of elastic.  I said \\\"OW\\\" he did it again all of a sudden I was crying and I couldn\\\'t stop.  Wave after wave of sadness engulfed me and I had no control at all over it.  All I could do was cry and Dex, he held me close and safe and let me.  Got to work, more later.  Wave after wave for an hour with no possibility of holding it back.  And finally it lessened and the tide went out.  I\\\'m going to smash every one of my boxes and I\\\'m not going to make any more.  From now on I\\\'m going to release emotions as I feel them (try to anyway).  I am the living flame and I will not be consumed by myself if I can help it.  Hopefully less than a month and we\\\'ll have our own place.  I am ready to have my home.  Our home.  It\\\'s odd to be able to share everything with someone and every day I feel stronger and more secure.  I\\\'m still a little leary @ the thought of Dex and another woman, but even that is getting better with time.  Work again, maybe more tomorrow.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>9/24/04:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nOkay, so I\\\'m a fucking coward, and to top it off I\\\'m a fucking liar too.  I wish I knew what made me change my mind today.  About Jason I mean.  You want to hear something really fucked up, being with Jason doesn\\\'t bother me near as much as the thought that if I do this there\\\'s no going back to it being just Dex and I.  I talk tough about how great it would be and how I know no one can come between us, blah blah blah and yeah the thought is kinda intriguing but at the same time every time I think of me with another man or Dex with another woman it\\\'s like a cut from a razor blade. Not enough to cause serious damage but enough to know it hurts.  I didn\\\'t even realize all this was going on inside until today.  It started to bother me that Dex was so nonchallant about the whole thing.  Then it seemd like every time I turned around he was going into another room.  Lots of I love you\\\'s but little to no affection.  Then the bedroom.  I wanted, needed him inside me and told him in every subtle way I knew.  In other words the poor guy was clueless.  He seemed bound and determined not to go there.  It\\\'s so confusing because it\\\'s not like this is a new thing.  Dex prefers to get women off by other means, has since we got back together.  But I guess today I needed to fell possessed by him, needed to feel that oneness because I was getting more scared by the hour and incapable of expressing it.  So, bewildered, hurt, angry and not really sure why, I withdrew.  Unable to express what I was feeling or why I kept my mouth shut for fear of saying something I didn\\\'t really mean or even worse making a complete fool of myself.  The harder he pushed to know why I was upset the further I withdrew until by the time he went to work he was angry and frustrated.  It\\\'s not that I didn\\\'t want to tell him, more like I couldn\\\'t.  So I went home on the verge of tears and called Jason to lie and tell him I started the rag today and now I\\\'m writing to you, crying, trying to sort out what the hell my problem is!!!  Answer, obviously I\\\'m not adult or woman enough to handle this yet.  God that sucks.  I wanted to be open-minded as the other women he\\\'s been with, to not be in their shadow in any way, to be open-minded enough to say sure, go ahead and not feel hurt or jealous or angry.  But I guess all I am is a frightened little girl that\\\'s incapable of expressing herself to herself much less to him who can\\\'t quite grasp the concept that toys are meant to be share.  Maybe that\\\'s what sets me apart from the others.  To me Dex isn\\\'t a toy, he\\\'s my life and the thought of allowing another woman to be with him in any way even coming close to what we share makes me ill.  Or vice versa for that matter.  The idea is fun for me to fantasize about but when it comes to the moment of truth, right now I just don\\\'t have it in me.  I learned the hard way a long time ago not to go against myself to make him happy.  It\\\'s scary to think that I would do anything in the world to keep him happy.  How sad and pathetic is that??  Ok, I\\\'m crying now, time to stop.<br><br>'),(3748,'Go star gazing, fall asleep and wake up with the sunrise. Make her mixtapes and listen to the ones she makes you while holding her as youâ€™re both laying on your bed. Give her that old misfits pin you still wear on your jacket. Sew patches on your clothes together. Offer your jacket to her even when itâ€™s 20Â°C out. Bake a pie together. offer to be a pincushion, so she can practice her piercing techniques. Buy her random things you find at the mall. Hold her jacket when she goes in the pit. kiss better all the cuts and bruises she gets while in the pit. Take her out for coffee at midnight. Buy her a mini bonzai tree and possibly a goldfish. Send her a bunch of lyrics that remind you of her. send her anonymous love letters. Share hairdye. Buy her cigarettes. Paint your old converse sneakers together. go on a date in the giant oak tree. drive her 20 miles out the city on a back road late at night and ask her to dance...make sure to hold her after giant dogs try attacking you and she is crying hysterically. Go on bus adventures together. boycott pornography. Steal her candy from the corner store. Look up random words in the dictionary and read them out to her. Let her fall asleep in your bed. Look at clouds whilst holding hands. Take her to a protest. Buy her a hatâ€¦with ear flaps. Take her to the comic book store. Sit on her lap. Write her name on dirty car windows. Walk for 3 hours in the pouring rain just to see her for a mere 10 minutes. Make out on benches, in a tree, on the streets, in front of the local venue, beside bus stops, under bridges, on your roof. Fall in Love if nothing else.'),(3749,'All right, enough with the soap opera,  I thought it would help to share that whole experience, but instead it just made me depressed.  I\\\'ve been enough of that for over 3 weeks now.  I have no job, no car, no real idea what I\\\'m doing.  What I do have is me and my kids, the occasional lay, some good parties when I have the time and moolah and all in all I\\\'m happier now that he\\\'s gone than I ever was when I was with him.  I\\\'m content to prey on the scandalous people out there, no strings and have a good time.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFUCK IT!! Life is for the living.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThat was at like 10 o\\\'clock last night, and I was kinda right.  But for whatever reason, I seem to need to rehash it all again.  Maybe it\\\'s simply the need to look back now that I\\\'m not in the situations and learn from them some more, who knows, who cares, so here we go.....the saga continues.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n9/25/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAt the rate that I keep self-analysing, I should just become a therapist and get it over with!!!  Jeanne\\\'s here, I\\\'ll try again later.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWell, I\\\'ve been talking to Jeanne and I feel a little better.  At least there\\\'s someone I can talk to without freezing up with the words stuck in my throat.  More later.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nShe says when this happens to have Dex drop it for awhile until I feel comfortable and then drop it on me out of nowhere and see if I can talk about it then.  She says she used to do the same thing with Justin for a very long time. I talked to her about the Jason issue and my fears and concerns regarding that.  She says that first I need to get either really drunk or high and that she strongly recommends that Dex be there when it happens.  Thinking about it that way makes me feel calmer already, not much mind you, but some.  Maybe it will have to be that way for awhile, maybe forever, if ever at all.  It feels better thinking about it like that.  I don\\\'t feel near as sick thinking of this happening with both of us being there, more secure.  Whether it be Summer or Jason, even imagining that it is set for tonight there are butterflies in the stomach but even they lessen when I think of Dex there.  He made a few offhand comments today that kinda hurt.  One was about me being enlightened enough when we were 80 and letting him go after a 20 year old, think I\\\'m being oversensitive with taht one.  The other was, \\\" I think it\\\'s great that you need me so much right now, but I see us in the future in the bigger picture.\\\"  I don\\\'t want to smother him, but I feel like a drowning person and he\\\'s the only lifeline in sight.  Sometimes like the past few days when I\\\'m feeling particularly desperate and he feels so distant I can\\\'t help but panic and cling even tighter.  There are times when I wish Chant would take over (guiltily I must admit) if only because he has ever been my champion and although he tends toward being an asshole, I feel as though I can talk to him like I can\\\'t seem to talk to Dex.  I know this sounds crazy as in all reality Chant is Dex and vice versa, but that is the way I feel.  Chant frequently travels the bond to chat with me, always introducing himself by calling me \\\"Baby Doll.\\\"  It\\\'s weird because I can hear the Texas accent in my head.  Dex says Chant loves to taunt him when he knows what\\\'s rong and I\\\'m locked inside and can\\\'t seem to find the words to share with Dex and let him in on the secret.  God, I must be insane.  What kind of freak trusts her soul mate\\\'s alternate personality more than him??? AARGH!!Anyway, more later.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n9/25/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI am so glad to have a friend like Jeanne, someone I can talk to, no holds barred and be completely blunt with.  No pretending to be someone I\\\'m not.  I hope I will be able to talk to Dex later without stumbling over myself like an idiot.  I don\\\'t know why but I\\\'m afraid to talk to him about this.  That\\\'s the first and only time in 3 months I\\\'ve been afraid to talk to him about anything.  I wonder why and come up with vague ideas but nothing concrete.  I think part of it is knowing that I\\\'m to blame for the current tension between us causes by my pathetic inability to communicate properly.  Ok, enough Sunni-bashing and feeling guilty for something that I\\\'m honestly trying to fix.  I\\\'ve got to stop beating myself up over everything in our lives.  I need to accept my limitations as a person and stop dwelling on them.  Otherwise I\\\'m going to ruin the best thing that\\\'s ever happened to me.  Later.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n9/26/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWe talked, I cried.  He says the only reason he brought up Jason is he thought it would help me open up.  I still think it was the thought of doing it alone that freaked me out.  I was thinking about this and Dex and I could be in bed with ten people and it would still be private and special and intimate because it would be an experience we were sharing together.  For instance the Tine thing we did, that didn\\\'t hurt until she isolated me.  He kept telling me he wanted to be what I wanted, what I needed.  I don\\\'t want him to chameleon into some imaginary dream lover because he is my dream lover.  I haven\\\'t even broke the surface of who and what he has become.  Yet he knows me so intimately it\\\'s like being naked while he\\\'s still fully clothed.  I\\\'ve forgotten how to live in the moment and accept good, bad, indifferent.  I don\\\'t just want to hear how much he loves me and how happy he is.  I want it all, when he\\\'s sad, frustrated, angre and somehow he\\\'s holding that separate from me.  Acceptance is the key.  Work.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n9/26/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt\\\'s the sharing of everything I need. To make him climb the walls and lose control.  Seing the darkness in him and being scared but knowing I am safe no matter what.  The passion, the tears, that dark and the laughter.  I tried to tell him last night and expressed it poorly that I want to hear the horrible things he has done because he needs to share it and I want to share it because it shaped who he isnow but that it doesn\\\'t matter because I love him.  All of him, the dark, the light, the craziness, and the sanity.  I need to stop searching for what was and explore what I have before it is gone.  There\\\'s my worst fear out in the open that I will finally be able to let him in again, that he will decide I have no value and walk away.  I had nightmares for years where he was leaving me over and over in a thousand different ways while I begged and pleaded for him to stay, to not leave me all alone.  No matter how many times he says he\\\'s not going anywhere, that is my fear that he will turn away from me, that I will beg, that he\\\'ll go anyway and leave me as an empty shell.  I\\\'ll try to share that tonight.  Maybe I should just start writing letters, it\\\'s not as hard.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThat would be the coward speaking again.  Of course it\\\'s easier to write, there\\\'s no one there to interact with.  Just a pen, a piece of paper and my thoughts. Why do I always need him to reach out first.  Why should he be forced to drag things out of me with the jaws of life?? Acceptance to work on:\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n1)So long as the Creator doesn\\\'t take him, he will be there for the rest of my life.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n2)Just because he\\\'s attempting things with me he\\\'s done with another woman, her \\\"shadow\\\" has no place in our bed.  Try it, decide if I like it and live for that moment.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n3) I have value, he is as wrapped into me as I am him.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWork.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n10/4/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI just love these little pep talks I give myself.  I don\\\'t have to worry about being Angie\\\'s shadow, I sometimes feel like I am her shadow.  Like anything Dex and I do he will think, \\\"I remember the first time I did this with Angie.\\\"  He told me today that he\\\'s used to getting head, quick, getting her off, also quick, her jumpstarting him and them fucking.  He started it out as the chicks he\\\'s been with.  I\\\'ve tried over and over to tell him the way I like to do things.  Which, how ironic is this, I learned from him and yet he still goes through what I\\\'ll call Angie routine every time.  She would just love this.  He\\\'s finally with the woman he loves and Angie\\\'s controlling their sex life.  Time #6 taht I finally got up the balls to ask him for anal: he looked at me and said \\\"Baby I\\\'m about to come and went back to his hand and the porno.  15 minutes later, he finally did go.  I even tried to be blunt once and told him that although oral from him is great, I could get it that way from any local vibrator.  It went zooming right up to him, slapped him in the forehead and landed in his lap and he still couldn\\\'t catch it.  I am trapped in the Angie Routine and I honestly have no idea how to escape it.  So I guess I am indeed her shadow, going behind her and doing everything that she\\\'s already done.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n10/10/04\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWell, thankfully that maudlin shit is over for the time being.  I say live for the moment.  He\\\'s not with her or anyone else.  He\\\'s with me so I say fuck it.  I need to lighten up (a lot), loosen up and enjoy my life.  I have 3 beautiful children ( a little guilt there as I hardly ever see the babies thanks to my fucked up schedule).  But anyhow, I have a wonderful man who loves me, a great job with benefits and good pay, I\\\'m waiting to see how bad child support is gonna screw me.  We\\\'ve got a place, school will be dicey, but we\\\'ll see and hopefully soon I can go back to school.  Better yet, I pray soon I\\\'ll have my divorce.  On the down side my parents just screwed us out of our computer.  God Dex was livid.  I was willing to overlook it as my parents have never screwed my over before, but Dex put it like this.  He took it whent they talked shit about him to me, they were rude to him when I was on the phone.  Took it when Mikel overheard them talking shit and brought it to him and still it was his idea to give them the $175.  Then they don\\\'t even call to tell us what\\\'s going on, me or him.  I have to call my mom and drag it out of her.  SO God help me this is gonna get ugly.  My parents are on emergency status only until they can show him the same respect he\\\'s shown them the last 4 months.  That oughta set them both off on a tangent I\\\'ll probably never hear the end of.  WWIII all over again.  I figure what the fuck, according to my code of what\\\'s just, he\\\'s in the right on this one.  Not that anyone seems to be able to keep track of my code but me.  Justin is Justin again HURRAH, about damn time.  Jeanne is on bed rest for the next 2 months and thank the Creator doesn\\\'t have to give birth in Helena.  I don\\\'t know why I short-circuited, but unfortunately it continued through most of this past week.  Needy, bitching for no apparent reason that even I could see.  Dex, thank God, has remained as patient as can be expected considering what a whiny little bitch I\\\'ve been lately.  Maybe partly because he gets off the bracelet on Tuesday??? No analysing tonight, I figure I\\\'ve done enough of that for awhile.  And what did all that self-torture really do for me anyway.  Made me see the really ultimately weak and whiny part of me.  Trying to accept that THAT is part of me is a bitter pill I\\\'m still trying to choke down.  Anyway, how long does the scared excuse work anyway, used it with Dow then, with Dex now.  Both are convinced that the other totally fucked me up and in some ways they would both be right.  But I think maybe I was fucked in the head before either one of them came along.  Maybe that\\\'s why no one but Dex could ever come even close to who I am.  I do know that twice in the last few weeks I\\\'ve felt cut off from the bond and so horribly alone I can\\\'t even begin to describe the feeling of desolation it gave me.  I can\\\'t tell if it\\\'s him or me, I have to assume it\\\'s me.  The last one was the worst by far.  It left me sobbing uncontrollably on the couch, wrapping tighter and tighter into myself.  Maybe it\\\'s just me facing my worst fear, I honestly don\\\'t know.  All I know is that shaking, weak and desolate, I fought my way upstairs and into my lover\\\'s arms.  And bam it was back again.  Internal, mmhmm, has to be internal.  Poor rainman imitation there.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nAnyhow, back to my original thought.  Live life for the moment because tomorrow it could all be gone.  Did I mention I got a glimpse of what I look like in there right now.  Dad\\\'s description of a fairie in a bubble wasn\\\'t far wrong.  Unless I\\\'m deluding myself again, he anything\\\'s possible.  If my darkest and most closely held secret wish can come true my love with me and loving me back then anything can happen.  Life is full of surprises and I intend to enjoy them all, good bad or indifferent.  OK, just a few dark questions and then I\\\'m signing off for tonight.  Why the sarcasm when he\\\'s asking me if I want it to be just him and me forever.  What\\\'s wrong with wanting that?? Kay, done, chat later.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n----------\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nOkay guys, enough from my good old journal for today.....at least we\\\'re almost current now....i\\\'m telling you, soap opera written by crackheads, I mean seriously....:D\r\n'),(3750,'<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n\r\n<p>on dundas street near bathurst i walked by an orange wall.  there was a woman holding her head in a stairwell, wearing a red shirt.  i walked over, knelt down, and photographed her.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>walking down the sidewalk towards me another woman said</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hey you take picture of me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at first i thought it was an accusation but she said it again</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you take picture of me</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i saw it as an invitation.  i stood up.  she made for the van parked beside the building and leaned on it.  she was missing a few teeth and a steady pace, her face was old but her smile was genuine.  she spoke with the european accent i know from my grandparents.  opposing her age was a comfort and confidence that made me smile in return.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>while i shot her i wondered if she\\\'d modeled in an earlier life, her younger face now awash in the shoe boxes of old european men .  she seemed happy now.  was she happy then?</p>\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">'),(3751,'Im cooking macaroni in the microwave.\r\n<BR>Im cooking your heart in my hand\r\n<BR>On your knees you lie in chains,\r\n<BR>Stand up son, be a man.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>I\\\'m concentrating on my sitcom.\r\n<BR>I\\\'ve crashed what you rebuilt\r\n<BR>You\\\'ll shower me with love and jewels\r\n<BR>I\\\'ll shower you with guilt.\r\n\r\n<BR><BR>Please don\\\'t leave me darling\r\n<BR>Being sadist\\\'s far too fun\r\n<BR>I\\\'ll pretend to hear your sobs\r\n<BR>Once my macaroni\\\'s done.\r\n'),(3752,'Walking to the store in the evening twilight,<br>\r\nAround 4 p.m. on Halloween night.<br><br>\r\nI look ahead as far as I can see up the road ahead,<br>\r\nThere is no one but me and the trees and the dead.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFrom somewhere inside a voice whispers true,<br>\r\n\\\"You walk your path alone, there is no one but you.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter multiple betrayals and uncounted knives in the back,<br>\r\nI am not too concerned about this journey and the support it lacks.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThere is comfort to be found in counting on no one else,<br>\r\nIn knowing that pain won\\\'t be found coming from myself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSomehow though, I was surprised to find I wept,<br>\r\nLife is beautiful, but so is death.'),(3753,'<p>I hear no sleigh bells or the prancing <br>\r\nof reindeer feet.  There is no snow <br>\r\nor children laughing or ladies dancing <br>\r\nor fractals of frost across windows. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>But the music still gets me. <br>\r\nSome of it, sure, sounds forced or dated <br>\r\nbut in the swell of certain melodies <br>\r\nI get lost and forget to be jaded. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The ring ting jingles of speakers <br>\r\nfrom Macy\\\'s to the Piggly Wiggly <br>\r\nshall try to drive the weaker <br>\r\nof us to more glad and giggly </p>\r\n\r\n<p>consumption, and small children <br>\r\nwho are new to it all, will dream <br>\r\nPlatonic ideals of toys, golden <br>\r\nand glowing that will seem&#151; </p>\r\n\r\n<p>until they are finally received <br>\r\nand turn out not to glow&#151; <br>\r\nto be salvation itself.  I believe <br>\r\nin that anticipation even though </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\'tis the season for the blues and such <br>\r\nstress that it makes minds brittle. <br>\r\nI like expecting too much <br>\r\nbetter than wanting too little. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Amid the ivy and holly <br>\r\nand all the other crap <br>\r\nwe\\\'ll do our best joyous and jolly <br>\r\nand buy some more presents to wrap. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>So for all the days with the sun diffused <br>\r\nand all the ways we\\\'ve overspent, <br>\r\nand for every cheery lyric overused, <br>\r\nhere\\\'s to comfort, joy, and sweet disappointment.</p>\r\n'),(3754,'So I was thinking a lot about harmonic series today. For anyone who doesn\\\'t know what that is, it\\\'s this:<br><br>\r\n<i>1+1/2+1/3+1/4+1/5+1/6+1/7...</i> off into infinity.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat many of you probably already know is that the sum of this series does not converge. It adds up to infinity. However, what\\\'s striking about this is not so much the fact that it adds up to infinity, but more along the lines of how long it takes to get there.<br><br>\r\n\r\nA calculus teacher once told me that, even with a computer, it takes a really long time to get to 20, a rediculously long time to get to 30, and that getting to 40 is pretty much out of the question.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo here\\\'s the scenario:<br><br>\r\n\r\nYou have access to the world\\\'s most powerful supercomputer... 70.72 teraflops, that\\\'s almost 71 trillion operations per second... for an unlimited amount of time. Keep in mind that this computer is so large that it could probably only fit in a small building. Your mission, should you choose to accept it, is to use harmonic series to count to 1000.<br><br>\r\n\r\nKeep in mind that 1000 is an extremely small number, considering that the sum of the harmonic series goes to infinity. But, nonetheless, the question is, how long will this take?<br><br>\r\n\r\nFirst we need to find the number of terms we\\\'ll be adding up, which is easy because harmonic series can be represented as an integral of the function 1/x. So, to determine the number of terms, we\\\'ll solve for t (I\\\'ll be using calculator notation - S is an integral sign):<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>S(1/x,x,1,t)=1000</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nAnyone with sufficient calculus skills should be able to do that in their head. <i>t=e^1000</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nSo here\\\'s how we\\\'ll break it down to find the time that the calculation will take:<br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>time=(e^1000)/(70.17x10^12 operations/s * 3600 s/hr * 24 hr/day * 365 day/yr)</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n<i>time=8.8335x10^412 years!</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nThat\\\'s how long it\\\'ll take you to count to 1000 with a supercomputer using harmonic series. To put that in perspective, that\\\'s approximately 10^403 times the cosmologically estimated age of the universe.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnother really crazy thing about counting that high with the harmonic series, is that at that point, the terms are so small that if you wanted to count up one more digit to 1001, that one digit would double the amount of time needed.<br><br>'),(3755,'  Went to a meeting for the first time in about ten years. Didnâ€™t feel any different except that I was having trouble not twitching. Kept getting real close to crying as well. I didnâ€™t see anyone I remembered and wouldnâ€™t have minded if I did. A friend of mine once asked if I would be too embarrassed to go back to meetings, being as high profile in the fellowship as I was. I said no but, in my mind I knew that those kinda hypothetical questions are pointless and the answers worthless; you canâ€™t say what youâ€™ll do when \\\"whatever\\\" happens. You donâ€™t know \\\'til youâ€™re there. As it turned out, I would have been fine with seeing someone I knew from back then. I think that THEY might have been a little embarrassed to see old Ack doinâ€™ the shake and bake in the back of the room like when he came to the program in â€™86. What do ya say to the poor bastard?<br><br>\r\n\r\n  I introduced myself as a newcomer and sat still for as long as I could. Like I said, I was having trouble keeping dry eyes, and couldnâ€™t stop thinking about whether Chris would have the money for the system Iâ€™d built for him when I got back home. There was no more cat food and, with one exception, Iâ€™d never let them down when it came to chow. Thatâ€™s what got me to go to the meeting. Not the fact that my system was toxic (and with the diseases Iâ€™ve picked up over the years, I got no business playinâ€™ this shit), not the fact that Iâ€™m a week or two from being thrown outta my apartment \\\'cause Iâ€™ve no real source of income, not the fact that, having previously run it into the ground to the point that I was living on the street, talkinâ€™ to people who werenâ€™t there and spending every third weekend in the Psych ward at general. Not the fact that, having gotten C&S for six years, run a non-profit to help drunks and junkies get back into society, been Vice-Chair of NA of San Francisco Area and as such, ran more than a couple H&I meetings thus staying close to the newcomer and seeing other peoples \\\"research\\\" rather than having to go out and make my own. Nope.<br><br>\r\n\r\n  Instead, I sat there prayinâ€™ to God and whatever else I could think of prayinâ€™ to (When youâ€™re BiPolar/Schizophrenic, this is an area where one has multiple options) that a dealer would keep his word (ironically, Iâ€™ve allowed things to deteriorate to the point where a dealer is one of the most reputable people I have regular contact with) so that my cats could eat without my having to resort to another old out of practice habit â€“ shoplifting. <br><br>\r\n\r\n  So I left early. I figured Iâ€™d log on to Swinney and post some kinda response to the \\\"Meat Puppet\\\" accusation. Itâ€™s really a shame: this site was the only place Iâ€™d found that was some kinda sanctuary, and as it turns out, theyâ€™re all so convinced Iâ€™m part of somebody elseâ€™s \\\"THEM\\\" trip that they donâ€™t even bother to ask me directly about it. Well, whatever. As Iâ€™ve said for quite a long time, anything that happens to me is just a smidgen against the things I perpetrated back in my twenties, back when people were cardboard cutouts to be used or discarded based on their relevance to my needs/desires. Sure, I paid a great deal for what I did then and continued to pay some hard dues after cleaninâ€™ up and becoming Mr. Suburban NiceGuy. But like the man said, give each man what he deserves and who amoungst us shall \\\'scape whipping (my apologies for paraphrasing but Iâ€™m not exactly in my right mind). <br><br>\r\n\r\nAs far as the conspiracy thing goes, youâ€™ve got my name and Iâ€™m listed in the San Mateo white pages. Gimme a call and Iâ€™ll tell ya whatever ya want to know. <br><br>\r\n\r\n  Oh, you may want to make that call today or tomorrow as the phone is gonna be cut off Wednesday morning (unless I manage to sell my car tomorrow. Cross your fingers [those who give a shit anyway]) Good Life to yâ€™all.<br><br>\r\n\r\n      Achilles Gaubert<br>\r\n  Master of Impracticalities<br>\r\n(B.S. in Microcalifragilistics)<br>\r\n     Homo Sapiens Sapiens<br>\r\n A Bay Area Favorite Since 1962<br>\r\n'),(3756,'<p>i walked through chinatown.  a boy sat alone on a front porch.  he reached down and drew a breath, dipping a bubble maker into a small jar at his feet.  his reach was clumsy, it did not regard the soapy water he\\\'d spilled.  he brought his arm up and blew into the circle on the end of the stick.  bubbles streamed out on his breath, he hooted and laughed, stomped his feet with joy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the celebration stopped, his face grew even with concentration, he reached down and dipped the bubble maker in the jar.  he blew a stream of bubbles and laughed gleefully, clapped his hands and stomped his sandals on the cement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a group of three children his age came walking by his porch.  a metal fence stood between them.  the boy watched them with great hope which did not fade as they passed and left without acknowledgement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a younger brother came to the screen door, checked on him, and disappeared back into the house.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>his left had not moved.  his right hand again dropped to splash some soapy water on to the concrete beneath his feet.</p>\r\n\r\n<center><img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\"></center>'),(3757,'the day started at 3:18 a.m.  woke up, had to pee, didn\\\'t know where the hell i was, finally realized it was her bedroom, saw the time on the clock radio, got up and stumbled around, felt along the wall until i found the bathroom.  did it, got back in bed, back to sleep.  then, in a dream, i was back up, wandering around the condo, entered a doorway, and, all of a sudden, was in my living room.<BR><BR>the walls and floor were a brilliant white.  the far wall, the front wall of the apartment, didn\\\'t have a window, but there was a door.  there was blood and gore all over the walls and floor.  on the floor, against the far wall, four headless bodies were lined up, their feet pointing in my direction.  even without the heads, i recognized them, the features of their physiques.  two women, two men.<BR><BR>in the far right corner stood a man, about six-seven, six-eight, bald, dressed in white.  his face is a shifting amorphous blur.  the only features which are static are his exceedinly evil eyes.  he\\\'s a familiar figure from dreams, hallucinations, visions, visitations, manifestations, what have you.  he started appearing to me seven years before i met him in the flesh.<BR><BR>janice said, <I>come on back to the kitchen.  there\\\'s somebody i want to introduce you to.</I>  and there he was, leaning back against the counter.  i wet my pants.  he turned the eyes on, and i couldn\\\'t say anything for a full two minutes.  shook uncontrollably.  they just chuckled.<BR><BR>these days, he\\\'s just a nuisance.  just fucking with me.  of course, i know that rationally, but the animal mind is still terrified.  he\\\'s harmless.  the countermeasures don\\\'t work on him.  hell, he\\\'s the one who told me how to set it up.  the last time i saw him was over a year ago.<BR><BR>i was in the assisted living facility (out in the middle of nowhere).  it was about one in the morning, and i had just finished getting high in the gazebo out back.  (kiki drove out there in the middle of the night every couple of weeks to bring it to me.  i had some money left over once the a.l.f. rent was paid, and that\\\'s where it went.)  i was looking out over the back yard, and just over the fence, at the start of the forest, where we saw deer every once in awhile, there he stood in a bucs t-shirt.  he stared at me for a second, then raised up his hand and made a gun with his forefinger and thumb and squeezed one off at me then gave me a big wink.  and i assumed the fetal position and shut my eyes and stayed that way for about fifteen minutes, and after that, he wasn\\\'t there anymore.<BR><BR>he isn\\\'t alone in my living room.  in the other corner, there\\\'s some kind of creature wearing a white sweater, has tits, bald, black carapace-like mask instead of a face.  looks like it\\\'s made out of black plastic.  i head for the door, but it\\\'s like i\\\'m moving underwater.  they catch me, and suddenly, there\\\'s a table in the middle of the room.  they sling me on it and strap me down at the ankles, thighs, waist, wrists, chest, upper arms, and neck.  the woman-thing pulls my jaw down, and gary drives nails into the roof of my mouth.  no pain, but still....<BR><BR>karyssa wakes me up, says i was moaning.  i\\\'m sweating.  she asks about the dream.  i tell her.  slowly.  she doesn\\\'t say anything.  hugs me.  i fall back asleep.<BR><BR>i awaken, some time later.  she\\\'s rubbing my stomach, my chest.  it starts, and then our bodies are roughly parallel, she\\\'s beneath me, her legs hooked over my shoulders, thighs beneath me, calves on either side of my head.  and we can\\\'t stop.  we cannot stop.<BR><BR>i reread <I><B>nightwood</B></I> recently.  i read it every two-three years.  it\\\'s still on my coffee table.  i was looking at it a couple of days ago.  i\\\'d never noticed the copyright page before.  it has the library of congress classifications as to what it\\\'s about, etc.  and third in line there was \\\"sexual addiction.\\\"  and i thought back over the plot and could see how that might be one description of the events depicted.  and it had to be pointed out to me for me to see that that\\\'s what it was.  i hadn\\\'t noticed anything abnormal.<BR><BR>(on the table beside it was another book.  i\\\'d picked it up when i got my grandmother\\\'s chrismas present.  it was a new edition of a book i read periodically as well.  i bought it because i\\\'ve had some things overdue at the library for four years, and they won\\\'t let me check anything out.<BR><BR>the book is the most highly regarded work of a guy who lived in the first half of the last century.  he wrote five novels (well, four and a half - he died in the middle of the last one.  he was an alcholic.  it killed him at a [relatively] young age.)  his novels didn\\\'t make any money, to speak of.  he made his living with the stories he sold to magazines.  he would write them first the way he wanted to, and then he\\\'d change them until he thought they were commercial enough to sell.<BR><BR>even with the magazine sales, there wasn\\\'t enough money.  he and his wife lived extravagantly, and he was always turning to his agent, editor, and friends for support.  eventually, he found his way to hollywood.  he wasn\\\'t out there long.  he worked for one studio, wrote three screenplays.  he insisted, pretty much, on working alone, which totally went against the grain of the assembly line development system.  one of his screenplays was produced.  the movie is mostly forgotten.<BR><BR>years passed, and the holdings of the studio he worked for were sold to a larger studio.  decades of files were thrown out.  reams of scripts and notes for projects that were made or shelved, records of the thousands of movies and stories and ideas that were developed at that studio.  as documents were being shredded, somebody recognized his name on a number of files.  they amounted to about a thosaund sheets of paper.  that someone had the foresight to set that material aside.  they hid it.<BR><BR>the sad thing is that so much other work was destroyed.  in those days, some of the best writers in the world worked in hollywood.  they were people of wit and vision and grace, so different from the ranks of anonymous hacks that are employed there today.  [don\\\'t get me wrong - there was a lot of sausage produced in those days as well.  and there was ten times as much of it then, since the studios produced about a movie per week apiece, instead of the ten or twelve or fifteen or twenty per year that each makes now.]  but it\\\'s like i was telling her - hopefully the good stuff gets preserved.  but it doesn\\\'t always.<BR><BR>so, luckily, the material was saved.  the man who recognized it brought it to the attention of the larger studio, which now owned it.  academia claimed that it should be available for study.  the studio gave the papers to a univeristy in the southeast and allowed for their use under certain conditions.<BR><BR>i heard about an unproduced screenplay in the collection.  it was originally intended to be a vehicle for joan crawford, but the hayes office (standards and practices) deemed the subject unsafe for public consumption.  and so it was shelved.<BR><BR>through an internet search, i found the university that housed the collection.  the papers apparently constituted the only copy of the material, but one has to imagine that the studio protected its interest and made duplicates.  after all, at any moment, it might decide to produce the script.  [it would owe the author\\\'s estate nothing.]<BR><BR>i had designs upon this script.  i had heard it was like a visual fugue.  i thought that i might read it, alter it enough so as to render it unrecognizable, and sell it as my own.  i contacted the library at this university.  i spoke to the librarian of the rare works section and asked whether i might be able to peruse the material if i made the journey to his institution.  he said it was available usually only to academics and that i would have to speak to the professor who was the nominal caretaker of the work, the man who was responsible for it being given to the univeristy.<BR><BR>i called his office and, after a few tries, was able to speak to him.  he asked after my interest in the material.  i said i was a writer who had read all the rest of the author\\\'s work and wanted to do look at it for my own enrichment. [ha.]  he kind of <I>voir dire</I>\\\'d me.  asked which was my favorite of the author\\\'s novels.  i said of course The Big One and the one right after that.  the first two were immature and the last unfinished.  which was my favorite of his stories and why.  i think i surprised him and made points with my answer.  it wasn\\\'t one of the obvious ones.  it was one that resonates with me for personal reasons, and i told him about those reasons.<BR><BR>told him those reasons were becoming a thing of the past for me.  he said, well, you\\\'re halfway home, then.  think of what kind of work he and [two contemporaries he\\\'s  often grouped with] could have done if they weren\\\'t alcoholics.  and then i got show-offy and remembered this thing one of those contemporaries used to do, train himself to write better than those who had gone before him.  [\\\"can write better than that guy.  can write better than turgenev if i try harder.  never will write better than shakespeare.  he\\\'s the champ.\\\"]  i said, at this point, i can already write better than he could when he wrote those first two novels.  and the professor said, \\\"maybe you can, but <U>will</U> you?  that\\\'s the bitch kitty right there.  whether you will or not is up to you.\\\"  and then he told me that i could not obtain access to the material, that it was reserved for those who needed it for their studies, that i would have to imagine what it must be like and perhaps write something better.<BR><BR>i opened up the book i bought and saw that this professor had written its introduction.  i did some research and found that he\\\'s considered the world\\\'s foremost authority on the guy.  he teaches at this podunk-ass state univeristy, and that\\\'s how the papers ended up there.)<BR><BR>she took me home, and i got there at six and took a bit of a sink bath, and jamie came for me at six-thirty.  i took a gym bag with clothes, etc.  we worked out, swam, showered, etc.  i dressed in a polo shirt, dockers, deck shoes.  i had told him where i was going and he offered me a ride over there, even though it was just on the other side of the highway.  we got there, and as i was getting out of the navigator, karyssa pulled up and parked beside us.  they checked each other out.  i shut the door, and he took off.  she got out.  she said, \\\"wow.\\\"  i said, \\\"yeah, right?  see what you\\\'re missing out on?  see how you are?\\\"  she shot me a look, and i sighed and hung my head in mock shame.<BR><BR>i waited in an office inside.  on the desk, there was some kind of publication, a report or something, from the united states institute of peace.  i picked it up and opened it.  it was like an academic journal.  put it back down.  after a minute, karyssa\\\'s boss came in.  cheryl.  we talked.  i told her a little about myself.  she said i could come volunteer, but it wouldn\\\'t be very exciting work.  mostly driving around, taking people places, picking up donations, delivering things.  also, random office work, answering phones.  left.  winked at the female on the way out.<BR><BR>crossed the highway, east on main, got home around ten.  internet.  at eleven-thirty, got my jar of change out of the top of my closet.  been collecting it for awhile.  since august, maybe.  went looking for bill.  found him in the park by the bridge.  i said, \\\"you want to go get something to eat?\\\"<BR><BR>we walked through town, up past my place and the hospital to kash \\\'n karry.  they have a machine there that you put change into.  it takes 8.95% of it and gives you a receipt for the rest.  you take it to the front desk, etc.  came to thirty-two and change.  i said, \\\"you want to go to christina\\\'s?  or the chinese place?\\\"  i was trying to think of where we could get something approximating a square meal.  not sandwiches or pizza.  when you\\\'re on the street, nutritious meals are few and far between.  any kind of meals, in fact, as the funds in the Sustenance account so often get transferred to the Entertainment one.  he said christina\\\'s.  before we left the plaza, we went to the dollar store, and i got wrapping paper and tape and bows and asked if they a cardboard box in the back, maybe, that was big enough for the atari.<BR><BR>so, we ate.  he got meat loaf with carrots and lima beans and mashed potatoes and a salad and a piece of pie.  i had soup and salad and bread.  we had a good time.  drew a few stares.  he was a little whiffy.  he had his backpack with him.  he was not presentable.  i think i lost points with the management.<BR><BR>the bill was twelve and change.  i paid, left a three-dollar tip.  we headed up to city hall.  we sat on the bench in front of the library and talked for about forty-five minutes.  i gave him the rest of the money and went home.<BR><BR>that afternoon, i called david and told him i was leaving for brooksville in the morning, so he needed to come get his present.  he said, \\\"dude.  i didn\\\'t get you anything.\\\"  i told him not to worry about it.  he came over and i took the vaporizer out of the bag and gave it to him.  he knew it was for smoking, that was pretty obvious, but nothing beyond that.  i showed him (or, rather, <I>told</I> him) how to use it.  he gave me a hug.  we talked for a while.  he got up to use the bathroom.  when he came back, he came up behind me (i was in the recliner) and started rubbing my shoulders.  we started kissing and touching, then i remembered the promise i\\\'d made and told him i couldn\\\'t, that christie was going to come by in a little while to pick me up.  we talked a little more, then wished each other a merry christmas, and he left.<BR><BR>i wrapped c & j\\\'s presents.  she came for me at five.  we were all going out to celebrate the anniversary of our first...<A HREF=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3499\\\">acquaintance.</A> (that little poem is humorous.  you can tell i was fucked up when i wrote it because even though it was only a few days after christmas, i could not calculate the date of the previous tuesday [\\\"tuesday, december 22\\\"], and i did not know the name of the motel where i was residing. [called it the \\\"travel inn\\\"] [i was on the bus last week, headed for the mall, and there was a guy on there with a suitcase, probably came from the greyhound station.  he was looking for cheap lodging, and all the bums sitting around him recommended either the budget inn or the similar place across the highway.  we approached them, and he didn\\\'t know which to go to.  we told him they were the same price, roughly.  i said, \\\"they are both very fine establishments, sir.  just flip a coin.  i\\\'ve stayed in them both.  i don\\\'t know which is worse.\\\"  he was on the bus again on the trip back south.  he must have decided he could do better.]<BR><BR><I><B>flip a coin</I></B>.<BR><BR>we waited on jamie to get home.  he arrived in a bad mood.  christie said, \\\"what are you so mad about?\\\"  i said, \\\"he\\\'s made \\\'cause he\\\'s ugly.  if you were that ugly, you\\\'d be mad, too.\\\"  \\\"i\\\'m not ugly.\\\"  \\\"no, of course, you\\\'re not.  you know that.  so don\\\'t be mad.  get glad, bitch.\\\"<BR><BR>he changed clothes, came back out into the living room.  he looked at christie.  \\\"are we goin\\\' over there?\\\"  she turned to me:  \\\"we thought we should go over to the landing and have a drink.  just to commemorate it.\\\"  \\\"i\\\'m not supposed to go into bars.\\\"  \\\"well, we\\\'ll be with you.  no big deal.\\\"<BR><BR>we walked from their house, up the dirt road, around the curve, and there it was:  long, low-slung light purple building with green trim.  no windows.  one car in the parking lot.  we went around the side to the door.  sign on it said they open at six, and it was just after.  rex, the bartender, was the only one inside.  he saw us:  \\\"hey!\\\"  of the nights we were together, before, we spent time there every night but one.<BR><BR>he said, \\\"mr. matt.  well.  are you over your hangover yet?\\\"  \\\"hangover?\\\"  \\\"the one you must have had starting new year\\\'s morning.\\\"  \\\"i never get hangovers.  never got.\\\"  he turns to jamie:  \\\"did i ever tell you this?\\\"  he takes a rocks glass off the shelf and sets it on the bar.  \\\"you drank six of those full of stoli in about two hours.  couldn\\\'t tell a thing was wrong with you.  talked fine, walked fine, making jokes.\\\"  jamie:  \\\"no, you just said he\\\'d been drinkin\\\', but he seemed alright when he left.\\\"  \\\"he did.  he said he was leaving, and we were going to call a cab, but, like i said....\\\"  me:  \\\"well, i don\\\'t remember anything after the second one.  that\\\'s typical for blacking out for me.  nobody can tell i\\\'m drunk.  and i operate just like i was sober.  not outta hand or anything.  drive just fine.  drive to tampa, drive to gainesville, wherever.\\\"  rex:  \\\"well, i hope i\\\'m not the first one to tell you this, but you\\\'re one lucky motherfucker.\\\"  \\\"i really don\\\'t think luck had anything to do with it.\\\"<BR><BR>so we settle into the stools, and christie orders a seabreeze, and jamie asks for a jack and coke.  i\\\'m on the end.  jamie, christie, me.  rex comes down to me.  \\\"and what about you?\\\"  they all look at me.  \\\"uh, pineapple juice and orange juice.\\\"  \\\"pineapple-orange.  interesting choice, mr. matt.  we don\\\'t get too many requests for juice.  good for you.\\\"<BR><BR>we talk about this and that.  i grin thinking of something and tell them that the only other time i\\\'ve stayed out of a bar for a year and then come back was when i got trespassed out of the hilltop in brooksville one fourth of july then came back a year later and showed my ass again and ended up getting trespassed to the <U>next</U> july 4th.  good times.<BR><BR>three or four more people wander in.  rex opens the cash register and takes out some change and goes over to the jukebox.  i know what\\\'s coming.  first song is <B><I>bizarre love triangle</B></I>.  he played that every night, before.  just a little ribbing, i guess.<BR><BR><I>it\\\'s no problem of mine<BR>but it\\\'s a problem I find<BR>living a life that I can\\\'t leave behind<BR><BR>rex serves the people down at the other end of the bar then comes back over to us.  i tell them about a bad christmas eve a long time ago.  so long ago that it\\\'s funny now.<BR><BR>we tell him bye and go to this pizza place up off of ridge road.  that\\\'s the first restaurant we ate at.  cheese pizza and salad.  they have chianti.  i have a coke.  we sit at a square table, occupying three sides of it.  for some reason, it feels unnatural, like there should be two of us or four of us.  like electrons.  something i learned about electrons a long time ago that i can\\\'t remember.  they seek a stable condition.<BR><BR>back at theirs, i give them their gifts.  christie says, \\\"yeah, hemingway\\\'s good.  i\\\'ll enjoy this.\\\"  jamie is enthralled.  \\\"where\\\'d you get this?\\\"  tell him.  he looks at all the cartridges, looks at the adapter stuff.<BR><BR>they both look at me.  christie says, \\\"well, we got you a present, but there\\\'s nothing we can give you right now.\\\"  i just kind of look at them.  i say, \\\"uh-huh.\\\"  jamie says, \\\"you\\\'ll get it on monday.\\\"  \\\"what is it?\\\"  \\\"well, we thought about it for a long time, and it isn\\\'t something you need, i mean, you can live without it, but it would make your life a lot nicer.\\\"  \\\"sounds good.  what is it?\\\"  christie:  \\\"cable.  for six months.  digital with all the channels we get and hbo and those digital radio channels.\\\"  \\\"jeez, ya\\\'ll shouldnt\\\'ve done that.  i can\\\'t take that.  that had to cost five or six hundred bucks.\\\"  \\\"nah, c\\\'mon.  listen:  it makes us happy to make you happy.\\\"<BR><BR>we all hug.  jamie hooks up the atari and we play space invaders and asteroids and defender and baseball.  i start thinking about things and get sick to my stomach.  ask if one of them to take me home.  christie says she will.  hug jamie and wish him a merry christmas.  won\\\'t see them before then.<BR><B>in the car, we\\\'re both quiet.  christie will try to start a conversation, and i\\\'ll put the stopper to it.  finally, she says, \\\"what is it?  tell me.  no matter what it is, you can tell me.  it\\\'ll be alright.\\\"  \\\"i can\\\'t.\\\"  quiet.  \\\"i\\\'m serious.  it doesn\\\'t matter what it is.  you\\\'re gonna get yourself in trouble if you don\\\'t let people help you.\\\"  \\\"i can\\\'t say what it is.  i just can\\\'t.\\\"  quiet.  \\\"okay.  don\\\'t.  but don\\\'t say i didn\\\'t try.\\\"  quiet.  get to my apartment. we both get out of the car and walk around to the front of it.  we look at each other.  she says, \\\"okay, merry christmas, alright?  have a good christmas.\\\"  \\\"you, too.\\\"  we hug.  she opens her door.  she says, \\\"call me.  okay?\\\"  i nod.  she backs out, and i wave.  she waves back.  i keep waving until the car disappears over the hill.'),(3758,'<p><img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/bike.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/garage.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/catbird.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/cactus.jpg\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/surf.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/cream.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/seasons.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/suncar.jpg\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.homorobotic.org/swinney/lilbike.jpg\">\r\n</p>'),(3759,'<p>It\'s not just the headache that I\'ve had since I woke up this morning. I\'m having one of those days where I feel like I need to expell something. (I can\'t find the right word for what I mean, because it all ultimately sounds like I\'m saying I\'m full of shit (which may very well be the case).) What I mean to say is that there\'s something inside of me that needs to come out, and I\'m not sure what it is. It could be that I need to purge myself creatively, or maybe I just need to throw up...I\'m couldn\'t say with any certainty.</p>\r\n<p>I have this overwhelming sense of urgency, but the line between physical and spiritual is so blurry at this point that I\'m not sure what it is that needs to be released.</p>\r\n<p>PS - My boss asked me if I was high when I came in this morning (which around here wouldn\'t be the end of the world, just fodder for \"you remember when\" stories yet to be told). That would explain a lot, but just isn\'t true.</p>'),(3760,'<P><I>when you have once seen the glow of happiness on the face of a beloved person, you know that a man can have no vocation but to awaken that light on the faces surrounding him; and you are torn by the thought of the unhappiness and night you cast, by the mere fact of living, in the hearts you encounter.<BR><P align=right>- albert camus</P></I><BR><BR>christmas<BR>dinner at sister\'s apartment<BR>home by a little after two<BR>phone call<BR>cd, yautia from latin market taken with<BR>her condo<BR>cd in<BR><I><B>pierrot lunaire</B><BR>what the fuck is this?</I><BR>cd out<BR>massive attack, <I><B>blue lines</B></I>, on repeat<BR><BR>preparation of sancocho criollo (vegan)<BR>one of the nicest kitchens i\'ve ever been in<BR>all kinds of cookware<BR>stew left to simmer<BR><BR>bedroom<BR>the act<BR>lazing<BR>talking very quietly<BR>the act<BR>stereo off<BR>sleep<BR><BR>evening, about sunset<BR>cocteau twins, <B><I>treasure</B></I><BR>stew<BR>french bread<BR>spinach salad<BR>last of a chocolate pound cake<BR>home<BR>meeting in tarpon springs<BR>home<BR><BR>dvd player sister gave hooked up<BR><B><I>spartan</B></I> she gave watched<BR>(strong, strong recommendation)<BR>(mamet fixation known,<BR><B><I>house of games</B></I> watched together<BR>[first time for both of us])<BR>phone call halfway through<BR><I>there\'s a convenience store right there on grand near their house.  i\'ll call you from there, and you can pick me up.</I><BR><BR>part of <B><I>a moveable feast</B></I> read<BR>walk around neighborhood<BR>central and madison<BR>one block south of me<BR>punk band getting drunk in their carport<BR>conversation about christmas seemingly negating saturday night<BR><BR>sunday morning<BR>phone call from christie at eleven-thirty<BR>pleasantries<BR><I>so you guys want to get together tonight?<BR>yeah....  yeah.  what time you want us to pick you up?</I><BR><BR>phone call to david in the early afternoon<BR><I>it\'s whoever was in that jeep, right?<BR>... we weren\'t serious, were we?  i mean, we were just havin\' a good time is all it is, right?<BR>i <U>thought</U> we were serious.<BR>i\'m sorry.  i\'m really sorry.  i don\'t know what to tell you.<BR></I>(silence)<I><BR>it\'s alright, dude.  whatever.  it\'s cool.  i\'ll see you around town, alright?</I><BR><BR>italian restaurant on the highway<BR>(every fucking thing on the highway)<BR>tagliatelle with pesto<BR>salad<BR>their house<BR>conversation about our christmasses<BR>christmas eve evening for them at jamie\'s brother\'s house in gainesville<BR>(parents\' townhouse too small for all the family)<BR>christie\'s mom\'s house in lakeland christmas eve night, christmas day<BR><BR>band-aid off the wound probably the best way to do it<BR><I>is there somebody else?<BR>yeah.<BR>i knew it.</I><BR>no anger<BR>sighing<BR>her head hung<BR>nothing from jamie<BR>look exchanged between them<BR>silence<BR><I>i tried to get \'er to understand.  she wouldn\'t go for it.  didn\'t want to have anything to do with it.  she didn\'t care.<BR></I>crying.  christie.  christie crying<BR>hard look from jamie<BR><BR><I>i mean, this was never gonna be a forever thing.  we all knew that, right?  we knew that goin\' into it.<BR></I>jamie staring at me<I><BR>i dunno.  i don\'t know what we knew.  all we know is that we love you.<BR><BR>we weren\'t gonna go on the rest of our lives with this, right?  we all knew that.  when was it gonna stop?  a year from now?  five years from now?  i wanna get married and have kids, an\' i know you guys do, too.  that\'s not gonna happen if we\'re all together.  it\'s just gonna be harder if we wait.  it isn\'t a phase we\'re goin\' through.  it was just a period of our lives that we spent together.  and you guys have a future together, an\' it\'s without me.  i don\'t wanna fight (choke) i don\'t wanna fight about it.<BR></I><BR>crying.  me.  sobbing.<BR><BR>jamie yelling for a second<BR>jamie crying<BR>not boo-hooing<BR>tears rolling down his cheeks, though<BR>first time i\'d seen him cry<BR><BR><I>i\'m sorry.  i\'m sorry, i\'m sorry, i\'m sorry.<BR></I>(no words.  crying.<BR>christie crying hard<BR>me crying hard)<BR><BR><I>i\'m just gonna go.  it isn\'t gonna get any easier.  i\'m sorry.</I><BR>get up<BR>manage to stop crying<BR><I>give me a hug</I><BR>she gets up<BR>hug her<BR>hug him<BR><BR><I>i\'m always gonna love both of you.  i\'m sorry it had to go down this way.  don\'t cry, alright?  don\'t cry.  it\'s alright.  it really is.</I><BR><BR>out the front door<BR>don\'t look back<BR>orpheus learned that<BR>lot\'s wife learned that<BR><BR>down the dirt road<BR>around the curve<BR>start crying<BR>to the paved road<BR>left is the highway<BR>across it is the casino boat parking lot<BR>right is the intersection of grand and whatever the hell the name of this road is<BR><BR>past a trailer park<BR>motorcycle repair shop on the other side of the street<BR>at the intersection is alex\'s place on the nortwest corner<BR>convenience store at the northeast<BR>police station on the southeast<BR>the fat iranian woman (not named alex) who owns the bar sits on the patio out by the road<BR><BR><I>(semi-thick accent) you alright, player?</I><BR>ignore her<BR><BR>sit on the concrete by the pay phone in front of the store<BR>crying<BR>stop for awhile<BR>start again<BR>sit there about half an hour<BR>finally stop<BR><BR>study the sky<BR>the void yawns<BR>clicks its teeth<BR>orion and the big dipper<BR>up there forever<BR>(well, orion only in the winter)<BR>north star up there forever<BR>asteroid could come along, and your ass would be out of luck<BR>water over there across the highway<BR>tidal wave could come along, and your ass would be out of luck<BR><BR>the in-between time<BR>the interval<BR><BR>get up and get two quarters out of my pocket<BR>pick up the handset<BR>put it back<BR>sit<BR><BR>get up<BR>cross grand and head west toward the highway<BR>turn down the dirt road<BR>the living room light is on<BR>the porch light is on<BR>i can hear them talking<BR>they\'re on the porch<BR>in back of the house<BR>can\'t tell what they\'re saying<BR>walk away<BR><BR>back to the store<BR>sit in front for fifteen, twenty minutes<BR>alex\'s place is directly across the street<BR>the cotee river lounge is down grand a little way<BR>bikes parked in front of it<BR>moose lodge next to it<BR>(can\'t go in there)<BR>gay bar is last in line<BR><BR>decide on the gay bar<BR><BR>walk down there<BR>parking lot enclosed by a chain-link fence<BR>one car in it<BR>it\'s twenty to eleven<BR><BR>in the door<BR>through the area where the restrooms, the pool tables, the jukebox are<BR>rex is down all the way at the end of the bar<BR>reading a newspaper spread out on it<BR><BR>the bar is sixty or seventy feet long<BR><BR>(something about the situation struck me as being strangely familiar, and it came to me later when i got home.  it reminded me of the scene in <B><I>the shining</I></B> where jack goes into the closed bar in the hotel, and the ghost caretaker guy is there behind the bar.)<BR><BR>rex must notice me out of the corner of his eye because he looks up.<BR><I>mr. matt.  whatcha doin\' in here, man?</I><BR><BR>i come to rest in front of him.<BR><I>i got thirsty.<BR><BR>and mommy \'n daddy aren\'t with you this time, huh?  what about that?  (pause)  seriously, though, no fuckin\' around - why are you in here?</I><BR><BR>i just look at him.<BR><BR><I>well, okay.  i guess if i refuse to serve you, you\'ll just go someplace where they don\'t know you, huh?  (pause)  whatcha gonna have?</I><BR><BR>i stare at his eyes for about ten seconds, cannot speak.  he leans forward and widens his eyes.<BR><BR><I>yeeesss?</I><BR><BR>i blurt, <I>pineappleorange.<BR><BR>ah, yes, the ever-popular pineapple-orange.</I><BR><BR>he gets a tall glass off the rack in back of him and fills it halfway with pineapple juice from a quart-sized container and tops it off with o.j. from the handheld nozzle thing that also dispenses sodas, tonic, etc.<BR><BR><I>two dollars.</I><BR><BR>i reach in my pocket and take out the packet of bills in it and unfold three dollars and hand them to him.  he doesn\'t thank me.  i look down at my drink sitting there on the bar on a napkin, the vibrancy of the juice.  he looks at me quizically.  i start crying and turn and walk away towards the door.<BR><BR>he says, <I>okay.  alright.  take care.  have a happy new year.</I><BR><BR>back to the store<BR>sit there<BR>cry some more<BR>walk back to c & j\'s<BR>the bedroom light is the only one on<BR>back to the store<BR>get it together<BR>dial her number<BR><BR>she answers<BR><BR><I>do you know what you made me do?<BR><BR>what?!<BR><BR><U>do</U> <U>you</U> <U>know</U> <U>what</U> <U>you</U> <U>made</U> <U>me</U> <U>do</U>?  HUH?<BR><BR>i didn\'t make you do anything.<BR><BR></I>(silence)<I><BR><BR>this isn\'t gonna work.<BR><BR>why not?<BR><BR>because i don\'t love you.  i just thought i did.<BR><BR>oh, really?<BR><BR>i got confused.  i mean, between the sex bein\' great and the feeling of freedom i got from being open with you, that and likin\' you, i mean, i like you a whole lot, i\'m really fond of you, but....  i guess i tricked myself into thinkin\' it was love.  i mean, jesus, we\'ve only known each other for two weeks.  not <U>even</U> two weeks.  and here i\'m thinkin\' we\'re gonna get married or somethin\'.  i guess i was just deludin\' myself.  i have been known to be delusional every so often.  i don\'t know.<BR><BR>i don\'t know, either.  (pause)  well, at least you\'re being honest about it.  you aren\'t going to let yourself use me and hide your intentions.  that\'s something positive, anyway.  you want to come over here and talk about it?<BR><BR>i don\'t think we\'d better.  let\'s just let this be it.<BR><BR>well, what can i say?  i really think we could work this out.  i thought we had a real future.  (pause)  was it bad?<BR><BR>breaking up with them?  you know how i can tell how much i love them?  the way it hurts.  how much it hurts.  whereas, with you, i\'m not feeling too bad about it.  no offense.<BR><BR>sure.  well, you want to chalk it up as a growth experience?<BR><BR>definitely.  that and hormones.<BR><BR>i guess.<BR><BR>i think i left my schoenberg cd over there.<BR><BR>you want me to bring it to you?<BR><BR>probably not a good idea.  keep it.  do yourself a favor sometime and get really high and listen to it.<BR><BR>i\'ll keep that in mind.  (pause)  i guess this is it, huh?  take care of yourself.  okay?  if you ever change your mind....<BR><BR>i\'ll be sure to let you know.<BR><BR>(blurts)  i love you.  i really do.<BR><BR>i love you, too.  but not the way i would need to.  i love you like i love everybody else.  i gotta go.  be good.  bye.  i\'m sorry.<BR><BR>i\'m sorry, too.</I><BR><BR>she hangs up.<BR><BR>it\'s late.  the store\'s closed.  i don\'t cry.  there aren\'t many cars going by.  i told the iranian woman one time, four years ago, that i lived in downtown new port richey, and she said that this area is downtown <U>port</U> richey.  it\'s just bars, a store, a laundromat, the police station, trailers, houses.  somebody told me back then that there were quite a few prostitutes living in this vicinity.  freelancers.  living together in twos and threes.  never met any of them.<BR><BR>grand boulevard is the safest way to get back downtown.  <U>my</U> downtown.  both washington street and united states highway nineteen are sketchy at this time of night.  i head south on grand past the bars.  the river runs on my right.  the houses on the other side of it are right on the water, whereas those over here have the road in between.  they\'re less valuble.<BR><BR>i used to walk this road on nights when i lost all my money on the casino boat and couldn\'t afford a cab.  i would have nothing in my pockets but keys and weed.  the stars were up there, never changing.  a streetlight would cast sharply defined shadows on the wall of a house close to mine.  i would go past it, behind the abandoned catholic church and the building beside it:  father felix hall.  it was where they used to have their shindigs, i guess, bingo and whatnot.  i would come up indiana, toward adams, toward central, following the sidewalks east and south. i would make my way, uncertain but free, somehow hopeful, somehow calm, knowing that i was close to my home, a place that i knew was my place in this world.</P>'),(3761,'<P>\r\nGot a call from him on Sunday, asking me to reintroduce him into my son\'s life.  Fucker blew my 7 year old off for over a month with 2 phone calls after being in his life non-stop for 6 months.....felt good to lay down the law.  Told him he fucks my kid off one more time and he\'s done. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nSo I get a phone call from his irate parents later that night.  He ripped off over $300 from their checking account via the internet. Merry fucking christmas!!!Every time I turn around there is another line that he crossed that I never thought he would.   The person I knew is gone, lost forever to the needle and the next isch.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nIt\'s hard to look at that familiar face, hear that familiar voice and realize that the person I knew and loved for so long no longer exists.  He has no soul, he has no honor, there is nothing of him left.  I feel pity and loathing and sorrow because I could not guide him to the light and now he is lost forever in darkness.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI have seen the path he will tread and thank the creator every day that I didn\'t follow him trying to save him from himself.  I can only do so much, I am only a guide, not a savior.  And if he won\'t heed my warnings, I must walk my own path and find the next lost soul to guide.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nMaybe next time I will be more successful, maybe not....that\'s for the creator to decide not me.  All I know is that I have finally let go of what could be and am trying to deal with what is.  Not easy for a dreamer like me.  My guide told me how to cut the bond between us and not catch the ricochet from it.  I keep meaning to do it, but I am afraid of the emptiness that will ensue...the sure knowledge inside that I am alone in the world. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n Co-dependent in the extreme and pathetic I know, but there is some comfort in feeling that connection and knowing that whether I am with him or not, I am not alone. Being able to reach out and feel another person through that bond, I\'m not sure I know how to live without it now that I\'ve experienced it.  So much mumbo jumbo to others and a fact of life for me. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n I do know that neither of us can move along our paths as we were meant to until it\'s gone, but what can I say, I\'m a coward.  Soon though it will be gone because I want to be free, I want my life back, I want my soul back, I want my heart back so I can take them where they will be treasured and not stolen or used.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThere are many things I believe that others do not, or don\'t understand, or choose not to, depending on the person.  Spiritually, I am meant to be a warrior, a defender, a protector and right now I am protected only by instict and a small wooden sword.  Learning that you are a just person is a harsh lesson because sometimes doing the \"just\" thing goes against every standard I live by.  What is just is not always what\'s right and requires an acceptance for grey areas of morality I never allowed myself to be in before.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nA voice whispered to me not too long ago \"There is no morality, only the spirit and the Unspoken Laws.\"  Believing that to my core is not an easy transition, especially considering my conservative upbringing.  But more and more I see it to be true in my life.  So like a child I stumble and look to the Creator for approval (aka Hall Pass) before every step I take, I\'m sure he\'s sick of it by now.  But if the laws are Unspoken, how else am I supposed to find my way to myself???  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThis is quite a bit more than I meant to say, kind of silly, but I\'ll leave it as random thoughts and writings often speak for your inner truth.\r\n</P>\r\n<P><I>added line breaks to the text block. --aus.</I></P>'),(3762,'<table width=\"300\" align=\"center\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td>\r\n<small>\r\n<p>\r\nwe picked up the texans at o\'hare.  the change was most noticable in my little nephew. \r\ni\'ve collected his depictions of the local flaura and fauna from the very moment that he began to make them.  \r\nwhen he lived in illinois, the prairie state, i pinned his renderings of bunnies, squirrels, and flowers on my board.\r\nnow he sends drawings of gila monsters and armadillos.\r\nhe sings too: \"texas, my texas...\"\r\nhis mother, my sister #1, wore a red holiday sweater, with matching red holiday socks.\r\nher husband works for ibm.\r\nthe family is republican.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img1%%\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" border=\"1\" bordercolor=\"black\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwe were all stuck together, in a small car, in airport traffic.\r\nmy mother was along for the ride; she started to nag.\r\ni knew that i was about to crack under the stress, and so i broke out the glove.\r\nan old, small pair lay hidden under the dash, for use in tire changes - or other emergencies.\r\nthis was an, \"other emergency.\"\r\ni put one on my left hand, and then, with my right, used a bic pen to draw eyes on it.\r\ni put my mother\'s hat atop it, and then moved my thumb like a jaw.\r\nthe gloved hand proceeded to speak as my mother spoke.\r\neveryone laughed, at first.\r\nbut by the time that we had reached our destination the glove was complaining that it only had one grandchild.\r\nand only my little nephew was still laughing.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img2%%\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" border=\"1\" bordercolor=\"black\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsister #2 rolled in the driveway behind the wheel of a used honda.\r\nthree bumper-stickers were on the back:\r\n(1) lesbian cowboy; (2) kerry-edwards 2004; (3) amnesty international.\r\nshe wore a festive jet-black t-shirt that she bought at a north side thrift store, with a long-sleeved lilac-purple t-shirt underneath.\r\nshe was going to cook something, and she wanted everyone to give her room to do it.\r\nso she stuck her arms out, and traced some geometric figure in space.\r\n\"ok, umm, this is my triangle.  everyone stay out, ok?\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni pulled the gloves out, again, and made a face on the right one too.\r\nand then the left \"mother\" glove argued, in a severe tone, with the right glove - which, oddly enough, sounded like a hippie \"daughter.\"\r\nsister #2 almost wet her pants.\r\nmy little nephew had enough, and he drew a picture of rockets with the letters \"U.S.A.\" destroying the talking gloves.\r\nthis is the way that six-year-olds solve problems.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img3%%\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" border=\"1\" bordercolor=\"black\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe following day, sister #1 and i had a huge fight; she left in a huff.\r\nthe day after that, sister #2 and i traded back rubs.\r\nand everything was sort of ok, again.\r\nsister #2 is leaving for ny, ny in the morning.\r\nshe told me about the time that she spent at rockaway beach.\r\nand i thought of the ramones show, and cabaret metro.\r\ntonight i reflect on the presents: the gift certificate from sister #1, and the vietnamese ceramics from the mennonite project for third world artisans that sister #2 gave me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img4%%\" width=\"255\" height=\"144\" border=\"1\" bordercolor=\"black\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npensive, drinking wine with rousseau and black flag\'s <i>slip it in</i> - still so much a child of the 80s, in my straight leg, high-waisted levis - i find it hard to believe that we came from the same womb and home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni see in my own family - as if it weren\'t obvious - so many of the same divisions that i see in my country around me.\r\nand the thing that i don\'t see - in the big picture, or in the little picture - is some way of talking and laughing and bringing everyone together.\r\ni would like to see that especially because, maybe the first time, i would really like to have children.\r\n[hi mom.  no, it\'s not the glove at the keyboard.  it\'s really me: paul.]\r\nit seemed like every card this year included a picture of some friend\'s or relative\'s child.\r\nand they\'re all up on the bulletin board behind me, along with my nephew\'s drawings - ringing like so many biological alarm clocks or salvation army bells, whichever.\r\ni\'m just tired of the holidays, and the politics, and the war, and the waste, and the competition, and the deception, and the same damn thing over-and-over again.\r\ni hope for a happy new year, for everyone.\r\n</p>\r\n</small>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3763,'<p>TO THE EDITOR\r\n</p><p>\r\nFiremen and cops get all the credit.  They work on the weekends, they work on holidays, you can hire a cop to protect you from your friends when you throw a christmas party.  The firemen will come when your christmas tree catches fire.  But what do they do for you when it gets really bad, when you become depressed because you are all allone in your apartment or home on christmas eve, on christmas day?  And they won\'t save you from your anxiety from being stuck in the home of your parents, your \'home\'.  When you are watching TV or hiding among the relics of your childhood in a room with a bed too small for you.  No cop, no fireman.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI am a movie theater attendent.  I rip tickets.  I clean isles.  I work holidays.  I don\'t just work 9-5 either.  I work 10AM-1AM on holidays.  I am your savior from it all.  Whatever it is, I am here for you.\r\n</p><p>\r\nLast night I ripped tickets for: Lemony Snicket (escaping from kids), Ocean\'s Twelve (escaping from work), Spanglish (escaping from non-english speaking in-laws), the Polar Express (escaping from kids and feeling like a kid again), Blade: Trinity (kill the blood sucking boredom), National Treasure (escaping), Christmas with the Kranks (attempting mental suicide), Flight of the Phoenix (having escaped in all the other movies..), The Incredibles (escaping for the 6th time).\r\n</p><p>\r\nNobody tips.  We movie attendents don\'t get bonuses either.  Legally, it would be too dificult for the movie corporation to swing that, my manager tells me.  He gave me a Starbucks card.  (A croissant and a mocha latte later, it is spent.)  \r\n</p><p>\r\nThanksgiving and Christmas are two of our biggest days.  It is a conspiracy by the movie industry to force me to work.  Never does a blockbuster movie get released on a saturday, except if that saturday is a holiday.  (I have not mentioned it but, 4th of July also, Memorial Day.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nTHIS CHRISTMAS, THE ______ ARE BACK!  ..AND CHRISTMAS WILL NEVER BE THE SAME!\r\n</p><p>\r\nTHIS CHRISTMAS, GO FOCK YOURSELF!  (I came up with that one for the Meet the Fockers movie.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nIn writing this, I would like the general movie going community to the plight of the ticket ripper.  Consider that we workers of the movie industry, DO NOT LIKE BEING HERE ON HOLIDAYS.  I\'m passing around a patition, please do the same.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHere is the text:\r\n</P><P><B>\r\nPETITION TO HALT THE FORCED LABOR OF CONSUMER ENTERTAINMENT THEATER WORKERS ON MAJOR HOLIDAYS BY THE WORKERS OF SUCH THEATERS WHO WOULD RATHER BE AT CHRISTMAS PARTIES, WITH THEIR FAMILIES, OR JERKING OFF IN A LAZY BOY.\r\n</p><p>\r\nSIGN HERE:\r\n</B></P>\r\n<PRE>\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n___________________________________________\r\n</pre>\r\n<P>\r\nSigned,\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Consumer Entertainment Theater Workers Of The Holidays</i></P>'),(3764,'<p>\r\nI was sitting at my desk doing a few updates on my website and the doorbell rang. I knew who it was before even getting up to look at the â€œwhose outside my doorâ€ monitor I have.\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nChurchies! Who would it be today? Those wonderful Anglicans who never want to come in for tea and scrumpets? Those pesky Baptists who think the music I listen to is satanic? Or those lovable Jehovahâ€™s Witnesses? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nIt was the Jehovahâ€™s. A father son duo. The son held his bible tightly against his chest, his suit nice and tidy. The father had his attachÃ© case at his side and spoke English. Great! I thought. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Hello! How are you two fine looking gentleman doing today? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: Hello! Fine! How are you today? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Grrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrreat! Iâ€™m watching some pornography, would you like to join me? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: We are Jehovahâ€™s Witnesses. We are from around the neighborhood and we are spreading the word of Jesus Christ our savior.\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: That sounds awesome! I am sorry, but I am not very religious. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: Being religious sometimes comes from inside, it isnâ€™t always about going to church. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Thatâ€™s true, but I donâ€™t even believe in god. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: Where are you from? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: I am from Canada. Toronto Canada. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: Ah Canada. A very beautiful but cold country. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Yes, You can imagine how many clothes the Jehovahâ€™s witnesses have on in Toronto right now! Itâ€™s a nut biting winter in Toronto this year. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: What do you do in Korea? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: I am a teacher. I teach English to little kids. Some of them are Jehovah, some of them are Buddhist, and well some of them are other things. I think also some of them are like me and are non-believers. It is a very strange group of snots. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: A Teacher! Did you know that Jesus is the GREATEST TEACHER EVER?\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Well that may be true, but lets be honest. Most of his students arenâ€™t listening. Did you know that George Bush loves Jesus too, and that he sent young men and women to kill other young men and women? It is also true that your President Mr. Moo Hyun sent Korean men and maybe women to their deaths in Iraq. I think Jesus would be very cross, donâ€™t you? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: *laughs* yes, you are correct. But we must all try quite hard to follow and believe in the teachings of Jesus. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Oh no doubt. I have read the bible three times, I have read the Koran, The book or Mormon, and the teachings of Buddha, and I think they all have good things to say. I just think people get all weird and crazy and tend to let themselves become crusaders instead of lovers. Would you not agree? I still, to this day, like star wars better. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: *surprised* You have read the bible three times? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Yes, I went to roman catholic high school in Toronto. I read the book of Mormon at a Marriott hotel that I worked at, and I read the Koran for shits and giggles. I read the teachings of Buddha in my hotel room in Osaka. I think perhaps the best one was the teaching of Buddha. But the book of Mormon has its moments. I kind of like the part where the white people are told by god to kill the red people. You knowâ€¦American Indians.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: I would like to give you something to read. *rummages in his attachÃ© case*.\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: The watchtower? I love that magazine!\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovahâ€™s son: *awe*\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: yes you know the watchtower? \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: yes it is in the subway in Toronto a lot. Its good commute reading. I learn a lot about why gambling is bad, and how and why I should trust the bible. Oh did you know that there have been statistics done, and there has been more death and destruction because of the bible than any other book in recorded history? WOW!!!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: Well people do strange things upholding their beliefs. I myself am a pacifist and would never hurt someone else â€˜in the name of Jesus Christ our saviorâ€™ because I do not think Jesus would ever tell me to do so. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: Well I have some things to do, but it has been a pleasure talking with you. I hope you have a great day! *shakes hands* Happy new year! \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nJehovah: perhaps we will meet againâ€¦may the word of Jesus our lord and savior be with you. \r\n</p>\r\n<p >\r\nMe: you too my son. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3765,'Moored amidst the bouys of<br>\r\nsocial he<br>\r\nhides what little he has<br><br>\r\n\r\nwith what little he has.<br>\r\n\"Defend your-<br>\r\nself!\" (a cry from the man<br><br>\r\n\r\nin red.)  But the pre-con-<br>\r\ndemned have no<br>\r\ndefense.  The first limestone<br><br>\r\n\r\nsets sail.  Lists against his<br>\r\nskin.  Syphons<br>\r\nmaroon oil from within.'),(3766,'All day Thursday I kept getting this feeling that something I was doing was altering the course of my life....you know just one of those things.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI didn\'t really do anything different really.  Chose to go to an acquaintance\'s house instead of where he\'s been staying.  Hung out most of the night chilling out, listening to tunes etcetera....midnight run to Walmart, that kind of thing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nGot a call from Dow telling me the babies were really sick, offered backup and stayed the night there helping him with them. They were still sick on New Year\'s Eve so I stayed there then too.  Was a little bummed because I got invited to a lock and gack party at Shannon\'s (where Dex has been staying lately).  Oh well, another time, no big deal.<br><br>\r\n\r\nOut of the blue I get this call from Chris.  JL had just walked into Shannon\'s bedroom, grabbed the bitchbeater off of the wall and threw it on the bed.  Told them, \"Any of you fuckers reach for that and I\'ll kill you.\"  Apparently this chick Dannie started to, he gets right into her face and puts his hand in his coat.  Dex got Chris out by saying he had nothing to do with anything and JL let him go.<br><br>\r\n\r\nChris was panicking on the phone and babbling about how he didn\'t know what to do and maybe he should call the cops.  Told him not to be stupid, he calls the cops and JL is after him now. Told him if Dex had wanted backup he wouldn\'t have gotten him out of there and the best thing he could do was go home to his old lady and pray for the best. <br><br> \r\n\r\nThank the creator for sick kids, cuz that\'s probably where I would have ended up considering I had nothing better to do!<br><br>\r\n\r\nOn a different note,  I got a call from this 16 year old I had hung out with like 3 weeks ago for a whopping 24 hours named Tracy.  She had wanted to call and say \"thank you\", said I saved her life. I guess she was going to commit suicide until she talked to me that night.  Now she\'s going back to get her GED, first time in school in over 3 years.  Moving to Seattle with a friend after that and getting her life back on track.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI guess sometimes I am a halfway decent guide. It\'s good to know that every once in a while I really do make a difference.  I think everyone should have a call like that at least once a year if only to remind us that we all make a difference at one point or another to someone.'),(3767,'<P>Springfield, Oregon...over on near Thurston Elementary, if 10 blocks can be called near...I am 9, my sister is 6.  Asked mom before school if we could go to the park together.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nshe smiled her tired smile and said \"Sure, just make sure to tell grandpa espy where you\'re going.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nCouldn\'t wait, barely paid any attention at school that day, bouncing in my seat on the bus.\r\n</P><P>\r\nFinally home and free.  Put on my skates with their metal wheels.  Helped my sister into her plastic, fit over the shoe kind and shouted eagerly to grandpa....\"We\'re going to the park\"...he yells back okay, home before dark.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEagerly we skate to the park with it\'s awesome hills, playground, lush green grass, warm sunshine and huge protective trees.  Playing and laughing and hiding and skating....\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe world turned dark as his voice echoed towards us \"What the hell do you two think you\'re doing??\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nHis voice thundering, his eyes like lightning...it is a rhetorical question, yet still I try to defend us from his wrath.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMom said we could go, we told grandpa.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\"I asked him where you were and he said he had no idea.\"  Brandishing a truck radio antenna that looked a mile long he tells us \"You girls better beat me home or you\'re getting a beating with this.\"  Then he turns and hops into his truck.\r\n</P><P>\r\nEven at nine I understood a lose/lose situation.  There was no way we were gonna beat him home, me with my metal wheels, her in her plastic and uphill on pavement the whole way home.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\"MOVE IT!!\"  Startled we start off desperately trying to keep ahead of his truck.  He is slowly driving behind us hanging out the window brandishing his almighty \"sword\" and telling us we had better hurry our little asses up.  I wonder now exactly how fast he thought we could move, shaking from fear on metal and plastic wheels, and even better my sister was just learning how to skate.  Frequently she would fall and cry and I would desperately drag her behind me until she got her feet, afraid he would run us over if the truck caught up.\r\n</P><P>\r\nFinally we could see our house up there on the corner, I started to relax, he was going to let us make it.  About half a block from the house he sped past us and whipped into the driveway.  I wanted to scream in frustration and terror but was panting to keep my breath as it was.  We scurried into the house and to our rooms to await our fate.  He never did spank us with that damn thing, although we were both sworn to secrecy.  I think Uncle Mason who was in the truck with him, talked some sense into him and maybe he beat the truth out of his viscious old man.  To this day I believe Grandpa Espy lied on purpose just so he could hear us get beaten.  Now that I am older I know he was that kind of person.\r\n</P><P>\r\nYears later, now 27, I get a good chuckle from this story, simply because it does make a funny picture.....2 little girls on horrible skates running like mad from an angry father swinging a truck radio antenna like a baton and chasing them with his Ford Truck.  HEHEHE....laugh at the pain, it\'s the best way to survive :D\r\n</P>'),(3768,'asdfafds'),(3769,'last monday night, i was awakened by something scratching and running around on top of the ceiling.  i had heard rats up there a couple of months ago, but they had gone away.  these sounded like big, fast rats.<BR><BR>call the landlord.  he says he\\\'ll put poison out around back where he thinks they might be coming in.  he comes over wednesday to do it, knocks, wakes me up at five in the afternoon.  he says, <I>those rats are attracted by the fruit trees out back.  the cats usually take care of them.</I><BR><BR>he\\\'s referring to some of the roughly seventy-six hundred stray cats in this neighborhood.  the czech (slovak?) guy next door puts food out, too, so they\\\'re always around.  (and i think it\\\'s mating season, because you always see them in pairs.  open the front door at three in the morning or turn the corner of van buren and central at four in the morning, and there they are, in your yard, in somebody\\\'s yard, not doing it, but standing about two feet apart.  like they\\\'re in conference or something.  he\\\'s probably telling her all the wonderful things he\\\'ll do for her.  [damn toms are spunky as hell, too.  you approach them, and they stand their ground.  usually, i\\\'m halfway afraid they\\\'re gonna jump me.])<BR><BR>i told the landlord how i was washing dishes and turned on the faucet, and the pipes made a sound, and one of these rats tore off across the top of the ceiling towards the living room and how fast it was and how big it sounded, and he said, <I>you know, that might be a cat up there that you hear.  probably after some rats.</I><BR><BR>he never specified what he was going to do about the situation.  as he was leaving, he said, <I>by the way, get some of those oranges.  they\\\'re delicious.</I>  i thought, <I>by the way, fuck those oranges.  they\\\'re the whole reason i\\\'ve got the goddam roller derby upstairs in the first place.</I><BR><BR>it\\\'s okay, though.  once i get to sleep, i don\\\'t notice it.  i\\\'m sleeping like a bear for about fourteen hours a day, and nothing puts a dent in it.  better than drinking, i guess.<BR><BR>but i <B><I><U>am</B></I></U> gonna get me some of those fuckin kumquats.'),(3770,'<p>\nDear friend,\n</p><p>\nRecently, I have learned that you have attempted suicide.  I cannot\ndescribe the sorrow that this knowledge brings.  I am extremely\ngrateful that you did not succeed, if only because I have at least one\nmore chance to try to communicate with you. \n</p><p>\nUnlike most people (and perhaps I am a bad friend for this) I am not\ngoing to serve you cliches about suicide being a bad choice.   I am\nnot even going to tell you not to do it.\n</p><p>\nI will attempt to describe how much I would miss you though.  This\nis not an attempt to guilt you into living.  Fuck that shit.   You\nneed to find the will to live within yourself.  I don\'t think you can\nfind that will for other people.  I certainly couldn\'t.  That is why I\ncan not offer you any of the usual platitudes.  Regardless, it is very\nimportant to me that you understand what your life means to me.    \n</p><p>\nI am committed to going onward, myself.  Come what may.  I\'m committed\nto having a future and a past.  I have a set of my own reasons for\nthis (that alas, I doubt you would share many of).  But the important\npart, the thing I want you to see here, is that the more people that I\nlove that are with me down the road, the easier time I\'ll have ahead\nof me.  And conversely, any time that some one that I love disappears\nfrom my life, the more it erodes my own will to live.\n</p><p>\nYou are an old and dear friend, and although our lives have grown\napart, we have a long and storied history.  That shared history is\nunique, and can not be replaced.   And the sharing is the important\npart, here.  I can tell anyone a story from my past.  But unless they\nwere there, their interest can only really be passing.  When I am\nancient, who is going to want to listen to my stories?  Who is going\nto keep me honest when I tell them?  Only the few that were there.\n</p><p>\nIn terms of personal history, you are one of the very few people that\nknows both how I\'ve changed, and how I\'ve stayed the same, over the\nyears.  There is a large degree of intimacy in that, in and of itself. \nBeyond this, I would feel your absence as more than a  mere personal\nloss.  \n</p><p>\nYour presence makes this world more interesting, more unique, more\nemotional, and more sincere, wherever it is.  It is a victory of\nbeing.  Though that is something that is probably difficult to see,\nfrom the inside, so to speak, it is a powerful and positive force in\nthis world.  One that would be sorely missed.\n</p><p>\nAll of this is just a long-winded way of saying that you are both\ncared about and valued.  Deeply.  And probably by a lot more people\nthan just me.  \n</p><p>\nRead that last paragraph again.  Really, stop, take a breath, and read\nit again.  If you remember nothing else about this message, I want you\nto take away that.  It won\'t change your situation, and it might not\neven change the way you feel about things, but it is something that I\nneed you to understand. \n</p><p>\nAnd though I\'ve got a lot more that I\'d like to share with you, it is\nmy deepest hope that I have many years left to do it in.\n</p>\n<p>\n<dl><dt>\nYour friend,\n</dt><dd>\n     Glen<br></dd></dl>\n</p>'),(3771,'<P>i did it.<BR><BR>it\'s been a year.  i haven\'t drank, used, gambled, been jailed, been suicidal.  and in eleven more days, it\'ll be a year since i\'ve been hospitalized.  my previous record for abstinence from substance use was six months and change.  1993.  with narcotics anonymous.<BR><BR>it\'s been a good year.  more good than bad.<BR><BR>i was thinking about how things would be if i had died.  my mom and grandma and aunt keep flowers on my family\'s graves.  they would have been out there today.<BR><BR>if you leave my parent\'s house, the house i grew up in, go down the dirt road to croom, go east to mcintyre, south to mondon hill, and west, you pass the house my grandfather grew up in (where it used to be, anyway.  it was vacant for a long time, and then the family let the fire chief burn it down.  for practice is what mom said, but that didn\'t make sense to me, since it burnt all the way down. whatever.  my mom\'s uncle dick lives in a trailer beside where it used to be.), my mom\'s cousin connie\'s house, and the house where lenore and gordon, my mom\'s aunt and uncle, raised their family.  turn south on jasmine, and you pass the house where mom grew up.  my grandmother sold it in the early 90s and moved into an apartment.  further south is the city cemetary.  (for the white people, anyway.  i don\'t guess it\'s <I>de facto</I> segregation, but the black people bury theirs in a cemetary in the woods way outside of town off of fort dade.  matter of choice.  and if i was black, i\'d probably choose that, too.)<BR><BR>my grandfather\'s buried towards the front, towards jasmine.  it\'s a double-berth spot with a vacancy there for grandma.  beside him are his mother and father.  all three of them started in or around tifton, georgia and ended up here.  mother, alzheimer\'s, 1968; father, heart attack, 1973; him, cancer, 1986.  his feet point toward my brother\'s grave.  jack daniel\'s/budweiser/methaqualone/automobile/oak tree, saturday night/sunday morning before he was supposed to leave for college, 1985.  (he also managed to kill his best friend, who was riding shotgun.)  born and died in brooksville.  (i would probably be in the spot beside my brother.  two boys who never grew up.)<BR><BR>beside where my grandmother\'s supposed to go are her parents.  all three of them started in some town outside of hamburg, germany and relocated to brooklyn in 1928.  my grandparents met in a coney island skating rink during the war and married a year later.  my grandma moved to florida and lived with her husband\'s family until the war was over.  my grandfather got stationed somewhere in southern california, i don\'t know where, but it was someplace near san pedro, because that\'s where they lived.  my mom was conceived there and born in brooksville in january, 1946.<BR><BR>sometime between then and when my aunt was born in may of \'49, my grandmother was pregnant.  her father called and said her mother had flipped and kicked him out of the apartment.  my grandma took my mother and my grandfather\'s two sisters (also in their early 20s) on the bus to new york to see if she could patch things up.<BR><BR>from the behavior that i\'ve heard about, it\'s a pretty safe bet that my great-grandmother was bipolar.  (so, i get it from her and my father\'s father.)  when my grandma got to the apartment, the bedroom she used to share with her sister was full of stuff her mother had recently bought, stuff like bath towels, trinkets, bric a brac, china, souvenirs.  she had spent all the savings.<BR><BR>i don\'t know what else transpired during the visit (i\'ll have to ask before it\'s too late), but the upshot was that my great-grandmother killed herself with an overdose of sleeping pills, and, subsequently, my grandmother miscarried.  when the dust settled, everything in the apartment was sold or given away, and my great-grandfather returned with the rest of them and lived out his days in a little house grandpa built behind theirs.  he died of a heart attack in 1972 on a stool at the virginia grill off the courthouse square with a glass of schlitz in front of him at eleven o\'clock in the morning.  he walked the two or three miles into town every morning and came back in the afternoon.  (he wasn\'t alcoholic, he was \"german.  that\'s just how he was raised.\"  who knows.)<BR><BR>so.  suicide.  substance abuse.  at the time, you don\'t consider how it\'s going to effect the other people in your life.  i\'m glad i\'m here.  i\'m glad i\'m clean.<BR><BR>my sponsor is giving me a party at his house after the meeting tonight.  that\'s outside of the program.  in a.a., on your anniversary, you get a poker chip.  in narcotics anonymous, you get a key tag.  in gamblers anonymous, well, you damn sure don\'t get a poker chip.  you get a pat on the back, is about it.<BR><BR>when you get a year, and they recognize you, everybody asks, \"how\'d you do it?\"  and the stock answer is \"one day at a time.\"  or \"through the grace of god and the help of (insert program here).\"  i don\'t know what i\'m going to say, because i really have no fucking idea.  i haven\'t been working any steps, really.  i think the whole thing\'s a crock.  i\'ve been doing research into rational recovery, and it really resonates.  it posits that addiction isn\'t a disease - it\'s a choice.  it isn\'t the result of a spiritual or moral difficiency.  you don\'t have to go to meetings the rest of your life.  you just have to decide you aren\'t going to engage in those behaviors anymore.  after tonight, i\'m going to taper off with the meetings and then stop attending them.  i expect i\'ll encounter resistance.  it\'ll probably be like trying to leave a cult.  luckily, there isn\'t any overlap between the groups on one hand and the case management people and my family on the other.  i don\'t know what would happen if they found out.  i\'m not incompetent, i don\'t think.  but then again, who knows what i\'d do with a wad in my pocket.<BR><BR>fuck it.  i\'m here.  i\'m alive.  i\'m happy.  oh, i almost forgot, i called karyssa at her job at a little after nine this morning and talked for over an hour.  i told her i just wanted to talk for awhile and get to know each other better and just spend some time together before we decide to get further involved.  well, who in the hell am i kidding, right?  we\'ll see what happens.<BR><BR>the library has dvds of <B><I>the sopranos</B></I>.  i\'ve never seen the show.  i\'ve been watching a couple of episodes a day lately.  my favorite thing from it, so far:<BR><BR>Tony:  You can\'t put the shit back in the donkey.<BR><BR>amen to that.</P>'),(3772,'<P>forgot to fill my scripts yesterday, so when i got home last night, there weren\'t meds to take and, consequently, i didn\'t sleep all night.  looked at the internet, watched tv, read, called the female at six-thirty and talked until about nine.  it\'s so liberating to be able to talk to somebody about anything at all, anything i that goes through my brain.  i guess i was sabotaging my chances at being happy when i broke up with her.  I Sabotage Myself.  once i recognize i\'m doing it, i can stop it.  i said i didn\'t love her, but i do.  i can\'t deny it.<BR><BR>so, walked up to the pharmacy at main and the highway and waited an hour and a quarter for them to fill it, and now i\'m home waiting for the medicine to take, and the rest of the day is fucked because i\'ll probably sleep until eight or nine o\'clock.  but at least i\'m not in jail.  that\'s what i tell myself on bad days.<BR><BR>if i was in jail, back in \'95 in hernando county, anyway, i\'d be getting high right about now.  we lived in a housing block with three cells on the bottom and three on top, showers on both floors to the side, toilets in the cells, common area in front with aluminum picnic tables and a tv on the wall.  cells held eight apiece, four sets of bunks.<BR><BR>breakfast was at six or so, then everybody went back to bed until around eleven.  that was when my homeboys zack, gene, felt (short for felton.  can\'t remember if that was his first or last name.), and i convened on felt\'s bunk down below mine, blanket hanging from mine to make kind of a little fort in there so we could hide and kind of disperse the smoke.  the wife of a guy named little red brought the weed and the papers in and slipped them to him during their contact visitation.  the guard who was supposed to be searching the guys afterwards was on the take.  he finally got caught somehow or other.  something about cashing one of her checks down at the pick kwik.<BR><BR>anyway, he was the same guard who came through every morning about eight o\'clock screaming, \"getcher asses up.  clean this goddam piss-smellin\' unit.  ya\'ll think ya\'ll\'re runnin\' shit.  ya\'ll ain\'t runnin\' SHIT!\"  we always ignored him and went back to sleep.<BR><BR>the guys arranged to have their people pay little red\'s wife for the weed on the outside.  as i recall, it went for about twenty bucks a joint.  i didn\'t have any money, but they let me smoke anyway.  i think it was because i entertained them when i was high.  i was fun to be around when i was stoned.  made them laugh.  they made me laugh, too.  made the whole thing a lot easier.  if i had to pick one ethnic group to live with the rest of my life, it would be black people.  i don\'t want to make any generalizations, but, as a whole, i\'ve found them to be more comforting and funnier than anyone else.<BR><BR>they were all in for selling crack.  state-level.  the county rented out space to the federal corrections system, and there were some big federal-level drug dealers in there with us, too.  (also five or six bank robbers.  i asked them how they got caught, and, invariably, they said that somebody close to them turned them in.)  after i got out, later on, i checked the state prison website.  all three of them were in different places, all very far away from brooksville.  i don\'t know why they do that.  it\'s inhumane.  much about corrections is.  crack is such a stupid drug, anyway.  if it weren\'t for it, the prisons would be half-vacant, between sales, distribution, possession, theft to pay for it, related violence, prostitution....<BR><BR>i used to tell them they were bad little boys.  daddy hadn\'t been around enough to keep their ass beat.  i told them i used to get mine beat on a regular basis when i was little, said my daddy would take me into his office.  said he had a little stuffed donkey in the corner, and he\'d say, <I>boy, bend over and look at my jackass.</I>  and then he would <U>light</U> <U>that</U> <U>ass</U> UP.  (actually, i made the donkey up, and the years when i got spanked were before the years when dad had an office.  i was being enterTAINing.  when i got a whipping, tim was usually involved.  we\'d get into some kind of trouble together, or we\'d have been fighting.  tim would always get his first (not by choice.  that\'s just the way dad did it.), and he\'d bend over, and i\'d be standing behind them, and he wouldn\'t look at me, just take it, stoic as hell.  and then when my turn came, he\'d stand behind us and make faces at me, and i couldn\'t help but look, and i\'d laugh and get asked what was so funny and would not be able to resist giving a smart answer and get it worse.  he was mean.  he was the mean one, and i was the smart-ass.)<BR><BR>they asked then what the hell was i doing in there.  i said that was a long story.  i didn\'t get into it.<BR><BR>i was wearing glasses.  a lot of things had been lost.  all my contacts.  and my glasses.  mom and dad got the optometrist to make me a new pair and brought them to me.  when i wear them, i rock the john lennon/james joyce round frames.  the fellas called me clark kent.  or professor.  or solitaire (i sat on my bunk and played a lot.).<BR><BR>after lunch, we went into the yard.  i worked out and they played basketball.  they would do push-ups and chin-ups, crunches, in the cell at night before lockdown.<BR><BR>we\'d take our showers at night.  usually, even though there was space and nozzles for six people in the shower, only one guy would go in there at a time.  somewhere along the line, though, we got it into our heads that we were going to start holding \"contests\".  you\'ll do a lot of things when you\'re bored.  i don\'t know who came up with the idea, probably me, but i suspect it was one of those group consensus things that starts off as a joke and slowly builds.<BR><BR>anyway, every three or four or five days, we\'d all get our shower things and troop in there like we knew what we were doing.  (i don\'t know what the rest of the guys thought was going on.)  it was one big shower with a curtain across the front.  we\'d line up at the back four abreast and soap up, and, well, you went for time or distance or, optimally, both.  it was highly erotic.  for everybody, i think.  moreso, for me, at least, because it was fraught with peril.  the situation could easily have taken an ugly turn.  they knew that, and they knew i knew that, and that made it nastier, i think.  i was big, but they were all bigger than i was, and there were three of them.  it was really just an excuse to masturbate together, but i guess if they called it something else, it made it okay in their minds, and they weren\'t queer.<BR><BR>looking on the website over the years, i saw where felt escaped from some medium-security camp in north florida and had more time tacked on his sentence.  then they all got out one by one, and i haven\'t seen any of them on there since.  i\'ll run into them some day.  all those people from your past - you\'ll run into all of them someday.  sooner or later.</P>'),(3773,'<p>I remember the smell.  I don\'t remember where it was (though I can guess), or when (though I can guess that too), or who I was with at the time (which I could narrow down to maybe a dozen people if I\'d guessed the when and the where first.)  It was probably in a large, disreputable-looking coffee urn rescued from a rummage sale or a startup gone shutdown, plugged precariously into the same circuit as the sound system with a paint-spattered extension cord labeled \"please return after stealing.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This urn would\'ve been sitting on a none-too-sturdy folding table, stained by years of holding up urns in equivalent situations, covered now by a pristine dryclean-only cloth hand-made in India and embroidered with Celtic designs and a big Om in the middle.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\'d be a line there, too -- a few sober folks looking for caffiene to keep them going through the night, not-so-sober folks looking for a change in experience, or trailing a friend, or simply thinking \"hey, that\'s a good idea\" and then forgetting what the line was for but having a fine old time anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Cups were either in short supply or massively overpurchased -- there\'s never anything in between.  Probably no sugar.  There\'d be a box of soy milk, and more already mixed in.  Maybe some fruit scattered around, or arranged into an intricate mandala, following the patterns of the impromptu tablecloth that would surely never fully recover.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And so finally, having followed the smell -- cinnamon, cloves, ginger, and more much harder to identify from across the room, a melange of exotic goodness direct from some silk road bazaar -- having followed that smell, waited in line, conversed (if you can call it a conversation) with the skinny guy who had earlier tried to give me a six-inch redwood sapling with instructions to plant it somewhere unexpected (\"the native plants can come back, man, they just need a little help\") and telling two complete strangers that I have no idea where to get any \"toast\" (and also no idea what they were talking about, but I was still new then and I wasn\'t about to exhibit my complete uncoolness -- though I later learned it didn\'t mattter.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After traversing the melange and the line with the trees and the \"toast\" (or lack of \"toast\") and my own perceived uncoolness, I finally got my first cup of good spicy nub chai.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And I remember it was so good, I immediately got into line again.</p>\r\n'),(3774,'<p>He\'s a basic brown bear, not nearly as tattered as most I\'ve seen -- but it looks like he\'s been around the block a few times.  Kind of a plain expression, a little sad, not as goofy as most, maybe he remembers which blocks he\'s been around.  Fur like an old beloved shag carpet -- but shorter -- and you\'d never find a carpet like that anyhow, those carpets only exist in the memories of our feet and maybe our behinds.<p>\r\n\r\n<p>But this is a bear with a past, and unlike most bears -- even the ones with pasts -- he\'s got a file.  Not one of those important files that the FBI has been failing to squeeze into a new computer system for the past decade or two, no, files on teddy bears are pretty far down the priority list compared to potential terrorists or Bram Cohen.  And anyway, most of this bear\'s file is from an earlier, more easily propogandized era.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you were to obtain the proper clearances to know about a small industrial park deep in the hills of West Virginia, maybe an hour from Morgantown only because the roads are so bad, then you could probably also get clearance to go to the second floor and ask the really amazingly bored guard to unlock filing cabinet 635-B to let you root around inside.  You might assume it\'s \"B\" for \"bear,\" but you\'d need an even higher clearance (and a native fluency in Moscow slang of the Kruschev era) to find out what it <u>really</u> means.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So finally, securely cleared and with file in hand and satisfied with your unimagined misconception about the apellation \"B,\" that same bored guard could lead you to an extremely uncomfortable chair where you\'d sit down, open the file, and finally learn the whole truth...except for one thing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Though the rest of the description matches, and nobody else has noticed through more than 25 years of surveillance of <u>very</u> questionable legality, the picture in the file...well, the eyes and nose are all wrong.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\'s the wrong bear.</p>\r\n'),(3775,'<p>In the future, we\'ll have flying cars and hovering stoplights and drive-through windows on the 39th floor above the CEO\'s office.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the future lampshades will glow whether there\'s a bulb behind them or not.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the future rugs will be self-vacuuming but sometimes they\'ll get the cat and you\'ll have to go down to the crawlspace to let poor Fluffy out, but it\'s okay because</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the future there\'ll be machines to bathe cats and the cats won\'t mind it so much.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the future, the soy content of hamburgers will be pushing 90% but the ham content of soyburgers will be more or less the same.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What else?</p>\r\n'),(3776,'<P>\r\nthe maintenance man replaced light on the side of the house with a 5000 watt klieg light.  it is brighter than any natural light.  when i come home from work, i don\'t need to turn on the lights in the house.  i can see just fine with the blast of white light from the windows.  the light is brighter than the sun.  there are two times of day now, day, and ultra-day.  when people talk about the light at the end of the tunnel when they die just before the paramedics hit them with the defibrillator, that light originates outside my living room window.  it is bright as hell. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nat night, i feel like a police chopper is circling my apartment.  when i close the bedroom door to keep the brutal light of perfection from which no imperfection can hide\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni thought first i would jam a broom stick into it.  I live on the second floor and it is mounted on the awning outside my window.  unscrewing it is not an option for two reasons:  i would fall to my death trying, it is ruffly the temperature of the sun by the time i get home from work.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ntoday, Saturday, just before dusk, i remembered i had to seize my opportunity to deal with this abomination; (oh god, it just turned on but back to the) life sucking light...\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ntoo lazy to risk death while trying to remove it, i aimed it into the sky.  for miles around, men in tuxedoes and top hats, women wearing long strings of pearls and high heels and fur coats, will think they are going to the right place when they knock on my door.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nmisguided souls will descend upon my corner of the fourplex looking for absolution and an eternity of peace and happiness only to find a sink that spawns gnats and some old computers.  \'wait a minute, was i this bad?\'  then they will realize their miscalculation and head upwards.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nmeanwhile outside, having turned the light to its new position, i can see there is already a problem.  there is a clear fire hazard and life is in danger.  the tree in the court hard may very well burst into flames.  it is already turning from a spring green to a sort of early death fall yellow and brown. the leaves are shriveling after only 5 minutes.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni will keep you posted...\r\n</P>\r\n\r\n'),(3777,'<p>She was always frail in my memories, though<br>\r\nI\'ve seen a photograph where she seemed less so<br>\r\nholding her newborn great-grandson, me,<br>\r\nsurrounded by the forgotten hues of the early seventies.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and I remembe when I was nine<br>\r\nshe taught me to play gin rummy<br>\r\nsitting around the table at my grandparents\' house in Gaithersburg<br>\r\nwith my father\'s three orange paintings on the walls.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she let me win that first game, then said<br>\r\n\"now you know how to play, Jesse, I\'m not going to let you win again.\"<br>\r\nso we played a few more hands before dinner<br>\r\nand I think she let me win a few anyway.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>then nearly twenty years later, not so long ago<br>\r\nRose still alive, playing gin rummy in the kosher old folks\' home<br>\r\nso happy to see me, though she<br>\r\nonly recognized me by my size.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she recognized me the same way some time later<br>\r\nher hundredth birthday party, and<br>\r\n(thanks to my sister\'s oversight)<br>\r\nfive generations together to celebrate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>she was so frail then, and so proud<br>\r\nmaitriarch of our family for so many years<br>\r\nI wish I had asked more questions when she could hear them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>My great-grandmother Rose died a few months ago, at the age of 100.</i></p>\r\n'),(3778,'<PRE>\r\ntoo fast, too smoky,                                                            \r\nstrange unwelcome feeling on the dance floor                                    \r\nand I\'ve been there long enough,                                                \r\nhadn\'t planned on staying out late anyhow --                                    \r\nbut then I turn around, and                                                     \r\nthere you are                                                                   \r\nan unexpectedly bright light                                                    \r\nin the dark back corner of the room,                                            \r\nbouncing, happy, beautiful.                                                     \r\nand then you smile at me...                                                     \r\n                                                                                \r\ntales tell of ships sailing,                                                    \r\nof great battles fought for a glimpse                                           \r\nof smiles less brilliant than yours.                                            \r\n                                                                                \r\nI\'d have stayed if you\'d asked,                                                 \r\nmy discomfort forgotten,                                                        \r\nbasking in the glow of your presence                                            \r\n                                                                                \r\ninstead I\'m sitting in my car                                                   \r\nlistening to jazz                                                               \r\npulled over to write to you                                                     \r\nas I don\'t think I ever have,                                                   \r\nwondering when I\'ll see your smile next.\r\n</PRE>'),(3779,'<table border=\"0\" width=\"400\">\r\n	<tr align=\"left\" valign=\"center\">\r\n		<td colspan=\"2\">\r\n		<p><img src=\"%%img6%%\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n\r\n		<p>i walked down queen east. a man appeared in a doorway with blood on his face.  there were a lot of people about but the street was quiet.  i stepped in to a cloud of something that had just happened, thick with silence.  i turned back to photograph the man again.  his eyes were wide but looking elsewhere.<img src=\"%%img4%%\" border=\"1\" align=\"right\"></p>\r\n\r\n		<p>i turned again, to leave the scene.  i waited at the streetcar stop instead.  the cloud condensed. the bleeding man stepped out of the doorway to meet a few hipster kids, one black, one white, neither looking like they belonged in that area of town.</p>\r\n\r\n		<p>they met at the curb and a woman joined them.  i couldn\'t hear what they were saying, but they were talking calmly.</p>\r\n\r\n		</td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n	<tr>\r\n		<td align=\"left\" valign=\"center\">\r\n\r\n			<table border=\"0\">\r\n				<tr align=\"center\" valign=\"center\">\r\n					<td>\r\n						<img src=\"%%img7%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n					</td>\r\n				</tr>\r\n\r\n				<tr align=\"center\" valign=\"center\">\r\n					<td>\r\n						<img src=\"%%img2%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n					</td>\r\n				</tr>\r\n				<tr align=\"center\" valign=\"center\">\r\n					<td>\r\n						<img src=\"%%img3%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n					</td>\r\n				</tr>\r\n				<tr align=\"center\" valign=\"center\">\r\n					<td>\r\n						<img src=\"%%img1%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n					</td>\r\n				</tr>\r\n			</table>\r\n		</td>\r\n		<td align=\"left\" valign=\"center\">\r\n			<p>in a burst, the bleeding man pulled a broken broom handle from his side pocket and raised it to strike the white boy. he and the black boy jumped back and retreated across the street.  the white boy collected himself, then came charging back but not before his friend could grab hold of him.  they wrestled in the middle of the street for a moment, the white boy struggled free and closed the distance.  he had stopped out of the swing range of the broom handle.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>they exchanged threats.  the woman looked on.  another man came to join them.  the woman turned and saw me shooting photos.</p>\r\n		</td>\r\n	</tr>\r\n	<tr>\r\n		<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"left\" valign=\"center\">\r\n\r\n			<p><br>no photos</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>i shot more</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>no photos.  we\'ll smash your camera.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>the bleeding man and the bystander looked at me.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>we\'ll smash your camera.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>no photos.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>i put down the camera.  the boys and the bleeding man slowly backed away from each other.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>the bystander man came to me.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>no photos.  they\'ll smash your camera.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>i told him i\'m a photojournalist.  he said it didn\'t matter.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>the boys walked closer.</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>the white boy said</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>i just lost four hundred dollars.  can\'t i have even one little rock?</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>that\'s what you\'re down here for? said the woman</p>\r\n\r\n			<p>i just want a souvenir</p>\r\n\r\n		<p>the woman urged them to leave now before they get more hurt.  there was blood in the boy\'s nose and on his hands, red dots on the back of his coat.</p>\r\n\r\n		<p>they were young and out of place.  their eyes were cloudy, vague.</p>\r\n\r\n		<p>i told them they should leave, and with that, i left them.</p>\r\n\r\n		<p><img src=\"%%img8%%\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n	</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3780,'then I would be celebrating in three months instead of today!\r\n\r\nhead aches SUCK ! ! !'),(3781,'then I would be celebrating in three months instead of today!\r\n\r\nhead aches SUCK ! ! !'),(3782,'<p>“I’ll call the French,” said my desk-mate as he loomed, waiting for me to relinquish the chair – I’d been working nights, he the days, with the only sign of our passing a few moments of conversation and the lingering smell of stale coffee.  “It sounds like a name the French would use.”  I think he had a thing for the telephone operator at the French consulate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>“Yeah, could be.  Never know with our Turks.”  There’d been strange communications from the Turkish station before, but our task is to interpret only as far as is necessary to pass it up the chain.  Anything more without a direct order…well, that’s how people end up doing field work.  And you may have heard there’s a war on, or at least there will be soon if we’re not careful.  The coffee may be bad here, but it’s better than being shot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It was a short, hurried signal, slipped between radio frequencies in the dead of night like they all are, and as usual some of it made sense and some didn’t.  This time the parts that didn’t make sense seemed (from contextual clues) to be about some foreign operative, codenamed “the bear” or even “the lost bear,” or maybe it was the bear that was lost.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Problem was, the official list didn’t include any foreign operative codenamed “bear.”  It’s too obvious – Russkies, bears – and though we aren’t always successful, we pride ourselves on not being obvious.  And even if there had been an operative by that name, we didn’t have any mission underway to follow anyone in Istanbul at that particular moment.  So I have no earthly idea what they were on about, and said so in my shift report.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So the next night I got to work and found a scrawled note on the blotter: “Bear not French.  May be late, have date with Veronique.”  Oh, just wonderful.  Last time he was late, I had to tell the Director that we’d figured out where Castro’s brand new missiles were pointed.  That’s how people end up doing field work, and you may have heard there’s a war on.  But Castro stayed quiet that night, as did the Turkish station, and I never did find out who the bear was.</p>\r\n'),(3783,'<img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/3783_1.jpg\"><img src=\"http://swinney.org/journals/images/3783_2.jpg\">\r\n<P>this is me.  taken with a polaroid camera i don\'t like.  (karyssa has a 35mm.  nothing digital.  we aren\'t really tech-minded people.  she doesn\'t have a cell phone, either.  can\'t stand them.  says she doesn\'t want to be available to everyone at all hours.  plus the additional cost.  [she can\'t for the life of her see why you need a phone in your home and also a mobile one.  she\'s right - the marketing people have tricked us into thinking we can\'t do without all this stuff.]  this is going to work out pretty good since she\'s thrifty, and i\'m way, way not.  she buys music, but not very much of it.)  couldn\'t get good lighting.  couldn\'t disable the flash.  sunlight pics looked awful.  same with pics taken in darkness with flash.  lights above bathroom mirror only good light source.<BR><BR>and then shooting from above was the only way to keep the flash out of the picture.  i kind of look like a prizefighter and/or gorilla.<BR><BR>and then with the light coming from there, my head is cocked at the only remotely flattering angle.  looking straight ahead left shadows.  i am frustrated at every turn, and yet here it is.<BR><BR>paid the drugstore five bucks to scan it.  used some program that was on my computer when parents gave it to me that\'s called \"imaging\".  not very versatile.  tried to make it the right size for a lj icon but couldn\'t.  probably would have been too small, anyway.<BR><BR>okay, so now you have somewhat of an idea of what i look like.  more pics at a later date.'),(3784,'<p>\r\nWhen one believes one has come to the heart of the matter, the only thing left to do is to rip the motherfucker out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s the only way to be sure.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThat being said, I prefer to dilly-dally \'round the edges of things.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3785,'sunnyvale, cloudy and cool<br>                                                 \r\nnear to where people make bombs<br>                                           \r\nor maybe just the machines which put those bombs<br>\r\nin the place somebody wants to see explode                                      <br>\r\nbut over here, calmly                                                           <br>\r\na programmer from Pakistan plays halting harmonica                            <br>\r\nout of tune from the pings of the crosswalk                                     <br>\r\nas we begin to move across,                                                     <br>\r\neach clad in logos--                                                            <br>\r\ntargus, north face, and of course our own--                                     <br>\r\nwalking quietly towards the buildings that house our cubicles,<br>\r\nwhere we will sit and dream and hack but not often wonder<br>\r\nwhat have we accomplished, really<br>\r\nin the end\r\n'),(3786,'<P>*  i met with tom, my counselor at the case management office, wednesday.  he\'s the certified addictions professional there.  he\'s been in recovery for seventeen years.  (\"in recovery\" means twelve-step involvement.  they don\'t think you ever recover, that it\'s a lifelong process.)  i told him about the deep misgivings i have concerning twelve-step programs and that i was cycling them out of my life and defied him or my family to do anything about it.  i informed him that the program wasn\'t keeping me clean and sober - i was.  i said that judging by the relapses and death i\'d seen, it didn\'t work.<BR><BR>he said, <I>nothing works.  you\'re right - you\'re staying clean because you want to.</I><BR><BR>i told him i\'d been researching rational recovery and liked what i\'d read.  he told me it came from the work of the psychologist albert ellis and they were open to exploring it with me.  he said they were open to anything that i thought would help me and would be willing to do.  i had thought they were doctrinaire about twelve-step and wouldn\'t consider anything else, but he said rational recovery was basically <A HREF=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_Behavioral_Therapy\">cognitive behavorial therapy</A>, and they could do that with me.  which is nice because i haven\'t been getting any individual psychotherapy up to this point.<BR><BR>with other members of the treatment team (doctor, program coordinator, vocational specialist), we also discussed other things i\'d like to pursue.  i\'m volunteering (started at karyssa\'s place friday morning.  they handed me some directions and the keys to a van [THEY TRUSTED ME WITH A VAN!], and i picked up some people and drove them to a lawyer\'s office in tampa, waited in the lobby for an hour and a half, took them to lunch at pizza hut with the forty bucks they gave me [THEY TRUSTED ME WITH MONEY!] [pizza hut was what they all decided on after some discussion.], then took them home.  [there were a woman with a nine year old girl and a five year old boy from belarus and two brothers, twenties, maybe, from eritrea.]), continuing with the yoga and the gym (i\'ve been slacking some lately since jamie hasn\'t been around to motivate me.  i\'m going to start back on a schedule, though probably not as often.  four days a week, i imagine.), and planning on starting at the university of south florida this summer and finishing my degree.  i have a fair amount of credits in computer science, but it really doesn\'t interest me.  i just took that up because i thought i\'d get a good job out of it and make some big money, but there\'s no passion there.  i\'m going to major in english and, if nothing else works out, teach undergraduate-level classes and write books.  that way, i don\'t have to live in poverty until my big break comes.<BR><BR>and i really think the volunteering could turn into at least a part-time job.  i asked for a bump in my - allowance, i guess you\'d call it.  explained that i\'d like to go out with my girlfriend and at least be able to pay for my share of it.  mom goes, <I>you should pay for ALL of it.</I>  mm-hmm.  explained that no, it was a different girlfriend than the one morgan met.  mom goes, <I>why don\'t you just start a harem?</I>  (i\'m going to be working on the issues i have with my parents, too.)  they said they\'d give me twenty-five bucks a week with the proviso that i get some kind of part-time job soon.<BR><BR>*  karyssa and i have been spending a lot of time together.  lately, we just about always spend the night together, here or over there.  woke up friday morning at six-thirty and hung out and stuff then ate breakfast, and she went home.  i turned on the computer, and the internet wasn\'t working.  turned on the tv, and the cable wasn\'t working.  cable company said somebody could come to fix it saturday, but i\'d have to be home from eight to eight.<BR><BR>went to the library to use the internet.  checked out some videos.  hung out with k. at mine that night.  did not go to a meeting.<BR><BR>(haven\'t been to a meeting since monday night - gamblers anonymous.  that was my last.  told my sponsor tuesday afternoon, and he came by that night, and we talked, then he left.  i thought i had made myself clear, but he came back wednesday afternoon.  i met him at the door and didn\'t ask him in.  asked him to please leave and not come back, to not call me.  he looked really disgusted.<BR><BR>he said, <I>you\'re gonna lose all this.  everything you worked for.  i\'m gonna end up readin\' about you in the paper one mornin\'.</I><BR><BR>i told him it was a fallacy to suggest that just because somebody left a twelve-step program, they were going to go out and use.  he didn\'t say anything, stomped off to his car, took off.<BR><BR>i told my friend brian i\'d keep in touch with him, call him once a month to tell him how i am.  asked him to tell everybody else.  and to ask them to leave me alone.)<BR><BR>we made dinner (cauliflower and mushroom gougere and zucchini fried with onions and garlic and oregano then simmered in vegetable stock until the liquid evaporates.  her recipes.  i was just the chop boy.  i\'ll get them from her and post them.)<BR><BR>we watched <I><B>hud</B></I>.  we\'d never seen it.  really good.  larry mcmurtry\'s choice.  this one and <B><I>the last picture show</B></I>.<BR><BR>went to bed around ten.  i must be getting old.  i don\'t see the appeal of staying up all night anymore.  (well, it\'s becoming rarer, anyway.)  it was cold enough in the apartment to merit sleeping in the living room on the sofa bed.  (the only heat source i have is a baseboard heater under the front window.)  the sofa bed sleeps alright.  the situation works out as long as you stay in bed.  the bathroom can get a little frosty.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>woke up around seven.  had to be up and stay home until the cable guy came.  i happened to glance out the window and saw that there was a car parked in the middle of the yard between the palm trees.  using my considerable powers of deductive reasoning, i figured out that it must be connected to my neighbor somehow.  i couldn\'t see why it was parked there when there was room around back of the duplex where everything else is.  their carport is back there, and the door that leads from it into the kitchen.  they never use the door on my side.  the landlord has all kinds of shit back there in the yard.  there\'s a pop-up camper, a boat, an out-of-commission car, but there\'s still plenty of room for another car.<BR><BR>i\'ve never spoken to this neighbor.  the only time he\'s said anything to me is when i was coming back from the grocery store and decided to cut through the back yard for some reason.  he was standing at his sink, where there\'s a window, and when he saw me, he started yelling in czech or slovak or whatever it is he speaks.  i don\'t know who he thought i was.  i kept going, went around the side of the house, and, as i was turning the corner by my carport, he popped out of his front door and eyed me silently.  i just looked at him and unlocked the door, and he muttered something and went back inside.<BR><BR>i got dressed and went around back and knocked on his door.  the car belonged to his friend.  asked him to move it around back, said it didn\'t look good in the middle of the yard.  told him i appreciated it.  he said okay and nodded, and that was it.  (a few months ago, the landlord was taking liberties and parked the shit car that\'s around back now in my driveway.  i told him i didn\'t want it there.  he had it parked all the way at the end of the driveway.  he said, <I>well, you don\'t have a car.</I>  and i said, <I>but people i know do, and i don\'t want them having to park on the street.</I>)<BR><BR>so he moves it, and we have breakfast, and she says she has stuff to do and goes home.  i can\'t go anywhere, have to wait.  i read and do some yoga and try to write a little.  around eleven, i\'m in the recliner, reading, and i see the cable guy pull up on the street in front of the carport.  he sits in the truck for a minute then gets out, and he\'s barely out when the neighbor, george, crosses my field of vision from the left and intercepts him.  there\'s a brief exchange, and george goes back inside, and the cable guy goes around the side of the house.  ten minutes later he\'s at the door.  says he can\'t fix it yet.  i\'m going to have to call the power company and tell them that the neutral needs to be replaced, that it\'s using the cable for a neutral and is melting the cable.  i ask him what the neighbor wanted.  says he told him his cable was out and can he fix it?  asked if he had called the company about it.  no, he hadn\'t.  i don\'t know if his ended up getting fixed or not.  cable guy left.  said he would call around four or five to see if the power company had come out.<BR><BR>so, i call the progress energy.  they get there an hour later.  two trucks.  neighbor again heads them off at the pass.  his lights are flickering.  did he call about it?  no.  takes them about ten minutes to fix it.  have lunch, go to the library, use the internet and check out as many videos as i can.  back home.  the cable guy calls, arrives.  george is out there again before the guy can get out of the truck.  i hope that after all that, the poor bastard got his fixed, too.  so it\'s fixed; all is sweetness and light.<BR><BR>i hose off, she picks me up, we go to the house of one of her coworkers.  it\'s his birthday.  we stayed for half an hour.  i don\'t even know what she got him - we left before he opened his gifts.  i don\'t know what excuse she gave them.  there was absolutely nowhere else we had to be.<BR><BR>there was an open bar.  i ordered a virgin mary and felt like a total pussy.  really, i have no business ordering non-alcoholic isotopes of real drinks. i should stick to juice or coke or water. those look like there could be alcohol in them, anyway. nobody has to know.<BR><BR>the workforce at her place is pretty evenly split between ex-hippie types and jesus freaks.  so it makes for lively interpersonal relations.  i\'m still learning who\'s who.<BR><BR>she managed to get pretty gassed in half an hour, so i drove us back to mine.  she played the stereo loud on the way back, dancing around with her upper body and laughing.  we get inside and i take a leak, check the voicemail, check the computer.  she comes up behind me and starts rubbing on me.<BR><BR>i said, <I>listen:  let me just go ahead and nip this in the bud.  i make love in the morning.  i make love at noon.  and i make love at night.  i do NOT make love at five-thirty in the afternoon.  five-thirty is teatime.  some standards <U>must</U> be upheld.</I><BR><BR>she cracks up.  i just look at her, all serious.  after a little bit, she stops laughing and just looks at me, not sure if i\'m joking or not.  i start to grin at the corners of my mouth then bust up.  she starts laughing again.  we hug.  i pick her up and spin around.  we\'re still laughing.  she says, <I>stop.  stop.</I>  i plop her down on the couch.  she lays down.  i go over to the radio and turn it on and try to dial in the oldies station.  <I><B>the saturday night dance party</I></B> starts at six.  mostly disco.  it\'s good for doing the do to.  i think i find it, but a commercial\'s on.  i stand there and wait until music comes back on to see if i have the right station.<BR><BR>a commercial i\'ve heard before starts off with <I>two brothers fighting crime.  one\'s an fbi agent...</I>  i turn off the volume really quickly and say, in my best announcer voice, <I>and the other one\'s a gigolo.</I><BR><BR><I>shut up.</I><BR><BR><I><U>i\'d</U> watch that.  wouldn\'t you?<BR><BR>you don\'t have any sense.  you know that, don\'t you?<BR><BR>oh, i\'m well aware.</I><BR><BR>it\'s warm in the living room with the heater on full blast, but the sofa bed wasn\'t made for sex, i don\'t think.  we put all the blankets on the main bed and get under them.  it wasn\'t too bad.  i fall asleep, unusual for me, but a good sign, i think.  she wakes me up after half an hour, and we go make supper.  just vegetable soup and cornbread and tea.<BR><BR>we clean up and make chocolate milkshakes (i got a blender and juicer for christmas.  or, rather, i got some money allocated to me to spend on myself while supervised by my sister, and i got a juicer and blender and some dvds and cds and ordered some books:  <B><I>the code of the woosters</B></I>  [for work.  it\'s pretty witty and silly.  i\'m a third of the way into it.  he appears to be setting things up for some kind of piefight-level event in the end.] and <B><I>the power and the glory, the heart of the matter, the end of the affair, the quiet american, a burnt-out case,</B></I> and <B><I>the comedians.</B></I>  [i\'ve never read any graham greene.  i\'m going to read them all {and nothing else} in that order while writing a novel about prostitutes.  hopefully i can finish doing both at the same time.]) and put in <B><I>five easy pieces</B></I>.  i\'ve seen it; she hadn\'t.<BR><BR>after it ends, she says, <I>would you ever do that to me?</I><BR><BR>i say, <I>you know, now that i think about it, why did he ever hook up with her in the first place?<BR><BR>are you evading my question, mack?</I><BR><BR>she started calling me that all on her own one day.  there was another girl a long time ago who used to call me that.  i don\'t know what became of her, but this girl here could call me that for the rest of my life, and i wouldn\'t mind.<BR><BR>*     *     *<BR><BR>we woke up at hers yesterday morning around nine and did some yoga.  i\'ve never done yoga with another person outside of a class.  it was quite nice.  she\'s taken a lot of classes and knows substantially more about it than i do.  after the asanas, we laid on the living room floor in the corpse postition for fifteen minutes then did some pranayama and chanting.  breakfast, read the paper.  she got ready, then we went to mine, and she waited, read some of my stuff on the computer while i hosed off.  she\'s the first person outside of you guys to read most of it.  (other than when my dad found some legal pads in my room and read part of <B><I>the infidels</B></I> and pronounced it \"filth\".)  i didn\'t care what she saw.  there are pics of nude men and women on my computer, and it didn\'t bother me that she might find them.  for me, that\'s saying volumes.<BR><BR>we drove to tarpon springs, had a big, leisurely lunch, then went to dunedin and walked around, went in all the galleries and shops for several hours.  it must be serious, because it didn\'t bother me in the least that i was missing the nfc championship.  i wanted to be with her a lot more.  also says volumes.<BR><BR>we got back to mine at the top of the fourth quarter, though.  she went home.  my family was going to pick me up at six-thirty for dinner, just as the afc championship was starting.<BR><BR>it was mom, dad, grandma, and my niece (age 8).  my sister was studying.  she\'s in her last semester of nursing school.  dad let me drive.  a shocker.<BR><BR>went to appleby\'s.  dad and i sat on the outsides of the booth benches and, occaisonally, one of us would ease out and go over to the tv over the bar and check the score.  mom put a stop to it when the food arrived.<BR><BR>paige was sitting in between grandma and i.  they had given her a little activity book and a couple of crayons when we came in.  the book was centered around the rudolph animated christmas special produced by rankin/bass.  they needed to get a new book.  i helped her with a word search, looking for \"snow\" and \"nose\" and \"winter\".  none of the words were backwards or diagonal.  she didn\'t need my help.  she just wanted me to spend time with her doing something.  she would circle a word in red crayon and then do the next with blue, alternating.<BR><BR>it was the first time i\'d seen them since christmas.  (all of them except paige.  i\'d seen her and my sister a couple of times.)  i hadn\'t missed them much.<BR><BR>they took me home.  i arrived at the bottom of the third quarter.  after it was over, i called karyssa, she came over.  i put the videos i was done with in their plastic bags.  (they keep them in bags with handles that snap shut at the top to keep the videos and cds from getting separated from their boxes.)<BR><BR>we stepped outside.  the titty chill factor was quite high.  it was supposed to freeze.  we drove into the parking lot behind city hall, and i hopped out to go put the videos in the drop box.  when i got around on the back side of the jeep, the bench over in the alcove by the library came into my field of vision.  bill was parked on it, backpack beside him.  he saw me.  i walked over there.<BR><BR>he said, <I>whassup, white man?</I>  (he\'s white, too.  i don\'t know why he calls me that.)<BR><BR><I>why aren\'t you at the army?<BR><BR>wouldn\'t let me in.  drunk.<BR><BR>well, what are you gonna do?<BR><BR>i dunno.  stay here.  go out to the woods.<BR><BR>if you stay here, they\'re gonna come along and arrest you eventually.  do you want to go to jail?<BR><BR>go out to the woods, then.</I><BR><BR>silence.  silence.  we look at each other.<BR><BR><I>hold on a second.</I><BR><BR>i walk around to karyssa\'s side of the jeep.  she opens the door.<BR><BR><I>what?</I><BR><BR>i put my face close to hers, look in her eyes, explain, and ask her something.<BR><BR><I>absolutely not.<BR><BR>well, <U>i\'m</U> going to, then.  i\'ll call you at work tomorrow, okay?<BR><BR>alright, if that\'s what you want to do.</I><BR><BR>we hug, kiss, she departs.  i go back to bill.<BR><BR><I>come on.</I><BR><BR>he doesn\'t say anything, looks at me for about five seconds, stands up, gets his bag, and we walk north through the parking lot.  we go around the firehouse and turn north on madison.<BR><BR><I>i want you to understand that this isn\'t gonna be a regular thing.<BR><BR>oh, no.  no.</I><BR><BR>we arrive and go inside.<BR><BR><I>hey.  this is alright.<BR><BR>thanks.  you want somethin\' to eat?  you like grilled cheese?<BR><BR>yeah, that sounds good.</I><BR><BR>i sit him down in the recliner and show him how to work the remote control.  make three sandwiches and some sweet tea and peel and section a tangerine from the tree in back.  put it all on a tv tray and take it around to him.  he had the country music channel on.  he ate, and we made small talk.  when he finished, i put everything back in the kitchen.  he came in there with me and sat on the barstool and flipped through a photo album i\'d left on the bar.  i cleaned up the dishes.  he asked me questions about the pictures.  he noticed the calendar on the wall by him.  it\'s in spanish.<BR><BR><I>where\'d you get this?<BR><BR>latin market out on the highway.  you know where i\'m talkin\' about?<BR><BR>yeah, right there by davis hardware.<BR><BR>yeah.  check it out.  every goddam day belongs to one saint or another.  some of \'em are doubled up.  when\'s your birthday?<BR><BR>august seventeenth.<BR><BR>look and see what your saint is.  i got <U>three</U> of \'em on mine.  don\'t ask me who they are, though.<BR><BR>san jacinto.  thass fucked up.<BR><BR>why izzat?<BR><BR>san jacinto county is right there where i was the guest of the state of texas.  stayin\' with miss estelle.<BR><BR>who?<BR><BR>estelle unit.  thass the name of the prison.  huntsville, texas.  which is also the place where they take \'em outta this world.  different prison there.</I><BR><BR>i knew he\'d been in prison in texas.  i had no idea where, though.  he\'s from laredo.  i spent some time there.  that\'s where i used to cross the border.  quite a bit, anyway.  we figured out that we used to run in some of the same circles, drink in the same bars, had one dealer in common.  but, then again, about half the people in town knew that dealer, it seemed like.  laredo\'s kind of a fast town.  well, nuevo laredo is, really, but it\'s all kind of the same thing.<BR><BR>i finish up.<BR><BR><I>hey:  do you wanna take a shower?<BR><BR>yeah.  that\'d be nice.<BR><BR>well, lemme get the shower goin\' first so it\'ll warm up the bathroom.</I><BR><BR>i get some boxers, sweatpants, an undershirt, socks.  get out a towel.  he goes in there.<BR><BR><I>listen:  don\'t go into the medicine cabinet.<BR><BR>i\'m not into pills.<BR><BR>good, \'cause there\'s nothin\' in there with any kick, anyway.</I><BR><BR>he comes out feeling better.  we decide to watch a movie.  we look around the cable universe, and he doesn\'t see anything that interests him.  the only videos i have are <B><I>long day\'s journey into night</I></B> and the second season of <B><I>curb your enthusiasm</B></I>.  i\'ve never seen <B><I>l.d.j.i.n.</B></I>, but i kind of know what it\'s about, and i wouldn\'t have appreciated somebody laying that on me, so that leaves the other.  i\'ve seen the show, but none of the second season.  i assure him that it\'s funny, explain the conceit, make some chocolate shakes, pop in the first dvd.  foolishness is good sometimes.  he laughed a lot.<BR><BR>we watch five episodes, two and a half hours worth, i guess.  i shut the tv off, he helps me move the coffee table over against the door and pull out the sofa bed.  i go in the bedroom and undress, put on this black t-shirt i sleep in.  it used to be david\'s.  it\'s big and comes down to around my knees.  if i wear it out, i tuck it in.  most of the time, though, i don\'t wear anything under it and think of it as a dress, i guess.  swan around the house in it.<BR><BR>i keep my underwear on and grab the pillows out of the closet and head back in there.  he\'s already in bed.  i give him a pillow and lay the other down on the other spot.  i go close the bedroom and bathroom doors and open the blinds a little so there will be some light and turn off the lamps and pull back my side of the covers.<BR><BR><I>you\'re sleepin\' in here, too?<BR><BR>yeah, jack.  there isn\'t any heat in the bedroom.<BR><BR>yeah, okay, sure.  (pause)  hey.  you\'re wearin\' somethin\' under that shirt, aren\'tcha?<BR><BR>maybe.  maybe not.</I><BR><BR>there\'s a little orange light coming through the blinds.  i take a finger and run it down the top of the blanket halfway between us.<BR><BR><I>you see this line?  this is the maginot line right here.  you\'re gonna lose anything that comes across it.<BR><BR>well, what if i go around it?</I><BR><BR>we laugh with abandon.  two crazy guys who don\'t have to get up for work in the morning.<BR><BR>he says, <I>i don\'t know what you\'re worried about, anyway.  you couldn\'t get hit on in the greek navy.<BR><BR>sheeit.  i KNOW <U>i\'m</U> fine.<BR><BR>i think we could take a vote on that.<BR><BR>we who?  you got some backup?<BR><BR>go to sleep.<BR><BR>okay.  goodnight.</I></P>'),(3787,'<h3>\r\nSleep brings dreams to life\r\n</h3>\r\n<h3>\r\nEven though i sleep alone\r\n</h3>\r\n<h3>\r\nAsleep i\'m happy\r\n</h3>'),(3788,'<p>\r\n<b>A Fool\'s Progress</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[1]</b> The flush face in the mirror belonged to Jay Gatz.  I bloomed a bad case of that quintessentially American disease of the mind that sends us marching off to prove ourselves to people who don\'t really care.  Here I am in a nation of easy marks, lapping up the carnies\' spiel about that great prize behind the glass: ours for the taking, if only we\'ll play the game.  On the tv screen a throng of citizens in a cage, in Freedom Plaza, violently protest the peaceful transition of power from George Bush, to George Bush.  And it seems to be confirmation that the real crisis in our post-modern world involves semantics.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[2]</b> Like a good little donkey I\'ve pulled my cart for 37 years.  But I don\'t seem to have gotten any closer to that damned carrot - despite the exhortations to keep trying, and to pull just a bit harder.  Well, the cart is stopping.  And if you\'ve won life\'s lottery, and you\'ve been riding in the back, I\'m through listening.  What I see up ahead, in the distance, begins to look like Gatsby\'s green light: year-by-year receding before me.  But for Zelda\'s money what would your life have been, Fitzgerald?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Douche Bags</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[3]</b> There\'s a big story here, and no one is telling it.  Yes, the fix is in.  And without being driven to despair, it\'s helpful to develop an awareness of the factors underlying the transformation of our nation.  Economic levers can be used to move political mountains.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[4]</b> California aside, in the election of \'04 the \"Big Money\" states that John Kerry won were clustered around the Great Lakes, i.e., the old manufacturing region known as the Rust Belt: NY (31), PA (21), IL (21), MI (17).  Those four states provided a total of 90 electoral votes.  But in the election of \'80 things looked like this: NY (41), PA (27), IL (26), MI (21).  And those four states provided a total of 115 electoral votes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[5]</b> What happened?  Chiefly, there was a demographic shift in America, because there was a flight of Capital from America.  The position of Labor - the most important element in the Left\'s coalition - weakened.  As goes Labor, so goes the Left.  The Democratic Party depended upon intelligent working men and women who voted their interest.  And as they have lost, who has gained?  In the election of \'80 things looked like this: TX (26), FL (17).  And in the election of \'04 things looked like this: TX (34), FL (27).  This is a game of numbers, nothing more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[6]</b> To blame anyone out of power for the results of the \'04 election is to ignore the careful work on the part of the Bush family.  How, and why, did they manage to move from the rather insignificant state of Connecticut to control the highest offices and Republican Party machinery in precisely those places - TX and FL - that would be most necessary for a successful Presidential bid?  Maybe, a sudden gust of wind blew them thither - the same sort of gust that carried Hillary Clinton to NY.  California, you\'re slipping - and don\'t say, \"I\'ll be back.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>The Eye</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[7]</b> I should be afraid to write such things, no?  As federal law enforcement builds a database of fingerprints, the Census Bureau provides information about Arab-Americans to Homeland Security, my state collects digital portraits when it licenses drivers, my toll highway authority forces patrons to carry transponders in their vehicles, various marketing firms and credit bureaus track my purchases, and my e-mail, cell use, and library habits are examined, I am free.  And, per his inaugural address, my President has promised to give this freedom to the world.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[8]</b> In 1942 President-to-be Eisenhower\'s brother supervised the relocation of Japanese-Americans to detention camps.  Their property was sold.  Many were incarcerated for a period of nearly three years - never having done anything wrong.  The action amounted to the issuance of a Bill of Attainder - expressly forbidden by the Constitution - but the Supreme Court upheld its legality.  See paragraph [9] below.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>We Hold These Truths To Be Self-Evident, That All Men Are Created Equal</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[9]</b> We are free: free to compete.  What do (1) genetic inheritance, (2) the environment into which one is born, (3) malicious acts, and (4) random events have to do with: gender, race, class, intellectual potential, beauty, athletic ability, physical health, and mental health?  And what do any of those things have to do with your ability to succeed in this life?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[10]</b> In 1865 racially based slavery was ended in the United States.  And African-Americans were free to compete.  See paragraph [9] above.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[11]</b> Yes: as \"Paul the consumer\" I desire [some of] the low-priced, high quality goods that competition is supposed to provide.  But, as \"Paul the working man\" I find that I am unable to sell my labor as cheaply as most Indian, Mexican or Chinese nationals.  And if I\'m not working, I can\'t afford a God damned thing.  See paragraph [9] above.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[12]</b> Absent manufacturing, the solution is to find work in the service economy.  Roughly 80% of the jobs in America, now, are in \"service.\"  Observation: the people served will tend to be those favored by the circumstance of their birth.  \"May I cook for you?  Bring you your food?  Massage your feet?  Drive you about town?  And, please, whatever your desire, feel free to act like dirt - because you are superior, as evidenced by your surfeit of possessions.\"  But we won\'t call this slavery.  And if you do this work, it is wrong to feel humiliation; it is wrong to feel resentment.  See paragraph [9] above. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[13]</b> I apologize for my lack of ambition.  Let us all become doctors and investment bankers.  See paragraph [9] above.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Leo Strauss And The Philosophers Of The Future</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[14]</b> Semantics: that branch of semiotic dealing with the relationship between signs and symbols and the things to which they refer.  The words [signs] that I type are inherently meaningless associations of equally meaningless letters to which you gentle reader [hopefully] attribute some meaning.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[15]</b> Voltaire is said to have discouraged one of his companions from discussing atheism while the servants were present: \"Only their belief in God stops them from cutting our throats while we sleep.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[16]</b> There is not truth; but there are truths.  There is not morality; but there are moralities.  What a fellow like Voltaire believes to be true is at odds with what he finds useful for his servants to believe to be true.  Voltaire is not alone, of course.  The idea of master morality and slave morality is well developed in Friedrich Nietzsche\'s writing.  Nietzsche will go so far as to deny that any such thing as \"morality\" exists in the \"real\" sense (see Twilight of the Idols \"The Improvers of Mankind\" among others.)  After Nietzsche will come the philosopher of the Third Reich: Martin Heidegger; and after Heidegger will come Leo Strauss.  Strauss will obtain a professorship at the University of Chicago, where he will maintain the necessity of hiding the \"truth\" while simultaneously telling \"noble lies\" to the citizens of democratic regimes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[17]</b> It is striking that there are \"Straussians\" within the President\'s cabinet.  Especially when one notes the President\'s frequent invocation of religion: while employing its symbols, there is a discord between his actions and the teachings of the faiths.  Show the flag; hold your hands in prayer; speak of peace and freedom; play upon the emotions invested by your citizens in these things.  \"It is enough that they should know your purpose after you have accomplished it, \" said the Italian.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[18]</b> When you have manipulated the economy, positioned yourself for maximum political advantage, collected reams of personal information about me, demonstrated a penchant for denying entire groups of people liberty and property, and cultivated an attachment by my fellow citizens to ideas that you believe to be false and symbols you believe to be meaningless, I must wonder what sort of war you are waging, and what sort of freedom you are bringing.     \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Fin</b>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>[19]</b> My name is Paul Edward Germanos, and I wrote this.\r\n</p>'),(3808,'<p>so as i write this i\'ve finally come home from an evening in the\r\ndrunk tank.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for as much as this was a horrible experience, for which i\'m sure i\'ll\r\npay out the ass in insurance for the next million years, probably lose\r\nmy license for a while, and a bunch of other things, i have to admit\r\nthat it was a bizarre and entertaining experience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i met a guy named will, 18, who apparently was arrested for being black\r\non a friday. mr. walker, a 60-something man from peoria, il with a\r\nbarely intelligible creole accent. a man who\'s name I forget from salt\r\nlake city, who turned himself in because of some outstanding warrants.\r\nhe came prepared, with a snickers bar and some carmels. jay, a latino,\r\nfellow dui. chris, a white guy in his 30s, blew an .07 but they locked\r\nhim up anyway.  an older white gentleman in his 50s, very well dressed,\r\narrested two blocks from his house for dui because he forgot to turn his\r\nlights on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the whole scene reminded me very much of highschool. langorous and\r\nbureaucratic, vaguely hostile, painted cement walls. we talked and got\r\ncomfortable with each other as people in the same stupid predicament\r\noften do. at one point we had an impromptu spelling bee officiated by\r\nmister walker.  it\'s very hard to spell words when you don\'t understand\r\nwhat the person\'s saying.  i spelled mississippi backwards.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there was a man a few cells down who, for every moment that he was\r\nawake, was threatening, at the top of his lungs, to \"choke the first\r\nbitch that opens this door.\" i was impressed by his stamina. he kept\r\nthis up for at least five hours. it was more humorous than anything\r\nelse. the oakland pd were nice folks, much nicer than the chp.  not\r\nspeed but polite and easygoing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>as time went on people were released, sometimes in batches, sometimes\r\none at a time. i wondered as they walked out why they didn\'t say\r\nanything to the people they were leaving behind, no goodbyes, no good\r\nlucks. when i left, around 11am the next morning, mister walker, as yet\r\nuncharged as far as i could tell, and jay the latino were still there. i\r\nwas getting antsy, and asked a passing guard for an update on my\r\nprocessing. he asked my name and birthday and unlocked the cell.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it didn\'t occur to me to say goodbye to jay and mr. walker.</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3789,'<p>\r\n<i>If/My parents are crying/Then I\'ll dig a tunnel/from my window to yours.</i></p>\r\n<p>\r\n--The Arcade Fire, <i>Neighborhood #1 (Tunnels)</i></p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe everyday random shit of my life seems profound, and I feel the urge to share it. I guess that the world is filled with half-baked art projects that began that way. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have become less critical. There is the motivating force of life, the cycle of joy and regret, and I am swept along like everyone else. I have petty victories and defeats. At the end of each year I come out ahead.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCoffee every morning.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFive years ago I lived on David\'s kitchen floor, in a one-room studio. We played video games, smoked pot, and ate junk food. It was good. The sun shone every day almost, and it was pleasantly warm from the first of March until about the middle of July. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere wasn\'t a war in sight. </p><p>\r\nNow, i have all kinds of shit. My days are filled. There are people everywhere, everywhere. I have an HDTV. I\'m restoring a boat. I go to school on my scooter and then to work. I read and read and read.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone at work is nice, and well-dressed. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSchool engages me. Criminal Procedure (aka, search-and-seizure), torts (latin for twisted), income tax. I no longer fall in love. I am breaking up with a girl because she bought a dog. </P><p>\r\nThe dog sleeps in the girl\'s bed!</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat would you do?   \r\n '),(3790,'The everyday orbits of my life seem profound, and I feel the urge to share them. In this, I am not alone, but--</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFair warning. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have become less critical. There is the motivating force of life, the cycle of joy and regret, and I am swept along like everyone else. I have petty victories and defeats. At the end of each year I come out ahead.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCoffee every morning.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFive years ago I lived on David\'s kitchen floor, in a one-room studio. We played video games, smoked pot, and ate junk food. It was good. The sun shone every day almost, and it was pleasantly warm from the first of March until about the middle of July. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nNot a war in sight. </p><p>\r\nNow, i have all kinds of shit. My days are filled. There are people everywhere, everywhere. I\'m restoring a boat. I go to school on my scooter and then to work. The sun shines, and I read and read and read.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone at work is nice, and well-dressed. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSchool engages me. Criminal Procedure (aka, search-and-seizure), torts (latin for twisted), income tax. I no longer fall in love. I am breaking up with a girl because she bought a dog. </P><p>\r\nThe dog sleeps in the girl\'s bed!</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhat would you do?   \r\n '),(3791,'<p>I am slowly filling in<br>\r\nthe missing pieces of my life,<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>All the things I thought were lacking<br>\r\nsomehow appear without strife,<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ve got the apartment I can mostly afford,<br>\r\na new DVD player for when I get bored,<br>\r\nand my friends can be found in a great loving horde--<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so often it feels<br>\r\nlike everything\'s going great,<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I write self-indulgent poems<br>\r\nand stay up way too late,<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>perhaps through repetition<br>\r\nof the question I can\'t clear<br>\r\nI could settle on an answer<br>\r\nbut that still won\'t bring you near,<br>\r\nor perhaps you sit there reading<br>\r\nand the screen becomes a smear<br>\r\nas the words just keep on flowing<br>\r\nto a song I cannot hear,\r\ncalled \"where are you?\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'ve got a new job where I can finally excel,<br>\r\nmy sofa, my poof chair, my hybrid as well,<br>\r\nforty-five miles per gallon, that really feels swell--<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can clearly see resolving,<br>\r\nother questions that I ask--<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>if I really set down to improving<br>\r\nI could write a list of tasks,<br>\r\nbut where are you?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>perhaps through repetition<br>\r\nof these questions I can\'t clear<br>\r\nI could settle on an answer<br>\r\nbut that still won\'t bring you near,<br>\r\nor perhaps you sit there reading<br>\r\nand the screen becomes a smear<br>\r\nas the words just keep on flowing<br>\r\nto a song I cannot hear,<br>\r\nand still I sit here slowly wondering<br>\r\nhow much is fate, and how much fear...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and, where are you?</p>\r\n'),(3792,'<p>\r\ntalking dirty is hot.<br>\r\nsending dirty messages back and forth during a slow day at work.<br>\r\ngoing to the bathroom, beating off because you can\'t think.<br>\r\ncalling your lover and listening to the orgasm, hot.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnaked girls.<br>\r\nthe punk rock bartender in her old bad religion t-shirt.<br>\r\nso thin it\'s sheer, tighty whiteys, hot.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbreaking away from the makeout session.<br>\r\nputting her hand down her skirt and masturbating.<br>\r\nnot letting you touch, this is hot.\r\n</p>'),(3793,'<PRE>\r\nafter 3 hours of gasoline covered carb work,\r\nmy BMW motorcycle ROARED TO LIFE!\r\n\r\nBLASTING through sleepy san diego,\r\na cop in his prowler spilled his coffee on his crotch.\r\n\r\nmothers strolling their babes rushed their hands to their ears,\r\nbabies new to this world fell to fear and burst into tears.\r\n\r\nyoung girls wet their panties,\r\nqueers grabbed their dicks.\r\n\r\nnazi fighter pilots did barrel rolls in their graves,\r\nelderly jewish men held their grandkids tight.\r\n\r\nand i with my maternal german blood did yell,\r\n\r\n        \"YEAH-HEAH-HEAH !\"\r\n\r\nBarron Von Swinburn\r\n</PRE>'),(3794,''),(3795,'<p>there\'s an old proverb that says<i>\"when you waste the last of your photos on a crying teenage couple, a wagon full of puppies will arrive\".</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>at bloor and avenue road, a teenage couple was crying, the girl was disconsolate, the boy offered restrained appeals. i shot a lot of frames, leaving few left on my card.  i walked back to the corner.  a man with a miniature wagon full of puppies rolled up.  i asked him to photograph his puppies. he told me he could do better than that, and had me help him carry the wagon up the stairs of the church on the corner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>we let down the wagon on a raised lawn in front of the church. the man opened the door and pulled out zippered bags of puppies.  he set the bags down and a dozen puppies flowed forth, the sun shone a hard winter light on their fur. instantly pedestrians vaulted out of their lines of routine to smile and make for the puppies.  the puppies greeted them at the edge of the raised lawn, happy and licking, running and barking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>people who\'d been strangers a second before now spoke to each other and smiled. a woman told me she was late, but couldn\'t stop hugging a shivering black puppy and talking to it sweetly.  the man who brought the puppies set about photographing them and had to step around a crowd that kept growing.  the cold was forgotten for smiles, some people just held the puppies tight and the puppies nuzzled back, their eyes both closed and lost for the time it took the crosswalk lights to change.</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img1%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img2%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img3%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img4%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img5%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img6%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\"%%img7%%\" border=\"1\">\r\n<br><br>'),(3796,'<P>\r\nsometimes i need to forget about my problems.  to do this, i create a\r\nproblem that drowns all others.  i take a hot bath.  the article\r\nbefore you details my method for a hot bath.  you are encouraged to\r\nfind your own.\r\n</P><P>\r\nfirst, i find something that needs to be off my mind.  sometimes it is\r\nbeing drunk and alone.  drunk and alone is not always something i want\r\nto forget about.  tonight it is though.  i felt those early pangs of\r\nloneliness.  i considered watching a movie.  i dont want to though.  i\r\ndo not want to be entertained.  i want life.  so i decide to take a\r\nhot bath.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\npersonally, i like a clean bathtub when i take a bath.  i clean my\r\nbathtub more often than any other area of my environment.  usually\r\nonce a week, i want to take a bath so i clean it and then start the\r\nritual.  i just said ritual.  yes, i am a man.  yes, i have a\r\n\'ritual\'.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nfirst off, done cleaning the bath, turn the hot water on.  as a\r\nprerequisite, the water heater needs to be redlining.  the water\r\nheater has to be turned up such that when you are doing dishes, you\r\nexclaim at least once every time you do them, \"OW FUCK!\"  you burnt\r\nyourself, because that is what this ritual is all about.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthe water is running in the tub now, hot.  so hot you wouldn\'t dare to\r\nput a foot in it.  while the tub is filling in this way, toss some\r\nbath salt in there.  i use epsom salt because it is good for my\r\ncorns.  you might use some lilac.  i have to have something in there.\r\ni\'m not going swimming for christ sake.  i\'m not taking a shower.  i\r\nneed some aroma mixed in with the steam.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nhere in san diego, we have these wall heaters to make up for the fact\r\nthat the land lord won\'t repair the central heat.  why should they,\r\nthey say this to themselves, it never gets cold here.  i couldn\'t\r\nagree more.  i am happy with the lone wall mounted heater in the\r\nbathroom.  it is good for heating your knees while you are doing #2\r\nin the winter.  it is also good for raising the temperature of the\r\nbathroom when i am going to take my ritual hot bath.  i turn that\r\nsucker on now.  get it going early.  keep the door closed while you do\r\nthis.  aroma steam and wall heater heat mix for a nice broth to burn\r\nyour insides while you cook in the bath.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nstrip.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni like to brush my teeth while the system red lines.  that way when i\r\nget out of the bath later, i am essentially ready to die in bed.  i\'m\r\nnekid.  i\'m clean.  my teeth are brushed.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nthe bath should be almost full enough to cover me.  this is just enough water to\r\nbarely cover my stomach.  i don\'t need to be actually submersed.  that\r\nis a waste of water on top of what is already a huge waste of water.\r\nbesides, i think if i were actually able to competely submerse myself,\r\ni would have a heat stroke and die.  so here i am, my bath is nearly\r\nthe requisite level.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nit is time to start adding cold water.  the bath is far too hot.  add\r\ncold water with a bowl.  fill the bowl and  pour it around the tub.\r\ndo this several times and check the temperature with your foot.  it\r\nshould hurt like hell, but be on the edge of bearable.  the extreme\r\nouter frontier of bearable.  stop when you are stairing into the abyss\r\nof pain and feel you can make it back.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nwhen you can stand with one foot in the tub, try to feel the\r\ndifference.  concentrate on it.  one foot is burning.  one is cool.\r\nnow burn the other foot, put it in the tub.  here i cannot sit down.\r\ni would burn my nads off.  it will take some adjusting to get to the\r\npoint i can put my butt down.  as mentioned before, the water is not\r\nvery deep.  so with my butt on the tub floor, the water still won\'t\r\nburn my nads off if i hold them up.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ncupping my nads with both hands, i sit down.  i\'ll move quickly\r\ntowards laying my back on the tub floor, and then towards submerging\r\nmy legs.  i like to get them in the hot water as quicly as possible.\r\nwhat is left is a series of these submersions of my upper and lower\r\nbody.  eventually my nads adjust and i can let them go.  i will\r\nsubmerse my head to the ears underwater and listen to my heart for a\r\nwhile.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nmy body temperature has risin.  i\'m cooked.  parts are on fire,\r\nespecially my toes and feet which were the first in.  the candles are\r\nnice, as is the wall heater.  i usually have some music going.  not\r\nreally meditation music.  tonight i had shellac.  sometimes, kaki king.  sometimes i have\r\nsome old time music, or folk.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nwhen i\'m done, i pull the plug on the tub.  if i forget, it takes\r\nseveral minutes to drain morning while i wait to take a shower and go\r\nto work.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\ni towel off and start to sweat.  i\'ll sit in my chair like i am now,\r\nsweating.  sweating and feeling my feet burn, and in a daze.  an hour after the bath, i still feel the burn on my buns and feet.  this is the way it should be.\r\n</P>'),(3797,'<P>I started asking people what their favorite titty bars are.  I\'m just curious.  So here we go:</P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>A:  What is your favorite accessible titty bar that you can go to right now, tonight?</B><P>\r\n\r\n<P><B>B:  What is your favorite titty bar experience ever and where was it?</B></P>\r\n\r\n<P>You are encouraged to expound.</P>'),(3798,'<p>What is really the point of religion? Sometime after the cradle of civilization came the birth of all \"three majors\".  The emergence of religion (Judaism, Christianity and Islam), one could argue, has been responsible for quite a few wars witnessed by man since the dawn of man\'s cognitive faculty.  The rise of civilization brought forth the emergence of agriculture, mathematics, science, architecture... military warfare.  Suffice it to say, most of us are aware of the historical beginnings of the most common and mundane aspects of modern, albeit complicated,  life.  The dawn of thinking fertilized the soils of many disciplines.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n \r\n<p>One day in urban San Francisco, (perhaps due to work boredom), an email discussion with one of my bestfriends, Chimeres, and our purported \"Irish-Catholic\" buddy caught me perturbed.  Our friend, Chet, is of a Catholic-based breed where his belief posits that being good to society,\r\nthus being selfless, is an ideal one should uphold to.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> If not alarming, the concept distrubed me quite a bit.  I feel that most mainstream Catholic behavior really attempts to paint a magnified picture of themselves.  In order to repress the \"dirt\" or so-called sinful nature of one\'s existence, is it possible that Catholics tend to waste time and energy indulging in activities that could seemingly uplift themselves.  In a simplistic realization, I believe that all these stem from some underlying individual insecurity that one is unable to face or overcome.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To add insult to injury, I also came across a thread in my High School newsgroup.  The gyst of the emails eludes to a situation where one person is struggling with some form of cancer.  Emails float back and forth.  The normal\r\nprayers and well wishes flow through the wires.  While I hesitate to judge where religion is concerned, at least to a certain point, where I believe that religion is essential in certain collectivist societies comprised of individuals who are not capable of self-reflective actions; thus a need of some form of fundamental law, like the Hamurabi law in the beginning of civilization, necessitates to allow people to co-exist and survive, I am perplexed why the content of the message somehow struck a dissonant chord.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The email thread solicits actions from its members: ... \".. so what are their classmates doing about it (the lady with a terminal disease - cancer) in addition to prayers?  In one case, where Lady X was struck with cancer, her friends raised money, provided financial aid to her family and raised \"X\" number of pesos to help alleviate her current dilemna.\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m just a little bit stumped! (slap my face if I\'m off); however, do I perceive an arcane medieval behaviour where the patrons of art/saints/sponsor appear in a deus-ex machina fashion to save face for the day?  Do they\r\nstill really exist in these latin countries?   Does the measure of friendship and love really rely on monetary values?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Beware! It really is not the measure of friendship or love.  I feel that the act of donating money (or sympathy or love or altruism for that matter) represents a form of self-aggrandizement.  It is a way to make the GIVER feel better about themselves.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Like, a pat in the back, the collectivist saint could potentially relieve himself of his misfortunies vis a vis in living a good life; \"Give a donation,\" states his conscience, \"Poor person, they\'ll die anyway ... at least I am healthy and alive\" ...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Perhaps, I am a bit too analytical and cold, but at the end of the day, I will not succumb to any form of emotional coercion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What about deeds?  What about acts?  What about geniune concern?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\'m sorry, really sorry ... but I cannot be forced to love or care for someone that I have not spent time with; for someone I do not know nor someone whom I cannot fathom the soul.  I will not be coerced to \"take one for the team...\".  I believe in taking the time to get to know\r\neach object or thing or spirit of this world.  I am free; and will do as I please, for as long as I do not harm you, at least I will try.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I will give you my time, maybe even my life, only if i CHOOSE and WILL it ... but please, spare me the holy alphabets.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For in the end, who really knows ... science is still in a quandry - is the universe imploding or exploding?  Did we know that there is such a thing as anti-matter (dark matter) - 80% of our reality we DO NOT KNOW ?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>*sigh*</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If there is a god, or a unifying force ... i think this force is fair, and just and beautiful and sweet .. he will not favor the white, or the black, the rich or the poor .. how obnoxious of us to think he will even favor homo sapiens. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>If there is a god or a unifying force, he will be just to all things - both rock and plant and mammal and crustracean ... all of them will have beautiful and ugly qualities ... all found in this earth or perhaps, not in our lifetime, all found in beyond.</p>\r\n \r\n<p>If there is a god, he will not judge us either for eating meat and not being a vegetarian, because this god, if exists, knows that things in his universe is as blessed and sacred, bad and evil ... as beautiful as Michelangelo\'s sculptures and as macabre as Mt Vesuvio\'s wrath on the people of Pompei.</p><p>If there is a god, he would know you are vegetarian only because you want to digest faster perhaps and have a certain lifestyle, he would know that you would rather take your cancer patient and care for her in person than donate \"X\" number of dollars for the child she will leave behind. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Because if there was a god, he would know that her child will be stronger post mortem, and he would have known that the few times you spent with her were worth more than a million shares of GOOGLE stocks. </p>'),(3799,'<p>\r\ni\'ve become enamored of hip hop music recently. i am not sure why. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nit just fascinates me, this music of anger and ignorance, this culture that celebrates the best and worst of a disenfranchised population.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nmusic on the cusp of tragedy and sorrow. children celebrating the degradation of their brothers and sisters, killing them softly with their songs.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni understand that people sing about what they know, about what they dream, and about what they fear.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nit\'s black history month.  i have an idea the dr. king would not be down with the hip hop music. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni am confused about why it is that obviously intelligent men and women are willing, in fact eager, to glorify a life of violence and mayhem, long after they the cash that enables them to rise above the crack filled streets.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni\'m not talking about the obvious thug music, but the less obvious, the insidious beauty of songs like soldier, by destiny\'s child, with it\'s catchy rifs and beautiful video images.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>If your status ain\'t hood <br>\r\nI ain\'t checkin\' for him <br>\r\n\r\nBetta be street if he lookin\' at me <br>\r\n\r\nI need a soldier\r\nThat ain\'t scared to stand up for me <br>\r\n\r\nKnown to carry big things \r\nIf you know what I mean <br>\r\n\r\nIf your status ain\'t hood\r\nI ain\'t checkin\' for him <br>\r\n\r\nBetta be street if he looking at me <br>\r\n\r\nI need a soldier\r\nThat ain\'t scared to stand up for me <br>\r\n\r\nGotta know to get dough\r\nAnd he betta be street</i> <br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwhy would they do that to each other? money? surely not, because one of the things the nouveau riche find out real fast, regardless of their backgrounds, is that money does not bring happiness.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni have this conspiracy theory that someone, i am not sure who, is committed to ethnic slavery and genocide, that these people own the record labels and airwaves, and encourage their artists to glorify a lifestyle that is guaranteed to bring sorrow and misery.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nand then again, maybe it\'s just that these people have found a way to make a shitload of money, and they don\'t much care how they get it.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3800,'i have had a headache for three days. today i lost my vision while hanging out the washing, and proceeded to have a migraine. it was a bad one, where the pulses of pain in my brain felt more like waves which would be strong enough to become tsunamis. i felt a difference in the air, in the weight of the air, in the way it was getting into my head and it was making my head hurt. <i>the weather was making me have a migraine this time</i>, i thought to myself. and so i did a <a href=\"http://www.weather.com/newscenter/topstories/health/achesandpains/010712pressuremigraines.html\" target=\"_blank\">little research</a>. yes, it happens. and so it was.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni kept this, from today when i had first lost my eyesight, and i was trying to communicate with <a href=\"http://www.logy.org/%7Exep/\" target=\"_blank\">xep</a> on <a href=\"http://www.icb.net/\" target=\"_blank\">icb</a>.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<*qq*> Leepu.<br>\r\n/topic alana got migraine<br>\r\nI will not say \"Springfield\" just to get applause<br>\r\n/m qq i got migraine i cannot see very well<br>\r\nbut it is nice to know you are there.<br>\r\n/m zev i got migraine. beep me if you are present.<br>\r\n/m zev i canno t see very well.<br>\r\n<*zev*> mew alana<br>\r\n/m qq i am glad you are there though qq.<br>\r\n/m zev mew xep.<br>\r\n<*zev*> i am playing the clarinet<br>\r\n/m zev also my connection ois lagging muchly<br>\r\n<*zev*> doing scales<br>\r\n<*zev*> i\'m sorry you have a migraine<br>\r\n<*zev*> think of oliver sacks<br>\r\n/m zev i have been :)<br>\r\n/m zev there is a blothch of swirling colours.<br>\r\n/m zev and i think i may vomit soon.<br>\r\n<*zev*> is it beautiful?<br>\r\n/m zev also i sent you a an email.<br>\r\n<*zev*> people used to think they were mystical visions. is it like<br>\r\n<*zev*> that?<br>\r\n/m zev i am so happy you have a job. it must be a real relief.<br>\r\n/m zev can you beep me each time you write somrething because i can\'t see\r\nenough now to know<br>\r\n<*zev*> it is only one day a week for now but it will be with a nice<br>\r\n<*zev*> veterinarian<br>\r\n/m zev but i will try.<br>\r\n<*zev*> also it doesn\'t pay very much but i don\'t mind<br>\r\n/\'m zev wow it is so trippy.<br>\r\n<*zev*> i get to wear scrubs<br>\r\n<*zev*> *pet pet pet*<br>\r\n<*zev*> you should be resting<br>\r\n/m zev i sleep in scrubs sometimes. they are ace.<br>\r\n<*zev*> alana we need to leave now. you should sleep.<br>\r\n/m zev it is beautiful in a way, but it is kind of yucky too because the<br>\r\nlines are so pointy. i have a picture of it on my migraines page.<br>\r\n<*zev*> i will think a lot of healing thoughts for you<br>\r\n/m zev i am going to try and draw it<br>\r\nI will not demand what I\'m worth<br>\r\n/m zev i am drawing it<br>\r\n/m zev iyt is weir dbut i willscan it and show it to you wehn i sam better<br>\r\n'),(3802,'what not to do, ever:\r\nnever take family vacations.\r\nnever ignore my foreboding panic attacks.\r\nnever drink with family.\r\nnever travel to resorts with all-included drinking and eating.\r\nnever assume a vacation will be restful and joyous and sunshiney.\r\nnever drink more than 2 glasses of champagne in succession.\r\nnever try to have fun with drunk people unless you are one of the drunk people.\r\nnever watch the free \'entertainment\' at resorts unless you are there to heckle.\r\nnever spend time with people you don\'t like in a foreign country (or anywhere you are not paid to be, for that matter).\r\nnever be in the presence of economically-depressed peoples when also in the presence of stupid, arrogant drunks.\r\nnever visit a beach resort with people who HATE THE HEAT AND THE WATER.\r\nnever lay down for 10 minutes when you\'ve been drinking unless you really mean to lay down for 4 hours.\r\nnever dive into the ocean with your sunglasses on your head.\r\nnever leave your digestive enzymes or your comfortable shoes at home.\r\n</p>\r\nthings i did enjoy while on \"vacation\":\r\nkissing an iguana.\r\ndrinking 2 dos equis amber ales alone on a swing at the bar on the main drag in playa del carmen, listening to 80s rock.\r\neating yummy cinnamon and coconut milk oatmeal every morning.\r\nfeeding the kitties that hang around the dining areas.\r\nwatching a bunch of peahens cluck around after the one lovely peacock.\r\nfarting in the room i shared with my sister so she got grossed out.\r\nseeing a dead guy in a car accident on the way to a fun-filled day at a water park (fascinating, really).\r\nlistening to the tropical birds all morning.\r\nsnorkeling where the riviera maya meets the caribbean sea, and seeing thousand of fishes and a handful of manta rays.\r\ntubing down the river between mangroves.\r\nfinding another seat on the plane after hours of the child behind me screaming and the audio feed for the movie (my only solace from the screaming) going dead.\r\nbuying presents for my boyfriend.\r\nreading vice magazine i brought from home.\r\nfloating to the swim-up bar on my air-mattress and ordering non-alcoholic drinks, then floating away with them.\r\nwatching \"50 first dates\", and then listening to the new age and classical programmed music channel on the airplane.\r\nnot visiting the mayan ruins and feeling smug about it.\r\nnot getting my period or cramps until the flight home.\r\nnot drinking tequila or corona even once.\r\n<p/>\r\nthings i was happy to learn:\r\nthe father of the bride has come out of the closet to his family. he hit on all the pretty-boy gay dancers, and then on me. go figure.\r\nyou can learn little bits of bullshit spanish from subtitled tv (soy un mujer dolores = i am a sad woman. chinga du madre = fuck your mother) (spelling is optional).\r\nit is almost impossible to stay on an air-mattress in the waves.\r\nthere is much more salt in the caribbean sea than in the pacific ocean.\r\nthe mayans were very, very short.\r\ni am not scared of sealife anymore.\r\nfish will swim where people swim, even in the wild.\r\na new sliver of a moon sits on its bum over mexico, instead of standing on tiptoes like it does here. '),(3801,'<p>here it is. \r\n<p>most of the time, they begin like this. sometimes on crowded trams, sometimes while sitting on the couch, sometimes when i wake up, sometimes when i am working. immediately, there is this disturbance in my field of vision. it is slowly swallowed up by the lack of bloodflow. forty minutes later, the throbbing will become to strong to ignore. BOOM BOOM BOOM. i will fantasize of the ecstatic aftermath, of laying on the couch, hungover from the migraine, wondering at how beautiful life is. later, i lose sensation down one side of my body. my limbs seem to be enormous. objects become different realities. the meaning of far and close take on entirely new meanings. i may lose the capacity to create words which make sense. \"thrrbbbbmmmm\". or \"jjjjjiiiijjjel\". i will be sweating, shaking, and then be shivering from cold. my body is confused. i cannot stop vomiting. every 15 minutes, like clockwork. how will i keep these painkillers down? will i have to leave this bed and go to the doctor to get the drugs injected?\r\n\r\n<p>i have had 15 years with this unavoidable part of my life.\r\n\r\n<p>i used to be terrified of getting them. sometimes i would have them everyday for weeks. sometimes, my parents wouldn\'t believe me. sometimes i didn\'t know if i would be alive at the end of it. or if there would be an end of it. slowly, we have learnt to live with one another. i do my best to look after myself and keep myself out of known danger (nitrates and nitrites, chicken flavour, etc etc) and your end of the bargain has been to slowly deconstruct you. i found oliver sacks and suddenly i saw this whole new way of living and learning.\r\n\r\n<p>i became an explorer of migraine. not everyone get to experience the incredible altered senses. or the extreme pain. the massive rush afterward.\r\n\r\n<p>and so here we are.\r\n\r\n<p>alana and migraines. slowly getting to know each other.'),(3804,'<P>i am drowning in a person i do not know.  simple things overwhelm me and i am unable to make even the smallest of decisions.  my mother says i have overdosed on stress and that just like when you overdose on any drug i need to just fight my way through it saying it is just the stress.....that doesn\'t help much when i can\'t hold a single thought in my head for more than a few seconds.  can\'t remember the last time i was able to follow a train of thought in a conversation for more than a minute before it is completely derailed and i am left helpless and with no clue as to where i was going with that.......see what i mean....just now forgot where i was going with this......never mind.....i\'ll just leave it with the first sentence.....i am drowning in a person i do not know.</P>'),(3806,'<P>well, wednesday afternoon has gone by, and there isn\'t any wonderful event to tell you about, but here are a bunch of commonplace ones:<BR><BR>*  last night we had dinner here, made couscous aromatique (i\'ll put the recipe in the comments later) and, for dessert, peaches over vanilla ice cream.  i have a bar, but it\'s too low and the barstools too high to eat there.  i have four tv trays.  the most i\'ve ever used at one time is three.<BR><BR>there\'s a room to the west of my apartment.  it\'s about half as big as my place.  at the back of my kitchen closet, directly opposite the door, there\'s some plywood covering a six and a half by three foot hole.  if you removed those boards, there would be a step on the other side, and then you\'d be in the room.  it has jalousie windows along the front and side, and there\'s a door that opens onto the alley beside the house.  when my lease gets renewed in april, i\'m going to expand over there and pay some more rent.  how much more i don\'t know.  i\'m going to have to get a job by then, though.  we were talking about this room at dinner.<BR><BR>she said, \"are you going to put a dining table in there?\"<BR><BR>\"oh, definitely.  and some chairs, also.\"<BR><BR>she gives me a look.<BR><BR>i say, \"i\'m going to call it The Jungle Room.  make it more intriguing.  <I>Who knows what lies behind the door of The Jungle Room?</I>\"<BR><BR>\"uh-huh.  yeah.\"<BR><BR>i tell her that eventually i\'d like to put a futon or bed in there and make it a guest room, also.<BR><BR>she says, \"yeah, but they\'d still have to come in here to use the bathroom.\"<BR><BR>\"or they could hang it out in the alley.\"<BR><BR>she smirks.<BR><BR>\"could go out back with the cats and the rats, under the orange trees and the stars.  very romantic.  word would get around, and everybody\'d wanna stay here.  potentates and such.\"<BR><BR>\"sure.\"<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  after we cleaned up, we watched <B><I>the french connection</B></I>.  gene hackman was wearing a hat that was at once ridiculous and ultracool.  it was a sort of amish job with a narrower brim.<BR><BR>i said, \"i want a hat like that.  i could <U>work</U> a hat like THAT.\"<BR><BR>\"well, at least i know what i can get you for your birthday.\"<BR><BR>hackman was a badass.  supposedly did his own driving for that chase.<BR><BR>after an exhaustive search, the cops finally found all the heroin concealed in that car.  the dealers should have put some junkies on that job.  junkies can hide stuff better than anybody alive.  hide it so well sometimes that you can\'t ever find it again.  outstash it.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  after the movie, she hung out for awhile then went home.  when it was bedtime, i discovered i was out of geodon (antipsychotic).  actually, i knew i was out of it.  i had enough of everything else to last until today, when i had a doctor\'s appointment.  i just thought everything else would cover for it.<BR><BR>but it helps me sleep.  so i didn\'t sleep all night.<BR><BR>something occurred to me while i was laying there trying to fall asleep:  there was a little boy who was in kindergarten and first grade with me, played little league, went to the same church, etc.  best friends.<BR><BR>we kissed for about a minute in my room when he was spending the night one time, and again in the back of his dad\'s car where his parents had left us while they went in a friend\'s house for minute.<BR><BR>the kissing wasn\'t the big revelation.  i hadn\'t suppressed the memory or anything.  (it was just one of the many things that i denied for a long time and then came to terms with.)<BR><BR>laying there, it occurred to me that they left us in the back of that car for a good twenty minutes.  and i saw that i\'d never realized that, yes, his parents did seem like burnout types.  it all clicked into place:  i bet they were in there buying pot.  smoking a joint.<BR><BR>my parents weren\'t friends with his.  my parents are very naive and innocent people.  i bet they never suspected a thing.<BR><BR>he moved away the summer after first grade.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  after two in the morning, i resigned myself to a sleepless night and did some writing.<BR><BR>i\'ve started a correspondence with a pretty well-known science fiction writer.  he lived in brooksville for a few years in the early seventies, and his wife taught spanish in the county\'s one high school during the time my aunt taught english there.  (during my aunt\'s one-year teaching career.)<BR><BR>we\'ve e-mailed a few times.  i\'m going to try not to abuse it.  he\'s probably pretty busy.  we talked about burroughs.  (william seward.  not edgar rice.  [but they\'re both sci-fi, i guess.])  we talked about horror.  he writes horror, occaisonally.  i told him how i wanted to do a novel about secret societies and espionage, like knights templar, etc., but i did some research, and it quickly bled off into illuminati and aliens and lizard people, and i just got too creeped out.  i asked him if he believed in all that, and he gave me a very roundabout answer.  never quite answered the question.<BR><BR>in one of his novels, there are five people sexually involved.  a ménage à cinq, i guess.  i told him i read the book when i was fourteen or fifteen, and it gave me ideas later on.<BR><BR>i sent him six (brief) chapters of something i\'m working on.  in the six chapters, there are five points of view.  he said all my narrators sound the same.  i thought, <I>what about this?  a genre writer is lecturing me on narrative voice.</I>  but he has scores of published books, and i have none, so he\'s well worth listening to, i guess.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  so i went to see the doctor this morning.  he always wants to talk to me.  i don\'t know why.  i mean talking aside from ascertaining my condition.  we talked about mania addiction, inducing mania with alcohol and sugar and other means, lithium toxicity, the trade deficit, growing anti-semitism and anti-muslim feeling in europe, martin luther, the evolution of european power structures vis-a-vis the church, <B><I>paradise lost</B></I> (i claimed to have read it, and he quickly called bullshit on that.), total information awareness, dwindling civil rights, nascent fascism, the conditionality of love,  my prospects, the decrease in mental illness symptoms as one ages, and all kinds of other shit.<BR><BR>he tied me up for an hour.  all the nutters and cutters out in the lobby looked kind of pissed to be kept waiting so long.  i saw one little round girl there, michelle.  she said she just got out of the hospital.  she showed me the insides of her forearms, stitches all up and down them.  i never mutilated myself.  not like that, anyway.  <A HREF=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borderline_personality_disorder\">borderline personality disorder.</A>  must be rough.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  i went to the pharmacy, waited half an hour for them to fill the scripts.  home, vowed to stay up so i wouldn\'t disrupt my sleep schedule by going to bed at one in the afternoon.  went to the library, checked out <B><I>the godfather:  part II</B></I>.  hadn\'t seen it in awhile.<BR><BR>the first scene with hyman roth, the old jewish mobster guy, came on, and i tried to figure out who played him.  i had known at one time and had remarked on it the first time i saw the picture because the actor playing him wasn\'t an actor.  i remembered that much.  he was a writer or director or something or other in the entertainment world such as a producer or agent, but not an actor.  but none of those choices sounded right.<BR><BR>so, for the rest of the movie, i was trying to figure out who he was.  i could have stopped it at any time and looked it up on the internet, but i took it as a challenge.  and i just didn\'t know.<BR><BR>the credits came up, and it turned out he was played by <A HREF=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Strasberg\">lee strasberg</A>.<BR><BR>lee strasberg.<BR><BR>holy shit.<BR><BR>that hit me like a thunderbolt.  what a bold and masterful casting decision.  what subtlety.  that\'s just a pure genius move.  that would be like, i don\'t know, maybe quentin tarantino casting sydney pollack.  no, that doesn\'t approximate it at all.<BR><BR>and it was a beautifully understated performance.  he <U>was</U> an actor, even though he was in only eight films.  but he had to be one of the great actors of all time to teach what he did and who he did.  that casting is one of the little extras that are woven into great art.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  i looked up the term <I>peen</I> this afternoon and found that it was derived from <I>penis</I> and <I>pinkstink</I> and <I>poon</I>.  i would never have figured out what it meant in ten thousand years.<BR><BR>and then the little slang dictionary used it in a sentence:  <I>(website) will greatly excite your peen.</I>  or something to that effect.<BR><BR>well, i enjoy good pornography just as much as the next guy, so i checked it out.  it was a link to a picture that was absolutely horrible.  god, it was disgusting.  people shouldn\'t mislead others into looking at something gross or scary.  it just isn\'t nice.  that\'s one of the cardinal sins of the internet, in my opinion.  they should at least give you a warning.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  she came over tonight, and we made pasta with - what would you call it?  vegetable sauce, i guess.  it\'s my creation, just a rough recipe:<BR><BR>heat up some olive oil and saute garlic, onion, half of one of those little packets of badia oregano, fresh basil, a zucchini and a yellow squash sliced and quartered, and however many sliced black olives you want.  add a 14 oz. can of diced tomatoes, a 14 oz. can of tomato sauce, salt, pepper, a little sugar, and a little balsamic vinegar and cook it down until it\'s as thick as you want.  serve it over pasta.  we had rigatoni, which is not a great value deal for pasta, i guess, because it takes up a lot of room in the box, being tubular.  who knows.<BR><BR>for dessert:  strawberries, blueberries, and blackberries with whipped cream.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  so now i\'ve been trying to get to sleep.  after i\'ve been up awhile, sometimes it\'s hard to calm down.  adrenaline or something, i guess.  i\'m experimenting with the computer.  my speakers don\'t work (sound card is shot, i think.), but i have a high-speed connection and a cd burner, so i should be able to download songs and listen to them on a cd player or dvd player.  i think.  i don\'t know.  i\'m going to try.  i\'m going to look for some live new order.  any technical advice would be appreciated.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  well, it\'s almost 1:30 a.m.  i\'m going to try to get some sleep.  goodnight, all.</P>'),(3803,'<P>i thought it was thursday from the time i woke up<BR>until somebody corrected me this afternoon.<BR><BR>i walked to the post office sometime after lunch<BR>to mail a birthday/valentine\'s day card to my grandmother.<BR>(her birthday\'s monday.)<BR><BR>it was nice out,<BR>not sweat weather yet,<BR>so i decided to take the long way back,<BR>west on - missouri?<BR>(i don\'t know.  almost all these damn streets are named after<BR>states or presidents,<BR>and they run together after awhile.)<BR>north on grand<BR>back east on main.<BR><BR>i was walking on the north side of the street,<BR>against the traffic.<BR>i heard,<BR>from an approaching vehicle,<BR>female voices hooting and yelling at me.<BR>it was a little after two,<BR>when the high school lets out.<BR>this phenomenon occurs exclusively at this hour<BR>or at night.<BR><BR>sixteen and seventeen year-old girls<BR>seem to dig me.<BR>(at least from a distance, anyway.)<BR>(i think women grow a little more discerning<BR>as they get older.<BR>either that,<BR>or they aren\'t<BR>so vocal about it anymore.)<BR><BR>(i have noticed, though,<BR>that they will yell at me<BR>only when they are in groups<BR>and only when i\'m wearing semi-nice clothes.<BR>i think alcohol may also be involved somtimes.)<BR><BR>if it weren\'t for that damned age of consent,<BR>i\'d be set like chet.<BR>not really.<BR>when you\'re seventeen,<BR>\"women\" your age look full-grown,<BR>but when you get to be my age,<BR>they look like children.<BR>they don\'t start to look mature<BR>until they hit twenty-two,<BR>twenty-three.<BR><BR>so, when it happens,<BR>i don\'t look at them.<BR>i just throw my hand up<BR>and wave<BR>and send them my best wishes<BR>and let them go grow up<BR>somewhere.<BR><BR>so this bunch passed me,<BR>and i didn\'t look at them,<BR>and then from behind me,<BR>hey, hey<BR>hey.<BR>i kept going.<BR>heyheyheyhey.<BR><BR>i turned around<BR>and went back to see what they wanted.<BR>it was a jeep with the top up.<BR>it contained two fine young women.<BR>neither one of them<BR>looked like they had any family values.<BR>rather, they looked to be full of the kink.<BR>one of them was my girlfriend,<BR>so i could vouch that for her.<BR><BR>- we were just at your apartment.<BR><BR>- i bet i wasn\'t home.<BR><BR>- shut up.  d\'you want to go to the beach with us?<BR><BR>- aren\'tchoo supposed to be someplace?<BR><BR>- i took the afternoon off.<BR><BR>- must be nice to be able to take the afternoon off.<BR><BR>- ha.  come on.<BR><BR>i got in.<BR><BR>- do you have a swimsuit?<BR><BR>- no, i just swim in my underwear<BR>like the redneck i am.<BR><BR>we headed towards my place.<BR><BR>- you gonna introduce me<BR>to your buddy here?<BR><BR>- this is megan.<BR><BR>- hi.<BR><BR>- hi.<BR><BR>- do you know my name?<BR><BR>- yes, i do.  i\'ve heard a lot about you.<BR><BR>- i just know that you have.<BR><BR>- hey, i realize we just met,<BR>but do you think i could use your bathroom<BR>when we get back to your apartment?<BR><BR>- five dollars.<BR><BR>- quit.<BR><BR>i changed<BR>and grabbed the beach towel,<BR>and we headed out to green key.<BR><BR>they were wearing t-shirts and shorts<BR>over the two-pieces.<BR>we were laying on our stomachs:<BR>megan, karyssa, me.<BR>after a while,<BR>i rolled over on my side<BR>and propped my head up on my upraised arm<BR>and stole glances at all the people.<BR><BR>shortly, karyssa saw what i was doing<BR>and assumed the same position,<BR>only facing me.<BR>she kept watching me<BR>to see where my eyes went,<BR>i guess,<BR>so that put the stopper to that.<BR><BR>one of the places i was looking<BR>was her friend\'s ass.<BR>she has this<BR>perfect<BR>round<BR>ass.<BR>the brothers<BR>used to call an ass like that<BR>an onion,<BR>i guess because it\'ll bring tears to your eyes.<BR><BR>i got up and went to the bathroom<BR>just so i could get<BR>some alternate views of it<BR>on the way back.<BR><BR>i laid down on my stomach<BR>and minded my business<BR>as they talked of<BR>inconsequential things.<BR><BR>karyssa got up<BR>to go to the bathroom.<BR>my head was pointed away from megan.<BR>i raised up a little<BR>and turned it toward her.<BR>she was facing me.<BR>my left forearm<BR>was obstructing my view<BR>of her face.<BR>i moved it away,<BR>and we grinned at each other.<BR><BR>we didn\'t say anything.<BR>just looked at each other.<BR>after a little bit,<BR>we stopped grinning<BR>and just looked each other in the eye.<BR>that lasted a good twenty seconds.<BR>finally, i said, <I>what?</I><BR>she said, <I>what?</I><BR>we looked at each other some more,<BR>and then i turned my head<BR>and faced the other way.<BR><BR>i thought,<BR><I>you, girl, all you females -<BR>where were you<BR>when i needed you?</I><BR>that\'s the way it works,<BR>i guess.<BR><BR>a man and a little boy<BR>with british accents<BR>walked by.<BR>the man was trying to explain<BR>to the boy<BR>what a manatee looked like.<BR>it sounded like<BR>he didn\'t have too firm a grasp of it<BR>himself.<BR><BR>k. came back.<BR>we stayed for another half-hour.<BR>i went out in the water.<BR>they stayed on the beach.<BR>on this part of the west coast,<BR>the waves are negligible.<BR>i swam around the point,<BR>out to the buoy<BR>and back<BR>as fast as i could.<BR><BR>we dropped megan off first.<BR>she got out,<BR>and i got into the<BR>front passenger seat.<BR>i told karyssa<BR>we were flirting with each other.<BR>i said, <I>well, here\'s what we were doing</I><BR>and described it<BR>then said, <I>if that\'s flirting.</I><BR><BR>she said, <I>oh, that\'s flirting.<BR>matter of fact,<BR>that\'s worse than flirting.</I><BR><BR><I>how is that worse?</I><BR><BR><I>trust me - it\'s worse.</I><BR><BR>she told me she appreciated my honesty<BR>and dropped me off<BR>without another word.<BR><BR>like they say,<BR>honesty gets you nowhere.</P>'),(3807,'<p>\r\nI am getting ready to complete my first bulk order. I have been negotiating and\r\nhaving samples made for more than a few products. So far its been bathmats, bed linen, room slippers, beach slippers, various sizes of terry towel, and a few others I can not think of. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs part of my training to become a full on “global sourcer” I had to write a sales contract. I was given a few different contracts and was told to look at them and amend what was needed and rewrite them all into one contract. The first contract I was given was of a sale between a Korean company and a company in Uzbekistan. The contract with full of all sorts of crazy references regarding “presidential decrees” and other things associated with a Central Asian Dictatorship. Quite weird really. Things like “In respect to presidential decree 93, all products…yadda yadda yadda…” I basically just deleted them all and kept the skeleton of a contract.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe second contract I read was the contract I had signed with my school. I used it for placement of clauses and other “Korean” content. I deleted the sections regarding alcohol and drug use while working. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe third contract I read and edited was a contract between Korea and the United States regarding patriot missile sales. It was full of very specific clauses regarding the technical aspects of a missile not actually hitting its target. It scared me a little bit. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the end, I got a complete sales contract made. I was quite proud of myself. Nevertheless, my boss had a big problem with clause 8. I will let you decide where the problem lies. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n8. FORCE MAJEURE\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n8.1\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Parties shall be deemed to be released from their responsibilities for any delay\r\nin performance which results from any cause beyond a party’s reasonable control\r\nincluding but without limitation to any Act of God, embargo or other governmental\r\nact, regulation or request, fire, accident, strike, war, pirates, UFO attack, and riot.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n8.2\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe party who is unable to fulfil its obligations because of the circumstances outlined in\r\n8.1 above shall immediately and in any event within 3 days notify the other party of\r\nThe occurrence rendering such fulfillment impossible and shall present a document\r\nconfirmed by an authorized organization. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n8.3\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShould the circumstances outlined in clause 8.1 last for more than 3 months, either\r\nparty shall have the right to refuse to fulfil further obligations under this Contract and in\r\nsuch event neither party shall have any right or claim against the other for\r\ncompensation or loss occasioned by the happening of any of the events in clause 8.1\r\nhereof.\r\n</p>\r\n \r\n'),(3809,'<P>*  last transmission from the fort.  i\'m going to turn in the cable modem tomorrow.  i can\'t afford it anymore.  i was paying $30/month, and now they want $45.  i\'ll have to wait until i get a job.  for awhile, i\'ll be restricted to accessing the net from the computers at the library for an hour a day on weekdays and saturdays.<BR><BR>and my computer\'s fucked up.  i wasn\'t getting any sound.  the device manager said my ES1869 plug and play audiodrive (wdm) was working properly.  i read on a forum that i could try to to fix the problem by uninstalling the driver.  and then on restart, the computer would reinstall it, and everything would be okay.  i did that, and then the computer would not use the driver in the file.  i went to the ess website.  it said they would not release the driver (for windows me) for download, that it was on the installation disk.  i didn\'t have the disk.  it said to perhaps try the one for windows 95/98/98se.<BR><BR>i downloaded it.  the computer didn\'t recognize it, i guess.  and then, somehow, the device manager decided that the audiodrive was working properly.<BR><BR>but now the computer\'s fucked up.  it\'s slower than usual, and freezes more than it did:  it\'s just wonky in general.  i tried a system restore that didn\'t help.  i\'m just going to have to take it to somebody once i get a job, i guess.  there are some other things wrong with it.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  i was listening to wmnf community radio friday afternoon.  during drivetime, they play alt country/old country.  a loretta lynn/conway twitty duet came on.  first the intro,<BR><BR>then conway sings:<BR><BR>     <I>you\'re the reason i\'m ridin\' around on recapped tires</I><BR><BR>then loretta:<BR><BR>     <I>and you\'re the reason i\'m hangin\' our clothes outside on wires</I><BR><BR>then both of them:<BR><BR>     <I>and you\'re the reason our kids are ugly</I><BR><BR>that\'s some funny shit.<BR><BR>i remember that when conway died some years ago, he had been dead for about two weeks, not cold in the ground yet, when a cheaply-produced commercial advertising a greatest hits album appeared on tv.  if i was somebody like him, i\'d put something in my will to discourage that, but i guess your loved ones need a little something, and that would be the time to capitalize on it.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  we watched <B><I>gangs of new york</B></I> - when? - friday night, i guess.  in one scene, leonardo dicaprio had his shirt open.  it was the only flesh he showed for the duration of the film.  if i was making a movie with him, i\'d encourage him to take his clothes off quite frequently.  sell some tickets.<BR><BR>so i was surveying the promised land when, out of the corner of my eye, i saw karyssa looking at me.  i must have appeared transfixed.  i turned to her.<BR><BR>she said, <I>do you think he\'s hot?</I><BR><BR>i hit pause on the remote.  <I>how am i supposed to answer that?  what kind of a question is that?  if i say yes, you\'ll get mad, and if i say no, you\'ll think i\'m lying.</I><BR><BR>that\'s just one of the things we\'re going to have to deal with in our relationship, i guess.<BR><BR>after it was over, i told her about how the whole movie, except for the computer generated stuff, was filmed in a studio.  a studio in rome called cinecitta.  i told her how much was spent on it, and how i thought that, ultimately, it wasn\'t worth it.  she didn\'t think it was that great, either.<BR><BR>it was okay for what it was, i guess, but it could have been a lot better.  it was facile.  it should have been more opaque.  it should have been different in a hundred ways.<BR><BR>the original script was probably a lot better; it probably got dumbed-down.  the budget was huge, and there were massive cost overruns.  in the middle of shooting, scorsese and dicaprio both gave back signicant chunks of their salaries.  i imagine that, at the onset of the project, those who financed it said, <I>yes, we understand you have director\'s cut, but if you want us to pay for it, you\'re going to make something we think is commercial.</I><BR><BR>i told her about fellini, and how nobody really noticed it when he died because river phoenix checked out on the same day.  probably wouldn\'t have garnered much attention, anyway.<BR><BR>she told me about derrick may.<BR><BR>i related what i knew about the technological singularity thing that the sci-fi writer told me about.  and how the shit storm, it\'s calling us.<BR><BR>she told me to hush, and we went to bed.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  she\'s good to me.  i don\'t want to have to have somebody look out for me for the rest of my life, but it\'s nice to know that she\'s there.  when i had the flu several weeks back, she took me over to hers.  i had a fever.  she gave me tylenol, and tucked me in.<BR><BR>when i have anything worse than a low fever, i start to hallucinate pretty easily.  nothing serious - look at the blinds, and a duplicate copy starts to move around them, back and forth.  that kind of thing.  trails.  plus low-grade audio stuff:  gentle basso rumbles that sounded like they might be thunder way out on the water or boat engines in the channel.  also high, faint, distant laughter, which was beginning to unnerve me.  these were what they call breakthrough symptoms - ones that are beyond the power of the medication.<BR><BR>an hour later, eight or nine p.m., things weren\'t any better.  she brought in a wadded-up  sheet and a blanket.  she told me to get up.  we spread the blanket out over the bed.  the sheet was damp.  it went over the blanket.  she told me to lie over on the far right of the bed and roll myself up.  when i got to the far left, she fastened the parts of the bedclothes that were hanging off the side of the mattress to the body of the jelly roll with safety pins, and i motored back over to my side.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>this is what babies must feel like.  it must be a real rush when they can finally do this.</I>  alright!  now i don\'t have to look at that fuckin\' ceiling anymore.  ah, shit, this view isn\'t much better.  now all i\'m gettin\' is blanket and mattress and punk-ass elmo over there.  <I>they have more range of motion in their arms and legs than i do in this thing, though.</I><BR><BR>she said, <I>wait.  get back on your stomach.<BR><BR>why?<BR><BR>just do it.  c\'mon.</I><BR><BR>she left the room.  after a couple of minutes, i heard what sounded like a kitchen appliance whirring.  blender, juicer, food processor.  something like that.<BR><BR>i had my head turned towards the door.  she came in with a metal mixing bowl and some gauze.<BR><BR>i said, <I>what\'s that?<BR><BR>pureed garlic and olive oil.<BR><BR>what are you gonna do with it?<BR><BR>i\'m going to put it on the soles of your feet.<BR><BR>are you gonna make pasta puttanesca on my stomach when i get better?<BR><BR>what?!<BR><BR>nothin\'.  i\'m kinda out of it.</I><BR><BR>i couldn\'t see what she was doing.  i felt her bunch the bottom of the bedclothes up around my calves and then slather the stuff on.<BR><BR><I>that feels weird.<BR><BR>yeah, i know.  my mom used to do this to us.</I><BR><BR>she told me not to move, then went into the bathroom and washed her hands and wrapped my feet in gauze and pulled the bedclothes back down.  i felt her lightly pushing on my calf.  i contracted my back muscles and turned my head a far as i could to try to see what she was doing.  she had folded the flap of excess fabric at the bottom of the bedclothes over the top of the roll and was pinning it on, holding it down with one hand while she did so.<BR><BR><I>be still.</I><BR><BR>same thing on the other side.<BR><BR><I>okay.  now you can roll back over.</I><BR><BR>she put the damp washcloth back on my forehead and got ready for bed and got in and pulled the comforter over us.  she situated herself on her side next to me, her head propped up on her hand and arm.<BR><BR><I>you\'re going to have garlic breath in about half an hour.<BR><BR>just remember thatchoo did it.<BR><BR>and then you\'re going to start sweating during the night, and the room\'s going to stink in the morning.<BR><BR>ha.  hafta air it out.</I><BR><BR>she lowered her head down over mine and kissed me.<BR><BR><I>go to sleep.<BR><BR>i\'ll try.</I><BR><BR>my extrasensory perception was still revving a little.  it was quiet.  (except for the far off laughter and rumbling)  i sang my little song to her:<BR><BR><I>karyssa petersen<BR>she\'s got some yellow hair<BR>she\'s got a little sister named sierra<BR>she\'s got a brother zack<BR>petersen<BR><BR>do you think she can stay on her own all night?<BR>NO!...</I><BR><BR>and then i throw in something about current events there at the end.  it hardly ever rhymes.<BR><BR>i have one for my niece, too.  i made it up when she was about two and a half, i guess:<BR><BR><I>paige allen<BR>paige allen<BR>you are a paige allen<BR>you\'re not grandma dogs<BR>and you\'re not grandma cows</I><BR><BR>(the formation of the nomenclature designed to distinguish the sets of grandparents from each other occured just prior to the song\'s composition, if memory serves.  joe\'s parents have a doberman pinscher and a german shepherd and (had) a poodle, and mom and dad live next to a dairy.)<BR><BR>the fever broke somewhere around two a.m., and i felt a lot better when i woke up.  and the room stank.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  i\'m writing a novel about prostitutes that will be safe for my mother to read, should she elect to do so.  i don\'t know if i\'m going to hobble myself like this in the future.  i might write under a pseudonym.<BR><BR>when i sell this book,  my parents are going to want to know where all the money came from, and then they\'ll want to see the proof.  but, in the future, i\'ll have the freedom to do whatever i want, because once you have a lot of money, nobody can detect when you get more of it should you do something like write under another name.<BR><BR>i don\'t want to cause my family grief.  they live in a small town, and i\'ve brought enough shame to them already.<BR><BR>i have to be able to justify all that money only because i want to buy their house.  they wouldn\'t sell it to me if they thought the money came from some unsavory enterprise.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  read at age nineteen, <B><I>fear and loathing in las vegas</B></I> was one of the main things that made me want to try l.s.d.  it and <B><I>the electric kool-aid acid test</B></I>.  and the beatles.  and <B><I>on the road</B></I>, somehow.  (probably because of the depictions of neal cassady in both it and <B><I>t.e.k-a.a.t.</B></I>, the correllation of which suggesting to me, for the first time, that there might be an intergenerational continuum that extended back throughout history for who knows how far, a network of beneficence that encircled the world and conspired to hold it together.  this was a notion that eightteen years of christian tutelage had somehow failed to awaken in me.)<BR><BR>i don\'t know how my life would have turned out if i had never tried l.s.d.  somehow i suspect it would be something like:<BR><BR><I>i live in dayton, ohio, with my wife of ten years and my three children.  i\'m a junior vice president at a tire company.  my wife and i associate with only three other couples and their children.  i\'m having an affair with another man from my workplace.  i smoke the occaisonal joint.  even in the alternate universe, i\'m plagued with alcoholism and bipolar disorder.  my wife and i are solvent only because she discovered, after much heartache, that she would have to handle the money.</I><BR><BR>i don\'t know if that\'s better or worse than what i\'ve got now.<BR><BR>i wonder if those closest to hunter s. thompson know why he killed himself.  if he left a note.  it would interesting to know how many writers who commit suicide leave notes.  perhaps the ones who don\'t feel that they\'ve already said their piece.<BR><BR>with writers who kill themselves, it\'s tempting to look back into their oeuvre for clues that might predict their fate.  i can\'t see any in his.  there must have been a lot of things he kept to himself.<BR><BR>you wonder how well the people of the future will know us.  you wonder how long the chronicles of our victories and miscreants, our upheavals and explorations and efforts, our pasttimes and joys and deadly mundanities, our chroniclers, will last.  will our descendants be able to relate to us at all?  will they be just like us?  how far back can we ourselves remember?  we\'d like to think that our legacy will endure forever, but it\'s inevitable that someday we\'ll all be the stuff of myth, and someday after that, we\'ll be forgotten.</P>'),(3810,'<p>I am at that age where I must indignantly refer to my maturity at every given opportunity. Like when I get carded at a bar, I\'m all \"hey I\'m 28, so bring it on, I love it!\" and then the bodega owner or bartender, through some etiquette of gender, must tell me they thought I was <i>so much younger</i>.</p>\r\n<p>Take my cab driver from the airport tonight (oaktown 2 strong). We rapped about where I\'m from, where the chinese guy in the back is from, and where he is from (\"where you from baby\" is another topic for another article, filed under \"being hit on by a stranger with the ambiguous ethnicity line\"), which happens to be the former French colony Guinea. He made a big show of changing the talk-radio station to an English-speaking station. I said \"hey...heh...I er studied French back in high school.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"Was that a long time ago?\"</p>\r\n<p>\"Yeah. what? how old do you think I am??\"</p>\r\n<p>\"No, I mean uh, that you look so young, but you talk like you know a lot.\"</p>\r\n<p>\"Nice save. I\'ll be 29 in a couple of days so it was about 11 years ago.\" </p>\r\n<p>And then there\'s all this obligatory feigned surprise. Why. Quel insulte. Was it my indignance? Is it my femaleness? Is it that old adage that you should never ask a \"lady\" her \"age\", implying of course that a \"lady\" would be \"offended\" to have to share the truth, which would obviously expose her as \"older than she wants to be\" because \"younger is more fuckable\" and a woman\'s value is determined by \"how much you want to fuck her\"? </p>\r\n<p>Whenever I talk with Hillary about age, she confirms how glad or proud she is to be a grown lady. She remembers her college days, what a clown she was back then! well-dressed, good hair, but she didn\'t know so much, did she! Now she knows a thing or two. It makes her <i>hotter</i>. I keep trying to remember what thing I know now that I did not know when I was 21. </p>\r\n<p> These things I know are mostly boring facts about American political history, more cautious tips about how to manage money, more random instructions about how to care for the human body according to various health disciplines from around the world. I suppose I never read Julia Kristeva in college. When I was younger, I didn\'t need help requiring boys to give me orgasms, I didn\'t need help asserting myself in meetings, I knew my way around audio-visual equipment and the most basic construction tools (but not computer hardware, and I still don\'t). I did not know basic plumbing (now I know to open up the toilet and pull the rubber ball out etc if I happen to shit in a public broken toilet). I didn\'t know how to be a good roommate (workin on it) or houseguest (bring booze, make the bed, change the toilet paper if the roll runs out, don\'t try to interact with the hostess first thing in the morn).</p>\r\n<p> I confess that I google biographical information about cultural figures I admire. I discovered that Patti Smith was 29 when her band\'s first album <I>Horses</i> was released. Well, yes, of course this gives me hope that I could still be some kind of poet-rock star! No actually it doesn\'t. But it reminds me that you can be sweaty and androgynous and irreverent and loud and disgusting and animal even if everyone else your age is getting married and buying property and going toilet shopping and getting knocked up. on purpose.</p>\r\n<p> My mother\'s annual birthday card arrived today. Here is an excerpt from her handwritten text:\r\n<blockquote> I still remember the day you come to this world was the happy day of my life Just like \"The all angel get together send you to me made my dream come true\" (The song that I made you sing for me)</blockquote></p>\r\n<p> Now I must ask you to listen to <i>Horses</i> and tell me: who is the poet, Patti Smith or my mother?'),(3811,'<p>You arrive again unannounced<br>\r\nlike the cat who newly shares my apartment<br>\r\npouncing into bed this morning<br>\r\nand extending one pink paw <br>\r\nto explore the contours of my chest.<br>\r\nWe lay like that for several minutes,<br>\r\na warm tangle of unfamiliar tendencies<br>\r\nand involuntary purrs, until the coffee<br>\r\nmachine beeped that it was done.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yesterday on the hill where I\'ve often <br>\r\nimagined kissing someone but have mostly<br>\r\nhiked alone, we lay like on-screen lovers <br>\r\nin the grass, your head easily come<br>\r\nto rest on my abdomen, my hand <br>\r\non your chest and the sky wide open.<br>\r\nA white haze clung to the city\'s pretty <br>\r\nshoulders and I quietly wondered why<br>\r\nthis took so little time, and why other<br>\r\nthings take so long yet it hardly <br>\r\nmatters once they\'re finished&#151;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>like those chairs that took me<br>\r\nfive months to get around to recovering,<br>\r\nor the car that\'s taken a year to<br>\r\nget sold.  For fifteen months <br>\r\nI performed daily rites, got lost <br>\r\nin several narratives that led nowhere, <br>\r\nglanced sadly at piles of mail unsorted, <br>\r\nnever catching up or ever quite aware<br> \r\nof how fluidly the days could pour <br>\r\ninto place once you sauntered <br>\r\nout of the past and sat down to dinner.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So let us bow to the power of <br>\r\nunfinished business, wager that the<br> \r\nworst  is over, and allow some hours<br>\r\nto roll over us like welcome tides.<br>\r\nLet us be present for tiny crises<br>\r\nand alert on the phone, but alone<br>\r\nin the same room when there\'s work<br>\r\nto be done.  And let us conjure a story<br> \r\nwhere no one needs saving, where<br>\r\ntime curls back on itself, and<br>\r\nlaughably, in the nick of it, everyone<br> \r\ngets just what they needed, <br>\r\ndespite impatient protests,<br>\r\nand where the sound of each others\'<br> \r\ncomplicated sighs never gets old.</p>\r\n'),(3812,'<p>\r\nI hit the GIGANTIC book store five minutes before it closed.  The lady behind the counter made a sour face when I asked about Hunter\'s last effort.\r\nWithout answering my question, she walked me to the impromptu A-Z memorial display of his titles.\r\nAnd there it was:\r\n<i>Hey Rube: Blood Sport, the Bush Doctrine, and the Downward Spiral of American Dumbness</i>,\r\nstanding proud under the artificial light.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA ghost looked out from the cover, posing like one of my hillbilly relatives: ass planted on the fender of what appeared to be a GM product, thin lips wryly pursed, \r\neyes locked in a half-squint.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe breaking news was on late-night a.m. radio.  As much as anything, I wanted to figure out why he put a bullet in his head.\r\nI always want to know, \"Why?\"\r\nOver the years I\'ve chambered a round and slipped the muzzle in my mouth on more than one occassion.\r\nPeople close to me have gone all the way.  \r\nIt\'s got to cross every thinking person\'s mind, at least once.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\r\nChariot seeks Star: energy spent on a journey without a destination, without a hope.\r\n</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA queer mix of emotions ran through me as I listened to the pundits speculate about his last day; cowardly dicks pounced on the warm corpse, flags waiving, railing against anti-establishmentarians.  Iwo Jima!  I had no desire to blame him, or to believe anything too quickly.\r\nSixty-seven years: that\'s not bad, from my thirty-seven year perspective.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI stopped reckoning the scores in orthodox terms some while ago.\r\nAnd I began to care more about what was half-done, what happened in spite of best efforts, and what needed to be suffered through.\r\nYou know: I cared about intentions.  I cared about people who really lived, and survived.  Whatever the truth of it all, my tribe now felt smaller.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAt the age of fifteen, I bought the 1981 edition of <i>Hell\'s Angels</i>; at the age seveteen, I bought the 1983 edition of <i>The Curse of Lono</i>.  They were good books for me, and I\'m glad that he wrote them.\r\nI can\'t write more than that I was given hope that it was possible to live a real life, a human life, outside the system.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHumor grandpa, and think back to a time when all copy was sold at small, independent shops.  Imagine: the owner of my digs wheeling his chair over hand-made plywood ramps while book selling, everything in the small space smelling of geezer, newsprint, and tobacco.\r\nThe Dead Kennedys were trading <i>Fresh Fruit for Rotting Vegetables</i>.\r\nAnd it was a BIG DEAL to hear the word FUCK coming out of your speakers, just like it was a big deal to see Hunter use that word in his writing.  \r\nI always had a feeling, deep in my teen-gut, that Hunter and The Dead Kennedys were telling the truth about the ugliness behind the <a href=\"http://www.artic.edu/artaccess/AA_Modern/pages/MOD_5.shtml\" target=\"_blank\"><i>American Gothic</i></a> veneer that RR was pasting everywhere.  \r\nTwenty years later, living in a <i>Gothic</i> revival, I feel the same.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI miss Hunter S. Thompson because he was a link between what was and what is.  Older people are good in that respect.\r\nI recall a nice lady from my mother\'s church; her name was Teru Nakamura.  She made Christmas tree ornaments from walnut shells.\r\nWhen she was a little girl, American soldiers took her from her home in California and brought her to a detention camp.\r\nNow she\'s gone, and so is her memory of that experience.  Ditto for my cousin Mike: he was a Green Beret who lived the Dark Side in Vietnam.  Ditto for a thousand other characters like Teru and Mike.\r\nI wish, too late, that I had learned everyone\'s stories.  Things weren\'t always as they are now in the good old USA.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhen I read Hunter\'s last book, I realized that he had seen the slip.  And I realized that he had lost his peers.\r\nIt\'s a Hell of a thing to feel that the motion of your life has been spent in a circular rut.\r\nRumsfeld, Bush Sr. and Cheney have been in the Executive [but for Carter & Clinton] since 1969; they\'ve survived all the old hippies.\r\nYou were right, Hunter: these are the same people who brought us Vietnam.  \r\nI wish, maybe childishly, that more people from your generation were still fighting the good fight: for freedom, for equality, for the hope of an American Dream.\r\nThere is something to be said for honesty and companionship in every circumstance, but more so now than ever...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>\r\n\"Politics is the art of controlling your environment.  That is one of the key things I learned in these years, and I learned it the hard way.\r\nAnybody who thinks that it \'doesn\'t matter who\'s President\' has never been Drafted and sent off to die in a vicious, stupid War on the other side of the World - or\r\nbeen beaten and gassed by Police for trespassing on public property - or been hounded by the IRS for purely political reasons - or locked up in Cook County Jail\r\nwith a broken nose and no phone access and twelve perverts wanting to stomp your ass in the shower.  \r\nThat is when it matters who is President or Governor or Chief of Police.\r\nThat is when you will wish you had voted.\"\r\n</i>\r\nHST\r\n</p>'),(3813,'<P>amongst all the other defective ones, i must have a bad shit-stirring gene.  it\'s gotten me into trouble recently, so i decided, about a month ago, to get a little cannister of mace to carry around in my pocket in case my mouth writes a check my ass can\'t cash.<BR><BR>i went to the grocery store thursday night at a little after eleven for a box of farfalle, a can of crushed tomatoes, some capers, a gallon of orange juice, peaches and plums, two eggplants, and a coke.  when i finished putting my items on the conveyor belt, i looked around.  two young men had gotten in line behind me.  there was a case of budweiser on the belt beside them.<BR><BR>they were probably in their early- to mid-twenties.  the first looked like he might have been italian, really cute, babyface, dimples.  the other one was redheaded, freckles, not as cute as his friend, but not bad.  a little on the chunky side.<BR><BR>however, their look was totally undermined by the hairstyle they were sporting:  on both, the sides were slicked back, and all the hair across the front of the head was gathered together and spiked, a la ed grimley, only bent back about halfway up.<BR><BR>there was a woman between us, maybe fifty, blonde hair feathered back, dark roots.  she looked exhausted.  there was a bottle of merlot among her things on the belt.  i zeroed right in on it.<BR><BR>i turned back around.  the cashier was scanning my stuff, and the guy at the end of the setup was bagging it and putting it back in the cart.  it had all fit in the kiddie seat to start with, but, bagged up, it took up more space somehow, so it went into the body of the cart.<BR><BR>the cashier told me the total.  i held up my food stamp card.  if there\'s tax on any of the items, they have to take it off.  i was about to swipe the card through the reader when some saturnine force possessed me, and i turned and said, <I>gentlemen.</I><BR><BR>i pointed at the cuter one\'s forehead and flicked my finger back and forth between the two of them.<BR><BR><I>what\'s with the hair?  because, you know, if it was just one of you doin\' it, you MIGHT be able to get away with it.  you could say it was some kinda guido flashback kinda thing.  but when you BOTH do it, you look like idiots.  you look like a pair uh assmunchers.  just a little advice.  from me to you.</I><BR><BR>the redheaded one said, <I>fuck you, you punk.  what\'s up with YOUR hair?</I><BR><BR>(he was right.  usually i keep the back and sides all the same length, but i\'d tried to put a fade in it that afternoon and fucked it up.  i didn\'t discover it until i was on the way to the store.  i was feeling around back there and found it uneven.  i didn\'t think it was bad enough for anybody to notice.  i got karyssa to fix it friday night.<BR><BR><I>why don\'t you go to a barbershop like a human being?<BR><BR>i told you - i don\'t like strangers touchin\' me.</I><BR><BR>[hugs and handshakes i\'m okay with.  nothing beyond that, though.]<BR><BR><I>you\'re weird.<BR><BR>yeah, but i\'m cute.</I><BR><BR>so, i didn\'t have much room to talk.)<BR><BR>i turned away from them and swiped my card and started punching buttons.<BR><BR>the italian one said, <I>i better not ever see you around here anywhere.</I><BR><BR>i didn\'t look up.  without moving my head, i glanced at the guy at the end of the counter.  he was black, about my size, maybe late teens.  he met my gaze and grinned.  i grinned back then looked away and finished punching the buttons.<BR><BR>i didn\'t look at them.<BR><BR>i said, <I>i\'ll be right out front.  why don\'t you come get a good look at me?</I><BR><BR>i looked up and turned and stared at them.  the printer on the cash register was ticking away.  the woman between us looked like she was trying to be invisible.<BR><BR>the cashier held out the receipt.  i took it and turned and pushed the cart towards the door.<BR><BR>outside, in the area to the right of the doors, there were rows of carts.  i pushed mine over there and lifted out the bags.  they were the plastic kind with the holes in them that made handles.  i arranged them so that the weight would be balanced:  orange juice and the bag with the produce in it on the right, the bag with the can of tomatoes and the pasta and the one with the plastic bottle of coke and the glass jar of capers on the left.  i picked them up and went over to the other side of the door and set them down and leaned against the side of the building.<BR><BR>i pulled the receipt from my pocket and read it by the anemic yellow light coming from the fixture overhead.  it said i had forty-four dollars and change left in my food stamp account.  they wouldn\'t put anymore in there until the tenth of march.  i folded it up and put it in my hip pocket, the one that didn\'t have my wallet in it.<BR><BR>(in the old days, when the paper stamps they gave you were worth fifty cents on the dollar on the black market, if i had had that much left, i could have gone to somebody\'s house and gotten twenty bucks worth of whatever i liked and then pawned something of mine or someone else\'s when the groceries ran out and i felt like eating again.<BR><BR>out of the three pawnshops in town, i usually took my merchandise to honest john\'s.  the proprietor, who was as much a thief as any of them, who was, in fact, so crooked that his name wasn\'t even john but larry, was at least funny as hell.  a little levity is always welcome in a desperate hour, and laughter makes getting shafted not as bad.  sometimes it was even worth it.<BR><BR>those were the days when the florida legislature had not yet enacted a statute that forced pawnbrokers to file police reports on every item brought in and its bearer.  the land was verdant and full of promise, and times were generally fat.)<BR><BR>the middle-aged woman came out and saw me and quickly looked away and stepped up her pace a little.  a minute later, out came the young\'uns.  i was about fifteen feet away from the door.  we stared at each other.<BR><BR>the italian one turned his palms toward me and shrugged.<BR><BR>he said, <I>wassup?</I><BR><BR>the redheaded one put down the budweiser.<BR><BR>i said, <I>i dunno.  what IS up?</I><BR><BR>the redheaded one said, <I>you\'re \'bout to find out.</I><BR><BR>the doors slid open, and the store manager stepped out and assessed the situation and popped back inside without saying anything.  we had all turned to look when he had come out, but now returned to the business at hand.<BR><BR>i took turns staring at them.<BR><BR>the italian one said, <I>wassup, punk?<BR><BR>i wouldn\'t be callin\' anybody a punk with hair like that.</I><BR><BR>the redheaded one took a step forward, then his friend did the same.<BR><BR>i said, <I>oh!  so it\'s gonna take both uh ya\'ll, huh?  if that\'s the way it\'s gonna be....</I><BR><BR>i reached in my pocket and felt my keys first, then the cannister.  i pulled it out and said, <I>who wants to get maced first?  step right up here and see what it feels like.</I><BR><BR>the redheaded one said, <I>oh, god.  what a pussy.<BR><BR>if you had the balls to let me fight you one at a time, i\'d stomp BOTH your asses.  now, who\'s the pussies?  i\'m just makin\' it a fair fight.</I><BR><BR>we stood there.  i stared at them, watching them both, alternating quickly between one and the other.<BR><BR>after a second, the manager came back out.<BR><BR>he said, <I>fellas, the police are on their way.</I><BR><BR>i said, <I>we aren\'t doin\' anything.<BR><BR>you\'re loitering.</I><BR><BR>the two of them looked at each other and started backing away.  i gathered up my bags and started walking backwards along the sidewalk.  we traded some more compliments.  they moved into the parking lot, walking forwards and backwards, talking to me, making promises.  they were headed toward what looked like a late-model cougar.  real late.  like late eighties.  black, with a patch of dead paint on the side.<BR><BR>when i reached the corner of the building, i fast-legged it around the side and then along the back of the shopping center.  their car had been a pretty good ways out into the lot, and i hoped i had enough time to disappear before they reached it.  i hoped they thought i had headed west (the direction i was going before i turned the corner) instead of east.  when i got to congress, i turned right, south, and started down the side of the road.<BR><BR>(there was a sidewalk <U>across</U> the road, but not where i was.  new port richey\'s really good at that.  it\'s like they\'re saying, <I>okay, motherfucker, if you want to walk on some cement, you\'re gonna do it on <U>this</U> side of the street.</I>  i think somebody must be getting rich off the taxes.)<BR><BR>i was hoping that they\'d think i had beat feet down one of the cul-de-sacs branching off of kentucky or tennessee or whatever the hell that avenue is.  i walked along at a pretty good clip, trying not to lose my footing, looking back every ten seconds or so.<BR><BR>congress is arrow-straight and tree-lined.  i passed a retirement community that sprawled over several acres across the road and, on my right, a couple of apartment complexes.  i was about halfway to indiana when i saw a car approaching from  the north.  when it was about seventy-five yards away, it suddenly started to accelerate.<BR><BR>i transferred the bags i was holding in my right hand to my left and, in the streetlight, saw the shape of the box of farfalle giving one of the bags a straight edge.  i reached in and pulled out the can of tomatoes and got a good grip on it.<BR><BR>as the car got close, it veered toward toward the shoulder where i was, and then the nearest wheel crossed the line marking the edge of the road, and the car came up on the grass.<BR><BR>i launched the can of tomatoes at the windshield and jumped down in the ditch.  the water came up to mid-calf.<BR><BR>i scrambled up the far bank.  the car drove entirely onto the shoulder and slammed on the brakes, stopping seven or eight yards from me.<BR><BR>i headed for the apartment complex that loomed to the west.  car doors slammed.  i ran in between two buildings, hung a left, entered a recess where i suspected a flight of stairs probably stood, found one, and hauled ass up to the landing as quietly as possible.<BR><BR>the fullish moon was peeking under the edge of the roof, illuminating everything except the corner by the door.  i eased over into it.<BR><BR>after a second, i heard, <I>he\'s dead.  he is fuckin\' dead.</I>  then there was something i couldn\'t make out, and then it was quiet.<BR><BR>on the landing beside me, there was a white plastic chair and a little round table with a plant and an ashtray on it.  after five minutes or so, i sat down.<BR><BR>i set the bags down by my feet and put my elbows on my knees and rested my head on my hands.  somebody in the apartment across the landing was playing something just loud enough for the bass to be heard.  it sounded like it might have been reggae.  whatever it was, the beat was turned around.<BR><BR>my gaze wandered over my surroundings and came to a stop when it lit upon the ashtray.  i picked it up:  a q-tip bent in half, ashes, and lots of cigarette stubs, including, like i thought i saw, three of the hand-rolled variety.  that were visible, anyway.<BR><BR>i got up and held the ashtray beyond the railing and upended it.  the butts dropped like bullets, and the ashes sifted down through the moonlight like sugar through a glass of tea.  now it was all on the cement down there.  somebody\'d have a present in the morning.  <I>hell,</I> i thought, <I>it\'s all for the greater good.  i\'d clean it up if i could.</I><BR><BR>i sat there for about half an hour.  to pass the time, i tried to remember the names of all the teachers i had had from kindergarten up through high school.  i knew about half, i guess.  i could remember a lot more faces than names.<BR><BR>a bit of eggplant was visible through a slight opening at the top of one of the bags.  i remembered a story that luis, my therapist, had told me that afternoon.<BR><BR>he said that the last time he tried to get clean (and succeeded), he felt that he needed to burn all of his bridges for it to work, so he held up all of his dealers.  (just for their money, not for the skag.)  then he went to rehab.<BR><BR>the recovery center was a christian outfit, and, every saturday, they went to different parts of the city to hand out fliers to let people know there was a way out.  a couple of months into his stay there, the pastor announced one morning that they were going up to  the bronx, to morrison avenue.  that was luis\' old stomping grounds.  he said he broke out in a sweat.<BR><BR>he explained to the pastor why he couldn\'t go there, and the pastor said that that was nonsense, that god would protect him.  and he told him he didn\'t have any choice:  he could come with them, or go home.<BR><BR>they went right to the intersection where the local checkmate outlet was that he had jacked two months before.<BR><BR>(he explained that checkmate was a heroin distribution network that ran itself like a business.  they had locations in neighborhoods all over the city.  \"checkmate\" was what they had printed on their baggies.  [\"checkmate\" is a hell of a thing to name your product, don\'t you think?  you could take that either way.  kind of gallows humor there.])<BR><BR>they weren\'t there long when one of the runners spotted him handing out fliers, and, pretty soon, the manager appeared with three or four guys behind him.  luis said he knew his time had come.  he said he thanked god for his life and told him he\'d be right there with him soon and asked for a merciful death.<BR><BR>the manager came right up in his face.  luis said he was shaking uncontrollably.<BR><BR>the manager said, <I>i don\'t know what you\'re doin\', but i want you to keep doin\' it.  more power to ya.  and i\'ll give you some advice:  if i ever see you coppin\' again, anywhere in the world, you\'re dead.</I><BR><BR>luis said, <I>remember that.  and remember what it says:</I>  <B>Fear not, for I am with thee.</B><BR><BR>but it also helps if you don\'t run your big fat mouth.<BR><BR>i went downstairs and saw that the car was gone.  i briefly looked beside the road for the tomatoes, but couldn\'t find them.  they were probably down in the ditch under a foot of water.  (i went back friday morning and still couldn\'t find them, had to go back to the grocery store and get some more.)<BR><BR>when i got home, i put away the groceries and wrung out my socks and hung them over the shower rod, then set my shoes in front of the vent at the bottom of the refrigerator, toes pointing out, under all the eggplants, the one above, the two below.</P>'),(3814,'Still no real purpose yet and still kinda feel like i\'m caught in a windstorm only  my bubble seems to have found a rudder of sorts so it\'s not quite so insane around here.  reality is kinda scary and way better than most horror novels i\'ve ever read.  weird shit happens.  i was pretty tight with my ex\'s girlfriend, i thought we were friends.  she decided to mindfuck me at a weak moment about a week ago.  i haven\'t spoken to her since.  \r\n\r\ni have little to no tolerance for insincere people these days....sick to death of getting treated like a doormat...sick to death of just taking it...but really too fucking nice to really do too much about it so i have just started cutting people off first go around depending on how bad the offense is.  it\'s lonely, but i don\'t bleed so bad anymore either.  '),(3815,'<P>\r\nHST died by his own gun.  I wanted to hold a wake.  I didn\'t think the\r\nwake would happen.  I don\'t own a gun.  I was sitting at Sonic\'s\r\nhouse, though, playing Starsky and Hutch the video game with me at the\r\nwheel and Sonic on the laser gun.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI announced, \"I want to shoot a .357.\"  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nShe said, \"OK, FRANK WE WANT TO SHOT GUNS!!!\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n \r\nFrank was in the next room and responded, \"What kind?\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAnn, \".357!\" and to me, \"His dad has an AK-47 too!\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nFrank, \"My dad collects illegal firearms.  He has an AR-15 and a\r\nsniper rifle that can shoot the daisy off a hippie\'s ear at 200\r\nyards.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAustin, \"Sweet!  Lets go!\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAnd so we drove from Ventura to Santa Barbie.  Frank\'s parents live in\r\nthe hills.  From their front yard, you can defend America from\r\ninvading foes landing on the beach with long range artillery.  There\r\nare mountains on the two sides and behind.  It is a sweet location.\r\nYou could even place the heavy shit on the hillside and take out troop\r\ncarrying ships before they even get close to landing.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nWe parked and hopped on the 4-wheelers.  I ran over a patch of flowers\r\nby accident.  Frank was doing circles on two wheels.  Sonic was\r\ndriving the John Deer mini truck.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nFrank raided the gun closet:  The AK-47, the .357, and a crazy tight\r\nlittle black number that kicked like a mule.  We rode down to the\r\nshooting range, a nook off the road with a beautiful back drop of\r\nrocky ledges and streams.\r\n</P><P><I>\r\nThis tribal custom made headlines in Ethiopia when a 14-year-old schoolgirl shot dead her rapist and would-be husband with an AK-47 assault rifle. She was acquitted of murder, to the astonishment of the conservative public. \r\n</i></P><P>\r\n\r\nAfter absolutely no instruction at all, I was holding a loaded .357.\r\nI had not shot guns since I was a kid.  I shot some kind of rifle\r\nthing that had a bolt action on it and nearly broke my thumb when the casing shot out the left side.  And, I\r\nhad the most awesome air rifle that great for shooting birds and my\r\nbest friend.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nWe hung up some targets.  It was your basic dart board looking thing.\r\nI pumped off a dozen rounds.  We took turns. But then...\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThe AK was loaded.  Chinese.  \"Made in China,\"  Shit, Bitch.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>There was only one AK-47 assault rifle to go between him and his five fellow recruits, so Abdullah started his military training under Soh with a piece of wood.\r\n</I></P><P>\r\nIt was not as heavy as I imagined.  On inspection, it was really a\r\nhuffy of a gun.  It was not a high quality.  It was hardly sweet.  If\r\nBrowing made an AK-47, well there would be quality.   No, this was\r\nlike a Mexican Fender.  Still, it had mystique and would definitely\r\nfuck you up like Jimmie Hendrix might with a Mexican Fender.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI had seen the SLA movie a month ago.  I have a shirt with Patty Hurst\r\ngripping an AK-47 ready to blow your capitalist pig ass off, daddy.\r\nPeople who hold these things have been known to want Americans dead.\r\nHaji, Charlie, Red Commie Bastard Ruskies, and this was made by the\r\nChinese, all wanting somebody like me dead.  Right now, over there,\r\ndead.\r\n</P>\r\n<P><I>\r\nDavid Hill, 21, is accused of killing the 29-year-old officer and wounding his partner, David Barry, after firing on them with an AK-47 assault rifle Easter weekend. \r\n</I></P>\r\n<PRE>\r\nPAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA \r\nPAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA \r\nPAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA \r\nPAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA \r\nPAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA PAKKA \r\n</PRE>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nIt was semi-automatic.  Our clips had 25 rounds a piece.  I smacked my\r\nclip on my helmet and said I was in the shit.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>Also the methamphetamine dealer riding around in his vehicle high on methamphetamine with several packets of methamphetamine, 5 pounds of marijuana and a fully automatic AK-47 assault rifle is non-violent.\r\n</I></P><P>\r\n\r\nWith the hand guns, you pop a round off and it hits the target, that\r\nis it.  With the AK, you hit the target and foliage for the next 20\r\nfeet bursts in various directions, just like you would.  It is a\r\nshredder of life, and that is just semi.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nBack at the ranch house, I called Sonic\'s dad.  He is a gun collector\r\nand a former G-Man who worked in various embassies.  He speaks with\r\nauthority.  I asked him to tell me about the AK-47.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nSonic\'s Dad on the AK-47: \r\n</P><BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n\r\nCurrently, there are two kinds of AK-47\'s in use, the old one, like\r\nyou were shooting, and the new updated one, the AKM.  Both are manufactured\r\nthroughout the Middle East region, the fully automatic kind.  Right\r\nnow, the middle east is a flea market of weapons from WW2, Korea, and\r\nVietnam.  You can get anything.  It is really great, I hear.  Come\r\nvisit, bring a gun!\r\n</BLOCKQUOTE><P>\r\n\r\nOn the radio in the last few months, they said 3 million AK-47 rifles\r\nare in the world.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nEwan McGregor and Charlie Boorman riding motorcycles around the world\r\nstay with a random Russian guy out in the middle of nowhere and he\r\ncomes down the stairs during the dinner party with a AK-47 in one\r\nhand, and a guitar in the other.  He proceeds to sing Russian Elvis\r\nsongs while playing guitar.  I think his AK was the automatic kind,\r\nprobably Russian.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>\r\nA man with an AK-47 assault rifle opened fire in a historic town square Thursday, killing his ex-wife and a bystander in a rampage prompted by a child support dispute. \r\n</I></P><P>\r\n\r\nI have an image in my mind of an Iraqi shooting off his AK into the\r\nair in celebration of something.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI am shooting a French kidnapper in a beret who is holding a blonde\r\nhostage.  I put one shot in each eye, one in the chin, and one in the\r\nheart that happens to also cause her bra to drop from one breast.  I\r\nam the hero.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<I>Kasal lost about 4 inches of leg bone after being struck repeatedly by bullets from an AK-47 assault rifle, and he still faces a bone-grafting operation in about a month.\r\n</I></P><P>\r\n\r\nThe AK-47 was really sweet for a huffy.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI feel bad for anyone on the receiving end of it.  This is nothing\r\ntoo.  The guns the US is using in Iraq will vaporize a person like a\r\nstar trek phaser, instant blood cloud.  Of course these are the bigger\r\nguns on the humvees.  As far as the receiving end of an AK goes, you\r\ncould do worse I guess.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<I> Two city workers were shot to death southeast of downtown Los Angeles Thursday by a co-worker with an AK-47 assault rifle, police said.\r\n</I></P><P>\r\nShooting this gun was an eye opening experience.  When our government\r\ncollapses and people take to the hills, I wonder how many Americans\r\nwill have Chinese AK-47 rifles.  I hope I have one with a grenade launcher.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSee you in the shit.\r\n</P>\r\n<P><I>(Itallic text from a google search.)</I></P>'),(3816,'<P><I>\r\n(Austin\'s new bike comes to us from the accursed lands north east of\r\nLos Angeles County.  There it did absolutely nothing in a retired fire\r\ninspector\'s suburb of the rural town\'s garage.  In during its life, it\r\nwas once dropped in the garage resulting in a small mark on the\r\nplastic cyclinder gaurd.  Afterwards, metal gaurds were installed to\r\nprotect the plastic gaurds.  The previous owner noted, \"I don\'t think\r\nthere is an accessory to protect the metal bar protector of the\r\nplastic gaurd that protects the cylinder head.\"  Other than that, the\r\nbike is a virgin from which no blood has been drawn.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThere was a brief 70 mile ride to get the bike back to LA, then\r\nanother 130 mile ride to get it home to San Diego.  That is where this\r\ninterview takes place.)\r\n</I></P>\r\n<P>\r\nAustin Swinney: So, I hear you just moved to San Diego. (I\'ve been\r\nhere a Year now.)  Where did you come from and how long have you been\r\nhere?\r\n</P><P>\r\nNew Bike: Yes.  I just got here.  Before that, I was in Los Angeles,\r\nlast night really, and prior to that, I don\'t know where I was.  Some\r\ngarage.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS: Interesting.  So you are a 2002, What happened between your\r\ncreation in 2002 and now?  Where has your career taken you, where have\r\nyou been, what have you done, and most importantly, WHERE DO YOU SEE\r\nLIFE TAKING YOU?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB: What year is it?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS: (2005)\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB: No shit, bitch, really?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS: Yes.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB: Hm.  I can\'t remember what I did in the last year.  All I remember\r\nis a sort of white room, being surrounded by crap.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS: That would be the garage.  That is Terrible.  Bikes go into\r\ngarages and people forget about them, that is where they die.  Some\r\npeople have like 5 bikes or more.  You came from a garage with lots of\r\nbikes didn\'t you?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB: I guess so.  Yes, I think they were bikes.  Yes, bikes.  I do\r\nremember something from when I was first born.  I went on a trip,\r\nmaybe.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS: That must have been where you got your 12,023 miles.  That was\r\nyour first owner.  That owner went somewhere, but you don\'t have a\r\nmark to show.  I am going to mess you up pretty good.  First of all,\r\nYou are not going to see any garage time any time soon.  Maybe never.\r\nSecondly, you may get dropped.  I\'m going to go ahead and apologise\r\nnow for that.  If you feel that you don\'t want to proceed with this\r\ninterview, I understand.  Do you need some time to yourself to think\r\nabout it?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB:  [Startled as if from a trance.]  Huh?  What were you saying?\r\nSomething about dropping.  Oh, OH! Oh yeah, you said you were going to\r\ndrop me.  Shit.  Really?  Can\'t you just not drop me? [as: no]  Oh,\r\nwhy not?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS:  Well, I\'m taking you on some dirt roads, things might get\r\nhairy.  I plan to cruise around looking for the shit.  Chances are\r\nyou are going to hit it.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB:  Hm, well alright.  I guess that is fine with me.  I\'m just a\r\nmotorcycle.  Hey, so here is a joke: what did one BMW say to the other\r\nBMW?  Nice shaft, HAHAHAH AHGAHAHAHAHA!  LOLZ..!\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS:  Hey, what is that smell?  Did you backfire?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB: HA!\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAS:  For starters, I\'d like to take you for a week in Baja.  That will\r\ninvolve some dirt.  Nothing serious, I am scared of the dirt.  But you\r\nmight touch down there.  After that is a ways up the coast.   Then\r\nthere is a wedding in Texas.  I\'m not sure I\'m taking you for that.  I\r\nmay just fly. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nNB:  Alright, whatever.  I don\'t have a GPS Nav, so bring a map.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nAS:  Nice chatting.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nNB: Ride me, oh, oh ... OH OH YAH BABY... OH RIDE me  rrrrriiiiiiiiIIIIIIIiiiiiide me!  LOLZ!!!!\r\n</P><P>\r\nAS:  Oh brother.\r\n</P>'),(3817,'<P>*  the chasco fiesta was this weekend.  it\'s this native-american themed thing they have every year in march.  chasco is a queen from some legend.  i don\'t know.<BR><BR>from thursday to yesterday, there was a fair by the lake and down by the river, rides, people selling crafts, live music.  and some native americans came and camped by the river, and they had demostrations of - what? - dances, skills.  lectures on i don\'t know what.  history, maybe.  and they also had food for sale.<BR><BR>there are some other native-american groups who boycott and protest, say the whole thing\'s racist.  i can see that.<BR><BR>but aside from all that, the main thing is the PARADE.  it goes through downtown, around one side of the lake, etc.  native-american themed floats.  and they throw beads.  i\'ve never seen any breast-baring, though.<BR><BR>the parade is really just an excuse for this huge bacchanalia.  i\'d say eighty percent of the adults in attendance are BLASTED.  everybody has an open container of alcohol.  this is illegal, of course, but i think the police either turn a blind eye, or there aren\'t enough of them to deal with it.  so chaos reigns.  people walk around with bottles and cans of beer just not giving a fuck.<BR><BR>they have three or four of these things a year.  christmas, etc.  i avoid them, just stay in the house.  they are all a pain in the ass for anybody who lives downtown:<BR><BR>there are fucked-up people who will try to park in your driveway and your yard.  there are fucked-up people driving and walking around, clogging the streets.  fucked-up people massed in the convenience store buying more beer and talking shit.  fucked-up people walking through your yard and leaving trash behind.  all of them wanting their ass beat.<BR><BR>(the men, anyway.  a lot of the women probably do, too, but i could never oblige them.)<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  so, saturday afternoon, during the parade, we stayed inside the apartment, made brownies, played dominoes, talked.  the oldies station was on.<BR><BR><B><I>all you need is love</B></I> came on.  then <B><I>a whiter shade of pale</B></I>.  a little bell rang in the back of my mind, but it was muffled.<BR><BR>and then the intro to <B><I>eight miles high</B></I>.<BR><BR>i said, <I>that can\'t be a coincidence.  well, i guess it could be, because most radio programmers wouldn\'t know about stuff like that.<BR><BR>about what?<BR><BR>all three of those songs sample other pieces of music.<BR><BR>sample?<BR><BR>not like you\'re thinking of.  well, i guess it <U>is</U> like what you\'re thinking of.<BR><BR></I><B>all you need is love</B><I> has part of </I><B>la marseillaise</B><I> in it.  so does </I><B>the 1812 overture</B><I>.  so they could be quoting either one or both.  they also sing part of the chorus of </I><B>she loves you</B><I>.<BR><BR></I><B>a whiter shade of pale</B><I> is based on </I><B>sleepers awake</B><I> by bach.<BR><BR>and the lick that roger mcguinn plays on twelve-string in </I><B>eight miles high</B><I> is taken from a john coltrane piece called </I><B>india</B><I><BR><BR>who cares?  you know what your problem is?  you talk too goddamned much.</I><BR><BR>she punches me lightly in the chest.<BR><BR>i say, <I>you\'d better run.</I><BR><BR>she takes off.  i catch up with her in the living room.  tickle fight on the couch.<BR><BR>we stop for a breather.  i say, <I>don\'t you feel better now that you\'ve learned something today?<BR><BR>i could have gone to my grave not knowing that, and it would have been alright.<BR><BR>i\'ll bet you that not one of those people down at that parade knows what i\'ve just told you.<BR><BR>do you think that makes us superior to them?<BR><BR>well, that\'s one of the ways.<BR><BR>snob.</I><BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  more music.<BR><BR>we had dinner at hers last night and were listening to some of her stuff while we ate.<BR><BR>she said, <I>this is what hendrix would have sounded like if he\'d made techno.<BR><BR>girl, what do you know about jimi hendrix?<BR><BR>i know about a lot of shit.  you\'d be surprised.<BR><BR>well, i don\'t think hendrix is really an apt comparison.  it has that same kind of lysergic quality, sure, but i\'m not hearing the kind of stupendous technical and emotive prowess that jimi had with his playing.  there\'s nothing analogous with this.  this is more like what pink floyd would sound like if they made techno, i guess.<BR><BR>it\'s funny you should say that.  they use pink floyd samples sometimes.<BR><BR>huh.  i bet there are some pink floyd songs that would make good techno remixes:  </I><B>interstellar overdrive</B><I> definitely.  probably some more.<BR><BR>i don\'t think i\'ve ever heard that.i wish i knew how to do that and had the equipment.  i\'d put it on the internet.  i wouldn\'t know where to begin, though.<BR><BR>i met a guy that lives in my neighborhood that\'s a producer.  i\'ll bet he could do it.<BR><BR>hit him up.<BR><BR>yeah, i have his number.  i bet there are some hendrix songs that would be good remixes, too.  i know, at the very least, that the beginning of </I><B>voodoo child (slight return)</B><I> has <U>had</U> to be have been sampled at least once.<BR><BR>i\'m not sure i know which song you\'re talking about.<BR><BR>you\'d know it if you heard it.  sounds kind of like scratching.  </I>(i try to mimic it for her.)<I><BR><BR>no, still isn\'t familiar.<BR><BR>i\'ll play it for you tomorrow night.  if i knew how to find mp3s, we could listen to it now, but since neither of us can do that....<BR><BR>we\'d be lost in the big city, wouldn\'t we?<BR><BR>you said it, not me.</I><BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  watched <B><I>william shakespeare\'s romeo + juliet</B></I> (as opposed to - i don\'t know - <B><I>matt mccord\'s romeo + juliet</B></I> [mine would be hellacious, too.  you\'d better believe it.]) after dinner.  again with leonardo dicaprio.  and again with the questions.<BR><BR>she said, <I>would you fuck him?<BR><BR></I>(hit pause.)<I>would i fuck him like he is, at the age he is, in this movie?  well, i\'d have to want to be with a twink.<BR><BR>what\'s a twink?<BR><BR>like he was at this age.  very young, looks barely legal, cute, small build.<BR><BR>okay, well, if you were in the mood for that, would you fuck him?<BR><BR>mercilessly.<BR><BR></I>she huffs.<BR><BR><I>i\'m just being honest.<BR><BR>okay.  whatever.  i don\'t blame you.  i would, too.<BR><BR>see.  there you go.</I><BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  the movie reminded me of an episode of <B><I>martin</B></I> in which the cast is involved in a production of <B><I>romeo and juliet</B></I>.  martin is romeo; gina, martin\'s girlfriend, is juliet.  gina\'s friend pam plays juliet\'s nurse.  martin and pam have an adversarial relationship like fred and esther did on <B><I>sanford and son</B></I>.<BR><BR>the only scene shown (part of a dress rehearsal) is the balcony one.  <I>romeo, romeo, wherefor, etc.</I>  martin climbs the ladder.  he and gina talk.  pam steps out on the balcony.<BR><BR>martin:  who\'s that?<BR><BR>gina:  that\'s my nurse.<BR><BR>martin:  she looks like your patient.<BR><BR>i just about died.<BR><BR>     *     *     *<BR><BR>*  i think it\'s admirable that baz luhrmann decided to stick to the original text, but it\'s probably a given that there\'s no way the target audience could have liked it.  he probably shot himself in the foot with that.  i hope they had a good opening weekend, because there couldn\'t have been too much word of mouth.  (that\'s how a movie stays in theatres long enough to earn:  word of mouth.  always.)<BR><BR>i don\'t know.  if the visuals hadn\'t been there, i wouldn\'t have known what was being said.  as it was, i barely got the gist of it.  (i\'ve never studied shakespeare.  not in high school or college.  oh, we kind of got a synopsis of <B><I>romeo and juliet</B></I> and <B><I>macbeth</B></I> in high school.  themes and whatnot.  but we never read them.<BR><BR>i\'m pitifully ill-read.  and ill-educated.  sometimes i feel like a rubber ball bouncing around in a room with a three-foot ceiling.  i\'m like one of those guys out in bumfuck mississippi [wait.  that\'s an oxymoron, isn\'t it?] making sculpture out of scrap metal.  one who eventually gets discovered by some exploitative dealer from atlanta.)<BR><BR>so, neither karyssa nor i knew what was being said, really.  rather, we were missing out on the subtleties, the full effect, the puns and wordplay and beauty of it that i\'ve heard are in there.<BR><BR>(but it\'s no great shakes of a plot, though.  i mean, really, if you think about it.)<BR><BR>she said, <I>i wonder if the audience in shakespeare\'s time could catch everything that was being said.<BR><BR>i don\'t know.  </I>(pause)<I>  listen, how would you like to get a copy of it and read it together sometime and see if we could puzzle it out?<BR><BR></I>she looks at me for a long second and grins a little bit.  <I>i\'d like that a lot.</I><BR><BR>zing.  i have my moments.</P>'),(3818,'Finally, I am learning to swim and the person I am becoming is more familiar terrain.  \r\n\r\nNo more crackheads, no more slacking off, no more limbo for me.\r\n\r\nI have been clean for 3 weeks now.  I know that doesn\'t sound like long, but when you\'re dealing with a meth addiction it feels like an eternity.  Within a week of quitting, I found a job I love and the picture is slowly becoming clear.\r\n\r\nI still have the feeling of waiting for road signs to the next path through the wilderness I am meant to walk. But at least in the meantime I am not floating aimlessly with nothing to do but wait.  I have no true regrets for the last year of my life.  Valuable life lessons learned and funny stories to remember when I get older, some of which I am already giggling about.  Life is crazier than fiction, but I wouldn\'t have it any other way.'),(3819,'<P>By the time we got there, the future had already arrived.  We\'d known that was a possibility, of course – a dream, even – the relativity lab had been on the verge of a breakthrough for decades.  But to wake from cryosleep and discover that the first manned mission to Alpha Centauri – our mission – had landed in a strip mall...</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Well, it was a bit much.</P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>In the 2030’s, as the popular phrase went, retro cool was so retro that it was cool again – and the American vision of the future circa 1953 was more popular than it had been at any time since 1954.  So when the European Space Agency’s public relations wonks got to work choosing a crew for the First Manned Space Mission to Another Solar System (I could hear the capital letters every time anyone talked about it), they knew the captain would have to be an American.  A barrel-chested, dark-haired, plain-spoken American with a devotion to duty even stronger than his simmering sexual attraction to the female who was constantly in need of his protection.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>It was a good image, anyway, and ESA-PR took full advantage of it.  A whole series of actresses and models paraded past on the second season of “So You Want To Be An Astronaut,” trying out for the role of the voluptuous female scientist.  I know it was wrong, but after the contest was down to the final six people I only voted for the one who wouldn’t sleep with me.  Turns out she was a pretty good scientist, too...not that it mattered, in the end.</P>\r\n<hr> \r\n<P>After four years our ratings were decreasing, so they decided it was time for the big finale, the actual take-off.  The viewers totally expected that our first take-off attempt would fail, both because outside scientists said so and because it wasn’t the end of the season yet.</P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>And there we were, final episode, all of the other networks showing re-runs or animal shows, going out live to the largest share of the viewing audience ever in the history of broadcast media...tearful goodbyes to our loved ones as the twelve of us boarded our ship...quick flash-backs to earlier episodes...and finally...an explosion!  One of the gantries, sabotaged!  ESA control room technicians tearing their hair out!  A daring rescue attempt, photogenic firemen jumping through burning rocket fuel to save the intrepid crew!</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Of course, the network knew about this all along.  So did we. “So You Want To Be An Astronaut,” already on top, made broadcast history again by scheduling a <u>second</u> season finale one week after the first.</P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>And there we were, final episode plus one, the other networks already into re-runs – going out live to an audience share beating even our previous record…tearful goodbyes to our loved ones as the twelve of us boarded our ship for the second time…quick flash-backs to earlier episodes…interviews with the security personnel making sure that nothing would go wrong this time…and finally...we were in space.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>At that point the computers took over, and we went to sleep.</P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>Five years later, I’m told, they figured it out. Before my ship had moved beyond the last measurable vestiges of our sun’s gravitational pull, some now-forgotten blogger published the plans for a faster-than-light drive, and amid both national security concerns from the remaining independent governments and intellectual property disputes from the multinational corporations…the secret was out.  People were going to other planets in less time than it had taken me to get from Portland to Brussels a decade earlier.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>With a flip, a twist, a quick sleight-of-handlessness from one scientific metaphor to another, the adventurous Human hordes hurtled outwards into the galaxy.  And if they’d stopped along the way, returned to real space in just the right spot to take a gander at the empty expanse and flush the pissoir, they might’ve seen us sleeping quietly in our ship, nearly forgotten.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>In fact, that may well have happened – to this day we don’t know whether the graffiti on the fuselage reading “yes, I wanted to be an astronaut” appeared during the voyage or after we landed.</P>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>The ground crew was supposed to issue the signal to wake us up a few months before landing, but apparently the funding had been cut and...well, we’re very lucky the automated systems were able to land us okay.  We’re also lucky that additional automated systems knew to wake us up after the main engines shut off, because otherwise we’d’ve been towed for blocking the Krispy Kreme drive-through.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We disembarked, and looked around.  It looked a lot like Earth.  Styles were different, but no more than we’d already seen within our lifetimes...and all we could smell was sweet doughnut frosting.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The first thing we heard, of course, wasn’t anywhere close to our script.  The morning shift manager, red-faced, yelling “you can’t park that here!”</P>\r\n\r\n<P><i>to be continued, perhaps...</i></P>'),(3820,'<P>sitting in kiki\'s driveway in the cutlass.<BR>listened to <B><I>volunteers</B></I> twice already.<BR>is it a tuesday? does it matter?<BR>days are an esoteric concept<BR>in The Eternal Hour of Need.<BR>there are only Good Minutes<BR>and Long Minutes.<BR><BR>around five aye em every morning,<BR>he puts a sign inside the screen<BR>of the picture window beside his door:<BR><BR><B><CENTER>go Away!<BR>I will call The Police!</CENTER></B><BR><BR>(i always used to think,<BR><BR><I>sting isn\'t gonna do shit about it, kiki.<BR>in fact, he will HELP me with his superpowers.</I>)<BR><BR>he puts the sign up<BR>whenever he feels satiated, i guess.<BR>could be four; could be six.<BR>you roll up and see it,<BR>feels like you lost the lottery by one number.<BR><BR>one time i knocked anyway.<BR>for maybe ten minutes.<BR>he finally pulled the curtain back,<BR>showed me a thirty-eight.<BR><BR>at about a quarter after ten,<BR>he appears in the carport, fires one up.<BR><BR>(carolyn doesn\'t like him to smoke cigarettes in the house,<BR>but a bale of weed a day is no problem.)<BR><BR>he walks out and sits on the trunk of his towncar.<BR>i\'m parked halfway between the street and the house.<BR>he beckons me in.<BR><BR>this is The Quarter.<BR>at one point, who knows when,<BR>the dirt lanes here became city streets,<BR>and the residents got to choose the names.<BR><BR>there\'s school street.<BR><BR>easy street.<BR>(the downtrodden often have a dark [har] sense of humor.)<BR><BR>there\'s lincoln avenue.<BR>(just to remind certain people.)<BR><BR>there\'s summit drive for someday.<BR><BR>torrance used to live in those apartments over on summit.<BR>i went there one day about two weeks after he lost his job<BR>at the rock mine.<BR><BR>the landlord never evicted anybody,<BR>just took off your front door and let you fight it out with The Elements<BR>and The Vermin.<BR><BR>i walked in, and he was on his bed,<BR>playing his tenor saxophone.<BR><BR>he could improvise, jam beautifully,<BR>but the only straight songs i ever heard him play<BR>were <B><I>the star-spangled banner</B></I><BR>and also <B><I>barbara ann</B></I><BR>and <B><I>take the a train.</B></I><BR><BR>(the last two comprised the marching band\'s halftime show<BR>during the fall of his junior year,<BR>shortly into which he dropped the class but kept the sax,<BR>ditching his contract with makin\' music.<BR><BR>[it\'s up on the square,<BR>has had the same accordion, cello, congas in the window<BR>for twenty years or so<BR>{or maybe they\'ve been replaced?<BR>no,the accordion keys are as yellow as an old<BR>three-pack-a-day man\'s teeth.}<BR><BR>a hundred years {or more} ago, there was a drugstore there.<BR><BR>everybody in town<BR>used to go up to the square<BR>on saturday night<BR>and, among them, they\'d think,<BR><BR><I><CENTER><U>you</U> i will marry.<BR><U>you</U> i have missed.<BR><U>you</U> i just saw<BR>at the hardware store<BR>this morning.<BR><BR><U>you</U> i\'ll see in church.<BR><U>you</U> i\'ll see in court.<BR><U>you</U> i will kill<BR>in a knife fight<BR>this evening.</CENTER></I><BR><BR>looking around at everybody,<BR>i\'d think,<BR>(without knowing i was thinking it):<BR><BR><I><CENTER>the same people all the time -<BR>this is heaven.</CENTER></I><BR><BR>and:<BR><BR><I><CENTER>the same people all the time -<BR>this is deadly.</CENTER></I><BR><BR>{every once in a blue moon, a man driving a purple sedan<BR>would come through town on saturday night<BR>and stop to eat at the cottage dinette.<BR><BR>he was the only outsider ever among us then,<BR>except for the occaisonal out of town relative<BR>or a bunch of mean boys from dade city.<BR><BR>one night, my grandfather went over there and talked to him<BR>and found out that he cleaned pipe organs for a living.<BR><BR>my grandfather could talk to anybody.}<BR><BR>one night in the summer one year,<BR>pete, the druggist,<BR>was either drunk<BR>or had been slipping into his inventory.<BR><BR>usually, only us kids got ice cream<BR>{chocolate, chocolate, strawberry},<BR>but, for some reason that night, mom and dad wanted some, too.<BR><BR>we all got up on stools at the counter and gave our orders.<BR><BR>pete, waving the scoop around<BR>like he was conducting an orchestra,<BR>looking each of us in the eye,<BR>said,<BR><BR><I>well, now,<BR>the whole GOD DAMNED mccord family wants ice cream tonight.<BR>what about that?</I><BR><BR>we kids just laughed.<BR>we thought he was being funny.<BR><BR>{swearing was gravely forbidden around our house,<BR>so we thought it was hilarious anytime anybody did it.}<BR><BR>mom and dad didn\'t say anything.<BR>dad paid, and we ate,<BR>and pete wandered off among the canes and walkers.<BR><BR>after that, we went to the drive-in.<BR>the seven o\'clock show<BR>was always something kids could see.<BR><BR>{a few years later pete was found shot dead<BR>on the tracks under the bridge.<BR><BR>The Police thought it was somebody from Out Of Town,<BR>but who knows how they came to that conclusion.<BR><BR>later on, it was discovered<BR>that there was some Funny Business with The Stuff he had in stock.}<BR><BR>{pete lived with his mother in that little white house<BR>on the corner of ellen and main<BR>that fell under the water tower\'s shadow<BR>around four in the afternoon in the summer.<BR><BR>i know it had to be right around then,<BR>because by five-thirty,<BR>it would hit the vacant lot several blocks east of there<BR>where we\'d sit in your car every afternoon<BR>from four-thirty, when you got off at the theatre<BR>- sweeping up after the kiddie matinee -<BR>to six, when you had to go home.<BR><BR>you\'d pick me up<BR>- it was after the wreck and before the new car -<BR>(and i was free at four-thirty,<BR>so it had to be during that two, three-week window<BR>between the job at the rock mine<BR>and the one at the hardware store.<BR>it was in the middle of july, the purest month, untouched by school),<BR>and we\'d go over there and get high and be silly<BR>and listen to whatever we thought certified us<BR>as The Coolest People In Town.<BR><BR>do you remember that?<BR><BR>the cops never bothered us.<BR>we\'d never get away with that today.<BR><BR>neither one of us is there anymore.<BR>(well, i know I\'M not.<BR>if YOU are,<BR>you\'re doing a very good job of concealing yourself.)<BR><BR>i had a dream about you this morning:<BR><BR>you gave me your number in a booth at the a & w.<BR>then you left.<BR><BR>it was on a folded-up piece of paper.<BR>i looked down and saw that there were triangles and half-circles<BR>cut out of the edges.<BR><BR>when i unfolded it,<BR>there was a hole where your number had been written.<BR><BR>i had a dream about you this morning.<BR>i got up and wrote this poem.}])</P>'),(3821,'<i><P>It was March of 2005 when Congress acted to save the life of Terri Schiavo, who had already been clinically brain-dead for fifteen years.  In their wisdom, our elected officials decided to side with her parents, who insisted that continued prayer would save their daughter, rather than her husband, who said that Terri herself would never have wanted to be kept alive in that state.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>By 2007, Terri Schiavo -- the first person to have her entire life controlled solely by the Federal Government -- was a popular choice to be a write-in candidate for President amongst many faith-based conservatives.  In an attempt to capture some of that momentum, Congress passed the \"Right to Pray Act,\" which conservative commentators described as \"finally confirming the God-given right to continue praying for a loved one\'s life, even after multiple failures of medical science.\"  One commentator even went so far as to say that the liberal elite doctors wouldn’t get away with murdering people anymore.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>After a number of constitutional challenges failed, it became simply obvious that nobody was allowed to die so long as there was one person left alive to pray for them.  Dozens of churches reorganized themselves to pray full-time for the sick and dying, many of whom had been forgotten during the fights against communism, abortion, homosexuality, and Hollywood.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>This story takes place fifty years later.</P></i>\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>John met a man in a barbershop yesterday, and prayed for him as I bound his wounds and connected the automatic defibrillator to his chest.  \"You’ll be all right now,\" I said, loving him even though he was a sinner – as evidenced by the sign he wore.  It said \"I have inoperable cancer, please let me die.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"As Jesus was resurrected, so shall you be.\"  John was good with the flowery words -- his father was a minister.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"You think that guy was the barber?\" I asked John as we wandered down MLK Boulevard, which was named after a famous preacher from the 1960’s.  \"I could use a haircut.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>John replied, \"we’ll check back in a few days and find out.  He might not be happy to see us, but he’ll be happy to be alive.\"  John’s faith is strong, and it sustains us both in these dark times.</P>\r\n<P>\"Amen.\"\r\n<hr>\r\n<P>We couldn’t go back to the barbershop right away, because there was a prayer vigil for the death cult that had been hiding out in the Oakland hills.  John’s father always told us that it was okay to kill people like that to protect the American Way of Life, and we kept on doing it even though God had abandoned John’s father, ignoring our prayers and letting him die in that accident last year.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"My father may have been a flawed man,\" John muttered as he handed out the ammunition, \"but he was right about this -- and so are we.\"  John’s a lot like his father.  I think he’ll be a preacher some day.  Sometimes he already is.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Ten of us set forth on the prayer vigil, each carrying three weapons and a Crucifix.  My cousin Samuel led the way.  This was his first vigil, and he was excited.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The death cult lived in an abandoned university building, surrounded by books and old computers and junk.  We peered through the windows and saw an old man on a bed inside, coughing.  \"Do you think they’re going to kill that man?\" Samuel whispered to me after we moved further away.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"If they are, I guess we’d better get in there before they do,\" I replied, and went to confer with John while the others spread out to the other windows.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"This changes things,\" John said with an air of frustration.  \"I had hoped to just set the books on fire and get away quickly, but now it looks like we have to rescue the old man.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"But couldn’t some of them have gotten away from the fire too?\" I asked, surprised.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Sure, but they’d’ve been burned and then we could’ve saved them.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"So how do we save that guy?\" I asked.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Two of us will have to go inside and get him,\" John said determinedly.  \"We’ll set fire in only one part, and use that as a distraction.  Then when the old man is safe from dying, we’ll torch the rest of it and get away.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We got everyone back from the windows and decided that Samuel and John would go inside, while I’d go with Samuel’s cousin George to set the fire while the others stayed back, ready to light up the rest of the place as soon as Samuel and John and the old man got free.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Are we really sending all of these people to burn in hell?\" Samuel whispered to John as they moved off.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"That’s in God’s hands,\" John recited solemnly, and then they were too far away for me to hear any more.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>We waited impatiently for another half hour or so, watching and quietly praying, until the people inside moved away from the old man, over to their table.  They had candles lit, and were serving food.  I could see John around the corner of the building; he finished his final prayers, and motioned for me to get started.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>George carried a half-full bottle of vodka that we’d prepared, and he lit it on fire as we ran up.  I carried a hunk of broken concrete, almost as big as my head, and heaved it at the window, which shattered nicely.  With perfect timing, George threw the bottle.  I didn’t see the fire catch hold of the books, though -- I was watching the death cult to see what they’d do.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>As we had expected, all of them (except the old man, of course) jumped up and ran towards the fire. I saw Samuel and John run inside to get the old man from the bed -- but then a woman inside saw me, and yelled something I didn’t bother to hear.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>After that it was chaos.  People running in all directions, vodka bottles flaming.  After a while I found John struggling to carry the old man by himself.  He didn’t know what had happened to Samuel.  We found a ravine, and put the old man down on the ground to catch our breath and decide what to do next.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"What were those people doing, anyway?\" I asked John, but the old man heard too.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"They were -- they were praying,\" he stammered painfully.  \"Praying for me, and for civilization.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"But...I thought you death cult people hated prayer,\" I asked.  \"I thought you only used doctors, and science.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>He could hear the disdain in my voice as I used those words -- doctors, and science -- and shook his head ruefully.  \"Death cult?  Ha!  There used to be medicine, but you destroyed it.  All we had left was prayer.\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>I looked at John for guidance, but he seemed as shocked as I was.  What had we been fighting for, then?  \"Does that mean we won?  There’s no more science?\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"Yes,\" the old man said, not even looking at us anymore.  \"We pray for life, or for death, it doesn\'t matter which one anymore.  Blind faith has won out over reason, over intellect, over everything that makes Humanity great.\"  And then he died, and we did nothing to stop him.</P>\r\n'),(3822,'<P><I>It is an illusion that we were ever alive,<BR>Lived in the houses of mothers, arranged ourselves<BR>By our own motions in a freedom of air.<BR><BR>Regard the freedom of seventy years ago.<BR>It is no longer air.  The houses still stand,<BR>Though they are rigid in rigid emptiness.<BR><BR>Even our shadows, their shadows, no longer remain.<BR>The lives these lived in the mind are at an end.<BR>They never were...<BR><BR>                                  - Wallace Stevens</I><BR><BR><B>the people who know you best</B><BR><BR>my sister and my niece picked me up sunday to go to brooksville.<BR>i waited for them on the stoop,<BR>walked over and picked a japanese plum<BR>and bit into it.<BR>they\'ll be ripe in a few more days.<BR><BR>mom called before they came<BR>and asked if i could get some baking soda on the way,<BR>said she\'d pay me back.<BR><BR>it was almost two-thirty when they got there.<BR>paige has to ride in the back seat<BR>until she\'s a certain weight or height,<BR>i forget which.<BR>she thinks that front seat\'s the promised land.<BR>i used to let her ride up there somtimes<BR>when i had a car.<BR>i made a pact with her to keep it a secret.<BR>her mother would have had my ass.<BR><BR>i told them that mom called.<BR>i said that the latin market<BR>was probably the cheapest place to get it.<BR>paige went in there with me.<BR>the young woman who owns it is always in there<BR>behind the counter.<BR><BR>we got the baking soda and some cokes and some skittles<BR>with my food stamp card.<BR>i asked the young woman,<BR><I>how is the life?</I><BR>and she said,<BR><I>very tranquil.</I><BR><BR>we chatted for a second.<BR>i looked down at paige as i was talking.<BR>she was staring at me.<BR><BR>the young woman asked her,<BR><I>do you speak spanish?</I><BR>paige looked at me.<BR>i told her what the young woman had said.<BR>she shook her head.<BR><BR>the young woman said, \"not yet, huh?\"<BR>paige grinned.<BR><BR>brooksville is a forty-five minute drive from<BR>the greater port richey/new port richey<BR>metropolitan area.<BR>on the way there,<BR>i discovered that i had bought baking powder<BR>instead of baking soda.<BR>so we ended up stopping at publix<BR>once we got to brooksville.<BR>(i said i wasn\'t going in wal-mart.<BR>i can\'t stand that place.)<BR><BR>my sister\'s about to finish nursing school.<BR>the parents have been supporting her for almost two years<BR>while she\'s been at it.<BR>she\'s already had a job promised to her<BR>once she passes the state boards.<BR><BR>she\'s open, buoyant, steadfast, kind,<BR>smarter than me in some ways.<BR>i could never have learned everything she\'s had to.<BR>maybe fifteen years ago....<BR><BR>she\'s going to be an o.r. nurse<BR>at the hospital where she\'s worked for eight years<BR>scheduling surgeries.<BR><BR>she\'s dated a string of men<BR>since she and joe separated five years ago.<BR>some of them paige has met,<BR>and the rest of us, just a few.<BR>she tells me about all of them.<BR>i don\'t know what she\'s looking for.<BR><BR>my niece is pretty much like her mother,<BR>just not as open.<BR>you sense that she has some secrets already,<BR>that her parents\' divorce has taken away<BR>some of her innocence<BR>and some of her optimism.<BR><BR>it\'s her spring break this week.<BR>she\'s staying with grandma and grandpa,<BR>going camping with them in the motor home.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the city</B><BR><BR>coming into town from the south,<BR>i saw seven new housing developments under construction<BR>by the highway.<BR><BR>the sprawl has always been out in the western part of the county,<BR>in spring hill.<BR>people have told me that that area looks like<BR>long island with palm trees.<BR><BR>the sprawl has stayed out there<BR>for thirty years,<BR>and now it\'s moving into town.<BR><BR>there isn\'t any urban planning going on<BR>that i can see.<BR>no parks, public spaces, bike lanes,<BR>just houses, shopping centers, wider roads.<BR><BR>they\'re building a huge hospital<BR>in what used to be a cow pasture<BR>outside of town.<BR>all the orange groves, farms, ranches, dairies,<BR>are eventually going to be sold<BR>for sums the owners will not believe;<BR>and the longer they can afford to hold onto the land,<BR>the more valuble it will be.<BR><BR>a wal-mart opened two or three years ago.<BR>since then, more than a dozen businesses, large and small,<BR>have gone under:<BR><BR>a grocery store, a department store, a drugstore,<BR>fabric, sporting goods, video stores,<BR>an optometrist, garages, a toystore, a tire dealer,<BR>more.<BR><BR>lots of places for lease.<BR>i don\'t know what will take their place.<BR>something will fill the vacuum, i\'m sure.<BR>starbucks, maybe.<BR>places catering to people who work in tampa<BR>and live in brooksville.<BR><BR>and there are other differences:<BR>the grocery store is now a big clearance-item place.<BR>there\'s an appleby\'s where the pizza place used to be.<BR>it had been there for a good twenty-five years.<BR>alice\'s restaurant has a new name.<BR><BR>the police department moved from downtown<BR>to out near the park.<BR>three trailer parks have become housing developments.<BR>they\'re adding on to the courthouse and county government offices,<BR>annexing more land to the north.<BR><BR>but there were still azaleas and dogwoods blooming downtown<BR>in the yards of the old houses<BR>on the last of the brick streets.<BR>maybe those will last.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the people who know you best</B><BR><BR>when i go into a restaurant in brooksville these days<BR>there\'s only a slim chance that i\'ll know anybody in there.<BR>the old inhabitants have died off,<BR>their ranks diluted by new arrivals.<BR>and there is virtually none of my peer group left.<BR>can\'t say that i blame them, really, though.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the house</B><BR><BR>i hadn\'t seen the place since thankgiving.<BR>the storms took out a lot of trees.<BR>(magically, none fell on the house or the cars.)<BR>dad\'s been busy since then getting them cut up and hauled off.<BR><BR>you can see the house from the dirt road now,<BR>whereas, before, you could walk around naked in the yard<BR>with no worries.<BR>dance, skinny-dip, fuck on a blanket, whatever.<BR><BR>the police got a helicopter in the mid-nineties.<BR>they got a show every once in awhile,<BR>but never did anything.<BR>i kept the sex hetero, though.<BR>and i quit once my uncle starting flying.<BR><BR>they\'ve had the house painted.<BR>the shed\'s gone, the concrete pad it stood on<BR>broken up and sodded over.<BR><BR>the legendary opium chamber has been dismantled,<BR>the screening i\'d put on the captain\'s walk taken off,<BR>the furniture and the weight bench sold.<BR><BR>and, then, of course, the living room was remodeled<BR>three years ago, i guess,<BR>the carpet we used to dance on taken up<BR>and replaced with wood,<BR>the shelves that held the stereo and the old records,<BR>the photo albums and souvenirs<BR>and little models of steam engines<BR>that my great-grandfather made<BR>taken out and everything sold or thrown out<BR>or put in the attic.<BR><BR>they sold the piano.<BR><BR>they built a sun room on the one side of the house<BR>that didn\'t have anything on it.<BR>it\'s carpeted and fully furnished.<BR><BR>all the rooms have new paint or wallpaper.<BR><BR>the yard is newly landscaped.<BR>lots of flowers and shrubs,<BR>ornamental trees.<BR><BR>the thing that really fucked me up, though,<BR>was that dad took a long piece of butcher\'s paper<BR>and pinned it to the wall by the pantry<BR>where all our heights had been recorded<BR>from the time that we\'d stand still long enough<BR>for him to do it<BR>until we were eightteen.<BR><BR>he took a ruler and copied the markings onto the paper:.<BR>us three kids, my six cousins, paige and my cousin\'s daughter<BR>and the german exchange student<BR>who lived with us during my senior year<BR>just long enough<BR>for the word to get around school<BR>and back to the exchange student coordination people<BR>that we were knockin\' booties.<BR><BR>(she was removed posthaste.<BR>proceedings weren\'t curtailed much,<BR>just made way less convenient.<BR>down the hall in your brother\'s old room<BR>is pretty convenient.)<BR><BR>then he rolled us all up<BR>and put a rubber band around us<BR>and put us in the junk drawer.<BR>and then stripped off the wallpaper<BR>that had been there for forty years.<BR><BR>they\'re getting ready to sell the house.<BR><BR>i think:<BR><BR><I>where are we going to go for christmas?<BR>some condo someplace?<BR><BR>what\'s going to happen to everything in the attic?<BR><BR>where are all those pictures going to hang?</I><BR><BR>they just got direct tv,<BR>so i\'m hoping that\'s a sign<BR>that they aren\'t going quite yet.<BR><BR>also, a nine hundred home development<BR>is going in to the north of them<BR>where the dairy used to be,<BR>so i think they might be hoping that that might raise<BR>property values some once the houses are built.<BR><BR>i\'m going to try to write my way<BR>into some money<BR>and buy it from them.<BR><BR>or at least give them some money<BR>and ask them not to sell it.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the people who know you best</B><BR><BR>my mom is kind, booksmart, guileless, funny.<BR><BR>i wouldn\'t be surprised if she\'s never had orgasm.<BR><BR>(and i wouldn\'t be <B><I>too</I></B> much more surprised<BR>if my dad\'s never had one, either.<BR><BR>my brother and i got our attitudes about sex<BR>from my grandfather,<BR>my mother\'s father,<BR>an incredibly witty,<BR>profane,<BR>nasty<BR>man.<BR><BR>he toned it down around my father<BR>but let us have the full force of it<BR>when we hit eleven or so,<BR>with plenty of hints and preparation<BR>long beforehand.<BR><BR>i don\'t know where my sister got hers.<BR>probably her friends.<BR><BR>and i don\'t know where my mom got hers,<BR>because her mother and her sister<BR>are pretty healthy.)<BR><BR>my dad is kind of broken<BR>(my mom, too, really).<BR>he was pretty attractive<BR>(at least he photographed well)<BR>until, at 23, a drunk driver hit him one night<BR>and killed his fiance<BR>and disfigured his face,<BR>crushed it a little,<BR>fucked up his nose.<BR><BR>drunk driving has been<BR>a recurrent theme in his life.<BR><BR>and i doubt that he\'s ever been drunk.<BR><BR>that\'s probably for the best.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the church</B><BR><BR>it\'s changed since i stopped attending at age eightteen.<BR>they use a live band now.<BR>there\'s a video screen.<BR><BR>we were there for the easter cantata.<BR>my dad was a disciple without any lines.<BR><BR>out of a full house,<BR>i saw maybe twenty-five people from the old days.<BR><BR>i\'ll be there again sunday,<BR>and then at my aunt\'s lake house in inverness<BR>for dinner and much egg concealment and recovery.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the people who know you best</B><BR><BR>outside the church,<BR>my grandmother took me aside<BR>and slipped me a twenty.<BR><BR>i told her there was no way i could take it.<BR>she insisted.<BR>she said she had some extra this month.<BR><BR>i tried again, but she wouldn\'t take it back.<BR><BR>i thanked her.<BR>it will certainly help.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the room</B><BR><BR>back at the house,<BR>we ordered pizza.<BR>i washed some clothes.<BR><BR>before we left,<BR>i went up to my room.<BR><BR>they had gotten rid of the desk, the bookshelf,<BR>the nightstand, the chest of drawers,<BR>the lamp.<BR><BR>the bed was still there<BR>as well as the two paintings my grandmother made for me<BR>when i was five or six.<BR>(there must be twenty of hers<BR>throughout the house.<BR>i\'ll wonder what they\'ll do with them.<BR>not to mention all the photos.)<BR><BR>everything else except for some hanging clothes was boxed up.<BR><BR>i rooted around and found a few books,<BR>and then my sister and i left.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>the bedroom\'s gone.<BR>the house is gone.<BR>the church is gone.<BR>the city\'s gone.<BR><BR>i feel like one of those refugees.<BR>i have no idea where my home is anymore.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>where are all those photos going to hang?<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><B>the people who know you best</B><BR><BR>my girlfriend and i<BR>have keys to each other\'s places now.<BR>and she told me the pin for the gate to her complex<BR>and the security system in the condo.<BR><BR>we discovered that both of us use the same pin for everything<BR>and came up with it the same way.<BR><BR>hers is to her voicemail, too,<BR>so i felt i needed to give her mine.<BR>mine\'s for the food stamp card, the library,<BR>and the voice mail.<BR>hers is also for the atm, security system at work,<BR>other things.<BR><BR>so we trust each other.<BR>that feels so good.<BR><BR>she came over after work last night<BR>with groceries for supper.<BR>i was in the bathroom.<BR><BR>i heard her try the knob.<BR>then she knocked.<BR><BR>i yelled, <I>use your key.</I><BR><BR>(she hadn\'t used it before.)<BR><BR>i could hear her trying it.<BR><BR>i yelled, <I>pull the knob a little.</I><BR><BR>she probably didn\'t understand a word i was saying,<BR>but she figured out.  no problem.<BR><BR>she came in.<BR>i pushed the bathroom door to.<BR><BR>i said, <I>hey.<BR><BR>hey.  why is the door locked?<BR>are you afraid somebody\'s going to come in here<BR>and get you?<BR><BR>yeah, some mean girl, probably.</I><BR><BR>i finish up and go out there.<BR><BR>she says,  <I>jesus, man.  what the hell?<BR><BR>what?<BR><BR>go put something on.<BR>why is it that every time<BR>i come over here lately,<BR>you\'re walking around buck naked<BR>with a pair of dress socks on?<BR><BR>is that a racial slur?<BR></I><B>buck naked?</B><I><BR>that just occurred to me.<BR><BR>who fucking knows?<BR>put something on.</I><BR><BR>i go over to the dresser<BR>(i keep all my folded clothes<BR>in a double dresser in the living room.)<BR>and get out a white t-shirt<BR>and the silk valentine\'s day boxers.<BR><BR>i said, <I>i remember telling a girl<BR>i was going out with<BR>that i walked around my apartment naked.<BR>and she laughed and said she did that, too.<BR><BR>it was during that little interval<BR>after you\'ve met someone<BR>and before you\'ve slept together<BR>but both of you know that you\'re going to.<BR><BR>man, that\'s one of the best parts<BR>of a relationship.</I><BR><BR>she looks at me.<BR><BR><I>you say that like you want to experience it again.</I><BR><BR>i didn\'t know what to do.<BR>i went over and hugged her.<BR><BR>i didn\'t know<BR>if it was a promise<BR>or a consolation.<BR><BR>later, after we ate and watched <B><I>amelie</I></B>,<BR>we were in bed talking, touching each other,<BR>not planning on doing it but did it anyway.<BR><BR>afterwards, i said:<BR><BR><I>i wish i could paint.<BR>i\'d paint you sitting on a hardwood floor<BR>against a white wall<BR>near a corner.<BR><BR>there\'s a window in the wall<BR>perpendicular to your\'s<BR>that\'s letting in slanting light<BR>through venetian blinds.<BR><BR>there are thirty or forty albums in a row,<BR>vertical,<BR>leaning against the wall,<BR>starting in the corner<BR>and ending close to you.<BR><BR>you\'re holding an album with your left hand,<BR>looking at it.<BR>it\'s tilted,<BR>but we can see that it\'s <B></I>let it bleed<I></B>.<BR><BR>in your right hand,<BR>you\'re holding a big fat burning dubois.<BR>the smoke\'s spiralling upward<BR>through the layers of light.<BR>there\'s a plastic black ashtray<BR>on the floor by your leg.<BR><BR>you\'re wearing that t-shirt<BR>that you wear in bed sometimes<BR>that\'s almost the color of your eyes.<BR><BR>you\'re looking down at the album,<BR>but we can still see your face.<BR>your hair\'s hanging down<BR>over your left eye.<BR><BR>you\'re smiling like you\'re curious and amused.<BR><BR>i wish i could paint that,<BR>and it could hang on the wall forever.</I><BR><BR>i thought:<BR><BR><I>whether it\'s<BR>your wall,<BR>my wall,<BR>or our wall<BR>i don\'t know.</I></P>'),(3823,'For Ernie, collecting supermarket trolleys was not just a dead-end minimum wage job, It was a vocation. On some occasions he had been known to collect and return the trolleys of the adjacent hardware store during slack periods at the supermarket. If those trolleys were not in their respective trolley bays, there was one (and only one) man for the job.<br><br>\r\n\r\nHis job however was not the reason d’être you might imagine. Ernie also had an unhealthy interest in road haulage, a truck spotter if you will. Ernie could tell you everything you never wanted to know about a wagon. Where the company was based, where they were previously based, and what goods they generally exported to which destinations.<br><br>\r\n\r\nEddie Stobart exports Coca-Cola to Germany.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAs you might imagine, Ernie had an impressive collection of trucking paraphernalia. Frequently sporting an Eddie Stobart, or Maersk Sealand baseball cap, he would regale me with tales of truck drivers he had known and loved, conversed with, and no doubt bored rigid. Mugs, pens, caps, t-shirts, high visibility reflective safety jackets. His tactics were textbook.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt was often rumored that Ernie might be a cross-dresser; and as unlikely as it seemed the clues were no doubt there. The telltale vestiges of mascara and eyeliner, the smooth lady-like hands, the well attended fingernails. All easily unnoticed but nonetheless present under close scrutiny; And I couldn’t help thinking he’d look good in a dress.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor almost a year Ernie would tell me of his dream to be transferred to the Carlisle branch of the Supermarket. Other topics of conversation often included the exciting possibility of the supermarket introducing \"new-fangled\" coin-op trolleys. I felt it impertinent to point out that such a move would effectively render him unemployed. Nod and smile. Be agreeable.<br><br>\r\n\r\nEddie Stobart is based in Carlisle<br><br>\r\n\r\nA seven year old boy in the decrepit body of a single man in his mid to late fifties. Ernie\'s all-consuming fancies, his ham-fisted demeanor and his unhealthy penchant for Coca-Cola would never fail to brighten the tedium of my working day. I laughed at him, not with him; he was a nice guy.<br><br>'),(3824,'<P><B>October, 1976</B><BR><BR>It\'s Hot.  Not Sweaty Hot, though.  I can feel the wind from the ceiling fan on my legs.  I must have kicked the covers off.  I have on a pajama shirt and pajama shorts.  It feels like they\'re made out of the same thing sheets are made out of.  And Underwear on, too.<BR><BR>I open my eyes and sit up.  It\'s Dark, but you can see things a little, and there\'s light on top of the trees on the other side of The Yard.  The window\'s open.  It\'s Hot, but Mama said we weren\'t going to have The Air Conditioner on anymore.  She said it costs too much Money, and it\'s October.<BR><BR>The curtains are blowing around a little bit from the ceiling fan.  There\'s Circus stuff on them.  There\'s Pictures of Clowns and elephants and lions and tigers.  I\'ve got Circus sheets, too.<BR><BR>I used to be Afraid of The Circus.  I thought the lions and tigers would Get Out of that cage and Eat me.  Daddy told me he would Save me, but that isn\'t why I\'m not Afraid.  Because who would Save Daddy?  I just Know they won\'t Get Out.<BR><BR>Beside the window, there\'s a Painting of a bear that Grandma made for me.  It isn\'t a Real bear.  It looks like one from Cartoons.  Or Smokey the Bear.  It\'s Smiling.<BR><BR>On the wall by my bed, there\'s another Painting Grandma made for me about Outer Space.  There\'s Blackness and lots of Stars and Planets and The Sun and The Moon and The World.  And there\'s a Rocket on top of The World like it just Took Off.  With Fire Shooting Out Of The Bottom.  I liked it when we went to see The Rocket Take Off, so Grandma made me a Painting about it.<BR><BR>There are other Paintings from her in The House.  Mama and Daddy and Morgan and Timmy have some in their rooms.  And there\'s one Downstairs with flowers in it, and there\'s one about a bunch of mailboxes with a field behind them, and it\'s about to rain.  There\'s black and purple-looking clouds, so you know it\'s about to rain BAD.  And there\'s another Painting with a thing in it that they used to pull behind a horse on a farm.  It\'s made of wood, and it\'s all Broken, and it\'s sitting in some high weeds.<BR><BR>I get up and pull up the sheet and the blanket and put the pillow on top.  I go over to the dresser.  It\'s orange, and the carpet\'s green, and the parts of the bed that are wood are wood color, and the nightstand and The Toybox and the bookshelf are white.  There\'s a lamp on the nightstand.  Part of it is a Clown, and there\'s a big Clown made out of Material hanging on the back of my door that has pockets for all my shoes.<BR><BR>Grandpa dresses up like a Clown and paints his face sometimes when there\'s a Parade or when we have a Birthday Party.  He can do Magic Tricks, too.<BR><BR>The closet door\'s slid open.  I leave it open because I used to be afraid that there were Robbers in there.<BR><BR>The mirror\'s on top of the dresser.  Two of my teeth in front are missing.  The top ones.  The new ones haven\'t grown in yet.  I\'ve got blue eyes.  My hair looks like Timmy\'s, except his is blonde and brown, and mine\'s just brown.  It\'s combed straight down all the way around like his is, except that I\'ve got these two Cowlicks.<BR><BR>I think, <I>You looked different when you were a Little baby and when you were a Little kid, and you\'ll look different when you\'re a Big kid and when you\'re a Teenager and when you\'re a Daddy and when you\'re an Old Man, but it\'ll always be you.  And right now, you look like you\'re six years Old.</I><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>I go into the hallway and use The Bathroom and go into Timmy\'s room.  He has Baseball stuff in his room like I have Circus things.  I play Little League, too, but he got First Pick.  He always gets First Pick.  But not really, I guess.  It just seems that way.<BR><BR>Timmy (Driving Drunk, 1985) is lying on his back.  He\'s snoring a little, and he doesn\'t have any covers on.  And his pajama shirt\'s off.  His pajamas are like mine, except his are blue, and mine are green.<BR><BR>I go over to him and flick his titty real hard.  He jumps and opens his eyes and comes out of the bed like a Jack-In-The-Box and tackles me.  We wrestle around.  He gets on top of me and pushes me down into the floor.  It hurts a little.<BR><BR>He says, <I>Now say you\'re Sorry.<BR><BR>Uh-uh.</I><BR><BR>He pushes harder.<BR><BR><I>Say it.<BR><BR>Noooooo.</I><BR><BR>He bounces up and down.<BR><BR><I>You better say it before you get Hurt.  It\'ll be Your Fault.</I><BR><BR>It hurts too bad.<BR><BR><I>I\'m Sorry.  Stop it.<BR><BR>And </I><B>I\'ll Never do it again.</B><BR><BR><I>I\'ll Never do it again.  Get off.</I><BR><BR>He rolls off of me, and we stand up.  I look at him like I Hate him.  I do Hate him some.  But it isn\'t Right.  He sure is Mean, though.  Someday when I get Big, I\'m going to Make him go to The Doctor.<BR><BR>We Stare at each other.  I turn around and start to leave.  He comes with me.<BR><BR>I say, <I>Make your bed.<BR><BR>Don\'t tell me what to do.</I><BR><BR>I wait while he does it, then we go to Morgan\'s room.  She doesn\'t have anything like Circus stuff or Baseball stuff.  She\'s got a lot of dolls, though.  Some of them she can\'t even Play with.  They\'re just for Show.  Maybe she can when she gets Big.  There\'s a dollhouse that Grandma and Grandpa made for her over in the corner.  It\'s two stories tall.<BR><BR>She kicked her covers off, too.  Her pajamas have Minnie Mouse on them.  She\'s almost four.  Her Birthday is November twelth.  Timmy\'s nine.  His is on March thirtieth, right before mine.  Mine\'s April fourteenth.<BR><BR>I tickle her in the sides.  She wakes up and pushes my hands away and sits up and looks at us and rubs her eyes.<BR><BR><I>Where\'s Mommy?</I><BR><BR>Timmy says, <I>She\'s sleepin.  It\'s Saturday.  We\'re gonna go watch Cartoons.  C\'mon.<BR><BR>I hafta go Potty first.<BR><BR>Okay.</I><BR><BR>We wait until she comes out, then we all go Downstairs.  Mama and Daddy Sleep Late on Saturday morning.  They Work hard all week long.  Saturday morning\'s the only time they can Sleep Late.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>It\'s almost Hallowe\'en.  Mama has Decorations up.  She has them for all the Holidays.  There\'s a Skeleton on the pantry door with arms and legs made out of folded paper.  There\'s a Ppicture of a Witch on a broomstick and one of a Ghost, and there\'s a big plastic Jack-O-Lantern by The Fireplace.  Daddy says we\'re going to wait til a few days before Hallowe\'en to get a Real pumpkin because it doesn\'t take long for them to rot in This Weather.<BR><BR>Timmy gets the breakfast stuff out on Saturday.  We put a chair up to the counter, and he climbs up and gets the cereal and the bowls out of the cabinets.  You have to be Careful, though, because all the chairs in the kitchen and dining room are real tall, and they tip over easy.  So Timmy gets up on it, and I hold it steady.<BR><BR>We\'ve got Count Chocula this morning.  We all Take Turns picking the cereal out at The Grocery Store, or we get in Fights.  Timmy and me do, anyway.  Whatever has the best Prize is the one we get.  Mama puts it in her Checkbook whoever picks.  It goes Morgan, me, Timmy, then back to Morgan.  She has our Initials in there:  MLM or MMM or TJM.  By where it says Publix.  That\'s the name of The Grocery Store.<BR><BR>Timmy gets out the cereal and bends over and puts it on the counter and raises up and gets ready to close the cabinet.  I pull the top of the chair back.  The front feet raise up a little bit, and then I let go.<BR><BR>He almost Falls, but he grabs the inside of the cabinet and keeps his Balance.  He Looks at me like he can\'t Believe what just Happened and jumps down.<BR><BR><I>What\'re you tryin to Do to me?</I><BR><BR>He Shoves me.  I brush his hands away.<BR><BR><I>I coulda got Hurt.  Bad.  REAL Bad.</I><BR><BR>We Stare at each other.<BR><BR>He says, <I>You feelin Froggy, huh?</I><BR><BR>I keep Looking at him.  He Punches me in the shoulder.  I Look at him some more.<BR><BR><I>Do Somethin.  Why don\'tchoo Do Somethin, you little Sissy?</I><BR><BR>I just Look at him.  He Slaps me across the face.<BR><BR>I suck in air and hold it, then it all comes out, and I start Crying.  They\'re both Watching me.  I\'m Crying Hard.<BR><BR>I say, <I>I\'m Tellin.  I\'m gonna Go Tell.</I><BR><BR>I start walking towards the door.  Timmy puts his hand on my shoulder.<BR><BR><I>Wait, wait.  I\'m Sorry.  Don\'t Tell.  Please don\'t Tell.</I><BR><BR>I brush his hand away and keep walking.<BR><BR><I>Go ahead n Tell then, you Damn Sissy.</I><BR><BR>Morgan makes an O with her mouth and slaps her hand over it.<BR><BR>I go Upstairs and try to open Mama and Daddy\'s door.  It\'s Locked.  Saturday morning is the only time it\'s Locked.  Any other time, if you have a Nightmare, or if you\'re Sick during the night, you can go right in there.<BR><BR>I knock on the door.  I\'m still Crying.<BR><BR>I hear Daddy say, <I>Just a minute.</I><BR><BR>After a little while, the door opens.  He\'s wearing a T-shirt and Underwear.<BR><BR>He says, <I>What Happened?</I><BR><BR>I go to Mama\'s side of the bed.  She picks me up and sets me in her Lap and Hugs me and rubs my hair.<BR><BR>She says, <I>What?  What?</I><BR><BR>Daddy says, <I>Stop Cryin n Tell us what Happened.</I><BR><BR>I Suck It Up.<BR><BR><I>Timmy Hit me, n he Slapped me across the face.  An he Cussed.</I><BR><BR>Daddy says, <I>An what\'d <U>you</U> Do?<BR><BR>Nothin.<BR><BR>Uh-huh.  What\'d he Say?<BR><BR>He called me a Sissy.<BR><BR>No, what\'d he Say when he Cussed?<BR><BR>I won\'t get in Trouble?</I><BR><BR>Mama and Daddy Don\'t Ever Cuss.<BR><BR><I>No.<BR><BR>The D Word.<BR><BR>Damn?<BR><BR>Is there another D Word?<BR><BR>I don\'t know.  Maybe.</I><BR><BR>He leaves.<BR><BR>I hear him Yell out, <I>Timothy James.</I><BR><BR>You know you\'re in Trouble when they say both your names.<BR><BR>Mama holds my chin and turns my head and looks at my face.<BR><BR><I>Yeah, I can see it.  It\'s startin to get Red on this side.</I><BR><BR>She leans back and Looks at me and doesn\'t say anything for a second.<BR><BR><I>Did ya\'ll have breakfast yet?<BR><BR>No.  Almost.</I><BR><BR>Then I hear Daddy Yelling, <I>You.  Matthew.  Come down here.</I><BR><BR>I look at Mama.  She Points to the door.<BR><BR>I think, <I>Well, maybe it isn\'t Too Bad.  He didn\'t say Michael.</I><BR><BR>When I get to the kitchen, Morgan\'s sitting in the chair.  He probably asked <U>her</U> what Happened before he asked Timmy.<BR><BR>Daddy says, <I>Both of ya\'ll come with me.</I><BR><BR>He starts walking to The Garage.  That\'s where all the Spankings are.  I start Crying.<BR><BR>He says, <I>Stop it.</I><BR><BR>I Suck It Up, and we get out there.<BR><BR><I>Timmy, get over here and bend over.</I><BR><BR>He Spanks him.<BR><BR><I>That one\'s for Hittin him.</I><BR><BR>Spank.<BR><BR><I>And that one\'s for Slappin him.  Men don\'t Slap.  Only girls and ladies Slap.</I><BR><BR>Spank.<BR><BR><I>And that one\'s for Cussin.  Now get back over there.</I><BR><BR>Timmy stands by me.  He isn\'t Crying.  He doesn\'t ever Cry when he gets Spanked.<BR><BR>Daddy Points at me.<BR><BR><I>And you, sir.  If I ever hear of you doin anything like that again, you\'ll get a Belt Whippin like you\'ve Never had before.  He coulda Fallen offa there n Broken his arm, or he coulda Broken his neck if he Fell just right.  You need to be aware that Everything you do has a Consequence.  You better Watch It.  Now, ya\'ll go eat breakfast.  I\'m goin back to bed for awhile.</I><BR><BR>We go back to the kitchen.  Timmy and I Stare at each other.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>One Saturday morning, we Turned On the TV, and it was like a Rainbow.  It had rectangles that were different colors on it.  We got Daddy up and told him the TV was Broken.  He came down and laughed and said we got up too Early, and we\'d have to Play a Game until Cartoons came on.<BR><BR>On Saturday, we Watch The Little Rascals and The Three Stooges and Superman, and then the Cartoons with colors come on:  Sylvester and Tweety and Bugs Bunny and Captain Caveman and The Wonder Twins.<BR><BR>Mama and Daddy come down in the middle of The Wonder Twins and tell us that when it\'s Over to go brush our teeth and get our Swimming things.  Then they eat breakfast.<BR><BR>When I\'m in My Room getting my stuff, Timmy comes in and tells me to look and see if his towel is in my chest of drawers Somewhere.  We go through all the drawers and start to look in The Closet.<BR><BR>He looks at me and says, <I>You aren\'t gonna Watch it.  You\'re too big of a Sissy.<BR><BR>You Watch me n see if I don\'t.</I><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We\'re all in The Garage waiting for Daddy to get Ready.  He\'s always Late.  We get in the car.  It\'s green, and it says <I>Ford LTD</I> Everywhere on it.  The lights in the front are like eyes.  They have covers over them, and when you Turn them On, the covers raise up and go back inside the car like eyelids.<BR><BR>The kids are in The Back Seat.  Morgan\'s in The Middle.  She\'s sitting on that seat for Little kids that pulls down from inside the back of the big seat.  (Nobody ever wore a seat belt.)<BR><BR>Grandma and Grandpa\'s house is close to ours.  It has a barn beside it and a little house behind it.  And there\'s A Big Boat in The Yard and a field by it where the cows are and a garden and a little pen by The Barn where Pig lives.  When we get there, we can see Grandpa (Cancer, 1986) in The Barn doing Something.  He\'s Mama\'s Daddy.  We go in there.  He sharpening a little curvy Knife with that long round thing with a handle on it.  He puts them down on the workbench, and we give him Hugs and Kisses.  I See that there\'s a piece of Material on the bench with four other Knives laid out on it.<BR><BR>Mama and Morgan go into The House, and we stay in The Barn.  Grandpa picks the Knife and the sharpener back up and starts doing it.<BR><BR>He says, <I>Whatta ya say, McCord?</I><BR><BR>Daddy says, <I>I say I\'m glad to see Payday and Sundown.<BR><BR>I hear ya.</I><BR><BR>I Look at all the knives.<BR><BR>Grandpa sees me Looking and says, <I>You gonna Help me, son?</I><BR><BR>I Smile, but I don\'t open my mouth.  It isn\'t really a Smile.<BR><BR>Daddy says, <I>Well, let us go in and Say Hello to Hilda.</I><BR><BR>Grandma and Mama are sitting in The Living Room talking.  Grandma has The Crossword Part of The Paper on her lap.  Morgan\'s Playing on The Floor.  They keep a Toybox in here for us.<BR><BR>We Hug and Kiss Grandma, then Tim and I get out the Hot Wheels cars and roll them across the floor.  The floor\'s made of wood.<BR><BR>After a little while, I get up and go to The Bathroom.  There are a lot of Pictures on the walls in the hallway on the way there.  Some are of Us, and some are of Grandma and Grandpa and their Families, and some are of Lynn and Tom, and some are of Mike.  Lynn is Mama\'s sister.  Mike is her brother.  Lynn and Tom live by where The Rockets go Up.  They don\'t have any kids.  Mike lives in Town over behind Publix.  He has a Girlfriend named Linda.  He has long hair, and it sticks out Everywhere.<BR><BR>There\'s babies and kids and Grown-Ups.  And there\'s some of when Grandpa was in The Navy.  He was in The War.  The second one.  Grandma\'s Daddy was in the first one.<BR><BR>The Picture has the Ship he was on in the back with all the men standing in front of it in rows.  And there are Pictures of Mermaids around the outside and a man with long hair and a beard, and he\'s holding a Pitchfork.  I\'m going to have to See who that is.  It isn\'t The Devil.  The Devil has Horns.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>This Summer, Grandma and Grandma took Timmy and me on a Boat Trip on The River.  We Camped Out for two nights.  Morgan didn\'t get to Go.  She was too Little.  She got Mad.  I don\'t Blame her:  if they got to do Something, and I didn\'t, I\'d get Mad, too.<BR><BR>When we had The Fire at night, Grandma and Grandpa Told us Stories.  Timmy asked him about The War.  The Scariest part was when a Submarine sunk his Ship, and he was in The Water for three days.  His Mama and Daddy thought he was Dead.<BR><BR>When he was Saved, The Navy sent them a Telegram.  The man took it to their grocery store Uptown.  When Grandpa\'s Mama was Old, she Told him that was the only Time she saw his Daddy Cry.<BR><BR>Then Grandma Told us about when her Daddy was in his War.  He got Hurt, and The Army thought he was Dead and left him there, and some of those ladies that are like Preachers found him and took him to where they lived.  He was there for A Long Time.  He couldn\'t Ever move one of his hands again.<BR><BR>He Finally Got Back to where he lived.  Everybody there thought he was Dead.  He went to where his Girlfriend lived.  It was Grandma\'s Grandma and Grandpa\'s house.  He knocked on the door, and when she saw him, she Fainted.  And then they got Married.<BR><BR>Germany was The Bad Guys in both Wars.  I asked Grandma how was Germany The Bad Guys if Opa (Heart Attack, 1972) was Fighting for them.  Was he a Bad Guy?<BR><BR>She said, <I>No, Opa was a Good Guy.  When you get Older, you\'ll Understand.</I><BR><BR>(I still don\'t understand.)<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Timmy\'s Gone when I get back from The Bathroom, I go Outside looking for him.  He isn\'t in The Barn.  I look in, and there\'s just Grandpa and Daddy talking.  So that means he\'s in Opa\'s House Looking at The Machine Shop.<BR><BR>When Oma (Intentional Overdose, 1947) Died in New York, Opa came to Brooksville to live with Grandma and Grandpa.  Grandpa built him a little house in back of theirs.  It has a front room and a bedroom and a bathroom in back.  He had his workshop in The Front Room.<BR><BR>When he was a Teenager, he learned all about Machines, and then when he was a little Older, he went to different cities and worked for awhile in each one.  And then The War came.  And after that, he couldn\'t open his own shop because Times were Hard.  Everybody was Poor.  He and his friend had to find metal in The Trash and make it into things people could use.  They Traded the things they made for food.<BR><BR>The door\'s open.  I can see The Machine Shop sitting on stands in the middle of the room.  I move my head a little more Inside, and I can see Timmy in there Messing with it.  We aren\'t supposed to Touch it.<BR><BR>I try to make my voice Deep like Daddy\'s and say, <I>Timothy!</I>  Then I come Busting through the door.<BR><BR>His eyes get Big, then he sees it\'s me, and he says, <I>You little Turd.<BR><BR>You are.<BR><BR>You better not Start it.<BR><BR>What did they do?<BR><BR>Started to paint a little bit.</I><BR><BR>The Machine Shop Really Works.  It has little men and little drills and saws and all kinds of stuff.  Everthing moves.  It has wheels up above the Machines that some rubber belts turn, and that makes Everything Go.  It didn\'t used to have belts.  There were long leather strings like shoelaces.  Mama and Lynn had to tie them because Opa just had One Good Hand.<BR><BR>It has little lights that Light Up.  I don\'t know how it does that, because it doesn\'t Plug In, and there aren\'t Batteries.  You just Hook Up an air hose.  On one end of it, there\'s a room that says Power Plant on the door, and at The Other End, there\'s The Office with The Boss Standing Outside Watching Everybody and Making Sure They\'re Working Hard.  It\'s all in a glass case with the top off.  It\'s about as long as Daddy and half wide as it is long.  Grandpa and Mike are Fixing it.<BR><BR>I Look at it for awhile, then I go back in the rest of The House.  There isn\'t Anything in here Anymore.  The Potty doesn\'t Work.  It isn\'t Hooked Up.<BR><BR>I go back to The Front Room and tell Timmy, <I>Let\'s go See the cows.<BR><BR>Go get Morgan first.</I><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We\'re all out by The Fence Looking at the cows.  The big bull\'s named Max.  And there\'s Mabel and Maxine and Marty and Margaret and Mildred and Babe.  They\'re all girls except for Max and Marty.  Marty\'s like a Teenager cow.  Timmy Told me he Saw Grandpa and Daddy and Mike Do Something to Marty One Time.  So the meat wouldn\'t be Tough.  After that, I had a Nightmare that they Did that to me.<BR><BR>They didn\'t Do that to Max, though.  They aren\'t going to Get any meat from him.  One Time, Daddy and Grandpa and Timmy and me were standing by The Fence Watching the cows, and Max Got Up On Top Of Mabel, and Grandpa and Daddy Grinned at each other, and I asked if he was Hurting her, and Daddy said, <I>No, they\'re just Playin\'.</I><BR><BR>Then Timmy started Giggling, and Grandpa said, <I>And just what do you Know about it, Mister Tim?  Tell us all.</I><BR><BR>And Timmy\'s face got Red, and he didn\'t say Anything.<BR><BR>Daddy and Grandpa come out to where we are, and Grandpa says, <I>Yeah, McCord, All Hands been Suckin\' on Babe.</I>  He meant all the other cows were Getting milk from her.<BR><BR>Timmy says, <I>Let\'s go See Pig.</I><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Us Kids go over by The Barn where The Pen is.<BR><BR>Morgan says, <I>Let\'s get some corn for him.</I>  So we go into The Barn where all the feed is.<BR><BR>Grandpa calls up to us, <I>What\'re ya\'ll doin\' In There?</I><BR><BR>I say, <I>We\'re gonna get some corn for Pig.</I><BR><BR>He says, <I>No, don\'t.  He can\'t eat Right Now.  Ahhh, I just fed \'im before ya\'ll got here.</I><BR><BR>So we go over to The Pen, and he comes to See us, and he Pokes his nose in between the boards and Grunts some.  Morgan bends over some and says, <I>Hey.  Hey there, Little Pig.</I><BR><BR>He isn\'t Little, though.<BR><BR>Timmy says, <I>Morgan, Pig isn\'t gonna be here Anymore.</I><BR><BR>She stands up Straight and Looks at him.  <I>Why not?<BR><BR>It\'s too Hot for \'im here.  He\'s gonna go live with some other boys \'n girls where it\'s not so Hot.  Up in Georgia.<BR><BR>With Mike and Donna?</I>  Those are our cousins.  We have some more besides them, but they\'re the ones we See the most.<BR><BR>He says, <I>No, not them.  Some other people.  He\'s gonna ride The Train up there.  They have A Special Pen for \'im.</I><BR><BR>She doesn\'t say anything for a second.  <I>On The Choo-Choo Train?<BR><BR>Yeah.</I><BR><BR>I\'m standing behind her.  I Look at him with my mouth open like I can\'t Believe what he\'s Saying and like I\'m Laughing, too, At The Same Time.<BR><BR>She says, <I>Oh.  Can we go See \'im Sometime?<BR><BR>Maybe.  Maybe when we go See Mike and Donna.</I><BR><BR>She Looks at me.  <I>He\'ll Miss us, won\'t he?  He\'ll be Sad.</I><BR><BR>I say, <I>Maybe Grandma\'ll let us Take A Picture of him.  Why don\'tcha go See.</I><BR><BR>She goes in The House to get Grandma\'s Camera, and Timmy goes over and gets in The Boat and Pretends like he\'s Steering it.  The Boat\'s named The Melyn.  M for Mike, E for Elsie (that\'s Mama\'s Name), LYN for Lynn.  You say it melon like watermelon.<BR><BR>She comes back out with The Camera, and Timmy climbs up on the boards and Takes some Pictures then gets back on The Ground and Takes some more.  Then we all go back Inside and Play, then me and Timmy go get in The Boat and Pretend, and then Grandpa drives his truck around to the side of The Barn where The Pen is.<BR><BR>Daddy\'s with him.  Grandpa backs the truck up to where The Gate is, and they get some boards out of The Barn and lay the ends of them on the back of the truck and the other ends on the ground in front of The Gate.  Grandpa opens The Gate up and says, <I>You boys go in there and Chase him Out Here.</I>  Timmy and me go Inside.  Pig Runs Away from us.  He\'s Scared.<BR><BR>Daddy and Grandpa stand on both sides of the boards, and we Finally Shoo him up into the back of the truck.  Timmy pulls the end of one board off the truck, and I pull the other one, and Grandpa puts up The Tailgate.  Daddy and me get in back with Pig, and Timmy and Grandpa get inside and slam the doors.  Timmy Always gets to Ride Up Front.  It isn\'t Fair.<BR><BR>I See that piece of Material all rolled up and sitting on The Floor over by Daddy.<BR><BR>When we drive through The Front Yard, I see Mama and Morgan standing on The Side Porch Watching.  Morgan\'s Waving.  And she\'s Crying, too.  I don\'t Think she Believes what Timmy Told her.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We\'re going to Grandpa\'s sister\'s house.  It\'s just right around The Corner.  We pull up to The Barn.  Mike\'s there, and Willard and Kenneth.  It\'s Willard\'s Place.  He\'s Married to Grandpa\'s sister.  He used to be in The Marines.  He has really short hair.  When it\'s Just Us around, Grandpa calls him That Son Of A Bitch Willard.<BR><BR>There\'s The Barn and The Trailer and a Pool and a garden and a Boat.  The Boat\'s called The Tension Easer.  Grandpa says That Son Of A Bitch Willard doesn\'t know how to name Boats.<BR><BR>Kenneth is Married to Grandpa\'s other sister.  He and Mike are hauling some chains and rope out of the barn.  Mike backs his truck up and stands on The Tailgate and puts the rope through a pulley that\'s attached to the middle of the top part above where the barn doors open.  There\'s a firepit dug in The Ground with A Fire going in it and a washtub sitting on top that\'s just about as Big as a pig.  Kenneth gets a smaller washtub out of the barn and puts it out where the rope is, not under it, but close.<BR><BR>Mike moves his truck, and Grandpa backs his under the rope.  Pig starts squealing.  He knows what\'s going to happen.  He\'s probably pretty smart.  Willard climbs up into the back of the truck and kind of sits on Pig backwards to hold him still and ties the end of the rope around his back legs.  He\'s making a lot of noise now.<BR><BR>MIke pulls on the other part of the rope, and Pig goes up.  He\'s squealing really hard.  It sounds like he can\'t breathe.  Willard gets down and lets down the tailgate and Grandpa pulls the truck away and parks it.  He gets out and picks up the bundle of material from the back and comes over to us and lays it down on the ground and unrolls it.  Kenneth moves the empty washtub under Pig\'s head.<BR><BR>He tells Mike to hold him steady and picks up the little short curvy knife and walks over to Pig and puts one hand on his belly and stabs him in the top part of his chest.  Pig screams.  He sounds just like a person.  He sounds just like a little boy.<BR><BR>I suck in air and hold it, and then it all comes out, and I start crying hard.  They all look at me.  Then they all look away.  Blood is running down into the washtub.  Pig is still squealing some.  Then he stops.  Willard is still looking at me.  I\'m still crying hard.  I run away behind the barn and sit against the side of it.  After awhile, I stop crying.  I sit there a long time.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Tim comes around the corner and stands in front of me and looks at me, then he sits down beside me.  He doesn\'t say anything.  We sit there, and then we go into the trailer and play with Morgan and some of our cousins.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We have hamburgers for lunch, and I look at mine and think about where it came from.  It\'s good, though.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We have to wait half an hour after we eat to go swimming, so we go in the living room and listen to songs on the player piano.  They\'re songs from a long time ago.  You put rolls of paper with holes in it in the machine, and it blows little puffs of air, and when it finds a hole, it plays that key.<BR><BR>When it\'s time, Timmy and me go into one of the bedrooms to change.  He starts telling me about it.<BR><BR><I>And after all the blood came out, they put him into that tub full of water and scraped all his hair off, and then they put a rod through both his back legs and hooked a chain to it and pulled him back up there, and then they cut him all the way up the middle, and his guts fell out.  That\'s when I left.</I><BR><BR>I really don\'t want to hear about it.<BR><BR>I say, <I>I don\'t want to see that again.<BR><BR>Me, either.  I didn\'t know....</I><BR><BR>The door opens, and Mike pokes his head in.<BR><BR>He says, <I>What\'re ya\'ll doin\' in here?  Checkin\' out pee-pees?</I><BR><BR>Timmy puts his hand up and hangs him The Bird.  Mike comes in and shuts the door behind him.<BR><BR><I>Oh, my goodness.  Oh.  My.  Goodness.  What is your Mama gonna say?  Come here!</I><BR><BR>He grabs Timmy and holds him upside down.  We both start laughing.<BR><BR>Mike looks at me.  <I>Come get his head.  Come work on that head.</I><BR><BR>We\'re laughing a lot.  I put my hands on both sides of his head and move it back and forth.  Not Hard, though.  Soft.  I Look at his eyes, and it\'s like Everything stops.  We Look at each other\'s eyes for a second.  Brother.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>I sit by the side of the Pool for A Long Time.  I don\'t go in The Water.  Everybody else is Playing.  They\'re going off The Diving Board and going down The Slide.  Some of our cousins are here.  Timmy and me are the only boys.<BR><BR>Timmy Swims over and pushes out of The Water and sits beside me.<BR><BR>He says, <I>You aren\'t gonna Swim?  You aren\'t Ever do Anything again?</I><BR><BR>I Look up at him.<BR><BR>He says, <I>C\'mon.</I><BR><BR>I Jump in, and we Play Marco Polo with Kim and Stephanie.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We Swim, then we get out and Play for A Long Time.  Hide and Go Seek and Freeze Tag and Duck, Duck, Goose and Red Rover and Red Light, Green Light.  Then we all eat again, and after we eat, all The Grown-Ups sit out on The Porch and Tell Stories and talk.  I sit there and Listen to them while the rest of the kids Play because it\'s Good to Know Stories so in case Everybody\'s Bored, you can Make Time Go By Faster.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>There\'s Mama and Daddy and Grandma and Grandpa and Mike.  And then there\'s Margaret (Cancer, 2004).  She\'s Grandpa\'s sister.  She\'s Married to Willard.  And there\'s Mildred (Cancer, 2005).  She\'s his other sister.  She\'s Married to Kenneth.  Both his sisters are Twins.  Not The Kind That Look The Same.  The Other Kind.<BR><BR>They both talk A Lot.  Kenneth doesn\'t say much.  Grandma says that\'s because he doesn\'t have The Chance.  Willard talks A Lot, though.  I don\'t know how that Works.<BR><BR>And there\'s Aunt Ruby (Alzheimer\'s Disease, 1988).  She\'s Grandpa\'s Mama(Alzheimer\'s Disease, 1968)\'s Little sister.  Grandpa\'s Mama Died before I was Born.  I can just barely Remember his Daddy (Heart Attack, 1973), though.  They\'re Buried in The Graveyard down The Road from Grandma and Grandpa\'s house.<BR><BR>Mama and Grandma and Lynn and Us Kids walk down there sometimes on Sunday Afternoon when The Men turn off Tne Football Game and lay down on The Living Room Floor and Take A Nap.  We can\'t Figure Out why they do that when there\'s beds all over The House.<BR><BR>Grandma\'s and Grandpa\'s Mamas and Daddys are down there, all next to each other.  When Grandpa worked for The Funeral Home, he bought a bunch of Graves, so they\'re there for All Of Us.  We were down there, and Tim was standing on An Empty One, and Mama told him to get off, that it wasn\'t Nice.  He said, <I>It\'s Empty.</I>  And she said, <I>It doesn\'t matter.</I><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Aunt Ruby\'s talking about Her New Boyfriend.  His name\'s Elmer.  Margaret says, <I>Where\'dja Meet \'im?</I><BR><BR>And Ruby says, <I>Oh, at that Juke Joint down at The Crossroads where we have Those Scandalous Parties.</I><BR><BR>Grandpa says, <I>That\'s that place where you walk in Backwards so they Think you\'re Leavin\'.</I><BR><BR>Willard says, <I>Or you put you hat on Backwards.  Or you walk in Sideways.</I><BR><BR>Grandpa says, <I>Yes, Willard.  Any of those Methods are Worth A Shot.</I><BR><BR>I\'m Pretty Sure Grandpa doesn\'t Think Willard is too Bright.<BR><BR>I don\'t know what Scandalous Means, but I Know what a Juke Joint is.  Grandpa Told me it\'s where there\'s a Jukebox that\'s Playing Shit-Kickin\' Music and Everybody\'s Drinking Beer and Everybody\'s got Cigarettes and The Ones Who Drank Too Much Beer are Dancing.  He doesn\'t go there.  The only Music he Listens to is Hank Williams.  <B><I>You\'re Cheatin\' Heart</I></B> and one about somebody Lost on a road Somewhere.<BR><BR>Everybody\'s Drinking Beer except Mama and Daddy.  They don\'t Ever do that.  And Everybody\'s got Cigarettes except Mama and Daddy  and Grandma and Aunt Ruby.  Nobody\'s Dancing, though.  Not yet.<BR><BR>Grandma and Grandpa let us Take Little Sips out of their Beer Sometimes.  I don\'t Know if I Like it or not.  Grandpa told Timmy and me to Never Drink In The Morning.  He said that\'s how Opa Died.  He\'d go up to The Virginia Grill and start Drinking at Nine O\'Clock In The Morning.  And one Day he had A Heart Attack up there.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>I\'m sitting on The Edge Of The Porch, and Grandpa comes and sits down Beside me.  I Look up at him, and he Looks at me, and I Look at him.<BR><BR>He says, <I>You Learned Somethin\' Today, huh?  Well, that\'s The Way The World Works, son.  That\'s The Way The World Works.</I><BR><BR>He Pats me on the leg.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>When we Leave, every Family Gets a grocery bag.  I know What\'s In There.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>We walk back over to Grandma and Grandpa\'s.  It\'s Dark, and there aren\'t any cars or trucks.  It\'s almost Hallowe\'en.  That means Christmas will be here Before Long.<BR><BR>Right after Grandpa\'s Birthday, we were over at their house while Mama and Daddy had a Date in Tampa.  We were Playing out in The Yard on The Tire Swing.  We were Taking Turns.  Grandma and Grandpa were sitting in lawn chairs Watching us.<BR><BR>Morgan was on The Swing, and I was Pushing, and Grandpa said,<BR><BR><I>Boys, I\'m gonna Tell ya\'ll Just One Time, so You Better Listen Up.  It is exactly three Months until The Fat Man comes, and as you might Know, he\'s got a Nice List, and he\'s also got a Naughty List.  And if your Ass finds itself on That Naughty List, you\'re Shit Outta Luck.  So you better Clean Up Your Act.</I><BR><BR>We all Giggled cause he Cussed.  He said,<BR><BR><I>Shorty don\'t have Nothin\' to Worry about.  She was Born Good.</I><BR><BR>Morgan Smiled Real Big and Patted herself on the head.<BR><BR>It\'s almost Hallowe\'en.  I Think I\'m going to Be an Astronaut.  We can take my Football Helmet and cover it with Aluminum Foil and cover Me with Aluminum Foil and put a little Flag on the sleeve and one on my chest, and that\'ll be It.<BR><BR>There\'s going to be a Party at Church, and then we\'ll Go Trick Or Treating.  We\'ll all have A Big Time.</P>'),(3825,'<table width=\"452\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td align=\"left\" valign=\"top\">\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img1%%\" width=\"450\" height=\"600\" border=\"1\" alt=\"TROJAN\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce upon a time they found outside their city a thing of great wonder.  And, taking it in, they learned only too late what it contained.\r\nThey were not to be fooled, a second time, by a wooden horse hiding Hellenes.  But a 100 foot Jesus filled with Nazis...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMachiavelli, Niccolo.<br>\r\nTrans. Leo Paul S. de Alvarez,<br> \r\nChicago: Waveland Press, 1980, 89<br>\r\n<u>The Prince</u>, Chapter 6 + 6 + 6\r\n<p>\r\n\"...he appears to be, when one sees and hears him, all pity, all faith, all integrity, all humanity, and all religion.  \r\nNothing is more necessary than to have this last quality...for the vulgar are always taken in by the appearance \r\nand outcome of a thing, and in this world there is no one but the vulgar.\" (109)\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3826,'<P>\r\nWe swam through pools of gasoline. We braved the dark water. The monoliths rising out of sea were no deterrent, just the rainbow film on the surface.  Our skin blistered and bubbled.  Tears fell from our eyes.  No one watched from above.  No one knew what had happened until it was too late - until our skin ruptured - until the bones of fish, dead from failed spawn attempts, washed up onto the shore in piles, mass graves, poked through our feet bringing with them the septic allergens that caused swelling, that caused unbearable itching.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWe couldn\'t sleep, sprawled on the the floor of our siblings\' bedrooms, crying in the night, begging for relief.  The same room where he knocked on the window to tell me he was actually Satan.  The same window the cat pawed at to offer up the severed head of an erstwhile rodent.  The same window I snuck in and out of to ride my youth through the night.  The window I snuck out of the night he picked me up and drove me to his house with the waterbed where my virginity was taken without a second thought, without asking, without me knowing what was happening, without my express consent or dissent.  \r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nIt just happened.  Unceremonious and harsh.  Painful and unfulfilling.  On that same waterbed where I pulled the skin on my fingers tight and wondered what the bones looked like, what I would look like with no skin.  And years later I still do that and I still wonder what my skeleton looks like and I look at pictures of emciated victims of their own psyches.  Sometimes I admire their commitment, their obsessive behavior but mostly I just feel so sad.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nJust love yourself. \r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>Just love yourself.  <BLOCKQUOTE>How am I not myself?</BLOCKQUOTE></BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n</P>'),(3827,'<P>\"Good night,\" you said, and there was so much tied up in those two words -- as much hope as trepidation, culmnating from the sense pervading our conversation that it would soon be time to end that conversation so that it could start anew in time -- I like to say it\'s percolating, drip drip through the filter and hopefully only the good flavors slipping down to the pot, leave all the bitterness behind in the grounds to be discarded into a big orange bucket to make room for fresh new ideas and then percolate those new ones in the same way as well.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Far away it seems as we murmur in each others\' ears, sometimes echoing, sometimes unexpectedly silent, secrets of deep emotion split into packets, into bits, reassembled into sound, and on the other end they say the same thing but meaning doesn\'t always carry like we\'d intended.  So much more can be said with a touch, a caress, a quiet sigh.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Sigh, I practice the I, threading visibly through the thought processes where once the I was encouraged to unravel -- I wish I could explain the value that intention held for me at the time, now it\'s just a part of who I am, or how I fit.  But for you, the I is all; it seems almost sacreligious to have such a strong I when all is.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>(Here the author pauses for a gulp of water, and sees schmutz in the bottom of the purple plastic cup; where did it come from? Can\'t have been in the water originally, not with this glorious sediment filter.)</P>\r\n\r\n<P>And if all is then how are we, aspects of all -- I was going to say how are we so far apart, yet we aren\'t, there is closeness indescribable and (as we have seen) unbreakable, yet we aren\'t, there is distance insurmountable, yet we can, there is love, but will we?  And therein lies the roof over our problem (that was a typo but it still fits) -- will we?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>The focus on I is very different from the focus on we; the I leads inexorably towards hedonism and/or egoism, the former now a virtue to so many, the latter no longer a common topic of discussion.  We is closer to all is yet much is unclear between the all and the we and the I, and every we is a different size, but the we of the you and I is...still undefinable.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>This maze I keep following, one concept leading to another but none leading to the finish...it makes for some interesting writing, I suppose, maybe it\'ll be easier to read again if I\'m stoned, but oh! the frustration of seeing the goal and \r\nnot knowing how to get there, or if there is even really there to get.</P>'),(3828,'<p>\r\nI woke up bright eyed today but my cough was still there. The mixture of Canadian Nyquil, Korean over the counter cold medicines and Sleeping pills I had picked up at the Airport in Singapore hadn’t quelled the demon inside my throat and nose. The cold has not died. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI won’t say life has been hard these past few months. I have been trying to wean myself off of Paxil and so far it has been interesting to say the least. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI decided to make an initial jump down from thirty to fifteen milligrams because I was running low on Paxil. There were some complications in Canada and my new shipment wouldn’t arrive for a couple of weeks. Paxil is fucking expensive even by Canadian prescription drug standards. Depending on the size of the pill, they can go from anywhere between 2 and 5 dollars a pill. I had done some reading on the net regarding the weaning process and learned that by taking a B complex Vitamin and a Lecithin supplement, the route towards not taking Paxil would be less harsh. This seems to be the case and I am quite happy I found that out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n30-15mg<br>\r\nThe first two days at 15mg was cake and so I let my guard down. By the third day my vision had trouble keeping up with my head movements, my speech was slurred, I had terrible head aches, and my stool was liquid. To top that off I was constantly confused and feeling stressed out and once and a while I would get an electrical shock in my mouth. At times the electrical shocks were quick and just startled me, but by the end of the week I would go an hour with a constant zap zap zap zap in my mouth, neck, and throat. At first they scared the shit out of me, but upon further reading, I found out that they were quite common and nothing to worry about. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI decided to stay on the 15mg dose until I got back from my trip to <a href=http://www.drivkid.org/tokyo/>Tokyo</a>. I was meeting an old friend who was going to be there at the same time, and so I didn’t want anything to hamper the situation. I had a great time, and only a few zaps occurred. My stool had returned to normal, I was less confused and my eye sight had returned to normal. Nevertheless, the headaches were still there and still are as I write this. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n15-10mg<br>\r\nWhen I got back from Japan the plan to move from fifteen to ten milligrams of Paxil went into effect. Unlike the previous drop, the five milligram drop was much less a hassle. The headaches and zaps remained, but for the most part, everything else was normal, though I did feel a bit more anxious at times. I wanted to make another drop but chose to wait until I got back from a business trip in Australia. It is a good thing I did. My trip to Australia was really stressful and so I think it was wise of me to stay at ten milligrams. I had a good time in <a href=http://www.drivkid.org/adelaide/>Adelaide</a> as I was able to spend more quality time with my office crush who was also present on the business trip. Damn she is so beautiful. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n10-5mg<br>\r\nWhen I got back from Adelaide, it was time to drop from ten to five milligrams. I started taking my five milligram pills later in the day so that I would have more Paxil while I was teaching the kids. I found out almost immediately that my temper would flare, my emotions would spike, and that the headaches and zaps were ferocious. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy mood swings were most evident at the office. I would go from smiling and making jokes to just sitting in my chair and staring off into the wall for sometimes more than ten minutes. I wept in the bathroom a few times a day and just felt like a complete mental basket case. It was by far the worst jump so far, and as I am writing this it is where I stand. I have been on five milligrams now for two weeks and I have decided it would be best if I stayed on it for two more weeks. My brain needs time to adjust to the lower dose and so time I will give it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis phase has made me really anxious regarding the rest of the drops. When I first began the drops I notified my mom and managers at work. I wanted them to know what to expect from me with regards to my mood swings, sullen face, and slow often slurred conversation. My mom and managers have been very supportive. There have been a few times in the office where my coworkers have gone to my boss and asked if I was mad at them. There have been a few times I have blown up in class and made kids stand up with their hands up holding a book for too long. There have been times where I lie in bed and just stare at the ceiling for hours and get back out of bed and go to work without having slept a wink. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI decided to get off Paxil because I don’t think I need it anymore. I really don’t. My life in Korea is good. I have good friends here, two stable jobs, and a nice apartment. Paxil has caused me to gain weight, and gaining weight is perhaps one of the reasons I chose to get on Paxil. My weight depresses me, and no matter how much I walk or how much I watch my diet, the weight just keeps on coming. Enough is enough I say. I guess a second less important side effect of being on Paxil is \"sexual dysfunction\"... and while this has had an effect on my \'self love’ life, it bears no impact on my non existent sex life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nComing off Paxil I have noticed that things are different. I feel so much less inclined to just get up and go out for a walk. I feel so much more lazy, melancholy and lethargic. This isn’t to say that I am Depressed, unmoving, and dormant. I still get out once and a while, work my 60+ hours a week, and complete the week and month long routine. I have just noticed a film of contempt forming on the periphery that if not checked, will become what it was in high school and early university. I don’t think it will come to that though. I hope not at least.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTo make a long story short, I was shocked at how hard it was going to be to get off Paxil. My shrink in Canada told me I could get off of it whenever I wanted and didn’t mention the weight gain. He did mention sexual dysfunction, though even then I didn’t care about that. Through my reading I have discovered that Paxil is evil, and that the drug company that makes it (Glaxo) has set aside a large chunk of cash in preparation of some up coming legal actions. Apparently some Paxil users who chose to just stop taking their pills have committed suicide. The withdrawal symptoms from getting off Paxil are far worse than the symptoms you get when starting. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI just want to say that if any of you are thinking about taking anti depressants, please do your reading. Clinical testing will tell you what will happen when you start using a drug, but very rarely tells you what will happen when you decide to get off the drug. I will not say that Paxil didn’t help me. It has. It helped me a lot. But this isn’t to say that I (or anyone else) deserve to go through the withdrawal symptoms associated with getting off of it.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3829,'<P>lately, two or three times a week, i\'ve been taking my guitar and a camp chair to the amphitheatre by the library and city hall.  it\'s very small, quarter circle shaped, all below ground level, tiny stage.  i go around three, after the sun\'s down far enough over the rim.  i\'ll play for two, two and a half hours or until i get tired of it.<BR><BR>my set list yesterday was:<BR><BR><B><I>safe european home<BR>pressure drop<BR>under pressure<BR>dancing in the streets<BR>mickey\'s monkey<BR>upside down<BR>i want you back<BR>controversy<BR>alex chilton<BR>back of a car<BR>eight miles high<BR>disturbance at the heron house<BR>oh yeah<BR>flesh number one (beatle dennis)<BR>don\'t shake me, lucifer<BR>gary floyd<BR>you were always on my mind<BR>pop-a-top again</I></B><BR><BR>(tune up time)<BR><BR><B><I>velouria<BR>telegram sam<BR>all the way from memphis<BR>the kids are alright<BR>heavensent<BR>dreaming<BR>all down the line<BR>expressway to yr skull<BR>hot fun in the summertime<BR>right back where we started from<BR>more, more, more (part 1)<BR>the have nots<BR>you\'ll never walk alone<BR>high fidelity<BR>head over heels<BR>walk away, renee<BR>badlands<BR>more than this<BR>personality crisis<BR>toys in the attic<BR>temptation<BR>mystery achievement<BR>on a plane</I></B><BR><BR>i walked down there and set up.  there were four kids, maybe fifteen, sixteen, skating on the concrete steps that made up the seats.  i could feel them looking at me.  little whispers, giggles.  i finished the first verse, and the biggest one approached me:<BR><BR>- Hey!<BR><BR>i keep playing and singing.  he looks me in the eye and comes closer.<BR><BR>- Hey!  Hey!  You, motherfucker!<BR><BR>i stop and look at him, wait.<BR><BR>- What the FUCK are you playin\'?<BR><BR>- It\'s called punk rock, you little bitch.<BR><BR>- oh, it\'s called PUNK rock, and I\'M the bitch.<BR><BR>ignore him.<BR><BR>- the song\'s called <B><I>safe european home</I></B>.  it\'s by the clash.  it\'s about when the singer and the guitarist went to jamaica and got jacked when they tried to score some weed.  it\'s the first song on <B><I>give \'em enough rope</B></I>.  that was their second album.  i\'m playing the rhythm guitar part.  well, in the song, it\'s rhythm as lead, really.  i\'m singing the lead vocal, except in the break and the coda where the background vocal becomes the lead.  i\'m playing an open D tuning.  this is a six-string washburn acoustic guitar, the strings were made by d\'addario, the pick is one of the twelve remaining cheap trick picks out of a batch of thirty-one that i asked for and got from a guitar tech after a concert of theirs in 1988, and i am a human being.  does that clear it up?<BR><BR>he does the little snort-like exhalation thing and shakes his head a little.<BR><BR>- you don\'t like punk rock?<BR><BR>- naw, dawg.<BR><BR>- but you guys\'re skaters.  you\'re supposed to <U>love</U> punk.  you never heard of the dead kennedys?  nobody?<BR><BR>- nah.<BR><BR>- whatta you like?  hip-hop?<BR><BR>- yuh damn right.<BR><BR>- well, i like hip-hop, too, but there\'s other music in the world, y\'know?  if you keep an open mind, you\'ll get a lot more outta life.  (beat)  y\'know what?  i\'ve got somethin\' for you.  i have to go get it from my apartment.  it\'s right up the road.  it\'ll take five minutes.  will you watch my guitar?<BR><BR>i look deep into his eyes.<BR><BR>- yeah.<BR><BR>i scoot home and go through the crates and find the cd i\'m looking for in the bottom left one under a hamfistedly-mastered bartok collection and <B><I>abraxas</I></B>.  it\'s labeled in felt-tip pen in jamie\'s handwriting - neat, even, elegant, shapely art deco-like letters.  the only other place i\'ve seen that style of writing has been on blueprints.  they must teach it in engineering school.  two semesters of it.  uppercase, lowercase.<BR><BR>(he made a bunch of mix cds for me out of stuff in my collection.  i\'d tell him what i wanted on each one and in what sequence.  i don\'t know how to do stuff like that.  he was happy to do it.  he was really sweet.  always did stuff like that for me.)<BR><BR>the label says,<BR><BR><CENTER><B>THE \"CLASH\"</B></CENTER><BR><BR>when he gave them to me, i saw that and thought it was pretty funny.  like they were the supposed clash.<BR><BR>as an afterthought, as i\'m headed for the door, i stop and grab <B><I>tougher than leather</I></B>, too.<BR><BR>when i get back down there, the one i was talking to is sitting in the chair holding the guitar, plucking at it.  another boy has picked my set list up off the concrete, and he and one of his friends are looking at it.<BR><BR>when he sees me, the spokesman stands up and holds the guitar out to me by the neck.  i take it and lay it in the case.  i give him one of the cds.<BR><BR>- you heard of run-dmc?<BR><BR>- i\'ve heard the <U>name</U><BR><BR>- well, that\'s a great album.  you\'ll like it.  if you like old school.<BR><BR>i give him the other cd.<BR><BR>- and that one.  there\'s nothin\' but great songs on there.  i\'m not sayin\' you\'re gonna like it, but just do me a favor and listen to it one time.  you might be surprised.  there\'s some hip-hop on there, too.<BR><BR>- i thought you said it was punk.<BR><BR>- well, they started off punk, then they did all kinds of music.  they had open minds.<BR><BR>a little sigh.<BR><BR>- alright.<BR><BR>- do you want me to show you something on the guitar?<BR><BR>he looks at me.<BR><BR>- here, siddown.<BR><BR>i get the guitar out and give it to him.  give him a pick.  show him how to hold it.<BR><BR>- now take your pointer finger over here and push all the strings down in one of those sections.  and strum it.  that\'s a chord.  now, you can play that one again or move to another one.  play it as fast as you want or whatever.<BR><BR>i try to think of a hook i can teach him from a song they\'ve heard of, and the only one i come up with is the one from <B><I>i\'m coming out</I></B> that\'s in <B><I>mo money mo problems</I></B>.  but then i realize, somewhat morosely, that that was before their time.  and i don\'t know anything more recent.<BR><BR>he fools around for a little while, then i gesture for it.  he gets up and gives it to me, and i settle into the chair.  (that\'s what you do when you get old.  you settle.  settle down.  settle for less.)<BR><BR>- yeah, man, the clash.  that\'s all you need.  at one time, the clash was The Only Band That Mattered.  you start listening to them, and then you can call yourself a skater.<BR><BR>- alright, dawg.  whatever.<BR><BR>- alright.  now ya\'ll sitcher punk asses down.  you\'re about to hear somethin\'.<BR><BR>they didn\'t sit for long.  but they stuck around and skated until five, when the security guard from city hall got off work and came out and chased them away on his way to the parking lot.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i have a job.  i\'m teaching sandeep spanish.  the local mexican men make up a large chunk of his customer base.  he knows enough to cash checks and count out money, but that\'s about it.  and i put up a card on the bulletin board at the library offering tutoring in all subjects.  i can give tom, the p.s.o.f. client i tutored in video editing and wrote the term paper for, as a reference.<BR><BR>oh, and i got sandeep good the other day.  he\'s always giving me a hard time when i go in there.  the thing he does every time is, well...  i keep telling him my name is matt, and every time i go in there, he always says, \"hello, mott.  <U><I>thyew</I></U>.\"<BR><BR>so i knew he spoke hindi and telugu and tamil and something else.  there\'s another guy i talk to who owns a store right by the laudromat i go to.  i found out he speaks hindi and malayalam.  (palindrome.  and not just in english.)  i was hoping he spoke telugu.  that\'s the main language of andhra pradesh, where sandeep\'s from.  so it had to be hindi.  (actually, sanskrit would have been ideal, but nobody speaks sanskrit.)<BR><BR>so i asked him if he would help me out, told him what i wanted to do, what i wanted to say, and he said it very slowly, and i wrote it out phonetically and memorized it.<BR><BR>i went in there, into the main street food mart, which briefly had a sign designating it as such until one of the two little inconveniences that passed through here of late rendered it nameless once more.<BR><BR>- hello, mott.  <U><I>thyew</I></U>.<BR><BR>i stopped by the rack of cheap wine and turned and looked him in the eye and said, at the top of my voice, in hindi:<BR><BR><CENTER><B>THAT IS NOT MY NAME.  I DO NOT HAVE A NAME.  I AM THE MAN WHO GOES AROUND NAMELESS.  <U><I>MATT!</I></U> IS ONLY A WORD THEY PUT ON ME TO YOKE ME TO THIS WORLD.</B></CENTER><BR><BR>he looked at me for about five seconds.  then he said:<BR><BR>- get the fuck outta here.  who told you that?<BR><BR>- april <U><I>fool\'s</I></U>.<BR><BR>- go get your goddam coca-cola.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>we went to The Mall the other day, and she bought a bread maker.  we went back to hers and made two loaves and had a late dinner.  not too long ago, if you had told me that someday soon, i\'d be spending a saturday night baking bread, i would\'ve told you to shoot me right in the eye.  it was fun, though.  things change.  little by little, everything changes.</P>'),(3830,'<p>as i write this i am accepting and feel no little fear.  being honest with myself the other day, always a scary proposition, i realized i am still very much in love with Dex.  I think i will always be in love with him.  something about being with him makes me whole even as he is tearing my life apart and turning everything to chaos.  the only promise i have made to myself that i can keep is that i will never again be controlled.  but when passion overcomes, when does commonsense ever truly play a part. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>so i am accepting, scared to death of what may come of it, and trying to just go with the flow.  it is very easy to say \"there are no boundaries only the spirit and the unspoken laws.\" quite another story all together for a timid person like me to learn to live it.</p>'),(3831,'This photo was the only thing on a floppy I found in the machine I was using here at the library yesterday.  I thought I\'d share it with you:<BR><BR>\r\n<center><img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3831_1.JPG\" width=\"500\" border=\"3\"></center><br><br>\r\n\r\nI can\'t decide if it\'s art or amphetamine psychosis.  Maybe both? <br><Br>The aftercare guy at the rehab told me that in his bad old days, he used to get wide-eyed on speed and go work on an ongoing project he had out in his garage:  building an airplane out of garbage bags and two by fours.  And he absolutely believed that it would fly.<BR><BR>Good times.\r\n'),(3832,'<b>Underhill Avenue, Prospect Heights, Brooklyn:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3832_1.jpg\" border=\"1\"><br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3832_2.jpg\" border=\"1\"><br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3832_3.jpg\" border=\"1\"><br><br>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/3832_4.jpg\" border=\"1\"><br><br>'),(3833,'<p>empty fields, running<br>\r\noff to the mist, are you<br>\r\ngoing there? I have<br>\r\ncome from there. spices<br>\r\nfill my nose, as they\'re<br>\r\ncrushed in my fist, oh this<br>\r\nweight I bear, I have<br>\r\ncome from there. let the<br>\r\nwaters flow, running<br>\r\noff to the mist, are you<br>\r\ngoing there? I have<br>\r\ncome from there. let the<br>\r\nroad we ride slowly<br>\r\ncease to exist, oh this<br>\r\nweight I bear, are you<br>\r\ngoing there, are you<br>\r\ngoing there, are you<br>\r\ngoing there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>something with tabla & wailing</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>empty heart, running<br>\r\noff to be kissed, are you<br>\r\ngoing there? I have<br>\r\ncome from there. our dream<br>\r\nfilled my mind, as I<br>\r\nceased to exist, oh this<br>\r\nweight I bear, I have<br>\r\ncome from there. let the<br>\r\nteardrops flow, running<br>\r\naway from this, are you<br>\r\ngoing there? I have<br>\r\ncome from there. let the<br>\r\nlove we seek never<br>\r\ncease to exist, oh this<br>\r\nweight we share, never<br>\r\ngetting there, never<br>\r\ngetting there, never<br>\r\ngetting there.</p>\r\n'),(3834,'<p>\r\n<b>Saturday Night:</b> Another day of carpentry, and I\'m ripped to the bone: too sexy for my shirt, too sexy for my pants,\r\ntoo sexy to unscrew my ass from the god damned sofa and go sleep in my bed.  So there I lie: caked in sawdust, full of wine,\r\nand dead to the world.  Sweet Action!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>1:00 am:</b> M-E-O-W-squeaksqueak!!!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI open my good eye.  There\'s Tabby with a wee rodent wriggling in her jaws.  \"Good girl!\"  \r\nHaving roused me, she turns 180 and wags her sassy ass out the room.  I follow, armed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn a corner, she releases.  It\'s all up to Daddy now.  Shovel in hand, I swing.  WUMP!  Strike one.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRecapture; release; strike two.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe looks up at me, and I read the meaning in her eyes: \"Dick.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"%%img1%%\">\r\n<p>\r\nI remind her that she pees in the wall sockets, and starts electrical fires.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe cops an attitude: one paw on hip, one paw moving...Z-Snap.  \"Oh no you di\'nt - not here in fron\' of the mouse!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThird pitch, rodent in the corner, and...WHACK!  Out of the park, past Waveland, crashing into a dope fiend under the \r\ncrumbling terra cotta at the Red Line near Irving.  \"Holy Cow!\" comes the voice from the booth, \"That thing could\'a bought\r\ncrack in Uptown! Hawhawhaw! Drink Budweiser.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>3:30 am:</b> I\'m at bat again; it\'s a double header.  There lies the second mouse: no shirt, no pants, dead to the world, blood spilled like wine.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs the sun rises, Tabby gets praise and Mr. Comby-comb, but no kisses.  For her, it\'s nature. But I go back to sleep with mixed feelings...\r\n</p>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"./images/3834_2.JPG\">'),(3835,'<p>\r\nEnough time has passed for me to look back on my time in Ulster without sentiment. It is tempting in retrospect for ones recollections to take on a certain floral tone; which is perhaps the reason my photographic record of this period is almost entirely monochromatic. The term Ulster, is somewhat politically incorrect. Historically referring to the northern-most province of Ireland which existed before British rule, It nowadays more commonly refers to the six counties in the North of Ireland which remain a part of the United Kingdom, officially and internationally known as Northern Ireland.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut this is not a history lesson. Nor a forum for political or sectarian debate.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNorthern Ireland’s introspective, mono-cultural society, is the most unique and interesting (not to mention cold and rainy) place I have ever known. It is a uniquely concentrated microcosm of all mankind’s most peculiar idiosyncrasies, levered into an economically starved cultural wasteland that the 1960s forgot. My time in the province began in early 2001 as, in search of an abstract concept called ‘happiness’, I crossed the Irish sea:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOver seas from coast to coast. To find the place I loved the most. Where the fields were green, to see you once again. But that was then, and this is now, and time and tide as they say wait for no man. Things progress. There are innumerable things about that time in my life which I deeply regret, but few things which I would change.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFate is an unforgiving master, but is not without a sense of humor, albeit twisted. I hear whatsername lives in England now, with the father of an old school friend of mine. They live near my hometown. I don’t. There was a time when I felt differently on such matters, but more as time passes I’m beginning to notice certain synchronicities. Circumstances unfold in an oddly coincidental manner, as if somehow predestined. I am unsure as to what I believe anymore, but I no longer believe that things just happen.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNo photos remain save for one very clear one; of the back of a head. It may be her. It is named 004.jpg which intimates very little, though it does nevertheless appear to have been taken on the street on which I lived at the time. In the background is Ferguson’s Bakery, though when it was last used as such is an interesting postulation. Cross Street in Lisnaskea was, and no doubt still is at the time of writing almost entirely derelict but for my flat (flat 2), a flat occupied by a reclusive alcoholic (flat 1), Big T’s Taxi Company, a newly built SP Office (betting shop) and the bustling rear entrance of a bar on the parallel Main Street.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCertainly not a candidate for ‘friendly neighborhood street of the year’, more a litter strewn alleyway frequented by drunks, lovers and junkies. There was occasional talk of redevelopment, but where the motivation, not to mention the financial backing for this was to be found I deemed highly questionable in this forgotten corner of nowhere in particular.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUnsurprisingly, life in Northern Ireland, for an outsider, can initially be ever-so-slightly unsettling. Watching the local television news for the first time was a pivotal moment, and one which I remember:\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBelfast seemed such an angry little city. Each morning a group of catholic schoolgirls in the Ardoyne area of the city would journey along a predominantly protestant street where they would be met with zealous protest by the street’s residents who strongly objected to this thoroughly disrespectful act.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis initially petty situation escalated into a full scale urban conflict. Each morning, local police of the former “Royal Ulster Constabulary” in their famous armored land rovers would attempt to retain some semblance of civil order as these small children were collectively indoctrinated into a culture of implicit hatred; Live on local network television.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI found myself almost unable to comprehend the scenes played out before me. As much as I understood the motivations behind these social tensions, I found myself unable to fully rationalize the dogmatic views held on either side. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou all believe in the same god. Fucking Listen to him.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3836,'<p>\r\nThere has been some discussion on the topic of the impending U.S standoff with China. Everyone knows that there already is one with North Korea, and just across the DMZ, South Korean’s are becoming increasingly vocal about their resentment of the United States’ Asian policies.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt is something else to think about the growing diplomatic problems between Japan and almost all of its neighbors. Territorial and resource disputes, and Japanese textbooks have all sparked up the mainlanders, and Japan has increasingly had to posture itself and backtrack. Diplomats have been spending a good proportion of their time addressing these issues, and yet no one across the seas is listening. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have a grim picture in my mind of what may occur in the next few decades. I see two very powerful forces forming, and I do not think there is much hope for peace. The ‘world war’ machine as we know it has pledged manifest destiny and will work its way east until it reaches the powder keg of Asia. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTHE TWO KOREA’S\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhile not an entirely bad thing, I see the two Korea’s coming together and living in relative peace in a two nation partnership. I don’t see South Korean politics moving North, and I don’t see North Korean politics moving South. What I do see is an increasing economic partnership, which may eventually become a military one.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe North and South have not been this close since before the two sides began shooting at each other. With the generation that grew up learning to hate losing their grip on South Korean society and politics, a new breed of younger, less afraid, and less pessimistic citizens are growing up in a society that is rapidly changing, becoming more liberal, and last but not least less prone to believing the propaganda machine. The national security law that bans the playing of the DPRK national anthem, and the reading of North Korean texts, is being whittled away allowing more and more South Koreans the opportunity to learn about their neighbor without fear of going to jail. The terms communist and demon are being used less and less to refer to the North and public opinion is in support of the continuing normalization of relations. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe simple fact that South Koreans could watch the North Korean athletes during the Olympics, the World Cup Qualifiers, and that North Korean athletes have competed in various sports competitions in South Korea should be telling enough of how far their relationship has come. It should be said also, that when I tell people this back home in Toronto, they are astonished. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere are economic zones in North Korea that have South Korean businesses producing products for the South Korean, North Korean, and world Markets. In fact I just lit a cigarette with a lighter produced in the DPRK. \r\nOne of Korea’s leading telecommunications companies is setting up large phone networks to link the economic zones up with South Korea. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is the famous Hyundai bribe paid to North Korea that opened the way for the building of a resort on a respected and revered mountain, and more corruption involving the establishment of the sunshine policy and the shaking hands of Pyongyang and Seoul. A Nobel peace prize was involved. And this is all after a number of trips by South Koreans up into North Korea to at long last meet each other again, if for only a few days, after so many years. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI see big things happening between North and South Korea. I see them building a lot of bridges and becoming more and more economically and militarily engaged.  Though I do not see them becoming one flag, I see them happily coexisting together, much in the way Russia and Belarus do. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe South understands that the North would be too expensive to prop up if unification were to occur, and not unifying cleans Southerner hands of any responsibly to hold the elite in the North accountable for their past actions. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe North also provides the South with a land route to both Russia and China. With the ability to pump oil in by pipeline, and ship goods out by truck and train, South Korea could lower the time it takes for their goods to reach a market, and ease concerns over oil availability.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI very strongly believe that the South will move further and further away from the United States, and will become friendlier towards China and North Korea.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3837,'<p>\r\nat dundas and university in cold winter march i crossed the intersection and waited.  a hot dog vendor closed his cart for the day, hitched it to his bumper and started home.\r\n<br><br>\r\nan old woman pushing a walker crossed the street toward me.  she moved slowly, frail steps, asked a woman to help her get her walker up on the lip of the sidewalk.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe alignment i was waiting for happened but i was lazy and shot from the chest. i knew immediately that i\'d wasted a good chance to find something.  i was disappointed and so set after the old woman for redemption.\r\n</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"%%img1%%\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n<p>\r\nshe pushed along and i passed her, walked a few blocks ahead.  it took her a long time to catch up.  i walked back towards her at another intersection, timing my steps to meet her just as she met the curb on my side.  she stopped short and looked directly at me. i raised my camera and shot a frame from the eye.  she ignored  that and motioned to me.  she spoke chinese to me, loud and firm, but kind.  i asked her if she wanted me to lift her walker up on to the curb through the snow. she repeated her request and then pointed across the street.  i asked her if she wanted me to help her cross the street.  she reached out and grabbed my wrist.\r\n<br><br>\r\nher grip was strong and vital, with no sign of the frailty of her steps.  she pulled my arm to the handle of her walker, put my hand on the grip.  she kept her hand firmly gripped on my wrist and hand, pointed her walker across the street and we shuffled together.  i lifted the walker each time up and over the streetcar tracks.  we made slow but sure progress to the other side.  her grip remained tight, we crossed in silence, i photographed her just before we met the sidewalk while her hand was still holding mine.  she said thank you when we parted ways.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img src=\"%%img2%%\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\ni turned down mccaul street, walked to the short street that connects to grange park.  a cabbie was stuck in a snowbank and someone was trying to give him a push out of it.  i walked to them and offered to help, and they accepted with a smile.  the cabbie got back in his car and we leaned into the hood and began rocking the car.  i held my camera in my hand while pushing, with a free finger i adjusted the exposure and locked focus, before the last push that set the cabbie free i shot a frame of the other pusher.  we didn\'t need to speak.\r\n</p>\r\n<p><img src=\"%%img3%%\" border=\"1\"></p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe cabbie waved a thank you from inside his car, i walked away into the sun, smiling and thankful that i had missed that first shot.\r\n</p>'),(3838,'<p>it\'s not writer\'s block, per se...more a crushing, jostling amount of thoughts filling thoughts with no space for the writing voice (or any kind of writing sound) to come through.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s not writer\'s block, per se...it\'s waking like a robot, working a day job, coming home and working freelance jobs, falling asleep on the couch and the days pass in a haze.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s not writer\'s block, per se...more like a drive to try and \'get ahead\', save some dough, not have to worry about credit card interest anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s not writers block, per se...but when it\'s not work it\'s the other obligations that fill up the hours - do the washing, clean the apartment, see the parents, have quick quality time before i have to turn back to the machine.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>it\'s not writer\'s block, per se...but whatever it is, everything it is, i\'m ready for it to go away.</p>'),(3839,'<p>\r\nThe Captain was an unusually cryptic fellow. His liquid curls of lifeless brown hair held a pensive air of injustice around him like a bad smell. He spoke almost exclusively in riddles, shrouding himself in an indistinct aura of ambiguity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy relationship with The Captain began and ended in two thousand and four, whilst running a small fast-food outlet in a bustling shopping precinct. During this course of time I became favorably accustomed to the eclectic nature of humanity. The heterogeneity of its form is a warm amalgam in which I find endless wonderment.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI ultimately concluded that the justification for The Captain’s most peculiar diction, must surely be attributable to some form of brain damage, or conceivably to an extraordinary combination of psychological problems. In either case, his dreadful affliction was undoubtedly linked with whatever appalling misfortune had beset him. The Captain, you see, had a missing hand which was transposed with a rather insincere latex replica set in a brown leather glove. He had also clearly been the victim of extensive burns, both facial and otherwise.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom what I could gather, The Captain’s unusual verbal syntax was a simple consequence of the fact that there were certain words that he couldn’t pronounce. An individual with a severe stammer will battle in vain to express a particular word though ultimately forgo this fruitless toil in favour of another, phonetically simpler word which expresses the same intent. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Captain’s situation was no doubt similar to this. It was only that his repertoire of expressibity had been so enthusiastically pruned by his bizarre affliction that the articulation of even the most basic notion had become a frustrating if not impossible task. He did not stammer nor stutter, nor was the basis of his vocal ability at all impeded. His choice of wording however, was often irregular to the point of absurdity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nImagine for a moment, if you will, ordering a cup of tea without saying “tea”\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Captain and I spoke at length on numerous occasions. Many a time would he innocently entice me into the addled inner core of his intricate syntactical labyrinth. This of course afforded me a welcome reprieve from the tedium of my daily schedule. The sum total of what I concluded in disentangling from his meandering syntax could however, be scratched onto the back of a cheap prosthetic appendage. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Captain told me he once worked on “big jobs”. What exactly he meant by this I do not know. He was never able to articulate any more about his former profession than this. I felt a sense that he may have worked within the bounds some form of heavy industry. Perhaps his misadventure was in some way linked with a hazardous career. He would talk incessantly of being cheated out of what was rightly his. He lived exclusively on disability benefit, and drank a lot of tea.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe Captain told me he had three children. They didn’t visit him anymore. His brother lived in “The bottom bit of Spain; it’s England” which I took to mean Gibraltar. I told him I had visited there as a child. I told him about the monkeys on the rock, and caught a flicker of recognition in his good eye. It occurred to me in that moment what it must mean to him to be understood. How it must feel to achieve successful communication, and how frustrating his day to day life must be.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn one particularly tedious afternoon, The Captain seemed remarkably agitated. He was restless again, and angry about being cheated. He told me he wanted to “go back to the Irish”. He revealed that he planned to accomplish this “Not after the next Christmas, but after the next Christmas after next”. I believe The Captain had lived in Ireland for a time, and I got the impression that that was where his children were. He seemed more perturbed than normal. Attempting polite conversation, I told him that I had lived in Northern Ireland for a short while. He told me that he would show me what had happened to him the next time he came to see me. I assumed that he planned to bring along something in the order of a newspaper clipping.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can only assume that he forgot or simply changed his mind. The Captain never mentioned his accident again, and out of common courtesy neither did i.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3840,'<p>\r\nI danced, played pool and talked at the bar.\r\n</p><p>\r\nLater, I played on the dance floor, danced at the bar, and talked around the pool table.\r\n</p><p>\r\nMy new glasses on, I went to the bathroom and stared at myself in the mirror. I played fool at the bar, danced around the pool, and had a serious conversation on the dance floor.\r\n</p><p>\r\nHolding on to my cocktail, I messed up at pool, but I won at the bar.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThen came the point when I lost grip on my glass. I fell on the dance floor, and had a serious conversation in the bathroom.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd just as I was about to start feeling ashamed, someone said \"take it easy\" - or was it \"ki o tsukete\", or something else? - and I knew with horror and delight that I will have to do it all over again the next week.\r\n</p>'),(3841,'<p>\r\nFrom time to time, ungovernable events will inevitably transpire. Circumstances will come asunder, and as deftly as one might attempt their suturing, the resultant vestige of scar tissue invariably becomes an enduring retrospection into the turbulence of ones unrelenting self-pity.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the night bus into town I sat transfixed by the attractive young lady sat delicately in front of me. I speculated that perhaps she might sense the lurid intent of my gaze as it strenuously penetrated her from behind. I was unable to entertain any further notions as I was rudely distracted by the discourteous conduct of another passenger. An ungainly young man sat restlessly in the abutting row of seating. I watched him absorbingly as he plucked out charred pieces of foam from holes left by malicious cigarette burns. He began to openly propel little rolled up balls of seat-foam. With the repeated snap of a sullied forefinger, these buoyant fragments soared in every direction. Our lines of sight coalesced on more than one occasion, yet each time I strove to appear increasingly nonplussed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs I absent-mindedly inventoried the tiny burn holes in the lap of my track-pants, Carla began painstakingly recounting details of her most recent convergence with a gentleman named Alan. I listened as intently as I ever do. It’s not that I’m not interested in the finer points of Carla’s sordid on-off relationship with Alan, I’m merely preoccupied with the finer points of my own predicament. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis Alan fellow is a superfluously dull character. He works a four day week at my local Sexual Health Clinic, where for over a year he was my designated support worker. My genital afflictions have long since subsided, and my association with Alan has ceased. An intolerable cramp developed in my right metatarsal region. A blisteringly sharp pain, not unlike the sensation of red hot nylon dripping onto ones naked flesh. I must have expressed this discomfort outwardly, as Carla rolled her eyes; taking offence at my demeanor.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCarla’s relationship with Alan is anything but conventional, though I seriously doubt whether Alan realises the full extent of its unorthodoxy. Dependant on ones moral perspective, one might consider Miss McDermott to be somewhat salacious. With my social values loitering somewhere outside the electrified perimeter of Michael Jackson’s Kiddy-Ranch®, I feel it somewhat hypocritical of me to pass judgement.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n“Oi, oi Bollock-chops!”\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis is how I was greeted by Tall Si on my exit from the 104 night bus outside Magee’s. This defunct cargo vessel of yesteryear is now steadfastly anchored under a busy rail bridge. Fully licensed for the sale and consumption of intoxicating beverages and spirits. There had been an incident last year in which the boat had broken free of its allegedly permanent mooring. Perhaps in high winds, or good old fashioned high spirits, It had drifted a short distance downstream and sunk another vessel. This rogue of the tidal flow, a seasoned veteran of the many inter-city waterways of old. Upon finding himself fettered and fancied for the purposes of drunken merriment, he simply yearned for a glimpse of the freedom he had once enjoyed. This old soldier’s frustration with what it saw as a lack of vocational satisfaction, and his overzealous hunger for extrication were both ultimately unleashed onto his capricious young tormentor. Caroline was a houseboat.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCarla says you can’t make an omelette without breaking eggs. That isn’t altogether true. You can buy frozen, microwaveable, single serving omelettes from most good supermarkets nowadays. If the mood took you, you could alternatively reconstitute some powdered egg to create a sufficiently palatable dish without compromising the structural integrity of a single unfertilized chicken embryo.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut that really is beside the point. My only impetus for contemplating such a banal hypothesis is that of occupying my mind with something other than what has been troubling me for the past fortnight. Carla says it doesn’t matter. What does she know? It’s not that I don’t appreciate her emotional input at this difficult time, but I can’t shake that age old metaphor of the visually impaired playing shepherd to their equally disadvantaged peers.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am not certain at what juncture a relationship moves from mere acquaintance into the kaleidoscopic realms of mutual esteem, or where this transcendental line resides. I am certain however that I would not care to be considered a friend of Tall Si.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTall Si is a wanker. Everyone knows Tall Si. He’s the man with the gear. His look is invariable. He has oily shoulder length hair, which generally afflicts the shoulders of an equally disheveled T-shirt. His clothing tends to be emblazoned with witty consumerist parodies. My personal favourite is his red “Cock-a-Koala” T-shirt. It centers around some rather distinct graphic imagery and is somewhat fetching. He often augments this unparalleled chic with grubby fluorescent wristbands, some of which glow-in-the-dark. Last night however, in the fluttering orange-red glow of the hurriedly erected 60-watt paper lanterns that adorned the boat’s exterior, his unkempt appearance assumed a moderately Christ-like quality. I found myself momentarily at least, at an utter loss in my contempt for him. My temporary and somewhat foolhardy reverence however, was bluntly interrupted by it’s very subject…\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n“Choo fuck my wife?”\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSi doesn’t have a wife. He isn’t married. He doesn’t even have a girlfriend.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI replied as obtusely as possible: “Yes… up the gary!”\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSi laughed heartily; noticeably gratified by the fact that I had not only acknowledged his pathetic catchphrase, but embellished it with an equally witty retort.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n“Kong hay fat choi motherfucker!” Was his considered reply and valediction.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI told Carla that this would no doubt secure me the usual discount on my next score. I explained to her that although Tall Si was an moron, his shit was top-notch. None of that low-grade shit that floods out of Amsterdam. They manufacture that tripe from oil and tyres and fucking tiny pieces of plastic mixed with gravy granules and fuck knows what else. She just fell through the entranceway of Magee’s, vomiting onto my cheap footwear whilst soddenly mouthing something about won ton soup.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3842,'<p>\r\nYesterday came to an uncharacteristically mellow close. It was balmy. Summer is on the horizon, of that I have little doubt. The bins behind the bingo hall had been left open again, revealing themselves to be empty. Empty; but for a sodden paper carpet of dashed hopes and broken dreams. The fluorescent marker inks as they bled together had produced every colour of the rainbow. I found it strangely reminiscent of the many psychotropic incidences of my distant youth.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe yellow brick wall on which we nestled was heated pleasantly by the setting sun, and our world took on a sudden orange hue. We sat in this warm ambience, drinking, smoking and talking. I sat bewitched by Carla’s enormous eyes as we spoke about nothing in particular for what seemed like an eternity. I ventured beyond her stony facade and found myself somewhere new. Somewhere even more deeply calming than this heavenly sunset.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs I reveled aimlessly in her company, Carla began recounting a story that she seen on the breakfast news. They warned of an individual targeting young female drivers. Upon his intended victim stopping at a red light, this fresh-faced fellow would bashfully tap on her window. He would concernedly gesture toward a tyre as if to suggest a puncture, then once sufficiently opened, a live rat would be swiftly introduced through the window. This action was invariably followed by his startled victim springing from her vehicle, leaving the attacker free to snatch handbags and whatever else caught his eye. They went on to advise extra vigilance, keeping windows closed and doors locked whilst driving. Carla doesn’t drive, and neither do i.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAlan acts as willing chauffeur to the ravishing Miss McDermott whenever he can, anywhere within the M25; but he is attending a cystitis seminar in Milton Keynes. I told Carla that such a concrete-grey wilderness was the perfect setting for a cultured man like Alan. She just laughed and adjusted the flimsy straps of her H&M floral-print summer dress. Our evening soundtrack was a heady mix of traffic noise layered with the tinny overtones of a distant bingo announcer. This was further interspersed with the intermittence of equally muffled applause. We sat in relative silence for a while; listening.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo little ducks. Quack, quack! … Tuh-wenty Twooo!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCarla says that Bingo helps to keep pensioners minds sharp. It gives them an alternative focus in the way that youth centres and halfpipes are designed to sway kids from the more traditional pastimes of smoking crack and getting pregnant. The blue-rinse brigade could be out on the streets of an evening, getting in my way. They could be tripping on broken paving slabs, and filing lawsuits against the council. They could equally be let loose on our roads. Free to hold up the traffic with an over-cautious driving style. If lucky they might not get robbed at a busy intersection. They’re happiest though, when huddled together in rickety consumerist surroundings. Never happier, inanely marking off the numbers on their throwaway bingo cards, winning instant cash prizes with which to buy biscuits. The bus will take them safely home.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo fat ladies… - declared the bingo caller, deliberately overstressing each syllable.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n“You and your mother!” - I interjected bluntly.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMy breach of Carla’s reverie was met with an expression that I took to be a combination of both constrained amusement and outward disdain in equal measure. I counteracted this with a sheepish grin followed by a pointless yet comprehensive visual inspection of my worn-out Reeboks, the toes of which made invisible circles in the warm night air.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEighty Eiiiight!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom the rusty steel door beneath the air-conditioning vents, sprang three disheveled bingo hall employees in their ludicrously blue and yellow striped outfits. They were two floppy haired punk kids and the gummy girl from the corner shop, now sporting a brace. She smiled a gummy, orthodontic smile in my direction. I pretended not to notice whilst I observed one of the punk kids writing onto the side of a beige air-con unit with a marker pen. I made a mental note to take a closer look after they had gone back inside, though this note somehow got misfiled. It undoubtedly read something lame, however. The other punk kid presented gummy with a half hearted peck on the cheek, before hesitantly approaching me. He asked for a light which I begrudgingly obliged him before dismissing him with a discourteous grunt. If it had not been for that fact that I was smoking a cigarette at the time I would have falsely informed him that I did not smoke, and therefore no, I didn’t have a light. He had dyed jet black hair and a polished silver stud in his lower lip. I noted the lack of enthusiasm in his black nail polish. I am not fond of punk kids, or goths.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSiiix and Niiine…\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSomething happened last night. I woke early this morning to find Carla flaccidly hunched like a ragdoll over one arm of my tatty velour sofa. Donal was asleep in the kitchen again. He was too fucked to walk home. That much I remembered. Carla appeared to be entirely naked from the waist down. From the physical evidence before me I concluded that something fun had happened. Her stupor from her aroma, I took to be attributable to excessive alcohol consumption. It was roughly 7.00AM, and I remembered little of the previous nights course of events. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHad I been Carla’s welcome assailant? In the warm glow of morning glory I decided that whatever the case, such a glistening invitation was too welcoming a prospect to ignore. I entered slowly, not wanting to arouse unnecessary consciousness. She merely stirred and fell back into unconsciousness as my gratitude was released into its accommodating host. I think I love her.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(3843,'Across the drawbridge, in the cold dark night stands two, enormous, giant doors to a castle hiding in the depths of a forrest in the valley of Frederick in Switzerland.  Approaching the doors, they slowly, and loudly open towards you with chains jingling, lightning flashing, and rain drops visible only to the light from the sky; pooring down creating a coloseus change in texture for your feet to walk in.  In the doorway, lukewarm air wisps by pulling at ur clothes and hair to escape its prison.  Your shoes make sloshing noises as you walk down the entry steps made from marble.  Your guide asks you if you would be ready dinner, and you state interruptedly that vines and cobwebs in this castle must be jurasic comparing to the small petiteness of the ones outside these walls.  Then the doors shut, with a continuing echo, lights flicker on; electricity?!  \"Right this way, sir.\"  And you follow to the dining room.\r\n'),(3844,'I\'m really tired. More tired than I\'ve ever been. I really wish I could lie down. I should lie down. I open my eyes and focus on the nearest surface. The carpet. I am lying on the floor. Another new low, quite literally. I could not be lying down much more than this, I think, but I might be more comfortable on the bed.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAt the gym. I haven\'t worked out why it might not behoov me to do body building yet, and am on a roll. I have a regular workout three times a week, and do the aerobic stuff even though it\'s not my favourite. My body responds to the weights. I look up. Through the window some blokes are doing something, they are doing something, wait a minute, they are doing something I want! \'Hey! That\'s tai chi! I want to do that, where do you do that?\' in a greedy needy grabby way, but which appears to come out quite acceptable to the guys. \'Yeah, the bloke, Loz, who teaches us cab and flab does tai chi. He normally only teaches advanced, but we talked him into it. We just started so you\'ll have to talk to him, and start this week.\' This is how I started tai chi.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\'ve nearly finished the form. I find the learning really hard. I find the sparring really hard. I can\'t face starting to learn the next form, the sword form. I give up tai chi. I smoke dope for a year. One night, stoned, I have a revelation about what the sword form might be for. Damn.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSchool is a cross between prison and having a big state wide babysitter, isn\'t it? I hate it. And if there was an O level in staring out the window fantasising about what you would do in the event of an apocalypse and you are a survivor, I would get an A. There are various methods of bunking off sports and games and I know them all. I am really skinny due to having grown tall rapidly and am really self conscious for other reasons but which I project on to that. That body. Plus there is a side issue of clothes. We are not well off. There is a uniform for gym, which I do not own. The girls who look anywhere near human all have it and I do not. I have cast off make do and mend crapola which make my limbs look like bits of string hanging out of the sleeves and legs. And I have no physical form that I can capitalise on. Grace is not marketable in this arena it\'s all about teams, hand - eye, running, all the things I am rubbish at. <br><br>\r\n\r\n\'You fight like a girl!\' I am told. I find it hard to care about this. I love these guys. Tai chi is perfect for cab drivers, is my opinion - life is a journey. <br><br>\r\n\r\nNic comes to Brighton. We look for yoga to go to and find this. We are standing in tadasana. Head high, shoulders slightly back and down, arms hang gently. Crucially the feet, these are planted with every toe splayed out nice and comfortable. Knees upward, tail down, pelvis flat. Perfect posture. Eyes shut. \'What is yoga?\' Nic says later he nearly answers, just as well he doesn\'t. \'What is yoga? Pause. In Hatha yoga, we connect with the breath. We do not force the breath, but we notice it...\' and he\'s off. At first we think he is talking directly to us, specifically, noticing our little aches and pains because he seems to speak so intimately about our own niggling ow-y bits. Then one of us peeks once. He, too has his eyes shut.<br><br>\r\n'),(3845,'<p>\r\nHave I mentioned before that I do not drive? This is not altogether true. What is true is that I do not officially own a motor vehicle. What is true is that I do not possess a valid UK driving license, MOT test certificate, motor insurance certificate, or road tax. As far as the DVLA are concerned I am a non-entity. I simply can’t justify the overheads incurred during conventional car ownership.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhere not so long ago you could sell a tainted car for scrap and make a small profit. The government now erect fairly hefty financial hurdles along this particular pathway. There are other cheaper ways to dispose of unwanted motor vehicles nowadays, not all of which involve their torching on a deserted backstreet. This situation serves only to make it easier than ever before to secure the acquisition of a single-use motor vehicle. Especially easy that is, if you have a friend named Yuri.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUnlike his ragtag clientele, Yuri is a naturalized British citizen. I stop deliberately short of using the term legal, as his immigration status is arguably his sole legal attribute. Yuri’s country of origin is the war-torn Baltic state of Serbia, but considers himself to be thoroughly British. His Eastern-European roots invariably fail his Britishness on all counts. His suffering attempts at a Jamie Oliver mockney slant are reassuringly poor for example. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYuri’s livelihood pivots on the resale of deathtraps to illegal immigrants and assorted unsavory others. Those who like me, cannot abide the entanglement of red-tape involved in legitimate car ownership. Individuals who could not navigate the necessary bureaucracy in any case due to their questionable immigration status. When it comes to matters of business Yuri’s deep-rooted racial prejudices tend to take a back seat. Croatians, Kosovans, Montenegrins, Lithuanians; even ethnic Albanians and Turks. If you want to buy a insanely cheap yet structurally unsound car, no warranty, no return, Yuri is your man. His stock is accrued from a wide variety of sources, though mainly from dodgy eastend garages. Cars deemed un-roadworthy by the authorities will often be taken from their owners in part-exchange to be passed on to backstreet middlemen like Yuri. Profit margins are close to the bone but turnover is exponential. It’s a growing industry and the marketplace is vast.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYuri’s merchandise is inexpensive yet highly transitory. If his cars don’t fall to pieces within their first few months of use, they will be towed away by the council, or acquisitioned by unscrupulous bailiffs. It’s all a part of the unparalleled experience that is driving one of Yuri’s motors. When one is finished with ones single-use automobile, one simply abandons it by the side of the road where it becomes the councils problem. If you’re feeling industrious there’s always the option of torching the damn thing but there’s little point. These phantom machines are untraceable. Untraceable to you at least.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI tell you all this for a reason though. She called last night. I hadn’t heard from her in days, but last night at around eleven thirty, the phone rang. I was half asleep as I answered but instantly detected the familiar tone of distress in her voice. It was her sister again, on the run from a loyalist paramilitary group calling themselves the Red Hand Defenders. Carla had received a blunt message that she was to collect her sister from a B&B just outside Holyhead. She was to escort her to safety. What happened after that was “her concern”\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCarla and her sister were born and bred in a catholic ghetto of <a href=\"http://www.citynoise.org/search/west+belfast\">West Belfast</a>. Whilst Carla had wisely left home at the age of eighteen to pursue the golden streets of London, her sister Eadie had foolishly shacked up with a fellow named Steve from Derry’s (predominantly protestant) Waterside. Of course, on the Waterside they call it Londonderry. You can call it what you like though. You can call a piece of shit a fairy cake, but it’s still gonna make your breath smell bad if you’re stupid enough to eat it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIf looking to gentrify an area, there must surely be a more fitting prefix than London. Miltonkeynesderry would perhaps have been a more neutral alternative. But I digress; How the couple had come to meet, let alone come to marry was anyone’s guess. Without doubt it was a civil ceremony. No minister on either side of the divide would dare administer such brazen unorthodoxy. Steve was now dead, neatly dispatched with a customary double-tap to the head. It was their own fault, and retribution had been a long time in coming. Eadie’s world was unpicking its seams, and now of all people, Carla wanted me to help her in stitching it all back together. I decided without delay that it was the least I could do. My immediate reply was abrupt but courteous.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNot a word was uttered as we chugged along Marylebone Road. The silver 1986 cut-and-shut Ford Escort that Yuri had seen fit to loan me was in fairly good condition for its age and chequered history. He had assured me that despite my misgivings, the car was structurally sound. This was prime stock and Yuri made a point of nervously reiterating this fact several times before allowing me to pull away. I deliberately neglected to mention that I was contemplating a grueling return trip to North Wales. In the rear view mirror of my pimped-out new ride, Yuri’s unkempt look of bemusement grew smaller and dustier; ever more distant as I sped toward the city. Toward where I was to collect Carla. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNot a word was spoken between Carla and I as we catapulted onto the Westway at a breakneck fifty five miles an hour. As we passed the grand union canal and joined the M40 motorway I felt a familiar rush of expectation. There is still a residual crumb within me; an evolutionary relic which hungers for wide open spaces, rolling hills and fresh air. I always seem to come alive outside the M25 - The London Orbital. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBeaconsfield, Oxford, Banbury, Royal Leamington Spa all passed by in an awkward silence that continued until we arrived at a point where communication became a prerequisite of survival. If we could navigate the entangled mass of homogenous conurbations which comprise the midlands, we would find our way clear onto the more placid winding country roads of the Westcountry and Wales. If we took a mistaken turn on one of these spaghetti-like concrete arrangements we could equally find ourselves circling for all eternity. Somewhere hellish. Somewhere like <a href=\"http://www.citynoise.org/article/362\">Wolverhampton</a>.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTo be continued…\r\n</p>'),(3846,'<p>\r\n<b>End of the shift, pump in hand, gassing up the car:</b> I see her as she crosses the street, heading straight for me.  It\'s 5 A.M. and the new morning sun lights up her hot pink sweater.\r\nShe\'s tall, blonde, shapely, and out of place in my neighborhood.\r\nProstitute?\r\nHer mouth opens, and I see good teeth before I hear proper English.\r\nHmmm...not a working girl, at least not a cheap one.\r\nScam?  Cop?  Damsel in distress?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"HEY!  Can you help me, please?\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"You know, this isn\'t the best place to hang out.  On this block, in the past year...\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"I need to get home.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"There\'s another guy waiting for this car.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"Please...let\'s go!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHer story\'s cut short as she catches sight of a group of young men moving towards us, coming from the direction she\'s just left.\r\nI sigh, pay, and unlock the doors.\r\nShe hops in the front.\r\nInsults pop from the gaggle of would-be toughs; instead of protesting the seating arrangement I step on the gas.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDown the first ramp to 90/94 we speed.\r\nInbound, running toward the heart of the city, the concrete vein burns orange to the black steel mirrors.\r\nDark glasses save my vampire eyes; two years without sun, a legacy of Byzantine corruption, I look the part.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter 12 hours on the road, I\'m fighting to stay awake.\r\nAnd I don\'t immediately notice her hand on my thigh...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"You\'re cute.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"Huh?\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"None of what happened last night, happened.  Sometimes you just do things, and have fun, and then it didn\'t happen\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"Huh?\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"My fiancee is waiting at home, for me.  And none of that stuff, with those boys, happened.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFiancee...lucky man.  The hand continues to move on my thigh.  Hot pink sweater.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"You\'re cute.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nStory: A driver agrees to provide transportation for a woman in exchange for a service.  They pull up to her address, and she directs him to the parking lot on the side of the building.  She goes to work.  He\'s enjoying himself, and doesn\'t notice her partner walking around behind the car.  The next day he\'s found there: pants down around the ankles, brains on the dash, a bullet through the back of his head.  People like to tell that story, because they like to ask who was more cruel: the man who pulled the trigger, or the woman who agreed to hold him in her mouth - knowing what would happen.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe get to \"their\" condo.  High rent. She pays, lots.  I pull away.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*BAMBAMBAM*\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSomeone\'s pounding on the trunk of the taxi, as I drive.\r\nRearview mirror: two guys, panting, running top speed, trying to get me to stop.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"HEY!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI do stop - with the intention of screaming at them.\r\nOut walks the third member of their group: six feet tall, long flaxen hair, and a body like Elle MacPherson.\r\nHmmm.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"It\'s 6 A.M. and there was a guy waiting for this car at 5 A.M.  I can\'t...\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"Please!!!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEveryone gets in: (Male 1) a random African-American asshole-of-a-wanna-be-hipster-but-really-a-suit-with-money-and-drugs-that-the-other-two-want; (Male 2) a DJ from the \"coolest\" club in town; (Female 1) a Lithuanian model.\r\nWe make several stops for their \"supplies.\"  The model changes her clothes in the back seat, while I drive.\r\nDestination: the DJ\'s Wicker Park home.\r\nThey invite me in.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nVeteran Driver: \"NEVER GO IN!\"<br>\r\nVeteran Cop: \"NEVER GO IN!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s 7 A.M.  Oh well.  Cellphone: check.  Knife: check.  I\'m going in.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI follow the DJ up to the top floor.  He spins.  The model dances with herself.  The third guy lays out his stuff; he offers me lines and a pipe.  I decline; he consumes everything.\r\nThey all partake.  Paranoia sets in.  Am I a cop?  I break another rule, and have a Heineken.  I\'ve never mixed alcohol and the taxi.  But, then again, I\'ve never gone in a building with anyone.  \"Never get off the boat, unless you\'re going all the way.\"  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMore people, wealthy people, show up.  I, the taxi driver, am a curiosity: a bearded lady, or some other stock carnie freak.  I mean nothing to these people.  I mean nothing to anyone with money.  Any one of them would laugh while I died.  Pizza.  8 A.M.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWeirdness escalates.  I slip out.  Get coffee, Paul.  Get straight.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSober, cold, tired, I look at the clock: 9 A.M.  I don\'t care about the other driver waiting for the car; I don\'t care about making money; I need to sleep.  Sleep.  The light turns red.  I\'m drifting...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\"HEY!\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe wants to go to his parents home, near Midway Airport.  He doesn\'t like the religiosity of his Mexican neighbors.  He\'s a gay Black man in his late thirties.  He\'s fathered a child for a Lesbian couple that dances at Mitchell\'s...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNo...oh no.  Go away.  No more stories...no more taxi...\r\n</p>'),(3847,'<p>\r\nHours drag by. Especially three-to-six. <i>This is your life.</i> Computerscreen flourescent eternal white glow. Whose idea was this?\r\n</p><p>\r\nTap tap tap tap tap. No window. Gray cloth half-walls with no adornment, projected imagination backdrop. Kayaking. Hot-air balloons. </p><p>\r\nBlonde hair. Curly on the sides, stray straight swoop across HD sky blue \"limpet pool\" circular, liquid color. Delicately receding chin, often set firmly, thinly, like steel wire on the mast, a hint of quiver in the wind.</p><p>\r\nEmails never come. Giant men in suits, hearty laughter of Vegas trips and softball. Their shoes are all perfect. The coffee maker swooshes and roars. They look at me, unsure.</p><p>\r\n\r\n\r\n  '),(3848,'I was going to talk about some fights I have had in my life. Let me just say by no means am I a tough guy. I have gotten my ass kick plenty of times almost near death a couple times. Fighting sucks no matter how you try and spin it. I have felt bad when I got my ass kick and I have felt worse when I kick some ass. I have come to the conclusion that the only reason why someone fights is because there scared. Now I’m not talking about professional fighters like boxers, UFC and K1 those guys really know how to kick ass. I’m talking about the average street fight. The only time I ever fought was because I was afraid of what might happen to me. And I also had a lot of rage from my second step farther who slapped me around on me regularly, but that is for another post.\r\nThere are a bunch of fights that I could talk about. There is the one were I got my ass beat almost to death. There are the ones were I knocked the mother fucker out. And there a bunch other little ones in the middle. I want to talk about the one were I shit my pants. It was a summer night I was hanging with all my friends from the park. All my friends were older than me and real tough kids. Needless to say they got me into most of my fights. I was drunk of course and we decided to go over to Somerville. Somerville was the next town over from Medford where I lived. And we hated each other. There was this church that we just happen to walk by and there was a bunch of kids hanging out side the parking lot. I guess they had some type of late night communion class or some horse shit. So me and my drunken friends decided to go over and cause some trouble. I was shit faced and I had to take one of the biggest dumps of my life. I had been holding this shit cake in my ass for like two hours but I was so drunk I kept forgetting about it. And then all of a sudden I could feel the dog start to wag his tail and I would have to stand still for like five minutes until the muscles in my ass could take back over and fight this wall of shit. At this point I had full control over my ass. I was walking around the parking lot looking to start some trouble. And that’s when I saw her. She was blonde blue eyed and fucking gorgeous and she was looking right back at me. Dam I don’t think there is a better feeling in the world than looking across at a girl who is looking right back at you with that same look your giving her. The look of I want you to bitch. So I called her over. I got right up close to her and started asking her what her name was, and where she was from and I ws telling her how beautiful I thought she was. I totally forgot about this meat loaf that was dying to exit my body. This chick was into me and I was into her. It was going great just then this kid about my age steps in between me and my new girlfriend. He had a real mean look on his face and told me – this is my cousin so why you just take off and stop bugger her. I have to tell you I was scared. This kid looked pretty tough and he was pissed at me for trying to make moves on his cousin. Once he saw that I didn’t say anything back to him he started getting more confident and his voice was getting more enraged with me. Fucking get the fuck out of here asshole he said to me. The girl was telling him to stop, telling him we were just talking, but he wouldn’t have any of it. So I did what I had to do. I sucker punched him. There are two things you need to do if you’re ever going to use the sucker punch. First you need to be a pretty good actor. They have to think you are scared or just a nice guy and there is no way you want anything to do with them. And second you need to knock them out or at least hurt them bad. Let me tell you there is nothing worst than sucker punching some guy and him looking back at you with a smile. Cause now you really pissed him off. But that didn’t happen in this case. I leaned back and told him – hey man I don’t want to fight you and before the word fight got out of my mouth I hit this kid with everything I had. One punch and he went down to his knees. It was great the sucker punch worked. Now I just had to keep hitting him until he gave up, but I couldn’t. My friends were yelling at me hit him hit him but I couldn’t I could just sit there and not move. It wasn’t out of fear or that I felt bad. It was because my asshole muscle had just said FUCK IT and took the night off. You see I had been using every ounce of energy to keep my asshole shut so I didn’t shit my pants. But in all the confusion of meeting this girl, her fuck faced cousin fucking with me I had forgot about the chili brewing in my ass. And when I haled off to give the sucker punch I took all that energy away from my ass muscle and put it into the punch. Now I’m standing over this kid looking like a real badass with a nice hot dump in my pants. All of sudden one of the priest comes out and everyone just ran. I was walking the streets with a big dumb in my pants and I was pissed. That’s when a truck pulled up to me and it was my friends they told me to get in so I did. They were going off about the punch I had just thrown for around 30 seconds everyone was going nuts about me, and then it hit them. One of my friends cried out – what the fuck is that smell and then everyone in the truck started going off. I just told them I shit my pants. Cut to tires screeching and me being kick out of the car like I was some twenty dollar whore. I took off down the street and ran into these bushes in front of this house. I was standing right under someone’s window cleaning my ass with my sock. I could even hear the TV and them laughing in side the house. Not knowing some kid was cleaning his ass right out side there window. It was a mess. I couldn’t get it all of the shit cleaned up, but I got most of it. But I still smell like a baby who messed his Diaper. I was so mad at this point. This kid really fucked up my high. The girl was gone my friends were gone my underwear was gone and I had one sock. So I went to finish what I had started. I was going to really beat the shit out of this kid. I grabbed a picket from this fence and was on my way back to that church. As I got to the corner I saw the kid walking away probable going home. I called out to him but he wouldn’t turn around. I had given him a pretty good punch so the tides had change a little. He was probable afraid of me now. I kept yelling come on you pussy fight me fight me fagot. That was always a good word to use if you wanted to get someone to fight you. I kept calling him back using every word in the book. And then it happen he just turned around and said fuck it. I have to say that kind of scared me. Now he had the power. He was pissed. But I just charged him and I had a stick in my hand. As soon as we met I just started slamming away on his head. Just then I heard tires screech. I thought it was the cops so we both kind of stop for a second just to see who it was. It was the priest. He walked over to us and told us to knock it off and then gave us some speech about people and life and god. It was just like a church of latter day saints commercial. We just shook and said sorry as the priest had his hand on both of our shoulders. You can’t fuck with a priest I don’t care who you are if a priest gets involved fight over. A priest has the same powers as your mother. May be that’s why it’s so easy for them to get little boy ass. As we were about to leave with the spirit of Jesus in our harts the priest says what the hell is that smell, I think one of you boys step in some dog shit. We all checked out feet and walked away. Now I know that may sound like a movie ending but the priest really did say that.'),(3849,'Being the nerd I am, I made a list of all the concerts that I\'ve ever gone to,starting with Duran Duran when I was 13, and ending(most recently)with Elton John earlier this month.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nLooking over the concerts I\'ve been to, it amazes me  the different namds I have seen. How many of the bands are still around(quite a few of them-Duran Duran are currently touring the UK).\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThere is only one concert that I\'ve been to that I would say was sub par.Ride, in 1993. I didn\'t know what the deal with the band was at the time(turns out later, there was lots of inter-band strife),but it showed,Definitely not one of my favorite shows.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThe show I had the MOST fun at was when I saw Travis  in Dallas back in 2000.I knew several other list members would be there but I had no idea what they looked like.I was outside of the venue, listening to the band soundcheck, and was talking to this girl.My brother said something about where we had driven from, and she said \"Oh, are you Katie?I\'m Brianne!\"I had been talking to one of the list members for ages and didn\'t know it!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThe show itself was afreakingmazing.That was my frst time seeing Travis(and I\'ve seen them several times since.)\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nThe US Rollercoaster tour was really good.It was the Jesus and Mary Chain and a couple of other bands.Well, lt\'s just say that the show ran late(which is a possibillity when you have multiple bands on a bill) let\'s just say that the person who was picking me up that night was not happy about having to wait a little longer *g*'),(3850,'<p>\r\nAt my office job I get to smoke whenever I want. The place to smoke is outside the office by the elevator and stairwell. There is a large window that opens out onto the main street. While I suck back the wonderful Dunhill smoke I look out the window at the action out on the sidewalk and street. Over the course of this year I have seen a few very strange things. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor the most part, the people that walk by are oblivious to my watchful eyes, though once and a while I am caught staring at a hottie, an old man, or some other random passerby. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>The garbage posse</b><br>\r\nEvery morning just after I get to work and check to see what kind of email I have, I stroll out to my smoking spot by the elevator and make sure the garbage posse is ok. The posse is made up of retired old people who go around the neighborhood and pick up trash. It’s something that happens throughout the entire city. This posse is quite large; made up of about twelve women. The leader of the posse is an older man with a terribly scarred face. The scars seem to be burns. As much as people with disabilities are mocked in this country, he is respected by the women. He puts the women into squads and hands out gloves and bags. The women all have their own private brooms and dustpans. They sit around for a while and gossip and then head off to pick up trash. Its strange though. I haven’t seen them in a week or so. Perhaps they have given up their quest to make the streets trashless.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>The knife chase</b><br>\r\nAbout a week ago while having a smoke by the window I was looking at a nice bird in the tree across from me when I saw someone run out of the building I work. The man running out of the building was in his thirties and looked a bit destitute. He wasn’t a vagabond, but he definitely looked disheveled. A few seconds after he ran out, the man who cleans the building and wipes down the import cars in the parking lot came out wielding a 4 inch utility knife. He chased after the unknown man and stopped about twelve meters away from the front door and just glared at his prey. The prey obviously nervous stopped and started pleading with the old man who cleans the building. Something was said and the old man started after the younger man again. This time though the garbage posse was returning from their daily mission and intervened in the disturbance. I reported the incident to my manager and was told that he had seen the old man in more than a few fights with people on the street and was told that he had some sort of mental problem.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>The crying lady with a sore stomach</b><br>\r\nI was having a cigarette on a cold day in February and saw something sad. A woman was walking down the street clutching her stomach moaning. She was obviously in a lot of pain. She was walking slowly towards the hospital next door. Just a few meters away from the entrance to the hospital she bent forward still clutching her stomach and let out a blood curdling screech. She was obviously in a lot of pain and edged closer to the trunk of a tree and with one hand on the tree to prevent a fall she just stood there moaning and making absolutely horrible sounds. I thought about going down there to help her into the hospital, but just as the though occurred to me a few nurses who were returning from a break stopped to help her out.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Tears and a Tree</b><br>\r\nI guess being next door to a small hospital you are bound to see some tragic things. Not long after I saw the stomach pain lady propping herself up against a tree I saw an old man exit the hospital and just start weeping uncontrollably. I have no idea what had happened in the hospital, but I’m sure it was safe to say that he had lost something very dear. As he was walking down the sidewalk he just stopped, began hugging a tree and crying. It was one of the saddest things I have ever seen outside of a documentary. I thought about that man for some time after. His emotions were so honest and pure. It’s the saddest I have ever seen anyone. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>An accident</b><br>\r\nLooking out the window on a bright sunny spring day is especially nice. The birds, skirts, and beautiful women coming and going…oh yeah and buses hitting light posts. I was looking at a postcard image couple walking down the road hand in hand and heard a very close and very loud smash. I had not noticed the bus even coming down the street and when I looked straight ahead I could not miss a bus mirror wrapped around a light post. I looked at the situation for a few minutes and to this day I still can not understand how the bus had hit the light post or how the bus’s side mirror assembly had literally wrapped around the pole. The people on the bus got out and walked to the bus stop close by as the driver stood outside the bus looking in amazement at what happened. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Another accident</b><br>\r\nA taxi cut off another car and that car cut off a motorcycle. The argument that ensued was epic in proportion. The three vehicles didn’t even pull over to allow traffic to pass. They held up traffic for twenty minutes until a cop showed up to photograph the situation and get every ones statements. I made sure to go for another smoke 30 minutes after to check up on the situation and sure enough the three drivers were still going at it, though the cops had left the scene. It’s funny how cops will let people argue it out here. If this were in Toronto, I’m sure they would have been ordered to go about their business and get the fuck off the street. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Electro Buddhists</b><br>\r\nIn Seoul it is common for a Buddhist to walk down the street banging on a drum slowly. The monks go into shops and businesses and get money from the owners. It’s a strange occurrence that I still to this day don’t really understand. Nevertheless what I saw on that sunny day was quite different. I heard the drum sound coming from down the street and as the monks got closer I noticed that one of them had a bass drum on his back. The drums were drumming automatically at a slow rhythm. Strapped to the top of the bass drum was a megaphone with a wife that lead to a mic in his hand. Once and a while the slow walking monk would say something into the mic; his voice booming out of the megaphone. His counterpart would go in and out of shops collecting money which he put into an old brass kettle, which he was also banging on with a small stick. I started laughing and went back to my desk with a big grin on my face.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo you see, standing by the window is an interesting place to have cigarettes. I get to pollute my lungs, feed my addiction, and see some strange, sad, and wonderful things.  \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3851,'<p>\r\nCarla was noticeably agitated as she attempted to navigate us around Birmingham’s sprawling hinterlands. The M40 became the M42 somewhere south of its shadowy perimeter, and westbound seemed a natural choice. Map reading does not appear to be one of Carla’s strengths, though this accusation could arguably be leveled at the majority of women. It has often occurred to me that navigational aptitude is tightly coiled up within the Y chromosome. In retrospect though it was perhaps a mistake voice my theory as an icebreaker.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDefeated by the continuing silence, my imagination began to wander. I fancied myself in the place of Carla’s recently departed brother-in-law. I tried to imagine what must have gone through his mind in those sharp final moments. I tried to imagine the concoction of fear and concession he must surely have been party to on his long dark journey into oblivion. I pictured the wide-eyed flailing panic he would have exhibited as he was brutally dragged from his stuffy car boot into the cold moonlit milieu of wet lakeside woodland. His hands were ziplocked so harshly behind his back that the nylon cut cleanly into his flesh. Not that he noticed this as he was forced to his knees with what felt like a baseball bat to the back of the knees. I felt his unequivocal surrender as he was plunged further into the darkness. His head and shoulders customarily veiled with a musty potato sack. This was an archetypal gangland execution. It was a justified punishment and in such circumstances a bullet through the rear of each kneecap is a popular precursor to the main event. Ultimately, the poor bastard had no choice other than to accept his tragic fate. It was his own doing. Both Eadie and he must have known it would end this way. If he was honest he always knew that this night would come, but he could never have imagined how stark the cold reality would be. Some people will follow their heart no matter what the consequences. What remains of Carla’s brother-in-law is now steadfastly anchored to the bottom of Lough Neagh; another enduring testament to the fact that love doesn’t conquer all.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI thought about the fish feasting auspiciously on what was left of Steve’s face; a man I had never known. What troubled me more greatly though were the council estate chavs loitering on the footbridge. I spied them from a fair distance away. The Elizabeth Duke gold chains that they so proudly flaunted amplified the suns rays toward us, leaving persistent yellow speckles in my immediate field of vision which faded slowly to mere retinal bruises. The blistered stick-on sun visor offered minimal protection as we began changing lanes. I convinced myself that they were planning to deposit a brick through our windscreen as we passed beneath. You see that sort of thing on the news all the time. Evidently though, their intentions were entirely benign. They were simply killing time; time that should have been spent gaining an education. Gathering knowledge with which to haul themselves up from the lowest rung of society. The taxpayers will pick up the bill though. The DSS will pay their rent, and buy them booze and fags until, left to the mercy of the NHS, they die of lung cancer or liver failure. That is if the smack doesn’t claim them first. It’s a sad and vicious circle that is widening with the passing years.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCarla was raised in similar such environs. She is a typical example of what the tabloid press refer to as a geezer-bird. Her rugged demeanor undoubtedly stems from her somewhat ghetto upbringing. The streets of West Belfast are unforgiving custodians of youth. I have felt the wrath of her keen left hook on more occasions than I care to remember. I brushed Carla’s hostility aside, gratuitously clearing my throat and checking the mirrors. I am a careful yet confident motorist, but our circumnavigation of the west-midlands was an entirely haphazard affair; due partly in fact to Carla’s indirect refusal to cooperate. My directional instincts, coupled with a fair degree of guesswork led our stricken motor north onto the M5 for a junction or two. My stolen glances at page forty three suggested that the A5 could be followed all the way into Holyhead. I concluded that I should perhaps have briefly planned our route beforehand, but a last minute exit neatly secured the final leg of our journey to salvation.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(3852,'<p align=center>flow like coffee, your soul<br>                                                     \r\nthe beans, rough ground<br>                                 \r\nfor life to flow through,<gr>                             \r\ncreating a strong hot heady drink.</p>\r\n\r\n<p align=center>why add so much crap, all that<br>                                                  \r\nsaccharine and aspartame and<br>                                                \r\nimitation non-flavored creamer?<br>                                      \r\nit just covers up the taste of your<br>\r\nsoul beans -- whether<br>\r\nweak or bitter or chocolate, it\'s still<br>\r\nyour soul trying to seep through.</p>                                \r\n                                                                                \r\n<p align=center>this morning, perhaps<br>                                                              \r\nset aside the one-touch stain bucket, and<br>                                    \r\nrecycle your dusty bleach-colored filters--<br>                                     \r\nlet that fine-ground soul<br>                            \r\nswim freely, perhaps with some cardamom<br>                                      \r\nand rest at the bottom of a small cup, saying<br>                                  \r\n\"I\'m still here!\" as you sip.</p>\r\n'),(3853,'<P>years later, he could never figure out why he called the man, what he thought he would get out of it, where he thought it would go.  he was living in the projects next to the college, the only white person there and widely thought to be a cop.  he got a little over five hundred a month in checks.  the rent (including utilities) was one fifty-one.  he got food stamps, but he hardly ever cooked.  he hardly ever ate.<BR><BR>he called the man in mid-september.  his address wasn\'t in the phone book, so the man couldn\'t tell he lived in the projects.  he said he lived in the densely-wooded neighborhood at the top of the hill across the way where there was a tree that owned itself.  a couple of weeks later, the man called back and asked him to come over.<BR><BR>the studio was in a slightly high-toned neighboorhood maybe a mile from the college.  from the street, there was no way you could tell it was anything but a house, an unassuming little one-story job with wooden siding and a porch and a swing out front.  there wasn\'t a sign or a parking lot.  the only hint that it might have been something more was a thick piece of pressure-treated plywood laid across the left side of the steps going up to the porch.  but that didn\'t mean anything, really.  any house could have that.<BR><BR>the man could have passed for a real estate agent or loan officer.  in the interview, he lied and told the man he had extensive experience with sound recording and reinforcement.  he knew just enough to fake it.  as it turned out, it didn\'t matter.  all he ever did was put tape on the machine and fold up microphone cords.<BR><BR>the man showed him around.  when you came in the front door, you were in the living room.  it was basically a lounge area with couches, a coffee table, a TV and VCR, and a fireplace  with a framed print of james brown circa nineteen sixty-eight hanging above it.  the door to the vocal booth was to the left, and there was an adjacent bathroom that didn\'t have an exhaust fan and was consequently known as The Pisser.<BR><BR>a hallway ran from the front door all the way to the back of the house and ended at the door of the man\'s office.  walking from the living room back, the first thing on your left was a pair of double doors.  they led to the control room and were usually propped open.<BR><BR>the sound board was in front of you when you walked in.  he had expected it to be a lot bigger.  there were various kinds of processors and speakers mounted above it as well as a light such as you might see on top of a police car.  it lit up red and rotated (sans siren) whenever the doorbell rang.  there was also a curious little red sign with white lettering, the plastic rectangular kind slipped into grooved holders mounted on executives\' doors.  it said MEAT SALAD.  he forgot to ever ask what it meant, but years later (like now) guessed that perhaps it was a joke about vegetarianism, maybe.<BR><BR>there was an apple computer set up perpendicular to the right side of the sound board.  in the wall behind the computer stand, the twenty-four track, two-inch tape machine stood in a glass-fronted cabinet.  beside a stool in the corner, there was a rack that held the four or five reels of tape a typical album would take up.  in the other corner, a couch sat under the window that looked into the vocal booth.<BR><BR>the door to the tracking room was between the board and the couch.  it was a triangular room (vaguely diamond-shaped, really) that didn\'t have any right angles to it.  no parallel surfaces.  the ceiling was slanted.  it was lined with three or four types of wood (some highly varnished, some rough) and had windows looking into the vocal booth and the control room.<BR><BR>there was a drum kit in the middle of it that pretty much stayed set up.  bands would use it instead of bringing their own.  (which might go a long way towards explaining why everything recorded at that studio sounds the same.)  all the drums had microphones built into them.  you plugged a cord right into the shell.  under the window looking into the vocal booth, there was a hammond organ with a leslie speaker attached to it.<BR><BR>the walls featured cabinets holding various bits of equipment and supplies:  microphones, effects pedals, stands, cords, drum heads, strings.  a chest of drawers in what would be the corner (if there wasn\'t a wooden panel covering it) held small percussion instruments.<BR><BR>The Shitter was slightly down the hall on the right.  the kitchen was further on, across the hall.  as you walked in, there was a glass cabinet door on your left.  that was the back of the tape machine.  the kitchen contained a refrigerator, sink, microwave, coffee maker.  no oven, no dishwasher.  there were some cabinets above the counter.  high in one of these, on his first day there, he discovered a bottle of crown royal and a curious large wooden trinket box.  he thought, ho ho, what could this be?  it held a substantial quantity of the best marijuana he had smoked in his life.  by far.  he estimated that there was maybe half a pound there when he started.  when he finished his tenure, he estimated that there was not.<BR><BR>the isolation room was further down the hall on the right.  it had a terrazzo floor with blue flecks and green flecks in it.  the walls were covered from floor to ceiling with hexagonal tiles that matched the blue in the terrazzo.  the ceiling was pressed tin.  there were a vox AC30 amp, a fender twin, and marshall stack set up in the middle of the room.  there were cases piled up along the walls which contained various kinds of guitars, some of them rare and worth tens of thousands of dollars.<BR><BR>the last door on the left led to a little room with a couch and a pedal steel guitar with an unframed velvet painting of elvis presley draped over it as a cover.  it was a kind of auxilary lounge.  there was also a rack full of digital audio tapes the man had made of certain music recorded in his studio, music he had either engineered or produced <I>and</I> engineered.  and then there was the office.  just the usual.  desk, etc.  files with receipts, bills, equipment manuals.<BR><BR>he started on a monday or a tuesday in early october.  the working day ran from one to five, a break for dinner, then seven to eleven.  musicians aren\'t usually morning people.  for those eight hours, the man charged one thousand dollars.  for those with major label budgets, it was more.<BR><BR>of course, he wasn\'t paid anything for putting tape on the machine, folding microphone cords, stringing guitars, making coffee, and cleaning the bathrooms, but he supplemented his income in other ways.<BR><BR>when he started, a band from north carolina had been there working on an album for about a week.  the band featured a lesbian couple on vocals and guitar and two very white men on bass and drums.  the guitarist had been the bassist in a slightly notable college rock band.  all four members were on the high side of thirty and still holding out for greatness.  he felt sorry for them.  the guitartist\'s songs were well-constructed, had a few catchy melodies, but didn\'t have any life to them.  he likened it to the way that only a few people he encountered had that supernal light in their eyes.  he could see it in a girl\'s eyes fifty yards off and know that she was one of the original fuckmonsters.  only a few people had it.  everybody else was just marking time.<BR><BR>they finished recording the rhythm tracks, and the drummer and bassist left.  they left behind a drum machine.  they lost a drum machine.<BR><BR>a few microphones and effects pedals went next.  that all got funneled through a music store in town that bought used equipment.  also:  plastic-wrapped cds of albums the man had produced, vintage 45s, commemorative books.  all sold downtown.<BR><BR>in his spare moments, he perused the rack of dats.  most of them were of bands he\'d never heard of, but there were a few choice ones:  an album by a local duo whose raw, rudimentary style had garnered them a certain slight amount of recognition.  it had been produced and then abandoned by the record company.  well, it was about to be released.  in a warped kind of way, he felt like some sort of cultural robin hood.  that was how he justified it to himself, anyway.<BR><BR>on the weekends, the man was flying to nashville to remix an album for a local jam band.  (actually, he was going through hours and hours of sloppily-recorded performances and trying to salvage enough material for a live album.  and then remixing the best of <I>that</I>.)  so there were quite a few dats of these sessions lying around.  presently, there weren\'t so many lying around.<BR><BR>(the man must have felt like he was being gaslighted there at first.  and then he must have known.  he could never figure out why the man didn\'t confront him about it before he did.  maybe he was afraid.)<BR><BR>(the money was going in part to alcohol but mostly to a video blackjack machine in a bar owned by his friend.  the friend was running a clandestine gambling operation of sorts.  he wasn\'t on crack, which was what the studio owner eventually accused him of.)<BR><BR>he also found a dat in the rack that he suspected was the long-rumored solo album by the singer of the group that was scheduled to be in the studio at the end of the month.  everything he\'d heard about it suggested that it was laughable, unlistenable, embarrassing.  he left it where it was.  he had too much respect for the man.<BR><BR>the studio was originally the man\'s home.  when he and his wife decided to have children, they bought the house next door and moved into it.  they had twin three-year old girls who would unexpectedly burst into the studio and race each other through every room they could get into.  they had a taste for trying to take one out at the knees while one was at the kitchen sink washing coffee cups or the occaisonal champagne flute.<BR><BR>(the guitarist for the band that was going to be in the studio had twin girls as well, roughly the same age.  he asked the man if it was him or the guitarist who had been slippin\' around.  the man said it must have been the guitarist.)<BR><BR>the band from carolina soldiered on (the women, anyway), recording vocals, violin parts, guitar overdubs and punch-ins.  it was all run through a computer program named pro tools that took the element of chance out of music, so it was no big whoop.  a monkey couldn\'t fuck it up.  (which might be another reason for the homogeniety.)  they were supposed to finish a week before hallowe\'en, on a friday.  the other band was due in on monday.<BR><BR>late friday afternoon, he was told to wait on the porch for a ryder truck to arrive and help load some equipment into the basement.  when it got there, the driver took him aside and told him he was a country songwriter and slipped him a demo tape.  in the back of the truck, there were seven road cases on wheels plus an irregularly-shaped case strapped to the wall that obviously held and upright bass.  it reminded him of a sarcophagus, for some reason.<BR><BR>the boxy cases were all stencilled with the name of a trio that was from the city where the guitarist now lived.  a defunct trio.  they must have been borrowed or inherited.  or bought from the estate.  probably not bought.  he wondered why they didn\'t use their own cases and guessed they didn\'t have any out there to begin with.<BR><BR>he unlocked the gate in the fence between the houses and opened the garage door on the side of the basement, and they got everything in there.  the girls came out with their nanny and watched them for a second then went back in.  the nanny was pretty <I>foine</I>.<BR><BR>after they were finished, he locked the gate and lowered the door and checked out the contents of the boxes.  lots of synthesizers, a national steel guitar, a harmonium, a disassembled xylophone or vibraphone, he didn\'t know which, and a little case lined with blue foam with twelve small divots cut into it.  six of these held e-bows.<BR><BR>he pocketed one and thought, <I>with all these, they won\'t miss it.</I><BR><BR>he later gave it to a band that thought he was managing them.  they considered him the shit.<BR><BR>(it turned out they were going to use the e-bows on the pedal steel, but didn\'t look in the box until the last day of his stay there.  the absent one was the last straw, basically, and led to his immediate dismissal.)<BR><BR>in order to finish mixing the carolina band\'s album, they had to extend the session friday night to three-thirty.  he was supposed to be at a hole in the wall club at midnight to see the band he was pseudo-managing and complained vociferously that he was missing the show.  he was told to get over it.     <BR><BR>he was told to be there at ten-thirty on monday.  the studio owner (who was going to engineer the album) had told him he\'d try him out in the big leagues for a week and then pay him seventy-five bucks a day if he did alright.  the band was expected to be there four or five weeks.<BR><BR>everyone starting arriving around eleven.  the band\'s equipment guy showed up first, helping set things up.  then came the  guitarist and the additional sidemen (a guy from the guitarist\'s new city and somebody from a band he knew nothing about.  all he knew was that this guy had replaced a dead guitarist/vocalist in a reunion of a seminal seventies band.), the bassist, the producer, the assistant engineer.  (he himself was going to be the second assistant engineer.  if they had been in england, he would have been called the tea boy.)<BR><BR>the singer got there at twenty to one.  it felt like they were about to storm a beachhead.<BR><BR>everybody was talking about the headline in that morning\'s paper:  a young man man had been walking on a quiet street not far from the studio, in the same semi-genteel neighborhood, and had encountered a young woman who offered to perform oral sex on him for ten dollars.  he must have thought that sounded like a bargain.  she took him into some bushes between two houses and pulled a knife on him, cut his arm pretty badly and took his wallet.<BR><BR>the drummer was late.  they called him at quarter to two, and he said he was on his way.  in the meantime, the guitarist and bassist played some recordings they\'d made.<BR><BR>the studio owner had instructed him to carry out his duties while remaining as invisible as possible.  stay quiet.  don\'t engage anyone in conversation.  if they talk to you, fine, but not otherwise.  make plenty of coffee.  watch the toilet paper.  replace a roll when it\'s half-used.  above all, keep plenty of beer iced down.  in addition to the fridge, there were two coolers and quite a few bags of ice in the deep freeze in the basement.  they liked tecate and tuborg and amstel light.  also some microbrews.  the singer drank the odd bottle of perrier jouet.<BR><BR>his station was to be the stool in the corner of the control room, close to the tape machine.  once the tape was loaded, he was to go scout around and do everything else.<BR><BR>the drummer showed up, hung out for a minute, then rounded up his three bandmates and took them back to the room with the pedal steel.  they were back there for about an hour.  none of the rest of them said much.  the studio owner ran checks on the outboard equipment, and the assistant engineer organized the logs that would be used to remember what was on each track, the board settings, etc.  the equipment guy and one of the sidemen played backgammon at the small kitchen table while the other musician watched something on american movie classics.  george sanders was in it.<BR><BR>there were periodic torrents of shouting from the back room.  he went out on the porch and chain-smoked.  the drummer came out, said, <I>later on</I>, went down the street to his truck and drove off.<BR><BR>he went back in.  the other three were still in the back.  he went out to the porch and smoked some more.  twenty minutes later, the singer came out, didn\'t look at him, didn\'t say anything, sat in the swing.  the young man crushed his cigarette in the butt can and went in.<BR><BR>everybody else was in the living room, sitting or standing.  the bassist had been crying.  the guitarist was enraged.  he was doing most of the talking.  the young man went into the control room and got up on the stool.  he exchanged looks with the assistant engineer.  big eyes.<BR><BR>they talked out there for a long time.  the singer came back in and joined them.<BR><BR>after about an hour and a half, the bassist said, <I>so, what are we supposed to do now?</I><BR><BR>the singer said, <I>well, you could walk down prince avenue and get your dick sucked.</I><BR><BR>they exploded with laughter.  they laughed a long time, needed it, he guessed.<BR><BR>the guitarist said, <I>gentlemen, let us repair to the bar.</I><BR><BR>they probably went to the coolest place in town, cool partly because that\'s where they went the most, partly because of its jukebox, partly because of the parrots and mynah birds and budgerigars scattered around the room in cages, partly because of its clientele, partly because there was a sign on the door that said OPEN FOR INSPECTION, partly because it was sometimes tended by a mystic poet who translated the poetry of a much older mystic.  the bartender drank.  the older gentleman (presumably) did not.<BR><BR>the band came and went throughout the three days that followed.  no recording was done.  it was thought that they were hoping the drummer would come back.  he had been acting a little erratically ever since a brain injury a couple of years before.<BR><BR>the drummer came back wednesday afternoon.  the guitarist and bassist played their demos for him.<BR><BR>he said, <I>where\'s the hooks?  none of this shit has got the first fuckin\' hook.</I><BR><BR>he stayed for a little while then left again.<BR><BR>the young man had one conversation with any of them, with the guitarist.  he told him about his daughters.  he had gone down to the basement one day, and they were riding their tricycles around in circles.  he asked them what they were doing, and they said they were on tour.<BR><BR>he also told him about when he met bo diddley.  bo showed him all his guitars several of which he had built.  he looked at one with numerous switches and dials.  one of the dials had a large red plastic jewel glued to it.  he asked what that one was for, and bo said, <I>oh, that\'s the <U>magic</U> knob.  i\'ll turn that mother on high and steal your woman.</I><BR><BR>on thursday, the young man arrived before anyone else.  the studio owner was not around.  while he waited in the swing, he noticed that the lid of the mailbox mounted beside the door was pushed open with something.  he decided to check it out.  it was a box of blank checks.  he pocketed it.<BR><BR>when the studio owner showed up, he looked in the mailbox and said, <I>did you see some checks in here?</I><BR><BR>he said, <I>no.  no, i didn\'t.</I><BR><BR>the guy gave him a long look then unlocked the door.  that afternoon, the guitarist and bassist and the sidemen decided that would kill some time and prepare the pedal steel.  (<I>prepare</I> as in <I>john cage could </I>prepare<I> a fuckin\' piano.</I>)  they moved the guitar into the tracking room and busted out the e-bows, and then... bang.<BR><BR>the owner asked him to come back to his office and told him he wasn\'t going to call the police, he just wanted the young man to leave and stay gone.  the young man gave him his checks and told him everything else was gone.<BR><BR>word of the drummer leaving got out into the community thursday afternoon.  that night a local musician, a disabled man, was scheduled to give a benefit concert at a large antebellum theatre.  as an encore, he came back out and played a song from the band\'s first album.  second song on side two.  everybody in town was there.  the man had a wonderful high nasal peckerwood voice.  it was quite a transcendant moment.<BR><BR>the drummer\'s departure was announced to the press on friday.<BR><BR>they must have started recording the album that next week or sometime after that.  when it came out, he read the lyrics sheet and found a song that he thought was written about him.  or the drummer.  maybe both.  <I>you want to go out friday</I> could apply both to the young man complaining about missing the show he wanted to go to that friday night and also the drummer leaving the band officially on friday.  hallowe\'en.<BR><BR>and there was the phrase <I>the intern was a mess</I> and then his name not too far after that.  references to alligators.  they knew he was from florida.  one could also read addiction into it.<BR><BR>who knows.  it was fun.  something to write about.</P>'),(3854,'<P>\r\nIt was a thursday night, just before midnight, after a week long push to finish his project that he was <I>forced</I> to question the reality that life had put together...\r\n</P><P>\r\nBeep bop booop beep *pause* beep bop boop beep.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"This is Austin.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Look here fuck face, I heard about that stunt you pulled today.  You think I\'m going to let that \"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Huh?  Who is..?\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"slide?  Fuck that.  I know where you live, motherfucker!\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n*click*\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nWhat the shit.  Some crazy asshole.  Oh my god.  Where did I learn to talk like this.  Fuck.  Shit.  Oops.  Gah--uhm.  Hm.  This is just what I need, a little interruption from this crap.  I hate cell phones.  I think it is Miller Time, only really it is Bushmill\'s time; aka time to go to Nunu\'s.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n<i>The cool night air was a relief.  In the evenings, the proximity to the ocean makes itself known in the breeze.  Like waves of water washing up on shore with tangles of kelp and other green shit knotted up here and there, shells too, the breeze feels good to the touch.  It smells like beach.</I>\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nCarded again.  What the Hell.  I come here three nights a week for a year and a half, like they don\'t know me now.  They need my identification card.  <i>Look at me!</I>  Reading a book at a bar is I.  Mr. Smooth operator.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Hi.\" A little asian number with the black glasses like in Rushmore.  Looks like a freshman.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Uh.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"What are you reading?  Why are you reading a book in a bar?  I go to SDSU.  I\'m studying Econ.  I like popular fiction.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Like grocery store novels?\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Yes.  I read so much dry Econ stuff... I come here so much they don\'t card me anymore.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nDrop dead, kid.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Well, I\'ll see you later, OK? I\'m here, like, all-the-time.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI never saw her again.  But first, in walks the bouncer.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"IS THAT THE GUY?!\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nOh, nice, a commotion.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Yes, him.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"YOU LIKE TO FEEL CHICKS UP, ASS FACE?  FEEL UP THIS.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nDuring this time, I\'m sort of taking it all in.  Sure, I read books at bars, but that is just until the good stuff starts.  Like right now, when this bouncer gets done yelling and plants a fist right in my up turned face.  I think it was a sucker punch.  I was reading a book for Christ\'s sake.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI didn\'t have time to understand that the salty sugary candy in my mouth were teeth lolling about on my tongue and not glass shards or perhaps some candy.  I had effectively no time at all before the behemoth grabbed me by my clothes, out of the booth, and threw me into a wall.  No biggie.  The place is a shack.  I\'d rather hit the cardboard wall there than the concrete floor.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI don\'t always remember what I did.  Fantasy and reality gets all mixed up.  I\'m thinking about grabbing titty / I am grabbing titty.  I swear she was talking about school or books or something.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nYou don\'t have the kind of problem I have without learning to take a beating.  I think breifly about all the ways to learn to take a beating.  \'Take a beating.\'  That is a phrase that is well used in popular culture.  It is fairly universally applied though.  Take those bighorn sheep that head butt each other.  I\'m waiting for the boot to the stomach.  Just like in the movies, when you are getting beat up and you land on the ground, the boot is coming.  I mumble to the girl, \"It is economics.  You get beat down, you get beat on the ground.  You don\'t stop getting beat.\"  I\'m reaching all over the place.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAnd yet, the beating stops.  <i>Ass Face</I>.  Did he really call me that?  Ass face.  I\'m gathering my wits.  Aparently this beating doesn\'t end up outside.  I grab a bar railing and pull myself up.  There was just a fight.  Everyone is quiet, stairing at the scene.  It hasn\'t been long I don\'t think.  Where is this bouncer at?  What happened to him and why didn\'t he finish the job?\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nYes, teeth are missing.  Well, not missing really, just not attached.  I do not spit them out like in the movie.  I put them in my hand, picking them off my tongue.  Two of them.  These are keepsakes.  Maybe I can get a dollar from the faeries.  I live in Hillcrest after all.  If guy can\'t get a dollar from a faerie in Hillcrest, well, he isn\'t working the balls.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThe bouncer, my new pal, is laying on his back.  His red flannel shirt is getting redder.  \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Jesus man,\" the bartender said.  \"He was just doing his job.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n<I>Sirens from a distance getting louder.</I>\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Eh?\"  The bar has cleared back from me.  I\'m faced with an array of drinks, none of them mine.  I take one that looks like it might be a jack or rum and coke.  The bouncer moans.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\"Yyuuuh.  Muhhhhh.  Ffffth.\"   He stops breathing.  Now there is a problem.  I head for the back door.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n<I>It is a beautiful Southern California night like all the others.  The air is almost green with irrigation.  Crisp like breaking ice up for a drink with a pick.  One of those hand picks. </I>\r\n</P>'),(3855,'<P>i\'m looking at the last days of free cable.<BR>back to my friends at cbs.<BR>i never stopped watching<BR>the evening news and letterman, though.<BR>six months.<BR>another solstice ticked off on somebody\'s fingers.<BR>not mine.<BR><BR>i found a manila envelope<BR>in a box of books and papers mom and dad brought down to me.<BR>i\'ve asked them not to bring anything else.<BR>i can\'t shoehorn any more stuff in here,<BR>and i\'m holding out hope<BR>that they won\'t put the house on the market<BR>until all my stuff [and my sister\'s]<BR>is out of there.)<BR><BR>there\'s writing on the front of the envelope.<BR>a guy i met in the psych ward at community put it there.<BR>it says:<BR><BR><CENTER>Daimoku: NAM MYO HO REN GAY KYO<BR><BR>translation:  my infusion with the<BR>mystic law of cause and effect<BR>through sound</CENTER><BR><BR>- Ring a bell (chime, etc.).<BR>- Say the Daimoku 3 times (slow).<BR>- Face east, eyes open.<BR>- Envision in your mind what you want.<BR>- Say thank you.<BR><BR>- This is your <U>wish</U> <U>machine</U>.<BR><BR>hell, i thought, that sounds pretty good.<BR><BR>i didn\'t have a bell.<BR>hit the two junk stores on main street in search of one.<BR>closest thing i could find was a toy xylophone.<BR><BR>i used to have one just like it, i think.<BR>fisher-price.<BR>on wheels.<BR>yellow nylon string on one end<BR>so that if you were too lazy to pick it up and carry it,<BR>you could pull it behind you.<BR>another room,<BR>another show.<BR><BR>metal bars painted in rainbow order,<BR>red, the largest, on the left, and so on.<BR>there was one mallet with it,<BR>unfinished wooden head,<BR>wooden handle painted yellow.<BR>i can\'t remember if it originally came with one or two.<BR>probably one.<BR><BR>circa age three,<BR>my mallet doubled as a microphone<BR>when i pretended i was mike douglas.<BR>he used to open every show with a song,<BR>so whenever i felt there was a quorum<BR>large enough to confer some attention on me,<BR>i would bust out a number.<BR>fuck knows what.<BR>shades of things to come.<BR><BR>when the song was over,<BR>i\'d throw the mallet off to the side<BR>like mike did with his microphone.<BR>i guess there was a stage hand who caught his every day.<BR><BR>i wonder if john and yoko opened with a song<BR>when they would guest-host.<BR><BR>george, the guy in the other half of the duplex,<BR>disappeared six weeks back.<BR>i\'d noticed that his car had been missing,<BR>but thought he\'d gone back to prague<BR>on vacation or something.<BR><BR>the landlord came around a couple of weeks ago<BR>and asked if i\'d seen him,<BR>said he was ten days late on the rent.<BR>he went in the apartment (illegally, i think).<BR>when he came out,<BR>he said it looked like<BR>george didn\'t take anything with him.<BR>his clothes were still there<BR>as well as the furniture, TV, kitchen stuff,<BR>food in the fridge.<BR>spooky.<BR><BR>so mike gave him another week<BR>then sold everything in there.<BR><BR>i\'ve been spoiled,<BR>free to play<BR>my fucking<BR>PUNK ROCK MUSIC<BR>as loud as i want,<BR>as late as i want.<BR>my DESCENDANTS<BR>and my CRAMPS<BR>and my GERMS<BR>and my DAMNED.<BR><BR>(the flipside to that little number<BR>is that if you hear something in the night,<BR>you can\'t blame it on you lummox neighbor.)<BR><BR>mike put a red FOR RENT sign in the yard,<BR>out on the corner.<BR><BR>last night, i dreamed i burned the other half down.<BR><BR>i go to the library, the gym,<BR>the grocery store and the convenience store.<BR>i see people at those places,<BR>know them well enough to speak to, but that\'s it.<BR><BR>i see my family about once a month,<BR>the doctor once a month,<BR>and my therapist takes me to lunch every other week.<BR><BR>and i\'m happy.<BR>for now.<BR>it\'s good to be alone sometimes.<BR><BR>my therapist says i should join the writers group at the library,<BR>but i\'m not going to.<BR><BR>i\'ve run out of contacts,<BR>so i\'ve been having to wear glasses,<BR>which brings my self-esteem down by about fifty percent.<BR>they help keep the women off me, though.<BR><BR>in the last week, instead of swimming at the gym,<BR>i\'ve been biking out to the beach.<BR><BR>you go out main street to the highway, turn north,<BR>and the cut-off\'s just past the valu lodge.<BR>the beach is about three miles west of there.<BR><BR>i saw a couple of dolphins<BR>in the water off the causeway tuesday.<BR>or wednesday.<BR><BR>i lay on the towel i\'ve had forever,<BR>green and blue and yellow,<BR>and read <B><I>go down, moses</B></I>,<BR>try to keep all the characters straight.<BR>that\'s what i\'d like to do -<BR>a collection of interlocking stories.<BR><BR>after about an hour, i\'ll get in the water<BR>and swim as hard as i can,<BR>not too far out,<BR>underwater a little.<BR><BR>i think about the lyrics that go:<BR><BR><I>letting the days go by<BR>let the water hold me down</I><BR><BR>you can let the days go by all you want,<BR>but water doesn\'t hold you down.<BR>it spits you out.</P>'),(3856,'<P>\r\nWhen I first started writing all this, I wasn’t sure how public it would be (or where it would go, for that matter).  For now \"minimal\" is a good answer – also probably the safest one.  First off, my preference is to call it \"dancing\" as opposed to \"stripping\".  Dancing is definitely one of my favorite hobbies and now I’m paid to do it.  I also dance formally (meaning with a dance company), though at the moment I am paying someone else to teach me, as opposed to being paid to show my skills.  The day will come soon enough.  That section of my life is not, however, what this will cover.  My life is very segmented and needs to remain that way to maximize performance, and again, safety, so thanks for all your understanding.\r\n</P><P>\r\nLet me start this off by saying that this job, this industry, is one of the most difficult ones out there.  It is physically and emotionally demanding and can lead to addictions, break-ups, prostitution, you name it.   At the same time it can be completely rewarding.  Generally there are two ways of looking at it from a dancer’s perspective, as with most jobs.  You can despise the customers, loathe coming in, get in fights; beat yourself up inside and end up in debt over your head.  On the other hand, you can see yourself in a completely empowering position, have fun, get in terrific shape, and have people literally throw money at you just for doing what you love – dancing.  Being female of course also brings other monthly challenges, both physical and emotional, and more intense (in my case) than I can explain.  I am also bipolar but being unemployed for so long has left a pretty lasting depression burned into my brain.  In these first few days back to work I have managed to work about 20 hours straight, in stilettos, while still taking formal classes and going to a rehearsal, and had cramps on top of all of that.  My goal is to keep smiling and keep making money; so far it has worked. It\'s also nice to be able to come home to a retardedly sexy boyfriend that you love, and know that he knows you were thinking about and missing him through your stint at work.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI began dancing as an adult entertainer some time in 2002.  My bartender’s license led me to seek out related jobs, and an ad was in the paper for bartenders and drink servers.  The phone call was made; the interview was set; the management... lied.  At that point a warning light should have kicked in, but apparently it was more of a check engine light instead.  Upon arrival I was told that they were looking for only \"drink servers\", meaning girls that would do everything the regular dancers did excluding a stage show.  Normally drink serving was more of a bartender’s assistant, so I kept my hopes up and applied.  At least the outfits would be cute, right? \r\n</P><P>\r\nAfter the initial application, trip to the police department (San Diego clubs require a license to work if you’re not a general employee), and chat with the owner, I was in my knee-high PVC boots and prancing around the club.  Our shift choices were 3 p.m. to 12 a.m. or 9 p.m. to 4 a.m.  The latter was not an option until you were considered ready, which could have been anywhere from a week to a month.  Eventually a 6 p.m. to 10 p.m. shift was added, but it never really mattered.  After a few hours of getting used to being very scantily clad I realized that drink serving was a fictional position made up to get the girls ready for being a regular dancer.  I also realized it was the only way to make money.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThis next section covers the business aspect – how we are paid, how the clubs are paid, and how it all ties in.  First off, you have to pay $161 out of your own pocket to get started (unless you work out something with management and they cover you).  To get a license you need a double set of fingerprints, a background check through the Department of Justice, and a fee for the actual licensing.  This works out as $12 for the prints, a $32 money order (ONLY!) for the DOJ, and $116 (cash preferred) for the fee.  [The price in 2002 broke down to $110 total, however the manager covered me because I had less than $100 to my name when I started.  Next month the price is being raised to $200].  The fingerprints are taken in one office (I went to the Clairemont one since it had normal business hours), and the rest is done downtown.  The downtown office is only open Monday through Thursday, from 8 a.m. to 4 p.m., so not only do you need to drive around town, you need to have gas to get there.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNow that you’ve got your license and the club has agreed to have you work, what do you wear?  True, since this place is 18+ you get to be naked an awful lot (or topless at 21+ clubs), but you can’t be naked ALL the time.  In fact, you have to remain at least 6 feet away from all patrons when you’re performing nude.  This brings us back to the conundrum of what to wear.  For dancers just getting started with pole work, one trick is to wear boots.  These allow you to have a lot of friction and fake your way through some seemingly complex moves, or at least take your top off without using your hands to cling desperately on.  Ladies, this isn’t just wearing the wrong pair of shoes to the club and having some drunken fool step on your foot after you’ve waited in line for 30 minutes in the cold.  This is that plus a hangover (for good measure) and dancing (not just standing) nonstop for at least six hours - in those shoes.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI digress, back to the matter of being ready to go and having nothing to wear, seriously this time.  This is not just \"the ass pants\" being in the wash and having no idea where that black top that gives you perfect cleavage is.  This is not just feeling fat in every single outfit you own.  This is trying to figure out how to make yourself look cute in a faded bikini and fraying shoes, realizing that your \"stripper gear\" screams out that you just started working here one hour ago – and YOU HAVE TO BE NAKED for one customer who is not quite feeling up to tipping you that dollar.  This is the epitome of Having Nothing To Wear.  To top it off, you will be working with females who have regular customers, potential bitches, thieves and/or liars, and you don’t even have a change purse to carry around.  Quick, don’t forget to smile!\r\n</P><P>\r\nOnce you stop hyperventilating and come out of that corner, things will get better, albeit more expensive.  Rule Number One for shopping: A good pair of shoes is worth the money.  Shoes generally cost about $60 in shops, but get the $90 pair.  I recommend Tony Shoes – they evenly distribute your weight and are as comfortable as 6\" stiletto heels can get.  I know the saying is \"the higher the hair, the closer to God\", but that isn’t the case if you’re just getting started.  If you have weak ankles aim for a 5\" pair, and get something open-toed.  Ankle straps are also good, and usually quite cute.  In any case make sure that you can at least walk a little bit, stand on one foot, and walk without holding your arms out for balance.  High heels lengthen your legs and tighten up your lower frame, if you’re new though the best option is a pair with a platform sole as well.  This ensures that there will be less of that standing on tiptoes feeling all night.  Boots are usually easier to work with – they allow your foot to be basically flat while giving the impression that it is not.  Your spine and general posture will be completely affected by your choice of footwear, so either way you choose, opt for comfort and dependability.  More expensive ones of course will have a better life, and a quick secret for touchups is this: black shoes allow you to Sharpie over the scuffmarks.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNow that you’re a little informed and definitely in it for the duration, you’ve spent around $250 getting a license and a pair of shoes.  You need something to wear, and no, last year’s bathing suit is only going to cut it during the beginning of Monday’s day shift.  At my club the girls work about 35 hours per week, split into two shifts.  A basic wardrobe should include two pairs of boy-cut shorts (or the lace panty equivalent), a skirt, two or three tops, a dress, some form of underwear.  If you like the schoolgirl look then add in some knee-high stockings or fishnets.  Thankfully this is Southern California and there are places that sell dancer-specific clothing all over the place – including FamMart and Kobe’s Swap Meet.  Most clubs have a \"costume lady\" that comes in once a week or twice a month, mine currently does not.  Costume ladies or house moms are great for clothing repairs or custom items, all at affordable prices.  If you sew, even better – give me your name and number!  Frederick’s of Hollywood is also good for basic items.  The generic boy-cut shorts (or lace equivalent) can be found for $10, Hot Topic usually sells them for around $20 but tends to have a terrible quality of fabric and you’ll be buying another pair soon after.  Underwear is almost always on sale at Victoria’s Secret for anywhere from $5 to $20.  Items after that start around $30 per piece.  Color-wise I would opt for basic black pieces with one solid color (white counts), and save the multi-colors for when you start buying specific costumes.  Mixing and matching is your way to go.  Looking at the basic list I made, pretending you found things on sale, got a dancer’s discount (thanks, Dr. Love’s!), and tried to find quality material, your wardrobe right now can realistically look like this:\r\n</P><P>\r\nShorts $20, Skirt $30, Top $15, Top $20, Top $30, Dress $30, Underwear $20 = $165.\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou’re feeling better about things at this point, so you know that Wet \'n’ Wild coral lipstick is probably not going to cut it.  Good girl.  I will get my recommendations out of the way first.  I am rather pale and enjoy the pinup girl look or dramatic colors – so MAC (maccosmetics.com!) is my first choice.  For coloring and accents, it’s MAC all the way.  They have a fantastic Russian Red lipstick (made specifically for Madonna!), and a nice lip lacquer (lipglass) that compliments and gives it that wet look.  The lacquer tends to feather and get everywhere (on your drink, in your hair, smudged on your hand, etc), but it makes your teeth appear quite white in the dark.  Their pigments are also fun to play with and work well for different areas.  They also have a nice cream blush (cheekhue) that requires only a little dab to give you a glow, meaning it can last for quite awhile.  STAY AWAY FROM GLITTER.  Shimmer is good.  Glitter is not, excluding in very small doses in unexpected places – maybe a touch on the earlobe, or a dab in the hollow of your throat.  The previous manager used to hand out little packets of watery glitter and tell us to put it everywhere, because men that came in expected it.  If they went home with some glitter stuck to them, their wives would think they had a mistress and either a divorce or a phenomenal sex life would ensue.  Right.  He clearly never had glitter stuck to his contact lens.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBack to your budget (which I promise will increase) – did you know that Food4Less carries cosmetics?  Yes indeed they do.  If you don’t want to spend $25 on a tube of Stila lipstick (great color and texture, terrible feathering), Ralph’s also carries the exact same things as Food4Less, but usually at a dollar or two higher.  Walmart is also a faithful standby.  In any case, I recently picked up an entirely new base of things (foundation, concealer, sponges, eyeliner, shaving cream, razors, exfoliating scrub, nail polish) for about $40 at Ralph’s.  Another rule to go along with good shoes is the makeup rule – customers are paying for a nice face.  You know when your makeup is good, and when it’s bordering on circus time.  You also know the difference between daytime and nighttime makeup, as well as (hopefully) stage versus regular makeup.  Building from the ground up and getting a MAC gift card for your birthday, a basic makeup wardrobe consists of: moisturizer ($10 avg.), brushes ($16 avg.), sponges ($5 avg.), powder ($20 avg.), foundation ($25 avg.), concealer ($15 avg.), blush ($16 avg.), eye shadow ($15 avg.), eyeliner ($15 avg.), lipstick ($15 avg.), lip liner ($13 avg.), lip gloss ($12 avg.) mascara ($10 avg.), and possibly an eyebrow pencil ($13 avg.).  There are lots of little tools (blotting papers, tweezers, bags, etc), but that we will leave for another time – along with wigs, extensions, falsh eyelashes, etc.  Luckily you can pick up false eyelashes for a few dollars at Walmart if you’re into that sort of thing.  I am.  The above basic items total $200.  Non-MAC makeup will save you about $150. \r\n</P><P>\r\nOnce you get your makeup and wardrobe, a few other touches include nails, haircuts, any waxing, and possibly a massage to help you get through that first week.  Another set of absolutely unavoidable circumstances is this – you WILL bruise and you WILL be sore.  Our staff is very generous with their foot massages or backrubs, and most patrons will happily oblige as well, but there is nothing quite the same as a professional, peaceful massage.  Hot stones, being trodden on or happy endings optional of course.  Full nail sets are generally $25 (unless you head towards the border which is never recommended), and you can never have a set of nails that makes your toes look bad.  Get a matching set of toes, please.  Pedicure/manicure combos are common and can be done for anywhere from $25 to $65, depending on what sort of treatment you’d like done to your hands/feet.  Fills for nails are usually every two weeks, and are cheaper (around $20 last I checked).  Once your hands and feet are done your hair should be kept neat – long hair especially needs trimmed to keep the split ends at bay.  This is $15-25 every six weeks for a trim alone (my hair is waist-length).  I opt to shave and pluck as opposed to wax, but I believe waxing prices are $20 for whatever you need done. \r\n</P><P>\r\nThe last thing you will need is something to keep this all in.  Clothing, makeup, shoes, and money all have to go somewhere, and that giant purse will not cut it.  Most girls have their normal purse (of course), a makeup bag or box, a suitcase or carry-bag, and a little bag or box to carry out on the floor.  There is nothing more frustrating than having to run to the dressing room every time you get stage tips – especially if the place is busy.  Anyway, since most of us have something to carry clothes and shoes in, you can get the rest of what you need for about $50 altogether.  If you choose to leave things in a specific locker, don’t forget to grab a lock! \r\n</P><P>\r\nNow, totaling all this up is a high-end minimum, if that makes sense.  You can get by without the nails, the massage, the waxing, no stockings, etc.  However it is all for your benefit, so figure out what works for now, and what REALLY works.  This also does not cover transportation to and from work or food.  The bad news is that cab rides add up pretty quickly, but the good news is that you tend to forget to eat if you’re busy.  A quick recap is as follows, with totals.\r\n</P><PRE>\r\nLicensing:  $161\r\nShoes: $90\r\nClothes:  $165\r\nMakeup (MAC):  $200\r\nStorage:  $50\r\nMiscellaneous (Averaging):  $80\r\n---\r\n</PRE><P>\r\nGrand Starting High-End Minimum Total: $746\r\nKnowing you can pull this off for $300: Priceless\r\n</P><P>\r\nAnyway, the good thing about all this is that it is completely worth it – and this is the section where your purchases are justified.  Not only does it make you feel like you can accomplish a lot during your shifts, it allows you to cover the payments you need to cover – and I’m not just talking about your car payments or wanting a massage every week.  Before you can think of the rewarding parts, you have to pay your dues.  Different clubs have different fees, but it’s all along the same route so I’ll just use my club as the example.  Fees?  Did you just say FEES?  Yes, indeed I did.  A very common misconception is that we are paid hourly, which is slightly true but not in the normal way.  We do not receive a paycheck, and when the DJ says we \"work on tips and tips alone!\" it’s not just for show.  Here is the general breakdown for my club, I may be a little off in some of my guesses regarding dancer/club breakdowns, but I’ve only been back for two shifts so you’ll have to cope.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI will first say that at this point I hope you see the honesty and candid style in my writing.  Please keep that in mind for this next section – I also have fun explaining this part to patrons.  When you enter the club, there is an $8 cover charge (unless you have a free pass).  Once inside you then pay $10 for a minimum of 2 non-alcoholic drinks.  This tends to keep the riff-raff at bay, so to speak.  If a customer is planning to stay, they will usually be there for half an hour.  Our club gives out a pretty relaxed vibe and people feel comfortable, like they are hanging out with some friends instead of feeling as if it has to be all business.  This has both pros and cons of course.  If someone is going to buy some time with us, they watch two to three girls go up onstage (depending when they walked in), and then we know whether or not they plan on spending money.  At this point a dancer should have come to sit with them, and a good bartender will come over and ask if they want to buy us a ladies’ drink for $10.  If they do, we get $5. ($2 to the club, $3 to the bartender.)\r\n</P><P>\r\nPatrons can also buy some private dances from us, which is how we make our real money.  A note, there is no nudity, no touching, and definitely no sex allowed.  Some patrons tend to be pretty cranky at this sort of thing, but if they want that then Tijuana isn’t too far away. Besides, – what girl doesn’t want guys to give her money just to show off?  They can buy a private dance in a booth for $20/song ($5 to club, $15 to dancer), half an hour in a booth for $60 ($15 to club, $45 to dancer), or bling bling it in the Platinum Lounge/VIP Room/High Rollers’ Booth for $120 ($20 to club, $100 to dancer).  The latter I believe is 45 minutes but I am not certain.  If the DJ doesn’t run a special (usually 2, sometimes 3 for 1) we can also make our own where they get 2 songs for $20, or a chair dance for $10, etc.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe (the dancers) also sometimes pay a House Fee ($25), a Late Fee ($15), or a Dressing Room Violation ($5), and tips.  I’m not sure what the last one is but it’s new and hopefully something I never deal with.  Late fees (for your shift) are self-explanatory but not exactly encouraging when you start at 3 but know patrons don’t really come in until 5 or 6.  That part however goes along with the \"Get there early – why not?\" train of thought.  House Fees used to be $75 per day that you worked; then dropped to $35 per shift and be paid as soon as you had the money available, no exceptions.  If you did not make $35 during your shift (and there were a few days when we didn’t!), you owed it the next time you came in.  Now the House Fee is $25 and only paid if you work night shifts and do more than 4 dances.  Regarding tips, if you are taking home more than $100, you are expected to tip your DJ, your doorman, and your bartender, in that order.  Tips should be $3 to $5 per person.  I used to tip $5 to all three of those people plus the extra bouncer - it makes life a lot easier.\r\n</P><P>\r\nNow, the short version without the math:\r\n</P><PRE>\r\nPatron Pays...\r\nCover Charge $8\r\nTwo Drink Minimum $10\r\n\r\nOptions & Profit...\r\nBooth dances $20/song -  $15 to dancer\r\nHalf hours $60 -  $45 to dancer\r\nPlatinum lounge $120 -  $100 to dancer\r\nDrinks $10 -  $5 to dancer\r\n\r\nDancer Pays...\r\nHouse Fee $25 (more than 4 dances)\r\nLate Fee $15\r\nDressing Room Violation $5\r\nTips (Avg.) $5 paid to 3 or 4 people \r\n</PRE><P>\r\nAdding in some math, things hopefully begin to all become clear.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOur stage sets are 2 songs long, running for about 6 minutes total.  Now let’s say there are 2 patrons in the club consistently.  A shift is 8 hours solid (if you’re really into the groove shifts don’t end until it closes) and there are normally 5 girls per shift.  That’s all a very realistic average that we should all be able to work with, right?  Great.  So let’s say things are running smoothly, and in an hour all the girls are in proper stage rotation and getting dances while their turn is far enough away that they are not skipped.\r\n</P><PRE>\r\nRotation in 1 Hour\r\nDancers      Set A                 Set B\r\nCream        6 minutes             6 minutes\r\nChampagne    6 minutes             6 minutes\r\nLayla        6 minutes             6 minutes\r\nKira         6 minutes             6 minutes\r\nSassy        6 minutes             6 minutes\r\n</PRE><P>\r\nGoing by that mini-chart, and pretending that we’re studying for the SAT’s, we will assume both patrons tip $1 per set, each girl makes $4 per hour for 8 hours, and it will total $32 per shift.  Now let’s say everyone has a good shift (not great, just good, because I am not fabulous at math), and gets 5 private dances.  Assuming no specials are done, that’s $100 in dances done per girl – meaning $75 for the shift.  That brings us up to $107 for the shift, which isn’t bad by normal job standards, especially since it’s all paid under the table.  Since we’ve all done over 4 dances and it’s night shift, we have to pay house fee (-$25).  No one was late so the girls take home $82 each.  I opted to work day shift since my phone bill was due, so I got to take home the full amount.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThat example, being repetitive, was realistic and good – not great.  That also means that, because I can only do basic math, we’re assuming 5 days per week every week for a month went like that.  We are also assuming no ladies’ drinks, specials, or half hours were bought, only 5 girls worked at one time for only 8 hours each, and there was never more or less than $4 per hour tipped to each girl.  I hope that now you have a better understanding of exactly how this works, and also appreciate it since I stayed up until 6 a.m. writing this in the most proper format I could manage.\r\n</P><P>\r\nRecapping and making a point, if you’re taking home a minimum $2000 per month, how much of that original $746 will you need?  Can you do your own manicures?  Can your boyfriend/girlfriend/robot give you a massage at home?  Do you really need to stop at $tarbucks every afternoon?  What’s more important, getting your oil changed or getting ANOTHER belt masquerading as a skirt?  On the other hand – if that skirt-belt is requested by your favorite customer and encourages him to buy ANOTHER half hour with you, it all works out, doesn’t it?\r\n</P><P>\r\nWe seem to have gone off the beaten path somewhat, so my travels from behind the bar to on top of it will be narrated next.  Anyway, there you have it - A Dancer’s Guide To Getting Started, AKA \"Making Money versus Managing Money version 2.0: You Will Thank Me Later\".  Goodnight and good luck!\r\n</P>'),(3857,'<p>somewhere<br>\r\nnear where the mothership took off<br>                                   \r\nlies the way<br>\r\nthrough house.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh, that house with all that<br>                                                \r\nmeasured simplicity and<br>\r\ncomplex context<br>                             \r\nit\'s a soul thing,<br>\r\na spiritual thing                                            <br>\r\nweaving off the cheese in the breeze.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>some sunny afternoon we could go there<br>                                       \r\nthough the mothership is gone,\r\na ploosh arose, resonating<br>\r\nfrom the vibrant soul<br>\r\nfor if we want it there is more,<br>\r\nwe can find it<br>\r\nin the green and the grass leads on.</p>                                                \r\n                                                                                \r\n<p>somewhere in that house<br>\r\nthere is once again<br>\r\nthe feeling<br>\r\nof the heart open free.</p>                          \r\n\r\n<p>this jazz of the beat<br>\r\nof the scene where we meet<br>                               \r\none again,<br>\r\nmy heart<br>\r\nopen free.</p>\r\n'),(3858,'<P>the puerto rican family<BR>in the apartment building across the street<BR>must have spent seven hundred dollars on fireworks.<BR>i sat on the step and watched.<BR><BR>there was a little girl, two, maybe,<BR>another girl, five or six,<BR>a boy about nine.<BR><BR>they came over in march when the japanese plums were ripe<BR>(i found out they\'re also called loquats.)<BR>and knocked on the door.<BR>karyssa answered, and she came and got me,<BR>and i told them that of course they could.<BR><BR>i opened the blinds, and we sat on the couch and watched them.<BR>the boy held the small girl up,<BR>and she got all she could reach, which was not many.<BR>they were putting them in a pot.<BR><BR>karyssa said, <I>you should go out and help them.</I><BR>and i said, <I>i\'ll go pick some,  but i\'m not going to hold her up there.<BR>that\'s a good way to get into trouble.</I><BR><BR>i asked them, <I>what does one calls these fruits in spanish?<BR></I>ciruelos japonéses?<BR><BR>they didn\'t know.<BR>or weren\'t sayin\'.<BR>big secret.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>the roman candles and sparklers and whirligigs<BR>reminded me of when my uncles and their male cousins and friends<BR>had fireworks battles every christmas eve<BR>in the pasture behind the house where my grandfather grew up.<BR><BR>the cattle and corn<BR>and soybeans and potatoes<BR>and strawberries and peanuts<BR>were long gone.<BR><BR>my great aunt<BR>(maybe you say \"grandaunt\".  we don\'t.)<BR>that lived on the property<BR>eventually sold the pasture piece by piece<BR>to the retirement community that sprang up<BR>on the northern edge of it.<BR><BR>that was later,<BR>long after they all got tired of the fights, i guess.<BR><BR>the first year, i was six.<BR>mom wouldn\'t let dad go out there.<BR>i don\'t think he would\'ve gone, anyway.<BR>he doesn\'t let himself have too much fun.<BR><BR>all the kids, my siblings and cousins,<BR>wanted to go to the fence to watch,<BR>but there was no way<BR>they were going to let us.<BR>we were outta gas.<BR><BR>then my grandfather came up with the answer.<BR>they took us out there in cars<BR>and parked far enough into the pasture<BR>for us to see the action.<BR>it was lots better than the drive-in.<BR><BR>my grandfather took my brother and sister and i out there in his truck.<BR>he was giving some winstons fits,<BR>(nobody knew about second-hand smoke then.)<BR>probably worried that they\'d scratch his paint.<BR>he\'d do anything for us.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>they set them off in the parking lot across the street<BR>pretty steadily for two or three hours<BR>then quit around eleven.<BR><BR>i got rocked out of bed at quarter after twelve.<BR>the father and some friends were drinking and setting off more.<BR>the friends must have brought them.<BR>i went out and told them i was trying to sleep,<BR>that people had to work in the morning.<BR>so much for neighborhood amity.</P>'),(3859,'<p>it\'s one thirty in the morning and i should be sleeping. after all i have work in the morning. not film & tv neverland type work, but real work. the kind people who have real lives do. i\'m temping as a receptionist for very modest pay and i need to be up and alert and well-dressed and all that crap.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i won\'t go into the long and involved thought process that got me here, but there\'s a story i have never told another living soul in my entire life and i feel i need to tell it tonight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>here. now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in early to mid 1989 i was enrolled at auckland university. i say enrolled because \"attending\" or \"a student at\" are terms that don\'t really apply. my parents paid my tuition, rent and expenses, as well as a generous allowance, and i repaid them by fucking around and flunking out.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but i digress.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i had developed an unhealthy addiction to watching wwf superstars of wrestling every friday on TV2. this comic book world of heroes seemed so appealing to me that i became obsessed with it. so much so that when i saw an ad pinned to a notice board in the uni gymnasium (no idea what i was doing in there - getting chips and coke from a vending machine most likely) looking for people to register for a new greco-roman wrestling team, i signed up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>what a pale, spotty, stick-insect-like 18-year-old who until that point had been alergic to exercise was going to achieve in this endeavour, many might have wondered. but it seemed pretty cool and entirely reasonable to me at the time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>after getting a phone call from mike, the guy who was arranging the whole thing, we arranged to meet up. he wasn\'t exactly what i had expected. here i was picturing the kind of tough no-nonsense gym coach you see on american movies-of the-week.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mike was about five foot two, wore very colourful clothing, and as i recall had a gold watch with a holographic print of a large eye staring out from behind the clockface.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now i\'m sure there are those of you reading this now who can already see where this story is headed. and maybe at that moment way back then i could too. but like so many other things back then, i just followed along.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>i\'m thinking about how fucking stupid and obvious this all is before i type the next line.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>he explained that the club was still being set up and that there was no formal club night yet planned. so he suggested we go back to his place and go over the basics of greco-roman wrestling so that i\'d have had some practice by the first club meeting.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on the drive there i remember thinking that this felt wrong. i asked him a couple of times where we were going and he just smiled and said \"my place\". it was a part of the city i didn\'t know and that made me more uneasy than anything else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>well his place was a neatly ordered little townhouse unit in a block. i remember he had garden gnomes out the back. in the small lounge the coffee table had been pushed off the rug and hard up against the sofa.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>well he showed me a couple of books on college wrestling in the states and we went over some of the basic positions. we wrestled around on the carpet for a while and... <i>fuck i can\'t even tell you what i was thinking. not because i don\'t want to, i literally can\'t get a handle on it.</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p>anyway after about a half an hour time was up and i had to get back. i can\'t remember what for, or if i hadn\'t just made up something because this felt wrong, but for whatever reason it was time to leave.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mike offered the use of the shower. i can still picture the cramped stairway that led upstairs, and i remember looking at it for a moment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i declined. i have to wonder, sitting here tonight writing this, what might have happened if i had accepted the offer. anyway he said he was going to have one and disappeared upstairs for a while.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>he drove me back to the campus and said we\'d catch up about the club meeting. funnily enough this never eventuated.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i can\'t really describe how i feel about that day, looking back on it now, but it\'s the similar kind of numb feeling that i got as he drove me out to his place.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there are other chapters to this story, like the tales of my eventual forray into wrestling which yielded grand friendships and some mixed sporting success, although it can\'t have been pretty to watch. just picture what the love child of gollum and slash from guns\'n\'roses would look like if they sent him off to gym class in spandex...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but the other chapter in this story that i will tell you about came three years later. on a different tertiary course at another institution (parents\' money, fucked around, got kicked out), the staff were being introduced to us on orientation day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now a very familiar figure was sitting on the benches amongst the other lecturers, avoiding eye contact with me. strangely enough when the course convener introduced him, his name wasn\'t mike.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the course of the term year we had occassion to speak a couple of times, and were both completely civil, acting as if we\'d never met before that year.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i wonder very much, right now, what i would say or do if i saw him tomorrow.</p>'),(3860,'<P>I don\'t know where to start, I gues I\'ll start when I broke up whith my boyfrind Mario. I was going to my next class after the third period bell had rung and I saw Him. The previous night I had writen a really potheticly nice break up note and gave it to him on his way to lunch. That day after school I saw Mario, he looked pissed, but hey I don\'t blame the basterd didn\'t even say hi or any thing. About two months later I found out that he was going out with another girl named Olivia and he was so much happier with her then he ever was with me. I gues I was just jelouse that he didn\'t need me to make him happy so I thought I would kill him. Ok it\'s gunna be easy you\'ll see. HAH! Boy was I wrong. I was going to put rat poison in his food or water or something, because Mario would always, no doubt, ask me for water after school every single day but the problem was that he was starting to walk home with that whore Olivia, so the plan didn\'t work. I thought of other things like stabing him but the schools have a strict no weapons policey so that didn\'t work either. Later I had found that Mario had broken up with Oivia so he could be with me, not seeing how stupid that sounded, I beileved him and didn\'t even think about killing him. For the short three weeks we were \"hanging out\" not once did he ask me out, then I found out that Olivia and The ass were going out AGAIN and I felt that I had no other choice than to kill Mario to make me happy. The first time I ever even cosiderd giving him what he deserved I wasn\'t even to sure if I would go through with it, but the second time I knew what had to be done. I was going to kill Mario so he could never be happy and the people arownd him could never be happy. I thought, yes that would elimenate all my problems, but no. I\'m only in my teens, I still have my whole life ahead of me, I don\'t want to go to prison for something that an asshole deserved. So I was so going to kill Mario after those three long months and didn\'t even go through with the execution. Think what you want of me, but damn, if I was really that carless I would have done it hands down.</P>'),(3861,'<table width=\"400\" bgcolor=\"white\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td>\r\n\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img1%%\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" border=\"0\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Washington State, Whatcom County:</b> Residents of Glacier report strange activity in the area of <a href=\"http://www.nps.gov/noca/challenger/ch7b.jpg\">Austin Pass</a> (el. 4630) after sunset.\r\nAvid outdoorsman Buck Moon tells his story:\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\"I was working my way through the Baker Wilderness when this thing in a tree pooped on me.\r\nI figured it was a bear, and ran for my life.\r\nI haven\'t moved so fast since Dale knocked over my Budweiser.\r\nAnd all the while I was runnin\' this creepy voice from the sky was sayin\' stuff.\r\nIt called out to me: \'STOP EATING THE CHICKENS!\'\r\nGod damn, I thought, hippies.  It has to be hippies.\r\nEver since I left the decent people of Texas, I...\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nAnd then before I could finish my thought - WOOSH - this ungodly horror comes flyin\' down out of a big ol\' spruce!\r\nWell, I almost pooped myself.\r\nIt had a head like a man, with hair like spun gold.\r\nBut its body was all black and shiny - like some kind of PVC.\r\nAnd it had wings like a bat.  I swear it did.\r\nWell sir, I kept running.  I ran all the way back to our camp at Baker Lake.  \r\nI would have run all the way to Concrete, if I had the wind.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"%%img2%%\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" border=\"1\">\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI told the boys my story, and they did nothing but tease me - called it a \'chupacabra\' and told me to sell it late night A.M. radio.\r\nBut I had the last laugh: \r\nTwo days later we were drinking by the campfire when this thing swooped down out of the darkness.\r\nIt pooped on Dale.  It was an awful sight, and Dale will never be the same.  \r\nHe\'ll never eat chicken again, either.\r\n\'BOKBOKBOK!\' we heard it screaming.\r\nAnd then it flew off with our whiskey.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nWe filed a report with the Federal Rangers.\r\nThose bastards knew all about it even before we told them.\r\nThey said the creature was a protected species, nesting in the area, and looking for a mate.\r\n\'Don\'t bring whiskey into the woods,\' they warned us.  \r\nCarrying single malt scotch was a death wish.\r\nWe asked about its obsession with the poultry industry.  \'It\'s trying to get close to the female of its kind,\' they said, \'But mostly it just watches porn in its goth suit.\'\r\nSuit?  God damn.  God damn, indeed.\"\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3862,'<p>Yesterday: Get this coupon card in the mail from Victoria\'s Secret, guaranteeing me to a free pair of underwear, no purchase required.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Alright, I\'m totally cool with this, it\'s not even the first time I\'ve scored free underwear from a store without having to buy anything. But still, I do browse around the store for other things since I understand the hidden message behind the free underwear offer. Not to mention that I\'m a shopoholic, it takes me half an hour to buy a jar of spaghetti sauce.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Go into VS, I tell the sales girl folding pnaties by the door that I have this coupon, she tells me that yeah, only underwear on this side of the table, in white, pink, or black without any adornments.  It was not unlike the scene in The Jerk where Navin is working at a carnival booth telling people what they can win from the game. She had to explain it to me several times since I just wasn\'t really getting it...every single pair of underwear on the table, even if it looks plain W/P/B has some sort of embroidery, contrasting band, print, bow, or doohickeys. In other words, on a table covered by literally hundreds of pairs of colorful underwear, somewhere in the secret gall bladder compartment of the table there are maybe 8 pairs eligible for coupon redemption.  I take a small pink triangle piece of fabric with some strings on it and keep looking around, even tempted to buy some not-so-cottony underwear, then realizing I have enough pairs of underwear to outfit an army of women from Duran Duran videos plus some grandmothers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Walk around the corner, angels sing.  I see Loehmann\'s.  I love Loehmann\'s. I go in because I need a new pair of Pumas and maybe pair of pants. Deal of the day = 40% off clearance prices. I walk out with lots of tops and an evening gown for which I haven\'t planned an occasion to wear, but still, I\'m feeling pretty good about myself for getting 7 pieces of clothing for $100; the receipt tells me I saved $498.75 from department store prices. This odd pentagonal silk hankerchief top sporting a TV test pattern was $120, regularly sold at Loehmann\'s for $40, went on clearance for $19.99, I got it for $12.  Woo hoo! Score!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I love the two months of the year when I get an extra paycheck.</p>\r\n'),(3863,'<p>Imelda held the door open when he left. He walked down the stairs, got into his car and drove away. He did not wave farewell or even look at her. He did not care that there were bruises on her cheek. He did not feel the overwhelming feeling of failure that she did. She was a hooker and he was her regular two o\'clock Thursday. He went back to his family, to his wife, to his three kids each with their own separate bedrooms. </p>\r\n<p>She returned to the small motel room where she had been holding up for the past week. Imelda closed the door and leaned against it. She gripped her self, her arms held the only safety she knew. She tried to hold back the tears but it was no use. They flowed and they flowed hard. Why was she like this? Why had she chosen this life? </p>\r\n<p>Imelda threw herself on the bed. She could feel the wet spots, she didn\'t move, she didn\'t care. The rest of the afternoon was a haze of sex, crying and sleeping. The feeling of hunger had abandoned her long ago, along with love, hope and aspiration. </p>\r\n<p>The men were men. They were tall, they were short, they were fat, they were bones, and they all had money. Imelda was there for them. She was there for their money. They had it all, because they had her. She did for them what their wives where too scarred to do. Their partners were too pristine, too religious, or too frigid to do the things Imelda did. </p>\r\n<p>She was small and thin, with long black hair and a beautiful body. One of her doctor clients gave her a boob job. She looked good and she knew it, but there was something she neglected inside. She was only nineteen but she felt that she had screwed herself halfway around the globe. It was love that she was missing. </p>\r\n<p>“Dddrrrring.” It was the phone. </p>\r\n<p>“Hello. Who\'s this?” She said. </p>\r\n<p>“Hey… hello… it\'s me Nick. I meet you the other day at the book store.” </p>\r\n<p>“Oh yeah!” She remembered him now: a sweet guy about her age. She found him reading an anthology on pagan religions at the bookstore. “It\'s nice to hear from you.” </p>\r\n<p>“This is going to sound crazy, but I know what you are doing to yourself. You don\'t need it Imelda. You need to stop or else something is going to happen to you.” Nick said. </p>\r\n<p>“What are you talking about?” Imelda gave Nick no impression of her profession. She was in Barnes and Noble waiting for a John to pick her up in the parking lot. She met Nick and he had fascinated her, but she hid herself behind an imaginary school and a fictitious employment at the local hamburger stand. </p>\r\n<p>Nick was twenty-two and a student at South Texas Republic University. He was an archeology student with aspirations of becoming a professor and if that didn\'t work out, a writer. He talked about his research as a reaction to her flirtation. Flirtation was something that her past six years with men had given her. She worked the ability to get any man\'s attention. She remembered his eyes and the way they sparkled when we spoke of ancient rituals of warding ghost and binding spirits. </p>\r\n<p>Wiping off more tears from her eyes, she pretended not to feel the pain between her legs. Her last customer was a well-endowed trucker who liked calling her “Whore” and “My Little Slut.” She realized that she had not given Nick her number. </p>\r\n<p>“How did you get this number? I didn\'t give it to you.” She asked. </p>\r\n<p>“Look, I\'m kind of different. I know things. I dream of things that can\'t be explained and I try not to let them destroy me. I woke up and I just knew the number.” Nick replied. </p>\r\n<p>“What do you mean, what are you talking about?” She asked. </p>\r\n<p>“I know what you do Imelda and you have to stop. You are like your mother and your grandmother before that,” said Nick. </p>\r\n<p>Imelda\'s mind began to race struggling to find a bit of reason why Nick, a person she had just met, knew so much about her. He told the truth. She came from a long line of women who used what nature had given them, their bodies. She could remember sleeping in the motel closets while her mother did what she was doing. She could her the violence in their voices behind the walls and she could smell the sex afterwards in the room. </p>\r\n<p>She began to cry again, “Why are you doing this to me? Who are you?” </p>\r\n<p>“It\'s a ghost, Imelda. It\'s the ghost of your great-grandmother. She\'s trapped inside you. She went mad long ago. She went out by the river in a group of trees, she found something. It called her there when she was young. This thing, this monster controls her. She thought it would go away when she died, but it continued in your mother and now in you,” Nick told her. </p>\r\n<p>“How do you know these things?” She became frantic and the aching feeling burning in her loins continued as they spoke. She felt nauseated and she began to weaken. </p>\r\n<p>“You have to decide Imelda. You have to decide not to be this way. It knows that you know now. It\'s going to fight you, but you still have a chance. Fight it Imelda, fight it,” he said. </p>\r\n<p>Imelda dropped the phone. She could still hear Nick\'s voice telling her to fight it. Her thoughts were swirling, her vision blurred and she stumbled into the bathroom. She gripped the sink for balance and that was when she saw it in the mirror. </p>\r\n<p>Her face was contorting and shifting. She could see her great-grandmothers horrid incorporeal likeness flowing over her body. Her body shook from shock and terror. She tried to scream, she tried to match the likeness of the ghost face within. Her great-grandmothers eternal expression of woe and pain was all she could remember when reality escaped her and she collapsed on the bathroom floor.</p>'),(3864,'Sorry I have this story posted somewhere else exclusively...\r\n\r\nhttp://www.templeofdagon.com/writers/vito-del-valle/the-valley-of-the-urr/'),(3879,'so, there’s this woman, right. this woman is, to my mind, late twenties to mid forties – she may be of limited means, or she might be stinking rich.<br><br>\r\n\r\nbuying makeup in a department store is a matter of sensibility rather than strictly pocket. you may want the reassurance of the high end label or just the ability to keep the makeup on for any length of time before coming up in an allergic reaction to it’s contents and clawing it off. so. she is dressed up to a certain extent, but nothing that will be damaged by contact with public transport dirt, and nothing too flash – stealth wealth, that’s the ticket, subtle power daywear. surviving the journey into town intact, she picks through the crowds and enters the sparkling portico of the voodoo vendor, harvey nics. designer labels, escalators, atria, modern, flawless dummies, clean, well maintained assistants. she heads for one of the cosmetics counters. miles of bottles, acres of mirrors, women in full slap wearing slightly medical outfits. beautifully, flatteringly lit, with every surface a glowing reflection of the last. effervescently faceted, warmly seductive, and cool and glossy all at once. slipping into the hypnotic state of shopping for one\\\'s own deferred perfection. dreamy, dreamy, dreamy... an assistant swims into focus. perhaps coming in for the sell - and bang. she is shot by her stalking ex boyfriend and then he turns the gun around towards himself, and, with a little difficulty, crooks his finger as far as it can go and squeezes the trigger again, tight.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthis event passes for news in the papers, but of course i don\\\'t read them so i didn\\\'t see it. and, to be perfectly honest, in my opinion, it hardly constitutes news. it is a freak occasion, sure, but not really news as such. my psychiatrist tells me about it because we have a common interest in stalking, it is one of our conversational themes. i should say, this has nothing to do with my \\\'condition’. it is leisure conversation, not work. we also talk a lot about echo and the bunnymen, the human league, and the teardrop explodes, as well, because we are the same age, and of that era, and like to name drop a bit. we have quite a lot in common, except he is a psychiatrist and, unaccountably, a police surgeon.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthis particular conversation segues neatly into an aspect of my imaginative life which he then gets very excited about. he is getting a theory going about me. he is a professional psychiatrist, which means he is supposed to monitor my drugs, but i don’t take any. and he is an amateur psychoanalyst. aren’t we all, these days?<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nof course, it is normal to me, because everything is normal to the person whose habit it is. what it is, is that this particularly modern, tarantinoesque death among the cosmetics brings up something from my own mental landscape he likes the cut of. this is that, although i am rarely late for anything, in fact i am quite obsessive about timekeeping, i often get held up in my preparations by a clothes crisis. of course, like a lot of people, i guess, when dressing, i choose my apparel in consideration of various factors; physical and psychological comfort, aesthetics, appropriateness for purpose, the weather, the likely weather, how the costume might be \\\'read’ versus how i want to be seen at the apex of my day, and at any other time, in different contexts, for reasons of social camouflage and mutability, and I like a little sexiness or a bit of a twist, unless i am going completely under cover.<br><br>\r\n \r\n\r\nbut the thing he picks up on is that i also have \\\'could i die in this outfit?\\\'. it is also one of the reasons i wash my hair every day - i would hate to die with greasy hair. i have a fear of discombobulation on death, as in life. clothes, like makeup, are a form of protection. but clothes, no matter how perfect, cannot marshal reality when it is really sliding away from you.<br><br>\r\n \r\n\r\nif this woman had any time to do so before she died, i imagine she could see beyond the staging of the retail environment, because no matter how seamless it is, and i bet harvey nics is pretty damn seamless, my feeling is that her new eye-view; the ground, would afford her a glimpse of something different. something dirty. perhaps something unfinished, something misplaced. an element of lack of arrangement in a back stage area of the shop, not for customer consumption will have been visible. or, in a moment of her own unspiralling, a thread coming loose from a button on her blood spattered blouse.<br><br>\r\n \r\n\r\nworm-eye, in that moment that is measured short but experienced long, has several thoughts before expiring. that unarticulated thought connected to the inability to move, and a feeling of discomfort in suddenly irritating clothes. a deafening but hushing rushing sound closes down distractions. she spots a price tag on the instep of a bystander\\\'s high heel. why would a person go to the trouble of selecting shoes, trying them on, taking some steps, looking in a mirror, thinking about who they might be in these shoes, or how they might complete or intstigate a certain impression, or fulfil a particular function not covered by the current shoe collection, and. not. peel. off. the. label? okay, trying on is done, the decision is made to buy them, the purchase is completed, they are carried home, packed and cosseted and then nothing? the process of fetishisation is abandoned before handling them at home? surely they are not even perfect until you peel the label off? who is this person? the price tag is a door, the only portal left in the hall of mirrors. through it she leaves the building.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthen there’s the killer, finally united in death, forever, we think, with his imaginary girlfriend, the one who had a mind and a will of her own that he had to stop. he is hoiked from a last glimpse of the world of chewing gum, fluffballs, spiders and scuffed hems, up, up, and looking down, if the near-death tale tellers are to be believed, a new, vertiginous, view. looking straight down - it’s an unusual angle - life in plan. the two bodies like islands on a map, the sea of onlookers frozen mid ripple.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nso. where do you want to go now? we can go anywhere, you and i. do you want to be back in the room with me and the psych? he is interesting, with his clip on tie, down the cop shop, fitting pieces together with cracker and rebus, or here, in his office, his head full of guitars and working on a theory about me with my quiet, reined in life, and reckless, apocalyptic imagination.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe anatomises my thinking in a timeline made of post-it notes perched along the edge of the table like little yellow birds. a bank of scruffy and half heartedly arranged books behind him. a seroxat mug with mould growing inside. pictures of his wife and child badly printed out from his pc propped with poor posture. barely tended plants lining the window ledges, black leather sofas never sat on but used as extra table space. traffic moves along outside with a sound like ripping cat hairs off a coat with sellotape.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmove along, now, nothing to see here.\r\n'),(3880,''),(3881,'The window by the bed served as a canvass that framed a piece of the San Francisco skyline. A jagged sense of order appeared as the proud structures stood tall along the smaller buildings\' rooftops. A slice of the Golden Gate Bridge covered with fog to the left juxtaposed the pyramid. As a whole, the panorama was a potpourri of city elements that blended into a shape and character of its own, one that is very San Francisco. A loud booming noise emerged from a distant. Fireworks of red, green, white, yellow and rainbow colors painted the nocturnal sky (courtesy of PacBell Park).</br>\r\n\r\n<br>I felt a slight touch outlining the nape of my neck, I turned around and stared at him, while trying to silence the incessantly pounding rhythmn of my heart. A form of energy as in two opposing magnetic poles possessed these two beings. My body was paralyzed. Our lips were drawn towards the other until it formed into one. The fireworks continued, the view stood still, the bodies moved naturally obeying the laws of an invisible science. </br>'),(3882,'<p>“I have to have them!” I exclaimed. “At any cost.”</p>\r\n<p>Amelia looked at me; her mouth ajar and eyes wide in horror. I truth I’d already seen the 10p label scribbled on the back, so I wasn’t really going to have them at any cost, but I pretended I hadn’t, examining the big, ugly, orange plastic earrings with glee. I did have to have them, but ten pence was definitely my maximum budget.</p>\r\n<p>“They’re disgusting,” Amelia groaned.</p>\r\n<p>“I know,” I smiled mischiefously.</p>\r\n<p>I paid my ten pence over to the bric-a-brac stall holder and put the cheap earrings in my bag. Later that night I carefully wrapped them, making sure there was lots of glittery ribbon and a clearly marked gift tag.</p><br>\r\n<p>Two weeks later it was Christmas day and the obligatory visit to the boyfriends’ mother, which I’d been dreading. William, the boyfriend of two years, ignored me to speak to his mother in quick, fluent French. They always did that. I did actually know a little French, I’d sort of picked it up over the last couple of years, but they didn’t know that. It’s amazing what you can learn about someone when they think you can’t understand their words.</p>\r\n<p>The mother stopped talking suddenly and smiled at me. It wasn\'t a nice smile.</p>\r\n<p>“Oh my dear, but we are ignoring you,” she twinkled satisfactorily. As if to placate me, she ceremoniously gave me my gift from her. William had already opened the ipod she’d brought him, and of course was delighted, so I made sure I took plenty of time pulling off the ribbon and sticky tape. My gift was even better than I had expected; a pair of large pink granny knickers, at least three times too big. I stood up, holding the very big knickers against my size eight hips.</p>\r\n<p>“Oh thank you,” I smiled through grit teeth. “They’re so lovely I think I’ll put them on right now.”</p>\r\n<p>The mother blinked, as if butter wouldn’t melt in her mouth.</p>\r\n<p>I know your game, I thought, still managing to smile at her. My smile became far more genuine when I handed her my own carefully wrapped little gift.</p>\r\n<p>\"I thought they\'d suit you,\" I said, sincerely of course.</p>\r\n<p>Ah yes, two can definitely play at that game.</p>\r\n<br><p> Needless to say, that Christmas was the last I saw of either of them</p>\r\n'),(3883,'<PRE>\r\nIim a changeling sent to take your heart\r\nThis one that never tires\r\nYou think I am the perfect one\r\nI evolved from your desires\r\n\r\nA spirit with no name no home\r\nJust a soul of light and lust\r\nEnticing you to join with me\r\nEven though you\\\'ll return to dust\r\n\r\nYou\\\'ll keep me grounded in this world\r\nI\\\'ll give you all you need\r\nUntil the time to seek is near\r\nWhen I\\\'ll leave your love to bleed\r\n\r\nCould a taste of honey be so sweet\r\nWith the bitterness it leaves\r\nThis conscience can feel your loss\r\nAnd I see that your heart grieves\r\n\r\nBut I’m a changeling here to seek her path\r\nAnd the calling has begun\r\nTime to move and change and fly\r\nYou knew you weren’t the one\r\n</PRE>'),(3865,'Three weeks ago, a girl I used to know, back when I was living in a swirling cloud of doubt about the future, and also in an apartment with my little brother, sent me an email. She was living in her college town, bartending, trying to be a paralegal, and not doing so well. She had a boyfriend. Her email was very long and detailed, and at the end of it, she had attached nine pictures of herself, her garden, her cat, and her boyfriend.</p><p>\r\nShe was my only real friend in that town back then, five years ago, and every day she would come over to my apartment or I would go to her grandmother\'s house, where she was staying, and we would get high and hang out. We each had two or three other friends, but the core group was her and me. She liked me because I didn\'t act like a regular guy, either wanting to fuck her or wanting her to go away. In reality, of course, I wanted to fuck her. Being of a certain personality type, I never pressed the issue. It never seemed like the right time. I thought that if it was going to happen, it would happen naturally. I liked her. She was a sweet girl.</p><p>\r\nOn two occasions, I thought that it would happen. The air just seemed to have a certain quality to it, and outside events conspired. Was I imagining things? Maybe. But both times, she...well, I think that she knew it too. They say women are more intuitive than men. I\'ll just say she killed it.</p><p>\r\nWhatever the reason was, I never understood it.</p><p>\r\nHer recent email, calm on the surface, spoke to me of desperation. I always could read between her lines. It also spoke of self-centeredness, meaning, it was all about her life and problems, and conveyed no sense of an interest in mine.</p><p>\r\nOf course, last week she sent another email. She had broken up with her boyfriend. She had gotten a restraining order. No, she wasn\'t kidding. She wanted to move to her uncle\'s house in Alaska (from North Carolina). No, she wasn\'t kidding about that, either. She wanted to see me. She would probably be in town this week. I haven\'t heard from her.</p><p>\r\nAnd here it is, Friday.\r\n   '),(3866,'<P>\r\nI was doing a room service run. I was on the other side of the building from the restaurant and front desk. In other words, I was completely alone with no way to contact anybody. I turned a corner, and I saw this guy just screaming at some lady. Literally screaming. First off, I didn\'t appreciate seeing a person screaming at another person like this, especially in public. Secondly, I knew that this was just going to lead to a ton of complaints from other guests. So, I walked over to them and asked the guy what was wrong. He said, \"Nothing\'s wrong, everything\'s okay.\" So...not being the kind of guy to start a fight (heh), I said, \"Alright, well, you\'re going to have to keep it quiet. I\'m going to have to tell the front desk about this.\" He said something along the lines of, \"Alright,\" and then he just stopped talking altogether.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSo at this point, I didn\'t know what the hell to do. But hey, I had an order to run. So, I went around the next corner, took the elevator to the next floor up, and did my job. This took approximately 2 minutes. I went back down the elevator, turned the corner, and right in front of me is a rather disturbing sight that will stay with me for a long time. This crazy guy is beating the shit out of this woman (presumably his girlfriend/wife), and nobody is doing a goddamn thing. He\'s seriously beating her like a rag doll. She\'s on the ground. He\'s standing and swinging at her. Half of his punches were missing (presumably because he\'s insane), but still...she wasn\'t in very good shape. So, I made my move.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI ran at top-speed to the conflict. The guy looks behind himself and sees me, but by the time he sees me, I\'m already in the process of lifting him up and shoving him back against the wall. The second he hits the wall, he\'s trying to break free and attack the woman some more. So, I decide to punch him in the stomach to settle him down. This apparently only upset him, because he took a swing for my face. Sadly for him, I was able to block it with my left forearm, while simultaneously punching him in the face with a right.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nHe went down for the count.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nAnyway, after delivering the rightly deserve beating to Mr. Crazy, I lifted him up to his feet and told him to come with me. I dragged him to the front office, where they called the police. A very funny 5 minutes passed while we were waiting for the police. Basically, I held onto the guy\'s shoulder, kind of like a football coach would do to his star player. Like a \"good job\" type thing. Very funny, indeed.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nThe cops showed up rather quickly, and took Mr. Crazy away. I had to answer a bunch of questions, and I got to leave work an hour early. I\'m not in trouble or anything. In fact, everybody was very proud of me. It felt good. I felt like Batman or something. </P>'),(3867,'<p> An aftermath of passion leaves me with an emptiness and silence that fills the human spirit with nothingness.  A numb feeling experienced like a society traumatized by the shell shock effect caused by a massacre of thieves or an army of barbarians that raped the bodies and souls of maidens from an unassuming village of the enemy.  Love is not a battlefield.  However the loss of a loved one devastates the human spirit and rapes the soul into pieces. The net result makes one feel like you are devoured of life, of the running flow of blood through the veins. As if the pumping heart ceased to function for a very long instant while each waking moment becomes a struggle to search for each grasp of oxygen intake, to breathe, to force its every membrane into living.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> For each great love lost, the necessary force and energy needed to catapult one\'s spirit towards recovery becomes more tedious.  To seek \r\ncomfort from music, from work, from books, from family, from one\'s own creative endeavors; while at the sunset of each sojourn the twilight paints the blue skies with dark hues of gray then black until the evening brings the cold and grim reality back into one\'s consciousness and reminds you that, you are alone, and he has gone.</p>'),(3868,'we\'re nearly there.<br>\r\nthe ripening of the moon<br>\r\nthe tides are risen<br>\r\nand <br>\r\nyour ovaries are swollen<br><br>\r\n\r\ni wait for you like <br>\r\ni wait for a lover<br>\r\nsometimes i think you\'ll never come back<br>\r\ncan\'t keep my feet still<br>\r\nwant it to happen<br><br>\r\n\r\nand there you are<br>\r\nwhen i wake up<br>\r\ni\'ve dreamed of you<br>\r\nof blood, of life, of cycles, of death<br>\r\nof cleansing<br><br>\r\n\r\nmy whole body<br>\r\nit deflates<br>\r\nwith all of that relief<br>\r\nand i think about<br>\r\nhow much i have come to love you, me<br><br>\r\n\r\ni bathe myself love myself nurture myself<br>\r\ni wrap myself up in my red sarong and<br>\r\nremember about the truth of the earth,<br>\r\nwhere we are from,<br>\r\nconnection, love, blood, flow<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow is the time, the<br>\r\ndark moon when<br>\r\nour mothers sat in the longhouse,<br>\r\nsurrounded by aunties and grandmothers and the spirits of the red tent<br>\r\nand told secrets<br>\r\nand taught the ways of being us<br><br>\r\n\r\nyou are my seasons<br>\r\nyou are my ocean<br>\r\nyou are my moon<br>\r\nyou are mine<br>\r\nsomething that they can never know<br>\r\nand<br>\r\nthe power they will never have, of life<br>\r\nof life.<br><br>'),(3869,'<P>\r\ni woke up in an alley, my head aching, in the same suit i wore to work\r\nlast, whenever that was.  from the appearance of my suit, several days\r\nago, maybe a week.  there is blood on my pants, my shirt, and under my\r\nfingernails.  pity the fool whose blood this is.  my nose is intact,\r\nso it isn\'t my blood.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nwhiskers are a time line for me.  they let me know the last time i was\r\nme.  i always shave, every morning.  he doesn\'t.  he never shaves.\r\napparently he doesnt change his clothes either.  i horrify myself.  i\r\ndo not know what it is like to be homeless, just to wake up among them..\r\n</P><P>\r\nmy wallet isn\'t gone.  that is always nice.  i stopped carrying credit\r\ncards, or a bank card, after some bad experiences waking up like this\r\nwithout a wallet.\r\n</P><P>\r\nif i lost anything, it is my brain.  the empty box that is my skull is\r\nacknowledging the lack of courtesy payed to it of late by my body.  it\r\ndoes so by hurting like hell.  i stand up.  standing up was the wrong\r\nidea.  i settle back into some trash.  moments like these, life is too\r\nhard.  i dont know what to do, and if i did, i wouldn\'t feel like doing\r\nit.  i just want the pain to stop.  not moving seems to be the\r\nsolution.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\'m in the sun, it is nearly high noon and the rays are moving out\r\nfrom above the 3 story building and over the alley.  the sun makes me\r\nfeel bad again.  i realize that if i don\'t get out of the sun, i\'ll\r\nbarf.  the pain of sitting here will soon surpass the pain of moving\r\nforward down the road of life.\r\n</P><P>\r\nreally though, i feel myself fading.  i don\'t want to go on.  a black\r\nout is coming soon.  deep breaths.  i roll over so that at least i\r\nwon\'t get sunburned.  blackness is encroaching on my field of vision.\r\nwith a free arm, i pull some trash paper over my head.  it doesnt\r\nstink.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni start dreaming.  i\'m in the back of a cab, the cab driver has\r\nherpetic sores around his lips.  it is night time.  i imagine he\r\ndoesnt go out much in the day looking like that.  he is probably most\r\ncomfortable with his back to people.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\na cop sticks a knight-stick into my ribs, \"Sir! Let\'s go.  Time to wake\r\nup.\"  i feel better but hardly good.  the pain is not all binge drink\r\nleaving my system, some of it is just neglect pain.  my stomach is\r\nempty.  my foot feels like something in it is broken.  my back is\r\nstiff.  the cop is talking trash.  who cares.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni wonder if i still have an apartment.  the cop just wants me to start\r\nwalking.  i\'m ok with that.  perhaps i told him good bye.  where am i?\r\nbroadway and 9th.  i don\'t have keys on me.  i probably don\'t have a\r\ncar anymore.  it is a 25 block walk home.  a bus going somewhere in\r\nthat direction rolls up and i get on in the back.  the driver doesn\'t\r\nbother asking for money.  people get up and move seats away.  it must\r\nbe my good looks.  at least i\'m still good for a laugh.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni had to climb in through the window to my apartment.  it stinks of\r\ntrash and gnats are everywhere.  \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3870,'<p>Those days alternated between being full of light and excitement and dancing down the hallway with my new girl and being dark, drugged out, lying alone back-down on the floor trying to forget my old one. San Francisco made a man of me, if a delicate and flawed one. These days I feel like I\'ve lost some of myself, lost some sense of the size of things, of what matters to me. So stuck in foul weather and worse moods amidst the turmoil of my community always graduating to something else and going somewhere but not having any idea where just going, moving, getting pushed out, pushed around by no money and less sense, drinking its way to tomorrow with only the next drink planned and the one before that almost forgotten I feel like this time is a pastiche of every time before and after it none of which ever happened all jumbled together into the right now this moment this ticking clock.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The past has passed and the future is less certain than ever and I don\'t know what to hold on to anymore don\'t know what to worry about so I worry about everything don\'t know how to love like I used to when it felt so vital like a beating heart shaking me each moment reminding me that something must be done some line must be written some smile must not be forgotten and some breeze must be swallowed deeply so that I might whisper it into the ear of my lover.</p>\r\n<p>I drink to make things harder sometimes, because I can\'t stand it when it is easy when there isn\'t something shoving me from behind taking the wind out of me something bringing the blood to my face, I act out because it\'s the only thing that doesn\'t feel like acting, doesn\'t feel like some false performance meant to satisfy a non-existent inquisitor waiting around each corner for me to take the wrong turn and end up failed and wasted and broke and broken and unsure and dope-sick for the sun and an eastern gale heading over western lands that took me here but hasn\'t been back to pick me up.</p>\r\n<p> Everything is mixed up but static, full of life but on hold, like those old pictures of cornucopias they tacked to the wall each November. I am a twenty-seven year old man. Or that\'s what they tell me. I still feel like a boy.</p>'),(3871,'<P>\"Wait.  I wanna try somethin\' I saw on the internet.\"<BR><BR>\"Oh, yeah?\"<BR><BR>\"Oh, yeah.  It\'s a one-at-a-time type proposition, though.\"<BR><BR>\"Isn\'t it all.\"<BR><BR>\"I tell you what - I\'m thinkin\' of a number between one \'n ten.  If you can guess whether it\'s even or odd, you can go first.\"<BR><BR>\"What does it matter if it\'s between one and ten if you just want me to guess whether it\'s even or odd?  Just go, jackass.  What do we do?\"<BR><BR>\"Okay.  Stand back here behind me.  Wait.  Put your underwear back on first.  Okay.  Gimme your hands.  Just your pointer fingers.  Alright.  Put this finger over here on this side \'n this one on the other, \'n put your thumbs back behind here.  Now what you\'re gonna do is pull it open.\"<BR><BR>\"I hope you know that if it tears, it\'s gonna mad hurt.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, I hope you know that if it does, <U>you\'re</U> gonna mad hurt.  I\'m talkin\' about just a little bit.  We\'re not gonna try to drive a Mack truck through it.  Look.  Look around here so you can see what you\'re doin\'.\"<BR><BR>\"This would be a lot easier if I got around in front.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, if you wanna get in front, that\'s your business.  If it was me, I\'d be off to the side, at least.  Heh heh heh.\"<BR><BR>\"What is it?\"<BR><BR>\"This reminds of when I was in the hospital gettin\' my gall bladder taken out.\"<BR><BR>\"Why?  Did you do this in the hospital?  With the nurse helpin\' you out?\"<BR><BR>\"Not hardly.  When I was in the emergency room, there was a Mexican guy on the other side...\"<BR><BR>\"How do you know he was Mexican?\"<BR><BR>\"Because of his accent.  That \'n I heard him tell the social worker his mother lived in Reynosa.  <U><I>Any</I></U>way, a coupla nurses went over there \'n closed the curtain around \'im, and very shortly, he starts screamin\' \'¡Dios mío!  ¡Dios mío!\'.  I asked my nurse what the hell they were doin\' to \'im, \'n she said they were puttin\' in a catheter.\"<BR><BR>\"Uh-huh.  So did you get one, too?\"<BR><BR>\"Don\'t jump ahead.  So.  I\'m in there a coupla days, \'n then the morning of, they put me on some good drugs \'n take me down to prep me.  One of the nurses gets all the stubble off my belly, \'n then she asks, \'Does he need a catheter?\', \'n the doctor says I do, \'n I say, \'Listen - ya\'ll put me all the <U><B>way</B></U> out before you start fuckin\' around with no catheter.\'\"<BR><BR>\"Why did you say that?  \'No catheter.\'  Makes you sound like some kinda redneck.\"<BR><BR>\"That\'s the way I talk sometimes when I\'m messed up.  More or less.\"<BR><BR>\"So did they wait until they\'d put you out?\"<BR><BR>\"I guess.  Heh.  I can just barely remember when they were tryin\' to wake me up, \'n the nurse said, \'How was it?\', \'n I said, \'Piece \'a cake.\'\"<BR><BR>\"Yeah.  Matt McCord.  Man.  Myth.  Legend.\"<BR><BR>\"Damn straight.\"<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>my parents took my niece<BR>to disney world in the motor home last weekend.<BR>school started monday.<BR><BR>she had a full summer -<BR>got to go to north georgia<BR>and see all her cousins on<BR>dad\'s side of the family<BR>then go camping with them on sapelo island<BR>off the georgia coast.<BR><BR>got to go to a free concert in nashville<BR>that some country singer<BR>(i forget his name.  an australian.)<BR>gave for his fan club.<BR><BR>she got to stay with her dad for awhile,<BR>with his mom for awhile.<BR><BR>stayed with mom and dad<BR>at their time-share in pigeon forge.<BR>went to saint augustine for a week.<BR>and got to see the shuttle take off.<BR><BR>my sister invited me over last friday night<BR>for dinner and a movie.<BR>at the time of the invitation,<BR>i thought that maybe she\'d extended it because<BR>she thought we hadn\'t had any quality time in a while.<BR><BR>i told her i\'d cook<BR>if she bought the food.<BR><BR>(i didn\'t want to eat her cooking.<BR>i can cook better than both her <U>and</U> my mom.<BR>by far.)<BR><BR>we went to the grocery store and got stuff for<BR>pasta with caponata<BR><BR>(\"What\'s caponata?\"<BR><BR>\"It\'s a tomato sauce with zucchini and eggplant and bell pepper.  Other stuff.\"<BR><BR>\"No meat?\"<BR><BR>\"Trust me - you\'ve had enough protein today.  You\'ll be alright.\"<BR><BR>\"I know my protein requirements, goddamit.  I\'m a registered nurse.  They cover that shit, y\'know?  I\'m just asking a question.\")<BR><BR>and spinach salad,<BR>and also bought a cherry pie.<BR><BR>i thought we were done,<BR>but on our way to the checkout,<BR>she hung a sudden left<BR>and headed up the beer and wine aisle.<BR>she stopped in front of the wine<BR>and began to study the bottles,<BR>picking them up and reading the labels.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>I will be a son of a bitch.  She has got some balls to drink in front of me.  That\'s alright, though.  I\'ll remember that.</I><BR><BR>she proceeded to select<BR>four bottles of some sort of red wine<BR>and put them in the buggy<BR>two at a time.<BR><BR>(i avoided looking at it,<BR>so i didn\'t know what variety of red it was.<BR>i later learned that it was merlot.)<BR><BR>i could smell it,<BR>everything in the bottles and cans and jugs<BR>and whatnot.<BR><BR>i never go down that aisle when i\'m shopping.<BR>go out of my way to avoid it.<BR><BR>i waited while she set the last two in the buggy,<BR>and when she straightened up and met my gaze,<BR>i greeted her with a bemused expression.<BR><BR>\"You <U>do</U> know that I won\'t be joining you, right?\"<BR><BR>She chuckles.  \"No, I invited this girl from work over, too.  I thought you\'d like to meet \'er.\"<BR><BR>\"Oh, come on.  What the hell is this?\"<BR><BR>\"She\'s bipolar, too, so I thought you\'d have that in common, at least.\"<BR><BR>I thought, <I>Great God in Heaven.</I><BR><BR>\"I\'m not datin\' any woman who\'s bipolar.  That\'s just askin\' for trouble.\"<BR><BR>\"You don\'t know that.  It could turn out really well.\"<BR><BR>\"That would be just like datin\' an addict.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, <U>that</U> I can see.\"<BR><BR>\"Is she a nurse, too?\"<BR><BR>\"Yeah.\"<BR><BR>\"Have I seen \'er before?  Does she work upstairs?  At the villa?\"<BR><BR>\"Nah, they don\'t let anybody with a mental health diagnosis work in there.\"<BR><BR>\"Oh, yeah.  I told you about that one nurse, didn\'t I?  The one who told me she heard voices?\"<BR><BR>\"The one who said she was a hooker?\"<BR><BR>\"<U>Used</U> to be.  Said she was a high-class call girl in Chicago.  Shit.  She never woulda made no high-class one.  She couldn\'ta sold it for two dollars on payday.  Some kinda ugly.\"<BR><BR>\"Didn\'t she get in some kinda trouble over that?\"<BR><BR>\"Yeah.  I told my roommate what she said, \'n he slipped up \'n mentioned it to one \'a the patient care techs, \'n then it got back to her, \'n she called me into the office \'n asked me if i wanted her to write some fucked-up shit in my chart for the doctor to see.  luckily, one \'a the techs was sittin\' right there when she said it.  i told \'er to get \'er effin\' supervisor down there.  i dunno what finally happened with it.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, it was highly inappropriate for her to tell you all that, \'n then turn around \'n say she was gonna falsify your chart.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, does your friend have kids?  I\'m not gonna take on that kinda baggage.\"<BR><BR>\"No.  No kids.\"<BR><BR>later, in the kitchen:<BR><BR>\"Capers.  I always wondered what capers tasted like.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, haul one outta there \'n see.\"<BR><BR>at around six-thirty,<BR>her road dog lorrie rolls up.<BR>she has been graced in the tit department<BR>either by god or by science.<BR>pretty cute,<BR>but not cute enough.<BR><BR>they get into the wine before dinner.<BR>one glass, and then we eat.<BR>i don\'t think morgan picked out<BR>the right kind of wine(s?) for the food.<BR>she probably doesn\'t know anything about it.<BR>i don\'t either.<BR>i was never a wine person, really,<BR>so it doesn\'t bother me too much to watch them drinking it.<BR><BR>i ask lorrie, \"Do you drink often?\"<BR><BR>\"No.  Uh-uh.  Three or four times a year, maybe.\"<BR><BR>\"I wish <U>I</U> could do that.\"<BR><BR>it doesn\'t take them long to get silly.<BR><BR>i\'ve rarely been around anyone drunk while sober.<BR>it\'s pretty amusing.<BR>i think i had as much fun they did.<BR><BR>after we clean up,<BR>i put in the dvd i\'d checked out of the library.<BR>by this time,<BR>they\'re pretty toasty.<BR>all laughy \'n shit.<BR><BR>morgan asks, \"Isn\'t this about Vietnam?\"<BR><BR>\"Yeah, it\'s <U>the</U> movie about Vietnam.\"<BR><BR>\"Well, hot DAMN!\"<BR><BR>they laugh uncontrollably.<BR><BR>i don\'t know about you,<BR>but i have some kind of deep-seated need<BR>to expose people i love<BR>to art that moves me.<BR><BR>i guess everyone does,<BR>that it\'s basically a form of communication,<BR>but i think i invest a lot more in it<BR>than most people.<BR><BR>i\'m probably compensating for an inability to<BR>completely reveal myself<BR>through coventional means.<BR>(that also might be part of why i write.)<BR><BR>so, i wanted them to have The Experience,<BR>but, instead, it was like an episode of<BR><I>mystery science theatre 3000</I>.<BR><BR>i knew it was going to be bad<BR>when they started singing along with the doors<BR>at the beginning.<BR><BR>(<I>doo-doo-doo</I>ing over the intro,<BR><I>oh, wow</I> when the trees burst into flames,<BR>then recovering and catching up with jim.)<BR><BR>my sister says, \"Spoon.\"<BR><BR>lorrie says, \"Yeah.  I saw that.\"<BR><BR>i say, \"What?\"<BR><BR>morgan:  You didn\'t see that spoon?<BR><BR>\"What spoon?\"<BR><BR>\"Go back.\"<BR><BR>(i had the remote.  of course.  control freak.)<BR><BR>and there it is,<BR>as the camera pans over the nightstand:<BR>a pack of cigarettes with a zippo lying on top,<BR>a water glass (that photographed so beautifully),<BR>and a spoon.<BR>the handle is unbent.<BR>it appears clean.<BR><BR>i say, \"How about that?\"<BR><BR>morgan:  Well, that\'s pretty good that you didn\'t notice it, there, Pookie.  That\'s a good sign.<BR><BR>\"But I\'ve <U>never</U> noticed it.  I\'ve seen this forty or fifty times, \'n this\'s the first time I picked up on it.\"<BR><BR>lorrie:  <U>Forty</U> or <U>fifty</U> <U>times</U>?<BR><BR>me:  I don\'t think it means anything.  He doesn\'t seem like a junkie.<BR><BR>(it was the original version, not <I>redux</I>.<BR>i didn\'t mention the opium smoking<BR>in the french plantation sequence,<BR>didn\'t want to get into the fact<BR>that there even <U>was</U> another version<BR>and make them think they were missing out on something.)<BR><BR>i don\'t think he was meant to be a junkie, though.<BR>it would have shown him using (prior to the opium).<BR>i <U>am</U> shocked that i never noticed it, though.<BR><BR>...\"He had a heart attack filming this scene.\"<BR><BR>...\"Hey!  Harrison Ford!\"<BR><BR>\"Why isn\'t that guy wearing a uniform?\"<BR><BR>\"He\'s C.I.A.\"<BR><BR>\"And what the <U>hell</U> is wrong with his hair?\"<BR><BR>...ford\'s character:  Your mission is to blah blah and terminate the colonel\'s command.\"<BR><BR>willard:  (uncertainly) Terminate...the colonel.<BR><BR>the general:  He\'s out there operating without blah blah....<BR><BR>and then jerry, big c.i.a. man, looks at willard and says, \"Terminate...with <U>extreme</U> prejudice.\"<BR><BR>and my sister says, \"<U>Kill</U>.  <U>Him</U>.  <U>Fuckin\'</U>.  <U>Dead</U>.\"<BR><BR>and they just about wet themselves.<BR><BR>(after it was over, morgan said, \"I bet that\'s what Dan was.\"<BR><BR>dan is her ex-father-in-law.<BR>ex twice.<BR>first, dan left joe\'s mother,<BR>and then joe left morgan.<BR><BR>dan was a lurp.<BR>they were hardcore motherfuckers<BR>who went into enemy territory<BR>for weeks at a time<BR>for purposes of reconnaisance<BR>and for just generally puttin\' the hurt on people, i guess.<BR><BR>when he got drunk enough<BR>(which is to say not infrequently),<BR>he would regale you with tales of his exploits.<BR>he would often boast of having \"seen the gates of phnom penh\".<BR><BR>i often wondered<BR>(but never asked)<BR>if they had let him in.<BR>probably not.<BR>probably afraid he\'d drink all their beer.<BR><BR>joe told morgan<BR>that from when he was seven<BR>(when dan and his mother got married)<BR>until he was thirteen or fourteen,<BR>he was constantly afraid<BR>that dan was going to kill them.<BR><BR>i\'ve often thought about what might have happened<BR>after willard started back down that river.<BR>might have been a hell of a picture.)<BR><BR>and then willard\'s in the boat.<BR><BR>lorrie says, \"Is that Morpheus?!\"<BR><BR>i say, \"Yeah.  Laurence Fishburne.\"<BR><BR>\"God, he looks young.  When was this made?\"<BR><BR>\"Seventy-seven, seventy-eight, I guess.  He was fourteen.\"<BR><BR>\"Did you see that movie?\"<BR><BR>\"<I>The Matrix</I>?  Yeah.  I didn\'t really care for it.\"<BR><BR>\"Oh.\"<BR><BR>and then chief says, \"I pulled a few special ops in here.  Took a guy up north of the Do Long bridge.  He was regular army, too....  Heard he shot himself in the head.\"<BR><BR>they think that\'s funny as hell.<BR><BR>they think virtually everything robert duvall says is hilarious.<BR>(i guess it <U>is</U> pretty funny.)<BR><BR>and they make cracks about almost everything else.<BR>they\'re very witty.<BR>quick.<BR>i\'m not quick.<BR>i always think of the perfect rejoinder five minutes too late.<BR><BR>i feel compelled to pause it<BR>and explain who bill graham was<BR>and how he died in a helicopter crash<BR>going home from a show.<BR>i don\'t think they gave a shit.<BR><BR>the sampan massacre gave them pause, of course.<BR><BR>and then there was something about marlon brando<BR>they couldn\'t take seriously.<BR>his voice,<BR>his delivery,<BR>something.<BR><BR>they kept mimicing him and laughing.<BR><BR>\"in the form of gardenias....\"<BR><BR>when it was over,<BR>lorrie went to the bathroom.<BR><BR>morgan said, \"Well.  What do you think?\"<BR><BR>\"About her?\"<BR><BR>\"No.  About this crazy movie you\'ve seen five hundred times.  Yes, her.\"<BR><BR>\"She\'s nice \'n all, I guess, but she doesn\'t really cock my pistol.  You know what i mean?\"<BR><BR>\"Well, who does?\"<BR><BR>\"Think about every female you\'ve seen me with since the beginning of time.  Cast your memory back.  Somebody like that.\"<BR><BR>\"You know, I hate to say it, but you\'re starting to get to the point where you won\'t be able to be so choosy anymore.\"<BR><BR>\"Oh, I beg to differ.\"<BR><BR>man, she pissed me off.<BR><BR>so i was pissed off at that,<BR>pissed off at the whole ruse to begin with<BR>(though I know I shouldn\'t have been.<BR>she was just trying to help me out.),<BR>and pissed that they didn\'t appreciate the movie,<BR><BR>so we were talking afterwards<BR>(they opened another bottle),<BR>mostly about them,<BR>mostly about their jobs,<BR>how much they love them,<BR><BR>and i asked them<BR>(i don\'t know why i asked them.<BR>i must be evil.<BR>am i evil?):<BR><BR>\"Do you guys know you could be drafted?\"<BR><BR>well, that took the red right off their candy.<BR>they had received no word of this.<BR>i filled them in.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>You know, I might not be making twenty-five dollars an hour (in Florida), but at least I can rest easy at night knowing that my name isn\'t on a list somewhere.  At least not on that kind of list.</I><BR><BR>we wrap it up around quarter to eleven.<BR>lorrie offers me a ride home.<BR>i accept.<BR>on the way, i make a reevaluation.<BR><BR>when we get there,<BR>i ask if she wants to come in<BR>and get the nickel tour.<BR><BR>we make it as far as the kitchen,<BR>where we soon find ourselves<BR>frantically fucking,<BR>standing up,<BR>with her against the kitchen wall<BR>between the refrigerator and the bar.<BR><BR>on her way out,<BR>i refrain from asking her for a nickel.<BR><BR>the whole thing took about fifteen minutes.<BR><BR>i haven\'t heard from her since.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>and then it transpired that the neighbor lady got to see my dick.  she lives three or four houses up the hill from me, on the other side of the street.  husband, two or three little kids.  they try to keep the house nice, the yard nice, maintain the property value, but it\'s kind of pointless because the people next door have junk cars parked in the yard and a lawn that\'s half sand.<BR><BR>so, she\'s kind of snobbish when, really, she doesn\'t have any room to be, living in this neighborhood.  she\'s outside with the kids a lot, and when i walk by, i always greet her, and she always ignores me.  looks away.<BR><BR>so, one afternoon last month, after trying to be neighborly for over a year, i got tired of her snubbing and told her that it was considered common courtesy to respond to someone when they addressed you.  and then she pretended like she hadn\'t heard me.  and then i called her a cunt.  and then i went home.<BR><BR>so after lorrie left friday night, i took my meds and passed all the <u>way</U> out.  sometimes, i don\'t sleep, but when i do, i sleeps hard.<BR><BR>i wake up when someone taps on my bedroom window.  (this had also happened about two months before at about two-thirty on a sunday morning.  i was in bed reading, tap tap, it\'s a very drunk young man who wants his money, has the wrong house.  he tries to barge in when i open the door.  he almost got very fucked up.)<BR><BR>i grab my glasses off the computer stand and pull the blinds open.  nobody there.  only that one-eyed cat lurking by the number three palm kind of half-watching me.  clock says it\'s ten after midnight.<BR><BR>i go out into the living room and turn the lamp on.  instead of looking through the blinds in the front window, which would have been safer, i unlock the door and throw it open.  i do not realize that i am naked.<BR><BR>at that moment, in slow motion, the silver kia sportage that i recognize as belonging to the neighbor lady appears from behind the far side of the duplex, slows, and turns at the corner.  our eyes meet.  she slams on the brakes.  after what seems like a good five minutes, i finally react and jump back and push the door closed.<BR><BR>(it wasn\'t open that far.  i don\'t know what kind of view she got.  much later, i left the door open and went out by the road and tried to gauge what she could have seen, if the streetlight and the lamp inside would have provided enough illumination.  i concluded that she could probably give a pretty good description.  that area is kind of showcased by dint of the fact that everything above it and below it is tanned.  [except for the four little scars on my abdomen from the gall bladder surgery.  three on the right side {my right} going down diagonally from just east of the midline and then one right under the navel.  it looks like i was stabbed with a letter opener.])<BR><BR>so i slammed the door and jumped over and peeped through the blinds.  she was already halfway up the block.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>Well, to look at it logically, she\'s either going to call the cops, or she isn\'t.  If she does, it will take them half an hour or an hour to get to you.  You can either turn the lights off and pretend you aren\'t home or get dressed and wait for them to come.</I><BR><BR>i pulled on some sweat pants, opened the blinds, turned on cbs.  i sat up until the end of <I>the late, late show</I> (one-thirty), then went to bed.<BR><BR>so far, nothing\'s happened.  i haven\'t walked up her way since then, though.  been going the long way \'round.  i should stroll by one afternoon and offer to give her a jump.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>he came over last night.<BR>the knitters were on <I>letterman</I>.<BR>after it was over,<BR>i played <I>under the big black sun</I> for him.<BR>he wasn\'t into it.<BR><BR>i said, \"You know, I bet the guys in Tool like this music.\"<BR><BR>\"They can have it.\"<BR><BR>sigh.<BR>i need to find some people i can relate to,<BR>but i guess that would involve leaving<BR>the five block-square box i live in.<BR><BR>(and until last week,<BR>i never knew that<BR><I>good morning, midnight</I><BR>was a novel.<BR><BR>i\'m going to have to get that on loan<BR>from another library.<BR>i wonder if it\'s as good as <I>wide sargasso sea</I>.<BR>probably not.)<BR><BR>afterwards, i made some iced tea,<BR>and we sat out on the stoop<BR>and watched for shooting stars.<BR><BR>we had to be quiet<BR>because people moved into the other half of the duplex,<BR>and whoever sleeps in the bedroom next to mine<BR>leaves their window open at night.<BR><BR>(they\'re from guatemala.<BR>this must be cool weather to them.<BR><BR>the landlord showed me the apartment<BR>before they moved in.<BR>i\'d never seen it.<BR><BR>there\'s a short wall between<BR>my bedroom and bathroom doors.<BR>i have a bookcase there.<BR><BR>directly behind that wall,<BR>a hallway runs between the two bedrooms<BR>in the other apartment.<BR><BR>if you took out the wall,<BR>the hallway would extend into my aparment<BR>and flow right into the living room.<BR><BR>it reminds me of something.<BR>i just don\'t know what.)<BR><BR>after about twenty minutes,<BR>a barefoot girl<BR>wandered up the sidewalk,<BR>saw us,<BR>entered the yard,<BR>and headed our way.<BR><BR>it didn\'t take long<BR>to ascertain that she was shitfaced.<BR>david turned to me<BR>and looked me in the eye,<BR>held my gaze.<BR><BR>i said, \"absolutely not.\"<BR><BR>she was lost.<BR>i invited her in<BR>and let her use the phone.<BR>i talked to somebody named rusty,<BR>gave him directions.<BR><BR>i offered her some tea.<BR>she just wanted water.<BR>we talked for a little while.<BR>she said she worked at a pizza place in holiday.<BR><BR>she passed out on the couch<BR>within half an hour.<BR>i got the <I>peanuts</I> blanket and covered her up,<BR>and david and i went back outside<BR>to wait for her people to get there.<BR><BR>i told him about the perseids,<BR>how they have occured at the same time every year.<BR><BR>i said, \"My dad\'s birthday was yesterday.  He\'s sixty-five.\"<BR><BR>\"So, he was born in a meteor shower, huh?\"<BR><BR>\"Yeah, but I don\'t know what good it did \'im.  He\'s never done anything fast in his life.  And he sure as shit hasn\'t been lucky.\"<BR><BR>\"Huh.\"<BR><BR>we walked out into the yard<BR>that should have been mowed two weeks ago<BR>and looked towards the various compass points.<BR>it was overcast.<BR>you couldn\'t even see regular stars.<BR><BR>david said, \"I don\'t think we\'re going to be able to see anything.\"<BR><BR>\"No.  No wishes tonight.\"</P>	 '),(3872,'A couple of weeks ago we decided to cruise out to Ryan\'s Steakhouse for dinner. It was a Wednesday night which means that macaroni and beef was on the hot bar, indeed the only night of the week that it is served. Wednesday night is also kid\'s night at Ryan\'s, complete with Dizzy the Clown wandering from table to table entertaining the little bastards. It may seem that the events about to be told have little connection to those two circumstances, but all will be clear in a moment.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe went through the line and placed our orders for the all-you-can-eat hot bar then sat down as far away from the front of the restaurant as possible in order to keep the density of kids down a bit. Then I started my move to the hot bar. Plate after plate of macaroni and beef were consumed that evening, I tell you - in all, four heaping plates of the pseudo-Italian ambrosia were shoved into my belly. I was sated. Perhaps a bit too much, however.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI had not really been feeling well all day, what with a bit of gas and such. By the time I had eaten four overwhelmed plates of food, I was in real trouble. There was so much pressure on my diaphragm that I was having trouble breathing. At the same time, the downward pressure was building. At first I thought it was only gas, which could have been passed in batches right at the table without too much concern.<br><br>\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, that was not to be. After a minute or so it was clear that I was dealing with explosive diarrhea. It\'s amazing how grease can make its way through your intestines far faster than the food which spawned the grease to begin with, but I digress... I got up from the table and made my way to the bathroom. Upon entering, I saw two sinks immediately inside the door, two urinals just to the right of the sinks, and two toilet stalls against the back wall. One of them was a handicapped bathroom. Now, normally I would have gone to the handicapped stall since I like to stretch out a bit when I take a good shiite. But in this case, the door lock was broken and the only thing I hate worse than my wife telling me to stop cutting my toenails with a pair of diagonal wire-cutters is having someone walk in on me while I am taking a shiite.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI went to the normal stall. In retrospect, I probably should have gone to the large, handicapped stall even though the door would not lock because that bit of time lost in making the stall switch proved to be a bit too long under the circumstances. By the time I had walked into the regular stall, the pressure on my ass was reaching Biblical portions. I began \"The Move.\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nFor those women who may be reading this, let me take a moment to explain \"The Move.\" Men know exactly what their bowels are up to at any given second. And when the time comes to empty the cache, a sequence of physiological events occur that can not be stopped under any circumstances. There is a move men make that involves simultaneously approaching the toilet, beginning the body turn to position ones ass toward said toilet, hooking ones fingers into ones waistline, and pulling down the pants while beginning the squat at the same time. It is a very fluid motion that, when performed properly, results in the flawless expulsion of shiite at the exact same second that one’s ass is properly placed on the toilet seat. Done properly, it even assures that the choad is properly inserted into the front rim of the toilet in the event that the piss stream lets loose at the same time; it is truly a picture of coordination rivaling that of a skilled ballet dancer.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI was about halfway into \"The Move\" when I looked down at the floor and saw a pile of vomit that had been previously expelled by one of those little bastards attending kids night. It was mounded up in the corner so I did not notice it when I had first walked into the stall. Normally, I would not have been bothered by such a thing, but I had eaten so much and the pressure upward was so intense, that I hit a rarely experienced gag reflex. And once that reflex started, combined with the intense pressure upward caused by the bloated stomach, four plates of macaroni and beef started coming up for a rematch.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhat happened next was so quick that the exact sequence of events is a bit fuzzy, but I will try to reconstruct them as best I can. In that moment of impending projectile vomiting, my attention was diverted from the goings-on at the other end. To put a freeze frame on the situation, I was half crouched down to the toilet, pants pulled down to my knees, with a load of vomit coming up my esophagus.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow, most of you know that vomiting takes precedence over shiite no matter what is about to come slamming out of your ass. It is apparently an evolutionary thing since s**tting will not kill you, but vomiting takes a presence of mind to accomplish so that you do not aspirate any food into the bronchial tubes and perhaps choke to death. My attention was thus diverted. At that very split second, my ass exploded in what can only be described as a wake...you know, as in a newspaper headline along the lines of \"30,000 Killed In Wake of Typhoon Fifi\" or something similar. In what seemed to be most suitably measured in cubic feet, an enormous plug of shiite the consistency of thick mud with embedded pockets of greasy liquid came flying out of my ass.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut remember, I was only halfway down on the toilet at that moment. The shiite wave was of such force, and of just such an angle in relation to the back curve of the toilet seat, that it ricocheted off the back of the seat and slammed into the wall - at an angle of incidence equal to the angle at which it initially hit the toilet seat. Then I sat down. Recall that when that event occurred, I was already halfway to sitting anyway and had actually reached the point of no return. I have always considered myself as relatively stable gravitationally, but when you get beyond a certain point, you\'re going down no matter how limber you may be. Needless to say, the shiite wave, though of considerable force, was not so sufficient so as to completely glance off the toilet seat and deposit itself on the walls - unlike what you would see when hitting a puddle with a high-pressure water hose; even though you throw water at the puddle, the puddle gets moved and no water is left to re-form a puddle. There was a significant amount of shiite remaining on about one-third of the seat rim which I had now just collapsed upon.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNow, back to the vomit...<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhile all the s**tting was going on, the vomit was still on its way up. By the time I had actually collapsed on the toilet, my mouth had filled up with a goodly portion of the macaroni and beef I had just consumed. OK, so what does the human body instinctively do when vomiting? One bends over. So I bent over. I was still sitting on the toilet, though. Therefore, bending over resulted in me placing my head above my now slightly-opened legs, positioned in between my knees and waist. Also directly above my pants which were now pulled down to a point just midway between my knees and my ankles. Oh, did I mention that I was wearing not just pants, but sweatpants with elastic on the ankles. In one mighty push, some three pounds of macaroni and beef, two or three Cokes, and a couple of Big, Fat Yeast Rolls were deposited in my pants...on the inside...with no ready exit at the bottom down by my feet. In the next several seconds, there were a handful of farts, a couple of turds, and the event ended. Yet I was now sitting there with my pants full of vomit, my back covered in shiite that had bounced off the toilet, spattered on three ceramic-tiled walls to a height of about five feet, and still had enough force to come back at me, covering the back of my shirt with droplets of liquid shiite. All while thick shiite was spread all over my ass in a ring curiously in the shape of a toilet seat.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd there was no f@cking toilet paper. What could I do but laugh. I must have sounded like a complete maniac to the guy who then wandered into the bathroom. He actually asked if I was OK since I was laughing so hard I must have sounded like I was crying hysterically. I calmed down just enough to ask him if he would get the manager. And told him to have the manager bring some toilet paper. When the manager walked in, he brought the toilet paper with him, but in no way was prepared for what happened next. I simply told him that there was no way I was going to explain what was happening in the stall, but that I needed several wet towels and I needed him to go ask my wife to come help me. I told him where we were sitting and he left. At that point, I think he was probably assuming that I had pissed just a bit in my pants or something similarly benign.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAbout two minutes later, my wife came into the bathroom not knowing what was wrong and with a certain amount of worry in her voice. I explained to her (still laughing and having trouble getting out words) that I had a slight accident and needed her help. Knowing that I had experienced some close calls in the past, she probably assumed that I had laid down a small turd or something and just needed to bring the car around so we could bolt immediately. Until I asked her, I\'m sure she had no idea that she was about to go across the street and purchase me new underwear, new socks, new pants, a new shirt, and (by that time due to considerable leakage around the elastic ankles thingies) new sneakers. And she then started to laugh herself since I was still laughing. She began to ask for an explanation as to what had happened when I promised her that I would tell her later, but that I just needed to handle damage control for the time being. She left.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe manager then came back in with a half-dozen wet towels and a few dry ones. I asked him to also bring a mop and bucket upon which he assured me that they would clean up anything that needed to be cleaned. Without giving him specific details, I explained that what was going on in that stall that night was far in excess of what I would expect anyone to deal with, what with most of the folks working at Ryan\'s making minimum wage of just slightly above. At that moment, I think it dawned on him exactly the gravity of the situation. Then that manager went so far above the call of duty that I will be eternally grateful for his actions. He hooked up a hose. Fortunately, commercial bathrooms are constructed with tile walls and tile floors and have a drain in the middle of the room in order to make clean up easy. Fortunately, I was in a commercial bathroom. He hooked up the hose to the spigot located under the sink as I began cleaning myself up with the wet towels.<br><br>\r\n\r\nJust as I was finishing, my wife got back with the new clothes and passed them into the stall, whereupon I stuffed the previously worn clothing into the plastic bag that came from the store, handing the bag to my wife. I finished cleaning myself off and carefully put on my new clothes, still stuck in the stall since I figured that it would be in bad taste to go out of the stall to get redressed, in the event I happened to be standing there naked and some little bastard kid walked in. At that point, I had only made a mess; I had not yet committed a felony and intended to keep it that way.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I finished getting dressed, I picked up the hose and cleaned up the entire stall, washing down the remains toward the drain in the center of the room. I put down the hose and walked out of the bathroom. I had intended to go to the manager and thank him for all he had done, but when I walked out, three of the management staff were there to greet me with a standing ovation. I started laughing so hard that I thought I was going to throw up again, but managed to scurry out to the car where my wife was now waiting to pick me up by the front door.<br><br>'),(3873,'<P>david wanted me to go to ybor city with him saturday night.  i told him that there was absolutely no way i could, but that he should go ahead and ask someone else and enjoy himself.<BR><BR>i told him about the first time i went to ybor city, in august of 1985, with my brother and our girlfriends.  it was the first and only time tim took me out.<BR><BR>he had left home immediately after graduating, taking an apartment downtown, renting by the week, working at the hardware store on the courthouse square, waiting until the end of august, when he would move to gainesville and start school.<BR><BR>i was spending the night with him that night.  he said we weren\'t going to bed until the sun was well up.  we went to the movies drunk at university square mall (i have no idea what we saw.), then went to steak \'n shake and cruised around tampa until three or four in the morning, drinking jim beam and coke.<BR><BR>somewhere in the wee hours, tim decided he needed to show us ybor city.  in those days, it was wild and woolly with total class and cred, a shadowy netherworld that was miles away from the bourbon street-style amusement park it is today.  god, it was so much more interesting then.<BR><BR>the high point of our tour was when we rolled down the street past where the labor temple opened up a few years later (wow.  i think i could probably write a pretty long book called <I>nights at the labor temple</I>.  much went on.) and saw two guys standing on the sidewalk under the streetlight.  one was jacking the other off.  he stopped and stared at us until we passed by.  tim said he was going to go back and sell me to them.<BR><BR>(and now that i think about it, it was the labor temple <I>then</I>.  the building itself has always been called that.  it\'s an old union hall.  the reggae club of the same name that was located in the building didn\'t open until several years after this story takes place.)<BR><BR>tim died three weeks later at around three-thirty or four in the morning on august twenty-fifth.  he was due to leave for gainesville that afternoon.<BR><BR>saturday, i was in brooksville and thought about the summer when i was nine, and we had a book of neat things for kids to do, and tim and morgan and i made a crystal set radio one day when we were bored.  i told paige, my niece, about it, and she wanted to make one, so we went to radio shack to get the parts.  (my sister let me drive her car.  wow.)<BR><BR>i couldn\'t find the book that had the instructions in it, but there were some on the internet.  it took about an hour to finish.  we could pick up seven stations using fifty feet of stranded insulated wire as an antenna.  could have probably done a lot better if we weren\'t in the woods.  (we managed to pick up only one twenty-five years ago, but we didn\'t have much of an antenna then, as i recall, and there weren\'t as many stations.)<BR><BR>that night at dinner, i suggested that we each tell what we remembered most fondly about tim.  i thought mom was going to cry, but she didn\'t.  nobody had mentioned him, the impending anniversary, all day.<BR><BR>we came up with:  kind, funny, made friends easily, determined, strong, loyal, always stayed busy, helped people, pushover, liked to be around people but also by himself, loved chinese food and ribs and cheesecake, had a goofy side, all the girls thought he was hot (always made me look bad), good athlete, liked animals, liked little kids, tight with a dollar, curious, loving.<BR><BR>there\'s an anne sexton poem called <I>the truth the dead know</I>.  she never explicity states what that truth is, but i\'m guessing it boils down to <I>no worries</I>, basically.  they probably think it\'s funny that we let things upset us so.  they know that none of it means much.</P>'),(3874,'<P>\r\nThe main character seems to have it together. She is in her twenties and is an office clerk. She is nothing out of the ordinary. Very calm and collected. There is a certain sadness in her eyes but it’s not something that people pick up on or ask about on the whole. She is the sort of person that would hold herself together in a time of crisis- or so she leads people to believe. Her internal dialogue is quite different to the idea of the girl she allows the world to see.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI hate Mondays. What a cliché to state. It’s not like there is anyone that loves Mondays. Is there? Mondays are de-tox days. They are the days that I wish I didn’t smoke. I spend all weekend smoking like a train then come Monday I go to work where I am a \"non smoker\". I guess it’s my own fault. I never intended to be a smoker. It just kinda happened and now I don’t know why I should stop. It’s not like I enjoy it, but it’s not like I don’t enjoy it. It’s something to keep me occupied whilst I’m wasting precious time.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nMondays. It’s true what they say about cigarettes and mood swings. It’s pretty fucked. It’s like you stop smoking and every fucked up thing that has ever happened to you in your life comes back to plague you, even if you thought you dealt with it. You just have to keep reminding yourself that you don’t really feel that way. It’s just the chemicals. I won’t be overwhelmed by a fucking need to inhale that smog... oh, my eyes are watering just thinking about it.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI got home from work. She was sitting in the lounge with two boys. One I recognized as the guy Jenny had scored some coke off at a club once. The other had an English accent. \"Here\" she said. Jenny handed me a glass of wine that she had saved for me in the kitchen. I thought it was a little odd because there was plenty of wine, and vodka, and scotch in the lounge room. I drank it. I drank more. And more. We were playing a drinking game so it was kind of expected. You know the one with the cards and they are all assigned a task. For some reason the tasks were all overtly sexual. I played anyway. I walked into the kitchen and the English guy followed me. He told me his name and said he was from Liverpool. He asked if he could have some toast. I made him some but he said he would prefer it darker. So I left him there to make his toast and went back to the lounge. Jenny started kissing Coke Boy. Then she started yelling at me to fuck the English boy. I thought they were joking. Get real! I didn’t want to. I was in love with someone that didn’t love me and I just couldn’t do that with a stranger. I kept saying no. for about an hour I fended off \"You’re beautiful\" comments and \"I wish you’d fuck me\" comments. I even told them I had my period but that didn’t matter apparently. You know it’s fucking exhausting trying to defend yourself for so long. You just run out of energy. Eventually I took English boy into the hall and said \"Look, can we just say we did to get her off my back?\" He asked me why I didn’t want to kiss him and I told him the truth. I was in love with someone else and I didn’t want to. I was looking at the wall behind him and suddenly felt like I was going to pass out. I’ve never felt so dizzy in my life. Jenny was fucking Coke Boy on the couch. Giggling and screaming. I walked in the bed room and laid down on the unmade bed. I was so dizzy. I could barely move. The English boy followed me in there. I vaguely remember him taking off my clothes, and taking off his. . I was so lethargic. He kept whispering to me \"I wanna fuck you\" and I kept saying no. He started to touch me but I could barely feel a thing. He put his fingers inside me. I didn’t know what to do. I mean I had no control but I didn’t want him to fuck me. Jenny and the Coke boy were fucking beside me. So I grab the English boys cock and tug. At least this way he won’t fuck me. He’s breathing heavily and still saying he wants to fuck me and I ignore him. I wish he’d hurry up. Jenny is so fucking noisy. I can’t remember this guy’s name. I think the English boy has come and I let go of him. I pass out as he puts on his clothes. He whispers in my ear that he’s sorry. Then he leaves. Jenny spiked my drink.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nJesus, I need a cigarette. It’s just the cigarettes talking. It’s done with. Work is so boring. It’s not as if I have any major responsibilities here, thank god. That would make Mondays so much harder. I only an hour to go. And an hour and a half until my first cigarette for the day. \r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI need to quit. I need to stop thinking. I just need to stop.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n(blackout). \r\n</P>'),(3876,'The main character seems to have it together. She is in her twenties and is an office clerk. She is nothing out of the ordinary. Very calm and collected. There is a certain sadness in her eyes but it’s not something that people pick up on or ask about on the whole. She is the sort of person that would hold herself together in a time of crisis- or so she leads people to believe. Her internal dialogue is quite different to the idea of the girl she allows the world to see.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nI hate Mondays. What a cliché to state. It’s not like there is anyone that loves Mondays. Is there? Mondays are de-tox days. They are the days that I wish I didn’t smoke. I spend all weekend smoking like a train then come Monday I go to work where I am a \"non smoker\". I guess it’s my own fault. I never intended to be a smoker. It just kinda happened and now I don’t know why I should stop. It’s not like I enjoy it, but it’s not like I don’t enjoy it. It’s something to keep me occupied whilst I’m wasting precious time.\r\nMondays. It’s true what they say about cigarettes and mood swings. It’s pretty fucked. It’s like you stop smoking and every fucked up thing that has ever happened to you in your life comes back to plague you, even if you thought you dealt with it. You just have to keep reminding yourself that you don’t really feel that way. It’s just the chemicals. I won’t be overwhelmed by a fucking need to inhale that smog... oh, my eyes are watering just thinking about it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI got home from work. She was sitting in the lounge with two boys. One I recognized as the guy Jenny had scored some coke off at a club once. The other had an English accent. \"Here\" she said. Jenny handed me a glass of wine that she had saved for me in the kitchen. I thought it was a little odd because there was plenty of wine, and vodka, and scotch in the lounge room. I drank it. I drank more. And more. We were playing a drinking game so it was kind of expected. You know the one with the cards and they are all assigned a task. For some reason the tasks were all overtly sexual. I played anyway. I walked into the kitchen and the English guy followed me. He told me his name and said he was from Liverpool. He asked if he could have some toast. I made him some but he said he would prefer it darker. So I left him there to make his toast and went back to the lounge. Jenny started kissing Coke Boy. Then she started yelling at me to fuck the English boy. I thought they were joking. Get real! I didn’t want to. I was in love with someone that didn’t love me and I just couldn’t do that with a stranger. I kept saying no. for about an hour I fended off \"You’re beautiful\" comments and \"I wish you’d fuck me\" comments. I even told them I had my period but that didn’t matter apparently. You know it’s fucking exhausting trying to defend yourself for so long. You just run out of energy. Eventually I took English boy into the hall and said \"Look, can we just say we did to get her off my back?\" He asked me why I didn’t want to kiss him and I told him the truth. I was in love with someone else and I didn’t want to. I was looking at the wall behind him and suddenly felt like I was going to pass out. I’ve never felt so dizzy in my life. Jenny was fucking Coke Boy on the couch. Giggling and screaming. I walked in the bed room and laid down on the unmade bed. I was so dizzy. I could barely move. The English boy followed me in there. I vaguely remember him taking off my clothes, and taking off his. . I was so lethargic. He kept whispering to me \"I wanna fuck you\" and I kept saying no. He started to touch me but I could barely feel a thing. He put his fingers inside me. I didn’t know what to do. I mean I had no control but I didn’t want him to fuck me. Jenny and the Coke boy were fucking beside me. So I grab the English boys cock and tug. At least this way he won’t fuck me. He’s breathing heavily and still saying he wants to fuck me and I ignore him. I wish he’d hurry up. Jenny is so fucking noisy. I can’t remember this guy’s name. I think the English boy has come and I let go of him. I pass out as he puts on his clothes. He whispers in my ear that he’s sorry. Then he leaves. Jenny spiked my drink.<br><br>\r\n\r\nJesus, I need a cigarette. It’s just the cigarettes talking. It’s done with. Work is so boring. It’s not as if I have any major responsibilities here, thank god. That would make Mondays so much harder. I only an hour to go. And an hour and a half until my first cigarette for the day. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI need to quit. I need to stop thinking. I just need to stop.<br><br>\r\n\r\n(blackout). <br><br>\r\n'),(3877,'<P><I>If God is good he is not God,<BR>If God is God he is not good;<BR>Take the even, take the odd,<BR>I would not sleep here if I could<BR>except for the little green leaves on the wood<BR>and the wind on the water....</I><BR><BR>my cousin died yesterday.<BR>i never met him.<BR>he spent his life<BR>(most of the summer)<BR>in a little plastic box<BR>in a hospital in tampa.<BR><BR>he had undergone heart surgery<BR>and a colostomy<BR>and was probably blind in one eye.<BR><BR>he was my mother\'s brother\'s daughter\'s son.<BR>his sister is four years old.<BR>she never saw him, either.<BR>they wouldn\'t let her in the ward.<BR><BR>my parents visited him.<BR>my sister and i intended to,<BR>but couldn\'t seem to get it together<BR>and go down there.<BR><BR>i had been home from the library<BR>about twenty minutes yesterday afternoon<BR>when the phone rang.<BR><BR>mom said he had been plagued with<BR>bouts of swelling in his abdomen<BR>for a couple of days.<BR>it had gotten so bad that it was<BR>cutting off circulation to his legs.<BR><BR>at some point in the afternoon,<BR>it was determined that his intestines were dead.<BR>his parents decided to take him off life support,<BR>and he died shortly after four o\'clock.<BR><BR>i made some tea<BR>and went out and sat on the step<BR>and waited for david.<BR><BR>the landlord hasn\'t cut the grass<BR>for about five weeks.<BR>it looks awful.<BR><BR>i called the city about it monday.<BR>they said they\'d send him a letter,<BR>fine him if he didn\'t cut it.<BR>i didn\'t know what else to do.<BR><BR>the kids were riding bikes<BR>in the parking lot across the street.<BR>there\'s a new tentant over there,<BR>young guy,<BR>who looks like he might be trouble.<BR>bad news.<BR><BR>david usually shows up around quarter to six.<BR>he gets off work at five,<BR>goes home, takes a shower, gets high.<BR><BR>(that\'s what i always did<BR>when i was working:<BR>get home from work,<BR>get home from school, whatever,<BR>smoke some weed,<BR>decompress.<BR>it was mandatory.)<BR><BR>i kept looking in his direction<BR>(in the direction of his grandmother\'s house)<BR>west on pennsylvania<BR>until he appeared at the top of the hill.<BR><BR>he grinned when he saw me.<BR>his face was a little pink,<BR>his hair wet.<BR>(he has jaw-length hair, parted in the middle.)<BR><BR>i found a new oldies station on the radio.<BR>the other one had changed their format<BR>from sixties to sixties and seventies,<BR>and then started venturing into the eighties,<BR>playing john cougar mellencamp<BR>and journey and the cars,<BR>and i decided it was time to move on.<BR><BR>this new one plays late fifties, early sixties,<BR>a few from later on.<BR>they apparently have a huge library.<BR><BR>it trips david out pretty hard<BR>that i know the words to all the songs.<BR><BR>(there was one new to me this morning, though:<BR><I>last exit to brooklyn</I>.<BR>i had no idea that was a song.<BR>probably because i\'ve never read the book or seen the movie.<BR>it might figure in those.)<BR><BR>it trips him out even harder<BR>that i actually <U>like</U> the music,<BR>that i\'m in love with it, even.<BR>i subject him to it for only so long.<BR><BR>last night,<BR>in the space of the hour that we listened to it<BR>while i made dinner,<BR>there seemed to be quite a few songs<BR>related to new orleans,<BR>among them <I>mr. tambourine man</I><BR>(inspired by mardi gras)<BR>and <I>saturday morning</I>.<BR><BR>yesterday afternoon, i saw on salon.com<BR>that fats domino is missing.<BR>when the song came on,<BR>i asked david for the pesto<BR>(he was sitting at the bar<BR>paging through an old issue of <I>men\'s fitness</I>)<BR><BR><I>that green shit in that bowl right there by your hand</I><BR><BR>and explained who fats was<BR>and how marty, this crackhead i was in rehab with,<BR>claimed to be his cousin.<BR><BR>the other night, david asked<BR>if i\'d ever been to new orleans,<BR>and i told him that i lived there<BR>for six weeks one spring<BR>when i was a little older than he is now.<BR><BR>in march of \'ninety-three,<BR>my friend erik and i thought that<BR>it would be prudent to leave miami for awhile,<BR>maybe for good,<BR>thought we might as well see someplace new,<BR>picked new orleans,<BR>stocked up on the necessities,<BR>and headed out.<BR><BR>i thought that if we didn\'t like it there,<BR>we could go on to mexico.<BR>there was plenty of it i still hadn\'t seen.<BR><BR>in those days, i had what i considered to be<BR>a great deal of money<BR>(all of it obtained fraudulently)<BR>and was in the thrall<BR>of way too many things.<BR><BR>our chief concern was one of supply,<BR>but there was no shortage.<BR>it was everywhere.<BR><BR>you could step out of a club at five in the morning,<BR>and there would be little black boys<BR>- nine, ten -<BR>standing there ready to sell you<BR>bags of your pleasure.<BR><BR>we never bought from them, though.<BR>we found our connection the second night there,<BR>a coon-ass taxi driver<BR>who was eventually party to us<BR>getting jacked for several thousand dollars.<BR><BR>(my thinking on the subject when we set out<BR>was that if we couldn\'t hook up in time,<BR>we\'d proceed straight to the border.<BR>i knew people in nuevo laredo<BR>who could fix us up<BR>once we\'d crossed over.)<BR><BR>we got there on a monday afternoon,<BR>found a paper,<BR>and rented a furnished apartment<BR>in the garden district<BR>for a month.<BR><BR>after the rigors of travel,<BR>driving straight through from coconut grove,<BR>we resumed our nocturnal ways.<BR><BR>after a week,<BR>i decided that if Night had a capital,<BR>it was new orleans.<BR><BR>nowhere else that i\'d lived,<BR>not key west or gainesville<BR>or miami or mexico city,<BR>had presented me with nights<BR>that were as joyous,<BR>as fraught with possibility<BR>and adrenaline<BR>as the ones i killed there.<BR><BR>(and, yes,<BR>each of them felt like they had to be killed,<BR>to be wrestled, exhausted, euthanized,<BR>or they would have never ended.)<BR><BR>the water boiled.<BR>i poured in a little olive oil<BR>then dumped in the orzo,<BR>gave it a stir.<BR><BR>david said, <I>what the hell does this say?  \"PIN number.  Was that the year you lost your virginity?\"</I><BR><BR>he held a little slip of paper out to me.<BR><BR><I>jeez, i forgot all about that.  where\'d you find that?<BR><BR>it was in this magazine right here.  what\'s it mean?<BR><BR>it\'s dialogue.<BR><BR>oh.  writing.</I><BR><BR>he said \"writing\" in a tone<BR>that was either dismissive or amused.<BR><BR>i explained the scenario:<BR>two men.<BR>one learns the other\'s PIN number.<BR>the four digits comprise a year<BR>that fell not long before the one they\'re in.<BR>he seizes the opportunity<BR>to rib his companion.<BR><BR>i don\'t think he thought it was too funny.<BR><BR><I>the cbs evening news</I> was on.<BR>complete chaos in new orleans.<BR>i remarked on how much<BR>the mind relies on order,<BR>how stressful the lack of it is.<BR><BR>i told him how<BR>this had first come to my attention<BR>during the brief time i\'d worked<BR>at the funeral home my grandfather had founded.<BR><BR>i saw that the american death ritual,<BR>to the extent that it was embodied by the funeral industry,<BR>existed solely to restore equilibrium to the lives of the bereaved<BR>as fast as it could.<BR><BR>it did this through denial.<BR><BR>get the corpse out of the house as soon as possible.<BR>not that long ago,<BR>the deceased would be kept at home.<BR>their loved ones would sit with them through the night<BR>until they were buried.<BR><BR>the body is embalmed so it doesn\'t begin to smell.<BR>the face is made up.<BR><BR>a lot of people these days<BR>don\'t let themselves undergo a grieving process.<BR>if it hits them at all,<BR>it won\'t be until years later.<BR><BR>we got out the tv trays<BR>and sat on the couch<BR>and ate the pasta and sauce<BR>and salad and bread.<BR><BR>i said, <I>well, at least that isn\'t us.</I><BR><BR>and david said, <I>that <U>might</U> be us.  maybe this month, next month.</I><BR><BR>i didn\'t say anything.<BR><BR>a little later,<BR>they showed film of an intersection.<BR>on one corner,<BR>a mob of people was looting a walgreen\'s.<BR><BR>on the opposite corner,<BR>a man was blowing a trumpet,<BR>playing <I>he\'s got the whole world in his hands</I>.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>you could take that in a couple of different ways.</I></P>'),(3878,'<br> show me how one can really get over a sexual hangover that meant more than an experience that catapulted into the dire need to express the darkness encroaching within while userping from you whatever form it can take? ... an experience that culminates all forms of love one has experienced?<br>\r\n\r\n<br>the first kiss, the first crush, the first love, the high school fling, the first loss of womanhood, the entering into adulthood, the experiments, the child, the man, the false marriage, the liberation, the flings, more adult flings, and then the one ... the one you think was real, at a ripe and sensual age of womanhood ... where your body still embodies that beauty encaptured through ages, the teenage body, the botticelli body, the zenith of sex ...<br>\r\n\r\n<br>and then the letdown, because of certain fundamentals that does not seemingly fit within the paradigm of your now very rennaissance, intellectual and mature womanhood, albeit the mind of a true-free-mason ....<br>\r\n\r\n<br>and when you decide you do not want to compromise ... you push him just oh so far away ... you wait : days : weeks : months : maybe years? justifying why he cannot and possibly be a part of you ...<br>\r\n\r\n<br>and one simple ordinary day . . . you take your shopping bag, after a few exchanges of nonsense with strangers and the cashier, you realize, that there was this one soul that symbolized the embodiment of everything that you needed, yet lacked some of what you were supposed to have . . .<br>\r\n\r\n<br>then in that twilight . . . you realized you have lost him ...<br>\r\n\r\n<br>the rest ... is history ... a glass of wine, a warm fireplace, that dreaded stick of cigarette, your laptop, a blog site ... fingers through your hair, outlining your face, eyes closed, senza tears, but with all the doubts of whether you have done the right or wrong ... whether you have chosen the right juncture of your one and only life ...<br>\r\n\r\n<br>there is no simple answer .. unbreak my heart .. then break it again . .<br>\r\n\r\n<br>for tomorrow i will mend it once more.<br>'),(3875,'<P>\r\nDead Kittens and the \"Such is Life\" Scenario.\r\n</P><P>\r\nElizabeth is a 25 year old girl from a small city in Australia. She is well educated and has come to the understanding that a well- rounded education only creates confusion as the more knowledgeable you become, the more you realize you know nothing at all. After a year in an interstate city, taking too many drugs and partying way too hard, Elizabeth finds that she has to fit back into the little city she originated from. She feels very anachronistic and disassociated. She thinks she’s getting dumber and more forgetful. She has a general skittish, giggly, restless demeanor and is in the middle of a momentary midlife crisis.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nElizabeth- \r\n</P><P>\r\n\"Fucking Jesus! I think I’m going crazy. What the fuck have I been doing with my life?!(laughs) I should be a rock star by now! Or have written a book... hell, I could have been an astronaut! Or what ever the fuck I wanted to be. Or maybe I could have even learned to play guitar in the first place... I can’t even remember what I’ve done in the last twenty five years. I know I’ve done heaps of stuff. I had wicked jobs and gone to uni and been a misfit and created a stir and now what? I’m working in an office, staring at a computer screen. I have nothing tangible to show for my little life. Dead memories.\r\n</P><P>\r\n I\'m spending my time hung over or wasted. Wasting my time wasted. Wishing I was anywhere but here. Why am I here? Why am I not somewhere else? Why the fuck did I move to Sydney last year? Man.... What a waste of a year. Spent another year wasted. I never wanted to move to Sydney. I was going to move to Melbourne. The year before I moved to Sydney was the only year in my life I actually knew what I wanted from this fucked up little hole of a planet. I was in love and passionate about creating. Creating is like playing God! (giggles)Opps, I’m going to hell for that statement. And I was going to move to Melbourne and then London and go to Chelsea art school and be famous. I was going to take over the world and people fucking knew it. The stupid things we do for cock. And now I swear I’m loosing it a little. I’m a little all over the place. Fucking disjointed to say the least and I find it so fucking hard to be inspired.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI remember when I was in high school and my teacher in year 10 said,\r\n</P><P>\r\n \"Elizabeth, what do you want to do when you leave school?\"\r\n</P><P>\r\nI shrugged my shoulders and said \"I dunno\" and that was it. She had my life planned. I was going to be this prodigy fashion designer, after I moved to Melbourne of course, \'cause apparently there’s no future in this little town. Then she left the school and that little dream went down the illustrious, ivory, toilette. That was also the last time I saw my dad. And actually the first time I’ve seen him since I was four(sigh).\r\nBut anyway, after she left the school, my English teacher decided I should be an English and art teacher, which I guess I could be. I wouldn’t mind going back to uni. It’s so safe. So precious. I am forced into being inspired. Surrounded by sexy young minds. Young... fucking young.... I went back to Uni a little while ago for a visit and they were... young... not a line on their little faces. I like it when they think I’m younger than I am. I was jealous. My lost youth.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMum wanted me to be a doctor. She knew I didn’t want to grow up.\r\n</P><P>\r\nWhat was that quote I thought was brilliant earlier? It was so romantic, in the Keats way not the Mill’s and Boon way. Ahh... fucken... I didn’t write it down because I thought it was so poignant I’d remember it. Damn it.\r\nI spent a lot of my life dancing. Feeling free. I was told I could be a stripper. Gee, what a compliment. I did belly dancing for a week and got moved into advanced class. It’s all in the hips, baby. It’s amazing how some chicks can’t move there hips separately from their bodies. God, it’s so embarrassing when your in your room really going for it and the music’s so loud you can’t hear when your mum walks in and has a giggle at your enthusiasm for contemporary dance.\r\n</P><P>\r\nThe quote was something like \"in order to feel love we must be willing to feel pain.\" I have felt more pain than love and that simple self indulgent thought makes me feel more guilt than sanity. I live here, in this beautiful city with beautiful people. Why am I not content? I haven’t seen my family killed or starve. Stop being so ungrateful. I’m so fucking sick of self indulgent people.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAhhh! So fucking uninspired. Why aren’t you inspiring me? I’m running around in frustrating circles thinking disjointed thoughts and your not fucking helping. I think I’m loosing it for real this time. Slipping away into permanent psychosis. Maybe I should write a monologue about it? Be inspired by my inability to get inspired. Yeah, type out my thoughts. Hand them over to be manipulated and paraded. Then sit in an audience and watch a girl (or maybe a guy) tell me about myself. Make my insanity known to the world so people can relate to it and wonder if the tale is really based on a true story. Well, sorry folks... \r\nit\'s not.\"\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\n\r\n(Black out)\r\n</P>'),(3884,'test'),(3885,'<PRE>\r\nUnder a blue light bulb\r\ndancing to the drums, \r\nOn the wall night burns\r\nand dawn\\\'s waiting by the door.\r\nShe\\\'s on the floor again,\r\ngoat-like, breathing blue,\r\nbowing to the moon.\r\nKnife to dew-lap,\r\nincantations fall\r\nlike warm rain -\r\nWitches gather burdoch\r\nand butane.\r\n\r\nKnife to dew-lap,\r\nthe door\\\'s ajar...\r\nshe\\\'s screaming for grace\r\non a savage Moor,\r\na sorcerer crouches down in the dark\r\nof a monkey puzzle tree.\r\n</PRE>\r\n'),(3886,'<p>\r\none needs to take special care when sleeping on carpet, especially carpet that is cheap, and poorly maintained to boot. specific care needs to be placed on eliminating all contact surfaces between yourself and said carpet. as if you are not careful you will chafe your entire body into one gigiantic rash and the following day at work will be a living hell.\r\n</p>'),(3887,'<p>i have moved many times in my diminutive years of life.  i have gone from one side of the county to the other and back agen atleast twice.  i have gained and lost friends in many, many states.  i have gained new interests and dislikes.  i have gained knouledge a plenty.  i have made more enemys than i can count and have never backed down to any of them.  with each passing day i wonder \\\"will i still be here in afew years?\\\"<p>\r\n<p>  now i live away from the things i love the most in life.  away from the world i previousely lived in.  the world of swords and sorcery, of sith and jedai, of 50 foot tall robots ducking it out.  but at least i have made peace with what i truly am.  i an a geek, plain and simple.  but now we geek are not as we once were.  now we ar freeless and proud.  we were what we are as a badge of honor.  we truly rule this world for now all have alot of geek in them.  though they may not admit it they do.<p>\r\n<p>  i now must cut these ravings short, but i will returne another day. Fair thee well fellows in this adventure known as life.<p>'),(3888,'A strange thing happened last week. Strange, but good, so I thought I’d share it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFirst, a quick fill in...<br><br>\r\nFour months ago I found a wild kitten, or rather, she found me. I was walking past a disused electrical station when she crawled out from a crack in the fencing, mewed at me and then collapsed. She was almost dead from starvation, her skinny, emaciated body covered in ticks and fleas, one eye glued shut with blood, and a ripped ear. I took her home and called her Wafer (she was wafer thin). The next day, the vet said she probably wouldn’t survive; she was just too far gone.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFour months later and you’d barely recognize her. She’s still small, but incredibly healthy, with fluffy tiger stripes, clear green eyes, and as sharp as a razor. I’d looked after cats, many of them, via the cats protection league. They seemed to think the most damaged cats just \\\'took’ to me. It’s true, they did, but Wafer was even more special; I’d just \\\'taken’ to her too. Even my big old wild-cat, Pecan, took to her, and he generally didn’t like anything.<br><br>\r\nOver time, I’d cautiously reintroduced Wafer to the outside world. I’ve a tiny house and a tiny garden in the town centre – surrounded by other tiny houses with tiny gardens. Wafer loved being outside, but wouldn’t venture further than she could see me. Sometimes she’d go into the neighbors gardens, but she’d always come running back within seconds of me calling her. I would just leave the patio doors open and work while she ran around.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLast week, I was working at home on a new art project; a CD booklet for an indie band. Wafer was doing her usual zooming in and out of the house, climbing the curtains, jumping on me, attacking the PC cables, anything that caused mischief. I kind-of loose myself when I’m doing artwork. <br><br>\r\nSuddenly a barrage of images flashed through my mind... a huge blue car...cars, loads of them... I’m scared and my heart is racing. <br><br>\r\nWafer; I knew something had happened to her. I immediately went outside and called her. Nothing. But she always came within seconds. I waited two minutes and called again. Still nothing. Half an hour...still nothing. I quickly scoured the house, just in case she was curled up somewhere, blissfully asleep, but she wasn’t and I knew she wasn’t anyway.\r\nI fought the waves of panic that keep surging through me and put on my boots, grabbed my keys and left the house. For over an hour I walked the immediate streets, calling for her, but she was nowhere in sight. I went to the building sites around me – anywhere a cat might hide. I even had all the builders scouring the half-finished flats near my house for her.<br><br>\r\nI eventually go back home, knocking on neighbors doors on route, asking them to keep an eye out for her. They were all animal-friendly, so they didn’t mind my asking. \r\nBack at home, calling from the back garden again, looking in neighbors gardens – I was driven by a strange adrenalin that felt quite surreal and detached.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter eight hours had passed I started making a \\\'missing’ flyer. Before I had finished, my mum called. I told her what had happed and how I kept getting image flashes as well as the constant fear, which didn’t feel like mine. Half an hour later she turned up at my house. \r\n\\\"Is she dead?\\\" Mum asked. She didn’t ridicule what I was telling her. She believed me.\r\n\\\"No,\\\" I replied. \\\"But she’s very frightened.\\\" Don’t ask me how I knew that. I just did.\r\n\\\"Then go with it,\\\" mum said, very calmly as she opened the front door.<br><br>\r\nWe went outside and, almost trancelike, I began walking... to the end of my street, down two roads, cross over the busy main street. Down another road past the backs of the shops, bins, more houses. Still trancelike I began walking an area I barely knew. I crossed over another side road. All the time my instincts were pushing me, while my logic argued \\\'but this is too far –a cat would never come this far. But she would have to climb many high walls and cross many roads to get here; she would not be here.\\\"<br><br>\r\nI pushed aside my logic and suddenly stopped walking. \\\"Here,\\\" I said<br><br>\r\n\\\"Call to her,\\\" mum said.<br><br>\r\n\\\"Wafer! Wafer!\\\"<br><br>\r\nI heard a mew and spun around. On a small patch of ground behind a car workshop, a small cat cautiously stuck her head from behind a bush. She was shaking. Two seconds later she had jumped into my arms, shaking and purring at the same time. I carried Wafer home.<br><br>\r\n\r\nRight now, Wafer is curled up on my lap while I type. I cant explain how I did what I did. I’m no psychic. But, I have learnt that our instincts and logic battle one another. Our logic, though useful, can often misguide us. Our instincts are never wrong.\r\n'),(3889,'<p>\r\nOsaka, Japan. Sunday, October 9 - 12, 2005.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo years ago:<br>\r\nWhen I first went to Osaka a couple of years ago on a \\\'Visa Run\\\' I strolled the streets close to my hotel looking for a place to get wasted. Walking up and down the side streets there were many places of interest, but because of all of the unknowns and fashion show type places that surrounded me, I craved a place to just chill and get trashed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt was on one of those side streets that I found the N&G.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe N&G is the kind of place I donâ€™t want people to know about. It is the kind of place someone finds and wants to keep secret. It is the kind of place one feels welcome, and it is the kind of place that contains many \\\'colors\\\'. It was the faint sound of Bob Marley playing through the door that took me there and in that I figure it was destiny.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI cautiously walked in and found myself inside a museum of tequila. The bar was small with only a few stools and even fewer places to sit. Three of the four walls had shelves filled with bottles of tequila imported from anywhere and everywhere. I had walked into an empty tequila bar playing one of my favorite Bob Marley songs (Johnny was) and said hello to the bar owner. After a quick bow, I sat down on one of the stools and asked for a bottle of Corona and a shot of tequila.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe owner, referred to from now on as Haru spoke English and introduced himself to me. He had travelled the world looking for teqilla and could speak English at a level we could both comfortably converse.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI told Haru about my life in Korea as an English teacher, some of my world views, and taste in alcohol and music. He was genuine in his questions which made me respect him almost right from the start. He was a man in his early forties, married with no children. His wife was a fashion designer and had her own brand of casual wear for men and women which from what I was told by Haru is quite popular in Japan.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHaru was a man who liked to relax, and owning his small bar was his way of doing that. His bar was small, frequented by friends, friends of friends, and random people like myself. He took pride in his collection of over 400 different bottles of tequila and his taste in music was also quite inviting.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe got to talking about what I missed about Canada and the topic of erbz obviously came up. Haru was suprised to hear that erbz were something difficult to get and even more dangerous to have in Korea. While still dangerous to have in Japan, erbz were much easier to get and it was at that point that Haru handed me a small pipe and directed me into a small bathroom to partake in some home grown goodness. After that, a few of his friends showed up and it became clear to me that they were there to nurse a beer and also visit the bathroom. Nevertheless, instead of having each one of them go in and out of the toilet, he simply walked over to the front door, locked it up, and got a session going. It was grand. A few of his friends spoke moderate amounts of broken English and I had fun telling them about the situation concerning erbz in Canada with particular attention and respect handed out to Vancouver. (a city I don\\\'t actually like all that much)\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter a few rounds he lit a few incents and opened up for regular business again. It was at that point Dr. K and his daughter came in for a beer and a few shots of tequila. Dr. K was, I think trying to hook me up with his daughter, but none of that was on my mind. Haru chatted with us and his friends and Dr. K and his daughter invited me out for some Sake and Karaoke. Ready for more adventure I accepted and went on my way, thanking Haru and his friends for the good time they had shown me. That was my first experience at the N&G.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOne year ago:<br>\r\nOn my second visit to Osaka for my yearly \\\"Visa run\\\", I was unable to find the N&G again. I searched and searched but just could not remember where it was. I thought it might have just closed down. Nevertheless, I found a live roots reggae sound system performing in a basement bar and had a great time. While there were no erbz involved, the crowd was much larger, and as I was the only foreigner there, my only actions were to order bottle upon bottle of Red Stripe, and nod my head in approval of chewnz I felt worthy of a \\\"Boh!\\\".\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThis year:<br>\r\nI got back to Osaka on October 9th with an insane headache. I had gotten back to Korea on September 22nd with a prescription for Valium, which I had abused and doctor hopped to get more and more. Mixing it with copious amounts of Gin and tonic, other sleeping agents and on my final binge night with lines of opium, the day before my flight to Osaka was spent mostly in a constantly nodding off routine of sleep, wake up, vomit, and then back to sleep. Getting back to Korea on September 22nd filled me with stress as I still had unanswered questions regarding work, little money, and an insane amount of angst and frustration.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUnderstandably, my friends, who I had spent so much more time with before leaving korea for my Canadian visit were busy with other things, so most of my days were spent going for walks off my head on valium and gin into clinics trying to get more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOsaka was what I needed to clear my head and I knew that this time it was my duty and mission in life to once again visit the N&G. I arrived in Osaka on Sunday in the early afternoon. I checked into my hotel, downed a few cans of Asahi courage and pounded the pavement with the N&G\\\'s business card in hand. Pacing up and down side streets I was unable to find it. This is when it dawned on me to ask traffic police how to get there. I found an older gentleman who was more than happy to use sign language and a pen to draw me a map showing me two streets and dots representing traffic lights. I followed my scrawl 5 lights down and then 2 lights left and low and behold I was standing in front of the N&G. It was still early and the door was locked. Across the way were two punk rockers listening to anti-flag and so I said to them â€œsup punks!â€ One of them spoke English well enough to say â€œwhen sun go down, door will openâ€. I laughed and said thanks and handed them both my mini cd. â€œWhat genre?â€ they asked with excitement. â€œCrazyâ€ I said with even more excitement and with a sharpie and receipt from 7-11 I drew a map as I headed back to my hotel for a nap. On the way back to my Hotel, hunger overcame meâ€¦and as I had no idea what in the hell to eat, I looked for the international symbol of ignoranceâ€¦.the golden arches. The place was filled to the gills with Japanese hotties chomping on mc-recipes they do not offer in Canada. I walked up to the cash where an insanely cute Japanese girl started just jibber jabbering to me in Japanese. I looked at her and laughed and then looked down to the picture menu and pointed at what looked to me like an egg mc-muffin. With one finger I said â€œoneâ€ and then she jibber jabbered away for a good ten more seconds and as a feeling of being totally useless overcame me I just said â€œokay okay okayâ€. I made sure to get my order for take out as whenever I encounter McDonalds, its always safe to be near a bathroom you can trust. I paid 500yen (about 5 or 6 dollars) and was on my way. I got back to my hotel room and opened the bag to find not one, but seven fucking burgers waiting for me. â€œChrist!â€ I ate a teriyaki burger and one of the egg things and that was it. I was done. Embarassed by how much I had ordered I left my room and dumped the remaining burgers in the trash by the vending machines. I crashed for a few hours and finally woke up at around 10pm where I spent another good 30 minutes sitting on the john scolding myself for the ultimately poor choice of McDonalds cuisine. Nevertheless the toilet in my hotel room is the coolest thing I have ever had the pleasure of sitting on. When you are done your business, you press a button: Blue for boys, and pink for girls. A nozzle comes out from somewhere and bathes your unmentionables with a nice jet of warm water. You can pressâ€œstopâ€ at anytime you want, but as a virgin of the TOTO WASHLET I just sat and thanked whoever came up with that brilliant invention. Anytime after a mcdonalds episode, I do a few jumping jacks just to make sure that the mc-mongol hordes have left my system. They had notâ€¦and I once again had the pleasure of TOTOing myself.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFinally...It was time to use the map I had made just a few hours prior. After a much less daunting task, I found the place and it was open. I walked in and Haru immediately remembered me. We both smiled and he introduced me to two of his friends who were also there sitting with him. He had a big smile on his face and pulled a big bag of erbz out of his pocket and patted me on the back with it. â€œWelcome back my friendâ€¦it has been long timeâ€. â€œHi Haru, How have you been? I hope you are well.â€ He introduced me to his friends and we sat and drank a couple of coronaâ€™s, some tequila, he sauntered over to the front door and locked it. I handed them all my mini cd, chiamattt.com stickers, and we smoked for a while and he kept the door locked. One of his friends was playing the guitar while his other friend played a strange kind of harmonica that looked like a keyboard with a pipe you blew into with your mouth. Needless to say after the beer, tequila and hittin the pipe hard, I rooked out. Sensory overdrive and a clear case of PMSS.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPost mcdonalds stress syndrome causes an insane urge to use a bathroom. I told Haru and his friends my mcdonalds story and they laughed for a long time, knowing exactly what I meant as they had also been to other countries and encountered similar experiences. Haru motioned me to the bathroom in the corner and while it wasnâ€™t a TOTO, it let me just sit in a nice place, relieve myself and let my blood pressure descend. I sat there for about thirty minutes and let the rooking pass. Haruâ€™s friends played the â€˜girl from ipanemaâ€™ and it was at that point I knew I was alright to go back out into bar. Hitting the pipe again, Haru pulled out a bottle of â€œStetsonâ€ cologne and sprayed the place down. He apologized to me and I said with a smile that I understood and took the bottle and sprayed some on my fingers. He laughed and it was at that point he pulled out a bottle of a very very special kind of tequila. He had put some erbz in a bottle with tequila and had let it sit for ten months. He asked me if I wanted some, but I insisted that he not touch it for ten years. I made up a story about how he could sell it on Ebay in twenty years and retire. We all laughed. I hung out for a couple more hours, and as it was getting late and I didnâ€™t want to over stay my visit I paid my bill and made plans to have dinner with Haru the next day.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOsaka has always been really good to meâ€¦and last night was no exception. With the N&G rediscovered, I hung out with Haru at his bar on the 9th, 10th, and 11th, and we have become quite good friends. With email exchanged, a map in hand, and a desire to visit more often...I hope to see Haru again some time in the near future.\r\n</p>'),(3890,'<p>Doing laundry is loneliness.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If doing laundry feels lonely, then so should doing dishes, but it doesn\\\'t.  Especially if you\\\'re doing them by hand.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Warm water touches your skin.  As the clean dishes pile up (or better still, are arranged neatly to dry on a few layers of paper towels), you see your accomplishment growing.  You can even smell your accomplishment.  Dish soap may somehow evoke painful childhood memories for some of us, but that\\\'s the exception to the rule.  Soap smells clean to most of us, and nothing more.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>And if you sweat a little while doing dishes, you feel your accomplishment oozing out of your pores, and afterwards, drying on your clothes.  It\\\'s not  so unlike leaving it all on the field, and sitting around the locker room afterwards in your sweaty clothes before you shower.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But doing laundry is lonely.  Necessary.  A task that the wise get out of the way.  The wisest among us look to get time on our side.  It\\\'s like having your money work for you, having time on your side.  If you can manage your time well enough to schedule some small, half hour to forty minute task during the wash cycle, then you\\\'ve got a free shot at conquering your wash and your time at once.  Drying cycles take longer, so if you haven\\\'t squandered the time the wash cycle took, you\\\'re in the bonus round.  You may now devote an hour or more to something else while the machines work for you.  Hell, you can even afford to get adventurous, get caught out and about and doing for the rest of the day.  The only penalty for such freespiritedness being the obligation to do a quick fluff before you fold your clothes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And that could be days later.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But even with such benefits to be garnered from time management, the best we can hope for from doing laundry is not losing our day (or days).  A shitty thing to hope for, and a pyrrhic victory if it comes to pass.  If you\\\'re afraid of losing, it\\\'s more likely to happen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe that\\\'s it -- doing laundry feels like a loss, even though you\\\'re getting clean clothes in the bargain.  You\\\'re usually in some sort of basement.  There\\\'s either cement or antiseptic, life-devoid linoleum under your feet.  The offal of doing dishes is crumbs and crud -- both of which could support life.  Say, a mouse.  You don\\\'t want mice, but knowing you could have life around you is better than the utter lack of it.  A shithouse rat and a laundry-room mouse are probably close cousins, and not long for this earth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Our first and (if you\\\'re responsible and conscientious) last acts in doing laundry are cleaning out the lint filter in the dryer.  You may be getting clean clothes, but the deterioration of your possessions you hold in your hand.  That soft purple felt is decay.  The dust in your house is dead skin cells.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>It is said that we all die alone.  Few people do laundry in groups.  Whether you do your laundry in your basement or in the designated laundry area of your apartment complex, the laundry room is one of the most silent on the premises.  No matter the number of people doing their laundry around you, there\\\'s little to no chit-chat.  Laundry is by nature an anti-social pursuit.  Who wants to be around the death and decay if they can avoid it?  Better still, who wants to talk about it?  When you face death, it\\\'s best to do it bravely, but you don\\\'t have to like it or talk about it.  And if you\\\'re not particularly brave when you\\\'re about to die, you can beg and plead and death might just grant you a break.  But your laundry never offers quarter.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>And like death, your laundry will have to happen at some point.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you do your laundry at night this all makes perfect sense.  Cold cement or linoleum and the dark of night can evoke visions of a morgue past midnight.  If you do your laundry during the day, you feel your loss and loneliness even more acutely.  Look up from your work, through the semi-transparent curtains that you\\\'d never hang anywhere else in your house, through the half-sized window that flips up, or perhaps down, to open (if it hasn\\\'t been painted shut), and you see life in motion outside.  A bird, someone passing through the alley on a bike, or kids playing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>These things are not for you.  You\\\'ve got  laundry to do.</p>\r\n'),(3891,'I’m an idiot. A fool. A dumbf**k. Clueless.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI have an IQ of 148, so I’m supposed to be intelligent (ha ha) and people think I’m pretty (aren’t pretty girls supposed to get there own way?) But I know I’m just plain old stupid...<br><br>\r\n\r\nI give people the benefit of the doubt. They let me down.<br>\r\nI don’t tread on toes to get what I want. They tread on mine.<br>\r\nI help when they ask me to. They just ask for help because they’re lazy.<br>\r\nI try to be positive. They zap my energy by constantly moaning.<br>\r\nI work hard for my goals. They take the credit when I get there.<br>\r\nI try. They don’t.<br>\r\nI give. They take.<br>\r\nI’m an idiot.<br><br>\r\n\r\nNice guys finish last and the race was won long ago, yet I’m still running. \r\nIs it possible to have a clean conscience and be happy with oneself, yet not finish last?\r\nYou can tell me, but it probably won\\\'t make any difference; I\\\'m an idiot you see.\r\n'),(3893,'<p>I am afraid of forceps. I don\\\'t know why.</p>\r\n<p>This is something that has plagued me the duration of my otherwise uninteresting life. I say plagued here for dramatic effect; in practice, it is quite easy to avoid forceps. The fear is not restricted to any specific size or shape of forceps, but is open to anything with a pinching-and-grabbing function. This has sometimes included my own hands. In fact, I am afraid of the very fingers typing these words. This may not last, and by the end of the next paragraph, I may be perfectly able to pick up a sandwich and take a bite without fear that I will slip and be strangled.</p>\r\n<p>My mother has told me that she can remember my fear all the way back to when I was yet unable to crawl. She would try to change my diaper, and I would scream and cry until she moved her hands away.</p>\r\n<p>These days, I have trouble in my love life. I have to tie down all my partners. This may sound kinky to you, but to me it\\\'s just for protection. In the heat of passion, who knows what could happen? Hands, fingers, muscles, squeezing, and then I\\\'m lying on the floor, dead. I guess this has provided me with a steady stream of lovers, but they all get bored and want \\\"new kinks.\\\" They don\\\'t understand despite my laborious explanations.</p>\r\n<p>Scissors I do not have a problem with. This shouldn\\\'t surprise you, as scissors are not intended for grabbing. While they can, in theory, grab things, it is difficult and often unsuccessful. Why are scissors not more threatening than forceps? Cutting can be much worse than grabbing. Yet I don\\\'t fear a cut or even an incision, making a vasectomy relatively simple to submit to.</p>'),(3894,'<P>\r\nI just felt the need to share a recent experience I had. I\\\'m employed at local warehouse. Last week it was my turn to clean the sh*tters at night. I received a cryptic phone call from another employee instructing me to \\\"go check out stall number three\\\" in the men\\\'s bathroom. They quickly hung up before I could ask why. I had to check it out though, and was greeted by one of the most amazing things I have ever seen. Words can not do justice to the sight that I beheld. It was the single largest piece of sh*te that I have ever seen in my life. I was not aware that a human being could produce a grogan of such magnitude. It was more than a foot long and close to six inches in diameter. It looked for all the world like a great brown two-liter bottle staring up at me out of the bowl.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMuch of the log was jutting out of the water and it looked like it would almost reach the bowl rim itself. I flushed the toilet a couple of times, but the beast just sat there, unmoved, and appeared to be taunting me. It was clearly not only enormous, but quite hard as well. Comparatively speaking it was definitely more rock than mud in composition. By this time every male employee (and a couple of females too) had been in to gaze in utter amazement at the sheer girth of the great brown trunk of shite. One even\r\nmanaged a picture or two with a disposable camera. I\\\'ve submitted a copy so that others here may share in my bafflemnt at how such a thing could even exist.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nBut, we had a duty to perform and we had to get rid of it somehow. But how? Taking it out of the toilet was not an option as it could be dropped, or God forbid, come into actual physical contact with one of us. One thing was certain though, it was simply too large and rigid to go down in its current form. The first attempt at dislodging the giant thing was a chemical strike in which an entire gallon of liquid plumber was poured into the toilet in an attempt to break the turd up. An hour later this had managed only to\r\ndecrease the girth of the poo by an inch or so, and it was still in one solid, massive piece. In the end, myself and another intrepid employee managed to break it into smaller chunks with a stick (a long one). Retching and gagging all the while, we at last managed to flush the beast. We were all strangely dissappointed, however, in ruining what was obviously a work of great effort on someone\\\'s part, and a true natural wonder and testament to the human anus\\\' ability to expand. This thing would have given the most slack-anused man cause to strain and break into a sweat. I find myself wondering just what kind of poor soul is cursed to take such monstrous dumps.\r\n</P><P>\r\n\r\nI know it would suck if I had to break my sh*te up with a stick every time I sh*tted just to get it to flush. A lot of folks may wonder, \\\"why is he going on and on about a fucking piece of sh*te?\\\". If you had seen the SIZE of that godammed sh*te you would no longer wonder. While utterly disgusting, the sheer mass of the great grogan was hard to get our minds around, and as such, was quite fascinating. This is certainly something special. I am actually disappointed by the picture\\\'s inability to convey the gigantitude of the crap. Keep in mind it\\\'s a big commercial type toilet, and that the entire bottom of the bowl is covered, and that the sh*te is clearly much wider than the drain hole of the toilet.\r\n</P>'),(3895,'<P>thirty-five years old and has to sit at the kiddie table.  it is a<BR><BR><B>god.<BR>damned.<BR>shame.</B><BR><BR>it wasn\\\'t so bad, though.  my sister sat out there with us.  conversation centered around the domesticated turkey, flight, lack of (<I>then why do they have wings?</I>); <I>harry potter</I> (which two of the children are not allowed to see); <I>narnia</I> (which they are); and christmas.<BR><BR>and then somebody brought up the dead baby.  we had to talk all about him.  what he was doing at the moment.  chloe (three) opined that he was having thanksgiving, too.  <I>how?  he doesn\\\'t have any teeth!</I>  round and round.<BR><BR>and then my sister said something to me, and i responded with the smart-ass <I>i love it when a plan comes together.</I>  we cast around for a second trying to determine the source of this adage and then realized that, duh, mister george peppard\\\'s character hannibal used to say it all the time on <I>the a-team</I>.  i tried to explain about mr. t.  a physical description, and then an impression.  all the younger ones thought it was hilarious, but my niece, already oh so cool at age nine, just looked at me like i was crazy.<BR><BR>and then the door to the dining room opened, and my cousin\\\'s stupendously hot wife stepped out into the solarium to see what was so funny.  (i tell you that it is a solarium, but if i use that word around them, they tease me.  to them, it is a sun porch.)  her son explained, and she wanted me to do it again.  i was embarrassed, didn\\\'t want to.  she looked at me kind of saucily and said, <I>come on.  just one time.</I>  i established strong eye contact and repeated the impression, all the while trying to psychically convey the fact that, despite the shirt and tie, i wasn\\\'t wearing any underwear.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>baby, i know all about chris, size, lack of.  you should come to where the flavor is.  the deconstructed life.  forsake that big ass house up in inverness.  vote democrat.  but if you don\\\'t mind, you can leave these two little hellions up there.</I><BR><BR>and then later on, i told cameron that i pitied the fool that don\\\'t clean his plate, and he shot me a bird.  can you believe that?  five years old.</P>'),(3896,'<P><I>we do it all for fun you know<BR>get loaded after the show<BR>it\\\'s all part of the scene<BR>don\\\'t hate us, we\\\'re just kids<BR>people say we\\\'re behind the times<BR>go ahead and see if I care<BR>do you remember<BR>when you were our age?<BR>do you remember?</I><BR><BR>i was reminded by somebody\\\'s lj the other day that:<BR><BR><B>Hüsker Dü rocks my fucking ass.</B><BR><BR>the first time i heard them was the saturday night after thanksgiving, 1985 (see: <A HREF=\\\"http://www.lyrics007.com/David%20Bowie%20Lyrics/1984%20Lyrics.html\\\">beware the savage jive of</A>).  peanut\\\'s older brother darryl was home from college and brought <I>zen arcade</I> to a party we were having at jimmy duncan\\\'s house.  he threw it on around eight-thirty.  we were all stoned to the bone and well beered-up.  i was like, \\\"what the fuck is this?\\\"  it was more than i\\\'d bargained for that night, way more than i expected.  it was like going to a church picnic and having the president show up.<BR><BR>i asked darryl if i could take it home and tape it and get it back to him the next day.<BR><BR>\\\"five bucks.\\\"<BR><BR>i said, \\\"sold.\\\"<BR><BR>i fished a long abraham out of my wallet, and he popped the ninety-minute cassette out of the stereo and handed it to me.  i slipped it into one of the pockets of my letterman\\\'s jacket.  (football, wrestling, baseball.  purple and gold.  hernando high.  this was before we knew purple was the color of <I>el maricon</I>.  i don\\\'t know how the boys manage these days.  god knows it would take an act of god and an act of congress both to change the school colors.)<BR><BR>darryl said, \\\"i\\\'m leavin\\\' to go back to hogtown at three o\\\'clock.  don\\\'t make me come lookin\\\' for you.\\\"<BR><BR><lj-cut text=\\\"continued\\\">the music invigorated us, infected us with something.  mr. lagedrost (aka fagedrost), an effete (albeit married) math teacher at our school who lived next door to the duncans, was hosting some species of soirée around his pool that evening.  from jim\\\'s backyard, all we could see of it was the tiki torches burning above the board-on-board fence.  all we could hear were jimmy buffett\\\'s greatest hits to date and the occaisonal shriek of drunken laughter.<BR><BR>around ten-thirty, jim strolled out of the house with an instamatic camera and walked over to the fence and held the camera up above it and took a picture.  the flash seemed strikingly bright.  a few seconds later, the head of lagedrost\\\'s jail matron of a wife popped up over the fenceline.<BR><BR>\\\"stop that.\\\"<BR><BR>jim extended unto her The Finger.  she stared at him a second then disappeared.<BR><BR>two minutes later, he took another snap.  soon, lagedrost came trotting around the corner of the enclosure.  jim strode up to him.  jim probably outweighed him by a good thirty pounds.<BR><BR>jim said, \\\"you better get your ass back in that house.\\\"<BR><BR>they eyed each other, then laggy said, \\\"alright\\\" and turned and headed back the way he came.  over his shoulder, he said, \\\"i\\\'ll let the police deal with you hellions.\\\"<BR><BR>jim said, \\\"suck a hellion\\\'s dick.\\\"<BR><BR>that put the kibosh on things for the night.  just about everybody left.  darryl, with his big collegiate legal expertise, said, \\\"ya\\\'ll don\\\'t hafta go.  we\\\'ll just all get in here \\\'n turn the lights off \\\'n pretend like nobody\\\'s home.  they can\\\'t bust the door down.  they\\\'ll just leave after a little while.\\\"<BR><BR>my friend bert said, \\\"darryl, man, you must be high.  what about the fifteen, twenty cars out in the driveway?\\\"<BR><BR>darryl said, \\\"forget ya\\\'ll.  i\\\'m goin\\\' to the hilltop.\\\"<BR><BR>jim said, \\\"<U>you</U> can\\\'t get into the hilltop.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you come up there \\\'n watch me.\\\"<BR><BR>and he was gone.  along with just about everybody else.  only three or four cars were left out there.<BR><BR>as it turned out, those of us who stayed followed his advice to the letter.  jim turned all the lights out and locked the place up, and we all went into the upstairs bedroom in the guesthouse and smoked and waited for the cops to get there.  we saw two of them walking around the house, shining their flashlights in the windows.  laggy came into the backyard where they were and talked to them for a minute.  fifteen minutes after they left, we did the same.  on our way out, jim noticed that the passenger side window of laggy\\\'s mercedes coupe was down far enough to accomodate a pee-hole, so he left a souvenir of the evening.<BR><BR>good times.<BR><BR>and don\\\'t even get me started telling you about the first time i heard <I>metal box</I>.</P>'),(3897,'I was a student representative in high school and went to this Youth Leadership seminar where one kid from every school in Cali met for a weekend at Loyola Marymount. They told us we were the future and how we should be, think and act. I don\\\'t think I absorbed anything. They separated us by boys and girls as far as the sleeping quarters were concerned, but one night I managed to get to the floor that had the girls on it. I spent the night with a girl that I had gone to Catholic school with when I was 5. She was now attending an all girls private school. Freak a leak. It was fun and after the weekend I went home, we didn\\\'t talk after that though. I couldn’t even remember her name.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThen one day I get a call out of the blue and a voice on the other line tells me she is the girl I met at the seminars, I thought it was the same girl. It had to be, I didn\\\'t give my number to anyone else. When I went to pick her up, I knocked on the door and it was opened by a heavy breathing fat girl that looked like she would have to side step to get through the door. I never would have forgotten her had we met. I later found out that when we were at the seminar, packets were handed out that had contact numbers for every person in attendance. Had I not thrown my packet on the floor upon walking off campus I would have known that. She had called me and I had mistaken her for the wrong girl. <br><br>\r\n\r\nSo there I was standing at her door holding back the look of utter shock. I knew right away that something was horribly wrong. At the time I had two over weight siblings and didn\\\'t want to do to her what I would have hated to have happened to my siblings, so I hugged her and said \\\"well, let’s get going!\\\" I walked her to my car; I opened my door and got in. Instead of unlocking her door, I sat in the driver\\\'s seat staring forward for about 5 seconds. I started the car. I stared for another couple seconds as she awkwardly stood outside the locked passenger’s side door, completely blocking any sunlight from entering the car from the right side. I contemplated the affects of both leaving her right then and there or of having a good night with her and then breaking her heart by not calling her the following day. <br><br>\r\n\r\nSo I did what I knew I had to. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nI dated her secretly for four years without any of my friends knowing. She gave a mean blow job. She eventually broke up with me because I was \\\"too needy\\\".'),(3898,'<pre>i won\\\'t make up my mind\r\nin order to fit in\r\ni won\\\'t just follow them\r\nand say that i have sinned\r\n\r\ni will not simply be\r\na voice lost in the crowd\r\ni\\\'d rather sit alone\r\nand scream my fears aloud\r\n\r\ndon\\\'t show me what to think\r\ndon\\\'t tell me what to see\r\nfor better or for worse\r\ni will just be me</pre>'),(3899,'there is no escape. what do i do? how do i get out of the hole i dug for myself?  how does i escape something that i created? i can\\\'t. its not possible. \r\n\r\ni need to learn to be happier with my prior decisions. quit being such a fucking baby, jesus christ. grow the fuck up and just handle your problems like a man.\r\n\r\nat the same time i think i need to be a man and get myself out of this fucking situation, be free of all the angst, the battles, oh the battles. and the letting myself down, over and over. jesus christ there are times at which i wish i invented a rocket-mother-fucking-ship so that i could get the fuck out of this hellhole and leave it all behind. at least then i would have a lot of money so i could get laser eyes, and a cool car.\r\n\r\nthere is no escape. there is no escape.\r\n\r\ni am emo, i know. i have come to the conclusion that i need professional help, either in the form of a dr. or in the form of changing my life, changing it all. getting rid of it all.\r\n\r\ni have become the thing i hate, and i stay up at night thinking about it constantly, there is no escape, i am driving myself crazy... what am i saying, i am already way beyond crazy, i don\\\'t know what i am going to do to myself, i want to go, go away, and never come back. at least then i could re-invent myself without all of this insanity.\r\n\r\nbut i wont, because i am a mother-fucking-king-of-all cowards, i get stomped on constantly. i let anyone tell me what to do, even myself. the part of me that thinks that i will be alright, that tells me \\\"just stick it out you pussy it will all be alright\\\" even thought it won\\\'t. i am a coward.\r\n\r\neven if i started over without all the crazyness, i would invent it - create the drama for myself. make my life more complicated that it actually is so that i feel important, smart, wanted. \r\n\r\nwhy do i do this to myself? it is torture. i can\\\'t stand it. i hate you all, especially you, mike joyce.\r\n\r\nfuck off and die.'),(3900,'there is no escape. what do i do? how do i get out of the hole i dug for myself?  how does i escape something that i created? i can\\\'t. its not possible. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni need to learn to be happier with my prior decisions. quit being such a fucking baby, jesus christ. grow the fuck up and just handle your problems like a man.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat the same time i think i need to be a man and get myself out of this fucking situation, be free of all the angst, the battles, oh the battles. and the letting myself down, over and over. jesus christ there are times at which i wish i invented a rocket-mother-fucking-ship so that i could get the fuck out of this hellhole and leave it all behind. at least then i would have 