 this hellhole and leave it all behind. at least then i would have a lot of money so i could get laser eyes, and a cool car.\r\n\r\nthere is no escape. there is no escape.\r\n\r\ni am emo, i know. i have come to the conclusion that i need professional help, either in the form of a dr. or in the form of changing my life, changing it all. getting rid of it all.\r\n\r\ni have become the thing i hate, and i stay up at night thinking about it constantly, there is no escape, i am driving myself crazy... what am i saying, i am already way beyond crazy, i don\\\'t know what i am going to do to myself, i want to go, go away, and never come back. at least then i could re-invent myself without all of this insanity.\r\n\r\nbut i wont, because i am a mother-fucking-king-of-all cowards, i get stomped on constantly. i let anyone tell me what to do, even myself. the part of me that thinks that i will be alright, that tells me \\\"just stick it out you pussy it will all be alright\\\" even thought it won\\\'t. i am a coward.\r\n\r\neven if i started over without all the crazyness, i would invent it - create the drama for myself. make my life more complicated that it actually is so that i feel important, smart, wanted. \r\n\r\nwhy do i do this to myself? it is torture. i can\\\'t stand it. i hate you all, especially you, mike joyce.\r\n\r\nfuck off and die.'),(3902,'Lowe\\\'s Home Improvement and Fry\\\'s Electronics are almost identical.  They may sell different sorts of merchandise, but that\\\'s where the differences end.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLast night, compelled by my desire to make my partner happier, I decided to make a stop at Fry\\\'s on the way home from lab. I wanted to purchase a new wireless router, since the one we have is flaky at best.  I knew the brand, model, and expected price.  Linksys, WRT54GL, $69.95.  The person I was buying it for was with me and did not object to the pit stop.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt was awful. People everywhere, not knowing why they were there.  Near the digital cameras, a baby threw it\\\'s toy on the floor. The father, dizzied by the array of gadgets and confused by his also confused wife, didn\\\'t notice, so I gave it back to the baby.  It seemed happy.  No widescreen LCD monitors at reasonable prices.  A close-enough router (WRT54G) for the same price: I decided to bite the bullet and Have Faith.  The batteries on the digital cameras were all dead.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAlready drained, we headed for the the checkout line.  It was long and awful. I didn\\\'t want to deal with it. My partner sensed my agony and suggested we just leave, so we did. I even put the router back on the shelf I got it from, hoping to spare someone else a bit of headache.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter leaving the store, I felt drained.  My eyes slowly readjusted to the incandescent car interior, a healthy 5400 K.  The store must have been damn near 2000 K. I was agitated, grumpy, and now I was hungry too.  Blech.  Thankfully I discovered the parking lot behind Ranchos, and was fed vegan chocolate chip cookies on the way home.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s the same thing when I go to Lowe\\\'s for some bit of hardware. I know exactly what I want.  It is a simple metal thing for holding a gate closed.  Another time it is a round dowel on which to hang clothes.  Neither time is it obvious where I should be.  Lowe\\\'s is also 2000 K inside, which is aggravated by the fact that you have to spend a lot of time walking around and getting opinions on where you might find your desired Thing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLowe\\\'s has a few basic sections: Things That Synthesize Sugar From Carbon Dioxide, Things That Can Be Cobbled Into A Bathroom, Light Fixtures, Bulk Wood, Paint, and Carpet. None of those seem like they would help you keep a gate shut or hang clothes.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAt least Lowe\\\'s has an antithesis that can be relied on: Ace Hardware on University in Hillcrest. Never stressful. Always helpful. Always well stocked. Close to home. Why do I feel compelled to sometimes go elsewhere? Is it the lure of the possibility of Anything?<br><br>'),(3903,'<table width=\\\"500\\\" align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<tr>\r\n<td>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo weeks have passed since my return to Chicago, and I\\\'ve yet to unpack.\r\nThe tent that I used might still travelling: back across the country, to its owner in San Diego.\r\nBut my bike\\\'s beloved saddlebags, no longer in equipoise, slouch mournfully on the desk behind me - Ziplocs filled with the flotsam of a journey\\\'s rude end still swelling each black, nylon vessel.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/tuba_city.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"sunset near the grand canyon\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSitting at the kitchen table, in the same state as my gear, I feel the force of a phantom crosswind pressing upon my head; \r\nmy muscles can\\\'t quite adjust to the diminished demands made upon them.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe cargo will find its way back where it belongs, in time.  Unpacking the meaning of the experience is proving to be a more difficult task.  I\\\'m left with a collection of sensations that I can\\\'t seem to weave into a fitting narrative, no matter the words or images that I choose to employ.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI can still hear the echo of Billy Joe\\\'s song about September.  His voice woke me as it fell down from the ceiling of the Apache casino lobby.  I rode too far in the Rockies, the second day in November; at night, between snowflakes and elk, I passed through Chama to Dulce.  And I lived because the native people took me in - their gaming establishment.\r\nIt was strange to have woken with poker and dice, to the sound Green Day and the smell of bacon.\r\nBut, I did.  And that was the trip.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/dulce_01.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"land of the jicarilla apache\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn west-central Texas, between Brady and Eden, I was caught in a terrible storm.\r\nThe rain was driven sideways; I was blown across a two-lane highway.\r\nBut there was no oncoming traffic.  Luck?\r\nCrawling on the shoulder at 20 m.p.h. till the blackest clouds fell into the background, the progress was slow.\r\nThen an oasis of a rest area appeared, and the sun broke through.\r\nThe lingering wind quickly dried my clothes - once stripped from my body.\r\nPerched on an old picnic table, virtually nude, in weather a bit too cool, surviving; that was the trip.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSomewhere in the mountains of Arizona, while drinking a free cup of coffee, I heard Buddy Martin tell the story of his family\\\'s arrival to the land near Prescott, in 1863; that was the trip.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/navajo_01.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"in the navajo nation\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nMaterializing in San Diego, clueless, and then finding Austin\\\'s key; that was the trip.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBehind me, to the East, lay the ugly cities of men.\r\nAround me, grey on grey, steel stamps were pasted on a portland sky.\r\nBut ahead of me, in my mind as much as before my eyes, the horizon widened and the land opened.\r\nMaking forward progress while mounted atop my tall bike, equine as Chiron, it was not so surprising that a good many horses ran to their fences\\\' girdle to meet me.\r\nIt is right to move with four limbs down, and head up, into the future tense.\r\nWe knew the goodness of the motion in which we participated, affirmed by the wind\\\'s embrace.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/paul_g_01.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"paul g - southern new mexico\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI promised myself that I would look for what was beautiful.\r\nAnd the camera, as well, was to be prevented from focusing on tragic subjects.\r\nI had the chariot and death; it was time to find stars.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFor that reason, it always seemed best to avoid the interstate highway system.\r\nCoast to coast the big pipe of the big road brought the big brands to big the people in their big cars.\r\nAnd if a few trees, and a few rabbits, and a few armadillio, and a few coyote, and a few deer, and a few special places, and a few mom \\\'n pop stores, \r\nand a few people too - sure why not - happened to get flattened in the process, well, the important thing was that it happened quickly.\r\nBecause if you can\\\'t move along at 85 m.p.h., everywhere, and all of the time, then everything just plain sucks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt makes me wonder what was wrong with a place like Bodes: In rural New Mexico, in Abiquiu, \r\nit was still possible to purchase a cast iron frying pan and a Betty Paige post card along with your fuel.  \r\nWhat sort of Communist would oppose an all-American institution like that general store?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTravelling on a lightweight motorcycle I was forced (further) outside our society\\\'s Bubble of Immediacy.\r\nThe bike set the trip\\\'s parameters: \r\nOperating a \\\"primitive\\\" device like the KLR650, you had to stop frequently; \r\nyou were forced to put money in the local economy; \r\nyou couldn\\\'t help but interact with people;\r\nyou felt the most subtle changes in the air.\r\nOh the horror!\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/austin_02.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"2222 inbound, austin, tx\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEven so, swarming about me were the tractor-trailers, trucks and full-size sport utility vehicles: massive drones bound for the hive, \r\nhiding anything remotely human beneath their giant glass and steel exoskeletons.\r\nMost of the \\\"people\\\" on the road were insulated from the environment in which we existed - together.\r\nAnd existing together was really getting to be a problem.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThere is an unacknowledged carnage in the violent wake of American progress.\r\nWhen you\\\'re out there alone - figuratively or literally - and that wake blows you off the road, you can\\\'t help but wonder about the cost of it all.\r\nIt didn\\\'t need to be this way.\r\nWhy should we not have a transportation system designed for more subtle vehicles?\r\nCanoes, cycles of all sorts, and other gentle craft parted and closed the fabric of being in a more delicate way.\r\nYes, I have some understanding of the economic and military forces that, because of an interest in transporting large volumes of material from (A) to (B), quickly, caused the interstate highway system to be.\r\nBut I think it\\\'s all wrong: wasteful and cruel.\r\nAnd that\\\'s the seed of the anguish.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n+ + +\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the way down, I decide to turn a stretch without protection.\r\nA billowing sail of an unbuttoning shirt forms around my chest.\r\nSpilling out, my father\\\'s dog tag skates, flashing silver on the black paint.\r\nCool, hungry tongues of wind leap against my skin and lick erotic.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOn the way back, through the last last five states that I pass, there\\\'s not another motorcycle on the road.\r\nIt\\\'s 37 degrees when I stop in LaFayette, Louisiana.  And I know that I\\\'m in trouble.\r\nBy the time I hit central Illinois, it\\\'s dipped down to 14.\r\n\\\"Ya know we\\\'re talkin\\\' about you on the radio,\\\" said the stranger at the rest area.\r\nHe and the other OTR truckers had a pool going.\r\nNo one collected.\r\nBut, even with two pairs of gloves and two plastic bags on each of my hands, my thumbs are nipped by frostbite.\r\nThe pain is so intense that it takes me 12 hours to travel the last 200 miles.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI am happy in these times.\r\nWho has understanding?\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n+ + +\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI tried to write the story before I left.  \r\nI tried to explain where I was going, and why.\r\nAnd yet the trip, and the meaning of it, wasn\\\'t available in text.\r\nThat is, I suppose, as it should be.\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"center\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://germz.org/KLR650/rideamerica/painted_desert.jpg\\\"  width=\\\"400\\\" height=\\\"300\\\" border=\\\"1\\\" alt=\\\"a good place for lunch\\\">\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRestless and consuming, I had no more thought than did fire.\r\nRolling over the prairie pumping thunder into the ground,\r\nthe only worry in my head was the Cowboy\\\'s Prayer: \\\"Oh bury me not...\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe BOOM-BOOM-BOOM of the single knocked sense senseless,\r\nand pensive - pissless.  That was the point of the thing.\r\nFey and reckless, plummeting full-gate, chest to tank,\r\nwheels turning, my Celtic heart sang to the unseen.\r\nI lived to ride it out, there, in the space in between.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<hr>\r\n</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>'),(3904,'<P>a little mexican restaurant crap store in the desert:</P>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">\r\n<P>austin in a chinese restaurant.</P>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img2%%\\\">\r\n<P>austin in baja turning 30.</P>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img3%%\\\">\r\n'),(3910,'<p>you\\\'re a live wire in my bathtub<br>\r\nmakes my senses all go<br>\r\nBOOM<br>\r\nas you shake your little beauty<br>\r\nall across my little room</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you\\\'re the monster in my closet<br>\r\nin the middle of the day -<br>\r\n\\\'cause there\\\'s sunshine on the curtains<br>\r\ndoesn\\\'t mean you go away</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you\\\'re the picture in my daydreams<br>\r\nmakes my hand move up my thigh<br>\r\n(which my nibbled lip would tell you<br>\r\nif you\\\'d let me catch your eye)</p>'),(3906,'<P><img src=\\\"%%file1%%\\\"></P>\r\n<p>she smells like the rain</p>\r\n<p>in a warmer place than here</p>\r\n<p>and plays the baseball</p>\r\n'),(3907,'<img src=\\\"http://i31.photobucket.com/albums/c360/six7nine/theriver.jpg\\\"><BR><BR>off the bus at the intersection of main street and the coastal highway.  to the north lie the mall, the movie theatre, my sister\\\'s place, weeki wachee eventually.  (i read that supergrass shot a video there not too long ago.  not familiar with them.  any supergrass people out there?)<BR><BR>to the south:  tarpon springs, all the hot young greek boys getting ready to dive for the cross on epiphany; and then clearwater, the elrons, cages in the basement of an old hotel.<BR><BR>pick up scripts at the walgreen\\\'s on the southeast corner of the intersection; head east.  (it looks like main street\\\'s fucking the river in this picture.)<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i31.photobucket.com/albums/c360/six7nine/thepark.jpg\\\"><BR><BR>cross the bridge, turn left into the park, behind the hacienda, cross bank street, cut through the top of the gloria swanson memorial parking lot, over to the lake.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i31.photobucket.com/albums/c360/six7nine/lakeonleft.jpg\\\"><BR><BR>around the lake, right on central between first baptist and their parking lot, three blocks to madison, take a left, go up a block.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i31.photobucket.com/albums/c360/six7nine/homestead.jpg\\\"><BR><BR>that\\\'s me on the northwest corner, nursing home across the street to the east, apartment buildings to the south.<BR><BR>look at those lucky people behind me with a pool.  i had no idea it was there.  i suspect i\\\'m gonna be sneaking over there in the dead of night sometime or other this summer.'),(3905,'i chant to them. they are colors and foods.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhite loaf<br>\r\norange loaf<br>\r\nla gata blanca<br>\r\ngato... orancita?<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe orange loaf.<br>\r\nthe orange one.<br>\r\nthe small orange one.<br>\r\nsmall.<br>\r\nv. small. <br>\r\nvee dot small.<br><br>\r\n\r\nyoko the pants <br>\r\nsqueaker<br><br>\r\n\r\nkittenbutt: your butt is made of kitten.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfranklin licks my nose thoroughly any chance he gets. he will actually insert his rough kitten tongue into my nostril. i alternately love and hate this. <br><br>\r\n\r\nyorca of the floor seas and franklin with butt made of kitten, they are friends.<br><br>\r\n\r\naustin swinney has never met franklin percival delano buckminster jonez but i think they would hit it off.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe end.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3909,'dear small midwestern city,<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am new here. you may have seen me around: i\\\'m the asian girl. you know, the one whose ancestors didn\\\'t come from scandinavia OR germany, the one who keeps asking for strange foods no one has ever heard of.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m writing, city, because i want to be friends. you seem lovely; i especially like your lakes and the ready availability of beef jerky. you have good coffee and good people, and i\\\'m even starting to like cheese curds.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsince i\\\'ve committed to a dissertation, i may very well end up dying here. so, city, in the hopes of a long and fulfilling relationship: please ask your people to stop staring at me. yes, i\\\'ve noticed the looks at the co-op and the dog park, the library and the bar. especially at the polka bar, where i admit i do not really fit in. i simply don\\\'t know what to say when they ask me where i\\\'m from, because they never seem to believe it when i say \\\"virginia\\\". you know, the other day the man at the store complimented me on the fluency of my english. city! you realize this has to stop. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t mean to be demanding, city. i realize there are not many of me around here, but it is starting to make me feel uncomfortable. if this doesn\\\'t stop, i\\\'m just going to have to move back to new york.<br><br>\r\n\r\nrespectfully,<br>\r\nmay'),(3911,'<P>\r\nOnce, there was a girl born with a heart so full of joy that her feet could never touch the ground.  She spent her early childhood skimming over the world, bouncing easily on a thick cushion of happiness and laughing heartily at everything she would see: a flower, bending in the breeze, the roll of a kicked pebble, the butcher, trimming the afternoon steaks.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nWhen she went off to school, however, the other children began to poke cruel fun at their floating classmate.  Her father (the town blacksmith) fashioned her a pair of iron shoes to help her to better fit in amongst her landlocked peers.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nUnfortunately, when the girl began to walk instead of float, the joy surged up through her lungs.  The girl began to sing -- all day and night, humming when she couldn\\\'t think of the words to fit.  She sang about breakfast, her teacher\\\'s dress, the funeral director\\\'s horse, the well-bucket, the way the boys would kick each other on the schoolyard.  The children at school covered their ears and wailed. \r\n</P><P>\r\nFinally, the teacher put a giant cork in the little girl\\\'s mouth, fastened on her head with a pink silk ribbon.  Her parents, exasperated, did not remove it.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nPlugged up and grounded, the girl began to draw.  She drew, fervently, all the joyful things that she saw in the world around her.  First, the girl used every sheet of paper in the house and emptied all the inkwells.  Then she commandeered her mother’s lipstick and painted all of the windows with ferris-wheels and tigers and tall men in kilts.  When the teacher turned her back at recess, the little girl covered the back wall of the schoolhouse with penny-candy, riding crops, golf shoes, and birds, scrawled in rich, red mud.  Infuriated, the teacher tied the little girl\\\'s hands behind her back.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nSo then, unreleased joy screaming though her bloodstream at thousands of miles an hour, the little girl exploded.\r\n</P><P>\r\nTwo days later, the town forgot all about her.  God has hated the world ever since.\r\n</P>'),(3912,'<p>i asked dictionary.com the meaning of \\\"kiefing screen\\\", and it said,<BR><BR><B><TT>Did you mean <U>Janus green</U>?</TT></B><BR><BR>and i laughed because Janice Green was the woman who stood in the parking lot in high school and made sure underclassmen didn\\\'t sneak out to lunch.  she wore a wig.<BR><BR>and didn\\\'t do her job very well.  at the start of our junior year, we all got fake passes from Kelly Brewer, who worked in the office.  the first day of our <U>senior</U> year, come the noon hour, we hauled it out to dana\\\'s omega, i think, and tooled it into the line going past her.<BR><BR>she said, \\\"what the hell y\\\'all doin\\\' here?\\\"<BR><BR>and bert said, \\\"we all failed.  gotta repeat senior year.  ain\\\'t that a bitch?\\\"<BR><BR>she just shook her head.  we never heard anything else about it.<BR><BR>and then later one night years later, we were hanging out in the starvin marvin parking lot, slobbery drunk, when she and some guy come out of the store, and she says, \\\"y\\\'all better get the hell outta here.  they in there callin\\\' the man on ya.\\\"<BR><BR>and then maybe four, five years after that, i found out firsthand that she\\\'d been working at the jail awhile.  it was pretty funny the night they brought me in, and there she was in a uniform behind the counter.<BR><BR>but, yeah, i would like to get a box with a kiefing screen.  that would be sweet.</P>'),(3913,'New York City is a city of spirit, heart and hard to decide for travel, even in the face of disaster. Home to such riches as the Statue of Liberty, 5th Avenue boutiques; the famous Broadway stages and the breathtaking Met, the Empire State Building, Central Park.\r\nNew York is one of the best tourist places in the world and in USA as well. Being the most excellent tourist spot the city New York has top rated hotels in USA providing range of facilities & various type of hotels,  well-located, the   finest amenities & services that make you feel at home away from home. <br><br>\r\nIn most of the cases specially while traveling to major cities & tourist places & attractions, the hotels close to New York City and near to any major & crowed attraction, the more money you can expect to pay to the hotel.  Here you have the option to choose a low cost hotel in New York.  One of the best option to save money is the stay with the hotel little far from the city like not in any posh area and try if you can manage with 2-3 star rating hotel in New York city. This will just save money for you. \r\nAs far as the shopping areas are concern a number of shopping centers you can find around any hotel you choose to stay in New York, but here again you can look for the cheap & better shopping places little far from the hotel. <br><br>\r\nWe should always try to save money in all protocol while traveling to any city in any country and because sometime New York goes very expensive in seasons so this would be a good idea to choose the better place to travel with discounted cost if possible.  Instead of paying more money to guide or travel agent try to use the direct services from the hotel like book your hotel online and try to contact local person for more information, to find out the best shopping area, attractions and all you want. Stay in a comfortable hotel with low cost and for shopping you can choose some less crowed region, for travel you can select the city bus for New York city travel, and off course try to keep your money safe although the crime rate is not high in New York but still when you are saving money this is very important to keep this safe for future travel or for next visit to New York City.\r\n<br><br>\r\nNew York Hotels New York is an online hotel booking center for New York Hotels where you can find the available hotels in the city and can book directly as per your travel dates. You can save big money here if booking online. Its very simple to search and Book. Don’t pay additional money to your local or new york travel agent, but book directly and save up to 50% money.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhttp://www.new-york-hotels-newyork.com'),(3914,'<hr>\r\n<h3>\r\nI\\\'m In\r\n</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nIn the window.\r\nThrough the door.\r\nSt-t-tarting...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nALIVE!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nFingers on the left hand spin the wheel clockwise.\r\nRight palm over ball slaps stickstickstick in reverse.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe 911\\\'s pedals jut up from the floor.\r\nStick-stomp-click-crash.\r\nRed plastic flashes like ruby slippers on the ground.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOut, and:\r\n1, 2, 3, 4...screaming.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe asphalt slips slick, spinning.  A turn.\r\nFlashing green, yellow, up the ramp - air.\r\nFalling.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBack.\r\nFaster.\r\nEngine, heart.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGod-I-fucking-did-it-and-I-can\\\'t-stop-flying.\r\nI\\\'m alive, I\\\'m really alive.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCrash.\r\n</p>\r\n<h3>\r\nSally\\\'s\r\n</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nKnock-knock.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRight palm falls down to the small of her back:\r\nside-to-side moving, heat, building.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nEyes locked.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nLeft hand: thigh, climbing - her toes turning outward.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nShe hunches down; we\\\'re still standing.\r\nSoaked.\r\nYes?\r\nYes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIt\\\'s a delicate path lightly traced through the silk.\r\nParting.\r\nYes?\r\nYes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nGod-with-every-kiss-on-your-neck-I-inhale-you-and-only-then-can-I-breathe...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs it rolls down, Valentine\\\'s cherry fabric sticky-shimmers on milky skin.\r\nBathed in warm-yellow 40W light, the pin-up girl poster watches.\r\nYes?\r\nYes.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI need to love you.  I can\\\'t be here if it\\\'s stealing.\r\n</p>\r\n<h3>\r\nTuesday\r\n</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nNight: wine, vodka, cough syrup mixed in a cup.  \r\nDirty bed; no shower.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA man who\\\'s overfed waits impatiently for his food.\r\nA young, wealthy couple complains about the service.\r\nTraffic builds.\r\nUseless shit is bought and sold.\r\nAnd various animals are led to slaughter.\r\n</p>\r\n<hr>'),(3915,'<P>so many books!  friday morning, my sister drove me up to a store near her place, and i sold the guy two hundred assorted paperbacks.  mysteries, thrillers, sci-fi, romance, westerns.  fifty bucks.  bought them for a dime apiece at the library.  plus seven percent tax.<BR><BR>i offered to split morgan off ten bucks, but she told me to keep it.  home.  voicemail from tom, the addictions therapist, wanting to know if i was coming to group.  i usually don\\\'t, but i called, and he said they\\\'d gotten me a cake already.  well, shit.  can\\\'t turn down cake.<BR><BR>maria was there.  two months after tom started bringing her and her baby to our twice-monthly luncheon dates, i figured out that he was trying to set us up.  i\\\'m slick like that.  not much gets by me.<BR><BR>(another example of this:  about three weeks ago, after visiting this site for almost five years, it occurred to me that simon might be a woman.  that remarks in e-mail like \\\"my lack of maleness\\\" didn\\\'t necessarily mean that he felt like a woman trapped in a man\\\'s body but just might refer to actual plumbing specs.  don\\\'t laugh.)<BR><BR>and then one day up at the case management office, maria slipped me her number, jotted it on a piece of yellow notepad she got from the receptionist, who evidently got it from the seraquel rep.  heart over the i in maria.<BR><BR>friday, she sat by me in the van and at the beach.  wanted to know why i\\\'d never called.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>well, mostly, it\\\'s because you like to cut on yourself.  it kind of freaks me out.</I>  borderline ain\\\'t anything to fuck with.<BR><BR>i halfway felt bad about it and promised i\\\'d call sometime.  she seemed satisfied with that.  i stopped going out to lunch with tom right after hurrican wilma, whenever that was, and didn\\\'t anticipate seeing her again for a long while.<BR><BR>we saw a dolphin just offshore.  when i got home, i put on some eddie cochran and smoked my last bowl and a half of \\\'dro and looked up dolphins in various dream dictionaries.  they evidently represent <I>willingness and ability to explore and navigate through your emotions; friendliness, communal living, rescue, communication, affection, spiritual guidance, intelligence, trust; positive messages from our unconscious minds; the mental attributes and accomplishments involved in the dream itself; a line of communication between the conscious and unconscious aspects of yourself; a pat on the dreamer\\\'s back; a very good omen.</I><BR><BR>at three, i called connor\\\'s cell.  told him how much i wanted.  he said he and justin would be over in awhile.  half an hour later, i was playing with myself, perusing pictures of a guy named andrew aponick, who kind of looks like a monkey but is pretty hot nevertheless.  a knock at the door.<BR><BR>they knocked twice more before i could get dressed and answer it.  there was a new guy with them.  glasses.<BR><BR>connor said, \\\"were you asleep?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"nah, i was on the bowl.\\\"<BR><BR>justin said, \\\"be careful.  that\\\'s how elvis died.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i\\\'m always careful.\\\"<BR><BR>connor turned fourteen last month.  i thought he was older than that when i first saw him, seventeen or eightteen, at least.  he\\\'s got floppy blonde hair and usually wears a  watch cap.  i have to be careful how i wear hats like that, or i look like a keebler elf.  he\\\'s got these huge red lips.  about the second time i got high with them, somehow the conversation came around to his mom, and i said, \\\"is your mom hot, connor?\\\"<BR><BR>and justin said, \\\"she\\\'s aight.  got some tig ol\\\' bitties.\\\"<BR><BR>justin\\\'s eightteen.  lives across the street with his mom and two younger sisters.  he\\\'s got a.d.h.d. and bugged-out eyes, usually.  reminds me of the weasel in the foghorn leghorn cartoons.<BR><BR>the other boy was named daniel.  fifteen.  he knew connor somehow.  i never got the whole story.<BR><BR>i got them sat down and said, \\\"alright, fuckboys:  who wants some ice cream?\\\"  it\\\'s a little ritual we have.  almost always chocolate.<BR><BR>just as connor started breaking up some weed, somebody prank called him.  he put it on speakerphone.  the caller had howard stern soundbites on their computer, and after connor\\\'d say something, they\\\'d respond with one that could be considered remotely appropriate.<BR><BR>justin said, \\\"give me that\\\" and took the phone from connor\\\'s knee and started cursing into it.  howard said, \\\"so, you got any poontang over there?\\\"<BR><BR>connor chuckled and said, \\\"yeah, we got plenty \\\'a poontang.  we got daniel.  we got matt.\\\"  he looked at me and laughed.  i laughed back.<BR><BR>i thought, <I>little boy, you just don\\\'t know.</I><BR><BR>after awhile, the caller switched to <I>napoleon dynamite</I> clips, all of them voiced by the title character.  we smoked a couple of bowls, and they split.<BR><BR>it was a little after four.  i surveyed my options for the evening.  the weed had made me vaguely hornier.  i hadn\\\'t come in almost four weeks.<BR><BR>i thought about her tight little body.  at the beach, she\\\'d had on shorts and a short-sleeved shirt with a low-cut neck that showcased the cleavage.  come to think of it, i\\\'d never noticed any scars.  surely, there had to be scars, right?  the other b.p.d. woman, michelle, had keloids all over her arms and face.<BR><BR>maria\\\'s lips.  her face.  she had looked at her roots in a compact and said, \\\"i need to paint my hair.\\\"<BR><BR>she asked me to guess her age not long ago, and i said twenty-four, and she said, <i>oh, my god, i love you.  i have twenty-seven.</i><BR><BR>it took about fifteen minutes to find the piece of yellow paper.  i invited them over for dinner.  she said, <I>no, the baby\\\'s sick.  i\\\'m not taking him out.  why don\\\'t you bring everything here and cook it here?</I><BR><BR>i got high at six then headed over there.  it was foggy out.  the lamps lining main street wore fuzzy halos of yellowy-orange light, and there was very little traffic for a friday evening.  east on main to congress, down to the alley past the synagogue, to the back of a little bungalow that was probably white in the daytime but coral-colored in the wash of the sodium nightlight.<BR><BR>there were a lot of oak trees in the back yard, along with a wooden picnic table with some high grass under it and an empty kiddie pool up under the eaves.  i made sure my eyes were open enough and knocked on the door.<BR><BR>vincent was in his playpen.  he\\\'ll be a year old towards the end of the month.  i got him out and sat on the couch with him.  he wasn\\\'t in the mood for any bullshit.  feverish-looking eyes, coughing.<BR><BR>i eased off one of his socks and did \\\"this little piggy\\\".  in puerto rico, they say <I>rosbif</I>.  he\\\'d watch me messing with his toes, then look at my mouth widening around the vowels, then back at his foot, and then at my face.  totally deadpan with rheumy brown eyes, thinking, <I>this man has obviously lost his fucking mind.</I>  i got a weak little laugh with \\\"wee, wee, wee, all the way home\\\" and the ending tickle, though.<BR><BR>i used the bathroom and was pleased to discover that she had a shower massager.<BR><BR>it didn\\\'t take long.  she put vincent down after we ate, around eight-thirty.  we watched a little tv, fucked, finishing in the shower.  her scars are on her forearms.  very faint.  you have to look for them.<BR><BR>later, in bed, she was touching me, my chest, my abs, my arms, my face.  she said, <I>you know, i always thought the sexiest part of a man was his jaw.<BR><BR>-  his what?<BR>-  jaw.<BR>-  \\\"jaw\\\".  what\\\'s \\\"jaw\\\"?<BR>-  what i\\\'m touching.<BR>-  oh.  jaw.  yeah.  you aren\\\'t the first woman i\\\'ve heard say that.</I><BR><BR>vincent woke up in the night, and i offered to go check on him.  she said, <I>put some pants on.<BR><BR>-  why?  do you think it\\\'ll scare him?<BR>-  it might.</I><BR><BR>i went in there and picked him up and said, <I>ah, little snotty one, don\\\'t cry.</I>  he didn\\\'t need to be changed.  i took him back into maria\\\'s bedroom.  she gave him some kind of medicine from a dropper and laid him down between us.  he was asleep within fifteen minutes, and she took him back to his crib.</P>'),(3916,'<P>\r\nOver the years, I have heard quite a few tall tales about the man known only as herf.  First of all, I want to start by saying that I don\\\'t think that is his real name.  Yet, that is the only name I have ever heard him referred to by.  Also, that I have never met herf.  Furthermore, that this story is sanctioned by him.\r\n</P>\r\n<FONT SIZE=2>\r\n<TT>\r\naustin> yo herfz, long time never seen \r\n<BR>\r\naustin> somebody told me once that you enjoyed running red lights for fun\r\n<BR>\r\nherf[02:38]> haha\r\n<BR>\r\naustin> it is one of the many tales surrounding you\r\n<BR>\r\nherf[02:38]> that\\\'s so not true\r\n<BR>\r\naustin> i could write a short story about you just of the hearsay\r\n<BR>\r\nherf[02:38]> do it!\r\n</tt>\r\n</FONT>\r\n<P>\r\nI came to San Francisco first, some time in 2000.  I met E there at a party in, maybe, 2001.  I\\\'m not sure.  She told me stories of herf.  Well, not really stories.  Three liners.  But it was always the same three lines on multiple occasions.  It went something like this:\r\n</P><BLOCKQUOTE><I>\r\n\\\"I like herf.  but every time I visit him, I end up doing coke.  I go over there, and it is just sitting out on the table [like a candy dish].  I end up doing the coke, and then talking my head off for hours.  Then I feel like a dumbass and leave.\\\"\r\n</I></BLOCKQUOTE ><P>\r\nBeing no stranger to sorting bolts, I brushed this tale off.  Maybe the man likes to party?  Don\\\'t we all here and there.  Still, it seemed odd that with enough regularity, she had come to expect there to be coke on the table whenever she went over to herf\\\'s house.\r\n\r\n</P><P>\r\nAt one point someone told me that herf had unimaginable riches.  He had been involved in some internet cash pyramid scheme.  I imagine that he had bags of cash next to his coke.  He was like a coke dealer who gave the coke away for free because he already had your cash.\r\n</P><P>\r\nSomeone told me once, and I don\\\'t remember who, that herf liked to drive through San Francisco late at night, coked up, with a car full of people.  He would race up and down the San Francisco hills in his Audi S4 not stopping at stop lights.  Racing through stop lights like a suicidal maniac, laughing all the while.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOne time, after becoming rich like the Count of Monte Cristo, herf went to a meeting to discuss possibly funding a startup.  He was a venture capitalist by now.  They did coke all throughout the meeting.  Everyone was very excited about the possibilities of success.\r\n</P><P>\r\nYou know I love Bushmills.  When I can\\\'t afford good scotch, I hit the shit hard.  I watch Miller\\\'s Crossing and drink Bushmills when they drink.  It tastes like apple juice to me.  Well, herf likes Bushmills, too.  One night he got drunk on Bushmills with this girl.  Who cares who she was.  Anyway, they got drunk on Bushmills--mmm--and she was on the rag.  They had \\\"all kinds of raunchy drunk sex.\\\"  In the morning, they awoke covered in the carnage of love.  herf reported (to whoever told me this story) that his face was bloody.\r\n</P><P>\r\nHerf really does well with the ladies.  The lady who told me this didn\\\'t know why.  \r\n</P><P>\r\nAnother friend told me once that herf had a girlfriend--we will call her A.  They were dating and he and A had friction surrounding herf\\\'s use of coke.  I know what you are thinking, the woman wanted herf to clean up.  She wanted her man to clean up...  When coke was around, herf couldn\\\'t stop doing it until it was all gone.  He would \\\'herf it up\\\' until there was no more.  The real problem was that she would buy coke for a party, or just to do a little here and there, and then he would snort up all the nose candy himself.  Angrily, she started hiding the coke.  But herf, he would tear the house apart until he found it.  So the friend telling me this, upon hearing the problem, suggested they get a safe to put the coke in.  They lived happily ever after.\r\n</P>\r\n'),(3917,'Hi\r\nAs in your guestbook to include support html?'),(3918,'Hi\r\nI can not find coordinates for a feedback.'),(3919,'Hi! Prompt, how to me to you to get?'),(3920,''),(3921,'asdfafsd'),(3922,''),(3923,'<p>i had a bunch of stems and seeds and a gram, maybe, of high mids.  (i don\\\'t know how much in buds, really.  enough for two good-sized bowls, anyway.)  i figured i\\\'d cook something and eat half of it for dinner and the rest at lunch tomorrow.<BR><BR>prep:  chopped half a white onion and a green pepper, crushed four cloves of garlic.  cleaned enough spinach to render a handful.  sliced a can of black olives.  opened a jar of sun-dried tomatoes and poured the oil into the big copper-bottomed saucepan.  cut the tomatoes into little strips.  they smell like raisins.  i like the oil-packed ones rather than the dehydrated ones that cost half as much.<BR><BR>(the grocery store i go to really isn\\\'t ashamed to put an arbitrarily high price on their luxury items, either.  they\\\'re like:  <I>you want these?  $4.99, bitch.  now take your simple ass to the register.</I>)<BR><BR>cleaned the rest of the weed.  burner on low medium.  you don\\\'t want to scorch it, as that will destroy the thc.  seeds and stems into the oil.  it\\\'s slightly rust-colored from the tomatoes, but slowly takes on a greenish tinge.  saute them for fifteen minutes.  strain the trash out.  bud in the oil.  fifteen more minutes.  add some oregano, cumin seed, and a bay leaf.  cook for two or three minutes.<BR><BR>by this time, the apartment was full of smoke.  it looked like a casino.  turned the a/c on and hoped it wouldn\\\'t carry out to the street.<BR><BR>saute onion, garlic, green pepper for seven or eight minutes.  add spinach, tomatoes, olives, salt, pepper, and a little pesto sauce, sugar, lemon juice, balsamic vinegar, and a couple tablespoons of non pareille capers.  cook it down for fifteen minutes.<BR><BR>make the pasta.  (if you want more protein, you could add cannellini or walnuts.  toast the walnuts for two or three minutes, if you do.)  top with grated parmesan, if desired.<BR><BR>finished eating at 8:20.  half an hour later, i can feel it creeping up.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>postscript:  didn\\\'t use enough weed.  will try 3 or 4 grams next time.  nothing more frustrating than a half-assed buzz.</p>'),(3924,'<p>i had a bunch of stems and seeds and a gram, maybe, of high mids.  (i don\\\'t know how much in buds, really.  enough for two good-sized bowls, anyway.)  i figured i\\\'d cook something and eat half of it for dinner and the rest at lunch tomorrow.<BR><BR>prep:  chopped half a white onion and a green pepper, crushed four cloves of garlic.  cleaned enough spinach to render a handful.  sliced a can of black olives.  opened a jar of sun-dried tomatoes and poured the oil into the big copper-bottomed saucepan.  cut the tomatoes into little strips.  they smell like raisins.  i like the oil-packed ones rather than the dehydrated ones that cost half as much.<BR><BR>(the grocery store i go to really isn\\\'t ashamed to put an arbitrarily high price on their luxury items, either.  they\\\'re like:  <I>you want these?  $4.99, bitch.  now take your simple ass to the register.</I>)<BR><BR>cleaned the rest of the weed.  burner on low medium.  you don\\\'t want to scorch it, as that will destroy the thc.  seeds and stems into the oil.  it\\\'s slightly rust-colored from the tomatoes, but slowly takes on a greenish tinge.  saute them for fifteen minutes.  strain the trash out.  bud in the oil.  fifteen more minutes.  add some oregano, cumin seed, and a bay leaf.  cook for two or three minutes.<BR><BR>by this time, the apartment was full of smoke.  it looked like a casino.  turned the a/c on and hoped it wouldn\\\'t carry out to the street.<BR><BR>saute onion, garlic, green pepper for seven or eight minutes.  add spinach, tomatoes, olives, salt, pepper, and a little pesto sauce, sugar, lemon juice, balsamic vinegar, and a couple tablespoons of non pareille capers.  cook it down for fifteen minutes.<BR><BR>make the pasta.  (if you want more protein, you could add cannellini or walnuts.  toast the walnuts for two or three minutes, if you do.)  top with grated parmesan, if desired.<BR><BR>finished eating at 8:20.  half an hour later, i can feel it creeping up.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>postscript:  didn\\\'t use enough weed.  will try 3 or 4 grams next time.  nothing more frustrating than a half-assed buzz.</p>'),(3925,'<p>\r\nHe is not content with who he is and does not know where he wants to go or what he wants to do. He has let his mind, body, and soul disintegrate and is sometimes ready to die. The difference between twenty-nine and eighty don’t seem so different to him. His heavy heart swings low and he dreams of better days, better situations, and better lots in life. He has conversations with himself involving characters and different lifestyles. He speaks to himself as though he were reading the script of a movie; reading down the page each characters voice the same. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe is not content with who he is and does not know where he wants to go or what he wants to do. He thinks about the same woman everyday and can’t get her out of his thoughts. He dreams of her being there and wakes up to the reality that she is gone. He might as well be eighty. He might as well be a widow. He might as well be done.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe feels sorry for himself and wants people to feel sorry for him. It is the kind of attention he has become used to. What he really wants is someone to look up to him and support him on things that he wants to do, though he rarely works hard enough to attain that. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHis life is a series of works in progress and nothing ever gets one hundred percent done. There is something to be learned from that…. he knows, but for him it is easier to just take pills and drink from dusty bottles purchased at the local variety store, and despite being an idealist and sometimes philosophical thinker, he cannot live the life he advises people to live. He cannot incorporate his overtly perceived wisdom into the everyday life he lives or the actions he commits.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe has convinced so many people that he is smart but inside he does not agree with them. He does not think he is stupid, but he certainly does not think he is smart. He does not think he is ugly either, but he certainly does not think he is handsome; even though taxi drivers and old women working in restaurants say he is.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe looks at the people around him and does not see the negatives that they live with. Perhaps it is because they hide them so well and perhaps it is because he only looks for the positives and then compares those positives with his negatives and ultimately loses. He wants to change locations again but knows that by doing so he will just be leaving problems behind. No matter where he goes, those problems will eventually catch up to him. He has left his problems behind on a number of occasions and no matter how far away from them he runs they always seem to be there just waiting to blitz their way back into his life. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe is not content with who he is and does not know where he wants to go or what he wants to do. He is exhausted. He cannot find energy. He cannot find happiness because he has not allowed himself to be happy. He has not cleared his mind of all those precepts and expectations that cloud and layer all that is good and wonderful about him. He cannot let go of the past and cannot look forward. He lives his life drink to drink, pill to pill, unintended/intended hospital trip to unintended/intended hospital trip. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe is lonely because he looks lonely. He is sad because he looks sad. He is alone because he looks alone. He is fat because he feels fat. He is not content with who he is because he is not content with himself. He knows all this and yet he cannot even muster up enough excitement to change, to clean up, grab life, and shake it up. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe needs a drink.\r\n</p>'),(3926,' 	\r\n<p>The first week, we were strangers to the city, viewing it with new eyes.<br>\r\nThe second week, familiarity seeps through the experience.<br>\r\nThe third week, we returned from the countryside, used to the streets.<br>\r\nThe fourth week, we begin to know the lie of the land, the feel of the city.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Travelling allows us to try on different lives – could we live in this city, getting our groceries from there, navigating public transport to work each day?  What would it be like, is it always this way?  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We had to return home - changed and yet not so different.  Perspective would give way to thought, empty days will become full of the mundane and routine.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>As we near our destination the view is a patchwork landscape with its trees and whorls of land swirling into the sea. The azure ocean below us is dotted with tiny islands and majestic Rangitoto stands in the distance.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>A glint of the city sparkles against the arches of the roads into our hometown.  As the plane lowers and swings around there is the Sky Tower in the distance, proud in the haze of the afternoon sun.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We arrive home to summer – humid air hugging our shoulders and caressing our faces. Tena koutou, tena koutou, tena koutou katoa – haere mai ki Tamaki Makaurau.  \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>We are safe in the knowledge that New Zealand will always be waiting when we venture forth again.\r\n</p>'),(3927,''),(3929,'<p>\r\nHe doesn\\\'t know what he wants, and he\\\'s not sure, but he thinks that she doesn\\\'t <em>really</em> know either. What she wants i mean. They\\\'ve always been like a pair of magents. As many times as they\\\'ve been torn apart they\\\'ve always ended up back together. Tight. Inseperable. Until the next time. But magnetism, like so many thing is in a way intangible. Unseen, but for it\\\'s effects. Unheard. You can\\\'t taste it, or smell it, yet at the same time you\\\'d be a fool to deny its existence. It\\\'s just one of those things. Right?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nCause he\\\'s always loved her. From the very beginning, and if not before, he loved her far too much. And she loved him back in her own way, and for her own reasons. She loved him from the very moment their eyes met that frosty morning on the roundabout. But they were much younger then, both in years and experience. So maybe it <em>was</em> love at first sight, or maybe it was simple naiveity, but whatever the foundation, what grew upon it was real and it was undeniable.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nYou see, way back then he didn\\\'t <em>know</em> what he was doing. He was just following his heart; but he quickly found himself doing this he\\\'d never imagined, and these things scared and excited him in equal measure. He took on her child whom he loved as his own, and helped raise him the best way he knew how. This he did without thinking or forethought, and surprises himself with what he has become.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn real life though, fairytales don\\\'t always have a happy ending. In real life people make mistakes that can\\\'t be undone. Bridges are burned and never rebuilt. But magnetism is a mystical and powerful force. He doesn\\\'t know what he wants, and he\\\'s not sure, but he thinks that she doesn\\\'t <em>really</em> know either.\r\n</p>'),(3928,''),(3930,'So there I was, driving across the California desert at about 90 miles per hour on my motorbike. I was feeling sick to my stomach after a long couple of days of on the sauce, and those bad eggs I had in Nevada were starting to act up. I had hot and cold flashes, cramps, and I was dizzy. I was about fifty miles from the closest toilet, it was 115 degrees, and that’s when it started to happen. The farts. Not normal farts, but hot wet slimy farts that smell like dead animals. The cramps in my belly were so bad that every time I farted I thought I was gonna burst. Then they got worse and as I farted a little bit of sludge would creep out of my ass. Instead of pulling over and dealing with the situation, I just kept driving faster and faster.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nAt about 110 mph it happened. The fart that ended it all. This fart was nuclear powered and it wasn’t alone. As the noise came, so did the pudding. I started shitting myself while the worst cramps of my life made me unable to fight it. The shit kept coming and filled my every nook and cranny leaving me sitting in a puddle of warm shit.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nAs it slowed down, the smell reached my nose and was so horrible, it immediately caused me to evacuate my stomach from the other direction. I started puking with a force that I don’t think I’ll ever match again, right into my helmet. This violent action caused me to lose control of my bike and I went launching out into the desert.\r\n\r\nWhen I finally came to a halt, about fifty yards from the Interstate, I bailed from the bike and finished puking out the side. In my weakened state, I evaluated the situation. I had pants full of warm caca, and a lap full of puke. On the bright side, I was still conscious. I looked at my odometer, and realized I was only 25 miles from the nearest toilet that was at a little oasis rest stop in El Centro, California. My delirium told me to drive on.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nAnd drive I did.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nTen minutes later I pulled in. There is a family having a picnic underneath a group of palm trees, and I can’t wait. I bail off the bike and immediately the shit starts pouring down my legs, and the puke splashes to the pavement. A little girl cries out \\\"daddy, what’s wrong with that man?\\\" The father covers her eyes as I throw up again and start heaving hard.<br><br>\r\n\r\nA single tear ran down my cheek.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nI grabbed the one extra pair of pants I happen to have stowed away and limp to the bathroom, wounded. No door, no stalls, no toilet paper. Just a stainless steel sink and a steel piss tub.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nI stripped naked and threw my clothes out. I started washing my shit-filled orifices as a man and his son walk in.<br><Br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Dear God!\\\" the man yelled as I washed my testicles.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I was finally done, I walked back to my bike, and regained my senses. The families were all gone, and as I looked in my ride, I was amazed to see that not a single drop of poo or puke had made it onto my beautiful bike. Not a drop.<br><Br>\r\n\r\nThat’s when I thought to myself, what a great story this is going to make...'),(3931,'<p> Today, we buried Dorothy. Today I ate salami, bologna, provolone and cheddar, potato salad, pasta salad, Wise (tm) Onion Rings, an Eggo waffle, coca cola, vodka and cranberry juice, some cheap red wine. I started eating 30 minutes after I took my synthroid at 8 and didn\\\'t stop eating until now, when I take time to write some things down while I finish another drink.</p>\r\n<p> My father has now been married four times. Number 1 was Mildred; I didn\\\'t know her very well but I remembered her bouffant, which she still wore in the 80s when I met her, and whose frosted edges were still impressed on my memory when I chose her name as my first chat nickname. (it was a coincidence; i actually wanted to be Mildred Pierce). Number 2 was Arraya, that\\\'s my mother, she has high cheekbones and laughs really hard and long. Number 3 was \\\"Mary\\\", the cultist, the born-again Christian, the psycho. I\\\'ll say no more.</p> \r\n<p>My father could never be single for very long. When Ardith, the wealthy feminist, refused his advances toward marriage, he cut it off and started working the personals. I recall editing his blurb, finessing it to emphasize his sense of humor and his threshold for a party. And lo, Dorothy answered, in a miniskirt - \\\"God bless america her legs went all the way up to her ass\\\" - cuddling  with him on the deck of the pool on the plastic lawn furniture, with a spritzer in hand.</p>\r\n<p>She was, in his late 50s, finally the Perfect Match. Her voice was twice as scratchy, her liquor tolerance just as high, her aptitude for rivalrous and mutually deprecating office-person humor 3 times as big, her knowledge of tax law and accounting far superior. I realized when I met her that my father could only respect a woman who would out-bookkeep him, someone who not only could stand the minutiae of earthly personal maintenance but who relished it. This was <i>it</i>, finally, when they were both possibly too old to really take advantage of true love. They cackled together, drank together, travelled together, smoked together. My father finally had a companion, someone he could not bully who likewise never bullied him, who shared in everything as a pal and partner.</p>\r\n<p>Dorothy has since died, and I keep trying to find a quick and perfect way to eulogize her for the posterity of my own memory. to fix some image of her in my mind, so that she won\\\'t be lost, so that I\\\'ve filed her properly. Being an aetheist, i am only assured of a successful death if I may assign the departed a properly edifying or poetic role in my consciousness. I still haven\\\'t made sense of the fact that a person you saw speaking and joking and smoking, in an instant does none of those things, thinks nothing, exists no more. She was once there, and now she is not there; but yet I remember her voice as though I have just spoken with her. As yoko Ono writes, and I\\\'m of course paraphrasing because google fails me, \\\"when you burn a chair in the world, how do you burn that chair in your mind?\\\" </p>\r\n<p>My father sits on the couch with Bob West, his old buddy from the air force, woh looks like Don Knotts and once woke me up playing Reveille on the bugle when we slept at his house. They have the Military Channel going in the background. Bob West is kind of a dick. He tells stories of the \\\"bra-burners\\\" he knew in college who veered \\\"to the right\\\" when they had kids and got married. I make sure to point out that I worship Satan and therefore it is a practice of religious freedom that I am a Liberal.</p>\r\n<p>The Japanese film <i>After Life</i> witnesses interviews conducted with freshly dead people who must choose one memory to bring to heaven with them. Conversely, or perhaps, complementarily, I begin to craft my One Memory of Dorothy here on earth.</p>\r\n<p>About ten years ago, I had a slumber New Year\\\'s Eve party at my father\\\'s house in Dracut, MA, the sort of 70s dream house that had decked out its basement with a built-in wooden bar and stone fireplace, where they had their Super Bowl parties and where I had my first french kiss. My prep school friends drove over from Cambridge and Brookline and Vermont to converge on my dank sub-suburban basement, smoke Coke cigarrettes, get wasted, take polaroids and holler. Shortly after the stroke of midnight, I felt self-conscious at the noise we were making, sure my father would disdain the drugs we were taking, and crept up the stairs to apologize to them.</p>\r\n<p>My house was built split-level, which means you can climb halfway up the stairs and peer through a banister to see what was going on in the living room before anyone realized you were there. I crept up halfway and poked my nose between the iron posts, and saw my father and Dorothy, slow-dancing cheek to cheek in the dimly-lit room. Dorothy, a die-hard Elvis fan, had cued up \\\"I can\\\'t help falling in love with you\\\", and they circled the floor a-rhythmically, half-singing in each other\\\'s ears. Both their eyes were closed and both mouths wore lazy smiles. They didn\\\'t hear us. I laughed and ran back to tell the joke.</p>\r\n<p>Now I hope to always remember her this way, as the lady who made my father slow-dance, my father who always poked his finger in the air yellin about how he \\\"never understood that whole Elvis thing\\\", my father the misogynist, my father the career military man. When he repeats hopefully that she is now \\\"in a better place\\\", I imagine her hovering above the clouds in her housedress, his hand on her waist, her gray curls billowing. The clouds are of course outfitted with the crappy wooden speakers and 1978 turntable in Dracut\\\'s living room. She clasps his hand and bumps her face against his, always nearly asleep but staying up to sing along just until the end.</p>'),(3932,'I cannot recall a single moment that isn\\\'t worth mentioning negatively. It is in me to be negative. It is in me to question everything I do and wonder why on earth I would do it. I am unhappy with it, but that is, in my opinion, what I want the most. I look for the negative aspects and pick them apart and understand them as such. I question them and he questions them. He is my worst enemy. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI am to everyone but myself. In but a very selfish way. <br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd that will be the end of me. And that will be the end of him. <br><br>\r\n\r\nNo face anymore. <br><br>\r\n\r\nA wealth of knowledge with no conceivable use and yet there it is. <br><br>\r\n\r\nA new journey is upon him...upon me..upon us. free at last. <br><br>\r\n\r\nthey were much obliged...those old ones...and I thank you. <br><br>'),(3933,'Ok, so I\\\'m leaving for lunch about 12:45ish and I walk out of the building and I see my boss out front. I say \\\"Hi, Bossman\\\". He say hello and asks if I am heading out to lunch. \\\"Yep\\\" I reply. He says his cab isn\\\'t here and could I give him a lift to SFO. \\\"Sure thing\\\" I unsuspectingly say.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFlash back to last night....<br><br>\r\n\r\nI go out to meet a friend to watch the Olympics. We meet up and he\\\'s got this most delicious herbal refreshment. So we catch said buzz in said car and back in the bar we go. When its time to leave, he gives me some that hes broken off and we part ways. I get into my car and realize how bad it smells. I also ditch said herbal refreshment into the center console where it stays.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m leaving for work this morning and I get into my car and BAM! The smell hits my like jackhammer. I laugh and proceed to my gopher hole you could call an office.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo back to the parking lot....We\\\'re walking over to my car and I\\\'m not even thinking about it. We\\\'re chatting him going away for a long weekend. I press the keyless entry and he opens the back door to put his bag in the back seat and I hop in the front. My face turns to horror as the engine starts and the bossman jumps in the front seat. The door shuts and my boss gets this look on his face and then lets out a long high pitched \\\"woooooooooooo weeeeeeeee\\\" while waving his hand in the air.<br><br>\r\n\r\nits about this time that I start pondering where I should look for work and is my resume up to date.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Smells like you hit a skunk\\\" Bossorama says while still sniffing the air.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI knew that was coming, I thought to myself. Not being able to think of anything clever to say, I shrug and keep the subject on his upcoming long weekend. \\\"Is the wife going with you\\\" I weakly say as my heart flutters a mile a minute and I try to figure the absolute fastest route to SFO from 16th street. This is going to be one long ride Im thinking.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"No, she can\\\'t make it\\\" say Bossman....and goes on to say something about her having a conference and hes going to see his brother. The chat goes on for a few more minutes and then silence. I\\\'m about 15 mins still away from the airport at this point when I hear \\\"So, Griece....\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd I just know its coming. I\\\'m fired. Gone. Canned. Kaput. Firedtown..population me. At least I get off early today, Im thinking. Fuck it.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Are you gonna catch me a buzz or what?\\\" That ladies and gentlemen is when the cartoon \\\"Wha-Wha-what!?!? Happened. He laughed and repeated the statement.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen he opened the car door at SFO to get out..he was laughing so hard he was crying and I was over in the passenger seat laughing. But I didnt know why I was laughing. It was more nervous laugher because after we smoked he just got the giggles and starting laughing.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWiping tears and still half laughing he snorts \\\"Have a nice weekend, Griece.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Thanks! You too!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen he waves turns to head to the airport, stops and wheels around. Looks at me and says \\\"I\\\'ll be you thought I was going to fire your ass didnt you?\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nThat man is now 30,000 feet up having a drink and still laughing his ass off.\r\n'),(3934,'{\\rtf1\\ansi\\ansicpg1252\\uc1\\deff0\\stshfdbch0\\stshfloch0\\stshfhich0\\stshfbi0\\deflang1033\\deflangfe1033{\\fonttbl{\\f0\\froman\\fcharset0\\fprq2{\\*\\panose 02020603050405020304}Times New Roman;}{\\f37\\froman\\fcharset238\\fprq2 Times New Roman CE;}\r\n{\\f38\\froman\\fcharset204\\fprq2 Times New Roman Cyr;}{\\f40\\froman\\fcharset161\\fprq2 Times New Roman Greek;}{\\f41\\froman\\fcharset162\\fprq2 Times New Roman Tur;}{\\f42\\froman\\fcharset177\\fprq2 Times New Roman (Hebrew);}\r\n{\\f43\\froman\\fcharset178\\fprq2 Times New Roman (Arabic);}{\\f44\\froman\\fcharset186\\fprq2 Times New Roman Baltic;}{\\f45\\froman\\fcharset163\\fprq2 Times New Roman (Vietnamese);}}{\\colortbl;\\red0\\green0\\blue0;\\red0\\green0\\blue255;\\red0\\green255\\blue255;\r\n\\red0\\green255\\blue0;\\red255\\green0\\blue255;\\red255\\green0\\blue0;\\red255\\green255\\blue0;\\red255\\green255\\blue255;\\red0\\green0\\blue128;\\red0\\green128\\blue128;\\red0\\green128\\blue0;\\red128\\green0\\blue128;\\red128\\green0\\blue0;\\red128\\green128\\blue0;\r\n\\red128\\gre