\jexpand\\viewkind1\\viewscale100\\pgbrdrhead\\pgbrdrfoot\\splytwnine\\ftnlytwnine\\htmautsp\\nolnhtadjtbl\\useltbaln\\alntblind\\lytcalctblwd\\lyttblrtgr\\lnbrkrule\\nobrkwrptbl\\snaptogridincell\\allowfieldendsel\\wrppunct\r\n\\asianbrkrule\\rsidroot4463112\\newtblstyruls\\nogrowautofit \\fet0\\sectd \\linex0\\endnhere\\sectlinegrid360\\sectdefaultcl\\sftnbj {\\*\\pnseclvl1\\pnucrm\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxta .}}{\\*\\pnseclvl2\\pnucltr\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxta .}}\r\n{\\*\\pnseclvl3\\pndec\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxta .}}{\\*\\pnseclvl4\\pnlcltr\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxta )}}{\\*\\pnseclvl5\\pndec\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxtb (}{\\pntxta )}}{\\*\\pnseclvl6\\pnlcltr\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxtb (}\r\n{\\pntxta )}}{\\*\\pnseclvl7\\pnlcrm\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxtb (}{\\pntxta )}}{\\*\\pnseclvl8\\pnlcltr\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxtb (}{\\pntxta )}}{\\*\\pnseclvl9\\pnlcrm\\pnstart1\\pnindent720\\pnhang {\\pntxtb (}{\\pntxta )}}\\pard\\plain \r\n\\ql \\li0\\ri0\\widctlpar\\aspalpha\\aspnum\\faauto\\adjustright\\rin0\\lin0\\itap0 \\fs24\\lang1033\\langfe1033\\cgrid\\langnp1033\\langfenp1033 {\\insrsid6558591 \r\nI was on the road for hours today, and the FM dial was feeling particularly nostalgic.  So many songs that reminded me of that summer when I learned too much about myself.  }{\\insrsid3485284 \r\n\\par \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid6558591 That summer which so eerily built its own soundtrack, pulling top-forty threads around itself until every moment stung with meaning.  That summer that we were always going somewhere.  Each lyric brings it back \\endash \r\n where I was when I heard it last.  And how I was never, and always, alone.}{\\insrsid7633606 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid6558591 \r\n\\par \\\'93}{\\i\\insrsid6558591\\charrsid15035063 how can you say I go about things the wrong way}{\\insrsid6558591 \\\'94\r\n\\par }{\\insrsid7633606 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid3496816 The window\\rquote s down, and the wheel is hot under my hand.  }{\\insrsid12611359 The speakers jitter }{\\insrsid8334519 nervously }{\\insrsid12611359 under the moderate pressure of the track\\rquote s backbeat.  }{\\insrsid3496816 There\r\n\\rquote s a pair of feet hanging out the passenger-side window, and a hand on my lap.  I\\rquote m counting the telephone poles as they swish cleanly by, counting the miles to the river.  }{\\insrsid14949838 The mid-afternoon sun }{\\insrsid12933464 glints}{\r\n\\insrsid14949838  golden}{\\insrsid12933464  as it washes over}{\\insrsid14949838  the freckles of my left arm.  }{\\insrsid3496816 I\\rquote ve got my new lipstick on.\r\n\\par }{\\insrsid5445440 \r\n\\par }\\pard \\ql \\li0\\ri0\\widctlpar\\aspalpha\\aspnum\\faauto\\adjustright\\rin0\\lin0\\itap0\\pararsid6558591 {\\insrsid6558591 \\\'93}{\\i\\insrsid6558591\\charrsid15035063 and they call her a sinner and they call her a whore}{\\insrsid6558591 \\\'94\r\n\\par }\\pard \\ql \\li0\\ri0\\widctlpar\\aspalpha\\aspnum\\faauto\\adjustright\\rin0\\lin0\\itap0 {\\insrsid6558591 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid5445440 We\\rquote re in an apartment on the Oakland side of Telegraph \\endash  the kind where there\\rquote s always an argument on the street outside.  They\\rquote re in the other room, and I hear the noises.  I\\rquote \r\nm reading Deepak Chopra to distract myself, because it\\rquote s the only book in the room.  I hate Deepak Chopra.  }{\\insrsid4213544 The guy with the heroin is gone, thank god, but I\\rquote ve been exiled to this inner room with no hope of escape \\endash \r\n no way to }{\\insrsid4010321 block out }{\\insrsid155663 the grunting and thumping.  I make my decision.  I set down the book, stand up, and start to unbutton }{\\insrsid947553 my blouse even }{\\insrsid155663 before my hand hits the handle.  I decide that I\r\n\\rquote m never going to make anybody else fight this fight.  I decide }{\\insrsid4799570 that complicated is better than }{\\insrsid8996604 miserable.}{\\insrsid5445440 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid3954123 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid16013744 \\\'93}{\\i\\insrsid15035063 when you go, don\\rquote t you know }{\\i\\insrsid16013744\\charrsid15035063 it\\rquote s the perfect ending}{\\insrsid16013744 \\\'94}{\\insrsid3954123 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid4463112 \r\n\\par I\\rquote m on a mechanical bull, biting back tears.  I\\rquote ve never been this jealous.  I know what they\\rquote re doing in the tack room.  The cowboys working the bull know }{\\insrsid6558591 it, too}{\\insrsid4463112 , and they\\rquote \r\nre sad for me.  One of them slides his hand up my thigh as he helps me down; holds onto my hand a moment longer than is necessary to get me to the ground.  He\\rquote s}{\\insrsid5194073 \r\n smiling when I look at him.  His eyes are so blue.  I think about it.  I }{\\insrsid4463112 can\\rquote t do it.\r\n\\par }{\\insrsid16013744 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid141565 \\\'93}{\\i\\insrsid141565\\charrsid15035063 i\\rquote m open to falling from grace}{\\insrsid141565 \\\'94}{\\insrsid16013744 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid4463112 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid918580 He answers our phone, even though}{\\insrsid3496816  it\\rquote s too late for someone to be calling.}{\\insrsid734016   He gives me a quizzica\r\nl glance as he passes me the phone, and my guard is already up by the time I hear the voice on the other end.  She tells me she\\rquote s pregnant, and the moment is so awkward that I\\rquote m tempted to ask her if it\\rquote s mine.  It\\rquote \r\ns been four years since we\\rquote ve spoken.  She tells me about her life, which makes me sad.  She\\rquote s dead-ended, and she\\rquote s acquiesced.  }{\\insrsid3485284 \r\n\\par \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid16189651 I\\rquote m frankly surprised that she still thinks of me.  }{\\insrsid3485284 \r\n\\par \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid734016 We never speak again.}{\\insrsid3496816 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid11105154 \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid7999539 It\\rquote s seventy degrees in Los Angeles, so the window\\rquote s down again as}{\\insrsid11105154  I\\rquote m listening to these lyrics move through the car, in this life that I\\rquote ve had since her.}{\\insrsid13258801   }{\r\n\\insrsid1975361 And I begin to realize that t}{\\insrsid10682751 hese proclivities that }{\\insrsid1975361 fashion the shape of my heart }{\\insrsid10682751 \\endash  these t}{\\insrsid7305079 hings that make you turn from me \\endash  they were born of }{\r\n\\insrsid1859649 honest }{\\insrsid7305079 necessity.}{\\insrsid9268318   }{\\insrsid1844760 \r\n\\par \r\n\\par Believe me when I say that }{\\insrsid9268318 I don\\rquote t mean for them to injure anyone else, though I\\rquote m sure they do.  And will.}{\\insrsid9057674 \r\n\\par \r\n\\par }{\\insrsid13114191 I don\\rquote t expect you to understand this, but please hear me:  }{\\insrsid9057674 I\\rquote m not going to apologize for loving you.}{\\insrsid3237043   But I will apologize for everything else.}{\\insrsid10682751 \r\n\\par }}');
INSERT INTO `articles_text` VALUES (3935,'<p>I was on the road for hours today, and the FM dial was feeling particularly nostalgic.  So many songs that reminded me of that summer when I learned too much about myself.</p>  \r\n<p>That summer which so eerily built its own soundtrack, pulling top-forty threads in knots until every moment stung with meaning.  That summer that we were always going somewhere.</P>\r\n<p>Each lyric brings it back – where I was when I heard it last.  And how I was never, and always, alone.</P>\r\n<p><i>how can you say I go about things the wrong way</i></p>\r\n<p>The window’s down, and the wheel is hot under my hand.  The speakers jitter nervously under the moderate pressure of the track’s backbeat.  There’s a pair of feet hanging out the passenger-side window, and a hand on my lap.  I’m counting the telephone poles as they swish cleanly by, counting the miles to the river.  The mid-afternoon sun glints golden as it washes over the freckles of my left arm.  I’ve got my new lipstick on.</P>\r\n<p><i>and they call her a sinner and they call her a whore</i></p>\r\n<p>We’re in an apartment on the Oakland side of Telegraph – the kind where there’s always an argument on the street outside.  They’re in the other room, and I hear the noises.  I’m reading Deepak Chopra to distract myself, because it’s the only book in the room.  I hate Deepak Chopra.  The guy with the heroin is gone, thank god, but I’ve been exiled to this inner room with no hope of escape – no way to block out the grunting and thumping.  I make my decision.  I set down the book, stand up, and start to unbutton my blouse even before my hand hits the handle.  I decide that I’m never going to make anybody else fight this fight.  I decide that complicated is better than miserable.</p>\r\n<p><i>when you go, don’t you know it’s the perfect ending</i></p>\r\n<p>I’m on a mechanical bull, biting back tears.  I’ve never been this jealous.  I know what they’re doing in the tack room.  The cowboys working the bull know it, too, and they’re sad for me.  One of them slides his hand up my thigh as he helps me down; holds onto my hand a moment longer than is necessary to get me to the ground.  He’s smiling when I look at him.  His eyes are so blue.  I think about it.  I can’t do it.</p>\r\n<p><i>i’m open to falling from grace</i></p>\r\n<p>He answers our phone, even though it’s too late for someone to be calling.  He gives me a quizzical glance as he passes me the phone, and my guard is already up by the time I hear the voice on the other end.  She tells me she’s pregnant, and the moment is so awkward that I’m tempted to ask her if it’s mine.  It’s been four years since we’ve spoken.  She tells me about her life, which makes me sad.  She’s dead-ended, and she’s acquiesced.</p>\r\n<p>I’m frankly surprised that she still thinks of me.</p>\r\n<p>We never speak again.</p>\r\n<p>It’s seventy degrees in Los Angeles, so the window’s down again as I’m listening to these lyrics move through the car, in this life that I’ve had since her.  And I begin to realize that these proclivities that fashion the shape of my heart – these things that make you turn from me – they\\\'re not honest, but they were born of honest necessity.</P>\r\n<p>Believe me when I say that I don’t mean for them to injure anyone else, though I’m sure they do.  And will.</p>\r\n<p>I don’t expect you to understand this, but please hear me:  I’m not going to apologize for loving you.  But I will apologize for everything else.</p>\r\n'),(3936,'It has been a long time since I have needed to write about my life. I while ago I made a decision to pursue a path I knew would put me in an environment that was not my own. An environment in which I would be constantly dealing with feelings of alienation.  Now I find myself wondering if I have lost my perspective... So may lectures about balancing rights trying to manage societal issues trying to maintain the pluralism.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI understand why so many succumb to working as legal counsel for corporations or doing strictly business law. It is the easiest approach really when you can abstract things to an economic question it makes it easier for you to make decisions, when you start thinking about the issues in terms of real lives you become paralyzed. The ability to abstract in this area is unparalled.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTry going home to your partner when you just spent your day listening to the darker side of human attempts to couple, what psychic insulation do you have from the constant stories of people screwing each other over? How do you think your relation with your kids is going to be impacted if you decided to do criminal law... And I thought military and accountants made for anal parents.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIn your desire to make a difference you try and justify the changes to your perspective along the way. You are learning the \\\'reality of the world \\\' and ask yourself what sort of price you would be willing to ask people to pay to make a principled stand on some issue. As you find yourself awash in a world full of people that are trying to negotiate similar struggles you find yourself reaching out desperately to find someone that understands why you wanted to do this to begin with. Maybe you are lucky and find someone that is willing to share a lie about what you will inevitably end up doing. Hell, you should count your blessings if you are so lucky. <br><br>\r\n\r\nIt is no longer about saving some shred of your original idealism that lead you to this pursuit but rather about trying to maintain some sense of humanity in your own mind. It is completely subjective at this point. Don\\\'t ever trust your lawyer. '),(3937,'<p>i always thought i\\\'d write a great book someday. when i was a young woman, there were all sorts of publishing possibilities open to me, but not enought time to write, with two babies on the hearth.</p>\r\n<p>when i was older, and the children left home, i was very busy learning to lasso the wuh wuh wuh.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>now i\\\'m ready to write, i have a dozen books in my head, but the likelihood of any of them being published, no matter how well i write them, is pretty much zilch.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a quick look at the NYT bestsellers reveals a bunch of books written by the same people who wrote last year\\\'s books</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s a catch 22 situation.  you can\\\'t get a book deal without an agent, and you can\\\'t get an agent without a book deal.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think i\\\'ll just do nanowrimo and forget about the rest.\r\n</p>'),(3938,'<P>\r\nDuring the summer of 1992 I was working in the carpenter shop at the University of Redlands in southern California. It was a sweet gig for a student waiting for the school year to start: free dorm housing, minimum wage, and minimum responsibilities. As was the custom back then, I ate at Cuca\\\'s, a nearby burrito stand, almost every day and almost always twice a day. Bean and rice burrito with lettuce, tomato, onion and jalapeño. For $1.50, I was really full and really regular.\r\n</P><P>\r\nOn this particular Saturday I had been lounging on Armacost beach, a wide stretch of grass in front of the library. It was called a beach because of the students\\\' sunbathing proclivities. I was stretched out in the lawn when the need to poop descended upon me. I stood up and made a beeline for the nearest \\\"friendly\\\" building, Cal/Founders Hall. The Conference Programs Staff were housed in that residence hall, and I had many friends who would let me stop by and push brown if need be. I stood at the door dialing numbers on the security phone for what seemed to be an eternity as my anus pulsed and my butt cheeks repulsed under the strain of the forthcoming load. Nobody answered.\r\n</P><P>\r\nMy next plan of attack was to cross Colton Avenue and use the public restroom in the courtyard of the Willis Center. I made it across the street in one bound and shuffled the rest of the way up the sidewalk. Curses! It was locked on the weekend. The only toilet within reach was now back across Colton Avenue and further west across the quad, in Bekins Hall, where I was housed that summer.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI was starting to walk like Eddie Murphy\\\'s \\\"White Man\\\" from SNL, keeping my butt cheeks so tight I was in danger of setting of a fusion chain reaction.\r\n</P><P>\r\nAs I crossed Colton and stepped up onto the far curb, the turd broke free of my ass\\\'s grasp and tumbled down the leg of my exercise pants (remember those? early nineties? think MC Hammer). It made a brief stop in my pants cuff and then plopped down in the grass between the sidewalk and the curb. It was a nice turd at about eight inches long and an inch-and-a-half in diameter. I was flabbergasted. I looked around to see how many had seen my misfortune. There wasn\\\'t a soul to be seen anywhere in that sweltering California sun.\r\n</P><P>\r\nBut I still had a logjam of which to attend. I noted my position so I could come back and dispose of the evidence after I was finished with the task at hand, and continued my long shuffle towards Bekins Hall. I got there with a minimum hassle, finished what I had started out there on the curb, wiped, and then checked my drawers for damage. The only skidmark in my pants was a tiny brown stain on the cuff where the turd took refuge from gravity\\\'s pull. Since I was a college student, I wiped it clean with some toilet paper and put them back on. Laundry is for people with jobs, right?\r\n</P><P>\r\nI grabbed an Albertson\\\'s bag and trotted back out on Colton Avenue to retrieve my dookie. I scoured every foot of grass between the curb and the sidewalk along Colton Avenue -- my poop was gone.\r\n</P><P>\r\nI had been away for fifteen minutes at the most. The stretch of Colton between the Willis Center and Bekins Hall is probably a quarter of a mile. I didn\\\'t see anyone when I looked around after the accident; and who would want my turd anyway?\r\n</P><P>\r\nI retraced my steps in both directions. There was no more turd out there along Colton Avenue in the grass between the curb and the sidewalk. Maybe it was the heat. \r\n</P>'),(3939,'<P>\r\nit was 1991. i was in love with A. A was in a band \r\non the lesbian \\\'scene’. and she loved the band, the \r\nscene, the whole hang out. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni didn\\\'t like the big hang out. \r\n</P><P>\r\ni had been horribly betrayed by big female groups \r\nbefore. how could i say \\\'let\\\'s keep a distance\\\'? it \r\nwas her livelihood and these were people she \r\nloved. so against my better judgement i quashed \r\nmy feelings and went along with it all (again). after \r\nall, they are all people, individuals, right? most of \r\nwhom i liked very much as individuals.\r\n</P><P>\r\nanyway, wouldn\\\'t you know it, but the time came \r\nwhen there was a falling out. A was scapegoated \r\nso hard she was driven out of the group. this hit \r\nher hard, and no wonder she high tailed it home to \r\namerica.\r\n</P><IMG SRC=\\\"%%file1%%\\\"><P>\r\ni did this painting in response to the situation. it is a \r\nself portrait as a fool. it was a good 3ft wide and 4ft \r\nlong, so it was quite a dominating image to live \r\nwith, quite apart from the pain that is locked inside \r\nof it, so you will not be surprised that it did not sell. \r\none day i was taking it down to the bins to get rid \r\nof it, with it’s dreadful mojo, just as the one woman \r\nout of that whole group  who had actually stayed \r\nfriends with me (at some cost to herself) came by. \r\n</P><P>\r\nB said, \\\'don\\\'t chuck it, i\\\'ll have it.\\\' so i gave it to her. \r\ni heard that some of the women from the original \r\nsituation saw this painting in her flat, and i believe \r\nthere was some recognition of it’s meaning. good. \r\n</P><P>\r\nhowever, like me, she had a small flat, and the \r\npainting was, as i\\\'ve said, difficult to live with. and \r\nat some point she lent it to her sister. then \r\nsomething weird happened, she told me her \r\nsister\\\'s boyfriend had vandalised it. i was not \r\nhappy with this, i didn\\\'t want it, but i also didn\\\'t \r\nwant it vandalised. another friend, C, said she \r\nwould like it. so i asked for it back. at the time B \r\nand C weren\\\'t talking to each other, and when B \r\nfound out i planned to give it to C she was furious.\r\n</P><P>\r\ntime passed, and one time i was in the same \r\ncompany as B and she agreed to meet me in a \r\ncafe. she brought along the painting - there was \r\nnothing wrong with it! and never had been, weird! \r\nwe talked about all the things that had happened, \r\nand as we left i forgot to pick it up. as i turned back \r\nto get it, she held my arm and looked into my eyes \r\nand said \\\'leave it\\\'. so i did.\r\n</P><P>\r\nB and C are friends again now, and the painting is \r\nlong gone. recently i was in that cafe and i said to \r\none of the people working there \\\'you\\\'ve worked \r\nhere a long time, do you remember anyone leaving \r\na painting here?\\\' i told him some of the story, and i \r\nsaid i would love to find out what happened to it \r\nnext, and maybe take a photograph of it in it\\\'s new \r\nhome, if it had one, even or even especially, if it \r\nwas in the basement covered in mould. he got \r\nquite excited by the story, though he said he didn\\\'t \r\nknow about the painting. he climbed up on to a \r\nstorage area, and looked for it, but it wasn\\\'t there.\r\n</P><P>\r\nalthough i think i am foolishly trusting sometimes, \r\nthat part of me which is so very soft is also my \r\nsensitivity which i need for making anything \r\ncreative. so i hereby rehabilitate her. \r\n</P><P>\r\nthis, then, is the story of the original silly cow \r\npainting. good luck, silly cow painting, wherever \r\nyou are. may your mojo be healing, whether by \r\nbalm or fire. \r\n</P>\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3940,'<CENTER>\r\n<TABLE BORDER=0 WIDTH=300 BGCOLOR=\\\"black\\\">\r\n<TR><TD>\r\n\r\n<P><FONT COLOR=\\\"white\\\">I went to Ducati\\\'s website about 3 months ago\r\nand pulled down desktop images for most of their bikes, and since then\r\nI have been studying them absent mindedly.  Usually I am looking at\r\nthe lower portion because I have a term window open.  Other images\r\nthat cycle by are Honda and Yamaha sport bikes.  </FONT></P>\r\n\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/01_1024.jpg\\\"><IMG WIDTH=300 SRC=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/01_1024.jpg\\\"></A>\r\n\r\n<P><FONT COLOR=\\\"white\\\">Of all the bikes, the Ducatis are the most\r\nstriking to me.  Of the Ducatis, the element that strikes me the most\r\nis the Ducati kick stand on the 749/999.  It is the most beautiful\r\nkick stand I have ever seen.  Witness the way it carrys on the\r\naerodynamics of the bike\\\'s fairing.  The kick stand is part of the\r\nfairing, not just a kickstand for setting the bike up. </FONT></P>\r\n\r\n\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/DucatiKickstand.jpg\\\"><IMG WIDTH=300 SRC=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/DucatiKickstand.jpg\\\"></A><BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<P><FONT COLOR=\\\"white\\\">The second most interesting and desireable\r\nelement is the way the front body fender has a corner at mid wheel\r\nwhere it cuts in.  It is interesting that the designer wanted to hide\r\na portion of the front wheel.  That is new to me.  Obviously they had\r\nto back off to let the front wheel turn. </FONT></P>\r\n\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/DucatiKickstand.jpg\\\"><IMG WIDTH=300 SRC=\\\"http://swinney.org/art/galleries/bikes/ducati/DucatiFender.jpg\\\"></A><BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<P><FONT COLOR=\\\"white\\\">Ultimately, this bike is far too uncomfortable\r\nto meet my delicate needs.  It is pretty to look at though.  I would\r\nput one in my living room in the winter if I lived someplace that had\r\nsnow.</FONT></P>\r\n\r\n</TD></TR></TABLE>\r\n</CENTER>'),(3941,'<p>\r\nHe woke up at an unknown hour of the night from a deep sleep and knew he needed to vomit. Thankfully the steel guards on the side of the gurney were not up and it was easy for him to slide out and stand up. His vision was blurred and he recalled being there but not getting there. He knew he had been in and out of consciousness. He knew he had been forced to drink a thick liquid. He had given blood. They had attached things to his chest.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe moved slowly as he picked up the tube connected to his wrist off the floor and looped it a few times; using the steel pole on wheels for support as he made his way to the door with the stencil of a man on it. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe fell to his knees and began to vomit violently into the toilet. The episode caused flashes of white, black, and sparks raced past his face. He collapsed backward and let his back and head hit the tile wall. His head fell and he became motionless. His eyes opened again and he remembered the comfort of the bed. He rose once again and began to move; the floor covered in black slippery liquid. He did not notice that the tube taped to his wrist had come undone at the point it connected to the tube from the bag hanging from the steel pole on wheels.\r\n\r\nHe was lucidly making his way back to the bed losing blood. He was in another world...a cloudy world. The world of too many things. He slid back onto the bed and fell asleep. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe had never been slapped awake before. He was startled, scared, thrust back into reality. A gaggle of nurses and doctors had surrounded the bed and as quickly as he had opened his eyes he closed them. Again he was jostled awake, this time not by being struck, but by the pungent smell of something in a small white tube. It made his eyes water and his head shake. “Are you awake? Can you hear me? How many fingers am I holding up?” He looked down to see a nurse removing tape from his wrist, taking out the needle, and placing cotton and some new tape onto the hole the needle had left. Again he fell asleep and again he was woken by the extreme smell. He answered the questions and a nurse placed a new needle and tube into his arm and reconnected it to the bag that was hanging from the steel pole with wheels. As he watched her he looked down to see that the floor was covered in blood and showed signs of being disturbed by frantic and constant movement. No more words. No more answers. The tube was reconnected and an absurd amount of tape had been used to secure that previously insecure section of tubing. The group around his bed had evaporated. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe lay awake for as long as he could as an older woman in a non-medical uniform came by and jabbered something Korean into his ear as she crouched down with towels to clean up the mess. A nurse walked past, looked him in the eye, and laughed like the devil. \r\n</p>'),(3942,'<p>Please excuse our prickly moods.<br>\r\nIt\\\'s gray Russian winter here and<br>\r\nwe\\\'re tender in the cold.<br>\r\nThere\\\'s this worn bitch of a life to trudge,<br>\r\nand no matter how mild a day it is<br>\r\nwe\\\'ll sing woe to the rafters <br>\r\nand ain\\\'t it grand <br>\r\nextra gravy on the side<br>\r\nand never enough sleep.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Doped on bourbon <br>\r\nand chocolate soufflé <br>\r\nwe\\\'ll join an army of stubborn aesthetes <br>\r\nwho weave stories and string up yarn<br>\r\ninstallations, teaching us light shift<br>\r\nand counter-turn, and touch, <br>\r\nteaching us arc and pathos, <br>\r\nthe stillborn morning,<br>\r\nnew rooms to move through<br>\r\nand hey, what a fine sky of stars <br>\r\nout there tonight.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is time, after all,<br>\r\nnever enough, but time enough<br>\r\nto be unnecessarily <br>\r\nbitter, to run out<br>\r\nof time, to grow lines <br>\r\nand gain weight and not<br>\r\nfinish what we started<br>\r\nor understand what\\\'s finished.<br>\r\nI\\\'m nothing if not good humored,<br>\r\nbut days of no consequence abound<br>\r\nand it\\\'s hard being Zen <br>\r\nwhen the traffic\\\'s bad.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For some, the inevitability of children,<br>\r\nfor us, the 1-2-3 temerity of love,<br>\r\nthe lovely way it <br>\r\ngets light and heavy all at once,<br>\r\nthe wonder of someone<br>\r\nwho barely understands why<br>\r\nyou chose him or why it<br>\r\nshould feel so important, <br>\r\nbut you do, and there are no words<br>\r\nand screw him if he doesn\\\'t get it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And then there are words, words<br>\r\nenough to repeat and sing along,<br>\r\nGary combing his hair,<br>\r\nBarry Gibb starting a joke,<br>\r\nPeggy Lee asking if that\\\'s all there is.<br>\r\nGet the joke right and there\\\'ll be laughs,<br>\r\nand yeah, Peggy, it\\\'s all a shit sandwich <br>\r\nexcept when it\\\'s not.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I laugh in bed at a movie while sick,<br>\r\nlaugh at people who feel sorry for the dead,<br>\r\nlaugh because every doomstruck newscast<br>\r\nand gray Russian day<br>\r\nis an absurdist play if you manage<br>\r\nto love it right.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And in time we may never learn<br>\r\npatience, or Tagalog,<br>\r\nor letting go, because there are<br>\r\nnights enough to argue that there is no<br>\r\nletting go, or forgetting,<br>\r\nonly shifting light and string theory,<br>\r\na new show downtown,<br>\r\na few missed connections<br>\r\nand whoa, who\\\'s that with<br>\r\nthe chin in the corner?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Exhausted adults<br>\r\nget that knowing sigh,<br>\r\nknowing already how the years blend,<br>\r\nknowing there\\\'s never <br>\r\nenough money or sleep<br>\r\nor furtive salvos of affection <br>\r\nwhen you crave them most.<br>\r\nBut we aren\\\'t like that yet.<br>\r\nNeither of us wants to die<br>\r\nor believe the best times are behind us<br>\r\nbecause there\\\'s always succor enough,<br>\r\ndelight beyond reason, <br>\r\nand thrillingly sane fights that are<br>\r\nworth our time in bread.  <br> \r\nSo pass that J over here,<br>\r\ngive us a sip, <br>\r\nfind me another lover and<br>\r\nsomebody take a look at this gorgeous<br>\r\nthing, will you?  I just really <br>\r\nwant to show someone <br>\r\nand hear you say hell yeah<br>\r\nand yammer until dawn about<br> \r\nhow much it matters.\r\n</p>'),(3943,'<p>\r\nIt had been a bad day at work. It had been a bad month. It had been a lot of bad things within a short period of time. He left work with a head full of misery and frustration. Questions with no answers and a job that did not foster the kind of brain activity that might have helped him quell that angst inside of him. It was a cold night and he walked toward the family mart and picked up four bottles of chang-ha. It was Friday night and he missed his relatives. Christmas was the only time of year his small family got together and forgot all of their problems and acted civil around each other, and not being able to be there again added to his misery and self destruction.\r\n</p>\r\n<p> \r\nHe finished two bottles outside family mart and added them to an already present pile of trash. He stuffed the other two bottles into his pocket and decided to catch a cab home and finish them there.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe flagged a cab and as he sat in the back something snapped. The cracks had turned into chasms and tears began pouring down his face. He finished the two bottles of chang-ha in his pocket and smoked profusely. The cab driver had no intention to tell a man in his state there was no smoking. As the cab entered the valley between the Yaksu and Oksu tunnels, he had the cab driver stop and wait. He got out and ran to an LG25 and picked up four more bottles of chang-ha. Chugging one as he paid, he left the shop in a sorry state indeed. Chugged the second one on the way back to the cab and got in; throwing the bottle onto another already present pile of trash. In broken Korean he began to try and tell the cab driver what was going on. The cab driver just nodded and he can remember a look of sincere concern on his face.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTraffic that night was slow, but finally they got to where he needed to get out. He gave the cab driver a big tip and apologized for the smell of smoke and alcohol. The cab driver was relieved to be rid of him, and drove off leaving a very drunk and broken down young man at the corner. He had composed himself enough to walk the streets looking somewhat normal. He got to his apartment and just fell to the ground in absolute despair. He was finished. He was done. He grabbed at bottles of imovane and valium and ate a whole bunch; washing them down with another bottle of chang-ha.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAfter about five minutes he knew he had done something wrong. He knew he had taken it too far. He knew he had to get himself to a hospital. As fucked up as he was, he packed a few more bottles of chang-ha in his pocket for the ride there. He left his apartment and his eyes closed.\r\n</p>'),(3944,''),(3947,'<p>\r\nA group of people were walking through a forest lost. As they walked the image would shift from them in the woods to some buildings along some railroad tracks. The railroad tracks were rusty and hadn’t been used since the cold war ended. The buildings were in shabby condition, though it appeared they were still hooked up to a power grid. From images of the group walking in the woods toward the relic of the past, images of what was inside flashed intermittently. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA young girl walked slowly through one of the buildings. She was carrying a handgun; her face deadpan and sullen. She had hair down just below her shoulders and she followed with her eyes what looked like a blurry balloon. Once and a while she would raise her hand and fire at the blur but it did not die, and she cursed at it.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTwo Japanese women were crouched over black body bags. The bags were filled with the bodies of unknown men. The two women were giggling at each other. One of them wore a black one piece dress and the other wore jeans and a blouse. Their hair was short and they had blood red eyes. They heard a sound and walked like crabs onto the ceiling and waited. From where they looked was a maze of black bags, stacked high, in a room that looked as though it went on forever.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe group of people arrived at the buildings and were confused by what they saw. The buildings invited fear and half of the group decided it would be best just to follow the railroad tracks north. The other half; hungry, tired, and curious decided to investigate the buildings. The group that had decided to follow the tracks sat down to rest while the group that decided to enter fanned out. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nScreams were heard from every direction. The air had been silent prior to the first scream, but just as the first scream ended, a second, third, fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh and so on occurred in what seemed like only a minute. The group sitting by the tracks were paralyzed with fear and one commented that the screams outnumbered the people who had entered. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey knew they had better leave and find help and they began running north along the tracks.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA man, in his late thirties had entered a building through a side door. He was with two other members of the group that had decided to go into the building.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThey had not heard the screams. In fact, none of the group that had entered the buildings had heard the screams. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe man and his tagalongs found themselves in an office. One of the tagalongs turned on a soviet era photocopier and pressed the green button. Unlike western photocopiers the entire top half of the contraption swiveled as if the entire bureaucracy of it was working to scan a single image. The image that came out was of them on the floor in a bloody mess. They were perplexed by this and a tagalong lifted the lid to find it covered in dry blood. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA woman in her forties was walking through a large room. She had her hands in her back pockets and walked slowly scanning in every direction with her head. She was clearly nervous and as she turned a corner she found herself face to face with the young girl with long hair. The girl looked up and smiled. The woman said hello and the girl began to back away and lift her arm. She fired two shots. One bullet screamed past the woman’s right ear as the other screamed past her left. She dropped to the ground but was not hurt, though her hearing was gone. She looked up and saw the girl walking away. She looked behind her to see a blurry object on the floor; blurry blood slowly making a blurry pool.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe three in the office entered yet another office. The new office was large and contained hundreds of copiers in three rows. Each person began walking down a row turning each machine on and pressing the green button as they walked. They reached the end of the room and turned back to collect what each machine had produced. The images collected were frame-by-frame stills telling the story of the photo that showed them on the floor in a bloody mess. Shivers ran down their spines as they witnessed each picture. Each row told the story of their death and one began to panic. “We are just dying! Who is doing this to us? Does yours show who is doing this to use? Who is doing this?” The pictures showed their death but no image of who was inflicting the damage appeared. They just stood there swapping photos. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman who had lost her hearing approached the blurry object. There was a ringing in her ear that caused some discomfort. She stepped in the blurry pool of blurry blood and noticed that it was slippery. She crouched down to see what it was. She tried to touch it but her hand could not find anything to touch. She got up and walked in the direction she saw the young girl walk in and found herself in front of a locked door.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA teenage girl with blonde hair had found some empty rail cars behind one of the large buildings and noticed some smoke billowing from a chimney in the last car. She raced toward the car and found the doors wide open. A stove inside was packed full of documents slowly burning. On the table she found an empty can of tuna. Just as she placed the can back on the table boots came down from the ceiling and connected with her jaw and knocked her out. She woke up soon after and noticed that she had been tied to the stove; the heat from it slowly cooking her back. She felt no pain but knew she would die. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nPanic came over one of the tagalongs and she began to cry uncontrollably and say she did not deserve to die like that. The others were calm, though they did nothing to quell the woman’s fears. They left her in the room and entered yet another office, this one covered in black dust. They found a refrigerator and noticed that the handle was free from dust and quite shiny. The man grabbed hold of it and pulled the door open. Dead mice began to fall from the space the door made as it opened and he jumped back and the door flung open; hundreds of dead mice piling up on the floor. The stench was overwhelming and they ran to another door and entered another room.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe women in jeans looked at the locked door. She put her ear against the door to listen for activity but the ringing in her ears made it impossible. She moved away from the door and with a swift movement of her leg kicked at it a few times until it swung open. The room was empty except for a table, chair, and typewriter. There were windows on three of the walls and she looked out each of them to see a garden filled with roses. Looking out the third window she saw the young girl with long hair walking slowly toward another building. She opened the window and called out to her. The young girl kept walking and ignored the cries coming from the window just a few hundred feet away. Tired and scared, the woman sat at the table and looked at the typewriter.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman who panicked pulled herself together and ran to the room the other two had entered. The smell of the mice made her eyes water and she ran to the door they had opened. She found herself once again with the two men. They looked at her and motioned her to come to where they were. They were looking cautiously out of a window and the man whispered to her to be quiet. She looked out the window in the direction they were looking and saw a young girl with long hair walking toward some rail cars. The girl stopped and slowly turned her head and looked straight at the three in the window and smiled. She began walking again and disappeared between two of the rail cars. “This place is fucked” said the man as they spoke softly. “Who is she?” asked the women who had panicked. “Was she in the pictures from the copiers?” the second man questioned. They stood there and looked around wondering what to do next.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe young girl with long hair entered the car where the teenager was bound to the stove. She had passed out but the young girl poked her. She sprang to life to see a young girl with long hair smiling at her. “It’s going to be ok” she said softly. She went to a dresser and opened the top shelf. She took out a knife and cut the teenager down; her back was burnt badly, though she felt no pain. “Who did this to you?” the young girl with long hair asked. The teenager looked down to see the young girl was wearing boots and responded “it was you?” The young girl laughed and said “yes” softly and began stabbing the teenager in the stomach and arms. She fell back and though she felt no pain, she knew she was dead. The young girl with long hair stepped back and shot the teenager in the face. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe three in the room who were resting stood up at the sound of the shot. The woman at the typewriter heard nothing, though what she read made her entire body tremble. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>You can’t hear. But you can see. It would be better if it were the other way around. You should have gone north you stupid bitch.</i>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe three in the room decided it was time to find the others. They began rushing through room after room until they found themselves in a small office. They had come to a dead end and decided to rest before heading back to find another way out. As the man in his thirties sat down at a desk, the other two decided to go into the previous room to look around. As soon as they left, the Japanese woman in jeans came into the room and began sniffing his face. He did not know what the do and just sat there and did not move. She began softly running her fingers down his face and neck and rubbing his chest. She stood up and as she stepped aside the Japanese woman with the black dress ran in and began to do the same thing though this time she seemed more interested and more passionate about it. The Japanese woman in jeans left the room and the woman in the black dress shouted “you are a liar!” and began biting at his face. He pushed her off and punched her three times in the face. The power of his punches were too much for her fragile face. With only three punches she was gone; the hair on his fingers covered in blood and brain matter.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nHe walked into the next room to find the tagalongs in a bloody mess on the floor. The images of the copier had come true for the two of them but he was determined to live. He looked up from the mess to find the young girl with long hair standing in the doorway. She smiled and looked at the mess and said “did you do that?” The man said nothing. He stood there and just stared at the girl. “Would you like to…” the Japanese woman in jeans came from behind the young girl with long hair and started biting at her shoulder. The long haired one let out a bone chilling scream and pistol whipped the Japanese woman in the face and turned and began stomping on the woman. The man ran toward the young girl and bear hugged her. She began to growl and spit. She was wild with rage.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe woman at the typewriter entered the room and saw the girl with long hair being held by the man who had been in her party. The man and young girl both looked at her and started screaming, but she could not hear either of them. She looked down to see a woman with a shattered skull on the floor and back up to the man holding the girl in a bear hug. She began to move toward the two, and sensing something wrong the man began to pull the young girl back into the other room. He did not know what to expect from the woman who could not hear. She would not respond to his questions, nor did she respond to the enraged young girl with long hair. Once in the room the woman who could not hear looked to see the two other members of the party laying on the floor in a bloody mess. She looked up at the young girl with long hair and smiled.  \r\n</p>'),(3945,'walking down ossington.  a guy sees my camera.\r\n\r\ndo you want to photograph me?\r\n\r\nyes.\r\n\r\nclick.\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"col301.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n\r\nclick.\r\n\r\n<img src=\\\"col302.jpg\\\" border=\\\"0\\\">\r\n\r\nare these going to be in the paper?\r\n\r\nno.\r\n\r\nthanks.\r\n\r\nno problem.\r\n\r\ngoodbye.\r\n\r\n\r\n'),(3946,'walking down ossington.  a guy sees my camera.\r\n<br><br>\r\ndo you want to photograph me?\r\n<br><br>\r\nyes.\r\n<br><br>\r\nclick.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://inconduit.com/image/rand/por301_.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\nclick.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://inconduit.com/image/rand/por302_.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">\r\n<br><br>\r\nare these going to be in the paper?\r\n<br><br>\r\nno.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthanks.\r\n<br><br>\r\nno problem.\r\n<br><br>\r\ngoodbye.\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(3948,'<PRE>\r\nyou make me think of\r\nbears, and trees\r\nand quiet places that\r\nsmell like rain\r\n\r\neyes full of storms,\r\nflashing sunshine as a flirt\r\nwhen the weather comes,\r\ni run into the \r\nwide bower \r\nof your wingspan\r\nand breathe deeply of you.\r\n\r\nall loki smiles\r\nand odin import\r\nthe poetry of war\r\nwrites the legacy of your body\r\n\r\nthat i writhe beneath\r\nlike freydis,\r\nhiding you\r\nso very carefully\r\nwith one hand on my axe.\r\n</PRE>'),(3949,'ay six: 6.342km, 304.4kcal, 10571 steps)\r\n\r\nand magicians and apothecaries of words and blood and love as it flows from me into you into me again and everywhere\r\n\r\njohn and his friend henry used to live here quite a while back. the parties were legendary as you can imagine, they would sometimes go on for days, dawn after night after day of dragons dancing and singing and kissing, john then got a few books of verse published and buggered off to nw3, never found out what henry did\r\n\r\nbermondsey it scares me\r\n\r\nwhite grounds of blue houses, painted and barred for your convenience\r\nmetallic louvres of air leading to gelding place: there are no handles on those steel doors, is there a code or a touch or a thought?\r\n\r\nthe royal marines with their high pitched ropes have taken over lilliput hall\r\n\r\nonce upon a time, your beige brick double glazed imitation cottage roofs came from ikea (inside your fairytales you play happy families most weekends)\r\n\r\nbermondsey it scares me (everything is on sale in bermondsey)\r\n\r\ni cant be going the right way which way wrong way turn the map around and around jamaica road is wide i like it, where does it go\r\n\r\non balconies: oh hello, putting your washing up? yes im putting my washing up while our caretaker washes the windows, hello, james, his poor wife is very ill, such a nice man and all, james, fancy a cuppa? let me finish this window first, is your son coming later? (fade out)\r\n\r\nfollow a path of dried leaves, crunching under foot, estate teenage boys, open the gate for the car, breathe in breathe out, this is no hellmouth, there was no cemetary here, no ghosts or crooked leylines\r\n\r\nbermondsey it scares the shit out of me\r\n\r\nand then i get to the river'),(3950,'<P>day six: 6.342km, 304.4kcal, 10571 steps)\r\n</P><P>\r\nand magicians and apothecaries of words and blood and love as it flows from me into you into me again and everywhere\r\n</P><P>\r\njohn and his friend henry used to live here quite a while back. the parties were legendary as you can imagine, they would sometimes go on for days, dawn after night after day of dragons dancing and singing and kissing, john then got a few books of verse published and buggered off to nw3, never found out what henry did\r\n</P><P>\r\nbermondsey it scares me\r\n</P><P>\r\nwhite grounds of blue houses, painted and barred for your convenience\r\nmetallic louvres of air leading to gelding place: there are no handles on those steel doors, is there a code or a touch or a thought?\r\n</P><P>\r\nthe royal marines with their high pitched ropes have taken over lilliput hall\r\n</P><P>\r\nonce upon a time, your beige brick double glazed imitation cottage roofs came from ikea (inside your fairytales you play happy families most weekends)\r\n</P><P>\r\nbermondsey it scares me (everything is on sale in bermondsey)\r\n</P><P>\r\ni cant be going the right way which way wrong way turn the map around and around jamaica road is wide i like it, where does it go\r\n</P><P>\r\non balconies: oh hello, putting your washing up? yes im putting my washing up while our caretaker washes the windows, hello, james, his poor wife is very ill, such a nice man and all, james, fancy a cuppa? let me finish this window first, is your son coming later? (fade out)\r\n</P><P>\r\nfollow a path of dried leaves, crunching under foot, estate teenage boys, open the gate for the car, breathe in breathe out, this is no hellmouth, there was no cemetary here, no ghosts or crooked leylines\r\n</P><P>\r\nbermondsey it scares the shit out of me\r\n</P><P>\r\nand then i get to the river\r\n</P>'),(3951,''),(3954,'Okay, I\\\'ve managed to make myself incontinent for the last couple days. Any time a person makes a major change in their diet, there is going to be hell to pay. I\\\'ve eliminated fatty foods, carbonated beverages, and alcohol from my diet for Lent, as well as for the purposes of getting into shape. (\\\"Round\\\" and \\\"pear\\\" are shapes, yes?) Ladies and gentlemen, in a situation like this, do not plan to go anywhere for at least three days without planning for restroom availability every twenty minutes. My body was on a serious poop purge when I changed my diet. The noxious materials oozing from my bunghole would have had Hans Blix and the UN in an uproar. Kim Jong Il and the Iranian regime were shooting me e-mails asking if they could build a pipeline to my restroom.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThen the phone rang. It was my wife. She forgot her keys and needed me to run them to work for her. I left home and got caught in traffic. No sooner had everything ground to a halt when the oh-so-familiar pressure against my sphincter and lower abdomen began to rise. I tried to lie to myself and say it was only gas, but I could feel the liquid and foam sloshing against the inner walls of my rectum. To be blunt, I had to shit. I had to shit BAD.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI looked to my left: cars. I looked to the front and rear: cars. I was wedged in tighter than a thong on Spring Break. I looked to my right: a small patch of wooded area. I thought: \\\"Hey, bears do it!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo I opened up a nearby bag from a discarded salad and grabbed a few remaining napkins. I unbuckled my seatbelt, began to open the door and --<br><br>\r\n\r\n-- and traffic started moving again. Here I was, gearing up for release, and they clear the wreckage ahead and start waving us through. ARGH!<br><br>\r\n\r\nI made my way, post haste, to my wife\\\'s workplace. By then there\\\'s sweat on my brow, I\\\'m doubled over in pain like a recently butt-raped hunchback, and I\\\'m doing baby-steps inside. I enter the front door and make a beeline to the men\\\'s room.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLOCKED!<br><br>\r\n\r\nLadies\\\' room:<br><br>\r\n\r\nLOCKED!<br><br>\r\n\r\nI knock on the men\\\'s door and hear a child\\\'s voice say, \\\"I\\\'m going.\\\" I don\\\'t knock on the ladies\\\' room because I don\\\'t want the person inside to think I\\\'m a purveyor of a bizarre fetish looking for \\\"scraps\\\" of some sort.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo I wait.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe sound of a child humming \\\"Sponge Bob Squarepants.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nI wait some more.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe humming of some other nameless tune.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI drop to one knee.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe kid is up and I hear the toilet flush, some more humming, and the clattering of a child trying to put his pants back on and taking his sweet-ass time about it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI punch the door and scream, and then praise God when I hear a door unbolt and see an older woman shuffle out of the ladies room. I kid you not when I say this: I CRAWL inside the ladies\\\' room and barely manage to get my undergarments down before my backside shoots off like an old side-by-side double barrel shotgun. *POOM* *POOM*<br><br>\r\n\r\nI was Vesuvius, and I rained my doughy wrath down on the tiny toilet town of Pompeii.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAfter about fifteen minutes of lightening-fast Hershey blasts, I\\\'m finally panting and feeling better. I deliver three courtesy flushes the entire time, but I already know I\\\'ve created the perfect Dutch oven inside the tiny restroom.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI wipe, wash, open the door, and almost run over two teenage girls waiting for the restroom I just defiled. I skirt past and cast a quick glance behind me as I observe the aroma registering on both their faces.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI hand the keys off to my wife, exchange some quick pleasantries, and depart post haste, right past the same two teens who give me their best evil eye for making their restroom the Chernobyl of the west coast for the next one hundred years.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLadies and gentlemen, I am not proud of that one. '),(3952,'<p><B>midnight (before everything went south):</B><BR><BR>The Three Little Honkies are sitting on the floor around the coffee table, rolling joints on the marble top.  I tell them they look like they\\\'re working in a sweatshop.  We have to explain to Jesse what that is.  He\\\'s eightteen, Rob\\\'s nineteen, Justin\\\'s eightteen.  They\\\'ve formed a loose rap alliance, without a name as of yet, but I call them what I call them.  Unbeknownst to them.  They aren\\\'t all that great.  Rob shows the most promise, maybe.<BR><BR>We\\\'re listening to a CD they made on Jesse\\\'s computer.  Tracks without vocals.  Every once in awhile, one of them will be inspired to spit some rhymes on top of it.  Connor\\\'s sitting on the kitchen floor, behind the bar, noodling on his B.C. Rich along with the samples, the amp at a moderate volume.  His licks sound kind of like a cross between the Isley Brothers and Einstürzende Neubauten.  The body of his guitar has serpents painted on it.  That particular model is evidently called \\\"The Deceiver\\\".  He\\\'s fourteen.<BR><BR>There\\\'s a bowl of loquats on the end table beside me and another near where Rob\\\'s working.  They don\\\'t like them.  I\\\'ll eat one maybe every ten minutes or so, putting the big black seeds on a napkin.  Justin packs a bowl, takes a hit, passes it to me.<BR><BR>Rob and Jesse start to tell me how they threw some kid off a bridge recently.  I suspect they\\\'re making it up but don\\\'t say anything.  They like to mess with my head.<BR><BR>Jesse says, \\\"So, he\\\'s just sittin\\\' there lookin\\\' at us, and I go, \\\'So, how\\\'s the livin\\\' on this bridge?\\\'  And he just looks at us.  Then he goes, \\\'Y\\\'all need to fuck off.\\\'\\\"<BR><BR>And then Rob evidently threw him in <a href=\\\"http://www.clubkayak.com/cfkt/trips/pithlachascotee_river.html\\\">the Pithlachascotee</a>.<BR><BR>I say, \\\"How old was he?\\\"<BR><BR>Rob:  I dunno.  Nine, ten, eleven.  Somethin\\\' like that.<BR><BR>Me:  Well, what if he couldn\\\'t swim?  Didja think \\\'a that?<BR><BR>Rob:  I woulda jumped in after \\\'im if I saw that.<BR><BR>They say he made it out alright, climbed out at the park under construction on the north shore, on the west side of Grand.  I tell them that sounds like something out of Italian cinema, kind of give them the defining characteristics of the genre, provide a few examples.  In the middle of my diatribe, Rob turns to Jesse and whispers something.  They both giggle.<BR><BR>The Guatemalans next door bang on the other side of my bathroom wall.  I tell the guys to turn stuff down some.  Justin gets up and goes to the CD player, and it sounds like Connor cuts the gain on his amp a little.  We haven\\\'t heard anything verbal out of him in awhile.  He\\\'s supposed to be moving to Virginia soon.  Evidently, his mom thinks there won\\\'t be any bad influences there.  He\\\'s been cutting school for four or five months.  He lives about a mile away, to the north.  The rest of us live on my block.<BR><BR>I say, \\\"Connor.  Come sit in here with us.  Bring everything in here.  I like to watch you play.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Nah.  Fuck all y\\\'all livin\\\' room niggas.  All you Southside niggas.\\\"<BR><BR>They\\\'re rolling ten joints for an order for one of Rob and Jesse\\\'s customers, some guy who waits tables at a bar/restaurant downtown.  The guy\\\'s aunt owns it.  He just moved here, sans connection, and luckily ran into Jesse somewhere or other.  He came from Detroit, dad just died, acts/models, moving to L.A. in a couple of weeks when he turns eightteen.<BR><BR>Jesse:  And his e-mail address is \\\"modelboi[REDACTED]@hotmail.com\\\".  B-O-I.<BR><BR>Justin:  Oh, yeah, he\\\'s a fag.  No doubt.<BR><BR>Rob:  You let \\\'im suck your dick, didn\\\'t ya?<BR><BR>Jesse:  <u>Noooo.</u><BR><BR>Rob:  But you would, wouldn\\\'t ya?<BR><BR>Jesse:  (chuckles softly)  I might.  He looks just like a girl.  No shit.<BR><BR>They all laugh.<BR><BR>Three minutes later, the guy calls.  His aunt\\\'s about to close, and he\\\'s begging her to stay open for fifteen more minutes so Jesse can deliver his weed.  Jesse assures him he\\\'ll be there inside of ten, tells him to leave the money behind the toilet on the left.  He hangs up and says, \\\"Fuck off.\\\"<BR><BR>As they\\\'re finishing up, Rob feels moved to elicit.  He\\\'s talking about us, the room.  Unspoken is the desperate camaraderie we share.  Who would hang out with us if not each other?  At the end, he\\\'s rhyming about me:<BR><BR><BLOCKQUOTE><I>Homeboy\\\'s talkin\\\' about Italian cinema<BR>Like he\\\'s fruitier than a loquat enema.<BR><BR>LO-quat.<BR>LO-quat.<BR>lolololo loquat.</I></BLOCKQUOTE>He turns to Jesse and says, faux <I>sotto voce</I>, \\\"They\\\'re also called Chinese plums.\\\"<BR><BR>I grab one out of the bowl and bounce it off his head.  \\\"Japanese.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Whatever.\\\"<BR><BR>Jesse leaves in his unregistered \\\'94 Ford Tempo.  (<I>It\\\'s got a V-6, man.</I>)  Every few days, he\\\'ll steal a new license plate for it.  Drives it around late at night only.  I tell him he should just run; he\\\'d get there in three minutes.  Nothing doing.<BR><BR>Justin starts telling a needlessly convoluted tale about somebody named Cornbread.  I burst out laughing:  \\\"Is he black or white?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"He\\\'s white.  That\\\'s why he\\\'s Cornbread.\\\"<BR><BR>I say I want to be called Pumpernickel from now on.  No, Sourdough.<BR><BR>Connor pipes up, \\\"No, we\\\'re gonna call you Honey White.\\\"  They all laugh.<BR><BR>\\\"You little bitch, are you into my bread again?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Yeah, I\\\'m on a toast jag back here.\\\"<BR><BR>I get up and go into the kitchen.  He\\\'s got my two-slice Sunbeam on the floor beside him along with a saucer, a butterknife, the jar of strawberry jam, the loaf of Meridian Honey White.  I grab the bread and throw two slices in the machine:  \\\"Okay, one for you, one for me.  And that\\\'s it.  Unless you got a dollar.\\\"<BR><BR>I would dearly like to feed him something.  It isn\\\'t baked, but, oh, does it rise.  Rises like the sun.  (Some girl in college told me that\\\'s why Hemingway called the book that.  Little jokey-joke.  Little wink wink nudge nudge.  Seems to me like the title\\\'s more universal, but that could be part of it.  A little black humor.)  First time I saw him, I thought he was a girl.  At a distance.<BR><BR>We wait over half an hour on Jesse, but he doesn\\\'t come back. We all nod knowingly over this. Justin and Rob bicker over who gets to roll the blunt.  For some reason, this is an enviable task.  I don\\\'t know why.  Connor comes in and sits with us on the Big-Ass Sectional Blue Couch, and Justin turns off the CD.  Nobody says much as we smoke.<BR><BR>Breaking a minute\\\'s silence, Justin says, \\\"Connor, I have to make a confession:  I fucked your Mom.\\\"  Connor looks at the floor, doesn\\\'t say anything, just sits there all biscuit-ersed.  The rest of us look at each other and stifle great gales of laughter.<BR><BR>Rob says, \\\"Connor.  Connor.  So did I.  And it was <u>HOTT-TUH-TUH-TUH</U>.  I waxed that ass like a Coupe de Ville.  She does it <u>ALLLLLLL</U>.  Two thumbs up.  Way up.\\\"<BR><BR>Connor looks at him, at Justin and I, and finally starts laughing:  \\\"Shut up.  You did not.\\\"<BR><BR>Somehow, simultaneously, spontaneously, they both pull straight faces and nod earnestly.  He starts wrestling with Justin.  They almost knock over the bong.  I pick it up and put it beside me.<BR><BR>They stop after a second, and everybody settles back down to the business of cashing the blunt.  It gets quiet again.  After a second, I say, \\\"Connor, I never fucked your mom, but, to be honest with you, I\\\'m think I\\\'m gonna hafta try to hit that shit before you leave.\\\"</p>'),(3953,'<P>I thought of two stories I should write today. One was about my trip from hell on a bus between Dublin and Belfast. Short version -- I almost peed my pants while wedged in a 6-inch \\\"seat\\\" between two Irishmen with 60-inch biceps. Or so they seemed, right then, those bulging arms just waiting to crush little American me, as I pissed down their legs and mine. The other I wrote in my mind as I was on my way to work, looking at the headless bodies standing in the aisle of the bus in front of me, thinking of how strange humans are from that perspective. I want to write more, but I\\\'m ever so busy with work and when I\\\'m not busy I\\\'m still busy, but these are excuses.</p><p>The other day a close friend told me she had read everything I\\\'ve posted here (on my blog), which really surprised me. I had told her the url around the time we first met, when we were talking about her blog, and I always just kind of assumed she never read it, or read a bit and gave it up, you know. But she did read it, and apparently all of it, and now I have a whole new set of wonders about her enthusiasm for me and her encouragements for me to write. She wants to work together, she said, she wants to be my editor. Thrilling, incredibly flattering, and scary. The opportunity to work with someone that I share mutual love, respect, and trust with is unexpectedly tantalizing. Oh and, I should point out that I think she is an incredibly talented writer, herself. She\\\'d be slumming it to work with the likes of me, right. Anyhow it never occurred to me that such an opportunity would ever arise. I should do it, yes? The first piece she wants me to write is something about usage dictionaries, or dictionaries in general, perhaps. Or whatever comes to mind associated with them. A passion of mine of course but I\\\'m not convinced I can make it interesting for others. I should try, though. To that end, I\\\'m accepting suggestions, should you have any ideas about what you would like me to write. Inevitably it seems that everything I write is about myself, and that still troubles me, but if people I love want to read my words, I guess the least I could do is give it a whirl. </p><p>I am nervous about trying to write more, because the idea of writing, or being a writer, in spirit if not in practice, is deeply intertwined in my notion of myself and wrapped tightly around my delicate heart. Right now, though I write little for public consumption, I have this idea that I am able to write well, that my talent is beyond the average, that some do enjoy my work and that many might. To write more is to threaten that comfort, however naive and egotistical it is. I\\\'m ever courageous when situations are meaningless, and ever timid when it\\\'s really on the line. The thing is, and I\\\'m going to be unusually honest here, the real issue is that I don\\\'t want to do it if I\\\'m not great. I understand the ridiculousness of that urge, the impossibility of it. But I can\\\'t stand the idea of being a mediocre writer. I\\\'d rather be a mediocre anything else, or even a terrible anything else. I decided some years ago that writing was my only shot at creating art, and to test that conviction is to threaten its existence. In the deepest reaches of me, art is the only thing that matters, besides love. Love is about people, of course, but then so is art. At least to me, and besides people, art is the only other thing in this universe that can make me feel.</p><p>Everyone\\\'s definition of art is personal, and mine is this -- an externalized form of the self. To make and share art is to repackage oneself in a new form, and offer oneself up to the world with an entirely new vulnerability. With little defense against the ham-fisted or the critical, and no hope of ever turning back. When you make your art (and by extension yourself) public, you\\\'re trading some part of your sovereignty in return for potential praise. With my delicacy, my crystalline security, my need for external reinforcement, that seems an awful risk. What\\\'s more, the urge to take that risk seems to be in large part fueled by my ego, and my ego and I are as ever locked in a bitter feud.</p><p>The urge, let alone the act, to make public art, seems supremely egotistical. That self-driven motivation imbues the act with much complexity, when I consider doing it myself. So often in my life I feel this struggle betwixt my ego, my ability, and my relationship with others. I experience a perhaps strange but to me perfectly normal dual sense of myself, one that is both unfailingly egotistical and confident as well as fundamentally, deeply insecure. I tend not to challenge that sense too aggressively, by sticking to my comfort zones. Hence the lack thus far of any significant effort from me to do much that is outside of my previous experience. To put my words out there for others, to demand their attention and hope desperately for their praise, is intimidating indeed. And there is more, there is the largely theoretical but nonetheless deeply considered issue of how I am received by others. To try to write, to strive to be a great writer, is to strive for a new public identity, one that would be closer to my own sense of my self. If I were to accomplish that effort and gain an additional label as \\\"writer\\\" or \\\"artist\\\", would that really make any difference? Would it help that fundamental problem of mine, of feeling misunderstood and alone (always the thirteen year old, I am), or would it be just another detail to further crowd the ever-growing distance that I feel between me and most everyone else?</p><p>To so many, I\\\'m just a computer person, or a geek, a nerd, or perhaps \\\"smart\\\", whatever that means, but never a writer, never an artist, that is for someone else to do, they have decided. Maybe my ego has carried me too far off the sensible plain, but each time I\\\'m reminded of this phenomenon, it unnerves me, sending my inner monologue into a quiet yet explosive rage. It feels like a defensive act, when they do it. Oh, you\\\'re one of <i>those</i> people, we know what <i>they\\\'re</i> like. They aren\\\'t a threat, because <i>they\\\'re not like us</i>. They\\\'re \\\"computer people\\\". (Kill!) See I\\\'ve never identified myself with any of those categories, or any other category for that matter. I\\\'m just me, and that seems a perfectly complete and well-specified identity. That said, though I don\\\'t now identify with any categories, I do strive to be considered a member of one -- artists. Though I understand that the desire to be categorized as such, by its very nature, requires external reinforcement, I don\\\'t think it is simply egotistical. Instead, I think my urge to be associated with that community comes from my insecurity, not my ego. I want to let other people decide, because I\\\'ll never be able to convince myself that I\\\'m worthy of that label without them. </p><p>To be considered an artist seems like a true challenge, something that is not only legitimately difficult, but also meaningful, and meaningful in a way that very little of what I do is now. (My relationships with people are meaningful, but what else?) Though I spend much of my time doing what amounts to busywork, I have not lost sight of the broad ideological goals that constitute my motive force. Part of that motivation is the desire to contribute something to this world that I can be proud of. I act on that desire in my daily life, through my relationships with people, but I crave a broader challenge. I crave vulnerability, because without making myself vulnerable, my actions seem hollow. If I were to achieve the project of becoming a productive writer, I think I could finally be proud of myself. I can\\\'t be sure, but my sense that I would be proud is bound up in the feeling that my urge to pursue this challenge is driven by both halves of my bipartite self, my ego and my insecurity. I simultaneously believe that I have the talent for it and that it is an impossibility. Rather than feeling like a conflict that would threaten to further separate my selves from each other, that dual drive feels like it has the potential to be an incredibly substantial unifying force. It feels as though, if I were to become a successful writer, my public and private identity might finally fuse. My ego <i>and</i> my insecurity might finally find their rightful home, together at last, as the two proud parents of my art.</p><p>But oh, there is so much fear in this, advancing from every direction. Fear that my sense of being capable is an illusion, fear that I am nothing more than an arrogant fraud, fear that my efforts would fail. Fear that this whole realm of thought, all of this verbosity that I\\\'ve just spilled, is delusional. Those fears have kept me largely silent for years now, but perhaps the time has come to face your fears, Brian. Why don\\\'t you just quit goddamn worrying, quit goddamn thinking yourself into endlessly Escherian corners, and just.fucking.do.it. Write.</p><p>Right.</P>'),(3955,'I want mp3 player. What will advise?'),(3956,'vroom vroom in the dwarfmobile, deeper and deeper under ground, what would you like today, madam? oh, i will have one trillion coins today, thank you\r\nup, on some different stairs, feed the birds tuppence a bag, feed the rats with wings banned from every single rail station in london\r\non a day like this the city is a hostile boiling cage of compasses and throgmortons (heehawing happily after a good lunch)\r\non a day like this the city doesnt like me and i dont like it back\r\nglass steel concrete concrete steel concrete 17th century christopher wren church glass steel concrete concrete concrete glass\r\nblinded by the neon sign, ‘welcome to the plantation palace,’ you will never want to leave\r\npast mincing lane where the underwriters are the butchers of today\r\nthrough seething lane and crutched friars into the savage gardens\r\nthis is where yeah thats ok\r\nthis is where oh this gas is bugging me, my stomachs been funny all day, all we had was that pizza last night\r\nthis is where i guess i could\r\nthis is where are we there yet mummy\r\nthis is where your boyfriend said hed text you didnt he i bet you he forgot i bet you he got off with whatshername shes such a bitch she really is are you bovvered\r\nthis is where oh that looks interesting look at those drawings\r\nthis is where you can be so selfish sometimes i never said a word i hate arguing in public\r\nthis is where what this way\r\nthis is where i cant remember\r\nthis is where i love you\r\n\r\npine trees in fair street still smell sweetly (secret garden gate, secret garden, unkempt)\r\nwhat does ‘dare quam accipere’ mean?\r\nthe cat and cucumber café has been closed due to the stupidity of the name. also affected: topnotch health clubs (“dare 2b different”)\r\nunder the bridge, druids are celebrating deities of porsche and scrap metal \r\n'),(3957,'vroom vroom in the dwarfmobile, deeper and deeper under ground, what would you like today, madam? oh, i will have one trillion coins today, thank you<br>\r\nup, on some different stairs, feed the birds tuppence a bag, feed the rats with wings <br>banned from every single rail station in london<br>\r\non a day like this the city is a hostile boiling cage of compasses and throgmortons<br> (heehawing happily after a good lunch)<br>\r\non a day like this the city doesnt like me and i dont like it back<br>\r\nglass steel concrete concrete steel concrete 17th century christopher wren church glass<br>steel concrete concrete concrete glass<br>\r\nblinded by the neon sign, \\\'welcome to the plantation palace,’ you will never want to leave<br>\r\npast mincing lane where the underwriters are the butchers of today<br>\r\nthrough seething lane and crutched friars into the savage gardens<br>\r\nthis is where yeah thats ok<br>\r\nthis is where oh this gas is bugging me, my stomachs been funny all day, all we had was <br>that pizza last night<br>\r\nthis is where i guess i could<br>\r\nthis is where are we there yet mummy<br>\r\nthis is where your boyfriend said hed text you didnt he i bet you he forgot i bet you he<br> got off with whatshername shes such a bitch she really is are you bovvered<br>\r\nthis is where oh that looks interesting look at those drawings<br>\r\nthis is where you can be so selfish sometimes i never said a word i hate arguing in public<br>\r\nthis is where what this way<br>\r\nthis is where i cant remember<br>\r\nthis is where i love you<br><br>\r\n\r\npine trees in fair street still smell sweetly (secret garden gate, secret garden, unkempt)<br>\r\nwhat does \\\'dare quam accipere’ mean?<br>\r\nthe cat and cucumber café has been closed due to the stupidity of the name. also affected:<br> topnotch health clubs (\\\"dare 2b different\\\")<br>\r\nunder the bridge, druids are celebrating deities of porsche and scrap metal...<br>\r\n'),(3958,'Hi\r\nTo write the letter, it is necessary ...'),(3959,'<p>a day off.  out the front window, i see justin go by, splay-legged, head forward, eyes slightly bugged.  he\\\'s carrying a notebook dense with phone numbers and line drawings and verse, the panoply of his art, his connections, his women, his tricks, his boys, his family here and in new hampshire.  there\\\'s also a calendar in there full of nuggets like:  <b>PEACH SNOPS $12.00</b> and <b>75$ FOR A GUN</b>.<BR><BR>no doubt on a quest for <i>the stickiest of the icky</i>.  they\\\'ve got that whole thing memorized, always using stuff from it:<i><blockquote>i\\\'m just unwindin\\\'.  i\\\'m just gettin\\\' started, bitches.<br><br>now, darkness, the tables have turned!<br><br>fuck yo\\\' couch, nigga!<br><br>(sung) cold-blooded!<br><br>darkness is spreading!<br><br>the milk\\\'s gone bad!</blockquote></i>they were always complimenting people and things by calling them \\\"mad niggerish\\\".  i knew it was getting bad when i started doing it, too.  i almost used it in conversation with miller on the way to tarpon springs, but stopped myself in time.  he gave me a couple of days work last week.  mostly stuff around their office:  raking, washing the boat, helping them work on the bilge pump (me:  Cuss it one time, Jim.  Talk to it.), yardwork at his business partner\\\'s house.<BR><BR>on the way to his house, jim told me about taking \\\"spaniards\\\" to the titty bar, about the one-legged tree trimmer that miller had sponsored the year before (\\\"He had graduated from the actual trimmin\\\', though.  All he did was drive the truck and yell \\\"Woo-Hoo!\\\" when the branches hit the ground.\\\"), about Miller\\\'s using days, two years ago plus, when he would steal from the business to buy coke.  they\\\'re general contractors.<BR><BR>then miller picked me up at six thursday morning, and i couldn\\\'t figure out why until he stopped at the bait store.  i wanted to work on his head.<BR><BR>for the next three days (thursday, friday, monday), i worked for misty, the woman who lives next to their office.  miller had told me all i knew about her, that she was an excellent cook and was looking to join the crew of a boat at least 43 feet long.  for the high season.<BR><BR>she was in her late fifties, bony thin, blonde, looked a little like a rabbit.  at nine, after i\\\'d been raking for two hours, she came out and asked if i\\\'d eaten anything that morning.<BR><BR>\\\"at five o\\\'clock.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"oh, goodness.  well, let me put together a little something.\\\"<BR><BR>ten minutes later, she called me up to the porch:  cafe au lait, homemade poppyseed bread and biscotti, strawberries.  i thought i\\\'d died and gone to heaven.  she told me she\\\'d moved down from ithaca four months ago, had curated here and in europe and south america, had worked with indian tribes in venezuela and brazil, acquiring folk art and textiles and helping them market same.<BR><BR>i told her about getting my mcse and working a contract job for a month in tampa, how i probably wouldn\\\'t go back into that, that it didn\\\'t seem challenging enough.  i told her i write, that i want to be a professional writer.<BR><BR>she gave me a long look and said, \\\"i know several very well-known writers.  i get to read things before they\\\'re published.\\\"<BR><BR>she went in the house and came back out with a four inch-thick paperback.  it wasn\\\'t like any book i\\\'d ever seen:  there wasn\\\'t any cover art, just fairly nondescript type that declared that what i was holding were the (un?)corrected proofs of <i>Until I Find You</i> by john irving, scheduled publication date July, 2005.<BR><BR>i said, \\\"are these galleys?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"mm-hmm.  he\\\'s getting more loquacious as the years go.\\\"<BR><BR>she said john and her \\\"companion for many years\\\" (\\\"<I>The relationship was platonic.</I>\\\") spent their third year of college together in austria.<BR><BR>\\\"oh, yeah?  that\\\'s where <i>Setting Free the Bears</i> came from, huh?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"mm-hmm.\\\"<BR><BR>she also knows allison lurie and amy tan.  knew lawrence durrell when she lived in england.  through his daughter.  that\\\'s what she says, anyway.  for good measure, just in case, i took my shirt off when i went back to raking.  the sun was coming out, and i thought that maybe she hadn\\\'t seen a well-built young man up close in awhile.  couldn\\\'t hurt.<BR><BR>so, saturday night, i\\\'m at miller\\\'s watching the ncaa semifinals, and he asks if she\\\'s had me work on anything inside yet.<BR><BR>\\\"no, all that stuff\\\'s scheduled for monday.  gotta hang some doors, fix the toilet, work on her computer, put in a cat door.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah.  uh-huh.\\\"<BR><BR>he told how she had had him doing a few things, mostly putting traps in the attic for the squirrels, and how on a break, in the kitchen, he was enjoying a glass of orange juice, and she came over to him and took the liberty of putting her hand hand on his crotch.  and that was evidently all she wrote.  he said they got together a few more times, but she turned out to be too demanding.<BR><BR>i said, \\\"that\\\'s <u>quite</u> alright.  i\\\'m not into older women.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you know, ben franklin\\\'s advice on the subject was that women age from the top down.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"have you <u>seen</u> pictures of benjamin franklin?  motherfucker couldn\\\'t afford to be choosy.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"don\\\'t kid yourself.  he was one of the most powerful men in the world.\\\"<BR><BR>misty told me she had two vertebrae fusing in her neck and couldn\\\'t lift anything heavy, so she had asked a friend of hers to come over monday morning and help me move some furniture.  miller and i pulled up that morning, and the two of them were on misty\\\'s porch.  the friend was a woman in her late forties, maybe.  miller said, \\\"you play your cards right, and you could end up bein\\\' the pivot boy.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah.  i\\\'d be battin\\\' cleanup.  be like, <i>Excuse me, this is where I step in.</i>  pass.\\\"<BR><BR>a break at ten, biscotti and coffee, talking about ruskin and morris and rosetti and frank lloyd wright.  collaborative art.  communal living.  it was so nice.<BR><BR>nancy leaves, and misty takes me to a cuban diner for lunch.  we discuss mali, west african music, tom waits, the writing life.  she tells me i should finish my bachelor\\\'s anywhere at all then try to get into a good writing program.  she says she knows everybody in the department at cornell.  but will be happy to read anything i\\\'d like to show her in the meantime.  and would like to have me back soon to teach her how to use the computer.<BR><BR>ching ching.</p>'),(3960,'Good site. Me very much has liked.'),(3961,'hi\r\nWhy I can not insert the image into my message?'),(3962,'hi\r\nPrompt how to get rid of advertising?'),(3963,'Hallo you! Great web site. I found exactly the information I was looking for. I will recommend your page to all my\r\nfriends!'),(3964,''),(3966,'<pre>i always remember you\r\nbut life is a series of obstacles.\r\nmontreal was full of things i wanted to take home\r\nso i concentrated on taking photos\r\nwith my mind\\\'s eye instead\r\ni would say i miss you\r\nthat your paintings make me ache\r\nbut wordboats are too shallow\r\nto carry everything i feel</pre>'),(3965,' Go south to TJ!'),(3967,'A few friends and myself had traveled the few hours to from the Bay Area to Las Vegas, where we had reserved a hotel room at the Imperial Palace Hotel and Casino. Since I was not driving, the festivities began at our local watering hole several hours prior to the trip. Of course, my favorite beverage, whiskey, was liberally consumed. Before we hopped on I-580 for the trip, we decided it would be a good idea to swing by Taco Bell. Already very drunk at this point, I chowed down four beef-and-bean burritos in the parking lot.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIn retrospect, this was a bad idea.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe got to the hotel, checked into our room, and immediately went to the casino floor for some blackjack. For once, I was actually doing pretty well, even though I was again consuming surprisingly large amounts of whiskey while gambling.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd then it hit me. Something was afoul in my intestines. It was not happy, and it demanded immediate release. I tried ignoring it for a few minutes, and it subsided. And then it came back. Nature was screaming, and would not be ignored.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI stood up and it hit me even harder. I shoveled my chips into my pocket while squeezing my cheeks together and did the old bunny-hop to the nearest men\\\'s room. I got into a stall and dropped my pants while simultaneously squatting. Unfortunately, quite a bit of the nastiest poo my body had ever released got all over my boxers. Shit!<br><br>\r\nTen minutes later, it was over. I looked at my soiled undergarments and realized that it was best to abandon them next to the toilet. I threw them to the side, used half a roll of toilet paper to clean up, and walked back to the blackjack table.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen I sat down, it looked like my chip stack was significantly smaller than what I had left the table with. I chalked it up to being intoxicated and not paying attention, and I continued to play. I ended up winning about $150. Not bad. On my way back to the room, I thought I saw several of the employees pointing at me and suppressing giggles with their hands. Again, I assumed it was the whiskey. I went to my room and passed out.<br><br>\r\n\r\nThe next morning, our designated driver was in much worse shape than I was, so I drove. Halfway home, I was pulled over by a state trooper. For the life of me, I couldn\\\'t find my license. He forgave the speeding, but ticketed me for failure to produce a driver\\\'s license. There went my winnings. Shit!<br><br>\r\nA week and a half later, a random package arrived in the mail. It was from the Imperial Palace. Inside the package was my driver\\\'s license, a cashiers check for $93.12 (the balance of the money I had lost, minus shipping costs) and, no joke, a brand new pair of boxers.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo lets recap. Apparently, when I slipped down my pants in the bathroom, my driver\\\'s license (which had been removed from my wallet when I was carded at the blackjack table) and $100.00 in chips had fallen out of my pocket. I likely then threw my boxers right on top of the chips and my license. So a member of the Imperial Palace staff must have found all of this and relayed this story to other members of the staff.<br><br>\r\nAlthough I doubt I will ever summon the courage to return to the Imperial Palace, I must commend them for their service.'),(3968,'Greetings to all! Excuse for this message, but at you excellent design of a site! Very much it was pleasant to me, I shall come here very often!'),(3969,'<p>saturday night, miller and i and some other program people went to tarpon springs to eat at a little greek place off the strip then to The Dry Dock for the eight o\\\'clock meeting.  coffee afterwards, home by ten-thirty.<BR><BR>when i got out of miller\\\'s truck, i heard some norteño in two-four time coming from around the side of the house.  from a distance, or through concrete block, you can hear only the bassline, so it sounds like an oompah band or circus.  it\\\'s pretty much all they play, so there\\\'s always a festive atmosphere around ye olde hacienda.<BR><BR>abenicio and another latino guy were sitting in lawn chairs in aben\\\'s carless driveway like a couple of crackers.  there was a half-empty case of modelo negro between them.  i sat on my haunches and talked with them for awhile.<BR><BR>it didn\\\'t take long to ascertain that this other guy was shit-faced.  aben was sipping, slow-walking it, but his friend was fucking pounding them.  aben introduced me as mateo.  to roberto.  beto.  from chiapas.<BR><BR>i had a hard time understanding him.  we talked about the <i>ruinas</i> at chetumal, about the people i knew who lived in belize, in consejo, right across the border, about the treacherous countryside around there, the bandits.<BR><BR>after about ten minutes, beto evidently forgot my name and started calling me \\\"armando\\\".  i decided i could live with that and didn\\\'t correct him.  and then half an hour later, we all just started calling each other \\\"heuvos\\\", which is bound to happen.  \\\"sí, heuvos.\\\" and \\\"no, heuvos.\\\"  it doesn\\\'t get much more macho than that.<BR><BR>and of course the conversation eventually drifted around to <i>el subcomandante marcos</i>.  i said that i thought it was funny that he called himself the <u>sub</U>comandante.  beto said, <i>well, you know, all these modern revolutionaries have a sense of humor.</I>  i replied that i thought that it was always that way.<BR><BR>and then the kitchen door sprang open and aben\\\'s son, two years old, bounded out wearing a shoebox on his head.  crossways, so that he looked kind of like napoleon.  the men glanced at him and wrote it off, i guess.  i said, <i>i like your hat, little brother.</i>  he just looked at me.  didn\\\'t say anything.  i think he\\\'s scared of me.  he asked his dad for a sip of beer.<BR><BR>beto had offered me a beer when i first hunkered down with them, but aben explained the situation.  after an hour, i decided i\\\'d had enough and bid them good night.  i went back around to my side of the duplex and sat on the step for awhile and watched the clouds and the palm trees and the full moon.</p>'),(3970,'1:15pm.<br><br> My cell-phone alarm goes off to rouse me from my afternoon nap, and I quickly silence it, before anyone in the adjacent office wonders why a reville-playing alarm goes off at about the same time each day right after the lunch-hour. I collect my thoughts and smooth out my pillow-hair as I fold up the insulating foam I use as a pillow and toss it into the cabinet, put on my glasses, align my work ID. I yawn and walk out to the elevator, where it dings before I can even press the button. Someone gets off and I get on. Nice timing. <br><br>\r\n\r\nI ride 7 floors down to the lobby, where I purchase a bottle of Diet Dr. Pepper from the vending machine. I walk back to the elevator, ding, it opens before I can press the button, same person gets off as got off a few moments earlier. Sweet. I ride up 7 floors and survey the tiny bubbles that have formed in the neck of my bottle of Diet Dr. Pepper. I compare them to bubbles in champagne, or beer. I decide I wish it was a bottle of <i>The Champagne of Beers</i> instead of Diet Dr. Pepper. I curse Newark for being a relatively dry town; some days, you just need a drink at lunch; not like my old job, where there was a bodega on every corner. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nI get off elevator and walk into my office, where I discover a giant, shiny black refrigerator in the place where the server cluster usually sits. Woah. I look around, startled. The phone on the wall has been replaced by a pink-and-teal neon sign shaped like a palm tree, bearing the words <i>\\\"Miami Vice, Y\\\'all!\\\"</i>. I am confused. I look down at the floor and its covered with cherries... I can hear a coworker out in the hall coming in to invite me to a clambake.<br><br> \r\n\r\nThen it happens. Reville! My cell phone alarm goes off to rouse me from my afternoon nap, and I quickly silence it, before anyone in the adjacent offices wonders why a reville-playing alarm goes off at about the same time each day right after the lunch-hour. I collect my thoughts and smooth out my pillow-hair as I fold up the insulating foam I use as a pillow and toss it into the cabinet. I yawn and walk out to the elevator to go downstairs to get a Diet Dr. Pepper.'),(3971,'they say that asians lack some sort of enzyme necessary to break down alcohol. i tend to think this is true, if only because i am victim to the \\\'asian flush\\\' and i spent more than one saturday night/sunday morning in college bowing down before the porcelain gods. and yet i find that i am often at my most lucid when slightly drunk, teetering on the edge of warm-witty-thoughtful and slobbering fool. <br><br>\r\n\r\nright now, for example: home at my parents\\\' house for a week, where it is frequently cocktail hour. there\\\'s a real liv! blue jay at the bird feeder and the magnolia tree has started to bud. drinking some wine with my dad, eating chips and cheese (yes, we are southerners) and, miraculously, it happens. <br><br>\r\n\r\ndon\\\'t care if i don\\\'t get my phd, don\\\'t care if i dont get a 4.0, don\\\'t care if my relationship spontaneously combusts and i am on my own, again. don\\\'t care if i\\\'m not smart enough, if my hips are too wide for my jeans, if i fall off my skateboard all the time. what matters: nighthawks, good food, bffs, my God, patsy cline, the strain of my moist inner thigh.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni\\\'m riding the sweet subtle virginia wave away. from what? \r\n\r\n'),(3972,'<P><I>\\\"Dying in our sleep is a luxury our kind is rarely afforded.\\\"</I><BR>--Elle, <I>Kill Bill</I>, (2003)</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Esquivel von Radoulavich\r\n<BR>Radavasquez Madagascar IXXIV\r\n<BR>Who loved to surf,\r\n<BR>You are gone to the oven.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Burn there like you did in your short time.\r\n<BR>You lost your life like so many brave souls\r\n<BR>At Khe San, at Lan Doc, \r\n<BR>at apartments in the Mission.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Floor sea wanderer,\r\n<BR>Closet forest visitor,\r\n<BR>Night watchman.\r\n<BR>You wore your black fur like a ninja.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>In the closet you waited, \r\n<BR>Under the bed you hid,\r\n<BR>At the toes you struck,\r\n<BR>Await for cats, you ambushed.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Warlord of the pre-dawn early morn,\r\n<BR>Born of the meanest streets:\r\n<BR>The cage town, the asylum, \r\n<BR>The SPCA.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>You escaped the oven that time\r\n<BR>To achieve great things.\r\n<BR>Domination. Respect. Love.\r\n<BR>... In your short life.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>SO LET FLY YOU BLACK CAT!\r\n<BR>O’er the couch, under the bed, \r\n<BR>Out of the closet,\r\n<BR>And into the fire!</P>\r\n<BR><BR><BR><BR><BR>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://elly.org/this/older/2006/05/esquivel.php\\\"><img src=\\\"http://elly.org/days/2006/05-23-2006/youmissthekitties/esquivelsears.jpg\\\" width=200></A>'),(3973,'<PRE>\r\ni always used to wake up here\r\nwith a start, gasping\r\nmy dreams\r\nslain where they lay\r\nand bleeding all over the steering wheel\r\nas i drove screechingly home\r\n\r\nand still you were beside me\r\nyour memory sat quiet and unwavering \r\nwatching me from the passenger’s seat\r\n\r\nnow,\r\nthere is no home to screech to\r\nmy anchor cut,\r\nthe belly of my hold emptied\r\ninto a sea i used to trawl comfortably\r\nbut now surges choppy beneath me\r\nas i scan a murky sky for bearings\r\n\r\nand still you are beside me\r\n\r\ni still wake up sharply\r\nstung by the whiteness of uncurtained sunlight\r\nalways too early\r\nblinking\r\nnaked\r\noverexposed\r\n\r\nand your blue eyes open beside me\r\nand there’s nothing you do not see\r\n\r\nand still you stay.\r\n</PRE>'),(3974,'<p>Have you ever noticed that sometimes you will be driving your car, or in the supermarket, or somewhere else totally anonymous, and you\\\'ll just look at someone, and they\\\'ll be looking back?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m sure you have.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I value that funny feeling you get when you suddenly look away, and you realize that was \\\"weird\\\". I\\\'m sure the other person feels the same way. But what is that? Why are we conditioned to feel uncomfortable when a person we don\\\'t know makes contact with us when we don\\\'t expect it?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Another thing is when you have something strange and seemingly unique in common with another person. For instance, a very strange and specific obsessive compulsive disorder. Or a few strands of grey hair behind your ear in exactly the same place. Or you both always look at the clock at 11:11 PM. You hang on to that. You like it. It\\\'s like having someone who \\\"gets\\\" you in that special way.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s just good. But why does making direct contact with an accidental stranger make you feel uncomfortable, but making subtle accidental contact with a person you know makes you feel good?</p>'),(3975,'<p>\r\nI spent a good part of my morning reading through some of my older swintries. It was interesting to read them and see the comments; jokes, jabs, and friendly support.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI wonder where some of the people who used to swinney.org are today? From what I can tell, swinney.org was a place I came when I wanted to complain, gripe, force depression onto others, or make a rare joke. I think perhaps the only posts I still like are the IKEA and dream posts.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSwinney.org is a place I can always come to check up on the past and I like that a lot. It is strange that you can come to swinney.org and see people who were your friend and know that some time after that post was written those people don’t like you anymore. That is perhaps the lasting beauty of swinney.org\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI have changed since my first post. I use paragraph breaks, I try to use correct spelling, and I try to be consistent with capitalization. The life I lived in Toronto and Ottawa is much different from the life I live in Korea. Nevertheless, I have tentatively decided that the end of Korea is near, and that it is time to move on and experience the culture and everyday routine of another country. That might be a good reason to explain an absence of old swinites from swinney.org; moving toward other things. \r\n</p>\r\n'),(3976,'I need to read some William S. Burroughs.  I\\\'d thought of that before, and I\\\'m being reminded of that now, when I look around.\r\n\r\nLast night I had a hell of an adventure.  On a three-day drinking binge to end the workweek.  I\\\'m not sure what I was looking for.  And I don\\\'t like the drinking.  But I do like the desire and willingness to let myself go.\r\n\r\nSo I was sitting on my back steps, pretty much gone.  Head between my knees.  Not sick.  But not in my \\\'right mind,\\\' either.  Drunk on a big bottle of wine and much of a little, and having just smoked a joint.  Head between my knees, I listened to the birds, and asked them to let me become one of them.  And they did.  And once I was part of them, a cat wanted to explore me.  She walked towards me and I couldn\\\'t see her because the railing of my steps blocked my vision.\r\n\r\nPlus, my attention had become focused on Adam.  He was talking with some girl.  He lives two doors down and just around a bush.  Like the prairie dogs in my back yard.  It was Adam\\\'s black cat, and she served to introduce us.\r\n\r\nI\\\'m not sure if meeting Adam is good or bad.  I woke up this morning with a hangover, and off my normal sleep schedule, and a little bit afraid of a bunch of things.  Backsliding into drinking, for one.  Not feeling like I\\\'m taking my job seriously enough recently, for another.  And my horoscope this morning warns me to \\\'be careful of the company I keep.\\\'  Adam is in the art world and leads a loose and free life, and he\\\'d be a fine enabler of early-20\\\'s behavior.  I\\\'m 31.\r\n\r\nBut part of me thinks it\\\'s all for the best.  How could it be otherwise in a benevolent universe?  That\\\'s what I wondered when I wandered into Adam\\\'s house, invited by his cat, and met him and Sherry.   I was flying high, and risked being totally myself.  I had wine stains on my shirt and Adam\\\'s place was nice.  Much, much nicer than mine.  He and Sherry were curious about what my place looked like, and I ended up taking Adam inside for a look, even though my place was an absolute wreck.  It\\\'s disordered normally, but after a three-day binge there are bottles and boxes and crumbs.\r\n\r\nI fear the lack of structure in my life.  But I\\\'m proud of the courage to let go, let a stranger -- a NEIGHBOR, even -- in my messy house.  No one\\\'s mom would ever do that.  I haven\\\'t practiced my karate on my own time in two weeks.  And I need to find my niche in my job (and it\\\'s a tough job), and perform consistently there.  But my second-favorite movie is \\\'Fight Club,\\\' and one of its central tenets is to have the courage to let go.  \r\n\r\nSo I have work to do, and sober responsibilities to face, certainly.  But at the same time, I need to not panic and grasp gasping for the structure, but embrace the uncertainty instead.\r\n\r\nAnd if I feel like writing about all of this instead of doing some cleaning first, that\\\'s ok, too.\r\n\r\nAnd I don\\\'t know where William S. Burroughs fits into all of this, but maybe I\\\'ll figure that out as time goes by, and I go with it.\r\n\r\n'),(3977,'I need to read some William S. Burroughs.  I\\\'d thought of that before, and I\\\'m being reminded of that now, when I look around.<br><br>\r\n\r\nLast night I had a hell of an adventure.  On a three-day drinking binge to end the workweek.  I\\\'m not sure what I was looking for.  And I don\\\'t like the drinking.  But I do like the desire and willingness to let myself go.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSo I was sitting on my back steps, pretty much gone.  Head between my knees.  Not sick.  But not in my \\\'right mind,\\\' either.  Drunk on a big bottle of wine and much of a little, and having just smoked a joint.  Head between my knees, I listened to the birds, and asked them to let me become one of them.  And they did.  And once I was part of them, a cat wanted to explore me.  She walked towards me and I couldn\\\'t see her because the railing of my steps blocked my vision.<br><br>\r\n\r\nPlus, my attention had become focused on Adam.  He was talking with some girl.  He lives two doors down and just around a bush.  Like the prairie dogs in my back yard.  It was Adam\\\'s black cat, and she served to introduce us.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m not sure if meeting Adam is good or bad.  I woke up this morning with a hangover, and off my normal sleep schedule, and a little bit afraid of a bunch of things.  Backsliding into drinking, for one.  Not feeling like I\\\'m taking my job seriously enough recently, for another.  And my horoscope this morning warns me to \\\'be careful of the company I keep.\\\'  Adam is in the art world and leads a loose and free life, and he\\\'d be a fine enabler of early-20\\\'s behavior.  I\\\'m 31.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBut part of me thinks it\\\'s all for the best.  How could it be otherwise in a benevolent universe?  That\\\'s what I wondered when I wandered into Adam\\\'s house, invited by his cat, and met him and Sherry.   I was flying high, and risked being totally myself.  I had wine stains on my shirt and Adam\\\'s place was nice.  Much, much nicer than mine.  He and Sherry were curious about what my place looked like, and I ended up taking Adam inside for a look, even though my place was an absolute wreck.  It\\\'s disordered normally, but after a three-day binge there are bottles and boxes and crumbs.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI fear the lack of structure in my life.  But I\\\'m proud of the courage to let go, let a stranger -- a NEIGHBOR, even -- in my messy house.  No one\\\'s mom would ever do that.  I haven\\\'t practiced my karate on my own time in two weeks.  And I need to find my niche in my job (and it\\\'s a tough job), and perform consistently there.  But my second-favorite movie is \\\'Fight Club,\\\' and one of its central tenets is to have the courage to let go. <br><br> \r\n\r\nSo I have work to do, and sober responsibilities to face, certainly.  But at the same time, I need to not panic and grasp gasping for the structure, but embrace the uncertainty instead.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd if I feel like writing about all of this instead of doing some cleaning first, that\\\'s ok, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd I don\\\'t know where William S. Burroughs fits into all of this, but maybe I\\\'ll figure that out as time goes by, and I go with it.\r\n'),(3978,'i\\\'m not there for you<br>\r\nand i have proven that<br>\r\ni may never be<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut<br><br>\r\n\r\ni will rage against<br>\r\nthose that have shouldered your burden<br>\r\nunasking<br><br>\r\n\r\nfor it was my wing<br>\r\nyou were to run to<br>\r\neven if i held it <br>\r\nclosed<br>\r\nagainst your questing hand<br><br>\r\n\r\nit would be better to<br>\r\nwatch you shiver next to me<br>\r\nthan to see you warm<br>\r\nwith someone else.<br>'),(3979,'<p>if i could, i\\\'d put out a photocopied newsletter about once a week, just a one-page thing i\\\'d hand out on street corners.  but i\\\'m not about to go to all that trouble, so i\\\'ll just whip this out instead.  i\\\'d call the newsletter <B>what the hell, people?<BR><BR>what the hell, people?</b>  sometime monday afternoon, it was decided that city hall and the library would be closed \\\"for the hurricane\\\".  i saw the head librarian downstairs behind the counter demonstrating her \\\"hurricane dance\\\".  and then the buses were running yesterday.  and the physical plant employees had to work, driving their trucks all over town.  because all \\\"the hurricane\\\" did was blow a little moss out of the trees, basically.  so the message here is that if you have a desk job, a little wind is too dangerous for you, but if you\\\'re a grunt, tough cookies?  bullshit.<BR><BR><b>what the hell, people?</B>  or, more specifically, <b>what the hell, michael romano?</b>  when my landlord renewed the lease, he jacked the rent up thirty bucks a month but told me if i mowed the yard every couple of weeks, he\\\'d give it back to me.  so far (two months later), he hasn\\\'t brought the mower over for me to do it.  grass is about two feet high.  i\\\'m so tempted to call code enforcement on him again.  i ask him about it every once in awhile.  all he does is smile when he takes that extra thirty.<BR><BR><b>what the hell, old ladies?</B>  if you\\\'re going to pull out your eyebrows and draw black lines where they used to be, at least try to approximate the original form and location.  i saw this one woman at the grocery store who had lines starting about an inch north of the bridge of her nose and going out arrow straight toward her temples, parallel with her hairline.  made her look very mean.  which i\\\'m sure she was, anyway.<BR><BR><b>what the hell, Girl with the Old Lady\\\'s Name?</b>  yes.  you.  Delores.  she\\\'s been gaming on me <u>hard</U> at the library for two weeks, then i see her at the park while i\\\'m sitting on the seawall by the river yesterday afternoon.  she pulls in the parking lot, sees me, parks, gets out, places a call on her cell phone, and walks past without looking at me and sits on a bench about fifty feet away.  i don\\\'t bother her.  after half an hour, she leaves, kind of huffily.  and doesn\\\'t speak to me at the library today.  evidently doesn\\\'t understand that she wasn\\\'t the flavor of cheese i was fishing for.  okay to flirt with, but definitely not a pickup.<BR><BR><b>what the hell, Señor?</b>  my neighbor\\\'s son is named Regalio, but i call him Señor.  he\\\'s too little to have a name that big.  i gave him a harmonica, so now i\\\'m the shit.  but he wakes up at the crack of dawn, before the light hits the window, before the chickens, and plays this videotape in his (and his granny\\\'s) bedroom, adjacent to mine.  boogie woogie piano and high-pitched voices singing spanish.  every morning at six ay em.<BR><BR>i know it\\\'s a video because i asked him about it.  he and Abuela were out in the yard the other day, and i took them a couple of homemade grape juice popsicles.  he told me that he likes to name all the animals he sees.  the stray cats, etc.  later that day, he was on the sidewalk in front of my apartment beating hell and guts out of a grasshopper.  i said, <i>What did you name that one, Señor?</i><BR><BR>i think they\\\'re moving soon.<BR><BR><b>what the hell, matt?</b>  i make jokes that very few people get.  i don\\\'t know why.  this morning, i wrote <i>put your sweet lips a little closer to the phone</i> on a bathroom wall.  like to amuse myself, i guess.</P>'),(3980,'deep emerald green waters ... <br>shallow blue waters ..<br>\r\nthe coast of porto cervo ... the laughter of family ..<br>\r\na toast, a dinner .. a coffee .. light showers ..\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe sparkling sun, a crisp cold breeze, <br>summertime in munich, a golden sunset at landshut ...\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ntowering coconut leaves, humid and hot inland .. <br> coolbreeze by the stony shores of boljoon ... <br> our summer house, the kerosene lamps, blackjack, the large reptilian sigh\r\nof our house tuko .. <br> my cousins, my baby sister, the cock-a-doodle doo ..<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe golden sun rays against the ancient structures, <br> the cobblestones, ... humidity abounds while vespas fly across the roundabouts ..<br>\r\nfading shades of late afternoon light, twilight over the aeternal city ..<br>\r\na glimpse of Piazza del Popolo atop the Villa Borghese,<br>\r\noverlooking droves of Romans strolling through the Corso..<br>\r\nan apertif, a dinner, a glass or two of wine.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nanother summer has arrived,<br> while nostsalgic thoughts of ancient cities, tropical islands cloud my heart... <br>\r\ni look forward to a summer here at home \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe temperamental micro climate,<br> sometimes hot, sometimes cold <br> .. a pocket of fog fills the air, the sunset by the bay.<br>\r\nthe skyscrapers, the city noise, the silver bridge, the golden bridge, the steep and rolling hills.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nperhaps tomorrow, maybe day after, a tender wet kiss ..<br>\r\n<br>\r\na summer fling? a summer love? conveniently here at home. '),(3981,'I just went to the corner bodega to get a little hair-of-the-dog and some chrome cleaner to polish up this rockin\\\' retro chair Sine scored for us at the Park Slope fleamarket. While I was in there, this dude came in all tweaking and talking to the fridges and Ho-Ho\\\'s, plantains and pork-rinds and shit, trying to find the beer. I chose <i>Presidente</i>, he, a big 4-0 of the <i>Eight-Double-Oh</i>. <br><br>\r\nHe cut me off at the checkout cause I was too busy trying to talk- in fractured Spanish- to the owner about how the Mets were routing Arizona. Dude tossed down 2$ in change and Nelson was like <i>\\\"Papi, its 2.75$\\\"</i> and the tweeker went apeshit. A lot of neighborhood folks were in there to have a beer, puff a Black & Mild and watch the game, so everyone sorta aped when the dude when chicken oriental, and most looked at me (because, well, they think im a cop- long story for another day). <br><Br>It was obvious that he was on a variety of chemicals (though I cant point the finger- just cause I have prescriptions for mine doesnt really set me that far apart at times), so I just told Nelson I\\\'d pick up dude\\\'s whopping 75 cents, and dude immediately chilled. He was all like hugging me (yay tweaker sweat) and telling me he <i>loved me</i>, giving me fist pounds and saying <i>\\\"You pretty cool for a white guy, even if you *are* a cop!\\\"</i><br><Br>And the funniest part is that if anyone in my hood was a cop, it so <i>totally</i> wouldn\\\'t be me... I mean, for real.'),(3982,'my brother john and i walked out to <a href=\\\"http://www.humboldt1.com/~popenoe/scenes/WeddingRock.htm\\\">wedding rock</a> a few weeks ago to watch the sun set over the pacific.  i hadn\\\'t been out for an ocean sunset since moving to california, and he had never seen the sun drown itself.  it was his last night before returning home to colorado, and it seemed the right way to end his trip.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>while walking to patrick\\\'s point, my phone rang.  strange, because there was usually no signal out there.  it was my other brother, sobbing.  he had been lonely for months; drugs and mental illness separated him from his partner.  he was broken and couldn\\\'t mend.  he had called to say goodbye.  i tried to keep him on the phone; he hung up.  i called our mother (always the fixer, even now) and  she raced to his house.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>john and i climbed the rock, shaken.  we watched the sun fall down through the sky and waited for news.  i thought about icarus and the golden gate jumpers. white horse waves beat on the  black rocks below us.  as the light faded, the sun dropped into the fog bank.  the sphere gave way to an egg.  the egg, burnt orange, broke into an ur-sun - a glowing ziggurat lit by geissler tubes.  a minus sign.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mom called long after the dark had settled.  he was ok.  not ok, but alive.  she\\\'d caught him in the garage duct taping a garden hose to his exhaust pipe.  his dog was sitting in the truck, waiting for a ride.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i described the ziggurat to ollie last week.  he asked me if i\\\'d seen the emerald flash - \\\"you know, when the sun dips and shines through the ocean before it vanishes.\\\"  i had not; it was obscured by humboldt fog. </p>'),(3983,''),(3985,'Seems like everyone is getting pregnant and married and God knows what else. I don\\\'t think any of that is for me just yet. I\\\'m quite satisfied with my cat-son and simply living with my special someone.\r\n\r\nI\\\'m buying a laptop when I get home.'),(3986,'<p>and then during the three and a half-month span that ended with the first of july, we all had birthdays.  everybody but connor.  jesse and justin were nineteen and rob was twenty and i was thirty-six.  and connor was fourteen until december.  on the evening of rob\\\'s birthday, june twenty-ninth,  i broke out the guitar and sang <a href=\\\"http://www.azlyrics.com/lyrics/neilyoung/sugarmountain.html\\\">sugar mountain</a> for him.  he said it was gay.<BR><BR>connor knocked on my bedroom window late one friday night in the middle of june.  i was in bed with the window unit set on sixty-six, reading and dozing and in great danger of waking up at five in the morning with the lamp on beside me, a worthless first edition of <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/0449912000/qid=1152229851/sr=2-1/ref=pd_bbs_b_2_1/103-1086458-0009420?s=books&v=glance&n=283155\\\"><i>my secret history</i></a> lost among the sheets.<br><br>i went into the living room and turned on the porch light and opened the blinds and the jalousies.  connor explained that he was being so diplomatic because he was the only one i didn\\\'t piss off.  i said i wasn\\\'t into that shit no more.  and so on.  he assured me it was all cool and headed across the road toward justin\\\'s.<BR><BR>about seven or eight minutes later, i tried to call him.  his cell phone had been disconnected, and it was too late to call their homes.  where they all lived with mommy.  and had no cell.  except rob.  who had never offered it.<BR><BR>and they don\\\'t get up until noon.  but one o\\\'clock saw me eating in a diner on main, biding my time, faking the funk, and by two-thirty i was walking home with a half-ounce in my shoe.<BR><BR>and it rocked on.  i kept up with the a.a. meetings for a week then abandoned the pretense.  over the course of three days, the rest of the jitterbugs reconciled with me, justin then jesse then rob.  along about then, connor started telling me that they were 