ned haze of leering uncertainty and I\\\'m certain this sense of self loathing is a fairly new development.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSpending my day to day life in a constant state of mild confusion certainly isn\\\'t the most productive use of my time, but time-management never was my forte. I\\\'m more of a procrastinator. The kinda guy who never comes good on promises. The constant underachiever. Must. try. harder. But. Can\\\'t fucking be bothered if I\\\'m honest. Which I\\\'m not. An why is your \\\'girlfriend\\\' naked in your kitchen in the middle of the day anyway. And where do you find women like that. I\\\'m betting that Ben Affleck has them lining up. The cunt.\r\n</p>'),(4069,'I am browsing the photos you have posted on your site. <br>\r\nI pretend that I have a reason to hate you. I also pretend that I’m not affected by your image and that you probably don’t think about me anymore.<br> \r\nI know you do; I’ve read the transcripts of arrogance and bitterness. <br>\r\nI want to show you up. <br>\r\nI want so badly for you to regret everything and compare all to me.<br> \r\nSometimes I want to write you but I am afraid you will see me as transparent and manipulative, desperate and lonely, afraid of the world. <br>\r\nI am not, really not, I just want to be in contact with you somehow.<br> \r\nThis isn’t realistic because I know how you thoroughly amputate people like me from your life. Hell you were almost to the bone when we were still together. <br>\r\nI wish you didn’t see me as some sort of a mistake. I guess that’s it. I don’t look at it that way. <br>\r\nI wish you could be proud of the time you spent with me and take it with you as a memento of things in your life that shaped and influenced you. <br>\r\nI guess I don’t really want to see you. <br>\r\nI want to see the you that is in my dreams and memories and photos. <br>\r\nI miss that person immensely. <br>\r\n'),(4070,'\\\'I can\\\'t go on like this any more.  This feeling, this feeling is horrible. Everybody is gone. I am alone.  There is no future.  I want to just sleep. Disappear. Go away all together. I have no one. Nobody to talk to. To love. To confide in. I am worthless, a waste.  I have no future.  No energy. Nothing.<br><br>\r\n	You have me.<br>\r\n	\\\'You don\\\'t count.\\\'<br>\r\n	I am worth something.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'I have never been so hated by people in my life as I am now.  I can\\\'t speak without offending people.  Hurting people.  I just want to cry.  I want to cry and get all this misery out.  But no matter how much I cry, nothing changes.  I wake up every day and I cry.  The feelings get worse.  I don\\\'t leave  my house.  I don\\\'t pay bills. None of this matters.  Nothing matters, we all just die in the end.\\\'<br><br>\r\n	I don\\\'t hate you.<br>\r\n	\\\'You don\\\'t love me either.\\\'<br>\r\n	True.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'All those that I love have turned their backs on me.  I can\\\'t do this alone.  I can\\\'t bear to see the next day alone.  What have I done to push them away?  I have done nothing but try to be happy and make everybody around me happy as well.  They all leave me.  They abandon me.  Don\\\'t they understand that I need their help? Want their help?  They are the ones who make me who I am.  Make me feel happier.  And they just leave me. Alone.\\\'<br><br>\r\n	I am here.<br>\r\n	\\\'I know.\\\'<br>\r\n	Let me help.<br>\r\n	\\\'I am scared.\\\'<br>\r\n	I know.<br>\r\n	\\\'I don\\\'t know what to do.  I can\\\'t trust my choices any more.\\\'<br>\r\n	You tell me what you want.<br>\r\n	\\\'I don\\\'t know!\\\'<br>\r\n	\\\'I just want the pain to go away.  I want to feel nothing. I want to sleep.\\\'<br>\r\n	Then hold me.  Squeeze me.  Let me kiss you.<br>\r\n	\\\'I can\\\'t.\\\'<br>\r\n	Then leave me.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'Make me feel no pain.  Make the hurt and agony go away.  Make me sleep deeply and peacefully.  Make it so I can hurt nobody again. And nobody can hurt me.\\\'<br>\r\n	Take me.  Squeeze me. .  Feel my cool lips touch you.  Feel my breath on your brow.  <br><br>\r\n	\\\'Wait.\\\'<br>\r\n	It\\\'s too late.<br>\r\n	\\\'NO! Wait! I remember something.  Something nice.\\\'<br>\r\n	What is it?<br>\r\n	\\\'I remember my mother.\\\'<br>\r\n	She is dead.<br>\r\n	\\\'I know, but I remember something nice.\\\'<br>\r\n	What do you remember?<br><br>\r\n	\\\'I remember it was a sunny day.  I was only a kid, probably around 7 or 8.  We were at the beach my whole family.  My father was there.\\\'<br>\r\n	He\\\'s dead.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'My brother was there too.\\\'<br>\r\n	He too is dead.<br>\r\n	\\\'And we were all laughing, smiling and playing in the sand.  There was something particular though.  I remember looking at my mother.  The way the sun hit her.  I think it was getting close to sunset, her skin was golden and her eyes were full of life.  Her hair was blowing in the breeze and she was smiling.  She was smiling at me.  She was looking at me as if nothing else mattered in the world, only that I was happy.  She wanted me to be happy. <br><br>\r\n	\\\'I was asking her inane questions about the sand, quantity of the sand.  You know, all the \\\'Why is the sky blue?\\\' type of questions.  And with every question I asked her, she had an answer.  She never tired of my questions.  Some times her answers were silly, other times they were simple, but never were they impatient.  Each answer invited another question.  Then my father joined in with the answers, helping mom out.  My father knew the answers to everything.  Towards the end of the questions and answers my mother said to me, \\\'Son, someday you will be a great man, just like your father.  Someday you will be whatever you want to be.\\\'  At the time, I think I wanted to be an astronaut, or was it the president of the United States?<br><br>\r\n	\\\'My father was always big and strong. He taught me how to use tools properly.  Taught me how to shoot guns. Drive a car. And even taught me how to ride a bike.  The day I learned how to ride a bike was one of the best days of my life.  It had to have been a weekend, my father and I spent the whole day trying to teach me how to ride a bike without the training wheels.  He\\\'d push me, I\\\'d fall, over and over again.  I wanted to give up but he wouldn\\\'t let me.  If we got too far down the road he would push me and the bike up the hill.  Then at the top of the hill he would pick up the bike, with me on it, and turn it around like it was nothing.  We start the whole process over again until finally, I figured it out and could ride a bike.  Once that happened, he chased me all over the place to try to keep me from crashing in to cars, running over a cliff and running into trees.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'I remember my brother.  My older brother was always looking out for me.  Keeping the bullies away from me.  He was always by my side.  He was my best friend.  Nothing ever came between him and me.  The best times we had were as kids.  Nothing else mattered in the world.  We would make forts in our room.  Play army against an endless assault of invisible enemies.  He was Han, I was Luke and together we would fight to get out of the trash compactor, which would usually be our bathtub.  We had adventures together that Indiana Jones could only dream of.  Nobody could best my brother at any sport. Nor could anybody break the bond between us.\\\'<br><br>\r\n	\\\'Do you feel better now?<br>\r\n	\\\'Yes. Yes I do.\\\'<br>\r\n	Good.<br>\r\n	\\\'I realize now that I can change things.  I can make my life better. It will be hard but I know deep down that my family would not want me to wallow like I have been.\\\'<br><br>\r\n	It\\\'s too late.<br><br>\r\n	\\\'Why? You don\\\'t think I can just put you down, walk out that door and get my life in order now?  You think that I can\\\'t fight for what I think is right now?  My memories of my family, the happy times the times filled with love and understanding.  I now realize what I haven\\\'t realized before.  I can make this happen.  I can become happy again.  I can get help.  I might not even need help.  I can feel the surge of power and motivation lift me up.  I am even smiling now.  I see the images of my family and I am smiling! Nothing can stop me from being happy again.  I was happy before, I can be happy again.\\\'<br><br>\r\n	It\\\'s too late.<br>\r\n	\\\'Why? Why do you keep saying that?\\\'<br><br>\r\n	I say that because I already blew you a kiss.\r\n'),(4071,'<p>On the highway outside my apartment a steady stream of cars and trucks race back and forth to the edges of the earth. When they reach the end of the road I imagine they float heavily off into space. The drivers never taking their foot of the gas.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I open my door and watch them speed through my life. Half second hellos followed by half second goodbyes. I spy on my neighbors across the street and eye them with disdain and distrust. We are two islands separated by an ocean of traffic. The naked punks in the old purple house stand smoking on the balcony, balls to the wind, oblivious to my suspicion. The old drunks slip into their basement apartments, their bodies carrying a heavy grey that pushes down on their backs and blends into the overcast sky.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Each car that passes is like a crashing wave. My front yard has become a beach, filled with the debris of beer cans and fast food wrappers that have washed ashore. The trucks that roar down the street are perfect storms that swallow cars and spit them out onto lands I will never see. The fumes seep into my head and I drift hazily back inside.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Finally, I hear a thunderous bang. I run outside and see a two-door sedan wrapped around a tree directly outside my house. The highway shuts down and everyone comes pouring out of their homes and cars. We all stand in stupid wonder and carefully creep toward the twisted metal coffin to catch a glimpse of this wrecked wave.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The naked punks, the bull dykes and the old drunks all come closer, chattering to each other in guilty excitement, as if to absolve themselves from this sin. Inside the car are two women’s bodies, each mangled and melded into the metal. The driver is slumped over, one arm hanging outside the window, head bleeding. Beside her is a younger woman, her face pointing to the sky, as if pleading to the gods. I stand and stare.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Parts of the car are strewn along front lawns up and down the street. Bumpers, hubcaps and headlights have been thrown from their frame. I begin to collect the pieces into a pile - as some sort of sombre ceremony. Suddenly a cop yells at me to put them down, \\\"This is a crime scene. Don\\\'t touch anything.\\\" I put down a hubcap and go talk to some of the other drivers. Like broken records, they repeat over and over again, “Did you see what happened?”</p>\r\n\r\n<p>“She popped out of a side street and wasn’t looking and this truck is coming down the street and just fucking nails her and then she’s out,” says a suit-and-tie business man in wild-eyed frenzy. “I mean right fucking out. And she goes ripping down the street and she’s clipping trees one-by-one. I follow her about 10 blocks and then finally ‘boom!’ She’s done.”</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The fire trucks come blazing in and the firemen pull and plow at the car with axes but are unable to set the women free. It takes 15 of them with the Jaws of Life to finally cut through this beast and pull the limp bodies loose. Blood is all over their hair and clothes. I want to get closer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Finally the police officers push everyone away and I watch the show at a distance. The bodies get carried away into ambulances. I talk with my neighbours about the accident. We all agree they will die. We all agree that that is tragic. We are all taking to each other for the first time. And we will never talk to each other again. When the action dies down, everyone drifts lazily back inside.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At night I go to the Mexican stand on Commercial for a burrito and dream about living in Mexico with girls who probably dream about living in Canada. On my way back I walk through the middle of the closed off street. The cop guarding the highway stops me and we talk about the women’s chances. The car still hasn’t been towed away and sits there as a deformed and disfigured reminder. I go take a better look. Standing beside the car is an old drunk in a Blue Jays shirt who lives across the street from me. He tells me it’s a shame to leave the wreck there. I ask him how he thinks the Jays will do this year. He says he’s from Newfoundland.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For the first time since I had moved here, the street falls to silence. For the first time there\\\'s no cars screaming violence and I walk freely on top of this concrete ocean that had always threatened to drown me. I tell you, I actually hoped those women would die that night. I thought it would teach all those speeding cars a lesson. I hope it would shutdown this wretched highway for good. And I guess I thought it would give me a better story to tell at work in the morning. Now you tell me, what kind of peace is that?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There\\\'s not a single sounds for the rest of the night. I look out my window and say goodnight to the disfigured statue. At some point, quietly, it gets taken away. At around 5 a.m., the first group of grogy workers begin their journey to work; oblivious to the destruction that laid before them; never taking their foot off the gas. I awake to the soothing sound of waves warmly splashing up on my beach. I stretch my body across my bed, look at the clock and drift easily back to sleep.</p>\r\n'),(4072,'<p>\r\nwhen i think back now, and realize how young we were, i am stunned with the brilliance of the match, amazed by the grace granted that kept us together.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nsomeone asked me today how i knew he was the right one, i was only eighteen, how could i possibly have known?\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s been thirty five years, so long ago, but my memory of meeting him is sharp, almost like it was yesterday.  he was tall, and impossibly skinny, wearing worn blue jeans, scuffed acme cowboy boots, and a tan shirt.  his long brown hair was as scruffy as his boots, wavy and tucked behind his ears.  his eyes were so green, and when he saw me, really saw me, the color seemed to change to a deep grey. portent of things to come, for today those eyes are as blue as the sky, and it doesn\\\'t surprise me much, as he has ever been a chameleon.\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhow did i know? there was never a question of it, from the moment our eyes locked, it was a foregone conclusion.  i have a memory of shock.  not bells or fireworks, or anything like that, but a sudden knowledge that, from this moment on, we belonged to each other.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnot love at first sight, and not sexual awareness, although there was that, too, but just this sudden knowing, and surprise that it was possible, for he was nothing like any boy or man i had ever been attracted to in the past.\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin that moment, i learned what it meant to leave behind pieces of myself that had been, and build new pieces to fit the puzzle that was us.\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhen i look at him, i am often surprised to see that he is a man, middle aged and beginning to paunch a bit, hair thinning, moustache sprinkled with grey and gold, the laugh crinkles at his eyes so deeply embedded now.\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbecause, in my mind, especially when he is not with me, i still see that boy, my boy, and i am still reeling from that certain knowledge that we belonged together.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nto be adored.  that is the greatest thing of all.  we adore each other.\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n'),(4073,'<p>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for the last four years, i\\\'ve been on a board of directors for a not-for-profit corporation.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m actually quite brilliant in some ways. i have talents that the organization needed, talents