ce man sweep garbage into a dustpan fashioned from a large rectangular can.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n“Sookyyyyyyyyy, it’s time to wake up! Wakey wakey!” as I left the bathroom; my eyes fixated on the half finished cup of coffee on the desk. Knowing my breath smelled like morning smoke, I took a few sips of coffee and swished it around in my mouth hoping to minimize the smell before my morning mission to wake Sooky up. No can do. It’s early and she’s tired. I can understand. Monday is a bitch, and work is work. I slide back out of bed feeling somewhat defeated and before heading back to the bathroom window I tune into the bbc news; turning up the volume to wake up levels. As I inhale and exhale nicotine goodness, Sooky cheerfully calls out good morning and I crane my neck to see her sitting on the edge of the bed sipping on the mug of illy caffeine goodness that I had made earlier.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe get ready for work and part ways in the driveway. I head to the subway, while she heads for the bus. I light another mild seven and send short text messages to the woman I love so much. I pass men in suits and wonder what time they’d woken up, schoolboys and girls standing on corners waiting for friends, and old women carrying bags of vegetables. I use the bathroom of an office tower; entering through the back. The man at the security desk glances at me briefly. I make no indication I notice his existence.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs I emerge from the front of the office tower I am confronted with a consistent wave of office workers heading toward me. Some looking fresh faced and raring to go, while others look blank and somber. Commuters are a boring breed. We simply deal with it because we have to. There are those of us who simply head to work with nothing in our ears, and nothing to watch, while others pass the time listening to music on their mp3 players or staring at visual media on their DMB phones, PSPs, and other audio DMB/DIVX players. I dislike the visual commuters. They walk slower, don’t pay attention to where they are going, and are constantly distracted by what they are watching. They annoy me in some subtle way.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI travel 13 stations north to get to work. The commute is boring and I don’t usually get a seat until the seventh stop. After the seventh, eighth and ninth stations, the train is pretty empty and I can stretch my legs. For the most part, the remainders of the passengers left on the train get off at my station. We rush up the stairs, through the turnstile, and out exit two. Half run to get across the road before the light turns, and the rest of us make our way up ‘institute hill’.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI get to the office and press power on the computer. While the slow piece of shit with two cdroms boots up, I clean my mug and fill the humidifier. I log into windows, startup firefox, log into icb, nate, gmail, and check my outlook. With nothing to edit I begin reading the news and get settled into my day of not really doing anything.\r\n</p>'),(4384,'I’m nearing hour 60 something this week and I can’t explain how tired I am. They have gone back to Taipei today and left us an impossible list of tasks given the time we have to get them done. I fear that if we don’t get this thing out the door on time, I and many others won’t have a job that much longer. It’s all about the tools they need for tuning, the API’s they have asked for, and the tests I will run. <br><Br>\r\n\r\nLast night I had a dream I found two small orange kittens, their names were Sharpness and Noise Reduction respectively. Noise Reduction didn’t play much of a role, but I spent the better part of my dream trying to save or find Sharpness. At one point someone ran her over with a car and one side of her was left with missing fur and road rash. I remember her falling into some kind of tiered grate and having to reach down between the bars to grab the scruff of her neck to pull her out. She bit down on my forearm to ensure her own safety in case I didn’t have a good grip. Some things have been resolved today, but at this rate I fear I will find myself tonight deep in a dream about a monkey named Brightness that keeps disappearing and reappearing throughout the branches of the Contrast tree.  <br><Br>\r\n\r\nI have been exposed to far too many conversations this week I was not able to translate. I think my brain is now compensating for this.\r\n'),(4385,'<P>\r\ni\\\'m going on 4 days without drinking.  that wouldn\\\'t be a big deal were it not that i\\\'m consciously doing it.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni battle bugs and mold on my rose bushes.  aphids attack them, keep them from blossoming.  their excrement feeds the mold.  i killed the bugs with pesticide.  i pulled all the leaves off to get rid of the mold.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni had a dream that my puppy had aphids and mold.  i was very sad.  it took so long to find the pesticide to kill them on the roses.  now i have to find the pesticide to kill them on dog.\r\n</P><P>\r\ni\\\'m sleeping much better without the booze, without the sleeping pills, a bad combination.  use it when you can\\\'t sleep and don\\\'t want to wake up.  \r\n</P><P>\r\ni stopped drinking two weeks ago, then started again.  now i have stopped again.  i was sleeping, and then i wasn\\\'t again.  now i have started sleeping again.  now i am awake again.\r\n</P>'),(4386,'The habitual art-films are megaphoning again. The Marxist\\\'s fancy nightcap-culture wrote about all of those knickknacks before the bleach, soundproofing and hamsters. Before greasy stuff started pasting mackerels. Back before we massaged stuff with an overhead prosecutor named The Cubist-Accusal Gator.<br><br>\r\n\r\nFables (and perhaps a little hot glucose) go discounted on Midsection Nights at Gangland\\\'s! And then they leave me hanging with my Dogcart, my Stravinsky road overexposed while Sagittarius unclothes his bedsores and bird-flakes. The architecture of mother\\\'s house, old reenactment stores, Chinese newscasts and the view from the rickshaw (atop the canebrakes) still give me misgivings at night. Take it from the flounder markets to the estate snakes but just don\\\'t start lying in infected breastfeed with your handguns, your handgun out with a wildly diverse group of papergirls.<br><br>\r\n\r\nTake me on when no one else will take you off.<br><br>\r\n\r\n'),(4387,'There is a payphone in the underground walkway in Rockefeller Center- its number is: (212) 246-8697. I called the number, and after 6 rings a fellow answered. I said <i>\\\"Is this Rockefeller Center?\\\"</i>. With surprising self-assurance he said <i>\\\"Yeah, it is!\\\"</i>. I said <i>\\\"Just a moment, Sir- let me connect your call\\\"</i>. I put him on hold and on my second line dialed the number of the phone right next to him (212) 246-8704. He, of course, answered it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI conference-called the 2 phones, so that the man, who did not hang up the first phone, was talking to himself. I guess he was standing in between the 2 phones with one phone to each ear. He must have felt so busy. That\\\'s when I hung up, so the guy could continue his conversation in private. '),(4388,'<p>jesse and i take a break from cutting up clavinet samples and the solo from <a href=\\\"http://youtube.com/watch?v=CRg5fsWCWo4\\\"><i>some of them are old</i></a> and ride the bikes to the beach.  the sun is going down but it still feels like you\\\'re encased in a giant sponge.  across the highway and out the two-lane to green key.  an incredibly hot woman with a german shepherd on a leash jogs past us going back to town.  we say hi hello.  she just smiles.<BR><BR><i>she needs a bigger dog.<BR><BR>a lot bigger.<BR><BR>the hound of the baskervilles.</i><BR><BR>somebody\\\'s \r\nplaying the guitar.  the same guy who was out here last year at sunset on september 30th, a sunday, playing <i>september.  do you remember?</i>  tourists from germany and india.  fiddler crabs in the surf and the smell of the ganj from somewhere off in the trees.<BR><BR>sometimes i forget myself.  somebody told me that when you stop wanting it, it all comes to you.  but you \r\nreally have to stop wanting it.  like a little trick you play on yourself.  like suspending disbelief.</p>'),(4389,'Right there...<br><br>\r\nJust to the left of the hiphop lyrics, 242, Prada, no-future, Principia Discordia and Banksy.<br><br>\r\nThat\\\'s it right there, between the art gallery, OSX rumors and Noam Chomsky.<br><br>\r\nWhen you have taken the key, a penguin guard comes and takes you to the Emperor Penguin. Go forward, back, back. Then right, then forward and talk to the Emperor twice.<br><br>\r\nThe penguins get out of your way... <br><br>\r\nSo go in.\r\n\r\n'),(4390,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/4390_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"3\\\"><br><br>\r\n\r\nThis is a tale explaining the manner in which my way of life was rotated along a Y axis until it reached a position roughly 180 degrees from that which it started If I could have 60 seconds of your time, simply place your posterior in the selected location and I will relate to you the details of how I was made the male monarch of the district of the City of Los Angeles, California commonly referred to as Bel-Air (coordinates 34.08333 -118.44778).<br><br>\r\n\r\nIn the western region of the \\\"City of Brotherly Love\\\" known as Philadelphia my mother expelled me from her womb and indeed that is also where I spent my childhood in my mother’s care. The majority of my time was spent in a recreational area containing such diversions as a jungle gym, swing set, sand box, etc. I was typically at the height of leisure while frequently at a temperature slightly below what might be considered standard room temperature. Outside of my educational institution I was engaging in a game of basketball with some of my friends when a couple of gentlemen who seemed to be of the disposition to cause a great deal of mischief began causing a great deal of chaos and disharmony in the area in which I lived. I was involved in one rather small bout of fisticuffs after which my mother became concerned for my general safety and well-being and she informed me that I would be moving in with her sister and her sister’s husband in the previously mentioned community of Bel-Air.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI puckered my lips and exhaled forcefully to produce a shrill note in order to gain the attention of a taxicab driver and as the driver approached I observed his California vanity plate which in place of the traditional jumble of alpha-numeric characters, used only the letters F, R, E, S, and H, spelling out the word \\\"fresh\\\" and from his rearview mirror dangled a pair of oversized, fur-covered cubes decorated to look like the six-sided dice commonly used in gambling and board games In such a situation I could have made a statement about the unusualness of this particular taxicab to the point of it being nearly unique Instead I cogitatively decided against it and instead informed the driver that he should deliver me to what was to become my new home in the community of Bel-Air.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWe pulled up to a large domicile sometime between the hours of 7 and 8 o’clock And in a loud tone of voice I informed the cab driver that at some undetermined point in the future I would again detect his odor through my sense of olfaction I gazed about the region of land that I was destined to rule, reflecting on my arrival Where I would claim my rightful place upon the throne, from which I would govern the community of Bel-Air as monarch... <br><br>'),(4391,'On September 18 of 2005, there is a new record in digital video camera industry, announcing XL, H1. Which was canon’s first DVC featuring high definition. Originally developed based on the needs of professional and enthusiast videographers, the XL H1 retains the operability of Canon\\\'s very popular XL2, while increase recording in the professional function and supporting high definition. The flagship DVC for the company of Canon, the XL H1 is ideal for use all across the spectrum of professional content making, from film and television production to filming weddings. Canon develop aim of delivering the highest possible quality of recording available today, The XL H1 also comes equipped with a HD 20X zoom video lens, the XL 5.4-108 mm L IS II, which contributes to Canon\\\'s development aim of delivering the highest possible quality of recording available today. As Canon\\\'s first HD video camera, the XL H1 camcorder and 20X HD video zoom lens will provide broadcasters with a low budget 1080i resolution option for ENG, documentary or even reality television productions. Film makes will also fall for the extensive Cine controls and 24 frame rate option the XL H1 has to offer. Jackpack features include uncompressed digital HD-SDI output for seamless integration into broadcast studios or high quality image, which transfer to non linear editing systems.\r\nThe genlock feature will allow movie sets to easily synchronize camera settings across multiple camcorders and SMPTE time codes will allow for streamlined tape and edit management. The XL H1 also offers three 1/3 inch 1.67 megapixel interlaced CCDs that capture images at 1080i resolution. It also features selectable frame rates of 50i and 25F, so capturing fast motion and just about everything else is possible with the XL H1.\r\nPDAaccessories.com is also specialized in digital camera accessories like Canon, Sony, JVC,  Gateway,  Nikon etc. Camera Chargers, Camera cases,  Digital Camera Batteries and Camera Mounts are also available. .\r\nHave a look\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.pdaaccessories.com/digitalcamera.asp\\\">Digital Camera Accessories</a>\r\n'),(4392,'a light from the sky</br>\r\nfell down to the earth</br>\r\nbeneath a small tree</br>\r\nthe light had no face</br>\r\nand no name</br>\r\nand no body</br>\r\nand so it became fire</br>\r\ntaking hold of the still tree</br>\r\nwhich would share it´s bark</br>\r\njust to dance with joy</br>\r\nand shine</br>\r\nand the light took the tree</br>\r\nand both became fire</br>\r\nlike in love</br>\r\nand the tree was fire</br>\r\nand the fire was light</br>\r\nshining in between the darkness</br>\r\nof the night</br>\r\nuntil the last branch became black</br>\r\nand had no force of life</br>\r\nthe fire stopped</br>\r\nand light became dark</br>\r\nand the ashes that once shone</br>\r\nbecame earth\r\n</br></br></br></br>\r\n'),(4393,'<p>what i like best about some songs are the wordless bits<BR>too much for words.<BR>like Oh! in <a href=\\\"http://youtube.com/watch?v=s2Vjm5LOMN8\\\">one fine day</a><BR>or Aw! in <a href=\\\"http://youtube.com/watch?v=RNtGYdm2rOY\\\">evil</a><BR>and Oh! again in <a href=\\\"http://youtube.com/watch?v=KBNz3mL9-Kg\\\">living life</a><BR><BR>even <i>Come on, Bobby, let\\\'s go</i> in <a href=\\\"http://youtube.com/watch?v=tSMUtLHPH7Y\\\">this one</a><BR>an aside, technically, i guess.<BR>but expressive of the same joy,<BR>the same fatality.<BR><BR>i\\\'m getting baptized tomorrow<BR>at our lady queen of peace<BR>at three.<BR>if you\\\'re around.<BR>if you\\\'re not around.<BR><BR>baptists immerse only with a confession of faith,<BR>and infants don\\\'t confess too well.<BR>so i am a little lost soul.<BR>confirmation is ahead.<BR><BR>(there was another water ceremony once,<BR>but we don\\\'t mention that.<BR>heh.)<BR><BR>it\\\'s not so important to me,<BR>but important to others,<BR>one in particular,<BR>and she\\\'s very important to me.<BR><BR>some words are useless things.<BR>unless sung.<BR><BR><i>the future\\\'s coming on</i><BR><BR>comeoncomeoncomeoncomeoncomeoncomeoncomeon</p>'),(4394,'The middle part of a DV camcorder is the CCD, or Charge Coupled Device. By recording the light intensity to recognize an image or shape, along with the color levels to reproduce a full color picture, sensor creates a video picture. There are many tape based DV camcorders that have both color eyepiece viewfinders and a flip out TFT LCD screen to view the action as well as the previous recorded footage. Most tape-less models however, only offer the TFT screen to preview your captured video and to view while you make a recording. A zoom lens can be a great feature, when you need to get closer to the action. You should always base your final decision on the optical zoom and don\\\'t get swayed by the digital zoom figure however; as no matter how large it may be. The optical zoom will give you a true indication of the video image, where digital zooms interpolate the available pixels to zoom in on an image. Optical zooms will normally range around the 10X mark, with digital zooms ranging from 100X and above. The auto focusing systems are great as well, although manual focus options can be ideal for low light or when focusing on a subject against a landscape. The manual focus control can be found as either a ring on the lens or as a dual button system on the body, being available on most video cameras. Feature should be avoided unless you don\\\'t intend to edit the video on a computer; most video cameras these days will provide a tilting feature in the camera itself. The title effects on many cameras are quite limited and cannot be removed afterwards. Almost all new high definition video cameras will use a rechargeable Li-ion battery, even though a spare battery might be useful as the batteries are unique to each camcorder maker and normally each model. Most of the latest cameras allow you to capture digital still images onto a removable flash memory card. The most popular media is SD or MMC, but Sony and Samsung cameras often capture to a memory stick. You can always perform a capture of the screen in your editing program, if your video camera doesn\\\'t offer still image capabilities. Always remember that the image resolution when capturing a still this way will always be 640X480, which is actually less than 1 MB, or Mega Byte. Some of them are available at PDAaccessories.com, it deals in Nikon, Gateway, JVC, Sony, Canon etc. Camera Chargers, Digital Camera Batteries,  Camera Cases and Camera Mounts are also available.\r\nHave a look……\r\n<a href=\\\'http://www.pdaaccessories.com/digitalcamera.asp\\\'>Digital Camera Accessories</a>,\r\n<a href=\\\'http://www.pdaaccessories.com/nikon-digitalcamera-accessories.asp\\\'>Nikon</a>\r\nPosted by:\r\nFatima Zaheer'),(4395,'sometimes she yearns to stand up<br>\r\nin it, heck, she even wants to jump<br>\r\nup and down, rock it, topple the boat<br>\r\nupside down, so they\\\'re both floating<br>\r\nin the water, swimming free<br><br>\r\n\r\namongst the schools<br>\r\nof multicoloured fish...<br>\r\ntheir boat feels too small<br>\r\nfor two people, they keep bumping<br>\r\ninto one another, pained smiles<br>\r\nstretching their faces as they hoist the sails<br>\r\nclean the galley, do the thousand boring<br>\r\ndaily chores. but usually she prays<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\nthat they\\\'re both rowing<br>\r\nin the same direction<br>\r\nthe water placid, the wind benevolent.<br>\r\nthe ship big enough for the two of them.<br>\r\non occasion she thinks of past ships<br>\r\nof the pirate who preferred storms<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhich terrified her, of the sailor<br>\r\nwho kissed stars onto her skin<br>\r\nof the gentle giant who would race her to the shore<br>\r\nwhere they would collapse, laughing<br>\r\nthe sun reflecting their joy<br><br>\r\n\r\ntoday the sky is clear,<br>\r\nher husband ignores the mermaids<br>\r\nsunbathing on the brutal rocks<br>\r\ncalmly looking into her eyes<br>\r\nuntil their song ends<br><br>'),(4396,'<p><b>invisible planet</b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><blockquote><p align=justify>At that\r\n moment the memories stop, and terror, or tedium, sets in.  It\\\'s hard to tell\r\n which is which in this memory, because the boredom of being in a\r\n lonely place or having a lonely job, and even of being so far in the past\r\n and having to wear those uncomfortable clothes and hairstyles is\r\n terrifying, more so than the intentional scariness of the plot, the criminals,\r\n whoever they were.<BR><BR>Imagine that innocence (Lilian Harvey)\r\n encounters romance (Willy Fritsch) in the home of experience (Albert\r\n Basserman).  From there it is only a step to terror, under the dripping boughs\r\n outside.  Anything can change as fast as it wants to, and in doing so\r\n may pass through a more or less terrible phase, but the true terror is\r\n in the swiftness of changing, forward or backward, slipping always just\r\n beyond our control.  The actors are like people on drugs, though they\r\n aren\\\'t doing anything unusual - as a matter of fact, they are performing\r\n brilliantly.</p><BR><P align=right>- John Ashbery<BR>\\\"The Lonedale\r\n Operator\\\"</P></blockquote><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b>1.  the\r\n message</b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><center>It\\\'s so hard to be good under the capitalistic\r\n system.</center><BR><blockquote><P align=right>- Genevieve Larkin<BR>\\\"Gold Diggers of\r\n 1937\\\"</P></blockquote><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i31.photobucket.com/albums/c360/six7nine/theriver.jpg\\\"/><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>lesser secrets</u></b><BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/fig04.jpg\\\"/></center><BR><BR><p align=justify>Vanessa’s been sitting out on the bridge at night,\r\n watching the river.  This is the third night in a row that I’ve gone out to\r\n run and she’s been there waiting on me:  up on the rail, feet hanging\r\n down over the resurrection ferns that grow from the pilings.  On the\r\n way home, I stop and climb up beside her.<BR><BR>Won’t look at me.\r\n  Listening to some music.  Pinch, pinch.  “Hey.”<BR><BR>“Go piss up a\r\n rope.”<BR><BR>The streetlights haven’t come on yet, and the few people left\r\n in the park are milling around like the next stop is the gas chamber.\r\n  Dennis appears from behind the bandshell.  His umbrella\\\'s up, but that doesn\\\'t mean much.  A knot of goth kids sitting by the river will soon discover that they should have left five minutes ago.<BR><BR>I say, \\\"If you decide to jump, can I have your walkman?\\\"  (Here at the hump, it\\\'s about twenty feet to the water.)<BR><BR>\\\"My <i><u>Walk</u></i>man?  What\\\'re we - in nineteen eighty-seven?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Yeah.  We are.  And when I push your ass offa here, you\\\'re gonna know it.\\\"<BR><BR>The Great Stoneface.  She studies some lawn clippings that are being carried out toward the gulf.  Also some leaves from a sea grape, roundish and broad, about the size and shape of the pasteboard fans they used to hand out at church, the ones with wooden handles like Popsicle sticks.<BR><BR>I retreat to the little pavillion at the east end of the bridge.  There\\\'s a round marker set above the entrance that commemorates the fact that golf great Gene Sarazen used to live here.  Like anybody gives a shit.  Big claim to fame.  The gravitas is somewhat deflated by the graffito below it:  METAL UP YOUR ASS.  A titanium shaft, maybe.  Did they have those then?<BR><BR>Two women and six kids cross Bank Street and head this way.  Once past the Hacienda, one of the little girls peels off and makes for the playground.  Her mother says, \\\"Where you goin\\\'?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"I\\\'m takin\\\' a short cut.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"You \\\'bout to take a short cut to an ass-whoopin\\\'.  Get over here.\\\"<BR><BR>They all pass laughing.  Vanessa looks this way, grinning, curious about the joke she missed, maybe just happy to see somebody happy.  A shower rolls in from the east, and she takes a collapsible umbrella from her backpack.  Made of transparent plastic, it fills with the smoke from her cigarette.  Alone on the bridge, lit with a hot coral glow by the last of the sun, she seems to constitute part of a glyph, a letter from a secret alphabet, the punctuated arc, the dome above her, all heralding the advent of something intransigent and impassable.<BR><BR>Dennis appears at my elbow, chewing something.  \\\"This is the stringiest damn chicken I ever put in my mouth.  What the hell?\\\"  He walks over the the garbage can in the corner and spits something into it.  \\\"Did you get shot?\\\"  Points at my chest.  I examine the t-shirt I\\\'m wearing to see if there\\\'s some spot or shadow on it that he could be hallucinating into a bloodstain.  Nothing there but the rabbit scribe, looking saucy as hell.  Who knows.<BR><BR>\\\"Not that I can recall.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Well, be careful.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Will do.\\\"  He watches as an elderly couple passes us, bloodless and wary, the woman shivering in the slightly cooler air left by the shower.<BR><BR>\\\"They look like they used to be my aunt and uncle.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Think so?  Ask \\\'em.  There they are.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Nah.  They never did like me.\\\"  Three Latino men with backpacks wait until a car crosses over before easing around the far end of the bridge and disappearing into the field behind the abandoned marina.<BR><BR>Dennis says, \\\"Well, I think I\\\'m gonna go bivouac over here in Swaziland tonight.\\\"  He indicates the overgrown field.  \\\"We call it New Puerto Rico.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Okay.  Happy motoring.\\\"<BR><BR>He shuffles around, looks at his feet.  \\\"Do you think I could touch you for five?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Not for <i>ten</i>.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Heh.\\\"  He goes away.  The rain makes thousands of circles on the surface of the water, spreading and fading, all superimposed on the ripples from the wind.  A kid of about five wanders up:  \\\"I used rto be horse.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Yeah?  What color were you?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Red.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Like a fire truck?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Uh-huh.\\\"  That\\\'s what Shay\\\'s going to be like.  Goofy like that.  He just can\\\'t talk yet.  The boy goes over the the aluminum rails at the side of the pavillion and tries to spit into the river.  It falls short by twenty feet.  He stands there and zones out on the rail until Big Sister comes along with an umbrella and drags him away.<BR><BR>After falling a good twelve minutes, the rain calls it a day.  Two girls of ten or eleven leave the shelter of the overhang in front of the restrooms and begin to dry the swings and slides with paper towels.  Presently, they start to holler:<BR><BR>\\\"That\\\'s my man right there.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Naw, that\\\'s <i>my</i> man.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"You better not be cheatin\\\' on me, you son of a bitch.  My brother\\\'ll whoop your ass.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"You hear her, you son of a bitch?  You seen her brother?\\\"<BR><BR>I pivot around so I can see what\\\'s coming down the sidewalk from town.  Jay.<BR><BR></p><BR><BR><u>Jay\\\'s arc:</u><BR>(little\r\n kid) \\\"I used to be a horse.\\\"  \\\"What color were you?\\\"  That\\\'s what Shay\\\'s going to be like.  Goofy like that.  He just can\\\'t talk yet.<BR>\\\"heavy metal\r\n drummer\\\" - Jimi Hendrix - William Blake - landlady being from the same neck\r\n of the Illinois woods as Jeff Tweedy - \\\"monday\\\" comes on his\r\n mp3 player, reminded of....<BR>\\\"Thass right.  I\\\'m bad.  I\\\'m\r\n nationwide.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"These cops seem to think I\\\'m out here sellin\\\' it.  And I\\\'ll\r\n tell you something.  I am.  But not like they think.\\\"<BR><BR>t-shirt\r\n with mayan rabbit scribe god on it<BR>bridge with v. on it looks like a\r\n tarot card.  - \\\"I couldn\\\'t tell you.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"The Movie\r\n Star\\\"<BR>smoke<BR><BR>\\\"That\\\'s my man right there.\\\"<BR>\\\"Naw, that\\\'s my man.\\\"<BR>\\\"You\r\n better not be cheatin\\\' on me, you son of a bitch.  My brother\\\'ll whoop your\r\n ass.\\\"<BR>\\\"You hear her, you son of a bitch?  You seen her\r\n brother?\\\"<BR><BR>no security.  they are fucking around at the Haji.<BR>That Little\r\n Sumbitch By The River<BR>That Little Sumbitch In The Woods<BR>\\\"make it\r\n a banqueting hall.\\\" - masques<BR><BR>stuff left around town as\r\n offerings.  who collects it?  a little man comes around<BR>psychogeography - \\\"<u>That</u>, I believe.\\\"<BR><BR>stubble on his\r\n legs<BR>\\\"Whattayou want for supper?\\\"  \\\"Caldo de pollo.  \\\'N call over there \\\'n get\r\n a sixteen-inch Capri.\\\"  He gestures toward the west.<BR><BR>\\\"Walkin\\\'\r\n the earth like Papa Smurf.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"You gotta have your shades on when\r\n you go to Brooksville.  Slip into town disguised as\r\n my<u>self</u>.\\\"<BR>\\\"Uh-huh.\\\"<BR><BR>parents out of town<BR>\\\"I don\\\'t care who you are, it\\\'s\r\n hard to live in the N.P.R.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"GATHER WEAPONS & AMMO\\\"<BR>\\\"Wocka,\r\n wocka, wocka.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Did you get my goddamn omega-3s?\\\"<BR>\\\"Oh, Good\r\n God, yes.  Here.  Have six or seven of \\\'em.\\\"<BR><BR>calls him\r\n morris<BR>mark is a dabbler.  turned jay onto magick.  jay took the ball and ran\r\n with it.<BR><BR>one of the bodyguards drives.  the other on trim patrol.\r\n  or whatever.<BR><BR>Krayzie - \\\"There goes Krayzie.\\\"  \\\"Remind me to have them call him when they get here.\\\" - remembers \r\n\r\nlater that he forgot to remind him.  no matter.  he\\\'ll think of it soon again, anyway.<BR><BR>\\\"Are you going to the \r\n\r\nreunion?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Mickey Grisbo.  Sounds like a Cajun hit man.\\\"<BR><BR>cooch tent<BR><BR>calls one arson<BR>\\\"this silly pink bridge\\\"<BR>\\\"I\\\'m like Noah - I got two of everything.\\\"<BR>\\\"Green door, what\\\'s that secret you\\\'re keepin\\\'?\\\"<BR>[mention that it would have been no shortcut as the nearest bridge is two miles downriver.]<BR>calls Jay \\\"Infidel\\\"<BR><BR><u>Third arc:</u><BR>...like God abroad in the night.  \r\n\r\nAmazed at how Good He is.<BR>armadillo<BR>car being taken for test drive<BR><BR>\\\"I\\\'m gonna tell him to get outta our \r\n\r\nhouse.\\\"<BR>\\\"No, you <u>don\\\'t</u>.\\\"<BR><BR>found on either side of bridge:  - scrap metal receipt.  - survey report from \r\n\r\n1963.<BR><BR>A.A. - time to catch the eight o\\\'clock meeting - decides to skip it<BR><BR>coming home to wife and kid, caught \r\n\r\nin rain, sheepish.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>-  Yeah, I was in Dade City last year, and somebody had put up all these fliers on the \r\n\r\ntelephone poles downtown.  \\\"SHIATSU DOG MISSING.\\\"  Call blah blah whatever.  And then on one of them, somebody else had come \r\n\r\nalong and written, \\\"IF I FIND IT, I THINK I\\\'LL KEEP IT.  MY SCIATICA\\\'S BEEN GIVING ME HELL.\\\"<BR><BR>-  Dade City.  Yeah, I \r\n\r\ncan see that.  I think that\\\'s how spies keep in touch with each other.  Doing stuff like that.  Writing on bathroom \r\n\r\nwalls.<BR><BR>-  Are they the ones that write, \\\"PUT YOUR SWEET LIPS A LITTLE CLOSER TO THE PHONE\\\"?<BR><BR>-  I couldn\\\'t tell \r\n\r\nyou.  Dude.  There\\\'s a guy here named Roachmeat.<BR><BR>-  Nice.  So.  Any famous last words you want to enter into the \r\n\r\nrecord here?<BR><BR>-  Yeah.  How about \\\"FUCK <U>ALL</U> Y\\\'ALL\\\"?  What would you suggest?<BR><BR>-  Well, something notable.  \r\n\r\nSomething classy.  What did Frank Sinatra say?  \\\"I\\\'m losing.\\\"  Or Anna Pavlova:  \\\"Get my swan costume ready.\\\"<BR><BR>-  Ha.  \r\n\r\nHang on a second.  I think they\\\'re coming back.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Wanna know a secret?  People hear what they want to hear.  \r\n\r\nA person is more likely to believe something the more it resembles the truth as they already see it.<BR><BR>It took me a long \r\n\r\ntime to realize that.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center>FLORIDA DEPARTMENT OF LAW ENFORCEMENT<br>INVESTIGATIVE \r\n\r\nREPORT</center></b><HR>Case #:  JA-5A-000088<BR>I.R. #:  75<BR>Description:  Interview - Taped - Mark Allen Brauer<BR>Office \r\n\r\nof Origin:  New Port Richey Field Office<BR>Case Agent:  Ellis, Robin D.<BR>SAC/ASAC:  Nouza, Vincent J.<HR>(S)  Mark Allen \r\n\r\nBrauer<BR>(V)  Jason Jay Osborne<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>Get outta here.<BR>No?  Well, good for you.  Might be hope for you \r\n\r\nyet.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>-  So what\\\'s the lighter for?<BR>-  It makes fire.<BR>-  I tell you what.  I tell you what.  Do not \r\n\r\npiss me off.<BR>-  It\\\'s better to be pissed off than pissed on.  So they say.<BR>-  Take your shoes \r\n\r\noff.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>\\\"Where\\\'s my Steely Dan t-shirt?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"<u>I\\\'ll</u> give you a compliment, Joey.  You have a pretty \r\n\r\nmouth.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"Never trust a man with a bird on his shoulder.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"A good cat joke is not wasted on a lesbian.  No, \r\n\r\nindeed.\\\"<BR><BR>the baby like a chorus to what they say.  singing, babbling, cooing, shaking the rattle.<BR><BR>hidden behind \r\n\r\nthe curve of the earth<BR><BR>i write in restaurants.  burger king.  wendy\\\'s.<BR><BR>\\\"They should put jukeboxes in \r\n\r\nB.K.\\\"<BR><BR>people who start up conversations with complete strangers<BR><BR>terrazzo - expensive to fix<BR><BR>girl\\\'s been \r\n\r\nto B.K. only three times in her life.<BR><BR>\\\"<i>All you suckah emcees from around the way</i>\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"I wanted to run a \r\n\r\nfoursome.\\\"<BR>\\\"You should have some cards printed up:  JOEY T., FOURSOME ADMINISTRATOR.\\\"<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>cherry \r\n\r\nlaurel<BR>chickasaw plum<BR>cigar orchid<BR>cocoplum<BR>dwarf lantana<BR>firebush<BR>geiger tree<BR>gumbo \r\n\r\nlimbo<BR>inkberry<BR>Judas tree<BR>needle palm<BR>penny grass<BR>pond cypress<BR>puncture vine<BR>resurrection fern<BR>satin \r\n\r\nleaf<BR>sea grape<BR>silver palm<BR>spider lily<BR>stinking cedar<BR>swamp lily<BR>trumpet creeper<BR>wandering Jew<BR>wild \r\n\r\ntamarind<BR>zephyr lily<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>the prisoner\\\'s\r\n \r\n\r\ncinema</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>stellification</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>shit\r\n list</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>grappa\r\n punch</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>people take pictures of\r\n each other</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>mystery\r\n achievement</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>the ones\r\n who lived</u></center></b><BR><BR><p align=justify>He said, “The Bank of America\r\n building’s what’s known as a fort.  The F.B.I. office alone qualifies it, but\r\n then you also got stuff in there dealing with emergency management,\r\n telecommunications.  Some holding cells.”<BR><BR>Hollis.  Yeah.  When he\r\n laid this piece here on me, back in February, we were coming home from\r\n Clearwater one Saturday night around eleven, stop-and-go traffic on 19,\r\n and Hollis is playing the piano on the dashboard, steering with his\r\n forearms and talking to somebody via a headset:  “God, yes.  You were\r\n drunker than a crosseyed Mexican on Cinco de Mayo.  We finally just left you\r\n on the floor.”<BR><BR>Some teenagers in a car in the next lane watch as\r\n he finesses it, now flailing, the wheel held under his gut, now\r\n teasing out mincing little arpeggios, his arms locked at two and ten.  Pasty.\r\n  Stringy black hair that looks like he cut and dyed it himself.  Buddy\r\n Holly glasses.  Always sweaty.  High on something,  maybe.<BR><BR>The\r\n boy riding shotgun says something and they all laugh.  Up ahead, a dog\r\n almost falls out of a truck, and I start looking for owls.  The fake\r\n kind, meant to scare away birds.  I can see those things a mile off, just\r\n like my sister can spot toupees.<BR><BR>Two on a gas station.  One on\r\n a hotel.  And Hollis punches me on the shoulder and says, “Are you\r\n listening to me?  Jesus Christ.  I might as well be out at the beach\r\n packing sand up my ass with a little red shovel.”  He swings the eyes on me.\r\n  Whatever it is that the rest of it is designed to camoflauge, the\r\n eyes betray.<BR><BR>He tells me about issues of defensibility and\r\n scalability, the flat roof, barricades and air purification systems and stores\r\n of weapons and food.  And then, like he’s handing me a consolation\r\n prize, he says, “I tell you what.  If the shit ever goes down, head over\r\n there ‘n tell ‘em I said you’re cool, ‘n they’ll let you in.”<BR><BR>I\r\n thanked him for what I thought were probably so many lies, but I visited\r\n the bank the next day and walked through a vestibule and into a back\r\n hallway with restrooms and elevators and a directory in a glass case, the\r\n kind with letters stuck into the grooves in the matting.  And, lo,\r\n there he was.  Parked up on the third floor with a shiteating grin.  The\r\n Man.<BR><BR>There were also several firms with cryptic names clustered\r\n together on the upper floors.  SUNCOAST PROFESSIONAL SERVICES.  BNN\r\n MANAGEMENT, LLC.  THE DAVIS GROUP.  Vague enough to be anything at all.\r\n  And right down the hall from the F.B.I., the telemarketing outfit a\r\n friend of mine’s been working at for two or three years, ripping Canadians\r\n off on Disney vacations.  The feds probably couldn’t care\r\n less.<BR><BR>And then he was back on the phone, and the traffic let up some, and sea\r\n birds flew over the bright stench of the highway to fish among the\r\n lithe sea grasses several miles to the west in Anclote Sound.<BR><BR>I\r\n tried to imagine what “the shit going down” might look like.  Tanks and\r\n vans in the street.  Helicopters landing on the bank building, the mall,\r\n the high school, coming and going all day and all night.  Curfew at\r\n sundown.  Rifle fire in the park at dawn.  The telemarketing scumbags\r\n carrying on, business as usual.  I decided I’d rather take my chances with\r\n the rest of the peons.<BR><BR>Then right before we got back into town, I\r\n saw an inflatable gorilla on the roof of a car dealership and was\r\n reminded of the trip Marvin and I took to the Keys.  I had showed him the\r\n owls and got him hooked on looking for them, too.  Somewhere in South\r\n Florida, he woke me up and said, “Look.  Burger King’s done got ‘em a\r\n woman for their roof.”<BR><BR>She was big, Russian peasant-looking in a\r\n uniform and a black apron, walking around like she’d lost something up\r\n there.  “Burger King say, ‘We ain’t fuckin’ around with these owls\r\n anymore.  Damned birds’re gettin’ too smart.’”<BR><BR>I said, “I guess that\r\n must be what they call\r\n evolution.”</p><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>sexuality\r\n \r\n\r\ndogs</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>psychogeography</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>scenes from\r\n next summer</u></center></b><BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><center><u>roll over\r\n and take it</u></center></b></p>'),(4397,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/4397_1.jpg\\\" border\\\"1\\\"><br><br>\r\n\r\nI dated a girl, years ago, who lived in a really shitty part of Brooklyn. I\\\'m talking like every single building on her block besides her building and the school had burned to rubble years ago and everyone seemed content to just leave it at that.<br><br>\r\nThe only non-shaft view in the place was out the tiny bathroom window. It overlooked the waste loading dock at Woodhull hospital. More or less. That was further down the block, but the 4 or 5 buildings that shoold have intervened were gravel lots. <br><br>\r\nThat was back when I was a Manhattanite. I hated her place, and wasn\\\'t terribly familiar with Brooklyn at that time, except for Williamsburg, where I had happily lived, in a very insular part, years earlier. It was outerboro; it was creepily dead blocks devoid of people, vast empty schoolyards and hospital parking lots. I went there as little as possible. You had to take the G train to get there, for god\\\'s sake. <br><br>\r\nAt that point in my life, I was prone to say snippy, idiotic things like <i>\\\"I\\\'d never live out here. I love the Upper West Side\\\"</i> and often more snarky things, generally in order to assuage the secret guilt of my outrageous Manhattan rent, which required two jobs and bearing a cathartic amount of stress in a endlessly manic environment. I mean, seriously. Who would want to live on a bombed-out block out in the sticks when <i>The City</i> is always in view, just right there across the river, winking at you...<br><br>\r\nI went on a long bike ride today; down to Far Rockaway and the beaches there. There are mysterious abandoned machine-gun pillboxes in an old abandoned fort on the beach. It was amazing. I met some interesting folks, tested out my latest bike mods, took some great photos, rode home as night fell as I\\\'ve done five hundred times before... my kind of day.<br><br>\r\nI decided to follow a less-direct-but-comparatively-traffic-free route home from downtown Brooklyn, and ended up in an area that looked somehow familiar. It begged some fleeting memory from years back; a handfull of subtle details, the sorts of things that might never catch any eye but mine. I stopped at the light and did a slow 360, feeling a bit like a Google Street-View camera. <br><Br>\r\nThere were tons of the ubiquitous cookie-cutter Section 8 houses that have gone up recently in the neighborhood. A new brownstone-size house or two, like the ones near my place. Then I saw the school. I knew that a ways behind me was the waste loading-dock, even though I could no longer see it. The old rickety apartment building was there to my left, newly remodeled with a nice wide sidewalk in front and some landscaping. Here? What?<br><br>\r\nI clipped my feet into the pedals and rode the four or five remaining blocks to where I live now. Land of the cheap(er) rent, the G train, the sprawling industrial lots. Brooklyn, the thing about which I used to be so ill-informed, despite the fact that I fancied myself to be such a universal thinker.<br><br>\r\nIt makes me wonder what I\\\'m blind to now, the things I might never otherwise consider outside of this moment; the things I\\\'ll still find myself right in the middle of almost a decade later, only vaguely recognizing the territory, incongruous not so much because it has changed, but because I am now much more well-informed.'),(4398,'<p>i squirm and stir against the heavy duvet. nice to be smothered. restrained. comforted. safe.</p>\r\n<p>so long since the bad time. the motels. the needles.</p>\r\n<p>i try not to think about all the wasted time. all the angry stares, and the voices.</p>\r\n<p>i could drag myself out of bed i suppose. i could have been earning money today had i worked a little harder the last month, but that\\\'s gone now.</p>\r\n<p>i have to see my family for lunch in a few hours, and what seemed like a nice idea when i arranged it now seems like so much pressure and stress.</p>\r\n<p>my life is so much better than it used to be, and yet so many bad things still linger.</p>\r\n<p>i keep reminding myself that i\\\'m lucky to even be alive, but somehow that\\\'s cold comfort.</p>\r\n<p>the demons are all still here, they just wear different masks.</p>\r\n<p>one day it might all be better.</p>\r\n<p>one day.</p>'),(4399,'<p>year is end.<BR>yesterday i wanted to.<BR>yet i do marvel.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>you all know the story of the other woman.<BR>you cry, waking from a nightmare.<BR>you do not do, you do not do.<BR><BR>you hitched a thousand miles.<BR>you leave dead friends in.<BR>you might come here sunday on a whim.<BR><BR>you two sit at the table late.<BR>you, once a belle in shreveport.<BR>your eyes were the brown gold of pebbles under water.<BR>your mind and you are our sargasso sea.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i am a gentleman in a dustcoat trying.<BR>i am a landscape, he said.<BR>i am inside someone.<BR><BR>i bought every kind of machine that\\\'s known.<BR>i burned my life, that i might find.<BR>i cannot find my way:  there is no star.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i caught a tremendous fish.<BR>i didn\\\'t make you know how glad i was.<BR>i do not know more than the Sea tells me.<BR><BR>i had nothing to do with it.  i was not here.<BR>i had over-prepared the event.<BR>i had walked since dawn and lay down to rest on a bare hillside.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i have a life that did not become.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i have done it again.<BR>i have eaten.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i have had to learn the simplest things.<BR>i make a pact with you, walt whitman.<BR>i place these numbered wrists on the pane.<BR>i placed a jar in tennessee.<BR>i prove a theorem and the house expands.<BR><BR>i said i will find what is lowly.<BR>i saw the best minds of my generation destroyed.<BR>i saw the spiders marching through the air.<BR>i think i must have lied.<BR><BR>i walked in a desert.<BR>i walked on the banks of the tincan banana dock and sat down.<BR>i want to be known as the most brilliant man in america.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i wanted you, nameless woman of the south.<BR>i went to the dances in chandlerville.<BR>i went up and down the streets.<BR>i would have been as great as george eliot.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>i\\\'ll miss the small birds that come.<BR>i\\\'m gonna walk to the graveyard.<BR>i\\\'m scare a lonely.  never see my son.<BR>i\\\'ve known rivers.<BR>i\\\'ve stayed in the front yard all my life.<BR><BR>if external action is effete.<BR>if it should be true that reality exists.<BR>if there are any heavens my mother will (all by herself) have.<BR>illness, an.<BR><BR>in a dark time.<BR>in a shoe box stuffed in an old nylon stocking.<BR>in a station of the metro.<BR>in every part of every living thing.<BR>in our content, before the autumn came.<BR>in the dark.<BR>in the grave no flower.<BR>in the shreve high football stadium.<BR>in the sleepless sleep of dawn, in the dreamless dream.<BR>in the waiting room.<BR>in this photo, circa.<BR>in water-heavy nights behind grandmother\\\'s porch.<BR>incident.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>insistently through sleep, a tide of voices.<BR>interrupted forms.<BR>invisible man.<BR>is an enchanted thing.<BR>is the total black, being spoken.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR>it is a willow when summer is over.<BR>it is hard going to the door.<BR>it is not now i learn.<BR>it was never in the planning.<BR>it\\\'s my lunch hour, so i go.<BR>it\\\'s wonderful how i jog.</p>'),(4400,'Two nights ago, I paid $4.49 for a loaf of bread.<br><br>Not fancy-schmancy bread, just standard whole-grain sliced bread. I made a sandwich with two slices last night.<br><br>Tonight, when I got home, the loaf was completely moldy; I had to toss it out.'),(4401,''),(4402,'<p align=justify><b><u>private life</u></b><BR><BR>gina\\\'s orecchiette, bread, mixed greens with olives.  black and green and kalamata.  a strawberry trifle and <i>the pope of greenwich village</i>.  it hasn\\\'t aged well but not much does.<BR><BR>sex in the overstuffed chair in the den.  i cast around for something to concentrate on and light upon eric roberts:  <i>how does one fill the days?  dinner theatre, voiceovers, living off the sister?</i>  i prescribe her two our fathers in the shower and we\\\'re asleep by ten.<BR><BR>i leave in the night, as is my wont, and ride the beach cruiser up to jesse\\\'s.  the riverwalk\\\'s dark.  i don\\\'t have lights or reflectors, but the back fender came loose three weeks ago, and it beats a baleful deathknell to the hapless drunks on the sidewalk and in the middle of the road.  at quarter to one, things are just getting started uptown.  aside from a little variation - vigil at four, dinner with her mom - this is the first half of every saturday night.  limned and tinted enough for our purposes here, anyway.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>catholic boys</u></b><BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/l_0f0d8d539ab289d20067c8833c0bc34d.jpg\\\"/><BR>jesse\\\'s italian and hungarian.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/l_da60e13f81ed7e02fb5c00a514de952c.jpg\\\"/><BR>robbie\\\'s irish and norweigan.  his girlfriend remembers how, in second grade, jesse stood up in the middle of class one day and said, \\\"catherine cook.  cook me some pancakes.\\\"  got the big laugh.  he hasn\\\'t changed much.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/m_ad9906af2f534edd8b83939307d75dc3.jpg\\\"/><BR>brandon isn\\\'t catholic.  he\\\'s jewish and irish, among other things.  according to jesse.<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/l_1ba323874bd74aa955fa7142036a7693.jpg\\\"/><BR>i don\\\'t know what the gestures mean.  illuminati hand signalling, maybe?  something to do with all the blue clothing?  it\\\'s fun to pretend, i guess.  i have to save my red clothes for when i go to brooksville.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>fully scripted</b></u><BR><BR>when i get there, jesse\\\'s mom is drunk.  they\\\'re in his room trying to make a beat, and she\\\'s in there singing on top of it:  <i>And Sailor Sam and Dapper Dan were looking for that.  BAND on the RUN....  I\\\'ll stop the world AND MELT WITH YOU....  COME AND GET YOUR LOVE....</i>  sam adams in one hand, newport in the other.  she looks like al lewis - grandpa munster - with long brown hair.  robbie says, \\\"sharon.  take five.\\\"<BR><BR>they stop to smoke a blunt, and she clears out.  (parole.)  the freestyling commences with all available haste:<BR><BR>brandon:  <i>and then there\\\'s alicia.  i heard she was a queefah.</i><BR><BR>jesse (addressing robbie):  <i>you\\\'re gonna get gangbanged by mark, dana, and j.b. all together.  in windy weather.  you\\\'ll be wearin\\\' a sweater.</i><BR><BR>robbie:  <i>i don\\\'t care who you are - it\\\'s hard to live in the n.p.r.  city police have got no love, peepin\\\' my action from up above.  all this shit bringin\\\' the heat downtown....</i><BR><BR>me:  <i>i am the selector eye.  i am the dubmaker, i.  dub it in, dub it out.</i><BR><BR>jesse sometimes accuses me of preparing my rhymes beforehand, and he\\\'s right.  it also helps that i spit them fast, faster than bizzy bone, which results in a lot of it being damned near unintelligible, but there you go.  i have also been known to crib from time to time, de la soul and a tribe called quest and <i>too much monkey business</i>, <i>subterranean homesick blues</i>, <a href=\\\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=16kh-AP4OCU\\\"><i>life is a rock (but the radio rolled me)</i></a>, <i>it\\\'s the end of the world as we know it (and i feel fine)</i>, the phone book, whatever, as long as it rhymes.  i don\\\'t really value improvisation.  not in terms of freestyling, playing, comedy, or acting.  unless it\\\'s very, very good.  which we are not.<BR><BR>after twenty minutes of this, on top of the beat they\\\'ve made, called \\\"HATER\\\", we repair to the living room to watch ufc.  sokoudjou is fighting.  brandon says, \\\"c\\\'mon, blackie.\\\"  somebody comments on matt serra\\\'s cool hair.  or mine, maybe.  i don\\\'t know and don\\\'t ask.  jesse looks out the window every two minutes until he says, \\\"jackie chan\\\'s home,\\\" then goes next door.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>drummers</u></b><BR><BR>we all drum.  jesse\\\'s probably the best.  he also plays keyboards and guitar (he\\\'s left-handed).  brandon plays alto sax and other woodwinds, should they become available.  rob just rhymes, mostly.  i didn\\\'t want this to turn into some \\\"something old, something new\\\" shit, but there you are.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>cosmology</b></u><BR><BR>jackie is the young woman who lives next door.  built in the classical mode, she favors scoop-necked blouses that frame the clovage.  what has done cloven.  she stays there with j.b., Mysterious Man About Town, who always seems to have a fine nineteen or twenty year old woman bedding down in his spare room, even though he is in his late thirties and out of shape.<BR><BR>jackie hostesses at some restaurant.  she wont\\\' tell us where for fear that we\\\'d go there and embarrass her or get her fired.  she shouldn\\\'t worry about it:  we hardly ever have enough money to eat anywhere that would employ a hostess, and in the off chance that we did, we\\\'d spend it on something else.<BR><BR>according to jesse, she\\\'s had her hand down his pants, but nothing came of it.  we doubt the veracity of this.  for one thing, she\\\'s got a boyfriend who looks like marlon brando must have at age twenty.  jesse\\\'s brother damien says, \\\"some girl sticks her hand down my pants...it\\\'s on.\\\"  robbie:  \\\"on like donkey kong.\\\"<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/l_1b4bd9ab76d2628babaa3c8fc03d621a.jpg\\\"/><BR>(damien\\\'s thirty.  also on parole, he and his mom got out of prison in the spring after doing a year and half apiece for sales and possession.  in any other family, jesse would be the black sheep, but with his, he\\\'s the mascot.  the hero.)<BR><BR>we\\\'re in the kitchen speculating on the goings-on next door.  brandon:  \\\"she\\\'s over there gettin\\\' slayed.\\\"  robbie:  \\\"who by?  not that kid josh.  (<i>her boyfriend</i>)  he\\\'s a homo from way back.\\\"  brandon:  \\\"nah.  he\\\'s just a prettyboy.  he gets his.\\\"<BR><BR>there\\\'s never much to eat around their house.  i think jesse might be somewhat malnourished.  left to his own devices, he does tend to rhyme about food a quite a bit.  <i>steak and shrimp</i>.  rhymes with pimp, limp, crimp, chimp, the gimp, hemp, wimp, and the goodyear blimp.  and in those words, you can almost see his entire cosmology.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>predilection</b></u><BR><BR>we watch two tivo\\\'d episodes of <i>sanford and son</i> then sit on the front porch so they can smoke cigarettes.  bikefest 2008 is ongoing, still bringing all the heat downtown in the form of the police chopper circling the city and a lot of nondescript vans parked in alleyways and back streets, each holding one or two middle-aged men, most likely fbi or fdle agents.  jesse returns, along with their cat shady.<BR><BR>me:  \\\"is shady male or female?\\\"<BR><BR>jesse gathers her up and lifts her leg, holds her up to me hindparts first.  \\\"does that look like a male to you?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"sorry.  i didn\\\'t feel like performing an examination.  just making conversation.\\\"<BR><BR>robbie:  \\\"look at that swollen ass.  why\\\'s she fuckin\\\'?  isn\\\'t she fixed?\\\"<BR><BR>jesse:  \\\"she hasn\\\'t been.  girl cats dont\\\' like to fuck.\\\"<BR><BR>robbie:  \\\"i know monkeys do.  we took my sister\\\'s kids to busch gardens, and went through the monkey part, and this one chimp gets wood, *pop*,\\\" he puts his fist between his legs and extends his thumb, \\\"and this girl chimp, i don\\\'t know if she heard it or smelled it or what, she looks back around over her shoulder like, <i>what is <u>that</u>?</i>, and she was backin\\\' ass up.\\\"<BR><BR>this amuses sharon to no end.  \\\"vive le difference,\\\" she says, laughing.<BR><BR>jesse decides it\\\'s time to call corey, time for some dro.  he asks me for his number, knowing that i have a hard time forgetting anything.  \\\"nah,\\\" he tells him, \\\"bring your chimp ass up here.  i got no whip.\\\"  he hangs up, chuckling.  \\\"i wish i had a little brother.\\\"<BR><BR>sharon says, \\\"kitchen\\\'s closed, bub.\\\"<BR><BR>jesse:  \\\"what about dad?\\\"<BR><BR><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/R18805.jpg\\\"/><BR>(his father\\\'s in prison for manslaughter.  broke some guys\\\'s spleen in a bar fight a while back.  he should be getting out next year.  he and sharon split up before he was incarcerated.)<BR><BR>sharon:  \\\"stranger things have happened.\\\"<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>chatter</u></b><BR><BR>mark and richie roll up shortly after two, when the bars close.  they are in their late 40s.  mark has long black hair, a mustasche, a texas accent, and is missing a few teeth.  he is a Mysterious Man About Town and part-time mason.  he also seems to operate an unlicensed motel within his home.  richie, another mason, blonde, solid, disconcertingly wholesome-looking, is staying with him until his wife moves out of their house.  sharon directs mark\\\'s attention to a basket of his laundry which she has washed for him.  she exchanges pleasantries with richie, smooths back her hair with one hand and draws deep on a newport.  when jesse looks his way, robbie bends at the waist, puts his hands in front of him as if on an invisible wall or headboard, and opens his mouth into a circle as if in ecstasy.  this is code for:  <i>your mama\\\'s hittin\\\' the skins with that man.</i>  he tries to wind his best friend up every chance he gets.<BR><BR>mark pulls about five dollars in quarters out of his pocket.  he says, \\\"that\\\'s what i hate about goin\\\' to the bar - i never know what to do with all this change.\\\"<BR><BR>sharon says, \\\"take your drunk ass to the laundromat.\\\"<BR><BR>mark invites all who can over for a smoke.  we gather in their driveway and pass the bowl.  there\\\'s a large roll of window screening propped against the shed door.  mark says, \\\"where\\\'d that screen come from?\\\"<BR><BR>richie:  \\\"it was in your ass.  that\\\'s what was stoppin\\\' your shit up.\\\"<BR><BR>jesse says, \\\"it was comin\\\' out like spaghetti.\\\"<BR><BR>we listen to jesse complain about his living situation.  richie offers to let him stay with him once the wife moves out.  he says, \\\"once you get past the cock and balls, there\\\'s some good pussy there.\\\"<BR><BR>jesse says, \\\"no thanks.  i don\\\'t fuck hermaphrodites.\\\"<BR><BR>mark leaves to visit the bootlegger.  he sits inside the cab of his truck with his legs sticking out and ties his boots.  richie calls to him, \\\"me no punk bitch.  why you hit me?  me no punk bitch.\\\"  laughs uproariously.  i decide he must be imitating some mexican guy who works with them.  mark leaves.  richie yells, \\\"white trash!  chicken head!\\\"  mark flips him off.<BR><BR>we sit in mark\\\'s yard in lawn chairs and talk mock shit to sharon and damien across the street.  it\\\'s been windy for the last three days.  i lose the thread, drift into reverie.  brandon says, \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P4TbrgIdm0E\\\">miles davis et john coltrane.  so what?</a>\\\"  pronouncing \\\"et\\\" with a hard T, he\\\'s been saying that all night, ever since i showed them that clip with an eye toward sampling it.<BR><BR>i am lost in my thoughts.  i hear jesse say, \\\"an abominible snowman would donkeyslap your ass, bro.\\\"<BR><BR>and then brandon says, \\\"matt\\\'s got that flair.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"is that good?\\\" i ask.<BR><BR>\\\"hell, yeah.\\\"<BR><BR>robbie and brandon arrange for a couple of girls to pick them up.  i ask why they never invite me, assure them i wouldn\\\'t mind some nice young stuff now and then.  brandon says, \\\"oh, you must have some socks on.  he thinks he\\\'s dressed up if he\\\'s wearin\\\' socks.\\\"<BR><BR>robbie:  \\\"we\\\'re afraid you\\\'d make us look bad.\\\"<BR><BR>me:  \\\"they don\\\'t share their group activities.  that\\\'s probably due to some secret proclivities.\\\"<BR><BR>laughter, maybe a little too loud, a little too nervous.  \\\"naw, man.  naw.\\\"<BR><BR>my rabbit is missing again.  it\\\'s a grey stone rabbit half an inch high that i use as a fetish.  it has a habit of falling out of my pocket and making for the hills.  i\\\'ll find it two days later in jesse\\\'s room.<BR><BR>robbie starts running down the menu they will savor.  jesse says, \\\"yeah, right.  you\\\'ve been goin\\\' out with your girlfriend for four years, and all you\\\'ve done with her is missionary.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"that\\\'s right.  that\\\'s what i like.  missionary and crumpets.  straight-up biscuits.  and some tea.\\\"<BR><BR>kenny, a friend of theirs, pulls up over at j.b.\\\'s.  he says, \\\"you all look like a buncha crackers sittin\\\' out there like that.\\\"<BR><BR>robbie says, \\\"what skin is it off yours, you fish-eyed fuck?\\\"<BR><BR>we implore kenny to come share his bounty with us.  \\\"hell, no,\\\" he says.  \\\"this is for the head.  it\\\'s goin\\\' right to the face.\\\"<BR><BR>richie goes in and gets the hand drums off the wall.  they have handles and cords extending from the drum heads with strikers on the end.  one plays them by twisting them back and forth.  one in each hand, he plays them and sings,  <i>this old man, he played one.  he played knick-knack on my thumb.  with a knick-knack, paddy-whack, give a dog a bone, this old man came rollin\\\' home.</i>  almost on cue, we see mark\\\'s truck topping the hill.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>with the best intentions</u></b><BR><BR>sharon has calmed some, seems to be winding down for the night.  i sit on the porch for a second while jesse gets ready to go get the dro.  dana, the middle-aged woman who lives next door, calls out, \\\"sharon, my feet are as big as your head.\\\"<BR><BR>sharon yells back, \\\"you\\\'re probably gonna die.\\\"  she was a nurse in her past life.  before the unfortunate incarceration.<BR><BR>damien asks my honest opinion of jesse\\\'s chances at making it in the music industry.  i say, \\\"i don\\\'t know.  that\\\'s pretty much up to him.  but you know something?  it wouldn\\\'t surprise me if he did.  who knows?  i can\\\'t figure him out.  i\\\'m usually pretty good at reading people, but i can only go so deep with him.  there\\\'s something else going on there we don\\\'t know about.<BR><BR>damien says, \\\"oh, i got him figured out.  i got you figured out, too.\\\"<BR><BR>sharon decides i can have some old books i\\\'ve asked about, ones concerning electronics and radio physics.  \\\"what do you want with \\\'em?\\\" she asks.<BR><BR>\\\"i just enjoy reading and learning about things.  i think my ideal house would be a public library.  if it had a kitchen and a shower.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"damn, dude.\\\"<BR><BR>i go look for jesse.  we have to meet his connect downtown in half and hour.  he\\\'s in his room.  i figure he\\\'s probably researching who represents swizz beatz or the runners, but he\\\'s drawing on a large piece of paper, overlapping circles and triangles.<BR><BR>i say, \\\"you ready, iamblichus?\\\"  he doesn\\\'t ask who that might be.  i find him incurious about most things.  or maybe he looks into stuff when i\\\'m not around.<BR><BR>i say, \\\"let me score a light bulb.  and some toilet paper.\\\"  he gives me a withering look, but goes to the hall closet and gets them.  i put them in my backpack.<BR><BR>we leave his mother and brother on the porch.  they\\\'re both fairly lit.  gina and i watched <i>chinatown</i> recently, and the epiphanic moment for me - and jake gittes - was toward the end of the movie when evelyn mulray says their destination is 1712 alameda, and jake knows that things are once again going to end very badly, that, as a fictive personage, he is trapped in a cycle of doom.  jesse tells me about the time before, about his mom keeping a setup in the bathroom to cook coke and heroin, about boxes of jewelry stowed in her room that she had accepted as payment.<BR><BR>a long time ago, my dad told me a story about three drunks who used to hang out under a bridge in north georgia where he\\\'s from, how, one day, they burst into town ashen of countenance, swearing that an angel had appeared to them by the creek and told them they\\\'d better quit drinking.  he said they were scared straight for a week and a half, then were back at it.  i guess time always tells.<BR><BR><HR><BR><BR><b><u>idiot wind</u></b><BR><BR>we stop at the laundromat to use the bathroom.  five or six mexican men are hanging out inside, drinking modelo.  jesse takes a whiz, and i get a coke out of the machine.  they discuss us freely, speculating that we are officers of the law or homosexuals or both.  a slight mexican man with glasses comes in, a rare sight among the immigrant population.  i say, \\\"¡ah puch está aquí!\\\" by way of letting them know i can understand them and just to shit them up some.  they laugh and talk to me until it\\\'s my turn in the bathroom.<BR><BR>we go downtown to main street food mart.  vinnie, the night clerk, (his name\\\'s vini<i>x</i>, x being a string of ten letters or so.  the mexican guys calls him \\\"primo\\\" - \\\"cousin\\\".) has a little thing for me.  he\\\'s prone to say things to me like, \\\"i have a cobra.  it\\\'s very tame.  you should come see it.\\\"  i\\\'m always like, \\\"uh-huh.  right-o.\\\"<BR><BR>we buy a bunch of junk, and the total comes to $11.11.<BR><BR>vinnie asks, \\\"are you students of numerology?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"past masters,\\\" i say.<BR><BR>corey shows, business is conducted, and we head toward my apartment.  they\\\'re building a train depot downtown.  just for show, it\\\'s part of the community redevelopment effort.  somehow, i don\\\'t trust a town that doesn\\\'t have a train running through it.  i miss that whistle.  instead, there\\\'s the horn from the casino shuttle every day at nine-thirty, eleven, three-thirty, and seven.<BR><BR>this concerns a weekend in october, 2008, back when it was warm.  it\\\'s cooler now, but the same wind blows, bringing some things, taking some things away.</p>'),(4407,'<center><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/landscape_with_fall_of_icarus_fs.jpg\\\"/></center><BR><BR><blockquote><blockquote><blockquote>About suffering they were never wrong,<BR>The Old Masters: how well they understood<BR>Its human position; how it takes place<BR>While someone else is eating or opening a window or just walking dully along;<BR>How, when the aged are reverently, passionately waiting<BR>For the miraculous birth, there always must be<BR>Children who did not specially want it to happen, skating<BR>On a pond at the edge of the wood:<BR>They never forgot<BR>That even the dreadful martyrdom must run its course<BR>Anyhow in a corner, some untidy spot<BR>Where the dogs go on with their doggy life and the torturer\\\'s horse<BR>Scratches its innocent behind on a tree.<BR><BR>In Brueghel\\\'s <i>Icarus</i>, for instance: how everything turns away<BR>	Quite leisurely from the disaster; the ploughman may<BR>Have heard the splash, the forsaken cry,<BR>But for him it was not an important failure; the sun shone<BR>As it had to on the white legs disappearing into the green<BR>Water; and the expensive delicate ship that must have seen<BR>	Something amazing, a boy falling out of the sky,<BR>Had somewhere to get to and sailed calmly on.<BR><BR><BR><p align=right>w. h. auden, \\\"musée des beaux arts\\\"</p><BR><BR><BR><BR><p align=justify>\\\"The little light which flickered out with his demise grew in power and intensity as the fact of his death became more largely known.  He has lived more wondrously and vividly since he departed this earth than he ever did in life.  One wonders, had he come back <i>in this life</i>, what sort of poetry he would have written, what his message would have been.  It was as though, cut off in the prime of manhood, he was cheated of that final phase of development which permits a man to harmonize his warring selves.  Operating under a curse for the major part of his life, fighting with all his powers to find egress into the clear, open spaces of his being, he is beaten to earth for the last time just when one feels that the clouds were lifting.\\\"</p><br><p align=right>henry miller, \\\"the time of the assassins\\\"</p><BR><BR><BR><BR><center>THEY SAY THAT BOGART<BR>KNEW IT FROM THE START<BR>THEY SAY THAT ERROL FLYNN<BR>LIKED TO WORK IT IN<BR>THEY SAY THAT CLARK GABLE<BR>ONCE DID BETTY GRABLE<BR>IN THE CLOAKROOM AT CHASEN\\\'S<BR><BR><i>\\\"Complimentary mustache ride, ma\\\'am?\\\"</i><BR><BR>That doesn\\\'t work.  emPHASis on the wrong sylLABle.<BR><BR>WORKS IF YOU SING IT, MARY.<BR><BR><i>They say if you never left home, you\\\'d never have to go back.</i><BR><BR>THE NEW KIDS ON THE BLOCK KILLED LAURA PALMER</center><BR><br><p align=right>graffiti, south beach, 1992</p></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote><BR><BR><BR><BR><i><b>november 21, 2007</i></b><p align=justify>you don\\\'t ask if you can drive.  you enjoy riding shotgun, the chance to entertain, look through her ipod, roll a joint on a partly-folded roadmap.  there isn\\\'t time to ask.  she picks you up at a pharmacy miles from town where you wait out front on a metal bench, the manager emerging to smoke a cigarette and eye your backpack and guitar and the bottle of orange juice you felt compelled to buy as gratuity for the use of their restroom.  you try to make small talk but must come across as shifty, breaking eye contact every fifteen seconds to check with the highway.  as he\\\'s turning to go back in, you see her volkswagen pull up to the light.  she never apologizes for being late.<BR><BR>she tells her boyfriend she\\\'s taking qigong lessons and until very recently was.  now you both go every other week.  (or every third week.  something like that.)  men and woman are trained separately at your school, but people tend to bump into each other.  she tends bar at a club downtown and her boyfriend works the door.  he looks like he could peel some onions, but you don\\\'t think it\\\'ll go that route.<BR><BR>\\\"when did you say you\\\'d be home from class?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"ten or eleven.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"but he\\\'s working tonight.  he won\\\'t get home \\\'til two or three o\\\'clock, anyway.\\\"<BR><BR>she sneers.  \\\"i\\\'d at <u>least</u> like to do <u>some</u>thing of what i told him i would.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"oh, look at this.  her\\\'s getting a conscience all of a sudden.\\\"  she looks over grinning and punches you in the shoulder then turns back to the road.  as you ride in silence, you watch how her face, framed by a blur of cypress, is suddenly cast tangerine by a lone streetlight hanging in the wilds of shady hills.</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>b-town.  you get a room at the oaks, register under your name, pay with her money.  she never asks for the change.  later, when she awakens you, you won\\\'t know where you are.  you\\\'ll fall back asleep, and though a few elements of an interrupted dream will return, its star will have up and gone, one of those people whose voice is among the things you miss most about them, their conversations with you.  these days, those voices are available only in dreams or on tape, either saying the same things every time or spouting absurdities, viciously deadpan.  halfway awake again, you will wonder at how one man will make a prayer of his life while another will make a box.  however finely appointed.  however halting the tongue.<BR><BR>\\\"what do you want for christmas?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"take me to chuck e. cheese.\\\"  she gestures with the remote.<BR><BR>\\\"what the fuck for?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"it would be fun.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i mean, i\\\'d rather take a beating.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"asked me what i wanted.\\\"  she turns back to the t.v.<BR><BR>\\\"okay.  you got it.\\\"<BR><BR>(i don\\\'t know how far the cabal has spread, so i\\\'ll just go ahead and tell you that chuck e. cheese\\\'s is a franchised pizza emporium/arcade where the children like to hold their annual councils and exact tribute from peers and family indiscriminately.  the older restaurants, built during my youth, were quite elaborate, featuring hourly shows with music and costumes and pipe organs that shat bubbles.  chuck himself is a big fucking rat, always a nice mascot to have in the hospitality industry.)<BR><BR>the key goes into a slotted box beside the office door.  you can see the old man and woman watching television in a parlor behind the desk.  the old woman\\\'s eating something from a plate on her lap, licking her fingers before wiping them on a napkin.  she feels you watching, glances up and starts to rise, thinking you need towels or want to chat.  she\\\'s known you for about five hundred years.  the duration.  she used to play the part of Checkout Lady at the grocery store.  asked how old you were, you\\\'d hold up two or three fingers.  you wave and turn and walk away, get in the car and wait.<BR><BR>\\\"where to?\\\"</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>another joint outside the restaurant.  your waitress used to buy you beer during the latter years of your minority.  she was in her mid-twenties then, going out with your friend wes.  cheryl knew the change game, too.  you had to watch how much money you gave her.  it takes her a full fifteen minutes to recognize you, having recently moved back to town after being gone for nearly twenty years.  she seems nervous, the cheeriness forced.  people usually ask the young woman where she\\\'s from, but cheryl blinks once at her accent and moves right along like she doesn\\\'t want to engage you any more than necessary.<BR><BR>outside, waiting in the car while you pay with some more of her money, the young woman has spotted the applebee\\\'s across the highway and wants to have a drink before she drops you off.  you hesitate, but the fireside\\\'s closed indefinitely, and you can\\\'t think of anyplace else you\\\'d feel comfortable taking her.<BR><BR>and as you feared, applebee\\\'s has become the default reunion site.  walking through the parking lot, you spot bert\\\'s suv and dave\\\'s bike, as well as a late-model thunderbird with athens-clarke county plates.  they\\\'re at two adjoining tables in the outer bar area.  taking them in with a brief glance, you lock eyes with sam for a second then look away, guiding the young woman to a place at the bar proper where they won\\\'t be in your line of vision.<BR><BR>she orders a mimosa then breaks a minute\\\'s silence by going to the restroom.  twenty hot seconds later, tommy gets up and makes his way over to you.  you pretend not to notice.  after the initial glance, you\\\'ve been regarding them solely with your peripheral vision, and you don\\\'t turn when he sits on the barstool beside you.<BR><BR>he\\\'s wearing a plain white t-shirt and jeans, a baseball cap and tennis shoes, like he thinks this is how people dress here now.  forward intelligence (in the person of your mother and sister) has kept you abreast of everyone\\\'s movements and activities while you\\\'ve been in exile.  during the eightteen years or so since you\\\'ve seen him last, tommy\\\'s become a financial analyst on wall street.  he lives on the upper east side, competes in triathalons, is still single.  his family moved here when you were in second grade.  you and david murphy were his first friends.<BR><BR>dave\\\'s holding court over at the tables, drunk.  nothing unusual for him on a wednesday night, much less one that falls before a holiday.  you\\\'ve heard every word he\\\'s broadcast since you walked in, telling them about getting lost in tampa, his adventures while wandering around for hours looking for his car.  <i>there were hos runnin\\\' every which a\\\' way.</i>  in contrast to some, you see dave every time you come up.  he\\\'s a fixture.  you couldn\\\'t run him off with a stick.  he would have come over before too much longer.  it\\\'s been awkward for him, too, like being a child of divorce.<BR><BR>tommy says, \\\"we all couldn\\\'t help but notice that you look a lot like this guy we used to know.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"did this fellow you\\\'re talking about have three balls?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"not when we knew him.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, i didn\\\'t grow it in my spare time, son.  stroll on.\\\"<BR><BR>then you\\\'re both grinning, and though neither of you offers a hand, you can tell sanctions have been lifted.  he says, \\\"jeez.  how are you?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i\\\'m still here.  still here with all of you, apparently.  can\\\'t get shed of ya.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah.  doesn\\\'t look like it.\\\"  he leans back some, so as to take you all in.  \\\"well, sir, i have to tell you - i\\\'ve heard some very disturbing shit about you over the years.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i believe it.  it disturbed <u>me</u> pretty good.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"so i gather.\\\"<BR><BR>you look down at the bar and raise your eyebrows and nod a little.  laugh through your nose.  tommy turns and looks behind you.  the young woman puts her hand on your shoulder.  introductions.  an invitation to join them.  you grab your soda and walk over and look at each of them in turn:  \\\"robert.  raymond.  david.  samuel.  jonathan.\\\"<BR><BR>bert says, \\\"beanass.\\\"  they erupt with laughter.  as jon goes to see about moving to the banquette up front, you explain to the young woman how peanut laid that nickname on you in second grade.  because you have a small ass.<BR><BR>dave says, \\\"she\\\'s like, \\\'yeah, who <u>you</u> tellin\\\', chief?\\\'\\\"<BR><BR>you say, \\\"i thought there was a moratorium on that.\\\"<BR><BR>ray:  \\\"on what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"on calling me that.\\\"<BR><BR>tommy says, \\\"i don\\\'t think there was ever anything official.  it just kind of fell into disuse.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, it can keep on fallin\\\'.\\\"<BR><BR>your group slowly moves up into the outer course of tables, like a school of dead jellyfish being cast up onto a beach.  after ten minutes of chit-chat, the young woman begs off, says she still has to go to wal-mart.  \\\"i don\\\'t know why i have to cook.  if i was home, tomorrow would just be another thursday.\\\"  you walk her to the car and get your things.<BR><BR>\\\"can one of them give you a ride home?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah, i\\\'m sure.  it\\\'s no big deal.  i could walk there in about half an hour....  i\\\'ll call you tomorrow.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"e-mail.\\\"<BR><BR>you kiss her.  over her shoulder, you see dave and ray kneeling on the seat of the banquette and watching you through the plate glass window.  ray\\\'s at least a foot taller than dave, lanky, wry, brooding, and in his old age has taken to wearing glasses with aviator frames and semi-transparent lenses that dave tells him make him look like a cop.  he dealt up until about six years ago, when he and his wife got busted and would have gone up the road but for a faulty warrant.  scared straight, more or less, he and fauna moved back to town, onto his parents\\\'s ranch.  dave says the glasses are just more evidence that he\\\'s now in the employ of the federal government, working it off.<BR><BR>when dave sees you looking, he turns sideways and puts his hands out in front of him and starts pumping his hips.  the rest of you have at least eight inches on him, which is way ironic, considering he was the tallest by far until everybody turned eleven or so.  and he used to lord it over all of you like he was hot shit.  skeeter got his comeuppance.  did he not.<BR><BR>ray grins and gives you the thumb\\\'s up.  you take your hand from the young woman\\\'s hip and wave them off.  she breaks away and turns to look, but they\\\'ve seated themselves.<BR><BR>\\\"what is it?\\\" she asks.<BR><BR>\\\"nothing.  just the fools i seem destined to spend my life with.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"be glad you have them.  some people\\\'s fools are dead.\\\"</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>you go back in, pack on your back, tumbler of soda in one hand, guitar in the other.  the hostess gives you a coy little smile.  you can\\\'t figure out why some women decide it\\\'s flirting time only after they\\\'ve seen that you\\\'re attached.  as you approach the banquette, jon calls out, \\\"oh!  woody guthrie, back from the dead!  sing us a little bit of it:  <i>this land is your land, this land is my land.</i>\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"shut the fuck up.\\\"<BR><BR>you ask if they think it would be alright to put your stuff in an empty adjacent booth.  jon says, \\\"yeah, we\\\'re buyin\\\' much oh aitch tonight.  they won\\\'t say anything.\\\"  he\\\'s a cardiac surgeon in spring hill, twice divorced.<BR><BR>you offer to sit in the chair out in the aisle, but bert says, \\\"i got it.  s\\\'awright.\\\"  you slide in beside ray, who puts his arm around you along the top of the seat.  he takes your drink and sniffs it, examines your eyes.  you say, \\\"get off me, man.\\\"<BR><BR>dave says, \\\"so where\\\'dja find her, king salami?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"the findin\\\' place.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, you\\\'ll hafta give me directions sometime.\\\"<BR><BR>dave has never married, owns a tree service but spends much of his time in bars with women and guy friends.  he and jon are road dogs.  each other\\\'s wingmen.  a little later, a group of girls two booths down that they must know starts to get a little rowdy, and jon says, \\\"you young ladies need to pipe down.\\\"<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"simma down!  simma down, now!\\\"<BR><BR>one of them, a stunning blonde, clutches her breast and jogs it a little, saying, \\\"eat the left one.\\\"<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"i think that can be arranged.\\\"<BR><BR>bert says, \\\"she looks like a certain friend of coleslaw here.\\\"  tommy grins.  (his last name\\\'s cole, and he always seemed to have the cabbage.)<BR><BR>sam:  \\\"is she still a <a href=\\\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weeki_Wachee_Springs\\\">mermaid?</a>\\\"<BR><BR>tommy:  \\\"yeah.  i dunno.  part-time, maybe.  she got married to some guy from chicago.  probably happy as a clam.\\\"<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"well, who isn\\\'t?\\\"  you all laugh.  dave turns to sam:  \\\"what about that girl you used to go out with?  the one with the...\\\"  he pantomimes curves then holds his hands a foot away from either side of his head.  \\\"...hair?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you mean lori?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah.  whatever happened to her?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"she became a nun.  believe it or not.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"wow.  what a waste.\\\"<BR><BR>jon:  \\\"a nun.\\\"<BR><BR>sam:  \\\"well, she joined a religious order.  that\\\'s what she is, basically.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"religious order.  sacrificin\\\' chupacabras, shit like that?\\\"<BR><BR>sam shrugs.<BR><BR>\\\"okay, marilyn, whatever.\\\"<BR><BR>you\\\'re probably the only one left in the universe who still calls him sam.  he usually goes by beau, his middle name.  after earning a business degree from the university of south florida, he decided to try to make a go at being a professional musician, drumming for the impotent sea snakes, which you gather was somewhat of a novelty combo:</P><P><center><img src=\\\"http://i452.photobucket.com/albums/qq241/mmm679/ISS_96_01.jpg\\\"/><BR>(that\\\'s him second from the left.)</center><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>after leaving the group (due to some difficulty with the vitamin C), he ran an escort service in tampa (called \\\"beau\\\'s hos\\\", according to dave) before moving to athens and owning a succession of bars, one of which was originally called \\\"boba fett\\\'s\\\" after a nickname he picked up somewhere along the way.<BR><BR>you say, \\\"heard you ran afoul of george lucas, there, mister samuels.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah, we had to cease and desist.\\\"<BR><BR>bert:  \\\"mister samuels?\\\"<BR><BR>you:  \\\"\\\'beau samuels\\\'.  that\\\'s his pseudonym, evidently.\\\"<BR><BR>jon:  \\\"his scene name.  got to make that scene.\\\"<BR><BR>you:  \\\"you aren\\\'t makin\\\' porn under that name, are you?  like on some other planet where they don\\\'t have google?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"not that i know of.\\\"<BR><BR>jon:  \\\"not that you know of?  that\\\'s your answer?  nice.\\\"  he and sam are both half-italian/half-WASP, and so they tend to rank on each other far more than they do on the rest of you.<BR><BR>it continues on for another half-hour, the group dynamic falling back into its old rhythms, until ray points to a t.v. hanging over the bar:  \\\"there\\\'s andy\\\'s little brother.\\\"<BR><BR>tommy turns and checks it out.  \\\"andy didn\\\'t make it, did he?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"didn\\\'t look like it.\\\"<BR><BR>tommy asks, \\\"did y\\\'all ever see him when you were in gainesville?\\\"  looks at you and jon.<BR><BR>you ask jon, \\\"you remember when we were at the hardback that time he was there?\\\"  to tommy:  \\\"we were standing by the bar when he came up for a pitcher or something, and he kept glancing at us like he thought we looked familiar, but he didn\\\'t say anything.\\\"<BR><BR>jon:  \\\"everybody in town thought he was a major asshole.\\\"<BR><BR>you:  \\\"well, you think everybody\\\'s an asshole.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"lots of people are,\\\" he says cryptically.  not so cryptically.<BR><BR>you:  \\\"that was the only time i saw him when jon was around, but about a year after that, i was chillin\\\' at this guy <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3879\\\">marcus</a>\\\'s house, this dealer that i met through <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=3896\\\">darryl</a>, like on a saturday afternoon, five or six people there, smokin\\\' out, listening to neil young or whatever, and he comes in with his girlfriend, and everybody just plays it off, acts like it\\\'s no big whoop.  they sit on the couch, and he keeps checking me out like maybe he thinks he knows me somehow.  the bong goes around a few times, everybody\\\'s zonin\\\' out, and he\\\'s next up in the rotation, and this girl is trying to pass it to him, but he\\\'s just kind of oblivious, got his eyes shut, listening to the music, and she\\\'s gettin\\\' kind of flustered, doesn\\\'t know what to do, doesn\\\'t want to bother him, but it\\\'s his turn, so i say, <i>hey, andy, you\\\'re up, man</i>, and he doesn\\\'t react at first, nobody does because nobody there was named andy, so they don\\\'t know if i\\\'m high or what.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"so his girlfriend nudges him, and i guess it comes to him, because his head snaps up, and he stares at me for like thirty seconds straight, everybody else is starting to freak out a little bit, then he breaks into a grin and says, <i>i\\\'ll be damned.</i>  i got up and stepped into the kitchen and motioned for him to come in there so we could talk, and he was really nice.  really down to earth, believe it or not.  he said the year or whatever it was that they were here was just about the only normal childhood he ever had.  i told \\\'im god help him if he thought we were normal.\\\"</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>around ten o\\\'clock, tommy announces that he has to go.  bert says, \\\"that\\\'s good, because we\\\'re gonna need an hour or two to talk about you.\\\"  he promises to come by your parents\\\'s house the next day after dinner.<BR><BR>\\\"yeah, they\\\'d all like to see you,\\\" you say.  \\\"just stay away from my sister.\\\"  he pulls something halfway between a grimace and a grin.  still a bit of a sore spot.  dave tells him he\\\'ll see him there, having arranged to drop by to look at a live oak that your dad said was leaning badly, threatening to fall on the garage.<BR><BR>tommy leaves in a rented lexus.  five minutes later, the conversation has wound around to jon saying, \\\"any one of us could do what he does.  some of us.\\\"<BR><BR>bert says, \\\"would you like to make a list?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"of what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"of which of us you think could do what he does.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"uh, no, i don\\\'t believe i\\\'d care to.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"uh-huh.\\\"  bert is an assistant football coach at the high school.  also teaches p.e. and driver\\\'s ed.  he\\\'s always been the voice of reason.<BR><BR>jon:  \\\"he\\\'s a glorified bookie.\\\"<BR><BR>sam:  \\\"actually, he\\\'s a glorified oddsmaker.\\\"<BR><BR>you visit the restroom.  when you return, dave is standing at the bar watching something on TRUtv - two black women beating the hell out of each other - and discussing it with a dark-haired young woman who looks vaguely familiar.  you stand with them and watch it for a second.  after the two women, it moves on to other acts of mutual combat caught by convenience store surveillance cameras, network affiliates, cell phones.  jon joins you.  they tell the young woman they\\\'re surgeons.  presently the young woman asks dave, \\\"so, what kind of surgery do <u>you</u> do?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"mostly amputations.\\\"<BR><BR>everybody decides to repair to ray and fauna\\\'s for a smoke.  jon can\\\'t go; something about having to wake up early to drive to naples, to his mom\\\'s house.  you stand alone with him in the parking lot and wish him well.  you say, \\\"you know, we all used to be the same.  pretty much.  what happened exactly?  it\\\'s like we were a beam of light that passed through a prism or something.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"<u>life</u> happened.  what the hell do you think?  it\\\'s okay - we\\\'ll probably all end up in the same nursing home.  those of us who make it that far.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"care to make a list?  wanna handicap it?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"nah.  fuck the odds.\\\"<BR><BR>everybody else is sitting in bert\\\'s tahoe, waiting on you.  you say, \\\"well, i\\\'m not waitin\\\' \\\'til the nursing home.  i\\\'m gonna move in with tommy.  i mean like right after new year\\\'s.  watch me.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i don\\\'t doubt it.  not for a second.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"so.  see you at the reunion this summer?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"nah.  they don\\\'t want me to go down there.  i\\\'d take a gun with me.  two guns.\\\"</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>you see town from the back seat.  ray\\\'s rolling a joint, and dave sits between you, on the hump.  you pass the former site of ye olde fireside inne.  \\\"what happened to the fireside?\\\" you ask.<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"burnt down!\\\" (laughter) \\\"many\\\'s the night i was sittin\\\' in there that i said, <i>mister inne, we are too close to that damn fire.  we need to back up some.</i>  and wouldn\\\'t you know, he never listened to me.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i mean, do they think it was insurance fraud or something?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i have no idea.  ask agent mcguire here.\\\"<BR><BR>ray pretends to be exasperated:  \\\"the feds wouldn\\\'t be investigating something like that.  that\\\'s in the local jurisdiction.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"see - what\\\'d i tell you?  knows about jurisdictions and whatnot.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"shut up.\\\"<BR><BR>at a stoplight, you watch a boy and a girl run down the sidewalk on opposite sides of the street.  the boy hides behind a hedge and waits until the girl goes past and turns to look for him, call his name.  he jumps out and runs over to her.  you can hear their laughter through the open window.<BR><BR>dave thinks he knows a woman in a pontiac, wants bert to turn around so he can see if it\\\'s her:  \\\"i could tell for sure if i could see her in the light.\\\"<BR><BR>bert:  \\\"yes, but at night, it\\\'s dark, and all hot blonde girls will tend to resemble one another.\\\"<BR><BR>at the bottom of snob hill, bert gives the road a cursory glance, rolls through a stop sign and almost gets t-boned by a truck coming around the curve.<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"cutler, ya big round-headed son a bitch, what\\\'re ya tryin\\\' to do to us?\\\"<BR><BR>sam:  \\\"i can see the headlines now.\\\"<BR><BR>you:  \\\"so where was ty tonight?\\\"<BR><BR>ray:  \\\"oh, he\\\'s moved up to the big time.  he usually avoids us like the plague.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"and what about peanut?  anybody know what\\\'s become \\\'a him?\\\"<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"last i heard, he was livin\\\' in some crack hole down in saint pete.\\\"<BR><BR>ray:  \\\"i kinda feel guilty, seein\\\' as how i was the one who got him high for the first time.\\\"<BR><BR>sam:  \\\"kinda took the ball and ran with it, didn\\\'t he?\\\"<BR><BR>you pass the turn-off for your parents\\\'s house.  bert:  \\\"let\\\'s go see if matt\\\'s home.\\\"<BR><BR>you:  \\\"i\\\'ll bet he isn\\\'t.\\\"<BR><BR>you enter the lake lindsay metropolitan area.  the lake lindsay vortex, you suspect.  central to it is <a href=\\\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinsegut_Hill_Manor_House\\\">chinsegut hill</a>, the highest point in the county.  raymond robins, big white socialist, named it that.  he claimed to have made his fortune in the alaska gold fields, and \\\"chinsegut\\\" is supposedly an innuit word meaning \\\"the spirit of lost things regained.\\\"  prior to moving to brooksville, he was the head of the red cross mission to russia after the revolution.  he was also working for american intelligence, trying to keep russia in the war against germany.  at times, he was the only liason between the revolutionaries and the u.s.<BR><BR>he and his wife were major contributors to brooksville\\\'s life of the mind, founding the public library, the first bookstore (in a private home downtown), sponsoring chatauquas twice a year, establishing local civic organizations for young people.  a nationally-known speaker, he caused a sensation when he disappeared for two months, finally turning up in a small town in north carolina claiming amnesia.  eddie money, a former history professor and a friend of your father, considers him some sort of charlatan.  \\\"a highly complex man\\\" at the very least.  but that might just be eddie.  you have a standing arrangement with your sister that you will pay her fifty bucks if she will ask him if he has two tickets to paradise.  she never takes you seriously, knowing that you rarely have fifty bucks to your name.<BR><BR>during the red scare, years after the robins had passed away and the property had been deeded to the u.s. department of agriculture and the university of florida, some boy scouts discovered a bronze plaque that had been set into the ground near the house commemorating robins\\\'s friendship with lenin.  the city fathers got on the horn to washingston, and the u.s.d.a. site superintendent was ordered to dig it up.  it was taken to a smithy, melted down, and dropped into the middle of the lake.<BR><BR>almost twenty years later, the community\\\'s other illustrious personages moved into a commune on the far shore.  the first your peer group saw of them was at the methodist church down the road from ray\\\'s house, in the middle of the Great Go-Cart Craze of 1979.  on saturday afternoons all that spring (at least until the novelty wore off), jakie, the church\\\'s wizened handyman, would help you all build your own carts out of scrap.  <i>beanass won\\\'t sell us no wheels,</i> was the rueful cry upon the land.  you had the market cornered.<BR><BR>the boy who was your age told you his name was andy.  later, after jon ran into them at the flea market and heard his mother call him by his real name, he told you how the rich boys in winter park had made fun of him and decided that he wouldn\\\'t let it happen again in the new town.  none of you cared.  it was the south.  you knew a boy named rafael pickleseimer.  you knew identical twins named eugene and newgene.  you even knew hippie kids - a girl named easter sunny day, in particular.<BR><BR>for all you saw of him, he might as well have been living in a parallel dimension:  they weren\\\'t enrolled in school, didn\\\'t attend a church, he wasn\\\'t active in cub scouts.  lake lindsay was pretty much the only overlap.  swimming in the lake, playing in the tree fort in the woods beside ray\\\'s house, hide and seek in the graveyard at night.  and at the movies all that summer, the kiddie matinees.<BR><BR>that spring, his father changed their surname to something which made the boy\\\'s name only slightly less whimsical (their last name had been bottum), and sometime that winter, they disappeared.  the next you saw of him was on television two years later.  your brother called you into the den:  <i>am i crazy, or is that that hippie kid?</i>  you had to wait until the closing credits to make sure.<BR><BR>his family had escaped a cult in which he had been sexually abused since early childhood, stowing away on a freighter from venezuela to florida.  they lived with his maternal grandparents in winter park for about six months before moving to brooksville, and from there went to california.  in an interview, he claimed to have been celibate from ages 10 to 14.  you did not major in mathematics, but you can count fairly well, and can determine that he was eight when he arrived in brooksville and nine when he left.<BR><BR>in 2003, during the six months you were warehoused in the assisted living facility a quarter mile from ray\\\'s parents\\\'s place, you would sometimes sneak out at night and walk down to the lake, passing the tree where your brother bought it, the church, the cemetary, down to the site of the former commune.  all that remained was an abandoned house and a trailer.  you\\\'d sit on a stump at the edge of the overgrown lawn and try to find the thread again, summon yourself back to yourself.  it took a long time, until years after you\\\'d exiled yourself to new port richey.<BR><BR>back with the guys, almost to ray\\\'s, you ask if he remembers seeing you back then on the road at night.  \\\"i can remember seeing quite a bit of your middle finger.\\\"<BR><BR>dave:  \\\"heh, heh, heh.\\\"</P><HR><P ALIGN=JUSTIFY>ray tells bert to turn his lights out and park behind the barn.  he sends you to the door alone.  the others hide in a stand of trees fifty feet from the house.  fauna turns on the porch light and opens the door.  you say, \\\"boodgie, boodgie, boodgie!  what\\\'s good, mama?\\\"  you only belatedly notice the .38 in the pocket of her robe.<BR><BR>she studies you a second, steps out and looks around.  \\\"ray had better be here someplace, by god.\\\"  laughter from out in the trees.  \\\"goddamnit, ray!\\\"  she turns back to you:  \\\"i\\\'m telling you right now:  i\\\'m not gonna take any shit off of you.  i\\\'ve got children in this house.  you cross me, you\\\'re gettin\\\' the cops called on you.  or shot.  maybe both.\\\"<BR><BR>the others have walked up during this.  ray says, \\\"naw, baby, he\\\'s cool.  it\\\'s not like that.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, alright.\\\"  she hugs you hard.  \\\"don\\\'t ever do anything like that again, okay?\\\"<BR><BR>ray\\\'s parents have gone to louisiana to be with his sister and her family.  fauna takes you into the kitchen and seats you at the table, goes back to making potato salad while the guys mill around the pool.  you talk for a little while then glance at the betty boop clock over the refrigerator.  \\\"jeez.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i was supposed to be at mom and dad\\\'s a couple of hours ago.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"so call.\\\"<BR><BR>your mom says, \\\"oh, bert\\\'s there?  let me speak to him.\\\"  he takes the cordless phone into the den and returns five minutes later:  \\\"she put the arm on me.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah, she\\\'s good at that.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"she said i was not to let you do anything you shouldn\\\'t and that it was okay for you to stay at my place tonight, to have you home by ten in the morning.  she told me she still knew people at the county office, said she could make life difficult for me if she had to.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"you know who she knows down there?  joanne mcnair.  in purchasing.  that\\\'s exactly who.  what\\\'s she gonna do?  monkey around with your jockstrap order?\\\"<BR><BR>the guys head out to the barn to look at ray\\\'s hot rod.  dave says, \\\"you know what i realized the other day?  jesus could leave all the doors open he wanted to.  people would ask \\\'im, <i>what?  were you born in a barn?</i>  then they\\\'d be like, <i>oh, yeah, that\\\'s right.  sorry.</i>\\\"<BR><BR>ray\\\'s the last one out.  he winks, tells the both of you he has his eye on you.  fauna takes you down the hall and into the rooms of her sleeping children, meredith, four, and jimmy, four months.  the baby\\\'s named after her brother, killed two years previously in afghanistan.<BR><BR>you go out by the pool so she can smoke a cigarette.  you sit on lounge chairs and watch the water.  \\\"fauna?  dear child?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"what?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"i feel obligated to tell you that should our raymond ever falter in the line of duty, you can rest assured that i\\\'m still workin\\\' the night shift.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"pass.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"oh, i wouldn\\\'t be so hasty.  i might have a new trick or two up my sleeve.\\\"<BR><BR>she pretends to consider it, looking up at one section of the ceiling then another.  \\\"even so.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"well, am i at least on the list?\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yeah.  right after produce.  and my grandpa.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"yes.  still got it.\\\"<BR><BR>the baby wakes up five minutes later.  she lets you give him his bottle.  he has his uncle\\\'s eyes.  after he\\\'s done, you walk him around, burp him.  you take him up to the screened enclosure and show him the cows standing along the fence watching.  \\\"look.  big black and white ones.  moo!\\\"  he laughs.<BR><BR>fauna says, \\\"don\\\'t get him all riled up.  he won\\\'t go back to sleep.\\\"<BR><BR>\\\"okay.\\\"<BR><BR>you show him the stars, the clouds, his father and his father\\\'s friends walking back from the barn.  you want to tell him something useful, something true, but nothing comes to mind.  you end up making a wordless commitment:  <i>you see those trees, that horizon?  just on the other side of that is the future.  i\\\'ll see you there, alright?</i></p>'),(4406,'<p>as a research project for an a free online writing course, i had decided to write about going to a therapist from the mind of a person very different from me.  i invented a businessman and i invented some generic problems for him to discuss with a therapist.  i spent my beer money on a bad suit from value village that managed to not look so bad as long as i kept moving.  the secret with that suit is that you can\\\'t stop.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i combed my hair very precisely and i shaved.  i wore the suit and knocked on his door.  he was a gentle man and listened to my non specific problems about not doing well at work and worrying about paying all the bills.  for most of the time, he just listened to me, nodding and encouraging me to talk more.  i had gone five times and he still hadn\\\'t asked me anything.  i asked him if he was going to ask me anything but all he said was that he needed time to learn about who i was.  i felt satisfied for the guy i was playing.  i wanted him to do well.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the sixth time i showed up to the office, i saw in the hallway another person reading something that had been posted on the door. the door was locked.  i walked my suit over to him and asked him what was going on.  he told me the therapist had been a fake, with no accreditation whatsoever, and that the police finally caught up with him.  the police notice on the door looked official, and had a number for patients to call.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i asked the guy out for a beer.  he was in his forties, a t-shirt, and jeans.  we talked about our problems.  i wanted to tell him about my ruse, but i couldn\\\'t figure out how to explain it.  his main concern was if his wife was happy.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n '),(4403,'<center><img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/4403_1.jpg\\\" width=\\\"300\\\"></center>\r\n\r\nEverything in my house is bathed in yellow. Bright, psychedelic yellow. I can\\\'t justly explain it, and cant seem to take an accurate photo of what\\\'s really no more than just ambient light, but its amazing. You\\\'d think bright yellow light would be really irritating; at least that was the first thing I thought when it happened, but its quite nice, actually.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhen the big overhead light burns out in my house, its a substantial chore to change it. Its a series of parallel ten-foot long fluorescent tubes chained between a joist beam and the fire-sprinkler pipe free-floating about twenty feet above. Plus, I neither have a ladder nor the skill to pull off that high-wire balancing act without sending shards of phosphor-powdered glass into the nooks and crannies of every single thing I own, which is apparently another curse of living in one giant room. If you spill a beer, burn the bread you\\\'re baking, or have epic gas its bound to reach every corner, all the way from the \\\"bedroom\\\" part, through the \\\"kitchen\\\" and over to the \\\"office\\\" and \\\"workshop\\\". It sort of feels like living on a boat, for some reason, and indeed if you drink enough, it starts to rock in the brackish harbor-tide just so.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBefore I even \\\"borrowed\\\" the ladder to climb up with, I already planned to just go on and do something about the funky, garish, industrial, impossibly fluoro lights once and for all, or live in a lit-like-an-industrial-showroom hell all winter. Walked to Home Depot last weekend too far in the rain and bought some wiring, a junction box and even some of those little plastic caps you usually find all over construction sites that screw onto electrical connections and look like little red-caps or miniature 12g shells or something. Hacking this into the existing switch and overhead circuit would be easy-peasy, and it was, and happily happened with no incident, unfortunate missteps, shockingly-shocking shocks or spontaneous losses of balance.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI was proud, and proudly so, I paced to the breaker-box to activate my handiwork, and realized that I had laughably managed to forget to purchase the key element, a bulb; and it was one of those sockets that takes a 500w outdoor floodlight, which they don\\\'t usually stock at the <a href=\\\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/rhodamine/2479227529/\\\">Amplo bodega</a>.<br><br>\r\n\r\nBack to Home Depot, but this time late... and it\\\'s two degrees above freezing and your ears burn from the wind-bite because your headphones aren\\\'t big enough to act as earmuffs but you\\\'re a man on a mission and you bounce to the fucking light bulb section ASAP, skip past the self-checkout and actually manage to score a car back to the Wick, having spent enough time walking the wrong direction through Bed-Stuy this week already, thanks. You grab the ladder from where it\\\'s stashed in the basement elevator pit, pop that mamacita into the socket and <i>finally</i> throw that switch with a flourish you\\\'ve been practicing for four days, and you\\\'re immediately bathed in lemon-yellow.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMislabeled bulb, cheeky karma for something you did, no doubt. But it\\\'s nice, actually. It\\\'s a laid-back shade of citrus and sort of makes everything look like it would if you were wearing polarized Ambervision goggles. It\\\'s the pallor of one of those high-contrast, over-saturated rock videos from like 2000, or perhaps the glare of one of those dystopic movies which sort of burns your eyes but not in an entirely bad way...<br>'),(4404,'You ask me for a Hamburger.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIf you asked me for a hamburger, and I gave you a raccoon.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIf you asked me for a hamburger, but it turns out I don’t really exist. Where I was originally standing, a picture of a hamburger rests on the ground.<br><br>\r\n\r\nYou awake as a hamburger. You start screaming only to have special sauce fly from your lips. The world is in sepia.<br><br>\r\n\r\nWhy are we speaking German? A mime cries softly as he cradles a young cow. Your grandfather stares at you as the cow falls apart into patties. You look down only to see me with pickles for eyes, I am singing the song that gives birth to the universe.<br><br>\r\n\r\nYou ask for a hamburger, I give you a hamburger. You raise it to your lips and take a bite. Your eye twitches involuntarily. Across the street a father of three falls down the stairs. You swallow and look down at the hamburger in your hands. I give you a hamburger. You swallow and look down at the hamburger in your hands. You cannot swallow. There are children at the top of the stairs. A pickle shifts uneasily under the bun. I give you a hamburger. You look at my face, and I am pleading with you. The children are crying now. You raise the hamburger to your lips, tears stream down your face as you take a bite. I give you a hamburger. You are on your knees. You plead with me to go across the street. I hear only children’s laughter. I give you a hamburger. You are screaming as you fall down the stairs. I am your child. You cannot see anything. You take a bite of the hamburger. The concrete rushes up to meet you. You awake with a start in your own bed. Your eye twitches involuntarily. I give you a hamburger. As you kill me, I do not make a sound. I give you a hamburger.<br><br>\r\n\r\nYou ask me for a hamburger. My attempt to reciprocate is cut brutally short as my body experiences a sudden lack of electrons. Across a variety of hidden dimensions you are dismayed. John Lennon hands me an apple, but it slips through my fingers. I am reborn as an ocelot. You disapprove. A crack echoes through the universe in defiance of conventional physics as cosmological background noise shifts from randomness to a perfect A Flat. Children everywhere stop what they are doing and hum along in perfect pitch with the background radiation. Birds fall from the sky as the sun engulfs the earth. You hesitate momentarily before allowing yourself to assume the locus of all knowledge. Entropy crumbles as you peruse the information contained within the universe. A small library in Phoenix ceases to exist. You stumble under the weight of everythingness, Your mouth opens up to cry out, and collapses around your body before blinking you out of the spatial plane. You exist only within the fourth dimension. The fountainhead of all knowledge rolls along the ground and collides with a small dog. My head tastes sideways as spacetime is reestablished, you blink back into the corporeal world disoriented, only for me to hand you a hamburger as my body collapses under the strain of reconstitution. The universe has reasserted itself. A particular small dog is fed steak for the rest of its natural life. You die in a freak accident moments later, and you soul works at the returns desk for the Phoenix library. You disapprove. Your disapproval sends ripples through the inter-dimensional void between life and death. A small child begins to cry as he walks toward the stairway where his father stands.'),(4405,'<p>\r\nAfter yoga class, I sneak out like a thief.  My stolen inner peace \r\nwrapped about me like a cloak, I make for the door.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes my mind is even quiet enough not to wonder how far \r\nI\\\'ll make it, that night.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes I don\\\'t even make it out the door.   A friend or\r\nacquaintance will ask me a question, or make some comment, and the\r\nsocial machinery lurches into motion, seeking relationships, spinning \r\nup at least enough language for a reply.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce language is in motion, I\\\'ve been caught.  The chatter\r\nchatter chatter of my thoughts will almost certainly arrest my inner\r\nquiet.  I\\\'ll still make out with a handful of calm from the practice,\r\nbut no big score that night.   \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut I\\\'m usually able to slip out with the goods.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThough sometimes, I only arrive on the street before passers-by in\r\nconversation with each other or admonishing their pets engage the\r\nnoisier bits of brain.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOther times, I\\\'ll only make it as far as  the corner, before someone\r\nasks me for directions, again spinning language up, and engaging\r\ngeography as well, to expand and fill the empty spaces created in the\r\npractice. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOnce or twice, as I cross the street I\\\'ve been nearly killed by an\r\ninattentive driver.  Nothing decimates my inner peace more quickly\r\nthan the hot flush of anger.  Thanks to the practice, it\\\'ll dissipate \r\nquickly, but not before it has stripped me of that night\\\'s haul.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nOften I\\\'ll make it past Church St., past the not-on-a-corner\r\ncorner-store, and the bars, and the outdoor smokers between drinks, \r\nwith my inner quiet still intact.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes past the steps of the Safeway, where the homeless and the\r\ngutter-punks tend to congregate, without encountering a dramatic\r\nincident.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSometimes, past the Mint, in between Karaoke numbers and\r\nmore smokers between drinks.  If I make it this far, inner\r\nquiet still in place, my odds of a good haul get markedly better.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNext up is the corner store favored by the more upright homeless\r\nfolks of my neighborhood, who will still sometimes take a last\r\npanhandling potshot at my inner peace.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd then off the street, the elevator to my apartment is the final\r\nstretch of the gauntlet.  Neighbors, friends of neighbors, and the\r\noccasional pizza delivery man, all potentially waiting to add some\r\nnoise to my quieted mind.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nBut sometimes, just rarely, I can make it all the way back to my\r\napartment, with my inner peace still intact.  My stolen treasure safe.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nUntil morning, at least.\r\n</p>\r\n'),(4408,'my mom naked and pregnant in someone\\\'s house in lake tahoe.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n                       is it the same house where megs ripped her lip open on the dishwasher while sparrowe was washing her hair in the bathroom and linda drove her to the truckee ER on the shoulder the whole way screaming out the window? linda huran\\\'s house. we said linda huran like it was one word, so fast that it sounded like lindawan and we refused to stop even after the wedding to rick and the wilson name change. linda was loud and from chicago. she went to high school with john belushi. everyone used to say she was voted funniest in her class instead of him. she called her enormous tits her puppies. my little sister adored her.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n                         my mom is skinny, pointy shoulders and nice legs, they used to say you could only tell she was preggo if she turned to the side. i don\\\'t know if she still had it then but before i was born she drove a 68 GTO for a while. it had glass packs, she would tell me, as if i understood what that meant. she was trying to say that everyone turned to stare as a 98 pound California angel roared by in ridiculous michigan muscle. her ex husband sold her pinto one day while she was out. traded it and his guitar, that she bought him for a present, for the GTO. when she left him, it was all she got. she tore out of chicago in it, back to berkeley and graduate school and food stamps and meditation and the hall closet that was my first bedroom.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n             my mom in this tahoe house, sun-bleached hair and really pregnant so it must have been a summer month. i was two weeks late being born. she says that set the stage for the rest of my life. she was up in tahoe, tanning and relaxing, with janet and their gang. crazy women. i know them my whole life, they take turns with me staying at their place. i meet a lot of boyfriends and husbands and dudes with pockets full of things.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n                          we would go up to that tahoe house winter or summer and rick\\\'s friend mike was often there with his wife cindy. i spent time with the guys while my mom and janet and linda went out. dad and rick would take me for hikes and we\\\'d throw rocks and talk about birds. they taught me how to play sunshine of your love and rebel rebel on rick\\\'s guitar. mike was the guy who was always up when i got up in the morning. he\\\'d be in the hot tub with a beer. he taught me the words to \\\"keep your hands to yourself\\\" by the georgia satellites—he sang it to himself a lot under his breath. i loved it. he was a sexy blond guy with an aging surfer\\\'s body. always tan and somehow never in shoes. i heard he died not too long ago in a boating accident. i put that record on and sang my ass off in his honor.\r\n\r\n'),(4409,'<p>When your personal boundaries dictate that you can\\\'t really write online about your relationship, what else do you write about?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I used to write about my dreams a lot, and I used to keep an online journal, like a lot of people. And I wonder now, sometimes, where all that creative energy evaporates to? Is it toward arguments? Have I sought out an academic lifestyle so I can hone my argument skills to a fine point, ever refining my language and ability to articulate, defend and theorize? Will I eventually theorize my way to singledom? Let\\\'s not talk about that here. It\\\'s inappropriate. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>We can talk (and type) instead about the moment of deja vu I had tonight, while at a talk, a curatorial lecture actually. If that\\\'s what you can really call an hour of your time spent listening to an overeducated person try to academically rationalize a fascination with rock concert riot footage that is circulated online. Is chair smashing at guns and roses and bit torrent downloading really something that needs to be theorized, or is it just more lucrative to write about it in academic circles than it is on your blog?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The thing the really got me, is that it pushed my brain back, way back, to when I wrote a little piece called <i>beautiful things beg to be broken</i>. For me, that piece was important. It wasn\\\'t the best written piece of writing I ever managed, but it was about something real. Something that was inside that wanted to get out, that needed to get out. It was, in a loose way, about the shattering of perfection for the sake of loving that perfection. About obsession that can\\\'t become possession, and denial of embodiment, and all sorts of strange things that are felt and hard to say, and even harder to say well. So when I thought about those crowds and their disappointed rage, their love of the spectacle, their irrational passion for g\\\'n\\\'r, it made me wince, remember, and yearn.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> I think I\\\'m going to start writing here again. It might take a while to produce something worth reading. A lot of it will probably be about art, school, not being creative, doubts, life in general, what I ate for lunch, and maybe occasionally my dog. It will probably never be about my relationship, even though that\\\'s  what I need to write about the most.</p>'),(4410,'<img src=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/images/4412_1.jpg\\\" border=\\\"3\\\"><br><br>\r\n\r\n<b><i>Meeting #1:</b></i><br><br>\r\n\r\nTopics Covered in Discussion:<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>1. Properties of cells/water/chemical bonds:</b>\r\nFrom the Becker text, we discussed the chemical makeup of cells,  the characteristics of carbon (i,e:  valence electrons, types of bonds involving carbon), functional groups, bond energy (van Der Wall, induced dipoles, hydrophobic, etc).  From the Purves text, we discussed the general characteristics of living organisms and their nature within the science of biology.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>2. Molecules/molecular structure:</b> \r\nWe listed the properties of nucleic acids, their structure, and their importance.   Our group compared notes on the topics of large molecules, basic polymers and chains, and more detailed aspects of polymers, such as polysaccharides, lipids (steroids, fats, adipocytes, saturated, etc...), aldehydes, ketones, Amines, Carboxyls, Hydrocarbons and their properties.  Also, monomers and polymers were related to these topics.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>3. Membranes/Proteins:</b> \r\nWe discussed, in particular, the makeup of cell membranes, and the part proteins play, particularly in the lipid bylayer, etc.  We diagramed the micelle, alanine, lysene, and other such chains and emulsifiers and their role in forming more involved structures. Other particular topics we compared notes on were energy, enzymes and catalysts.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>4. The Fluid Mosaic:</b>\r\nOf particular interest, we resolved some confusion regarding gel electrophoreasis, and, as related to the last topic, cell assymetry and the role of membranes.  Regarding the membrane, we discussed ATP and its role, energy pathways within the cell, and, in particular, metabolism.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>5. Cellular Transportation:</b>\r\nIn particular, we compared notes on the various means of inter and intra cellular transportation.  That is, we focused mainly on the passing of material through the membrane, by means of passive transport, active transport (involving ATP) and the variety of transport types facilitated by proteins.<br><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<b>Problem Set Collaboration:</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nWe spent a good amount of time discussion our various questions regarding the problem sets in the problem book.  In particular, we spent time resolving some questions that centered around exponential growth etc.  We collaborated, to resolve minor discrepancies, on the topics of cellular components, bacterial growth (E. Coli), chains, protein structures, enzymes, and finally cellular pathways (in relation to our previous discussion of cellular transportation).<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>I.</b><br><br>\r\n\r\nOur signatures verify that all of us exerted equal effort and time to not only produce this report, but to adequately cover the material contained within it. '),(4412,'test'),(4413,'Whats up with people offering you water? Today, three different friends (on three different occasions) procured giant tankards of bottled water from their bags, took giant satisfying gulps and were all like <i>\\\"Want some water? No? Suit yourself...\\\"</i><br><br>\r\nDid I look dehydrated or something?<br><br>\r\nWhat ever happened to offering a guy a smoke or a beer? Fucking bottled water. '),(4414,'<p>he woke with a heavy chest. the first light peering through the blinds. sounds of the world waking up. he knew he had to move. had to start the day. join the rest of the human machine once more.</p>\r\n<p>he could feel each breath pushing at his rib cage. hear the loud sigh as the air was pulled in and pushed out.</p>\r\n<p>so. very. tired.</p>\r\n<p>he tried hard not to think of all the things he had done wrong. all the people he had hurt. all the pain caused.</p>\r\n<p><i>just put one foot on the floor</i></p>\r\n<p>the bathroom door was just nine steps from the bed. so short a trip. so little effort to get there.</p>\r\n<p><i>come on move. just one leg. push it out from under the covers</i></p>\r\n<p>so much easier to just curl into a ball. feel the warm embrace of the bedsheets. shield himself from the light with the pillow.</p>\r\n<p>warm. dark. quiet.</p>\r\n<p>isolated. numb. free.</p>\r\n<p><i>pain</i></p>'),(4415,'Old Man says <i>\\\"Young man says Old Man\\\"</i><br>\r\nwhile Young Man says <i>\\\"\\\'Young Man\\\', says Old Man\\\"</i>,<br>\r\nunable to say no until the time came to run,<br>\r\nlaboriously plodding like through wet sand<br>\r\nuntil my every bone was bared<br>\r\nand I was reduced to nothing but<br>\r\na penis and a pancreas so inefficient at its task<br>\r\nthat I often felt lightheaded<br>\r\nnauseous<br>\r\nconfused<br>\r\nbut now I finally feel like my old self again.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd the reason for this is...<br>\r\n<i>\\\"I DID IT FOR THE KIDS.\\\"</i><br><br>\r\n\r\nDo your part to help. Tell me one of your darkest secrets, or perhaps your most banal one.<br><br>\r\n\r\nMine is that I pick my nose. Lately, I\\\'ve gone through absolutely no effort to hide it\r\ndespite the fact that whenever I stick my finger into my nostril (I prefer my pinky, it\\\'s smallest and the most nimble picker) I immediately fall to my knees and slide towards the nearest toilet, as if gravity suddenly turned horizontal with a homing device attached.<br><br>\r\n\r\nAnd when I look into the toilet, I see myself looking back, my reflection distorted into ever-fluxing patters by the minute ripples my hyperventilation is causing on the surface.<br><br>\r\n\r\nSuddenly, a sparkly, spiral tunnel, bright as a rainbow of flashing neon, opens up like a vortex, connecting my eyes to those of my reflection. Entwined, we suddenly share the same vision and have become melded into a singular individual.<br><br>\r\n\r\nI am writing this for you because I feel an irresistible urge to tell as many people as possible that I am myself, trapped inside an identical copy of myself.<br><br>\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s very similar to wearing a big, warm, down-filled winter coat on a hot, muggy day, perhaps the hottest of the summer.\r\n'),(4416,'<p>I saw the moon last night and I thought of you. I thought of telling you about it, and about the stars that were faintly visible around it.  The colour of the sky around the moon can never be reproduced, never simulated, never made new. It can never really be told. </P>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nIs the colour around the moon at night what caused the colour of your eyes? Of course there is some other rational explanation for it. Maybe something about genetics. About where we come from. The colours of our eyes, like a clue to remind us of our origins.</P>\r\n\r\n<p> \r\nPlural under the bluish moonlight you fade in and out of yourself inside my own eyes. Like a composite, you are stacked together, one layer at a time. Rendered in my mind in a ghostly way. There is no truth in you. Even the spectral you is missing, and I am alone, remembering colours under the moon. You are exposed as unreal under the moon. Tested, your eyes disappear, and your translucence is betrayed, as what would be rumpled clothes under the yellow lamps of street lights are exposed as whisps of smoke that can never be grasped. </P>\r\n\r\n<p> I closed my eyes, and walked inside.</P>'),(4417,'There is a 17 year old half-Puerto Rican, half-Italian vixen that lives next door to me. She and her four older sisters. And her single out-of-work dad. He\\\'s a plumber, he just had a stroke at 42, he sits at home all day smoking his pipe, whose mouthpiece is almost always anxiously lodged in the gap where his top-left canine tooth is missing, as well as its two adjacent teeth.<br><br>\r\nHis daughters have perfect teeth. Perfect everything. They are trouble, and everyone in the neighborhood knows it, particularly the younger, hipper, whiter set... which is their usual quarry.<br><br>\r\nI got home much earlier than usual today, and found myself entering the building at the same time as the bubbly Boricua, who had just gotten out of school. Quickly improvising some small-talk that didn\\\'t come off sounding like <i>\\\"So... when do you turn 18?\\\"</i>, we chatted our way down the hall, waved, and keyed into our respective doors, faces rife with neighborly smiles and mock-flirtatious giggling.<br><br>\r\nI sat down my bag of groceries, as I heard her twist her deadbolt lock. As I clicked on the lamp and stooped to pet my kitten, who was circling underfoot in the dark kitchen, I heard a glass smash, and then an eerily unsurprised scream.<br><br>\r\nThrough the paper-thin walls of this quickly-gentrifying Bushwick basement came muffled cries while the booming, lispy voice of a drunk man demanded money, blabbering something incoherent about a cellphone bill.<br><br>\r\nIt reached a crescendo when she screeched that they could afford going over their minutes if he didn\\\'t drink all day, got a job or stopped shooting up.<br><br>\r\nThe shrieks turned to heaving sobs as the skin-meets-skin slap of punches and slaps echoed through the wall with even more broken glass and stumbling<br><br>\r\nNow I know why she always wears sunglasses.'),(4418,'<p>After 3 a.m. you can walk home if you want to. Maybe you shouldn\\\'t, but you definitely have to leave.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>There is a lot of violence in Vancouver, and you want a piece of it. Just enough to satisfy your own needs. A punch in the face now and again never hurt anyone, right? A square kick, but it has to be a kick that is true. Who can fake violence, really? Physical contact with menace behind it is not something you play at. You just enact it. It consumes you. You hurl things and they shatter, or slap, or miss and hit the pavement. But everything you are is in the throw. You can\\\'t reserve yourself and just test it out. That kind of bitter anger (or passion) comes from a well that is very deep inside what you might call a soul if you weren\\\'t enlightened. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Physical contact, then, or violence? Or something else. Your whole body shaking with rage, with frustration, with what you don\\\'t know or can\\\'t solve. Shaking and trying to fill in the gaps that are there. This is where involuntary action comes from. This is where you strike from, if you really mean it. And it isn\\\'t really mean, because at that point you\\\'ve let go of thinking up clever ways to analyze a situation. You aren\\\'t trying anything. You just are the situation, you are making it, you and your touch are it, and everything you touch is it. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s in there and its out there. There\\\'s a lot of it in Vancouver, you see it when you walk, when the pavement smells fill you and you are overwhelmed by traces of the violent moments of others, or the moments of truth. At 3 a.m. those smells are swept away by the wind that shakes you for different reasons, shivers and shrivels you, as you make yet another good decision and get into the car and go home, with the truth carefully tucked inside, where no one else will dare to touch it. </p>'),(4419,'<p>i am choosing not to google this novel or its author. i don\\\'t want to know. all i want to know about this novel is that i found it laying on a table in the poetry room at city lights, marked \\\"FREE.\\\" and it was free. it is free!\r\n</p><p>\r\nit is print on demand, self published. i haven\\\'t read it. my cursory interaction with it has resulted in a faint understanding of its contents. it takes place all over san francisco. there\\\'s a character named dahlia and a character named walker. it involves a lot of relationships between a lot of people. i think it may be the author\\\'s attempt to document and express his experiences with polyamory? \r\n</p><p>\r\ni don\\\'t know. i may never read it. it\\\'s become a talisman. it\\\'s actual contents no longer matter. \r\n</p>\r\n<img src=\\\"%%img1%%\\\">'),(4420,''),(4421,'<p>I am angry.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I have spent considerable time trying to learn the intricacies of the legal system trying to believe in the pursuit of some semblance of justice. Three years to get a J.D. degree and about the same working in the field - I think I have to leave it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Vengeance is the only thing that describes my desires. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Working away all those years to get where I am: trying, straining to believe, lying to myself…  only to come to the conclusion that the phrases are true when they reference what money can buy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>More of the same cannot be tolerated I will either leave this behind me or lose myself in forcing change. Let this be a shot across the bow. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>My misery will not be in vain.</p>\r\n');
