29.129.162','en'),(2834,885,'2001-05-09 12:26:14',0,'lostbluesoul','Definitely use the crayon...\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'64.12.102.52','en'),(2835,884,'2001-05-09 12:28:49',0,'lostbluesoul','And here I thought I was the only one who had a strange \r\nthing about keeping my feet covered.  I have had major \r\nproblems falling asleep lately.  By the time I do, I have \r\nto get up in a few hours again...',2,0,0,0,'64.12.102.52','en'),(2836,883,'2001-05-09 12:30:37',0,'lostbluesoul','beautiful. well said/quoted.',2,0,0,0,'64.12.102.52','en'),(2837,881,'2001-05-09 12:33:52',0,'lostbluesoul','If you find a real answer to that question, let me know.  \r\nAs of now, I sacrifice the social life one week, let the \r\ngrades slip next week; juggle it all and pass out in the \r\nend. It\\\'s not worth it. What is?',4,0,0,0,'64.12.102.52','en'),(2838,883,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','did you get freaky with a style sheet?',3,0,0,0,'207.8.25.110','en'),(2839,883,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i believe what we see here is an entire embedded html page.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2840,884,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<b>chia</b>, i don\\\'t know if you\\\'re trying, but your recent \r\nswintries have been written so beautifully that i actually \r\nread every word.  it reminds me of a certain flavor of \r\nhenry miller rant, like the ones you find deep within \r\nthe \\\'_exus\\\' trilogy.',2,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(2841,873,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','grrrrr',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(2842,885,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I\\\'m curious to know what you know about olive oil.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n-daritalianwin',1,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(2843,886,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','for my part, i hope you stay.  was at D&D the other night \r\nand thought bout you.  must drink pints soon.  i like pints \r\nmuchly.',2,0,0,0,'207.8.25.110','en'),(2844,886,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>that is bummin\\\' dude.  dont leave for that reason.  there\r\nis bound to be job around there that can keep you in the\r\narea.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>your rent is corn dog cheap! maybe you could find\r\nsomething fun like a music store job or something.  i know\r\nyou wont like that idea, but you know what i mean.</P>\r\n<P>i guess it is a priority decision.  either you can get a\r\njob :-( even if it has to do with books ) or you can go to\r\nhouston.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>if you go to dallas, you will never stop complaining for\r\nthe rest of your life about the time you had to go to\r\ndallas.</P>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2845,886,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','call me insane but i\\\'ve recently become obsessed with \r\nhouston. if i hadn\\\'t just moved. perhaps i\\\'d move there.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(2846,886,'2001-05-10 02:30:47',0,'jo','you\\\'re insane.<BR>\r\nbut then again, it\\\'s my home town. i\\\'ve had enough of it, \r\nthank you very much.<BR>\r\nbut there <b>are</b> good things about houston: museums, \r\nmontrose, various parks and such. and i have a lot of fond \r\nmemories from living there. and the music scene has gotten \r\na lot better in the last 10 years!',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2847,889,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','Bitterness can be a good tool at times I think.  But so far \r\nin my life it\\\'s become all consuming.  It\\\'s important that \r\nyour bitterness be rational and not rationalized.',1,0,0,0,'207.205.216.21','en'),(2848,892,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','i\\\'m not so sure i\\\'d like working as a geek.  the company i \r\nwork for often does the electric for offices.  and i\\\'ve \r\nfound that anyone who works in these places more often than \r\nnot enrages me.  once i heard a woman complain about her \r\nspeeding ticket.  she said that it should be legal to go 10 \r\nmph over the speed limit.  i asked her if she\\\'d like to \r\nfeel what 240 volts is like.  she had nothing to say to \r\nthat.  but she had this wonderful smug look on her face.  \r\n\r\nworking is one thing, i want to get back my err... \\\'geek \r\nheart\\\'.  ',1,0,0,0,'207.205.216.21','en'),(2850,890,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','geek\\\'s a state of mind, not a specific set of things you\r\nknow.  it\\\'s the desire to understand, the desire to take\r\nthings apart (the more complex the better) just to figure\r\nthings out.  I don\\\'t think there is ever a \\\"too late\\\" for\r\nanything that lives in your mind.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhat do you need to be a geek?  curiosity and a library\r\ncard.  Thats about it.  If you want to be a code geek, well,\r\nget a cheap spare pentium machine (like something from\r\nused-pcs.com here in central texas, eh, p166ish) and run\r\nsomething like linux on it.  $5 at a place like\r\ncheapbytes.com for a distro CD set and maybe some books from\r\nan inexpensive book seller (Half Price, bookpool.com),\r\nO\\\'Reilly and Manning are good publishers.  You\\\'ll want the\r\nspare machine so you can have the freedom to fuck it up\r\nexperimenting.  My point is that the tools you\\\'d need are\r\nminimal in terms of money cost, so all  you really need is\r\ncuriosity.  The first six months you spend with linux will\r\nseem quite confusing but if you stick with it, it becomes\r\nalmost transcendant.  or something.  :-)\r\n<br><br>\r\nIf you want to be a science geek, all you need is the\r\nlibrary card, paper, and pencils.  Maybe a decent\r\ncalculator.  Math is good for reading that doesn\\\'t require a\r\nhuge lab to implement.  Plus math is part and parcel of\r\neverything else so any math you learn is good in any of the\r\nsciences.  My number one weakness as a chemistry student is\r\nthat I\\\'m just not so great with mathematical thinking.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2851,892,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','reminds me about the fifth floor of the GAB on the UNT\r\ncampus up in denton, tx.  Nobody every had classes there, or\r\nknew anyone who did.  No offices were up there.  Turns out\r\nthat\\\'s where a lot of computer stuff was.  I eventually had\r\na programming lab up there my last semester in TAMS.',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2852,873,'2001-05-10 07:20:30',0,'rusty','<p>\r\n:(\r\n<p>\r\nI am sad..\r\n<p>\r\nI made xep grrr...  and I don\\\'t know why... \r\n<p>\r\nnot fair...\r\n',6,0,0,0,'64.163.194.78','en'),(2853,891,'2001-05-10 08:45:10',0,'rusty','That was so beautiful!  I love you!  ;)\r\n<p>\r\nThe beauty in the horror is overwhelming...\r\n<p>\r\nHaha... and you\\\'ll laugh at it soon enough...\r\n<p>\r\nSooner than you think...\r\n<p>\r\nDon\\\'t fight for the sanity... that\\\'s what you\\\'re missing...\r\nWe were all insane to begin with, and pretending at sanity \r\nis what makes it all so much harder than it already is...',1,0,0,0,'64.163.194.78','en'),(2854,873,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i thought elly made xep grrr.',1,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2855,889,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','*hug*',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2856,873,'2001-05-10 10:07:36',0,'rusty','maybe so...  I can get insecure...  oh well, sometimes it\\\'s \r\nfunny to mis-interpret... either way I managed to piss \r\nsomeone off, which helps my <a \r\nhref=http://tender.lumplings.com/crap/orator.txt>cause</a>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'64.163.194.78','en'),(2857,890,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','indeed, the state of mind is what i can\\\'t seem to quite get \r\nback.  i have a powerful computer, i have over $10,000 in \r\nbooks on my shelves.  aha.  Introduction to the Technology \r\nof Explosives alongside Nothingness, which is right next to \r\nthe Yeats.  It\\\'s that when i come home from work i am \r\nunable to code.  I\\\'m so worn down.  And when i\\\'m talking to \r\nothers, i can\\\'t just think about fun (to me anyway) geeky \r\nthings, instead i stand apart a bit and watch the \r\nenviroment for danger.  It\\\'s my midset which i complain \r\nabout, last night was a rare moment of lucidty for me.  ',3,0,0,0,'207.205.216.35','en'),(2858,888,'2001-05-10 15:09:27',0,'sic','thats some deep shit man',0,0,0,0,'129.65.91.183','en'),(2859,886,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','thanks, i know.<p>\r\ni think really though, it\\\'s all about all this art there is \r\nto see. and the fact that my little sister moved there. and i \r\nmiss her.\r\nand it\\\'s about this too:\r\n\r\n<a href=\\\"http://homorobotic.org/photologs/texASS/\\\">texASS</a>\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nyes, hi.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(2860,888,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','my book at home is jealous of you',2,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(2861,893,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','and i of ladies most deject and wretched that sucked the <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.huney.org\\\"><font \r\ncolor=\\\"white\\\">huney</font></a> of his music vows...<br>\r\nthe lady drowns everytime and the same play is played \r\nout<br>\r\nin a parade of jade<br> \r\nbeneath the heath<br>\r\na promenade<br>\r\nbetter to burn out than<br>\r\nfade<br>\r\nknew this before the world<br>\r\nwas made<br> \r\nback when you dihn\\\'t need a grade<br>\r\nbefore my heart was flayed<br>\r\nopen<br>\r\nthen we said we\\\'d trade<br>\r\nno one<br>\r\nand nothing for this now<br>\r\nwhen my sweat wet your brow<br>\r\ni killed more monks than mau<br>\r\nthe trade i played broke the dow<br>\r\nhow?<br>\r\ncigarette broke my vow<br>\r\nfrom the back to the bow<br>\r\ni am not not the tao<br>\r\ni am great sacred cow<br>\r\ni am the expression \\\"wow\\\"<br>\r\nlike you, upside down<br>\r\nmom<br>\r\nhappy mother\\\'s day <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.mscreant.com\\\">mscreant</a><br>\r\n;-0',1,0,0,0,'207.8.25.110','en'),(2862,886,'2001-05-10 17:41:23',0,'jo','also: \\\"if you go to dallas, you will never stop complaining \r\nfor the rest of your life about the time you had to go to \r\ndallas.\\\" i wholeheartedly agree.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2863,894,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','it\\\'s right there...and here...and shhh<br>\r\nword<br>\r\nshh is the word<br>\r\nahhh<br>\r\nlet it out on an ahh',2,0,0,0,'207.8.25.110','en'),(2864,894,'2001-05-10 18:09:23',0,'zb','<p><font face=\\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">the joker is \r\nloved <br>\r\n  and mscreant is loved <br>\r\n  and dj1_luna is loved <br>\r\n  and kdawg is loved <br>\r\n  and austin is loved <br>\r\n  and thebmg is loved and loved <br>\r\n  and life is good <br>\r\n  and worth celebrating <br>\r\n  and worth cherishing </font></p>\r\n<p><font face=\\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">may all your \r\ndesires come trueâ€¦ </font></p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.171.37.54','en'),(2865,894,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Veer Moll','I once found a copy of \\\"The Prophet\\\" next to my toilet. \r\nTime of origin: at least 6 months ago. I don\\\'t know who \r\nbrought it there.\r\n\r\nAnyway, I\\\'ve been reading it every time I take a shit. It\\\'s \r\npretty good.',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(2866,896,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','so are you saying i have a grace period?',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2867,897,'2001-05-10 21:56:18',0,'j','nice',0,0,0,0,'212.20.106.175','en'),(2868,898,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nschool\\\'s easy. the hard questions only come out when you\r\ngraduate.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2869,896,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<br>\r\nmy mom called and wanted me to take her to joan jett for \r\nmother\\\'s day.<br>\r\nbut i couldn\\\'t.<br>\r\nso i guess i\\\'ll take her out for lunch or some type of deal. \r\ni think that\\\'s the best idea. not sure. my mom is cool. i \r\nwant her to do something nice. <br>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(2870,896,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i just made a hand colored crazy sunrise happy mother\'s day\r\ncard for my mom. she loves that stuff, i\'m really excited\r\nfor when she gets it. to thank me, she\'ll call and then bug me about\r\nmoving back to nyc again. ah, moms.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2871,901,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>\"no comment.\"<BR><BR>\r\nno really, was she seventeen seconds away from screaming or\r\nmaking out some more.  i thought she was horrified for a\r\nsecond, then she is seeing the same thing you are\r\nseeing?</P>',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2872,901,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','you\\\'d have to ask the woman who\\\'s being written about.  her \r\nfeelings were never known to me.  only her words and \r\nactions.',3,0,0,0,'207.205.216.44','en'),(2873,891,'2001-05-10 23:18:03',0,'astronomy domine','\\\"a war between the man and the woman. \r\nThere is a war between the ones who say there is a war \r\nand the ones who say there isn\\\'t. \\\" L.C.',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(2874,896,'2001-05-10 23:18:55',0,'auntie','of course, sweetie, you always have a grace period with me, \r\ni am your mother and i love you very much and when you do \r\nfind a present, it is always the most wonderful present you \r\ncould think of.  i love my red shoes.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndad bought me that scarab bracelet i saw in the jewelry \r\nstore last year -- remember?  it was on sale for half price.',1,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(2875,898,'2001-05-10 23:19:58',0,'auntie','what weeb said.',3,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(2876,898,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i\'ll take hard questions over school anytime.  school for me\r\nwas eternal suffering.  never any rewards.  alway being told\r\ni am a failure.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nquitting and moving on with my life was one of the best and\r\nperhaps one of the few real decisions i ever made for\r\nmyself.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nothers can swallow the let down so long as i do not have to\r\nswallow the failure anymore.',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2877,898,'2001-05-11 00:33:15',0,'mojo23','School sucked because there were was never enough time and\nalways too much to do.  You never got the feeling you were\nactually done anything, because you should always be reading\nmore.\nNow, in the real adult world, I try to run my own business,\nand have the same problem.  There are never enough hours in\nthe day, I never feel prepared enough, and I could always\nread just one more website before bed...\n',4,0,0,0,'158.252.213.175','en'),(2878,902,'2001-05-11 00:35:30',0,'dza','i have an opinion, but it is not supportive.',4,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(2879,902,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','hmmm.  when i was younger, my ex-fiance got involved with \r\nanother woman, ultimately she and i both became involved \r\nwith this person.  while it worked out okay, no shouting \r\nmatches or violence, i always felt uneasy about it.  while \r\nthe sex was excellent, it often seemed that many things \r\nwere going unvoiced.  when the sexual tension between the \r\nthree of us evaporated there was still tension.  years have \r\npassed and i can\\\'t shake the feeling that this woman and i \r\nwere both tolerating each other while seeking possetion of \r\nmy fiance.  love and sex (recognizing the differences \r\nbetwixt the two) are so bloody complicated, but the fact \r\nremains that most people seem to wish for control over \r\ntheir mates sexual liasons.  jealousy has not often been a \r\nproblem for me, but perhaps that\\\'s because i make it so \r\nclear that i go only for monogamous situations and that i \r\nwill react badly otherwise.  i know my limits now, and i am \r\nnot ashamed of my desire for one partner nor my demands of \r\nthat partner to either stick by me or walk.  i think you \r\nshould not worry about what you feel too much.  however, \r\nyou should also be honest with yourself and your partner.  \r\nbut what the hell do i know anyway?  i can\\\'t even get my \r\nhand to go home with me anymore.  heh.',3,0,0,0,'207.205.216.44','en'),(2880,902,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I\\\'m going to try to say this in the least snarky way \r\npossible. I\\\'m new at it, so here\\\'s hoping.</p>\r\n<p>It seems like most people can\\\'t make a relationship \r\nbetween <b>two</b> people healthy and strong. I don\\\'t think \r\nthat the chances improve when another person or two is \r\ninvolved.</p>\r\n<p>Polyamory seems like one of those ideas (like communism \r\nor etc.) that works for a very small subset of people. It \r\nalso seems that many of the people who are drawn to it are \r\namong the least likely to have it work for them.</p>\r\n<p>At least, this is my view based on the collossal failure \r\nof \\\"polyamory\\\" I have seen around me in various \r\ncommunities.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(2881,902,'2001-05-11 02:53:47',0,'jo (is also snarky)','\\\"dza: i have an opinion, but it is not supportive.\\\" um, hi, \r\nthat\\\'s the equivalent of the childish, \\\"i have a secret but \r\ni can\\\'t tell you what it is.\\\" either have the balls to \r\nstate your opinion, supportive or no, or don\\\'t comment.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2882,896,'2001-05-11 02:57:37',0,'jo','you\\\'re a good mom, auntie. happy soon to be mom\\\'s day.<BR>\r\nalso, i wish scarab bracelets were done with silver more \r\noften than gold, as silver is far more flattering on me. \r\nand i really dig scarabs.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2883,902,'2001-05-11 02:58:12',0,'sic','my bf and his best friend holler and oggle at girls while im \r\naround, which i fuckin hate. i guess deep down i always \r\ncompare myself to the girls. and of course i cant win.',0,0,0,0,'4.16.224.121','en'),(2884,899,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','is this fiction?',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2885,891,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\nhe once wrote me a poem on the back of a business card \r\nwhile we drank beer with my friends in a pub. he stopped me \r\nin a park on the way home and read it to me softly, face to \r\nface.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(2886,891,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\njust to clarify for austin (who emailed me)<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<I>From: Austin * <austin@swinney.org><BR>\r\ndo you know incitor?  he was your boyfriend?<BR></I>\r\n<BR>\r\nnooo... the \"he\" is someone different and i have no idea \r\nwho incitor is.\r\nwho are you incitor? \r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(2887,902,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i would say that monogamy is the worse failure, what \r\nwith over 50% of marriages still ending in divorce. \r\nthough i suppose that depends on what you consider to \r\nbe \\\"a successful relationship\\\".<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhow about: a relationship based upon honesty and \r\nmutual respect, that brings happiness to its \r\nparticipants, during some period of time. i know of \r\nmany (monogamous) couples who are miserable, but \r\nstill together. does unyielding exclusive togetherness \r\ndefine success?',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2888,902,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/18807\r\n89086/o/qid=989554679/sr=8-1/ref=aps_sr_b_1_1/102\r\n-2564661-5452141\\\">the new love without limits</a>, \r\nbtw, is an excellent book. even if the idea of multiple (or \r\nflexible) partners isn\\\'t something you ever want to try, \r\nthe thought exercises involved with working through \r\njealousy are just as useful (and perhaps more so) in \r\nthe context of monogamy.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2889,883,'2001-05-11 05:56:00',0,'mojo23','Most of us grow less gracefully than these images into my \r\nmind.  lovely.  thank you.',1,0,0,0,'158.252.241.123','en'),(2890,902,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','That 50% stat is widely repeated but I do think that it is\r\nwrong.  It\\\'s 3am and I\\\'m taking a break from an all nighter\r\nso I can\\\'t be overly buggered to find evidence (like\r\nhyperlinks to the place(s) I read refuting it).  The gist of\r\nthe argument was that they compared the divorces happening\r\nin one year to the marriages happening in the same year, or\r\nsomething.',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2891,904,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Cheap plastic coffee maker (wal-mart): $19<br>\r\nGood, store-ground coffee (pick a flava\\\'): $8/Lb<br>\r\nBig Mug That Doesn\\\'t Suck (anywhere you can find it):\r\n$5ish<br>\r\nHaving Coffee You Like And Not Having to Hide the Fat Corpse\r\nof The Stupid Boomer: Priceless\r\n',4,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2892,903,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>spell check your work, do not apologize.</p>\r\n\r\n<H1><a href=\\\"http://m-w.com\\\">Merriam-Webster/Franklin \r\nSpelling Helper</A></H1>',6,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2893,898,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<p>\r\nI\\\'m not bitching that it\\\'s hard.  I accept the difficulty of\r\nmy major (aside, half-joking: the people that say real life\r\nhas harder questions to offer than school had to have been\r\nliberal arts or business students. ;-)  I\\\'ve spent more than\r\na few years between the working and collegiate worlds, so my\r\nwry observation is not purely based on an academic\r\nperspective.).  It\\\'s just that I don\\\'t care about that shit\r\nany more.  just flat out don\\\'t.  There are areas of\r\nchemistry that I still find fascinating (organic being the\r\nfirst one, the hardest but most enjoyable thinking I\\\'ve ever\r\ndone was spending a semester working on drug precursors),\r\nbut the remaining 2 classes I have to take is all a bunch of\r\nabstract, theoretical, pchem bullshit like that (adv.\r\ninorganic and a pchem lab if anyone is curious).  And I need\r\nto get some air before I hit it again.\r\n</p>\r\nThat is the intellectual side.  The practical side is that I\r\nneed the income that working can provide.  My finances are\r\nnot particularly germaine to this forum, suffice to say my\r\nmind and my wallet are in accord over this move. ;-)</p>\r\n<p>And on the intellectual side too, I plan to learn a lot\r\nat my new job, the environment will be very conducive to\r\nthat I feel.  It involves things that I love doing but that\r\nI can\\\'t really take courses on (perl, for starters).  I want\r\nto read books that don\\\'t have equations in them (I have my\r\neye on a nicely bound compilation of Shakespeare at 1/2\r\nPrice).  I want to learn to play chess better.   I want to\r\nlearn how to cook Italian food, and Tex-Mex, and maybe some\r\nThai or Cantonese (perhaps Szechuan).  Etc. Etc. Etc. \r\nLearning is a process I plan to continue until the moment I\r\ndie, so being in school or no is of little difference (just\r\nthat my chemical curiosity is constrained by not having\r\naccess to $big_int dollars worth of lab equipment; something\r\ntells me having an NMR machine powerful enough to  zap\r\nmagnetic media in a 100 foot radius and powerful enough to\r\nglitch a pacemaker at 30 would not be appreciated in an\r\napartment complex).</p>\r\n<p>And on an emotional level I\\\'m just fatigued.  I\\\'ve been a\r\ncollege student since I was 16.  I just turned 23 a few\r\nmonths ago.  I\\\'ve been really sick.  I\\\'ve had to work.  I\r\nwas exhibit A in a custody battle from 16 to the minute I\r\nturned 18 (not that my dad really gave a shit about me I\r\nthink, I was just a tool to influence my mom).  This life\r\nhas just worn me down.  I can\\\'t think of the last time I had\r\nfun without thinking that I should be doing something\r\nschool-related.  You can only run for so long until you have\r\nto ask yourself why you are running.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2894,899,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<p>*bbbzzzzztttt!*</p>\r\n<p>I can imagine the sound that must have made.</p>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2895,903,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<blockquote>(please dont mine my spelling\r\nmistakes..)</blockquote>\r\n<p>*BOOM!!!!*  Aaaaauuugh!</p>\r\n<p>Yeah, that would be bad.  I don\\\'t think anyone here would\r\ndo that.  ;-)</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2896,896,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','could you maybe like desolder the bug part and resolder it\r\non a plain silver bracelet or something?  (not a\r\nmetallurgist so I don\\\'t know if this would work)  silver is\r\ncool.  my highschoolsortofcollege ring is stainless steel\r\nbecuase a) i liked to color better than gold b) it was\r\ncheaper by about a factor of 5 and c) i think stainless\r\nsteel better represents what TAMS stood for (eh, it was a\r\nmaths and sciences place with lots of protoengineers (the\r\nonly club with more members than the engineering one was the\r\nmath one))',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2897,899,'2001-05-11 10:14:33',0,'incitor to le','i wish it was fiction, but it isn\\\'t.',4,0,0,0,'207.205.216.66','en'),(2898,891,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','just this thing you know?  i\\\'m no one important, but my \r\nguess would be that none here know me excepting of course \r\nDarwin, Dawn, and any they might have spoken of me to.',0,0,0,0,'207.205.216.66','en'),(2899,907,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','You should store yourself in a cool dark place for a few \r\ndays. Keep a low profile. Disconnect the phone. Maybe even \r\nsleep in some other room or some other area of your \r\nlivingspace than you normally would.\r\n\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(2900,898,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'BlasÃ© Moll','I\\\'m going to try to stay in school for as long as possible. \r\nThen I will self-detonate.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(2901,896,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'BlasÃ© Moll','I hired my sister to get something on my behalf for \r\nMother\\\'s Day.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(2902,907,'2001-05-11 13:18:07',0,'auntie','gather up your receipts and such, wolves at the door \r\nsuggests an audit by the irs.',1,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(2903,902,'2001-05-11 16:13:30',0,'bmg','i think the point is that there are a lot of people that get \r\ndivorces, multiple times too. It could be 50% or it could \r\nbe 25% still, we are talking about a lot of people. I have \r\nthree or four friends that come from a family that is still \r\ntogether... it seems weird to me, i dont know any \r\ndifferent... it has allways been like that.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(2904,898,'2001-05-11 16:32:39',0,'bmg','sounds like you wish you were a liberal arts student; \r\nmaybe you should drop and take up art. try not to be \r\nburdened with other peoples questions, only your own.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(2905,900,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','set the gearshift for the high gear of your soul...',2,0,0,0,'207.8.25.110','en'),(2906,904,'2001-05-11 17:39:00',0,'sean','measuring cup with sharpie line for perfect coffee level:\r\npriceless',1,0,0,0,'204.162.86.241','en'),(2907,898,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>haha! i knew someone would assume that i was a liberal arts\r\nmajor. au contraire, i was both a chemistry degree (hence\r\nyes i took p-chem and physical chem, physics, higher math,\r\netc etc) and an english double major. i still think the\r\nquestions of what kind of person you want to be in all\r\naspects of your life is a much larger one than the\r\nassignments you get in school, no matter how thought-provoking. </p><p>i\'m not saying school isn\'t butt-breakingly hard. but guess what? real life is harder! to the joy of most twenty-somethings out of college, none of this is easy. all those hours discussing anti-capitalist philosphy (or pro-reaganomics, depending on your politics), but what happens when you get your first paycheck? are you first in line for that shiny matte silverblue volvo? all that talk about changing the world, but do you fall in step? hard questions, no easy answers.\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2913,902,'2001-05-11 19:19:35',0,'mojo','<p>All: My parents divorced when I was 3.  I never knew that it was unusual to divorce, and in fact thought it a good thing that my utterly incompatible parents knew when to call it quits, for the sake of us kids and all those around them.  I never thought monogamy-till-death was natural or right.  Ever.\r\n</p><p>\r\nI have always known, even in my serial monogamy days, that something was missing in that picture.  I married without the usual belief that it would last forever, and I believe that is why I have remained friendly with my ex since, and even (although less so) during, the separation process.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThe poly thing seemed natural and wholly good...for all involved.  If it causes me pain, I went toward it willingly and with honesty.  Yes, it does hurt, which explains the letter above, but it gives me much more in return for the pain.  And I am learning so much about myself in the process.  \r\n</p><p>\r\nI do not think this lifestyle is for everyone, and I do not expect I will keep it up for another 75 years.  But I may.  I\'ve seen the alternative and it\'s not very attractive.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIncitor: that situation seems like it was pretty classic.  There are always feelings that lie beneath the surface, and if they are not openly admitted and shared, they will deconstruct the relationship\'s foundation.  it\'s all about honesty, and admitting what you are feeling.\r\n</p><p>\r\nDarwin: thank you for your comments, because they were your own experiences.  I would say the same thing about most poly  couplings I\'ve seen.  They don\'t work very well.  It\'s hard.\r\n</p><p>\r\ndza:  I appreciate your honesty, and realize that we are fighting against something very ingrained.  I might not know how to proceed at every step, but I am proceeding because the only alternatives are to recede or stay put.  Neither is a real option for me.  Go forward or die trying.\r\n</p><p>\r\ns5:  you\'re yummy...wanna play with me?  heehee\r\n</p><p>\r\nAll: Thanks for the constructive comments.  I am, I believe, in one of the only successful relationships of this kind I\'ve ever witnessed.  We\'ve been at it for 3 years running now, and it only gets better.\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'216.15.96.236','en'),(2909,907,'2001-05-11 18:22:17',0,'jo','what says your horoscope, steve?',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2910,904,'2001-05-11 18:22:45',0,'PreLinear','(correction)\r\nA million years ago, I took one of my best friends in as a \r\nroommate, as he had just gotten kicked out of his parents \r\nhouse. He and had very different coffee habits, and would \r\npractically refuse to drink each others coffee. \r\n\r\n<P>So we bought two identical coffee makers, and he wrote \r\nhis name, <a href=\\\"http://www.duanedenison.com\\\">DUANE</A>, \r\nin magic marker on one, and I wrote my name, <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.benderbooks.com/\\\">LYNN</A>, on the other. \r\nWe refused to turn each other\\\'s coffee pot off, much less \r\nclean it out.\r\n\r\n<p>Sometimes both of us would forget to turn the coffee \r\npots off when we left in the morning. God, what a smell.\r\n\r\n',6,0,0,0,'207.8.3.193','en'),(2911,902,'2001-05-11 18:48:28',0,'dza','<p>\r\n\r\nmo<b>jo</b>, i envy you a little bit.  my parents have been \r\ntogether for over 30 years, making me 100% programmed for \r\nmonogamy.  i have no problem giving whole, unconditional \r\nlove, and i do feel jealousy and possessiveness.  for me, i \r\nconsider those feelings healthy warning signs alerting me \r\nto the fact that something isn\\\'t right with either me,\r\nor the relationship.  this is where choice comes in.  i \r\ncould push and struggle to reprogram myself and my feelings \r\nfor a person that obviously isn\\\'t satisfied with the \r\nrelationship (and believe me, i\\\'ve tried that - ouch), \r\n<i>or</i> i could realize that the person/relationship \r\nsimply isn\\\'t right, and that i should not be afraid to \r\nstrike out again and take my chances in finding someone \r\nthat is.  after trying both, i\\\'m sure you can guess which \r\none i think is better. \r\n\r\n</p> \r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nbut we\\\'re all different, and i\\\'m sure life might be a \r\nlittle easier if i could attain such a silky state of \r\nimpeccable polyamory.  good luck to you.  life is short, \r\nyet there is so much we can achieve.  :)\r\n\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n\r\n<b>darwin</b>, i like what you had to say.\r\n\r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(2912,907,'2001-05-11 18:57:16',0,'mojo','I think you need to go dancing to throw those wolves off your trail.',2,0,0,0,'216.15.96.236','en'),(2914,893,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<PRE>\r\ni got cat scratch fever\r\nshe left me for the scent in the wind \r\nwith scribbles of scratches on my hands and face\r\nshe didnt want to be touched\r\nnot by me -- not by desire\r\n</PRE>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2915,904,'2001-05-11 19:33:19',0,'mojo','It sounds like your entire office could use a Coffee Free Day, like Amherst University just had. It was an art project  that got the administration, cafeteria and student body electorate involved.  \r\n\r\nThey covered up the coffee pots in the cafeterias and draped the place with signs announcing a coffee-free campus.  People moaned and complained, but they also went outside and paid upwards of $5/bean for expresso beans, from planted opportunists.  \r\n\r\nYou might also consider trying tea.  Much easier to mix, and no nasty smell.  It might get you over your need to kill the hippie (although, in the clearer, tea-induced state of mind you might think of a better way to dispose of the body..',2,0,0,0,'216.15.96.236','en'),(2916,904,'2001-05-11 19:57:16',0,'LinearBeanWay','A Coffee-Free Day? In most of the places I\\\'ve worked, that \r\nwould be a career-threatening move.\r\n\r\n<p>You\\\'d have better success with a \\\"Nobody-Gets-Laid Day.\\\"\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'207.8.3.193','en'),(2917,898,'2001-05-11 20:25:40',0,'jo','bmg: not be burdened by other people\\\'s questions? i was a \r\ndouble major in english and religion/philosophy and i spent \r\nthe entirety of my college career poring over other (most \r\ndead) people\\\'s questions. but i agree with you that school \r\nburn-out is very often helped by a switch in focus, and i \r\nthink it sounds like that\\\'s exactly what orion wants to do \r\nwith his post-collegiate life, explore other things, so \r\ncheers to you, orion!<BR>\r\ni think it\\\'s easy to forget that academia is like any other \r\nundertaking: there are some people for whom it is perfect \r\nand others for whom it is a nightmare. \\\"school\\\'s bad\\\" \r\nblanket statements simply aren\\\'t true for a lot of people, \r\nmyself included.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2918,907,'2001-05-11 20:37:05',0,'Cupcake','i think you should run naked into a forest and join a wolf \r\npack. that way, when you figure out who the dishonest \r\npeople are, you can sick your new wolf friends on them. \r\nhooray!',1,0,0,0,'169.229.48.151','en'),(2919,898,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I couldn\\\'t possibly care less about changing the world.  I \r\njust want to be happy and reasonably calm.  \\\"Real\\\" life is \r\nso much easier than having to balance school and \\\"real\\\" \r\nlife.  It\\\'s the combination of the two that\\\'s the killer.\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nAnd I was half-joking, so I ended up being half-right.  ;-)\r\nA 50% error isn\\\'t _too_ bad...',3,0,0,0,'129.116.78.131','en'),(2920,907,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Hey steve, can I come over and cook you dinner?</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(2921,907,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','try not to smell like a rabbit  :-)',1,0,0,0,'129.116.78.131','en'),(2922,907,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i am the wolf. *drool* *bark*',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2923,899,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to incitor','hey. i thought you were dylan. and the dylan i knew of was \r\na sysadmin, so that is why i am confused.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2924,901,'2001-05-11 21:36:46',0,':','ugh.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2925,907,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','i had a dream a few days ago. i was on a mountain and below \r\nme a man was running away from wolves. i dont know who the \r\nperson was but he had a gun. for some reason i knew he only \r\nhad three bullets. a bullet for ever dozen wolves. he shot \r\nthree and was mauled. i woke up mad at the fact that he \r\ndidnt use the third bullet on himself. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.144.14','en'),(2926,899,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Incitor is my big loveable skinhead friend Dave.</p>\r\n<p>And dylan is a network engineer.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(2927,899,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','ok. stuff makes more sense now.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2928,907,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','in one of my most realistic dreams i got out of bed and saw \r\nthat i had turned into a freaky looking wolf man.  maybe it \r\nhad something to do with the fact that i grew up in the \r\nTeenWolf house.',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(2929,907,'2001-05-12 04:00:29',0,'lou gehrig','i once had a dream about michael j. fox.',5,0,0,0,'207.113.58.110','en'),(2930,909,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>avant garde radio rocks my lame ass.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthat is all i do all day: sit and listen to avant garde\r\nradio.  so perfect, so aweful.  it makes me question the\r\nmeaning of bad music.</P>',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2931,908,'2001-05-12 04:53:57',0,'jo','HAPPY BIRTHDAY BMG!!',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2932,907,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to adam','you GREW UP in the TEEN WOLF house? please elaborate on \r\nthis. this warrants it\\\'s own entry. title: i grew up in the \r\nteen wolf house. blurb: it wasn\\\'t as cool as you might \r\nthink.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(2933,908,'2001-05-12 06:53:12',0,'rusty','Hurray!!!!!!!  Life is beautiful!!  ;)  Is that a bad cliche \r\nnow?!  :/\r\n<p>\r\nFuck it!!  ;)',1,0,0,0,'64.163.194.75','en'),(2934,908,'2001-05-12 10:25:02',0,'chaya','it is really very pleasing to me that elly is wearing my \r\ngoggles in this picture.  hello goggles!  how are you today?',5,0,0,0,'158.252.214.40','en'),(2935,908,'2004-07-27 05:14:09',63,'olivia sez','hey, i have goggles too.  except i used to use them as\r\nsunglasses.  people kept pointing out that they were in fact\r\nski goggles, but i never believed them until a few months\r\nago when i re-dug them up.  i wore mine bike riding the\r\nother day, and people stared at me.  whatever!  i don\\\'t have\r\nany other sunglasses!',1,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(2936,909,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','people are afraid to talk to me. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.144.14','en'),(2937,909,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>afraid?<BR><BR>fear is not the issue.  <BR><BR>do you\r\nthink you are entitled to an ear?  <BR><BR>i do not.  nor do\r\ni expect anymore for myself.  if one of your friends gets\r\ntired of hearing the chiamattt tape play over and over again\r\non repeat forever, then you get intrusive -- track them down\r\nand confront them about it.  your friends do not want to\r\nhave a confrontation with you, they want you to back off and\r\ndeal with your problems and come back when you are feeling\r\nbetter.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\ndo not take me wrong here, i am not saying friends are not\r\ngood to rely on in times of need, but do not be\r\npredictable.  have something new to say once in a while.  or\r\nfind some friends that do not like stimulation.<BR><BR>\r\nwe are your friends and we respect you a great deal when you\r\nhave something to live for.  what happened to the commie\r\npinko always right and beyond reproach chiamattt that made\r\nme argue with you?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni still think che guevara was demented.  do you still think\r\nhe is on par with ghandi, or that sometimes non-partisan\r\npeasants need to die as in, \\\"you have to break a few eggs to\r\nmake the cake\\\"?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ngrip it.  even if you have changed your mind, you still have\r\na mind to be proud of.\r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2938,910,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','i saw a deer get shot once. it was horrible. id much rather \r\nhave seen it poo, so that i could laugh like a little kid at \r\na petting zoo. i love guns. i respect them. however i am at \r\nodds at what guns are good for. they are good for preventing \r\nland slides and avalanches. they are good at shooting things \r\ninto space. they are good at making a roof and frame go up \r\nfaster. they take life the fastest tho. perhaps as fast as \r\nhistory takes away culture and language. its all relative. \r\nguns show us what time does. guns show us how things end in \r\none moment and begin in anther.',6,0,0,0,'64.229.144.14','en'),(2939,910,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','oh yeah. the sock scene in that movie made me cry. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.144.14','en'),(2940,909,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','if i played for the other team, id track you down and pin \r\nyou to the ground and kiss you. thanks!',6,0,0,0,'64.229.144.14','en'),(2941,908,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\ni remember having fantasies about being carried when i was a\r\nlittle kid. they were pre-sexual fantasies, i think, when i\r\ndidn\\\'t think men and women did anything except kiss and then\r\nthe girl swooned, and the guy carried her away. oo err! hot\r\nand sexy!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2942,912,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','shoulder pads and power ties. fridays are dress casual \r\nday. pizza on alternate tuesdays and beer busts. your \r\nboss drives a boxster.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2943,912,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<p>Yep! A job is South Bay is far worse than no job at all, \r\nbecause there isnt one in the city for you. Right? Right!\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nThe \\\'dot com\\\' economy has collapsed. Face it, if there is \r\nwork out there, you may have to travel to shitty locations \r\nto find it.</p>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(2944,913,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>if you are trying to pick an arguement, why not first pick a\r\ntopic.  you have glazed over:</P>\r\n\r\n<LI>socialist insecurity\r\n<LI>materialism\r\n<LI>humanism\r\n<LI>idolatry\r\n<LI>spousal abuse\r\n<LI>war criminals\r\n<LI>civil rights leaders\r\n<LI>terrorism\r\n<LI>consumerism\r\n\r\n\r\n<P>did you pick your arguements up from a bleeding socialist\r\nleaflet hand out session in a university campus lounge\r\narea?  there is a suprising lack of detail. i look forward\r\nto your book coming out, and i hope this that you wrote\r\nabove is the preface.  you might call it <B>The Rise And\r\nFall Of Practically Everybody</B></P>\r\n<P>burn! burn!</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2945,913,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','i was just spewing. stop being such a literary critic for \r\nchrist sake. gawd.',6,0,0,0,'64.229.146.8','en'),(2946,913,'2001-05-12 21:41:52',0,'kaz','yea, whatever.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2947,913,'2001-05-12 22:07:48',0,'jo to austin','i stand in baffling admiration at how argumentative you \r\nare. i think i like the austin that picks fights, a LOT.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2948,912,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','or you could \"pull an elly\" and force yourself to take the \r\njob because money = happiness and success, and it\'s such \r\ngood money of COURSE you should take it, and the commute \r\nwon\'t be that bad, and so what if the windows don\'t open, \r\nthe people are kind of bland but mostly nice... but it\'s a \r\ngood job and maybe you can just work it for six months and \r\nsave a lot and then not work for six months.\r\n<br><br>\r\nand quit in a panic, three whole weeks later.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(2949,912,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','Next week is week number 3. Who is placing bets? What are \r\nthe odds?\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(2950,913,'2001-05-12 22:42:13',0,'jo','having said that: who exactly are you accusing here, \r\nchiamattt? \\\"american society\\\" and \\\"you\\\" are both used, but \r\ni seriously doubt anyone here condones genocide, was proud \r\nof any of those tactics used by former american presidents \r\nthat you mentioned. hell i wasn\\\'t of voting age when any of \r\nthem were elected. how much personal responsibility do you \r\nwant american individuals to take? the things that you have \r\npointed out <b>suck</b> and i\\\'m the last person to beg out \r\nwith \\\"one person can\\\'t make a difference,\\\" but seriously, \r\nwhat is the point of this? if it\\\'s just be angry, good, be \r\nangry, angry people fuck shit up, cause revolutions. if \r\nit\\\'s to be accusing and make us blind americans realize \r\nwhat big fucking jerks we are, you\\\'ve failed.',5,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2951,912,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>yeti: i\\\'ll take weeb to show in the third!</P>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(2952,913,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','it\\\'s important not to equate \\\"america\\\" (ie. the land and \r\nits people) with \\\"the american goverment\\\" (ie. the \r\npresident, the congress, and the major corporations \r\nthat pull the levers).',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2953,913,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','furthermore, you are saying conflicting things. on one \r\nhand, you feel that americans should be willing to \r\nadopt a socialist system where their basic needs are \r\ntaken care of by the government. on the other hand, you \r\npoint to several instances of how the american \r\ngovernment is corrupt and violent.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2954,912,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','no one should be required to feel okay with \r\ncompromising their values just because economic \r\ntimes are rough. while sometimes you have to buck up \r\nand pay the rent however you can, the prospect of a life \r\nwhose only purpose is to maintain base survival needs \r\ncan feel unfullfilling and depressing.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni reference you to maslow\\\'s <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://chiron.valdosta.edu/whuitt/col/regsys/maslo\r\nw.html\\\">heirarchy of needs</a>.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2955,908,'2001-05-13 02:12:41',0,'bmg','i had a birthday party... many of us were wearing \r\ndresses... it was really fun, damage was at an all time \r\nlow... music was listened to, booty was shaken, \r\npeople\\\'s were laughing, hugging, kissing, smiling... it \r\nwas all so much fun... and to think that they showed up \r\nin celebration of me! well, at one point i thought about it, \r\nbut actually i was so giddy celebrating the life of them \r\nthat i forgot that it was for me! wow, i love peole... and i \r\nfeel like a hundred bucks... thank you friends. thank you \r\nle, yeti, austin, jo ... and everyone else that couldn\\\'t \r\nshow up, but would have liked to.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(2956,913,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','good bye. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.139.194','en'),(2957,910,'2001-05-13 04:11:26',0,'sic','late las nite i saw these two college kids on tv hunting a \r\nplastic snake. i then flipped over to jerry springer and two \r\nsisters were doing it with each other. sad sad world',0,0,0,0,'4.16.224.92','en'),(2958,914,'2001-05-13 07:26:54',0,'jo','at this party i met lots of people but what i want to say \r\nright now is: <a href=\\\"http://www.miasma.com\\\">ert</a> is \r\nsuch a neat neat girl.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2959,914,'2001-05-13 07:41:32',0,'chaya','HEY.  happy fuckin birthday, scooter.',1,0,0,0,'158.252.242.24','en'),(2960,916,'2001-05-13 14:21:27',0,'auntie','you must retain a sense of the ridiculous in order to \r\nsurvive.',1,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(2961,916,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','you\\\'re telling me, i just spent three hours cleaning a\r\nbathroom that hadn\\\'t been cleaned in two years.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2962,913,'2001-05-13 23:10:02',0,'jo','i think that everyone should go look at <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.hole.hu/~calver/kepek/america.gif\\\">this \r\nstunning piece of art</a> that really sums up how america \r\nviews the world.<BR>\r\nhere be dragons!',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2963,914,'2001-05-14 00:22:23',0,'roadkat','aiiieeeeee. this is a small world. justin and i have hung \r\nout before. not only is he entertaining, interesting to \r\ntalk to, and a great cook....but he also has tons of weird \r\ncrap in his house. or at least he did before his roommate \r\nmade him clean it. ',1,0,0,0,'32.102.170.95','en'),(2964,908,'2001-05-14 01:26:19',0,'dj1_luna','i was d dj at BMGs party and i was good. i must say.',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(2965,913,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','i don\\\'t think i\\\'ll bore everyone with what i have to say on your post, however, since i do spend much of my dwindling free time involved in constitutional activism, i\\\'d be glad to speak with you through email or another medium.  even pen and paper.  ',3,0,0,0,'207.205.221.217','en'),(2966,917,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<pre><tt>\r\nalongside a narrow yellow strait,\r\nseparating the coming and going,\r\nthe half-dreaming from the half-awake.\r\n\r\ni blink,\r\na wave of blurred rush or leisure\r\ntempts passing or impact.\r\n\r\ni fall asleep with the sun,\r\nbefore racing against the still air again\r\ntomorrow.\r\n</tt></pre>',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2967,912,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\'ve gotten a hold of myself now. i realize those awful\r\nstereotypical fears for what they are. the people there are\r\nprobably interesting and nice and do yoga and balance their checkbooks. mantra: work is\r\nwork. it\'s a paycheck. <p>i shall endeavor to keep it\r\ntogether. <p>thanks for all your reality bitchslappin\',\r\npals!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2968,912,'2001-05-14 05:58:03',0,'roadkat','<p>hey now...some of us dorks might have grown up in South \r\nBay. im not saying who, but it could be true??? ha. course \r\ni do heckle my friends that work there. dont let the cube \r\nlife suck you dry i say. but then again, they are my \r\nfriends. if it wasnt for them i wouldnt know about getting \r\na good <a href=\\\"http://www.fuel44.com/\\\" \r\ntarget=\\\"_blank\\\">fuel</a> fix when im hankerin for some \r\nrockabilly.</p>',3,0,0,0,'32.102.169.227','en'),(2969,908,'2001-05-14 07:18:10',0,'Linear','BMG\\\'s party was definitely a good time - a lot of people I \r\nhadn\\\'t seen in a long time.',0,0,0,0,'207.8.3.193','en'),(2970,918,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','this describes one of my worst fears.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2971,918,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','my first reaction was, did you take photos? but i don\\\'t \r\nthink i would have either.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2972,918,'2001-05-14 10:19:08',0,'jo','wait. what?<BR>\r\nare you fucking serious?<BR>\r\nis this the 17th century?',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2973,912,'2001-05-14 12:40:04',0,'nobody','(eoj)  [i just wanted to be a nobody]\r\n<BR>\r\nand then there are other people who might have to do the\r\nsame thing\r\n<BR>\r\nand who might drive you to work sometimes depending on where\r\nit is.  this is sometimes called carpooling.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.3.15.93','en'),(2974,913,'2001-05-14 13:16:29',0,'newbie','s5: I suggest that you think about your comment. It is \r\nimportant *to* equate the American government and the \r\nAmerican people. Why? Because when a country outside of ours \r\nlooks to us, or thinks of us, they do so as one. The \r\nAmerican government is representative of the American \r\npeople, whether you\\\'ve voted for the members who compromise \r\nit or not. Why do you suppose that there are countries in \r\nthe world who hate Americans? Because they\\\'re evil? No, more \r\noften than not because the American government has had a \r\nhand in some event that has effected their people in a \r\nnegative way. \r\nIt sucks, but that\\\'s the way that it works. If you don\\\'t \r\nwant the government to reflect upon you as an American \r\ncitizen, then I would suggest that you try and do what you \r\ncan to prevent the American Government from doing eff\\\'ed up \r\nthings. ',3,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(2975,918,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','don\\\'t you live in texas?',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(2976,913,'2001-05-14 16:55:05',0,'erin','i am agreeing with newbie here. i spent time reading the \r\nabove comments and found myself struck time and again at how \r\noften people wanted to seperate themselves from \\\"America\\\", \r\nseperate themselves from our past, and our present...how can \r\nyou make any claims on our future?. why is that? just too \r\nugly? avoidance some how clears you of responsibility? and \r\nwho cares that chiamatt spoke of a broad range of issues? you \r\ncan only handle one dirty shirt at a time? i love it when you \r\ndisect the way a person brings up an issue instead of \r\naddressing the issue at all. yeah, thats great. VERY \r\neffective.  \r\n',0,0,0,0,'129.105.107.184','en'),(2977,920,'2001-05-14 17:14:49',0,'jo','what\\\'s a peewee egg?',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2978,919,'2001-05-14 17:18:43',0,'ert','olivia, do you know the song Strange Fruit by Billie \r\nHolliday? it is a song about hanging bodies. that song - \r\njust thinking about it - it makes my heart beat heavy and \r\nslow.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.3.15.90','en'),(2979,914,'2001-05-14 17:36:03',0,'ert','i met jo! i like jo a lot! \r\n\r\nalso, justin still has a lot of weird stuff everywhere.',1,0,0,0,'64.3.15.90','en'),(2980,914,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','happy birthday, scooter jonez.<br>\r\nit was sure nice to see you.<br>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(2981,913,'2001-05-14 17:57:46',0,'bmg','<p>maybe this doesn\\\'t pertain to the discussion above, \r\nbut as I was thinking about this post I decided to post \r\nthis:</p><p>\r\nSo somehow the \\\"isness\\\" of our present nature is out \r\nof harmony with the eternal \\\"oughtness\\\" that forever \r\nconfronts us. And this simply means this: That within \r\nthe best of us, there is some evil, and within the worst \r\nof us, there is some good. When we come to see this, \r\nwe take a different attitude toward individuals. The \r\nperson who hates you most has some good in him; \r\neven the nation that hates you most has some good in \r\nit; even the race that hates you most has some good in \r\nit. And when you come to the point that you look in the \r\nface of every man and see deep down within him what \r\nreligion calls \\\"the image of God,\\\" you begin to love him \r\nin spite of. No matter what he does, you see God\\\'s \r\nimage there. There is an element of goodness that he \r\ncan never sluff off. Discover the element of good in your \r\nenemy. And as you seek to hate him, find the center of \r\ngoodness and place your attention there and you will \r\ntake a new attitude.<br>MLK<p>\r\n\r\nafter readin all of <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.creighton.edu/mlk/speeches/enemies.\r\nhtml\\\">this</a> it made the rest of this post seem very \r\ndepressing to me... I say offer resolution to these \r\nproblems... if there is no resolution it is nothing more \r\nthan a complaint. Complaints are fine, but resolutions \r\nmake people understand your complaint.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(2982,918,'2001-05-14 18:04:41',0,'jo','okay i went and read your entry. for some reason, just from \r\nthis, i got the impression that he had been hanged rather \r\nthan it being a suicide, not that it\\\'s any less \r\ngruesome.<BR>\r\nholy shit.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2983,913,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','there is no \\\"us\\\" and \\\"them\\\" anymore.  there is only \\\"us\\\".  \r\nthose lines you see on maps are imaginary.  the generation \r\nthat holds our fathers and mothers will be retiring soon.  \r\nit will be up to us to clean up the mess.  there is nowhere \r\nelse for any of us to go.  this world is our home, our \r\ncountry.  despite all the things we may disagree about, \r\nthere is so much more on which we can find consensus.  so \r\nmany of us are apathetic.  there are things you could be \r\ndoing to improve the situation:  things other than TV, \r\nchat, indie rock, drugs, and wallowing in your post-\r\nadolescence.  we are the light; we are the refuge; there is \r\nno place to take shelter but ourselves.',1,0,0,0,'209.4.238.10','en'),(2984,920,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','you should be proud of the way you handled that.  when i worked in a place similar to that i\\\'d have been ready to punch the guy after he refused to take my word.',2,0,0,0,'207.205.216.181','en'),(2985,920,'2001-05-14 22:13:21',0,'sic','its like the anorexic girls who would demand their fat free \r\ntortillas. damn calm down we dont have any',0,0,0,0,'129.65.178.99','en'),(2986,918,'2001-05-14 23:50:45',0,'alanna','the thing is that at the time nobody had any idea if it was \r\na suicide or if someone had murdered the man and strung him \r\nup.&nbsp; i assumed the former while sam questioned the \r\nlatter.&nbsp; either way, it was pretty...shocking?\r\n',6,0,0,0,'156.3.140.242','en'),(2987,920,'2001-05-14 23:52:20',0,'peewee eggman','i am a small egg. not large...but small. chickens dont lay \r\ntoo many of me because they are force fed in long large \r\nwarehouses in quebec where the owners turn the lights on and \r\noff 8 times a day. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.140.69','en'),(2988,913,'2001-05-15 02:01:28',0,'s5 to newbie','<p>in fact, i have thought a great deal about that \r\nstatement. i would not have posted it otherwise.</p>\r\n<p>while it\'s true that most americans have the right to \r\nvote, it\'s difficult to hold citizens accountable for the \r\nactions of the US government. large corporations and \r\nfree trade organizations (WTO, NAFTA, FTAA) get \r\ncandidates who serve their financial agendas into the \r\nelections.</p>\r\n<p>(my favorite example: prison corporations lobby for \r\nharsher sentencing laws and support \"tough on \r\ncrime/drug war\" candidates, for the purpose of boosting \r\ntheir profits.)</p>\r\n<p>and voters are misinformed/uninformed because \r\nmajor media sources have the same financial agenda \r\nto protect. (only a minority of the country has the \r\nbenefit of alternative press.) consider that we live in a \r\nmedia-shaped culture. now reread your daily paper, \r\nand notice which news items are disguised as \r\npropaganda or advertising.</p>\r\n<p>i say \"don\'t equate US citizens with the US \r\ngovernment\" in response to the sentiment that \r\n\"america sucks\". america doesn\'t suck. we have a \r\ndiverse culture, creative minds, art, innovation, a high \r\nstandard of living, and beautiful countryside. there are \r\nproblems here, our government does horrible things, \r\nbut americans are generally good people.</p>\r\n<p>i feel saddened that americans are hated overseas, \r\nand disgusted that our representatives were voted off \r\nthe UN human rights panel (though \r\nwell-deserved).</p>\r\n<p>and yes! i agree that one should work for change \r\nrather than whine that things are broken. in the last year \r\nand a half, i\'ve funded 4 organizations (3 national, 1 \r\nlocal) that work for social justice, helped organize \r\nrallies and conferences, donated consulting time to \r\nhelp non-profits organize online, handed out \r\npamphlets, spoke to the media, worked with political \r\nstrategists, read lots of books on causes i care about, \r\nand spread the good word to my communities, as best \r\ni can.</p>\r\n<p>sometimes though, there are <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.alchemind.org/DLL/ocbcopinion.htm\">\r\nfailures</a>.</p>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2989,919,'2001-05-15 02:23:45',0,'tristessa','yo olivia, i\\\'ll be your partner anytime.',6,0,0,0,'137.99.161.130','en'),(2990,912,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\ni really like the eoj. we ate chocolate cake together today.\r\nsoon, i shall paint his car.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(2991,919,'2001-05-15 08:44:25',0,'-','<p>\r\nsoul takes soul as eye take eye<br>\r\ni will wait for you<br><br>\r\ndid you ever wake up<br>\r\nand find yourself alone<br>\r\ndid you ever wake up far from hom<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.spfc.org/songs-releases/song.html?\r\nsong_id=187&song_type=-1\\\">e</a>?\r\n</p>\r\n',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(2992,913,'2001-05-15 08:45:00',0,'mojo23','<p>As a Canadian, I have to reply to Jo\'s excellent graphic:\r\nWe might be hayseed, but we are not friendly.  Compliant and\r\nchickenshit, maybe, but not friendly.  If there is any\r\ndegree of anti-American sentiment overseas, you know how a\r\ngood Canuck talks about \'stupid Americans.\'\r\n</p><p>\r\nAfter living here a year now, I am no longer among the\r\ndissers of Yanks.  I love them, and I love the countryside\r\n(it IS beautiful, as S5 points out.)  They are not stupid,\r\nas a national flaw--they are in trouble, and trying to hide it.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd I am glad to see that someone (S5-i love your courage)\r\npointed out the fact that American people should not be\r\nblamed for the atrocities and idiocies the government commits.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAs I Watched very closely my first US election this November\r\nthrough January (sic,) I was extremely concerned that the\r\nmajority of my friends who had voted were not going to get\r\nthe leader they wanted, and that they, the rest of the\r\nnation, and the world, would have to sit by and wait out\r\nthis administration, just as they have had to do in the past.\r\n</p><p>\r\nPaying attention to the news, voting, thinking carefully\r\nabout how you spend your money, watchdogging the digbad\r\ncorporations,and all the things they tell you to do to be a\r\nresponsible citizen does nothing to make the government\r\nresponsible to its citizens.  Even state iniatives are\r\noverridden by little BIG Texan Oil Men (see s5\'s failures\r\nlink.)\r\n</p><p>\r\nI wanna move to Australia.\r\n</p><p>\r\nAnd no comments about complete arguments, please...we are\r\nnot in political philosophy class here.\r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'158.252.242.132','en'),(2993,923,'2001-05-15 08:48:30',0,'mojo23','I hope it is just a shared name.  May she sing to you again.',1,0,0,0,'158.252.242.132','en'),(2994,923,'2001-05-15 09:58:48',0,'sawx','<br>\r\n<small>*m00f*</small>',1,0,0,0,'144.132.7.52','en'),(2995,923,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','it was her...\r\nmy friends in wellington are all gathered around at one \r\nhouse drinking and remembering her.\r\ni wish i could be there with them.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(2996,920,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','fat free tortilla?  wtf!  Isn\\\'t like the whole point of a\r\ntortilla that it be soft and chewy and stuff?  Lipid content\r\nwould seem to be a prerequisite for all that.  fat free\r\ntortilla my white honkey ass...',4,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2997,919,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','dude! d00d! ok, it\\\'s just a fun word to type',3,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(2998,925,'2001-05-15 13:08:20',0,'jo','moms and daughters, man. toughest relationship ever. ever.',1,0,0,0,'205.188.198.13','en'),(2999,925,'2001-05-15 15:14:12',0,'bmg','<p>wow, um... that is intense. As a moma\\\'s boy, I \r\nwould like to tell you that you gotta stand up and be \r\nstronger than that; go home and hug and kiss yo \r\nmomma cause you might not have her around much \r\nlonger... ad then I realize that I have done much of the \r\nsame as you. I talk to my mom at most once a month \r\nmaybe twice. I keep the conversation surface level, and \r\nI try to keep it under half an hour. -- but I am getting \r\nready to go visit her, and hopefuly be able to spend \r\nsome quality time with her. -- I hope you work \r\nsomething out with your mother, I really do, and I won\\\'t \r\ntell you all the reasons why - I am sure you know them. I \r\nhope it works out.</p>best friendly, nice and \r\nsupportive</p>',2,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(3000,925,'2001-05-15 16:54:44',0,'auntie','i was 40 before i came to terms with my mother.  it was a \r\nhard relationship always, and she is not an easy person to \r\nlike many times...<br><br>\r\n\r\nbut, i finally decided that i didn\\\'t want to die with her \r\nfeeling confused about who i am as opposed to who she \r\nthought i was, and i also wanted to make an attempt to \r\nteach her to be more of a real person herself, and less of \r\na peer parrot...<br><br>\r\n\r\nso i told her that i was going to follow the serenity \r\nprayer in our relationship -- accept the things i could not \r\nchange, find the courage to change the things i could, and \r\nlook hard for the wisdom to tell the difference.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni am glad i made the effort.  it\\\'s been 7 years now and we \r\nhave learned which subjects are off limits, and when i see \r\nher being too judgemental (her greatest fault), i call her \r\non it gently and when she sees me being too procrastinating \r\n(my greatest fault) she does the same.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe\\\'s still not nice many times, but she is less horrible \r\nthan she was 7 years ago, and i see progress.<br><br>\r\n\r\nshe will never really approve of me, i think.  but that is \r\nokay, for my own children don\\\'t always approve of me, mandy \r\nis often very unhappy with me, for example, and that\\\'s okay \r\nbecause you are allowed to be different than your kids and \r\nthey are allowed to be different than you.<br> <br>\r\n\r\nmake the effort, even if it only a call once a month.  \r\ndon\\\'t talk, listen. <br><br>\r\n\r\nnice supportive<br> ',2,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(3001,918,'2001-05-15 17:03:10',0,'auntie','sometimes things are so profound you don\\\'t really \r\nunderstand your reaction to them until years later.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni sat in a labor room with a young wife who knew she was \r\ndelivering a dead baby one time.  for hours.  and then when \r\nthe child was born, we took pictures of her and her husband \r\nand the poor dead baby so they could remember what he \r\nlooked like, and i know this was not a macabre thing to do, \r\nit was a helpful thing, but even now i don\\\'t remember the \r\nactual scene where i stood and sat and held her hand and \r\nheld her sobbing husband and nope, none of that.  i do \r\nremember what the pictures looked like.  they haunt me.',0,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(3002,917,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<br>\r\nsomehow you have me singing...<br>\r\n..here i am, stuck in the middle with you...<br>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(3003,925,'2001-05-15 19:44:34',0,'ert','<P>\r\nit\'s interesting that you say you don\'t want to hear her \r\nunsolicited opinions, yet you say you want to vocalise your \r\nown opinions to her about her. auntie knows.. try \r\nlistening. <br><br>\r\n\r\nit sounds like you do care about your mom but you are \r\nfrustrated by her. a big part of the problem in dealing \r\nwith other people\'s problems, is when you do, you end up \r\nshouldering a lot of it yourself and if you are having a \r\nhard enough time carrying your own weight, it can be too \r\noverwhelming deal with another\'s.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndo you know why she is the way she is? when you feel like \r\nyou can handle it, open a dialogue with her. you don\'t need \r\nto tell her all the things you find wrong with her. if you \r\nask her about herself, and encourage her to talk and let \r\nher know you can handle hearing what she has to say, she \r\nmay feel more able to face her problems. she may be very \r\nwell aware of her shortcomings but afraid to voice them \r\nwith you out of fear of criticism. being a mom myself, i \r\ncan say, it\'s evil as hell to think you\'ve done your kid \r\nwrong or let them down in some way, but yeah, it can be the \r\nkid who says, yeah mom, you\'re not perfect but i love you \r\nanyway. it can be you to teach her to be accepting. <br><br>\r\n\r\nremember if you want her to accept you, you have to accept \r\nher too. listen to what she is saying. assume good. keep \r\nyour judgement at bay. assume she started out as a little \r\nkid with dreams and goals, hopes and desires. find out what \r\nhappened. find out if she\'s given up. people can survive \r\nunbearable pain. knowing that someone cares about us can \r\nmake it easier- talk/listen to her when you can. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni have two moms. my biological mom: i called and told her i \r\nlove her. my step-mom: i ignored this mother\'s day as many \r\nin the past. she has done me wrong in SO many ways. when i \r\nam feeling very magnanimous and strong, i can talk to her \r\nand listen to her and shoulder the immense agony i know she \r\ncarries with her all day every day.. and i know that that \r\nis the reason she is such a jerk. dialogue with her is \r\nusually exceedingly taxing on me, and sometimes it leaves \r\nme feeling icky, like i\'ve been brought down to her level. \r\ni pity her. i disapprove of her. i dislike her. i avoid \r\nher. despite my wishes to the contrary, we are members of \r\nthe same family though and i am not rid of her. when there \r\nare problems between us, it hurts us and those around us. i \r\nmake the effort because she is, for now, a part of my life. \r\nif i could not have to deal with her, i wouldn\'t. life is \r\noften like that though, you can\'t choose every person you \r\nhave to deal with. if anything, it is good practice to \r\nlearn how to interact with people you have a hard time \r\ntolerating, or more appropriately to your situation, \r\ndealing with aspects of a person you care about that you \r\nhave a hard time tolerating. ok.\r\n</P>',0,0,0,0,'64.3.15.90','en'),(3004,913,'2001-05-15 20:09:09',0,'stella (aka newbie)','s5:\r\nI think we\\\'re basically on the same page here. It does \r\ndefinately suck that US citizens are blamed for the \r\nstupidity and poor policy of the government, but \r\nunfortunately, that\\\'s the way it works. I don\\\'t think that \r\nmany non-Americans who are like mojo, I think that the \r\npolicy of our \\\'super nation\\\' sets the standard for the \r\nconsciousness of the citizenry. \r\nI also applaud your efforts to help create change in CA,  (I \r\njust moved to NY from SF; I miss it :() If more people took \r\nsome time to help out, even doing something as easy as \r\nhanding out flyers, things could be a lot different. \r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3005,913,'2001-05-15 20:14:27',0,'stella (aka: newbie)','has anyone heard of a writer named William Upski Wimsatt?\r\nif not, you should check out a book called \\\"no more \r\nprisons\\\".. very very interesting book.',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3006,925,'2001-05-15 20:31:28',0,'stella','My sister and my Mother haven\\\'t been on speaking terms for \r\nalmost 4 years. They\\\'ll be brief and polite if confronted \r\nwith each other, but otherwise no phone calls, no email, \r\nnothing. I know why. I know that my sister blames my mother \r\nfor my fathers alcoholism and that she thinks my mother weak \r\nand spineless. I just moved in with my mother again and I \r\nsee the effect that this silence has on her. She tries, \r\nevery time my sisters name comes up, to pretend that it \r\ndoesn\\\'t hurt her, but I\\\'ve seen the tears glimmer in her \r\neyes. Especially when she speaks of the two little boys that \r\n she doesn\\\'t have contact with, her only grandsons. \r\nI don\\\'t know why you and your Mom don\\\'t talk, and it\\\'s none \r\nof my business, but she\\\'s your only Mom. Just make sure that \r\nwhen she\\\'s gone, you\\\'ll still feel that it was worth it. ',1,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3007,913,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','we\\\'re definitely on the same page. <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/18871\r\n28425/o/qid=989960637/sr=8-1/ref=aps_sr_b_1_1/102\r\n-2564661-5452141\\\">no more prisons</a> is a great \r\nbook, a bunch of us here have read it.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3008,921,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','is your roomate a flirt or is she just a female who is open? a female who has a personality is often accused of being \"a flirt\" when she\'s actually just existing in the manner that men are free to exist in. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3009,921,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Men who actually look people in the eye, etc. get called \r\n\\\"flirts\\\" too.</p>\r\n<p>Maybe not as much, but..</p>\r\n<p>In the very least, girls who are friendly and open and \r\ncut and etc. should not be surprised when boys around them \r\nget stupid and fall for them.</p>\r\n-darwin',2,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3010,921,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','maybe they shouldn\\\'t be surprised, but they shouldn\\\'t be \r\nblamed either.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think many people are surprised when other people fall \r\nfor them.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3011,921,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','le and darwin:  she is a flirt.  or perhaps flirt is to weak to describe Ari.  As an example, i had my friend kyle over to discuss a project we had been working on.  She comes out of her room topless, and upon being introduced begins to rub her breasts all over kyles arm.  She isn\\\'t just open and friendly.  She behaves as if every person she meets is going to be invited to her bed.  I\\\'ve lost count of the number of times i\\\'ve been watching the football match (soccer) after a workout, and she\\\'ll walk on by and stick her hand up my shorts and grab my cock.  It\\\'s upsetting, and i\\\'ve told her so, yet she continues.  Perhaps she doesn\\\'t realize that people will notice her and love her without such behavior.  She herself is begining to realize this.  ',3,0,0,0,'207.205.216.205','en'),(3012,925,'2001-05-15 23:50:20',0,'mojo 23','I suppose I\\\'ve been pretty, excuse me, VERY judgmental\ntoward her. Clearly I need to talk to her soon, and to\n*really* talk to her.  She hasn\\\'t been that bad, just\naccusatory and distant, and I can\\\'t say I\\\'ve never treated\nanyone the way I *feel* she treats me.\n\nI feel ashamed now. Damn all you mothers who posted, for\nmaking me look at this in another light...and thanks.\n\n',0,0,0,0,'158.252.241.176','en'),(3013,921,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hm, i have a hard time believing this. and if that\'s \r\nreally the case, we call that \"sexual assault\", not \r\nflirting. perhaps you should invite your housemate to \r\ncome here and tell her side of the story, and we can \r\njudge for ourselves.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3014,927,'2001-05-16 01:56:50',0,'kinbote','All the Pretty Horses bored me as well.\r\nI would read WATCHMEN by Alan Moore. or something by Haruki \r\nMurakami. MAO II by Don DeLillo. or you could read the back \r\nof shampoo bottle? the credits at the end of a film? Archie \r\ncomics? Infinate Jest? maybe another sci-fi book will do. ',3,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(3015,927,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i read the watchmen as a young pre-teen and it \r\nchanged me view of super heros.</P>\r\n<P>Rorschach was my favorite character.</P>\r\n<P>personally i think the Infinite Jest is MUCH better than A Staggering Work Of ... Genius, and recommend it to everyone.</P>',2,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3016,927,'2001-05-16 03:38:16',0,'kinbote','Stagerring work... is over rated. there must have been a \r\nreally bad streak of bad books around this time last year \r\nthat made that book seem so amazing, but that\\\'s just me \r\npersonally - i don\\\'t like funny stuff like that, all the \r\npages it took to get to the 1st chapter made him a wanking \r\nprat in me book. where has talent gone?',0,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(3017,931,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nwant the dog. that\\\'s my dog!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3018,927,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>\r\ni have only read two books in the pomo emo guy fiction\r\ngenre, and these two books were it. the two books were\r\ncompletely different in how they wanted to show the story to\r\nthe reader but they seemed to evoke the same thoughts about\r\nlife, boys, boy thoughts. i think i have come the closest to\r\nunderstanding how it must be to be a guy from reading these\r\nbooks. this is no small feat. </p><p>\r\ncomparison of most salient points:<br><br>\r\nHWOSG       vs       Infinite Jest<br>\r\n----------------------------------<br>\r\n1. Life Metaphor: frisbee (metaphorically beautiful) vs. tennis (mathematic prose gymnastics)<br>\r\n2. Main relationship: brothers (affecting but not maudlin\r\naccounts of bro-bro love) vs.  tennis buddies  (a little\r\nsuperficial but vastly entertaining)<br>\r\n3. Storyline: semi coherent vs. completely whacked out there\r\nstoned on mars style <br>\r\n4. Picture of the Author on the back cover: <a\r\nhref=\"http://www.flakmag.com/features/images/eggers_edit.jpg\">unpretentious\r\nbut dorky</a>, vs the <a\r\nhref=\"http://www.davidfosterwallace.com/images/pictures/david_foster_wallace.jpg\">bohemian\r\npretty boy</a>. \r\n</p><p>i think both books are definitely worth reading. i think egger\'s book is worth spending the time over, compared to DFW\'s book; but DFW is a genius when it comes to essays.</p> \r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3019,929,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','buck showed up at a maz party once',0,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3020,921,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','she totally wanted it. look at how she was dressed.',0,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3021,931,'2004-07-27 05:13:57',63,'olivia',' <p>i misread it as twin falls idaho.  it might just be that\r\ni have built to spill on the brain:</p>\r\n<p>\r\nChristmas, Twin Falls, Idaho is her oldest memory<br>\r\nShe was only two<br>\r\nIt was the first time she felt blue<br>\r\n<br>\r\nCafeteria, Harrison Elementary<br>\r\nBeneath a parachute<br>\r\nI saw her without shoes<br>\r\n7UP I touched her thumb and she knew it was me<br>\r\nAlthough she couldn\\\'t see<br>\r\nUnless of course she peeked<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMy mom\\\'s good she got me out of Twin Falls, Idaho<br>\r\nBefore I got too old<br>\r\nYou know how that goes<br>\r\nThat\\\'s where she still was the summer she turned 17<br>\r\nIn 1983, two weeks after me<br>\r\nLast I heard was she had twins or maybe it was three<br>\r\nAlthough I\\\'ve never seen<br>\r\nBut that don\\\'t bother me<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>to me, that dog looks kinda psycho.  like it\\\'s secretly\r\nrabid or it might turn mean sometime soon.  no offense\r\nmeant.  it just looks almost <i>too</i> friendly.</p>',3,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3022,931,'2001-05-16 11:07:22',0,'sawx','the dog looks like someones crazy uncle, as opposed \r\nto like, someones dad or something\r\n<br><br>\r\nsorry if that don\\\'t make the sense ',2,0,0,0,'144.132.7.52','en'),(3023,927,'2001-05-16 12:55:40',0,'sawx','kick out the jams^H^H^H^H pynchon, motherfuckers!',2,0,0,0,'144.132.7.52','en'),(3024,927,'2001-05-16 14:19:51',0,'stella','Hey Austin:\r\nI don\\\'t know what kind of books you like, but whenever \r\nsomeone tells me that they need a good book to read, I \r\nalways recommend the same one author. Mark Helprin. \r\nMost people haven\\\'t heard of him, he was a speechwritter for \r\nthe Clinton adminitration (I *think*) and he is the most \r\nbrilliant, hysterical, insightive writer that I have ever \r\nread. His writting is amazing. My three favorite are:\r\n\r\nSoldier of the Great War\r\nMemoirs from an Antproof Case \r\nWinter\\\'s Tale.\r\n\r\nAll action packed and extrememly entertaining. All were \r\nbooks that I was very sad to turn the last page in and all \r\nare books that I wanted to keep reading as long as possible.\r\nLet me know, if you read any, how you liked \\\'em.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3025,927,'2001-05-16 14:21:50',0,'stella','mmm! \r\n<b>\r\n I haven\\\'t figgered this text thing out yet... Here\\\'s a more \r\ndecipherable list.\r\n<b>\r\nSoldier of the Great War;  <b>  Memoirs from an Antproof \r\nCase;  <b> Winter\\\'s Tale.\r\n<b>\r\nPhooey!',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3026,927,'2001-05-16 14:27:24',0,'jo','there need to be some new pictures of dfw avaialable. i\\\'m \r\ntired of staring at the same 5 over and over.<BR>\r\nnot that i want to <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://sent.diaryland.com/051001.html\\\">jump his \r\nbones</a> or anything.<BR>\r\n(i still prefer hwsg over infinite jest. of course, i \r\nhaven\\\'t read the latter. because if i\\\'m going to read a \r\n1,000 page book, i want it to have a resolution. i mean, \r\njust for fun.)',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3027,927,'2001-05-16 14:28:40',0,'jo','also: </b> &lt;/b&gt;',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3028,931,'2001-05-16 14:31:41',0,'jo to kat','i think it\\\'s a pretty cute dog, kat. <BR>\r\njo to olivia: i. love. that song.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3029,927,'2001-05-16 14:34:01',0,'jo','</b></b></b></b> stella, the html is &lt;BR&gt;, not \r\n&lt;b&gt;, which is bold.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3030,927,'2001-05-16 14:43:38',0,'stella','Jo: yeah. Thanks. I wasn\\\'t paying attention. \r\n<br> \r\nI\\\'ve just proven myself to be the irritating newcomer, \r\nhaven\\\'t I? ',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3031,902,'2001-05-16 16:03:04',0,'bridgie','i\\\'m considering entering a poly relationship myself. i\\\'m \r\ngoing through the same things you are, i think. <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://hellojed.diaryland.com/010514_85.html\\\">here</a>\r\n is my extended blather on my situation; it struck me as \r\nquite similar to yours. i\\\'m not sure what i\\\'m going to do \r\nyet, really. i won\\\'t know until more happens. but i wanted \r\nto say hey, and thanks. it\\\'s good to know that someone else \r\nis doing this too. :)\r\n\r\nand remember (this is the part i\\\'ve gotten bad at): you\\\'re \r\nallowed to feel however you want. don\\\'t feel bad for \r\nfeeling jealous. work through it, but if deep down you want \r\nmonogamy, don\\\'t deny that to yourself.',1,0,0,0,'24.4.255.64','en'),(3032,927,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','Three words: <i>Zorba the Greek</i>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3033,927,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Phung Moll','I would recommend:\r\n\r\n\\\"Easy Travel to Other Planets\\\" by Ted Mooney\r\n\r\nund\r\n\r\nany collection of Barthelme\\\'s\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3034,926,'2001-05-16 19:28:55',0,'auntie','first -- you appear to me to be very depressed.  perhaps \r\nclinically depressed.  women are always aware of depression \r\nbehaviours, but most male people seem to think that when \r\nyou are depressed you sleep a lot and wimp around.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis is not necessarily so.  some people become mean when \r\nthey are depressed, and a very common behaviour of a \r\nprofoundly depressed person is to \\\"see how bad i can be and \r\nstill be loved.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nso the depressed person starts making \\\"tests\\\" for friends \r\nand family and strangers and even animals.  the tests are \r\nusually really unacceptable behaviours that astound the \r\ndepressed person as much as the people to whom he/she \r\nexhibits those behaviours.<br><br>\r\n\r\nif you are doing any recreational drugs of any sort, \r\nincluding alcohol, you need to stop right away.<br><br>\r\ntake a look at your diet.  too many colas or coffees?  too \r\nmuch sugar or red meat?  these things can affect your mood. \r\nremember that white bread, potatoes, white rice, and many \r\nother foods that you think are carbohydrates also turn to \r\nsugar as soon as they hit your bloodstream.<br><br>\r\n\r\ndrink loads of water and watch your diet for 72 hours.  if \r\nyour mood and unacceptable behaviours do not improve \r\nmarkedly, you really need to go to a doctor and get checked \r\nout.  i\\\'m not talking about a shrink, i\\\'m talking about a \r\nmedical doctor, preferably an internist or \r\nendocrinologist.<br><br>\r\n\r\npost your city and someone will know who best to \r\nsee.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni have been watching your messaging for a couple of months, \r\nand i must say i have noticed that they seem to show your \r\nmood becoming more and more unmanageable.  or maybe you are \r\njust an asshole.  i hope not.  most people aren\\\'t REALLY \r\nassholes, they are just going through periods of flux that \r\nmake them behave like one.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnice supportive and constructive.',0,0,0,0,'207.192.73.199','en'),(3035,927,'2001-05-16 19:42:20',0,'auntie','there are some books that everyone thinks they know about \r\nand don\\\'t really need to read, and some books that everyone \r\nread as a kid and can\\\'t really understand at all until they \r\nbecome a grownup.<br><br>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Penrod by Booth Tarkington.<br>\r\nA story of a boy who is all things that boys are and more.\r\n<li>Shogun by James Clavell<br>\r\nSex, violence, history, good, evil, anger, love, not for \r\nkids.\r\n<li>Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn by Mark Twain<br>\r\nForget the teevee movie.  These books are all about being a \r\nboy and growing up to understand how important it is to \r\nmake up your own mind, and the ride is funny as shit \r\nbesides.\r\n<li>Catch 22 by Joseph Heller<br>\r\nIt\\\'s about the war and it isn\\\'t about the war.  It\\\'s the \r\nultimate Murphy\\\'s Law with characters you will get to know \r\nand a three legged dog, too.\r\n<li>Some silly shit to read that is very fun would be the \r\nMASH series by Richard Hooker.  Most people don\\\'t know that \r\nthere are actually many M.A.S.H. books and they are fun to \r\nread.\r\n</ol>',1,0,0,0,'207.192.73.199','en'),(3036,927,'2001-05-16 20:11:40',0,'jo to stella','not at all. everyone makes mistakes.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3037,926,'2001-05-16 21:24:04',0,'incitor in reply to auntie','<p> \r\nokay, that was a really nice comment, thank you.  however.  i am depressed, i do suffer from clinical depression, i have for a long time, and i am aware of many of the signs.  so no worries there.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni do not tend to think about as you say \\\"how bad one can be and still be loved.\\\"  some love me, and will continue to, darwin has stood as my friend through some hard times.  no matter what i did or said or felt he lent his support.  there are others in my life who are much the same.  analyzing my behavior as best i can, i feel comfortable in saying that i am not testing to what limit i push people.  those i love know i love them, i make it as clear as i can.  few of my friends claim to be astounded by my behaviors, it is more subtle than that.  i\\\'ve always been somewhat distant, and standoffish amongst strangers.  the places i\\\'ve been in life have required it.  more than anything it\\\'s what i think that worries me, not what i do.  and for that matter why are certain friends of mine \\\'immune\\\' to this process?  why am i able to find myself amongst them, but not others?  could it be that there is more to my thoughts than just the depression...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI don\\\'t do drugs, i refuse to sink back to those days.  i drink, maybe a few beers a month.  i drink no less than 5 liters of water a day, which is the minumum required by my workout schedule.  i take a heavy dose of vitamins, the exact numbers of which i worked out with my physician.  many of the activities i used to partake in, which helped moderate my agression are unavailable to me right now.  however i am working to resolve that.  and in case you\\\'re wondering, i do not pick fights to help myself out.  i was heavily involved in the SCA as a heavy fighter, and performed other martial activities.  i wonder if the fact that most of my so called friends here have no drive for anything better than getting fucked up or laid has had an effect on my mindset.  Often i sit and think that i\\\'d be better off getting rid of them all, since we share almost nothing.  I want something better from life than just slumming about taking fun in when possible.  Else i would not be spending so much time involved with political activism and certainly would not be working hard on a business.  i know for certain i am not an asshole.  there are many who know only the gentlest touch from my scarred and calloused hands.  and many have felt my compassion.  depression is a war i\\\'ve fought for my whole life.  this is something more.  could it truly be that i need to uproot once more?  what do you think.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\noh, and i\\\'ve only been posting here for a little over a week.  :)  and to be honest, there is much strife currently.  perhaps this is all just a reaction to the major disruptions i am dealing with right now.  \r\n</p>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'207.205.220.47','en'),(3038,934,'2001-05-16 21:24:59',0,'Mike','I\\\'ll be going out that way in July ,see you when you get \r\nback!',1,0,0,0,'206.224.71.188','en'),(3039,927,'2004-07-27 05:13:57',63,'olivia',' oo!  someone else knows about murakami.  read.  read!  i\r\nfind it pretty hard to get bored with books, no matter how\r\nexciting the last one i read was.  but i tells ya, nothing\r\ncould get me through cryptonomicon.  it\\\'s SO FUCKING\r\nBORING.  i always thing shakespeare is fun to read and also\r\nanything which is slightly challenging to read.  i\\\'m reading\r\nwurthering heights for the 10th time right now.  i\\\'ve been\r\nreading a lot of comic books lately.',1,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3040,934,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i\'m leaving for albuquerque on the 28th of this month. perhaps we\'ll \r\nwalk past each other and not even know it.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3041,921,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','i\\\'d be glad to have her meet you and tell you her side.  however, her side would be no different.  there were three people other than her in my home at the time.  she doesn\\\'t normally behave so extreme, but she has the potential.  what can i say?  she has problems just like everyone i know, myself definitly included.  ',3,0,0,0,'207.205.220.47','en'),(3042,934,'2001-05-17 00:04:06',0,'LinearB','I like Albequerque. \r\n\r\n<p>My favorite part of arriving in Alb is when I unpack, \r\nand first open the toothpaste, the altitude and air \r\npressure makes it ooze out of the tube onto the counter - \r\nas if its saying \\\"Hey guys! Everybody out. We\\\'re here!\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>Alb used to have lots of cool independent bookstores. \r\nThey\\\'re all gone now.\r\n\r\n<p>The best photography bookstore in the country (<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.photoeye.com\\\">http://www.photoeye.com</a>) \r\nis in Santa Fe.\r\n\r\n<p>I think Joel-Peter Witkin still lives out there. His \r\nsister, Cynthia had a tattoo shop in Alb.',4,0,0,0,'207.8.3.193','en'),(3043,935,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>believe it or not, we censor each other <TT>:)</TT>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin fact, we get in arguements about this shit.  so rest\r\nassured, if you lost a post to the machine, at least\r\ntwo people argued about it, maybe three.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit should also be noted that the person that sent you \r\nthe notification email is not necessarily the same person\r\nthat wanted your article dropped.  so dont hold\r\na grudge.\r\n</P>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3044,935,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','always the taurus\\\'s getting slagged. i dont understand.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.140.69','en'),(3045,929,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','id eat anyones pancakes',6,0,0,0,'64.229.140.69','en'),(3046,935,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>\r\ni got censored too when i was a new swinney poster, and i\r\nwas pissed, because i didn\\\'t know that swinney posts had a\r\nquasi-guideline. now, i have to agree with swinney even\r\nthough i ripped him a new asshole at the time. the writing\r\non this site shouldn\\\'t be bloggy, they should be constructed\r\nand and have some sort of point, or context. \\\'i found this\r\non the ground\\\' is not enough context, but \\\'i found this on\r\nthe ground and i wonder why this xxx was yyy and it\\\'s an\r\ninteresting question because of zzz\\\' is context. i will have\r\nto weigh in on swinney\\\'s side on this post. it didn\\\'t seem\r\nlike enough substance. of course it\\\'s subjective. of course\r\nsome uninteresting blogs will fall through, because it is\r\nsubjective. but i guess my point is, if you\\\'re writing\r\nsomething and it\\\'s more like a \\\'thoughts\\\' thing, use\r\n\\\'thoughts\\\' instead. try to decide whether you\\\'re trolling\r\nfor comments or whether you really have something to say. \r\n</p><p>\r\nbest blathering friendly </two cents>.</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3047,935,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<p>\r\nthere is a <a \r\nhref=\"http://swinney.org/journals/faq.phtml\">FAQ</a> \r\nnow, linked off the main page, that roughly describes \r\nthe editorial process. (perhaps the link should be more \r\nprominent?)\r\n</p><p>\r\nrecently, a bunch of us met together, in person, and \r\nargued about how to be better editors, and how to keep \r\nthe site fresh and interesting to read.\r\n</p><p>\r\nonline communities die (ie. become irrelevant) under \r\none or both of these circumstances:\r\n</p><p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>the community becomes exclusive, never open to \r\nnew members, and thus ideas suffer inbreeding.\r\n<li>the community becomes diluted, like a revolving \r\ndoor of small talk and rehashed exchanges (the AOL \r\neffect).\r\n</ul>\r\n</p><p>\r\na healthy community constantly walks the dangerous \r\nline between those two extremes. in the beginning, this \r\nis easy to do: most participants have been around long \r\nenough to have rough idea of the original spirit.\r\n</p><p>\r\nbut once a community becomes more open and more \r\nvisible, a crisis point is inevitably reached. members \r\nwho identify themselves as \"the core\" will say things \r\nlike, \"what happened?\", \"i don\'t like the site anymore, \r\nwho are all these people?\", or \"these new people just \r\ndon\'t get it.\"\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe irony of \"getting it\" is that online communities rarely \r\nbegin with a charter. rather, the group mind attracts and \r\nshapes the style and content of communications. so \r\nnew members arrive, and attempt their best guess of \r\nwhat is needed/expected. often times, even \r\nwell-intentioned contributions result in tedious flame \r\nwars.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthe crisis point is thus uncovered when in-fighting \r\nforces a group to define itself: what are we?\r\n</p><p>\r\nusually there are three solutions: (1) clamp down on \r\nmembership (ie. elitism), (2) open the floodgates (ie. \r\ndilution), or (3) develop a balance based on fairness.\r\n</p><p>\r\nwe\'ve chosen (3). we argued about how to manifest (3). \r\nand we agreed on values, rather than dogmatic \r\nguidelines: \r\n</p><p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>thoughtful self-expression should be encouraged.\r\n<li>contributions should contribute, rather than \r\ndemand.\r\n<li>friends should not get special treatment, to prevent \r\nelitism from permeating the site.\r\n<li>the author should be respected. if a post is trashed, \r\nthe author should be sent a friendly explanation, with \r\nencouragement to try again using a new approach.\r\n</ul>\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere are countless joke lists, BBSs, bloggers, and \r\ndiary sites on the internet. this site is a different format \r\nthan the others, and specifically requests attention to \r\nthoughtful self-expression.\r\n</p><p>\r\neditors do not censor. censorship is a loaded word that \r\nshould be reserved for discrimination of particular \r\nideas and people. on the contrary, anyone can post \r\nhere, and a full range of ideas and outlooks are \r\nrespected.\r\n</p><p>\r\nposts that you find crap, i might find interesting, or \r\nvice-versa. for example, i personally don\'t find it \r\ninteresting to read countless tales of \"i\'m sad about the \r\ngirl who got away\", but others receive value from \r\nreading them. either because the reader finds it useful \r\nto compare the story to their own experience, because \r\nthe reader has a need to provide sympathy, or because \r\nthe post gives further insight into the author\'s \r\npersonality.\r\n</p><p>\r\ncyan, the message you received when your post was \r\ntrashed did not include the words \"crap\" or \"sucks\". it \r\nwas gentle and polite. you are an amazing writer, i love \r\nreading your journal, and i consider you a friend. that \r\nsaid, i agreed with the person\'s decision to not activate \r\nthe post.\r\n</p><p>\r\nplease be patient. we are doing our best to encourage \r\nan open community, that can continue to grow without \r\nimploding. we are doing our best to filter in a manner \r\nthat is both respectful to our friends, and welcoming to \r\nstrangers. it\'s tricky. we are doing our best.\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3048,929,'2001-05-17 03:05:35',0,'LinearB','and pizza with gravy!',0,0,0,0,'207.8.3.193','en'),(3049,927,'2001-05-17 03:25:56',0,'stella','Ugh. I tried Cryptonomicon. It was horrible. ',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3050,934,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','a year ago, lil old new zealander me was supposed to be in \r\nalbuquerque.\r\ni\\\'m really pleased it never happened because i might not be \r\nwhere and who i am now if i had gone.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(3051,935,'2001-05-17 03:42:56',0,'cyantist','i appreciate everyone\\\'s attempt to make this a place that is \r\na better online community than most.  it just seems that \r\nthere would be a better way to do it than peer revue.<br>\r\njust because i posted this, doesn\\\'t mean that i like anyone \r\nhere any less.  steve, you know i am very fond of you.  so, \r\nthat is not the basis of my rant. <br>\r\nweeb, thank you *very* much for your post.  it made me feel \r\nbetter that i wasn\\\'t the only person.  up until today, i had \r\nyet to see the link with the rules about what can and can\\\'t \r\nbe posted and why a post would be passed or rejected.<br>\r\ni also thank all of you for allowing me to voice my opinion. \r\n that is very important to me.',1,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3052,935,'2001-05-17 03:43:38',0,'cyantist','and yes, s5, you are right.. no one used the word \\\"crap\\\" at \r\nall.  i did.',1,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3053,936,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','i am famous in my town.  they call me \\\"movie man\\\" or, when \r\nreferring to my friends and/or co-workers and i \r\ncollectively, \\\"the witches.\\\"  i don\\\'t know where they got \r\nthat.  could be that i (and occaisonally others i know) wear \r\nall white sometimes, for my (our) own reasons. also famous \r\nbecause i walk everywhere in a town where almost nobody else \r\ndoes.  if they do, it\\\'s assumed they\\\'re destitute or off \r\ntheir meds.  (both of which i\\\'ve been, at various \r\ntimes.)<BR>fame is like this:  for the longest time i was \r\nfamous only to myself.  i was a celebrity, but nobody knew \r\nit but me.  i imagined people said as i passed: \\\"there goes \r\nthat matt mccord.\\\"  of course, a lot of people in town did \r\nsay that, but they were less than adulant when they \r\ndid.<BR>then i became famous locally.  in the \r\npaper.<BR>today, their love; tomorrow, the world.<BR>i have \r\nthis need.  it\\\'s probably some kind of defeciency in my \r\nlife.  i wanna be adored.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.10.119','en'),(3054,935,'2001-05-17 06:04:24',0,'cyantist','i just thought about this more, go figure...\r\n<br>\r\nhere\'s the point i wanted to make.  after some chat, a \r\nfriend of mine pointed out..\r\n<PRE>\r\n<*friend*>your post was pretty weak\r\n/m friend i bet it was\r\n/m friend i don\'t feel well\r\n<*friend*> not a very good one to complain about\r\n/m friend what post is weak?\r\n<*friend*> the short one\r\n/m friend yeah\r\n/m friend well, that is what i wanted to say\r\n<*friend*> i mean\r\n<*friend*> if like 20 people sent something like that every \r\nday\r\n/m friend but I DON\'T\r\n<*friend*> swinney wouldnt be very interesting, would it?\r\n/m friend that is the point\r\n/m friend i show that i get comments\r\n/m friend every time i post\r\n/m friend i should be allowed one wimpy post or two\r\n<*friend*> hm maybe\r\n</PRE>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nbasically, all i\'m trying to say is .. that if someone posts \r\nsomething often and it is consistently good... what is wrong \r\nwith a post that is somewhat lacking?  if i posted more than \r\none posting that lacked \"content\", i can understand nudging \r\nme and saying, \"hey there.. that needs help\". however, i\'m \r\npretty consistent with my posts.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nanyhow, i didn\'t see that link until today.. because it has \r\nonly been up for two days.. so how could i have seen it?\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nok.. i\'m officially done ranting.  i\'ll try to live up to \r\nthe standard of swinney.org and make sure that every post \r\nthat i have contains acceptable content.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3055,927,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<p>\r\nTry something by Walter John Williams if you\\\'re into science\r\nfiction at the moment.  <em>Hardwired</em>, <em>Voice of the\r\nWhirlwind</em>, <em>Angel Station</em>, etc.  Or you could\r\neven read <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/p3rlm0nk/cli.html\\\">some more\r\nstephenson</a> (that essay I think mutated into a book later\r\non, but not the one you\\\'d think from the &lt;title&gt;). \r\n</p>\r\n<p>Or if you like fantasy (I usually don\\\'t but I made an\r\nexception for this series), try reading Robert Jordan\\\'s\r\n<em>Wheel of Time</em> series.</p>',1,0,0,0,'66.68.181.63','en'),(3056,937,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','on the drum bridge in the tea garden in golden gate park, \r\ndarwin and jo and i met a madman. he was eating a snickers  \r\nbar (his second, he told us: <i>first one was so \r\ngood..</i>). he carried a bamboo stick and was scruffy with \r\nlittle chippy teeth and a leather sunburn. bulbous burnt nose \r\nsticking out from under blond eyelashes and a black \r\nstocking cap. chewing the chocolate bar with a big wide \r\nopen mouth, words moving out of his head there from his \r\nmaw, his gaping chocolate-soiled maw. he told us that woody \r\nharrelson was on late night teevee. <i>woody does yoga</i> \r\nhe told us. i think darwin tried to interject something \r\nabout oprah doing yoga, or maybe i just thought about \r\ndarwin telling me about oprah and christy turlington doing \r\nyoga. he told us he saw woody harelson and he told us that \r\non late night teevee, woody may as well have admitted \r\ngrowing pot in costa rica. woody. ',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3057,937,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','cyan: it should be noted that there was a heated fight \r\nabout this article being activated. it ended with swinney \r\nscreaming and punching the wall.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3058,937,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','apocalypse now',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3059,937,'2004-07-27 05:13:57',63,'olivia','who would call their kid woody?\r\n\r\ni mean, really.',6,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3060,937,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>woody harrelson is really cool.  he climbs the golden \r\ngate bridge with leftist banners in tow.</P>\r\n<P>how much cooler than that can you get?</P> \r\n<P>not much.</P>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3061,937,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','it\\\'s my fault. swinney and i have been drinking since 8 \r\ntonight. earlier we got into a fistfight over some girl on \r\nthe way to sushi.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3062,937,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>You got the story confused, le, but I guess it\\\'s close \r\nenough. Hope so, otherwise something bad will happen.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',5,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(3063,937,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','poor memory + creative energy = artistic license',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3064,927,'2001-05-17 08:52:29',0,'annette','Are you guys kidding? Cryptonomicon was excellent! I guess \r\nI\\\'m a math geek of old and have an interest in cryptography \r\nand related matters anyway, but still.\r\n\r\nCurses, I want to read it again now, but I have so many \r\nunread books I would feel bad doing so. I keep buying them \r\nin armfuls and I read fast but not quite that fast.\r\n\r\n<i>The Debt To Pleasure</i> by John Lanchester is superb. \r\nIt has little connection to the rest of this comment but I \r\njust finished it and I had to tell somebody how good it was.',2,0,0,0,'203.109.252.19','en'),(3065,927,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it wasn\\\'t the math and crypto speak that bored me.',1,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3066,936,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','almost famous',2,0,0,0,'66.68.186.134','en'),(3067,937,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','yesterday walking down haight street in front of amoeba a\r\nwoman walked up to elana and i said poked elana in the chest\r\nand said \\\"wow, where did you get those AMAZING orange\r\nbuttons???\\\"  elana shrunk back a little and just stared. \r\nthe woman said \\\"i didn\\\'t mean to be poking at you but those\r\nbuttons are just so cool!\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nwe kept walking.  elana had no orange buttons.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3068,936,'2001-05-17 16:32:45',0,'zb','<p><font face=\\\"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\">where i live, \r\nthe duvalhouse in austin, it has a long legacy of brilliant \r\nexciting wonderful people who have lived there before and \r\nwho are destined to live there in the future. i feel proud \r\nand honored to live there now and to know the people of \r\nthis home and all who are associated with it. and yes, it \r\nis inevitable that one of us shall become a world-renowned \r\nicon someday, a name discussed at the family dinner table \r\nor on entertainment tonight. but perhaps we already are, \r\neach of us in our own ways, i truly believe we are all \r\nalready famous. <br>\r\ni want to create this family tree of sorts, a flow chart, a \r\ndynamic representation of the connections of everyone and \r\neverything. and the branches of this tree would reach out \r\nforever and touch incredible things and travel extravagant \r\ndistances and accomplish remarkable feats. <br>\r\nand span time. <br>\r\nwe are spanning time. </font> </p>',0,0,0,0,'216.171.37.54','en'),(3069,937,'2001-05-17 17:37:05',0,'jo','xep that is my new favorite story.<BR>\r\ni sorta dug the guy on the bridge, even though he weirded \r\nyou out, els. i mean, he was strange, but he had a good \r\npeaceful energy. he just wanted to hang out and eat \r\nsnickers and be weird.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3070,934,'2001-05-17 18:50:13',0,'Quiet','I don\\\'t like to comment, but I will.\r\n\r\nI am from Albuquerque, New Mexico. I live here all day \r\nlong, every day. It sure is pretty, but it is also quite \r\nboring!\r\n\r\nHowever, if you are coming to visit old friends or family, \r\nthen it is A+! Albuquerque is a two day town, that is, \r\nunless you plan on spending all your time with people you \r\nlove.\r\n\r\nNow then. I shall go back to actively listening and \r\ndefinitely not responding.',1,0,0,0,'24.4.255.169','en'),(3071,939,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Unless you have some source of chemical free morning \r\nglory seeds, you will have quite a trip.. to.. the \r\nhospital. The seeds sold in stores have been treated with \r\nnoxious chemicals to prevent people from tripping on them. \r\nEven untreated seeds contains lots of various alkaloids \r\nwhich make the trip systemic (ie not just mental) and quite \r\nlikely very unpleasant.</p>\r\n<p>The thing that really concerns me is that 5 minutes \r\nbrowsing the morning glory pages at <a \r\nhref=http://www.erowid.org>http://www.erowid.org</a> could \r\nhave given you this same information. If you\\\'ve decided to \r\nget high to expand your mind, <u>surely</u>  you are aware \r\nof this resource..</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.erowid.org/psychoactives/faqs/natural_highs\r\n_faq.shtml#mgs\\\">This Page</a> has some info you should \r\nread. Specifically :<p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nMost commercially available Morning glory seeds are treated \r\nwith chemicals to thwart consumption. Seeds are also \r\nsometimes treated with Methyl mercury to prevent spoilage. \r\nChemically treated seeds can cause severe nausea, vomiting \r\nand diarrhea. ... Nausea is common even with untreated \r\nseeds. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Really, there are much better (and less unpleasant) \r\nhighs than this crap.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(3072,939,'2001-05-17 21:01:19',0,'pkf','<p>we are silly, us humans. once, we bought morning glory \r\nseeds from the local nursery, took them straight out of the \r\npacket, ground them with a mortar & pestle, and made a tea. \r\nfriends in wimberley. we didn\\\'t know the specifics about \r\npreparation, but it didn\\\'t do anything anyhow. we were also \r\nknown to pick mushrooms from under cow patties all \r\nafternoon long, and then be too afraid to eat them after \r\nreferencing the handy mushroom guide. drugs are better \r\nbought from an unknown source. i really don\\\'t want to think \r\nof a bathtub batch full of acid juice and hair in the \r\ndrain. smoke that pot. \\\'shrooms. o, whippits are cool too. \r\nand no, i don\\\'t really want to know what they do to my \r\nbrain...</p>\r\n<p>i should try meditation instead.</p>',4,0,0,0,'216.171.37.54','en'),(3073,940,'2001-05-17 21:13:32',0,'jo','ewww. EWWWW.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3074,940,'2004-07-27 05:13:57',63,'olivia','you could make your own flag.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nespecially if they won\\\'t take it down.  make a big big\r\nflag.<br>\r\n<br>',2,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3075,940,'2001-05-17 21:34:03',0,'auntie','i don\\\'t want to make a flag.  it would be too hard, i would \r\nhave to make a flag that had every insignia on it the klan \r\nhates and i don\\\'t even know all the stuff they hate, but i \r\nguess it\\\'s just about everyone not in the klan.  it would \r\ntake years.',0,0,0,0,'207.192.75.94','en'),(3076,941,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','being mindful of the decisions you make isn\\\'t \r\npompous. it\\\'s realistic and allows you to resolve \r\nconflicts.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3077,937,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','he didn\\\'t weird me out too much. i had my usual amount of \r\nbig city concern that he\\\'d get overwhelmingly weird, but i \r\nliked him in the end.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3078,941,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','sorry if i upset you pal. wasn\\\'t referring to specific\r\nactions, just ruminating on the emergence of the community\\\'s\r\nself-awareness. we\\\'ve all seen what happens when people\r\nimbue their community with an animus; it becomes a shallow\r\nversion of its former unassuming self, and eventually it\r\ndisintegrates, or somebody blows it up. maybe there\\\'s even a\r\nfuneral held for it.\r\n\r\ni bet there\\\'s an equilibrium point, but if so it\\\'s subtle,\r\nand i haven\\\'t participated in a community that\\\'s attained it\r\nfor very long.',0,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3079,941,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','so isn\\\'t your post contributing to that? :)',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3080,941,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','but i know what you\\\'re getting at. cyborganic took itself \r\ntoo seriously in that respect, and it was tedious. but i \r\nstill think that occasional bursts of self-awareness are \r\nuseful, as long as they\\\'re not the constant focus.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3081,940,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','auntie, i have a rainbow flag you can have if you want. \r\nlots of people use the flag as a symbol of gay pride but i\r\nhave always seen it as a symbol of celebrating diversity and\r\nacceptance of every color or type of person.  there are also\r\nflags around san francisco now that just have an \\\"=\\\" sign on\r\nthem to represent equality.  should we send you these\r\nthings?',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3082,940,'2001-05-17 23:36:27',0,'auntie','i called mark and he said that \\\"i was in the army for 20 \r\nyears laying my ass on the line so people could be free to \r\nsay and think what they want and you just need to leave it \r\nalone, nothing you can do or say will change him and if you \r\ntry to say anything it will just make him more of a donkey \r\nthan he already is, he\\\'ll get bored with it in a day or \r\ntwo.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark is prolly right.  people like that enjoy the hoorah \r\nand hoopla that goes with creating division and \r\ncontroversy<br><br>\r\n\r\nhe also said not to go out in the front yard because \\\"some \r\nother idiots will probably come around and shoot at the \r\nstupid sonofabitch.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark is right, and i am right, and that guy is wrong, and \r\nanyone who sees it is going to be horrified.  the other \r\nneighbors are freaked out, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\nit turns out the house on the other side is up for rent, \r\nthe first house in the neighborhood to be for rent ever... \r\nand he probably put it up to scare the realtors.  mandy \r\nlooked more closely when she was walking the dog.  she said \r\nthe flag was crispy new.  he must have bought it when he \r\nheard the house was going to be rented.  i didn\\\'t know \r\nthere were stores that sold them....<br>',0,0,0,0,'207.192.75.1','en'),(3083,940,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','i know a whole lotta brothers in lecanto who would love to \r\ngo over there around 3 in the morning and put the fear of \r\ngod in him. just say the word....\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.51','en'),(3084,940,'2001-05-18 02:39:49',0,'auntie','but matt, that\\\'s what the klan did to the brothers.  it\\\'s \r\nwrong, and two wrongs really don\\\'t make one right, just \r\nlike your mom always told you.  when something is this \r\nserious, you can\\\'t let your emotions rule.  my first \r\nreaction was to go over there and have a screaming fit.  \r\nbut nothing would be accomplished.  the neighbors have all \r\npretty much agreed to ignore the flag, and if HE brings it \r\nup, they will all tell him that it offends them and he \r\nshould take it down, but none of us are going to affirm his \r\nobvious need for comment by taking notice of it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nat the same time, we can never forget what history tells us \r\nabout letting deeds of this sort go unpunished.  he is, \r\nlike i said, old and not smart, he may believe that our \r\nsilence is approval, which is why i am going to give him an \r\nopportunity to try and bring it up to me, so i will have \r\nthe opportunity to tell him that it offends me greatly, and \r\ni wish he would take it down and burn it.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni will be sure to remind him that jesus loves the little \r\nchildren of the world, red and yellow, black and white... \r\nhe professes to be religious, perhaps that will open his \r\nears and his heart.',0,0,0,0,'207.192.75.70','en'),(3085,940,'2004-07-27 05:13:57',63,'olivia','i didn\\\'t mean a flag that tries to compete with the KKK \r\none.  I mean one that says \\\"I do not discriminate based on \r\nskin colour\\\" or something like that.  you know, just to \r\nprotect yourself against the drivebys.  maybe \\\"Any skin \r\ncolour is ok with me\\\" or whatever.  I dunno.  just a \r\nsuggestion.  i reckon making a flag would be fun.  and \r\nyou\\\'re craftsy right?',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3086,939,'2001-05-18 03:16:46',0,'sawx','<br>\r\ni\\\'m a big fan of the other kind...',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3087,940,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','or you could petition the city to rename your street \\\"rosa \r\nparks memorial boulevard\\\".',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3088,939,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nnot everyone knows about erowid. i can\\\'t think of a reason\r\nto ever say to someone that they should know of a resource,\r\nbecause if they did, they wouldn\\\'t be asking. unless you\r\nwant to rub their faces in it. not very nice.',6,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3089,939,'2001-05-18 06:45:41',0,'stella','darwin: thanks for your advice, but it\\\'s all that I\\\'m aware \r\nof already. I am not unfamiliar with erowid, and I\\\'ve \r\n*certainly* browsed for more than five minutes.  I was \r\nasking more for someone who had actually ingested the \r\nmaterial and what kind of experience that they had. \r\nWe\\\'ve obviously already determined that the chemical free \r\nversion would be more appropriate, and we\\\'ve gathered about \r\n6 different methods of preparations to avoid bad things \r\nfrom happening. But again, thanks. \r\n<BR>\r\nWeeb: Thanks for being nice :)',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3090,908,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','oh um, hi.\r\n<br>\r\nhere are olivia\\\'s <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.limpoc.com/waaa.jpg\\\">goggles</a>.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nthat is not olivia though.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3091,933,'2001-05-18 07:44:50',0,'spudlee','woah thats some pretty big news. hope all is well, say hi \r\nto her for me! (and tell her if she breaks yer heart, \r\nshe\\\'ll have me to contend with. hehe)',2,0,0,0,'203.54.215.50','en'),(3092,939,'2001-05-18 07:54:43',0,'Xepmom','In 1961, I injested some ground up morning glory seeds mixed \r\nwith applesauce. I\\\'m not sure that the taste was unpleasant, \r\nbut I clearly remember having difficulty swallowing the \r\nmixture. It was if my throat/swallow apparatus just said -- \r\nno thanks. Had mild nausea all night and a vague feeling of \r\npsychaedelia in progress. It was a long time before \r\napplesauce had any appeal again. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.174.155.193','en'),(3093,942,'2001-05-18 11:43:00',0,'ert','those are fine qualities indeed',0,0,0,0,'64.168.252.82','en'),(3094,943,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','please delete this entry. thanks.',6,0,0,0,'64.229.140.69','en'),(3095,943,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i archived this post. it will not show up in the list.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3096,942,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'Austin\\\'s Dad','On second thought Austin, the greatest $value$ of that cell \r\nphone may be the ability to call your Mom and Dad.  \r\n\r\nThought I would check your site...look good.  Just a \r\nthought, I have always felt one of the true tests of a \r\ngentleman is his ability to express himself without \r\nresorting to superlatives such as \\\"f--k.\\\"  \r\n\r\nSend me an e-mail update on your job search...Love Dad',1,0,0,0,'208.189.162.130','en'),(3097,942,'2001-05-18 14:01:14',0,'jo','spoken like a true dad. this makes me want to call \r\n<b>my</b> dad.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3098,939,'2001-05-18 14:26:59',0,'stella','Yeah, extreme nausea is a concerning side effect to the \r\ntrip.\r\nOn the erowid site, the FAQ tells all about how the husk of \r\nthe seed contains a chemical that causes vomiting, but that \r\nit can be reduced and even (with the right treatment) \r\ncompletely eradicated. If the whole seed is ground up and \r\ningested, without some kind of rinse, it definately causes \r\nproblems. \r\nOne of the reasons that I\\\'m not sure if this stuff is worth \r\nthe effort. ',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3099,944,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i have days like that chia.  saving face and all the\r\ngames we play seem so irrelevant and it feels like you\\\'ll\r\ndie if you don\\\'t just get to feel the way you feel for a\r\nwhile.</p>\r\n<p>i guess that\\\'s what people mean by \\\"just let it all\r\nout\\\".</p>',2,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3100,908,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>that\\\'s my room.</p>\r\n<p>are you stalking me?</p>\r\n<p>please be stalking me.</p>',1,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3101,944,'2001-05-18 16:36:50',0,'chia','i have been brutally honest and i have let almost everything \r\nout the past few months. i try. i try again. irrelevant is a \r\ngood word. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.140.69','en'),(3102,942,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\ni found myself in a weird blues bar last night seeing james \r\ncotton perform...totally by drunken sleazy accident so i \r\ncalled my old dad at like 11:30 and yelled into the phone, \r\ndad! i\\\'m at this blues club, seeing james cotton, i just know \r\nyou\\\'d love this!<br>\r\nand he\\\'s like ok yeah, sounds cool. call me tomorrow ok when \r\nit\\\'s a little less loud.</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(3103,941,'2001-05-18 18:42:43',0,'uhuru','<p>Being a loner who appreciates the ideals of community \r\nand sometimes occasionally finds herself in its midst, I \r\nfeel the same--that community for community\\\'s sake alone \r\ndefeats its own purpose (supposedly to be greater than the \r\nsum of its parts)...</p>\r\n<br>\r\n<p>A good friend of mine recently got \\\"adopted\\\" by the \r\nRythym Society, and I have barely heard from her in a \r\nmonth.  I feel like she got swallowed into a weird cult.  \r\nNot to say that their parties don\\\'t rock the house, but the \r\n\\\"community\\\" of Rythym Society is so ridiculously self-\r\nconsicous that it almost contradicts the ostensible idea of \r\nwhat community is supposed to be.  I\\\'m all for rythym, \r\ndon\\\'t get me wrong (although I can\\\'t spell it).  But the \r\nidea of \\\"once you\\\'re in you\\\'re in, and everybody\\\'s your \r\nbest friend\\\" just rubs me the wrong way.  My posse is the \r\nanti-posse.  We don\\\'t have meetings.  We run into each \r\nother at the laundromat.</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'216.185.7.75','en'),(3104,941,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i read in \"Africa\" by John Reader that usually, a person is\r\nmost comfortable in a community size from 150-200 people,\r\nmax. most people can only hold in their head about 50 people in their\r\npersonal community circle, which includes family, friends, and \r\netc. i think for me, the number is more like 30. like that\r\nsilly song says, people need people, and we\'re all more\r\nalike than you\'d think... i wonder how big an online\r\ncommunity has to be to start to feeling too big. but how do you\r\nmeasure that? would it be a bigger number than the face-to-face\r\nnumbers? how have we adapted to this new medium? does it change everything? hmm. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3105,938,'2001-05-18 19:26:49',0,'hedwig','<p>if everyone used craziness as an excuse to evade \r\nresponsibility, then nothing in this world would ever have \r\na chance to prosper and grow... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>in my opinion... we are all allowed to break down once \r\nin awhile, but the real strength lies in getting back up \r\nand facing your problems... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>you can only run away for so long.... turning a blind \r\neye will not erase the past... or fix the present... or \r\nmake the future palatable...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>nothing quite like unsolicited advice from a \r\nstranger...</p>\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'198.92.137.130','en'),(3106,942,'2001-05-18 21:49:05',0,'chaya','i have one too.  you really have to show me the beer \r\ntrick.  really a lot.',6,0,0,0,'158.252.211.69','en'),(3107,942,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i have requested that austin lets me film the beer bottle \r\ntrick with the DV cam. mpgs will be posted.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3108,943,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','it will show up on the comments, though. when you\\\'re this \r\nfragile, put yourself first for awhile, chia.  spoil \r\nyourself a little.  nobody else is going to take care of you \r\nthe way you can.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.92','en'),(3109,942,'2001-05-18 22:28:09',0,'bmg','<p>oop! Dad busted yah! harhar...</p>\r\n<p>albuquerque is fun, don\\\'t let anyone tell you that it is \r\nnot.</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.189.128.88','en'),(3110,934,'2001-05-18 22:31:37',0,'bmg','<p>I like ABQ a lot. I grew up here, and I have pretty good \r\nmemories of here. I like the downtown college area, it \r\nreminds me of austin, without being in Austin... would I \r\nmove here? Yah, I would. Just not now. Someday I would like \r\nto return here... I love the evenings.</p>',1,0,0,0,'209.189.128.88','en'),(3111,940,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>You should burn a Star of David on their lawn. That\\\'d \r\nshow \\\'em.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(3112,942,'2001-05-19 02:38:58',0,'kat','mine\\\'s an ericsson. i feel left out with no nokia. i dont \r\nthink the big E would withstand opening a beer. but it is \r\ngood for calling mom and dad. like tonight. i said, \\\"hey \r\ndad im in montana and the truck is making a funny noise. do \r\nyou think its bad?\\\" and he said, \\\"you better stay put for a \r\nfew days and get it fixed, cause if you wait and it breaks \r\nwhile you\\\'re in Canada...well then you\\\'re fucked.\\\"',4,0,0,0,'32.100.201.140','en'),(3113,939,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','<P>Me and my pal Val took some Morning Glory LSD back in \r\n1994. It was blue blotter, and definitely tasted different \r\nfrom other blotter I had eaten. Suffice it to say, the \r\nwoman who gave it to me told me to take it in small \r\namounts. It was very powerful stuff. It was springtime, and \r\nI was visiting Val at Auburn Uni. We ended up staring at \r\ndogwood blossoms and grass and little violets all day. It \r\nwas a really nice trip, from what I remember. No adverse \r\nside effects, a little woozy, but lsd does that to me \r\nusually. Intense visual trip. Really nice.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.39','en'),(3114,947,'2001-05-19 11:23:40',0,'sawx','<br>\r\nwireless mice are going to suck  until someone comes \r\nup with wireless electricity :)\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe weight of the batteries means you\\\'re more likely to \r\nhurt yourself using one...\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have one of these, and its very nice - but unix types \r\nmight be after something with a few more \r\nbuttons<br><br>\r\n\r\n<img \r\nsrc=\\\"http://store.apple.com/Catalog/US/Images/learnm\r\nore_pro_mouse.jpg\\\">\r\n',3,0,0,0,'144.132.7.52','en'),(3115,946,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','that\\\'s just beautiful, man. what city was this? in america?',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.39','en'),(3116,946,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','also, HOW DID YOUR INTERVIEW GO!@#!$',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.39','en'),(3117,940,'2001-05-19 15:00:24',0,'stella','<P>I once heard Henry Rollins on the radio, he was hangin\\\' \r\nwith the dj\\\'d at this station. The subject of the KKK came \r\nup, and he starting talking about some of his ideas on how \r\nto deal with them. </P>\r\n<P> The best one was to wait until the KKK was having a \r\nrally and bring a large faction of the Rainbow Brigade to \r\nthe rally. Wait until the press show up to make yourselves \r\nknown and once the cameras are rolling, have all the gay \r\nguys grab a KKK member and kiss him *SMACK* on the lips, \r\nmaking sure that the cameras capture the image. Rainbow \r\nBrigade flees before getting pulverized. \r\n</P>\r\n<P> The news is swarming with photos of KK members being \r\nkissed by men, ruining the rally. All the KKK members \r\nfriends now know that they have been kissed by men, thus \r\nhumiliating the members and having the overall effect of \r\nridiculing the KKK. </P>\r\n<P>\r\nHe explained it much better than I did, but you get the \r\noverall idea. Not likely to work out that way, but the \r\nthought is HIGHLY amusing. </P>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3118,940,'2001-05-19 15:21:38',0,'auntie','rollins is how elly managed to deal with puberty.  there\\\'s \r\na song on country radio about \\\"i think i was dancing with a \r\nman\\\" -- mark sent me the mp3 via email not too long ago, i \r\nlaughed my ass off.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmark drove here from alabama last night to get me.  he is \r\ngoing to discuss the problem with my neighbor and suggest \r\nto him that the rainbow coalition may get word of the flag \r\nand decide to send a militant to rent the house next door, \r\nor picket the street or something.  i had forgotten we have \r\nseveral black families in the neighborhood.  i guess i \r\ndon\\\'t think in terms of color...  i would guess that many \r\nof our snowbird neighbors from nj and ny are catholic, \r\njewish, etc.  it must be more dreadful for them than it is \r\nfor me.',0,0,0,0,'24.164.21.183','en'),(3119,947,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i want a laptop i can use in the bathtub.  i think it\\\'s\r\npossible.  moldable keyboards and all that.  they did it\r\nwith radios... why not computers?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3120,947,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<p>xep, check <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.geotechnology.co.uk/\\\">this</a> out.\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.naval-\r\ntechnology.com/contractors/computers/thomson/>and \r\nthis</a><br><a href=\\\"http://www.man-\r\nmachine.com/mouse1.htm\\\">and this too</a><br><a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.marinecomputing.com/\\\">and also \r\nthis</a><br><a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.dolch.com/html/laptops.html\\\">And!</a><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.amrel.com/\\\">okay no more</a><br>\r\nYou should look for anything that is us mil-std 810E or us-\r\nmil ip54 certified. Those are the standards regarding water \r\nand dust.</p>\r\n<p>yeti needs coffee now. :)</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3121,946,'2001-05-19 20:26:21',0,'bmg','That is awesome... Sometimes, I LOVE PEOPLE... Most of the \r\ntime I just like them... i wanna give that officer a kiss \r\non the cheek...\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.189.128.88','en'),(3122,942,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<STRONG>FIGHTING THE GENTLEMEN\\\'S FIGHT SINCE 1975</STRONG>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3123,946,'2001-05-19 22:18:37',0,'jo','austin, baby!<BR>\r\ni had a cute moment last night. my friend jason and i were \r\ndriving the video store to rent hamlet and we were driving \r\nthrough hyde park and it was just gorgeous and he turned to \r\nme and said, \\\"best city in america.\\\" i laughed: \\\"austin \r\nis?\\\" \\\"yes. YES.\\\" i just loved that.',1,0,0,0,'172.138.91.212','en'),(3124,942,'2001-05-20 00:01:13',0,'bmg','<p>word</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.189.128.88','en'),(3125,949,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','this conundrum and similar have been discussed a lot. it \r\nwas discussed on swinney.org recently. <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?\r\nid=813.txt\\\">here</a>. :)',2,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3126,949,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','of course in <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?\r\nid=813.txt\\\">that</a> post and it\\\'s comments we were \r\ndiscussing ONLINE diarists which is a different thing than \r\nwriting in your own private journal. i have thoughts on \r\nthat too, but i am hungry. later.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3127,947,'2001-05-20 05:47:31',0,'auntie','i want a wireless optical mouse, too.  ',0,0,0,0,'207.192.75.31','en'),(3128,950,'2001-05-20 05:50:58',0,'auntie','i always want to believe everyone, and if anyone does a bad \r\nthing, i immediately try to find a way that they did it by \r\naccident, not on purpose.  unfortunately, there are people \r\nwho do bad things on purpose, and many thieves and liars, \r\nas well as greedy assholes who would cheat people like me \r\nin a heartbeat and never feel the slightest twinge of guilt \r\nabout it.',2,0,0,0,'207.192.75.31','en'),(3129,946,'2001-05-20 13:21:16',0,'mscreant','yes, austin. i don\\\'t know how the interview went. not well, \r\ni\\\'m assuming. i\\\'m no good at pimping myself.\r\n\r\ni\\\'ve been kicking my ass wishing i had asked the cop\\\'s name \r\nor something... so i could thank her again. she made my day.',2,0,0,0,'66.68.34.51','en'),(3130,950,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\ni liked this. it\\\'s very unusual prose to come from s5, i\r\nthink, which is why i like it even more.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3131,947,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','didn\\\'t tesla do something with wireless electricity?',3,0,0,0,'207.205.217.102','en'),(3132,952,'2001-05-20 19:04:18',0,'dawn','What is it about fighting that calms you? Is it simply the \r\nrelease of energy? Something more agressive? Do you feel \r\nmore comfortable sharing yourself while doing something you \r\nenjoy?',2,0,0,0,'206.97.109.66','en'),(3133,953,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i liked this story. it reminded me of some of my favorite \r\nnights out, the nights when you say to yourself  \\\"i\\\'m \r\nsomewhere sketchy\\\", and nod knowingly to your \r\nsurroundings.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3134,953,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\nLast night I noticed that my Edward\\\'s Banana Split Pie \r\n(from Burger King) was made in Atlanta, Georgia.  They get \r\nthe little things all the way over here to lil ol\\\' New \r\nZealand. Craziness! Have you tried them? They are yum. I \r\nlike the strawberry bits the best.<BR>\r\nI spent most of the time I was eating it wondering if \r\nAndre3000 from Outkast had ever tried one. He\\\'s from \r\nAtlanta, don\\\'tcha know?',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(3135,953,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','fast food restaurants terrify me.<BR>\r\nAndre 3000 bit that shit off my homie from Miami, <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.ursula1000.com/\">Ursula 1000</a>. But I \r\nforgive him because Outkast is dope, forward backwards and \r\nsideways!\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.39','en'),(3136,953,'2001-05-20 23:02:07',0,'s:','<a href=\"http://www.ursula1000.com/\">Ursula 1000</a>. But I \r\nforgive him because Outkast is dope, forward backward and \r\nsideways!',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.39','en'),(3137,952,'2001-05-21 00:51:39',0,'incitor to dawn','it\\\'s a release of energy, but also of anger and agression.  it\\\'s something that i both enjoy and am good at.  i know many people here, and few have the skills my partner and i have.  this brings us both pride, but also drives us to teach others.  last night i was in a bad mood, wanting very much to inflict my anger on another.  instead i hooked up with a good friend, and together we vented something inside us, drawing us and our other friends together.  a destructive urge in us brought not pain but joy.  all humans have a warrior side, and those who are most in touch with this often need to find a safe and healthy outlet for it.  the book _fight_club_ was a good, if overdone example of this need.  i\\\'ve been under immense stress of late, and have had some extemely disappointing contact with others, this has made it hard for me to behave in what i consider a moral way.  i\\\'m young enough yet to easily fall back into patterns i last lived when i was 17 if i don\\\'t carefully monitor my anger levels.  a night which could have ended in my arrest and the harming of innocent people instead ended with sweat, blood, grins, and backslapping.',1,0,0,0,'207.205.217.101','en'),(3138,949,'2001-05-21 02:02:41',0,'jp','I think journal writing is good for a lot of reasons.<BR>\r\nFor one thing it can help you articulate and identify\r\nfeelings and memories. Also this gives you access to\r\nprevious periods in your life that you would otherwise have\r\nforgotten.<BR>\r\nIf your interested in writing it\\\'s good just for the aspect\r\nof making yourself pump out the writing as a kind of\r\npractice.<BR>\r\nBut it can get overly obsessive and indulgent also I\r\nthink.<BR>\r\nOverall, I\\\'d say don\\\'t worry about it.<BR>\r\nThe fact that that you\\\'re becoming more aware of how you\r\nwere is enough to say that it\\\'s all good.<BR>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3139,954,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','you need a website',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3140,954,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://diaryland.com/signup/\\\">http://diaryland.com/\r\nsignup/</a>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3141,945,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','this is amazing. like. keep on.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3142,954,'2001-05-21 11:46:59',0,'auntie','lovely -- touching a soul in need',0,0,0,0,'166.72.176.198','en'),(3143,954,'2001-05-21 12:08:40',0,'mscreant','children are wonderful, intuitive, symphathetic creatures. \r\nmy daughter amazes me everyday. while most of us would let \r\na stranger continue on their journey, crying, without much \r\nthought, my daughter is always trying to comfort them. \r\nwhether it\\\'s a kid on the playground she\\\'s never seen \r\nbefore, who is crying, or an adult who needs a smile and a \r\nkind word... she\\\'s always in tune. and always wanting to \r\nhelp. we are always too busy thinking about a million \r\nthings that don\\\'t really matter and that aren\\\'t happening \r\nright now... and children are always living in the moment, \r\nperfectly aware of every emotion going on around them. if \r\nwe could only slow down... we could learn so much from \r\nthese little miracles.',2,0,0,0,'66.68.190.232','en'),(3144,954,'2001-05-21 16:16:19',0,'zb','thank you.',0,0,0,0,'216.171.37.54','en'),(3145,952,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Incidentally, this is one of the reasons that I like \r\nCapoeira so much. It\\\'s training mind and body for a \r\nconfrontation between two people who are each trying to \r\nfool the other. It doesn\\\'t matter that people aren\\\'t \r\nusually actually hitting each other, it\\\'s a very real \r\nexperience where you _could_ get actually hurt if the two \r\nof you aren\\\'t careful. The person you are playing a game \r\nwith has a responsibility to control themselves, just as \r\nyou have a responsibility to watch out for them. It\\\'s \r\ncombat but it\\\'s <b>friendly</b>. Most of the time, \r\nanyway.</p>\r\n<p>I recently watched \\\"fight club\\\" again, and I couldn\\\'t \r\nunderstand two things. First, how two weeks of fighting \r\nonce or twice a week turned weak people into super-studs. \r\nSecond, why it was a <u>good</u> thing to lose teeth and \r\nbreak ribs. They should have just played Capoeira and \r\npreserved their health.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(3146,952,'2001-05-21 21:15:40',0,'incitor to darwin','okay, to reply i need to explain a few things.  the only thing preventing a man (or woman for that matter) from being what you called a \\\'super stud\\\' is the inability to cope with the bodies adrenal response.  the fight or flight reaction, if it is truly a survival instinct, is a poor one.  the function of the higher level brain is impaired, motor coordination is lessened, and tunnel vision begins.  also, most persons can only last for around 4 minutes before exhaustion sets in when they are under the adrenal response.  \r\nthe good news is that with as little as four hours of training, i, or a select few others, can teach anyone how to make the most of this.  with a bit more training, which my course can provide, you will find that even when the adrenaline dumps to your bloodstream, you can continue to speak and think normally.  your motor coordination will improve, and even the tunnelvision can be controlled.  that is why in fight club, they became so tough seemingly overnight.  it is why i have easily beaten blackbelts in street fights.  and it is why i have seen a 90lb. woman strike a 240lb. man so hard with her elbow that it knocked him off his feet and sent him through the air a good 3 feet.  \r\nit takes suprisingly little to become adept at focusing your adrenal response in useful ways.  but it takes the most realistic training possible, or preferably, the real thing.\r\nas for why it may or may not be good to go balls to the wall and end up hurt.  well, lemme say that until i took my first severe beating, i never truly understood how you can harm a human being.  until you have felt flesh and bone give way beneath your knife hand, you cannot comprehend the movements\\\' power.  until you have fought a life or death conflict, you will never know you can.  combat is not friendly.  it is life or death.  the reason i don\\\'t get into fights anymore, the reason i walk away no matter what, is that if someone forces me to fight, i will kill that person.  i will put a knife in them before they even understand what happened.  or i will bite their carotid artery, sever it, and wait the two minutes till they die.  fighting can be fun, it can put you in touch with the warrior within, but combat, that is for killing.  and i won\\\'t fight on the street unless it IS combat.  ',4,0,0,0,'207.205.220.8','en'),(3147,952,'2001-05-21 22:43:06',0,'conan the barbarian','what is best in life? to crush your enemies, to see them \r\ndriven before you, and to hear the lamentations of their \r\nwomen.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3148,952,'2001-05-21 22:46:24',0,'DSM-IV','the doctor is <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.accg.net/antisocial.htm\\\">IN</a>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3149,952,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','okay.  how amusing that someone would even suggest i suffer from anti-social personality disorder.  why don\\\'t i go through each of the points hmmm?\r\n<p>\r\ni have little enough concer for societal expectations.  neither i suspect do most communists or socialists living here in america.  nor drug dealers and the like.  for that matter, consider that most people on here probably choose their own path.  as for the rules...well, i follow more rules than almost anyone i know.  i don\\\'t do drugs, i\\\'ve never once driven while drunk, i don\\\'t randomly assualty people, i never steal from anyone (in fairness i should admit i do make use of warez).  I don\\\'t carry an illegal firearm, my home is not riddled with booby traps.  It\\\'s been a very long time since i last had illegal explosives in my possestion.  so i think i do alright.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ndo i violate the rights of others?  i have never silenced a person using force.  i have never taken someone\\\'s firearms against their will.  i have never forced someone to provide me sleeping space.  i have never enslaved or attempted to enslave anyone.  i could go on, but you get the idea.  in my youth i admit i was a violent and cruel person.  drugs and alcohol and hate are a bad mix for any human.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nunlawful behavior.  i think that\\\'s been covered.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nanyone who knows me well knows that i value the truth more than almost anything.  i have lied like most people, but today it\\\'s a rare die when i lie escapes my lips.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile i am not a parent, i have had a hand in raising several children.  i love kids.  they are the one thing in this world sure to bring a smile to my face.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'ve held my current job for two and a half years.  even though i despise it.  before that i held a job for three years.  i left to make more money and stop working midnights.  holding a job has never been difficult for me.  i\\\'ve been fired only once, but was rehired two weeks later after my direct superior was fired for getting rid of me.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni was once violent in the extreme, i still have an agressive side.  but i have not acted on that in the last 6 years, excepting a few thankfully rare situations where i had to defend myself.  i refuse to take the bait when someone tries to goad me into conflict.  i\\\'ve walked away from fights in front of dozens of witnesses.  when i\\\'ve worked security i have talked down probably over a hundred people, refusing to strike them even when they have struck me. as for irritability...well, i am a pretty grouchy guy.  i make no excuses for this behavior, i struggle with it every day.  on the other hand; my friends always get my warmest smile and a hug and/or handshake.  there have been many nights spent cuddled up on the couch with a warm girly friend watching the simpsons or the soccer match.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni must say that i am disappointed that someone would suggest this about me.  could it be, just maybe, that people like me are a threat to those who don\\\'t understand us?  any one of you who ever met me, would have no need to fear me.  you might not like me, i might not like you (and i\\\'d certainly let it be known), but you\\\'d have to actually threaten my life or the life of an innocent before i raised my hand to you.  i have so often noticed in the so-called intellectual community here where i live, that i am considered a knuckle-dragger.  snide comments, and witticisms are common.  the worst i\\\'ve ever done to these people was remind them that all that protects them from me, other than my self control, is vapor.  i can kill quite easily, and the police can arrest me and off to prison i go, but my victim would still be dead...  consider that i\\\'ve never killed.  Am i such a threat to your world?  that i teach people to protect themselves, does that make me so very horrible?  am i slandered because i am sick, or because some people fear me without cause?\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'207.205.220.8','en'),(3150,952,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Maybe the DSM-IV anonymous coward was suggesting that \r\n<u>I</u> suffer from antisocial personality disorder! Did \r\nyou ever consider that??? No, of course not! It\\\'s all about \r\n<b>you</b> isn\\\'t it!</p>\r\n<p>I probably qualify under more of the criteria than you \r\ndo, anyway.. :P</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',4,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(3151,952,'2001-05-22 00:49:32',0,'a local creep','<p>\r\ni, however, steal stuff, do drugs, drive while drunk, and \r\nwear dirty underwear on occasion.<br>\r\ni most certainly don\\\'t go around punching people though. i\\\'d \r\nprefer to slash their tires.<br>\r\nyour pal, creep.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(3152,952,'2001-05-22 01:47:50',0,'anonymous coward','touchy, touchy!',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3153,952,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','incitor. give me your birthday, time of birth, and location.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3154,957,'2001-05-22 04:33:28',0,'kat','<p>no belief. in dreams made of happiness. not that. \r\nwrithing pain. you\\\'ve got too much inside. mountains. like \r\nblindness. we all know. in our own way. our own worlds. \r\nthere is never too much to scream for. too much to leave. \r\nthe mirror looks both ways. its this that amazes. no savior \r\ncan bring the power. what you feel when you come \r\nthrough...on the other side.</p> ',4,0,0,0,'32.100.201.50','en'),(3155,956,'2001-05-22 14:49:03',0,'jo','can we have a moratorium on talking about censors slash \r\nbeing censored/edited? i think it was pretty well exhausted \r\nwith cyan\\\'s post and the comments. if you\\\'re pissed off \r\nabout it, get your own diaryland site where you can write \r\nwhatever you want.<BR>\r\nconstructive.',3,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3156,956,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','drunken meatster',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3157,952,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','exactly what kind of fighting is this?',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3158,957,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','ive heard the mirror looks both ways before. have you \r\neverlooked at the rear side of a mirror? its not a mirror. \r\nits just gray. gray like life. the brutal reality of life on \r\nthis planet of problems. like a good book with an unhappy \r\nending. everyone dies regretting something. does a bear?',6,0,0,0,'64.229.148.96','en'),(3159,960,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','talking always helps. why do you think i talk so much. ',1,0,0,0,'64.229.148.96','en'),(3160,952,'2001-05-23 02:19:47',0,'incitor to adamajah','it\\\'s a system similar to krav maga, SCARS, or SAFTA.  not a martial art, but a scientific approach to conflict.  my partner and i are building a business around it.  we\\\'ve based it mostly on my training in rapid escalation doctrine.',3,0,0,0,'207.205.217.167','en'),(3161,949,'2001-05-23 03:35:07',0,'Pandora','I suppose there truly is a society out there who\nunderstands.  Thanks. ;)',2,0,0,0,'66.66.213.181','en'),(3162,959,'2001-05-23 03:52:46',0,'Pandora','Sometimes an entire year can pass without you even knowing \r\nit. Time is the non-spatial continuum... All that means is \r\nthat time is negligible as long as you keep moving around. \r\nGood luck at the Exchange. ;)',2,0,0,0,'66.66.213.181','en'),(3163,961,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>everytime <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=942.txt\\\">my\r\nphone</a> rings i jump.</P>',3,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3164,956,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','howabout a moratorium on talking. at all.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3165,960,'2001-05-23 04:32:08',0,'sic','i like it when you journal :-p',0,0,0,0,'4.16.224.120','en'),(3166,963,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','wow kdog. that is fucking awesome. s5 and i have been \r\ntalking about doing vipassana for some time. it sounds \r\nintense. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3167,963,'2001-05-23 14:59:15',0,'mike','I think I could use some if that..',0,0,0,0,'206.224.71.188','en'),(3168,963,'2001-05-23 14:59:19',0,'mike','I think I could use some if that..',1,0,0,0,'206.224.71.188','en'),(3169,963,'2001-05-23 15:15:30',0,'bmg','<p>my latest struggle was; Do I go to Albuquerque for 11 \r\ndays of debauchery, or do I go to Vipassana Meditation \r\nCourse in Kaufman, TX. I chose debauchery. Yet, I think \r\nthat with my maturity at this point I would not be able to \r\nmake such a comitment that I have seen you make. Your \r\nintensity overwhelms me; and one day I would like to have \r\nthe same inensity... I remember as a boy looking at a \r\npicture of a US Marine and thinking that he must be the \r\ntoughest, strongest and sharpest human on earth. I thought \r\nthat if I did it I too would be just like that. I did not \r\nrealize that it would require such dedication and \r\nmotivation to maintain that edge. So I look at this course \r\nand I think, \\\"hmmm, 10 days! That isnt so bad, but could I \r\nstick with it for three more months 2 times a day?\\\" Is \r\nthere a moment when you know you are ready for this?',1,0,0,0,'209.189.128.78','en'),(3170,962,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','in the past 24 hours ive been having heated conversation with \r\nmyself, over and over, paradox and happy doubles. I tried \r\nlying to myself a few times to see what\\\'s true. I endorse \r\nthis exercise. this morning I pulled the coffee mugs down one \r\nby one and tossed each angrily into the sink. what are you \r\ndoing, asks jay. theyre dirty, look at all the dirt, the \r\nspots! the spots! they were in the dishwasher, it\\\'s clean \r\ndirt, he pleaded. this is gemini. i hate gemini.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'4.18.197.3','en'),(3171,962,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>lately i\\\'ve been trying to figure out why i\\\'ve always \r\nbeen obsessed with bob dylan.  yesterday\\\'s salon feature \r\nwas about him and his 40 year song writing fetish for \r\ndoubles/counterparts/dopplegangers/mirrors/GEMINIs as a \r\ntheme.  is bob a gemini?</p>      \r\n<p>egg, don\\\'t hate us.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3172,963,'2001-05-23 17:39:19',0,'adam','go k-dog :)',2,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3173,960,'2001-05-23 18:55:13',0,'MightyBluJ','I think the thing i most recall about the center were the \r\nbouncy rubber floors.  In any weather, rubber floors take \r\nthe edge off. ',1,0,0,0,'204.64.42.46','en'),(3174,962,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','he sho is.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.10.123','en'),(3175,963,'2001-05-23 19:55:33',0,'dog','Your transformations and evolutions consistently stupefy \r\nme. If nothing else, they make me recognize that I need to \r\ndig up buried doug. I love you.',1,0,0,0,'64.163.234.16','en'),(3176,962,'2001-05-23 20:12:23',0,'jo','he\\\'s a taurus-cusp-gemini. his 60th birthday is today; \r\nthat\\\'s the reason for the salon article.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3177,960,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<P>said doors can be seen at <a href=\"http://zlilo.com/~sammich/doors/\">the doors folder</a>\r\n</P><P>\r\nI have a bunch of lumber I probably wont use, if anyone has \r\nan idea for a clever coffee table or some such, you can pitch \r\nit to me and use our workshop if you want. <a href=\"mailto:egg@lotus.org\">egg@lotus.org</a>\r\n</P>',1,0,0,0,'4.18.197.3','en'),(3178,967,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i am in love with you, mirah, and the word \\\'mollified\\\'',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3179,962,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i think my favorite song in this world is \\\"visions of \r\njohanna.\\\" \r\n<p>\r\nand then my friend andy is a gemini and he is the most \r\nglamorous person i know. his life is like a blues song.\r\n<p>\r\nthe problem with geminis is they are duplicitous pricks but \r\nyou have to be enchanted and impressed with them anyway.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'4.18.197.3','en'),(3180,962,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','andy is a gemini? he stole my weed that one time.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni like \\\'visions of johanna\\\' a lot too. it was a high school \r\ncry-to-it for me.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3181,960,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to MightyBluJ','<P>is this the mohawked mighty blu j?  house of commons nude\r\nswimmer?  millitant bicyclist?   used to work at apple\r\ncomputers in austin?  has a radio show?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>nice, welcome aboard.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>ps: those were made of shredded car tires.</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3182,967,'2001-05-23 22:11:32',0,'Lunesse','I had lucky charms for breakfast this morning.<p>\r\nBut the two days before I had healthy cereal!<p>\r\nurp<p>\r\nLunesse',6,0,0,0,'209.213.209.50','en'),(3183,967,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','the problem with kittens is that they turn into cats. the\r\nproblem with new lucky charms is that they turn into old\r\nones. but you already know that part.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3184,968,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','something something.. \\\"look for the worst in people and you \r\nwill surely find it...\\\" something abraham lincoln.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(3185,953,'2001-05-24 03:43:44',0,'keuch2','ha, great.  I\\\'ve had the same kind of experience many times.',3,0,0,0,'129.130.229.87','en'),(3186,966,'2001-05-24 03:48:44',0,'keuch2','good... what\\\'s that picture of?  unfortunately, valencia \r\nhas lost to bayern munchen already, thru an agonizing \r\npenalty kick definition...',2,0,0,0,'129.130.229.87','en'),(3187,966,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','the picture is of me(brown) taking pics of the fascist riot \r\npolice in quebec city for the ftaa rally. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.140.228','en'),(3188,963,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i dont understand this stuff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"seeing things as they are\"?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>\"rediscovered ... 2500 years ago\"?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>where did he rediscover it from, and how long had  it \r\nbeen lost?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i dont understand this stuff.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>simple paradise: bed, fruit, tickles.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>simple paradise: the most perfect black bean taco made \r\nby me.</P>\r\n<P>i sat on a park bench for five hours last week.</P>\r\n<BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n i spent an\r\nentire day sitting in the lobby of the westin because no one ever bothers\r\nyou if you sit in hotel lobbys.  they just assume you are waiting for\r\nsomeone.  there were a lot of people wandering through.  some of the knew\r\neachother. --<a href=\"http://logy.org/~xep/cracklywords/\">Xyzyx Lerpy</a></BLOCKQUOTE>\r\n<P>i think i am <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.cix.co.uk/~antcom/mtl.html\">caught in a \r\nmobius loop</a>.</P>',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3189,967,'2001-05-24 05:51:41',0,'SSJ3 Gostu','that is by far the best non book thing i have read in a \r\nvery long time.',1,0,0,0,'208.59.199.233','en'),(3190,966,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','your posts are so much easier to read with paragraph breaks!',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3191,968,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<p>Dude.</p>\r\n<p>You\\\'re not \\\'right.\\\'<br><br>At all.<br><br>You\\\'re a \r\nselfish, judgmental, disgusting asshole.</p>\r\n<p>Through several posts here, you\\\'ve glorified in detail \r\nhow poorly you have treated people who were once your \r\nfriends but who have slighted you in some way.<br><br>That \r\nmakes you look really bad.</p>\r\n<p>You need to learn compassion and caring for people.</p>\r\n<p>Just a thought.</p>',6,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(3192,968,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>hm...  can i pay you five dollars to tell me what you \r\nthink of me?  we have to meet in person though.  five \r\nminutes.  like a palm reader, or astrologist.  i want you \r\nto use your methods and keep your verdict under five \r\nwords.</P>\r\n<P>five dollars, five minutes, five words.</P>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3193,966,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hear, hear! a resounding vote for paragraphs!',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3194,967,'2001-05-24 13:28:15',0,'jo','quiet awe.',1,0,0,0,'205.188.200.213','en'),(3195,968,'2001-05-24 14:05:38',0,'mike','Thats a great idea Austin',1,0,0,0,'206.224.71.188','en'),(3196,968,'2001-05-24 14:49:25',0,'romano','Who made all of you the karma police? Aren\\\'t you afraid of \r\nbeing thought \\\"judgmental\\\" \\\"asshole\\\"s yourselves?',1,0,0,0,'209.4.238.10','en'),(3197,968,'2001-05-24 15:00:01',0,'jo','i\\\'ve had several friends who\\\'ve used \\\"looking out for me\\\" \r\nor \\\"knowing what\\\'s best for me\\\" as an excuse for \\\"being a \r\nbig fucking dick.\\\" ',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3198,969,'2001-05-24 15:02:07',0,'jo','(hi is anyone else excited about waking up this morning to \r\nall this new good stuff at swinney.org? i know i am.)<BR>\r\nhi steve! thanks for sharing this with us.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3199,962,'2001-05-24 15:07:31',0,'jo','my favorite dylan song is \\\"visions of johanna,\\\" too, but i \r\nthink there is an obvious reason for that.<BR>\r\ni sure do like \\\"tangled up in blue\\\" an awful lot.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3200,962,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','Im still obsessed with gemini today. arent there something \r\nlike 3 planets in gemini right now? is this why I am a chat \r\naddict agin? I hate myself.\r\n<p>\r\nle: yes andy is a gemini, his birthday is coming up. I think \r\nhe has stolen weed from everyone I know.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'4.18.197.3','en'),(3201,968,'2001-05-24 18:54:18',0,'alanna','<TT>\r\n<P>\r\nlet me make a promise: \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nweeding out the horrible people that somehow get in your \r\nlife and every now and then including someone who actually \r\nhas potential is *much*, much better than letting everyone \r\nin and wasting so much time and tears on the assholes you \r\nnever could see as assholes until they screw you over and \r\nleave, all the while not having the time to let good people \r\nin because the bad people are crowding the room.\r\n</P>\r\n</TT>',3,0,0,0,'152.163.201.142','en'),(3202,968,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>so is it safe to say that we have strains of philosophy\r\ngoing here?</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i am really interested to know what they are.  how to\r\nfind the good friends.  i am always judging everything\r\naround me.  i do make allowances though.  and i am\r\ninterested to know what is inside them all. there might be\r\nan occasional person i am scared to know and i stay away\r\nfrom them.  but on the whole i am pretty interested to talk\r\nto people despite what others say about them.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>lately maybe this is not the case so much.  i hear so\r\nmuch information on everyone before i meet them.  i wonder\r\nwhat is said about me often.  i will never know though\r\nunless i believe what people tell me.  i distrust any\r\npostitive comments about me.  i fish for insults from my\r\nfriends to show me that they are telling the truth.  i think\r\nthey must not like some things about me.  but we would still\r\nbe friends anyway.  i dont like eveything about anyone. \r\nthere is always something but it doesnt bother me.  it makes\r\nthem a more full character.  gives me more to think about\r\nthem and wonder.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and then there is wondering how people are getting\r\nalong.  they dont get along because of differences that\r\ncause friction.  we argue in our actions, not words, then we\r\ncomplain about each other and give each other advice on the\r\nothers.  our days are spent trying to be warmer and fuzzier\r\ntogether despite the diversity and differences.  i have\r\nbegun to stack these people up.  all these strange problem\r\ncausing beautiful people. i know who is made happier by\r\nwhat.  some people like treats.  some like hugs.  some like\r\nthe reassuring words -- \\\"i like you.\\\"  it is figuring people\r\nout that i enjoy more than computer programming or food.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i could just roll over and die otherwise and it would not\r\nmatter much.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>darwin, speak please.</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3203,968,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>austin, sometimes i try to imagine what you think of \r\nme.  have you figured me out?  you\\\'ve known me for a long \r\ntime, through several stages, and you\\\'ve even lived with \r\nthe fam.  if i were you, i would think of me:</p>\r\n<p>austin.o0O(adam\\\'s kind of a push-over when it comes to \r\nwomen, but he\\\'s good natured.  he\\\'s not gifted with words, \r\nand he can be boring sometimes, but he\\\'s creative.  why \r\ncan\\\'t he be more creative in instigating social \r\nactivities?!  that boy\\\'s got his shit together, but why is \r\nhe making video games?  he needs to take more risks.  hmm i \r\nwant doughnuts...)</p>\r\n<p>ok, this is getting weird.  it\\\'s not feeling like a very \r\nhealthy activity to guess what your friends think of you.  \r\nactually, friends are much more interesting the more \r\nillusive their character is.  i hope no one feels like \r\nthey\\\'ve figured me out.</p>           ',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3204,970,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\noh dear. how long do you have to stay?<br>\r\nwhat state/town are you in? perhaps, i can think of something \r\nfun to do nearby. i\\\'ve traveled lots of backroads through \r\nmost states at one time or another.<br>\r\nblah. vacationing with family. especially when it\\\'s not even \r\nreally yours.</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(3205,964,'2001-05-24 22:54:16',0,'keuch2','this is my first posting ever.  I thought the \\\'title\\\' space \r\nwas for the title of my website.  well...\r\n</br>\r\nthe actual title for this is <b>\\\"Help! I\\\'m an Accordion \r\nTrapped in a Man\\\'s Body\\\"</b>.  Pretty crappy, huh?',1,0,0,0,'129.130.231.195','en'),(3206,971,'2001-05-24 23:26:37',0,'mojo23','Let\\\'s hope the next society builds its stuff out of the \r\nwaste we left behind.  \r\nI envision large moving markets of waste from the last \r\nhundred years, piled high, people auctioning off the best \r\nof it, for use in their construction projects.  \r\nFences will be made of old bed frames and shower doors, \r\nhouses from styrofoam cups and take-out boxes, moving \r\nvehicles from wooden apple crates and old computer towers.',3,0,0,0,'158.252.210.59','en'),(3207,968,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I do a lot of thinking about this subject. Like I\\\'ve \r\nmentioned before, my reaction to being hurt by people has \r\nbeen the cultivation of a serious bitterness. The thing I \r\nforgot to mention is that despite myself, I continue to \r\nbelieve the best of people. It\\\'s intricately related to my \r\n<b>need</b>. For example, if I <b>need</b> someone because \r\nof the way that make me feel, I\\\'ll be much more likely to \r\nmake exceptions for their behavior. I\\\'m not too happy about \r\nthis, but at least I\\\'m somewhat aware of it. Of course, the \r\nother people who I believe the best of are people who I \r\nreally like.</p>\r\n<p>As time progresses, it seems like the primary criteria \r\nthat I select people on is self-awareness and awareness of \r\nthe needs of others. I\\\'m developing a model of human \r\nsociety which is something like : \\\"99% of a person\\\'s \r\nbehavior is dictated by primal needs.\\\" I <b>know</b> that \r\nthis applies to me too. From my observations, it seems to \r\napply to other people to varying degrees. What I cannot \r\nhandle is when people around me don\\\'t mind hurting others \r\nto make themselves feel better. I also can\\\'t handle people \r\nhurting themselves to make themselves feel better or \r\nunrepentant compulsive behavior (constant drug \r\nuse/drinking/sexual validation) or people who don\\\'t listen \r\nor pay attention.</p>\r\n<p>The big problem I can\\\'t reconcile is this :</p>\r\n<p><b>I can\\\'t deal with people who still have 1 or more \r\nproblems that I don\\\'t have anymore.</b></p>\r\n<p>If I\\\'ve figured something out (like, for example, that \r\ncheating on your S.O. is a totally pointless exercise that \r\nwill hurt way more than it will feel good), then I \r\nabsolutly cannot deal with people who haven\\\'t figured this \r\nout. I judge them very harshly, especially if they are \r\nolder than me. This is in part due to getting shit because \r\nI\\\'ve been the youngest in a community and people being real \r\ndicks and saying \\\"you\\\'ll understand when you\\\'re older\\\" at \r\nthe same time as they\\\'re making a complete and total mess \r\nof their lives.</p>\r\n<p>There are cases in which I can deal with people who fit \r\nthis description.. if they somehow communicate to me that \r\nthey are aware of their failings and sincere about their \r\npersonal growth. For example, I know more than one person \r\nwho is seriously disturbed, but who I still care about and \r\ntalk to and etc, because I feel they are <b>really \r\ntrying</b> to make change in their life. It\\\'s very hip to \r\nbe into personal growth and exploration (esp. in \r\nCalifornia) but it\\\'s not very hip to admit that you\\\'re not \r\nperfect. It seems that some people get the message that \r\ntheir failings are \\\"charming\\\" or \\\"just part of my \r\npersonality\\\".. and proceed to get really loud about them. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>This of course implies that there is a fixed continuum \r\nof what makes a person \\\"good\\\" or \\\"bad.\\\" I think that each \r\nperson has a set of standards, no matter how \\\"openminded\\\" \r\nthey claim to be. My standards are higher than others, but \r\nif you pass them (whether because of my need or yr value) I \r\nleave myself wide open. This means that the people who get \r\npast the first line and turn out to only be really good \r\nliars increase my bitterness towards humanity in general. \r\nNobody likes being proved wrong. Like Alanna said above, is \r\nit worth being wrong about 10 people to find 1 person who \r\nis what they appear to be?</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m sorry that this is so rambling and poorly organized. \r\nI probably should have written an outline. What I will say \r\nis that despite my failings, I have gotten a lot of things \r\nright. I can do productive things, I can be nice to people \r\nI care about, I have made significant personal change. This \r\nis more than I can say for a lot of other people around me. \r\nI figure this a big reason why I only feel really close to \r\n5 or 6 people.</p>\r\n<p>These people, for reference (in no particular \r\norder) :</p>\r\n<ol><li>incitor aka davey</li>\r\n<li>agthorr</li>\r\n<li><a href=http://www.cyantology.com>cyan</a></li>\r\n<li>dawn</li>\r\n<li>dylan</li>\r\n<li><a href=http://www.kobek.com>jarett</a></li></ol>\r\n<p>I\\\'ve known most of the people on this list for 6 or more \r\nyears. There\\\'s some way that the length of time you know \r\nsomeone/amount of time you spend with them figures into \r\nthis, but I\\\'m not quite sure how. It\\\'s worth noting that I \r\nhaven\\\'t seen many of these people in person for a long time \r\n(incitor: 2 years, jarett: 1 1/2 years, agthorr: 1 year) \r\nbut they\\\'re still my friends. I feel like the people on \r\nthis list recognize me for who I am, and they <u>like</u> \r\nme for it. Even when I was much more fucked up, they \r\nrecognized that I was trying <b>really really really \r\nhard</b> to be a nice, decent, considerate, well-rounded \r\nperson. Maybe more people appreciate me than I think.. it \r\ncertainly wouldn\\\'t be the first time I was paranoid. Maybe \r\nI\\\'m not good at recognizing that people are sincerely \r\ntrying to be nice people, so I judge them too harshly.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t think I have anything else to say right now.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',3,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3208,971,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>A few years ago my brother had a plastic mug from the \r\nUniversity of Pennsylvania bookshop that said \\\"Reduce, \r\nReuse, Recycle\\\" on it. I didn\\\'t think about it much then, \r\nbut I\\\'ve kept it in mind now. I recycle everything that my \r\nmunicipality accepts, and often pick up recycleable litter. \r\nI make sure not to take a plastic bag at the store if I \r\ndon\\\'t need one. I find ways of turning used things into new \r\nthings, like my soap holder, which is made out of a juice \r\nbottle.</p>\r\n<p>Before the power crisis, I didn\\\'t think much about power \r\nconsumption. Now, I try to make sure the major appliances \r\nare set to a semi-responsible level, that windows are \r\nclosed when the heat is on, that lights and monitors are \r\noff, etc. I make an effort to shut off my stereo and other \r\nthings in my room if I will be out for more than an hour or \r\ntwo.</p>\r\n<p>The unfortunate fact of the matter is that you don\\\'t get \r\nsomething for nothing. The price of our modern society, \r\ngood general health, long life expectancy, neat toys, etc. \r\nis lots and lots and lots of waste.</p>\r\n<p>I find keeping \\\"reduce, reuse, recycle\\\" in my head helps \r\nme be as responsible as I can, within the boundaries I\\\'ve \r\nestablished for my life.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',3,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3209,972,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','what a charming story! proving once and for all, that \r\nwithin everyone is the desire to get away with \r\nsomething naughty; and if not me, i\\\'m satisfied enough \r\nto live that experience through you.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3210,971,'2001-05-25 01:49:54',0,'s5 to mojo23','mad magazine ran an article, probably 10 or 15 years \r\nago, about exactly that. if i ever come across the issue, \r\ni\\\'ll scan it in. they even outlined a method of \r\ncompressing organic waste (graphically depicted, of \r\ncourse) into sealed blocks, suitable for contruction.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3211,971,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','my friend morgan always said that manufacturers should take \r\nresponsibilty for the waste packaging that comes with your \r\naverage everyday product.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nfor instance:  a box of macaroni and cheese.  the packaging \r\nis a plastic bag inside a box.  it would be cool to be able \r\nto deposit the used box and bag at your supermarket, where \r\nkraft come pick up the used packaging and take it to their \r\nplant, where they recycle it.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni think the standard response morgan gets when he talks \r\nabout this idea is that \\\"it\\\'s too hard\\\". other things to \r\nthink about i guess are the fumes from the trucks that \r\nwould be picking up the waste, toxic fumes generated and \r\nlet loose on the atmosphere by the recycling process, the \r\nseemingly insurmountable task of getting companies to \r\nrealise that extra packaging is just a form of advertising \r\nwhich is not only unnecessary but also adding to our waste \r\nproblems.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni think they already have something like this in germany.  \r\nthose germans, they sure are smart.<br>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3212,962,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>speaking of gemini, i would like to trade something for \r\na horoscopey forecasty type thing of my year to come, \r\nstarting on my birthday until the next.</p>\r\n<p>can anyone do one of these for me?</p>\r\n<p>what i can trade:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>code</li>\r\n<li>a box full of random oddities</li>\r\n<li>time equivalent to that of getting together the \r\nforecast put to a physical or mental task of your \r\nchoice</li>\r\n<li>vegan chocolate cake with chocolate chips</li>\r\n</ul></p>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3213,975,'2001-05-25 02:44:33',0,'bmg','I like you; And I wish I could caress words like you.',2,0,0,0,'209.189.128.78','en'),(3214,975,'2001-05-25 03:20:03',0,'dawn','re: friends not needing to share the same beliefs..\r\n<p>\r\na bit ago i was friends with a skinhead. she wasn\\\'t a nice \r\nnon-nazi skin. she was wholeheartedly dedicated to her \r\nbeliefs. somehow, we ended up being pretty good friends. i \r\nthink we both ignored the fact that she was supposed to hate \r\nme.\r\n<p>\r\ni introduced her to incitor, and she really liked him. she \r\neven thought he was cute. later when i told her he was \r\njewish she was appalled. (she said \\\"I can\\\'t believe i looked \r\nat a jew\\\'s butt,\\\" which was rather amusing).\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'s too bad that someone i enjoyed spending time with, and \r\neven thought to be intelligent and kind, wasn\\\'t able to \r\nenjoy and pursue the friendships she honestly wanted to. but \r\nat least i had fun trying to show her she was wrong.\r\n<p>\r\nanyway, point being there are worse things than turning away \r\npeople who you feel an immediate negative response to. \r\nusually gut feelings <i>are</i> right. but, you certainly \r\nare more likely to miss a few good friends that way.',2,0,0,0,'206.97.109.89','en'),(3215,975,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','i always grin when i remember that.  what a great night.  ',1,0,0,0,'207.205.217.227','en'),(3216,971,'2001-05-25 04:01:04',0,'sawx','assorted brain farts, sorry for the lack of cohesion:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy favorite recycling story is about how so many plastic \r\nsoft drink bottles are consumed in australia, and the \r\nprocess of recycling these so energy ineffecient - that the \r\nmost environmentally friendly solution is to put them in \r\nall in boat to indonesia where they are burnt on the \r\nbeach....\r\ni don\'t know if its true or not, but if it is, that\'s \r\npretty fucked up\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\npersonally i think my recycling karma isn\'t too shabby, \r\ni\'ve never owned a car, take public or human powered \r\ntransport whenever possible, and i generally refuse to deal \r\nwith printed documents at work\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmorgan is a nice hippie\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\noh yeah, when i was a kid and there was a big power \r\nshortage in auckland (not the big famous one a few years \r\nago when the cables broke, the one before that) i wrote and \r\nillustrated a comic book about a guy who went around \r\nkilling fat rich people during blackouts, i did most of the \r\nwork during physics and english class at high school\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy 5th form physics teacher looked like papa smurf',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3217,971,'2001-05-25 04:02:22',0,'sawx','<br><br>\r\noh fuck, i forgot my line breaks too - sorry<br><br>\r\nlunch now :)',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3218,971,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','I don\\\'t know. I kind of like garbage.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3219,971,'2001-05-25 06:08:57',0,'jo','also it\\\'s worthwhile to mention that some solutions to \r\nenvironmental problems create new ones. dartmouth, for \r\ninstance, requires that every student have a computer, and \r\nstudents and faculty rely primarily on email for \r\ncommunication. this has saved <b>drastically</b> on their \r\npaper waste. however, as a result of all these computers, \r\ntheir energy use is now through the roof.',1,0,0,0,'205.188.200.213','en'),(3220,969,'2001-05-25 06:44:45',0,'cyantist','wow.  that post makes me speachless.',1,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3221,976,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but where are the paragraph tags?  this could be a work of \r\ngenius, but i just can\\\'t read it this way.  picture is \r\npretty, though.',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3222,977,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','a face only  a mother could love.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3223,977,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i think it is important to remember that you can treat\r\nyourself to the tendancies of something without actually\r\nbeing that thing.  you can eat in a vegan fashion without\r\nbeing vegan.  there is no need to label yourself as such.\r\ndon\\\'t not stick to something just to avoid the absolute.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3224,976,'2001-05-25 08:00:28',0,'jp','yo chia, I hear what you\\\'re saying.<BR>\r\nAll I have to say is this:<BR>\r\nHere\\\'s just the feeling I\\\'m getting. I don\\\'t even know where\r\nit comes from, but I think unexpectedly good things will be\r\nhappening for you. More like internal things though. I think\r\nyou\\\'re making progress inside and things are going to open\r\nup and get better for you. It\\\'s just a hunch.<BR>\r\nThink about this: if your goal is inner directed and not\r\nmaterial then the goal, or state, you\\\'re seeking is\r\nattainable within yourself.<BR>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3225,975,'2001-05-25 08:47:59',0,'jp','On judgement:<BR>\r\nI\\\'ve taken that personality test that measures four\r\ndualities of your personality -- introverted-extroverted;\r\nintuitive-sensing; thinking-feeling; perceiving-judging;<BR>\r\nSome traits have varied at different time I\\\'ve taken it but\r\nthe perceptive as opposed to judgemental rating is always\r\nconsistent.<BR>\r\nI have no \\\"philosophy\\\" on being judgemental or not, but the\r\nreal problem with being judgemental is that it would tend to\r\nclose off experience. I think being \\\"judgemental\\\" is only a\r\nproblem when it makes someone narrow-minded.<BR>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3226,977,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i agree with xep on this one. i say i am vegan because it\\\'s \r\nthe quickest way to explain a lifestyle, even an aspirant \r\nlifestyle. you say \\\"i am vegan\\\" as shorthand for \\\"i\\\'m \r\ntrying to cause less suffering and be more mindful.\\\" but i \r\nam not a food nazi. if i crave something badly i eat it. \r\nit\\\'s not about extremism or denying oneself - i think the \r\nstress of a fanatical lifestyle is possibly worse for you \r\nthan the damage done by eating a burger for every meal.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3227,973,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','no one wanted to activate this. it languished. i think we \r\nwere all disturbed by the \\\"9 INCH POOP CHUTE\\\" keywords. but \r\nsince it is the first thing you ever submitted anywhere \r\never.... well. i like the part about life being about \r\nspitting things into napkins. good luck.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3228,977,'2001-05-25 11:03:59',0,'jo','i was really sick earlier this week and ate campbell\\\'s \r\nchicken noodle soup and my roommate offered to not eat meat \r\nthat night to make up for it. i thought that was nice.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3229,975,'2001-05-25 11:06:35',0,'jo','judgmental. no \\\"e.\\\" judgmental.<BR>\r\nsorry. it was starting to irk at me.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3230,978,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','thank you',1,0,0,0,'64.24.141.167','en'),(3231,972,'2001-05-25 15:00:47',0,'mojo','s5: I don\\\'t believe for a nanosecond that it would suffice\nyou to live through other people\\\'s naughty pleasures.\n\nYou may not shoplift, but I *know* you have your vices...fat\nvegan challenge, anyone??',4,0,0,0,'158.252.217.160','en'),(3232,977,'2001-05-25 15:04:50',0,'erin','yay (!) for moms who smile easy to apologies. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'129.105.107.184','en'),(3233,978,'2001-05-25 15:13:24',0,'mojo23','This was very inspiring.  I am one of those people who often\nwhines about the vegan evangelists around me.  It is a sick\nlittle pleasure I am ready to shrug off.\n\nGo girl.   You look beautiful in your new skin, and I hope\nyou continue to allow your spirituality guide your health.\nClearly it is working for you.',1,0,0,0,'158.252.217.160','en'),(3234,978,'2001-05-25 15:30:28',0,'hedwig','<p>i can\\\'t stand those diet sites.  last year i signed up \r\nfor one and lasted a total of 5 days.  like you once were, \r\ni\\\'m not \\\"fat\\\", but i am chubby... and i\\\'ve gone through a \r\nvariety of diets and exercises.  i always wind up quiting \r\nfeeling a little more self-defeated... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>right now i\\\'m kinda at the point where it just doesn\\\'t \r\nmatter... that perhaps this is the way i\\\'m supposed to \r\nbe... but i\\\'m not happy with it... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve often thought of going vegetarian... it\\\'s not too \r\nfar off from where i am now... i don\\\'t like fast food but i \r\nhave a voracious sweet tooth...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i want to avoid corporate chains like the diet websites \r\nand exercise clubs... it feels so fake... i just want to \r\ntreat my body well so that my life\\\'s experiences don\\\'t end \r\nprematurely...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have digested your viewpoint... and am inspired by \r\nit... i don\\\'t want to approach another diet with the \r\nobjective to lose weight... i want to be comfortable in my \r\nown skin... i want to be healthy... i wonder what that \r\nfeels like... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i found nothing preachy about your article.. </p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'198.92.137.130','en'),(3235,978,'2001-05-25 15:31:03',0,'jo','hey els would you consider posting the fat vegan email?',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3236,973,'2001-05-25 16:16:10',0,'asutin','<P>i fixed the shoddy html on this one.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>come to think of it, no one has touched on that topic\r\n<B>re: censorship sucks</B>.  a good lot of you dont know\r\nhtml.  and it is the blessed censors that mark this shit\r\nup!</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3237,978,'2001-05-25 16:23:02',0,'yokito','<P>mew fat.  mew fat. mew.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>mew wet cat food.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>mew.</P>',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3238,978,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','that was really nice.',2,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(3239,973,'2001-05-25 17:12:23',0,'jo','good point, asutin.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(3240,978,'2001-05-25 17:24:53',0,'I can change, I swear','A few points from the peanut gallery, fatso: \r\n\r\n(1) 200 pounds + 5\\\' 9\\\" equates to the grand sum of fat. I \r\ndon\\\'t care if you wanna be self delusional but don\\\'t \r\ninflict your interior fantasy self on the rest of the \r\nworld. Namely, me. \r\n\r\n(2) 5\\\'9\\\" + 150 pounds equates out to STILL A CHUB. I\\\'ve \r\nseen the photographic evidence on your long running \r\ndocumentary cam, sister. You\\\'ve got a pot belly that\\\'d make \r\ncertain South African pigs sweat with envy. If they could \r\nsweat. \r\n\r\n(3) None of this means anything, because, you could lose \r\n100 pounds and you\\\'d still be a painfully ugly individual \r\nspreading dismay to all the weary eyes in all of the world.\r\n \r\nSo Long and Thanks for All the Fish, \r\nDouglas Adams 1952-2001 (RIP)',1,0,0,1,'216.165.3.96','en'),(3242,973,'2001-05-25 18:47:24',0,'narcoleptic','that wasn\\\'t shoddy html, you buffoon, the extraneous / \r\ndenoted quoted verse\r\n\r\noh well\r\n\r\nbright and young and gifted/ in my autobiography/\r\n\r\netc etc',4,0,0,0,'205.142.76.11','en'),(3243,978,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p><font size=2>\r\nnice cat dong.</font></p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3244,978,'2001-05-25 19:50:51',0,'yokito','mew mew rowr mew\nmew mew rar mew\ncatbaitmewrowwrr\npurrchomp\n',1,0,0,0,'63.109.213.59','en'),(3245,978,'2001-05-25 20:09:32',0,'keuch2','that\\\'s excellent.  I know very few people who have the will \r\npower to do that.',1,0,0,0,'129.130.231.191','en'),(3246,978,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Let me suggest vigorous exercise as a good way to whip \r\nyour body into shape. There are many different forms this \r\ncan take. For me, this means :</p>\r\n<p><ol><li>Riding my bike</li>\r\n<li>Dancing</li>\r\n<li>Training <a href=http://www.omulu.org>Capoeira</a></li>\r\n<li>Smooching</li>\r\n<li>Playing Frisbee</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n<p>\r\nAs Outkast says :<p>\r\n<pre>\r\n\\\"Get up, Get out and Do Something /\r\n Don\\\'t let the days of your life pass you by /\r\n Get up, Get out and Do Something / \r\n Don\\\'t spend _all_ your time trying to get high\\\"</pre>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin     ',2,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3247,971,'2001-05-25 20:36:34',0,'darwin to o','<p>The exact scheme you described for making manufacturers \r\nresponsible for packaging waste is in place in Austria. It \r\nworks very well for them, and the packaging shrinks as a \r\nresult.</p>\r\n<p>Simple idea, but too complicated for Americans \r\nprobably.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3248,976,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','paragraph tags! WE DONT NEED NO STINKIN TAGS! ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.135.101','en'),(3249,978,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','wonderful.  i love to read your writing, and this one is now a favorite.  for myself i actually do write down everything i eat.  i research most every meal i am to consume.  however, i do this because i am trying to gain lean weight, while losing fatty tissue.  most days i eat over 4000 calories of food.  my daily minimum protein requirement is 260 grams.  i avoid carbohydrates as much as possible excepting before and after a heavy workout or fight practice.  i drink 6 - 7 liters of water a day.  i eat meat, alot of it.  mostly fish these days (those essential fatty acids!) however, where as i once considered fruits and vegetables a last resort, they now provide 60-70% of my diet.  i sometimes wonder if i could be happy without meat.  even more often i wonder how i could gain the needed protein without meat.  your writing here makes me think tonight might be a good time to do the research on this.',2,0,0,0,'207.205.220.168','en'),(3250,975,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>dictionary.com says : \\\"judgÂ·ment also judgeÂ·ment \r\n(jjmnt).\\\"</p>\r\n<p>At least according to them, \\\"judgement\\\" is an accepted \r\nvariant of \\\"judgment\\\".</p>\r\n<p>altavista search results :</p>\r\n<br>word count:<br>\r\njudgement: 950202<br>\r\njudgment: 3460065<br>\r\n<p>Either that\\\'s a whole lot of misspellings or people use \r\nboth of the variants..</p>\r\n<p><a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.lineone.net/dictionaryof/englishusage/d0082\r\n319.html\\\">This webpage</a> says either spelling is \r\nvalid.</p>\r\n<p><a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/doctor/faq/j/judgment.html\\\">\r\nThis one</a> says that \\\"judgment\\\" is the preferred \r\nspelling.</p>\r\n<p><a href=\\\"http://w2.xrefer.com/entry/300720\\\">This \r\npage</a> suggest that \\\"judgement\\\" is the British spelling, \r\nversus \\\"judgment\\\" as the American spelling.</p>\r\n<p><a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.knuten.liu.se/~bjoch509/works/kant/cr_judge\r\nment.txt\\\">Last but not least</a>, Immanuel Kant (as \r\ntranslated by a British Guy) feels it\\\'s \\\"The Critique of \r\nJudgement.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin<br>\r\nUnemployed.\r\n',3,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(3251,978,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to incitor','i know some really buff vegans. it can be done. protein \r\nisn\\\'t really the problem, everything has so much protein. i \r\nthink the issue is more iron and amino acids. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3252,978,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le for jo','i don\\\'t really want to post the fat vegan email because it \r\nhas some trade secrets about my job as well as some trade \r\nsecrets about menu plans for fat vegans. i will say, \r\nhowever, that i watched steve mix sugar and olive oil and \r\ndrink it last night. \r\n',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3253,978,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to everyone','thanks guys. i like you.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3254,978,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','hi.  my name is xep and i\\\'m a buff vegan.  i just ate an\r\nentire bean and rice burrito in about 7 minutes.  protein is\r\neasy.  hail seitan.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3255,978,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Pong Moll','I like beans.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3256,978,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','you guys are funny. i like yous.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3257,973,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Proto Moll','KNOW I NOT THE HTML\r\nAM OKAY????\r\nGOODNESS\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3258,981,'2001-05-26 01:34:05',0,'narcoleptic','<PRE>causality is overrated</PRE>',4,0,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(3259,982,'2001-05-26 03:11:53',0,'photos y requerdos','<BR>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.candyraver.com/satori/images/oc5.jpg\\\"\r\nalt=\\\"cody cakes!\\\">',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3260,975,'2001-05-26 03:30:25',0,'jo','jesus. i apologize.<BR>\r\nalthough i guess this is one of those words where both \r\nspellings are now considered correct because 2/3 of people \r\nspell it incorrectly, because when i was in school and had \r\ngrammar and spelling nazis for teachers (god bless them. i \r\nhated them then but OH it was so worth it), \\\"judgmental\\\" \r\nwas the only correct spelling.',1,0,0,0,'152.163.194.137','en'),(3261,973,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','whatever butt much, there is no such thing as an <B>R</B>\r\ntag.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3262,975,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i am not feeling this darwin, it is JUDGEMENT.  no more fat\r\namerican language!',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3263,980,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','today driving down fell street a car tried to merge into me\r\non my motorcycle.  i tried to hit my horn but the little\r\ncontrol thinger on my handlebar was loose and had slipped\r\ndownward so i ended up hitting the turn signal.  the car\r\ncontinued to merge and i became a deer in headlights. \r\nluckily they saw me at the last minute and swerved back into\r\ntheir lane.  it made me kind of cranky.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3264,975,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','who bloody well cares in the end, how judgement is properly spelled.  the key is that everyone understood!  jo, i liked that you whacked me on that.  i\\\'m too often lazy about checking my spelling when i type.  however, thanks to many british and irish friends, i\\\'ve taken up at time both their spelling and speach patterns. :)',1,0,0,0,'207.205.218.10','en'),(3265,983,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nwell im back. back from yet another jaunt to the pub. not \r\nhte same one. i dont want to be cut off. i went to the \r\nmayflowerII and ordered a shot of canadian whiskey and a \r\npint of sleeman cream ale. i read the economist. three \r\narticles. one on dubya\\\'s evangelical crusade, one on chavez \r\nin ven, and one on cuba and its loan repayments. the \r\nbartender asked me if i had had a rough night, explaining \r\nthat ordering two drinks usually meant that. i answered with \r\n\\\"no no, i only want to make sure i have enough money...\\\". he \r\nbeleived it and i beleived it for about a split second. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat must i do to releive the pain of lonliness? what must i \r\ndo to curtail my need for some sort of substance? give me \r\nadvice. yell at me. its more then most do around here. god i \r\n just want to take a nice cold bath. god i want to kick a \r\nchair from under my feet. but i dont. i dont because ive \r\nsince learned that killing one self is harder then anything. \r\nits harder and easier all at once...yet ive chosen the worst \r\npossible methods in the past and for that ive learned that \r\nsuicide is harmless to oneself yet disastrous to the ones \r\nwho actually do care. \r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\ni hate my world. \r\n</p>',6,0,0,0,'64.229.135.101','en'),(3266,973,'2001-05-26 04:46:39',0,'keuch2','when I read this I thought they could be good lyrics for \r\nsongs.\r\n<p>\r\nchorus:\r\n</br>\r\nSUBJECT TO VETO\r\n</br>\r\nSUBJECT TO VETO',3,0,0,0,'129.130.229.234','en'),(3267,982,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','are you that guy from the real world?',0,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3268,982,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','what\\\'s a woob? and how can i get some mail ordered to casa \r\nweeb?',2,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3269,983,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','There is nothing to say to something like this. Not because \r\nof indifference but because of a realization that nothing \r\nsaid could be meaningful. Anything more would be \r\nsuperfluous. I give you my textual equivelant of a nod.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3270,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','perhaps confusing is the wrong word.  maybe, deceptive.',3,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3271,985,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i took that five years ago and was INTJ:</P>\r\n<OL>\r\n<LI>Introverted\r\n<LI>iNtuitive\r\n<LI>Thinking\r\n<LI>Judgemental\r\n</OL>\r\n<P>i try to be the opposite of those now.  I try to be all\r\nfun and nice.  I try to mind my own business.  I try not to\r\nthink about things.  I try not to be judegemental.</P>\r\n<P>i trying has helped lessen the severity over the years to\r\ntolerable levels.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>wait, yours is a different test than the meyers-briggs.',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3272,985,'2001-05-26 07:32:36',0,'sawx','<br>\r\nsounds a lot like you to me, o<br><br>\r\ni got a 5, which i am not too sure about at all....',1,0,0,0,'144.132.7.52','en'),(3273,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i\\\'m an ENFP.',3,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3274,986,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','my name is jo and i had friends who decided to call me oj. \r\nthat then turned into them calling me ojie for a whole \r\nyear. i think they were kinda cute.',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(3275,985,'2001-05-26 09:43:54',0,'jp','I find it really easy to become totally obsessed with these\r\nkinds of personality sorters, astrology and I-ching also.\r\n<BR>\r\nI took the enneagram test and came out as a 2, with 4 and 5\r\ncoming tied close behind.<BR>\r\nFor the Meyers test it\\\'s varied from ENTP, INFP to ISNP most\r\nlately.<BR><BR>\r\nOf course, none of these categories are accurate. Partially,\r\nin a rough sense they might get close but overall they\\\'re a\r\nself-fulfilling distortion. <BR>\r\nSometimes there have been people that I happened to think\r\nwere a certain astrological sign. Everything about that\r\nperson I\\\'ll see through the lens of \\\"oh they\\\'re a capricorn\r\nor whatever. I  can tell...\\\". But then I\\\'ll realize that the\r\nsign was something else. This is happened more than once.\r\nThen I\\\'ll have this moment of disbelief and confusion, and\r\nI\\\'ll doubt astrology entirely. I\\\'ll think for a minute,\r\n\\\"I\\\'m  an idiot to believe in that crap.\\\" <BR>\r\nBut then, I\\\'ll reinterpret all previous reactions in my\r\nmemory and put it through the new filter of the corrected\r\nsign. Then after a while the filter, or label, I put on the\r\nperson slowly changes.<BR>\r\nFunny thing is, I still believe in it, with something of a\r\ngrain of salt though. Only because I\\\'ve found that the more\r\nstrongly a person has a belief the more likely it is to be\r\ntotally wrong.<BR>\r\nThe most interesting thing I noticed about those kinds of\r\nthings is how they decide to categorize temparament,\r\nmotivation, and behaviour, etc. and the explanation they\r\ngive to relate it all together.<BR>',0,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3276,986,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','oh eoj. that sucks. i wish i had known that when i saw you \r\ntonight, i would have hugged you lots.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(3277,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hi, i\\\'m an ENFP - extraverted, intuitive, feeling, \r\nperceiving. these days i\\\'m just barely \\\'E\\\' and highly \\\'P\\\'.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nin mayan astrology, i\\\'m a red galactic serpent. my tone \r\nis \\\'8\\\'. i was born in the year of the tiger. also i\\\'m a \r\ncapricorn, i like rollerblading, moonlit strolls, and \r\ncountry line dancing. i look cute in a skirt.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3278,985,'2001-05-26 10:49:21',0,'tomato','i got:\r\n\r\nPrimary Intelligence:	Mental\r\nCoping Style:	Competency\r\nSocial Style:	Withdrawn\r\nHierarchical Style:	Control\r\n\r\nit would be interesting to me to find out the difference \r\nbetween how others filled this out for me as opposed to \r\nhow i filled out for myself.  gemini?\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'165.247.200.7','en'),(3279,985,'2001-05-26 10:56:25',0,'tomato','control really scares me.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.200.7','en'),(3280,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hm. many of us seem to be scoring \"withdrawn\". eh. i \r\nthink i am very outgoing!\r\n<h1><font color=\"#FF0000\">FUCK THIS TEST</font></h1><br>\r\n(ps. i\'m a 9)',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3281,985,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i too am a 4 and an enfp. surprise.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3282,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeah, but you\\\'re not a gemini on the verge of cancer.  no\r\none can take that away from me.',4,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3283,983,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','I am well aquainted with these feelings you have, Chia. \r\nAfter 20 years or so of tearing, pulling, fraying the knot \r\ninside my guts, I have loosened it FINALLY. I can offer \r\nadvice, strong words, a slap in the face, kindness. \r\nWhatever you feel you need. But not in a public forum, so \r\nkindly email me if you wish. I may be able to impart a bit \r\nof hope, and maybe even some wisdom. The darkest, burning, \r\nscreaming, agonizing immobilizing fustrating doomsaying \r\ncreatures can inhabit us. It took me many months of weekly, \r\nsometimes bi and tri- weekly therapy sessions and fun times \r\nexperimenting with antidepressants, but my god man I made \r\nit. Hopefully, you and nowhere near as entrenched as I was.\r\nPlease, dude, write me up. You really aren\\\'t alone in your \r\nsuffering.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(3284,984,'2001-05-26 16:24:15',0,'keuch2 to all','hello, i\\\'m new to this website.  this is my second posting, \r\nbut i never introduced myself, so hi, i\\\'m keuch2.',1,0,0,0,'129.130.231.239','en'),(3285,969,'2001-05-26 16:30:13',0,'keuch2','Jorge Luis Borges says that time is circular, not linear; \r\nlives are like parallel paths that overlap, and events \r\nrepeat themselves in different planes of existence.\r\n</br>\r\nAstor Piazzolla, a tango musician, often dreamt about the \r\nHour Zero; the hour just after midnight, and hour of \r\nabsolute beginning and absolute end.',3,0,0,0,'129.130.231.239','en'),(3286,985,'2001-05-26 16:33:55',0,'chaya','heh. the internet tells me what some people have been \r\nsaying all along, that i am \\\"too much, too loud, too \r\neverything.\\\" surprise. i\\\'m an 8.<br><br>i\\\'ve been having \r\nsome bigger thoughts on the nature of identity and \r\npersonality but they\\\'re still percolating and i\\\'m curious \r\nto know what other people think. is your \r\nidentity/personality an immovable pillar? is it mutable, \r\ninfluenced by circumstance or life experience? how much of \r\nyour core being sticks with you as you evolve as a human? \r\nwhat motivated you 5 years ago, what motivates you now, \r\nwhat do you think will motivate you 5 years from now?\r\n<br><br>hi. i\\\'ve been eyeing that bookshelf full of \r\nexistentialists again. can you tell?',4,0,0,0,'158.252.213.111','en'),(3287,985,'2001-05-26 16:44:07',0,'jo','4, infp.<BR>\r\nwas an enfp as a senior in high school and an infp as a \r\nsenior in college. hmmm.',1,0,0,0,'64.12.103.66','en'),(3288,985,'2001-05-26 16:47:00',0,'jo','<p>\\\"<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.lessons4living.com/enneagram4.htm\\\">Famous \r\nExamples of Fours</a>: </p>\r\n\r\n<p>Jackie Onassis, Jeremy Irons, Paul Simon, Bob Dylan, \r\nAnne Rice, J.D. Salinger, Edgar Allen Poe, Prince, Judy \r\nGarland, Vincent Van Gogh, Marlon Brando, James Dean, Patsy \r\nCline, Elizabeth Taylor, Janis Joplin, <b>France - the \r\nCountry.</b>\\\"</p>\r\n<p>france - the country? what the fuck?</p>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.12.103.66','en'),(3289,985,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i took the test once and got a 7. i didn\\\'t agree with this\r\nassesment (leader, idealist, etc) so i took it again with\r\nanswers that i felt were just as honest and got a 2\r\n(compliant, compassionate).  neither of these are how i\r\nwould describe myself.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni took such pride in being an INFP when i was 16 that if i\r\ntook it again now and was rated as something else i feel it\r\nwould dis-respect my former self.\r\n<br><br>\r\nhmm, fear change much?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3290,985,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','o, the first time i met you you didn\\\'t talk at all.  you sat\r\nquietly on the couch and sucked your thumb.  the next day we\r\nmet again and again, you were very quiet and listened to\r\nstories we had to tell.  and the day after that, i have a\r\nvery clear memory of you standing in front of our house,\r\nholding a motorcycle helmet, silent, up against the booming\r\nvoice that is gregor. in fact, i think most of our\r\ninteraction the first time we met was: \r\n<br>\r\n[me] want to do {blah}?\r\n<br>\r\n[you] yeah!\r\n<br>\r\nand it would be done, probably in silence.\r\n<br>\r\nsecret silent badasses.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3291,985,'2001-05-26 17:26:19',0,'mscreant','I scored a 5. I\\\'m an INTP, and one of those damned \r\ngemini\\\'s. No matter which test I take, they all say the \r\nsame thing: control freak. *frown* I disagree.\r\n\r\nHere\\\'s one that was kinda fun:<br>\r\n\r\nhttp://www.quizbox.com/personality/test48.htm\r\n',2,0,0,0,'66.68.190.232','en'),(3292,960,'2001-05-26 18:19:23',0,'kat to austin','i noticed that same welding job. ive been considering it \r\nmyself...tho i think i will pass for a more pussy job of \r\nassisting at a publishing house. their thing is art \r\nbooks...pretty progressive stuff. i like building things. \r\nbuilding books would be cool.',3,0,0,0,'32.102.90.152','en'),(3293,985,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','i got 4. 4 4 4. FOUR. FOR. ',6,0,0,0,'64.229.135.101','en'),(3294,987,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nwordless sympathy. you are strong, we\\\'re all here for you, \r\nand this will fade into the past. you will take the \r\nlearning part with you, and grow stronger because of it. \r\nyou are stars and phoenix and the sun rising. *hugs* ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3295,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','of course, no organization can more accurately \r\ndescribe human behavior than <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.thespark.com/\\\">thespark.com</a>. \r\nand shall we all take the <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.speakout.com/ActivismTools/selectors\r\n/religion/\\\">religion selector</a> again?',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3296,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i\'ve always been curious how accurate tests like enneagram or \r\nkeirsey could possibly be. rather than test your \r\nresponses to real situations or question your value \r\nsystem, they ask you to assess yourself directly. they \r\nmay as well present you with a list of personality types \r\nand ask you to choose your favorite.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit\'s my opinion that people answer test questions \r\nbased on who they want to be or think they are, not \r\nbased on who they actually are.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthough i particularly like the religion selector, because \r\nthe results are not obvious in advance from the \r\nquestions.<br>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3297,985,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Vileness Moll','ICH BIN: INTJ\r\nTYPE: 1??? (perfectionist closest fit), don\\\'t really work \r\nwell with that test.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3298,985,'2001-05-26 20:22:32',0,'cait','<p>oh, how dorky. I am an 8. Translation: spoiled \r\nbrat.<p>What\\\'s the link for the 4 \r\nletter-I-can\\\'t-remember-the-name-offhand test?',0,0,0,0,'63.77.235.79','en'),(3299,987,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','thank you for sharing this experience.  in time this ben \r\nwill become prey, and never harm another as he did you.',2,0,0,0,'207.205.218.26','en'),(3300,987,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>That\\\'s one of the more disgusting situations I\\\'ve ever \r\nheard of.</p>\r\n<p>It seems like in all of them, including something that \r\nhappened to me about a year ago, it\\\'s always the details \r\nthat end up bothering me, the little things that make me \r\nwonder at how people can be capable of such complete \r\nsickness.</p>\r\n<p>I know someone who was raped, but what I can\\\'t believe \r\nis that she went to her friend for help afterwards, and \r\n<i>he raped her too.</i> I get sick just thinking about it, \r\nabout how someone can be capable of doing that. It amazes \r\nme how human beings can be so utterly disgusting.</p>\r\n<p>And what amazes me more is how people have the strength \r\nto get past it and go on with their lives; cheers to you \r\nand yours.</p>',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3301,985,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','Type 8',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3302,987,'2001-05-26 21:20:00',0,'jp','I\\\'m so glad that you managed to get away from that\r\nscumbag. That story could have had a much worse ending.<BR>\r\nThis guy never got caught? I\\\'d make it a full-time thing to\r\nput his ass in jail, or least give him some grief. A DNA\r\nsample\\\'s pretty strong evidence.<BR>\r\nThen again sometimes it\\\'s best to not get involved with\r\npoison like that or be consumed with revenge.<BR>\r\nYou\\\'re okay now and are moving on -- that\\\'s the good\r\nthing.<BR>',0,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3303,985,'2001-05-26 23:17:01',0,'jp','Took the religion selector test: Mahayana Buddhist 100. No\r\nsurprise there. Also \\\"Neo-Pagan 90\\\", not sure about\r\nthat.<BR>\r\nTook the thespark.com test and found I am an...<B>\r\nAccountant?!?</B>. Damn, I need to ge out more or\r\nsomething.<BR>\r\nRE: wondering if personality changes over time or is molded\r\nby experience...<BR>\r\nDefinitely yes in my case. I\\\'ve noticed 3 year cycles where\r\nwho I was involved with, where I lived, what I was doing\r\n(work/school) have slowly affected my personality in a\r\npermanent way.<BR>\r\nLike for example, I\\\'ve been a programmer the last 4 years.\r\nThat\\\'s definitely pulled me in the more introverted,\r\nconcrete-thinking, less emotional direction. A lot different\r\nthan how I used to be in college.\r\n ',0,0,0,0,'24.0.133.128','en'),(3304,985,'2001-05-26 23:58:07',0,'annette','I too am a four. That description is one of the most \r\naccurate I\\\'ve seen out of these type of tests.\r\n\r\n<br><br>I come up as an INFP or INTP depending upon my mood \r\nwhile taking the Keirsey test, neither of which fit very \r\nwell. Actually, I read all the temperament descriptions and \r\ncan\\\'t find myself in any of them.',2,0,0,0,'202.180.73.231','en'),(3305,985,'2001-05-27 02:34:17',0,'chaya','yeah 5, so that is part of what is floating around in my \r\nhead waiting to be crystallized into coherence.  i also \r\nwonder this: how good is it to *want* to be another way, \r\nhow close does that get you to realizing personal change?  \r\nobviously it\\\'s not enough to simply wish you were someone \r\nelse, but how much influence does pure will exert on the \r\npsyche?  i want to believe it\\\'s a lot, but in practice i \r\nmostly just see people claiming to be something they \r\nhaven\\\'t put any effort into becoming.  and then that again \r\nbegs the question of just how mutable our personalities \r\nreally are, anyway.  ',3,0,0,0,'158.252.242.212','en'),(3306,989,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','that dialog is so familiar.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3307,989,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i remember trying to talk you out of even applying for that \r\njob, then trying to talk you out of interviewing. i guess \r\nwhat i mean is, you can still decline to take this job. sigh.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(3308,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','to s5 and chaya: i try to be as honest as i can be when\r\ntaking a test.  base it on past experiences, really think\r\nhard when it comes to taking them.  what is the point of\r\nlying to yourself?  all these sort of personality tests,\r\nastrological profiles and such have always said the same\r\nthing about me.  the same things that my mother said about\r\nme when i was growing up.\r\ni don\\\'t know.  i don\\\'t believe in anything, so it doesn\\\'t\r\nreally matter to me.  i can\\\'t keep this sort of information\r\nin the back of my mind anyway, so it\\\'s not as if i can apply\r\nit to my life.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nto xep:  probably the reason i was so silent was because i\r\nwas afraid of you realising i\\\'m a big dork.  i was in the\r\nposession of the first stages of a massive crush.',3,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3309,985,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','http://logy.org/~xep/days/2001/030201/dorks.jpg',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(3310,989,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','other options: (1) telecommuting (2) a different job',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3311,985,'2001-05-27 08:11:56',0,'<TT>austin','<BR><img src=\"http://logy.org/~xep/days/2001/012701/batheggplant.jpg\">',4,0,0,1,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3312,989,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i forgot about that telecommuting thing.  i must remember\r\nthat.</P>',2,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3313,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','sometimes people confuse the process of inner \r\ntransformation with the wearing of a costume. but there \r\nis no doubt that everyone possesses the ability to grow \r\nand transform; often when we think we were are \r\nsteering the ship, we are simply living up to \r\nundiscovered potential.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nchicken/egg<br>\r\nfate/freewill<br>\r\ndestiny/determinism<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nis personality mutable? sure. but my suspicion is that \r\nwhat we often associate as the will to change, is really \r\nthe inner drive to change, bubbling up to the surface \r\nwhenever the time is right.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3314,985,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\nI got 6. did anyone else? I looked at the stats and it \r\nseemed like one of the least common ones. dumdedoo.',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(3315,985,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\nOooh. I like the religion selector.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nLiberal Quaker ...click to learn more (score=100)<BR>\r\nUnitarian Universalist ...click to learn more (score=85)<BR>\r\nNeo-Pagan ...click to learn more (score=85)<BR>\r\nMahayana Buddhist ...click to learn more (score=80)<BR>\r\nTheravada Buddhist ...click to learn more (score=80)\r\n<BR>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m off to find out what a unitarian universalist is. weird.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(3316,989,'2001-05-27 08:08:29',0,'bmg','can you work from home any?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.189.128.78','en'),(3317,989,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\nthese are the things that are constantly on my mind.<br>\r\nwhy move back to sf from la? take a step back to the stifling \r\nplace you grew up and your mom grew up and your grandparents \r\ngrew up. why? cuz of a job? because you dropped out of high \r\nschool and college and you want to make your parents proud, \r\nyou are afraid they will think you are an idiot.<br>\r\nthe job i took at dance magazine...i didn\\\'t want it, i don\\\'t \r\neven think i want it now but i wanted my parents to know that \r\ni could fix my credit and pay my bills and make good in the \r\npublishing world like they had.<br>\r\nbesides, i didn\\\'t know what else to do.<br>\r\ni adore you already, austin. and i promise, as flakey as i \r\nmay be, i won\\\'t let you go that easy. we can still be coffee \r\npals in the hippy state of santa cruz.\r\n-your pal, simon\r\n',1,0,0,0,'205.179.35.170','en'),(3318,985,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','starlajo: peter got a 6. shh don\\\'t tell him i told you. he \r\nis a secretive virgo.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3319,990,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<p>spent the morning reading websites about women at \r\nwartime, women soldiers, women who <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.redstone.army.mil/history/images/females/ph\r\noto07.jpg\">worked making bombs during WWII</a>...\r\n<p>found an amazing <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.salon.com/mwt/feature/1999/11/11/women/inde\r\nx.html\">article on salon.com</a> about the women who served \r\nin vietnam:</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>All women had to eat at the 24th Evac Hospital. So when \r\nwe went there we had to eat with the patients. Some of them \r\nhad missing arms, legs, eyes, and had IVs sticking out and \r\nall these little gadgets hanging from that walking thing.\r\n</p><p>\r\nThere were only two redheads there in the first place. And \r\nI would sit down with the patients and they would start \r\ncrying. And many, many of them asked me if they could touch \r\nmy hair, because they saw very few round-eyes and everybody \r\nwho was there had an aunt or a friend or schoolmate who was \r\nredheaded.\r\n</p><p>\r\nIt was so traumatic that I quit going. I don\'t think I ate \r\n20 times in the mess hall because they would cry. How can \r\nyou sit there and eat while these soldiers are crying?\r\n</i></p></blockquote><p>...\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy dad was in the army for over 20 years. i have a lot of \r\nconflicted feelings about the military. i associate \r\nchildhood comfort and pleasant memories with seeing tools \r\nof death, men in uniform marching, the smell of my dad\'s \r\nBDU\'s when he hugged me. fond memories of visiting dad in \r\nthe motorpool and climbing around on a missile launcher. i \r\nstill haven\'t done the thoughtwork on how to reconcile my \r\nlove for the surroundings of my childhood with my hatred of \r\nthe politics and violence of war. my father is proud of the \r\ntime he has spent in service, and i am proud of him. i \r\ndon\'t resent him for participating, i love him too much to \r\nfault anything he does. \r\n</p>\r\n',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3320,990,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i am having the worst morning, and trying really hard not to\r\ncurse.',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3321,990,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','i think that there is honor in being a warrior.  a warrior-soldier stands apart from society in a way.  he or she accepts that the call to war might come.  war is hateful to man, as it should be.  nonetheless, war is sometimes necessary, if not for soldiers my people would have not escaped the death camps of Hitler.  that soldier caused them to die in the first place only serves to remind us that war cannot be given up.  peace will never be a state we live in, it will always be what we strive for.  to hate the politics that lead to war is understandable.  to hate the men we call upon to fight is unforgivable.  when we disagree with a war or police action, we must turn our outrage not upon our soldiers, but upon the politicians who have so utterly failed to examine the peaceful options open to them.  the men who led to my writing of the piece have killed more people than i can imagine.  sadly the soldiers they killed are more like them than the politicians who sent them to fight.  they are good men, loving and quick to aid those in need.  they are my uncles, my step-father, my chosen-father, my grandfathers, five of my closest friends, and many more distant friends.  hate not the soldiers, hate only the war that kills them. ',1,0,0,0,'207.205.218.39','en'),(3322,985,'2001-05-27 21:27:36',0,'caitgrr','<p>Uhhhh, excuse me, where is the KEIRSEY test link?? hmmm?',0,0,0,0,'63.77.235.125','en'),(3323,991,'2001-05-27 21:39:34',0,'cait','<p>The pigeons in NYC are absolutely wonderful.<p>Often \r\ntimes I\\\'ve dragged my boyfriend to evil Petco in Union \r\nSquare to buy bird seed and then into the actual park to \r\nfeed the pigeons. They are magnificant, funny, intelligent, \r\nkind, dirty!, and wonderful birds with no shame. The male \r\npigeons are always trying to fuck the womenz and the female \r\npigeons are always scooting away asap.<p>One time I saw a \r\npigeon stomp right over to a little sparrow that was eating \r\nsome disgusting reminant of food and the pigeon looked at \r\nthe sparrow and then started pecking it\\\'s head. That was the \r\nonly time I questioned my love for those angry birds. But, \r\nhey, we\\\'ve all got a Bad Side. ',0,0,0,0,'63.77.235.125','en'),(3324,987,'2001-05-27 22:00:53',0,'kat','is is the sad truth that there are many people out there \r\nlike this. i have been in too many similar situations. some \r\nincluding relaatives [family, but not someone i\\\'d ever call \r\nfmaily again]. i have moments of pure rage. the urge to \r\nreact. to act out. a demanding action demanding response. \r\nbut such things never bring the relief i might be searching \r\nfor. it is just my faith that life can be better that keeps \r\nme going. that and the many other people i meet who grace \r\nme with amazing unexpected acts of love. i horde those \r\nunconditional moments like sweet sunfilled sips of wine.',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.146','en'),(3325,989,'2001-05-27 22:09:06',0,'kat','breakfast at Zachs...its the shit. besides i will give you \r\nrides in the beast to sf. whats a two hour drive to see \r\ngood friends compared to 6000 miles? the peninsula is \r\nsmaller than you\\\'d think.',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.146','en'),(3326,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','keirsey.com.  but, it\\\'s not giving you full test results\r\nanymore, unless you pay up.',3,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3327,982,'2001-05-27 22:49:28',0,'cody','1.  I am not on the real world\r\n2.  woob is a music producer(s)',0,0,0,0,'64.130.32.137','en'),(3328,990,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i agree, incitor - that\\\'s what i was getting at, i guess. i \r\nlove my father the soldier, and the soldiers i grew up \r\naround that were good people, but i hate the aspects of \r\nhumanity and politics that cause war.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3329,993,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<p>hmm. quitting pot made you dream.\r\n</p><p>\r\nthere are a lot of elements at work in this dream.. \r\nwatching videos of your friends having sex with other \r\npeople is probably about you being jealous/envious/desirous \r\nof relationships with stinder/coleen and realizing that \r\nother people have done that but not you...\r\n</p><p>\r\ni bet the person on the phone was me! *ego*\r\n</p><p>\r\ni dunno about the communicating with the unknown \r\norganization. maybe the upper crust apartment was some sort \r\nof symbol for your desire to be successful? maybe the woman \r\nyou invited in was a symbol for your want for success, and \r\nsean\\\'s reaction to her was your worry that sean doesn\\\'t \r\nsupport you in your goals...\r\n</p><p>\r\ni dunno dude. you should read about jungian dream analysis, \r\nand lucid dreaming. it\\\'s cool. ok.\r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3330,985,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I got a \\\"5\\\" which only proves that I am steve five.</p>\r\n<p>Interesting that almost no-one else here got a 5.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m an ENTP, too, or at least I was when I took this test \r\non January 21st, 1999. <a \r\nhref=http://deity.loa.com/~darwin/temperament.html>see?</a><\r\n/p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',4,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3331,985,'2001-05-28 03:40:12',0,'cyantist','like darwin, i\\\'m an entp.  however, i scored a 7 on this \r\ntest.  ',1,0,0,0,'64.172.164.74','en'),(3332,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','only 10 more comments to go, to rival the legendary \r\nauntie \"missionary or doggie-style\" <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=632.txt\">post</a> comment count, \r\nproving that we are not just obsessed with sex but also \r\nwith self-analysis.',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3333,993,'2001-05-28 03:58:07',0,'kd.o.g.','you could try Inner Work by robert a. johnson (jungian \r\npsychologist).  his other books helped me as well.  ',2,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(3334,985,'2001-05-28 04:17:23',0,'s5 to o','i\\\'ve also been watching the countdowns converge. do \r\nthese meta-comments count too? hi everyone. *38*',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3335,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','they sure as hell count, you can bet your sweet aunt \r\nnelly.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsongs: ohia are the featured artist on audiogalaxy.com \r\ntoday.  i also bought 2 of their 7\\\"\\\'s up the road at tiny \r\nraoul records.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe other day i saw a guy wearing a modest mouse tshirt.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nnext thing i know i\\\'ll hear apples in stereo at the \r\nsupermarket, and my elly music immersion will be \r\ncomplete.<br>',4,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3336,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','typical 4. you people are all the same.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3337,985,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i made a <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/le/elly.html\\\">page</a\r\n> on jo\\\'s site about all the tests, including the <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.colorquiz.com\\\">color quiz</a>. i\\\'m \r\nsurprised no one mentioned the colorquiz yet.<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m a neo-pagan.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3338,970,'2001-05-28 04:57:34',0,'alanna','<P>\r\ni\\\'m north of albany, ny - a very pretty place, lots o\\\' tall \r\nevergreens and mountains and creeks and \r\nsuch...unfortunately it\\\'s rained almost nonstop since we\\\'ve \r\nbeen here, which does nothing to help the mood (bleck), but \r\ni\\\'ve made sam get out with me when we can swipe his \r\nparents\\\' car.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nwhich brings me to a very interesting observation: sam\\\'s \r\nparents have the usual two cars, and one is a standard, and \r\nsam cannot drive stickshift.&nbsp; i\\\'m under the impression \r\nthat his parents have always had at least one standard car, \r\nbut he never learned.&nbsp; lazy bum - what if there was an \r\nemergency and the stick car was all that was around?&nbsp; \r\nhe\\\'d just be screwed, now, wouldn\\\'t he?\r\n</P>',4,0,0,0,'64.12.103.66','en'),(3339,984,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','welcome!',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3340,978,'2001-05-28 05:10:58',0,'alanna','<P>\r\nif i wasn\\\'t so fat and lazy i\\\'d be vegan woman.&nbsp; \r\nvegetarianism is very easy, but sloughing off that last bit \r\nof useless ick stuff is proving to be difficult indeed.\r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://www.autrui.com/images/oph-at-\r\necl.htm\\\">ophelia</A> (goddess that she is) suggested that i \r\nshould find myself some people who think the same way i do \r\nabout food and life, and join a co-op and meet people \r\nthere, and when vegan-ism\\\'s the norm of the community it\\\'s \r\ntoo easy to slip right in and enjoy food to its \r\nfullest.&nbsp; i believe her, but now i wish nice vegan \r\nfriends would move in next door.&nbsp; i am too lazy for my \r\nown good, heh.\r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.12.103.66','en'),(3341,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','well, on the religion test i got this:<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAtheist/Agnostic...(score=100)<br>\r\nHumanist...(score=100)<br>\r\nTheravada Buddhist...(score=100)<br>\r\nUnitarian Universalist...(score=100)<br>\r\nLiberal Quaker...(score=54)<br>\r\nNeo-Pagan...(score=33)<br>\r\nTaoism...(score=20)<br>\r\nMainline to Liberal Protestant...(score=12)<br>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES (3342,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','and on reading the explanations, i can only identify partly \r\nwith Atheist/Agnostic.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI don\\\'t have any proof for any sort of higher being or any \r\nkind of identifiable pattern which points to right and \r\nwrong.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni respect, or even envy people who can believe so totally \r\nin something, like some people do with religion.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni wish i didn\\\'t have to think the way i do.  but as long as \r\ni\\\'ve lived with it and as much as i\\\'ve agonised over it, i \r\ncan\\\'t see any other alternative.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsometimes i become frustrated with myself and go on a \r\nbender thinking that i am just too lazy to think about \r\nthings and come up with real opinions.  but i can have \r\nopinions.  they\\\'re just not ones i really really believe.  \r\nanyway, when i am this frustrated i turn over and over the \r\nconcept of nothingness in my mind, looking for some spark \r\nor some wave of truth to wash over me - that\\\'s it!  that\\\'s \r\nwhy i\\\'m alive! - but nothing ever comes.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe closest i\\\'ve ever got to the type of deep-felt truth i \r\ncrave is love.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nemptiness; meaninglessness; no cause; no effect.  \r\nnothing.<br>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3343,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','buddhism and neo-pagan scored 100%. islam and \r\ngreek orthodox were at the bottom of my list, bringing \r\nup the rear at 0%. (irony: i was baptized greek \r\northodox.)',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3344,985,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','greek boys in skirts:<br>\r\n<p>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.greece.org/FDF/kids.gif\"><img \r\nsrc=\"http://www.greece.org/FDF/kids.gif\" width=480 border=1></a></p>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3345,985,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<p>\r\n<font color=\"red\" size=\"+3\">I KNOW WHAT YOU ARE DOING</font>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ndon\'t think i haven\'t noticed that the two of you are \r\nPRATTLING ON like so many old biddies at a quilting bee, \r\nwith the SOLE PURPOSE of bringing the comment number on \r\nthis post up to the legendary MISSIONARY OR DOGGIE STYLE \r\ndebacle of yore. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<i>(moment of silence for harrowing sex-related \r\nembarassment brought on by my own mother discussing sexual \r\npositions in public forums)</i>.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<br><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://beatthief.com/horror.html\">the horror</a>.\r\n<br><br>\r\n*punches wall*\r\n',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3346,985,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','ps. i have had a lot of coffee today',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3347,985,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<img src=\\\"http://www.gee.kz/peter/yc.jpg\\\">',1,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(3348,994,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','PUNT DOG! HAHAHAHAHA<BR>\r\nI also prefer to floss and brush in the shower.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(3349,985,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','GOD DAMN IT KYLE JUST KICK IT ONE TIME WHAT THE FUCK',6,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3350,990,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Poh\\\'ng\\\'kng Moll','<p>In my readings about war, what always stands out is the \r\ngreat difference between the people making the decisions \r\nand the people who are actually fighting on the line, and \r\nthis is how I resolve my feelings about warfare in \r\ngeneral.</p>\r\n<p>I feel little or no resentment or dislike for the people \r\nwho actually risk their lives and participate in war. \r\nRegardless of whether or not they\\\'re actually good people, \r\nor what they think they\\\'re fighting for is actually a good \r\ncause or not, or a lie or not, they are nevertheless \r\nrisking their lives and sanity by exposing themselves to \r\nlife in its most brutal form, and that\\\'s something that \r\ndeserves some kind of recognition, if not respect.</p>\r\n<p>I save my bitterness for the politics, ideologies and \r\nthe misguided decisions that often prolong and fuel wars. \r\nMaybe if people were more critical in their values these \r\npeople wouldn\\\'t have the power that they do, but this is an \r\nunrealistic thing to expect from human beings. We\\\'re a \r\nspecies of yes-men.</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3351,994,'2001-05-28 08:16:49',0,'keuch2','top five reasons not to listen to radio stations:\r\n<p>\r\n1) i just received a copy of radiohead\\\'s new single\r\n</br>\r\n2) i can\\\'t stand radio djs\r\n</br>\r\n3) napster\r\n</br>\r\n4) commercials\r\n</br>\r\n5) i can amuse myself playing a musical instrument',1,0,0,0,'65.30.38.40','en'),(3352,984,'2001-05-28 08:26:30',0,'keuch2','thanks!',1,0,0,0,'65.30.38.40','en'),(3353,994,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<p>\r\n6) college radio uber alles. spin that dial to the left!\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3354,994,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>5 reasons i can move to santa cruze, no order</P>\r\n<UL>\r\n<LI>i have a job here</LI>\r\n<LI>maybe i could live with xep\\\'s brother</LI>\r\n<LI>green</LI>\r\n<LI>beach</LI>\r\n<LI>air</LI>\r\n<LI>sky</LI>\r\n<LI>(peace||love||happiness)</LI>\r\n<LI>dude bro bra discount<LI>\r\n</UL>\r\n<P>ok, eight.</P>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'64.174.154.109','en'),(3355,995,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i knew some people in austin that went to gnostic mass.  \r\nthere were a couple unsavory characters that came to one of m \r\nparties who were gnostic mass people and friends of my \r\nroomate.  whenever i ran into one of them at the grocery \r\nstore, which seemed like often, he would say, \\\"yes i am going \r\nhome to make love juice cookies\\\" and give me this slimy smile \r\nlike maybe i was interested.  it gives me chills even now.  i \r\nnever stopped asking him what was going on and getting that \r\nanswer because i always liked to make sure that he was still \r\ndoing the same thing whenever i saw him.  it hightened the \r\nwierdness of it all.  and isolated event is one thing, but \r\nthat is all the guy ever seemed to be doing.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i also knew he worked at a porn shop in south austin.  \r\nperhaps that is where he got the juices, scraping off the \r\nmovie cubby in the wee hours of the morning into a little \r\nphotographic film container or something.</P>\r\n',4,0,0,0,'64.174.154.109','en'),(3356,994,'2001-05-28 17:41:05',0,'kat','it brings me great joy to brush my teeth in the shower. not \r\nsure why. it allows for really zestful messy brushing. \r\ndoing the loofah thing is also nice. ',2,0,0,0,'32.100.71.44','en'),(3357,978,'2001-05-28 18:23:02',0,'stella','<P>\r\nle: thanks. that was nice. \r\n</P>\r\n<P>\r\nI was a fat girl. Now I\\\'m chubby. I made several painful, \r\nlife-altering decisions that hurt, a lot, but in the end led \r\nme to the most crystalline happiness that I\\\'ve ever known. \r\nEveryone should be as happy. The result of my courage and \r\ntears, was (is?) that my body  decided that happiness was \r\nall the comfort that I needed. I stopped being indulgent. \r\nNow my happiness feeds me. I\\\'m on my way to being someone \r\nwhom I never thought I was. I\\\'ve changed. \r\n<P>Changing.</P>\r\n<P>Changling.</P>\r\n<P>Chrysallis.</P>\r\n</P>\r\n<P>Can someone, anyone, maybe elly, post some links or \r\nwhatever to direct me to worthwhile vegan recipes? Or email \r\nme.</P>\r\n<P> Whichever you prefer</P> \r\n<P> luna_stella@email.com</P>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3358,997,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the best thing i\'ve read so far on this topic was a book \r\nthat austin recommended: <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/02264\r\n58083/\">the structure of scientific revolutions</a>. <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.unamerican.com/\">srini</a> suggests \r\nthat spreading memes, as units of cultural \r\nprogramming, are essential to the process of \r\nrevolution.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3359,973,'2001-05-28 20:13:55',0,'narcoleptic','in france, ve haf ze R teg',6,0,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(3360,994,'2001-05-28 21:02:12',0,'kd.o.g.','aus:  dude, bro, bra discount HAhaHAha!  i remember \r\ncalifornia',1,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(3361,997,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>(variation on beastie boys)<BR><BR>\r\nretrograde motion<BR>\r\ncoconut lotion<BR>\r\nhad to dis the girl because she got too emotional</P>',3,0,0,0,'64.51.232.105','en'),(3362,994,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\n... *confused* bra discount? ',1,0,0,0,'207.69.48.246','en'),(3363,997,'2001-05-29 03:00:25',0,'jo','austin i don\\\'t understand why you aren\\\'t dissing this whole \r\ncomputer job thing and becoming a rap artist. i mean, we\\\'ve \r\nall heard <a href=\\\"/music\\\">your genius</a>. steve can be \r\nyour dj, yo. steve needs to be making music, too.',1,0,0,0,'64.12.105.137','en'),(3364,993,'2001-05-29 03:35:59',0,'jp','I don\\\'t know that much about dream analysis but here\\\'s some \r\ngeneral stuff I\\\'ve remembered:<BR>\r\nblack people sometimes can represent \\\"shadow\\\" figures. If \r\nyou\\\'ve read Jung he gets into what your shadow means. \r\nBasically something like repressed personality traits that \r\ncoud also be seen as unrealized potential.<BR>\r\nHouses can symbolize your psyche as a whole and the \r\ndifferent rooms can be different compartments or phases or \r\nyourself.<BR><BR>\r\nIf you want to keep your dreams intense try the herbal \r\nsupplement Gingko Biloba. Whenever I take it I notice more \r\nvivid dreams as well memory flashbacks of totally random \r\nthings.<BR>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'63.172.123.3','en'),(3365,993,'2001-05-29 03:46:09',0,'stella','valerian root is also very good for deep, intense dreaming. \r\nwhenever i take it, i wake up feeling like my brain has \r\nworked some *serious* shit out. just be careful, it smells \r\nlike dirty feet. ',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(3366,994,'2001-05-29 04:03:35',0,'annette','I tried shower brushing for the first time today. It\\\'s nice.',2,0,0,0,'202.180.73.231','en'),(3367,998,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','\\\"dotconomy\\\"',6,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(3368,993,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','no thats ok. they are intense enough. haha. last night i \r\ndidnt dream. that was a good.',6,0,0,0,'64.229.135.101','en'),(3369,998,'2001-05-29 14:20:59',0,'mscreant','I ran into an old friend from high school one day. He was \r\nshouting at passersby that they government was after him. \r\nOnce I realized who he was, I stopped to talk with him. He \r\ndidn\\\'t recognize me at first, but once I told him who I \r\nwas, he went to this weird place, where we were still in \r\nhigh school. He started talking about everyone we knew from \r\nschool as if we were still hanging out during that era. I \r\ntalked to him for awhile, and the people who walked by \r\nwould give me the dirtiest of looks... surprised I guess \r\nthat I was talking to this homeless guy, with my daughter \r\nin tow, nonetheless. He was pretty harmless, save the \r\nmoments when he would tell me that whoever was walking by \r\nwas a secret agent sent to get him, and he would scream \r\nobscenities at them. I offered him money for food, but he \r\nrefused it. Instead, I gave him my bottled water.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nA few days later, a friend and I went to see The Gaza \r\nStrippers, and afterwards we passed by Jake asleep on a \r\nbench at 2 a.m. We woke him up, and started talking to him. \r\nHe began talking about how his angel mother had been \r\nseduced by Jesus, and how he was going to kill the guy when \r\nhe got the chance. Then he asked me if I had come to seduce \r\nhim as well. I was a little worried at this point, because \r\nhe seemed to be rather out of it, talking about his \\\"angel \r\nmother\\\" being gang raped, and how she enjoyed it, and she \r\nwas nothing but a whore... and how when he died, God was \r\ngoing to make him the king of fucking. Before my friend and \r\nI took off, I again offered Jake some money. He refused it, \r\nsaying he had this friend who gave him money for weed in \r\nexchange for favors, and would sometimes let him sleep at \r\nhis place. As he told us about this \\\"friend\\\" he grew \r\nincreasingly angry. Misty and I politely said goodbye (as \r\nbest we could), and took off.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAbout a week later I was downtown with my daughter and \r\nboyfriend. We ran into Jake again. He was barefoot, and it \r\nwas hot as hell (think Texas... August). We convinced him \r\nto go eat something with us. While we were eating, I \r\nconvinced him to let me take him to my place so he could \r\nuse the shower and clean up, and afterwards, I promised to \r\nby him some shoes. My boyfriend and I took him home. Jake \r\ntook a shower. When he came out he said he needed to leave. \r\nWhen I asked why, he said his friend Mandra told him he was \r\nno good for us, and he didn\\\'t deserve to be with such nice \r\npeople. When I asked him when Mandra told him this, he said \r\nwhile he was in the shower. Damn. I was pretty worried at \r\nthis point, but this was my friend, and I desperately \r\nwanted to help him get off the streets. I took him to \r\nAcademy and bought him some All Stars. He was so excited. \r\nAnd so clean. He was beginning to look like my old friend \r\nagain.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nDavid and I decided to let him stay the night that night (I \r\ncouldn\\\'t justify taking him back to some street corner). I \r\ncooked dinner, and while we ate, Jake began talking to \r\nhimself (or whatever voice was talking to him), and then \r\nverbally lashed out at us, accusing us of insane things. \r\nWhen we went to bed, I made David sleep on the floor in \r\nfront of my bedroom door, so that Jake would not be able to \r\nget in. Should he try.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI offered to help Jake in so many ways. I offered to help \r\nhim get clothes. I offered him money. I offered to help him \r\nget into a psyche hospital (as it turns out he was \r\nschizophrenic and since he was still smoking pot, he would \r\nnot be admitted until he stopped. He wouldn\\\'t stop). \r\nEverytime I tried to help Jake get out of his situation, he \r\nrefused my help. The morning after he spent the night was \r\nthe last time I ever saw him.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nSome of these people living on the streets genuinely have \r\nno choice. Jake had been hospitalized for schizophrenia \r\nbefore, but they only let them stay in the hospital for so \r\nlong. And the only way the police will put someone in the \r\nhospital is if they are a danger to themselves or to \r\nsomeone else, which is how Jake was hospitalized in the \r\nfirst place. They didn\\\'t try to help him though, they just \r\nkept him there, drugged up for awhile, then released him \r\nback on the streets.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAfter this experience I couldn\\\'t help but wonder how many \r\nother homeless people have friends in the same town? \r\nFriends from way back? And how many of those friends walk \r\nby, looking at the ground, pretending not to notice them? I \r\nwish I had more money, and more time, and the emotional and \r\nmental ability to truly help Jake. It was a terrible time, \r\nI was scared for myself, and I was scared for Jake. But, if \r\nhe had let me, I would have done everything I could to help \r\nhim.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI miss my friend Jake.',2,0,0,0,'66.68.190.232','en'),(3370,983,'2001-05-29 16:53:03',0,'keuch2','<p>\r\nSometimes everything is wrong. Now it\\\'s time to sing along.\r\nWhen your day is night alone, (hold on, hold on)</br>\r\nif you feel like letting go, (hold on)</br>\r\nwhen you think you\\\'ve had too much of this life, well hang \r\non.</br>\r\n<P>\r\nEverybody hurts. Take comfort in your friends.</br>\r\nEverybody hurts. Don\\\'t throw your hand. Oh, no. Don\\\'t throw \r\nyour hand.</br>\r\nIf you feel like you\\\'re alone, no, no, no, you are not alone\r\n<p>\r\nIf you\\\'re on your own in this life, the days and nights are \r\nlong,</br>\r\nwhen you think you\\\'ve had too much of this life to hang on.\r\n<p>\r\nWell, everybody hurts sometimes,\r\neverybody cries.</br>\r\nAnd everybody hurts sometimes. So, hold on, hold on.',1,0,0,0,'129.130.231.179','en'),(3371,996,'2001-05-29 17:16:55',0,'joyrider','<p>Home can be fantastic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We have a sparrow nest in the eaves of our house - \r\njust above a bedroom window.  First thing every \r\nmorning for me is coffee on the back stoop, smoking, \r\nand bird watching/listening.  I listen to the high-pitched \r\ninsistant babies, hidden behind old asbestos panels.  I \r\nwatch the parents dodge sprinklers and wires and pick \r\ninsects out of the air.  It\\\'s seven minutes of stillness \r\nevery day that I really need.  I\\\'m glad it\\\'s there.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I like your house\\\'s name.  I live somewhere called \r\nKyuden White Trash, which isn\\\'t pretty or calm, but it fits \r\nwell.</p>\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'64.42.23.113','en'),(3372,985,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I oscillate between ENTP and ENTJ if this is a Meyers-\r\nBriggs derivative.  I try to be ENTP and not J.  :-)',1,0,0,0,'24.162.55.137','en'),(3373,989,'2001-05-29 20:12:41',0,'gorilla','Very cute. I love ya, but stop whining! You can always quit.\r\nTake that road my friend, you have not arrived & you never \r\nwill, just keep walking driving moving, it\\\'s all any of us \r\ncan do! Fuck the hippie talk! This reality...or maybe I\\\'m \r\nwrong?\r\n\r\n:)',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(3374,994,'2001-05-29 20:44:21',0,'bark','in a shower, weather permitting\r\n<p><img src=\\\"http://www.oddhours.com/hs/brushing2.jpg\\\">',4,0,0,0,'12.110.65.91','en'),(3375,998,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>As a temporary citizen of Newark, association with the \r\nhomeless can\\\'t be avoided. Some of them were okay.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The only one I knew by name was Robert. He was old and \r\nblack and had a beard and funny-looking teeth. He would \r\nwalk around the campus because he loved going to the \r\nlibrary.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Robert was really, really smart. I was always very happy \r\nto see him. I\\\'d let him bum cigarettes from me and we\\\'d sit \r\nfor hours talking about history and philosophy. I remember \r\nhe was always talking about this theory that the Egyptians \r\nhad more influence over Greek culture than most people are \r\nwilling to admit, and he was also very focused on a man who \r\nwas burned as a heretic in Italy during the 16th century, \r\nperhaps because he was supporting such views. People would \r\nlook at us while we were talking, but the cops would wave \r\nto him, they knew him. I did too, for a little while.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(3376,998,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>\r\ndarwin, you knew me when i was homeless.  for a while you even gave me a place to stay, ahhh, those pasta dinners and Burger King with ants...  while i was homeless i came to two important conclusions.  1.)  i was homeless essentialy by choice.  While i did not intend to give up my home, i did it to myself and did not work hard enough to rectify the situation.  the fact that for some of that time i had a child on the way reflects poorly on me.  2.)  most of the homeless i met during that time, like me, refused to work towards getting off the streets.  they felt that either society owed them a place to live (which belief i reject, although sympathize with) or that somehow, everything would suddenly get better for them.  automagically as it were.  today it is rare i help a homeless person who asks me for money.  sometimes, when i feel good, i will offer to buy them food.  but i never give them money.  more often i will direct them to one of the local hostels where they can at least get a shower and usually some new clothes.  i will give them the adresses and fone numbers of several places which either can employ them, or help them find employment.  it is rare that they listen to me, more often they just walk away after giving me a dirty look.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nrewinding a bit...you mention a physical assualt.  our course teaches two forms of awareness.  Full awareness, which is used everywhere but in your home or secured locations which you are well familiar with (friend\\\'s homes for instance); and relaxed awareness, which is used in those places you are familiar with and are reasonably secured.  at home i wear only one weapon (three or more being usual for outside my home) and my reflexes are relaxed.  if someone grabs me from behind at home, they don\\\'t have to worry about getting hurt.  not so outside.  unfortunately, the correct use of awareness requires two things hard to come by in most people.  the willingness to conciously practice it until it becomes an automatic part of life, and a willingness to see everyone as a potential threat.\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'207.205.218.95','en'),(3377,998,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to incitor','you carry weapons? what the fuck is wrong with you?',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(3378,998,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','when i was visiting sf xi and i were walking back to le\\\'s \r\nhouse from somewhere, and anyway there was a bum lying in \r\nthe middle of the pavement, motionless.  i was pretty \r\ndistressed and wanted to do something, see if he was dead \r\nor whatever.  i stopped and asked xi but he said the bum \r\nwas sleeping, and that you get used to that sort of thing \r\nin sf.  still, i wondered.  if i couldn\\\'t tell he was \r\nsleeping, not dead, how could anyone else?<br>\r\n<br>\r\non the streets the only weapons i carry are instinct, rage \r\nand my body.<br>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3379,1000,'2001-05-30 02:39:41',0,'sawks','<br>\r\nqbert is <b>hard</b>, big ups on your honorable \r\nquest<br><br>\r\nalso congrats on the job, and the 1000 posts, and all that\r\n',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(3380,1000,'2001-05-30 03:35:14',0,'bmg','<p>Cheers, brother.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.68.78.158','en'),(3381,1000,'2001-05-30 03:41:08',0,'xepmom','Bring you own pillow and blankie -- we\\\'re a little short and \r\nit gets cold out there. Welcome to snug harbor.\r\n',2,