oduce some of the most inspirational, \r\nbreathtaking pieces of work in the world.  i mean just look at the mona lisa.  \r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(4752,1222,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','spider house is this really great indoor/outdoor cafe in austin. \r\nthere\\\'s a huge outside area with christmas lights and crap old \r\nlawn furniture and lanterns. i got drunk there once and tripped \r\nover all the chairs. they have good veggie chili. it\\\'s a good place. \r\nit\\\'s one of the things i love about austin actually, the cafe run: \r\nspider house and austin java co.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4753,1222,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i like getting hit on.  i don\\\'t think it\\\'s sexual \r\nharassment if you tell them what\\\'s what and they take it \r\nwith good grace (and humour too)...',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4754,1222,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','hey, didn\\\'t ben brown whip out his willy in the parking \r\nlot?  is that sexual harassment?',4,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4755,1221,'2001-06-28 07:10:39',0,'o to le','stop flirting with me',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4756,1222,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>not everyone likes getting hit on. the lowest \r\ncommon boundary has to be set in public space. I like \r\nwalking around with my titties flopping all over the \r\nplace, but some people dont want to see them. so i \r\nbandage em up so as to be more proper in public.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.93.231','en'),(4757,1222,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','a symptom of humans becoming less tolerant of the \r\nmanifestations of humanity?',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4758,1221,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to o','<br>\r\n<img \r\nsrc=\"http://www.logy.org/~xep/days/2001/062401/elly.xep.jpg\"\r\n width=\"360\" border=\"1\">',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4759,1221,'2001-06-28 07:59:43',0,'o to le','do you want me to make a mess in my panties?  you\\\'re going \r\nthe right way for a spanked bottom.',4,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4760,1221,'2001-06-28 08:00:20',0,'o to le','it\\\'s got something to do with the hole near the top of your \r\nfishnets.',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4761,1209,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>This is the excellent foppery of the world, that,\r\n	when we are sick in fortune,--often the surfeit\r\n	of our own behavior,--we make guilty of our\r\n	disasters the sun, the moon, and the stars: as\r\n	if we were villains by necessity; fools by\r\n	heavenly compulsion; knaves, thieves, and\r\n	treachers, by spherical predominance; drunkards,\r\n	liars, and adulterers, by an enforced obedience of\r\n	planetary influence; and all that we are evil in,\r\n	by a divine thrusting on: an admirable evasion\r\n	of whoremaster man, to lay his goatish\r\n	disposition to the charge of a star! </p> <p>-- \r\nWilliam Shakespeare, King Lear, Act 1, Scene 1</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4762,1222,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I\\\'m confused by the concept of public space as a place \r\nwhere a person can \\\"mind their own business.\\\" I thought \r\nthat was called \\\"private space.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m also confused by this \\\"lowest common denominator\\\" \r\nthing.. is this to mean that we should all adhere to \r\nbehavior which couldn\\\'t possibly offend? Does such behavior \r\nexist?</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin</p>\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4763,311,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I\\\'d like a remake of \\\"the seventh guest\\\" starring me as \r\na spooky FMV ghost.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4764,1219,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','law of thermodynamics',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4765,742,'2001-06-28 10:40:44',0,'darwin the italian','<p>canola oil is a poor substitute for olive oil.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4766,1223,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i also like the words \\\"parasite\\\" and \\\"vampire\\\".',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4767,1223,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>to quote <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.akiko.ndirect.co.uk/words/advice.html\\\">Uncl\r\ne Bill</a> :</p>\r\n<p>\r\n\\\"\r\n<p>If, after having been exposed to someone\\\'s presence, you \r\nfeel as if you\\\'ve lost a quart of plasma, avoid that \r\npresence. You need it like you need pernicious anemia. </p>\r\n<p>\r\nWe don\\\'t like to hear the word \\\"vampire\\\" around here; we\\\'re \r\ntrying to improve our public image. Building a kindly, \r\navuncular, benevolent image; \\\"interdependence\\\" is the \r\nkeyword -- \\\"enlightened interdependence\\\". </p>\r\n<p>\r\nLife in all its rich variety, take a little, leave a \r\nlittle. However, by the inexorable logistics of the \r\nvampiric process they always take more than they leave -- \r\nand why, indeed, should they take any? </p>\r\n<p>\r\n-- Willam S. Burroughs, \\\"Words of Advice for Young \r\nPeople\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4768,1223,'2001-06-28 11:28:43',0,'ida','ain\\\'t it delicious, bein\\\' so pernicious! fuck them mormon sons o\\\' \r\nbitches!',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4769,311,'2001-06-28 12:39:11',0,'Joanna','I spoke too soon.  I now have a job making cd roms and I \r\nfucking hate it.  I want a PR job again.  ',0,0,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(4770,311,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I resolve this problem by hating jobs, period. =)</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',1,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4771,1220,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','if you could grow facial hair, you\\\'d pet it all the time too.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4772,1214,'2004-02-07 03:45:28',534,'griece','<p>Ye gods, Jojo, tone it down.  I am duly impressed \r\nwith the muscularity of your brain.  But I am not sure \r\nsuch pedagogy is appropriate for this venue.  \r\n\r\n<p>I guess I don\\\'t know what happened here.  What I \r\noriginally wanted to do was open a discussion based \r\non a simple sentence, \\\"truth is stranger than fiction\\\" \r\nand argue that fundamentally, there is no truth.  I guess \r\nI used ideas that are beyond the scope of my \r\nvocabulary and in trying to dumb it down so I  could \r\nunderstand it I guess I came across as pretentious or \r\ncondescending.  Weeb\\\'s semi-rancorous posts are \r\nunderstandable, indicating that my fractious persona \r\nworked to some degree, but now that I have come \r\naround to demonstrate a willingness to discuss and \r\nthink that past is working against me.  (I am not the first \r\nto have past indiscretions work against me, though our \r\npublic theater has ignored a certain dynastical \r\npoliticianâ€™s historical brushes with Johnny Law.  If \r\nAmerica can forgive, why canâ€™t Weeb?  Why canâ€™t \r\nWeeb?).  I never meant to hurt anyoneâ€™s feelings or to \r\nhave my comparatively primitive thoughts and ideas \r\npassed through red-hot refinerâ€™s fire of JoJoâ€™s obviously \r\nwell-schooled brain.  \r\n\r\n<p>So rather than get everybodyâ€™s didactic panties in a \r\nbunch, Iâ€™ll back off.  But not before trying one more time, \r\nthis time recognizing that I am swinney.org leper, a \r\npariah who seems to have violated an unspoken code \r\nof honesty and caring. What I was trying to say, <I>all I \r\nwas trying to say,</I> was TRUTH IS FICTION.  Still, at \r\nthis point it seems rather pointless to go on with all this \r\ndithering and reiteration because I guess the point has \r\nbeen firmly rammed into the non-fertile soil and itâ€™s \r\npretty obvious to all of us what has been said, and there \r\nwill be no conversation consisting of a give-and-take of \r\nideas, but proclamations and high-minded \r\ndissertation.  The mighty JoJo half-baffles me and, if my \r\ndictionary wasnâ€™t across the room somewhere, under a \r\npile of pizza boxes, I would be harvesting a \r\nembarrassment of new words from JoJoâ€™s clever \r\nposts.\r\n\r\n<p>Weebâ€™s last post on this â€œthreadâ€ is â€œyouâ€™re just full \r\nof hot air, see jojoâ€™s remarks.â€  I assume she is \r\naddressing me, though this is not clear without a \r\nsalutation of some sort (â€œsalutationâ€ is clearly the wrong \r\nword in this context, but Iâ€™m left rather grappling for a \r\nword that sounds smart and witty, because even \r\nthough it is WAY out of my league, I feel the need to \r\nâ€œcompeteâ€ with jojo).  But I guess I agree with Weeb.  \r\nThis discussion has gone in completely the wrong \r\ndirection from what I intended and now feels more like \r\na wrestling match in which there are lots of posturing \r\nand flexing muscles, even some body slams and \r\npummeling, but nobody ever really gets hurt.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4773,1222,'2001-06-28 13:07:18',0,'bmg','<p>I would comment right now, but i gotta go study over at \r\nspider house...</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4774,1222,'2001-06-28 13:36:12',0,'jo','ben whipped out his willy in the parking lot of <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/benpinesforaustin.txt\r\n\\\">trudy\\\'s</a>, so he\\\'s safe.<BR>\r\nit\\\'s true that i\\\'ve been hit on at a spider house, but \r\nnever by the boys that i wish would hit on me.<BR>\r\ngirls, act for yourselves! make a little cardboard sign for \r\nyour own table that says, \\\"studying please! go away!\\\" don\\\'t \r\ndrag in management. sheesh.',1,0,0,0,'172.158.38.204','en'),(4775,1218,'2001-06-28 14:25:46',0,'woof','\\\"If love is Aces, give me the Jack.\\\"',5,0,0,0,'12.110.65.48','en'),(4776,1222,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Prim Moll','<p>There is a place like this kind of near me. It is called \r\nCafe 52. I don\\\'t like it but sometimes there is no where \r\nelse to go.</p>\r\n<p>I like going there during the day when no one is there. \r\nOn rainy days I sit outside and no one is there and I drink \r\nhot choco and watch the rain and force myself to read \r\nboring things that I can\\\'t focus on anywhere else.</p>\r\n<p>At night it is crowded. Boisterous people consort with \r\none another. It is all too foolish. Improper conduct.</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4777,675,'2001-06-28 15:14:23',0,'danielle','did you guys take out mail boxes with garbage cans too?\r\nhehe',1,0,0,0,'131.232.18.99','en'),(4778,1219,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Veer Moll','<p>We are sending a squad over to the planet presently, \r\nsir.</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4779,1218,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Priss Moll','<p>oi it make me glad i\\\'m a eunuch</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4780,1222,'2001-06-28 15:32:39',0,'danielle','egg: I don\\\'t understand why, if a girl is being hit on, she \r\ncan\\\'t just stand up for herself and just tell the person to \r\nfuck off. \r\n\r\nhow is a guy to know that a girl isn\\\'t interested? what if a \r\nguy sees a girl and is honestly interested or attracted to \r\nher? he can\\\'t express this without it being sexual \r\nharrassment? there are certain context\\\'s that i could see \r\nthis in, such as if my boss was interested me and wouldn\\\'t \r\nlay off or whatever.. but a guy in a coffee shop can\\\'t tell \r\na girl she\\\'s cute?\r\n\r\nhmmm.',3,0,0,0,'131.232.18.99','en'),(4781,1214,'2001-06-28 15:34:23',0,'jojo the unimcunible','baby, baby, don\\\'t be like that.  i was just playing.<br><br>\r\ni\\\'m sorry i went all mediaeval-Scholastic on your ass.  i \r\nthought it was amusing, to adopt such a tyrannically \r\npedantic attitude about a nominatively \\\"open field\\\" \r\ndiscourse -- pretensions to \\\"objectivity\\\" really revealing \r\nthe commentor to be an ass, <i>viz</i> Nabokov\\\'s \\\"Pale \r\nFire.\\\"  it was meant to be obvious bullshit.  boys don\\\'t \r\nget my jokes, i don\\\'t know why.  *pout*<br><br><br>\r\ni think this is a kinda provocative issue, or at least a \r\nfunny topic that avails soapboxers much opportunity declaim \r\non this and that.  and i <i>love</i> this and that.',2,0,0,0,'209.242.148.3','en'),(4782,1222,'2001-06-28 15:55:10',0,'mscreant','i never got hit on at spider house. *pout*',2,0,0,0,'66.25.186.181','en'),(4783,1222,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>we had that same problem in the 24-hour reading room in \r\nmy college library.  hitting on people wasn\\\'t the only \r\nthing that happened there, either- yikes!</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4784,1222,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to danielle','<p>I think if you\\\'re intuitive enough to read signals from \r\npeople than you can tell what\\\'s proper and what\\\'s not in \r\nthose settings. If Im reading a book and somebodys \r\nlike, yo whutsup, I am capable of saying, get away from \r\nme-- yeah. but at the library for instance, thats just not \r\nusually suave behavoir, yo. </p>\r\n<p>I also dont know who would put up such a sign, \r\nsince all the ladies <i>here</i> seem to love getting hit \r\non. but if somebody cared enough to raise the issue, \r\ncould it be a problem?</p>\r\n<p>ill never post a comment to this article again, but Id \r\nalso like to add that if spider house serves alcohol then \r\nit would be dorky to try to study there.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.93.231','en'),(4785,1215,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Sodi\\\'ch Moll','<p>Antarctica, Antarctica, oh god. I get crazy thinking \r\nabout the Antarctica. Ohhh, even the word is beautiful, I \r\nwant to touch it, the i and the c and the a. If there were \r\nonly some way to live there. It must be so quiet! I wish I \r\ndidn\\\'t start thinking about it. I ache.</p>\r\n  ',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4786,1219,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<BR><BR>0OOOOORRRRRRRRIIIIIIIIIOOOOOOOOONNNNNNNNNN\r\n<BR>\r\nwhatup dog.  i am having a trepan seminar today in the\r\nbathroom.  you  should come check it out.  it might improve\r\nyour life.',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.80','en'),(4787,1220,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to s5','<P>excuse me, COULD?</P>\r\n<P>listen here buddy, i can grow facial hair!</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.80','en'),(4788,1222,'2001-06-28 17:12:41',0,'danielle','egg: i know you wont reply but i just have one last thing to \r\nsay. i think its all about context and hey, some people are \r\nretarded and can\\\'t pick up social cues even if they hit them \r\nin the face. if you\\\'re at the library, sure, valid point, \r\ndont be hitting on people. duh! but a coffee house is more \r\nof a social setting, right. yah.. thats all :o)<P>I never \r\nunderstood the whole studying in a coffee house/bar thing \r\neither. i had friends who did it all throught high school \r\nand i think the only thing they learnt was how to blow smoke \r\nrings.',1,0,0,0,'131.232.18.91','en'),(4789,1223,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i love you, auntie. when you come into town next, i\\\'m \r\nmaking you cookies.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4790,1220,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i am je t\\\'amoured of the word \\\"nozzles\\\"',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4791,1222,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','The bathrooms at my university are <b>legendary</b> for\r\ncruising.  There is a site dedicated to cruising, run out of\r\nSF, that lists <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://www.cruisingforsex.com/listings.php?o=30&m_id=TORONTO&m_name=Toronto&r_id=ON-CA&r_name=CANADA%20-%20Ontario&d_sex%5B%5D=CTO\\\">bathrooms\r\n</a> in my school.  There was an article on this in the\r\nValentines Day issue of the school paper (<i>The Love & Sex\r\nIssue</i>).<br><br>I read this article and then went to an\r\nS&M tutorial before going to an A.I. lecture that day.  What\r\na world we live in.',1,0,0,0,'206.47.244.62','en'),(4792,856,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>flower pictures are a complete genre of photography, me\r\nthinks.\r\n</P><P>\r\n<a href=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/\">Viv at\r\nfirstpersonparticular</a> is really good at flower fotos.\r\n<P><a\r\nhref=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/062301.html\"><img\r\nsrc=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/pics/20010623_hoyaflower.jpg\"></a>\r\n\r\n<P>and her cat emily.</P>\r\n<P><a\r\nhref=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/062301.html\"><img\r\nsrc=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/pics/20010623_butterwouldntmelt.jpg\"></a></P>\r\n\r\n<P>she is also good at posting cute picturs of kissing her\r\nhusband persifal.</P>\r\n\r\n<P><a\r\nhref=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/062601.html\"><IMG\r\nSRC=\"http://firstpersonparticular.com/archive/2001/pics/20010624_parsifalandviv1.jpg\"></a></P>\r\n\r\n<P>and she writes really well.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and she is not a dumb twentysomethingcuss like most\r\npeople i know.</P>',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.59','en'),(4793,1222,'2001-06-28 18:25:57',0,'bmg','<p>i just found out that there is a topless barber shop \r\nhere in austin, named \\\"sexy scissors.\\\" Apparently, people \r\ngo there to get their hairs cut, while enjoying toppless \r\nservice... i wonder if you can pay to watch other people \r\ngetting haircuts?</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4794,1220,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','the australians were mystified by the word \\\"spiggot\\\"',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(4795,1221,'2001-06-28 18:38:21',0,'kat','i was having sex. but it started getting boring. id rather \r\njust go solo than put up with that. i can only dream of \r\nfinding a guy who\\\'d be willing to wear a pink tutu while \r\ngiving it to me. ',4,0,0,0,'12.64.44.41','en'),(4796,1223,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','auntie: thanks, I needed to hear that. much of the time I \r\ncreate, people seem to get great joy of taking the wind out \r\nof my sails when I actually do decide to share something. I \r\nhave never ever been able to figure out why people enjoy \r\nthis. Now I know how to deal with them, but still, it \r\nhurts. To Darwin: Thank you too, I fucking LOVE that quote. \r\nI can even hear the Bill Laswell bassline behind his voice.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4797,1220,'2001-06-28 18:42:54',0,'kat','the mojo hardly ever stinks. she cleans herself like a cat \r\nconstantly. i love rubbing my face in her fur and smelling \r\nher doggy smell. i dont even mind it when she gives me \r\nkisses [sans toungue action]',1,0,0,0,'12.64.44.41','en'),(4798,1218,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','ANNE MAGNUSON : BONG WATER : ROCK',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4799,1216,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','for fuck\\\'s sake, stop quoting the goddamn bible. Le, love \r\ncan conquer all of those things, but it is _self_ love, not \r\nlove from someone else. This may seem like cliched boring \r\ncrap you may not want to hear, but take a look at me and \r\nrealize the change you see is from learning to love all \r\nparts of myself, ESPECIALLY the burning angry self loathing \r\npart that wants to trip me up and laugh at me, then kick me \r\nin the teeth for trying to get up. It is such a difficult \r\nprocess, this learning to love yourself, to contain the \r\nlove within yourself instead of tossing it out at others \r\nhoping they will throw the ball back at you, but coated in \r\nchocolate and puppydogs and picket fences. I swear on the \r\nbible.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4800,1218,'2001-06-28 18:58:26',0,'leonard to egg','i luv me some rasburry jelly donuts.',6,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(4801,1212,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','RUSH : FUCKIN MATH ROCK!',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4802,1222,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','the latin american library at the university of florida is \r\n(was, anyway) a notorious gay pickup spot (for guys). it \r\nwas assumed that if you were a male and were there after \r\nnine p.m. or so, you weren\\\'t interested in garcia marquez \r\nor whatever. i think context determines a lot of this \r\nstuff. and instinct. you can tell when somebody wants to \r\nget hit on and when they want to be left the fuck alone. \r\nbody language and that.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(4803,1212,'2001-06-28 19:49:17',0,'danielle','math rock can be so boring. i like it better when shit is \r\nmade up on the spot and the musicians screw up and \r\nmake the music interesting. ',1,0,0,0,'207.34.94.238','en'),(4804,1222,'2001-06-28 19:56:44',0,'puppy','bmg wrote:<blockquote>there is a topless barber shop here in\naustin, named \\\"sexy scissors\\\"...</blockquote>\n\n<p>*scratches bald head* this might be discrimination... how\ncome all the people with hair have all the fun?\n\n<p>btw, i second the great veggie chili @ spider house.',0,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(4805,1216,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Hedaijha Moll','<p>fuq the bible: <i>At tlatha i\\\'gars an sucht turnn at \r\ntlatha i\\\'akek an sucht. -- at thal az sucht\\\'e i\\\'an</i></p>\r\n<p>\\\"To be able to lose a thing is to be able to value it\\\" \r\n(sorta) -- the first Law of Things</p> ',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4806,1221,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hi.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4807,1201,'2001-06-28 21:04:59',0,'Jojo','It\\\'s 16 million o\\\'clock.  It\\\'s Thursday.  I made myself a \r\npromise, and my goddesses I shall keep it.\r\n\r\nFare well.',6,0,0,0,'209.242.148.3','en'),(4808,1221,'2001-06-28 21:05:22',0,'adam','revealing: maybe.  cryptic: yes.  austin, you\\\'re so... \r\nyou.<br>\r\n\\\"best friendly\\\"\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(4809,1221,'2001-06-28 21:35:03',0,'danielle','i think my boyfriend would wear a tutu. im not sure id \r\nwant him to though. maybe a cowboy hat or something.',1,0,0,0,'207.34.94.238','en'),(4810,1226,'2001-06-28 22:16:45',0,'dahlia','ahhhhh kali. she who grants liberation by tearing off the \r\nveil of illusion. back in time a ways when i was in the \r\nthrall of kali, i wrote her this:\r\n\r\nholy mother, do not be tamed\r\nspring forth from my head, roll out\r\nyour sacred bloody tongue.',2,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(4811,1220,'2001-06-28 22:53:52',0,'s5 to austin','<br><br>i haven\'t shaved in a week and my facial hair \r\nis now thick enough to pet. i wonder if dogs envy that \r\nabout people. just like how we envy their ability to lick \r\ntheir own genitals.<br>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\nrowan to bowser : lemme tell you dawg. if my wrists \r\ncould do that, i\'d pet myself allll day long.\r\n</tt></pre>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4812,1227,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<a \r\nhref=\"http://www.ilovelulu.com\">http://www.ilovelulu.com</a>',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4813,1227,'2001-06-28 23:29:28',0,'pepe','el stupido senor griece....senor griece?',0,0,0,0,'209.178.146.112','en'),(4814,1229,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\\\'ll share with you my today\\\'s hooray: papaya. the sneaky \nway it smooves and \ngrooves you, and doesn\\\'t turn on it\\\'s husky charm til the \nend of the chewing cycle. mmmmmmm, the swallowing.\n\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4815,1228,'2001-06-29 01:03:39',0,'romano','IMHO, Radiohead sux.  It is not good music, and anyone who \r\nthinks it is should call the police about their missing \r\ntaste or their missing soul.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.142','en'),(4816,1229,'2001-06-29 01:24:08',0,'jo','yeah. i like you a lot.',1,0,0,0,'172.150.198.53','en'),(4817,1229,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\nwords and places in sf that please me:<br>\r\nN JUDAH<br>\r\ntire beach<br>\r\nsutro<br>\r\npronouncing lyon st all frenchy in my head<br>\r\ntreat st.<br>\r\nlucky alley<br>\r\nbalmy alley<br>\r\nalvarado<br>\r\nnaps II<br>\r\nthe 8 market<br>\r\nthe 37 corbutt<br>\r\nwawona<br>\r\nquintara<br>\r\nland\\\'s end\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(4818,1228,'2001-06-29 02:28:51',0,'karmapoliceman','dear romano,\r\n I beg to differ. I think it is amazing music. It is a \r\nmatter of Taste, not the soul. to be so \\\"let me look down \r\non these radiohead people\\\" is nasty.',3,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(4819,1229,'2001-06-29 03:02:28',0,'auntie','i still want to know the name of those trees in front of \r\nthe civic center.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwords from sf that make me feel lovely:<br><br>\r\n\r\nelly<br>\r\nrabat<br>\r\npeter<br>\r\ntuggy<br>\r\ncafe flores<br>\r\ncoffee<br>\r\nfive<br>\r\njim and christie<br>\r\nxep and eric<br>\r\nwalking<br>\r\ndogs tied to things outside stores<br>\r\nthose nameless trees<br>\r\nbeauteous colors on doors and houses<br>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(4820,1229,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','those trees are like the bald rex cats.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4821,1229,'2001-06-29 03:09:56',0,'wb','what about \\\"stan-yan\\\" or \\\"gough\\\", the tenderloin. what a \r\ngreat word that is; that somehow predicts what it became. \r\nnob hill. \r\n\r\nviva this city yes yes yes.',2,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(4822,1229,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<br><br>\r\ni\'ve always wanted to live on church street and wake up \r\nto wine glasses, clinking against each other in the \r\nkitchen cupboards, as the J rumbles by every \r\nmorning.<br><br>\r\nwhen i lived by the van ness stop, i had a game where i \r\nwould guess the next trains over the loudspeaker.<br>\r\n<pre><tt>\r\napproaching. van ness. two cars. K. L.\r\n</tt></pre>',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4823,1230,'2001-06-29 04:04:02',0,'jo','when i was a kid, my mom was in therapy to get over being \r\ncodependent and painfully shy.<BR>\r\nit worked too well!<BR>\r\ni have vivid memories of her demanding respect from \r\nsalespeople at foley\\\'s and penney\\\'s.<BR>\r\ni would crawl into the clothing racks, sit in the very \r\nmiddle of them, and run my fingers up and down the cold \r\nmetal squareness of the rods until she was done.<BR>\r\nshe also started conversations with strangers! oh horror! \r\nshe was so delighted to be in texas (having grown up in \r\njersey and penn) where lots of strangers make polite \r\nconversation in elevators and grocery lines that she did it \r\nall the time.<BR>\r\nat the time, it was embarassing and awful but now i know \r\nthat she\\\'s rad. and i feel like my mom everytime i lean \r\nacross a coffehouse counter to tell the counter girl how \r\nmuch i like her pants.',1,0,0,0,'172.150.220.68','en'),(4824,856,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>\r\nwhen my grandmother, whom i was very close to, died almost \r\na month ago, i took photos of the flowers that i was given. \r\ni miss my nanna.<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.starla.co.nz/flowers/9a.jpg\\\"><BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://www.starla.co.nz/flowers\\\">flowers</A>',2,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(4825,1229,'2001-06-29 04:24:39',0,'jo to simon','i take special delight in pronouncing the names of the \r\nstreets and districts in new orleans by their correct \r\nfrench pronunciation in my head.<BR>\r\noutloud, i still pronounce chatres st as \\\"charters,\\\" to \r\nplease the locals.',1,0,0,0,'172.150.220.68','en'),(4826,1218,'2001-06-29 04:32:08',0,'wb','great post. thank god for this site unleashing a new \r\nexcellent writer to read online.',2,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(4827,1234,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','if marijuana were legal, you would probably mail order \r\nit from places like <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://greenstranger.com/\\\">this</a>.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4828,1221,'2001-06-29 06:36:07',0,'kat to s5','woo me with a reading in a tutu and let\\\'s see what happens.',1,0,0,0,'12.64.30.10','en'),(4829,1224,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Friendly people frighten me and sometimes their \r\nfriendliness is kind of offensive. I don\\\'t consider myself \r\nfriendly, I probably strike people as cold and sort of \r\nrude, but I\\\'ll generally try to help people if it seems \r\npossible or reasonable. Of course, I was born and raised \r\nand live in New Jersey, where the skies glow pink and the \r\nrivers sometimes catch on fire. Still, I think there\\\'s \r\nsomething to be said for having low (realistic?) \r\nexpectations.</p>\r\n<p>I was once at this hippy festival thing, packed with \r\noldschool pothead burnouts and cute young kids alike, and \r\nit was during this bad heatwave. I was having trouble \r\nfinding water and I eventually got very sick, and at one \r\npoint I collapsed on the side of a trail. I was squirming \r\nand crying and kind of throwing up, obviously in pretty bad \r\nshape, and lots of people walked by. But no one helped or \r\neven asked if I was okay.</p>\r\n<p>Perhaps Joisey, my smelly, rusty mother, has taught me \r\nwell.</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4830,1234,'2001-06-29 06:49:48',0,'kat','ice cream does more for me than drugs ever did.',2,0,0,0,'12.64.30.10','en'),(4831,1234,'2001-06-29 07:08:08',0,'bmg','I wish i had the ice cream.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4832,1228,'2001-06-29 08:15:07',0,'toot','it\\\'s also important to note that mixing GHB and alcohol \r\nwill KILL YOU, so its unlikely they\\\'d be selling it at the \r\nshow. well, that is, unless they meant to kill you.',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4833,1229,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','<P>the talking bus that stops outside my window, day in, \r\nday out.</P>\r\n<P>\\\"Seventy-One Haight Noriega to 48th Avenue and Ortega.\\\" \r\nOf course it had to rhyme.</P>',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4834,1228,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i have no opinion about radiohead. i didn\\\'t relate to \r\n\\\'creep\\\'.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4835,1224,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I am the sort of person who talks to random people. I \r\noften say some shit that doesn\\\'t neccesarily make sense for \r\nrandom people to say to each other. If people don\\\'t like \r\nit, well, that\\\'s too bad.</p>\r\n<p>I also semi-frequently offer to help people on the \r\nstreet who look as if they might need help. Old ladies \r\nwalking in the rain (when I\\\'m in a car), people with too \r\nmany packages, etc. The weird thing about this is that the \r\npeople that I offer my help to almost never accept. I once \r\noffered to help this lady carry her like 30 packages, and I \r\neven said \\\"I\\\'m really not going to steal your stuff, \r\nseriously, you can hold my wallet!\\\".. I guess people just \r\ndon\\\'t believe other people want to help them.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4836,1228,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','you\'d be surprised how many people don\'t know that \r\nmixing GHB with anything is dangerous. at my \r\nhousewarming party, two idiots (who i didn\'t invite) \r\nconsumed two week\'s worth of GHB, several shots of \r\ntequila, and snorted oxycodone. they were dragged out, \r\nnot long after, by paramedics. the guy downstairs, \r\nalmost semi-conscious, insisted that the EMT called \r\nhim \"doctor love\". the other guy, the guy on the roof, died \r\nbriefly in the hospital. luckily he pulled through. i don\'t \r\nunderstand how one moment, you can be dead, then \r\nsuddenly you\'re alive again.\r\n',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4837,1232,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I am a jaded raver. It seems like it\\\'s rare when the \r\nmusic at a party makes me really lose my shit and dance \r\nlike I used to. I miss it. But, of course, it has been \r\nreplaced by other things, like playing Capoeira.. =)</p>\r\n<p>Being so lost in the moment is very cleansing.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',1,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4838,1229,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','haha yay sine.  today after eating at <i>hanabi</i> i stood\r\non <i>haight street</i> and mimicked the bus just before\r\ngoing to <i>cafe international</i>.  words:\r\n<br>\r\nyeah, elly, divisadero.\r\n<br>\r\nbelcher st\r\n<br>\r\nchurch n\\\' market\r\n<br>\r\ntelling people i lived in \\\"nob hill flats\\\" instead of the\r\ntenderloin\r\n<br>\r\nbeulah\r\n<br>\r\nbelvedere (come here, boyah, and fetch me a mint julip.\r\naaaaah magnolia blossoms.)\r\n<br>\r\nkezar kezar kezar kezar\r\n<br>\r\npronouncing guerrero \\\"GRRR rrrr ooo\\\" as i once heard a\r\ntourist do.\r\n<br>\r\nno name.  thailand.  ananda fuara.  pancho villa.  noc noc.\r\n<br>\r\n<bold>SQUAT AND GOBBLE</bold>\r\n<br>\r\nok that\\\'s enough.\r\n(snobby tobble)\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(4839,1232,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>joker... i feel you.  i liked the \\\"i need to dance all \r\nthe poison out of my mind\\\" part- i felt that once and was \r\ndropped to the dancefloor in a writhing, freaky trance, \r\ntransducing the ripping, phasing snares into a fierce \r\nassault of fist-pounds against the floor. priceless \r\ntherapy.  i\\\'d live that epiphany a million times if I \r\ncould, even with the broken watch and bruised wrists and \r\nhands.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4840,1229,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<tt>smilign is good.  no one ever smiles in nyc. ugh.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4841,1229,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<tt>smiling is good.  no one ever smiles in nyc. ugh.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4842,1229,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i always say and write \\\"san francisco\\\", instead of \\\"sf\\\", \r\njust because i like the way the name of the city looks \r\nwhen it\\\'s spelled out. i\\\'m glad that i never have to leave \r\nhere.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4843,1224,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>ooh, that is cool, especially knowing how un-nice the A \r\ntrain crowd can be sometimes, especially as it gets \r\nuptown...</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4844,1232,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the ground is softer in the desert.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4845,1232,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>yesterday i was walking up 1st avenue thinking of how \r\nmuch i like the hot weather.  i concluded, though, that i\\\'d \r\nrather hang out in a desert sometime, where its a remote, \r\ndry, nice hot, and not a humid urban stink hot. i\\\'ve never \r\nbeen to the desert.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4846,1214,'2004-02-07 03:45:28',534,'griece','To JoJo,',1,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4847,1224,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','It really is a matter of the environment.  When I was a \r\nyoung man in Idaho, I crashed my bike into a tree, and \r\nsomeone from accross the street immediately came to my \r\naid.  He took me into his house, cleaned me up, and gave me \r\nsome bandaids.  People where I grew up aren\\\'t afraid of \r\nbeing mugged or raped or murdered by their neighbors, and \r\nby extension aren\\\'t afraid to offer and/or accept a helping \r\nhand.  This is very unlike NYC, or even the wherever you \r\nare Darwin.\r\n\r\nOkay, one more anecdote, just because it\\\'s on my mind: This \r\nis in Idaho.  I had just bought a mattress at Goodwill for \r\nmy apartment, and I was walking down the street to my \r\napartment with the thing on my head when someone in a \r\npickup pulled up next to me and told me to throw the thing \r\nin the back.  This person I had never met before drove me \r\nto my place, helped me lug this thing into my bedroom, \r\nblah, blah, blah.  Small town livin\\\'.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4848,1234,'2001-06-29 14:00:11',0,'mscreant','Maybe nipple woman was afraid you would discover her \r\nBodyperksâ„¢!<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nDraw attention to your natural assets...try Bodyperksâ„¢ \r\ntoday! What is it? Let\\\'s just say it\\\'s a way to enhance the \r\nassets that you already have, and for only $20 you can\\\'t \r\nhave more fun!<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<IMG \r\nSRC=\\\"http://www.mscreant.com/images/perkynipples.jpg\\\"><BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nAnd as if that isn\\\'t bizarre enough, they come in different \r\ncolors for that \\\"natural\\\" look:<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.mscreant.com/images/peach.jpg\\\"><BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.mscreant.com/images/mocha.jpg\\\"><BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<A HREF=\\\"http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?\r\nViewItem&item=1609407248\\\">perky nipples</A>',2,0,0,0,'66.25.186.181','en'),(4849,1227,'2004-02-07 03:45:28',534,'griece','pepe es el pendejo',1,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4850,1214,'2004-02-07 03:45:28',534,'griece','<p>What I meant to say before I clicked the wrong \r\nbutton is that I\\\'m kinda bored here and swinney.org \r\nnow that griece has lost his edge.  It\\\'s rather boring to \r\npost diary entries and such.  I dunno.  ',6,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4851,1229,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>Whenever I see the intials \\\'sf\\\' the first thought in my \r\nhead is \\\'SciFi,\\\' quickly followed by \\\'science fiction.\\\'  \r\nThen, hours later, I say to myself, \\\"Oh yeah, <I>San \r\nFrancisco</I>.\\\"</P><P>Peter: I\\\'m in NYC and I smile.',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4852,1228,'2001-06-29 15:18:29',0,'danielle','I bought Ok Computer. I\\\'ve listened to it so many times. \r\nWhenever I hear any song off it, I think of grade 12. I\\\'m \r\nnot sure how I feel about that. Anyways, Kid A came out and \r\nI bought it, hoping to have the same affect on me that Ok \r\nComputer did. <P> I bought it when it came out and its still \r\nin its plastic wrapping.<P> I think I\\\'m becomming apathetic \r\nabout music. Several of my \\\"favorite\\\" bands have released \r\nnew albums recently and I haven\\\'t bothered to buy or \r\ndownload any of it. I just sit in my computer room late at \r\nnight, listening to Alkaline Trio over and over. ',2,0,0,0,'131.232.18.99','en'),(4853,69,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>do you know what time your dad was born? you \r\nknow what im thinkin....</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4854,1234,'2001-06-29 15:44:11',0,'DEA','youre busted.',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4855,1228,'2001-06-29 16:02:05',0,'romano','OK Computer was OK.  All the stuff after that has been \r\nEmperor\\\'s New Clothes, though.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.241','en'),(4856,1237,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i think i see love in his eyes.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.120','en'),(4857,1229,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i like to say LE in my head instead of elly (L-E). le like \r\nthe. i don\\\'t know. it pleases me.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(4858,1228,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>WHO the F--- is \\\"ezra\\\". i cant get into this name \r\nchanging thing. cant you just make up fictional stories \r\nabout people you know?</p>\r\n<p>I used to pretend I hated the song \\\"creep\\\". when it \r\ncame on WFNX in Boston I would listen only if \r\nsomoene was not in the car.  hillary chute wrote me a \r\npoem that said \\\"you secretly liked the song about being \r\nthe creep.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>tim kicked the singer in the head at cbgbs from the \r\npit when we was kids. the dude dedicated a song to \r\nhim. early 90s nostalgia!!! bring it on!!</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4859,1229,'2001-06-29 17:48:15',0,'caitsad','I wish I knew what it was like to live in a place I \r\nlove.<br>\r\nManhattan and I had a brief 3 month dance, with week long \r\nvisits every month before and after the summer for 2 years. \r\nI love her to death and miss her often. If I wasn\\\'t in a \r\nserious relationship, Manhattan would take me under her \r\nwing and nurture me.<br>\r\nI still want to experience California. <br>but, until \r\nthen... boston, please take good care of me.',0,0,0,0,'216.150.228.190','en'),(4860,1235,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','\\\"tangible testament to the tenative\\\"<br><br>\r\nwow, peter.  the way you write makes me question my \r\nsexuality.  when i read your poetry, there\\\'s a barrage of \r\ncascading word-colors that are every bit as fun to speak as \r\nread...<br>\r\nkudos',2,0,0,0,'66.68.48.125','en'),(4861,1235,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','mmm, shivers.  reminds me of dt\\\'s \\\"poem in october\\\" (my all \r\ntime fave) in parts.<br><br>\r\n\\\"the rain wringing wind blew cold in the wood far away \r\nunder me\\\"',2,0,0,0,'66.68.48.125','en'),(4862,1229,'2001-06-29 18:36:37',0,'jo to simon','YES!@ i also do this. especially with her IM name because \r\nit\\\'s \\\"le elly.\\\" which i read not as \\\"elly elly\\\" but \\\"luh \r\nelly,\\\" THE ELLY.<BR>\r\nhi els.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(4863,1229,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>Yeah.... le.... I\\\'ve been say \\\'lay\\\' in my head everytime \r\nI see it.  In fact, I didn\\\'t make the connection (L-E, Elly)\r\nuntil it was thrust upon me.  Whatever, I don\\\'t even know \r\nthe person, so I\\\'m not sure if it really \r\nmatters.</P><P>Anyways, Caitsad, Cape Cod was the place for \r\nme.  Sitting out on the deck enjoying a good beer watching \r\nthe sun set over the harbor.  Working as a ballroom dance \r\ninstructor and a pizza delivery man.  Living in a small \r\nroom with my best friend in a building lovingly refered to \r\nas \\\"the crack house.\\\"  Sometimes living on $15 a week.  It \r\nwas definiately a love/hate relationship; I remember hating \r\nit the entire time I was there, but when I reminisce, I \r\nonly feel the love.',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4864,1229,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<p>what a lovely post and set of comments. it makes me sad \r\ntoday however because my girlfriend and i have begun the \r\nserious discussions of moving away, to some place where we \r\nmight still be able to make a decent living and afford rent \r\nat the same time. nothing\\\'s set in stone yet but I find \r\neven <i>talking</i> about leaving this city washes me in \r\nsoft blue heartache. oh oh oh oh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have been living in SF for ten years now and it has \r\ngradually become the hometown I never had. When I first \r\nstarted writing here I was going to do a post \r\ncalled \\\"decade\\\" where I\\\'d run through a few of my favorite \r\nmemories, places, things, sounds. then the idea seemed \r\nsilly so I abandoned it. but here we are, le\\\'s beaten me to \r\nit. perhaps i should do this post anyway, just so it can be \r\nsaid.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m glad you were feeling good yesterday, le!</p> ',0,0,0,0,'205.186.177.181','en'),(4865,1228,'2001-06-29 18:51:42',0,'jay','1) why did the name of the band in question need to get \r\nmentioned and argued over? that wasn\\\'t the point, people.<p>\r\n2) egg: I like pseudonyms. They work for me.',3,0,0,0,'63.203.225.62','en'),(4866,1234,'2001-06-29 18:56:31',0,'liver','<br>\r\nat a bar in tokyo some friends of friends were talking \r\nabout a product they sell there called \\\"nippless.\\\"  it\\\'s \r\nspecifically meant for situations like the one above when \r\nyou don\\\'t want to wear a bra, but you don\\\'t want to flaunt \r\nthe fact that you have nipples.\r\n<p>\r\nas they were sitting around, drinking beer and laughing at \r\nthe sillyness of stealth nipples, i found myself wondering \r\nwhether i should ask where i could find these nipplesses.  \r\never since i got my nipples pierced and realized that some \r\nof my favorite shirts are similarly revealing, i\\\'ve \r\ndiscovered that there are days when i don\\\'t want to walk \r\naround the office looking like i have six erect nipples.\r\n<p>\r\nfortunately, the japanese have come up with a solution for \r\nthis.  the nippless!  i will put them on my bust for \r\nswimming, dancing and t-shirt time!\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.moistpanties.org/nippless.jpg\\\">\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.moistpanties.org/nippless2.jpg\\\">\r\n',3,0,0,0,'64.81.72.119','en'),(4867,1222,'2001-06-29 19:05:44',0,'jay to egg:','[on the use of the word \\\'fags\\\']-- I understand you and your \r\npals on chat like to use this casually, as a general \r\nmoniker, ironically perhaps, and I don\\\'t have a problem \r\nwith that. What you find amusing is your business. However, \r\nwhen used in print, in a context like this, it\\\'s easy for a \r\nreader unfamiliar with your lingo to misunderstand the \r\nmanner in which you\\\'re using this word.  You could easily \r\nbe calling bmg and his buddies fags, not \\\'fags.\\\' So I say, \r\nlet\\\'s not be misunderstood.',3,0,0,0,'63.203.225.62','en'),(4868,1222,'2001-06-29 19:06:47',0,'jay to bmg & others:','i think if bmg says this has been a popular pick-up joint, \r\nyou gotta take his word for it, and there ain\\\'t nothing \r\nwrong with that. If you want a quiet place to study, you \r\nshould go to the library, ladies.',3,0,0,0,'63.203.225.62','en'),(4869,1222,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to jay','<p>hi. youre right. the word \\\"fag\\\" has often given me \r\npause because it refers to the Inquisition when gays \r\nwere used as burning sticks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and thats no joke. aint nuthin to forget.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4870,1228,'2001-06-29 19:11:48',0,'jay to egg (again)','P.S. Is \\\'egg\\\' not a pseudonym that just gets used a little \r\nmore regularly. And since you use it in day-to-day life, \r\nhow do you separate fiction from the real?',3,0,0,0,'63.203.225.62','en'),(4871,1228,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>honey baby love muffin sugar bear, I dont seperate \r\nfiction from the real! thats why im so loveable and \r\ndifficult to make do stuff.</p>\r\n<p>thats my excuse at least.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4872,1236,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>I was the embodiment of nerd when I was in high school, \r\nso I missed out on the whole fucking in the back seat of a \r\ncar era in my life.  It\\\'s funny that even though I know \r\nit\\\'s nothing special, and I have much better options \r\navailable to me, I still feel like I missed out on \r\nsomething.  In fact, I once almost had sex in my sister\\\'s \r\ncar just to have the experience, but my girlfriend at the \r\ntime had already been there, done that, and didn\\\'t want to \r\nrelive those memories.',3,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4873,1228,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','jay, welcome to swinney.org. where the comments \r\nhave but a fleeting connection to the posts.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4874,1222,'2001-06-29 20:48:50',0,'bmg','you can call me a fag, i don\\\'t care.',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4875,1237,'2001-06-29 20:52:50',0,'bmg','i\\\'m proud of you griece; good job. <br>\r\n\\\'best friendly \\\'',1,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4876,1229,'2001-06-29 21:03:07',0,'annette','\\\'Luh\\\'? \\\'Lay\\\'? \\\'El-ee\\\'? I\\\'ve always been say(think)ing \r\nit \\\'lee\\\'. Gosh. How oblivious am I. ',2,0,0,0,'202.180.121.2','en'),(4877,1229,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','don\\\'t worry, everyone does it. besides, oblivion is a \r\npositive characteristic. chaotic good.\r\n<br><br>\r\nfrot n hobble.',0,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(4878,1237,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Poll Moll','<p>i feelz all warm inside like gooey nutellaglob</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4879,1237,'2001-06-29 22:21:52',0,'truthfan','It sucks that he had the foresight to wear that helmet to \r\nthe bar with you and still ended up getting his teeth \r\nknocked out.  I guess it\\\'s hard to quaff pints with a \r\nmouthpiece... ',3,0,0,0,'12.75.41.145','en'),(4880,1222,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to bmg','um. i think you missed the point mang.',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.186','en'),(4881,1222,'2001-06-30 00:29:40',0,'bmg','oh? i often do.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4882,1222,'2001-06-30 01:33:39',0,'darwin to egg','<p>Can you provide a reference for the \\\"fag inquisition\\\" \r\nlink? I\\\'ve heard this out of a number of people\\\'s mouths \r\nbut I\\\'ve never heard a single reference for the validity of \r\nthe statement. Suffice to say, a link from 16th century \r\nSpain to modern English seems pretty implausible to me. It \r\nseems too pat and fits too directly with modern \r\nhomophobia.. FWIW, I searched the web and found no such \r\nreference.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4883,1230,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\nmoms are so great for this sort of thing.<br>\r\nmy mom\\\'s laugh can be heard for blocks.<br>\r\nbut the best part was when my little sister was thirteen and \r\neverything any of us did made her DIE of embarassment.<br>\r\nexample: sparrowe (my mom), megs and i were driving down the \r\n5 to san diego and it was super super hot and sticky, no A/C, \r\nspeeding down the highway, listening to prince. and sparrowe \r\nsees a field with a fence around it and SPRINKLERS! so she \r\npulls over and she and i jump out of the car and run, hopping \r\nthe fence and yelping, into the sprinklers. my sister sat in \r\nthe car, red faced, and died a little embarassed overheated \r\n13 year old death. and that is not even the worst of it. we \r\nstripped off our shirts and dug around in the back of the car \r\nfor new shirts and truckers on the overpass were honking and \r\nwaving and so we just laughed and waved back and megan sat in \r\nthe car with her hand over her eyes and some sweaty piece of \r\nhair on her forehead.<br>\r\nheehee!\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(4884,1222,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<br><br>\r\n<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.wordorigins.org/wordorf.htm#Faggot\\\">\r\nhttp://www.wordorigins.org/wordorf.htm#Faggot</a><br\r\n>\r\n<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=fag\r\ngot\\\">http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=fag\r\ngot</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nand some commentary:<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.wordwizard.com/clubhouse/founddisc\r\nuss.asp?Num=1284\\\">http://www.wordwizard.com/club\r\nhouse/founddiscuss.asp?Num=1284</a><br>\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4885,1222,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>hmm. maybe some freshmen don\'t know that they\'re setting \r\nthemselves up to be bothered in that cafe and justifiably \r\nwant to be left alone. when i think about cafes, (and sure, \r\ni don\'t know this one, and would prolly avoid all scamz0r \r\ncheezfesty places) i think: peace, quiet, and soy lattes. i \r\nthink it\'s that attitude of \"she should know that she\'s \r\ngonna get hit on\" that could be unintentionally dangerous \r\nfor women. have some respect. not all girls like or even want to get hit upon by most guys that do that sort of thing in cafes. unless she\'s winking at you and licking her chops...\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4886,1229,'2001-06-30 04:32:57',0,'alana','i am late and i wish i was still living on shotwell in that \r\napartment watching the colourful school with that cat and \r\nthat friend but instead i shall just wait until my next \r\nadventure there and know that it was good to have lived \r\nthere for a while. thank you for the reminder, elly.',3,0,0,0,'203.108.0.57','en'),(4887,1238,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','in-cho-co-late!<br>\r\n*dips dahlia in chocolate*<br>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4888,1222,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<i>testify sister!</i> as a hetero man living in the castro \r\ndistrict of san francisco, i have all the perspective i need \r\non this one ... eof.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4889,1229,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','<P>essemar:</P>\r\n<P>you would definitely like my pal ufo!. check out <a \r\nhref=\"http://www.xlr8r.com/issue/50/story3/index.html\">this</a> where he talks about sf and what it means to him.</P>\r\n<P>on the way into the city:<BR>\r\n\"seventy-one haight noriega to market and second \r\nstreet.\"</P>\r\n<P>Also, divisadero. MMM. Phucket. I miss spaghetti \r\nwestern. trocadero. club deco.</P>',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4890,1229,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','hi. duh. don\'t talk and type at the same time, sine. \r\nsomeone fix that?\r\n<p>\r\n<i>fixed. --editor</i>\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4891,1222,'2001-06-30 07:35:56',0,'bmg','<p>okay... just a little background on this place. it is a \r\nnice cafe nestled in the university campus area. they serve \r\nalcohol, and they do have great chile. my 48 year old uncle \r\ngoes there to pick up on the young men. a girl i know has \r\nonly dated men that she has met there. the difference \r\nbetween this place and a dance club is that, for the most \r\npart, the clients that go there are laid back, agreable \r\ngentle people (much like us here, right?). this place has a \r\ngood atmosphere and a community appeal to it. i go there \r\nalone mostly, and nearly every time i go there i end up \r\ntalking to some person i don\\\'t know. sometimes i initiate a \r\nconversation, sometimes not. that doesnt mean that i was \r\nhitten on them, nor they me. sometimes conversations start \r\nand end on just a friendly note. i think (my opinion) that \r\npeople think they are getting hit on when they aren\\\'t, i \r\nknow its happened to me and others thought i was doing it \r\nto them when there was nothing there... this shit leads \r\nnowhere... you cant pick up on someone in a bar, you cant \r\npick up on someone in a cafe, you cant pick up someone \r\nonline, you cant pick up anywhere... its all \r\ntaboo.</p><p>women should pick up on men, not the other way \r\nround. we men obviously don\\\'t know where to get any.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4892,1235,'2001-06-30 08:18:18',0,'jo','i walked very very slowly from my mailbox to my apartment \r\ntoday, completely wrapped up in reading peter postcard \r\nprose.<BR>\r\nyou are gifted, gifted.',1,0,0,0,'172.138.168.136','en'),(4893,1240,'2001-06-30 08:34:15',0,'moebius the perfectionist','<br>oy, screwed up the line breaks there. ought to look \r\nlike this:</br>\r\n\r\n<p><center><tt>She said: What is history?<br>\r\nAnd he said: History is an angel<br>\r\nblowing backwards into the future<br>\r\nHe said: History is a pile of debris<br>\r\nAnd the angel wants to go back and fix things<br>\r\nTo repair the things that have been broken<br>\r\nBut there is a storm blowing from Paradise<br>\r\nAnd the storm keeps blowing the angel<br>\r\nbackwards into the future.<br>\r\nAnd this storm, this storm<br>\r\nis called<br>\r\nProgress.<tt></center></p>',0,0,0,0,'205.184.149.221','en'),(4894,1240,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le channelling kristen hersh','<i>strange angels make this planet glow</i>',4,0,0,0,'64.51.232.102','en'),(4895,1240,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>KRISTEN HERSCH IS FROM RHODE ISLAND.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',2,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4896,1240,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','once i had a friend named michael who lived in seattle.  he\r\ntook the train out to iowa to visit.  he looked a lot like\r\njohn lennon.  on the train he said he grew very frustrated\r\nbecause he was annoyed at the people sitting behind him. \r\nthey were talking talking talking the whole time and it just\r\nsounded all bad so to spite them he farted as much as\r\npossible.  the problem was the air circulation machines on\r\ncross country (and maybe all) amtrak trains are too powerful\r\nto allow a healthy fart to travel more than a foot or so. \r\nwe met him at the train station and in the car on the way\r\nback he offered us crackers and mustard.  he rode a bmw.  an\r\nearly 80s r100, i think.  once upon a time michael got fed\r\nup with living in the city.  he decided he wanted nothing to\r\ndo with society anymore so he went to montanna.  he lived in\r\nthe woods, off the land.  trapping animals to eat and wear\r\nand sleeping in piles of pine needles.  he called this\r\n\\\"going abo\\\" as in aboriginal.  his beard grew long and he\r\nlost touch with reality somewhat.  this went on for about a\r\nyear until one night, sleeping in the middle of a grove of\r\ntrees he had a dream.  the dream was that a giant helicopter\r\nwas flying over his grove, huge spot lights glaring down. \r\nthe helicopter shouted down at him, through the megaphone\r\nand lights and wind and noise and it said \\\"GO BACK TO THE\r\nCITY.  MICHAEL, GO BACK TO THE CITY!\\\"  so he did.  he went\r\nback to seattle and became a  motorcycle mechanic.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(4897,1228,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<br>hey, I was there, and more-or-less enjoyed the show \r\nfrom my lofty perch in the lawn sectrion. but I had pretty \r\nmuch all the same complaints (minus the GHB scare). \r\nthat \\\"well known band\\\" is one of my favorites, but the \r\nexperience as a whole solidified my belief that someday \r\nsoon they\\\'re going to incorporate the Shoreline Ampitheater \r\nconcert experience (plus commute traffic jam) into one of \r\nthe nine circles of Hell. </br> ',2,0,0,0,'204.30.201.136','en'),(4898,1239,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>The Goddess of Victory is obviously waving her panties \r\nat you.</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4899,1238,'2001-06-30 23:22:09',0,'chaya','hey so i\\\'m a little late BUT.  dahlia.  this made me grin \r\nreally big.  a lot.  ',1,0,0,0,'158.252.216.131','en'),(4900,1239,'2001-07-01 06:26:04',0,'cait','time to play QUEST FOR GLORY 2: TRIAL BY FIRE!@$',0,0,0,0,'216.150.228.207','en'),(4901,1237,'2001-07-01 06:32:38',0,'ouch','did he just fall off his bicycle?',4,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4902,1239,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','quest for glory IV was my favorite because it had weird \r\ntransylvanian creatures and ghosts and i would play it late at \r\nnight and get all scared. in fact i still have it on 10 floppy disks. ',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4903,247,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i relate heavily to this. the gory dreams, and feeling like a medium. \r\nif these visions are no part of my personality, and they come to me \r\nin the night, they must be someone\\\'s. not mine. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4904,1242,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yay dave. sounds like toronto is shaping up to be fun. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4905,1242,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','It\\\'s currently raining in Ottawa.  It\\\'s supposed to clear \r\nup by tonight, though, so the day should be a graet Canada \r\nDay filled with much drinking insanity.<br><br>Dave, you \r\nshould be in Ottawa.  Us Toronto kids made the journey to \r\nda nashunz capito\\\' for this weekend.  I\\\'m typing this on \r\nSimon\\\'s computer... he just gave my shoulder <i>the humping \r\nof a lifetime</i> and Adam is playing Quake 3 while \r\ndrinking rum & tea.  Nerds!<br><br><br>I heard that Shimon \r\nwas good in Pittsburgh last weekend.  Shame that he didn\\\'t \r\ndeliver at Turbo.  I used to like Turbo when it was still \r\nJet and I haven\\\'t been to Turbo since it changed.  I hear \r\nmixed stories about the place (one such story being that it \r\nis just... <b>LOUD</b>).',2,0,0,0,'24.112.162.74','en'),(4906,1240,'2001-07-01 16:49:31',0,'jo','xep, you tell the best stories.',1,0,0,0,'172.160.192.9','en'),(4907,1239,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Trial By Fire was irritating because of that quasi-copy-\r\nprotection-maze thing. My favorite Hero\\\'s Quest game is \r\nstill #1.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',3,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4908,1222,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to darwin','<p>well you know, if it\\\'s not on the web, it must not be \r\nvalid.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.94.35','en'),(4909,1237,'2001-07-01 20:12:22',0,'concerned','do you only beat up little boys?',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4910,1239,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','what was the $1000 for?',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4911,1244,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<CENTER><H1><FONT COLOR=\\\"RED\\\">HAPPY \r\nBIRTHDAY KAT</FONT></H1>\r\n<P>\\\"best friendly\\\"</P>\r\n</CENTER>',1,0,0,0,'216.175.94.35','en'),(4912,247,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','we\\\'ve had a couple of stray cats come around. they look like \r\ntwins. i feed them sardines or tuna when i can. i think \r\nthey\\\'re familiars. work goes much better when i feed them.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.162','en'),(4913,1222,'2001-07-02 01:49:20',0,'darwin to egg','<p>What is your comment here supposed to mean? \r\nNotwithstanding that you can find reference to most facts \r\n<u>somewhere</u> on the web, I\\\'m not the one who made a \r\nunsupported statement of fact. </p>\r\n<p>Why not give me your <b><u>non-web</b></u> reference? \r\nWhy not <u>back up</u> things that you say? Until then, why \r\nnot shut the hell up? I\\\'m getting tired of hearing poorly \r\nsupported neo-progressive rhetoric.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',6,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(4914,1244,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','you don\\\'t look 32 by any stretch.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4915,1244,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'m yearning for a roadtrip too. let\\\'s go. i can\\\'t drive but i\\\'m a good \r\npassenger. ',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4916,1222,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1223.txt\">DNR</a>',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4917,70,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i am taken aback.  i wrote this.  what a perfect \r\nrepresentation of what it was like living in glendale, of \r\ngoing to work.</P>\r\n<P>i just started a new job and i have not got my \r\npattern down enought that i could write it down.  but \r\nwhen i do and i write about it,  the result will be much \r\nlike what i wrote here.</P>\r\n<P>glendale was so depressing, my little two story \r\ntrailer home.  i hated being there.  i would sit and stare \r\nat the radio.  i didnt drink.  i didnt write.  i read some.  \r\nsometimes it was cold but usually not.</P>\r\n<P>in the four months that i was there, i never put \r\nsheets on the bed.  i slept on top of the bed every night.  \r\nit was not my bed, just the one that came with the \r\nfurnished apartment.  not sleeping \\\'in\\\' the bed allowed \r\nme to not feel the springs stick through the mattress.  \r\nand also not move in.  i was there temporarily, \r\nsquating.</P>\r\n<P>so much happened up and down broadway ave in \r\nglendale.  it was the street that was glendale to me.  my \r\nwork was on it.  my apartment was on it.  mario\\\'s deli \r\nwas on it.  me and leonard would walk up it to lunch.  \r\nthen back.</P>\r\n<P>i moved from glendale in december to los felize.  \r\nthat was a great place.  i caused social trouble there \r\nthough.  by the time i left, people wanted me to \r\ndisappear.  <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=\r\n509.txt\\\">i miss kiki though</a>.  i would love to have her \r\naround now.</P>',2,0,0,0,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4918,1244,'2001-07-02 04:28:16',0,'jo','i\\\'m yearning for one, too. i think that when <a \r\nhref=\\\"http://standing.ontheoutside.com/girl/\\\">melissa</a> \r\nmoves here in the fall, i might try and convince her to go \r\nto mexico on a long weekend with me. it\\\'s only like 4 hours \r\nto the border downs and another hour or so into the \r\ninterior. i could go for some kerouac-style debauchery.',1,0,0,0,'64.12.106.66','en'),(4919,1244,'2001-07-02 04:29:23',0,'jo','downs=towns.',1,0,0,0,'64.12.106.66','en'),(4920,1242,'2001-07-02 04:53:31',0,'Danielle','It was super hot in Calgary today, for Canada day. We went \r\nto a punk rock show but it was a total bust because they \r\nput on a show at the skate park with Chixdiggit. Who the \r\nhell likes chixdiggit anyways? Certanly not me. I prefer \r\nhardcore metal bands from Winnipeg and weird new wave bands \r\nfrom Vancouver. Anypoo.. we didnt even go to the skate park \r\nat the end of the night to see the lazer show. Pfft. This \r\nis shaping up to be a lame holiday.',3,0,0,0,'24.68.193.134','en'),(4921,1244,'2001-07-02 05:15:48',0,'kat','oregon, washington....victoria, canada. later this year??? \r\nwe can take the red beast.',4,0,0,0,'12.64.90.21','en'),(4922,1223,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<FONT size=\"-1\">\r\n<PRE>\r\nmysql> desc comments;\r\n+------------+---------------+------+-----+-----------+\r\n| Field      | Type          | Null | Key | Default   |\r\n+------------+---------------+------+-----+-----------+\r\n| comment_id | int(11)       |      | PRI | NULL      |\r\n| article_id | int(11)       |      |     | 0         |\r\n| timestamp  | timestamp(12) | YES  |     | NULL      |\r\n| username   | varchar(255)  | YES  |     | anonymous |\r\n| comment    | text          | YES  |     | NULL      |\r\n| nature     | int(4)        | YES  |     | NULL      |\r\n| DNR        | int(4)        | YES  |     | 0         |\r\n| ip_addr    | text          | YES  |     | NULL      |\r\n+------------+---------------+------+-----+-----------+\r\n8 rows in set (0.01 sec)\r\n</PRE></FONT>',3,0,0,1,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4923,1246,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','hi mang.',1,0,0,0,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4924,1244,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','vancouver. late july - early august. s5.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4925,1246,'2001-07-02 06:27:59',0,'tenacious d','we reign',1,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(4926,1246,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','dude, take my advice: get out of there and see the world. i\r\ngrew up in columbia and rock hill. since then i\\\'ve lived in\r\ncentral new york, california, and australia. columbia is not\r\na city, it\\\'s an armpit.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(4927,1246,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','the lizard\\\'s thicket is pretty good tho.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(4928,1241,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','me: born and raised, lakeland general hospital, july 17 \r\n1973 2:30 am. lived there 17 years, finally escaped to \r\nMiami. I go to visit sometimes and find it to be beautiful \r\nand serene and quaint rather than boring. hot as all hell, \r\nas always, unbelievably hot after you forget what it is \r\nreally like. Lots of mosquitos, frank lloyd wright, and \r\nbuildings with matching people that have NOT CHANGED since \r\nthe 50\\\'s. now there are more gagns, kids, highways, strip \r\nmalls, but still there are pockets of Lakeland that are \r\nexactly as they were when I was born. but very few.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4929,1241,'2003-07-24 15:44:23',165,'joker','3rd grade to 17 years old.  it\\\'s got the home town taint, \r\nunfortunately, for me.  i agree about many of the beautiful \r\nthings you mentioned, most especially frank lloyd wright.',1,0,0,0,'66.68.48.125','en'),(4930,1242,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>I went to free concert in Central Park in celebration of \r\nCanada Day with Cowboy Junkies headlining.  I got there a \r\nlittle late and plopped myself in a long line to get in.  \r\nFortunately (so to speak) the heavens burst forth and God \r\nspat a lougie on us.  That managed to thin the crowd \r\nconsiderably leaving only the people who really wanted to \r\nbe there... and those that didn\\\'t care about getting wet.  \r\nIt ended up being a great show until it got shut down half \r\nway through due to lightning.  so it goes... had a good \r\ntime anyways.</P>',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4931,1245,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>mmmm.... The need to work (or more importantly, the need \r\nto eat and pay rent) forces me out of bed every morning far \r\nearlier than I would like.  The knowledge that if the only \r\nthings in my life were work and TV, I would have to kill \r\nmyself forces me to go to dance class every day.  My \r\nlonliness forces me to go to clubs and events during the \r\nweekends.  My roommates force me to clean the bathroom and \r\nthe kitchen.  My room remains a pit.</P><P>I miss cool \r\nsummer nights that feel like they\\\'ll last forever.  I miss \r\nthe bright smiles of strangers.  I miss lying my head in \r\nthe lap of a young girl staring at the stars while she \r\nstrokes my hair and we share a sort of innocent \r\nsensuality.  I miss the smells and sensations of moments \r\nthat I didn\\\'t appreciate until years after the fact.</P>',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4932,1247,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>The creature that I can\\\'t make out is chasing me.  \r\nChasing me through fire doors and down corridors, and I am \r\nlaughing.  I am reveling in the exhiliration of flight and \r\nthe tension of hide and seek because I know I can\\\'t be \r\ncaught by this particular beast.</P><P>A great friend of \r\nmine once pointed out that we create our own dreams.  \r\nNightmares don\\\'t descend upon us; they well up from within \r\nus.  I stopped having bad dreams from that moment on - when \r\nshe told me this.  There\\\'s nothing inside me that I should \r\nfear.</P>',3,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4933,1247,'2001-07-02 17:06:10',0,'humph','<br>\r\ni think it <i>could</i> be all the drugs.\r\n<br>',1,0,0,0,'63.210.214.57','en'),(4934,1248,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>There has never been a point in my life when every \r\nsingle thing about it was bad.  There has always been \r\nsomething \\\'good\\\' holding me where I am or leading me \r\nsomeplace new.  In a way, this has done more to imprison me \r\nthan liberate me because all I\\\'ve done in my life is follow \r\nthe fucking carrot.  There have been a number of times when \r\nI\\\'ve thought to myself, \\\"Fuck the carrot! I\\\'m going \r\nsomewhere else!\\\"  But the carrot ends up being to damn \r\nalluring, so I follow, follow, follow.  You\\\'ve seen \\\"Fight \r\nClub,\\\" right?  Our possesions end up possessing us, period. \r\n</P><P>So, what am I getting at here?  I guess I\\\'m jealous \r\nin some warped way.  If your life is as bad as you make it \r\nout to be, then you are the free man and I am the slave.  \r\nYou have nothing holding you to where you are.  You have an \r\noppourtunity to abandon everything and everyone you know.  \r\nIf I was in your position, that\\\'s exactly what I would \r\ndo.</P>',3,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4935,1248,'2001-07-02 18:17:17',0,'jo','that\\\'s some pretty flip advice for you to give when you \r\ndon\\\'t know the particulars of these situations. it\\\'s easy \r\nfor you, a stranger, to say, \\\"sounds like everything sucks. \r\nrun away.\\\" <BR>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(4936,1248,'2001-07-02 19:17:09',0,'essemar to jo','True, true.  Honestly, the advice came from my own desire \r\nto drop everything in my life and run away, but the fact \r\nthat everything about my current situation is \\\"good\\\" (i.e. \r\ngood job, nice apartment), keeps me chained to where I am \r\nin this bizarre meta-physical sense.  I want to cut the \r\nchains, but it would feel like cutting off my own foot.  \r\n<P>The other thing is that I felt like giving advice, but \r\nwhat\\\'s the point of telling someone something they\\\'ve \r\nalready heard?  I never expect anyone to blindly follow any \r\nsort of path I would lay out for them, but I like to think \r\nthat I can point out options that someone may not have \r\nthought of.  And... I really am a little jealous.</P>',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4937,1248,'2002-04-20 20:49:51',273,'quinn','<pre>\r\nI\\\'ve adressed you complaints point for point\r\n\r\n-get a job and make some money\r\n-go there\r\n-relearn how to love\r\n-do what you can for your mother she needs you now\r\n-when you get older your father will be worse\r\n-listen to your music at a lower volume\r\n-always use the stairs\r\n-stop wasting your time with jealousy\r\n-make some friends or stop bitching about it\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-enjoy all aspects of your dream life\r\n-psychological analysis is for the weak\r\n-eat better\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-no one is hiring in the economy, get a crappy job and \r\nwait it out\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-buy Folgers.  Make do.\r\n-good for you\r\n-put your cell phone in the garbage and cancel your \r\naccount.  \r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n-learn how to meditate\r\n\r\n</pre>',1,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4938,1248,'2001-07-02 21:38:21',0,'dasher','<p>the one thing on the list that really makes me feel \r\nsomething is \\\"married but no longer in love.\\\"  the rest of \r\nthe stuff is sorta run-of-the-mill day to day tragedy \r\nstuff.</p>\r\n<p>not to trivialize your cell phone bill or being out of \r\ncoffee.  i know those things suck too.</p>\r\n<p>but.  married and not in love is a good place to start.  \r\nit is my contention that a happy fulfilling love \r\nrelationship can mitigate a lot of other horrors (fiscal, \r\nfamilial, caffiene deprivation).  and therefore, if \r\ndiscontent is not where you want to be, this is where i \r\nwould start.</p>\r\n<p>marriage and long term relationships in general are \r\nreally not about \\\"being in love.\\\" if being in love means to \r\nyou what it means to me, then it is in fact something to \r\navoid in deep partnership.  that may sound crazy, or \r\nblasphemous even, since love is a religion in our culture.  \r\nin actuality, the minute you \\\"fall out of love\\\" with your \r\npartner, you are on the path of true and human love.  let \r\nme explain:</p>\r\n<p>when you are \\\"in love\\\" you are seeing the object of your \r\naffections as just that: an object.  you are using her as a \r\nscreen to project your ideals onto.  when she no longer has \r\nthat smooth veneer a new lover has (and inevitably loses), \r\nthe projection starts looking a little wobbly.  soon it \r\ndoesn\\\'t work at all.  that\\\'s right, folks, there is a REAL \r\nHUMAN BEING under that projection-generated Princess of \r\nyour Dreams.  i knew a man who believed that someday, \r\nsomewhere, he would meet the Queen of his Dreams.  this was \r\nhis term to mean the Woman that would Solve All of His \r\nProblems.  he went through relationship after relationship, \r\nalways giving up when the real woman began to assert \r\nherself and the projection became wobbly.  finally he met a \r\nwoman with whom he could take the time to get real (also, \r\nmaybe, he grew up a little).  they are happy.  what \r\nhappened is not that he found The Queen, but that he \r\nwithdrew this projected goddess and projected it onto his \r\nown inner screen, allowing his partner to be a real person!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>but i digress.</p>\r\n<p>my original point being that being married but not in \r\nlove is a good starting point for some self-exploration.  \r\nthat old projection is scratchy, or faded, or just plain \r\nnot there.  so who are you married to?  do you like her (or \r\nhim--i forgot whether you are male or female)?  maybe there \r\nis a woman there that you have not really seen.  maybe what \r\nyou did see, the goddess-image, is ready to be assimilated \r\ninto your psyche and guide you and light your way (anima, \r\nfeminine soul, is good at that).  maybe when her light \r\nstarts shining and scattering around in your life, all of \r\nthese other things won\\\'t look so bad.</p>\r\n<p>i certainly don\\\'t think you made a mistake by \r\nenumerating your woes. i hope that you are eventually able \r\nto find meaning in what is happening in your life. ciao.</p>',3,0,0,0,'216.185.7.75','en'),(4939,1248,'2001-07-02 21:41:19',0,'Zelig','how is meditation supposed to solve any real problems?',4,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(4940,1248,'2001-07-02 21:43:22',0,'dasher','<p>the one thing on the list that really makes me feel \r\nsomething is \\\"married but no longer in love.\\\"  the rest of \r\nthe stuff is sorta run-of-the-mill day to day tragedy \r\nstuff.</p>\r\n<p>not to trivialize your cell phone bill or being out of \r\ncoffee.  i know those things suck too.</p>\r\n<p>but.  married and not in love is a good place to start.  \r\nit is my contention that a happy fulfilling love \r\nrelationship can mitigate a lot of other horrors (fiscal, \r\nfamilial, caffiene deprivation).  and therefore, if \r\ndiscontent is not where you want to be, this is where i \r\nwould start.</p>\r\n<p>marriage and long term relationships in general are \r\nreally not about \\\"being in love.\\\" if being in love means to \r\nyou what it means to me, then it is in fact something to \r\navoid in deep partnership.  that may sound crazy, or \r\nblasphemous even, since love is a religion in our culture.  \r\nin actuality, the minute you \\\"fall out of love\\\" with your \r\npartner, you are on the path of true and human love.  let \r\nme explain:</p>\r\n<p>when you are \\\"in love\\\" you are seeing the object of your \r\naffections as just that: an object.  you are using her as a \r\nscreen to project your ideals onto.  when she no longer has \r\nthat smooth veneer a new lover has (and inevitably loses), \r\nthe projection starts looking a little wobbly.  soon it \r\ndoesn\\\'t work at all.  that\\\'s right, folks, there is a REAL \r\nHUMAN BEING under that projection-generated Princess of \r\nyour Dreams.  i knew a man who believed that someday, \r\nsomewhere, he would meet the Queen of his Dreams.  this was \r\nhis term to mean the Woman that would Solve All of His \r\nProblems.  he went through relationship after relationship, \r\nalways giving up when the real woman began to assert \r\nherself and the projection became wobbly.  finally he met a \r\nwoman with whom he could take the time to get real (also, \r\nmaybe, he grew up a little).  they are happy.  what \r\nhappened is not that he found The Queen, but that he \r\nwithdrew this projected goddess and projected it onto his \r\nown inner screen, allowing his partner to be a real person!\r\n</p>\r\n<p>but i digress.</p>\r\n<p>my original point being that being married but not in \r\nlove is a good starting point for some self-exploration.  \r\nthat old projection is scratchy, or faded, or just plain \r\nnot there.  so who are you married to?  do you like her (or \r\nhim--i forgot whether you are male or female)?  maybe there \r\nis a woman there that you have not really seen.  maybe what \r\nyou did see, the goddess-image, is ready to be assimilated \r\ninto your psyche and guide you and light your way (anima, \r\nfeminine soul, is good at that).  maybe when her light \r\nstarts shining and scattering around in your life, all of \r\nthese other things won\\\'t look so bad.</p>\r\n<p>i certainly don\\\'t think you made a mistake by \r\nenumerating your woes. i hope that you are eventually able \r\nto find meaning in what is happening in your life. ciao.</p>',3,0,0,0,'216.185.7.75','en'),(4941,1246,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','yeah but it takes money to go places. i\\\'m gonna leave when \r\ni graduate.',2,0,0,0,'63.10.152.216','en'),(4942,1250,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>what to do today?<br>\r\nmatinee and more writing.<br>\r\nsun hat and short dress.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4943,1239,'2001-07-02 22:04:05',0,'uhuru','being a total slacker.<br>\r\nguess it\\\'s so I don\\\'t quit.',6,0,0,0,'216.185.7.75','en'),(4944,1250,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\nthe sun is out, yes<br>\r\nbut i just spilled steaming hot<br>\r\ntea right on my crotch.<br>\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(4945,1250,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>new gentleman-friend<br>\r\nsweaty sweaty in the bed<br>\r\nlife moves in circles</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4946,1250,'2001-07-02 22:17:15',0,'fernando','<p>i wish i could go out<br>\r\nhot day, litter box is full<br>\r\nguess ill just pee here.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.91.42','en'),(4947,1245,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\ndude. just go to Target! at tar-zshay, you can let your \r\ntroubles fly away, like the petals on a hawaiian lei, to go \r\nfar far away, to come again, another day. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni lay around the house too. it\\\'s boring. i play music. no \r\ncable, cable is the mind-killer. we don\\\'t need no stankin \r\nSopranos, homes!\r\n</P>',2,0,0,0,'63.10.152.216','en'),(4948,1248,'2001-07-02 22:24:48',0,'trouble','dasher,\r\n\r\n\\\'trivial\\\' shows faint humor.  you give me ideas to think \r\nabout, thank you.\r\n\r\nquinn,\r\n\r\nseeing the past present in the other now, i breathe in.\r\nseeing the wounds of the past present in the other now, i \r\nbreathe out.\r\n\r\ni\\\'m cured, thanks for the lesson',4,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(4949,1250,'2001-07-02 22:30:05',0,'dahlia','<p>ah hh hh hh hh<br>\r\nmm mm mm mm mm mm mm<br>\r\noh hh hh hh hh</p>',2,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(4950,1250,'2001-07-02 22:31:20',0,'dahlia','oh! right! linebreaks!\r\n\r\n*sigh*\r\n\r\n*wanders out to porch again*',0,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(4951,1248,'2001-07-02 22:36:53',0,'dasher','<p> sorry i posted my long post twice.  maybe i am the one \r\nwith big trouble.</p>\r\n<p>wow!  that was a super fast cure! \\\"learn to meditate in \r\n15 minutes or less\\\"</p>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'216.185.7.75','en'),(4952,1250,'2001-07-02 22:44:57',0,'chi chi','<p>\r\nhaiku is for wimps<br>\r\ntry something new today please <br>\r\nfive -seven- five, ugh. </p>',0,0,0,0,'64.172.44.107','en'),(4953,1248,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nman. responding to this is like walking a tightrope. a step \r\nin the wrong direction, and it won\\\'t work and it\\\'ll make me \r\nsound like an asshole.\r\n</P>\r\n<p>\r\nif the main focus of your attention rests on yourself, the \r\nproblems will jump right out at you. if it rests on \r\nsomething else, they will be only secondary distractions. i \r\nhave found, too, that when you concentrate on something \r\nbesides yourself, you get really good at what you are doing \r\nand can feel the pure beauty of life. the flow. or whatever.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ntake writers for example, i think you all write, don\\\'t you? \r\nat least here, where there is just as real an audience as \r\nanywhere else. if you focus on each word, or phrase, you \r\nmight obscure or even miss completely your actual goal, \r\nwhich is simple communication of ideas. if you have this \r\nmonth\\\'s Atlantic Monthly, you might have read the very good \r\ncriticism of many of our modern-day \\\"literary\\\" writers, how \r\nthey just bog down in the mire of so-called \\\"artistic\\\" word \r\nchoices and phrases.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni don\\\'t know if that helps. i\\\'m sorry to hear all that, and \r\ni hope you are having some good times. as Don Juan said to \r\nCarlos in the desert, \\\"The basic difference between an \r\nordinary man and a warrior is that a warrior takes \r\neverything as a challenge, while an ordinary man takes \r\neverything as a blessing or a curse.\\\"\r\n</p>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'63.10.152.216','en'),(4954,1248,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<br>\r\nforce yourself to find joy in daily things.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwalk, think, and sing to yourself.  it helps me.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nremember:  it wasn\\\'t always this bad.  it won\\\'t always be\r\nthis bad.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit helps to try and laugh at yourself.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhat is the worst thing that can happen?',2,0,0,0,'203.45.52.132','en'),(4955,1249,'2001-07-02 23:06:12',0,'not_loco','what did the letter say?',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(4956,1250,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>hey i liked it, mang<br>\r\nirritating mosquito<br>\r\ngo bite somewhere else<br>\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(4957,1245,'2001-07-03 01:04:46',0,'dj1_luna','You\\\'re nuts, Soprano\\\'s is Awesome.',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(4958,1251,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','are you?',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4959,1249,'2001-07-03 02:38:19',0,'heathcliff','how come i hear (and have gotten) mail from women talking \r\nabout their new boyfriends fucking them better, but i \r\nnever hear about any girls who get an email/letter from \r\ntheir ex saying their new girl is a better fuck?',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(4960,1250,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','<BR><BR>listen: did you know<BR>just about any damn \r\nthing<BR>can be a haiku?\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.171','en'),(4961,1251,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','no more than any of the rest of us.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.171','en'),(4962,1251,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','actually, that sounds like a great idea for a novel. nothing \r\nbut first chapters. kind of like an italo calvino thing.  \r\nthey\\\'d have to be really good chapters, though.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.171','en'),(4963,1251,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nno, i\\\'m just being facetious. \r\nthe chapters- they\\\'re not that good.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nsteve- this is the same tyler that you wrote that long \r\nemail to. that one time. way back when.\r\n</p>',2,0,0,0,'63.10.151.226','en'),(4964,252,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nbeautiful blue sky\r\nbefore no clouds above me\r\nthunderstorms now\r\n<p/>\r\n<p>\r\ntrue story\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.226','en'),(4965,252,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nman. not enough p\\\'s.\r\n</p>',2,0,0,0,'63.10.151.226','en'),(4966,1251,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hm. i\\\'m gonna go out on a limb here. were you trying to \r\ndecide whether or not to move somewhere?',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4967,89,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nand the moral of the story is....in haiku format...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\npee in the bowl now\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut if you do not flush it\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ncold pee on butt hole\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.105','en'),(4968,1251,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p>\r\nit\\\'s the right limb.\r\n<p>\r\ni was asking you if you liked living in sf i think.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'63.10.151.105','en'),(4969,1251,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','aha!',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4970,1250,'2001-07-03 04:59:54',0,'lulu','haikus suck the shit out of a rhinoceros bunghole.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(4971,1256,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i just like to see the sunrise.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(4972,1253,'2001-07-03 07:14:10',0,'WEEN','<i>I HATE THE SMITHS AND STEVEN MORRISSEY',0,0,0,0,'216.150.228.166','en'),(4973,1241,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','the brick-paved streets',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(4974,1252,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>i wish i were starting school today. I would wear a \r\nlittle jumpsuit. and carry a Lain plastic pencil case. </p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.105.28','en'),(4975,1253,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>once i was riding on a new jersey transit train to \r\nvisit a friend in jersey.  it seemed that my travel-time \r\ncoincided with an efflux of fans leaving madison square \r\ngarden after that evening\\\'s metallcia concert.  such \r\nstrange folk are the usual for jersey transit, so i didn\\\'t \r\nthink twice.  the conductor kept announcing the next stops \r\nas usual, and all was fine.</tt><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<tt>then, all of a sudden, as the conductor was announcing \r\nthe next stop, my stop, i heard a scuffle come over the \r\nintercom - sounds of a struggle were broadcast to the \r\nentire train - then silence.  all of a sudden, this scruffy \r\nvoice came on the intercom, and loudly proclaimed \r\n<i><b>\\\"METALLICA RUUUUULESSSSS!!!!!!\\\"</i></b></tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4976,1254,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>rent an old caddie to cruise the strip in.  preferably \r\na black or gold one, but white also works.  pink is always \r\nappropriate, though...</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4977,1256,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>i think i\\\'m that way with people.  loved the part about \r\npancake batter.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4978,1252,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br>\r\n<tt>my mom made pictures of me on every first day of school \r\nfrom pre-school until the end of highschool.  i have this \r\ncrazy ontage of me growing up, always trodding up the \r\nstaris of a new school or the like.  very interesting.</tt>',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(4979,1248,'2002-04-20 20:49:51',273,'quinn','meditation teaches you how to live-in-the-world.  it does \r\nnot solve problems.  it calms one down and makes \r\nworldly problems diminish in importance.',0,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(4980,1249,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>heathcliff: Men tend to pride themselves on their sexual \r\nprowess, so it makes for an obvious target of attack.  It\\\'s \r\nan easy way to hurt your ex if he\\\'s male.  It\\\'s not as \r\neffective on women.  Then again... maybe men just aren\\\'t as \r\nvindictive and know talking about something like that is \r\njust going to hurt the other person.</P>\r\n<P>not_loco: It was a forked tongue hidden behind a fake \r\nsmile.  The tone of the letter was very friendly expressing \r\nforgivness and gradual understanding and suggesting we make \r\na go at being friends, but it also mentioned that fact that \r\nshe never thinks about me anymore, is having a great time \r\nspending time with all her multitude of friends, is making \r\nnew friends every day, gets a new boyfriend every couple \r\nweeks and has many one-night stands in between, and has \r\njust an absolutely perfect life in general.  Basically, she \r\nknows that my lack of social skills is a huge frustration, \r\nand sometimes source of depression, for me, and flaunted \r\nthe fact that she has more friends than she wants and more \r\nrelationships than she needs.  It\\\'s one thing to talk about \r\nthe events in one\\\'s life, it\\\'s another to dwell on the \r\nthings that will make someone jealous and bitter.  In fact, \r\nI flat out told her at one point that hearing about these \r\nthings makes me jealous and bitter and that she shouldn\\\'t \r\ntell me.  I\\\'ve just never had many friends, and I\\\'m mostly \r\na gimp when it comes to romantic relationships.  \r\nWhatever...</P>\r\n<P>I had a really good dance class last night and was able \r\nto sit down with a friend afterward and talk through this \r\nwhole stupid thing.  She thought EM is still bitter about \r\nbeing dumped, is maybe still a little in love with me, and \r\nin a damaged emotional state (hence going through lovers \r\nlike tissue).  I don\\\'t know.  It doesn\\\'t mean much.  I\\\'m \r\njust glad I have some friends and life this time around, so \r\nI was able to bounce back within 24 hrs. unlike last time \r\nwhen I was in limbo and ended up quagmired in an emotional \r\npit for months.</P>\r\nUng..... once again really long and kinda whiney.  I guess \r\nwhen you spend two years with someone and break up it\\\'s a \r\nmajor event your life and there\\\'s a lot to talk about.  So \r\nit goes...',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4981,1256,'2001-07-03 14:48:24',0,'puppy','I am the same way... I will stay up for hours on end, afraid\nI might miss something special; once I fall asleep, there is\nnothing worth getting out of bed for...\n\n<p>I think it might have something to do with maintaining\nthe status quo; maybe I am afraid of change...',2,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(4982,1242,'2001-07-03 14:51:15',0,'Shane','Hey Dave...\r\n\r\nI spent Canada Day at the United Nations Compound in Seoul. \r\nI don t really know why it was called the compound that is \r\na rather hostile name... anyways the Canadian music they \r\nhad was East Coast fishermen singing the oldest and most \r\nAtlantic tunes that I have ever heard!\r\nI did get to drink some canadian beer tho and eat a \r\ncanadian steak (whatever that is).\r\nSo i guess it was all good.\r\nI reflected a lot on what I left behind but honestly the \r\ncanadians I was with just didn t understand me ( does \r\nanyone).',4,0,0,0,'211.47.0.57','en'),(4983,1250,'2001-07-03 15:04:09',0,'heathcliff','well fuck a donkey\r\nwith a nickle.\r\nrude girls\r\nare the dirties\r\nand if you say gentleman\r\nit souonds like its business\r\n',4,0,0,0,'66.68.35.57','en'),(4984,1252,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to Peter','<p>your mom is like, a conceptual artist.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.245','en'),(4985,1256,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','ditto.... I think it\\\'s due to an innate rebeling against a \r\ndiurnal lifestyle.  There must be a vampire in my family \r\ntree somewhere.  Ooooo... sunlight bad.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(4986,1250,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','fuck a donkey with a nickle?',0,0,0,0,'216.175.103.245','en'),(4987,1252,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>too bad you are not going to teach in santa cruz.  i hear\r\nit is just like \\\"hot for teacher\\\".</P>\r\n<P>those pictures, walking up the steps.  did they make it\r\nall worth it?  i like that idea.  it certainly sounds much\r\nbetter than painting cardboard white and splattering the\r\nword LOVE on it with wax dipped paint brushes.  <a\r\nhref=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=snafu\\\">snafu</a>\r\ntold me about that last night at a cheap immitation denny\\\'s\r\nover a couple corona\\\'s that had the bottle necks dipped in\r\nsalt and the waitress shoved a lemon wedge in them.  lemon! \r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.62','en'),(4988,1256,'2001-07-03 17:53:01',0,'kat','i crave sleep. dreaming sleep. then waking up slow. i could \r\nlie there for hours while waking. letting my mind wander. \r\nno cohesiveness to speak of.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.125','en'),(4989,1253,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','i think i like the smiths and metallica about equally. \r\nsomething strange\\\'s going on in my brain. or my soul. \r\nsomething. i can\\\'t tell anymore if music or art or whatever \r\nis good or bad. i like it all about the same. like the new \r\nr.e.m. album. is it good or bad? who knows. it\\\'s different. \r\nafter a few listens, it starts to grow on you. or \\\"dots and \r\nloops.\\\"  all the songs sound the same. i couldn\\\'t identify \r\none on \\\"name that tune\\\" for a lot of money. curiouser and \r\ncuriouser.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(4990,1256,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','when i have no responsibilities, i\\\'ll often slip into \r\ncircadian free-fall, like a cat. nap a couple of hours, \r\nstay awake for awhile, eat whenever, no set schedule. i \r\nthink we aren\\\'t necessarily diurnal creatures by default. i \r\nhave heard that working nights will fuck you up, though.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(4991,1257,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i think i might be the crowned prince of moving away. and \r\naway. and away. hang in there, pal. sometimes when you move \r\nyour problems do go away. and sometimes the act of moving \r\nmakes you figure out a way to deal with them. and sometimes, \r\nyou move and things are just exactly the same. or worse even. \r\nin the case of my reluctant return to sf in february of 2001.\r\n',2,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(4992,1252,'2001-07-03 20:07:40',0,'chi chi','i think just about everywhere is \\\"hot for teachers\\\" cause \r\nonly fools want to go into careers that include really bad \r\ncafeteria food, homework, and metal detectors all with \r\nno hazard pay.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.44.107','en'),(4993,1258,'2001-07-03 20:18:20',0,'preciosa','so how \\\"hard\\\" do you think about me exactly, champ?',4,0,0,0,'63.203.225.62','en'),(4994,1252,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>teachers are sexy.</p>\r\n<p>cmon just imagine yourself in a heated melodrama, \r\nyour hair in a tight bun and a healthy 17 yr old licking \r\nhis/her chops at you from the back row. when you walk \r\nby to pass out an assignment you get your \r\ntweed-cloaked ass grabbed.</p> ',0,0,0,0,'216.175.92.253','en'),(4995,1257,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>simon. the prodigal fag.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.175.89.249','en'),(4996,1252,'2001-07-03 21:07:25',0,'bmg','i wanna be a teacher.',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(4997,1256,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Veer Moll','<p>I hate sleep. It\\\'s just really boring, a waste of time, \r\nit takes so long to finish. And it\\\'s sooooo boring. Just \r\nsitting there, doing nothing. In a bed. The time can be \r\nsalvaged in the form of dreams, sometimes, but usually not. \r\nAvoid at all costs.</p>\r\n ',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(4998,1259,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>oakland is like brooklyn in many ways. last night the \r\nboys brought over a bottle rocket. I cannot say what thrill \r\nit gives me. we shot it off a long tube from my roof and i \r\ntripped over myself craning my neck to look at it.</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.86.7.208','en'),(4999,1252,'2001-07-03 22:30:53',0,'chi chi','<p>young boys are your fantasy, hon.<br>\r\ncan you see me in a tweed skirt and my hair in a \r\nbun?</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.172.44.107','en'),(5000,1256,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i was in the navy a few years ago; when we were underway my \r\nschedule was 6 hours on, then 6 hours off 24/7. so i\\\'d be \r\nup 7 or so hours and then down for 5. i got ten hours a day \r\nbut was never really satisfied. my life was: work, eat, \r\nsleep, work, eat, sleep. i was always surprised to see that \r\nit was either day or night. time passed very quickly this \r\nway, thank god.',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.220','en'),(5001,1262,'2001-07-03 22:52:56',0,'stella','<P>*oh*</P>\r\n<P>That was nice.</P>\r\n<P>Thanks.</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(5002,1258,'2001-07-03 22:59:33',0,'Himself','<h2>Zelig. Please report to my office.</h2>',6,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5003,1262,'2001-07-03 23:26:27',0,'sic','aww *sentimental pisces*',0,0,0,0,'207.62.162.110','en'),(5004,1257,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon to egg','hello egg.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(5005,1259,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\ni recall a night of shooting bottle rockets out the window of \r\nthe house of failure onto the people standing outside of the \r\ncassanova, all of whom michelle had just invited to her house \r\nfor an after party. she forgot five seconds later and dragged \r\nme upstairs to find the bottle rockets.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(5006,1262,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>weird.</P>\r\n<P>\\\"This shit is REAL!\\\"</P>\r\n<P>i dont have much to say about this except that \r\nmeeting people out of the social circle is hard some \r\ntime. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have stories to tell about puppy showing up in my \r\nlife.    i think i have already told them on this site \r\nthough.</P>',2,0,0,0,'216.175.100.78','en'),(5007,1262,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','awwww!  that is SO SWEET.',2,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(5008,1262,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','this is maybe one of the most beautiful things i\\\'ve ever \r\nread. and it\\\'s about the exact MOMENT your life changed, \r\nthere when you kissed her. archetypal. this is what we all \r\nwant. what we all wish for.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.79.31','en'),(5009,940,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','that can also be arranged....\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.79.31','en'),(5010,1265,'2001-07-04 16:37:14',0,'auntie','i am sure the sf kids will show you good places to have fun \r\nwith alcoholic beverages.  ',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(5011,1265,'2001-07-04 17:31:18',0,'gentrifier','your geography class lied.  ',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(5012,1264,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i have written the accompanyment.  when are we going \r\nto cut the track?',3,0,0,0,'216.175.94.213','en'),(5013,1266,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','the afternoon is slow. tornado warnings up in barrie a few \r\npeople had to leave to go pick kids up from school and be \r\nwith panicing partners.\r\n<br>\r\nmy phone is older then the rest of the phones in the office. \r\nit isn\\\'t enough that im working on the only non imac in the \r\noffice but im also working on a phone that doesnt even have a \r\nhold button or a line display. its an old rotary phone that \r\ngot turned into a phone with buttons. with each click of a \r\nnumber a static sound bellows out of the non-volume \r\ncontrollable receiver. the phone even plugs into an old style \r\njack, whereas the other phones plug into nice new big shiney \r\njacks. ive done my research. earlier today i pulled a George \r\nand slept for a few minutes under my desk. the beauty of a \r\ncubicle is that you can hide under the desk and pull stuff up \r\non the sides and make it look like the station is empty. \r\nthere is no fear of anyone sitting down at my desk because of \r\nthe embarassing ammount of lowtech garbage. \r\n<br>\r\nto make things a little more themed, i went into the storage \r\nroom and picked out some really tackie staplers and one of \r\nthose swingy things with various stamps hanging off and an \r\nold dried up ink pad. from the back of the mac i hung a chain \r\nof paperclips. if anyone is to ask why i have it there, ill \r\nsay its to help my monitor reception.\r\n<br>\r\nunderneath my desk there is a big blue hunk of steel. it has \r\nknobs and latches all over it. after looking it over a few \r\ntimes i was unable to predict its purpose. I then turned to \r\nmy co-worker and asked him what it was. He told me it was a \r\npage projector from the late seventies. I beleived him. He \r\nsaid it was probably worth more then my entire cubicle; \r\ncontents and all when it was first purchased. when turned on, \r\nthis thing makes the most awesome sound. whizzing and \r\nverrrrlangalanging louder then the most annoying powersuppy \r\nand perhaps more bone chilling then a very dusty and old cpu \r\nfan. I could not imagine being in a class listening to a \r\nprofessor drone on about something with this thing on in the \r\nbackground. If one were to turn it on at night noise \r\nregulations would most definately be put to shame. \r\n<br>\r\nevery 15.4 seconds my computer makes a strange noise. \r\nbrmmmmmm tic tic. takes about .6 seconds for the noise to \r\nstart and end. its a quiet nice noise. a cuddling noise that \r\nyou could fall asleep too. like the purr of a cat with its \r\nhead next to yours on a futon. im not ashamed of the \r\npmac7100, it at least has more personality then the imacs. \r\n<br>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'142.214.129.50','en'),(5014,1262,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','woah :)\r\nlovely stuff.\r\ni liek happy endings.',2,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(5015,1267,'2001-07-04 22:17:18',0,'danielle','what i hate is stye\\\'s. i get stye\\\'s on my eyes every few \r\nmonths and i can\\\'t figure out why. first i feel a pain \r\nsomewhere around my eyelid. then it starts to throb. \r\nwhen i wake up the next day my eye is swollen shut and \r\nits red and disgusting. the only thing i can do is hot \r\ncompresses and while those work, they are not \r\npermanent so it goes down and comes back, hurting \r\nwhen you blink and if you get something in your eye, \r\nforget trying to get it out. your eye will scream in pain if \r\nyou try to rub it.<P>blah.',2,0,0,0,'207.34.94.238','en'),(5016,1267,'2001-07-04 22:17:56',0,'danielle again','i just noticed that i always get styes in the same eye. \r\nconcidence?!',1,0,0,0,'207.34.94.238','en'),(5017,1265,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','well, he said SOMETHING like that; maybe he was making some \r\nother point.',1,0,0,0,'63.10.152.58','en'),(5018,1268,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','if life were three\'s company, mrs. roper would say to mr. \r\nroper, \"ohhh SHOOT. it\'s raining ... well maybe we can \r\ngo inside - <i>and make a little fireworks of our own</i>,\" and \r\nwiggle her eyebrows in that mrs. roper way. and then \r\nmr. roper would say \"nnghh. not tonight helen.\"',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5019,1269,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','Well, if it doesn\\\'t work out, you can definitely come over \r\nhere.',1,0,0,0,'63.10.152.58','en'),(5020,1268,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','it did need a little brightening up there at the end didn\\\'t \r\nit? thank you steve.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.10.152.58','en'),(5021,1267,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Vileness Moll','<p>I used to get cold sores all the time. The kinds inside \r\nyour mouth. I think it runs in my family. Then again, I \r\ndon\\\'t really sleep and only eat when I remember to.</p>\r\n<p>All of this changed when I started taking very high \r\ndoses of zinc. I originally started taking zinc as a \r\nteenager to get rid of acne (which it did). But I also \r\nstopped getting cold sores. I also stopped getting sick in \r\ngeneral -- I\\\'d catch a cold along with everyone else, but \r\nthey\\\'ve never been able to get a food in the door, you know?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>It\\\'s all about the zinc.</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(5022,1268,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s always me who is right.',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(5023,1268,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','insanity is one of the more complex forms of self-indulgence \r\nhumanity has invented for itself. your friend needs your \r\npatience and your love. if you grant him his delusions (i.e. \r\nnot argue with him about what is real, etc.), he may get \r\nbored with the little game he\\\'s playing and at least be \r\n\\\"normal\\\" enough to get along with the rest of us. that\\\'s \r\nwhat happened with me, anyway. he\\\'ll come around.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.65','en'),(5024,1268,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','thank you\r\n<p>\r\ni have been doing just that only because i didn\\\'t know what \r\nelse to say. playing along, like \\\"sure man, right on, \r\nsounds great.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.180','en'),(5025,1267,'2001-07-05 05:00:12',0,'jo','no, not coincidence. my dad gets styes, too, and always in \r\nthe same eye. i got pinkeye 9 times in one year, and once \r\nor twice it was both eyes but usually it was just the right.',1,0,0,0,'172.164.130.234','en'),(5026,1268,'2001-07-05 05:01:19',0,'jo','steve, my love for you is boundless right now.',1,0,0,0,'172.164.130.234','en'),(5027,1270,'2001-07-05 05:03:16',0,'jo','<P>um, hi. yes.</p>\r\n<p>also: yes.</p> ',1,0,0,0,'172.164.130.234','en'),(5028,1272,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','tyler: you are a prolific article writer. perhaps you should\r\nconsider diaryland.com.',0,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5029,1270,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','bring me a boy who will do all that for me.  but he should \r\nwrite me beautiful songs too, and sing me to sleep with \r\nthem.',1,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(5030,1264,'2001-07-05 06:02:40',0,'sic','when puffy signs all the papers...yeaaa boyeee',0,0,0,0,'207.62.162.110','en'),(5031,1272,'2001-07-05 06:07:14',0,'r/one','<p>take a newspaper, lay flat.. take each edge and pull \r\ntogether making a parachute of sorts..  wrap edges together \r\nby twisting or using string, make it about 3 inches long... \r\nwait for a breeze.. take your blue-tip matches and strike \r\nthem against your fatigues and set the tip on fire.. watch \r\nit float upward from the heat and begin to incinerate...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>finally yell, \\\'get yo ass out of the tent marty regan \r\ncause it\\\'s heading your way!\\\'.. proceed to watch marty \r\nstumbling out of ten seconds before it goes up in flames \r\nand you get kicked out of scouts</p>\r\n\r\n<p>fuck, i miss being a kid</p>',4,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(5032,1272,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','ix: you mean i suppose either \\\"you should also write \r\nthere,\\\" or \\\"you should write there instead of here\\\". \r\n<p>\r\ncome on with it, buddy.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.61','en'),(5033,1270,'2001-07-05 07:07:45',0,'dancer','isn\\\'t this the trick to all GIRL\\\'S hearts??????',4,0,0,0,'210.54.201.34','en'),(5034,1270,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Pong Moll','<p>I guess that does sound pretty good.</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(5035,1257,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','I feel this inexorable need to offer words of comfort \r\nbecause I understand the feeling.  I know where you\\\'re \r\ncoming from, but I don\\\'t have the words to make you feel \r\nbetter.  So it goes.... Finding your place in the world can \r\ntake a lot of work and strife, but once you\\\'re there, it \r\nall seems worth it.',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5036,1272,'2001-07-05 14:07:00',0,'stellaluna','<P>well, I tend to just read these here articles; don\\\'t post \r\nmuch myself. I\\\'m more of a voyeur than a participant, if you \r\nwill.  I\\\'m a reader. I love to read. I think Tylers stuff is \r\ninteresting and genuine, and I enjoy his posts.</P>\r\n<P> I didn\\\'t necessarily take ix\\\'s comment as a negative \r\none. Perhaps it was, perhaps not. That\\\'s for ix to answer. \r\nIf it was intended to be negative, I wouldn\\\'t let it stop \r\nme, if I enjoyed writing as much as Tyler appears to.</P>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(5037,1266,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','It\\\'s still virtual shit for the sole reason that I\\\'m \r\nconsuming it off of the net.  Maybe it could be refered to \r\nas <I>shit-Lite</I>(tm), for those who need to restrict \r\ntheir fecal intake but need at least a facsimile of that \r\ndistinctive taste.',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5038,1270,'2001-07-05 15:32:21',0,'bmg','i sure would like to have some of that.',0,0,0,0,'66.68.76.248','en'),(5039,1267,'2001-07-05 15:37:24',0,'essemar to Ms. Moll',' When you refer to the kind inside your mouth, I think \r\nyou\\\'re refering to canker sores, not cold sores.  I get \r\nevil little pain sources inside my mouth too, but they are \r\nnot accompanied by any sort of tingling.  Therefore, they \r\nare not Herpes related (I hope).  Anyways.... I just \r\nstopped getting them as I got older, no life-style changes \r\nnecessary.  My dad swears by zinc though as a cold remedy; \r\nhe also mentions studies where high doses of zinc are \r\nlinked to colon cancer or something like that.',4,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5040,1270,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','So, how would one go about winning a punk rock boy\\\'s heart?',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5041,1271,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','I cleaned my kitchen and wrote a letter.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5042,1271,'2001-07-05 16:52:01',0,'kat','i went and saw that guy again. the one i don\\\'t know. i \r\nstill want more. i am going back.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.183','en'),(5043,1270,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','\r\nthree or more pabst blue ribbons chased with a good a long \r\ncrosstown adventure on your balloon tire bikes, a good long \r\nmakeout session at mission rock staring at the oilslicks \r\nshining in the sun and throwing bottle tops at tattered sea \r\ngulls. somewhere there should be a run to the hospital for \r\na tetanus shot. a date to go to the ymca for a swim, \r\nfollowed by throwing parts of your sandwiches at the \r\npidgeons at the docks. someone loses a shoe. someone else \r\ngallantly offers someone a ride home on their back. etc. \r\netc.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(5044,1262,'2001-07-05 17:12:29',0,'ckoneonme','goes to show that being indecisive can lead to great things!',2,0,0,0,'163.181.251.1','en'),(5045,1262,'2001-07-05 17:14:55',0,'johnny','that\\\'s funny!  I didn\\\'t even catch-on to what the story was \r\nabout until i started to recognize that it was Matthew \r\ntelling a story.  I recognized his writing/conversation \r\nstyle to be (probably) Matthew after about the 5th \r\nparagraph.  When I saw \\\"Guiness\\\", that was the give-\r\naway...  \r\n\r\nBy the way, I did try, without luck, to find Kerby Lane \r\ncentral.  After 3-4 U-turns at 2:30am, in rainy weather, \r\nafter enough pitchers of beer, I decided it was best to \r\nhead home ... figured my constant U-turns would not impress \r\nany cop that might be watching.  It was probably for the \r\nbest that I went home, in more ways than one.\r\n\r\nThanks, it\\\'s a great story!\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.117.77.2','en'),(5046,1262,'2001-07-05 17:24:36',0,'Robin (prego)','Wow!  Thanks for sharing that with me.  I have heard the \r\nstory about how y\\\'all met, but not with as much detail.  \r\nYou have brought tears to my eyes.\r\n\r\nYou guys are a special couple...',1,0,0,0,'148.78.255.44','en'),(5047,1270,'2001-07-05 18:36:05',0,'secret girl','hi. be my boyfriend?',1,0,0,0,'209.85.86.26','en'),(5048,271,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','<p>\r\ni too am so so bored.<br>\r\nand believe me, it\\\'s not for lack of stuff to do.<br>\r\nall i do is sit here in my AAFRO CLUB tshirt.<br>\r\nand wonder if just slid under my desk and through some \r\nimaginary hole in the carpet<br>\r\nwhat might happen.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(5049,1270,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','this post was charming and cute. my cold \r\nraisin-shaped heart has been warmed.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5050,271,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>Too much expectation</P>\r\n<P>leads to too much desire</P>\r\n<P>for outlandish sensation</P>\r\n<P>like stoking a fire</p>\r\n<P>ever higher, ever higher</P>\r\n<P>\\\'til it burns to the heavens</P>\r\n<P>and it\\\'s still not enough</P>\r\n<P>\\\'cause the fire\\\'s always hungry</P>\r\n<P>it can\\\'t be satiated</P>\r\n<P>Why do we feed a monster</P>\r\n<P>that will never be placated?</P>',4,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5051,1274,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Wow... that\\\'s really intense.  It reminds me of my own days \r\nof overly long revealing emails and phone conversations \r\nriddled with an excitement more than sexual.',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5052,1274,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','im a sucker for long distance relationships.  all you do \r\nis have sex and do recreational activities when you see \r\neach other, so you think youre really in love. and you \r\nprobably are. have fun, dont look down when the plane \r\ncrashes.',0,0,0,0,'216.175.90.140','en'),(5053,1273,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','could have been a strawberry milk tasting...',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.47','en'),(5054,1267,'2001-07-05 22:14:25',0,'.','green: www.phytoclear.com. it fucking works. ancient \r\nchinese herbal formula. did I mention it WORKS? I feel your \r\npain. put this on when you start to feel tingly, and it \r\nwill be like it never happened.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(5055,1241,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','joker: I haven\\\'t lived there since 1991.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.205','en'),(5056,1265,'2001-07-05 23:32:11',0,'gay male couple','what? huh? eh?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(5057,1274,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i always smell pillow cases after the fact. animal brain.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5058,1271,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i ate soup.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5059,1273,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i once counted how many licks it would take to get to \r\nthe tootsie roll center of a tootsie pop. over a period of \r\ntwo years, i collected a range of data, based on \r\nseasonal differences and type of lick. the results: \r\n250-350 licks. the owl was wrong.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5060,1273,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<h1>THE OWL WAS WRONG</h1>',6,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(5061,1273,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','so what were the rankings!@? i want facts!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(5062,1274,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to s5','do you know about my Lowpil (pronounced \\\"lo-pee\\\")',0,0,0,0,'216.175.99.195','en'),(5063,1275,'2001-07-06 04:03:18',0,'qq','Yes, it is sad. I have a tendency to assume that nobody else\r\nlikes the music I listen to. If it comes on in the CD\r\nchanger when other people are here I usually turn it off. It\r\nmakes me sad that no one is interested in it any more -\r\neither they find it dull and boring, or \\\"i like classical\r\nmusic! It\\\'s soothing background music!\\\" which is the same\r\nthing. As if it were all the same, etc. Sometimes xep makes\r\nfun of me because whenever I buy music it\\\'s often yet\r\nanother recording of the well tempered clavier or the\r\ngoldberg variations or the solo cello suites. heh. :) None\r\nof which I consider soothing background music, since they\r\nall pretty much seize my attention and don\\\'t let go. but I\\\'m\r\nrambling aimlessly now. la la la. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(5064,1275,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','maybe part of it is that it seems like people don\\\'t just\r\nsit down and listen to music anymore.  they put on music\r\nwhile they\\\'re doing something else so everything becomes\r\nbackground music, even the modern stuff.  maybe it can be\r\nblamed on the intense sensory overload that society bombards\r\nus with.  maybe it\\\'s our slacker generation x apathy (hork\r\nhork).  people just don\\\'t take the time to learn what the\r\nmusic is made of.  their lack of understanding turns into\r\ntheir distaste or bordem with it.  if the music moves you\r\nthough, and for some people just understanding what it\\\'s\r\ncomprised of is enough to be moved, you can\\\'t help but give\r\nit your full attention.  i can\\\'t listen to cirque du soleil\r\nsoundtracks without it taking over my thoughts.  the music\r\nitself isn\\\'t as important to me as where it brings me. \r\nknowing that the music was made to be performed while\r\namazing atheletes and artists parade around doing their\r\nthing is the understanding and beauty of it.  in fact, in\r\nthe very same cd changer that qq spoke of, i skip the cirque\r\ncds if other people are around because i figure the music is\r\nnot at all interesting to them.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(5065,1270,'2001-07-06 04:26:03',0,'darkbloom','this was in someone\\\'s profile @ www.makeoutclub.com',1,0,0,0,'24.93.61.58','en'),(5066,1275,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','when i listen to complicated/involved music, like mozart, \r\nor phish, or miles davis, i find that there is a \\\"point of \r\nno return\\\", beyond which you are sucked behind in the wake \r\nof the music and you have to give up control of your own \r\nemotions. this, I believe, is what the poor souls can\\\'t get \r\nover. Have you ever noticed that often, when you try to get \r\nsomebody else to listen, they seem to lose interest and \r\nstart talking, playing with something, remembering \r\nsomething important, etc. EXACTLY at the point of no \r\nreturn. \r\n<p>\r\nCoincidence?\r\n<p>\r\nAs usual, I have to go with the buddhists again on this \r\none. One cannot recieve instruction on the nature of being \r\nuntil one\\\'s karma is ready. I mean, it\\\'s not like I set out \r\nto be someone who appreciates the power of music, it just \r\nhappened. God knows there\\\'s nothing more annoying than \r\nsomeone who sets out to appreciate something.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.10.152.18','en'),(5067,1270,'2001-07-06 05:37:13',0,'jo','hahaha. yeah that\\\'s hardly surprising. i was browsing make \r\nout club just today. it\\\'s so tempting. it\\\'s so crush-\r\ninspiring.',1,0,0,0,'64.12.106.66','en'),(5068,1275,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i listen to music a lot.  all day when i\\\'m working \r\n(although i like silence, too), when i get home, while i\\\'m \r\ncleaning, everywhere.</p>\r\n<p>there\\\'s stuff that i listen to as an event, like light \r\ncandles, burn incense, lie in bed listen to.  like \r\nradiohead, or sigur ros, or art of fighting or augie \r\nmarch.  or pavarotti!</p>\r\n<p>sometimes i play techno on my turntables.  that\\\'s really \r\nintensely being involved with other peoples music.  you\\\'re \r\nreally deconstructing it.</p>\r\n<p>also, i go to concerts to listen to live music a lot.  \r\nsome people seem to go to smoke cigarettes and gossip with \r\nfriends.  but i go to see the emotion on the face of the \r\nband and to listen to the music.</p>\r\n<p>i don\\\'t think i\\\'m so different to anyone else?</p>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(5069,1275,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>oh, and qq:  i love classical music, and not as \r\nbackground.  i think it should be turned up so that your \r\nbody vibrates to it.</p>\r\n<p>me and ix wanted to start a classical music/opra club, \r\nthat people could come to and dance, and maybe have comfy \r\nchairs for the old dudes that like loud mozart.</p>\r\n<p>how rad would that be?  i think everyone would have to \r\nwear smoking jackets.</p>',3,0,0,0,'210.8.201.242','en'),(5070,1275,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<BR>O, I\\\'d be there in a flash. It sounds excellent.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(5071,1270,'2001-07-06 07:17:53',0,'cyantist','-- as a young punk girl, this list that you made.. made my \r\nheart flutter.  now, i demand a bit more.  however, the \r\nlist still makes my heart skip a beat.  what you \r\ndescribed.. is *perfect*',1,0,0,0,'24.221.161.16','en'),(5072,1275,'2001-07-06 13:25:35',0,'puppy','tyler said \\\"Have you ever noticed that often, when you try \r\nto get somebody else to listen, they seem to lose interest \r\nand start talking, playing with something, remembering \r\nsomething important, etc. EXACTLY at the point of no \r\nreturn.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>I am with you there... I was trying to share a piece of \r\nmusic with a friend and when it got to the point of no \r\nreturn he said: \\\"it\\\'s too long.\\\" DOH! tell me you didn\\\'t \r\nagree with the arrangement, tell me the melodies were \r\ncontrived or the textures uninteresting.... but don\\\'t tell \r\nme it\\\'s too long. It made me wonder if he even listened to \r\nthe music at all or if he was listening for the beginning \r\nand the end while watching his stop watch....\r\n\r\n<p>there\\\'s more to music than a 3.5 minute ditty.',1,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(5073,1273,'2001-07-06 14:13:41',0,'cheshire','I\\\'m sitting here ... reading your entry... drinking my cafe \r\nmocha from the coffee chain everyone loves to hate, when I \r\nsaw that you ingested 18oz of chocolate milk in over a few \r\nminutes (right?)... All of the sudden, my stomach starts to \r\nchurn and I get a very oogey feeling... kinda like *I* just \r\ndrank 18oz of choc. milk rather quickly... \r\n\r\nGag...trying desperately hard not too... ',4,0,0,0,'198.92.137.130','en'),(5074,1275,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>VIVALI\\\'S HEADBANGER\\\'S BALL!!!</P><P>Bring it on, Baby!\r\n</P>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5075,1275,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>My dad played a lot classical music for me while I was \r\ngrowing up.  Warm fuzzy memories of being cradled in his \r\nbig, soft warmth while Holst lulled me to sleep.  This is \r\nthe only reason I have any appreciation for this huge genre \r\nof music.  Personally, I find it very difficult to become \r\nengrossed in the interlocking melodies and complex harmony \r\nstructures.  I grew up in the era of MTV.  I want straight \r\nforward, easy to understand rythms and melodies, so even \r\nthough I possess the Bach and the Beethoven, I hardly ever \r\nlisten to it.</P>\r\n<P>Basically, I only like classical music that I can mosh \r\nto.</P>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5076,1273,'2001-07-06 15:20:05',0,'jo','i always suspected that owl was up to no good.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(5077,1272,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','what i mean is: you might enjoy having a journal at \r\ndiaryland. it gets a bit old to see every fourth swinney \r\narticle by tyler. it\\\'s got nothing to do with whether i like \r\nwhat you have to say or not.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(5078,1273,'2001-07-06 15:26:31',0,'agent cooper','\\\"the owls are not what they seem\\\"',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(5079,1275,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','no vivaldi on my shift. carmina burana, endlessly looped.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(5080,1275,'2001-07-06 15:39:42',0,'qq','I wonder if that\\\'s really what it is: most people today want\r\ntheir entertainment preprocessed and ready-to-eat. If you\r\nhave to work a little to follow/understand/appreciate\r\nsomething, why bother when there\\\'s something available to\r\nyou that\\\'s already simplified, pre-digested, and pared down\r\nto the bare button-pushing bones? \r\n\r\nOk, I\\\'m exaggerating a lot here obviously, and there\\\'s\r\ncertainly modern/popular music that\\\'s every bit as complex\r\nand subtle as a lot of classical music - and there\\\'s plenty\r\nof classical music that is repetitive boring schlock. But\r\nmaybe there\\\'s still some truth to the idea that we\\\'ve grown\r\nused to having everything spoon-fed to us? \r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(5081,1275,'2001-07-06 16:16:31',0,'puppy','Beethoven\\\'s Ninth is good moshing music...',2,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(5082,1261,'2001-07-06 16:20:28',0,'puppy','out of curiosity, how the hell did this get past the \r\n<strike>censors</strike> editors? If I had sent this in, I \r\nwould have been sent a nice supportive e-mail telling \r\nme \\\"good luck; try again.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(5083,1277,'2001-07-06 16:27:23',0,'hh','I\\\'m with you. I am a waif lover.',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(5084,1277,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar',' Yeah.... Juliette Lewis is hot.  She\\\'s really hot.  You \r\nknow who else is hot?  Monet Mazur is hot.  She\\\'s really \r\nhot.  I dated this girl, Erin, for a couple weeks who looks \r\nlike Monet Mazur.  Erin is hot.  She\\\'s really hot.  he he \r\nhe he he he....<P>(<I>Beavis and Butthead</I>)</P>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5085,1277,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<P>Seriously, I am straight and I have no doubts about my \r\nheterosexuality, but I do find myself occasionally \r\nfantasizing about swollen members and soft beer bellies.  I \r\nsometimes get crushes on other men, and I think Christian \r\nSlater is totally hot (maybe that\\\'s just because I want to \r\nlook like him).  It\\\'s just that none of this can ever turn \r\ninto anything sexual/romantic because that\\\'s not the way I \r\nbend my wrist.  They\\\'re just amusing fantasies.</P>\r\n<P>Of course, I also seem to be some sort of magnet for gay \r\nmen, so I definitly have had the chance (and have sometimes \r\nhad the chance forced upon me) to explore that facet of my \r\nbeing and know what is really there.  I\\\'ve also known a \r\nfair number of women who have had and enjoyed sexual \r\nencounters with \\\'their own kind,\\\' so to speak.  To them it \r\nwas a wonderful and exhilirating experience that they will \r\ncherish in their memories as they grow old with their \r\nhusbands.</P>\r\n<P>Does that help?</P>',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5086,1275,'2001-07-06 17:33:01',0,'essemar to qq',' I totally agree with you.  I find myself flipping through \r\nchannels, wandering through city streets, cruising the web, \r\njust looking for the instant fix.  I crave a life reduced \r\nto sound bites and fast food.  I don\\\'t have the time to \r\n<I>understand</I> something.  If I can\\\'t consume and \r\ncomprehend it in less than 30 seconds, then I don\\\'t want \r\nit.  All I really want is Britney Spears doing a strip \r\ntease at the MTV awards show while singing regurgitated \r\nbubblegum pop that has become so homogenous that the only \r\nselling point is the youth of the \\\"artist\\\" and the size of \r\nher tits.<P>Or something like that.</P><P>BTW, Puppy, try \r\nout some Russian shit if you haven\\\'t already.  They knew \r\nwhere it was at.  I\\\'d recommend Prokofiev\\\'s Romeo and \r\nJuliet suite, particularily \\\"The entrance of the \r\nMontagues.\\\"</P>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(5087,1275,'2001-07-06 17:56:14',0,'puppy','i am all about Russian art (esp. 19<sup>th</sup> century \r\nRussian lit). My all time favorite piece: The Voyavoda, by \r\nTchaichovsky... I definetly appreciate your recommendation; \r\nI don\\\'t think i have heard it before.\r\n\r\n<p>Actually, I think Tchaichovsky has two pieces by the \r\nname of The Voyavoda... the one I am referring to is 12 \r\nminute opus; I don\\\'t think I have heard the other one and I \r\nhave no idea how to differentiate the two...',1,0,0,0,'192.35.232.99','en'),(5088,1275,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to xep','<P>on music as background.  i keep music playing in the\r\nbackground all the time.  life without music in the\r\nbackground sometimes makes me uncomfortable, usually when i\r\nhave nothing to do with my hands and mind.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>also, i check out of whatever i am doing often and only\r\nlisten to the music.  when it becomes less interesting i go\r\nback to what i was doing before.  i might stop for my\r\nfavorite song, or for several songs.</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.62','en'),(5089,1261,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i agree.  trash this.',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.62','en'),(5090,1277,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','you know who\\\'s hot? everybody. seriously. i would fuck any \r\nhuman being and selected members of the animal kingdom if i \r\nthought it was consensual. i\\\'d fuck a monkey for a klondike \r\nbar. you\\\'ll have to excuse me. i ain\\\'t right.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(5091,1272,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','I was operating under the assumption that all articles were \r\nread and then approved, sometimes by more than one person, \r\nbefore they showed up on the site (according to the FAQ, \r\nwhich I read and tried to follow). So, when I felt like \r\nwriting, I turned one in. After that, it\\\'s in their hands, \r\nunless that process has been changed. It\\\'s their site; it\\\'s \r\nmy writing. If you have a problem with how many show up, \r\ntalk to them. Or