19.20','en'),(7388,1635,'2001-08-20 20:00:55',0,'jo','awwwwww players.<BR>\r\nfried zucchini and a HUGE dr. pepper at 3 AM.',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(7389,1484,'2001-08-20 21:08:24',0,'justino','okay so thanks to all your creative outpourings my problem \r\nnow is that i have twelve swinney ad banners in rotation so \r\nswinney is more likely than anything else to pop up.  so i \r\nwant to balance the odds some, but my voodoo stacking of \r\nscripts and virtual server includes isn\\\'t coming through \r\ninto anything visible.  so i had a vision for a small C \r\nprogram I could write to put this all together right.  But \r\nI haven\\\'t written C code in like four years.  Hurm.  Maybe \r\nI\\\'ll sketch out the program and then dig out my textbook \r\nfrom College.  I used to be able to do stuff like this: \r\nhttp://www.links.net/vita/swat/course/unixc/ .<p>\r\n\r\nWhat I was thinking to do is create files representing the \r\nHTML involved in posting each particular ad.  Then write a \r\nprogram that indexes each of those files within a Swinney \r\nsubdirectory.  Each file is matched against a number, and \r\nthe file represented by the current number is copied into \r\nthe rotation.  A chron job runs each night that iterates \r\nthe number one further in the rotation, and the according \r\nfile is copied over the old one.  I think I could figure \r\nout how to write that in C.  could I use that to rotate \r\nSwinney ad banners on Links.net?  Or do I have to write it \r\nin shell script or perl or something?',4,0,0,0,'216.27.176.51','en'),(7390,1638,'2001-08-20 21:12:23',0,'justino','the first part of this writing reminded me of breaking the \r\nwaves.  urging a partner to participate in vicarious sex.  \r\nyimney.  uplifting.<p>\r\n\r\nthe second part reminded me that home is fleeting, but yeah \r\nprobably in the bay area for most all the people i \r\nassociate with.  nothing like leaving to make you crave \r\nit.  i cower a bit anticipating my own dista