66','en'),(10678,228,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','heh, a <a href=\\\"http://www.fluffer.com\\\">movie</a> i worked on had a wrap party here. it was fun. as far as i remember.',1,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(10679,1972,'2001-10-23 04:34:46',0,'jm3','``don\\\'t fondle it?\\\'\\\' what\\\'s that about? you motherf*cking grapple with it. enough now.',4,0,0,0,'64.250.146.24','en'),(10680,1972,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i live in columbia.<br><br>\r\nthere are many great blues songs about women:<br>\r\n\\\"it must be jelly \\\'cause jam don\\\'t shake like that\\\"<br>also<br>\r\n\\\"squeeze my lemon(and the juice run down my leg)\\\"<br><br>\r\nduring some of the lonlier days of my life, when i was young and trapped in the navy(hey guess what--smart and pretty young girls usually don\\\'t want to date guys in the military), I would go to the movies alone, and then go to a coffeeshop because I was only 19. There I would sit, drinking coffee and reading, and maybe getting smarter but not much. I didn\\\'t eat cake, I ate grilled PB&J\\\'s with curly fries for 2 bucks. but i eventually made friends with people etc.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.237','en'),(10681,1971,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','\\\"length\\\"',0,0,0,0,'65.140.48.237','en'),(10682,1972,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i keep rereading this, looking for the thing that \\\"eating cake alone\\\" is supposed to be a euphemism for.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(10683,1972,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','all i see are names: woody guthrie, sonny boy willamson. i aspire to folk music, i clutch a c tuned harmonica, i am just trying to learn a couple of songs so i can mess with them. who are the great women of blues? who are the women harmonica players? i need a role model. does anyone know?',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(10684,1972,'2001-10-23 09:13:58',0,'jim','seeing movies alone is one of my favorite things to do.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(10685,1972,'2001-10-23 09:15:41',0,'ryan','i know of no women harp players (that are recorded that is) and i have never seen a women play a blues harp.<p>\r\n\r\nwomen of blues: billie holiday and bessie smith of course.  ella perhaps.  blues and jazz fuck each other a lot.  \r\n<p>\r\nif you are looking for something to play, try blind willie johnson for some good christian blues or some leadbelly.  you could play them like a woman, take their shit and put your stuff into it.\r\n<p>\r\nor r.l. burnside, that sailor mouthed 68+ year old mofo we listened to near sactown.  but he\\\'s pretty new.  (another but) but he says a lot of bad words.<p>\r\ntake something old, and make it yer own.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.52','en'),(10686,1971,'2001-10-23 09:44:30',0,'lulu','*sigh*',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(10687,1972,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','google saves. <a href=\\\"http://www.harmonicalinks.com/female.html\\\">female blues harp players</a>',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(10688,1972,'2001-10-23 11:27:43',0,'trouble','for some female blues singers check out Big Mama Thornton and yeah, you can\\\'t miss Bessie Smith.  especially her version of \\\'need a little sugar in my bowl\\\'.  whoa....',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(10689,1972,'2002-04-20 20:49:51',273,'Quinn','I got R.L. Burnside in my earphones as we speak.  But you must check out \\\'Yank Rachel\\\'s Tennesee Jug Busters.\\\' No female harp, but good stuff on the mandolin.',0,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(10690,1973,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i live less than three miles from work, yet it takes me almost 45 minutes to get there each morning.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(10691,1972,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my hometown was bessie smith\'s hometown. they recently renovated a drab part of the \'hood into \'<a href=\"http://www.chattanoogafuntours.com/BessieSmith/bessiesmith.htm\">bessie smith hall</a>\' where they play her music 24/7. every year, they have the \'bessie smith strut\', also, where everyone gets really drunk, listens to great blues, and shakes their collective ass across downtown <a href=\"http://www.chattanoogafun.com/\">chattanooga</a>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(10692,1973,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i am thinking about riding a bicycle to work.  fifteen miles is a ways.  i think it would take about an hour.  their are hills.  fifteen miles per hour sounds about right.  i dont have a trick super itallian bicycle like the guys at work.  ',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10693,463,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<br>mmm..... gravy<br>mmm..... pizza<p>It\\\'s lunch time, and that picture is making me start to drool.  I hate oysters, but I like pizza, and I like gravy.  Gravy makes just about everything taste better.</p>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10694,1973,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I would bike to work if it weren\\\'t ~10 degrees all winter. and hell-cabs and death-traffic.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(10695,1973,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>aus, remember that time i got a purple hip racing home from Ruta Maya in the rain?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsqueeeek THUD sliiiide\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(10696,1973,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','I have a friend who used to commute on bicycle until he spent one morning unconscious in a drainage ditch with a broken collar bone.  When he finally came to, he managed to drag himself to some little old lady\\\'s house where he called up a cab to take him to the hospital.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10697,1973,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','the best thing about hills is that you can go around them. hills bad.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(10698,1973,'2001-10-23 18:20:10',0,'jo','yeah, my best friend and i were both going to go buy bikes and ride around the neighborhood together - which we still might do! - but we were trading bicycle horror stories with one another and we each know a handful of people who\\\'ve been seriously injured after being hit by a car while riding their bikes. she knows one girl who was in a coma for a week and one boyfriend of a friend who died. urban bicycling is scary. at least if someone hits my car, i\\\'m in a truck.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(10699,1973,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','',0,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(10700,1973,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p> oh, i also remember another austin TX biking escapade:  the H.O.C. drunken bike ride.</p>\r\n\r\n<p> we all got 32oz cans of coors and took to the streets.  Romas ate shit when he tried to jump a curb with two bigbeers in his hands.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p> we ended up at the austin airport runway where we had to yank on Romas\\\' pants to keep him from jumping the fence to steal a runway lightbulb.  \\\"Romas, you don\\\'t have to prove anything man!\\\" </p>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(10701,1973,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>all these crash stories remind me of the time my step-brother was run off the road on his honda bike; this, after separating some thugs from $50 over a game of pac-man.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10702,1972,'2001-10-23 19:55:13',0,'bmg','while you all are reading way too deep into this, I am going to go eat cake, alone; just for kicks. ',0,0,0,0,'66.68.81.166','en'),(10703,1973,'2001-10-23 20:00:03',0,'bmg','<p>this morning I showered, whent to class, came home from class, took a nap, woke up and had a beer. this is not the cycle yet, but it is seems as though i could turn this into one soon. </p>',0,0,0,0,'66.68.81.166','en'),(10704,1972,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','i always liked the duchess, bo diddley\\\'s guitarist and occaisonal harp player.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(10705,1972,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Mmmm.... chocolate rasberry cake.  There\\\'s some damn good pastry shops on Bleeker street.  It\\\'s too bad I can\\\'t get some cake since I have no one to go with.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10706,1973,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','one of the first times i hung out with adam was a drunken bike ride where we bought coors 32s and drank them on top of the REI when they were over on Lamar by the river.<BR><BR>that was perhaps the infamous summer of drinking?',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10707,1973,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to bmg','and one time i had to take bmg\\\'s bottle away because he was a raging drunk.<BR><BR>DO YOUR HOMEWORK!<BR><BR>*slaps bmg\\\'s neck*',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10708,1972,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to essemar','what happened to the girl from the slut story?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(10709,1972,'2001-10-23 23:38:49',0,'ryan','to fix it all, may i suggest \\\"bob\\\'s awesome lasagne\\\"?:\r\n<p>\r\n<pre>\r\n8Â ouncesÂ lasagna noodles\r\n1Â poundÂ ground beef\r\n1/4Â cupÂ minced onions\r\n1Â teaspoonÂ salt\r\n1/2Â teaspoonÂ garlic salt\r\n1Â (32 ounce) jarÂ spaghetti sauce\r\n1Â (16 ounce) packageÂ large curd cottage cheese\r\n1Â poundÂ mozzarella cheese, shredded\r\n</pre>Â \r\n<p>\r\n<b>Directions	</b>\r\n	<p>\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Bring a large pot of lightly salted water to a boil. Cook noodles in boiling water for 8 to 10 minutes, or until al dente; drain.\r\n<li>In a large skillet over medium heat, saute ground beef, onions, salt and garlic salt until meat is brown. Drain excess fat, add spaghetti sauce to beef mixture, and bring to a boil. Reduce heat, and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\r\n<li>Preheat oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C). Grease a 9x13 inch glass baking pan.\r\n<li>Line bottom of pan with three lasagna noodles. Spread 1/3 of sauce mixture over noodles. Layer 1/3 of the cottage cheese over the sauce. Sprinkle 1/3 of the mozzarella over the cottage cheese. Repeat this layering process until all ingredients are used up.\r\n<li>Bake in the preheated oven for one hour. Let stand for 10 minutes before serving.\r\n</ol>',1,0,0,0,'209.162.49.77','en'),(10710,1973,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p> first time i met BMG, he was visiting austin at 31st St. house and wouldnt leave the Maker\\\'s Mark alone<br>\r\n<br>\r\nso austin had to hide it</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bad BMG!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>;Ãž</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(10711,1972,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to ess','<br><tt>...and the girl from central park? and deborah? and glow? heh heh heh. dude. you rock. i think i was asked to leave, once, from that place on bleeker (near father demo square?). i was out of my head and dropped my stack of records into their canoli and couldnt muster any apology besides a hearty laugh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10712,1973,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to BMG','<br><tt>in light of these recent comments, i cant help but feel like i should bring you a 64 oz. bottle of Olde English \\\'800\\\' Malt Liquor. At the bodega next to my place, theyre $1.09. God\\\'s Golden Manna.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10713,1968,'2001-10-24 02:49:11',0,'Danielle','Peter- haha. It would probably take me the same amount of time to travel to Italy as it would to Jersey ;) but at least I could sort of speak the language there. haha.\r\n\r\nI\\\'m not sure <i>why</I>Italy. Too many movies I think. Seems like a good place to have lots of fat babies.',1,0,0,0,'24.64.80.199','en'),(10714,1968,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i dunno... them jersey-ites speak a special dialect. usually only folks from south brooklyn can decypher parts of it.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10715,1955,'2001-10-24 03:25:04',0,'maria','that\\\'s funny -- humanity is what i don\\\'t like, it\\\'s the individual people that make things better. ',1,0,0,0,'134.241.2.49','en'),(10716,1950,'2001-10-24 03:31:34',0,'maria','belated comment : rolling rock, 4 bucks a pitcher, oberlin inn. mondays and thursdays.\r\ncome visit and hang out with me!!',1,0,0,0,'134.241.2.49','en'),(10717,1973,'2001-10-24 04:06:47',0,'jo','AHEM bring us both liquor AHEM.<BR>\r\ni just realized that about 75% of the time i\\\'m with bmg, i\\\'m drunk or on my way to drunk. cheers to that, my friend!',0,0,0,0,'172.149.213.67','en'),(10718,1950,'2001-10-24 04:09:27',0,'jo to maria','um, i\\\'m sorry, that doesn\\\'t compare to turbodog $7 pitchers because a) rolling rock is gross and should only be drunk when it is the only option and b) turbodog is actually a <b>good</b> beer. if it were $15/pitcher, i\\\'d probably still drink it.<BR>\r\njust not as often.',0,0,0,0,'172.149.213.67','en'),(10719,1968,'2001-10-24 04:15:55',0,'Danielle','Yah and I\\\'m from Canada so- I\\\'m screwed!',1,0,0,0,'24.71.223.143','en'),(10720,1974,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','me too.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve never been to philadelphia.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(10721,1972,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','After a few really good conversations on the phone and plans to get together, the girl from Central Park got a bad deja vu and decided it would be a bad idea to spend time with me.  Deborah was too, uh, <i>messy</i>.  Glo (the girl from the slut story) and I simultaneously broke up with eachother a couple days ago, literally.  We met in the park, and the first words out of each of our mouths were, \\\"I\\\'m here to break up with you.\\\"  She wants us to continue hanging out though, which I translate to mean that she wants us to continue having sex while maintaining her freedom to fool around with others.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10722,1972,'2001-10-24 13:33:43',0,'embarassed mofo','hmm. situation: not-so-happy breakup, preceeded by several weeks of awkward lack-of-interest in sex. now that we\\\'re apart, she comes by quite often for the bootycall. why is this? i like the booty (with her) too much to question it, but it would be nice to know why it wasn\\\'t this good when we were together. neither of us gets jiggy with anyone else. we just pretend we arent dating, then fuck 5 times a week, not seeing eachtoher much else besides that. we both still have feelings for eachother. this is weierd. advice?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(10723,1974,'2001-10-24 16:41:58',0,'mikey','<BR><BR>Oh, the wake-up bomb<BR>My head\\\'s on fire and high esteem<BR>Get drunk and sing along to Queen<BR>Practice my T-Rex moves and make the scene<BR>Yeah, I\\\'d rather be anywhere doing anything \r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.33','en'),(10724,1974,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i like philadelphia. even the bus station. or perhaps especially the bus station. and <a href=\\\"http://www.collphyphil.org/muttpg1.shtml\\\">the mutter museum</a>!!!',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(10725,1974,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i now, helplessly, have the queen song <i><a href=\\\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=653879025/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/artistid=QUEEN/itemid=273993\\\">\\\'good old-fashioned lover boy\\\'</a></i> in my head...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(10726,1975,'2001-10-24 18:41:10',0,'kat','is this some kind of <b><i>Swinney Illustrated</i></b> thing? should i be thinking porny?\r\n\r\n<p>...who wants to come take group nudie pics in my pink bedroom with me? this might not involve ME, but i promise there will be mangos.</p>',4,0,0,0,'63.193.14.253','en'),(10727,1975,'2001-10-24 18:46:11',0,'kat','oh wait...i apologize. i\\\'m always thinking \\\'porny\\\'',1,0,0,0,'63.193.14.253','en'),(10728,1975,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','but we already have a Mary Kate and Ashley Olsen calendar for 2002 at my house! Leonard you slow-poke.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(10729,1975,'2001-10-24 19:46:08',0,'leonard to egg','oh, go outside. it\\\'s not so bad.',2,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(10730,1972,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Someone recently said to me, \\\"Stop looking and it will come to you.  Don\\\'t question it.  Just sit back and enjoy it for as long as it lasts.\\\"  It usually ends, eventually.  The advice continues to hold.  Don\\\'t question it.  Just go with it.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10731,1976,'2001-10-24 22:31:23',0,'','<pre>\r\n                       <strong>GLOBAL POPULATION GROWTH</strong>                 Billions\r\n                                                           ____ -->|11\r\n    You are here----------------+                 _ - ~~~~         |10\r\n     (gradual economic decline) |             _-~                  |9\r\n                                |        _ -~                      |8\r\nOverpopulation begins-+         V    _ -~                          |7\r\n (unsustainability)   |           _-~                              |6\r\n                      V       _- ~                                 |5 <!--960425--><a href=\"http://www.census.gov/cgi-bin/ipc/popclockw\">(now)</a>\r\n                    ====== _-~   [----- projected by U.N. -------> |4\r\n                       _-~ *****                                   |3\r\n             ____ ---~       ^                                     |2\r\n   -- ~~~~~~                 |                                     |1\r\n   --|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|--\r\n     1900  1920  1940  1960  1980  2000  2020  2040  2060  2080  2100\r\n                             |\r\n                  <strong>Massive environmental</strong>\r\n           <strong>destruction & loss of species begins</strong>\r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n',0,0,0,1,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10732,1976,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<pre>\r\n                       <strong>GLOBAL POPULATION GROWTH</strong>                 Billions\r\n                                                           ____ -->|11\r\n    You are here----------------+                 _ - ~~~~         |10\r\n     (gradual economic decline) |             _-~                  |9\r\n                                |        _ -~                      |8\r\nOverpopulation begins-+         V    _ -~                          |7\r\n (unsustainability)   |           _-~                              |6\r\n                      V       _- ~                                 |5 <!--960425--><a href=\\\"http://www.census.gov/cgi-bin/ipc/popclockw\\\">(now)</a>\r\n                    ====== _-~   [----- projected by U.N. -------> |4\r\n                       _-~ *****                                   |3\r\n             ____ ---~       ^                                     |2\r\n   -- ~~~~~~                 |                                     |1\r\n   --|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|--\r\n     1900  1920  1940  1960  1980  2000  2020  2040  2060  2080  2100\r\n                             |\r\n                  <strong>Massive environmental</strong>\r\n           <strong>destruction & loss of species begins</strong>\r\n\r\n</pre>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10733,1976,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>it is true.  WE ARE ALL GOING TO DIE, and so will our kids.  this is always been the case.  perhaps a few will live on for some value of survive.  the species will evolve in the coming millennium that it take to begin recording things like history again and regaining the knowlege of how to grow and make stimulants from new plant species that have evolved allong side us.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>in light of this i go to work because that is the progress that i was brought up on.  i should go live somewhere the slowness and agony of minutes and seconds drags by so that i might savor the last few particles of the universal clock that are alloted to this thing called life.  all life. that is water based anyway.  cosmic life will rip on.</P>',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10734,1976,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>in 10 years, these people are going to have jobs and kids. thats why they party now, probably. they know they can\'t do this when theyre 30. so, its kind of a <i>live for the moment, for the moment</i> kind of thing. maybe you\'re yearning because youre growing up more than them, or maturing past wherever they are.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10735,1976,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>er, or maybe <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0375404120/qid%3D1003963623/ref%3Dsr%5F11%5F0%5F1/002-6860025-4951244\\\">this</a> is what people do when they start yearning...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.121.20','en'),(10736,1976,'2001-10-24 22:54:56',0,'lou','this made me cry.',1,0,0,0,'216.175.65.209','en'),(10737,1975,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','er, this is frightening.',5,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(10738,1975,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','is <B>o</B> going to sell them through her porn site?',0,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10739,1976,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>austin is wise. All of this will end.</p>\r\n<p>All hail The Fall!</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(10740,1973,'2001-10-25 02:50:24',0,'erin','i\\\'ve never been able to leave the makers mark alone.',0,0,0,0,'205.188.195.39','en'),(10741,1973,'2001-10-25 04:39:22',0,'jay','If you live in California, go to the <a href=\"http://www.makersmark.com\">Maker\'s Mark website</a> and become an ambassador. I did! They put your name on a barrel of bourbon that you\'re allegedly able to taste when it\'s ready (some years from now). Yes, I love me some Maker\'s Mark. Oh, and they send you a free shot glass.',1,0,0,0,'66.108.63.224','en'),(10742,1976,'2001-10-25 06:51:32',0,'jim','<p>\r\nmy friends keep talking me out of starting organized conversations about what seems like one of the defining aspects of our generation: an unspoken acceptance of a near certain dystopic future.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\neven the most conservative estimates acknowledge that the oil will run out by 2040.   there will be nine billion people alive on Earth.  I\'ll be 65.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI\'m an archivist.  how do I justify my passion for documenting the present for a future I don\'t take for granted?  how do I rectify my fierce optimism about the future of my friends, family and loved ones with a reluctant pessimism about the future of the rest of society?  how do I revel in the joy of now while being prepared for what comes next?\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwhy aren\'t these interesting things to talk about?\r\n</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(10743,1976,'2001-10-25 07:20:03',0,'ryan to jim','<p>\r\nmy parents did duck and cover drills in school because the evil reds were coming with their rockets.  the president was shot.  then the vietnam war started raging and my parents left the country for the peace corps so my father wouldn\\\'t have to fight in that one.  are you sure it\\\'s just our generation?\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'209.162.49.120','en'),(10744,188,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'m in austin again. i have noticed that there are lots of clean looking girls here with ruddy cheeks and smooth ponytails swinging just above their tanned shoulderblades. perfect teeth and baby blue tank tops. jeans with platform heels. they smoke. they don\\\'t smoke. they ride their bikes, they sit in ruta maya clutching one knee to a tanned collarbone, a rounded pale blonde red tanned collarbone, listening to music with twang or enduring their friend\\\'s bad poetry. afterward they make a constructive comment because this is their duty as the friend of an artist on a warm night in texas, lone star in hand. coffee in hand. trip to whole foods in their future, trip to whole foods in their past too. ',4,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(10745,1976,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<b>to jim:</b> because there\'s nothing we can do about them, seemingly. we are personal people with small problems close to us, money, work, things we think about today because they force us to. we\'d rather being laying quietly doing nothing, we only do as much as we can to ensure we can lay quietly later. \r\n<br><br>\r\ngreen energy is important; politics puts it off because of money. on npr the other day i heard a great point: if we had pulled out of the middle east (instead of staying to protect oil interests) and spent the money we saved on planes, guns, soldiers, and recovering from national terrorism, on green power instead, we would have saved more than we\'d spent. but green power is too expensive. too expensive. more expensive than lives lost fighting over oil yet again?\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe future is dystopic, but talking about it implies you\'re an optimist: you can do something. there are two kinds of people: people with governmental power and people without. the people without are always affected by the people that have it, but they\'ll never truly have a say in what the gov\'t does. people with progressive ideas should go into the system, work it from within, not fight against it. but what progressive person do you know who wants to play the yale to washington dc game? we don\'t, we are artists or flakes or seekers or spirits, or regular folks just getting by. it hurts us to play their game. in the large picture it helps us, and our kind, but in the small daily picture of donning a suit or going to law school to get into the ring, to get our points on the table, it hurts us and we don\'t want to do it. it\'s a fight. \r\n<br><br>\r\nmarching and protesting is not totally in vain, nor is writing to your congressman demanding green power, telling them what you think. fighting against big business isn\'t even totally in vain, but on the scale of in vain to useful, it\'s easy to see why most of us feel these things fall closer to in vain.\r\n<br><br>\r\nso, that said, it\'s not that it\'s not interesting to talk about, i think people are talking about it. but it\'s sad to talk about, because i think most of us feel pretty hopeless and scared and just defeated before we\'ve even gotten past one sentence of the discussion, that old white men who are run by money and industry, men who know nothing of me, who don\'t care, are ruling me. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<i>although the masters make the rules for the wise men and the fools, and i\'ve got nothing ma, to live up to</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\nmost of us don\'t have an inkling of how to stop the train at this point. stop breeding? march? go to law school? why don\'t we care enough about long term to sacrifice short term? we\'re human.\r\n<br><br>\r\nwe\'d rather sleep, fuck, make art, sleep, fuck, buy things, worry about ourselves. the interesting topic isn\'t really the dystopic future but the hope that it won\'t be that way, and the methods for getting us out of it. i know that i personally just need someone to tell me what to do, or i\'ll keep doing nothing. \r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(10746,1975,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'ll send you everything you ask for as soon as i get back from texas. promise. everyone else better hop to it.',4,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(10747,1976,'2001-10-25 14:12:08',0,'kat','i am feeling halfway optimistic today. i didn\\\'t cry myself to sleep last night. and tho the dreams weren\\\'t the greatest, at least i didn\\\'t have the nightmares again. i have been wanting for awhile to move up farther into the mountains and build a strawbale house with solar power. but i dont want to live alone. <i>...she was an old woman who lived in a shoe.</i> i\\\'d be happy with a barn. a treehouse? well, okay most anything. as long as...??? \r\n\r\n<p>i am feeling halfway optimistic today</p>',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.62','en'),(10748,1976,'2001-10-25 14:12:11',0,'jo to jim','fellow cancer: thanks for using \\\"archivist\\\" rather than \\\"packrat.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(10749,1975,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>how about months = cities? do we have 12 cities? i\\\'m picturing a cheesy photo a la start-up huddle with chais and non-fat skinny mochas in hands and sullen no sleep faces. and perhaps some boxen in the background for the SF crowd. oh wait, that\\\'s so 1999. maybe a group shot at a park, enjoying our unemployment days? and a shot of the austin folks near barton springs all in bikinis? the LA people in front of some fancy bar, looking chic and well-shorn? melbournians, eating some dingo? </p>\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(10750,1975,'2001-10-25 16:48:54',0,'jo to weeb','that is SUCH a good idea.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(10751,1975,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Is there a deadline for this?',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10752,1975,'2001-10-25 17:51:19',0,'bmg','when we go to barton springs we don\\\'t wear bikinis, I wear my sweater all the time. I think there will be a large portion of the austin crew together this weekend... i will get with jo and see what we can come up with... otherwise I will just submit a picture of my hairy chest....',0,0,0,0,'66.68.81.166','en'),(10753,1975,'2001-10-25 17:59:17',0,'leonard','<b>deadline:</b> i\\\'d like to have all submissions in by 7 november 2001. thanks/',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(10754,1976,'2001-10-25 18:29:47',0,'stella','<p>That was very well put, le. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>A friend of mine once asked me if I ever thought that the human race could achieve peace. I answered that no, I didn\\\'t; I don\\\'t feel that the human race is peaceful by nature. If we were, living peacefully would come as naturally as sleeping. Our nature is to conquer, to rule; power is what we crave. But that doesn\\\'t mean that there aren\\\'t those of us who acknowledge that striving for peace would achieve a longer existance. Perhaps we\\\'re higher evolved, more intuitive, more sensitive. It goes the same for ecology and the environment. To respect our resources and live \\\'green\\\' is beyond our capacity. THe majority can\\\'t do it, won\\\'t do it. But that doesn\\\'t mean that those of us who strive to live respecfully should junk our belief systems. We will keep on keepin\\\' on, even though we know it\\\'s in vain. Sometimes that knowledge alone is enough to live for.</P.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(10755,1976,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>\\\"<a href=\\\"http://people.ne.mediaone.net/neirr/millions.htm\\\">millions</a> <a href=\\\"http://donaldk0.tripod.com/millions/\\\">now</a> <a href=\\\"http://trout.sputcorp.com/reviews/frame_reviews/millions_r.htm\\\">living</a> <a>\\\"http://www.watchtowernews.org/media/knorr4.html\\\"</a>will <a href=\\\"http://dianedew.com/jwmillns.htm\\\">never</a> <a href=\\\"http://www.inkblotmagazine.com/rev-archive/tortoise1.htm\\\">die</a>\\\"',3,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(10756,1979,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','you really shouldnt end a sentence with a preposition. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(10757,1975,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>i want to see a picture of uncle swinney and yeti in thongs with yeti spanking swinney with some kind of fancy computer hardware device. in a wooded basement with gun racks on the wall holding stuffed lambs and donkeys and pandas. that would be september. </p>\r\n<p>le riding a unicorn with a megaphone through downtown san francisco yelling at people. many passersby.november</p>\r\n<p>any self-portrait of simon in new york in the winter.december</p>\r\n<p>essemar and peter with little mustaches as two jersey shore guidos on the boardwalk, or alternately, in tall mohawks and ratty leather jackets on the coney island roller coaster.  or perhaps a more serious/poetic photograph in front of the current ruins? june.</p>\r\n<p>chi chi as a giant in a miniature heba hobba breeding scene using miniature plastic dollhouse horses. may.</p>\r\n<p>olivia in a 3 piece suit with s5 in a bikini on her lap, drinking a mai tai, lips puckered. august. </p>\r\n<p>lulu dressed as a hemorrhoid on halloween.october.</p>\r\n<p>adamajah eating a handsome pie with a tall tower of cream and ice cream on top and a cherry.april</p>\r\n<p> and leonard as a male underwear model for sears. this is the swinney.org semiotic equivalent to baby new year. january.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(10758,1979,'2001-10-25 19:50:46',0,'essemar to egg','That\\\'s not really true.  That\\\'s just something the old school grammarians who wacked knuckles with ruler used to say.  A preposition is a fine thing to end a sentence with.  (Sometimes impossible to avoid when using split compound verbs.)',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10759,1979,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I\\\'ll show you my dangling participle if you show me your split infinitive? ;-)  pr0ny grammar!',1,0,0,0,'66.25.29.204','en'),(10760,1975,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','For March: egg\\\'s breasts with her smiling face superimposed over the nipples, tweaked to look like the sun in Teletubbies.<br><br>fertility and rebirth.',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(10761,1975,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Houston city theme.  What would that be?  Probably me sitting in my car motionionless on I-10 with no AC.  Need i mention it\\\'s 90 fucking degrees ... and it\\\'s almost fucking _November_?!  In the immortal words of Denis Leary: \\\"Whaaaat the fuuuuck?!\\\"  Oh, and a power line in the background above a low line of faceless warehouses.  Houston sux.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.29.204','en'),(10762,1971,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','\\\"porksword\\\" I\\\'ve been giggling to myself for 10 minutes thinking about that word.  :-)  That\\\'s one thing I love about swinney.org is all the funny words people come up with.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.29.204','en'),(10763,1975,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to egg\\\'s scenario','<P>i think we could pull off the one with me and yeti if you take the picture.</P>\r\n<P>my only reservation is seeing it hang on my dad\\\'s office wall.</P>',1,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10764,1976,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to Darwin','<P>millions is really a small number in this sort of discussion. Lets say more than two billion.  so Billions.</P>',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10765,1976,'2001-10-25 21:15:05',0,'bambi?','<img src=\\\"http://dieoff.org/Crash2.GIF\\\">',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10766,1976,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to Darwin','<P>oh my bad.  i thought it said millions now living will die, which of course is a given.</P>\r\n<P>i have the reading skills of a copy editor.</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(10767,1976,'2001-10-26 15:30:14',0,'-I+++','we can prevent every single one of those deaths, if we act now.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(10768,1975,'2001-10-25 21:50:03',0,'adamajah to egg','<p>yes, i <i>do</i> have a sweet tooth</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.120.104.101','en'),(10769,1975,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to adamajah','i was thinking it could be shot in \\\"house of pies\\\" too, with one of those crochety waiters featured in the background. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(10770,1975,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon to egg','hey, i have a few shots of me in my old apt. on 4th street in the winter. perhaps i\\\'ll send one along',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(10771,1976,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Wsht\\\'qui\\\'jit\\\'ong Moll','Why bother? Why try to preseve something that doesn\\\'t work? Why try to save a civilization that\\\'s backwards, decadent, and inherently self-destructive? That thrives on fear and mediocrity? That takes, except for perhaps the hard sciences and commerce, all of our best qualities and shits on them? <i>Why pity</i> such a thing when it finally gets what it deserves? Don\\\'t we deserve better?\r\n ',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(10772,1907,'2001-10-26 01:52:36',0,'o to kat','<p>oh, you wanted a dead celebrity.  rats.  i watched dogma last night and thought you looked like the lead actress chick in that.  you know, the one who jay is always trying to fuck.  but, she\\\'s not dead, as far as i know.</p>',3,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(10773,1907,'2001-10-26 02:32:58',0,'kat to o','linda fiorentino? ...someone else said that just last week. i get catherine zeta jones a lot too. personally i wish i looked like lassie. i\\\'d be able to save the world. everyone would love me. i\\\'d get petted all the time. fed. brushed. washed. and i could chase after hot mandog flesh like Old Yeller. (or was he a she too?) ',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.112','en'),(10774,1971,'2001-10-26 02:38:53',0,'wyndrider','On the subject of girth:\r\nThe better the cushion, the better the pushin!',3,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(10775,1780,'2001-10-26 04:16:03',0,'virgo','i like robbie, he gives good virgo \\\'you will meet a tall handsome stranger, who will tell you that you have a borderline personality-- and will run you ragged with buying lotto tickets.\\\'',0,0,0,0,'12.81.83.179','en'),(10776,1907,'2001-10-26 05:51:17',0,'o to kat','<p>but, in dogma, she does save the world.  you\\\'re a hero!</p>',3,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(10777,1976,'2001-10-26 10:00:23',0,'sospirando','<p>this is sort of a downer topic, and this post is no different, i guess... so skip it if you\\\'re into bunnies and daisies today. sorry its so long, too.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nle articulates a struggle i live with daily... some days i get up and say \\\'we\\\'re so doomed\\\', other days i get up and say \\\'we\\\'re so doomed but fuck it, i\\\'m going down swingin\\\'. i think i realize the futility of it most of the time, all the more since Sept 11th -- which, bad as it was, pretty much put the kabosh on, or at least set back so-called progressivism (however you want to describe it... leftism, greenism, social-democraticism, naderism, commonsensism) for years to come, and that  unconstitutional PATRIOT act shit they just passed (which couldn\\\'t be further from) has opened the door (wider) to the potential for some creepy creeping fascism/totalitarianism. <a href=\\\"http://www.citypaper.net/articles/101801/news.godfrey.shtml\\\"> its already slowly, sublty getting into some scary Orwellian-type shit.</a>. i hear Bush say this will be \\\"a long protracted struggle against the enemies of freedom\\\" and can only think \\\"Long Live Oceania\\\".</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe urge to grab someone who\\\'s willing to go and run as far the fuck away as i possibly can and set up a little shack where i can\\\'t get the paper or see television or even get online (shock, horror!) occurs often, almost daily... this is pure and total selfishness, i realize, but also may be a way to achieve some sort of modicum of personal happiness, which becomes not as selfish if one\\\'s outlook is the way mine is? or self fulfilling prophecy, more like? perhaps. cop out? i don\\\'t know. is being happy in the face of all of it the biggest fuck you you can give \\\'em or is it the sad, selfish extreme of personal unilateralism, as so well drilled into all of us since day one? and will running away help or just turn me into the unabomber or militia member or some dumb shit like that? {sigh} </p>\r\n<p>\r\ni often wonder what this place would be like if we were all taught and brought up to collaborate, rather than compete, with each other...\r\n<p>\r\ni try in my waking hours in my own way to support progressive causes, more than just reading the leftie rags while i sip my half-caf soy milk double latte (just kidding... my no-frills cup of coffee...), and am more actively involved in that aspect of things than any of my friends, but i usually do so with a heavy sense of futility - which in it\\\'s own way is also selfish, - i suppose if i also knew what to do that would _really_ make a difference, i might do that instead. i\\\'m even a freak enough to write letters to my representatives and senators sometimes, knowing full well that they\\\'ll go straight to the round file if they in any way interfere with the big business agenda (and most of them do), or maybe even get my name sent over to be put onto John Ashcroft\\\'s long list of lefties...  so much for \\\"democracy\\\". and then, to top it off, simply for being critical and showing a desire to improve and somehow make a little more honorable  what my country\\\'s government does in my name, even my own family thinks i\\\'m \\\"unamerican\\\"!</p>\r\n<p>\r\nle said something there about the \\\"progressive\\\" people being the somewhat aware people, or some such, and that awareness fuckin\\\' numbs you after a while. the Trade Center attacks were shocking, yeah, but they didn\\\'t _hurt_ anyone who knows even a little bit about what\\\'s been going on for decades -- we are numb at this point. if you look into the death and destruction brought on by the CIA since their inception following WWII, it\\\'s a history of nothing but support of or direct involvement in oppression, torture and death to just about any worker\\\'s or democratic movements anywhere in the world that threaten to take hold, because those things are antithetical to the almighty \\\"US Interests\\\". why do you think hatred of us exists in the world? because we are \\\"free\\\"? i can\\\'t believe they get away with that line. it\\\'s more because we prop up governments that oppress the fuck out of people. our companies go into their countries under the rubric of \\\'free-trade\\\' and knock out local economies, and sometimes even kill dissidents, as Chevron and Shell have done, both directly and indirectly, in Nigeria. in the last decade alone, our foreign policy led directly to the deaths of hundreds of thousands of Iraqi people, many of them children. the gulf war purposefully destroyed a lot of Iraq\\\'s water infrastructure and the sanctions that followed prevented equipment and chlorine for water purification, ultimately causing a lot of otherwise preventable civilian deaths and prompted Madeliene Albright to utter the now famous quote \\\"we think the price is worth it\\\". well, as a taxpaying American, i say it\\\'s not worth it, and furthermore, fuck you, Madeliene Albright, and fuck you Clinton Administration. fuck you Bush the first, fuck you Reagan (especially), fuck you Carter, Ford, Nixon and Kissinger, and down the fucking line. your hands are all bloody.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nin my heart, i don\\\'t think humankind is meant to be the ones to evolve to the higher plane or whatever it is that\\\'s supposed to happen, if anything is supposed to happen anyway (which is another discussion). humankind has made some cool creations and built some pretty interesting societies --AND we have beer, mexican food and john coltrane... i think some people here are ready to think about one connected consciousness, harmonious living and all that new age stuff, but the ones in power couldn\\\'t give a flying fuck about anything other than getting themselves elected next time around. and god knows in the last election, we had no choice (well, we had one, but a lot of people lost their nerve) about this issue that couldn\\\'t be of more dire importance: foreign policy and our place in the world and how we get along with our fellow humans, cuz our \\\'candidates\\\' were both sucking the neoliberal, free-trade cock, as if there were anything else they could do, which will stop at nothing to elbow its way into every nook and cranny of this world where there\\\'s anything that can be called a \\\'market\\\' (not a \\\'people\\\' but a \\\'market\\\'). the US will be a 3rd world country soon as all the jobs continue to go overseas because its cheaper and people here get sucked into the \\\'service economy\\\' because its all that\\\'s left, when before they had better paying manufacturing or whatever jobs...</p>\r\n<p>\r\nso what does one DO? create? yeah! fuck? i guess (i think i\\\'ve forgotten about how to do that at this point, but i guess people do it), eat, drink, dance, rock out, laugh, and do our best to forget about it... ? </p>\r\n<p>\r\nor do we do all those things and also fight the greed and antipathy to the last breath? are they mutually exclusive? will it matter one way or the other? blah. as austin said, cosmic life will \\\"rip on\\\", and whether we remain a part of it or not makes no real difference anyway, right?</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbut doesn\\\'t our ability to even ask the question make us more responsible than that? and doesn\\\'t our relative luxury compared to a lot of the world make us more responsible to try to spread some of that luxury around a bit and improve conditions for all while we\\\'re all stuck on this rock? </p>\r\n<p>\r\nin the face of all that ranting, i still must have a shred of optimism about the whole thing - or else why would i care enough to follow it at all, think about it all, right? i still believe in the idea of critical mass-  once a vocal minority begins to congeal and grow, then the voice gets louder and if it makes sense, more folks will join on. for now, i guess this means keeping the infrastructure of non-violent opposition alive until enough folks come looking for something, either out of conscience or out of necessity. and they\\\'re slowly starting to. the message becomes quite clear when airline workers are laid off by the thousands and the gov\\\'t bails out the industry, which was failing anyway, but doesn\\\'t even bother to make sure that the workers keep their jobs (and one of the chief lobbyists for the airlines is the Senate Majority Leader\\\'s fucking WIFE?!).</p>\r\n<p>\r\nanyway, screw this babbling, i\\\'m off to red wine-ify myself to sleep. sorry if i went on and on, and accept a glassful from me for reading this far...</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmy candle, while extremely dim, remains lit. love, peace, hope, stop the fucking bombing, and all that kinda hippie shit to all y\\\'all.</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.170.190.204','en'),(10778,1975,'2001-10-26 11:27:54',0,'jo','i just spent <i>far</i> too long trying to imagine how one would construct a hemorrhoid costume.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(10779,1975,'2001-10-26 11:33:39',0,'jo','one of <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/2001/peter_pool/11_jo_peter.jpg\\\">peter and me</a> from last night\\\'s turbodog introduction.<BR>\r\nbmg: i\\\'m thinking maybe tonight? in costume?',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(10780,1983,'2001-10-26 14:08:52',0,'ghost','<b><i>balance and flow</i><b> it is all cyclical. tidal pull. moon phase metamorphosis. pain...raging...content. it is all cyclical. please remember this! \r\n\r\n<p>[and yes...it is okay to talk to yourself]</p>',2,0,0,0,'32.102.64.193','en'),(10781,1983,'2001-10-26 14:10:15',0,'hopeless','</b>sigh',0,0,0,0,'32