ing the drudgery of grad school again this spring. road trips rule. im gonna see if i can get my car past the 600k mark!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'66.65.50.61','en'),(11278,2043,'2001-11-11 19:08:31',0,'jo','elly millican, i am in boston in an mit lab. stefan and jessica and kristy say hello. well they don\\\'t really but i say they do. okay but listen! the reason that i am posting a comment is because just the other day, my friend john ratliff said: \\\"sprouts are useless! they are like pubic hair but less fun!\\\" okay bye.',1,0,0,0,'18.63.1.12','en'),(11279,2043,'2001-11-11 19:31:47',0,'drew','mmmm...sprouts.\r\n<p>kill your main character or send him away.  let everyone else talk about how much they hate him.</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11280,2043,'2001-11-11 20:22:59',0,'da','',0,0,0,0,'209.86.221.48','en'),(11281,2043,'2001-11-11 20:25:35',0,'da','and i thought i was the only person with sprout issues.  hunh!  you nice people have fun down there.  if you fly south enough at first when you\\\'re leaving town, wave or flush the airplane toilet a couple times when you see the channel islands.  i\\\'m sitting here staring at the clouds and waiting for stuff to fall on my head like usual.',1,0,0,0,'209.86.221.48','en'),(11282,2043,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','sprouts are just not in style at all anymore. no need to pretend they\\\'re not completely worthless.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.4.106','en'),(11283,2041,'2001-11-11 20:32:22',0,'and','silence',1,0,0,0,'32.100.70.25','en'),(11284,2043,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','I used to grow sprouts in jars with little metal screens in the lids. Yay for sprouts! :)\r\n',1,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(11285,2043,'2001-11-11 21:22:11',0,'auntie','i like sprouts on tuesdays.',4,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11286,2044,'2001-11-11 22:28:00',0,'sawks','holy shit, that ftp has the mad commodore64 goodies\r\n\r\nthnx',2,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11287,2043,'2001-11-11 22:35:34',0,'drew','is never in style.  therefore i feel for sprouts.  we have a kinship.  of course, sprouts taste weird and crunchy and stringy and wrong, reminding you that you\\\'re cruelly eating them before their time, and i, so far as i know, do not taste this way.  but i like them anyway.  i don\\\'t like veal anymore, though.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11288,2043,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','some sprouts are like nuts but less hurty to eat. those are good. some sprouts only germinate in between those spaces between your teeth, they\\\'re evolutionary engineered to wedge themselves betwixt tooth and gum, to thrive. rainforests of thick ropey vine and bushery, whose only purpose is to destroy the yummi slurpi smoothness of your avocado. they take over. hang out on your uvula like teenagers. i like em when i\\\'m feeling masochistic about being healthy.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11289,2043,'2001-11-11 23:52:16',0,'ook','<P>i cannot handle eating sprouts since i worked at a food inspection lab, testing for germs. sprouts and spices are the germiest of all foods. sprouts can neither be washed properly nor sterilized as sprouts, nor even in the seed stage. they are one big, moist, incubated surface area for microbes. degoutant! spices are still ok by me because i need them. i don\\\'t need the sprouts.</P>',1,0,0,0,'209.148.183.248','en'),(11290,2025,'2001-11-12 00:00:41',0,'justino','dilute dilute',4,0,0,0,'43.224.178.14','en'),(11291,2025,'2001-11-12 00:01:00',0,'justino','ok.',4,0,0,0,'43.224.178.14','en'),(11292,2025,'2001-11-12 00:04:32',0,'fuller-cousteau-ruskin-justin','WE\\\'RE ALL ONE OR NONE!  \\\"LISTEN CHILDREN ETERNAL FATHER ETERNALLY ONE!\\\"  WE\\\'RE ALL ONE OR NONE!  EXCEPTIONS ETERNALLY?  NONE!\r\n\r\namen\r\nhttp://www.drbronner.com/',1,0,0,0,'43.224.178.14','en'),(11293,2042,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Thanks kids.  You\\\'re swell!  I feel welcome now.<br><br>Mike, could I cash in that rain check for our reading week bender voyage into the USofA.  Do you remember the one?  The trip where we rent a car and drive southward, not turning back until we successfully find <b>Champagne Velvet Grape</b>?<br><br>That could... scratch that.  That <i>would</i>, get messy, regardless of whether said product still existed.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.72.123','en'),(11294,2045,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','amen.',2,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11295,2045,'2001-11-12 06:00:06',0,'jay','<p>Was it dire necessity, then? Will you be separated again?</p>\r\n<p>I always love your posts, Auntie. Even if you\\\'re not a crier, you experience emotion quite plainly enough.</p>',2,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11296,2030,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','whee! le and peter made their connection so, barring mechanical troubles (which happened to me on my last lax->akl->mel flight), i\\\'ll be picking them up tomorrow morning. fingers crossed!',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11297,2042,'2001-11-12 06:56:15',0,'cynn','it\\\'s your\\\'s to cash in whenever you want.  personally, i can\\\'t think of many other road trips that could compete with a drive to the other side of the continent, followed by what could only be described as a surreal week on a prehistoric lakebed.\r\n\r\n</salespitch>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.83','en'),(11298,2025,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>washing the \\\"extreme dirty crotch\\\" genitals with dr bronners gives the sensation that one has had hot sauce applied to one\\\'s pussy - kind of a cooling and burning sensation all at once, like a pussy snake bite with an ice compress on it.</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11299,2042,'2001-11-12 07:03:08',0,'cynn','&lt;/salespitch> ...\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.83','en'),(11300,2045,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>I still have these pangs like, if you can spend long periods of time seperated from someone you love then it\\\'s proof of your will/discipline/king fu mastery. I often fail at this challenge, but when I meet it I am very proud of myself. Is this a sick mark of youth? Of pride? Have I not yet learned what real love is?</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11301,2042,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','don\\\'t be afraid of a little <h1>WE</h1> in your life, le.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11302,1958,'2001-11-12 17:55:36',0,'Nadia','Me! Me! :D Running round in circles!',0,0,0,0,'161.74.55.19','en'),(11303,2036,'2001-11-12 18:09:13',0,'coyo','<p>i would like to hear more about why the young dotcom marketing hipsters in their etnies and hoodies weren\\\'t any cooler than their gray slacks and oxford clothed parents.</p>\r\n\r\nmy take:\r\n<p>we still blew the same smoke up each other\\\'s arses the same technical empty marketing-argot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"We need to get a focus group total customer satisfaction marketing survey in this ASP space.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but somehow it was worse: we lacked humility. so young, 19, and 20, and 21 making big bucks, driving new cars, buying new Gap Supersize meal deal outfit number 5s.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At least our parents had to scrimp and sweat and live in a trailer house before they became yuppie trash.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m glad the dotcom crashed-- humility will shape us, like the generations that grew up during the Depression.</p>',3,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(11304,901,'2001-11-12 18:43:32',0,'bmg','I read this again and again. What ever happened to incitor? ',1,0,0,0,'66.90.152.145','en'),(11305,2043,'2001-11-12 19:22:11',0,'jo','i actually very much like sprouts. both bean and alfalfa. especially with ranch dressing and thinly sliced red cabbage and cucumbers. mmmm.',0,0,0,0,'18.56.0.30','en'),(11306,2025,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','the crotch as a peppermint lifesaver candy. perhaps it sparks in the dark if you lick it enough. ooh! there is no checkbox for porny comments!@',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11307,901,'2001-11-12 20:06:13',0,'ghost','i have him all to myself<br>\r\nlike the sky<br>\r\nhe breathes green truths<br>\r\nand rains death drops<br>\r\nof zero<br>\r\nand silent strength\r\n',4,0,0,0,'32.100.70.248','en'),(11308,2047,'2001-11-12 20:11:38',0,'ghost','i once had a pigeon shit on my head while waiting in the subway in London. yes, underground...in the middle of a massive crowd of business commuters. one of my more humbling moments. <i>[i could have killed that bird with my bare hands]</i>',1,0,0,0,'32.100.70.248','en'),(11309,2046,'2001-11-12 21:07:15',0,'ryan','oregon is good.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.41','en'),(11310,2025,'2001-11-12 21:18:30',0,'memoryboy','my jewish ex, bess, i will never forget your dr. bronners peppermint soap... showering after a lovely winter\\\'s early evening of love-- i still take a sniff of the stuff, when i\\\'m in the hippy stores to remember',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(11311,2043,'2001-11-12 21:20:58',0,'anti-sprout coalition','i hate it when they microwave sprouts, that\\\'s the worst. i go to new leaf, grab the tofu turkey sproud sandwich and scrap those little guys off.\r\n<br>\r\ni do like their little sperm shape.',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(11312,2047,'2001-11-12 21:34:05',0,'no one','goddamn, you make me miss nyc. i wish i knew where i belonged.',1,0,0,0,'63.215.86.154','en'),(11313,2043,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I don\\\'t have much to add to the sprout debate except that I generally agree with elly--they\\\'re unruly. I\\\'m not sure what the point of eating them is--you might just as well chew on some lawn clippings.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'m a regular patron of many SF cafes, but the Abir is my current favorite. It\\\'s warm, friendly, homey. Even though I moved away from the neighborhood awhile ago (I had a studio at the corner of Page & Lyon for five years) I continue to gravitate there on a regular basis. I just wish they\\\'d quit playing that g-d dmx cable music. Yesterday it was \\\"New Wave Hits of the 80\\\'s\\\" all day long. A few days before that it was all boy bands & Britney. Aaargh!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I like the Abir\\\'s vegie sandwiches ok, but what really hits the spot for me is their tuna/pasta salad. Mmmmm.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11314,2042,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','is that <b>we</b>, or <b>we are borg</b>? ;)',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11315,2025,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','so glad to know your ex was jewish. some of my best friends are jews.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11316,2025,'2001-11-12 22:18:44',0,'clamato','some of my best friends are juice!',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11317,2025,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','listening to the rain swashling around 580 in infinite circles. isabel allende, at the mills 2000 graduation ceremony, asked \\\"what is this with people calling each other \\\'MY PARTNER\\\' now? doesn\\\'t anyone fall madly in love anymore?\\\"</p><p> and I must say that I am most irritable when Im solving, explaining, describing, and designing. That the logopoeia of my imagination is stunted when I have to deal with such pragmatics as time, place, position on the page, and navigation in a way that all is clean and orderly for everyone.</p> <p>that is what the term \\\"partner\\\" is. it is an egalitarian way of describing a relationship that has thought through all the practical and fair uses of language to create something devoid of power dynamics and bloodlines.</p><p> I am not musing about my own love relationships or anything else literal like that. Im just saying that saturn is catching up with me, and that is what a rash is. It is your blood asking to circulate with more clean regularity than you\\\'ve given it. it is telling you that your body\\\'s time to keep sane in a messy room and young without regular exercise and comfortable without unpleasant work is running, running, running out.  I wanted to record this thought even though it is a total clusterfuck and makes little sense, so that I can remember it later.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11318,2047,'2001-11-12 22:26:13',0,'green','J.Crew fuck J. Crew you should watch IRONMAN -TATSUO\r\n\r\nIts great',3,0,0,0,'198.81.16.189','en'),(11319,2048,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','the dean of the art dept. at UT (dave demming ?) welds dogs together for a living.  they are steel dogs.  he sucks as an artist.',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11320,2043,'2001-11-12 22:52:50',0,'Pink Godzilla','you will eat my beautiful sushi...you will love the tuna and sprouts...you will worship the Manresa roll like no other. ',4,0,0,0,'32.102.109.196','en'),(11321,2042,'2001-11-12 22:59:08',0,'slowshuffle','ix you is, or ix you aint my baby?',4,0,0,0,'32.102.109.196','en'),(11322,2042,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','>:(',5,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11323,2025,'2001-11-13 00:17:46',0,'drew','i don\\\'t like \\\"partner\\\" at all.  it\\\'s as bad as the term \\\"relationship.\\\"  the emotions and situations expressed by those words are exactly what i don\\\'t want in my love life.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11324,2025,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','\\\"partner\\\" should be reserved for business relationships. i never use it. \\\"fucktoy\\\", \\\"lapmonkey\\\", and \\\"snugglebucket\\\" are acceptable replacements, though.',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11325,2025,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','my personal favourite is \\\"cumdumpster\\\".',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11326,2025,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','\\\"cumdumpster\\\" is good. but i think the word \\\"boyfriend\\\" is sort of odious. so is \\\"girlfriend.\\\" I dont know why exactly.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11327,2025,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i like \\\"lover\\\". it sounds like something  clandestine is taking place. boyfriend/girlfriend is fine too. though often it\\\'s nice to refer to people by name, rather than by a role or title.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11328,2049,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','<BR><BR>\\\"Oh, it\\\'s such a perfect day<BR>I\\\'m glad I spent it with you<BR>Oh, such a perfect day<BR>You just keep me hanging on<BR>You just keep me hanging on<BR><BR>You\\\'re going to reap just what you sow\\\"<BR><BR><BR>today, i helped my dad and my uncle put a roof on the house.  i hated it, but i\\\'ll look back on it fondly someday.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.208.28.82','en'),(11329,2045,'2001-11-13 02:14:59',0,'auntie','yes, it was a necessity -- this time. it was go to kuwait or be fired from his job.  so he went.<br><br>\r\n\r\negg -- really creative people often have no control over so much of their lives, they try to find ways to prove to themselves that they are strong, sometimes the things they choose to rule are not the right ones.  don\\\'t try to rule love. it falls under inspiration in the category of things that should not be tampered with by imposing rules upon it.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthis is one of the things people who are married successfully for a long time have learned, i think.  it\\\'s a hard thing to learn, and even harder to live.<br><br>\r\n\r\nexample -- from the outside, mark and i look like such disparate souls, and we interact publicly in ways that many people don\\\'t understand.  that\\\'s not important.  i don\\\'t care what my friends think of him, he doesn\\\'t care what his friends think of me.  we have some friends together, people who have known us for a long, long time.  most of our friends we have \\\"apart.\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\ndo you understand what i mean by that?  when mark is off doing whatever he likes to do, that is the time i make for MY friends, by phone or in person. when i am busy in front of the machine or making something and don\\\'t want to be talked to, that is the time he makes for HIS friends.  i don\\\'t dislike his friends, they are just not people i enjoy spending time with very often, and vice versa.  we worked this arrangement out over the years without really discussing it, it just evolved.<br><br>\r\n\r\nauntie advice -- be a master of control about cooking or something else that doesn\\\'t involved controlling the interactions you have with people or animals. you\\\'ll find it makes life less complicated, for living things thrive best on spontaneity (i never can remember how to spell that.)<br><br>\r\nlove and kisses, mom',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11330,2025,'2001-11-13 02:39:45',0,'drew','\\\"buddy\\\" is nice, but it\\\'s more of a pet name and an inside joke than something i could use in conversation with a third party.  i like \\\"boyfriend\\\" and \\\"girlfriend\\\" just fine, though when we get to be as old as lou reed and laurie anderson it will start to sound weird.  another pet peeve of mine is women using the word \\\"girlfriends\\\" to refer to close (but not that close) female friends.  this is not only confusing but inexplicably sexist.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11331,2049,'2001-11-13 02:44:42',0,'memoryboy','<p>i tell myself sometimes \\\'oh just go do-- such and such with grandma, dad, mom (eventhough they will bitch and moan and complain, often about eachother, the whole time) because... someday they will be gone.\\\'</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there is such a sad, wistful feeling to family, especially family that hasn\\\'t always been good to be in.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but then what was a mixed, impatient, cranky day, becomes something else bathed in the golden light of nostalgia.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sort of like the reverse process of eating. what was shit, becomes a fine meal (everything is a body-function metaphor for me lately). I might try colonic cleansing.</p>',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(11332,2049,'2001-11-13 02:57:34',0,'kat','<p>and yes...well, we could say that you must say what you feel when you feel it. the love. whatever. hey yo, i love you! and would that be so hard? would that be so horrible? would it make a difference? i don\\\'t no. it seems most people runaway from the good stuff as much as they say they want it. but then well you could end up feeling <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1612.txt\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">like this</a> and then what??? i shut myself off these days...mostly cause i\\\'ve run out of energy to do much else. hibernation. it is hard to be in anyone\\\'s company for more than an hour or two...though that hour or two is usually quiet awesome. after that the lights go out. i\\\'m okay with this, but most don\\\'t seem to understand. and i crave to be near those that do...because they\\\'re the ones i can be with for hours on end and it doesn\\\'t matter if i sit still or rant or cry or smile my smile and say nothing. cause they know i am still grieving.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>yes. i didn\\\'t realize this till the other day. i am still grieving. and i find it almost impossible to say her name. and i don\\\'t know why.</p>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.182','en'),(11333,2025,'2001-11-13 03:05:10',0,'chi chi','i call my female friends \\\"girlfriends\\\" and i also refer to my male friends as \\\"boyfriends\\\". i don\\\'t see anything sexist about it.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.44.87','en'),(11334,2047,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>everyone\'s</i> your friend in new york city. my favourite three subway moments:<br><br><b>1.</b> Seeing a PCP-fueled obese man perform feats of super-human strength on the L train, levitating himself from the bars, etc.<br><b>2.</b> Watching a little kid try to stand on his tiptoes to look out of the front of the C train as it was approaching 103rd; a kindhearted old construction-worker sitting nearby offered his toolbox for the kid to stand on... the mom promptly drug the kid away, while saying \"Bobby, <b><i>NO!</b></i> dont you know that that man could <i>rape</i> you?\"[gotta love them upper west side moms.]<br><b>3.</b> riding the B train while tripping, and proclaiming to all the other riders (with interpretive dance) that i was certain that the destination sign said \"Bergen Wonderdwarf\", although i still have no idea what that means.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11335,2025,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my somewhat ex-lover used to introduce me to people by sayting \\\"this is mine\\\". i liked that; it made me feel like i was made of gold.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11336,2051,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','yah this evening of work isnt going that well already again. Too many distactions. need I say, too much fun. I had trouble yesterday working because I had a kitty lion crawling all over the house shaking its velvety pink ass at me, and tonight kristin jay and I are boisterously belting out show tunes. i showed kristin some chair-dance moves and now shes begging me to choreograph for her show this weekend. some kind of absurd \\\"all that jazz hands\\\" shit from my 10 yr old jazz dancing class. and then I just remembered, the deadline. the deadline. I wish I were dead.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11337,2049,'2001-11-13 04:34:10',0,'auntie','being sick sucks, but one thing it teaches you is what your dad tried to tell you.  there are moments in time that will be with you forever, moments of joy and moments of sadness and grief.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen you share your dad with us, no matter how far and deep into the woods he travels, you leave a trail of pebbles, not bread crumbs, and the rest of us pick up the shiny pebbles and ponder their beauty and they become a moment for us, too.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni will never think of xavier cugat again without thinking of how delighted your dad must have been to have seen him that night.  it\\\'s a pebble laced with veins of gold.<br><br>\r\n\r\nauntie advice -- don\\\'t sweat the other marriage, the other kids, the things that make him sad.  this is a time for him to look back on the things that gave him joy, and to share them with you.  there\\\'s enough pain in his world already without diggng up the past.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlove and kisses, mom',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11338,2051,'2001-11-13 05:28:34',0,'kat','jezzus fuck...thanksgiving is next week? fuckit. i\\\'m begging out of the holidays this year. i wanna do the end of August through September again. can we put this on repeat loop please??? ',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.182','en'),(11339,2043,'2001-11-13 06:07:58',0,'ross','<p>\r\nWhat does this have to do with the price of bean sprouts?\r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'64.81.65.69','en'),(11340,2046,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i <a href=\"http://www.reed.edu\">consider</a> <a href=\"http://elly.org/there/eugene/\">oregon</a> regularly.',4,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11341,2043,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yay ross is here!',4,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11342,2043,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i thought i was going to die at manresa once when i was about 10 and got sucked out into the sea in a current called rip.  the toe called under.  (the job called blow.)\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11343,2025,'2001-11-13 09:53:50',0,'drew','chi chi, no one else i know does that.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11344,2051,'2001-11-13 09:54:30',0,'drew','i know this will sound facetious, but right now nipples are one of the only things that still make sense to me in a world gone mad.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11345,2051,'2001-11-13 10:29:49',0,'wb','two things i\\\'m unsure about this post:<BR>\r\n1. being a \\\"buy nothing\\\" commie but going to see harry potter.<BR>\r\n\r\n2.jollitude.<BR><BR>\r\nim sure that doing whippits from whipped cream is something i did when i was exactly 14 years old.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11346,2025,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','what does <i>egalitarian</i> mean? it comes up a lot in books about religion. i never bothered to look it up but i\\\'ve been assuming it means \\\"equal.\\\" ',4,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11347,2025,'2001-11-13 13:57:43',0,'<b>dictionary.com</b>','<i>use</i> me.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11348,2051,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i was at a book store last night and a little kid was running around the isles chanting, \\\"Harry Potter...Harry Potter..!Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..Harry Potter..\\\"  He was like a krishna.  it was then that i realized, this harry potter stuff is going to be everywhere.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11349,2049,'2001-11-13 17:58:32',0,'i try','effexor xr 75mg',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11350,2048,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','welding large sculptures of animals together. ooh that\\\'s rough. it reminds me of a guy who lives downtown la and has shows at the coffee shop down there. ugh. man. we shot some scenes of a movie in his place. we were there for like two weeks and everyday he wore the same pair of nut-hugging, ass-sagging pants.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11351,2051,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','i am going to have two children, a boy and a girl, and name them Harry Potter One and Harry Potter Two.\r\n\r\nshiot, i gotta buy xmas presents.',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11352,2051,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to adamajah','just get everyone a box of whippets!',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11353,2051,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to wb','and i quote, \\\" So Im not exactly a commie, but I support the anti-consumer spirit, with my mouth if not my money.\\\". that was re: being \\\"buy nothing\\\" and still going to see harry potter. i have it covered, pal. dont take me on.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11354,2048,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>oh, welding statues together is like art cars. I have a problem with art cars. why? can you explain it to me simon? I appreciate the festive-ness. I dont like art cars the same way I dont like pschedelia. why? is it just burning man?</p>\r\n<p>but art food. now that is another story.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11355,2052,'2001-11-13 21:07:18',0,'for feet rot','i\\\'ve been trying these new desinex(tm) wipes, they sure do the trick, but i think i will have to burn all my shoes-- i\\\'m like imelda with my shoes...',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11356,2036,'2001-11-13 21:09:56',0,'hey','where\\\'s my boxster??!!',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11357,2051,'2001-11-13 21:14:46',0,'leonard','can\\\'t wait to see <a href=\\\"http://www.filmjerk.com/011106potter.html\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">harry potter</a>.',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(11358,2048,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','what is psychedelia? ravy t-shirts and tie-dye flyer graphics? or the actual psychedelic drug(s)?',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11359,2025,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','trying to find a way to tell other people what your love means to you-- is pretty hard. i always flounder and fall back on \\\"my boy,\\\" \\\"the boy\\\" or similar nebulous inexactitudes. i shudder to think what will happen when i get married- will i ever get used to introducing anyone as \\\"my husband?\\\" shudder!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11360,2025,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','steve sometimes refers to me as \\\"my person\\\", which i like.  i think it\\\'s still okay to call someone your lover or person or boy or boyfriend or whatever, even if you\\\'re married.  it\\\'s better than \\\"the old ball and chain\\\", at any rate.',2,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11361,2048,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i like to do psychedelic drugs. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11362,2025,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i tend to say \\\"the old man.\\\" or \\\"my old man.\\\" or \\\"The Dude.\\\" if im introducing someone to someone, i usually just use that persons name. otherwise i say, \\\"this is baby bubba sea lion boo boo noogy noogy, isnt he the boooooo\\\"....I do refer to girlfriends as girlfriends. I dont know why I dont say boyfriends. I dont like boys. theyre not my friends. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11363,2048,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i think she\\\'s dissing alex grey, weeb',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11364,2025,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','misandry!',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11365,2052,'2001-11-14 05:40:53',0,'jo','i am in boston right now. it\\\'s cold and today on the street i ran into 3 people that i knew of the total dozen people i know in the city. i\\\'d say those are some pretty great statistics!',0,0,0,0,'172.173.214.204','en'),(11366,2025,'2001-11-14 05:50:20',0,'jo','peter: just like a cancer to find sentimentality in terms of possession. stuff like that makes me squishy, too.<BR>\r\ni usually say \\\"boy,\\\" too, but this became tricky when i called my boy \\\"boy\\\" whether we were dating or not and he called me \\\"girl\\\" and introduced me typically as: \\\"this is joanna. THE joanna.\\\" which is also great because it\\\'s nice to know stories are being told about you.',0,0,0,0,'172.173.214.204','en'),(11367,2048,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i love Stubb\\\'s sauce and marinades. \\\"My life is in these bottles.\\\" \\\"Ladies and gentlemen, I\\\'m a cook.\\\" it cracks me up. \r\n\r\ni like to marinate pork chops in a ziploc bag. i fill a bowl with water, and dunk the bag with chop inside, zipper-opening up, which forces all the air out; then i beat said chop with my mighty meat hammer to really get the sauce in there.',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.193','en'),(11368,2054,'2001-11-14 08:42:56',0,'jim','I adore Holzer.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11369,2025,'2001-11-14 09:36:45',0,'jim','<p>\r\nmy wife frequently refers to me as her beloved.  this makes me feel like the best thing in the whole universe.  I call her \\\"my love\\\" or \\\"my sweetie\\\" or \\\"my wife\\\" to other people. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nI call her different things when we are alone.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\n I like \\\"lover\\\" for lovers who aren\\\'t her.  \\\"girlfriend\\\" and \\\"boyfriend\\\" have never seemed very passionate to me, and while I like some aspects of \\\"partner,\\\" it\\\'s lacks some essential joy.\r\n</p>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11370,2048,'2001-11-14 10:42:50',0,'ryan','lsd and oklahoma city + some beer, turned out pretty good.  a movie came out of it.  all 4 minutes of it.  more people should try lsd in strange places.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.151','en'),(11371,2025,'2001-11-14 10:45:45',0,'ryan','i always called people by name, but that is to be assumed i suppose. <p> i remember vividly my father and mother getting into it a bit and my father calling my mother \\\"dear\\\" and my mother replying \\\"i am not a forest animal\\\"   -- laying in bed as an 8 year old hearing this screamed has some impact on you.  names is is.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.151','en'),(11372,2055,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.goaskalice.columbia.edu/\\\">go ask alice</a>?</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11373,2055,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','when she\\\'s just small.',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.102','en'),(11374,2055,'2001-11-14 14:54:49',0,'The Doormouse','<br><tt>\\\"feed your head\\\"</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11375,2025,'2001-11-14 15:15:56',0,'ghost','i think i would swoon if someone introduced me as \\\'their love\\\' ...i never seem to end up with very romantic PARTNERS. heh. is it too early to ask Santa to put a squeezy in my stocking??? ',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.212','en'),(11376,2055,'2001-11-14 17:17:14',0,'jo','no, <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0689817851/\\\">go ask alice</a>.',0,0,0,0,'172.152.46.192','en'),(11377,2055,'2001-11-14 17:26:26',0,'critic','no, <a href=\\\"http://www.moviesunlimited.com/goto/553015.asp\\\">go ask alice</a>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11378,2055,'2001-11-14 17:31:03',0,'rad gamer','no, <a href=\\\"http://www.sharkygames.com/games/reviews/a/alice/\\\">go ask alice</a>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11379,1964,'2001-11-14 17:39:30',0,'Strawberry Shortcake','Cake is so played out. Try listening to Gloria Gaynor.',0,0,0,0,'209.182.158.45','en'),(11380,1964,'2001-11-14 17:58:09',0,'critic','yeah, like gaynor isn\\\'t overplayed <i>at all</i>...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11381,1964,'2001-11-14 18:23:55',0,'Strawberry Shortcake','Critic, taking things pretty literally, aren\\\'t we?',0,0,0,0,'209.182.158.45','en'),(11382,2048,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','yeah, STUBB\\\'S BBQ sauce is wicked tasty mang',2,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11383,2054,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I loves Holzier, too. Back when I was a UCSC frosh, I wrote dozens of self-made Holzierisms on 3x5 cards and added them to an expansive, chaotic collage I had going on in the hallway wallspace around my dorm room door. There were so many cards I ended up sticking them to the ceiling and hanging them from air vents. People would wander by on their way to somewhere else and get stuck there, trancelike, trying to make sense of it all.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>My favorite Holzierism (and the only one I can remember now, 14 years later) was \\\"THE WONDERS OF THE UNIVERSE ARE IN YOUR PANTS.\\\"</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11384,2011,'2001-11-14 18:49:18',0,'joyrider','<p>pynchon\\\'s amazing.  even \\\"slow learner,\\\" his early short stories anthology, has some really brilliant moments.</p>\r\n<p>once, i threw that book against a wall - feeling spiteful and jealous and inspired pretty much all at the same time. </p>',3,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(11385,2055,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','no, <a href=\\\"http://www.allmusic.com/cg/amg.dll?p=amg&sql=1ALICE|COOPER\\\">go ask alice</a>. (And get your snake fed while you\\\'re at it.)',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11386,2036,'2001-11-14 19:44:58',0,'Nastchenka','',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11387,2051,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','just read the first three harry potter books last night on my palm pilot (warez\\\'d books with a warez\\\'d reader, heh).  suprisingly good.  I might actually go buy the dead trees at some point.  I know I\\\'m going to go watch the movie.<br><br>\r\n<br>\r\n\\\"The nipple is the only intuitive interface.  All else is learned.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(11388,2048,'2001-11-14 21:51:27',0,'jo','you know, usually you bastards will get on some tangent about stubb\\\'s or magnolia migas or fish tacos and i will JUST GO EAT THE FOOD but now you\\\'re choosing to reminisce about austin food when i\\\'m in fucking boston and like half the planet away from stubb\\\'s? jerks.<BR>\r\n[x] best friendly.',0,0,0,0,'172.163.188.196','en'),(11389,2048,'2001-11-14 21:53:22',0,'jo','oh also, i recommend \\\"tofu pups,\\\" they\\\'re my favorite all purpose tofu dog that taste and smell most like hot dogs, least like plastic.<BR>\r\nhowever, never ever ever grill on a barbecue any brand of tofu dog. you will be very sad.',0,0,0,0,'172.163.188.196','en'),(11390,2045,'2001-11-14 22:40:22',0,'stella','You\\\'re a *good* Mom. \r\nI hope to such a person someday, full of wisdom and raw experience. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(11391,2048,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>try living in nyc and having a newly developed craving for austin food. mmmmm... <i>Magnolia Migas</i>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11392,2048,'2001-11-14 23:22:14',0,'sims to egg','i feel similarly. art cars, welded animals, burning man, i don\\\'t know. i just can\\\'t hang.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11393,2048,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>art cars</i>. i rode in a gypsy cab once that had <b>18</b> (i counted) jesus and/or mary statues stuck to its dashboard. i definitely buckled up!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11394,1003,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<img src=\"http://www.swinney.org/art/galleries/misc/savem.jpg\">',1,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11395,2056,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','It\\\'s always nice when, out of nowhere, the puke story makes an appearance.<br><br>The puke story is a personal favourite.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.73.122','en'),(11396,2055,'2001-11-15 03:53:17',0,'alice','who the fuck is alice?',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11397,615,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','THIS IS SO HILARIOUS. there is a debate in here about art criticism quite similar to one one of my newer posts. you people. pet topics.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11398,2048,'2001-11-15 05:07:58',0,'kdown','creation.sublimation.creation.sophistication.creation.manipulation.creation.constipation.creation.masturbation',2,0,0,0,'32.102.109.127','en'),(11399,2059,'2001-11-15 05:54:12',0,'wb','pussytina, jollitude',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11400,2059,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','there have been a lot of nina simone mentions in my life lately.',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11401,1003,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','??',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11402,2056,'2001-11-15 17:21:13',0,'stella','<P>On Bourbon Street the other night, we were stolling around, having a marvelous time. I (luckily) looked down and narrowly missed stepping in a large pinkish gray pool of vomit. Thanking god for having the forsight to look down, I burst out in grossed-out laughter when a gentleman walking in our opposite direction did NOT have the luck to notice the mess. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>We reached the end of the block and there, in the shadows, of a doorway, was the defiler. Poor guy was propped up, half passed out, heaving all over himself. Ruining a perfectly good Armani shirt. A few things popped into my head at that moment:</P>\r\n<P>\\\"Ew\\\"</P>\r\n<P>\\\"He\\\'s gonna get robbed\\\"</P>\r\n<P>\\\"He\\\'s gonna be ruined/humiliated tomorrow\\\"</P>\r\n<P>\\\"Gee, I remember doing that once\\\"</P>\r\n<P>\\\"Hmm, I wonder if that\\\'s the same person who puked back there...\\\"</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Thing is, on Friday night on Bourbon Street, it probably WASN\\\'T the same person. </P>\r\n<P>I love New Orleans. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(11403,2062,'2001-11-15 17:45:13',0,'Nastchenka','I am curious about toronto. does it have a low income housing shortage the way san francisco does?',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11404,2062,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a memory that sticks out from childhood: watching mash with my dad as he mused and laughed over the medical/army discrepancies, being an army officer/doctor as he was. my mom bought him a tiny b&w teevee so he could watch mash reruns in his office while pretending to be seeing patients.<br><br>\r\nre: low income housing- now that everyone is fleeing NYC in terror (good riddance), there are plenty of nice manhattan flats for ~$500-1100. Hmm.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11405,2061,'2001-11-15 18:14:19',0,'critic','dude. you puked. in front of a cop. <i>and werent even drunk!</i>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11406,2062,'2001-11-15 18:20:47',0,'Broadcast','Don\\\'t memorize the leaves / They\\\'re coloured to deceive.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.73.122','en'),(11407,2059,'2001-11-15 19:20:28',0,'mo','poison and perfection...i dig that.',2,0,0,0,'207.189.37.83','en'),(11408,1003,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','there is a mosquito that lives in our bedroom. it only wakes up between 2-4 am to snack on my neck. then it goes away. it comes every night. but you know what? i\\\'ve made peace with Buddy. i don\\\'t mind him anymore. he feels the love and only bites me once a night now, instead of 12. we have come to a mutual agreement. one bite is a small price to pay for an insect friend.',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11409,2056,'2001-11-15 20:40:40',0,'shane','nice.',2,0,0,0,'209.52.67.73','en'),(11410,2062,'2001-11-15 22:34:46',0,'MASH','M*A*S*H was loved by millions!!  you think you are the only person who watches the reruns??  think again, fan.',4,0,0,0,'207.189.37.83','en'),(11411,2063,'2001-11-15 23:30:10',0,'trouble','\\\'a lake opened up below us\\\'<br><br>i love that thought. *best friendly*',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(11412,2049,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>I liked this post quite a bit. I am terrified of the day when my parents will get sick.</p>\r\n<p>All I can do is try to love them the best I can, I suppose.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin\r\n',3,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(11413,2063,'2001-11-16 00:47:33',0,'n','i really dreamed it',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11414,2063,'2001-11-16 00:47:59',0,'n','in those exact words, can you dream in words?',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11415,1324,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','you guys are funny. ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.52.228','en'),(11416,1003,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','the mosquito in our bedroom is named nancy.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11417,2067,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the mouth on this girl!  my word!',4,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11418,2062,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','modest mouse have some lyrics about M*A*S*H.  i used to watch it a lot when i was skyving off tech.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11419,2067,'2001-11-16 04:41:28',0,'erin','so when exactly were you Irish that night? and also impressive work and dramatic flair. i like.',0,0,0,0,'199.174.163.75','en'),(11420,2042,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','<p>you could drive outside the city in the wee hours of sunday morning and try to catch a glipse of the leonids.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i know i\\\'d like to do that, but i\\\'m not sure if it\\\'ll happen.</p>',2,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11421,2062,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nthere isn\\\'t a city in the world that doesn\\\'t have a low income housing shortage. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nduring the early 80\\\'s the peterson government did a good job spending money on low income housing. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nbob rae, ndp leader during the late 80\\\'s legislated a lot of good things for toronto. He stepped in and set up rent controls and forced landlords to clean up sketchy buildings. he legislated that buildings had to offer low income floors in buildings. ie. I lived on the 35th floor of the manulife building in toronto. the first twelve floors when i lived there were low income apartments. subsodized by the ontario government. they had separate entrances and parking and everything. it was very strange, but it was good. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmike harris came in after rae promising to boost ontario\\\'s slumping economy and cut government spending. one way he got real estate going again was by getting rid of rent controls and subsodized dwellings. If that wasn\\\'t far enough, he slashed funding offered to city co-ops(i lived in a co-op for 4 years in ottawa. they are good). \r\n</p>\r\n<P>\r\ncanadians think toronto is dirty. americans think its clean. t dot o dot. \r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11422,2042,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','When I walk to work on Sunday morning, it will be approximately 5:45am.  I can see stars when I walk to work in the morning, I imagine I will be able to check out the meteor shower while crossing the Allen.  So long as the cloud-cover keeps it\\\'s end of the bargain and remains non-existant, that is.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.73.122','en'),(11423,2067,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to erin','the drinking. the ranting. the drinking. oh yeah, and the drinking. ',4,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11424,2065,'2001-11-16 07:10:38',0,'chi chi','the best puke i ever had (and i\\\'ve done a lot of that puke till there is nothing left, and you just heave and heave and heave and try not to smack your head on the toilet bowl kind of puking) was last february on e. a bunch of us took to the northern california coast for a weekend in a great house in a \\\"private community\\\". the tone of the weekend was set that first night: rain, booze, a fireplace that didn\\\'t draw properly, and lots of weed. the second night a bunch of us did e. this was only my second time, and very different from my first. i got so overheated i though i was gonna combust, and i knew i was gonna puke. i hate puking, but i always know when it\\\'s gonna happen, no false-alarms with me. so i ran out side into the drizzle of the night barefoot leaned over the rail had my little and wonderful puke and let the rain fall all over me cooling me. it was the best feeling i have ever had. i felt so great.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.44.47','en'),(11425,2059,'2001-11-16 08:43:55',0,'lulu','cuntiholio.<br>\r\n<p>!!</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(11426,2064,'2001-11-16 11:51:50',0,'critic','you got puke drunk from one 40?!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11427,2062,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','dave, i like you.',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11428,2066,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<p><a href=\\\"http://www.wickedclean.com/myexsucks/\\\">bitter?</a></p><p><a href=\\\"http://teensbymail.com/\\\">horney?</a></p><p>generally <a href=\\\"http://stileproject.com\\\">fucked up?</a></p><p>looking for an <a href=\\\"http://links.net\\\">instant life?</a></p>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.18.20','en'),(11429,2064,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Actually, it was two, but thanks for comin\\\' out, math skills.<br><br>*best friendly*',1,0,0,0,'64.229.73.122','en'),(11430,2066,'2001-11-16 18:28:24',0,'stats','we all have inner demons to struggle with...welcome to the club. nobody said life was easy. or without obstacles. it\\\'s only what you make of it. challenge yourself, and challenge yourself, and then challenge yourself again. this is what we call growing up.\r\n\r\n<p>...and don\\\'t take your angst out on other people, they\\\'ve got shit to deal with too. it\\\'s a fine line between contentment and chaos for all of us.</p>',3,0,0,0,'32.100.71.20','en'),(11431,2067,'2001-11-16 18:50:37',0,'erin go bragh','next time you wanna get Irish for the night do it with Jameson\\\'s cuz really you were acting Scottish for the night, yo. but still, bravo. well done. ',0,0,0,0,'147.126.46.140','en'),(11432,2062,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','hi chiam',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11433,2062,'2001-11-16 20:51:28',0,'sims','you know, the two times i have been to toronto, i remember thinking damn this city is clean. woowee. but then again, i am from san francisco and was then living in brooklyn. in comparison, i was impressed.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11434,2066,'2001-11-16 21:07:03',0,'auntie','when the world is crashing on your head and the tunnel seems endless, the only way out is one step at a time, through the sludge.<br><br>\r\n\r\nmake a tiny goal, a little one, one that you can accomplish in one short span of time, an hour, a day, a week.  even if it is just - clean kitchen counter - or put dirty laundry in basket to wash - or even - brush teeth after meals. <br><br>\r\n\r\nonce you have accomplished one tiny goal, the next one is easier, because you have given it the name of GOAL.<br><br>\r\n\r\nadd a couple more GOALS to your day as you feel able, and pretty soon you will feel like you have accomplished something you can look back on and see as a tiny triumph.<br><br>\r\n\r\neach tiny triumph brings you closer to a bigger GOAL, and now it is time to make a list of things you would do if you had the means to do them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nneed a job?  okay, what kind of job would you have if you could have any job?  what would you need to know to do that job?  do you know any of it now?  is it possible that there is a company that has your dream job out there and also has need for a serfdom sort of employment, too?  apply for serfdom.  once enslaved, you will be able to see better what sort of people are doing your dream job and what you might need to know and do and become to work next to them.<br><br>\r\n\r\neverybody starts at the bottom and it sucks, but that\\\'s how it is.  better to be a serf than a non-serf, if you see my point.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnow you are thinking i am full of shit and you don\\\'t want to hear what i have to say, that\\\'s okay, because now you go look in the mirror and say - \\\"i am better than that, i don\\\'t have to be a serf, i know how to do (whatever it is you know how to do) that will get me a better job than that,\\\" and say it loud.<br><br>\r\n\r\nso quit screwing around, since you know how to do whatever it is that precludes serfdom, go find someone who will hire you as a non-serf.  half the frigging world is unemployed these days and i am building frigging shopping carts for twenty frigging bux an hour and on call day and night 7 days a week and i wish i could take a nap, but i can\\\'t because i gotta pay the bills, and so does everyone else.<br><br>\r\n\r\nstop wasting time being miserable, it\\\'s wearing on the rest of us.  besides, we all like you better when you are happy. okay?',4,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11435,2062,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nle i like you more.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni personally think toronto is a relatively clean and safe city. There arent too many hoods i wouldnt live in. There are a few i wouldnt want to live in. However I havnt been to a city where there hasnt been places i shouldnt live. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nmike harris stepped down as premier. there will have to be an election next spring sometime i guess. i hope to god the ndp wins, but most likely the liberals will win. i dont much like the liberal government we have now federally. However things on the provincial level are much different. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhi adam. how are you dude? havnt seen you round on the bci.\r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11436,2063,'2001-11-16 21:32:47',0,'trouble','can i dream in words?  i can translate my dreams into words pretty well but i\\\'m not sure that\\\'s what you mean.  i think you are on the defensive here, i really liked the wording of that and that\\\'s all i meant.  it creates a beautiful picture for me.',1,0,0,0,'208.255.153.146','en'),(11437,2062,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i am <b>I</b>ntermittently <b>C</b>hecking <b>B</b>anter.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11438,2066,'2001-11-16 23:36:11',0,'drew','<p>the trouble is that pretty much everyone who is disposed to feel this way at all (and i am) feels this way no matter what happens.  so many, many things expand to fill the space provided.  work, for example, is gaseous and expands to fill available time.  material desires expand to fit available money.  and unhappiness expands to fill the available void in your heart.</p>\r\n<p>when i was working at the job i had before this last one, i had a list of complaints a mile long.  when i started this one, it seemed fantastic at first, and then what do you know, i got another list going, and even though there were a lot of cool things about my new job, suddenly it sucked just as hard as the old one.  now i have no job and both of the previous ones seem awesome by comparison, especially the first one.  i have plenty of \\\"legitimate\\\" worries now, like how to pay the rent, where to go, how to get another job, whether the cats will eat me if they don\\\'t get their friskies.  but i realize i wasn\\\'t any happier before, when all i had to worry about were things like: am i getting all the fun i can get in my life?  which video game should i buy?  am i too fat?  does my hair look good?  why do boys hate me?  i\\\'m working, performing, and learning martial arts, but shouldn\\\'t i be working on my novel?  my sorrows, tiny in mass, expanded in volume to fill the void i created for them.</p>\r\n<p>we don\\\'t have complete control over the void.  that would be kind of a middle-class white guy thing for me to say.  some reasons for unhappiness have a mass that can\\\'t be compressed much further than it already is.  i could be hiv positive, or have cancer, or a family member of mine could.  i could have no money and no credit and no place to go, not even my parents\\\' house.  i could have an addiction to something more debilitating and expensive than the internet (hard to imagine, i know).  i could have had my limbs blown off by a land mine or a bomb. i\\\'m sure you can think of worse; i\\\'m thankful and lucky that i can\\\'t.  my point here is just that yeah, sometimes the void is big because it has to be.</p>\r\n<p>but most of the time, those feelings of \\\"my life isn\\\'t enough\\\" are telltale signs that there\\\'s large-scale expansion going on.  that\\\'s natural.  but you don\\\'t have to let the void get any bigger than you can handle.</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11439,2066,'2001-11-17 13:48:42',0,'elfboy','<p>i can\\\'t add much to what person drew said, and said well. but. go ahead and fucking whine, if that\\\'s what people want to call it. because outside all the new-age gurgle-boo-boo-fuck the hard fact of the matter is that life just sometimes sucks.</p>\r\n<p>it\\\'s totally unreasonable to expect that everyone should react to constantly taking 3 steps back to gain 1 forward by being fucking optimistic about it, painted smile 24/7 and a painful tic in your jaw and a pounding head at 5:49 a.m. and if nothing else you should be able to write about whatever the fuck you want in a forum that appears to be about expression BUT</p>\r\n<p>at the same time it is worth considering that somehow, through all the ugly fucking shit, yeah, it is worth it. even if you don\\\'t know why. fuck it. might as well.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.214.13.19','en'),(11440,2043,'2001-11-17 14:11:18',0,'elfboy','<p>goddammit, i was going to say that. no, really. yeah. kill of the main character you don\\\'t like and the story will (may, should) open up for you. </p>\r\n<p>i think the muses expect you to roll with the twists and turns and definately, they need a sacrifice now and then.</p>\r\n<p>i like the taste of lawn clippings, actually.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.214.13.19','en'),(11441,2066,'2001-11-17 14:17:03',0,'stats to elf','',0,0,0,0,'32.102.64.210','en'),(11442,2066,'2001-11-17 14:21:27',0,'stats to elf','hey mang, noone here ever said life didnt suck sometimes...and noone here said don\\\'t post your whiny rants. the fact that you\\\'re implying personal attacks took place only goes to show what a negative attitude can do for ya. sometimes we all breed our own shit. when you\\\'re down it\\\'s a little too east to blame it on others, a little too easy to see everything in a halo of vomitous anxiety. especially when people share advice and it isn\\\'t what you wanted to hear [that being maybe something that just backs up the rant rant rant banter that\\\'s on replay in your head].',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.210','en'),(11443,2063,'2001-11-17 14:28:15',0,'elfboy','<p>thanks for writing out your dream it put a little fragment of another world in my head. since i can\\\'t travel right now it helps.</p>\r\n<p>how do you dream in words? what i mean is, do you see text, or does a voice speak to you?</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m extremely visual. which is, ironically, how i began writing. not that i\\\'m a writer, i just write. so i have purely visual dreams. little or no dialogue that i can remember, although it was there, sometimes.</p>\r\n<p>one of my favorite remembered dreams was the one where i was in a ship, in a very crowded harbor/bay filled with vast and small ships from every fantastical era that ever was. none of these ships were in the water though. they all floated in the air, about an eighth of a mile up. i was in a ship which required that you steer it with a wheel. like a pirate ship. and i kept crashing into other ships because i was busy running around deck, looking at all the other ships.</p>\r\n<p>(if anyone has any sort of interpretation to offer along the lines of how my life is, then you\\\'re really missing the point.</p>\r\n<p>drunken lingo may end here.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.214.13.19','en'),(11444,2066,'2001-11-17 15:42:47',0,'elfboy','<p>actually i didn\\\'t think i was implying anything. just airing some thoughts. all i\\\'m saying is that sometimes it\\\'s understandable to have a negative attitude. speaking of that, you can\\\'t really assume that i have a totally negative attitude unless you deliberately choose to ignore the last paragraph of my post.</p>\r\n<p>i know it\\\'s not a freaking hallmark card, but i\\\'m not getting paid to make greeting cards.</p>\r\n<p>i take the point of all who posted, including you. i\\\'m just adding one more, is all.</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.245.170.180','en'),(11445,2067,'2001-11-17 16:21:24',0,'i say','good show',0,0,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(11446,2068,'2001-11-17 21:38:39',0,'critic','so im depressed because i dont have any money?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11447,2068,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you\\\'re right about the rent. When I lived in Podunkia, my rent was $350 for a luxury place. Now, my NYC rent is $2,285 for something not-that-special. The unfortunate thing is, it\\\'d be next to impossible to move back to Podunkia after living in NYC for 6 years... even if I wanted to.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11448,2068,'2001-11-18 00:15:29',0,'moebius, rambling again','thanx auntie, for making these thoughts concrete. i\\\'ve been kind of surprised that more swinney folks aren\\\'t talking about this. perhaps it\\\'s because it\\\'s because the war/recession blues are just too awful & pervasive & bloody <i>obvious</i> to go on about here in any grand detail. it\\\'s easier (and more fun, i suppose) to tell puke stories. we\\\'d rather laugh than cry.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>that said, i\\\'m happy to see this piece here, even though it discusses a subject that I\\\'ve been very resistant to: the possible necessity of relocating from my beloved cosmopolis (sf, despite all the changes) to assumedly dull, straight, insular, monocultural, isolated teeny town USA. but we need to be told there\\\'s life outside of the urban domain, and we need to be reminded that--several times over--times like these have come and gone. on both accounts, in both cases we\\\'ll survive and continue to flourish, despite whatever temporary sacrifices we have to make in the meantime.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the other day, this <a href=\\\"http://www.nytimes.com/2001/11/14/nyregion/14GENE.html?searchpv=past7days\\\">article</a> showed up in the NY Times. it kinds of puts things into perspective for those swinneyites who happen to be on the younger end of the scale. it reminds me of when i came to SF ten years ago, the last time we had a recession on. i was fresh out of college with a seemingly useless creative writing degree, and the phrase \\\"NO FUTURE\\\" seemed to be superimposed over everything i saw. distress & depression reigned. and for awhile there i seriously worried that i\\\'d be a menial temp office lackey (at best) for the rest of my life. ha. now i feel that if i could make it though those lean times i can do it again, no problem.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>well, i could go on for days, but this is supposed to be a comment, not a post of me own. one last: peter, one thing i do miss from those early \\\'90s recession days is the rent on my first SF apt: $800 for a two bedroom (plus garage). sigh.</p>',1,0,0,0,'64.164.192.186','en'),(11449,2068,'2001-11-18 00:25:21',0,'drew','<p>small town america is definitely cheaper.  it\\\'s the place where i went to junior high and high school.  it\\\'s hard to tell if i learned to be depressed there or if it was a natural byproduct of adolescence. but it is definitely a horrifying, spiritually debilitating place for those of us who aren\\\'t naturals for it.</p>\r\n<p>on the other hand, urban america is overhyped.  i didn\\\'t move here just for the big city, and i never really wanted to live in it.</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11450,2068,'2001-11-18 00:53:45',0,'garbageman','dude. smalltown america might be cheaper, but <i>you get what you pay for</i>.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11451,2068,'2001-11-18 01:12:24',0,'lunesse','do you?\r\n\r\nI don\\\'t think I get what I pay for in the SF area anymore. I pay for very little parking, lots of SUV\\\'s with one passenger and a KEEP TAHOE BLUE sticker on the bumper. I pay to live near, and for a long time, in, a city that denies the homeless are a problem, that is full of cell phone talking, parking on the sidewalk, complaining about the long existing clubs in the neighborhood they just moved into for the hip loft fast money business jerks.\r\n\r\nI used to think so. I used to think the extra rent was worth living in this great town. It still is a great town, but now it\\\'s evicted its dancers and musicians, so that the spaces can now stand empty since the dot com types that took them over as offices are gone.\r\n\r\nI used to think living here would spoil me for everywhere else. In some ways, that\\\'s true. But San Francisco became some sort of playground for the rich and quick. They came here after college, school, their old job, and sucked up all the homes, the apartments, and threw their money everywhere. Then it all fell apart and some of them have left, leaving their inflated prices and lifestyles behind, to a degree, so that dental assistants can\\\'t afford homes for their families, 50k a year isn\\\'t enough for two people to live on and have many extras, and the Trocadero became a foofy sushi and jazz joint.\r\n\r\nYou don\\\'t always get what you pay for these days. Sometimes, you do indeed get less.\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'66.126.181.186','en'),(11452,2068,'2001-11-18 01:14:38',0,'whiner','if you dislike it so much, <i>then move</i>.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11453,2068,'2001-11-18 02:52:58',0,'jim','<p>\r\nalthough I\\\'ve come to recognize the \\\"San Francisco has been ruined by [ rich people / SUVs / the internet / landlords / homeless / disco / queers / hippies / college students / beat poets / the fire / the earthquake / the gold rush ]\\\" allegation as being as much an established city tradition as Bay to Breakers or Critical Mass, I\\\'m starting to find it a little tedious.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nthere is intense magic to be found in this city just about every day.  it\\\'s possible to have combinations of experience here that just aren\\\'t possible anywhere else.  if enduring the things that aren\\\'t perfect about San Francisco, combined with exhorbitant cost of living here is enough to conceal that magic from you such that you don\\\'t feel like you\\\'re getting what you pay for, I urge you to consider checking out other places.  the world is so big.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nyou\\\'ll either find out that you were right to leave and find the perfect place for you, or you\\\'ll realize that the unquantifiable everyday San Francisco is worth every penny and come on back.  you\\\'ll be happier either way.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\none more thing.  wealth is a shitty litmus test to measure people by.  I know lots of people who are, and lots of people who aren\\\'t.  the ones who are, who drive SUVs and live in lofts and dig foofy sushi and jazz joints are just as incredible and interesting and human as the people who aren\\\'t.  they have the added bonus of being capable of funding the kinds of creative projects that help make San Francisco the extraordinary place that it is. \r\n</p>\r\n\r\n',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11454,2069,'2001-11-18 02:54:34',0,'','burn baby burn!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11455,2068,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>right on, jim. the same tediousness stands here in nyc, but in the form of old people, i-banking, homeless, village-vs-uptown, manhattan-vs-boros/everywhere else, scenesters vs hipsters, terrorism, bombings, killings, crack, nypd burtality, and a zillion other too-numerous things. the truth is, i know good people who probably fit into all those categories (well, not hte terrorism, but...), and they all make for such a unique living experience that i\'d never leave nyc; they make it absoutely peerless. well put!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11456,2062,'2001-11-18 03:43:31',0,'deb','M*A*S*H has to be one of the greatest shows of all time.  I could watch M*A*S*H reruns for hours.  Or at least listen to the theme song...',3,0,0,0,'152.163.213.66','en'),(11457,2068,'2001-11-18 04:40:14',0,'glaxo welcome','take 1 effexor gelcap 75 mg time release, once a day...',0,0,0,0,'12.81.85.236','en'),(11458,2068,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','\\\"I have travelled a good deal in Concord; and everywhere, in shops, and offices, and fields, the inhabitants have appeared to me to be doing penance in a thousand remarkable ways.\\\" --H.D. Thoreau',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.49','en'),(11459,594,'2001-11-18 12:21:47',0,'elfboy','<p>i have seen interesting things yeah. </p>\r\n<p>but not the same. earlier tonight i was in an airplane hangar. the walls were lined at intervals with little altars. there were exposed metal i beams above. there was netting and green lights and burning sage.</p>\r\n<p>on the way home i saw areas of the city i haven\\\'t seen before, even though some of the streets i walked on were the same as other streets i had walked on. but not when they were fine-tuned. i saw the coolest loading dock in an industrial area of some city or another. when you walk through parts of a city where you currently live that you haven\\\'t walked through before, sometimes, you can stop and stand and realize you could be in a different city.</p>\r\n<p>someone i know made a song about walking through downtown on a rainy day when the trash trucks were out clanging the alleys and the seagulls were wheeling and screaming. the song is just sounds. electronic sounds. that\\\'s all.</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.244.110.54','en'),(11460,2070,'2001-11-19 00:46:43',0,'ryan','<p>picking one\\\'s nose is one of the most rewarding human experiences, especially after spending some time in a dry and dirty climate, say the sierras near mono lake in the summer.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>very satisfying.</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.162.49.167','en'),(11461,2070,'2001-11-19 02:22:35',0,'critic','a friend of mine works in montreal, and claims that she sees a disporportionate number of folks picking their noses there. based on that fact, as well as your post, does that mean that canadians are less reserved about nasal digging?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11462,2070,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>i got in an arguement with a communictions professor, in class, about nasal digging in traffic.  she thought it was rude and gross, and just who would ...</P><P>\r\ni was like, step back.  why would you even give a shit what people think about your nasal digging while in traffic?\r\n</P><P>\r\ni do it in class all the time and i have to see you schmucks every day... oh wait, but then i am the one who doesnt care.</P>\r\n<P>hansom SWM who likes <BR>\r\nnose picking, farting, stuff like that.<BR>\r\nthink  its funny? call me</P>',4,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11463,2070,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow. i\\\'m half asleep and reading about nose picking while working at this phantom tiny yet very irritating booger way up in my right nose hole. it won\\\'t show its face and fight like a man. i feel it, but it can\\\'t ... find it. *pick* *yawn*',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11464,2070,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','i\\\'ve been furtive about the nosepicking in traffic thing ever since a particular event that occured during my old commute across the Bay Bridge.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it was one of those days when there\\\'s a backup behind the tollbooths, a backup behind the metering lights, and then a long slow crawl across the bridge into the city. while i was waiting in the toll backup, i absentmindedly mined for a nugget or two while i listened to \\\"As It Happens\\\" or whatever on the radio.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>before long i realized somebody was trying to get my attention. no, wait, <u>somebodies:</u> a car full of obnoxious, pimply high school kids. i looked in their direction, they all laughed, mimed booger-picking, made faces, flipped me off. i shifted into another lane. they shifted too. one of \\\'em showed me his ass. soon other drivers were staring at me, trying to figure out what the fuss was about. for the first time ever, i wished i carried a gun in my car (just to scare \\\'em, sure, of course).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the kids shadowed me all the way across the bridge, never stopping with the pointing & gestures & rude behavior. i tried to ignore them, and they tried harder to get under my skin. i was trapped. i finally lost them coming off the Fell Street exit. i considered following them to wherever they were going, but decided that 4 on 1 weren\\\'t great odds. gave up. whatever. goddamn it. goddamn it. if only i had a gun.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and so now i just would rather not pick my nose in front of the world when i\\\'m commuting. but i dunno--sometimes i think i should pick \\\'n\\\' be proud about it, and screw those bullies. this is America, after all.</p>\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11465,2069,'2001-11-19 19:00:46',0,'n','former and future? is there no present?',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11466,2063,'2001-11-19 19:05:56',0,'n','i guess what i meant about dreaming in words is that when i wake up (or go to sleep, whichever it is)my mind only allows me one interpretation--only those words fit the feeling/image/sensation. Limits and also destroys limits, because words are only symbols of a thing, one can interpret them according to their entailments, or contrary to them. ',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11467,2068,'2001-11-19 19:17:16',0,'n','i think sf is becoming a better place to live, slowly but surely, after the crash. i think what has happened is that 1) the people that were here only for the money are gone 2) the people that really care about the city and all its idiosyncracies are re-evaluating why they\\\'re here in the first place. Which can run the gamut from, hey, I\\\'m unemployed, so I\\\'m going to go volunteer at a homeless shelter to hey, i\\\'m employed so I better figure out why i\\\'m employed and and do I want to be employed if the only reason i am is because i\\\'m doing a shitty ass-kissing job no one else wants to do, or, i\\\'m doing something really worthwhile so i better suck it up and deal with the everyday and appreciate what i have instead of complaining about my rent or the neighbor\\\'s music. it is also a time where people start looking out for each other--letting your friends stay rent free if you have a job, or spoiling the hell out of your roommate\\\'s pets if you don\\\'t. ',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(11468,2070,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to <i>n</i>','\\\"one of them showed me his ass\\\"<BR><BR>\r\ni believe the correct term for this would be used:<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"one of them <b><i>pressed ham</i></b>.\\\"',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11469,2071,'2001-11-19 21:04:15',0,'lush','midnight is the witching hour',4,0,0,0,'32.102.91.62','en'),(11470,2070,'2001-11-19 21:06:56',0,'jo','hahaha. austin swinney you are my hero.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11471,2070,'2001-11-19 21:30:37',0,'drew','look on the bright side...most of the time one has to pay to see high school ass.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11472,2072,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>Do you have to like someone\\\'s work in order to date her? like tracy emin said, \\\"I could never have sex with someone I didnt think was a great artist?\\\"</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11473,2072,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yes, i do. but i think it\\\'s more like i have to respect their work, and be interested in why/how they are doing it.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11474,2072,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i am actually currently having this dilemma with another girl whose art i\\\'m feeling questionable about.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11475,2072,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a contributing factor in the rift between a former lover an i was the aberrance in our musical tastes...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11476,2072,'2001-11-20 05:11:59',0,'drew','i can only date people i like spending time with.  there are no other rules.  it helps if the person is outstanding in some respect -- a bright mathematician, a gifted actor, a deep writer, a code wizard, or simply a genius of love -- but this isn\\\'t something i think about independently of the whole impression i have.  i know so many individuals whose talent or intelligence is very very high, and yet this doesn\\\'t automatically become attraction for me.  then again, i\\\'m attracted to a lot of individuals for whom i feel little affinity or affection or admiration that might lead to something \\\"serious.\\\"  so it\\\'s really about a combination of factors, and whether someone has reached some zenith of artistic achievement isn\\\'t as important for me as whether i respect the integrity of what they do and don\\\'t find it wholly off-putting.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11477,2066,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','yep, it\\\'s all in good expressiveness that i posted anyway. i\\\'m sure any further writings of mine won\\\'t be nearly as, uhm, well, like this one.\r\n\r\ni just had to get shit off my chest. and damn, wouldn\\\'t you know it, it helped a whole damn lot.\r\n\r\ni have personally noticed that without a continualy barage of \\\'shit to do\\\' in a day, my mind wanders and creates it\\\'s own theories and stories about my personal relationships and my personal hardships. and *that* is what expands the void in my heart.\r\n\r\nfor me, it\\\'s just all about keeping busy, and thankfully i will now. i found a job. it\\\'s something mindless and low-paying, but fills the gap.\r\n\r\ni just wish there was some way to take all that time i feel i wasted on doing nothing and store it away for when i just don\\\'t damn well have enough time in a day.',2,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11478,2070,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','pay to see highschool ass? what??!',4,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11479,2070,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Us folks up here in the arctic tundra and barren plains some call Canada (or America v2.0-RC1) are totally lax on the nose-picking.  I think it comes from the fact that a lot of people haven\\\'t severed those British ties and what-not and we take a more liberal European-ish social attitude towards things, I find.<br><br>To illustrate my point (and that of others): <a href=\\\"http://www.spacemoose.com/strips/pressed_ham.gif\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">Space Moose</a>.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.68.248','en'),(11480,2070,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Swinney is my daddy.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.68.248','en'),(11481,2072,'2001-11-20 07:36:05',0,'wb','<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.fortunecity.co.uk/cinerama/thriller/295/tracey_emin004.jpg\\\">',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11482,2072,'2001-11-20 07:36:52',0,'wb','might have to reload to get the image\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11483,2073,'2001-11-20 07:39:01',0,'lush','and then you have those who are just so caught up in the fantasy of reality. wanting it and wanting and needing it and craving it...then when the flame is suddenly there it\\\'s, \\\'holy fuck i\\\'m not good enough for this!\\\' or \\\'holy fuck i\\\'m too good for this!\\\' or maybe just \\\'holy fuck...?\\\' run away johnny, runaway quick!!! well i gotta say...the fantasy of it may seem oh so much better and safer, but one day the reality is going to kick your ass. you\\\'ve got to live life now, regardless of the fear and regardless of the fact that there is no such thing as perfection. we\\\'re all fucked up in our own special ways. and we always will be.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.197','en'),(11484,2074,'2001-11-20 08:16:17',0,'drew\\\'s language center','\\\"too few of us remain reluctant to abandon\\\" caused me to explode!  can\\\'t...parse...negatives...',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11485,633,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','strange, i haven\\\'t made a webpage that is either netscape friendly or black in ages. well, at least not one that i designed myself.',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11486,2070,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','why is nose-picking so frowned apon anyway? how else is the snot supposed to get out of your nose?!',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11487,2074,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','maybe the post-dotcom SF will breed a new generation of rebellion in music that\\\'s been staunchly driven by the poor and underclassed citizens seeking solace in sound. it truely seems the sonic efforts of those you have named are a true reflection and representation of Detroit in an aural way. sounds so desolate and haunting. \r\n\r\n<p>i can do nothing but think of that broken city when i hear thier music.',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(11488,2071,'2001-11-20 10:37:19',0,'Joanna','<B>Oatmeal Cookie Cocktails</B>:\r\n<LI>jagermeifter\r\n<LI>Baileys\r\n<LI>Butterscotch schnapps\r\n<LI>Cinnamon schapps\r\n<LI>Cream <BR>\r\nShake well with ice and serve dusted with cinnamon\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<I>Why is it that I know a thousand different cocktail  recipes but I couldn\\\'t change a tyre to save my life?</I>',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11489,2071,'2001-11-20 10:40:55',0,'elfboy','<p>yes. midnight is the witching hour but 3 a.m. is the hour of the spirits. apparently, there\\\'s a difference. i don\\\'t know.</p>\r\n<p>knowing cocktail recipes is far more important than knowing how to change a tire. the only time it\\\'s not, is when you have to change a tire.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.154.184.198','en'),(11490,2071,'2001-11-20 10:44:07',0,'elfboy again','do you have any good recipes for blue drinks? preferably involving vodka? howbout green?',0,0,0,0,'64.154.184.198','en'),(11491,2069,'2001-11-20 10:45:15',0,'elfboy','not at present.',0,0,0,0,'64.154.184.198','en'),(11492,2071,'2001-11-20 10:53:02',0,'Joanna','<BR>\r\n<B>Blue Lagoons</B>\r\n<LI>Blue Curaco\r\n<LI>Vodka\r\n<LI>Lemonade\r\n<BR>\r\nStir together.  You must decorate this with obscene amounts of cocktail fruit, umbrellas and novelty stirring sticks or it\\\'s just not the same.  \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<B>Illusions</B>\r\n<LI>Midori (there\\\'s your green)\r\n<LI>Malibu\r\n<LI>Vodka\r\n<LI>Pineapple Juice\r\n<BR>\r\nShake together and drink in shots. ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11493,2073,'2001-11-20 11:26:37',0,'deb','\\\'holy fuck...\\\' is right, my friend...',0,0,0,0,'152.163.207.61','en'),(11494,2071,'2001-11-20 11:32:36',0,'elfboy','cool, thank you.',0,0,0,0,'64.154.184.198','en'),(11495,2071,'2001-11-20 11:36:16',0,'Joanna','Always a pleasure, never a chore. ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11496,2074,'2001-11-20 12:13:38',0,'critic','<i>parsing negatives</i> is what detroit machine soul is all about. i love this piece of writing.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11497,2074,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to dcc','<br><tt>perhaps, indeed. trauma\'s wake can profoundly inject turbulence into music; the collective textures of off-the-cuff underground tracks here in nyc grew more affectively coloured, immediately following the fall of the WTC, and have piquantly spiralled deeper into some disturbingly dark rhythhms (embracing arrhythmically filtered white noise, sampled airline radio/black-box chatter, police scanner noise, choppy siren dopler shifts). since, i feel like i can further appreciate the effects on sound that are the result of even more subtle causes, sometimes a decade in the making, the lament of obsolete ways of life, the gasps of broken cities.</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11498,2073,'2001-11-20 12:38:24',0,'critic','i was never <i>afraid of falling in love</i> until i was wiped out by the act of <i>falling out of love</i>. well, not so much afraid as reluctantly jaded.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11499,1732,'2001-11-20 12:57:46',0,'elfboy','<p>\\\"And then there\\\'s silence, and we both stare at the ocean. The moment is heavily pregnant, dilated and crowning.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>that\\\'s an excellent line.....two lines.</p>\r\n<p>maybe you should put the 3000(?) words back in. i liked the story, i was interested enough in each part to read the successive parts, but this last seems a little rushed.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'m not really a writer though, but i\\\'m definately a reader. i liked it.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.154.184.198','en'),(11500,2074,'2001-11-20 14:16:42',0,'trouble','being born and raised in and around detroit and now living in tennessee i often carry the flag of the city on my back.  i do my best to dispell the bullshit the media shoves out, i try and highlight the good and the bad equally, i illustrate the history and the possibility of what the future holds but what i will never do is describe it as dead or dying.  dying was detroit in the late 70\\\'s during the height of white flight and then dead was detroit in the early 80\\\'s when the joke was \\\'whoever is last to leave please turn out the lights\\\', rebirth started with kirk gibson homerun to clinch the series for a baseball team in 84.  growth was the removal of coleman young from office and the election of new government, a pizza baron buying the hockey team and deciding the nightlife in detroit needed a restored theater district, new music coming from the basement of st. andrews from artists who didn\\\'t care,... <br><br>this flag gets heavy sometimes, i\\\'m not going to finish this because you get the idea.  you see, i\\\'ve always envisioned detroit as the scruffy kid in a rockwell picture... battered and bruised, dirty and clothes torn, bloody lip and tossed hair, standing straight and cocksure with a smile on his face standing in the middle of a field of weeds, getting up no matter how many times you knock him down.  i love the city, please visit it sometime and feel the way it moves and works, take the good with the bad, explore its past and talk to others about what they see as its future.  ',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(11501,2074,'2001-11-20 14:35:28',0,'critic','Upon re-reading this, a deeper and more poignant current struck me. Perhaps that this piece isn\'t so much about Detroit, itself, as it is about something much greater and arguably more obscure... people, history, the abandonment of the working class, a mature retrospective on a microcosm of our last generation\'s American Dream... maybe even all this as it fits into some <i>global trend</i> of the pursuit of technological manifest destiny... yes?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11502,2074,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to trouble and critic','<br><tt>these are meaty comments- thanks.  <i>Trouble</i>, i\'ve anticipated your response, and as a one time tennessean myself (who has never been to detroit, incidentally), i appreciate your clearly more qualified perspective!<br><br>\r\ncritic: you\'ve raised an <i>excellent</i> point, by the way, and i very much agree. fortunately for my lack of fluency in this cultural metachromatism, greater volumes have surely been written about such things.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11503,2074,'2001-11-20 14:52:27',0,'trouble to critic','i read into that too, my comment was a simple knee-jerk response.  this is a great article.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(11504,2070,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','well, another popular school of nasal evacuation thought is the <em>Snot Rocket</em>, sometimes known as a <em>Snolly Bomb</em>.  The pick vs. blow controversy shows no signs of abating any time soon.',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(11505,2070,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Dirty / Flaky Keys.<br><br>\r\nI think as programmer / sysadmin type people, we leave a trace of bitterness or aggression (or maybe excitement) in our keyboards.  Certain keys, used over and over again, map the patterns of our work.  As a perl programmer and sometimes admin, my $, @, %, {}, [], and CTRL keys get a lot more work than your average typist\\\'s keyboard probably does.  As an admin of certain CLI systems we all love, my | key is getting a heavy beating.  The sad thing is that I never learned how to type \\\"right\\\", but my own weird little muscle memory lets me type the languages I know <em>really fast</em>, like I never concretely learned how it felt to type the letter \\\'e\\\' correctly, but if I have to type \\\"use CGI; (return) use DBI;\\\" I don\\\'t even have to think about it.',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(11506,2073,'2001-11-20 15:44:27',0,'Bri','glad you wrote it out will...it\\\'s time for people to realize that there are more important things in this world than gpa\\\'s, a paycheck, a car...as the counting crows once said \\\"gonna get back to basics...\\\"',2,0,0,0,'66.68.172.160','en'),(11507,2073,'2001-11-20 15:48:48',0,'critic','too bad the <i>more important things in life</i> dont usually pay the rent!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11508,1955,'2001-11-20 16:04:40',0,'will','Hm.  Depending on the time or day, I agree with both of you.\r\n\r\nPandora\\\'s problem might be projection.\r\n\r\nStandard Evacuation Procedures are supposed to help keep people calm in the event of an emergency.  Though, as you said, in most emergencies they\\\'re going to go wild.  That\\\'s because private companies don\\\'t want to lose the production it would cost to truly train their employees on how to respond to a crisis situation.  Like kung fu or military combat exercises, reacting appropriately--in an organized and calm manner--in an emergency has to be second nature.  You WON\\\'T stop and think about it, it has to have been drilled into you and practiced so many times that you just DO things that way when it really happens.  One morning of sitting in a conference room, passing mail around, and discussing the routes of evacuation doesn\\\'t cover it AT ALL.  \r\n\r\nThe firefighters and police officers in New York didn\\\'t respond so well simply out of kindness and empathy for the human suffering that was going on in their city.  They responded using a Standard Operating Procedure which they\\\'d been trained and drilled on so many times that it was the ONLY normal way to do their job.  ',3,0,0,0,'152.163.197.187','en'),(11509,2074,'2001-11-20 16:28:18',0,'caitlin','you don\\\'t know anything about Detroit until you\\\'ve been there. pffft.',0,0,0,0,'140.186.33.36','en'),(11510,2074,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','The I-75 is rough driving through Detroit.  Large potholes.<br><br>Detroit has brought us the holy Trinity, though: Derrick May, Jaun Atkins and Kevin  Saunderson.<br><br>The city paid the producers of Robocop to blow the hell out of abandoned industrial buildings when they were filming the movie.  The city couldn\\\'t afford to demolish them.<br><br>Growing up an hour from Detroit and getting all your US-affiliate news stations coming out of Detroit is interesting.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.68.248','en'),(11511,2075,'2001-11-20 18:11:50',0,'ssj3gostu','dude! you read my mind. thps3 has owned me and all of my free time for the past week. i play it for hours and hours and just feel dirty afterwards.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.113','en'),(11512,2074,'2001-11-20 18:31:20',0,'trouble to fuzzy and caitlin','the potholes are fun, think of them as detroits special way of telling you to pay attention.  plus, it keeps independant garages thick with work in the winters performing alignments and front-end repair.  big supporters of gov. engler and his fucked up policies regarding truck weights to be sure..<br>where did you grow up?<br><br>caitlin, are you a detroiter as well?  i\\\'m really beginning to feel some love here.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(11513,2074,'2001-11-20 18:48:53',0,'the cosmos','I-75 runs through tennessee as well as detroit. how uncanny.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11514,2075,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i haven\\\'t played the game (yet) but tony hawk was my hero when i was a kid.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11515,2074,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I-75 is the magical Interstate that you take to drive to Florida.  I haven\\\'t been since I was 13, though, so I can\\\'t remember if it ends in Georgia or goes straight through to the Eff Elle.<br><br>Actually, I think when my family would drive there, we switched in Georgia in order to hop over to the east coast of Florida.<br><br>And, I grew up in the small city of Sarnia, ON.  Situated where the St. Clair River meets Lake Huron right across from Port Huron, MI and an hour drive, north,  from Windsor/Detroit; home of the Blue Water Bridges, the St. Clair Railway tunnel (under the river of the same name) and Chemical Valley.<br><br>Since that whole <i>War On Terrorism</i> became the cool thing to do, Sarnia has also become a <a href=\\\"http://www.observer-sarnia.com/2001/pts_010925.html\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">weapons manufacturer</a>.  It might be fun to drive around and attempt to find the location of the undisclosed assembly site.  That would certainly kill some time when I head home for Giftmas to visit with the family.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.68.248','en'),(11516,2075,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','justin put a tony hawk game and console at my house when i moved out of his, i guess to suggest that he was planning on parking his ass on my couch a lot at my new place. other boys who came over would entertain themselves with it. girls, I highly recommend having a video game laying around in order to entertain young lads who loiter. I personally am comforted by having random males hang around my living room. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11517,2075,'2001-11-20 19:56:36',0,'lads','who loiter is, i dare say, better than ladies who lunch',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11518,2075,'2001-11-20 20:35:45',0,'jo','i graduated from college a semester early so my last semester of school was fall 99 and i went to school in this tiny town in texas that exists in a weird pocket of weather where it is routinely 10 degrees colder there than it is in dallas or fort worth or denton, the three nearest cities.<BR>\r\nanyway, the point of this is to say that i spent the last semester of college evenly dividing my time between: 1) working my ass off on my independent study, 2) working my ass off trying to find housing and a job in austin when i left school and 3) stoned, sitting on my friend duncan\\\'s couch, watching adam and duncan play tony hawk.<BR>\r\nthe reason that i mentioned the cold is because i remember that <b>entire semester</b> as being <b>freezing</b> weather but surely it couldn\\\'t have always been freezing. it definitely snowed at least twice before christmas break, though.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11519,2075,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to egg','<P>even if they climb in through the window and are on <FONT SIZE=\\\"+2\\\">X</FONT>?</P>',6,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11520,2075,'2001-11-20 22:58:45',0,'see also','\\\"stop suckin\\\' on my ear woman, i\\\'ve almost got the hight score!\\\"',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11521,2074,'2001-11-20 23:23:58',0,'caitlin','yay for Detroit!\r\n<p>I grew up in Wayne, near the DTW airport in Romulus. It\\\'s a pretty awful place, but I miss it so badly right now. And, yes, the roads are terrible yay for dj assault and terrence parker.</p>\r\n---<br>\r\nwww.adni.net/~cait/\r\n',0,0,0,0,'140.186.33.36','en'),(11522,2074,'2001-11-20 23:26:11',0,'caitlin','the roads and music guys are completely unrelated, by the way.<p>also, gov. engler is evil.',0,0,0,0,'140.186.33.36','en'),(11523,2075,'2001-11-20 23:36:30',0,'Mario','seriously. i want to stand up and say that game sucks. Personaly I\\\'m an SSX fellow. I can\\\'t get a damn thing done in Tony Hawk 3 b/c my brain is all wired for the snow boarding game. the soundtrack is juvenile. But i guess some people don\\\'t want to grow up and that\\\'s half of it. ',5,0,0,0,'24.27.31.56','en'),(11524,2075,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>whoa, video game talk on swin>org???<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwell then, i have to say that i am an SSX freak, a GTA3 addict, and an ICO fanatic.\r\n</p>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11525,2075,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to austin','i only had trouble with that one boy and he hasnt ever returned. also, I would like to say that I have never sexually competed with video games and I never intend to.  (re: the comment about \\\"stop sucking my ear woman\\\").',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11526,2075,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\ni am currently addicted to civ3 for the pc. Its great. \r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11527,2068,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','some thoughts: (1) worry is self-fullfilling, (2) income does not equal happiness, (3) suburban living creates air pollution and sedentary lifestyles. people should live either in cities or farms. also: nothing satisfies more than satisfaction.',3,0,0,0,'209.244.135.227','en'),(11528,1732,'2001-11-21 09:52:59',0,'Joanna','oooh elfboy, you\\\'re checking up on me :)',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11529,2075,'2001-11-21 10:09:56',0,'sawks:','<br>\r\ni played street fighter 2 (and ensuing variants) in the arcade for at least 3 hours a day every day for about 4 years - at once stage when i was away at uni i ended up withdrawing the last 20 cents from my bank account to get the 60 i needed for another credit<br><br>\r\n\r\nnowadays i have a gig of <a href=\\\"http://www.mame.net/\\\">MAME</a> <a href=\\\"http://www.mame.dk/\\\">roms</a>, which are keeping me off the streets ...',2,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11530,2075,'2001-11-21 13:43:27',0,'Luigi','Metal Gear Solid 2 fools! That\\\'s the one! altough Grand Theft Auto is a wild one, a game were your health goes up when u get a blow job from a hooker in the game!',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(11531,2080,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yeah. i know.',4,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11532,2080,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>gostu, major props. the \"maybe we need to stop an\' let the music take control\" vocal track is jason jinx\'s <i>\'bring back that feeling\'</i> and hasn\'t left my recordbox since the day i got it... at the morillo show last week, he spun that about 4 times over a house mix of m. jackson\'s <i>\'billie jean\'</i>. lucky for me, i\'m catching morillo yet again this thursday. ohhh. and as far as substance goes, i think house <i>is</i> substance. ok, sorry for the music tangent; i know there are people here who will argue a genre\'s weaknesses to death, and thats not my intent. just giving a \'best friendly\' as this was a good read.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11533,2080,'2001-11-21 17:09:37',0,'JJ','She sounds awful.  You deserve someone much better than her.  It\\\'s too bad for her because I think she\\\'s really missing out.  \r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'205.158.25.253','en'),(11534,2082,'2001-11-21 17:18:36',0,'Jillian','I came downstairs today to find a mouse, asleep  -- not dead, on the carpet in the den. I put him in some old tupperware and poked holes in the top. I also gave him some celery leaves. Then I took my shower.\r\n\r\nWhen I came out of the shower and checked on him, he was bleeding from somewhere, and opening and closing his mouth. Prior to this discovery, I\\\'d had plans to just let him free outside. However, since he was obviously suffering, I purposely but indirectly fed him to the stray cat that lives outside our house.',1,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(11535,2074,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I just found out that <b>Derrick May</b> is going to be doing some spinning as well as a <i>live</i> show at <a href=\\\"http://www.centrofly.com\\\">centro fly</a> on saturday, december 15th.  i have guestlist spots, so <a href=\\\"mailto:peter@rhodamine.net\\\">email</a> me if any of you in/near nyc would like to join me!</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11536,2073,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','a few thoughts: I\\\'ve been in the same relationship for five years now, through all kinds of thick & thin. and there\\\'s been a slowdown of some sort over the years, but if there\\\'s blame to be placed for that, I\\\'d put it on my career. I spent several years working so hard I barely had time for her, let alone anything else. I was moving fast at work, but nothing was happening with the rest of my life: it was kind of mothballed away until I finally quit last year & broke out of the habitrail mindset for awhile. during that period of stasis, she was my only happy constant. (well, there were also comic books.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but also: as you get older, time seems to pass faster, and sometimes you feel slower as a result. the world\\\'s changing dramatically right now. people are reaching out to find other people to hold on to, even if they\\\'re not the <i>right</i> person by standards of conventional romance and/or maximum rocknroll aesthetics. post 9/11, a recession\\\'s winter chill settling in, growing through your 20s/30s/40s/and soforth: it\\\'s a strange time to be alone.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11537,2073,'2001-11-21 18:28:54',0,'critic','sometimes i want someone so badly that i cry. not boo-hoo sobs, but deep gasps of emptiness.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11538,2080,'2001-11-21 18:29:28',0,'erb','<p>sad.  serious cries for help (false alarms and actually getting pregnant, both).  i feel for you and her and the other dudes and the baby, too.</p>\r\n<p>2 things:</p>\r\n<p>*if you were both willing, you could forgive each other and definitely live in london or tokyo and be in love.  or paris, why not? (please use birth control, for goodness\\\' sake)</p>\r\n<p>*now is a great time to move to san francisco proper.  there are a million rooms and apartments for rent, at negotiable if not cheap prices.  and it\\\'s nice here.</p>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11539,2083,'2001-11-21 18:45:25',0,'Mr_Gallivant','the last 2 lines are a quote',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(11540,2082,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i thought it was a large mouse that was living in my oven, but it was a small rat.  ick.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11541,2073,'2001-11-21 18:48:21',0,'lush','deep gasps of emptiness...yea, i know that.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.211','en'),(11542,2083,'2001-11-21 19:04:19',0,'lush','...i am thinking there are things to be learned from you. do your friends see this? do you see this? take a deep breath and jump in. the water is cold, but i think you\\\'ll do just fine.',2,0,0,0,'32.102.64.211','en'),(11543,2080,'2001-11-21 19:12:35',0,'ssj3gostu','see, the funny thing is that i posted this last night from work, and when i did i got some sort of error message and i really didn\\\'t have time to try and post it again. so i really didn\\\'t know if had actually been submitted or not.\r\n\r\nand after a while i was ok with that, cause i really had the hardest time figuring out if i wanted anyone to read this or not. i didn\\\'t really know how it would come off, you know?\r\n\r\nlike, i didn\\\'t want this to be a \\\'look how nice i am and how much everybody else sucks\\\' sort of thing. cause it\\\'s not like that. like erb said, it\\\'s just sad.\r\n\r\nso when i woke up this morning and saw that it actually did in fact get posted, my heart sort of dropped. but i think i\\\'m ok with it being here. \r\n\r\njust try not to judge, ok?',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.112','en'),(11544,2080,'2001-11-21 19:16:06',0,'ssj3gostu to peter','yeah, that accapella kills me every time. actually, anything that has somebody talking always does it for me. have you heard the \\\'i get deep\\\' spoken work thing by duane harden? i put that over everything.',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.112','en'),(11545,2082,'2001-11-21 19:18:42',0,'umm','it\\\'s not a big leap from large mouse to small rat...',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(11546,2080,'2001-11-21 19:42:29',0,'lush','i didn\\\'t seem at all about who is right or wrong...it was only heartbreaking to me. sad and tragic for all involved. too easy for us all to fall into roles. to define ourselves by the pain instead of the misery...so that eventually the pain is all you live for. and all that you seek. Death of a Salesmen style. i think i am worthless...therefore i am.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.211','en'),(11547,2082,'2001-11-21 19:44:44',0,'shane','I thought that I had a small mouse living in my toilet but it turned out to be a South Korean sewer rat.',0,0,0,0,'24.64.223.204','en'),(11548,2080,'2001-11-21 19:56:14',0,'haight-j','Whoa.\r\nThat was really well written, almost too well written.\r\nNext time you\\\'re in the neighborhood, come by. We won\\\'t turn you away.',0,0,0,0,'209.182.158.51','en'),(11549,2074,'2001-11-21 20:22:03',0,'jo','peter, are you going to, as they say, rock the body that rocks the party?',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11550,2082,'2001-11-21 20:54:42',0,'cynn','when i lived in the ghetto house, we had a mouse problem for awhile.  one night i spotted it in the kitchen, so we all went in there to form a battle plan.  as the three of were discussing the most effective way to end it\\\'s existence, it ran out from under the stove and sent us all diving out of the way like it had tossed a grenade.  we ended up getting mousetraps and they finally brought the little bastard down.  simon didn\\\'t want the corpse in our garbage so i put it (and the deathtrap attached to it\\\'s throat) in a plastic bag and tossed the lot onto our neighbour\\\'s lawn.',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.243','en'),(11551,2080,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to gostu','<br><tt>the acapellas- <i>yes!</i>. all it took was a big nod and a tactfully timed <i>\\\"bring in the funk!\\\"</i> to get morillo to kick the old fav [<i>\\\"house music: is what we all need. it gets our spirits up and it makes us wanna get down\\\"</i>] a couple of times over kojak\\\'s <i>\\\'cant stop it\\\'</i>... very derrick-carter-esque. speaking of carter and acapellas, man, did you hear \\\"about now...\\\" yet?!.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11552,2074,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to jo','<br><tt>actually, jo, i\'m a bit torn between <i>\"shake dat ass an\' bounce those tits girl\"</i> and <i>\"b-boy rock dont stop c\'mon, pop n\' lock it b-girl to tha breaka\' dawn\"</i>...</tt>  ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11553,2080,'2001-11-21 23:17:33',0,'dug','well written, yes.  the pen is mighty  ',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.227','en'),(11554,2082,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','shane. are you back in canada? cynn....where is your site dude?',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11555,2084,'2001-11-22 09:46:00',0,'drew','<p>oh, wonderful...thank you for giving this phenomenon a name!  i like.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>those who have covered michael jackson\\\'s immensely campy guilty pleasure \\\"smooth criminal\\\" are known as alien ant farm.  it\\\'s not a bad name, actually, and it might be the best of the scowling covers i\\\'ve heard.  which isn\\\'t saying a lot, because it still makes one long for the original.  i loved how orgy tried to scream the words to \\\"blue monday,\\\" those words which only make sense mumbled, as bernard \\\"can\\\'t sing but ian\\\'s offed himself, so must give it a go\\\" sumner did.  a chord in there proved a little tough for the pretty boys in orgy, so they dropped it.  or did they add it?  i can\\\'t remember now.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i recently heard a scowling cover of a cars song.  can\\\'t recall which one (\\\"just what i needed,\\\" maybe?) but it was dreadful.  even worse was the cover of omd\\\'s \\\"electricity,\\\" which of all orchestral man(o?)euvers in the dark  should be performed on a tinkly little synth, not big crunchy (but, yes, electric) guitars.  the less said about limp bizkit the better, though surely it was dinosaur jr. who had the minor hit with a cover of \\\"just like heaven\\\"?  and whoever thought \\\"tainted love\\\" would sound good at half the tempo? were they just stoned and messing with the turntable speed?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the hypothesis that these bands are butching up the nancy boy hits is clever, and i think there\\\'s a sense with the record companies that this is what the kids want.  but it points to the deeper problem, which is that these bands are basically just noise filters akin to something you might find in photoshop.  when they\\\'re not stealing -- excuse me, covering old well-crafted songs, they are simply making loud angry noises and hoping they can fill up a CD with them.  the sex pistols made loud angry noises and yes, in their own special way, sucked as well, but at least they were catchy.  \\\"i wanna be anarchy\\\" is fun.  \\\"you can take that cookie and stick it up your\\\" is...not, so much.</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11556,2084,'2001-11-22 10:03:30',0,'wb','Good points drew. Another Sid/Sex Pistols cover that comes to mind is Eddie Cochran\\\'s \\\"Stepping Stone\\\" and \\\"Somethin\\\' Else.\\\" Clearly by choosing these two songs the message was they didn\\\'t want rock to be Yes and Floyd concerts but something closer to the roots of the music in the 50\\\'s and early 60\\\'s. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe question with these new covers is what is the point? What does it point to, what does it want? Do they think they are doing better versions of the originals? Or that it just being funny and cute is enough? Or as long as it sells that\\\'s enough. <BR><BR>Sidenote: Downloaded a great cover by Radiohead of \\\"Nobody Does it Better\\\" (Spy Who Loved Me). Great stuff.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11557,2084,'2001-11-22 11:55:39',0,'sawks','<br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://artists3.iuma.com/IUMA/Bands/Mr._Dope_America/\\\">MR DOPE AMERICA</a>',3,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11558,2084,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','<p>\r\nwayne, have you considered the idea that at least some of these bands, just like your vaunted cream and led zeppelin, want(ed) to pay tribute to music that influenced them? can you count the number of times you\'ve seen an act of any size throw in a cover or two during their set or encore? are you aware of phish\'s (an 80s/90s band that carried on the improvisational torch of parker and coltrane) tradition of covering an entire album at their halloween show each year?\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nit seems like you\'re just looking for new ways to harsh the popular music of our time simply because it doesn\'t appeal to you. fine, i enjoy doing that myself, but at least recognize that your favorite artists don\'t have a patent on sincerity.\r\n</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.141','en'),(11559,2084,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s hard to not like the scowling cover, even if the band covering it make you want to stick red hot pokers in your ears, if you liked the original.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni really like that alien ant farm cover of smooth criminal, because they haven\\\'t really deviated from the jacko format much.  also, the lead singer looks like my friend tim and there is a cute kid doing the moves in the video.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nlong live bad covers of good songs; they remind us of what we strive for and the price of failure.',3,0,0,0,'209.244.129.86','en'),(11560,2084,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','also:  perhaps the songs that people are covering are our answer to the lack of new standards?  orchestras play mozart over and over and over, nina and ella and countless others sing strange fruit, elvis and your family sing merry christmas to you...<br>\r\n<br>\r\nif that kind of music no longer appeals to our ears in this era, perhaps we need to define our own set of standards that remind us that some music endures and <b>is not disposable</b>.  perhaps, unconciously, this is the larger cause of the smaller manifestations; recognising that some combinations of lyrics and melodies are built to endure and have taken a life of their own outside of their original creators.<br>',3,0,0,0,'209.244.129.86','en'),(11561,2084,'2001-11-22 16:03:51',0,'critic','<i>an interesting observation ive noticed on swinney:</i><br>\r\n1. someone makes a good, lengthy, and often somewhat opinionated post<br>\r\n2. wb comments with some good (albeit sarcastic?) insight<br>\r\n3. someone(s) proceed to rain down pissy comment fury on wb.<br><br>\r\neither wb is just the biggest asshole rabble-rouser and i just dont know it, and you guys are giving him his just deserts, or hes just taking alot of shit for having balls enough to post his honest (albeit not always the most tactful) opinion (much as the original poser did).<br><br>\r\nI\\\'m not trying to start another argument here, i just thought this was an really interesting oft-repeated phenomenon. someone, tell me... is wb that much of an asshole that we need to keep him in constant comment-check, or are we just not taking his criticism well?<br><br>\r\n<b><i>\\\"Super best friendly\\\"!</b></i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11562,2082,'2001-11-22 16:11:40',0,'cynn','look closer.',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.61','en'),(11563,2082,'2001-11-22 16:24:05',0,'White Stripes','\\\"I think I smell a rat, Ohhhhh, I think I smell a rat!\\\"',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11564,2084,'2001-11-22 16:43:22',0,'jo','some thoughts:\r\n<ol><li>did anyone else cringe when teens thought that limp bizkit\\\'s cover of george michael\\\'s \\\"faith\\\" was an original song? actually, <strike>cringe</strike>, sigh at \\\"the ignorance of the youth today?\\\"</li>\r\n<li>can we also name the phenomenon of r&b acts covering folk songs? specifically janet jackson\\\'s covering both joni mitchell and carly simon in the last 5 years? um. i never expected my 17-year-old cousin to be walking around singing \\\"you\\\'re so vain.\\\"</li>\r\n<li>i wonder if when rock acts of the 60s and 70s covered blues songs and/or incorporated their melodies into new songs, blues fans got up in arms.</li>\r\n</ol>',1,0,0,0,'172.141.86.192','en'),(11565,2084,'2001-11-22 16:45:26',0,'jo','oh, plus, i\\\'d like to offer my vote for \\\"just what i needed\\\" being one of the best pop songs of <b>all time</b>. ',1,0,0,0,'172.141.86.192','en'),(11566,2084,'2001-11-22 19:01:19',0,'wb','ix, the point of the piece was not that every cover today is a Scowling Cover. certainly i\'ve heard of the work of phish and while i\'m not a fan, like serious musicians they do covers to explore songs, have some fun, pay tribute. they try to reinvent, not just pose.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11567,2084,'2001-11-22 19:02:33',0,'wb','critic, yes i am that asshole. thanks for your careful observation of the situation. ',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11568,2084,'2001-11-22 19:05:26',0,'wb','jo, i haven\\\'t heard the carly simon/joni covers you mention, but while i really don\\\'t like janet jackson, i\\\'m sure those are much different from what i\\\'m talking about here. she is probably looking for good songs to reinvent in the R&B world. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\n(btw, maybe it was \\\"faith\\\" and not a cure song on the MTV that made LB famous, thanks...)\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsure some blues fans were offended, but the point of the Beatles wasn\\\'t to poke fun at R&B, it wasn\\\'t just a cynical little toss off, it was a tribute. ',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11569,2084,'2001-11-22 19:09:09',0,'wb','o, i agree with you to a certain level about the need to find our own standards. i don\\\'t think this is the way it will happen. because these bands just want to find an easy way to get on the radio/mtv what they are producing is very disposable and at the end of the day all that will be left is the original.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11570,2084,'2001-11-22 20:53:07',0,'jo','my point in bringing up the blues covers was to say that i think it\\\'s all about perspective. obviously we know those were tributes. limp bizkit covering \\\"faith\\\" is in no way a tribute to george michael but alien ant farm\\\'s michael jackson cover is <b>very</b> respectful of michael\\\'s career. the video is just out and out fucking brilliant, and i don\\\'t even like the band. i was just trying to say that just because it is or appears to be a toss-off band doesn\\\'t mean the cover isn\\\'t done respectfully, with care, or as a tribute. ',1,0,0,0,'172.145.153.2','en'),(11571,322,'2001-11-22 21:05:33',0,'katchu would say','i swear there were enough bubbles in there, look he is fine!',3,0,0,0,'198.144.206.110','en'),(11572,2075,'2001-11-22 21:14:10',0,'cynn','scorched earth.  i hunted down an old copy of it and relapsed big time.  1024x768 is insanity.  insanity i tell you!\r\n\r\nmike\r\nps.  death\\\'s head and hot napalm ... mmm.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.116','en'),(11573,1713,'2001-11-22 21:37:42',0,'critic','this is one of the most interesting strings of comments ive read on this site...',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11574,2084,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','its really too bad you are not coming over. you should smell my spice rub.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11575,2084,'2001-11-22 21:48:22',0,'drew','<p>a few more thoughts about covers:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i love covers, by the way.  i think they\\\'re fascinating.  tribute albums are so interesting even when the covers are not so good.  i love, love, love hearing familiar songs reinterpreted.  but that doesn\\\'t mean that i always find the results appealing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>rasputina is selling a 5-song EP on their web site.  it contains a not so good cover of zep\\\'s \\\"rock and roll,\\\" a great \\\"all tomorrow\\\'s parties,\\\" and okay takes on floyd\\\'s \\\"wish you were here\\\" and marilyn manson\\\'s \\\"tourniquet.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>tori amos\\\'s latest effort is kind of monstrous, in my opinion.  she\\\'s done some really impressive covers (her \\\"thank you\\\" is wonderful, i think) but these are not among them.  part of the problem is that i\\\'ve always loathed \\\"i don\\\'t like mondays\\\".  another is that \\\"enjoy the silence\\\" has a bland melody, being, ironically, all about the arrangement.  and i have a blind spot, i guess, about neil young, so i can\\\'t stand the original or her cover of it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>anyway.  the idea that these scowling covers are tributes to bands that influenced them makes me sad.  because the bands performing them are so lacking in originality and creative spark of their own.  it would almost be okay if they were ripping off their influences, but unfortunately they\\\'re ripping off their peers.  it\\\'s all so sad and colorless.  i feel sorry for kids growing up with this stuff -- as did some people for me, growing up with new wave.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hey, are these category radio buttons actually meaningful in some way?  what effect do they have?</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11576,2084,'2001-11-22 21:51:19',0,'drew','forgot to mention: that radiohead cover (\\\"nobody does it better\\\") makes me kinda queasy.  ditto that rabbit in the headlights song where they use the word \\\"suburbanites\\\".   i can\\\'t quite put my finger on why.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11577,632,'2001-11-22 23:35:10',0,'critic','i also am a tremendously major fan of girly-on-top. any other type of sex should be prohibited.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11578,2084,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i disagree that \\\"the scowling cover\\\" is a trick that bands employ solely to make money for mtv or kroq. having spent years of my life playing in various garage bands and going to more indie and local shows than i can count, i can say with full confidence that any covers i\\\'ve ever played (or heard played by others), were played to entertain our fans and pay tribute to the songs we loved and grew up with.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni just really don\\\'t believe everything is a conspiracy to make money. sometimes people do things because they enjoy them, and sometimes the things catch on.\r\n<br><br>\r\nmy favorite version of war pigs was played on accordions by two women wearing full lederhosen. kitschy? gimmicky? sure. but it made me happy to hear. black sabbath and a sense of humor are two things i fully embrace.\r\n<br><br>\r\nrecognizing the structure of a style does not equal understanding its intent or context.',5,0,0,0,'209.244.134.76','en'),(11579,2087,'2001-11-23 04:13:53',0,'well','the motherfucker better come strapped',1,0,0,0,'209.208.10.152','en'),(11580,1732,'2001-11-23 05:29:30',0,'elfboy','well, if i\\\'m gonna trust your cocktail recipes, i have to know if you can write, right?',0,0,0,0,'209.245.174.232','en'),(11581,2075,'2002-03-05 20:16:36',214,'darwin','<p>Tony Hawk 2 is a much more robust game than SSX. SSX is a good game, but Tony Hawk 2 just has a lot more variety and a lot more going on in it. I\\\'m sure Tony Hawk 3 is even more amazing.</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',2,0,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(11582,2084,'2001-11-23 07:52:36',0,'wb','s5, your example of war pigs is the exact opposite of what i was talking about (ie it sounds original and unique). i wasn\\\'t talking about your garage band, trying to entertain your friends. i don\\\'t think everything out there is a shameless attempt at mtv, but it\\\'s funny not many of these bands make videos covering songs that are in direct lineage, ie hardcore/punk songs from the olde days. minor threat, bad brains, etc etc.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11583,2084,'2001-11-23 19:31:21',0,'drew','since we have wandered a bit let\\\'s remember that the operative word is \\\"scowling.\\\"  covers are universal; taking a fairly innocuous song and trying to make it sound really tough and evil and ludicrously rough-hewn is not.\r\n\r\n<p>are you saying that\\\'s part of the garage-band tradition -- playing the song all butched up so it sounds silly?</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11584,2087,'2001-11-23 19:35:21',0,'kavaboy','does your doppelganger like kava?',0,0,0,0,'12.81.83.142','en'),(11585,2087,'2001-11-23 21:35:16',0,'kavagirl','mine likes hot lube and glitter dildos',1,0,0,0,'32.102.91.233','en'),(11586,2068,'2001-11-24 01:04:58',0,'poupou','<p>Hi all, I\\\'m poupou.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I did exactly what auntie said. I got tired of sitting around in a one-room box in seattle waiting for contract agents to call, so i enrolled in grad school and got on a plane to Bloomington, Indiana.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s not necessarily the money part of unemployment that makes people depressed; it\\\'s lack of daily structure, lack of purpose, and the feeling that nothing you accomplish today is going to be needed by any single human being.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>When we lose our jobs we become unsituated, invisible. We go from spending 8+ hours a day around people to sitting in our holes feeling sorry that we\\\'re not working on our personal projects, instead chatting and logging every bowel movement on the internet. Well guess what people, humans are social creatures and we need support to be creative.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Anyway, I don\\\'t really have a solution to all of this but if you can become organizationally situated it will be the best thing for you. Go to school, join americorps, get a job at the waffle house and write a book about it later. Sitting around thinking about what you should be doing on your own is a stupid cultural fantasy.</p>',2,0,0,0,'156.56.126.67','en'),(11587,2089,'2001-11-24 03:47:50',0,'Joanna','\\\"and she\\\'s gone girl-scent fades less rapidly\\\"\r\nI dig that line',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11588,2084,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','yes, drew, that is exactly what i\\\'m saying.',3,0,0,0,'209.244.142.44','en'),(11589,2089,'2001-11-24 06:41:11',0,'elfboy','thank you.\r\n\r\n<p>incidentally, this peice was originally entitled \\\"first encounter\\\" but silly me, i fucked up and neglected to fill in the title field.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.214.9.89','en'),(11590,2087,'2001-11-24 06:44:41',0,'elfboy','<p>my doople-ganjer was actually more into snorting crank mixed with comet, i think, and preferred the glitter to be painted on his large collection of antique nairobian butt plugs. hey, you asked.</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.214.9.89','en'),(11591,389,'2001-11-24 16:26:41',0,'critic','yes, she is <i>extremely</i> hot!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11592,2082,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','A while back (about two weeks ago), a mouse landed the mouse version of the Darwin award in my apartment.  It choose to came into my apartment.  An apartment whose population consists of (usually) two indifferent humans and two Very Hungry And Boisterous Cats.  (You have to understand, this apartment has no pest problems, I don\\\'t know where the mouse could have come from.)  So Bob the big orange male cat and Mi-kay the medium sized japanese tortise shell calico female cat were on the little guy like a bad dream.  Bam bam bam!  By the time I even noticed what was going on behind me they\\\'d killed it (and I still haven\\\'t been able to get the blood stain out of the tile).  Then they were both staring at it like \\\"Now what? This is as far as our insincts have told us... Oh, btw human, <em>CAN WE HAVE SOME MORE CAT FOOD?!?!?! MEOW MEOW MEOW!!!</em>\\\"',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(11593,2087,'2001-11-24 21:23:36',0,'chi chi','how do you know that you\\\'re not the doppleganger?',0,0,0,0,'65.0.55.117','en'),(11594,2084,'2001-11-24 21:42:15',0,'drew','sits corrected',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11595,2089,'2001-11-25 02:23:43',0,'elfboy','oh. never mind. thanks austin.',0,0,0,0,'209.245.171.181','en'),(11596,2087,'2001-11-25 02:25:10',0,'elfboy','uhhhhhhh. mm. ~because i don\\\'t have doppleganger- breath? at least, my friends say i don\\\'t......',0,0,0,0,'209.245.171.181','en'),(11597,2072,'2001-11-25 06:12:16',0,'curious','is that egg??',1,0,0,0,'63.93.80.114','en'),(11598,2090,'2001-11-25 17:40:52',0,'hool','<p>i wrote a letter to the author of the Clifford books when i was young.  (the big red dog).  the author wrote back to me, and even included a hand drawing of Clifford on the letter.  alas, there was no mention of the meaning of life.</p>',1,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11599,2090,'2001-11-25 17:48:11',0,'lush','read <i>Ishmael</i>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.66','en'),(11600,2090,'2001-11-25 19:22:01',0,'jo','wow. my friend <a href=\\\"http://www.earmouse.com/\\\">matt</a> just sent me a long email yesterday about how someone had recommended a l\\\'engle book about dolphins to him so he was reading it even though he was sort of conflicted about reading a l\\\'engle book at 22.',0,0,0,0,'172.136.9.7','en'),(11601,1607,'2001-11-25 19:26:03',0,'lush','when i was ten there was a man who use to sit in his car jacking off by the sidwalk where the kids walked home after school. when i was eighteen i was sitting on some ocean cliffs in a secluded spot and this guy started doing his thing in the middle of the path and i had to walk past him to leave. when i was twenty-six my brother and i were visiting family and stuck in the same bedroom...i woke up to him noisily going at it. it both frightens and excites me to watch others masturbate. i have never seen a woman touch herself.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.66','en'),(11602,2075,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','it\\\'s all about Unreal Tournament',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(11603,2082,'2001-11-26 04:52:19',0,'fecolalia','i thought i had a mouse living in my walls, but it turned out that the 4-year old was still alive.',0,0,0,0,'66.20.104.208','en'),(11604,2090,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i didn\\\'t send it. after i wrote it (at 4am) i felt better and went back to sleep. who cares if life has any meaning or not? it\\\'s not like there\\\'s anything you can do about it.',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.23','en'),(11605,2072,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i dont put text on canvas.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11606,1607,'2001-11-26 08:16:43',0,'drew','i love stories about \\\"desperate\\\" and theoretically straight men getting off together.  it\\\'s not that it\\\'s \\\"taboo\\\" so much as borderline taboo for <em>them</em>...the inventiveness in trying to get off without wholly crossing one\\\'s own personal boundaries.  it\\\'s a pity i always get along better with women than men, or else i would really have enjoyed some of these men-only settings.  as it is i did pretty well temporarily corrupting some of my male friends growing up.\r\n\r\nas for ingesting one\\\'s own semen: it\\\'s something of a subterfuge tactic when one lives at home and must conceal evidence, but doesn\\\'t have a lot of intrinsic allure.  not trying it, though, is like driving 55 all one\\\'s life.',2,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11607,2072,'2001-11-26 13:35:12',0,'bmg','is she clenching those cheeks?',1,0,0,0,'66.90.153.83','en'),(11608,2081,'2001-11-26 15:32:15',0,'just me','... but when the box is full where do you turn?  The box may have to be emptied several times... do you fill it back up?  Maybe what\\\'s in the box is no good.  Maybe the box needs to be selective in what it lets in... but how far can you go?  Take a look in the box.  Examine the box and its contents.',6,0,0,0,'216.94.239.165','en'),(11609,517,'2001-11-26 15:58:44',0,'jesus','<p>\r\ni know karate. voodoo too.\r\ni got hair on my chest, i look good without a shirt......\r\n</p>',0,0,0,0,'209.245.171.8','en'),(11610,2093,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','suicide is lazy and cowardly.  kick your friends in the ass until they grasp this point.<br><br>\r\nbut yeah, this year sucked.  so did most of last year.  I\\\'m hoping 2002 will be nice to us all.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(11611,517,'2001-11-26 17:14:38',0,'T. W.','jesus, dude...are you biting my rhymes? my friends think i\\\'m ugly, i didn\\\'t realize you did too.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.109.231','en'),(11612,2093,'2001-11-26 17:46:00',0,'dissolved','<p>reading. angry. laughing. fucking bastards. quiet rant. this is not cowardice! get a life of your own. there is no honor in judging other\\\'s pain. loud rant. running away is. can i say it again? running away is. coward. i have heard this before...this angry petulant argument of you lazy sob...quit thinking about dying. but if that is how you feel then you never knew. the edge is the edge and if you are thinking about how it easy it might be or how lazy it is then you have not DANCED THE DANCE KISSING DEATH. so just shut the fuck up i say. you do not know the pain. the real pain. the lost love and the what ifs. the suffocation. oh no...you have no fucking clue. how dare you say my name. my name and this all in one breath. because you have no fucking clue. BROKEN PROPS WORTHLESS TALK. it all seems to be about get it fast...get it faster...get it over with...they always just wanna know did you cum yet...are you done yet...how heartless how soulless...if i\\\'m in it im in for the full ride...it\\\'s gotta be dark and deep and shining...i want to smell it taste it drink it savor it...i want it all. weakness to live for less. numbness. right. if you want it that way. well go for it the half-assed life. but the real question is, are you ready for the ride? BATTLE WORN WAR TORN ALL ABUSED BITTER AND LOOSE. are you ready for the wake up call? when it comes will you know what it is. will you flip the switch or make friends with the dark. will you say goodnight to it all. will you fight it like a tiger or lick your wounds and whimper. when the fear takes over do you let go. do you lust for it? this is not fear or avoidance or laziness. this all you can see. this drowning in it. this is wanting and wanting and wanting the pain to end. this is not knowing what else to do. this is going once....going twice....going down for good. only because you have said what you could say. and your platitudes and defenses are nothing but cement on my feet. DEAD COMPASSION WASTED IN YOUR MIND. if you\\\'re gonna love gotta love all of me, all of you. so lost in it. the not loving. it is a lesson to learn. not what we are born with. forgiveness. and risks. and compassion. loving me and loving you. the dont give a damn me and me the afraid to be outside me....me the love me cherish me and me the don\\\'t touch me...me always feeling lost never satisfied never comfortable me and me the so serene so content me...me the lost and shy me and me the down and dirty don\\\'t get in my way better do what i say me... in the end all we\\\'ve got to give...and to love...and to live with...is me. whatever that may be. BLINDING SLIDE COLD METAL RIDE ONE WAY TICKETS DEAD END SIGNS.</p> \r\n<p>erasure. erase it. erase me. you\\\'re already halfway there...and quit running away. you won\\\'t ever be a man until you can say it with honesty. face it. fuck it and face it. you know what i am saying? if you have to ask, then you\\\'ll never know.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>[X]BURN! BURN!</p>',6,0,0,0,'32.102.109.231','en'),(11613,2093,'2001-11-26 18:51:09',0,'zb','<p><br>\r\n  i have always felt the weight of the world.<br>\r\n  right now, it is heavier than i could have ever imagined.<br>\r\n  hang in there ~ it will be a dark winter...but everything will be lighter in the spring.<br>\r\nand yes, i really do believe this.<br>\r\n</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.201.202.217','en'),(11614,2093,'2001-11-26 18:59:13',0,'that guy to dissolved','yes, yes, yes. you are right, so right. mostly I think that there comes a time when one realizes what one has missed because of fear. i fear much, i fear that my words speak little of what is really going on. erasing your name doesn\\\'t help, avoiding contact doesn\\\'t help either. I think that anything i say, to you now will do nothing but fuel this more than it should. I don\\\'t run away, i simply stop doing - and i do this on purpose, as to avoid just this type of situation. pause, pause, breathe. i could describe pain to you, but we have so many different versions of pain. the pain you have experienced is not the pain i have experienced. yet I wouldn\\\'t trade my pain for anything, my pain (different than yours) is my pain - it may be that your pain may be more severe, but who is to say that it is? I am sure that there are many other people in this world that have experienced much more pain than I ever will, still pain is pain. comments are cheap, actions aren\\\'t. I like what you said, not to get me wrong, i think you understand much more than i do. i admit I am lazy, sometimes plain proud of it. I get excited rarely. I spend my time with myself, for myself, and that is sometimes mistaken for laziness - but its not. i dare not play, because I see no use in it.<br>best friendly.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.153.83','en'),(11615,2090,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Well, perhaps more insights into Madeline L\\\'Engle\\\'s take on the meaning of life in regard to \\\"A Wrinkle In Time\\\" will be revealed in the upcoming Disney/ABC four-hour miniseries adaption of the book (and nope, I\\\'m not kidding--I\\\'ve seen a trailer for it already). But somehow I doubt it....',2,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11616,2093,'2001-11-26 19:41:55',0,'???','uh, people...this aint makin much sense. not playing? what game are we talking about? dancing in the dark? er, isn\\\'t some of what u are saying about suicide.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.109.231','en'),(11617,2093,'2001-11-26 19:49:21',0,'dissolved','it doesn\\\'t matter. i chase dreams that aren\\\'t dreams. only storms to be lost in. everything i cherish dies. i bleed poisonous things. no. it is me who is venomous. double bones skull n cross on my heart. feeding the bears. it is no wonder that i\\\'m eaten alive. with this deathwish i have. we all self-destruct in one way or another...over and over again. and it doesn\\\'t matter. it really doesn\\\'t matter. cause i don\\\'t know much of anything either.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.109.231','en'),(11618,2092,'2001-11-26 19:49:34',0,'cynn','remember when we went out for dinner at the fish place and i got the old guy to sing johnny cash?\r\n\r\ni got to call 911 when i worked at the tilting planet.  some drunk guy came in with a computer and tried to sell it to us.  he also happened to be bleeding profusely.  then the guy who\\\'s house he\\\'d just broken into, gotten in a fight with, and gotten stabbed by came in and dragged him outside to hurt him some more.  that was one of my \\\"cleaning blood stains out of the carpet is not in my job description - i\\\'m going home\\\" moments.',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.67','en'),(11619,2093,'2001-11-26 20:19:03',0,'drew','it seems as though discussions of suicidal feelings always end up in abstracts.  \\\"we\\\" tend to think that there are concrete situations and problems that lead one to feel suicidal but i don\\\'t think i believe that.  it\\\'s what makes suicidal people so frustrating to those trying to help; no amount of practical discussion makes any difference.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11620,2093,'2001-11-26 22:46:09',0,'stats','fucking sweet meat is nothing compared to making love with a big passionate throbbing brain',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.22','en'),(11621,2092,'2001-11-26 22:55:19',0,'critic','but did you actually fall into the fire?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11622,2092,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I do remember that night.  We went to the Bridge Tavern.  It was my idea.  My parents had been taking our family there rather regularly for as far back as I can remember.  Now that I think of it, they should probably rename the place to <i>Bridges</i> Tavern, seeing as there are two of them now.<br><br>That was a fun outing, especially after you got your request for tha J.C.  Speaking of J.C., my friend Jesus was with us that night.  I wonder if he remembers that night.<br><br>[insert some ICQ action here]<br><br>Hrm, no response.  Oh well.  Can\\\'t blame a guy for trying.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.72.69','en'),(11623,2092,'2001-11-27 05:07:24',0,'um, hi','fuzzy, what are youi talking about? bridges tavern, JC, icq? i dont get it. maybe its some inside joke or something. <i>/me dislikes feeling alienated.</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11624,2080,'2001-11-27 05:25:15',0,'scott','Dear Gostu,\r\n    We both know that this is a disgusting fabrication, stemming from your sexual frustration. It is the hallmark of a little, insecure man, attempting to vindicate himself by spinning half-truths at the sake of someone he once counted as a friend. I hope your dick shrivels because you have sold out somebody who once trusted you, who once felt safe enough to share a bedroom with you. That you are so reprehensible that your roommate would not touch you, is your own fault, not hers. Please quit slandering her, because her greatest indiscretion on the night of which you speak was a predilection for watching televsion, while her pants stayed entirely buttoned. Just because your own life is small and boring, don\\\'t drag the good intentions and friendly overtures of a very, very sweet girl through the mud.\r\n-Scott (you know who I am)',6,0,0,0,'66.125.88.95','en'),(11625,2080,'2001-11-27 07:02:23',0,'ssj3gostu','hi scott. yes, i do indeed know who you are. you are the guy that wanted to come over to my house with a baseball bat and break the legs of my roommate becuase he was talking to a girl that you kinda liked a while back.\r\n\r\nhave you ever seen <i>a bronx tale</i>? it\\\'s a really good movie. robert deniro is in it. and chazz palminteri, he\\\'s in it too. it\\\'s about this kid, named c, who is tempted by the (apparently) sweet trappings of the mob, the easy money and cute girls and what not. and bobby deniro is the kid\\\'s father, and chazz plays the neighborhood don, and the two of them are constantly pulling at the kid in opposite directions. \r\n\r\nyou know, good and evil and all that.\r\n\r\nanyway, there\\\'s this great scene in the movie where c is after this other kid in the neighborhood becuase he owes him, like, twenty bucks. and c chases this kid around and can never catch up with him, and c- well, c is kinda pissed.\r\n\r\nand c tells chazz palminteri about it and chaz palminteri tells c- he says, \\\'look, that kid owes you twenty bucks right? so, think of it this way: for twenty bucks you can be rid of that kid for the rest of you life.\\\'\r\n\r\nit really is a great great movie.\r\n\r\nbut (and this is my point), see scott, you, you are my twenty dollars. \r\n\r\nsee, after i read your comment above i was all ready to rip into you, you know? like, i was going to ask you, like, who it was that you thought i was i was trying to vindicate myself to. and i was going to commend you for your use of the word \\\'predilection.\\\' and i was going to be angry and call you bald and just be terribly unoriginal.\r\n\r\nand, i don\\\'t know- i was just going to make a scene, you know?\r\n\r\nbut why bother? right? i mean really.\r\n\r\ni figure, if this is what she needs to put this to rest and leave me alone, then fine. she can have it. i will leave this here, and i won\\\'t comment on anything that you wrote up there and i\\\'ll let you call me a liar.\r\n\r\nand i\\\'ll even let you write back to this (and i won\\\'t comment on that comment) and i\\\'ll let you have the last word.\r\n\r\ni will do all of this, for you, in hopes that it will end the game that she has been playing for years and years.\r\n\r\nand if it doesn\\\'t end it- well, then, atleast i won\\\'t have to play anymore.\r\n\r\n[best friendly]',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(11626,2090,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','yeah, i saw that about the miniseries.<br><br>\r\nlush--bought it, read it tonight. i liked it, it made some good points, but it kind of had that &quot;Get in the car, son, you are coming with us&quot;-type argumentation. i can\\\'t put my finger on it, but i felt like its lightning pace and hasty gathering of a hodgepodge of religious and philosophical concepts hid more than a few <i>non sequiturs</i>. but i\\\'ll have to think about it for a while.',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.106','en'),(11627,2093,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','what in the living fuck is wrong with you? what about life is bad? this <i>is</i> Paradise, if you choose to view it that way; there are plenty of pleasures to support that theory. it also is hell, should you choose that perspective, and more than enough pain exists to back it up all day long. which brings me back, as always, to the core of my philosophy, which is that we should devote our lives to the lifted spirits of those within our sphere of influence. selflessness is the key. if your attention is directed at your own happiness, it will always seem to fall short.',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.106','en'),(11628,2093,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','...or whatever...like...',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.106','en'),(11629,2090,'2001-11-27 08:17:56',0,'lush','funny i was thinking of this same thing. went to see that French flick Amelie...it said more to me than anything i\\\'ve seen or read in a long time. or maybe that is only because i can relate to living in a dreamworld. i think so much is lost to adults because we think we have to be \\\'grown\\\' up and do things because that is how they are done. and i guess in some ways we do need to grow up, but then life is life is life. it is all there for the grasping as long as you can find the courage to reach out. and really you can live however you want to live...there will always be someone there to try and drag you down regardless. i think people cling so much to pain because we KNOW it. there\\\'s no risk in it. it is just the same over and over again...and you can say, oh well guess that\\\'s that. but the risk in those moments of joy? i think the secret is that there is no risk...it only makes you want more and then fear the possibility of losing it never having it again. do you choose fantasy? or reality? really there is no reason for us to be here at all. so why not enjoy it...either road brings mistakes and pain. the best we can do is revel in the gifts and know that it is possible to survive anything.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.22','en'),(11630,2093,'2001-11-27 09:33:34',0,'drew','tends to prefer meat and brain combined, personally',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11631,1143,'2001-11-27 09:48:25',0,'castiza','waiting?  for what may i ask...or for whom...?',0,0,0,0,'202.154.128.40','en'),(11632,1398,'2001-11-27 11:09:24',0,'elfboy','<p>\r\nthe dawn of man. 2001 music please.<br>\r\nbone to club to spear to cannon to nuclear missile. clay to glass to light bulb to circuits to fake tits. enter the neverending commercial, the mad race to make <b>everything</b> instant and disposable. want it, got it, don\\\'t want it anymore : next please. welcome to the empire of styrofoam and celluloid. have a seat, never leave it. your kids are fine, they just need more ritalin. you\\\'re fine you just need to stay up and watch info-mercials on how to cure insomnia. go ahead, swallow it, you\\\'ll feel better in the morning.\r\n</p>\r\n*     *     *     *     *     *     *     *     *\r\n<p>\r\nbut i get up every day just because it\\\'s worth my time to carve out my own little jewelled world. at some point(s) i network it with other little jewelled worlds. maybe this way the tide of newspaper lingerie ads and other empty debris can be turned. i mean, what else would i be doing?\r\n</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.154.191.111','en'),(11633,1546,'2001-11-27 13:00:02',0,'critic','brooklyn is even further east than spanish harlem!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11634,1398,'2001-11-27 13:05:38',0,'critic','um. elfboy, i dont get it.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11635,2088,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<p><i>\\\"Common sense ain\\\'t so common.\\\"</i></p><p>Cheers... and welcome, Pokey.  Have a beer.</p>',1,0,0,0,'198.45.18.20','en'),(11636,2088,'2001-11-27 16:41:09',0,'critic','i want a beer. email me one, <i>yo</i>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11637,2093,'2001-11-27 17:07:19',0,'hi','bmg?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11638,2096,'2001-11-27 17:37:31',0,'Herr Doktor','ok. let me get this straight... theres a <i>fish smell</i>, and it comes from the <i>canyons downtown</i>, the <i>methodical</i> canyons, no less. all the while, you are not <i>lacking in fire</i>, the <i>fish-bubble air</i> is being <i>driven through downtown</i>, and you <i>extrapolate</i> whether or not some starving chinese kids are getting <i>drunk</i> off your <i>beer breath</i>. hi.<br><br>\r\n[x] constructive<br><br>\r\n...maybe i just dont get it. maybe i\'m not <i>supposed</i> to get it; its just supposed to be disturbing/haunting/scatter-brained/dense for some metaphysical purpose. yea, thats more likely. but <i>why</i>...?\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11639,2096,'2001-11-27 18:16:12',0,'echo','i get it.',0,0,0,0,'67.163.164.15','en'),(11640,2097,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i\\\'d rather have a <i>chili</i> evening at burger king!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11641,2096,'2001-11-27 18:19:53',0,'critic','i\\\'ll have to admit, i dont really <i>get it</i>. although, i\\\'m not very bothered by this fact, as there\\\'s plenty of stuff here that i <i>do</i> get, so i just read that stuff.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11642,2097,'2001-11-27 18:28:35',0,'Herr Doktor','<b>note:</b> for that, i will now divulge peter\\\'s gassy chili recipe:<br>\r\n1 or 2 big \\\'ol cans o\\\' kidney beans (packed in water)<br>about 1/2 lb. (hydrated) sauteed, minced tvp<br>\r\nlots of chili powder<br>\r\n1/4 cup soy paste<br>\r\n1 big ol\\\' can o\\\' stewed tomatoes<br>\r\n1 big chopped onion<br>\r\nvarious other chili-riffic things/spices/pepper...<br><br>\r\ndump beans and tomatoes into pot and start heating. sautee chopped onions (or other chili things) and set aside. brown minced tvp in same pan with remaining oil, and brown it well, or you\\\'ll get gassy ;). dump soy paste into tvp along with a little water to hydrate. heat. dump tvp stuff into heating beans, et al. add whatever otehr chili-ish junk you want. add many, many tablespoons of chili-powder. cook for a couple of hours and add a little water if it starts drying out too quickly. cook until its a nice, soft sloppy goo. eat. repeat...\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11643,2096,'2001-11-27 18:31:05',0,'garbageman','dude, you said \\\'fraegnated\\\'',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11644,1602,'2001-11-27 18:34:40',0,'critic','no, its all about run-on sentences!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11645,2093,'2001-11-27 20:26:03',0,'bmg','hi?',0,0,0,0,'66.90.153.83','en'),(11646,2097,'2001-11-27 21:02:54',0,'what','the fuck is tvp?',0,0,0,0,'209.162.49.145','en'),(11647,2097,'2001-11-27 22:13:49',0,'tvp','texturized vegetable protein',1,0,0,0,'32.100.70.129','en'),(11648,2096,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiam','and people ask if im high when i write. ',4,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11649,2096,'2001-11-27 23:38:00',0,'jim','I like limes.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11650,2090,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i would say that there is no one overarching system of philosophy, one that applies to all people in all situations. motivations grow too complicated to predict, except of course the basic biological imperative stuff.  ',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.79','en'),(11651,2085,'2001-11-28 00:19:38',0,'berg','<p>You know.  There\\\'s other fish in the sea</p>',2,0,0,0,'216.180.197.49','en'),(11652,2093,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','somebody\\\'s been listening to rollins again.',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.140','en'),(11653,2080,'2001-11-28 01:23:37',0,'Scott','Glad you remember me, although, in apparently typical fashion, you have the details wrong. That girl I \\\"kinda liked\\\" was at the time, and still is, my girlfriend. And your roommate was both consistently coming onto her and trash-talking myself, while at the same time involved with another friend of mine. Call me crazy, but it rubbed me the wrong way. I thought it was funny how seriously you guys took it, personally.\r\nHave you ever seen As Good as it Gets? It\\\'s a heartwarming tale about a writer with OCD (ably played by Jack Nicholson), who slowly sheds off his sheltered life and learns to interract with others in a normal way. At one point during the movie, the maid of Jack Nicholson?s artist neighbor, played by Greg Kinnear, asks whether or not Jack will continue to look after Greg?s dog. After Jack agrees, the maid begins yammering about opening windows to let in God?s beauty and etcetera. Finally, Jack gets fed up and tells her to \\\"Sell crazy somewhere else. We?re all full up here.\\\"\r\nWhat does this have to do with you? Nothing. I just like the line. But I thought it was apropos to pull out a useless, labored movie quote, because yours is equally inapplicable. I?m not your twenty. If the woman in question were the scheming, Machiavellian harpy you pretend she is, then I?d be your twenty. If there was some sort of ongoing conflict in which she was an eager participant, I?d be your twenty. But what happened on Saturday night was just one person going over to her friends? house to hang out, you know, like normal people do. And you, soul of benevolence that you are, take it as an invitation not only to insinuate that she screwed herself silly on Saturday, but also to lay out some of her deepest secrets for the wide world to devour. Now, I won?t pretend to understand the convoluted dramas you kids imported with you from the East Coast, but spinning tales about her and presenting them as truth (I assume you intend this \\\"autobiography\\\" to be interpreted as true) just strikes me as vindictive and mean-spirited. And it\\\'s not up to me to call you \\\"liar,\\\" because your own words name you such. You see, I read the rather apologetic e-mail you sent to the young woman in question, admitting your lies, and telling her to smooth things over with a mutual friend. So, while I was momentarily abashed by the front of righteous indignation you threw over your purely low-class self-aggrandizement, it doesn\\\'t hold water. So quit blowing smoke up my ass, and your readers? asses, and leave the poor girl alone. I wish the best to you and yours at the House of Usher.  ',2,0,0,0,'208.142.129.212','en'),(11654,2096,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','it\\\'s like a really, really, really bad phish song.',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.140','en'),(11655,2093,'2001-11-28 02:06:11',0,'bmg','the thing i like about sonny rollins is his melodic inclinations. he lacks the creativity of coltrane, still he knows how to play...',0,0,0,0,'66.90.153.184','en'),(11656,2096,'2001-11-28 03:00:13',0,'steph','theres a good phish song? ah...i think thats an oxymoron. no, i know it is.',3,0,0,0,'64.12.103.184','en'),(11657,901,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','he died',4,0,0,0,'151.201.32.16','en'),(11658,2080,'2001-11-28 03:31:52',0,'uh...','email is best for handling personal grudges.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11659,2096,'2001-11-28 03:32:39',0,'well','every phish song is better than this...',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11660,901,'2001-11-28 03:33:22',0,'critic','i like incitor',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11661,2096,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','apparently steph is a britney spears fan.',0,0,0,0,'61.9.128.140','en'),(11662,2096,'2001-11-28 04:27:13',0,'anonymous','i have a complicated fetish that revolves around the act of eating chilled fava beans off of britney\\\'s bare abs. ohhhhhhhhhhhhh. call me weird if you like, but i cant think of anything more sexy. too bad her singing is so terrible.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11663,2096,'2001-11-28 04:31:15',0,'well, then','<img src=\\\"http://home.zonnet.nl/britney2/gallery32/britney15.jpg\\\"><br>can someone say <i>\\\"YUM\\\"</i>?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11664,2090,'2001-11-28 05:18:51',0,'auntie','the answer is, of course - 42<br><br>\r\n\r\nif that doesn\\\'t satisfy you, perhaps this will -- i believe that every life touches others, and we are each here to change the world into a better place, a place closer to whatever your view of heaven/eden/utopia/nirvana, etc. might be.<br><br>\r\n\r\nnone of us will reach the pinnacle of this heavenly existence during our lifetimes, but striving for it gets us all a little closer.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwhen we try real hard to be \\\"good,\\\" we get rewarded by a nice resting place between lifetimes - heaven.  if we screw up, we get hell.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin the meantime, every good thing you do comes back to you sevenfold, and every bad thing you do comes back to you seven times seven.<br><br>\r\n\r\nso, play nice.  and read lots of books and write to authors, because they like it a lot when you do, on account of authors are just people who put their brains down on paper in a way that entertains and/or informs the rest of us.<br><br>',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11665,2099,'2001-11-28 05:42:03',0,'Lunesse','Failed? Shoot. You still have a couple days.\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve got about 800 words to go. I could do it tonight, but I think I\\\'m going to read instead. Some quality reading, unlike my effort!\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'66.126.181.186','en'),(11666,2096,'2001-11-28 06:35:02',0,'well','no, actually. i would say \\\'ick\\\' and \\\'ew\\\' and \\\'she looks like a man in too much mascara\\\' and \\\'what\\\'s the appeal\\\'?',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11667,2096,'2001-11-28 07:24:11',0,'ha ha','Hedwig is sexier',3,0,0,0,'32.102.64.61','en'),(11668,2096,'2001-11-28 12:07:14',0,'well','her somewhat manly look is a major turn-on for some...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11669,2099,'2001-11-28 12:56:14',0,'critic','you tease us with your preface! this is some quality stuff... i think. any chance for more?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11670,2097,'2001-11-28 13:02:54',0,'critic','i love tvp!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11671,2096,'2001-11-28 13:05:29',0,'critic','britney\\\'s musical talent is dubious, and i\\\'d probably find anyone that really digs her music rather laughable. but, you can\\\'t deny the fact that, physically, she is quite attractive, at least in a boy-likes-blonde-all-american-girl kinda way. i wonder how lauded her pop-trash tunes would be if she was an acne-scarred brunette, a few pounds overweight (like most of her fans?).. hmm!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11672,2098,'2001-11-28 13:11:50',0,'Word Games','<i>Rearrange the words in the above article for added entertainment!</i><br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"Lucky for me that a dying alien barely does her, touched on the naked box. I\\\'m a comic, and was put in a grave for two years because i touched her on her naked heart, and this is not legal. I\\\'m barely going to die\\\"\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11673,2098,'2001-11-28 13:44:44',0,'Zelig','Excellent! Thatâ€™s what I call entertainment ! ',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(11674,2098,'2001-11-28 16:26:07',0,'bmg','heh. i  dig you zelig.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.153.184','en'),(11675,2097,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','6 oz. of tomato paste blenderized with 5 clovs garlic. put in pan with a cup of tvp, pre hydrated. simmer together with some veggie soup base and a dab of thai red curry. vegan sloppy joes!@ optional: tomatos, not dog links, vodka, hamburger buns.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11676,2097,'2001-11-28 18:15:02',0,'Herr Doktor','Bring on the tvp recipes! weeb, yours sounds good. i might try it tonight...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11677,2099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>failed, yes, because I <i>quit</i>.  my story was going absolutely <i>nowhere</i>!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11678,2099,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I\\\'ll freely admit that I think NaNoWriMo is a vastly cool idea. And it\\\'s something that I might\\\'ve taken part in if [a] I\\\'d somehow heard about it sooner (I wish I\\\'d been paying attention when y\\\'all were first talking about this) and [b] I hadn\\\'t just landed a new tech writing job after my all-summer \\\"sabbatical\\\" (\\\'tis hard to write *anything* at night after writing manuals all day).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But I was wondering how this would actually turn out for everyone. I\\\'m pretty sure that just about every established novelist would agree that just as Rome wasn\\\'t built in a day, 50,000 word novels aren\\\'t (often) written in a month. (Though I\\\'d make an exception for porn: a college friend of mine supported himself partly by churning out 150 page trashy porn novels every 3-4 days for $500 bucks per novel.)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In any case, Peter, I admire you (and all the other participants) for trying. So you didn\\\'t reach the summit of Mt. Everest this time. There\\\'s always next month, right?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And yr prologue--hey, it\\\'s ok! A tad bit overwritten & overwrought, but given the fact that even the NaNoWriMo organizers say that their event is about \\\"the timely production of crappy novels\\\" I\\\'d say yr piece starts out better than most. Now that the frenzy is over you have time to iron it out into something grand. </p>',3,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11679,1561,'2001-11-28 18:42:09',0,'kat','i am only here to read this post. i love this post. it\\\'s like when i\\\'m sitting at the bar at Denny\\\'s eating apple pie and the waitress winks and says...\\\'i heard it was against the law to eat pie without ice cream\\\' and then adds a scoop of melty vanilla to my plate and doesn\\\'t charge me for it.\r\n\r\n<p>my favorite part is when Jamazon says, <i>they\\\'re just fucking gay.</i></p>',1,0,0,0,'32.102.91.206','en'),(11680,2099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks for the props. as for the over-written-ness: the nanowrimo peeps advised developing a mechanism or process that helps the writing flow.  my schtick was to only write while fucked up and to not edit except for punctuation, typos, and <i>maybe</i> a little for continuity and structure here and there. in that context, i was rather pleased at how it turned out, heh! although i abandoned it due to the tedium, it was a good chance to do some writing that abberated from my usual perspective, and it was a blast.<br><br>as the end of nanowrimo draws nigh, i urge all the other swinney participants to post some of the fruits of their labours... could be fun.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11681,1561,'2001-11-28 18:50:19',0,'critic','shit! i havent read this post before. this is quite possibly the most sordidly funny thing ive ever read. im sitting here babbling over with laughter from it. some of you people are so strange. i love it.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11682,2099,'2001-11-28 19:02:47',0,'jack kerouac','my schtick was to only write while fucked up and to not edit except for punctuation, typos, and maybe a little for continuity and structure here and there. \r\n(and you know what truman capote said, don\\\'t you?  but he\\\'s an old fuddy duddy)',4,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11683,2098,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Jimmy Corrigan is the smartest kid on earth.',1,0,0,0,'141.117.243.87','en'),(11684,2099,'2001-11-28 21:00:03',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nwhen i was nineteen i dropped out of school to write a book, and i wrote the entire thing on ephedrine. i found it helped me from getting blocked- which is something i deffinately needed help with, cause i have a tendency to just stop and give up whenever the natural flow of words from my brain stops.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nit worked pretty well though. i wrote three hundred pages in about a month and a half.</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.211','en'),(11685,2099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes, uppers; i hit the 50k word mark less than two weeks into november.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11686,2100,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hooray for writing! and hooray for reel-to-reels, also. as soon as my extraneous roomies move out next month, thats the first thing i\\\'m unpacking- the giant, old, tube-driven, wood-encased reel-to-reel that i inherited from my mom; endless hours of constant non-stop droning, old mix reels she assembled in 1968, tapes of my mixes, tapes of city noise.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11687,2100,'2001-11-28 21:39:19',0,'oh dear','People really should avoid channeling Brett Easton Ellis when they write poetry. It\\\'s probably worse for your soul in the long run than any of the vices described here...',4,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11688,2100,'2001-11-28 21:43:33',0,'tru','it\\\'s not long enough to be epic',0,0,0,0,'156.153.254.42','en'),(11689,2092,'2001-11-28 21:58:52',0,'cynn','<p>\\\"i shot a man in reno, just to watch him die.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on that note, it turns out that we have both \\\"johnny cash at folsom prison\\\" and \\\"johnny cash at san quentin\\\" on vinyl.  jeah.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.106','en'),(11690,2100,'2001-11-28 22:01:33',0,'critic','bret e. ellis was a poet?! hrm. why can\\\'t people just insult your writing to your face? i really hate it when people on here post under pseudonyms, bleech. ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11691,2100,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','critic, is your name REALLY \\\"critic\\\"? wow!',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11692,2100,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','haha',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11693,2100,'2001-11-28 22:43:30',0,'critic','about as much as your name is really <i>egg</i>. fyi, this <i>is</i> the only name ive posted on here as.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11694,2100,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Oh, ok, I\\\'ll fess up and admit: insulting comment above was written by me. You\\\'re right, everyone who has said this before is right: you shouldn\\\'t go on w/negative criticism if you can\\\'t at least back it up with your name, or screen handle, or whatever. Bleech, indeed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And I regretted that snide comment after I posted it. It was a knee-jerk reaction to the happy-go-lucky \\\"sex, drugs & decadence\\\" posturing in the poem--which I think overall is kind of tired--without taking into account the fact that some of my favorite writers--such as Dennis Cooper and, yeah, Mr. Ellis--go on about such topics ad nauseam and I still get a kick out of them. For all I know the poem was meant to be a parody of said posturing. And I kind of enjoyed the poem\\\'s latter 1/2. So perhaps I ought to\\\'ve kept my trap shut.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And no, Ellis isn\\\'t a poet as far as I know. Sorry for the confusion.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11695,2099,'2001-11-29 01:41:52',0,'drew\\\'s nanowrimono','still only about 13,000 words of me.  i know drew could have finished me if he\\\'d tried harder.  after all, he\\\'s had the entire month free of work.  what a sad sack.  i keep hearing him talk about getting back to me and being faithful this time, but there are rumors he may be freelancing for the entire month of december.  i may never be complete.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11696,2100,'2001-11-29 02:25:14',0,'Bret Easton Ellis','Moebius, you are a <i>true</i> man. rock on witcha\' baad self.<br><br>\r\na haiku for the occasion:<br><br>\r\ndeath comes slow, among<br>\r\nthe blood-pulpy forms, i love<br>\r\neroticism!<br>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11697,2096,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','<p align=center>\r\nwho do you love?<br><br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p align=center>\r\n<img src=\"http://www.phans.com/pictures/022.jpg\">',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.76','en'),(11698,2096,'2001-11-29 02:56:52',0,'critic','i [heart] new york!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11699,2100,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Crossfading:<br><br>\r\non the decks<br>\r\neight beats to the measure<br>\r\nseven to one hi-hat<br>\r\ndownbeat<br>\r\nsyncopated snares<br>\r\nenveloped sustain<br>\r\nthe magnificent mix<br>\r\ntwo records harmonize<br>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11700,2096,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I love vaccuum solo\\\'s!<br><br>FISHMAN HIT ON ME.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.72.69','en'),(11701,2096,'2001-11-29 03:17:15',0,'trey anastasio','\\\"my favourite tune is \\\'squirming coil\\\'...\\\"',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11702,2096,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','You Enjoy Myself has always held a special place in my heart.  Due, in part, to road trip antics on several occasions.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.72.69','en'),(11703,2099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','nice picture.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11704,2096,'2001-11-29 12:04:35',0,'jim','he wrote the fucking book, man.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11705,2099,'2001-11-29 15:22:12',0,'flyboy','ah, drew\\\'s nanowrimo, my nanowrimo feels your pain. 14, 000 and something. bah.',2,0,0,0,'195.40.198.187','en'),(11706,2102,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','also, not much of the novel was fiction. i mean, it was fiction, but it was based on real things people told me or did or i knew about. do all writers do that? fiction writers? i think fiction is boring to write but i always read only fiction. howcome? if i wrote only fiction, would i always read autobiographies? help.',0,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11707,2102,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think fiction is fun to <i>read</i> because its <i>someone else\'s fiction</i>, a new story. having to transcribe your own story in logical paragraphs becomes tedious after the fun of thinking up the new story passes, and i think thats what makes fiction writing grating, at least for me.  i like writing true-to-life narratives because its like you\'re telling someone else a story, and that sharing vibe can motivate you through to getting it all down on paper. my only real peeve now is the act of revision; proofreading, editing out some beautiful lines just because they dont work, etc... i think i\'m going to start keeping a word file of all the pretty phrases and run-ons that i wrote (and subsequently fell in love with) but had to abandon for readability...<br><br>\r\nthe heart-falling-out dream is very interesting. i frequently have dreams where anatomical parts or organs fall out, slip away or otherwise disappear. fingernails, etc. the organ dreams are interesting, because i somehow have definite, recurring visualizations of certain organs, when i have no idea what they really look like in real life.  the teeth-falling-out/shattering dream must be a favourite of my subconscious, as i have that one quite often. i also have alot of dreams where i casually observe my own death. theyre not traumatic, just weird little slow-motion vignettes; quite pensive and thought-provoking, actually, sometimes even humourous and entertaining, although theyre fleeting as i usually forget them quickly as i wake. id write them into a nice story... if it weren\'t so damn tedious.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11708,2102,'2001-11-29 16:40:15',0,'hool','<p>i write fiction.  i don\\\'t find it boring. i find it difficult, but i\\\'m glad to do it.  i purposely avoid reading fiction, because i find myself affected by it too much; i sponge it into how and what i\\\'m writing.  i haven\\\'t read a fictional novel in about four years.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i could see how you can get bored writing under the gun of nanowrimo.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m sure that like it or not, everyone draws on their experiences to some degree when writing.  i try not to base any fiction i write on true stories - i don\\\'t find it inspiring, because someone else already lived the experience. creating a new experience is the only motivation strong enough to make me sit down in one spot long enough to barf out a complete story.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and its always best to beat emabarassment to the punch.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the part about capturing the centipede, the association with it, i identified a lot. it was good</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11709,564,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin today:','<PRE>\r\nrobotec9: i think three col is stretching our realestate\r\nrobotec9: but whatever\r\nrobotec9: i am not here to care\r\n1obotec9: i am here to DO\r\nrobotec9: AUSTIN SWINNEY IS A DOER\r\nrobotec9: NOT A THINKER\r\nrobotec9: DO DO DO DO DO DO DO DO DO DO \r\n</PRE>',1,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11710,2101,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i have few cans in my house.  people around me use heaps of cans and bottles, because every week the two enormous recycling bins in the alley are full to the brim so that sometimes i have to leave my cans in a paper bag on top of it.  i wonder, do the recycling collection men get annoyed by the presence of a paper bag in a restrictred can and glass zone?  do they throw the bag in there with the cans?  who sorts the cans and glass from un-recyclable stuff - people or machines?  is it really helping?  i feel good about recycling, though it is an ignorant kind of satisfaction.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11711,2102,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Well, they don\\\'t say \\\"write about what you know\\\" for nothing. It\\\'s easier to create fiction that\\\'s affecting and realistic when it\\\'s based on personal experience. As a result, it\\\'s not uncommon for writers\\\' first novels to be <u>very</u> autobiographical--and often they\\\'re some variant of the \\\"child\\\'s growth to maturity\\\" story--a genere so common that they came up with a term for it the 19th century: the <a href=\\\"http://www.ksu.edu/english/naomiw/classes/545/Bildungsroman.html\\\">bildungsroman.</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>My only trouble with heavily autobiographical fiction is that I get distracted by thoughts of \\\"did this really happen? did that really happen?\\\" These thoughts are annoying. In the long run I tend to be more awestruck by fiction writers who can make amazing stories up without having to experience them firsthand.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My problem as a writer is that for whatever reason my short and long term memory <u>sucks</u>. My memory\\\'s more of the \\\"random access\\\" variety. So whatever autobio stuff I write tends to have elements of fiction to it, anyway. I try to write what I know, but I don\\\'t know what I know. Sigh.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11712,2102,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','Hemingway wrote autobiographical fiction. He mnwent back and forth between real life and his imagination at will. Just tell a good story.',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.85','en'),(11713,2102,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','ps- i\\\'m cradleing my limp penis. ',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.85','en'),(11714,2101,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','it\\\'s all fun and games and angst until the blood starts coming up.',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.85','en'),(11715,2102,'2001-11-29 21:25:05',0,'wb','you haven\\\'t read a novel in 4 years!?? huzzah. this seems like a musician saying they haven\\\'t listened to music in 4 years. hard to believe.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11716,2103,'2001-11-29 21:32:20',0,'[wanted]','dude...you are breaking my heart.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.247','en'),(11717,2103,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im spinning at this party tonight; how badly do i want a shirt that says \\\"kill silence\\\"? ahh!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11718,2101,'2001-11-29 21:37:20',0,'lost and thinking','smoky people scare me sometimes. old smoky people sepcially. they stand and look at you with the corner of their eyes. yellowed fingers and nails. smoke scars. the wrinkles dripping off their face. it is not the act of smoking that bothers me....it the after effects. that smell that clings to your hair and your clothes. when you don\\\'t smoke this thing chokes you sometimes when you least expect. usually at that moment of wanting to hug one your smoky friends. come to me they say. and you are enveloped in ages of nicotine and toxic sweat. do you know you are slowly killing the good things? but then again...if you are puffing on the good havana cigar that\\\'s different. that\\\'s a smell i crave.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.247','en'),(11719,2103,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','to go off on a tangent, i made a shirt for my dj roommate with an old iron-on i found that says i (heart) to spin. and coming off the top of the i in spin is a little guy breakdancing (spinning on his arm).',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11720,2103,'2001-11-29 22:48:53',0,'ssj3gostu','i really liked this. that picture is great.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.179','en'),(11721,2102,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah, that is sort of odd, imo.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11722,2102,'2001-11-29 23:41:30',0,'hool','<p>to wb: it may seem like a musician saying they haven\\\'t listened to music in four years, but it\\\'s an improper analogy.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you\\\'re saying the musician would listen to _no_ music for four years, and by analogy, i would read nothing for four years.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i read. i read lots of non fiction.  i read short stories here and there.  i just choose to not read fiction novels.  i like them very much, but i like imagining my own stories even more, and then writing them down, and it\\\'s easier to do that if i don\\\'t read fiction novels.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>not so hard to believe.</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11723,2099,'2001-11-30 00:04:24',0,'jo','19,390.<BR>\r\ni\\\'m still sort of thinking i can do it. what\\\'s 30k in 30 hours? totally do-able.<BR>',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11724,2102,'2001-11-30 00:13:29',0,'ty','i really don\\\'t see the difference between reading fiction and nonfiction. when you read fiction, you suspend your disbelief anyway (if it\\\'s any good). in fact, the more it reads like nonfiction, the better in my opinion. i would think that to be a good fiction writer you would have a love of, ummm, fiction and would want to surround yourself with it. ',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.144','en'),(11725,2102,'2001-11-30 00:24:53',0,'wb','ok i agree its not like saying a musican listens to no music but it might be like a musician listening to none of the music he or she likes. ie. i like jazz but i don\\\'t listen to it for 4 years because i don\\\'t want my solo to sound like charlie parker. or a painter only letting themself look at cartoons/illustrations in the newspaper and not going to any galleries or museums. <BR><BR>is there some artist or writer who did this who gave you this idea? because i have only heard of it when i was in college and some guy proudly told the class about how he refused to read books for the same reason. i think he was a sci fi writer. <BR><BR>if you showed your story to somebody and they said: oh, this is like so and so novel, wouldn\\\'t you be at all curious to read it? don\\\'t you have any writers you truly love that you want to read their latest book? don\\\'t people say to you: oh you have to read this book, you\\\'d love it? are you just so stoic?',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11726,2102,'2001-11-30 00:38:47',0,'hool','tyler: \r\n\r\n<p>suspension of disbelief is one (not the only) defining line between reading fiction and non-fiction.  when i\\\'m reading non-fiction, say, an essay that\\\'s trying to convince me of some theory, suspension of disbelief should not happen.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think it\\\'s absolutely false to assume that a precondition for good fiction writing is consuming large amounts fiction of other writers. why would you say that?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m not saying i\\\'m a good or bad fiction writer.  who are you to say that the most important expression of love for fiction is consuming it? i love stories.  i express it by writing them instead of reading them.  why is that so hard to accept?</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11727,2102,'2001-11-30 01:03:17',0,'hool','wb: \r\n\r\n<p>i have heard of some artists / musicians who don\\\'t consume anything in their domain, interested only in creating.  if you want specific names, i\\\'ll have to do a bit of research.  but nobody gave me this idea that i remember.  i\\\'ve always been this way, and it\\\'s not limited to writing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i know of a few professional athletes who don\\\'t watch other games in their respective sports.  they only care about playing.  i\\\'m the same way. i find it hard to sit and watch an NBA game because i\\\'d much rather be playing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i don\\\'t push novels away with disgust.  there are many, many writers out there who no doubt write much better than i do, and i have loved reading their work.  but like i\\\'ve already said, i love writing my own even more.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve had many reccomendations of novels made to me after someone has read a short story of mine.  of course i\\\'m curious, but not enough to read it. there are no fiction writers out there that i love enough to have to read their latest book.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>reading is about experiencing something - a good novel makes you feel like you\\\'re right there, in the story.  i find that that experience is great when reading, but even better when i\\\'m creating the experience with my own daydreams and my own words - i find myself even more immersed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it has nothing to do with stoicism, or ego, or elitism.  it\\\'s not a matter of comparison, of \\\"i can write better than anyone else\\\". i can\\\'t. (i always keep in mind the words of Homer - no matter how good you think you are at something, there are about a million people who are better than you). i will again read fiction novels at some point in my life.  but for now, i enjoy fiction more in the long run when i\\\'m writing instead of reading.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11728,2102,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I have an idea of where hool\\\'s coming from. I\\\'ve heard a few established writers talk about this, too--when they\\\'re writing, they avoid reading other books, for fear of \\\"corrupting\\\" their own words with the ideas, styles, conceits of other writers. That\\\'s one side of the argument.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The other side is that it can be very demoralizing when you come up with a brilliant idea and then read a great piece by another writer that seems to impinge or even steal that idea. Some writers avoid reading other people\\\'s works when they write simply to avoid that kind of conflict.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>My story along those lines: Awhile back I was working on the script for a graphic novella (okay, <i>comic book</i>) that a friend was going to illustrate. It was to be about a young woman\\\'s adventures in a fabulous, immensely huge sentient city that was equal parts magic and science, madness and lucidity. Now, I knew that sort of thing had kind of been done before but I was able to put it aside. But when Vertigo Comics came out with 2-3 titles along the same lines (one of \\\'em being Warren Ellis\\\' <i><a href=\\\"http://www.transmetropolitan.com/\\\">Transmetropolitan</a></i>) I was so discouraged I pretty much gave up on the project. In retrospect if I\\\'d just quit paying attention to comics during that period perhaps I would\\\'ve gotten through to the end of the first script.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11729,2103,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i saw a bumper sticker once that said \\\"I hope you die before you become a dj.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11730,2102,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i havent managed to get through the debate, but le, there is promise in your prose. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11731,2099,'2001-11-30 01:34:14',0,'7:30 PM','22,008',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11732,2103,'2001-11-30 02:38:58',0,'earl','wake up and take those whte socks off<br>\r\n[X]best friendly <br>\r\n[X]nice supportive',1,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(11733,2103,'2001-11-30 04:06:18',0,'DJ Dork','see, theres the \\\"cool\\\" and then there\\\'s the \\\"cooler\\\" that choose to diss them with their \\\"cool\\\" bumper stickers and such. some might call these people \\\"playa\\\' hata\\\'s\\\". yucky.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11734,2102,'2001-11-30 04:39:01',0,'joyrider','<p>it\\\'s rad you even attempted to \\\'wrimo.  the idea was great and wound itself up in my head for a few days, but the more i thought about it the more intimidated i became. so i bolted and came up with a dozen reasons not to be a \\\'wrimo.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>these days i have just enough of an attention span to write bad freeverse and do journal anecdotes and email.  i have this recurring nightmare where a giant Reader\\\'s Digest eats my head.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>egg says your prose has promise - listen to her and do it again on your terms, not the \\\'wrimo\\\'s.  whatever works!</p>',2,0,0,0,'134.197.14.163','en'),(11735,2103,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i totally hate bumper stickers.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11736,2099,'2001-11-30 05:36:28',0,'11:35 PM','have stopped caring. petting kitten. listening to death cab for cutie. reading vonnegut.',0,0,0,0,'172.174.238.132','en'),(11737,2099,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','peter: i haven\\\'t read the article yet, but it just made me really happy to see a photo of the front door to your apartment.',1,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11738,2102,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i really like le\\\'s writing. even the stuff she thinks is no good. huzzah!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11739,2103,'2001-11-30 13:22:51',0,'dj1luna','<img src=\\\"http://www.achtungbaby.org/post/djisgod.jpg\\\">',1,0,0,0,'207.8.101.10','en'),(11740,2102,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','what weeb & joyrider & egg & countless others have said. I\\\'ve always adored le\\\'s stuff. if it wasn\\\'t for my interest in her writing I probably wouldn\\\'t have bothered with swinney.org in the first place. And I\\\'m happy I did. Cheers to all the writers here!',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11741,2103,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i am a deejay in the privacy of my own home.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11742,2102,'2001-11-30 18:48:01',0,'brook','A swinney lurker (someday I\\\'ll contribute I hope) delurking here. le, your stuff here and elsewhere is some of my favorite writing on the web. Whatever you do, saintdotnetty or fiction or otherwise, please don\\\'t stop writing. ',2,0,0,0,'63.193.246.9','en'),(11743,2103,'2001-11-30 20:21:24',0,'bmg','i spin quarters and paper on the bar.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(11744,2103,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i am a deejay for the pleasure of my \r\nhalf-way house neighbors</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11745,1826,'2001-11-30 21:01:51',0,'raul','Yeah, Alanna, damn the richies for not buying more things so you can feel better.',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.188','en'),(11746,2106,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','thank you for posting this. articles like this are the reason i\\\'m here.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(11747,2106,'2001-11-30 21:16:46',0,'k','my breasts are full of passion. lactating. with no release it goes sour. i ache to have someone suck me dry...so that i can fill back up again. it is endless. the milk. poisonous ambrosia. best when shared.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.250','en'),(11748,2107,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','thank you for posting this. articles like this are the reason i\\\'m here.',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.182','en'),(11749,2107,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i used to cook a lot more in college.  now i order pizza or thai food every night.  it seemed like a priviledge for a while, but it\\\'s gotten old real quick.  delivery can\\\'t replace 65 cent homemade tacos. </p>\r\n<p>oh man those costa rican worchestershire sauce black beans...mmmm</p> ',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11750,2101,'2001-11-30 22:08:07',0,'truth','cancer can has been taken hostage by the cigarette smoker. without free will, it is enslaved. but the cancer can won\\\'t get a voice box. cancer can isn\\\'t damaging the air we breath. if cancer can thinks there is anything noble on being subjecated by an ignorant ruler then cancer can is stupid. cancer can should be freed.',0,0,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(11751,2101,'2001-11-30 22:17:57',0,'iamnotignorant','',0,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11752,2108,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','big wobbly penis? heh',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11753,2108,'2001-11-30 23:03:51',0,'huge_wobbly_penis','no no. -huge-. wobbly penis.',1,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(11754,2106,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Great piece. Making the move to cohabitate with a lover--especially if it\\\'s the first time you\\\'ve ever done such a thing, or if you\\\'ve lived alone for a long time--can be really fucking scary. After my gal and I moved in together I was out of sorts for quite some time. Now it\\\'s three years later and things have been at some sort of equilibrium for most of that time. But there\\\'s still this small part of me that howls to the moon, screaming \\\"revolution now!\\\" every once in awhile.',2,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11755,2108,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','oh sorry, my bad. <blink>HUGE</blink>',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11756,2108,'2001-12-01 00:40:15',0,'huge_wobbly_penis',':D',0,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(11757,2084,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the dead kennedys:  viva las vegas.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ngenius.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11758,2102,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hi, you guys are really nice. i\\\'ve just walked in the door from a 25 hour journey home from australia. i am eating stale cocoa puffs. i don\\\'t think i will ever stop writing. i have tried but it never works.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.200.9','en'),(11759,2107,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','hey austin, sorry about opening your almond milk in the fridge.  i couldn\\\'t control myself.  it made the morning tea bearable.  slept on the living room floor and in the middle of the night i found myself reaching for the piano and break front which weren\\\'t there.  the things i bumped into so many times growing up... getting a drink of water in the middle of the night and stubbing my toe on them.  oh god, the snails.  the snails.  sometimes they crawl up the glass on the front door and say hello.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11760,2107,'2001-12-01 07:27:05',0,'coricidin','snails, snails, snails. my daddy used to try to kill them with beer.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11761,2102,'2001-12-01 07:31:49',0,'jo','ditto on both counts.<BR>\r\ni bought <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1585420093/\\\">the right to write</a> at the harvard book store and was annoyed by how perky she was about writing. the next day, in a different bookstore, i noticed a book called writing in the margin put out by the <a href=\\\"http://www.uglyducklingpresse.org/loudmouth/loudmouth1.htm\\\">loudmouth collective</a> and the first words of the book were: \\\"i\\\'m not sure i like writing.\\\"<BR>\r\ni bought it immediately and devoured it and wrote 4 thousand words in one sitting.',0,0,0,0,'172.135.71.48','en'),(11762,2106,'2001-12-01 07:32:55',0,'critic','this <i>is</i> a great piece. but, it troubles me. i cant help but feel this vibe, as if things were needlessly put to the test, anger made so that it could lash out, then make up, seeking proof or some kind of map of what intangibles exist. i get that feeling because ive often insinuated those feelings to the peace of the resolve thats sure to follow, the balancing sexual experiences, and the inevitable draw back together that never fails to result, time and time again.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11763,2107,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i <i>love</i> the statement \"i put the new tea kettle on that i bought because i fell asleep\", or at least, thats how i first read that line. i was like, \"woah. <i>profound</i>!\"',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11764,2102,'2001-12-01 07:41:48',0,'gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nmy friend pete used to work at the havard book store! one day natalie portman came in and he worked the register for her, and because of that i will hate him forever.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut that really doesn\\\'t have anything to do with anything :)</P>',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(11765,2109,'2001-12-01 15:28:27',0,'trouble','you\\\'re a good man charlie brown.',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(11766,2109,'2001-12-01 17:14:00',0,'bmg','hey, that was the same full moon that i saw - i love it when i make that connection.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(11767,2109,'2001-12-01 19:15:05',0,'jo','here\\\'s another one: my menstrual cycle is perfectly in time with the full moon. so you can rest assured that everytime you see a full moon, somwhere i am bleeding. isn\\\'t that romantic?',0,0,0,0,'172.149.147.56','en'),(11768,2109,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','mine usually is too.  but this month, it\\\'s hiding.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11769,2109,'2001-12-01 21:23:29',0,'it','might not be hiding. you might be getting some company in about 8 months:D',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.140','en'),(11770,2102,'2001-12-01 21:33:40',0,'finagle','pork-fried bells go:<br>ding-ding-ding,<br> ring-a-ling-ling<br> as i sing sing sing',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11771,2109,'2001-12-01 21:43:31',0,'that','is not the case.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11772,2109,'2001-12-02 00:21:26',0,'you','would have to name him s6, or o2.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11773,2109,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','eek you better watch out, that baby virus is going around!',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11774,2109,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','um, hi.  excuse me?  yeah.  i\\\'m not up the duff.  thanks.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11775,2110,'2001-12-02 02:08:47',0,'critic','yep. swinneys like crack. once hooked, it keeps you coming back. poo on those who swear to never come back. posting here #1.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11776,1644,'2001-12-02 02:10:48',0,'critic','reading this, pausing to think. ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11777,2110,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i\\\'ve considered being a cab driver.',3,0,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(11778,2107,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to xep','xeps brother david whom i live with now that xepsmom has turned the house over to the cubs, david walks out to the garage bare foot and steps on the snails and says, \\\"snail.\\\"  that is all he does.  i would fall over and start vomiting if i stepped on a snail barefoot.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nin college my friend JJ coined an expression that was used to describe someone\\\'s face when it was contorted in anxiety/horror/disgust.  he would say, \\\"that dude looks like he just stepped on a snail.\\\"',3,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11779,2110,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i think in any online community there are people who swear never to come back. if they\\\'ve been in the community for a while and liked it a lot and made good friends, swearing never to come back in usually a big lie meant to get attention. the term for this is \\\"tiny suicide.\\\" i have done this before. i tend to do it less as i get older. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni just got back to san francisco and despite my plans to go away soon, i do still like it here very much. i think of leaving as a \\\"break\\\" rather than a final thing. and i don\\\'t think i could go anywhere that was so far away that visiting often would be a pain. san francisco is sort of like my first girlfriend. i love her, but i never fucked anyone else and i want to see what it\\\'s like. especially since she\\\'s not putting out with the jobs right now.\r\n<br><br>\r\ngood luck with job hunts, mister trouble. it seems to be getting better.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.211.41','en'),(11780,2107,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','when i was a little kid i stepped on a banana slug barefoot.  i believe shrieking ensued.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11781,2107,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i miss garages and snails and walking around bare-footed. *sigh*</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11782,2110,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to s5','<br><tt>i think you would be the coolest cab driver ever. a little over-qualified, of course, but tremendously cool. my cabbie tonight got pissed off at me for making him drive me uptown. ack. he was not cool.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11783,2114,'2001-12-02 14:49:02',0,'Joanna','It <B>will</B> all be alright. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe fact that there are people like you who are brave enough to speak up to total strangers gives me hope. ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(11784,2114,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','from your description it does not sound like a pure case of hatred.  it sounds more like a case of bad brain chemicals.  i do not mean to say that\r\nis ever any kind of justification but perhaps give \r\nthe person had no control.  i am naive also and\r\ntry to always give people the benefit of the doubt.  also, racial hatred is so absolutely confusing to me that i cannot believe any sane\r\nperson would act that way.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11785,2113,'2001-12-02 19:40:11',0,'sumpin\\\' sumpin\\\'','Ommelette in the oven, wow!\r\n\r\nCisco dodgeball, do me, now!\r\n\r\nTatoos hurt, make me say, ow!\r\n\r\nNot all that moos is a, cow!',1,0,0,0,'32.100.70.196','en'),(11786,2107,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','you\\\'re padding around the sink washing dishes with your house socks on. the foot, it sinks and slips on something. you pause to comtemplate what just happened. snail? eyeball? terror comes of foot blindness. a cold front slowly approaches through your sock. as you say to yourself- oh shit, what did i step in- AEIII it gets even more coldcoldslimy. it invades the interior of your sock. it has penetrated your warm sock defenses. \r\n<br><br>\r\nyou frantically try to decipher the mess under your foot-- it\\\'s pale? wet? grainy??? snails aren\\\'t grainy... and let out a sigh of relief that it\\\'s an errant cube of tofu from dinner. whew. true story. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11787,1861,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','just wanted to point out: i love it when someone posts something infantile and worthless, and then complains about the quality of the responses he gets. what _are_ we supposed to respond with? shakespeare? oh. right. when we step in dogshit, i\'m sure we spout poetry.',6,0,0,1,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11788,2113,'2001-12-02 20:43:26',0,'\\\'nuf said','\\\"When there\\\'s too much money around, it gets frighteningly easy to occupy yourself with inconsequential trash.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.228','en'),(11789,2113,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hey, i\'m moving to oregon out of a jones for cheap rent! also, for reed college, the weather, the slightly grumpy upscale hippies, the brick buildings, and last but not least, the <a href=\"http://www.suicidegirls.com\">hot girls</a>.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(11790,2110,'2001-12-02 22:02:10',0,'\\\"The cabbie\\\" by treetop.','<p>In October, after a Widespread Panic concert in Berkeley, we decided to\r\nwalk around campus. At one point we smoked a bowl at a sundial by\r\na big tower. Shortly after we got in a taxi to go to our hotel. The cabbie\r\nwas surprised to find his cab full of hippies from Georgia and Alabama\r\nreeking of grass so he asked us if we would smoke with him. We said hell\r\nyea. At this point I noticed that my bowl was missing. I really wanted\r\nto find it. We smoked out the driver and he turned off the meter. So now\r\nthe cabbie is super blazed and wandering all over Berkeley\\\'s campus looking for \\\"The\r\nbig tower with a sundial!\\\" He was driving down one ways and running over\r\nplastic barriers. Surprisingly we found the tower. I ran up to the sun\r\ndial, but my bowl was gone. I thought it was gone forever. I walk back\r\nto the car and one of my friends says he just found it in his pocket! YAY!\r\nI think that he was the coolest cab driver I\\\'ve ever had. He drove us around\r\nfor about 45 minutes with no meter.\r\n<center><img SRC=\\\"http://members.aol.com/trentwall/satisfied.jpg\\\" NOSAVE height=165 width=250></center>\r\nThe scariest cabbie I ever had was this HUGE black guy eating a bucket\r\nof KFC that was sitting in his lap while listening to \\\"Ride the white pony\\\"\r\ndriving a 87 Impala about 70 miles an hour through New Orleans. That guy\r\nwas out of control!\r\n</body>\r\n</html>',0,0,0,0,'205.188.199.34','en'),(11791,1826,'2001-12-02 22:47:06',0,'alanna','<P>\r\num, who said you had to be rich to buy stock?\r\n</P>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'198.182.157.104','en'),(11792,1374,'2001-12-02 22:57:43',0,'alanna','^---  \\\'nuff said.',6,0,0,0,'198.182.157.104','en'),(11793,2109,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','that must be hard, asking somebody else for money.  i couldn\\\'t do it.  and think about all the people you see every day who are reduced to doing that to survive, what their pride had to go through first to get to that point where they could do that.  you strike me as being a standup guy.',1,0,0,0,'209.208.28.193','en'),(11794,2102,'2001-12-02 23:05:32',0,'alanna','\\\"i don\\\'t think i will ever stop writing. i have tried but it never works.\\\"  goodness, do i sympathize with that.  and, um, artists boycott similar art all the time.  look at mister francis bacon.  well, at least the first part of his painting career.  self-taught interior designer.  i would have liked for him to design my interior.',4,0,0,0,'198.182.157.104','en'),(11795,2110,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Well, I\\\'ve observed that in San Francisco these days the cab companies are hiring drivers--just at the moment that everyone else is eliminating their open positions. But the reason for this--according to the <a href=\\\"http://www.sfgate.com/columnists/cabbie/archive/\\\">Night Cabbie</a>--is that cab drivers can\\\'t make enough money to live on anymore, so they\\\'re quitting in droves. This is a result of several things: the decline in tourism (which began before 9/11), the dot.com bust (who has money for cabs anymore?), and the fact that several more cabs were added to the streets earlier this year (less fares to go around). So even though cabdriving seems like a romantic avocation, it sounds like more stress than it\\\'s worth these days.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The Night Cabbie\\\'s biweekly SF Chronicle column is great, by the way. He\\\'s a 50something ex-stockbroker who drives the 10pm-8am beat, and he has an endless set of stories to tell. If you read all of his columns from 1996 onwards you\\\'ll come away with a gritty, greasy shadow history of SF\\\'s past five years.</p>',1,0,0,0,'205.186.177.209','en'),(11796,2114,'2002-04-20 20:49:51',273,'Quinn','chemichals-schemicals...it\\\'s still scary.  ',0,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(11797,2114,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this weekend i tormented my brain chemicals to their most insane boundaries, and nothing like that ever crossed my mind; never has. hmm. although the physical act of the outburst itself is probably attributable to chemistry, the opinions definitely arent. i\'d have been 1000x more likely to stand up and rant about roast beef than \"jews\". ugh. but i digress. chemicals influence actions and thought processes, but opinions and views, ranging from the abstract to the intolerant, generally spawn from deeper sources, like circumstance, experience, and the way you were/weren\'t raised. just my 2 cents. not meant to stir the waters.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11798,2116,'2001-12-03 14:21:53',0,'critic','before i agitate anyone, let me say that im sure this is good writing. im just personally not fond of the story, it makes me feel uneasy. it reminds me of <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=griece\\\">griece</a>. its going to give me bad dreams.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11799,2116,'2001-12-03 14:23:18',0,'Beelzebubba','you said \"fuck\" nine times. i love it!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11800,2106,'2001-12-03 17:01:41',0,'erb to critic','<p>certainly, irrational lashing-out is not the healthiest way to deal with issues.  but in a good relationship, irrational behavior (even acting-out by immature, underdeveloped, afraid parts of the self) is allowed to exist, with the understanding that growth is the overall goal, and that balance will gradually be reached.  the boy is good, in that he doesn\\\'t take these things on as reflections on his ability or inablitity as a lover; he just observes and accompanies and comforts when he can.  distance obviously a necessary tool when confronting long-hidden pain.  i appreciate the comment though, and will take the opportunity to clarify that the entry does not condone the use of anger as a weapon or coercive compliance-gaining strategy.  i did not \\\"create\\\" the anger in order to have release, but recognized the irrational, strong feelings i was experiencing as a need for expression (rather than as an indication that someone else had wronged me in some way, etc.).  i guess that\\\'s why it felt like a discovery of truth.</p>\r\n<p>does that make sense?</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11801,2112,'2001-12-03 17:55:33',0,'who','was the rock star you stalked?',2,0,0,0,'209.208.77.132','en'),(11802,2106,'2001-12-03 18:06:59',0,'critic','of course! my spin on it was probably very well colored by how i would define it if it were i that was involved.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11803,2118,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i see an article by egg, i immediately know that i\\\'m going to be in for a substantially meaty read, long enough to be interesting without being too long, and filled with enough content to keep my attention. i like this.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11804,2118,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i woke up at jessica\\\'s on sunday morning, looked over at her and said, i just dreamt that a horse was in your apartment and it suddenly had a heart attack right there in your living room.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11805,2114,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','solution: lobotomies for all.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11806,2119,'2001-12-03 18:52:16',0,'erb!','this is a song i wrote in a dream:<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve got big plans for next year and that\\\'s a fact<br>\r\ni can\\\'t think of everything but i\\\'m not looking back<br>\r\ni\\\'m gonna hop onto the wing of a big old rickety plane<br>\r\ni\\\'m gonna fly across the sea and find my given name<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve got big plans for next year<br>\r\nbig big plans<br>\r\n<br>\r\nmy girlfriend\\\'s got some flowers in her bed today<br>\r\nthey\\\'re dried and dyed but she waters them anyway<br>\r\nwe watch the darkened tv screen for signs of life<br>\r\nhuddled underneath a witches\\\' blanket with a knife<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve got big plans for next year<br>\r\nbig big plans<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthe mannequin is on the lawn again<br>\r\nshe\\\'s stuffed full and aging visibly<br>\r\nshe\\\'s jerking like an angry puppet<br>\r\nshe\\\'s alive, she\\\'s alive, i know it<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve got big plans for next year<br>\r\ni\\\'ve got big plans for next year<br>\r\n(continue to fade)<br>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11807,2120,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i took a career/vocation test in high school too, except there were hundreds of questions and only like 5 possibilities at the end.  i can\\\'t remember what they were.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11808,2118,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>the night before last i dreamt that one of our senior programmers leapt off the roof of universal studios onto a trampolene, and then bounced so high that he missed it upon coming back down and landed on the cement.  i thought he was dead, but he got up and was fine.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11809,2114,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','It\\\'s so hard to know what to do in these situations: play it safe (and live with the guilt that you never stood up to the offenders)or take a stand and put your ass on the line? Either way you walk away feeling awful, and neither choice necessarily will keep you from the path of physical harm.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Abuse and prejudice on a city bus happened to me once too, but the hatred was of a different sort--homophobic gaybashing. It was New Years Day, and my good friend Chip and I were riding the 33 Stanyan from the Haight to the Mission. A few blocks into the ride, a group of five teenage boys started tormenting the other passengers, shouting slurs, throwing trash, and eventually physically shoving people around. Chip and I received a lot of their crap, perhaps because we were sitting together (nevermind that these boys started out as a tight little cluster in the back seat). People screamed at the driver, pleaded for him to do something. \\\"What can I do?\\\" he shouted back, and kept driving through the twisted streets of Twin Peaks. Practically everyone got off the bus at the next stop. Nobody really stood up to them. It was awful.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That happened eight years ago. And for long afterwards I harbored fantasies of standing up to those kids, of doing serious bodily harm to them, whatever it would take to get my dignity back. It\\\'s only recently that I can think about it and look at it somewhat objectively.</p>\r\n<p>So, Quinn--good luck. Congratulations for having the courage to at least take a stand.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11810,2118,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Oh, lordy: \\\"What\\\'s the rumpus?\\\" I found that phrase in an old story I wrote years ago and couldn\\\'t remember for the life of me where I lifted it from. Thanks, Egg!',0,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11811,2119,'2001-12-03 19:15:49',0,'will','<p>Only the sane ask themselves if they\\\'re insane.\r\n</p><p>\r\nOnly the young ask themselves if they\\\'re growing older. </p>',4,0,0,0,'198.214.178.119','en'),(11812,2120,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<p>Mine told me that I would be most well suited for a career as an army enlisted man, not even an officer...</p>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.18.20','en'),(11813,2120,'2001-12-03 20:23:45',0,'jo','GOD i would make SUCH a good archivist.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11814,2118,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i think that all movies and television are like dreams, because we have seen so much tv, that we dream like tv. i have camera angles in my dreams. i don\\\'t think it would be like that if hadn\\\'t watched a lot of movies.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.215.31','en'),(11815,2119,'2001-12-03 20:33:17',0,'critic','i dont get it. foie gras? what about foie gras?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11816,2118,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','i\\\'m surprised my dreams aren\\\'t like video games.  they aren\\\'t like tv either.  they are like ... dreams.  heh.',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11817,2119,'2001-12-03 20:48:30',0,'Pandora','Last night I got sucked into yet another episode of The Iron Chef. The challenger prepared one of his dishes with Foie Gras, which is liver from geese that have been specifically fattened to the limit of their internal capacity.  One of my roommates was watching the show with me and asked me if I knew what Foie Gras was. I said, \\\"Of course\\\". That\\\'s when I started to feel a little old.',1,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(11818,2112,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','it wasn\\\'t quite <I>stalking</I>.  i got his number off the producer\\\'s computerized filofax thingy.  just called him up drunk in the middle of the night a couple of times.  only thing i remember about the conversations was that i told him he had a nice voice.<BR><BR>i was living in georgia at the time if that narrows it down for you any.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(11819,2113,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i read this.  it was long, but i read it.  it brought back to me the time when i too had a demanding, ever-flirtatious hippie girlfriend.  we split apart when i moved to oregon for a college and she moved to austin, TX for a university.</p>\r\n<P>  i guess in a way i owe it her that i even know mr. swinney.  </p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11820,2120,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','you should act.  you\\\'d do well.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(11821,2118,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','wow. egg constantly humbles me with her swinntries.  sometimes i forget that there are people in the world who are smarter than i am.<BR><BR>if we could only make money doing this....',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(11822,2120,'2001-12-03 21:21:27',0,'ryan','i took a test like that in high school as well.  it didn\\\'t ouput nearly the quanity of choices, probably about three or four.  the number one choice i remember quite clearly:  bricklayer.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.49.65','en'),(11823,2120,'2001-12-03 21:32:57',0,'stella to le','I recommend sock puppets. They\\\'re fun!',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(11824,2102,'2002-04-20 20:49:51',273,'Quinn','g-zuz, i just couldn\\\'t put myself under the gun like that (a novel in a month).  too dificult for me to get at a novel in a year.  say: I\\\'ve been working on a novel for a year (off and on, mostly off) and realized that it was going the wrong direction and could only salvage some scenes here and there.  i turn to poems & stories for quick blurts of writing, but the novel is again on hold. but yr. work is admirable....pretty good stuff to begin with (not that my word holds much water).  but it reads like good writing should: like it was EZ. ',1,0,0,0,'198.185.18.207','en'),(11825,2120,'2001-12-03 21:41:02',0,'schooly d','i love school-- loans, neitzsche, drinking dew in the \\\'quad\\\' ah...',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11826,2119,'2001-12-03 21:43:07',0,'foreigner 1984','that was yesterday<br>\r\nand today life moves on<br>\r\nyou won\\\'t find me in yesterday\\\'s world<br>\r\ncause today lives on...<br>\r\n<br>\r\nooohhh..',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(11827,2118,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to le','<p>maybe we made tv to work out what we see in our dreams.  \\\"I wanna seeee movies of my dreams..\\\"</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11828,2120,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i want to take this funny test right now. i never got to/had to take one in high school. i\\\'m curious what it would say i should do.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11829,2120,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>has anyone watched the episode of \\\"strangers with candy\\\" in which amy sedaris playing a 40 year old in high school takes a career evaluation test and her score adds up to \\\"JUNKY WHORE\\\" and she says, \\\"JUNKY WHORE!\\\" all excited and then frowns. \\\"ive already done that one.\\\" oh god that brings a tear of sentimentality to my eyes.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11830,2120,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I avoided the aptitude test thingy in high school. I didn\\\'t want to know what some computer program thought I ought to do with my life. I also remembered my father telling me how he took an aptitude test after he was well along in his career--metalurgical engineering: he designs copper & nickel mines--and it told him he shoulda been a musician. And nothing about engineering at all. Which is all very strange, because he loves his job and is good at it. (but he also *loves* music and passed that love on to me)',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11831,2120,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','no one ever asked me to take one. and dude, amy, that was really funny.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11832,1227,'2001-12-04 00:04:58',0,'Javorai','y toby es un gordo puto',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.163','en'),(11833,2118,'2001-12-04 00:09:37',0,'Javorai','you think that if I watch enough South Park, I\\\'ll dream in 2-D?',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.163','en'),(11834,2120,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','the average SAT score is something like 800. 1200 is really good.',0,0,0,0,'65.140.48.203','en'),(11835,2120,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','ryan, i think you would be a really great bricklayer. ',4,0,0,0,'165.247.200.234','en'),(11836,2120,'2001-12-04 01:40:41',0,'secretgirlfriend to ryan shaw','you can lay your brick on me anytime. ',1,0,0,0,'63.251.235.41','en'),(11837,2121,'2001-12-04 02:42:55',0,'cynn','i like how i know what that link is without even looking.  rock steady, baby.',0,0,0,0,'216.8.153.7','en'),(11838,2116,'2001-12-04 03:26:17',0,'Javorai','It\\\'d be nice to have more Changs in the world.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.29.64','en'),(11839,2121,'2001-12-04 04:17:45',0,'critic','oh <i>no</i>. please, not mroe 9/11. war, fighting, media... must. escape. tired. topic.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11840,2121,'2001-12-04 04:21:10',0,'critic','...oh, and also: didn\\\'t israel \\\"jump on the bandwagon\\\" due to 25+ dead from suicide bombings that palistine officials did not deny responsibility for? such shallow reaons (note sarcasm). im sorry, i dont condone war any more than the next person, but im getting a little tired out of us panning the worlds events, sitting on our asses, watching it on tv, getting chunky on take out.<br><br>\r\nso, fuzzy, if not war, what\\\'s <b>your</b> solution/symptom treatment?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11841,2116,'2001-12-04 04:23:29',0,'critic','yeah, gotta love them lexus driving ninjas!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11842,2116,'2001-12-04 04:36:24',0,'Javorai','I just want to make something clear: despite the story\\\'s subjective message, there is nothing wrong with having a nice cold one once in a while.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.29.64','en'),(11843,2099,'2001-12-04 04:52:09',0,'Javorai','I can\\\'t wait for next November.  It will be my first nanowrimo.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.29.64','en'),(11844,2121,'2001-12-04 09:17:20',0,'origami','Well, I personally think that all beings on earth should do 5 jumping jacks, simultaniously.  That ought to take the edge off things..',0,0,0,0,'32.100.70.170','en'),(11845,2120,'2001-12-04 12:30:51',0,'bmg','<a href=\\\'http://www.collegeboard.org/sat/cbsenior/yr2000/nat/72-00.html\\\' target=new>you are so humble.</a>',1,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(11846,2118,'2001-12-04 13:40:18',0,'wanna','<p>see faces turn to backs of heads and slowly get smaller.</p>',1,0,0,0,'18.63.1.12','en'),(11847,2118,'2001-12-04 13:45:56',0,'wanna','be mister cholera and kick it too square to survive',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11848,2120,'2001-12-04 13:50:58',0,'szajic','<p>I took one of these tests in high school, too, and it said I should become a FIREMAN.  The second choice was a miner, but silicosis didn\\\'t sound like a lot of fun.  And anyway, my secret profession remains bartender.  But yeah, fireman.  Every since my apartment <a href=\\\"http://web.mit.edu/scz/www/img/western/1.html\\\">caught on fire</a> I\\\'ve had a lot of respect for firemen.  I\\\'ll be out with friends and a fire truck will drive by, sirens wailing, and I\\\'ll give \\\'em a thumbs up and salute or something.  People tend to think I\\\'m being patriotic in these trying times blah blah, but basically I\\\'m just showing a little love for those folks keeping most of my worldly belongings from being converted into CO2, water and soot.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh also.  Fucking SATs.  My little sister\\\'s having to deal with college applications now and my god, I hate standardized testing so much.</p>',3,0,0,0,'18.63.1.12','en'),(11849,2120,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to szajic','yeah stefan. standardized tests are crap. i am a smart person, but the first time i took the sat i got a 700 or something! i had to take a kaplan course to get the score up.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think it\'s really lame that to most colleges, their students are just a set of numbers. a while ago i looked into going to UC berkeley and i realized that the only way i could get in would be to get 1400 on my SAT to negate my 2.4 high school GPA. it doesn\'t matter what i\'ve been doing for the past few years, how many high paying geek jobs i\'ve had, etc.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni guess that\'s just a product of overpopulation, though. too many people for institutions to treat people as individuals.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\'m in college application paperwork hell right now, and i think that rounding up the paperwork to go to college is harder and more tedious than any html job i ever had. i\'m having to fax people for transcripts, call to follow up the fax, call and sit on hold, christmas themed hold music, give my ssn over and over until i start to feel dizzy. and today i just started worrying with the financial aid stuff. oh man. tax forms.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, i just sliced my palm open on a piece of bailing wire that is in one of my dreads. it\'s bleeding.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>hey matt</b>, i just saw your comment where you said i should act. i am actually a really really awful actor. this is something i realized in drama club when i was 17. i think i probably could be good at it, but the concept of acting scares and embrasses me in some really gut level way that i can\'t explain, so i never go the distance. i think i am mostly afraid that i would lose my already tenuous grasp of reality.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.0.226','en'),(11850,2122,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','actually, i kinda *do* want to know who it\\\'s about.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.0.226','en'),(11851,2122,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>me too.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11852,2122,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','is it about the girl who you made us all think is lame in the last entry?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11853,1046,'2001-12-04 17:47:27',0,'chi chi','i miss you lulu',0,0,0,0,'64.168.22.30','en'),(11854,2122,'2001-12-04 18:03:47',0,'p-diddy','i think its all about the benjamins, <i>baby</i>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11855,2120,'2001-12-04 18:04:32',0,'drew','i think \\\"safety-clothing-and-equipment developer\\\" sounds like the way to go.\r\n\r\nstandardized tests: i\\\'ve always done very well at them.  i think i must be very good at learning to think in a standardized way when called upon to do so.  i\\\'ve long suspected that my core competency is elaborate mimicry, and that everything i do is based on it: acting, singing, writing, yes, but also web design and usability and standardized-test-taking.  i spot patterns and learn to reproduce them, though it\\\'s not really a conscious process; i resist methodical thinking and prefer intuitive leaping whenever possible.  i think it\\\'s probably a form of laziness.  \r\n\r\nthis laziness is why i\\\'m horrible at chess; it\\\'s not that i\\\'m incapable of learning the patterns (i assume), but that i find the idea of playing a game that always starts in exactly the same way with no random elements kind of depressing.  such games are aesthetically pleasing and pure, but for recreation i look for surprises and chess only offers surprises when you understand the game well enough to be surprised.\r\n\r\ni am, however, thoroughly sick of chess as a metaphor and if i never see it again used in such a way i\\\'ll be happy.  also i adored strangers with candy, though i always forgot to watch it on a regular basis.',2,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(11856,2120,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i miss the clickety-clack whirring sounds that used to come from my old dot-matrix. for some reason, they were vaguely comforting; carrying memories of middle-school essays finally completed, funkily-printed ascii art, mom\\\'s grad-school thesis, printshop invitations for skating parties...</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11857,2122,'2001-12-04 18:41:16',0,'ssj3gostu to egg','<p align=\\\'justify\\\'>\r\nno! haha. no it is not. it is not about the girl that i made you all think was lame in the last entry. that\\\'s pretty funny though.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.100','en'),(11858,1046,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','me too.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11859,2122,'2001-12-04 18:42:36',0,'Pastrami on Rye','is it about me?!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11860,2120,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<p><b>to elly:</b> The whole college application/admittance rigamarole is a pain in the tucchus to be sure. But if Reed isn\\\'t your first choice institution, you could go there for a year or two and then transfer into UC Berkeley or Harvard or wherever. That kind of thing happens all the time. You could even transfer in from a community college, if it came to that. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Strangers With Candy\\\": another wonderful television phenomenon it appears I\\\'ve missed out on. Sigh. Well, that\\\'s what I get for living w/o cable for seven years. But! At least I have it now & can keep up with everyone when they drone on about the latest episode of \\\"Farscape.\\\" (okay, nobody ever does...but I\\\'m ready!)</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11861,2122,'2001-12-04 18:50:23',0,'ssj3gostu','nope, although you are quite delicious.',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.100','en'),(11862,2122,'2001-12-04 18:56:03',0,'Tommy','<img src=\\\"http://www.provalue.net/users/jds/people/ghond.gif\\\"><br>It is <b>I</b> that stoo speaks about!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11863,2120,'2001-12-04 19:05:28',0,'trouble','okidata printers spitting out banner after banner of perforated high school rah-rah nonsense.  i do remember that.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(11864,2121,'2001-12-04 19:26:47',0,'critic 2','yeah, let\\\'s not talk about the war anymore-- ignore it and it might go away.',0,0,0,0,'156.153.254.41','en'),(11865,2121,'2001-12-04 20:10:01',0,'erbie','<p>i sat watching the news last night and before i knew it, tears were dripping down my face.  yes, we\\\'ve heard a lot of ranting in the past few weeks, but it\\\'s <b>important</b> to be able to say, hey, i don\\\'t agree with this.  it\\\'s important to come to the point where you are actually outraged.  the reason the topic is tired is because it all feels so totally hopeless, what\\\'s the use of talking about it anymore?  if we just accept what the media and our government\\\'s spin docs tell us, then sure, we\\\'ll applaud israel.  yee haw.  blood for blood. great.</p>\r\n<p>the thing that struck me most is the unbelievable immaturity of all of this.  \\\"he hit me, i\\\'m gonna hit him harder.\\\"  especially seeing what is actually being bombed in afghanistan, which is not much, it seems not only immature, but like a mindbogglingly illogical thing to be doing.  i admit to naivete in the ways of the military, but it seems to me like the paranoiac rumors going around about us puttin\\\' in an oil pipeline could be true.  seems like there would have to be some other reason why we would bomb a bunch of already crumbling buildings and caves.  if anyone has any other paranoid or well-informed opinions about the other real underlying reasons why we\\\'re spending all this dough, i\\\'d love to hear it.  the topic will only be tired if we cease to think creatively about it.  if we share our thoughts about it, figure out what CAN be done (more visible protests?  more circulation of subversive websites?  more speaking engagements by cultural critics like good old noam?  bicycle activism to undermine the oil oligarchy?  jeez people, let\\\'s think!), maybe we\\\'ll all feel a little less despairing about the fucked up state of things.</p>\r\n<p>oh, yeah. during the news, they showed a commercial for paxil.  twice.</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11866,2120,'2001-12-04 20:34:38',0,'chi chi','i *heart*\r\n <i>farscape</i>',0,0,0,0,'64.168.22.30','en'),(11867,2121,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I\\\'m so frickin\\\' conflicted about everything going on in Afghanistan & Israel/Palestine I can\\\'t adequately express it at the moment (well, not w/o running on for too many pages). Short version: On one hand, I\\\'m happy to see some sort of end to the Taliban. But the cost and consequences of our actions there may be staggering. And what\\\'s going on in Israel...it\\\'s reprehensible, undefendable, horrifying. There\\\'s no chance for a \\\"peace process\\\" now. Anyone still trying to moderate for peace over there is being proven a fool in the eyes of their own countrymen on a daily basis. Doesn\\\'t matter whether you\\\'re Arab or Israeli now. Up until this weekend I was (despite certain reservations) on the Palestinians\\\' side in all this. Now I don\\\'t know what to think.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11868,2123,'2001-12-05 01:44:59',0,'Javorai','Good for ya, mate.  We support you.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.144','en'),(11869,2121,'2001-12-05 03:54:42',0,'Dorkus','I\\\'m a tip top mother<br> \r\nI\\\'m rooty tooty brother <br>\r\nI\\\'m a master of disguise <br>\r\nWith a kickass pair of eyes <br>\r\n<br>\r\nGotta sausage, gotta egg <br>\r\nGotta clothes line, gotta peg <br>\r\nGotta rotten wooden leg <br>\r\nBut you\\\'ll never see me beg <br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m a squeezy japaneezy <br>\r\nGotta blow my nose-a-sneezy <br>\r\nGotta hairball cough a wheezy <br>\r\nMy potatoes. Like \\\'em cheesy <br><br>\r\n\r\nI\\\'m a rag tag little man <br>\r\nTry to catch me if you can <br>\r\nWent to Spain and gotta tan <br>\r\nEthernet hub for my LAN<br>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11870,2122,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','ghond!!!',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11871,2122,'2001-12-05 14:50:40',0,'ghond','xep, do i remember you from back in our days on AmberMUSH? wow i guess that started back in late 1991. I played the character Jasra, from the books.I also played senusia, an original creation; I\\\'ve also recently begun playing Cordelia. \r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11872,1005,'2001-12-05 15:35:57',0,'jo','have you guys ever played the game where you name three people and your friends have to pick one person to go off a cliff, one person to spend a night with, and one person to spend the rest of your life with? we played the other day and i gave the choices of: 1. <b>ryan</b>, our waiter at kerbey lane who looked like a combination of nicolas cage and joseph fiennes but with longer hair, 2. <b>red-headed austin java company boy</b> and 3.<B>philadelphia shirt boy</b>, this server at spider house who always wears this shirt that says \\\"PHILADELPHIA!\\\" on it. and sara THREW THE REDHEAD OFF THE CLIFF. i couldn\\\'t believe it! ',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(11873,2125,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>self-imposed isolation. it can be good <i>or</i> bad, i suppose, depending on the motivation. it can range from a useful time of meditative insight to a depressive downward spiral.  it can be used as a introverted self-defense mechanism. i guess what you have to ask yourself is not only why you\\\'re doing it, but why you <i>think</i> you\\\'re doing it, and what your goals and motivations are. does that make any sense? i never know these days...</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11874,2125,'2001-12-05 16:27:31',0,'critic','we all have rough times, man, and we all deal however we can. i think you just have to find what works best for you and go for it. if what youre doing *works* for you, then go for it. if it starts getting tedious, then find another mechanism for dealing with it',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11875,2122,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','nope, not amber.  the randomgang muds.  my character name changed every six months or so.\r\nYogurt, Yaz Pistachio, Loopy Jigsaw, Gnat Moose Razzle Avocado, Orange, otolith.  you visited hugh\r\nand took him to a rave.  he raved about it.  you \r\ndrove cross country and thought your headlights weren\\\'t working but realized you had just forgotten to take your sunglasses off.  why do i\r\nremember these things 10 years later when they never had anything to do with me?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11876,2122,'2001-12-05 17:02:38',0,'hugh','<p>ghond, you freak!  dino!</p>\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'146.225.20.229','en'),(11877,2122,'2001-12-05 17:06:06',0,'ghond','hugh, as in... Vernon Lee! [the Head Glow Rat]. hugh did so much k that night that he lost his short term memory for over 40 days and nights.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11878,2122,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','yeah.. hugh always had a problem with moderation of his drugs.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11879,2125,'2001-12-05 17:22:46',0,'erb','<p>maybe it\\\'s a winter hibernation instinct.  i have been having very similar feelings, but since i\\\'m not in school, there\\\'s no waiting for \\\"it\\\" to be over.  i kind of miss that about school, the arbitrary breaking-up of the year into activity and non-activity.  my senior year of college was purposefully mellow.  i would get up each morning and eat oatmeal and listen to neil young\\\'s harvest moon.  i would go to the beach with my roommate and wander around the dunes.  i loved the semi-solitude and quietness of those mornings.\r\ni seem to have so much to say on this subject, i think i\\\'ll post my own post. </p>\r\n<p>my advice to you, though, is this:  try to enjoy the little tiny in-between minutes you get to yourself--mine include sneaking back into bed in my robe after i get out of the shower and snuggling in for five minutes or so; walking to the bus stop in the crisp morning air; settling in for an hour at lunch with a sandwich and coffee and my journal; shopping for dinner; reading a trashy sci-fi novel before bed; writing swinney posts at work.</p>',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11880,2122,'2001-12-05 17:24:01',0,'ghond','xep, did you know poonlarp? poonlarp was the man. he used to get us all our hookups.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11881,2122,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','fucking poonlarp.  we were engaged to be married.  at least that\\\'s what he told me.  he called me his little chicken of the sea.  and then it all turned bad.  he met some porpoise and turned a blind eye to me.  i was nothing but chopped monk fish liver.\r\ni hope he gets caught in a tuna net.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11882,2125,'2001-12-05 17:33:45',0,'Javorai','In the end, exam time will definitely give you masochistic pleasure.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.231.123','en'),(11883,2122,'2001-12-05 17:40:34',0,'ghond','xep, then that would make you... what? Dirque\'s half-cousin, once removed?! A wise doofhead once said \"it is a small world we live in, indeedy\".  Poonlarp parted ways with his aquarian lover due to a petty quibble over krill, so last i heard they were no more.  but, i read that on the da3m0nspawN bbs, which as we both know, is about as trusty as rusty surgical tools. i saw an episode of the iron chef once, where monkfish was the theme ingredient. i think monkfish are about hte ugliest beasties to ever grace the seven seas.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11884,2125,'2001-12-05 17:50:09',0,'ghond to jav','whats that suppposed to mean?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11885,2123,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','The rich have the same problems as the poor, the acts just take place on a different and not neccessarily better stage.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(11886,2122,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','classic, classic',4,0,0,0,'165.247.201.173','en'),(11887,2122,'2001-12-05 18:21:32',0,'ssj3gostu','honestly, i have nooooo idea what in the hell you guys are talking about, but you\\\'ve helped me get my highest comment count ever! so thanks :).',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.137','en'),(11888,2125,'2001-12-05 18:33:36',0,'ryan','<b>critic</b>, i think you should fucking stick it.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.98','en'),(11889,2125,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','or, alternately, SUCK IT. heehee',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11890,2125,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I\\\'ve been dealing with this same phenomenon for the past five or six years now; the old, almost nostalgic stress of \\\"finals\\\" has been replaced by the much more protracted (and often seemingly pointless) stress of \\\"software release crunchtime.\\\" For me this sort of thing has been known to go on from anywhere between two weeks and six months of non-stop, around the clock work. The tedium can be mind crushing, and the period around the holidays is the worst because you have so many obligations to deal with on top of the work at hand. And in that situation, the urge for self-isolation is entirely understandable. I need regular time to myself, where I\\\'m answering to the needs and expectations of my own whims and nothing else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I like erb\\\'s advice, btw. It\\\'s times like this when you have to take a lesson from the zen masters and try to \\\"be present\\\" at all times: hear the sounds of traffic (or nature) at night, enjoy the hike to the library for more research, appreciate the store customers (even the obnoxious ones). When it feels like all your personal time is being taken from you it\\\'s important to figure out how to take some of it back, even in incremental ways.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11891,2059,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','hi egg',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11892,1920,'2001-12-05 19:17:45',0,'li\\\'l sarah to egg','revisiting the above about the bathroom graffiti, i was at the ruby room the other night, just sort of hanging around in the bathroom with jessica and jenny and there was another little sarah there. and she insisted the bathroom graffiti couldn\\\'t be about her because she didn\\\'t know that many people and that it was her opinion that i looked considerably trashier than she. heehee. i took a photo.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11893,2059,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>hi, this is egg. it is hump day again. jay is coming home tonight. I am glad for this. ashley cleaned the house; i got down on my knees and scrubbed the tub. I have even put many of the cds back in their rightful case.  fernando has shit on my bed, so I am now changing the litter box.</p>\r\n<p>it is raining a lot here, and im feeling both domestic and randy. hump day has no meaning for me any longer however, because i do not hump on hump day anymore. again, dont cry for me, pussytina, ill get mine soon.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11894,1920,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to L.S.','I was thinking for sure that it was you. I cant imagine anyone else having bathroom graffitti written about them. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11895,891,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','whatever happened to incitor?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11896,1370,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','isnt \\\"shroud\\\" just another word for \\\"labia\\\"',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11897,2120,'2001-12-05 20:21:55',0,'da','kind of a bummer that california\\\'s been too busy building prisons to build some more damn colleges.  oops!',1,0,0,0,'128.111.96.65','en'),(11898,2122,'2001-12-05 20:52:52',0,'ghond','c\\\'mon stoo, fuck the comment count, its all about the <i>vibe</i> baby! gettin into it, doin it, swingin it... bringin it old school like never before! you know, countin it off like the geek sex machine that i am, man! xep knows. she\\\'s <i>down</i> with the Geek Logic, my man, reprazenting 9k networks of yore, eatin\\\' tangerines, mackin\\\' fine ladies, tweaking vi, MUD-ing.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11899,2125,'2001-12-05 20:58:12',0,'critic to nay-sayers','um. <i>hi</i>. my original comment was quite earnest and well intentioned. reread it, perhaps? concise, maybe. genuine, for sure. give me a break, you guys. if you find my comments to be that sour, it puzzles me why you would add to the sourness quotient you hate so much by dissing me. oh well. not meant to incite, so lets just let this one slide.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11900,2122,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i want to immortalize this conversation on a t-shirt i will never take off. is that a tattoo? okay, yes, one of those.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11901,2122,'2001-12-05 21:07:23',0,'ghond to weeb','weeb! i saw pictures of your shiny chrome-dome hair-doo on this here site. it would be remiss of me, however, to not digress right here and mention that you bear an <i>uncanny</i> resemblence to My Buddy, the <a href=\"http://www.ugcs.caltech.edu/~julius/\">\"Big Zee\"</a> aka Julius Yang; This here is the <i>truth</i>. perhaps you are of some relation, however, we\'ll probably never know. I hate to mention it, but you\'ll remember that The Zee got in a little too deep with that student ID thing. And, of course, it was downhill from there after Bernard the Squire got tied up in that Pelican Bay Hunger Strike thing.  Remember that shit he wrote? \"While the formal tongue of the Courts of Chaos may be Thari, the demons in the Courts use Dhemonh\'ka\"... HA HA!  So this is my attempt at keeping it all straight, at least for myself. Note: I did not invent this \"language\", nor do I contribute to it in any way. \r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11902,2122,'2001-12-05 21:09:36',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nnono, see, what it\\\'s <i>actually</i> about is winning! and see, this here is the twenty second comment, which puts me behind only elly\\\'s 23 and 25 comments on the first journal index. and anyway, elly doesn\\\'t <i>really</i> count, becuase she\\\'s a \\\'web celebrity.\\\' so in my mind i\\\'ve already won.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut! but, if we could get this up to 26? then my friend, i\\\'d be the big winner! so let\\\'s see what we can do!</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.137','en'),(11903,2122,'2001-12-05 21:13:24',0,'ghond','ah, but grasshopper stoo-san. your hunger for victory estranges you from enlightenment! comments exist in the reality of mere numbers while The Tales of Poonlarp lap quietly in the realm of countless <i>infinities</i>!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11904,2122,'2001-12-05 21:43:14',0,'ssj3gostu','enlightenment-shmightement! my attention span is far too short to care about any of the \\\'higher meaning\\\' stuff. the only thing that really matters is using your own achievements in relation to others as a measuring stick of your own self worth!',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.137','en'),(11905,2122,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i think the problem with the z/weeb comparison is that z is 80 feet tall and weeb is 3 inches tall.\r\nhowever, they both get down and get funky.  ok now.  let\\\'s get down and get funky.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11906,2122,'2001-12-05 23:11:28',0,'ghond','xeppie, you know i always loved you. shit, even when you were allergic to wheat gluten. or should i say <i>especially</i> when you were allergic to it. this here is the truth. i never loved you less than a silver dollar, and never more than a cowboy loves his caddillac.  That Poonlap is a daunting mutherfucker of a tease. Take to me, and we\\\'ll get to our feet and ruminate the centuries gone by!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11907,2122,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','if i\\\'m a web celebrity, why do i deny myself sausage? answer me that.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.200.226','en'),(11908,2118,'2001-12-05 23:21:27',0,'forcor','<p>\r\nI hanker for a hunka, a cube a slice a chunka, I hanker for a hunka cheese! That wasn\\\'t a commercial; that was one of a series of pseudo-PSA\\\'s that ABC used to run on Saturday mornings, between cartoons. \\\"The Bod Squad\\\" was what it was called. That strange anthropomorphic cheese head also sang memorable ditties like \\\"Don\\\'t Drown Your Food\\\" and \\\"Make a Saturdae.\\\" I sing these songs to myself far, far too often.\r\n<p>',1,0,0,0,'209.85.98.1','en'),(11909,2118,'2001-12-05 23:25:10',0,'forcor','<p>\r\nI got some of my facts wrong.\r\n\r\nhttp://www.yesterdayland.com/popopedia/shows/saturday/sa1725.php\r\n\r\ntells you all about it. \\\"Time for Timer\\\" it was called.\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'209.85.98.1','en'),(11910,2125,'2001-12-06 00:17:22',0,'erb','maybe it\\\'s the name \\\"critic\\\" that sets the fur a-bristle.',3,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11911,2125,'2001-12-06 00:18:59',0,'sims to critic','i was making fun of the other guy, intern',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11912,2122,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','ah yes, ghond and the BIG ZEE figures prominently in my memories of sitting around maadchen lake shooting peas at the eldery, roasting the occasional weaker of the species pond duck over an open fire, licking the frost off tree branches. perhaps some day we shall meet again, to press together, dirty old man to dirty old man and ponder beautiful women while picking our noses. salut dear freunde! ps: tell that knackerhead zee that he still owes me 10 bucks.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11913,2126,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','my birthdays sucked too for a while. then i decided. well, whatever! i don\\\'t care really. so last year i got on aplane last minute, went to nyc and partied my ass off. not really telling anyone it was my birthday except for this guy spanky who i met in the men\\\'s room. anyway....',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11914,2125,'2001-12-06 02:35:14',0,'critic','simon, heh. its all good.<br><br>\r\n\r\nerb, grab a dictionary:<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>critÂ·ic (krtk) <i>n.</i></b>:<br> \r\n<bl>One who forms and expresses judgments of the merits, faults, value, or truth of a matter.<br> \r\n<bl>One who specializes especially professionally in the evaluation and appreciation of literary or artistic works.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11915,2122,'2001-12-06 02:41:11',0,'ghond','weeb, i\\\'m thinking you probably got your 10$ worth from Mister \\\"my-pecker-is-like-beef-jerkey-and-freeze-dried-pemmican\\\" El Grande Zee, and should just cut your losses. That is, if youre thinking of what im thinking of, you know, the zebra incident at the shore. the Zee never really lets people down.  This here is the truth. I remember when he once told me, while roasting a autumn squab at <i>The Fresno Chicken Bucket</i>, \\\"its all relative, dude. a gizzard or a thigh. you treat your bitchez [sic] right and feel high as the sky\\\". Yo, i never claimed he was eloquent, i just said he was my friend.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11916,2126,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>PSA: nyc partying is heartily encouraged from this end, and every effort will thus be made to facilitate said partying!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11917,2127,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','it\\\'s just like you to check every box.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(11918,2127,'2001-12-06 05:51:59',0,'wb','oh give it 6 months of being called Prime Minister and i\\\'m sure you\\\'ll be singing a different tune re: \\\"just because they are simple doesnt mean im better than them.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(11919,2126,'2001-12-06 06:05:23',0,'chi chi','it\\\'s the anticipation of birthdays that are fun. all my actual days have been uneventful.<br>\r\n19:on planes jeddah-london-seattle-portland, with (unknown to me) pneumonia <br>\r\n20: in saudi with family, they decided i was getting too old for themselves, celebrated as a 1 year old.<br>\r\n21: after traveling alone in europe for a few months, and explaining that the 21st birthday is special to americans only in that you can legally drink, i turned 21 in saudi arabia, no alcohol allowed.<br>\r\n22: i don\\\'t remember much except it was in saudi i think.<br>\r\n23: driving from portland to bend. no one, including me remembered that it was my birthday.<br>\r\n24: my mom asked me how old i was.<br>\r\n25: coming up jan 3 (yay capricorn!) in egypt with my family',0,0,0,0,'64.168.22.30','en'),(11920,2126,'2001-12-06 06:06:19',0,'chi chi','oh, happy birthday!',0,0,0,0,'64.168.22.30','en'),(11921,2125,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I like erb\\\'s advice as well.  I think my life would be a lot easier if I had a beach to walk to in mornings.  That\\\'s one thing I miss about <i>back home</i> is the beach access.  Beaches are good.<br><br>Thanks for the stories and advices.  It was appreciated.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.64.230','en'),(11922,2126,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I think that my birthdays have sucked, as well, for about as long as I can remember.  This year wasn\\\'t so bad, I managed to drag a bunch of friends out to a decent party and one of them even stayed until close to the end with me!<br><br>My 19th birthday was pretty fun, what with going out and finding a bunch of my teachers there.  Nothing like celebrating your attaining the legal drinking age by having your highschool teacher\\\'s buying drinks for you and yelling at you.  Plus, everyone was home from University that time and their parents had all given them money to buy me drinks.  That was pretty fun.  I think my 18th was the year my parents didn\\\'t give me anything for my birthday and then tried to deny it afterward.',1,0,0,0,'64.229.64.230','en'),(11923,2126,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i usually do nothing on my birthday because everyone i know is always out of town. my birthday is the 29th of december, and usually people are still in wisconsin (or wherever they go) for christmas with mom and dad. so this year i said \'fuck it\' and left the country. i heartily recommend leaving everything behind and finding your own fun. (and not just on birthdays.)',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11924,2126,'2001-12-06 08:12:31',0,'o to s5','i plan to make sure that you get all the things you usually miss out on, this year.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11925,2126,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i was in australia for my birthday this year. i think we were on the road, or maybe we had just gotten back. most people forgot it was my birthday. i\\\'m not bitter about that, though, because i think everyone usually forgets everyone else\\\'s birthday. usually when i\\\'m at home i start planning my own birthday and inviting people, to avoid the mass guilt of everyone who forgets.\r\n<br><br>\r\nsteve didn\\\'t even tell me happy birthday this year, though. out of sight out of mind.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(11926,2126,'2001-12-06 16:59:13',0,'felicity','<P>I was going to make pretty much this exact post yesterday.  Today is my birthday and, true to form, it has been preceeded by a 2 week suckfest.  Here\\\'s what I was going to post yesterday: </P><P>Well, the losing streak continues.  Felicity has gotten the final\r\nconfirmation that once again...her birthday will suck.  I thought for\r\nsure that I had this one planned perfectly, but that just goes to show\r\nwhat I know.\r\n<BR>\r\nI had a date tomorrow night with someone that I\\\'ve known for a long time\r\nand been seeing for a few weeks.  (Notice I said \\\"had.\\\")  Here\\\'s some\r\nmath for the relationship impaired:\r\n<BR>\r\nex-girlfriend in town =Felicity?  Who\\\'s Felicity? = I get to sit around by\r\nmyself all night with my thumb stuck up my butt, wondering what my huge\r\ncharacter flaw is that makes me so fucking undesirable\r\n<BR>\r\nMy family isn\\\'t doing anything for me.  Anthony (the ex with whom I am attempting, however unsuccessfully, friendship for the sake of our two year old son)  had a typically\r\n\\\"Anthony\\\" response to the whole birthday thing.  And I quote:  \\\"I would\r\ntake you out to dinner or get you something for your birthday,\r\nbut...oh...didn\\\'t I buy you that CD a couple of months ago?  I guess I\r\ndon\\\'t feel so bad then.\\\"\r\n<BR>\r\nWow...now that I look at it in print, it\\\'s even more pathetic:  I can\\\'t\r\nget a single fucking guy to take me seriously.  My ex-boyfriend would\r\ngladly fuck me, but could care less about anything else unless it might\r\nmake my life miserable.  My family is so preoccupied with other things\r\n(important things, admittedly) that I get put on the back burner.  I\\\'m\r\nthinking I should just forget to get out of bed tomorrow morning.\r\n<BR>\r\nHow\\\'s that for a pity party?  Sorry to let it all out on you guys.  It\\\'s not\r\nas bad as I make it sound, and since it\\\'s becoming an annual tradition,\r\nI\\\'ve developed some coping strategies. </P>\r\nI never finished writing it, because it sounded so depressed that I figured I should try to make the day better.  I made a phone call, got a new dinner companion  for tonight, and fully intend to enjoy this day to the utmost of my ability (aided of course by high quality psych meds).',1,0,0,0,'216.42.125.249','en'),(11927,2127,'2001-12-06 17:05:15',0,'erb','didn\\\'t you have a warehouse job before that you wrote about? or am i imagining that? i work at a warehouse too, but in the \\\"cushy\\\" office part, according to the warehouse homies.  especially the freezer guy.  he says he would loooooove to trade jobs with me.  i say after a few rounds with the 7th graders, he\\\'d probably change his tune.',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11928,2126,'2001-12-06 17:13:58',0,'erb to felicity:','happy goddamn birthday!  you don\\\'t need a man to take you seriously!  you don\\\'t even need to take yourself seriously!  congratulations!  i have to say i still haven\\\'t invited anyone (except one guy who actually emailed me to ask, \\\"what do i bring to the party?\\\" i said, \\\"bring a date\\\" he said, \\\"how about some cheese instead?\\\"  augh.',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11929,2126,'2001-12-06 17:15:31',0,'ass-doobie-licious','hey mang, you could be doin a TON worse than spending the nite with your thumb stuck up your butt!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11930,2126,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','my 25th birthday will be hard to top for me.  after spending weeks in the outback, i suddenly realized my bday was in two days and i didn\\\'t want to spend it in Bamaga (!!!).we decided to try and make it back to civilization in time to celebrate so we somehow managed 1200km on 4wd only dirt roads in a day and a half.  we were booking!  we showed up at a 5 star resort outside of Cairns    totally covered in mud, having not bathed for days.  we got the most expensive room they had which turned out to be a two bedroom cabana on the golf course.  the place had 5 acres of swimming pools and was right on a beach... one of those picturesque beaches with coconut trees and white sand n\\\' all that.  we spent the next two days sitting poolside while people dressed in white brought us tropical fruity beverages. and then? back into the bush for another several weeks.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11931,2126,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','my birthday wish tends toward thinking of a project that I want done that requires the help of others, and guilting them into doing it. On my 25th birthday I made people come over and help me re-arrange my bedroom furniture.  ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11932,2126,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','This post echoes my feelings about New Years\\\' Eve. Somehow it\\\'s almost always anticlimactic. I spent the millenium turnover at a friend\\\'s parents\\\' posh house in Carmel Valley (CA) for example. We all played parlor games until the countdown, and then everyone went to bed. Blah.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>to le:</b> I always forget everyone else\\\'s birthdays. I have a teflon memory when it comes to names, and an utterly frictionless memory when it comes to dates. Example: I\\\'m 32 years old and I still end up standing around and scratching my head when I try to recall <i>my parents\\\'</i> birthdays. One\\\'s born on July 4 (or 6) and the other\\\'s born on June 4 (or 6) and I constantly forget which is which, who is when, and what they want for a present. At these moments I fear I\\\'m a sure candidate for early Alzheimers.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11933,2126,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','oh, and: my birthday falls on April 14 (yay Aries, rah rah). The day before tax day. So generally nobody\\\'s ever in a mood for much of a party then. No matter--I don\\\'t care. In recent years I\\\'ve taken to having a birthday party on a later date, whenever my friends are free. So I don\\\'t have a birthday, exactly: I have a birth<i>month</i>. Next April/May, if anyone wants to take me out to dinner, feel free to let me know.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11934,2125,'2001-12-06 18:59:19',0,'erb','critic, the definition explains my point: that critic=judge, and people don\\\'t post here to be judged or preached to.  read the FAQ.',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11935,2125,'2001-12-06 19:04:00',0,'erb','also, critic, i agree with your comment and didn\\\'t find it offensive.  i appreciate brevity and sincerity.  i\\\'m sure i could get used to your name.\r\n[x]best friendly',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11936,2127,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>my first job, in high school, was working in a mexican-imported tile warehouse.  i drove a forklift, climbed high on steel shelves to pluck tile, packed palettes, unpacked palettes, packed trucks, unpacked trucks, etc.  once, i drove the forklift into a palette and the fork blades punctured some expensive tile floor sealant.  i stealthily deposited them in the dumpster before anyone found out.</p>\r\n<p>then one saturday i quit my job after one of the other warehouse guys made a crude comment about one of the young little showroom girls, but it really was just an excuse.  i hated that job, but then again ... it \\\"BUILT CHARACTER\\\"!</p>',2,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(11937,2126,'2001-12-06 19:25:36',0,'erb','leos are funny: \\\"oh, august is the BEST time to have a birthday, darling.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(11938,2125,'2001-12-06 19:44:38',0,'critic\\\'s little rant','erb. today\\\'s been a sucky day, so i\\\'ll go you tit-for-tat with this:<br><br>\r\nCorrect, people <i>don\\\'t</i> post here to be judged or preached to, as you yourself pointed out.  This being said, am I the only one who finds it humorously ironic that you persist in doing those very things in response to my thoughtful (albeit terse) comments?<br><br>\r\nthe FAQ. um. yeah. read that like a year or so ago (are you new here?) just now did a \\\"find\\\" on it, and didnt locate the word \\\"judge\\\" anywhere. i <i>did</i> see an admonishment not to be a \\\"prick\\\" and not to \\\"kick others down\\\". i genuinely feell ike ive done neither, and kept with the general wit and spirit of this site. er, ive probably been a prick, but only in moments like now, in response to similar needless prickiness.<br><br>\r\nso, once again, can we drop this? leave me alone, please. you may not like my name. thats ok. you may even voice this. ok again. but stop nagging me please. thanks. [x] constructive\r\n\r\n',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11939,2125,'2001-12-06 19:53:16',0,'ghond','(lol). although both of you would be well advised to suck it, it does seem like critic has a point, imho. although, we could all be nicer about voicing our opinions/reservations. this here is the truth. its an important lesson, indeedy. like the chicken on the block, like the <a href=\\\"http://www.johnnyonthespot.com/\\\">Johnny on the Spot</a>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11940,2125,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hey critic, if you\\\'re such an oldtimer, howcome you\\\'re not using your real name when you post? and howcome i don\\\'t know who you are? ',4,0,0,0,'165.247.218.215','en'),(11941,2125,'2001-12-06 20:04:32',0,'critic','hi le. not an old timer, just a frequent long time reader and less-frequent occasional comment poster. in hindsight, i wasnt very well containing my ire in my earlier comment, so apologies if i came off as more of an asshole than i actually am (and i actually am, sort of) :) my goal today is to stop bing a prick and let this whole critic/anti-critic thing blow over. so, apologies again.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11942,2125,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','well critic, i think what happened was that people who were writing on the site thought that various peope were using the name \\\"critic\\\" to diss anonymously. which, you may agree, is a cowardly thing to do. so there became some animosity toward the name critic. and now, you\\\'re using the name critic pretty consistently, but we don\\\'t know jack about you. i think most people who write on this site are pretty forthcoming about at least some of their personal details, but you\\\'re just a random we know nothing about, and your moniker brings up negative connotations for many of us. so don\\\'t be surprised if there\\\'s a general lack of trust. have a nice day!',4,0,0,0,'165.247.218.215','en'),(11943,2125,'2001-12-06 20:14:23',0,'critic','oh <i>now</i> i get it. ok, thanks for finally shedding some light on that. sheesh, i was wondering why the cold reception of late, since ive been reading and commenting more regularly. i didnt know my name had history. i meant it to be anonymously siskel-and-ebert-ish, in a silly way... like, the critic who never personally posts, just comments :) something like \\\"ghost\\\" but with a twist.<br><br>\r\nincidentally, ive been coming back more often lately as a bulk of the writing here seems to be of a rather high quality these days. and youre right. pseudonyms do suck.  thanks for the insights, i really did aprpeciate the facts.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11944,2118,'2001-12-06 20:17:17',0,'jay','I remember that cheese commercial.  Something probably paid for by the New England Dairy Council or some shit.  But wasn\\\'t the little man made of two crackers with cheese in the middle?  Or did he just make a cheese cracker sandwich and roll it around while he sang?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11945,2125,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','you might consider writing an article or two, just in the name of participation.',3,0,0,0,'165.247.218.215','en'),(11946,2126,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','I am celebrating my 21st birthday this sunday. The actual day is on Wednesday.\r\n\r\nI don\\\'t know what it is going to be like... It\\\'s that combination of friends with family and old family friends I haven\\\'t seen for years that kind of scares me.\r\n\r\nMy 19th birthday was the most depressing thing.  I arranged a picnic, like I had for my 17th and 18th birthdays.  2 days before my birthday, I had moved back from living in another city for a year.  I\\\'d told everyone and people had said they were coming.  Only two people showed up about half an hour to an hour after the time I said it\\\'d start.  My ex boyfriend from 4 years earlier and my best friend.  \r\n\r\nI was quite upset.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(11947,129,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','this is fascinating. you complain of having to listen to liberal commentators snipe Bush (\\\"yawn\\\") and yet you then sarcastically commend the Green party voters for electing Bush. and <i>then</i> you sarcastically blast the republicans for their platform. are you confused, or are you a-political, and all-sarcastic?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(11948,129,'2001-12-07 04:28:06',0,'all-sarcastic','ding ding ding...we have a winner!!!',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.42','en'),(11949,2127,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','i keep thinking about it, and you COULD be the P.M. someday.',1,0,0,0,'209.208.77.108','en'),(11950,2131,'2001-12-07 20:25:05',0,'tender flesh','*be nice to me*',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.183','en'),(11951,2131,'2001-12-07 21:18:24',0,'stella','btw, fishing line works beautifully. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(11952,2131,'2001-12-07 21:31:53',0,'trouble','somewhere, a fish is smiling.  *best friendly*',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(11953,1889,'2001-12-07 21:41:49',0,'Walter Lippman','Copyright is a new form of censorship',2,0,0,0,'64.229.171.186','en'),(11954,2133,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','wow, thank you sims. the horses make me really happy. i wish they were still around sf, i have only ever seen one of them, and i think it was on a truck, never to be seen again. reminisce.',1,0,0,0,'66.123.206.189','en'),(11955,2133,'2001-12-07 23:47:09',0,'sims','M.I.H.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11956,2133,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','hey, I recall these horses, or ones like them. They appeared all over San Francisco in the early \\\'90s, often with the tag \\\"Reminisce\\\" and alternately tagged with other words starting with \\\"re\\\" like remember, revolution, etc. Are these new pics? Recently I think I\\\'ve started seeing them around town again. I always associated them with the last <i>re</i>cession--perhaps they\\\'re coming back.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Back in those days there were a number anonymous artists doing strange and unique things with graffiti. My favorites were the haunting blue dogs: collections of huge hound faces painted in shades of dripping gray (or, later, blue-gray) housepaint. they\\\'d show up in dark alleys and on the crumbling walls of abandoned buildings, their pitch-black eyes staring bleakly out into nothingness....</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11957,2133,'2001-12-07 23:49:12',0,'sims','these are old. from the early 90s.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11958,2133,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','when i first moved to sf i saw \\\"revolution\\\" tagged everywhere. ',4,0,0,0,'66.123.206.189','en'),(11959,2133,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','well, thanks for the post. they\\\'re lovely shots. and I\\\'ve been thinking about those horses lately.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(11960,2133,'2001-12-08 00:03:37',0,'sims','<p>\r\ni miss this kind of shit:\r\n<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.homorobotic.org/images/amaze14.jpg\\\">\r\n</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(11961,2133,'2001-12-08 10:30:38',0,'ryan','i want someone to explain to me in plain english, what the female => horse dynamic is.  plain fucking english.  no horoscopes.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.93','en'),(11962,2133,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','just sometimes chicks like horses.  you know, just like sometimes chicks like kittens?',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11963,2133,'2001-12-08 12:34:56',0,'kini','and bunnies. love the fwuffy bunnies.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.27.69.91','en'),(11964,2133,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i don\\\'t know ryan, ask freud.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni think it\\\'s got something to do with loving something that is strong enough to be terribly violent but more often than not chooses to graze quietly. \r\n',4,0,0,0,'165.247.205.91','en'),(11965,2133,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hey. boys can like horses, too. didnt you ever read <a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0140260706/qid=1007835593/sr=1-2/ref=sr_1_11_2/104-4730278-9207950\\\"><i>equus</i></a>??</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11966,2133,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to ryan','it\\\'s prolly close to/ or the same as/ the reason why boys go gaga over cars in their teenage years. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11967,2124,'2001-12-08 20:46:05',0,'how\\\'s','the dope in heaven? hard to score?',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(11968,2124,'2001-12-08 21:55:39',0,'SAMO','VERY HARD TO COME BY. LIMBOLAND YOU HAVE TO GO USUALLY.',1,0,0,0,'63.42.72.201','en'),(11969,2124,'2001-12-08 22:06:09',0,'*sigh*','alrighty, then.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11970,2133,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','My very first pre-adolescent romance was with a girl I met in junior high art class. Pretty much all she did was sketch pictures of horses. I liked to draw cats. So that was our initial bond, horses and cats, and a few weeks later she was my partner for every song at the halloween dance. (I won a a prize for my \\\"mad scientist\\\" costume that night. I was allowed to pick a record from a stack of records one of the teachers had purchased for the occastion. To this day I wonder why I picked ELO over the Rolling Stones.)</p>\r\n<p>Anyway, two weeks after that, she moved away and I never heard from her again. Sigh.</p>',1,0,0,0,'64.164.192.186','en'),(11971,2133,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','oh, and: I wonder if for some girls it isn\\\'t a sort of sublimated sexual thing. Some women just want a nice strong stallion between their legs, you know?',0,0,0,0,'64.164.192.186','en'),(11972,2134,'2001-12-09 04:58:23',0,'critic','lots of school. lots of compsci. lots of drinking. not much sleep. you <i>are</i> a busy man.',3,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11973,2133,'2001-12-09 06:59:51',0,'auntie','when le was a very tiny baby i used to sing her the lullabye about horses. i never could sing very well, but the babies liked it when i did.<br><br>\r\n\r\nhush-a-bye, don\\\'t you cry, go to sleepy, little baby.  when you wake, you shall have, all the pretty little horses...<br><br>\r\n\r\ndapples and greys, pintos and bays, all the pretty little horses...<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe christmas she was 6, i bought her a barbie horse and she loved it above all things, until \\\"my little pony\\\" came on the scene.  i still have all of her my little pony toys, including the stable, in a rubbermaid container in the garage.  i saved all of their toys from when they were small, but the pony toys were the hands down favorite for many, many years.<br><br>\r\n\r\ni like the horse pictures too.  i am very fond of graffiti with a message, when it is done with great love, as these obviously were.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11974,2120,'2001-12-09 07:09:47',0,'auntie','puppeteer!  le doesn\\\'t know that her uncle, my half-brother IS a puppeteer.  he is a puppet artist.  website <a href=\\\"http://www.monsterpuppets.com\\\">uncle of le - pierre the puppet maker</a><br><br>\r\n\r\nwhat a hoot!<br><br>\r\n\r\nprediction from mom - le will surprise herself in the educational arena, now she\\\'s old enough that being just like everyone else is a drawback, rather than an attribute, which is what it is in high school.  in high school they want you to sit down, shut up, never question, and always pretend like you think they are all-knowing.  i hated 90% of le\\\'s teachers.  they were idiots.',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11975,2126,'2001-12-09 07:17:39',0,'auntie','having had 48 of them, i will tell you what my granny june told me when i was 18 and bummed on my birthday...<br><br>\r\n\r\nbirthday celebrations should begin 30 days prior to, and continue 30 days following, the actual date of your birth.  anything you do or buy or receive that gives you joy during that time period is a \\\"birthday present,\\\" and should be announced as such to all and sundry.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsundry who may have forgotten your birthday will be overjoyed that they have such a nice flexible time period in which to choose your gift.  you\\\'ll have more presents, more days for dinner out, more chances for fun with more people.<br><br>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11976,2136,'2001-12-09 16:53:11',0,'auntie','the positive and constructive comment i feel compelled to make is that these are all questions that have plagued me, too, at various times in my life, mostly at three in the morning. <br><br>\r\n\r\ni would imagine that most of us have heard them in our heads at one time or another. <br><br>\r\n\r\nlove, or being in love, or having someone be in love with you, or all of the above, is out there, out there for everyone, the trick is finding the match that is yours, and the season in which it comes is not predictable. <br><br>\r\n\r\nto find purpose in your life is one thing, to find purpose in life as a whole is entirely another.<br><br>\r\n\r\nin order to find your own purpose, you must first pinpoint that which gives you joy.  for many people, this is found in the creation of a thing, for others, it is in the completion of a challenging task, and for still others, it is in bringing joy or knowledge to another.<br><br>\r\n\r\nagain, there is no season to discovering your purpose, it simply happens when the time is right, some of us will search for many years before our feet hit the path of purpose, some will be born knowing their paths.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlove is all about caring, caring is all about love, and families are all about every emotion, including hate, indifference, resentment, shame, frustration, and confusion.<br><br>\r\n\r\nfor many people, families are also about unconditional love.  they love you even though they disagree with you, you love them even though they make you crazy.  this is okay. there is no law that says you must always live within your family, or even near them.<br><br>\r\n\r\nthe road to finding all the answers is fraught with crossroads of decisions, and perhaps the meaning of your life, the purpose of your life, is to choose wisely.<br><br>\r\n\r\nonce again i feel compelled to remind you of the prayer made famous by bill w. - <br><br>\r\n\r\ngod grant me the serenity to accept the things i cannot change, the courage to change the things i can, and the wisdom to know the difference. <br><br>\r\n\r\nthrow in the golden rule - do unto others as you would have them do unto you - and you\\\'re pretty much headed in a direction that will lessen your angst.<br><br>\r\n\r\nlove and kisses, mom',2,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11977,2120,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow i am so enchanted with pierre the puppetmaker!@ i had the best time making a muppet for simon-- wow, imagine doing that all the time! i get giddy thinking about it... hmm, maybe i\\\'ll make my cousins a puppet for xmas...',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(11978,2126,'2001-12-09 20:55:32',0,'felicity to auntie','<p>If only I had know all those years ago what wisdom resided with me in that stairwell on Steubenstrasse, I probably would have spent more time soaking up your sage advice and less time trying to play Liesure Suit Larry with Elly on your old Apple when no one was looking.</p>\r\n<p>Everyone should be so lucky as to have an Auntie like you. - Mickey</p>',1,0,0,0,'216.42.67.184','en'),(11979,2136,'2001-12-09 22:26:00',0,'steph','at times like this, when things are less than spectacular, its good to know that there are people in the world who are feeling similar to you. complacency is no longer enough. i want fantastic things to happen. i am myself, and comfortable with that. i will make things happen, and you will too!',2,0,0,0,'64.12.103.157','en'),(11980,2133,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/grafeye.JPG\\\"><br><tt>some pretty graf i found behind my apartment today.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(11981,2126,'2001-12-10 00:23:22',0,'auntie','you thought i didn\\\'t know you were playing leisure suit larry?  lol! lsl was one of those games your mother would have killed me if she\\\'d known i was allowing you to know about, much less play, and you didn\\\'t have quite enough fun, so naturally, being the meddler i continue to be, i pretended not to notice.  i\\\'m glad it gave you joy.  love & kisses, auntie',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(11982,2127,'2001-12-10 05:19:16',0,'ballsack','chia: if you become prime minster, then put be down as your first minister of funk. ',0,0,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(11983,2138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','more of this on swinney please.  you are an amazing writer.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11984,2138,'2001-12-10 05:58:58',0,'hungry belly','this pear has got my number',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.162','en'),(11985,2138,'2001-12-10 06:05:46',0,'fractoid','<p>last night i had a dream that i ate rice krispies with rice dream and.. it was good.',0,0,0,0,'172.134.17.109','en'),(11986,2138,'2001-12-10 06:19:57',0,'jim','damn fine to see you here, my dear.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(11987,2138,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','gus, you are your name sake in the breakfast club.  silent silent silent until you shout out something so unexpected that no one knows what to say or do except wait eagerly for you to speak again.',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(11988,2138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','she kinda looks like ally sheedy, too.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11989,2138,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','<p>Thanks guys. You are nice. I\\\'ve been meaning to write stuff or find old stuff and put it up here for months. I\\\'ll keep doing it, but only if you promise you\\\'ll never tell me I look like ally sheedy again. </p>\r\n<p>I\\\'ll give you some more tomorrow.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(11990,2138,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<img src=\\\"http://www.etext.org/Zines/VelvetDonk/issue6/ally.jpg\\\">',2,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(11991,2138,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<img src=\\\"http://www.megspace.com/entertainment/ggordon/sheedy/as10.jpg\\\">\r\n<br><tt>at some random point in time in the 80\\\'s, perhaps circa 6th grade or so, i vaguely recall having a major crush on ally sheedy. i had managed to completely stuff this memory until just now, when i saw the comments here. odd.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11992,2137,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I havent started christmas shopping yet, at <i>all</i>. uh oh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11993,2139,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','editor: I goofed. please delete this.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.178','en'),(11994,2140,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','I love screwing up swinney posts, just to remind myself how dorky I am. hi.',1,0,0,0,'64.81.65.178','en'),(11995,2127,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','<p>well i quit.</p>\r\n<p>job sucked so bad</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(11996,2140,'2001-12-10 16:17:07',0,'hool','<p>any time i\\\'m writing a lengthy text entry to be submitted in an html form, i just open a text editor, write it in there, then cut n paste.  i\\\'ve lost too much lost work through browsers.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(11997,2140,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yep. ive lost my last two submissions here while typing them, somehow, and i cant motivate myself to rewrite them, ha. oh well. someday.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(11998,2129,'2001-12-10 19:45:47',0,'ashton','<P>\r\nbroken down outside fresno\r\n</P><P>\r\nliving on dark chocolate and water\r\n</P><P>\r\nwaiting for a car to pick them up\r\n</P><P>\r\nand take them to an autoparts store\r\n</P><P>\r\ntime for a new radiator\r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(11999,2129,'2001-12-10 19:50:06',0,'','how long were they stranded in the wilds of fresno?! i hope the water and chocolate held out until they could find civilisation!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12000,2138,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','Damn you people. I posted again, but I will definitely stop if you keep posting photos of ally sheedy!',6,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12001,2141,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','hi gus. good to have you here, and whatnot. i like this. thanks.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12002,2138,'2001-12-10 21:29:33',0,'huge_wobbly_penis','*wobble*',1,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(12003,2140,'2001-12-10 21:55:05',0,'huge_wobbly_penis','always write in a handy .txt file. always always. then cut n paste n save. then go back later and \\\'oh my god i wrote that?!\\\' shortly followed by \\\'oh my god people are -reading- this?!\\\'\r\n\r\nat least, that\\\'s what i do. doesn\\\'t work for everyone, of course.\r\n\r\nalso, when i think about you i touch myself. i touch myself.',1,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(12004,2141,'2001-12-10 22:06:01',0,'ophelia','floating. naked. open. in a river of rejected emotions. cursed with quiet.',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.168','en'),(12005,2138,'2001-12-10 23:12:42',0,'critic','fuuuuuck. say what you will, ally sheedy is still <i><b>hot</i></b>. i kinda dig that pic that peter posted.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12006,2142,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','<p>this:<br>\r\n\\\"we weave a treaty to settle your unknowing hand\\\" is my favorite.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12007,2144,'2001-12-11 01:15:37',0,'steph','god, that sounds like a synopsis of every relationship ending ive gone thru. ',0,0,0,0,'152.163.207.78','en'),(12008,2129,'2001-12-11 01:35:00',0,'ashton','<P>\r\na man can wait and wait for a ride,\r\n</P><P>\r\nsitting on the side of the Interstate.\r\n</P><P>\r\ncars just pass him on by.\r\n</P><P>\r\na woman does not have to wait so long.</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12009,2140,'2001-12-11 01:48:31',0,'<B>NOTICE</B>','please save all documents on your hardrive and use the \\\"upload a text file\\\" option when submitting entries.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nonly text files work, no MS Word docs.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhtml appreciated, dont use header info though.\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12010,2139,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','uh uh. this is good! no deletes... i cheat on editing my comments, but no deleting whole entries...',0,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12011,2144,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','That\'s weird because none of this one is true or real. ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12012,2140,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>why does your name have to be huge wobbly penis. that bothers me.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12013,1099,'2001-12-11 09:53:40',0,'lulu','you are all idiots.',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(12014,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'m just rereading this and feeling great about egg. dear egg, you\\\'re great.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.210.100','en'),(12015,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>let me just say <i>thank you</i>, egg, as without this article, i\\\'d have never found the <a href=\\\"http://www.thistothat.com/gom/current.shtml\\\"><b>glue of the Month</b></a> webpage!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12016,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter again','<br><tt>... actually, though; what a cop-out... this month, their \"glue of the month\" is (drumroll, please!): <i>Scotch Tape</i>.  Hmm. After profiling the veritable high rollers of the adhesives world, like Gelatin and Glycerin Glue and the like, they\'ve stooped to putting mere <i>tape</i> in the spotlight. this is strange. although, i suppose tape <i>is</i> an adhesive.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12017,2145,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>for some reason, i was reading this article backwards, like, from the bottom up. i liked reading it that way. its a good article.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12018,2145,'2001-12-11 13:35:00',0,'trouble','oh the joys of contract work, we feel your pain.  *best friendly*',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12019,2142,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiam','does the penis get rigid?',6,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(12020,2142,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>there\\\'s something vaguely disturbing about the concept of a <i>wobbly penis</i>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12021,2142,'2001-12-11 15:13:40',0,'sawks:','<br>\r\nhowzabouts LOLLING instead?<br><br>\r\n',3,0,0,0,'144.132.14.22','en'),(12022,2142,'2001-12-11 15:25:28',0,'jo','i was about to say that the \\\"wobbling\\\" is my favorite part.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12023,2144,'2001-12-11 15:27:28',0,'jo','in my crowd, people <b>definitely</b> leave and never come back.<BR>\r\nbut usually because we ask them to.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12024,2146,'2001-12-11 15:28:28',0,'Dv8','<p>The 100th Swinney very nice. I was laughing hysterically thinking of you asking all these people if their coworkers play with poo. As for the work thing I was thinking maybe we should start looking into apprenticeships. you know anyhting about apprenticeship?</p> ',1,0,0,0,'64.229.160.176','en'),(12025,2145,'2001-12-11 15:35:45',0,'jo','auntie, i know that search engines like google place a LOT of weight on how many people link to you. like she could search for \\\"doll+crazy+lady\\\" on google and she would be the 10th result but if maybe two people linked to her as \\\"doll crazy lady,\\\" her results would go up.<BR>\r\nin fact, this works so well that some people are using it for sabotage: see <a href=\\\"http://uber.nu/2001/04/06/index.html\\\">google bombing</a>.<BR>\r\nlike, if i wanted a group called <a href=\\\"http://elly.org\\\">lezbos against drunk driving</a> to be able to find their natural leader, elly, the queen of <a href=\\\"http://elly.org\\\">lezbos against drunk driving</a>, i would help elly\\\'s hits go up when someone searched for the word \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.elly.org\\\">lezbos</a>.\\\" ',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12026,2145,'2001-12-11 15:38:18',0,'jo','oh so the helpful advice with this is: maybe she could trade links with ten other doll sites? maybe she could spearhead a doll ring? maybe people who HAVE doll rings are the ones whose names keep coming before hers?',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12027,2142,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i dunno. it reminds me of that old toy commercial: <i><b>\\\"Weebles wobble but they dont fall down!\\\"</i></b>. i think \\\'meandering catatonic penis\\\' would be nice.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12028,2142,'2001-12-11 16:50:35',0,'speaking of','<p><a href=\\\"http://www.100things.com/100stories/family/family4.html\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">gratuitous\r\nuse of wobbly cock</a></p>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.244','en'),(12029,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','whatever happened to incitor?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12030,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','whatever happened to lulu?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12031,1099,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I don\'t know about lulu, but it seems to me incitor announced his disgust and intention to quit swinneydotorg in the commentary to <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1214.txt\">this piece</a>. I don\'t think we\'ve seen him since. The whole \"griece\" deception, unmasking, and subsequent ridicule deal sent him over the edge, it appears.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but then again only Darwin would know for sure. And whatever happened to Darwin?</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12032,1099,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','oh, darn it. maybe someone could edit that html. thanks.',0,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12033,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i fixed it, moebius.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni have to say, i have a lot of respect for the rigid morality of the east coasters, people like darwin and incitor, and i think it\\\'s great that when they say they\\\'re going to leave, and storm off in disgust, they actually stay gone. it\\\'s a consistency that i find admirable. unfortunately, my admiration of  those with more rigid or conservative moral ground is rarely returned by the subject of my admiration. i can like them, but i don\\\'t live up to so many things they consider important, nor do most of my swinneyite pals, and hence, we\\\'ll never be liked back. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve been thinking a lot about east coast vs west coast, and i think a lot of the above argument is related to that schism, a certain east coast rigidity and conservatism butting up against a west coast intellectualism and liberalism.  the widening and expansiveness of the west coast, acceptance and general laid back attitude that californians take in discussions like this really clashes horribly with the east coast demand for stern logic and  harsh lessons. \r\n<br><br>\r\ni think the west coast can accept and appreciate the east coast, but it can never go both ways.\r\n<br><br>\r\nthe exception to the rule here is peter. but he\\\'s a raver, so he might not count. :)\r\n<br><br>\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'209.86.5.225','en'),(12034,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','im from the east coast. all that means is that I prefer Tribe to Snoop. <i>although</i>, one might have noticed a recent obsession with deez nutz. i am becoming indoctrinated into the way of life here.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12035,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','but you choose to live here',4,0,0,0,'209.86.5.225','en'),(12036,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','ah yes! but its cold out there man!!!!!! and darwin chooses to live here too.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12037,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yeah dude, but if you ask darwin about living here he talks about how much he hates it! you don\\\'t do that.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.5.225','en'),(12038,2142,'2001-12-11 19:43:58',0,'huge_wobbly_penis','i love you all.',1,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(12039,1214,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>I still dont understand why it is important that the \\\"truth\\\" of biographical detail be the cornerstone of good community and good writing. this is the web, not a court of law. we are sharing writing, and as my pal jay says, \\\"The minute you commit something to the page, it\\\'s a fiction.  Life doesn\\\'t follow an articulate order, and our memories are inexact.\\\"</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12040,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','does darwin really hate it here? why doesnt he move! im burning with curiousity.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12041,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','well. he doesn\\\'t like us, or any of our friends, and he doesn\\\'t like the weather on twin peaks. there\\\'s some other stuff he doesn\\\'t like about sf but i can\\\'t remember now.  but he likes cyan, and he likes his capoeria friends because they actually \\\"do\\\" things. \r\n<br><br>\r\nit occurs to me we could be having this conversation on chat in /m, but nothing seems to matter today.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.1.124','en'),(12042,2141,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','nobody, not even the rain, has such small hands. -ee cummings',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12043,1099,'2001-12-11 20:19:36',0,'jo','all pants, all the time.<BR>\r\nit seems like today is good for <a href=\\\"http://www.hot-licks.org/\\\">aden</a> and drixoral and sniffling and wishing i hadn\\\'t gotten out of bed.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12044,1099,'2001-12-11 20:29:23',0,'jo','also, i\\\'m an enormous egg fan. egg, props to you. also, duh, east coast rap and hip hop is obviously much better, homes.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12045,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>le, i read your east-side-hizzy v. west-west-y\'all comment, and little wheels in my brain started a-churnin\', forming a thoughtful little rant, aligning mental words, ready to defend myself and all us \"east coast conservatives\" (heh).<br><br>\r\nthen i saw your <i>peter disclaimer</i>.<br><br>\r\nnow im all good and cant think of much to say. you\'re kinda right. many east coast folks i know belong somewhere like cali, and i cant help but wish we had more cali-ish folks here in the city. most new yorkers are assholes because alot of the redeeming ones move west.</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12046,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that first paragraph is <i>profound</i>. i firmly believe that the universe/fates/cosmos is trying, rather desperately at times, to get something across to us; all the while, humanity proves, time and time again, to be just ignorant enough to not get the message, constantly resisting countless tiny hints.</tt>',0,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12047,1099,'2001-12-11 20:50:43',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut see, i definitely have to say something about this- cause this too has been something i\\\'ve been thinking about alot. ever since i moved here (to sf from the east coast) i\\\'ve been sort of keeping a little tally of differences between new york and california.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand really, what i think it comes down to is manners. people in california have no manners.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nexample one: if i am sitting on the bus on the inside of the seat, and there is someone on the outside of the seat, and i am getting off the bus, that person should get up to let me out instead of moving their legs a quarter of an inch and making me climb over them.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\'justify\\\'>\r\nexample two: stop staring at me! i know i\\\'m really cute, but that doesn\\\'t mean you have to stare and stare and stare. people in sf are unrelenting with the eye contact. it\\\'s annoying and being annoying is being without manners.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand i don\\\'t think the concept of manners falls into \\\'rigidity and conservatism.\\\' i think it\\\'s more common sense. but then again, i guess you could say that my calling manners \\\'common sense\\\' is being rigid and conservative.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\'>\r\noh, and it\\\'s deffinately all about the WU.</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.177','en'),(12048,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i dont have a problem with staring. people who have a problem with staring are afraid of something. they have something to hide.  in general, people dont talk or walk as fast in california. these are the two things i long for sometimes. when I am walking down the street with lulu, i feel like im in a manual wheelchair. and once ive cut my pace in half she grabs me and says \\\"are we in enormous rush??\\\" and she groans and leans over, and suddenly i feel uptight.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12049,2147,'2001-12-11 20:55:32',0,'adumb','<p>like, wow.  some <i>profound</i> thoughts: Time is like a whisper up the grammatical spiral staircase of my mind.  Space is but stepping stone towards the discloser of an infinite truth, which comes forth like the ever-opening petals of a lotus flower. the universe beckons us with showers of invisible rain that settles into a dew on the innermost regions of our souls.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>*BARF*</b></p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12050,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>new yorkers have very little pretense. no one gives a fuck about how you act or what you look like. and its definitely not because theyre openminded, its simply because they just do not care.  this can be simultaneously comforting and alienating.<br><br>\r\nunfortunately, most new yorkers also lack a soul.<br><br>\r\nstoo, youre the kind of people i\\\'m talking about; good people. people that mnove west.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12051,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','new york is the best city in the world.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12052,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, the requisite <i>*barf*</i> comment. i could see this coming. people that post tehse comments without being willing to own up to their opinions should fuck off. these sorts are the ones that are missing <i>the message</i>, the ones i was describing earlier. hey, i put extremely little stock in \\\"new-agey hippie bullshit\\\", but i still think time and trends, history and fate are undeniably powerful things, and its all some vector, moving towards something. but thats just my barfy opinion, of course.</tt>',4,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12053,2147,'2001-12-11 21:01:35',0,'adumb','<p>that wasn\\\'t intended to be mean. i\\\'m just in a bitchy mood today.  i need some treats  :\\\\</p> ',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12054,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','but its not true that new yorkers dont care what you act or look like. everyone is deeply judgemental. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12055,1099,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>egg, you realize, of course, that <i>you rock</i>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12056,1099,'2001-12-11 21:03:23',0,'critic','new yorkers <b>dont</b> care. that is, unless you can descrive \\\"caring\\\" as how it effects them. they only care about how you look if it effects their look. they only care about how you act if it intrudes into their personal space. this here is teh truth.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12057,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to critic','im not sayin who, but *someone* needs to learn the difference between \\\"effect\\\" and \\\"affect\\\". </schoolmarm>.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12058,1099,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I have to say I feel kind of bad about Darwin, mainly on account of the fact that he got me formally started with the swinneyverse last summer (I documented the whole encounter in my fanboy-like <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1120.txt\\\">first piece.</a></p>\r\n<p>My impression was that he was a stand-up guy, and I wanted to get to know him better. But our next meeting--at a capoeria demo no less--crashed and burned over an argument about biotech. (That\\\'s the field my girlfriend works in and she was there to defend it. Her basic feeling was that he needed to understand the science better--a lot better--before he argued against it.) Anyway, I\\\'ve not heard from the guy since.</p><p>But my sense isn\\\'t that this is necessarily an east coast/west coast phenomenon. I\\\'ve been running into a lot of this kind of thing lately, and it sometimes seems to be to be more of an \\\"idealistic youth vs. jaded older person\\\" sort of deal. No offense, but that\\\'s how it came off to me.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12059,1099,'2001-12-11 21:19:59',0,'moebius to egg, peter, critic','Actually, just to clear the air a bit (or make it murkier, I dunno), there was a lot of press earlier this year about how <i>SF Bay Area</i> people were just as judgemental and unforgiving when it comes to personal space, appearance, and whatnot. The bad press was (I think) a reaction to the whole huge anti-dotcommer imbroglio that mostly seems moot nowadays.',0,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12060,1099,'2001-12-11 21:27:59',0,'Peer','<br><tt>perhaps its just the simple fact that humans, as a whole, are a rather judgmental breed. heh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12061,2145,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','I have no search engine advice. I have the other advice. keep saying no. saying no is the most liberating thing in the world. when I learned to say no, my life became my life and no one elses. enjoy your vacation auntie!',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12062,2146,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','this one didnt make me laugh. but I liked it. There are never enough cigarettes in stories. And I like your description of breaktime.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12063,2146,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to dv8','<br><tt>apprenticeships. in spite of the economy, somehow construction is booming here in the city. im not exaggerating when i say that there seems to be a major construction/renovation project on almost every other block in midtown. if anyone wants to make a mint in the next 5 years or so (and isnt averse to a little- er, alot- of physical labor), starting a union apprenticeship as an iron-worker in nyc is definitely your ticket.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12064,2147,'2001-12-11 21:58:10',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ni think there\\\'s meaning in everything, you just have to know where (or how?) to look.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nit\\\'s always neat when you write something about something that really happened, and then when reading it, you find all sorts of metaphors and symbolism.</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.177','en'),(12065,2148,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i wish I knew enough html so I wouldnt have to use pre tags but I dont. its ugly huh? yah.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12066,2148,'2001-12-11 22:04:42',0,'Hyper Text Marky Mark','<a href=\\\"http://www.htmlgoodies.com\\\">We are here to help you, Gus!</a>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12067,1099,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','my favorite was when egg said, \\\"come on people! there\\\'s a war on, get it together and pick up a gun!\\\" I think I\\\'ll make that into some other writing and then you guys can all come and incite riots, we can build forts and have real guns and shoot down each others forts.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12068,1099,'2001-12-11 22:10:26',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n<b>egg:</b>\\\'some to hide?\\\' really? i don\\\'t know. i know alot of people who are just private. i don\\\'t know if i\\\'m necessarily one of those people, but...</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n<b>peter:</b> thanks :)</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.177','en'),(12069,2148,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','no. you didnt get it. what I said was that I wish I knew more html, not that I wish I could learn more. but thanks.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12070,2147,'2001-12-11 22:15:01',0,'erb','re: the happenstance symbolism thing: totally!<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwoke up today thinking about a dream i was having about someone giving me cookies that weren\\\'t fully cooked.  as i eked my eyes open, there seemed to be some meaning to the dream, but now of course, it makes no sense.  usually it takes awhile for me to figure out what my dreams are telling me.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nre: new age hippie bullshit: was thinking about this yesterday.  i\\\'m no hippie, not really anyway, but i was taking stock of my habits and realized i sort of <i>act</i> like one.  ear candling, acupuncture, chinese herbs, yoga, meditation, etc.  haven\\\'t reached the point where i\\\'m doing anything with crystals.  crystals never really appealed to me. heh.<br>',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12071,2147,'2001-12-11 23:17:44',0,'stella','I\\\'m afraid to ask, but what is \\\'ear candling\\\'?',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12072,2147,'2001-12-11 23:56:40',0,'erb','<p>something i just discovered.  i got some ear candles (long thin tubes made of canvas and coated with wax) for my birthday and kind of falteringly said \\\"gee thanks.\\\"  then i had a friend do it for me, and it\\\'s actually quite pleasant.  what you do is this: </p>\r\n<p>lie down on your side, preferably with a good movie playing in front of you.</p>\r\n<p>cut a hole in a piece of cardboard and put one of the candles through it.  the cardboard catches any falling ash. yes, this sucker\\\'s gonna burn!</p>\r\n<p>gently insert the ear candle into your ear, and hold it at a slight angle.</p>\r\n<p>light it up!</p>\r\n<p>relax for 15 minutes and watch the movie.  you won\\\'t be able to hear anything except the merrily crackling flames. and a sort of whooshing sound.  that\\\'s the gunk being pulled out of your ear.</p>\r\n<p>when the candle is burned down to about 4 inches, extinguish it and remove it from your ear.<p>\r\n<p>look inside the candle.  yes, that\\\'s YOUR ear wax!  yes, you had THAT MUCH ear wax!  look at it!</p>\r\n<p>hear how clearly sounds ring in your ear! amazing!</p>\r\n<p>repeat other side.</p>\r\n<p>yay ear candles!</p>',3,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12073,2147,'2001-12-12 00:30:07',0,'ryan','<p>\r\nstay away from crystals.  \r\n<p>\r\none night in college i stopped by my friend ben\\\'s constantly flooding basement apartment in eugene, oregon and noticed that he had crystals all over the back of his couch, on the arm rests of his chair, everywhere.  we lit up a joint and then i started moving the crystals around, swapping them, twirling them, etc.  he just about assaulted me.  i messed up his harmonic field of convergence he yelled.\r\n<p>\r\nafter that i gave him one of those armpit crystals that you see around, that are supposed to function as some sort of speedstick. it had been given to me.  i used it once or twice, then handed it over.  it\\\'s still in his apartment and i still always think of my sweaty pits when i see that armpit crystal on his table harmonizing his fields.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.162.49.87','en'),(12074,1099,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','gulf coast is #3.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(12075,1099,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','another hooray for <a href=\\\"http://www.nytimes.com/2001/12/11/opinion/11VARG.html\\\">nyc.</a> ok, i know this retrocomentary has gone on long enuf. but this essay from today\\\'s paper reminded me of the discussion, esp. the bit about fanatacism & nyc. margo vargas llosa is such a swellegant writer.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12076,1099,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','has the record for the most comments been broken yet?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12077,2146,'2001-12-12 02:29:28',0,'chia to peter','me and dv8 are canadian. it would be hard to get a construction job due to the fact that there is not a lot of external demand for such jobs. maybe tho. who knows these days. ',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(12078,2147,'2001-12-12 03:49:39',0,'or','you could try <A HREF=\\\"buttcandle.com\\\">this.</A>',1,0,0,0,'209.208.10.9','en'),(12079,2147,'2001-12-12 04:11:02',0,'wow','that was slick. please do not be afraid of my HTML prowess.<BR><BR>class, can anyone tell us what\\\'s wrong with this link?',1,0,0,0,'209.208.10.67','en'),(12080,2140,'2001-12-12 05:19:09',0,'qq','In my opinion, there should be way more crews of artists than there are these days. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12081,2140,'2001-12-12 05:22:28',0,'qq','just to clarify, i am not \\\"huge wobbly penis\\\" at all.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12082,2133,'2001-12-12 05:26:59',0,'qq','<img src=\\\"http://limpoc.com/~eric/pier.jpg\\\">',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12083,1220,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i drug rowan in the house last night to sleep in my room because it is cold outside. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nat two AM i woke up to an aweful stench.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nrowan had farted the worst fart of all time.  the room was FOULED.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni pulled the covers over my head.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthree AM i woke up because rowan was panting.\r\ni put him outside.',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12084,2149,'2001-12-12 07:21:41',0,'ryan','i really like this.  but it is something to be read aloud, not read on the page.  it is good.  cargo pants.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.62','en'),(12085,2142,'2001-12-12 07:33:45',0,'qq','I knew someone once who told me a story of her childhood. She had some weebles, the kind that wobble but don\\\'t fall down. One of the weebles always rattled as it wobbled. It made her so curious that one day she smashed that weeble to see what was inside. Inside was a large dried out beetle.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12086,2133,'2001-12-12 08:11:09',0,'ryan','<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.sadtomato.org/img/random/10-mt.jpg\\\">',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.62','en'),(12087,2149,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','you should edit your work. think about it. hold it for a day and go over it with a finer comb. ever read \\\'A Tree Grows in Brooklyn\\\'? they give her some writing advice that I want to give you. And also, edit. leave out the stuff that doesnt need to be said. hi.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12088,1099,'2001-12-12 09:53:17',0,'cyantist','<br><br>\r\n*best friendly*<br><br>\r\nno offense, but i know that most of the people here don\\\'t actually come from the bay area or the west coast.  so, if you consider anyone who has migrated here a \\\"local\\\", then by those standards everyone here is a local.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(12089,2149,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but....i like it the way it is!',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12090,2140,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i wish for more artists who exist simply to remind us all of what true beauty is.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12091,2149,'2001-12-12 12:17:16',0,'critic','er, i like it too. gus, perhaps you should edit your comments. think about them. hold them for a day and go over them with a finer comb. oh, and also: <i>leave out the stuff that doesnt need to be said.</i> youre giving me bad flashbacks of middle school english class. ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12092,2149,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>me three. this was an amusing read. i work in biotech too, and its true: they throw nice parties, for some reason. if you only knew how many times i\'ve seen the chemistry geeks morph into eloquent socialites, hearts less set on inorganic synthesis as on smoked salmon consumption, aloof bioengineers getting a little trashed; amusing.  we never have djs though. oh well.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12093,2089,'2001-12-12 13:41:11',0,'bubba','punctuation is our friend.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12094,2149,'2001-12-12 14:30:10',0,'jo','my favorite: \\\"the lesser of the ivy leagues.\\\" hahahaha that\\\'s fantastic.<BR>\r\ni liked it a lot, too, but i can see what gus means. the really kicker details would be more powerful if this were a streamlined, punchy piece.<BR>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.160.37.131','en'),(12095,2137,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>i dont like shopping so much either. i wonder if i will ever have a wife who will make me do it. it seems there are things that a female traditionally does in straight spousal relationships - remembers birthdays, keeps track of social milestones, shops for things, thinks of who will need what food, when, and how much. and the few married ladies I know also \\\"man\\\" the accounting books. what if the ladies were not on top of these things? would the world just stop working? Im going to get married, and refuse to be on top of things, and see what happens.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12096,2145,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','yes, please tell this woman to shove it up her keister. love, weebie. also, you are on vacation. go get back to it!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12097,2149,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i liked the piece for what it was. a straight-on, slightly tipsy first person account. i liked it because it reminded me of my own dumb writing efforts: rambly, silly, long and unedited, kinda autobiographical, kinda essayish. shrug. the \\\"wanna watch me shuffle off to buffalo\\\" part made me giggle, because that\\\'s how i am with people i am close with. i think that the juvenile monkey girl is the best part of me. vive la inner goon! \r\n',3,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12098,2137,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','it\\\'s nice to share the mundane chores too, and not push them off to the other partner (which is what you\\\'re doing when you refuse to do essential things like shopping for food and keeping track of your finanaces). best friendly, from a person who has had partnerships where the other person just refused to do his fair share, driving me batty. perhaps this makes the relationship unecessarily uneven? promoting an oscar and felix complex??',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12099,2145,'2001-12-12 18:19:38',0,'auntie','and a lovely vacation it is, too! i sent her to <a href=\\\"http://www.jimworld.com\\\">www.jimworld.com</a> and told her to spend some time figuring out what she wants to do and how much she is willing to pay to do it.<br><br>\r\nnow i am off for a nap, i will catch up everyone\\\'s nice articles later.',1,0,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(12100,2131,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Once, I went home with a girl who had nipple piercings.  One of them was missing in the morning and she accused me of swallowing it.',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(12101,2149,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>That was really entertaining.  I wish i was there.  It\\\'s nice to hear about another industry that actually isn\\\'t in the shit can.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12102,2149,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah, i thought this was really great. i\\\'ve been to parties like this and remember thinking similar things and cracking up at them.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12103,2149,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','jeeze! nevermind. I guess a girl cant be drunk on the ineternet at 2am and give constructive critisism anymore.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12104,2149,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','dude, home slice, monie-luv, there\\\'s nothing wrong with a little constructive criticism. i just happen to disagree in this case! hi!',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12105,2149,'2001-12-12 20:13:57',0,'javorai','damn!',1,0,0,0,'129.130.229.147','en'),(12106,2149,'2001-12-12 20:16:37',0,'critic','yep. send constructive criticism via email. its embarassing to read it here :)',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12107,2149,'2001-12-12 20:17:04',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nhi guys. thanks for the comments.\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\ngus, i have to say that i never did read a tree grows in brooklyn, so i didn\\\'t get the refernce. but i think sometimes i\\\'m more interested in the story that i want to tell, then the actual writing part. a lot of the time i just end up writing in my own voice, as though i were talking, and as long as it has a nice rhythm to it, i\\\'m happy.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut it\\\'s all good. points of view are always welcomed.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\n[best friendly]</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.222','en'),(12108,2131,'2001-12-12 20:26:20',0,'critic','Essemar, approximately 99% of your insightful contributions on this site involve interactions with a striking variety of females. From now on, i dub thee \\\"Don Juan de Essemar\\\"!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12109,2149,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','Yah, I like your voice, I wouldnt want you to change that about it. And actually I didnt mean you should make it different than it is. just thought it would have hit me harder if it was a little more pointed in places. thats all!',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12110,1704,'2001-12-12 20:29:06',0,'And the Radio Man','says, women were a curse.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12111,2142,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','a weeble beetle! ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12112,2149,'2001-12-12 20:30:53',0,'critic','mental note: design future writings to hit gus harder. check! <i>[\\\'best friendly, :)]</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12113,2142,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>at one point, circa 6 yrs old, i had both the weeble pirate ship as well as the weeble haunted house. i was a veritable <i>Weeble Mack</i>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12114,1704,'2001-12-12 20:33:32',0,'And the Radio Man','fucks a model too.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.222','en'),(12115,1704,'2001-12-12 20:34:12',0,'And the Radio Man','says, \\\"Rock and Roll LIVES.\\\"',6,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12116,1704,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>we are all going to reseda someday, to dieeeeeeeee...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12117,2150,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i was little, i had a pet hamster named gus.  Now, whenever I see and swinney-aricle by gus, images of said hamster are conjured in my mind. i think this is some sort of compliment; gus was a good pet.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12118,2150,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','next time I\\\'ll have to write \\\"pockets full of hamster\\\".',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12119,2149,'2001-12-12 20:50:09',0,'mo','i think writing in your own voice is a good idea, but you have to know how to filter some of the crap out of your story.  \r\nsometimes it makes you seem unintelligent and like a person who likes to ramble on and on about every insignificant detail.\r\n',3,0,0,0,'66.88.4.15','en'),(12120,2150,'2001-12-12 20:58:46',0,'sims','this is a good one, gus. i like it.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12121,2149,'2001-12-12 21:13:30',0,'ssj3gostu','well, you never know. i just may be unintelligent and someone who likes to ramble on and on about every insignificant detail. ',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.222','en'),(12122,2149,'2001-12-12 22:11:18',0,'critic','thats right, stoo, you tell em!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12123,2147,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i had a friend who would do ear candles all the time. and every single time she did, she\\\'d get an ear infection. her doctor was constantly pissed at her.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12124,1997,'2001-12-12 22:53:47',0,'n','my born-again parents didn\\\'t celebrate \\\"Satan\\\'s High Holiday.\\\" we had to hide in the dark and watch the neighbor kids come ring the doorbell. you\\\'d be surprised at how pissed off kids and parents would get at our house, the only dark house in the neighborhood. One time, this kid and his Dad starting kicking the door screaming: \\\"I know you\\\'re in there, come out!\\\" We were going to call the cops if they didn\\\'t leave, but they did. Later, I remember seeing the kid at Sunday school.',0,0,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(12125,2150,'2001-12-12 23:14:55',0,'qq','This made me feel concern for the canned ham. \r\nI wouldn\\\'t have expected that.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12126,2148,'2001-12-12 23:30:58',0,'Javorai','Scary way of dying.  Being burned alive.  Imagine your skin boiling, your eyes bursting and your head lighting up like a candle.',0,0,0,0,'129.130.38.133','en'),(12127,1704,'2001-12-12 23:39:23',0,'jo','gone savage for teenagers with automatic weapons and BOUNDLESS LOVE',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12128,2150,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','two things.  thing one: a canned ham becomes an inanimate object.  it\\\'s about the can, not the ham.  if it was just the ham, with no can, it would be a chunk of a dead pig.  instead, it\\\'s all about the can.  thing two: mom, please tell your story about the canned ham.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.228.7.6','en'),(12129,2147,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','yeah, that earwax is there for a reason.  I mean, too much of the good (?) thing is not good, but if it were all bad evolutionary pressures would have eventually weeded out the earwax-havers from the gene pool.  (visualization of a bunch of electrical engineers named Gene wearing green and white plaid shirts (pocket protectors), bermuda shorts, and sandals with black socks standing in a kiddie pool look at each other like \\\"ya? now what?\\\")',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12130,2151,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','ok, we get it, you checked every genre. it\\\'s been done! sphinx of black quartz hear my vow!',4,0,0,0,'165.247.214.81','en'),(12131,2151,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','sphinx of black quartz hear your vow?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12132,2151,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','this poem reminds me of a song I wrote about the gas station in jims head. pretending everything is normal so you dont fall apart.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12133,2151,'2001-12-13 04:06:06',0,'stella','I can guess what one of le\\\'s pet peeves is...',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(12134,2151,'2001-12-13 05:32:21',0,'Peeve','le is a hard task-master!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12135,2151,'2001-12-13 05:35:13',0,'Javorai','Peeve?!?',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.110','en'),(12136,2147,'2001-12-13 06:38:30',0,'jo','isn\\\'t there a drugstore alternative to this earcandle thing? it\\\'s like a chemical-y solution that you put in your ear (akin to swimmer\\\'s ear, i guess) and then you use a simple turkey baster-like suction tube to get it all out. my parents used to do this about once a year. my mom also warned against assuming that q-tips do the trick because q-tips, despite how fantastic it feels to q-tip your ear after a shower, tend to just pack the wax deep into your ear and cause <b>more</b> of a problem.<BR>\r\nah, <a href=\\\"http://www.drugstore.com/products/prod.asp?pid=12439&catid=231&trx=PLST-0-CAT&trxp1=231&trxp2=12439&trxp3=1&trxp4=0&btrx=BUY-PLST-0-CAT\\\">here it is</a>. internet to the rescue.',0,0,0,0,'172.149.20.250','en'),(12137,2136,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i don\\\'t believe in free will anymore. ',1,0,0,0,'63.10.151.123','en'),(12138,2147,'2001-12-13 09:23:42',0,'Joanna','okay, so I\\\'m disgustingly intrigued by the whole ear candle concept (as is my sister who wants to take photos of me doing it).  Where on earth does one buy ear candles from?  Or more specifically, where in New Zealand?',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(12139,2147,'2001-12-13 12:32:20',0,'Ok People','see for yourself: take two ear candles. burn one in your ear. burn one in a glass. collect the residue in the base of both, after burning, and i bet you $100 they look/smell/taste identical. thats because they are. ear candling doesnt work, at least on any significant level.<br><br><a href=\\\"http://www.quackwatch.com/01QuackeryRelatedTopics/candling.html\\\">just because its alternative doesnt mean its good</a>. ear candling has proved, several times over, to be more harmful than it is helpful (at least physiologically). tests cponducted by everyone from private consultants to a large-scale investigation by the FDA hjave all showed that its not terribly useful, and can often cause mroe complication than you can imagine (paraffin cones in particular).<br><br>\r\nthe \\\"proof that it works\\\" images you often see of \\\"accumulated wax masses\\\" in the ends of used ear candles are actually just the melted wax from the tapers themselves, not from the ear. since ear wax is highly viscous and sticky, do yuou have any idea how much suction would be required to remove it?! more than enough to rupture the ear drum, and certainly more than the simple heat-rises effect that an ear candle can provide.<br><br>\r\nso, i mean this as BEST FRIENDLY as possible, jsut trying to caution some folks. if you find that it gives you peace of mind, calms you, soothes you, or does whatever, on whatever holisitc level, more power to you.  i just advise you not to use it for actual ear-wax removal, as it can lead to some pretty sketchy consequences, especially when you do it at home, yourself.<br><br>\r\nso, dont ear candle. you probably shouldnt even use q-tips (as stated above).  go for the peroxide/oil treatments, or professional consultation.<br><br>\r\nalthough alternative treatments are great for some things, there is a reason, after all, why we have licensed health professionals, and regulatory boards.... very few of which \\\"alternative treatments\\\" dare to ever conform to. ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12140,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>um. in a much less rant-y way, i might have to offer my two cents worth and somewhat agree with the above statement. the research lab i work in has a consortium with the <a href=\"http://www.earsurgery.org/\">manhattan eye, ear, and throat hospital</a> (yes, where the woman died of the \'thrax), and ive often heard physicians rant about the evils of ear candling, and the mishaps of people fouling it up, etc, often leading to some not-so-nice keloid tissue on the ear drums of effected individuals.<br><br>\r\nfor those of you interested in making your own decisions, or who like forming opinions from logical, factual sources, here are some helpful (albeit unsolicited, heh) links from some respected medical publications and from Medline:<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/htbin-post/Entrez/query?db=m&form=6&dopt=r&uid=8849790\">Ear candles--efficacy and safety</a><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://my.webmd.com/content/article/1685.50320\">Keeping Ears Clean, Properly</a><br><br>\r\nAnd one that i think to be a most useful link:<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://buttcandle.com/\">Butt Candling</a><br></tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12141,2147,'2001-12-13 12:51:22',0,'concerned ear candler','hrmm. im kinda squeamish now, after reading \\\"A limited clinical trial (eight ears) showed no removal of cerumen from the external auditory canal. <b>Candle wax was actually deposited in some</b>\\\" from that last linked article.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12142,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>by the way. all this talk of ears got me thinking. i often wish i could see what things looked like in my ears... and nose, and mouth, and lungs, and all sorts of stuff. i mean, ive seen the text-book photos that we\\\'ve all seen, and have a general idea of what the terrain looks like in those locales, but sometimes i want to see what <i>I</i> look like inside my specific parts. not for morbid reasons, just for pure, unabashed curiosity. strange. i have a hard time getting interested in stuff like space exploration or deep-sea research when i dont even know what some of my own body parts look like.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12143,2151,'2001-12-13 14:27:15',0,'Peeve','what does \\\"javorai\\\" mean? i think its an acronym<br><br>\r\nsome possibilities:<br><br>\r\nJoined Analog Version Object Retreival Access Interface <br>\r\nJournalling Access Video Omni-Random Access Input<br>Juncture Advanced Variable Object Rate Architecture Interaction<br>Joint Array Virtual-Oriented Router Analog Interpreter \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12144,2151,'2001-12-13 15:26:46',0,'er, or','Journeying Artificial Variant Optimized for Repair and Accurate Infiltration<br>Judge Assembled for Violent Obedient Robotic Assassination Individuals<br>Jocular Android Variable and Observation Replicant Actuarial Instigation\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12145,2151,'2001-12-13 16:30:13',0,'Javorai','Person Engineered for Efficient Violence and Exploration',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.123','en'),(12146,2151,'2001-12-13 16:47:09',0,'P.E.E.V.E.','No.<br><br>\r\nPractical Enhanced Erasable Video Engine<br>\r\nPre-Electronic Engine Volume Environment<br>\r\nPortable Erasable Enhanced Viewer Equipment <br>\r\nPractical Equipment Electronic Volume Equestrian<br>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12147,2122,'2001-12-13 17:24:49',0,'Poonlarp','It was all a big misunderstanding!\r\n',4,0,0,0,'146.225.20.229','en'),(12148,2147,'2001-12-13 17:38:33',0,'ryan','<p>\r\ni have a lot of ear wax problems and must go to the doctor to get it removed  although i have safely removed gigantic pea sized brackish chunks on my own with great pleasure.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nto do this i would put a few drops of mineral oil in my ears before heading to bed each night for about a week.  this softened the wax.  then it could be flushed out with skin temp. water using one of those ear bulbs that you can get at the drugstore.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nif you are really serious about removing wax and healthy ears you should really go to a ear nose and throat specialist.  they usually flush your ears with warm water and you don\'t risk setting yourself on fire.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\r\nwhatever you do, don\'t dig around in there:<br>\r\n<a href=\"http://www.medimagery.net/ArtWork/Q-tip-Puncture.gif\">click here to see the terrifying qtip puncture photo ryan posted</a> \r\n</p><font color=\"red\" size=\"-1\">i edited this comment because the qtip picture really REALLY freaks me out. -le</font></p>',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.30','en'),(12149,2122,'2001-12-13 17:52:09',0,'ghond','lissen here bitchez: dont even THINK of invoking the ante-delapidated name of Poonlarp unless you\\\'re fuckin serious. He\\\'s come a long way since his preimminent days with the <a href=\\\"http://www.starkist.com/pouch/index.html\\\"><i>New Flavor Fresh Pouch</i></a>, you know, and just doesnt need your pedagogy.<br><br>\r\nif, however, O <i>Fond</i> Poonlarp, you have found your way to this site, personally, i do commend you. the fates must be friendly. As I always said (in that regard), \\\"<i>For external use only</i> means that Vaseline and other petroleum jellies like it are intended for very dry skin and the lips on your face, as opposed to the ones further south.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12150,2147,'2001-12-13 17:57:49',0,'ghond','forget your pea-size-brackish-chunks-o\\\'-earwax, baby, and feast your eyes on <a href=\\\"http://coke.rotten.com/five-foot-long/\\\">the 5-foot-long fecal impaction</a>. now this is <i>serious</i> shit... er, no pun intended!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12151,2152,'2001-12-13 17:58:20',0,'sims','workin on the movies, the sunset blvd home depot was my home for many months.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12152,2147,'2001-12-13 18:10:53',0,'ryan','fiber, yo.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.30','en'),(12153,2152,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I grew up down south. All the Home Depots there say <i>\\\"Tennessee\\\'s Do it Yourself Headquarters\\\"</i>. When I moved up here to the big city, I was traipsing around Jersey one day and saw it: Home Depot: <i>\\\"New Jersey\\\'s Do it Yourself Heaqdquarters\\\"</i>.<br><br>\r\nNeedless to say, I felt more than slightly violated.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12154,2152,'2001-12-13 18:26:07',0,'the eggless moment','as a man and an artist, i must say, amy, that you intimidate me.',4,0,0,0,'209.85.98.1','en'),(12155,2152,'2001-12-13 18:28:45',0,'why are you','the egg man?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12156,2122,'2001-12-13 18:33:37',0,'Poonlarp, Bestest Dolphin Ever','<img src=\\\"http://www.provalue.net/users/jds/people/poonlarp.gif\\\"><br>Don\\\'t fuck with the Lords of Hell.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12157,1704,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>gone savage for teenagers who are aesthetically pleasing; in other words: Fly',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12158,2154,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','the last two stanzas are dynamite. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12159,2156,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','&quot;quitting my job was for the most part due to personal reasons&quot;\r\n<br><br>\r\nwell? there you go.',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.173','en'),(12160,2156,'2001-12-13 19:44:50',0,'um','doing drugs and banging chicks is \\\"doing something with your life\\\"',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.30','en'),(12161,2147,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to peter','re: wanting to really see the human body, not just diagrams:<br>\nyou\\\'ll want to see <a href=\\\"http://www.thehumanbodyfilm.com\\\">this movie</a> when it comes out. i know i\\\'ll personally be lined up with the groupies on opening night. wearing my eardrum costume. whoo!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12162,2154,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','self portraits in words are cool. (if thats what this is). hi.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12163,2156,'2001-12-13 19:54:51',0,'sims','well, hot damn, if so, i sure have done a lot with mine! woo WEE',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12164,2149,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Now, I dunno about everyone else, but I\\\'m still a bit confused--why would someone who likes to call themselves \\\"critic\\\" be so against criticism? It just doesn\\\'t compute.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12165,2149,'2001-12-13 20:20:54',0,'critic','<i>[shrugs, thought this matter had died long ago. guess not...]</i><br><br>\r\ncritics <i>give</i> criticism, not <i>take</i> it.<br><br>\r\n<i>[shrugs, wonders where i ever said i was \'against\' criticism...?]</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12166,2149,'2001-12-13 20:25:36',0,'treetop','<<<<< likes parties with free drinks, food, & beautiful women.',0,0,0,0,'172.152.67.47','en'),(12167,2152,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','Over the past months and days of reading swinney I\\\'ve developed a weird sort of mental image when I think of egg. It\\\'s not based on any of the various photos of egg/Amy that I\\\'ve seen here and there. No, it\\\'s just this weird vision: of a nice white jumbo-size egg, covered with shiny metal spikes, not unlike the ones you find on punk rock wristbands or diva dominatrix dog-collars. It\\\'s a dangerous, yet beautiful object: a jewel-studded hand grenade stuffed full of art and vitriol, perhaps. (Or maybe a sister to the odd quicksilver sphere from the movie <i>Phantasm,</i> which, you may recall, spends most of its time chasing people down and drilling their brains out. It was also the star of the film.)</p><p>Of course, none of this is intended to be a \\\"true\\\" reflection of the \\\"real-life\\\" egg, whom I\\\'ve never met in person. But for now I\\\'m kind of happy to hold on to this strange, captivating egg-concept. it adds to my appreciation of her writings\\\' innate jouissance.</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12168,2152,'2001-12-13 20:57:44',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\negg, is it weird that i hear ed norton\\\'s voice (from fight club) whenever i read anything you write? i think it means that you have a very strong narrative voice.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nyour writing is the best.</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.220','en'),(12169,2152,'2001-12-13 21:14:08',0,'oeuf','oh mon dieux! <i>jouissance</i>!! you are not allowed to use lacan terms here on swinney.org, our beloved, irreverent, punk rock word shack!',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12170,2154,'2001-12-13 21:29:57',0,'jo','yeah i\\\'m awfully fond of this.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12171,2156,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>for some reason, whenever i hear the term \\\"to bang someone\\\", the image of someone thumping another person, all friendly-like, on the back, with a big ladel or serving spoon comes to mind... just like you\\\'d bang the pots and pans as a kid, or a homemade gong, or something. musical banging. harmony, cacophony, rhythm. bang bang bang.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12172,2147,'2001-12-13 21:37:03',0,'jo','peter - YES YES ME, TOO!<BR>\r\np.s. please visit again, soon. austin misses you.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12173,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmmm. somewhere in austin lies a plate of migas with my name on it. they cry, \\\"peter: eat us!\\\".</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12174,2152,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','\\\"oh yes, its very Duchamp.\\\" ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12175,2150,'2001-12-14 00:04:14',0,'tube steak','<p>Is that a canned ham in your pocket or are you just happy to see me...<p>',0,0,0,0,'172.152.67.47','en'),(12176,2152,'2001-12-14 00:04:15',0,'leonard','nice pants.',4,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12177,2152,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i love home depot.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12178,2157,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','just make sure if you are looking for serenades, you should listen to the guy\\\'s music BEFORE you get involved with him, because if you dont like it, it\\\'s quite an awkard position for you. a soggy feeling. and the poor guy who gets you has to compete with elvis...<i>i know this world is KILLIN you-ooh...my aim is true-oooh...\\\"</i>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12179,2151,'2001-12-14 02:51:59',0,'Javorai','Prematurely Ejaculating Enema Vending Endorphin',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.46','en'),(12180,186,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','what is scrotum?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12181,2157,'2001-12-14 04:59:57',0,'*','but any man, save 1 from a hogpen could serenade to elvises competition; fruition. pharamaceuticals.',0,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12182,2147,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sometimes i learn things that change my life, sometimes depressed as i assimilate the fact that you\'ve been wrong about something for all your life, but the depression is never extreme and i am a much better person afterwards, never too late to renew this emotional fluctuation.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,1,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12183,2157,'2001-12-14 05:44:44',0,'jo','i have the same problem. but my boy singers never know i exist outside of \\\"oh that\\\'s the girl that talked to me before the show\\\" or \\\"that got really excited when i started singing \\\'following through\\\'\\\" or \\\"that brought me gummy peach rings at my show\\\" or \\\"that works with the girl i\\\'m fucking and who i talked to in harvard square.\\\"<BR>\r\nfucking <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/music/\\\">skinny white boys who play the guitar</a>.',0,0,0,0,'172.136.196.244','en'),(12184,2147,'2001-12-14 05:47:32',0,'jo','also <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/2001/peter_pool/\\\">a pool table and many pitchers of beer</a>. and thrift stores and 33 degrees...',0,0,0,0,'172.136.196.244','en'),(12185,2157,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to jo','<P>lo, <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/music/upload/07_tn_omen.mp3\\\">skinny boys all the way</a>.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12186,2152,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','hate hate hate home despot.  the suck so bad only lowe\\\'s sucks more.  /me uses panzerfaust on home despot.  gonads and strife? weeeeeeeeeeeeee!!!!',6,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12187,2152,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>I always figured that Home Depot was Kmart\\\'s equivalent in the home-repair world, and Lowe\\\'s was WalMart\\\'s equivalent, for some reason. i think it has to do with their colors.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12188,2152,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the home depot outside of sf used to be open 24 hours, and i would wander around the aisles at 3 and 4am just for kicks. now they close at 11pm, but oh, how i long for the old days.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12189,2152,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to s5','<br><tt>i think thats because home depot has its own special type of <i>white noise</i>. too-tall shelves, smooth cement floors, perpetually room temperature, buzzing PA system, lights, sounds, smells, and <i>colors</i>. it puts on a show whenver you walk in.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12190,2157,'2001-12-14 12:58:40',0,'trouble','holy shit.  the skinny boys?  anybody remember that crew?  super-j, shock the shock and the human jock box.  i need to upload that somewhere.<br><i><b>play for me jock box, play for me jock box.... puh-leeeeeze?</i></b>',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(12191,2158,'2001-12-14 15:29:49',0,'loud vinyl?','did the diner have the screechy vinyl?  the stuff where, if you\\\'re wearing denim, it sounds like the call of the wild if you so much as shift one buttock?',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12192,2121,'2001-12-14 16:11:47',0,'note','<a href=\\\"http://www.msnbc.com/news/670119.asp?0dm=N12PN\\\">Dec. 13</a> â€” Osama bin Laden says of the Sept. 11 hijackers: \\\"All they knew was that they have a martyrdom operation, and we asked each of them to go to America.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12193,2092,'2001-12-14 16:23:57',0,'lin','<p>I remember that night at the bridge tavern, if I remember correctly, the foodthere was terrible, or well, whatever I had was terrible.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12194,1472,'2001-12-14 16:57:59',0,'no one to ghost','<i>\\\"and have yet to discover the man who truly adores my gender bending disposition\\\"</i><br><br>\r\nwhy oh why cant i meet more women like this?! \r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12195,2156,'2001-12-14 19:01:36',0,'curious','what comes to mind when you hear \\\'gang bang\\\'? a group of ladels in leather jackets?',0,0,0,0,'207.202.199.22','en'),(12196,2156,'2001-12-14 19:23:54',0,'critic','gang bang = peeps in south central having a crips v. bloods shootout.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12197,2147,'2001-12-14 20:12:57',0,'erb','hi.  the effects of the candles were quite subtle, but not harmful to me.  maybe some people really do damage to themselves.  but i don\\\'t think it was a big deal.  i would say try it before you decry it as quackery.  i think (having done it three days ago)that it will not rupture your eardrum.  however, i appreciate the conscientious health concerns of swinney peeps.\r\nok',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12198,2147,'2001-12-14 20:16:39',0,'','try it before you decry it as quackery, yes. i feel like im coming down with a cold. perhaps its time to go to the apothecary and have him let some blood? or, i hear some great cutting-edge shamen are doing good things with leeches in germany.  then again, i might settle for my usual cure-all, a heavy dose of Dr. Wowzer\\\'s Amazin\\\' Heal-All Praise-Jesus Snake Oil.<br><br>\r\n[best friendly. sorry, i just couldnty resist!]',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12199,2156,'2001-12-14 20:20:39',0,'erb','peter, i love what you said.  i used to have this record as a kid that required you to play along with pots and pans and a big wooden spoon.  i had a lot of fun banging and clanging and thunking along to that record.  i had totally forgotten about it until i read what you wrote.  also: \\\"banging\\\" as a term for sex has always puzzled me, too.',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12200,2158,'2001-12-14 20:47:54',0,'mo','Wow.  This is a beautifully written story, Peter.  ',2,0,0,0,'66.88.4.15','en'),(12201,2158,'2001-12-14 20:54:14',0,'critic','wow, indeed. this is really a powerful piece of writing. that last paragraph is a real killer. its heartbreaking, somehow, and made my chin quivver.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12202,2156,'2001-12-14 21:46:07',0,'sureÃ±o','BLOOD',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12203,2158,'2001-12-14 22:25:36',0,'stella','<p> huh. I popped into swinney at LEAST 10 times today and each time didn\\\'t read this. now I\\\'m kicking myself in the butt for it. I could\\\'ve used this this morning. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12204,2158,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','\\\"flowing with hissy salsa music\\\" made me think of \\\'the fan man\\\'. the rest of it didnt. I like it. do you miss the idea of the diner more than the girl? Thank you for describing the diner. I like descriptions of diners. ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12205,2158,'2001-12-14 23:09:56',0,'Brooklyn','he misses the girl; i think the diner made him thnk of her every day walking home.  he does not miss me much, though, i hear, at least beyond my museum, garden, and bridges.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12206,2149,'2001-12-14 23:16:33',0,'dug','why am I teaching soccer?  i should be teaching  public speaking!',0,0,0,0,'12.146.203.167','en'),(12207,2156,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','when I hear \\\"gang-bang\\\" I think of a bunch of hoods all taking turns raping one woman. I dont like the sound of it. ya know? yah.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(12208,2156,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to erb','<br><tt>ME TOO! oh my god, how weird is that? i think thats why i have the bang-bang-bang imagery that i do. my mom made me a \\\"special gong\\\" out of an old cake-pan.  she punched a hole in it, braided a big coil of golden yarn thorugh the hole for a carrying-loop, and gave me a tennis-ball headed mallet to bang on it with (hmm, or to mute it with, perhaps). BANG. BANG. BANG.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12209,2149,'2001-12-15 01:25:59',0,'ssj3gostu','because you\\\'re a pretty lady?',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.128','en'),(12210,2158,'2001-12-15 01:31:24',0,'ssj3gostu','peter, i loved this. really really well written.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.128','en'),(12211,2151,'2001-12-15 03:06:01',0,'Javorai','Possibly Exhumed Example of Volatile Excrement',0,0,0,0,'129.130.229.70','en'),(12212,2151,'2001-12-15 04:33:55',0,'Peeve','ok, jav. you can stop now.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12213,2156,'2001-12-15 07:05:51',0,'trouble','i\\\'m going to take the comments to new lows here but... when i hear <b>\\\'bang bang bang\\\'</b> i can\\\'t help but think of an eddie murphy movie where in one scene it finds him talking to his best friends father (same actor who played cubes\\\' father in \\\'friday\\\') and the father has gotten word of eddie\\\'s character being in love or something and he proceeds to school him:<br><br><i>friends dad - boy, what\\\'s this i hear about you being pussy whipped?</i><br><br>eddie\\\'s character tries to shrug him off by laughing<br><br><i>friends dad - don\\\'t you know you gotta whip that pussy?</i><br><br>then proceeds to make the obligatory fucking motions while he yells <b>bang! bang! bang!</b> with each thrust.  i know i prolly fucked up the entire scene but even fractured like my version is it still makes me smile thinking about it.',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(12214,1790,'2001-12-15 07:17:49',0,'camboy to egg','i read that you were wanting to see some man ass. there are plenty of cam guys out there willing to share...\r\n\r\n<p><a href=\\\"http://www.doctorgrosz.com\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">doctorgrosz</a><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.cockybastard.com/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">cockybastard</a><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.jewishcheerleaders.com\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">jewish\r\ncheerleaders</a></p>\r\n\r\ncourse that last one doesn\\\'t have a cam up, but he\\\'s got plenty of quicktime action happening.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.114','en'),(12215,186,'2001-12-15 19:30:35',0,'we','can show you better than we can tell you.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(12216,2160,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','nice. thanks.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.71','en'),(12217,2160,'2001-12-16 01:54:05',0,'Borges','I thought of a labyrinth of labyrinths, \r\nof one sinuous spreading labyrinth \r\nthat would encompass the past and the future \r\nand in some way involve the stars. \r\n',3,0,0,0,'64.171.51.5','en'),(12218,2162,'2001-12-16 02:23:29',0,'lulu','let\\\'s just get one thing straight mr. excitement, just because i\\\'ve got bags the size of utah under my eyes, and i soak my liver in bourbon every night, and then jack myself off to sleep, does not mean that i\\\'m depressed.  on the contrary, i am merely in a state of absolute bliss, austin swiney of texas.  and in fact, in my state of bliss, i\\\'ve come to discover some fascinating things, like...the older i get, it seems that parents become more and more absurd and outrageously entertaining in their antics and philosophies.  it offers much food for thought during my forty minute drives to the local grocery store. however, by the time i arrive at the store, i seem to end up asking myself the question, \\\'why should i feel guilty about not spending time with the folks?\\\'  i mean for pete\\\'s sake, they raise your sorry ass to become a capable person so that you can lead a good life and be happy, and all the while, they\\\'re constantly yelling at you as they\\\'re changing your diaper to go out and get a job so that THEY won\\\'t have to keep buying MY huggies.  and then kindergarten rolls around and they\\\'re chewing your ears off about how when THEY were five years old when THEY had a part time job tutoring math and spinning yarn.  and when you finally grow up and leave home and get a job and an apartment, all they want is for you to come home and visit...\\\'just visit sometime, what\\\'s wrong, you don\\\'t like mommy? you don\\\'t like to see mommy? aayyy, what kind of lousy daughter are you?  HOW COME YOU DON\\\'T LIKE MOMMY?  WHAT DID MOMMY EVER DO TO YOU?  MOMMY LOVES YOU!\\\'  and then you end up moving back home, and the impending guilt only gives you strength and maybe even a little integrity when your parent comes into your room while your sleeping and rips the covers off you and belts \\\'GET UP LAZY BONES\\\'.',0,0,0,0,'209.178.153.179','en'),(12219,2162,'2001-12-16 06:37:34',0,'','I guess I see my Dad about once a year - and have for the last 20 years or so.  But each visit is  very special time together - whether it\\\'s a sailing trip, camping, hiking, 6 day canoe trip, or just hanging out for a few days at a holiday.  One time we spent a week at a tennis camp.  He is very good at organizing family get togethers - -  rented condos in aspen, and the Smoky Mts ...  So once a year doesn\\\'t seem bad - cuz I also get together with my Mom once a year - then there\\\'s the in-laws at least once a year - and there\\\'s only so much vacation. As I think about it just about EVERY vacation is spent with one family member or another.\r\n\r\nBut I call my 3 sisters and both parents each week,  My Dad sends a family update e-mail each week (includes what he\\\'s up to and a summary of Sunday night calls he receives).  Sends us pictures of what he\\\'s up to.   We play on the web (spades or backgammon).\r\n\r\nI think once a year isn\\\'t bad if that once a year is qualty fun time - something special - and you stay in touch in between.  (And Austin - we appreciate your check-in calls).\r\n\r\nA motorcycle trip with your Dad sounds like a blast.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'207.136.49.137','en'),(12220,2162,'2001-12-16 09:40:32',0,'bmg','my dad and i are meeting in houston this tuesday... i am very happy to see him... he travels to austin once a month or so just to see me... i feel lucky, i hope you get to see your dad more... ',0,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(12221,2160,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i like this.',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12222,2158,'2001-12-16 15:55:23',0,'critic','i just came on to read this again. it deserves two reads. or three. its very poignant.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12223,2158,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yeah. it is great.',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12224,2161,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','great story. ive read it a few times. its too bad its not linked on swinney. I like the fact that swinney content remains on swinney. if this story was to change locations, it would be forever lost on swinney.org. that is my only complaint. ',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(12225,2163,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','and then you sobered up and had to go out and shovel it. hahahaha ETOBICOKE IN THE HOUSE!',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(12226,2164,'2001-12-17 08:34:31',0,'east of tension','and north of corruption. nice',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.13','en'),(12227,2166,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Three things that always give me pause when I see them tossed out for the trash:<br><br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>Stuffed Animals\r\n<LI>Photographs\r\n<LI>Copiuous boxes from <i>new</i> technology\r\n</UL></tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12228,2162,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<p>heh... parents... familial commitments... I don\\\'t see my family as often as I should.  I really don\\\'t.  I feel guilty about this whenever I see them which makes me not want to visit.  I also just have a lot of other shit to do.  Such is the world.  I\\\'ve been fantasizing lately about a little house by a river in a small town.</p>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(12229,2166,'2001-12-17 17:14:37',0,'discarded','velveteen rabbit',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.151','en'),(12230,2166,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','there was a puddle in the alley behind thirdage that I used to make zach take pictures of in different seasons. I really loved this puddle. I kinda miss it and I cant find any of the photos.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.71','en'),(12231,2167,'2001-12-17 19:03:46',0,'critic','o how beauty is only skin deep!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12232,2167,'2001-12-17 19:34:16',0,'lin','<p> I know this article is a little cheesy with the ending message, but whatever, some of it is really significant to me</P>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12233,2166,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i love you man. that is brilliant postmodernist writing. you should send that in to the New Yorker or The Atlantic Monthly.',1,0,0,0,'65.140.49.140','en'),(12234,2165,'2001-12-17 19:44:39',0,'qq','For true men draw breath from their heels, the vulgar only from their throats. Out of the crooked, words are retched up like vomit.  When man\\\'s attachments are deep, their divine endowments are shallow.\r\n\r\n(chuang-tzu, transl. lin yutang)\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12235,2167,'2001-12-17 19:59:49',0,'erb','<p>wow! there is a lot of feeling in what you wrote!  as for cheesy, well, i myself am a big fan of cheese.  sometimes you have to dig down past the cheesy layer to get to the good stuff, anyhow.  as long as you\\\'re being true to what you feel, more power to you.</p>\r\n<p>hi critic!</p>',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12236,2162,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i guess it\\\'s good we didn\\\'t go over to my parents\\\' house for pork tenderloin, because then you would be seeing my dad, the meat-master, more than your own dad.  mmm, pork tenderloin.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12237,2162,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>yo lulu</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12238,380,'2001-12-17 20:29:31',0,'sims','my most recent best fortunes:<br>\r\n1. a man\\\'s best possession is a sympathetic wife\r\n<br>\r\n2. you will attend a party where strange customs prevail<br>\r\n3. you will advance socially.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12239,380,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i am much more excited about the cookie than the fortune.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12240,2167,'2001-12-17 20:49:57',0,'beware','<i>\\\'the ones who only get close so they can push you away\\\'</i>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.118','en'),(12241,380,'2001-12-17 20:51:08',0,'sims','but they\\\'re always so stale and whatnot',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12242,2167,'2001-12-17 20:52:26',0,'face','i don\\\'t think this article is cheesy at all. it speaks from the heart. it holds pain and wit and hope. \r\n\r\n<p>it has presence.</p>',3,0,0,0,'32.102.64.118','en'),(12243,2167,'2001-12-17 21:28:44',0,'erb','i don\\\'t think it\\\'s cheesy, either, for the record.  i was just relating to the fear that what you posted is cheesy.  i should have said, i feel like i have to dig down past the initial cliches/sentimentality/other surface modes of expression before something really truly juicy comes out. it takes practice to have faith in your writing. i always appreciate the support i recieve here, as well as seeing other writers supported.  it opens up that old rusty writing valve, having a lively audience. \r\n\r\n*warm fuzzies*\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12244,2167,'2001-12-17 21:40:35',0,'lin','<p>thanks, im still in that small vulnerable stage with my writing. i write a lot, but dont have a lot of criticism, so i always wind up disclaiming things.</p> \r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12245,2167,'2001-12-17 22:30:26',0,'hool','<p>being alone is good, important, and healthy.  you will be constantly unhappy if you don\\\'t learn contentment with solitude, because everyone is impermanent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>self confidence is magnitudes sexier than a pretty face, because it attracts people for reasons that will do you true good.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(12246,2167,'2001-12-17 22:37:28',0,'sims is a creep today','nice tits don\\\'t hurt either! heh',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12247,2167,'2001-12-17 23:13:49',0,'face to hool','',0,0,0,0,'32.102.64.190','en'),(12248,2167,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','solitude is important, but we are social beings. There is always that biological urge to pro-create and make some healthy offspring getting in the way of being forever all alone. (whether or not you actually want babies, your body is going \\\"do it do it do it!\\\")\r\n<p>\r\nTrue enough that self confidence is attractive, and in the long run, perhaps it holds people together, but boys still judge girls (and girls, boys) on their looks to start out with. Thats all there is in the first few minutes.',0,0,0,0,'216.175.109.100','en'),(12249,2167,'2001-12-17 23:18:52',0,'lin to hool','<p>your sentiments on being alone make a lot of sense to me in who I am. When I wrote that, I wasn\\\'t alone, now I am. Also, I feel like I work a lot better alone. I am content in being alone a lot, because it\\\'s the only way I ever feel like I get anywhere. I sort of have this goal of meeting someone I can be alone with, if that makes any sense.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12250,2162,'2001-12-18 01:35:47',0,'lulu','hey adamajah,<br>\r\nCall Me!',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(12251,2166,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','i always stare at peoples\\\' shoes...\r\nis it wrong to try and figure out what a person is like from their footwear?',1,0,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(12252,2167,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','<p>Someone once said, <i>\\\"When I find that person I\\\'ll spend the rest of my life with, it won\\\'t be someone to take the lonliness away.  It will be someone to share it with.\\\"</i>  Those words I heard so long ago really stick in my mind.</p> <p>Right now, I feel like a casualty on the battlefield of love, and I get that same sense from the person I\\\'m with.  We\\\'re together because it\\\'s convenient; it\\\'s better than being alone.  To her I\\\'m a pretty face on a sexy body housing a mind that\\\'s intelligent enough to be a tolerable dinner companion.  To me she\\\'s a respite from crawling the clubs hunting a body to keep me warm for at least one night.</p><p>I used to have a crush on her.  I thought I was in love with dreams of marriage and domestic bliss.  Maybe those thoughts could have lingered or developed into something more real were we not so cynical.  Damaged goods.</p>',2,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(12253,2167,'2001-12-18 14:46:23',0,'critic to essemar','ive learned- the hard way- that <b>usually</b> (dont flame me for saying this!) meaningful relationships dont start via one night stands and club-trawling. perhaps a good start for a healthy and meaningful relationship might come via different means than youve been trying, or been used to?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12254,2167,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Dear lord Critic!  Why the hell would I flame you for saying something that\\\'s so true?<p>Someone once told me that I\\\'m really a woman in a man\\\'s body.  I tend to get emotionally enthralled with people who shouldn\\\'t be more than a pair of tits and an ass to me.  Of course my first one night stand -ever- turned into a two year relationship.</p><p>Anyways, I was friends for months with the girl I\\\'m with right now before things became romantic which gives me hope.  Things are progessing <i>very</I> slowly, a first for me.  Typically, my relationships start with some girl staring at me with puppy dog eyes so long that I finally ask her out, and when we go out, she\\\'s only interested in getting me naked.  That or someone comes up to me in a club and says, \\\"I\\\'m going to take you home and fuck the shit out of you.\\\"</p>',1,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(12255,2167,'2001-12-18 15:31:32',0,'critic','i dunno. i always found it curious how so many guys i know complain that they dont have nice, rewarding long-term relationships- meanwhile, theyre out at clubs like every week in search of one night stands. its a basic cause and effect thing. maybe its that we want more, but we\\\'re too impatient and hormone-filled to ever get it.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12256,2158,'2001-12-18 15:38:05',0,'Herr Doktor','I get an interesting sense of place from this. for me, specific places have always carried different \\\"personalities\\\" or emotions. strangely, brooklyn has always existed, in my mind, much like the brooklyn written about here- kinda drab, kinda hopeless, even though its sunny and warm... even though ive never been there. odd. only a few specific places have an emotional place in my mind. brooklyn does for some reason, especially after reading this. the <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2074.txt\\\">detroit</a> piece had a similar effect on me. detroit is my hometown, though. i like writing that is about places. perhaps its just that swinney spans such interesting/good places.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12257,2167,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','critic, are you aware that even though you comment prolifically on this site, you have yet to post an article?  I was going to send you an email, but I can\\\'t find an address.<p>Anyways, I dig your comment about impatience.  I\\\'m so used to whirlwind romances that I\\\'m always looking for that instant intimacy with people.  Waiting for things to \\\"develope\\\" frustrates me.</p><p>Oh, and Lin, how would you feel about posting a jpeg of your \\\"pretty face?\\\"  Just curious.</p>',0,0,0,0,'198.45.19.20','en'),(12258,2167,'2001-12-18 16:46:26',0,'critic','critics comment. writers post. trust me, even if i did write, you wouldnt want to waste your tiem reading it.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12259,2161,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','They are thinking.',3,0,0,0,'64.229.64.230','en'),(12260,2162,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>LUUUUULUUUUUU!!!!  How was that?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>necesito tu numero de telefono.</p>',1,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12261,2169,'2001-12-19 04:12:22',0,'errrr','is there somethihg here i dont get? i dont get this <i>at all</i>. damn alienating posts!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12262,2170,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i read \\\"ass job at the bookstore\\\", you cant imagine what snippets ran through my head... i momentarily conjured mental images  of Walmart-esque greeters, baring ass at the bookstore, most likely in the kids section, aiding customers, reaching for books, etc... heh. what i really want to say, though, is props for the Dear Asshole letter. i wish i had the balls to do likewise.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12263,2169,'2001-12-19 04:17:16',0,'critic','yea, it must be some vague inside thing. i dont get it either.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12264,2169,'2001-12-19 04:26:18',0,'its the revenge','of <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/userpages.phtml?username=green\\\">green</a>.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12265,2170,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i think i lost it due to the unfairness of being treated like shit when i tried to be nice to him during this pathetic drama. it\\\'s weird, i think the tone of the letter is really mean, but the facts behind are perfectly true. anyway, i\\\'m over it, and calmer now. he\\\'s the one who has to go back to that fleabag of a bookstore, not i! huzzah! i buy you all virtual drinks.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12266,2170,'2001-12-19 04:56:17',0,'critic','not me, thanks- i have a virtual drinking problem!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12267,2170,'2001-12-19 05:18:51',0,'ssj3gostu','<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nyeah, that letter was great. if i had a favorite line, it would definately be \\\'Scott\\\'s Life According to Scott (As Interpreted by Scott).\\\' or maybe \\\'Don\\\'t make them regret being nice to you. Try not to call them from jail.\\\' if i could pick two favorites, those would be them.</p>\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nbut yeah, i\\\'ve deffinately been there with the shitty jobs. but it always seemed to me, the worse the job, the better it feels to be done with it. the post quit high is deffinately a nice thing.</p>',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(12268,2170,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','well sheeit. thanks for trying to get me to work there weeb! haha! I guess it would be a bad idea, though now I could probably get better hours eh? yipe yipe. if you get lonely, I can think up some problems and you can come over and organize my bookshelves and I can give you gus\\\' life according to gus\\\' (as interpreted by gus) for at least 2 or 3 hours.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.71','en'),(12269,2170,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','weeb, you\\\'re so cool. the letter you sent was amazing. more people should have the chutzpah and integrity that you have.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12270,2168,'2001-12-19 07:39:26',0,'addendum','<p>six hours later, it\\\'s all magically fixed somehow.  no scuttling will be necessary.  this is the second time that once things got stark enough for me to write about them, the situation somehow repaired itself.  whatever works.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nactual subject line of spam just received: <pre>Your life could be simpler.</pre>\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'209.86.219.79','en'),(12271,2168,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','re: scuttling\r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen yeti wakes up in the morning and goes around the bedroom looking for clothes and the day\\\'s CDs, he sometimes says \\\"yeti scuttle scuttle\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nre: titanic\r\n<br><br>\r\ni change the channel right before the iceberg hits. i don\\\'t like the disaster part, only the falling in love part. \r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12272,2169,'2001-12-19 12:13:25',0,'Javorai','Don\\\'t give me none of that jibba-jabba.',1,0,0,0,'208.52.53.141','en'),(12273,2169,'2001-12-19 12:29:19',0,'Herr Doktor','i should probably keep my big yap shut, but when items like this pop up on this site, i find it a bit baffling.  so. you took something that didnt make much sense in the first place and posted it here- although not under the name/title/email-address that was requested by the original author, you posted it under <i>your</i> name (ok, some vague connection to the \\\"self indulgent\\\" tangent here, maybe, but still). although none of that makes sense as the person could/should have just posted it themselves anyway, as they obviously had access if they emailed it to you. maybe i just dont get it. maybe im not supposed to. but, it seems like a real shame that much higher-content stuff- you know- stuff much higher on the readability/redeeming-virtue scale- gets edited out or non-posted all the time... and then something like this gets tossed out to baffle us. strange. no offense, essemar, as im sure this has a place somwhere, somehow... i\\\'m just making an observation here. [x] destabilizing',4,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12274,2169,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i rather liked it, for the same reasons i liked <a href=\\\"http://arctic.org/~dean/sircam/\\\">this</a>.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12275,2169,'2001-12-19 12:47:07',0,'ooh yes','gotta love random glimpses into people\'s sordid <a href=\"http://newyork.craigslist.org/w4m/2425013.html\">lives</a>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12276,2169,'2001-12-19 12:53:13',0,'Herr Doktor','now that you mention it... i too am attracted to random <a href=\\\"http://www.jamazon.co.uk/index.php?article=61\\\">slice-of-life</a> shit.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12277,2169,'2001-12-19 13:04:29',0,'Computers','\\\"Today the computer is a way of life. Most families own at least one computer in their home. We use computers for business, education and for Internet access. The Internet has exploded in these last couple of years. Soon computers will control the whole American system.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12278,2170,'2001-12-19 14:00:45',0,'trouble','i completely agree with s5, this was outstanding.  i will live vicariously through you if only for a moment or two and imagine my quitting my current job in a similar fashion.  best of luck on your new endevour whatever it may be. ',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12279,2170,'2001-12-19 15:22:27',0,'jo','weeb, you kick lots of ass.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12280,1475,'2001-12-19 15:26:09',0,'critic','peter, there is some unfair power in your writing. it hits me below the belt but <i>makes me like it</i>. ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12281,2168,'2001-12-19 17:29:52',0,'erb','that\\\'s funny.  i watched titanic too, and i have to say, kate winslet is hot hot hot. i like the whole thing (despite the silliness and leonardo\\\'s annoying hair-tossing) but due to an inability on the part of everyone else to stomach cheesy mush, i was forced to turn off the falling in love part and only return when the disaster had begun.  i sat there and folded laundry and cried thin wet tears, ignoring the heartless derision and leo-bashing from all sides.  so horrifying, the thought of all those people dying in the water.  when i was 9, i remember listening to a \\\'70th anniversary of the titanic\\\' radio show, with all of these very old survivors recounting the tale.  the way they described it was even more creepy--in real life, it wasn\\\'t all lit up, so there was just this eerie greenish glow from what lights were still lit on board, and screams and prayers all around in the dark freezing water.  much scarier than the movie.  ',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12282,2170,'2001-12-19 17:31:17',0,'stella','\\\'Don\\\'t make them regret being nice to you. Try not to call them from jail.\\\' is definately one of the better lines that I\\\'ve read recently.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12283,2170,'2001-12-19 17:54:15',0,'erb','i wrote a letter like that once, to a (much older)man (a director) who had tried to get me to sleep with him during an audition.  after i sent it, i felt good but also kind of weird.  even though i felt much better and less victimized after writing the letter, i wasn\\\'t sure that i had helped to decrease the chances he would pull his act again. as for this sorry ass bookstore owner, i feel sorry for him, in the same way i felt sorry for that director--in an icked-out kind of way.  we may not be able to change them, but we can tell them our truth and hopefully at some point it will sink in.  congratulations, weeb, for getting free of the weirdo headtrip it musta been working for this guy.  brava!',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12284,914,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<br>&lt;egg&gt; wayne<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; where is the <a target=\"window\" href=\"http://www.links.net/vita/sf/scoot/thirty/pix/group3.lg.jpg\">picture from your birthday party</a><br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; what picture<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; the group photo at justins house<br>\r\n&lt;simon&gt; hi<br>\r\n&lt;simon&gt; what\'s up<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; oh justins site somewhere   <br>                                   \r\n&lt;wb&gt; http://www.links.net/vita/sf/scoot/thirty/<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; look at all those fucking freaks<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; i was just clicking on old swinneys<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; are you nostaligic amy?<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; totally<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; for what and why<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; you miss may of this year already<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; is it because you met austin that day and you are nostalgic<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; for the early days?<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; HA!<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; no, i miss his orange vest<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; and le\'s funny hairstyle<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; its a fashion nostalgia<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; goats hair looked good then too<br>\r\n&lt;egg&gt; hey i forgot youre thirty!!!<br>\r\n&lt;wb&gt; thanks so did i<br>\r\n&lt;simon&gt; hello',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12285,914,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','when i look back on pictures of myself during that hair, i feel it was a mistake i will never forget. but i am about to go get more extensions braided in, in half an hour. ',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12286,1218,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','reading this and i was just at the ruby room on monday. what a bathroom they have in that joint.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12287,2170,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','yeah. i felt really bad for him too. when i returned his books, all i felt was pity, so i pocketed the letter and called it quits. but when i got home to his lame voicemail, i lost my temper and delivered it anyway. *shrug* it\\\'s weird because i think only 40% of me wanted to do it, but i did it anyway. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12288,2170,'2001-12-20 15:24:33',0,'jo to weeb','i do that. i\\\'ll carry out a plan even if i realize in the final stages of it that it was the absolute worst idea in the world. i can\\\'t help it. i <i>commit</i> to my terrible ideas.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12289,2170,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','that\\\'s funny. i can\\\'t even commit to my good ideas. i guess i have the opposite problem.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12290,2149,'2001-12-20 20:00:14',0,'f','what the f*$k are you talking about?',0,0,0,0,'66.88.4.26','en'),(12291,914,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to le','where are the photos of your new hairdo?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12292,2170,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<p>Sounds like this fellow was a real asshole.</p>\r\n<p>To be honest with you, I think that your letter should have either been constructive, or insulting, but not both. I came away from reading it with the feeling that if I was the one who recieved the letter, that I would disregard the constructive parts because the rest of it felt snide and angry.</p>\r\n<p>I\\\'m not saying that you arent justified in your wrath, but I do think that you pretty much ruined your chances of making any positive impression on this guy.</p>\r\n<p>I imagine you realized this and that is what kept you from handing it over in the first place. :)</p>\r\n<p>I wish I still had copies of all the similar letters I\\\'ve written.</p>\r\n<p><i>best friendly</i></p>',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12293,914,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to egg','<a href=\\\"http://elly.org/foto/caitlynhair/\\\">right here</a>',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12294,2162,'2001-12-21 04:27:17',0,'sic','its weird...i just had this urge to check up on you...i keep thinkin about you and your black bean burritos...if you swing on by we can go to the bars...i owe you a drink or two',0,0,0,0,'172.183.53.181','en'),(12295,2170,'2001-12-21 05:41:19',0,'Joanna','Weeb, so it\\\'s not really related to this particular article or anything, but I saw on your journal site that you\\\'re planning an NZ trip, so if you ever do, PLEASE look me up.  I think you\\\'d be awesome to meet, and I haev a very comfortable couch that everyone seems to sleep on.\r\n\r\nThank you, that is all.  ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(12296,2140,'2001-12-21 05:43:59',0,'sub','artists don\\\'t <i>remind</i> you of what true beauty is, art <b>is</b> beauty. <i>graphic designers</i> remind you what beauty is, and where the product is available.\r\n',6,0,0,0,'204.29.203.35','en'),(12297,2170,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to yeti','<p>\r\nyou totally hit it on the head. i was angry, and i wanted to be mean... but at the same time i didn\\\'t. when is it justified to react in such a manner? i don\\\'t know. i wish i didn\\\'t send it after all, actually. i wish i were the kind of person who could let it go and be able to close the door on that kind of situation with dignity. most of the time i can. but this was just one of those times where i reacted vindictively. however justified in some way, it still makes me think less of myself. in the end, maybe that\\\'s worse than feeling badly about someone else\\\'s behavior. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12298,2170,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to joanna','yes yes yeeeeeeeeesssssssss that sounds <bold>lovely!!@!</bold>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12299,2140,'2001-12-21 08:50:54',0,'jim','graphic designers also remind you where to stick it.',6,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(12300,2140,'2001-12-21 12:21:23',0,'to sub','yeah yeah, art is beauty. and im beautiful, so im a fuckin work of art. arghh... sweeping generalisations. must. die.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12301,914,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to le','\\\"during that hair\\\" <- *makes appreciative noises*',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12302,2171,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i ran in to mrs. russel at a wedding recently.  mrs. russel was my favorite teacher of all time.  in fact, this eighth grade english teacher was just about everybody\\\'s favorite teacher.  my favorite memory from that class was when me and chris liu had a cartoon battle on the chalk board.  it was my fred-the-caveman-and-family versus his calvin and hobbes-esque characters.  we never had tolkien as part of our curriculum though.</p>  ',2,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12303,2169,'2001-12-22 01:26:24',0,'THE POPE','NEFTONG is SUCH a SLUT!!!!',6,0,0,0,'198.81.17.182','en'),(12304,2080,'2001-12-22 07:01:27',0,'alanna to Gotsu and Scott','i personally wouldn\\\'t believe a word that came out of either of your mouths.  the difference is that gotsu never claimed to be telling the truth.  swinney.org is not for only truth.  gotsu seems like a writer.  writers do not only write truth.  be that as it may, scott seems to be thinking that he\\\'s telling the truth.  but he\\\'s full of bad, confronational, pissy vibes.  and he\\\'s on gotsu\\\'s front lawn yelling at him to get off his grass.  peh.  i think scott should quit taking women and \\\"the truth\\\" so seriously and do some writing of his own.  not just personal shit either.\r\n\r\n\\\'course, who cares what i think. =P',4,0,0,0,'198.182.157.98','en'),(12305,2149,'2001-12-22 07:03:58',0,'alanna to gostu','s, i wish i\\\'d met you more than once at the park and once at denny\\\'s.',2,0,0,0,'198.182.157.98','en'),(12306,2173,'2001-12-22 07:19:06',0,'city-life boy','someone is on the ball',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12307,2173,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/desk.JPG\">',1,0,0,1,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12308,2165,'2001-12-22 16:53:32',0,'dahlia','<p>http://www.spiritualitytoday.org/spir2day/884025chandrak.html</p>',1,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(12309,2173,'2001-12-22 18:37:12',0,'chaka khan','i feel for you',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.31','en'),(12310,2173,'2001-12-22 21:39:04',0,'treetop','<p>zoom zoom zoom in my four cornered room</p>',2,0,0,0,'172.147.209.215','en'),(12311,2173,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','tits',0,0,0,0,'203.117.145.102','en'),(12312,2171,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','you make me feel old. I dont even know who was IN my 8th grade class anymore.\r\n<p>\r\nmy 8th grade english teacher wore tight polyesther shirts and showed us a video of his dogs acting as humans. he was a big giant nerd. I graduated from high school in the 80s. \r\n<p>\r\nmy favorite english teacher (a different guy) taught us the same curiculum when I was in 10th grade as he was teaching at the jr college. he made us smart.\r\n<p>\r\nHe also kicked the underside of our desks a lot and said \\\"THE ANSWER IS NO! ... what was the question?\\\" when we raised our hands. he was one of the best teachers I ever had.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.175','en'),(12313,2173,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i loved a city once<br>\r\nand it left me for a better skyline<br>\r\nwith nothing but a rain filled gutter<br>\r\nand an already smoked butt.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.175','en'),(12314,2173,'2001-12-23 11:35:54',0,'treetop','<p>high on a housetop\r\n<br>surrounded by a glitter\r\n<br>a bright orange light shines\r\n<br>\r\n<br>ignights our chillum\r\n<br>in the deep vaste spark of night\r\n<br>puff puff give to you</p>',0,0,0,0,'172.170.249.229','en'),(12315,2176,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','the guy you described sounds like Swan, the old one that lives in the doorway of adobe books. he\\\'s the one who makes daily edicts on a rented typewriter, and photocopies them for the general populace. he campaigns for the pidgeons and rats in them, comparing our pest exterminations with the holocoast. his stuff is occasionally fucking brilliant. i like him lots. he has a pet pidgeon(s). i imagine them following him around and makes beeping noises, nuzzling into his collar with affection. ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12316,2176,'2003-05-10 16:09:14',79,'yeti','<p>That guy is known in my house as the bird man. We\\\'ve contemplated him on many occasions. We always wonder if we could work a deal out with him to call the pidgeons down to next to cars of people who are disliked. A swirling mass of pidgeons and feathers. When gone, the surrounding area is left with a peculiar stench and a whole lot of pidgeon shit.</p><p>For some reason, I always like seeing that man.</p>',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12317,2176,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','why did you think of Juice Newton? ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12318,2175,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yay! incitor!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12319,2176,'2001-12-23 21:05:12',0,'gus to egg','Lookin\' for love in all the wrong places. (except that isnt really juice newton. so it makes no sense. so really. um. i dunno why. sorry.)',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.175','en'),(12320,2175,'2001-12-23 23:10:36',0,'yessir','incitor\\\'s back in the house, kicking ass and taking names.  i expect articles by green, jamazon, griece, and darwin won\\\'t be far behind.  or are incitor and griece the same person?',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.150','en'),(12321,2175,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','griece was actually lulu, and then quinn became griece, and i think the name \\\"griece\\\" was retired.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12322,2175,'2001-12-24 00:38:44',0,'to yessir','...i\\\'ts people like you that drove the good ones away with the bad.',1,0,0,0,'32.100.71.252','en'),(12323,2170,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb\\\'s bro','rotflmao!\r\nok weeezie, don\\\'t forget packrat/kangaroo for\r\nxmas! KIT yu fookin\\\' fozzi...',0,0,0,0,'12.88.85.18','en'),(12324,2176,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','was it eddie rabbit? no, google says it was mickey gilley.',0,0,0,0,'203.117.145.102','en'),(12325,2175,'2001-12-24 01:58:44',0,'oh','boo-hoo.',1,0,0,0,'209.208.9.216','en'),(12326,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>hmm...not what i expected.  can\\\'t really say what i expected.  can\\\'t really say why i posted again.  bored, alone, wanted to write, but couldn\\\'t think straight enough for fiction; wasn\\\'t inspired to write a poem.  what the fuck.  swinney.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nequating me with griece.  sad.  i\\\'m real.  sad as that is.  i left here upset that too many weren\\\'t real.  now i don\\\'t care if that\\\'s true.  some like what i have to say, some don\\\'t.  it\\\'s no different from anyplace i go.  i\\\'m not in this thing to be popular.  or loved.  or even happy.  i\\\'m in this to survive.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nif you\\\'re one of the few who like to read whatever comes down my paths and out my fingers...stay tuned.  i\\\'ll be around.  unless some so wish as to silence me.  if you don\\\'t like what i type...  don\\\'t read it.  or if you\\\'re to fucking stupid to help it.  fuck you.\r\n</p>',4,0,0,0,'151.201.28.78','en'),(12327,2175,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i don\\\'t really like what you say but i like the way you say it.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12328,2175,'2001-12-24 02:33:18',0,'if','you look at \\\"yessir\\\"\\\'s comment, you\\\'ll notice that it can be interpreted in at least two ways. the first is: \\\"oh, shit, here comes that steroid-freak incitor again, spewing his aggression all over our serene website. i suppose this is a portend of fellow pains in the ass green, jamazon, griece, and darwin popping back up on the radar sometime soon.\\\"<BR><BR>or you could view it as \\\"yessir\\\" saying: \\\"yay, incitor, with his bold and incisive attitude toward life has posted again after a long absence. maybe fellow MIAs green, jamazon, griece, and darwin, who all disappeared from swinney roughly around the same time incitor did, will post again soon as well.\\\"<BR><BR>perhaps we can view \\\"yessir\\\"\\\'s kind of oblique, objective comment the second way. sometimes it\\\'s nice to give people the benefit of the doubt.<BR><BR><I>best friendly</I>',1,0,0,0,'209.208.5.81','en'),(12329,2170,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to bro','WOW it\\\'s my brother!@! no one else would know the nicknames for the parents... welcome!!! i didn\\\'t forget, i sent a package... did you get it?!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12330,2170,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow. WOW. ladies and gentiles, that was an appearance by my much beloved brother william! *sounds of crowd going wild* i am so proud. he\'s such a gangster. he even has a coke finger.',1,0,0,1,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12331,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>sometimes it\\\'s nice to crank off too.  but i won\\\'t do it in or on swinney.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>hehe.  steroid freak.</p>\r\n<p>why anyone would like the way i write what i do...that\\\'s insane.  if i could write, i\\\'d have been published more than thrice in my life, and might have even made some dosh.</p>\r\n',4,0,0,0,'151.201.28.78','en'),(12332,2175,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','who the hell is yessir?',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12333,2176,'2001-12-24 10:11:12',0,'hi','i like your use of the word \\\"shit\\\". also, if you ever have the chance, take the G train in NYC to the Myrtle/Willoughby stop. Walk around for a bit. Perhaps north to Bedford ave. Be sure to put your hood up, and walk quickly.',3,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12334,2174,'2001-12-24 10:14:57',0,'hi','you should be a rapper. flow it.',3,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12335,2175,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','where do you live again? it\\\'s not much better in california. you should dig up footage of LAPD \\\"riot\\\" control for a sample.<br><br>\r\npersonally, i believe that people are less violent when there are fewer of them trying to occupy the same amount of land.',3,0,0,0,'202.76.149.119','en'),(12336,2175,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>s5, youre right; america still loves its manifest destiny, and people seem to prefer to expand outward instead of upwards. thats why its so funny living here on this little island i call home; people act funny. they\\\'re probably lightheaded from their 6th-floor-with-kitchenette walkups on 138th street. urban sprawl is the answer, endless urban sprawl. manifest destiny is nothing unchecked mixed residential/industrial zoning cant cure.</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12337,2179,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiam','just like being dubya!',1,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(12338,2171,'2001-12-24 18:59:24',0,'OTIS','Good times.. good times indeed.',0,0,0,0,'64.228.119.51','en'),(12339,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>i live in pittsburgh.  and i\\\'m leaving for many reasons beyond this event.  this was recent.  i know many good cops here.  these days, as out society becomes less and less civilized, there\\\'s no place to go where cops are going to be wonderful.  this was just a further impetus to go, as the title suggests.\r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'151.201.30.92','en'),(12340,2178,'2001-12-24 20:15:13',0,'cyantist','i would like to see you as you are and in any which way you come.  darwin and i love you and we want you out here.',1,0,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(12341,2175,'2001-12-24 20:37:59',0,'cyantist','<br>\r\ni\\\'m so glad you are coming, and steve is right, san francisco is not the rest of california.  you will be at peace here if you relax and let the calm soothe you.  most people here are gentle.  \r\n<br>\r\nsorry, this post is going to be unpleasant... \r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen i lived in phoenix, i went through a similar experience and this brought back the bad memory of that night.  my friends and i were having coffee and we were just about to head home when two horse cops came up to us and asked my friends for id.  for some reason they ignored me.  i started to ask why they were questioning my friends and the horse cop batted at my head with the head of the horse and i ducked.  scared, i stood to the side and watched my horror unfold.  they pinned eric to a newspaper stand and removed his wallet without his permission after he refused to produce identity, but stated his name, birthday, and social security number to them.  chris, standing near me became outraged and i tried to pull him back into my darkness where there seemed to be safety.  we watched eric get hit in the head by the horse and fall to the ground.  an accident?  poor horse.  poor eric.  they threw him in the cop car and he came to screaming, \\\"you can\\\'t destroy my freedom, i am a citizen, and without prejudice, i withhold all of my rights\\\"  they screamed at him, and they screamed at the crowd that was now forming.\r\n<br><br>\r\nchris was no longer able to contain himself and started screaming back, and next he was into cuffs and up against the news stand.  they arrested him for disorderly conduct and false identity and about 12 other charges that were mostly bogus.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nthere was one cop near me on foot.  i walked over to him and asked as nicely as i could.  \\\"where are my friends going and can i pick them up?  why are you arresting them?\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nignoring my other inquiry, his only answer was, \\\"because we can\\\".\r\n<br><br>\r\neveryone got out many hours later, but not without a cost.  we never felt the same again after this.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nthis is just one of many stories i could tell about police brutality that i\\\'ve seen before and or have been a victim of.  \r\n<br><br> \r\none of my best friends was shot and killed by the police a few years ago, because he was drunk and heard a noise downstairs from his home.  he walked out barefoot and forgot his glasses so he couldn\\\'t really see that well.. i\\\'d imagine.  he brought a dull machete with him to protect him and began circling the perimiter of his apartment complex.  an unknown voice asked him to drop his weapon and he flailed it about and three shots were fired.  one went through the wall of a home and two hit abdiel and killed him on the spot.  he was 95 pounds and smaller than me. the cop got away with it.  in his defense, he felt scared for his life.  he didn\\\'t know what to do and with panic fired his gun.  that won\\\'t bring abdiel back to any of us, will it?  he\\\'s gone.  we all had to move, because we couldn\\\'t stand living on that street anymore.  \r\n<br><br>\r\npolice brutality is everywhere, even here.  but i must say, that i\\\'ve been harassed a great deal less in san francisco.  i\\\'ve been here two years and so far have not had a run in with the police.  i\\\'ve read stories though, and just like all cities this one is not immune.  however, it is easy to be a faceless person here and go unnoticed and i think that will be good for you.',1,0,0,0,'24.221.170.119','en'),(12342,2178,'2001-12-24 20:41:37',0,'redstripes','yes! please!',1,0,0,0,'32.102.91.193','en'),(12343,2176,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','I jest loves Swan; he\\\'s been a fixture of the 16th St at Valencia scene for years. \\\"Swan\\\" is the shortened version of his name, by the way--formally he goes by \\\"Lone Star Swan.\\\" And he has a few alternate names, like \\\"Messiah.\\\" I\\\'ve been fascinated by him for quite some time. When he first came to my attention (around \\\'93 or so), he was living in the abandoned carcass of a Volkswagon bug at the corner of 16th and Albion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>He\\\'s still making photocopies, by the way. A couple weekends ago I found that the good folks at Cafe Macondo (directly across the street from Mission Grounds & Adobe Books) have set up a tray for his newsletters next to the coffee cream & sugar fixin\\\'s.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Other folks love Swan too; if you do a search on his extended name you\\\'ll find a few web pages dedicated to him. Like <a href=\\\"http://www.spectacularopticals.com/Pages/Photo_Pages/AgentWorld_Pages/AW_01.html\\\">this one</a>. Or <a href=\\\"http://involution.org/swan.html\\\">that one</a>.</p>',0,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12344,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','',0,0,0,0,'151.201.30.92','en'),(12345,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>\r\ncyan my friend.  i\\\'m glad to be coming, if worried a bit.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni think that people are misunderstanding my view of what happened.  i don\\\'t and will never consider what the police did that night to be brutality.  everyone came out with lumps and bruises and cuts.  even i had a few bruises.  but it could have been worse.  by final count, there were in that room at the peek, over 15 police.  my primary concern is the quality of the police force in this city.  the mayor\\\'s policies relating to policing.  the fact that the chief of police is hated by his subordinates, so much so that he was kicked out of the union.  in general i am concerned less with brutality, which can be fought.  and more with the police state mentality that not only infects our law enforcement officials, but also ourselves.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAmericans keep asking the police to fix problems we wish to otherwise ignore.  we beg for more and more laws to bind us.  the result is a police force with too much power, and a populace incapable of behaving.  it results in \\\'us vs. them\\\', which leads to your terrible night in phoenix.  when young people challenge the police, they are setting themselves up.  they are moving from one operating system to another.  the reason i have so rarely had trouble with cops, is that i know how their operating system works.  i can safely (most of the time!) challenge them without  fear of being harmed or arrested.  i can look them in the eye as an equal.  and i also appreciate them, love them even.  i shake hands with cops who are doing their job right.  whenever i move to a new place, i seek out the officers in the area.  make my face known to them.  create some sort of dialogue.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe saddest part of your friend\\\'s death, is that the cop was right to do what he did.  and your friend was right to do what he did.  although the self defense instructor side of me of course thinks your friend made a few poor decisions.  it\\\'s a terrible thing.  and nothing can fix it.  but we can always try to learn and to change.\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'151.201.30.92','en'),(12346,2177,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','you might love them again one day. you might not. dont forget to leave yourself open to the possibilities. maybe you\\\'ll understand them better later, whether or not you want to. who knows. ',0,0,0,0,'64.163.215.175','en'),(12347,2177,'2001-12-25 02:02:05',0,'jo','also it\\\'s weird how opinions about family members change as you get older because you start out loving them just because they\\\'re family and you love your family, and then you get older and see that they\\\'re also people who are very flawed and sometimes manipulative and petty and let you down a lot, and then hopefully when you\\\'re a lot older you love them just because they\\\'re family and you love your family.<BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t say i\\\'d gotten to the \\\"lot older\\\" stage but i\\\'m working on it.',0,0,0,0,'12.238.225.165','en'),(12348,2177,'2001-12-25 05:30:07',0,'the thing is','<p>just because you happen to be born into a family doesn\\\'t mean you have to love them.  i think you make your own family, and sometimes blood relatives will fit into the category of People You Love Most not so much because they\\\'re the people you\\\'d pick to be around but because there\\\'s so much shared history there and because they know you so well that it doesn\\\'t matter.</p>\r\n<p>sometimes they\\\'re just assholes, though, and that doesn\\\'t really matter either.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.110.193.71','en'),(12349,2179,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1471.txt\\\">i was in vancouver in july</a> and i loved it there. i camped at the bottom of a glacier, by a fresh water stream. i want to go back.',1,0,0,0,'202.76.149.11','en'),(12350,2175,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the problem with the cop mentality is the notion of \\\"law enforcement officer\\\". their job should be to serve and protect, not to enfore the law. the law, when followed carefully, can easily be used to do shitty things to people - people who would otherwise rather be left alone.\r\n<br><br>\r\ntoday we were talking about living on a small island in the south pacific and seceding as our own nation. wouldn\\\'t that be nice? we could hold elections every year for silly things, like deciding the official state sponsored method for eating mangos.',3,0,0,0,'202.76.149.11','en'),(12351,2177,'2001-12-25 07:38:09',0,'lou to jo','<P>so that would be nice, but i have never ever subscribed to the whole \\\"you love them just because they are your family\\\" deal. ever.  which is almost depressing, but i want people to love me because i am me and because they love me, not because we\\\'re tied by blood.  and i feel the same way about other people.  and even though there are a lot of people i am related to who i wouldn\\\'t ever know or talk to because we are related, i still like and respect them and get along with them and like to see them and be with them and appreciate the ties. and then there are the ones who are totally creeps who i could never see again and wouldn\\\'t feel bereft.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and most of the people i saw tonight, unfortunately, fall into the latter category. and it makes me sad because i feel like i am missing things that other people get, like having a great and cool uncle who is only 20 years older than me who i used to really look up to and now can hardly stand to look at.  because it\\\'s not just his politics and religion and creep-ness, but also the way that he\\\'s treated me.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>and i don\\\'t think that people who treat other people like shit should be excused for it just because the people they are treating like shit have blood ties to them.</p>',6,0,0,0,'165.247.39.7','en'),(12352,2175,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','<p>s5: a friend of mine here, a cop actually, said pretty much the same thing.  she claimed that the biggest problem she faced in her job was that she felt police should be trained to and encouraged to use discretionary powers, rather than required (and trained) to simply enforce the letter of the law at all times.  she was one of the best cops i\\\'ve known in many years.  liked to hang out and play chess with punk rockers while drinking coffee.  i distinctly remember her coming into my work one night, crying, because she had been reassigned to a new district.  she was so upset because she new the area she was leaving so well, and felt that because she knew everyone and how she could best help them, she was more effective. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nand i agree.  sadly, most community oriented police programs i\\\'ve seen around the country, are considered by the police, to be a punishment job.  while she was never assigned to a C.O.P. unit, she had the concept down wonderfully.  and many locals i know missed her when she was gone.\r\n</p>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'151.201.30.92','en'),(12353,2177,'2001-12-25 17:46:21',0,'jo','i didn\\\'t mean that you should excuse bad behavior just because they\\\'re family. an aunt of mine, my father\\\'s older sister, is a cruel harpy who has ruined every holiday for everyone unfortunate enough to have to spend it with her. she\\\'s made my cousin karen, who was <b>my</b> idol growing up and an incredible woman, feel like an awful daughter by routinely showering guilt trips on her, and i loathe her for it. <BR>\r\ni think i\\\'m referring more to the fact that i myself have stopped looking to my family to be role models or saviors because they\\\'re <b>not</b> my friends, they\\\'re not my chosen circle, but i can see also that although i would never choose my sister-in-law as a friend, she <b>is</b> a great mom to my niece and nephew. so i love her for that, even when she occasionally does and says things that make me wish i could shoot laser beams from my eyes.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'12.238.225.165','en'),(12354,2179,'2001-12-26 02:33:22',0,'lin','<p>I remember feeling that way about things when I first moved out on my own. I had to make little incriments in my life in order to avoid a total shock. I know that eventually I\\\'m going to miss feeling small, but for now I can still achieve that every now and again, when I go to a new place.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.141.198.82','en'),(12355,2180,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','nice pics.',1,0,0,0,'202.76.149.30','en'),(12356,2180,'2001-12-26 07:11:37',0,'confused','is that \\\"bass\\\" as in \\\"teh fish\\\" or as in \\\"goes with treble\\\"?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12357,2177,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','blood is cheap.  love is earned.',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12358,2180,'2001-12-26 17:03:41',0,'bmg','its bass, like bass... however you like.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(12359,2180,'2001-12-26 17:04:20',0,'bmg','thanks s5.',0,0,0,0,'66.90.152.5','en'),(12360,2177,'2001-12-26 19:00:51',0,'lou to orion','amen.',1,0,0,0,'207.237.128.1','en'),(12361,2163,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','flugwar is making a come-back.\r\n\r\nare ya ready boys?!',4,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12362,2129,'2001-12-27 02:20:23',0,'ashton','<P>stripes mark the milliseconds of time without a noise other than the occasional click click click when the driver begins to nod off and the car moves slowly on an arc out of true.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>bup bup bup</P>\r\n\r\n<P>then when the driver wakes up</P>\r\n\r\n<P>bup bup bup</P>\r\n\r\n<P>and wonders off into the thoughts that disolve driving time.</P>',3,0,0,0,'66.25.38.246','en'),(12363,2180,'2001-12-27 03:04:46',0,'confused to bmg','no, no relativity! need. concrete. answers!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12364,2179,'2001-12-27 03:31:09',0,'treetop','  Owwww, were you on Mt. Seymore?',0,0,0,0,'172.166.203.108','en'),(12365,2180,'2001-12-27 03:35:28',0,'treetop','thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump, thump,& etc...',0,0,0,0,'172.166.203.108','en'),(12366,2184,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','abject terror and starvation.',3,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12367,2183,'2001-12-27 05:57:35',0,'hool','what evolutionary book were you reading?',0,0,0,0,'206.186.174.42','en'),(12368,2184,'2001-12-27 06:30:42',0,'Javorai','witty?  the tale is horrific.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.111','en'),(12369,2184,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','you\\\'re not getting enough MOZART. \r\n<p>\r\ni say, devote yourself to helping other people. don\\\'t spend so much time wrapped up in yourself. what makes you so goddam important, right? ',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.162','en'),(12370,2174,'2001-12-27 10:02:18',0,'lulu','barney?  is that you?  ',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(12371,2183,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','_The_Triple_Helix_',3,0,0,0,'151.201.31.210','en'),(12372,2184,'2001-12-27 17:23:06',0,'erb','i was thinking about this last night.  we <i>can</i> look forward to death.  whether it is just eternal rest or a reunion with the light,or something altogether unimaginable, it\\\'s nice to think that the pain we experience here will end.  kind of like knowing that summer vacation is coming...someday.<br><br>\r\nthen again, i do think there is meaning even in the pain.  as interchangeable as we may seem, we are all unique and beautiful.  and we all have something to contribute, even if it\\\'s just another variation on our evolutionary vector.<br><br>\r\nmaybe that goes without saying.\r\n[best friendly] ',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12373,2181,'2001-12-27 18:52:15',0,'Bean','Wow, I really <i>REALLY</i> like this piece... it\\\'s so dramatic, but not in an obnoxious manner. More like something you\\\'d see in a dream sequence, or a French film... It\\\'s so hopelessly romantic, and so unbearably innocent at the same time. Good grief, I hope (for your sake) that this was non-fiction and that you both somehow find each other again.',3,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12374,2184,'2001-12-27 19:36:45',0,'stella','<p>my friend is a phychiatrist, with a penchent for brain studies. he has a half a brain that he studies for fun, slowly dissecting and drawing. he also knows a lot about chemicals that live in and control the brain. mainly chemicals that deal with emotions. he tell me that the \\\"white light\\\" that so many people testify to seeing in a near death experience are merely certain chemicals in the brain firing off certain neurons triggered by the expiration of the body. </P> \r\n<P> I don\\\'t know anything about brain chemicals. this is an interesting theory.</p>\r\n<P> your post is interesting. based on what you say, I would suggest to spend your hours doing anything and everything that makes you content, happy, or gives you even the slightest thrill. after all, if we have no obligation, what difference does it make? practive hedonism.</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12375,2184,'2001-12-27 19:44:18',0,'','<p>tibetan buddists would tell you to stay away from the white comfortable light.  that\\\'s the path to rebirth.  each death is an opportunity to escape rebirth - dualism - desire.  to the author: get thee to a monastery, you need to get your monk on. </p>',0,0,0,0,'206.186.174.22','en'),(12376,2184,'2001-12-27 19:46:01',0,'hool','tibetan buddists would tell you to avoid the white comfortable light.  it\\\'s the path to rebirth. each death is an opportunity to escape rebirth - dualism - desire.  to the author: get thee to a monastery.  you need to get your monk on.',0,0,0,0,'206.186.174.22','en'),(12377,2184,'2001-12-27 20:17:40',0,'hool','i had technical difficulties so i seem to have posted twice.',0,0,0,0,'206.186.174.19','en'),(12378,2183,'2001-12-27 22:56:13',0,'hool','have you read \\\'Godel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid\\\' ?  the title you were reading reminded me of this book. ',0,0,0,0,'206.186.174.51','en'),(12379,2180,'2001-12-27 23:19:42',0,'mightybluj','it\\\'s bass as in \\\"Bass Masters\\\" the old fishin\\\' show on PBS.   Boy howdy does the BMG love his fish...',3,0,0,0,'204.64.42.245','en'),(12380,2183,'2004-02-03 19:35:30',533,'incitor','yes, i was in the hospital for around two weeks maybe five years ago.  and my doc let me borrow his copy so i wouldn\\\'t die of boredom.  sometimes it gave me a bit of a headache, but then i couldn\\\'t put it down.',3,0,0,0,'151.201.31.179','en'),(12381,2183,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','wow someone actually read godel escher bach? i thought that was one of those books people only ask each other if they\\\'ve read, when they haven\\\'t read it themselves. like naked lunch.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12382,2174,'2001-12-28 03:40:59',0,'Mr.','Barney, as in the big purple dinosaur?  Nope, sorry.  No Barney here.',0,0,0,0,'172.141.249.249','en'),(12383,2183,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i actually read <i>naked lunch</i> once. i dont reccommend it unless you\\\'re ok with non sequiturs and are in a particular state of mind. me, i was just bored.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12384,2184,'2001-12-28 04:42:20',0,'cogitoergoglum','<p>I like the suggestion to <i>get my monk on.</i> Very nice. Probably, not believing in any higher authority or order of any kind, I\\\'d need to find some monkish equivalency. Maybe that\\\'s what geekhood is about. </P>\r\n<p>I was surprised by the reaction thus far, to be honest. I expected a lot more accusatory comments along the lines of \\\"sophomoric claptrap\\\" and the like. My wail was a bit juvenile, I admit, but it\\\'s an honest feeling I can\\\'t shake these days. </P>\r\n<p>As for finding something that gives me pleasure because if nothing matters then... well, that\\\'s missing the point, isn\\\'t it? What do animals do? They just exist, and don\\\'t worry about their place in existence. Why do we know that? Because we worry about our place in existence. We can\\\'t be simply animals without denying our self-consciousness in a wholly unselfconscious way. If we know we\\\'re merely distracting ourselves from meaninglessness by engaging in meaningless acts, it\\\'s not going to work, is it?</p>',0,0,0,0,'32.101.37.85','en'),(12385,2185,'2001-12-28 04:46:50',0,'tree^','Last night I had a dream that I was eating rice dream and corn flakes.  I dreamed that it was really good.  So, this morning I got some rice dream and corn flakes to make my dream come true.  It actually was quite tasty.',0,0,0,0,'172.172.240.237','en'),(12386,2184,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','dude. meaning isn\\\'t just going to come to <i>you</i>. you have to seek it out, create it. it\\\'s up to you to make your life worthwhile. ',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.101','en'),(12387,2183,'2001-12-28 14:28:48',0,'stella','add another to that list of people who read \\\"Godel, Escher Bach\\\". Not me though. My man read it. Now he\\\'s reading a book on neurophilosophy... ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12388,2184,'2001-12-28 16:14:23',0,'pokey','<p>\\\"There are two ways to live your life.  One is as though nothing is a miracle.  The other is as though everything is a miracle.\\\"  - Al Einstein  <br/>\r\nTake this (and everything) as you will.  Good love.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'67.201.151.141','en'),(12389,2187,'2001-12-28 18:44:10',0,'jo','dude, props for taking joy in the details.',0,0,0,0,'172.150.223.185','en'),(12390,2187,'2001-12-28 18:46:59',0,'jo','also this reminded me of something i wrote a long time ago about <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/dream/\\\">the rain and candlelight</a>. ',0,0,0,0,'172.150.223.185','en'),(12391,1790,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to camboy','',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12392,2180,'2001-12-28 21:04:53',0,'bonus cup','Thanks to the BMG, sleep is now optional',1,0,0,0,'65.56.175.84','en'),(12393,2187,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>pretty: <i>\"rain, like a million diamonds, glittered in shards on your sleeve\"</i></tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12394,2183,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I watched the movie for naked lunch, and made a mental footnote to read the book.  That was six years ago, though, so that footnote\\\'s getting a little yellowed and crinkley.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12395,2185,'2001-12-28 23:14:25',0,'erb','<p>there sit next to me<br>\r\nthree iridescent golden boxes<br> \r\nsomething out of a greek myth.<br>\r\nthey fit one into the other like a snail into a shell<br>\r\nthe light shines off in diamonds<br>\r\nrainbows wiggle<br>\r\nlike fingers on keys<br>\r\nthere is nothing inside the largest box except more boxes. the smallest box is empty<br>\r\nbut should contain some treasure.  some idea at least.<br></p>\r\n<p>i dreamed last night of a  honeycomb house in pinks and mints<p>\r\nof five romanian sisters, dark haired, purple eyeshadow and green eyes<br>\r\none more beautiful than the next<br>\r\na room full of silver mirrors, wavy and unclear. each frame unique, gentle scallops.<br>\r\na swanky baby grand was in one of the pink or minty rooms<br>\r\ni caressed the silky ivories without making a sound.<br></p>\r\n<p>outside,<br>\r\nbeautiful sky, expanse of ocean and away across a city, a skyline, i lumber in--<br>\r\na bay full of roughskin black sharks bumping heads to get to me, frothing surface of teeth<br>\r\nooh my bare legs scissored through the clear water\r\nand back up the shore<br>\r\naway from the horizon i had been longing for.<br></p>\r\n<p>maybe the journey i had meant to take<br>\r\nis in these golden boxes<br>\r\nsitting nestled so innocently<br>\r\nand so full of possibility.<br>',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12396,2177,'2001-12-29 00:42:18',0,'dactyl','also, i think that an important part of the story was left out.\r\n\r\nyour family (well, mostly your father\\\'s family) is crazy.\r\n\r\nand i don\\\'t mean in that way that all families are crazy.  these people are crazy in that \\\"need treatment as soon as possible\\\" sort of way.  they\\\'ve all got an addiction to something (religion mostly) that they need to deal with.  and that craziness makes it harder to deal with them.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.39.7','en'),(12397,2183,'2004-07-09 19:12:25',72,'essemar','Yeah... I was home for Christmas for a while.  It was fun to bump into the people who were \\\"cool\\\" back in the day and see that they are still stuck in the crappy little town we grew up in.... huh...',0,0,0,0,'216.99.207.215','en'),(12398,2184,'2001-12-29 10:08:12',0,'kona','<p>existential angst, i feel, is a good thing to re- experience every now and then. read some camus, nietzsche, kierkegaard (well maybe not soren, as he tends to base many thoughts/arguements on G-d and you mention not being the G-d type.), sartre, socrates. tends to make me understand but not, feel inspired and in tune yet agonizingly seperated. \r\ni eventually teeter-totter myself to, what for me is, a middle balanced ground.</P>\r\n\r\n<p>taking individual responsibility for knowing who you are and for knowing where you stand on existential, ethical and religious issues. knowing what you believe and standing in your own little patch of (for me, forest green) truth.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mmmmmmmm. tasty. pass the peanut butter.</p>',0,0,0,0,'12.224.61.142','en'),(12399,0,'2001-12-29 10:08:55',0,'','',0,0,0,0,'12.224.61.142','en'),(12400,2186,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i think you should quit the band.',3,0,0,0,'203.202.116.174','en'),(12401,2188,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','the first time i met austin, he refused to talk to me, and talked to peter instead, about video games.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12402,2186,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i learned last year in professional writing that the accepted thing now is to address the letter to the position itself, like: Dear Human Resources Director, or Dear Personnel Manager...i know, it sounds stupid. also NO INDENTING PARAGRAPHS. ',1,0,0,0,'65.140.48.204','en'),(12403,2188,'2001-12-29 15:05:09',0,'jo','the first time i met austin was at a small cafe on the other side of a steep hill in a san francisco. steve and i walked to meet yeti and austin there and from there, we walked around the city, stopping for coffee again later. or maybe i am mixing up days.<BR>\r\ni was struck by how self-satisfied, small-boned, and soft-spoken he was.<BR>\r\nbefore reading this article, i didn\\\'t know you meant \\\"swinney sightings\\\" as referring solely to austin; i suspected that you meant any swinney denizens and i thought it would be like on the makeoutclub or suicide girls boards: \\\"i think i saw elly drinking lemonade on saturday! teehee! elly, was that you? you are <b>so hot</b>!\\\"<BR>\r\n(no offense to either moc or sg kids.)\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.186.64.39','en'),(12404,2186,'2001-12-29 15:15:31',0,'curious','is sugarcircle the female version of a circlejerk?\r\n\r\n[x] best friendly',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.115','en'),(12405,2188,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','self satisfied! \r\n<br><br>\r\nonce austin showed up at my house and took me to the airport. i had pockets full of archie mcphee toys so i put a rubber banana slug on the little rod that adjusts the rear view mirror. later he moved to santa cruz. i hadn\\\'t slept in a really long time when that day was happening.\r\n\r\n\r\n<br><br>\r\nsomeone sent me mail yesterday, asking if i was weirded out at the thought of random people recognizing me from sg. i\\\'m not. maybe i should be.\r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12406,2188,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','I tried to talk to austin at wayne\\\'s birthday party. he sneered at me and turned away. what humiliation. ',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12407,2188,'2001-12-29 19:12:11',0,'jo to egg','(it\\\'s okay because after the party when we got in the car, i said something about how beautiful you were and he agreed, enthusiastically.)<BR>',0,0,0,0,'172.171.46.159','en'),(12408,2186,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','if it is, someone should take pictures',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12409,2186,'2001-12-30 01:41:16',0,'erb','that\\\'s sugar<b>cicle</b>, you sicko perv.  but i like the way your mind works, alot. ',3,0,0,0,'64.171.51.5','en'),(12410,198,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','i love the world, and i love you.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12411,2184,'2001-12-30 05:55:07',0,'steph','apparently, you need to go see vanilla sky and get some perspective. that movie will change your whole sense of reality and your perception of life as you know it.',3,0,0,0,'64.12.102.42','en'),(12412,2186,'2001-12-30 06:23:28',0,'Grand Poobahs','we can make sandwiches!',0,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12413,2184,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','existential angst. you know, I find a few things that help. one of the things is beer. another is not thinking so much. the other is getting over yourself. I highly recommened all three things. The last two come with age, beer is available to all with either ID or a friend with ID. work with what you\\\'ve got.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12414,2186,'2001-12-30 10:10:17',0,'erb','dude i LOVE that song...you can be he burger...and i\\\'ll be the bun.  i just got back from a sandwich makin\\\' hot and sweaty club orgy...yeaaaaa release that sex-ule tension baby.  steamy steamy do you like reggae music?  uh, not enough to let you freak me.  ok.',0,0,0,0,'64.171.51.5','en'),(12415,2189,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>that\\\'s funny, i almost can imagine myself converting to any religion, but that belief thing gets me every time. i\\\'m into the pageantry and hoopla though. i like sparkly things and trees and stories about wrath, sin, and retribution. and elephants. the stories aren\\\'t so bad. \r\n</p><p>\r\nbut i think i\\\'ll just stay in my cozy little corner of finicky atheistic smorgasborg gorging, and buffet, thank you very much. picking and squeezing out the parts i like best, leaving the rest of the bonbons/hor d\\\'oeuvres  on the platter for other dinner party guests to stare at in revulsion. yum yum.\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12416,1716,'2001-12-31 06:34:54',0,'pokey','<p>\r\nGetting stoned is to me as rebooting is to my computer.<br/>\r\nOr so I\\\'ve noticed.  Sound familiar to anyone?\r\n</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'67.208.129.118','en'),(12417,1716,'2001-12-31 07:07:39',0,'rebooting','bmg has said that before',4,0,0,0,'32.100.71.72','en'),(12418,2191,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','<b>Bacchanal</b>\r\n<p>Lashes shudder, leaving trails <br>\r\nof traceable shadowings.<br>\r\nThe moon makes dancing <br>\r\nmore necessary than possible.<br>\r\nIf only they knew why <br>\r\nwe ran into the darker  woods,<br>\r\nvoices calling eagerly<br>\r\nto one another.<br>\r\nThen they might begin<br>\r\nto lock their doors at night.<br>\r\nEspecially now-<br>\r\nmight notice the urgent<br>\r\nunrattling, anxious calm<br>\r\nand tip-toe into the basement,<br>\r\nturn off all the lights and pray.<br>\r\nEspecially now-<br>\r\nwhen the moon is full.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>circa sometime inearly \\\'90s</i></p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12419,2190,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','it\\\'s been the worst year of my life too.  and the best.',4,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12420,2190,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think actually the bad parts were because it was the year of the snake.  snakes are no good for horses.  next year is the year of the horse, however, and i look forward to it rocking out completely.  bring on chinese new year.',4,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12421,2189,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i read the koran many years ago, and it seemed to me that muslims are meant to defend islam, to the death if necessary. which is different than attacking in the name of islam. but the difference between attacking and defending is often lost during the heat of conflict. all in all, i find muslims to be good people.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12422,2190,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<a href=\\\"http://www.chineseparade.com/\\\">february 23, 2002</a>: lunar year 4700, year of the horse. chinatown, san francisco.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12423,2184,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<p>\r\nLao Tzu was traveling with his disciples and they came to a forest where hundreds of woodcutters were cutting the trees. The whole forest had been cut except for one big tree with thousands of branches. It was so big that 10,000 persons could sit in its shade. \r\n</p><p>\r\nLao Tzu asked his disciples to go and inquire why this tree had not been cut. They went and asked the woodcutter and they said, \\\"This tree is absolutely useless. You cannot make anything out of it because every branch has so many knots in it - nothing is straight. You cannot use it as fuel because the smoke is dangerous to the eyes. This tree is absolutely useless, that\\\'s why we haven\\\'t cut it.\\\" \r\n</p><p>\r\nThe disciples came back and told Lao Tzu. He laughed said, \\\"Be like this tree. If you are useful you will be cut and you will become furniture in somebody\\\'s house. If you are beautiful you will be sold in the market, you will become a commodity. Be like this tree, absolutely useless, and then you will grow big and vast and thousands of people will find shade under you.\\\" \r\n</p>',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12424,2184,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','but...life rocks!  imagine how boring the afterlife is going to be!  this is the party, yo!!',6,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12425,2075,'2002-03-14 23:24:58',123,'dcc to chiamatt','<p>see, i had a short stint with civIII as well. but after playing a 10-hour straight game, i realized that it\\\'d be best if i stopped playing it altogether. i really forgot to eat 2 meals and missed the part of the day where there was sun outside.</p>\r\n<p>but damn, was that game sure compelling to play.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12426,2181,'2001-12-31 16:41:04',0,'hool','thanks.  the story is non fiction, and i may return to vancouver in the summer, so i\\\'ll let you know if i see her again.  it\\\'d be nice.',0,0,0,0,'64.229.162.85','en'),(12427,2190,'2001-12-31 19:05:56',0,'elizabeth','i\'m a snake.',4,0,0,1,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12428,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hi. i\\\'m a snake.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12429,2190,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I\\\'m a fire snake.  I thought your sign year was supposed to be a banner year for you?  Bah.  I think these chinese astrologers have a one-off error somewhere...',3,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12430,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'m a fire snake too, orion. this year wasn\\\'t a banner year for me at all. it was kind of horrible actually. and i\\\'m glad it\\\'s ending, both in the western and chinese calendars. but, if it hadn\\\'t have happened, i wouldn\\\'t have learned a lot of really important stuff. it was a neccessary but awful time. bring on the rebirth.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12431,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','also, this is one of my favorite things by oof in a while. i like detailed moments in time.\r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12432,2190,'2001-12-31 20:40:44',0,'jo','oof, you\\\'re nice. i hope good things happen to you.<BR>\r\ni\\\'m a horse, too, but this was the best year i\\\'ve had since. 98, i think. it fucking rocked.',0,0,0,0,'172.169.69.69','en'),(12433,2190,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i am a boar. this year sucked for me too. I did wonderful things, and wonderful things happened, but they were all hard. Maybe that doesnt mean it sucked, maybe that just means I wont appriciate it until later.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12434,2190,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','<P>i am a fire monkey! moooonkey!</P>\r\n<P>Not quite sure what this means apart from me being playful and cheeky.</P>\r\n<P>This year has been bad in parts, but the past 2 months have been bliss.  I think things suddenly right themselves for me... all these things at once.</P>\r\n<P>And now, it\\\'s summer and I am getting married in 5ish weeks and I get to see all of my favourite people in one place...</P>\r\n<P>If my Nanna was there, it would be perfect.  </P> ',2,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(12435,2190,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i\\\'m a tiger. meeeerowr!',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12436,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bok, bok.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12437,2190,'2002-01-01 09:35:39',0,'o to peter','are you a chicken according to chinese astrology?',4,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12438,2190,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','peter. you need to get on some sort of internet chat thinger. this is your new years resolution for 2002, if you choose to accept it.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12439,2190,'2002-01-01 10:42:06',0,'kini to o + starlajo','as a snake, i\\\'m sorry but i\\\'m going to have to end our friendship.',0,0,0,0,'203.27.69.94','en'),(12440,2190,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','kini, you know i love you and your slithery ways.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12441,2192,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','why not try being nothing but what you are?  strikes me that being always nice or an asshole is a certain path to destruction.',6,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12442,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>to o: alas, i am somehow a <i>snake</i>, although snakeish astro epithets rarely apply/make sense to my circumstances. except that i get along well with ox and chicken. moo. bok bok.<br><br>\r\nto s5: i will persevere and meet the challenge; so keep an eye out on the ol\\\' icb.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12443,727,'2002-01-01 19:40:19',0,'pokey','<p>\r\nThat was a good comment, Ding Dong.  Easygoing in tone, and covering a lot of ground.  Reminders help chase away the anesthesia.\r\n</p>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'67.208.181.4','en'),(12444,2192,'2002-01-02 03:43:56',0,'critic','year of the asshole? yikes, that cant be good for anyone, especially <b>you</b>/ sounds like you should just be yourself, for better or for worse.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12445,2190,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','<p>oof, I was doing the same thing you were doing, at the same time that you were doing it. Now that you have piled your things in this house I know you better. Every object or the ghost that left it behind fills me up with nostalgia today. </p>\r\n<p>and ps, this year sucked. a fire dragon needs to keep moving! ill be celebrating the lunar holidays from asia, y\\\'all.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12446,2193,'2002-01-02 12:15:52',0,'Ulysses S. Grant','my horse was named Cincinnatus.',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12447,2193,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>lucius quintus cincinnatus, anyone? cincinnati is an interesting place. ive only been there to row in races on harsha lake, so my sole perspective of the area is from the water. i wish i knew more places from the water than from land. </tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12448,2194,'2002-01-02 13:03:04',0,'patsy','i fall to pieces...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12449,2193,'2002-01-02 13:52:22',0,'jo','i like imagining if the earth were a person, what she would think about the way we choose to celebrate her birthday.',0,0,0,0,'172.150.203.29','en'),(12450,2193,'2002-01-02 13:56:01',0,'jo','am i dumb for being confused on the events here? she was supposed to be at the bus station but wasn\\\'t? are you in cincinnati? was/is she and then decided to go washington? help?',0,0,0,0,'172.150.203.29','en'),(12451,2193,'2002-01-02 14:28:51',0,'critic','this thinking of new year\\\'s as \\\"the earth\\\'s birthday\\\" is a new thing for me. ive never thought about it that way before.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12452,2193,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','thinking of new years as the earths birthday is retarded.  the earth could give a shit about one pathetic human year.  dude.',6,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12453,2193,'2002-01-02 16:13:33',0,'father earth','o, you rock. i fucking dream in eons, baby!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12454,2158,'2002-01-02 16:15:41',0,'garbageman','dude. brooklyn and <i>you</i>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12455,2180,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','nice',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12456,2190,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','snakes, don\\\'t worry. the chinese don\\\'t view the snake quite in the way that westerners do-- it is an animal respected for its <a href=\\\"http://chinese.astrology.com/snake.html\\\">intelligence and diplomacy</a>. they\\\'re tasty, too. oops, sorry, that was the asian in me. peter, regarding the icb thing, don\\\'t do it. it will suck up all your time when you\\\'re not looking. you will meet and want to hang out with all these cool people you meet online, but none of you will ever leave your houses and computers. it is a sad sad life!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12457,2190,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to weeb','<br><tt>oh yes. ive been icb/ircii-free for almost 7 years now, and have built a real life in all the resulting free time! viva self-control. however, it does make staying in touch easier...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12458,2193,'2002-01-02 18:59:27',0,'nice tidy street','<p>jo, sorry i\\\'m kind of used to writing for myself.  short story: i\\\'m in canada, she went to cincinnati, and then accepted a plane ticket to go hook up with some gigolo from washington.  boo to her.</p>\r\n\r\noh, and the happy birthday earth is kind of tongue-in-cheek.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12459,2192,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','the 2k1 seems to have been dolling out the poo to more than just you. i know i\\\'ve gotten my fair share of fuckovers in the past annum\r\n<p>i hartily cheer your introversion. shit happens, you suck it up and keep on going.\r\n<p>time for us all to do the same i guess.',2,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12460,2192,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>it seems to be a consensus; most everyone i know seems to think 2001 <i>really</i> sucked, er, as much as a \\\"year\\\" can suck.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12461,2194,'2002-01-02 19:58:59',0,'leonard','oh, it\\\'s opening time down on fascination street...',6,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12462,2190,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','you\\\'d think in the year of the snake a snake would prosper. BAH TO THAT! I have had the worse year of my life this year and i am a snake. hardship and trials were the de rigeur for me.',3,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12463,2193,'2002-01-02 20:54:16',0,'jo','dude, boo to her a lot.<BR>\r\ngirls are dumb.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12464,2192,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Yep, 2001 sucked the big donkey schlong for me.  But not as bad as 1998.  I mean, in one corner it\\\'s 2001 with (romance|school) problems and in the other corner it\\\'s \\\'98 with having my kidneys fail (and school problems, gee, wonder why?).\r\nI think 2002 is going to be good (or at least better) for everybody though, so my word for january is OPTIMISM.  :-)',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12465,2192,'2002-01-02 21:31:08',0,'stella','2001 was, for me, one of the better years that I\\\'ve had. I will remember it fondly. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12466,2192,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','i don\\\'t believe it\\\'s possible for an entire year to suck.  every experience, seemingly good or bad, is an experience and that\\\'s what life is built from.  if it was all good all the time you wouldn\\\'t notice.  without the bad you couldn\\\'t live.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12467,1236,'2002-01-02 22:41:28',0,'durer','<p>super-late comment time:  i had sex in a minivan.  the kind with the seats that fold down into the floor.  but that\\\'s a whole different game, because we could\\\'ve played racquetball in there.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12468,2193,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to jo','<br><tt>i just woke up from a insomnia-curing nap, and groggily sans glasses, read your comment as saying: \\\"dude. boom her alot. girls are da bomb\\\". i dont know about booming, but i got a huge laugh from you possibly saying \\\"girls are da bomb\\\"!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12469,1236,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>new, fun places to do it: the crane on columbus circle.<br></tt><img src=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/construction/14crane4.jpg\\\">',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12470,1972,'2002-01-02 23:29:58',0,'durer','<p>essemar, i live my life by those words, and it works for me too.  i also live my life by bmg\\\'s words.  avoid cake.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12471,2170,'2002-01-02 23:55:26',0,'durer','ah, fond memories of calling my ex-boss a shaved ape.  thanks for the reminder, i forgot about her a long time ago.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12472,2192,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','2001, the year of the 19 year old. thank god, it\\\'s over.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12473,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','all hail <a href=\"http://www.dammit.com/devotion.htm\">dammit</a>.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12474,2198,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','hi seanie',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12475,2198,'2002-01-03 02:14:43',0,'writhing with pain','that was fucking wretched.',6,0,0,0,'216.150.228.64','en'),(12476,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','sean <a href=\\\"http://www.craigslist.org/sfo/mis/2514508.html\\\">gets around</a>. sean did you write this? or did you paste it from craig\\\'s list?',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12477,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hey \\\"writhing with pain,\\\" get some balls and use your name.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12478,2198,'2002-01-03 04:37:58',0,'dammit','yeah i wrote it. i posted it to craigslist too cause i am a fucking attention whore.',6,0,0,0,'66.122.107.46','en'),(12479,2194,'2002-01-03 07:32:05',0,'Javorai','cool, a robert smith comparison.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.117','en'),(12480,2192,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','2001 sucked pretty hard, then took a turn for the best, right around the 22nd of august.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12481,2198,'2002-01-03 09:30:41',0,'ssj3gostu','this was good. i know a girl that\\\'s kinda like dixie and she scares me a great deal.',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(12482,2195,'2002-01-03 13:33:36',0,'critic','y2k... 2k1... 2k2... two-kay-two... does anyone just say \\\"2002\\\", or is it not hip to do that anymore? when will the madness stop? as for me, i wish it were MCMXCIX again.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12483,2200,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>stoo has reminded me of things that i like, and have neglected this past year:\r\n<UL TYPE=\"square\">\r\n<LI>writing on small square pads of paper (can also be used for origami)\r\n<LI>burning incense (nag champa)\r\n</UL><br>\r\ni shall do these two things more often, perhaps simultaneously.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12484,2200,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter also blabbers','<br><tt>...and i like stoo because he writes about things that people like:<br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>pads of paper\r\n<LI>candles\r\n<LI>incense\r\n<LI>tapes\r\n<LI>the strokes\r\n<LI>girls\r\n<LI>drugs\r\n<LI>chinese food\r\n<LI>coffee\r\n<LI>spinning\r\n<LI>san francisco\r\n</UL></tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12485,2195,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i, myself, like to painstakingly enunciate each syllable, being careful to articulate every subtle phonetic nuance: \"two thou-sand and two\".</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12486,2195,'2002-01-03 14:32:18',0,'stella','actually, I think that \\\"two kay two\\\" is a useful trick for duping unsuspecting swinneys into reading yet ANOTHER post on new year resolution/reflection/introspection. Christ-on-a-rockinghorse, will it ever stop!?',1,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12487,2195,'2002-01-03 14:36:31',0,'critic','i keep having recurring nightmares about emo folks walking around saying \\\"dude. two-kay-ONE was so like <i>bleak</i>, but man... two-kay-TWO is gonna be <i>rad</i>!\\\".',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12488,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the line \\\"Why, youâ€™re just a cute lâ€™il olâ€™ fireplug, ainâ€™tcha honey\\\".</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12489,2194,'2002-01-03 14:48:38',0,'The Stranger','I bet he read me because he thought it would make him as deep as Robert Smith',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12490,2194,'2002-01-03 14:51:24',0,'critic','dude. <i>the stranger</i> is so, like, 1957.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12491,2198,'2002-01-03 16:30:24',0,'trouble','wow.  in a day when i\\\'m already thinking back to high school for whatever reason, somebody taps into that and makes me recall the hi-top chuck taylor wearning, jeans rolled high having, smiths t-shirt with ratty cardigan sweater with P.I.L and Morrissey buttons slapped all over the place and yes, the long trenchcoat wearing crew that used to populate my art class and trash me for my boots, jeans and flannel shirt.  nothing like recalling high school cliques and less than average art students mad at the kid in the back cause he drew better than they did...  this was a great piece.  *best friendly*\r\n',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12492,2198,'2002-01-03 16:47:49',0,'critic','my friend used to have a fabulous t-shirt with a picture on it of a pickup-truck crashed into a tree- on the truck\\\'s hood was painted a rebel flag. under the picture was the acption \\\"My Dixie Wrecked\\\". it took me a year to figure that one out.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12493,2200,'2002-01-03 17:19:41',0,'jo','many of these are the same reasons i like michelle tea.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12494,2198,'2002-01-03 17:24:23',0,'erb','the craigslist storywriter! yay! hooray! the best part about the stories you posted to craigslist were the ravenous postings that followed: \\\"please post more.\\\"  \\\"yum.\\\"  and then the bitter ones: \\\"this isn\\\'t a <i>short story</i> contest, please keep your little <i>stories</i> to yourself.\\\"  i loved all of them.  i worry about your drinking, though.  go dixie!',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.156','en'),(12495,2200,'2002-01-03 17:26:17',0,'Unfortunate','Clifton Park, NY?',5,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12496,2199,'2002-01-03 17:32:19',0,'durer','it figures bob hope can get into heaven when he\\\'s still alive.  that guy\\\'s got every award and reward possible.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12497,2202,'2002-01-03 17:34:44',0,'durer','a former employer gave me $2000 in stock at new years, 2000.  it is now worth $18.34.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12498,2202,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weebinator','buy low. sell high. the whole world\\\'s for sale!! buy buy buy *glurk*',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12499,2202,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','my goal, as it is every year, is to floss regularly. so far I have flossed 1 out of three days in 2002.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12500,1975,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\\\'m still waiting for my calendar in the mail! ',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12501,2200,'2002-01-03 18:17:41',0,'ssj3gostu','yes, clifton park ny. been there?',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.235','en'),(12502,2203,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','WHO ARE YOU',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12503,2202,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i dont really do resolutions (although perhaps i should).  however, i had a surprisingly nice new years, which set a promising tone for twenty-oh-two.  actually, i don\\\'t feel like i\\\'ve ever had a \\\"bad\\\" year.  I am extremely thankful.</p>',3,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12504,2198,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','this was fucking fabulous.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12505,2202,'2002-01-03 18:42:22',0,'erb to egg','hi!  remember, the more you floss, the less you bleed!  i floss about mmm...4 or 5 times a week.  my mom sent me *glide* floss in the mail.  it\\\'s good, it really is.  good luck.',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.156','en'),(12506,2200,'2002-01-03 18:52:45',0,'Unfortunate','Yeah, actually I live about 10 minutes from there. My father lives there now... it\\\'s a silly little suburb-laden stretch of land. Are you from there, by chance?',3,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12507,2195,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','personally, it\\\'s just easier to type 2k2. i was just going that much further in emphasizing just how damn silly the whole abbreviation of 2002 is. i like it, i use it, but it still is funny to read. i think i\\\'ve been reading too many gamer mags and it rubbed off. NBA 2k2, etc.',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12508,2201,'2002-01-03 19:41:11',0,'d\\\'oh!','paragraph breaks #1!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12509,2202,'2002-01-03 19:44:01',0,'new york','move to me, austin. i will make sweet urban love to you, perpetually.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12510,2203,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes. there are lots of new people showing up here; i would like to get to know more about some of you, also.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12511,2200,'2002-01-03 19:48:31',0,'new york','clifton park is <a href=\\\"http://www.mapquest.com/maps/map.adp?zoom=5&mapdata=ULfEtX9%2beHcwK%2fNBsFPcWbatuLMGkXV9YJJdYPQHbRyZfOauo77Wfu%2fcPZbAOO3GGqtChXauCs%2bPR5hgsYWtTG6lCWu94aDUnVZtuy8DXGZGzq3ubhehr3e%2bfG%2fdy4X%2fT1B%2bCIU77VU2wpGn3EBgAgbzCwpC%2br5eBwF2HsRdLj1L87LcE6NXl%2bdBV6gOlqUAaOC1N4QA7tDmBRWncui2NiE1OuLOGbfCFRwOA%2feWQF3ymLQ9pT1kpMlqaG9yn8Tfr3UoWlXkSGisZn4rBCxkCxaySRl%2blIB7%2bfzVI6%2fG9bUt5qzAukbYaw%3d%3d\\\">here</a>.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12512,2200,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh shit; \'new york\' is also a <i>state</i>?!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12513,2192,'2002-01-03 19:49:19',0,'Dv8','<p>Reacting to all the various experiences of a year by deciding to lock in a specific approach to a new one doesn\\\'t seem appropriate. I would opt for limiting the asshole/ego declaration to perhaps a month or two. Otherwise you might get really bored and tiried - it takes a lot of energy to be an asshole and it gets boring really fast. Trust me. </p>  ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.163.254','en'),(12514,2202,'2002-01-03 20:35:20',0,'leonard to egg','get a chew toy.',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12515,2200,'2002-01-03 20:41:45',0,'Unfortunate','Yes, interestingly enough, NY is a state. And believe me, it never gets old having to explain to people that although I live in New York, I can\\\'t walk to Times Square. It\\\'s a neverending enigma to some people.',3,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12516,2200,'2002-01-03 20:42:14',0,'ssj3gostu','haha, yes. little known fact, there is more to new york than just the city. not that <i>much</i>, mind you, but still, it\\\'s there.\r\n<p align=\\\"justify\\\">\r\nand yeah, i grew up in clifton park.</p>',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.235','en'),(12517,2200,'2002-01-03 20:44:24',0,'Unfortunate','That\\\'s good. That\\\'s good that you grew up there, and managed to get out. It\\\'s a good sign. Good.',1,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12518,2200,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\\\"and managed to get out\\\".. whats that supposed to mean? and i guess it works both ways... i cant exactly walk to niagra falls ,you know?</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12519,2198,'2002-01-03 21:41:02',0,'wb','elly is a MISSED CONNECTIONS ho also?',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(12520,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think more of us are missed-connections junkies than we\\\'d liek to admit...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12521,2202,'2002-03-08 04:50:26',86,'egg to leonard','im upset that there is no calendar. but i sent you no pictures. it is my fault. we all share the blame.',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12522,2201,'2002-01-03 23:52:47',0,'Paul G','I\\\'m very sorry about the missing paragraph breaks or tags or whatever they are.  Having said that, would you be so kind as to tell me why every apostrophe became a question mark?  I know that I need help, but there isn\\\'t an adult in the room right now...',3,0,0,0,'64.158.237.239','en'),(12523,2202,'2002-01-03 23:52:58',0,'leonard','i\\\'m just plain upset about everything. sorry.',5,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12524,2201,'2002-01-04 00:04:16',0,'treetop','hi<p>just wait until \\\"&nbsp\\\" starts showing up in your posts. It is nice that you can go back and edit your last post now. :)  Oh yea, nbsp stands for \\\"no body spanks porcupines.</p>',0,0,0,0,'172.139.175.107','en'),(12525,2201,'2002-01-04 00:05:01',0,'&nbsp','\\\" \\\"',0,0,0,0,'172.139.175.107','en'),(12526,2167,'2002-01-04 00:16:02',0,'','the great struggle and contradiction of love (and i think with any human interaction) is that to get anything out of it, you have to put yourself on the line.  \r\n\r\nthere will always be plenty of people who will take your genuine thoughts and feelings, and throw them back in your face.  there are people that will nod and smile and end up not taking you seriously.  there are lots of people who just don\\\'t understand.  each of those ends up hurting.  nobody\\\'s radar is perfect, and we all make some mistakes.\r\n\r\ni think before you can expect honesty from someone else, you have to be honest to them.  to be honest to someone else, first you must be honest to yourself.  i\\\'m still working on the first step.  once i know myself, i can express that to someone, and even though that makes me ultimately vulnerable, it also makes me strong.  \r\n\r\nthat\\\'s what i hope, anyways.  maybe then i can throw off the \\\"serial monogamist\\\" label and stop stumbling from one pointless romance to another.  \r\n\r\nwhen i can express myself in person as well as i can in print, i\\\'ll know i\\\'m on the way.  with the people i care for, i too often retreat to sarcastic humour, to avoid actually saying anything.  a lot of people i know are the same.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12527,2167,'2002-01-04 00:17:09',0,'durer','<p>the great struggle and contradiction of love (and i think with any human interaction) is that to get anything out of it, you have to put yourself on the line.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there will always be plenty of people who will take your genuine thoughts and feelings, and throw them back in your face.  there are people that will nod and smile and end up not taking you seriously.  there are lots of people who just don\\\'t understand.  each of those ends up hurting.  nobody\\\'s radar is perfect, and we all make some mistakes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think before you can expect honesty from someone else, you have to be honest to them.  to be honest to someone else, first you must be honest to yourself.  i\\\'m still working on the first step.  once i know myself, i can express that to someone, and even though that makes me ultimately vulnerable, it also makes me strong.  that\\\'s what i hope, anyways.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>maybe then i can throw off the \\\"serial monogamist\\\" label and stop stumbling from one pointless romance to another.  when i can express myself in person as well as i can in print, i\\\'ll know i\\\'m on the way.  with the people i care for, i too often retreat to sarcastic humour, to avoid actually saying anything.  a lot of people i know are the same.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12528,2203,'2002-01-04 00:19:23',0,'durer','if anyone wants to talk, my icq is 40714432.  my email\\\'s kind of broken at the moment.  the \\\"i thought it\\\" board is driving me insane!',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12529,2203,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','icq drools. aim. AIM.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12530,2200,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','destiny should cut it out with the picky choosey once in a while. yah.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12531,2201,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','save as text only. ',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12532,2202,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','one year I made a resolution not to make resolutions. its been the only one I\\\'ve managed to keep. There are a lot of things I\\\'d like in the new year. I dont know how to find them. I hope they find me.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.54.42','en'),(12533,2203,'2002-01-04 01:48:39',0,'durer','that too, my aim screen name is jeeeeff.  that\\\'s 4 e\\\'s.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12534,2202,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin about resolutions','i really like to make resolutions.  they are never outlandish.  i never resolve to do things i know i wont do.  i resolve to do things that i think sound like something that i am very capable of and would enjoy, or would be an improvement over my current or past situation.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit is a developmental thing.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12535,2204,'2002-01-04 01:52:35',0,'just ask jesus','huh?',0,0,0,0,'172.139.175.107','en'),(12536,2205,'2002-01-04 01:57:28',0,'durer','<p>i make the same <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2167.txt\\\">comment</a> here as i did to lin earlier today.  i think whether you\\\'re a boy or girl, its the same struggle, as i think the sexes are more alike than anyone\\\'s comfortable admitting.  its a messy, angst-filled process though, and i keep catching myself going a little nuts when the moon comes out.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12537,2201,'2002-01-04 03:04:42',0,'Paul G','Thank you so much.  And, porcupines are really cool - except for that business about filling their dens with their own waste and then sleeping in it.  Edit?  There is so much that I do not understand.  Sigh...   ',3,0,0,0,'67.195.57.9','en'),(12538,2205,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','course it\\\'s the same. I just was being moody and listening to plainclothes man over and over. so you know.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12539,1824,'2002-01-04 04:16:56',0,'trouble to peter','whatever happened to this story?',1,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(12540,2202,'2002-01-04 06:16:58',0,'gus to austin','i agree with you about resolutions, I should have been more clear. I make resolutions whenever necessary. I do not make new years resolutions. Its like valentines day, a silly holiday, you should tell the people you love that you love them on any day, or every day. you should make resolutions when they strike you, not because its january. thats all! (Not that I wouldnt like to have a valentine, I would. I want to eat my cake ya know?)',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12541,2202,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i believe in re-evaluating everything every year. it\\\'s a really good thing to do. i don\\\'t know if this is the same thing as making resolutions because i\\\'ve never had to quit smoking.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12542,2194,'2002-01-04 07:34:04',0,'','yup.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.107','en'),(12543,2194,'2002-01-04 07:34:17',0,'Javorai','yup.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.107','en'),(12544,2203,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hey durer, try some ginger next time. ginger tea or the little cubes from the hippymart bulk bins. also: welcome!',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12545,2198,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','It is pretty sad that we all read missed connections. Im just SURE someone will post about me someday though. right. I wouldnt worry about seanie\\\'s drinking though, he\\\'s on the boring wagon <i>at least</i> half the time.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12546,2204,'2002-01-04 08:33:20',0,'rules','1. don\\\'t talk shit (to anyone)\r\n2. do be nice\r\n3. know when to break the rules',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12547,2204,'2002-01-04 08:34:22',0,'rules','4. remeber to use &lt;br&gt; when you want a line break %^)',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12548,2202,'2002-01-04 08:38:33',0,'predictions','this year will be:<br>\r\n1. mirth<br>\r\n2. rebirth<br>\r\n3. reconstruction<br>\r\n4. recuperation<br>\r\n5. happy<br>\r\n6. full of good suprises<br>\r\n7. lucky (Things Will Just Work Out)<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n[2002 &gt; 2001]\r\n',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12549,2206,'2002-01-04 12:43:02',0,'approaching','outbound train. three cars. L. L. M.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12550,2198,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like new york missed connections not so much for the actualy \\\"missed connections\\\", but for the odd stories/rants/digressions that appear there from time to time. strange shit.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12551,1824,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to trouble','<br><tt>believe it or not, i actually got it written. it turned out to be 3 lolling variations on the same situation, oddly enough. and it never digressed into a control-of-human-will fiasco; just mundane vouching for something, sometimes subtlely, sometimes banal.<br><br>the only person that read it is my flatmate, and he was rather surprised that the story was so deadpan, that such an ability wasn\'t used for evil/immoral purposes. sheesh. i find it curious that so many people are uncomfortable with the idea of using powerful abilities for everyday purposes.<br><br>[visions of victorian-era scientists dancing around in a shower of sparks, shouting <i>\"we shall use this electricity to rule the world!\"</i>]</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12552,2202,'2002-01-04 14:57:20',0,'uhhhh to predictions','mmmmKAY. sure.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12553,2206,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','no, that\\\'d be MUNI. ',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12554,2206,'2002-01-04 19:37:41',0,'underground','mind the gap!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12555,2206,'2002-01-04 19:45:26',0,'The MTA','This is a Bronx-bound 1 train, making uptown local stops. The next stop is 110th Street, Cathedral Parkway. Stand clear of the closing doors, please.<br><img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/110.JPG\">',1,0,0,1,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12556,2211,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>it will be ok, erb.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12557,2204,'2002-01-04 20:02:52',0,'geekboy','what does \\\"<b>%^)</b>\\\" mean?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12558,1703,'2004-07-27 05:00:32',578,'NetfÃ¶ng','whats a pram?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12559,1853,'2004-07-27 05:00:32',578,'NetfÃ¶ng','dude. didnt u see the matrix?',2,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12560,2210,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','um, did you say miss USA is a bull-riding commentator? does that seem weird to anyone?',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12561,2202,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to s5','i view the entire New Years thing as a reevaluation of life and then consideration for  moving forward. these considerations could be called resolutions.  Although, nowhere in my article do i say resolution.  and in fact, i have been trying to do each of those things i mention most of 2001.  some of them i resolve to FINISH in 2002, like paying off credit cards and destroying them.  or i resolve to finish polishing up my old motorcycle and actually ride it.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsometimes i make a list like the above just to get myself out of a rut.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://durak.org:80/sean/readme.php\\\"><img src=\\\"http://images.amazon.com/images/P/0201479486.01.LZZZZZZZ.jpg\\\"></a>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthis good book recommended to me by sean.  i pick it up every now and then, also when i feel like i am in a rut.  sometimes i pick it up when i think the entire planet is in a rut.  it has lots of examples of decision making processes that cause the general maintenance of life to go astray.',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12562,2211,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','the shower is the best place to cry. I thought this was beautiful.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12563,1824,'2002-01-04 20:31:57',0,'trouble','i think its in our nature to think of such extraordinary powers in grand fashion and only for devious plans.  comics, movies and bedtime stories have taught us to dream big.  too bad that these lofty ideas teach us little in the way of how we as flawed people could even handle such power.<br><br>i bet the story was a good read.<br><br>victorian-era scientists?  my favorite image is baron von frankenstein all giddy, frantic, looking up at the ceiling of his castle shouting \\\'it\\\'s alive!  it\\\'s alive!\\\' as the finger of the monster twitched.',2,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12564,2210,'2002-01-04 20:32:57',0,'felicity to simon','Strange but true.  Her biggest dream, when she finishes her journalism degree is to become a sports analyst.  Ideally she would like to do football play-by-play, but you take what you can get.  I\\\'d gladly leave my radio gig to talk rodeo if I had a full season contract from TNN.  It wouldn\\\'t be the strangest career move I\\\'ve ever made.',0,0,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(12565,2212,'2002-01-04 20:44:11',0,'stella','When I moved to NY and began the breath-taking task of learning the subway systems of NYC, I was often completely taken by her stations. The artwork is astounding... The tiles, murals and just general adornment. Every stop is a new treat. After 22 years of BART, with his boring, prefab \\\'70/80\\\'s colors, plastics and escalators, the filthy yet beautiful subways of NYC are very pleasing. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12566,2212,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','71 Haight Noriega.  48th Avenue and Ortega.\r\n<br>\r\n71 Haight Noriega.  48th Avenue and Ortega.<br>\r\n71 Haight Noriega.  48th Avenue and Ortega.<br>\r\n71 Haight Noriega.  48th Avenue and Ortega.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n71 Haight Noriega.  48th Avenue and Ortega.\r\n\r\naaaaaaaaaaaguughh \r\nBURN BURN!',6,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12567,2210,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah you\\\'re right. i\\\'ve made some pretty interesting job choices in the name of photography.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12568,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>to stella: youre right; the subway system is fascinating and beautiful. next time youre at south ferry, ask the conductor to let you \"ride through\"- if theyre nice, they\'ll let you ride through to where they reverse the direction of the trains- the grand, ornate, abandoned old city hall station. wow. next time youre passing where 93rd street would be on the uptown 1/2/3, look at the old abandoned station on the east side of the tracks. its still eerily lit, even though its not in service. god, this sort of stuff intrigues me in a weird way. also, the guy who designed most of the subway stations is the same architect that drew up the plans for the now-partially-charred cathedral of st. john the divine (yes, at cathedral pkway). ok, i\'ll shut up now, heh.<br><br>\r\nxep, your bus stop mantra <i>rhymes</i>. that, at least, is nice. it makes it conducive to repetition.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12569,2212,'2002-01-04 20:54:05',0,'stella','However, I did always enjoy riding Muni trains... those were always interesting. Whenever I rode one, I would be reminded of riding that rickety roller coaster at the local parking lot fair. Jarring, bumpy, uncomfortable and always a possiblity of danger.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12570,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>do the bart/muni trains use tokens?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12571,2212,'2002-01-04 21:00:18',0,'stella','<p>Ohhh, I will! I find this stuff fascinating too, in an eerie way. Must be a residue from all those tagging/breaking movies from the early 80\\\'s, where rebel graffiti artists would risk life and limb to be able to get to that deserted station to be able to practice what they love without the cops interfering!! </P>\r\n<p>Heh.. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12572,2212,'2002-01-04 21:00:50',0,'stella','Nope, hard cold currency. Or a BART/muni pass. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12573,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the PATH trains that run to newark from 34th street. they used to run from the wtc, but not anymore.<br><br>\r\nthat 93rd st. station has a GIANT mural in crazy 80\\\'s wildstyle with an accompanying rant sprayed next to it, debasing koch, reagan, cold weather, and as always, the <a href=\\\"http://www.nyc.com/go.aspx?bkurl=%2fsearch.aspx%3fn%3dnypd%26c%3d0%26e%3dname%26searchOptions%3d-1&url=http%3a%2f%2fwww.nyc.gov%2fhtml%2fnypd%2fhome.html&s=11&n=nypd&sn=Police+Department+(NYPD)\\\">nypd</a>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12574,2212,'2002-01-04 21:12:11',0,'ssj3gostu','have you seen <a href=\\\"http://www.darkdays.com\\\">dark days</a>?',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.243','en'),(12575,2212,'2002-01-04 21:15:57',0,'ryan','you can get muni tokens, yo.<p>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.sfmuni.com/fares/passvend.htm\\\">rolls of ten for $8</a>.<p>\r\ni was just up in portland.  they have a cool thing there, free fares in the free fare square which covers most of the urban area.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.107','en'),(12576,2212,'2002-01-04 21:19:48',0,'kat','...as dreary and unexciting as the modern day human transport system is on the west coast, it still leaves itself open to passionate window gazing reminiscing.\r\n\r\n<p>03.05.01</p>\r\n<p>riding the 2:35 BART home from San Francisco... jim is talking to me. grizzly adams beard, walking cane, some serious lazy eye problems. nice old guy. he wanted to know if i was married. no i say. boyfriend? no. and why is that? im just enjoying time on my own these days jim.</p>\r\n<p>oooohhhhhh....you\\\'re one of &quot;those&quot; he says with a sly wink. you have a girlfriend!?! [what the fuck] how typical i thought. but the temptation to be a smartass quickly faded.</p>\r\n<p>like i said, jim was a nice old guy.</p>\r\n<p>he was excited to hear that i worked with computers. he has monitor problems. while i did throw in my two cents on the possibilities of computer repair versus replacement...i have to admit, jim wasn\\\'t getting my full attention. in fact i think i missed half of what he said.</p>\r\n<p>i was too busy thinking about walking away from my lunch date. too busy remembering his mouth on mine, ....too busy trying to catch the scent of him still on me.</p>\r\n<p>so now i know. there is nothing quite like losing yourself in a kiss at the corner of 16th and Mission on a Monday afternoon with the\r\nwhole world walking by.</p>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.8','en'),(12577,2211,'2002-01-04 21:24:49',0,'James Hillman','Jung said, \\\"Creation is as much destruction as construction.\\\"  The ectopsychic instinctual force, because it comes from beyond the psyche, is more than human and mightier than its possessor.  One spends one\\\'s life trying to slow it, tame it, give it enough time and space, because its haste is the destructive devil within the creative impulse itself.  Suicide always remains the fundamental possibility of psychological creativity, as its reversal, since the destruction of soul is the counterpart of the creation of soul.  Ahem.',3,0,0,0,'64.105.32.155','en'),(12578,2204,'2002-01-04 21:34:54',0,'euclidiot','forgiven',2,0,0,0,'64.230.116.202','en'),(12579,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','any stories about public transit should be forwarded to the vegan from chicago. his name is jeremy, and he used to write only public transit related journal entries on his site. they\'re still there, but he won\'t link to them, so neither will i, even though it is tempting. but, his site, <a href=\"http://www.waitingforthebus.com\">waitingforthebus.com</a>, was supposed to be a zine about public transit. i hope he gets around to doing it.\r\n<br><br>\r\nalso, when my sorta-friend (i know her but don\'t know her) kamio used to commute, she kept a commuting journal at <a href=\"http://www.kommute.com\">kommute.com</a>. \r\n<br><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://elly.diaryland.com/011216_25.html\">i saw a woman puke on the 6 parnassus the other day</a>.\r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12580,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if anyone wants to send me a muni token, that would make my day. perhaps my week, even. [i have simple pleasures, undoubtedly]</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12581,2212,'2002-01-04 23:20:19',0,'kat','<p>i do not have any tokens to send...but i can mail you a cd i burned of songs i fell in love with while wasting my days away in Oklahoma. i can even include one small piece of genuine Okie slate rock from the shores of Broken Bow Lake. and i\\\'ll even up the ante some more by including a foto of me [age 25] with really really long hair wearing a University of Oklahoma tshirt.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i once got stuck in the snow for two hours because when OU football happens all public transport is used only for hauling slobbering fans back and forth to Owen Field...those of us who actually work on Game Days and have a life outside of college football can suck eggs if they need to get somewhere. i had to walk home. heh.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>[ps: i have a fever right now and am sick and feeling quite loopy]</p>',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.6','en'),(12582,2212,'2002-01-05 00:00:46',0,'sfmuni','a muni token? just frame a dollar.',0,0,0,0,'63.195.92.218','en'),(12583,2212,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','no, there actually is a token for muni. where do you live, peter? --> weeb@weeb.com',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12584,111,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','who is kibo',0,0,0,0,'66.47.229.243','en'),(12585,2212,'2002-01-05 02:47:09',0,'tree^','<p>N Judah Haikus found on Craig\\\'s list.</p>\r\n<br>\r\ntoo afraid to scream <br>\r\nback door please, back door <br>\r\ni keep on riding <br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nduring the delay <br>\r\nsome people read and others <br>\r\nwatch some people read <br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nthe metallic worm <br>\r\nfunnels through our cement tomb <br>\r\nright steps going down <br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nthe fat driver eats <br>\r\nTaco Bell and KFC <br>\r\nthe same company <br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nyou are beautiful <br>\r\nthe way you lean on the glass <br>\r\nwith neck like dove\\\'s crane <br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\nthe punks always smell <br>\r\nthat is their statement i guess <br>\r\nplease take a shower <br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\nautomatic mode <br>\r\nis a mode in which things run <br>\r\naccording to fate <br>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\ni get off the train <br>\r\nstill thinking about your face <br>\r\nwe would get along <br>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.154.202.14','en'),(12586,111,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','a guy who used to appear in any usenet forum where anyone mentioned his name, no matter what. a usenet legend. here: <a href=\\\"http://groups.google.com/groups?q=kibo&hl=en&btnG=Google+Search\\\">search google newsgroups for kibo</a>',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12587,111,'2002-01-05 11:42:47',0,'kibo','...otherwise known as the Candyman.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.199','en'),(12588,111,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i think of \\\'candyman\\\', i think of <i>knockin\\\' boots</i></tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12589,2211,'2002-01-05 17:08:14',0,'jo','wow. too awed to express it. this was beautifully written.',0,0,0,0,'172.140.211.202','en'),(12590,2213,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have discovered an industrial/residentially zoned apartment in 46th and 11th avenue here- 18,000 sq. ft.- it is large. it overlooks the hudson river and the sketchy parts of the west side. it is only $1k.<br><br>\r\ndespite all of this, i will most likely move tehre based on the fact that it is onlyaccessable by freight elevator.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12591,2213,'2002-01-05 19:13:07',0,'kitkat to Peter','you can always look forward to no more pee on your windows.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.33','en'),(12592,2213,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>true! however, as this place used to be a factory, im not sure if it has bathrooms, kitchens and the like isntalled (yet). so, peeing <i>out</i> the window might be status quo there.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12593,2217,'2002-01-05 19:33:03',0,'kat','been there done that. jeezus holy fuck. sometimes it was good. sometimes it was amazing. sometimes it was eternal hell. but it always ended. not really in a bad way...but not in a good way either. just a sort of blah, boring, predictable, oh-my-gawd you make me feel like screaming kinda way. my best advice is to NOT spend too much time talking about things you want from the future or from other people. have conversations...real conversations about music, politics, school, yadda yadda. it seems that once you start sharing that other stuff...the secret fantasy kinda stuff...people end up playing out those roles instead of just being themselves [good when you\\\'ve been with someone for awhile...but not so good when you\\\'re just meeting them]. honesty is big for me. i just wish people could be more honest with themselves. it can only lead to them being more honest in what they share with others. personals are a loaded gun. russian roulette style. i\\\'ve learned that just because you have a lot in common or both want the same things doesn\\\'t mean a romance of some sort will be happy. really it doesn\\\'t mean diddly.\r\n\r\n<p>but maybe you already knew this.</p>\r\n\r\n<i>...and yea, i still have a personal out there somewhere. i think it\\\'s funny.</i>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.33','en'),(12594,2217,'2002-01-05 19:36:56',0,'ps','i can\\\'t help it but everytime i come across the word \\\'anticipation\\\' i can see and hear Dr. Frankenfuter perfectly in my mind. *An-tic-I-pa-Tion*\r\n\r\n<p>[hubba hubba]</p>\r\n\r\n<p>now how come noone like him ever answered my ad?</p>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.33','en'),(12595,2216,'2002-01-05 19:40:30',0,'Einstein','\\\'gravitation cannot be held responsible for people falling in love\\\'\r\n',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.33','en'),(12596,2217,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i met this amazing girl once, in college. we hung out alot and got to know each other as friends. she moved for the summer, and thats when the emailing started. by the end of the summer, we had exchanged [checking pine folder...] 4,112 emails of pure, heartfelt sharing; the funny, the mysterious, the dark, the lovely.<br><br>\r\nshe came back. we meshed like nothing else and lived in a state of absolute bliss for 5 years. we were <i>in love</i>.<br><br>\r\nthen, we kind of grew apart, as people do when they grow up.  she got involved in some sordid relationships at work, and i went off to grad school. i dont see much of her anymore.<br><br>\r\nbut lately, ive been running into her; small wonder since nyc is rather large, and weve been hanging out as friends, which is possible now that we\'ve both matured some. i might even say that i consider her a good friend. yesterday she half-laughingly mentioned getting back togehter. what a love story that could turn into.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12597,2213,'2002-01-05 20:53:44',0,'Paul G','As the subway is to music, so too is the freight elevator to medical illustration.',3,0,0,0,'64.156.106.21','en'),(12598,1677,'2002-01-05 21:46:29',0,'eclipse','<p>Making a choice means taking a risk. Is it the risk we are afraid of, or are we so afraid to become indifferent that forces us to stop in our tracks?</p>\r\n<p>I think very few people really know who they are. And even fewer know what kind of people thay <i>want</i> to be.</p>\r\n<p>We seem to be in this together, and yet we feel so, so alone. </p>',2,0,0,0,'64.228.73.116','en'),(12599,2213,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i have a thing for frieght elevators. I think they are the ideal place for a clandestine quickie. oh yah.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12600,2217,'2002-01-05 22:26:21',0,'durer','i\\\'ve always kind of avoided email romances.  i think more than anything, i\\\'m embarassed by the social stigma of it all.  \r\n\r\nthat said, i met 2 recent girlfriends thru the internet.  we met up as friends, and it progressed to something more, but still.  i think the internet is trying to convince me that its my friend. ',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12601,2216,'2002-01-05 22:40:41',0,'Rilke','To love is good, too: love being difficult. For one human being to love another: that is perhaps the most difficult of all out tasks, the ultimate, the last test and proof, the work for which all other work is but preparation. For this reason young people, who are beginners in everything, cannot yet know love: they have to learn it. With their whole being, with all their forces, gathered close about their lonely, timid, upward-beating heart, they must learn to love. But learning -time is always a long, secluded time, and so loving, for a long while ahead and far into life, is - solitude, intensified and deepened loneness for him who loves. Love is at first not anything that means merging, giving over and uniting with another (for what would a union be of something unclarified and unfinished, still subordinate?), it is a high inducement to the individual to ripen, to become world, to become world for himself for another\\\'s sake, it is a great exacting claim upon him, something that chooses him out and calls him to vast things. ',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12602,2217,'2002-01-05 22:57:11',0,'trouble to peter','that\\\'s because 5 years is the limit for whatever reason.  trust me, my <i><b>research</i></b> is ongoing (yet coming to a final conclusion) and all indiciations point to 5 years being the cut off point.  <br><br>things fade in fives.',2,0,0,0,'24.181.44.135','en'),(12603,2217,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Interesting idea.  I\\\'d never really thought of the idea of a person who does the same things as you in the same places just at different times.<br><br>I like it, though.  I like it a lot, actually.',2,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12604,2214,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','is this a true story?',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12605,2213,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','there used to be a freight elevator at cp. i\\\'m sure it saw many a clandestine quickie.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12606,2212,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','peter, i would like to mail you something. i don\\\'t know what it will be yet. you should email me your address though. ',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12607,2217,'2002-01-05 23:12:58',0,'jo to kat','i did in fact know this. i\\\'m young, not stupid. i, too, have been there, done that.<BR>\r\nalthough i\\\'m going to point to the wisdom of nick hornby right now and say that as much as i wish it weren\\\'t true: \\\"it\\\'s not about what you\\\'re like, it\\\'s about <b>what you like</b>.\\\"<BR>\r\nSORT OF.<BR>\r\nmeaning that it goes both ways. i\\\'ve definitely seen people have everything in the world in common but zero spark. i\\\'ve also seen people who had buckets of chemistry but no common interests so they never spent any time together and when they did, they felt they were compromising themselves because it wasn\\\'t ever what they felt like doing.<BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t write this to say: \\\"hey look at my stellar new relationship!\\\" i wrote this to say: \\\"people are neat. also, just when you think you\\\'re alone, you\\\'re reminded that you aren\\\'t.\\\"\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.155.16.13','en'),(12608,2217,'2002-01-06 00:06:58',0,'kat','well as always my comments were more for me than anything. mostly because i know it too but somehow get caught up in the possibilities and dreams anyway. i am too open. i am stupid. and i\\\'m not from earth.',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.137','en'),(12609,2217,'2002-01-06 00:52:55',0,'Tree^','<p>Hi :)  I was going to add a comment here about meeting people online and dating them, but as I started to type it kept growing, and growing, and then it absorbed my keyboard, and then my spider plant. Now I cant find the dog, or myyyyyyyaahhhhhh!............</p>',0,0,0,0,'172.150.250.227','en'),(12610,2217,'2002-01-06 01:02:32',0,'eclipse','<p>I made some buddies in a geocities chatroom when I frist discovered the internet. My contact with one of them ended several years ago because he moved to another state to marry his online girlfriend, a girl he met in a chatroom.</p>\r\n<p>What a romantic idea. Highly unlikely, but then again, I know a 50 year old couple that met as students and got married in six months. They have one of the most happy relationships I\\\'ve ever seen. </p>\r\n<p>Enjoy the experience. How exciting! </p>',2,0,0,0,'64.228.73.116','en'),(12611,2218,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','nov 21 represent.\r\n<br><br>\r\na lot of times i sleep weird places just to keep things unusual. the floor, the couch, the other bedroom, friend\\\'s bed, lover\\\'s bed, new lover\\\'s bed, friend\\\'s floor, hotel, the chill room.\r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12612,2218,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','it should be illegal to abandon an austin mini.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12613,2218,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','xep, i agree wholeheartedly.',0,0,0,0,'172.144.171.243','en'),(12614,2218,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','NICE WEBSITE, yo.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12615,2217,'2002-01-06 08:20:58',0,'report','so it went really well. when we decided to ditch the second band and go do something else, he pulled his truck up to mine in the parking lot and i went over to his window and said, \\\"so was this a date?\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"yeah i think it was.\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"i do, too. did you have fun?\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"yes. i had a lot of fun.\\\"<BR>\r\n\\\"wanna seal it with a kiss?\\\"<BR>\r\nso i kissed him and it was nice and then we drove to my apartment, picked up my best friend, and went for beers. she approved. \r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.148.227.78','en'),(12616,2213,'2002-01-06 10:26:43',0,'Joanna','I don\\\'t think I\\\'ve ever ridden in a freight elevator before, but my sister\\\'s apartment building has one of those really old elevators with the two metal cage doors that you have to pull back, and it creaks whenever you step into it.  I love it. ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(12617,2217,'2002-01-06 11:28:32',0,'kat','jo you are the coolest',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.224','en'),(12618,2217,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>freight elevators. some peeps i know from college make music under the moniker of <a href=\\\"http://www.fe4.com/\\\">the freight elevator quartet</a>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12619,2219,'2002-01-06 15:28:39',0,'links.net','i like your format!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12620,2219,'2002-01-06 15:32:34',0,'anonymous','i had the best experience of my life and the worst experience of my life once when i ate acid and fucked. once was in the park [good]. once was on the rooftop in a foot of snow [bad].',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12621,2218,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>are you related to Judas??</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12622,2219,'2002-01-06 15:49:05',0,'durer','having to tell a friend that you don\\\'t want to date them is the hardest thing in the world.  a couple times, i even dated the friend just to take the \\\"easy\\\" way out.  with that in mind, now i don\\\'t get angry if i\\\'m on the other end of things, and get an honest \\\"no.\\\"',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12623,2217,'2002-01-06 15:52:16',0,'durer','jo, nicely handled.  write me a dating handbook!',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12624,2218,'2002-01-06 15:56:17',0,'','<p><b>to le:</b> question is, 1978?  i enjoy all the places you mentioned except the chill room, because i don\\\'t like to get covered with raver goo.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>to o:</b> thanks!</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>to peter:</b> *groan*  now ask me if i live in a church, and you\\\'ve got the top two things people say to me.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12625,2218,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p><b>to le:</b> question is, 1978?  i enjoy all the places you mentioned, except the chill room, because i\\\'m averse to sleeping in raver goo (even if it is my own).</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>to o:</b> thanks!</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>to peter:</b> *groan*  now ask me if i live in a church, and you\\\'ve asked the two questions i hear most.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ps: screw secret identities, i\\\'m gonna start using my name.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12626,2218,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','*smacks forehead*',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12627,2217,'2002-01-06 16:36:42',0,'jo to durer','hahaha, you have no idea how much i am the <b>wrong</b> person to be writing that.',0,0,0,0,'172.158.84.116','en'),(12628,2218,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sorry, heh. my bad. sense. of. humour. was either going to end up spouting an albrecht or a judas joke... erk!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12629,2218,'2002-01-06 16:58:01',0,'critic`','props to those of you who use ur real names!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12630,2217,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>since you dont know her, let me just go ahead and tell you that jo is REALLY COOL. she will probably never tell you this herself, although she should. \\\"hi, my name is jo and i am cool\\\" is what she should say.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12631,2217,'2002-01-06 17:40:35',0,'kat','water signs rock my world',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.252','en'),(12632,1530,'2002-01-06 19:01:10',0,'kat','\\\"<i>everything is edible inside. i mean eatable....i mean you can EAT everything inside!</i>\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>[destabilizing]</p>',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.252','en'),(12633,2217,'2002-01-06 19:10:35',0,'jo to peter','awwwww. that means so much coming from a kid who kicks so much ass, himself.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.149.254.35','en'),(12634,2217,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','hi, this and the report made me really happy. you\\\'re fabulous!',1,0,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(12635,2218,'2002-01-06 21:24:54',0,'escape from spain^','once i had to sleep on a bench in front of a closed train station in barcelona. very sketchy. the next night was spent in a cot on a rocky beach in itally just past mote carlo with 2 people from iraq. they were nice and woke me up before the resort security kicked me off their beach.',0,0,0,0,'172.130.71.150','en'),(12636,2217,'2002-01-06 21:32:36',0,'jeeff/durer','splish splash',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12637,1530,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mister wonka??</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12638,2218,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>benches I have slept on, known, and loved:<br><br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>the lobby of university hall @ harvard, where i was not-so-tactfully asked to leave; bad, considering it was storming a ton outside, and worse since i was scheduled to compete in the <a href=\\\"http://www.hocr.org/\\\">hocr</a> at 7 the next morning. all things turned out well as i got no sleep and the race was cancelled anyway.\r\n<LI>the bench at 116th and b\\\'way in nyc, safely tucked behind a guard booth, which was good, as the guard woke me moments after a clearly disturbed person started shovelling up the adjacent flower-bed at about 4 am.\r\n<LI>the bench in father demo square, just off of bleeker, where the nice policeman kindly asked me if i was \\\"too stoned to get home\\\".\r\n</UL><br><br>\r\nahh, city life.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12639,2218,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I once slept in Queen\\\'s Park, near the green house, with some other people the morning after a party.  We were waiting for the subway to begin running and were woken up by sea gulls flying overhead.  I believe that is my only outdoor sleeping adventure.<br><br>I need to live a little more, methinks.',3,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12640,2219,'2002-01-07 00:47:10',0,'Paul G','Hi.  I don\\\'t have any hard data to support this, but it does seem to be the case that when that sort of \\\"fuck-mojo\\\" exists between two people that there is hardly ever a problem.  On the other hand, the carefully planned and moderate encounters with someone who ought to be \\\"safe\\\" are the ones that lead to the use of the words \\\"screening at the County Health Department\\\".\r\n\r\np.s. I was waiting for the \\\"fuck and watch cartoons\\\" combination to be used in your story.',1,0,0,0,'67.24.9.64','en'),(12641,2219,'2002-01-07 01:13:46',0,'javorai','a dangerous thing, meeting people in digital places...',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.234','en'),(12642,2219,'2002-01-07 01:27:31',0,'Dv8','<p>I\\\'d have to say that my experiences in similar situations have been rather hit and miss. When I think about though it is the ones that decide the friendship is worth it that have ended up becoming my closests female friends. I wonder what it is in the initial romantic involvement that has impacted my relationships in this way?</p>  ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.164.172','en'),(12643,2219,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i met and hung out with some people from <i>this</i> website who are probably among the most intelligent and <i>best people</i> ive encountered; go figure.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12644,2219,'2002-01-07 02:14:25',0,'kat','a dangerous thing, meeting places...in digital people',1,0,0,0,'32.102.64.241','en'),(12645,2218,'2002-01-07 03:20:58',0,'a nice place to sleep^','in an open meadow next to a labyrinth on a tree farm close to to mt ashland (dead indian road)',0,0,0,0,'172.132.132.90','en'),(12646,2219,'2002-01-07 03:24:39',0,'fucking during cartQQns?^','scoobie doooooobie dooo!',0,0,0,0,'172.132.132.90','en'),(12647,2219,'2002-01-07 03:56:06',0,'jo to peter','props to that, homie.',0,0,0,0,'66.25.163.251','en'),(12648,2220,'2002-01-07 08:37:38',0,'snowflake^','<p><font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">i</font><font color=\\\"#FF0700\\\">n</font><font color=\\\"#FF0F00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#FF1600\\\">o</font><font color=\\\"#FF1E00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#FF2600\\\">a</font><font color=\\\"#FF2D00\\\"> </font><font color=\\\"#FF3500\\\">n</font><font color=\\\"#FF3D00\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#FF4400\\\">w</font><font color=\\\"#FF4C00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#FF5400\\\">w</font><font color=\\\"#FF5B00\\\">o</font><font color=\\\"#FF6300\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#FF6B00\\\">l</font><font color=\\\"#FF7200\\\">d</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#FF8200\\\">w</font><font color=\\\"#FF8900\\\">i</font><font color=\\\"#FF9100\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#FF9900\\\">h</font><font color=\\\"#FFA000\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#FFA800\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#FFAF00\\\">h</font><font color=\\\"#FFB700\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#FFBF00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#FFC600\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#FFCE00\\\">a</font><font color=\\\"#FFD600\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#FFDD00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#FFE500\\\">h</font><font color=\\\"#FFED00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#FFF400\\\">I</font><font color=\\\"#FFFC00\\\"> </font><font color=\\\"#F9FF00\\\">f</font><font color=\\\"#F2FF00\\\">a</font><font color=\\\"#EAFF00\\\">l</font><font color=\\\"#E2FF00\\\">l</font><font color=\\\"#DBFF00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#D3FF00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#CCFF00\\\">h</font><font color=\\\"#C4FF00\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#BCFF00\\\">o</font><font color=\\\"#B5FF00\\\">u</font><font color=\\\"#ADFF00\\\">g</font><font color=\\\"#A5FF00\\\">h</font><font color=\\\"#9EFF00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#96FF00\\\">s</font><font color=\\\"#8EFF00\\\">k</font><font color=\\\"#87FF00\\\">y</font>\r\n<br><font color=\\\"#77FF00\\\">m</font><font color=\\\"#70FF00\\\">y</font><font color=\\\"#68FF00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#60FF00\\\">s</font><font color=\\\"#59FF00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#51FF00\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#49FF00\\\">u</font><font color=\\\"#42FF00\\\">c</font><font color=\\\"#3AFF00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#33FF00\\\">u</font><font color=\\\"#2BFF00\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#23FF00\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#1CFF00\\\">\r\n</font><font color=\\\"#14FF00\\\">a</font><font color=\\\"#0CFF00\\\">l</font><font color=\\\"#05FF00\\\">t</font><font color=\\\"#00FF02\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#00FF0A\\\">r</font><font color=\\\"#00FF11\\\">e</font><font color=\\\"#00FF19\\\">d</font></p>',0,0,0,0,'172.139.12.93','en'),(12649,2219,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','&quot;We began to fuck.&quot;\r\n<p>\r\nthat was so funny.',1,0,0,0,'65.141.71.80','en'),(12650,2218,'2002-01-07 15:46:00',0,'stella','albrect durer is the shit. a bit gloomy, very iconographic, but very nice altogether. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12651,2219,'2002-01-07 16:26:10',0,'stella','When I moved to NY, I didn\\\'t know a sole. I\\\'m not a club/bar/party with strangers kinda gal, and I worked from home in a remote place, so meeting people was challenging. I posted a personal in yahoo! and was swamped with email. One stuck out from a very nice sounding lad. We emailed back and forth; long, funny, flirting email that were steeped in personal information (music preference, politics, religion, etc.). We met on a date two weeks after the email began. We\\\'ve been together ever since. We live together and are very matter of fact about our future together. He writes me love notes. We\\\'re sickening. I startle myself when I recall how we met, and smile. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12652,2218,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Stella','<br><tt>for sure... that albrecht d. is one <i>baaaaad</i> etching mutha!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12653,2219,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i always thought [if i were in such circumstances] that it\\\'d be hard to spill the beans on the old <i>\\\"we met <b>online</b></i>\\\" thingy.<br><br>\r\nuntil recently. two amazingly cool folks were visiting me. my inane pseudo-hipster-wannabe flatmate asked them how they met, and in perfect unison, with big grins, they belted out \\\"We met ONLINE!\\\".  I could see the admittedly tiny mental wheels of my flatmate churning: \\\"wait! cool people who are in love! met online? they have style! online meeting?!\\\".<br><br>\r\nit was one of those moments where youre fucking proud to be a geek!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12654,2219,'2002-01-07 17:11:09',0,'stella','Yeah, I was a bit hesitant to admit it at first too. In fact I purposely lied to my mother about how we met until she got to know him. The look on her face when I fessed up was PRICELESS! ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12655,2214,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>Oh, golden grill of mine<br>\r\nhow brilliantly you shine.<br>\r\nWhat a sweet excavation<br>\r\nfrom a pidgeon defecation<br></p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12656,2219,'2002-01-07 19:35:01',0,'blackkat','almost everyone i hang out with these days [romantically and friendly and work related] are folk that i met online. i\\\'d have to say i\\\'ve been pushing about a 90+ percentage on that since i moved back to Ca a year and a half ago. i don\\\'t work in a big company and i don\\\'t go to school and i don\\\'t really know a lot of party type people so meeting others the traditional way is so tediously slow i had to bypass it. the old-school faction of friends in my life thought it was funny at first...but now i sort of have this reputation and they seem to admire me for it. hell, i built a business online just because of forums i was hanging out in. and i sold it online. and now i\\\'m doing it again. i just happened to expand myself to other possibilities along the way. while it hasn\\\'t always been fun, it\\\'s definitely been interesting. ',4,0,0,0,'32.102.64.11','en'),(12657,2219,'2002-01-07 19:42:37',0,'jo','my parents are used to it. i\\\'ve been meeting and visiting and hanging out with friends i\\\'ve met over the internet since 96. \\\"how\\\'d you meet him?\\\" \\\"internet.\\\" is now a pretty standard exchange.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12658,2219,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','heh, i guess it beats, \\\"how did you meet him?\\\" \\\"in the back room at hole in the wall/the cock/the eagle/etc\\\"',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12659,2219,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','it got to the point in 99/00 that i couldn\\\'t remember if i\\\'d met certain people irl first then they\\\'d discovered icq/irc/personal website whatever or whether it was the other way around.\r\n\r\nworrying.',1,0,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(12660,2219,'2002-01-07 22:03:00',0,'ryan','i hate the fucking internet.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.170','en'),(12661,2219,'2002-01-07 22:10:23',0,'stella','The only reason why Mom was concerned/surprised is because I had an \\\'incident\\\' with a crazy person whom I met online. He decided he was in love with me and came to visit me in CA, without invitation/permission, from the midwest. He was creepy and got trashed by himself at my parents house when they were there. He ended up crawling around my kitchen on his hands and knees moaning that he was going to vomit. That\\\'s only the tip of the iceberg of crap that he pulled. Needless to say, Mom was less then pleased and has ever since raised a questioning eyebrow whenever I mention that I\\\'ve met someone on the internet. I think that she\\\'s okay with it now that things have worked out so well with the boy. She\\\'s gradually forgetting the other one. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12662,2219,'2002-01-07 22:49:49',0,'critic','i hate fucking the internet',0,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12663,2221,'2002-01-07 23:08:41',0,'treetop','<p>Hi Stella. People have done it recently and still are to some extent. Personally I like the conveniences of modern life too much at this point\r\nto drop out. I did stay on the <a href=\\\"http://www.thefarm.org/\\\">Farm</a>\r\nin Tennessee for a little while. It was a wonderful place. There are many <a href=\\\"http://www.ecovillages.org/\\\">ecovillages</a> around the world where you can get information and help. Here is a description of <a href=\\\"http://www.gaia.org/about/guide-ev-start.asp\\\">an ecovillage</a>.</p>',0,0,0,0,'172.186.234.185','en'),(12664,2221,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','</a>i know the feeling you\\\'re talking about.  it may not fit exactly with what you\\\'re talking about, but i\\\'ve always been interested in <a href=\\\"http://www.arcosanti.org\\\">arcosanti.org</a>.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12665,615,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','egg, you crack me up.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12666,2219,'2002-01-08 01:25:50',0,'outcome^','The silent treatment is over and everything seems to be working out. yay:)\r\n\r\n<p><b>Internet to Critic:</b> Please get your do-hicky out of my thing-a-mabob.  Thank you.</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'172.186.234.185','en'),(12667,2222,'2002-01-08 02:02:06',0,'critic','who are Richard Dorfmeister & Jazzanova and what\\\'s a WMF 2001? and whats a MZ-R900DPC, for that matter? i like your writing, its jsut slightly alienating. <i>[x]constructuve!</i> ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12668,2222,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Richard Dorfmesiter is one half of <a href=\\\"http://www.k7.com/data.pl?release=k7046\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">Kruder & Dorfmeister</a>.<br><br><a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/jahsonic/Jazzanova.html\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">Jazzanova</a><br><br>Sony\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://www.sonystyle.com/electronics/prd.jsp?hierc=8627x8647&catid=8647&pid=9711&type=s\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">MZ-R900DPC</a>.<br><br>Thanks and sorry at the same time.  I\\\'ll try and be more accomodating in the future.',2,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12669,2222,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i\\\'m all about the thievery corporation.  rastafari.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12670,2222,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','TC owns you.<br><br>I picked up a <a href=\\\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=1789301313/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/artistid=DUBTRIBE+SOUND+SYSTEM/itemid=640620\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">Dubtribe Soundsystem</a> album today.',1,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12671,2221,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I feel the same urges sometimes.  Sadly, I wouldn\\\'t live long without modern (and expensive) drugs.  So while I might be able to tune in and turn on, dropping out is sorta nixed. :-) Best of luck though!  Try seeing if your local library has something like Whole Earth (or the older Coevolution Quarterly).  There\\\'s several magazines out there for self-generated/self-sufficient power...  Besides, there\\\'s nothing wrong with working a \\\"straight\\\" job and yet working your homestead to be as independent as possible.  I think my dream house would be a submerged or subterranean house on a couple of wooded acres, skylights letting the golden glow from outside spill into a central library area...',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12672,2170,'2002-01-08 06:52:13',0,'zen','Dear Asshole was a modern masterpiece. Nice one.',3,0,0,0,'203.109.252.18','en'),(12673,2222,'2002-01-08 11:06:27',0,'p. diddy','i\\\'m all about the benjamins, baby',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12674,2223,'2002-01-08 13:18:54',0,'Javorai','\\\"Noocra\\\" is the anagram of Corona.  I told you not to trust that shit.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.24','en'),(12675,2223,'2002-01-08 13:23:34',0,'d0rkus','anagrams suck.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12676,407,'2002-01-08 13:43:57',0,'every full moon^','I get naked and run around ocean beach with glow in the dark bio-hazard symbols painted all over my body.',0,0,0,0,'172.138.99.137','en'),(12677,2221,'2002-01-08 14:31:35',0,'stella','thanks for the good references! there is some good information there. and Argosanti (sp?) is a very interesting idea. no, not quite what I meant. what we\\\'re thinking about is living life providing as much for ourselves as possible, but also being able to buy some stuff. the goal would be to purchase items ONLY from local, small companies rather than the corporate giants. as an example, buying a book from the local mom & pop not from barnes & noble. that is a very simplified example. we wouldn\\\'t necessarily be giving up certain comforts, just making them ourselves or obtaining them from alternative sources. it\\\'s harder than it looks. you don\\\'t know how deep the hands of the giants can reach. anyway.. I could ramble. I just wanted to thank you three for your imput.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12678,2217,'2002-01-08 15:08:27',0,'2nd date','we met for coffee and then saw \\\"waking life\\\" and then went back to my house to watch \\\"before sunrise\\\" because he hadn\\\'t seen it and then made out for four hours or so.<BR>\r\nand that will be the end of the date reports but suffice to say, things are nice.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12679,2217,'2002-01-08 15:14:41',0,'hmm','made out for 4 hours with no sex?!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12680,2217,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','making out for 4 hours without sex after \"before sunrise\" rocks arse!<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni would say after reading this that jo is getting her mack on in a serious fashion.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsome people just aren\'t all up in fucking on the second date, y\'know?',2,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12681,2217,'2002-01-08 16:40:54',0,'jo','o\\\'s dead on.<BR>\r\npacing, pacing, is everything.<BR>\r\nso, like, fucking on the next date, probably.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12682,2221,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','modern life is a direct extension of the primitive. in early agricultural times would you have longed for a return to the ways of the hunter-gatherer? also i don\\\'t understand the slamming of capitalism that goes on. just wanting to live makes you a capitalist, that\\\'s why systems based on this principle continue to exist while others don\\\'t, because the others don\\\'t account for man\\\'s natural tendancy to fight for himself above all others.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.131','en'),(12683,2221,'2002-01-08 17:18:08',0,'stella','<P>Considering who I am, yes, in primitive times I probably would have wanted to return to hunter gatherer.. I\\\'d like to do that NOW... </P><P>\r\nThe slamming of capitalism isn\\\'t the slamming of the basic foundations of the system, for me anyway. I am slamming the massive mega-corporations that cause massive amounts of damage around the world. The ones that cheer on the world bank and the IMF. Ones like AOL Time Warner who own huge amounts of other corporations and are continually buying more. Basically those who drive the little guys out. Once there is no longer room for the little guy, it becomes a monopoly. Monopolies put an awful amount of power and money in the hands of very few. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12684,2221,'2002-01-08 17:19:13',0,'but wait...','arent the \"little guys\" capitilists too?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12685,2221,'2002-01-08 17:23:57',0,'stella to','',0,0,0,1,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12686,2221,'2002-01-08 17:27:52',0,'stella to \\\'but wait\\\'','umm, did you read the whole post? not to be crabby with you, but it says right there that my personal problem isn\\\'t with capitalism as a system but the ways in which it works by the big corporations... Of course the little guys are capitalists. That\\\'s not the point. Perhaps my initial post has confused you. I should have worded it differently. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12687,2224,'2002-01-08 17:31:02',0,'hands','hands across america<br>\r\nhands across the water<br>\r\nhands of esoterica<br>\r\nhands upon your daughter<br>',4,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12688,2221,'2002-01-08 17:33:14',0,'but wait...','the \\\"little guys\\\" are the ones that ran us away from sourcing all our needs form the earth in the first place. this is perplexing. so the big corporations run the little guys over. it still estranges us all from \\\"mother earth\\\" and self-dependence, which is what you want to get back to, yes? (best friendly!)',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12689,2221,'2002-01-08 17:56:21',0,'stella','<p>I think that I\\\'ve somehow caused a confusion. My goal isn\\\'t to \\\'get back to the earth\\\', necessarily. It would be VERY nice to be wholly self-sufficient, but very difficult these days. It can happen, I just don\\\'t know that I have the level of determination to live in the manner the would be the result. </P><P>The goal is to live in a way that does not support massive corporate structures. Not to give my money to companies who perpetuate the demise of a true democratic society; companies who do evil things. To support small, local business and therfore putting money back into the hands of \\\'the people\\\' (jeez, that sounds cliche). Also, to move my life into an area where I don\\\'t need a lot of money. To not be materialistic. Ideally, I\\\'d like to own a farm (not a commune) where I could grow my own food, and provide for myself so that I didn\\\'t need to buy it. Be able to make my own clothing. Buy only things that I can\\\'t make. </P>\r\n<P>Apparently, I\\\'m not representing myself well. That\\\'s okay though. I understand what I mean...</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12690,2223,'2002-01-08 18:05:02',0,'leonard','<p>Here are anagrams of \\\"anagramssuck\\\", with a minimum word length of 4: </p>\r\n<p>1. MACASSAR GUNK <br>\r\n  2. ANAGRAM SUCKS <br>\r\n  3. SAGA CRAM SUNK <br>\r\n  4. SAGA MARC SUNK <br>\r\n  5. SAGA SCAN MURK <br>\r\n  6. SAGA RANK SCUM <br>\r\n  7. AMRA SACK SUNG <br>\r\n  8. AMRA SACK SNUG <br>\r\n  9. AMRA CASK SUNG <br>\r\n  10. AMRA CASK SNUG <br>\r\n  11. AMRA SANG SUCK <br>\r\n  12. AMRA SNAG SUCK <br>\r\n  13. SANA CRAG MUSK <br>\r\n  14. SANA RACK SMUG <br>\r\n  15. NASA CRAG MUSK <br>\r\n  16. NASA RACK SMUG <br>\r\n  17. RASA MACK SUNG <br>\r\n  18. RASA MACK SNUG <br>\r\n  19. RASA SACK MUNG <br>\r\n  20. RASA CASK MUNG <br>\r\n  21. RASA SCAM GUNK <br>\r\n  22. RASA SANG MUCK <br>\r\n  23. RASA SNAG MUCK <br>\r\n  24. SARA MACK SUNG <br>\r\n  25. SARA MACK SNUG <br>\r\n  26. SARA SACK MUNG <br>\r\n  27. SARA CASK MUNG <br>\r\n  28. SARA SCAM GUNK <br>\r\n  29. SARA SANG MUCK <br>\r\n  30. SARA SNAG MUCK <br>\r\n  31. CRAG MASK ANUS <br>\r\n  32. MACK SANG URSA <br>\r\n  33. MACK SNAG URSA <br>\r\n  34. MACK GAUR SANS <br>\r\n  35. RACK GUAM SANS <br>\r\n  36. SACK SANG ARUM <br>\r\n  37. SACK SNAG ARUM <br>\r\n  38. SACK GAUR MANS <br>\r\n  39. CASK SANG ARUM <br>\r\n  40. CASK SNAG ARUM <br>\r\n  41. CASK GAUR MANS <br>\r\n  42. SCAM GAUR SANK <br>\r\n  43. SCAN GAUR MASK <br>\r\n  44. SCAR GUAM SANK </p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12691,2221,'2002-01-08 18:08:17',0,'devils advocate','no, youre doing a great job of tackling these comments :)<br><br>\r\nyou were doign fine until you mentioned \"companies who do evil things\"... ok, so nike employs slave-labour, this can be argued... but what does barnes and nobles (your earlier example) do thats so evil? perhaps this is a rhetorical question... if you say that \"they take business away from the little guys\", i\'d have to anticipate that by saying that the smaller chains took business away from the local bookstones that took business away from the local bookbinders who took business away form the individual bookmakers, etc...<br><br>\r\nsome call it progress. for every one person who would rather go non-corporate/capitalist/commercial, theres about 50 zillion who would rather just go to the corner store and buy the damn book on sale. this raises the whole question of utility (and if its worth it to go au naturale, in the whole scheme of things) but thats another question entirely, so i wont go off on that.<br><br>\r\nbest friendly. im not trying to piss you off, i actually am genuinely interested in your thoughts re: all this...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12692,2223,'2002-01-08 18:15:02',0,'CRAG MASK ANUS','MACK SANG URSA',6,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12693,2224,'2002-01-08 19:26:33',0,'hands','for punting\r\nhands for good\r\nhands for hunting\r\nhands for food\r\nyum hands!',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(12694,2221,'2002-01-08 19:44:21',0,'stella','<P>I suppose the question should be \\\"who owns barnes & noble\\\"? If they\\\'re independently owned, which I doubt (and I\\\'ll admit that I don\\\'t know if they\\\'re independent or not), then they are a little guy. They would be a small corporation working against all odds and succeeding. However, I doubt that they are independent, in which case the sins of the father...</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12695,2221,'2002-01-08 19:54:33',0,'er...','so, if theyre not independently owned? what does that mean? how is that any different?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12696,2221,'2002-01-08 20:03:08',0,'stella','<P>These are not meant to be snotty questions:</P>\r\n\r\n<P>Do you understand the nature of the mega corporation? Do you know why some people are against free trade? Are you familiar with the IMF and World Bank and their policies? </p>\r\n<p>If so, you should know the difference. If not, do some reading and find out. I can\\\'t go into ithere and now (I\\\'m at work). If you\\\'re still interested, say so and leave an email address and we\\\'ll take this conversation off of Swinney as I\\\'m sure no one else is interested. </P>\r\n<P> Oh, and tell me who you are :)</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12697,2221,'2002-01-08 20:30:15',0,'jo','i\\\'m going to offer what i can -- b&n stores themselves are suspect because they\\\'re in cahoots with starbuck\\\'s. that\\\'s enough to make me hate them. b&n the brand -- meaning anything with b&n in the name, like bn.com which actually has NOTHING to do with individual b&n stores -- is like any other chain. they offer some things much more cheaply than independent stores could, like their bargain books and discounted bestsellers, but their average text is $2-4 more than you should spend. bn.com, my company\\\'s major client, is SATAN INCARNATE. not just because they\\\'re our client; i\\\'ve had friends who worked for them in nyc and cited them as being pure evil.<BR>\r\nhope that helped.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12698,2221,'2002-01-08 20:32:39',0,'stella','thanks jo... I felt like I was drowning there... ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12699,2222,'2002-01-08 20:42:38',0,'trouble','best of luck with your experiement.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12700,2221,'2002-01-08 20:51:03',0,'ryan','what are you going to wipe your ass with?',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.196','en'),(12701,2221,'2002-01-08 20:58:15',0,'critic to jo','not that its any of my business (read: not intended to piss you off, this comment!) but, er, uhm, you never mentioned <i>why</i> they were evil.... <br><br>i think chocolate and tickle me elmo are evil, too, but that doesnt make them so. hows about some hard evidence about why these corporations suck so hard? alot of us 20-30somethings love to diss all over corporate america, but cant explain why. its like we jsut need some cause to feel worth in our lives ;)',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12702,2222,'2002-01-08 21:00:09',0,'two week benders','dont get you out of academic probation, unfortunately.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12703,2221,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','another good example of the evil that mega-conglomerate-bookstores can do is illustrated with canada\\\'s chapters chain.  they bought up their 3 biggest rivals (among others) and are now a complete monopoly aside from tiny mom & pop stores.  a few short months after attaining their monopoly, chapters began banning politically controversial books like \\\'mein kampf\\\' and \\\'das kapital\\\'.  this decision was made solely by the ceo of chapters, heather reisman.  she labelled the books \\\'hate literature\\\' while admitting that she\\\'s never read them.  and this one person has the ability to make 2 books of giant historical significance nearly impossible to find in canada, for 30 million people.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12704,2221,'2002-01-08 21:25:52',0,'jo','okay. how about signing contracts agreeing to certain terms and then threatening, just before each deadline, to bail if there aren\\\'t huge changes made, huge changes that require a 20 person team to sacrifice their entire weekend just to come into work and attend to your needs? what about VP who makes insane demands upon your team and then, when confronted about it later, denies it completely? what about when the same VP requires that all HER people are chosen for positions and then, months later, upon reading their credentials, berates you for having allowed them to be given those positions when they\\\'re clearly not qualified for them? evil enough yet?',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12705,2221,'2002-01-08 22:06:01',0,'Starbuck & McDonalds^','We take money out of your community and invest it in opening more Starbucks and McDonalds in other communities.  We like to open up our new stores across from places like Big Bob\\\'s Burger and BBQ because we know that people in the community are already used to going to that particular area to eat and it will be successful.  Once we put Big Bob\\\'s out of business we will tear it down and build a Wall-Mart because the local hardware, sports, and clothing stores are just too placid.  Thank you for your support.',0,0,0,0,'172.138.99.137','en'),(12706,2219,'2002-01-08 22:08:52',0,'hool','<p>in pre-popular internet days, i met people from BBSs in areas surrounding the rural area i lived in. in those days, weirdness was the rule, not the exception.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>memories of the novelty of a multi node BBS where you could actually chat in real time with another person - though they probably lived in your area code and were really weird.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(12707,2227,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','hey gus. i remember this email, i think. i really do solemnly swear that soon i will come over with some pabst so we can look through those photos. i can\\\'t wait to see them.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12708,2227,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','yay gus this is rad! i want to read the whole travelogue again for sure! you should put it up on your site too!',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12709,2227,'2002-01-08 23:59:33',0,'sims','yeah, le, i agree',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12710,2227,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','yah I will keep editing and post them here for yas. then one day when I can figure out the right way to batch resize, I\\\'ll put the writings up with the photos somewheres.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12711,2222,'2002-01-09 00:10:33',0,'huge_wobbly_penis','i used to be scared of the taco. now i like it.',1,0,0,0,'140.174.185.248','en'),(12712,2222,'2002-01-09 00:26:08',0,'small_lonely_vagina','Yeaaaaaaaaa! for the Huge_Wobbly_Penis!!!!!',4,0,0,0,'209.247.141.56','en'),(12713,2228,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','I love finding notes in unusual places (especially ones of such a nature as this).  The more abstract ones force you to create an entire backing story to what produced the note and in that way, it\\\'s fun like a Choose Your Own Adventure.',1,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12714,2221,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the book <a href=\\\"http://www.commoncouragepress.com/danaher_globalize.html\\\">globalize this!</a> is an excellent collection of essays on the topic. also, the <a href=\\\"http://globalizethis.org/\\\">website by the same name</a> is another useful resource. check out the bookstore link.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12715,2227,'2002-01-09 05:39:05',0,'Paul G','Gus you are good; this really tasted Thai.',1,0,0,0,'67.24.8.43','en'),(12716,2229,'2002-01-09 05:54:11',0,'manwhore...','essemar?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12717,2228,'2002-01-09 05:56:12',0,'Paul G','When I was a kid my father took me to work with him on the west side of the city.  The plant that he worked at was under the El train.  We parked under one of the old iron supports for the tracks above us.  Leaning against the support, and resting on the dirty asphalt, was a polaroid photograph.  In the picture a Black guy was wearing a Santa suit and standing in front of a Christmas tree.  He had his pants down around his ankles, a big smile on his face, and he was proudly displaying his manhood.  It was July at the time.  Ho ho ho.',2,0,0,0,'67.24.8.43','en'),(12718,2229,'2002-01-09 06:38:31',0,'Scapegoat','Pardon?',0,0,0,0,'64.229.169.219','en'),(12719,2224,'2002-01-09 06:48:12',0,'Javorai','pelotudo imbecil hijo de puta, chupate esta mandarina.',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.158','en'),(12720,2221,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','&quot;We have met the enemy, and He is Us!&quot;\r\n<p>\r\n-- Pogo',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.131','en'),(12721,2229,'2002-01-09 07:12:58',0,'smaug','i am one BiG badASS dragon...and if you want to conquer me the only way is with the heart of a Hobbit.',6,0,0,0,'209.245.132.5','en'),(12722,2228,'2002-01-09 08:42:04',0,'carter','none of this makes me a bad person. i did nothing wrong. <a href=\\\"http://www.vh1.com/thewire/content/news/1451702.jhtml\\\">i\\\'m not a bad person.</a>',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(12723,2229,'2002-01-09 09:29:01',0,'Joanna','Gosh, this all sounds very familiar, just from the other side of the bed. ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(12724,2228,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','really nice find. perhaps a page torn from the notepad of <a href=\\\"http://www.canoe.ca/AllPopShowbiz/jan7_nick-can.html\\\">a journalist</a>?',0,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12725,2229,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','sometimes,  you just have to beat things into the ground and make them as ugly as possible in order to force yourself to understand just how bad they are, and just how badly you need to get out of the situation. \r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12726,2229,'2002-01-09 12:08:48',0,'critic','...and sometimes you just have to use maturity, common sense, and all the wisdom you can muster to understand and avoid such situations from the get-go.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12727,2229,'2002-01-09 12:12:14',0,'man whore','sorry \\\'scapegoat\\\', i assumed you might be \\\'essemar\\\' as your piece reminded me of his usual mack-daddy-in-the-ways-of-ego-sex in <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1907.txt\\\">this</a>.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12728,2229,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i hear the words \"man whore\", I inexplicably think of \"deuce bigalow\".</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12729,2228,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>aw, i didn\\\'t even think of the backstreet boys fiasco.  that\\\'s too bad, i had a whole different story built up in my head.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh well, at least i got to hear about the polaroid of santa claus-flasher.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12730,2228,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i think it\\\'s cute that he cried in the police car.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12731,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>there\\\'s something uncannily appealing about the instant gratification of a <a href=\\\"http://www.chemie.unibas.ch/holder/JPG/sx701.jpg\\\">polaroid</a>. pardon me if ive mused about this <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1772\\\">before</a>.<br><br>lately, having grown tired of digicams, ive been carrying my polaroid around the streets of manhattan, snapping pictures of random people doing even more random things, and then giving them the photo before it can develop. theyre typically nonplussed. they usually stand there, silently watching it develop as i walk off.<br><br>\r\nmy favourite: about two weeks ago, taking a picture of the Halleluia! Man (an aging eccentric who trots the avenues chanting \\\"halleluia, halleluia! jesus! god! love!\\\", noticing that the photo coincidentally developed with a halo-like haze around his head. ahh, the wonders of <a href=\\\"http://www.goinggallery.com/\\\">time-zero</a> film.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12732,2228,'2002-01-09 15:04:11',0,'jo to peter','peter, stories like this make me really excited that i know you.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12733,2227,'2002-01-09 15:06:13',0,'stella','I would like to know where gus\\\' website is. I would like to see it.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12734,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to jo','<br><tt>hi jo! you should see some of the wonderful lomos i made from the back of your pickup while zipping around austin.  [ill scan as soon as i get the old usb adapter going on].</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12735,2227,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\\\"One night in Bangkok makes a hard man humble\\\"</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12736,2227,'2002-01-09 15:09:01',0,'stella','mmmhmm, now I see. reading the bylines helps. I was unaware that gus = egg. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12737,2227,'2002-01-09 15:17:16',0,'huh?','gus = egg?!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12738,2227,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','gus is not egg. they are two different people. gus visited southeast asia last year, and wrote about her travels. this is one of her stories from that trip.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12739,2227,'2002-01-09 15:45:39',0,'stella','<P>Sorry!</P>\r\n<P>this -----> \\\"from march 2000 (egg is off to thailand! Im going to edit my southeast asia travelogue for swinney.org)\\\" is misleading. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12740,2228,'2002-01-09 16:55:31',0,'trouble','after speaking at an junior high school computer club about the internet and networking one morning i went into the bathroom to find a note written on toilet paper, neatly folded and piched between the coat rack on the back of the door. <br><br>the note was titled \\\'last will and testament of michael ------ \\\'.  in it he chronicled two hours spent hiding out in that very stall the night before during a dance.  he wrote he was going to die from a broken heart now that he spied his girlfriend kissing his friend that night before at the dance.  i have never ever forgotten that note and hadn\\\'t thought about it for months until i read this article.  i hope to god i still have it in the shoebox in my closet.  <br><br>i went back as a follow up on the class a few weeks later and asked some of the kids if they knew michael and they had told me he moved the week before.  <br><br>michael, i hope you\\\'re ok.',2,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(12741,2228,'2002-01-09 17:12:07',0,'!','LOMO! LOMO! today my friend john ratliff is wearing a shirt with a lomo on it that says in small non-threatening letters: \\\"don\\\'t think.\\\" i was excited by this.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12742,2228,'2002-01-09 17:13:27',0,'it should be known','that calling him just \\\"john\\\" would be like saying \\\"<a href=\\\"http://mr.unpopular.com/~szajic\\\">stefan zajic</a>\\\" instead of \\\"stefan fucking zajic.\\\" so wrong!',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12743,2229,'2002-01-09 17:41:50',0,'man-whore?','whore (hÃ´r, hr)<br>\r\nn. <br>\r\nA prostitute. <br>\r\nA person considered sexually promiscuous. <br>\r\nA person considered as having compromised principles for personal gain.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nno mention of gender...\r\n',0,0,0,0,'66.69.229.76','en'),(12744,2229,'2002-01-09 17:47:08',0,'rabble rouser','who said anything about gender? ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12745,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>lomo is nice, lomo is happy, lomo is definitely non-threatening. as of this weekend, my wall is finally covered with 28 sq. ft. of lomoness.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12746,2228,'2002-01-09 18:52:28',0,'jo to peter','drooooooool.<BR>\r\nbest. design idea. ever.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12747,2229,'2002-01-09 18:55:06',0,'man-whore?','\\\"man whore\\\" implies that normally, only women are whores. ',0,0,0,0,'66.69.229.76','en'),(12748,2229,'2002-01-09 19:15:46',0,'pony','arf! arf!',1,0,0,0,'209.245.131.161','en'),(12749,2229,'2002-01-09 19:26:56',0,'manwhore','no, \\\"whore\\\", being gender-less, as you so pointedly pointed out earlier, requires a modifier. man whore, girl whore, blue whore, fishy whore, cold whore, <a href=\\\"http://www.onetrickpony.org\\\"><b>pony whore</b></a>, etc. <br><br>\r\n<i>[x] arf! arf!</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12750,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/lomowall.JPG\"><br><br><tt>with 27 frames still to go!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12751,2229,'2002-01-09 19:34:59',0,'Men\\\'s Room?','\\\"men\\\'s room\\\" implies that normally, only women are rooms. ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12752,2229,'2002-01-09 19:36:38',0,'scape-goat?','goat (gt)<br>\r\nn. <br>\r\nAny of various hollow-horned, bearded ruminant mammals of the genus Capra, originally of mountainous areas of the Old World, especially any of the domesticated forms of C. hircus, raised for wool, milk, and meat.<br> \r\nA lecherous man. <br><br>\r\n\r\nno mention of scape.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12753,2217,'2002-01-09 19:57:21',0,'george','i didn\\\'t mean to read this',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12754,2229,'2002-01-09 19:58:36',0,'sims to men\\\'s room','actually, it doesn\\\'t',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12755,2217,'2002-01-09 20:00:12',0,'jo to george','weird! read what you like.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12756,2229,'2002-01-09 20:07:17',0,'men\\\'s room to sims','precisely my point...',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12757,2217,'2002-01-09 20:08:26',0,'critic to jo','so jo. what are you going to do someday when mike gets to know you really well and stumbles across this piece? that ought to be neat.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12758,2227,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','peter, I had that damn song stuck in my head whenever I was in Bangkok (and over all I was there a month. aiiee!)\r\n<p>\r\nstella, its not confusing! egg\\\'s leaving inspired me to edit my shit from when I was there! ',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12759,2217,'2002-01-09 20:27:32',0,'jo','he\\\'s already read it. i showed it to him.<BR>\r\ndude, i\\\'m a WIDE OPEN BOOK.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12760,2228,'2002-01-09 21:00:45',0,'agatha','<p>unrelated question:<br>\r\nhave you ever sent your film to lomo for developing?  if so, did they ever \\\"lose\\\" it?  this happened to my first and only roll of lomo film thus far, and i\\\'m wondering if it\\\'s a karmic sign or something.</p>',3,0,0,0,'12.229.43.233','en'),(12761,2229,'2002-01-09 21:09:27',0,'sims','i started to type a response to say i knew what you meant and here\\\'s why your point is flawed and then i was like oh my this is a really dumb arguement, so i stopped.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12762,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Agatha','<br><tt> everything tied to <a href=\\\"http://www.lomo.com\\\">lomo.com</a> seems to be kind of (however well intentioned) an effort to capitalize in on the lomo name/craze, so take their info with a grain or two of salt (i,e: \\\'the relentless winking eye\\\', \\\'shoot from the hip!\\\', etc). i dont reccommend using their service. ive heard alot of stories like yours. plus, they only offer you basically the same service that you\\\'d get at your corner rite aid, but they charge you twice as much and take twice as long to do it. all they do is throw in some stickers and some glitzy/cheez packaging. same goes for their film.<br><br>\r\nim all about 99 cent <i>Lucky Film</i> and $1.99 rite-aid developing!</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12763,2229,'2002-01-09 21:22:04',0,'men\\\'s room','i wasn\\\'t arguing.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12764,2227,'2002-01-09 21:48:09',0,'stella','So, like, where\\\'s your website? I like your writing. I\\\'m interested in reading more. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12765,2228,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah me too! let\\\'s hear it for the dollar store',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12766,2227,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i dont really have any writing at my website. there are poems on the old version of my webpage and thats it. oh and the askallison crap. you can look if you want, floozy.com/allison but really you wont find much.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12767,2229,'2002-01-09 22:46:14',0,'Scapegoat','<p>Being new to Swinney I have to admit I am really impressed. Following the link to Essemar\\\'s article I notice that emotional masochism is not something that I struggle against alone and I also see passing reference to my Alias there. I will attempt to become more of a regular contrubutor.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.174.27','en'),(12768,2205,'2002-01-09 22:56:32',0,'Ev','Good voice throughout the piece.  I like the Rapunzel detail: it personalizes it just that little bit more.',2,0,0,0,'64.171.254.122','en'),(12769,2219,'2002-01-09 23:18:15',0,'joyrider','<p><b>to treetop\\\'s outcome:</b>  excellent excellent.  nice to hear things are okay.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the Internet takes me all sorts of places,  like a raver commune in downtown columbus, to a desert trance party at the end a lonely dirt road east of phoenix,  to the arms of a few amazing women, to powell\\\'s city of books in green portland, and lots of places in between and elsewhere.  where should i say thanks?</p>  ',2,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(12770,655,'2002-01-09 23:19:30',0,'ev','I think I am going to start making a numeric list every day this year. This is inspirational.\r\n\r\n',2,0,0,0,'64.171.254.122','en'),(12771,2230,'2002-01-09 23:26:38',0,'ssj3gostu','i liked that. it reminded me of <a href=\\\"http://kelvinator6.diaryland.com/index.html\\\">this</a>.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.227','en'),(12772,2229,'2002-01-09 23:52:23',0,'Nancy Reagan','Just Say No!  ',4,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12773,1122,'2002-01-10 00:08:30',0,'joyrider','hm.  i get there by walking and thinking and writing down dreams (and puzzling them out later) and eating new things.  there are other ways, too.  sometimes i take psychedelic drugs.  reading good stuff works well.\r\n\r\ni am celibate, but that\\\'s more of an incidental thing.  i don\\\'t think i draw anything from that.  maybe if i thought about it positively i would?',2,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(12774,2225,'2002-01-10 00:28:05',0,'eclipse','Holy shit, Bartleby, I don\\\'t think I\\\'ve read something quite so realistic in my life. That\\\'s amazing. That was totally DEAD ON. Good job.',2,0,0,0,'64.228.68.132','en'),(12775,2229,'2002-01-10 00:42:44',0,'Scapegoat to Joanna','<p>You say it sounds familiar? I just don\\\'t understand why someone would do this after being told how things are going to end up.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.168.53','en'),(12776,2229,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i don\\\'t think thinking of deuce bigalow is inexplicable at all, given the context!',1,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12777,978,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>wow. i actually think this is the first article i ever read on here. interesting!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12778,2229,'2002-01-10 03:35:10',0,'Paul G','<p>Scapegoat: \\\"Then he shall take two goats...Aaron shall cast lots upon the two goats, one for the Lord and the other for Azazel...Then he shall kill [one goat as a ] sin offering for the people...and Aaron shall lay both hands upon the head of the live goat, and confess upon him all the inequities of the people of Israel...he shall put them upon the head of the goat, and send him away into the wilderness...to Azazel...\\\" Leviticus 16:7-26. (A very down and dirty compression of the text.)</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The scapegoat is the goat upon which the sins of the people are put.  The exact meaning of Azazel is unclear (or so I have been taught); it might be a place, or the proper name of some entity, or a concept of some sort.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This seems to have been a ritual that was not unique to the people of Israel, but rather it was practiced in other areas of the Ancient Near East as well.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To be a \\\"scapegoat\\\" is to have some wrong, done by others, placed upon you.</p>\r\n\r\n ',3,0,0,0,'65.230.72.98','en'),(12779,2229,'2002-01-10 03:52:26',0,'Joanna to Scapegoat','Because sometimes when you\\\'re in love, or when you\\\'ve  convinced yourself that you\\\'re in love, it\\\'s very hard to let go.  My story has slightly different circumstances than your story, but there were still a lot of similarities.   ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(12780,2227,'2002-01-10 04:23:44',0,'a humble man','<p>I loved Bangkok.  Some vivid memories:</p>\r\n<p>the thai kick boxing match where the entire crowd yelled in unison with each punch/kick and mass betting going on in between rounds via crazy hand signals</p>\r\n<p>I tell my travel partner \\\"I want to experience everything that life has to offer\\\" and then we come across a street vendor selling fried beetles, larvae, black scorpions and other disgusting things, so he challenged me to eat the scorpion and I couldn\\\'t.  It was so fucking revolting.</p>\r\n<p>the surreal sexual neon carnival atmoshpere of nana plaza, home of many go go bars.</p>\r\n<p><a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/Tokyo/Temple/1107/whyweb.html\\\">Thermae Coffeeshop.</a></p>\r\n<p>Riding a tuktuk home in the middle of the 85 degree night.</p>\r\n',2,0,0,0,'216.175.116.90','en'),(12781,2229,'2002-01-10 05:08:32',0,'caitlin','this is a great example of why lesbians should rule the world. maybe asexual women. Valerie Solanas would claim that all women are asexual. I dunno. maybe I am just an indecisive woman who does not understand the opposite sex. although, this reminds me that I really don\\\'t want to either. ',0,0,0,0,'216.150.228.8','en'),(12782,2229,'2002-01-10 05:30:23',0,'conscience to caitlin','yes, and a hearty HOORAY for SEXUAL PREJUDICE! ',4,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12783,2229,'2002-01-10 05:31:19',0,'A Woman','speak for yerself. im <b>definitely</b> not asexual.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12784,2229,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to caitlin','<br><tt>dont worry, we\\\'re not all man-whores</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12785,2229,'2002-01-10 05:58:59',0,'Scapegoat to Paul G','<p>Azazel is actually an angel that was the standard bearer for the rebellion against god and was cast down into hell and was renamed eblis that means despair. He was supposedly appeared to have the hind quarters of a goat and was know for fornicating with mortal women. He has been accredited with such fine things as introducing weaponry and cosmetics to humanity.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.168.53','en'),(12786,2229,'2002-01-10 06:32:39',0,'da to critic','...and that kind of maturity can only be purchased through the endurance and resolution of difficult experiences that.  and the next move to figure out after that is to somehow blend the passion of the initial swing of the pendulum with the maturity that results from the second.',3,0,0,0,'209.86.214.112','en'),(12787,2229,'2002-01-10 07:08:55',0,'Javorai','I guess I have to agree with le',0,0,0,0,'208.52.53.18','en'),(12788,2229,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','that Azazel sounds like the christianized interpretation of saytrs/pan. dont it? it do.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12789,2229,'2002-01-10 07:29:58',0,'Paul G','<p>Names are a dificult thing to be certain of in a language that employed no vowels.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In any case, the very most important things in the Judeo-Christian tradition are not named at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Azazel is a Hebrew word that means \\\"removal\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Eblis, or \\\"Iblis\\\", is Arabic, and it is the name for the being who is the functional equivilent of Satan, in Islam.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Satan is a Hebrew word that means \\\"adversary\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lucifer is a Latin name for a being whom Catholic exorcists place in a position superior to Satan.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Again, while there are instances in which names are given for a single being in two laguages e.g. Abaddon/Apollyon, it is difficult to believe that any such quasi-divine entity would rename itself or be renamed by other such beings in a different Semitic laguage e.g. Azazel/Eblis.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The traditions of Sumeria, Akkadia, Babylonia, Assyria, and Israel too, are old - very old.  I am not a linguist.  I apologize for any inacurracies in this resonse.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Having said that, the experiences that I have had in life thus far, and the little wisdom that I have managed to gather to myself, would both incline me to offer you the following warning:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Avoid both Demonology and (ref. your original post) unhealthy sexual encounters.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Every contact that you have with another being is an opportunity to affect them for good or for ill.  Take such occasions to do what good you can, and in so doing, improve yourself too.  Virtue is a habit, according to the Classics.</p>',3,0,0,0,'65.230.72.98','en'),(12790,2229,'2002-01-10 07:47:51',0,'Paul G to Gus','The Hellenes served as mercenaries in the armies of several states in the Ancient Near East, so that they brought home with themselves some of the culture of those \\\"more civilized\\\" peoples for whom they fought.  This process is not unlike that which took place when the Germans served as mercenaries for the Romans, ultimately displacing them.  In any case, Azazel is very much older than Pan or Christianity.  Another relationship between the Hellenes and the Ancient Near East is found in the early pages of Thucydides history \\\"The Peloponnesian War\\\" - Semitic people (Phoenicians) were said to have colonized the islands in the Aegean Sea.',3,0,0,0,'65.230.72.98','en'),(12791,2234,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i\\\'d like a job too. anyone here want to hire me? I\\\'d like to work part time. I\\\'ll be real nice and I wont steal anything. promise!',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12792,2234,'2002-01-10 08:03:24',0,'Scapegoat','<p>Bollucks to the gods, I say - we humans need to switch allegiances from gods to each others. Solidarity of humanity seems to be the answer to me. Too mush evil done following our gods or in their names, perhaps they are the temptation we need to be delivered from.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.168.53','en'),(12793,2229,'2002-01-10 12:21:18',0,'critic to da','actually, i dont agree at all. its probably not even \\\"maturity\\\", more like <i>common sense</i>. i truly think that the average human is embedded with the ability to be honest, rational, and to not act like an \\\"asshole\\\" (not my word, a trait mentioned in the article), even without having lived and aquired previous regrets and scars.\r\n\r\nin this day and age, many of us sometimes choose to writhe in uncomfortable situations like this (myself included, im not saying im holier than thou here, for sure) simply because either:<br><br>\r\n1. the sex is just too good<br>\r\n2. its too much effort to change<br>\r\n3. it makes us vulerable to level with the other person\r\n4. its embarassing to be honest<br>\r\n5. we\\\'re \\\"relationship-wary\\\"<br>\r\n6. we\\\'re impatient<br><br>\r\nalthough these all may be very applicable reasons in context, theyre nothing unsurmountable, or at least, certainly nothing that a little natural common sense, thought, rationality and honesty can resolve.\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12794,2229,'2002-01-10 12:34:23',0,'Scapegoat to critic','<p>I agree that avoiding such situations is the best approach but I feel like an effort has been put forth and things have been said regardless of embarrassment or vulnerability. I am relationship wary and that hasbeen expressed but does not seem to have registered. I am relationship wary and impatient for the situation to become a thing of the past.</p> ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.168.53','en'),(12795,2229,'2002-01-10 12:50:45',0,'critic to scapegoat','i feel you, man, for sure. ive been there myself. as much for myself as for you, i urge you to make a concerted effort to not let the necessity of impatience and wariness push this incomfortably further than it has to go. some things, invariably, require time and sensitivity, even if it seems painstaking to admit that at the moment. just bite the bullet, free the sensitive traits you know you have inside (every human does), brute-force past your wary/impatient tendencies, and get this resolved. <br><br>\r\nshit, its alot of work, i know, but its a hella lot better than racking up yet another regret/bad memory in your life.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12796,2234,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, yes. look at all the things humanity has done in the name of god... now imagine what we\\\'d get away with doing it in the name of <i>each other</i>...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12797,2229,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','is critic a taurus?\r\n<br><br>\r\njavorai: you agree about deuce bigalow?',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12798,2229,'2002-01-10 13:28:26',0,'critic','passion. mystery. well-equipped. i am a taurus indeed. are you perceptive or what?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12799,2233,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','BWAAAAAAAAAAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHA<br><br>Man, that so top\\\'s: \\\"Hey guys, wanna hear me rap?\\\"<br><br>Scoreboard stands Jeff: 1; everyone else: nill.',1,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(12800,2233,'2002-01-10 14:12:59',0,'uhm','er, fuzzy, what are you talking about?',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12801,2233,'2002-01-10 14:13:40',0,'anonymous for obvious reasons','yo, i\\\'d have hit that opportunity in a big way',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12802,2233,'2002-01-10 14:22:10',0,'Joanna','You don\\\'t think that having an older woman offer to pay you money is at all related to the life of a backstreet boy? ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(12803,2233,'2002-01-10 15:32:45',0,'jo','when my best friend lived in new york city for a semester, she used to start phone calls with: \\\"GUESS how many times i\\\'ve been mistaken for a whore!\\\"<BR>\r\n(she is, in absolutely no way, whorelike. whatsoever.)',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12804,2233,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>back when i lived on west end, there was a woman who loitered on the corner of 100th street called <i>The Deep-Throat Blow-Job Woman</i>.  My first encounter with her went something like:<br><br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b>*mumble* *mumble*?<br>\r\n<b>[me]</b> excuse me?<br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b> i said \"deep throat blow job?\"<br>\r\n<b>[me]</b> errr, <i>what</i>?!<br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b> DEEP THROAT BLOW JOB?!<br>\r\n<b>[me]</b> um, no thanks.<br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b> as in: DO YOU WANT ONE?<br>\r\n<b>[me]</b> erk, erhm, ack! <i>(grimacing)</i><br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b> right here! right now!<br>\r\n<b>[me]</b> <i>(walking off swiftly)</i><br>\r\n<b>[dtbj]</b> you know where to find me, boy! HAHAHA!\r\n<br><br>\r\nit appeared that she had similar exchanges of that nature with every single male passerby, and the choice odd female here and there. according to my friends who still make enough to pay west end rent, she\'s still going strong... and still not getting any takers.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12805,2234,'2002-01-10 18:27:31',0,'stella','Hearing a priest referred to as a \\\'tit\\\' is quite possibly the funniest effing thing that I\\\'ve heard all day. Thanks!',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12806,2233,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','When half my hair was platinum with 5 inches of brown on top like long old roots, i had a car or two slow down and honk and look over at me expectantly when I was waiting for the bus. I was super offended becuase all the whores around there have that bad crack acne and look like living turds.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12807,2229,'2002-01-10 19:18:51',0,'caitlinx0r','<i>I</i> didn\\\'t say all women were asexual. [see above for clarification]\r\n\r\nsecondly, most men are born with warm-hole seeking flesh things. and the instinctual drive to accompany.\r\n\r\nI don\\\'t hate men. In fact, I\\\'ve been with my boy for 3 years, and that will continue on for eternity. Sometimes I am reminded harshly that there really are two different sexes.',0,0,0,0,'216.150.228.225','en'),(12808,2237,'2002-01-10 20:19:37',0,'morrissey','All I ask of you <br>\r\nIs one thing that you\\\'ll never do... <br>\r\nWould you put your arms around me? <br>\r\n(I won\\\'t tell anybody)\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(12809,2236,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i am imagining a sort of \\\'martin sheen\\\' style gus riding in the PBR, rain soaking through everything, surfers riding the dark green wake of the boat.  am i close?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni have to change my thailand ticket this week. pity i cant stay go for a few months and have some real fun as opposed to the 10 day bangkok sex tour.',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12810,2228,'2002-01-10 20:27:13',0,'moebius to peter','<p>just wondering: have you ever done the \\\"traditional\\\" photography thing of developing yr own film & making yr own prints? ever since learned to do it that way, i\\\'ve only sent my film to a third party for developing & printing with reluctance--it\\\'s a control thing. and digiphotography is fast & convenient, but seems oddly soulless, too.</p>\r\n<p>on the other hand, doing all yr photography work by hand requires a)a decent darkroom and all the requisite equipment and b)time to get yr prints right, which is hard to come by if you have a day job. but still, if i had both of those in spades, i\\\'d take that route w/my photography. (though your lomo work has inspired me to try one out, maybe, someday.)</p>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12811,2228,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Moe','<br><tt>actually, since i took a high school photo class, ive been doing all my b&w film developing myself in my bathroom (its REALLY easy and cheap too: t-max chemistry). i sneak into the darkroom and print at the university up the street when i can (having some friends that still go there is key, as is being willing to \\\"borrow\\\" paper and supplies, heh). unfortunately, i havent been doing alot of b&w lately, at least that wasnt polaroid... so if the columbia fine arts peeps are reading this, IT WASNT ME!<br><br>\r\ntry the lomo out. you wont regret it. ask <a href=\\\"http://www.steve.org\\\">s5</a>, too; he\\\'s a new lomographer. buy some cheap film and go bananas.</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12812,2236,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a friend of mine went to thailand for 5 days once. she had a lot of good stories. but, the only roll of film she brought back consisted of two shots of a buddhist temple, and 22 shots of a woman shooting ping-pong balls out of her privates. <i>have fun!</i></tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12813,2236,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i didnt go to even one sex show, but I was NEAR them. and I saw some reallly pretty ladymen. \r\n<p>\r\nsorry austin, it was nothing like that. it was me in a boat with 6 other westerners and 1 cambodian lady with her daughter. (her daughter puked over the side a lot) but wait till you read my mekong story about the pigs. ',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12814,1641,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like that last stanza, for some reason:<br><br>\r\nMussels, cockles, whelks<br>\r\nThey\\\'re his passion. They\\\'re his joy<br>\r\nHe\\\'s a Gorilla<br></tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12815,2228,'2002-01-10 22:06:36',0,'agatha','<p>That\\\'s kind of what I thought.  I still haven\\\'t heard back from the last semi-angry email i sent them.  Bastards.<br><br>From now on, it\\\'s the photo hut downtown.  Thanks for the input.</p>',3,0,0,0,'12.229.43.233','en'),(12816,2228,'2002-01-10 22:13:27',0,'sims to photo-kids','dude, i am about to lose my mind being stuck in sf without a decent place to print my own color work. i\\\'m about to move back to new york just to use color edge and the labs at SVA.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12817,2234,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah',':D',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(12818,2233,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>i guess we all get mistaken for someone else from time to time.  i think what was so shocking about this is that she bore a resemblance to my grandmother.  my grandmother!  ...oh dear, i don\\\'t like what my mind is doing now.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12819,2232,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>i didn\\\'t have internet access yesterday (evil isp updating their network) and i literally began pacing around my room in circles before the end of the outage.  i considered knocking myself out with booze, but figured that would be too pathetic.  i have no answers.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12820,2232,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','a while ago i lent xep my laptop so she could use imovie to make a movie, and i could have just hung around her house then, chatting on other computers, but instead i imposed internet-less solitude upon myself in my studio apartment. it was kind of great.i stared at my feet, smoked a lot of pot, listened, REALLY LISTENED, to a lot of my records. just laying in bed stoned listening to record after record. sometimes i would be upset because i knew that parties and things were happening in the outside world, and that if i had the internet i would be able to find them. i would scroll through my cellphone looking for someone to call who wouldn\\\'t be amazed/annoyed with me for using the phone. there was hardly anyone. i started to think too hard, meditate, have serious existential dilemmas about the nature of truth, etc. it was good. ',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12821,2232,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'peter to le','<br><tt>it <i>is</i> good. i did that for about 6 months, with good results.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12822,2222,'2002-01-10 23:48:18',0,'Jah','dubtribe sound system be da steppin razah!  holy mount zion, hub ah all creation!',4,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(12823,2222,'2002-01-10 23:50:52',0,'supa dope fly','<a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1781\\\">i gave praise to jah in the driveway</a>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12824,2232,'2002-01-10 23:53:08',0,'jo','wow. elly. when i read comments like this, i realize how much our worlds, which feel like they\\\'re very similar most of the time, are completely different from one another.<BR>\r\nmy friends make fun of me if i check my email more than once a night.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12825,2232,'2002-01-10 23:54:49',0,'HEY','JOYRIDER! you link to me. rock the fuck on.<BR>\r\nthis is jo, by the way.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12826,2232,'2002-01-11 00:04:41',0,'boohoohoo','i link to you also jo. do i not get props?!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12827,2222,'2002-01-11 00:09:23',0,'ssj3gostu','i went to one of the dubtribe parties here in SF, and honestly? they\\\'re a little haughty taughty.',1,0,0,0,'12.146.203.247','en'),(12828,2232,'2002-01-11 00:20:57',0,'sims to le','yeah, actually, it\\\'s pretty near impossible to find me unless you call my cell phone. unless i\\\'m at work in front of this computer.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(12829,2228,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<br>yeah, developing b/w film at home is fairly easy as long as you have a good changing bag. it\\\'s not much more complicated than making quiche. the only tricky part is finding a decent place to hang yr film up to dry where it won\\\'t get dusty. i\\\'ve used my bedroom closet, but it wasn\\\'t an ideal solution.<br><br>there are other darkrooms in sf (such as <a href=\\\"http://www.exposure39.com/\\\")this one</a>, which does seem to offer color services), but they\\\'re all expensive ($12.50 an hour? oy.) and generally not open 24 hours. maybe i\\\'m crazy but i always loved staying up all night making prints.<br>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12830,2228,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<br>yeah, developing b/w film at home is fairly easy as long as you have a good changing bag. it\\\'s not much more complicated than making quiche. the only tricky part is finding a decent place to hang yr film up to dry where it won\\\'t get dusty. i\\\'ve used my bedroom closet, but it wasn\\\'t an ideal solution.<br><br>there are other darkrooms in sf (such as <a href=\\\"http://www.exposure39.com/\\\">this one</a>, which does seem to offer color services), but they\\\'re all expensive ($12.50 an hour? oy.) and generally not open 24 hours. maybe i\\\'m crazy but i always loved staying up all night making prints.<br>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.170','en'),(12831,2237,'2002-01-11 01:51:41',0,'older morrissey','you can pin and mount me, like a butterfly',0,0,0,0,'65.229.226.95','en'),(12832,2228,'2002-01-11 02:04:25',0,'ryan','there\\\'s a public darkroom in the harvey milk center at the top of duboce park.  they provide the chemistry.  it\\\'s just basic b/w though, no color or nontraditional photographic systems.  cheap too.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.14','en'),(12833,2233,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','your grandmother is an asian woman in her 60\\\'s? wow, i thought i knew you!\r\n\r\ni wish my walk home was as nearly as eventful.',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(12834,2232,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i\\\'m often away from atari while traveling. i don\\\'t miss being online when i can\\\'t get online. if all of the internet went away, well, that might be a different matter. i\\\'d probably read more and have different friends. i might even learn to cook.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12835,2237,'2002-01-11 03:07:20',0,'mozza','do you have a vacancy for a back scrubber?',0,0,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(12836,2235,'2002-01-11 03:51:47',0,'heat','jane marsh in \\\'the lover\\\'. jane marsh wearing that hat and leaning against the railing of the river boat. jane marsh kissing the window.',1,0,0,0,'209.239.210.95','en'),(12837,2235,'2002-01-11 04:01:39',0,'skotchin\\\' ardour','<br><img src=\"http://www.allysheedypages.com/works/film/breakfast%20club,%20the/allison%20-%20video%20capture%20pointing.jpg\"><br>sexy',1,0,0,1,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12838,2237,'2002-01-11 04:53:33',0,'wb','you people are fucking brutal!',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(12839,2237,'2002-01-11 04:53:57',0,'jimbo','this is the end. the end. the end.',0,0,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(12840,2237,'2002-01-11 05:34:44',0,'Sparagmos','<p>It\\\'s a hard thing to deal with watching friends in self destructive behaviours. One can not change it though only be supportive as the freind tries to come to their own realizations.</p> ',2,0,0,0,'64.229.166.197','en'),(12841,2241,'2002-01-11 06:39:30',0,'Sparagmos','<p>I couldn\\\'t agree more. The lights and decorations play a role but it is also a recognition that family and friends are just a little bit older. Seeing everyone come together is amazing but the problem is that they eventually all have to get back to life.</p>',1,0,0,0,'64.229.166.197','en'),(12842,2237,'2002-01-11 06:51:01',0,'Paul G','<p>There seems to have been a bit of discussion about human nature in several responses to submissions, other than this one, made to Swinney.  The question arose in certain replies to the Man whore article: Do men and women have different natures?  Perhaps the question was not put so directly to the reader, but it was evident that at least some of the posters presupposed that the author of said article was â€œacting outâ€ in a â€œtypically maleâ€ way.  And, having made such statements, a poster gives evidence that the question (regarding the existence of separate male and female natures) has not only been asked, but also answered â€“ if only in the mind of the poster.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lin suggests that, in her experience, at least some women capitalize upon the perception that they are â€œweakâ€ and â€œpawnsâ€ because it is in some way expedient for them to do so.  This gives rise to an interesting question: If it is the case that women tend to be perceived as â€œweakâ€ then might it be possible for a woman to gain some measure of strength through the exploitation of such beliefs, rather than the challenging of them?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the Laws Platoâ€™s Athenian stranger suggested that women possessed the ability to be both more cunning and also more secretive than men, as a result of their nature.  If this teaching were correct, then a woman who used such traits to â€œfoolâ€ a man would be employing her â€œnatural strengthsâ€.  True, in the context of the text the Athenian strangerâ€™s goal seemed to be the moderation of the aforementioned characteristics â€“ cunning and secrecy â€“ via alteration of the habits of the women.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is the friend of Linâ€™s, in A Guide To Madness, falling apart because she has followed a path that caused her to display the appearance of a â€œpawnâ€ or â€œweaklingâ€?  Is this then a cautionary tale?  Has Lin been confronted with the prospect â€œof losing respectâ€ for her friend because of this?  Would Lin argue that regardless of whether it is â€œnaturalâ€ for men and women to act differently it is dangerous to do so?</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Maybe Iâ€™m full of crap.  But, I felt very bad for writing: â€œyou can pin and mount me, like a butterflyâ€ after wb made his comment (it is my favorite Morrissey line).  So, it seemed the only remedy was to pay a bit of serious attention to Linâ€™s article, and the questions raised therein.</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'63.155.148.86','en'),(12843,2235,'2002-01-11 06:57:47',0,'kini','more, more, more. did you go to indonesia?!',0,0,0,0,'152.91.9.43','en'),(12844,2232,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to jo','well, it\\\'s not like the only way i could communicate with anyone would be email or chat, in order to get them to come out, but i find that most planning of meetings, drinks, parties, happens in email and then last minute hooking up happens on the phone. the problem was that without my email to look back through, i didn\\\'t remember what was happening. like, somebody sent mail on tuesday about a party on saturday, but on saturday, i had no way to look back through my mail to see what was up.\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou know what i mean? \r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12845,2241,'2002-01-11 07:29:17',0,'synapsis^','blink blink',0,0,0,0,'172.171.123.252','en'),(12846,2234,'2002-01-11 07:54:56',0,'kat','temp agencies',1,0,0,0,'209.239.194.205','en'),(12847,2237,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i do not, and never have believed that men and women have a different nature.  i can\\\'t even begin to explain or defend my viewpoint, because it\\\'s the result of so many tiny indications.  but, i firmly believe that when men and women act differently, it is because of the different expectations placed on them by society.  not because their brains are being held hostage by their genitals.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12848,2237,'2002-01-11 08:22:26',0,'pshaw','never let wb make you feel bad, he\\\'s the worst of the lot.',5,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12849,371,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i wish damanda would come back.',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12850,2235,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','no i didnt go there. I went to thailand, cambodia, laos, and burma. more to come.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12851,371,'2002-01-11 09:38:05',0,'horse','shoe',4,0,0,0,'209.239.202.162','en'),(12852,371,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>a game of horse shoes!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12853,2232,'2002-01-11 13:39:26',0,'jo to els','yeah i know completely. i was more sort of commenting on the fact that being <b>without</b> internet access is the norm with my group of friends, rather than the opposite.<BR>\r\nand that\\\'s probably really good for me. because i sure did sit in front of my computer for the majority of my college career and HEY as a result, i have a hefty archive of webpages but only like 3 friends left from college.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12854,2243,'2002-01-11 13:57:06',0,'trouble','there was a gentleman who \\\'lived / spent a great deal of time\\\' in the back of the record store i worked at in 92.  when we would take our smoke breaks we would go out and chat it up and share smokes.  he too was really a great guy, he had just fallen on hard times.  <br><br>most of those folk have stories to tell, its sad that many people just don\\\'t want to hear them.',1,0,0,0,'216.142.219.138','en'),(12855,2235,'2002-01-11 14:00:37',0,'trouble','these are great, thanks for posting them.<br><br>ally sheedy? jane march? yum.',1,0,0,1,'216.142.219.138','en'),(12856,2237,'2002-01-11 14:03:58',0,'jo','um, part of me begs to say, \\\"RIGHT ON, JEEFF!\\\" except that. science points to <a href=\\\"http://my.webmd.com/content/article/1685.50072\\\">testosterone levels</a> -- both in men and women, cause we produce it too, yo -- contributing to mood & behavior & ultimately social skills. i think there\\\'s something to be said for women biologically behaving differently.<BR>\r\nbut obviously social programming has a lot to do with it. i\\\'m betting that two women with the exact same hormone levels, one raised by an assertive independent icon of strong womanhood and the other raised by a stepford wife, are going to behave a little differently.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12857,2235,'2002-01-11 14:04:08',0,'ally sheedy is HOT','<img src=\"http://www.allysheedypages.com/works/film/breakfast%20club,%20the/allison%20-%20staring.jpg\">',1,0,0,1,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12858,2237,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','ISTR another study that said testosterone levels were manipulated by the organism\\\'s behavior, not the other way around.  So while there might be a correlation, I don\\\'t think causality has been established definitively.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12859,2237,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','either way, I think men and women have some biological differences. though we\\\'re free to act however we choose. (its just that most of us choose to act like assholes.)',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12860,2234,'2002-01-11 19:31:29',0,'God','i\\\'m certainly not helping with <i>that</i> attitude.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.161','en'),(12861,2232,'2002-01-11 23:40:08',0,'joyrider to jo','once i lived with people that gave me shit for being online more than an hour a day.  they were compulsive tv freaks.  now they spend all night on aol, tv on, putzing around e!-online.  \r\n\r\n<p>now my new housemates are mall metal loving gamers and spend more time online than i do.  my house is judgement-free.</p>\r\n\r\n<b>update:</b>\r\n\r\n<p><b>steve</b> said i could email him about server space though, so my hiatus might be short!  steve rocks!  <b.le</b> offered to help too.  so many nice people.</p>',2,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(12862,2232,'2002-01-11 23:43:21',0,'joyrider','2nd to last line should have said: <b>le</b> offered to help, too.  ',0,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(12863,2237,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','right you are, jo.  i was thinking more along the lines of our deeper motivations, rather than emotions or moods.  i don\\\'t have much knowledge of how hormones affect our daily thoughts, but i think our true motivations run deeper than that.  interesting article, btw.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12864,1638,'2002-01-12 04:55:52',0,'kona','its ok egg. feeling organic.',0,0,0,0,'24.198.99.50','en'),(12865,2232,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','hey chris. i wanted to email you the other day but didn\\\'t know how, since your domain is going away. what\\\'s a good address for you?',1,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12866,274,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','it\\\'s crazy to think that on the same date, a year later, i had sex in the same room featured in the background, in brisbane australia. sorry, austin.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12867,452,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<font size=\\\"72\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">FORESHADOWING.</font>',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12868,274,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','all those nights of dreaming finally become a reality for you!  all those nights of <i>me</i> dreaming, or <i>you</i> dreaming?\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nover christmas break i sat in my dad\\\'s study at the computer and i remembered about this post because as luck would have it my dad and i sat at the computer the same time the previous year watching this series of sun burned bikini babe pics.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhe was nagging me about being addicted to computers.  he said, \\\"you are going to turn into one of those things!\\\"  and now look at me! im a computer!  but anyway, he nagged me until he saw the \\\"sun burned o. bikini\\\" series.  then i think he understood.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nhi from xep and erics couch.  pendleton just raised his pink ears and said \\\"mreor\\\" and then fell back to sleep.  i think that was a hi.',1,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12869,2237,'2002-01-12 16:18:37',0,'lin','<p> I stand by the view that men and women are completely different in some ways, but that we have equal capability for strength and for how we choose to play up our roles. </p>\r\n\r\n<p> A man putting himself in a situation of compromise would often be looked upon more as pathetic, rather than a victim of their own human nature. Regardless, I understand that all human nature can allow for weak moments. The article for me was about forgivign a friend who should be stronger, and standing beside her to ensure that she makes it back up.</p> \r\n\r\n<p> I tend to get a little protective, a role I play that is no so gender specific. </p> ',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12870,2238,'2002-01-12 16:21:39',0,'lin','<p> People in Toronto are often like automata. Especially when you pass them in transit to or from work. </p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(12871,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.logy.org/~xep/days/2001/051401/pendletub.jpg\\\">\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12872,2247,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','he is the buddha.',0,0,0,0,'66.122.218.90','en'),(12873,2247,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','breakfast?',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12874,2247,'2002-01-12 18:45:43',0,'jo','this is favoritest article. i read it out loud to my roommate. my cat, anastasia, was listening in, and meowred in appreciation of lazy fat buddha cats.',0,0,0,0,'172.172.112.28','en'),(12875,2237,'2002-01-12 18:52:16',0,'jo','i tend to attract momtypes, both male and female. assemble the people i love and you would find a roomful of people who want to cook food for you, pet your head, lovingly support you, listen to your secrets, give you a good kick in the ass when you\\\'re too lazy. i was out with 8 people last night, four of whom were pisces. their college careers look like this: english masters, religion masters, music degree applying for grad school in ethnomusicology, romance languages degree, business degree (we still love her), art degree, design degree, english degree, art degree. we of course got too tipsy and affectionate.<BR>\r\nthis really has nothing to do with the post but i wanted to share it.',0,0,0,0,'172.172.112.28','en'),(12876,2247,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to weeb','<P>i just went out for brekky and guess where i went on haight?  kate\\\'s kitchen.  guess what i had weeb?  guess?  FRENCH TOAST ORGY!\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nfor those not clued in, weeb showed me the way to the french toast at kate\\\'s kitchen some time ago.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwhen the orgy was delivered, i had only tried the normal french toast, i saw the orgy and said, \\\"holy shit on shingle\\\" because it is a large plate with french toast, about 10 slices, covered with a layer of yogurt and honey, covered then with a layer of fresh fruit, and topped with a layer of granola.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit was a large meal.  i ate half and then some.  and then elly showed up and we talked about bcm.\r\n</P>',3,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12877,2247,'2002-01-12 19:01:02',0,'Paul G','\\\"Pendleton will climb ix...\\\" establish a base-camp at the halfway mark, and wait for Sherpas before continuing on to \\\"...the top of his head.\\\" ',1,0,0,0,'65.229.226.115','en'),(12878,2247,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','where is kate\\\'s kitchen?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12879,2247,'2002-01-12 20:19:35',0,'kat','food!?',1,0,0,0,'209.239.197.213','en'),(12880,2247,'2002-01-12 20:29:14',0,'joyrider','he is huge and noble.  \\\"pendleton\\\" suits him.  we have a new cat named aku, which i am told means \\\"evil\\\" in japanese.  i hope she isn\\\'t going to live up to her name.  so far she is mostly an outside adventure pig.',2,0,0,0,'24.205.196.37','en'),(12881,2247,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','what\\\'s a bcm?',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12882,2247,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','yum yum yum yum. kate\\\'s kitchen is at fillmore and haight, on the walgreens side. it\\\'s super yummy old fashioned breakfast food. not very vegan friendly... sigh.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12883,2247,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','my cats are not budda-like. they are freakazoid like. John Henry has slowed down some in the last couple years, but now his new pal kitten Sojourner is speeding him up. He likes to hide up high in the bedcloset to take naps and recover. Sojourner baas instead of meowing.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12884,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','This seems even more retarted, if possible, when I read it now.',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.248','en'),(12885,2244,'2002-01-13 00:25:53',0,'its 420 ca time^','you should move out to ca and join one of those cannabis thingies.  Or you could start visiting Amsterdam.  I reccomend going to ben\\\'s coffee shop.  He has this nice hash called zero-zero.  Copenhagen, Switzerland, and Vancouver are also fun options for anyone seeking that sticky green.\r\n<br> In Copenhagen there is an area called Christianshavn that was a military base unil a bunch of hippies took it over in the 70\\\'s and turned it into a great hash factory. In Interlaken Switzerland you can buy white widow off of the menu in one of the internet cafe\\\'s.  Of course there is always the option of bringing some good seeds back and starting a garden =)',0,0,0,0,'172.147.243.147','en'),(12886,2232,'2002-01-13 01:13:37',0,'joyrider','you can always always get me at ce@racer.net. nomatterwhat.  also - i will be on atari soon!  steve kicks ass.',2,0,0,0,'24.205.196.37','en'),(12887,2247,'2002-01-13 02:30:55',0,'jim to jeff','\"ballistic continental missile\".   just like an icbm only it stays within the country it is launched from.  also known in Washington as \"the suicide pill\".',6,0,0,1,'66.92.4.146','en'),(12888,2247,'2002-01-13 02:32:03',0,'jim to jeeff','\"ballistic continental missile\".   just like an icbm only it stays within the country it is launched from.  also known in Washington as \"the suicide pill\".',6,0,0,1,'66.92.4.146','en'),(12889,1847,'2002-01-13 02:41:50',0,'taco','de ja vu just happened to me..it\\\'s funny cuz at the moment it happened  i had to do something to make it stop it was almost as if i was entering a dream.... right.. all foggy hazy too familiar.. even the words she was saying.. so i stood up ... and looked away and said something completely different.... did she feel it too? It\\\'s not that i fight magical things.. it\\\'s only i don\\\'t like being teased.',6,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12890,2248,'2002-01-13 02:45:40',0,'','I used to think I was pregnant even when i wasn\'t having sex.. so ... i wouldn\'t call it stupid.. that girl not realizing ...now that... I just don\'t see how you wouldn\'t know.. major mental blockage. ',1,0,0,1,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12891,2248,'2002-01-13 02:45:49',0,'taco','I used to think I was pregnant even when i wasn\\\'t having sex.. so ... i wouldn\\\'t call it stupid.. that girl not realizing ...now that... I just don\\\'t see how you wouldn\\\'t know.. major mental blockage. ',1,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12892,2248,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','jesus is back, and she\\\'s a <a href=\\\"http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?file=/news/archive/2002/01/10/MNshark.DTL\\\">cannabalistic fish</a>.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(12893,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','<p>\r\npendleton has been picked up.  also, yvonne visited today.\r\n<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://logy.org/~xep/days/2002/011202/yvonneandpendleton.jpg\\\">',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12894,2246,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<tt>:)</tt>',1,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12895,2247,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i love pendleton. my favorite thing about him is that he\\\'s deathly afraid of laser pointers. pendleton has a look on his face that says \\\"i am recording data about you and sending it back to the home planet while you sleep.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12896,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','Think about it though. She was menstruating as usual, and her stomach was pretty much flat. Creepy. Isn\\\'t it true that if you get pregnant when you\\\'re very young, the tummy doesn\\\'t get that big? I dunno, but so I heard. This girl was...16, I think. But still...geez.',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.246','en'),(12897,2247,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','pls post the pic of pendleton in the sink while dish washing is occuring in another sink.  thx.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12898,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','You have better things to do with your time, brain cells, and money than drugs.  Get a fuckin\\\' hobby!  This lack of weedage may be some higher power\\\'s way of telling you that you should be doing something else with your life...  <em>*best friendly*</em>',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12899,2248,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<p>\r\nThat\\\'s just disturbing.  The immaculate shark and the stealth baby.  So that\\\'s two helpings of disturbing with a side order of the willies.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo here\\\'s two links to laugh at.\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.somethingawful.com/features/history/\\\">somethingawful.com\\\'s history foolishness</a><br>\r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.somethingawful.com/features/scificovers/index.htm\\\">somethingawful.com\\\'s remixing of bad old scifi book covers</a>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>I think everybody should wait on having kids until they know they\\\'re ready.  I mean, imagine how much our world would change if every kid came into a stable environment where they were loved and wanted?</p>\r\n&lt;rant subject=\\\"condoms\\\"&gt;\r\n<p>On a tangent to that topic, I think there is a conspiracy that follows me around and buys the same kind of condoms I use when I need some.  I mean, it\\\'s not like a lot of guys out there likely need/prefer Trojan ENZ Large rubbers (the dark green box), so why is it that when I _don\\\'t_ need them the shops all have like 10 boxes, but when I _do_ need them, I have to go to three or four places to get some?!?!  Bah!  Oh, and the trojan magnums are a total rip-off.  They\\\'re a little longer than the kind I like but they\\\'re skinnier (thus quite ouchy), and a box a) costs more and b) has half as many.  Bah^2!\r\n</p>\r\n&lt;/rant&gt;',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(12900,2249,'2002-01-13 06:13:47',0,'jo','my father was an opera singer, too. i saw every single opera performed by houston grand opera between 1980 and 1992, usually multiple performances. there was a fire extinguisher box in the lobby where i stored my half-drunk coca-colas between intermissions so i could stay awake.',0,0,0,0,'172.130.151.170','en'),(12901,2249,'2002-01-13 06:15:55',0,'jo','also, did your dad\\\'s opera friends come by a lot? my dad\\\'s sure did. they were all chubby with extraordinarily high cheekbones and deep, rolling laughs. opera people kick a lot of ass.',0,0,0,0,'172.130.151.170','en'),(12902,2244,'2002-01-13 06:28:51',0,'higher power to orion','i fucking <i>invented</i> drugs. lay off. stoners do tend to be a bit unambitious, as a whole, but thats no reason to diss <i>all</i> drugs. lighten up. lots of humans- high level thinkers in fact- have hobbies and lives and managed to balance them with drug usage. some might even go so far as to say that their lives are enriched by such usage.<br><br>\r\ndo you use drugs? have you ever? id really like to know the extent of your experiecne, and on what you base this opinion that there are so much better things to do with time/brain/money than drugs. do tell, please.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12903,2249,'2002-01-13 06:53:23',0,'Scapegoat','<p>I think you are straight up right about the websites and journals doing similar things, does for me at least.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.173.212','en'),(12904,2248,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','they have been been \\\"women who didnt know they were pregnant\\\" segments on all the talk shows for years. usually its some fatter person who just thought they had indigestion. If you\\\'re a kid and you dont know your own body at all, and you\\\'re an idiot, I suppose it\\\'s possible this could happen.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12905,2244,'2002-01-13 07:16:06',0,'druggie','yes. i agree. us druggies do tend to get a bit tired of the melodramatic \\\"dont do drugs!\\\" spiel. its like, \\\"ok guys, we get it\\\". i do drugs. i do drugs without losing friends, money, time, hobbies. i did well in school. i have a satisfying, well-paying job, i have a healthy lasting relationship with my partner. if anything, drugs have only enhanced the path of my life. <br><br>\r\nno i dont do smack or crack. those can probably be argued as bad, for sure. i play with psychadelics alot, do a ton of e, smoke weed now and then. i like 2cb, poppers and nitrous. just levelling with you here.<br><Br>\r\ni once had someone tell me to \\\"stop doing drugs!\\\" that they \\\"had a friend who went crazy due to an immense <i>addiction</i> to L.S.D.\\\".<br><br>\r\nplease.<br><br>\r\nthe drugs i do are no worse than the ciggarettes or liquor most of us indulge in- some might even say that my drugs aren\\\'t as degrading to the health. ive discovered my drugs to be enlightening to the mind, something smoking and drinking usually arent.<br><br>\r\nso, im sure your intentions were well-meant, but they are actually a little offensive, at least to me. save the anti-drug digression for the kids. we surely dont need it here. (constructive!)',3,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12906,2249,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','at this point web journals are a flash in the pan in the grand scheme of \\\"passions\\\" that give people purpose. wouldnt it be the <i>act of writing</i> that does it? you\\\'re just seeing more of the writing thanks to the journals maybe. hm? \r\n<p>\r\nfor me, dance is like the opera, though I dont dance much these days, and I let writing sometimes take its place, or partying, or reading. the little livingroom escapes from life.',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12907,2244,'2002-01-13 07:20:23',0,'another druggie to orion','i love your quote that says \"you should be doing something else with your life\". what a rather ignorant generalization it is to make that anyone that does drugs \"doesnt have a life\" or \"doesnt have a worthwhile life\". or even, errr... \"doesnt have a hobby\". yikes. i really wish i knew you better, and maybe id understand your statement, or could more logically pick it apart, but maybe im glad i dont, as what you said kind of frightens me, the fact that people actually think like this, no offense.',4,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12908,2249,'2002-01-13 07:22:39',0,'journalist','us online journalists are the amateur commentators of this digital, postmodern world we live in. \\\"flashes in the pan\\\" is probabyl a bit harsh, thanks.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12909,2244,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i believe that periodically killing brain cells is necessary to trim away at old neural paths.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12910,2244,'2002-01-13 07:27:28',0,'also','i also am intrigued as to why most people, when arguing with/advising a drug-user assume that its the be all and end all in their life. as in, \\\"you should do something more constructive with your life/money/time\\\". i dont know about other people, but i do drugs. theyre definitely not all i do, for sure. i have a nice, enriching life, and i find that other people, who are intelligent, ambitions and wise can often balance more than occasional drug use with their ongoing life processes and trappings, and exceed quite well in both matters. just my two cents. some of my most ambitious and creative moments in my career have come directly from occasional overindulgence in drugs. id be lying if i said that these creative moments didnt help to further my career and make it all the more successful.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12911,2244,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>&lt;sarcasm&gt<br> i avoid drugs like the plague because, as we all know, smoking dope makes impressionable children want to kill their parents! im also terrified about the addiction potential of acid, of course, and the horriffic terror of \"brain-holes\" that ecstacy causes, as well as the general mayhem and loose-morals that drug usage encourages! gulp... drugs even make people want to <i>dance</i>, and i hear that even that can be evil!<br>&lt;/sarcasm&gt</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12912,2244,'2002-01-13 07:48:08',0,'Herr Doktor to orion','first of all, let me say that im not a drug user, never have been. not because its bad, it just doesnt appeal to me.  what i do do, though, is i spend alot of time studying eastern religions. ive also been known to buy alot of books on the subject. ive probably burned my fair share of brain cells contemplating the complex matters the subject entails. my studies have nothing to do with my job, family, spirituality, income, or occupation. <BR><BR>based on your statement, my studies of eastern religions would be as \\\"bad\\\" as drug usage, and you could perhaps (maybe even with some accuracy) admonish me to get a hobby or get a life. but, i bet you wouldnt. whats the difference?<BR><BR>maybe you were just trying to be a rabble rouser? i really hope not. i also really hope you dont come on here and, after reading all these comments based on yours, say something to the effect of \\\"geez people! lighten up!\\\" because surely you knew this would result. surely you knew that posting that comment would get the goat of most of us, druggies and non druggies alike ;)<BR><BR>destabilizing...\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12913,2249,'2002-01-13 07:50:10',0,'Paul G to Jo','You know this story!  Yes, while as a rule my father preferred to leave the home to meet with the opera people, I remember being woken up occasionally in my childhood by the sound of booming and rolling laughter; something like the Flintsone\\\'s Barney Rubble - if only Barney had balls the size of coconuts: Ah ha ha ha.  I knew that meant that the opera guy that we called Uncle Bob was over; his wife: Aunt Gerri, was great too - she did crazy things like \\\"yoga\\\".  I would run downstairs in my little pajamas to see them, and eat whatever new food product they happened to bring.  Great people they were: lots of noise, lots of drama, lots of hugs (cuddles, in the Southern Hemisphere).  I think that they had a wonderful way of loosening up the German/Swiss home of my dear mother.  ',0,0,0,0,'63.155.148.111','en'),(12914,2244,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','the \\\"drug users are less productive/lazier/less active than non-drug users\\\" argument is tired. may i recommend <a href=\\\"http://veryimportantpotheads.org/\\\">this site</a> for a fresher viewpoint?',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12915,2244,'2002-01-13 09:12:32',0,'ssj3gostu','all i know is, one time, i saw helen hunt throw herself out of the second story window of her high school because she smoked angel dust and thought she could fly.\r\n<p>\r\nsuffice to say, i never want to smoke angel dust.</p>',1,0,0,0,'208.58.87.82','en'),(12916,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12917,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','<p>\r\ni think i\\\'m just going to have to refer you to the entire directory:\r\n<br>http://www.logy.org/~xep/days/2001/021901/\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12918,2247,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<h1>SINKLETON!!!</h1>\r\n(overwhelming affection for giant white pussycat &amp; it\\\'s owners)',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12919,2247,'2002-01-13 11:14:43',0,'MEOW!!','<img src=\\\"http://www.logy.org/~xep/days/2001/021901/ericandpendleton.jpg\\\">',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12920,2249,'2002-01-13 11:15:29',0,'Paul G to Gus','<p>Gus, you write beautiful things.  Whether you draw my attention to something small and near, like drops of rain, or something very large and remote, like Thailand, your writing reminds me of the richness and beauty of the world in which I live.  You are very good at creating a sense of place with the written word.  Presumably, while the act of writing those things is rewarding, it is even more rewarding to know that your work is being read by others, and appreciated.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Dance might be better suited to conveying some ideas than are words, and likewise words might be better suited to conveying some ideas than is dance.  But, it would seem to be the case that both would be more complete in some way if they had an audience.  And, both activities would seem to be fulfilling if both their practice and also their final execution served to bind the artist to other individuals either capable of performing the same act or of appreciating the act.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It seems too that personal websites and on-line journals would be better suited to some form of self-expression than other media, and less well suited to other forms of self-expression than other media.  I think that the particular concern for which the personal website or on-line journal is best suited is somehow related to \\\"individual growth\\\" inasmuch as they are very \\\"time sensitive\\\" and require constant tending by their keepers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I found Swinney via Olivia\\\'s website (<--insert link that I am not clever enough to make, here).  Now, I have followed that particular site longer than any other, and through it I have come to appreciate a bit about the unique potential that lies within the website/journal.  Even as an opera has a stage, and actors, and costumes, and a musical score, all brought together by the work of a composer, so too a website like Olivia\\\'s has similar parts, only they are brought together in a screen instead of a stage.  And, rather than telling a story about some heroes from the past, that site and others like it chronicle the personal struggles of their makers.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Even then, most of the themes are the same: finding and losing love; birth and death; finding and losing fortune; making a home and setting out to a new land; etc.  I don\\\'t expect to hit a particular site and be greeted by a declaration of intent, or a concise statement of the theme of the page, or anything of that sort.  A website like Olivia\\\'s is a fairly unique combination of text, and drawing, and photography, and layout & design, body manipulation, and fashion, all backed up by coding.  I think that the combination of these things in a time-sensitive format has great potential for artistic expression that is every bit as meaningful as opera, or writing, or dance, or what have you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I really need to shorten these up.  My apologies.</p>          \r\n',0,0,0,0,'63.155.152.58','en'),(12921,2249,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yipe!  hi.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12922,2249,'2002-01-13 11:37:05',0,'also','i really like this article.',2,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12923,2247,'2002-01-13 11:39:47',0,'tree^','you know yvonne!? I see her around the neighborhood occasionally.',0,0,0,0,'172.147.243.147','en'),(12924,2248,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','they thought my grandmother had a tumor, until they went to remove it. turned out to be my own mum. how \\\'bout them apples.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12925,2244,'2002-01-13 14:17:07',0,'jo to steve','you\\\'re not the only one. there\\\'s been research done to show that dying, weak brain cells are holding the others back and when you kill them, the remaining brain cells are streamlined and work at optimized levels. the article about this was circulated as a forward at my company for a while, as we are a ragtag team of drinkers and stoners.',0,0,0,0,'172.142.113.91','en'),(12926,2249,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to jo','i had no idea your dad was an opera singer.',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(12927,2244,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','hey jo! if you ever come across that article again, could you forward it to me?',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12928,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','everybody knows yvonne!  yvonne knows everybody!  she is the ubiquitous yvonne.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12929,2249,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i think that when you boil things down to their most basic elements, there are very few reasons that we do things.  i think you\\\'re right in drawing a link between opera singing and writing, and probably hundreds of other diverse activities.  it was nice to see the idea summed up so well in print.  thanks.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12930,2248,'2002-01-13 19:28:02',0,'taco','do u ever think that maybe if you want to believe something you will no matter what your body is telling yo u. The power of denial. ',1,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12931,2248,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','the power of denial is what keeps me from getting sick.  i firmly refuse to believe that i can get sick.  so far, i haven\\\'t had the flu in about 8 years, and i get maybe 1 cold every year, if that.  ',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(12932,1040,'2002-01-13 20:08:10',0,'1313313','good for you!',0,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12933,1678,'2002-01-13 20:11:39',0,'131313','when i turned 25 i had a party a good party filled with friends!! \r\nBut you know what i found out when i went to buy my candles ( i threw my own party) that packages are made up of 24.',0,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12934,1379,'2002-01-13 20:14:08',0,'13131','25 seems like a big deal to you. Hmmm 25 is good i like being 25 .. now 26 is coming around the corner.. so what does this mean now?',0,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12935,1245,'2002-01-13 20:19:31',0,'13131','as a writer are we merely writing for other authors. i can\\\'t help but feel the only people that read my poetry are other poets. ',0,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12936,2248,'2002-01-13 21:01:37',0,'eclipse','Stable relationship or no, it\\\'s always suprising what a pregnancy can do to a guy. He can be the most supportive person in the world. Or he can be your worst nightmare. You can say you know somebody so well, but it\\\'s quite possible that you don\\\'t. And you never know until that test comes.\r\n\r\nThe deal with having a kid, espically so young, is that it\\\'s all on you. The guy can bitch and complain about all his responsibility, but when it all comes down to it, it\\\'s all on YOU. The guy can get out of it if he really wants. And you can\\\'t.\r\n\r\nDo you think maybe you just want to test your relationship?\r\n\r\nOr maybe you\\\'re like the rest of us and just paranoid? :)',2,0,0,0,'64.228.64.134','en'),(12937,2244,'2002-01-13 23:56:08',0,'jo','yup. i\\\'ll look in my attach directories and see what pops up.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12938,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','I have no want or need to test my relationship. If I wanted to, I would never do it with a baby. Besides, my guy is 32, he has matured and doesn\\\'t act like a stubborn idiot like most guys my age. But sure, people CAN change. Not everyone chooses to do so though.',6,0,0,0,'130.67.15.249','en'),(12939,2248,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>j.-j. rousseau, author of <i>the social contract</i>, and  <i>emile</i>, was rumored to have had several children by a servant woman he never married, and that those children he put fairly immediately into an orphanage feeling that the education they would recieve in the orphanage would be the most <i>natural</i> one possible during that time period.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i have never heard the author of <i>the social contract</i> ever called bastard, though he is often described as one.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>he seemed to care about children\'s education enough to write a book about it.  that book <i>emile</i> went on to have rippling effects in society and perhaps provide a foundation for developmental psychology. </P>\r\n\r\n<P>this style of education was based on the single individual child.  in the book, the boy Emile has a single teacher whose name i forget, but may have been spoofed in voltaire\'s pangloss, <i>candide</i>.  the teacher gave the boy hard questions and what not.\r\n\r\n<P>i have no point.  i had to edit this comment quite a bid because my understanding of the man has blurred in the few years since college.  but lately i have been thinking about him.  i am wondering if it is my own way of dealing with the question of a family.  he is springing up in my mind and just standing around.  i do not remember enough of what i read in the past for him to say things, so instead he just colors them.</P>\r\n\r\n',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(12940,2248,'2002-01-14 01:17:36',0,'Billy Idol','32?! <i>Rock the Cradle o\\\' LOVE!</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12941,2245,'2002-01-14 01:44:11',0,'just thoughts','<p>maybe the question should be, why do i want to be in this situation?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>because being involved with someone who is already involved is in my experience mostly about avoidance.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you might say to yourself...this is not really such a horrible thing. but then again, it\\\'s not really anything to be proud of either.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and make no mistake. this is not just a relationship between you and her. it is between you and her and her significant other and her family and her friends and maybe even your family and friends...because believe me, if they ever find out about it they will all feel it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>good, bad, ugly, or totally indifferent. they will feel it. and remember it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>for a long long time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and the thing that they\\\'ll remember...is that it\\\'s not anything to be proud of.</p>',4,0,0,0,'209.239.207.57','en'),(12942,2245,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt> the name <i>nadnad_nixie_nox</i> reminds me of conjugating verbs aloud, en masse, in latin class, circa 8th grade:<br><br>\r\nsum, es, est, sumus, estis, <i>sunt</i><br>\r\nnixor, nixoris, nixitur, niximur, niximini, <i>nixuntur</i></tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12943,2244,'2002-01-14 02:42:31',0,'The Buffalo Theory^','<p>I have not seen anyone explain this as well as Cliff  Clavin, on Cheers. One afternoon at Cheers, Cliff Clavin was explaining the  Buffalo Theory to his buddy Norm. Here\\\'s how it went:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"Well ya see, Norm, it\\\'s like this...A herd of buffalo can only move as fast as the slowest  buffalo. And when the herd is hunted, it is the slowest and weakest  ones at the back that are killed first. This natural selection is\r\ngood for the herd as a whole,  because the general speed and health of the whole group keeps improving  by the regular killing of the weakest members. In much the same way, the human brain can only operate as  fast as the slowest brain cells. Excessive intake of alcohol, as we  know, kills brain cells. But naturally, it attacks the slowest and weakest brain  cells first.\r\nIn this way, regular consumption of beer eliminates the  weaker brain cells, making the brain a faster and more efficient  machine. That\\\'s why you always feel smarter after a few beers.\\\"</p>',0,0,0,0,'172.163.16.93','en'),(12944,2247,'2002-01-14 02:45:21',0,'hehe^','she is wearing a noc noc shirt in that picture.',0,0,0,0,'172.163.16.93','en'),(12945,2247,'2002-06-27 20:39:15',240,'xep','she works at the noc noc ',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(12946,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','Yeeeees. He turns 33 in June and I turn 20 only 12 days after. It doesn\\\'t sound that extreme when I\\\'m no longer a teen.',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.248','en'),(12947,2248,'2002-01-14 03:43:24',0,'tom','what about that hard deck rule?<p>\r\n(0.5 * one\\\'s age) + 7 = lower limit.<p>\r\n33/2=16.5<br>\r\n16.5 + 7 = 23.5<p>\r\nyour s.o. is breaking this rule.',1,0,0,0,'209.162.48.87','en'),(12948,2248,'2002-01-14 04:01:56',0,'yep','thats pretty much the accepted rule- the \\\"half-plus-seven\\\" rule.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12949,2248,'2002-01-14 04:16:05',0,'(old enough','to be your father)',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12950,2248,'2002-01-14 04:35:53',0,'taco','you\\\'re 20 you\\\'ll be changing.... ',0,0,0,0,'24.205.36.134','en'),(12951,2251,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','wordless words would be more like \\\"arfep\\\" \\\"afneower\\\" \\\"npknuw\\\" \\\"waeoivme\\\"',0,0,0,0,'64.172.57.85','en'),(12952,2251,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i love new york city.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12953,2245,'2002-01-14 08:57:45',0,'Paul G','Iâ€™ve read this several times now, and with each reading it seems to have less to do with love and more to do with predation.  If the woman that you encountered were in a wheelchair and struggling up a hill, would you have taken that occasion to rob her?  If not, then why would you exploit her emotional disability?  The fact that you describe her as being â€œlike a little girlâ€ who â€œwants to be told what to doâ€, taken in conjunction with the fact that she has had a physical affair, is evidence that she is/was at least temporarily in a very vulnerable state.\r\n\r\n<p>No, it is not always right for a woman to stay with her husband; lots of guys are jerks, and worse.  But, if you are a man yourself, and if this is real, you might repair some of the damage that has been done by supporting her while she grows/heals until she can ultimately decide what to do for herself.  Love and other passions are difficult to anticipate.  And, I am not one who ought to throw stones.  But, you have a choice about how you act upon those feelings; right?  Even if it is the case that your state of nature sounds more like Hobbesâ€™ than Rousseauâ€™s.</p>  \r\n',3,0,0,0,'63.155.148.18','en'),(12954,2251,'2002-01-14 11:02:41',0,'new york city','i love s5.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12955,2251,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to gus','<br><tt>i would have said <i>\"meaningless words\"</i> if i weren\'t such a big softy for consonance. i suppose i was leaning towards the \"<a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=wordless\">inarticulate; silent</a>\" meaning of \"wordless\".</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12956,2245,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Paul','<br><tt>speaking out of the context of this post, your advice to \\\"support her while she grows/heals until she can ultimately decide what to do for herself\\\" is a good lesson that I wish more of the guys i know would be willing to do.  <br><br>i was with a girl for several years who was still reeling from some of her life circumstances that occurred before i met her (it took me some time to grasp the extent of her deep tempest within). time proved that us being together, although very satisfying relationally and short-term, was really inhibiting and preventing her from growing up, repairing some of her long-term hurts and regrets, and learning not to be so dependent-to-a-fault on the men in her life. as college students, it was easy to overlook, but as career folks, it began effecting her career and life.  <br><br>eventually, we decided to kind of take some time apart. she\\\'s spent the past year alone- literally- spending obscene amounts of time and money on herself, growing up independently, finding her true self and recreating herself as a mighty person.  although it was kind of hurtful to martyr our relationship, it was for agood cause; she\\\'s grown into an amazing and succcessful person, and its been a beautiful metamorphosis to observe.  Although we may not be dating anymore, we\\\'ve grown to be the deepest friends, connecting on an unreal level.  Whoever ends up spending their life with her (whether it eventually be me or anyone else) is going to get the priviledge of sharing their soul with one of the most well-centered, rational, empowered people i know.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12957,2244,'2002-01-14 13:10:05',0,'Chemical Collage','<br><center><img src=\\\"http://www.live-wire.com/images/chem.jpg\\\"></center>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12958,2250,'2002-01-14 14:35:42',0,'critic','this piece is tremendously tasteless.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12959,2243,'2002-01-14 14:53:13',0,'Ladies And Gentlemen','The King has left the Building...',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12960,2247,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','there is a new cat in my life. she is \\\"the black one\\\". nothing has changed even though i am far away. every morning a magical black cat bats and beats on the door knob for someone to feed it. bat bat bat. i cant pack for chiang mai now because she sits in my bag. ',0,0,0,0,'203.146.223.82','en'),(12961,2250,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you and the cat, wakachicka.',4,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12962,407,'2002-01-14 15:26:04',0,'Chic','Ahhhhhhhhh, FREAKOUT! (<a href=\"http://www.cdnow.com/cgi-bin/mserver/SID=1001693826/pagename=/RP/CDN/FIND/album.html/ArtistID=CHIC/ITEMID=1159262\">Le Freak, c\'est chic!)</a>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12963,2250,'2002-01-14 15:29:10',0,'Pet-o-phile','Her purr is more like a passionate moan. Actually itâ€™s more like whining, crying, demanding release... from me. with delicate purrs, she seductively says to me, \\\"once youve tried <i>cat</i>, youll never go back!\\\".\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12964,632,'2002-01-14 15:32:04',0,'o to austin','what exactly is \\\"the russian officer\\\"?',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12965,632,'2002-01-14 15:37:19',0,'Russian Officer sez','\\\"You vill let me sodomize you, Comerade... you have <i>no choice</i>.\\\"',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(12966,1087,'2002-01-14 15:39:02',0,'o to B-p','the dream girl didn\\\'t seem in pain when she bled.\r\n\r\nit seems obvious that the dream represented some kind of self-sacrafice.  i wish i could remember what was going on in my life at that point.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12967,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','Everyone changes in one way or another, just not neccesarily for the worse. As for the age thing...I don\\\'t care how old people are, as long as they are nice and don\\\'t act like morons. I have friends from the age 15 to over 30. Age doesn\\\'t have to matter, it all depends on the *people* and how they are.',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.250','en'),(12968,209,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','ps i just want to clarify that the coloured table was not put there by me.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12969,2250,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','best thing i\\\'ve read here in weeks.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12970,2248,'2002-01-14 15:53:39',0,'','camilla, are you aware that there is an <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=64\">old article on this site</a> about you?',3,0,0,1,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12971,2248,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','camilla, are you aware that there is <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=64\\\">an old article on this site</a> about you?',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12972,2248,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','usually i am a big fan of the whole not dating anyone under 20 if you\\\'re over 25 yourself, but i think it\\\'s really shitty to just go ahead and voice your opinion on the relationship of someone you\\\'ve never met, based on some asshole formula.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ndicks.<br>',2,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12973,2251,'2002-01-14 18:41:19',0,'critic','youre right, this doesnt say anything. <i>but</i>, it does it so prettily. is this what postmodern writing is all about?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12974,2248,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Yeah, I\\\'m with o. on that one.  For me the range has been +- 3 years, but YMMV.  My fiancee is about four months younger than I am.  <i>*best friendly*</i>',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12975,2248,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','(I turn 24 in 18 days.  Holy crap, my birthday is on a friday!  Rock!  it\\\'s been shitty days like monday or tuesday for a while now)',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12976,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','Yeah, I know about that post. I tried the \\\"look mom, I\\\'m almost naked!\\\"-thing those days. Ended it long time ago.',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.249','en'),(12977,2248,'2002-01-14 19:08:27',0,'camilla fan','i like to see camilla almost naked again',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12978,2251,'2002-01-14 19:18:58',0,'Pandora','Tracing the plot finds skin touching skin (absence follows)... <i>DCFC</i>',6,0,0,0,'168.215.102.189','en'),(12979,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','I used to do drugs.  Then I got wise.  Let\\\'s examine the reality here:  Let\\\'s say you run through an a ounce of pot a week.  Further assume that ounce sets you back $50 (and you don\\\'t get arrested or anything, which is yet more money).  That\\\'s $200 a month.  \\\"But I get $200 worth of (fun|mind expansion) from it.\\\"  Um, ok, interesting value set there.  $200 is enough money to rent a movie every night and have many bucks left over (yes, I find watching a good movie more entertaining than any drug I ever did, even the more exotic psychedelics).  So much for the fun part.  Mind expansion?  Drugs?  Nigga, pleeez!  $200 a month is enough to attend a different informal class every month, or an interesting new book or two every week to read, or if you save up for a while, a semester\\\'s tuition.  And weed is a cheap drug!  Note that I\\\'m not exempting alcohol or tobacco here, spending money or time on those is just as retarded as things The Man doesn\\\'t like.  [And as an atheist, to me religion similarly falls into the fucking-waste-of-resources bin.  YMMV.]  Do whatever YOU like, it\\\'s ultimately a free-will universe, but...  <i>*best fucking friendly*</i>',4,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12980,2244,'2002-01-14 19:22:39',0,'duh to orion','thats the most judgmental thing ive ever heard. renting movies sucks anyway. i see buying drugs as similar to buying food. and who spends $200 a week on pot?! sheehs.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12981,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Herr Doktor:  No, I think that spending resources on thinking, studying, etc. is one of the <em>best</em> things you can do.  Now, personally, I wouldn\\\'t spend time on religions or philosophy, but whose to say quantum mechanics isn\\\'t a sort of religion?  No, I didn\\\'t post that to troll.  I knew it\\\'d probably be a little incendiary, but it is how I feel.<br><br>\r\nTo another person:  My experience is my business.  Would you trumpet to the heavens how stupid you were in the past?  No, I think not.  Anyway, what\\\'s the criterion for \\\"real drug user\\\"?\r\nSuffice to say that I feel I did enough in ignorance to regret the waste of those resources later.',4,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12982,2244,'2002-01-14 19:32:25',0,'duh','to duh:  And judgemental is a bad thing?  No, as intelligent organisms we perceive our environment, and formulate judegements about that in order to formulate interactions with it.  If anything, the act of judging is neutral.<br><br>\r\npot == food?  wtf?  you die without food, without pot you (fill in the blank, but it ain\\\'t death).  And I said $200/mo. not $200/wk.  This estimate also came built-in with some background assumptions which perhaps you didn\\\'t take the time to assimilate before replying.\r\n<br><br>\r\nWorthwhile things to spend resources on:\r\n<ul><li>love (family, friends, partner, etc.)</li><li>education/thought</li><li>charity</li><li>fill in the rest to your satisfaction</li></ul>',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12983,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','oops, sorry, that was me, not duh.',0,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12984,2244,'2002-01-14 19:35:48',0,'duh to orion','ok, we GET it... youve had problems in the past, and that makes you have an opinion. ok. but that doesnt ok you to get all preachy to us here. judgment is good, judgmental is BAD. if you really wanted to say your piece without ruffling any feathers, youd say it a little differently. the way it sounds now, youre just rabblerousing and anti-druggie. not all of us have had your past/experience/problems, so not all of us have the strong opinions you do.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12985,2244,'2002-01-14 19:37:42',0,'further irony','<blockquote>\r\nthats the most judgmental thing ive ever heard. renting movies sucks anyway.</blockquote>\r\nSo you use the perjorative \\\"judgmental\\\" against a viewpoint that conflicts with yours, then turn around in the next sentence and pass an un-explained judgment against something the \\\"judgemental\\\" person said?!?  Leaving aside the debate about whether the act of judging is a negative thing, you can\\\'t have your cake and eat it too.  For the record, $200 will buy you a lot of fun that\\\'s legal, not just renting movies.  (To me the fun and mind expansion things coexist.  I derive as much fun from $50 worth of chemistry books as I ever did from an ounce of marijuana.  More, in fact, becuase I can continue enjoying them long after the hypothetical weed would be up in smoke...)',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12986,2244,'2002-01-14 19:40:54',0,'destabilizing','die thread, die.',4,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(12987,2244,'2002-01-14 19:41:18',0,'dork','so you enjoy chemistry books. some of us enjoy drugs. its a matter of preference. lets not get int oa flame war here, ok? thanks :)',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12988,2244,'2002-01-14 19:42:52',0,'stella','<P>my .02 cents.</P><P> I don\\\'t think that Orion was being judgemental. If he were being judgemental, he would\\\'ve added that he thought you were stupid for doing drugs, or that you were a dumb stoner. I don\\\'t recall that he wrote anything of the sort. He was merely voicing an opinion that he feels that doing drugs is a waste of time/money. I don\\\'t agree with him, but I don\\\'t feel attacked by him either. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12989,2244,'2002-01-14 19:44:14',0,'to stella','yes, the \\\"get a fucking hobby\\\" and \\\"do something with your life\\\" from his original comments were a bit incendiary.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12990,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Preachy?  Hrm, that\\\'s the closest I\\\'ve ever been to a member of the clergy.  :-)  Yes, my value set places resources spent on certain things very low.  My original comment was intended more as an airing of my opinion than a desire to change someone\\\'s life.  I suppose it\\\'s natural to argue with someone\\\'s opinions that directly contradict your own (lord knows I\\\'ve done my share of arguing with religious folks and political conservatives), but really at no point did I climb up on the soapbox to say \\\"Hey you!  Yeah, you in the corner in the red shirt!  You\\\'re a fuckhead because you wear red!  Just thought you\\\'d like to know!\\\".',4,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12991,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','Haha!',6,0,0,0,'130.67.15.249','en'),(12992,2244,'2002-01-14 19:48:32',0,'like i said','the \\\"get a fucking hobby\\\" and \\\"do somethign with your life\\\" didnt accurately convey your warm feelings ;) ok. now that i have the last word, shall we end this argument?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12993,2244,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Hugs and kisses.  If nothing else, this has been the most interesting thread I\\\'ve read on swinney in a while.  (and I didn\\\'t say \\\"do something\\\" I said \\\"do something <em>else</em>\\\", i.e. I acknowledge that something is being done but that in my pretty-damned-far-from-humble opinion better things could be)',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12994,2250,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Gotta love cats.  :^)  ',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(12995,2250,'2002-01-14 20:28:33',0,'stella','When I was an evil little child, knee-deep in puberty, we had a little-girl-cat who was not fixed. I knew exactly what was going on when she went into heat. Venting my own hormone addled angst, I would torture her in her... state. Specifically, I would gently poke with a stick in a, um, sensitive area. Not kitty-rape-type of poking, but just enough to make her hate me. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(12996,2244,'2002-01-14 20:29:49',0,'dear thread','some suggestions. 1) die. 2) move to e-mail. ',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12997,2251,'2002-01-14 20:31:41',0,'damn you!','i will be singing death cab all afternoon! not that this is a bad thing, just. ',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(12998,2251,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\\\"no joy in mudville\\\"... oooh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(12999,2244,'2002-01-14 20:38:29',0,'ahh yes','the best way to get a thread to die... contribute a post asking it to die?  horay for circular logic! (x) best friendly',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13000,2250,'2002-01-14 21:27:37',0,'eclipse','To Critic: \\\"Tasteless\\\"?',0,0,0,0,'64.228.64.134','en'),(13001,2244,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','well, now i know how to get comments at least...<BR><BR>WEEDATHON \'02 UPDATE:<BR>we have one contribution coming in from [anon]. operators are still standing by. anonymous donors are always welcome. i thank you.',1,0,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(13002,2244,'2002-01-14 22:18:40',0,'stella to peter','Boy, you really ARE the shit! ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13003,2251,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i\\\'m glad to see that you took care of your porch situation. with extreme prejudice.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.16','en'),(13004,2249,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','i think web journals and places like this appeal to people because you can tailor other people\\\'s perception of you almost exactly. you control all the input; no one knows how annoying your laugh is, or whatever.',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.16','en'),(13005,2249,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i like the opera',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13006,2241,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','ya, getting back to reality just isn\\\'t fun, is it?',0,0,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(13007,2251,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Tyler','<br><tt> thats not <i>my</i> place!  no, why, thats the luxury apartment across the street. the razor-wire is protecting their garbage cans, believe it or not.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13008,2249,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like doing the <a href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/journal.htm\\\">journal</a> thing because it gives me a chance to put into words alot of things that no one would be interested in talking to me about; banal, everyday stuff. slice o\\\' life stuff.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13009,2248,'2002-01-15 01:57:59',0,'taco','just an opion who really cares what anyone thinks about one\\\'s relationship..Cam do u care?',0,0,0,0,'64.14.248.62','en'),(13010,2251,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Dude, that\\\'s <em>fucked up</em>.  I live in a \\\"luxury\\\" complex, and the closed we have to barbed wire is the battleaxe office lady who\\\'ll look at you real mean if you appear unruly (one of the criterions for being unruly, apparently, is being under the age of 30).',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(13011,2248,'2002-01-15 02:29:38',0,'humberhumbert','are the pictures broken on the link to an old article about the author of this post or it me?',1,0,0,0,'24.27.31.92','en'),(13012,2244,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','regarding the topics above, might i add that jail is a lot more fun from the outside? and to further guarantee this decadent pleasure, please to not say if you\\\'re receiving \\\"things\\\" in the mail or sending them. thank you.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13013,2251,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh yes, well welcome to nyc... this building\\\'s doorman has been known to physically hassle loiterers and the homeless, also. yuck.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13014,2244,'2002-01-15 03:38:51',0,'the names have been changed','to protect the innocent!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13015,2244,'2002-01-15 03:56:45',0,'hahaha^','$50 an ounce?  An ounce a week?  Planet Zorak?\r\n\r\n<p>Ok, First of all, anyone who smokes an ounce a week might have a problem, or more likely a headache!  I usto grow pot in my closet for personal use and the most I smoked would be an 8th of an Oz. in a week.  More likely, I would make an 8th last a month.  I don\\\'t smoke every day and don\\\'t see a need to.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Second, your going to have to pay a lot more than $50 for an ounce, unless your smoking some Mexican dirt weed full of seeds that tastes like gasoline.  Personally, I prefer organically grown quality kind bud.  I don\\\'t like the chemicals.\r\n\r\n<p>Regardless of law and popular hysteria, I reserve my right as a human being to do what I want to my mind and body as long as I do not harm others.  Luckily, I live in a community where it is somewhat tolerated.  There are loopholes in the the law.  Some governments and companies like Budweiser might disagree with my philosophy for various reasons.  It works in Holland, Switzerland, and more recently Canada.  Expand your mind, or at least open it a little for the rights of others.</p>',1,0,0,0,'172.128.136.215','en'),(13016,2244,'2002-01-15 04:00:43',0,'three cheers for','<a href=\\\"http://www.alchemind.org/\\\">cognitive liberty</a>!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13017,2244,'2002-01-15 04:01:10',0,'by the way','what the hell ius \\\"kind\\\" weed and why is everyone always calling their fucking weed \\\"kind\\\"?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13018,2244,'2002-01-15 04:02:02',0,'i tghink this would','have been alot more interesting as Weebathon',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13019,2244,'2002-01-15 04:05:17',0,'thank you editor^','I dont think that anyone would want their name listed as a contributor.  On the other side of things, I would be afraid to open my mail if I had posted this.  DEA agents could be hovering around your house just waiting on that tasty package. You never know!  <br><br>Pot is not worth taking risks like this and going to jail for it!',0,0,0,0,'172.128.136.215','en'),(13020,2244,'2002-01-15 04:10:47',0,'kindbud^','is high grade [seedless] marijuana from the sativa and indica strains, or could be a hybrid of the two.  U could almost call it designer pot because people have worked for decades crossbreeding plants to produce desired flavors and high THC content.',2,0,0,0,'172.128.136.215','en'),(13021,2244,'2002-01-15 04:15:11',0,'The DEA','consider yerself busted',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13022,2244,'2002-01-15 04:31:05',0,'the dea','once i snorted ecstacy off of the bumper of a cop car in a concert parking lot.  i felt bad about it the next day.',0,0,0,0,'172.128.136.215','en'),(13023,2244,'2002-01-15 05:15:03',0,'kat','allo kind people...i think y\\\'all are takin orion\\\'s post a bit too literally. there are all kinds of folks in this world. even ones who smoke EVERY day. and maybe several times a day. i have known people who are into that. and then some of us don\\\'t really smoke at all. and some of us smoke occasionally. for various reasons. i am sure at least once in awhile we are all guilty of escapism...whatever medium it is that we use to do so. not to say that this is a bad thing. but it\\\'s a pretty BIG gray area between \\\'i am so good i never do drugs\\\' and \\\'i am just as good and i do them whenever i feel like it\\\'. you gotta admit there\\\'s some muddly shit goin on in between there that even good people get trapped up in once in awile...and sometimes more than once in awhile. ',1,0,0,0,'209.239.198.241','en'),(13024,2254,'2002-01-15 06:09:26',0,'Ev','I wrote a paper on this in college. It\\\'s nothing like being there.\r\n\r\nThis country knew about it and did nothing substantive to stop it. We even protested when Vietnam DID go in to put the Khmer Rouge out of business.\r\n\r\nPol Pot wrote about all this when in school in France. He had it all mapped out just like it happened.\r\nYou probably learned this already.\r\n\r\nThing is, we coulda done something.\r\n\r\nWhat DO you say? I guess you say nothing.',1,0,0,0,'64.171.254.122','en'),(13025,2253,'2002-01-15 06:11:41',0,'kat','santa cruz pornography: Frenchy\\\'s on the corner of 41st Ave and Portola [next door to Pleasure Point Pizza]. heh',1,0,0,0,'209.239.196.81','en'),(13026,2253,'2002-01-15 06:12:59',0,'ps','i really like the huge mountain, sky of clouds imagery. very nice.',1,0,0,0,'209.239.196.81','en'),(13027,2253,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<i>sometimes strange encounters with men</i>',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13028,2253,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','<p><img src=\\\"http://www.randomhuman.com/img/hotchat.jpg\\\"></p>',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13029,2253,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','it will be good for your art. i bet we see a lot more swinnies from you.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13030,2254,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','this made me think really hard the first time you mailed it out, and now it is making me think hard again.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13031,1552,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','by the way, ann quit today.  she did not say goodbye.',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(13032,2254,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','i read something about how the US supported pol pot and how he was buddies with henry kissinger.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13033,1928,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','another song that does the \\\"stop\\\" thing: break your neck by \\\"busta rhymes\\\". channel [v] plays it often.',6,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13034,2253,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','beat off. beat off. beat off. repeat. rinse. repeat. rejoice in your fecundity! now is the time to make cartoons about huge phalluses taking over santa cruz.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13035,2253,'2002-01-15 07:21:43',0,'Sparagmos','<p>With your use of imagery you almost make your situation enviable.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.160.178','en'),(13036,2253,'2002-01-15 07:22:39',0,'Sparagmos','<p>I wish that was my though process when I\\\'m in such a situation.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.160.178','en'),(13037,2250,'2002-01-15 07:55:01',0,'meow','poor kitty!',0,0,0,0,'172.157.9.6','en'),(13038,2254,'2002-01-15 08:50:44',0,'qq','someone said, \r\n\\\"Irony became obsolete the day Henry Kissenger was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize\\\"\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(13039,2254,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','yah. its weird. and I havent been to another place with less culture. even the food isnt good. some of the monks push and shove eachother, they arent the mellow happy fellows you see in other southeast asian countries. the expats are all bitter. homeless people sleep on the sidewalks in phnom phen, where I never saw them in other big cities of the other countries where they at least had their shanty towns by the train tracks. I stayed there a long time. There was a lot of satelite tv. over half the population is under 15. its like they are still trying to rebuild, repopulate, and rediscover why theyre alive.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13040,2253,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','you guys are gonna think this is funny, but my sexuality has been turned off like a faucet by my provincial thai relatives. the thuoght of sex genuinely makes me uncomfortable! i am ashamed of all things vaginal. someone save meh!',0,0,0,0,'203.153.139.189','en'),(13041,2253,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i get the same way when i am with my family. i can\\\'t even wank when i\\\'m home visiting.',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13042,2253,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my favorite: when my down-home family came to visit, and my mom met my then-gf and asked me (in slight southern drawl), \\\"so, are you two having <i><b>sex</i></b>? I have a feeling you probably are\\\"... talk about point-blank uncomfortable squirming.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13043,2254,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the statement: \\\"trying to rebuild, repopulate, and rediscover why theyre alive\\\"</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13044,2250,'2002-01-15 13:12:01',0,'critic to eclipse','yes, <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=tasteless\\\">tasteless</a>.<br><br>this is, of course, only my two cents, so no offense intended. i just got bad vibes off of the \\\"cat is affectionate, and therefore sexually frustrated\\\" overtones, and what they might imply. although im starting to see that the \\\"when am i going to get laid?!\\\" concern is probably just a projection of your personal concerns onto said feline. (best friendly)',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13045,2255,'2002-01-15 13:26:53',0,'five movies','in one night? damn, boy!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13046,2255,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>writing in <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1514\\\">diners</a> is a <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1540.txt\\\">good thing</a>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13047,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','I couldn\\\'t care less. What people say has no impact on my relationship. I do get sick of hearing that I should go for someone younger and all that nonsense however.\r\nTo the person looking for pics: The pics appear broken because I removed them from my server where they once were located. And by the way; My parents have seen them. Ie: They are not nudie pics. Visit a porn site. :D',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.249','en'),(13048,1472,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','I am 5\\\"84 tall and weigh somewhere around 157 lbs. I guess that makes me a mountain of lard. :D',3,0,0,0,'130.67.15.249','en'),(13049,2253,'2002-01-15 14:27:41',0,'jo','really? i think my best sexual encounters have happened while in <a href=\\\"http://chat.carleton.ca/~rfairchi/hayden.html#forever\\\">my parents\\\' house</a>, whether they were there or not. usually not.<BR>\r\nin fact, the bed that i now sleep on is the same one that was in the guest room all through college when i came home to visit, so i still sleep on the same bed which carries all these fond memories of shy gropings.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.157.51.43','en'),(13050,2248,'2002-01-15 14:54:06',0,'old lecherous man','see, its all about when you <i>started dating</i>. if you just met, fine. but if you were dating when you were 13 and he was 26, then thats problematic, i think. not that its any of my business!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13051,2255,'2002-01-15 16:12:51',0,'trouble','\'ghost world\' and thought about returns to school seem to be on everybody\'s mind these days.  great article.<br><br>peter, tell me you have written in a waffle house.  <i><b>tell me</i></b> :)',1,0,0,0,'208.255.153.146','en'),(13052,2255,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive definitely written in a <a href=\"http://www.wafflehouse.com\">waffle house</a> (or \"Wa-Ho\" to your average south-eastern tennessean); you can do alot of neat things in waho\'s. admittedly though, playing the \"<a href=\"http://www.wafflehousesongs.com/\">waffle house song</a>\" on the jukebox is my favourite waffle house activity, especially while flirting with and talking to the country-girl waitresses and short order cooks. theyre usually damn good, hard-working folks with peerless stories to tell. (note: in every waffle house, if more than 20 minutes pass without a song being played, the jukebox automatically plays \"the wafflehouse song\"). i love the waffle house and all it stands for. mirrored walls, picture menus, fat and haggard patrons, chain-smoking cooks, lockless doors, etc.</a>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13053,2254,'2002-01-15 16:29:37',0,'trouble','damn.  thank you for posting this.  <br><br>my father served in viet nam for almost 6 years and came home a mentally damaged man but with stories and information that i still go through every now and then.  part of him never left that place and a few years later when this happened he began to relapse.  the aspects of genocide are almost too vast to completely understand.  i guess point of reference is essential to even attempting to comprehend such things.  i feel a tinge of anger every now and then whe people say things like \\\'i can imagine how bad it was\\\' when talking in the presence of vets who were over there and saw atrocities that some can still not talk about.  some things are too impossible to imagine.',1,0,0,0,'208.255.153.146','en'),(13054,2248,'2006-07-06 18:09:51',754,'Camille Henrikke','It\\\'s been 5 months today, so we met when I was 19 and he 32. Noone we know think it\\\'s a big deal. I don\\\'t see why random strangers on the net think so.',3,0,0,0,'217.199.39.244','en'),(13055,2248,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','random strangers love a good scandal.',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13056,2255,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Heaven on a plate: double order of waffle house hash browns with onions, cheese, and jalapeno peppers.  Mmmm... so bad yet so tasty.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13057,2253,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to weeb','that sounds like a death metal song.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(13058,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','It\\\'s not a scandal though. ;)',3,0,0,0,'217.199.39.244','en'),(13059,2248,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to C.H.','<P>there may also be a cultural link here.  perhaps the values of the equasion that somebody enumarated are different for the united states MTV culture.</P>\r\n<P><B>constructive</b></P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(13060,2120,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','We don\\\'t have these tests in .no as far as I know. Blah! I like tests. I want to take that test as well. :/',3,0,0,0,'217.199.39.244','en'),(13061,2248,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','<P>furthermore, i am listening to the W H I T E S T R I P E S (girls scream) and there is this song, \\\"I\\\'m Finding It Harder To Be A Gentleman\\\".  I think it is a fine song for this post.  I would put the lyrics inline but i dont have them and i am sure some one will consider following up with those, if they can get past the general feeling of dorkiness and highschool mentality that goes allong with writing lyrics on websites.</P>\r\n<P>on the hole, one can generally clutch that entire album when dealing with relationships.</P>\r\n<P>cheers!</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(13062,2248,'2002-01-15 18:22:32',0,'chat','<p>\r\n*simon*   i just read your comment to camille about listening to the white stripes and i was going to add that in a bizarre coincidence, i\\\'m listening to father figure by george michael but i didn\\\'t think camille would appreciate/be amused by the comment\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n*austin*   go for it\r\n</p>',1,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13063,2254,'2002-01-15 19:05:07',0,'qq','for correctness\\\' sake: \r\n\\\"Political satire became obsolete when Henry Kissinger was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.\\\"\r\n\r\n-Tom Lehrer\r\n\r\nyay.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(13064,2248,'2006-07-06 18:08:16',754,'Camilla Henrikke','I wonder who \\\"camille\\\" is. ;D',6,0,0,0,'217.199.39.244','en'),(13065,2245,'2002-01-15 19:48:24',0,'Paul G','Hail and well met, noble Peter.',1,0,0,0,'63.155.149.82','en'),(13066,2254,'2002-01-15 19:53:38',0,'Scapegoat','<p>I\\\'m struggling to come up with something to say in response to this. I find the facts relayed twisting my gut. It due to stuff like this that I find myself hating humanity at times.</p> ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.166.21','en'),(13067,2254,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>articles like this make me think (apart from wow and all the associated stuff):</p>\r\n<p>wouldn\\\'t it be cool if there was a \\\"best of swinney\\\"?  if people could add karma for a post and the top 10 or so were displayed somewhere?</p>\r\n<p>i would give this post karma.</p>\r\n<p>ps swinney i would code this if you were keen.  thx.</p>',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13068,2248,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','camilla, oops!',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13069,2254,'2002-01-15 20:04:02',0,'BAH to','preferential treatment of posts. BAH I say!!<br><br><center><b><h1>BURN! BURN!</b></h1></center>',6,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13070,2254,'2002-01-15 20:06:41',0,'true dat to bah','i agree. rating posts would probably be a bad idea. i like this post. but more for political importance than for actual good writing or emotiveness or writing skill. i have posts i like that are beautifully written yet say nothing, and wonderful pieces that were penned in the shittiest writing style. i think to pit all these against each other would be a real treavesty to writers and readers alike. just my two cents of course. id say best friendly, but this is more \\\"destabilizing\\\".',0,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13071,1963,'2002-01-15 20:09:25',0,'bobby mcgavin crustacean','why do we never see ghost here anymore? i miss ghost. ',1,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13072,2253,'2002-01-15 20:16:58',0,'leonard','is someone in thailand or something?',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES (13073,2253,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to austin','your cup runneth over. your CUP RUNNETH OVA YA YA YA YAAAA. *thrashsmash*',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13074,2253,'2002-01-15 20:21:24',0,'non sexual times','can be good times for inversion, creativity, etc. focus your energies towards those things.... and whack it like a madman!',1,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13075,2253,'2002-01-15 20:22:14',0,'bootycall to aus10','its so cute how monogamous you are ;)',1,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13076,2254,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>no dicks, not rating, just a \\\"i like this post i vote it for best of\\\".  no bad karma or variable good karma points.</p>\r\n<p>wouldn\\\'t it further encourage the kick-ass standard of writing on the site, eh!?</p>\r\n<p>anyway i suppose this is a bad idea or something.  okay.</p>',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13077,2254,'2002-01-15 20:49:52',0,'dick (just for disagreeing?!))','words like \\\"best of swinney\\\" and \\\"top ten\\\" sure make it sound like rating. i say <i>hells no</i>',0,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13078,2254,'2002-01-15 20:54:17',0,'OH FINE THEN!!!','spoil my fun.  MEANIE.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13079,2254,'2002-01-15 21:00:55',0,'meanies & dicks','cant we all just <i>get along</i>?',2,0,0,0,'66.65.50.73','en'),(13080,2250,'2002-01-15 21:13:46',0,'ryan','if you don\\\'t get that cat laid, she\\\'s going to go into heat every 2-3 weeks until she does.   and i think i may have read someplace that it\\\'s actually unhealthy for them to go through this cycle and never get pregnant.  maybe you should get it spayed?',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.180','en'),(13081,2250,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i heard that if you don\\\'t get your female cat\\\'s reproductive system butchered straight away, then you risk having them go in and out of heat entire life.',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13082,2253,'2002-01-15 21:59:57',0,'co','kat: i\\\'ve never seen you at mr. frenchys house of quarter porno booths and gloryholes delight.\r\n\r\ncome by and we\\\'ll shop for buttplugs.',2,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(13083,2254,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Vong Moll','<p>It\\\'s pretty interesting, what people can do.</p>',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13084,2253,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Yeah, my fiancee is about to leave to teach english in japan for a year.  Oh well, this has three benefits:  a) my right arm will be like THE ARM OF GOD b) I\\\'ll get better at math (\\\"Sex is the mathematics urge sublimated.\\\") and thus chemistry c) When she comes home will probably be some of the most explosive sex any two human beings should have the right to expect in their time on this green earth.  ',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13085,2253,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','s5: that is a really cool picture!  There\\\'s something about the color lines in her hair that remind me of cat5 cable. Sort of a Ghost In The Shell effect...  I guess this references an earlier post about the fundamental eroticism of networking gear.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13086,2254,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','My fiancee\\\'s dad was a marine doorgunner in Vietnam.  The one story he told me about what it was like that really stuck in my head concerned boots.  Apparently when he got there, they had a severe shortage of boots, so they had standing orders to strip the boots off of the casualties before they were sent to the hospital (or morgue, as the case may be).  So every pair of boots represented some man (boy more like) who was injured or dead.  It was a pile in the hanger in his words about twenty feet in circumfrence and about 10 feet high.  Nice thing to see upon arrival, eh?  [and the further ick factor of wearing boots than until very recently were inhabited by somebody else, potentially a dead somebody else]<br><br><br>War sucks.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13087,2248,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','just a vowel movement ;^)',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13088,2253,'2002-01-15 23:34:45',0,'unfortunate','my boyfriend has been away at school for three months now and if he doesn\\\'t come home to take care of me soon i think i\\\'ll die. unlike some girls, i can\\\'t seem to take care of such things simply SOLO. why... why... WHY MUST I BE FORED TO SUFFER!?!??! it\\\'s so unfair...',3,0,0,0,'64.228.64.134','en'),(13089,2250,'2002-01-15 23:38:20',0,'eclipse','<p> Projection is right! :) </p>\r\n<p> Humm... Poor kitty... I think I\\\'ll take her to the vet... Just as soon as some money comes up... Those operations are expensive! </p>',0,0,0,0,'64.228.64.134','en'),(13090,2253,'2002-01-15 23:56:42',0,'wolfwood','I have yet to surpass Parent\\\'s Home Sex, fond memories. swoon and daydream.',1,0,0,0,'24.27.31.92','en'),(13091,2250,'2002-01-16 00:05:12',0,'ryan','female cats will also tend to spray urine all over the house while in heat as well.  smells nice.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.205','en'),(13092,2253,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>backyard new jersey pool sex, anyone?</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13093,2248,'2002-01-16 02:59:46',0,'Jeg forstar ikke','vaaer sa god',0,0,0,0,'172.150.14.56','en'),(13094,2255,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i hate waffles. (indeed, I have only recently started to like breakfast food at all thanks to eggs and bacon being a part of \\\"breakfast food\\\")but man I wish I had the motivation to walk the block and a half to the all night diner and sit there drinking weak tea and having one order of french fries over the course of several hours in the middle of the night. le should get me to go out to sparkys in the middle of the night more. we\\\'re on our own now that jim is back to living a life within reasonable daytime hours. ah well.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13095,1963,'2002-01-16 03:40:26',0,'kat to bobby','that chicky only writes <a href=\\\"http://www.onetrickpony.org\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">here</a> now.',4,0,0,0,'209.239.201.171','en'),(13096,2257,'2002-01-16 04:00:13',0,'Mister Love, phd','carbohydrates make us gain weight, Dr. Cruikshanke',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13097,2255,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','Yesterday I picked up a Ghost World poster at Silver Snail.  It\\\'s now beside the Mononoke Hime poster above my bed.<br><br>It doesn\\\'t take much to fall totally in love with Enid, does it?<br><br>Glad I could offhandedly influence your viewing choice.  Just spreading the love.',1,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(13098,2255,'2002-01-16 04:30:22',0,'whats','\\\"Ghost World\\\", \\\"Silver Snail\\\" and \\\"Mononoke Hime\\\"?!\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13099,2250,'2002-01-16 04:37:45',0,'2 Live Crew','I\\\'ma dog in heat<br>\r\na freak without warning<br>\r\ngot an appetite for sex<br>\r\ncause me so horny.<br>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13100,2255,'2002-01-16 06:29:04',0,'Scapegoat to what','<p>Ghost World - is a kick my ass movie. Silver Snail - is a comic shop of legend here in Toronto. The Monoke reference is I\\\'m guessing related to the anime princess monoke movie (I\\\'m guessing here though)</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.166.21','en'),(13101,2255,'2002-01-16 07:44:13',0,'qq','with xep not here i should be on a completely fucked schedule in no time at all. lets go to sparky\\\'s.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(13102,2257,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','<br><b>Jan 14</b>: I find a quarter under the couch cushion.<br><br>\r\n<b>Jan 15</b>: My dinner date falls asleep on the sofa failing to leave behind any money whatsoever, but makes up for it by buying me a cookie.<br><br>\r\nI find a penny outside the Roxie and yet, I do not get lucky.\r\n\r\n',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13103,2257,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>January 14:</b> spot a quarter on the floor of the 1 train. weigh positives of contribution to laundromat fund <i>versus</i> negatives of touching anything on the apallingly filthy subway floor. decide to leave it for someone more desperate.<br><br><b>January 15:</b> buy coffee at corner street-vendor and get 25 cent change in nickle and pennies. wish this ammount was in quarter form for said laundry fund. <br><br><b>January 16:</b> neatly stack the pennies in two stacks of ten on my desk at work, carrying out an experiment to see how long they\'ll stay before whomever steals my staples steals them also.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13104,2253,'2002-01-16 14:15:03',0,'jo to peter','are you asking if we\\\'re up for driving to nj and sneaking into someone\\\'s backyard and having sex? because if so: yes. trespassing. public place. pool. new jersey. sounds like a recipe for magic.<BR>\r\non the other hand, i think that \\\"fast times at ridgemont high\\\" really ruined me on poolside shenanigans. phoebe cates doesn\\\'t do it for me.',0,0,0,0,'172.131.0.224','en'),(13105,2253,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>lets just say that poolsex has been described as <i>squeaky</i>. carrying out the act in public at herparentshouse, nj simply added points for risk/creativity/tackiness. yowzers! however, mass orgies in any public place in jersey definitely rack up good karma points, for sure, or at least good <i>mullet</i> points [see comment <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1253\\\">here</a> for more info on jersey mullet pride]. </tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13106,2255,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','werd up to the toronto crew.  umm, just a note that if you\\\'re looking for good comic stores in toronto, don\\\'t pass up a chance to visit \\\'the beguiling\\\' at 601 markham street (just south of the suspect video store near honest ed\\\'s).  as far as i\\\'m concerned, it blows the silver snail away.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13107,2257,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<br><b>january 14th:</b>  saw a penny on the ground, but left it.  the ground is too cold and wet and goopy.<br><br>\r\n<b>january 15th:</b>  i forget.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>january 16th:</b>  had to walk home from the ambient ping again because i lacked bus fare.  damn penny.  another weirdo tried to kiss me.  what, is my fly down?',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13108,2255,'2002-01-16 14:59:16',0,'critic','question: whats the draw as far as comic collecting and fantasy/adnime goes? believe you me, im not trying to sound judgmental, this is actually an inquisitively genuine question. i work with an anime-obsessed individual that is like <i>so</i> into it, and i just dont get it at all. i know its amusing, but so is scooby-doo and such. what am i missing? why dont i get it? perhaps its a matter of personal preference, but im pretty open to anything . i just dont get the anime thing AT ALL.',3,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13109,2257,'2002-01-16 15:08:28',0,'critic','<br><b>jan 14:</b> spent the last dollar-bill in my pocket on a cup of coffee. the bill was tattered and moist.<br><br><b>jan 15:</b> found one of those golden-dollar-coins on the elevator at work, and spun it on my desk all day, hourly daydreaming about and contemplating what to purchase with it.<br><br><b>jan 16:</b> put the golden-dollar-coin in my pocket this morning when i got dressed, determined to buy coffee with it. got to the coffee booth, noticed said coin was missing. must have lost it on the bus.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13110,2255,'2002-01-16 15:35:33',0,'jo','\\\"looking right with someone\\\" <b>is</b> a weird concept. ',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13111,2255,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my ex gf looked like she could have been my twin sister. people often mistook us for siblings, and were quite scandalized when we did anything even remotely romantic. we used to play it up to get peoples goat. interesting. but, we did <i>look good</i> together. now she looks good together with someone else. (sigh)</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13112,2257,'2002-01-16 15:43:37',0,'Herr Doktor','<br><b>January 15:</b> discover that loose change is falling out of my right pants pocket (the official pocket for carrying loose change) via a small nickel-size hole.<br><br><b>January 16:</b> put on clean pair of pants, having no pocket-hole. resolve to sew up pocket-hole in other pants before more change is lost. go about buying things today, aquiring more change.<br><Br><b>January 16:</b> will have forgotten to sew up hole, will wear pocket-hole pants, losting all change aquired on the 15th.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13113,2255,'2002-01-16 16:20:35',0,'Scapegoat to Peter','<p>I hear you there. Whats worse is when I have to realize that my ex actually looks better with someone else. Thats happened a few times.</p><br>\r\n<p>Scapegoat to Jo - I like to think that the looking good with each other doesn\\\'t mean anything but that depends on the person you are looking or not looking good with.</p> ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.166.21','en'),(13114,2257,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<b>14</b>: use debit card, no change.<br>\r\n<b>15</b>: use debit card, no change.<br>\r\n<b>16</b>: use debit card, get cash back to lend to coworker.<br>\r\nChange free living is nice.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13115,2255,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Scapegoat','<br><tt>the scary thing is that <i>i</i> actually looked substantially better when i was dating her, and i think it was somewhat mutual; since we broke up both of us have kind of slacked off in appearance. she cut off all her hair, we both put on a few pounds, we both started caring less about wardrobe details and trendiness. ha. interestingly enough, now were hanging out alot again as kind of slightly-more-than-friends, and our old look is starting to come back. its a very odd vibe going on here. strange, the synergism that can orbit two people at times</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13116,2257,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>perhaps. unfortunately, laundromats, parking meters, soda machines, news-stands and panhandlers dont currently accept plastic.... <i>yet.</i></tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13117,2257,'2002-01-16 16:55:41',0,'analog uper digital','!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13118,2255,'2002-01-16 17:14:45',0,'jo to scapegoat','i don\\\'t think it means anything either but it is weird that it\\\'s sort of a universally-said thing. especially because i think people mean different things when they say it. you can mean just aesthetically, as in: \\\"their physical appearance complements one another,\\\" or you can mean that they never looked right together like you never caught them sharing that loving eye-to-eye whisper or they never seemed entirely comfortable in one another\\\'s presence, etc. <BR>\r\nthere\\\'s also the SLIGHT implication when someone says that about a couple that one member of the couple wasn\\\'t punching his own weight. in other words, \\\"dude, he was so not attractive enough to be with her,\\\" which is a whole different thing altogether.<BR>\r\nthis is interesting. i\\\'m 5\\\'2\\\" round cancer woman, all breasts and hips and belly and laughter and i spent a long time with a <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/college/p_writing.jpg\\\">6\\\'4\\\" blond beanpole</a>. i used to stand on the bed to put my arms around his neck. but despite this, people often said we <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/college/p_symphony.jpg\\\">looked good together</a>.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13119,1253,'2002-01-16 17:40:04',0,'friend of leonard','<p align=\\\"center\\\"><img src=\\\"http://gs.cdnow.com//graphics/BIOART/AMG/L/60/83/00376083.jpg\\\" width=\\\"162\\\" height=\\\"170\\\"> \r\n  <br>\r\n  <font size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\"> <b>would you put \r\n  your arms around<br>\r\n  </b></font><font size=\\\"1\\\" face=\\\"Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif\\\"><b>me? \r\n  i won\\\'t tell anybody.</b></font> </p>',2,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13120,2242,'2002-01-16 17:54:10',0,'blue stocking','this was *magic*\r\n',2,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13121,2264,'2002-01-16 18:17:19',0,'kat','<i>...calling for butt plugs?</i>\r\n\r\n<p>naughty little tea drinking boy</p>',1,0,0,0,'209.239.204.90','en'),(13122,2264,'2002-01-16 18:18:02',0,'cookie','call me cookie',3,0,0,0,'156.153.255.236','en'),(13123,2264,'2002-01-16 18:33:36',0,'er, mmmkay,  then','<i>[alienated]</i>',4,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13124,2264,'2002-01-16 18:47:25',0,'hey','i drank tea once...',3,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(13125,2258,'2002-01-16 19:31:33',0,'trouble','never had quite an experience like that, usually something like that follows some sort of bodily warning of things to come but... you were high and being high especially on acid would have left you ignoring such warnings.  i suppose anybody would have reacted the same way.<br><br>\\\'interior humor\\\', now that\\\'s the <i><b>phrase that pays</i></b>.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13126,2258,'2002-01-16 19:39:42',0,'my interpretation','of \\\"acid\\\" was as in <i>stomach acid</i> not as in ye olde blotter variety ala albert hoffman.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13127,2264,'2002-01-16 19:41:00',0,'littlegirlkat','there is a picture of me somewhere...xmas circa 1977, wearing my star wars t-shirt and playing my newly aquired <a href=\\\"http://www.pong-story.com/atpong2.htm\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">pong home tv gaming system</a>. thanks mom! ',4,0,0,0,'209.239.204.34','en'),(13128,2264,'2002-01-16 19:46:25',0,'coyo','that website is a video game goldmine-- i\\\'m lost for hours',0,0,0,0,'156.153.255.243','en'),(13129,2258,'2002-01-16 19:47:58',0,'trouble to the interpetor','now see, a re-reading of that was warranted and i think you are correct.  but i will still stand true to my belief that while on acid, a person would in fact be prone to ignore such things as stomach tremors, the jimmy-shakes, the hot and cold flashes and possibly the final expulsion of \\\'the cookies\\\'.  <br><br>\\\'acid is the little square that takes you from here <i>to there</i>\\\'.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13130,2181,'2002-01-16 21:13:22',0,'m-arrk','Iwas there and we did do a whole lotta tomfoolery, and she did smile at him and she was as he described her. It was sucha timeless moment that he captured so well.  Why do we long for summer days so much? It seems opbvious with a moment like that.  When you reach perfection it just seems like forever.',0,0,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(13131,2250,'2002-01-16 21:27:08',0,'cait','<p>You should have thought about the money before acquiring an animal.  Surgeries aren\'t just expensive. Being a good pet owner is. There are many problems that could come up before you have enough money! Shame!</p>\r\n<p>><a href=\"http://www.adni.net/~cait/SCREW.html\">Maureen</a> nods.</p><img src=\"http://www.adni.net/~cait/maureen_tongue.jpg\">\r\n ',0,0,0,0,'140.186.34.213','en'),(13132,2253,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','sex on the front lawn of <a href=\\\"http://www.lsuc.on.ca/about/about_osgoode_en.jsp\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">osgoode hall</a> is one of the best ways to get muddy.  especially on the trip home, when you look at each other, and realize what just happened.  excuse me, i am in rapture.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13133,2253,'2002-01-16 21:32:48',0,'jo','jesus lord, now i wanna have sex on the lawn at osgoode hall. yo, palatial.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13134,2253,'2002-01-16 21:40:25',0,'once again','the topic of <i>the wild swinney orgy</i> pops up. i know who <i>i\\\'d</i> hook up with!',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13135,2253,'2002-01-16 21:42:19',0,'critic','<br><b>him:</b> \"yo baby, was i <i>good</i>?\"<br><br><b>her:</b> \"no baby, you were <i>OS<b>GOOD</b>E</i>!!!\"',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13136,2253,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow. thanks for reminding me of being 15 in NJ and running around at night skinny dipping in community pools and giving someone my first hand job. ahem. that was a long time ago. and i blame it all on my wild friend who also left all her jewelry on top of the car and realized as we sped away, chased by rent-a-cops, that the jewelry was still there/gone gone gone. argh teen angst. also, still have crush on phoebe cates. yum.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13137,2253,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','i never did anything wild.  once my best friend macked HEAVILY on the girl down the street while sitting on the curb in front of my house.  the neighbors came over in the evening and told me to tell my friends that that sort of behavior was going to warp their young childs\\\' minds.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni still remember that girl had a guns and roses welcome to the jungle tee shirt.  i do not remember her name though.  my friend\\\'s name was dax.  he had a fierce mullet and an oh-im-soooo-cool expression at all times.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nthat was summer of 87 or maybe 86. i didnt get any action until i was well into college, summer of 97 or 98.  ',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.31','en'),(13138,2253,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i am recalling hand jobs behind the bathrooms at the golden gate bridge taking the long way home from school.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13139,2264,'2002-01-17 00:40:27',0,'who you calling','stinky?',0,0,0,0,'156.153.254.42','en'),(13140,2253,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','this made me think of trampoline sex, back yard, july before senior year.',1,0,0,0,'150.176.174.11','en'),(13141,2263,'2002-01-17 02:16:52',0,'maybe','they only glow in the dark and are black in the daylight',0,0,0,0,'172.140.10.52','en'),(13142,2264,'2002-01-17 02:17:40',0,'nookie','I like cookies',0,0,0,0,'172.140.10.52','en'),(13143,2264,'2002-01-17 06:40:48',0,'anonymous cowherd','Uh oh, looks like swinney.org is getting porny again!  :^p~~~~',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(13144,2267,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i loved this story; particularly because of what happened to me yesterday morning: i was at 72nd street to catch the m72 crosstown to work. that stop is also the stop for the m5 downtown and the m57 uptown. a stuffy old well-dressed gentleman (obviously more experienced in taxi commutes than taking the bus) rather gruffly poked me and said: \\\"tell me which bus goes across town and when it comes\\\". I replied that what he wanted was the m72 (which was coming in a few minutes, on clockwork, as it does each day) and <b>not</b> to take the 5 or 57 unless he wanted to end up in greenwich village or on riverside headed uptown. before i could finish, the m5 rolled up and he immediately dashed and got on without looking or asking, glancing at me over his shoulder as i followed, shouting, in an attempt to correct his error. he gave me this lok as if to say \\\"young man, you are <i>insane</i>. i didnt make it in time. as the bus huffed off, he raised his hands in the air and mouthed a very obvious <i>\\\"Fuck YOU!!\\\"</i> at me through the windows.<br><Br>\r\nwhy? i dont know. its not like i didnt tell him correctly, and its not really easy to confuse 5/57 with 72. why are transit people so weird?! better yet, why are upper east-siders such a pretentious bunch? thats clearly where he was heading. i only work there, heh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(13145,2267,'2002-01-17 14:44:25',0,'Sparagmos','<p>Really liked this story - one question though McDonald\\\'s an all American meal?</p>',1,0,0,0,'64.229.161.37','en'),(13146,2267,'2002-01-17 18:13:04',0,'AMERICA','give us cheap burgers and reconstituted fries or give us DEATH!',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13147,2267,'2002-01-17 18:51:27',0,'McDonalds','You diss me now, but you <i>know</i> i get your love when its dinner-time...',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13148,2267,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<br>We\\\'re starting to develop a real tradition of public transportation/commute stories here on Swinney, and it\\\'s all making me pine away a bit for the good old days: when I\\\'d ride the <a href=\\\"http://www.transitinfo.org/cgi-bin/muni/map/system?ZOOM=2&XPART=3.5&YPART=3&d.x=10&d.y=13\\\">6 Parnassus, 7 Haight, and 71 Haight-Noriega</a> to my downtown job; or the <a href=\\\"http://www.transitinfo.org/cgi-bin/muni/map/system?ZOOM=2&XPART=4&YPART=4.5&l.x=8&l.y=11\\\">24 Divisadero</a> to the Castro; or the <a href=\\\"http://www.transitinfo.org/cgi-bin/muni/map/system?ZOOM=2&XPART=3.5&YPART=1&u.x=7&u.y=7\\\">22 Filmore</a> to the barren lands of the Marina; or the <a href=\\\"http://www.transitinfo.org/cgi-bin/muni/map/system?ZOOM=2&XPART=4&YPART=4&r.x=12&r.y=15\\\">33 Stanyan</a> to the shining heart of the Mission. And then there was the year I spent as a regular passenger of the <a href=\\\"http://www.transitinfo.org/cgi-bin/muni/map/system?MODE=ZOOM&ZOOM=1&XPART=0.5&YPART=1&Map.x=195&Map.y=188\\\">38 Geary</a>, SF\\\'s powerhouse line, which ferries more passengers per day than any other in the city. Amazing stuff happens there.</br>\r\n<br>There\\\'s a guaranteed story in every ride, if you\\\'re willing to pay attention. Pickup artists, homeless comedians, teenage gaybashers, beautiful mistakes, runaway drama queens, acts of amazing kindness, crimes and misdemeanors, and many many simple moments of zen bliss. If only Swinney had been around back then.</br>\r\n<br>But a few years ago I started commuting out of the city, and except for a few quirky carpool stories, and a few interesting road rage incidents, there\\\'s not so much to tell about my travels. It\\\'s all about zen (and the lack of it) now.</br>\r\n<br>Cheers for the entertaining transit stories--keep \\\'em coming.</br>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13149,2267,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter loves Transit','<br><tt>I\\\'m actually- believe it or not- planning a trip this summer: the goal is to get from my apartment in new york city to some predetermined place in sf <i>using only public transportation</i>. believe it or not, ive alredy worked out about 60% of the trip. but, if any of you have any good info about how to get across that crazy area from the midwest to cali do <a href=\\\"mailto:peter@rhodamine.net\\\">let me know</a>. the same goes for if any of you would like to join me.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13150,2267,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon to peter','i am so jealous. why didn\\\'t i think of this, i ask myself. man i wish i was taking a public transit only cross country trip. it\\\'s like a dream i never really knew i had.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13151,2267,'2002-01-17 20:40:00',0,'ryan','is the dirty dog considered public transit?',0,0,0,0,'209.162.48.29','en'),(13152,2267,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','An all american meal is from maybe 4 or 5 years ago, it isnt on the menus anymore, but you can ask for it. its a regular small cheeseburger, a small fries, and a small drink. It\'s about 2.50. I cant stand the thought of supersize, or the NEW BIGGER supersize. they are trying to kill us with that shit.',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13153,2267,'2002-01-17 20:44:58',0,'sims to ryan','not by my book',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13154,2267,'2002-01-17 21:20:54',0,'stella to peter','have you checked the green tortoise?',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13155,2267,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','the green tortoise is totally not public transportation. ',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13156,2267,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','<br>why isn\\\'t the Green Tortoise public transportation? I\\\'d say any sort of transport that involves sitting with a bunch of strangers is public transportation, whether it\\\'s a bus, ferry, steamboat, jeepney (if you happen to be in the Phillipines), or even airline. As opposed to a private car, plane, or bike. Peter could ride over here in a carpool van and that would still = public transportation. In my humble opinion.</br>',3,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13157,2267,'2002-01-17 22:14:40',0,'erb to gus:','are you the gus i met at jiffer\\\'s house and sometimes see in my yoga class?  the playwright gus?  ',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(13158,2267,'2002-01-17 22:32:27',0,'ryan','the green tortoise only does the \\\"adventure packages\\\" now but i believe it still may have one coast to coast trip. <p>\r\ni fondly remember riding that stinky hippy bus down from eugene to sf a few times in 94, 95 -- to visit a stupid but nice smelling girl.<p>\r\nit\\\'s sad they don\\\'t do that anymore though, it used to be about $35 for eug-->sf.  cheap for the budget conscious!',1,0,0,0,'209.162.49.58','en'),(13159,2267,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','anytime you have to be with the same group of people for an entire journey, and its a \\\"package\\\", and you have to pay for group food, its not public transportation. \r\n<p>\r\nerb: I dont know? who\\\'s jiffer? do you mean kristies yoga class on sanchez? you know Im a girl right? I wrote a play once...',0,0,0,0,'64.163.214.196','en'),(13160,2267,'2002-01-17 22:46:46',0,'erb','okay if you don\\\'t know jiffer then it\\\'s a no go.  i do know a girl named gus (who was friends with jiffer\\\'s cousin ashley) who goes to bikram yoga and also writes plays. kristie\\\'s class rocks too, though. i love it that she reads poems.  anyhow, nice writin\\\'.',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(13161,2267,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Queer Moll','Peter: I was thinking about this the other day, except taking only trains. And no Amtrack. Just trying to take state lines through the country. PATH to NJT, NJT to SEPTA, SEPTA to MDTA and on and on and on. And you would get special powers if you reached the Pacific and made it back.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13162,2267,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to Moll','<br><tt>thats what prompted me to post this... i rode the MTA subway to the path to NJT and back this morning, and all teh little wheels started tunring...</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13163,2268,'2002-01-18 01:47:15',0,'kat','it is okay to enjoy yourself. to touch yourself and feel ecstasy. to hold yourself. to love yourself. to LOVE yourself. to love yourself like you do others. to explore your body and your mind like a new lover. it is okay to go slow. it is okay to cry. it is okay to want more and to be angry and to release it all. it\\\'s okay to let go. to let go...and start over. it\\\'s never too late to let go and start over.\r\n\r\n<p>never too late to explore yourself.</p>',1,0,0,0,'209.239.204.71','en'),(13164,2268,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','you\\\'re very brave to write about something like this in a place like swinney, even if it\\\'s fiction.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13165,2270,'2002-01-18 12:05:46',0,'Joanna','I suspect that I think this about a lot of swinney men, but I\\\'d like to have children with you, based on this article.  ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(13166,2268,'2002-01-18 15:54:42',0,'hool','fantastically well written.  blunt and elegant.',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(13167,2267,'2002-01-18 16:47:24',0,'jo','hey gus i\\\'m rereading tolkien, too.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13168,2267,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>i had a friend who rode transit as a hobby.  one summer during the school break, she decided to ride every transit line in the city, all the way around.  she\\\'d get on at a stop, and ride it around to the same stop, and say, \\\"thanks,\\\" to the bus driver on her way out.  i wonder what they thought about it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>when she came to visit me a couple years ago, we spent the entire two days riding around on the subway, not going anywhere.  it was interesting.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13169,2268,'2002-01-18 18:31:56',0,'lotus to kat','thank you. funny how it is hardest to give one\\\'s own self permission?',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13170,2268,'2002-01-18 18:32:57',0,'lotus to le','thank you. i wrote it a while ago, actually, and kept feeling like i was going to explode. the people here are kind.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13171,2267,'2002-01-18 18:33:45',0,'stella','So would that make a cross country train ride not public transportation? It\\\'s for several days with the same people, and you could buy a \\\'package\\\'... \r\nDoes the Green Tortoise only have packages? It must\\\'ve changed some since the last time I looked into it. I know that it\\\'s considered a vacation-type experience to some, but I know that a few years back it could be used as \\\'transportation with flare\\\'. However, I think that the definition of \\\'public transportation\\\' in this case should be defined by the person making the journey (Peter) as he is the one to set any boundaries for himself.',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13172,2268,'2002-01-18 18:34:01',0,'lotus to hool','you flatter me, my dear. i\\\'ve read all your articles, and they flow so smoothly i forget i\\\'m reading...it is like i am right there experiencing it. thank you for giving me strength.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13173,2095,'2002-01-18 18:36:36',0,'bluestocking','\\\'state of suspended hope\\\' is one of those perfect phrases i am always looking for without knowing it. good luck to you.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13174,2267,'2002-01-18 18:38:05',0,'sims to jeeff','did you ever see that almodovar movie \\\'live flesh\\\' where the boy is born on the city bus so he and his mother get lifetime free bus passes?',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13175,2056,'2002-01-18 18:40:46',0,'blue stocking','my puke story:\r\n\r\ni\\\'m sitting at my birthday dinner, at my absolutely favourite chinese restaurant, laughing and talking with my loved ones, and trying to ignore the funny feeling in my belly. the meal is delicious, my dad is paying, and everyone\\\'s ordered fabulous dishes. the shrimp is spicy, and i am inhaling the tomatoes with my friend rice. we eat so much that there\\\'s no room for dessert. we signal for the bill. while the waiter goes to get it, i suddenly open my mouth and heave. there was so much vomit we couldn\\\'t just cover it with napkins. my mom lifted the entire red tablecloth and encased the puke with it.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13176,2272,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','Did you mean \\\"sacred\\\" and profane?',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.249','en'),(13177,2267,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','my gut instinct is to agree with gus. public transit is blank stares from made up suits at 7am. public transit is puke on the seat next to you. public transit is the smell of fat men and on rainy days, reactivated urine. green tortoise is people on vacation, happy looks, everyone introducing themselves and someone singing along with their guitar. the seats are ripped out, and you lay down on a comfy bed-like surface while the driver blasts your tape mix. GT is not public transportation, but it is in a bus and they do go places. i know, i know. it\\\'s a bus. it goes places. but it\\\'s not public transportation, trust me. sometimes there are smelly people though, but it\\\'s because they\\\'re europeans.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13178,2267,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','and also one more important disctinction: it\\\'s not funded by public money from the government. it\\\'s a private business designed to give you a cheap but comfy \\\"travel experience\\\".',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13179,2267,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i><a href=\"http://www.livejournal.com/talkread.bml?itemid=20548534&nc=7\"><b>public transportation</b></a></i> is something that has an acronym for a name and lets you ride for a handfull of change/swipe/token</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13180,2268,'2002-01-18 20:28:13',0,'anonymous','I hope that rain falls again on the inner desert.  One day you shall be free, and the heavens will weep with joy.',1,0,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(13181,2272,'2002-01-18 21:42:39',0,'Scapegoat to gus','<p>Yes, sacred and profane - even when I reread shit I miss so many typos. Damn it</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.169.94','en'),(13182,2266,'2002-01-18 21:48:39',0,'Sparagmos','<p>I wish I could see this cowgirl, whether she is real or not. Seeing someone that can be described in this way puts a smile on my face and I can\\\'t help but feel a little more alive.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.169.94','en'),(13183,2266,'2002-01-18 22:22:34',0,'Paul G','Yes, yes, yes.  I read this quite a few times, but I wasn\\\'t sure how to respond.  She is a part of the American interior though - between Colorado and Kentucky, and between Texas and the Dakotas.  She probably drives a truck, and is a single-mom too.  She may not earn her living through her work outdoors, but that is where she is at home.\r\n\r\n<p>I fell in love with a cowgirl.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I have heard that there are cowgirls in Argentina, and I think that they are called \\\"jilleroos\\\" in Australia...yep...something like that.  They all have that same look and aura about them though...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Cool poem!</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.155.152.139','en'),(13184,2273,'2002-01-18 23:27:47',0,'critic','so new yorkers have souls <i>after</i> all',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13185,1971,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','lately i have been getting skinny.  i think it is because egg is gone.  i do lots of exercises: karate and running.  im trying to save money and pay off all my debts with interest rates over 7%.  <P>i think about going out to eat and imagine myself eating the food that i want, shrip tempura sushi, chicken curry, and i savor it.  then i keep driving past the 41st street exit to pink godzilla, past the star of siam in old soquel, home to perhaps a bowl of shredded wheat, perhaps a plate of spaghetti, or a single pizza boat.  the result is a skinnier me.  skinny for all the meals that i dont have with egg.  no egg\\\'s red curry.  no bagel with egg.  no eggs and bacon.  no egg.  too bad skinny boys are not sexy.  i certainly am not feeling it.  \r\n</P>\r\n<TT>\r\n<BR>da> austin what gives wit that, speaking of.  she goes away for a\r\n<BR>da> week and you\\\'re freaking?\r\n<BR>austin> i have not freaked a soul\r\n<BR>austin>im a regressed bachelor hermit that goes to see late shows during the week by his self\r\n</TT>\r\n<P>i guess i was feeling sexy earlier in the week, but that has completely worn off.  no more hump days for me.  not for a while.</P>',4,0,0,0,'205.179.19.164','en'),(13186,2271,'2002-01-19 00:55:05',0,'jo','haha. grandpas rock. this was really good, jeff.',0,0,0,0,'172.174.242.45','en'),(13187,2267,'2002-01-19 00:57:19',0,'mental note','have baby on bus.',0,0,0,0,'172.174.242.45','en'),(13188,2271,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah, i quite liked it too.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13189,2270,'2002-01-19 03:43:33',0,'Paul G','Well, what do you say after that?  I am celibate.  But, which one of the Joannas are you?  [curious]',0,0,0,0,'63.155.148.6','en'),(13190,2271,'2002-01-19 08:11:50',0,'ryan','my sis\\\'s name is lindsay.',0,0,0,0,'209.162.49.42','en'),(13191,2270,'2002-01-19 09:41:02',0,'your guide to joanna\\\'s','Joanna = joanna hubris mcleod\r\njo = joanna mmmbed vaught\r\nstarlajo = jo starla eaton\r\n\r\nrecite, commit to memory. there will be an exam.',0,0,0,0,'203.27.69.91','en'),(13192,2274,'2002-01-19 10:23:26',0,'zelig','Dear members of Creed,\r\n\r\nPlease commit suicide.\r\n\r\nthanks,\r\nsomeone with good taste',1,0,0,0,'24.27.31.92','en'),(13193,2271,'2002-01-19 11:16:34',0,'i usto live','@ 1903 lindsay ave',0,0,0,0,'172.186.49.226','en'),(13194,2271,'2002-01-19 11:17:28',0,'for the first','13 years of my life',0,0,0,0,'172.186.49.226','en'),(13195,2267,'2002-01-19 16:01:22',0,'jeeff to sims','sounds interesting, i\\\'ll add that to my list of movies to watch.  the list has about 1000 movies.  and i haven\\\'t actually written it down.  but i\\\'ll add it, nonetheless.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13196,2274,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','Bah! It\\\'s all enough to make me want to start a pir8 radio station.  Radio KACK or something.',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(13197,2267,'2002-01-19 18:36:38',0,'stella to gus/weeb/peter','Ah... I see the distinction that you\\\'re making now. ',0,0,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(13198,2274,'2002-01-19 19:47:01',0,'yack radio','what musik would u like to play?',0,0,0,0,'172.159.246.202','en'),(13199,545,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin of yeti','<P>here is a list of things i have asked yeti for recently:</P>\r\n<P>\r\n_ a monitor that i still have not returned\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ along with a keyboard and mouse(not returned)\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ a hub (returned)\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ host my dad\\\'s website\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ debug my server at work\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ host bmg\\\'s website\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ a copy of FreeBSD\r\n</P><P>\r\n_ information on butt sets\r\n</P><P>\r\nim going to see him this weekend. *best friendly*\r\n</P>',1,0,0,0,'205.179.19.164','en'),(13200,2270,'2002-01-19 21:02:14',0,'starlajo to paul and \\\'guide\\\'','that\\\'s right. very important not to get us mixed up. although - possibility for amusement is there.',1,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(13201,2274,'2002-01-19 21:15:19',0,'Usher','U got it bad.',0,0,0,0,'63.208.132.240','en'),(13202,2274,'2002-09-09 00:01:42',195,'matt','<BR><BR>there\\\'s internet radio, right? isn\\\'t technology advanced enough now to start a station online, play whatever you want, sell ad space if you want (or not)?<BR><BR>if the programmer\\\'s taste was good enough, and you could access the feed from any number of music-playing devices, the station would succeed (by whatever measure you\\\'d like to apply), and people\\\'s taste might improve as a result of being exposed to better music. the people who like shitty music probably just don\\\'t know there is better stuff out there.',1,0,0,0,'209.208.29.72','en'),(13203,2274,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to matt','<br><tt>theres already a million stations that do that, actually. <a href=\"http://us-dc1.streams.bassdrive.com:8024/listen.pls\">bassdrive.com</a> is one of my favourites. good idea, though!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13204,2270,'2002-01-19 23:58:58',0,'Paul G','Ahhh.  Ohhh.  Ooops.  Well, if you get two pictures and find them appealing then I will, for a small fee, dance for your bachelorette party.  However, if you find them unappealing then I will, for a slightly larger fee, remained fully clothed and stay in the States.  [silliness]\r\n\r\n<p>Thank you, Guide.</p>',0,0,0,0,'63.155.148.100','en'),(13205,545,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i have yeti. he is sitting next to me playing snake on his cellphone, he is trying to beat his high score. he said \\\"ohhh i\\\'m so fucked, i got infinite snake loop!\\\"',0,0,0,0,'165.247.222.12','en'),(13206,2274,'2002-01-20 02:10:31',0,'dipshit','canada has its own music?',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13207,1411,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','ok so im in bankgok and last night i was watching some sort of dubbed version of some kind of adventure gameshow in which these two teams had to compete on how to make a pumpkin-slinging canon out of junkyard materials!!. they kept saying \\\"fucktong\\\" very clearly over and over and that was all i could understand. also, the sky train here begins at the stop \\\"On Nut\\\" and ends with the stop \\\"Mo\\\' Shit\\\". that just kills me.',0,0,0,0,'203.148.185.229','en'),(13208,2274,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','who is craig david?',3,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13209,2274,'2002-01-20 08:26:34',0,'drew','<p>actually i\\\'m pretty sure that people who like craig david know there\\\'s lots of other music out there.  if they heard it, they might possibly learn to like other kinds of music, but i hardly think they are just einsturzende neubauten fans waiting to blossom.  i think those of us with comparatively esoteric taste in music have to accept that a large number of people enjoy pleasant, bland, uncomplicated tunes that stick in their heads, sung by men and women with pretty voices and faces and clothes.  they\\\'re just not going to enjoy tuneless howls made by zitfaced teenagers from ohio or throbbing electroglam made by transsexual freaks from manhattan, unless perhaps the specific tune is fairly innocuous and appears in a friends episode or a tom hanks movie.  their relationship with music is not like that.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>however, i do believe that a lot of people today have the potential to like \\\"quality\\\" electronica, simply because so much of it is devoid of any specific or explicit persona; there\\\'s no messy sex or politics or religion or philosophy or even poetry to get in the way.</p>',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(13210,2271,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i don\\\'t know anyone called lindsay, which is why i chose the name.  i also don\\\'t have a grandfather.  like i mentioned in the \\\'blurb\\\', this is a mix of fantasy and reality.  altho if it provokes realistic reactions, i guess that\\\'s a big compliment.  thanks!',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13211,2253,'2002-01-20 08:33:39',0,'drew','that live hot chat action girl is so cute!  i think i want to be her.  as for phoebe cates: i was so in love with her when i was a kid and gremlins came out.  that hasn\\\'t changed.',0,0,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(13212,2274,'2002-01-20 09:01:47',0,'ssj3gostu','i <3333 the artful dodger.',1,0,0,0,'208.59.199.233','en'),(13213,2274,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to drew','<br><tt><i>\"transsexual freaks from manhattan\"</i>... im from manhattan. i just got back from 8 hours of dancing to and enjoying some <i>quality music</i>. music is out here that has more personality than most all of the status quo, is messy, and has every conceivable muse clutched within its throbbing grasp. its out here. its called <i><a href=\"http://www.sonicnet.com/artistinfo/960870.jhtml?_requestid=142596\">new york soul</a></i>.</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13214,2270,'2002-01-20 11:18:17',0,'Joanna','What do you mean what Joanna am I? there\\\'s only one of me! (\\\"I am Joanna McLeod of the Clan McLeod - there can be only one\\\" if you wanna get all Highlander about it).  Some swinney-ites know me as \\\"Joanna with a Capital J\\\" if that helps to seperate me from jo-mmmbed.  I like capital letters.  \r\n\r\n<BR>\r\n<I>best friendly</I>',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(13215,2270,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','\\\"a joanna in the hand is worth two in the bush.\\\" -- unattributed',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13216,2270,'2002-01-20 13:52:59',0,'o to s5','is that because Joanna knows all about bush?',1,0,0,0,'144.136.20.148','en'),(13217,2270,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','oh shit! sorry there! i forgot that don\\\'t exist!\r\ndamn... won\\\'t be doing that one again in a hurry.\r\n',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(13218,2270,'2003-03-07 17:56:26',69,'starlajo','nor will i be stuffing up my grumpy sarcastic comments ever again... i meant to say that i forgot that _I_ don\\\'t exist.\r\n\r\ni only just woke up, it\\\'s just gone 7, cut a girl some slack.',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(13219,2270,'2002-01-20 20:26:01',0,'jo','uh. hi. ',0,0,0,0,'172.157.126.168','en'),(13220,2277,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i woke up this morning to two little clusters of bed tremors and shaking and i sleepily pondered what if this is the big one, but then realized it was just jeff scratching his head and then i went back to sleep.\r\nit\\\'s going to be a really great day. sunny. sniff that air!',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13221,2277,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','they should throw out that xmas tree. do they smoke inside? those things go up like crazy. now Im going to worry about them burning to death.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.249','en'),(13222,2277,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','they don\\\'t smoke inside. i watered the tree the other day. yeti and i have a bet going about how long it will be there.',4,0,0,0,'165.247.214.131','en'),(13223,2271,'2002-01-20 23:39:29',0,'deb','any idea for the reader as to why she would have come back? curious...',3,0,0,0,'130.68.234.202','en'),(13224,2273,'2002-01-20 23:40:40',0,'deb','unlike most critics? ;)',5,0,0,0,'130.68.234.202','en'),(13225,2273,'2002-01-20 23:49:45',0,'critic','i have no soul. this much is true.',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13226,2277,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>props for NeXT</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13227,2273,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to critic','<br><tt>why wouldnt we?</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13228,2277,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','<a href=\\\"http://www120.pair.com/mccarthy/nextstep/intro.htmld/\\\">intro to NeXT environment, with many screenshots</a>  [on the \\\"what\\\'s this do, what\\\'s that button for?\\\" level.]',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(13229,2277,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','if you feel like tinkering with a STEPish thing, but don\\\'t want to track down an original NeXT or the appropriate NeXT/OPENSTEP for your (intel, sparc, parisc) machine, there is a GNUSTEP that basically runs on top of [linux, bsd, whatever].  You can download a bootable cd-image (barebones linux with just gnustep) to tinker.  I haven\\\'t tried it yet, but it looks like it might be <a href=\\\"http://simplygnustep.sourceforge.net/\\\">cool.</a>',1,0,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(13230,1948,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i had to stop listening to music on the subway because i\\\'d always start laughing or something, and scare everyone around me.  also, the soundtrack to my favourite movie makes me cry.  i really like the feeling it gives me, but i think that listening to music just so you can become sad is unhealthy.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13231,2273,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','something to do with the manhattan project?',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13232,2273,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','critic is my least favorite person on swinney.org.',0,0,0,0,'165.247.202.133','en'),(13233,2273,'2002-01-21 03:30:54',0,'ssj3gostu','that\\\'s kinda mean.',1,0,0,0,'208.59.199.233','en'),(13234,2273,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','no, it\\\'s not.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13235,2273,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to jeeff','<br><tt><i>re: the manhattan project:</i> back in the 40s, some of the ongoing research for the manhattan project was taking place at my university; it seems that they were recruiting strapping, burly football players as work-study employees, having them shuttle large amounts of materials from lab to lab on hand-carts through the tunnels connecting all the buildings. <br><br>well, it turns out that the \"materials\" were highly radioactive chemicals, and that within a few years, <i>100%</i> of those football-team workers had died, either of leukemia or hodgekins disease. they wrangled a nice class-action suit against the university, and won, as faar as i know.<br><br>\r\ninteresting</tt>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13236,2273,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to ssj3gostu','<br><tt>speaking of manhattan, when you gonna come visit, stoo? last night was 8 hours of chilling with/listening to derrick carter. for some reason, of all the people i know, i think youd appreciate that most of all :)</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.29.138.17','en'),(13237,2277,'2002-01-21 06:36:03',0,'kat','i like country living. my favorite is when the dog comes inside smelling of pine and grass and sunshine and fresh wind. \r\n\r\n<p>but sometimes it\\\'s wonderfully nice to have others to share the quiet with.</p>',4,0,0,0,'209.239.207.195','en'),(13238,2275,'2002-01-21 06:45:03',0,'Paul G','I would like to eat your salmon - no, that is not a metaphor.  Salmon is one of the things that keeps me from being a vegan.  The smell of almost anything grilling is a very powerful sensory experience.  Maybe it is some sort of trigger for something primitive, buried deep within the brain.  The night, the moon, the fire, the snow...those things are all buried deep.',1,0,0,0,'63.155.152.120','en'),(13239,2277,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','ellen used to show me how the NEXT thing worked at the mills computer room. those were the days. the glory days of female independence and the amerian right way of living.i came home here on soi santisuk for lunch because im a freeloader, and theres this old crinkly wrinkle brown guy here doing some handyman work in the kitchen, and in trots my cousin\\\'s ex-boyfriend like in a sitcom. you know how in a sitcom there are always people just sort of trotting in and out of each other\\\'s apartments? that is my life currently, like that show 227. and i jumped a bit because the last time I saw this sucker I was screaming at him to get the fuck away from the car door, but he was polite and friendlty and dashing as hell here, bringing a handyman to fix some broken door in his ex-girlfriend\\\'s cousin\\\'s family\\\'s apartment. I really have no idea how or why he is here to fix his cousin\\\'s ex-boyfriend\\\'s door, my cousin told him to take a hike days and days ago. my cousin\\\'s father told him to take a hike. but he is still here, offering to give us rides places and having doors fixed. really, I dont get it. but everytime I ask questions like \\\"I thought you said you broke up with him, why is he taking me out to the movies nad paying for everything and giving us a ride to the place we are going to have a drink with your new boyfriend?\\\"  I just get an answer from someone like, \\\"its hard here to be unpleasant to people....you see, he is still her boyfriend, she is just not his girlfriend.\\\"  you dont know how many times a day this sort of logic is condescended to me.  I am truly in a foreign country. ',0,0,0,0,'203.148.185.249','en'),(13240,2270,'2002-01-21 08:28:07',0,'Paul G','To Starlajo - Ah, yes: \\\"stuffing up\\\".  This reminds me of the first time that I interacted with a group of people from NZ.  Someone asked if I knew what a \\\"Kiwi\\\" was.  I said: \\\"Why yes, it\\\'s a bird that roots around in the mud...\\\".  They didn\\\'t stop laughing for hours, or bother to explain why my words were so funny.  Why doesn\\\'t everyone speak American?  Oh, don\\\'t be a woosie.\r\n\r\n<p>To Joanna - Oh it\\\'s you.  Well, I do have a big chest; if you\\\'re still scanning.  Kisses.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To Jo - Hi.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To S5+O - I will contemplate the meaning of the aphorism of the bush.  The sphinx too was a woman. ',0,0,0,0,'63.155.152.120','en'),(13241,2270,'2002-01-21 09:49:24',0,'kat','hey Sphinxy...how\\\'s it hangin?',4,0,0,0,'209.239.207.195','en'),(13242,2270,'2002-01-21 10:15:06',0,'Paul G to Kat','It\\\'s pretty good - I can still lift the cat off my lap with it.  I\\\'m just not sharing right now...',0,0,0,0,'63.155.152.120','en'),(13243,2270,'2002-01-21 13:04:14',0,'too much','information!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13244,2277,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the feminist-NeXT connection doesnt die there... i managed to score one in \\\'97 from a tres butch compsci major who was agressive enough to elbow out all the others and snag it from the budgeting-out trash-pile from the university engineering school\\\'s annual clean-out. to this day, i have no idea how she schlepped that and an old-skool sparc 6 blocks down broadway, staying on her feet amidst the trailing nest of cables, evading ambushes by the tech-geek-marauders.<br><br>i ended up selling that machine to some digital animator major in the architecture school for $100. i regretted that decision the moment i took the cash. oh well. but, its not like i was going to move that beast all over manhattan with me.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13245,2277,'2002-01-21 13:14:17',0,'ebay','<a href=\\\"http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=1322724673\\\">you know you want it</a>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13246,2274,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','ok who put me on the craig david mailing list. i dont think its very funny. ',6,0,0,0,'24.43.182.49','en'),(13247,2274,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','ha. HAHAHA.\r\n<br>\r\ndefinitely a good prank. i\\\'ll have to remember that one for the future.',0,0,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(13248,2278,'2002-01-21 14:22:32',0,'jo','how much orlando bloom am i going to get if i see this movie? he\\\'s in black hawk down but