story on his caller ID to see who had been calling him. (OK...maybe laughing isn\\\'t the appropriate emotional reaction to reminiscing on my past as a stalker.)  Now I have a really firm grasp of the difference between a full-fleged relationship and just being fuck buddies.</p><p>I\\\'m glad you understand a little bit more of where I stand, and I wish you much more enjoyment and considerably fewer guilty feelings in whatever you choose to do in the future, masturbation or otherwise.  If I knew you personally, I\\\'d give you a bottle of really nice lotion that I have.  I\\\'ve been told it contributes to phenomenal hand jobs, so I\\\'m assuming it would also function well under solo marathon conditions.</p>',1,0,0,0,'207.87.74.1','en'),(13728,2346,'2002-02-02 02:08:09',0,'leonard','i like shaving my <a href=\\\"http://www.estronaut.com/a/nipple_hair.htm\\\">chest hair</a>.',1,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13729,2342,'2002-02-02 02:11:42',0,'leonard','there was this guy at my school looking for love in the bathroom stalls. he wore a t-shirt that read \\\"Carpe Penum\\\". son of a bitch kept trying to grab my crotch.',6,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13730,2347,'2002-02-02 02:33:30',0,'humbert_humbert','i did one too. :*',0,0,0,0,'24.27.31.92','en'),(13731,2345,'2002-10-06 17:40:35',261,'sine','that poem is actually lyrics to a song\r\n\r\ncalled Love\\\'s Secret Domain by Coil.\r\n\r\nget it...LSD. I knew you could.',1,0,0,0,'205.179.65.211','en'),(13732,2345,'2002-02-02 05:44:48',0,'me again with the music trivia','',0,0,0,0,'205.179.65.211','en'),(13733,2345,'2002-02-02 05:45:45',0,'sine again yo:','<a href=\\\"http://home.kscable.com/weddle/reviews/lsd.html\\\">a linkie-poo</a>',1,0,0,0,'205.179.65.211','en'),(13734,2345,'2002-02-02 05:51:00',0,'lyrics','Love\\\'s Secret Domain \r\n\r\nOh rose, thou art sick\r\nSeduce... let loose\r\nThe vision and the void\r\nBlood sickle... honey suck\r\n\r\nIn little children\\\'s heavy heads\r\nMy dreams erupt while in my bed\r\nInnocence is dripping red.\r\n\r\nIn dreams I walk with you\r\nIn dreams I talk with you\r\nIn dreams...\r\nYou\\\'re mine\r\nAll of the time.\r\n\r\nHeads on fire and drunken lights\r\nDays devoured by hungry nights\r\nIn love\\\'s secret domain\r\nThis is mad love\r\nThis is mad love\r\nIn love\\\'s secret domain.\r\n\r\nSweet tortures fly on mystery wings\r\nPure evil is when flowers sing\r\nMy heart,\r\nMy heart is a rose\r\nThis is mad love, Oh\r\nThis is mad love\r\nIn love\\\'s secret domain...\r\n\r\nGive sanity a longer leash, but\r\nSome of us have sharper teeth\r\nIn love\\\'s secret domain...\r\n\r\nIn dreams I walk with you...\r\nIn dreams I talk with you...\r\nIn dreams...\r\nYou\\\'re mine\r\nAll of the time.\r\n\r\nOh rose, Thou art sick\r\nThe invisible worm\r\nThe vision and the void\r\nThe blood sickle cuts.\r\nAnd the honey sucks.\r\n\r\nOh rose, thou art sick !\r\n',1,0,0,0,'205.179.65.211','en'),(13735,2342,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i took a latin prose class once; it was a blast translating pliny\\\'s lyrical expressions: <i>\\\"i will bugger you with the full length of my mast\\\"</i> and <i>\\\"no lips are sweeter than those of a littel boy\\\"</i>. it was tough doing it with an academic-ish straight face in front of my age-old professor. heh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13736,2349,'2002-02-02 06:24:40',0,'cunt','poor jeremy. am i the only one that feels for this guy?',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13737,2349,'2002-02-02 06:37:53',0,'heathen','jeremy deserves every second of his life with me.',0,0,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(13738,2344,'2002-02-02 07:08:23',0,'kat','yay for roberta flack and johnny cash!',1,0,0,0,'209.239.196.204','en'),(13739,2348,'2002-02-02 07:12:58',0,'Joanna','I love this article and I love the feeling that you\\\'re describing.  ',1,0,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(13740,2344,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i listened to johnny cash all night tonite.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13741,2349,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>i witnessed a baby crapping for the first time two days ago. it was pretty obvious what he was doing, even without the meaty squirts. he looked like a little old man, with his lower lip puckered out, straining a out a log.\r\n</p><p>\r\ndid i mention the endless crying? imagine a fire engine mated with a telephone in the middle of the night multiplied exponentially that can only be stopped when you get the nipple into his mouth. a black hole of need. you can throw a universe of breast milk, burp-up wipe towels, rattle-y toys, and aching arms into it, but he\'ll still resent you for not being his mom.\r\n</p><p>\r\ni only took care of this infant for two days, and only during the days, but it was enough. i glimpsed the edge of the abyss, and i am in awe of anyone that can jump in. heathen\'s writing feels totally true. babies *are* a big ball of want. that\'s all they know.\r\n</p><p>\r\nplease keep writing here.</p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13742,2348,'2002-02-02 09:33:51',0,'Scapegoat','<p>This was beautiful.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.171.153','en'),(13743,2349,'2002-02-02 16:13:49',0,'dahlia','<p>public expressions of love do not generally cause me to feel sorry for the loved one. </p>',0,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(13744,2346,'2002-10-03 18:45:24',70,'Fuzzy','This was absolutely perfect.',2,0,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(13745,2347,'2002-02-02 18:03:20',0,'deb','very, very nice...',2,0,0,0,'64.12.103.29','en'),(13746,2349,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','perhaps if you know heathen and jeremy you have a better understanding of what goes on, but yeah, in the last heathen swinney i felt sorry for jeremy too.  only because he is portrayed a little unmercilessly as kind of bumbling.  is he bumbling?',3,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(13747,2349,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<a href=\"http://jeremy.org\">jeremy</a> is pretty much the least bumbling person i can think of. he\'s totally graceful, incredibly intelligent, and very... deliberate. meaning, he\'s present.',4,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13748,2339,'2002-02-02 23:25:07',0,'Scapegoat','<p>Thank you.</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.171.153','en'),(13749,2349,'2002-02-02 23:31:17',0,'heathen','jeremy is okay.  you\\\'re probably just projecting.',0,0,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(13750,2349,'2002-02-02 23:34:18',0,'kat','i\\\'m guessing he\\\'s a fire or earth sign...[sagittarius maybe]???',1,0,0,0,'209.239.194.243','en'),(13751,2345,'2002-02-02 23:37:27',0,'dude','coil sucks. ',0,0,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(13752,2348,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i love this feeling too. wow.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13753,2341,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','can there be reconciliation now?',1,0,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(13754,2349,'2002-02-03 01:24:44',0,'dahlia','<p>i find the commentary far more merciless than the original posts, myself. fortunately, i think jeremy might like mercilessness.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(13755,2349,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','jeremey isnt bumbling, but sometimes if you\\\'re in the right place at the right time, you can see up his skirt. (or the wrong place at the wrong time, depending).',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(13756,2348,'2002-02-03 01:43:19',0,'hool','if i were the girl that jeeff writes about here, and i came to swinney and read this post, i would want to find him quickly and wrap myself around him again. ',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(13757,2348,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>thanks guys, i still have the warm fuzzies in my stomach.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and hool, maybe that\\\'s the idea.  *wink*</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13758,2349,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to kat','good job. he is a sagittarius.',0,0,0,0,'165.247.219.37','en'),(13759,2341,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>\\\"don\\\'t take it personally\\\" is a myth and an impossible standard.  it is also a way for people to get away with saying shitty things to your face.  i think \\\"don\\\'t take it personally\\\" is one of the most fucked up things existing in the world today.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, on swinney.org, if you voice an unpopular opinion about someone or something, you\\\'re likely to have someone (or a bunch of people en masse) jump down your throat.  that to me seems to conflict with people wanting to post under their own names.  just a thought.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>to critic: i strongly disagree with your comment.  if people ignore the stuff that stinks in the world, then we all have to walk around smelling each others shit.  fuck that.</p>',4,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(13760,2349,'2002-02-03 06:50:36',0,'sigh','i tried once to get heathen to stalk me, but now she\\\'s busy with child...',0,0,0,0,'12.81.66.134','en'),(13761,2348,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if a girl ive written about here came to swinney and read my posts, she would want to find me quickly and wrap me around a post. heh. naah, just kidding. jeff, this is so nice. im experiencing these feelings again lately, and its wonderful. its been a while, and im glad you mentioned them- its kind of tuned me in in good ways.</tt>\r\n\r\n\r\n \r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13762,2341,'2002-02-03 07:55:56',0,'shit stinks','whether you ignore it or not.',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13763,2341,'2002-02-03 08:18:46',0,'jo','since i was the first person to introduce the ugly \\\"t\\\" word into recent swinney discussion, i would like to say that i officially retract my use of the word. what i meant to say was: \\\"random out-of-nowhere character attacks of strangers, couched in left-handed compliments, confuse me.\\\" that and nothing more.',0,0,0,0,'172.173.96.87','en'),(13764,2348,'2002-02-03 08:21:44',0,'jo','also oh my god i\\\'d completely forgotten about using steamroller as a sweet precursor to foreplay. ah! sexual tension of my college years! how i miss you!<BR>\r\njeff, you kick ass. cheers to butterflies in the stomach.',0,0,0,0,'172.173.96.87','en'),(13765,2348,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','yay, crushes!',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(13766,2345,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','im sitting at the fancy mall trying not to hear the cover band playing \\\"open your heart\\\" downstairs. i am only here because of a misunderstanding. I just wanted to feel like I was sharing something of myself with yous. theres a girl wearing a lot of makeup next to me. i ate a really disgusting lollipop. Im having fun and stuff but i somehow find myself going places that i would never go at home - the mall, for instance. and i gain very little cultural enrichment from it.if i hear another cover band perfectly render a song that was on the radio when i was 7 years old ill just scream and puke and die. i just listened to this monk go on and on about how you shouldnt love your body or your family because when you die noone would pay as much for your body as they would fried chicken. monks think theyre really a riot.',0,0,0,0,'203.148.143.24','en'),(13767,2349,'2002-02-03 17:33:17',0,'heathen to sigh','did you fill out the <a href=\\\"http://heathen.net/currently/stalkee.html\\\">application</a>?',0,0,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(13768,2328,'2002-02-03 18:27:24',0,'curious','but wait...this conflicts with <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2229.txt\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">past stories ala manwhore</a>. what happened?',1,0,0,0,'209.239.195.86','en'),(13769,2350,'2002-02-03 18:30:53',0,'bling bling baby','bling bling',1,0,0,0,'209.239.195.86','en'),(13770,2351,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i wish my town had a streetcar.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13771,2350,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i went to a place called <i>van gogh\\\'s ear</i> in kennilsworth NJ once. it seems to have been alot like the place you described.</tt> ',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13772,2350,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i was at van gogh\\\'s ear in guelph once, and it wasn\\\'t a wholly bad experience.  then again, i was with the girl i \\\"had my eye on,\\\" so that improved things for me.  i also stayed on the main floor, which i hear was a good choice.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13773,2340,'2002-02-03 20:25:03',0,'suga\\\'','yack!',0,0,0,0,'172.170.214.120','en'),(13774,2345,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to monk','<P>but wont you die sooner if you eat too much fried chicken?</P>\r\n<P>oh god, i am turning into  a one_liner@swinney.org.  the deprevity.</P>',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13775,2328,'2002-02-03 20:47:11',0,'Scapegoat to curious','<p>If you read all the way through manwhore I think you will find that it doesn\\\'t really conflict all that much. Manwhore expressed frustration because of a situation that was void of the intimacy I was looking for. This is a situation where I got burnt for the exact opposite. Damned if you do, damned if you don\\\'t.</p> ',0,0,0,0,'64.229.171.153','en'),(13776,2350,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Pee Moll','I was lost in Kennilworth, NJ a few weeks ago while trying to get somewhere unimportant. I took a piss in the Dunkin Donuts. It was very cold outside.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13777,2349,'2002-02-03 21:15:23',0,'jeremy','heather <em>says</em> i lost the key, but in fact we just couldn\\\'t find it.  that it showed up near my laundry is only more evidence of the temporal-spatial vortex inside clothes driers.',0,0,0,0,'204.120.52.1','en'),(13778,2353,'2002-02-03 21:22:00',0,'killing time','beep beep<BR>\r\ntime is a oneness,<BR>\r\na singularity<BR>\r\nchukka chukka<BR>\r\ndig the phenomenology<BR>\r\nthe positive,<BR>\r\nthe negative:<BR>\r\nthe absolute<BR>\r\ncuz i\'m about to recruit<BR>\r\nthe H-dog, the Heidegger<BR>\r\nthe Nazi philosopher<BR>\r\nhe knows what it\'s about<BR>\r\nnow I\'ve completed the mission<BR>\r\nof this temporal inquisition --<BR>\r\npeace out<BR>\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13779,2345,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','one line comments are block-rockin.',1,0,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(13780,2345,'2002-02-04 00:44:15',0,'when yer dead','there is no more laying pipe. no more eating fried chicken.',1,0,0,0,'209.239.207.57','en'),(13781,2351,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','streetcars are the elderly old ladies of public transit.  slower, prone to breakdown, not as flexible as the bus, but strangely charming sometimes.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13782,2349,'2002-02-04 00:57:19',0,'heathen','i am going to chain the mail key to a cinderblock.  it is hopeless to think that it might not end up in his pocket again, but at least this way i can enjoy the spectacle of him dragging his skirt around behind him. ',0,0,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(13783,2345,'2002-02-04 03:27:36',0,'bOk b0k','mmmmm, fried monk...',0,0,0,0,'172.140.51.35','en'),(13784,2349,'2002-02-04 03:38:48',0,'yeah','i filled two (2) out. my dryer puts grease spots on my new clothes, gack!',0,0,0,0,'12.81.90.196','en'),(13785,2354,'2002-02-04 04:07:20',0,'Paul G','Yes: there is something about guests that makes you see things differently - as if looking through their eyes.  Or, maybe it is your past and future coming together, and that is disorientating. ',0,0,0,0,'207.220.191.188','en'),(13786,2353,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','i dont like nazis. ',1,0,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(13787,2353,'2002-02-04 04:40:15',0,'and all the dopplegangers sing','doo de doo de doop...doop de doo de doo de doo...doo de doop de doop de doo...',4,0,0,0,'209.239.210.9','en'),(13788,2353,'2002-02-04 05:57:18',0,'dr. dre','<br>an\\\' all da n*ggas sing\r\nswing down, sweet chariot style, an\\\'\r\n<i>let me ride!</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13789,2354,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i think its something about s5 and o.  i believe them to be magic. i fell in love with nyc again after they were here, and its only gotten stronger since then. i think they somehow break paradigms.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13790,2355,'2002-02-04 06:11:09',0,'New York City','come to me, tyler.',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13791,2355,'2002-02-04 06:32:42',0,'nyc','*stench of urine*',0,0,0,0,'165.247.212.119','en'),(13792,2355,'2002-02-04 07:36:39',0,'kat','a place is a place is a place. you can have fun anywhere if fun is all you\\\'re looking for. if you really want to learn go where you\\\'ll be challenged. find the people that are doing the things you crave...immerse yourself in that world. ',3,0,0,0,'209.239.210.9','en'),(13793,2354,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','*melt*',1,0,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13794,2355,'2002-02-04 11:19:21',0,'opp','yea you know me!',0,0,0,0,'172.140.51.35','en'),(13795,2355,'2002-02-04 11:22:44',0,'the beauty of an idea','arizona, colorado, montana, wyoming, california,  and oregon.',0,0,0,0,'172.140.51.35','en'),(13796,2347,'2002-02-04 13:52:50',0,'blue stocking','i am so glad you\\\'re doing these. i think it is hard to juxtapose words with a photo, but you do it so well. the content as well as the placement.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13797,1561,'2002-02-04 14:16:42',0,'trouble','random swinney article strikes again!  i would like an update on which minerals are in fact gay as well as the current status of the great wall<br><br>this article and its comments got big smiles from the coffee crew here.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13798,1561,'2002-02-04 14:21:48',0,'trouble','austin, can i make a plea for the ability to edit our comments or must we live in regret of such errors in text? *nice supportive*',2,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13800,2353,'2002-02-04 14:54:34',0,'<i>the go getter</i>','<br><img src=\\\"http://www.provalue.net/users/jds/people/ambar.gif\\\"><br>austin swinney, why do i love you so?!',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13799,2355,'2002-02-04 14:36:40',0,'trouble','as wild as it sounds there appears to be a mad art scene in alaska.  my cousin and her man o\\\' the moment recently relocated there and they have found a ton of artists, craftsmen and so forth.  not much of a music scene although poetry slams are common.  when asked why this might be she commented on how close people become up there, everybodys creativity feeds everybody and folks appear to be obsessed with \\\'leaving something behind\\\'.  while this may sound morbid it\\\'s really not.  the people really seem to just want to be their most creative and leave their work for everybody to embrace or in equal cases ignore, it doesn\\\'t matter to them.  life is at its most basic it would seem in alaska.  <br><br>i am also told that there is a flood of good homemade hooch, maybe that explains some things as well.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13801,2348,'2002-02-04 15:22:35',0,'trouble','there is something to be said for the newness of crushes and blossoming sex.  there is <i>also</i> something to be said for the quiet walks home afterward in the middle of the night with mind awash in remembrance of the quiet moments spent exploring your new lover while the single parent sleeps with her good ear to the pillow.  those walks were fantastic.  walks in the middle of the night are always magical.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13802,2354,'2002-02-04 15:48:25',0,'jo','weird to see either o or s5 capitalized.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13803,2355,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Mad Moll','Alaska seems like a pretty nice place. The unfrozen part of it anyhow. No big cities + artistic people sounds like a win/win situation as far as I\\\'m concerned. I knew this really great guy from Sitka who carved things. Big things. And small things. Sitka Sitka Sitka.',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13804,2358,'2002-02-04 17:40:50',0,'and?','',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13805,2353,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','wow, you mean there is love in the world <B><I>go getter</I></B>, after all?',2,0,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13806,2353,'2002-02-04 17:54:20',0,'<i>the go getter</i>','<br><img src=\\\"http://www.provalue.net/users/jds/people/ambar.gif\\\"><br>yes darling. i dream of the day you might tell me to <i>suck it!</i>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13807,2355,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','alright. i\\\'ve gone through the list, checking out schools on the web, and here\\\'s my new short list:\r\n<p>\r\nHumboldt State(CA)<br>\r\nSonoma State(CA)<br>\r\nHunter College (NY)<br>\r\nStony Brook(NY)<br>\r\nSimon Fraser U. (BC)<br>\r\n<p>\r\nsuggestions?',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.186','en'),(13808,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i work a few blocks from hunter college. its in a chi-chi-ish part of the upper east side, near madison avenue and all the fashion places. my point? i dunno. just rambling!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13809,2355,'2002-02-04 18:08:07',0,'just curious','why those spots?',1,0,0,0,'209.239.202.39','en'),(13810,2355,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>oh man. you are so lucky. i\\\'d be partial to the florida keyes, the southwest, anytown in southern california, maybe colorado? the southwest is beautiful and full of small little dusty towns. but what i wanted to say to you is MAUI MAUI MAUI!@! fresh coconut smoothies, cane juice with a squert of lime, warm nights, beautiful views of the ocean, surfing at night with the jellyfishies glowing contentedly under you.... cheap rent. healthy food. cute girls and friendly people. oh sigh.\r\n</p><p>\r\nnew york is great place, but not everyone\\\'s up of tea. it\\\'s unsavory, like living in someone\\\'s armpit. but a very learned and exciting armpit. </p>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13811,2355,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','new addition: CSU L.A.\r\n<p>\r\nwhat does chi-chi mean?\r\n<p>\r\nwhy these? i don\\\'t know. they have good websites. ',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.186','en'),(13812,2355,'2002-02-04 18:28:10',0,'kat','well i would pick UC Santa Cruz and the slugs over Humboldt or Sonoma anyday. same feel, better location...and SC is a very eclectic place. arty or sporty or hippie depending on what you\\\'re into [and SF is not that far away]. humboldt and sonoma are pretty isolated. and i would choose anyplace in SF over LA. but then again...this is just me, i\\\'m probably biased. northern CA is so different from down south. and if you could choose maui over LA...well i know where i\\\'d be going.',4,0,0,0,'209.239.202.39','en'),(13813,2353,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i deposit time in a savings account.  i am a mild miser.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(13814,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/cgi-bin/dict.pl?term=chichi\">chi chi</a>.<br><br>also: an armpit it may be, but for real... its been pretty heavily deoderant-i-fied lately. <i>unsavory</i> it isnt, i mean, unless you have a penchant for slumming it in spanish harlem or something, which your average person doesnt. 8 years and no memorable unsavory experiences yet :)</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13815,2350,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>i like your cat-point of view articles.</p>',0,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(13816,2355,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','i second Kat\\\'s vote for UC Santa Cruz. but then again, that\\\'s where i went for four years, and they were mostly wonderful, so i\\\'m biased. going to school in the forest, looking out over Santa Cruz from a hilltop and watching the fog roll in & consume the city every afternoon, hanging out with interesting people from around the country...<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut then again, the creative writing classes there aren\\\'t much better than what you just described. that was my major, and i regret it now. you\\\'d be better off just taking lit classes (or whatever you fancy) and doing your creative writing on the side.<br>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13817,2351,'2002-02-04 19:17:35',0,'Scapegoat','<p>Street car over bus anyday</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.171.153','en'),(13818,2346,'2002-02-04 19:39:26',0,'blue stocking','thanks',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13819,2355,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt the wet','simon fraser is a nice school. but fuck bc. they have a harris-like premier now cutting and slashing at all corners. im sure that by the end of the year simon fraser will be nothing but a bunch of portables on an indian reserve. ',1,0,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(13820,2351,'2002-02-04 20:22:20',0,'synchronicity','can be unnerving.',3,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(13821,2345,'2002-02-04 20:27:48',0,'joyrider','\\\"windowpane\\\" is on the same album.   it is essential coil.  iffen you\\\'re in to coil, that is.  very dirty bass groove in this one.',0,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(13822,2355,'2002-02-04 20:33:07',0,'jo','people who recommend their alma maters after going there is usually a solid compliment to the school in and of itself. ',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13823,2348,'2002-02-04 21:07:48',0,'joyrider','<p>nothing beats beats crushing the pillow (she slept on the night before) to your face and *inhaling* with eyes shut.  she will flood in and fill up your brain and chest. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>girlsmell is rad rad rad.</p>',2,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(13824,2348,'2002-02-04 21:26:19',0,'jo','i missed my boy so much on friday that i asked my friend winston if i could hold his hoodie, suspecting that it had boysmell. it did. i buried my nose in it and used it as a cover and wrapped myself in boysmell for a little while. it was reassuring.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13825,2348,'2002-02-04 21:48:11',0,'hool','is there a common distinctive scent in boysmell that makes it attractive?  or is it more on an individual basis?\r\n\r\nsomeone should market boysmell cologne and girlsmell perfume, for those lonely unscented nights.',0,0,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(13826,2355,'2002-02-04 21:58:16',0,'ty','this is great, i really appreciate all the input. what is a \\\"harris-like premier\\\"?',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.186','en'),(13827,2348,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i am totally having these feelings today, while i sit here at work, yawning from not really getting any sleep.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13828,2355,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','to kat: UCSC is not an available school. btw, sonoma is only 1hr north of SF. I don\\\'t think hawaii is realistic, it would cost a whole lot of money just to get there. besides i will probably be shipping my car wherever I go. New York...well, I\\\'ve always lived on the East Coast. ',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.186','en'),(13829,2358,'2002-02-04 22:07:18',0,'Pong Pong','I went home, dude. :(',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13830,2355,'2002-10-18 20:39:20',75,'tyler','what about san jose?',1,0,0,0,'165.247.164.186','en'),(13831,2355,'2002-02-04 23:03:04',0,'kat','oh think not 1 hour young soldier...that would be more like two with traffic at most any time of the day. 101 from GG Bridge north through Santa Rosa is one of the worst stretches of freeway traffic this side of Cali [aside from parts of 880]. and san jose? blech. not to say that sonoma isnt a nice place...if you can\\\'t do sc tho maybe shoot for another place. SF? or even east bay. Oakland or berkeley would be better than san jose. but now that we\\\'re narrowing the field i can think of a lot of other states i\\\'d want to try out if i couldn\\\'t end up on prime beach real estate. seattle? boulder? portland? and coyo who sometimes posts on here seems to have a fond liking for montana.',1,0,0,0,'209.239.210.194','en'),(13832,2355,'2002-04-12 05:27:03',57,'weeb to peter','you know i love new york, but come on. there\'s trash everywhere, vacant lots are mini-garbage landfills, where i grew up in chinatown, the streets and gutters ran with mysterious foul fish innards and offal from the meat markets. after a day outside, your nose is coated with black mucus. your shoes start taking on that \"new york\" odor. you can disagree, but new york when compared to other cities, comes off as decidedly grimy. maybe i notice the smells more because i\'m lower to the ground? :)',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13833,2348,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','i think the common element is something too subtle to be able to describe.  probably pheremones.  because every girl i know smells different, but they all smell like girls.  mmmm..',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13834,2358,'2002-02-04 23:25:49',0,'leonard','and it was fun.',6,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13835,2348,'2002-02-04 23:52:33',0,'jo','YES! yes. i\\\'ll second that. also, boys can\\\'t smell boysmell. i know this because when i took the hoodie, i said, \\\"mmmm boy,\\\" and melissa said, \\\"can i smell it?\\\" and she said, \\\"mmmm boy.\\\" and then all the boys smelled it and were like, \\\"what? i don\\\'t smell anything.\\\" but maybe that was too general of a test.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13836,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to weeb','<br><tt>i really wouldnt say that nyc is any dirtier than any other 5-million-plus resident us city; the idea of it being a messy shithole is a rather antiquatted stereotype. nope, not trash everywhere; rarely see vacant lots in manhattan, or \'landfills\' for that matter; the one vacant lot in my neighborhood was turned into a rather beautiful community garden. <br><br>also: chinatown is sometimes a stinky mess, but you know the maybe 25 stinky square blocks of chinatown isnt a good representation of the 26 square miles of boroughs. and i mean, show me a fish-market that doesnt stink? ive seen larger and stinkier in SF! incindentally, ive never experienced the black mucous, not in 8 years of living here. no stinky shoes (inless you aim for stepping in pudles and dogshit). all in all, i would say that its not any messier than any other major city.<br><br>there\'s a chance that you might be remembering nyc of, say... 15 years ago. its long gone, for sure! ok, ill end my rant here. yes i like my city; but i really hate to see it typecast inaccurately. sure, you often hear about its seedy, dirty side, but thats because its alot more interesting to talk about than its shiny, pretty side. also, im sure the bay area is much more naturally beautiful, and it makes you only remember the seedier side of nyc, but for real... thatd be like me asking of all of SF looked like what i see on \'nash bridges\' reruns :)</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13837,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i think SF is dirtier, more crowded, and less safe than most of nyc. it\\\'s very block to block here, and it\\\'s such a compact area of land that you don\\\'t get far before you\\\'re in a BAD part of town. it can happen without knowing. in new york you pretty much have to mean to go to the bad part of town.  ',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13838,2355,'2004-07-27 05:00:32',578,'Netfong','thats true. at least i was surprised to see that nyc is alot more picturesque and clean and accessable than toronto is, by far. ive never been to SF, but i can imagine it must the  pastoral paradise to end all if you think nyc is bad. also, theres no urban sprawl in nyc. and le is right- you have to mean to walk through the bad parts of town. (best friendly!)',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13839,2355,'2002-02-05 00:15:45',0,'SF Fishmarkets','i stink to high heaven!',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13840,2355,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','nyc has plenty of urban sprawl. it\\\'s called new jersey and connecticut. :)',1,0,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13841,2348,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','for some reason, the story about huffing the hoodie grosses me out ayh!  maybe it\\\'s just the thought of inhaling the pheromones of any other boy than mine.',3,0,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13842,2355,'2002-02-05 00:56:45',0,'erb','<p>i went to humboldt state!  and it is indeed isolated, but also beautiful and a great tightly knit community.</p>\r\n<p>also! i bought my tickets for NYC today! i will be arriving at JFK at one minute before midnight on valentine\\\'s day.  maybe peter and i can share a valentine\\\'s minute. armpit city, here i come!</p>\r\n',1,0,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(13843,2355,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','le\\\'s comment made me think a little about some of the SF residents i\\\'ve known over the years who somehow manage to live for years, seemingly, without escaping their circumscribed territories. yuppies who avoided just about anywhere more downscale than the marina. gay men who rarely left their castro/mission terrarium. and neobeatnik hipsters (such as myself, in my early twenties) who wouldn\\\'t go near pac heights. i remember a coworker (nob hill resident) who, upon hearing my (then) western addition address, shrieked \\\"but that\\\'s the <i>ghetto!!!</i>.\\\"<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit\\\'s all pretty amazing, when you realize how small this place is. i think some of those border distinctions were eroded in recent years, but only because the entire city went the way of the marina/pac heights in recent years, cost-of-living-wise.<br>',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13844,2355,'2002-02-05 02:04:48',0,'kat','two thumbs up for warehouse living in headless point',4,0,0,0,'209.239.196.160','en'),(13845,2358,'2002-02-05 02:30:20',0,'treetop','you skipped you linguistics exam?  bad. bad.',0,0,0,0,'172.151.191.50','en'),(13846,2348,'2002-02-05 02:45:24',0,'whats','steamroller?\r\n',0,0,0,0,'172.151.191.50','en'),(13847,2355,'2002-02-05 02:50:42',0,'treetop to weeb','how cheap is it to live in maui? i think i could be into the island life.',0,0,0,0,'172.151.191.50','en'),(13848,2348,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','basically, if you\\\'re laying beside someone lengthwise, you just roll over on top of them and then roll back again.  try to crush them like a steamroller.  it\\\'s great for initiating foreplay.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13849,2348,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','unless you\\\'re a sumo wrestler.  then it would initiate death.',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13850,2348,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this weekend involved secret stealthy kiss-forays between superbowl plays. when, however, i put on the marvin gaye, no further seduction was necessary.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13851,2355,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','hmm it *has* been a long while since i\\\'ve lived in nyc (1982), but the last time i visited, a year ago, i certainly did get the black boogers effect. so did all of my family members (don\\\'t ask me how i know that). but maybe we live in a dirtier part of town than you do (brooklyn). i agree it\\\'s been cleaned up since then, *and* i still love it, but i can\\\'t agree that it\\\'s a clean city. sorry. we\\\'re just going to have to disagree! i certainly didn\\\'t live in the nice parts, so maybe that had something to do with it. taylor, when i was in maui, i saw rooms for rent signs advertising rooms for 200-300$ a month. but that was a year ago. dang. maui. yeah.',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13852,2355,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','hmm it *has* been a long while since i\'ve lived in nyc (1982), but the last time i visited, a year ago, i certainly did get the black boogers effect. so did all of my family members (don\'t ask me how i know that). but maybe we live in a dirtier part of town than you do (brooklyn). i agree it\'s been cleaned up since then, *and* i still love it, but i can\'t agree that it\'s a clean city. sorry. we\'re just going to have to disagree! i certainly didn\'t live in the nice parts, so maybe that had something to do with it. taylor, when i was in maui, i saw rooms for rent signs advertising rooms for 200-300$ a month. but that was a year ago. dang. maui. yeah.',1,0,0,1,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13853,2348,'2002-02-05 05:50:58',0,'ssj3gostu','hi! i know i\\\'m a little late on this, but this was very nice. reading it made me feel warm.',1,0,0,0,'64.121.92.56','en'),(13854,2348,'2002-02-05 06:07:25',0,'international luvah','let the spirit move ya<br>\r\nlet me groove ya<br>\r\nlet\\\'s get it on',4,0,0,0,'209.239.197.155','en'),(13855,2355,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<font size=3><p>actually, the fish remains and meat trimmings were left in the gutter NOT near a meat or fish market. random stuff gets dumped, just like in sf, but i\'ve never seen fish bones/beef fat just dumped in the middle of a city block randomly before, or since. also saw an entire car abandoned on the highway, a fridgerator, and an entire front axle attached to two tires. i\'ve seen two men frozen to death from the winters there. end defensive blab.\r\n</p><p>\r\nand peter, before you get all mad at me, i have to say, i was only responding to tylor\'s questions about where to go-- and you have to admit, nyc isn\'t for everybody. we both know there is nothing better than new york air in the morning, or the smell of summer off a hot sidewalk. there is nothing i would rather be doing than walking into chatham square library, or elizabeth street, eating the little egg shaped chinese muffins. or on prince street, getting a fresh loaf of italian bread and eating some fresh pickles from the barrel (50 cents for 2, 5 for a dollar). the amazing thing about new york is it\'s inherent contradictions. the city is amazing and squalorous, beautiful and painful. i don\'t think you need to defend it :) it\'s going to continue to mesmerize and enchant. </p></font>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13856,2355,'2002-02-05 07:47:31',0,'Scaoegoat','<p>Just to clarify an earlier question a \\\'Harris like Premier\\\' means that the politicians in B.C. are going to be trying to cut back on any gov\\\'t funding for the universities there. Not to mention that the political and economic climate there is really bad right now. They have steadily increasing unemployment due to the softwood lumber dispute and massive cut backs to government. It has caused me to think twice about moving out there, it could be a really depressed place in the next few years. Harris actually refers to the Ontario Premier \\\'Mike Harris\\\' that has done more then enough to screw with the education system here. SFU is a nice school though, I\\\'ve visited once and have several friends that are there.</p>  ',1,0,0,0,'64.229.160.71','en'),(13857,2348,'2002-02-05 08:22:03',0,'jo','i hardly think \\\"huffing\\\" is the right verb here. there was no dramatic sucking in. it wasn\\\'t a wife-beater he\\\'d worn while playing basketball; it was a hoodie that\\\'d been on top of a t-shirt and a sweater all day. which now that i think of it makes it kind of more impressive that it retained boysmell.',0,0,0,0,'172.138.168.212','en'),(13858,2353,'2002-02-05 10:16:37',0,'jebro','it\\\'s tricky when you think you have alot of it.  time.  but we have very little.  man\\\'s gotta move and enjoy.  hello austin. (and friends)',0,0,0,0,'195.250.155.30','en'),(13859,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh weeb, i dont get mad :)<br><br>also: i\\\'ll have to admit that youre probably right, at least on some levels. i dont find the city so dirty and all, but its definitely not for everyone; i think its just that this physical place of NYC has given me such personal catharsis and salvation... moments i\\\'d wish for anyone trying to make sense out of their world while maturing in it. so maybe its not so much the <i>place</i> as the <i>experience</i> (or vibe if you will) that i wish everyone could experience.  as this characteristic is based mostly on people, its probably available wherever good people are. my \\\'hood-pride just got in the way of me seeing that, thats all!</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13860,2348,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>clothes-smell? boh. I say only a few things beat grabbing girl after walking around city all day, burying face in hollow cleft inside right collarbone and scapula and slowly inhaling several times while pretending to be hugging. and some people find vicks inhalers to be euphoric. rarrr.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13861,2023,'2002-02-05 13:25:29',0,'','Uterine amnesia giving way to Sepultura?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13862,2359,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sometimes i cant sleep at night; i lay there awake, staring at my ceiling in black and white. i let the sound of the trains passing under my apartment lull me, the hiss of city busses slithering past soothe me to sleep, and sometimes it actually works.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13863,2355,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>uh oh. im just remembering the three dead bodies we found in the harlem river in 4 years of crew pracitces there. maybe you have a point, heh.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13864,2355,'2004-07-27 05:00:32',578,'Netfong','i think the rule is that, to be able to effectively or meaningfully criticize a place (or to defend it for that matter), you actually have to be <i>currently living there</i>, and probably for more than a few years at that. not singling anyone out here, of course, just generalizing. like, if anyone that wasnt from toronto (or canada for that matter) were to criticize my hometown, id have to kick them to the curb! in the same way, id save my opinion on San Fran, as ive only been there fora week or two here and there on vacations.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13865,2357,'2002-02-05 13:44:18',0,'Nefong','i like your writing, blue stocking.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13866,2357,'2002-02-05 13:45:42',0,'blue stocking','oh! thank you very much.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13867,2359,'2002-02-05 13:46:49',0,'blue stocking','ooh.\r\nthis is just marvellous.',0,0,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(13868,2359,'2002-02-05 15:52:05',0,'jo','that photo kicks so much ass.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13869,2348,'2002-02-05 15:53:59',0,'jo','(also there\\\'s the good chance that while you\\\'re doing this, she\\\'s giggling quietly and has her arms slung around your neck. RAH!)',1,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13870,2348,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>for sure!  also: squeezing.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13871,2359,'2002-02-05 16:48:41',0,'mojo23','um, ditto.</p>\r\nwhat they said.',4,0,0,0,'209.53.140.12','en'),(13872,2354,'2002-02-05 16:52:37',0,'mojo23','i think it\\\'s s5.  he helped me to see vancouver all afresh and bright when i arrived here all bedraggled and uninspired.  he needs to come back soon, as the glow is fading...',4,0,0,0,'209.53.140.12','en'),(13873,2354,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i believe s5 to be a magic mojo man. i often wonder if he, in fact, thinks this of himself, too.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13874,2217,'2002-02-05 17:11:31',0,'jo','(hi. today\\\'s our one month anniversary. i am cheesy.)',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13875,2360,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1597.txt\\\">wet streets</a>, also.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13876,2353,'2004-08-24 18:01:43',83,'adamajah','<p>\\\"it\\\'s tricky to rock a rhyme<br>\r\nto rock a rhyme<br>\r\nthat\\\'s right on time<br>\r\nit\\\'s tricky\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>hey jeff</p>',1,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(13877,2348,'2002-02-05 18:26:23',0,'kat','drew says i smell like gingerbread and sunshine....*melting*',4,0,0,0,'209.239.196.158','en'),(13878,2348,'2002-02-05 18:30:05',0,'dont you love','how romantic geek boys can be?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13879,2355,'2002-02-05 19:06:22',0,'stella to tyler','<P>Is sfsu on the list? I went to sf state. Granted, I graduated a while ago, but when I was there, the art/music scene was pretty interesting. </P><P>\r\n\r\nSonoma state is nice, but Kat\\\'s right. 101 north is a beast of a highway. Plus, you\\\'ve got that massive toll into SF that makes going in on any regular basis a big thorn in the side (What\\\'s the toll at the GG these days? It was $4 last time I was there). Plus, Rohnert Park, where Sonoma State is, is less than appealing. Very small town. </P><P>\r\n\r\nHumboldt state... Arcada. *sigh*. I left SF and lived in Arcada for a little while. I loved it. VERY small town, very far from SF (esp if you don\\\'t have your own transportation), but its such a neat little town. NOTHING can compare to a sunny summer day in Arcata, CA. Trinidad cove beach. Plus that drive up through the redwoods is undeniably gorgeous. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13880,2358,'2002-02-05 19:08:32',0,'stella','sometimes its just IMPORTANT to go back home. Especially on a day like this. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13881,2348,'2002-02-05 19:15:12',0,'stella','<P>yesterday I was home, feeling sorry for myself in my premenstral state. I spied the boys Yankee cap, all gross, stained brown around the inside band. I put it to my face and INHALED. Whew. However gross I knew it to be, the smell of my boy inhaled into my lungs and brain calmed me and made me smile. </P><P>\r\nIf that\\\'s gross, it\\\'s okay. </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13882,2349,'2002-02-05 19:25:09',0,'stella','Umm, Heathen, your website is hysterical. And your son is gorgeous. ',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13883,2360,'2002-02-05 20:14:03',0,'wet','dreams',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13884,2355,'2002-02-05 20:29:13',0,'treetop to stella','ggbridge is $3 and the bay bridge is $2.  I have been planning on going to sfsu to work on my mba.  it seems like a great school and the instate tuition is sooooo afordable. (900 a semester for my mba program)  the only problem is that i need a job to pay the rent. also, arcata is nice and i have some friends who go there. it is a small place to me and i personally would get a bit bored there.  lots of good weed there though.',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13885,2359,'2002-02-05 20:37:35',0,'leonard','this makes me feel warm inside.',2,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13886,2361,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ive been thinking about my family and my growing-up years alot lately, and reading peices like this always help me unearth more and more of the gems about my life that ive forgotten. this was really awesome, trouble.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13887,2361,'2004-07-27 05:00:32',578,'Netfong','nice piece. i like faygo red pop, though. by the way, im also one of those people that likes reading longer swinney pieces ;)',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13888,2355,'2002-02-05 20:46:51',0,'stella','<P>Hrm, $3? Maybe it just seemed like more because I was so poor back then. </P>\r\n<P>SFSU is a great school. Great programs, teachers, etc. I liked it a lot. </P>\r\n<P>Arcata is a bit slow... which is why I only lived there for 5 months. I needed a serious break from reality. It\\\'s a great place for that, for sure.</P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13889,2355,'2002-02-05 20:49:45',0,'stella','<P>Also, my brother lives in Alaska. I would move there in a HEARTBEAT if it was an option. Talk about slow, though. Alaska, while absolutely heaven on earth, is very slow. However, I don\\\'t know many other places where you can sit in a hot tub all night and not realize it because the sun never set. </P><P>\r\n\r\nps - I know that ArcaTa is not ArcaDa... I\\\'m just slow myself sometimes... </P>',0,0,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(13890,2353,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to jebro','i have a letter for you.',1,0,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13891,2361,'2002-02-05 22:12:39',0,'pussil','bravo matt. well written. it was not quite the ending i had hoped for, but the journey had me spellbound.',2,0,0,0,'216.118.127.194','en'),(13892,2360,'2002-02-05 23:06:22',0,'nobody','do it.',0,0,0,0,'66.69.229.76','en'),(13893,2361,'2002-02-05 23:19:24',0,'trouble to pussil','the ending you hoped for?  please explain when you get a chance, i\\\'m really open to suggestions.  bear in mind though that when it comes to andy and his story i know deep down the outcome will always be something along the lines of <i>\\\'...and the hero plodded off triumphant into the sunset\\\'</i> or similar verbage.',1,0,0,0,'68.47.160.8','en'),(13894,2361,'2002-02-05 23:27:57',0,'beautiful line','<i>\\\"somewhere, somehow along the way andy became a man without anybody looking\\\"</i>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13895,2361,'2002-02-05 23:50:54',0,'joyrider','there are not many things more triumphant than a human being that quietly reverses a life on the skids.  good for your cousin.\r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'m going to go listen to aaron copeland now.</p>',2,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(13896,2361,'2002-02-05 23:52:57',0,'pussil to trouble','fine choice.  excellent. i much prefer that one! my problem is one with specifics. the hero of such lofty and messianic stature that you painted must not plod through law school, unless he is to end up an atticus finch, and at that point, and only then leave us, triumphantly, and in the senset. but not in utero, incubating in potential, having pushed sysiphus\\\' rock up the hill, but not made it stay.',2,0,0,0,'216.118.127.194','en'),(13897,2357,'2002-02-05 23:55:05',0,'joyrider','the bit about incantations / language / magick sort of jumped out.  i was thinking about something similar after chatting with le once.  you reminded me to think about it some more.  thanks!',0,0,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(13898,2361,'2002-02-06 00:04:17',0,'trouble to pussil','good points.  i didn\\\'t mean to somehow elevate the kid to god-like stature but rather just celebrate his effort and his successes.  perhaps i went too far and on the same token maybe if i had somehow illustrated more the obstacles he faced would there be a better understanding of how grand his placement is in my little world.',1,0,0,0,'68.47.160.8','en'),(13899,2362,'2002-02-06 01:38:13',0,'click here to join','more wet dreams',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13900,2362,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','i\\\'m getting really tired of people who don\\\'t use their names to make rude comments. get some balls. ',4,0,0,0,'64.130.166.109','en'),(13901,2363,'2002-02-06 01:52:36',0,'Enrique Florido','I EAT YOU FOR LUNCH!',6,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13902,2362,'2002-02-06 01:53:46',0,'click here to join','My comment was not intended to be rude and I wish to remain anonymous.  I made a valid point about water, dreams, and websites that require you to sign in.  Oh, the <i>irony</i>!  I could never be anonymous in a site like that.',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13903,2362,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>people\'s comments can reveal alot about their personality; maybe people post anonymously because therye intimidated by the ugly head their personality can occasionally rear. oh well. i think alot of new folks post anonymously, too. come on, folks, use your/a consistent name! let us get to know you.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13904,2363,'2002-02-06 01:57:54',0,'mahir','i kiss you!',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13905,2363,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i prefer fuck<i>tong</i>.',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13906,2363,'2002-02-06 02:28:06',0,'treetop','i sometimes call fucktards analfuckfacedbastards, but only if i am really upset.',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13907,2362,'2002-02-06 02:36:36',0,'treetop','why is mia nearing the end of her long short life?',0,0,0,0,'172.139.21.77','en'),(13908,2363,'2002-02-06 02:38:51',0,'heathen','i have a special fondness for brian\\\'s change.  i can remember a huge tupperware container of pocket change sitting on his desk at maz.  he let me raid it once to scrape up enough money to go across the street to the scary store for a copy of barely legal.',0,0,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(13909,2363,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','heathen. <br>\r\nyou really paid for a skin mag with spare change?<br>&nbsp;<br>\r\nyou did. <br>\r\n*uncontrolled giggling* <br>',1,0,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(13910,2363,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i like \\\"fuckarsecunts\\\".  also, brian left approx $400 AU in change in his drawer at nirvana.  we had a fine time counting it.',2,0,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13911,2363,'2002-02-06 03:43:14',0,'also','\\\"fuckchops\\\" works nicely',0,0,0,0,'12.81.84.120','en'),(13912,2363,'2002-02-06 03:43:44',0,'homeless bastard','well now...aren\\\'t we all wondering who\\\'s feelin a little jealous cause others are laying pipe and he aint???',1,0,0,0,'209.239.193.102','en'),(13913,2363,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','i remember the first time I bought a burrito and had enough money coming in to leave my change in the tip jar. now I scrape it up and use it for the bus.\r\n<p>\r\nI have a distant cousin who used to say \\\"well fuck me runin\\\'!\\\" every chance she got. I kinda liked that. but it aint no \\\"fucktard\\\".',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(13914,2363,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin on change','oof has a cauldrin of change, a huge, HUGE, stailess steel (10 gallon?) pot that he has a few inches of change in.  the plan being to fill the entire thing, i suspect the pot will weigh hundreds of pounds.  when it is full, to then go burry it in a play ground.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nwell thought out plans, #1.',2,0,0,0,'64.174.154.185','en'),(13915,2365,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','my friends call that place \\\"cafe hairdo\\\". When my dad was up here having breakfast with me a few weeks ago, he wouldnt shut up at the waitress. Im not so embarassed anymore. I just feel sorry for the waitress!',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(13916,2363,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','eat a tard, fucktong.',0,0,0,0,'203.148.185.174','en'),(13917,2364,'2002-02-06 06:38:17',0,'Scapegoat','<p>Props to Conner! I love kids for shit like that</p>',0,0,0,0,'64.229.161.229','en'),(13918,2365,'2002-02-06 07:05:22',0,'pony','yay for auntie!  [we love her]',4,0,0,0,'209.239.193.61','en'),(13919,2364,'2002-02-06 07:06:54',0,'kat','my neighbors six year old always use to talk about seeing the dog\\\'s \\\'liptstick\\\'',4,0,0,0,'209.239.193.61','en'),(13920,2362,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Dthqlatthng Moll','I once had a dream that I drowned. I was climing on a tree, and beneath the roots of the tree there was deep water. I fell through a crack in the roots and sank. I died. It was very relaxing.\r\n',1,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13921,2365,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>today, as i was riding the bus home from work, a normal, nice looking man got on and sat down across from me; he noticed the woman next to him reading the classifieds section for free-lance jobs, and asked her what she did, explaining that he himself had also been scouring that same page for work earlier in the day for java work. <br><br>she gave him this traumatized look that screamed \"a <i>stranger</i> is <i>talking</i> to me!\" and squeezed as far as she could away from him on the rush-hour packed bus, frowning a sour frown the whole way, as if he had just asked her the most lecherous thing you can imagine, when it jsut seemed he was sharing an empathetic moment.<br><br>whenever i see things like this, i always feel a little sad inside; theres something to be said about shared human experiences, and people\'s (lack of) willingness to talk to each other. id love to see the day when you can get on the bus and say hi to folks that youve traveled with daily, usually for years, without raising any eyebrows. as of now, its like were locked in some weird sort of imposed silence.<br><br>when i was little, i used to always be so embarassed when my mom talked to strangers in lines and in stores; now i think i understand why she did it. i like to talk to people <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1940.txt\">on the bus</a>.</tt>',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13922,2361,'2002-02-06 12:20:50',0,'herr doktor','no one can tell the story as well as the person who wrote it, dude. i like what you set up and the way you ended it. this was fun to read and actually almost went by too fast. (nice supportive!)',2,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13923,2365,'2002-02-06 12:21:26',0,'herr doktor','i feel the same way. yet i never talk to people in public for some reason.',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13924,2364,'2002-02-06 12:22:11',0,'herr doktor','i dont get it... \\\"lipstick\\\"?',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13925,2365,'2002-02-06 13:37:51',0,'trouble','i\\\'m with the Doktor on this.  i feel the same way and in some cases i feel moreso compelled to speak but then more times than not i am/was too shy to do so.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13926,2364,'2002-02-06 13:43:31',0,'trouble','lipstick, damn.  it took me only a second to get it and then took me almost a minute to stop laughing.  <br><br>i thought that in my 20+ years that i had heard every description out there... lipstick is a first.  i wonder if that\\\'s just what he likened it to or if somebody told him that.  whatever the case that\\\'s original.',1,0,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13927,1248,'2002-02-06 15:38:44',0,'herr doktor','this is a case where its as fun to focus on the comments as it is on the writing. i love how, by just presenting his problems in a no-nonsense manner, it was assumed that he wanted <i>advice</i> about them, heh heh. i think its interesting how its our human tendencies to assume that, and offer copious responses starting with things like \\\"i don\\\'t know if this will help, but...\\\".<br><br>we all have our problems, yes, and probably more solutions to other people\\\'s problems than we care to count. but when push comes to shove, it takes a certain kind of person to post their deepest darkests amidst a world where unsolicited off-the-cuff \\\'advice\\\' mongers clearly reign, and then to be cool with all the comments.<br><br>no offense to any of the commenters- some had good thoughts, some didnt. just ranting on human tendencies, in general, here. hope youre doing good these days, trouble. \\\"BEST FRIENDLY\\\".',1,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13928,2364,'2002-02-06 15:42:41',0,'doktor','ahhh. duh. i get it now.',6,0,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13929,2363,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','i think fucktard originally came from egg, but i can\\\'t remember. maybe elly.\r\n\r\nthere\\\'s a pile of change on the thing with the tv on it downstairs here at xep\\\'s house. couple of keys in there too.',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(13930,2363,'2002-02-06 15:53:37',0,'jo','seriously, the posts where we just list cusswords we like a lot are some of my favorites. i\\\'m on a \\\"jerkoff\\\" kick. it pleases me.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13931,2363,'2002-09-05 18:42:54',18,'ix','ps, what am i, german now? moseberger?',0,0,0,0,'216.15.97.21','en'),(13932,2365,'2002-02-06 15:57:38',0,'jo','auntie, i used to be mortified when my mom did it, too. now i initiate all the conversations with strangers. and elly does it, too, more than most people i know, especially when <a href=\\\"http://elly.diaryland.com/020204_66.html\\\">she\\\'s in a great mood</a>. so you did a good thing.',0,0,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(13933,2365,'2002-02-06 17:29:33',0,'erb','i was thinking about the public transportation talking embargo yesterday.  the code of noninteraction.  my proposed solution (at least on the subway) is to have talking cars and nontalking cars.  that way the people who are talking or wish to strike up conversation wont have to endure the evil glares and frosty stares of the silent ones.  sometimes, like today, i am a silent one.  when i\\\'m working something out in my head.  some days, i love chatting people up like crazy.  ',2,0,0,0,'64.105.32.157','en'),(13934,2363,'2002-02-06 18:13:30',0,'yeagermeister','<p>F U C K&nbsp&nbsp&nbspT H E&nbsp&nbsp&nbspD E C K&nbsp&nbsp&nbspS O L D I E R S !</p> ',1,0,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(13935,2364,'2002-02-06 18:13:34',0,'kat','i didn\\\'t even think about that...thought it was a pretty common term. at least those of us who heard him say it had heard it before and there was this moment of tense silence with all of us looking at each other and doing our best not to die laughing.',4,0,0,0,'209.239.204.179','en'),(13936,2363,'2002-02-06 19:27:37',0,'bmg','florido was a fucktard, but munoz was a fuckrag, and there are many more. i spit at them...',1,0,0,0,'66.90.144.25','en'),(13937,2365,'2002-03-05 05:48:49',124,'gus','oh jeeze this reminds me. me and le need to write up a swinney about the militant vegan stranger we met in the zietgiest patio who stole our eazy cheez. Vegan no more after that, but we have some fine pictures. Im not sure who started talking to him first.',0,0,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(13938,2363,'2002-03-05 20:17:36',17,'austin to bmg','your roomate just busted my balls for calling you a fucktard.',3,0,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13939,2367,'2002-02-06 20:30:15',0,'doktor','nice. i like how this just sort of <i>rolls</i>. i spent what was a very nice part of my lunch hour reading it. cool.',1,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13940,2365,'2002-02-06 20:31:47',0,'doktor','a militant vegan who stole your cheese? geez. the idea of a \\\'militant vegan\\\' is pretty silly, if you ask me. by nature, isnt it something that tends to be non-oppressive?! \\\"constructive\\\"',3,0,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13941,2365,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','<br>\r\n<b>to jo</b> oh  my god, you\'re so right. i do. i do. that whole journal entry is about various conversations initiated with unwitting strangers. my poor kids will hate me. i\'m the worst for it when i\'m in austin too, because being in austin puts me in a great mood. that and all the BEER and COFFEE.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>to al</b> yeah we really do need to write the thing. i have the pictures.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<b>to doktor</b>no of allegience to any eating style or lack of eating style indicates more or less compassion or militance in it\'s followers. some people are just more passionate than others. that said, we use the term militant vegan as a joke, because i\'ve actually never met any particularly militant vegans. it\'s an exaggeration. the vegan in zeitgeist wasn\'t very militant at all, being that he ate our eazy cheese.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.3.82','en'),(13943,2367,'2002-02-06 21:13:45',0,'josta','This was lovely--every teenager should be so lucky. I\\\'m still a sophomore in high school, and I go with a close-knit group of friends to a small, out of the way pizza place every few weeks. We aren\\\'t as regular about it as you were, and we all go to different schools, but I hope years from now we\\\'ll remember our experiences as happily and eloquently as you remember yours.',2,0,0,0,'64.108.95.101','en'),(13942,2365,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','in retrospect, i\\\'m not sure \\\"militance\\\" is a word.',4,0,0,0,'209.86.3.82','en'),(13944,2363,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','over the last year or so i\\\'ve found myself shouting \\\"shitbiscuits!\\\" whenever things go wrong or i do something stupid or someone cuts me off on the freeway. i have no idea where this exclamation came from. nobody i know says it, and i\\\'ve never read or heard it anywhere else (as far as i can remember). it just popped out of my mouth one day, Tourettes-style, and i love it.<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>shitbiscuits. shitbiscuits. shitbiscuits. mmm.\r\n<br>\r\nif you ever see me post here, and you see that i\\\'ve made some lame mistake with my html again, you can be sure i howled \\\"shitbiscuits!!!\\\" when i realized my mistake.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13945,2365,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p><a href=\\\"http://www.planetvegan.org/\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">militant vegan</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>i have met the owners of that site.  while incredibly nice people, i think they almost called the firing squad when i announced that i\\\'m a complete carnivore.  my professed love for vegans did not help one bit.</p>',0,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13946,2365,'2002-03-15 00:17:55',129,'moebius','back when i first moved to san francisco i used to love cafe flore partly because i was new to the city and when i went there, people would chat <i>me</i> up, which seemed to come in handy because i\\\'m fairly shy when surrounded by strangers, i wanted to make new friends. then it gradually dawned on me that no, this wasn\\\'t just friendly chats i was having, these guys were <i>hitting on me</i>, and for awhile i was kind of weirded out and quit the flore for less meat-markety environs. latent homophobia, i suppose.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve since gotten over that sort of thing, but i still don\\\'t hang out at the flore that often. too noisy, crowded, and chatty, and all i want to do these days is read my comix in peace. it\\\'s good for brunch, though.',1,0,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13947,2365,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i\\\'d say that most of the more interesting drama of my high school career played out at the tables (and of course bathroom) of cafe flore. i hardly ever go there anymore but whenever i do, i laugh to myself about it all.',0,0,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13948,2365,'2002-03-08 04:52:47',65,'le to jeeff','that is a nice looking site.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nwhen i think \\\"militant\\\" i think agression/violence towards others who don\\\'t share your beliefs.  i still don\\\'t think think speaking rudely to someone who eats meat (tho it\\\'s shitty) counts as \\\"militant.\\\" how many times have i had a meat eater speak rudely to me about being vegetarian/vegan? that type of rudeness isn\\\'t limited to vegans, and i wouldn\\\'t call it \\\"militant\\\" unless i was feeling like being hyperbolic.  \r\n<br><br>\r\nthis is sort of tangential, but i think a lot of people think vegans are being self righteous, but i don\\\'t think most vegans are. i think that this perception comes from paranoia/defensiveness on the part of the non-vegans, and from an ingrained defensiveness or need to explain oneself that the vegan has developed due to being constantly picked at for being vegan.  i guess i\\\'m saying, no one is any worse than anyone else.\r\n<br><br>\r\n',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13949,2365,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','also. fanatic != militant',0,0,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13950,2363,'2002-02-07 00:17:30',0,'bmg to austin','my roommate? Wendy Woo? ',0,0,0,0,'66.90.144.25','en'),(13951,2365,'2002-02-07 00:43:15',0,'dahlia','<p>i think that the reason so many folks think all vegans are self-righteous is that the self-righteous ones are so vocal. the humble vegans, well, you probly don\\\'t even know they are vegan. </p>',0,0,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(13952,2363,'2002-02-07 00:58:38',0,'Enrique Florido','DO NOT DISRESPECT ME. I WILL EAT YOU FOR LUNCH.',2,0,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13953,2365,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','fanatics need to learn to relax and lead by example, rather than force.',0,0,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13954,2367,'2002-03-08 04:59:53',174,'Nosta Moll','sigh. this one reminded me of the old diner my friends would go to in HS -- \\\'The Parsonage\\\'. The old waitress (\\\"margie\\\") would give us free coffee and light our cigarettes. It was torn down a few years ago to make way for a Rite Aid. so it goes.\r\n',6,0,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13955,2365,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','<p>true comments, all of them.  like i said, i professed my love for vegans, but it was like an iron curtain had gone down between these people and myself.  luckily it passed with time.  ..but i think fanatic vs. militant is a question of semantics anyways, who cares.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i also have a lot of really nice vegan friends.  vegans seem to gravitate towards me because i am their polar opposite.  altho if it wasn\\\'t for my fucked up brain, i\\\'d probably be one too.</p>',1,0,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13956,2363,'2002-02-07 04:44:51',0,'wendy woo','whatever.  we all know you like to fuck squinty-eyed sailors up the ass.<br>\r\nfucktard.',1,0,0,0,'66.90.144.25','en'),(13957,2363,'2002-02-07 06:57:47',17,'austin','testing, you fucktards.',3,23,0,0,'64.174.155.121','en'),(13958,2363,'2002-02-07 07:09:39',65,'le','ooh, you mean, i could comment directly to the author?',6,54,0,0,'165.247.205.192','en'),(13959,2363,'2002-02-07 07:10:55',17,'austin','wendy woo, are you a squinty eyed sailor?',4,41,0,0,'64.174.155.121','en'),(13960,2363,'2002-02-07 07:48:00',59,'s5','noice',1,5,17,0,'210.86.56.73','en'),(13961,2365,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','some of my best friends are vegan!  NO REALLY!!',4,47,0,0,'210.86.56.73','en'),(13962,2367,'2002-02-07 16:50:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to eat at a diner called the parsonage in jersey. it was torn down for a rite aid, also. how odd, if were talking about the same place.</tt>',1,155,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(13963,2363,'2002-02-07 20:48:58',17,'austin','is this thing on?',6,17,59,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13964,2368,'2002-02-07 22:26:41',17,'testing','on?',4,3,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13965,2363,'2002-02-08 00:59:07',90,'leonard','it\'s on',6,7,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(13966,305,'2002-02-08 03:42:21',65,'le','i appear to be a dork.',4,22,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13967,2370,'2002-02-08 03:42:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i used to feel <a href=\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1958.txt\">this way</a> about the city. now, im learning that it also has a warm side, and a side that is best experienced with a like-minded companion.</tt>',1,165,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13968,2370,'2002-02-08 06:08:26',17,'austin','is this thing on?',6,17,0,0,'64.174.155.76','en'),(13969,2370,'2002-02-08 06:11:42',66,'Peter','blah',1,85,0,1,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13970,2370,'2002-02-08 06:12:02',66,'Peter','blah',1,85,0,1,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13971,2370,'2002-02-08 06:29:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oops. the comment posting thing seems to be screwy. apologies for the low-content repetition.</tt>',1,93,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13972,2370,'2002-02-08 06:39:39',17,'austin','that was me testing your error string.',4,38,66,1,'64.174.155.76','en'),(13973,2367,'2002-02-08 06:53:26',174,'Mad Moll','<p>That\'s the one. Corner of 27 and Parsonage Rd. I still live right by there. Basically every meaningful conversation I had as a teenager took place at a booth in the Parsonage. Stoned and staring over a plate of onion rings in the wee hours of the morning. Arguments about the stupidest things with free refills to fuel the fire. It was a home.</p>\r\n<p>Now it\'s a Rite Aid :(</p>',1,381,66,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13974,2367,'2002-02-08 11:07:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>small world. unfortunately, the infamous edison family restaurant on oaktree road has succumbed to a gas station, if memory serves correct. thankfully, the dutchess and windsor diners remain!</tt>',1,204,174,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13975,2372,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','peter you are nice!  big luff!',1,30,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13976,2372,'2002-02-08 14:00:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt>that place had a good <i>pad thai</i> to boot. yum!</tt>',1,51,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(13977,2367,'2002-02-08 14:17:53',67,'trouble','i take quiet joy in the thought that even though i\'m 500 miles away from Menka\'s Kitchen, the morning is probably progressing just as it always has within those four walls.  i don\'t fear demolition either because of its unattractive placement on warren road and the will of Menka herself to keep it open.  <br><br>i\'m glad this piece struck a nerve with you guys, places like this are magical.',1,393,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(13978,2371,'2002-02-08 17:41:49',70,'Fuzzy','There was a standing ovation for How\'s Your News? when I saw it at the\nFilm Festival this past year.\n\nAlso, a few weeks ago I saw 6 episodes of Degrassi Jr. High all on 16mm\nprints at UofT. That was like a party. With the oooh\'s and aaah \'s and all\nthat. Everyone was going crazy. It was so much fun. I know what you\'re\ntalking about. And this Jesus Christ Vampire Hunter sounds like a great\nfilm. Where was it playing?',1,505,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(13979,2372,'2002-02-08 18:29:23',82,'simon','dude, i forgot all about that place. my favorite thing was the tofu with chili and basil.',1,89,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13980,2372,'2002-03-05 01:46:04',17,'austin','is this on?',0,11,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13981,2369,'2002-02-08 19:35:10',82,'simon','this freakin rules',0,18,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13982,2372,'2002-02-08 20:07:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>austin: its on <i>sometimes</i>. there still seem to be some glitchez.</tt>',1,83,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13983,1556,'2002-02-08 20:28:17',130,'treetop','does this monster work?',0,23,0,0,'172.136.75.37','en'),(13984,1556,'2002-02-08 20:28:46',130,'treetop','yay',0,3,0,0,'172.136.75.37','en'),(13985,2372,'2002-02-08 20:31:28',130,'treetop','i was trying to add a picture that leonard took, but it wont let me. maybe swinney has html heartburn?',0,102,0,0,'172.136.75.37','en'),(13986,464,'2002-02-08 20:49:44',17,'austin','workie?',5,7,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(13987,2367,'2002-02-08 21:00:37',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Weirdness. What brings you down here, anyway? The Dutchess is like two blocks from my house. That\'s my town.</p>\r\n<p>And it\'s true, I think, that there\'s something \'special\' about these places -- I suppose that they have a sense of personality (even if it\'s a personality we sometimes don\'t care for) when so many other places don\'t.</p>\r\n<p>Incidentally, I think this is why places like Friday\'s, Chili\'s, etc, have this very contrived/manufactured sense of personality in how they decorate and advertise themselves. It makes a kind of sense when you consider that most of the patrons probably spend a good part of their day in a cubicle.</p>\r\n',1,648,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(13988,2365,'2002-02-08 21:22:14',148,'jeeff','exactly. ;)',0,11,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(13989,1453,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','oh, this is weird. i thought i was just fixing an old post--updating it so the nameclick brought you to my webdiarything, putting topic categories under it, etc. didn\'t realize it would be reposted. just for fun, cause i could. well.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsorry about that!',0,262,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(13990,2367,'2002-02-09 00:06:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt>there\'s something nice about scotch plains, edison, woodbridge. the malls, the parks, the odd sprawl between metropark and rahway. the dunkin doughnuts that sells drugs, the black 57\' chevy thats been for sale for like 5 years in a guy\'s drive, and of course <i>friendley\'s</i>. for about a year, my every friday night involved hitting the dutchess and then walking to the blockbuster across the street (or, if they were open, palmer video) and renting movies.</tt> ',1,474,174,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(13991,2371,'2002-02-09 00:10:59',176,'Scapegoat','<p>I\'m glad to hear about the standing ovation. The movie was playing at The Royal Theater. Degrassi memories take me back, I also used to watch a lot of \'My So-Called Life\', I haven\'t seen much of the new Degrassi stuff. Just before Jonovision went off the air they had a Degrassi reunion show.</p>  ',0,301,70,0,'64.229.160.250','en'),(13992,2373,'2002-02-09 00:25:34',65,'le','uhhh. for starters, this could use some paragraph breaks.',4,57,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(13993,165,'2002-02-09 00:36:04',82,'simon','dude, for some reason, after reading this i just started singing HOT WINGS! to the tune of eighteen by alice cooper.',0,116,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(13994,2373,'2002-02-09 00:45:05',124,'gus','also this:<br>\r\n<b>walking home one night from the which she attended</b><br>\r\nmakes no sense.',0,93,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(13995,2373,'2002-02-09 04:33:48',59,'s5','<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/020530902X/\">S5 wanted to link this</A>.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nbut this is austin here editing the link because it was broke before.  so i am going to go ahead and ammend this comment as s5 and i are good pals. <BR><BR>instead of that link there, use this link to <A HREF=\"http://sut1.sut.ac.th/strunk/\">Elements of Style by William Strunk, Jr.</A>. although that one that s5 gave you is a fine link, if you order that book from amazon it will cost you money and it is a WATERED DOWN version, DILUTED by E.B. White.  E.B. white was a good writer and a student of Strunk, but he was not William Strunk.  Strunk wrote it first and wrote it best.  the copyright has expired on the original:\r\n<BR>\r\n-- austin',1,50,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(13996,1453,'2002-02-09 04:36:49',67,'trouble','i agree with ess, this smoked.  i\'m looking forward to the next installment.',2,76,129,0,'68.47.160.8','en'),(13997,2369,'2002-02-09 04:48:48',67,'trouble','fuck.  this was like a small present.  something that you should have had in your hand when it was meant for you but somehow, someway got misplaced and only now do we get to open it.  this was a great gift for a friday night. *best friendly*',1,241,0,0,'68.47.160.8','en'),(13998,2370,'2002-02-09 05:49:49',122,'hool','swinney is playing tricks with my mind.',0,39,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(13999,2369,'2002-02-09 06:13:23',122,'hool','this was so funny i wanted to barf.\r\n\r\nbest reading material ever on mushrooms.  new hero.',1,90,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14000,2373,'2002-02-09 06:57:48',179,'Jack Cassidy','Public Triangles?\r\nAs in... 05 - Parhelic (Confeld 2001) minutes into which all are admitted?  Weird.  Everyone seems to enjoy that song.  I thing they enjoy the blops while I bob to the bloops.  Ah... B words.  Love the B-words.',2,229,0,0,'24.102.28.154','en'),(14001,2370,'2002-02-09 07:30:29',65,'le','that looks like a parking lot in sf, near critical path.',0,56,0,0,'165.247.220.46','en'),(14002,2372,'2002-02-09 08:47:44',124,'gus','I will eat anything with chilli and basil. well almost anything. I made some chilli and basil based food just tonight...',0,120,82,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(14003,2374,'2002-02-09 19:53:41',17,'austin','testing\r\n<BR>\r\n<A HREF=\"http://www.showerhead.org\">TESTING</A>',5,68,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14004,2374,'2002-02-09 20:00:52',17,'austin','<a href=\"http://showerhead.org\">TESTING</a>',5,49,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14005,2374,'2002-02-09 21:41:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>testing to see if i can make a comment</tt>',1,51,0,1,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14006,2374,'2002-02-09 22:03:53',17,'austin','<a href=\"fucktard.com\">testing</a>',0,40,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14007,2374,'2002-02-09 22:05:16',17,'austin','<a href=\"http://fucktards.com\">test</a>',4,45,66,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14008,2374,'2002-02-10 01:05:50',75,'tyler','pepto bismol for the soul: kind bud',1,35,0,0,'165.247.164.183','en'),(14009,2376,'2002-02-10 01:28:28',174,'Mad Moll','It takes courage (among other things, perhaps) to say, with pride, <i>\\\"Better them than me!\\\"</i>',5,102,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14010,2377,'2002-02-10 06:59:38',67,'trouble','this was a great piece.  i\\\'m glad you have so many good memories.  i\\\'ve found myself waxing nostalgic all week about my grandparents and this complimented some of those thoughts beautifully.  i dig tough old people, especially tough old grandfathers.  ',1,258,0,0,'68.47.160.8','en'),(14011,2369,'2002-02-10 17:41:24',17,'austin','saturday morning i woke up around 8 and walked out the back of my house, through blue balls park and past the car lot, to downtown soquel.  it is about a mile.  i had to wait around for a few minutes because karate starts at 9 on saturdays.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nafter i had changed into my karate pajamas and did stretching we went into exercises and my partner was this guy i have never seen there.  i forget his name.  but he had a wooden leg on that had a styled foot.  this food had clearly been around.  i bet he walks barefoot on the board walk in santa cruz with it in the summer time because it was all beat to hell.    it had a shine though on the pinky toe and in patches around the foot.  i think he probably made some effort to keep it looking good, but his effort had slacked or whaned, or the foot was just way too worn out to make pretty.<BR><BR>  \r\n\r\nas we went through our exercises i found that i could ignore the foot for the most part.  i did not want to draw attention to it.  im sure this guy gets more, \\\'what happened to your foot? shark getcha?!\\\' than i get, \\\'austin are you from austin?\\\'<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nhowever, every time he leaned too far to one side when throwing a punch, i saw the wooden leg being used to leverage him in the back.  he could not extend a punch without throwing that leg back and off to the side like an anchor.  when i saw that i could not help but snicker.<BR><BR>\r\n\r\npunch...hobble...punch....hobble...punch.  so on and so forth.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\nat one point we switched to kicking exercises and i lost control.  \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nHey, watch it with that thing!  \r\n<BR><BR>I didnt know we were exercising with weapons!  \r\n<BR><BR>This isn\\\'t baseball!  \r\n<BR><BR>Oh your mad now, you going to \\\'break a foot of in my ass\\\'?\\\"\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nit was a bad scene.  i couldnt concentrate and kept bursting into laughter.  the man was trying to be nice, but it was getting to him.  the class was annoyed with my constant laughter and the meanness of making fun of a crouching tiger.  the last thing i remember i could see the shiney pinky toe flashing before my eyes.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni woke up a short time later and everyone was cleared out.  i got my stuff together, changed out of my karate pajamas and went home.\r\n\r\n',3,2254,0,0,'64.168.23.236','en'),(14012,2378,'2002-02-10 21:14:06',124,'gus','I would like to take this opportunity to thank my father for the free smoke. Thanks for quitting after I moved out of the house. Thanks for smoking while I sat on your lap. Thanks for the asthma. ',0,196,0,0,'216.175.109.251','en'),(14013,2377,'2002-02-10 22:18:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>rock steady</i>, paul g. i really enjoyed reading this, both the first time and the second time. its <i>nice</i> to read.</tt> ',1,138,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14014,2377,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','aw!  this is really kickass.  i like your grandpa!',2,50,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14015,2379,'2002-02-10 23:53:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im not sure if this was the goal, but this piece made me crack up; a good sunday-evening read. thanks...</tt>',1,117,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14016,2379,'2002-02-11 05:00:50',118,'drew','the goal is A TRIP TO CANCUN!  WOO-HOO!',0,39,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(14017,2377,'2002-02-11 06:08:56',175,'Paul G','Thank you all very much for your kind words, especially you: Trouble â€“ I have enjoyed your writing, but I have never left any sort of compliment for you â€“ shame on me.\r\n\r\n<p>Comments have been the focus of much attention of late.  I study political philosophy.  It has been tempting for me to look at Swinney as something like a little â€œstateâ€ in which a critical decision making point had been reached: the competing values of order and freedom needed to be weighed against each other and a new balance struck.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>The absolute freedom to say whatever one would, anonymously, seemed to have become a source of irritation.  The â€œrulersâ€ of Swinney therefore established a new protocol that forced its â€œcitizensâ€ to relinquish their anonymity.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>Anonymity has its value: in most republican forms of government the citizens expect to be able to make their preferences known, anonymously, at the ballot box.  Sometimes, people know that their views are unpopular and would hesitate to express them if they knew that they would be subject to retaliation from the greater community.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So too at Swinney the liberty to post anonymously may have afforded certain people the opportunity to say things that they believed but would not have said in their own name for fear of reprisal.  And, again, some of the anonymous posts were simply hysterical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, of course, another party might perceive what strikes one person as humorous as hurtful.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Last night I used the â€œeditâ€ option MANY times to try to polish a post that I had made.  It occurred to me, as I was engaged in that activity, that someone had taken the time to write the code for that option, and someone was hosting the page that I was editing, and someone was involved in maintaining the site that all of this was taking place on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe, if Swinney is like a little â€œstateâ€ now, it was also like the Garden of Eden for a time too.  Even as Adam and Eve were said to have been forbidden only one thing, so we too had been given only one commandment: â€œDonâ€™t be a prick.â€  Well, we both seem to have lost what we once had.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Balancing the right to free speech with the need to preserve some minimal order within a community, any community, is a very difficult task.  The people who have put Swinney together have done a great thing.  People, like myself, who use the site ought to remember all the love and attention that has gone into the creation of this thing.  So, to all of you who have made this thing possible: Thank you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I only hope that Swinney will continue to tolerate the expression of multiple points of view and honest attempts at humor that have made this such a wonderful place.  It is a real forum â€“ and for anyone interested in philosophy, or democracy, or free speech, that is a very precious thing.</p> \r\n\r\n  \r\n\r\n\r\n',0,2859,0,0,'199.182.173.140','en'),(14018,2377,'2002-02-11 06:47:21',65,'le','1. nice post. nice comment. the comment could have been it\\\'s own post, as it will no doubt elicit many responses.\r\n<br><br>\r\n2. i think thet \\\"state\\\" analogy is interesting, but flawed for the reason that swinney.org is a private enterprise, not a governmental system, and people don\\\'t have to \\\"live\\\" here. know what i mean? i\\\'m not sure swinney.org should be held to the same sorts of standards as a gov\\\'t is, and i also don\\\'t think swinney.org neccessarily needs to be a bastion of free speech. there are places for free speech: it\\\'s called, make your own website. if there\\\'s something anonymous commenters REALLY NEED TO SAY about a swinney.org post, they\\\'re welcome to do so in other forums, such as email and various other websites. no one is being quelled, the bar is merely being raised.\r\n<br><br>\r\n<tt>:D</tt>\r\n<br><br>',5,862,0,0,'165.247.205.64','en'),(14019,2377,'2002-02-11 12:30:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i learned an important lesson once: it went something like: <i>\\\"think before you speak. if, after contemplating what you want to say, it sounds logical and worth saying, say it. if not, dont\\\"</i>. not that that applies on all levels here, but i think that urging people to post under their (or at least a consistent) name makes folks <i>think</i> a little harder about what they\\\'re going to say, and how it reflects on their own image/persona; a good lesson. on a personal level, i was getting rather tired of seeing a beautiful post (that an author had spent alot of thought and emotion writing) hit with strings of spurious comments from people who obviously didnt think much about what they were saying, or didnt think it important enough to take credit (or just had no respect at all for the author or their time to just be a little more civil).<br><br>\r\nas for taking credit for what\\\'s said here: if people want to post spiteful or tangential commments, they still can... just under their own name. whats so bad about that? if its something that needs to be said, say it. its not like anyone here is going to personally hate you for it... especially since not all of us on here even <i>know</i> each other. ha. but for real. i make it a chef goal in my life to be as humanly <i>nice</i> to people as possible, yet i utter the occasional <i>\\\"fuck you!\\\"</i>. it happens. speak your piece. have the guts to use your name! (er, when i say \\\"you\\\" here, im just referring to the general public).<br><br>\r\nas for raising the bar, <i>yes</i>! thats always a good goal. now, as the content level is rising, us editors have an incentive to take even a closer look at submissions, proofreading them and editing their formats for readability ;)<br><br>\r\nbest friendly, <i>ya heard!</i></tt>',1,1814,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14020,2376,'2002-02-11 12:33:37',66,'Peter','<br><tt>we cant live on, live on without you.<br>we cant live on, live on without you.</tt>',1,91,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14021,2379,'2002-02-11 15:01:56',151,'stella','this magazine phemonenom has been around forever. I remember when I was in junior college, kids agressively selling magazines would appear in droves on our campus. I would find myself cornered for up to half hour at a time trying to get one of the maggots to stop trying to talk me into buying a subscription so that they could win a trip to Hawaii. I was such a sucker. I actually bought one once. One other time, the kid trying to sell to me ended up trying to seduce me. He got me back to his motel room (they shipped these kids into town) under the guise of playing video games. I\\\'ve often wondered what company is behind the whole thing. ',0,646,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14022,2379,'2002-02-11 16:02:58',66,'Peter','<br><tt>alas, this post got me thinkinking... and my comment ended up as a <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2383.txt\\\">post</a>, heh.</tt>',1,164,118,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14023,2383,'2002-02-11 16:31:20',67,'trouble','go to the hospital and grab leaflets on things like \\\'testicular cancer\\\', \\\'colitus\\\' and \\\'living with an amputee\\\'.  then when you see them approaching whip some of them out, act like you\\\'re reading them.  when asked <b>\\\'do <i>you</i> like comedy?\\\'</b> look up in horror and make the leaflet visable.  then reply with a quiet voice, <b>\\\'i <i>used</i> to\\\'</b>. ;)<br><br>good piece peter!',1,417,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14024,1607,'2002-02-11 16:45:47',67,'trouble','how did i miss this the first time around?  quite an interesting read, the comments warrant almost another article entirely.',1,124,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14025,2379,'2002-02-11 16:59:41',118,'drew','i can\\\'t believe they \\\"ship them into town\\\" just to sell magazines.  what an incredible waste of time and money and transportation.  i think the one girl said she was from new york city; i hope she was lying because that\\\'s way too far to come to push entertainment weekly on me.',0,289,151,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(14026,2383,'2002-02-11 17:05:22',118,'drew','<p>that\\\'s demented.  they must be pretty awful comedians if you can shut them up with your \\\"German\\\" line; they ought to be able to banter with you at least a little.  still, if they were good comedians they probably wouldn\\\'t have to hawk themselves on the street.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'ve always had really mixed feelings about the whole \\\"get in the audience\\\'s face\\\" philosophy.  as a performer, i hate doing it, and as an audience member, i hate receiving it.  i don\\\'t pay good money to be harrassed; just entertain me already, goddamn it!</p>\r\n<p>finally i would just like to add that the loss of any greasy spoon, and particularly a 24-hour one that DELIVERS!!! is a tragedy.  (though probably not in the classical sense, so i suppose it\\\'s more of \\\"a shitty thing.\\\")</p>',0,797,0,0,'65.12.14.45','en'),(14027,2379,'2002-02-11 17:42:42',151,'stella','I\\\'m in NY too (not NYC). This happened to me in the SF Bay Area. The guy with the motel room told me that he was from San Diego, about 8 hours south. I\\\'m sure that this company is a national deal. I wold *hope* that it is, that\\\'s a long way to ship some jerk to sell magazines. ',0,287,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14028,2383,'2002-02-11 17:52:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes, theyre pretty lousy comedians to be performing on weeknights on the upper west side. a fun tidbit i forgot to mention: when i was in my intermediate state (that is, the time when i would actually say <i>\\\"listen, you ask me every freaking day. please. no. more.\\\"</i>) this girl replied \\\"well, you havent been asked by <i>me</i>\\\" and gave a seductive wink and air-kiss... I managed to steer clear before she could flash her amateur-comedian cleavage.<br><br> yes, \\\"tragic\\\" is the word for the loss of Grandma\\\'s. as well as your typical greasy-diner fare, they delivered smokes and 6-packs... <i>24/7</i>, the lo-tech predecessor to kozmo.com, i suppose. sigh. <i>nacho mama\\\'s</i>, another good and vegan-friendly neighborhood institution is now a starbucks. ouch.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,807,118,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14029,2383,'2002-02-11 18:08:50',67,'trouble','i would use \\\'tragedy\\\' in this case.  but then i get unusally attracted to such places and often have a flare for the dramatic.',1,132,118,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14030,2384,'2002-02-11 18:28:52',151,'stella','<P>heh. my brother is a newsman. On hot days, newsmen wear suits and ties up top, but shorts and sneakers, or sandals, on the bottom. I think this is hilarious. Now I can\\\'t watch the news without wondering what the newsman looks like when he stands up, a strange kind of centaur-type half businessman, half loafer. </P><P>\r\nNewswomen don\\\'t do it, from what I gather. Odd. </P>',0,382,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14031,2383,'2002-02-11 18:51:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Two Words:<br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>waffle\r\n<LI>house\r\n</UL><br>can i get an <i>amen</i>?!</tt>',1,113,67,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14032,2384,'2002-02-11 19:12:27',67,'trouble','\\\"Where do newsman live?\\\"<br><br>many years ago i used to work in a video store.  it was a small video store located in birmingham mighigan.  birmingham is to metro detroit what beverly hills is to LA.  many of metro detroit\\\'s sports stars and local tv celebrities live and play in the city limits.  many of them rented videos from our store as well.  it was one of the few video stores in the area (blockbuster had not yet become the goliath it is now) and we were the only store in birmingham at the time that rented porno\\\'s.  channel 4 was/is the undisputed king in local news.  the only two newspeople in metro detroit making over a million a year are both employed by channel 4.  eli zaret was/is a prominant sportscaster at that station.  very popular guy with a very popular sports show.  early on in the 90\\\'s both he and his wife were customers of our store often renting 8,9,10 movies at a time.  many of these movies were porno\\\'s.  not porno\\\'s of the usual variety but wild, out-there porno\\\'s of the very select variety.  porno movies have a higher late fee if returned late and another fee if the tape wasn\\\'t rewinded.  his weekly trip to our store often cost him in upwards of 60$, my boss treated he and another anchorman named bill bonds like royalty despite their habits of tying up our most popular tapes for weeks on end.  bill bonds is/was an alcoholic, fond of coming in minutes before we closed at 12 drunk and wasting time \\\'browsing\\\' and trying to talk his way out of late fees.  employees of the store had to call customers on occasion when the tapes became really, really late.  messages left on answering machines like... \\\'uh, hi, this is video---, our records show you have \\\'buttslammers 21\\\' and its 14 days late.  would you please return the tape as soon as possible?  thank you\\\'<br><br>so, i guess from my little slice of newsman life i would conclude that newsmen live in upscale hoods 20 or so minutes outside of the city they are employed in, are fond of renting porno\\\'s and getting freaky with their wives, are unable to locate the \\\'rewind\\\' button on the remote when finished with the tape, are unable to tell the date when <i>those</i> tapes are due, at times are drunken and beligerent about owing a few bucks despite making 6 figure incomes, bold enough in their habits not to be fazed when they find recorded messages indicating late rentals of movies that might be illegal pending trial if found in their possession in some places in america and lastly i have found that newsmen in general have poor taste in film.  <br><b><i>Eli Zaret, you still owe me money!</b></i>*best friendly*',1,2672,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14033,2383,'2002-02-11 19:17:40',67,'trouble','with the entire congregation of the 1st baptist church of knoxville behind me i say <i>aaaaaaaaa-men</i>.',1,105,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14034,2383,'2002-02-11 19:18:45',175,'Paul G','And all God\\\'s children said: \\\"Amen.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>p.s. If you have a story about handling snakes in Appalachia this would be the time to tell it.  Or, is that just a Kentucky thing?  Oh, I saw the snakes, but they told me that I was, \\\"kinda Jewish lookin.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>p.p.s. Isn\\\'t that a chapter in \\\"Moby Dick\\\" - \\\"The tale of the Wa-Ho\\\"?</p>',0,363,0,0,'207.221.71.233','en'),(14035,2383,'2002-02-11 19:23:58',175,'Paul G','Yes: Chapter 54, \\\"The Wa-Ho\\\'s Story.\\\"',0,46,0,0,'207.221.71.233','en'),(14036,2384,'2002-02-11 19:27:30',67,'trouble','quinn, forgot to mention in all of that that i dug the piece.  :)',1,65,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14037,2383,'2002-02-11 19:47:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>WaHo!!<br><br>i often get mistaken for jewish, for some reason. the <a href=\"http://www.jewsforjesus.org/\"><i>jews for jesus</i></a> are always approaching me on the train, asking if im jewish. one girl in college dumped me because she thought i was jewish... and i wasnt. oh well!</tt>',1,299,175,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14038,2383,'2002-02-11 20:14:36',175,'Paul G','Well, reference this comment and your original post, the problem seems to be unwanted advances by strangers - be they \\\"comic solicitors\\\" or \\\"religious proselytizers\\\" - something that every denizen of a major metropolitan area can sympathize with, I\\\'m sure.\r\n\r\n<p>What you need, Peter, is something that will broadcast your affiliation for you.  You need colors - Swinney colors.  And when the irritating urban stranger ignores your colors, you need a Swinney sign to flash.  And, if they still don\\\'t take the hint, you need some scripted comment like: \\\"Yo!  Step your bitch-ass back, fucktard!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Or, you could just take the stuff that they hand out - either way.</p>',0,695,0,0,'207.221.71.233','en'),(14039,2383,'2002-02-11 21:01:14',129,'moebius','<br>when i first moved to this city (sf) it seemed like i was always running into comics, not the other way around. and for some reason it was always <i>female</i> comics. who are actually fun friends to have when it comes right down to it. one of them turned out to be the only shining beacon of light at the ugly soulgrinding engineering corporation that employed me during my initial years here. she was a bad ass comedygrrl, won awards for her work & otherwise kicked ass. another comic answered my newspaper personal ad & our second date was me seeing her act at the (now long-gone) Other Cafe.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nso i had ultimate respect for all the comics trying to make a go of it here--it\\\'s really hard work, making a living out of making people laugh. but when the comedy scene died and the struggling comics started showing up at open mic poetry readings, reading \\\"poems\\\" that were really their routines in verse, i got fed up. that was <i>my</i> scene, for awhile anyway, and they kinda killed it off, too. grr.',1,1025,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14040,2383,'2002-02-11 22:41:11',174,'Mad Moll','<p>We used to have people hired by comedy clubs walk around Newark asking people to sign up for this discount thing. Usually they hired hot girls or hot guys. One spring, someone would ask me almost every day if I wanted to join while I was eating lunch.</p>\r\n<p><u>Solutions:</u></p>\r\n<p>1. \\\"The Face\\\" -- I have an unhappy-looking face in general, but when I really try, I can make it very obvious that I hate you and your mom and your kitten and the toys it plays with. Once -- and nobody else was around, he had nothing else to do -- this comedy guy was about to walk up to me, saw my face, and then just turned around. (Maybe I am just really ugly?)</p>\r\n<p>2. \\\"The Klingon\\\" -- when The Face fails when confronted by the persistent or inperceptive, I\\\'d cop The Klingon. When asked: <i>\\\"Do you like comedy??????!\\\"</i>, I would look at them for a minute and then say, very loudly, <b>\\\"I do <i>not</i> laugh.\\\"</b> They all took it well.</p>\r\n',6,970,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14041,2383,'2002-02-11 22:59:36',124,'gus','lately more than one person has told me I should go into stand up comedy. If I ever do, I promise never to accost you on the street. unless Im being paid a very large sum of money to do so.',0,189,0,0,'64.160.55.70','en'),(14042,2383,'2002-02-11 23:19:23',59,'s5','the same thing is happening in melbourne. there is a world-wide plague of street comics affecting our nations. kill them all immediately.',6,137,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14043,2376,'2002-02-11 23:25:16',59,'s5','the monkeys with the faces torment me in my sleep.',4,50,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14044,2376,'2002-02-12 00:06:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the singing bulldog with the face. those are his lines.</tt>',1,52,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14045,2380,'2002-02-12 01:39:29',83,'adamajah','<p>and who is dis lucky laday?</p>',0,34,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14046,2383,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','no, gus. no stand-up comedy. i feel i must take a stand. sorry.',1,63,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14047,2382,'2002-02-12 04:26:58',183,'lin','<p>but what is next.. it\\\'s -20 C in those cornfields, and there is no part time job left to attend in between contemplating and sleeping amongst nature.<br>\r\nWhat could come of dreaming in reverse? Only more thoughts of the past, when you seem to struggle so strongly to free yourself from this. There are many futures to be had.</p>',0,336,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(14048,2085,'2002-02-12 06:52:51',184,'doug','<p> i think you are wrong.  while it is easier to walk away, i think it is better to know than always wonder.  even if the answer you get is not the one you want, life goes on.  but i\\\'m one to talk.</p>',0,205,0,0,'24.42.76.242','en'),(14049,2388,'2002-02-12 13:39:14',155,'blue stocking','you are *good*',0,14,0,0,'216.191.251.62','en'),(14050,2387,'2002-02-12 14:18:01',176,'Scapegoat','I really liked this. Logic is a chicken shit a lot of the time I find.',0,70,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14051,2388,'2002-02-12 14:30:00',176,'Scapegoat','I can remember having a few encounters with unique people at the Oak on Bank. It seems to be a good spot for that. I\\\'ll be thinking about you and lucy all day now.',0,166,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14052,1638,'2002-02-12 14:55:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>Allen Ginsburg is my Fuckin Dentist</i></tt>',1,55,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14053,2383,'2002-02-12 16:36:36',74,'jo','thank you for the jews for jesus website. it is <b>MAGICAL</b>.',1,63,66,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14054,2383,'2002-02-12 16:48:08',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i tend to cut the <i>jews for jesus</i> more slack than i do the rest of the usual assortment of subway soap-boxers and shakedown artists, preachers and panhandlers. they have an amazingly low-key vibe, are very subtle, seem to be well educated, and <i>they respect the words \\\"no thanks\\\"</i>.  they are also respectfully dedicated to their cause without being eccentric, at least as far as i can tell. its very zen, compared to the usual ilk.</tt>',1,461,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14055,2388,'2002-02-12 17:08:37',120,'joyrider','<p>i couldn\\\'t help but think of the old 19th century photos of  american indian chiefs - staring out from aged sepiatone daguerreotypes with sad old eyes.  those photos have a finality, a fatalism to them that can be overwhelming.  every now and then, though, you find one where the chief\\\'s mouth is turned up in the slightest smile. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>your story and pictures got me thinking of those photos.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i really hope to see you post again. soon.</p>',0,465,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(14056,2388,'2002-02-12 17:23:40',122,'hool','thanks for the support.  i couldn\\\'t sleep for most of the night because i was too wired from the day\\\'s events.\r\n\r\nbtw, if you want to see the full series of the blanket/body pics, <a href=\\\"http://inconduit.com/image/blanket_body/images.html\\\">you can find them here</a>',0,280,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14057,2387,'2002-02-12 17:41:32',123,'dcc','logic be damned, but if the irrationality of the world is beating the snot out of you maybe it\\\'s time to whup some ass back.',2,127,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14058,2387,'2002-02-12 17:54:23',184,'doug','<p>thanks for the compliment.  i find that i rely heavily on logic, and when it doesn\\\'t work the way it normally does i seem to fuck myself over.  live and learn i guess.</p>',0,177,176,0,'24.42.76.242','en'),(14059,2388,'2002-02-12 18:03:39',123,'dcc','\r\n<p>sometimes I wish I was a friendlier person. i very rarely find myself in \r\nsituations like <a href=\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2388.txt\">this</a>. i \r\nsuppose the years of big city life have warped my sense of people. i almost never talk to \r\npeople on the street anymore.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>the only people that i have had a good chat with are those that moved \r\nhere from Out East. often new brunswick or nova scotia. almost always down \r\non thier luck. they\'re the only ones that have been kind enough not to \r\ninvade my space without permission. such genuine people, no pretenses apparent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>regardless i do often feel compelled to talk to more people i walk past, \r\nto see what stories they can tell. maybe i should stop listening to my \r\nhead once in awhile.</p>\r\n',2,141,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14060,2379,'2002-02-12 18:12:20',17,'austin','i subscribed my mormon mother to one of the liberal magazines that one of those maggots was selling once.  it is the IRL version of junk mail, those people.  the single mother from some inner city shitland took my money but my mom in suburban plano texas never got the magazine.',4,278,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14061,41,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i would totally go on a second date at short notice if i liked the miss or fella and if i wasn\\\'t doing anything else more exciting!</p>',3,141,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14062,41,'2002-02-12 19:49:21',17,'austin','<P>yes well, it didnt go smoothly and i think i was intimidated by her years.  she was like 10 years older than me i think.  it was not something that i cought on to immediately.  she said very openly on the first date that she wanted a man who could pay the bills and let her hang out at home with the kids.<BR><BR>\r\nat the time i was in no position to shell out money to have the gold touched up on her gucci sunglasses.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nas a side note, this was not the first 10 year older dating situation with a Korean woman that i had been in.  i dated a female korean sculptor when i was staying with the parents in plano texas my sophmore year in college.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nshe too was very serious about settling down.\r\n</P>',3,720,59,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14063,77,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','this is so cool, austin :D',1,26,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14064,2389,'2002-02-12 20:57:23',175,'Paul G','I can\\\'t wear any type of watch or ring or band, or any sort of metal or crystal on my body.  I used to joke about those types of things interfering with my \\\"magical powers\\\" - but it\\\'s really no joke.\r\n\r\n<p>When we were being taught about restraint and submission techniques at the jail, the topic of spontaneous and fatal reactions to non-lethal forms of force arose.  The instructor was quite convinced that piercings interupted the \\\"flow of the body\\\'s energy\\\" and that the application of pressure to certain areas, in conjunction with the piercings, was sufficient to cause death.  No one would argue that there is electro-chemical energy in the body - whether it is \\\"ki\\\" is another matter.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is a tremendously liberating moment that follows the placement of momentos in the garbage.  Maybe it\\\'s not good to enjoy the process of removing the relics of the past, but it isn\\\'t good either, maybe, to become a custodian of the past to the degree that the head becomes most comfortable when it is looking down and back at familiar things.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I still keep a box of things from my ex.  But, that box is well sealed and removed from the track of my daily routine.  And, unlike Pandora, I don\\\'t go peeking.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some things are too painful to let go of I guess - and so they get hidden.  But it does seem like there will come a time when letting go of it all will be like the new beginning of Spring, and not the dying of the past like Autumn</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I can\\\'t wear watches.</p>',0,1537,0,0,'67.24.11.43','en'),(14065,2391,'2002-02-12 22:21:26',75,'tyler','\\\"at the very least, love doesn\\\'t buy bread.\\\"-<br>\r\nWhen love beckons to you, follow him, though his ways are hard and steep. And when his wings enfold you yield to him, though the sword hidden among his pinions may wound you. And when he speaks to you believe in him, though his voice may shatter your dreams as the north wind lays waste the garden.-Khalil Gibran',1,372,0,0,'165.247.164.74','en'),(14066,2389,'2002-02-12 22:25:03',177,'pony','dearest peter....you DONT HAVE TO exercise her. nope. in fact, it\\\'s better if you don\\\'t. revel in the good memories. and do your best to grow and learn from the not so good ones. embrace the time that you had. it happened. it was special. it meant something. and it will always hold energy. don\\\'t think of it as having to close old doors....it\\\'s really only about opening new ones. what this really is, is about you. not her.',1,437,0,0,'209.239.201.108','en'),(14067,2391,'2002-02-12 22:46:46',122,'hool','<p>i think many westerners, when they hear of \\\'arranged\\\' marriages, they furrow their brows and consider it a custom of foreign, less developed societies.  many passionate rants on the right to choose, the value of true love, and so on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i think that many westerners live in arranged marriages already.  it\\\'s just that they did the work of arranging a veil they pretend to be love over an ugly yet secure couch, instead of having their parents do it for them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>lotus: if things turn out badly with the arranged marriage, find me, and i\\\'ll donate what i can to buy you a one way ticket to ireland, on the condition that you never stop writing.</p>',0,676,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14068,2389,'2002-02-12 22:47:54',82,'dictionary.com','exercise or exorcise?',0,21,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14069,2389,'2002-02-12 22:52:49',177,'pony','like i really fucking care how i spell when i write swinney comment',0,67,0,0,'209.239.201.108','en'),(14070,2391,'2002-02-12 23:35:00',174,'Mad Moll','<p>Hmm. This post was really interesting.</p>\r\n<p>I think a lot of people tend to view love in a very absolute and kind of romanticized way. Absolutes protect us from the ambiguities and uncertainties inherent in all human activity. We need to feel <i>so safe</i> with someone we love, we tend to wash over the possibility of things not always being what they seem with these idealized, souped-up versions of our true feelings. In the attempt to continually convince ourselves of the supremecy of true love, we end up with nothing more than a reflection of our own needs. So little good seems to come from self-proclaimed selflessness.</p>\r\n<p>Not to be overly cynical. I\\\'m sure it\\\'s out there for some people, although I doubt in proportion to the number who claim to have found it. (Think of that friend, hypothetical or otherwise, who\\\'s already found \\\'The One\\\' several times over.)</p>\r\n',6,905,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14071,2389,'2002-02-12 23:40:05',174,'Mad Moll','I think it is silly when people take perfectly intuitive spiritual things and cloak them in pseudoscience for no reason.',6,120,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14072,2388,'2002-02-13 00:52:51',148,'jeeff','toronto can be a cold cold city sometimes.  i\\\'ve heard comparisons to the streets of london england.  i think its one of most noticeable ways our culture is different than in the states.  as soon as i cross the border, it seems like everyone\\\'s talking to each other.  go figure.',0,284,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14073,2389,'2002-02-13 01:23:07',82,'dictionary','no need to get testy, my dear, i was just having a good-natured laugh over a typo. i wasn\\\'t trying to be a dickwad.',0,118,177,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14074,2391,'2002-02-13 01:52:55',124,'gus','i really liked this. And I think it is possible that you can fall in love with someone in an arranged marriage. why not? it may take 10 years, but you just never know. ',0,168,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14075,2389,'2002-02-13 02:20:42',124,'gus','the difference in spellings can cause all sorts of problems. I used to not give a shit about spelling, till I realized it made me look like I was fucking stupid. so I pay more attention.\r\n<p>\r\nthrowing away mementos, exorcizing. I recently took out some of my heaviest mementos and burned them up. Am I liberated? maybe not. but I no longer have to look at them and think \\\"why?\\\"',0,384,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14076,2391,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>i know true love exists, but i also believe that love which develops over time is just as valid.  some people will never find their soulmate,  but they might find comfort and tenderness in someone else.</p>\r\n<p>it might be possible to fall in love with anyone, given the will and the right circumstances.</p>\r\n<p>lately i have been thinking about idea of a soulmate as a wandering spirit.  it presents itself within certain people, but if the people involved don\\\'t recognise or keep feeding the spirit, it will move on.</p>\r\n<p>maybe in an arranged marriage situation, the soulmate spirit will show up in each spouse, after a while.</p>',3,642,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14077,2387,'2002-02-13 04:37:09',59,'s5','logic is always silly because it\\\'s just a pile of stuff built on top of other things that were arbitrary to begin with.\r\n\r\nflip more coins.',1,142,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14078,2392,'2002-02-13 05:24:09',122,'hool','<p>classification is how we learn, how we\\\'ve always learned.  to divide things up so that we can examine the relationships between them.  our ability to do that is what makes us human, so it will always be this way.\r\nit\\\'s better to ask how we can improve our application of categorization.</p>',0,299,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14079,2392,'2002-02-13 05:46:06',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nno i think thats wrong. call me the idealist, but perhaps if we lived life in a hyper specific nature things would be different.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nas i said, there is room for classification. we can classify the kinds of music we listen to...the type of car we buy. im not saying\r\n\\\'classification\\\' as an idea is bad. what im saying is that we let this \\\'model\\\' become the benchmark on how we develop and maintain every way of life.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nclassification can so easily turn into hierarchy. class. segregation. diference of opinion.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nperhaps i think the impossible of our people. perhaps i trust too much in how i have been raised. in the road that i took in life. \r\n</p>  ',3,697,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14080,2392,'2002-02-13 05:52:53',174,'Mad Moll','<p>I don\\\'t really get what you mean.</p>\r\n<p>If someone told me they thought cats were more \\\"spiritual\\\" than dogs, I would just assume they are a cat person and leave it at that.</p>\r\n<p>If we say that everything is \\\"equally spiritual\\\", I think it kind washes out the entire meaning of what spirituality is. For me it means (or at least requires) a personal connection to something that supercedes any other kind of understanding.</p>\r\n<p>Perhaps in your attempt to avoid classification, you\\\'re inadvertently creating just another another system to impose upon and sequester experience. By putting equality on a pedestal, you rule out the possibility that people might tend to feel connected to one set of things over another, or you might rule out the possibility that some people are \\\'better\\\' than others in some spiritual sense. Or, even worse, you might find yourself disqualifying other people\\\'s experiences despite yourself because it doesn\\\'t fit with your idea of equality.</p>\r\n<p>Danger danger, everywhere.</p>',6,1049,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14081,2392,'2002-02-13 05:57:39',122,'hool','<p>\\\"what im saying is that we let this \\\'model\\\' become the benchmark on how we develop and maintain every way of life. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>classification can so easily turn into hierarchy. class. segregation. diference of opinion. \\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and i said: </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"it\\\'s better to ask how we can improve our application of categorization.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>which is exactly what you\\\'re talking about in your above statements</p>, but then you started out with: </p>\r\n\r\n<p>\\\"no i think thats wrong.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\nso i don\\\'t understand what you\\\'re saying.\r\n',0,568,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14082,2387,'2002-02-13 06:31:18',70,'Fuzzy','Logic doesn\\\'t work unless you are coming from a solid base.  If you begin in error, logic can\\\'t help you.',3,111,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(14083,2389,'2002-02-13 06:42:22',175,'Paul G','Yes dear reader, it is true: I am a pseudo-scientific ass-monkey, and I can\\\'t spell.  Now, if you\\\'ll excuse me, I need to parade around the house in my rainbow-colored diaper and sing \\\"Be-Bop-A-Lula\\\" by Gene Vincent.\r\n\r\n<p>p.s. I refuse to learn how to spell until I get an extra banana.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>p.p.s. I think that Peter used the word \\\"exercise\\\" at least twice in his article and so the Pony used it too - having just seen it.  I, also, used the word \\\"moment\\\" prior to typing \\\"momento\\\".  Is that a sufficiently \\\"pseudo-scientific\\\" explanation?  Oh well: \\\"She\\\'s my baby...\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>(I hope that our papers don\\\'t get marked down!)</p>',0,685,0,0,'207.221.71.154','en'),(14084,2389,'2002-02-13 06:48:00',177,'pony','on the virtues of spelling: when i was YOUNGER i worried a lot about perfection...keeping all the bills paid, balancing the checkbook, crossing my t\\\'s, dotting the i\\\'s, flossing my teeth every day, dusting the nooks and crannies. these days i\\\'m not so great at that. i\\\'m not so great at it because i realized i was suffocating a lot of myself. the creative side. which is the side i live for. i realized part of the obsession with perfection was about living up to other people\\\'s image of what i should be. is this what i want to spend my energy worrying about? so then i learned that it IS possible to get good things done without perfection. and better yet, i can even get GREAT things done without perfection. [this includes the occasional spelling or grammatical error]. and the bonus is that a lot of folk still love me and like me and also think i do good things, regardless of my rebellious and messy ways. so i don\\\'t begrudge myself in the least bit because of it. in fact i even think it\\\'s kinda funny.',1,1032,0,0,'209.239.202.189','en'),(14085,2389,'2002-02-13 06:52:15',177,'pony','dude you are so crazy....maybe tubcat will give you a special banana?\r\n\r\n<p>:P</p>',1,82,175,0,'209.239.202.189','en'),(14086,2389,'2002-02-13 07:10:46',175,'Paul G','Oh God Pony!  In my dreams!  I\\\'m gonna put on the red light, lube up, and just pray that he does!  Take me now Tub!  I\\\'ll give you my tray and make your bed!  Toss my salad you naughty kitty!',0,197,0,0,'207.221.71.154','en'),(14087,305,'2002-02-13 07:41:01',128,'Javorai','that joy was fruit of electronically driven impulse. welcome to the year 2000.',0,78,0,0,'129.130.229.223','en'),(14088,2389,'2002-02-13 07:55:36',17,'austin','i said that i \\\'take solstice in the Internet\\\' the other day.  i meant solace.',0,83,82,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14089,2387,'2002-02-13 08:07:03',184,'doug','i have to assume that i start with correct information.  i trust my brain.  sometimes i just dont follow what it is telling me.',0,127,0,0,'24.42.76.242','en'),(14090,2391,'2002-02-13 08:24:27',175,'Paul G','Wise words, O.',0,14,0,0,'207.221.71.154','en'),(14091,2384,'2002-02-13 09:59:26',96,'dj1luna','\\\" My true place in the world, it turned out was somewhere beyond myself, and  if that place was inside me, it was also unlocatable. This was the tiny  hole between self and not-self, and for the first time in my life I saw this nowhere as the exact center of the world. \\\" Paul Auster, The Locked Room \r\n',1,309,0,0,'24.27.31.92','en'),(14092,2391,'2002-02-13 13:47:22',67,'trouble','\\\'just because you\\\'ve courted each other beforehand, even lived together, doesn\\\'t guarantee a happy marriage\\\'<br><br>totally agreed and in my case, a supported notion.  i knew her for 2 years prior to dating, we dated for 6 years prior to marriage, we have been married 6 years this year and we are now on the brink of divorce.  that\\\'s 12 years.  trust me when i say <i>you never fully know somebody</i>.  to know every single facet of somebodys nature is impossible.  there are an indefinite amount of factors that go into a successful marriage.  it\\\'s not luck, it\\\'s not fate, it\\\'s not destiny and it\\\'s not the movies.  my best to you lotus, i hope that this life will leave you happy. *nice supportive*',2,730,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14093,2391,'2002-02-13 13:53:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"that love which develops over time\\\"</i> rocks.</tt>',1,69,59,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14094,2388,'2002-02-13 13:56:54',67,'trouble','the pictures are outstanding.  you are really talented.',1,55,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14095,2389,'2002-02-13 13:59:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Dear Grammar Police,<br><br>\r\nPlease to <i>suck it</i>. The meanings of both <a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=exorcise\">exorcise</a> and <a href=\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=exercise\">exercise</i></a> apply here.  <br><br>Also: if you must make snarky comments on my posts, please use your real name; I want to know what the real you thinks. I think I can speak for us all, also, in saying that if you cant put your name to it, we dont want to hear it (at least in most situations). Frankly, \"good natured laughs over typos\" are getting a little tedious, now. It really sucks to put time, thought and emotionality into writing about experiences that lie deep in my heart... only to come back and find anonymous comments making fun of a good friend of mine\'s typo. How anticlimatic.<br><br>\r\nThat rant having been ranted, thanks, all, for reading and commenting! <i>[best friendly]</i></tt>',1,902,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14096,2380,'2002-02-13 14:47:24',67,'trouble','\\\'The other day I took a nap at my uncle\\\'s house\\\'<br><br>that rocks.  i am fond of naps and i often do so in the strangest places.  i have napped at friends houses on chairs, i have napped at their friends houses (whereas i was a stranger), i have napped in cars, in bars and in parks on picnic tables during my lunch hour.  there is something to be said for the ability to find comfort not only in place and surroundings but in yourself in ways that allow you to do to take the time out and nap.  everybody should take the time to nap.  nice little slice of life BMG.  *best friendly*',1,593,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14097,2387,'2002-02-13 14:55:45',148,'jeeff','try living on intuition for awhile.  it\\\'s a whole nother kind of logic, and it never seems to fail me.',0,105,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14098,2389,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i completely agree with peter. if you have something so stupid to say, that you can\\\'t sign your name ot it, why not keep it to yourself? if it\\\'s not a comment worth reading 5 times, then don\\\'t post it. let\\\'s keep the quality of conversation as high as possible. my vote\\\'s for no more anonymous snarking.',1,318,66,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14099,2389,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','plus, i\\\'m going to use the phrase, \\\'toss my salad, you naughty kitty!@\\\' at the next possible naughty instance. whoo yeah!',0,130,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14100,2389,'2002-02-13 15:28:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>toss my salad, you naughty kitty!</i><br><br>people seem to forget that having asshole-ish desires to be snarky is inevitably natural. i dont mind if people i know make asshole comments with their real names. like: if weeb were to say: \"dude. this fucking <i>sucked</i>\", my first impression wouldnt be \"fucking weeb!\", it\'d be \"well, weeb is smart n\' cool, so she might have a point. lets see what about this sucked so hard\".<br><br>when \"mister anonymous pseudonym\" gets all manky, the only vibe i get is anger. no constructive-ness at all.<br><br>and i think i can speak for most of us when i say that our egos arent so fragile that we need to have criticism veiled in sarasm and anonymity. i like anonymity and getting complimented as much as anyone, but by posting here, i open myself to criticism. i wish people would respect that and act accordingly. ok end rant here! :)</tt>',1,589,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14101,2389,'2002-02-13 15:45:11',67,'trouble','i agree with you peter and weeb.  i\\\'m all for a little continuity with names.  comments will be comments and in the end we need to realize that even a bad comment was a comment made by somebody <i>compelled</i> to do so <i>after</i> reading the piece.  i thought though that the new swinney.org changes allowed only registered people to make comments on the pieces?  if this is true then consider yourself flattered if somebody went through all the trouble of registering a bullshit name to hide behind, logged in, found your piece and <i>then</i> told you that you or the piece sucked. *best friendly*  ',1,607,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14102,2387,'2002-02-13 16:44:36',123,'dcc','that only works if your intuition isn\\\'t as useless as a two-legged goat.',0,75,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14103,1559,'2002-02-13 17:05:52',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ahh, i really like this</tt>',1,36,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14104,2389,'2002-02-13 18:47:43',129,'moebius','<br>\r\nkitties tossing salads:</b> oh dear, this actually happened to me. one morning i\\\'d finished a shower & was squatting to look under the bed for a missing shoe. all of a sudden: a very cold little nose where the sun doesn\\\'t shine. wrong! wrong! so very wrong!<br>\r\n<br>and my girlfriend saw it. \\\"oh, Miranda\\\'s tossing your salad!\\\" she exclaimed. i was ashamed. but Miranda wasn\\\'t. oh no, she was quite fine with her little display. i\\\'m very careful around her now.',1,489,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14105,2392,'2002-02-13 19:00:54',186,'ook','<p>on making more productive and meaningful use of classifications:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a big step can be recognizing the context of the classification. for cliched instance: separating things into good or not good... good for what? it can be recognized that although all the things just exist and are experienced, some have more vitamins than others, or whatever :)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in other words, recognizing that classifications are useful tools for thinking, but are not absolute.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ya. om, etc.</p>',3,495,0,0,'24.80.176.212','en'),(14106,2391,'2002-02-13 19:05:09',129,'moebius','my girlfriend and i have a relationship where the love has grown over time. we met right as i was in the throes of a terrible breakup. honestly, i don\\\'t know why Mols stuck with me during that first year or two: i was a wreck, always pining away a little for something that would never be, sometimes just going through the motions to get through the day.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut she was incredibly kind and good and thoughtful and a million other things. and she stood by me during my most awful moments and stressful times. she\\\'s also called me on my bullshit over & over, and i\\\'m a better person for it, I think.<br><br>it\\\'s been five years now and we\\\'re tighter than ever. i have mad love and respect for her. i sometimes wish we\\\'d had those moments of hot crazy ultradevoted passion that you typically have in the early days of a relationship. but i also think that sort of thing can cloud your mind and prevent you from dealing with the incontrovertable truths of your relationship, once the music\\\'s died down and you start hitting those inevitable patches of turbulence.',1,1088,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14107,2392,'2002-02-13 19:35:51',124,'gus','we classify because we have to figure out a way to put <b>ourselves</b> on top. everybody wants to be number one.',0,113,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14108,2391,'2002-02-13 19:40:15',151,'stella','<P>this is a very interesting post. it\\\'s made me think a bit about a bunch of stuff. first off, lotus, more power to you for being honest enough with yourself to determine that this was something that you want. not enough people have the ability to look deep inside and ask those questions, much less answer them. </P><P>\r\ni\\\'m in a relationship with the man whom i think is \\\'the one\\\'. i\\\'m in love as never before. and honestly, i\\\'ve never been hurt as deeply as when he hurts me. that\\\'s the biggest problem with such strong emotions. it\\\'s not possible to have a relationship with another person and not hurt them. he has cut me so deep that i don\\\'t know if it\\\'ll ever heal. i\\\'m trying. but even with this pain that i have to deal with, i don\\\'t think that i could consciously choose to not have him as my partner. i couldn\\\'t ever be the one to say that love doesn\\\'t matter too much, because to me it means everything, even if it means that i\\\'ll choose to leave him and never be with another parnter again. to a degree, i wish i could take the option of security and comfort. my life would be my less hectic and much easier. but to give up those moments of pain, i would also have to give up those moments of such intense joy and wonderment, those moments when my heart soars and my head spins. i don\\\'t think that is my life would be as full if i didn\\\'t have those brief moments. </P>\r\n<p. again, lotus, this isn\\\'t a slam at you and your decisions. i respect that you\\\'ve chosen the path that you\\\'re about to embark upon. i just couldn\\\'t so it myself. </P>',0,1616,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14109,2391,'2002-02-13 21:38:56',56,'kat','over the years i have become a firm believer in the fact that there is no such thing as \\\'the one\\\' ...and actually i find this comforting. it means i get many chances at great love, and what could be better then that? sure, there are some people we connect with on a more intimate \\\'soul\\\' level but that\\\'s not always instantaneous, nor is it always about romance. knowing someone and loving them deeply doesnâ€™t necessarily mean being together is the best for both of you. recognizing that is what unconditional true love is all about. yes, you will love this person like you love no otherâ€¦because everyone is different, as is every love. and nothing is permanent. everything evolves to some degree. pain is really a state of mindâ€¦its power is what you *allow* it to be. if you choose to be alone because you feel youâ€™ll never love again, or not love as deeply, then that is a choice YOU are making. something akin to closing doors rather than opening them. much of this has to do with perspective and awareness. opening yourself to loving/being loved can feel like an extremely vulnerable state but in actuality it is you at your strongest. itâ€™s about having faith and loving yourself first. believing in yourself. soulmates are there, we just need to remember that they come in many shapes and sizesâ€¦and not always the kind we expect or think we want.',1,1365,0,0,'209.239.195.245','en'),(14110,2387,'2002-02-13 21:57:18',176,'Scapegoat','hey don\\\'t diss the brethren',0,30,123,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14111,2389,'2002-02-13 22:14:36',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'ve been thinking about the whole name issue and I agree that consistency when comment is just common courtesy or so  it would seem to me. I am however guilty of using more then one name to post but I figure that is acceptable as I use the same two names consistently and I think that they reflect two different sides to me and maybe my writing. Seeing as how I haven\\\'t been around that long though I\\\'m curious about peoples thoughts on this. ',0,453,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14112,2391,'2002-02-13 22:35:42',176,'Scapegoat','I liked what o and Mad Moll had to say. I myself think that there is too many meanings for the word love. It is so sought after that many people eventually just determine that what they have in their own long term relations is love. It allows everyone to lay claim to the word and its power if they feel the need. Imho though, who\\\'s to say that this is wrong (definetly not me). I think this happens in any kind of serious coupling regardless of the circumstances that lead to the two people coming together.   ',0,514,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14113,2392,'2002-02-13 22:50:21',176,'Scapegoat','Number one, what though? Assuming that your right about the number one thing. I don\\\'t buy that we all want to be number one, I see a lot of people around me not trying to be number one at anything really. As for the whole equality in spirituality issue - I can recognize the notion of it being related to something that supercedes understanding perhaps but, why would that mean that other creatures or matter are the lesser? I think the notion of spiritual inequality is rooted in the three peoples of the books being exclusionary to begin with. They all seem to feel to a degree that they are the chosen ones or theirs the best path.',0,637,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14114,2392,'2002-02-13 22:53:22',176,'Scapegoat','Oh! and I think we over categorise/specialise/classify. Too much of this breds in weakness in both our existence and our thoughts. I\\\'m not saying it is all bad but too much of it these days.',0,193,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14115,2387,'2002-02-13 23:46:22',183,'lin','<p>its a funny business, trusting your intuition. I like to think I can trust my intuition, but I also question what I call intuition a lot. Critical thinking about something, in the form of questioning motives is really helpful for deciding what to do in these sorts of matters.\r\n<p>Though I will say that I\\\'ve often doubted my intuition because I wanted it to be wrong, often in a case where I have an intuition about something that conflicts with how I want to feel about a person. In that case I tend to go with what I want to believe. (Usually in terms of giving people more credit then they are worth.) There is something to be said for giving people \\\'chances\\\', but I guess it\\\'s possible to draw lines for yourself, basically a wake up and smell the coffee sort of thing. </p>',0,794,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(14116,2392,'2002-02-14 00:14:30',183,'lin','<p>my thoughts:\r\n<p> classification is the way we learn to distiguish ourselves from what we are not, as a method of learning how to put a name to the things that are absent. There are problems with this because boundaries in \\\'reality\\\' begin to get blurred on what is and is not real. Subjectivity is becoming more and more a way of life. Ie: It doesn\\\'t matter what you think if your idea doesn\\\'t jive with mine. We are different individuals, with different lives. Things that are the same can have a different meaning to each of us. (ie: i might like cats more - though i secretlt prefer dogs). I think it\\\'s important to distinguish ourselves from each other, though I would agree that we don\\\'t necessarily need to hold one thing above each other.</p>\r\n<p> But there can be a problem with this. If we prohibit people from thinking perhaps that they are right, while others are wrong, what becomes of the way we live? I recently read a book that you should pick up chia, it\\\'s called The World Jones Made, by Phillip K. Dick. The issue of not having anything to believe in was dealt with. If we establish our equal, then lots of problems are solved, but what will people have left driving them?</p>\r\n\r\n',0,1222,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(14117,2392,'2002-02-14 02:20:14',56,'kat','\\\"If we establish our equal, then lots of problems are solved, but what will people have left driving them?\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>very rarely do i look to other people to find \\\'drive\\\' for myself. in fact i tend to feel inferior and find all things pointless when i jump to comparisons. motivation can come from the urge to explore and the urge to create. and also, there can be passion in simply wanting to better yourself..in knowing that there is more inside of you yet to bloom. to believe that there are new possibilities in growing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>on the other hand there are always cravings for that new Harley, or an obsession with Friend\\\'s reruns to keep you living.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,676,0,0,'209.239.201.38','en'),(14118,2389,'2002-02-14 03:04:15',174,'Mad Moll','I think anonymity is swell.',6,27,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14119,2389,'2002-02-14 03:24:03',175,'Paul G','By the way, Peter, I think that this was a very good article and also a very good photograph.  Looking back at the 20 comments that preceded this one, the article seems to have escaped attention.\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s funny how many references there were in the article to circular movement: the walking that you did in \\\"concentric blocks\\\"; the car that was \\\"awakened for 5-minute bi-weekly circles\\\"; and of course the movement of the hands of time in a circular fashion.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You\\\'re in a holding pattern, eh?  As goes the car, so goes the relationship: \\\"I keep the car in case she might ever want to use it.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You move it in circles, just enough to keep it alive, in case she needs it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I must admit, it\\\'s hard to imagine you teasing a non-native speaker - but then again, it\\\'s hard to imagine myself tossing the little treasures of some former lover in the trash.  Things happen...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Rock steady, Peter.  It was a good piece.</p> ',0,982,0,0,'64.158.229.95','en'),(14120,2379,'2002-02-14 03:43:27',130,'treetop','my first pad away from home was in an apartment complex.  we would always get people knocking on the door trying to sell us stuff.  one day this cute girl knocked and tried to sell us magazines.  we invited her in and burned a joint with her and sent her on her dazed happy way.  didnt buy a magazine..',0,302,0,0,'172.137.106.136','en'),(14121,2166,'2002-02-14 03:52:59',187,'kandrer','the other day I stopped on my way to work:\r\non the pavement, close to the wall of a building, at this place dogs love to piss a few drops on their way to work, there were a few tiny gum bears candy (don\\\'t know the exact name of this things for US kids). It\\\'s made of gum, it looks so neat, you can see light thru it, colored in red, green, yellow and etc.\r\nIt took me a good part of the rest of my way to work to understand (I commute soo far, stupid me): I just stopped like I used to do when I was a kid; the beloved candy fell on the hell\\\'s dirty floor and it\\\'s now forbidden to take it back: you have to leave it here, though it still looks so clean and yummy.\r\nWas it my first frustration in life? I guess I should I keep on staring at candies on the sidewalk and avoid looking at tiny ladies\\\' skirts.\r\n\r\n(sorry I lost the point about l\\\'ami abandonne\\\' and the trashed technology thing; I just read this remark about stuffed animals in the trash, and pictures, yeah, pictures in the trash, that\\\'s something big)',1,1039,0,0,'208.223.155.220','en'),(14122,2389,'2002-02-14 04:39:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like the photo, also; thanks for noticing. you rarely see the empire state building in a non-lit-up state, so i thought it was an interesting shot.<br><br>im not so much in a holding pattern these days as i was last fall. things ended with the <i>watch girl</i> at the end of august, after 5 years... but i guess some thoughts are surfacing now, as ive finally started dating again this past month, and have come across an amazing person, who is quite wonderfully different than <i>watch girl</i>. it makes me pensive. its a nice feeling, but still pensive. even after more than 6 months, i still need to work on getting over <i>watch girl</i> some, if i want my curent relationship to live up to its potential... erk.</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,737,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14123,2392,'2002-02-14 05:00:54',183,'lin','I should probably elaborate a little. If we no longer have distinctions, and if we cease to specify things, then how are we to have preferences for one over the other? Personal preferences, or societal preferences? In an extreme case no one will stand up, because there is fear of stepping on someone\\\'s toes.. in that case, how would things get solved? <br>\r\n\r\nThis is all in theory though.. just ruminations on the idea. I agree with the personal passion for creation, and betterment of self, but I wonder about the rest of the world sometimes...</p>',0,554,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(14124,2389,'2002-02-14 05:08:35',175,'Paul G','Spelling is wrong here, most likely, but the artist is named: Maxfield Parish.  He did quite a bit of advert. work - he had a similar quality of light in many of his pieces.  The blue field behind the structure in your photo - worth a peek to compare the object/ground contrast and quality of light in his twilight pieces and yours...nice old word: \\\"twilight\\\" - yep.  You usually offer full natural light or full synthetic - the \\\"twilight\\\" is nice - esp. for the passage of one thing into another, and indefinite boundaries, etc.',0,541,0,0,'206.217.112.36','en'),(14125,2389,'2002-02-14 05:18:57',175,'Paul G','Yeah, it\\\'s Parrish.  I can\\\'t find the really groovy deco that I\\\'m thinking of - but it\\\'s not so unlike the architecture of the building.  This has the twilight blue, anyway: http://www.independentweb.com/parrish/1926a.htm',0,233,0,0,'206.217.112.36','en'),(14126,2396,'2002-02-14 05:50:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like this; especially the last paragraph. lately, ive really been liking writings that give me a good sense of <i>place</i>. like, after reading this, i have a wonderfully detailed image of the places and things youre talking about. its fun, new images; new looks into other people\'s day-to-day lives.</tt> ',1,320,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14127,2389,'2002-02-14 06:04:27',66,'Peter','<br><tt>small world: my mom is a big deco.parish fan. i grew up under the watchful gaze of parrish woodnymphs bathed in gauzy lighting.</tt>',1,140,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14128,2389,'2002-02-14 06:06:16',66,'urban photo','that photo reminds me of this:<br><img src=\"http://gs.cdnow.com/graphics/COVERART/local/L/73/12/00077312.jpg\">',1,116,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14129,2389,'2002-02-14 06:08:21',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i was listening to that <a href=\"http://www.livejournal.com/talkread.bml?itemid=23421790\">here</a>, actually.</tt>',1,124,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14130,2389,'2002-02-14 06:38:45',177,'pony','twilight is my favorite time of day',4,35,175,0,'209.239.208.119','en'),(14131,2396,'2002-02-14 07:17:42',124,'gus','thats true, I can picture the workplace. but, I think you drink too much coffee man. Most of my friends are addicted to coffee, and sometimes I think I dont really know them, they are all drugged up all the time... who would all these people be without coffee!? everything would be so serene.',0,292,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14132,2397,'2002-02-14 07:20:55',124,'gus','this is like a diary entry, it means something to you, but it means less to me. you give details but I need more. why did you give the details you did? how come you told me where zoe is from and how long she\\\'s been here but you didnt tell me what color her hair is, what she looks like?',0,289,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14133,2389,'2002-02-14 07:25:14',124,'gus','btw, I dont really care if any of you spell correctly or not, as long as you dont care if I arbitrarily decide to think you\\\'re a nincompoop. (nincompoop! what a word). \r\n<P>\r\nthis comment thread made me laugh. thought I think some of the stuff I found funny wasnt supposed to make me laugh. \r\n<p>\r\nanyway.',0,308,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14134,2383,'2002-02-14 07:30:17',124,'gus','I will do what I want. more or less. and thats how it is.',0,57,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14135,2389,'2002-02-14 08:37:48',175,'Paul G','Happy St. Valentine\\\'s Day to everyone in the thread!  Love!',0,62,0,0,'67.24.8.176','en'),(14136,2389,'2002-02-14 09:11:54',175,'Paul G','The loving moment has ended.  Do tell: Why is it that a spelling error constitutes probable cause to believe that an individual is mentally deficient, while the omission of capital letters or apostrophes does not?  \r\n\r\n<p>I have always liked both the style and also the content of your writing, Gus.  But, aren\\\'t you missing a few capital letters and apostrophes in your own comment?  Ought we to assume that you really think that \\\"dont\\\" and \\\"wasnt\\\" are correct usage?  No, of course not.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know - the silliness is one thing, but some of the language is, well, hurtful.  I\\\'m quite the free speech advocate - sometimes I\\\'m just confused about the purposes for which it is employed.  Certainly, I\\\'ve made my own poor attempts at humor...</p>   ',0,785,0,0,'67.24.8.176','en'),(14137,2389,'2002-02-14 11:13:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this whole argument could be avoided if we would all just simply write in esperanto!</tt>',1,97,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14138,2397,'2002-02-14 13:08:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt>looks can be deceiving.</tt>',1,36,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(14139,2399,'2002-02-14 13:11:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>stories about falling in love always make me smile and feel warm inside; especially when theyre about falling in love with a machine.</tt>',1,146,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(14140,2399,'2002-02-14 13:48:52',182,'bmg','<p>Can you take pictures of this bike? I mean your bike, not just a replica. A picture of you sitting next to it, beer in hand - maybe egg too. Rarr, that sounds great. I want something to work on like that... i will do yard work today...',1,238,0,1,'66.90.145.8','en'),(14141,2399,'2002-02-14 13:49:08',182,'bmg','<p>Can you take pictures of this bike? I mean your bike, not just a replica. A picture of you sitting next to it, beer in hand - maybe egg too. Rarr, that sounds great. I want something to work on like that... i will do yard work today...</p>',1,242,0,0,'66.90.145.8','en'),(14142,2399,'2002-02-14 14:23:45',67,'trouble','aren\\\'t projects like this fun?  i have a 1973 VW Bus i\\\'m currently working on restoring.  learning new things is always cool.  would love to see pictures of the bike as well austin.',1,187,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14143,2399,'2002-02-14 14:34:42',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh my goodness. i just remembered a strange dream i had last night; there was a mysterious man in it, and for some reason i had the distinct impression that <i>this man was none other than austin swinney</i>; the man, however, looked uncannily like <a href=\"http://www.dem.de/entertainment/stars/d/dolph_lundgren.jpg\">dolph lundgren</a>. odd, how dreams work sometimes.</tt> ',1,389,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(14144,2399,'2002-02-14 15:25:17',74,'jo','really? i had a dream the other night that i had <a href=\\\"http://www.sethgreenonline.com/pictures/magazines/jump1.jpg\\\">sex with seth green</a>, possibly because i\\\'ve watched a lot of season 4 buffy reruns lately. mmmmmmm, oz.',0,234,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14145,2399,'2002-02-14 15:36:10',74,'jo','oh! but austin: this was great. i am complete awe of people who can refurbish machines.',1,87,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14146,2398,'2002-02-14 15:38:44',74,'jo','wow. gus. i liked this so much. this would be a really great beginning of a screenplay.',0,87,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14147,2399,'2002-02-14 18:12:50',82,'sims','hi austin! i just wanted to say something quick about beers and the el camino and my dad\\\'s garage and the scratchy radio and good times and things like that.<br>\r\n\\\"best friendly\\\"',0,187,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14148,2396,'2002-02-14 18:46:33',129,'moebius','<br>i\\\'ve been thinking of posting a scary piece i wrote years ago during one of my first corporate office jobs. not so much about the workplace but the twisted mindset i was in back then, lost in an unfamilar city, living in a cubicle environment for the first time. but oh, i fear you people would think i\\\'m an ax murderer afterwards. or maybe not.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhmmmmmmm. i\\\'m feeling reckless.',1,401,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14149,2397,'2002-02-14 19:00:21',177,'pony','i liked that it was vague. vague and yet also incredibly detailed. stepping on the baby pictures. yea. stepping on the baby pictures. haziness that leaves you wondering is a nice thing.',1,185,0,0,'209.239.207.18','en'),(14150,2392,'2002-02-14 19:36:56',123,'dcc','<p>isn\\\'t language itself classification?</p>\r\n<p>if we didn\\\'t have names for things, how would we communicate ideas? the words that i use denote particular classification of what i am trying to explain or describe. new words for with more specific meaning denote a more tightly aimed idea or description.</p>\r\n<p>whether it\\\'s purely descriptive or as an expression of feeling you\\\'ll need to use words to describe and classify what you experience to other people, if you choose that as your means of expression.</p>',0,526,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14151,2397,'2002-02-14 19:38:23',17,'austin','that thing about baby pictures is out of any sort of context.  if there is supposed to be some symbolic meaning in your mind there, why not develop it into a paragraph.<BR><BR>\r\ni enjoy the idea of rummaging around in an abandoned multiple story building with old computers in it. like the set of a sci fi after math film, you could run with that.<BR><BR>\r\nalso i would like to know if the brit is hot or not.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\\\"constructive\\\"?',3,442,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14152,2399,'2002-02-14 19:45:56',83,'adamajah','i remember you workin on your dirt bike out back at the sloped-floor crack house.',0,81,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14153,2397,'2002-02-14 19:46:13',124,'gus','yah sure. sometimes vague is good. but I want to be able to see the main subjects sometimes. or maybe it just didnt speak to me. what can you do.',0,145,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14154,2399,'2002-02-14 19:48:37',17,'austin','that was not a dirt bike out back of the sloped  floor crack house, that was a honda 1978 honda hawk-variety bike that was a piece of shit.  i left that bike in the back yard when i vacated the sloped crack house floor.',4,219,83,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14155,2396,'2002-02-14 19:51:51',67,'trouble','moebius, i\\\'m practically begging you to write such a piece.  i wish i could have made this little slice o\\\' wednesday afternoon a little more interesting but it was what it was... a boring afternoon spent within a boring company.  in talking with several people now it seems as though we are all in (informal) agreement when we say we would love to read more slices of everyday life from folks.  i want to hear about somebody\\\'s morning, right down to the angst over whether to use the toothbrush after it fell onto the floor.  every little detail.<br><br>for the record, the brush fell with the head of it on the rug and i picked it up in <i>under a second</i>... i swear.',1,680,129,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14156,2165,'2002-02-14 20:18:35',155,'lotus','i think i\\\'m in love.\r\ni\\\'ve been reading a bunch of older pieces to try to get more familiar with the writing of people here.\r\n\r\nyou are talented.',0,151,0,0,'216.191.251.62','en'),(14157,2397,'2002-02-14 20:50:20',176,'Scapegoat','I didn\\\'t mind the vagueness, it didn\\\'t even seem that way to me too much. I liked the whole adventure into the abandoned building. It reminded me of some of the times when I\\\'ve found spaces like this and been able to take advantage of them for a place to party or crash for a few days.',0,294,0,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14158,2399,'2002-02-14 21:08:00',83,'adamajah','<P>just goes to show ya how much <i>i</i> know about bikes...  that\\\'s hilarious:  you just left it in the backyard when you moved.</p>',0,137,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14159,2392,'2002-02-15 00:11:17',174,'Mad Moll','People who are afraid to classify are really just afraid to make mistakes.\r\n\r\nIt\\\'s possible to not make mistakes, but it\\\'s usually not possible to get anything done at the same time.',6,188,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14160,2404,'2002-02-15 00:18:20',67,'trouble','\\\'the best i could offer her was orange go-jo\\\'<br><br>oh let me count the ways in which <i><b>you rock!</i></b>  this was a great post.  would you come tinker with my VW?  it needs a good tinkering.\r\n',1,205,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(14161,2400,'2002-02-15 00:20:55',174,'Mad Moll','This was very nice. Things about the moon and about water make me dreamy.',6,73,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14162,2404,'2002-02-15 00:22:22',166,'heathen','poor jeremy.',0,12,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14163,381,'2002-02-15 00:26:01',82,'simon','oh look! now it\\\'s valentine\\\'s day again and whatnot.',0,58,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14164,2404,'2002-02-15 00:52:56',176,'Scapegoat','I have a confession to make. I tend to think that tom boys usually make for the most attractive and appealing women. Not being a female, I don\\\'t know the conflict between \\\'utilitarian\\\' mentality vs. trappings of girlishness. It seems to me that they should be able to be balanced though. I have a few female friends I have grown up with that used to be tom boys and have since changed, at times I lament and wish they still were like that. Hope that you find some balance but, the whole utilitarian thing - very sexy.  ',1,528,166,0,'64.229.163.227','en'),(14165,2404,'2002-02-15 02:41:04',177,'pony','take one skirt [length optional], add combat boots, top with bustier push-up bra, add striped t and hoodie...oh, don\\\'t forget the hello kitty panties...then apply much fierceness with gusto, and pigtails. [don\\\'t even talk to me about fingernail polish, ugh gross, would rather see it on guys] ',4,299,0,0,'209.239.197.98','en'),(14166,2404,'2002-02-15 02:52:38',69,'starlajo','agreed about nailpolish on boys.\r\n',0,34,177,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(14167,2404,'2002-02-15 03:00:53',129,'moebius','<br>heathen: interesting, informative, fun essay. it\\\'s weird, all the odd complexities that gender issues can put us through. you avoided the \\\"femininity cop-out\\\" only to be boxed in, oddly enough, by your utilitarian ethic.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nin your description of the friend who wanted to see some ankle i was reminded of the victorian sensibilities of 100-plus years past. requests for the same back then would\\\'ve been similarly rebuffed.<br><br>here\\\'s to more experiments, for all of us. i\\\'m going to try lederhosen next.',1,536,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14168,2404,'2002-02-15 03:15:17',129,'moebius','oh, and i second Scapegoat\\\'s comment. i\\\'m generally thrilled by tuff girls of all types. but if they\\\'re wearing cutie undies under the leather, flannel, and/or combat gear, i certainly won\\\'t complain. yum.',1,217,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14169,2404,'2002-02-15 04:04:51',124,'gus','I never put in any engines, but I refused to wear dresses for most of my life. I still dont wear them, they make your legs cold! besides, when I do, everyone can actually tell Im wearing leg warmers. shh. \r\n<p>\r\nI saw your legs that night too. you looked like a total girl, no no, thats not right, you looked like some crazy dame out of the 40s. and you know, they were pretty good at heavy drinking, heavy smoking and punching out whoever got in the way (at least in the movies) so perhaps it\\\'s appropriate after all.\r\n<p>\r\nYah, being a girl is ok, except when they squeal in groups. then I skunk away and pretend Im not really one of them. unless Im secretly mocking them. which happens.',0,692,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14170,2397,'2002-02-15 04:55:58',130,'treetop','hi\r\n<br>\r\nyes, this is like a diary or a journal entry.  may i refer you to the <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/faq.phtml\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">\\\"what should i write\\\" section of the faq</a>?  what do you look like?  what color is your hair?  innie or outie?  burt or ernie?',1,290,124,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14171,2397,'2002-02-15 04:56:54',130,'treetop','the above commet was to gus :',0,29,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14172,2397,'2002-02-15 04:57:22',130,'treetop','the above commet was a comment',0,30,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14173,2397,'2002-02-15 05:11:06',130,'treetop','<br>to austin:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\noooh yess, she is beautiful.  since gus asked i will give a description:  brown hair, usually braided in several braids, blue eyes, about 5\\\'5 and probably about 130.  she has a boyfriend who is cuban, lucky guy.',0,242,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14174,2397,'2002-02-15 05:14:54',130,'treetop','<br>to scapegoat:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nyes! you got it. that is the main idea.  exploring through an old building, dreaming up places to crash, party, or to film a cheap movie.',0,168,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14175,2397,'2002-02-15 05:16:38',130,'treetop','<br>to pony:\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhi :)',0,31,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14176,2404,'2002-02-15 05:24:13',184,'doug','i really liked this.',0,20,0,0,'24.42.76.242','en'),(14177,2399,'2002-02-15 05:37:46',130,'treetop','dude!  i have wanted a bmw bike since i was 8.  i am going to get one in the next couple of months.  having a bike in the city would be so much easier than a car.  i\\\'ve got to save up some flow first.  i wonder how well the other one runs?',0,242,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14178,2404,'2002-02-15 06:03:48',175,'Paul G','Sometime, if it\\\'s ok, let\\\'s go drink Jaegermeister and shoot handguns together.  Do you like Camille Paglia?',0,114,0,0,'64.156.104.45','en'),(14179,2404,'2002-02-15 06:09:52',130,'treetop','hi, you are welcome to borrow my <a href=\\\"http://members.aol.com/sshkdwnst/denatongue.jpg\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">skirt</a> sometime :)',0,140,0,0,'172.136.163.185','en'),(14180,2190,'2002-02-15 06:59:41',17,'austin','this is the year of the WATER HORSE.',5,36,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14181,2404,'2002-02-15 07:25:03',166,'heathen','<p>\r\njeremy actually has a ton of skirts like this.  he makes them himself because he can\\\'t find ones that are quite up to his standards.  god bless him, he once made me one to show his love.  it was atomic green corduroy and all bunchy at the waist.\r\n</p><p>\r\nhe said to me:<br>\r\n\\\"i made the pockets large enough for you to carry your books in!\\\"\r\n<p/><p>\r\nif that isn\\\'t love, i don\\\'t know what is.\r\n</p>',0,419,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14182,2404,'2002-02-15 07:29:28',166,'heathen','yeah, i\\\'ve felt totally boxed in.  there is so much that i have rejected because i didn\\\'t want to be perceived as being weak that it\\\'s really put a limit on my happiness.  and there are otherthings i hav rejected out of fear of them never being offered to me.  it\\\'s funny that i am just realizing it now.',0,316,129,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14183,2404,'2002-02-15 07:44:15',175,'Paul G','Oh God!  I wasn\\\'t being sarcastic.  I may be a fucktard, but I do those things.  I liked the piece a lot and I was trying to be friendly.  You sound like one of my sisters.  I\\\'m sorry.',0,190,0,0,'64.156.104.45','en'),(14184,2404,'2002-02-15 07:49:25',75,'tyler','you should add a picture. mmm sexy tomboy.',1,42,0,0,'165.247.164.250','en'),(14185,2404,'2002-02-15 07:56:12',75,'tyler','isn\\\'t life a mystery...',1,26,0,0,'165.247.164.250','en'),(14186,2404,'2002-02-15 08:07:11',166,'heathen','<p>me <a href=\\\"http://heathen.net/images/alarmcall.jpg\\\">before</a>.\r\n</p><p>\r\nme and poor jeremy <a href=\\\"http://heathen.net/images/lurve.jpg\\\">now</a>.\r\n</p><p>\r\naustin would like to point out that titillated has two Ls.  go to hell, austin.\r\n</p>',0,259,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14187,2409,'2002-02-15 08:33:13',124,'gus','<p>Heartache is what happens when someone doesnt (or you worry they dont) love you, so perhaps we give ourselves extra significance at this time, because of our perceived insignificance in the eyes of the other. As I keep saying, we all want to be number one. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI think giving up on someone is one of the hardest things. We want the people we love to either be number one with us, or at the least, number two just next to us. To give up on them is to let them go, and to realize its more important for you to be yourself than to please them.</p>\r\n<p>and Never apologize for any creative performance within the performance.\r\n</p>\r\n\r\n',0,643,0,0,'64.163.215.122','en'),(14188,2404,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','call chaya!  we\\\'ve found the spokesmodel for dainycore.',3,58,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14189,2404,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','also, i recently talked s5 into buying a cargo lava-lava, which is like a more heavy-duty sarong, worn in the pacific islands.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nit has big cargo pockets in the side, for all s5\\\'s things.  he has many things in his pockets.  so many things.',3,252,0,0,'203.96.111.201','en'),(14190,2404,'2002-02-15 12:17:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>id have to third scapegoats comment; tough girls. yes. i once dated a girl simply based on the fact that she once, when mad at me, flipped me onto the sidewalk. id never date a girl who couldnt hold her own, or at least do some damage.</tt>',1,248,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14191,2404,'2002-02-15 13:04:43',67,'trouble','jeremy is one lucky hombre.',1,27,0,0,'68.47.202.98','en'),(14192,2404,'2002-02-15 14:36:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>speaking of \\\"hombres\\\"... there appears to be a new safe-sex ad campaign going on in the ads on the subway. it seems like every other car is plastered with posters that say <i>\\\"un hombre en un hombre\\\"</i>. in the ad, the <i>o\\\'s</i> are formed by little rolled up condoms, which of course adds quite an interesting vibe to the posters, especially since (if memory serves me correctly) they can be translated as meaning \\\"a man in a man\\\". safe sex du jour.</tt>',1,486,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14193,2404,'2002-02-15 14:47:30',74,'jo','heathen, those pictures are great. can we have more? i\\\'m a picturewhore.',0,75,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14194,2409,'2002-02-15 15:52:24',151,'stella','weeks of public abuse and private consolation? that doesn\\\'t sound entirely healthy... but far be it from me to say that I don\\\'t understand the situation.. just make sure thatyou keep yourself safe. abuse is abuse and no matter how much love you have, abuse will eventually kill it, taking a bit of your spirit with it. I\\\'ll keep my fingers crossed for you. ',0,366,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14195,2404,'2002-02-15 16:08:54',151,'stella','I think that I might die if my boy made me a skirt, especially if it had pockets big enough to put my books in!! What a hunk you have!',0,134,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14196,2409,'2002-02-15 16:37:18',122,'hool','<i>how can two people exist within the same body?</i>.   that\\\'s not non sensical - it\\\'s well put.',0,103,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14197,2404,'2002-02-15 16:55:38',167,'uke','poor jeremy!',0,12,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14198,2404,'2002-02-15 17:45:18',129,'moebius','<br>just wanted to add one more thing, esp. after seeing yr. snapshots...you keep referring to yr man as \\\"poor jeremy.\\\" well, i think he\\\'s LUCKY! and i\\\'m sure he feels the same way. rock on with yr bad self.',1,219,166,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14199,2404,'2002-02-15 17:53:55',166,'heathen','more <a href=\\\"http://heathen.net/extrovert/\\\">pictures</a> and an adventure.',0,81,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14200,423,'2002-02-15 18:18:31',82,'simon','hey bmg, that <b>is</b> a nice \'effin\' picture.',1,53,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14201,2396,'2002-02-15 18:53:46',185,'orion','POS = Peice of Shit or Point of Sale?  Of course, I suppose it could be both depending on how much you like your job?  It\\\'s hard to type around a teriyaki bowl, if the bowl is big enough... ;-)',1,196,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14202,2403,'2002-02-15 19:00:22',67,'trouble','most excellent.  i take quiet joy and feel a small pocket of reassurance that somebody.. somewhere.. feels (or in this case felt) the same way i feel about leading a life within a cube.  i have to get out of here.  <br><br>detailing the morning scramble was great as well.  how come wallets are always not where you expect them to be when you are running late?  i would like this explained.',1,390,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14203,2404,'2002-02-15 19:06:56',185,'orion','I laughed out loud twice while reading this.  I think my boss now knows I\\\'m fucking off, I never laugh at code (well, derisively maybe, but not happily like I did twice).  Irony bites me because, while I\\\'m pretty masculine, I\\\'m not terribly mechanically inclined as would be the stereotype.  Cars and engines and other macroscopic things mystify me (it\\\'s electrons that are my thing).  You rock!  :-)  ',1,414,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14204,2403,'2002-02-15 19:09:30',82,'simon','i have this problem all the time. what the hell, right? also, i\\\'ve had a full-time office job for just over a year now after mostly working \\\"freelance\\\" or just when i needed to for most of my life. whenever i try and write something like this about it, i get too depressed and want to take a nap.',0,305,67,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14205,2396,'2002-02-15 19:11:45',67,'trouble','orion, i can\\\'t believe i didn\\\'t see POS being perceived as \\\'piece of shit\\\' while writing it although you are correct, it applies to both.  this job is neither challenging nor exciting.  i\\\'m a drone, i\\\'m a tool.',1,228,185,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14206,2411,'2002-02-15 19:13:14',185,'orion','Cool post.  I think the wander thing is spring-time, affecting all living things.  Not so sure I agree about the sterility of words, some words and groups of words pack a powerful sensory punch, enough to make you laugh or cry.',1,227,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14207,2396,'2002-02-15 19:32:09',82,'simon','heh, i too automatically think Piece Of Shit when i read POS.',0,61,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14208,2403,'2002-02-15 19:52:54',174,'Mad Moll','This was really depressing. Made me remember how much I hate cities, how much I hate offices. Nice piece though.',6,112,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14209,2404,'2002-02-15 19:55:59',148,'jeeff','very entertaining.  tonite i am going to an anti-valentines day party dressed in a black cocktail dress, black fishnets, a silk scarf and clip-on earrings.  secret life of a girl indeed.  maybe i\\\'ll take pictures.',0,216,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14210,2399,'2002-02-15 19:58:40',148,'jeeff','i need to buy a bicycle.  but maybe i\\\'ll build a fake motor with a pull-string, that just spins whirlygigs on the handlebars or something.',0,141,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14211,2397,'2002-02-15 20:01:47',148,'jeeff','this reminds me of the time i went exploring the abandoned brick factory with paul.  too bad i don\\\'t still have the photos online.  its very much like walking through a crypt.  if you like that kind of thing, check out <a href=\\\"http://www.infiltration.org/\\\" target=\\\"new\\\">infiltration.org</a>.',0,307,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14212,2404,'2002-02-15 20:18:59',185,'orion','<p>\r\nhey, pockets for things are very important.  you never know what you might need, so you might as well take as much with you as you can.  :-)\r\n</p>  \r\n<p>currently my pockets contain[1]:\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>keys (car, my apt., my mom\\\'s apt.)</li>\r\n<li>Spec\\\'s liquor store discount doohicky.  b33r!</li>\r\n<li>swiss army knife (it\\\'s got a cool, really bright LED flashlight)</li>\r\n<li>handspring visor PDA -- chess, Kant, and various inane scribblings (plus obscene cave art for the cubicle world)</li>\r\n<li>mechanical pencil, 0.5mm lead</li>\r\n<li>big wallet that used to have a chain until the connector broke, I\\\'ve had this wallet for seven years.  one of the buttons is long gone, the zipper hasn\\\'t zipped since 1998, but it\\\'s just too well worn to let go.</li>\r\n<li>lint.  not sure what I\\\'d use that for but there it is...</li>\r\n<li>one quarter, one dime, and three pennies.  oh, and another penny in another pocket.  I have no idea how long the pennies have been in there, the quarter is part of change from lunch today and the dime I found in the parking lot three days ago.  I think that eventually these will migrate to my wallet, but they might end up in the wash instead.</li>\r\n<li>one screw, like you\\\'d find on the back of a computer case unless it\\\'s one of those screwless cases</li>\r\n</ol></p>\r\n<p>[1] propz to the old school list posts</p>',1,1373,59,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14213,2404,'2002-02-15 20:21:13',185,'orion','Jaegermeister!!!  At last, somebody else who likes that!  *best friendly*',1,73,175,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14214,2404,'2002-02-15 20:24:22',185,'orion','http://www.specsonline.com/',1,27,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14215,2404,'2002-02-15 20:31:19',67,'trouble','mmmm, jaegermeister.  i love the liquid equivalent of a german boot to the face.  i can remember when i was younger and i tried it for the first time at a wedding.  my uncle who is the most prim and proper guys you could ever encounter quietly leaned over and advised me to \\\'watch out son, that stuff will <i>fuck</i> you up\\\'.  jaegermeister, it is good!',1,360,0,0,'216.142.219.138','en'),(14216,2404,'2002-02-15 20:35:38',123,'dcc','<p>if you don\\\'t, <b>i</b> will.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>back on topic:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>but really, tough girls with a soft edge are the real beauties in my books. women are hard as nails for different reasons, and if you can use your female sexuality in a tough way, damn girl, that\\\'s the key.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>there\\\'s nothing stronger than a woman that knows how to use the fact that she\\\'s a woman to put herself in a power position over any man. male clothing is not required for that!</p>',2,476,148,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14217,2404,'2002-02-15 20:36:01',82,'simon','i love reading people\\\'s lists too.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nin my pockets:<br>\r\ntwo quarters<br>\r\na nickel<br>\r\na ten dollar bill<br>\r\nmy driver\\\'s license<br>\r\na girl from high school\\\'s phone number<br>\r\na valentine from an unknown admirer<br>\r\na flyer for a club called hot pants<br>\r\na bart card<br>\r\nand a ticket stub from a show/talk at yerba buena<br>',0,351,185,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14218,2404,'2002-02-15 20:37:46',123,'dcc','excuse me, the 1st part of my comment was directed at jeeff, but somehow the \\\"to\\\" field-dealy didn\\\'t work it\\\'s magic.',0,129,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14219,2404,'2002-02-15 20:56:47',151,'stella','I absolutely LOVE Jaegermeister. It can cure any ailment that I may have, probably because of its total numbing quality. Jaegermeister makes me brave and want to climb trees. In Germany, it\\\'s made with opium. Is anyone going to Germany soon? Will you bring some? Please? ',0,274,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14220,2396,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','trouble--some may consider this to be blatantly unethical, but in my opinion when you\\\'re in one of those dead-end dull & uninspiring office jobs that leave you spinning your weels: steal time away from them while you wait for better career opportunities. work on those story ideas that you\\\'ve been mulling over. craft poems. write letters to friends who haven\\\'t heard from you in awhile. screw the system. i have friends who have written entire novels at work, just because they could.<br>\r\n<br>\r\noddly enough, over the past few years of more productive and interesting employment, i\\\'ve often gotten nostalgic for the simpler days of <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2403.txt\\\">that horrid first office job</a> because there, in a way, i had the freedom to do whatever i wanted for half of the day--as long as the boss thought i was \\\"working.\\\"<br>',1,895,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14221,2404,'2002-02-15 23:07:22',148,'jeeff','anybody ever heard of \\\'escorial grun\\\'?  a polish liquor that has disappeared from the face of the planet, but was probably the closest thing to absinthe i could find i canada.',0,181,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14222,2398,'2002-02-15 23:13:00',82,'simon','gusbert, i  just wanted to say that at first i skimmed over this story because i was in a hurry. i read it just now and i\\\'m awfully glad i did.\r\n<br>\r\np.s.  it reminds me of a black rubber band i wore on my wrist until it broke.',0,231,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14223,2412,'2002-02-15 23:57:57',82,'simon','oh lulu, my dear poopoo, i\\\'m so sorry about dear old nando.',0,62,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14224,2412,'2002-02-16 00:04:36',115,'lulu','sometimes i think i can still hear the ding-a-ling of his bell...coming to get his food.*snif*',0,94,0,0,'216.244.32.174','en'),(14225,2399,'2002-02-16 00:19:51',17,'bark','Another cool thing about the <a\r\nhref=\"http://www.oddhours.com/hs/img/bikesnow.jpg\">/5</a> is that you\r\ncan run with the beasts in the <A href=\"http://www.motomelee.com\">Moto\r\nMelee</a>. But good luck winning the Long Distance <a\r\nhref=\"http://www.oddhours.com/hs/img/bike.jpg\">trophy</a>.  ',3,305,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14226,2412,'2002-02-16 00:31:46',65,'le','nando is like someone\\\'s old grandfather who feels girls up, but it\\\'s hard to be really mad at him because you know he\\\'s just a crazy old coot. but you don\\\'t want to live with him.',0,191,0,0,'209.86.1.223','en'),(14227,2399,'2002-02-16 00:32:26',17,'austi