w<br>\r\ni\\\'m the mutha fucking best<br>\r\nhoarse threatening croaks<br>\r\n',1,91,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14369,2435,'2002-02-20 01:05:21',148,'jeeff','<p>guys trading old clothing is nothing new, i once did a design job for a garbage bag full of the guy\\\'s old clothes.  that and a roll of police caution tape.  it was great.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, i don\\\'t know if its a male thing, but i always have just one pair of pants that i like to wear.  it changes from pair to pair every six months or so, but i seem to only be able to like one pair of pants at a time.  shopping is agony.</p>',0,433,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14370,2434,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','you guys make me feel square and unhip. never has this chick seen any meth or coke- and not in high school! hrmm.',1,113,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14371,2434,'2002-02-20 01:21:27',64,'Joanna','I also have never seen coke or meth. I\\\'m finally in circles where i could get it, if I had say a spare $300 for a gram or so perhaps. New Zealand is a very expensive place to do drugs, I suspect. ',0,199,57,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(14372,2434,'2002-02-20 01:22:10',82,'sims to asstin','but that night was effin\\\' funny, you have to admit. what with all the ballet girl talk we were witness to.',0,109,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14373,2434,'2002-02-20 01:36:04',66,'Peter to weeb','<br><tt>weeb, when you finally make it to the city... my neighbor sells both, so i can provide you with an exquisite sample of the most elegant of the nasty street drugs.</tt>\r\n\r\n \r\n',1,182,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14374,2434,'2002-02-20 01:37:24',66,'$300 a gram?','sheeeeit, dog! its $20 in jersey.',1,33,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14375,2434,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i want to do ballet.',3,20,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14376,2434,'2002-02-20 01:46:22',82,'yeah','i don\\\'t know bout you, homes, but i never paid more than 60 tops.',0,68,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14377,2438,'2002-02-20 02:11:20',200,'chiclet','another birthday,\r\nrainy, dreary, as always.\r\ndecay...  or nourish?',0,67,0,0,'207.172.166.155','en'),(14378,2438,'2002-02-20 02:32:23',130,'treetop','i loved my slinky\r\nuntil the thing was mangled\r\ntime to recycle',0,63,0,0,'172.161.111.170','en'),(14379,2439,'2002-02-20 02:39:08',130,'treetop','<br>associating poop\r\n<br>with the smell of beefy stew\r\n<br>lost my appetite',0,76,0,0,'172.161.111.170','en'),(14380,2434,'2002-02-20 03:58:55',175,'Paul G','It\\\'s the exchange rate - multiply NZ Dollars by .40 to convert to American Dollars, or multiply American Dollars by 2.5 to convert to NZ Dollars...roughly.  It\\\'s been a little while since I checked.  The $300 Kiwi would equal $120 American?  I have no calculator and a low Quantitative GRE score.  Local supply and demand affects prices of all goods. ',0,357,0,0,'131.156.159.229','en'),(14381,2434,'2002-02-20 04:38:24',64,'Joanna','I suspect it\\\'s mostly to do with supply, what with us being at the bottom of the word and there being very few places that manufactor here, it all has to be imported which is complicated and difficult and stuff, i guess. \r\n\r\nI don\\\'t really know all that much about drugs, but I do try to be aware of how the world works.  ',1,328,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(14382,2434,'2002-02-20 04:51:03',174,'Mad Moll','<p><b>Mad Moll One-Man Transcendental Party Pack:</b></p>\r\n<p>- rowboat</p>\r\n<p>- Pabst</p>\r\n<p>- Dunhills</p>\r\n<p>- trash bags (for padding/pillows)</p>\r\n<p>- walkman with tape of Fraebbblarnir\\\'s <i>Viltu Bjor Vaena?</i></p>\r\n<p>- bacon & mayo sandwiches</p>\r\n',5,264,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14383,2434,'2002-02-20 04:54:57',65,'le about madmoll','ladies and gentlemen. i present to you, a pisces.',4,49,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14384,2434,'2002-02-20 05:12:38',201,'treetop_dont_like_ham4199275686_11001011101101','<br><br>\r\n<b>The <i>Alabama Slammer</i> Party Pack:<br>\r\n<i>Casually packaged in a jacked up Chevy Silvarado pickup truck</i><br>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>Comb in back pocket for mullet\r\n<LI>Sixer of budwieser\r\n<LI>Fifth of Jim Beam\r\n<LI>Lynyrd Skynyrd\\\'s greatest hits tape\r\n<LI>Your mothers valium perscription\r\n<LI>12 guage shotgun\r\n<LI>Quarter bag of mexican dirt weed\r\n<LI>Two of your cutest cousins</UL><br>',0,421,0,0,'172.133.104.22','en'),(14385,2434,'2002-02-20 05:14:39',130,'treetop','did i just write that?',0,22,0,0,'172.133.104.22','en'),(14386,2438,'2002-02-20 05:29:29',148,'jeeff','they should\\\'ve sold clothes in all combinations of the numerals 5 7 & 9.  i\\\'d love to see a size 975 pair of pants.',0,121,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14387,2432,'2002-02-20 05:35:16',148,'jeeff','transvestite hookers are an interesting breed the whole world over.  i used to work the night shift at the CN tower here in toronto, and my walk home went through their territory.  some nights, packs of them would chase me up the street yelling \\\"tantalizing\\\" things at me.',0,278,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14388,2424,'2002-02-20 05:39:47',148,'jeeff','i could tell you what it looks like to me, but that\\\'d be cheating.  altho i can say that reading the accompanying text is really interesting to me.  it\\\'s cool to get an idea of what\\\'s motivating you in this project.',0,224,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14389,1106,'2002-02-20 05:49:18',148,'jeeff','a weekly reality check would be an interesting type of government dole.  i\\\'d support that.',0,93,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14390,564,'2002-02-20 06:30:44',148,'jeeff','empowered women who foist generalizations on men (and other women) confuse me.  why bring yourself down to the level of a chauvinist?',0,133,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14391,564,'2002-02-20 06:42:42',65,'le','hey jeeff, who are you talking to?',4,34,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14392,2441,'2002-02-20 07:14:42',65,'le','dave, are you sure these women aren\\\'t <a href=\\\"http://www.jesus-is-lord.com/incubus.htm\\\">demons who have intercourse with you while you\\\'re sleeping</a>?',4,164,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14393,2441,'2002-02-20 08:39:50',177,'pony','my favorite part is when they say, \\\'Watching tv can channel spirits right into you.\\\' oh and also, \\\'Forget medieval times and folklore, these demons are for real!\\\'\r\n\r\n<p>[x] <i>destabilizing</i></p>',4,209,65,0,'209.239.207.137','en'),(14394,2429,'2002-02-20 09:00:52',128,'Javorai','thanx for the comments...\r\n</br>\r\nwhen I read poetry I like to feel the rhythm of the lines, and as the words sound in mi mind they might turn into images, or they might just mean what they are saying.  sometimes it is just a person you cannot see who is shouting things at you, pieces of things, and you have to put all of these together.  and sometimes someone might overwhelm you with his/her prose.  anything the writer feels is reflected in whatever he/she is writing, may it be honesty, indifference, sadness, joy, ignorance, hate, stupidity, genius, etc., and that is the beauty of the art of writing words.  even if you cut letters from a newspaper, spill glue on a page and drop the letters there the outcome reads like a map.',0,735,0,0,'129.130.231.136','en'),(14395,2340,'2002-02-20 11:19:34',200,'chiclet','at shoney\\\'s, the grits were multidimensional.  the options presented by the self-service breakfast bar seemed endless.  why not have grits with salt and butter and tabasco for the main course and follow up with a lovely dessert of grits with maple syrup?  you _can_ have your grits and eat them too.',0,302,0,0,'207.172.166.44','en'),(14396,2340,'2002-02-20 13:29:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no, its a matter of principle. you simply cannot eat grits with maple syrup.</tt>',1,89,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14397,2441,'2002-02-20 13:33:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\\\"the flesh is WEAK\\\"</tt> ',1,39,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14398,2441,'2002-02-20 13:34:33',66,'um','the following part really makes me wanna go join up... <i>convert and increase your risks of demon-rape by 90%!</i>:<br><br>\r\n\r\n\\\"I know there are countless women that this (demons sexually abusing them) is happening to, because every Christian woman I have spoken to about it (sex demons), 9 out of 10 it has happened to.\\\" NINE out of TEN!! \r\n',1,349,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14399,2433,'2002-02-20 13:36:18',67,'trouble','paul, i really dig these family pieces.  you have a real way of recalling the details that make up complicated family relationships.  it\\\'s refreshing to get different views on what i\\\'ve come to believe are universal feelings about the people we love and loathe.',1,267,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14400,2439,'2002-02-20 14:52:52',123,'dcc','reminds me of a previous feline i shared an apartment with. (I\\\'d say *my* cat, but it definitely wasn\\\'t, thankfully).i\\\'ve never smelled poo that was so vile coming from any creature ever. it would literally clear the room every time a dump would be laid. i can\\\'t describe the smell. i\\\'m glad i can\\\'t.',0,318,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14401,2432,'2002-02-20 15:06:53',123,'dcc to jeeff','but which is better: transvestites or old ladies?',0,49,148,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14402,2435,'2002-02-20 15:13:05',123,'dcc','i must be some sort of guy-freak then. i can\\\'t wear the same pair of pants 2 times in a week.',0,96,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14403,2441,'2002-02-20 16:34:19',93,'chiamattt','thanks for all that insight. ',5,29,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14404,2441,'2002-02-20 16:50:36',93,'sane person','chiamattt you are a loser. ',0,27,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14405,2441,'2002-02-20 16:54:24',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im <i>sane</i> and i think chia is neat.<br><br>also: when oh when is the novelty of posting unnecessary <i>mean shit</i> going to wear off? mister faq says: <i>swinney.org is not a place to come if you define yourself by the ability to kick others down, or kick them when they are down. also, dont come here to be annoying. be thoughtful. be challenging. do not be a prick.</i></tt>',1,391,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14406,2441,'2002-02-20 17:05:24',148,'jeeff','<p>i don\\\'t have a whole lot of insight to add (join the club) but i\\\'ve never ever had sexual dreams.  until recently, and they\\\'re slowly ramping up in intensity.  oh boy!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>last night tho, i dreamt about playing on the harlem globe trotters.  thats more status quo for me.</p>',0,293,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14407,2432,'2002-02-20 17:07:34',148,'jeeff','i\\\'ll take the old ladies any day, at least i have a fighting chance with them.',0,81,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14408,2441,'2002-02-20 17:10:12',67,'trouble','regarding the mean spirited comments, i thought you had to log in anymore to comment.  does this mean that somebody went through the trouble of creating a name just to bash a post or person?  confusion.',1,202,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14409,2433,'2002-02-20 17:48:13',175,'Paul G','Thank you so much.  I was convinced that this was another \\\"zero comment\\\" piece.  The writing isn\\\'t terribly subtle or rich - but then again neither were the people about whom I wrote.\r\n\r\n<p>I think that there is a lot to be said for the social orders of \\\"more primitive\\\" people in which contact across the generations is the norm.  Young men especially need to know that certain behavior isn\\\'t acceptable and will result in sanction by the group.  They need to have models - when they\\\'re left to create their own rules, bad things happen.</p> ',0,564,0,0,'65.230.72.35','en'),(14410,2441,'2002-02-20 17:50:26',82,'simon','i\\\'m a big chiamatte fan myself but i\\\'m not sure if i\\\'d say i\\\'m a totally sane person.',0,97,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14411,2433,'2002-02-20 17:53:49',82,'simon','i agree with trouble. also, when i read this, i had some thoughts about it but i couldn\\\'t exactly come up with the right comment for it.',0,139,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14412,2441,'2002-02-20 18:14:33',83,'adamajah','hi chia',0,7,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14413,2441,'2002-02-20 18:23:50',177,'pony','well apparently someone was bored enough to create an account under <b>dictionary.com</b> just so they post as the grammar police.',1,130,67,0,'209.239.208.23','en'),(14414,2441,'2002-02-20 18:26:15',93,'chiamattt to sane','hi! ',0,4,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14415,2433,'2002-02-20 18:52:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hi. sometimes no comments means the piece left people speechless, you know... :)</tt>',1,93,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14416,2433,'2002-02-20 19:04:19',67,'trouble','that\\\'s pretty much what i was going to add.  we all read dozens of articles on this site.  some leave us unsure of how best to word what we feel and in my case i often opt not to say anything if i feel my words would come out confusing or possibly be taken out of context.  your pieces click with me and i\\\'m pretty clear on how i feel about them because the last 20 days or so it seems as though all i do is analyze my relationships with my family and the history within our clan. *nice supportive*',1,504,0,0,'216.82.34.12','en'),(14417,2442,'2002-02-20 19:15:14',123,'dcc','<i> ... and your mind just spins its wheels in the hopes of latching onto some thought that would follow you into dreams.</i>\r\n\r\njust beautiful words. thank-you for bringing such beauty into my afternoon.',1,204,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14418,2443,'2002-02-20 19:19:20',123,'dcc','want to buy an american flag?',0,29,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14419,2443,'2002-02-20 19:27:19',93,'chiamattt','make shift patriot, the flag shop is out of stock. ',0,51,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14420,2441,'2002-02-20 19:30:17',82,'hey dude','hi',0,2,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14421,2441,'2002-02-20 19:39:54',74,'jo','by the way, in case anyone was wondering, that dictionary.com alias wasn\\\'t mine. i may be a stickler for grammar, but i\\\'m not a fascist. i really just came in this little white box to tell you all that chia and peter both make me smile. taureans and cancers, rah! also i just had really excellent mediterranean food and i\\\'m full of happiness.<BR>\r\n[x] destabilizing.',4,375,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14422,2443,'2002-02-20 20:31:32',123,'dcc','not this shop.',0,14,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14423,2443,'2002-02-20 21:09:59',185,'orion','seen recently: \\\"Where was your flag on Sept. 10?\\\"  Heh.  So true.  These \\\"well now I guess I\\\'m a patriot\\\" people make me sick.  Patriotism does not come from a piece of cloth, it comes from within.  True patriotism requires not only support of the state but the willingness to honestly and openly examine it\\\'s doings, to change it for the better if need be.  Just my bitter two hundredths of a USD.',4,416,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14424,2443,'2002-02-20 22:12:42',154,'ryan','i like the germans:<p>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://www.saturn5.com/ryan/bush-derspiegel.jpg\\\">',6,89,0,0,'216.26.62.63','en'),(14425,2441,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i\\\'m reconsidering my opinion of chiamatt after he didn\\\'t vote for my cousin, but some other bikini bitch.',3,111,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14426,2441,'2002-02-20 22:33:22',62,'also','it seems you can post as whoever you want to, but it will record what username you were logged in as when you did it.  you dig? so you can still make silly names for yourself, but it can be found out who you are. okay.',3,218,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14427,2441,'2002-02-20 23:24:38',174,'Mad Moll','<p>chia, I think you answer yourself pretty well. You naturally think about having sex with women, or having a future with a woman, or doing other social things with women, and these things are represented in your dreams. But you lack emotional involvement with them, and this absence is reflected as well. The subconscious is like a very creative child that can only access things in a symbolic/experential sort of way -- if that experience isn\\\'t there, it\\\'s most natural to draw a blank. If I were you, though, I would be less concerned about this absence and more concerned about the presence of anger towards other people and things.</p>\r\n<p>Speaking of anger, though, I don\\\'t think there\\\'s anything wrong with calling someone a loser or a dickhead in response to a post. It\\\'s a honest reaction, no? A little more explanation would be nice, but so what?</p>\r\n',6,878,93,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14428,2441,'2002-02-20 23:43:43',203,'insane','ima smash some bottle in your face. let the chase begin.',0,56,93,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14429,2441,'2002-02-20 23:44:23',203,'insane to sane','i\\\'ll find you.',0,17,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14430,2441,'2002-02-20 23:46:04',203,'insane to insane','i\\\'m talking to you dicknose.',0,31,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14431,2441,'2002-02-21 00:07:37',93,'chiamattt','insane is my friend. LAND HO!!!',0,31,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14432,2443,'2002-02-21 05:35:00',175,'Paul G','With all due respect, not everyone who had a flag flying was a warmonger or jingoist.  Thousands of people died hideous deaths quite suddenly one day last September.  Many of them wore a flag on their shoulders in the form of a patch: firemen, paramedics, policemen, et al.  Now, if you or your friends or anyone in your family happened to be in that line of work, then flying a flag was a sign or remembrance; it was a memorial.  No, it didn\\\'t really fix anything.  Yes, that type of mass demonstration can be used to \\\"make political hay.\\\"  But, it means a lot to people to know that their loss is felt by their communities and countrymen.  I\\\'m so torn about this issue.  I know well the sort of herd-like behavior that is the subject of fear and scorn.  And, I depend upon the exchange between the Left and the Right in order to preserve my own freedom.  But, there is another part of me that wants to make it very clear that this is bigger than you, or me, or any one person.  Sometimes, don\\\'t you need to say: \\\"Just back off - don\\\'t fuck with it.\\\"  Go find out what someone who was burned over most of their body looks and smells like before you sound off.  I\\\'m not saying anything at all about what we are doing in other parts of the world.  Just stop for a second, and think about the families here.     ',0,1337,0,0,'207.220.191.81','en'),(14433,2442,'2002-02-21 06:32:57',175,'Paul G','That warm, moist air is always pregnant with memories - it touches your skin, ready to deliver.  The Spring is coming...',0,120,0,0,'205.184.176.155','en'),(14434,2446,'2002-02-21 06:37:44',175,'Paul G','I wish that I was a kid again...',0,32,0,0,'205.184.176.155','en'),(14435,2447,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>once in melbourne i was on a tram watching a scene between a two women and a child i estimate to be about 3 or 4.  the woman was completely abusing the child while he screamed and cried, telling him that he was worthless, pathetic, naughty and horrible, that his mother didn\\\'t love him, and that if she was his mother that she would abandon him.  the other woman sat and laughed as the insults got worse and the child screamed louder.  every time the crying got louder, the woman took it as her cue to ramp up the quality of the insults and the other woman would laugh even more.</p>\r\n<p>eventually we had to get off the tram because the scene had made my friend and i start to cry.  everyone else on the tram was watching with horror.  nobody said anything.</p>',4,768,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14436,2447,'2002-02-21 06:58:05',175,'Paul G','God it is so fucking horrible.  This is the other part of life that no one has the stomach to deal with.  What the fuck do you do to fix things?  You can\\\'t, can you...\r\n\r\n<p>Some of these people make cages for the kids.  They lay there and get sores on their bodies.  They have insect crawling over them.  The kids get brought to the hospital at less than their birth weight months after they are born.  I don\\\'t know what to think except that their parents should be killed.  Go ahead, someone: try and defend the parents.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Are there any more innocent victims?  Are there any more un-natural victimizers? </p>\r\n\r\n<p>What is ever going to make things right for that little girl?  God this sucks so hard.  This hurts so much.</p>',0,742,0,0,'205.184.176.155','en'),(14437,2445,'2002-02-21 07:42:40',175,'Paul G','Sinister - dexter.  You just talk about what\\\'s in your heart.\r\n\r\n<p>You write many nice things.</p>',0,102,0,0,'205.184.176.155','en'),(14438,2449,'2002-02-21 07:55:28',185,'orion','wot?  teriyaki seems like a happy enough occurence to be repeated... ;-)\r\nI know just how you feel though.  The past two or three weeks have been rough at work for me.  Long, boring projects that seem to grind by sooooo slloowwllyy i could just throw something to hear a noise...',1,279,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14439,2449,'2002-02-21 08:40:32',59,'s5','i\\\'m afraid to find out what\\\'s inside the briefcase.',4,57,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14440,2447,'2002-02-21 13:21:15',151,'stella','<p.What is perhaps the sickest part of this story, is that Amy got pregnant on purpose, in the midst of drug addiction, because she thought that it would make the Dad stop using. Of course, she didn\\\'t consider that *she* was also doing her share of meth, pot, ecstacy and coke. She also didn\\\'t consider that 22 was too young to be married and having children, if you\\\'re obviously not ready. She also didn\\\'t didn\\\'t consider that it would be a bad idea to have a child if you really didn\\\'t want one. </P><P>\r\nThe poor child was an object for manipulation from the beginning, she was planned that way. </P>\r\n<P> It\\\'s certainly a case for parent licensing, isn\\\'t it?</P>',0,690,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14441,2447,'2002-02-21 13:24:41',151,'stella','<p>Er, what in the world happened? Most of my post was cut off</P><P>\r\n\r\nThat was supposed to begin saying:\r\nThe sickest thing about this whole story was that Amy decided to get pregnant on purpose, to try and get the husband/father to stop doing drugs. Not considering that she was doing her fair share of speed, pot, coke and weed. She apparently didn\\\'t consider that getting married and having a child at 22 wasn\\\'t a good idea, in you obviously weren\\\'t ready to be married and have a child. But then, she also didn\\\'t consider that having a baby when she really didn\\\'t want one wouldn\\\'t be a great idea either. </P>',0,635,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14442,2446,'2002-02-21 13:38:53',156,'lotus','<nostalgic sigh> i had a very happy childhood until i was about 8.',0,66,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14443,2449,'2002-02-21 13:39:50',156,'lotus','excellente!\r\nyou take a good photo, and make it better.',0,55,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14444,2443,'2002-02-21 14:13:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>with maximum respect to those who arent, of course, <i>a <b>lot</b></i> of nyc cops are still assholes.  the city\\\'s hero-phile perspective has begun to seriously wither as people start remembering the cops who beat up their brother, the cops who vaguely arrested a friend for no reason, the cops that hit on their girlfriend, the cops that push them around, the cops that wolf-whistle at business women in midtown, the cops who are so very close to the line of being a criminal themselves that the line is only distinguisable by a shiny badge...<br><br>im one of those folks who tends to give police officers respect by default; thats how i was raised, and it sure seems to be the appropriate way to treat them if a situation arises. all the cops i met growing up in a smal southern town generally had hearts of gold. unfortunately, this isnt the case for probably 7/10 of nyc cops.</tt>',1,898,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(14445,2441,'2002-02-21 14:24:42',174,'Mad Moll','i\\\'llcutyou',6,13,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14446,2445,'2002-02-21 15:22:32',155,'blue stocking','thank you very much for reading, and especially for responding. it is good to know someone is listening.\r\n<br>\r\n(i\\\'m a feedback junkie)',0,138,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14447,2443,'2002-02-21 15:57:23',176,'Scapegoat','I agree with Chia about the stock market needing to be regulated somehow. I\\\'m not a hardcore mercantilist or anything but some of the developments in the last couple of years have been ridiculous. The other issue when ever you are talking about high tech stocks is that you have to consider the fact that with network economics that artificial inflation of value is something that many folks strove towards. Especially with new products a strong stock price was part of putting forward a strong public image that could make or break products, the whole issue of making people think the product is going to be the industry standard. Once a product was established as a standard then the artificial stock inflation could be cut back.<br><br>\r\nOn issues of patriotism - regardless of country I feel that true patriots will be questioning and applying a critical eye to things more now then ever. I\\\'ve been burned by a couple of people due to this attitude, usually by people that weren\\\'t very aware of things going on in the world pre 9/11. I then point out to them that my family is part of the military thats over there now and we tend to take our politics very seriously. This usually puts a stop to the attacks on my critical view on things. Without a critical view we won\\\'t do things right when we are over there and it <b>will</b> come back to haunt us. I guess on a personal level I am just a little put off by people that due to events have felt a need to spout off about things when they were apolitical before hand and haven\\\'t taken anytime to bring themselves up to speed on all the details. Blind patriotism bothers me as much as blind faith.',0,1666,0,0,'64.229.166.113','en'),(14448,2441,'2002-02-21 16:11:01',93,'chiamattt','i didnt vote for anyone <b>o</b>. i dont vote for pit crew girls. ',0,66,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14449,2447,'2002-02-21 16:17:12',176,'Scapegoat','This sadly reminded me of several children I knew during highschool. My hometown seemed to have more then its fair share of children in this position.',0,150,0,0,'64.229.164.140','en'),(14450,2443,'2002-02-21 16:18:20',93,'chiamattt to peter','',0,0,0,0,'66.185.84.79','en'),(14451,2443,'2002-02-21 16:21:41',93,'chiamattt to paul g','<p>\r\nok. i dont quite understand what you are saying. there are police officers, firefighters, and paramedics all over the world. they have dangerous jobs. they live. they die.\r\n</p>',0,182,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14452,2449,'2002-02-21 17:58:33',82,'simon','that pretty much sums up how i feel about work these days as well. replace the teriyaki place with a big smelly yeast factory and that\\\'s my walk to work.',0,156,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14453,2443,'2002-02-21 19:05:25',129,'moebius','<br>so last night, chia, there was a special program on the public radio over here that pretty much expands on your article and runs with it (or so it seemed) for an hour. it\\\'s called \\\"Blind Faith: What Enron Says About America,\\\" and you can listen to all of it (plus hear extended segments of certain parts) <a href=\\\"http://marketplace.org/features/blind_trust/\\\">here</a>. it\\\'s worth checking out--some of the commentaries are pretty incisive, taking on Enron, our corrupt financial system, and the greed that drives it. i came in at the end, and intend to listen to the rest over the next few days. (and here\\\'s another good program from PBS Frontline: <a href=\\\"http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/dotcon/\\\">Dot Con</a>.)<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'ve nothing substantial to add to your statement, really, except that i totally agree. at first it was wonderful, all this magic prosperity, but after awhile you knew a price would have to be paid. i\\\'m not surprised by this recession at all.<br><br>if only certain friends of mine could lose their anger at missing out on their chance to become a millionaire. it was always a gamble, pals. now it\\\'s back to real life again. ',1,1204,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14454,2449,'2002-02-21 19:14:19',129,'moebius','<br>...and if you replace this photo with a shot of two tired guys driving cars in opposite directions on the freeway, you will have tapped into my experience perfectly. sigh. would much rather walk to work instead.<br><br>excellent piece.',1,239,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14455,2450,'2002-02-21 20:08:14',93,'chiamattt','very nice. i liked that a lot. :)',1,33,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14456,2449,'2002-02-21 20:39:40',123,'dcc','<p>i also would like to be able to just walk to work. instead i am jammed into a stuffy subway car or streetcar with uptight business-people and smelly street-people.</p>\r\n<p>some days its amazing to revel in the pure humanity of it; overheard conversations, seeing that glimpse of someone with an amazing sense of fashion. others, like today, wet and grey, it\\\'s just pure torture.</p>\r\n<p>lately i\\\'ve been pocketing my gameboy as distraction, although i feel like there is something missing from my commute when i can\\\'t fully experience the sights and sounds and smells.</p>',0,584,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14457,2449,'2002-02-21 21:01:08',206,'josta','i do the same thing on the bus. it always feels like cheating, but galaga makes infinitely more sense to me than conversations that are basically two people just rephrasing \\\"[insert person here], like, sucks\\\" over and over to each other. \r\nhool, all of your pieces are rad and this one was no exception.',2,309,123,0,'206.187.150.21','en'),(14458,2441,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh, okay then. in that case, ROCK ON CHIAMATT!!',3,47,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14459,1138,'2002-02-21 21:19:07',123,'dcc to auntie','but divorce does allow people to live happily ever after.',0,57,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14460,2449,'2002-02-21 23:59:42',122,'hool','<p>it is very likely that these two guys were walking to their cars (it was later afternoon) to drive home.  downtown ottawa is like that for parking.  i hope, for their sakes (and the environments) that they were indeed just walking straight home. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>currently i\\\'m in school, and not working, but i\\\'ve put in over 2 years already of hardcore high-tech brain whoring.  when i did work in this city, i\\\'d have to take a 1.5 hour bus ride each way out to awfully manicured suburbs to the tech sector.  i always felt more and more defeated by the repetition.  some people find comfort in repetition; i find comfort in choosing what i repeat.  <i>this is not for me</i> is the mantra that seemed to come out of it all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>odd.  come end of april, i will have a computer science degree from a highly respected university program, and a killer resume with all the right buzzwords (mostly to do with wireless), and i have absolutely no desire to work in high tech in that context, ever again.  i\\\'m not complaining - i\\\'m actually quite happy to have come to this realization sooner rather than later. </p> \r\n\r\n<p>i will still use a computer as an income generation tool, (among many other things) but i <i>need</i> to have the joy of my imagination be part of it.  i\\\'m starting to set things that way, and places like swinney are a great help in motivation.  thanks to everyone who sends their support, and just as much thanks to those who provide substantial disagreement.</p>\r\n\r\n',0,1505,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14461,2450,'2002-02-22 00:10:19',122,'hool','<p>very sad and very well written.  i\\\'ve been in that situation, like may other people.  i felt that vague dread in my stomach form an acute lump in my throat while i read this, same feeling i got when it actually happened to me.  i\\\'d offer advice, but i don\\\'t really think it\\\'s necessary; if you can write about the experience this well, you\\\'re already well on your way to recovering from it.</p>',0,412,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14462,2451,'2002-02-22 01:24:50',166,'heathen','are these hall & oates lyrics?',0,30,0,0,'66.87.85.129','en'),(14463,2432,'2002-02-22 03:10:10',124,'gus','You paid too much for those silk pants.\r\n<p>\r\nLadymen are strangely accepted as almost a third gender in thailand. Some of the prettiest women in the world are the men in Bangkok.',0,179,0,0,'207.217.47.50','en'),(14464,2450,'2002-02-22 03:35:42',123,'dcc','thank you for the concern and compliments hool, but, it is a fictional account. sorry to disappoint, but i really just have an active imagination.',0,146,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(14465,2451,'2002-02-22 04:58:40',66,'Peter','<br><tt>everybody wants to be <i>hollywood</tt>',1,47,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14466,2454,'2002-02-22 06:17:01',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i have never been to southern california; from what pictures and first-hand accounts ive encountered, i think it sounds like a lovely locale. terrain and topology sculpting a true <i>place</i> that is all its own, in the landscape.</tt>',1,244,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14467,2453,'2002-02-22 06:31:50',122,'hool','this was good. did you like working in high tech?',0,49,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14468,2452,'2002-02-22 06:35:05',174,'Mad Moll','<p>What?</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t think this has much to do with those who are victims or members of their families.</p>\r\n<p>It has to do with the millions of people who aren\\\'t.</p>\r\n<p>That\\\'s his entire point, no?</p>',6,218,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14469,2453,'2002-02-22 06:36:36',59,'s5','this post was really funny and good.',3,36,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14470,2453,'2002-02-22 06:40:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt>this is good. ahh, the job market, the job market. i got fired from a freelance job today because someone searched my client\\\'s site for \\\"shit fuck\\\" and the query popped up on his <i>most recent searches</i> list. how quaint. he told me it was <i>\\\"tawdry\\\"</i>, and that i should have somehow prevented such things from happening.</tt>',1,356,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14471,2443,'2002-02-22 06:46:36',174,'Mad Moll','I\\\'m not a patriot because I care about firefighters, their kids, their puppies, their calendars.<br>\r\nI\\\'m a patriot because my country can and will mess your country up even when it doesn\\\'t have to.<br>\r\nI\\\'m also a patriot it\\\'s the Right Thing to Do.<br>\r\nGood day. And God Bless.<br>',6,299,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14472,2452,'2002-02-22 06:51:02',122,'hool','your argument seems to be based out of context.  and the workers who died deserve the same remembrance that rescue workers who\\\'ve died in non terrorist related events receive.',0,178,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14473,2452,'2002-02-22 06:52:29',59,'s5','i believe it\\\'s possible to feel compassion for the individuals while recognizing the absurdity of the surrounding situation. it doesn\\\'t have to be one or the other.',3,170,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14474,2453,'2002-02-22 06:54:40',62,'priest','<p>twenty hail marys and a promise never to do it again.  absolution guaranteed.  move along.  next!</p>',4,104,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14475,2453,'2002-02-22 07:26:08',177,'pony','i am going nail nails.',4,22,0,0,'209.239.208.30','en'),(14476,2450,'2002-02-22 07:47:01',184,'doug','i liked this.  although fictional it portrays what you want it to do very well.  made me think.',0,95,0,0,'24.141.198.82','en'),(14477,2452,'2002-02-22 08:46:46',175,'Paul G','This bit of writing began as a response to the suggestion that everyone who displayed an American flag was a jingoist. There are people who have said that every home with a flag flying contains a family that is salivating at the thought of the human blood that is to be spilled in the American response to the events of last September. \r\n\r\n<p>When I tried to point out that the flag might be flown as a type of memorial - that is to say in an apolitical manner - at least one fellow displayed what seemed to be a rather callous disregard for the lives of emergency service workers.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>It really does seem to me that people, in elected office and in the general population, have appropriated the meaning of the event in order to advance their own agendas.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>To say that the people who died, \\\"had it coming,\\\" because of their national origin or occupation or whatever is really insensitive, I think. My point, or my most important point anyway, was that everyone who died had real, human connections - and they themselves deserved the same concern as any other human would... </p>\r\n\r\n<p>The events of last September were different: quantitatively and qualitatively, than anything that preceded them.  Isnâ€™t there a difference between dying in a fire that was started accidentally as opposed to dying in one that was started as a result of an act of arson?  In the case of the arson, but for the actions of a man or men the death would not have occurred.  The idea that so many people would have died in a single event that did not need to occur, in an undeclared war, on American soil, is a novel thing.   \r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know what to do about any of this. I don\\\'t have any wonderful solutions.</p> \r\n\r\n<p>I just wish that people would stop and think about the human cost of it all, and also recognize the complexity of the situation.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Yes: innocent life is a thing to be protected - wherever it is. And, the money that was raised following the events of last September has proven to be a real problem.  And the other good causes that have been neglected are another source of tension.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We\\\'re a republic: we elect people to represent us, and we don\\\'t deal well with subtleties. We, the people, tend to rally behind big, simple ideas.  Whether the ideas are pro-war, or anti-war, we have a habit of falling in-line behind them and moving with the herd.  I\\\'m not too sure where that\\\'s going to take us this time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>p.s. Yay for s5: â€œfeel compassion for the individuals while recognizing the absurdity of the surrounding situation.â€  Yes, I think thatâ€™s itâ€¦</p>\r\n',0,2624,0,0,'65.230.72.24','en'),(14478,2445,'2002-02-22 10:57:27',200,'chiclet','just read all of your posts and really enjoyed them.  lush words and imagery, evocative.',0,88,0,0,'207.172.166.251','en'),(14479,228,'2002-02-22 12:17:25',59,'s5','i dig red bars. like the place we went to in santa cruz. red means trouble and red bars mean even bigger trouble. except santa cruz cancels it out, somehow.',1,156,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14480,228,'2002-02-22 12:50:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the red bar round these parts is <i>madame x</i>; it is interesting only because it bears an uncanny resemblance to <a href=\\\"http://www.twinpeaks.org/archives/images/jpgs/TheWaitingRoom.jpg\\\">the waiting room</a>, a\\\'la twin peaks. the <i>holland bar</i>, a seedy dive near the Port Authority bus terminal is strikingly similar to the <a href=\\\"http://www.twinpeaks.org/archives/images/jpgs/pinkroom-3.jpg\\\">pink room</a></tt>',1,445,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14481,2445,'2002-02-22 14:01:11',155,'blue stocking','oh! merci beaucoup chiclet! *happy*',0,35,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14482,2453,'2002-02-22 14:06:19',155,'blue stocking','i love reading pieces where it feels like you\\\'re actualy listening to the person talk.</P><P> i\\\'m in high tech right now (hi hool!) and it is hilarious. my mom was freaking out \\\'cause i wore jeans and beach flip flops to work my first day at a new job. she was shocked \\\'is that how a professional dresses these days?\\\' and i had to explain how i could wear my pyjamas for all they care. she still doesn\\\'t believe me. anyway, i look forward to all the stories in your future career.',0,498,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14483,2452,'2002-02-22 15:33:03',74,'jo','<p>\\\"This bit of writing began as a response to the suggestion that everyone who displayed an American flag was a jingoist.\\\"</p>\r\n<p>who said \\\"everyone?\\\" i\\\'m pretty sure that no one said \\\"everyone.\\\"</p> \r\n<p>furthermore, i don\\\'t know about anyone else\\\'s experience, but the night of september 11th and the first few days afterwards, there was a <b>startling</b> correlation between the people who were flag-crazy and the people who suddenly wanted to kill foreigners about whom they knew nothing.</p>\r\n<p>i think that anger and/or bitterness at sudden patriotism arises because people are skeptical when a huge segment of the population <b>very quickly</b> feels <b>very strongly</b> about anything, because it has historically led to rash decision-making void of serious contemplation, and regret.</p>\r\n<p>paul, no one is saying that they don\\\'t feel for families affected for loss.<BR>\r\nhowever, i <b>do</b> feel anger towards americans who feel as if what happened to us was a tragedy and and the only just reaction is to slaughter thousands of more innocent people. it\\\'s nationalistic, cruel, and xenophobic.</p>',2,1147,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14484,2452,'2002-02-22 16:38:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>re: instantaneous patriotism... maybe its not so much that some folks are jumping on the patriotism-bandwagon so much as maybe the events gave them pause to actually stop and consider things like national pride and social solidarity.<br><br>its kind of like music. yes, some bands are front-runners and in it for the wrong motives, but also some bands that begin explode in popularity and band-wagon fame do so because theyre actually <i>good</i>. just an oversimplified analogy that probably has nothing to do with this discussion.</tt>',1,545,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14485,2452,'2002-02-22 16:48:27',66,'george w. bush','*i* \\\"understand the need to turn it all into a political issue\\\"! ',1,71,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14486,2453,'2002-02-22 17:05:51',209,'deathpants','Good job, now when do I get my vinyl stickers, man?',2,51,0,0,'64.173.140.67','en'),(14487,2452,'2002-02-22 18:20:00',154,'ryan','in the feb 2002 issue of  harpers magazine, in the harpers index on page 11 it states:<p>\r\n<b>estimated percentage change in fuel efficiency of an suv if it flies a small american flag from its antenna: -0.5%</b>',0,212,0,0,'216.26.61.222','en'),(14488,2365,'2002-02-22 19:10:39',123,'dcc','vegans are so tasty.',0,20,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14489,632,'2002-02-22 19:33:51',66,'<pre>yo</pre>','<pre>let me get some action from the back section!</pre>',1,56,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14490,632,'2002-02-22 19:35:35',66,'Peter to austin','<br><tt>we still dont know what the \\\'russian officer\\\' is!</tt>',1,68,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14491,2454,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','it really is. you can say what you want about la, but living there afforded me the opportunity to drive the El through forests, canyons, desert and along ocean-side cliffs.  i miss that.',0,186,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14492,2445,'2002-02-22 21:15:40',88,'deb','i feel so lost, i haven\\\'t been here for so long.  that was nice...',0,69,0,0,'151.198.102.28','en'),(14493,2452,'2002-02-22 21:28:39',175,'Paul G','jo: \\\"...people are skeptical when a huge segment of the population very quickly feels very strongly about anything, because it has historically led to rash decision-making void of serious contemplation, and regret.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>That is a good summary of the Classical argument against democracy: in the Greek, demos kratos is literally mob rule.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>On the one hand, our political rhetoric is filled with references to \\\"the genius of the People\\\" and we champion the principle of a people\\\'s democratic right to self-determination.  On the other hand, we have depended upon action by the Federal government to correct the wrong choices that people have made e.g. slavery and discrimination, in the domestic context.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>There are repressive and anti-democratic regimes in place in many states in the world.  On the one hand, if we deal with or support them that violates our commitment to democracy.  On the other hand, is it possible to entertain the notion that some of the choices that the people of those states would make if they were suddenly \\\"free\\\" would be as wrong as the choices that we made when we held slaves?  If a particular people chooses to practice female genital mutilation do we need to respect their choice?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If we accept that the people should do whatever they choose to do, then we need to accept human slavery within our own borders.  On the other hand, if we accept that sometimes there is a \\\"moral imperative\\\" to act, then we need to be ready to consider the possibility that our Federal government will engage in military action that appears to be anti-democratic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>That is the argument, as I see it.  The one thing that I try to remember in all of this is that the fighting and dying is being done by real people.  The coverage of the events of last September was not so very different than that which the Pentagon shares when it conducts operations.  Like the video of military targets being destroyed that has become a part of every press conference, so too the video of the WTC focused on the BUILDINGS collapsing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If we hope that any sort of positive resolution is going to be achieved, then we would do well to remember the humanity of the people, here and there.  It\\\'s terribly complex, and some forms of expression actually weaken the good arguments that they are associated with.</p>       ',0,2388,0,0,'65.229.226.185','en'),(14494,2446,'2002-02-22 21:38:33',88,'deb','Until you were 8?  I suppose we could all point out some sort of turning point...good or bad.',4,93,0,0,'151.198.102.28','en'),(14495,2447,'2002-02-22 21:43:56',88,'deb','A woman called our office to ask a question. Her son was screaming in the background because he wanted some type of toy or food.  She told us to wait while she tried to \\\"shut her son up\\\" and then told us that \\\"the little shit obviously wasn\\\'t going to shut up so we\\\'d have to talk over him.\\\"\r\n\r\nDays later, after she hadn\\\'t gotten things her way, she called and started crying about how her son was going to have surgery and wanted to know how she was going to handle this and that.\r\n\r\nI suppose it depends on the mood of the parent.  Don\\\'t they say that all parents love their kids, even when it doesn\\\'t seem like it?',0,645,0,0,'151.198.102.28','en'),(14496,2452,'2002-02-22 21:44:07',154,'ryan','where\\\'s \\\"there\\\"?\r\n<p>\r\nwe seem to be fighting a war against a certain \\\"tactic\\\" (i.e. terrorism) rather than an actual enemy.\r\n<p>\r\nit\\\'ll be great for the defense stocks though!',0,194,0,0,'216.26.60.243','en'),(14497,228,'2002-02-22 22:08:12',177,'pony','santa cruz....the red room. yes. where the bartenders are surly and the drinks are stiff. i also like the debutante pics on the walls. nothing like wearing a ballgown with a tiara in a place like the redroom.',4,208,59,0,'209.239.202.245','en'),(14498,2452,'2002-02-22 22:17:52',176,'Scapegoat','It seems to me that there isn\\\'t a whole lot of apolitical grieving. I think responses to the events become politicized so easily because people are looking for reasons behind what happened and that tends to make people think in political terms. If the grieving where to be completely apolitical without any allusion towards political structures such national identity and political movements than what makes this different from the other global tragedies as far as grieving is concerned. If people grieved apolitically then we would have had similar cultural responses to genocide in Rwanda, Bosnia or other things like Bhopal, India. The response to Sept. 11th was different then the response to these things. I\\\'m not saying that I like the fact that things are like this but it just seems to be the reality to me. Maybe I\\\'m out to lunch on this one I don\\\'t know. I think there is a big difference between having a political response to the events and trying to politically capitalize on them though. I think that the notion that the war is against a tactic is a little odd though, I don\\\'t think that would ever be won. Trying to deal with specific groups that are willing to employ said tactics, makes more sense to me. This being said when ever anybody loses someone close it sucks.   ',3,1303,0,0,'64.229.164.140','en'),(14499,2452,'2002-02-22 22:35:33',148,'jeeff','<p>i think its incredibly naive to think a government acts according to some kind of mythical \\\"moral imperative\\\" even in the most extreme circumstances.  where is the evidence for that?  if you look at the big picture, a state acts like an animal.  it tries to prolong its own existence in a struggle for survival on a macro scale.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>ranging from WWII up to the present, i can think of countless times that america has rejected so-called \\\"moral\\\" actions if they weren\\\'t necessary for the survival of the state.  the western world ignored genocide in bosnia, after also ignoring genocide in germany, china and cambodia.  did the extermination of jews, or the rape of nanking drive america into WWII?  no, an attack on american soil did.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>a country enshrined in democracy will violate its own ethical guidelines and deal with non-democratic neighbours if the prize is big enough.  after WWII, america buried evidence of japanese experimentation and vivisections of live humans by dr. shiro ishii of unit 731 in ping fang, china, in order to reap the economic benefits of those experiments.  there\\\'s never been any restitution.  other non-democratic partners include libya, saudi arabia, cuba before fidel castro, iran, guatemala, china.  the list goes on and on.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my point is this:  countries are not run on ethics.  america is not in afghanistan because of ethics, it is there to eliminate a threat.  whether that is right or wrong does not matter, because it was inevitable.  it\\\'s like arguing the ethics of a rain cloud.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>global politics is humanity\\\'s most faithful version of darwin\\\'s \\\"survival of the fittest.\\\"  and if you try to justify your country\\\'s actions according to a magical principle of international altruism, you will drive yourself insane.  same thing for me and canada.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>personally i find world politics in the 20th & 21st centuries to be a laundry list of stupidity and tragedy.  but i live on this planet, and as such, i pretty much have to accept the rules that were layed down before me.  change is slow.  change to human nature is much slower.</p>',0,2149,0,0,'24.42.192.253','en'),(14500,2452,'2002-02-22 22:44:03',74,'jo','<p>paul? you\\\'ve made some pretty giant leaps in both direction and logic throughout this, but taking that comment of mine and making it sound as if being skeptical of mob mentality is anti-democratic is by far the most erroneous and offensive to me.</p>\r\n<p>especially because, no matter how the word democracy translates literally, mob mentality is at the very heart of the most gruesome, evil, tragic acts in history. i\\\'m sorry, but i can\\\'t support any argument that says that stopping to seriously examine a situation rather than just going with the crowd is wrong and anti-democratic.</p>',0,601,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14501,2439,'2002-02-22 22:53:30',124,'gus','my baby kitten farts the stinkiest stinkers. it scares me, it\\\'s so stinky.',0,77,0,0,'207.217.47.41','en'),(14502,2454,'2002-02-22 22:55:22',90,'randy newman','Hate New York City <br>\r\nIt\\\'s cold and it\\\'s damp<br>\r\nAnd all the people dressed like monkeys<br>\r\nLet\\\'s leave Chicago to the Eskimos<br>\r\nThat town\\\'s a little too rugged<br>\r\nFor you and me, you bad girl<br>\r\n<p>\r\nRollin\\\' down the Imperial Highway<br>\r\nWith a big nasty redhead at my side<br>\r\nSanta Ana wind blowin\\\' hot from the north<br>\r\nAnd we was born to ride<br>\r\n<p>\r\nRoll down the window, put down the top<br>\r\nCrank up the Beach Boys, baby<br>\r\nDon\\\'t let the music stop<br>\r\nWe\\\'re gonna ride it till we just can\\\'t ride it no more<br>\r\n<p>\r\nFrom the South Bay to the Valley<br>\r\nFrom the West Side to the East Side<br>\r\nEverybody\\\'s very happy<br>\r\n\\\'Cause the sun is shining all the time<br>\r\nLooks like another perfect day<br>\r\n<p>\r\nI love L.A. (We love it)<br>\r\nI love L.A. (We love it)<br>\r\n<p>\r\nLook at that mountain<br>\r\nLook at that tree<br>\r\nLook at that bum over there, man<br>\r\nHe\\\'s down on his knees<br>\r\nLook at these women<br>\r\nThere ain\\\'t nothin\\\' like em nowhere\r\n<p>\r\nCentury Boulevard (We love it)<br>\r\nVictory Boulevard (We love it)<br>\r\nSanta Monica Boulevard (We love it)<br>\r\nSixth Street (We love it, we love it)<br>\r\n<p>\r\nI love L.A.<br>\r\nI love L.A.<br>\r\n(We love it)<br>',1,1243,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(14503,2441,'2002-02-22 23:08:04',93,'chiamattt','three t\\\'s',0,12,0,0,'66.185.84.79','en'),(14504,2452,'2002-02-22 23:15:39',124,'gus','in any case, is it so bad that a group of people with a common trait feel connected to eachother and so then display the element that stands for their common trait? It happens all the more when tragedy occurrs. jingoist or not.',0,227,0,0,'207.217.47.41','en'),(14505,2453,'2002-02-22 23:23:33',124,'gus','yah, i worked on the internet since 94 as a manager, running some lil departments and then as a big high paid consultant.......... and I just got a job demonstrating high end fancy ovens, mainly by cooking in them and answering questions, to wealthy consumers and/or schmancy oven dealers. woo.',0,294,0,0,'207.217.47.41','en'),(14506,2452,'2002-02-22 23:51:49',59,'s5','during this entire debate, not a single person has stopped to recognize that \\\"jingo\\\" rhymes with \\\"dingo\\\".',4,117,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14507,2452,'2002-02-23 00:43:50',129,'moebius to s5','<br>you\\\'re correct on that account. finally, a comment in this thread i can get behind w/o having to think hard about my response.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nof course, when your average <a href=\\\"http://www.dingosanctuary.org/\\\">dingo dog</a> starts getting riled up about flag worship and foreign policy and such (very rare, i\\\'m told), it\\\'s called <i>dingoism.</i><br>\r\n<br>\r\nbut both social phenomenons seem to bring on the same basic type of yapping and yelping no matter who\\\'s involved.',0,491,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14508,2452,'2002-02-23 01:02:50',175,'Paul G','I am no authority on the political thought of Abraham Lincoln; however, I did refer to the issue of human slavery in the U.S. for a reason.  Some people will say that the American Civil War was fought for economic reasons.  And yet, the records of the people who fought that war would seem to indicate that slavery was the critical issue.  In a private letter to Joshua F. Speed, dated August 24, 1855, Abraham Lincoln wrote the following: \r\n\r\n<p>â€œIn 1841 you and I had together a tedious low-water trip, on a Steam Boat from Louisville to St. Louis.  You may remember, as I well do, that from Louisville to the mouth of the Ohio there were, on board, ten or a dozen slaves, shackled together with irons.  That sight was a continual torment to me; and I see something like it every time I touch the Ohio, or any other slave border.  It is hardly fair for you to assume, that I have no interest in a thing that has, and continually exercises, the power of making me miserable.  You ought rather to appreciate how much the great body of the Northern people do crucify their feelings, in order to maintain their loyalty to the constitution and the Union.â€</p> \r\n\r\n<p>Now, the abolition of human slavery seemed to be a â€œmoral imperativeâ€ to Lincoln.  Remember: that was a private letter.  It wasnâ€™t a speech for political purposes.  In his private letters Lincoln wrestled with Biblical, and purely logical, and legal arguments against slavery â€“ throughout his life; it was obviously something that he felt to be wrong.  And yet, while he was not alone, he was not in the majority.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>This will sound like heresy, but: what is good is not always democratic, and what is democratic is not always good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Again, in my home state of Illinois, Abolitionists were hanged.  They felt that they were agents of morality, but they were not very popular.  Who were they to impose their values on other people?  Why couldnâ€™t they just â€œbe coolâ€ and let things alone?  Well, you know the end of the story: roughly 500,000 combatants died in the war that followed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The U.S. is a republic, and not a democracy, precisely because the Framers of the Constitution feared that decisions would be made rashly, devoid of any serious contemplation, and would lead to regret.  Being skeptical of the mob spirit is a very patriotic thing.  For people like Lincoln, attacks upon the institutions and symbols of our republic constituted the greatest threat to our life as a nation.  He warned of the â€œmobocratic spiritâ€ in his speech on the â€œPerpetuation of Our Political Institutionsâ€ that was delivered on January 27, 1838.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I donâ€™t know if there is enough spirit left in us, as a people, to engage in the same sort of activity as occurred during the Vietnam War.  But, it seems that itâ€™s all too easy to begin to repeat some of the mistakes that were made then â€“ by both sides.  If you see a flag flying and rush to judgment about the person who flies it, then arenâ€™t you as guilty of prejudice as the person who sees a peace sign and rushes to judgment about the person wearing it?  The people who wear uniforms are black and white, gay and straight, male and female, Left and Right, etc.  Just as the nation as a whole is.  Thereâ€™s a complex story behind every one of those people.  Just like thereâ€™s a story behind every American flag, and peace sign, etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Painting with a broad brush doesnâ€™t seem very helpful right now.</p>     \r\n',0,3455,0,0,'65.230.18.252','en'),(14509,2452,'2002-02-23 01:42:32',175,'Paul G','I would like to offer a very sincere apology to all of those whom I have offended with my amateur philosophy or pseudo-psychic inclinations.  I do my best to present the contents of my heart and mind in my discourse; they\\\'re not always easy to understand or pleasant sounding.  \r\n\r\n<p>I do have some distant relatives in Australia, but I am no dingo per se.  And I am certainly no red panda.  Until I learn more about the bandicoot I cannot refute that comparison.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now, I\\\'m going to prepare myself for Tubcat\\\'s arrival, and the real party that\\\'s sure to follow.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Thank you.  Good night.  And may your God go with you.</p>',0,652,0,0,'65.230.18.252','en'),(14510,2452,'2002-02-23 01:55:07',129,'moebius to paul g','<br>well, i, for one, haven\\\'t been offended. it\\\'s actually been fairly entertaining. and lord knows people have seen my dingo moments in this forum and others over the past few months. yip yap!',1,199,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14511,2452,'2002-02-23 02:23:14',175,'Paul G','Poem - by Jan Kemp\r\n\r\n<p>A puriri moth\\\'s wing\r\nlies light in my hand</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my breath can lift it</p>\r\n\r\n<p>light as this torn wing\r\nwe lie on love\\\'s breath.</p>\r\n',0,172,0,0,'65.230.18.240','en'),(14512,2452,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','paul g\\\'s: maybe a little brevity would help me comprehend your arguments?',3,76,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14513,2452,'2002-02-23 03:30:31',59,'s5','i haven\\\'t figured out yet if the two sides of this argument are agreeing, disagreeing, or arguing two completely different things. anyone want to do a recap?\r\n<br><br>\r\nbut i think we all agree that dingos <i>can</i> be domesticated, despite their reputation as baby-eating mongrels. the question remains: <i>should</i> they?',3,328,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14514,2452,'2002-02-23 03:39:14',175,'Paul G','Time for a little Weeb -\r\n\r\n<p>1 - People fly flags for all sorts of reasons.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2 - I hoped to point out that some people left an American flag up, somewhere, as a memorial â€“ their reasons were, essentially, apolitical.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3 - Some of us left flags up because we, or our friends, or our family members worked in the emergency services.  Some people flew flags because they were veterans, or they had friends, or family members in the military.  Some people just didnâ€™t know what else to do.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>4 - I can\\\'t offer up any sort of blanket defense for the actions of every member of every element of the emergency services at all times and in all places.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>5 - But, for that very reason, to make some sort of blanket statement about them, or the people who flew flags to remember them is, in my mind, to be guilty of a horrible form of prejudice.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>6 - Yes: it\\\'s possible for mass demonstrations of any kind to be employed for purposes other than those that the demonstrators intended.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>7 - Clever political leaders might claim that the sudden appearance of so many symbols is a mandate for a certain type of action.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>8 - We\\\'re a republic: we elect people to represent us, and we don\\\'t deal well with subtleties. We, the people, tend to rally behind big, simple ideas. Whether the ideas are pro-war, or anti-war, we have a habit of falling in-line behind them and moving with the herd. I\\\'m not too sure where that\\\'s going to take us this time.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>9 - Everyone has their own pet theory about the course of action that we ought to take, and it often seems to have less to do with this particular circumstance and more to do with their own political views.  It really does seem to me that people, in elected office and in the general population, have appropriated the meaning of the event in order to advance their own agendas.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>10 - This will sound like heresy, but: what is good is not always democratic, and what is democratic is not always good.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>11 - There are repressive and anti-democratic regimes in place in many states in the world. On the one hand, if we deal with or support them we violate our commitment to democracy. On the other hand, is it possible to entertain the notion that some of the choices that the people of those states would make if they were suddenly \\\"free\\\" would be as wrong as the choices that we made when we held slaves? If a particular people chose to practice female genital mutilation do we need to respect their choice?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>12 - If we accept that the people should do whatever they choose to do, then we need to accept human slavery within our own borders. On the other hand, if we accept that sometimes there is a \\\"moral imperative\\\" to act, then we need to be ready to consider the possibility that our Federal government will engage in military action that appears to be anti-democratic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>13 - I just wish that people would stop and think about the human cost of it all, and also recognize the complexity of the situation.  The real story of the events of last September, and of all human conflict, is to be found in the empty spaces of the families who have lost the people that they loved and needed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>14 - My point, or my most important point anyway, was that everyone who died had real human connections - and they themselves deserved the same concern as any other human would...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>15 - I donâ€™t know if there is enough spirit left in us, as a people, to engage in the same sort of activity as occurred during the Vietnam War. But, it seems that itâ€™s all too easy to begin to repeat some of the mistakes that were made then â€“ by both sides. If you see a flag flying and rush to judgment about the person who flies it, then arenâ€™t you as guilty of prejudice as the person who sees a peace sign and rushes to judgment about the person wearing it? The people who wear uniforms are black and white, gay and straight, male and female, Left and Right, etc. Just as the nation as a whole is. Thereâ€™s a complex story behind every one of those people. Just like thereâ€™s a story behind every American flag, and peace sign, etc.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>16 - Is it too much to ask people to just remember the dead and maimed, and set politics aside?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>17 â€“ I am not a dingo.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>BONUS POINT - gus: in any case, is it so bad that a group of people with a common trait feel connected to eachother and so then display the element that stands for their common trait?</p>',0,4531,0,0,'65.229.226.135','en'),(14515,2452,'2002-02-23 04:50:32',177,'pony','ha ha...my favorite part is when he says, \\\'i\\\'m not a dingo\\\'.',4,69,0,0,'209.239.204.23','en'),(14516,2449,'2002-02-23 08:00:02',75,'tyler','hool- that\\\'s a fucking great picture. that would be a terrific poster, and i don\\\'t mean that in a derogatory or diminishing way. i would love to have that on my wall.',1,172,0,0,'165.247.164.108','en'),(14517,2454,'2002-02-23 08:49:31',17,'test','test',5,4,0,0,'64.174.155.238','en'),(14518,2452,'2002-02-23 16:50:55',148,'jeeff','i could argue my point forever because i really do believe in it, but i don\\\'t have much stomache for arguments to begin with.  i don\\\'t expect to change your opinion, and you\\\'re not going to change mine, so i\\\'m not going to try.  one note tho, when i used the words democratic or anti-democratic, i probably should\\\'ve said ethical or unethical.  i don\\\'t believe democracy is any more desirable than most forms of government, but ethical/unethical is such a subjective term.  oh well.',0,500,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14519,2452,'2002-02-23 16:53:05',148,'jeeff','ps. if you really do want to have a nice non-confrontational discussion about it, <a href=\\\"mailto:jeff@jeffpriest.com\\\">email me</a>. ',0,139,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14520,2449,'2002-02-23 18:00:39',122,'hool','<p>i use a digital cam, and i have somewhat high rez digital negs of most of my shots (~2.2 meg jpegs, not as good as an 11 meg TIFF, but well, bah).  and, i\\\'m starting to try to sell prints, so long story short, i could probably send you a 12x18 (max) inch print, assuming you cover the shipping and printing costs, of course. not quite poster sized, but close. right now, not really looking to make any kind of big profit.  </p>\r\n\r\n<p>roughly, for that size, it would be about $30 CDN with shipping. i have a paypal account</p>\r\n\r\n<p>8x10 prints are wayyyyy cheaper @ $4 USD each, and i can have them done totaly automated online, through shutterfly.com, mailed directly to you from them.  i\\\'ve used shutterfly before, and the quality is indistinguishable from traditional analog prints.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>so if you\\\'re interested, or anyone else (in any photos, not just this one), email me (hool@rogers.com). i\\\'d only ask a small amount on top of printing & shipping for my time.</p>',0,990,75,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14521,2452,'2002-02-23 19:56:08',175,'Paul G','<p><b>Jeeff</b>, your argument is not a new one.  You sound like the type of person that people in International Relations would call a <b>Realist</b>.  You seem to want to make me out to be what those same people in <b>I.R.</b> would call an <b>Idealist</b>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Over 2000 years ago <b>Thucydides</b>, in <i>The Peloponnesian War</i>, had his Athenians affirm the role of power in political life, in their dialogue with the Melians: \\\"you know as well as we do that right, as the world goes, is only in question between equals in power, while the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must\\\" (5.89).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I\\\'d like to think that I have something other than opinion; that\\\'s a bit of an insult to anyone friendly with the <b>Socratics</b> - but I know it wasn\\\'t intentional.  Even as the offense that I gave to you or <b>jo</b> was not intentional.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I thought that I was careful to say, again and again, that I don\\\'t have the solution.  And it seemed to me that many people who claimed that they did were simply employing the events of last September to further their own agendas and pet theories.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The only thing that I hoped to achieve with this post was to give people the opportunity to reconsider some of their opinions.  We\\\'re in a terribly complex situation right now.  Everyone involved is a real, feeling human being who is motivated by an incredibly complex set of beliefs and experiences.  Making sweeping generalizations about anyone just isn\\\'t going to help.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Carrying on a private dialogue would have been an act that was contrary to my own purpose.</p>   ',0,1644,0,0,'65.230.18.8','en'),(14522,2452,'2002-02-23 20:30:01',175,'Paul G','<p>This is a good place to <a href=\\\"http://www.loa.org\\\">buy American books.</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>This is a good place to <a href=\\\"http://www.hup.harvard.edu/loeb/author.html\\\">buy Classical books.</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>These guys have some stuff <a href=\\\"http://home.freeuk.net/ethos/theory.htm\\\">on-line.</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>And, I think this is just an <a href=\\\"http://www.utm.edu/research/iep/westtime.htm\\\">overview.</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>There is no substitute for primary sources, or the experience of thinking through them yourself...</p>',0,537,0,0,'65.230.18.8','en'),(14523,2456,'2002-02-23 22:09:15',124,'gus','wait, are you really wondering why the shit jobs dont show up when you search for \\\"shit job\\\"? ',0,100,0,0,'207.217.47.50','en'),(14524,2452,'2002-02-23 23:06:24',65,'le','ugh.',6,4,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14525,2456,'2002-02-24 00:27:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i no longer want to be a doctor; id like to be a hot-dog vendor, please.</tt>',1,85,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14526,2452,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','good lord.',1,10,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14527,2452,'2002-02-24 08:44:16',185,'orion','Paul G.:  Thanks for taking half of what I said out of context, cocklick.  You are a fucktard.  Normally I\\\'d be more eloquent but I\\\'ve been at work 60 of the last 72 hours because of fucking morons, so my moron-tolerance-level is pretty fucking small right about now. BURN! BURN! BURN!',6,291,175,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14528,2453,'2002-02-24 08:51:46',185,'orion','Dude, that <em>sucks</em>! I mean, yeah you could write filters or something, but still, blaming YOU for what J. Random webtwat did is retarded.  Oh well, if the client was that clueless/anal retentive, then hopefully the pain of losing the money will be offset by the joy of not having to talk to them anymore.  (I feel your pain, I once got in trouble becuase somebody grepped through a filesystem looking for \\\"fuck\\\" and found instances of it in the binary mdb file (Cr)A(p)ccess stores its datadrivel in.  a) i didn\\\'t enter that data, b) it had already been DELETE\\\'d, c) how the hell was I supposed to know access never really deletes anything?!?).',1,663,66,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14529,2453,'2002-02-24 08:52:24',185,'orion','that was to Peter.  %^)',1,23,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14530,2456,'2002-02-24 08:55:31',185,'orion','i think flipdog(.com, naturally) does basically what you\\\'re after.  it search\\\'s company\\\'s career/job/blahdyblahblah pages for positions.  Of course, in this job market we all have one position: bent over.',1,213,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14531,2457,'2002-02-24 08:57:47',185,'orion','I think bibimbap rules.  But not as much as yukgaejang.  ;-)  Yeah, the urge for domesticity can be suprisingly strong sometimes.  I find that not only do I miss my fiancee, I miss my roommate a lot too (and of course we were never more intimate than drinking beer and bullshiting together).',1,291,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14532,2457,'2002-02-24 09:00:59',185,'orion','(fiancee is teaching in japan, roomie went to grad school in south africa.  if i somehow had the $$$ to go see them both, that\\\'d be a coverage of, what, 15, 16 time zones?  heh)',1,180,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14533,2457,'2002-02-24 09:33:17',176,'Scapegoat','I hear you on the desire to get domestic. It isn\\\'t something that really seemed to exert itself before in or out of a relationship but now the desire is pounding my psyche with the force of a jack hammer. Dangerous a little but a nice change to always feeling not ready for things. ',0,285,0,0,'64.229.164.140','en'),(14534,2457,'2002-02-24 14:24:53',65,'le','i used to be afraid of being domestic, too. it\\\'s not the domesticy you have to be afraid of tho, it\\\'s the \\\"TO THE EXCLUSION OF ALL ELSE\\\" part of domesticity some (most) couples get trapped in. also, i think after a certain period of interpersonal intensity that most people go through in their early 20s, you start to just get sick of the dramatic shit of being wild and crazy fucking lots of people doing tons of drugs being single, you take what you need from it, and retreat into a more stable and comforting domestic life. i used to resent that, but god, stability kicks ass once you get it sorted out right, i think.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni should go to bed, huh? (6.24am)',4,678,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14535,2457,'2002-02-24 14:27:16',65,'le','also, i know a nice boy who will be in ithaca soon. he enjoys comic books, long walks on the beach, oral sex, and candlelit dinners. he would probably be glad to ignore you over a cup of coffee while reading the paper. drop me an email if you want to know more. ',4,262,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14536,2460,'2002-02-24 15:57:31',122,'hool','this is the last photo text thing for a bit.  next time i\\\'ll actually write a full page of text. imagine that.',0,113,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14537,2419,'2002-02-24 16:34:30',212,'dharmaBum','I use to live below a Russian couple who danced balley.  They spoke almost no english at all. They also had the weird habit of moving furniture at 3am, probably to practice. Once in awhile they would even have loud shoutfests in Russian.  All of this made them interesting neighbors.',0,283,0,0,'24.29.113.80','en'),(14538,2460,'2002-02-24 18:50:46',93,'chiamattt','if it aint broke, dont fix it. ',1,31,0,0,'66.185.84.80','en'),(14539,2457,'2002-02-24 20:27:03',210,'may','today it\\\'s sunny and warm (50 degrees being balmy for ithaca this time of year)\r\ni made myself breakfast and sat on my roof to read the paper all by myself. and thankfully i feel a little less out of sorts.\r\n\r\nbut last night it would have been nice not to have to hug my pillow to death.',0,290,0,0,'128.84.25.22','en'),(14540,2420,'2002-02-24 21:14:20',185,'orion','wrt the male mind, I think that <a href=\\\"http://sinfest.net/d/20011129.html\\\">this</a> sums it up pretty well. ;-)',1,119,74,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14541,2460,'2002-02-24 21:16:52',151,'stella','Yeah right? Like someone was complaining... ',0,44,0,0,'199.97.98.12','en'),(14542,2452,'2002-02-24 22:28:43',151,'stella','s5 can come to my house to play anytime! I laughed so hard, I snorted, and that ALWAYS means that a good time is being had. ',0,124,0,0,'199.97.98.12','en'),(14543,2457,'2002-09-05 18:40:19',18,'ix','<p>\r\nwell it wouldn\\\'t be a newspaper. probably comic books or maybe polyhedron.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhat is gimme? i don\\\'t recognize the name. the only places i ever drank coffee in ithaca were collegetown bagels and short stop.\r\n</p>',0,232,0,0,'209.239.198.121','en'),(14544,2464,'2002-02-25 03:05:51',59,'s5','you should go with your instinct, at all times.',3,47,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14545,2458,'2002-02-25 03:16:55',59,'s5','copulating in the air sounds fucking rad. i wish for wings.',3,59,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14546,2458,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeah, but what would you brace yourself against?  i guess it would be okay if you wanted to make love sweetly, but no good for hardcore fucking.',3,144,59,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14547,2458,'2002-02-25 03:33:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its all about synchronous wing-flapping...</tt>',1,55,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14548,2464,'2002-02-25 03:34:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>they say love can blind. sometimes infatuation does the same thing.</tt>',1,80,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14549,2463,'2002-02-25 03:40:14',196,'jasonp','BTW, the above was from the I Ching Book of Changes, a ancient chinese book of divination based on Taoist and Buddhist philosophy. Formatting and emphasis is my own.<BR><BR>\nContext of the divination -<BR>\n<I>subjective:</I><BR><BLOCKQUOTE>\ncouch, seasonal joint...<BR>\nwow -- trance release<BR>\nlife patterns, who am I?<BR>\npathetic<BR>\nlost<BR>\nwhy?<BR>\nreverie, drifting, astral travel...<BR>\nneighbors talking<BR>\nparanoia, projection<BR>\ndread, anxious constriction<BR>\nput it together: fuck it, so what?<BR>\nget a grip, chill<BR>\nit\\\'s all good<BR>\n</blockquote><BR>\n<i>objective:</i><BR>\n<blockquote>\nUnemployed and liking it after a year on the treadmill, smoking a jay at 2am cuz why the hell not? It hasn\\\'t been part of my routine in the last 5 years. Trippin out. Getting some good insights, but at the same time getting bummed out examining my life as a whole. It\\\'s a strange mixture of pleasure and depression as the fantasy is a kind of release but I\\\'m getting down on myself. It was like a nostalgic tour the ghetto of my life; reconnecting with my younger past, but then remembering what a fucking mess I was, and still am. <BR>\nThen I hear the neighbors talking on the patio below and I\\\'m convinced it\\\'s about me. I blew smoke out the door to avoid later smell, so that must be what got them up and talking. There are two alternate realities in my head: in one they\\\'re all pissed off and uptight outraged at my casually lighting up right out my door, conspiring to complain. In the other it\\\'s just a Saturday night and they\\\'re just loud, loquacious drunks talking about whatever. Logically I deduce that since I cannot make out any coherent sentences or ever words that they\\\'re saying what reason would I have to think it\\\'s about me? Then the flip side: because I just sent over a huge cloud of weed over, because they normally don\\\'t chatter away at 2am outside on their patio, because they sound kind of outraged about something...<BR>\nLaying in bed phrases coming in clear here and there: \\\"he must know\\\", \\\"I can\\\'t believe it\\\". Identity shifting as I try and settle on regressed adolescent, free thinking individualist, weekend hippie wannabe, neurotic basket case, or just some guy trying to take it easy and wants to be left alone.<BR>\nAfter this disturbing mix of dread, insight, anxiety, and escapism I think \\\"enough of this\\\". <BR>\nI get up and throw an I-ching...<BR>\n</blockquote>\n<BR>It put it all into perspective.<BR>',0,2500,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(14550,2463,'2002-02-25 03:50:00',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>[just noticing how comments on here are getting alot longer lately...]</i></tt>',1,90,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14551,2463,'2002-02-25 04:05:07',59,'s5','i wish someone would write about the Tao in a way that\\\'s accessible to people in general, without quoting ancient texts or being self-helpy. maybe fiction.',3,158,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14552,2463,'2002-02-25 04:18:33',66,'Peter','<br><tt>or, you know, those comic-book versions of the great classics.</tt>',1,75,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14553,2463,'2002-02-25 04:28:24',59,'s5','yes! there\\\'s an excellent comic (it\\\'s really a graphic novel, but i can\\\'t bring myself to say <i>graphic novel</i> without irony) about lao-tzu that permanently flipped my switch. i wish i could remember the title.',3,223,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14554,1040,'2002-02-25 05:01:14',59,'s5','chiamattt has been growing on me lately. i like talking to him about politics. also, he snaps amazing <a  href=\\\"http://www.chiamattt.com/portfolio/03.html\\\">photographs of dangerous situations</a>. even though he can talk a lot, he\\\'s a good guy and if he lived in my city, sometimes i\\\'d invite him over to smoke bongs with me. this comment is not off-topic.',1,368,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14555,2463,'2002-02-25 05:18:36',196,'jasonp','Here\\\'s that comic version of the tao te ching\n<a href=\\\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0385472595/qid=1014612833/sr=8-1/ref=sr_8_3_1/102-8917860-1937768\\\">\nThe Tao Speaks</A><BR>\nIf it came off as self-help like, that was from my presentation of it. Yeah, but the \\\"self-help\\\" and \\\"new-age\\\" aspect associated with things like that makes me cringe too.<BR>\n\nEastern philosophy, buddhism and taoism, have a fundamentally scientific nature to it.\nThere\\\'s a fairly small number of concepts and ideas to it which seem simple, but they\\\'re really sophisticated and subtle.\nThey have entire vocabularies and belief systems describing consciousness and psychology of the mind. ',0,700,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(14556,2463,'2002-02-25 05:29:39',59,'s5','oh, i didn\\\'t think you were being self-helpy. i just meant in general.',3,73,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14557,2458,'2002-02-25 06:13:57',124,'gus','obviously o, you havent read the studies about fucking in zero g. They\\\'ve got things for this!',0,97,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(14558,2464,'2002-02-25 06:26:00',124,'gus','I went with my instincts for a long time in my first \\\"adult relationship\\\"... but somehow I never went with it during the abusive times. (hi there cycle of abuse! hi there self blame! hi there manipulation!) There is no excuse for abuse, even if it doesnt leave bruises. If you stand for it, you will run the risk of wondering for the rest of your life what it was you did that made you deserve it. \r\n\r\n\r\n',0,410,0,0,'64.160.53.241','en'),(14559,2457,'2002-02-25 07:34:37',74,'jo','ix likes good music and quotes my so called life. i think he\\\'s swell. though we\\\'ve never met. but i hear good things about his oral sex prowess.',0,150,0,0,'172.149.85.166','en'),(14560,2464,'2002-02-25 08:55:19',194,'gypsy','i went with my instincts. \\\"he\\\'s not too mean. i can save him. i can even him out. i can make him be nice all the time. the raw material is there. i can calm him. he\\\'s really not that mean. he only shouts at me sometimes for no reason.\\\" etc.etc.etc. the minute you start asking yourself how mean is too mean is the second the relationship is doomed.',1,360,0,0,'66.171.51.162','en'),(14561,2464,'2002-02-25 11:22:43',214,'darwin','<p>\\\"he\\\'s an asshole to everyone **BUT ME**!\\\"</p>\r\n<p>\r\nseriously though, people seem to tolerate assholes until they recognize the person is being an asshole to <u>them</u>. I\\\'m personally of the opinion that you can\\\'t seperate parts of people and only be friends or lovers with the parts you like.</p>\r\n<p>wrt going with yr gut :</p>\r\n<p>\r\nyour desires sometimes masquerade as your instincts. there\\\'s a reason why people have big forebrains, and it\\\'s not just to keep from being killed by predators. it\\\'s also to choose which person is going to fuck up your life if you make out with them.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nunwerth : </p>\r\n<p>\r\nit sounds like the guy you are dating has issues that he really needs to work out. if people are warning you against him, there\\\'s probably a good reason. </p><p>this isn\\\'t to say that he\\\'s \\\"unsavable\\\" but it\\\'s important to realize that saving someone isn\\\'t a basis for a romantic relationship. saving someone is for professionals.. not only is it extremely difficult to provide theraputic assistance to someone you\\\'re romantically involved with, but if they somehow actually <u>DO</u> the work and fix themselves, the basis of your relationship changes radically.\r\n<p>what I\\\'m saying is, if he is aware of his personal problems, and you can be there with him and help him get into counselling or some other functional method of dealing with his shit, good. if he\\\'s unaware of it, pretty much nothing that you do will make him aware of it, and you\\\'ll get hurt in the process.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nTake care of yourself before you take care of other people.. or you\\\'re doing everyone a disservice.\r\n</p>\r\n',2,1674,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(14562,2464,'2002-02-25 11:30:48',0,'hawkwind','<b>STATISTICALLY, MORE PEOPLE SURVIVE IF THEY THINK ONLY OF THEMSELVES</B>',4,74,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14563,2464,'2002-02-25 11:37:02',214,'darwin','I love the internet because people can say \\\"clever\\\" things that seem to be vaguely on-point without actually having to say anything. That rules.\r\n\r\n-darwin',4,161,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(14564,2464,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','that was me, i was just reminded of that hawkwind quote because you said, take care of yourself first. so i posted the hawkwind quote. i wasn\\\'t trying to be clever, or even on topic, only stream of conciousness. i agree with your response to unwerth here, and i think your point about people putting up with assholes until the person is an asshole to THEM is a really good point.\r\n<br><br>\r\nare you happy now, i responded with something relevant!@#@@@\r\n',4,456,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14565,2458,'2002-02-25 11:54:58',64,'Joanna','Please please please put on  an animal suit and run around in the streets of Auckland, New Zealand.',1,99,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(14566,2458,'2002-02-25 12:08:36',66,'Peter','<br><tt>two words: <i>\\\"love swing\\\".</i></tt>',1,50,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14567,2464,'2002-02-25 12:09:12',214,'darwin','<p>I saw the sign, and it opened up my eyes, and I\\\'m happy now.</p>\r\n<p>Seriously though, I thought it was some person named \\\"hawkwind\\\" trying to imply that I feel that helping people is for suckers or something. Hence, snark. =)</p>\r\n<p>Is it just me or does the \\\"thus spake\\\" not have \\\"le\\\" in it, which makes it sorta.. uh.. useless wrt \\\"darwin to <i>person</i>?</p>\r\n<p>\r\n-darwin',1,405,0,0,'24.221.170.233','en'),(14568,2457,'2002-02-25 15:54:08',210,'may','gimme is a place down on cayuga st. by fall creek.\r\nmmm good coffee and lemon poppyseed bread. ',0,95,0,0,'128.84.25.25','en'),(14569,2463,'2002-02-25 18:22:24',151,'stella','you should read the tao of pooh. and the te of piglet. VERY reader friendly.',0,76,59,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14570,2465,'2002-02-25 18:25:24',123,'dcc','\\\" ... sometimes the heaviest tools can only be hefted by the hand of fate\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>beautiful. thank you, peter</p>',2,118,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14571,2463,'2002-02-25 18:27:05',148,'jeeff','except for the part where the book jumps up and punches you in the face.',0,72,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14572,2463,'2002-02-25 19:18:17',151,'stella','uuhhh, ok. ?',0,12,148,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14573,2463,'2002-02-25 19:59:29',120,'joyrider','for easy tao/zen/hindu lessons, check out stephen batchelor and alan w. watts.  you can find both at powells.com or any decent bookstore\\\'s metaphysical/eastern thought stacks.  \r\n\r\n<p>i\\\'ve been raving about watts to le for about a month and keep threatening to send her a book.  maybe i should get around to doing that.  ooh, and that astral matrix/mckenna collab cd.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>being a hippie raver rules.</p>',2,415,59,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(14574,2463,'2002-02-25 20:04:22',120,'joyrider','<b>s5</b>:  this is also pretty <a href=\\\"http://www.saturn5.com/~chris/tao.txt\\\">accessable</a>.\r\n\r\n',0,105,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(14575,2465,'2002-02-25 20:22:33',195,'matt','this is really well crafted. impressive.',1,40,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(14576,2463,'2002-02-25 20:55:05',151,'stella','that\\\'s very nice. ',0,21,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14577,2464,'2002-02-25 22:37:06',59,'s5','but your friends and people on the internet give you advice about your lovelife for so many reasons, none of which have anything to do with you. they lack the full story. all you can ever do is listen to your instincts, including the instinct when you realize what you desired is wrong. \r\n<br><br>\r\ncertainly, if your physical safety is at risk, that\'s a different matter altogether. but that\'s not the subject of your post, is it? if he\'s simply a man with a loud voice and a temper, well, soft voices with ill-intent can be far more damaging.\r\n<br><br>\r\nyou seem to be worried about how inexperienced you feel. then go out and have some experiences.',3,660,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14578,2464,'2002-02-25 22:52:34',66,'Peter to s5','<br><tt>you should have an advice column called \\\'the level head\\\'; i think you could help alot of people.  i also like how you said: <i>go out and have some experiences</i>.</tt> ',1,184,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14579,2463,'2002-02-25 22:53:03',196,'jasonp','s5: that was the coolest translation of the tao te ching I\\\'ve read yet.<BR>\nI got two of the \\\"cartoon\\\" books last night \\\"The Tao Speaks\\\" and \\\"Zen Speaks\\\". They\\\'re good.<BR><BR>\nHere\\\'s an \n<a href=\\\"http://www.akirarabelais.com/i/i.html\\\">\ni-ching page</A> (320k) that\\\'s as good as any book I\\\'ve seen. It doesn\\\'t give any background or explanation on casting hexagrams though.',0,415,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(14580,2466,'2002-02-25 23:01:28',66,'Peter','<br><tt>frankly, i think breasts are <i>very</i> over-rated.</tt>',1,65,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14581,2469,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','thinking over your post/comment too much is vastly preferable to no thinking at all. welcome.',1,93,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14582,2469,'2002-02-25 23:14:34',204,'Nastchenka Morova','does commenting on your own post count as a comment? hooray...i finally did it',0,78,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(14583,2466,'2002-02-25 23:21:08',124,'gus','so this is where I say I saw this geneticist speak this weekend about how he found the gene for osteoperosis and arthritis, and how it relates to breast cancer (the same genes that build bones build lactating mamory glands) and how he might have a cure for those bone diseases... its in phase 2 trials. it\\\'s all very interesting.',0,332,0,0,'64.172.56.220','en'),(14584,2469,'2002-02-25 23:22:26',124,'gus','it counts! ',0,11,0,0,'64.172.56.220','en'),(14585,2466,'2002-02-25 23:24:25',59,'s5 to gus','do you remember his name?',3,25,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14586,2466,'2002-02-25 23:57:05',70,'Juvenile','Back dat ass up!',1,16,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(14587,2466,'2002-02-26 00:13:49',66,'Peter to gus','<br><tt>id be interested in hearing more about this, especially his proposed cure; for my grad work, i work in a research lab that\\\'s currently over-obsessed with a certain variety of rheumatological osteoporosis and a certain cure. if you give me his name, i can dig up tons of science-dork articles for myself on medline and look oh-so-smart wt work tomorrow!</tt>\r\n',1,370,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14588,2463,'2002-02-26 00:18:41',120,'joyrider','Hotei used to wander from town-to-town with a sack full of sweets and bread.  He gave this stuff to children and ran sort of  a travelling zen kindergarten.  whenever a zen student asked him something esoteric and stuffy, he would ask for a penny.  when they\\\'d shell out , he\\\'d smile and bail on the guy without an answer.  he spent those pennies on sweets for the children and wine for himself.  he was a bad ass.',0,420,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(14589,2468,'2002-02-26 00:48:02',83,'adamajah','(i can\\\'t believe this isn\\\'t getting any comments)',3,55,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14590,2468,'2002-02-26 00:57:21',114,'unwerth','applause.\r\ni can\\\'t even begin to form a coherent respone to this yet.... just applause.\r\n\r\n-xtal',0,99,0,0,'65.80.133.254','en'),(14591,2464,'2002-02-26 01:04:01',114,'unwerth','you are absolutely correct and i thank you for it.  it\\\'s not someone else\\\'s responsibility to give me these experiences... and i have to realize that experiences are valuable regardless of whether they are enjoyable or not.  in many ways that\\\'s what makes a person i suppose... a culmination of both.  if nothing else, i have at least learned a great deal from this whole thing... which is the most one can hope for.\r\n\r\n',0,429,59,0,'65.80.133.254','en'),(14592,2466,'2002-02-26 01:19:35',124,'gus','i dont know that it\\\'s a cure as much as a way to stop it from getting worse? im not really sure, he had mentioned being able to help osteoperosis by getting two shots a year. lets see, his name was... josef penninger and he\\\'s an associate professor of immunology at the university of toronto. working with genes OPGL (bone growth) and CD45 (immune system) and AIF (cell death). so says the bio.',0,400,0,0,'64.172.56.220','en'),(14593,2468,'2002-02-26 01:25:13',17,'austin','<P>i never had to get used to santa cruz until my girlfriend left.  and all my san francisco friends left the country on vacations,  all the people i impose upon.  then i found myself sitting at home on friday nights reading the count of monte cristo and taking parts off and putting parts back on my motorcycle.  i found myself getting up at 7:30 am on saturday and going to t\\\'ai chi at 8:00 am, then karate at 9:00 am.  i started walking to the coffee shop.  and i started doing less of other things: going to movies to be with surrogate friends on the silver screen, driving to san francisco to eat dinner with one or two people.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i still go to sf one night on the weekend, or maybe none.  i sit in the garage and listen to blue grass music.  i go to the coffee shop and stare at the fish tank.  i write letters to people.  solitary things.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>i worry that when said girlfriend gets back from thailand, i won\\\'t want to get off work on fridays and fight traffic to oakland and be away from this <i>home</i> that i am patterning out.</P>',4,1060,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14594,914,'2002-02-26 02:03:18',195,'matt','i miss egg',1,10,0,0,'209.208.77.175','en'),(14595,2466,'2002-02-26 02:03:20',66,'Peter to gus','<br><tt>opgl! wow, thats the exct project im working on now; opgl and the interaction of a bisphosphonate drug called \\\'alendronate\\\' on osteolast cultures. did hetalk aboutany ofthese things? small world. coool. ok, sorry, im a science-dork.</tt>',1,251,0,0,'216.139.154.221','en'),(14596,2466,'2002-02-26 02:32:37',124,'gus','heh! no he didnt talk about drugs specificaly. just gene therapy in general. mostly he talked about little mouses in a funny austrian accent. he had a small stuffed animal mouse as a visual aide, and slides of mice that had various weirded out genes in them.\r\n\r\n<p> occassionally one of the slides he was showing would be of one of his kids and he\\\'d say \\\"and zere is my zecond attempt at cloning myzelf, zo far it iz vorking out very vell!\\\"\r\n<p>\r\nscience is kinda cool.',0,478,0,0,'64.172.56.220','en'),(14597,2469,'2002-02-26 02:36:06',195,'matt','i think i had aboritis once. hurt like a motherfucker, as i recall.',1,67,0,0,'209.208.28.26','en'),(14598,2466,'2002-02-26 04:57:04',148,'jeeff','<p>gus, that is a great quote!  and a great typed austrian accent.  congratulations to you.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>and back to the subject at hand, i have a very well-developed chest due to my own genes.  but as a guy, i often get very self-conscious thinking that i look \\\"busty\\\".  as a kid i would slouch horribly to try and hide my barrel chest.  now i have bad posture.</p>',0,369,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14599,2464,'2002-02-26 04:58:08',75,'tyler','my 2c<br><br>\r\nyou may be naive, but there\\\'s nothing horrible about that. that\\\'s just purity of heart running up against the evil in other people. don\\\'t let it make <i>you</i> evil though.\r\n<p>\r\nps- any abusiveness is too much. how abusive are you towards those more helpless than you? my guess is not at all, and that is what you should demand.',1,354,0,0,'165.247.164.128','en'),(14600,2468,'2002-02-26 05:01:05',148,'jeeff','the internet is a small place.',0,30,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14601,2468,'2002-02-26 05:06:53',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bunnies, kitties and rainbows</tt>',1,42,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14602,2468,'2002-02-26 05:25:51',175,'Paul G','So, <b>puce</b>, you\\\'re arguing on behalf of <a href=\\\"http://www.columbia.edu/~ta63/exist.htm\\\">Existentialism</a>?\r\n\r\n<p>I am not a doctor of philosophy, but I do have a first aid card.  Is there something that I can do to restore your faith in <b>God</b> and humanity?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You know, I think that Nietzsche might have been a Socratic; and that\\\'s not a joke.  I would be interested in learning of the origin of the paraphrase that you used - it would be helpful to me in the work that I am doing.  I know that he made reference to inoculation in more than one text; but I had supposed, for that reason, that he understood his ideas to be good for the body (of the people) only in small amounts.  Maybe I\\\'m way off on that...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s interesting that Nietzsche made use of Judeo-Christian and Greek references to the extent that he did.  It has made me wonder about his real relationship with them...</p> \r\n\r\n<p>This: \\\"your mind and heart are forever divided...\\\" holds true in regard to the English; but you\\\'re Armenian, and I\\\'m Lebanese.  Oh, that\\\'s good and funny.</p>\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,1120,0,0,'65.230.18.1','en'),(14603,2468,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i don\\\'t think anyone is arguing anything. and puce, i really liked this. thank you.',1,86,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14604,2468,'2002-02-26 05:42:40',175,'Paul G','I\\\'m so happy that I\\\'m not on your Bozo List, weeb.\r\n\r\n<p>I need to explain something.  When I use the word <b>argue</b> it has a certain meaning: a collection of reasons for, or a summary of, a position; it\\\'s not confrontational.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><b>puce\\\'s</b> article reads like \\\"a collection of reasons for, or a summary of, the position\\\" of the Existentialists.  And it was titled <i>Philosophy</i>.  I liked it too.  I\\\'m trying my best to interact with it in a positive manner, and engage it intellectually.  <i>*Best Friendly*</i>',0,549,0,0,'65.230.18.1','en'),(14605,2465,'2002-02-26 05:50:35',175,'Paul G','This is a great piece of writing, Peter.  It\\\'s a wonderful stand-alone story.  This thing should be in circulation.  You should be collecting royalty money.  And you wrote it less than 24 hours after saying that you didn\\\'t feel like writing...',0,249,0,0,'65.230.18.1','en'),(14606,2468,'2002-02-26 05:59:09',154,'ryan','puce, i\\\'m with you.  let\\\'s take zoloft together and watch the rain.\r\n<p>\r\n*best friendly*',1,95,0,0,'216.26.3.79','en'),(14607,2467,'2002-02-26 06:07:18',175,'Paul G','First, I suppose that I should say, \\\"sorry,\\\" to you for tainting your post with my presence inasmuch nothing good is likely to follow me.  Having said that, however, I really enjoyed your article.  The format made it just a bit difficult to access.  It might not be cool to ask you this, but if you get the chance would you explain why you chose this font and layout?  Was it just an experiment?  I\\\'m not being a jerk, just trying to understand it and enjoy it more.  Thanks.  ',0,486,0,0,'65.230.18.1','en'),(14608,1787,'2002-02-26 06:24:28',59,'s5','where has essemar been. bring him back.',3,39,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14609,2466,'2002-02-26 06:41:30',185,'orion','Breasts?  Overrated?  Dude, I think it\\\'s time we had a come-to-Jesus discussion about your use of el pipe-o crack-o.  ;-)',1,124,66,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14610,2469,'2002-02-26 06:43:49',185,'orion','commeting would be easier if the cookie mechanism for this password thing wasn\\\'t fuqt.   bring me the head of austin swinney.  i shall mount it on my war chariot.',1,165,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14611,2469,'2002-02-26 06:44:23',185,'orion','which, btw, is a rather abused looking 1990 mitsu galant.  yea verily, a mighty steed it is...',1,94,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14612,2389,'2002-02-26 06:51:05',217,'jra','i am way late on this conversation, in more ways than one, but have to say that part III of your post gave me such a warm feeling, reminding me of one of my favorite magnetic fields songs. \\\"you share secrets with lou, but we\\\'ve got secrets, too-- well just one: i only keep this heap for you. \\\'cause i\\\'m the luckiest guy on the lower east side... i\\\'ve got wheels and you wanna go for a ride...\\\"',2,412,0,0,'152.163.201.211','en'),(14613,2025,'2002-02-26 06:52:31',59,'s5','<p>\r\nupdate: (about skin problems)\r\n</p><p>\r\nafter leaving california for 2.5 months to bask in the clean, sunny air of australasia, all of my skin problems have cleared up. as if nothing ever happened. including the unsightly case of <a href=\\\"http://www.aad.org/pamphlets/pityrias.html\\\">pityriasis rosea</a> that covered my body for two years. zot. gone. goodbye.</p>',3,374,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14614,2460,'2002-02-26 07:04:22',114,'unwerth','a completely nerdy question... what kind of camera are you using for your digital pieces?',0,89,122,0,'65.80.131.233','en'),(14615,2470,'2002-02-26 08:24:55',120,'joyrider','this reminds me of goethe\\\'s \\\"erlkonig\\\" - shouldn\\\'t have read it before bed.  ',0,89,0,0,'24.205.196.37','en'),(14616,2453,'2002-02-26 08:50:52',168,'laney','i have a friend who was laid off from a newspaper job and decided, randomly, to become an actress. quickly realizing such hollywood dreams *may* be out of reach at this point, she swallowed her pride and got a job as a barista at megacoffee,inc. after six months she has, quite literally, never been happier. hey man, it happens!',0,329,0,0,'152.163.201.196','en'),(14617,2465,'2002-02-26 08:55:28',168,'laney','i love that you lost the hammer just when you were old enough and wise enough to realize its significance. what a fantastic story!',0,130,0,0,'152.163.201.196','en'),(14618,2466,'2002-02-26 09:17:06',176,'Scapegoat','Being a guy and all I can\\\'t say that I could relate to what it would be like to lose a breast really. But I don\\\'t think that not having breasts is a reason not to let anyone touch you or to isolate yourself.<br><br>\r\nHere is were I get a little weird and talk about one of my mothers friends - my mothers best friend from college lost both breast to cancer. But I have to admit as far as attraction to older women I thought she was damn sexy - no breasts necessary. ',3,472,0,0,'64.229.168.31','en'),(14619,2463,'2002-02-26 13:55:46',123,'dcc','penny for your thoughts?',1,24,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14620,2468,'2002-02-26 14:59:00',174,'Mad Moll','<p>There is much to be said for those who can acknowledge the temporary,  recognize the \\\'futility\\\', and dare to move with purpose all the same, to \\\"reach for the future with creative hand, and everything that is or has been becomes for them a means, an instrument, a hammer.\\\"</p>\r\n',6,293,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14621,2463,'2002-02-26 15:32:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>sounds like some of the dealers here in nyc.</tt>',1,57,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14622,2468,'2002-02-26 15:33:46',66,'Peter to Moll','<br><tt>a <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2465.txt\\\">hammer</a>!</tt>',1,96,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14623,2465,'2002-02-26 15:42:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>its interesting how fate\\\'s cycles work.<br><br>curiously, at the boathouse docks that i was working on when i dropped the hammer, someone usually dives a few times a year to retrieve the costly things that people drop overboard or off the docks (thats why I was diving there in the first place)... titanium boat parts, custom riggers, rings, watches, countless pieces of equipment, pace computers, etc. i always liked to imagine that someone recovered the hammer and has given it yet a third life.<br><br>i suppose that even inanimate objects can be reincarnated; that is, if they don\\\'t rust.</tt> ',1,612,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14624,2465,'2002-02-26 15:43:40',66,'long time retriever','first time dropper',1,18,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14625,2469,'2002-02-26 15:47:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>someone recently (and brutishly, i might add) ripped the hood ornament from my car. remember in the 80\\\'s when all the hood-rats sported mercedes ornaments on their necklaces? well, odds are there\\\'s now someone roaming the upper west side wearing the rusty emblem of an \\\'84 corolla. maybe theyre going for a retro thing; i dont know.</tt> ',1,355,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14626,2469,'2002-02-26 15:51:32',66,'old corollas','<a href=\\\"http://www.onthenet.com.au/~paulp/corolla/sford.jpg\\\">rock</a>.',1,77,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14627,2464,'2002-02-26 17:19:53',148,'jeeff','hm, your situation gives me a bad gut feeling.  i would say follow <i>my</i> instincts, but you\\\'re still better off doing what you want.  \r\n\r\n<p>to the comment about instinct being confused with desire, i think in that case you need to do some serious inward searching.  i find that my instincts often run directly contrary to my desires.  and they\\\'re very seperate.  it\\\'s like desire is a velvet-throated jazz singer, while intuition has the voice of a nagging old spinster.</p>',0,488,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14628,2464,'2002-02-26 17:27:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my <i>desire</i> sounds alot like <a href=\\\"http://www.geocities.com/Area51/Corridor/4216/TOM_JONES.jpg\\\">tom jones</a>, the welshman with the golden voice whereas my <i>intuition</i> smacks of <a href=\\\"http://www.manteno.k12.il.us/finearts/images/albums/this1s4u.gif\\\">barry manilow</a>.</tt>',1,310,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14629,2389,'2002-02-26 17:42:21',66,'and the','name of the song is?',1,20,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14630,2158,'2002-02-26 17:53:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i just reread this; that 6th paragraph always makes me feel liek crying. you\\\'d think after not being with this person for 7 months, id get over it. ahh well, i guess some things take time... lots of time.</tt>',1,220,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14631,2467,'2002-02-26 18:08:56',156,'lotus','Paul! you underrate yourself.\r\nI was playing around with line breaks for this attempt at a poem, which explains the weird formatting. as for the font, the kind editor who activated this piece chose it, not me.\r\nthanks so much for reading.',0,238,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14632,2472,'2002-02-26 18:26:18',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>blurred landscapes, elegant decay</i></tt>',1,53,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14633,2467,'2002-02-26 18:38:19',175,'Paul G','Great.  I am interested in learning about new people and new things.  HTML is a very new thing to me.  Someone took the time to send me an e-mail with some information in it a few days ago and I have been trying to learn more.  \r\n\r\n<p>Comments have the effect of saying to a writer: \\\"Make more, please.  We are reading.\\\"  And so I always feel bad when a piece is left without them.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So, ahem: \\\"Make more, please.\\\"</p> ',0,439,0,0,'65.230.18.45','en'),(14634,2467,'2002-02-26 18:39:38',66,'Peter to paulg','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.htmlgoodies.com\\\">click me</a>.</tt>',1,69,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14635,2467,'2002-02-26 18:56:58',175,'Paul G','Again: Great.  I hope that it\\\'s ok to say: \\\"I don\\\'t understand this.  Please help me.\\\"  And I hope that it is also ok to say: \\\"I do know something about this other thing.  Please let me offer it to you.\\\"  We all have different backgrounds, and different ways of communicating.  \r\n\r\n<p>Lotus has a very richly descriptive and intimate style.  The font here seemed to contrast with that - it had a rather cool and mechanical feel to it.</p>',0,456,0,0,'65.230.18.45','en'),(14636,2467,'2002-02-26 18:57:57',66,'machines','we have souls, too.',1,19,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14637,2467,'2002-02-26 19:04:56',175,'Paul G','You have souls - the souls that you\\\'ve stolen.  Lotus writes about human interaction, and Nature.  In any case, the blue text on grey hurts my eyes.  Fin.',0,157,66,0,'65.230.18.45','en'),(14638,2389,'2002-02-26 19:17:19',168,'laney','\\\"the luckiest guy on the lower east side,\\\" from 69 love songs, vol. 1. pure brilliance.',0,93,0,0,'198.81.17.31','en'),(14639,2467,'2002-02-26 19:24:53',129,'moebius to lotus','quickly, before i read any of the other comments appended to this post, I just want to say: <i>this piece rocked me.</i> for some reason i wish i hadn\\\'t waited \\\'till today to read it. it reminded me a little of my childhood year in a tropical land, drinking juice from kalimansi berries and chasing after the giant toads that came out during the monsoons.',1,361,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14640,2473,'2002-02-26 19:42:59',123,'dcc','I LIKE MY COAT!',4,15,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14641,2467,'2002-02-26 19:53:59',66,'machines to paul','<br><tt>we stole <i>nothing</i>. you created us, gave us souls.</tt>',1,68,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14642,2471,'2002-02-26 20:09:47',218,'da','*love*',1,6,0,0,'128.111.96.65','en'),(14643,2465,'2002-02-26 20:33:37',123,'dcc to peter','but even when rusted they can begin a new life. maybe not the same kind of life they had before, but a life nonetheless. as a piece of art, an archeological remnant.\r\n<p>who knows, maybe the hammer lodged itself deep within the mud to only be found in a few centuries as a dicovery of science of the early 21st century.',3,319,66,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14644,2467,'2002-02-26 20:35:14',123,'dcc','i want your soul.<br>\r\ni will eat your soul.',0,44,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14645,2464,'2002-02-26 21:11:37',124,'gus','instincts can lie if you want them to. that\\\'s all Im saying. talk to a few people who\\\'ve been in abusive relationships, learn from their mistakes. no one ever said the mistakes you learn from had to be your own.',0,217,0,0,'64.163.215.61','en'),(14646,2467,'2002-02-26 21:22:29',175,'Paul G','You know, Lotus used the \\\"M\\\" word in this piece and did not get a comment about it.',0,89,0,0,'65.230.18.45','en'),(14647,2467,'2002-02-26 21:23:04',66,'open the machine','and slurp out its soul like an oyster on the half-shell',1,55,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14648,2467,'2002-02-26 21:24:41',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>m</b>ango? <b>m</b>onsoon?</tt>',1,42,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14649,2467,'2002-02-26 21:25:28',175,'Paul G','Mango!',0,6,0,0,'65.230.18.45','en'),(14650,2473,'2002-02-26 21:35:13',176,'Scapegoat','This wasn\\\'t meant as a fashion post, so please go on liking the coat. I think I might even have a 3/4 length around here somewhere, not sure though. ',0,152,123,0,'64.229.164.236','en'),(14651,2467,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','lotus, i love all the stuff you\\\'ve written here.  thanks.',1,60,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14652,2453,'2002-02-26 22:03:53',219,'Fred','I don\\\'t really have anything to add here. I just wanted to say hi and stuff.\r\nAlso, I like cupcakes. And naps. Naps rule. But so this morning I woke up and I was starving and trying to send out some more lame letters and resumes but I was so hungry and was almost out of cigarettes and I live in a six floor walk up and I didn\\\'t want to have to go downstairs/outside when I remembered this local place that delivers 24 hrs a day so I looked up the menu online and ordered the sirloin strips and mashed potatoes smothered in a delicious, light cream sauce...and it fucking rocked. Cheap too ($6.95!) ALSO--THEY DELIVER CIGARETTES. So this morning at about 5:30 I was eating steak and mashed potatoes and listening to some corny old timey radio show (Pat Novak For Hire) and thought that sometimes life ain\\\'t so bad. Also, any chance you could swipe some nirtous chargers?',1,879,0,0,'172.168.242.251','en'),(14653,2465,'2002-02-26 22:11:36',174,'Mad Moll','<p>This reminds me of something that happened to me a few years ago. I was trekking along some abandoned train tracks that run through the Dismal Swamp with a ladyfriend. While exploring the area of an old horse farm, the girl realized she\\\'d lost her favorite kerchief along the way.</p>\r\n<p>All this time we were carrying this giant statue of the virgin mary. There\\\'s always a lot of junk on the tracks left by people passing through or, more often, by nearby factories. One of them apparently makes trophies and holy relics.</p>\r\n<p>In any case, it was clear to me that The Tracks had taken something from us in exchange for the virgin we\\\'d taken from her. Over a little concrete bridge, we solemnly dropped our statue of the virgin over the side, letting her vanish at the bottom of the stream, returning it to the place it belonged.</p>\r\n<p>Sure enough, the girl found her kerchief a few moments later.</p>\r\n<p>I don\\\'t \\\"believe\\\" in anything, but I know that some places are different, and different things happen there.</p>',1,1045,66,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14654,2470,'2002-02-26 22:16:04',174,'Mad Moll','erlkonig :~(',1,12,120,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14655,2417,'2002-02-27 00:31:47',82,'simon','have you seen aretha franklin\\\'s tits lately, talk about your mile long cleavage! jeez!',0,89,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14656,2472,'2002-02-27 03:50:52',75,'tyler','that\\\'s a really nice poem.',1,29,0,0,'165.247.164.144','en'),(14657,2158,'2002-02-27 11:03:49',200,'chiclet','when i got to that paragraph i felt like crying too...',0,54,66,0,'207.172.166.22','en'),(14658,2472,'2002-02-27 12:00:19',65,'le','i miss amy.',0,11,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14659,2472,'2002-02-27 13:25:27',86,'egg','<p>I dont have what it takes to comment eloquently on this piece here, so</p> \r\n<p>here\\\'s an austin swinney style brief report on my birthday. this weekend i left bangkok saturday morning after fusty and eve and i sat in traffic in the back of a car all friday night, we were like fuck it. we went to a tropical island 3 hours from bangkok and ate a lot of food. we took naps, i gave massages, justin drooled on his laptop and eve and I wore halter tops. it was really awesome, except i have a lot of freckles now. I said, \\\" I want SUNKAYA (pandanus leaf custard) for my birthday cake. I want to see \\\"HOTEL!\\\" (a B thai horror movie).\\\" After a very trying and stressful time at an internet cafe being a bitch, I got email from jay saying they are not going to give me unemployment checks and I will have no money for rent when I get back. Then I brought my farang friends home and it was really awkward and noone talked. and there was a pandanus leaf birthday cake, some orchids, a book on environmental economics in Thailand, some fake orchid hair ties, a turquoise charm necklace, and some coca-cola waiting for me. then me and my cousin Can who was born in Canada and justin and eve went to see \\\"rong ram Pee\\\"  (which means \\\"Ghost Hotel\\\" literally but was translated as \\\"HOTEL!!\\\" exactly spelled like that). it was a poorly executed movie that I would have rented when I was in 5th grade and would have been made in the 70s in the US. then I went home and blabbed about how stressed I am, and then I fell asleep for two hours, and then I went to thai language school. Basically to make a long story short, I got everything I wanted, and much more, except It\\\'s All Good yellow cake, Jay dinner at the Bend, and austin\\\'s dick. sorry if that is revealing and personal, it is hot here and I don\\\'t have a lot of patience for discreet innuendo</p>',0,1885,0,0,'203.148.205.189','en'),(14660,2467,'2002-02-27 15:05:34',156,'lotus to Paul','what a marvellous and positive attitude you have! thank you so much for the encouragement. and i love your \\\'make more\\\' shorthand. i think i\\\'ll steal it for myself, when i encounter something on here that just makes me light up, or, drop my jaw, in real life no less. <br>\r\n<br>\r\ni\\\'m working on the first serious version of my webpage, and am completely html-ignorant. so i\\\'m doing a lot of \\\'view source\\\' and exploring. it is so good to learn! and of course, asking questions is the best way to learn. \r\n<br><br>\r\nthank you peter for the link',0,562,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14661,2467,'2002-02-27 17:01:56',156,'lotus to moebius','can i admit that the biggest reason i write is to connect to other people, and more specifically, to receive responses like yours?',0,130,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14662,2467,'2002-02-27 17:03:57',156,'lotus to o','thank you so much for reading, and for commenting. i am _really_ enjoying the swinneys (human and article)',0,106,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14663,2479,'2002-02-27 20:31:44',124,'gus','I like this post. better than your first few. it feels more comfortable, more like you\\\'ve gotten into your own groove or something? something.',0,145,0,0,'64.163.215.61','en'),(14664,2479,'2002-02-27 21:02:14',156,'blue stocking','thank you, gus.',0,15,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14665,2477,'2002-02-27 21:35:52',82,'simon','hi. thanks for writing more rubber band stories. keep them coming, gusbertini.',0,78,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14666,2456,'2002-02-27 22:18:07',82,'ben dover','don\\\'t i know it!',0,19,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14667,2480,'2002-02-27 22:26:52',90,'leonard','truth decay.',1,12,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(14668,2480,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i see the dentist on monday.  i am totally petrified.  luckily i am seeing a man who is commonly referred to as \\\"Dr. Nitrous\\\".  all my wisdom teeth have to come out.  s5 is making me go.  i am scared.  he says he will make me food that i can drink through a straw, though.  the last time i saw a dentist i had one cavity, and was told to get my wisdom teeth out within that year.  that was 5 years ago.  i am afraid i have a million cavities.  i am afraid the dentist will be mean to me.  I AM AFRAID.',4,507,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14669,2480,'2002-02-28 00:34:21',129,'moebius, commenting at length','<br>\r\noh dear. i\\\'m sorry. you guys are in the same approximate place i was about two years ago, when i saw a dentist for the first time in, hmm, five years. it was awful.<br><br>first off, i had to come back for not just cleaning, but \\\"scaling,\\\" which was painful & bloody & embarassing. the dentist wouldn\\\'t even <i>examine</i> my teeth \\\'till this was done.<br><br>then the dentist did examine me and hoo ha, everything was falling apart. crowns needed to be replaced on around six, seven teeth. one tooth was missing a huge chunk (somehow i\\\'d managed to ignore it). root canals were prophesied. awful awfulness. and i still had three wisdowm teeth, which had crowded up everything & screwed up the smile my childhood braces originally gave me.<br><br>upshot: fastforward to today. approx $5000US later all my teeths are in good shape again. and clean clean clean. still have to remove one wisdom tooth. am considering braces again. moral of story: go see yr dentist now to save money later. and get them wisdoms out when yr young. o, you\\\'re on the right track.',0,1083,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14670,2480,'2002-02-28 00:40:30',129,'moebius','oh, ironic sidenote: for the past 20 years my mom\\\'s worked for dentists, as an assistant, or office manager, or computer specialist. every time she called me she\\\'d close with: <i>matt, have you seen a dentist? go see a dentist.</i> did this have anything to do with my predicament? probably something, yes. only Freud would know for sure.',1,344,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14671,2480,'2002-02-28 01:50:34',177,'pony','dearest o...do NOT be afraid! \r\n\r\n<p>not all tales of dentistry are horrible. when i got my wisdom teeth out i had not been to the dentist in almost ten years. he recommended that i go get a cleaning and checkup, but he was very nice about it. he was there for the wisdom teeth and that was his focus. i remember not too long ago you said something about bad headaches. i almost mentioned getting your teeth checked. i had nasty migraines before i got mine out and almost zero since the deed was done. and it wasn\\\'t that bad....no nitrous even just a local. two of them were impacted. i can\\\'t say it was a glorious time but it wasn\\\'t really that bad at all. i didn\\\'t feel anything and the nurse was sweet and when it\\\'s over everyone is supernice to you while you\\\'re recovering [noodles and jello are the best].</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also i only used tylenol after. no power pills. and i\\\'m a wimp.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>last note: it wasn\\\'t for another four years till i went to the regular dentist. they said my teeth were awesome. the cleaning took 30 minutes at the tops maybe. and i only had 2 cavities.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>[and i almost never floss or brush my teeth three times a day.]</p>',2,1184,0,0,'209.239.199.69','en'),(14672,2480,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter to O','<br><tt>o, if you ask dr. nitrous nicely, he might let you keep your teeth! if theyre not impacted (requiring them to be broken up to be removed) they\\\'ll coem out nicely and all in one pretty piece. i have mine. i plan on making some interesting artsy thing with them someday.<br><br>fear not the oral surgery; its not terribly invasive, and even so, theyll fill you with so many drugs you\\\'ll swear they should be illegal.</tt>',1,433,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14673,2480,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>hello nice people!</p>\r\n<p>for the record, i brush 2 times a day and go through periods of flossing.  i think my teeth are actually in pretty good shape.  i hope.</p>\r\n<p>kat, i am really hoping that the migraines are connected to the teeth.  that would be REALLY cool.  if that doesn\\\'t work i\\\'ll go see a chiropractor.  maybe i should get my eyes tested too.</p>\r\n<p>sadly, all 4 of my wisdom teeth are impacted, so i doubt i\\\'ll get to keep them.</p>\r\n<p>having a body really sucks!  i can\\\'t wait till we can just be little clouds of conciousness floating around without a care in the world.</p>',3,611,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14674,2480,'2002-02-28 02:36:38',129,'moebius','<br>hmm. i knew i should\\\'ve added that in all cases, getting the actual work done on my teeth was generally NOT that painful, and usually done surprisingly quickly, too. no, the pain, for me, anyway, was in the wallet. ouch!\r\n<br><br>and yeah, personally, if i\\\'d brushed and flossed like i should, most of this wouldn\\\'tve been an issue. my teeth gunk up quicker than most people. dentists warned me of this when i was a kid and i didn\\\'t listen.<br><br>o, you will survive. your pain will be minimal. and you\\\'ve got s5 to help you through it, a benefit most of us can\\\'t claim. so: huzzah!',0,605,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14675,2480,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','attention! \r\n\r\nliquid foods party at o\\\'s!! i\\\'m picturing tofu pie with crushed vicodin \\\"meringue\\\" topping. mashed potatoes with codeine gravy. but mostly you\\\'ll be sooo woozy from the anesthesia that you\\\'ll make s5 giggle with your new understanding of reality on the way home. but do stay away from rice, the little grains get stuck in your new holes. that said, i must tell you that i got a pruning book today. bliss.\r\n',1,439,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14676,2479,'2002-02-28 05:42:10',174,'Mad Moll','<p>I liked this one, but I think I like your earlier posts better. The feeling of this taking place in a scene seems a little forced to me.</p>\r\n',6,145,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14677,2478,'2002-02-28 05:48:49',174,'Mad Moll','I like it. Is it a myth?',6,24,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14678,2481,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','smokers suck.  bah smoking.',3,27,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14679,2481,'2002-02-28 09:54:15',59,'s5','if you date a health freak guy and quit smoking but then later break up with the guy, you might start smoking again.',3,116,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14680,2481,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i know health-freak people who smoke like chimneys, and rampantly self-destructive folks who are vehemently anti-smoking. geez. how odd is our world, sometimes?!</tt>',1,174,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14681,2479,'2002-02-28 13:31:04',156,'lotus','actually, on rereading gus\\\'s comment i got worried, because this was written before the other ones. so i though -- hmm -- am i getting _worse_?\r\n<br><br>\r\nthanks, Mad Moll.',0,175,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14682,2481,'2002-02-28 15:10:30',65,'le','you should choose a day to quit, and on that day, get your tongue pierced. ',4,75,0,0,'165.247.204.99','en'),(14683,2481,'2002-02-28 15:40:14',174,'Mad Moll','<p>sometimes I too wonder if I\\\'ll ever stop smoking. I have no real desire to quit. I used to quit for weeks, sometimes months at a time, just to prove to myself that I could, but eventually I would realize that life with cigarettes was simply better.</p>\r\n<p>It is not even that the process of smoking itself is that great, but it <i>adds</i> to other experiences in a very significant way. that cup of coffee becomes something quite different with a cigarette. the relaxing chat with a friend becomes that much more pleasant when the sharing of tobacco is involved. that seaside dinner becomes that much more satisfying with a postmeal smoke. These are the sorts of things that make me think twice.</p>',6,707,0,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14684,2481,'2002-02-28 16:37:08',210,'may','i like the reward system.\r\n\r\nlast time i did something good i got my eyebrow pierced. the first time was my belly button. now i think i want a tattoo, but i can\\\'t decide...i\\\'d probably just get the tattoo and keep on smoking though. sheeyut.',0,247,65,0,'128.253.117.156','en'),(14685,2481,'2002-02-28 17:08:44',74,'jo','i always used to say that i\\\'d quit for good when i decided to have kids. but i\\\'m not having kids for maybe 4 more years and i think i want to quit before then. personally, i just can\\\'t help it. i <b>love</b> smoking.',0,225,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14686,2481,'2002-02-28 17:32:48',177,'pony','it is easy to love <b>not</b> smoking too. it\\\'s all about perspective. there is no reason why that cup of coffee can\\\'t be amazingly special without a ciggie. it\\\'s all about how you visualize/feel it. i heart coffee very much, but i only drink it once or twice a week. so when i do i cherish it. it\\\'s possible to adore other healthier fetishes. and the best part is filling your life with them. ',4,406,0,0,'209.239.199.186','en'),(14687,2482,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"...you can see that love, forever-love, was a lie...\\\"</i>.<br><br>although this might be the case for your parents, that doesn\\\'t mean its the case for <i>forever-love</i> in general. ive been thinking of <i>forever-love</i> alot lately, and although the cases where it\\\'s failed are many, so are the cases where its successful. [im saying this for myself more than anyone else, by the way].<br><br>i enjoyed reading this.</tt>',1,449,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14688,2482,'2002-02-28 18:08:26',156,'lotus','thanks peter. <br>\r\ni don\\\'t think \\\'forever-love\\\' is a lie, at least, i fervently hope it isn\\\'t. but i\\\'m getting bitter? anxious? something when i look around and see so many loves either breaking or fading or disappearing. this was written several years ago, btw. a lothas changed since then :)',0,309,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14689,2479,'2002-02-28 18:21:51',124,'gus','i meant the poemy things I saw early on. I didnt like those as much. dunno why.',0,79,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14690,2479,'2002-02-28 18:29:22',156,'blue stocking','well, they are very different afterall. thank you for reading though. i keep wanting to try new things out, and sometimes they work, and sometimes they fail terribly!',0,166,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14691,2483,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>getting shaking, personal news is bad; getting it <i>via email</a> is worse.</tt>',1,89,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14692,2483,'2002-02-28 18:54:22',156,'lotus','i always think it is better to reply, than to read something and move on without acknowledging that it stirred/kicked/dazzled a response you can feel in your finger prints. but i almost didn\\\'t respond to this, because i didn\\\'t know what to say. well, i did, but it seemed inappropriate.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhy does it feel wrong to say this piece was beautiful? that it made me ache, yes, but admire it all at the same time. \r\n<br><br>\r\nthank you',0,446,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14693,2480,'2002-02-28 19:02:56',124,'gus','when I was 9 or so I had liek 7 or 8 teeth removed. some of them are about an inch long, root included. I have them in a little envelope. they\\\'re gross.... but really cool.<p> Now I have to get all my wisdom teeth out too... I only have 3, I am putting it off which is stupid since my dental ins will expire in like 4 months.',0,328,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14694,2480,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','let\\\'s share the pain, gus.  s5 can make enough for two.',2,58,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14695,2481,'2002-02-28 20:57:39',151,'stella','I stared smoking when I was 9. I quit, cold turkey, at 23. How? I saw what the 40 years of smoking did to my mothers skin. She looks all leathery and yucky. I guess I\\\'m vain, but that thought pretty much did it. That, and the fact that a very nice boy stopped dating me when he found out that I smoked. I figured that the pool of boys available to me would be much larger if I didn\\\'t have a nasty habit. And that proved to be true. Quitting was hard, very hard and you\\\'ll only succeed if you REALLY don\\\'t want to do it anymore. I had quit several times before this one, but in my heart wasn\\\'t ready to (I loved it too), and wasn\\\'t successful until this last time. That was 7 years ago... ',0,706,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14696,2482,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh lotus you are such a lovely writer.',2,38,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14697,739,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>ps, i don\\\'t get mad, really.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, sawks should write here.  he rocks.</p>',2,90,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14698,2481,'2002-02-28 22:11:09',174,'Mad Moll','I sometimes worry about girls being turned off by smoking, but on the other hand, a girl who smokes is a <i>big</i> turn-on for me, so I guess it evens out. I don\\\'t think it\\\'s so much a matter of addiction or about the people around me (I only have 1 other friend who smokes), I think I could only quit if <i>I</i> honestly stopped enjoying it.',6,350,151,0,'24.190.43.87','en'),(14699,2483,'2002-02-28 23:09:03',204,'Nastchenka Morova',' i agree. there is some part of pain that is beautiful--a purer, harsher beauty than pleasure. that deep peace that comes when you can sob no longer.',1,149,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(14700,2481,'2002-02-28 23:14:22',204,'Nastchenka Morova','my two cents worth on this is, don\\\'t let anyone else pressure you into either smoking or not smoking. You can\\\'t let yourself get into the frame of mind where you do it because it looks good on someone else. I never started smoking, and even though my girfriend smokes and it seems that everyone in my age group smokes, I won\\\'t start. Same thing, if I did smoke, I wouldn\\\'t let someone manipulate me into stopping.',0,425,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(14701,2480,'2002-03-01 00:06:00',204,'Nastchenka Morova','I went to the dentist for the first time in 3 years. He was nice to me...he said that I looked pretty good for flossing (never) I lied and said I flossed once a week. He taught me this trick: jam your toothbrush up in your gums until they bleed--once a month or so. The blood will flush out all the plaque that makes your gums nasty and swollen. Then, tie a knot in your floss. This gets all the stuff that is down in the recess between your teeth. Summary: blood is good. Makes healthy teeth.',0,493,0,0,'199.4.18.2','en'),(14702,2480,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','that is really evil!  did your dentist tell you how much it would hurt to brush your teeth days after that torture?',5,115,204,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14703,2480,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','yeah, i\\\'m not sure i buy the \\\'jam things into your gums\\\' technique. i\\\'d rather just floss gently. it would be nasty to see the result of years of scarred gums from jamming your toothbrush in them...',0,210,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14704,2443,'2002-03-01 02:55:10',222,'satan','bulls on parade.\r\n\r\nsure 3,000 people died in one horrific day. it sure will make a good movie someday. mel gibson will star as a fire fighter who saves pamala anderson from the towers. U2 will do the soundtrack, the film will be shot in holywood and america will be proud.\r\n\r\nwhy doesn\\\'t 3 billion people living on less than $2 dollars a day get your flags and guns out? because flags are self serving. they are useless pieces of cloth done up with a song and a dance. can someone who is waving their flag post sept 11 please explain in detail why now? why the sudden wave of compasion? i suppose this is the world\\\'s only tragedy? no, it is america\\\'s tragedy. i repeat, your flag is self serving. your flag is killing the world. the sooner people stop buying into nationalism the sooner humanity can evolve. stop singing about borders and divides. stop chosing your heors and vilians based on geography. please turn off america and see the world with new eyes.\r\n\r\ni don\\\'t believe the stock market has to be regulated. the stock market has to go down. it is a straight up sham that has as much substance as american morality.',6,1137,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14705,2441,'2002-03-01 02:58:39',203,'insane to chiamattt','i don\\\'t know you and have no wish to grow spears for the kings of france. keep my name out of your mouth. the reason god gave me three legs was to teach myself how not to walk.',0,179,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14706,2460,'2002-03-01 03:01:45',203,'insane to hool','please stop taking pitcures of me. i ventured into the light for a brief moment and was quickly returning to my sanctuary of darkness. the sun burns my thoughts. please leave me be.',6,181,0,0,'24.68.8.198','en'),(14707,2460,'2002-03-01 05:38:08',177,'pony','is that a picture of Garth Brooks in the window?',4,48,0,0,'209.239.204.113','en'),(14708,2443,'2002-03-01 06:50:17',59,'s5','s/satan/troll/',6,14,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14709,2482,'2002-03-01 07:13:42',175,'Paul G','So, <b>home</b> has a physical and a metaphysical dimension.  The things that you could touch remained in place.  But, the comfort and familiarity that were connected to home were derived from the things that you could not see or touch.  Love was like a hammock hung between parents, in which rest was found.\r\n\r\n<p>I don\\\'t know.  In a better world children would rein in egoism.  Still, it\\\'s a lot to ask of any two people not to show their humanity.  It\\\'s too easy to forget that children feel more than they can express.  Adults become so callous.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Your content is different, but your style is rich and textural and flowing like that which is employed by <b>gus</b>.</p> ',0,691,0,0,'65.229.226.241','en'),(14710,2487,'2002-03-01 08:12:33',154,'ryan','i like that there are no comments on this one.',1,46,0,0,'216.26.60.112','en'),(14711,2489,'2002-03-01 08:15:46',196,'jasonp','hmmm, <B>Bon Jovi\\\'s - Living on a Prayer</B><BR>\nI was working at Pizza Hat as a deliver driver, my first job at 18 right after HS graduation.<BR>\nIt brings back that very specific memory of being in the lounge area with the other drivers, folding boxes, talking, hanging out, eating pizza -- and that song was playing all the time. That song really encapsulated working there. The other \\\'87 PizzaHut song that summer was <b>U2 - With or Without You</b> which you couldn\\\'t escape from on the top 40 station, which was all that the AM radio in my delivery truck could get. <BR><BR>\nI just went over about a hundred songs in my head and when I first heard them.<BR>\nI could write an entire book making lists like this...<BR>',0,731,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(14712,2488,'2002-03-01 08:29:44',185,'orion','spike her coke with ajax.  >:-)',1,31,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14713,2489,'2002-03-01 08:39:06',154,'ryan','that\\\'s a good song, see the thorn twist in my side, and all that.  D, A,  Bm, G then  repeat. stuff. ',0,104,0,0,'216.26.60.112','en'),(14714,2443,'2002-03-01 08:44:19',154,'ryan','estimated percentage change in fuel efficiency of an suv if it flies a small american flag from its antenna: -0.5%',0,114,0,0,'216.26.60.112','en'),(14715,2487,'2002-03-01 09:11:01',196,'jasonp','Self-destruction through substance abuse can be glamorous... true.<BR>\nI have a whole catalog of stories but I don\\\'t like to retell them anymore since they\\\'re not as amusing as they used to be and that\\\'s not where it\\\'s at as far as I\\\'m concerned.<BR>\nHere are/were my own personal idols of glamorous and romantic self-destruction through reckless substance abuse:<BR>\n<UL>\n<LI>No One Here Get\\\'s Out Alive - top of the list definitely. Jim Morrison was an adolescent demi-god for me. I read this bio at the age of 14 in one sitting. Later a major inspiration to do a lot of acid and drink heavily.\n<LI>Tom Wolfe, Electric Kool Aid Acid Test - more intellectual but still firmly established a nostalgic yearning for a 60\\\'s type coming of age that would never materialize.\n<LI>Jack Kerouac, On the Road - never finished this but it\\\'s required beat reading.\n<LI>Hunter S. Thompson\\\'s Fear and Loathing - absolutely hilarious. A great read. Inspired me to try and drive on every substance I\\\'ve ever tried. You haven\\\'t lived until you\\\'ve driven around town on acid with a 40oz of OE in between your legs at 3am.\n<LI>metaphyical books by Robert Anton Wilson and Tim Leary - ditto more fodder for psychedelic exploration\n<LI> the movie Repo Man - not sure why I liked this movie so much, but I loved it. Not even a drug movie but it was still an inspiration to drive around all night on speed.\n</UL>\n<BR><BR>\nWell, it seems like your common sense already tells you what you\\\'re doing is wacked so no need to comment on it in any other way. <BR>\nI hope you take it easy though before something catastrophic happens. You *can* get over it.<BR>\nI stopped drinking for almost two years. Had my first beer last week. My original goal was only to stop for 1 year anyway. First it was a single beer. Then last night, a tall-boy of malt liquor. The \\\"pull\\\" to drink more and the subsequent feeling like shit the next morning make me realize that I hadn\\\'t missed anything the last 21 months to want to go back.<BR>\nI don\\\'tf eel like breaking in my liver and brain again to go back to old habits.<BR>\nI won\\\'t be *religious* about not drinking but for the most part it\\\'s no more for me. That and an occasional toke every few months or so, that\\\'s it for me.<BR>\n\n\n',2,2301,0,0,'68.8.65.87','en'),(14716,2488,'2002-03-01 13:55:39',123,'dcc','once you\\\'re there you\\\'ll have to be doing paper work, not design. which is worse? soon you won\\\'t wonder why she\\\'s so mindless. after spending hours and hours in a day pushing paper you\\\'ll understand.',0,214,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14717,2489,'2002-03-01 14:09:15',123,'dcc','<br>\r\n<b>Guns\\\'n Roses: Welcome to the Jungle</b> - Memories of my friend having snuck a ghetto blaster to school (complete with batteries) and rockin\\\' out in the schoolyard during recess. Sometimes I wish I could just have a nice 20 minute break to run outside. I also wish that 20 minutes actually was a long time like it was back in elementary school. \\\"... It\\\'s all fun \\\'n gaaames!\\\"\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Aphex Twin: SAW2</b> - Since the cd has little with track names. It was also my discovery that the world of music is more than just guitars and rock and concerts, but can be something more subtle and sensual.\r\n<p>\r\n<b>Jill Scott: Gotta Get Up (Another Day)</b> - My realization that someone, somewhere, at any given time is very likely thinking and dreaming something very similar to my thoughts. It makes me realize that i am still human and everyone around me is too. that we\\\'re all still connected in some primal way; genetic memory if you will.',1,963,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14718,2487,'2002-03-01 14:43:38',74,'jo','i, too, read that biography of jim morrison. that and 3 others. i was 14. it was a thing.<BR>\r\ni also read pretty much every single piece of beat literature.<BR>\r\nright now i\\\'m reading the biography of tim and jeff buckley.<BR>\r\ni swear to <b>god</b>, i read normal things, too.',0,281,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14719,2482,'2002-03-01 14:49:33',156,'lotus to o','you are such a lovely person! that comment totally made my day. btw, i went to look for your articles, but i couldn\\\'t find any?',0,130,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14720,2482,'2002-03-01 14:56:29',156,'lotus to Paul G','when i reread this after posting it, i thought, good God, this is terrible and juvenile and etc etc, but it was written very sincerely, and on some level, it is still true (sweeping generalizations and all)\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nyou are such a considerate and thoughtful reader. you make me want to write well.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni went back and read a few of gus\\\'s pieces. i plan to read all of them over the next couple of days. but i had to stop after reading one on bibles and muslims and gus\\\'s thoughts, which just, which affected me so much i had to leave the computer and go for a walk. i\\\'m a muslim, and recent events just, they make me frantic. not just sept 11, but what is happening in india right now, the burning of the train, and the burning of innocent muslim\\\'s homes, the stabbings. and palestine, i don\\\'t even know what to feel anymore. it all seems so hopeless. i want to stop reading the news because every day i am shocked by the depravity of humans. i was talking to my dad today, and i was so frustrated i kept having to mash my hands into my eyes to stop crying so i could finish my sentences. he said when he was growing up, back in my homeland, the same thing happened. as a kid, he saw hindus and muslims fight. and it was the, the umm, \\\'goondas\\\' (the thugs) who would throw cow dung inside the homes of their enemies, and use the name of their religion as justification. the delinquents would do all this stuff, and the real muslims, they were busy praying in mosques and studying islam. islam means peace. *peace* violence, especially violence against innocents, is a sin! it is <i><b>PROHIBITED</i></b>\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nsorry, i\\\'ve gone on this insane tangent, and this is the wrong place to post this, and i don\\\'t know.',0,1755,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14721,2486,'2002-03-01 15:20:17',156,'lotus','thank you. *hug of gratitude*',0,29,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14722,2487,'2002-03-01 15:22:23',156,'lotus','<i>\\\"what does trixie\\\'s soul look like? he was in the library today looking at art books. one of them contained a reproduction of a painting by matisse - \\\"the joy of life.\\\" the colors were vibrant, clashing, unfettered. in the center, a ring of figures danced in a meadow. couples embraced. women and men exulted in their nudity. he thought his soul might look something like that.\\\"</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\ni love matisse, and i can see this so clearly.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni will use Paul\\\'s phrase: \\\'make more\\\'',0,516,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14723,2486,'2002-03-01 16:34:38',74,'jo','oh my god i miss little kid hugs. by far the best part about working with children. hugging all day.',0,100,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14724,2489,'2002-03-01 18:18:05',206,'josta','fun topic, jeeff! jasonp, whenever i think of pizza hut i think of REM, specifically \\\"Man on the Moon,\\\" and vice versa. i don\\\'t know whether this is true everywhere, but where i live pizza huts always have these wonderful little semi-arcades with a galaga machine, a street fighter machine and a jukebox, and the jukebox always seems to have a lot of REM.',2,364,0,0,'204.210.196.23','en'),(14725,2489,'2002-03-01 18:18:21',17,'austin','ever since i found out bon jovi is a character on ally mcbeal, my television viewing has gone from an hour or two a week of watching Magnum P.I. re-runs to three or four because <i>i cant wait</i> to see <B>bon jovi</B> do one of his impromptu guitar tracks to some unsuspecting baby or old woman on the street or just serenading ally after she had a long day of berating fools in the court room.',3,396,196,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14726,2488,'2002-03-01 18:21:47',206,'josta','<a href=\\\"http://www.thecomplainet.com\\\">new year\\\'s resolution</a>',3,73,0,0,'204.210.196.23','en'),(14727,2488,'2002-03-01 18:25:13',171,'myaliasisclever','that is not  entirely true. she designs, too. she also gets paid more. granted, she has much more responsibility. however, i think i have much more experience as an art director than she does. how can she begin to critique someone\\\'s design if her own design style is dryer than palm springs on a warm summer day. \r\n\r\ni am just concerned that the president of the company is going to think i am a slow worker because he hasnt any idea what is going on. i suppose if he mentioned something to me. i could just explain to him the situation. however, i dont know how good it would be to make waves in my first few weeks or months in this company. \r\n\r\n-s',0,652,0,0,'216.172.239.48','en'),(14728,2488,'2002-03-01 18:32:09',83,'adamajah','<p>don\\\'t worry, bad managers always get what they deserve in the end</p>',1,75,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14729,2488,'2002-03-01 19:27:18',177,'pony','i highly recommend keeping samples of all your work for each project [and especially anything with commentary marked on it]. if the place where you work in worth anything they\\\'ll have some kind of review process setup. that\\\'s your chance to show people what you\\\'ve been doing [including the process of revisions you\\\'ve had to deal with]. if they don\\\'t do group reviews or you review only with that specific manager then ask for time with other executives with the comment that you work best that way [communication between all departments].\r\n\r\n<p>...on the other side of things, having a file of all of this is going to help you in the long run. i did this to some extent but not anywhere near what i NOW wish i had done. it helps with motivation when you\\\'re down, makes it easy to put portfolios together for future projects, and is a visual barometer of how your style might be evolving.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i now keep what i call a log book diary and make notes on where my head is at when i\\\'m doing stuff, what was easy, what was hard, anything really that will tell me later on down the road what was going on at the time....cause i certainly ain\\\'t gonna remember five years from now.</p>\r\n\r\n[ps: when anyone marks your comps or sends you revisions be sure to have them initial or sign it. i\\\'ve had people flatout lie about stuff when it suited their needs and i wasn\\\'t thorough enough to make sure i had absolute proof it was done via their authority.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>okay. enough of that!</p>',4,1513,171,0,'209.239.202.75','en'),(14730,2489,'2002-03-01 20:00:12',90,'leonard','HA HA HA. don\\\'t lie. you want to see him in his tight jeans.',6,63,17,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(14731,2487,'2002-03-01 20:32:21',148,'jeeff','jo, \\\"it was a thing.\\\"  hurrah!',0,36,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14732,2487,'2002-03-01 20:46:42',74,'jo','it was <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/doors/\\\">totally a thing</a>.',0,72,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14733,2488,'2002-03-01 21:55:20',120,'joyrider','our art director is rad.  our gm is rad.  our clients suck, though.  the problem one of them, anyway) with working for entertainment industry clients is they check proofs at 3am after snorting an 8ball.  when they get the final product they flip out and *insist* they never saw proofs.  grr.',0,291,0,0,'216.210.186.130','en'),(14734,2487,'2002-03-01 22:03:17',177,'pony','<blockquote>\\\"at eleven years old, i had poems like \\\"ode to the death of my cock\\\" memorized\\\"</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<p>...that just took me back to my childhood days of sneaking around grandma\\\'s house to watch monty python on the upstairs tv, reading shakespeare\\\'s othello, and sifting through the hustler magazines i\\\'d found in my mom\\\'s closet. by the time i was twelve she was giving me books like Clockwork Orange, Clan of the Cave Bear, the Jungle, and anything else i asked for. i think maybe she noticed my \\\'advanced\\\' interests....mom certainly didn\\\'t. which was probably a good thing.</p>',4,620,74,0,'209.239.210.101','en'),(14735,2487,'2002-03-01 22:47:42',124,'gus','clan of the cave bear is advanced? my 5th grade teacher read it to us. hm. <br>\r\nAll I can think about is this poor woman, turning 50, probably not so happy about being 50, and she cant even get a family member to show up to the lonely franchise lunch restaurant. It\\\'s her birthday, and she\\\'s eating alone.  ',0,314,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14736,2482,'2002-03-02 00:05:08',175,'Paul G','<p>Just for the sake of clarity: I think that it\\\'s <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2189.txt\\\"><b>Cases of Bibles</b> <i>every day is christmas eve</i></a> that you are referring to.  <b>gus</b> seemed to be very careful to show great respect for the three faiths - Islam, Buddhism, and Christianity - that were mentioned in the article.  The subtle mockery, if there was any, appeared to be of those Christians who attempted to convert people without really understanding what they were involved in (I think).  And <b>gus</b> was careful to reveal the Christian background that gave the standing to make the critique.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It\\\'s a brave thing to deal, in a sensitive way, with the religious divides that seem to be the roots of so much of the conflict in our world - or that provide the means for ambitious leaders to motivate people to harm each other.  None of us have clean hands.  Everyone can point to a time and place in which they were someone\\\'s victim, and use that memory to inflame the passions of their community.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I hope, <b>lotus</b>, that you\\\'ll keep writing and sharing all the more now.</p> ',0,1159,0,0,'65.230.18.217','en'),(14737,2482,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','my articles are written under \\\"olivia\\\" :)',1,47,156,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14738,2487,'2002-03-02 00:47:43',177,'pony','\\\'advanced\\\' meant content topics not necessarily quality of writing. i\\\'m wondering if we\\\'re talking the same book...Jean M Auel? 480 pages filled with myriad rampant prehistoric sex scenes? i can\\\'t even begin to imagine someone reading that outloud to a fifth grade class. i am seriously destabilizing here.',4,321,124,0,'209.239.199.216','en'),(14739,2489,'2002-03-02 01:08:35',200,'chiclet','<p>\r\nOHMIGOD i had no idea.  now i\\\'ll have to start watching ally again, dammit.\r\n<p>\r\nbon jovi to me means playing spin the bottle at 6th grade birthday parties.\r\n</p>',0,171,17,0,'207.172.166.117','en'),(14740,2482,'2002-03-02 01:13:27',156,'lotus','oh Paul, and everyone else, <br>\r\nsorry if i was not clear -- i was moved by gus and his obvious tolerance. there are too few people in the world as open minded as him. i\\\'m sorry if my message seemed like it was criticising him. i meant the opposite. bleh.',0,259,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(14741,2482,'2002-03-02 01:14:04',156,'lotus','yay! i will go look at olivia now.',0,34,0,0,'24.156.95.178','en'),(14742,2487,'2002-03-02 02:00:57',124,'gus','I remember there were some sex type things, but having never read it with my own eyes, maybe he left something out for us. or maybe it was some other bear book, we may never know.',0,179,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14743,2487,'2002-03-02 04:33:46',177,'pony','mmmmm, gratuitous book sex and puberty. sorry for getting off topic Trixie. ',1,76,124,0,'209.239.197.186','en'),(14744,2489,'2002-03-02 05:15:08',124,'gus','in high school me and a friend of mine went around telling everyone that bon jovi meant \\\"good gravy\\\" in italian. absolutely no one questioned us on that.',0,159,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14745,2482,'2002-03-02 06:02:13',124,'gus','hi, gus is a girl! him\\\'s a her. hi hi. this has nothing to do with anything, except well.. you know.',0,103,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14746,2482,'2002-03-02 06:08:21',124,'gus','oh, and also, thanks for the nice comments I\\\'ve gotten out of this comment thread as well. If I werent so sleepy, I would write a new swinney RIGHT NOW just because.',0,168,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14747,2482,'2002-03-02 07:01:32',148,'jeeff','while we\\\'re clarifying things, i write my articles under the pseudonym \\\"austin swinney\\\"',0,96,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14748,2490,'2002-03-02 07:02:02',124,'gus','If I wasnt so sleepy, I would have edited this a LOT more. It\\\'s basically in the same form it was in when I originally emailed it to some friends almost a year ago. ah well.',0,176,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14749,2482,'2002-03-02 07:03:34',124,'gus','ok i gave in and posted \\\"a new swinney RIGHT NOW just because.\\\" and now I go to sleep.',0,92,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14750,2274,'2002-03-02 10:26:17',154,'ryan','<img src=\\\"http://us.news1.yimg.com/us.yimg.com/p/rids/20020228/s/1014875581.3825721455.jpg\\\">',0,98,0,0,'216.26.60.81','en'),(14751,2274,'2002-03-02 10:35:28',59,'s5','i fucking hate nelly furtado. BURN! BURN!',6,41,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14752,2274,'2002-03-02 11:19:03',62,'axe','nelly, i long to penetrate your large, spherical head.',6,54,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14753,2472,'2002-03-02 14:49:25',195,'matt','this is very nice. poignant. <I>the day we played rodeo</I> reminds me of that chris isaak quote about pushing a girl in a cardboard box like it\\\'s a car.',1,156,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(14754,2487,'2002-03-02 14:51:56',195,'matt','paul who? of tarsus? somehow, to my mind, that seems to fit. i like to think of my drug use as sacramental.',1,107,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(14755,2472,'2002-03-02 17:09:44',65,'chris isaak','<tt>    \\\"It\\\'s easy to find someone who wants to go to bed.  But \r\n   it\\\'s real hard to find somebody who\\\'ll let you say, \\\"Look, \r\n I got a cardboard box and a carpeted floor.  Here\\\'s my  plan. You get in the box and hold this flashlight and I\\\'ll \r\n push you around like you\\\'re in a car.\\\"            \r\n<br><br> - singer Chris Isaak in Mademoiselle magazine</tt>',4,387,0,0,'66.123.206.159','en'),(14756,2488,'2002-03-02 18:21:12',75,'tyler','hi. send an anonymous letter to him thinly disguising the people involved. it can\\\'t really backfire on you, because you will have one of my favorite concepts on your side: plausible deniability.',1,197,0,0,'165.247.164.146','en'),(14757,2472,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh i used to love that game!  i\\\'ll play!',1,43,65,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14758,2274,'2002-03-03 07:48:56',69,'starlajo','i think you should leave nelly alone. she seems nice.\r\n\r\nalso: baby got back.',0,77,0,0,'203.96.111.200','en'),(14759,2274,'2002-03-03 09:12:29',154,'ryan','born and raised in victoria, british columbia, to parents of portuguese descent, nelly furtado has always been surrounded by music. her mother is a former church choir singer, while her father -- a fan of portuguese music styles, known as \\\"fado\\\" -- sings as a pastime. her grandfather played instruments, and even her great-uncle was a renowned portuguese marching band score composer. \r\n<p>\r\nall of these family elements inspired nelly\\\'s passion for music and a broad spectrum of musical genres at that. by the age of four, young nelly was playing the ukulele and singing in portuguese and english, and was the trombone player of a jazz band, concert band and marching band, from nine to eighteen. her vocal chords and dance skills were also useful with her involvement in musical plays.\r\n<p>\r\n<h3> hip-hop\\\'s what she\\\'s got</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nshe knew early on in her childhood that she wanted to be a performer, and her dreams were realized via several chance opportunities and events throughout her life. nelly received a tape recorder and microphone when she was eight years old, which served as a key part of her musical influences. \r\n<p>\r\nexposed to the rush of new hip-hop acts spawning in the early \\\'90s, nelly became fascinated by the sounds of tlc, bel biv devoe, ice-t, mariah carey, and the new sampling methods used at the time. with the help of her tape recorder, nelly would sample the sounds that impressed her, similar to those used by popular \\\'90s acts. she sang into her microphone and recorded original sounds with the \\\"scratch effect\\\" of her keyboard.\r\n<p>\r\na self-proclaimed r&b and hip-hop lover, nelly was later exposed to the british rock sounds of the mid \\\'90s, after a trip to her ancestral portugal. teenage nelly\\\'s eyes were now open to groups such as u2, radiohead, oasis, while she discovered portishead thanks to a mix tape that a friend of hers made. the tribal beats and bossa nova sounds evident in her music come from the music she was exposed to later on, such as a brazilian compilation album that combined african and portuguese styles.\r\n<p>\r\n<h3> whoa, nelly, wow!</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nthe album is the product of her participation in a toronto young performer\\\'s talent show at the age of 18. the manager of the philosopher kings, a popular funk/pop group, who was also present at the show, was impressed by nelly\\\'s talent.\r\n<p>\r\nthe only real recording experience she had up to this point was singing back-up vocals for her friend\\\'s toronto-based hip-hop group. but that was enough to cut a demo tape with the recording team of gerald eaton and brian west (of the philosopher kings). after much convincing on the part of eaton and west, nelly returned to toronto to record more tracks.\r\n<p>\r\nthe fruit of this work is what led to nelly\\\'s record-contract with dreamworks records. her album, entitled whoa nelly!, which has already spawned the hit singles \\\"i\\\'m like a bird,\\\" \\\"turn off the light\\\" and \\\"...on the radio,\\\" was released in the fall of 2000.\r\n<p>\r\nno stranger to touring, nelly was part of the lilith fair tour, with a bill consisting of female performers such as beth orton, chrissie hynde, and of course, sarah mclachlan (who is one of nelly\\\'s idols). the singer-songwriter also contributed a track to the soundtrack for the film, brokedown palace. \r\n<p>\r\n<h3>a soaring bird </h3> \r\n<p>\r\nand she has her share of fans. elton john praised nelly\\\'s work and talent \\\"on the record\\\"; she was invited to pay tribute to aretha franklin at a tribute concert for the queen of soul; she released a duet with rap producer and singer missy elliot, \\\"get ur freak on,\\\" appearing on the lara croft: tomb raider soundtrack; and she was invited to perform at the area: one tour, and was named one of the most talented and beautiful women in the world by the tour organizer, moby. \r\n<p>\r\nher critically-acclaimmed debut album not only showcases the mix of musical backgrounds -- african beats, hip-hop, folk, urban style, bossa nova, and the portuguese \\\"fado\\\" -- it also highlights a new talent that will definitely soar like a bird.\r\n<p>\r\nwhat else do you need to know?',0,4176,0,0,'216.26.3.181','en'),(14760,2274,'2002-03-03 10:01:41',62,'o to starlajo','<p>nelly does <b>NOT</b> have \\\"back\\\".</p>\r\n\r\n<p>her music reaches my ears as disposable poppy crap.  i don\\\'t like it.  i wish they\\\'d stop playing her in places like the supermarket where i have to go.</p>',1,216,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14761,2488,'2002-03-03 19:47:33',148,'jeeff','that\\\'s incredibly cowardly.',0,30,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14762,2486,'2002-03-03 22:33:10',151,'stella','Last week I went to an elementary school here in town to observe a classroom dedicated to autistic children. The woman who runs the class, after an hour or so, brought me to a \\\'regular\\\' first grade class to introduce me to a little girl who had spent three years in her class, and who had progressed enough to be mainstreamed into a non-special ed class. What stuck me the hardest about this little button wasn\\\'t that you\\\'d never know that she was autistic unless someone told you, but her openess. Upon being introduced to me, the little girl took me hand and started rubbing my fingers, almost like a soft caress, absentmindedly while we chatted. That alone filled my heart with such joy. ',0,703,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(14763,2490,'2002-03-03 22:43:16',0,'testing','testing',4,7,0,1,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14764,2492,'2002-03-04 02:57:54',75,'tyler','yep.',1,4,0,0,'165.247.164.146','en'),(14765,2492,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','reminds me of the story about the dog who got a credit card application... ;-)  but don\\\'t take the dog/you dichotomy of interest too far, odds are that some bored sole stumbled across the dog\\\'s resume and now, becuase of the ethereal osmosis that silly stuff undergoes on teh \\\'net, all of their bored office monkey friends are looking at it and forwarding it to  their bored office monkey friends and ...  now if Dog gets an actual offer, we know the tech economy has gone to the dogs... (*rimshot*) ',1,509,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14766,2491,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','don\\\'t sweat it.  look at it this way, you showed you cared about your friend by showing up and having a good time and contributing to the general atmosphere of mirth at the party.  I\\\'m all about spending time with my friends on my birthdays, I don\\\'t give a rat\\\'s patootie about gifts.  Physical things are just the   \r\ntransient crud we burden ourselves with, the true gifts in life are companionship and love.  look at it this way, you gave her a story to smile and laugh about in the years to come.  :-)  so chill.',1,528,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14767,2491,'2002-03-04 07:14:25',185,'orion','oh, and apparently I am Not In The System if you click my nick.  how cool is that? ;-)',1,86,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14768,2494,'2002-03-08 04:52:53',65,'le','10 points for good use of the word \\\"caryatid.\\\"\r\n<br><br>\r\nthere were some people right behind me at the beachwood sparks/modestmouse/etc show who were TALKING REALLY LOUD in valley girl voices about some kind of drama. that would have been ok if we\\\'d have been far back from the stage, but we were really close to the stage. isn\\\'t really close to the stage reserved for people who actually give a shit about the band, rather than people who need to have a conversation about their interpersonal problems? i gave them more than one pointed glare. it totally sucked, i had to  edge my way away from them.\r\n',4,616,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14769,2494,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','unless i REALLY LOVE THE BAND BECAUSE THEY TOTALLY ROCK OUT DOOOOOD all i want is a table and chair in the back to sit at during rock shows. and wayyy up in the back, too, tilted down just a little bit, oohh yeah just like that, so i can see the band over all big heads and the big hair.',3,287,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14770,2494,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>there need to be two sections at every gig:  me, and other people.  the only people that are ever around me at gigs are abnormally tall, talking, smoking, headbanging, screaming, elbowing, sweating, non-personal-space-respecting fucktards.</p>\r\n',6,248,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14771,2494,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','but you know, bimbo\\\'s is the best venue on earth. i want that statement to be here, in writing. you can sit or stand anywhere and still hear the band at a pleasantly loud volume with crystal clear sound, sometimes while catching a glimpse of a passing hot cocktail waitress.',3,277,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14772,2494,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','apart from that one bitch waitress who must have some weird vendetta against us.   while we waited 45 minutes to order some chips, we doodled this:<br>\r\n<img src=\\\"http://saturn5.com/olivia/images/misc/waitress_bitch.jpg\\\" border=\\\"1\\\">',4,244,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14773,2494,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if there was ever a person for dancing, rocking out, and otherwise getting your bootyshake on, that would be me. however, i think more of us bootyshaker types should realize that its not always appropriate to getcha\\\' groove on in every venue. there should be a rule that if a certain percentage of the crowd <i>is not dancing</i> then you shouldnt, either; it can detract from the show and annoy alot of people.  <br><br>i was at a show this weekend and, having shaken said ass to mr. dj for a few hours, i went downstairs to catch the band in a decidedly non-ass-shakable set. but lo and behold, there was a single guy, smack in the middle of the crowd, attempting to mosh to tambourine-jangly-hipster-euro-pop, annoying the hell out of the lucky, and elbows-to-the-face for the unlucky.<br><br>this kind of thing is annoying. for ass-shaking, god created the dancefloor. for rocking out in place, the good lord done give us rock venues.</tt>',1,954,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14774,2493,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i always find it interesting when pieces have disclaimers/explanations in their preface.</tt>',1,101,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14775,2490,'2002-03-08 04:50:11',86,'egg','are you talking about \\\"chiang mai\\\"?',0,41,0,0,'203.146.223.82','en'),(14776,2496,'2002-03-08 04:52:11',66,'Peter','<br><tt>as for where youre from, why not write a <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2333.txt\\\">story</a>?</tt>',1,134,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14777,2491,'2002-09-17 20:56:11',148,'jeeff','this is where you tell her that you gave her the most special gift of all.  magical sneezing soap.',3,98,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14778,2493,'2002-03-04 16:55:12',0,'shanE','I agree with you Adam. the relationships doesn t have to be with another and I believe that too many people base their worth (like yo said) on what other s value and believe.\r\nShit if you think it s bad over there come check out Asia. Goddamn Seoul is very superficial! \r\nThis whole belief does give people a way to spend their time... you know the whole I m better than you shit.',3,380,0,0,'211.168.56.102','en'),(14779,2494,'2002-03-04 17:04:46',0,'drew','<p>I Am Not Alone.  but really, i do think talkers are much worse.  at least we didn\\\'t have to struggle to hear the band (though that might be because they had turned their amps up past 11 to sonic death ray).  and at least these two were not like the giant frat boy who stood in front of us at the beautiful south concert and pumped his fist to every song.  pumped his fist.  to the beautiful south.  perhaps there is no one right way to show appreciation for a band, and we should all feel free to mosh to the innocence mission and sway prettily to skinny puppy but somehow that seemed quite out of order to me.</p>\r\n<p>i\\\'ve only seen two shows at bimbo\\\'s so far.  one was the charlatans uk, and that was pretty painful because the band is now in a serious-suckage phase from what i can tell.  the other was laurie anderson, and though i sat in the back i had a pretty good experience.  only problems were the big-ass posts in my way and the parking.  oh, and the damn bathroom attendants.  i don\\\'t like social experiences in bathrooms with strangers, particularly not ones involving tips.  i\\\'m pretty good at washing and drying my own hands at this stage.</p>',0,1177,0,0,'65.172.230.2','en'),(14780,2493,'2002-03-04 17:08:52',0,'?','whos adam?',1,10,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14781,2493,'2002-03-04 17:08:55',0,'lotus','interesting post. two things: <br><br>\r\n(1) my a. was the first person to tell me that perhaps, a sustained passion for art (novels/films/poetry/photography/sculpture/paintings/music/dance/etc) could fulfil the human need for a relationship. there are many ways to love, to be in love, even, and with many creatures. not just people. when i first heard this i was so shocked i was rendered speechless. i don\\\'t know if i agree, or at least, i don\\\'t know that this is true for everyone. some people maybe. not me. though i want to not have this consuming desire to be part of couplehood. gah. i don\\\'t know. <br><br>\r\n(2) when i\\\'m writing, when i\\\'m making, i don\\\'t care about \\\'merit\\\', i just want to be absorbed/immersed in it, and just, just please myself. but the moment i reach a point of potential completion with something, i have to know what others think of it. my opinion is most important during the process itself. but afterwards? i care. possibly too much.',0,988,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14782,2496,'2002-06-12 21:13:52',93,'chiamattt','O CANADA<BR>\r\nOUR HOME AND NATIVE LAND<BR>\r\n',1,44,0,0,'66.185.84.79','en'),(14783,2496,'2002-03-14 23:24:39',123,'dcc','i hope that\\\'s not all you know chiam.\r\n<p>but really, i don\\\'t feel much like a patriot, but there\\\'s nothing like a good party on capital hill to remind you of why i like living where i do.',0,197,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14784,2493,'2002-03-04 19:34:50',0,'lifeonmars','<p>disappointed when you find yourself working for the praise of others?\r\n<br>what better reason to work?  Why is self-absorption championed in the notion of creativity?  I don\\\'t believe in it. <br>I wasted most of my young creative life thinking I had to do art out of some pure noble need to express to myself what I already knew.  It was only when i realized that art can fulfill base needs of pride; ego; attention; sexual frustration; that I was able to do some work.  Yeah; its shit and I\\\'ve got a lot of catching up to do; but I will work for all the wrong reasons; money; fame; sex but I will be working and my work will be awesome.</p>',0,650,0,0,'198.96.85.8','en'),(14785,2493,'2002-03-04 21:24:58',0,'lin','<p>lifeonmars: part of the the act of creation can turn into commodification, but in this case, i understand more where hool/adam is coming from. Knowing him personally, he can already do whatever he needs to be quite successful, if that was his desire. I suspect a strong reason for his desire to maintain creativity for his own purposesm rather than the accolades of friends and admirers is because he vies his creative process more as a way of life than a means to support himself. </p>\r\n<p>When creativity (for me) ceases to be about making life better, and more about making life easier, it looses a lot of its truth. It\\\'s possible to survive off of artistic and creative endeavours without bending to the will of those you\\\'re surrounded by. It\\\'s a damn hard thing to be dedicated to your own ideas, while still allowing the influence of others to aide you in your developement. </p>\r\n<p> That\\\'s something I wish I could say I do. I try to try. It\\\'s enough for now. </p>',0,990,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(14786,2494,'2002-03-04 22:32:17',0,'jo','weird. the modest mouse show that *i* went to several months ago was nearly ruined by talkers. the odd thing about that is that these two girls were obviously huge modest mouse fans; they repeatedly IDed the song and the album it was on within the first few milliseconds. the ANNOYING thing was that they did this OUTLOUD.<BR>\r\nthe only way i\\\'ve found to fix this is to get up front and STAY UP FRONT, against the stage, for the duration of the show. then it\\\'s so loud that you don\\\'t hear anything else. :) you get better <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/music/\\\">pictures</a> that way, too.',0,612,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14787,2496,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yay lotus, you rox0r.  i don\\\'t know where to tell people i\\\'m from either.',1,79,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14788,2494,'2002-03-05 20:15:16',59,'s5','someone needs to invent special earplugs that filter out conversations but let through music in all its 110dB glory.',3,116,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14789,2494,'2002-03-05 00:05:02',0,'drew','hey, jo, that white stripes fella is awful cute!  i can see why you got so many pictures of him.',0,96,0,0,'65.172.230.2','en'),(14790,2496,'2002-03-05 02:08:05',185,'orion','Heh, I sort of know where you\\\'re coming from.  I mean, it\\\'s blindingly obvious to anyone that I\\\'m a) American and b) Texan.  But I really don\\\'t know what to say when somebody asks me what town I\\\'m from.  I was born in Houston, spent twelve years in Nacogdoches.  Spent two incredible, mind-expanding years in Denton, and um, five years in Austin. %^) four year degree, what? I \\\"grew\\\" up in Nac, but I really don\\\'t feel anything in common with those people.  It\\\'s like my time there was just a holding pattern, waiting to land a the the real destination.  You could say that I came of age intellectually in Denton (and I\\\'ll always harbor a little love in my heart for the DFW area, well, not FW, but at least D(allas|enton)).  The city I think that I have the most emotional kinship to is Austin.  But I don\\\'t know...',1,849,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(14791,2496,'2002-03-05 02:01:22',17,'austin','you really just have to pick something and stick to your guns.  i am austin from austin and that is the end of the story.  lucky for me that people like to accept this.  in ancient times, cities had creation myths.  i think that is a fantastic idea and decided to appropriate it for myself.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\ni was created in austin, texas.',3,333,185,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14792,2496,'2002-03-05 01:28:18',17,'testing','testing',6,7,0,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14793,2496,'2002-03-05 01:29:45',17,'testing','testing',5,7,17,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14794,2479,'2002-03-05 02:11:48',17,'austin','testing',5,7,124,1,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14795,2496,'2002-03-05 05:10:22',185,'orion','I think I was conceived in Austin but born in Houston.  I guess it makes sense that I\\\'ve always felt the most home in Austin, with Houston perhaps being second.  %^)  Of course, I could just answer \\\"Mars\\\".',1,214,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14796,2496,'2002-03-05 05:11:12',185,'orion','oops, that wasn\\\'t to o, that was to austin.  heh.  probably time to put down the 40.',1,87,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14797,2496,'2002-03-05 05:11:57',185,'orion','m\\\'kay.  odd, it say\\\'s it\\\'s to o when it\\\'s on all.  Congratulations, o, you are everyone.  :-)',1,105,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14798,2479,'2002-03-05 05:43:17',17,'austin','testing \' test\r\n\r\n\r\ntest \" test',4,37,124,1,'64.174.155.52','en'),(14799,2490,'2002-03-05 17:25:27',124,'gus','egg. chaing mai and around.<br>\r\nhey that series list is cool but it would be even cooler if they were in chronological order. yah.',0,131,0,0,'64.160.55.45','en'),(14800,2493,'2002-03-05 23:39:48',202,'lifeonmars','<p>well, I actually have no need to critique hool\\\'s perspective of his own creative process.<br>I was actually responding to the predominant cultural belief that I felt underlies his comments; <br>1. of the nature of art & creative genius as being morally superior to the structure of our society; rather I see art as being directly part of the social structure.  Art (in western society) is a metaphor for capitalism.   \r\n<br>2. of art being an intrinsic solitary process; whose function is soley to be art. this is a modern concept that certainly does not hold true for many cultures in the past and present.<br>\r\nThat\\\'s what I mean when I say I\\\'ll do art for all the wrong reasons.  I was being a little tongue-in-cheek when I said that I\\\'ll work for money, fame and sex. The truth is I\\\'d rather work for those reasons than to put myself above my fellow beings; to say that I\\\'m working for such high-falutin\\\' ideals as my own personal expression and to, god forbid, make the world a better place. That\\\'s for beauty queens. I\\\'m right down there with the hoi-poloi.  I\\\'m not a good person.  I spend most of my free time plotting how to get laid. I\\\'ve never done a thing for anyone else. I think Shakira\\\'s hot. <br>\r\nBut I still want to create.  So I had to ignore the narrative of what an artist should be and do it for my own reasons. I thought I\\\'d finish off this comment with some punchy line about the role of an artist and how I actually am one but I\\\'d rather just say that I don\\\'t think I\\\'m good enough.</p>   \r\n  ',0,1569,0,0,'198.96.85.36','en'),(14801,2493,'2002-03-06 01:24:36',17,'austin','<P>hm, art, yes.</P>\r\n<P>i am not an artist.  some people like to say programming is art.  i think it is not.  that is professionalism looking for validation in virtue.  being creative is an act separate from art.  do i bring things into the world?  yes i do that.</P>\r\n<BR>\r\n<P>_ i have been getting fat.</P>\r\n<P>_ i have not had sex in 2 months.</P>\r\n<P>_ i stopped running recently when i hurt my back.</P>\r\n<P>_  the only inspiration i can find is to sit in a red bar and draw post cards of random thoughts.</P>\r\n<P>_ concerning myself with this fat belly is something i do now too.</P>\r\n<P>_ coding swinney.org is actually just tap dancing, or a seal on a beach ball where i am the seal and this website is a beach ball.</P>\r\n<BR>\r\n<P>i never created anything until i began using the internet.  if it went away, i would rechannel that energy into building demolition cars.  there is a possibility that i might then move on to objects that are not meant for any use welded out of car parts, but again that would not be art, that would be crafts.</P>\r\n<P>i can feel my fat gut right now.  it is hard to sit forward with the fat rollilng forth.</P>\r\n<P>i feel like going to eat some food.</P>\r\n',4,1196,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14802,2496,'2003-03-05 23:09:34',129,'moebius','<br>\r\ngolly. excellent piece again, lotus. and this time, instead of bringing back memories of tropical lands, you\\\'re reminding me of the winters and summers i spent in ontario (first toronto, then oakville). my family ended up there too, for a spell. i came this --> <-- close to becoming a dual us/canadian citizen.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nand so this now leads me to remark (to myself if nobody else): where the heck do <i>i</i> call home? good good question. time for my origin story. keep yr eyes peeled, swinneyites....',1,515,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14803,2493,'2002-03-06 04:41:24',59,'s5','what\\\'s wrong with self-satisfaction? if you create art to receive praise, it\\\'s still self-satisfaction. why not cut out the middle man. seek an audience for context, not praise.',5,183,202,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14804,2496,'2002-03-06 05:59:20',175,'Paul G','Wow - another great story, Lotus.  Your references to Asia, and the Middle East, and England, coupled with your family\\\'s travel to the New World, really make you seem like family.\r\n\r\n<p>Identity is a difficult thing to deal with - like a container of some kind that accumulates experience, but whose exterior is all that most people view.  It is a world of appearances, isn\\\'t it?  Write on, Sister.</p> ',0,409,0,0,'64.156.107.22','en'),(14805,2493,'2002-03-06 06:21:04',175,'Paul G','I don\\\'t know, close to 20 years ago someone told me that: \\\"Art is an attitude embodied in a method.\\\"  It still works for me.',0,133,0,0,'64.156.107.22','en'),(14806,2493,'2002-03-06 06:31:27',176,'Scapegoat','When art is done for praise it places hurdles other then skill in the way of the <i>goal</i>. Artistic expression is not immune to the physical realities of our society. Praise may be acheived easier through elaborate technique that could only be practiced with access to the means of production for such a technique. Art for praise is competitive.<br><br>\r\nWhen art is done due to passion these same hurdles are not as much an issue. If the goal is not praise but personal growth, catharsis, etc. then lesser means of production can provide the same impact.<br><br>\r\nIMHO praise is not a negative but I don\\\'t think that it is ever going to be a source of art that impacts strongly... rambling.....\r\n     ',0,708,0,0,'64.229.163.38','en'),(14807,2494,'2002-03-06 06:43:04',176,'Scapegoat','Mmmm... White Stripes. ',0,23,0,0,'64.229.163.38','en'),(14808,2498,'2002-03-06 06:43:28',175,'Paul G','The forearm touching was sure to turn his head.  The only thing that would have provoked a stronger reaction would have been a thigh/knee grab - that might have caused whiplash.',0,177,0,0,'64.156.107.22','en'),(14809,2493,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>hey, austin.  i think that <a href=\\\"http://praystation.com\\\">programming can be art</a>, depending on your motivation for it and the end result.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s like what paul g says.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>programming or painting or glassblowing, it\\\'s only the vehicle for art, it\\\'s not the art itself.  the finished product is the art, and you get to choose what that is.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>you can paint a room or paint a picture.  the paint part is irrelevant.  the motivation and the result is what\\\'s important. </p>',3,518,17,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14810,2493,'2002-03-06 14:09:54',66,'Peter','<br><tt>Austin: coding as art; thats like saying painting isnt art because people paint their garages or utility-sheds and that isnt art. well, people paint canvasses also.... so, like, people code for professional purposes, yes, so maybe the backend of a financial company\'s site isnt art... but coding can still be (or be used for/as) art, i think... unfortunately, a bulk of the truly artistic coders out there are left-brained enough to not really ever make the leap as \'artists\' (and, consequentially, most of the artists are too non-analytically minded to become true code heroes).<br><br>i might even make a good argument that this site itself is a work of art, and not only because of the writing, but because of the <b><i>concept</b></i> brought to life by hundreds of hours of coding.</tt>',1,471,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14811,89,'2002-03-06 14:33:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>ha. a huge renovation is going down on the floor where i work, and has been for months.  my floor\\\'s bathroom is the only one open to the construction workers; its downright <i>amazing</i> how many of your unspoken bathroom points of etiquette and tact they break daily... as well as leaving chicks-with-dicks graffiti and huge puddles of mis-aimed bodily fluids...</tt>',1,380,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14812,2493,'2002-03-06 14:39:49',202,'lifeonmars','<p>hello scapegoat, you said personal growth, catharsis are superior motivations for art to praise?<br><br>do you know how much bad art exists that came about through personal growth and catharsis?<br><br>Hell, that in itself is a reason to eschew the contemporary noodle of thought concerning the creative process<br><br>\r\nlet\\\'s forget about praise for a second.  It\\\'s not praise I\\\'m after; its the idea of what creative process is necessary in order to create this crazy thing called art.  Maybe writers like you are not seeking direct praise, but you\\\'re seeking to fit your work into the definition of art. and so therefore; you can say to yourself; wow, I did this for my own personal growth and catharsis; it must be art.  Of course I\\\'m exaggerating and being a little cruel.  Personal growth, catharsis, all those things are important to the creation of art and so is praise.  What is praise?  You want people to read your work and understand it; to love it as much as you do.  When I write; I write for an audience (unless I\\\'m writing in my journal). I write constantly with my audience in mind.  And you would say that there is something dishonest, lacking in integrity to do so?  I totally disagree and I think that this is just an important part of creation.<br>Our culture has mythologized the role of the artist; placed him above the mere person.  This trend, in my opinion, has resulted in elitism and bad art.  I don\\\'t know about the realm of fiction so much; but as far as visual art, I\\\'ve worked in tons of art galleries; commercial and public; and I think contemporary art is impotent.</p>     ',0,1635,0,0,'198.96.85.105','en'),(14813,2493,'2002-03-06 14:43:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt>welcome to this installment of \\\"i\\\'ve got an inner art-critic, yes i do!\\\" [or \\\"<b>You Suck:</b> <i>A Treatise on Bad Art</i> by lifeonmars\\\"]. sorry, although all sarcasm is intentional, i just gotta throw down the old <i>\\\'best friendly!\\\'</i></tt>',1,274,202,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14814,2494,'2002-03-06 15:09:30',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>Last Night:</b><br><br><b>The Girl:</b> \\\"yeah, so i heard about these <i>White Stripes</i>, finally...\\\"<br><br><b>Me:</b> \\\"YEEEAHH!!! White Stripes!\\\"</tt>',1,177,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14815,2496,'2002-03-06 15:36:57',156,'lotus','i was thinking about my friend hool\\\'s post on art/creativity/relationships. and i\\\'ve come to the conclusion that i\\\'m never more happy then when i\\\'m in the midst of creating something. and even if i never got any other reward than making it, i would still keep doing it. but if i didn\\\'t get responses. acknowledgement. feedback. if i wasn\\\'t told that yes, someone is out there, looking or reading or listening or touching what i send out into the world, then i would stop sharing it. <br> <br>\r\nthis is just a long way of saying thank you to you all :> and a threat that i\\\'ll keep posting for a while yet!',0,625,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14816,2493,'2002-03-06 15:40:00',156,'lotus','i also made this comment in another thread:\r\n<br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve come to the conclusion that i\\\'m never more happy then when i\\\'m in the midst of creating something. and even if i never got any other reward than that of making it, i would still keep doing it. <br>\r\nbut if i didn\\\'t get responses, acknowledgement, feedback; if i wasn\\\'t told that yes, someone is out there, looking or reading or listening or touching what i send out into the world, then i would stop sharing it.\r\n<br>\r\nit is not about praise, though praise is *sweet* it is about connection. even a \\\'this is terrible\\\' makes me glad i shared something. someone noticed. and cared enough to let me know.',0,682,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14817,2493,'2002-03-06 15:53:18',122,'hool','<p>i tried to comment last night right under austin\\\'s first comment, but swinney was broken and i couldn\\\'t post, and it still doesn\\\'t work under IE, so i\\\'ll give netscape a try:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>i agree with austin. there is a separation. and, i wasn\\\'t talking about art, really. i did mention it, but it wasn\\\'t the focus. it\\\'s one of those words that tends to become the focus as soon as it appears. i wasn\\\'t talking about morality, either. so i don\\\'t feel that i have much to reply with. i wasn\\\'t talking about creating things for money - nothing right/wrong with that.:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>to o: i feel that art is in the process and not the final product.. i heard that on an episode of Northern Exposure when i was younger and it\\\'s stuck with me since. but i think that boils down to a semantic dissection, so there\\\'s no point in arguing it too heavily:</p>\r\n',0,883,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14818,2493,'2002-03-06 17:07:03',175,'Paul G','Whether it\\\'s:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>idea-to-method-to-product, or\r\n<li>idea-to-method/product<\r\n</ul>\r\nthe notion seem to be that any means might be used, \\\"programming or painting or glassblowing, it\\\'s only the vehicle for art, it\\\'s not the art itself.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>Maybe there will be a permanent record of the activity of art-making; maybe there won\\\'t be.  But, somehow, through some means, the artist needs to move beyond the idea phase and communicate.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Vehicle - What\\\'s more important: the auto or the people that it carries?  Of course, there is a great deal of attention paid to the way in which things travel.</p> ',0,635,0,0,'65.230.18.158','en'),(14819,2493,'2002-03-06 17:07:47',202,'lifeonmars','<p>god yeah I got an inner art critic!<br>geez louise, what d\\\'ya think I went to school for?<br>besides, these are not criticisms i\\\'m pulling out of thin air; I know my post-modernist dogma.  I\\\'ve done my readings.  And I\\\'ve had to work with a lot of bad art in my young life.  Imagine staring at bad art all day long, 5 days a week; and then dealing with the pretentious gaggle of artists, clients, and gallery people, blech. That coupled with late nights and early mornings and a lot of art theory can turn anyone into a critic.<br>I\\\'m not trying to pick on hool; and I know he\\\'s very talented; I just think that praise is an excellent motivator to get you working. (I know hool from the distant past and he might remember me as the small yappy dog type; a real pleasure at parties. Not to be taken too seriously.  I like to \\\'discuss\\\'.) Maybe I side with Freud: the artist; seeks love, attention, status, and finding none; turns to art. <br><br>of course I\\\'m being a big hypocrite as I always am; I\\\'m droning on about seeking praise and validation from others while saying things that I know will directly incite criticism. am I doing this because I\\\'m essentially a contradictory, inflamatory person?  Am I writing this nonsense because I\\\'m wearing my angry sweater today? Well, yes. I can never resist a good argument.  The party invitations are fewer these days.<br><br>\r\nBut I also have my own artistic vision.  I think we have a tendency to fall into cliches about what an artist or what art should be.  I think I just get really bored of hearing the same mantras over and over again.<br><br>\r\nBecause I do agree that the process of creating something; writing or painting or whatever is wonderful in and of itself; and is the few moments of joy I experience in my otherwise depressed emotional condition... but what gets me working in the first place?</p>',0,1898,66,0,'198.96.85.214','en'),(14820,2493,'2002-03-06 17:48:59',175,'Paul G','What motivates <b>Lifeonmars</b>? (time is short...must write other things...)\r\n\r\n<p>On the one hand, lifeonmars says: \\\"The truth is I\\\'d rather work for those reasons (money, fame and sex) than to put myself above my fellow beings; to say that I\\\'m working for such high-falutin\\\' ideals as my own personal expression and to, god forbid, make the world a better place. That\\\'s for beauty queens. I\\\'m right down there with the hoi-poloi.\\\"</p>\r\n\r\n<p>And, he/she argues that it is good to: \\\"eschew the contemporary noodle of thought concerning the creative process.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>Because: \\\"Our culture has mythologized the role of the artist; placed him above the mere person. This trend, in my opinion, has resulted in elitism and bad art.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>And yet, he/she has standing to say such things because: \\\"these are not criticisms i\\\'m pulling out of thin air; I know my post-modernist dogma. I\\\'ve done my readings.\\\"\r\n\r\n<p>Is there anything more removed from \\\"the common man\\\" than post-modernist dogma?  Isn\\\'t the reading of such things, and by implication the ideas that flow from the readings, an elite activity?  Those things are the stuff of the real beauty queens of the art world: the ones who mock everyone else.</p>',0,1263,0,0,'65.230.18.158','en'),(14821,2493,'2002-03-06 18:45:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>what the world needs now is post-modernist dogma, sweet post-modernist dogma.<br><br>\r\n<i>[fleeing crowds; a publicity still from an obscure production, acetate slivers of asiatic crowds fleeing, looking up at something awesome: <b>post-modernist dogma</b>.]</i></tt>',1,275,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14822,2493,'2002-03-06 18:58:52',176,'Scapegoat','<b>Lifeonmars</b> Bad art? due to personal growth and catharsis, eh. I think not. First \\\'bad\\\' is so subjective, second just as much \\\'bad art\\\' has been made in the search for praise of this I can assure you.<br><br>\r\nA conversation overheard on the subway of epistemology<br><br> \r\n<b>Postmodernism</b> : \\\"I\\\'m so misunderstood!\\\"<br><br>\r\n\r\n<b>Common Man</b> : \\\"I hear ya there buddy.\\\" ',0,411,0,0,'64.229.163.38','en'),(14823,2493,'2002-03-06 19:22:27',175,'Paul G','<p><b>Post-Modernist Dogma</b>: <i>\\\"I will destroy you and your city\\\'s conventions - right after I finish with Tokyo and Mothra.  Thanks for waiting.\\\"</i></p>\r\n\r\n<p><i>\\\"Not even Tubcat can save you now.\\\"</i></p>\r\n\r\n',0,230,0,0,'65.230.72.207','en'),(14824,2493,'2002-03-06 19:35:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\n<b>tubcat:</b> \\\"you are the urban myth, the dichotomy of classical elementality adorned, smoked by the turgid haze of futurism!\\\"<br><br>\r\n<b>mothra:</b> \\\"take a numba\\\', and dont be a hata\\\', y0!@#$#\\\"</tt>\r\n',1,233,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14825,2493,'2002-03-06 22:29:17',202,'lifeonmars','<p>i see, post-modernism was the <i>wrong</i> term to bally around you guys.<br>i thought cushioning with the derogatory \\\"dogma\\\" might lessen its pretentious blow, but it still seems to raise a few bristles on the back of necks<br>sorry guys, next time i\\\'ll leave those terms in the philosophy 101 classroom. \r\n<br><br>Paul G: so you\\\'re saying that because I\\\'m educated, I\\\'m elitist?  sure I can buy that.  You\\\'re probably right.  However, post-modernist writers are usually seeking to contradict the elitism of modernism, which is the school of thought I fall into, not very original of me I know; but my opinions are very much inspired by what I\\\'ve read. <br>Sigh. I\\\'m not trying to mock anyone.  I\\\'m just trying to include me. </p>\r\n</p>         ',0,779,0,0,'198.96.85.171','en'),(14826,2493,'2002-03-06 22:53:19',59,'chickenman','<font size=\"100\" color=\"#FF0000\">BORK!!!</font>',3,59,0,1,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14827,2493,'2002-03-06 22:55:18',62,'chickenman','<font size=\\\"100\\\" color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">BORK!!!</font>',3,59,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14828,2493,'2002-03-06 23:00:48',66,'dictionary.com','wondering what \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=bally\\\">bally</a>\\\" means? me too. ',1,105,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14829,2493,'2002-03-06 23:04:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>bristling at the mention of \\\'postmodernity\\\'? <i>please</i>.  also, i dont think <i>any</i> term is the <i>wrong term</i> to use <i>around us guys</i>. i think its just that we were cleverly poking fun at your out-from-the-woodwork art-critic rant, thats all. i know i was ;)</tt> ',1,294,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14830,2487,'2002-03-07 00:25:24',88,'deb','You know, the sick part is that while I was reading that, I forgot that you were talking about yourself..',4,105,0,0,'198.139.249.90','en'),(14831,2500,'2002-03-07 02:09:33',130,'treetop','just dont pull out tooo slow, thats how i got here.',1,51,0,0,'172.160.189.83','en'),(14832,1865,'2002-03-07 05:07:22',130,'treetop','hi, this title sucks!',0,21,0,0,'172.160.189.83','en'),(14833,2493,'2002-03-07 07:25:21',175,'Paul G','Hi.  So, we meet again, dictionary.com.  I think that \\\"bally around\\\" was meant to be <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=bandy\\\">\\\"bandy about\\\"</a> - but that is only a guess.\r\n\r\n<p>What struck me as pretentious was not the mention of Post-Modernism, but rather the assumption that the Post-Modernists had a monopoly on the truth - and, having become familiar with the teachings of the Post-Modernists, that it was acceptable to inform others that the art that they made was bad, and the reasons for which they made their art were bad as well.  The people who chose to add comments to this post are dear to me, in varying degrees, because of the things that they have taught me.  Each of them has something good and different to offer.  I\\\'ve come across as an ass more than once, I know.  It\\\'s a difficult thing, sometimes, to be challenging (which the FAQ says is good) without being seen as a prick (which the FAQ says is bad).  Provoking people to re-examine their opinions is challenging.  Simply telling them that the reasons that they do things are wrong, and the things that they make are bad, seems to be more prick-like, to me.  I love philosophy; please, teach me what you know.</p> <i>*constructive*</i>',3,1244,0,0,'65.229.226.177','en'),(14834,2487,'2002-03-07 10:09:21',20,'jim','you went to one hell of a school if they were reading you Clan of the Cave Bear in fifth grade, Allison.',6,104,124,1,'66.92.4.146','en'),(14835,2493,'2002-03-07 14:29:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>oh well; i guess, deep down, i was hoping it was as in the esteemed lyric: <i>\"like dre said, its on the highest of mountains, the lowest of valleys, the dopest of <b>bally</b>\'s from cali to cali all around the world...\"</i></tt>',1,247,0,0,'128.6.32.96','en'),(14836,2496,'2002-03-07 18:56:30',76,'chi chi','i agree with austin, find a place and stick to it. i always tell people i\\\'m from portland, or. even though i spent most of my youth in various parts of saudi arabia. i lived in portland full time only the last three years of high school, and then i moved to california, but it\\\'s still \\\"home\\\"',0,303,0,0,'63.230.172.185','en'),(14837,2493,'2002-03-07 19:04:49',183,'lin','<p> I wrote an entire post, and tried to submit it, and then I got an error message say that there was no email address on file for me, and my whol post got deleted. So in any case. I was going to say art is all about reading signs, but I\\\'m not in the mood for re-posting. ',0,276,0,0,'64.228.73.63','en'),(14838,2505,'2002-03-07 19:17:07',17,'austin','merry christmas lin :)',1,22,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14839,2493,'2002-03-07 19:38:08',122,'hool','<p>do yourself a favor - every time you\\\'re about to post an entry, <i>before</i> you do it, hit ctrl-A, then ctrl-C, so you have a clipboard copy of your entry before you send it off into HTTP post land.  i hate losing entries.</p>',0,234,0,0,'134.117.193.18','en'),(14840,564,'2002-03-07 19:49:42',187,'kandrer','sorry, Auntie, but I am sure woman recruiters can be quite the same dumb. It must be something about the job title: \\\"recruiter\\\". And not to say about \\\"Human Resources\\\" jobs. This morning in the Caltrain I spent 5 minutes reading over the shoulder of a (big and famous company) HR -girl-. She was typing on her laptop the reasons why some employees should have a promotion and some others should not. I got scared. (I know, that\\\'s not a proof that what she wrote was wrong.)',2,488,0,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(14841,564,'2002-03-07 20:22:15',17,'austin','<P>\r\nmatsukaze> ew HR people outside my office\r\n</P><P>\r\nrobotec9> hide\r\n</P><P>matsukaze> the speak in painfully friendly, syruppy, over-nice, sing-songy\r\n            tones to one-another\r\n</P><P>robotec9> you know i wondered if we would get allong if we worked together,\r\n           and i am not convinced of that yet, but i know this much, we would\r\n           both be repulsed by some of the same people\r\n</P><P>matsukaze> you can work with me\r\n</P><P>matsukaze> i despise syruppy dripping oozing false kindness\r\n</P><P>matsukaze> \'\'oh, no, you take my chair, I\'LL STAND, its no problem, really *smile*\'\'\r\n</P>',3,638,187,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14842,2496,'2002-03-07 21:12:53',129,'moebius','after years of worrying over the question \\\"where\\\'s your hometown,\\\" i\\\'m happy to finally say: san francisco. all those other places i lived were just weird layovers on my journey to this city, this life.',3,214,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14843,2493,'2002-03-07 21:38:52',202,'lifeonmars','<p>first of all, i don\\\'t really speak english, i\\\'m just translating from this farsi to english dictionary.<br><br>Paul G,\r\nI should clarify; you seem to think that I was insulting everyone; the only person I may have was scapegoat; but I know him personally and that is the nature of our \\\"friendship\\\"; i buy him drinks and we have shouting matches that usually leaves one of us crying (i won\\\'t say who).  I\\\'m <i>sorry</i>, but the expression \\\"personal growth\\\" makes me wanna retch. I\\\'m surprised that in this talk-show, self-help saturated society no one else felt the same way. Other than scapegoat, the <i>only</i> person I made fun of or insulted here was myself!<br><br>also, thank you lotus for your comments.  It was nice to see someone respond to my comments (as messy as they were) seriously; and I liked what you had to say<br><br>\r\nPeter, what is so clever about making fun of someone for having an opinion on art? Would you prefer if I said that its all great, except for the stuff done by those assholes who are looking for praise?  This is getting way too personal.  Rather than defending my ideas; here I am defending myself...    <br><br>       \r\nBack to Paul G; I\\\'ve been trying to talk about what I think, without having to resort to lecturing on and on. A lot of my reasons seem fairly obvious to me, so I thought writing them down would appear condescending. I have not formalized my opinions on this subject, but I\\\'m just reacting to a basic gut feeling that there is something insincere on insisting on the autonomy of art. I think that its a social process and is inextricable from our economic structure.  Again, many contemporary writers have argued something similar (I can give you the names of some essays if you\\\'re interested). I also negate the superiority of art as well.  We tend to think of it as a powerful, revolutionary force but I think its greatest power is that in establishing social hierarchy.  That the role of art in contemporary society (can anyone think of a good synonym? I\\\'m sick of this word) has more to do with creating a new hierarchy as the old ones crumble. We question and mock everything; particularly the political leaders who should be at the top of the social pecking order.  Sure, there\\\'s business people and professors and what not, but what we really admire and respect is genius.  To be one, or just to associate with them; is a good way to climb to the top of the pile.  So, I think that when one decides to be an artist, or consider themselves artistic; it has just as much to do with a sense of self-esteem and identity as it does with a pure joy of art.  Because think about it; every child draws (if they have crayons) for mostly intrinsic reasons.  We all start out with the ability for artistic expression.  Why do some stick with it and others give it up?  I believe its a subconscious or conscious choice that has little to do with talent but more to do with how one perceives themselves.  I had a bit of talent when I was a child and yet I chose to give up art as a teenager because I could not fit myself into the modern concept of an \\\"artist\\\".  I felt that I did not have talent, originality, genius, or any self-expression.  I also believed  in those grand ideas of what art should be; revolutionary, beautiful, unique, active rather than passive; and able to communicate with thousands of people rather than a couple of art snobs. In the same way that I don\\\'t drive; I thought I\\\'d do the world a favour and relieve it of another bad artist<br><br> \r\n\r\nAnd yet; although I have hardly any talent; art keeps calling me back, and I ended up accidentally studying art history in school, and next thing you know I was picking up brushes after 5 or 6 years of ignoring them and producing the most wonderfully mediocre crap but enjoying myself immensely.  And what got a brush in my hand?  I wanted a job; I signed up for a design program; I wanted some attention; I decided to have an art show that pretty much forced me to create some serious stuff.  See?  All the wrong reasons.  And yes, the stuff I do is truly mediocre.  But I realized that while I know, deep in the heart of me; that I am NOT talented I can also recognize a lot of other no-talents.  And they work, and call themselves artists, and get much self-esteem and gratification from it, some of them even make a lot of money off it. And they made me realize that I too can paint and call myself an artist.  The difference is, that instead of feeling like a sinner every time I pick up a brush to desecrate the altar of art, instead I took art off its pedestal and that allowed me to paint without hating myself for producing crap.<br><br>\r\nI think I am really suspicious of a culture that claims that if someone is really true to themselves and does not work for extrinsic reasons; their work will be really good and make a million dollars and win them fame.<br><br> god, I could moan on about this subject forever.</p>',0,5008,0,0,'198.96.85.230','en'),(14844,2493,'2002-03-07 22:10:09',66,'Peter to lifeonmars','<br><tt>um, sorry lifeonmars, or whatever... i wasnt making fun so much as i was finding a little humor in what appeared to be a slight rant, from someone id never seen post on here before; perhaps i thoguth you were yet another alias from whomever\\\'s bickered about art on here 50 times before. as for defending yourself, i havent paid close enough attention to the argument to see whats being defended/knocked/critiqued in the first place, so i maybe i best just shut up :)</tt>\r\n',1,484,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14845,2505,'2002-03-07 22:46:36',83,'adamajah','<p>A doodle on a napkin, a private little sketch done late at night, a drawing of a tree at your campsite - this is the purest form of art.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nA Frank Stella, a Lari Pittman, an Ed Ruscha, etc., at LACMA - this is the most tainted, dishonest, diluted form of art.</p>\r\n\r\n<P>At least, sometimes I feel like this.\r\n</P><p>\r\nAnd I have to admit, getting a degree as a painter helped me to come to this conclusion.</p>',3,421,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14846,2496,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i\\\'m sticking to auckland, new zealand then.  even thought i love all the places and countries where i grew up.',3,113,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14847,2493,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','<p>\r\ni always find it really hard to read things on the big question of \"Why People Make Art\" or \"What Art Means to Me and Why It Should Mean That To You Too.\" For one thing, why I do something might not have anything to do with How i do it. i could be perfectly serious about making ketsup and pickle collages, for example. any hypostheses that someone can come up with will probably be partly true, in different percentages, for anyone depending on the time of day, phase of the moon, etc. but to sound so absolutely certain... that\'s the way to foolsville.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso what i want to say is, i enjoy the wordplay here. i enjoy reading about why other people do what they do, be it programming, or picking their nose, or painting things various shades of white. but be aware, laddies and lassies, that you don\'t know dick about how other people think and their intentions. people are nebulous and unfathomable and beautiful. and that just about sums up my rant.</p>',1,973,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14848,2493,'2002-03-08 01:09:22',83,'adamajah','<P>Yeah, I agree.  Intent is a very important consideration when discussing the meaning of a piece of art, and yet it is at the same time, meaningless.  It is usually very important to the artist, and usually unimportant to the viewer in his/her process of gaining an experience from the work.  Then again, I have often gained a <i>hightened</i> experience from a piece of art when i have been aware of the artist\\\'s intent.  The same goes for the context of the piece in time and location... er, history.</p>',3,511,57,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14849,2504,'2002-03-08 02:01:46',176,'Scapegoat','more please',0,11,0,0,'64.229.163.38','en'),(14850,2504,'2002-03-08 02:32:26',130,'treetop','matt, i really like this.  florida...  i passed through tampa once on my way to a phish show in the everglades.  we ate some mushrooms and walked around the dali museum.  i wasent really that impressed with the museum, it was too small.  maybe it dosent take mushrooms to enjoy dali.  we also stopped in a little place called venice beach where you could find lots of little sharks teeth mixed in with the sand.  we tried sleeping on the beach and cops came, tried to find our very well hidden drugs to no avail, and then wrote us a warning for sleeping on the beach at 4am.  florida  :)',0,587,0,0,'172.145.37.230','en'),(14851,2493,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','to adamajah: actually, that\'s not what i meant. what you\'re describing (trying to figure out what the artist is trying to convey) as intent, is in my opinion, quite okay. what is less savory, in my opinion is placing a value judgement on the morality of all artists, and assuming that artists create out of a need for applause and ego gratification. that\'s just mean.\r\n\r\nthose who think famous artists are necessarily publicity whores are putting the cart before the horse. just because they deserve recognition doesn\'t mean they\'ve given up their souls for it.',1,569,83,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14852,2504,'2002-03-08 02:47:32',66,'Peter','<br><tt>back in tennessee, we called florida \\\"the land of deadbeat daddies and alimony dodgers\\\". heh. i always wondered what florida called tennessee...</tt>',1,163,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14853,2493,'2002-03-08 02:48:02',59,'s5','i find it upsetting when people think they understand everyone else\\\'s motivations just because they\\\'ve become really good at projecting their own pet issues onto the world around them.',5,190,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14854,2493,'2002-03-08 02:50:16',66,'Peter to s5','<br><tt>nicely put. concise without being terse. oh someday that i could be so efficient with words.</tt>',1,105,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14855,2493,'2002-03-08 04:11:54',17,'austin','<P>discussion of art</P>\r\n<P>not for the faint of he<B>art</B>!</P>\r\n<P>oh but wait a minute, is this the same s5 who onced exclaimed that he would yell \\\"THIS IS NOT ART\\\" at a large monochrome canvas in a gallery if given the opportunity?</P>',3,248,59,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14856,2496,'2002-03-08 05:39:57',175,'Paul G','(jafa)...(South Island took control of the keyboard again.  All Blacks!)',0,72,0,0,'65.230.18.13','en'),(14857,2508,'2002-03-08 07:33:14',124,'gus','i liked this one. my mom was the yeller, the grab you by the upper arm way too tight at mervyns on the way back from church becuase you\\\'re acting, like what? like a KID... because you\\\'re a KID. standing around with one kid on each arm, both of us in tears, as she runs into the neighbors and smiles and makes nice and squeezes the shit out of our arms.\r\n<p>\r\nshe drove off and left us once at the pre-school becuase she was mad that we didnt hurry. Im sure she doesnt remember this, but I do. I wont ever forget it. it made her threats that she\\\'d never return as she drove off every couple nights when I was in jr high less worrisome. nothing was quite as bad as being abandoned at the pre-school.',0,706,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14858,2508,'2002-03-08 08:07:44',176,'Scapegoat','what is it to be \\\'bad\\\'?<br><br>\r\nI read this and immediately started thinking about my own father. I was bad so often when I was little that I quickly realized that my values were not the same as those of my father/family. How does one tell if one is really being \\\'bad\\\' or simply challenging opinions and values that are not shared with a parent? This sort of conflict still happens with my family today. More from my mother though.<br><br>\r\nAnyway, this was provocative for me. ',0,491,0,0,'64.229.163.38','en'),(14859,2508,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i loved this post!  i seemed to be exceptionally bad child.  my mum would tell me off for asking embarrasing questions.  i remember having moments of panic, turning around in supermarkets or malls and finding her gone.  i went to a shrink when i was two, i think.  i ask:  which is more likely: that you or your two year old is crazy?  my mum is a screecher.',3,358,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14860,2508,'2002-03-08 13:58:25',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you have not experienced true parental pain until youve experienced life with a <i>southern</i> mom, heh. [especially if she thinks it evil that you moved to nyc and became a \'yankee\']</tt>.',1,204,0,0,'128.6.32.96','en'),(14861,2508,'2002-03-08 14:18:00',81,'felicity','I always thought that having a German mother was a particularly tortuous form of existence.  She didn\\\'t yell much, but she was exceptionally good at placing blame for anything and everything on anyone but herself.  I had long left the roost before I realized that it wasn\\\'t actually my fault that my sister performed poorly in school.  My mother had always made me feel guilty that I had been born first and therefore had a monopoly on her time and affection - one I was supposedly unwilling to relinquish when my sibling arrived a couple of years later.  I know there\\\'s no logic to her argument, but that never made me feel any better about being a \\\"superachiever\\\" while my sister was merely \\\"average.\\\"  (Mom\\\'s words, not mine.)',2,753,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(14862,2508,'2002-03-08 14:23:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i had the germanic dad. wowee. it was like, \\\"hello, do you have emotions?\\\"</tt>',1,93,0,0,'128.6.32.96','en'),(14863,2504,'2002-03-08 14:54:26',195,'matt','i know a few jokes about georgia and alabama, but none concerning tennessee.',1,76,0,0,'64.132.148.219','en'),(14864,2493,'2002-03-08 16:10:12',202,'lifeonmars','<p>i\\\'d promised myself I\\\'d shut up... but I feel I have to respond to weeb\\\'s comment.  I agree with some of what you\\\'ve said, about the variety of human experience and how you can\\\'t really generalize; but I think you go to far in saying that I\\\'m placing a morality judgment on all artists.  I think that there are hundreds of reasons why people do creative work and I do not think any of them immoral.  I\\\'m just trying to question; why do we think it is better to do art for self-gratification than for the enjoyment of others (i.e, praise)?  Couldn\\\'t it be successfully argued that it is more immoral to do art for your own personal enjoyment rather than for others? - That is iff we\\\'re going to think in christian terms and place morality judgments.  I do not think that creating art is moral or immoral.  Unless of course; it is for actual religious purposes.  Of course there are questions of the morality of any sort of work; anyone who\\\'s had to work, (and cares about these sort of things) has had to ask themselves whether their work is ethical.<br><br>As for coming off as a twitty know-it-all on the subject; I find that\\\'s the risk anyone takes when they decide to disagree or, take a stand on anything.  Of course you shouldn\\\'t take my word for it; I\\\'m not an authority on anything.  I\\\'m just one person out there who\\\'s got some questions and some opinions. also, when I\\\'m writing i\\\'m usually in a humourous or tongue-in-cheek sort of mood and I guess that just doesn\\\'t come across in writing very well; like when I first made those comments about post-modernist dogma and having done my readings, I thought I was being <i>really funny</i>, while everyone else seemed to think I was showing off or something.</p>',1,1776,0,0,'216.123.164.138','en'),(14865,2508,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','to Peter: bwahahaha. that is very funny.',1,40,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14866,2508,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','also, i want to become a good parent. how to you keep from becoming a screaming meanie? kids will be kids, and become annoying sometimes. dragging their feet for the umptheenth time, destroying things, etc. but unlike a puppy, you\\\'re not allowed to discipline them or pen them up. what then?',1,294,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14867,2508,'2002-03-08 18:00:26',17,'test','test?',6,5,0,0,'205.179.19.142','en'),(14868,2493,'2002-03-08 18:34:59',83,'adamajah','<p>Oh!  Well that goes without saying...</p>',2,44,57,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14869,2493,'2002-03-08 18:43:28',148,'jeeff','i heartily second chickenman\\\'s comment.',0,42,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14870,2493,'2002-03-08 19:21:26',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i thought i was really funny too, until someone told me otherwise. heh.</tt>',1,84,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14871,2508,'2002-03-08 19:24:09',66,'Peter to weeb','<br><tt>and you thought i was just being witty when i said <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2383\\\">\\\"I am German. I have no sense of humour\\\"</a>! :)</tt>\r\n\r\n',1,188,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14872,2500,'2002-03-08 20:48:34',187,'kandrer','hem... you know maybe I am not trying enough to be clever, or maybe I\\\'m actually stupid, but your comment is ambiguous to me. OK, let\\\'s say i don\\\'t understand it at all. i\\\'m really a non-native speaker, you see. i *need* to make people repeat. I used to ask my only American friend Cliff to decrypt for me, but now he\\\'s away; i am back to be only a dumb frenchie. thanks for coming anyway... :)',3,409,0,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(14873,2500,'2002-03-08 21:10:02',66,'voice of reason','as in, his dad pulled out too slow when he was fucking his mom, so she had HIM, see?',0,84,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14874,2508,'2002-03-08 21:25:17',65,'le','heh \\\"felicity,\\\" i recall your mom being a meanie head. ',1,61,81,0,'209.162.48.22','en'),(14875,160,'2002-03-08 23:32:32',187,'frenchie','bold. i am so glad i can understand that one, soooo glad. thanks, thanks a lot.',2,79,0,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(14876,2508,'2002-03-09 00:01:36',122,'hool','<p>i was walking back to my place just now, after a trip to the variety store. i approached a parked minivan, the door opened, and a mom\\\'s yelling voice poured out of it.  she slammed the door after getting out, i saw a young girl in the back seat, and then the mom  passed in front of me to the video store, leaving me in a wake of expletives.  \\\"fucking son of a.. \\\" and other such love. i thought about yelling \\\'bitch\\\' just to see what the reaction would be.  i fantasized that she would reply \\\"fuck you, asshole\\\" and then i would\\\'ve said \\\"fair enough\\\" and continued on my way.</p>',0,613,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14877,2509,'2002-03-09 04:01:17',65,'le','<pre>\r\n:(\r\n\r\n:(\r\n\r\n:(\r\n\r\n</pre>',5,31,0,0,'209.162.49.129','en'),(14878,2509,'2002-03-09 04:05:29',59,'s5','disturbing.\r\n',4,13,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14879,2510,'2002-03-09 04:55:00',17,'oracle from the matrix','what\\\'s really going to cook your noodle is would you have read it if i hadn\\\'t said anything?',4,98,0,0,'64.174.155.162','en'),(14880,2510,'2002-03-09 05:15:24',66,'Peter to le','<br><tt>about this lack of interest in postmodernism... could you please discuss this <i><b>at length?</i></b></tt>',1,101,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14881,2509,'2002-03-09 05:21:14',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>[slightly more than utterly confused here]</i></tt>',1,57,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14882,2509,'2002-03-09 05:31:59',177,'pony','<i>This one a long time have I watched. All his life has he looked away... to the future, to the horizon. Never his mind on where he was. Hmm?</i>',4,146,0,0,'209.239.199.218','en'),(14883,2510,'2002-03-09 06:12:35',123,'dcc','the mighty toronto beast has thus risen.',0,40,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(14884,2509,'2002-03-09 06:37:14',185,'orion','Paul, you remind me of fiery diarrhoea.<br><br><br><i><b>BURN!!! BURN!!!</b></i>',6,80,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14885,2510,'2002-03-09 06:43:56',185,'orion','I have to love post modernism, even indirectly, for it gave rise (however inadvertently) to my favorite language.',1,113,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14886,2510,'2002-03-09 06:56:45',148,'jeeff','there are more canuckians than you know.  i know of at least 9 that post here.  we are <i>everywhere</i>!',0,105,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14887,2510,'2002-03-09 07:39:14',185,'orion','a, ahem, <em>very hot</em> canadian woman was one of the nurses that looked after me in intensive after i got a kidney transplant...',1,132,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(14888,2509,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i laughed at this. i think you write a lot better when you cease and desist the didactic battering ram style. congrats. also, i like peter. he rad.',1,147,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14889,2510,'2002-03-09 09:31:39',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'m curious as to what other locations have official location status already?',0,80,0,0,'64.229.167.158','en'),(14890,2510,'2002-03-09 09:47:20',59,'s5','we have new york. isn\\\'t that close enough? bloody canadians are always trying to get included.\r\n<h1>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">U</font><font color=\\\"#FFFFFF\\\">S</font><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">A</font>!\r\n</h1>\r\n<h1>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">U</font><font color=\\\"#FFFFFF\\\">S</font><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">A</font>!\r\n</h1>\r\n<h1>\r\n<font color=\\\"#FF0000\\\">U</font><font color=\\\"#FFFFFF\\\">S</font><font color=\\\"#0000FF\\\">A</font>!\r\n</h1>\r\n',4,471,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14891,2510,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','hey, scroll down.',3,17,176,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14892,2509,'2002-03-09 10:09:54',124,'gus','i dont get it.',0,14,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14893,2510,'2002-03-09 17:25:22',122,'hool','<p>i second the motion for adding TO to the city list.  right now i\\\'m still in ottawa, but i\\\'m moving to toronto in a month and a half.  scapegoat, dcc, jeeff, chiamattt, fuzzy, lifeonmars, to name a few are all from trahno.</p>',0,234,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14894,2510,'2002-03-09 17:46:14',66,'cletus the slack-jawed yokel','i post from down here in alabammy. i think you should add alabammy to the list too. irwin, my brother/father agrees. he done got me on this internet by pedallin and pedallin to generate enough electricity to power my apple two e. he says if you got them damn yankees and weird texans an flaky california boys, and now yer gettin them damn furiners from up there in that canada place, you oughts to add me too. down here in alabammy.',6,432,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14895,2509,'2002-03-09 17:51:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>this comment from paulg\\\'s lj makes an attempt to sort things out:</b><br><br>\r\nIf someone wrote only for the Swinney audience, they would make use of certain references:</tt><br><br> \r\n\r\n1) beakfast food <br>\r\n2) SFBA <br>\r\n3) motorcycles <br>\r\n4) Drug use <br>\r\n5) found objects <br>\r\n6) etc. <br><br>\r\n\r\nOne could in fact make a stereotypical Swinney post quite easily. That\\\'s what Mr. Post Modernism said: \\\"Write for the audience, not yourself.\\\" <br><br>\r\n\r\nSo: \\\"I was already carrying a mango (s5), and a map of Texas (several people), and a rubber band (gus), and my favorite camera (peter), and a part for my motorcycle (austin et al).\\\"<br><Br> \r\n\r\nIt was all nonsense...silliness. I tried to submit it anyway...',1,747,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14896,2513,'2002-03-09 18:10:50',66,'Peter','<br><tt>mmm, magnolias migas</tt>',1,33,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14897,2513,'2002-03-09 18:11:54',66,'Burt Bacharach','\\\"do you know the way... to <i>san jose</i>?\\\"',1,50,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14898,2512,'2002-03-09 18:15:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>my mom used to tell me that good things came in odd numbers, and that three was a particularly lucky number. we had a screen painting of three birds on a limb, and she explained that there was a reason why there were three... not two, not four. thanks to her, i now have a similarly unexplicable tendency to prefer things in threes.<br><br>also: three micron slices of avocado! my job, for some odd reason nowadays, requires me to spend like 50% of my time cutting these tiny plastic blocks into 3 micron slices. this is not an easy job, i can assure you; your dinnertime must be wrought with scientific precision!</tt>',1,628,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14899,2511,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o',':) :) :)<br>\r\n<br>\r\nthanks for having babies.<br>',2,49,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14900,2510,'2002-03-09 20:03:03',148,'jeeff','you are brilliant!  i officially change the name of toronto to \\\'trahno\\\' for eternity.',0,91,122,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14901,2510,'2002-03-09 22:39:31',176,'Scapegoat','Thank you, whenever answers are right in front of me tends to be the moments at which I have the hardest time finding them.<br><br>\r\n<b>to s5</b> New York? Have you been to Canada? Your welcome to come up and stay if you want to see for yourself but I assure you that there is a discernible difference between here and New York.<br><br>\r\n<b>to cletus the slack-jawed yokel</b> what is a furiner?     ',0,400,62,0,'64.229.167.107','en'),(14902,2510,'2002-03-09 22:46:14',66,'Peter tp Scapegoat','<br><tt>well, sorry to offer the euphemism, but lets just say ive been to both, several times, and i choose to live in nyc :). just my personal two cents, not meaning to offend, i just had to step up for my hood... have you ever been to nyc? youre welcome to visit any time.</tt>\r\n',1,281,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14903,2510,'2002-03-09 22:48:13',66,'furiner','furiner = \\\"foreigner\\\"... as in the band that sang \\\"juke box hero\\\" and \\\"midnight blue\\\" and \\\"feels like the first time\\\". <i>nb: they are from new york.</i>',1,177,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14904,2511,'2002-03-09 22:59:26',176,'Scapegoat','This made me roar. ',0,19,0,0,'64.229.167.107','en'),(14905,2510,'2002-03-10 00:38:24',59,'s5','i\\\'ve been to canada. quebec, toronto and around british columbia. two of my best friends live in vancouver. and if it weren\\\'t so rainy and yuck i\\\'d consider living there. but otherwise i love canada and canadians, and i like to give them all a good natured ribbing from time to time.\r\n<br><br>\r\n:D',2,306,176,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14906,2513,'2002-03-10 00:41:25',83,'adamajah','<p>say \\\"hi\\\" to the bird-roaches for me</p>',1,48,0,0,'68.65.228.54','en'),(14907,2510,'2002-03-10 02:03:15',176,'Scapegoat','<b>to Peter</b> Steppin up for your hood, I can completely understand. To answer your question about New York I\\\'ve only been once when I was still rather young but its on my list of American cities to visit along with New Orleans, Eugene, Seattle, and San Fransico. The majority of my U.S. experience has been centered around Las Vegas due to family connections there.<br><br>\r\n<b>to s5</b> two of my best friends are in Van aswell and I hear you on the rain issue. I couldn\\\'t live there though as I find myself never wanting to be too far from the province of Quebec. Montreal has really endeared itself to me.<br><br>\r\n\r\n ',0,630,0,0,'64.229.167.107','en'),(14908,2513,'2002-03-10 02:07:31',59,'s5','i\\\'d like to see the bats in austin. they do the same thing in melbourne too, in the big park along st. kilda road. at dusk they get really antsy and decide that they need to go somewhere else.',1,195,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14909,2510,'2002-03-10 02:34:07',124,'gus','I am post modern. I can not stand post modernism. This is a problem.',0,68,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14910,2500,'2002-03-10 04:08:27',130,'treetop','to kandrer:<p>my comment was just meant to add some humor :)  it seems that you have good english within your writing.  sorry to confuse you.</P>',1,145,0,0,'172.146.64.48','en'),(14911,2512,'2002-03-10 04:13:58',130,'treetop','<p>give me cucumber<br>a slice of avacado<br>and then fluff the rice</p>',3,72,0,0,'172.146.64.48','en'),(14912,2504,'2002-03-10 04:15:19',130,'treetop','owwwww, give us alabama jokes please! :)',1,40,0,0,'172.146.64.48','en'),(14913,2510,'2002-03-10 04:30:54',151,'stella','<p>Post-modernism is a bunch of non-sensical hooey. I had to take a class in post-modern history (oxy-moron?) to fulfill my degree in fine arts. The only thing of significance that I remember is when I asked my professor if she could define \\\'discourse\\\' from a post-modern standpoint (after she\\\'d used the term at length in several lectures) and she replied \\\"no, I can\\\'t\\\". Whether she didn\\\'t know or was just being difficult, I still don\\\'t know. </P><P>\r\nWhat a waste of time. </P>',0,504,0,0,'168.100.203.247','en'),(14914,2514,'2002-03-10 04:58:08',17,'austin','beautiful',2,9,0,0,'66.90.145.8','en'),(14915,2510,'2002-03-10 05:00:08',130,'treetop','to gus:<p>maybe you are \r\n<a href=\\\"http://www.jonmattox.com/grids/ideas/postmodernism.html\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">post-post modern</a>?</p>',0,145,0,0,'172.146.64.48','en'),(14916,2514,'2002-03-10 05:44:50',65,'le','yes, it is beautiful. ',1,22,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14917,2510,'2002-03-10 06:40:21',65,'le','POST IRONIC',4,11,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(14918,2509,'2002-03-10 10:15:52',124,'gus','i always hate being a part of someone else\\\'s post modernism. I am not here for you. Im here for me.',0,102,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14919,2511,'2002-03-10 10:18:47',124,'gus','hee hee. ',0,9,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14920,2514,'2002-03-10 10:24:55',124,'gus','sometimes it occurrs to me that my true love, john henry, will die before I do. and my eyes well up. \\\"ach! forget about it! he\\\'s just a cat!\\\" i say... but he isnt!',0,172,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14921,2509,'2002-03-10 12:58:12',130,'sarcasm','how modern',0,10,0,0,'172.172.98.160','en'),(14922,2513,'2002-03-10 16:15:13',186,'ook','<p>my favorite activity in jakarta is to sit on a balcony just before sunset and watch the fly-catching birds swoop around like oversized tropical swallows.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>at some point, as the sun gets lower, you can notice that one of the birds is not swooping but rather is fluttering awkwardly around. about 5 seconds later all the swooping swallows are gone and the sky is full instead with fluttery bats making leather flapping noises and little squeaky noises, but there are the same number of flying creatures at all times. they work in shifts.</p>',1,551,0,0,'202.158.31.58','en'),(14923,2515,'2002-03-10 16:41:37',186,'ook','so i\\\'m thinking that i could pretty easily steal that spool of cables - usually i just steal bricks. mmm, bricks. but what i\\\'d really like to steal is that random police man. my own police man, to have standing around looking uniformed! ya!\r\n\r\nyou probably didn\\\'t mean like that, but i have minimal infrastructure at my house on purpose (see above note on bricks) so it kinda went by.',2,393,0,0,'202.158.31.3','en'),(14924,2515,'2002-03-10 16:42:03',186,'ook','bravo though! i like seeing work go on in the neighbourhood. on my street there are these kick-ass very slow dumptrucks with enormous yellow vacuums out the back for sucking up leaves. potential damage in the range of eardrums, cancer less so.',1,243,0,0,'202.158.31.5','en'),(14925,2517,'2002-03-10 20:45:52',130,'treetop','was ist das?',0,12,0,0,'172.172.98.160','en'),(14926,2517,'2002-03-10 21:09:45',148,'jeeff','mmm, slashy.',0,12,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14927,2514,'2002-03-10 21:12:54',148,'jeeff','my cat is 16.  i feel like i should be getting her a driver\\\'s license, but instead she\\\'s becoming old and feeble.  this really touches a cord.',2,148,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14928,2510,'2002-03-10 21:18:23',148,'jeeff','i am post office.\r\n\r\n<p>*groan*</p>',0,35,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14929,2508,'2002-03-10 21:21:28',148,'jeeff','for the quintessential germanic father, you must watch julien donkey-boy.  werner herzog is amazing.  the clint eastwood scene is maybe the funniest thing i\\\'ve ever seen in film.',0,181,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14930,2510,'2002-03-10 21:26:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>you guys.</tt>',1,22,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14931,156,'2002-03-10 22:01:32',128,'Javorai','why, what brilliant news!',0,25,0,0,'129.130.231.114','en'),(14932,2509,'2002-03-10 22:13:05',175,'Paul G','This was, I think, the first piece of art that I made for Swinney.\r\n\r\n<p>At issue was the subject of convention.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Post Modernism sets itself against the conventions of Modernism.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>But, Post Modernism has its own conventions - there are things that ought and ought not to be done according to various schools of Post Modern thought that are themselves the product of the elite activity that they so frequently rail against.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Likewise, Swinney has conventions.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Some articles that are posted on Swinney receive many, glowing comments; others receive no comments or they receive nasty remarks.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I took more time to write this piece than anything else that I have submitted to Swinney.  There are several layers to it.</p>  \r\n\r\n<p>On the surface it (this piece) appears to be a bit of rambling non-sense.  And, to a degree, that is all that it is.  It should have provoked laughter.  At least Weeb laughed, at first.  But, something happened.  Weeb\\\'s opinion changed, and Peter\\\'s did too.  I like most of you, and I like Peter most of all.  The outrageous claims made in the piece ought to have deflated the notion that I was seriously mocking Peter, or Swinney as a whole.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>No, Weeb: I\\\'m not disconnected from reality, as you suggested to the writers-list subscribers.  I wrote an odd looking piece for Swinney, that\\\'s all; if I yelled such things in a public library that would be a bad sign.  Other people have submitted more <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=1058.txt\\\">confrontational</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=666.txt\\\">bizarre</a> pieces to Swinney in the past (I don\\\'t have a knowledge of the entire collection - those were pulled from what I have read).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Oh well, such is life.  I laughed about the fact that Godzilla might be a good metaphor for Post Modernism and its activity.  I laughed about the fact that Prince Charles really does speak out against Post Modernism and Modernism.  There are layers to this piece.  It is art.  It was just made by the wrong person.  No baiting, no lectures, no more.</p>    \r\n\r\n\r\n',0,2175,0,0,'65.230.18.151','en'),(14933,2517,'2002-03-10 22:24:02',59,'s5','this is art.',4,12,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14934,2517,'2002-03-10 22:56:39',66,'Peter','<br><tt>it slices, it dices, it even parses errors</tt>',1,55,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14935,2509,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','everything i said was bullshit.\r\n\r\nintent: irrelevant. reaction: everything.',4,76,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14936,2515,'2002-03-10 23:41:19',66,'Peter','<br><tt>also: please wear your <a href=\\\"http://zapatopi.net/afdb.html\\\"><i>aluminum foil deflector beanie</i></a>.</tt>',1,124,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14937,2515,'2002-03-10 23:43:22',66,'ok, so','youve got the highly pure glass  fibres... now what?',1,52,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14938,2513,'2002-03-11 02:59:31',74,'jo','austin swinney, here are some things:\r\n<ol><li>quack\\\'s at 43rd and duval is still there; i had breakfast and journal-writing there yesterday.</li>\r\n<li>i am in love with the neighborhood between guadalupe and speedway, 30th and 38th, just <a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/movetoaustin/\\\">south of my apartment</a>, particularly the walk from my place to spider house, along the creek. mmm.</li>\r\n<li>i\\\'m fond of the names in the cement, too. my favorite is \\\"terry edwards,\\\" because he usually puts a date after his name. tonight, while walking, i saw \\\"oct 1957\\\" and \\\"oct 1972.\\\"</li>\r\n<li>showdown? turbodog? <a href=\\\"mailto:joanna@unpopular.com\\\">interested</a>?</li></ol>',1,704,0,0,'172.135.150.181','en'),(14939,2514,'2002-03-11 04:17:26',122,'hool','<p>this was very good, very fitting to remember a cat.</p>',0,58,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14940,2514,'2002-03-11 04:22:14',59,'s5','agree on both counts. cats are family.',2,38,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14941,2513,'2002-03-11 05:23:13',200,'chiclet','i spent one summer on a ranch outside of austin when i was about 9.\r\nbreakfast tacos are one of the things i remember most.  along with\r\nscorpions, seeing boris becker win wimbledon, and basking in the sun\r\non the rocks, all lizard-like, at barton springs.',0,256,0,0,'208.59.199.233','en'),(14942,2500,'2002-03-11 06:44:31',187,'kandrer','no, *i* am sorry. now i understand. i didn\\\'t even know if your comment was a critics or not... :} too many up, down, out, in, etc in English. it\\\'s puzzling for frenchies. for example, why do you guys say \\\"Tie me up\\\" or \\\"beat someone up\\\"? In both cases \\\"down\\\" would have sounded better to me, i think. ',1,325,0,0,'166.90.33.233','en'),(14943,2513,'2002-03-11 06:51:52',17,'austin','i went to showdown today and bought two turbo dogs for 1.60.  i had a hard time with this price.  i thought i heard the barkeep wrong.  later bmg went back for two more and informed me that that was the sunday happy minutes.  15 minutes of cheap prices on good beer.  hell yes.  im sure i\\\'ll be up for some more tomorrow.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nalso, ruta maya is no more.',3,363,74,0,'66.90.145.8','en'),(14944,2518,'2002-03-11 06:53:33',70,'Fuzzy','This is amazing.',1,16,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(14945,2514,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','the only okay use of cloning is for cats.',4,41,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14946,2513,'2002-03-11 08:22:58',65,'le','??? that is sad, what happened to ruta maya? everything else in that neighborhood is lame. except alamo.',4,104,0,0,'209.86.5.73','en'),(14947,2513,'2002-03-11 16:16:41',74,'jo',':) i have a showdown shirt that says something like, \\\"proudly celebrating 20 years of happy minutes.\\\" 3-3:15 every day, $.80 pints.<BR>\r\nruta maya switched management. the old management is moving it south of the river, defeating the entire purpose of ruta maya, that being that it\\\'s the only coffeehouse in that part of downtown. elly\\\'s right -- the only thing worthwhile left there is alamo (and i guess the gingerman). sad. where will little fledgling bands play?',0,478,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14948,2510,'2002-03-11 16:29:10',156,'lotus','hool sucks. i am no longer his friend. <br><br>\r\nyes! i am from canada. ottawa to be precise. and i always read what le posts.',2,126,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14949,2500,'2002-03-11 16:32:22',66,'Peter','<br><tt>we say \\\"to beat someone down\\\", also. at least those of us that live the ghetto-fabulous life do.</tt>',1,115,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14950,2510,'2002-03-11 16:38:46',66,'Peter','<br><tt>not to beat a dead horse, but im amused when people say things like \\\"<i>Post-modernism is a bunch of non-sensical hooey</i>\\\"; like most anything else that can be utterly generalized, it kind of all depends on your point of view and depth of understanding.</tt>',1,274,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14951,2510,'2002-03-11 16:40:00',66,'yeah','whats <i>hooey</i> about <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=postmodernism\\\">this</a>?',1,99,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14952,2510,'2002-03-11 16:43:20',66,'Peter','<br><tt><b>also:</b> I\\\'d like to give shout outs to <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=futurism\\\">futurism</a> and <a href=\\\"http://www.dictionary.com/search?q=modernism\\\">modernism</a>.</tt>',1,212,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(14953,2190,'2002-03-11 16:44:11',156,'lotus','hello. i am a horse. my friend yi says that this would be a bad year for me to make any major changes. like getting married. hmm.',0,129,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14954,2513,'2002-03-11 18:09:22',83,'adamajah','<p>holy crap.  what happened to ruta maya?  not that i would have ever gone back there, but that place is for some reason a memorable one from my time in austin. i guess there was a period when i would always ride down there on weekends and sit with my sketch book on the loading dock.</p>',3,289,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(14955,2510,'2002-03-11 18:52:50',151,'stella','<P>\r\nI realized after I posted that that what I had said dissed an entire body of work of post-modernists thought, when in fact I meant that I though, specifically, that post-modern art is a bunch of non-sensical hooey. Also, and this is why I shouldnt post when I\\\'m cranky, not all of the artwork that would fall in this category is so bad. The whole theory of \\\'the grid\\\' is pretty interesting. However, what I do have the problem with are the \\\'artists\\\' who do things like shoot themselves, crucify themselves to the top of a VW Bug, build a loft in an art gallery and live on it for a week, and call it art. I just can\\\'t find anything artistic about shooting oneself, or locking myself in a high school locker for a week. </P>\r\n<p>Peter, I think that studying something for 5 months is a sufficient period to know whether or not the subject has any validity on a personal level. Finding it to be not worth anything to me personally is my point of view, and if it amuses you, that\\\'s okay by me. </P>',0,1021,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14956,2510,'2002-03-11 19:25:22',129,'moebius','<br>here\\\'s my postmodern moment for you: <br><br><b>Canadian Girls Kick Ass!!!</b>\r\n<br><br>that\\\'s all. thanks for your time. ',1,132,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14957,2510,'2002-03-11 19:42:15',66,'Peter to stella','<br><tt>i guess i dont just see <i>postmodernism</i> as merely a genre of art. so what might be true about a few faceless, over-ornamented pieces of sculpture or something doesnt necessarily jive with the rest of the scheme. at least to me; and most of us here have spent at least a few years studying art/philosophy/sociology, etc. to know better than to make generalizations that appear to be penned in words that seem to step on the toes of the fans of that style. pardon my gruffness, by teh way. i like you stella :) im just getting sick and am heading home early before i post mean things about life in general. hooray!</tt>',1,630,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14958,2517,'2002-03-11 19:58:39',90,'leonard','this makes me want to learn another language, like arabic.',5,58,0,0,'64.210.3.222','en'),(14959,2518,'2002-03-11 20:23:59',128,'Javorai','mesmerizing, how there\\\'s such beings still alive and walking around there...',0,79,0,0,'129.130.93.85','en'),(14960,2517,'2002-03-11 20:31:00',128,'Javorai','he\\\'s got one of those Sega keyboards that can go on \\\"turbo mode.\\\"',0,74,0,0,'129.130.93.85','en'),(14961,2510,'2002-03-11 20:57:41',151,'stella','<P>Be careful making assumptions, Peter. I\\\'ve been studying art since I was 13. That\\\'s 17 years. I have a degree in fine arts. My photography has been published and I\\\'ve won awards for my work. Just because I don\\\'t like a certain style of artistic expression doesn\\\'t mean that I don\\\'t know what I\\\'m talking about, nor does it mean that I\\\'m uneducated in the style that I dislike. I didn\\\'t post to step on anyones toes, I\\\'m not like that. I realize that my original post was overgeneralized and a bit rash, which is why I amended my statements in my second post. \r\nI apologize if I appeared to step on your toes, I certainly didn\\\'t mean to. I didn\\\'t say \\\"Post moderism is stupid and so is everyone who likes it.\\\" I said that in my opionion, it wasn\\\'t worth much. It\\\'s just my opinion. In the big picture, I know that it doesn\\\'t amount to much. I was just participating in a discussion. </P>',0,940,66,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14962,2510,'2002-03-11 21:01:34',151,'stella','btw, peter, I like you too. I think that your energy and vision and artwork (from what I\\\'ve seen) is quite unique. I don\\\'t know too many people on this board on more than a very shallow basis; I don\\\'t actually have personal discussions with anyone (not that I wouldn\\\'t like to), so I know that no one would know me well enough to know that I am not the kind of person who stirs things up for fun. But I\\\'m not.. trust me. :)',0,438,66,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(14963,2520,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','[x] your writing is very nice i am a fan. hello.',1,48,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14964,2510,'2002-03-11 22:10:05',207,'tim','is this thing on?',1,17,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14965,2510,'2002-03-11 22:33:29',207,'chris burden','shoot me!',1,9,151,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14966,2190,'2002-03-11 22:36:11',129,'moebius','you know i\\\'m so not interested in fitting into other people\\\'s astrologies, whether they be chinese or vedic or aztec or whichever. so i think i\\\'ll start one of my own.\r\n<br><br>\r\nfrom here on out, my sign is <i>infinity</i>, quasarpulse rising, harlequin retrograde. i was born in the year of <i>Sillyputty (tm).</i>\r\n<br><br>\r\npop quiz: based soley on this statement, what sign am i, <i>really?</i>',1,408,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(14967,1356,'2002-03-11 22:50:11',69,'starlajo','so, who categorised this as lesbian?\r\nsure, it\\\'s not much of a secret that i dig the girl-love... but this is hardly about that, it\\\'s about me and my boy. so there!',0,170,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(14968,1356,'2002-03-11 23:47:05',74,'jo','\\\"it\\\'s not much of a secret that i dig the girl-love\\\"<BR>\r\nhahahahaha.<BR>\r\nHAHAHAHAHA.<BR>\r\nthanks, jo, that was hysterical to me for some reason.',0,155,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(14969,2519,'2002-03-11 23:52:43',148,'jeeff','i don\\\'t mean to be rude, but i don\\\'t get the point of this.  what was so terrible about this situation?  what was so memorable about it?  i find many of your sentences a bit strangely constructed.  either something\\\'s been lost along the way, or i\\\'m crazy.  could be either.\r\n\r\n<p><i>[x] best friendly</i></p>',1,320,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14970,2518,'2002-03-12 00:00:55',88,'deb','Today, the next day, I realized that I spelled hypochondria incorrectly.  I suck. Sorry.',6,88,0,0,'65.58.163.252','en'),(14971,2522,'2002-03-12 00:14:19',88,'deb','I like this. It\\\'s beautiful.  I do not feel happy today, and somehow, this made me feel better AND able to relate at the same time.  I don\\\'t know if that makes sense or not, but that is the way it goes...for now.',3,218,0,0,'65.58.163.252','en'),(14972,2510,'2002-03-12 00:24:45',66,'Peter','<br><tt><a href=\\\"http://www.rhodamine.org/futurecity/attnyc.jpg\\\">this</a> is the first image that pops to mind when i think of <i>postmodern</i>; it is modern, it is the pedestal for classical ornamentation; it was iconoclastic. i have a hard time considering this (or anything about it) to be <i>hooey</i>. yes, this is a generalization of sorts, too! but, just thought id let you know what i was thinking.<br><br>also: i have an immense fever now. forgive my rantiness!</tt>',1,482,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14973,2519,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i think kandrer\\\'s native tongue is french, which explains the strange sentance structure.',3,92,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14974,2519,'2002-03-12 00:45:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>if i spoke french as well as kandrer spoke english, id have it made!</tt>',1,81,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(14975,1356,'2002-03-12 00:55:09',69,'starlajo','pleased to... umm... make you laugh.\r\n<BR>\r\nhah.\r\ndeary me.',2,59,74,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(14976,2519,'2002-03-12 05:54:02',148,'jeeff','alright that makes sense.  i agree with peter, i have a hard enough time with one language.  still, i wouldn\\\'t mind seeing more of an explanation of the story.',0,162,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14977,2519,'2002-03-12 06:39:42',59,'s5','i think the point is, canadians can be native french speakers too. hence the moment of embarassment.',3,100,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14978,2519,'2002-03-12 07:22:18',176,'Scapegoat','<b>Kandrer</b> was it the accent of the women or your own talking that became jumbled? I\\\'ve heard that the french from Quebec is a bit of a distinct dialect in comparison to the French in France.<br><br>\r\nunecessary inflammitory political statement <i>Vive le Quebec libre!</i>',0,280,0,0,'64.229.167.20','en'),(14979,2525,'2002-03-12 07:43:53',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'ve got a lot of good memories tied to staying up to watch the sunrise but the most beautiful sunrise was when I was really little. My mother was moving with my sister and I from northern Alberta just south of the North West Territories down to southern Ontario. Being that far north tends to do weird things to the whole sunrise/sunset cycle. My Mother had known that we wouldn\\\'t be seeing the same sorts of sunrises, in those days I was always up before sunrise for my hockey practices before school. About 45 mintues to an hour out of town the morning we left, my Mother stopped the car and grabbed a picnic breakfast she had prepared. The three of us sat and ate our breakfast and watched the sunrise with my Mother saying it might be the last one we would ever see like it. I remember this sunrise as the most beautiful and I don\\\'t think that I have seen any quite like it since to be honest. To see one more like this would be a good thing to add to my lifes to do list.    ',0,990,0,0,'64.229.167.20','en'),(14980,2525,'2002-03-12 09:18:28',64,'Joanna','It was a million years ago, and I was but a naive 17 year old girl. I\\\'d stayed up all night on IRC talking to a boy who\\\'d just told me that he fancied me.  We typed and we typed and we typed away to one another and watched the sunrise together, just from different cities and different islands.  It was pretty fucking cool. ',1,330,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(14981,2525,'2002-03-12 09:44:53',89,'sawks','<div style=\\\"font: 14px georgia\\\">\r\n\r\nmost memorable sunrise in recent times was near the end of last year, about 6am on the roof of this big warehouse building in melbourne\\\'s chinatown - i had turned up 5 hours earlier for a drum and bass session not really knowing what to expect, it turned out to be one of those nights where stuff just fell into place nicely<br><br>\r\n\r\nhad been out drinking and smoking with the local design posse, decided to rock up to this party and ran into a heap of friends, got in for for free - and it turns out to be on the roof of the building, rammed out, and with a nice rig<br><br>\r\n\r\ni hadn\\\'t been out for a boogie in months and it was well nice to stand there surrounded by melbournes neon cityscape, a wobbly bassline and a bunch of happy kids (including many of my countryfolk), watching the sky turn red...<br><br><br>\r\n\r\nalso the 4hrs of red moon rising last new years eve (caused by the sydney bushfires) that i witnessed from the top of nz was pretty special too...\r\n\r\n</div>',0,1027,0,0,'144.132.7.47','en'),(14982,2525,'2002-03-12 10:26:09',154,'ryan',' 9000\\\' bivouac on mt rainier. word.Â ',0,39,0,0,'216.26.60.213','en'),(14983,2524,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o',':(<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsometimes dads are dumb.',3,38,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(14984,2520,'2002-03-12 15:15:56',156,'lotus','you are very sweet.',1,19,62,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14985,2525,'2002-03-12 16:12:24',123,'dcc','i remember one morning waking up as the sun did. i was camping on the top of a ridge in central ontario, muskoka. it has a slightly hilly quality, but not really as rugged as some parts of canada.\r\n<p>\r\ni woke and exited the tent that i had make-shifted out of a tarp and rope (not terribly mosquito-proof, as i discovered). i came out to see the amber and red highlights on the lush green evergreens and maples in the short valley below. this was enhanced by the magical night-fog that was slowly receeding back into the placid lake nearby. the lake itself reflected like a golden mirror. \r\n<p>\r\nonly ten to twenty minute later the sun would be high enough to have pushed the silken matte of fog back into the now black waters. it made me wonder just how long the fog would sit in the valley since i hadn\\\'t seen it there when i had turned in for the night. \r\n<p>\r\nthis scene wasn\\\'t completely serene as a friendly porcupine had decided to climb a nearby tree. i had never seen a porcupine with my own eyes. all surly and spikey and shuffling up the rough bark. s/he stopped scratching it\\\'s way up the branches a couple times to be sure i wasn\\\'t some hungry predator coming to get needled. those spikes are really just long, bristly hairs you know.',1,1260,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14986,2526,'2002-03-12 16:32:34',81,'felicity','This made me cry.',1,17,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(14987,1544,'2002-03-12 16:51:38',123,'dcc','or vomiting into it ... repeatedly.',2,35,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14988,2525,'2002-03-12 18:41:58',124,'gus','hungover, new years day, the beginning of 1991 up on a big lookout point rock up in the castle rock park above saratoga in the santa cruz mts. over looking the valley. \r\n<p>\r\nbut the best was the sunset over the plain of jars last june in phonsovan, in the north of laos. I just sat and watched as the larger than life jars became shadows.',0,339,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14989,2526,'2002-03-12 18:44:47',124,'gus','it was nice.',0,12,0,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(14990,2526,'2002-03-12 19:21:17',155,'blue stocking','thank you very much to you both, for reading and letting me know what you thought.',1,82,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14991,1350,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','where can i get me some of this Reality Vaporizeur? ',1,52,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(14992,2526,'2002-03-12 19:29:19',207,'tim','yes, i very much liked it. it reminds me of someone.',1,52,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(14993,2523,'2002-03-12 19:56:51',122,'hool','<p>sometimes i stare for longer than appropriate amounts of time at a less than appropriate distance at people on the bus.  but only the ones who try to read what i\\\'m reading when they\\\'re sitting right beside me.  fuckers.</p>',0,232,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(14994,2523,'2002-03-12 20:04:03',123,'dcc','i\\\'ve recently concluded that i\\\'ve become a very \\\'good\\\' toronto-ite. i sit and mind my own business and don\\\'t (god forbid!) talk to anyone i don\\\'t know.\r\n\r\n<p>cold and unfriendly, just like everyone else.\r\n\r\n<p>it\\\'s sad really, but i guess that\\\'s how it works. too bad for strangers that i don\\\'t want to be that way. i think i\\\'ll try to strike up a conversation with someone on the streetcar today.',0,427,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(14995,2523,'2002-03-12 20:05:08',156,'lotus','what are you reading?',1,21,122,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14996,2523,'2002-03-12 20:06:18',156,'lotus','i live in ottawa, and have only visited toronto, never lived in. the longest i\\\'ve stayed is, umm, a week? and my experiences have led me to conclude that people smile more at strangers here than they do in ottawa. ',0,217,123,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14997,2523,'2002-03-12 20:08:54',156,'lotus','argh! clearly i am no longer super-multitasking-girl. i managed to say the exact opposite of what i meant. ahem.\r\nanyway:\r\n<br><br>\r\n1. i have never lived in toronto, just visted it for long weekends etc\r\n<br><br>\r\n2. people smile at/strike up conversations with strangers a lot more in ottawa than they do in toronto.',0,318,156,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(14998,2523,'2002-03-12 21:04:41',148,'jeeff','i am a bad trahntonian.  i smile at people and look them in the eye and stuff.  and the weirdest things happen to me because of it.  at least it keeps life interesting.',0,168,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(14999,2523,'2002-03-12 21:12:08',123,'dcc','it\\\'s always that way. and thank you for being more clear. i was about to disagree with you, but .. ottawa is a much friendlier town. i have never stayed more than a few days there at a time. then again, when i go, most frequently it is Canada Day. and hell, when you fill a town with a few thousand drunk people, of course everyone is friendly.',0,347,156,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15000,2523,'2002-03-12 21:12:15',66,'canada','thas rite. reprahzent an gimme all mah props!',1,45,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15001,2523,'2002-03-12 21:15:04',123,'dcc','sometimes i wish i had interesting things happen to me. but when you recall the story of that <a href=\\\"http://swinney.org/journals/article.phtml?id=2233.txt\\\">fateful tuesday</a> with that older asian woman, i\\\'m not so sure anymore.',2,240,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15002,2523,'2002-03-12 21:31:08',207,'tim','this weird thing happened to me on the street car the other day. i looked up and smiled at some lady. when she glared, i looked down and didn\\\'t really look in direction the rest of the ride. when she got off, she walked past me and said, \\\"dead bitch\\\" and hissed. and i was like uh, huh, um, what?',1,305,148,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15003,2519,'2002-03-12 22:11:21',187,'kandrer','jeeff, you\\\'re right, right, and right. the story wasn\\\'t clear. i usually try to have a native (or better) reader to make sure it makes at least half of the sense i wanted in it. i should not write directly inside swinney. it\\\'s just that i read many posts about Canadian that night, and that reminded me my mess up with one. it seemed a big point to me since i am really paranoid about the missunderstanding and comments i get really often thanks to my accent and mistakes in english. in this story *i* was the accent criminal. it\\\'s not a really interesting matter; let\\\'s say it\\\'s rather a common way for me to avoid speaking about any serious critics i could make about my life in the US.\r\nit\\\'s a bit the same with my written mistakes: i can protect myself behind weird style so people will forgive me possible misjudgments (it seems it kinda works here, see how supportive you all have been ;) thanks a lot, seriously)\r\nto Scapegoat: yes, my own talking became jumbled (i had to check jumbled in dictionary :}). and yes, that\\\'s also what the Canadian lady told me when i started to apologize; but in that case her french had been no dialect; just her accent and my bias made me wrong. i don\\\'t know if Canadian is so different; i thought it was just a story of vocabulary (like gas/oil line/queue and other little tricks between US and GB english)',1,1374,0,0,'64.156.225.90','en'),(15004,2525,'2002-03-12 22:20:04',187,'kandrer','i envy you. i can\\\'t imagine i would be awake at dawn without being out of my mind of paranoia. i have my best memories with waking up at sunset :)',1,149,0,0,'64.156.225.90','en'),(15005,2526,'2002-03-12 23:12:06',69,'starlajo','i like this.\r\nyou should write here lots.',2,41,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15006,2519,'2002-03-13 01:05:29',65,'le','dear kandrer. do not worry and do not apologize. i enjoy your disturbed english, it makes me think about language. also, in some way it leaves more to reader interpretation and that is nice.',4,190,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15007,2519,'2002-03-13 01:34:47',66,'Peter','<br><tt>yes; sometimes, when a piece of writing is well written but doesnt have \\\'flow\\\'... it makes me stop and kind of pay more attention to whats being said. i like that feeling. sometimes, flow is overrated</tt>',1,219,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15008,2523,'2002-03-13 03:12:44',130,'treetop','to dcc:\r\n<p>\r\none possible solution is that someone farted next to the man so he moved to the other end of the car.  smelly things are usually my motivation for moving around in a car.\r\n<p>\r\ntoday on the N line i felt as if someone was looking into me.  i looked around briefly and noticed a guy next to me smiling.  i smiled back and then turned back to gaze at other people in the reflection of the window.  some of them gaze back, other were oblivious.</p>',2,459,0,0,'172.151.200.175','en'),(15009,2515,'2002-03-13 04:06:11',185,'orion','now what is, in all likelyhood, you are very, very <i>itchy</i> ;-)',1,67,66,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(15010,201,'2002-03-13 04:56:39',65,'le','OH MY GOD I LOVE ROBOTEC9!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!',6,48,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15011,2523,'2002-03-13 05:07:19',122,'hool','<p>in the situation i remember, i was reading some free computer magazine i picked up at the bus stop.</p>',0,106,156,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(15012,2523,'2002-03-13 05:09:55',122,'hool','<p>i smile at strangers and look them in the eye, too.  most people are pretty open to it, but many don\\\'t realize what\\\'s happening until it\\\'s too late.  i can often see a delayed flinch of recognition just as they pass out of my periphery.  often i prefer to hide under my hat, especially when it\\\'s cold.</p>',0,320,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(15013,2529,'2002-03-13 06:21:50',127,'pokey','<p><i>Everything and anything can be a metaphor for \\\"life.\\\"  I like this one.</i></p>\r\n',0,93,0,0,'67.201.151.144','en'),(15014,2529,'2002-03-13 06:49:36',59,'s5','dude. what\\\'s wrong with laser tag. at least you can\\\'t lose an eye.',1,72,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15015,2529,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you like laser tag!?!?!?  i love laser tag!!! no wonder we got married!!!!',1,74,59,1,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15016,2529,'2002-03-13 08:21:08',59,'s5','OMFG!!! STFU!!!!',5,16,0,1,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15017,2529,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you like laser tag!?!?!?  i love laser tag!!! no wonder we got married!!!!',1,74,59,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15018,2529,'2002-03-13 08:22:31',59,'s5','OMFG!!! STFU!!!!',5,16,59,1,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15019,2529,'2002-03-13 08:23:45',59,'s5','OMFG!!!!! STFU!!!!',5,18,62,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15020,2529,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','HOMOFAG!!!!',1,11,59,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15021,2527,'2002-03-13 11:23:13',64,'Joanna','I think the friends that really matter, the ones who will ALWAYS be a part of you never get lost in the first place, or they will find you again. Without wanting to sound too trite, I believe that what is meant to  be will be.',1,226,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15022,2527,'2002-03-13 13:22:56',176,'Scapegoat','I think that friendships are linked to tides they ebb and flow. Every once and a while they run a ground too. I do have to say that I feel the people that really matter have a tendency to find you again even after some lengthy periods of time. ',0,244,0,0,'64.229.163.63','en'),(15023,2523,'2002-03-13 13:52:38',156,'lotus','it is not cold today. so i wore a skirt for the first time in, umm, a year? people always want to read over my shoulder when i pick up the express. i can\\\'t read free will astrology in peace!',0,193,122,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15024,2528,'2002-03-13 13:54:12',156,'lotus','this is cool.',0,13,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15025,2529,'2002-03-13 14:25:15',123,'dcc','man, i want to play paintball even more now. gotta get the crew together to shoot paint at each other at the good ol\\\' smelly sgt. splatters.',3,143,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15026,2523,'2002-03-13 14:38:06',123,'dcc to treetop','I\\\'m sure I would have noticed that and relocated too. I know *I* didn\\\'t make brown air!\r\n<p>\r\nalso, just as this man got up, he looked at me, looked directly at me as if he was checking to see that i was staring at him. i noticed this and continued to stare just over his right shoulder. then he got up and sat far far away.\r\n<p>i\\\'m still laughing at it even though it was now two days ago.',2,399,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15027,2527,'2002-03-13 14:42:08',123,'dcc','i do agree with that ebb and flow, but in my case the flow seems to be more prevalent than the ebb.\r\n<p>in my observation, everything moves in cycles, the closeness of friendships is no exception.\r\n<p><b>to Joanna:</b> i suppose you\\\'re right. i just don\\\'t like to accept fate in that way, i guess. one part of me just wants to let the flow of the world around me pull me along, another part of me just wants to fight against it, to push back, to take what <i>i</i> want from life. i\\\'m not sure which part of me will win in the end.',2,540,176,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15028,2526,'2002-03-13 15:05:06',155,'blue stocking','thank you very much, tim. i have looked for articles by you, but didn\\\'t find any?',0,84,207,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15029,2526,'2002-03-13 15:06:37',155,'blue stocking','hello! thank you for reading this. your comment means a *lot* to me, because, well, i\\\'ve been cheating. i\\\'ve been posting things i\\\'ve written several months, even a couple of years ago. this article was the first really recent thing i\\\'ve written. and i\\\'m always paranoid that i\\\'ll never write anything again. so thank you for reading, and for being so encouraging!',0,382,69,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15030,2529,'2002-03-13 18:28:21',83,'adamajah','is this where you currently work?  sounds fun.',0,46,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15031,2529,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','yeah it does. i\\\'ve never had the urge to play paintball in my life but it sounds sort of fun.',1,96,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15032,2526,'2002-03-13 19:00:02',207,'tim','hi blue stocking. my name is really simon but for some reason, swinney.org wouldn\\\'t let me post any comments so i started using the nickname that gus gave me (tim).',1,167,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15033,2529,'2002-03-13 19:01:07',123,'dcc','anyone i know that has or does still enjoy playing quake-style games always loves playing paintball.\r\n<p>i\\\'m also convinced it makes for a better paintball player.',0,166,207,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15034,2529,'2002-09-16 21:29:19',82,'simon','i\\\'ve never played quake before. but maybe i\\\'d like it too. i usually play games like cruisin\\\' usa and pole position and g \\\'n\\\' r pinball. but, as always, i\\\'m open to new stuff.',1,193,123,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15035,2526,'2002-03-13 20:05:31',155,'blue stocking','if you write under the name simon_fk then i just had a fantastic time reading some of your recent articles. merci!',1,114,207,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15036,2526,'2002-03-13 20:40:04',207,'tim','wow, thanks! i really appreciate it. i don\\\'t post here terribly often...but when i have more time, i\\\'d like to.',1,117,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15037,2525,'2002-03-13 22:45:34',82,'simon','comment test',0,12,0,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15038,2524,'2002-03-13 23:19:17',128,'Javorai','amazing.\r\n</br>\r\nthis makes me want to take one of those psych of family systems classes next semester so I can learn how to perform unethical experiments on my own family circus, just to poke fun at them.<p>\r\nthey could change them for good, though, which is also an inspiring thought.',0,286,0,0,'129.130.229.52','en'),(15039,2528,'2002-03-14 01:29:00',88,'deb','sometimes it\\\'s the simple things that mean the most... very nice.',3,68,0,0,'198.139.250.192','en'),(15040,2529,'2002-03-14 02:18:05',17,'austin','yeti likes knows an aweful lot about paintball, and he plays counterstrike until 3 a.m. every night.',4,100,123,0,'205.179.19.155','en'),(15041,2529,'2002-03-14 02:42:14',65,'le','unreal tournament actually.\r\n\r\na yeti likes games of any sort. they also play cribbage on yahoo games. yes, a yeti enjoys a good hand of cribbage. also, peanuckle. \r\n\r\ni would like to learn to play canasta.\r\n\r\ni do not enjoy paintball or lasertag.\r\n\r\nmethinks chiamattt should be careful not to become one of those guys who wears camoulflage everywhere.',4,353,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15042,2529,'2002-03-14 03:56:44',123,'dcc','if chiamatt buys a paintball gun, it\\\'s all over.',0,51,0,0,'24.112.135.178','en'),(15043,2529,'2002-03-14 04:02:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>canasta!</tt>',1,21,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15044,2529,'2002-03-14 04:06:38',122,'hool','<p>chia: how many times in a day do you say to that? </p>',0,57,0,0,'24.114.48.31','en'),(15045,2529,'2002-03-14 05:01:56',93,'chiamattt','testes testes one two three',0,27,0,0,'24.42.194.13','en'),(15046,2529,'2002-03-14 05:02:29',93,'chiamattt','i do the rules about ten or eleven times a day. blah. ',0,54,122,0,'24.42.194.13','en'),(15047,2529,'2002-03-14 05:05:58',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nive owned a paintball gun. i used to own a minimag. i put about three grand into the thing and then sold it and picked up a computer.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nhttp://www.airgun.com/Gallery/Custom/BlueSplashMini.jpg\r\n</p> ',0,213,123,0,'24.42.194.13','en'),(15048,2529,'2002-03-14 05:14:12',93,'chiamattt','to be honest dude. laser tag is geigh. paintball just seemed to have more too it. i dont play anymore. In fact i havnt played paintball in over six years. today i walked out onto the playing field for the first time in five. it was strange being out there with goggles on. I felt weird. there was a time i loved paintball. a time when i had no phear. oh i have stories. another time maybe. then one day i got hit close to the eye working and it was all over. phear set in. being a part of paint ball like i was made it less and less fun, and then getting hit just under the eye by some newbie rental ended it there. I used to be a real handy man around the guns. I went down to rochester to get certified in fixing some types of guns and knew the ins and outs of the automag, black knight, vm68, and most other guns that used a closed bolt/sear pin type assembly. now paintball guns have computers inside. they need batteries and well the whole scene in general has taken the route most extreme sports have taken. gawdy and flashy. I mean just look at that minimag pic. My mag was gray and chrome. pretty much the way it looked coming out of the box. I mean i invested in an expansion chamber, butt pack and all sorts of barrels and dodads, but they all seemed to make me better at paintball....not as much so that i \\\"looked\\\" like i was good at paintball. its a real big thing these days. fuck that shit yo. no doubt its a fun game....it just got really old really fast working paintball. ',3,1495,59,0,'24.42.194.13','en'),(15049,2529,'2002-03-14 05:20:33',17,'austin','geigh?!<BR><BR>that just made me snort my cream of wheat.',1,57,93,0,'64.174.154.61','en'),(15050,2529,'2002-03-14 05:44:27',59,'s5','<pre>\r\n<FONT SIZE=\"-1\">\r\n*chiam* laser tag blows dude.\r\n/m chiam haha\r\n/m chiam dude, it totally rocks\r\n/m chiam I LOVE LASER TAG YOU ASSHOLE!!!!!!\r\n*chiam* ok well i dont agree. i talked to you on the swinney post.\r\n*chiam* laser tag is for those who bruse easily. haha\r\n*chiam* i know people that say paintball is a bigger rush then\r\n*chiam* skydiving.\r\n/m chiam oh i\'m sure it is\r\n/m chiam it\'s like a mini war\r\n*chiam* no man. not war at all.\r\n*chiam* i dont like it when people compare it to war\r\n*chiam* its nothing like war.\r\n/m chiam okay. but aren\'t there two teams who are trying to eliminate the other\r\n team by shooting them?\r\n/m chiam i mean, of course it\'s all good fun and it\'s safe and fun and all\r\n\r\n*chiam* well you see. that game is called elimination. but there is\r\n*chiam* capture the flag, attack and defend. breifcase. speedstart.\r\n*chiam* predator. all sorts of games with objectives other then just\r\n*chiam* pure elim.\r\n/m chiam true nough\r\n\r\n*chiam* like most tournies are caputre the flag.\r\n*chiam* you get more points for bringing the flag home, then you do\r\n*chiam* for marking an opponent.\r\n/m chiam so okay. it\'s more like geneva convention war. :D :D :D\r\n*chiam* its fun i guess. i dont play anymore.\r\n*chiam* lol. yes!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!\r\n/m chiam heheh\r\n*chiam* ok bed. i have to work tomorrow. be cool brotha.\r\n</font></pre>',1,1357,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15051,2529,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','you guys are such phaegs.  you need to settle this on the field.  no \\\"stay cool dude\\\", FIGHT TO THE DEATH.',1,112,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15052,2529,'2002-03-14 06:41:40',154,'ryan','pinochle<p>\r\n<i>*best friendly</i>',0,34,0,0,'216.26.60.6','en'),(15053,2526,'2002-03-14 06:43:10',124,'gus','I\\\'VE MISSED YOU TIM!',0,23,207,0,'64.160.55.183','en'),(15054,2529,'2002-03-14 10:01:14',65,'le','FUCK YOU RYAN!!!!!!!!!!',6,23,154,0,'209.162.49.115','en'),(15055,999,'2002-03-14 10:04:38',65,'le','i have totally encontered the united negro hamburger fund guy. more than once!! it\\\'s cool that we all see the same characters and that gives our city an identity. it\\\'s lame that our recurring characters have shitty lives sometimes.',4,237,0,0,'209.162.49.115','en'),(15056,999,'2002-03-14 10:22:25',59,'s5','12 galaxies zegatronic rocket society',0,37,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15057,999,'2002-03-14 13:55:29',66,'Peter to s5','<br><tt>that sounds like a dr. bonner-ism</tt>',1,46,0,0,'128.6.32.94','en'),(15058,762,'2002-03-14 16:13:42',128,'Javorai','Seal?',0,5,0,0,'129.130.38.232','en'),(15059,2532,'2002-03-14 16:19:49',74,'jo','oh, see, what i was doing was sitting at restaurants wishing they would all go home. i didn\\\'t know you were supposed to <b>approach</b> and/or <b>talk</b> to them. so that\\\'s the key?',0,188,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15060,2528,'2002-03-14 19:13:06',206,'josta','you have talented papers/notes. mine just contain Cheesy Sex Haiku (like this from today\\\'s chemistry lecture): <br>\r\nelements balanced <br>\r\nkinetic intensity <br>\r\ntime on an axis',2,183,0,0,'204.210.196.23','en'),(15061,2534,'2002-03-14 21:52:39',69,'starlajo','I have been debating with myself for ages as to whether I should post this or not...\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI know I am supposed to ignore that this ever happened to us Joanna, but it has been going on for FAR too long now and I am publically going to express my frustration instead of making stupid cryptic comments all the time.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThis is how it is:\r\nYou pushed me away and began to almost pretend that I don\'t exist whilst still talking to the person who was equally as wrong as me. That is what I don\'t understand.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nFine, ignore both of us if that is your problem, but don\'t make one of us out to be the evil one, when we were both equally part of the same act that hurt you.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI lost you as a friend almost TWO years ago. It still hurts. When will you realise that if you are prepared to speak to one of us, both of us should therefore be okay? It\'s been two years and I\'m still hanging around waiting for you to be sensible about this. No more cryptic comments from me. I am going to be straightforward with everything, even if I have to do it here because I\'m sure you wouldn\'t read any email I sent.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nWhat I\'m trying to say is that I am here for you if you want that.  I mean it! I hate seeing you upset and struggling.\r\n',1,1239,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15062,2534,'2002-03-14 21:56:55',69,'starlajo','There were supposed to be paragraphs in there somewhere, but I seem to be technically incompetent when venting.',3,111,0,1,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15063,999,'2002-03-14 23:39:06',129,'moebius','<br>i\\\'m pretty sure there\\\'s more than one member of the United Negro Hamburger Fund in SF. in my years living in the upper haight i ran into several of them.<br><br>way way back when i first moved to this town the Chronicle\\\'s \\\"Question Man\\\" went around asking homeless folks what their best panhandling line was. the hamburger fund was one of the responses. for some reason i cut this whole question man piece out and kept it with me. for <i>ten years.</i> <br><br>i\\\'ve never adequately explained to myself why i did this. perhaps i thought it might come in handy someday?',2,590,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15064,2217,'2002-03-15 00:21:49',228,'belly','<p>oooooooh how exciting and lovely! there is so much love around, it makes me want to put glitter on my lips and make sparkly kissy marks on things!</p>',0,153,0,0,'203.167.247.214','en'),(15065,2534,'2002-03-15 00:52:16',190,'dahlia','damn, that got me all weepy.',2,28,0,0,'66.92.4.146','en'),(15066,999,'2002-03-15 01:21:40',59,'s5 to peter','it\\\'s about <a href=\\\"http://www.12galaxies.20m.com/\\\">this guy</a>',3,73,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15067,2528,'2002-03-15 03:12:07',128,'Javorai','I love this.  I\\\'d love to write something like this, its clever and beautiful.',0,81,0,0,'129.130.231.239','en'),(15068,2522,'2002-03-15 03:39:27',128,'Javorai','thanx! ;)',0,9,0,0,'129.130.231.239','en'),(15069,2534,'2002-03-15 03:41:57',0,'jacinta','honey i am *always* up in the middle of the night.  and even if I wasn\\\'t I\\\'m quite used to being woken.  so if you get all sad and lonely again and everyone else is asleep you can always txt/ring me.  being sad and alone sucks, so don\\\'t do it again, k? :)\r\n<br>\r\nJ ',2,274,0,0,'139.80.123.34','en'),(15070,2534,'2002-03-15 03:42:47',64,'Joanna','Oh man, I didn\\\'t mean to make you weepy!  It\\\'s all okay now,she rang me  twice this morning andthen this afternoon I sent her a silver sparkly purse as a graduation gift, and her father apparently isn\\\'t mad at me, and I\\\'ve been faxing drawings of flowers to the person I had the fight with, and so it\\\'s all okay, really! Yay for happy endings.',1,358,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15071,2534,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','bozo lists make for misunderstandings.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nhi joanna.  i miss you.',1,73,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15072,2534,'2002-03-15 04:25:23',64,'Joanna','<B>Jac</B>: Cous, you little lurker you! Hey! I really must email you/txt you. I hope all is well down south. \r\n\r\n<BR><BR>\r\n\r\n<B>o</B>: miss you too! if $1500 falls into my lap soon, I will come and see you and yeah, that\\\'ll be choice. ',1,239,0,0,'203.97.2.242','en'),(15073,2540,'2002-03-15 05:49:58',59,'s5','is this because of what i said about paintball?',1,47,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15074,2540,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','some of the countries you listed have nukes too.',3,48,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15075,2540,'2002-03-15 06:14:58',59,'s5','those are the countries bush just named as new nuclear targets. supposedly the thing now is that the US is going to build some new \\\"small\\\" nuclear bombs.',0,159,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15076,2540,'2002-03-15 06:19:18',185,'orion','quote from a weps designer: \\\"It\\\'s easy to make low and micro yield devices, we call them \\\'failures.\\\'\\\"  (ok, so he builds things that I as a scientist wish we could uninvent, but at least he\\\'s funny)  iirc i saw that in the latest wired.  but icbw.',1,265,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(15077,2540,'2002-03-15 06:20:07',185,'orion','oh, and canada has nukes?  wtf?  that\\\'s a new one on me.  For some reason I thought they didn\\\'t.',1,102,0,0,'209.163.146.106','en'),(15078,2540,'2002-03-15 06:49:29',59,'s5','\"The Canadian Government does not have nuclear weapons, opposes nuclear proliferation, and officially supports disarmament.\"\r\n<br><br>\r\n<a href=\"http://watserv1.uwaterloo.ca/~plough/cnanw/cnanw.html\">according to this.</a>',3,234,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15079,2540,'2002-03-15 08:19:50',176,'Scapegoat','Canada opposes nuclear weapon proliferation but when it comes to nuclear power generation we are one of the worlds leading exporters of reactors. Our reactor brand name is CANDU. <br><br>\r\nAs far as Canada having nukes, it makes no sense with so many parked south of the border. I was amused by s5\\\'s link, there was a statement about Canada threatening nuclear war by its allies. I couldn\\\'t stop laughing as images of a kid threatening a bully with violence from an older family member entered my head.<br><br>\r\nIn my sick and twisted mind this post conjured up images of Chia and I riding a couple of H-bombs down out of the sky Dr. Strangelove style.<br><br>\r\nIn reality though the low yeild tactical nuke idea freaks me out. I think Bush may be gung ho enough to try and pull this off unilaterally. Tell me I\\\'m wrong please. I\\\'m obviously still trying to learn how to love the bomb. ',0,898,0,0,'64.229.161.77','en'),(15080,327,'2002-03-15 08:54:42',187,'kandrer','well, it seems that you mentionned at least a few things that could have been stopped: sandwiches at lunch, gym, etc...<br>\r\n[x] best friendly stupidly constructive',1,164,0,0,'63.208.128.43','en'),(15081,2540,'2002-03-15 08:58:22',129,'moebius','well i won\\\'t tell you you\\\'re wrong. so far it seems like the gw bush administration is turning out to be the scariest bunch of people we\\\'ve had in control of this country in my lifetime. for every remotely \\\"good\\\" move he makes, it seems like ten horrible moves are made, either overtly or covertly.\r\n<br><br>of course, right-wing conservatives felt exactly the same way about Clinton, so i guess this is their revenge. sigh.',1,439,176,0,'67.25.107.166','en'),(15082,2540,'2002-03-15 12:37:38',66,'Peter','<br><tt>thanks to this site, i think ive thought about canada more in the past week than i have in my entire life. <i>go canada, go canada, get bizzy, get bizzy!</i></tt>',1,170,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15083,2540,'2002-03-15 12:41:54',59,'s5','swinney.org = infotainment',0,26,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15084,2540,'2002-03-15 13:23:09',151,'stella','What scares me is that god only knows what is going on in the world that would prompt GW to start discussing nukes. I\\\'ve been WAAAAY out of the news loop since 9/11, partially by choice and partially due to time constraints, so it could be readily available information that I\\\'ve just missed. Considering that the attorney general overturned the FIA (Freedom of Information Act), meaning that information is now classified and restricted, who knows whats really going on in these countries where we\\\'re fighting the \\\'war on terrorism\\\'. ',0,550,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15085,2217,'2002-03-15 14:43:19',74,'jo','okay so i can\\\'t resist telling this story:\r\n<p>on maybe our third date, mike told me that he\\\'d had a dream the night before that we\\\'d met at the edith frost show and had been dating ever since and in the dream, i was sleeping beside him and when he woke up, he was sad that i wasn\\\'t.<BR>\r\ni didn\\\'t ever think i\\\'d meet a boy who <i>literally</i> dreamed about being in a relationship with me. i\\\'m the kind of girl to whom boys say: \\\"joanna, you are the <b>coolest girl i know</b>. did i tell you about this HOT CHICK I WANT TO BONE?\\\"<BR>\r\nhe\\\'s tender and smart and affectionate and funny, so crazy funny, and i have a bigger crush on him the longer i know him.</p>',1,693,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15086,2540,'2002-03-15 17:23:22',128,'Javorai','good.  when are you going to start bombing every country in the world?  please tell me so I can call my parents and tell them to move to Antartica.',0,147,0,0,'129.130.231.19','en'),(15087,2542,'2002-03-15 17:23:53',74,'jo','will some nice editor fix the smart quotes in this?',0,51,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15088,2542,'2002-03-15 17:35:32',17,'austin','if you get me the hex values, i\\\'ll fix it.',4,45,74,0,'205.179.19.155','en'),(15089,2542,'2002-03-15 17:37:30',74,'jo','no, i mean. fix the errors that the smart quotes have caused and just turn them into regular apostrophes and quote marks? the squares are distracting from kandrer\\\'s nice piece.',2,179,17,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15090,2542,'2002-03-15 20:31:32',187,'kandrer','oh... sorry. I tried to fix this myself. mmm... it looks like it worked... here is my rules of thumb list as of today:<br>\r\n[x] do not type a post directly from the swinney\\\'s edit box<br>\r\n[x] do not copy/paste to swinney from Word (specially a Japanese version of it)<br>',1,275,0,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(15091,2542,'2002-03-15 20:40:32',187,'kandrer','i tried to add a picture afterward, and it seems i failed that... this is the pier with my step brothers fishing, another day.',1,126,17,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(15092,2540,'2002-03-15 21:05:12',129,'moebius','well then, you should think about moving to Montreal sometime....',0,65,66,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15093,2519,'2002-03-15 21:40:22',187,'kandrer','thanks a lot :} though, i feel better when i apologize. BTW, will you believe it? i just realized yesterday, (by some late brain association binding no one can pretend to understand) that i made again this mistake Cliff already explained to me: the title was supposed to be \\\"How i treat FOREIGNERS\\\" and not strangers (moron!).<br>\r\n=> Austin, is there a way to edit another than the most recent post? for slow second thought people...',1,440,0,0,'208.223.155.99','en'),(15094,2540,'2002-03-15 21:59:50',185,'orion','You too can have a <a href=\\\"http://rpsc.raytheon.com/\\\">job in antarctica</a.  (also see <a href=\\\"http://www.theice.org\\\">theice.org</a> for a cool (no pun intended) personal site about living and working there).  ISTR I first saw this site linked from swinney, so I\\\'ll take this opportunity to reinject it... ;-)',1,326,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(15095,2540,'2002-03-15 22:00:43',185,'orion','damnit, the first link ran over.  here\\\'s a link to the <a href=\\\"http://www.theice.org\\\">second site</a>',1,111,0,0,'66.25.24.179','en'),(15096,2538,'2002-03-15 22:29:21',148,'jeeff','i love backpacking shows like lonely planet/pilot guides.  and this was just as engrossing as one of those.  very nice writing.',1,127,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(15097,2538,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i really enjoyed this.  especially this part: <i>. I am firmly against capital punishment, but I say throw them ALL into a squat toilet together, lock the doors, and then force them to listen to blaring techno music for the rest of their natural lives!!! </i>.',2,260,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15098,2538,'2002-03-16 03:00:41',130,'treetop','i really like this too.  it makes me want to don my pack and go to africa.  hmmmm, flight are really cheap. a friend of mine just flew from sf to spain for $230!  my traveling bone is itching again...',1,200,0,0,'172.170.143.109','en'),(15099,2538,'2002-03-16 05:10:00',92,'kini','<p>so do it.</p>\r\n<p>p.s i really enjoyed this one, too.</p>',0,60,0,0,'203.27.69.94','en'),(15100,2540,'2002-03-16 05:24:50',175,'Paul G','<a href=\\\"http://www.pva.org/vetday/kids5.htm\\\">Hi.</a>  Why make a joke of the loss suffered by \\\"<a href=\\\"http://www.dav.org/\\\">all of those</a> <a href=\\\"http://www.pva.org/\\\">brave</a>, <a href=\\\"http://vvof.org/vvofaopg.htm\\\">heroic</a>, <a href=\\\"http://www.vva.org/Benefits/ptsd.htm\\\">u.s soldiers</a>\\\" and their \\\"loved ones\\\" instead of just questioning the politics that put them in harm\\\'s way?\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n',0,455,0,0,'67.24.115.200','en'),(15101,2540,'2002-03-16 09:19:35',176,'Scapegoat','I understand the point you are making (I think) but the tongue and cheek biting comments in the post (to me at least) are mainly aimed at the political decisions to put these troops in harms way and not the troops themselves. If I\\\'m misinterpreting I\\\'m sure Chia will point this out. The one link you provided was to a site related to agent orange, think of the ecological and health fallout due to the use of small <i>tactical</i> nukes to both the people that live in the area of deployment, not to mention the american or allied troops that will inevitable be exposed to fallout in any mop up activities.<br><br>\r\nI think the point is that the leaders don\\\'t really think about the average human, their own soldiers U.S. or otherwise only get a little bit more consideration. They are more interested in speed of victory so as not to have to keep up public support for any serious fighting. It sickens me that this Public Opinion management imperative would probably be the main reason for the deployment of these <i>tactical</i> nukes. Maybe I am out to lunch though but I don\\\'t think these  concerns are so far fetch.<br><br>\r\nI apologize if I come on strong with any of these comments it just that with my Father having been over to Afghanistan already I have a hard time not commenting on it. It has come close to home not in the same way as in the case of victims families but still close.',1,1408,175,0,'64.229.160.111','en'),(15102,2540,'2002-03-16 11:17:21',154,'ryan','nuclear war makes me wet.',0,25,0,0,'216.26.60.234','en'),(15103,2345,'2002-03-16 18:10:34',17,'austin','found poetry somehow justifies bad poetry.  all you bad poets of the world, pick up a sharpie and take to the streets!',4,119,65,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15104,2540,'2002-03-16 18:50:37',175,'Paul G','The last paragraph of the post seemed to portray those who serve as something like bumbling janitors who, from time to time, injure themselves while they maintained the machines that do the real work of fighting.\r\n\r\n<p>If Chia really does think that the U.S. soldiers are brave and heroic, and the feelings of their loved ones are important, then I misread the post - sorry.  It really seemed like mockery, to me.  And it\\\'s not just the U.S. is it?  The Commonwealth states are bound up very closely in this...</p>\r\n\r\n<p>One thought in regard to tactical nuclear weaponry: It might be the case that such instruments allow for a proportional response to the use, by other parties, of biological or chemical devices.  Thus, the widespread report of their manufacture would serve as a deterrent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bad art theory is just funny.  But, as the history of the 20th century shows all too well, bad political theory and bad economic theory get people killed.  Are some subjects more worthy of sober discussion than others?</p>  ',0,1030,176,0,'65.229.226.171','en'),(15105,2540,'2002-03-17 00:49:43',93,'chiamattt','<p>\r\nlet me be clear. the post was pure sarcasm. i am a pacifist in daily life, but believe in armed conflict if that conflict is justified. what IS justified is sometimes hard to justify though, as this \\\'war\\\' against terrorism seems to be. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni am sure that the men and women training in any military, rebel group, police force, and so on have the \\\'right\\\' intentions for joining...\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nwhile im sure the soldiers that die flying those multi million dollar helicopters, aircraft, and tanks have what it takes to be brave and heroic, their presence in afghanistan wrong. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\n<pre>\r\nTERRORIST THREAT  -  GLORIOUS TROOPS\r\nCOWARDLY VIOLENCE -  FOREIGN POLICY\r\nRELIGIOUS FANATIC - PATRIOTIC CITIZEN\r\n</pre>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nI dont know which side of the fence you sit on...but i tend to think that any kind of aggression towards a \\\'people\\\' is wrong...and having an army of foreign troops within the borders of a nation, dictating to those who have lived there their whole lives is wrong. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnuclear weapons are wrong. i do not believe there is any situation where nuclear weapons are justified. no matter how many lives saved, more lives are taken. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthe chain of command dictates that those on the ground are peons and are not accountable for the actions their actions. this is why killing a man in battle is not \\\'murder\\\' but killing a man who has surrendered is. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnuclear war insists that even those who surrender die, and for this reason i think nuclear weapons are unjustified in every situation. \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nfurthermore, after calling those who flew the aircraft into the wtc cowards, how is it we can listen to those same people call for the use of nuclear weapons against \\\'terrorist\\\' nations.  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nnuclear war is terrorism. \r\n</p>',1,1827,0,0,'24.42.194.20','en'),(15106,2540,'2002-03-17 02:31:45',187,'kandrer','bravo!<br>\r\nand biological war too.',1,35,0,0,'63.208.134.84','en'),(15107,2535,'2002-03-17 08:58:08',124,'gus','So this made me think of the short story The Swan by Roald Dahl. Its a beautiful story, but it is also upsetting and tragic. It does not have a happy ending. I think I\\\'ll dig it out and re-read it tomorrow.',0,209,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15108,2536,'2002-03-17 09:31:43',59,'s5','bukkake',4,7,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15109,2535,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','wow, gus. <a href=\\\"http://www.angelfire.com/md/Topperites/swan.html\\\">that</a> was good.',1,93,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15110,2529,'2002-03-18 10:22:50',222,'satan','serious shit. fucking hell.',0,27,0,0,'24.68.109.138','en'),(15111,2529,'2002-03-18 10:24:04',222,'satan','those fucking tit fuckers banned my boney hot ass for what? all i wanted to say is that they are all bitch tits and paint ball bitches are bitches. swallow my real deal soul sucking dick and spit out a liver. punk mother fucker.',0,228,93,0,'24.68.109.138','en'),(15112,2529,'2002-03-18 10:26:01',65,'le','wow cool satan, are you a friend of chiamattt\\\'s from PAINTBALL or IRC??? they are sort of the same thing. male agression.',6,124,0,0,'209.86.3.186','en'),(15113,2543,'2002-03-18 12:32:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>various detritus that somehow we deemed to be of some arcane use</i><br><br>nice.</tt> ',1,97,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15114,2538,'2002-03-18 15:23:17',123,'dcc','i wish i hadn\\\'t read this at work. it made me just want to leave. \r\n<p>spectacular.',2,86,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15115,2536,'2002-03-18 16:55:38',81,'felicity','The gentleman in the preceding conversation happens to be a huge fan of bukkake. I believe he heads some kind of bukkake-arranging organization in the Boston area.  I, myself, don\\\'t get the appeal.  To each their own, I suppose.',1,231,0,0,'216.42.123.44','en'),(15116,2535,'2002-03-18 19:26:13',156,'lotus','mmm. i grew up on the words of roald dahl! thank you. ',0,54,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15117,2535,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','me too.  my favourite was \\\"revolting rhymes\\\" and i can probably still quote the entire thing.',1,99,156,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15118,448,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','check dat ass out. isn\'t it round? isn\'t it furry?? don\'t you want to TOUCH IT??!! she likes to sleep on my chest, and nibble at my shirt. purrrr.',1,186,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15119,2544,'2002-03-19 06:31:52',124,'gus','I wish I had a longer attention span, Im going to have to read this a little at a time. heh!',0,92,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15120,2525,'2002-03-19 08:35:30',168,'laney','on a first date after eating a pill, staying up all night talking and making out. we got smoothies and sat on a picnic bench. the half-sun\\\'s reflection on the ocean water at that moment was one of the most intense sights i had seen in a long while.',0,251,0,0,'165.121.120.169','en'),(15121,1216,'2002-03-19 09:30:29',118,'drew','<p>i think the main reason it\\\'s devastating to read that list is that we all are taught, and not just by school or parents but by books and music and art and plays and things that \\\"we\\\" are inclined to trust a little more, that love is supposed to conquer everything, love is a leviathan that can swallow up any problem and digest it into harmless poop. this is supposedly a beautiful sentiment (except for the poop) but, as it takes us a long time to learn, life doesn\\\'t always imitate art.</p>\r\n<p>let\\\'s give things like loss and time credit.  those are very big things.  those are four-letter words at least as abstract and heavy as love.  depression, codependence, poverty, these are longer and more specific, but also quite heavy, and codependence in particular is what we call love when love is misbehaving and trying to do -- yes, more than it\\\'s supposed to do.</p>\r\n<p>love\\\'s no more than a connection between two people, but no less either.  it\\\'s there so things like poverty aren\\\'t pitch-black, so things like misunderstandings aren\\\'t always fatal, but mostly it\\\'s there for its own sake, and it\\\'s delicate, precarious, certainly supple and not brittle but definitely thin, strong like spidersilk, but spidersilk can break too.</p>\r\n<p>spiders are used to this, and when their webs break they just spin them again.  perhaps they complain under their spider breath but we can\\\'t hear them.  they do spin again, though, and by morning you don\\\'t know the web\\\'s been broken.</p>\r\n<p>i am <em>still</em> out of work.  if you want company you haven\\\'t seen in months and months, maybe it\\\'s finally time i came up.</p>',1,1669,0,0,'65.172.233.241','en'),(15122,2542,'2002-03-19 11:29:19',65,'le','hi.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni wrote about my grandma in my journal elswhere. funny thing about death, you can seem ok about it at first but it\\\'s sneaky. since she has died i\\\'ve had more and more intense memories of her. yesterday and the day before i was consumed by the memory of exactly how she opened her arms up to hug me when i saw her, and how she called me sweetheart. that was all i thought about on the way to sleep, my grandma opening her arms to hug me, one specific time that she did that, in front of the trailer in alabama. it was almost tangible. \r\n</p>',4,561,0,0,'165.247.204.82','en'),(15123,2542,'2002-03-19 11:33:05',65,'le','also:<br><br>\r\n\"don\'t worry, you are just going to die\" is a nice calm sentiment. i wish i could have thought of something to say to my grandma that made sense. i wanted to acknowledge that i knew she was on her way out and tell her i was ok with it. when i spoke to my grandma on the phone the week before she died, she said she\'d like to see me. i said, <i>i\'m far away</i>. she said, <i>i\'d like to see you. maybe i\'ll see ya? nothing\'s impossible. you know? nothings impossible.</i> \r\n<br><br>\r\n',4,309,0,0,'165.247.204.82','en'),(15124,2545,'2002-03-19 13:45:03',123,'dcc','<i>attempting to serve pasta to four people with only two forks</i>\r\n<p>\r\nthat i would love to see.\r\n<p>\r\nstories of peoples cats make me wish i wasn\\\'t so allergic to them.',1,175,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15125,2535,'2002-03-19 13:45:20',156,'lotus','how can you choose? matilda. the witches. the twits. henry sugar. the BFG. george\\\'s marvellous medicine. umm, fantastic mister fox. oh! now i have to go find them all again. i\\\'m forgetting one of my favourites. the one about the boy and his father.',0,254,62,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15126,2535,'2002-03-19 13:46:01',156,'lotus','oh! danny the champion of the world. also i still have boy and going solo on my bookshelf.',0,90,62,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15127,1597,'2002-03-19 19:28:03',195,'matt','whatever happened to corey haim?',1,32,0,0,'209.208.17.107','en'),(15128,1597,'2002-03-19 22:21:19',74,'jo','ask and <a href=\\\"http://www.angelfire.com/ny2/CoreyHaim/Groove.html\\\">ye shall receive</a>.',0,96,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15129,2471,'2002-03-19 22:22:03',168,'testing','<a href=\\\"vizbang.com/pix/02002/mammoth-spring/\\\">this is us having fun in mammoth</a><br>',0,94,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15130,2471,'2002-03-19 22:25:10',168,'laney','<a href=\\\"http://vizbang.com/pix/02002/mammoth-spring/\\\">having fun in mammoth take two</a><br>',0,99,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15131,2545,'2002-03-19 22:58:29',168,'laney','<p>i used to own only two forks. they had once been part of a set of six, bright-lime-green-handled numbers from ikea, but i must have lost four of them somewhere along the way. at some point i started inviting new friends over to cook for them, but the first time i did so i realized i only had two forks. poor matt had to try eating angel hair pomodoro with a spoon and knife. margaret had to scarf down what she could while tina took a break to refill her wine glass.</p>\r\n<p>it turned into quite a joke for a while. â€˜â€˜hey, come over to jenâ€™s for dinner. and bring your own fork.â€™â€™</p> \r\n    \r\n',0,597,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15132,1597,'2002-03-19 23:32:00',70,'Fuzzy','Corey Haim lives near me.  He recently got out of detox and is living with his mother in Toronto.  He went to a co-worker\\\'s party recently and was induldging in very non-sober behaviours.',1,190,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(15133,1512,'2002-03-19 23:43:29',124,'gus','I put up a personal ad the other day. Just as an experiment. there are 2 people who responded who are interesting enough to at least be pals. One who is funny but, who knows if they\\\'re cute. I need cute. Lord knows I need them to be cute.\r\n<p>\r\nWhy did I put up a personal ad? because I myself am afraid of becoming \\\"the perpetually single and wittily self-deprecating supportive gay friend\\\" which is a terrible role for me seeing as how Im not even gay. truly, how did I end up here?',4,493,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15134,2547,'2002-03-20 01:23:46',59,'s5','hi cat :) good to see you writing here.',1,39,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15135,1597,'2002-03-20 06:01:24',75,'tyler','i don\\\'t give my subconcious that much credit for developing a theme for my dreams. i think it is lazy like me and just throws some little bullshit story together to tickle my frontal lobe so it will be distracted and i can sleep.',1,232,0,0,'165.247.164.153','en'),(15136,1597,'2002-03-20 06:01:43',75,'tyler','i don\\\'t give my subconcious that much credit for developing a theme for my dreams. i think it is lazy like me and just throws some little bullshit story together to tickle my frontal lobe so it will be distracted and i can sleep.',1,232,0,0,'165.247.164.153','en'),(15137,2545,'2002-03-20 07:41:21',124,'gus','My old cat honey that I had at my parents house has some stories. once she disapeared for over a week. I had given up on seeing her again. We\\\'d already had a puppy stolen from our yard years before and no amount of driving around and yelling \\\"HERE GINGER\\\" did any good. but one night my boyfriend at the time heard a noise at the door. he opened it and there she was, little honey, dirty and covered in grease. \r\n<p>\r\nAnother time I heard a car screech to a halt in front of the house. (honey used to like to lounge on the double yellow) so I looked outside, there she was lying upside down in the street. I ran out thinking she was dead, she wasnt, she rolled over and ran into the house. nothing was terribly wrong with her, except the skin of her paw had been badly damaged and wouldnt grow back. They tried experimental burn victim techniques and nothing worked. What did work was they cut a slit into her side fur, stuck her arm in, cut another slit, and pulled her arm out, so that part of her arm was INSIDE her body. She hobbled around this way, leaking gut goo for a few weeks. the body fur grew onto her arm and they snipped it off. After that her body stripes didnt match up, and one paw had really long body fur, like a little muff.',0,1253,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15138,1597,'2002-03-20 12:37:06',66,'Peter','<br><tt>corey haim lives near you... there\'s something oddly cool about being able to say that.<br><br>a friend of mine grew up in a house in front of a junk-yard in minneapolis. his claim to fame- a story that he often retold- was that he lived in front of the junkyard that had menudo\'s tour jet in it, as scrap. <i>menudo\'s</i> tour jet, man. wow.</tt>',1,366,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15139,2548,'2002-03-20 12:42:56',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>speeding somewhere on route 1 just screaming Eminem songs out the window into the night wind without a care in the world</i></tt>',1,140,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15140,2548,'2002-03-20 14:05:54',87,'bmg','did he make it???',2,17,0,0,'66.90.145.176','en'),(15141,2548,'2002-03-20 14:22:33',65,'le','he made it.',6,11,0,0,'165.247.213.71','en'),(15142,2548,'2002-03-20 15:23:38',74,'jo','LOVE! is a many SPLENDORED THING! austin, i hope you have a good time in thailand. you, too, eggerific.',0,103,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15143,2548,'2002-03-20 15:49:27',93,'chiamattt','the loveeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeeee boat. exciting and newwwwwwwwwwwwww. come aboardddd we\\\'re expecting youuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu',0,159,0,0,'24.42.194.20','en'),(15144,2545,'2002-03-20 15:57:09',151,'stella','<p>eh? her arm went through her body? As in went in one side and come out the other? How does that work? Poor little kitty!</P>\r\n<P> my baby cat was found in the engine of a car. I heard a kitten meowing from our third floor apt. I went investigating and found an itty bitty kitten lodged inside the bumper of some car parked on our street. Grabbed the tools, popped part of the bumber off and birthed the baby. She was about three weeks old at the time. We didn\\\'t realize that she was white until we got her clean. I don\\\'t know how long she was in that car, but it was ticking like it had just been shut off when I got to her. The owners of the car had no idea. But my logic was that they\\\'d rather have a portion of the bumper removed (and then replaced) than have a small cat be dragged throughout the engine. I figured that would cause more damage. She\\\'s a little retarded from the experience, but in all is quite healthy. </P>\r\n\r\n',0,946,124,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15145,2545,'2002-03-20 16:01:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>cats are cute; <a href=\\\"http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=/news/archive/2002/03/19/state2120EST0199.DTL\\\">too many cats</a> can be scary, though.</tt>',1,173,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15146,2547,'2002-03-20 16:42:00',122,'hool','<p>when my maternal grandfather died, i was there in the hospital.  four of his five kids were there.  at one point, while he was still breathing, i felt the urge to go for a walk around the floor we were on.  so i did.  when i came back, he had died, and they were all crying.  i didn\\\'t feel sad.  i felt kind of peaceful, and glad for him, because he\\\'d been destroyed by chemo, and was in pain for a long time. </p>\r\n\r\n<p>i hitchhiked home that night from the city.  i wound up having to walk about 3 hours in the summer night from the outskirts of a small town i was dropped off in to my home out in the country.  only a few cars passed me, and none stopped to pick me up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my mom, and even the attending nurse were worried about me, because i didn\\\'t express any emotion.  they figured i was really upset to the point that i was shellshocked.  quite the opposite.  i felt calm, accepting, and positive.</p>',0,927,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15147,2545,'2002-03-20 17:48:40',218,'da','<a href=\\\"http://vizbang.com/pix/kitties.jpg\\\">\r\n<img src=\\\"http://vizbang.com/pix/kitties.jpg\\\"width=300 height=200>\r\n</a>',1,131,0,0,'128.111.96.65','en'),(15148,2548,'2002-03-20 18:26:07',83,'adamajah','<p>eat something i haven\\\'t eaten so you can come back and show off in front of me by eating it and getting grossed out</p> ',1,126,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15149,2545,'2002-03-20 18:31:22',83,'adamajah','<img src=\\\"http://www.yeager.tv/images/Fam_HotCacaOnaFlyingCarpet_sm.jpg\\\">',0,79,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15150,2545,'2002-03-20 18:36:47',124,'gus','not through the body, like, not through the middle of the cat, just through the skin. like a pocket open at both ends. her little arm went in, her little paw stuck out at the other end of the skin pocket. it was sort of positioned like superman, one arm back along side the body, one arm out normal in front.',0,308,151,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15151,2545,'2002-03-20 19:23:35',74,'jo','<a href=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/2002/jimmy_in_sink/01.jpg\\\"><img src=\\\"http://www.mmmbed.com/pictures/2002/jimmy_in_sink/01.jpg\\\" width=200></a>',1,161,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15152,2548,'2002-03-20 19:38:16',124,'gus','ooh I hope egg shows him my favorite thai ice cream bars. and I hope they go to a noodle soup cart. I miss my noodle soup cart lady SO MUCH.',0,140,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15153,2548,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yeah egg we totally dropped him off last night.  you should have him now, no?',3,77,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15154,2544,'2002-03-20 21:57:58',122,'hool','<p> it was long, yes, but still interesting.  i read it all, in one sitting, and i liked it a lot.  i like travel stories.</p>',0,126,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15155,2545,'2002-03-20 23:52:13',65,'le on bandwagon','<img src=\\\"http://still.hungry.com/~le/yoko.jpg\\\" height=\\\"240\\\" width=\\\"320\\\" alt=\\\"mew\\\">',4,107,0,0,'165.247.205.121','en'),(15156,2548,'2002-03-21 04:31:27',59,'s5','his car is in my garage, his ass is on the plane. all is well.',2,62,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15157,2545,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p><a href=\"http://randomcat.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://randomcat.com/img/cam.jpg\" height=\"240\" width=\"320\" border=\"1\" alt=\"cat royalty\"></p>\r\n\r\n<p>she has her own website.</a></p>',2,234,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15158,2545,'2002-03-21 05:06:16',66,'Peter','<br><a href=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/flw.jpg\">\r\n<img src=\"http://www.rhodamine.org/images/flw.jpg\"width=300 height=200>',1,135,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15159,2542,'2002-03-21 05:12:08',187,'kandrer','le, be careful not to think about this last phone call in a wrong way. i guess you regret, since you could have been there and see her a last time, and tell her this and that, and so on. that\\\'s exactly what my stepmother was saying: you can\\\'t help blaming on yourself. in her case she had fights with her father sometime before he died, if i remember well. and everyone can find his/her reason. maybe it\\\'s the only difference between alive people (when they are far away) and dead ones (anytime): you have chances to fix things up with alive ones. mmm... it seems i am gone again on my usual frozen-thought path. sorry. you know, well before my grandmother fell sick, i had already the habit of thinking that she might die soon. so i went to visit her, a few times more than i would \\\"normally\\\". and she was happy to see me, of course. and she used to say: <i>you know, it makes me happy to see you, you can come anytime</i>. and of course i was feeling guilty for not actually <i>comming anytime</i>.<br>\r\ni am bad at saying nice things, and i don\\\'t believe in spirits and everything, but in any way one can understand it, what is happening to you might be what happened to me: she is now taking a new place in your life, inside your head (or heart if you prefer). the fact that she\\\'s dead should not change so much your understanding of her, your relationship. for me i can say that i am closer from her now.<br>\r\n<i>wow</i> i am so surprised to be able to sound like that!... i just realized all this talking that i used to consider as bullshit since i am atheist can make sense anyway.<br>\r\nok, i just meant: feel in peace. :)',1,1650,65,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15160,2548,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','we miss you egg. i can\\\'t believe you took your entries, AND austin away from us. fooey.',1,90,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15161,2548,'2002-03-21 05:51:38',64,'the girl in \\\'Patriot Dames\\\'','Oh baby! Park it in my garage!',0,30,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15162,2548,'2002-03-21 06:06:04',17,'austin','fuck, i am in bangkok and i dont remember my flight out of here?  where am i supposed to be going.  this si fucking classic.',6,124,0,0,'202.47.239.57','en'),(15163,2553,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh god, how was this categorised as a tradgedy?  this deserves cult status.',1,75,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15164,2553,'2002-03-21 09:01:39',59,'s5','there is a factory that builds your coworkers. no one wanted animatronics anymore so they repurposed the assembly line. someone might scream but in the long run, no one would notice.',2,182,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15165,2550,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh sweetie.  come over and have a hot shower sometime.  shit, that is heartbreaking.',1,84,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15166,2553,'2002-03-21 12:29:10',66,'Peter','<br><tt>in space, no one can hear you scream...</tt>',1,52,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15167,2545,'2002-03-21 13:17:46',151,'stella','that\\\'s a very strange thing to do to a cat, or any animal for that matter. very strange and probably very painful. did she look funny hobbling around with her arm stuck to herself? the oddity of the whole thing probably would have had me snickering a little. ',0,262,124,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15168,2553,'2002-03-21 13:22:07',151,'stella','boy, I thought my job was bad!',0,30,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15169,2554,'2002-03-21 13:32:48',66,'Peter','<br><tt>im gonna be clothed in light; there aint gonna be any darkness within 50 feet of me.</tt>',1,97,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15170,2552,'2002-03-21 13:38:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i keep my car sparse and spartan inside; but, this is more out of necessity than personal prerogative, as keeping it this way prevents break-ins.  there are things in my trunk, but i forget what. someone tried to break into the trunk last august and managed to jam the lock/latch in the process, so the trunk is now permanently sealed shut, contents entombed for 7+ months; i like to think of the trunk as my own personal time-capsule.</tt>',1,386,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15171,2550,'2002-03-21 15:47:22',88,'deb','If I ever have my own dishwasher again, I\\\'ll let you do your dishes...',2,73,0,0,'64.158.0.128','en'),(15172,2553,'2002-03-21 16:33:59',122,'hool','<p>bathroom naps.  they are your friend. when i worked in cube farms, or really, at any job actually, i\\\'d go to the bathroom, sit in a stall, prop my head up with my hands, and sleep for as long as i could (or until i fell off the toilet).  when the bathrooms in some places were mercifully private, i\\\'d just lay on the floor.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>then i started working at hep high tech places that had futons.  at first i was a bit shy about using them, then, last summer, i became quite brash, and would sleep on the futon within clear view of everyone, hat over my face, shoes off, sometimes snoring.  you just don\\\'t care after a while.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in the golden days of high tech, i worked at a place that had two kegs in an isolated kitchen.  there were only about fifty employees, and we just moved into a gigantic warehouse sized building.  the kitchen was a long walk away from anyone.  so i\\\'d go up there, sit on one of the futons, and drink beer until i got drunk.  sometimes i\\\'d bring the beer back to my cube.  i don\\\'t think anyone noticed.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>being drunk at work is nice, although sometimes i didn\\\'t plan it too well, slugging back a few pints in a few minutes to make the 1.5 hour bus ride home more bearable.  being stuck on a bus with no stops for 10 to 20 minutes at a time doesn\\\'t jive with a bladder full of beer.  i remember one time coming to a major transit station, running off the bus, and out to a loose collection of trees.  it was night (working late), kind of rainy, and cold.   it was one of those relieving pee sessions where you feel compelled to shout and moan as thanks to sweet relief.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>in conclusion, i don\\\'t like work.</p>',0,1689,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15173,2553,'2002-03-21 16:46:16',66,'Peter','<br><tt>imagine what life would be like if you didnt have to do <i>anything</i> you didnt want to do... like work.</tt>',1,119,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15174,2553,'2002-03-21 16:53:39',176,'Scapegoat','I am terrified of cube farms. terrified. I break out in cold sweats and start to tremble.',0,89,0,0,'64.229.163.243','en'),(15175,2553,'2002-03-21 18:26:04',124,'gus','ah! the high tech futon days... I was so underpaid in the beginning that no one would dare bother to wake me up. And frankly, they were all too busy smoking pot on the roof to notice much.\r\n<p>\r\nI\\\'ve almost always been stuck in the open cubeless environment. I used to dream about having a cube, just a little bit of imagined privacy. \r\n',0,340,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15176,2556,'2002-03-21 18:28:08',124,'gus','lotus is my favorite.',0,21,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15177,2552,'2002-03-21 19:21:31',74,'jo','I LOVE LISTS. rock on, laney, this was a great swintry.',0,55,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15178,2553,'2002-03-21 19:31:04',123,'dcc','at one place i was at they installed a nice new, soft couch in the office. we didn\\\'t have cubicles and the couch ended up next to my desk. that 3pm nap really helped out.',1,173,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15179,2556,'2002-03-21 19:33:18',123,'dcc','i am amazed at the beauty of this story but wish i didn\\\'t find it so.',0,72,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15180,2552,'2002-03-21 23:21:12',69,'starlajo','i love inventories!<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\nI amuse Thomas when we go out because I always take so much with me: a sweater, in case i get cold, passport for id incase i feel like having a drink, eye drops, glasses if i am wearing my contacts and fear they will irritate me, eyedrops...\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI love knowing what other people carry.  I don\\\'t drive, but if I did, I suspect my car would be full of things like that too.',2,412,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15181,2545,'2002-03-21 23:24:34',69,'starlajo','I love Chelsea. I miss Chelsea. I want to live with Chelsea.\r\n<BR>\r\n<BR>\r\n<IMG SRC=\\\"http://www.starla.co.nz/lj/chelseacat.jpg\\\">',2,133,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15182,2552,'2002-03-21 23:47:05',168,'laney','thanks for nice comments! in further surveying tricky\\\'s innards today i found two more gems that could only be classified under entertainment:</p>\r\n* unopened package of glow-in-the-dark body paint<br>\r\n* one roll transparent packing tape<br>\r\nhmmmmm....</p>\r\n',0,263,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15183,2553,'2002-03-21 23:54:44',195,'matt','after a while, it\\\'s just as boring as working.',1,49,66,0,'209.208.9.239','en'),(15184,2556,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','mine too.',1,9,124,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15185,2553,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i read that as \\\"gigantic whorehouse sized building\\\".',1,58,122,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15186,2553,'2002-03-22 00:51:33',122,'hool','<p>if it were in fact a whorehouse, the futons would make even more sense.  and i\\\'d probably sleep a lot less on them, unless i layed down a buffer of napkins or toilet paper.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>mmmm, napkins or toilet paper.</p>',0,223,62,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15187,2553,'2002-03-22 01:08:45',129,'moebius','i have sometimes thought, in my more lunatic moments, that cubicle workdays would be eaiser if everyone could break out into syncopated song and dance numbers every now and then.<br><br>',0,186,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15188,2553,'2002-03-22 01:13:45',129,'moebius','back in the hightech boom days a software company i worked for allowed drinking during work hours (or at least didn\\\'t explicitly forbid it). the hardcore \\\"tools\\\" developers on the top floor kept beer instead of sodapop in the break room fridge. and some people even went so far as to brew their own beer in their offices.<br><br>but then something happened: a woman at the new jersey office left a company holiday party, got in her car, started it up, and proceded to drive home. drunk off her ass. and somewhere from point a to b, she plowed through a family of cyclists, killing a few of them.<br><br>needless to say, that was it for the drinking at work.',0,666,122,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15189,2556,'2002-03-22 01:17:24',129,'moebius','breathtaking. literally.',0,24,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15190,2552,'2002-03-22 03:28:26',218,'da','glow in the dark bodypaint.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n!!!<br>\r\n!!!<br>\r\n!!!',1,62,0,0,'209.86.221.62','en'),(15191,2556,'2002-03-22 04:33:44',122,'hool','<p>sh: the end of this made me think of the virgin suicides, summer, and the first house i lived in when my parents were still together.  i remember arguments.  i remember quiet.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>my parents argued away from us.  my dad always sounded so desperate.  i think it formed the way i think of him, and the reason that i distanced myself from him when i was younger (and still now).  i remember sitting at the top of shag carpet stairs, warm, summer evening heat, deep wind, listening to my parents argue.  i was very young.  i felt sad and voyeuristic at the same time.  i knew something was wrong.  like the way you know it\\\'s going to rain when you smell that asphalt/ metallic smell rise out of the ground.  your story made that happen to me.</p>',0,754,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15192,2559,'2002-03-22 12:07:13',65,'le','this is great. \\\"for the love of garfield\\\"',4,47,0,0,'165.247.222.62','en'),(15193,2557,'2002-03-22 14:09:44',183,'lin','<p>Glad to her things are looking up. It\\\'s been a while since I\\\'ve heard positive things from you, I hope it continues to go well.</p>',0,140,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(15194,2559,'2002-03-22 15:11:01',239,'Poonlarp','<br>seafood smells are good, indeed,<br>\r\nseafood tastes are what i need<br>\r\navast ye mateys, cast off to sea<br>\r\nand bring back a juicy tuna for me.\r\n',1,153,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15195,2559,'2002-03-22 15:16:14',123,'dcc','hmm, yes, seafood would be good. i think it\\\'s sushi time.',0,60,0,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15196,2559,'2002-03-22 18:23:16',83,'adamajah','<p>I only read articles that are written by cats.<br>\r\n :)\r\n</p>',1,64,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15197,2560,'2002-03-22 19:12:03',239,'Poonlarp','this is not nebulous.',1,174,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15198,2560,'2002-03-22 19:13:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>but would your tuna be <i>dolphin-safe</i>? you had to know it would be inevitable that someone would ask this, so i thought i might as well be the one.</tt',1,164,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15199,2550,'2002-03-22 19:13:54',124,'gus','Ive had just about enough hotwater for things these last couple days. so things are going much better!',0,102,0,0,'64.160.55.38','en'),(15200,2556,'2002-03-22 20:42:47',156,'lotus','i\\\'m shameless. i read all five of these comments out to my precious boy, and he said \\\"wow. no wonder you are so uncharacteristically happy today.\\\" he\\\'s right. i haven\\\'t stopped giving random spontaneous smiles since i read them this morning. <br><br>\r\nthank you so much for reading, and even more for making me glad i posted this.',0,345,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15201,2556,'2002-03-22 20:45:27',156,'lotus','*you* write incredibly well. i\\\'ve been reading (and in some cases rereading) your archived pieces and am jealous. and content. < / thank you note >',1,150,124,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15202,2556,'2002-03-22 20:50:39',156,'lotus','dear dcc, your response made me so...so... <br><br>several months ago, i read a book review where the person said \\\'this is sad and beautiful and true\\\'. since then, when i revise a piece, that\\\'s what is at the back of my head. what i want most in the world, is for someone to read something i write, and think it is sad, beautiful and true.',0,348,123,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15203,2556,'2002-03-22 20:51:42',156,'lotus','oh you are so kind! you have been so encouraging i really want to write something you will love. it is the best help in facing a blank screen.',0,142,62,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15204,2556,'2002-03-22 20:54:36',156,'lotus','i went back and reread all of your articles. <br><br>\r\nyou write beautifully, and transform the quotidian into the marvellous.',0,126,129,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15205,2556,'2002-03-22 21:00:15',156,'lotus','i don\\\'t remember my parents ever fighting before the age of ten. looking back i realise they were probably just hiding it from us. and weren\\\'t as successful as we got older. now they don\\\'t even bother to pretend that they don\\\'t make each other miserable. last year, abbu told me \\\'marrying your mother was the biggest mistake i ever made in my entire life\\\' <br><br>\r\ni really want to read the virgin suicides. it is high up on my book list. <br><br>\r\nyet another thing i love about ottawa is our summer thunderstorms. with the rain just drenching everything. i get out and just soak in it. revel in it. <br><br>\r\ni\\\'ve been reading your comments, and you write some of the best ones on here. you really *listen* to what is being said, and respond to it thoughtfully and honestly. *hug*',0,804,122,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15206,2538,'2002-03-22 21:03:09',156,'lotus','i read this, and some of your other articles, and it didn\\\'t feel like i was reading anymore, it felt like i was right there, *experiencing* and it was so interesting and vivid and real. and so i am living vicariously through you.',0,232,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15207,2540,'2002-03-22 21:05:19',156,'lotus','i am so glad i was introduced to swinney. travelogues, rambles, poems, rants, opinion pieces, all in one place. this made me think about lots of stuff i can\\\'t quite articulate yet.',0,183,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15208,2557,'2002-03-22 21:09:23',156,'lotus','this is fantastic. i am in awe of people who can write about apparently ordinary things in a way that makes you want the piece to not to stop. eurgh. i didn\\\'t put that very well, but hopefully you know what i am trying to say. i\\\'m glad you feel better.',0,258,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15209,2559,'2002-03-22 21:11:34',156,'lotus','wow. this is wickedly good and imaginative and was exhilirating to read. wooh.',0,78,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15210,2553,'2002-03-22 21:14:52',156,'lotus','thanks for the article. it\\\'s become a shorthand reference for how work is going. when hool asked me on icq, i said, \\\'its okay. i\\\'m not at the kandrer stage. yet.\\\'',0,174,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15211,2560,'2002-03-22 21:15:54',156,'lotus','mmm. seafood. mmmmm. PMd seafood.',0,33,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15212,2552,'2002-03-22 21:17:36',156,'lotus','i love reading other people\\\'s lists. this post reminded me of my sister\\\'s car, where there\\\'s barely any room for passengers among the books, magazines, chocolate wrappers, sneakers, gym clothes, orange plastic cones...',0,227,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15213,2550,'2002-03-22 21:19:41',156,'lotus','i want to write as well as you do.',0,34,124,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15214,2551,'2002-03-22 21:21:21',156,'lotus','okay. i just realised why my poems rarely get comments on here. what do you say in response to a poem? you don\\\'t just want to say something pathetic like \\\'this is good\\\'. but, it is friday afternoon and i am braindead. i will end by saying i have marked this to read again. thank you for posting it.',0,307,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15215,2560,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','are you a suicide girl by any chance?',3,37,239,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15216,2560,'2002-03-22 23:12:49',239,'Poonlarp','no',6,2,62,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15217,2550,'2002-03-22 23:13:45',124,'gus','I like your writing! It\\\'s good!',0,34,156,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15218,2551,'2002-03-23 00:03:34',195,'matt','thank you for commenting on it.',1,31,0,0,'209.208.9.82','en'),(15219,2553,'2002-03-23 00:16:17',148,'jeeff','at my old cubefarm, one day everyone broke out whistling the theme to the andy griffith show.  the entire floor.  about 50 people.  it was good.',0,144,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(15220,2552,'2002-03-23 00:19:23',148,'jeeff','there\\\'s a car permanently parked on the street near the grocery store that is packed to the roof with magazines, newspapers and other general stackable things.  the only open space in the entire thing is the driver\\\'s seat.',0,228,0,0,'24.156.231.200','en'),(15221,2553,'2002-03-23 00:28:39',124,'gus','at one old job, the new bosses decided that my entire dept should march through the office playing kazoo. horrible torture. I hate kazoos with all of my being!',0,159,0,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15222,2551,'2002-03-23 00:35:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>x marks the spot.</tt>',1,30,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15223,2559,'2002-03-23 01:25:55',168,'laney','this mentality of a cat reminded me of the hilarious interactions i witnessed with my friends\\\' three kitties last night. if you have more than one cat and want some good solid entertainment, get yourself a \\\"turbo-scratcher\\\" (avail. at petco for a mere $14.95) and some catnip. oh, is it funny.',0,302,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15224,2562,'2002-03-23 02:46:23',130,'treetop','hi gus:\r\n\r\n<p>I like travel stories.  I also like spelunking.  I\\\'ve been through several medium sized caves in the south east.  One we actually camped out in.  It was called Key Cave and is along the Tennessee river close to Muscle Shoals.  Key Cave is large and has several passages that go on and on and on into a fatman squeeze that keeps getting smaller and smaller.  Do you know of any caves in the Bay area?  I\\\'d like to break out my headlamp again.</p>',2,465,0,0,'172.157.70.74','en'),(15225,2562,'2002-03-23 02:57:14',124,'gus','ive only seen real small little cavelets in the bay area. I dont know if there are real caves around. You might need to go to the pinnicles or to some other place in the southern half of the state.',0,197,0,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15226,2559,'2002-03-23 04:08:41',128,'Javorai','cat-astrophe',0,12,0,0,'129.130.229.215','en'),(15227,2554,'2002-03-23 04:22:48',128,'Javorai','good attitude',0,13,0,0,'129.130.229.215','en'),(15228,2560,'2002-03-23 10:02:13',65,'le','i\\\'m glad poonlarp had the foresight to check \\\"cult\\\" on this.',4,69,0,0,'209.86.4.102','en'),(15229,1838,'2002-03-23 13:01:13',168,'laney','i\\\'ve been sick-ish for the last two weeks. and it\\\'s true. it really is *tripping* on nyquil. i came home early from work the other day and took a giant swig of it. phone kept ringing, even though no one EVER calls me. my brother had to write me email to make fun of how delerious i was.',0,292,0,0,'165.121.125.222','en'),(15230,2561,'2002-03-23 16:26:36',74,'jo','\\\"blast sigur ros\\\"<BR>\r\nahahahhaa.<BR>\r\ni pictured one of your neighbors screaming at you: \\\"turn down that infernal rock music!\\\"<BR>\r\n(p.s. i really love articles where people describe their homes and their home life.)',0,229,0,0,'172.157.220.225','en'),(15231,2564,'2002-03-23 17:59:43',65,'le','<br><font color=\"red\">\r\n<b>\r\nWHEELIES WERE POPPED<BR>\r\nSWEAT WAS DRIPPING<BR>\r\nBROKEN CONCRETE HUMAN GRATER<BR>\r\nSUPPLY BASE BEHIND ENEMY LINES<BR>\r\nJAWS<BR>\r\nDEEP PERSONAL LEVEL<BR>\r\nJAWS<BR>\r\nCHEWING ME UP<BR>\r\nSCREAM MERCY<BR>\r\nand fall back into a dream.\r\n</b>\r\n</font>\r\n<br><br>\r\non another note, i wish i could check everything between best friendly and burn burn. just sometimes.\r\n',6,392,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15232,521,'2002-03-23 19:07:03',65,'le','i am posting this comment a little over a year to the day of this debacle.',6,74,0,0,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15233,2561,'2002-03-23 19:42:11',210,'may','actually, more like the basketball players who live next door turning up \\\"you\\\'s a ho\\\" even louder.',0,107,0,0,'128.253.117.237','en'),(15234,521,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i want a world map with a directory of swinneyites in each city. so i can see how far away potential fuck buddies are. you know.',1,128,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15235,2560,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','poonlaaarrrrrp we llooooovve yoouuuu',1,36,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15236,2564,'2002-03-23 22:31:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>Swinney is going for the gold</i></tt>',1,49,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15237,521,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','HI TITS.<br>\r\n<br>\r\non st patricks day, i did indeed think of that night.',4,73,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15238,2560,'2002-03-23 23:06:05',59,'s5','*shrug*',4,7,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15239,2564,'2002-03-23 23:53:48',124,'gus','Now, do it during monsoon season in the midst of a treacherous thunderstorm. Also, get back to me after you ride your bike through 2 feet of standing water, turning it into 2 feet of standing mud for your return trip! (Egg, you just lift your legs up and put them around his waist and you\\\'ll come out of it sparkly clean... unless he falls over).<br>\r\nAlso... hi! Don\\\'t forget to eat the fish with three flavors sauce, a nice pomfret should be good. MMM MMM MMM. do it now! in the south!',0,493,0,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15240,2564,'2002-03-24 01:20:29',66,'Peter','<br><tt>two words: <a href=\"http://www.ecst.csuchico.edu/~durian/\"><b>durian fruit</b></a></tt>',1,43,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15241,2564,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i really really want some durian now. thanks, peter!@',1,53,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15242,2564,'2002-03-24 17:16:52',180,'auntie','if you had ASKED me first, i would have told you that mom does NOT allow motorcycling to the unknown without paramedics in tow.',4,127,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(15243,2564,'2002-03-24 20:33:48',124,'gus','no way, no durian. no jackfruit, no breadfruit. no! what you want are MANGOSTEENS! eat\\\'em up yum.',0,100,0,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15244,2564,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','durian meat look like brains to me.',1,31,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15245,2568,'2002-03-25 06:24:45',195,'matt','also:<BR><BR>spanakopita<BR>boy love<BR>city-states<BR>and melina mercouri',1,74,0,0,'209.208.9.200','en'),(15246,2568,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i thank god for the greeks, daily.  sometimes, hourly :D:D:D:D!',1,63,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15247,2568,'2002-03-25 12:24:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>\r\n<UL TYPE=\\\"square\\\">\r\n<LI>classic literature\r\n<LI>tzatziki sauce\r\n<li>ouzo\r\n<li>architectural elements\r\n<li>spanakopita\r\n<li><a href=\\\"http://www.oeaw.ac.at/kal/agm/\\\">ancient music</a>\r\n</UL></tt>',1,215,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15248,2348,'2002-03-25 13:47:20',64,'Joanna','ahhh, this is the article I was looking for.  I finally got my tshirt that I\\\'d lent to the object of my affections back from him, and since he mentioned that he\\\'d worn it again, as soon as I got home I buried my face in it.  Mmmmmmmmm boy.  Unfortunately, the way things are between us now, I think his scent in my tshirt will be all I\\\'ll have left, so maybe I\\\'ll have to ration out my huffings.   ',1,410,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15249,2570,'2002-03-25 15:10:21',64,'Joanna','You always write really interesting things Peter.  Thank you!',1,61,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15250,2570,'2002-03-25 19:23:02',66,'Peter','<br><tt>no, thank <i>you</i>... for reading them.</tt>',1,54,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15251,2568,'2002-03-25 22:06:32',124,'gus','<p> dont forget:</p>\r\n\r\nthe theater<br>\r\nbacchic frenzy<br>\r\nand<br>\r\nthe naming scheme for most of freuds theories.',0,116,0,0,'64.172.57.187','en'),(15252,2568,'2002-03-25 22:23:26',187,'kandrer','- this Greek guy sitting at the cubicle in the corner who drives me crazy just by smiling at me all the time\r\n- paranoia: madness, from <i>paranoos</i>, demented - <i>pars</i>, beyond + <i>noos</i>, mind. (etymology from <a href=\\\"http://ablemedia.com/ctcweb/showcase/roots.html\\\">Roots of English</a>)',1,306,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15253,2556,'2002-03-25 23:06:05',187,'kandrer','<p><i>\r\nAnd don\\\'t tell me how to make it pay,<br>\r\nI write a new song every day <br>\r\n</i></p>\r\n<p>\r\nthere is something i feel i should say here, and i can\\\'t. sad beautiful and true things are nice to read about or see in movies. there is a price you pay to <i>produce</i> such things, though...<br>\r\nmmm... once again i lost my point. i\\\'ll try again later.\r\n</p>\r\n',1,374,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15254,2568,'2002-03-25 23:26:42',129,'moebius','<br>\r\nand didn\\\'t the frisbee basically evolve from the discus?<br><br>oh nevermind me, i just wanted to add something.',1,121,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15255,2568,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','s5, this needs to be one of them new-fangled series articles.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ni mean, you\\\'re more than just greek right?',1,118,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15256,2568,'2002-03-25 23:49:08',59,'s5','<p>\r\nhm!\r\n</p><p>\r\nwell, i just created a series called \\\"I Am [Ethnicity]\\\". if anyone wants to write an article about their ethnic background or a country they grew up in, i\\\'d be happy to add it to the series.\r\n</p><p>\r\nmy other 50% is italian. check out my nose sometime.\r\n</p>',1,287,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15257,2569,'2002-03-26 02:55:49',183,'ali','i miss you',1,10,0,0,'24.42.108.124','en'),(15258,2569,'2002-03-26 03:18:27',64,'Joanna','Ahh, essay procrastination, that\\\'s what I\\\'m doing rightnow. And it ties in nicely with you mentioning Starbucks, since my topic is : \\\"Discuss how an understanding of the dimensions of branding should enhance the effective intergration of marketing communication in an organisation\\\". \r\n\r\nAnyone care to write it for me?',0,330,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15259,2568,'2002-03-26 06:08:11',89,'sawks','i wish i knew what i was!',0,25,0,0,'203.10.110.133','en'),(15260,2348,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','premenstrual syndrome == fuck me syndrome. i always figure this out a too late each month.',1,90,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15261,2572,'2002-03-26 07:07:34',124,'gus','ok! i actually read this all in one go! I was having fun reminising about poipet, the bordertown just inside the cambodian border from thailand, we also had some trouble with our arranged ride. I think it\\\'s standard in border towns... <p> There are many countries that prefer to use the dollar. I didnt see any signs accepting euros in asia, but the dollar is everywhere. I\\\'ve also read that somewhere around 65% of US paper currency is in circulation OUTSIDE of the US. several countries, Ecuador is one, have abandoned their own currency in favor of the dollar. and in wealthier countries, many still keep their savings by purchasing US dollars for their bank accounts. I\\\'d never really thought about it, but now I have a better understanding of the knowing nod you get anytime you say you\\\'re from \\\"Amerika\\\".',0,828,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15262,2569,'2002-03-26 07:19:03',124,'gus','Im just going to say here that Im glad my two majors were theater and dance. I never had to discuss branding or marketing communication unless I was mocking it in some sort of movement interpretation fiasco. hehehe.',4,215,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15263,2568,'2002-03-26 07:30:09',99,'wb','you forgot man-boy love.',0,24,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(15264,2568,'2002-03-26 07:30:45',99,'wb','oh excuse me, i notice somebody added \\\"boy love\\\" which i guess the survey will accept as a correct answer.',0,112,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(15265,2570,'2002-03-26 07:33:01',99,'wb','good post. god i fucking love the subway in that city.',0,54,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(15266,2564,'2002-03-26 07:42:10',99,'wb','funny i just tried durian for the first time last week. it does look like brains. also something from the Aliens franchise but light colored.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt DOES sort of smell like what mango, kiwi and papaya would smell like if you made a fruit cocktail of those fruits and puked it back up.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nIt didn\\\'t really move me either way. I wasn\\\'t grossed out and I certainly didn\\\'t like it that much. \r\n<BR><BR>\r\nThe friends who brought it had tried it and after my two bites I was charged with its disposal.\r\n<BR><BR>\r\nI\\\'m looking at the web link and I can imagine it being a good thing in recipes, cookies, cakes, maybe in ice cream, the durian flavor tempered with cream and sugar.',0,698,0,0,'216.103.215.241','en'),(15267,2514,'2002-03-26 09:55:03',231,'alana','<p>i love cats.</P> \r\n<p>i have a grey cat.</p> \r\n<p>she is very grey.</p>\r\n<br>\r\ni am sorry your cat had to leave.\r\n',3,117,0,0,'203.212.151.31','en'),(15268,2569,'2002-03-26 10:50:18',64,'Joanna','My major was Multimedia, which I did at the end of my Bachelor of Communication Studies, so I got to do stupid little Flash things instead of write essays for a year.  But then I hated the job I got out of it and now I\\\'m doing a graduate diploma in Public Relations.  Some people might say that this means I have no soul, but I disagree.\r\n\r\nStill, I kinda wish that I could have pursued theatre or something like that beyond high school, but if the truth was to be told, I don\\\'t really have a passion for it.  ',1,516,124,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15269,2569,'2002-03-26 12:05:24',176,'Scapegoat','<b>Jesus Christ Vampire Hunter</b> strikes again, I\\\'m glad to hear that you got out to see this one Fuzzy. I have the poster up on my wall. I haven\\\'t been out to see a movie in a while I\\\'ve been limited to watching Baise-moi, Doctor Zhivago, and Sgt. Kabuke NYPD over and over again. I have my eye out and hope to go and check out 101Reykjavik, wish I had the proper phone number for you I\\\'d see if you want to join.<br><br>\r\nI never made forts but I used to dress up to write essays when I was in school. Different costumes depending on the topic, I used to get a lot of visitors when I lived in rez if people knew I was writing something.<br><br>\r\n    ',0,666,0,0,'64.229.163.20','en'),(15270,2564,'2002-03-26 12:27:48',86,'egg','actually, the viewpoint we were headed towards was called somethign like \\\"Durian Peak\\\". there was this secluded durian grove on the way up. we stopped there and climbed a nice boulder. he never puts in the calm, peaceful stuff,. ',0,235,0,0,'203.147.49.250','en'),(15271,2570,'2002-03-26 12:28:04',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i was watching <a href=\\\"http://us.imdb.com/Title?0150230\\\">the cruise</a> this weekend, a documentary about a brilliant eccentric who expresses his city-love by leading oddly metaphysical tours on those otherwise awful nyc double-decker bus tours. i found myself musing about how nice it would be to get paid to just ride the subways, experience them, write about them... to generally be an observer to the odd phenomenon which is the nyc subway system.</tt>',1,471,99,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15272,2570,'2002-03-26 12:35:08',66,'Peter','<br><img src=\\\"http://www.nycsubway.org/slides/redscrap/redscrap01.jpg\\\" height=\\\"300\\\">',1,96,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15273,2570,'2002-03-26 12:36:15',66,'Peter','<br><tt>er, <a href=\\\"http://www.nycsubway.org/cars/redbird-scrap.html#reef\\\">this</a> is also oddly interesting...</tt>',1,124,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15274,2564,'2002-03-26 12:38:51',66,'Peter','<br><tt>supposedly, scores of people die every year from falling durian fruit. <i>be careful</i>, heh...</tt>',1,109,86,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15275,1398,'2002-03-26 13:40:46',65,'le','',6,62,0,1,'216.27.183.161','en'),(15276,2564,'2002-03-26 15:10:53',151,'stella','larb is yummy',0,13,0,0,'168.73.245.56','en'),(15277,2569,'2002-03-26 15:15:19',75,'tyler','i\\\'m in the same boat. i have a paper due in two days comparing the sonnet writing styles of Shakespeare and Edmund Spenser. whoopee.',1,135,0,0,'165.247.164.18','en'),(15278,2566,'2002-03-26 15:24:07',195,'matt','after reading this again, i\\\'ve decided it\\\'s kinda sub-par.',1,64,0,0,'209.208.9.203','en'),(15279,2566,'2002-03-26 15:34:55',239,'Poonlarp','i liked it. im a sucka\\\' for prose, though.',1,45,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15280,2564,'2002-03-26 15:35:27',239,'Poonlarp','larp is yummy',6,13,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15281,2572,'2002-03-26 15:38:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>when i was living in ecuador, the dollar could buy a <i>lot</i> more than its equivalent in sucre could; and i dont mean just exchange-wise. one american dollar bill was worth more than the ecuadorian equivalent of ten us dollars. i was able to survuve for months on about $300us, which was still living quite large considering that the average ecuadorian laborer makes less than the equivalent of about $1000us a year. i remember buying an item that was priced at 60,000 sucre (about 20 some odd us dollars, then) for two american one dollar bills.</tt>',1,287,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15282,2569,'2002-03-26 16:02:03',123,'dcc','101 Reykjavik is playing just over that the Carlton Cinema\\\'s here in Toronto. Hmm, i think i should go see it very very soon.',0,128,176,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15283,2572,'2002-03-26 16:19:07',122,'hool','once again, i liked this story.  you speak of taking photos.  can you post them here along with your stories? ',0,110,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15284,2568,'2002-03-26 16:47:34',93,'chiamattt','political science? source? ',6,27,0,0,'24.42.194.20','en'),(15285,2568,'2002-03-26 16:52:43',66,'Peter','<br><tt>democracy; republic.</tt>',1,33,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15286,2569,'2002-03-26 18:53:40',124,'gus','see I\\\'d compare shakespeare and spenser over branding communication techniques anyday. or actually, I\\\'d prefer marlowe or webster to spenser, but hey.',0,156,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15287,2569,'2002-03-26 19:02:43',82,'philip marlowe','\\\"and i don\\\'t mind if you don\\\'t like my manners, i don\\\'t like them myself. i grieve over them on long winter evenings\\\"',1,132,124,0,'198.136.186.46','en'),(15288,2570,'2002-03-26 20:58:36',74,'jo','SPEED LEVITCH! speed levitch rocks, peter. i wish i could talk like him.',0,72,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15289,2569,'2002-03-26 21:03:15',124,'christopher marlowe','Not you dummy, me.',0,18,82,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15290,2570,'2002-03-26 21:04:13',66,'Peter','<br><tt>speed levitch is amazingly brilliant. my goal is to find him here in nyc and say hi.</tt>',1,97,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15291,2570,'2002-03-26 21:29:39',129,'moebius','didn\\\'t speed levitch turn up in a sequence of <i>Waking Life</i>?<br><br>i coulda sworn that was him on the bridge, saying \\\"On really romantic nights of self, I go salsa dancing with my confusion.\\\"',0,206,74,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15292,2569,'2002-03-26 21:55:38',69,'starlajo','you _have_ to see 101 reyjavik.\r\n\r\n',3,35,123,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15293,2569,'2002-03-26 21:59:44',69,'starlajo','reykjavik, damnit.\r\n\r\nmust type more carefully.',3,47,0,0,'210.54.242.102','en'),(15294,2570,'2002-03-26 22:45:27',74,'jo','absolutely. that was him.<BR>\r\n\\\"beware. and. beware. AND. beware.\\\"<BR>\r\nBOUNCE BOUNCE! speed levitch!<BR>\r\nplease <a href=\\\"http://www.wakinglifemovie.com/pop_clip3.html\\\">go watch! go watch!</a>',0,205,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15295,2573,'2002-03-26 23:47:30',154,'ryan','<p>\r\nwhen my grandmother died a few years ago we had a small service that although was christian in denomination, at the time it struck me as quite buddhist.\r\n<p>\r\nas the ceremony closed, and people had said what they needed to say, the band played \\\'take me out to the ballgame\\\'.\r\n<p>\r\nshe loved baseball, especially the red sox.\r\n<p>\r\ni then retreated to my mountain and sat for a bit.\r\n<p>\r\nsorry.',0,405,0,0,'63.180.14.34','en'),(15296,2570,'2002-03-27 00:51:55',66,'Peter','<br><tt><i>\\\"id rather participate in life than write a thousand stories\\\"</i>. nice. speed levitch, i love you almost as much as you love the brooklyn bridge.</tt>',1,168,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15297,2571,'2002-03-27 01:08:27',195,'matt','this is pretty and lyrical. geese are the totem animal for capricorns.',1,70,0,0,'209.208.79.145','en'),(15298,2569,'2002-03-27 01:30:03',195,'matt','there was an article in salon a month or so ago about how it\\\'s widely believed that marlowe wrote all the plays attributed to shakespeare. willie was apparently just the front man. marlowe was also a british spy, is thought to have translated <U>don quixote</U> into english, and was in on compositing the king james version of the bible.',1,341,0,0,'209.208.77.183','en'),(15299,2569,'2002-03-27 02:13:26',124,'gus','there are just way too many theories about who shakespeare might have been, and I dont believe any of them yet. I even saw a play recently at A.C.T. downtown called The Beard of Avon that suggested, oh crap I forget his name and I threw out the program, Lord somebody important wrote the plays. it was a cute play. \r\n<p>\r\nMarlowe gets my affection for Edward the Second. ',0,371,195,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15300,2570,'2002-03-27 02:18:55',124,'gus','dear quicktime, <br>\r\nwhy do you hate me?<br>\r\nLove gus',0,55,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15301,2569,'2002-03-27 05:01:02',176,'Scapegoat','Just saw 101Reykjavik made me wish I was in Iceland. I spent a day in Reykjavik one summer on a lay over to germany when I was about 15. ',0,137,0,0,'64.229.163.20','en'),(15302,2573,'2002-03-27 05:30:22',180,'auntie','two weeks ago today my mark\\\'s mother died, after a long battle with breast cancer.<br><br>\r\n\r\nwe were all relieved on some level.  but yesterday i went shopping for the easter basket stuff for the kids, and when i do that i always buy card for the parents.  i chose a \\\"dad\\\" card for mark\\\'s father, and couldn\\\'t find one for mine, so i moved over to the \\\"mother\\\" cards.  i chose one for my mom, and then i started looking for one mark\\\'s mother might like.<br><br>\r\n\r\nsuddenly i felt like i might cry in the hallmark shop. i haven\\\'t cried for her at all, i didn\\\'t expect that i ever would, as we were not close, although i loved her as you do anyone in your family, and she loved me, i never knew who she really was.  i still don\\\'t, but i really hate only buying one mom card.<br><br>',1,815,0,0,'24.162.119.106','en'),(15303,2568,'2002-03-27 15:22:00',148,'jeeff','chest hair!',0,11,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15304,2569,'2002-03-27 15:38:51',123,'dcc','i have long wished to go to iceland. to see it\\\'s ethereal beauty.\r\n<p>\r\nto have the chance to possibly see things like <a href=\\\"http://antwrp.gsfc.nasa.gov/apod/ap020121.html\\\">this</a>.\r\n<p>fine, that was 11 years ago, but the fact that it happened there is just so spectacular. the last time hekla errupted was 2 years ago, maybe it will happen again.\r\n<p>i hope to go there when it does again, and maybe see something this amazing.',0,442,176,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15305,2569,'2002-03-27 16:28:04',176,'Scapegoat','That looks amazing. I really want to emigrate to one of the Scandinavian countries to tell you the truth. Iceland, Norway, Sweden, and Finland are probably the countries that I want to travel to the most. Next would be Russia and the Baltic States. ',0,249,123,0,'64.229.161.246','en'),(15306,2569,'2002-03-27 18:13:20',74,'jo','lots of poets and scholars worked on \\\"translating\\\" the king james bible. shakespeare, in one of the psalms he translated, made the 16th word \\\"shake\\\" and the 32nd word \\\"spear.\\\"<BR>\r\nalso, i\\\'m with gus. they have been researching shakespeare\\\'s authorship for hundreds of years: there is nothing conclusive one way or another; it is likely that not every word attributed to him was actually written by him but by someone else in his \\\"style.\\\" <BR>\r\nthe same can be said for the bible, while we\\\'re on the topic. only 7 of the 13 letters of the new testament that are attributed to paul were written by him. we know that for a <b>fact</b>. they\\\'re still called the paulian letters, however, because they were written in his name. point being that even if marlowe wrote every fucking word, he <i>wanted</i> shakespeare to have the credit. maybe that\\\'s the more interesting story.',0,911,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15307,2569,'2002-03-27 18:15:41',74,'jo','jesus i\\\'m such a fucking english major.',0,42,0,0,'216.141.109.62','en'),(15308,2569,'2002-03-27 19:04:54',123,'dcc','it must be my ancestral blood calling me back to the northern countries. my family is from the baltics, from the coastal areas. not so far off either, i am first generation born in canada.\r\n<p>in the summer sometimes a few days around the summer solstice it doesn\\\'t get completely dark. well, that\\\'s what my grandmother told me.',0,334,176,0,'204.92.73.229','en'),(15309,2569,'2002-03-27 19:54:17',66,'Peter','<br><tt>on days like this, i regret being a biochem major. ask me about metabolic precursors or trans-synaptical receptor mediated processes and im all good. ask me about shakespeare and all you get is a big <i>duuuh?</i> heh. so go, you english major, you!</tt>',1,231,74,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15310,2569,'2002-03-27 20:12:01',129,'moebius','<br>i love it when swinney people become momentarily professorial and academic. glimpses of scholarly depth beneath the facade of flippancy.<br><br>also: i was a lit major too, but my academic focus was on creative writing. at the time it was a blast but now i deeply regret not taking the comparative lit track instead. i\\\'m surrounded by comparative lit prodigies in my current profession and i would kill to speak as authoritatively on lit as they do.<br><br>peter, when you come out here, you and my girlfriend can talk about trans-synaptical receptor mediated processes and such until the cows come home. or not. :D',1,622,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15311,2569,'2002-03-27 20:30:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>the cows couldnt come home without trans-synaptical receptor mediated processes, hehehe. sorry, couldnt resist. god, im a dork.</tt>',1,140,129,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15312,2569,'2002-03-27 20:31:47',239,'Poonlarp','i get the idea that you swinney fans are quite smart. ',1,54,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15313,2483,'2002-03-28 01:01:52',88,'deb','I hadn\\\'t seen this post before. It\\\'s scary to find out that someone has died, no matter what the cause. Even worse to find out that they wanted to...',4,155,0,0,'198.139.250.74','en'),(15314,1949,'2002-03-28 02:16:01',122,'hool','so i\\\'m reading this much longer after it was written and commented upon.  but i liked it, felt it, know it.',0,110,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15315,2569,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i for one, am merely an unemployed pleb highschool dropout, and couldn\\\'t give a fuck about english, biotechnology or branding.  i sure love laying around sucking my thumb though.',4,181,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15316,2568,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','mmm chest hair',3,14,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15317,2568,'2002-03-28 06:27:08',70,'Fuzzy','My Operating Systems prof is Greek.  He he screams a lot and hast chest hair bursting out the top of his button-up shirts.  When he becomes excited his face becomes red and he flails his limbs.  He screams at me because I have too much memory in my system.  I like him best.',1,274,0,0,'24.153.1.179','en'),(15318,2569,'2002-03-28 07:19:15',124,'gus','I coulda been smart, but I never applied myself!',0,48,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15319,2568,'2002-03-28 09:02:37',175,'Paul G','<b>City-states</b> existed in the Ancient Near East at least, oh, 2,000 years before they did in Greece.  And <b>republic</b> is a Latin word not a Greek word.  The Latin word respublica is translated something like \\\"affairs of the public\\\" - which in Greek would be politike: \\\"affairs of the city\\\".\r\n\r\n<p>The Greeks also gave the written word, and Orthodox Christianity, to the Slavic and Bulgar peoples: Russians, Serbs, Bulgarians, et al.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Supposedly, it was the public debate that Athenian democracy required that gave birth to the systematic analysis of philosophic and political questions - roughly 5th century B.C. - but who really knows...</p>  ',0,673,0,0,'65.229.226.8','en'),(15320,2568,'2002-03-28 10:46:49',154,'ryan','ahhhh, city states.  those were the days.',0,41,0,0,'63.178.115.46','en'),(15321,2569,'2002-03-28 10:47:11',154,'ryan','<b>TAG</b><p>\r\ntalented and gifted',0,34,0,0,'63.178.115.46','en'),(15322,2568,'2002-03-28 12:54:36',239,'Poonlarp','although <b>republic</b> <i>comes</i> from a latin word, so do some huge majority of english/western words. plato wrote a nice little ditty called <i>the republic</i>, yes? plato was a happening greek, i think (although i might be wrong- im not literature minded, nor well versed in who has hot and happening in ancient medeterranean social circles). i think the point here wasnt linguistics or semantics, but raw political innovation and the obvious greek tie-in there. construcitve.',3,484,175,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15323,2574,'2002-03-28 13:32:30',156,'lotus','this is awesome. <br>\r\ni want to be able to meld pictures with words as swiftly and beautifully as you do.',2,106,0,0,'216.191.251.36','en'),(15324,2575,'2002-03-28 13:35:34',239,'','I got cheese for ya steak<br> \r\nPhilly-style, gimme dues<br> \r\nGot a girl like a rake<br> \r\nKeep my cash in my shoes<br><br> \r\n\r\nGot some bubbles in my bath<br> \r\nGot a face that\'s heart shaped<br> \r\nGot some lube in my ass<br> \r\nJust in case i get raped<br><br> \r\n\r\nGot a present for ya girl<br> \r\nCus ya girl broke my legs<br> \r\nGot a light dimmer switch<br> \r\nAnd a pocket full of pegs<br><br> \r\n\r\nGot a chain round my neck<br> \r\nAnd a can of baked beans<br> \r\nCus i\'m swabbing the deck<br> \r\nAnd i\'m wearing tight jeans<br>',1,554,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15325,567,'2002-03-28 13:43:09',66,'Peter','<br><tt>hmm. \\\"<i>eating placenta</i>\\\"... such nice imagery that puts in my mind at breakfast time, heh.</tt>',1,114,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15326,2574,'2002-03-28 13:44:03',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like this line: \\\"<i>I do most of my important things with my hands. i feed myself with my hands</i>\\\". nice.</tt>',1,128,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15327,2574,'2002-03-28 18:23:58',83,'adamajah','<p>i like this a lot too.  it really helps that you incorporated an image - an option most writers on swin>org neglect.</p>',2,123,0,0,'66.13.23.218','en'),(15328,2568,'2002-03-28 21:50:21',175,'Paul G','I am going to appear to be difficult again - to those who can still see me.  Plato wrote a book that he called: <i>Politeia</i>.  When Cicero translated that book into Latin he decided to title it: <i>The Republic</i>.  Politeia is something like \\\"the class of citizens who rule\\\" or, \\\"regime\\\" (Bloom).  Now, in the text that we call <i>The Republic</i> Plato has his Socrates describe regime types.  In order, from best to worst, they are: 1) kingship/aristocracy; 2)timocracy; 3) oligarchy; 4) democracy; and 5) tyranny.  There is no mention of \\\"republican\\\" government in <i>The Republic</i>; it\\\'s not Greek.\r\n\r\n<p>Greece has had a rough time of it for several centuries.  They\\\'re caught in the middle of some rather large conflicts: East-West; Christian-Islam; Europe-Asia.  It might be appropriate to say something about the Turkish genocide of the Armenians and Greeks in Asia.  Hmmm...Samuel Huntington: <i>The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of the World Order</i>.</p>   ',0,1010,0,0,'65.230.18.183','en'),(15329,2574,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh i love the picture.  at first i didn\\\'t see that there was text as well, until i scrolled down to tell you that i love the picture.',1,136,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15330,2569,'2002-03-29 04:01:07',124,'gus','heh, they called our gifted program MGM (mentally gifted minors) but apparently everyone was so upset with the whole mentally gifted moron taunts, which strangely never bothered me, they changed it to G&T, gifted and talented. conviently, the weekly meetings were usually scheduled during math. so while I am excellent at problem solving and whatever, I still dont know long division. Hurray for california\\\'s public schools!',0,427,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15331,2575,'2002-03-29 05:05:04',59,'s5','or flax seeds, which are both dolphin and tuna safe.',5,52,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15332,2575,'2002-03-29 05:21:09',69,'starlajo','poonlarp, i think i love you.',0,29,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(15333,2574,'2002-03-29 05:23:11',69,'starlajo','you have beautiful hands! such a nice picture.\r\ni am full of happy love for people today!',0,89,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(15334,2575,'2002-03-29 10:26:19',154,'ryan','can someone explain flax seeds to me?  i see them at hippy stores, i hear about them, etc.  what do they do?  why should i eat them?  what can i make with them?',0,160,0,0,'63.178.69.48','en'),(15335,2575,'2002-03-29 11:05:49',66,'Peter','<br><tt>flax seed are like on eof the few (and one of the most potent) veggie-friendly sources of omega-3 fatty acids...</tt>',1,125,154,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15336,2575,'2002-03-29 11:11:36',59,'s5','or you can use flax seed oil and get all the same benefits.',0,59,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15337,2575,'2002-03-29 12:15:03',239,'Poonlarp','very true! flax seed, wow. however, i must point out that one can not make a tasty tuna-salad sandwich with flax seed, or oil for that matter. call me a stickler for details, but im just crazy \\\'bout that fish!!',1,213,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15338,2580,'2002-03-29 13:12:53',64,'Joanna','they all have four words in their title? or they all came out the same year? ',0,77,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15339,2580,'2002-03-29 14:42:34',176,'Scapegoat','Do they all have a theme related to communication barriers? just guessing, probably a bad guess.',0,96,0,0,'64.229.160.20','en'),(15340,166,'2002-03-29 15:36:37',65,'le','i\\\'m trying not to journal online anymore. it\\\'s really hard. i don\\\'t know why it\\\'s so hard. maybe just because it\\\'s a habit?\r\n<br><br>\r\nit\\\'s really nice to think about not having a journal tho, after months of not having one, no one will know where i am anymore. no one will have kept track of me, and no one will have anything to say about what i\\\'m doing except people who actually know me. that seems nice.\r\n<br><br>\r\nbut i worry that not writing online means being forgotten. i don\\\'t know who i think i\\\'ll be \\\"forgotten\\\" by or why it should care. it\\\'s an uneasy feeling tho. but, if keeping a journal was just a way to insinuate myself into the lives of other people who are just as obsessed with reading/writing journals as i am, then i will be glad to break the habit.\r\n<br><br>\r\nit was funny that this article about keeping a journal came up on a morning where i was thinking about it.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni miss austin, he is far away in thailand. come home austin, call me and leave me voicemails.\r\n\r\n',4,1035,0,0,'165.247.219.24','en'),(15341,2581,'2002-03-29 17:27:59',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i like this: <i>Ich kann nie auf allem wirklich abrechnen</i>...</tt>',1,77,0,0,'204.168.247.48','en'),(15342,2580,'2002-03-29 18:04:26',129,'moebius','<br>well i always sucked at that \\\"what\\\'s the connection\\\" game. i have no idea what the connection is.<br><br>historical note: but the other deal with that game was they eventually began soliciting listeners for connected songs. if they played your connection, you\\\'d also get a prize.<br><br>anyway, congratulations. it\\\'s always nice to win things. and i didn\\\'t know halstead had a solo album coming out. nifty.',1,428,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15343,166,'2002-03-29 19:02:45',129,'moebius','<br>i started keeping online journals very recently, and since then i\\\'ve found myself considering the <a href=\\\"http://www.wired.com/news/culture/0,1284,50443-2,00.html\\\">\\\"blog phenomenon\\\"</a> much more carefully than before. i\\\'ve come up with a lot of things to say about it--many more than i should yap about in this comment--but not many conclusions.<br><br>for years i was afraid to start an online journal. i admired people like le, who managed to pretty much let it all hang out online, but i also saw the pain it sometimes seemed to put them through. fights with friends, misunderstandings, stalkers, paranoia. i used to think: maybe i\\\'m too old to keep an online journal. maybe it\\\'s something you only do until you know better.<br><br>but eventually i decided i didn\\\'t know any better, either. so i kickstarted something into action last december. and it felt great. the thing is, all my life i\\\'ve always written for readers. never just myself. it\\\'s just the way i\\\'m wired. so i get bored with private journals. and i spent way too long--years actually--without writing anything for anyone. it\\\'s wonderful to have the power to publish, and to know that some things i say might touch an unknown reader out there.<br><br>in the end, for me, it\\\'s not about honesty or keeping it real. it\\\'s just about the writing, having fun, and making a few friends along the way. if that sounds naÃ¯ve, well i guess i\\\'ll learn the hard way.',0,1476,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15344,2577,'2002-03-29 19:18:09',187,'kandrer','<p>OK, now - and with this one - i think i have it clear:<br>\r\nto be really honnest, what i feel like telling you is:<br>\r\n<br>\r\nwhy not get really far away from your parents for a while?<br>\r\n<u>and</u><br>\r\nwhy not wait until you know better the guy you\\\'re gonna get married with?<br>\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\ni completely know it\\\'s none of my business, and i am damn aware than my advice might be a bad one. and it\\\'s not the right place to tell this. but shit, are you actually <i>living</i> in this life you describe in your writing? and going to get married at 23 years old to someone who hides <i>details</i> like that?<br>\r\n</p><p>\r\ni hope i\\\'m fucking wrong. i\\\'m sorry.\r\n</p>',1,684,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15345,166,'2002-03-29 20:32:08',187,'kandrer','<p>\r\ni am already missing reading le\\\'s journal. if at first i asked myself questions about the <i>why write this?</i>, soon after i didn\\\'t care at all. i just enjoyed reading. like you say, Moebius, i don\\\'t think this is related to honesty (for the reader either); le writes good stuff. the fact that it\\\'s about her (maybe) and true (maybe) doesn\\\'t change anything. well, i hope it doesn\\\'t.\r\n</p><p>\r\nso if you write here instead, le, then everything is fine :)\r\n</p>',1,485,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15346,2575,'2002-03-29 20:51:02',129,'moebius','<br>we eat a lot of tuna at our house. and i\\\'m not sure if it\\\'s just because we like the taste of the tuna, or if it\\\'s the communitarian aspect of the meal.<br><br>see, tuna is also our two cats\\\' favorite food above all others. i get the impression they\\\'d mainline tuna into their little kittie veins if they could. whenever molls and i open up a can, we share a bit of it with our furry pals. probably this is encouraging bad behavior. but it\\\'s kinda fun, to have molls & me & maylande & miranda all munching happily away at once like a family around the dinner table.<br><br>of course, we could get a kid or two. then we could have that experience a lot more often, with other more \\\"tuna-safe\\\" meals. and our dinner conversation topics could expand as well! hmmm. i\\\'ll have to ask molls about that, see what she thinks.',1,848,0,0,'148.87.1.171','en'),(15347,2575,'2002-03-29 21:22:12',66,'Peter','<br><tt>go moe!</tt>',1,20,129,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15348,2576,'2002-03-29 22:33:43',239,'Poonlarp','can\\\'t believe how no one has commented on this yet.',1,54,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15349,2576,'2002-03-30 01:07:54',187,'kandrer','well, as often (always? i have not been here enough) with Peter, once i read it, i feel kinda afraid to make any comment. could i clap? i think about a noisy museum: if every person looking at a painting had to say something...</p>\r\nnow that i\\\'m here... thank you Peter, very very nice! :)',1,292,0,0,'208.223.155.15','en'),(15350,2582,'2002-03-30 02:23:23',176,'Scapegoat','This sounded like a good \\\'good friday\\\' to me. Productive and enjoyable. I am jones\\\'in for a donut now too. Thats saying a lot as in one of my previous employment incarnations I was a donut baker. Thank you Peter. ',0,222,0,0,'64.229.161.124','en'),(15351,2580,'2002-03-30 02:51:27',168,'laney','hint: focus less on the song titles, more on the artist',0,55,0,0,'64.175.184.131','en'),(15352,2581,'2002-03-30 03:21:05',148,'jeeff','goals?  zen.',0,12,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15353,2580,'2002-03-30 03:22:25',148,'jeeff','i won a calculator at a baseball game when i was a kid.  it was the best.',0,73,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15354,2577,'2002-03-30 03:27:11',148,'jeeff','i\\\'m also a horse, and i also take it with a big grain of salt, and i\\\'m also making tons of changes in life this year.  bring on the bad luck!',0,147,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15355,2582,'2002-03-30 03:28:53',148,'jeeff','i yearn for new york.',0,21,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15356,2582,'2002-03-30 03:31:40',64,'Joanna','They actually have a status entitled \\\"Delinquent\\\" ? What do they want you to do about it - spend more time with your phone, maybe take it to church and try to teach it some moral fibre?',1,191,0,0,'203.97.2.243','en'),(15357,2577,'2002-03-30 07:42:54',187,'kandrer','ok,i didn\\\'t sleep enough, i guess i could have been more cautious in my previous comment. it\\\'s just... you know lotus, you seem to be much too gentle to know how to get rid of your future husband if he reveals himself as someone you can\\\'t stay with. (i know a bit about macho-men since i\\\'m french.) ok, suppose you want security and love comes after in your priority list, good. then what could be wrong in taking more time to get to know him better *before* getting married? love relationship are not stable enough, fine. but that does not make arranged mariage better in any way. love could be found <i>even</i> in arranged mariage, maybe <i>after a few years</i>... but you are the one that will spend these <i>few</i> years without love. isn\\\'t it better to spend a few years of love <i>first</i> and well, if love has to fade away, move on? there is no way to predict how people change in years, but some people are a bad match to begin with. just because there is no way to be 100% sure of the \\\"true love\\\", you don\\\'t have to go for someone choosen by somebody else than you. please take your time to know him better. please.',1,1153,0,0,'206.15.2.207','en'),(15358,2583,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','oh kandrer, what a lovely entry!  you have many nice qualities.',2,63,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15359,2583,'2002-03-30 10:04:31',59,'s5','i share your fondness for women\\\'s clothing.',1,46,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15360,2584,'2002-03-30 10:07:48',69,'starlajo','this is why i refuse to travel to such places. i wouldn\\\'t cope at all.',0,73,0,0,'203.96.111.202','en'),(15361,2584,'2002-03-30 10:18:58',59,'s5',':(',0,2,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15362,2584,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','yes, we say \\\"toilet\\\" in many parts of the world.  s5 finds this extremely distasteful.<br>\r\n<br>\r\ntraveller\\\'s secret #1: always take your own toilet paper.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nsorry for the belated advice.',2,205,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15363,2584,'2002-03-30 11:02:38',59,'s5','though it sounds like, in thailand, asking for a toilet by name is the thing to do.',0,83,0,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15364,2583,'2002-03-30 12:42:06',176,'Scapegoat','I don\\\'t think there is a better way to introduce yourself, really. I\\\'ve thought about this before in regards to letting other here at swinney in on who I seem to be and I think this is probably the best way to do it. I haven\\\'t done it yet cause I don\\\'t know what I would say exactly. Anyway, it is nice to know more about you. I\\\'m with you on the  difficulty expressing feelings and the tendency to be ambiguous hoping loved ones understand anyway.',0,463,0,0,'64.229.161.124','en'),(15365,2584,'2002-03-30 12:45:38',65,'le','<br>\r\nthat sucks.\r\n<br><br>\r\ni decided a while ago that if i ever go to asia (i hope to, at some point), i will plan ahead. to give myself plenty of time to beef up my leg muscles. squatting will be an important skill.\r\n',0,220,0,0,'165.247.217.195','en'),(15366,2576,'2002-03-30 12:50:35',176,'Scapegoat','This was one of those post that I read several times and can\\\'t initially make any comments. Sometimes I just can\\\'t figure out how to put my reaction into words. Speachless, kinda. ',0,186,0,0,'64.229.161.124','en'),(15367,2580,'2002-03-30 12:54:58',176,'Scapegoat','All bands who\\\'s main vocalist went on to a solo career? Oh this is exciting I think I might actually have it now.',0,116,0,0,'64.229.161.124','en'),(15368,2584,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','i\\\'m glad i read swinney and eat breakfast at the same time.',1,62,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15369,2584,'2002-03-30 19:51:28',122,'hool','i thought this story was shitty.  harharhar.  but seriously, it\\\'s one of those things you don\\\'t really think about with travel, or camping, until you get there.  squatting is quite the common denominator.  ',0,212,0,0,'66.185.84.207','en'),(15370,2584,'2002-03-31 00:29:34',66,'Peter','<br><tt>once, in ecuador, i stopped at a soda stand to poop; this is because they had a toilet... it was right up next to the 8 lane highway, with a cinderblock wall that covered to your chest. (note: they also had a stack of <i>reader\\\'s digests</i> to wipe with, as well as a very dirty can to dispose of said <i>digests</i> in) so, you got to wave hello to scores of passing tourists and tour-busses while shitting! it was fun.</tt>',1,437,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15371,2584,'2002-03-31 01:40:25',92,'kini','i did the bus thing on a thirty one hour ride from java to bali. this particular bus had a handle contraption (for steadying purposes) dangling from the ceiling, not unlike the ones above hospital beds. there are many winding, mountainous roads in java. this was evidenced by the amount of crap all over the floor.\r\n\r\n<p>however, given the choice between a filthy western style toilet or a squat, i\\\'d take the squat any day.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>oh, and also - it\\\'s kinda funny to get somewhere that actually has western style toilets, only to see footprints all over the seat. brought to you by locals who prefer the old method.</p>',0,628,0,0,'203.27.69.91','en'),(15372,2578,'2002-03-31 01:57:07',74,'jo','wow.<BR>\r\nwow.',0,14,0,0,'209.163.199.174','en'),(15373,2580,'2002-03-31 02:01:29',74,'jo','YAY neil halstead!<BR>\r\ni never win these contests, either. when i was in middle school, i loved calling in so much that i had two different radio stations\\\' numbers on speed dial.',1,182,0,0,'209.163.199.174','en'),(15374,2580,'2002-03-31 02:03:30',168,'laney','scapegoat wins!',0,15,0,0,'12.235.52.208','en'),(15375,2584,'2002-03-31 04:47:35',187,'kandrer','i\\\'m so glad about this post and the comments; i laughed my ass off (sorry).<br>\r\nbut pooping is a <b>serious</b> subject. as many hidden human activities (here, think about your own) it sadly lacks of documentation and deep studies. therefore, i will keep my pooping related <i>material</i> for a long to come exhaustive encyclopedia.',1,337,0,0,'166.90.39.88','en'),(15376,2583,'2002-03-31 05:12:23',187,'kandrer','hey! who categorized this as cult?! you guys make me blush :}</p>\r\n<p>\r\no: you\\\'re so nice i can never believe you. how can u express yourself like this? sometimes, i dream that i do it, and i am scared by what makes it out of my own mouth.\r\n</p><p>\r\ns5: i am also jealous <i>of you</i> :).\r\n</p><p>\r\nScapegoat: about ambiguity, i had guessed, since your <i>nuke them all</i> post :). i love your pseudo too.</p>\r\n<p>\r\nthank you all, thank you. sigh.',1,452,0,0,'166.90.39.88','en'),(15377,2583,'2002-03-31 05:32:25',187,'kandrer','naaah... it was chia\\\'s post and you commented it. then either i didn\\\'t have guessed, either i guessed by using these mysterious paranoid insight powers i am so happy about :}',1,180,176,0,'166.90.39.88','en'),(15378,2583,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','<p>kandrer, i was being completely sincere!  i recognised a lot of my own qualities in your list.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>also, the answer to being jealous of womens clothes is to have the clothes of your dreams made especially for you.  the more men that do this, the better the mens fashion industry will get for expressive boys.</p>',2,321,187,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15379,2578,'2002-03-31 10:05:05',65,'le','this is awesome.',0,16,0,0,'165.247.202.59','en'),(15380,2584,'2002-03-31 19:13:42',124,'gus','gas station toilets in my thai experience were in the basic rural poo/high tech area. they tended to have a toilet, it often didnt work. Or sometimes there would be a small shit house with a row of stalls and squat toilets, each with its drippy faucet forever leaking leaking into a trough that you could dip an old plastic tub into to splash your ass off.\r\n<p>\r\nI visited pay toilets that ranged from the rural low tech, to a more functional variety, but more than once I paid my money to squat over two dirty 2x4\\\'s and piss into the sawed off end of a clorox bottle. there was to be no wiping, and no splashing. the interior was hosed down after each use, most often by a toothless old man.\r\n<p>\r\nYou may not want to think about this until you\\\'re there, faced with the footprints on the seat, or the mucky algae water in the trough that\\\'s breeding mosquitos in your guesthouse bathroom, but you\\\'ll think about it everyday once you ARE there.',0,955,0,0,'64.163.215.164','en'),(15381,2582,'2002-03-31 20:59:44',66,'Peter','<br><tt>i dont get it; this is only the second bill ive gotten from them since getting the service last june. ten months, two bills; id say its they who were <i>delinquent</i>, not i. needless to say, im changing services in june when my contract rolls around again.</tt>',1,271,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15382,2587,'2002-04-01 00:16:15',176,'Scapegoat','I\\\'d buy that book.',0,21,0,0,'64.229.168.17','en'),(15383,2583,'2002-04-01 00:32:54',148,'jeeff','good idea, o.  i\\\'ve always wanted some cool clothes.  the only thing that holds me back is that my body is always fluctuating so clothes i buy one day don\\\'t fit me the next.',0,179,0,0,'24.102.28.74','en'),(15384,2583,'2002-04-01 00:36:12',176,'Scapegoat','Thank you Kandrer for your nice words ',0,38,187,0,'64.229.168.84','en'),(15385,2580,'2002-04-01 00:38:31',176,'Scapegoat','<b>I win !! Ya Hoooo!!</b>',0,26,0,0,'64.229.168.84','en'),(15386,2583,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','elastic is the answer :D!  oh, and velcro straps.',2,49,148,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15387,2583,'2002-09-01 23:16:03',57,'weeb','VELCRO. it is the answer. i like it better than buttons, because it\\\'s easier to sew and never comes off like a button. mew.',0,126,0,0,'63.197.69.58','en'),(15388,2581,'2002-04-01 04:21:52',75,'tyler','this reminds me of <i>Junky</i> by william s. burroughs, when he talks about why he and his friends are junkies. he says that being a junky is what gives their lives meaning; needing the next shot motivates them to live.\r\n<p>\r\n&quot;after drinking enough all I will really want out of life is some tylenol or asprin.&quot;\r\n<p>\r\ni\\\'m not saying you are addicted to anything or preaching to you--that one line just jumped out at me. i\\\'m no psychologist. best friendly.\r\n',1,474,0,0,'165.247.164.18','en'),(15389,2581,'2002-04-01 04:50:49',239,'note','when someone says \\\"im not preaching to you\\\" they probably are.',1,68,0,0,'24.90.89.134','en'),(15390,2584,'2002-04-01 07:19:32',89,'sawks','mr swinney and other travellers, you should have packed one o\\\' <a href=\\\"http://www.theimageworks.com/toilet/toilet5.htm\\\">these</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nsomeone needs to invent what is basically a bumbag (ok, a \\\'fannypack\\\' for you americans) with a roll of tp inside it, and a wee dispenser outlet<br>\r\n<br>\r\nif you were squatting with a friend, you could grab your slice of tp from the other guys pack, in a manner reminiscent of apes grooming each other<br>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n',3,473,0,0,'144.136.21.180','en'),(15391,2584,'2004-07-27 05:13:48',63,'o','i love you sawks.  please write articles on swinney.org.',1,56,89,0,'65.84.40.62','en'),(15392,2581,'2002-04-01 07:36:26',176,'Scapegoat','I take the comparison to a beat as a compliment, I think.',0,57,0,0,'64.229.166.245','en'),(15393,2584,'2002